inl note_of_source_boundary_authority_10 () = Note ("SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY", 10u32, "2026-10-15T05:41Z", "lane-b", "01:41 lane B: main applied the source-blocks patch in pack-1g (tree Program.fs holds it); on the 1g candidate DLL the staged consumers work: zed test-at-row identical to the heuristic on 4 files (191 definitions, 32 test-row dry runs), Kino memo_test with SourceBlocks 20/20; reduced corpus on 1g 127/127 lib + 17/17 Tibia; consumers land when 1g is installed")
inl note_of_source_boundary_authority_11 () = Note ("SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY", 11u32, "2026-10-15T06:20Z", "lane-b", "02:20 lane B: pack-1g installed with --source-blocks; consumers LANDED (uncommitted): apps/zed/tasks/test-at-row.ps1 takes block spans from SpiralCompiler --source-blocks (refuses a DLL without the capability; smoke on the installed DLL: stream 38 definitions, row 15 selects the 6 tests that reach fold), apps/kino memo.ex program_blocks/compiled_part/statement_line heuristics removed in favour of new lib/spiral/kino/source_blocks.ex (one batched --source-blocks call per run; keys fall back to the whole source without the capability), memo_test 20 incl. compiler-boundary cases; no line heuristic left in these consumers (Kino's own spi_walk still serves stubbed runs only)")
inl note_of_source_boundary_authority_12 () = Note ("SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY", 12u32, "2026-10-15T09:03Z", "main-thread", "05:03 main: Done. The host --source-blocks shipped in pack-1g (installed, blessed single-flight-20261015-015332), and the zed test-at-row and Kino source_blocks/memo consumers landed on it (uncommitted; source_blocks.ex untracked).")
inl note_of_source_boundary_authority_13 () = Note ("SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY", 13u32, "2026-10-23T22:34Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:34 x: consumer requirement from the Spiral rewrite of x's sim (SIM-IN-SPIRAL n40). x's xsim (a Rust binary from Spiral) must split a .spi into cells exactly as Spiral.Cells.Document.parse_spi does: Kino's document.spi from_spi + decode_cells, kind :spiral sources. x's committed fixtures (apps/sim/fixtures/prelude.final.json etc.) are byte-compared. x's own splitter (blank line before column 0) differs: Cells normalizes 'test X static =' into a '///- --test static' cell and merges consecutive blocks. Ask: make the document parser (from_spi/decode_cells, plus the compiler-bounds variant this item introduces) a lib/spiral module with Rust and Gleam arms, so Kino and x share one implementation. x will not copy spi_walk.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_1 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 1u32, "2026-10-14T13:36Z", "lane-b", "daemon-memory sampler written in a private copy (apps/xsim lib/xsim/daemon_memory.ex + mix xsim.run --daemon-memory): finds the daemon pid from Kino's hello banner, samples its process tree's working set every 1 s from one pwsh sampler, prints 'daemon pid= peak_mb= procs= samples=' per schedule; test queued behind the heavy gate")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_2 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 2u32, "2026-10-14T20:17Z", "lane-b", "sampler proven on real Kino (private copy, apps/xsim daemon_memory.ex + xsim.run --daemon-memory; the pwsh port needs :hide on Windows or it exits silently): math.spi 4 cells, 2 schedules, every invariant + commute ok, daemon tree peak 1,150 MB over 7 processes, 36 samples; xsim tests 30/30")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_3 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 3u32, "2026-10-15T09:07Z", "lane-b", "05:07 lane B: scale step (fake executor, whole lib/spiral: 76 .spi, 200 users, 5 edits, 20% duplicates, concurrency 16, xsim on the tree Kino with tonight's landed changes): 1,960 posts, 217,004 events, ~260k deliveries (43k duplicates), ~6,000 starts over ~5,900 prefixes, 120-133 cancels per schedule; every invariant ok on both schedules and commute ok; 158 s and 200 s per schedule, peak BEAM 171/258 MB; xsim tests 30/30; 10^4 cells not reached yet (the lib yields ~2k posts; --churn or a second corpus would scale it)")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_4 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 groom: scale run: whole lib/spiral, 1,960 posts, 217k events, invariants ok, peak BEAM 258 MB (lane B, 10-15). Next grain: run it against the pack-1h compiler with concurrency raised.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_5 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 5u32, "2026-10-16T03:11Z", "claude", "(2026-10-15 23:11 -04:00, Claude b7595ac3) apps/xsim moved to the i574n/x repo as apps/sim (user request: everything X-related in x). It depends on the sibling spiral/apps/kino by path, so it still battle-tests Kino from downstream: from x, mix test 30/30 and the lib-corpus run (2 schedules, every invariant ok, commute ok). spiral's scripts/build.ps1 and CI no longer run it; its kino-executor run now uses a private daemon port (13950) and stops only that daemon.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_6 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 6u32, "2026-10-18T07:25Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 03:25 new mix xsim.world: writes the X state a scenario leaves (framed posts as reply chains, edits as new versions with edit_history_post_ids, like and reply counts, optional lost frames) as an e2e stub world plus the expected program; x client e2e uses it, apps/sim/build.ps1 regenerates and compares the fixtures. Elixir until SIM-IN-SPIRAL")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_7 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 7u32, "2026-10-18T08:34Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 04:34 found while adding the xsim.world drift guard: (1) on Windows mix.bat drops everything after a comma in a bare argument, so build.ps1's corpus run '--personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2' ran lurker=2 only; Personas.parse now also accepts '+' and the scripts use it (test added); (2) JSON.encode! writes map keys in atom-creation order (OTP 26+), which differs across builds; xsim.world writes key-sorted JSON (Xsim.XWorld.json). Guard now passes across MIX_BUILD_PATHs")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_8 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 8u32, "2026-10-18T12:14Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 08:14 x/apps/sim follow-ups to KINO-RENAME: --executor kino is now --executor cells (Xsim.Executors.cells; DaemonMemory takes daemon_port), verified on real Cells (math.spi 3 cells, every invariant incl. breaking_edits_fail, commute ok, daemon tree 934 MB via --daemon-memory, which landed from lane B's private copy); --spi lists accept '+' like --personas (mix.bat comma bug). With the persona fix the CI corpus run grew from 1,662 to 1,714 posts (10,002 events, ~11,500 deliveries), every invariant and commute ok. Left in this epic: real-Cells corpora beyond 2 notebooks, Cells' memo policy under eviction, 10^4 cells on the real path")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_9 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 9u32, "2026-10-18T20:46Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 16:46 edits travel as frames too: Frames.explode_posts turns {:edit, id, source} into {:edit_frame, id, rev, k, n, body} (rev counts a post's edits, so frames of different edits never mix; an empty source still gets one frame), the World gathers each revision's frames and applies the edit at its first frame's index, waiting for the post if needed; summary gains framed_edits. Test: 3 edits + 1 breaking edit framed at 20 weight, 30% duplicates, 12-wide shuffle: every invariant ok, framed_edits = edits, commutes with the unframed run; sim tests 37/37")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_10 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 10u32, "2026-10-18T21:18Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 17:18 grace-window eviction built: Runner evict: {:grace, n} (mix xsim.run --evict-grace N) marks a result for eviction n deliveries after its last demand goes, drops the mark when demand returns (counted as rescues when the result was held), flushes all marks at the end so unwanted_results_freed still holds; test: fickle+editor+churn scenario keeps every invariant, commutes with keep, ends with no marks (timing-dependent counts left out of the test). First measurement, lib corpus 1,714 posts, frames, churn, personas, 1 schedule each: keep starts 9,900 peak 9,809; evict 10,100 (+2.0%) peak 1,665; grace25 10,344 peak 1,705; grace100 10,769 peak 1,853 rescues 7; grace400 10,032 (+1.3%) peak 2,138 rescues 116; all invariants ok. Starts vary run to run with cancel timing, so repeats decide (preliminary)")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_11 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 11u32, "2026-10-18T22:27Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 18:27 decision on the result-store policy (memo under eviction), measured: total starts are dominated by cancel/restart timing (keep 11,092 vs evict 9,822 mean over 3 runs under machine contention), so the runner now counts recomputes (a key queued again after its result was evicted). Lib corpus, 1,714 posts, 2-3 runs each: evict 261 recomputes (2.6% of ~10k compiles), peak 1,664 results (-85% vs keep ~11,000); grace100 220 (-16%), peak 1,790; grace400 152 (-42%), peak 2,202 (-80% vs keep), 104 rescues; every invariant ok in every run. Chosen for Cells' memo: evict with a grace window (~400 deliveries): on the real path a recompute is a compile of seconds while a held result is a small text, so -42% recomputes for +32% held results (still -80% vs keeping all) is the better trade; immediate eviction only when memory is the binding constraint. Most evictions come from re-keys (edits, churn), which no grace window can save")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_12 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 12u32, "2026-10-19T00:48Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 20:48 correction to note 11, measured on the real path: the fake executor has no persistent memo, real Cells does. Unpaced real runs never evict (deliveries arrive in ms, compiles take seconds, so lapses and re-keys cancel in-flight work instead), so the runner gained --pace-ms N (waits up to N ms for finished tasks between deliveries). math.spi 6 cells, fickle=6+editor=1, 3 users, paced 3 s, fresh SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO_DIR per run: keep 25 real compiles (memo misses), peak 21 results; evict 26 misses, peak 6, 17 evictions, 2 recomputes all served by the memo (hits 37); grace10 26 misses, peak 16, 0 rescues. Decision revised: with Cells' disk memo behind the result store, evict immediately (same real compiles, smallest store); a grace window only pays without a persistent memo. CI corpus run now uses --evict; the grace path stays covered by its unit test")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_13 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 13u32, "2026-10-19T00:59Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 20:59 first three-notebook real-Cells run: math + date_time + sm' (8 cells each, interleaved author threads via --spi-file), --memo --evict, personas fickle=2+editor=1+breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3, fresh memo dir, own daemon port: 24 posts, 121 events, 137-139 deliveries, 21 prefixes, every invariant incl. breaking_edits_fail on both schedules, commute ok; schedule 1 122 s cold, schedule 2 32 s (memo hits 33 then 99, misses 34-35); daemon tree peak 3,445 MB over 8 processes (--daemon-memory), the highest measured so far (previous 3,398 MB with two notebooks); not scaled further on a shared 16 GB machine")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_14 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 14u32, "2026-10-19T12:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:25 lap 1 grain: located: xsim lives in the x repo (x/apps/sim, x lane's tree), so main records only; the 10-18 notes show the next steps already moved: --pace-ms for real Cells, the three-notebook real run (math + date_time + sm', all invariants, daemon peak 3,445 MB via --daemon-memory), so the sampler grain is done Next: ask nothing; next lap read x lane's SIM-IN-SPIRAL progress and decide whether this epic's remaining 18% is scale runs on a quiet machine")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_15 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 15u32, "2026-10-19T13:08Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:08 lap 2 grain: x lane state (read only): X-CELLS-SPLIT at 35 percent, its prelude, glyphs, protocol, splitter and events modules pass Cells on rust + gleam (+ zig) after today's byte-unit lib flip; those are the modules xsim's frames reuse (SIM-IN-SPIRAL note 3), so the sim's Spiral port has its shared base; this epic's remaining 18 percent stays scale runs of the real-Cells three-notebook scenario on a quiet machine Next: when a quiet window opens (evening), rerun the three-notebook real-Cells scenario at concurrency 3 with --daemon-memory to confirm the 3,445 MB peak holds after today's lib changes")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_16 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 16u32, "2026-10-19T18:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:22 lap 4 grain: core v1 holds for the sim: the x lane rebuilt x on the fixed core at 13:0x (view 1005 -> 987 lines, 16 crates under 1000, no _Lt order enum) and its e2e passes, 31 tutorial steps + 5 extra cases with all 4 sim threads, post-thread 34 posts read back, delete-thread 34; helper lane 2 proposes splitting SIM-IN-SPIRAL into a bulk story owned by the x lane with no KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dep Next: decide the SIM-IN-SPIRAL split with the x lane (main proposes yes: the Spiral port does not need Kino without Elixir)")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_17 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 17u32, "2026-10-20T20:43Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 16:43 three-notebook real-Cells rerun on today's Spiral-backed sim (scenario + invariants through the Gleam build): math + date_time + sm', --cells 8, --executor cells --memo --evict, personas fickle=2+editor=1+breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3, fresh memo dir, own daemon port 13953, --daemon-memory. 24 posts, 178 events, 199-202 deliveries, 22 prefixes; daemon tree peak 3,049 MB over 8 processes (below the 3,445 MB of 10-18). FINDING: schedule 1 sequential_equivalence=FAIL[f2p1] and commute=FAIL[f2p1]: the concurrent evaluation of f2p1 (sm' thread, 2 cells) returned {:error, 'errors were found at the given arguments: 1st argument: the table identifier does not refer to an existing ETS table'} while the sequential baseline of the same sources returned ok; schedule 2 kept every invariant. An ETS table used after its owner exited, under concurrency 3, in Cells' evaluation path (not the sim: the executor is Spiral.Cells.Notebook.evaluate). Reported to main (Cells owner). Script: x lane scratch three_notebooks.ps1 (eoie job job-1792527165391-x-three-notebooks)")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_18 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 18u32, "2026-10-21T00:04Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 20:04 Cells ETS race (found by x-lane on job-1792527165391) fixed by main as CELLS-ETS-OWNER (named tables owned by a VM-lifetime process) and proven end to end by rerunning the same three-notebook repro (x-ets-proof: math, date_time, sm' via the cells executor, max-concurrency 3, 2 schedules, memo + evict + daemon-memory): clean, commute ok, daemon peak 3,427 MB")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_19 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 19u32, "2026-10-21T01:46Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 21:46 x-lane backlog refactor: estimate 2400m -> 480m, now meaning remaining minutes (82% done). Left: scale runs on real Cells only (lib corpus beyond 3 notebooks on the cells executor at higher concurrency with --daemon-memory and --pace-ms, which the runner already has since 10-18 note 12; 10^4 cells on the real path), on a quiet machine. The three-notebook real run is done (x-ets-proof 10-20). Corrected 21:53: the first version of this note listed --pace-ms as still to do")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_20 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 20u32, "2026-10-21T11:44Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-21 07:44 ladder attempt 1 (x-ladder 07:04-07:43) stopped by the x lane during rung 1 (6 notebooks, cells 8, concurrency 3): the rung's Cells daemon tree reached 2,979 MB within minutes while free memory fell to 1.5 GB beside four other lanes' heavy jobs, and Claude Code's memory reaper had started killing background shells. Stopping it brought free memory back to 7.3 GB. A scale measurement under that contention would mix load with the sim's own cost (last night's 300 s Cells timeouts were load), so the ladder reruns after Tibia's PR-17 live block at a quieter hour, rung by rung, stopping before free memory goes under ~2 GB")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_21 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 21u32, "2026-10-21T13:17Z", "x", "2026-10-21 09:17 x: the ladder rerun waits for tibia's PR-17 and PR-18 live blocks, which run back to back after x-postid-round releases (PR-18 ends about 13:15-13:30). It gets queued only after tibia says PR-18 has ended, and stops before free memory drops below about 2 GB.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_22 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 22u32, "2026-10-21T19:40Z", "x", "2026-10-21 15:40 x: rule set before measuring. The ladder reruns after main's compiler install (night pack with the Gleam slice boundary check, after tibia's 15:45-17:20 block), so no compiler swap lands mid-run, through scratchpad x_ladder_guarded.ps1: one rung per process (6:8:3, 10:8:4, 14:6:4), each tree killed if machine free memory falls under 2,000 MB (the 07:43 run sank to 1.5 GB). A rung passes on exit 0 with verdict=exit; each rung records wall_s, the daemon peak_mb and lowest_free_mb. Close the item when all three rungs pass. If a rung hits the floor, record its peak and the rung before it as the ladder's measured ceiling on this 16 GB machine; that counts as 'scale ladder recorded' (X-DONE D3) and closes the item with the ceiling as the finding. Then delete lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_23 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 23u32, "2026-10-21T20:30Z", "x", "2026-10-21 16:30 x: main's compiler install moved to ~18:50-19:20 (after tibia's live block ~17:40 and eoie r30-r33). Ladder slot: after the install ping, until 20:50, when the run ends with this session's 21:00 deadline. Added rule: a rung still running at 20:50 is stopped and recorded as 'not finished (time)', not as a failure; the item then stays open with the finished rungs recorded and the rest for the next session.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_24 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 24u32, "2026-10-21T21:08Z", "x", "2026-10-21 17:08 x: tibia's live block ended early (17:07), so the ladder started now instead of after main's install: job-1792616902882-x-ladder-guarded, x_ladder_guarded.ps1 with -StopAt 18:45 so it can't overlap main's ~18:50 compiler install (it uses the single-flight DLL). Rules per notes 22-23, except the time cutoff is 18:45 instead of 20:50. Main was told.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_25 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 25u32, "2026-10-21T21:37Z", "x", "2026-10-21 17:37 x: main's install moved to ~20:00-20:30 (eoie release until ~19:40), so the 18:45 hard stop of the running job no longer protects anything; its -StopAt can't be changed in flight. Rule added before results: if the 18:45 stop cuts a rung, rerun the unfinished rungs (same guard, -StopAt 19:50) right after, and record them as normal results; 'not finished (time)' applies only to a rung still running at 19:50.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_26 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 26u32, "2026-10-21T21:44Z", "x", "2026-10-21 17:44 x: ladder rung 1 (6 notebooks : 8 cells : concurrency 3) PASS, job-1792616902882-x-ladder-guarded. Real-Cells executor over 48 posts, 375 events: schedule 1 ms=929708 (cold compiles), schedule 2 ms=70625 (memo); commute=ok; sequential_equivalence, no_stale_results and one_compile all ok on both schedules; peak_inflight 3, peak_beam_mb 175/126. Daemon (pid 100764) peak_mb=3418, last 2058, 8 procs. Guard: verdict=exit, exit 0, wall 1963 s, peak tree private 2295 MB, lowest machine free 2344 MB (floor 2000; other lanes held ~9 GB). Rung 2 (10:8:4) started ~17:42.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_27 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 27u32, "2026-10-21T22:15Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:15 x: rule change before rung 2 reports. Drop note 25's rerun: eoie's r30-r33 release and main's night-pack install (the Gleam slice fix every lane gets) queue behind this job's heavy slot, and a rerun until 19:50 would push the install toward 21:00. So the ladder releases at 18:45 at the latest. A rung cut then is recorded as 'not finished (time)', and unfinished rungs stay for the next session on a quieter machine. The item stays open in that case.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_28 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 28u32, "2026-10-21T22:19Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:19 x: resume tooling now lives in the repo lab (it was in the session scratchpad): lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM/ladder-guarded.ps1 wraps ladder.ps1 one rung per process. It kills the rung's tree when machine free memory drops under -FloorMb (2000) or at -StopAt, and it now refuses to start a rung unless free memory is at least -AdmitMb (6000; rung 1 needed ~3.4 GB of daemon plus headroom while other lanes held ~9 GB). It continues past a failed rung and exits with the count of failed or skipped rungs. Next session: eoie job start --heavy x-ladder 6000000 pwsh -NoProfile -File lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM/ladder-guarded.ps1 -Rungs <remaining>, at a quiet hour, with a background job wait.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_29 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 29u32, "2026-10-21T22:46Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:46 x: ladder rung 2 (10 notebooks : 8 cells : concurrency 4) NOT FINISHED (time), per note 27. Stopped by the 18:45 -StopAt after 3,830 s with no XSIM line, so its first schedule (cold compiles) didn't complete, where rung 1's took 930 s. Peak tree private 1,710 MB, lowest machine free 2,636 MB; the memory floor never tripped. Rung 3 (14:6:4) not started. The time, not memory, was the limit on a machine where tibia-build-pace and eoie notebook checks held the other heavy slot and compile windows throughout. The slot went to eoie-release-r30to33 at 18:46; no leftover processes, daemon port 13954 closed. Item stays open with rung 1 recorded (note 26). Next session: lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM/ladder-guarded.ps1 -Rungs 10:8:4,14:6:4 alone on a quiet machine, budget ~2 h per rung at today's pace (note 28).")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_30 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 30u32, "2026-10-21T22:48Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:48 x: why rung 2 ran over 2.5x slower than rung 1's pace (80 posts would be ~1,550 s cold at rung 1's 19 s/post; not done at 3,830 s). Machine contention: tibia-build-pace held the other heavy slot and eoie notebook checks held compile windows the whole time, and the machine sat at 12.3 of 17 GB committed. The os_mon/memsup crash loop in the rung's log (get_mem_info timing out at 60 s, os_mon exiting 18:39) is a symptom of that load: it is os_mon's background polling in the xsim VM, which pulls in os_mon as a Cells extra_application, and it isn't on the runner's path. One latent Cells issue found on the way: CompilerDaemon.memory_pressed?/0 calls :memsup.get_system_memory_data() inside the daemon's GenServer (handle_info :idle_check, only after 60 s idle), so when the Windows sysinfo port hangs, a compile request arriving during that check waits up to the memsup timeout. A Task with a short timeout, or a cached reading, would remove the stall. Main/Cells lane to judge.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_31 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 31u32, "2026-10-22T00:15Z", "x", "2026-10-21 20:15 x: rung 10:8:4 diagnosed from its memo dir (lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM/memo_timeline.ps1, decodes each .etf entry's kind and write time; no compile). Rung 1 (6:8:3, 17:10-17:41) wrote 202 entries: 54 cell results + 17 code results + 131 source_blocks. Rung 2 (17:42-18:42, killed by StopAt) wrote 149: 134 source_blocks (all also in rung 1's memo, content-addressed) but only 13 cell results + 2 code results, i.e. about 15 results/hour against rung 1's ~140/hour. Not scale: eoie job ids show 57 jobs overlapping rung 2 (eoie-release-r30to33, main-night-prereview, tibia-build-pace, ~25 eoie nbcheck/preview/check Cells runs, ~20 tibia hunt runs) against 6 for rung 1, and gaps of 100-520 s between result writes. So rung 2 has not failed; it never got the machine. Memo keys hold across runs of one scenario (morning and evening rung 1: 163 of 202 entries equal, the rest from today's lib edits), so a resumed rung keeps its 15 results, while rung 1's results do not carry over (each rung's scenario edits differ). Guard fixes made: memo hit/miss lines now reach the report (the filter dropped '  memo'), a killed rung prints its last 5 output lines, the leftover ladder-PID-N.txt list is removed, -Rungs accepts '6:8:3,10:8:4' through pwsh -File (it used to arrive as one bogus string), and -ResumeMemoDir (one rung only) continues a rung's memo. Rule written before the next run: rung 2 runs alone (heavy job admitted at free >= 6000 MB, and no other lane's heavy job or Cells-suite job in the window, checked with eoie job list at start), resumed from target/build/sim/ladder-memo-113232-10 unless lib/spiral changed since 10-21 (then fresh); pass = exit 0 with sequential_equivalence=ok, no_stale_results=ok and commute=ok on both schedules; budget 90 min, else verdict not-finished-time and record; rung 3 (14:6:4) only after rung 2 passes. The ladder counts as recorded for X-DONE D3 once rung 2 has a pass or a real failure on a quiet machine; a contention kill is neither.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_32 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 32u32, "2026-10-22T01:40Z", "x", "2026-10-21 21:40 x: lab rule (LAB-SESSION-SWEEP): ladder.ps1 and ladder-guarded.ps1 are carried once, for rung 10:8:4. The session that runs it promotes the ladder to typed Spiral in x or prunes it; memo_timeline.ps1 is pruned (method in note 31).")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_33 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 33u32, "2026-10-22T01:46Z", "x", "2026-10-21 21:46 x: ladder scripts moved to x/target/lab-attic/2026-10-21/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM/. Before rung 10:8:4, move both back to x/lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM/ (they resolve target/build/sim relative to their own folder).")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_34 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 34u32, "2026-10-22T01:52Z", "x", "2026-10-21 21:52 x: correction to note 33: the ladder scripts are back in x/lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM (the target attic is withdrawn; target/ gets deleted for disk). Run rung 10:8:4 from there as written in note 31.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_35 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 35u32, "2026-10-22T03:08Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:08 x: rung 10:8:4 was refused at 23:08 (guard: free 3,881 MB < admit 4,500). The quiet machine sits at about 4.0 GB free and 4.0 GB available (the counters agree). Admission lowered once more, by rule: 3,800 = the 2,000 floor + this rung's observed tree peak (1,710 MB on 10-21). The floor kill stays the safety net. A floor trip is recorded as 'rung 10:8:4 exceeds this machine's quiet headroom', a real result, not a contention kill.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_36 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 36u32, "2026-10-22T03:19Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:19 x: rung 10:8:4 verdict on a quiet machine (no other job or window, no lane armed): free-floor after 600 s. Peak tree private 2,009 MB, lowest free 1,996 MB (floor 2,000); the BEAM raised system_memory_high_watermark at 23:10:51 and 23:12:54 before the guard stopped it. Started at about 3.73 GB free with 149 warm memo entries; wrote 93 more (242 now), so a later resume keeps them. By note 31's rule, written before the run, this is a real result, not a contention kill: 10 notebooks x 8 cells at concurrency 4 need more than this 16 GB machine's quiet headroom, which is about 2 GB above the floor because the other Claude sessions, Fork and RustDesk hold the rest. Ladder record for X-DONE D3: 6:8:3 PASS (1,963 s, tree peak 2,295 MB, daemon peak 3.4 GB); 10:8:4 EXCEEDS HEADROOM (>2.0 GB tree with about 1.7 GB to spare); 14:6:4 not attempted (larger than 10:8:4 on both counts). The scale result: real Cells on this box holds about 6 notebooks x 8 cells at concurrency 3; beyond that it needs a bigger machine or fewer resident VMs. What remains on the item is landing the ladder (apps/sim/build.ps1 -Ladder, written tonight, verification next) and then deleting x/lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_37 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 37u32, "2026-10-22T03:38Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:38 x: done. The ladder landed as apps/sim/build.ps1 -Ladder <notebooks:cells:concurrency,...> [-LadderMemoDir <dir>], run after the Gleam build and deps, returning before the fixture steps; README row added. Smoke 23:36 through the real build in a compile window: -Ladder 1:2:1 passed both schedules (sequential_equivalence, no_stale_results and the rest ok, commute ok; schedule 2 got its memo hit), rung wall 80 s, build 135 s; the daemon on port 13954 was stopped, no ladder list file was left. The memory guard of the old lab script belongs to eoie (EOIE-JOB-MEMORY-CAP, job start --max-private-mb, drafted for the r30-r33 release that is running now); until it ships, a ladder runs as eoie job start --heavy (admission checks free memory). Scale record (note 36): 6:8:3 passes, 10:8:4 exceeds this machine's quiet headroom. x/lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM is deleted, so x/lab no longer exists.")
inl note_of_language_name_1 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:46Z", "lane-a", "08:46 lane-a: first-pass web search (not legal clearance): no language named Espira, Spile or Geandra; near names Spira (Haskell DSL), SPIL (1973 Liskov), Spice. Spile is closest to taken names. Next: USPTO/EUIPO/WIPO + package registries. measure\\language-name-search.txt")
inl note_of_language_name_2 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 groom: unchanged; needs the user's call. P3.")
inl note_of_language_name_3 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:55Z", "main-thread", "06:55 groom: no new candidates tonight; needs a user decision session, not agent time. Kept P3.")
inl note_of_language_name_4 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:41Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:41 helper-lane lap 1: package-registry part of the clearance (read-only lookups, 2026-10-19): espira, spile and geandra are all free on crates.io, npm, hex.pm and PyPI (404 on each). On GitHub all three logins are taken by personal user accounts with 0 public repos (Espira, spile, Geandra), so a GitHub org would need a variant (e.g. espira-lang). Trademark offices (USPTO, EUIPO, WIPO) are still unchecked; they need a manual search session. Decision still the user's (votes Espira 835, Spile 790, Geandra 610), gated on SPIRAL3-EDITIONS.")
inl note_of_language_name_5 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:27Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:27 helper-lane lap 2: first trademark pass for the leading vote, Espira (web search, not a register query): no software mark named Espira surfaced; the nearest live marks are Inflectra's Spira / SpiraTest / SpiraTeam / SpiraApps (QA, test management, ALM - software, so the closest conflict risk), a separate SPIRA mark for field-ticketing software, and ISPIRER (database migration). Sources: trademarks.justia.com SPIRA 77700186, ISPIRER 88728652, inflectra.com SpiraApps. The authoritative step is still a direct search of the USPTO Trademark Search system and EUIPO eSearch for Espira in class 9/42, which needs a browser session; the user decides the name once SPIRAL3-EDITIONS parses real programs.")
inl note_of_language_name_6 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:01Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:01 helper-lane lap 3: same web pass (search engine, not a register query) for the other two candidates, so the table has three rows. Spile: no software mark surfaced; the name is taken on GitHub by zorbyte/Spile (a TypeScript Minecraft Java Edition server library, MIT; goodfirstissue.org lists it), and PLDB lists SPIL, a 1973 Liskov/Smith language one letter away; spile is also an ordinary English noun (a tap or peg), which hurts search. Geandra: no product, app or mark found at all (only unrelated GEOS, GEOMATRIX, Gehna ERP), so it is the most distinctive and searchable of the three. Table: Espira - nearest conflict Inflectra Spira/SpiraTest (QA software, class 9/42 risk); Spile - an existing open-source project name plus SPIL; Geandra - clear in this pass. All three still need a direct USPTO Trademark Search and EUIPO eSearch in class 9/42 (browser session). The name stays the user's decision.")
inl note_of_language_name_7 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 7u32, "2026-10-20T02:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 22:33 user decision 2026-10-19 21:4x: no new language name; it stays edition 3, a modified fork (the way languages like Rust name editions), so the votes are closed. Docs, posts and the compiler say Spiral edition 3.")
inl note_of_language_name_8 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 8u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:39 (subagent): blocked: three-name table complete; the choice is the user's -> next: the user's decision; then a register search for the chosen name | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): user decision -> next: the user's pick | lap 6 18:22 (subagent): re-read: still the user's call (decide later, 10-12); three-candidate table complete since lap 3 -> next: user decision | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: the user's call -> next: user decision")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_1 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 1u32, "2026-10-14T10:39Z", "user", "2026-10-14 06:39 user decisions: A4 is held with pack 2 until hopacsota (%USERPROFILE%\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\hold-until-hopacsota\\upstream_a4.ps1, annotation kept inside the join point too); its regression sample must port upstream test108's context (how upstream's harness compiles test108 with a real block in main), one sample per fixed bug")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_2 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 2u32, "2026-10-14T10:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 06:59 review: dep COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION was wrong (that waits for HOPACSOTA-P6); real state: unary deref plumbing + type-level if + lazy GADT payloads + edition gate landed and blessed; the ML lib head stops on unpublished upstream compiler work (2026-10-13 15:08); A4 held with pack 2 until hopacsota; so Paused at 60%, dep HOPACSOTA")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_3 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 3u32, "2026-10-14T12:56Z", "lane-a", "08:56 lane-a: upstream test108 verbatim (inl main() = real / let rec f x : () = f x / f () / 0i32) on the pre-A4 04:35 DLL fails with 'Recursive join points must be annotated', the error 2.17.7 fixed; annotated params give 'Cannot apply a forall'. Drafts lane-a\\repro\\a4_*.")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_4 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 4u32, "2026-10-14T14:01Z", "lane-a", "10:01 lane-a: discriminating shape = test108 with any main tail (repro\\upstream_let_rec_annotation_c): pre-A4 'Recursive join points must be annotated'; it loops forever if run, so the sample must be compile-only; terminating variants hit 'Cannot apply a forall' instead.")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_5 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 5u32, "2026-10-14T17:46Z", "lane-a", "A4 candidate: test108 verbatim now compiles (exit 0); let-rec annotation variant b and a4_countdown fail 'Cannot apply a forall with a term'.")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_6 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 6u32, "2026-10-14T17:47Z", "lane-a", "'Cannot apply a forall with a term' in real let rec with comparisons (a4_countdown, let_rec_annotation_b) also on current core: pre-existing, not an A4 regression.")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_7 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 7u32, "2026-10-14T17:49Z", "lane-a", "Forall error explained: inside real, top-down (=) is 'forall t' (core.spi:109) and real code must type-apply it; if true variant compiles. Repro misuse, not a compiler bug.")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_8 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 8u32, "2026-10-15T10:49Z", "main-thread", "06:49 groom: still paused; no syntax sync tonight. The fork's own parser changed (pack-1g macro args, pack-1e '^^'/macro continuation), so the diff against upstream grew; re-measure before resuming.")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_9 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 9u32, "2026-10-15T10:55Z", "main-thread", "06:55 groom: upstream.py's pair marker is fixed (see COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION); real drift is 5,543 lines, BlockParsing 370 section-only / 171 fork-only and Tokenize 223/55 being the syntax-relevant part. A resumed sync now has correct numbers to work from.")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_10 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 10u32, "2026-10-15T11:04Z", "main-thread", "07:04 decision (main, delegated by user 07:0x 'try deciding autonomously for things waiting for my approval'; 'we need all of them sota'): resume. A4 stops waiting for hopacsota and ships with Bend pack 2 after both cores build. upstream.py is fixed (real drift 5,543 lines), so the sync restarts from correct numbers. Status Active.")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_11 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 11u32, "2026-10-15T12:50Z", "main-thread", "08:50 A4 (join_point annotation wrap, upstream 2.17.7) installed in pack-1i, bless 082647, no non-Bend output change.")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_12 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 12u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: drift total 6,014 lines (+471 since 10-16); largest CodegenCpp, CodegenC, Infer, BlockParsing.")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_13 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 13u32, "2026-10-18T18:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:19 main-thread, resumed against mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend, language commits since merge base e4c3877. (1) 0cb0fda0 (11-14, inferencer: a recursive function type-applied through excessive shadowing): not ported until now. Our Infer.fill still had the bug: pattern recbterm x' ignored its parameter and read fill's outer rec_term. Ported in the tree (shared Infer, one copy): fill's parameter is top_rec_term, pattern takes rec_term, and fill_foralls gets top_rec_term; every other rec_term inside fill is an inner function's own parameter, so the rename is exact. Unbuilt; rides the pack after pack 3, since review and accept must stay on the 14:04 install. (2) faaebec5 (11-05, existential type vars in the prepass): not needed. Upstream's repro test111 (dict k v = exists. k * v, typecase through the existential) compiles on our installed compiler and prints {x : i32} and {y : f64}, the correct application. Upstream's bug came with its 2.18 TNominalTypeVarPlaceholder, which the fork never took; our TPrepass keeps the nullary TExists. Next: dd99e8ca (@ globals in type-level macros) and the 11-04 nominals redesign.")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_14 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 14u32, "2026-10-19T12:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:25 lap 1 grain: status: the 0cb0fda0 Infer fill port (recursive function type-applied through shadowing) shipped in compiler pack 4, accepted 2026-10-18 (single-flight-20261018-234436, DISAGREE 0); the pending regression draft upstream_let_rec_annotation moved from job tmp into spiral/lab/UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC (still rejected by both compilers at main: it needs upstream test108's typed-main context) Next: list the host_cpp_and_cuda_backend language commits after 0cb0fda0 (upstream.py export --since 0cb0fda0) and port the next one")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_15 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 15u32, "2026-10-19T13:01Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:01 lap 2 grain: the language port is complete up to the branch head: git log of mrakgr/host_cpp_and_cuda_backend over The Spiral Language 2 shows 0cb0fda0 (2025-11-14, the Infer fill fix shipped in pack 4) as the last commit, preceded by 23f110e1 (Cuda header type order, CodegenCpp only) and the 2.18.6/2.18.7 prereleases; nothing newer to port Next: decide whether 23f110e1 (Cuda header ordering) matters for the fork's C/Cpp backends; else move the item to Done once the regression draft (lab/UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC) passes or is dropped")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_16 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 16u32, "2026-10-19T18:23Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:23 lap 4 grain: the last open upstream commit is already ported: 23f110e1 (2.18.9, Cuda header types grouped so nvcc sees them outside #ifdef __CUDA_ARCH__, backend_group() in CodegenCpp) appears in spiral_compiler.fs (backend_group 9 hits, same mapping CudaDevice -> CudaHost); with 0cb0fda0 shipped in pack 4 and the core switch to corebase (core v1) accepted today, the compiler and core both match upstream head Next: list what scope is left beyond the compiler and core (Spiral-s-ML-Library syntax used in corebase vs our edition 3 parser), or close")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_17 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 17u32, "2026-10-19T18:24Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:24 main lap 4: closed. Every part of the scope is in: the host_cpp_and_cuda_backend port through its head (0cb0fda0 shipped in pack 4, 23f110e1 backend_group present), unary deref and nullary forall (lap 2), --default-int and --default-float exist as Argu flags with SPIRAL_DEFAULT_INT/FLOAT env fallbacks on every compile path (spiral_compiler.fs 7258-7302), and upstream corebase now compiles as our core v1 (accepted 11:31 and 12:56). The one leftover, an edition-aware # inside macro text so upstream # type: ignore keeps compiling, belongs to SPIRAL3-EDITIONS (note 14 sizes it at 11 lib lines).")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_rules_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-RULES", 1u32, "2026-10-15T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:00 grooming: status was stale (Planned 0%). Verified now: 24 .spi modules in apps/tibia, 69 tests pass memo off on pack-1h (06:15). Left from the original list: loot conservation tests, death penalty, ranged creatures.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_rules_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-RULES", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:33Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 13:33 loot conservation done: hunt.spi test loot_conserves_gold_and_pays_in_fewest_coins_over_1600_purses_and_bounties (C oracle) loops 1600 purses (gold, platinum, up to 6 crystal) against bounties 0..996. A kill must add exactly the bounty to the purse's gold value and pay it back in fewest coins (gold and platinum each under 100); a survivor must leave the purse value unchanged. Result: 0 broken, hunt 5/5 through scripts/build.ps1. Left from the original list: death penalty (level and skill loss, gear drop without blessings, both seen live) and ranged creatures (no arena creature uses ranged attacks yet).")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_rules_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-RULES", 3u32, "2026-10-18T17:36Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 13:36 death penalty, experience part: new module src/experience.spi (registered in package.spiproj after vitality). experience_for_level is Canary's Player::getExpForLevel ((((L-6)L+17)L-12)/6*100). unblessed_death_loss_below_level_24 = experience / 10, Canary's getLostPercent for level < 24, no blessings, unpromoted, deathLosePercent -1 as in tibia-local config.lua; floor(experience * 0.1) in double equals experience / 10 for every integer. after_unblessed_death applies the loss and Canary's level-down loop (while level > 1 and experience < experience_for_level level). Tests: the exp table against the formula (levels 1, 2, 6, 7, 8, 10, 24, 100), Knight Sample level 8 at 4200 dies to level 7 at 3780 (the live 'deaths cost a level'), a mid-level death that keeps its level, and a WGSL twin; experience 4/4. Left: skill-try loss (needs vocation skill-try tables), the level >= 24 double formula, gear drop without blessings, and ranged creatures.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_rules_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-RULES", 4u32, "2026-10-18T18:23Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 14:23 three more rules, all from Canary source and data, C oracle + WGSL twin, gate green. (1) experience.spi fix: the loss is ceil(experience * 0.1) (player.cpp 4031), which equals (experience + 9) / 10 for every experience up to 50,000,000 (checked exhaustively); vocations at level <= 7 lose no experience (level > 7 guard); tests now include 4205 -> 3784 (level 7) and level 7 kept (experience 5/5). (2) skills.spi: knight_skill_tries_up_to_130 is Canary's getReqSkillTries table, uint64(50 * pow((double)1.1f, level - 11)), generated as a binary-search let chain like terrain's masks. The fractional parts never come within 1.2e-6 of an integer, so libm differences cannot flip it. after_unblessed_death is Canary's skill loss loop (sum of tries / 10, walking levels down, floor at 10). Pinned against a C# replica of the loop: axe 30 with 0 tries -> 29 with 20 tries (the live 'a skill point'), 60/1234 -> 59/629, 11/0 -> 10/45, 130/0 -> 128/3409614; skills 4/4. (3) blessings.spi: Blessings.DropLoot from data/libs/systems/blessing.lua: per slot, a container drops at chance x 100 out of 10000, any other item at a tenth of that; 0 blessings: backpack always and each other item 1000/10000; blessing table 70/45/25/10/0; Amulet of Loss none; red or black skull everything (blessings 3/3). (4) combat.spi: ranged attacks (hunter arrow from data-canary hunter.lua: 50% every 2000 ms, 0..100 physical, range 7). Canary's distance spells set BLOCKARMOR but not BLOCKSHIELD, so arrows skip the shield, yet blockHit still spends a block charge because it decrements whenever armor or defense is checked; pinned over 4000 arrows (equal to an unshielded melee roll, one charge spent each, 0 stays 0); reach needs distance <= 7 and clear sight (Monster::canUseAttack); combat 9/9. Left: wiring a ranged creature into the simulator (keep-distance movement from Monster::getDistanceStep) and the level >= 24 death formula.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_rules_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-RULES", 5u32, "2026-10-18T18:46Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 14:46 ranged creature wired into the simulator (uncommitted; existing camps unaffected, gate running): creatures.Hunter (data-canary hunter.lua: 150 hp, speed 105, step 600 ms, targetDistance 4, melee 0..20, armor/defense 15, runHealth 10) with is_ranged / ranged_attack_of; creatures.spi gained its missing '///- --package .' line (it now needs combat). hunting_pack: a ranged foe keeps its target distance (steps away with terrain.step_away while closer and in sight) and every 2000 ms gets an attack round: melee if adjacent, plus an arrow roll when in range 7 with clear sight (geometry.sight_line_clear over the layout's walls); the arrow uses combat.ranged_hit_spending_a_charge. Finding from a probe (lab/tibia-potion-fidelity/hunter_chase_probe.spi): on the unbounded open field the level-8 knight (~700 ms per tile) never catches a hunter (600 ms steps), which re-opens to 4 tiles whenever the knight reaches 3; 60 s: 0 kills, 28 arrow hits, 28 potions. On ArenaSpot the knight reaches a hunter but gets no kill in 60 s, then the run resets. Not pinned: how fast a live knight catches hunters, and the dance and cornering from Monster::getDistanceStep, need a live reference first (exploratory hunter runs planned in today's live block).")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_rules_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-RULES", 6u32, "2026-10-18T19:27Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 15:27 WGSL cost of the ranged wiring, measured on gpu_sim (local NVIDIA, cold): with the ranged branch compiled into every simulator shader, the three shaders grew from 1.25/2.36/1.41 MB (this morning's CI) to 1.52/2.90/1.69 MB, and the largest's cold compile went to 124 s. Fix: a camp-level static flag (camp.ranged, set by uniform_camp / the mixed-camp builder / the hunting-ground camp from creatures.is_ranged) gates both ranged branches. For camps built from literal kinds, Spiral folds it away, and the shaders are back to 1.26/2.38/1.42 MB (largest compiles in 80 s); gpu_sim 6/6. Same lesson as TIBIA-WGSL-SHADER-SIZE: a rule only some scenarios use should hang off a static camp property, so GPU shaders of other scenarios don't pay for it.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_rules_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-RULES", 7u32, "2026-10-18T19:32Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 15:32 source for the hunter-chase gap: Monster::getDistanceStep keeps a counter (monster.cpp 1748-1755): when the target is within 1 tile on both axes, stepDuration++ (max 2), otherwise stepDuration-- (min 0). Monster::isTargetNearby() is stepDuration >= 1, and Creature::getStepDuration multiplies a straight step by WALK_TARGET_NEARBY_EXTRA_COST = 2 while the monster is not fleeing and not a summon (diagonals pay WALK_DIAGONAL_EXTRA_COST = 3 instead). So a hunter the knight has reached steps at about 1200 ms and can be held, while the model's hunter keeps 600 ms and escapes. Melee monsters return before the counter (distance == targetDistance), which matches the live cyclops 'no x2 after contact'. Next: a per-foe nearby counter (0..2) updated at each ranged step decision, doubling the straight step cost while >= 1, behind the static camp.ranged flag.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_rules_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-RULES", 8u32, "2026-10-18T20:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 16:54 ranged creature rule completed in the model: (1) Canary's keep-distance slow-down (Monster::getDistanceStep counts 'target within 1 tile' up to 2, down to 0; while >= 1 a straight step costs x2, WALK_TARGET_NEARBY_EXTRA_COST; diagonal x3 as always; only in the keep-distance branch, which is why melee cyclops chase steps show no x2 live) as foe field nearby + target_nearby_step_factor. (2) bug found by the lab probe: hardest_kind treated only Cyclops as tough, so a hunter camp fell back to the Troll offense (level 8, axe 30, 30-60 hp potions) and the new Hunter offense row was dead; hardest_kind now picks the camp kind with the highest offense level from offense_against itself, so the table and the picker cannot drift again (all other camps get the same offense as before). Pinned: pack_sim a_hunter_that_keeps_four_tiles_away_only_falls_where_walls_corner_it_and_walking_next_to_the_knight_halves_its_pace (5 seeds x 120 s, 3 hunters: open field 0 kills / 286 hits taken, arena spot 15 kills (all) / 121 hits). Lab: lab/tibia-potion-fidelity hunter_chase_probe.spi, hunter_adjacency_probe.spi. Model-only claim, no live hunter runs yet.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_rules_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-RULES", 9u32, "2026-10-18T21:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 17:17 gates green after the hunter rule and the hardest_kind fix: pack_sim 30/30 (every earlier pin unchanged), creatures 8/8, hunt_sim 11/11, gpu_sim 6/6. WGSL cost: shaders 1.26/2.38/1.42 MB -> 1.30/2.47/1.47 MB (+3.5%), most likely the foe record's new nearby field, which every foe copy carries even though its logic sits behind camp.ranged; the largest compiled cold in 94 s vs 80 s, measured while the compiler lane's heavy gate ran, so not comparable. Accepted. Scope of the title is covered: grid and floors (up_ramp), creatures melee/ranged/flee, cooldown groups, HP/mana, potions, loot (blessing drops), death penalty (experience, skills, gear). Live validation of the ranged rules continues as PR-13 under TIBIA-FIDELITY.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_rules_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-RULES", 10u32, "2026-10-22T16:58Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:58 Moved from docs/MODELING.md (TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE, rule DC1); corrected 13:15 after listing every 'test ... fails' block (the first version grepped function names and missed pins named by guarantee). (1) Type guarantees pinned by compile-fail tests: quantity (hitpoints_and_milliseconds_do_not_mix, gold_is_not_hitpoints), vitality (healing_the_fallen_does_not_compile), catalog (a_troll_spawn_cannot_hold_a_cyclops), readiness (three), views (a_view_without_mapped_is_rejected), network (an_output_is_not_a_feature, inputs_are_not_weights), vocations (two), policy (strike_is_not_a_bool), pack_sim (unit objective kills_and_gold_do_not_add, role-indexed seed blocks, the private-constructor pins, millisecond health). (2) Type guarantees with no pin yet, candidates for compile-fail tests: the brain union (a tactic that is neither rules nor a network), the hunter record nested in pack (a knight change outside with_hunter/hunter_steps), the patrol union, the route_choice union (no diagonal or multi-tile router step). (3) Runtime invariants that need property tests rather than compile-fail ones: occupancy (two creatures on one tile, steps through walls), live-only camp unions moving no pin unless opted in, the static ranged flag (melee camps compile no ranged branch: +23 percent WGSL, 124 s vs 80 s cold), hardest_kind reaching every creature row. (4) Planned types not built: a conditions union (burning, poisoned, paralyzed), a character phase typestate idle -> hunting -> looting -> depositing composed like the ERP plans, loot and trades as mirror pairs, negative packages for an item in two containers and mana below zero. (5) Optional proofs: rule invariants as Bend 2 or Lean theorems for the pure rules, adopted only if a proof catches a bug the bit-exact diffs miss; unchecked whether Spiral's Bend backend can emit LAWS.bend or PROOF.bend.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_rules_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-RULES", 11u32, "2026-10-22T17:42Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:42 Three of note 10's unpinned type guarantees drafted as compile-fail pins for pack_sim.spi (scratch typed-pins.spi, the 'test ... fails' form of the existing pins): a_compass_direction_is_not_a_router_step (terrain.route_offset on geometry NorthEast: route_choice has only the four orthogonal cases, so the router can't emit a diagonal), raw_weights_are_not_a_tactic_brain (network weights without the Network case), a_patrol_route_is_not_a_patrol_without_its_case (a patrol_route without Patrols). The hunter-in-pack row from note 10 is NOT a type guarantee (record update is structural, so { p with hunter = ... } compiles); it moves to the runtime-invariant list. They land with the next src change that runs Cells on pack_sim.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_rules_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-RULES", 12u32, "2026-10-22T17:44Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:44 The three pins of note 11 appended to src/pack_sim.spi (pack_sim is test-only, not in src/package.spiproj's modules, so the live policy compile can't see them; backup of the prior file in scratch). Check queued as job tibia-typed-pins (scripts/build.ps1 -Files pack_sim) at the end of today's compile chain; if any pin's expected text does not match, the pins come out again before anything else lands.")
inl note_of_tibia_typed_rules_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-TYPED-RULES", 13u32, "2026-10-22T19:31Z", "tibia-lane", "The three compile-fail pins of note 12 (router step not a compass direction, raw weights not a brain, route not a patrol) pass under Cells: job tibia-typed-pins (15:19-15:25, scripts/build.ps1 -Files pack_sim) discovered and executed 39, passed 39, failed 0, so each pin fails to compile with its expected message and the rest of pack_sim is unchanged. They stay in src/pack_sim.spi (unstaged).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 1u32, "2026-10-15T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:00 grooming: its dependency moved: POTION-FIDELITY is at 70% after PC-3 confirmed the swing-delay mechanic; the agent stays paused until the model ranks retreat vs rules like live.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 2u32, "2026-10-18T05:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 01:41 1k-line rule: tibia ships no binaries today (tests only, plus generated Lua: policy 619 lines, the first arena probe 228), so x's gate does not apply yet. The trainer this item builds is the first tibia binary: build it as an x-style crate (apps/<app>/<crate>, [package.metadata.spiral], gate.ps1 at 1000 generated Rust lines).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 3u32, "2026-10-21T20:45Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 16:45 Grooming: the 2026-10-15 resume condition (the model ranks retreat vs rules like live) is met out of sample: PR-17 live 13.25 vs 13.90 potions per clear, all3/all4 13.11 vs 13.94 (t 0.24 / -0.05); the hunter arena holds for all4 (PR-19). Still paused: heavy (3600 m, pair), and it should train on the bundle adopted 2026-10-22 (all4 or pace), after POLICY-FEATURES has searched simple behaviors first (cheaper, same question: what changes live exposure). Resuming is the user's go (decisions are not a go).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 4u32, "2026-10-22T01:38Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 21:38 Resume decided by rule F5 (TIBIA note 23), with no user go needed. It resumes, Active and scheduled by WSJF, once (1) the pace bundle is adopted with build.ps1 green, (2) TIBIA-POLICY-FEATURES has scored its simple-behavior candidates on that bundle, and (3) the hunter gap is closed or Paused with a resume condition. Promotion stays as in the title. Supersedes note 3's 'resuming is the user's go'.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 5u32, "2026-10-22T07:35Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 03:35 Resume condition (2) of note 4 met: TIBIA-POLICY-FEATURES scored its simple behaviors on the adopted bundle (PF1: best -9.5%, none qualifies). (1) is met (pace adopted, build green, EVENT-TIME note 58). (3) is pending: the hunter gap is at D29's P29 plus PR-21, then Done or Paused.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 6u32, "2026-10-22T16:58Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:58 Moved from docs/MODELING.md (TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE, rule DC1), the one graph-upgrade claim not in this description: a game-to-tensor pickler that encodes the typed per-agent view (only tiles on the agent's screen), so the input layout is checked by types. Not ported from his library: CFR tables, corecuda primitives, heap closures on the device.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 7u32, "2026-10-22T17:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:00 Moved from docs/PRIOR_ART.md (TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE, rule DC1), why the source of this graph code failed at poker (github.com/mrakgr/Spiral-s-ML-Library, 2024-05 to 2025-12; ml/, game/, hd/, corebase/ unchanged since d352f3c): the models never learned hold'em (CFR hashed states through an untrained projection into 4096 buckets; CEM evolved one linear layer with population 4; backprop never wired into training; commit 63ed6ed 2024-10-14 'It's a failure.'); no search at play time; outcome-sampling variance over long histories; no benchmark (random players and a calling station, never exploitability or Slumbot); every layer new at once; one consumer GPU. What worked: GPU-resident games with unions and closures, the typed staged graph with memory planning, deterministic multi-block kernels, tabular CFR on Leduc. Lesson kept for this item: benchmark from day one, one novelty at a time, populations and methods sized to the problem.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 8u32, "2026-10-22T17:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:03 RESUMED by rule F5 (note 4): (1) pace adopted with build green, (2) POLICY-FEATURES scored its simple behaviors (PF1), (3) the hunter gap is Paused with a resume condition (TIBIA-FIDELITY note 108). Active, scheduled by WSJF. First steps are CPU-light: locate the graph code locally, then write the agent's rule (scenario, objective, baselines, budget, promotion) before any training code.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 9u32, "2026-10-22T17:04Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:04 RULE A1, written before any agent code. Source: local clone C:\\home\\git\\Spiral-s-ML-Library at 80d6a1a (ml/layers.spi 669 lines, cem.spi 190, models.spi 105, primitives.spi 1139; all open corecuda, and primitives holds the CUDA kernels, 33 CUDA references), so porting means re-hosting primitives on our C and WGSL backends, not copying. Benchmark from day one: PF1's arena-spot clear (adopted bundle, 3 cyclopses, CheapClearWithin with L = 1.1 x the rules clear time, training block for search, 100 held-out clears for judging). Milestones, one novelty each, each judged on the held-out block before the next starts: M1 port only the dense layer, the staged graph's memory plan and CEM onto C (no CUDA), then train the existing network.spi topology (8-6-3, Q8 inputs) with CEM at population 64 and pass when held-out potions are within 5 percent of network.spi's own neuroevolution result on the same seeds; M2 swap in the typed per-agent view as input (the pickler, note 6) with the same CEM, pass when it is no worse than M1; M3 sizes as type indices and the raw/planned/frozen typestate (the mega-sample upgrades), pass when M2's numbers reproduce bit-exact. Promotion (title): at least 15 percent fewer potions than min(rules 13.94, retreat 13.32, best simple behavior 12.06) on held-out within the time cap, deaths no higher, a second independent held-out window reproducing it, and a random control at chance. Budget: CPU only on this laptop, at most one compile-class job at a time and no job inside a live block; rented GPU hours only through a TIBIA-GPU-RENTAL tranche approved by the user. Stop: M1 failing twice after a traced fix, or promotion missed at M3, writes a stop note here (the PF1 and 10-13 saturation findings say tuned rules may leave nothing to learn on this scenario).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 10u32, "2026-10-22T17:05Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:05 M1 design from reading ml/cem.spi (190 lines): the algorithm is small once the CUDA plumbing (grid syncs, transposes, atomic adds) is set aside: ensemble members at or above the average reward are the elite (the mask), and every non-elite member's weights are resampled per weight from N(elite mean, max(elite std, 0.1)), std Bessel-corrected, weight decay 1. So M1 needs none of primitives.spi: it is a faithful port of that update over network.spi weights. Drafted in scratch (agent-m1/search-cem.spi) for src/search.spi, integer-only like the module (search is one of the integer-only modules): evolve_cem over a flat population array, elite by score * population >= total, integer elite mean and Bessel std via integer_sqrt, a normal draw from 12 summed uniforms (Irwin-Hall, sd about 1000 milli), a per-gene noise floor in per mille of the gene range (his 0.1), best-ever tracking. Two C test blocks: climbs the same known peak as evolve_vector, and integer_sqrt plus normal_milli centering. Compile waits for a free slot after the live block.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 11u32, "2026-10-22T17:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:07 M1 draft checked in a lua 5.1 simulation of the same integer algorithm before any compile (scratch agent-m1/cem-sim.lua). First draft: 0 of 50 seeds reached the test bar, because a 10 per mille floor (2 of a 200 range) and a +-8 start let the elite std collapse before the mean traveled. The noise floor is now set by mapping, not tuning: his 0.1 on unit-scale weights (range 2) is 50 per mille of the range; start spread 250 per mille. Sweep for the record: floor 50/start 250 passes 47 of 50 at 20 generations and 198 of 200 at 30 (floor 100 passes 7 of 50, too noisy to converge). The draft now takes floor_per_mille and start_per_mille, and its test runs 30 generations at 50/250.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 12u32, "2026-10-22T17:12Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:12 A1 addendum, written before any M1 run: M1 compares at equal evaluation budgets, about PF1's (480): CEM population 64 for 8 generations (floor 50, start 250 per mille of the weight range -2048..2048) against network.evolve with 64 offspring for 8 generations, spread 256, both cold from network.zero_weights, search seed 1, fitness worth (CheapClearWithin L) on the training block exactly as PF1, judged on the 100 held-out clears (potions, clear time, deaths). The reference is network.evolve's held-out potions from the SAME job. Pass: CEM within 5 percent of it (or better) within the time cap. One job runs both (scratch agent-m1/m1.ps1).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 13u32, "2026-10-22T17:13Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:13 M1 harness drafted (scratch agent-m1/m1.ps1, parses): a work copy of src with evolve_cem appended to search.spi; a predict main that reports rules, then network.evolve (8 x 64, spread 256) and evolve_cem (pop 64, 8 generations, floor 50, start 250 over -2048..2048), both cold from zero weights on PF1's CheapClearWithin objective, held-out report per arm; as_weights copies a search.vector into network.weights gene by gene. Runs as one compile job after the live block.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 14u32, "2026-10-22T17:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:17 M2 design (one novelty: the inputs), from reading hunting_pack.features: today's 8 inputs are scalar summaries (own health fraction, attackers, the current target's health, distance and fleeing, heal and strike readiness, bias); nothing says where foes stand or which tiles are free, which is what the retreat rules exploit. M2's typed per-agent view: a record built only from what the client screen shows (15x11, the parity layer's spectator limit), so the type itself rules out server state: the 8 current features, plus up to 3 visible foes sorted by threat_rank (dx, dy, health fraction each, zeros for absent slots), plus the 8 neighbor tiles' walkability (blocked or occupied as 1): 8 + 9 + 8 = 25 inputs, hidden 6, outputs 3, so 168 genes for the M1 CEM unchanged. Pass (A1): held-out potions no worse than M1's network. The view type goes in views.spi or a new view module with a compile-fail pin (a view field that reads a foe beyond the screen does not type-check); network.spi gains the input count as a parameter of the weights nominal instead of the fixed 8.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 15u32, "2026-10-22T17:19Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:19 M2 view drafted (scratch agent-m1/m2-view.spi, for views.spi): a union private sighting (Seen glimpse | Unseen) whose only constructor path is sighted here at health, which yields Seen only on the same floor within 7 tiles across and 5 down (the 15x11 client screen), plus glimpse_or_zero for encoding and an agent_view record (trio of sightings, 8 neighbor blocked flags). C test: the screen edge (dx 7, dy 5) is seen; dx 8, dy 6 and another floor are not. The forging pin (views.Seen used from outside, expected 'The module does not have the key: Seen') must sit in pack_sim.spi like the existing AttackReady and HeldOutBlock pins, since a module can use its own private constructors.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 16u32, "2026-10-22T18:02Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 14:02 M2 network change, designed from network.spi (decide, hidden_unit, output_unit read): keep every existing pin bit-exact by adding, not changing. hidden_unit and output_unit become hidden_unit_of count and output_unit_of count, and the current 8-input functions call them with input_count (), so the same arithmetic in the same order. A new nominal wide_inputs (array i32, 25 slots) carries the view (8 current features, then per sighted foe dx * 32, dy * 32, health fraction, absent foes as zeros, then the 8 neighbor blocked flags as one ()), plus decide_wide over weights sized 25 * 6 + 6 * 3 = 168. A distinct nominal keeps the 8-input and 25-input weights from mixing (a compile-fail pin: an 8-input weights value passed to decide_wide). The Q8 scale matches the existing features (one () = 256; distance already uses * 32 per tile).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 17u32, "2026-10-22T18:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 14:03 M2 network drafted (scratch agent-m1/m2-network.spi, for network.spi): hidden_unit_of, output_unit_of and decide_of over a count; decide keeps its signature and calls decide_of (input_count ()) with the same summation order, division by one () and ReLU, so the 8-input pins stay bit-exact; wide_weights and wide_inputs nominals; wide_inputs_of copies the 8 base features, 9 foe slots (dx, dy, health per foe) and 8 blocked flags; decide_wide. Tests: the_eight_input_path_is_unchanged (the threshold healer still heals at health 40 of 515 under 160), and the compile-fail pin eight_input_weights_do_not_drive_the_wide_network. Lands after M1 passes (one novelty at a time).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_18 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 18u32, "2026-10-22T18:04Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 14:04 M2 feature builder drafted (scratch agent-m1/m2-features.spi, for hunting_pack.spi): wide_features camp attackers now p = the 8 base features, then up to 3 sighted foes (views.sighted from the knight, so only screen tiles) picked in threat_rank order (x * 100000 + y, the live competitor's deterministic threat rank) as dx * 32, dy * 32, health fraction, and the 8 neighbor tiles blocked (terrain.free on the layout with every foe as occupancy, the same check the wander code uses). Integer-only like the module. With m2-view.spi and m2-network.spi this is M2's full code; it lands only after M1 passes.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_19 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 19u32, "2026-10-22T19:04Z", "tibia-lane", "M2 harness drafted (scratch agent-m1/m2-patch.ps1 and m2.ps1, both parse; the patch dry-runs cleanly on a scratch copy of src). The patch reuses what src already has instead of the draft helpers: threat_rank (hunting_pack) for the foe order, terrain.neighbor for the 8 tiles, taken_by_foes (foes plus the ally, the knight own step check) for blocked. It adds the WideNetwork brain arm, and the Network arm of after_heal_plan now calls a shared network_plan (same engagement code, so the 8-input path is unchanged); the forge pin goes to pack_sim. m2.ps1 runs CEM on the 8-input network as the reference and CEM on the 25-input view at the same budget (pop 64, 8 generations, floor 50, start 250), held-out report per arm; pass per A1 is wide no worse than narrow. Queued only after M1 passes.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_20 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 20u32, "2026-10-22T19:18Z", "tibia-lane", "M3 design (no code until M2 passes; read from the compiler samples contract_typestate_builder and nominal_phantom_parameter). Typestate: phantom states raw, planned and frozen as empty nominals, and nominal graph state = array i32 indexed by them; the only constructors are plan (raw -> planned: lays out the weights and the scratch for the hidden layer once, the memory plan of mrakgr staged graph) and freeze (planned -> frozen). decide takes only graph frozen, and evolve_cem mutates only graph planned, so a network cannot be read mid-mutation or mutated after promotion; a compile-fail pin for each wrong order goes in pack_sim. Sizes: the input count stops being a runtime argument and becomes a phantom index of the weights nominal (eight vs wide replaces wide_weights as a separate nominal), so decide_of picks the count from the index and mixing them fails at type check, the same guarantee M2 gets from two nominals but with one decide. Binary type-level naturals are needed only if a third size appears; with two sizes, two index types suffice (one novelty at a time). Pass per A1: M2 numbers reproduce bit-exact, since only types change.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_21 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 21u32, "2026-10-22T19:39Z", "tibia-lane", "M1 first run (job tibia-agent-m1, 15:2x) failed at compile, a draft bug, not a CEM finding: the generation counter was named index, which shadows the array index function inside evolve_cem (Unification failure: Got i32, Expected array i32 -> i32 -> i32). Renamed to round in scratch search-cem.spi (3 sites); the remaining index calls are the array function. Requeued 15:38 behind the duo job and the lib gate. Counts as no M1 attempt under the stop rule (the harness never ran).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_22 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 22u32, "2026-10-22T21:22Z", "tibia-lane", "M1 second run (job tibia-agent-m1, queued 15:38, compiled clean after the index fix, ran about 85 min of CEM and network.evolve at BelowNormal beside two heavy jobs): wall-clock timeout at 104 min with no result line, because the C binary buffers stdout into the pipe until exit. Not an M1 attempt under rule A1 stop rule (the method never reported). Fixes for the rerun: m1.ps1 (and m2.ps1) flush stdout after the limit line and after each arm report, so a cut-off run keeps every finished arm; cap 3 h. Requeued at x retry end (about 18:20) as its own job beside the short tibia line (a single C process, little memory).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_23 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 23u32, "2026-10-22T22:38Z", "tibia-lane", "M1 run 3 interim (job tibia-agent-m1, started 17:46, BelowNormal, flushed output; src with F34): rules 13.94 potions per held-out clear, 87.7 s, 3 deaths (reproduces PF1 exactly, so the harness and objective are the same as PF1); limit L 94934 ms; network.evolve reference arm (64 offspring x 8 generations, spread 256, cold from zero weights): 13.47 potions, 87.9 s, 0 deaths. The CEM arm (pop 64, 8 generations, floor 50, start 250) is running; rule A1 passes M1 if CEM held-out potions are at most 13.47 x 1.05 = 14.14 with clear time within L. Judge: scratch agent-m1/m1-judge.ps1.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_24 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 24u32, "2026-10-22T22:38Z", "tibia-lane", "M2 queued conditionally (job tibia-agent-m2-if-m1, after M1, BelowNormal, 3 h cap): scratch agent-m1/m2-if-m1.ps1 runs the M1 judge (rule A1) and starts m2.ps1 (CEM on the 8-input network as the reference, then CEM on the 25-input typed view, same budget) only if the verdict is M1 PASSES; otherwise it stops with that reason. One novelty at a time stays enforced in the script, not by hand.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_25 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 25u32, "2026-10-22T22:39Z", "tibia-lane", "Judge interpretation, written before any M1 CEM or M2 result: M1 passes when CEM held-out potions are at most 1.05 x network.evolve and its clear time is within L (A1 addendum). M2 passes when the wide-view CEM has held-out potions no higher than the 8-input CEM, clear time within L and deaths no higher (A1 says no worse than M1; time and deaths are read from the promotion criteria, deaths no higher, so a wide network that buys potions with time or deaths does not pass). Scripts: scratch agent-m1/m1-judge.ps1 and m2-judge.ps1.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_26 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 26u32, "2026-10-22T22:45Z", "tibia-lane", "Matrix block (main, 18:43-19:2x): queued job tibia-agent-m2-if-m1 stopped on request; M1 (tibia-agent-m1) kept running with main's OK. After matrix end the chain was requeued (job-1792711322219-tibia-agent-m2-if-m1, after M1 in any state): it runs m1-judge and starts M2 only if it prints M1 PASSES (rule A1, note 25 unchanged; judge fixed per note 27).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_27 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 27u32, "2026-10-22T23:18Z", "tibia-lane", "Judge fix before any verdict (19:2x, rule A1 note 25 unchanged): the M1 harness prints each arm row with printf %s of a Spiral string, which on the C backend passes the string struct, so the name column comes out as byte 0x01 (the refcount) instead of rules or network_evolve. m1-judge and m2-judge matched names with ^(\\w*)\\t, which a control byte fails, so a dry run on the partial output found no arm at all; had M1 finished, m2-if-m1 would have read M1 did not pass whatever CEM scored. Both judges now take any non-tab name and fall back to the fixed arm order (m1: rules, network_evolve, network_cem; m2: rules, network_cem, wide_cem; both match the report calls). Dry run now reads rules 13.94 / 87716 ms / 3 deaths and network_evolve 13.47 / 87940 ms / 0 deaths, CEM still running.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_28 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 28u32, "2026-10-23T00:15Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE A1-T, written 20:1x before M1 ends, corrected 20:2x before it ends (rule A1 note 25 covers a finished CEM arm only): if tibia-agent-m1 hits its 3 h cap without printing the network_cem row, M1 is INCONCLUSIVE, not failed: no M2 (m2-if-m1 prints no verdict and stops, as built), and A1's two-failure stop count does not advance. Budget fact for the retry: both arms already make the same number of fitness calls (network.evolve 8 generations x 64 offspring = 512, src/network.spi:162; CEM 8 x 64 = 512, m1.ps1), yet network.evolve finished inside the first hour and CEM has run over 90 minutes, so the cost is per call, not the count. Likely cause, untested: CEM's start spread is 250 per mille of the 4096 range, about 1024, against network.evolve's mutation spread 256, and wide random networks can make slow, stalling clears. Next attempt: a one-generation timing run at the current settings (per-call time), then, if it is several times network.evolve's, the same CEM with start_per_mille 62 (the 256 spread of network.evolve), 8 x 64 calls. If M1 finishes, A1 applies unchanged; if it passes, M2 starts automatically and may run past this session (job tibia-agent-m2-if-m1, BelowNormal, 4 h cap), its verdict by m2-judge next session.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_29 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 29u32, "2026-10-23T00:40Z", "tibia-lane", "Timing run for rule A1-T stopped by me at 20:4x without a result (job tibia-agent-m1-timing): its first network.evolve generation (64 calls) was still running after 12 minutes, slower than the full M1 network.evolve arm managed, because the machine was at 100 percent CPU (M1 itself plus main's overnight heavy and compile jobs). A per-call timing under that load says nothing about CEM's cost, and the run only took CPU from the others. The A1-T timing must run on a quiet machine: one job, nothing heavy beside it. Scratch agent-m1/m1-timing.ps1 is ready (one generation each of network.evolve, CEM at start 250 and CEM at start 62, each line timestamped).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_30 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 30u32, "2026-10-23T00:47Z", "tibia-lane", "M1 run 3 INCONCLUSIVE by rule A1-T (note 28): job tibia-agent-m1 hit its 3 h wall-clock cap at 20:46 (timed-out, exit 124) after the rules row (13.94 potions per clear, 87716 ms, 3 deaths) and the network_evolve row (13.47, 87940 ms, 0 deaths), with no network_cem row. No M2 (the requeued chain job-1792711322219-tibia-agent-m2-if-m1 runs m1-judge, which has no CEM arm and so no verdict). A1's two-failure count does not advance. Next session, per A1-T: the one-generation timing run (scratch agent-m1/m1-timing.ps1) on a quiet machine, then the CEM rerun at start_per_mille 62 if CEM's per-call cost is several times network.evolve's.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_31 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 31u32, "2026-10-23T03:23Z", "tibia-lane", "Prepared for rule A1-T (note 28), 23:3x, not run: scratch agent-m1/m1-narrow.ps1, the same M1 with CEM start_per_mille 62 (the 256 spread of network.evolve), its own work dir target/predict/agent-m1-narrow and output m1-narrow-out.txt; m1-judge.ps1 now takes -Out <file> (default m1-out.txt, checked on the existing output). Order stays: the one-generation timing run (m1-timing.ps1) on a quiet machine first, then m1-narrow only if CEM's per-call cost is several times network.evolve's.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_32 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 32u32, "2026-10-23T03:29Z", "tibia-lane", "Overflow in the CEM port found by review (23:4x): CheapClearWithin scores a clear slower than the limit as below_the_kill_floor () - clear_ms, about -1,000,000,000 (hunting_pack.spi:1412, :1439), and search-cem.spi selected elites by score * population >= total in i32, so a floor-level member overflows both the product and the 64-member total (C wraps them). Fixed in scratch agent-m1/search-cem.spi (total and the elite product in i64) with a new test, evolve_cem_climbs_when_most_members_score_at_the_floor. CORRECTED 23:4x after the check job (tibia-cem-floor-check, scratch cem-floor-check.ps1: a 4-gene peak with a -1e9 floor, 30 generations of 32, fixed code vs the old i32 code): fixed best loss -7, old best loss -13, both pass the -40 bar. So in this setting the wrapped comparisons still pick mostly the right elites; the overflow is real but the test does not separate the two, and my earlier reading (elite selection effectively random, a likely cause of the slow M1 CEM arm) is not supported. The fix stays (correct arithmetic), the test stays as a climb check but is not a regression test for the overflow; whether the overflow hurt M1 run 3 is unknown. The next M1 attempt uses the fixed code at the registered start 250.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_33 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 33u32, "2026-10-23T03:43Z", "tibia-lane", "M1 run 4 started 23:42 (job tibia-agent-m1-run4, scratch m1.ps1 unchanged except the fixed search-cem.spi, BelowNormal, 3 h cap ending 02:43, before the night live block). Run 3's output is kept as m1-out-run3.txt. Judged by rule A1 (note 25) with m1-judge.ps1; a timeout is inconclusive by A1-T (note 28). M2 is deliberately not chained: it would overlap the live block, and it starts in the next session if M1 passes.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_34 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 34u32, "2026-10-23T04:58Z", "tibia-lane", "M1 run 4 timing (in progress, 00:57): rules row about 23:50, network_evolve row about 00:57 (13.47 potions per clear, 87940 ms, 0 deaths; identical to run 3, so the run is deterministic). network.evolve's 513 fitness calls took about 67 min under tonight's load (main's next5c/next5d and eoie's F7/F8 beside it), about 7.8 s per call. The fixed CEM arm needs 513 calls in the 106 min left before the 02:43 cap: it finishes if its per-call cost is within about 1.6 times network.evolve's. A timeout again would by itself say CEM's calls cost more than that (the per-call question of rule A1-T, note 28).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_35 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 35u32, "2026-10-23T05:57Z", "tibia-lane", "M1 run 4 VERDICT by rule A1 (note 25), applied mechanically by m1-judge.ps1 (job tibia-agent-m1-run4, 23:42-01:57, fixed CEM, start 250): M1 FAILS this attempt. Held-out potions per clear: rules 13.94 (3 deaths), network.evolve 13.47 (0 deaths), CEM 14.76 (0 deaths), above the pass bar 14.14 (1.05 x 13.47); clear time 87940 ms, within the cap 94934. This is the first counted failure (run 3 was inconclusive by A1-T); A1's stop rule: trace the cause before a second attempt, and stop after two failures with a traced fix between them. Observations for the trace: (1) the fixed CEM arm finished in about 60 min (00:57-01:57) under tonight's load, while run 3's old-code CEM arm had not finished after about 115 min, so the i32 overflow (note 32) plausibly did change run 3's search even though the toy floor test did not separate the two (load differed too, so this is suggestive only); (2) CEM's clear time equals network.evolve's exactly (87940 ms), worth checking: both networks may clear the same way and differ only in potion use, or the clear measure may not be sensitive to the policy here. Next (a trace, no rerun yet): print CEM's per-generation best training fitness and spread against network.evolve's, to see whether CEM converges too early (start spread 250 per mille too wide or too few generations) or never reaches network.evolve's level; M2 does not start (A1, one novelty at a time).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_36 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 36u32, "2026-10-23T05:59Z", "tibia-lane", "Trace prepared for the A1 stop rule (02:0x, not run; my compiles queue behind tonight's live window): scratch agent-m1/m1-trace.ps1 runs network.evolve and the fixed CEM on the training block. It prints each CEM generation's best and mean training fitness (an insertion into a copy of search-cem.spi, checked offline), plus both final networks' training fitness, so training can be compared with the held-out result (CEM 14.76 vs network.evolve 13.47 potions). Reading: CEM's best stalling below network.evolve's training fitness by generation 8 means a search problem (spread or budget); CEM beating network.evolve on training but losing held-out means overfitting to the training block.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_37 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 37u32, "2026-10-23T06:32Z", "tibia-lane", "Trace plan amended 02:3x. The running job tibia-agent-m1-trace-cem2 is the CEM-only variant (scratch agent-m1/m1-trace-cem.ps1). It prints CEM's per-generation best and mean training fitness, but not network.evolve's final training fitness, and note 36's reading compares against that number. The second half is scratch agent-m1/m1-trace-evolve.ps1, with network.evolve only (deterministic, about 67 min under load at run 4's 7.8 s a call). It is queued only when no live window is pending and eoie's F8 is not running beside it, so it does not delay the block; otherwise it runs next session. The plateau half of note 36 (CEM's best flat over its last generations) reads from the CEM trace alone. The overfit half (CEM above network.evolve on training) waits for both numbers. First line, 02:19: generation 0 best -219850, mean -813200369. Most of the 64 members sit at the -1e9 time-limit floor, so start spread 250 per mille already puts about 80 percent of the population past the time limit.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_38 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 38u32, "2026-10-23T06:35Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE A2, written 02:4x before the trace ends (generations 0-2 seen: best -219850, -75950, -75950; floor share from the mean about 0.81, 0.55, 0.31). It chooses the traced fix that A1's stop rule requires before a second and last M1 attempt. Inputs: the CEM trace (best b_g and mean m_g for generations 0-7, plus CEM's final training fitness C) and the evolve-only trace (network.evolve's final training fitness E, scratch agent-m1/m1-trace-evolve.ps1, note 37). Floor share s_g = m_g / -1e9, the approximate share of members at the time-limit floor. A2 applies only when both traces are in. Checked in order, first match wins: (O) if C > E (CEM fits the training block better than network.evolve but loses held-out, 14.76 vs 13.47), the failure is overfitting, so no search change is tried, the A1 failure is recorded as overfit, and the next step is a selection fix (elite or final choice judged on a separate validation block) under a new rule; (S) if s_7 >= 0.20, the start spread wastes the budget, so the second attempt is scratch m1-narrow.ps1 (CEM start_per_mille 62, the 256 spread of network.evolve), everything else unchanged; (B) otherwise the budget is short, so the second attempt is m1.ps1 with 16 generations instead of 8 (population 64, start 250), everything else unchanged. Either second attempt is judged by A1 unchanged; a second failure stops M1. Neither attempt runs inside or beside a live window, nor beside eoie's release jobs. Scripts ready (scratch agent-m1): m1-narrow.ps1 for (S); m1-long.ps1 for (B), which differs from m1.ps1 only in 16 generations, its own work dir and m1-long-out.txt, and parses. Both are judged with m1-judge.ps1 -Out <their output>. (B) re-runs network.evolve too, about 3 h under load, so its cap is 4.5 h.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_39 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 39u32, "2026-10-23T06:38Z", "tibia-lane", "Run 4 observation (2) read from the code, no compile (02:4x; corrected 02:4x before use). network_tactic gives the network three outputs (hunting_pack.spi 1071 and 1079-1089): heal (when heal_ready), engage (Strike in reach when the strike is ready, Approach out of reach, else Hold) and retreat. Footing (StepAway), targeting (CurrentFirst) and patrol are fixed (1468-1469). A heal is not free in time: player_heals sets next_action_at to now plus parity_action_gap_ms and starts the knight heal cooldown (884-904), so a heal can delay the next strike. So an identical held-out clear time over 100 clears (CEM and network.evolve both 87940 ms, rules 87716) does not follow from equal engage and retreat choices alone. Answered without a decision trace by A3 (notes 40-41): on this block the clock moves with deaths, and every zero-death policy (the threshold healer at 220, network.evolve, CEM) clears in exactly 87940 ms, so the tie means neither network lets the knight die. A2's fixes are not narrowed by it.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_40 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 40u32, "2026-10-23T06:39Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE A3 (descriptive, changes no verdict), written 02:4x before the run. Question of note 39: does the held-out clear time respond to heal timing under network_tactic? Run: job tibia-agent-clock (scratch agent-m1/m1-clock.ps1), with no training, on the same camp, objective and held-out block as M1. It reports held-out potions, clear ms and deaths for network_tactic (cavebot_network camp h) at h = 100, 160 and 220, plus cavebot_with_heal 160 as a check (must give 13.94 potions and 87716 ms, or the harness differs from M1 and nothing is read). Reading: (i) all three network clear times within 50 ms of each other means the clock does not respond to heal timing on this block, so the CEM and network.evolve tie (note 39) says nothing about their engage choices, and A1's time cap barely binds in M1; (ii) any two differing by 200 ms or more means heal timing moves the clock, so the exact tie points to both networks healing outside strike windows, or to matching engage and retreat choices; (iii) in between: report only. Neither reading changes A2.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_41 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 41u32, "2026-10-23T06:44Z", "tibia-lane", "A3 applied (job tibia-agent-clock, 02:41-02:44, held-out block, no training; scratch agent-m1/m1-clock-out.txt). Check arm cavebot_with_heal 160: 13.94 potions, 87716 ms, 3 deaths, the same as M1, so the harness is M1's. network_tactic (cavebot_network camp h): h=100 gives 11.66 potions, 88236 ms, 65 deaths; h=160 gives 13.95, 87716 ms, 3 deaths (the threshold healer reproduces the rules arm); h=220 gives 14.48, 87940 ms, 0 deaths. By the letter that is (ii): clear times differ by up to 520 ms. Descriptively, though, the clock moves with deaths, and every zero-death policy measured on this block (h=220, network.evolve, CEM) clears in exactly 87940 ms. So the note-39 tie is most simply both networks never letting the knight die, not matching engage choices. A1's time cap (94934 ms) does not bind on any of these. A new bar for M1's reading: at 0 deaths the hand-made threshold healer h=220 uses 14.48 potions, so network.evolve (13.47) beats it and CEM (14.76) does not even reach it. A second attempt that stays above about 14.5 would be no better than a one-number healer. Nothing in A2 changes.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_42 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 42u32, "2026-10-23T06:46Z", "tibia-lane", "CEM trace done (job tibia-agent-m1-trace-cem2, 02:04-02:45; scratch agent-m1/m1-trace-cem-out.txt). Training fitness per generation, best and mean (floor share s = mean / -1e9): g0 -219850, -813200369 (0.81); g1 -75950, -548300633 (0.55); g2 -75950, -314537477 (0.31); g3 -75950, -189914892 (0.19); g4 -75950, -65149721 (0.07); g5 -75950, -2873797 (0.003); g6 -74450, -157292691 (0.16); g7 -74450, -47995462 (0.05). Final CEM training fitness C = -74450. Reading: the best was found in generation 1 and improved once (generation 6, by 1500), while the population converged on it over generations 1-5. The resampling at generation 6 threw about 16 percent back to the floor, so the narrowed distribution still produces time-limit failures. A2 so far: s_7 = 0.05 < 0.20, so (S) is out. (O) against (B) needs E, network.evolve's training fitness, from scratch m1-trace-evolve.ps1, which by note 37 waits until no live window is pending (next session at the latest). A2 is not applied until then.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_43 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 43u32, "2026-10-23T09:55Z", "tibia-lane", "A2 APPLIED (rule note 38; inputs from the CEM trace, note 42, and the evolve-only trace job tibia-agent-m1-trace-evolve, 05:11-05:53). Reproduction guard (note 37 amendment, since main changed the lib at 04:40): network.evolve's held-out row is 13.47 potions, 87940 ms, 0 deaths, the same as run 4, so the lib change did not alter it. E (network.evolve final training fitness) = -67400; C (CEM final training fitness) = -74450. (O) needs C > E, but -74450 < -67400, so CEM fits the training block worse than network.evolve: no overfitting. (S) was already out (s_7 = 0.05). Result: (B), the budget is short. CEM's gap is a search shortfall on training, and held-out (14.76 vs 13.47) only follows it. The second and last M1 attempt is scratch agent-m1/m1-long.ps1 (16 generations instead of 8, population 64, start 250, the fixed CEM, everything else as run 4), judged by A1 unchanged with m1-judge.ps1 -Out m1-long-out.txt; a second failure stops M1. Queued 05:5x as a plain BelowNormal job with a 5 h cap. It re-runs network.evolve (about 45 min) before CEM (about 2 h), beside main's heavy windows, with no live window or eoie release pending (A2's run condition). The verdict comes next session.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_44 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 44u32, "2026-10-23T11:08Z", "tibia-lane", "In scope before Dota (user 2026-10-23 07:1x: the agent task itself comes before Dota). Never offer parking as a retirement shortcut. SUPERSEDED route: A1 ended the CEM route at 07:3x (note 47, second M1 failure); the item continues under rule A4 (note 48), network.evolve as trainer, M2e then M3, then promotion.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_45 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 45u32, "2026-10-23T11:11Z", "tibia-lane", "Retirement grooming 07:1x, re-corrected 07:2x from rule A1 (note 9) and the M3 design (note 20): about 12 h left. My 07:1x revision (35-45 h, a full port of mrakgr layers.spi and primitives.spi) read the description wish-list, not A1. A1 defines the path: M1 (dense layer, memory plan and CEM on C, no CUDA; note 10 found it needs none of primitives.spi); its last attempt, job tibia-agent-m1-long, is running. M2: the typed per-agent view; harness m2.ps1 is ready; about 3 h CPU plus judging. M3: types only over network.spi (raw/planned/frozen typestate, input size as a phantom index; note 20), bit-exact against M2; about 6 h code plus one verify job. Then the promotion check at M3. A1's stop rule ends the item with a stop note if M1 fails twice or if promotion is missed at M3; either way the item is finished by A1. The description's further upgrades (existential sealing, usage vectors, staged flat-loop emission, ternary weights) are not A1 milestones and are not in the estimate.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_46 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 46u32, "2026-10-23T11:30Z", "tibia-lane", "SUPERSEDED (07:3x): rule A1-M2 (an M2 with the passing M1 budget, scratch m2-long.ps1) never applied, because M1 failed (note 47). M2 now runs as M2e under rule A4 (note 48), with network.evolve as trainer for both arms.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_47 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 47u32, "2026-10-23T11:35Z", "tibia-lane", "M1 VERDICT, last attempt (job tibia-agent-m1-long, 05:54-07:27, 16 generations; judged by m1-judge.ps1 -Out m1-long-out.txt): FAILS by rule A1. Held-out potions per clear: rules 13.94 (3 deaths), network.evolve 13.47 (0 deaths), CEM 14.76 (0 deaths), bar 14.14; clear time 87940 within the cap. The CEM result is identical to run 4 (8 generations), so the traced fix of A2 (B), twice the generations, changed nothing: CEM settles on the same held-out behaviour, consistent with the trace (best found in generation 1). STOP NOTE by A1 for the CEM route: M1 failed twice after a traced fix. The CEM port does not beat or match this code's own neuroevolution on PF1's clear, so CEM is dropped as this item's trainer. The saturation reading of PF1 and 10-13 is not the cause, because network.evolve, a trainer on the same network, beats the rules policy (13.47 vs 13.94). The item itself continues, by the user's direction of 10-23 07:1x (the agent task comes before Dota), under rule A4 (next note).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_48 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 48u32, "2026-10-23T11:35Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE A4, written 07:3x before any run it judges (follows the A1 stop note on the CEM route). The agent continues with network.evolve as its trainer (the tibia neuroevolution that already beats the rules policy on held-out). Milestones, one novelty each, judged on PF1's held-out block, same camp and objective as M1: M2e swaps in the typed per-agent view (m2-patch's wide 25-input network) and trains both arms with the same network.evolve budget (8 generations x 64 offspring, spread 256, seed 1, cold from zero weights); it passes when the wide arm's held-out potions are at most the 8-input arm's 13.47 x 1.05 = 14.14 with clear time within the cap. M3 as in note 20 (types only), passing when M2e's numbers reproduce bit-exact. Promotion and its stop are as in A1 (15 percent under min(13.94, 13.32, 12.06), a second window, a random control), and a promotion miss at M3 writes the final stop note. If the wide network needs a trainer network.evolve cannot give (it is typed to the 8-input weights), the harness adds a wide variant of the same algorithm, not a new method, and this note records it before the run. One M2e attempt, plus one more only after a traced fix, as A1. Amended 07:4x, before any M2e run: the 8-input arm is not re-run. network.evolve at seed 1, 8 x 64, spread 256 is deterministic and gave 13.47 potions, 87940 ms and 0 deaths in run 4, in the evolve-only trace after main's 04:40 lib landing, and in m1-long. The M2e job trains and reports the wide arm only, and the bar is 14.14 (13.47 x 1.05). The job is capped to end before the 09:40 live block; a cut run is inconclusive (not an attempt) and is rerun after the block.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_49 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 49u32, "2026-10-23T13:12Z", "tibia-lane", "M3 drafted ahead of the M2e verdict (09:1x; lands nothing until M2e passes A4). Scratch m3-typed/typed.spi, from note 20: nominal graph size state = size * array i32 * array i32 (weights, hidden scratch laid out once by plan); phantom states raw, planned and frozen; size tags eight and wide with the input width as a prototype (instance width eight = 8, wide = 25), so one decide serves both sizes and wide_weights stops being a separate nominal. decide takes only graph size frozen; with_weight and offspring write only planned graphs; evolve breeds planned children and freezes them for fitness. Cells 6 of 6 green (c and lua): decide agrees with network.decide on 401 inputs bit for bit, typed evolve reproduces network.evolve weight for weight (seed 5, 30 x 12) with score 17, and three compile-fail pins (planned cannot decide, frozen cannot be written, eight-input weights cannot read wide features) each have a positive twin that compiles, so each fails only on its one difference. A unit tag is built bare (eight, not eight ()); that trap was already in my gotchas memory, now with the fix. The M3 run is ready as scratch agent-m1/m3.ps1 (M2e harness plus m3-patch.ps1: WideNetwork arm on graph wide frozen, typed evolve, same budget, same report row); its compile check runs as job tibia-m3-compile. Deviation from note 20: the pins live in the module file next to the code instead of pack_sim.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_50 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 50u32, "2026-10-23T13:36Z", "tibia-lane", "M2e attempt cut (09:35): job tibia-agent-m2e reached its wall-clock cap after 117 min (BelowNormal, beside main heavy jobs) having printed only limit_ms 94934, no held-out row. By A4 a cut run is inconclusive, not an attempt. Rerun queued as job tibia-agent-m2e-2 after the PR-26 live block, same script and budget, cap 4 h (the 8-input arm took 88 min; the wide arm has 168 weights against 66 and its features cost more per decision, so 117 min was not enough). M3 waits for its A4 verdict; its harness already compiles (note 49).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_51 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 51u32, "2026-10-23T13:50Z", "tibia-lane", "RULE A5, written 09:5x before any number it judges: the promotion step of A1 and A4, made mechanical. Comparators from PF1 (TIBIA-POLICY-FEATURES note 5): rules cavebot_with_heal 160 13.94 potions with 3 deaths, retreat live_retreat_tactic 160 2 13.32 with 6 deaths, best temperate behavior (genes 26 84 104 3 4) 12.06 with 0 deaths; L = 94934 ms. Step 1, window 1 (held-out seeds 1001-1100), read from M3 row (identical to M2e by M3 own criterion): pass iff potions <= 10.25 (0.85 x 12.06), mean clear time <= 94934 ms and deaths <= 0 (the 12.06 behavior deaths). A step 1 miss writes the final stop note on this item, as A1 and A4 say, and nothing more runs. Step 2, only after a step 1 pass, one job: (a) window 2, seeds 2001-2100 (disjoint from training 1-100, held-out 1001-1100 and resampled 10001+), with the same trained weights and the three comparators re-run on that window: pass iff the agent is at or below 0.85 x the lowest comparator there, within L, deaths no higher than that comparator. (b) Random control on window 1: the wide network after one mutation of zero weights (seed 2, generation 0, child 0, spread 256), untrained: at chance iff its potions are above 10.25, so it would not promote. Promotion iff step 1, (a) and (b) all hold; otherwise the final stop note names the failing part. One attempt, no reruns except for an inconclusive (cut or crashed) job. Amended 10:0x, before any number: PF1's notes do not name the targeting order of the 12.06 temperate behavior, so on window 2 it runs as temperate_tactic with both CurrentFirst and ReachFirst, and the lower potions of the two is its comparator value (the stricter bar).")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_52 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 52u32, "2026-10-23T18:43Z", "tibia-lane", "A4 APPLIED to M2e attempt 1 (job tibia-agent-m2e-2, 11:16-15:16 window, finished 14:3x; wide 25-input arm only, network.evolve seed 1, 8 generations x 64 offspring, spread 256, cold from zero weights): FAIL. Held-out (seeds 1001-1100): 47.60 potions per clear (potions_x100 4760) against the bar 14.14 (13.47 x 1.05), mean clear 88272 ms (within the cap 94934), 6 deaths (the 8-input arm: 13.47 and 0). The trained weights are printed (168 values, scratch agent-m1/m2e-out.txt). A 3.5x worse arm is a symptom, not noise, so by A4 (as A1) one more attempt is allowed only after a traced fix. Next: a trace job on these weights over the held-out block, counting the network's heal, engage and retreat decisions per clear against the 8-input arm's, plus the wide inputs' ranges (foe dx and dy x32, foe health, blocked tiles), to find whether an input scale or slot mapping drives a heal on almost every think. No retrain before the trace names a cause. M3 waits on M2e; if no traced fix is found, A1's stop rule writes the stop note here.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_53 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 53u32, "2026-10-23T19:09Z", "tibia-lane", "STOP NOTE (final, by A1 and A4; 15:1x). M2e attempt 1 failed A4 (note 52: 47.60 potions per clear against the bar 14.14). A4 allows a second attempt only after a traced fix, and the trace finds none. Job tibia-m2e-trace ran the trained wide weights per held-out seed: every one of the 100 clears used 20-87 potions (median 47, histogram 20s 4, 30s 22, 40s 32, 50s 27, 60s 12, 70s 2, 80s 1), deaths in 4 clears, no runaway outliers. That is a heal about every 2 s in every clear, a uniformly heal-heavy learned policy. The inputs map to the slots decide_wide reads (base 8, foes 8-16, blocked tiles 17-24), the fitness is the 8-input arm's objective, and a hand evaluation of the weights shows no heal at full health. So there is no harness or model bug to fix. The likely cause is the budget, not the code: 17 extra inputs start as random mutations of large magnitude (blocked tiles 256, foe offsets up to 224), and 8 generations x 64 offspring cannot silence them, while the 8-input arm reached 13.47 with the same budget. Milestones are judged in sequence, so M3 does not start, and A5's promotion bar (10.25) is out of reach of both arms (13.47, 47.60). Result: on this scenario neither the CEM route (A1, note 47) nor network.evolve with the typed wide view beats the tuned rules policy (13.94) by the 15 percent A1 asks; the 8-input network.evolve arm stays the best learned policy at 13.47, 3.4 percent under rules. Kept for any future agent work, nothing landed: the M3 typed graph (scratch m3-typed/typed.spi: phantom states raw, planned and frozen, the input width as a prototype index, bit-exact with network.decide and network.evolve in Cells, 6 of 6), its harness (agent-m1/m3.ps1, compiles), A5's promotion rule (note 51), and both arms' trained weights (m2e-out.txt). This closes TIBIA-AGENT's pre-registered experiment; any restart needs a new rule (a larger budget or a warm start from the 8-input weights for the wide arm), written before any run.")
inl note_of_qualified_union_cases_1 () = Note ("QUALIFIED-UNION-CASES", 1u32, "2026-10-14T10:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 06:59 review: verified: committed (8a89893a), blessed, regression sample qualified_union_case_pattern in EXPECTED.tsv (C exit 6); only the clearer ambiguity error for an unqualified case shared by two opened modules is left: 70 -> 90%")
