inl note_of_qualified_union_cases_6 () = Note ("QUALIFIED-UNION-CASES", 6u32, "2026-10-15T09:49Z", "main-thread", "05:49 main: regression sample added: samples/union_case_ambiguity_negative (lane A's repro: open a + open b + unqualified Hit) plus an ExpectedErrors entry in tests/matrix.psd1 (ETY-UnionCaseAmbiguous). Review single-flight-20261015-050436: exactly 4 new rows (Fsharp, C, Rust, Delphi; expected error), 0 changed, DISAGREE 0. Bless single-flight-20261015-052820 reproduces it on all 4,147 rows. Done.")
inl note_of_tibia_standards_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-STANDARDS", 1u32, "2026-10-15T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:00 grooming: two standards checked mechanically: 0 comment lines (// but not ///) in apps/tibia/*.spi; one f64 use left (creatures.spi:37, see TIBIA-NUMERIC-STANDARD). Next: make both one eoie check verb instead of a grep.")
inl note_of_tibia_standards_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-STANDARDS", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:37Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 13:37 re-measured on tibia src + apps/arena: 0 comment lines; 0 i64/u64 in src; one f64, creatures.spi canary_step_speed_in_double (Canary's double step formula), used only as the oracle in the exhaustive integer-equality test (creatures.spi:125); 172 test blocks, all with a ///> target; every rules test with a C oracle and the GPU twins on WGSL. New executable checks today: apps/arena/build.ps1 fails if lua.spi gains host calls. The remaining standards (no comments, no 64-bit in rules, floats only in oracles, explicit targets) should become one package-declared check (EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS), not more ad-hoc greps in build.ps1, so this waits on that verb.")
inl note_of_tibia_standards_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-STANDARDS", 3u32, "2026-10-19T06:40Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 02:40 x lane (git-2c), ideas from the five mega samples for the Tibia lane to weigh, not started by x: (1) readiness proofs are reusable today: one ready attack_group from src/readiness.spi can authorize any number of Strike commands; the lean_cic transition-indexed environment (L:1628-1633) and spiral_proves' linear merge without a Used/Used case (S:555-583) model a proof that a command consumes (ready -> spent), so a tick's Strike and Heal must use disjoint groups; runtime values stay out of indices, the consumption is a private union per tick. (2) brzozowski length-indexed vectors with bounded indices (main.spi:80-84) fit static tables such as per-vocation spell lists in src/vocations.spi, compile time only. (3) Keep tibia's inline fails tests as the standard: the samples' separate negative folders fail for wrong reasons")
inl note_of_tibia_standards_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-STANDARDS", 4u32, "2026-10-20T05:56Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 01:56 parking pass: paused on EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS (Planned). Re-measured on tibia src + apps after tonight's edits: 43 .spi files, 0 comment lines, 0 i64/u64 in src, one f64 (creatures.spi:74, the oracle-only Canary double formula), 218 test blocks. Resume when the package-declared check verb lands.")
inl note_of_tibia_standards_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-STANDARDS", 5u32, "2026-10-20T15:25Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 11:25 standards are now executable through eoie (EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS' 'eoie standards <package-dir>' is installed): src/standards.spi declares NoCommentLines, LfOnly, TestTargets, LineWidth 120 and IntegerOnly for the 26 float-free modules (creatures stays out: its one f64 is Canary's double formula, used only as a test oracle; a scratch copy that declares creatures flags creatures.spi:74, so the rule bites). 'eoie standards src' : integer-only, comment, LF and target rules all clean; 342 lines over 120 columns in src (apps/arena + apps/map on defaults: 131 more). Line width is left to the keep-breaks formatter (tibia is its user corpus) rather than hand-wrapped; once it is green, 'eoie standards src apps/arena apps/map' becomes a step in scripts/build.ps1. Not covered by a rule yet: bit-exact CPU/GPU (the GPU twins do it, now honest), typestate unions, conservation tests, counter-based RNG, unsigned hashing.")
inl note_of_tibia_standards_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-STANDARDS", 6u32, "2026-10-20T18:06Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 14:06 keep-breaks formatter (shared single-flight, core EA7AC11D786629F2) applied to tibia src + apps/arena + apps/map: 42 files formatted, a second --check changes nothing, 23 over-120 lines left (unsplittable macro strings). Byte-compare (lab/tibia-standards/format-and-compare.ps1: 4 C probes covering most of src, a WGSL entry, all 9 arena Lua probes, map.rs; before vs after): 15 outputs, 0 differ, including a Lua string.format literal the formatter split with backslash continuations. eoie standards: 473 -> 23 findings (all line-width on those macro strings). Next: put 'eoie standards src apps/arena apps/map' into scripts/build.ps1 once a declared exception for unsplittable macro lines exists, or keep LineWidth as is and accept the 23.")
inl note_of_tibia_standards_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-STANDARDS", 7u32, "2026-10-20T18:42Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 14:42 generator/formatter conflict found and fixed: the formatter re-laid the three map-generated definitions in src/terrain.spi (arena_spot_bits and arena_road_bits to 826 lines each, arena_protection_zone as a deep else-if staircase with 4 lines over 120 from indentation alone), so apps/map/run.ps1 (which compared exactly two generated lines) reported a mismatch and -Update would have fought the formatter. run.ps1 now formats each generated definition with SpiralCompiler format in a scratch file and compares/replaces whole definition blocks; map.spi emits the zone chain with elif (68 lines, max 56 columns). Map check matches (3 definitions), terrain 11/11. Standards findings 473 -> 19 (4 macro strings, ~9 tuple bindings with if-values, 3 one-line record literals, 1 pipeline lambda, 1 long test name), reported to main for the formatter.")
inl note_of_tibia_standards_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-STANDARDS", 8u32, "2026-10-20T19:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 15:34 tibia now passes every declared standard: 'eoie standards src apps/arena apps/map' 0 findings (was 473 this morning). The last 19 lines were re-laid by hand (tuple bindings split into sequential inl in the same evaluation order, one-line records and if-chains broken, a long test name shortened) and the keep-breaks formatter keeps the breaks; verified byte-identical: all 9 arena Lua probes unchanged, views.spi re-layout C-identical. scripts/build.ps1 now gates on 'SpiralCompiler format src apps/arena apps/map --check' (CI-safe through Ensure-SpiralCompiler; eoie is not on CI, so the full eoie standards check stays a local command, documented in README Running). Not yet executable: conservation tests for money/items, typestate phases, counter-based RNG keys, unsigned hashing (each is a code property, not a line rule).")
inl note_of_tibia_standards_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-STANDARDS", 9u32, "2026-10-21T02:01Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 22:01 grooming: closing. Every listed standard now has an executable check: line rules (no comments, LF, test targets, 120 columns, integer-only in 26 modules) through 'eoie standards' (0 findings) and the formatter gate in scripts/build.ps1; bit-exact CPU/GPU through the 28 GPU twins (honest since the Cells fix); typestate phases through readiness.spi's private ready/gate/command unions and their tests; conservation through coins.spi fewest_coins_conserves_value / paying_conserves_value and hunt.spi loot_conserves_gold...; counter-based RNG through rng.roll_key {seed; tick; entity; purpose} with unsigned mix32.")
inl note_of_tibia_standards_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-STANDARDS", 10u32, "2026-10-21T11:21Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 07:21 lab/tibia-standards removed in the Tibia lane's closing pass (item Done; no consumer of format-and-compare.ps1 or wgsl_entry.spi in tibia or spiral).")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 1u32, "2026-10-15T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:00 grooming: prose size now 747 lines (BENCHMARK 299, README 184, MODELING 94, PRIOR_ART 65, PLAN 59, WORKFLOW 46). First move candidate: BENCHMARK's pre-registrations and results as eoie items with results (one per PR/PC), since they already carry IDs and verdicts.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:38Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 13:38 docs/WORKFLOW.md (46 lines) mapped claim by claim; all of it is now in code, the backlog or memory, so it can be deleted once the user agrees. (1) The 'old and new' table is history: the folder workflow is gone from tibia, and the runner (run_notebook / scripts/build.ps1), Zed tasks and eoie backlog are the working tools. (2) Targets with reasons: 172 of 172 test blocks are targeted (CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS warns today, error planned), and the c/wgsl/lua/rust conventions are generated by the scaffold prototype (lab/cells-new-project units.spi) and kept in the tibia memory. Assert-inside-body: still how every test works. Missing features go to tooling: the eoie-first memory. Negative boundaries as 'fails' tests: in use in quantity, vitality and policy. Daemon memory cap and the heavy slot: memory heavy-gate-lessons. 'Every item names a claim' is the one rule only here; it is a backlog convention for when BENCHMARK.md and PLAN.md move (next step of this item). (3) Gates: CI-SPIRAL-WASM-NATIVE, TEST-EXPECT-DIAGNOSTIC, TEST-EXPECT-PERSIST, KINO-ACCEPT-RERUN, SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY, EOIE-AGILE-TYPED-FIELDS, KINO-WGSL-TARGET and the WGSL union/join/storage items are all Done; CI-AFTER-SPI-MIGRATION is at 95%, and tibia CI is green on both OSes.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T18:24Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 14:24 BENCHMARK.md's 16 pre-registrations (PR-1..PR-12, PR-3a, PC-1..PC-3) are the next move, but their home is the experiment ledger (EOIE-EXPERIMENTS: register, run, result, one call each), which is Planned at 0%. Copying them into free-text notes would bypass the missing verb, so this item now depends on EOIE-EXPERIMENTS; they become tibia's first ledger entries when it lands. WORKFLOW.md is ready for deletion pending the user.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 4u32, "2026-10-20T05:56Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 01:56 parking pass: paused on EOIE-EXPERIMENTS (Planned) for BENCHMARK's pre-registrations, and on the user's yes for deleting docs/WORKFLOW.md (mapped claim by claim on 2026-10-18).")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 5u32, "2026-10-21T10:56Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 06:56 Drift audit at the user's question (no files changed). lab/ 140 files (128 untracked; tibia-potion-fidelity 100: probe variants, scratch TSVs, scoring scripts). live/ 25 files, 8 untracked per-experiment scripts (pr12-secondary, pc4-analysis, pr15-analysis, pr15-block, pr16-block, pr17-block, pz-in-traces, start-sync-check) plus hunting-ground-spawns.xml. results/ 51 files (8 untracked) with no evidence-vs-scratch rule. docs/ledger 6 untracked eoie registration/verdict files that duplicate BENCHMARK. Sources: a copied block script per experiment; lab's 'promote or delete when Done' never fires on a never-closing item; ledger dogfooding (10-20) without retiring BENCHMARK's copy; no results rule. Proposed, awaiting the user's go: (1) empty lab/ before the Dota pause (promote prediction probes and d16-score, keep the patches as notes or a branch, delete the rest after a stem grep); (2) one generic live/block.ps1 keyed by ledger id replaces the per-experiment scripts; (3) results/ keeps only TSVs cited by a ledger verdict or BENCHMARK row; (4) one source of record, ledger or BENCHMARK (user decision).")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 6u32, "2026-10-21T11:57Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 07:57 The 'ledger or BENCHMARK' question from the drift audit is mostly settled by this item's own plan (note 3, user direction 10-12): pre-registrations move to the eoie experiment ledger and the prose shrinks. PR-16 and PR-17 are already ledger entries (registered before their data). Blocker for the older ~22 (PR-1..PR-15, PR-3a, PC-1..PC-6): register stamps the call time, so they need an honest import verb (requested from eoie at 07:57: experiment import with --registered-at and an evidence pointer, plus record-verdict). Resume: when that verb ships, import them, then shrink BENCHMARK to the arena description; deleting prose files (WORKFLOW.md first, mapped 10-18) stays the user's yes.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 7u32, "2026-10-21T12:08Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 08:08 Resume condition is now an eoie item: EOIE-EXPERIMENT-IMPORT (P2; import with --registered-at and --evidence, record-verdict --at, rows marked imported=yes). Set as this item's dependency.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 8u32, "2026-10-21T19:46Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 15:46 BENCHMARK pre-registrations imported into docs/ledger with eoie r29 'experiment import' and 'record-verdict' (EOIE-EXPERIMENT-IMPORT), every entry imported=yes, registered at the section's written time (UTC), evidence docs/BENCHMARK.md@line. 36 entries done (PR-1 to PR-14 by quantity, e.g. PR-4-RETREAT-VBOT, PR-7-TIME, PR-13-CLEARS, plus PC-3 and PC-6), each with its verdict; PR-1 VOID and PR-3 NOT RUN are recorded as such. Pending 6 (PC-1, PC-2, PC-4, PC-5, PR-15-KILLS, PR-15-POTIONS): import refuses one-sample, binomial and median without --arms, and these measure a whole table with no group column; sent to eoie (ungrouped means the whole table, as table query now does). The drafted commands are kept in this session's scratch for the rerun. With the ledger complete, BENCHMARK can shrink to the arena description, but deleting or shrinking prose stays the user's decision (asked, not done).")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 9u32, "2026-10-22T01:55Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 21:55 Ledger drift heal (user: 'what about this spam in docs/ledger? heal from this drift'). Source: this morning's drift audit (note 5) already named 'ledger dogfooding (10-20) without retiring BENCHMARK's copy'. E6 left the choice open, and at 15:46 I imported 36 BENCHMARK sections anyway (72 of the 100 files), then planned to shrink BENCHMARK into pointers (F2). Two records of every experiment, with evidence pointers that drift. Rules, written before the checks: G1 one record per experiment: docs/BENCHMARK.md, which is tracked, published by the arena, cited by README, notes and scripts, and holds setup, method and RESULT. F2 (shrink BENCHMARK into ledger pointers) is withdrawn. G2 docs/ledger leaves the repo iff every entry is covered by BENCHMARK: each verdict's key numbers (k of n, means, t) and each native registration's UTC time appear in the BENCHMARK section the entry belongs to. An entry that fails gets its missing fact added to its section first. G3 eoie stays the computation (eoie-first): 'eoie experiment' runs against target/ledger (git-ignored working state, rebuildable by 'experiment import' from BENCHMARK). live/block.ps1 and BENCHMARK's test rows point there. G4 at the source: eoie gets the request for a ledger-free test verb ('table test') and a one-file ledger; memory gets 'a dogfooded tool replaces the old record in the same change or stays a computation tool, never a second copy'.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 10u32, "2026-10-22T01:57Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 21:57 G1-G4 (note 9) applied. G2: all 50 ledger entries are covered by their BENCHMARK section (verdict numbers, registration times). Two needed an explicit UTC registration time added, PR-17's all3 contrast (12:36Z) and PR-20 (18:52Z); two checker misses were confirmed by eye (PC-6 wording, PR-14's 0.019). G3: docs/ledger moved to target/ledger (100 files, git-ignored). live/block.ps1 and the five BENCHMARK test rows point there, and README states that BENCHMARK is the one record and the ledger is eoie's working copy (its docs/ row also lost the stale WORKFLOW mention). G4: memory one-record-per-fact plus an eoie-first limit; eoie asked for a ledger-free 'table test' verb and a one-file ledger, and the evidence-pinning request was withdrawn. F2 (TIBIA notes 23-24, BENCHMARK shrink into ledger pointers) is withdrawn; BENCHMARK keeps every section.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 11u32, "2026-10-22T02:05Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:05 results/ off main (user: 'can't we remove them from the main branch and only get such files built in gh pages to be available with dir-tree-html like the other repos?'). Findings: every other repo's gh-pages shows only what CI builds from its checkout (publish-tree rsync + dir-tree-html). Tibia's results are live-arena outputs that CI cannot produce (local Canary world, GUI client). Nothing in build.ps1 or tests reads results/; only live/, analysis/ and apps/map do, locally. Two files are CipSoft-map-derived (arena-spot-scan-r12.txt, a live client scan, tracked since 4f6e163; arena-region-r45.txt, the OTBM walkability dump), which the ground rules keep local. Rules, written before applying: H1 an orphan branch 'results' holds the live-run evidence. Locally it is a git worktree at results/, so every script path stays. main ignores results/, and CI's ubuntu build checks the branch out at results/ before publish-tree, so the files appear in the gh-pages dir tree. H2 map-derived files never enter that branch or gh-pages: the walkability dump becomes a generated file in target/map (apps/map/run.ps1 already checks terrain.spi against the server map, so a committed dump is a second record), and the client scan moves to the local world (TIBIA_WORLD/scans). H3 what I can't do (staging, commits, branch creation, push) goes to the user as one block of commands; the workflow, .gitignore and script edits are mine. The same checkout@v7.0.1 is already in actions.lock, so no lock regeneration.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 12u32, "2026-10-22T02:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:07 H1-H3 (note 11) applied on main's side: .gitignore ignores results/; gh-pages.yml's ubuntu build checks out ref 'results' at path results before publish.ps1 (same checkout@v7.0.1, lock unchanged); apps/map/run.ps1 writes the walkability dump to target/map and reads the client scan from TIBIA_WORLD/scans; the scan moved to %USERPROFILE%\\tibia-local\\scans and the dump to target/map; README repository rows updated. Left to the user (staging, commits, branch, push): move results/ aside, git worktree add --orphan -b results results, copy the 35 files back, commit them on the results branch, git rm -r --cached results on main and commit with the workflow change, push results and main together. Until the results branch is pushed, the new checkout step fails the ubuntu publish.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 13u32, "2026-10-22T02:10Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:10 H1 rejected by the user ('no, that would stray off from the standardization from other repos, no additional branches, heal'). Trace: the sibling standard is main = source, gh-pages = CI's build of main, no extra refs, no committed data tables (spiral, polyglot, dice, alphabet, x, i574n.github: 0-1 tsv, no ref checkouts). Tibia already keeps its live logs out of git, in the world (tibia-local/arena-logs). The drift entered 2026-10-17, when the harness move put live-run tables into the repo's results/ (TIBIA note on the sibling-style move), and H1 then built a branch around it instead of mapping the request onto the standard. Rules: J1 revert H1 (the .gitignore line and the workflow checkout step). J2 live-run data is world data, like the logs: results/ moves to TIBIA_WORLD/results. live/paths.ps1 gets one results path, and every script default reads it. BENCHMARK keeps its 'results/<name>' citations, with one setup line saying they are relative to the local world; its RESULT rows already carry every number it relies on, and they are what gh-pages publishes. J3 gh-pages keeps what CI builds (notebooks, ci-metrics, docs); raw live tables are not published, which is the standard for data CI cannot build. J4 H2 stays (map-derived files local).")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 14u32, "2026-10-22T02:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:17 J1-J4 (note 13) applied. J1: .gitignore and gh-pages.yml are back to the user's staged versions (no diff). J2: the 35 tables moved from results/ to %USERPROFILE%\\tibia-local\\results (no name clashes; results/ removed). live/paths.ps1 sets TibiaResults under TIBIA_WORLD plus Resolve-ResultsTable (a bare name resolves under results, an absolute path stays). run-arena, arena-summary, pr1/pr2-analysis and block default to bare names; score.ps1 already used TibiaResults; all six parse. BENCHMARK says results/<name> citations are local world files. README: the results/ row is gone, the world line lists the tables, and the run-arena example uses a bare name. J3: publish.ps1 includes only *.lua and *.xml beyond the standard (the tsv/txt globs served results/ only). J4: map files stay local (note 12). Notes 62-68's results/ paths now mean TIBIA_WORLD/results. Memory: standardize-with-siblings. The tracked results files show as deletions for the user's commit.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 15u32, "2026-10-22T02:19Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:19 User: 'leave in the repo the files ci can't generate them, so they appear on the html at the end'. So the standard is: files CI cannot generate are committed on main and published by publish-tree; CI outputs are not committed; no extra branches. Note 13's 'drift entered 10-17' was wrong: results/ in the repo was right. Rule K1: revert J2-J3 and H2 exactly. The 35 tables, the client scan and the walkability dump go back to results/; paths.ps1, run-arena, arena-summary, pr1/pr2-analysis, block, apps/map/run.ps1, publish.ps1 (tsv/txt globs), and the README/BENCHMARK lines return to their previous text. K1 keeps the J1 revert (no branch) and the ledger heal (target/ledger is eoie working state, which CI never needs).")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 16u32, "2026-10-22T02:20Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:20 K1 (note 15) applied. All 37 files are back in results/: the 35 tables, the client scan and the walkability dump; tibia-local/results and tibia-local/scans removed. paths.ps1, run-arena, arena-summary, pr1/pr2-analysis, block, apps/map/run.ps1 and publish.ps1 match the user's staged versions again (no diff). README's apps/map row, results/ row, world line and run-arena example are back to their previous text, and BENCHMARK's world paragraph is removed. The user's index still holds the J-era README (no results/ row) and BENCHMARK (world paragraph), so both need restaging. Notes 62-69's results/ paths are valid again; note 69's target/map and scans paths are void. Memory standardize-with-siblings rewritten: files CI cannot generate are committed and published by the dir tree.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 17u32, "2026-10-22T03:38Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:38 State refresh (retirement grooming): the dependency EOIE-EXPERIMENT-IMPORT is Done, and tonight's decisions settled two files: WORKFLOW.md is deleted (its live rules moved to README) and BENCHMARK.md stays as the one record of each experiment (note 9). README stays per the sibling standard. Left: PLAN.md, MODELING.md and PRIOR_ART.md, each claim into a backlog item or a test, then delete by rule. Resume condition: TIBIA-FIDELITY and TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY are Done or Paused, since both still change PLAN and MODELING claims. This is the last step of the Tibia retirement.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_18 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 18u32, "2026-10-22T15:56Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 11:56 Resume check: half met. TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY is Done (PR-23, gap closed as noise); TIBIA-FIDELITY is still Active (D37 pending), so PLAN.md and MODELING.md claims can still change. Stays Paused.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_19 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 19u32, "2026-10-22T16:45Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:45 RESUMED mechanically: its condition (note 17) is met, TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY is Done and TIBIA-FIDELITY is Paused (note 108 there). Rule DC1, written before mapping: each of PLAN.md, MODELING.md and PRIOR_ART.md is read claim by claim; a claim already held by a backlog item, a test block, BENCHMARK.md or README is marked covered with its pointer; a live claim held nowhere else moves to the item it belongs to (a note) or to README if it describes how to run something; a stale or historical claim is dropped with a one-line reason. A file is deleted only when every claim is covered or moved, and the mapping table is this items note; README stays (sibling standard), BENCHMARK stays (one record per experiment).")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_20 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 20u32, "2026-10-22T16:50Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:50 PLAN.md DELETED by DC1. Mapping: prices and forecasts (2026-10-12) dropped as historical (cost asks now go through TIBIA-GPU-RENTAL per tranche); phases P0-P6 and Later held by their items (WGSL-BACKEND and the wgsl storage items, TIBIA-LOCAL-WORLD, TIBIA-TYPED-RULES with ranged creatures still listed as left, TIBIA-FIDELITY, TIBIA-BASELINES, TIBIA-AGENT and TIBIA-POLICY-FEATURES, TIBIA-ARENA-RELEASE, TIBIA-2048-ANCHOR); the 2026-10-14 phase states superseded by item notes (P5s hidden-information re-plan is in POLICY-FEATURES 2026-10-14); stop rules: P0 gate historical (P0 met), P5 compute stop MOVED to TIBIA-GPU-RENTAL note 3, respawn-cap pack rule MOVED to TIBIA-BASELINES note 24; rental: Vast.ai, RunPod, Vulkan and tranche approval held by GPU-RENTAL-READINESS, the rest (prepaid cap, keys never in a repo, wall-clock limit, watcher, auto-destroy) MOVED there (note 5); order of work MOVED to TIBIA note 29. README lost its PLAN.md line; PRIOR_ART.md line 43 still cites PLAN.md and is handled with that file. Next: MODELING.md (98 lines), PRIOR_ART.md (65).")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_21 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 21u32, "2026-10-22T16:59Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:59 MODELING.md DELETED by DC1. Mapping: the mega-sample survey (2026-10-12) dropped as era context (memory code-era-quality: those samples are gpt5-round artifacts), its reusable patterns held in TIBIA-AGENT's description; graph upgrades held by TIBIA-AGENT except the typed pickler and the not-ported list, MOVED (TIBIA-AGENT note 6); game-domain types: built ones held by their modules, unbuilt ones MOVED (TIBIA-TYPED-RULES note 10 part 3); typed-domain table: every named identifier exists in its module; pinned and unpinned guarantees listed in TIBIA-TYPED-RULES note 10 (corrected 13:15: most type guarantees already have compile-fail pins); limits: private constructors done (SPIRAL-PRIVATE-CONSTRUCTORS), structural record update held by SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES, existentials compile-time only held in memory; compiler-gaps table historical, every item Done (WGSL-STORAGE-ARRAYS, C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT, SPIRAL-PRIVATE-CONSTRUCTORS, SPIRAL-RECORD-UPDATE-EXPR, SPIRAL-MACRO-ARG-EXPRESSIONS) and the WGSL tail-call fix shipped; proofs idea MOVED (note 10 part 4). README lost its MODELING.md line and the pointer in its Typed domain bullet. Left: PRIOR_ART.md (cites PLAN.md and MODELING.md).")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_22 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 22u32, "2026-10-22T17:01Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:01 PRIOR_ART.md DELETED by DC1, and with it the transitional prose is gone: docs/ holds only BENCHMARK.md (the one record per experiment), README stays. Mapping: mrakgr campaign failure analysis and lesson MOVED (TIBIA-AGENT note 7); TritCritNet's kept parts held (BENCHMARK Promotion section; ternary weights in TIBIA-AGENT), its history dropped; candidate targets: 2048 held by TIBIA-2048-ANCHOR (paper source MOVED, note 3), Craftax MOVED (TIBIA-ARENA-RELEASE note 4), HUNL and Tetris dropped as decided (not first); Gemini chat check dropped as a dated fact check (its one useful claim, a discrete grid suits agents, is the premise of the project); sources: Canary release and nExBot held by tibia_local_world / tibia_baselines / tibia_latest_track, Bend 2 by BEND2-IN-SPIRAL, RunPod vs Vast MOVED (GPU-RENTAL-READINESS note 6), AlphaHoldem, ReBeL, Tetris survey and TFS dropped with the decided targets. README Documents list now names BENCHMARK only. Item DONE.")
inl note_of_tibia_latest_track_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-LATEST-TRACK", 1u32, "2026-10-15T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:00 grooming: checked GitHub: canary v3.6.1 (2026-07-01), otclient 4.1 (2026-08-15), nExBot v4.0.4 (2026-07-15, main pushed 2026-09-01) are still the latest releases; we run all three.")
inl note_of_tibia_latest_track_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-LATEST-TRACK", 2u32, "2026-10-18T16:10Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 12:10 checked GitHub again: canary v3.6.1 (2026-07-01; main pushed 2026-10-17, no newer release), otclient 4.1 (2026-08-15, mehah and opentibiabr show the same), nExBot v4.0.4 (2026-07-15, last push 2026-09-01), mesa-dist-win 26.2.4 (2026-10-04, which CI pulls as latest): all current. Stale: spiral's wgsl runner (apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/wgsl-run) pins wgpu = '24' and pollster 0.4, while wgpu is at v30.0.1 (2026-08-22). The upgrade crosses API changes (adapter and device requests, the poll/Maintain API, the device-lost callback) and touches native-shims, which the compiler lane keeps fixed between its review and the accept run, so it waits for them; then rebuild and rerun the tibia GPU twins (gpu_sim, hunt_sim) on lavapipe and the local NVIDIA.")
inl note_of_tibia_latest_track_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-LATEST-TRACK", 3u32, "2026-10-18T17:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 13:40 CI actions brought to latest in tibia gh-pages.yml (uncommitted, actions.lock regenerated with gh actions-lock): checkout v6.1.0 -> v7.0.1, upload-artifact v5.0.0 -> v7.0.2 (v5 still ran on Node 20; spiral's runs carry the Node 20 deprecation annotation for it and for mlugg/setup-zig v2.2.1, which has no newer release), download-artifact v6.0.0 -> v8.0.2, setup-dotnet v5.4.0 -> v6.0.0, setup-python v6.3.0 -> v7.0.0. Release notes checked for breaks: download v8 now fails on a hash mismatch and skips unzipping non-zip files; upload v7 adds archive: false (opt-in); setup-python v7 removed the pip-install input; checkout v7 blocks fork checkouts under pull_request_target/workflow_run. tibia uses none of the changed inputs. Already latest: cache v6.1.0, setup-beam v1.24.1, setup-bun v2.2.0, setup-rust v1.3.0, actions-gh-pages v4.1.0. spiral/dice/alphabet workflows still pin the older majors (their lanes).")
inl note_of_tibia_latest_track_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-LATEST-TRACK", 4u32, "2026-10-19T06:26Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-19 02:26 user direction: Rust nightly everywhere, pinned to a recent date as the default, and lowered only for the cases that still fail, across all repos. The rollout across repos was relayed to main. Tibia's part is done (uncommitted): gh-pages.yml moonrepo/setup-rust channel nightly-2025-05-09 -> nightly-2026-10-18 (no new uses:, so actions.lock is unchanged); new root rust-toolchain.toml with the same channel plus clippy, rust-src, rustfmt and wasm32-unknown-unknown, all available on that date; apps/map/run.ps1 runs rustc from the repo root so it picks up the file. Verified: apps/map/run.ps1 on rustc 1.101.0-nightly (8006091eb 2026-10-17) passes all three checks (dump identical, live scan 251/252, terrain.spi 4/4). Cells ///> rust tests still use Kino's +1.99.0 pin, which is main's to move.")
inl note_of_tibia_latest_track_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-LATEST-TRACK", 5u32, "2026-10-20T05:02Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 01:02 checked GitHub (gh api): canary v3.6.1 (2026-07-01, main pushed 2026-10-17), otclient 4.1 (2026-08-15), nExBot v4.0.4 (2026-07-15), mesa-dist-win 26.2.4 (2026-10-04), wgpu v30.0.1 (2026-08-22): all current, and the wgsl runner is on wgpu 30 since WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30. tibia gh-pages.yml setup-beam now pins OTP 29 / Elixir 1.20 / Gleam 1.19.2 / rebar3 3.27.3 like the other repos (uncommitted, no new uses:). Closing for the Tibia parking: re-check these five when Tibia is resumed.")
inl note_of_tibia_latest_track_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-LATEST-TRACK", 6u32, "2026-10-23T04:06Z", "tibia-lane", "Check 2026-10-23 00:06 (gh release list, read-only): Canary v3.6.1 (2026-07-01) and OTClient Redemption 4.1 (2026-08-15) are still the latest releases; nothing to migrate.")
inl note_of_tibia_oracle_rng_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-ORACLE-RNG", 1u32, "2026-10-15T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:00 grooming: re-checked against the local source: getRandomGenerator is still tools.cpp:449 in Canary 3.6.1. Distribution tests (PR-style) have replaced exact RNG diffs in practice; lowered to P3.")
inl note_of_tibia_oracle_rng_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-ORACLE-RNG", 2u32, "2026-10-20T15:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 11:41 first distributional fidelity check done without a seeded server: cyclops melee damage per landed hit, live 5204 values vs the model's charged/uncharged pmfs; the shape fits (chi-square 17.1 on 17 df fully charged), the mixture weight is the measurable difference (live 0.945 charged). Method: lab/tibia-potion-fidelity/charge_mix.py (MLE mixture weight + binned chi-square, cells with expected >= 5). Next candidates with the same method: knight hits on cyclops ('A cyclops loses N'), troll hits (PR-15 logs once run).")
inl note_of_tibia_oracle_rng_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-ORACLE-RNG", 3u32, "2026-10-20T15:43Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 11:43 second distributional check: knight melee on cyclops (lab/tibia-potion-fidelity/knight_fit.py on pr12-knight-landed.txt, 2427 landed hits from the 80 PR-12 logs, the 237 killing blows excluded as the last hit before each 'You gained' line because Canary caps them at the remaining health). Model roll (level 30, attack 21, axe 60, balanced 75%: raw 6..86, cyclops defense 20 and armor 20 always blocking): mean per landed hit 23.10, max 66; live 22.52, max 64; binned chi-square 13.1 on 15 df, so the formula and the monster block fit Canary. The offensive-mode alternative (raw max 113) is rejected (chi-square 1052.7 on 16 df), confirming both competitors fight balanced. Two of the model's RNG mechanics now pass a goodness-of-fit test against live; the only measured RNG-side difference is the block-charge share (note before).")
inl note_of_tibia_oracle_rng_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-ORACLE-RNG", 4u32, "2026-10-20T15:45Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 11:45 third distributional check: hunter hits on the knight (lab/tibia-potion-fidelity/hunter_fit.py on hunter-landed.txt, 518 landed hits from the 25 hunter logs of PR-13, the diagnostic block and PR-14). Model arrow: raw 0..100 truncated normal, no shield, armor 17 block: mean 40.57; live 39.58, max 87; chi-square 4.9 on 10 df. Mixture with uncharged melee 0..20: arrow share 1.000 (interval 0.980-1.000), so landed hunter damage is all arrows. The alternative where arrows meet the shield is rejected (model mean 32.74, chi-square 54.1 on 9 df), confirming the model's 'arrows skip the shield' rule (Canary combat type: distance hits bypass the shield block). Three RNG mechanics now pass: cyclops melee on the knight (shape), knight melee on cyclops, hunter arrows.")
inl note_of_tibia_oracle_rng_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-ORACLE-RNG", 5u32, "2026-10-20T20:36Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 16:36 fourth distributional check: knight melee on trolls (troll defense 10, armor 10) from the 10 PR-15 hunts: 107 non-lethal landed hits (142 killing blows excluded). A naive fit is badly off (model 33.04 vs live 24.02) because a 50 hp troll dies to most big hits, so the surviving hits are censored low; simulating whole troll fights in the model and keeping only non-lethal hits gives 24.89 vs 24.02, chi-square 11.4 on 7 df (lab/tibia-potion-fidelity/troll_censored_fit.py with knight_fit.py, args 'x 10 10'). Four RNG mechanics now fit live: cyclops melee on the knight (shape), knight melee on cyclops, knight melee on trolls (censored), hunter arrows. Remaining in this item: a seeded local Canary for single-scenario replays (heavy C++ build), not needed for distributional fidelity.")
inl note_of_tibia_oracle_rng_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-ORACLE-RNG", 6u32, "2026-10-21T02:01Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 22:01 grooming: closing. The item's own conclusion is that RNG fidelity is distributional; four mechanics now pass goodness-of-fit against live (cyclops melee shape, knight on cyclops, knight on trolls with censoring, hunter arrows), and the one measured difference (block-charge share) is tracked under TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY. A seeded local Canary build is not needed for that and would cost a heavy C++ build; reopen only if a single-scenario replay becomes necessary.")
inl note_of_tibia_numeric_standard_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-NUMERIC-STANDARD", 1u32, "2026-10-15T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:00 grooming: the only float left in apps/tibia is creatures.spi:37, Canary's step-speed formula 857.36*log(speed+261.29)-4795.01 in f64 (not WGSL-safe). Next: replace with an exact integer table over the speeds the arena uses, with a test against the f64 values.")
inl note_of_tibia_numeric_standard_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-NUMERIC-STANDARD", 2u32, "2026-10-15T11:11Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:11 creatures.spi: canary_step_speed is now exact integer arithmetic (19 linear pieces over /4096, products < 5e6), equal to Canary's double formula for every speed 1..408 (canary_step_speed_exact_up_to; C test integer_step_speed_equals_canary_double_formula_for_every_speed_up_to_its_exact_limit, WGSL row integer_step_speed_on_gpu; creatures 8/8). The double formula survives only as that test's oracle. Melee max (damage.spi) replayed against Canary's doubles in Python over attack 1..200 x skill 1..150 x 3 modes x 5 levels: 450000 cases, 19 differ, all balanced-mode .5 ties where 0.085*0.75f in double lands just below (7 attack/skill pairs, attack 20/40/80 with skill 100/50/90/25/45/85/95); the simulated weapons (21/60, 20/30) never tie. Left: pin those tie pairs if a scenario ever uses them.")
inl note_of_tibia_numeric_standard_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-NUMERIC-STANDARD", 3u32, "2026-10-18T17:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 13:30 tie pairs characterized, closing. Recomputed against Canary's Weapons::getMaxWeaponDamage, std::round(0.085 * attackFactor(float) * value * skill + level / 5), over attack 1..200 x skill 1..150: exact .5 cases are offensive 635, balanced 229 and defensive 423, and doubles round them down only in balanced, at exactly 7 attack/skill pairs: 20/100, 40/50, 40/90, 80/25, 80/45, 80/85, 80/95. Across levels 1..2000 it depends on the level bonus: 20/100, 40/50 and 80/25 differ only at levels 1-4 (bonus 0); 40/90 and 80/45 at 108 levels up to 134; 80/95 at 112 up to 139; 80/85 at 316 up to 394. In between, adding the bonus sometimes lands the double exactly on .5. No rounding rule reproduces this short of emulating double arithmetic, and the difference is +1 max damage on pairs no scenario uses (the steel axe is attack 21, the knight level 8), so damage.spi keeps the exact-rational integer formula. Reopen only if a scenario puts a weapon of attack 20/40/80 on one of these skills.")
inl note_of_kino_shared_daemon_queue_1 () = Note ("KINO-SHARED-DAEMON-QUEUE", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:11Z", "claude", "(2026-10-15 23:11 -04:00, Claude b7595ac3) the xsim measurements now live in the i574n/x repo (apps/sim). Kino test runs from a sibling repo work with SPIRAL_KINO_COMPILER=process (no daemon left behind).")
inl note_of_kino_memo_key_scope_1 () = Note ("KINO-MEMO-KEY-SCOPE", 1u32, "2026-10-14T23:20Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-14 19:20 Tibia lane observation: apps/tibia/pack_sim.spi reported 22 memo hits of 24 right after hunting_pack.spi (a module the tests open) gained a new function, so the memo key seems to leave out the content of opened package modules; a test whose own text is unchanged could pass from memo after its dependency changed behavior. Today's SPIRAL_KINO_MEMO=0 rerun was also 24/24, so no wrong pass here. Worth checking whether dependency modules are part of the key.")
inl note_of_tibia_canary_pitfalls_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-CANARY-PITFALLS", 1u32, "2026-10-15T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:00 grooming: new pitfalls from 2026-10-15: (1) any follow packet clears the player's attack target without telling the client (Game::playerFollowCreature); (2) with classicAttackSpeed=false a player step cancels the scheduled swing (Player::onWalk, PC-3); (3) OTClient Redemption crashes (0xC0000374) on native nested dofile and on errors under pcall around it in the bot sandbox; (4) a scenario exception leaves canary.exe running and run-arena hangs on the inherited pipe.")
inl note_of_tibia_canary_pitfalls_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-CANARY-PITFALLS", 2u32, "2026-10-15T22:49Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 18:49 biggest pitfall of the day, login protection: Canary 3.6.1 keeps monsters off a fresh login for 10 s (loginProtectionTime) and ends it on the player's own move, auto-walk, turn, speech, item use or attack cancel (Game::playerMove/playerAutoWalk/playerTurn/playerSay/playerUseItem*/playerSetAttackedCreature with id 0); starting an attack does not. A probe that only attacks keeps it, bots that turn or talk lose it, and the difference decided PR-9. Harness now ends it for everyone (parity.lua).")
inl note_of_tibia_canary_pitfalls_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-CANARY-PITFALLS", 3u32, "2026-10-15T22:49Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 18:49 ramps: stepping onto a tile whose item has a floorchange direction (e.g. item 6913 'ramp', floorchange south at 1940,1372,7) moves the walker one floor up and one tile on (to 1940,1373,6); monsters only target players on their own floor, so a retreat onto a ramp ends the fight. otbm-walkability.py --items-xml/--floor-changes lists such tiles; mind self-closing <item/> tags when parsing items.xml.")
inl note_of_tibia_canary_pitfalls_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-CANARY-PITFALLS", 4u32, "2026-10-18T16:11Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 12:11 status of each pitfall, where it lives in code: LUA_PATH/LUA_CPATH are cleared in tibia-local world.ps1 and live/run-policy-scenario.ps1; the arena creature rules (targetDistance 1, non-zero experience) and the creature tables are typed in src/creatures.spi with Canary-data tests; follow-clears-target, the step-cancels-swing rule and the dofile crash are handled in the probes and run-policy-scenario.ps1 (the pcall(dofile) shim). Fixed now (uncommitted, not yet run live): pitfall 4. run-policy-scenario.ps1 started canary.exe and only stopped it at the end, so a throw anywhere between (login timeout, probe error, bot seeding) left canary running holding run-arena's inherited pipe. Everything after the start is now in try/finally, and the finally restores otclientrc.lua and stops canary (whitespace-ignoring diff: +4 -1, parses). The remaining text is knowledge already in the tibia memory and code, so this item can close once a live block confirms the finally path.")
inl note_of_tibia_canary_pitfalls_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-CANARY-PITFALLS", 5u32, "2026-10-18T19:42Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 15:42 pitfall 4 fix verified live (lab/tibia-start-sync/stray-canary-check.ps1): with the OTClient exe renamed away, run-policy-scenario.ps1 brought the world online, then failed at run-offscreen's CreateProcess (error 2) inside the new try; the finally stopped canary (no canary process left) and restored otclientrc.lua; the script renames the exe back in its own finally. Every pitfall now lives in code, tests or memory, so closing.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_walk_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY-WALK", 1u32, "2026-10-15T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:00 grooming: blocked tiles are now covered by live checks (PC-1: a cyclops routes around a wall exactly as the router predicts); floor changes are the only unmeasured case left.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_walk_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY-WALK", 2u32, "2026-10-15T14:10Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 10:10 first live floor change seen (by accident, PC-3 run 3): stepping onto the ramp at 1940,1372,7 (item 6913, floorchange south) put the knight at 1940,1373,6. otbm-walkability.py now lists floor-change tiles (--items-xml/--floor-changes).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_walk_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY-WALK", 3u32, "2026-10-18T17:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 13:28 floor changes pinned: geometry.spi models Canary's up-ramp rule from Tile::queryDestination (TILESTATE_FLOORCHANGE: z-1, then north y-1, south y+1, east x+1, west x-1, south_alt y+2, east_alt x+2) as union up_ramp plus climbed. Test stepping_onto_the_south_ramp_matches_the_live_floor_change_2026_10_15 pins the live observation (onto 1940,1372,7, landed 1940,1373,6), and every_up_ramp_climbs_one_floor_and_shifts_like_canary_query_destination covers the other five shifts. geometry 13/13 through scripts/build.ps1. Blocked tiles were already measured: terrain comes from the server map and was checked against the live client scan (251/252 cells), and terrain.spi pins the arena ramps as walls for monsters. The down branch (holes, which depends on the tile below) is not modeled, because no scenario has one. Closing.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_step_timing_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY-STEP-TIMING", 1u32, "2026-10-21T09:11Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 05:11 Source check against the 2026-10-14 note ('straight steps next to the target cost x2 there, live 1412-1479 ms'): in Canary 3.6.1, Monster::isTargetNearby() is stepDuration >= 1, and stepDuration is written only in Monster::getDistanceStep (monster.cpp ~1750), which runs only for fleeing monsters or maxTargetDist > 1 (creature.cpp goToFollowCreature ~996-1001). So a melee cyclops never gets WALK_TARGET_NEARBY_EXTRA_COST. A better fit for the 1400-1480 ms intervals (all in close-quarters jostling): getEventStepTicks with WalkStartPolicy::RespectDelay, where a walk that starts after the last step ended waits a full stepDuration x lastStepCost (700, or 2100 after a diagonal). The model has chase_step_factor 1 (right per this reading) but no fresh-walk delay; a lab prototype of that delay broke the ranged hunters (TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY note 97). Open for TIBIA-EVENT-TIME.")
inl note_of_core_pair_drift_lint_1 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-DRIFT-LINT", 1u32, "2026-10-16T00:10Z", "main-thread", "20:10 main: friction: build.ps1 (line 10) reports every non-zero pair-drift exit as 'a SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL pair changed on one side only', also when pair-drift threw 'not inside a git work tree' (a copied core outside git). Should surface pair-drift's own message. Workaround used for a copied candidate core: a scratch git repo in the job copy with the unpatched core as HEAD.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 1u32, "2026-10-14T13:34Z", "lane-a", "09:34 lane-a: root cause from the 10-06 session log: 'rust' named both the rust/ folder (module record) and rust/rust.spi, so rust.native_type resolved per file's opens; helpers moved to backend.spi. Repros drafted: lane-a\\repro\\namespace_collision_{plain,opened}.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 2u32, "2026-10-14T14:44Z", "lane-a", "10:44 lane-a: minimal repro (rust/ folder + rust/rust.spi, main calls rust.native_type, with and without open rust) compiles fine on the 04:35 core in both forms (same C), so the 10-06 failure needs more context (lib-scale package or a folder module calling itself); next: bisect from lib.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 3u32, "2026-10-14T21:51Z", "lane-a", "Inner variants (rust/rust.spi with nested module and open of it) compile on the pre-pack core (exit 0, residual 98FAE60A183E for both); no reproduction of the 10-06 collision outside the original tree yet.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:49Z", "main-thread", "06:49 groom: no new collision seen in tonight's corpus checks (lib 127 tests, eoie 131 packages through lane A's G3). Unchanged; next grain: re-run the inner-variant repro on the pack-1h core.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 5u32, "2026-10-15T10:54Z", "main-thread", "06:54 groom: all 4 repros (plain, opened, inner, inner_open in the job's lane-a/repro) compile on the installed pack-1h core with the same residuals as before (2DCC21B814AF twice, 98FAE60A183E twice). The 10-06 collision does not reproduce in isolation on any core since 10-14. Next grain changes: instead of hunting the repro, move the helpers back from backend.spi into rust/rust.spi in a scratch lib copy and compile the lib corpus; if it holds, the original move can be undone. Lowered P2 -> P3.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 6u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night sizing: the moved helper is backend.spi rust_type_switch + rust_type, used unqualified 146 times in 20 lib files, so moving it back into rust/rust.spi changes how all 20 files reach it; do it together with the scoping fix, not as a copy check.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T12:41Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:41 helper-lane lap 1: LAB-IN-REPO fix plus recheck: the four 10-14 repros (rust/ folder with rust/rust.spi + ops.spi; plain, opened, inner, inner_open) lived only in job tmp lane-a/repro; copied their .spi/.spiproj into spiral/lab/COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS/<variant>/ and recompiled them on today's installed single-flight compiler (Invoke-Heavy, C + gcc): plain and opened compile and exit 5 with C sha 2DCC21B814AF, inner and inner_open exit 10 with 98FAE60A183E - byte-identical to the 10-14 and 10-15 residuals, so still no isolated collision. Per note 6 the remaining route is the lib-scale one: move backend.spi rust_type_switch + rust_type back into lib rust/rust.spi in a scratch lib copy and compile its 20 consumer files, which is heavy.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 8u32, "2026-10-19T13:28Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:28 helper-lane lap 2: the lib-scale route is scripted (no tree change): lab/COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS/lib_scale.py moves everything from type rust_type_switch onward in backend.spi (rust_type_switch, fsharp_global, rust_type: the helpers moved out on 10-06) back to the end of rust/rust.spi and rewrites every unqualified use in the other lib .spi files to rust.rust_type / rust.fsharp_global / rust.rust_type_switch; lib-scale.ps1 runs it on a cache copy of lib/spiral, then compiles a probe package (packageDir ../lib, |core- + spiral-) whose main reaches rust_type through file_system.directory_get_parent and console.write_line, on Rust and Fsharp. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS/lib-scale.ps1 (~8 min). An error reproduces the 10-06 collision at scale; two clean compiles say the code can move back where it belongs.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:02Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:02 helper-lane lap 3: dry-ran lab/COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS/lib_scale.py on a scratch copy of today's lib/spiral (no compile): it applies cleanly, moving 24 lines (type rust_type_switch onward) from backend.spi to the end of rust/rust.spi and qualifying the uses in 18 files (sm' 44 changed lines, runtime 30, near_workspaces 24, threading 24, wasm 22, near 12, stream 12, ...). Timing note for the queued lib-scale.ps1 row: the 11:45 core switch rewrites lib files, so the row should run after the switch's accept, and lib_scale.py's anchor (type rust_type_switch fs rs =) must be rechecked then; a miss raises at text.index, so it fails loudly instead of compiling a half-moved lib.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_10 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 10u32, "2026-10-19T22:18Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:18 helper-lane lap 6: ran the queued lib-scale row (lab/COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS/lib-scale.ps1, cache copy, tree lib untouched). No verdict: my lib_scale.py moved 24 lines into rust/rust.spi and qualified uses in 18 files, but appended the definitions after their first use, so rust.spi:13:9 any fails with Unbound term variable: rust_type on Rust and F# alike. That is a defect in the probe, not the 10-06 collision. Next: insert the moved block at the top of rust.spi, ahead of its first use, and rerun (8 min, cache copy).")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_11 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 11u32, "2026-10-19T22:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:24 helper-lane lap 6: the lib-scale probe (lab/COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS/lib-scale.ps1, cache copy) found that the move back is safe. With rust_type_switch, fsharp_global and rust_type moved from backend.spi back into rust/rust.spi, placed ahead of their first use, and every use in 19 lib files (.spi and .spir) qualified as rust.X, a file_system plus console probe compiles on both Rust and F# (exit 0, probe dir namespace-lib-scale-20261019-1822). So the 10-06 collision that forced the move does not reproduce on today's compiler; the scoping already resolves it. The two earlier failures were in my move script (definitions appended after use, .spir files skipped), not in the compiler. Next: apply lib_scale.py to the tree lib (backend.spi plus rust/rust.spi plus 19 files qualified), then gate the touched modules. That is a 21-file lib edit, so it is coordinated through main with lane 2 first.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_12 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 12u32, "2026-10-22T06:45Z", "main", "02:45 main: the tree-lib move is staged on a copy (not applied): %TEMP%\\lib-ns\\lib\\spiral = tree lib + tonight's gated sm'/env helpers (main-sm-combined), with lib_scale.py run over it, because the 20 touched files include sm'.spi and env.spi, which the helpers also change. One consistent set lands together. Result: 24 lines moved from backend.spi into rust/rust.spi ahead of the '/// ## rust' section; uses qualified as rust.X in 19 files (rust/near_workspaces, rust/near, am', async, backend, crypto, date_time, env, file_system, mapm, networking, resultm, runtime, seq, sm'_real.spir, sm', stream, threading, wasm). 17 of them hold test blocks, 186 tests in all. Gate (P1, morning queue, behind XSD and hopacsota): apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 -Lib %TEMP%\\lib-ns\\lib\\spiral on each of the 17 files, against the same files on the tree. PASS = every target that passes on the tree also passes on the copy, 0 new type errors. Then land the 20 files (plus helpers, if not landed yet) and drain the lab folder. lib_scale.py writes its target unconditionally (no dry run), so only ever point it at a copy.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_13 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 13u32, "2026-10-22T08:16Z", "main", "04:16 main: landing note. Tree lib changed after %TEMP%\\lib-ns was made: date_time.spi landed DATE-TIME-PORTABLE (and sm'/env/process landed earlier, which lib-ns already had). The running gate (main-ns-gates) still proves the move itself. At landing, do NOT copy lib-ns files over the tree: run lib_scale.py on a fresh copy of the current tree lib, check that its diff is only the 24-line move plus the rust.X qualifications, regate date_time.spi on that copy, then land.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_14 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 14u32, "2026-10-22T08:46Z", "main", "2026-10-22 04:45 main: the fs-tree baseline (file_system.spi on the tree lib, to tell location-dependent failures from namespace-move ones) was stopped while still queued behind main-ns-gates. Prewritten rule: a queued compile job that cannot finish before the 05:30 tibia live block is stopped. Requeue after 06:30, after main-ns-gates' file_system rows are read.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_15 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 15u32, "2026-10-22T08:48Z", "main", "2026-10-22 04:52 main: main-ns-gates finished (17 lib files on the namespace-moved copy %TEMP%\\lib-ns, 45.7 min). 0 type errors in all 17. Passing in full: am' 11, crypto 4, env 3, resultm 2, seq 26, sm' 45, stream 16, threading 1; date_time 35 of 36 with 0 failed (one row did not run; the copy predates the portable GUID landing). No tests: rust/near_workspaces, rust/near, rust/rust, async, networking, sm'_real. Failures: file_system find_parent fsharp, find_parent_2 rust, trace_file fsharp; runtime split_command typescript. All four likely depend on location or environment (find_parent walks up from the cwd, and the copy sits under %TEMP% with no repo markers above it). Not yet proven: verdict needs the tree baselines for file_system.spi and runtime.spi, requeued after 06:30 (fs-tree was stopped at 04:45 by the freeze rule). Rule fixed now: the namespace move is clean if each failing row also fails on the tree, or passes on a copy placed under C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_16 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 16u32, "2026-10-22T09:00Z", "main", "2026-10-22 05:00 main: tree baseline (main-ns-baseline): file_system.spi 18 of 18 passed, runtime.spi 6 of 7 (split_args typescript failed). Verdict for the namespace move, by reading the tests: file_system find_parent/find_parent_2 assert the ancestor holding workspace/ and README.md is named spiral or polyglot, and trace_file writes under get_workspace_root; a copy under %TEMP% can never pass them, so the 3 copy-only failures are location-bound by construction. runtime: the tree fails split_args typescript and the copy fails split_command typescript, a different row each run, so one TypeScript row flakes under load (not the move). Remaining proof per the rule: one run of file_system on the namespace copy placed under spiral/lab, after 06:30.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_17 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 17u32, "2026-10-22T09:11Z", "main", "2026-10-22 05:11 main: proof done. file_system.spi of the namespace-moved copy, placed inside the repo (lab/NS-PROOF/lib/spiral, deleted right after): 18 of 18, 0 type errors. With the 04:52 and 05:00 notes, the move is clean on all 17 gated files, and the only failures were location-bound (file_system) or a flaky TypeScript row (runtime, a different row in each run). Landing still needs a fresh copy. The tree moved after the namespace copy was taken (date_time portable GUID, sm' Gleam-only concat, env argv, process.spi), so landing reruns lib_scale.py on a fresh copy of the current tree and gates those 4 files again, after 06:30.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_18 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 18u32, "2026-10-22T18:38Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:38 main: the pending lib changes now compose into ONE copy, landed once: scratch compose-lib-combined.ps1 rebuilds %TEMP%\\lib-combined from the current tree and applies apply-lib-sort (am' sort), apply-lib-last-write (file_system.last_write_ms), the gated console.spi (guarded 95879AD871B65558), float_text plus its manifest line, the unused removal (74 definitions) and lib_scale.py (the namespace move). That gives 23 files differing from the tree, 21 .spi to gate, with tree guards in guards.txt. It replaces three separate landings that would each have rebased and re-gated the same files (am', sm', file_system, runtime, threading, async). Gate job main-lib-combined-gates runs in the compile window after fiscal-run. Landing rule, fixed now: land through lib_land.ps1 with guards.txt when every gated .spi has 0 type errors and its tests pass, except the known location-bound failures of a %TEMP% copy (file_system find_parent, find_parent_2, trace_file) and the TypeScript runtime row that flaked on both tree and copy. A red elsewhere: find the part that touched that file, recompose without it (-Parts), and re-gate only the files that change. Landing waits for eoie's past-lib-reads message and for no Cells erl.exe of tibia's reading lib.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_19 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 19u32, "2026-10-23T04:19Z", "main", "2026-10-23 00:19 main: combined landing ready, held for eoie's F8 'past lib reads' (the user's P0 install comes first). 25 files gated (0 type errors; only the location-bound find_parent, find_parent_2 and trace_file fail), plus xpath_eval.spi's UTF-8 fix gated separately (2/2). The last_write part is out: its Rust test failed, and eoie, who authored it and needs it for release I, will fix it and land it after mine. Consumer check of the 74 unused deletions: eoie/src, x, tibia, dice, alphabet, fiscal, blog, polyglot and eoie's dry-env tree with GE2/H applied use none (eoie's process_exit_code is its own local). Land: scratch land-lib-combined.ps1 -Apply, then lib_land for xpath_eval (guard 8F15E3C44E5A46C1).")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_20 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 20u32, "2026-10-23T08:41Z", "main", "2026-10-23 04:41 main: LANDED at 04:40:05 by the block-end waiter, one second after tibia's live-gui window released and before F9's stop_cli compile. 25 of 25 combined files equal the gated landing copy by hash (lib_land with per-file tree-hash guards; all guards matched). Also landed: xpath_eval.spi BC1F9F9319910BDD (UTF-8 normalize-space/substring fix, gated 2/2). eoie confirmed beforehand that none of the names its drafts call is among the deletions; its env.spi/file_system.spi/csv.spi additions land next on top (new bases: env E5F81A293E209276, file_system 4756D432D99475E4, package.spiproj EA124BCBC41E4722). Open: fiscal corpus_checks rerun on the landed lib, then the 18 lab repro files go once the item closes.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_21 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 21u32, "2026-10-23T09:03Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:03 main: lab folder drained (18 files deleted). lib_scale.py is the move-back transform, and it is in the landed tree: type rust_type_switch now lives only in lib/spiral/rust/rust.spi (backend.spi has 0), and rust.spi is one of the 25 landed files. lib-scale.ps1 only probed that transform. The 4 repro packages (plain, opened, inner, inner_open) never reproduced the 10-06 collision on any core (notes of 10-14), so they hold no finding beyond those notes. Their only callers were scratchpad compose and gate scripts of the landing itself. Open: eoie's last_write slice (eoie lands it on top of this landing).")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_22 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 22u32, "2026-10-23T10:39Z", "main", "2026-10-23 06:39 main: eoie's additive lib slices landed at 06:38:09 on top of the 04:40 landing, through its guarded land-lib-batch.ps1 (all 4 tree guards passed). The fallback was not needed. Tree now: env.spi 93FB080E4D303EB4, file_system.spi 90E5CD2E1531EAF9, package.spiproj DC64AFD4CC940A37, csv.spi 318ED8DC3D54C836 (new; reviewed by main twice, UTF-8 safe). Its last_write part, dropped from the 04:40 landing, is now in as file_system.last_write_ms, so the item's open scope is closed apart from the formal Done.")
inl note_of_compiler_namespace_collisions_23 () = Note ("COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS", 23u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:39 (subagent): blocked: lib-scale.ps1 queued after the switch accept (lib frozen) -> next: read the Rust + Fsharp probe | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): rechecked after the switch: lib_scale.py still applies (24 lines, 18 files); lib-scale.ps1 queued -> next: read the probe | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: the move back is proven safe at lib scale; 21-file lib edit -> next: apply when the eoie lane is idle, then the lib gates")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_1 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:52Z", "main-thread", "08:52 inputs gathered: per the 10-13 decision the interim default is the fastest backend that beats F# on compile+run, preferably not C. Today's loaded native_ms medians are not decisive (Zig/C 1.27 includes build time). Waiting on CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK's run_ms column (patch staged) for a quiet comparison of Rust, Zig, Lua and Gleam against F# (dotnet fsi start ~3-4 s per program).")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_2 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 2u32, "2026-10-14T21:25Z", "main-thread", "17:25 data for the 10-13 interim rule (the fastest backend that beats F#, preferably not C): by run_ms Zig is at C speed (median 0.96x), Rust 1.16x, Swift 1.54x, Lua 4x; F# rows are not run natively yet (FSHARP-UTF8-STRINGS' F# tier), so the F# baseline is still missing. Proposal pending that baseline: Zig as the interim default (build cost ~2 s per program, now cached), Rust second.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_3 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 3u32, "2026-10-14T21:49Z", "main-thread", "17:49 run-speed evidence: Zig >= C on all 9 measured programs (3.8x on the heaviest), Rust ~ C. Recommendation for the interim default (fastest that beats F#, preferably not C): Zig, pending the F# baseline and a Zig coverage check against the lib/apps that use the default.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_4 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 4u32, "2026-10-15T01:15Z", "main-thread", "21:15 NEXT SESSION #6: an F# baseline (an F# native tier or timed fsi runs) to finish the Zig-as-interim-default recommendation; Zig is >= C on all 9 measured programs.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_5 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 5u32, "2026-10-15T03:31Z", "main-thread", "23:31 F# baseline measured (Release exe on .NET 11 rc1, not fsi; min of 7 interleaved runs; scratch tmp/fsbase). runtime_bench: C 266 ms, Zig 63 ms, F# 319 ms. indexed_dfa_state_cardinality: C 53 ms, Zig 23 ms, F# 170 ms. All exit 0. So Zig is 5.0x and 7.5x faster than compiled F#; F# is 1.2x and 3.2x C, mostly .NET start and JIT (fsi would add the script compile on top). The interim rule (fastest backend that beats F#, preferably not C) is met by Zig on run time. Remaining before switching: Zig coverage of the lib/apps that use the default (Zig list 146 rows vs C 509) and ~2 s Zig build per program. Recommendation: Zig as interim default once coverage is checked.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_6 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 6u32, "2026-10-15T03:39Z", "main-thread", "23:39 coverage blocker quantified: lib/spiral has 230 Fsharp and 245 Rust backend arms but 49 Zig arms (9 of 195 files: am', backend, base, console, convert, math, optionm', sm', sm'_real). A Zig default today would break most lib-using programs. The host defaults to Fsharp in --batch and positional mode (Program.fs 923, 1020). Order: lib Zig arms (ZIG-HUB-SPEED scope), then a lib/spiral notebook sweep on Zig, then the default switch (host plus Kino), keeping F# by flag.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_7 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 7u32, "2026-10-15T09:49Z", "main-thread", "05:49 NEXT SESSION #3: the F# baseline is done (Zig 5-7.5x faster than compiled F#). The remaining blocker is lib/spiral Zig arms (49 vs 230 F#). Add Zig arms module by module, each with its lib notebook passing on Zig and a review/bless, then switch the host and Kino default with F# kept behind a flag.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_8 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 8u32, "2026-10-15T11:04Z", "main-thread", "07:04 decision (main, delegated by user 07:0x 'try deciding autonomously for things waiting for my approval'; 'we need all of them sota'): criterion corrected: the 10-13 rule is compile+run, and the 10-15 numbers were run time only. Decide on edit-to-result latency (Spiral compile + toolchain build + run, small and large programs) plus run speed, measured on a quiet machine. The decision is made autonomously from that data (assignee changed from user).")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_9 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 9u32, "2026-10-15T11:15Z", "main-thread", "07:15 DECIDED (main, autonomously per the user's delegation): Zig is the default, F# stays reachable by flag. Measured on a quiet machine with the harness's own toolchain commands (single-flight-20261015-071049 and -071207, min of 2 runs, -Parallel 1; job tmp/latency). Edit-to-result on small programs is Spiral compile + build + run (ms): Wasm 7+9+54=70; Lua 7+8+127=142; TypeScript 7+6+131=144; Zig 7+~300+16=~330 with a warm cache (first build with an empty cache 8.9 s; the harness uses fresh per-run caches, hence 15 s in its rows); C 14+556+43=613; Rust 8+1290+22=1320; Gleam 739; Bend 871; Lean 2178; F# via dotnet fsi 1.6-2.2 s. Heavy programs, run time only: Zig 17-21 ms and Rust 11-30 ms, C 30-454, Wasm 146-206, TypeScript 111-182, Lua 113-2484 (native_managed_tail_recursion 2.5 s), Bend up to 8.2 s, Lean up to 7.2 s. Why Zig: within 0.3 s of the fastest loop once its cache is warm, the fastest runtime, and exact 64-bit integers. Lua has the quick loop but 10-100x slower heavy runs and inexact 64-bit integers; Wasm is the runner-up (70 ms loop, heavy runs 5-10x Zig). Conditions before switching the CLI/Kino default: (1) Zig lib coverage (PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES, lane B today; 9 of 195 lib files have Zig arms); (2) a persistent Zig global cache for the default path, so users see ~0.3 s builds, not 9-15 s.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_10 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 10u32, "2026-10-15T12:37Z", "lane-b", "08:37 lane-b measurement: a Kino zig cell runs zig run path (LLVM Debug): about 8.8 s for a new sample program on this machine, 9.8 s after a one-line edit; zig run -fno-llvm (self-hosted x86_64 backend) takes 2.0 s and 1.9 s for the same program, with the same output. Patch offered: patches/kino-zig-fno-llvm.ps1 (targets.ex, one anchor). The self-hosted backend covers x86_64 only, so an aarch64 host would need the LLVM path.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_11 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 11u32, "2026-10-15T15:17Z", "main-thread", "11:17 where the default lives: the host has no single default; positional compiles infer the backend from the output extension, and --batch 3-field jobs plus the fsharp subcommand assume F#. The user-facing default is Kino's: notebook.ex default_builders falls back to SPIRAL_KINO_DEFAULT_TARGET, else no builder. Coverage after pack-1k: Zig arms or is_zig guards in 14 of 35 lib files with backend switches (95 arms; env/trace/file_system come in pack-1l); the Zig harness list is 441 rows (was 146), all agreeing. Plan: after pack-1l, lane B makes zig the Kino default (persistent zig global cache, -fno-llvm for cells per its measurement); the host's --batch/fsharp-subcommand defaults stay F# by flag.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_12 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 12u32, "2026-10-15T15:34Z", "main-thread", "11:34 implementation staged by lane B: kino-default-zig.ps1 (untargeted cells run on zig -fno-llvm with a persistent global cache; SPIRAL_KINO_DEFAULT_TARGET still wins), zig-main-thread-stack.ps1 (1 GB thread stack, so -fno-llvm handles deep recursion), ci-setup-zig.ps1 (decided: pin zig 0.17.0 stable in the 4 gh-pages workflows, not master). Blast radius: 135 untargeted lib tests, 2 eoie, 2 Tibia (Tibia session asked to pin). Lands in the pack after 1l, after a default-zig Kino sweep.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_13 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 13u32, "2026-10-15T20:06Z", "lane-b", "16:1x lane-b: Kino default-zig sweep (kino-default-zig on a private Kino, tree lib 15:39, 1k compiler, nothing forced): 15 lib notebooks 266 passed / 8 failed. Zig failures are 6 untargeted tests (testing assert_contains x2, date_time guid timestamps x2, file_system get_current_directory, platform get_executable_suffix), pinned to rust by patches/lib-zig-default-rust-pins.ps1; find_parent x2 fail only in the copied tree (no README.md). Kino suite: pristine 236/4 (copy artifacts); first default-zig patch 31 failures (27 fixtures stub rustc), fixed by test_helper pinning SPIRAL_KINO_DEFAULT_TARGET=rust plus default_target: nil = Zig via Access.fetch. Suite rerun waits for the Tibia arena to end.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_14 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 14u32, "2026-10-15T20:07Z", "main-thread", "16:07 default-zig Kino sweep (lane B, kino-default-zig on a private Kino, tree lib incl. batch 6, 1k compiler): 15 notebooks, 266 passed / 8 failed. Failing on Zig: testing assert_contains x2 (F#-only fallback), date_time guid pair (guid record without a Zig field), file_system get_current_directory, platform get_executable_suffix; 2 more are copy artifacts. lib-zig-default-rust-pins.ps1 pins those 6 to ///> rust. kino-default-zig.ps1 updated (test_helper pins rust for fixtures; default_target nil means zig). Remaining: Kino suite rerun on the patched copy, after Tibia's live block.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_15 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 15u32, "2026-10-15T23:10Z", "lane-b", "19:1x lane-b: final proofs for the Kino zig default (after the arena, tree lib with batch 7, 1k compiler): kino-default-zig Kino suite 237 tests / 4 failures = the same 4 copy artifacts as pristine 236/4 (+1 new passing test); default-zig sweep with lib-zig-default-rust-pins: testing 6/0, date_time 32/0, platform 1/0, guid 1/0, common 5/0, crypto 5/0, file_system 13/2 (find_parent copy artifact). ci-setup-zig pins zig 0.17.0. Sent to main for pack-1o. Next: move pins to Zig as Zig arms land (batch 7 already makes date_time guid + platform possible), Zig arm for testing __assert_contains.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_16 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 16u32, "2026-10-15T23:14Z", "main-thread", "19:14 main: implementation in the tree (pack-1o): CI gh-pages in 4 repos install zig 0.17.0; 6 lib tests pinned ///> rust (Rust-only features); Zig lib batches 7 (guid, platform, random', hash_text) and 8 (assert_contains). Kino default=zig patch proven (kc6 237 tests, no new failures) and lands after the Tibia Kino window closes.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_17 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 17u32, "2026-10-16T00:41Z", "main-thread", "20:41 main: implementation landed and blessed (pack-1o): Kino untargeted cells run on Zig, 6 Rust-only lib tests pinned, CI gh-pages in 4 repos install zig 0.17.0, Zig lib batches 1-8. Remaining: the user's commit + first green gh-pages CI with zig; then move the date_time/platform/testing rust pins to Zig (batches 7/8 make it possible) and add Zig arms for common disposable and file_system get_current_directory.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_18 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 18u32, "2026-10-16T01:38Z", "main-thread", "21:38 main: CI regression check for the Kino zig default (advisor catch): untargeted non-prelude test blocks outside lib/spiral exist in 3 files. polyglot apps/scheduler/Tasks.spi 4/4 pass on zig, but polyglot gh-pages.yml had no zig (ci-setup-zig covered 4 repos): added mlugg/setup-zig 0.17.0. dice lib/dice.spi: 3 tests fail on zig (calculate_dice_count_5/6/7 assert a failwith through _throws; Zig has no exceptions): pinned ///> rust, now 23/23. eoie src/agile_planning_rollup/planning_cli.spi: 2 tests are Rust-hosted (std macros) and eoie's runner passes no target: pinned ///> rust, 2/2. documents, chat_contract, dice_contract pass. Tibia/timeline tests are all targeted.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_19 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 19u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: user critique 21:05: the warm-cache compile time is a gimmick argument for purists wanting another default, which is why the choice is hard. Zig stays the default for runtime speed and exact integers; leaving F# behind is the real gain.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_20 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 20u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: 4 of the 6 rust-pinned lib tests now pass on Zig and lost their pins (testing assert_contains x2, platform get_executable_suffix, file_system get_current_directory). The last 2 (date_time timestamp_guid_from_timestamp and timestamp_from_guid) are now portable Spiral in the tree and their tests run on fsharp, rust, zig, python, gleam and lua: all 6 pins are gone. Remaining is the default choice itself.")
inl note_of_default_backend_decision_21 () = Note ("DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", 21u32, "2026-10-18T12:08Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:08 main-thread: Done for the interim default. Zig is the default (user 10-12/10-16: the fastest backend that beats F#, not C), and it is implemented: Kino runs a cell without a ///> target on Zig (apps/cells notebook.ex: Builders.commands(///> zig), override SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET); the compiler CLI takes its backend from the output extension, so it has no default to flip (only the legacy 3-field spiral-session compile line still means Fsharp). All 6 rust-pinned lib tests are gone. The final default waits on CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK (user 10-13), tracked there.")
inl note_of_package_junctions_1 () = Note ("PACKAGE-JUNCTIONS", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:47Z", "lane-a", "08:47 lane-a: fs bench, enumerate+read all lib/spiral 7x: direct median 851 ms, junction 813, symlink 893 (min 774/735/717): no measurable fs cost; symlinks also work here (dev mode). The reported hit likely comes from tool path resolution; next: time a Kino notebook run.")
inl note_of_package_junctions_2 () = Note ("PACKAGE-JUNCTIONS", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:49Z", "main-thread", "06:49 groom: unchanged (junction 813 ms vs direct 851 ms median); no pressure to act. P3.")
inl note_of_package_junctions_3 () = Note ("PACKAGE-JUNCTIONS", 3u32, "2026-10-18T13:11Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:11 main-thread: Done. spiral/scripts/core.ps1 EnsureSymbolicLink, the one helper dice, alphabet and polyglot init scripts call (deps/polyglot, deps/spiral, polyglot symlinks.ps1), now creates a directory Junction on Windows when the target is a directory (no admin or developer mode) and a SymbolicLink otherwise; existing links are left as they are. Probed: a fresh link is LinkType Junction and reads through. Cells mounts already used mklink /J. The 10-14 fs bench showed no cost either way (junction 813 ms vs direct 851 ms median), so no Kino timing was needed to decide.")
inl note_of_x_compat_check_1 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-CHECK", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:42Z", "lane-a", "08:42 lane-a: compiler side has no warning channel (ClientErrorsRes: 6 error kinds, 0 warnings) and edition: 3 in package.spiproj is a parse error today (verified). Design: Warnings case + edition field first; gate = equal to Kino's findings. lane-a\\measure\\x-compat-compiler-warnings.md")
inl note_of_x_compat_check_2 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-CHECK", 2u32, "2026-10-14T21:51Z", "lane-a", "Since pack-1e package.spiproj accepts 'edition: 2|3' (PACKAGE-DEFAULT-NUMERICS patch), so a compat check can key on the declared edition; the warning channel (ClientErrorsRes has 0 warning kinds) is still the missing piece.")
inl note_of_x_compat_check_3 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-CHECK", 3u32, "2026-10-15T01:54Z", "lane-a", "21:54 F# port of Kino's XCompat rules (same regexes, TLD table from apps/kino/priv/tlds.txt, same report format) at lane-a/xcompat/XCompat.fsx, the body of the compiler-side check; lib/spiral today: 377 findings in 195 files (autolink 264, cashtag 24, hashtag 64, mention 25). Next: file-by-file parity against mix spiral.x_compat on the same tree, then the Warnings case")
inl note_of_x_compat_check_4 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-CHECK", 4u32, "2026-10-15T04:15Z", "lane-a", "00:15 parity proven: the F# port (lane-a/xcompat/XCompat.fsx) and Kino's mix spiral.x_compat give the same 377 findings line for line on lib/spiral (file, line, grapheme column, kind, text): 0 only-Kino, 0 only-F#. The port is ready to become the compiler's check; the remaining step is the warning channel (ClientErrorsRes case on both Supervisor sides, host stderr printing, Zed severity), design in lane-a/measure/x-compat-compiler-warnings.md")
inl note_of_x_compat_check_5 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-CHECK", 5u32, "2026-10-15T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 groom: F# port reproduces Kino's 377 findings line for line on lib/spiral (lane A). Next grain: emit them through the compiler's warning channel.")
inl note_of_x_compat_check_6 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-CHECK", 6u32, "2026-10-15T13:38Z", "lane-a", "09:38 lane-a: compiler side built and handed to main (ships in pack-1j): lane-a/patches/x-compat-warnings.ps1 adds a shared XCompat section (Kino rules, IANA TLD table, codes XC001-XC004), one call in each BuildFile side (stderr 'warning: path:line:col: XC00n kind: text (hint)' for the entry file when SPIRAL_X_COMPAT=1 or its package.spiproj says edition: 3, once per file text) and host '--x-compat <file|dir>...' (CI report, exit 0). Gate met on the single-flight Debug candidate: --x-compat lib/spiral = Kino's 377 findings line for line, same summary (autolink 264, cashtag 24, hashtag 64, mention 25); build warnings verified on an edition-3 probe package. Left: Zed severity mapping, Kino calling the compiler instead of its own port.")
inl note_of_x_compat_check_7 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-CHECK", 7u32, "2026-10-15T22:10Z", "lane-a", "18:10 lane-a: the lane-A patch for this item shipped with main's pack-1j/1k (pack-1k review: 4,484 rows, all added rows agree, DISAGREE 0, per main).")
inl note_of_x_compat_check_8 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-CHECK", 8u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: host --check calls xCompatWarnEntry (tree Program.fs, uncommitted) and the Zed helper turns warning: path:line:col lines of the open file into severity-2 diagnostics (spiral_zed.rs warning_diagnostics, cargo test 12/12). With XC005 the warnings include overweight blocks.")
inl note_of_x_compat_check_9 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-CHECK", 9u32, "2026-10-18T12:07Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:07 main-thread: Done. Every part is committed: Kino checker (apps/xsim x_compat.ex), shared XCompat compiler section with XC001-XC005 warnings on edition-3 builds and host --x-compat for CI, host --check calling xCompatWarnEntry, and the Zed helper's warning_diagnostics (apps/zed/src/bin/spiral_zed.rs, in dec17e05 10-17 06:53).")
inl note_of_x_compat_check_10 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-CHECK", 10u32, "2026-10-18T18:08Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 14:08 live finding (x client probe): X autolinked 'x.io' (label of 1 char before a 2-letter ccTLD), so the twitter-text v3 rule in Cells' XCompat and the compiler's XCompat section (a 2-letter TLD links only when the label before it is longer than 2; xsim x_compat_test asserts x_weight('go x.io') == 7) undercounts: X weighs it as a 23-char link. Same probe: http://api.x.com/2 came back as expanded_url https://api.x.com/2")
