inl note_of_x_client_agent_json_1 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-AGENT-JSON", 1u32, "2026-10-18T05:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 01:53 done: crate xc_jsonl, README step 'Output for programs' doc-tested by the e2e, live read of NASA with --depth 1 --hints gives 5 objects (3 shown + 2 referenced)")
inl note_of_test_fails_specific_1 () = Note ("TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: Kino fixes in the tree (uncommitted): fails text matched with space runs collapsed (the compiler pads Got:), and the memo key includes the parsed fails expectation. ExUnit case added (notebook_report_test.exs); mutants fail it; full Kino suite 240 tests, 1 failure = the known private MIX_BUILD_PATH artifact. Ready to commit.")
inl note_of_test_fails_specific_2 () = Note ("TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:10Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:10 helper-lane lap 1: verified: the Kino side is committed (59b477ba: notebook.ex collapse_spaces on both sides, expectation code plus substring in the memo key, notebook_report_test.exs). Census of fails clauses still pinned only to Unification failure, all repos: tibia/src 18 (vocations 2, vitality 1, readiness 3, quantity 2, policy 1, pack_sim 5, network 2, catalog 1; tibia lane owns them, not touched) and x/apps/client/wire.spi:534 an_app_token_cannot_be_signed (x lane). The 4 other x/tibia negatives already pin Expected or key text. Remaining gap in Cells: expected_compile_error accepts any matching diagnostic anywhere in the package; nothing checks that the error span lies inside the test body. Side find: notebook.ex defp unsupported has a glued line (do    text = ...), harmless Elixir but a bad-edit scar.")
inl note_of_test_fails_specific_3 () = Note ("TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:03Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:03 helper-lane lap 2: design for the span-in-body check, from reading Cells: (1) a test cell's source is ///- options, its ///> target rows, an empty row, then the body rows (document.spi test_cell_source, line 496), and the cell's program ends with that body, so the body is the program's last k lines with k = rows after the cell source's first empty row; (2) SourceMap.remap rewrites main.spi:L:C to source:line:col but keeps the original in parentheses (source:line:col (main.spi:L:C)), and leaves it unchanged when the mapping is ambiguous, so program line numbers survive in every message. Rule: in notebook.ex expected_compile_error, scan the message's diagnostics (each starts at a main.spi:L:C location); pass only if a diagnostic whose text contains the expected substring has L greater than program_lines - k; otherwise fail with the expected text appears only outside the test body (line L). That keeps tibia's 18 bare Unification failure negatives honest without rewriting them, as long as their errors point into the body.")
inl note_of_test_fails_specific_4 () = Note ("TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:05Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:05 helper-lane lap 3: DONE with evidence (implemented, uncommitted): Cells notebook.ex now requires a fails expectation's matching diagnostic to sit in the test's own body. body_head takes the cell's first body line (after its ///- and ///> lines), body_floor finds the last program line equal to it in the SpiralError's program text (details), and in_body? splits the message at each main.spi:L:C location (kept even after source remapping) and accepts only a segment with L past that floor that contains the expected text; with no locations or no program the old rule applies. A failure located before the body gives: the expected error ... is reported only before the test body (which starts at program line N). Tests: new ExUnit case (an error at main.spi:1 from a broken definition fails, the same error at line 4 in the body passes), and the 4 stub errors of the two older fails tests moved from main.spi:3:5 (the wrap header line) to 4:5 (the body); mix test notebook_report_test.exs + notebook_test.exs: 42 passed (bounded eoie job). Real compiler check (lab/TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC/inside.spi and outside.spi through run_notebook, process compiler): the body-located Unification failure passes, the definition-located one fails. README documents the rule. tibia's 18 bare negatives keep passing as long as their errors are in their bodies.")
inl note_of_test_fails_specific_5 () = Note ("TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC", 5u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 6 17:42 (subagent): Done (status re-read; pack 9/10 installs touched none of its evidence) -> next: - | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): Done (status re-read) -> next: -")
inl note_of_hopac_docs_reread_1 () = Note ("HOPAC-DOCS-REREAD", 1u32, "2026-10-17T13:06Z", "main-thread", "09:06 main-thread fork: API inventory done against Hopac 0.5.1's Hopac.xml (424 members; gpt5parallel uses about 20). The re-read's adoption list lives in HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH; LF-derived scheduling ideas in HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING.")
inl note_of_hopac_docs_reread_2 () = Note ("HOPAC-DOCS-REREAD", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:11Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:11 helper-lane lap 1: cross-checked every Hopac API PLAN.md names against Hopac 0.5.1's Hopac.xml (~/.nuget/packages/hopac/0.5.1, 497 doc entries incl. types): all exist - Alt.choose/chooser/prepare/random, Job.start/queue/server/iterateServer, Hopac.run (and startWithActions for the D13 top-level handler), IVar.Now, Promise.Now(.isFulfilled), Scheduler.create/kill/run/wait, Stream.Src and Stream.Src.tap, Stream.debounce and Stream.buffer (both take a Stream, i.e. Promise of Cons, so the D14 HUD is Src.tap then debounce/buffer as written), Stream.mapPipelinedFun/Job (banned by PLAN line 351). No PLAN text names a member that 0.5.1 lacks. The full prose docs (Programming.md, Docs/*) are not on this machine; the re-read proper still needs them.")
inl note_of_hopac_docs_reread_3 () = Note ("HOPAC-DOCS-REREAD", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:04Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:04 helper-lane lap 2: read the Hopac 0.5.1 doc comments (Hopac.xml summaries) of the members PLAN and HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH rely on; four caveats PLAN should carry: (1) Scheduler.kill: only for a local scheduler known to be idle, so the per-build scheduler (HOPAC-DEPTH adoption 1) must Scheduler.wait for idle before kill, including on the failure path where jobs may still run; (2) Job.conCollect: not optimal for fine-grained parallel execution and raises AggregateException when several jobs fail, so D16.4's codegen-per-method should chunk methods and unwrap the first diagnostic in single-flight order; (3) Stream.Src.tap returns only the remainder after the tap, so a HUD attached mid-build misses earlier fills - D14 needs a snapshot of counts plus the tapped stream; (4) IVar.fill errors on a second write while tryFill silently ignores it: D3 fill-once should use fill (double commit becomes an error, the Alt.paranoid idea) and never tryFill. Promise.Now.isFulfilled is documented as not for polling, consistent with PLAN's ban list (line 346).")
inl note_of_hopac_docs_reread_4 () = Note ("HOPAC-DOCS-REREAD", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:41Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:41 helper-lane lap 2: DONE (lap-2 pick): re-read Hopac's prose docs (Docs/Programming.md, Docs/Internals.md) on top of the 0.5.1 reference comments, and recorded the outcome in PLAN.md itself (uncommitted): a new section Hopac 0.5.1 caveats (docs re-read 2026-10-19) before Gates every phase runs, with five bullets - Scheduler.kill only on an idle local scheduler (D5's per-compile scheduler waits first, failure path included), conCollect is coarse and raises AggregateException (D16 codegen chunks, first failure in D6 order), Stream.Src.tap sees only later events (D14 starts from a snapshot), D3 uses IVar.fill not tryFill, and choose's left bias plus withNack's lazy instantiation (as the G2 ban list and D16.2 assume) with the Gleam caveat that selectors follow mailbox order (fixes D15's table row claiming selector order; HOPACSOTA-P7). Everything else in PLAN matched the docs: deterministic left-biased choice, <*> must be safe both sequentially and in parallel with a measured sequential cutoff (Phase 3), server GC (already on: SpiralCompiler.fsproj ServerGarbageCollection true), and blocked jobs costing no thread.")
inl note_of_hopac_docs_reread_5 () = Note ("HOPAC-DOCS-REREAD", 5u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 6 17:42 (subagent): Done (status re-read; pack 9/10 installs touched none of its evidence) -> next: - | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): Done (status re-read) -> next: -")
inl note_of_ci_single_pipeline_1 () = Note ("CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE", 1u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: done in the trees, not committed: spiral gh-pages.yml gains schedule (17 6 * * *) and workflow_dispatch (suite input) with a compiler job (ubuntu, 360 min, zig 0.17.0 added, oracle artifact) running scripts/workflow.ps1 -Compiler -Suite; the build job skips schedule; compiler.yml removed. eoie: eoie.yml removed, gh-pages.yml (ubuntu + windows, rust 1.88.0 + rustfmt, setup-beam, zig) runs scripts/workflow.ps1, whose init.ps1 clones spiral as a sibling the way dice clones polyglot and runs spiral init-app-build.ps1 + mix deps.get; the eoie.yml steps became Invoke-Block calls. polyglot gh-pages.yml gained setup-zig. Next: the user's commit, then the first runs.")
inl note_of_ci_single_pipeline_2 () = Note ("CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night audit of the committed HEADs: one workflow file per repo, every build job runs pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1. The last deviation (x ci.yml xc-windows building xc and checking --help inline) moved into x/scripts/workflow.ps1 -Xc, tested locally; uncommitted in x. Every job of every repo now delegates to scripts/workflow.ps1; done once pushed and green.")
inl note_of_ci_single_pipeline_3 () = Note ("CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE", 3u32, "2026-10-18T12:08Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:08 main-thread: Done. Audit of the committed HEADs: spiral, eoie, dice, alphabet, polyglot, i574n.github and tibia each have only gh-pages.yml, and x has only ci.yml; every run step is pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1 (x's Windows job calls workflow.ps1 -Xc, committed in a85e58a). Green status is tracked in PIPELINES-GREEN.")
inl note_of_suite_map_1 () = Note ("SUITE-MAP", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night map: harness 6,906 rows (examples 6,073, contracts 724, frontier 104, mega 5); rows and native-ran per backend: F# 967/0, C 519/493, Zig 457, TypeScript 447, Python 444, Kotlin 444, Swift 443, Lean 443, Gleam 443, Wasm 441, Lua 439, Rust 427/415, Delphi 414/402, Bend 401, Ebpf 52, Wgsl 50, OpenQasm 31, Calyx 28, Cpp 16/12. Spiral test blocks: spiral/lib 375, apps/tibia 185, x 48, dice 32, polyglot 24, blog 21, eoie 11, alphabet 2, kino 0. ExUnit: Kino 240, x sim 30. eoie hand-written Rust tests: 25 files, 85 fns (vendor excluded).")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_1 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: XC005 adds more X constants to the shared section (280, weight ranges, 23 per link); they belong in the postability profile with the TLD list.")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_2 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 2u32, "2026-10-18T17:15Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:15 P3 -> P2: user 2026-10-16 asked that X-specific things hardcoded in shared tooling move to or be parameterized from the x repo; the compiler section belongs to the compiler lane")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_3 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 3u32, "2026-10-21T01:46Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 21:46 x-lane backlog refactor: assignee pair. The x half authors the postability profile in the x repo (the 280 budget, weight ranges, 23 per link, the defused-dot rule from splitter.spi and the TLD list now in Kino priv/tlds.txt, as one data file both the compiler and Cells read). The compiler half is main's: XCompat reads the profile instead of constants. 240m covers both halves; the x half goes first so main codes against a real file")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_4 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 4u32, "2026-10-21T02:03Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 22:03 x half delivered (uncommitted): x/postability/x.json (profile x, version 1: post weight_limit 280 and link_weight 23; code_point_weight default 2 with the four weight-1 ranges 0-4351, 8192-8205, 8208-8223, 8242-8247; links: domain_pattern, top_level_domains_file tlds.txt, short_domains_always_linked t.co, two-letter TLDs link only when the second label is longer than 2; entities hashtag/mention/cashtag; posting: block, header c{block} {k}/{n}, one-post budget header, defused dot rule [A-Za-z0-9_-] . [A-Za-z] with U+200B and +1 weight, block_limit formula) plus x/postability/tlds.txt. Checked: the four regexes are byte-equal to spiral_compiler.fs xCompat*Pattern, and the TLD list equals both the compiler's xCompatTopLevelDomains and apps/cells/priv/tlds.txt (1,286 entries, 0 differences), so either hardcoded copy can be replaced by the profile with no behavior change. Compiler half (main): XCompat and Cells read the profile path instead of constants")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_5 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 5u32, "2026-10-21T02:04Z", "main", "22:04 main received x's half: C:\\home\\git\\x\\postability\\x.json plus tlds.txt (uncommitted). It covers the 280 limit, 23 per link, the code-point weight ranges, the domain/hashtag/mention/cashtag regexes, t.co, the two-letter TLD label rule, the posting header and the defused-dot block budget. x verified that all four regexes are byte-equal to the compiler's xCompat*Pattern literals and that the TLD list equals xCompatTopLevelDomains and cells/priv/tlds.txt (1,286 entries, 0 differences). Compiler half pending (main): read the profile instead of the constants, with no behavior change; it lands after the postable-budget install that X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT waits on.")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_6 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 6u32, "2026-10-21T02:42Z", "main", "22:42 compiler half written: lab/X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE/profile.ps1 (dry run clean on the evening pack core). It replaces the 170-line XCompat region (TLD list, regexes, 280, weight ranges, 23 per link, header and defused-dot budget) with a PostabilityProfile record loaded from JSON with System.Text.Json and cached per path. Discovery: SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE, else the nearest postability/*.json walking up from the checked file, so x/apps/client finds x/postability/x.json and needs no flag. A repo without a profile gets no platform rules (platform-neutral core); only x/apps/client declares edition 3 and nothing else sets SPIRAL_X_COMPAT, so no other lane loses checks. The host --x-compat and xCompatWarnEntry resolve the profile per file. Gate chained after formatter-c9b (it edits the tree host only while nothing else builds): --x-compat finding lines must be identical to the budget-only candidate on x client HEAD copies and the current client, and 0 on dice.")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_7 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 7u32, "2026-10-21T05:45Z", "main", "2026-10-21 01:45 main: compiler half gated on the exact evening pack (core A9A71772B249FB8B, Debug candidate x-profile, host restored byte-for-byte): with x/postability/x.json as the profile, --x-compat finding lines are identical to the budget-only candidate on the x client HEAD copies (29 findings, 27 files) and on the current x client found by location (0 findings, 36 files); dice, which has no postability profile, now gets 0 findings (budget-only reported 396: autolink 222, hashtag 10, overweight 164), so X rules apply only where a profile says so. Installs with the evening pack; x gets told to rerun --x-compat after the install.")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_8 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 8u32, "2026-10-21T07:20Z", "main", "2026-10-21 03:20 main: compiler half INSTALLED with the evening pack at 03:19 (tree core A9A71772B249FB8B, host with profile lookup). x told; x's round4 includes the client build with --x-compat (expect 0).")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_9 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 9u32, "2026-10-21T08:49Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-21 04:49 compiler half landed in main's evening pack (03:19): --x-compat takes its rules from the nearest postability/*.json (x/postability/x.json for the x client) or SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE, and a repo without a profile gets no X findings; verified on x: 0 findings in 36 files on the installed compiler (x-cellid-round4). Cells' own XCompat still carries its constants (the Cells side of the profile is what is left, if main keeps it in scope)")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_10 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 10u32, "2026-10-21T08:53Z", "main", "2026-10-21 04:53 main: confirmed by x on the installed pack (round4, 04:4x): the x client reports 'x-compat report: 0 finding(s) in 36 file(s)' with rules from x/postability/x.json. Cells on the same compiler: 34 of 35 notebook files pass, including the cell_id slice. The one failure, exsss gleam tests hitting the 300 s cell timeout under load, passed on the old compiler tonight; x reruns it on a quiet machine. x marks XC005-POSTABLE-BUDGET done on its side. Left here: the platform-neutral naming of the profile fields and a second profile (a non-X platform) to prove the core holds no X knowledge.")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_11 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 11u32, "2026-10-21T10:41Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-21 06:41 x side: x/postability must be committed: since the evening pack the compiler finds rules only in the nearest postability/*.json (or SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE), and a repo without one gets no X findings, so a missing profile would make x's --x-compat gate pass vacuously. x.json trimmed to the 17 keys spiral_compiler.fs postabilityProfileOfFile reads (dropped version, posting.top_level_block, posting.header, posting.block_limit, defused_dot.inserted_before_dot, which nothing reads); tlds.txt stays, the profile names it by path. Also folded apps/sim/frames/.gitignore into apps/sim/.gitignore (the compiler host creates the output folder, so a clone without frames/src is fine)")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_12 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 12u32, "2026-10-21T11:04Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-21 07:04 done (X-DONE deliberation 1 rule): x delivered x/postability/x.json + tlds.txt (trimmed to the 17 keys the compiler reads), main's evening pack made --x-compat read it (0 findings on the x client, round4), so spiral core no longer hardcodes X for the compiler. The Cells half moved to the main-owned CELLS-POSTABILITY-PROFILE")
inl note_of_x_compat_parameterize_13 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE", 13u32, "2026-10-22T02:23Z", "x", "2026-10-21 22:23 x: lab assimilated. spiral/lab/X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE/profile.ps1 (the compiler half's patch) was checked against today's compiler sources: its replaced region and both call-site edits are present (xCompatFindings profile ... in the warning loop and the --x-compat file loop; postable weight from profile.weightLimit minus the profile's header), and the old call texts are gone. Folder deleted. Status back to Done.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:15Z", "main-thread", "23:15 main: review. History: run_target (polyglot e8e47636, 2023-10) emitted both arms into one F# file under #if FABLE_COMPILER_RUST / #else, so its API is a union target plus a function of it; when the compiler gained BackendSwitch it was re-implemented on lib backend_switch but kept that API and a 7-case union (Gleam Lua Fsharp Cuda=Python Cpp Rust TypeScript). Consequence: a run_target '| _ =>' arm never reaches Zig, C, Lean, Wasm, Bend or Delphi (they hit backend_switch's per-backend no-arm placeholder), which is why 33 is_zig () guards appeared (14 lib files) next to 45 is_rust (); usage 10-15: run_target family ~150 uses in 21 files, backend_switch records 217 in 30, direct BackendSwitch 4. A Zig case in the union would break every exhaustive run_target match, and GADTs or prototypes do not help because the backend is a compile-time constant, not a value to refine.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 2u32, "2026-10-16T03:15Z", "main-thread", "23:15 main: benchmark (job tmp/dispatchbench: generate.ps1 + run-batch.ps1, 400 generated functions per style, one warm --batch process, first-compile ms, medians over 4 rotated style orders; Rust column = parse + typecheck + peval, Zig/C = peval + codegen): run_target 1,955 / 1,135 / 851; is_zig guard 2,899 / 618 / 1,294; record listing all 14 keys 3,263 / 1,734 / 1,437; record with only the needed keys + a default (backend_switch_or) 1,920 / 941 / 838. Decision: backend_switch_or (fallback) { needed keys } everywhere: cost-neutral or better than run_target, its default really covers every backend, every arm still validated, no union, no guards; listing every key is the slowest form and is avoided. Grain 1 done: backend_switch_or added to lib/spiral/backend.spi, platform.is_windows migrated (is_zig guard + run_target -> one record with Rust/Fsharp/Gleam/Zig + failwith default): Rust/F#/Zig/Gleam outputs byte-identical, C changes from the uncompilable backend_switch_has_no_C_arm placeholder to a runtime failwith, platform.spi Kino test 1/1. Next: the 33 is_zig guard sites file by file with the lib sweep (lane-b lib-sweep.ps1) before/after, then the standalone run_target sites; run_target and its union are deleted when the last caller is gone.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 3u32, "2026-10-16T04:20Z", "main-thread", "00:20 main: grain 2 done in the tree: all 32 is_zig () guard chains in lib/spiral (am', base, common, console x9, crypto, env, file_system x5, guid x2, listm', sm' x4, testing, trace x3) rewritten by tmp/dispatchbench/guards-to-records.ps1 into inl otherwise () = <old else branch, unchanged> + backend_switch_or otherwise { Rust/Zig keys from the old branches }; is_zig () now has 0 callers outside backend.spi. Needed by hand: type annotations on 4 sites (record arms are separate fields, so a bare macro arm is a value-restriction error; a typed_arm helper does not work because Spiral generalizes the local otherwise) and Kino's cell console.spi gained CppHost/C/Delphi/Wasm/Bend keys (lib records validate all 14 keys, and inside a Kino cell console.write_line is Kino's 9-key switch). Proof: probe programs for 15 migrated functions x 8 backends (tmp/dispatchbench/probes.ps1): 118 of 120 outputs byte-identical, same 91 compile; the 2 differences are substring on C (old: is_rust () evaluated to the backend_switch_has_no_C_arm placeholder as a C bool; new: the real default) and file_delete on Zig (extra unused trace globals, BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK). Lib sweep of the 13 touched notebooks 126/126 before and after; harness rows of the 7 lib-using samples 53/53 parity with the oracle, DISAGREE 0; Kino mix test 239 (1 known env-only failure); Tibia combat.spi 7/7. Semantic lesson: a record arm is evaluated (for its type) under every backend's context, an if-guard branch never was, so code moved into a default must be valid under every key. Not done: apps/spiral regeneration; the standalone run_target sites (~100) and the 45 is_rust () uses (step 3).")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 4u32, "2026-10-16T04:48Z", "main-thread", "00:48 main: design revised with evidence. Converting the ~100 run_target matches to records would need a type annotation on every arm (records make each arm a separate field; matches unify their arms), so the scalable unification is the other way round: complete union backend (+ Zig, C, Lean, Delphi, Wasm, Bend; run_target_args' maps every BackendSwitch key), so every | _ => really reaches every backend and Zig gets | Zig => arms; the 24 step-2 records whose default is a run_target fold back into it (tmp/dispatchbench/fold-zig-arms.ps1). Proof in a lib copy: probes 120/120 identical to the unfolded union version, 113/120 identical to the current tree, the 7 differences all C rows moving from backend_switch_has_no_C_arm placeholders to the _ default; only base.spi's test-only exhaustive match needed a | _ arm. Cost (400 generated functions, 4 rotations, machine loaded by a concurrent review): the complete-union run_target is +28% (Rust), +16% (Zig), +15% (C) over today's 8-key run_target, and +28/+24/+38% over a record with a default. Decision: complete union for value-level dispatch (correctness on the Zig default and every backend outweighs the cost); records stay for type-level switches and keyed defaults; is_rust/is_zig go. Applying to the tree after the pack-1p review frees the lib, with a sweep of every run_target notebook.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 5u32, "2026-10-16T04:49Z", "main-thread", "00:49 main: is_rust () decision: the ~30 standalone uses in lib (async, resultm, threading, date_time, am', parsing, runtime, convert, wasm, testing, file_system) stay. They are plain two-way if splits, not guards in front of a run_target; a static if evaluates only the taken branch, so it compiles cheaper and avoids the every-backend evaluation trap that the Kino console hit. Rule: a guard in front of a dispatch is drift and becomes an arm (done for all is_zig sites); a standalone binary predicate is allowed.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 6u32, "2026-10-16T07:18Z", "main-thread", "03:18 main: final design applied to the tree lib (14 files from the proven copy tmp/dispatchbench/libunion; backups tmp/rotation/lib-before-union): union backend has every key, run_target_args' maps each, lib dispatch is run_target matches, backend_switch_or removed (no code references left). AGENTS.md lib dispatch paragraph rewritten (guard chain in front of a dispatch = drift; standalone is_rust () predicate = fine). Lib sweep on the installed pack-1p DLL: 14 of 38 notebooks pass (am', crypto, guid, parsing, physics, runtime, sm', testing, trace, rust/testing, ...) before a memory reap; the remaining 24 run after the pack-1q probes.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 7u32, "2026-10-16T08:58Z", "main-thread", "04:58 main: lib sweep of the tree lib (complete-union design) on the installed compiler: 38/38 notebooks pass, 329 tests, 0 failed (02:41-03:08 first 19 before a memory reap, 177 tests; 04:47-04:58 the other 19 on pack-1q, 152 tests: date_time 32, seq 26, listm' 22, stream 16, file_system 15, math 14, ...). Harness: the pack-1q review shows 0 residual changes on every non-Kotlin/Swift backend, so the rewrite is output-neutral on all harness samples. Left: a Kino mix test on the tree lib, and the kino console.spi switch (records with every key) is fine as is.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 8u32, "2026-10-16T10:07Z", "main-thread", "06:07 main: Kino mix test on the tree (union-design lib, installed pack-1r compiler): 239 tests, 0 failures, 1 excluded (06:04-06:07). All proofs in: probes 120/120 vs the fold, harness 0 residual changes on non-Kotlin/Swift backends (pack-1q review), lib sweep 38/38 notebooks 329 tests, Kino 239/0. Done; the 14 lib files + backend.spi + AGENTS.md paragraph are ready to commit.")
inl note_of_x_client_token_refresh_1 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-TOKEN-REFRESH", 1u32, "2026-10-18T05:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 01:53 implemented (crate xc_token, net xc_post_form) and stub-tested: the e2e stub serves /2/oauth2/token with single-use refresh tokens; 3 README steps (renew, spent token refused with 400/exit 4, renew again from the rewritten file); in-place write keeps the file's ACL and CRLF/LF endings; not yet run live (the live call spends the single-use refresh token, waiting for the user's ok)")
inl note_of_x_client_token_refresh_2 () = Note ("X-CLIENT-TOKEN-REFRESH", 2u32, "2026-10-18T06:05Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 02:05 live run on the user's go: xc refresh ~/.secrets/x-api.env renewed both tokens (expires_in 7200), file ACL unchanged, all 9 keys kept in order; the new access token reads /2/users/me with 200 as @fc1943s")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_1 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:58Z", "main-thread", "23:58 main: repro: file_system.file_delete as a backend_switch_or record; on Zig the validated default arm calls trace Critical (Rust path), and trace's own Zig arm registers spiralHold/GetLocalTime/spiralClock globals, which then appear in the Zig output although nothing uses them (probe tmp/dispatchbench probes-before/after file_delete main.zig). AGENTS.md says validation pushes into a throwaway statement list and its own variable counter; globals are not covered. Fix direction (shared section, both cores): snapshot the globals table before validating each non-current arm and restore it afterwards, like the statement list; expect residual-only shrinkage on rows whose records reach global-emitting code. Harmless today (unused definitions), but it makes records emit more than if-guards did.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_2 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 2u32, "2026-10-16T05:16Z", "main-thread", "01:16 main: fixed in pack-1p (tree core, both cores): backend_switch_validation_floor names the 1 <<< 30 validation counter base, and the 4 Global sites (single-flight term Global; gpt5parallel term Global, nested term Global, worklist replay, raw unit annotation) record a global only when s.i.contents < floor, i.e. outside any validation, nested evaluations included. Review: 0 harness rows changed by it. Probes on the guard-migrated lib: 17 outputs lose only unreferenced leaked globals (SpiralUse in Rust, std_* type aliases in F#, unused Gleam imports, spiralClock/spiralHold/GetLocalTime in Zig, spiral_node_fs/spiral_trace_state in TypeScript); file_delete Zig is byte-identical to its pre-migration output again. Install + bless pending.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_3 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 3u32, "2026-10-16T05:51Z", "main-thread", "01:51 main: fix withdrawn from pack-1p. The corpus check on the candidate failed lib file_system 12/15 (get_source_directory, get_full_path, trace_file: dotnet fsi failed): the suppressed globals included F# type aliases (type std_path_PathBuf = string, std_string_String, Ref) that F# code uses. They come from backend.rust_type -> fsharp_global, called inside Rust arms; in an F# build those arms are only validated, and the nested fsharp_global switch then runs its Fsharp arm with s.backend = Fsharp = env.backend. The compiler comment at the Global site documents this as intended (F#-only declarations run in every build). So the 'leak' is load-bearing for F#. Real fix: make rust_type's F# alias come from the type use (the nominal's F# side) or from a declaration evaluated outside arms, then suppress globals in validation; until then the unused extra globals (Zig spiralClock etc.) stay. Status back to Planned.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_4 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 4u32, "2026-10-16T06:35Z", "main-thread", "02:35 main: correction: the withdrawn change did alter one harness row, portable_convert_parse Gleam residual (review 003349 80ff3f0a vs blessed d09c5594, exit and verdict equal). Lib file_system F# is 15/15 on the installed pack-1p without it.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_5 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 5u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night design conclusion: validation must keep emitting nested Fsharp globals (backend.rust_type F# aliases are needed in F# builds), so the fix is dead-global pruning after codegen, not suppressing globals during validation. Measured: 0 leaked helpers in the 457 sample Zig outputs (only prelude and union constructors are unreferenced) and 0 of 1,551 Rust fns; the leak only shows through lib code (trace), so the check must be a lib Kino probe.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_6 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 6u32, "2026-10-18T12:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:19 main-thread: implemented in the tree (not built yet), the dead-global pruning from the 10-17 design, on the final text so it is core-independent: UnusedGlobals.prune (shared CodegenUtils section) runs in both cores' build helper on every backend's output. A global is dropped only if it starts with a keyword declaration (fn/func/fun/function/def/const/let/var/type/struct/class/enum/interface/module, optional pub/export/local/static/inline/async), its declared name occurs nowhere else in the output, and it does not head a multi-part global; removal repeats to a fixpoint. Imports, includes, attributes, comments and C static definitions are always kept, so F# aliases that F# code uses stay. Expected effect: residual-only shrinkage on lib rows (Zig spiralClock/spiralHold/GetLocalTime, unused F# std_* aliases, unused Rust helpers); 0 behavior change. Verification: build + full review vs bless, after the current bless.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_7 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 7u32, "2026-10-18T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:30 main-thread: rule tightened before review (git-0e found that a rust_global blob starting with a private fn whose callers sit in the same blob next to pub fn API would have been dropped whole): a candidate must also be a single item (only the declaration and closing braces at column 0) with no pub/export token anywhere; extern \"lib\" fn declarations count. Probe on the installed first build: file_system.file_delete on Zig loses spiralClock, spiralHold and GetLocalTime (144 lines), zig build-exe -fno-emit-bin ok; 6 lib-backed Zig samples byte-identical in size. git-0e will run the eoie regeneration with -CargoCheck after the install as the cross-crate check.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_8 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 8u32, "2026-10-18T14:04Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:04 main-thread: Done. Installed in pack 2 (all 3 modes 08:30-08:44) and reviewed: single-flight-20261018-084525 vs bless 061211 has DISAGREE 0 and no residual change from pruning on any harness row (the 13 residual-only rows are git-0e's extended multiline_triple_string); the lib leak probe (file_system.file_delete on Zig) drops spiralClock, spiralHold and GetLocalTime and still passes zig's semantic check; git-0e's eoie regeneration on this compiler with -CargoCheck: 139/139 owners byte-identical, every pub fn and multi-item rust_global blob kept.")
inl note_of_rust_union_impls_1 () = Note ("RUST-UNION-IMPLS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: tag half done in staging: pack-c/rust-lazy-tag.ps1 stores each union tag impl and emits it only when a UnionTag op uses it. All 427 Rust rows: 0 behavior cells, 127 residuals; tag impls 194 -> 4 (the 4 are stale outputs, SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS), main.rs -30,353 bytes. Default half: 478 impl Default in 210 files, of which only 57 are reachable from a ::default() use (array creation roots, closure over nested defaults incl. RefCell-wrapped ones; no untyped or derived uses exist), so reachability-based emission drops 421 (88 percent): staged as pack-c/rust-lazy-default.ps1 verified on all Rust rows: 0 behavior cells, 201 residuals, exactly 57 impls left, -52 KB; not yet in a full review. In x/apps/client all 55 Default impls and all tag impls are unused: view.rs 1,885 -> 1,663 lines without them (still over the gate, the rest is RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION), args.rs 995 -> 853. Pack: night pack 10-17 (scripts %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\packs\\night-1017, each with -DryRun; final review single-flight-20261017-053315 on the exact pack: 6,906 rows, DISAGREE 0, residual-only Zig 457 and Rust 415, 3 intended Rust exit cells (shown as 2 NATIVE-DIFF rows); builds in single-flight, hopacsota and gpt5parallel; hopacsota inline and random:23 equal single-flight on every compile and residual cell; corpus 144 of 144; Zig at C speed on the bench).")
