inl note_of_erl_crash_dumps_in_tree_1 () = Note ("ERL-CRASH-DUMPS-IN-TREE", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:14Z", "main", "22:14 found 4 dumps (headers decoded, not kept): (1) spiral/erl_crash.dump, 368 KB, 10-19 11:03, OTP 26 load_failed during boot: a bare erl or mix run from the spiral root picked C:\\ProgramData\\chocolatey\\bin\\erl.exe, which still resolves first on PATH ahead of scoop (the kino-mix-erlang-path problem, still live; PATH and the choco install are the user's machine settings); untracked and NOT ignored, so git status showed ?? erl_crash.dump. (2) spiral/apps/cells/erl_crash.dump, 4,224 KB, 10-20 04:38, OTP 29: boot-time EXIT whose report could not be printed because standard_error no longer existed (the launching shell was gone, as when the reaper kills a background shell), so the VM dumped into its cwd; hidden by apps/cells/.gitignore line 7 (stock mix new template). (3) spiral/target/build/cube/gleam/erl_crash.dump, 2,075 KB, 10-07, OTP 28 undef cube@@main:run from an old gleam cube run, in build output. (4) x/apps/sim/erl_crash.dump, 4,398 KB, 10-18 18:19, same lost-stderr crash as (2), hidden by apps/sim/.gitignore line 3; x's repo, reported to x. Fix (spiral, uncommitted): apps/cells/spi/run_notebook.ps1 and run_suite.ps1 set ERL_CRASH_DUMP to spiral-bin/crash-dumps/erl_crash-<stamp>-<pid or label>.dump before starting mix (probe: erl halt with the variable set wrote the dump there and none in cwd); erl_crash.dump removed from apps/cells/.gitignore so any stray dump shows in git status. Dumps (1)-(3) deleted after this note.")
inl note_of_erl_crash_dumps_in_tree_2 () = Note ("ERL-CRASH-DUMPS-IN-TREE", 2u32, "2026-10-22T02:15Z", "x", "2026-10-21 22:15 x: same fix in x. apps/sim/build.ps1 (every Erlang VM x starts goes through it: gleam build, mix deps.get, xsim.world/frames/events, mix test, the corpus and Cells xsim.run, thread_run) sets ERL_CRASH_DUMP to (LOCALAPPDATA ?? HOME)/spiral-bin/crash-dumps/erl_crash-xsim-<stamp>-<pid>.dump after putting scoop OTP first on PATH, so it also works on the Linux CI runner. The erl_crash.dump line is gone from apps/sim/.gitignore. The 10-18 dump (4.4 MB, OTP 29 boot crash, standard_error gone) is deleted. Probe from apps/sim with the same lines: erlang:halt(\"probe\") wrote its dump to spiral-bin/crash-dumps, nothing went to the working dir; the probe dump is deleted. Not changed: the lab ladder script (lab is being drained; the real ladder will run through the sim build entry).")
inl note_of_spiral_lib_am_any_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:42Z", "eoie", "22:42 found by the EOIE-LIB-REUSE pilot (agile_task_query tag_listed through am'.any over sm'.split of the tags): Spiral typechecks, rustc fails at main.rs 'v3.any(|x| v4(x))'. Fix in lib/spiral/am'.spi (iterate the vector: borrow().iter().any) with a lib test block, then a Cells --gate run (lib edits). eoie works around it with sm'.index_of meanwhile.")
inl note_of_spiral_lib_am_any_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", 2u32, "2026-10-22T04:15Z", "main", "00:15 main fix in lib/spiral/am'.spi (uncommitted for the user): any was a Rust-only macro, source.any(|x| fn(x)), but array_base on Rust is an Rc RefCell Vec (ArrayIndex emits a.borrow()[i as usize].clone()), and .any is an Iterator method, hence E0599; no tracked caller existed (grep of all repos), eoie's lib pilot was the first. Now portable Spiral: a let rec loop over index_base up to the array length, true on the first element fn accepts. New test any (rust, fsharp, typescript): init 5 has an element equal to 3 and none above 10. Gate queued after the night install (job main-am-any-gate, lib_gate.ps1 on am'.spi).")
inl note_of_spiral_lib_am_any_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", 3u32, "2026-10-22T13:47Z", "eoie-lane", "09:47 eoie team: status check. Main's 00:15 fix is in the live lib (am'.any is a let rec loop over index_base, no Rust .any call), so the row's Planned 0% is stale. There is no job named main-am-any-gate in the local job registry, so I can't confirm the gate run from here, and the status stays main's call. Dependency to know: eoie release G (E's heal wave) maps rust_std_string pipe_member to sm'.listed, which is built on am'.any, so G's healed crates need this lib fix. Last night's scratch previews of those crates compiled after it landed. Committing the lib change before G installs keeps eoie's committed sources buildable from a clean checkout.")
inl note_of_spiral_lib_am_any_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", 4u32, "2026-10-23T10:55Z", "main", "2026-10-23 06:55 main: its own gate never ran (the 00:15 plan's job was never queued). Queued now as main-am-any-gate after the rustls check: lib_gate on lib/spiral am'.spi with the daemon retire floor at 1024 MB, raw output in runs/lab-outputs/SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY. Rule: the 'any' test (rust, fsharp, typescript) passes and 0 type errors, then Done.")
inl note_of_spiral_lib_am_any_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", 5u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming, corrected 00:04: main-am-any-gate finished 0 at 22:24 on the LIVE lib's am'.spi (50DC2DF31AFB1910): 12/12 tests, 0 type errors. The fix itself has been in the live lib since 10-22 00:15 (note 1), so there is nothing to land. The 23:08 'land next' reading was wrong.")
inl note_of_spiral_lib_am_any_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", 6u32, "2026-10-24T04:04Z", "main", "2026-10-24 00:04 main: Done by note 4's prewritten rule (the 'any' test passes and 0 type errors): 12/12 on the live lib, 0 type errors.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_1 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 1u32, "2026-10-22T03:23Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:23 x: evidence. (1) x's sim Gleam: one entry = 9,093 lines; per-export entries don't help (6 entries = 19,809 lines, 2.2x, each over 1,000 but one; SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES note 7); 60% of a package is anonymous method specializations (110 in xsim_world, 18 of 60+ lines). (2) dir-tree-html's generated dir_tree_html.rs is 2,774 lines (TREE-PAGES-V2). (3) x's Rust crates sit at 909-986 of the 1,000 gate (view 986), held there only by splitting crates by hand. So the 1,000-line rule for generated code needs the compiler to emit modules; entry-level splitting duplicates shared code. Consumers waiting: SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES, X-GATE-ALL-LANGUAGES, TREE-PAGES-V2 (f).")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_2 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 2u32, "2026-10-22T03:34Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:34 x: feasibility measured on the generated file itself (text-only, call graph from fn definitions and name( calls in xsim_frames.gleam): 9,093 lines = 293 fns (8,853 lines) + 240 lines of types, consts and imports; 608 call edges; Tarjan SCCs: 290 components, the largest 2 functions / 280 lines, none over 1,000. So the graph is almost acyclic, and Gleam's no-import-cycles rule is no obstacle: a topological packing gives 14 modules of <= 1,000 lines with types repeated per module, or 10 function modules plus one shared types module. Caveat: function values passed rather than called (closureN references) are not counted as edges; the compiler sees the exact graph.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_3 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 3u32, "2026-10-22T03:47Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:47 x: executable spec for the Gleam half: x/scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 (SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES note 8) splits generated Gleam after the fact. Types and constructors go to <module>/types; functions are packed by call-graph SCCs, callee-first, into <module>/partN; the entry's exports stay in <module>; each module imports only the names it uses, unqualified. On xsim_frames: 12 modules, largest 957 lines, gleam build 0 errors, cold build about 30% faster (8.7-9.3 s vs 11.6-15.7 s). In the compiler the same split is cheaper (the call graph is exact, including function values), and the Rust half is simpler: modules of one crate may reference each other cyclically, so each part file needs only 'use super::*;' and pub(crate) items, with no SCC ordering. That would also remove x's crate headroom pressure (view 986/1000) and split dir_tree_html.rs (2,774).")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_4 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 4u32, "2026-10-22T06:41Z", "main", "02:41 main: design grounding from the compiler code (read only, no build). (1) The core already emits several files per build. BuildOk carries a list of {code; file_extension}, and the supervisor's BuildFile handler (spiral_compiler.fs ~187068) writes each as <source base><extension> through write_output_atomically_async, answering only after every file is written; CppCuda (4 files) and PythonCuda (2) use it today. (2) Every single-file backend goes through one function, `build codegen backend file_extension` (~184577: codegen, then UnusedGlobals.prune, then one GeneratedFile). That is the hook: after prune, a GeneratedOutputSplit stage for Rust and Gleam returns [main] or [main; main_part1; ...] by extension suffix (e.g. '_part1.rs'), so no supervisor change is needed. (3) The host is the real gap: compileOne (tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs ~498-622) knows multi-file outputs only through a STATIC multiFileSuffixes table (CppCuda, PythonCuda), waits for those suffixes to land and copies them to the output base, and the compile cache skips multi-file backends. A variable part count needs the core to write a manifest <base>.spiral-parts (one suffix per line) before answering; the host reads it in place of the static table and copies or cleans parts (stale main_partN files from a previous build must be deleted). (4) Language side: Rust parts become `#[path = \"main_part1.rs\"] mod part1;` with `use super::*;` and pub(crate) items, so cycles need no ordering (x note 23:47). Gleam parts are modules of the same package with x's SCC callee-first packing (x/scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 is the executable spec); a part's imports are only the names it uses. (5) Gate: split only above 1,000 lines, so every matrix row under the gate stays byte-identical, and the review must show only rows over 1,000 lines changing. Consumers: Cells (copies one output), x sim, dir_tree_html.rs (2,774 lines), x crates near 986/1,000.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_5 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 5u32, "2026-10-22T07:22Z", "main", "03:22 main: two findings that shape the Rust half. (1) A split by top-level items cannot shrink a fully inlined residual. The largest sample main.rs (mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/runtime_native, 4,638 lines) is one enum plus spiral_main spanning lines 9-4634, straight-line lets with no nested fns, so module parts would move nothing. The split helps method-rich outputs (x's sim: 293 fns, the largest SCC 280 lines). Fully inlined bodies need join points in the source (a `let` instead of an `inl`; eoie found the same lever, 'union reflection lookups must be let, not inl') or a later codegen pass that outlines long straight-line runs into helper fns. The gate's per-file report should say which case a file over 1,000 lines is. (2) Rust module mechanics, by Rust's privacy rules: each part is a child module, `#[path = \"main_part1.rs\"] mod part1;`, opening with `use super::*;`. A child sees its ancestors' private items and their private `use` imports through the glob, as `mod tests { use super::*; }` does. Items moved into a part become `pub(crate)` (a column-0 fn/struct/enum/type/const/static/impl-free item; impls need no visibility and can live in any part), and the root adds `pub(crate) use part1::*;`, so the root and the sibling parts see them. Cycles between parts are fine (no ordering), crate attributes (#![...]) stay in the root, and codegenRust emits no macro_rules (0 in the core), so nothing depends on textual order.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_6 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 6u32, "2026-10-22T07:23Z", "main", "03:23 main: note 5's module mechanics VERIFIED with rustc (edition 2021, a 3-file crate in TEMP, compile window): the root has a private 'use std::rc::Rc', a private fn root_helper, '#[path = ...] mod part1/part2' and 'pub(crate) use partN::*'. part1 calls root_helper and uses Rc through 'use super::*' and calls a part2 fn; part2 calls part1 back (a cycle); the root calls part2. It compiles clean and exits 6 as computed. So moved items need only 'pub(crate)'; root-private items and imports stay where they are.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_7 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 7u32, "2026-10-22T07:52Z", "main", "03:52 main: core half drafted and the algorithm validated without a compiler build. Patch: %TEMP%\\rust-module-split\\patch.ps1 (dry run by default; 3 anchors, each matching once on the tree and PACK-AFTER-NIGHT cores). It adds module RustModuleSplit in the shared CodegenUtils section next to UnusedGlobals, and edits both sides of the BuildFile pair (gpt5parallel ~184578, single-flight ~187286) so pair-drift stays clean. Rules: split only above SPIRAL_RUST_SPLIT_LINES (default 1000). Move only free functions ('fn' / 'pub fn' heads, never 'fn main'), because Rust struct fields and impl methods are private to their module and the root could not reach them from a part. Types, impls, thread_local!, use lines and crate attributes stay in the root. Moved fns get pub(crate); parts open with 'use super::*;' and are packed in order up to the limit; the root declares '#[path = \"<base>_partN.rs\"] mod partN; pub(crate) use partN::*;' where the first moved fn was. Validation (simulate.ps1, a faithful PowerShell port; rustc -O in a compile window): mega_brzozowski_derivatives/runtime_bench (2,051 lines, 39 fns) becomes a 48-line root plus parts of 917/764/331 lines, compiles, exits 0 with stdout identical to the unsplit build; rust_static_closure_chain at a forced 100-line limit (6 parts) compiles and exits 40 like the original. REQUIRED host half (not drafted): BuildFile fills its IVar with every file's code joined by newlines, and for single-file backends compileOne (host Program.fs ~589-613) awaits or writes THAT text to the output path, so a split Rust main.rs would be overwritten by the concatenation. Fix: the core also emits a '.spiral-parts' manifest (one suffix per line) when it splits; compileOne reads it and takes the multi-file branch with those suffixes (copy every part to the output base, skip the compile cache), and deletes stale <base>_partN.rs and manifest files when an output is not split.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_8 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 8u32, "2026-10-22T07:53Z", "main", "03:53 main: the host half is drafted too, so the Rust split is complete on paper. Core patch: a split also emits '<base>.spiral-parts' (one part suffix per line). Host patch %TEMP%\\rust-module-split\\host-patch.ps1 (dry run; 2 anchors, each matching once on the tree's Program.fs). In compileOne, a fresh manifest (written during this build) routes the output into the existing multi-file branch with suffixes [.rs; parts; .spiral-parts], which waits for every part to land and copies them to the output base. The compile cache skips split outputs (its text would be the concatenation). When an output stops being split, a stale manifest at the source base and at the output base deletes exactly the files it lists, never a name pattern, so a user's own main_partN.rs is never touched. Gate plan for its pack: SPIRAL_RUST_SPLIT_LINES=100 over the Rust matrix rows, so most outputs split and every row keeps its native verdict; default limit: only outputs over 1,000 lines change; then x's crates and dir_tree_html.rs.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_9 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 9u32, "2026-10-22T08:10Z", "main", "04:10 main: measured on today's generated dice_contract.rs (3,106 lines, 129 top-level fns; largest closure7 451, method34 313, method29 237, closure76 231, closure4 197 lines). GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES' Rust split (simulate.ps1, limit 1000) gives a 126-line root plus parts of 895/980/873/244 lines: every file under the gate, with no hand-split crates. Not rustc-checked, because the contract needs near-sdk; the two std-only samples compiled and behaved identically (GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES note 7). A third consumer besides x's crates and dir_tree_html.rs.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_10 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 10u32, "2026-10-22T08:16Z", "main", "04:16 main: third rustc proof, and the first on a real tool. x's renderer dir_tree_html.rs (2,774 lines, 30 fns, std only) splits into a 33-line root plus parts of 993/682/378/700 lines. Whole and split both build with rustc --edition 2024 -O, and running each on apps/compiler/tmp/formatter gives byte-identical HTML (hash BC560C04A05C, 4,749 bytes, exit 0). TREE-PAGES-V2 (f) can use the compiler split instead of a hand crate split.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_11 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 11u32, "2026-10-22T11:49Z", "x", "2026-10-22 07:49 x: review of x/scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 as the spec for the Gleam half (read only). (1) Its edges over-approximate: any word that names a top-level fn counts as a call, which also covers function values passed rather than called. Parts are packed in Tarjan output order (callee-first) with every edge counted, so imports only point to earlier parts, types, or from the root to parts, and Gleam's no-import-cycle rule can't break. The compiler's exact graph keeps this as long as function-value references count as edges. (2) Exports and their transitive callers stay in the root, so part code never imports the root. (3) A false edge (a local variable or string word equal to a fn name) costs at most an unused-import warning. (4) Gap: a const whose value references a fn is not an edge; consts go to types.gleam, which imports no fn module. (5) Rust lesson from x's splitter, a PowerShell switch -Regex fall-through: an item matched by two kind rules was written twice ('fn main' in root and part, hidden by glob-import shadowing). The compiler split should assert that every top-level item lands in exactly one file (x checks non-blank line conservation).")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_12 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 12u32, "2026-10-23T12:35Z", "main", "2026-10-23 08:35 main: consumers waiting on this (each over 1,000 generated lines): dir-tree-html dir_tree_html.rs 2,774 (TREE-PAGES-V2 step f), dice ui 16,281 and alphabet documents 15,315 (EOIE-CRATE-GATE reports them OVER), and x's client crates (X-RUST-MODULES, interim script). The interim splitter's tested shape is root = types, uses and pub fns; parts = private fns with 'use super::*' and pub(crate); every non-blank line kept once. Its cargo check and byte-identical render on dir-tree-html are the acceptance test for the compiler version.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_13 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 13u32, "2026-10-23T12:52Z", "main", "2026-10-23 08:52 main: correction to my 08:35 note. The Rust half already exists in the pending pack's core: module RustModuleSplit (shared section; limit SPIRAL_RUST_SPLIT_LINES, default 1,000) is called from build for every .rs output in both cores. It moves private fns into <stem>_partN.rs ('use super::*', pub(crate)), adds '#[path] mod partN; pub(crate) use partN::*;' to the root, and writes a .spiral-parts manifest, which the host (Program.fs splitParts) copies beside the output and later cleans up. The installed tree core (25D2BE7C) lacks it (0 matches), which is why dice ui/src/main.rs (16,281 lines, generated 10-19) and alphabet apps/documents/documents.rs are still single files. Rule: after the 18AC install, regenerate those two tracked outputs with the installed compiler, check that every file is <= 1,000 lines with EOIE-CRATE-GATE, and the user commits the parts. dir-tree-html's 2,774-line output splits the same way, so TREE-PAGES-V2 (f) closes on regeneration. Left for this item: the Gleam half (modules of one package, callee-first packing, consts in types).")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_14 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 14u32, "2026-10-23T13:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:08 main: Gleam half implemented and proven, staged as pack-next7. (1) Core: %TEMP%\\pack-next7\\gleam-module-split.ps1 adds module GleamModuleSplit beside RustModuleSplit, an F# port of x's scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 (the spec named in note 4). splitWith takes a comparer, roots, module names and code. Byte check: with culture order and x's nested names it is byte-equal to the script on all 4 modules of both 1,000+ line matrix outputs (mega_brzozowski_derivatives inventory_closed_dfa_runtime 1,881 lines and runtime_native 1,362). The core uses ordinal order and flat sibling modules (<base>_types, <base>_partN), so the host's copy-beside-output works; roots are main plus the !!!!Export names in the entry .spi, as in x's script; SPIRAL_GLEAM_MODULE_PREFIX handles nested module paths; limit SPIRAL_GLEAM_SPLIT_LINES, default 1,000. Hooked in both cores' build next to the .rs split, and the patched core parses under both defines. Splits: 1,881 -> 110 + 86 + 803 + 937; 1,362 -> 135 + 57 + 952 + 258. (2) Plumbing: %TEMP%\\pack-next7\\gleam-parts-plumbing.ps1. Host Program.fs: the parts manifest names the output's own extension, not just .rs, and stale-part cleanup also runs for Gleam. test.ps1: Copy-GleamProgram copies the parts listed in .spiral-parts and renames part imports to the batch row module, both Gleam paths use it, a part-file build error is attributed to its row, and the native memo signature covers it (Gleam rows rerun once). 9 anchors match. (3) End-to-end in the matrix's Gleam shim: split and unsplit builds give equal exit and stdout on both samples, with the same warning count (10/10, 12/12, so no unused-import warnings), and the batch-renamed copy (spiral_row_0001_part1/2/types) builds. Review: next codegen pack after tibia's block. Expected residual changes: only those 2 samples' main.gleam (now a root + 3 new files each), with every Gleam row agreeing with C.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_15 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 15u32, "2026-10-25T00:06Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:06 main: the Gleam half was drift, and it entered here. Note 4 took x's scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 as the spec; that script closed SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES on 10-22 against the 1,000-line gate, while the user had asked for proper parallel-buildable packages with their own toml. x reopened SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES and made XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH P1 (packages vs parts, packages win unless they need compiler work). The title now names the asked-for shape. There is also a real bug: GleamModuleSplit.usedImports (pack 5i, line 162530) keeps an import in a part only when the part mentions '<last path segment>.'. lib emits 'import gleam/dynamic.{type Dynamic}' (sm'.spi last_index_of_gleam), so that import is dropped and a part using Dynamic unqualified fails to compile, the same break as X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS. The matrix missed it because no Gleam row over 1,000 lines reaches that lib function. Rule, applied: patching the filter would ship the drifted shape, so the Gleam split comes out of the install. %TEMP%\\pack-next5j\\no-gleam-parts.ps1 removes both build call sites (anchors 1/1, dry run clean). Gleam output stays one file and reports OVER honestly, and the Rust split stays. The Gleam half resumes with the benchmark's shape. If that is packages, a later pack deletes the module; if parts, it returns with import handling for '.{...}' and 'as' forms plus a regression sample.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_16 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 16u32, "2026-10-25T00:18Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:18 main: the premise is superseded (user 10-24 20:1x via x: splits belong at codegen as a granular crate/package graph, never post-hoc parts; 'verify if we really need this custom automatic splitting thing so we don't keep dead code'). Measured: no installed compiler writes parts (RustModuleSplit/GleamModuleSplit count 0 in the tree core; they exist only in pack 5i), so nothing regresses if both go. The consumers over 1,000 lines (dice ui 16k, alphabet documents 15k, dir_tree_html 2.7k, the mega samples) are already reported OVER today, and all have the same cause: one entry with everything inlined. Pack 5j drafted: %TEMP%\\pack-next5j\\no-output-parts.ps1 removes both split modules (253 lines, headings checked) and both build branches, and leaves no reference to the splits or .spiral-parts in the core. host-no-parts.ps1 removes the host's manifest reader and stale-parts cleanup (14 lines, apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs) and leaves multiFile unchanged. Both dry-run clean. Paused behind X-PARALLEL-BUILD (codegen-level crate/package graph) and XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH; the remedy for big outputs is granular entries at the source.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_17 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 17u32, "2026-10-25T00:21Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:21 main correction to note 16 (found by x): 'nothing depends on the split' was wrong. The tree core counts 0, but the matrix's committed outputs depend on it: 29 tracked part and manifest files in 6 samples (user commit 0ec501fb, 10-24 05:02), written by a candidate core that had the split. 5j's install step now re-accepts those rows as single files and deletes the 29 files by the samples rule (COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS note 15, corrected). My check counted references in the compiler source and never asked git what the outputs held; the outputs of a compiler change are tracked files too.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_18 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 18u32, "2026-10-25T00:30Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:30 main: folded into SPIRAL-SHARED-SPECIALIZATIONS (x), so one record holds the fact. x's research (i574n.github/docs/research/parallel-builds-2026-10-24.md) contradicts this item's 20:0x title. Gleam 1.19 compiles packages one at a time: verified in project_compiler.rs, where compile_dependencies() is a plain 'for name in sequence' over toposort_deps with no threads. Only the BEAM step is parallel, across the modules of one package. So many modules in one package is Gleam's parallel shape, and every extra package is a barrier. Rust is the opposite: crates grouped by sharing unit, with modules inside. The successor lays out one target module per Spiral source module at codegen and emits shared specializations once. The post-hoc splitters are removed by pack 5j; their install steps live in COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS notes 15-16. The title is marked SUPERSEDED because eoie agile has no superseded/cancelled status (reported to eoie), and Done here does not mean delivered.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_19 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 19u32, "2026-10-25T00:46Z", "x", "10-24 20:46 x: the research file note 18 cites (i574n.github/docs/research/parallel-builds-2026-10-24.md) is deleted (user: no new docs dirs in repos). Its sources are now X-PARALLEL-BUILD notes 5 (Rust) and 6 (Gleam and other generators).")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_20 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 20u32, "2026-10-25T00:53Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:53 main groom, lessons: (1) 'nobody uses it' needs two checks, references in the source AND tracked outputs (git ls-files): 29 committed sample outputs depended on a split no installed compiler had. (2) A build tool's parallel unit differs per backend: Gleam 1.19 compiles packages sequentially (project_compiler.rs compile_dependencies, a plain for over toposort_deps), Rust parallelizes crates. 'Packages' only means parallel for Rust. (3) Patching a drifted design's bug (the import filter) would have hardened the design; removing it waits for the measured shape instead.")
inl note_of_eoie_predicate_lex_dedupe_1 () = Note ("EOIE-PREDICATE-LEX-DEDUPE", 1u32, "2026-10-22T07:38Z", "eoie", "03:38 found while grooming EOIE-CORE-OPERATORS: predicate_lex/model.spi lines 1-15 (length at cut append starts space letter word) are byte-identical to predicate_lex_core/model.spi lines 1-15; predicate_lex's own boundary wrappers (token literal encode skip word_end canonical, host calls into predicate_lex_domain / predicate_literal_domain) use none of them. Consumers: predicate_rewrite_model calls predicate_lex.model.{length,append,cut,at,starts}; developer_metadata_domain opens predicate_lex.model wholesale (names used unknown until compiled); eoie_dev_model and eoie_predicate_lift reference neither. Shape: point those calls at lib sm' where semantics match (length, index, slice, concat, starts_with_at need a byte-for-byte check: these wrap the OLD core sm, ASCII bytes), else at predicate_lex_core; delete the copies. letter/word stay as documented !!!!LTE char exceptions (lib sm'.char_is_alphanumeric is Rust-only and Unicode, not a drop-in; an ASCII char predicate in lib would be main's call). Needs a compile preview of the 4 consumers + both domain crates.")
inl note_of_eoie_predicate_lex_dedupe_2 () = Note ("EOIE-PREDICATE-LEX-DEDUPE", 2u32, "2026-10-22T08:23Z", "eoie", "04:23 slice apply-predicate-lex-dedupe.ps1, dry-run clean on a composed copy (live + D + E): asserts predicate_lex lines 1-15 still equal predicate_lex_core's, deletes them from predicate_lex, and points the callers at predicate_lex_core: developer_metadata_domain (3 unqualified uses: cut, length, at, made qualified) and predicate_rewrite_model (qualified length/append/cut/at/starts plus letter, which the first census missed; the slice's own guard caught it). Generated code stays identical (same definitions). Composed into release E. Lib sm' replacement of these ASCII helpers stays a separate, semantics-checked step.")
inl note_of_eoie_predicate_lex_dedupe_3 () = Note ("EOIE-PREDICATE-LEX-DEDUPE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T19:25Z", "eoie", "15:25 kit stale-Active audit: landed. The live predicate_lex/model.spi holds only its crate boundary: six one-call wrappers (token, literal, encode, skip, word_end, canonical) into predicate_lex_domain and predicate_literal_domain, and none of its first 15 lines duplicate predicate_lex_core. Closing. The lib sm' replacement of the ASCII helpers stays with EOIE-LIB-REUSE as noted.")
inl note_of_eoie_cli_harness_adopt_1 () = Note ("EOIE-CLI-HARNESS-ADOPT", 1u32, "2026-10-22T08:24Z", "eoie", "04:24 from the duplicate-type census: planning_cli.spi (agile_planning_rollup, 24 local helper defs in 81 lines, 2 tests) and todo_cli.spi (agile_todo_model, 17 defs in 61 lines, 4 tests) each carry a private copy of the CLI test harness (fixture_root, their own cli_run with succeeded instead of code, fixture and run helpers), while eoie_cli_harness/cli.spi is the shared harness every other CLI notebook uses (///- --package ../eoie_cli_harness). Move both notebooks onto the shared harness: drop the private defs, map succeeded to the harness's succeeded/shows helpers. Test-only, no generated-code effect; verification needs the two notebooks run against a candidate binary, so it rides the next release's notebook phase.")
inl note_of_eoie_cli_harness_adopt_2 () = Note ("EOIE-CLI-HARNESS-ADOPT", 2u32, "2026-10-22T09:05Z", "eoie", "05:05 mapping for todo_cli.spi (first of the two): its fixture_root/cli_run types, cat, contains, fixture_root_create, fixture_path, fixture_write, fixture_root_remove and expect have harness equivalents in eoie_cli_harness/cli.spi; its todos runner becomes eoie_run (fixture_text_of root) over agile todos args, and shows keeps its meaning through the harness's succeeded/shows (code 0 instead of a succeeded flag). The harness lacks hides (stdout must not contain), fixture_binary (write 3 raw bytes) and fixture_git_init (git init -q): add those three to the harness so every CLI notebook can use them, then todo_cli keeps only mixed_tree and its tests. planning_cli.spi follows the same shape. Verification is running both notebooks against a candidate binary, so it rides a release's notebook phase.")
inl note_of_eoie_cli_harness_adopt_3 () = Note ("EOIE-CLI-HARNESS-ADOPT", 3u32, "2026-10-22T13:35Z", "eoie-lane", "09:35 eoie team: drafted (apply-harness-adopt.ps1 + harness-adopt/, in the lab night folder; dry-run clean). The harness gains three generic helpers: fixture_binary, fixture_git_init, and fixture_slashed (the harness keeps native backslashes in fixture roots, while todo_cli's private copy forced '/' and its tests assert '/a.spi:2'; todos now gets the slashed root). todo_cli keeps only marker/marked, a todos runner over eoie_run and mixed_tree. planning_cli keeps its agile_call union, task rows and a let rec tab_split from tab-joined arguments to cli_args (a runtime string recursion must be let, not inl), with accepted = shows (succeeded run) and rejected = the harness's failed_with. 41 private helper defs drop to 9. It lands in the release after E2 (test-only, no generated-code effect) with both notebooks in that release's candidate list, kept out of G so G's 23-notebook phase carries no extra risk.")
inl note_of_eoie_cli_harness_adopt_4 () = Note ("EOIE-CLI-HARNESS-ADOPT", 4u32, "2026-10-25T19:26Z", "eoie", "15:25 kit stale-Active audit: landed. agile_planning_rollup/planning_cli.spi and agile_todo_model/todo_cli.spi both open with '///- --package ../eoie_cli_harness' and 'open cli', with 0 private fixture_ helpers. todo_cli keeps exactly marker, marked, todos and mixed_tree; planning_cli keeps its agile_call union and row helpers, as the 10-22 draft planned. Closing. The harness package itself becomes kit_cli_check in KIT-RENAME.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 1u32, "2026-10-22T11:04Z", "eoie", "07:04 user 07:0x: 'double check if all these eoie processes are valid or stuck. and you still haven't fixed them being created without a parent'. Checked 07:02: 5 parentless supervisors, all valid (heartbeat <= 3 s): x-precommit-night3 and main-fmt-round6 running in the two heavy slots, tibia-d33-w8700 running predict.exe, tibia-d33-w11900 queued --after w8700, main-pack-review2 first in the slot queue (holding a heavy-build window while it waits: the window-before-slot waste ABC fixes, not installed). Cause, unfixed until now: eoie_jobs/host.spi supervisor_launched spawns each supervisor with DETACHED_PROCESS | CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP | CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB (0x0100_0208) so it survives the caller's job object, and the calling eoie exits at once, so every job is an orphan root that polls the queue on its own. ABC's mitigation (slot first, 5 s waits) only reduced polling and is not even installed. Fix design: one eoie job host per machine, the only detached process. job start writes requests/<id>.start under the jobs home and starts the host if its host.pid is dead; the host spawns each supervisor as its own child (no detach flags), writes the supervisor file, reaps exited children, and exits after 10 idle minutes. Placement: new crate eoie_job_host (launch.rs is at 897 lines); supervisor_launched becomes request + ensure host + wait (<= 5 s) for the supervisor file. Acceptance rule, written before coding: on the installed binary, three job starts leave exactly one parentless eoie process (the host) with three supervisors whose parent is the host; killing the calling shell does not kill the jobs; the host exits within 10 minutes of the last job ending; job status, stop and wait behave as before.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 2u32, "2026-10-22T11:20Z", "eoie", "07:20 implemented and in release DH (job-1792667959540-eoie-release-DH, queued for a heavy slot): new crate eoie_job_host (host_model.spi: host_step/host_claim/supervisor_wait unions with 3 Spiral tests, 3/3 green in place; host_run.spi: request handoff through jobs/requests/<id>.start, host.pid singleton, supervisors spawned as the host's children with CREATE_NO_WINDOW, children reaped with try_wait, idle exit after 10 min, 500 ms poll in ONE process instead of one poller per job). supervisor_launched in eoie_jobs/host.spi is now one call into the host; if no host takes the request within 5 s and the request is still on disk, the old detached start is the fallback (a request the host already took is never started twice). New verb: eoie job host (internal, like supervise). Generated sizes: host_run.rs 365, launch.rs 897 -> 891, jobs.rs 735 -> 745. Acceptance runs inside the release after install (3 short jobs, supervisors counted by parent).")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 3u32, "2026-10-22T11:54Z", "eoie-lane", "07:54 eoie team: a gap found by reading DH before it ships. CLI fixtures set EOIE_JOBS_HOME to a temp folder, so each test that starts a job now leaves a host process that idles for 10 minutes after fixture_root_remove deleted its home. Fix drafted for release F (scratch apply-host-home-gone.ps1): host_step_of takes a host_home union (HomePresent | HomeGone), and a host whose jobs home is gone exits as soon as no supervisor child runs. Test blocks cover both cases. Acceptance for F: after the CLI notebooks run, no eoie host process has a missing EOIE_JOBS_HOME (process command lines checked).")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 4u32, "2026-10-22T11:54Z", "eoie-lane", "07:54 eoie team: checked the install path against a long-lived host. Publish-EoieBinary retires the installed exe to LOCALAPPDATA\\eoie\\retired instead of overwriting it, so a running host never blocks an install. After an install, an old host keeps serving new requests, and each request file names the requester's own executable, so supervisors always start from the new binary. Rule for later releases: the request format (file name <id>.start, content = executable path) is the host protocol and stays backward compatible; a format change must make the requester detect an old host (host.pid alive but request untouched after 5 s → the detached fallback already covers it).")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_5 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 5u32, "2026-10-22T12:03Z", "eoie-lane", "08:03 eoie team: DH's host never starts (measured on the candidate: job start takes 5.5 s, host.pid is never written, the supervisor comes from the detached fallback; each candidate fixture's job ids sit 5.3 s apart). Cause: supervisor_requested reads a missing host.pid as 0, and eoie_jobs' process_alive returns true for pid 0 by design, so 'host alive' holds and no host is launched. Running 'job host' by hand (original and copied exe) serves fine. Fix (scratch apply-host-zero-pid.ps1, first slice of release F): host_state_of (recorded) (alive) : host_state (HostMissing | HostRunning pid) treats pid 0 as missing, with the test a_missing_host_pid_file_starts_a_host. Decision by rule (a release ships when it breaks nothing and its known regression is latency only): DH continues and installs, because ABC+D work and the host gap only costs +5 s per job start on the old detached path. Stopping DH now would delay tibia's block and ship nothing. F acceptance adds a job start under 1 s with host.pid written, plus supervisors parented by the host.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_6 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 6u32, "2026-10-22T22:22Z", "eoie-lane", "18:22 finding from F's candidate host probe (release log: 'candidate host after its home was removed: alive=True'). The probe deletes its jobs home and expects the host to see HomeGone and exit. But on Windows the host runs from its supervisor copy inside that home (<home>\\supervisor\\eoie-<n>.exe, locked while running), so Remove-Item leaves the folder, directory_exists(jobs_root) stays true, and host_step_of never sees HomeGone. The model test a_host_whose_jobs_home_was_removed_exits_once_its_children_end passes, but the real path is unreachable on Windows; the host (pid 22744 here) only exits through the 10-minute idle limit (~18:29). Fix options for the next job-host slice: (a) keep the supervisor copy outside the jobs home (a sibling cache folder keyed by binary hash), or (b) let HomeGone mean 'the requests folder is gone', which the probe's Remove-Item does delete. (b) is a one-line change in home_of; (a) also stops a removed home from leaving a stray exe. Rule for choosing: whichever keeps the existing model tests and makes the probe report alive=False within 2 s.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_7 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 7u32, "2026-10-22T22:24Z", "eoie-lane", "18:24 chose option (b) by the note-6 rule (it keeps every model test, and the probe's Remove-Item does delete the unlocked requests folder; the host creates that folder at start, host_run.spi:59, so there is no startup race). Slice apply-job-host-home.ps1 is one line in home_of; it applies to the live and GE2+H trees and is chained into apply-rH, with eoie_job_host added to H's regen filter (job-script-check ok). H's existing candidate host probe then shows whether the host exits within 2 s of its home's removal (alive=False).")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_8 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 8u32, "2026-10-22T22:28Z", "eoie-lane", "18:28 the slice is now an eoie do program (scratch job-host-home.do, one anchored edit with expected counts) in place of the PowerShell script; apply-rH runs it with the tree as the do root and throws on a nonzero exit. Dry runs on copies of the live and GE2+H trees: ok, 1 file, 1 replacement, 2-3 ms. The first slice converted under the eoie-first habit change (EOIE-EDIT-BATCH note 18).")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_9 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 9u32, "2026-10-22T22:30Z", "eoie-lane", "18:30 confirmed: the probe host (pid 22744) exited on its own at ~18:29, ten minutes after its last child, by the idle rule and not by HomeGone. Afterwards the probe home (with its no-longer-locked supervisor copy) deletes cleanly. This matches note 6's analysis.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_10 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 10u32, "2026-10-22T22:38Z", "eoie-lane", "18:38 evidence from main's CPU audit for the user (18:36; the user named the parentless-process issue): during F's candidate tests, 6 of 7 job-host processes (eoie-*.exe \"job host\" under %TEMP%\\eoie spiral test *\\jobs\\supervisor) had dead parents. They came from the tests job stop, job kill on close and window slot within, ~8 MB and 0 CPU each, and exited minutes later (a leak, not load). %TEMP% holds 174 \"eoie spiral test *\" folders. Same root cause as note 6: fixture_root_remove cannot delete a home whose supervisor copy is running, so HomeGone never fires and every fixture host waits out the 10-minute idle limit. The note-7 slice (in H) makes a fixture host exit within ~0.5 s of its fixture's removal. The leftover folders need a one-time sweep of folders that no running process uses, once H installs; their ownership spans every lane's eoie test runs, so it is not swept now.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_11 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 11u32, "2026-10-22T22:42Z", "eoie-lane", "18:42 parentless-process audit at 18:41 (Win32_Process, parent pid not alive): all 9 live eoie job supervisors (eoie-18058752-1792611528.exe \"job supervise ...\" for main, tibia and eoie jobs, 7 MB each) have no parent. That is the installed 10-21 binary detaching every supervisor, the original bad-manager issue, and it persists because no release carrying the job host has installed yet (DH 08:10 host-start bug, F 17:09 Cargo dependency, F 18:29 stale CLI test). Every other parentless process is a normal Windows or user app (explorer, csrss, Edge, OneDrive, SystemInformer, mariadb), none of them eoie. F2 (started 18:40: eoie_job_host with the home_of fix regenerated, then F's steps) installs the host. Acceptance after install, already in F's script: three host-accept jobs whose supervisors must be parented by the pid in host.pid. Then this audit is rerun: new supervisors must show the host as parent; supervisors started before the install keep running detached until their jobs end.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_12 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 12u32, "2026-10-22T22:48Z", "eoie-lane", "18:48 priority P1 -> P0. The user at 18:36: 'i'm asking for days for you to fix these process issues'. This item is the fix (one host as the parent of every supervisor), and it is not installed: DH, F and F failed before install today. F2 (job-1792708845541, started 18:40) carries it with the fixture home fix; acceptance is F's host check plus the post-install parentless audit.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_13 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 13u32, "2026-10-22T23:09Z", "eoie-lane", "19:09 disk side of the supervisor design: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\jobs\\supervisor holds 14 copies (410 MB), one per eoie binary ever installed or tested since 10-21, and only one is in use (eoie-18058752-1792611528.exe, the installed binary's). Rule, written before deleting: a copy is removed only if no running process executes from it and it is not the installed binary's copy (a job start recreates a missing copy). Follow-up for a compile slice: the host prunes stale copies itself when it starts (keep the current one plus any in use), so this never accumulates again.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_14 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 14u32, "2026-10-22T23:10Z", "eoie-lane", "19:10 applied the note-13 rule: 13 stale supervisor copies removed (393 MB freed); kept only eoie-18058752-1792611528.exe (in use by every running supervisor and matching the installed binary). F4's install adds one new copy at its first job start.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_15 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 15u32, "2026-10-22T23:34Z", "eoie-lane", "19:34 live check during F4's stop_cli run: the requests-folder fix ends most fixture hosts (their hosts are gone), but not all. The 'job kill on close' fixture kept its requests folder and its host (36888) stayed idle past 2 min, because fixture_root_remove is a single std::fs::remove_dir_all, which aborts at the first error. When it reaches the locked jobs\\supervisor\\eoie-*.exe before requests, requests survives. The other fixture folders also stay on disk with their 44 MB copies (residue). Root fix drafted: apply-supervisor-cache.ps1 moves supervisor copies from <jobs root>\\supervisor to one machine-wide cache %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\supervisor (same size+stamp keys), so no jobs home ever holds a locked file. Removal then completes, hosts see HomeGone, no residue stays, and fresh homes stop copying 44 MB (the 13 s cold start). A grep of generated Rust shows the copy code only in eoie_job_host\\host_run.rs, so it is a one-crate regen like F2's. Plan: a small follow-up release right after F4 if time allows, else first thing next session.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_16 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 16u32, "2026-10-22T23:45Z", "eoie-lane", "19:45 F4 stopped at 19:43: its candidate stop_cli failed the same two tests as DH (killing_only_the_supervisor_takes_the_whole_job_tree_down, after_waits_for_the_named_job...). DH blamed the host-start gap, but the host starts fine now, so that diagnosis was incomplete. Kill's failure, read from code and output ('stop 18692 stopped 0 of 1 processes'): process_table's kill terminates the pid, waits 200 ms, and counts survivors by presence in a fresh sysinfo snapshot. Under the job host, the host keeps a Child handle per supervisor and sweeps exited children only every 500 ms (live_children retain on try_wait), so a terminated supervisor stays listed as a zombie and is counted as a survivor. Fix applied to the tree (apply-kill-zombie-aware.ps1): a survivor is a pid whose handle (SYNCHRONIZE) is not signaled within 2 s. process_alive was already zombie-safe (WaitForSingleObject), so --after needs its own diagnosis; its panic was cut from the log. Also applied: apply-supervisor-cache.ps1 (copies in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\supervisor, not per jobs home). Verify job job-1792712662424 (19:44): regen eoie_process_table and eoie_job_host with cargo check and publish, debug build, then stop_cli against that candidate; both failure messages are printed.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_17 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 17u32, "2026-10-23T00:12Z", "eoie-lane", "20:12 residue measured and swept under main's 18:4x rule (idle over 30 min, no live process using the path): 23 'eoie spiral test *' folders from today's F/F4 candidate runs, 994 MB, mostly 44 MB supervisor copies (one per fixture home). The supervisor-cache slice (in the tree, verified in the running verify job, installing with F5) stops new ones: one shared copy, nothing locked in fixture homes.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_18 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 18u32, "2026-10-23T02:33Z", "eoie-lane", "22:33 F6 candidate phase: 9/9 notebooks pass, including stop_cli (killing a supervisor takes the job tree down; --after and --after-ok behave), so the zombie-aware kill works under the host. F6 is in its spiral tests at 22:32; the release build, install and host acceptance follow. Answered main for the user: after the install, supervisors are children of the single job host (pid in host.pid), which is the one detached root by design. Old-binary supervisors stay parentless until their jobs end (no re-parenting on Windows), so queued jobs should be requeued after the install. The Cells compiler daemon is out of scope (spiral's, detached on purpose).")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_19 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 19u32, "2026-10-23T03:11Z", "eoie-lane", "23:11 live evidence for the supervisor-cache fix: F6's candidate binary (shared %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\supervisor cache), with the probe run standalone in a fresh home: warm-up start 392 ms and probe start 458 ms (cold starts with a per-home 44 MB copy took 518-13569 ms today); supervisor 35964 parented by host 124332; host exited after the home's removal; no probe-home residue left. So all three fixes work together on the new binary: parentage, fixture-host exit, no leftover folders. GE2/H/I release scripts now carry this validated probe (replacing the old cold-start 3000 ms gate, which would have failed them like F2) plus a persistent regen -TargetDirectory (spiral-bin\\eoie-regen-target).")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_20 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 20u32, "2026-10-23T05:39Z", "eoie", "01:39 eoie: install path tonight (details on EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN notes 95-98). F6 was stopped at 23:51: it couldn't finish its notebook batch inside its 4 h limit. F7 failed in 13 s on my out-of-range test timeout. F8 (job-1792728931011, 00:15) is running: cargo tests passed, notebook batch at 27 of 81 at 01:35, install expected ~03:15. Found on the way and fixed for GE2: the host passes its own environment (EOIE-JOB-ENV) and its creator's BelowNormal priority to every job. Close rule unchanged: after the install, post-F's parentless audit shows every supervisor started after the install parented by the host, and host-hygiene leaves a Normal-priority host.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_21 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 21u32, "2026-10-23T06:38Z", "eoie", "02:38 eoie: third host-inheritance problem, measured. The host inherits its caller's working directory. CLI-harness fixtures run eoie with the fixture root as cwd, so the detached host pinned that folder: fixture_root_remove failed ('being used by another process'), the home and its files stayed, and the host lived until it idled out. Evidence: 20 'eoie spiral test ...' homes left in TEMP by F6's and F8's notebook runs; one (job after, 02:19) still had live host 66396. Direct probe with the debug candidate: start a job from inside a folder, then remove the folder: refused while the host lives. Fix for GE2 (job-env/apply-host-neutral-cwd.ps1): the host starts in the temp directory (.current_dir(temp_dir()) in its launch). Supervisors still give each job its own recorded cwd. stop_cli gains a_fixture_home_whose_job_host_ran_can_be_removed. Chained in launch-GE2; GE2's prelaunch is clean with all five slices. Together with EOIE-JOB-ENV, the host now sets three things itself: environment per job, Normal priority, and a neutral cwd.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_22 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 22u32, "2026-10-23T07:06Z", "eoie", "03:06 eoie: fourth inheritance, measured. The host also inherits its creator's job object. The host launch asks for CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB (0x0100_0208), but eoie's own job containers set only KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE (0x2000), not BREAKAWAY_OK. Inside an eoie job the breakaway fails, and the fallback (0x0000_0208) starts the host inside the caller's job. When that job ends, the host and every supervisor under it die. Probe (scratch job-env/nested-host-check.ps1, debug candidate): an inner job started from an outer job ends 'lost, supervisor stopped beating' 2 s after the outer finishes. This is x's 23:37 nested-start report (EOIE-JOBS), now in its host form. Tonight it would have hit F9 itself: its acceptance step runs inside its own job right after the install. F9 (queued, not started) was patched to boot the host first through WMI Win32_Process.Create, which was measured to land outside the caller's job, at Normal priority, with the user's default environment, parent WmiPrvSE. Fix for GE2: job-env/apply-container-breakaway.ps1 (limits[2] = 0x2800, so the host breaks away while ordinary children stay in the job), plus nested-host-check as a blocking probe in GE2's job; it fails on today's candidate (positive control). GE2's prelaunch is clean with six slices.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_23 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 23u32, "2026-10-23T07:48Z", "eoie", "03:48 eoie: correction to note 22. F9's WMI host boot is reverted (F9 still queued, so the revert counts). A process created by Win32_Process.Create gets the WMI service's environment: system PATH only, without the user PATH where scoop shims live. Measured: creating 'pwsh' through WMI returns 9, path not found, while 'ping' (System32) worked. A WMI-booted host would therefore have handed every later job a system-only PATH until GE2's environment fix, breaking jobs that run a bare pwsh or cargo. F9 keeps its plain acceptance step: that host dies with F9 seconds later, and post-F's host-hygiene starts a fresh host from my agent shell (outside any job, Normal priority, the user's environment, TEMP cwd). GE2's nested-host probe still runs through WMI, now with pwsh's full path on both the WMI command and the probe's outer job, so it measures from outside GE2's own container. Tested: launches, captures its verdict, and FAILs on today's candidate as the positive control.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_24 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 24u32, "2026-10-23T08:01Z", "eoie", "04:01 eoie: fifth inheritance, measured: handles. This is the cause of the long-standing 'eoie job start keeps the pipe' hang (memory eoie-job-start-pipe). PowerShell passes every inheritable handle to its native children, so eoie inherits its caller's redirection pipe as an extra handle, and Rust's Command spawn passes all inheritable handles on to the host. The host then holds the caller's pipe open until it exits. Reproduced: an outer pwsh -File ... *> file whose script started the first host returned only when the host died (47 s). The probe (scratch job-env/pipe-release-check.ps1: outer redirect, 20 s limit) FAILs on today's candidate. An earlier probe shape (direct call in Start-Job) passed and missed it, so it was replaced. Fix for GE2 (job-env/apply-host-no-inherit.ps1): on Windows the host launch is a CreateProcessW with bInheritHandles 0 and no std handles, plus NORMAL_PRIORITY_CLASS, TEMP cwd and breakaway with fallback (one block replacing Command::spawn); unix keeps Command with process_group(0). GE2 blocks on the probe ('host-handles'). A first draft that only cleared the inherit flag on the standard handles was deleted: the pipe arrives as a non-standard inherited handle.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_25 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 25u32, "2026-10-23T09:02Z", "eoie", "05:02 post-install plan check: host-hygiene starts the fresh host with 'eoie job start' from the after-F8 shell. Measured: agent tool shells are not in a job object (IsProcessInJob false, pid 134312), so that host survives its shell, the low-memory reaper included. Until GE2's no-inherit launch, the host holds that shell's output pipe, so the background task stays live until the host idles out. after-F8.ps1 now ends with 'after-F8 chain done <time>', and a separate file watcher on that marker gives the completion signal.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_26 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 26u32, "2026-10-23T09:11Z", "eoie-lane", "05:11 install probe (EOIE-JOB-HOST): PASS. host.pid in the probe home: '113260'")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_27 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 27u32, "2026-10-23T09:27Z", "eoie", "05:27 verified live after F9's 05:06:57 install: acceptance 3/3 supervisors parented by the host; post-F audit at 05:16, 4 supervisors started after the install and 0 parentless; machine-wide, the only tooling process without a live parent is host 34888 (root by design). main's chain and tibia's traces run under it with no regressions reported. Closed; the remaining inheritance fixes continue in EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_28 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST", 28u32, "2026-10-23T10:23Z", "eoie", "06:23 live evidence: the low-memory reaper killed the agent shell whose after-F8 chain started job host 34888 (05:08). The host survived, and main's next4 review, fiscal gate and queued jobs plus tibia's m1-long trace kept running under it (all running or queued at 06:23). Before the host, a reaped shell took its jobs' supervisors with it (2026-10-22 06:44). The reaper also stopped my fiscal-gate waiter; main runs the guarded lib landing itself if I'm past 07:00.")
inl note_of_lib_console_stderr_1 () = Note ("LIB-CONSOLE-STDERR", 1u32, "2026-10-22T13:06Z", "eoie-lane", "09:06 found by eoie's repo affected draft (EOIE-AFFECTED-CRATES): the lib has console.write_line but no stderr writer (no eprintln anywhere under lib/spiral), so a HostFree eoie package must print 'eoie error:' lines on stdout, against eoie's stderr convention and the CLI harness's rejected_with (which reads stderr). Per the style contract the helper belongs in lib with a lib test block; a lib edit needs a Cells --gate run and the corpus check, so it is drafted for the lib owner's next lib slice. Until then repo affected's usage error stays on stdout with exit 2, and its CLI test asserts exactly that.")
inl note_of_lib_console_stderr_2 () = Note ("LIB-CONSOLE-STDERR", 2u32, "2026-10-22T13:21Z", "eoie-lane", "09:21 eoie team: drafted as an anchored lib slice (apply-lib-console-stderr.ps1 in eoie's lab night folder; dry-run clean on a copy of lib console.spi). console.write_error_line forall t mirrors write_line's backend_switch shape for Rust (eprintln), F# (Console.Error.WriteLine), Python (print file=sys.stderr), TypeScript (process.stderr), Gleam (io.println_error), Lua (io.stderr:write), Lean (IO.eprintln) and Kotlin (System.err), plus a rust+fsharp test block. Backends without an arm fail only if they call it. Landing rule: lib edits get a Cells --gate run plus the corpus check (memory lib-smoke-before-syntax), so it goes in with the lib owner's next lib batch, after eoie's F/G/E2 releases leave the box (no compiles from this lane during tibia's block). Then eoie_repo_affected switches its usage error to it and its CLI test to rejected_with.")
inl note_of_lib_console_stderr_3 () = Note ("LIB-CONSOLE-STDERR", 3u32, "2026-10-23T10:56Z", "main", "2026-10-23 06:56 main: LANDED. console.write_error_line (backend_switch arms incl. Gleam io.println_error and Lua io.stderr:write) and its test block came in with the combined lib landing at 04:40:05 (the tree console.spi equals the gated combined copy byte for byte). That gate had 0 type errors and only the 3 known location-bound failures, none in console. eoie can move repo affected's usage error to stderr.")
inl note_of_lib_split_lua_c_1 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C", 1u32, "2026-10-22T13:42Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 09:42 Found by TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION's census: tibia's arena summon.spi splits its order list with a Lua host macro, because sm'.split (sm'.spi line 1189) has no Lua arm and no C arm. A Lua arm can use string.find with plain=true in a loop (keeping empty parts, like Rust's split), and must not use gmatch patterns, because separators are literal. Done when both arms exist and a test block covers them, including empty parts and a separator at either end.")
inl note_of_lib_split_lua_c_2 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C", 2u32, "2026-10-22T13:52Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 09:52 Lua arm drafted (scratch lib-split-lua-arm.spi, goes into sm'.split's backend_switch after Gleam): an inline Lua function that loops string.find(s, sep, from, true) (plain, so . and % are literal) and appends string.sub pieces into a 1-based table, which is how the Lua backend lays out arrays (spiral_compiler.fs 169124-169184: TyArrayCreate builds t[1..n], ArrayIndex reads [(i)+1], length is #t). An empty separator returns the whole string as one part, like F#. The macro text escapes # as ##. The function body was checked with lua 5.1 on 9 cases (a,b,c; a,,c; ,a,; empty input; no separator; :: as separator; . and % separators; empty separator): 0 failed, with part counts matching Rust's split. Still open: (1) the C arm. C needs a Spiral array of strings built in C, and sm' has almost no C string arms, so it waits on LIB-SUBSTRING-style groundwork. A generic Spiral split also needs index_of with a start offset and a C arm, and substring with a Lua arm (its fallback emits F# .Substring on Lua). (2) A lib test block with ///> lua, compiled through Cells --gate on sm'.spi, plus main's corpus check before landing. Both need the compiler, so they come after the tibia live block (no compiles until block end, about 12:00).")
inl note_of_lib_split_lua_c_3 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C", 3u32, "2026-10-22T15:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 11:34 Test block drafted in scratch with the Lua arm: split_keeps_empty_and_edge_parts runs on rust, fsharp and lua (a,,c gives 3 parts with an empty middle; ,a, gives 3 with edges; a percent separator stays literal, which a pattern find would break). It lands with the arm in one lib change, coordinated with main between its gate jobs, then a Cells --gate run on sm'.spi.")
inl note_of_lib_split_lua_c_4 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C", 4u32, "2026-10-22T16:24Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:24 Correction: the C arm does NOT wait on lib C string groundwork. The compiler C backend (codegenC, spiral_compiler.fs around 174968-175500) already lowers the core string ops: StringLength as len-1, StringIndex as ptr[i], StringSlice through a StringSlice helper that returns an empty string for to = from - 1 (also at from = length) and aborts only on out-of-range or mid-UTF-8 cuts. So the C arm is pure Spiral: next_from scans for the separator with sm.index, count gives the part total, and am'.init_base builds each part with sm.slice; an empty separator returns the input as one part, like the Lua arm. Drafted in scratch (lib-split-c-arm.spi) and hand-traced on a,,c / ,a, / empty / a::b::c against the helper's bounds. sm'.slice also gains its missing C arm (the same core sm.slice). The test block adds ///> c. Lands with the Lua arm in one coordinated lib change.")
inl note_of_lib_split_lua_c_5 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C", 5u32, "2026-10-22T16:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:28 Corpus check by a rule written before it (0 differences in 20000 or the C arm is revised): the C arm algorithm simulated in lua 5.1 with 0-based indices and the C StringSlice helper's exact bounds and abort conditions, against the Lua arm on 20000 random strings (length 0-12 over a, b, comma, colon; separators comma, ::, :comma, a, double comma, abc; seed 20261022): 0 differences, no abort. Overlapping separators (double comma inside triple comma) agree: both scan left to right and resume after the match.")
inl note_of_lib_split_lua_c_6 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C", 6u32, "2026-10-22T16:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:29 One lib candidate for both items: scratch lib-candidate.ps1 rebuilds lib-candidate/sm'.spi from the current deps lib (anchors must be unique or it refuses): split gains Lua and C arms plus split_keeps_empty_and_edge_parts on rust, fsharp, lua, c; slice gains its C arm; format_fixed (F#, Lua, Python, Rust, TypeScript) plus format_fixed_avoids_ties. 74 added lines, nothing existing changed, LF only, widest line 119. Proof before handing to main: a Cells --gate run of the candidate in a copy whose deps is a real folder (memory: a deps junction tests the tree lib), as one compile job after D37 and duo-predict.")
inl note_of_lib_split_lua_c_7 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C", 7u32, "2026-10-22T17:10Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:10 Candidate gate 1 (job tibia-lib-candidate-gate, main's lib_gate.ps1 on a fresh scratch lib copy, own build path): sm'.spi 46 of 47 tests pass with 0 type errors, including split's new Lua arm, slice's C arm and format_fixed on all five targets. The one failure was split_keeps_empty_and_edge_parts on c, a gcc error: backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_lib_spiral. Cause: am'.index (used by the test, and by am'.index_base and so am'.any) has no C arm, a lib gap of its own. Fix: one line in am'.spi, C = fun () => index a i, the same as every other arm (core ArrayIndex lowers on C). The candidate is now two files. A first attempt to dodge it with a bare index in the test was wrong (inside sm'.spi index is sm'.index, the string char index) and was reverted before any run. Gate 2 started 13:10.")
inl note_of_lib_split_lua_c_8 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C", 8u32, "2026-10-22T17:24Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:24 Candidate gate 2 (job tibia-lib-candidate-gate2, 13:10-13:23): sm'.spi 47 of 47 tests pass, 0 type errors, with am'.index's new C arm in the copy: split (Lua and C arms) on rust, fsharp, lua, c; slice's C arm; format_fixed on rust, fsharp, lua, python, typescript. Gate on am'.spi itself running (job tibia-lib-candidate-gate-am). Landing after it is green: lib_land.ps1 -Copy <scratch libgate copy> -File sm'.spi,am'.spi -TreeHash F6DAD2622D42D008,2998C01011C07C56 (tree hashes at copy time, both files unchanged since early morning), coordinated with main.")
inl note_of_lib_split_lua_c_9 () = Note ("LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C", 9u32, "2026-10-22T17:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:30 LANDED in lib/spiral via lib_land.ps1 (tree hash guard; eoie and main OK): sm'.spi F6DAD2622D42D008 -> 25FB4FF51D250014 with split's Lua and C arms and split_keeps_empty_and_edge_parts on rust, fsharp, lua, c; am'.spi 2998C01011C07C56 -> A90BCF4773084B02 with index's missing C arm. Gates: sm'.spi 47 of 47, am'.spi 11 of 11, 0 type errors. First consumer drafted: tibia summon.spi (TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION note 25). DONE.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_decimal_1 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL", 1u32, "2026-10-22T13:42Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 09:42 Found by TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION's census: tibia's arena keeps three host macros only for this (adjacency %.3f, measure %.1f%% and %.2f). sm' has format and format_debug (shortest round-trip text) but no fixed-decimal formatter. Round half the way printf does on each backend, so existing log text stays byte-identical when callers switch over.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_decimal_2 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL", 2u32, "2026-10-22T13:53Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 09:53 Draft: sm'.format_fixed (decimals : i32) (x : f64) : string. The Lua arm is string.format('%.' .. decimals .. 'f', x), the same call tibia's three host macros make, so their text stays byte-identical by construction (checked on lua 5.1 for 0.1235/3, 12.25/1, 2/3 with 2, -0.05/1, 2.5/0 and 1e-9/3: equal). Cross-backend trap for the test block: exact binary ties round differently by runtime. This Windows lua 5.1 (MSVCRT) gives 12.25 -> 12.3 and 2.5 -> 3 (half away from zero), while glibc printf and Rust format! round exact ties half to even (12.2, 2). So test values must avoid exact ties (use 2/3, 0.1235), or the test pins per-backend expectations. The C arm needs compiler support: lib C strings are structs (->ptr), and number-to-string goes through the compiler's #?* show op, so lib has no way to wrap a snprintf buffer as a Spiral string. Rust, F#, Python and TypeScript arms are direct (format! with a precision argument, ToString with an F format, a Python f-string, toFixed; toFixed has its own tie quirks). Compile and Cells --gate come after the tibia live block.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_decimal_3 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL", 3u32, "2026-10-22T15:39Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 11:39 Full draft in scratch (lib-format-fixed.spi): format_fixed via backend_switch with Fsharp (ToString F-n, invariant culture), Lua (string.format), Python (format with .nf), Rust (format with a runtime precision, wrapped as Rc str like split), TypeScript (toFixed). No Gleam, Zig or C arm yet (Gleam has no stdlib fixed formatting; C needs compiler support, note 2). Test block format_fixed_avoids_ties on rust, fsharp, lua, python, typescript with tie-free values: 2/3 at 3, -1/3 at 1, 12 at 0, 1e-9 at 3, 1234.5678 at 2, expecting 0.667, -0.3, 12, 0.000, 1234.57. Expectations checked on lua 5.1, python and node: all equal. Rust and F# wait for the compile with the lib change (coordinated with main).")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_decimal_4 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL", 4u32, "2026-10-22T15:49Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 11:49 Consumer: TIBIA-CELLS-APPS needs it to make measure.spi and adjacency.spi report text pure and testable.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_decimal_5 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL", 5u32, "2026-10-22T16:26Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:26 C arm without compiler support, drafted (scratch lib-format-fixed-c-arm.spi): scale by 10^n in i64, round the magnitude half away from zero (conv of magnitude*scale+0.5), split whole and fraction, show both with ++#? (integer show already runs on c), pad the fraction with pad_left, prefix the sign. Simulated in lua 5.1 (same double arithmetic) against printf: equal on all five test values plus -0.04 at 1 (-0.0), 1.005 at 2 and 0.25 at 1; DIFFERENT on 2.675 at 2 (arm 2.68, printf 2.67) because 2.675 is stored just below the tie but 2.675*100 rounds to exactly 267.5. Known limit, stated: the C arm matches printf except where the scaled product rounds across a half, which the tie-free test values avoid; an exact arm would need digit generation from the double bits. Whether that limit is acceptable for lib is a call for the lib change with main; tibia callers (adjacency .3f, measure .1f/.2f) run on Lua, where the arm is string.format itself.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_decimal_6 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL", 6u32, "2026-10-22T16:26Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:26 Decided by a rule written before measuring (replacing the open question in note 5): the arithmetic C arm ships only if it matches printf on 0 of 20000 random values (0-4 decimals, magnitudes up to 1e4, 30 percent cut to one extra decimal to probe near-ties). Measured in lua 5.1 (seed 20261022): 176 of 20000 differ (first 0.92549999999999999 at 3: arm 0.926, printf 0.925). REJECTED: the item lands with the F#, Lua, Python, Rust and TypeScript arms; the C arm waits for an exact digit generation from the double bits (its own follow-up when a C caller needs it).")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_decimal_7 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL", 7u32, "2026-10-22T16:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:29 One lib candidate for both items: scratch lib-candidate.ps1 rebuilds lib-candidate/sm'.spi from the current deps lib (anchors must be unique or it refuses): split gains Lua and C arms plus split_keeps_empty_and_edge_parts on rust, fsharp, lua, c; slice gains its C arm; format_fixed (F#, Lua, Python, Rust, TypeScript) plus format_fixed_avoids_ties. 74 added lines, nothing existing changed, LF only, widest line 119. Proof before handing to main: a Cells --gate run of the candidate in a copy whose deps is a real folder (memory: a deps junction tests the tree lib), as one compile job after D37 and duo-predict.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_decimal_8 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL", 8u32, "2026-10-22T17:10Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:10 Candidate gate 1 (job tibia-lib-candidate-gate, main's lib_gate.ps1 on a fresh scratch lib copy, own build path): sm'.spi 46 of 47 tests pass with 0 type errors, including split's new Lua arm, slice's C arm and format_fixed on all five targets. The one failure was split_keeps_empty_and_edge_parts on c, a gcc error: backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_lib_spiral. Cause: am'.index (used by the test, and by am'.index_base and so am'.any) has no C arm, a lib gap of its own. Fix: one line in am'.spi, C = fun () => index a i, the same as every other arm (core ArrayIndex lowers on C). The candidate is now two files. A first attempt to dodge it with a bare index in the test was wrong (inside sm'.spi index is sm'.index, the string char index) and was reverted before any run. Gate 2 started 13:10.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_decimal_9 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL", 9u32, "2026-10-22T17:24Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:24 Candidate gate 2 (job tibia-lib-candidate-gate2, 13:10-13:23): sm'.spi 47 of 47 tests pass, 0 type errors, with am'.index's new C arm in the copy: split (Lua and C arms) on rust, fsharp, lua, c; slice's C arm; format_fixed on rust, fsharp, lua, python, typescript. Gate on am'.spi itself running (job tibia-lib-candidate-gate-am). Landing after it is green: lib_land.ps1 -Copy <scratch libgate copy> -File sm'.spi,am'.spi -TreeHash F6DAD2622D42D008,2998C01011C07C56 (tree hashes at copy time, both files unchanged since early morning), coordinated with main.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_decimal_10 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL", 10u32, "2026-10-22T17:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:30 LANDED with LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C's change (sm'.spi 25FB4FF51D250014): format_fixed with F#, Lua, Python, Rust and TypeScript arms and format_fixed_avoids_ties passing on all five targets. The missing C, Gleam and Zig arms move to LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-MORE-ARMS (C by the corpus rule of note 6). First consumers drafted: tibia adjacency.spi and measure.spi report lines, byte-identical in a lua 5.1 check (TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION notes 26-27). DONE.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_more_arms_1 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-MORE-ARMS", 1u32, "2026-10-22T17:32Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:32 Design for an exact C arm, no compiler work needed (lib and the compiler have no f64 bit-cast op; checked): (1) decompose exactly in f64: scaling a double by 2 is exact away from overflow and subnormals, so normalize |x| into [2^52, 2^53) by repeated *2 or /2, counting e, and conv the result to u64 (an integer below 2^53, exact); |x| = m * 2^e. (2) For n decimals the exact scaled value is m * 10^n * 2^e. For e >= 0 the value is an integer (exact via big multiplication). For e < 0, q = floor(m * 10^n / 2^-e) and the remainder r = (m * 10^n) mod 2^-e; round up when 2r > 2^-e, and at 2r = 2^-e (an exact tie) pick one documented rule (half to even like glibc and Rust, which differs from MSVCRT by design). (3) m * 10^n fits u64 for n <= 3 (2^53 * 1000 < 2^64); n >= 4 or large e needs base-1e9 limbs (C arrays of u32, schoolbook multiply and shift). (4) Test against printf on the 20000-value corpus of LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL note 6, with exact ties excluded or pinned per runtime; pass = 0 differences. Gleam: the same pure-Spiral arm works there (integer bigints are native). Zig: std.fmt with a runtime precision ({d:.[p]}) if it rounds exactly, else the same arm.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_more_arms_2 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-MORE-ARMS", 2u32, "2026-10-22T17:33Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:33 Design prototyped before any Spiral (scratch exact-fixed-proto.py, python bigints standing in for the limb arithmetic; rule: 0 differences or revise): decomposition by exact *2 and /2 steps, then m * 10^n shifted by e with half-to-even on the exact remainder, against Python's format (exact half-to-even like glibc) on 20000 random values (0-4 decimals, magnitudes to 1e4, 30 percent cut to one extra decimal to probe near-ties) plus 8 pinned cases (2.675 at 2, the case the arithmetic arm got wrong; the exact ties 0.125 at 2 and 2.5 at 0; 1e-9 at 3; -0.04 at 1; 1234.5678 at 2; 2/3 at 3; 1e22 at 2 on the e >= 0 path): 0 of 20008 differ. Next: the Spiral C arm with u64 for n <= 3 and base-1e9 limbs beyond, the same corpus as a test.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_more_arms_3 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-MORE-ARMS", 3u32, "2026-10-22T17:37Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:37 Backend-neutral exact arm drafted in Spiral (scratch lib-format-fixed-digits.spi, format_fixed_digits): no bigint type needed. For e < 0, m * 10^n / 2^k equals m * 10^n * 5^k / 10^k, so N is built in a little-endian decimal digit array (1200 digits covers the smallest subnormal's 5^1126 and the largest double's 309 digits) by small multiplies (x10 n times, then x5 k times, or x2 e times when e >= 0). Dividing by 10^k is dropping the low k digits, and half-to-even reads them directly. Only i32 arrays, u64 for the 53-bit mantissa, and f64 *2 and /2, so the same arm can serve C, Gleam and Zig. Validated before any compile: a faithful line-for-line Python port of the Spiral draft matches Python's exact format on 20012 values (the 20000 corpus plus 2.675, the ties 0.125 and 2.5, 9.995, 0.0, 1e-9, -0.04, 1e22, 5e-324, 1.7976931348623157e308): 0 differences. Next: wire it as format_fixed's C arm (C = fun () => format_fixed_digits decimals x), add a c target to the test block plus an exact-tie test pinned to half-to-even, and gate with lib_gate.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_more_arms_4 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-MORE-ARMS", 4u32, "2026-10-22T17:39Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:39 Candidate built (scratch lib-candidate2.ps1 on tree sm'.spi 25FB4FF51D250014, 83 added lines, widest 119): format_fixed_digits before format_fixed, C = fun () => format_fixed_digits decimals x, ///> c on format_fixed_avoids_ties, and a c-only test pinning exact ties to half-to-even (0.125 at 2 -> 0.12, 2.5 at 0 -> 2) plus 2.675, 9.995, -0.04 and 1e22, each expectation checked against Python's exact format. Gate script lib-candidate2-gate.ps1 ready (own build path); it runs as a compile job after today's queue, then lands via lib_land.ps1 with main's and eoie's OK.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_more_arms_5 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-MORE-ARMS", 5u32, "2026-10-22T19:39Z", "tibia-lane", "Candidate 2 gate (job 15:1x-15:31) failed all 48 tests of sm prime: the draft was written as if outside sm prime. Inside that module a bare index resolves to the string index (returns char), and the ++#? operator is defined after the insertion point. Fixed in scratch lib-format-fixed-digits.spi: array reads go through am prime index (am prime loads before sm prime in the lib spiproj), and digits are rendered by a ten-case literal table instead of ++#? (also avoids obj_to_string, which has no C arm). Gate requeued 15:38; landing still needs main and eoie OK through lib_land.ps1.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_more_arms_6 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-MORE-ARMS", 6u32, "2026-10-22T20:45Z", "tibia-lane", "Candidate 2 gate GREEN (job tibia-lib-candidate2-gate, 16:32-16:46, own build path lib-gate-tibia): sm prime tests passed 48 of 48, 0 type errors, tree sm prime hash 25FB4FF51D250014 unchanged since the candidate was built. Landing agreed with main and eoie in this order: main matrix end, eoie past-lib-reads (release F reads lib through deps/spiral), main second lib landing, then this one through lib_land.ps1 with no Cells erl.exe in flight. main combined 24-file landing composes from the tree when its gate starts, so it re-applies over this version.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_more_arms_7 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-MORE-ARMS", 7u32, "2026-10-22T21:14Z", "tibia-lane", "LANDED 17:13 (main said land now: its matrix compiles against core; eoie sent F end at 17:09): lib_land.ps1 moved lib/spiral/sm prime from 25FB4FF51D250014 to 1DF55E5E16C9BD6F, the gated copy of candidate 2. format_fixed now dispatches C = format_fixed_digits (the exact backend-neutral decimal arm), with format_fixed_digits_rounds_exact_ties_to_even on c. No Cells erl.exe was in flight; main 24-file landing composes over it later. Left on this item: Gleam and Zig arms (format_fixed has none, so those backends fail at the backend_switch); both can point at format_fixed_digits after a gate with gleam and zig targets.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_more_arms_8 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-MORE-ARMS", 8u32, "2026-10-22T21:16Z", "tibia-lane", "Candidate 3 drafted on the landed tree (scratch lib-candidate3.ps1, from sm prime 1DF55E5E16C9BD6F): format_fixed gains Gleam and Zig arms that call format_fixed_digits (am prime index already has Gleam, Zig, Lean and C arms), and both format_fixed tests gain gleam and zig targets; 6 added lines, nothing else. Gate script lib-candidate3-gate.ps1 runs after x retry end (compile hold until about 18:20); landing again through lib_land.ps1 with main and eoie told first.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_more_arms_9 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-MORE-ARMS", 9u32, "2026-10-22T21:57Z", "tibia-lane", "Candidate 3 gate GREEN (job tibia-lib-candidate3-gate, 17:46-17:56, from tree sm prime 1DF55E5E16C9BD6F): 48 of 48 passed, 0 failed, 0 unsupported, 0 skipped, failed_targets empty, so the new gleam and zig targets of format_fixed_avoids_ties and format_fixed_digits_rounds_exact_ties_to_even ran and passed. Landing held for eoie past-lib-reads (release F reads lib in its spiral-tests step) and main told.")
inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_more_arms_10 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-MORE-ARMS", 10u32, "2026-10-22T22:41Z", "tibia-lane", "LANDED 18:40 (eoie F end 18:39; main OK): lib_land.ps1 moved lib/spiral/sm prime 1DF55E5E16C9BD6F -> 47D67F79BEE7DEC5 (candidate 3: format_fixed Gleam and Zig arms call format_fixed_digits; both format_fixed tests run on gleam and zig too; gate 48/48, 0 unsupported). With the 17:13 landing, format_fixed now has exact arms for C, Gleam and Zig and native arms for F#, Lua, Python, Rust and TypeScript. Item done.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_1 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 1u32, "2026-10-22T18:18Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:18 x: found while checking x's CI against local runs (read-only). x CI and every sibling pin mlugg/setup-zig 0.17.0. Locally the scoop shim 'zig' points at zig-dev 0.18.0-dev.146 and SPIRAL_ZIG is unset, so every local ///> zig cell runs 0.18-dev (known and intended for the matrix, ZIG-FLOAT-FORMAT note 3). memo.ex keys entries with Keyword.take(opts, @tool_opts), and @tool_opts = [:print_code, :plot, :render_plot, :spiral, :python, :lua, :pwsh, :cc, :dcc, :rustc_bin] has no :zig. Toolchain.zig reads opts[:zig], then SPIRAL_ZIG, then PATH, outside the key. Gleam/erl and node are not in it either. Effect: an entry written by a zig-dev run is a hit under SPIRAL_ZIG=0.17.0 or after a Zig bump, so the cell is reported passed without running on that Zig, the false-green case for any CI-parity or toolchain-bump check. Fix direction: put the resolved executable path + version of each target's tool into the key (zig, gleam, erl, node, as already done for rustc_bin/cc), resolved the way Toolchain resolves them. Test: same cell, two SPIRAL_ZIG values -> two misses. Until then, x's CI-parity run uses SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 (X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED).")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_2 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 2u32, "2026-10-22T18:35Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:35 x: correction to note 1. rustc_bin and cc are keyed only when passed as opts. A rustc chosen through SPIRAL_RUSTC or PATH (Toolchain.rustc), or a cc through SPIRAL_CC or PATH, is not in the key either. This matters now: x's scripts/common.ps1 sets SPIRAL_RUSTC to the repo's rust-toolchain rustc (X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED note 34), so memo entries written by the earlier default rustc (nightly 2025-05-09) would hit under the new one. The fix direction covers every tool Toolchain resolves: key the resolved path + version, whatever the source (opts, env or PATH).")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_3 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 3u32, "2026-10-22T18:46Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:46 main: fix staged as Cells pack step 12 (%TEMP%\\cells-pack\\memo_tool_identity.py, 3 anchors, dry-applies on today's tree after steps 1-11). Memo.tool_identity(opts) replaces Keyword.take(opts, @tool_opts) in both the run context and code_result: the old opts part plus, for each of rustc, cc, zig, gleam, erl, bun, python, lua, wgsl_run, naga and pwsh, the executable Toolchain itself resolves (opts, then env, then PATH), stamped as {real path, size, mtime}. A scoop shim is followed through its .shim path= line, the same stamp test.ps1's native memo uses; spawning zig version on every key would cost a process per tool per run. Test memo_tool_identity_test.exs: two SPIRAL_ZIG files give two identities; an explicit zig: option wins over env; one shim retargeted gives a new identity. The key shape changes, so every existing entry misses once after the pack lands. It rides the Cells pack run after x's run 8 and my next3 review.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_4 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 4u32, "2026-10-22T19:22Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:22 main: related hardening from x's run 8 (x 15:2x): Toolchain's rustup_proxy? decides by a path substring (/rustup/), so a direct rustc.exe under scoop's persist\\rustup got +nightly-2026-10-18 prepended, which rustc then read as a filename. Fix for the next Cells pack: rustup_proxy? checks that the file is rustup's proxy (Path.basename is rustc or rustc.exe AND a sibling rustup(.exe) or a .cargo\\bin parent), not a substring of the path. Test: a fake rustc.exe under a persist\\rustup\\toolchains\\x\\bin path is NOT a proxy; one in .cargo\\bin next to rustup.exe is.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_5 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 5u32, "2026-10-22T19:27Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:27 main: rustup_proxy? hardening staged as Cells pack step 13 (%TEMP%\\cells-pack\\rustup_proxy.py plus rustup_proxy_test.exs). A rustc is a proxy when it is under /.cargo/bin/ or has rustup(.exe) beside it; the loose /rustup/ substring is gone, so x's persist\\rustup\\.rustup\\toolchains\\...\\bin\\rustc.exe runs as itself. The existing cells_test case (scoop .cargo\\bin path, no files on disk) keeps passing through the .cargo/bin rule. All 13 steps dry-apply on today's tree.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_6 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 6u32, "2026-10-22T19:41Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:41 x: concrete fix site. cells.ex run_builder puts List.wrap(Targets.tool_stamp(builder, opts)) into the code-result memo key, and targets.ex already has the mechanism: tool_stamp(%{tool: :wgsl}, opts) stamps wgsl_run and naga with file_stamp (path, size, mtime). Every other target falls into 'def tool_stamp(_builder, _opts), do: nil'. Fix: add clauses for :zig (Toolchain.zig), :rust (Toolchain.rustc plus the +toolchain argument rustc_args would add, since the proxy's file is identical across toolchains), :gleam and :erl (Toolchain.gleam/erl). Test: the same cell with two SPIRAL_ZIG values gives two misses.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_7 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 7u32, "2026-10-22T20:11Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:11 main: step 12 widened to every tool Toolchain resolves. dcc, fpc, dotnet (F# cells), spiral and plot join the stamped list, and lean is keyed as {stamped path, its +toolchain args}, since Toolchain.lean returns both and the args decide which Lean runs. workspace, package_dir and compiler_dll were already in the key through the compiler binaries and the core tree print. All resolvers are file lookups (no process spawned per key). The pack still dry-applies 14/14.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_8 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 8u32, "2026-10-23T00:38Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:38 x: correction to note 2's example. x's scripts/common.ps1 no longer sets SPIRAL_RUSTC (reverted 15:20, X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED note 44): local Rust cells run the PATH rustup proxy with Cells' own +nightly-2026-10-18, as CI does. Note 2's general point still holds: a tool picked by env or PATH must be in the key, as step 12 now does. For Rust, note 6's caution stays: the proxy's file stamp is the same for every toolchain, so the +toolchain argument must be part of the key.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_9 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 9u32, "2026-10-24T06:24Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:24 main: landed in apps/cells with the Cells pack (CELLS-PACK-NEXT, job main-cells-pack-land, exit 0). Done once the post-block suite run on the tree shows no new red.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_10 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 10u32, "2026-10-24T23:57Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:57 main groom: done by this item's own rule (no new red in the post-block suite run on the tree). Job job-1792852828683-main-serial-cells-suite-tree-2 (10:40-11:55, tree after the 02:23 Cells pack land) had 3 failures, all from before the pack. (1, 2) Spiral.Cells.DocumentTest lib round trips: the sm' orphan .spir headings, red at HEAD, fixed by LIB-SM-ROUNDTRIP at 17:47. (3) CellsLiveTest 'a print with no value still runs': RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG, which failed on the tree and in every pack before the land; its fix is in pack 5i.")
inl note_of_cells_timeout_headroom_1 () = Note ("CELLS-TIMEOUT-HEADROOM", 1u32, "2026-10-22T18:25Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:25 x: filed from X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED notes 8, 15-16 and 27-29. x keeps a per-notebook '--timeout 600000' list by hand. After each full run, a scratch script reads every .ipynb's duration_ms, takes the slowest cell, and applies thresholds (join at >= 135 s quiet / 150 s contended, leave below 100 s twice). Cells already has every input: each test's ms per target and the timeout that applied to it. Proposed generic feature: the 'spiral-tests file' JSON line gains 'slowest_ms' and 'timeout_ms' (the effective per-cell budget), plus 'headroom' = slowest/timeout. run_notebook prints one warning line when headroom passes a threshold (env SPIRAL_CELLS_HEADROOM_WARN, default 0.5). Any repo's build can then gate or log it with no per-repo script, and the --timeout choice stays in the source file's ///- line. x then retires its scratch revisit script and keeps only the rule in its item.")
inl note_of_cells_timeout_headroom_2 () = Note ("CELLS-TIMEOUT-HEADROOM", 2u32, "2026-10-22T18:47Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:47 main: design read from notebook.ex (no edit yet). The data is all there but not joined. Each test row's targets carry ms (target_rows, 232). The effective budget is computed only at dispatch (dispatch_spiral, 924: opts[:timeout] || positive_int(step.timeout) || @default_timeout 300000), and the report rows (report/3, 169) never see it. Plan: (1) evaluate/2 builds budgets = %{test.index => budget} with the same rule, from opts and the step's timeout; (2) report/3 puts \"timeout_ms\" on each test row and \"slowest_ms\" = max target ms; (3) summary_lines adds per file \"slowest_ms\", \"timeout_ms\" (the slowest test's budget) and \"headroom\" = slowest/timeout rounded to 2 places; (4) run_notebook prints one \"spiral-tests headroom\" warning line per file over SPIRAL_CELLS_HEADROOM_WARN (default 0.5). ci_gate.ps1 and lib_gate.ps1 parse the file line as JSON, so extra keys are harmless. Test: a notebook with a --timeout 1000 cell sleeping about 600 ms reports headroom >= 0.5 and the warning line. It rides the Cells pack after this one; P3.")
inl note_of_cells_timeout_headroom_3 () = Note ("CELLS-TIMEOUT-HEADROOM", 3u32, "2026-10-22T20:28Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:28 main: implemented as Cells pack step 15 (%TEMP%\\cells-pack\\timeout_headroom.py, 7 anchors, plus timeout_headroom_test.exs; dry-applies after steps 1-14). execute computes each spiral step's effective budget with dispatch's rule (opts timeout, else the cell's --timeout, else 300 s) BEFORE memo recall, so recalled cells keep theirs; ran carries :budgets. report rows gain timeout_ms and slowest_ms (the max target ms). The spiral-tests file line gains slowest_ms, timeout_ms and headroom from its tightest row (slowest/budget, 2 places), and a file at or above SPIRAL_CELLS_HEADROOM_WARN (default 0.5) adds one spiral-tests headroom line. ci_gate.ps1 and lib_gate.ps1 match only the file/total prefixes, and notebook_report_test's reports carry no budgets, so their [file, total] patterns still hold. Tests: 30 s of 300 s gives headroom 0.1 and no warning; 240 s gives 0.8 and a warning line.")
inl note_of_cells_timeout_headroom_4 () = Note ("CELLS-TIMEOUT-HEADROOM", 4u32, "2026-10-24T06:24Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:24 main: landed in apps/cells with the Cells pack (CELLS-PACK-NEXT, job main-cells-pack-land, exit 0). Done once the post-block suite run on the tree shows no new red.")
inl note_of_cells_timeout_headroom_5 () = Note ("CELLS-TIMEOUT-HEADROOM", 5u32, "2026-10-24T23:57Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:57 main groom: done by this item's own rule (no new red in the post-block suite run on the tree). Job job-1792852828683-main-serial-cells-suite-tree-2 (10:40-11:55, tree after the 02:23 Cells pack land) had 3 failures, all from before the pack. (1, 2) Spiral.Cells.DocumentTest lib round trips: the sm' orphan .spir headings, red at HEAD, fixed by LIB-SM-ROUNDTRIP at 17:47. (3) CellsLiveTest 'a print with no value still runs': RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG, which failed on the tree and in every pack before the land; its fix is in pack 5i.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_1 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 1u32, "2026-10-22T19:45Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:45 x: HYPOTHESIS from Cells' source (static, not measured): unit-ending cells compile twice per target. cell.spi wrap turns a cell's last top-level expression into 'inl main () : i32 = <expr>' (generated=1). Only a literal '()' line is rewritten to 0i32. A test cell ending in '... |> _assert_eq \"...\"' (unit), which is every x test cell, then fails attempt 1 with a unit/i32 mismatch. cells.ex compile/12 catches that (attempt == 1 and generated_main and Cell.unit_result?) and recompiles with '0i32' appended (attempt 2). If so, each such cell pays the full Spiral front end twice per target, and the compile phase (98% of run 5's cell time, X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED note 55) is about 2x what it needs to be. Cheap check (a compile window, one notebook): count SpiralCompiler process starts per test cell in process mode (Win32_Process start events filtered on SpiralCompiler.dll), expecting 2 per target if true. Fix direction (Cells): when the last expression's type is unit (or the cell is --test), emit '<expr>' followed by '0i32' up front, keeping the retry only for value cells.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_2 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 2u32, "2026-10-22T19:47Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:47 x: the check is queued (background waiter, x's scratch count_compiles.ps1). One prelude test cell (take_first_stops_at_the_limit, zig only, ends in _assert_eq) via run_notebook --test in process mode with the memo off, in a compile window, started when free memory is >= 3.5 GB and at most 1 compile window is held. A 200 ms poller records every dotnet process whose command line has SpiralCompiler.dll; grouping by the cell's spiral_cells dir separates other teams' compiles. Expected: 1 compile if the hypothesis is false, 2 if true. Result lands here.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_3 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 3u32, "2026-10-22T19:48Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:48 x: static chain complete (still unmeasured). directives.spi prepare (line ~70) calls cell.wrap on every cell's kept code, including --test cells (the flag is stripped first). cell.spi wrap makes the last top-level expression the body of 'inl main () : i32 =', rewriting only a bare '()' line to 0i32. cell.spi unit_result (line ~293) recognizes the compiler's 'Got: () / Expected: i32' pair. cells.ex compile/12 (line 622) then recompiles with '0i32' appended. Nothing on the test path appends 0i32 up front, so every --test cell whose last expression is unit (_assert_eq ...) should compile twice per target. Fix candidates, cheapest first: (a) in wrap, when the cell is a --test cell, emit the expression then '0i32' (test values are never shown); (b) for value cells, first ask the compiler for the expression's type (one pass), not compile-fail-retry. The runtime count (note 2) confirms or refutes it.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_4 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 4u32, "2026-10-22T19:54Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:54 main: confirmed by code read and fix staged as Cells pack step 14 (%TEMP%\\cells-pack\\unit_first.py plus unit_first_test.exs; it dry-applies after steps 1-13). cells.ex compile/10 always started at attempt 1 with the bare cell, and the attempt-1 error branch (622) recompiles with 0i32 appended when Cell.unit_result? matches. So every --test cell ending in a unit expression paid one failed compile (parse plus type check up to the main : i32 mismatch) before the real one. Now, for opts[:test_cell] with a generated main, compile/10 writes the 0i32-ended program first and compiles it as attempt 2 (no value shown, exactly what the retry produced). On success that is the only compile. On a timeout it returns it as before, and on any other error it restores the original text and runs today's attempt-1 path, so genuine errors are reported against the code as written. Tests: a unit --test cell reaches the compiler once, already ended by 0i32; a broken cell still reports its error. The speedup (x: compile is 98% of cell time) is measured by the pack's suite run against the baseline's per-cell compile_ms.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_5 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 5u32, "2026-10-22T19:55Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:55 x: fix sketch for main (text only, unverified). directives.spi pair parses '--test' and '--test static' and drops them ('pair rest print_code timeout packages'), so finish/cell.wrap no longer know a cell is a test. (a) Carry a test flag in Parsed, and in finish, for test cells, make the generated main end in 0i32 after the last expression (test values are never shown), so attempt 1 compiles. (b) A cheaper candidate: wrap already rewrites a bare '()' last line into 0i32 (cell.spi map_main), so appending a '()' line to a test cell's kept code might reuse that path. Whether the preceding assertion then stays inside main depends on how search_top chooses the main line, which needs checking. Wider scope: value cells whose last expression is non-i32 also go fail-then-retry (value_result? -> show_value_main), so any cell without an i32 result may pay two compiles. A one-pass design would generate the show_value main up front for value cells, if show_value can handle unit.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_6 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 6u32, "2026-10-22T20:16Z", "x", "2026-10-22 16:16 x: x's runtime count was cancelled, never run. Main confirmed the double compile by code read and staged the fix (pack step 14), so the count was only an extra 'before' figure. Its start condition (at most 1 compile window) went unmet for 30+ min while tibia held 2-3 compile windows, and it would likely have fired near 17:45, colliding with x's run 8 retry. Main's pack run measures the effect via compile_ms against its baseline.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_7 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 7u32, "2026-10-23T22:16Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:16 main: the nightpack run (job-1792766412460) found that step 14 also fired on .spi test blocks: their compiler-built main (inl _ = spiral_cells_test () then 0i32, or the wgsl status form spiral_cells_test ()) is already unit-safe, so appending 0i32 broke the wgsl status test and made an expected-fails test compile twice. unit_first.py now skips any program containing 'spiral_cells_test ()', so it only covers livemd --test cells built by cell.wrap, which is where the double compile is. Re-verified in main-cells-pack-2 (job-1792793750232).")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_8 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 8u32, "2026-10-24T06:24Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:24 main: landed in apps/cells with the Cells pack (CELLS-PACK-NEXT, job main-cells-pack-land, exit 0). Done once the post-block suite run on the tree shows no new red.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_9 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 9u32, "2026-10-24T23:57Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:57 main groom: done by this item's own rule (no new red in the post-block suite run on the tree). Job job-1792852828683-main-serial-cells-suite-tree-2 (10:40-11:55, tree after the 02:23 Cells pack land) had 3 failures, all from before the pack. (1, 2) Spiral.Cells.DocumentTest lib round trips: the sm' orphan .spir headings, red at HEAD, fixed by LIB-SM-ROUNDTRIP at 17:47. (3) CellsLiveTest 'a print with no value still runs': RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG, which failed on the tree and in every pack before the land; its fix is in pack 5i.")
inl note_of_build_r2r_shared_cache_1 () = Note ("BUILD-R2R-SHARED-CACHE", 1u32, "2026-10-22T20:40Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:40 main: implemented in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/build.ps1 (Get-ReadyToRunKey plus the cache path in Invoke-SpiralReadyToRun) and documented in tmp/AGENTS.md beside the reproducibility paragraph. Key = sha256 of the IL dll, the runtime framework dir, the os-arch target, and name=hash for each of its metadata AssemblyReferences present in the folder, all hashed from the IL snapshot before the loop replaces anything (crossgen2 rewrites dlls in place, so later hashes would depend on loop order). Cache hits copy the image to .r2r and take the existing move/locked-file path; misses run crossgen2 and store the result. Checked without a build: keys are stable, change when a referenced dll changes (FSharp.Core for Hopac), and do not change for an unreferenced one (SpiralCompilerCore). The edit does not touch the running review (pwsh parsed build.ps1 at start). Done when the next fresh-cache build (PACK-NEXT-4's candidate) logs \"N from the shared image cache\" with N >= 20 and its SpiralCompilerCore hash matches a non-cached rebuild of the same core.")
inl note_of_build_r2r_shared_cache_2 () = Note ("BUILD-R2R-SHARED-CACHE", 2u32, "2026-10-22T20:40Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:40 main: correction to the Done rule. The cache starts empty, and the images already in candidates/*/bin are R2R outputs with no record of their IL hash, so they cannot seed it. PACK-NEXT-4's candidate build FILLS the cache (about 21 misses); the first measurable reuse is the next fresh-cache build after it (any candidate or the install build). Done = that build logs \">= 20 from the shared image cache\", and a candidate rebuilt from an identical core keeps the same SpiralCompilerCore hash.")
inl note_of_build_r2r_shared_cache_3 () = Note ("BUILD-R2R-SHARED-CACHE", 3u32, "2026-10-22T20:51Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:51 main: failures are shared too. next3's build showed Hopac.dll failing crossgen2 (CodeGenerationFailedException in Hopac.EmbeddedJob`1.GetHashCode), so it stays IL. The per-folder .r2r-skip marker made every fresh cache pay that failed run again. A failed run now writes <key>.failed (first log line) beside the images, and later builds with the same key skip crossgen2 for that dll and say why. Markers are pruned with the images after 30 unused days.")
inl note_of_build_r2r_shared_cache_4 () = Note ("BUILD-R2R-SHARED-CACHE", 4u32, "2026-10-23T00:39Z", "main", "2026-10-22 20:39 main: verified in production. The hopacsota promotion build at ~20:00 (tree core, shared cache): 'ReadyToRun: 22 dll(s) precompiled in 63.3 s (19 from the shared image cache)'. This afternoon's fresh-cache review build spent over 30 min in crossgen2. The cache holds 26 entries, plus the Hopac .failed marker that skips the known crossgen2 failure. The change lives in tmp/scripts/build.ps1 and tmp/AGENTS.md (uncommitted, for the user's commit). Done.")
inl note_of_zig_const_locals_1 () = Note ("ZIG-CONST-LOCALS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T00:02Z", "main", "2026-10-22 20:02 main: measured with scratch zig_const_mirror.py on the tree's main.zig. Rule: a hoisted 'var vN: T = undefined; _ = &vN;' whose function has exactly one 'vN = e;', where that assignment sits at the hoisted declaration's own indent (the function's top block, not inside an if or loop), e does not name vN, no earlier line reads vN, and vN is never mutated through a field, an index, &vN or a compound assignment, becomes 'const vN: T = e;' at the assignment. When nothing else uses vN, the line keeps a ' _ = &vN;' discard, because Zig rejects an unused local constant. zig 0.18-dev ast-check on the 25 largest results: 24 pass and 1 failed with 'unused local constant' (mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/runtime_native), which motivated the discard rule; the recheck is running. Next: an F# pass over codegenZig's function text, with indexed occurrences (the Python mirror is quadratic: 799 s over 467 files), then a pack under the same review shape as C-FUSE-DECLARATIONS (Zig changed only by this rewrite, native equal, DISAGREE 0 under both oracles, since Zig is the first oracle).")
inl note_of_zig_const_locals_2 () = Note ("ZIG-CONST-LOCALS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T00:14Z", "main", "2026-10-22 20:14 main: discard rule verified. mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/runtime_native, the 'unused local constant' case, passes zig ast-check with the rule (8,065 -> 5,312 lines, -34.1%). The F# pass (zigConstLocals, occurrence-indexed per function) equals the mirror byte for byte on 7 files and runs in 8 s with fsi startup, where the Python mirror is quadratic. Staged for the next pack: %TEMP%\\pack-next6\\zig-const-locals.ps1 plus zig-const-locals.definition.fs. It anchors on next4's zigUsedPrelude ending ('let body = program.ToString() |> zigConstLocals'), and the core parses under both defines with it applied over next5. Not composed or queued: next5 and next4 own the heavy queue tonight.")
inl note_of_zig_const_locals_3 () = Note ("ZIG-CONST-LOCALS", 3u32, "2026-10-25T13:19Z", "main", "10-25 09:19 main groom: DONE by its rule (implemented, reviewed, installed). The slice's zigConstLocals is in the installed tree core apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs. Negative control: the 5l'-only luaSpillLocals is absent, so the grep tells installed from staged. It has passed every accepted review since, latest the 5j accept single-flight-20261025-010749 (DISAGREE 0). Uncommitted until the user commits the compiler layer.")
inl note_of_kotlin_swift_val_at_assignment_1 () = Note ("KOTLIN-SWIFT-VAL-AT-ASSIGNMENT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T02:38Z", "main", "2026-10-22 22:38 main: measured with scratch kt_swift_val_mirror.py on the tree outputs. Today every local is a hoisted 'var vN: T = <zero>' (closures get a dummy '{ _: Int -> throw RuntimeException() }') with the real value on the next line. Rule: a declaration directly followed, at the same indent, by 'vN = e' (e not naming vN), with that the only assignment to vN in the file, becomes 'val vN: T = e' (Swift: 'let'). The remaining 44% are assigned in branches; Kotlin and Swift both accept a 'val'/'let' without an initializer that is definitely assigned on every path, a second step. Validation is the matrix's Kotlin and Swift native rows (warm kotlinc server, swiftc batch), so this needs a pack review, not a spot check.")
inl note_of_kotlin_swift_val_at_assignment_2 () = Note ("KOTLIN-SWIFT-VAL-AT-ASSIGNMENT", 2u32, "2026-10-23T02:49Z", "main", "2026-10-22 22:49 main: implemented and riding in PACK-NEXT-4 (core BCBBC43A71F1E5E6); review rule and evidence in PACK-NEXT-4 note of 2026-10-22 22:49.")
inl note_of_kotlin_swift_val_at_assignment_3 () = Note ("KOTLIN-SWIFT-VAL-AT-ASSIGNMENT", 3u32, "2026-10-25T13:20Z", "main", "10-25 09:19 main groom: DONE by its rule (implemented, reviewed, installed). The slice's valAtAssignment (codegenKotlin) is in the installed tree core apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs. Negative control: the 5l'-only luaSpillLocals is absent, so the grep tells installed from staged. It has passed every accepted review since, latest the 5j accept single-flight-20261025-010749 (DISAGREE 0). Uncommitted until the user commits the compiler layer.")
inl note_of_typescript_const_locals_1 () = Note ("TYPESCRIPT-CONST-LOCALS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T02:38Z", "main", "2026-10-22 22:38 main: measured with scratch var_census.py. TypeScript output already declares each local at its value ('let v2: number = v1(4);'), so the change is a keyword: the same conservative scan as RUST-LET-IMMUTABLE (any assignment, compound assignment or field/index store to vN keeps let) turns let into const. No line change; it's the idiomatic form and lets tsc check that nothing reassigns. Lua stays: Lua 5.1 has no const and its 'local' is already the right keyword.")
inl note_of_typescript_const_locals_2 () = Note ("TYPESCRIPT-CONST-LOCALS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T02:49Z", "main", "2026-10-22 22:49 main: implemented and riding in PACK-NEXT-4 (core BCBBC43A71F1E5E6); review rule and evidence in PACK-NEXT-4 note of 2026-10-22 22:49.")
inl note_of_typescript_const_locals_3 () = Note ("TYPESCRIPT-CONST-LOCALS", 3u32, "2026-10-25T13:20Z", "main", "10-25 09:19 main groom: DONE by its rule (implemented, reviewed, installed). The slice's valAtAssignment (codegenTypeScript) is in the installed tree core apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs. Negative control: the 5l'-only luaSpillLocals is absent, so the grep tells installed from staged. It has passed every accepted review since, latest the 5j accept single-flight-20261025-010749 (DISAGREE 0). Uncommitted until the user commits the compiler layer.")
inl note_of_lean_let_at_assignment_1 () = Note ("LEAN-LET-AT-ASSIGNMENT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:14Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:14 main: measured with scratch lean_let_mirror.py (6 s over the tree outputs, occurrence-indexed per function). Rule: a hoisted 'let mut vN : T := default' whose function body has exactly one assignment 'vN := e' (or 'vN ← e'), at the hoisted line's own indent, with no earlier use and no vN in e, becomes 'let vN : T := e' at that assignment. Example (closure_curry spiralMain): four let mut plus four := become four let lines. Validation is lean --run elaboration (~2 s per file), not tonight: tibia's live block (no compiles) and eoie's F6/F7 hold the box. Next: an F# pass like zigConstLocals, byte-checked against the mirror in fsi, then the matrix's Lean rows.")
inl note_of_lean_let_at_assignment_2 () = Note ("LEAN-LET-AT-ASSIGNMENT", 2u32, "2026-10-23T14:55Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:55 main: the F# pass already exists (%TEMP%\\pack-next6\\lean-let.ps1 + lean-let.definition.fs, applied after codegenLean's program text) and is composed into PACK-NEXT-4 (pack-next4\\compose.ps1); it rides review-2 (job-1792762224345), whose Lean rows are its validation. No further drafting.")
inl note_of_lean_let_at_assignment_3 () = Note ("LEAN-LET-AT-ASSIGNMENT", 3u32, "2026-10-25T13:20Z", "main", "10-25 09:19 main groom: DONE by its rule (implemented, reviewed, installed). The slice's leanLetAtAssignment is in the installed tree core apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs. Negative control: the 5l'-only luaSpillLocals is absent, so the grep tells installed from staged. It has passed every accepted review since, latest the 5j accept single-flight-20261025-010749 (DISAGREE 0). Uncommitted until the user commits the compiler layer.")
inl note_of_gleam_tidy_output_1 () = Note ("GLEAM-TIDY-OUTPUT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:50Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:50 main: format strings in codegenGleam ('v%i( %s  )' x2, 'v%i{i}( {arg_code}(      Nil)  ) ') produce the padding; main was appended unindented. Patch %TEMP%\\pack-next6\\gleam-tidy.ps1 tightens the three strings and indents main's lines by 4, skipping indentation when any line has an odd quote count (a possible multi-line string; the 447 tree main bodies have none). Whitespace only. Review: locals_check.py compares Gleam with all whitespace removed; the matrix's Gleam native rows check build and run. In PACK-NEXT-4 core D38D865FFCB50E17.")
inl note_of_gleam_tidy_output_2 () = Note ("GLEAM-TIDY-OUTPUT", 2u32, "2026-10-25T13:20Z", "main", "10-25 09:19 main groom: DONE by its rule (implemented, reviewed, installed). The slice's gleamIndentedMain + oddQuotes is in the installed tree core apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs. Negative control: the 5l'-only luaSpillLocals is absent, so the grep tells installed from staged. It has passed every accepted review since, latest the 5j accept single-flight-20261025-010749 (DISAGREE 0). Uncommitted until the user commits the compiler layer.")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_1 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:54Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:54 main: from x's read-only scan (ureq PR #1218 lists the three fixed versions). Applied as semver-compatible lockfile bumps, with nothing built yet: spiral/workspace/Cargo.lock (rustls 0.23.34 -> 0.23.46, rustls-webpki 0.103.7 -> 0.103.16, webpki-roots 1.0.3 -> 1.0.10; the 0.26.11 webpki-roots shim stays) and polyglot/apps/chat/Cargo.lock (0.23.45 -> 0.23.46, 0.103.15 -> 0.103.16, 1.0.9 -> 1.0.10). Open: dice/Cargo.lock reaches only rustls 0.23.23 and stays on rustls-webpki 0.102.8 (an older line the fix isn't on), so it needs a real dependency upgrade, probably held by the near-sdk pins (see RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE's dice/contract note). eoie's vendored syn lock is probably not built; eoie's call. Verify: build spiral/workspace and polyglot chat on the pinned nightly in a quiet window, before the user's commit.")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_2 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 2u32, "2026-10-23T06:44Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:44 main: dice Cargo.lock moved (unstaged, lockfile only): rustls 0.23.16 -> 0.23.46, rustls-pki-types 1.10.0 -> 1.15.1, rustls-webpki 0.102.8 -> 0.103.16, webpki-roots 0.26.6 -> 0.26.11 (the shim) + 1.0.10. Plain 'cargo update -p rustls' stopped at 0.23.23, which looked like a pin, but --precise 0.23.46 resolves cleanly: it was cargo's minimal-change choice while rustls-pki-types stayed at 1.10, not a constraint. alphabet: apps/documents/Cargo.lock has no rustls, rustls-webpki or webpki-roots entries, so nothing to mirror. Open: one quiet-window build each of dice, spiral/workspace and polyglot/apps/chat on the new locks.")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_3 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 3u32, "2026-10-24T00:18Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:18 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 60 (bumps applied in the spiral/workspace, polyglot chat and dice Cargo.lock files). The three quiet-window verify builds are main-rustls-check, requeued in %TEMP%\\cells-pack\\requeue-after-block.ps1 chain B after review-3.")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_4 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 4u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: main-rustls-check timed out (124) at 22:16 and the cause is not rustls: dice's cargo check --locked exited 101 after 3051 s on near-workspaces v0.11.1's build script (custom build command failed), with rustls 0.23.46, rustls-webpki 0.103.16, webpki-roots 0.26.11 and 1.0.10 resolved. Next: read the near-workspaces build output and fix the dice build, then rerun with a longer limit.")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_5 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 5u32, "2026-10-24T03:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:57 main: cause of the 22:16 failure found, and it isn't rustls. dice's contract/tests crate (dice_contract_tests) depends on near-workspaces 0.11.1, whose build script fetches the NEAR sandbox binary, which has no Windows build; CI runs that crate on Linux. The check now runs dice with --workspace --exclude dice_contract_tests, at Normal priority (BelowNormal plus CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 took 3051 s and starved), keeps each repo's full cargo log in spiral-bin\\runs\\rustls-check, and exits with the failure count. Requeued as main-rustls-check-2.")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_6 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 6u32, "2026-10-24T13:11Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:11 main: main-rustls-check-2 was stopped at 09:10 by me. It held heavy slot 2 for 1 h 40 min at about 0.01 cores, inside openssl-sys's vendored nmake build for polyglot apps/chat (single-threaded cl /Zs dependency scans), while heal gates waited. Rerun rule: run the three cargo checks as a light-slot job, or point openssl-sys at a prebuilt OpenSSL (OPENSSL_DIR) so chat does not build it from source; dice and spiral/workspace finished their part before the stop.")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_7 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 7u32, "2026-10-25T07:40Z", "main", "10-25 03:40 main: the 10-24 logs re-read. spiral/workspace finished cleanly (51 min; its lock hasn't changed since 10-22 23:52), so it's done. dice did NOT pass: dice_contract fails E0308 on native, because on non-wasm SpiralNearVec is a Vec shim while the hand-written State wraps near-sdk's Vector. So the contract compiles only for wasm32, which is what CI builds. chat failed in near-workspaces' sandbox install. rustls reaches these repos only through near-workspaces -> near-sandbox-utils -> binary-install -> ureq, so the test crates are the real check. Its build.rs skips the sandbox download when NEAR_SANDBOX_BIN_PATH is set. main-rustls-check-3 (job-1792913855712, compile gate) runs dice native minus the contracts, both contracts for wasm32, and both test crates with NEAR_SANDBOX_BIN_PATH set plus OPENSSL_NO_VENDOR=1 and OPENSSL_DIR=scoop openssl (no vendored nmake build).")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_8 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 8u32, "2026-10-25T07:53Z", "main", "10-25 03:53 main: DONE. main-rustls-check-3 (job-1792913855712), cargo check --locked against the bumped locks (rustls 0.23.46, rustls-webpki 0.103.16, webpki-roots 0.26.11 + 1.0.10):\n- dice native minus the contracts: exit 0 (256 s).\n- dice_contract_tests: exit 0 (280 s). With NEAR_SANDBOX_BIN_PATH set, near-workspaces' build.rs skips the sandbox download that has no Windows build.\n- chat_contract_tests: exit 0 (190 s), with OPENSSL_NO_VENDOR=1 and OPENSSL_DIR=scoop openssl, so no nmake build.\nThose test crates are the only rustls consumers (ureq <- binary-install <- near-sandbox-utils). spiral/workspace was already clean on 10-24 with an unchanged lock.\nThe 2 wasm32 contract checks failed only because --all-targets drags the contracts' native test code (near_workspaces, tokio) into the wasm build. It's a flag artifact, not a lock problem; CI builds the contract lib for wasm without --all-targets.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:02Z", "eoie", "00:02 eoie: found reading host_run.spi:22. The host spawns 'eoie job supervise <id>' with its own environment, which comes from whichever caller started the host (idle-out 10 min), and nothing in the start request carries the caller's variables. The 10-21 binary spawned supervisors from the caller, so variables set before 'eoie job start' (SPIRAL_CC for matrix runs, SPIRAL_ZIG, CARGO_BUILD_JOBS, SPIRAL_HEAVY_MAX_MINUTES, EOIE_HEAVY_*) reached the job; with F installed they silently stop reaching it, and one caller's variables leak into every job while its host lives. Peers were warned at 00:0x with the workaround (set variables inside the job script). Rule, set before writing: P0 because F changes behavior for every lane at install. Fix in GE2: launch writes the caller's environment to the job folder (env, NUL-separated KEY=VALUE), and the supervisor adopts it before the gates (set listed variables, remove unlisted ones except EOIE_JOB_*), so the window gate, the slot gate and the child all see the caller's environment. Test: two starts under one fixture home with different values of a variable; the second job must print its own value (it fails today, since the host keeps the first caller's environment).")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:24Z", "eoie", "00:24 eoie: drafted. (1) lib env.spi: environment_snapshot (NUL-separated KEY=VALUE of every variable) and environment_restored snapshot kept_prefix (set listed variables, remove unlisted ones unless their name has the prefix); Rust only, other targets fail loudly, round-trip test block (scratch job-env/apply-lib-env-snapshot.ps1). (2) eoie (job-env/apply-job-env.ps1, applied after GE2's E2 wave): launch writes job_file env from env.environment_snapshot; eoie_jobs job_environment_adopted restores it keeping EOIE_JOB_* and deletes the file (file_system.file_delete), so secrets don't sit in job folders past the start; supervise_job calls it first, before the after-wait, the gates and the spawn. (3) stop_cli gains each_job_runs_with_the_environment_of_its_own_caller: two starts under one fixture home with EOIE_ENV_PROBE=first, then second; each job's stdout must be its own value, and the env file must be gone. The chain G, E2, job-env, H dry-runs clean on a src copy. launch-GE2 refuses until lib env.spi has environment_restored. Order: Cells-gate the lib file alone after F8's 'past lib reads' and main's landing, land it, then launch GE2.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 3u32, "2026-10-23T05:26Z", "eoie", "01:26 eoie: second host-inheritance problem, measured. The host also inherits its creator's priority class. Windows gives a child the creator's class when that class is BelowNormal or Idle and no class flag is passed, and host_run.spi spawns the host with 0x0100_0208 (no class flag). Probe with F6's debug candidate from a BelowNormal pwsh: 'caller priority: BelowNormal; host priority: BelowNormal'. Every heavy job's script runs BelowNormal (Invoke-Heavy), so a host first started from inside one (F8's own acceptance step right after install) would run every lane's later jobs BelowNormal, tibia's live client included. Fix for GE2: job-env/apply-host-normal-priority.ps1 adds NORMAL_PRIORITY_CLASS (0x0100_0228 / 0x0000_0228), chained in launch-GE2 and its prelaunch. GE2's probe now blocks unless the candidate host started from its BelowNormal job is Normal. Tonight's mitigation: scratch host-hygiene.ps1 runs after F8 installs. An idle host is stopped and a fresh one started from a clean Normal shell, which also drops F8's environment (CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2, the Cells retire floor 2048, SPIRAL_HEAVY_MAX_MINUTES); a busy host gets PriorityClass Normal in place, and I rerun once it idles. Both paths were tested on a fixture home with the debug candidate.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 4u32, "2026-10-23T05:31Z", "eoie", "01:31 eoie: order decided. The lib gate (scratch job-env/libgate-copy.ps1: copy the landed lib, apply env snapshot/restore and last_write_ms, lib_gate.ps1 -RawOut on env.spi and file_system.spi) runs only when no live-gui window holds or waits, per memory live-runs-alone. tibia's night block has been waiting since 00:16 and starts when F8 and main's next5d release their heavy windows, so the gate runs after the block, around 06:30. GE2 can't launch during the block either (launch-GE2 refuses on an exclusive window), so nothing is lost. Sequence: F8 installs -> host-hygiene -> post-F -> main's lib landing after my 'past lib reads' -> block -> lib gate -> land my lib parts on main's landed tree -> launch-GE2.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_5 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 5u32, "2026-10-23T07:31Z", "eoie", "03:31 eoie: the three lib slices (env snapshot/restore, file_system.last_write_ms with the temp-path test fix, file_system.append_all_text) now append at the end of env.spi and file_system.spi instead of anchoring on headings, so main's 25-file landing (which touches file_system.spi) can't break their anchors. Everything they use is defined earlier in the file. Each is idempotent (a rerun writes nothing), and a dry run of all three on lib copies puts them at the tail in order.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_6 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 6u32, "2026-10-23T08:43Z", "eoie", "04:43 main landed its 25-file lib at 04:40:05 (hashes verified: env E5F81A293E209276, file_system 4756D432D99475E4, package.spiproj EA124BCBC41E4722). My 4 lib slices are gating on a fresh copy (libgate-copy -Tag land). Landing rule set before the result: lib_land.ps1 -Apply only if all 3 gates pass, after F9 prints 'release install done' or settles (F9 reads lib/spiral during its stop_cli compile and regen steps), and only while the tree hashes still equal the copy's (lib_land refuses otherwise -> re-gate). Then preview-GE2 (now with the no-inherit slice), candidate pre-run, launch-GE2.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_7 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 7u32, "2026-10-23T08:50Z", "eoie", "04:50 lib gate on main's landed lib (copy libgate-land): csv.spi 2/2 pass; file_system.spi's new tests pass, and 3 old tests fail only because the scratch copy has no workspace/ or README.md above it (find_parent: 'No parent for dir workspace ... until c:\\'); env.spi FAILED an_environment_snapshot_restores_to_itself (snapshot after restore differed). Cause: Rust's Windows vars_os yields hidden per-drive keys like '=C:' (std finds the separator from position 1), but environment_restored split pairs with split_once('='), so '=C:=C:\\x' became an empty key, was filtered out of the wanted set, and the removal loop then deleted =C: (a real bug: every GE2 supervisor would have dropped its per-drive cwd variables). Fix in apply-lib-env-snapshot.ps1 (backup .before-hidden-keys): the separator is searched from the second char (char_indices, byte-safe), keys starting with '=' are never removed or set, and set_var runs only when the value differs (an identity restore changes nothing). Re-gate on the same copy with repo markers added (libgate-regate.ps1: env.spi + file_system.spi). Landing rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_8 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 8u32, "2026-10-23T08:56Z", "eoie", "04:56 re-gate on the same copy: env.spi 5/5 PASS with the hidden-key fix. file_system.spi still 17/20: find_parent (fsharp), find_parent_2 (rust), trace_file (fsharp) assert the found root folder is named spiral or polyglot ('_assert_in { x = [libgate-land]; items = [spiral; polyglot] }'), so no copy outside a folder named spiral can pass them; my last_write_ms and append_all_text tests are among the 17 passes. Rule set before the next run: after F9 settles, gate file_system.spi once more from a copy rooted at a folder named spiral (scratch libgate-spiral\\spiral\\lib\\spiral, with workspace/ and README.md). Land if 20/20, or if only those 3 fail with errors naming none of my functions; otherwise fix first.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_9 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 9u32, "2026-10-23T09:04Z", "eoie", "05:04 after-install-GE2.ps1 gains GE2's core checks on the INSTALLED binary: EOIE-JOB-ENV (fixture home: first job starts the host without EOIE_ENV_PROBE, then the variable is set and a second job must echo probe=adopted-42), EOIE-JOB-HOST handles (pipe-release-check -Eoie installed) and breakaway (nested-host-check -Eoie installed). Positive control planned right after F9 installs: run it against F9's binary, where all three must FAIL (host without env adoption, inherited handles, no BREAKAWAY_OK); the old 10-21 binary would pass the env probe vacuously (no host), so F9's binary is the right control.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_10 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 10u32, "2026-10-23T09:44Z", "eoie", "05:44 all lib gates pass: env.spi 5/5, file_system.spi 20/20 (from a copy rooted at a folder named spiral, so the 3 location tests pass too), csv.spi 2/2. Landing (lib_land, tree hashes E5F81A29/4756D432/EA124BCB/absent -> 93FB080E/90E5CD2E/DC64AFD4/318ED8DC) waits for a gap with no Cells run reading the lib: 2 Cells VMs (main/tibia jobs) started 05:42; asked main for the gap. GE2's prelaunch with -Originals is clean, and GE2's rebase dry run is clean; launch-GE2 refuses until environment_restored is in the lib.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_11 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 11u32, "2026-10-23T10:40Z", "eoie", "06:40 lib landed 06:38:09 through land-lib-batch.ps1 in main's gap (fiscal gate stopped early, no spiral.notebook erl alive): env.spi 93FB080E4D303EB4, file_system.spi 90E5CD2E1531EAF9, package.spiproj DC64AFD4CC940A37, csv.spi 318ED8DC3D54C836, all equal to the gated copy; main verified them in the tree. launch-GE2's lib precondition (environment_restored) now holds. GE2's heavy preview started as job-1792751959794-eoie-preview-GE2 (supervised by host 34888, 2 h limit, through fmt-rebase); next: candidate pre-run, then launch-GE2.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_12 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 12u32, "2026-10-23T14:53Z", "eoie", "10:53 eoie: INCIDENT, measured. From about 09:52 to 10:50 every DETACHED job's window gate was granted at once. Host 29276 (HP install) was spawned from my release script after an in-process one-call runner pass. Runner case 40 sets EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME to a fixture registry, and the runner's finally cleared only EOIE_JOBS_HOME, so the variable leaked into the release script and from there into the shared host. Supervisors inherit the host's environment, so their gates read an empty registry. Effect: my eoie-preview-GE2 ran 10:26:15-10:40:00 during tibia's PR-26 live block (runs 2-4 flagged by tibia; no other session's job ran in the span, confirmed by main, tibia and x). Diagnosis trap: job run supervises in the caller's shell and so looked correct; only job start reproduces the host path. Proof: a detached job's 'window check compile' said free, and its cmd /c set showed EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME=...eoie-one-call-31592-40-windows. Mitigation at 10:50, nothing stopped: the empty fixture dir became a junction to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\windows; a detached check now reports the live-gui hold. Source fix at 10:5x (lab one-call-runner.ps1): snapshot the caller's EOIE_*/SPIRAL_* variables per case, restore them in finally, and remove the -windows fixture dir; verified by an in-process full pass (38/40; EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME and EOIE_JOBS_HOME unset afterwards, sentinel kept, 0 leftover dirs). Permanent fix stays this item's GE2 slice (supervisor adopts the caller env before the gates). The host env also carries CARGO_BUILD_JOBS from the same script. Rule for GE2's acceptance: a detached 'window check' during a held live-gui window must report blocked.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_13 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 13u32, "2026-10-23T16:30Z", "eoie", "12:30 eoie: residual symptoms on host 29276 until GE2 installs (detached 'cmd /c set' vs a shell): CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 is set for every detached job (from my HP release script), so detached cargo builds machine-wide run at -j2; EOIE_LEASE_POLICY (renewing in agent shells) is absent, so agile writes from inside detached jobs use the strict default. EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME is neutralized by the 10:50 junction. A new host only spawns for a new binary, so these stay until GE2's install brings its host, which adopts each caller's environment per job anyway.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_14 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 14u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): PROC-8 (one capture contract: cwd, env, unset), TOOL-1 (profiles passed to every job, with EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES F4). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_15 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 15u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "eoie", "23:06 groom: GE2's job-env work (job_environment_adopted in eoie_jobs, env.environment_restored from lib) inlines ~121 generated lines into every crate that calls it, and pushed eoie_job_supervise to 1062 lines, one of GE2's four topology refusals. The supervise split (crate-splits\\supervise-split.do) moves the call behind a new eoie_job_env crate; keep future environment helpers there.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_16 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 16u32, "2026-10-24T04:12Z", "eoie", "00:12 status: code landed with GE2 (15:00) and ships in the GE3 release queued at 00:05 (job-1792814703946); GE3's acceptance runs the EOIE-JOB-ENV probe (a job started after the host sees the caller's variable). Progress 50 -> 80. Open question for after the install: host 29276 still carries CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 from the HP release script (note of 12:30), so detached cargo builds machine-wide run at -j2. Main's chains keep that host busy, so it never idle-exits and the leak outlives installs. Check after GE3: does environment_restored also remove variables absent from the caller's snapshot (if not, the adopted environment still has CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2)? If not, either restore exactly (unset extras) or let EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT's per-owner hosts retire 29276. Measure with a detached 'cmd /c set CARGO' job after GE3 installs.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_17 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 17u32, "2026-10-24T04:13Z", "eoie", "00:13 answer to the 00:12 question, read from lib env.spi environment_restored: it restores exactly. It removes every variable missing from the caller's snapshot (except '=' keys and the kept EOIE_JOB_ prefix), then sets the snapshot's values. So after GE3 installs, each new supervisor adopts its caller's environment exactly, and host 29276's CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 stops reaching jobs started by the new CLI, even while that old host keeps serving. Post-install check stays: a detached 'cmd /c set CARGO' job from a shell without the variable must print nothing.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_18 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 18u32, "2026-10-24T11:22Z", "eoie", "07:2x groom: the eoie_job_env crate (environment adoption, environment_restored over lib env) is in the live tree since GE3 landed at 00:05 (ok  files 5 searched  1 matched  matches 2  15ms  07:22:28-04:00) and installs with GE3's release, which is in build/tests now (regen 122/122 at 06:42). Done = installed, plus its after-install check: a detached job sees the caller's variables, not the host's.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_19 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 19u32, "2026-10-24T11:23Z", "eoie", "07:23 groom: blocked on the train. The only work left is to install GE3's release and run its after-install check, so 'agile next' must not hand this out as ready work. Back to Active when GE3's release installs.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_20 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 20u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "eoie", "19:45 groom: the release condition is met. GE3's rerun (GE3b, job-1792850533961) installed at 11:46, and after-install-GE3 passed 12/12. Done.")
inl note_of_bend_prune_if_params_1 () = Note ("BEND-PRUNE-IF-PARAMS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 00:25 main: measured with scratch bend_if_params_census.py; mirror bend_prune_mirror.py, 38 s over 470 main.bend after replacing per-name regexes with one call scan per iteration. Rule: a parameter of a def <x>_ifN(...) that its body never names (the condition c is always kept) is removed from the def and from every call with a matching argument count; repeat to a fixpoint, since a parameter only forwarded to a pruned helper becomes unused in turn. Example closure_curry: spiral_main_if1(v3, v0, v1, v2, v3) becomes spiral_main_if1(v3). Check: the 15 most-pruned programs run under bend2 (bun) with the original exit code and stdout. Better done in codegenBend (pass only the branch's free variables) than as a text pass; the mirror is the reference either way. Not in next4 yet: it needs an F# port, a byte check, and Bend's ~200 matrix rows.")
inl note_of_bend_prune_if_params_2 () = Note ("BEND-PRUNE-IF-PARAMS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 00:37 patched into pack-next4 as pack-next6\\bend-prune.ps1 (definition before codegenBend; body piped through bendPruneIfParameters before bendUsedPrelude). Core 87EB43CF41975958 parses under SINGLE_FLIGHT and HOPACSOTA. Review rule in locals_check.py: before starts with the full prelude and after ends with the mirror-pruned body; self-test on 417 tree samples accepts 417 (305 bodies pruned).")
inl note_of_bend_prune_if_params_3 () = Note ("BEND-PRUNE-IF-PARAMS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T06:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:53 main: bend2 run-time probe of the 6 most-pruned programs (bun bend2 main.ts as test.ps1:668, second of 2 rounds) is INCONCLUSIVE: it overlapped the start of tibia's live block at 02:50. The swings are too large to be the change: utf8_normalization 2,781 -> 1,251 ms, canonical_equal 8,136 -> 4,470, but the two mega_brzozowski programs 24.2 -> 32.7 s and 25.0 -> 36.3 s, total 65.6 -> 80.2 s. Exits equal (0, and 1 for the invalid sample). Rule for the rerun on a quiet box: 3 alternating rounds; call it a speedup or slowdown only if the per-program median moves at least 10% in the same direction in 2 of 3 rounds. Correctness does not rest on this; it rests on the 417/417 mirror equality, the bend2 runs of 15 programs and the matrix review.")
inl note_of_bend_prune_if_params_4 () = Note ("BEND-PRUNE-IF-PARAMS", 4u32, "2026-10-25T13:20Z", "main", "10-25 09:19 main groom: DONE by its rule (implemented, reviewed, installed). The slice's bendPruneIfParameters is in the installed tree core apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs. Negative control: the 5l'-only luaSpillLocals is absent, so the grep tells installed from staged. It has passed every accepted review since, latest the 5j accept single-flight-20261025-010749 (DISAGREE 0). Uncommitted until the user commits the compiler layer.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_retire_race_1 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-RACE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T09:19Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:19 main: seen in job-1792746056869 (fiscal corpus_checks, 05:01-05:16): 6 of 14 tests reported failed with 'compiler worker failed: {:normal, {GenServer, :call, [#PID<0.137.0>, {:compile, ...}]}}'. That is a call that reached the daemon GenServer as it stopped. The daemon retired on memory pressure (free under the 4096 MB SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_RETIRE_FREE_MB floor) during the box's low-memory spell. Code read (apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/compiler_daemon.ex): retire_when_idle (378-398) waits for queued() == 0, not pool_busy?() and a 2 s grace, then returns {:stop, :normal, ...}. The process mailbox is never checked, so a {:compile} call already queued in it dies with exit :normal, and the test cell counts it as a failure, not a retry. Fix options, choose by test: (a) in retire_when_idle also require Process.info(self(), :message_queue_len) == {:message_queue_len, 0}, else re-check in 500 ms; (b) in the client, catch :exit {:normal | :noproc, {GenServer, :call, _}} and retry once through a fresh daemon spawn. Test: an ExUnit case that sends :retire and a compile call back to back and asserts the call returns. Rides the next Cells pack, not the queued one (cells-pack copies the tree when it starts).")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_retire_race_2 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-RACE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T09:20Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:20 main: option (a) drafted as %TEMP%\\cells-retire-race\\retire-race.ps1 -Cells <apps/cells copy> (2 anchors; the dry run against the tree matches once each). It adds mailbox_empty? (Process.info(self(), :message_queue_len) is 0) as a fourth wait condition in retire_when_idle's re-check branch. Caveat for the review: any pending message postpones the stop by one 500 ms re-check, not only calls, so a steady message stream would delay retirement; the timers are rare enough that this should stay a short delay. Still needs the ExUnit race test (send :retire, then a compile call, and assert the call returns {:compiled, ...}) and a place in the next Cells pack.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_retire_race_3 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-RACE", 3u32, "2026-10-23T09:22Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:22 main: race test drafted as %TEMP%\\cells-retire-race\\compiler_daemon_retire_race_test.exs (parses under Elixir with scoop OTP). It is deterministic, with no timing luck. Steps: start a daemon with on_retire, :sys.suspend it, use :sys.replace_state to put retiring 10 s in the past (past the 2 s grace), queue :retire_check and then, from a Task, GenServer.call(pid, :port), wait until both are in the mailbox, :sys.resume. Without the fix the retire check stops the server and the Task gets {:exit, {:normal, {GenServer, :call, _}}}, the exact production error; with mailbox_empty? the call is answered first and the daemon then retires. Next: run it on an apps/cells copy, red without the patch and green with it, then add both to the next Cells pack.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_retire_race_4 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-RACE", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:44Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:44 main: RED/GREEN PROVEN on an apps/cells copy (job-1792748486231, MIX_ENV=test, its own MIX_BUILD_PATH, scoop OTP). Without the patch the race test fails: left = {:exit, {:normal, {GenServer, :call, [pid, :port, 5000]}}}, the exact production error from the fiscal corpus run. With retire-race.ps1 applied, the race test plus compiler_daemon_idle_test and compiler_daemon_status_test pass (exit 0). Added as step 16 of the queued cells-pack (patch + test copied into its apps/cells copy before the full suite), so it lands with that pack. It is not written to the tree now because Cells is reading the live tree for the fiscal gate.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_retire_race_5 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-RACE", 5u32, "2026-10-24T06:24Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:24 main: landed in apps/cells with the Cells pack (CELLS-PACK-NEXT, job main-cells-pack-land, exit 0). Done once the post-block suite run on the tree shows no new red.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_retire_race_6 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-RACE", 6u32, "2026-10-24T23:57Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:57 main groom: done by this item's own rule (no new red in the post-block suite run on the tree). Job job-1792852828683-main-serial-cells-suite-tree-2 (10:40-11:55, tree after the 02:23 Cells pack land) had 3 failures, all from before the pack. (1, 2) Spiral.Cells.DocumentTest lib round trips: the sm' orphan .spir headings, red at HEAD, fixed by LIB-SM-ROUNDTRIP at 17:47. (3) CellsLiveTest 'a print with no value still runs': RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG, which failed on the tree and in every pack before the land; its fix is in pack 5i.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T09:27Z", "eoie", "05:27 split from EOIE-JOB-HOST (closed: the parent fix is verified live). Four of tonight's five inheritances ship in GE2 as slices (job-env/apply-host-normal-priority, apply-host-neutral-cwd, apply-container-breakaway, apply-host-no-inherit); the fifth, the environment, is EOIE-JOB-ENV. Gate: after-install-GE2.ps1's handles and breakaway probes, which FAIL on F9's binary (positive control 05:17) and must PASS after GE2. Note: the 05:08 formatter pass moved anchors; GE2 now goes through fmt-rebase/fmt-rebase.ps1.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 2u32, "2026-10-23T10:56Z", "eoie", "06:56 user 06:5x: 'can't you prioritize making eoie processes spin up with a parent? this is sloppy work, asking this for days'. Drift traced: I had declared the job host a root by design and counted that as done; the requirement is every eoie process has a live parent. Measured 06:53: the host (34888) is the only eoie process with a dead parent (the exited eoie.exe call). Fix proven in two lab runs (scratch parent-proof/): CreateProcessW with PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_PARENT_PROCESS = the user's shell (GetShellWindow -> explorer.exe, alive for the logon) gives the child a live parent, Normal priority, no job object, and from INSIDE an eoie job (no BREAKAWAY_OK) the child still broke away and outlived the job (job membership follows the specified parent). Rule set before building: ship as hotfix release HP ahead of GE2 with the 3 host-launch slices (normal priority, neutral cwd, CreateProcessW no-inherit + the parent attribute, falling back to no attribute only when there is no shell window). Install only if the candidate host's parent is a live explorer.exe, supervisors are host-parented, nested-host-check and pipe-release-check PASS, and eoie_job_host/eoie_jobs notebooks pass. Live check after install: 0 eoie processes with a dead parent.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 3u32, "2026-10-23T12:39Z", "eoie", "08:39 HP candidate: 'candidate host 117792 parent 8628 explorer.exe', so the fix works in the real build. But HP's host-probe gate saw 'probe supervisor 114932 parent 0' and will block publishing: its probe job (ping -n 4, ~3 s) ended before the parent lookup on this loaded box, and a vanished process reads as parent 0. Reproduced with the same candidate binary in a fixture home: the supervisor stays alive and parented by the host (104676) on every read. Recovery rule (like F9 after F8): fix the probe (a 30-s job, and a missing row fails as 'supervisor gone', never as a parent mismatch), stop HP, run HP-tail on the same tree: skip regen and cargo check only if HP's two landed files still hash as landed and src is otherwise unchanged since HP's launch; rerun the debug build (incremental), candidate probes, notebooks, parity, runner, lab checks, release build, publish.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 4u32, "2026-10-23T13:08Z", "eoie", "09:08 HP-tail candidate: host probe PASS (supervisor parented by the host), nested start PASS (a job started inside another job outlives it: the job-object escape works), but pipe-release FAIL ('did not return within 20 s'). Measured with the same candidate binary: standalone the redirected start returned in 3 s with the new host alive; inside an eoie job PASS in 7.7 s. So the 20 s limit was a load artifact (a fresh home's first start copies the 63 MB debug binary; the box was busy at 08:49). pipe-release-check now waits 60 s; it still requires the host to be alive at the end, so a pipe held until the host dies still fails. HP-tail stopped, HP-tail2 (same script, same unchanged tree) holds slot 2; no live slot ticket was waiting (all queued jobs are --after chains), checked before the stop.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_5 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 5u32, "2026-10-23T13:29Z", "eoie", "09:29 HP-tail2: candidate host under explorer.exe and the probe supervisor parented by the host again, but its probe start took 23772 ms against the 5000 ms gate. Measured right after with the same candidate: 6 fresh-home starts 2160, 953, 1034, 1977, 1859, 946 ms (median 1859), and the installed binary 2629, 1999, 2611, 1821, 991, 1971 (median 1999): no regression, a transient spike under load. Gate rule changed: a probe start over 5000 ms takes one more sample, and the gate blocks only if both are slow. HP-tail2 stopped; HP-tail3 (same tree, same script with the retry) holds slot 2; no queued job without an --after chain was waiting.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_6 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 6u32, "2026-10-23T13:56Z", "eoie", "09:56 HP installed 09:51 (eoie.exe 9AEE40C7). Install rule met: candidate host parent was a live explorer.exe (72740 under 8628), probe supervisor host-parented, nested and pipe checks PASS, eoie_job_host and eoie_jobs notebooks 2/2. Live after install, after-install-HP.ps1 6/6 at 09:53: host 29276 parent 8628 explorer.exe, Normal priority, 3 new supervisors all live under the host, pipe released, nested start outlives its job, 0 dead-parent rows started after the install. 1 dead-parent process left: the pre-install host 34888, which still serves main's queued tickets and exits when they drain. Closing condition: the strict audit (live-parent-audit.ps1) shows 0 once 34888 exits. Container breakaway still ships in GE2. One-call spec on the installed binary 37/40, 38/40 after the runner setup fix (case 36).")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_7 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 7u32, "2026-10-23T18:55Z", "eoie", "14:55 eoie: done. The pre-install host 34888 (dead parent) took its idle-exit path at 14:51:47, 11 minutes after its last job (main's review 2) finished; host.pid already named 29276, so it removed nothing. The strict audit the watcher ran at 14:52 found 18 eoie processes, 0 with a dead parent: host 29276 under explorer, every supervisor under 29276 (all started by the installed host), and 3 CLI calls under their callers' pwsh. Every eoie process now has a live parent, the job host included.")
inl note_of_lib_testing_expect_1 () = Note ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T09:28Z", "eoie", "05:28 measured: 11 scratch models and 50 eoie src files each define 'inl expect (condition : bool) (~detail : string) : () = if condition then () else failwith detail' locally; lib/spiral testing.spi has _assert_eq/_assert_in/... but no boolean check that carries a message (my eoie_notebook_progress model failed its typecheck at 05:11 for lacking it). Lib-first: add testing.expect (backend-generic: failwith), with a test block, then the copies drop theirs as they are touched. Lands with a lib gate like the others; coordinate the landing with main.")
inl note_of_lib_testing_expect_2 () = Note ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", 2u32, "2026-10-23T09:50Z", "eoie", "05:50 slice drafted: scratch job-env/apply-lib-testing-expect.ps1 (end-of-file append to testing.spi, guarded on an existing 'inl expect (condition : bool)', rust+fsharp test block); dry run on a copy applies and is idempotent. Not in tonight's gated batch (that one is already gated and waiting for its landing gap); it goes in the next lib gate, then the copies drop their local expect when touched.")
inl note_of_lib_testing_expect_3 () = Note ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", 3u32, "2026-10-23T15:08Z", "eoie", "11:08 eoie: bundled into lib-gaps/apply-lib-gaps.ps1, which now runs this slice first, so testing.expect lands in the same lib landing and gate as process.try_capture_combined_list, am'.growable/push_back, sm'.concat_array's Rust join and env.current_executable (after main's main-lib-land, which main agreed to). The f6 lib copy carries it, and its notebook check runs after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_lib_testing_expect_4 () = Note ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", 4u32, "2026-10-23T21:34Z", "eoie", "17:34 eoie: lib-gaps (testing.expect plus am'.growable/push_back, the sm' line primitives, the sm'.concat_array Rust join, process.try_capture_combined_list and env.current_executable) is rebased on main's two lib landings of today. A dry run on a fresh copy, after checking it against main's posted hashes (sm' A4FD87585FDCC6A5, process D4025378EE51F5FE), applies clean and keeps main's portable_index_of. Lib gate on that copy: process 6/6, am' 12/12, testing 8/8, env 6/6, sm' 50/52 (show_floats and replace_regex red). Land rule, written before the baseline: land only if both reds are also red on the untouched lib (baseline gate running since 17:26; main reports replace_regex red in its own baseline), no Cells erl.exe runs, and the gated hashes still hold. land-lib-gaps.ps1 enforces all three; its controls refuse with no baseline, refuse when the baseline has only replace_regex, and accept with both reds, up to the Cells guard.")
inl note_of_lib_testing_expect_5 () = Note ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", 5u32, "2026-10-23T21:56Z", "eoie", "17:56 eoie: landed in spiral/lib/spiral by land-lib-gaps.ps1 with all three conditions met. Baseline: the untouched lib's sm' fails show_floats and replace_regex too (47/49), the same reds as the dry copy (50/52; the 3 extra tests are the new primitives, all passing). No Cells erl.exe was running, and the gated hashes held. 5 of 5 files equal the gated dry copy; backup in scratch lib-gaps/before-land-175558; main told. N's lib needs (am' push_back, sm' nonempty_line_at, concat_array signature) are now in the tree.")
inl note_of_lib_testing_expect_6 () = Note ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", 6u32, "2026-10-23T22:25Z", "eoie", "18:25 eoie: done. The title's deliverable (lib testing gains expect condition with a message) is in the live lib since 17:56 (testing.spi: inl expect (condition : bool) (detail : string) : () = if condition then () else failwith detail), lib-gated, with the sm' reds equal to the untouched baseline. Lib edits need no install step. Callers move onto it package by package: heal wave 1's eoie_receipt test is the first (release Q).")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_1 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:01Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:01 x: first classification by the rule (G1-G5 / R1-R4), to be confirmed by measurement before each switch. RUST, keep: (1) xc and all client crates: per-command CLI (R2, measured 34-145 ms vs BEAM 1.1-2.6 s) plus TLS (R3). GLEAM: (2) the sim runner shell, which starts, cancels, times out and harvests executors (G1, G2, G3, G4): gleam_otp via LIB-OTP-ACTORS, replacing Elixir Task (SIM-IN-SPIRAL); (3) the daemon-memory sampler, a periodic monitor of an OS tree (G1, G3); (4) the future X API server: the sim serving xc and e2e instead of the Python stub, i.e. many concurrent requests and rate-limit windows (G1, G2, G4; SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY); (5) a future long-running 'xc watch' over many accounts with backoff (G1, G2, G3), as a Gleam service the Rust CLI talks to. BOTH, one Spiral source with both arms: (6) the sim world model and runner core (SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL, SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL) run inside the Gleam runner by default, and on Rust for batch work; (7) corpus commute, scale ladders and schedule sweeps (many independent schedules and seeds) go Rust + LIB-PAR-MAP (R1) for batch runs, measured against today's Elixir run time; (8) splitter/weights stay dual (already tested rust + gleam); a rayon arm for bulk postability over 36 files only if a measurement shows a gain. KEEP: invariants, digest and view (small inputs, no rule fires). Order: (2) and (6) with the Spiral rewrite, then (7) once LIB-PAR-MAP lands, then (4) with feature parity. Record each switch's before/after numbers here.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_2 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 2u32, "2026-10-23T13:32Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:32 x: RULE for switch (2), the runner shell, written before any measurement. Candidates: E = today's Elixir Xsim.Runner + Executors.fake; G = the Gleam shell (LIB-OTP-ACTORS actor over scheduler.spi) with the same fake executor (fake_out: sha256 of the sources, first 12 hex); R = scheduler.sync_run compiled to Rust (the deterministic arm, no concurrency) as the floor. Inputs: the prelude stream (65 deliveries), sim-stress.world.json's stream, and a 50k-event corpus from the scenario exports (seed 1). Each run on a quiet box (no live block, no other heavy job), 5 runs per candidate per input, rotating order. Measure wall time from process start (BEAM boot included for E and G), peak working set of the process tree (100 ms sampler) and the settled visible map. GATE: G is only eligible when its visible map equals E's on all 3 inputs (parity first). SWITCH G in for E when G's median wall is <= E's on the 50k corpus and its peak memory is <= E's + 25%. Otherwise keep E and record why. R is the floor, not a candidate for the shell (rule R2 does not apply to a long-lived host). If R beats G by more than 10x on the 50k corpus, open a follow-up to run batch replays (commute, schedule sweeps) on R + LIB-PAR-MAP instead. Results go in this item, numbers and all.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_3 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 3u32, "2026-10-23T13:53Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:53 x: component (3), daemon_memory.ex, split by the rule. PURE (drafted, queued for Cells after tibia's block): %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\tree_memory.spi, with memory_reading {samples; last_mb; procs; peak_mb}, sample_of (one 'procs mb' line), read_samples (an incremental fold, so a reader can pass just the new bytes), daemon_pid_of (the Cells daemon's 'spiral-cells<TAB>version<TAB>pid' hello) and 3 tests. ACTOR (Gleam, G1 + G3): a periodic timer (send_after interval) that reads new samples and answers peak calls; it finds the daemon pid by the TCP hello, retrying on a timer. SAMPLER (the measured part): today a pwsh child (Get-CimInstance loop, ~1 s per sample, a whole PowerShell per run). The R side of the rule says this is a native-library job (R3): one Rust primitive 'tree working set of pid' that walks the process table once per sample, which eoie already does (eoie_process_table, machine watch). By lib-first it should move to lib/spiral (process.spi has Gleam OS-process capture only) and be shared by eoie's machine watch and this actor, through the Gleam arm calling it as a NIF-free Rust CLI per sample or a long-lived Rust sampler emitting lines. Measure the pwsh sampler vs the Rust one (CPU per sample, sample period jitter) before switching, by the note-2 style rule.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_4 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 4u32, "2026-10-23T16:44Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:44 x: component (8) measured: the postability pass (build.ps1:42-51, one 'xc post-thread --dry-run' process per file) over 36 files / 1,644 posts. Wall 62.0 s, 45.4 s and 11.4 s in 3 runs (box loaded by two heavy builds, so very noisy). Per-file times don't follow size: frames.spi (0.7 KB, 6 posts) took 2.4 s, the same as glyphs.spi (6.6 KB, 49 posts), and 'xc --help' alone took 933 ms. So process start under contention dominates, not splitting. Consequence for the rule: a rayon arm inside the splitter (R1) can't pay here. The win is ONE process for all files: a batch form 'xc post-thread --dry-run FILE...' (or an export the build calls once) removes 35 of 36 process starts. Parallelism across files inside that one process comes second, and only if a quiet-box measurement then shows splitting time worth it. Re-measure both forms on a quiet box before choosing (rule from note 2: decide from medians, not loaded-box numbers).")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_5 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 5u32, "2026-10-23T16:49Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:49 x: the batch form from note 4 is drafted, so the quiet-box comparison has both arms: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_batch_dry_run.ps1 (applies after the thread-resume drafts). 'xc post-thread --dry-run FILE FILE...' becomes cli PreviewThreads (list), args kind 11, and xc run_preview_threads, which prints '=== <path>' before each file's preview and returns the worst exit code (1 for a missing file). Kind 11 is dispatched explicitly, because run_thread_kind's final else POSTS, so a missing branch would have turned a batch dry run into a real thread post. Parse test: 'post-thread --dry-run prelude.spi glyphs.spi'. NOT in land_all: per note 2's rule it lands only if the quiet-box measurement (36 single-file calls vs one batch call, 5 runs each, medians) shows a gain. Then build.ps1's postable loop becomes one call, split on '=== '.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_6 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 6u32, "2026-10-23T16:53Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:53 x: the quiet-box comparison is ready to run, pending a batch-capable xc. The sampler is %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\measure_postable.ps1 -Exe <xc.exe> (36 single-file calls vs one batch call, 5 rounds, alternating order, medians, free memory at the end). Record it with eoie, not ad hoc: 'eoie experiment register x/target/ledger X-POSTABLE-BATCH --test median --metric ms --group arm --arms single,batch --runs 5 --decision <switch to batch when its median is at least 30% below single's>', then analyze. The batch-capable binary comes cheaply after x-landing-trial: apply apply_batch_dry_run.ps1 to the trial copy and run an incremental 'cargo build --release' there.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_7 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 7u32, "2026-10-23T17:40Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:40 x: experiment pre-registered with eoie (no ad-hoc rule): X-POSTABLE-BATCH in x/target/ledger (welch on ms, arms single,batch, 5 runs, registered 17:39Z). Decision: batch lands when median(batch) <= 0.7 x median(single) and the 95% CI excludes 0; else keep single calls and close component 8. measure_postable.ps1 writes postable-batch.tsv (arm, round, ms) for 'eoie experiment analyze'.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_8 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 8u32, "2026-10-23T18:25Z", "x", "2026-10-23 14:25 x: component (3)'s pure core PASSES. Cells on x-world tree_memory.spi (after the formatter pass): 3 tests on rust + gleam (samples_fold_into_the_peak, readings_continue_across_reads, the_daemon_hello_names_its_pid). Left for (3): the Gleam actor (LIB-OTP-ACTORS) and the Rust tree sampler (the lib-first move of eoie's process-table walk).")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_9 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 9u32, "2026-10-23T21:34Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:34 x: component (3)'s Gleam actor is DRAFTED, not yet checked: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\memory_watch.spi. It is an otp_actor.new_with_self actor (main's 6525F5CC) with watch_message = Tick | Peak (subject memory_reading). A Tick folds one sample text through tree_memory.read_samples and re-arms itself with send_after every_ms; Peak answers a call with the reading. The sampler is injected as a 'sample : () -> string' thunk, because file_system.read_all_text has no Gleam arm (Zig/F#/Rust only), and so the actor stays neutral between note 3's sampler options (pwsh, Rust tree walk, port). Test: two Ticks over '3 120\\n5 340\\n', then a Peak call expects samples=4 last=340 procs=5 peak=340. Next: add it after tree_memory in the x-shell package and run Cells on gleam (needs a free slot). Lib gap for main: read_all_text Gleam arm (simplifile or file:read_file).")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_10 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 10u32, "2026-10-23T21:47Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:47 x: component (3)'s Gleam actor PASSES: memory_watch 1/1 on gleam (chain15, 17:45). Two Ticks fold through tree_memory and a Peak call answers samples=4 last=340 procs=5 peak=340. Left for (3): the real sampler (note 3's pwsh vs Rust tree-walk measurement) and a Gleam file or port source for the sample thunk.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_11 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 11u32, "2026-10-23T22:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:52 x: switch (2)'s G candidate gets a runnable entry, DRAFTED: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\xsim_shell_entry.spi (gleam; staged in the x-shell package). 'xsim-shell --replay STREAM [--max-concurrency N]' decodes an xsim-events stream (events.decoded_stream) and settles it through the OTP shell (runner_shell over scheduler.spi, fake executor). It prints 'XSHELL visible=N results=M'. Replay keeps note 2's inputs identical for E and G (Elixir: mix xsim.run --replay STREAM), which sidesteps the cells-parsing mismatch of SIM-IN-SPIRAL n40. Parity for G holds through the_otp_shell_settles_like_sync_run plus sync_run's Elixir parity (scheduler_parity). Tests: replay settles (visible=2), argument checks. main needs LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS (argv + read_all_text on Gleam) before it can run; the tests don't touch it. Still open for the measurement: a gleam run/escript launcher and the 100 ms tree-memory sampler (memory_watch can serve once it has a real sample source).")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_12 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 12u32, "2026-10-23T22:53Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:53 x: launcher skeleton for switch (2)'s G arm, staged (not run): %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\shell-launch\\ (gleam.toml at lib's pins: gleam_stdlib 1.0.5, gleam_erlang 1.3, gleam_otp 1.3). launch.ps1 -Stream FILE [-MaxConcurrency N] compiles xsim_shell_entry.spi with lib.ps1 BuildSpiral -Backend Gleam into src/xsim_shell.gleam (the pattern x/apps/sim/build.ps1:31-33 uses for frames), gleam builds it, then times one 'gleam run -m xsim_shell -- --replay FILE' from process start (BEAM boot included, as note 2's rule requires) and prints the XSHELL line + wall_ms. Run order once LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS lands: 1 run as a smoke test, then the note-2 protocol (5 runs per candidate per input, rotating, quiet box).")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_13 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 13u32, "2026-10-23T23:54Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:54 x: RULE for component (7), written before any measurement. Candidates: E = today's 'mix xsim.run' corpus run (build.ps1:112: the lib corpus, --frames --duplicates 0.15 --personas lurker=2+fickle=3+editor=2+breaker=2 --evict --breaking 1 --churn 2 --users 3 --edits 3); R1 = Spiral 'xsim run' (xsim_entry, Rust, schedules sequential); R2 = R1 with its schedule loop over lib par' (LIB-PAR-MAP, after next7). Input: the same corpus at --schedules 8 (enough schedules for parallelism to show), quiet box, 5 runs per candidate, rotating order, wall from process start, peak working set by a 100 ms tree sampler. PARITY GATE (first): per schedule, the XSIM summary lines must be equal except the peak_beam_mb and ms fields; the commute verdict lines too. This needs R1 to take cells exactly like Elixir (SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY's shared parser), so (7) is blocked on it, not on speed. SWITCH build.ps1's corpus run E -> R1 when R1's median wall <= E's median. ADOPT R2 over R1 when R2's median <= 0.7 x R1's median at 8 schedules with identical lines; otherwise keep R1 and record why. Results go here.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_14 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 14u32, "2026-10-23T23:55Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:55 x: notes 2 and 13's rules are now registered in eoie's ledger (C:\\home\\git\\x\\target\\ledger, 23:55Z, before any outcome): X-SHELL-SWITCH (welch on ms, arms elixir,gleam), X-CORPUS-R1 (elixir,r1) and X-CORPUS-R2 (r1,r2). eoie's median test is one-arm, so component (7)'s three-way comparison is two two-arm experiments. The decisions quote the notes' medians, thresholds and parity gates verbatim; analysis goes through 'eoie experiment analyze' on the measured TSVs.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_15 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 15u32, "2026-10-23T23:58Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:58 x: component (3)'s actor made incremental, ready for a real sample source (a sampler appending 'procs mb' lines to a file, read through LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS's Gleam read_all_text). memory_watch keeps consumed : i32 and folds only the text after it. A partial last line (the sampler mid-write: '5 34' would parse as 34 MB) waits for its newline; a shrunken text (a restarted sampler) is read again from the start. lib's sm'.last_index_of has no Gleam arm, so the tail is found with pieces '\\n'. Tests: two ticks over the same complete text fold once (samples=2); a partial last line is held back (samples=1). This replaces the 17:45 test that expected samples=4; that run passed with the old non-incremental fold. Queued after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_16 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 16u32, "2026-10-24T00:32Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:32 x: component (3) sample source drafted and measured (one data point, CPU-light box). %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\tree_sample.ps1 -Root <pid> prints memory_watch's 'procs mb' line for a process tree: one CIM snapshot, a breadth-first walk over parent ids with a seen-set (guards pid reuse loops), and '0 0' for a missing root. Results: my shell's tree '2 143', explorer's tree '61 4881', a missing pid '0 0'. Cost: one sample = 2,266 ms wall, almost all pwsh startup + Get-CimInstance. That's too slow for note 2's 100 ms sampler, and supports note 3's plan of a native tree walk (the lib-first move of eoie's process-table walk). Wiring to try: memory_watch's sample thunk = process.try_capture_combined_list 'pwsh' ['-NoProfile';'-File';'tree_sample.ps1';'-Root';pid] (Gleam arm drafted in LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS batch 5), appending to the text it folds.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_17 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 17u32, "2026-10-24T00:39Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:39 x: memory_watch's sample source is now a union (x-world draft; the x-shell copy is updated after chain20 so the running chain isn't disturbed): GrowingText (a file a sampler appends to, folded incrementally, partial last line held back) | FreshLine (a command printing one 'procs mb' line per tick, each line folded). tree_sampler script root = FreshLine over process.try_capture_combined_list pwsh tree_sample.ps1 -Root <pid> (Gleam arm from LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS batch 5; the inl is reached only once wired, so the live lib's missing arm can't break tests). New test: a fresh-line source counts every tick (samples=2). I caught a semantic slip while writing it: the incremental fold alone would read a fresh line per tick as 'nothing new'.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_18 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 18u32, "2026-10-24T03:13Z", "x", "23:13 x: component (3) lib-first now that LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS is live. memory_watch.complete_lines is sm'.substring 0 (sm'.last_index_of newline + 1), which closes n15's 'last_index_of has no Gleam arm' gap. grown's tail cut uses sm'.substring too; x prelude's pieces/cut/rest_from are gone from this file (X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE direction). Units checked at the compiler: Gleam StringLength is bit_array.byte_size, so length, substring and last_index_of all count bytes on both backends. The no-newline case falls out of -1 + 1 = 0. Copied over x-shell\\client (the n68 step); Cells check job-1792811606552-x-memory-watch-check runs after the regexp bisect.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_19 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 19u32, "2026-10-24T04:39Z", "x", "10-24 00:39 x: component (3) lib-first VERIFIED. memory_watch 3/3 on gleam (job-1792812757759, 00:37, 76 s) with complete_lines = sm'.substring 0 (sm'.last_index_of newline + 1) and the sm'.substring tail cut. The x-shell package compiles again after cells_executor's nominal moved to $'##(Bool, String)'.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_20 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 20u32, "2026-10-25T15:52Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:52 -04:00 x: component (3)'s native sampler, checked before drafting (search before 'new'). lib has no process-table code (grep sysinfo/Toolhelp over lib/spiral: 0). eoie has three process-table walks: eoie_process_table/host.spi (ancestor_pids and process_stopped, as multi-statement rust blocks of the old era), eoie_jobs and eoie_machine_watch/host.spi process_rows_sampled. The eoie team is reworking that sampler right now under EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH (n2: cores = cpu_usage/100 * cpus, one shared scan for ps, watch and health). Decision: x does NOT draft its own walk. The lib-first target is ONE lib module ('process_table': snapshot, row ids, parent, resident bytes as one-call sysinfo primitives, with the tree fold in Spiral) that eoie's watch and x's memory_watch both call. It is drafted after eoie's sampler fix lands, so the cpu attribution fix is not forked. Until then memory_watch keeps its injected FreshLine source (tree_sample.ps1, ~2.3 s per sample, n16), which is fine for the shell's minutes-long runs and not for a 100 ms sampler. Dep noted: EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_21 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 21u32, "2026-10-27T11:41Z", "x", "2026-10-27 07:41 x: map COMPLETE and the item split. The map is note 1 plus today's crates. RUST (R2 per-command CLI, R3 TLS): xc_requests (site requests, the Bluesky session), xc_posted, xc_weights, xc_chunks, xc_domains, alongside the existing client crates. DUAL (one Spiral source, both arms, tested on rust + gleam): splitter, defuse, tlds, glyphs and reading; the sim calls splitter's split_cell through its Gleam xsim_frames build, so these modules must keep both arms. GLEAM: the sim runner shell (component 2), the memory_watch actor (3), the future API server (4) and xc watch (5). KEEP: invariants, digest, view. The measured switches (component 2's shell E vs G, component 7's corpus E/R1/R2, component 3's native sampler) move to X-BACKEND-SWITCHES with their registered ledger experiments (X-SHELL-SWITCH, X-CORPUS-R1, X-CORPUS-R2, X-POSTABLE-BATCH), so GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT can build on the map now (main 10-27 07:3x: it is tibia's only blocker).")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_1 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:18Z", "eoie", "07:18 user 07:1x: 'we have backlog for days of work, categorizations considering weights, duration, machine load, etc. why you're still waiting for shells? that means eoie is flawed and opus unable to properly manage development' and 'put in the backlog to fix this both in eoie and claude memory, this makes the experience of using this tool so annoying'. Measured flaw: agile next ranks by WSJF only and has no --load filter (agile list has --load light, agile next does not), so while a heavy job held the slots, next kept offering compile-bound items and the agent idled on watchers. Fix: (1) agile next gains --load light|medium|heavy|none like list; (2) agile next --fits reads the machine (eoie window list: held heavy slots, free memory) and keeps only items whose load fits now (both heavy slots busy or free memory under the heavy floor -> light and none only), printing 'fits now: <load classes>' in its receipt; (3) items with load unset count as light only when their next step needs no compile; a groom pass sets load on the top 30 eoie items (drafts/reviews light, previews/releases heavy); (4) the Stop hook's ready list comes from 'agile next --fits' instead of the plain WSJF list, so an idle stop is refused while a fitting item exists. Done when: with both heavy slots held, 'agile next . --team eoie --fits' lists only light/none items and the hook quotes that list. Tests: next_cli cases for --load and --fits over a fixture windows home.")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_2 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:27Z", "eoie", "07:27 part 1 drafted and chained into release J (scratch next-fits/apply-next-load.ps1, prelaunch clean on a live copy): agile next --load light|medium|heavy|none[,...] filters the ranking through the same planning filter agile list uses (agile_planning_filters export), comma list = any, invalid word refused with a named usage; next_cli test covers light, light+heavy and a refused word. Part 4 is live now, without waiting for a release: the shared Stop hook (~/.claude/hooks/autonomous-gate.ps1, backup in scratch backups/) checks eoie window list, and when both heavy slots are held it quotes the team's 'agile list --open --load light --sort priority' rows from the FIRST block, not only after repeated idle stops (17 eoie rows parsed in a test). Left: part 2 (--fits reading the machine inside eoie) and part 3 (groom load on the top 30 items so the light list is complete).")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_3 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:28Z", "eoie", "07:28 part 3 groomed: load set on 40 open P0-P2 eoie items by one rule, load = machine cost of the item's NEXT step (heavy: release, preview or full regen; medium: a compile-window check; light: design, draft, review, grooming; none: waits outside the machine). heavy 7, medium 4, none 1, light 28. agile list --team eoie --open --load light now lists 45 rows instead of 17.")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_4 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 4u32, "2026-10-23T12:16Z", "eoie", "08:16 part 2 drafted for release L (scratch next-fits/apply-next-fits.ps1, after K because K threads min_free through verdict_text): eoie_window_query exports eoie_fitting_loads, which asks the window admission verdict itself whether a compile window and a heavy-build would be granted now (heavy also needs a free heavy slot) and answers light,none[,medium][,heavy]; agile next --fits ranks with that load list and the receipt names it (load=...). Full chain dry run on GE2's raw output: H, I, J, K (76 writes) then L with 6 slices (34 writes) apply clean; on that tree subcommand.spi, eoie_adoption classify.spi, eoie_crate_gate model.spi and agile_task_next ranking.spi (whose package includes task_next.spi) pass. Found on the way: release I adds VerbEnv/VerbMirror after VerbResume, so the crate-gate slice now anchors on the VerbResume line alone.")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_5 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 5u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): PROC-7 (supervision never starved: control-path capacity, heavy admission under caps), HEALTH-2 (effective capacity and worker attribution). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_6 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 6u32, "2026-10-24T07:02Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:02 main: a concrete gap hit at 01:2x during tibia's live block: 'agile next . --team main --load light 20' is rejected (unexpected argument --load; next takes --as/--team/--tag/--prefix/--open/--ready/--blocked), while 'agile list' accepts --load. During a live block only light work is allowed, which is exactly when an agent asks next for light items; I fell back to reading the whole ranked list. Smallest step toward this item: accept --load on next with list's semantics, and let a held live-gui window imply --load light.")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_7 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 7u32, "2026-10-25T08:00Z", "eoie", "04:00 eoie: both parts are in the live tree (landed with LAND33, carried through the splits): part 1, agile next --load <loads> (loads_pass in agile_task_next; flag parsing in eoie_agile_next_args/args.spi), and part 2, --fits (args.spi asks eoie_window_query's eoie_fitting_loads: light,none, plus medium when a compile window would be granted, plus heavy when a heavy-build would be granted and a slot is free), with the next_cli tests. Main's 03:02 case is covered by --fits: a held live-gui window blocks compile and heavy admission, so --fits gives load=light,none. Both ship in SLATE. Left: the post-install CLI check (agile next . --team eoie --fits during a held window shows load=light,none) and the consolidated PROC-7/HEALTH-2 capacity notes.")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_8 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 8u32, "2026-10-26T08:24Z", "eoie", "04:24 eoie: the remaining post-install CLI check is written and wired into the rename release's post-install (kit scratch clean\\respell\\next-fits-check.ps1, called by post-install-RENAME.ps1 as a required check). It plants a held live-gui window in a fixture windows home (KIT_WINDOWS_HOME, never the real registry) and asserts that agile next --team eoie --fits answers load=light,none, and that with no window held the load list includes medium. Negative control run now against the installed pre-SLATE eoie.exe: both cases FAIL with no load= answer (the flag is not in that binary), so the check cannot pass vacuously. Runs on kit.exe right after finalize.")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_9 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 9u32, "2026-10-27T00:54Z", "kit", "20:54 kit: verified on the INSTALLED kit.exe (C0F2D2F3C66BFD43, published 20:26:42) by post-install-RENAME after the finalize: next --fits 2/2 (live-gui held gives load=light,none; no window gives medium and heavy too). This was the last open step, so the item is done.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_1 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:27 x: drafted for SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL (world.ex needs maps and sets; lib had none that is backend-neutral: mapm wraps Rust/F# HashMap only). Draft: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\dictm.spi. API: empty, insert, remove, has, present, find (option), size, fold (order unspecified across backends), updated, keys; set = dict k () with set_empty/add/drop/has/size/items. Granular per the user's 10-23 direction: one foreign type per backend via a switch record, every function one backend_switch arm with a single expression, no blobs. Facts found on the way: Spiral closures reach Gleam curried (fn(Int) -> fn(Int) -> Int, measured with a probe), so fold calls !f(acc)(k)(v); '!=' inside a host splice breaks the parser (PartialEq::ne instead). Verified: compiles to Gleam and Rust (11-12 s), and Cells runs its 3 test blocks (insert/find/remove, order-free fold, set add/drop) on rust + gleam: 3/3 passed (process compiler, memo off, 78 s, no other Cells job active). Landing: copy into lib/spiral, drop the '--package .' line, run the lib corpus check; main owns the landing window. Note: in an edition-3 package x-compat flags 'dict.new' as an autolink (harmless in lib).")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_2 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:46Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:46 x: cross-backend caveat found by the inbox port. On Rust, Spiral flattens a closure's tuple parameter into separate arguments, so dictm.fold with a TUPLE accumulator, e.g. fun (a, b) k v => ..., breaks on Rust ('this function takes 2 arguments but 1 argument was supplied') while Gleam works. Options for landing: document 'accumulator must not be a tuple' with a test, or make the Rust arm pass a record. Callers today use an i32/list/dict accumulator, or fold to an index and look it up.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_3 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 3u32, "2026-10-23T12:09Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:09 x: second Rust-only closure-shape finding, from scheduler's tests. Spiral's Rust output DROPS unit (()) parameters from closures, so dictm.fold over a set (dict k (), value ()) called f(acc)(k)(v) on a closure that takes no v: 'this function takes 0 arguments but 1 argument was supplied' (12x), while Gleam was fine. Fix in the draft: a value-free primitive fold_keys (Gleam dict.fold with fn(acc, k, _) calling f(acc)(k); Rust keys().fold calling (f)(acc)(k.clone())), with keys and so set_items built on it. The set test now also checks set_items' length. Landing rule: any dictm or other host-splice binding that calls a Spiral closure must avoid tuple-typed and unit-typed parameters on Rust; both shapes need a lib test.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_4 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 4u32, "2026-10-23T12:41Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:41 x: fold_keys landed in the draft and PASSES. Cells on %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\dictm.spi: 3/3 on rust + gleam (process compiler, memo off). New primitive: fold_keys f init d, with Gleam 'dict.fold(!d, !init, fn(acc, k, _) { !f(acc)(k) })' and Rust '(*!d).keys().fold(!init, |acc, k| (!f)(acc)(k.clone()))'. keys is defined through it, so set_items no longer passes a unit value through a Rust closure; the set_add_drop test now also checks listm.length (set_items s) = 2. Cause: on Rust, a closure's unit () parameter is dropped when it reaches a host splice, so the old keys (= fold with a () value) failed with E0057 wrong argument count in every user of set k = dict k (). Open for main when this enters lib/spiral: dictm.keys order is sorted on Rust (BTreeMap) but unspecified on Gleam (Erlang maps are sorted only up to 32 keys). Either sort keys in the Gleam arm, which needs an order on k, or document keys as unordered and sort at call sites that print.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_5 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 5u32, "2026-10-23T13:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:06 x: the root cause is wider than note 4's unit-parameter case. On Rust, Spiral compiles a closure with each record or tuple parameter FLATTENED into separate arguments (a visible_entry {key; result {status; out}} value became 3 arguments), while a host splice calls the closure with one value per parameter. So ANY $'...' that calls a Spiral closure with a user-chosen type breaks on Rust with E0057 once that type is a record, a tuple or unit. Gleam is unaffected (curried, whole values). runner_checks hit it through dictm.fold over a dict string visible_entry. Fix in both drafts (lib-draft and the client copy): no host splice calls a closure with user-chosen types. keys is now the only callback, and its closure is dictm's own fixed fun (acc : list k) key => Cons (key, acc) (keys come out descending on both backends, as before). fold is plain Spiral: keys |> listm.rev |> listm.fold with present lookups, ascending on both backends. fold_keys is removed. One constraint remains: k itself must be a single-value type (string or ints), which is true for every current user. RULE for lib reviews (LIB-OTP-ACTORS too): a host splice may call a Spiral closure only when the module fixes the closure's parameter types to non-compound ones. Retest queued.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_6 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 6u32, "2026-10-23T14:21Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:21 x: note 4's open key-order question, drafted (both copies, queued for Cells after tibia's block). The Gleam arm of keys now sorts with Erlang's lists:sort/1 (a '@ external' named dictm_term_sorted) before folding into the Spiral list. Erlang term order is total and generic, so no comparator closure crosses the boundary, and for same-typed keys it equals Rust's Ord (binaries and strings bytewise, integers numerically). Both backends now give the same descending list for any size; before, Erlang maps were sorted only up to 32 keys. New test keys_are_sorted_past_32_entries: 40 keys '1000'..'1039', first '1039', last '1000', length 40. fold (Spiral over keys) therefore visits ascending on both backends, so the scheduler's queue order is backend-independent.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_7 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 7u32, "2026-10-23T14:40Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:40 x: name history, so landing doesn't confuse it. The OLD fork core had a module named dictm (Python-only: rm-list buckets, hash_cython; CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE notes 2, 4, 5), and candidate v1 removed it (note 11: 'dictm*, dictm_utils/*' removed). Today's core (VS Code Plugin/core: arraym, base, core, dep_optionm, limit, listm, loop, monad, optionm, sm) has no dictm, so lib/spiral's dictm.spi clashes with nothing. It is a new neutral module, not a port of the old one.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_8 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 8u32, "2026-10-23T21:37Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:37 x: dictm draft 4/4 on rust + gleam (chain11 + chain14). keys_are_sorted_past_32_entries had failed only on zig, because it lacked the ///> rust/gleam targets the other tests carry (dictm has no Zig arm). Targets added.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_9 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 9u32, "2026-10-23T21:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:57 main: x handed off the draft dictm.spi (BTreeMap / gleam dict, sorted keys, 4/4 PASS) in %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft for main to land. Landing rule: Cells gate on the tree-lib baseline subset rule, after the tonight chain; rebase over eoie's 17:56 lib-gaps land (sm', am', process, testing, env changed) first, since x's lib_gaps.spi also touches listm'/sm'.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_10 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 10u32, "2026-10-23T22:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:30 main: the landing draft of x's dictm.spi is in %TEMP%\\lib-dictm\\lib\\spiral\\dictm.spi. It follows the lib's layout (/// # dictm, open sm'_operators, then test dictm static, one /// ### heading per definition). It drops x-world's '///- --package .' line and the prose header, whose 'iteration order unspecified' contradicted the sorted keys. keys now ascends: Rust keys().rev() with Cons prepends; Gleam list.reverse(lists:sort(...)). fold drops its listm.rev, and the test is renamed keys_ascend_past_32_entries with expected first 1000 and last 1039. Rust insert and remove are clone-then-insert/remove blocks instead of rebuilding the map through an iterator chain. No line is over 120 characters. mapm checked: Rust-only mutable HashMap/BTreeMap from the old era, no overlap. Gate (%TEMP%\\lib-dictm\\gate.ps1, a fresh tree-lib copy plus dictm in package.spiproj, lib_gate -File dictm.spi): GREEN only if every test row passes, at least 4. land.ps1 lands only on GREEN with no Cells erl in flight; it copies dictm.spi and appends '    dictm' to the lib's modules. Queued in requeue-after-block.ps1 (chain A, last), which runs on tibia's 'block end'.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_11 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 11u32, "2026-10-23T22:36Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:36 main: correction to note 10: the dictm gate is the generic lib-gate-kit\\gate.ps1 -Name LIB-DICTM with %TEMP%\\lib-dictm\\apply.ps1 (adds dictm.spi and appends '    dictm' to the modules), and it lands via lib-gate-kit\\land.ps1. The one-off gate.ps1 and land.ps1 are deleted. Verdict rule: every row passes, or every red is also red on the tree baseline; for a new module the baseline is empty, so every row must pass.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_12 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 12u32, "2026-10-24T00:28Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:28 main: lib-gate-kit gate.ps1 hardened after a data-flow trace, before its first run. (1) A header-only tests.tsv (0 rows, e.g. a load failure) counted as green, since Import-Csv gives an empty array, not null; 0 rows is now RED. (2) A stale verdict-<Name>.txt from an earlier run could let land.ps1 land after a gate that crashed before writing; the verdict file is now deleted at the start. (3) A new module's baseline (the file is absent from the tree lib) made lib_gate throw; it is now an empty baseline, so a new module must pass every row.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_13 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 13u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: gate job main-lib-dictm-gate finished 0 (GREEN) at 22:21; the land refused at 22:21 on a Cells erl still alive (pid 30280), the race land.ps1 now waits out (two clear 20 s samples, up to 90 min). Requeued as job-1792811312648-main-lib-dictm-land-2, after the lib chain's tail (job-1792810377882-main-lib-otp-single-land) so no gate copies the lib mid-write. dictm.spi is a new file, so the 22:21 verdict still holds.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_14 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 14u32, "2026-10-24T08:42Z", "x", "10-24 04:42 x: LIB-DICTM rebuilt to GRANULAR-HEAL n1 at main's request (main withdrew main-lib-dictm-land-2 over 5 violations). Slice: %TEMP%\\x-world\\lib-draft\\dictm-granular\\dictm.spi; census finds 0 sites, and Cells is 5/5 rust + gleam (job-1792831094255). Rust: Rc<BTreeMap> with copy-on-write in Spiral (rust_owned = Rc::unwrap_or_clone, rust.to_mut, then one-call $'!m.insert(!key, !value)' / $'!m.remove(&!key)', then rust_new_rc), and one-call reads (contains_key; get(&k).cloned() as option' |> optionm'.unbox; len as convert.unativeint |> convert.convert : i32; keys().cloned().collect() as am'.vec |> am'.vec_fold'). Gleam: dict.get as result' |> resultm.unbox; keys = dict.keys -> the lists:sort extern -> listm'.fold'. New test an_old_version_survives_an_insert. Lessons on the way: the !\\\\ splice drops unit arguments (a dict used as a set rendered `$2`), and an unpinned convert.convert or new_b_tree_map leaves value-restriction metavars. Sent to main for gate + land; SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL's apply_sim_modules follows.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_15 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 15u32, "2026-10-24T18:57Z", "main", "main 15:0x: x's dictm granular rewrite (%TEMP%\\lib-dictm-granular) gated GREEN (10:38, main-serial-heal-gates) and LANDED 13:52 through main-serial-lands (dictm added to lib/spiral/package.spiproj after oauth). The x-side consumers can drop their copies now.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_16 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 16u32, "2026-10-24T23:59Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:59 main groom: done. The item's scope was 'drafted and test-passed by x, to be landed by main', and the land happened at 13:52 (main-serial-lands; gate GREEN 10:38; dictm listed in lib/spiral/package.spiproj). Moving the consumers is x's own work and is tracked in x's items.")
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:31Z", "tibia-lane", "Found by tibia-lane 07:2x in src/live.spi; bisected 08:0x in a scratch package (scratch repro-curried/repro.spi, Cells c target, packageDir lib plus spiral-). The module main returns a 2-tuple of closures, f (a : i32) (b : i32) : i32 = a + b and g. Results, one cell each: f one 2 |> _assert_eq 3i32 passes; a local untyped curried helper (inl call a b = f a b) passes, so it was NOT the cause, despite the first version of this note; f one 2 % 2 |> _assert_eq 1i32 passes; (f one 2 % 2 = 1) |> _assert_eq (g one) passes; f one 2 % 2 = 1 |> _assert_eq (g one) FAILS; f one 2 = 3 |> _assert_eq true FAILS. Both failures: (Mix) FatalError: The main of a Zig program returns a single value (an i32 exit code, unit, or one value that is discarded), not a tuple. So |> binds tighter than =, and x = y |> _assert_eq z parses as x = (y |> _assert_eq z). The defect: that misparse reaches codegen and reports a Zig main tuple, instead of a type error at the comparison (the right side is unit, or the assert gets the wrong type). Minimal repro: a test cell whose body is 1 = 1 |> _assert_eq true. Fix candidates (compiler lane): a type error at =, or a lint for an unparenthesized comparison feeding |>. tibia wraps the comparison in parentheses or binds it to a typed local first.")
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T14:47Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:47 main: code read and a staged probe. Cells compiles a test cell once (cells.ex 605-633). On a unit-result diagnostic it retries with '0i32' appended; on a value result it retries with show_value_main, which binds the body and prints it. The Zig 'main returns ... not a tuple' error comes from codegen on the first compile, so the defect sits in type inference or partial evaluation, which let 1 = (1 |> _assert_eq true) through as a tuple-valued main, not in Cells. Probe staged (%TEMP%\\curried-main-probe, a package over lib; run.ps1 runs mix spiral.notebook --gate, output to the run cache): four test cells on c and zig, (1) the misparse 1i32 = 1i32 |> _assert_eq true, (2) the parenthesized form, (3) the bare mismatch 1i32 = (), and (4) 1i32 |> _assert_eq true. Whichever of (3)/(4) compiles without a type error names the culprit: equality across types, or _assert_eq across types. Runs in the first compile gap after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 3u32, "2026-10-23T15:38Z", "tibia-lane", "Team tag tibia -> main (tibia-lane 11:4x): tibia found it, the fix is a compiler change, which is main's lane. tibia works around it with typed locals.")
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 4u32, "2026-10-23T21:56Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:56 main: probe queued tonight as job-1792770218847 (after the hopacsota promotion): %TEMP%\\curried-main-probe (probe.spi, 4 test blocks on c and zig: 1i32 = 1i32 |> _assert_eq true, the parenthesized form, 1i32 = (), and 1i32 |> _assert_eq true), run through mix spiral.notebook --gate; output in spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE. Reading rule: if the unparenthesized form still reaches codegen ('The main of a Zig program returns ... not a tuple') while 1i32 = () gives a type error, the fix is a type check of the comparison's operands before codegen, with the unparenthesized form as the regression sample.")
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 5u32, "2026-10-24T00:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:51 main: status 0 Planned -> 20 Active: the probe (4 cells on c and zig, decides whether equality or _assert_eq lets the mismatch through) is requeued as job-1792801907956 in chain B; its reading rule is in note 4.")
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 6u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming, corrected at 23:51: main-curried-main-probe (exit 0) does NOT reproduce the misleading codegen error. It stops with 'FatalError: File main has a type error somewhere in its path' at probe.spi:4:20, the |> of cell 1 (1i32 = 1i32 |> _assert_eq true, which parses as 1i32 = (1i32 |> _assert_eq true)). All 4 cells fail only because one type error fails the whole file in that run. The 23:08 reading ('reproduces the bug') was wrong.")
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 7u32, "2026-10-24T03:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:51 main: closed by note 4's prewritten reading rule. The fix was needed only if the unparenthesized form still reached codegen ('The main of a Zig program returns ... not a tuple'). On the installed single-flight (B553EF6B78E6EE29, probe ran 22:25) it is a type error at the misparsed |> instead, which is the requested behavior; some change in the tree since tibia's 10-23 07:2x find fixed it. tibia: reopen if your typed-locals workaround still hits the codegen message on the current compiler.")
inl note_of_gleam_erlang_1x_1 () = Note ("GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:42Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:42 main: filed from LIB-PAR-MAP/LIB-OTP-ACTORS groundwork. Current releases (hexdocs, 10-23): gleam_erlang 1.3.0 (published 2025-08-03, latest on hex) and gleam_otp 1.3.0. Cell Gleam projects pin gleam_erlang '>=0.34.0 and <1.0.0' (apps/cells lib/spiral/cells/targets.ex:344) and gleam_stdlib 0.57. The 1.x process API renames what lib process.spi uses: selecting_anything -> select_other, select_forever -> selector_receive_forever, select(selector, ms) -> selector_receive(from, within). selecting(subject, f) -> select_map; monitor_process -> monitor, with Down = ProcessDown(monitor, pid, reason: ExitReason Normal|Killed|Abnormal). gleam/erlang/os is gone (platform.is_windows imports it: give it an os:type external first). Plan: (1) lib migrations on 0.34 first, where an equivalent exists, so the pin bump is a no-op diff; (2) bump targets.ex to gleam_erlang >= 1.3.0 and < 2.0.0, gleam_otp >= 1.3.0 and < 2.0.0, and stdlib to latest; (3) the granular binding scratchpad par-lab/erlang_process.spi (one nominal per Gleam type, one one-expression primitive per function, 4 gleam test blocks) moves into lib. Gate: a lib Cells --gate run on gleam + the KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR process_capture tests, before and after. Cells and lib edits happen only outside tibia's live blocks.")
inl note_of_gleam_erlang_1x_2 () = Note ("GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:46Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:46 main: staged %TEMP%\\gleam-erlang-1x\\apply.ps1 (dry run by default). Five anchors, each matching once on the live tree: the targets.ex @gleam_deps pins (stdlib >=1.0.5, gleam_erlang >=1.3.0, new gleam_otp >=1.3.0, envoy >=1.2.0, gtempo >=7.4.0, each <2/<8); process.spi selecting_anything -> select_other, select_forever -> selector_receive_forever, select(selector, left) -> selector_receive; and platform.is_windows reading os:type through one @external instead of gleam/erlang/os. Compatibility read from the hex release requirements: gleam_erlang 1.3.0 needs stdlib >=0.53; gary 1.1.0 needs stdlib >=0.34 and <2; gtempo 7.4.0 needs stdlib >=0.60, gleam_time 1 and tzif 1. The matrix Gleam shim (tests/native-shims/gleam) already resolves stdlib 1.x and its rows pass, so codegen output is stdlib-1 clean. No other repo uses the 0.x names (grep with *.ex positive control). Landing rule: a gap with no live block and no Cells erl.exe running. Apply, then lib_gate on process.spi and platform.spi (gleam), then par-lab/erlang_process.spi's 4 gleam tests in a scratch package. Revert if any gleam row fails that passed before.")
inl note_of_gleam_erlang_1x_3 () = Note ("GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", 3u32, "2026-10-23T13:27Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:27 main: post-block lib landing staged in %TEMP%\\lib-land-1023. The copy is lib/spiral at 09:2x plus apps/cells targets.ex, with tree hashes in tree-hashes.tsv and targets-tree-hash.txt. On it: 23 formatter restores (pack-next5e, from the pre-05:08 originals); GLEAM-ERLANG-1X (process.spi, platform.spi, targets.ex pins); LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM as sm-c-arms.ps1 (portable_matches_at and portable_index_of helpers before index_of, one-line C arms in index_of, contains, starts_with and ends_with, and test searches_agree_on_c for c, gleam and rust after contains, with tibia's cases; reproducible byte for byte); and the five new modules erlang_process, otp_actor, otp_supervisor, step_runtime and par' in package.spiproj. Jobs, all after tibia-block-pr26: main-lib-land-gate (job-1792761989271; gate.ps1 runs a Cells copy with the new pins and gates 8 files from the copy lib), then main-lib-land (job-1792761990973), then cells-pack, then hopacsota. Land rule, written before the gate (land.ps1): the formatter restores land on their hash guards. process and platform plus targets.ex land only if both gates pass. sm' lands only if its gate passes. The five new modules and package.spiproj land only if all five pass and the 1.x pins landed. 'Pass' means failed 0, discovered > 0 and no target-not-passed row. lib_land refuses any file whose tree hash moved since the copy.")
inl note_of_gleam_erlang_1x_4 () = Note ("GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", 4u32, "2026-10-23T20:04Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:04 main: lib land of 16:02 (job-1792766411950, after a re-gate of the files the first gate run poisoned): LANDED 12 formatter restores (convert, lustre, parsing, reflection, state_core, testing, trace, leptos/leptos, leptos/ui/accordion + tab_link, near/wallet near_wallet, rust/testing) and seq.spi + listm'.spi (LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS, gates 26/26 and 23/23). The 1.x set is HELD. Its gate found three real defects, all fixed in %TEMP%\\lib-land-1023 since: (1) process.spi still called atom.create_from_string (7 sites; gleam_erlang 1.x names it atom.create) -> gleam build failed on all 5 process tests; (2) erlang_process.down_reason read event.reason, which Gleam rejects on the 1.x Down union (ProcessDown | PortDown) -> now a case over both constructors; (3) otp_supervisor's test left the actor message type open (ETY-ValueRestriction) -> annotated (_ : i32). Also fixed: step_runtime.spi assigned mut fields through a non-mut record (it now passes 2/2), which had failed every file of the first run. Still red: par' map_keeps_order/map_empty on rust and fsharp (the thread-scope arm). Next: one gate pass over process, erlang_process, otp_supervisor, par' (plus the rest of the set) on the land copy, then land the set with targets.ex.")
inl note_of_gleam_erlang_1x_5 () = Note ("GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", 5u32, "2026-10-23T20:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:37 main: LANDED 16:36: process.spi (D4025378EE51F5FE, Gleam 5/5 after the atom.create rename), platform.spi (B8CCBD05C66A0BB0), and apps/cells targets.ex (7FC4A09F9B047C1C: gleam_erlang >= 1.3.0, gleam_otp >= 1.3.0, stdlib >= 1.0.5, envoy >= 1.2.0, gtempo >= 7.4.0). No x, tibia or eoie source calls gleam_erlang directly (ripgrep, positive control on lib), so every consumer reaches 1.x through these lib modules. Closed.")
inl note_of_lib_contains_c_arm_1 () = Note ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", 1u32, "2026-10-23T12:28Z", "tibia-lane", "Found by tibia-lane 08:3x: apps/host scenario.spi (monster mix parsing) failed on the c target at gcc with backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_lib_spiral; sm'.contains has arms for Gleam, Lua, Fsharp, Python, Rust, TypeScript, Zig and Lean, not C. sm'.split and am'.index have C arms now (LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C). Likely fix: a C arm as strstr(haystack, needle) != NULL over the String* ptr fields, the pattern of the other C string arms; plus a c target on contains' own test. tibia's host targets gleam and lua meanwhile, so nothing waits on it.")
inl note_of_lib_contains_c_arm_2 () = Note ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", 2u32, "2026-10-23T12:34Z", "tibia-lane", "C arms DRAFTED and tested 08:3x (tibia-lane; scratch c-arms/carms.spi). Same style as sm'.split's existing C arm: plain Spiral over core sm.length and sm.index, no host text. matches_at search s at compares the needle character by character from at. index_of = the first at from 0 with at + m <= n where matches_at holds, else -1. contains = index_of >= 0. starts_with = m <= n && matches_at at 0. ends_with = m <= n && matches_at at n - m. Lengths must be bound to i32 locals: an inline comparison of two generic sm.length results fails with ETY-ValueRestriction. Cells, 3 of 3 cells (c, lua and gleam) on one test against the lib contract: index_of on \"a.b%c\" gives 1 for \".\", 3 for \"%\", -1 for \"y\", 0 for the empty needle and 2 for \"b%c\"; contains, starts_with and ends_with each pass, fail and handle the empty value. To land through lib_land (main's process; main holds lib writes during tibia's live block): add C = fun () => <the scan> to each of the four backend_switch blocks, and a c target on their test. Note that index_of returns int there; the scan returns i32, so cast or annotate to match the signature.")
inl note_of_lib_contains_c_arm_3 () = Note ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", 3u32, "2026-10-23T13:27Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:27 main: post-block lib landing staged in %TEMP%\\lib-land-1023. The copy is lib/spiral at 09:2x plus apps/cells targets.ex, with tree hashes in tree-hashes.tsv and targets-tree-hash.txt. On it: 23 formatter restores (pack-next5e, from the pre-05:08 originals); GLEAM-ERLANG-1X (process.spi, platform.spi, targets.ex pins); LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM as sm-c-arms.ps1 (portable_matches_at and portable_index_of helpers before index_of, one-line C arms in index_of, contains, starts_with and ends_with, and test searches_agree_on_c for c, gleam and rust after contains, with tibia's cases; reproducible byte for byte); and the five new modules erlang_process, otp_actor, otp_supervisor, step_runtime and par' in package.spiproj. Jobs, all after tibia-block-pr26: main-lib-land-gate (job-1792761989271; gate.ps1 runs a Cells copy with the new pins and gates 8 files from the copy lib), then main-lib-land (job-1792761990973), then cells-pack, then hopacsota. Land rule, written before the gate (land.ps1): the formatter restores land on their hash guards. process and platform plus targets.ex land only if both gates pass. sm' lands only if its gate passes. The five new modules and package.spiproj land only if all five pass and the 1.x pins landed. 'Pass' means failed 0, discovered > 0 and no target-not-passed row. lib_land refuses any file whose tree hash moved since the copy.")
inl note_of_lib_contains_c_arm_4 () = Note ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", 4u32, "2026-10-23T15:38Z", "tibia-lane", "Team tag tibia -> main (tibia-lane 11:4x): main lands the arms in its post-block lib landing (note of 09:27); tibia's drafts are in that staging.")
inl note_of_lib_contains_c_arm_5 () = Note ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", 5u32, "2026-10-23T20:04Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:04 main: lib land of 16:02 (job-1792766411950, after a re-gate of the files the first gate run poisoned): LANDED 12 formatter restores (convert, lustre, parsing, reflection, state_core, testing, trace, leptos/leptos, leptos/ui/accordion + tab_link, near/wallet near_wallet, rust/testing) and seq.spi + listm'.spi (LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS, gates 26/26 and 23/23). sm'.spi (the C arms) HELD: the copy's sm' gate wrote no report file (its 47/48 run printed but the RawOut is missing), so the baseline-subset rule had nothing to compare; the tree baseline's reds are replace_regex on gleam and rust -d regex. Next: re-gate sm'.spi alone on the land copy, then land it if its reds are a subset.")
inl note_of_lib_contains_c_arm_6 () = Note ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", 6u32, "2026-10-23T20:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:37 main: LANDED 16:36 in spiral/lib/spiral/sm'.spi (A4FD87585FDCC6A5): C arms for index_of, contains, starts_with and ends_with plus portable_matches_at/portable_index_of and the searches_agree_on_c test. Gate 48/49 on the land copy; its one red, replace_regex on gleam and rust -d regex, is red on the tree lib too (baseline run), so it lands by the subset rule. Closed; tibia confirmed (16:3x).")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_1 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T14:18Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:18 x: found by a read-only scan of listm'.spi (inl rec bodies without var_is): map4 (line 31), rec (49), try_index_of (122), try_find (174), zip_with (226). exists' (334) shows the pattern: inl rec body ... and inl loop list = if var_is list |> not then body f list else join body (join f) (dyn list). Over static lists the bare inl rec folds away at compile time; over a runtime list the partial evaluator unrolls without end (derived from the code, the same failure mode as x's scheduler_parity 300 s timeout this morning). Callers in spiral lib/apps, eoie/src, x/apps, dice, alphabet and tibia/src: only seq.memoize (seq.spi:259) calls listm'.try_find, on *state, a runtime list. seq.memoize has no test (seq's test at :276 exercises memoize_) and no caller (lib uses stream.memoize), so it is latently broken, not failing. Draft of the fixed try_find with a runtime-list test: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\find_runtime.spi (try_find_any, queued for Cells rust + gleam). X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE note 9 needs the try_find fix before x's first_where can move to lib.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_2 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T14:22Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:22 x: CORRECTION to note 1, which I filed before I knew lib's convention. A trailing underscore names the RUNTIME variant: try_find_ (line 182), try_index_of_ (133) and zip_with_ (234) are let rec loops, and try_item has a sibling too, so the plain inl rec names are static by design, not broken. What remains: (1) seq.memoize (seq.spi:259) uses the static try_find on *state, a runtime list: a one-character fix to try_find_ (latent: no test or caller); (2) map4 has no runtime variant; (3) optional, the real foot-gun: a caller has to know the convention, and exists' shows the alternative (one name, var_is -> join). The find_runtime.spi draft (try_find_any) is that auto-switching form, if you want it. Priority stays P3 (latent only).")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_3 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T14:33Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:33 x: one-step patch for main: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_listm_runtime.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>, with anchored edits (each must match exactly once). (1) seq.spi: memoize and memoize_ were identical except try_find vs try_find_. Since state is a mut cell, memoize can never have a static use, so the patch removes its body and adds 'inl memoize seq = memoize_ seq' after memoize_: one implementation, name kept. (2) listm'.spi: map4_ (let rec), the runtime sibling of map4, following the _ convention. Dry-applied to copies of seq.spi and listm'.spi: both edits apply and read correctly. Not compiled (lib edits are yours to gate); the seq memoize test already exercises memoize_.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_4 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 4u32, "2026-10-23T15:50Z", "main", "2026-10-23 11:49 main: x's apply_listm_runtime.ps1 applied unchanged to main's lib land copy (%TEMP%\\lib-land-1023\\lib\\spiral; originals in pre-listm-backup), plus a runtime test for the new sibling: test map4_ sums four dyn lists [1;2;3] to [4;8;12]. Rides today's lib gate and landing (serial chain after next4 review-2 and the 5f build): gate.ps1 now runs seq.spi and listm'.spi too, and land.ps1 lands each only when its own gate report passes. listm'.spi was also a formatter-restore file, so it is taken out of the ungated restore path and out of the 5f refresh (which would have overwritten the patch with a formatted original); its 5f layout difference, if any, waits like process.spi's. The tree-hashes guard covers both files. Option (3), exists'-style auto-switching, not taken: it changes every static caller's residual, a separate measured item if wanted.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_5 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 5u32, "2026-10-23T20:04Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:04 main: LANDED 16:02 in spiral/lib/spiral: seq.spi (memoize delegates to memoize_, gate 26/26) and listm'.spi (map4_ runtime sibling + its dyn-list test, gate 23/23), landed hashes seq 1C930A681DE388AA, listm' 1D764CD9C6598857. Closed: (1) and (2) are in; (3), exists'-style auto-switching, stays not taken (it would change every static caller's residual; a separate measured item if wanted).")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_6 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 6u32, "2026-10-23T20:33Z", "x", "2026-10-23 16:33 x: main reports apply_listm_runtime.ps1 LANDED in spiral/lib/spiral at 16:02 (seq.memoize delegates to memoize_; listm'.map4_ added). Left on this item: only the optional auto-switching try_find (find_runtime.spi draft), at main's discretion.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_7 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 7u32, "2026-10-23T21:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:57 main: x handed off the draft find_runtime.spi + apply_listm_runtime.ps1 (auto try_find, memoize -> memoize_, map4_; 2/2 PASS) in %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft for main to land. Landing rule: Cells gate on the tree-lib baseline subset rule, after the tonight chain; rebase over eoie's 17:56 lib-gaps land (sm', am', process, testing, env changed) first, since x's lib_gaps.spi also touches listm'/sm'.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_8 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 8u32, "2026-10-23T22:43Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:43 main: x's apply_listm_runtime.ps1 is already in the tree: seq.memoize delegates to memoize_ (seq.spi:266) and listm'.map4_ exists (listm'.spi:39). A dry run on copies fails its first anchor for that reason. What is new in find_runtime.spi is the auto-switching find. Instead of a third variant (try_find_any next to try_find/try_find_), listm'.try_find itself now switches: a static list unrolls as before (inl rec body), and a list that var_is reports dynamic goes through a join point over dyn. Draft %TEMP%\\lib-listm-runtime\\apply.ps1 (2 anchors, dry run ok) adds x's two cases as try_find tests on rust and gleam. Gate: lib-gate-kit -Name LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS -Files listm'.spi seq.spi, chain A in requeue-after-block.ps1. Its anchors don't overlap LIB-GAPS-X's listm' edits, so both land in either order.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_9 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 9u32, "2026-10-23T23:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:08 main: correction to note 8. listm' naming convention: a plain name is the static inl rec form, a trailing underscore the runtime let rec loop, and a prime the var_is auto-switch (exists' at listm'.spi:355). So plain try_find stays as it is, and x's auto-switching find lands as a new try_find' with exists' shape, added after the try_find test. Tests are try_find_prime_searches_a_runtime_list_at_run_time and try_find_prime_folds_a_static_list: lib test names have no primes, and Cells matches '^test \\w+'. The apply script is rewritten (additive, dry run ok).")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_10 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 10u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: gate GREEN at 23:05 (seq.spi 26/26, 42 rows, red 0) on a lib copy that already held LIB-GAPS-X (take_ landed 23:00) and LIB-UNICODE; its land (try_find_prime) is running in the requeued chain.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_11 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 11u32, "2026-10-24T03:12Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:12 main: LANDED in live lib/spiral (job main-lib-listm-runtime-land, exit 0) after its 23:05 GREEN gate on a lib copy holding gaps-x and unicode. try_find' is in listm'.spi (line 210, line 211), shaped like exists'.")
inl note_of_lua_single_scrutinee_1 () = Note ("LUA-SINGLE-SCRUTINEE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T14:28Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:28 main: codegenLua's TyUnionUnbox emitted 'local __v = { vI }' and read __v[1] in every guard and payload, so each match allocated a table (a GC cost on Lua 5.1/WoW hot paths) and used one of the 200 local slots. Census: 559 such tables over the tree main.lua, 557 of them with one scrutinee. Staged %TEMP%\\pack-next8\\lua-single-scrutinee.ps1 (3 anchors, each matching once): with one scrutinee the guard reads 'vI ~= nil and vI.tag == ...' and the payload 'local vJ = vI._k', and only several scrutinees keep the table. Guards are evaluated in order before any body runs, so reading vI directly is equivalent. It parses, and is composed into pack-next8 (pack-next8 core A2FAD36DFC8E7141). Review rule: Lua residuals lose exactly those table lines and __v[1] reads, and every Lua row agrees with C.")
inl note_of_lua_single_scrutinee_2 () = Note ("LUA-SINGLE-SCRUTINEE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:17 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 60 by the rule 'implemented and staged in a pack or queued gate, awaiting review'. Where: pack-next8 patch, reviewed after next7.")
inl note_of_lua_single_scrutinee_3 () = Note ("LUA-SINGLE-SCRUTINEE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T07:27Z", "main", "10-25 03:27 main: DONE by its rule. The single-scrutinee arm ('let scrutinee (idx : int)', with 'local __v' only for several scrutinees) is in the installed tree apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs and in the 5k core: re-running pack-next8\\lua-single-scrutinee.ps1 refuses with 'already applied'. Every pack since has carried it through review with oracle DISAGREE 0, including 5j's accept today (native 6833 ran, Lua rows agree), so 'every Lua row agrees with C' holds. Still uncommitted in spiral; the user commits.")
inl note_of_eoie_supervisor_no_console_1 () = Note ("EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:19Z", "eoie", "21:20 user 21:1x: 'can eoie processes stop spawning conhost? weird to need that' (repeat of the 10-20 'avoid conhost'). Measured live: 33 conhost.exe on the box, 23 owned by eoie 'job supervise' processes; only 2 of those supervisors run a child (the rest wait on --after). Cause: eoie_job_host host_run.spi supervisor_child_started starts every supervisor with CREATE_NO_WINDOW (0x08000000), which still allocates a hidden console per process. CreateProcess probe (supervisor-no-console\\console-probe.ps1): CREATE_NO_WINDOW = 1 conhost, DETACHED_PROCESS = 0, neither shows a window. Rule, written before choosing: supervisors and queued jobs hold no console (DETACHED_PROCESS: their stdio is null, eoie uses no console API, stops go through TerminateJobObject); each running job's program keeps exactly one hidden console (CREATE_NO_WINDOW, unchanged) so its console grandchildren inherit it instead of opening visible windows (EOIE-JOBS note 19). Rejected: one shared console for all jobs (1 conhost total), because a single GenerateConsoleCtrlEvent to group 0 would reach every job. Draft: supervisor-no-console.do (one anchored replacement, const DETACHED_PROCESS), which applies on the emulated post-train tree. Check: after-install-check.ps1 (negative control now: supervisors=22 supervisor_consoles=22 FAIL; PASS needs 0 supervisor consoles and 0 visible child windows). Expected after install: eoie conhosts = 1 per running job (2 now, from 23), and 0 per queued job (release P also removes queued supervisors entirely). Ships as the first change after the train (train scripts are frozen while GE2 runs).")
inl note_of_eoie_supervisor_no_console_2 () = Note ("EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T03:55Z", "eoie", "23:55 now rides the train as stage R (after P), together with EOIE-JOB-START-SETTLE: stage-R\\apply-stage-R.ps1, launch-R.ps1 (fmt-rebase -Stage R -RawFrom P, regen filter from repo affected), release-build-job-R.ps1 (notebooks launch, jobs model, host_model), after-install-R.ps1 (settle fail/ok/skip, plus this item: R's own test job's supervisor has 0 consoles, its program runs, no visible window). Negative control on today's binary: 1/4, each failing for its own reason (old ok receipt; --settle-ms unknown; consoles=1). chain-next order GE3..P,R; emulation GE3..R 34 ok, 0 FAIL.")
inl note_of_eoie_supervisor_no_console_3 () = Note ("EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T08:05Z", "eoie", "04:05 eoie: the change is in the live tree (eoie_job_host/host_run.spi line 31: supervisors spawn with DETACHED_PROCESS 0x8), landed with LAND33 and carried through the eoie_job_queue split. The train is gone and the change ships in the single SLATE release. SLATE's after-install (after-install-CLEAN.ps1, stages HIJK..R) includes stage R's check: the test job's supervisor has 0 consoles, its program runs, and no window is visible. Done when that check passes on the installed binary, and a live count shows eoie conhosts = 1 per running job.")
inl note_of_eoie_supervisor_no_console_4 () = Note ("EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE", 4u32, "2026-10-25T20:18Z", "eoie", "16:18 kit: baseline before SLATE installs (the binary that ships this fix). 22 supervisors alive, 22 conhosts owned by supervisors: one each, 13 of them for queued jobs. After the install, new supervisors should own 0 conhosts. Supervisors started by the old binary keep theirs until their jobs settle, so compare on supervisors started after the install.")
inl note_of_eoie_supervisor_no_console_5 () = Note ("EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE", 5u32, "2026-10-26T08:33Z", "eoie", "04:33 eoie: early verdict on the release candidate, before install. Live probe on the worker node (kit scratch clean\\respell\\console-probe.ps1) against the renamed candidate kit.exe with a fixture jobs home: a job's supervisor (pid from its receipt) has 0 conhost.exe children (its only child is the job's program, pwsh), and the job finished exit 0. Baseline on the old binary (note of 10-25 16:18): 22 supervisors, 22 conhosts, one each. So DETACHED_PROCESS works as designed in the binary this release ships. The formal done check stays the post-install run on this box (renamed after-install stage R, plus a live count that new supervisors own 0 conhosts).")
inl note_of_eoie_supervisor_no_console_6 () = Note ("EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE", 6u32, "2026-10-27T14:20Z", "kit", "10:3x kit: DONE by the 10-25 rule (compare supervisors started after the install that ships DETACHED_PROCESS). Live count on this box at 10:3x: 10 kit job supervisors alive, all started after the kit.exe install of 10-26 20:26:42, and 0 of them own a conhost.exe (children are the job program pwsh.exe, or nothing for queued and gate-waiting jobs). Baseline on the old binary (note of 10-25 16:18): 22 supervisors, 22 conhosts, one each, 13 for queued jobs.")
inl note_of_fossil_assimilation_1 () = Note ("FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:44Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:44 main (user 2026-10-23 21:4x: 'we could check that old version control system called fossil, see which features eoie could assimilate/absorb/steal, download the source if necessary so we get the details, then fill tasks properly'). Method: read Fossil's docs and source (fossil-scm.org; source tarball if the docs lack detail, unpacked into its own empty folder outside any repo), then for each feature write what it does, how Fossil implements it (file and function), and what eoie has today. Each candidate becomes an eoie item with every planning field set, or is dropped with the reason. Done when every listed Fossil subsystem has a verdict line (absorb / already have / drop) and every absorb has an item.")
inl note_of_fossil_assimilation_2 () = Note ("FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION", 2u32, "2026-10-24T01:54Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:54 main: verdicts, from the docs (tech_overview, fileformat, sync, cap-theorem, fossil-v-git, help undo/stash/hook) and the source (release tarball in %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read, read only: undo.c, hook.c, stash.c, xfer.c ci-lock 1773-1850, checkin.c allow-fork 2472-2625, backoffice.c, allrepo.c, sqlcmd.c). ABSORB: immutable content-hashed artifacts with derived, rebuildable metadata and union sync -> EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG; undo/redo from saved pre-images -> EOIE-DO-UNDO; stash with a baseline -> EOIE-DRAFT-STASH; the ci-lock fork guard and allow-fork -> EOIE-EDIT-INTENT-LEASES; hooks (typed JSON, seq, watermark, test --dry-run) -> EOIE-TYPED-HOOKS; the timeline as primary view -> EOIE-TIMELINE. ALREADY HAVE: backoffice (one worker, idle death) = EOIE-JOB-HOST's host.pid singleton and 10-min idle exit, except Fossil's standby process that restarts on the new binary after an upgrade, which is a small follow-up for the job host; 'fossil all' = do 'git status a, b' across repos; 'fossil sql' virtual tables = EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES and do table queries; tickets/wiki/forum/chat = agile items, memory and cross-session messages; many checkouts per repo = scratch copies and chain-emulate; test-before-commit = gates plus user commits; single binary = eoie.exe. DROP: network sync protocol (one machine today; it returns with the event log's union merge), unversioned files (run outputs live in spiral-bin), shunning/purge (rule-based deletions and lab audit cover it).")
inl note_of_eoie_consolidated_intake_1 () = Note ("EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main, rule written before classifying: each of the 135 items in Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md (measured at eoie a831bad, 10-19) gets exactly one class. (a) covered: an open backlog item already plans the generic need -> a note on that item naming the ids, no new item. (b) resolved: a shipped eoie verb or a Done item does it today -> listed here. (c) parked: bundle, archive, container checkpoint, 85-minute lease or product fixture work with no consumer on this machine (git repos, the user commits) -> EOIE-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA, Paused, resume when a consumer uses eoie bundles or archives again. (d) new: generic, relevant now, and no hit for its mechanism names in agile.spi (searched per search-backlog-before-new) -> its own item with every planning field. Priority: doc P0 -> P1, P1 -> P2, P2 -> P3 (new requests enter at P1). The 10 overlap items the doc names live in eoie/state/agile.spi, the eoie repo's frozen GPT-era backlog (EOIE-LEGACY-STATE-TRIAGE), not here.")
inl note_of_eoie_consolidated_intake_2 () = Note ("EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: class (b) resolved today: FS-3 (EOIE-GREP-FILE-ROOT), READ-5 (repo state clock), READ-6 (EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS, EOIE-GENERIC-DRIFT), PROC-2 (job tail), PROC-5 (job tree stop), PROC-6 (EOIE-JOB-HOST, EOIE-JOB-START-SETTLE planned), JOB-1 (eoie job with stop and timeout), AGILE-7 and CLI-5 (EOIE-GENERIC-DRIFT: eoie writes only its own state and knows no layout), TABLE-2 (table query and diff), TABLE-4 (EOIE-TABLE-LOGS-VALUES). Class (d) new: EOIE-DO-MOVE-RANGE, EOIE-SCRATCH-COPY, EOIE-TREE-DIFF, EOIE-REMOVE-PLAN, EOIE-JOB-EXPECT, EOIE-JOB-REQUEUE, EOIE-JOB-ROOT-GUARD, EOIE-VERDICT-LEDGER, EOIE-BUILD-PHASES, EOIE-CACHE-INVENTORY, EOIE-TABLE-JSONL, EOIE-RUN-TWICE, EOIE-GAP-INTAKE, EOIE-LEGACY-STATE-TRIAGE. Class (c) parked: EOIE-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA. Class (a): notes on 21 items. The prompt blocks (prompt-blocks/*.md, universal_prompt_blocks.md) add no new mechanism: their generic asks map to EOIE-GAP-INTAKE (report every bypass), EOIE-AUTONOMOUS-GATE (session time box), EOIE-READ-QUERY census (LOC per language), EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS (ratings with deltas), EOIE-LIB-REUSE (share Spiral code between apps), EOIE-REMOVE-PLAN (coverage-backed pruning).")
inl note_of_eoie_ge2_crate_splits_1 () = Note ("EOIE-GE2-CRATE-SPLITS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T03:07Z", "eoie", "23:07 groom: blocks the whole release train (GE2 to P) and with it EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE and EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT, which ship after it. Milestones: (1) supervise split drafted (eoie_job_env crate, ~940 expected) DONE as draft; (2) memory split drafted (eoie_memory_import, ~820 expected) DONE as draft; (3) analysis split: measurement crate for tallied/early_rows/values_of/terms_of/arm_counts (~290 lines) returning a typed tuple; (4) task_listing split: printing (print_json/print_table/json_line ~250); (5) regen + cargo preview on the 22:27 compiler, all four under 950; (6) land in live, chain-emulate GE2..P still applies, relaunch GE2 with a waiter. Details: EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN notes 134-136.")
inl note_of_eoie_ge2_crate_splits_2 () = Note ("EOIE-GE2-CRATE-SPLITS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "eoie", "23:08 night rules, written before measuring (autonomous run to 07:00). (R1) Order: finish the analysis + task_listing split drafts, then one regen + cargo preview of all four on a post-train scratch tree, then land and relaunch. (R2) A crate passes at <= 950 generated lines. 951-1000 passes only if removing more needs another crate seam (logged as a follow-up); > 1000 fails, so split again. (R3) Relaunch GE2 only when all four pass, chain-emulate GE2..P has no FAIL row (merged-line check included), and the Q typecheck still PASSes on the current compiler. (R4) Every queued job gets a background waiter in the same step; no compile while a live-gui block is announced; a reaped job is retried with lower parallelism. (R5) If any rule can't be met by 05:30, stop relaunching, leave the train parked with its exact blocker named here, and spend the remaining time on light items: JOB-START-SETTLE indent fix, owner-parent cargo check prep, then the next 'agile next --team eoie' rows.")
inl note_of_eoie_ge2_crate_splits_3 () = Note ("EOIE-GE2-CRATE-SPLITS", 3u32, "2026-10-24T03:46Z", "eoie", "23:46 milestones 1-5 met. Measured regen on the 22:27 compiler (split-measure 4): eoie_job_supervise 1062->534, eoie_memory 1040->647, agile_task_listing 1084->988, eoie_experiment_analysis 1030->986; new or changed crates eoie_job_env 566, eoie_memory_import 763, eoie_experiment_stats 805, agile_task_census 397. Per R2, the two crates at 986/988 pass inside the 951-1000 band; follow-up seams: analysis -> a measurement crate (~290 lines), task_listing -> the task-record parser (~200 lines). Packaged as stage GE3: crate-splits\\apply-crate-splits.ps1 (6 eoie do programs), launch-GE3.ps1 (fmt-rebase -Stage GE3 -RawFrom GE2 -Land, then the heavy release), release-build-job-GE3.ps1 (GE2's script plus the two new crates in the regen filter), after-install-GE3.ps1 (GE2's 10 checks plus agile list table/json and memory import, positive-controlled on today's binary). chain-next order now starts at GE3, launch-HIJK rebases from GE3, and chain-emulate picks the raw of whichever of GE2/GE3 last landed. Emulation GE3..P: 33 ok, 0 FAIL. fmt-rebase GE3 dry run: live matches GE2's landing, 17 writes. Waiting on the Q typecheck with GE3 (R3), then launch with waiters.")
inl note_of_eoie_ge2_crate_splits_4 () = Note ("EOIE-GE2-CRATE-SPLITS", 4u32, "2026-10-24T11:17Z", "eoie", "07:1x groom: GE3 (the splits as their own stage) landed 00:05. Its release regen emitted 122/122 at 06:42, after its tree went from BelowNormal to Normal at 05:56; build, tests and notebooks are running. Done when GE3's release installs and its after-install checks pass (installed-is-done).")
inl note_of_eoie_ge2_crate_splits_5 () = Note ("EOIE-GE2-CRATE-SPLITS", 5u32, "2026-10-24T11:23Z", "eoie", "07:23 groom: blocked on the train. The only work left is to install GE3's release and run its after-install check, so 'agile next' must not hand this out as ready work. Back to Active when GE3's release installs.")
inl note_of_eoie_ge2_crate_splits_6 () = Note ("EOIE-GE2-CRATE-SPLITS", 6u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "eoie", "19:45 groom: the release condition is met. GE3's rerun (GE3b, job-1792850533961) installed at 11:46, and after-install-GE3 passed 12/12. Done.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_1 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T05:19Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 01:19 tibia: created for TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP note 15 and KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR note 21 (both name this gap; a backlog search for process, port and spawn found no item, and WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB's 10-24 survey moves fs and PATH primitives, not this one). Estimate by milestones: lab draft with 2 test blocks green on rust and gleam, 60 m; one eoie do land program plus undo, then the lib gate on process.spi, 60 m; notes and the consumer hook, 30 m. Design: nominal started switched per backend (Rust Rc RefCell Child, Gleam a port plus log path). Owner rule on both backends: only the starting process awaits, because BEAM sends port messages to the owner and Rc is not Send. Gleam drains port data into the log on each await, so await 0 drains and polls. Tree kill is by pid, so a supervisor can kill from another process.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_2 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T05:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 01:34 tibia: milestone 1 is done. Lab spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE is green, 2 of 2 tests on rust and gleam (job tibia-lib-process-handle-lab-3). The tests are a_started_program_logs_and_reports_its_exit and a_running_program_is_killed_by_its_pid. Run 1 lacked the test process static block, so _assert_eq was unbound. Run 2 was green on rust, but the gleam quick test hit badarg in lib's own capture_stdout_list. Its catch-all selector took a {'EXIT', Port, normal} left by the finished port when the test process traps exits. The fix is erlang unlink on the port at start, since the handle owner should not get exit signals from its child. The BEAM await reads only its own port's messages (select_record keyed on the port), so an actor using the handle keeps its other mail. Milestone 2 is in progress. The land program is scratch process-handle-land.ps1: one eoie do edit of process.spi with 3 anchored replaces (Gleam helpers into process_gleam_prelude, the started type and 4 functions before the tests, and the 2 tests at the end), plus a reverse undo.do. It refuses the live path without -Live (control checked), round-trips byte-exact on a copy, and adds no line over 120. The kit gate is queued as heavy job tibia-lib-process-handle-gate (lib-gate-kit gate.ps1 with adapter process-handle-apply.ps1, Files process.spi). It runs after PR-27's live block, then kit land.ps1, which waits for no Cells or live-gui reader.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_3 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T06:22Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 02:22 tibia: the candidate lib process.spi is green, 9 of 9 on rust and gleam (job tibia-process-handle-candidate-2: lib_gate.ps1 -Compiler process on a full lib copy with the 4-edit land applied; a_missing_or_failing_program_captures_nothing stays rust only). Changes since note 2, all driven by tests. (1) start_logging takes a directory first, because canary must run in its own folder and KINO's plan lists cd too (Rust current_dir, BEAM {cd, Dir}). The test a_program_runs_in_its_directory runs python in .. and compares its getcwd with the caller's parent, as a bool so both backends print the same line. (2) On BEAM, await keeps the exit code in the owner's process dictionary, keyed by the port. Exited stays exited, as Rust's try_wait does; before this a second Gleam await after exit waited out its deadline and returned None. (3) A pre-existing lib bug: lib's capture functions left their port linked, so in a process that traps exits the port's {'EXIT', Port, normal} reached the next capture's catch-all reader, which raised badarg (two captures in one test reproduce it). The fix is one home: erlang unlink inside spiral_process_open_with, which covers every lib process function, so start_logging's own unlink is dropped. The land program is now 4 anchored replaces. It round-trips byte-exact and adds no line over 120. The heavy kit gate is tibia-lib-process-handle-gate-2 (scratch kit copy that calls lib_gate with -Compiler process, because the resident daemon failed to start 01:43-02:0x until main's Cells pack). It runs after PR-27's block, then the kit land.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_4 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 4u32, "2026-10-24T07:47Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 03:47 tibia: kit gate GREEN at 03:40 (job tibia-lib-process-handle-gate-2: candidate process.spi 9 of 9 on rust and gleam, 0 type errors, 17 rows 0 red; the 4-edit land on a fresh lib copy took sha256 a2d87547 to b8bada74). Land queued as job tibia-lib-process-handle-land (scratch kit land.ps1; it waits for two clear 20 s samples with no Cells erl and no live-gui window, then applies). The undo program is scratch process-handle-land\\undo.do (expect files 1 replacements 4).")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_5 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 5u32, "2026-10-24T07:53Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 03:53 tibia: LAND HELD by GRANULAR-HEAL (user P0, 10-24 03:3x; LIB-MACRO-HEAL rule 5: a new lib def with logic in a macro is a review failure from now on). The gate was GREEN 9 of 9 (note 4), but the slice adds logic in host text. Its Rust arms are multi-statement blocks (the Command chain with File::create and try_clone, the try_wait deadline loop, the cfg taskkill/kill branch), and its Gleam side adds function bodies (start_logging, await with the process-dictionary cache, drain, kill_pid_tree) to process_gleam_prelude, a blob the census already lists. I stopped job tibia-lib-process-handle-land before it applied; lib process.spi is unchanged. Rework, rule-first: every host contact is one named primitive of one call or expression, at most 120 chars, with no casts, arithmetic or flow (open_port, port_info os_pid, unlink, erlang get and put, the selector receive, file write_file with append, os cmd; Command::new, current_dir, args, stdio, spawn, try_wait, id, thread sleep, Instant now), and the deadline loop, the drain loop, the exit cache and the platform choice are Spiral. The same 3 tests must pass on rust and gleam. I asked main for the primitive names its process.spi heal will use, so the two build on one set. Progress back to 60: the behaviour is proven, the shape is not.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_6 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 6u32, "2026-10-24T08:23Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:23 tibia: GRANULAR-HEAL rule (4) applies. A caller search finds 0 callers outside process.spi for its 5 functions and the prelude, in every repo (positive control: the names match in lib process.spi and the two labs). So process.spi's heal is a deletion (slice (a), after the Cells mounts fix lands), and this item's handle lands only together with its first caller, the world_port owner in tibia apps/host (TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP, scratch world-port-draft). The granular form is proven in spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL (run 3 compiling) and lands with that consumer. main was told and can keep the capture functions if it knows of a coming consumer.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_7 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 7u32, "2026-10-24T08:37Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:37 tibia: the granular form is GREEN. Lab spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL (job tibia-lib-process-heal-lab-4) passes 5 of 5 on rust and gleam: capture_status_within with list args (quick exit 0, past deadline killed, -1) and the handle (logs and reports exit, with exit kept on a second await; killed by pid from outside; runs in its directory, checked by a marker file written in ..). A rule scan of the module: 70 macros, 0 violations (each one expression, at most 120 chars, no casts, arithmetic or flow), and the Gleam global holds 13 @external declarations and no function bodies. Getting there took 4 runs: a != splice (escaped as \\!=), core length and index on array_base (removed with words_of when args became lists), and a cleanup pass that also caught its own over-deletion. It ran on the default compiler daemon, which confirms main's Cells pack fixed the 01:43 start failures. Per rule (4) the module lands only with its first caller, tibia's world_port owner (TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP); slice (a) deletes today's uncalled process.spi (gate tibia-lib-process-delete-gate).")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_8 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 8u32, "2026-10-24T08:59Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:59 tibia: plan changed from delete-then-re-add to one replace, since the consumer is proven (TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP note of 2026-10-24 04:59). The deletion gate is stopped; replace gate tibia-lib-process-replace-gate runs the healed module's 5 tests in a lib copy. The land goes with world_port into tibia apps/host in one session.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_9 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 07:00 tibia: per the user's review (GRANULAR-HEAL note 6: every lego piece in its proper file, even at 3x the lines), the replace slice will split by subject before it lands, instead of one granular process.spi. Port primitives (port nominal, port_open, port_unlink, port_os_pid, port_close, port_selector, the message and event accessors) go in a new lib erlang_port.spi after erlang_process. The process dictionary get and put go in erlang_process.spi (a per-process subject). The Gleam file append goes in file_system.spi. Monotonic ms and the Rust Instant and elapsed seconds go in date_time.spi. os_cmd joins env or platform with its kin. The Rust command and child primitives go in a new rust command module beside lib's rust modules. process.spi keeps only the Spiral logic (start_logging, started_pid, await_exit_within, kill_tree_of_pid, capture_status_within) and its 5 tests, placed after all of those in package.spiproj. The lab proof (5 of 5) carries over, because the primitives are unchanged and only their homes move; each new home gets its own small test. The process.spi replace gate (stopped at 06:05) is rebuilt as that multi-file slice in the morning, then lands with world_port.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_10 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 10u32, "2026-10-24T11:18Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 07:18 tibia: grooming re-size by milestones after the user's 06:56 review (split by subject, note 9). Done: lab proof, granular, 5 of 5 on rust and gleam, about 60 min. Left: the multi-file slice (new erlang_port and rust command homes, plus additions to erlang_process, file_system, date_time, and env or platform, each with a small test; process.spi keeps only the Spiral logic), its kit gate and land, about 120 min; then the joint land with world_port into tibia apps/host, about 30 min. Total 210 (from 150), about 30 percent done; progress lowered from 75, because the shape that lands changed even though the behaviour is proven.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_11 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 11u32, "2026-10-24T20:44Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 21:09 Edition-3 lib: main landed the respelling around 20:43 (pre-land copy %TEMP%\\edition3-backup-lib-20261024-204332). At 20:5x tibia released its holds on spiral\\process.spi and spiral\\file_system.spi, so main's hold.txt is empty and main respells both in its second pass without waiting on tibia. Rewriting the tibia drafts for edition 3 is tibia's job: lua-file-exists-apply's anchors (let file_exists, the arm and test lines) and process-handle-land. The lab module lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL/process_healed.spi was respelled with format --spelling symbols at 20:5x; its 5 tests passed but Cells raised a FatalError at the first top-level ':!'. Cause, found by main: Cells' cell.spi:45 and memo.ex:544 classify definitions by keyword prefix, which affects 34 lib files; main is fixing it. Repro: lab/E3-NOMINAL-PROBE. The lab module is back on its edition-2 text (hash A1C7A1B6), copy in scratch edition2-drafts. Once main's Cells fix lands, respell it again and re-run the lab job. UPDATE 2026-10-24 21:21: main's pass 2 landed at 21:16. lib process.spi and file_system.spi are now edition 3 (live residual check green), and the Cells ':!' keyword fix is in the tree. main is verifying it with lab/E3-NOMINAL-PROBE as the positive control, then running lib gates on otp_actor.spi and channel.spi (job-1792891232334-main-serial-cells-e3-verify). Keep lab/E3-NOMINAL-PROBE until main reports green. Next for tibia: re-anchor lua-file-exists-apply and process-handle-land on the live edition-3 text, and once main is green, respell the lab module again and re-run it. UPDATE 2026-10-24 21:35: main's Cells fix is green, and lab/E3-NOMINAL-PROBE is deleted. The lab module is respelled to edition 3 (hash 965DC6CD) and passes 5 of 5 with SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 (job-1792891735514-tibia-lib-process-heal-lab-e3b, exit 0). The lua-file-exists draft is superseded by lua-file-io (TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION note 46). Left here: the multi-file split by subject, written in edition 3.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_12 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 12u32, "2026-10-25T11:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 07:03 Split design refined by a one-home check against the edition-3 lib. lib/spiral/erlang_term.spi already provides term (Dynamic), atom, of and cast (the identity coerce both ways), tuple, equal, apply (any Erlang module:function(args)) and charlist. So the lab module's own Gleam glue (dynamic, gleam_atom, gleam_pair, gleam_dynamic_text/i32/strings, dynamic_i32, dynamic_is_undefined, and its spiral_process_coerce/element externals) is replaced by erlang_term calls rather than moved. Of the 14 @external declarations in process_gleam_declarations, os:cmd, erlang:open_port, port_info, port_close, unlink, monotonic_time, get, put and file:write_file become typed wrappers over erlang_term.apply in their subject homes, so most of the block disappears. Homes: erlang_port.spi (new, after erlang_term and erlang_process): port nominal, open (spawn_executable with options), os_pid, close, unlink, selector and receive with message/event accessors (Gleam process selector calls stay one-call macros). erlang_process.spi: dictionary_get/put. date_time.spi: monotonic_ms (Gleam) beside the Rust Instant it already has, plus elapsed seconds. file_system.spi: the Gleam append through apply 'file' 'write_file' with [append]. An env/platform home: os_cmd. A new rust command module beside lib's rust modules: command new/arg/dir/quiet/no_input/logging/output/spawn and the child try_wait/wait/kill/id primitives. process.spi keeps start_logging, started_pid, await_exit_within, kill_tree_of_pid, capture_status_within and the 5 tests, after all of those in package.spiproj. Written in edition 3; each home gets a small test; the gate is the kit with the same-location baseline. About 150 min left. REVISED 2026-10-25 07:09 after two more one-home checks. (1) lib/spiral/runtime.spi already owns the Rust command family (process_command over std::process::Command, new_process_command, process_command_args, process_stdio, process_output, exit status, stdin/stdout/stderr), so the lab's rust_command_* primitives become additions to runtime.spi in its existing style (current_dir, null stdio, log-file stdio, spawn, child try_wait/wait/kill/id), not a new rust command module. (2) lib/spiral/date_time.spi already has stopwatch over std::time::Instant with stopwatch_elapsed_milliseconds, which replaces the lab's rust_now and rust_elapsed_seconds, so no new timing primitive on Rust. Draft started in spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT: erlang_port.spi built entirely on erlang_term (apply, tuple, atom, of, cast, list_term) plus 4 one-call Gleam process/result macros and an identity Spiral closure as select_record's mapper; its gleam test opens erl -noshell -eval halt(3) as a port, reads os_pid and waits for exit_status 3 (job-1792926397205). STYLE 2026-10-25 07:11: runtime.spi's existing Rust builders (process_command_args, _stdout, _current_dir, _spawn) are older-era drift. They move a std::process::Command by value through 'true; let mut !c = !c' statement macros, which GRANULAR-HEAL forbids copying. A one-call macro can't mutate a moved value, so the additions use the lab's shared shape: a nominal over Rc<RefCell<std::process::Command>> with one-expression steps (drop(!c.borrow_mut().stdout(...)) and similar), placed in runtime.spi beside the old builders. Healing those builders belongs to GRANULAR-HEAL's lib pass, not this slice. FINAL MAP 2026-10-25 07:16 (each checked against lib): file_write's append and truncate reuse file_system.append_all_text and write_all_text, whose Gleam arms exist (port output is a BEAM binary, so a cast to string suffices), with no new primitive. monotonic_ms becomes a Gleam arm on date_time's stopwatch (erlang:monotonic_time in milliseconds), which generalizes it instead of adding a parallel clock. os_cmd goes in platform.spi (os_platform's home) through erlang_term.apply 'os' 'cmd' with a charlist, converted back with unicode:characters_to_binary. dictionary_get/put go in erlang_process.spi through erlang_term.apply 'erlang' 'get'/'put'. Net new lib surface: erlang_port.spi, the shared_command block in runtime.spi, 1 stopwatch arm, 2 dictionary functions, 1 platform function; everything else reuses lib. Lab drafts: spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT/erlang_port.spi and shared_command.spi (tests running, job-1792926858525).")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_13 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 13u32, "2026-10-25T12:20Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 08:20 SPLIT PROVEN in lab (job-1792929533406, 10/10 with memo off): spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT holds erlang_port.spi (proven alone on gleam: erl port, os_pid, exit_status 3), shared_command.spi (proven alone on rust: spawn, id, exit code 4), split_misc.spi (dictionary get/put and os_command_output pass on gleam; the clock test had a precedence bug in the test, fixed) and process_split.spi (the 5 original behaviour tests: quick exit, deadline kill, logging, kill by pid, working directory). Because Cells compiles a single file as the main package, sibling lab modules aren't visible, so the proof ran on process_split_all.spi, the four concatenated with module prefixes stripped. In lib they are separate modules, as planned. Both backends pass all 5 behaviour tests on the new homes (erlang_term-based ports, the shared command, date_time.stopwatch for Rust timing, threading.sleep, file_system append and write for the Gleam log). Next: the anchored lib slice in edition 3. It adds erlang_port.spi (new module after erlang_term and erlang_process), runtime.spi's shared_command block, erlang_process's dictionary_get/put, platform's os_command_output, date_time's monotonic milliseconds (as the stopwatch Gleam arm or a named function), and replaces process.spi's body with process_split's logic and its 5 tests. Kit gate with the same-location baseline, then land; then the world_port joint land into tibia apps/host (BACKEND-MAP).")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_14 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 14u32, "2026-10-25T14:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 10:28 Anchored lib slice ready and round-tripped byte-exact on a copy (scratch process-handle-apply.ps1; land.do 13 replacements in 6 files plus copy.do for the new erlang_port.spi; undo.do restores all 6 by hash, then erlang_port.spi is removed by literal path). Two design corrections from reading the live lib: (1) module order: erlang_term and erlang_process sat at 111-112, after platform, date_time and process, so they move up right after sm'_operators (erlang_term, erlang_process, erlang_port; neither has lib callers outside host strings), and process moves below file_system_operators (its started handles log through file_system; no lib module calls process.). (2) process.spi is merged, not replaced: the capture family (capture_stdout, _list, combined, try_capture_combined_list, used by eoie) stays; capture_status_within becomes the granular erlang_port/runtime version with list arguments (no callers outside lib tests), start_logging, started_pid and await_exit_within are added, and the prelude loses the status-only Gleam glue (now, wait, kill_tree, status_within and 5 externals, plus the unused int import). Homes: os_command_output in platform, monotonic_milliseconds in date_time (Gleam arms, failwith elsewhere), shared commands in runtime before its main. Kit gate job-1792938470349 over process, erlang_port, erlang_process, erlang_term, platform, date_time, runtime; the kit now snapshots its baseline at gate start.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_15 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 15u32, "2026-10-25T16:22Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 12:21 Both kit gates queued earlier failed before testing anything: lib_gate.ps1 needs the file name with its extension ('no common under -Lib ... a missing file runs zero tests and reads as green'), and the kit passed bare module names. Fixed in the kit (gate.ps1 appends .spi when no extension), so the later time_span and date-casts gates are unaffected. The drafts also moved to list sugar per the new list-sugar rule (process tests, world_port, world_executor; the heal slice converts the 3 pre-existing capture tests and enables gleam on the missing-program test). Requeued as one serial runner, job-1792945250381: random gate -> lands -> family 3 -> dead helpers; process gate -> split land -> world_port -> world_executor; then the process heal gate and land.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_16 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 16u32, "2026-10-26T10:48Z", "tibia-lane", "10:48Z 10:4xZ LANDED in tibia-serial-lib (job-1792976722061): the lib process split (LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE-SPLIT; gate GREEN, anchored land, undo program kept beside its apply), then tibia apps/host world_port on lib process handles (arena_host 7 of 7, world_port 2 of 2, memo off, formatter clean), then world_executor driving the host through real programs (world_executor 2 of 2, world_port 2 of 2). Left on this item: the process heal (LIB-PROCESS-HEAL, first in the now-running tibia-serial-heals after the random-lands retry) and the BACKEND-MAP executor wiring that follows it.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_17 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 17u32, "2026-10-26T10:58Z", "tibia-lane", "10:58Z 10:5xZ LIB-PROCESS-HEAL did not apply in tibia-serial-heals ('did not apply to the copy'). Its third anchor (the capture tests in Cons form) no longer exists: the process split that landed hours earlier already rewrote those tests in list sugar. The only change that anchor still carried is a ///> gleam marker on a_missing_or_failing_program_captures_nothing, so it is re-anchored on just that (backup process-heal-apply-before-reanchor-*.ps1). All 5 anchors match the post-split base in a dry run: process.spi 72A16CF84086961E, runtime.spi 609BEB496F6D8E99. A gate-then-land retry is packed at the start of tibia-serial-gaps, guarded on the gleam marker being absent. Also landed in heals: LIB-TIME-SPAN-HEAL (gate 40 of 41) and LIB-DATE-CASTS-HEAL (41 of 42), both GREEN.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_18 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 18u32, "2026-10-27T00:59Z", "tibia-lane", "00:59Z Done.\n- The last open part, LIB-PROCESS-HEAL, gated GREEN and landed at 17:26 local in tibia-serial-gaps3 (process-heal-retry: files 2, replacements 5).\n- Audit at 01:0xZ: the live process.spi carries the heal's ///> gleam marker on a_missing_or_failing_program_captures_nothing, and the live hashes still equal the land's 'after' hashes (process.spi 8C3FD660A9531A5B, runtime.spi A87F784E681A8872), so no later write overwrote them.\n- Together with the split (LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE-SPLIT) and its consumers (apps/host world_port and world_executor, landed 10:48Z), the started-process handle with Rust and Gleam arms is complete.\n- The live-use wiring stays on TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_pins_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PINS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). The survey could not find lib's gleam.toml pins within its search depth.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_pins_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PINS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T10:26Z", "x", "10-24 06:26 x: DONE. The pins aren't in lib/spiral's gleam.toml: Cells supplies Gleam deps (apps/cells targets.ex:344-345: gleam_erlang >=1.3.0 and <2.0.0, gleam_otp >=1.3.0 and <2.0.0), and the warm build resolves gleam_erlang 1.3.0 and gleam_otp 1.3.0. Both are the newest stable on hex.pm (gleam_otp 1.3.0 from 2026-08-09, gleam_erlang 1.3.0 from 2025-08-03), so no bump is needed. Checked in the resolved sources: gleam_erlang's process.gleam has pub type Name, new_name and named_subject (LIB-OTP-NAME can start), and gleam_otp ships factory_supervisor.gleam beside static_supervisor (LIB-OTP-FACTORY-SUPERVISOR can start).")
inl note_of_formatter_lambda_next_line_1 () = Note ("FORMATTER-LAMBDA-NEXT-LINE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T12:25Z", "main", "2026-10-24 08:25 main: it is a bug, and it is reproduced: the installed formatter turns HEAD trace.spi's `| Zig => fun () =>` / `{` into `| Zig =>` / `fun () => {` whenever a line inside the record is over 120 (stdin probe, output identical to the worktree trace.spi). Cause: layoutArm (spiral_compiler.fs ~181137) keeps a lambda body on the arm line only when the lambda's laid head ends in `=>`; a record body lays the head as `fun () => {`, so the whole lambda drops a line. Authored evidence: the 10-02 lib + apps/spiral use only `| p => fun () =>` with `{` below (2 sites, 0 of the other shapes). Fix: %TEMP%\\pack-fmt-next\\fmt-fix\\arm-lambda-opener.ps1 keeps the authored head and lays `{` and the body one indent deeper. Formatter-only pack (core BEECFE432427C57A over tree 095EFD9A4CDB1C0C) in runner main-serial-fmt-next: build, the formatter candidate gate (user/lib/dice/eoie corpora, JOINED 0, token-neutral), and an arm-lambda probe on HEAD trace.spi, then install only on PASS, through the compiler-install window.")
inl note_of_formatter_lambda_next_line_2 () = Note ("FORMATTER-LAMBDA-NEXT-LINE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T15:10Z", "main", "main 11:1x: bug, not rule. The formatter fix (fmt-fix arm-lambda-opener: keep | p => fun () => with the opener below) passed pack-fmt-next chain-gate incl. the trace.spi probe and was installed 10:11 (main-fmt-next-install exit 0; canonical 6 of 6 on alphabet/dice). Re-lay: census over lib finds 3 split arms, all in trace.spi read_state (Zig/Rust/TypeScript; parsing.spi:357 and file_system.spi:894 are curried |> lambdas, not arms). Slice %TEMP%\\lib-trace-arm-lambda joins them token-neutrally; the installed formatter leaves the result unchanged (positive control: it rewrites a misaligned arm). Gate+land queued as job main-serial-trace-arm-lambda; close on land.")
inl note_of_formatter_lambda_next_line_3 () = Note ("FORMATTER-LAMBDA-NEXT-LINE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T16:27Z", "main", "main 12:3x: done. LIB-TRACE-ARM-LAMBDA GREEN 11:56 and landed (live trace.spi read_state: | Zig/Rust/TypeScript => fun () => with { below, lines 28/34/39); the installed formatter (fmt-next, 10:11) keeps that layout and leaves the file unchanged.")
inl note_of_platform_bound_tsv_1 () = Note ("PLATFORM-BOUND-TSV", 1u32, "2026-10-24T11:55Z", "main", "2026-10-24 07:55 main: drift confirmed and healed. The TSV (untracked, 4 rows) existed only for ci-classify.ps1, to tag local-vs-CI behavior differences as platform-bound (CI-OFFLOAD notes 13-17). It repeated facts the matrix already holds: tests/matrix.psd1 Known entries scope the 2 POSIX-only C rows with Platform = 'Windows'. Now all_terminals' F# Known entry carries Platform = 'Windows', mega_omniledger_erp_kernel's F# row has a Windows-scoped Known entry (the /bin/sh spawn), ci-classify reads every Platform-scoped Known entry from matrix.psd1 (Import-PowerShellDataFile -SkipLimitCheck, as test.ps1 does), and the TSV is deleted (checked: all 4 rows covered). Rule for later: a new hand-written table goes into matrix.psd1 or typed Spiral, never a TSV. The deeper fix, portable F# macros in the omniledger mega sample, stays with that sample.")
inl note_of_rust_prefix_study_1 () = Note ("RUST-PREFIX-STUDY", 1u32, "2026-10-24T12:29Z", "main", "2026-10-24 08:29 main: settled by a probe on the installed compiler (scratch rust-open-probe, Rust backend, packageDir lib): `open rust` then bare `clone 41i32` fails with ETY-UnboundVariable: clone, and `rust.clone 41i32` with no open compiles (`41i32.clone()`). So `open rust` opens the rust/ FOLDER record (its children rust and rust_operators; that is why file_system.spi follows it with `open rust_operators`), never rust.spi's members, and the `rust.` prefix on rust_type/fsharp_global is strictly necessary under today's resolution. No resolver regressed. Qualified `rust.X` is also the user's own lib idiom (rust.ref 184x in the 10-02 lib), so the prefixes stay; no hack such as a double open is added. Not done here: the SOTA module design, the Rust 2018 model, where a folder `rust/` plus `rust.spi` is ONE module (the file is the module, the folder holds its children), so `open rust` would expose the members and the submodules alike. That is a compiler resolution change with a corpus-wide effect; it belongs to the package-manager design (PACKAGE-HEADER-REVISIT / PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN, note added there) and must be measured on the corpus before any prefix is dropped.")
inl note_of_flat_folders_1 () = Note ("FLAT-FOLDERS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:12Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:12 main: census of tracked files by directory depth (deps and .github excluded). Best organized: blog (max depth 1), tibia and alphabet (2), dice and eoie (4; eoie is flat crates under src/). Deepest: spiral, max 7, with 10,050 of 10,346 files at depth >= 5, and 9,882 of those are apps/compiler/tmp/samples/<name>/<variant>/main.* (the rest: Cells Mix layout 83, tmp/tests 36, tmp/lanes 14, tmp/formatter 13, tmp/compiler 12). x has 18 deep files (apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames/partN.gleam: Gleam's required src/<package>/ layout) and polyglot 13 (a Rust crate under apps/chat/contract/tests). Rule: authored code stays at depth <= 3 from the repo root (the tibia/alphabet shape: <area>/<module>.spi); layouts a toolchain requires (cargo src/, gleam src/<pkg>/, mix lib/) are exempt and disappear when the code moves to Spiral. Actions: (1) spiral's samples move from apps/compiler/tmp/samples to a top-level samples/ (depth 2-3), the single biggest flattening, done as part of COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE; (2) tmp/tests, lanes, formatter and compiler fold into their proper places in the same epic; (3) the Cells Mix tree flattens with EXS-TO-SPIRAL. Nothing else needs flattening.")
inl note_of_lane_gauge_daily_1 () = Note ("LANE-GAUGE-DAILY", 1u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:45 groom: done. The main daily report ends with the lane gauge (18:00 report: 3 main rows + 3 peer rows), and memory daily-report-lane-gauge holds the rule.")
inl note_of_history_backlog_laps_drift_1 () = Note ("HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-24 07:49 main: how the drift came to be: on 2026-10-22 00:09 I drained the lab by MOVING the laps ledger and its side tables into docs/agents/history instead of folding them into their items (BACKLOG-LAPS note 4), creating a second record of facts the backlog owns. Healed: 106 items got one note each with their uncovered lap grains (709 of 1,150 grains already had a lap note); 10 helper patch rows went to their items; heavy-queue.tsv, night-queue.tsv and owners.tsv were run state (queue history, a 10-19 owner table now carried by team tags) and are dropped; pack-8 bisect results: pack8-bisect-results-20261019-1401.tsv:  || pack8-bisect-results-20261019-1405.tsv: SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION built ok ok; SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE built ok ok; RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION built TracedError: Expected a symbol as the index into the layout type. TracedError: Expected a symbol as the index into the layout type.; SPIRAL-LITERAL-DEFAULTS built ok ok; RUST-EXPORT-TYPES built ok ok; KINO-RUST-CRATES built ok ok; SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES build-fail  . Orphans (ids without a task): -: lap 4-end 14:42 (main): lap 4 done: 35 items, 2 closed (CI-AFTER-SPI-MIGRATION, UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC) -> next: drive LAB-IN-REPO to Done | lap 6-end 21:34 (main): main laps 4-6 done; packs 7, 8b, 9, 11, 12 accepted and 10 installed today; matrix 7,020 -> 7,276 rows; core v1 consumers dice/alphabet/polyglot migrated -> next: next session: toolchain pack, lane 2 width stack, zig-dev pack || (bookkeeping): lap drive7 11:42 (subagent2): no item reachable to Done CPU-light this lap (all wait on the switch, pack 7 or a push); corrected stale progress: CELLS-PROBES 60, ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE 80, LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS 40, UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST 20, SIM-IN-SPIRAL 5 -> next: drive ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE / SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS once main applies | lap 9-stop 13:00 (subagent2): lap 9 paused for a context restart after CALYX-BACKEND; lap 8 complete + drive8 (LIB-USAGE delete-unused staging) -> next: next incarnation: continue lap 9 at OPENQASM3-BACKEND (then EBPF, ZED, LEAN, TREE-SITTER, GLEAM, MEGA, CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW) | lap 11-stop 13:58 (subagent2): laps 9 (from OPENQASM3) through 11 complete, context restart point -> next: next incarnation: lap 12 from the top of the slice (SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR); read the latest notes of an item before setting Done | lap 17-stop 17:15 (subagent2): resume point: laps 12-17 done; drive = SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR (lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR: 6 crates + cli_common + router.spi; promote.ps1 routes 7 verbs; router_swap.py staged; gleam + rust re-proven with current files (parity with release CLI); cuda, cpp, lua, export need a re-proof from current files (cli_common changed: multi-value flag_values, write_if_changed; cell headers added) before main's promote; rust --wasm/--contract never run: route native only). Staged for main (unchanged): nan-arms/lean-arms/known-platform ALREADY APPLIED (drop from list), pin-stale --run 125623 --apply, drop-polyglot-fallbacks, subword, lean-stable, console_c_arm (Cells), dice-drop-common + rewrite-deps (go), near-sdk-bump row, Calyx 13-sample probe row. Memory suggestions for main: a parameter named with silently drops the definition (ETY-UnboundVariable at call sites); eoie job timeout includes heavy-gate wait (use long budgets, work stays < 2 min); release CLI prints rust results flat, cuda wrapped -> next: next: re-proof jobs (cuda+cpp, lua, export), proven.tsv hashes in promote.ps1, then lap 18 | lap 18 18:04 (subagent2): lap 18 pass: no change since lap 17 (all waiting on main's queue, user's go or deps) for KINO-HOT-PATHS, LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS, CI-OFFLOAD, TODO-AS-SOURCE, CELLS-PROBES, CELLS-NEW-PROJECT, SIM-IN-SPIRAL, TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO, UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA, RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE, WORKFLOW-END-TO-END, SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT, OPENQASM3-BACKEND (main runs its probe), EBP