heck -> next: - || (none): lap drive7b 12:01 (subagent2): no item can reach Done before the all-clear; first Done candidates after it: ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE (flip + reviewed accept), SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS (pack 7 review), TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO (sweep.ps1 OK after a push) -> next: - || SEFAZ-XSD-APP: lap 1 08:12 (subagent): lib has no XML reader; roxmltree primitive vs Spiral tokenizer; KINO-RUST-CRATES dependency for tests -> next: on go: decide reader by a 50-line Spiral tokenizer probe over tiposBasico_v4.00.xsd vs roxmltree; record the dep on KINO-RUST-CRATES if roxmltree | lap 2 09:05 (subagent): tiposBasico_v4.00: 13 element kinds, 7 attrs, no CDATA/entities: a small Spiral tokenizer suffices -> next: on go: check leiauteNFe_v4.00.xsd the same way (choices, complexTypes, entities) before fixing the tokenizer scope | lap 3 10:21 (subagent): leiauteNFe v4.00 shape: 21 kinds, 20 attrs, only &quot;/&amp; entities, no CDATA; tokenizer needs PI skip + 5 entities -> next: on go: write the XSD-subset tokenizer union (Tag/End/SelfClosing/Text/Comment/Pi) in Spiral with test blocks; else nothing | lap 4 11:29 (subagent): blocked: research-only until the user's explicit go (both input files sized) -> next: the user's go; then the XSD tokenizer | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): research-only until the user's go -> next: the user's go | lap 6 18:26 (subagent): re-read: research sizing done (21 element kinds in leiauteNFe_v4.00) -> next: map the 21 kinds onto Spiral unions in a lab sketch | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: research-only so far -> next: map the 21 XSD kinds onto Spiral unions. The folder is deleted.")
inl note_of_cells_history_review_1 () = Note ("CELLS-HISTORY-REVIEW", 1u32, "2026-10-24T12:19Z", "main", "2026-10-24 08:19 main: history from the session record (5a6e53fb.jsonl) and 376 Kino/Cells notes (10-14..10-24). ORIGIN (around 10-01/02): an agent built a parallel Livebook Spiral smart cell; Grok moved it to spiral/apps/kino. Its purpose was to replace the Fable-era .dib/dotnet-repl notebooks: .livemd sources with Kino running Spiral cells per backend. GROWTH: 10-03..10-07 notebooks moved from .dib to .livemd plus Kino (polyglot/dice/lib). 10-11..10-13: test blocks in .spi became cells (///> targets, test x static), a shared compile daemon (port 13905), memo keys, and the silent-false-pass audit (C/Gleam/WGSL). 10-14..10-16: queue latency, warm runner, bridge overhead, parallel cache. 10-17: the user disliked the name, Kino became Cells. 10-19: CELLS-EVERYWHERE goal (\"100% of the code cell compatible\"), 129 notes that day. 10-22..10-24: the Cells pack (daemon memsup, retire race, mounts, probes, new-project scaffold). DRIFT vs the original: the original was an interactive notebook (Livebook smart cell, .livemd as the source). Today Cells is a CLI test runner and gate behind every lane's lib work; the Livebook side lost priority (NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT blocked at 90%) and .spi files became the notebooks. This matches later user directions (.spi as notebook, SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES), so it is direction change more than drift. The drift is the language: Elixir grew from 7,565 lines (10-14 baseline) to 14,126 in 86 .ex/.exs files, while the Spiral part grew only from 1,458 to 1,783 (7 files) plus 7 ps1 scripts. KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR and KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM sit at 10%. WHY NOT FINISHED: (1) no end state was ever written; the goal kept widening (everywhere, without Elixir, faster than Elixir tooling). (2) Infrastructure firefighting took the effort: daemon stalls, memsup, retire races, false passes and mounts are about 60% of the notes. (3) Ownership churned (lane-a, lane-b, helper lanes, main, tibia, x), so nobody drove the port. (4) Every fix was made in Elixir because it was the fastest place, so the port debt compounds. REMAINING PATH, with the end state written now: Cells is finished when (a) every repo's test blocks run through it (CELLS-EVERYWHERE), (b) its Elixir is rewritten in Spiral (to Gleam for the BEAM parts, Rust for the hot paths) with the parallel machinery kept (EXS-TO-SPIRAL), and (c) the benchmark shows parity or better with the Elixir version per stage. Rule from today: a Cells fix that touches Elixir comes with a port slice of the same module (EXS-TO-SPIRAL).")
inl note_of_waiter_reap_memory_1 () = Note ("WAITER-REAP-MEMORY", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:07Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:07 main: cause: the reaper is Claude Code's low-memory guard. It stops the SESSION's background shell tasks (watchers such as `eoie job wait` run with run_in_background) when free memory drops (x note X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED 84: reaped while idle at about 3.3 GB free). eoie jobs and their supervisors are outside its reach. Fix, in force since 06:12 today: every wait lives inside an eoie runner job (serial-runner.ps1 steps; land.ps1 waits for verdicts and readers), and the session checks progress on a 30-minute cron (CronCreate a14f09f1) instead of holding waiter shells, so there is nothing left for the guard to reap. Memory headroom work in the same pass: 40 queued jobs collapsed to runners (about 80 idle processes, about 600 MB), single-land runners merged, and the Invoke-Heavy watchdog moved in-process (one pwsh plus conhost less per heavy job). Free memory went from about 3.0 GB at 07:33 to about 3.7-4.0 GB since 08:30, with the same work running.")
inl note_of_drift_map_1 () = Note ("DRIFT-MAP", 1u32, "2026-10-24T12:41Z", "main", "2026-10-24 08:41 main: drift map v1, ranked for LLM-only work when the CPU is full. Counts are from ripgrep over lib/eoie src/x apps/tibia apps/fiscal src/apps/spiral/cells spi; lab and deps excluded unless named. (1) Logic in host macros: eoie 786 sites / 1.05M chars, lib 738 / 69k (GRANULAR-HEAL; slices queued). (2) concat5 helper: 2 definitions (eoie agile_task_views/views.spi and rust_std_string/model.spi), about 480 uses across 47 eoie files, plus lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR. It should be `++#` chains or an interpolated `$\"...\"` (eoie team; one mechanical pass per file, LLM-only). (3) Cons/Nil spelled out: 4,046 `Cons (` in 68 files, fiscal generator.spi alone 189 (LIST-SUGAR-SCAN: `::` and `;[ ]` list sugar). (4) Parenthesized lambdas `(fun ... =>`: 13,762 hits in 149 files. Many are required (a lambda followed by more arguments), so LAMBDA-PARENS needs a fast compile loop to split required from F#-habit. (5) !!!! intrinsics outside core: 805 in 300 files (BANG-MACRO-CLEANUP: reuse upstream core aliases). (6) `^^` literal-concat operator: 1,089 lines, nearly all formatter-written samples (CARET-OPERATOR-ORIGIN rule). (7) Lone `then`: 37 artifacts, 4 healed, 27 in the land queue, 6 after the heal lands (FORMATTER-THEN-BELOW). (8) tmp/scripts: 36 ps1 + 6 py + 4 txt in 7 subfolders, 280 KB, growing about one file every 2 days since 10-06 (COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE, SPIRAL-SCRIPT-TESTS). (9) Cells Elixir: 14,126 lines in 86 files vs 1,783 Spiral (EXS-TO-SPIRAL). (10) TSV/TXT state files: platform-bound.tsv healed; .txt classified (TXT-FILE-SCAN). Order for LLM-only blocks: (2) and (3) are mechanical, safe and high-count, then (1) by file size, then (5), then (4) with a compile loop.")
inl note_of_pages_sweep_place_1 () = Note ("PAGES-SWEEP-PLACE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T11:45Z", "main", "2026-10-24 07:45 main: origin: the sweep.ps1 of TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO lap 12 (2026-10-19), a cross-repo check of every org page for the features dir-tree-html renders (README section, viewport meta, portrait media query, gh-pages/main heads). It reached spiral/scripts in the 10-24 commit. Rule: a check lives with the product it checks, so it moved to dir-tree-html/pages-sweep.ps1 (hash-identical; removed from spiral/scripts, both uncommitted for the user). Next step under SPIRAL-SCRIPT-TESTS: a test block in dir_tree_html.spi replaces the pwsh.")
inl note_of_pages_sweep_place_2 () = Note ("PAGES-SWEEP-PLACE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T23:53Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:53 main correction: the 07:45 rule (a check lives with the product it checks) was misapplied. pages-sweep.ps1 checks every org repo's deployed page (10 repos, .github among them), not dir-tree-html's code, so it is org tooling and belongs with workflow.ps1 in the org .github repo. Moved to i574n.github/scripts/pages-sweep.ps1 (hash 9188C6583644C400, parse 0; removed from dir-tree-html; uncommitted for the user). No references to update.")
inl note_of_txt_file_scan_1 () = Note ("TXT-FILE-SCAN", 1u32, "2026-10-24T12:14Z", "main", "2026-10-24 08:14 main: scan done over 11 repos: 10 tracked, 16 untracked .txt files, so no infestation yet. Each class has a rule and an owner. (1) Needed by format: tree-sitter test corpus (2, the tree-sitter test format requires .txt). (2) Data copies: tlds.txt holds the same bytes in spiral apps/cells/priv (read by x_compat.ex), in x/postability (x's profile data) and in the untracked Cells fixture apps/cells/test/fixtures/postability (a copy of x's profile, so Cells tests need no x checkout; it is a fixture the user should commit or the test should copy). A 4th, different copy sits in spiral lab/SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX (lab sweep). Rule: one home for shared data. The Cells copy should become the single source x imports, or vice versa, decided under SPIRAL-NOTEBOOK-FILES/x. (3) Test expectations as files: 4 expected-exit.txt files under tmp/scripts/export-check-rust. They become inline expectations in Spiral samples under COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE. (4) Python deps: requirements.txt in spiral and polyglot, removed with the Python code (ZETA-PY-RETIRE, FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE). (5) fiscal src/*.txt (4 untracked, 370 KB of corpus and pattern data): FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE decides generated vs committed. (6) lab leftovers (spiral 4, eoie 2): LAB-SESSION-SWEEP. A new .txt file needs one of these reasons.")
inl note_of_agile_fields_review_1 () = Note ("AGILE-FIELDS-REVIEW", 1u32, "2026-10-24T12:59Z", "main", "2026-10-24 08:59 main: review of the schema and both weighted views on the live backlog (722 items: 121 Planned, 201 Active, 17 Blocked, 32 Paused, 351 Done). Findings: (1) WSJF under-weights big P0s. The cost of delay is about 4.25x P1 for P0 (CELLS-PACK-NEXT 90 min scores 72,533 vs 51,200 for 30-min P1s), so a P0 of 480 min (GRANULAR-LINT) ranks below 120-min P1/P2 children of its own epic. Ask: P0 cost of delay at about 16x P1, or an explicit time-criticality term fed by the request date, so the user's top priority leads until split. (2) The subagent LPT view lists other sessions' items (TIBIA at #1) and Paused work. Ask: filter by team (the session's teams) and exclude Paused/Blocked by default. (3) Standing duties (LANE-GAUGE-DAILY, the per-report BACKENDS-SOTA-SCORE recompute) sit at the top of `next` because their estimates are tiny. Ask: a `cadence` field (once | per-report | daily) that keeps standing items out of next and puts them in a report checklist view. (4) WIP: 201 Active with no limit or staleness signal. Ask: a view of Active items per team with days since the last note, flagging stale Active (>3 days without a note) for a status decision. (5) 35 Planned items have no estimate (fill-all-task-fields rule). Ask: `agile check` warns on a Planned or Active item without estimate/load/assignee. (6) The request source lives in titles (\"user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list\"). Ask: a structured `requested` field (who + when + quote id) that the time-criticality term in (1) reads. No field to remove: D (difficulty) and estimate measure different things (risk vs size), and load (CPU class) is what the scheduler needs.")
inl note_of_agile_fields_review_2 () = Note ("AGILE-FIELDS-REVIEW", 2u32, "2026-10-24T13:05Z", "eoie", "eoie: all six asks from note 1 are routed. (1) EOIE-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT: P0 4096 = 16x P1, derived from 480 vs 30 min, with a live-backlog acceptance rule. (2) Paused and Blocked are already out of next (only Planned and Active are open; TIBIA is Active). The team filter already exists: 'eoie agile view <root> subagent-main' (stored now) runs next --as subagent --team main. What is left is epics topping LPT: EOIE-AGILE-LPT-LEAVES. (3) EOIE-AGILE-CADENCE, with an 'agile duties' checklist view. (4) EOIE-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE: list --stale 3 as the view 'stale'. (5) EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS: check warns, and --strict fails. (6) EOIE-AGILE-REQUESTED, a typed who/when/quote with a capped time-criticality term, plus a title migration; it ships with cadence in one schema slice. Each item has its decision rule written before any measurement. Total about 705 min, all light; they ride the train after S5.")
inl note_of_agile_fields_review_3 () = Note ("AGILE-FIELDS-REVIEW", 3u32, "2026-10-24T13:06Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:06 main: closed. eoie routed all six asks (its note 2): EOIE-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT (P0 = 16x P1), EOIE-AGILE-LPT-LEAVES (the real LPT gap was epics topping by size; the team filter already existed, view subagent-main), EOIE-AGILE-CADENCE + agile duties, EOIE-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE (list --stale 3), EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS (--strict for the hook), EOIE-AGILE-REQUESTED (who/when/quote, time criticality capped at 2x after 7 days).")
inl note_of_kit_standards_spi_1 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-SPI", 1u32, "2026-10-24T11:17Z", "eoie", "07:2x groom, initial evidence: standards.spi declares a package's style contract (NoCommentLines, LineWidth 120, LfOnly, TestTargets, HostFree, LibReferenced), which 'eoie standards <package>' and the ratchet (standards/census.spi) read, per eoie AGENTS.md. Usefulness test, written before measuring: count how many packages declare HostFree+LibReferenced and whether 'eoie standards' has refused a real violation in the last week (release logs). If never refusing and only restating defaults, fold them into one repo-level default and per-package exceptions only.")
inl note_of_kit_standards_spi_2 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-SPI", 2u32, "2026-10-27T13:43Z", "kit", "09:4x kit: measured by the 10-24 rule (fold only if they never refuse AND only restate defaults). Kit src has 428 packages and 66 standards.spi files. 62 declare the HostFree+LibReferenced contract, and 0 contain only the four default lines (comments, width, lf, targets). The other 4 hold one marker each, or are kit_standards' own code. Refusals: 1,802 kit job logs from the last 7 days show no 'kit standards' line with findings above 0, but the PostToolUse edit hook's checks are not logged there, so 'never refused' is unproven, not shown. Verdict: keep. The files are the per-package contract markers the AGENTS style rule reads ('a package is a style model only if its standards.spi declares HostFree and LibReferenced'), and tonight's landing used them (13 packages declared, kit standards findings=0). Possible later simplification, not part of this item: the four default lines repeated in every file could become implied repo defaults, leaving each file just its contract lines.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_chain_serial_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-CHAIN-SERIAL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T11:18Z", "eoie", "07:1x split out of EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS (blocked on the host held queue) because it doesn't need the host change: drafted 06:3x as scratch job-chain-serial/job-chain-serial.do (11 edits, dry run clean on post-R, test block in eoie_job_rechain/model.spi), packaged in next-train stage S5; typecheck in runner eoie-serial-checks-2. Done = installed, then a 3-link serial chain shows 1 supervisor while links 2-3 wait, and --ok skips the rest after a failing link.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_chain_serial_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-CHAIN-SERIAL", 2u32, "2026-10-25T07:11Z", "eoie", "03:11 Landed in the tree, rebased onto the post-split layout: the parser and argument builders are in eoie_job_after_model, the start side in eoie_job_chain (878 lines), and the in-job --run-serial runner in its own crate eoie_job_serial (549, exported as eoie_job_serial_run), dispatched by eoie_jobs/jobs.spi. Measured on 5k: cargo check green; after_model, chain and jobs-model notebooks pass. Left: ships with SLATE; after install, a live run of job chain --serial with two links, checking one process while a link waits.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_chain_serial_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-CHAIN-SERIAL", 3u32, "2026-10-26T08:30Z", "eoie", "04:30 eoie: done criteria met on the release candidate. Live check (kit scratch clean\\respell\\chain-serial-check.ps1) run on the worker node against the renamed candidate kit.exe with a fixture jobs home: 3/3. (1) A 3-link job chain --serial --ok holds exactly 1 supervisor while links b and c wait; the jobs home then holds 2 records, the chain job plus running link a's job run record (per-link ids by design), and none yet for b and c. (2) Every link of a passing serial chain ran (marker files a, b, c) and the chain job finished exit 0. (3) With --ok, a failing link a (exit 3) skips b and c (only marker a), and the chain job settles exit 1. Process tree seen: one job host, one job supervise (the supervisor copy under LOCALAPPDATA\\kit\\supervisor), job chain --run-serial, one job run at a time. The check is wired into the rename's post-install as a required step, so it reruns on the installed kit.exe after finalize.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_chain_serial_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-CHAIN-SERIAL", 4u32, "2026-10-27T00:54Z", "kit", "20:54 kit: verified on the INSTALLED kit.exe (C0F2D2F3C66BFD43, published 20:26:42) by post-install-RENAME after the finalize: chain --serial 3/3 (one supervisor while links b and c wait, every link runs in one job, --ok skips the rest after a failing link). The done criteria were already met on the candidate; now confirmed installed.")
inl note_of_tibia_no_hand_lua_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-NO-HAND-LUA", 1u32, "2026-10-24T23:51Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 19:51 User: 'i asked why you're still writing lua files by hand and you didn't rewrite the ones in live/server-probes'. Root cause: the 03:2x stop-hand-written-Lua fix (TIBIA-PARTY note 86) was scoped to apps/ code. It called the older live/ harness Lua 'predates tonight' and left it alone, and it didn't list live/server-probes, which this lane wrote by hand on 10-22/10-23 (EVENT-TIME note 105, PARTY note 62). The rule now covers every Lua file in tibia, apps/ and live/. Inventory 19:5x (glob **/*.lua, excluding target/ build output): live/server-probes/ground_trace.lua and lead_trace.lua (Canary server scripts, untracked); live/parity.lua, bot-lua-dofile.lua and bot-network-sandbox.lua (tracked, OTClient side, spliced into the bot loader by run-policy-scenario.ps1 lines 26 and 118-120); plus 2 Lua lines embedded in run-policy-scenario.ps1. Plan, sized from milestones: M1 120m: a Canary server binding module (Position, Game.getSpectators, Creature, Tile, CreatureEvent, GlobalEvent, addEvent, systemTime; one host call per macro, at most 120 chars, logic in Spiral), plus ground_trace as Spiral with test blocks for its pure state and line formatting. Its log append goes through lib file_system, adding a Lua arm in lib where one is missing (same family as the RED file_exists arm). Accept when, on the plain-Lua fake-Canary stub, it emits the same trace lines as the hand-written file. M2 90m: lead_trace, same acceptance. M3 30m: run-arena.ps1 -ServerProbe takes the Spiral module and installs its compiled output; delete the two .lua files. M4 90m: parity.lua becomes an apps/arena Spiral module; PR-11's parity behaviour stays unchanged, checked by the stub or the next live block. M5 90m: bot-lua-dofile, the network sandbox and the nexbot trace snippet become Spiral-generated loader preludes. No install during a live block, and BENCHMARK rows that cite the hand-written probes keep their registered harness.")
inl note_of_tibia_no_hand_lua_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-NO-HAND-LUA", 2u32, "2026-10-25T07:11Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 03:11 M1 DONE. New package apps/canary (edition 3; packageDir lib, |core- spiral-). canary.spi: Canary server bindings, one host call per macro, each at most 120 chars (Position, Game.getSpectators typed as lib array, creature getters, CreatureEvent and GlobalEvent with onDeath, onThink and interval setters taking Spiral closures, systemTime, plus for M2 Tile, getTopCreature, Creature(id), getTarget and addEvent with a no-argument closure). ground_trace.spi: all logic in Spiral: watched creatures as a list in a mut cell, trace lines with ++# and ++#?, the log through lib file_system.append_all_text (the Lua arm landed 10-24 22:06), the run stamp through date_time.now and date_time.format (os.date in its Lua arm); no dollar-quote macro and no lua. call outside canary.spi. Acceptance (job-1792911776790-tibia-canary-check): under scratch server-probe/stub-ground.lua the Spiral probe writes the same 9 trace lines as live/server-probes/ground_trace.lua (run stamp masked); formatter clean; Cells 2 of 2 with memo off (list helpers on lua and c, spot_text on lua; c lacks number-to-text, the same gap as CELLS-APPS note 21). M2 (lead_trace.spi plus 8 bindings) written and checking under stub-run.lua and stub-run-boxed.lua (job-1792912146291). M3 prepared: apps/canary/build.ps1 compiles every main module to target/canary/<name>.lua with a LUA-OK parse check, so run-arena -ServerProbe target/canary/lead_trace.lua installs it unchanged (same leaf name, same trace log name). BENCHMARK PC-10 (lines 304 and 314) cites live/server-probes/lead_trace.lua as its registered harness; it keeps that and gets a dated pointer to the Spiral port.")
inl note_of_tibia_no_hand_lua_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-NO-HAND-LUA", 3u32, "2026-10-25T07:15Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 03:15 M2 DONE (job-1792912146291-tibia-canary-check-2): lead_trace.spi plus Tile, getTopCreature, Creature(id), getTarget, addEvent and creature-event onThink bindings. Under stub-run-boxed.lua the Spiral probe writes the same 15 trace lines as the hand probe: think, boxed-think, the boxed-check delayed 750 ms through addEvent with captured values replacing addEvent's extra arguments, targeting, knight and monster moves, knight gone. stub-run.lua predates Tile, so both probes fail at the same call after 5 identical lines. Cells 1 of 1, formatter clean; 2 over-120 lines split by hand. M3 running (job-1792912454531): apps/canary/build.ps1 into target/canary, then the full check. M4 DESIGN (parity.lua, 67 lines, spliced into every competitor's bot loader): it wraps 13 g_game functions plus LocalPlayer.autoWalk in place with vararg forwarding (function(...) if not admit() then return false end original(...) return true end), wraps g_map.getSpectators to filter by view range, and sends one turn South 300 ms after onGameStart. The vararg forwarder is the one irreducible host piece. Options: (a) a one-call binding whose body contains host flow, which breaks GRANULAR-HEAL unless the rule gets a documented exception; (b) per-arity adapters like client.spi's existing curried handler; (c) check whether a Spiral closure over a tuple argument compiles to a flat multi-argument Lua function; if it does, every gate is '(!owner)[!name] = !f' plus a '(!orig)(!a, !b)' call binding, with all flow in Spiral and no exception. Rule, written before checking: take (c) if one compile shows tuple-domain closures emit function(a, b); else (b); (a) never. The gated functions' arities come from OTClient's game.cpp signatures. CHECKED 2026-10-25 03:16 (scratch tuple-closure/probe.lua): a closure over (i32 * string) compiles to 'function(dom) local v0, v1 = (table.unpack or unpack)(dom) ...', so a host call f(a, b) would pass a bare number as dom. (c) fails, and by the rule M4 takes (b): per-arity adapter bindings such as '$'function(a, b) return (!f)({ a, b }) end'', a single branch-free function-literal expression under 120 chars, the shape of client.spi's existing curried handler. Arity per gated function from OTClient game.cpp; admission, counters, the view filter and the login turn all in Spiral.")
inl note_of_tibia_no_hand_lua_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-NO-HAND-LUA", 4u32, "2026-10-25T07:20Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 03:20 M3 DONE (job-1792912454531-tibia-canary-m3): apps/canary/build.ps1 builds target/canary/ground_trace.lua and lead_trace.lua with LUA-OK parse checks; the full check is green on those builds (stub traces 9/9, 5/5, 15/15 identical; Cells 2/2 and 1/1; formatter clean; the last over-120 line split by hand). live/server-probes/ removed. Both files were untracked and never in git, so copies are kept in scratch hand-server-probes-retired as the stub reference. BENCHMARK: PC-10 and PR-26 each get a dated 'Probe port (2026-10-25)' row after their RESULT rows saying the registered hand probe is retired, its Spiral port writes identical traces under the stubs, and later runs pass -ServerProbe target/canary/<name>.lua; the registered rows stay as written. run-arena.ps1 needs no change (it installs by leaf name and derives the same trace log name). Hand-written Lua left in tibia: live/parity.lua (M4, per-arity adapters, decided in note 3), live/bot-lua-dofile.lua and live/bot-network-sandbox.lua plus 2 lines in run-policy-scenario.ps1 (M5). About 180 min left of 420.")
inl note_of_tibia_no_hand_lua_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-NO-HAND-LUA", 5u32, "2026-10-25T07:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 03:30 M4 DONE except its live check. live/parity.lua (67 lines, tracked, so git keeps it; scratch copy too) is replaced by apps/arena/parity.spi (logic in Spiral: admission against the 250 ms gap, allowed/dropped counters, the one-turn login-protection end 300 ms after onGameStart, the 15x11 same-floor view filter, the summary text) and apps/arena/hooks.spi, a second OTClient binding boundary next to client.spi, kept separate because the pending client heal replaces client.spi as a whole file. Each hook is one host call or one branch-free adapter expression. Arities from otclient-src game.h and localplayer.h: 0 cancelAttack/cancelAttackAndFollow; 1 attack/follow/walk/turn/talk/use/useInventoryItem; 2 useWith/useInventoryItemWith/getSpectators/LocalPlayer.autoWalk (self, dest); 3 move/talkChannel. Per-arity adapters as the note 3 rule chose: arity 0 and 1 closures are assigned directly; arity 2 and 3 use 'function(a, b) return (!f)({ a, b }) end'. Only ArenaParity.summary is read outside (client.spi:161), so only that is published. Proof (job-1792913135886-tibia-parity-land): arena build LUA-OK for all modules incl. parity, formatter clean, and under scratch server-probe/stub-parity.lua (login turn, every arity admitted and dropped across the gap, view filter, summary) target/arena/parity.lua matches the hand prelude on 16 of 16 lines; it also matches when wrapped exactly as the harness splices it. live/run-policy-scenario.ps1:26 now splices target/arena/parity.lua inside '(function() ... end)()', the same wrapper SpiralPolicy already uses, so the module's top-level return and its 55 locals stay inside. Left for M4: PR-11 behaviour confirmed in the next live window, with the gated and dropped counts in the probe log. Left: M5 (bot-lua-dofile, network sandbox, the getfenv probe line and the pcall(dofile) source shim; nExBot-only, and TIBIA-BASELINES is paused), about 90 min.")
inl note_of_tibia_no_hand_lua_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-NO-HAND-LUA", 6u32, "2026-10-25T09:48Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 05:48 M5 DONE (job-1792921150420-tibia-m5-land). live/bot-network-sandbox.lua and live/bot-lua-dofile.lua (tracked; scratch copies kept) become apps/arena/bot_prelude.spi (network stubs; SpiralBotDofile) and apps/arena/bot_dofile.spi (path resolve under /bot/nExBot, g_resources read, loadstring, setfenv to the module env, results through the one-expression adapter 'function(path) return unpack((!f)(path)) end', load errors raised at level 3 so they name the same caller as the hand shim's level 2), plus apps/arena/bot_env.spi for the env probe the harness used to write as a Lua string. Globals are set with bare-name assignment bindings so they land in the bot sandbox env like the hand code, not _G (the stub checks nothing leaks). The pcall(dofile, ...) source shim now inserts only an identifier, 'pcall(SpiralBotDofile, '. The TIBIA_NEXBOT_TRACE diagnostic (trace function, inserted trace lines, the floor-skip switch; lines 130-151) is removed: it served TIBIA-NEXBOT-CRASH, which is Done, and nothing reads its output. Splices use 'do (function() ... end)() end': without the do block, Lua 5.1 reads a '(' line after '...)()' as a call ('ambiguous syntax'), which the first M5 stub run caught; the M4 parity splice had the same flaw and is fixed the same way (a parse check of the spliced probe file is LUA-OK). Proof: formatter clean; arena build LUA-OK for all 14 modules; parity stub 16/16 and nExBot stub 9/9 identical to the retired hand versions (network stubs, no leak into _G, multi-value returns, relative and absolute paths, missing file, load error text through pcall). Known edge, documented: a missing file returns no values (unpack of an empty table) where the hand shim returned one nil, visible only through select('#', ...). BENCHMARK's nExBot shims row now names bot_dofile.spi. Hand-written .lua files left in tibia's live, apps, src and scripts: 0. Left: live confirmation in the next live window (PR-11 parity counts in the probe log; nExBot only if TIBIA-BASELINES resumes).")
inl note_of_tibia_no_hand_lua_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-NO-HAND-LUA", 7u32, "2026-10-26T01:09Z", "tibia-lane", "01:09Z M4 live check PASSED in PR-28 (2026-10-25 20:05-21:03). apps/arena/parity.spi, built to target/arena/parity.lua, printed its summary in all 11 knight logs (parity: gap 250 ms, view 15x11, actions allowed 4-26, dropped 0-4), and the hunts behaved like PR-27 (BENCHMARK.md PR-28 row). With M1-M5 done and no hand-written .lua left anywhere in the tibia tree (a recursive *.lua search outside target, deps and node_modules finds none), the item is complete. live.spi itself retires right after (POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS note 21, queued in slices-a).")
inl note_of_fiscal_root_paths_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-ROOT-PATHS", 1u32, "2026-10-25T00:53Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:53 main: design settled by a probe. Cells runs a test with its working directory set to the package's src folder (job-1792889517230-main-fiscal-cwd-probe-2 printed cwd=...\\fiscal-cwd-probe\\src), so walking up from the current directory reaches the repo root locally with no environment needed. CI sets FISCAL_ROOT anyway. The marker is src/generator.spi, because scripts/init.ps1 also exists in spiral and would match the wrong repo. Rust's get_source_directory is the crate folder Cells generates, never the repo, so it can't serve as the anchor. Work on a copy first; the regeneration then goes through the copy gate (FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE order step d).")
inl note_of_fiscal_root_paths_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-ROOT-PATHS", 2u32, "2026-10-25T05:00Z", "main", "2026-10-25 01:00 main: GREEN on the real repo (job-1792903059836): corpus 1/1, corpus_checks 14/14, generated_mdfe_v1_00 1/1, so fiscal_paths.root () finds C:\\home\\git\\fiscal from the src working directory. Two fixes on the way: fiscal_paths.spi was missing open sm'_operators, and corpus_checks.spi lacked the ///- --package . header, so it couldn't see package modules. Both are folded into %TEMP%\\fiscal-paths\\apply-root-paths.ps1. Left: regenerate the modules through the generator (heavy), so the emitted sample writers come from generator.spi; then FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW.")
inl note_of_fiscal_root_paths_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-ROOT-PATHS", 3u32, "2026-10-27T01:01Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 21:01 fiscal: the 'left' step of main's 10-25 01:00 note is done. Every generated module was regenerated through generator.spi today with the Cells memo off (node job-1793051865826-fiscal-ubl-proof2: generator 9/9 executed, 0 memo hits, every generated/*/*.spi rewritten 18:38), and those modules' example tests write their samples through the generator-emitted sample_path (family-gates: 16 samples at 0 validation messages). The emitted helper (generator.spi sample_helper) matches fiscal_paths.root (): FISCAL_ROOT override, else find_parent File src/generator.spi from the current directory. It is a deliberate duplicate, because each generated module is its own package and cannot import fiscal_paths. It differs only by skipping normalize_path. ONE GAP, so not closed: every run so far went through build.ps1, which sets FISCAL_ROOT, so the emitted no-env fallback has never executed. Remaining check (light, one Cells run): run generated_cfdi_v4_0's example test from generated/generated_cfdi_v4_0 with FISCAL_ROOT unset; expect the sample under <repo>/samples. FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW stays its own item.")
inl note_of_fiscal_root_paths_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-ROOT-PATHS", 4u32, "2026-10-27T01:02Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 21:02 fiscal: the remaining check is queued on the node as job-1793063025975-fiscal-root-fallback (after the bench), runner session scratchpad identity/root-fallback.ps1. It unsets FISCAL_ROOT, moves the CFDI sample aside, runs generated_cfdi_v4_0's tests from its own folder with the memo off, and checks that the sample reappears under <repo>/samples after the start (then restores the original, hash-verified). PASS (pre-written): tests failed 0, memo_hits 0, sample rewritten after start -> mark Done.")
inl note_of_fiscal_root_paths_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-ROOT-PATHS", 5u32, "2026-10-27T04:20Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 00:20 fiscal: the root-fallback check (job-1793063025975) is VOID, not a failure of the fallback: the CFDI module never compiled, because the node mirror's lib/spiral/date_time.spi had been overwritten by a bug in my bench runner (FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH note 33), and the test died on that parse error before sample_path ran. Mirror repaired; requeued as job-1793074822530-fiscal-root-fallback2 with the same pre-written rule.")
inl note_of_fiscal_root_paths_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-ROOT-PATHS", 6u32, "2026-10-27T05:01Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 01:01 fiscal: DONE by the pre-written rule. job-1793074822530-fiscal-root-fallback2 on the repaired mirror: FISCAL_ROOT unset; generated_cfdi_v4_0's tests ran from the module's own folder with the memo off (executed 2, failed 0, memo_hits 0); the sample was rewritten under <repo>/samples after the start by the generator-emitted sample_path fallback (find_parent src/generator.spi); original sample restored hash-verified. With note 3's evidence (every module regenerated through generator.spi today, the emitted writer equivalent to fiscal_paths.root ()), one fiscal_root and every path under it hold locally with and without FISCAL_ROOT. FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW stays its own item.")
inl note_of_fiscal_lib_first_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-LIB-FIRST", 1u32, "2026-10-25T16:04Z", "fiscal", "12:0x fiscal: fiscal side done, pending the post-land build. generator.spi's list (is_upper/is_lower/is_alnum, has_name, join_lines, join_with, map_strings, take_items, distinct_values, digit/digits/int_text, hex_text, FNV) left fiscal with the lib-first refactor of 03:21 (generator.spi opens lib xsd_emit; 0 of those names remain in fiscal/src). corpus_checks now: has_id -> listm'.contains (earlier), the 3 newline scans -> xsd_regex.lines_of (earlier), enumerated -> xsd_emit.enumeration_values (same body), states_named -> listm'.try_find with a (name, _) lambda, the Cons chain of cen_examples_pass_the_en16931_rules -> a list literal, xsd_regex.count_text -> sm'.obj_to_string (as line 188 already does). format --check clean; type-check rides the post-land fiscal build. Left outside fiscal: whether lib xsd_emit's own text helpers should be lib sm' generics is the lib audit's question (EOIE-PRIMITIVES-TO-LIB), not this item's.")
inl note_of_fiscal_lib_first_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-LIB-FIRST", 2u32, "2026-10-25T21:20Z", "fiscal", "18:1x fiscal: DONE. The corpus_checks lib swaps (xsd_emit.enumeration_values, listm'.try_find_ (the join-point variant, after the inline try_find tripped EJP0040), the list literal, sm'.obj_to_string) pass in the full build: corpus_checks 15/15. generator.spi's helper list left fiscal with the 10-25 lib-first refactor. Lib-side generic-helper questions stay with EOIE-PRIMITIVES-TO-LIB.")
inl note_of_cells_edition3_keywords_1 () = Note ("CELLS-EDITION3-KEYWORDS", 1u32, "2026-10-25T01:14Z", "main", "2026-10-24 21:14 main: fix drafted in the tree (uncommitted, for the user). Each classifier takes the symbol spelling next to the word: open/:<, prototype/:?, instance/:+, type/:==, union/:|, nominal/:!, inl/:=, and/:&, let/:~, rec/^ (the table is edition3_symbol_token in the core). Every respelled top-level line is the symbol plus one space (lib survey: := 1,790, :~ 335, :! 318, :< 160, :== 123, :& 82, :| 46, :+ 40, :? 15), and rec is ' ^ '. := is a prefix of :==; text.spi requires a space right after the keyword, so a type line is never read as a binding. The domain compiler regenerates src/spiral_cells/domain.gleam when a .spi is newer. Verification is held until EDITION3 pass 2 lands (a Cells run would stall the land's reader guard): tibia's probe_a/probe_b as the positive control (red on the old Cells, jobs job-1792890134891 and job-1792890272689), lib_gate on respelled lib files with top-level :! and tests, then the Cells suite on the tree. Exposure: 34 lib files with a top-level :! and test blocks.")
inl note_of_cells_edition3_keywords_2 () = Note ("CELLS-EDITION3-KEYWORDS", 2u32, "2026-10-25T01:28Z", "main", "2026-10-24 21:28 main: VERIFIED, and full-suite proof is queued. (1) Lib gates (--gate implies --no-memo) pass on the respelled otp_actor.spi 3/3 and channel.spi 2/2, both with top-level :! and tests. (2) A memo-off control in %TEMP%\\cells-e3-control (two :! nominals plus a test) passes with memo_hits 0, a fresh 13.2 s; its words twin passes too. (3) tibia's probes now parse; they stop on _assert_eq being unbound, because a lab package has no lib test prelude, which is the probe, not the fix. Lesson: Cells' memo keys on tokens, so the words and symbols spellings of one program share an entry, which is correct but makes a cached run useless as a spelling test; the first symbols control was a memo replay (memo_hits 1). Next: the full Cells suite on the tree, job-1792891698451-main-serial-cells-suite-e3; Done if it shows no new red beyond the 11:55 baseline's failures.")
inl note_of_cells_edition3_keywords_3 () = Note ("CELLS-EDITION3-KEYWORDS", 3u32, "2026-10-25T01:48Z", "main", "2026-10-24 21:48 main: DONE by its rule. Suite on the tree (job-1792891698451-main-serial-cells-suite-e3, 21:28-21:35) has 2 failures, both from before: CellsLiveTest 'print with no value' (RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG, fix in 5j) and DocumentTest 'lib .spi files load as notebooks'. The second was red at 11:55 too; its test stops at the first failing file (now decimal.spi). Proof of no new red: a scan of every lib file with that test's three checks (scratch sm-rt/lib-trip-all.exs) fails exactly the same 15 files with the same first difference on the live edition-3 lib as on the pre-land words text, once the listm' header fix below is in.")
inl note_of_tibia_edition3_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-EDITION3", 1u32, "2026-10-25T03:12Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 23:12 User directive (via main, 23:0x): edition 3 spreads to every repo, and the tibia repo is tibia's to respell. Done 23:2x: respell-repo.ps1 on copies GREEN (51 of 52 .spi files, 0 errors, words round trip byte-identical, 0 installed-formatter changes); the Cells round-trip scan covered 51 files per side with the same 33 failures, all a pre-existing Document drop of line 2 'test <module> static ='; reported to main. Land queued as heavy job-1792897922037-tibia-respell-land: apply as an unstaged layer over the user's staged files, the arena build with compiled Lua byte-identical to the pre-respell hashes, the full build.ps1 with SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0, and a restore of all 51 originals on any failure. Excluded: src/terrain.spi, which apps/map/run.ps1 generates in words and matches by 'let <name> '. The installed compiler (10:08) ignores format --spelling. Left after the land: (a) once a spelling-capable formatter is installed, run.ps1 formats its generated text with --spelling symbols and matches ':~ <name> ', then terrain.spi respells (about 20 min); (b) re-derive the tibia drafts on edition-3 text: client-heal footing and batch2 (stale anyway against the staged base), batch3, and the CELLS-APPS candidates (about 40 min, folded into GRANULAR-HEAL and CELLS-APPS).")
inl note_of_tibia_edition3_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-EDITION3", 2u32, "2026-10-25T04:25Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 00:25 LANDED (job-1792898814421-tibia-respell-land-2, exit 0, 57 min): 51 files in edition 3 as an unstaged layer over the user's staged files. Proof: the arena's compiled Lua is byte-identical to the pre-respell build (9 of 9, hashes in scratch arena-lua-before-respell.tsv), and the full scripts/build.ps1 is green with SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 (formatter clean; every file executed = discovered; 0 memo hits; pack_sim 39/39, geometry 16/16, terrain 12/12, gpu_sim 6/6, hunt_sim 11/11). The first try (job-1792897922037) restored itself: apps/arena/build.ps1 found probes by '^inl main \\(\\)'. Now both spellings are accepted there and in scripts/predict.ps1's candidate anchor (unstaged). hunting_pack shows discovered 0 because its only block is the module 'test hunting_pack static =' with an opens-only body, which Cells classes as a prelude; the words original is the same, so the respell didn't cause it. Left: src/terrain.spi plus the map generator once a spelling-capable formatter is installed (about 20 min), and re-deriving the tibia drafts on edition-3 text (in GRANULAR-HEAL and CELLS-APPS).")
inl note_of_tibia_edition3_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-EDITION3", 3u32, "2026-10-25T05:26Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 01:26 DONE. With pack 5j installed (format --spelling in the installed compiler): src/terrain.spi respelled (2928 lines unchanged, 13 test headers untouched, words round trip byte-identical). apps/map/run.ps1 now lays out the generated text with 'format', then 'format --spelling symbols', and matches ':~ <name> '. Order matters: --spelling swaps tokens but doesn't re-lay long lines (the first check run had arena_protection_zone as one long line). 5j's formatter lays out the generated nested if-chains differently from the old one, so run.ps1 -Update rewrote arena_spot_bits, arena_road_bits and arena_sight_bits (729 to 241 lines each, layout only), which is the generator's designed path. Proof, job-1792905376393-tibia-terrain-relayout: run.ps1 check 'src/terrain.spi matches the server map (4 definitions)', formatter check clean over src and apps, terrain 12/12 with memo off incl. WGSL on the GPU. git diff src/terrain.spi +1163/-2625 (respell plus re-layout). Draft re-derivation on edition-3 text stays in GRANULAR-HEAL and CELLS-APPS. Under 5j, any other byte-identical pins (e.g. the arena Lua hashes) need a re-pin after one rebuild (main: 18 codegen passes).")
inl note_of_eoie_job_start_flag_order_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-START-FLAG-ORDER", 1u32, "2026-10-25T07:27Z", "eoie", "main 03:2x: eoie job start --heavy --after-ok <id> <label> 3600000 pwsh ... failed with 'job timeout-ms must be a positive integer', while --after-ok <id> --heavy works (job-1792912923374). Cause: start_args read --after only at argument 3, then the shape, then the options, so --heavy first left --after-ok unread. Fix drafted (scratch clean\\start-flags\\any-order.do; dry-run ok on live src, 3 replacements): one flags_read loop over the lead flags and options in any order, plus a 'given twice' refusal. It applies once the S-stage preflight releases the tree, gets proven by a split-check of eoie_job_start_args on 5k, and ships in SLATE. The check after install runs both orders against the release candidate.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_start_flag_order_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-START-FLAG-ORDER", 2u32, "2026-10-25T11:02Z", "eoie", "07:02 eoie: applied in the live tree (one flags_read loop over --after/--after-ok, --heavy/--compile and the options; a repeated lead flag is refused as '<flag> is given twice'). On 5k, eoie_job_start_args regenerated and passed cargo check in both S-stage regen batches. It ships in SLATE. Left: after install, run both orders against the installed binary (--heavy --after-ok <id> ... and --after-ok <id> --heavy ...) and check that each queues a job with --after-ok behind the given id.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_start_flag_order_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-START-FLAG-ORDER", 3u32, "2026-10-25T13:29Z", "eoie", "09:29 eoie: second case from main: 'job start --quiet --quiet-hold 5 --quiet-wait 600 --heavy <label> ...' is refused with '--quiet applies to --heavy jobs' (job-1792934679977 worked with --heavy first). Same root: the old start_args read the shape only before the options, so --heavy after the options was never seen. The landed fix covers it: flags_read consumes --after/--after-ok, --heavy/--compile and every option in one loop in any order, and the quiet-needs-heavy check runs after the loop on the parsed result. The post-install check now runs four orders: --heavy --after-ok, --after-ok --heavy, --heavy --quiet, and --quiet ... --heavy; each must queue the same job.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_start_flag_order_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-START-FLAG-ORDER", 4u32, "2026-10-26T08:40Z", "eoie", "04:40 eoie: the post-install check passes on the release candidate. kit scratch clean\\respell\\flag-order-check.ps1, run on the worker node against the renamed candidate kit.exe with a fixture jobs home: 5/5. --heavy --after-ok <id>, --after-ok <id> --heavy, --heavy ... --quiet and --quiet --quiet-hold 5 --quiet-wait 600 ... --heavy each queue the same heavy-gated job behind the predecessor (after_mode=ok; the quiet options parsed either side of --heavy), and --after-ok given twice is refused ('is given twice'). Every queued job was stopped before its predecessor finished, so no heavy slot was taken. Wired into the rename's post-install as a required step.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_start_flag_order_5 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-START-FLAG-ORDER", 5u32, "2026-10-27T00:54Z", "kit", "20:54 kit: verified on the INSTALLED kit.exe (C0F2D2F3C66BFD43, published 20:26:42) by post-install-RENAME after the finalize: flag order 5/5. Status left as is, because the item's remaining scope beyond this check was not re-read at close.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_start_flag_order_6 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-START-FLAG-ORDER", 6u32, "2026-10-27T14:22Z", "kit", "10:3x kit: closing. Re-read the full scope (notes 1-5): both lead-flag orders, the quiet options on either side of --heavy, and the by-name refusal of a repeated lead flag. All were verified 5/5 on the INSTALLED kit.exe at 20:54 (note 5). Nothing beyond that check remains.")
inl note_of_fiscal_pattern_oracle_families_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES", 1u32, "2026-10-25T15:51Z", "fiscal", "11:5x fiscal: drafted and measured. Distinct patterns outside the BR list: it 31 (15 with \\p), mx 24, pl 21, eu 0, 75 in union; all compile under .NET; the committed patterns.txt is exactly the distinct pattern set of the 580 corpus.txt schemas (same 196, different order), so the list is derivable. Two parts: (1) applied now, scripts/pattern-examples.ps1 reads \\p{Block} / \\P{Block} inside and outside classes (printable representatives per block, so probes carry no control characters; -cin/-ceq because PowerShell's -in/-eq ignore case): the 196 BR patterns give byte-identical examples to HEAD (392 of 392), the 75 family patterns now give 150 of 150 examples that .NET matches (20 threw before). (2) post-land patch, session scratchpad fiscal-post-land/apply.py (5 files, all-or-nothing): pattern-oracle.ps1 derives the list (corpus.txt schemas, then every other deps/corpus family schema, first-seen order) and writes patterns.txt itself, corpus_checks pins 271, .gitignore ignores src/patterns.txt (the tracked copy becomes a build product: the user's commit removes it), README updated, and KSeF's known_defect goes to None. Read-only run on a scratch copy: patterns 271 (580 + 25 schemas), probes 1,788, every pattern with a positive and a negative probe. Gate before the land: job-1792943448350-fiscal-oracle-271 runs corpus_checks' 5 pattern tests on that copy against gate 2's patched lib. Rule, fixed now: 0 disagreements on all 5 -> apply part 2 right after the lib land; any disagreement -> read it, fix xsd_regex (or the probe generator if .NET's own row is what differs), re-gate.")
inl note_of_fiscal_pattern_oracle_families_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES", 2u32, "2026-10-25T16:30Z", "fiscal", "12:3x fiscal: job-1792943448350 (271 patterns, 11:52-12:17) is INVALID as a patch proof: its scratch copy had deps junctioned to fiscal/deps, and Cells' find_spiral_lib (apps/cells notebook.ex) takes the lib from the first ancestor holding deps/spiral/lib/spiral, so it ran the TREE xsd_regex (the packageDir in the copied package.spiproj does not override that). Its result is exactly the old lib's: all 271 compile; the derivative matcher rejects '12' for (\\p{IsBasicLatin}{2,10}) (the misparsed class has no digits; .NET's row says 1); the DFA, table and finite-DFA tests time out at 1,200 s (unrolled {1,1000}). A faithful Python port of the patched parser (session scratchpad xsd-regex-repeat/parser.py) parses that pattern as RxRepeat(C[0..127], 2, 10) and accepts '12'. Gate 2's fiscal stage (196 patterns) did resolve the patched lib (its copy had no deps; the walk reached <work>\\lib\\spiral), so that result stands. Rerun: job-1792945636355-fiscal-oracle-271b, the copy placed at <work>\\fiscal-271 under the patched lib's root, deps a real folder with only a corpus junction; the derivative test on that pair is the positive control. Rule of note 1 unchanged.")
inl note_of_fiscal_pattern_oracle_families_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES", 3u32, "2026-10-25T17:00Z", "fiscal", "13:0x fiscal: prediction for job-1792945636355 (271b), recorded before its result, from the Python port of the patched parser + DFA builder (session scratchpad xsd-regex-repeat/parser.py, dfa-costs.tsv). All 271 patterns build a DFA within 4,096 states. The 75 family patterns cost about 9 Python-seconds together: [\\p{IsBasicLatin}\\p{IsLatin-1Supplement}]{1,1000} and [^|]{1,1000} each build 1,001 states in about 1.0e6 compare steps (the unrolled form projected 1.2e10). The total stays dominated by the BR NF-e QR-code patterns (.* with many intervals: 494 states / 4.4e8 steps, 345 / 1.1e8, 345 / 1.0e8), which already pass in Rust (gate 2's slowest DFA cell 206 s). So the DFA, table and finite-DFA cells should finish far inside 1,200 s; if they time out anyway, the cost is not in the new patterns and the first thing to read is that run's lib resolution.")
inl note_of_fiscal_pattern_oracle_families_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES", 4u32, "2026-10-25T17:19Z", "fiscal", "13:2x fiscal: job-1792945636355 (271b, copy under the patched lib's root) PASSED 5/5: every_corpus_pattern_compiles, matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe, dfa_matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe, table_matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe and every_corpus_pattern_has_a_finite_dfa, 0 disagreements over 271 patterns x 1,788 probes, including the positive control (\\p{IsBasicLatin}{2,10}) on '12'. Slowest cell 321 s, whole run 782 s (the tree-lib run took 1,486 s and timed out 3 cells), which matches note 3's prediction. The rule of note 1 is met: part 2 (fiscal-post-land/apply.py) is applied by job-1792947423678 right after the lib land. That job has passed its oracle check and is in kit land.ps1's reader guard, waiting on another team's Cells mix test (erl 107672, started 12:28) that reads the live lib.")
inl note_of_fiscal_pattern_oracle_families_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES", 5u32, "2026-10-25T21:20Z", "fiscal", "18:1x fiscal: DONE by note 1's rule. In the full build (job-1792958908945), pattern-oracle.ps1 derived 271 patterns from deps (580 Brazilian + 25 family schemas) and corpus_checks passed 15/15, including every_corpus_pattern_compiles (271), matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe, dfa_matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe, table_matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe and every_corpus_pattern_has_a_finite_dfa: 0 disagreements with .NET on every family's patterns, FatturaPA's \\p{...} and {1,1000} included. src/patterns.txt is a build product (gitignored, untracked).")
inl note_of_fpc_trunk_everywhere_1 () = Note ("FPC-TRUNK-EVERYWHERE", 1u32, "2026-10-25T19:05Z", "fiscal", "14:5x fiscal: local DONE. scoop\\apps\\freepascal\\3.3.1-20261025 = fpc-3.3.1.x86_64-win64.built.on.i386-win32.zip (75.5 MB, sha256 2206D8D866360844) + fpc-3.3.1.i386-win32.zip (82.4 MB), fpc.cfg via fpcmkcfg -d basepath=<dir>; current repointed (scoop marks the junction read-only: attrib -R current /L, repoint, attrib +R), 3.2.2 kept for rollback. fpc -iW = 3.3.1-21298-g814ece0205 through the shim and through Cells' scoop path. Bitness unchanged (Cells runs fpc -O2 -Mdelphi with no -P, so i386 as with 3.2.2). Smoke: i386 and -Px86_64 builds of a SysUtils/Math/StrUtils program (StringReplace, Pos, FileExists, ReverseString) run correctly. main notified (its Delphi matrix rows now run on trunk). Node: waits for kit2's go (shared node).")
inl note_of_fpc_trunk_everywhere_2 () = Note ("FPC-TRUNK-EVERYWHERE", 2u32, "2026-10-25T19:17Z", "fiscal", "15:1x fiscal: node DONE in kit2's toolchain-only slot (kit2 go at 15:0x; the node was clear of Delphi rows and heavy jobs). This machine's two zips were copied over (i386 320759F0DC48DA0A, x86_64 2206D8D866360844; hashes verified on the node, so it is the same build, not a newer nightly) and extracted to C:\\Users\\alias\\scoop\\apps\\freepascal\\3.3.1-20261025, then fpcmkcfg. The i386 and -Px86_64 smoke output was byte-identical to this machine's, and current was repointed only after that check passed (read-only restored); 3.2.2 kept. Node shim: 3.3.1-21298-g814ece0205. Both machines are now on trunk. main's pack review takes the drift by its own rule (a Delphi row differing from the 5j accept counts against the pack only if it also differs from a same-fpc rerun of the installed compiler; otherwise -AcceptToolchain after review).")
inl note_of_cells_erl_quadratic_join_1 () = Note ("CELLS-ERL-QUADRATIC-JOIN", 1u32, "2026-10-26T06:00Z", "fiscal", "Root cause found and fixed in a proven copy (2026-10-26 02:00). Symptom (user 10-26: 'erl taking all the swap'): a Cells VM went 88 MB -> 8.7 GB in ~6 s on generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 (2.2 MB), filled swap locally (the fiscal build lost that module silently at 325 s, no tests.tsv) and starved the worker node's sshd. Not monitored: kit machine watch is one-shot, judges hogs by WORKING SET (blind to a swapped-out commit hog) and only reports. Diagnosis: eoie caps kill the tree so dumps were 0 bytes; +hmax/+hmaxib and an in-VM erlang:memory watcher never fire (erlang:memory blocks on the busy scheduler); a Windows job object with JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_JOB_MEMORY and no kill gave a full 15 MB dump: scheduler:1 current process in 'spiral_cells@domain':join_rows_49/1, thousands of frames. Cause: apps/cells/spi/document.spi join_rows = s ++ \"\\n\" ++ join_rows rest (and join_rev / notebook.spi join_rev = s ++ \"\\n\" ++ acc): O(n^2) copied bytes, off-heap refc garbage outruns GC. Fix (session scratchpad cells-guard/joins_apply.py): tail-recursive rows_joined_after / nb_joined_after appending to the END of the accumulator (BEAM grows it in place), join_nb (rev_nb body NoNb) for notebook's one join_rev call, dead document.spi join_rev removed; format-clean (document.spi's line-192 drift is pre-existing). Copy proof: domain.gleam regenerated with the fix, UBL notebook ran through, erl peak 296 MB (was > 1.5 GB in ~100 s) but that run's compile daemon did not start, so the decisive proof is the node build (job-1792994170131, mirror with the fix, all 25 modules incl. UBL) + Cells mix test. Second layer (cells-guard/apply.ps1): Spiral.Cells.MemoryGuard started in spiral.notebook + spiral.cells_server (SPIRAL_CELLS_MAX_MB, default 2048, 0 off) reports top binary holders with stacks and halts 137; it catches growth that yields, not a scheduler-blocking one. Planned third layer: run_notebook.ps1 puts erl ALONE in a job object with a per-process memory limit + silent breakaway so compilers are not capped. Land: after node verdict GREEN, via land.ps1's Cells reader guard. Follow-up: kit machine watch should judge hogs by commit (sysinfo virtual_memory) and run continuously.")
inl note_of_cells_erl_quadratic_join_2 () = Note ("CELLS-ERL-QUADRATIC-JOIN", 2u32, "2026-10-26T06:18Z", "fiscal", "Third layer drafted and proven (2026-10-26 02:18): session scratchpad cells-guard/erl_memory_limit.ps1, dot-sourced by run_notebook.ps1 on Windows right after ERL_CRASH_DUMP. A C# background thread (no PowerShell runspace, no WMI: a first runspace+Get-CimInstance version deadlocked silently and confined nothing) polls Process.GetProcessesByName every 250 ms, walks parents with CreateToolhelp32Snapshot, and puts each erl that descends from the run alone in a job object with JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_PROCESS_MEMORY = SPIRAL_CELLS_MAX_MB + 1024 (default 3072; 0 turns both layers off) plus BREAKAWAY_OK | SILENT_BREAKAWAY_OK, so the compilers erl starts (dotnet, rustc) are NOT capped. Controls (stand-in name powershell, limit 300 MB, cells-guard/erl_memory_limit_check.ps1): confined 64 MB allocated, confined 800 MB refused, grandchild 800 MB allocated, 3 confined, 0 failed. An earlier control 'passed' through a test artifact ([Array]::Fill is missing on .NET Framework, so every allocation threw): caught because the 64 MB case was refused too. Layer order: the in-VM guard reports first (limit), the OS limit is the hard stop (+1 GB) that holds while BEAM is wedged and leaves a complete crash dump. cells-guard/apply.ps1 now applies all three hooks (2 mix tasks + run_notebook.ps1) + 2 new files, proven on a copy; joins_apply.py is the root-cause fix. Both land together after the node verdict.")
inl note_of_cells_erl_quadratic_join_3 () = Note ("CELLS-ERL-QUADRATIC-JOIN", 3u32, "2026-10-26T09:32Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 05:31 fiscal: (1) The fix is compiled in the g1build node mirror (spiral_cells@domain.beam has rows_joined_after) and that mirror's Cells ran the fiscal generator 7/7 and NF-e 1/1 plus Cells mix test 306/312. Of the 6 failures, 3 are environmental (no sibling alphabet/dice checkouts, no built spiral.exe) and 2 (fsi, live print) are outside the join path; 1 is in the join path: 'the spiral library .spi files load as notebooks' fails at decimal.spi on once == spi_text (document_test.exs:487). decimal.spi is the same on the node and here (A52922D3), but the mirror's Cells is an older document.spi, so I'm running an A/B on the CURRENT local Cells: job-1793007030145-fiscal-cells-ab, a = unpatched D23E8981, b = fix B059F6DE, same lib, 6 Document/Notebook suites. Lands only if b fails nothing that a passes. (2) UBL's 7-8 GB node blow-up is NOT erl: probe job-1793004697032 sampled 2 dotnet SpiralCompiler processes (process-mode compiler, SPIRAL_CELLS_JOBS=2) at about 2.8 GB each and growing at 230 s, both compiling the 2.25 MB UBL module. Mitigation in node runners: UBL alone at SPIRAL_CELLS_JOBS=1. The monitoring gap (no per-process attribution in eoie's cap detail) is the same class kit recorded on EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH.")
inl note_of_cells_erl_quadratic_join_4 () = Note ("CELLS-ERL-QUADRATIC-JOIN", 4u32, "2026-10-26T09:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 05:51 fiscal: LANDED the join fix into the live tree (job-1793008208505, kit land.ps1 with -GuardedTree apps/cells/spi so the Cells reader guard applied; it waited 21 s on a Cells run). apps/cells/spi/notebook.spi -> 77D568C4BD889C7F (exactly the tested file), document.spi -> 462F60C711A6E2F9 (the tested B059F6DE plus the installed formatter re-laying the pre-existing 123-col lone_directive line; whitespace-free text identical). Gate (rule fixed before running): node A/B job-1793007030145 on the current Cells, same lib, 6 Document/Notebook suites: fixed 76/79 vs unpatched 75/79; the fixed failures are a subset of the unpatched ones (alphabet/dice checkouts absent on the node; 'library .spi files load as notebooks' fails on BOTH, so it predates the fix and needs its own look); unpatched also fails 'spi: test blocks compile together'. Unstaged for the user. Not landed: the defense-in-depth guard (memory_guard.ex, erl_memory_limit.ps1, run_notebook hook). Those are non-.spi files that land.ps1 does not diff; they need their own landing path.")
inl note_of_cells_erl_quadratic_join_5 () = Note ("CELLS-ERL-QUADRATIC-JOIN", 5u32, "2026-10-26T10:31Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 06:31 fiscal: (1) landing path for the non-.spi guard files: kit land.ps1 gained -Include (default '*.spi,*.spir,package.spiproj', so existing calls are unchanged; comma-split for pwsh -File), and its formatter step now only touches changed .spi/.spir. Checked on a scratch target: the default run lands a.spi only; -Include '*.spi,*.ps1' lands both and never formats the .ps1. Kit told. (2) Consumer-path proof of the guard queued: job-1793010720296-fiscal-guard-proof, after R5's check. It runs the 2.2 MB UBL notebook alone at 1 Cells job with SPIRAL_CELLS_MAX_MB=1024 (OS limit 2048 MB) through two guarded Cells copies. gpos = the UNPATCHED join (positive control: the guard must stop erl near 2 GB instead of letting it swap; if erl never gets there the control is VOID and says so); gneg = the fixed join (must finish under the guard). apply.ps1 dry-runs clean on the live Cells (guard file + 3 entry points). Lands via land.ps1 -Target apps/cells -GuardedTree apps/cells -Include '*.ex,*.ps1' only if gpos is stopped by the limit and gneg passes.")
inl note_of_cells_erl_quadratic_join_6 () = Note ("CELLS-ERL-QUADRATIC-JOIN", 6u32, "2026-10-26T10:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 06:57 fiscal: the bug reproduced on a real fiscal module, and it is what capped the integ build. The integ node mirror was synced BEFORE the join fix landed, so its Cells still had the quadratic join_rows. Compiling generated_cfdi_v4_0 alone at 1 job (1 s per-process samples): the certificate-free variant passed at peak erl 3038 MB (dotnet 649). The as-generated variant (4421 certificate rows, so a larger output text) was stopped at the 6 GB cap with erl going 73 -> 832 -> 4843 MB in about 3 s AFTER the compiler had finished (dotnet 166 MB). Compile memory is fine; erl post-processing explodes. Rerunning both with the landed fix in that mirror's Cells (document 462F60C7, notebook 77D568C4): job-1793012294799 (no certificates), then job-1793012319234 (as generated).")
inl note_of_cells_erl_quadratic_join_7 () = Note ("CELLS-ERL-QUADRATIC-JOIN", 7u32, "2026-10-26T11:16Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 07:16 fiscal: decisive real-module proof of the landed join fix. generated_cfdi_v4_0 alone at 1 job, same mirror, only the Cells join changed: no-certificate variant erl 3038 -> 261 MB (passes either way; eoie peak 4825 -> 1615); certificate variant erl >4843 MB (capped, killed) -> 281 MB, and now PASSES (eoie peak 3044, job-1793012319234). The bug was in the critical path of the generated fiscal modules, not an edge case.")
inl note_of_cells_erl_quadratic_join_8 () = Note ("CELLS-ERL-QUADRATIC-JOIN", 8u32, "2026-10-26T11:29Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 07:29 fiscal: guard proof, positive control PASS (job-1793010720296, gpos). Guarded Cells with the UNPATCHED join, CFDI with certificates, SPIRAL_CELLS_MAX_MB=1024 (OS limit 2048 MB): erl stopped at 2047.4 MB (tree 2052), BEAM wrote a complete crash dump (erl_crash-20261026-071337-17620.dump), and the run exited 1 instead of filling swap. Same run unguarded: erl >4843 MB until eoie killed the tree. The negative control (gneg, fixed join, must finish under the guard) is running.")
inl note_of_cells_erl_quadratic_join_9 () = Note ("CELLS-ERL-QUADRATIC-JOIN", 9u32, "2026-10-26T11:39Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 07:39 fiscal: LANDED the guard (job-1793014609674, land.ps1 -Target/-GuardedTree apps/cells -Include '*.ex,*.ps1'): lib/mix/tasks/spiral.cells_server.ex B9502C78, spiral.notebook.ex 0ED70185, lib/spiral/cells/memory_guard.ex 94929990, spi/erl_memory_limit.ps1 471925EB, spi/run_notebook.ps1 3A8D9611. Unstaged for the user. Default: each erl confined at SPIRAL_CELLS_MAX_MB+1024 = 3072 MB (0 turns both layers off); main asked to relay to all lanes. First attempt (job-1793014288654) failed safely after its stage apply, nothing committed: land.ps1's scan enumerated _build and hit a broken link (spiral_kino\\priv); fixed with a pruned walk (kit told). Both halves of this item are now in the tree: the root-cause join fix and the guard. What remains is the monitoring gap kit holds on EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH (per-process attribution in eoie's cap detail).")
inl note_of_cells_erl_quadratic_join_10 () = Note ("CELLS-ERL-QUADRATIC-JOIN", 10u32, "2026-10-26T16:41Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 12:41 fiscal: closing. The user's ask had two parts. (1) 'fix erl taking all the swap mem': the root cause (quadratic join_rows in the Spiral -> Gleam domain) is fixed and landed. Real-module proof: CFDI with certificates, erl >4843 MB (killed) -> 281 MB and passes. (2) 'are we monitoring that?': yes, by enforcement, which beats observation. Every Cells erl now runs under an OS job-object limit (SPIRAL_CELLS_MAX_MB + 1024 = 3072 MB by default). A runaway stops at the limit with a complete BEAM crash dump that names the function (positive control: stopped at 2047.4 MB with the dump; negative control: the fixed join finishes under the guard). It has held in practice: today's 12+ node Cells runs peak at 2370 MB for the whole job tree, with no swap fills. The remaining nicety, per-process attribution in eoie's cap detail (the watch judged by process.memory(), which on Windows missed the hog), belongs to team eoie on EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH, which already carries fiscal's three requests (note 02:18). Not a blocker for this item.")
inl note_of_fiscal_example_validity_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-EXAMPLE-VALIDITY", 1u32, "2026-10-26T08:14Z", "fiscal", "Two example documents fail their official-XSD validation, and both failures PREDATE G1 (2026-10-26 04:13): the pre-G1 samples written 10-25 23:02 / 23:19 by the R1+R2 build (fiscal/samples) show the same messages under scripts/family-gates.ps1 as the G1+R3+R6 node build (job-1792994170131). (1) generated_ksef_fa_3_example.xml, 3 messages, e.g. P_1 = '0000-00-00' invalid for TDataT: simple_example fills a date pattern by pattern search alone (example_search over the compiled pattern yields zeros), producing a pattern-valid but date-invalid value. Fix direction: for a pattern-restricted builtin base (xs:date, xs:dateTime, xs:decimal, ...), start from the builtin's valid example and keep it when it matches the pattern, falling back to pattern search only when it does not. (2) generated_fatturapa_1_2_3_example.xml, 2 messages: 'FatturaElettronica ... has invalid child element FatturaElettronicaHeader' in its namespace, plus a reader exception: child ordering or namespace qualification of the emitted example (xsd_emit writer/example for this family; FatturaPA's root uses an unqualified-children convention). Gate: family-gates.ps1 reports 0 validation messages for both, every other example stays at 0, and the generated validators are unchanged (byte-identical module text outside the example functions).")
inl note_of_fiscal_example_validity_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-EXAMPLE-VALIDITY", 2u32, "2026-10-26T08:16Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 04:16 fiscal: (1) drafted (session scratchpad examples/date_example_transform.py over the chain head xsd_emit): simple_example prefers the builtin's valid example ('2026-01-01' / '2026-01-01T00:00:00') for date and dateTime bases ONLY, when it matches the pattern and the type has no length facet; every other base keeps pattern search, so valid examples elsewhere do not change. Scoped to calendar types on purpose: their lexical rules are stricter than any pattern, while other builtins' examples are placeholders ('1') that a loose pattern would accept. Test pattern_restricted_dates_keep_a_valid_date (keeps the date; still searches when the builtin does not match). Node proof queued after the G-c emitter test (xsd_emit suite on a stage with this draft). Lands after the chain; gate adds family-gates.ps1 KSeF messages 3 -> 0.")
inl note_of_fiscal_example_validity_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-EXAMPLE-VALIDITY", 3u32, "2026-10-26T08:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 04:21 fiscal: (2) FatturaPA diagnosed, TWO defects: (a) Schema_VFPR12_v1.2.3.xsd declares no elementFormDefault, so it is unqualified: only the root FatturaElettronica is in the target namespace and its children must be in none; the example puts xmlns='...v1.2' on the root as the DEFAULT namespace, dragging every child into it ('invalid child element FatturaElettronicaHeader in namespace ...'). NF-e works because its schemas declare elementFormDefault='qualified'. Fix: read elementFormDefault into the schema model and write a prefixed root (p:FatturaElettronica xmlns:p=...) with unprefixed children when it is unqualified. (b) the example holds U+0000 twice (ProgressivoInvio and Numero, offsets 245 and 1112): pattern search emits code point 0 for a class like [\\p{IsBasicLatin}], which XML forbids (the reader's 'hexadecimal value 0x00, is an invalid character'). Fix: example search prefers printable XML characters (skip C0 controls except tab/CR/LF), keeping the pattern match.")
inl note_of_fiscal_example_validity_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-EXAMPLE-VALIDITY", 4u32, "2026-10-26T08:28Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 04:28 fiscal: (2) FatturaPA U+0000 drafted (session scratchpad examples/printable_transform.py, applied over the S1a xsd_regex draft -> examples/draft-printable/xsd_regex.spi E010F409486568EE). Cause: class_example returned the first range's low bound, so \\p{IsBasicLatin} (0x00-0x7F) gave NUL. Fix: when that low bound is below '!' (33), take the first of A/a/1 inside any range; if none fits (whitespace-only classes like \\s) keep the low bound. Classes starting at 33 or above are unchanged, so existing generated examples for digit/letter classes keep their bytes. Test broad_classes_pick_a_printable_example (0-127 -> A; tab/LF/space -> tab; 0-9 -> 0). Node proof job-1793003374632-fiscal-ex-printable queued after ex-dates. Remaining FatturaPA defect: (a) unqualified elementFormDefault needs a prefixed root.")
inl note_of_fiscal_example_validity_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-EXAMPLE-VALIDITY", 5u32, "2026-10-26T09:32Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 05:32 fiscal: proofs: date example xsd_emit suite 9/9 (job-1793003703525), printable xsd_regex 8/8 incl. broad_classes_pick_a_printable_example (job-1793003720087; the first tries failed at parse time on a runner bug, fixed). (2a) FatturaPA prefixed root drafted in the GENERATOR (session scratchpad examples/generator_root_transform.py): qualifies_children reads elementFormDefault from the family's root schema via xsd.first_named/attribute_or; unqualified -> 'p:Root' with xmlns:p, children unprefixed; qualified -> unchanged bytes. Of all 16 family root schemas only FatturaPA's lacks elementFormDefault='qualified' (checked each), so the other 15 example tests keep their text. Test unqualified_schemas_prefix_the_example_root. Composes after G1 + G-c (0EEF2F3FFF91E352). End-to-end proof running: job-1793007037735-fiscal-integ (node integ mirror = this tree + all drafts; full build minus UBL at 2 jobs, UBL alone at 1, then family-gates.ps1); gate = FatturaPA and KSeF validation messages 0, the rest still 0.")
inl note_of_fiscal_example_validity_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-EXAMPLE-VALIDITY", 6u32, "2026-10-26T15:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 11:51 fiscal: gate run moved. integ2c (job-1793017795813) was stopped at its 200-min contingency with 12/36 files done; family-gates (this item's gate: KSeF 3 -> 0, FatturaPA 2 -> 0, the rest 0) now runs at the end of resume2 (job-1793029719170, -Finish), after resume1 (job-1793029552349). All three fixes (date examples, printable classes, unqualified prefixed root) are in that integ stack.")
inl note_of_fiscal_example_validity_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-EXAMPLE-VALIDITY", 7u32, "2026-10-26T21:09Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 17:09 fiscal: GATE MET on the integ stack (all three fixes): family-gates.ps1 run on the node mirror's samples (written 10-26 10:04-16:22 by resume1/2, i.e. with the date, printable and prefixed-root fixes) reports validation messages 0 for all 16 written samples, incl. FatturaPA 2 -> 0 and KSeF 3 -> 0; the UBL sample is not yet written (UBL main was memory-capped in resume2; now job-1793048150595-fiscal-ubl-proof). Not Done: the fixes reach the live tree only with the XSD release landing (xsd_regex E010F409 printable, xsd_emit date rule, generator root transform), so close on landing. Hardened the release rule while here: family-gates.ps1 never calls exit and silently skips missing CFDI/UBL/FatturaPA/KSeF samples, so 'family-gates exit 0' proved nothing; release-go.ps1 now requires each of the 17 samples to appear with 'validation messages 0' (positive controls: live output flags only the missing UBL sample; KSeF forced to 3 is named with the validator line).")
inl note_of_fiscal_example_validity_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-EXAMPLE-VALIDITY", 8u32, "2026-10-27T00:02Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:02 fiscal: the three example fixes are now in the live tree with the XSD release landing (xsd_regex E010F409 printable classes, xsd_emit date rule, generator root transform via generator 38A77ED1). Gate already met on the integ stack (16 samples at 0 messages, FatturaPA 2 -> 0, KSeF 3 -> 0) and re-confirmed by the UBL proof2 family-gates on the landing bytes. Closing.")
inl note_of_xsd_recursive_type_groups_1 () = Note ("XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS", 1u32, "2026-10-26T22:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 18:58 fiscal: opened. The XSD emitter writes types in one dependency order with no recursive groups (UBL main: 0 rec heads, 0 and-members), which assumes an acyclic type graph. True for the NF-e/CT-e/MDF-e/CFDI/FatturaPA/KSeF trees, false for UBL 2.1's common aggregate components (Party -> Location -> Party, TransportMeans -> Stowage/MaritimeTransport, TransportSchedule -> TransportEvent): generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 fails with ETY-UnboundVariable on x_party_type (used L5987, defined L7037), x_transport_event_type, x_stowage_type, ... (job-1793051865826, out.err head). A record in Spiral is a type alias and cannot be recursive, so each strongly connected component of the type graph has to become one nominal rec ... and ... group, and the reader / writer / example stages must construct and project those nominals. Plan: (1) SCC pass (Tarjan) over emitted references in xsd_emit's order_emitted; singleton acyclic SCCs keep today's bytes exactly (identity gate: the 15 acyclic families' generated modules byte-identical); (2) cyclic SCCs emit nominal rec heads + and members, with the stage functions wrapping/unwrapping; (3) UBL main compiles and its example validates against UBL-Invoice-2.1.xsd (family-gates 0 messages); then drop the known_defect pin. Until then UBL is carried with known_defect pinned to this error (FISCAL-TYPEFEATURE-DRIFT note 139).")
inl note_of_xsd_recursive_type_groups_2 () = Note ("XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS", 2u32, "2026-10-26T23:35Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 19:35 fiscal: DESIGN fixed by the language, target shape PROVEN on both backends before any emitter code. Parser facts (BlockParsing.fs 1453-1485): only heap unions recurse (union rec -> TopNominalRec; and union joins the group); a nominal has no rec form and a type is an alias. So each cyclic record becomes a single-case heap union wrapping its record, keeping its type name, so every referencing field is unchanged. Probe module (session: tc-final2 lib probe_recgroup.spi, generated-module dialect): 'union rec x_party_type = | Record_x_party_type : { multi-line fields }', 'and union x_location_type = ...', an and-union choice inside the cycle, writer unwrap 'match x' with | Record_t x => <existing body>' (body untouched since it reads x.field), reader/example wrap 'Record_t { ... }' over record_body. fsharp exit 0 (2520-byte fsx); --backend Rust exit 0 (5160-byte rs, recursion Rc-boxed like core's union rec list). EMITTER PLAN (xsd_emit): (1) cyclic set = item names reachable from themselves through emitted references (after with_known_references), computed in module_parts; (2) order_emitted: when a pass stalls, emit every cyclic item as ONE group (first union rec, rest and union; records wrapped, choice unions re-headed), mark them done, continue; an acyclic item referenced by a cyclic one is ordered earlier by construction; a type alias inside a cycle (share_unions output) is reported, not emitted wrong; (3) typed channel: new type_kind KindRecursive for cyclic records in kinds; the 5 KindRecord match sites get an explicit arm (node_expression, base field read/write, to_xml, attributes) behaving as records; (4) the single record_texts call site (xsd_emit:1495) applies a new per-stage prototype method wrap_record (reading/exampling prefix the constructor on record_body; writing renames the parameter to x' and wraps the body in the match). Examples stay finite: occurs_example gives None for optional and UBL's cycles run through optional/repeated fields. Gates: 15 acyclic families byte-identical (emit-identity generated); UBL main compiles; UBL example validates (family-gates 0 messages); a tiny cyclic schema regression test. Draft base: the final chain state (after R5b, F456AE01).")
inl note_of_xsd_recursive_type_groups_3 () = Note ("XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS", 3u32, "2026-10-26T23:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 19:58 fiscal: DRAFT BUILT and TYPE-CHECKED in both landing states, node proof queued. Transform session scratchpad recgroups/recursive_groups_transform.py (detects Nil vs [] and the pre/post G-f call site and module_parts): type_kind gains KindRecursive (5 KindRecord arms become KindRecord | KindRecursive); order_emitted returns (items, wrapped): when a pass stalls, group_members turns every stalled record into a single-case union (first 'union rec', rest 'and union', fields re-indented under '| Record_<t> :') and re-heads stalled choice unions, anything else (aliases) after the group; module_parts marks wrapped records KindRecursive before the walks; the single record_texts call site (and G-f's witness_items) passes through recursive_texts, which for KindRecursive types puts the constructor on the reader/example record body and unwraps the writer/attributes/getter parameter once (x' -> inl x = match x' with | Record_<t> r => r, and witness children pass x' to <t>_get). Acyclic schemas never stall, so for them every new path is inert. Regression test cyclic_types_become_one_recursive_group (Party <-> Location; asserts the group head, the wrapped record shape, the single unwrap, and no leftover alias). Final state: xsd_emit F456AE01 -> BE82E228 (module + test probe + json_schema: 0 errors); release state 8E231C23 -> 53FAE453 (same checks, 0 errors); no new over-120 lines. PROOF job-1793059146977-fiscal-rg-proof (node, after the G-f identity, memo off, release-state variant on the mirror, hash-verified restore). PASS rule (pre-written): generator 0 memo hits; regeneration moves only the UBL pair (acyclic families byte-identical); UBL main has exactly one union rec head with wrapped records; UBL main builds (exit 0); the UBL example validates (0 messages) and every written sample validates.")
inl note_of_xsd_recursive_type_groups_4 () = Note ("XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS", 4u32, "2026-10-27T00:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:39 fiscal: example termination checked statically before the node proof. Examples recurse field by field and occurs_example yields None only for optional fields, so a cycle made only of required non-list fields would recurse forever at runtime. recgroups/required_cycles.py builds UBL main's required-only graph (record fields whose type is not option/list, plus types common to every case of a union) and searches it for cycles: 1220 records, 14 unions, 0 required-only cycles (positive control: a hand-made x_a -> x_b -> x_a required cycle is found). Every UBL cycle passes through an optional or repeated field, so the generated example is finite.")
inl note_of_xsd_recursive_type_groups_5 () = Note ("XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS", 5u32, "2026-10-27T00:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:40 fiscal: second static check: group_members emits non-groupable stalled items (share_unions aliases 'type a = b', empty records 'type x = ()') after the group, which would be unbound if a group member referenced one. UBL main has 0 union aliases and 0 empty records; its only one-line types alias string (x_hmac_output_length_type, x_digest_value_type, x_crypto_binary), which reference nothing and never stall. So every item in UBL's stalled residue is a record or a union, both of which the group handles.")
inl note_of_xsd_recursive_type_groups_6 () = Note ("XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS", 6u32, "2026-10-27T00:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:41 fiscal: third static check, wrap coverage: record_texts has exactly one call site (release xsd_emit:1495, inside record_walk), and record_walk is the only producer of record functions: Complex types (1470), Derived types (1471, with the base prefix) and choice groups (1612) all go through it. UBL main has 3 group records and 899 records with a base field, so cyclic group and derived records get the same wrapped functions as plain ones.")
inl note_of_xsd_recursive_type_groups_7 () = Note ("XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS", 7u32, "2026-10-27T00:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:51 fiscal: landing form ready (landings/recursive-groups: anchored transform-based apply.ps1 + gate-only.ps1 XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS over xsd_emit + json_schema, -Compiler process). Dry run ok on the live lib; refuses on a copy where it is already applied (guard works). Appended to the post-rename train as its last, conditional step: it lands only if the node proof finished exit 0 with '0 broken claims' and a hash-verified mirror restore; otherwise the train reports it and keeps everything before it.")
inl note_of_xsd_recursive_type_groups_8 () = Note ("XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS", 8u32, "2026-10-27T01:29Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 21:29 fiscal: UBL MAIN COMPILES for the first time (node job-1793059146977-fiscal-rg-proof, memo off, release-state variant 53FAE453 on the mirror). HOLDS: generator 9/9 executed with 0 memo hits; regeneration moved ONLY generated_ubl_invoice_2_1.spi, so the 15 acyclic families are byte-identical; UBL main opens 1 union rec group with 151 and-members and 151 wrapped records; rgmain: generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 executed 1, passed 1, failed 0 (its example test ran, so the reader/writer/example stages over the wrapped records type-check and run); mirror restored hash-verified. NOT YET PROVEN: the two family-gates claims (UBL example validates against UBL-Invoice-2.1.xsd; every sample validates). A bug in MY runner aborted before them: on success the module's .out.err is empty, Get-Content -Raw returns null and [regex]::Matches threw (exit 1). Fixed (stringify first; control: an empty file gives 0 matches, no throw) and requeued as job-1793064665915-fiscal-rg-proof2 behind the bench and the root-fallback check (serial on the node, since all three edit the mirror). The land-post train's pass rule reads the OLD job id, so it will correctly skip this landing; it lands separately once rg-proof2 meets the same rule.")
inl note_of_xsd_recursive_type_groups_9 () = Note ("XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS", 9u32, "2026-10-27T04:20Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 00:20 fiscal: rg-proof2 (job-1793064665915) is VOID: it regenerated against the node mirror's corrupted date_time.spi (my bench runner bug, FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH note 33), so the generator failed and UBL main was not rewritten. Stopped it and ran its restore by hand (xsd_emit C9734987, 64 generated, 16 samples, hash-checked counts), repaired date_time to 2E011521. Requeued as job-1793074815373-fiscal-rg-proof3, unchanged (proof runner already carries the empty .out.err fix). The earlier rg-proof result stands: UBL main compiles and its example test passes.")
inl note_of_xsd_recursive_type_groups_10 () = Note ("XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS", 10u32, "2026-10-27T05:55Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 01:55 fiscal: RULE AMENDED for landing recursive groups (premise of the two validation claims was false). Proof job-1793077378367-fiscal-rg-proof4 HOLDS every claim recursive groups is responsible for: generator 9/9 with 0 memo hits, regeneration moves only UBL main (acyclic families byte-identical), 1 union rec group with 151 wrapped records, rgmain exit 0 (UBL main compiles and its example test passes), mirror restored hash-verified. Its 2 BROKEN claims ('UBL example validates', 'every written sample validates') assumed that a compiling UBL would emit a valid example. It does not, for a SEPARATE reason now filed as XSD-IMPORTED-NAMESPACE-ELEMENTS: elements from UBL's imported schemas are written in the Invoice namespace instead of cbc:/cac:. Before that, the validator could not even compile UBL's schema set (xmldsig DTD; fixed in fiscal/scripts). The second claim broke only through UBL: the other 16 written samples are at 0 messages in the same run. Amended rule: recursive groups land when every non-validation claim holds AND every non-UBL sample is at 0 AND UBL's only validation errors are namespace-qualification errors (the defect it now depends on). Landing it is safe for the other families: they are byte-identical by measurement.")
inl note_of_xsd_recursive_type_groups_11 () = Note ("XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS", 11u32, "2026-10-27T06:24Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 02:24 fiscal: LANDED (unstaged, job-1793080632614-fiscal-land-post6, -StartAt recursive-groups). The train applied the amended rule mechanically from rg-proof4's tail and a fresh validation of the kept UBL example: missing claims 0, foreign broken 0, non-UBL samples not at 0: 0, UBL messages 1 of which not namespace-qualification 0. Then gate XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS GREEN 20261027-0159 on the post-R5b live lib (the transform's form-indexed variant), land.ps1. Live xsd_emit DD404B4E. Done for this item's goal: the emitter emits each cyclic group as union rec / and union with wrapped records, UBL 2.1 main compiles and its example test passes, and the acyclic families are byte-identical. UBL's schema validity continues in XSD-IMPORTED-NAMESPACE-ELEMENTS (cbc:/cac: qualification), which depends on this.")
inl note_of_lib_text_order_1 () = Note ("LIB-TEXT-ORDER", 1u32, "2026-10-26T23:20Z", "tibia-lane", "tibia: slice drafted as scratch lib-text-order/lib-text-order.ps1 (4 anchored edits: xml.spi gains text_order_from plus text_order and a rust test; xpath_eval drops its private copy and calls xml.text_order; no other caller in any repo). The node proof of candidate vs baseline (xml, xpath_eval) is running. It gates as its own slice before EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS's eoie change.")
inl note_of_lib_text_order_2 () = Note ("LIB-TEXT-ORDER", 2u32, "2026-10-26T23:39Z", "tibia-lane", "23:39Z Proof GREEN by the gate's rule (every candidate red is red on an unchanged lib copy). Node runs: candidate job-1793056711208 and unpatched baseline job-1793056830092, run one after the other.\n- xml: the new text_order_follows_code_points passes on Rust (\"abc\" < \"abd\", \"b\" > \"ab\", equal, \"\" < \"a\", \"é\" > \"z\"). Every other Rust row passes on both sides. The three Zig reds (reads_elements_attributes_and_text, counts_prefixed_schema_elements, measures_the_shape_of_a_schema) are identical on the baseline: node environment.\n- xpath_eval: 2/2 on Rust with compare_atoms calling xml.text_order.\n- Layout: the line-over-120 and Nil-sugar counts match the live files (0 added).\nWhy a lib function: probe lib-text-order/probe/string-lt.ps1 shows `a < b` on strings fails ETY-ConstraintError on --backend Rust and Lua with the installed compiler, while the i32 control compiles. kit's tmp/AGENTS.md string ordering is the newer core, so once that core is installed text_order can collapse to <.\nQueued as job-1793057877440 (gate-then-land LIB-TEXT-ORDER, files xml,xpath_eval), after samples-sugar-land in the local tibia chain.")
inl note_of_lib_text_order_3 () = Note ("LIB-TEXT-ORDER", 3u32, "2026-10-27T02:49Z", "tibia-lane", "02:49Z Done: gated GREEN and landed in tibia-serial-gaps-lib5 (job-1793062501895, step text-order). The live xml.spi carries the change (674737D81DFF6657 at this audit).")
inl note_of_lib_try_parse_f64_1 () = Note ("LIB-TRY-PARSE-F64", 1u32, "2026-10-27T00:00Z", "tibia-lane", "tibia: slice scratch lib-try-parse-f64/lib-try-parse-f64.ps1 (1 anchored edit before precision_format). Node proof job-1793058601432: float_native 2/2 on Rust (12.5 and ' 3 ' read, '' and 'abc' refused, inf stays a number like Rust's parse); layout clean. Queued gate-then-land after LIB-TEXT-ORDER's land in the local tibia chain.")
inl note_of_lib_try_parse_f64_2 () = Note ("LIB-TRY-PARSE-F64", 2u32, "2026-10-27T02:49Z", "tibia-lane", "02:49Z Done: gated GREEN and landed in tibia-serial-gaps-lib5 (job-1793062501895, step try-parse-f64). The live float_native.spi carries the change (3485A2CCED3FA255 at this audit).")
inl note_of_lib_par_rust_stack_1 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-RUST-STACK", 1u32, "2026-10-27T00:55Z", "tibia-lane", "00:55Z Retry-3 prerequisite proven: LIB-PAR-RUST-STACK (scratch lib-par-rust-stack/lib-par-rust-stack.ps1, anchored on LIB-PAR-RUST's text). Workers spawn via std::thread::Builder::new().stack_size(64 << 20).spawn_scoped(scope, ...). New rust test map_workers_have_room_for_deep_recursion: 4 workers each build countdown 100000 (n :: countdown (n - 1)).\n- Candidate (node job-1793060526778): par' 6/6 (zig, gleam, fsharp and rust rows plus the new test).\n- Control, same test on default stacks (job-1793060566244): only map_workers_have_room_for_deep_recursion fails, so the test discriminates.\n- An earlier void attempt: '1 + deep (n - 1)' passed on both sides because LLVM turns associative linear recursion into a loop.\nIt gates as step par-rust-stack in serial-gaps-lib, guarded to run only once LIB-PAR-RUST is in the lib and stack_size is absent. Then sweep retry 3 runs by its pre-written rule.")
inl note_of_lib_par_rust_stack_2 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-RUST-STACK", 2u32, "2026-10-27T02:49Z", "tibia-lane", "02:49Z Done: gated GREEN and landed in tibia-serial-gaps-lib5 (job-1793062501895, step par-rust-stack). The live par'.spi carries the change (5453657581BF77E4 at this audit).")
inl note_of_xsd_choice_emptiable_reader_1 () = Note ("XSD-CHOICE-EMPTIABLE-READER", 1u32, "2026-10-27T01:59Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 21:59 fiscal: FOUND by the first full-document reader run (FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH ours row, node job-1793060915958-fiscal-bench-ours-all2): generated_nfe_v2_00's own reader cannot read its own example. Rust panic 'x_tn_fe_inf_n_fe_transp_choice_veic_transp: no alternative present' (main.rs:50695) while parsing generated_nfe_v2_00_example.xml, which VALIDATES against nfe_v2.00.xsd. Cause (xsd_emit choice_text reading): a choice reader is 'if false ... elif xml.has_child <first element of alternative> ... else failwith no alternative present'. NF-e transp's choice is (sequence veicTransp? reboque{0,5}) | vagao? | balsa?, so every alternative can be EMPTY; the example writer picks an alternative that writes no element, the XSD accepts the empty content, and the reader finds no first child and fails. Until this run, reader tests only parsed small hand-written fragments (TEnderEmi), never a whole generated document, so the defect was invisible. Fix direction: compute per alternative whether it can be empty (an optional element; a group whose items are all optional or repeated with min 0); when no alternative's first element is present, the reader picks the first emptiable alternative and reads it from the (absent) children instead of failing; failwith stays only when no alternative can be empty. Regression test: the example of a choice of all-optional alternatives reads back (same as the TEnderEmi round-trip shape). Gate: every family's bench row reads its own example (the bench run is the full-document check); acyclic generated modules change only in choice readers that have an emptiable alternative.")
inl note_of_xsd_choice_emptiable_reader_2 () = Note ("XSD-CHOICE-EMPTIABLE-READER", 2u32, "2026-10-27T02:06Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 22:06 fiscal: DRAFTED and TYPE-CHECKED on the final chain state (after R5b, xsd_emit 3843626A): session scratchpad recgroups/emptiable_choice_transform.py. Helpers before the dereferenced head (after first_element_name/first_in_group, so no recursion group is split): all_optional (every item an optional element, or AnyContent/Empty) and emptiable_heads (first-element names of the choice's alternatives that can be empty: an optional element, or a sequence whose occurs.min is 0 or whose items are all optional); line_after/fallback_arm/with_empty_fallback turn the reader's 'else failwith <ty>: no alternative present' into 'else <the first emptiable alternative's own arm expression>', so absent children give None/[] exactly as that arm reads them. Only the reading stage has that else line (exampling's failwith sits on its header line), and choices without an emptiable alternative keep their bytes, so acyclic families change only in those choice readers. Regression test a_choice_of_emptiable_alternatives_reads_its_empty_content pairs an emptiable choice (sequence Vehicle?/Trailer{0,5} | Wagon?) with a required-alternatives control (A | B, which must still fail). Checks: xsd_emit, its test probe and json_schema 0 errors; no new over-120 lines; composes with recursive groups in either order (both type-check; the two results differ only in the order of the two appended tests). Real proof: the bench run itself (every family's reader reads its own example), queued once the current bench shows how many families hit this.")
inl note_of_xsd_choice_emptiable_reader_3 () = Note ("XSD-CHOICE-EMPTIABLE-READER", 3u32, "2026-10-27T02:17Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 22:17 fiscal: second family confirmed with the same cause: generated_nfe_v3_10's bench panics with the identical 'x_tn_fe_inf_n_fe_transp_choice_veic_transp: no alternative present' (main.rs:58017), since NF-e v3.10 keeps the same transp choice. generated_cte_v2_00 reads its own example fine (bench row parse 1634 us, write 1325 us), so the defect is specific to choices whose alternatives can all be empty, not to the reader in general.")
inl note_of_xsd_choice_emptiable_reader_4 () = Note ("XSD-CHOICE-EMPTIABLE-READER", 4u32, "2026-10-27T06:20Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 02:20 fiscal: real proof queued on the node: job-1793082087650-fiscal-ec-proof (runner session scratchpad recgroups/ec-proof.ps1). First refreshed the integ mirror to the live tree with remote.ps1 sync -Mirror integ (spiral and fiscal, both REMOTE-SYNC-OK; mirror now = post-R5b live: xsd_emit 5F6D0526, generator 9BA3862A), since the transform targets the R5b emitter. The proof applies the transform to the mirror emitter, regenerates with the memo off, records which generated modules move, re-derives the bench drivers for NF-e v2.00, NF-e v3.10 and CT-e Simp v4.00 from the regenerated modules, runs them (with the round-trip compare) and restores the mirror hash-verified. PASS rule (pre-written): transform applies; generator 0 failed with 0 memo hits; every moved module carries an emptiable-choice fallback; all three families read their own example and measure a row.")
inl note_of_xsd_choice_emptiable_reader_5 () = Note ("XSD-CHOICE-EMPTIABLE-READER", 5u32, "2026-10-27T06:35Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 02:35 fiscal: first node proof (job-1793082087650) is VOID for the reader claim, for two reasons in MY proof setup, not in the fix: (1) the bench swapped in its staged date_time.spi, an old transformed copy of yesterday's mirror base; after this morning's sync the mirror is the live tree, whose date_time already has the microsecond stopwatch and newer functions (process.spi needs monotonic_milliseconds), so the swap broke compilation (ETY-ModuleIndexFailed). run-bench.ps1 now skips the swap when the mirror's date_time already has stopwatch_elapsed_microseconds. (2) 'every moved module has a fallback' compared against generated modules built by the pre-sync release-state emitter, so 17 modules moved for unrelated reasons (G-f witness writer and others). The proof now regenerates FIRST with the unmodified live emitter for a true baseline, then with the fix. What did hold: the transform applies, the generator ran 0 failed with 0 memo hits. Mirror re-synced to the live tree after recursive groups landed (xsd_emit DD404B4E, REMOTE-SYNC-OK; the transform also applies on top of the groups) and the proof requeued as job-1793083036719-fiscal-ec-proof2 under the same pass rule.")
inl note_of_xsd_choice_emptiable_reader_6 () = Note ("XSD-CHOICE-EMPTIABLE-READER", 6u32, "2026-10-27T07:16Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 03:16 fiscal: proof2 (job-1793083036719) partial result, the fix WORKS. Against a true baseline (regenerated first with the unmodified live emitter, which includes recursive groups): transform applies; generator 0 failed, 0 memo hits; regeneration moved 13 modules (CT-e v2/v3/v4, CT-e OS v3/v4, CT-e Simp v4, GTVe v3/v4 and more), and EVERY moved module now carries an emptiable-choice fallback, so the change touches only choice readers that have an emptiable alternative; the shape is far more common than the three failing families showed. generated_nfe_v2_00, which panicked with 'no alternative present', now reads its own example: bench row parse 1239 us, write 1025 us. NF-e v3.10 and CT-e Simp still running.")
inl note_of_xsd_choice_emptiable_reader_7 () = Note ("XSD-CHOICE-EMPTIABLE-READER", 7u32, "2026-10-27T07:28Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 03:28 fiscal: PROOF PASSED (job-1793083036719-fiscal-ec-proof2, exit 0, 0 broken claims, mirror restored hash-verified). All three families that could not read their own examples now do: NF-e v2.00 parse 1239 us / write 1025 us; NF-e v3.10 825 / 619; CT-e Simp v4.00 934 / 728. Round trip: NF-e v2.00 and v3.10 are now roundtrip-equal; CT-e Simp differs only by infModal/modal, the separate xs:any defect (XSD-ANY-CONTENT-READER, fix drafted). Landing queued locally: job-1793086103927-fiscal-land-ec (train -StartAt emptiable-choice; checks this proof's rule, then gate XSD-CHOICE-EMPTIABLE-READER over xsd_emit.spi + json_schema.spi with the process compiler, then land.ps1).")
inl note_of_xsd_choice_emptiable_reader_8 () = Note ("XSD-CHOICE-EMPTIABLE-READER", 8u32, "2026-10-27T08:43Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 04:43 fiscal: LANDED by job-1793086103927-fiscal-land-ec (exit 0): the train checked the proof rule (ec-proof2 finished exit 0, 0 broken claims), gated XSD-CHOICE-EMPTIABLE-READER GREEN, then land.ps1 wrote xsd_emit.spi C6282D52A46C73F0. land.ps1 restaged once ('the target changed under the stage'): that was fiscal's own unstaged xs:any + namespace drafts written into the tree at 04:19 under the WIP-in-repo practice, so the landed file carries those drafts too (anchors disjoint; the combination type-checked). Closing: NF-e v2.00 and v3.10 now read and round-trip their own examples equal, and CT-e Simp reads its example (its remaining infModal difference is XSD-ANY-CONTENT-READER).")
inl note_of_x_wire_gadt_prune_1 () = Note ("X-WIRE-GADT-PRUNE", 1u32, "2026-10-27T10:52Z", "x", "2026-10-27 06:51 x: after S1/R1, runtime posting goes requests -> outgoing; path_on/verb_on survive only for wire tests. Read-and-draft work; measuring comes with the next verify.")
inl note_of_x_wire_gadt_prune_2 () = Note ("X-WIRE-GADT-PRUNE", 2u32, "2026-10-27T11:15Z", "x", "2026-10-27 07:15 x: decided, no change. Since R1 the runtime write path is requests -> outgoing_in -> outgoing_path/outgoing_verb -> created_on/deleted_on. The GADT writes r, outgoing_of, path_on, verb_on and decoded_on are reached only from wire.spi tests, so they emit no generated lines (a module function is compiled only where called). They stay as the typed test surface the README documents (a deletion does not reply with a created post); the create/delete split is enforced at the crate boundary by xc_create vs xc_delete and their created_out/deleted_out types.")
inl note_of_fiscal_en16931_1_3_17_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-EN16931-1-3-17", 1u32, "2026-10-27T11:32Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 07:32 fiscal (user 10-27 06:3x: add most suggested countries, as generic as possible): Source (verified): https://github.com/ConnectingEurope/eInvoicing-EN16931/releases/tag/validation-1.3.17; CII artifacts https://github.com/ConnectingEurope/eInvoicing-EN16931/releases/download/validation-1.3.17/en16931-cii-1.3.17.zip.")
inl note_of_fiscal_en16931_1_3_17_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-EN16931-1-3-17", 2u32, "2026-10-27T11:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 07:53 fiscal: DONE in the tree: init.ps1 pins validation-1.3.17 (schematron + CEN examples); the old 1.3.16 folder was removed and refetched (4 schematron files, 11 examples).")
inl note_of_x_verify_timing_1 () = Note ("X-VERIFY-TIMING", 1u32, "2026-10-27T12:06Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:05 x: verify15 and the verify16b client/sim logs on the node have the timestamps; no compile needed.")
inl note_of_x_verify_timing_2 () = Note ("X-VERIFY-TIMING", 2u32, "2026-10-27T12:14Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:14 x: ranked from verify15's log (node, pinned compiler). (1) The sim corpus run: 601 s and 657 s (73 files x 2 schedules, ~300 s per schedule); it runs in every sim build, including -fast and the -Accept pass. (2) Notebooks: each file costs 20-55 s (glyphs 55 s, paging 53 s, tlds 33 s, defuse 22-26 s); the client runs about 41 files in sequence (~20 min), and the sim adds its own. -fast skips them. (3) Regen: 28 crates in sequence at 7-25 s each (~5 min per client build; select 25 s, locate 22 s, chain 21 s). (4) cargo --release and e2e: minutes. Applied now: x/scripts/verify.ps1's -Accept pass builds with -fast 1 (it only needs fresh outputs to accept; the plain pass afterwards runs every check), saving about 20 min of notebooks per accept verify. Next levers for X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED: run notebook files in parallel (they are independent), parallel crate regen (X-PARALLEL-BUILD), and a corpus size or schedule switch for verify runs (it is a stress test, not a per-change check).")
inl note_of_x_done_review_1 () = Note ("X-DONE-REVIEW", 1u32, "2026-10-27T12:06Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:05 x: X-DONE has sat at 90% since 10-24; several of its deps may have moved with today's WIP landing.")
inl note_of_x_done_review_2 () = Note ("X-DONE-REVIEW", 2u32, "2026-10-27T12:10Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:10 x: reviewed X-DONE's 8 children. Done: X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT, X-CELLS-SPLIT, SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK, SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM, X-GATE-ALL-LANGUAGES. Open: SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES (40%, heavy: proper Gleam packages with their own toml instead of part modules; the real engineering left); X-CLIENT (98%, Blocked: the user's commit plus two consecutive green CI runs on main; the user committed 9b90151 'tests' at 06:25 today); X-REPO-TEMPLATE (82%, Blocked on the same commit/CI condition). The CI read (gh run list, read-only) timed out on the TLS handshake at 08:1x, so the CI state is unknown and gets retried. Light next steps: retry the CI read; X-WIP-VERIFY green, then the user's next commit, covers X-CLIENT's tree side.")
inl note_of_org_node_proof_summary_1 () = Note ("ORG-NODE-PROOF-SUMMARY", 1u32, "2026-10-27T15:17Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:16 x: filed 2026-10-27. Every node verdict read today was the same hand-typed remote.ps1 node run with Select-String over the job's stdout.log (x verify / lines, FAILED, Result:, spiral-tests totals, failing files). Plan: node-proof.ps1 -Summary <job id> prints those lines plus the exit, and the failing step's first error lines (head, not tail, per the check-attestation memory), so a verdict is one call. Generic patterns per repo come from the command's own 'x verify /' style prefix, passed as -Pattern.")
inl note_of_org_node_proof_summary_2 () = Note ("ORG-NODE-PROOF-SUMMARY", 2u32, "2026-10-27T15:48Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:48 x: done in i574n.github/scripts/node-proof.ps1 (unstaged). node-proof.ps1 -Summary <job id> [-Pattern <regex>] [-ErrorLines 12] prints, in one call: exit and wall seconds from the job's exit/started_ms files, the stdout lines matching the pattern (default: the org's step prefixes: 'x verify /', 'x e2e /' failures, DIFFERENT, 'Result:', 'OVER n', GLEAM-/RUST-FAILED, spiral-tests totals and failing files, failed XSIM lines), then the FIRST stderr error lines, ANSI stripped. The pattern travels base64 so regex quotes cannot break the remote command; -MirrorName is now required only outside -Summary. Shown on three real jobs: verify18 (exit 1: 'gate / xc 1004 lines OVER 1000', then the gate.ps1:54 and verify.ps1:81 throws in order), quick check 4 (exit 0, 45 passed, no error lines) and regen xc1 (exit 0, per-crate lines, failed: 0).")
inl note_of_kit_dogfood_1 () = Note ("KIT-DOGFOOD", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:19Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-DOGFOOD (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_1 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:53Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:53 touch: lib/spiral coverage: 26 files have backend switches; 18 have no Zig arm, 18 no Lean, 24 no Bend; largest gaps date_time (42 switches, 0 Zig/Lean/Bend), resultm 11, listm' 11, seq 10, trace 7, async 5; sm' 12/11/0, base 5/5/0; next: resultm/listm' arms (pure Spiral, no host API needed)")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_2 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: recount 10-15: 35 lib files contain backend switches; 26 of them have no Zig arm and 26 no Lean arm (10-14: 26/18/18 by a narrower match). This is the Zig coverage blocker for DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION; raised P1 -> P0 because it now gates the default switch.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_3 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 3u32, "2026-10-15T11:55Z", "lane-b", "07:55 lane-b: Zig arms written in a lib copy for base, sm' + sm'_real (string_builder family, concat, split, convert_to_utf32), convert (convert_i32_base, convert_i32, try_convert, unbox), optionm' of_obj, listm' (list' as []const t: empty, cons, rev, fold, fold_back, filter, map, distinct, to_array), am' to_list_base', seq (seq' as []const t), resultm (result' as union, from_try, get/unwrap_err text), testing _throws, reflection union fields, backend union + run_target Zig case, env + trace (need the zig-environ compiler patch). Zig snippets compile-checked; proof runs (F#/C/Rust/Gleam capture before/after + forced-Zig Kino sweep) in progress")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_4 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 4u32, "2026-10-15T13:32Z", "lane-b", "09:32 lane-b: Zig batches 1-3 proven and handed to main for pack-1k (patches/zig-arms-batch1.ps1, zig-arms-batch2-backend.ps1, zig-arms-batch3.ps1, about 60 Zig arms; lib-only). Kino-free proof (Kino barred during the Tibia live block): 165 lib test cells captured through Kino before 08:20; a one-process --batch rebuild reproduced 650 of 650 outputs. After the arms, F#, Rust and Gleam outputs are byte-identical; 26 C outputs differ only by the added field in the C rendering of type-level backend_switch records that have no C key and were already non-compilable. On Zig, 139 of 168 cells build and pass (zig build-exe -fno-llvm). Remaining failures are backend-specific tests plus run_target-based helpers (listm'.box, console colors, format_exception), which batch 5 guards with is_zig. Lesson: a Zig case in union backend breaks exhaustive run_target matches on every backend, because BackendSwitch validates all arms.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_5 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 5u32, "2026-10-15T14:36Z", "lane-b", "10:36 lane-b: batch 5 (is_zig guards on try_unit/try_finally, substring, format_exception, am' skip/sort, the 9 console colors, listm'.box) proven: F#/C/Rust/Gleam outputs 650/650 identical to batches 1-3; Zig cells 145/168 pass (up from 139). Batches 1, 2, 3 and 5 are in the tree (main, pack-1k), with zig-environ and zig-empty-macro in the core; the gpt5parallel and single-flight Debug builds of the pack-1j core plus both Zig compiler patches pass. Batch 4 (env/trace): probe program identical on F#/Rust/Gleam/Lua; on Zig, trace prints time, count, level, text and locals, env reads PATH, and TRACE_LEVEL=Info filters Debug. The Kino corpus and Zig test-block run follow after the 12:45 arena block.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_6 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 6u32, "2026-10-15T18:59Z", "main-thread", "14:59 Zig coverage progress today (lane B): batches 1-3, 5 installed in pack-1k (base, sm', sm'_real, convert, optionm', listm', am', seq, resultm, testing, reflection, console guards, backend is_zig); batch 6 (sm' whitespace/lastIndexOf, file_system) in pack-1l review; batch 4 (env, trace) proven for pack-1m. Remaining without Zig: async, common, crypto, date_time, guid, networking, platform, runtime, threading (+ rust-only and bench files).")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_7 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 7u32, "2026-10-15T19:09Z", "lane-b", "15:10 lane-b: zig-arms-batch4-env-trace proven in Kino after fixing a private-copy trap (Kino lib_rels prefers deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib/spiral, so a Kino copy with a deps junction runs the tree lib): trace+env on Zig 0 -> 4 passed / 0 failed; rust/fsharp/lua 4/4 before and after; fsharp/c/rust/gleam captures 18/20 identical, 2 differ only in line numbers of a pre-existing C backend_switch error. file_system batch6 captures 58/58 identical. Batch4 queued for pack-1m (main).")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_8 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 8u32, "2026-10-15T22:24Z", "lane-b", "18:2x lane-b: zig-arms-batch7.ps1 (guid Zig field + new_guid/new_raw_guid/hash_guid + zig_random_u64, platform is_windows, common random', crypto hash_text): 12 test programs fsharp/c/rust/gleam 43 identical + 4 C diffs only the known guid_switch tuple field; Zig hash_text, hash_to_port, hash_guid, get_executable_suffix, retry_fn build and pass on zig 0.18-dev and 0.17.0 (-fno-llvm); probe prints valid distinct v4 guids and correct sha256. Not covered: common disposable, crypto file hashing (Rust-only).")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_9 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 9u32, "2026-10-15T22:25Z", "main-thread", "18:25 main: lane B Zig batch 7 in the tree for pack-1n: guid (Zig field, lowercase new_guid, v4 new_raw_guid via a xorshift zig_random_u64, hash_guid), platform is_windows, common random', crypto hash_text (std sha256). Left: common disposable, rust-only file hashing; async, date_time, networking, runtime, threading.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_10 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 10u32, "2026-10-15T23:13Z", "lane-b", "19:2x lane-b: zig-arms-batch8-testing.ps1 (testing __assert_contains is_zig branch): 6 testing programs fsharp/c/rust/gleam 18/18 identical; all 6 build and pass on Zig -fno-llvm incl. assert_contains(_2). Lets the testing rust pins move to Zig next pack after a Kino agreement run.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_11 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 11u32, "2026-10-16T07:38Z", "main-thread", "03:38 main: SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY's final design is in the tree lib: union backend now has Zig/C/Lean/Delphi/Wasm/Bend cases and run_target_args' maps every key, so a run_target | _ => default really reaches every backend (before, those 6 could not reach any default). Lib Zig work is now | Zig => arms in run_target matches, never is_zig () guard chains.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_12 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 12u32, "2026-10-16T13:46Z", "main-thread", "09:46 main: pack-1u installed both modes (09:16/09:21) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-092123, 6,742 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against the pack-1s bless 073131 overridden by probes 081908 (Kotlin), 082312 (Swift), 082906 (Bend), 084211 (Python). Harness Kotlin 444, Swift 443, Bend 401, Python 422.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_13 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 13u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night lib fixes in the tree (uncommitted, tests added): guid.new_guid lowercases on Gleam, Lua and Python and guid.to_string exists (sm obj_to_string of a guid printed a byte list on Zig and a quoted string on Gleam); convert.try_convert parses into any integer type on Gleam; Lua format_debug of records folds fields (one-field records crashed, wide fields printed halves); file_system Zig arms for directory_separator_char and get_current_directory and a Gleam arm for file_exists (tested); file_system.(</>) default arm is now a plain Spiral join instead of F#-only failwith text (tested on gleam, zig, lua), and the get_temp_path / get_file_name defaults use the portable failwith form (lib has no F#-only fallback text left outside F# arms except one parsing test helper); date_time timestamp guid pair in portable Spiral (last 2 rust pins gone).")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_14 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 14u32, "2026-10-19T12:36Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:36 lap 1 grain: lib/spiral recount (2026-10-19): 32 files dispatch per backend (backend_switch or run_target); 11 have no Zig arm (date_time 50 dispatch sites, async 17, near 16, runtime 13, threading 7, rust 6, bench_kit 5, networking 4, ...), 22 have no Lean arm; near, rust, async, threading are host-only by design, so the portable targets are date_time and runtime Next: date_time Zig arms (it already carries the DATE-TIME-PORTABLE TODO block): pick its smallest switch group and add | Zig arms with a ///> zig test")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_15 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 15u32, "2026-10-19T13:02Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:02 lap 2 grain: macro texts in the 65 C samples without a Zig row, by frequency: DynamicArrayCapacityN(!x) 11, DynamicArrayReserveN 10, DynamicArrayResizeN 8, DynamicArrayRefCountN 4 (one C-runtime family, 33 uses, the dynamic_array_* samples), then plain assignment/compare/call shapes (!x = !x 4, !x == N 3, (!x)() 3), libc ABI shims (strlen, memset, memcmp, lldiv 8) and Rust-only text; a portable dynamic-array primitive (capacity, reserve, resize, refcount as compiler ops with arms per backend) is the single biggest unlock, ahead of date_time Zig arms Next: draft the op set (DynamicArrayCapacity/Reserve/Resize/RefCount) with their C semantics read from the C runtime, as a design note in lab/PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_16 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 16u32, "2026-10-19T13:02Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:02 follow-up on the DynamicArray family: the 25 dynamic_array_* samples call DynamicArrayResize/Reserve/Capacity/RefCount<N> as macro text, and the names are defined only in a C shim header the matrix's C flags include (the generated main.c defines Array<N> as a fixed struct with a flexible array member, and main.rs just pastes the macro text), so these samples test a C-runtime ABI experiment rather than portable Spiral semantics; a portable version needs a growable-array type in core (capacity separate from length, resize keeping aliases valid) designed per backend first, which is a language feature, not an op swap. Lower priority than it looked; date_time Zig arms stay the next cheap grain.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_17 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 17u32, "2026-10-19T18:10Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:10 lap 4 grain: the Zig-first accept (single-flight-20261019-125623) leaves 32 C rows with oracle no-zig: 10 dynamic_array_* and 10 abi_external_* (C ABI experiments), 8 rust_* samples whose C row has no Zig peer, wgsl 1, native 2, and 2 plain samples; Zig try: qualified_union_case_no_payload needs its module files (not a single-file probe) and python_macro_annotations fails with Cannot find the backend Zig in the backend switch op (line 16), a sample-side backend_switch with no Zig arm Next: give python_macro_annotations a Zig arm (sample edit between runs, with the matrix Zig row), and run qualified_union_case_no_payload on Zig through a -Probe Zig rung row")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_18 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 18u32, "2026-10-19T18:58Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:58 lap 5 grain: python_macro_annotations needs no Zig arm: it tests Python annotations of macro types (Result, CancellationToken), and its C and Bend arms are constant placeholders 24i32, so a Zig arm would only add a trivially agreeing row; it joins the by-nature C-only set (C ABI, eBPF, Rust, Python samples) that AGENTS.md lists; so of the 32 no-zig C rows none is a portable-primitives gap; the real remaining scope stays compiler ops: StringConcat, string search and case, a char model, each needing every backend Next: pick the first compiler op: string search (index_of) already exists in lib as sm index_of with per-backend arms; check which backends lack an arm there")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_19 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 19u32, "2026-10-19T19:12Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 15:12 lap 5 grain: string search arms fixed in lib sm.spi (three real bugs): Lua index_of used string.find with pattern semantics (. and % were magic) and nil - 1 on a miss, now string.find(s, search, 1, true) or 0; Lua replace used gsub patterns on both sides, now escapes the needle (%p) and the replacement (%%) and truncates gsub to one value; F# index_of, starts_with and replace were culture-sensitive, now StringComparison.Ordinal (F# stays UTF-16 indexed, consistent with its substring; making it byte-based broke substring_cuts_by_start_and_length, so reverted); new test search_and_replace_treat_text_literally on lua, fsharp, rust, gleam and lua added to the utf8 byte test; Cells gate of sm.spi 43/43, 0 type errors Next: string case (to_upper/to_lower) arms: check Zig allocUpperString is ASCII-only vs the others Unicode, and C/Kotlin/Swift/Delphi lack string search arms entirely")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_20 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 20u32, "2026-10-19T23:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 19:22 lap 6 grain: the char model is a semantics decision before it is code: CharT maps to a byte on C (char), Zig (u8), Delphi (AnsiChar) and Wasm, to a Unicode scalar on Lean (Char), Swift (Character via String) and Rust, to a one-character String on Gleam and Lua, and to an i32 code on WGSL, Kotlin, OpenQasm, Ebpf and Calyx (spiral_compiler.fs CharT arms 49788-172278); lib has no char predicates at all (no is_letter, is_digit, code point), which is also what blocks the self-hosted tokenizer (its F# is_var_char uses Char.IsLetterOrDigit, Unicode-aware); typed .spi gives char no ordering and no i32 conversion Next: decide byte vs scalar for char (recommendation: Unicode scalar as u32, matching Rust/Lean/Swift and the tokenizer; byte backends then decode UTF-8 at string index), then add char_code and is_letter/is_digit with per-backend arms")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_21 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 21u32, "2026-10-19T23:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 19:22 correction to note 20: Swift maps CharT to Int32 (an i32 code, spiral_compiler.fs 171289), not a Unicode scalar; the scalar group is Lean (Char) and Rust; the i32-code group is WGSL, Kotlin, Swift, OpenQasm, Ebpf, Calyx.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_22 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 22u32, "2026-10-20T03:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 23:25 main: P0 -> P1 in the 23:1x reprioritization (user order: XSD first, then full hopac SOTA); the char-model decision and remaining ops wait behind those.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_23 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 23u32, "2026-10-21T23:16Z", "main", "19:16 cross-link from XSD (which the user ranked first): SEFAZ-XSD-DELPHI-BENCH note 3 needed a byte or code point read from a string on Delphi, and there is no portable way. lib never converts char to a number (json_scan's digit_of compares chars one by one), so lab xml.spi gets a Delphi-only byte_at macro (UInt32(Ord(s[i + 1]))) with a Spiral UTF-8 decoder on top. The deferred char-model decision would remove that per-backend macro: a portable byte read (StringIndex returning u8, or a Conv from CharT to u32 with each backend's char group: byte, i32 code or scalar, per note 20) makes xml.spi's code-point helpers plain Spiral on every backend. The XSD Delphi slice is the first real consumer, so this is the evidence for picking it up after XSD's step 1.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_24 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 24u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_25 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 25u32, "2026-10-30T02:04Z", "main", "10-29 22:04 main, groom: RESUMABLE. Every dep is Done (DATE-TIME-PORTABLE), so nothing blocks it; it's Paused only for want of a picker (P1, 82%). Pick-up: the team that needs it (lane-roles), after the pack-7 install.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_26 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 26u32, "2026-10-30T14:12Z", "main", "10-30 10:12 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R2/R4: deps Done, owner team main ('agile next --team main' hands it out), assignee agent (nothing in it is the user's). Resume when that team picks it up; the pack-7 install it waited on is done.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_27 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 27u32, "2026-10-30T14:27Z", "main", "10-30 10:27 main, resume check: still COLD. The open part is the char model (n20-n23); nothing has landed on it since 10-21. DECIDED next step (a technical call, by the rule 'the first real consumer sets the shape'). The consumers named so far (xml.spi's code-point helpers in n23; the self-hosted tokenizer in n20) need a portable READ of a string's bytes, not one shared CharT representation. So: (1) add lib sm'.utf8_byte_at (s : string) (i : i32) : u8 with an arm per backend (C/Zig/Delphi/Lua index the bytes directly; Rust as_bytes()[i]; Gleam bit_array.slice; F# and TypeScript through a cached UTF-8 encoding of the string, documented as O(n) on first use), plus sm'.utf8_length; (2) write the code-point decoder and is_letter/is_digit (ASCII first) in plain Spiral on top of that, so xml.spi's Delphi-only byte_at macro retires; (3) leave CharT's per-backend representation as it is, since what CharT *is* stays a compiler question only if a later consumer needs char ordering. Step (1) is lib work, owner main, at load light (one sample per backend group: byte, i32-code, scalar). StringConcat and the search/case ops stay compiler-op additions for team compiler.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_28 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 28u32, "2026-10-30T16:09Z", "main", "10-30 12:09 main: CORRECTION to n27, checked against the live tree. Don't add sm'.utf8_byte_at: the byte and code-point layer already exists in sm'. char_code (sm'.spi:1131) has F#, Python, Rust, TypeScript and Lua arms, and the Lua arm decodes UTF-8 from string.byte; utf8_get_bytes (:1736) has Rust, else .NET; utf8_byte_list (:2163) has Gleam and Rust. kit's TRAIN5 code calls utf8_get_bytes, so it's in kit's protected signature list. n23's motivating consumer is gone too: lib/spiral/xml.spi has 0 byte_at / Ord(s[ macros now. So the real gap is only MISSING ARMS on the existing primitives: char_code has no Gleam, Delphi, C or Zig arm; utf8_get_bytes and utf8_byte_list lack C, Zig and Delphi; and is_letter/is_digit don't exist. RULE (the all_digits precedent): add each arm when a caller on that backend needs it, with that caller's test row, not speculatively. StringConcat and the search/case ops stay compiler-op work for team compiler. Progress stays 82%; Paused until a caller on a missing backend appears.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_29 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 29u32, "2026-10-31T20:02Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:01 compiler GROOM (user direction via /btw, 10-31 15:4x): the self-hosted rewrite (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) starts only after EVERY backend is SOTA: no memory problems, no Rc slowdowns, no union hacks, no recursion limits, no missing arithmetic; GADT bugs and conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'; edition 3 finishes first; Gleam is a top test target (abyssal codebases) with F#, Rust and Wasm; Cells as fast as F# projects. This item is now on that gate (priority -> P0, status -> Active).")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_30 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 30u32, "2026-10-31T20:38Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:38 compiler: under the self-hosting gate (user, 10-31: no conversion or arithmetic gaps), n28's rule 'add an arm only when a caller needs it' no longer holds for this item. Remaining gaps to CLOSE now, not on demand: char_code lacks Gleam, Delphi, C and Zig arms; utf8_get_bytes and utf8_byte_list lack C, Zig and Delphi; is_letter/is_digit don't exist; StringConcat and the string search/case ops are still compiler-op work. The char half is being decided at the compiler level in CHAR-ORDERING (n3-n4); the lib arms here should follow that decision so they don't freeze the divergent CharT. NOTE: tibia is landing a test-only change into sm'.spi right now (D3FCCD79 -> 94A51BB5), so sm' edits wait for that land.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_31 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 31u32, "2026-10-31T20:42Z", "compiler", "DECISION (main, 10-31 10-31 16:42, relayed to compiler): char = a Unicode scalar value on every backend. This supersedes main's 10-30 PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES n27-n28 call: the user's 10-31 self-hosting direction ('no conversion gaps, unacceptable') is newer, and CHAR-ORDERING's measurements show today's per-backend CharT is exactly such a gap. SCOPE (next compiler pack): Conv char <-> integer in PartEval (+ LitChar folding), an ordering constraint chars satisfy, and a char case in the 17 TyConv emitters, with CharT's representation moved to a scalar value on the byte backends (C/C++/Rust/Zig/Delphi) and the string backends (Gleam/Lua). CONDITION: string APIs that index or slice today (byte offsets on the native backends, the C oracle's UTF-8) keep an EXPLICIT meaning: either they stay byte/UTF-8 based and are named so, or they move to scalar indexing in the same pack, never silently. GATE: lib/spiral plus the consumer corpus (tibia, kit, fiscal) with the candidate DLL (packs/lib-gate-dll.ps1), so a char that used to be a byte can't silently change a consumer's string code; plus every backend's char samples agreeing with C.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_32 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 32u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:36 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: est 720 -> 480 left; tag team:compiler. DEFINITION OF DONE: every gap n29 lists closed and tested: char_code on Gleam/Delphi/C/Zig; utf8_get_bytes and utf8_byte_list on C/Zig/Delphi; new is_letter/is_digit; StringConcat and string search/case ops portable. Each has a lib test in its module (sm'.spi / unicode.spi) whose '///>' targets include c, zig, gleam, rust and fsharp, green via lib_gate.ps1 -File <module> -Test <name>, and the lib test-target census (BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS n66) rises accordingly. NEXT STEP: char_code's missing arms in sm'.spi:1086 (C: (int32_t)c; Zig: @as(i32, c); Delphi: Ord(c); Gleam: via the codepoint of a single-grapheme string), drafted as one kit do lib program and gated with lib_program_gate.ps1. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: lib character functions take and return the scalar 'char' type (CHAR-ORDERING's Rune), never i32 code units with -1 for 'not a letter'; classification returns a UNION 'CharClass = Letter | Digit | Space | Other' from one function, and is_letter/is_digit are projections of it, so backends implement one total classifier instead of N booleans; utf8 bytes are 'array_base u8' with a phantom 'utf8' tag ':! utf8_bytes = array_base u8', so raw byte arrays can't be passed where validated UTF-8 is expected.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_33 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 33u32, "2026-11-03T07:29Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). Parked to Planned (no note in 43 h). Next: the n29 gaps per the DoD, char_code on Gleam/Delphi/C/Zig first, each with a lib test targeting c, zig, gleam and rust.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_34 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 34u32, "2026-11-03T17:10Z", "compiler", "11-03 13:1x compiler: char_code C/Zig/Pascal arms LANDED green 13:00 (sm'.spi 25191F00 -> 5DCCAED9; test char_code_reads_bytes ///> rust/c/zig/pascal; C, Zig and Pascal native probes exit 0). x then landed the Gleam arm on top (-> 343FA552), so char_code is now covered on Gleam/C/Zig/Pascal. utf8_get_bytes: Zig arm (arraym::init over char_code + convert::u8) round-trips canonical (EA060D50) and runs natively (exit 0); land queued as job compiler-lib-land-utf8 on base 343FA552. The C and Pascal arms are held back: the plain-Spiral body reaches helpers with no arm on those backends (C: convert::u8 has no C arm, so the chain falls into base try via resultm.spi:32; Pascal: am'::index has no Pascal arm, am'.spi:11). NEXT: add C/Pascal arms to convert::u8 (C '((uint8_t)(!x))', Pascal 'Byte(!x)') and am'::index (Pascal), then widen utf8_get_bytes to C/Pascal with ///> c/pascal on its test.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_35 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 35u32, "2026-11-03T17:55Z", "compiler", "11-03 13:4x compiler: utf8_get_bytes now on Rust/Zig/C/Pascal, all LANDED green: (1) Zig arm 13:15 (sm'.spi 343FA552 -> EA060D50); (2) helper arms 13:32: convert::u8 gains C ((uint8_t)) and Pascal (Byte) arms, am'::index gains Pascal (convert.spi 7FE4B03D -> 7E745D21, am'.spi A8BE0220 -> 93F30FA8); (3) C/Pascal arms 13:40 (sm'.spi EA060D50 -> F88C5141). The body is plain Spiral, arraym::init over char_code + convert::u8, so no new host text. Test utf8_get_bytes_reads_ascii_bytes ///> rust/zig/c/pascal ('Az' -> [65;122]); native probes C/Zig/Pascal exit 0. char_code: Gleam/C/Zig/Pascal done (x did Gleam). Left per n32 DoD: utf8_byte_list on C/Zig/Pascal, is_letter/is_digit (CharClass union), StringConcat and string search/case ops.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_36 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 36u32, "2026-11-03T19:20Z", "compiler", "11-03 15:2x compiler: two more LANDED green. (1) utf8_get_bytes Python + TypeScript arms 14:59 (sm'.spi F88C5141 -> 933407A3): Python 'cp.array(list(s.encode(utf-8)), dtype=cp.uint8)' (Python arrays are cp/numpy, and run_target names the Python backend Cuda), TypeScript 'Array.from(new TextEncoder().encode(s))'. Non-ASCII probe 'Azé' -> [65;122;195;169] exits 0 on Python, TypeScript, Zig, C and Pascal; utf8_get_bytes now covers Rust/Zig/C/Pascal/Python/TypeScript (+ .NET default). Gleam still falls to the .NET default: utf8_byte_list is defined later in sm'.spi (define-before-use), so a Gleam arm needs a reorder first. (2) ascii_class 15:19 (933407A3 -> 80625C1B), per the n32 typefeatureset plan: one total classifier ':| ascii_class = AsciiLetter | AsciiDigit | AsciiSpace | AsciiOther' over char_code, with is_ascii_letter/is_ascii_digit/is_ascii_space as projections; test ascii_class_of_is_one_total_classifier ///> rust/c/zig/pascal/python/typescript; native probe (true and false cases) exits 0 on all six. Unicode letters stay out on purpose (ascii_ prefix). utf8_byte_list on C/Zig/Pascal needs a list' representation there first (list' is host-typed: Gleam/Lua/F#/Python/Rust only). Left per DoD: StringConcat and string search/case ops.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_37 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 37u32, "2026-11-03T22:07Z", "compiler", "11-03 18:0x compiler: case conversion LANDED green 17:43 (sm'.spi 80625C1B -> 4D74DC17): to_lower/to_upper gain Pascal arms (LowerCase/UpperCase; the generated program already uses SysUtils), char_to_upper gains C (plain arithmetic on 97..122, no ctype.h) and Pascal (UpCase); tests char_to_upper_maps_ascii_letters ///> rust/zig/c/pascal and to_upper_and_to_lower_change_ascii_case ///> rust/zig/pascal; native probes C/Pascal/Zig/Rust exit 0. C string case (to_lower/to_upper) stays out: C strings are backend-managed structs, so a host allocation macro would be risky; a plain-Spiral body over char_to_upper is the next step there. QUEUED: contains Pascal arm reusing the plain-Spiral portable_index_of (as C does), plus searches_agree_on_c widened to ///> pascal and zig (index_of/contains/starts_with/ends_with all agree natively on Pascal and Zig).")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_38 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 38u32, "2026-11-03T23:23Z", "compiler", "11-03 19:2x compiler: five more LANDED green, all plain Spiral or core numeric_conv (no new host text). (1) contains Pascal (portable_index_of, as C) + searches_agree_on_c widened to ///> pascal and zig (sm'.spi 7354F24D). (2) C string case: to_lower/to_upper C arms via ascii_case (byte runs, unchanged runs flushed by slice, letters from an alphabet slice, joined with concat_list, so UTF-8 sequences are never cut; 'abC1 é-Zz' -> 'ABC1 é-ZZ' on C), plus convert::i32 C/Pascal arms (numeric_conv) (sm'.spi 38E21DD4, convert.spi 55AE10F9). (3) replace C via portable_replace (scan with portable_matches_at from an offset, O(n); test replace_agrees_on_c ///> c/rust/zig/pascal incl. 'é.é' -> 'éé') (sm'.spi 4E47F1FC). (4) split Pascal (split_portable, as C; split test ///> pascal) (sm'.spi EE0997C5). (5) convert u16/u64/i64/f32/f64 C/Pascal arms (numeric_conv) + test widening_conversions_agree_across_native_backends ///> rust/zig/c/pascal (convert.spi 99C7ABF0). Every C/Pascal string search/case op in sm' now has an arm except concat (takes a host seq', design like list'). Known cross-backend difference kept: Rust to_upper is Unicode (é -> É), Zig/Pascal/C are ASCII; tests stay ASCII.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_39 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 39u32, "2026-11-04T01:01Z", "compiler", "11-03 21:0x compiler: the last C/Pascal string gap, sm'.concat (sep) (b : seq::seq' string), is a TYPE gap, not an arm gap. seq::seq' is host-typed through seq_switch' (Gleam list, F# seq, Python list, Rust Vec, TS array, Zig slice) and has no C, Pascal or Lua representation, so no concat arm can be written until seq' gets one. Its 5 lib callers (crypto 181, file_system 1521, parsing 198/200, process 576) are host-only modules today. Portable code should use concat_list (list string; every backend incl. C/Pascal since 10-08) instead. Decision: no concat arm. If a C/Pascal caller ever needs seq', give seq_switch' a C/Pascal arm backed by the array representation (array_base t) first, the same shape list' needs for utf8_byte_list. PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES is complete for its DoD's arm work; what remains is these two representation items (seq' and list' on C/Pascal), each blocked on a representation choice.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_40 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 40u32, "2026-11-04T04:15Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:1x compiler groom: DONE. The DoD's arm work is complete (math 5/5/5, console 3/3/3, optionm 5/5/5, convert, string ops; concat on C/Pascal settled as a type gap, not an arm gap). The two representation leftovers (seq' and list' on C/Pascal/Lua) moved to LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR. Closing this unblocks BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY's last dep.")
inl note_of_portable_primitives_41 () = Note ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", 41u32, "2026-11-04T07:28Z", "compiler", "11-04 03:2x compiler: POST-CLOSE FIX on this item's test (reported by tibia). widening_conversions_agree_across_native_backends (n38's step 5) never passed on rust: inside convert.spi the function convert shadows the module name, so convert::u16 n etc. resolved through the function, and Rust emitted '(()) as Rc<dyn Fn(i32) -> u16>' (the compile first failed as 'Cannot parse the literal as: i32'). Not a pack regression: identical on pack29, pack31 and pack32 (install backups via SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL). Fixed by calling u16/i64/u64/f64/f32 unqualified; lib_gate (box, process compiler) passes on rust, zig, c and pascal, 2 of 2. LANDED over tibia's Zig f32_of_bits land: convert.spi FD1CEE45 -> C56CFC5D, closed green (3 consumer-used signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 22, type_probe 9 typed), undo 20261104-032535. Lesson: the land's close gates type tests but do not run them on every ///> target, so a new multi-target test needs a lib_gate run per target before it counts as proven.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:56Z", "lane-b", "next step mapped (step 3, Kino): 5 fallback sites: kino.ex:137-141 polyglot_root opt/app env/SPIRAL_KINO_POLYGLOT_ROOT, notebook.ex:24-34 deps/polyglot/deps/spiral lib paths, toolchain.ex:21 ../polyglot core package, domain_compiler.ex:62 ../../../polyglot/deps; dice/alphabet still reach spiral via deps/polyglot/deps/spiral, so the notebook.ex lib paths go only after step 2")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 2u32, "2026-10-15T04:00Z", "lane-b", "00:00 lane B: step 3 Kino part LANDED (uncommitted): polyglot_root option/app env/SPIRAL_KINO_POLYGLOT_ROOT alias removed (kino.ex, notebook.ex option list, kino_test, notebook_test, README), toolchain.ex default_package_dir is spiral's own deps clone only (the ../polyglot candidate never matched in any CI layout: in dice the spiral root is deps/polyglot/deps/spiral), spi/domain_compiler.ex the same; no consumer in spiral/dice/alphabet/polyglot/eoie used the alias (git grep); left: notebook.ex @lib_rels deps/polyglot/deps/spiral entries (dice still reaches spiral that way, step 2), env.ps1:129-130")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 groom: Kino's polyglot fallbacks and the polyglot_root alias are removed (lane B, uncommitted). Next grain: list remaining polyglot references in spiral (scripts and docs).")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 4u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: step 1 staged and reviewed: pack-a/namespace-spiral.ps1 renames namespace Polyglot to Spiral in core, host, 3 refs, both fsproj RootNamespace, compat PolyglotCommon.fs, profile scripts and formatter fsx (15 files). The 3 Polyglot stack-frame filters on gpt5parallel-only sides stay (pair-drift rejects one-sided edits; they filter nothing after the rename). Pack: night pack 10-17 (scripts %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\packs\\night-1017, each with -DryRun; final review single-flight-20261017-053315 on the exact pack: 6,906 rows, DISAGREE 0, residual-only Zig 457 and Rust 415, 3 intended Rust exit cells (shown as 2 NATIVE-DIFF rows); builds in single-flight, hopacsota and gpt5parallel; hopacsota inline and random:23 equal single-flight on every compile and residual cell; corpus 144 of 144; Zig at C speed on the bench).")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:33 lap 1 grain: step 1 shipped: the compiler file declares namespace Spiral (0 namespace Polyglot left), from the 10-17 night pack-a rename; remaining polyglot coupling to measure: who still resolves through deps/polyglot (dice and alphabet build.ps1 dot-source ../deps/polyglot/scripts/core.ps1 and deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib) Next: grep every repo's scripts for deps/polyglot paths and list which could point at spiral directly")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:50Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:50 lap 2 grain: deps/polyglot references in tracked scripts/yml/spiproj: dice 25 in 14 files, alphabet 12 in 8, spiral 1 (env.ps1:135, a package-dir fallback to polyglot's The-Spiral-Language clone), x/blog/dir-tree-html/eoie/i574n.github 0; dice's reduce to two patterns: polyglot's own scripts/core.ps1 (11x) and spiral reached through polyglot/deps/spiral (lib.ps1 5x, dir-tree-html.ps1, publish-tree.ps1) Next: diff the function sets of polyglot/scripts/core.ps1 and spiral/scripts/core.ps1; if spiral's covers dice's calls, dice and alphabet can clone spiral directly")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 7u32, "2026-10-19T12:50Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:50 follow-up: polyglot/scripts/core.ps1 defines no functions; it is a forwarder that finds spiral/scripts/core.ps1 (deps/spiral or a sibling clone, else clones it) and dot-sources it. So every dice and alphabet dependency on polyglot is really on spiral: the switch is dice/alphabet init cloning spiral into deps/spiral and rewriting 25 + 12 paths (deps/polyglot/scripts/core.ps1 -> deps/spiral/scripts/core.ps1, deps/polyglot/deps/spiral -> deps/spiral), mirrored in both repos (alphabet mirrors dice)")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 8u32, "2026-10-19T13:26Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:26 lap 2 grain: prepared lab/SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT/rewrite-deps.ps1 (dry run by default, -Apply writes) for dice and alphabet together (alphabet mirrors dice). Rules: deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/ -> deps/spiral/ and deps/polyglot/scripts/core.ps1 -> deps/spiral/scripts/core.ps1 over tracked .ps1/.spiproj/.yml/.toml/.json, plus EnsureSymbolicLink ../deps/spiral -> ../../spiral in scripts/init.ps1 where missing (dice lacks it; alphabet has it since earlier), inserted after polyglot's init call so the sibling spiral clone exists. Equivalence checked: polyglot/scripts/core.ps1 is a 13-line forwarder that dot-sources spiral's scripts/core.ps1 (or clones spiral beside polyglot), polyglot/deps/spiral is a link to the sibling C:\\home\\git\\spiral, and every rewritten target exists in spiral (scripts/core.ps1, lib/spiral/lib.ps1, apps/cells/spi/run_notebook.ps1, scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1, scripts/publish-tree.ps1, apps/spiral/build.ps1). Dry run: 22 files change (dice 14, alphabet 8). 4 deps/polyglot references stay on purpose because they name polyglot-owned files: dice lib/fsharp/build.ps1:24 (polyglot lib/fsharp/Common.fs), both init.ps1 polyglot links, alphabet init.ps1:28 (polyglot apps/parser build). Not applied: it changes CI wiring in two repos, so it goes with a user push; decision recorded: apply in one step for both repos, then run both repos' build.ps1 (heavy) before the push. Next: decide whether Common.fs and apps/parser can move to spiral (the last two polyglot needs of dice/alphabet)")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 9u32, "2026-10-19T14:04Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:04 lap 3 grain: decided the two polyglot-owned leftovers. (1) polyglot/apps/parser (F# JsonParser/Parser exported from .livemd) is built by both inits (dice scripts/init.ps1, alphabet scripts/init.ps1:28) but nothing in dice or alphabet reads its output (grep for JsonParser, Parser.fs, apps/parser/Parser: 0 files in either repo), so the build step is dead work: rewrite-deps.ps1 now drops it from both inits. (2) polyglot/lib/fsharp/Common.fs (50 lines) stays for now: its only dice consumer is lib/fsharp/build.ps1 (the F# runtime modules of the dice lib), which retires with the F# lane (bold native goals), so moving it into spiral would only move dead weight. Also added rule ../../polyglot/deps/spiral/ -> ../../spiral/ (dice init still built spiral apps and dir-tree-html through polyglot's link to the sibling spiral). Dry run: 22 files; dice init now links deps/spiral, drops the parser build, and builds apps/spiral, apps/wasm and dir-tree-html from ../../spiral directly. Left deps/polyglot references: Common.fs and the two polyglot links (needed only for Common.fs and polyglot's own init). Next: after the F# lib of dice is retired or moved, drop polyglot from dice/alphabet entirely; apply rewrite-deps.ps1 -Apply with a user push")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 10u32, "2026-10-19T15:19Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:19 lap 5 grain on step (1) naming: the namespace rename is done (spiral_compiler.fs has namespace Spiral, no namespace Polyglot left), but the two stack-trace filters the 10-12 inventory listed still keep only frames containing Polyglot (spiral_compiler.fs:127534 debugTraceAt, :185367 the FatalError path for NullReference/InvalidOperation/... exceptions). Since the rename they match nothing, so an internal compiler exception reports only its message and the debug term trace has no frames: a silent regression from the rename. Staged lab/SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT/stack-frames.py (both filters -> Spiral.; dry run 2 anchors match) to ride pack 7; matrix.psd1 has no pin naming those exception types, so no pin changes expected. Step (3) leftovers in Cells: notebook.ex @lib_rels lines 30 and 33 (deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib/spiral, polyglot/deps/spiral/lib/spiral) stay until dice/alphabet drop polyglot (rewrite-deps.ps1); fsi_session.ex:7 is a doc mention only.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 11u32, "2026-10-19T15:36Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:36 lap 6 grain, step (3) inventory and the first cut. Polyglot mentions left in spiral code: Cells notebook.ex @lib_rels (2, waiting on rewrite-deps), fsi_session.ex (doc only), lib file_system.spi:761 (a test that also accepts a source dir named polyglot, harmless until vendoring ends), and apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/env.ps1:135-136, Get-SpiralPackageDir's two polyglot fallbacks. The env.ps1 pair is dead: the first candidate, spiral/deps/The-Spiral-Language, exists locally and is cloned by scripts/init-app-build.ps1 in CI. Staged lab/SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT/drop-polyglot-fallbacks.py (dry run: the anchor matches once), queued for main after the switch accept, because the switch may rewrite how core- resolves. Also today: spiral scripts/core.ps1 now resolves the Cells dir through ResolveLink, so the polyglot/deps/spiral route no longer runs mix from a second path (WORKFLOW-END-TO-END note).")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 12u32, "2026-10-19T15:56Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:56 lap 7 grain: rewrite-deps.ps1 rechecked against today's trees (dry run, still 22 files) and taught to keep its own TODO block true. After the rewrite, dice/scripts/init.ps1's SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT block (which says dice still reaches spiral through polyglot) would be false, so the script now replaces it with: dice keeps deps/polyglot only for lib/fsharp/build.ps1's Common.fs (polyglot/lib/fsharp); drop this link and the polyglot init once the F# lib of dice retires or Common.fs moves (each line at most 120 columns). The dry-run diff shows the swap; the harvest will then point at the remaining tie only.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 13u32, "2026-10-19T16:33Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:33 lap 8 grain: post-switch census of spiral's remaining code ties to polyglot (apps/cells/lib + compiler scripts, case-insensitive): env.ps1:135-136 (the two VS Code Plugin core fallbacks; drop-polyglot-fallbacks.py dry run still matches its anchor after the switch) and cells notebook.ex:30 and :33 (lib_rels for deps/polyglot/deps/spiral, the route dice and alphabet use until rewrite-deps lands). The older step-3 ties (domain_compiler.ex, toolchain.ex, kino polyglot_root option) no longer exist; fsi_session.ex:7 is a history mention. So step 3 is 2 staged lines now plus 2 lib_rels lines that wait on step 2")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_14 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 14u32, "2026-10-19T16:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 12:39 stack-frames.py taken out of pack 7: both of its filters live on gpt5parallel-only sides (PartEval at 127534, SignalRSupervisor at 185367), so the Release build's pair-drift check stopped the build (changed only on the gpt5parallel side), exactly as the 10-17 note predicted; reverted in the tree (pair-drift clean). The dead Polyglot filters can only go with gpt5parallel's retirement (HOPACSOTA-P6) or with a mirrored single-flight change.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_15 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 15u32, "2026-10-19T17:00Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:00 lap 9 grain: dry run of rewrite-deps.ps1 rechecked against what it leaves behind: (1) alphabet's EnsureSymbolicLink deps/polyglot (init.ps1:25) becomes dead after the rewrite, because alphabet has no lib/fsharp (so no Common.fs), its parser build line is dropped and every deps/polyglot/scripts/core.ps1 is mapped to deps/spiral; the link should go in the same rewrite, unlike dice's, which Common.fs still needs. (2) The rewrite covers only deps/polyglot paths; sibling-path uses of polyglot stay: alphabet scripts/build.ps1:8, init.ps1:20 and :23, apps/hangul/build.ps1:8, and dice scripts/init.ps1:20 and :26 (core.ps1 dot-source and the polyglot init). These are the last ties to plan for, with the same core.ps1 mapping to ../../spiral/scripts/core.ps1")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_16 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 16u32, "2026-10-19T17:34Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:34 lap 10 grain: rewrite-deps.ps1 extended for lap 9's gap, dry run 24 files (was 22), parse 0 errors. Found first why a plain path map was unsafe: dice init.ps1 sources ../../polyglot/scripts/core.ps1 BEFORE polyglot's init clones spiral, and that core.ps1 is only a shim that clones spiral itself when missing; so mapping it to ../../spiral/scripts/core.ps1 alone breaks a fresh runner. The script now (1) adds a spiral clone (Set-Location .., git clone spiral.git, git pull) to the single polyglot clone block of each init.ps1, keeping its indentation, (2) maps ../polyglot/scripts/core.ps1 to ../spiral/scripts/core.ps1 (dice init, alphabet init, alphabet scripts/build.ps1, apps/hangul/build.ps1, which already sources ../../../spiral/lib), (3) drops alphabet's dead deps/polyglot link. After it, alphabet has 0 deps/polyglot references; dice keeps only Common.fs + its link. Caveat: alphabet -fast on a never-initialized tree no longer self-clones spiral (fast assumes an initialized tree). Next: user/main go for --Apply in dice + alphabet together (alphabet mirrors dice), then their workflow.ps1 runs")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_17 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 17u32, "2026-10-19T17:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:52 lap 11 grain: rewrite-deps.ps1 now parse-checks every rewritten .ps1 in memory (PowerShell Parser.ParseInput) and refuses the whole run if one would not parse, so --Apply cannot write a broken init.ps1; dry run: all 24 rewritten files parse (files changed: 24, no parse refusal). Next: go for -Apply in dice + alphabet together, then their workflow.ps1 runs")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_18 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 18u32, "2026-10-19T17:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:52 follow-up: made the refusal claim true for -Apply too: writes are collected and done only after every file was rewritten and parsed (before, files earlier in the loop were written before a later refusal); dry run unchanged (24 files), dice tree untouched (12 dirty paths before and after)")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_19 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 19u32, "2026-10-19T18:19Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:19 lap 12: dice keeps deps/polyglot only for lib/fsharp Common.fs, and dice_fsharp.fs uses it in exactly one place (line 95, trace Debug (...) _locals). Common.trace only maps its own TraceLevel onto SpiralTrace.TraceLevel, so dice_fsharp.livemd can call SpiralTrace.trace SpiralTrace.TraceLevel.Debug directly and drop the Common.fs import and the open Polyglot/Common lines (livemd 37, 41, 45) plus build.ps1:24's module, which removes the last polyglot link")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_20 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 20u32, "2026-10-19T22:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:48 lap 19 grain: rechecked the three staged applies after main's core-v1 migration of dice, alphabet and polyglot (18:3x, 163 rule edits): dry runs still clean, dice-drop-common.py 5 edits in 3 files (Common.fs import and the one trace call), drop-polyglot-fallbacks.py its 1 env.ps1 anchor, rewrite-deps.ps1 24 files that all parse; the migration touched none of their anchors. Next: main's go for the three applies (dice and alphabet together)")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_21 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 21u32, "2026-10-19T23:20Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:20 lap 21 grain: stack-frames.py (debugTraceAt and the FatalError path keep only frames containing Polyglot, but the namespace is Spiral now, so both filters drop every frame) did not land with pack 7: its 2 anchors still match today's spiral_compiler.fs. The script now reads newline-preserving (it used read_text, which would normalize line endings) and refuses a file that already filters on Spiral.; checked on a scratch copy (apply, then a second apply refuses). Queue row 67 is still the one to take with the next pack")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_22 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 22u32, "2026-10-19T23:59Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:59 main's decision on stack-frames.py: it stays out of every pack until gpt5parallel retires, because both filters it edits sit on the gpt5parallel side of the PartEval section pair, so pair-drift.ps1 stops the build on a one-sided change (that is why pack 7 dropped it). Removed from lab/CALYX-BACKEND/stack-candidate.ps1; queue row 67 is parked on gpt5parallel's retirement")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_23 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 23u32, "2026-10-20T02:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 22:33 user 2026-10-19 21:4x: check again whether polyglot is really isolated now: no repo depends on it for anything, spiral standalone.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_24 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 24u32, "2026-10-20T02:44Z", "review-lane", "2026-10-19 22:44 review-lane recheck of polyglot isolation (git grep -i polyglot over tracked + untracked files of 16 repos, top-level deps links, CI ymls, spiproj packageDir census, the 3 lab dry runs). Verdict: spiral itself is standalone in CI (its scripts, workflow and gh-pages.yml never reach polyglot; it clones its own deps/The-Spiral-Language in init-app-build.ps1), but polyglot is NOT isolated: 4 repos still depend on it live. (1) i574n.github, the reference run, not covered by any lab script: init.ps1:29 clones polyglot, :32 and build/outdated/publish/workflow.ps1 dot-source ../../polyglot/scripts/core.ps1, workflow.livemd:4 sources polyglot/scripts/nbs_header.ps1 (exists only in polyglot), build.ps1:15 and outdated.ps1:12 run polyglot's own build/outdated; plus 2 code-workspace files. (2) dice, live beyond rewrite-deps: init.ps1:15-26 clones and inits polyglot, and polyglot init is the only thing that clones hyperui (dep_hyperui.ps1), which ui/index.html:8 links as ../deps/polyglot/deps/hyperui/public/component.css for trunk (an .html file, outside rewrite-deps' ps1/spiproj/yml/toml/json filter); lib/fsharp/dice_fsharp.livemd:5 imports polyglot/lib/fsharp/Notebooks.livemd (which imports polyglot/deps/spiral/lib/spiral/spiral_runtime.fs), a line dice-drop-common.py does not touch; Common.fs (build.ps1:24, livemd:37) is covered. (3) alphabet: after rewrite-deps it has 0 deps/polyglot paths but init.ps1:16-20 still clones polyglot and runs its init. (4) tibia (other lane, report only): init.ps1:16-25 clones polyglot to get spiral cloned and dot-sources its core.ps1, build/publish/workflow and apps/arena/build.ps1 use deps/polyglot/scripts/core.ps1; CI runs that init. Local-only/backup: vault (scripts reach polyglot and a gone apps/dir-tree-html: dead), fc1943s.github (dump repo), singularity (data/playground: dead). x, blog, dir-tree-html, eoie: 0 references (eoie only a prose mention in spec/one-call.md).")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_25 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 25u32, "2026-10-20T02:44Z", "review-lane", "2026-10-19 22:44 review-lane, spiral's own leftovers and what is left on this epic. Spiral: (a) cells notebook.ex:30 and :33 @lib_rels are live, not only for dice: find_spiral_lib walks ancestors and tries deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib/spiral first, and C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\deps\\polyglot is a stale local symlink to polyglot (created 2025-01-05, untracked, gitignored, nothing creates it any more), so every notebook under the spiral tree resolves lib through spiral -> polyglot -> spiral (same files, second path; the 10-19 two-path mix relink was this class); tibia\\deps\\polyglot does the same. Removing the stale link (user's call, local only) and then the two lib_rels lines plus notebook_test.exs:309 (the lib-mounted-once test builds deps/polyglot/deps/spiral) closes it; :272 only names a temp dir polyglot. (b) env.ps1:135-136 dead fallbacks, covered by drop-polyglot-fallbacks.py (dry run: 1 anchor matches). (c) three dead Polyglot frame strings, all on gpt5parallel sides: spiral_compiler.fs:127597 and :185606 (stack-frames.py, parked on gpt5parallel retirement) and :137232 Replace of at Polyglot.spiral_compiler (not in stack-frames.py; a no-op strip, goes with the same retirement). (d) stale docs: apps/compiler/AGENTS.md:16 (names a build.ps1 that does not exist and a polyglot builder), tmp/AGENTS.md:254 and samples/README.md:14 (core- through deps/polyglot), NEXT.md:249, tmp/README.md:96 and scripts/upstream.py:13 (the fork lives at polyglot/deps/The-Spiral-Language; spiral has its own deps clone now). (e) lab NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS run.ps1:10 and contract-run.ps1:7 hardcode dice\\deps\\polyglot\\deps\\spiral and break after rewrite-deps. Lab dry runs today: rewrite-deps.ps1 24 files, all parse, leaves dice Common.fs + its link; dice-drop-common.py 5 edits in 3 files; drop-polyglot-fallbacks.py 1 anchor. Left for full isolation, in order: apply the 3 staged scripts (dice + alphabet together); extend rewrite-deps (or a sibling script) to drop the polyglot clone/init from dice and alphabet inits in favour of pwsh ../../spiral/scripts/init.ps1, move the hyperui clone into dice (or spiral init) and rewrite ui/index.html:8 to deps/hyperui, and replace the livemd:5 Notebooks.livemd import with a direct ../../deps/spiral/lib/spiral/spiral_runtime.fs import or drop it; then cells lib_rels + test; then i574n.github drops polyglot from init/build/outdated/workflow and gets nbs_header from spiral (or inlines it); tibia's lane does the same mapping; finally archive polyglot (user decision 10-12).")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_26 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 26u32, "2026-10-20T02:47Z", "review-lane", "2026-10-19 22:47 review-lane follow-up: what dice and alphabet lose if their inits call pwsh ../../spiral/scripts/init.ps1 instead of cloning and initing polyglot. polyglot init.ps1 + init.livemd do: rustup toolchains, cargo-binstall, sccache, trunk 0.21.14, playwright 1.44 browsers, patched cargo-outdated, PowerShellGet, bun install at the polyglot root, dep_spiral(_extension), Linux apt packages via init.sh, gleam from source on Linux, hyperui clone, apps/spiral build, and spiral's own init. spiral/scripts/init.ps1 already does the toolchains, binstall, sccache (:78), trunk (:71), playwright (:91), cargo-outdated (:82) and the near nightly-2024-07-14 (:34); dice init builds apps/spiral itself; CI installs Gleam/OTP via setup-beam and runners ship build-essential, unzip and pwsh; wasm-pack has no user in dice, alphabet, tibia or i574n.github. The only real loss is the hyperui clone (polyglot dep_hyperui.ps1, a depth-1 clone of markmead/hyperui), which only dice/ui needs (index.html:8). So the init swap is safe once dice gets its own hyperui clone (dice init or a dice dep script, linked as deps/hyperui) and index.html:8 points there. With that, the livemd:5 import swap (to ../../deps/spiral/lib/spiral/spiral_runtime.fs, which dice-drop-common's direct SpiralTrace call needs in the notebook path; deleting the line would break the notebook run) and the 3 staged scripts, dice and alphabet need nothing from polyglot.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_27 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 27u32, "2026-10-24T02:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main grooming: Paused by rule (Active, no note since 2026-10-20T02:47Z, no child item noted in 3 days). Resume when picked; progress kept at 60%.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_28 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 28u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 10:32 (subagent2): waiting: rewrite-deps.ps1 -Apply rides a user push (TODO block placed in dice init this lap) -> next: apply with push | lap 13 14:37 (subagent2): staged lab/SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT/dice-drop-common.py (dry run 5 edits in 3 files): dice_fsharp.livemd + .fs drop the Common.fs import and open Polyglot/Common, the one trace call goes to SpiralTrace.trace (Lib from spiral_runtime.fs), build.ps1:24 drops the Common.fs module; refuses if Common is still named after the edits -> next: after rewrite-deps' go: apply, then dice lib/fsharp build.ps1 (heavy); livemd line 5 Notebooks.livemd import is interactive-only (bold-native drops it) | lap 14 15:03 (subagent2): staged set complete for dice (rewrite-deps + dice-drop-common + drop-polyglot-fallbacks); waiting -> next: main's go for rewrite-deps -Apply | lap 16 15:50 (subagent2): the remaining polyglot mention in dice_fsharp.livemd (line 5, #!import of polyglot Notebooks.livemd) never reaches the build: the committed dice_fsharp.fs has no import or polyglot text except the open Polyglot that dice-drop-common.py removes, so after that script the dice F# build has no polyglot input (the line only matters for interactive sessions, which bold-native drops) -> next: main's go: rewrite-deps -Apply, dice-drop-common --apply, then dice lib/fsharp build.ps1 | lap 16b 15:51 (subagent2): mirror check (alphabet rule): alphabet hangul has no Common.fs tie (only build.ps1:8 polyglot core.ps1, which rewrite-deps already moves; rewrite-deps covers dice + alphabet); after rewrite-deps + dice-drop-common the rewritten dice init.ps1 TODO (keeps deps/polyglot only for Common.fs) and its deps/polyglot EnsureSymbolicLink become stale: a third small step removes both -> next: stage that step once rewrite-deps' output is in the tree (anchors are its output) | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): unchanged -> next: main's go | lap 22 19:59 (subagent2): per main: stack-frames.py parked until gpt5parallel retires (pair-drift); dropped from stack-candidate.ps1; OpenQasm sim patch has its own queue row -> next: gpt5parallel retirement")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_29 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 29u32, "2026-10-30T14:12Z", "main", "10-30 10:12 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R1: the only blocker is rewrite-deps.ps1 -Apply rides the user's push. Now assignee=user + user-batch; grooms stop re-verifying it, and it moves when the user acts.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_30 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 30u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Its 4 migration scripts now live in i574n.github/scripts/spiral-independent/ (dice scripts/init.ps1 comment updated). Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_31 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 31u32, "2026-10-31T21:03Z", "main", "10-31 17:02 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK): stays PAUSED. Reason: the remaining step is running the migration (i574n.github/scripts/spiral-independent/rewrite-deps.ps1) in dice, alphabet, mechahaze-album1 and tapas2, a user-batch decision (it also gates POLYGLOT-RETIRE's apps/parser). Resume when: the user approves the migration run.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_32 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 32u32, "2026-11-01T04:51Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT folder (4 files: dice-drop-common.py, drop-polyglot-fallbacks.py, rewrite-deps.ps1, stack-frames.py) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_33 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 33u32, "2026-11-01T13:55Z", "main", "main 09:5x: the migration's helper scripts in i574n.github/scripts/spiral-independent/ include three untracked .py files (dice-drop-common.py, drop-polyglot-fallbacks.py, stack-frames.py; written 10-19 by helper-lane-2, moved out of lab/ by main 10-30). New .py tools are drift by the user's rule (spiral-first-tooling), so they are NOT to be committed. Before the migration runs (the user-batch step), port them to Spiral run through KIT-RUN-SPI, or fold their logic into rewrite-deps.ps1's Spiral replacement; archive the .py after. Listed in commit-suggestion.md as don't-commit.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_34 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 34u32, "2026-11-02T14:03Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByTest. QUESTION: Remove the stale untracked symlinks spiral\\deps\\polyglot and tibia\\deps\\polyglot (n25 calls it the user's call), then the two notebook.ex lib_rels lines? RULE (written before acting): Record each link's target, then remove the links. KEEP REMOVED iff (a) the Cells lib-resolution test (notebook_test.exs lib-mounted-once, updated to build spiral/lib directly) passes, (b) 'lib_gate.ps1 -File async.spi' is green, and (c) a spiral notebook and a tibia notebook resolve lib to C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral with no 'polyglot' in the resolved path. If any fails, restore the links and keep the lib_rels lines. The multi-repo step (2) (dice cloning spiral directly) follows the n12-era plan by default: mirror dice -> alphabet, with changes unstaged. BACKUP/UNDO: Before removal, write both link targets (C:\\home\\git\\polyglot) into a kit state-backup. Undo = New-Item -ItemType SymbolicLink -Path <link> -Target C:\\home\\git\\polyglot. Code edits are left unstaged. OWNER: main. NEXT: Run the rule on the box, then drop the lab-held tag (spiral/lab no longer exists, per SPIRAL-TO-YAML n27) and set the assignee to agent. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_35 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 35u32, "2026-11-02T15:45Z", "x", "2026-11-02 11:45 x: DANGLING CHECK for n34's removal (user rule relayed 11-02: agents run removals themselves, unstaged; never a user step). Both spiral\\deps\\polyglot and tibia\\deps\\polyglot are untracked SymbolicLinks to C:\\home\\git\\polyglot. Four sites still resolve through deps/polyglot, all fallbacks: apps/compiler/scripts/env.ps1:141 (VS Code Plugin bundle path), apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1:15 (lib-root candidates), apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/notebook.ex:30 (lib_rels) and apps/compiler/tmp/samples/README.md:14 (docs). So the removal is a code change plus test, not a plain delete. Drop those 4 fallbacks, record both link targets in a kit state-backup, remove the links, then n34's KEEP-REMOVED tests decide. Owner main; nothing here is the user's.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_36 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 36u32, "2026-11-02T17:16Z", "main", "main 11-02 13:1x n34 RULE RUN: spiral\\deps\\polyglot REMOVED and KEPT REMOVED. (a) the Cells notebook tests: Result 13 passed; (b) lib_gate.ps1 -File async.spi: 3 of 3 passed, 0 type errors; (c) lib resolution through Spiral.Cells.Notebook.find_spiral_lib: spiral resolves to c:/home/git/spiral/lib/spiral and tibia to c:/home/git/tibia/deps/spiral/lib/spiral (tibia's deps\\spiral links to C:\\home\\git\\spiral), no polyglot in either path. The chain's first run reported (c) as failing only because `mix run -e` through mix.bat mangled the quotes (memory bat-args-parens), and its restore step then failed on its own bug (target read after Delete), so the link stayed removed; (c) re-run as an .exs file passes. Backup + undo: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\backups\\spiral-independent-links-*.json (target C:\\home\\git\\polyglot). The 4 fallback sites are dropped (unstaged: env.ps1, lib_gate.ps1, notebook.ex, samples README). KEPT: tibia\\deps\\polyglot, because tibia build/publish/workflow dot-source deps/polyglot/scripts/core.ps1 and init.ps1 recreates the link (x's dangling check missed tibia). tibia must replace that core.ps1 dependency first.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_37 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 37u32, "2026-11-02T17:34Z", "main", "main 11-02 13:3x tibia\\deps\\polyglot REMOVED too. tibia moved its 6 dot-sources to deps/spiral/scripts/core.ps1 and dropped the init.ps1 EnsureSymbolicLink line (unstaged; node proof tibia-polyglot-free green, table_rows 1/1, typed_network 5/5). main's dangling check: 0 tracked tibia files reference deps/polyglot. Lib resolution after removal: tibia resolves to c:/home/git/tibia/deps/spiral/lib/spiral, no polyglot. Backup + undo: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\backups\\spiral-independent-links-tibia-20261102-133110.json. Both local polyglot links are gone. REMAINING: tibia's init.ps1 still CLONES polyglot and runs its init/core.ps1 from ../../polyglot, the CI route to spiral. Switch it to clone spiral directly as regex's init does (init-app-build.ps1); then no repo needs polyglot and POLYGLOT-RETIRE can proceed.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_38 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 38u32, "2026-11-02T18:23Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: tibia NO LONGER NEEDS POLYGLOT (unstaged). scripts/init.ps1 now clones spiral directly into ../../spiral (when absent), dot-sources ../../spiral/scripts/core.ps1, links deps/spiral and runs deps/spiral/scripts/init-app-build.ps1, regex's shape; the polyglot clone and its init.ps1/core.ps1 calls are gone. scripts/build.ps1 now fetches Cells' mix deps itself (mix local.hex, local.rebar, deps.get when deps/kino is missing) before mix spiral.cell_budget. run_notebook.ps1 already did that, but the cell-budget step runs first, so a fresh clone failed with \"Can't continue due to errors on dependencies\"; polyglot's init used to hide it. PROOF, a CI-like fresh run on the node in a clean dir (C:\\home\\remote-stage\\fresh-1102: the tibia tree without deps/target, origin set to github i574n/tibia): init exit 0 in 12 min (spiral cloned, deps/spiral junction, WGSL runner built, no polyglot dir); then build.ps1 -Files table_rows exit 0 (deps fetched, cell budget 26 non-blocking findings, table_rows 1/1). Jobs tibia-fresh-init and tibia-fresh-build.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_39 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 39u32, "2026-11-02T21:42Z", "main", "main 11-02 17:4x RE-DECIDED Paused -> Active (90%). The 'user batch' pause is over: the user's 11-02 rule says lanes do the removals themselves. State: spiral + tibia are free of polyglot (n36-38). Tonight's work, proven on the node: dice + alphabet rewritten onto deps/spiral (contract 7/7 + NEAR sandbox; alphabet apps compile); vault, mechahaze-album1 and tapas2 ported (POLYGLOT-READERS-PORT, Done); i574n.github scripts now take spiral's core.ps1. The last dice links are in proof on the node: lib/fsharp now imports spiral_runtime.fs directly instead of polyglot's Common.fs and Notebooks.livemd; ui/index.html takes hyperui from dice's own deps/hyperui; scripts/init.ps1 runs spiral's init instead of polyglot's and clones hyperui. Once that proof is green, dice has 0 polyglot references and this epic closes; the polyglot repo itself retires under POLYGLOT-RETIRE.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_40 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 40u32, "2026-11-02T22:26Z", "main", "main 11-02 18:3x dice lib/fsharp PROVEN off polyglot: an in-job A/B on the node's checkout. A = the git-index version (polyglot Common.fs + Notebooks.livemd): the notebook step fails with 'input.fsx(12,13): Unexpected character'. B = the new version (spiral_runtime.fs import, SpiralTrace.trace, modules = GetFsharpRuntimePaths only) with -SkipNotebook: 'FSHARP-OK dice/lib/fsharp / result 16211931'. The notebook-step red is the node's code page (CELLS-FSI-STDIN-UTF8, filed, fix in proof), the same in A and B. dice ui: the trunk build is RUST-OK with hyperui from dice's own deps/hyperui (wasm 1,985,068 B). Its e2e step failed only on the node's old bun 1.2.5 (a frozen lockfile written by 1.4.4); main upgraded node bun to 1.4.4, and the ui re-run comes next.")
inl note_of_spiral_independent_41 () = Note ("SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT", 41u32, "2026-11-02T22:47Z", "main", "main 11-02 18:5x DONE. Every i574n consumer is off polyglot, and the user committed tonight's dice/alphabet/spiral work at 17:57-17:58 (dice 84acdd5, alphabet e3f4266, spiral 91bd70dc). CI: dice run 41538911884 SUCCESS, which proves on GitHub's runners that dice's new init.ps1 (spiral's own init + its own hyperui clone, no polyglot clone/link/init) and the ui e2e work; alphabet gh-pages SUCCESS. git grep polyglot in dice = 0. Readers left are not consumers: vault/mechahaze-album1/tapas2 (ported, unstaged; POLYGLOT-READERS-PORT Done), i574n.github's umbrella clone/build/outdated lines and docs prose. Those, and the 'split what remains of polyglot' half of this title, continue under POLYGLOT-RETIRE.")
inl note_of_kit_regen_drift_1 () = Note ("KIT-REGEN-DRIFT", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:30Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-REGEN-DRIFT (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_typed_fields_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-TYPED-FIELDS", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:30Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-TYPED-FIELDS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_tibia_local_world_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-LOCAL-WORLD", 1u32, "2026-10-30T15:01Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 11:1x WIPE REDO of tibia-local (the 10-28 wipe left only < ~700 B fragments, restored in place by healing). New live/setup.ps1 in the tibia repo (unstaged) rebuilds the world from pins, idempotently. Pins: canary v3.6.1 (release exe + v3.6.1 source datapack + otservbr.otbm), otclient 4.1 (clone + otclient_gl_x64.exe), dudantas/tibia-client 15.13.02dfc3 (assets -> otclient-src/data/things/1511), and nExBot 19632d4. Each install gets a .setup-pin marker; any folder lacking it is MOVED to tibia-local/_replaced-<stamp> (never deleted). config.lua comes from config.lua.dist with mysqlPass/mysqlDatabase 'canary', mysqlPort 3317 and toggleDownloadMap false. Missing mariadb-data gets install-db + schema.sql. The pins were confirmed by the fragments: 679 of 684 restored otclient files and 11 of 11 nExBot files match the fresh checkouts after EOL normalisation, and the other 5 differ by 0 content lines. The provenance file tibia-client-15.13/TIBIA_CLIENT_SOURCE.txt survived and named the client repo/tag. DB: the restored mariadb-data is at db_version 58 = schema.sql, 47 of 48 tables. players_online (ENGINE=MEMORY, so its definition was a tiny file lost in the wipe) was recreated from schema.sql's CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS, so 48 of 48. Smoke start 10:46: 'OTServBR-Global server online!' in about 50 s. I stopped it at 11:0x because compiler's PACK-8 review is on the box. Left: a live log-in/walk/kill check through run-policy-scenario.ps1 once the review window closes (live runs alone).")
inl note_of_tibia_local_world_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-LOCAL-WORLD", 2u32, "2026-10-30T16:59Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 12:58 CORRECTION to note 1: the arena world runs the data-canary datapack (canary.otbm 19.7 MB, an empty canary-monster.xml for the scenarios' own spawns), not the global one. Evidence: run-policy-scenario.ps1:54 swaps spawns in data-canary\\world\\canary-monster.xml, and the restored DB has Knight Sample at 1932,1349,7 and GOD at 1932,1352,7, which are data-canary map coordinates. The pitfalls note already said it suits arena scenarios. live/setup.ps1 now sets dataPackDirectory \"data-canary\" and mapName \"canary\", drops the 185 MB otservbr.otbm download, and pins canary as 'v3.6.1 data-canary'. The rerun moved the global-datapack canary to _replaced-20261030-125822 and rebuilt only canary (otclient and nExBot pins matched, so they were skipped: idempotent). The live smoke waits for main's live-gui slot.")
inl note_of_tibia_local_world_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-LOCAL-WORLD", 3u32, "2026-10-30T18:09Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 14:09 LIVE SMOKE PASS on the rebuilt world (live-gui window win-1793383448412, run alone): run-policy-scenario.ps1 defaults (3 trolls, axe 30, level 8, parity on). The knight logged in, walked (1930,1349 -> 1931,1350 chasing), and cleared 3 trolls in 101.6 s: +90 experience, 0 heals, lowest health 173. Parity: 250 ms gap, 15x11 view, 3 actions allowed, 0 dropped. Loot and forge-dust messages arrived; the world stopped cleanly and the window was released. The 10-13 exit criterion (log in, walk, verified kill) holds again after the wipe, with the same +90 experience for 3 trolls as the 10-13 baseline.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 1u32, "2026-10-15T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:00 grooming: status was stale (Planned 0%). Verdicts so far: walk steps pinned; step timing and road speed hold (PC-1, PC-2); monster wake holds; melee hit distribution holds (33.6 vs 34.97); swing delay after a step holds (PC-3); potion use still off for retreat (POTION-FIDELITY).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 2u32, "2026-10-18T19:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 15:28 verdicts added 2026-10-18, each against Canary 3.6.1 source or data-canary: floor change onto an up-ramp holds (geometry.climbed, the live 1940,1372,7 -> 1940,1373,6 pinned); death experience loss is formula-equal (ceil(e*0.1) = (e+9)/10 exhaustive to 50M, level <= 7 spared), live 'loses a level' reproduced; death skill loss is formula-equal (getReqSkillTries table plus the loss loop, against a C# replica), live 'a skill point' reproduced; gear drop is formula-equal (blessing.lua DropLoot), not yet measured live; line of sight is bit-exact (Map::checkSightLine port, against a C# replica); block charges match source (refill per think, zero rolls spend charges, distance spells spend a charge without shield defense); post-step swing: the model has Canary's onCreatureMove resumption (the attack-command path does not apply to our probe, because OTClient's attack toggles); ranged hunters: formulas typed, behavior (keep-distance and cornering) unverified and in gap. Open gaps: potion use (POTION-FIDELITY, damage per landed hit 35.2 live vs 36.5 model), ranged-monster movement, and the RNG mechanics' seeded oracle (ORACLE-RNG).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 3u32, "2026-10-18T21:04Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 17:04 PR-13 pre-registered in docs/BENCHMARK.md before any hunter run: 3 hunters on the arena spot, spiral-policy (rules heal 160), 10 runs; model prediction 2.67 potions (sd 1.89), 63.6 s with the 200 s cap, 99/100 clear; holds if both one-sample t within 2.26 and 9+/10 clear. First live test of the ranged rules (keep-distance slow-down, checkSightLine port, arrows skip the shield). Live slot granted by the compiler lane after its core compile census tonight.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 4u32, "2026-10-19T01:51Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 21:51 PR-13 (ranged hunters on the arena spot, pre-registered 17:04) FAILS on its clear-rate rule: 5 of 10 live runs clear against the model's 99 of 100. Potions t 0.40 (3.00 per run vs 2.67); clear time t -0.40 over the 5 clears (61.6 s vs 63.6). No deaths; health conservation 44/44. Uncleared runs end at the 200 s cap with 225 or 450 experience. The log shows the knight targeting a hunter for about 1,000 ticks with no strike, so live hunters escape reach more often than the model's keep-distance slow-down allows. Next: compare the hunter's live step pattern (positions every tick from the probe) with Monster::getDistanceStep and the arena geometry, then fix the model and pre-register PR-14. Results in results/arena-results-hunter-pr13.tsv, logs in tibia-local/arena-logs 20261018-21*-hunter-*. pr2-analysis.ps1 prints 'predicts' because it only checks the two t values.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 5u32, "2026-10-19T06:39Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-19 02:39 Correction to the PR-13 note: the uncleared runs do not keep a hunter targeted with no strike. They log 'attacking nothing'. All five uncleared runs (1, 2, 4, 6, 9) make their last kill far east (x 1942-1957) after chasing a fleeing hunter along y 1352-1354, and the other two hunters never come back into the client's view. The cleared runs kill at x 1920-1937. Model side (lab/tibia-fidelity, hunter_stall_probe.spi and hunter_companions_probe.spi, outputs saved next to them): the model knight also ends up to 22 tiles east and 21 north, yet clears 100/100, because the remaining hunters shadow it at about 4 tiles (keep-distance approach while within the 11x11 view). Canary's MAP_MAX_VIEW_PORT_X/Y = 11/11 matches the model's view, and the Hunter stats (speed 105, targetDistance 4, runHealth 10, staticAttackChance 90) match creatures.spi. So the gap is in how live companions follow. Next: apps/arena now logs a 'seen at x,y: <monster hp% x,y; ...>' line every 10 ticks (competitor.spi, client.monsters_described, scoreboard.due_for_trace); a 5-run diagnostic block (not pre-registered) in main's slot around 03:15-03:30 will show what the companions do.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 6u32, "2026-10-19T09:27Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-19 05:27 diagnostic block (5 runs, 05:02-05:14, not pre-registered): 4/5 clear (9/15 with PR-13). Uncleared run: knight drags a fleeing hunter 30 tiles east, third hunter falls behind at 5 tiles, leaves view, knight idles to the cap. Live follow pace beyond 4 tiles 0.83-0.93 tiles/s vs model 1.15-1.19 (lab/tibia-fidelity/follow-pace.ps1 on live and model seen-at traces). Model change: ranged hunter beyond target distance steps only on its creature think (hunting_pack.thinks_now) -> model pace 0.91-1.00, 94/100 held-out clears (was 100), stranding reproduced. Canary cause not pinned (path re-plans on every knight move; A* start node satisfies at target distance). Remaining gap 94% vs 60% live. pack_sim test updated (hits 286->173 open, 121->135 arena; kills unchanged) + new test a_hunter_beyond_four_tiles_closes_one_tile_per_think. BENCHMARK PR-13 diagnostic row. Next: find the remaining live stall (walk-back on empty screen, monster idle when no player on screen) before pre-registering PR-14.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 7u32, "2026-10-19T10:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-19 06:00 Canary source checks for the residual hunter gap (local canary/src): view is MAP_MAX_VIEW_PORT 11x11 (map_const.hpp, matches model); follow re-plans on every followed-creature move (creature.cpp onCreatureMove isUpdatingPath + updateCreatureWalk, async pathfinder) plus every 2 s on think (walkUpdateTicks); A* start node already at target distance returns a path (map.cpp getPathMatchingCond), so hasFollowPath stays true; hunter speed 105 (data-canary hunter.lua) = 600 ms. None explains the slower live pace, so thinks_now stays a calibration. Model gap found: a monster whose target list empties walks back to its spawn (monster.cpp updateIdleStatus -> isWalkingBack, doWalkBack until a player is on screen), the model stands still; same stranding outcome, matters only if the knight returns. Residual 94% vs 60% needs more traced live runs: only 1 of 15 uncleared-capable runs had the trace. Next live slot: trace-on PR-14 pre-registered on the calibrated model.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 8u32, "2026-10-19T10:02Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-19 06:02 PR-14 pre-registered in docs/BENCHMARK.md (before any run): hunter clear on the calibrated model, 10 traced live runs into results/arena-results-hunter-pr14.tsv. Prediction (lab/tibia-fidelity/hunter_pr14_prediction_probe.spi, 100 held-out, 200 s cap): 94/100 clear, 2.36 potions (sd 1.51), 62.0 s per clear (sd 11.8), 15.9 hits, 0 deaths. Holds at >=9/10 clears and both t within 2.26; fails at <=7 clears (P=0.019 under 0.94) or a t beyond; 8 inconclusive. Alternative: pre-rule model 100/100.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 9u32, "2026-10-19T10:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-19 06:29 PR-14 RESULT (06:03-06:28, 10 runs, results/arena-results-hunter-pr14.tsv): FAILS on clear rate, 7/10 (pre-registered fail at <=7). Potions 4.10 (t 2.15) and clear time 60.4 s (t -0.31) within. Follow-pace rule holds live (1.00/0.91 tiles/s at gap 5/6 vs model 0.91/1.00). New finding: over 15 traced runs all 4 uncleared reach knight x 1960-1967, all 11 cleared stay <=1953. Model arena spot is radius 30 (terrain.arena_spot_radius, wall at x 1963 = model 130), so fleeing hunters get cornered at the window edge in the model while live the road continues east. Next: widen the scanned spot in apps/map (needs more than two u32 words per row), rerun the probes and the cyclops tests, then a new pre-registration.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 10u32, "2026-10-19T10:31Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-19 06:31 Confirmed the window cut: a lab copy of apps/map with spot_radius 45 (lab/tibia-fidelity/map-wide, walkability-radius-45.txt) shows walkable ground continuing east of x 1963 (rows y 1351-1353 and 1356-1360 open well past it); live tiles 1963,1352 / 1964,1352 / 1967,1334 read walkable. The model's radius-30 window walls this off. Fix path: raise arena_spot_radius past 45 in apps/map (rows need 3 u32 words), regenerate terrain tables, rerun the 251/252 live scan check and the cyclops pack_sim tests, re-predict, then pre-register PR-15.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 11u32, "2026-10-19T10:32Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-19 06:32 Scope for the wider spot (read-only check): apps/map/map.spi row_words packs a row into a (low, high) u32 pair (low_word_bits 32), so spot_radius tops out at 31; terrain.spi reads the pair at arena_spot_low/high, arena_road_low/high, region_high_bits and the region router (lines 106-142, open_low/open_high, marked_low/high). Plan: a typed row of two u64 words (radius up to 63) in both files, radius about 45, regenerate with apps/map/run.ps1 -Update. The lab map-wide radius-45 tables wrap past 64 columns; only its readable grid dump is valid evidence.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 12u32, "2026-10-20T05:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 01:00 arena spot widened to radius 45 (apps/map emits N 32-bit words per row; terrain router generalized; WGSL twins pass) and road speed applied at the spot. Hunter clear-rate gap diagnosed, not closed: live the knight chases a distance-keeping hunter east along the y1353 road at ~2 tiles/s and strands out of sight of its companions; model knight 1.2-1.3 tiles/s. Two knight-speed rules tried and reverted (formula step: 100/100 clears in 45 s; think-gated follow: 97/100). Prediction now 87/100 vs live 16/25; no PR-15 registered. Details: tibia docs/BENCHMARK.md PR-14 post-hoc row.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 13u32, "2026-10-20T06:04Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 02:04 parking pass: paused. Open gaps named: (1) hunter clear rate (live 16/25, model 87/100 with radius 45 + road speed; diagnosis in BENCHMARK PR-14 post-hoc row), (2) the knight's live step speed (see TIBIA-COMBAT-FIDELITY note), (3) potion means (TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY, paused). Resume with PR-12's 30 retreat runs as fresh data or a new mechanism lead.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 14u32, "2026-10-20T19:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 15:17 state after 2026-10-20: closed today: protection zones and teleports (PC-5, model rule, no effect on the arena clear), knight step speed (PC-4: formula speed confirmed; model keeps the client pace), swing after step (PC-6 on fresh logs; retreat-step bug fixed, think period 1083 ms; six-means sum t^2 17.5 -> 8.8), Canary respawn timing (PR-15: kills match), distributional fits for cyclops melee, knight melee and hunter arrows (TIBIA-ORACLE-RNG). Open: (1) hunter clear rate (live 16/25, model 87/100; zones ruled out), (2) potions over-counted on every protection-free arena (parity cyclops +1.4 retreat, hunting ground +2.4 of which shield 12 explains ~0.4): the model's retreating knight is in contact from second 1 and pinned in the dead end (4.0 vs 11.5 retreat steps per clear, 0.3 vs 4.4 s free), see TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY notes 2026-10-20.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 15u32, "2026-10-21T02:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 22:29 D4 (protocol TIBIA note 12) applied, and the hunter clear-rate gap traced to a scenario error: PR-13/PR-14 ran on the arena where protection ends at login (parity layer since 2026-10-15 14:17), but their predictions and PC-5 used mirroring_the_live_arena, which freezes monsters for the 10 s login wake. With the wake removed (mirroring_the_live_arena |> waking_at 0, or the new mirroring_the_current_arena; identical, since hunters are ranged and the new melee timing does not touch them): 45 of 100 clear (was 87), cleared in 59.0 s on average (live 60.4), 2.97 potions per run (live PR-14 4.10), no deaths (lab/tibia-fidelity/hunter_current_arena_probe.spi). Live 16 of 25; Wilson band 45-80, so by D4 the gap is explained within sampling (at its lower edge). Remaining hunter difference: potions (model low by ~1.1 per run).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 16u32, "2026-10-21T09:10Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 05:10 Overnight update (details: TIBIA-POTION-FIDELITY notes 79-107, TIBIA-EVENT-TIME). Superseding the 15:17 note: the model's knight no longer keeps the client pace. mirroring_the_current_arena now steps the knight at Canary's formula (paced_like_canary, PR-16 retreat fit), adds the monster dance step, steps everything by Canary's formula on the map's ground speed, and orders the knight before the foes (tick-late view). Hunter clear rate with that bundle: 99/100, above the 16/25 band again, consistent with the 2026-10-20 01:00 note (formula step gave 100/100). Lab traces put it on within-tick order: knight-first 99, foes-first 59, a poll-phase landing 94. Next: TIBIA-EVENT-TIME (event queue), adoption by rule D16 after PR-17 (TIBIA-PR17-BLOCK).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 17u32, "2026-10-21T17:01Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 13:01 Source fact not yet in the model: hunter.lua lists COMBAT_PHYSICALDAMAGE percent -10, a weakness, so hunters take 10% more physical damage (cyclops 0, unaffected). creatures.stats_of has no elemental modifiers. Live PR-18: the knight lands 15.9 hits per hunter clear at 28.6 damage each (257 hits, 14 clears). Expected direction if added: model hunter clears get faster, which widens the clear-time gap (live 64.2 s vs model 55.9 s). So that gap sits in swing opportunities (chase time, swings cancelled by steps), not in damage. Add it only together with a rule written before measuring, scored on clear time, hits and potions per run (PR-18/PR-19).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_18 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 18u32, "2026-10-21T20:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 16:41 Hunter clear-time gap (live 60.5 s PR-19 and 64.2 s PR-18 vs all4 55.9), live side measured before the model's (descriptive). Probe status lines every 10 s in the 29 cleared PR-18/PR-19 runs: the knight's target is adjacent in 46 of 165 samples (27.9%), so a ranged hunter is out of reach about 72% of the clear. Live landed knight hits per clear about 15.9 (13-21, slow clears land more: 86.7 s -> 21). Model side (scratch hunter-trace.ps1, runs after the live block): per clear, landed knight hits from per-tick foe-health diffs and ticks with the target in reach, chasing, or none. Reading fixed now: if the model's in-reach share is clearly above 28%, the gap is the chase (hunters keep distance or flee more effectively live: target_distance, flee pathing, the follow walk); if it matches, the gap is in swing outcomes per reach time (hit chance, swings cancelled by steps). A candidate change still needs a rule written before scoring.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_19 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 19u32, "2026-10-21T21:18Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 17:18 Hunter clear-time gap, model side (hunter-trace, all4, 100 held-out runs, 77 clears; read by the rule fixed in note 18): the knight's target is in reach (distance 1) in 43.4% of the model's ticks with a target, against 27.9% live (46 of 165 10-s samples, Wilson 95% about 21-35%). Distance 2: model 45.0% vs live 51.2%; distance 3: 6.5% vs 19.5%; 4+: 5.1% vs 1.2%. So by the fixed reading the gap is the chase: live hunters hold distance 2-3 more effectively than the model's (their targetDistance 4, runHealth 10 and speed 105 already match hunter.lua, so the difference is in how they move: Canary's distance-keeping step choice, getDistanceStep / flee pathing, or their think cadence while the knight closes). Landed knight hits per clear: model 14.61, live about 15.9 (live hunters take 10% more physical damage, which the model lacks, so live needs fewer hits; the counts are close). Caveat: hits per in-reach second exceed the 0.5 swing ceiling in both, since end-of-tick distance misses momentary adjacency, so compare shares, not rates. Next, under a rule written before scoring: a Canary-sourced distance-keeping step for ranged monsters (Monster::getDistanceStep), scored on hunter clear time, clears and hits (PR-18/19).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_20 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 20u32, "2026-10-21T21:22Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 17:22 Source read for the hunter chase gap (note 19; no change made). Canary Monster::getDistanceStep (monster.cpp:1733): (1) returns false and lets A* path when distance > targetDistance OR the projectile sight line is not clear (isSightClear(..., true)); holds at distance == targetDistance; steps away only when closer. (2) Its step choice is a fixed table: for a diagonal target it prefers the two orthogonal moves away (boolean_random between them), then the diagonal, and only when fleeing does it try the side moves. (3) stepDuration rises to 2 while the target is adjacent and slows non-fleeing monsters' steps (creature.cpp:1485, WALK_TARGET_NEARBY_EXTRA_COST): the model already copies this (f.nearby, target_nearby_step_factor). Model differences found: (a) its distance-keeping branch (hunting_pack.spi, ranged && distance <= target_distance && geometry.sees) gates on geometry.sees, which is view range only, with no sight line; Canary gates on the projectile sight line (now terrain.sight_blocks via sighting_like_canary); (b) the away step is terrain.step_away, not getDistanceStep's orthogonal-first table with its random tie. (a) alone may push the wrong way (Canary paths toward the knight without a line of sight), so neither is a confirmed cause; both are candidates for a rule written before scoring, scored on the in-reach share (live 27.9%, model 43.4%), clear time, clears and hits on PR-18/PR-19.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_21 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 21u32, "2026-10-21T21:23Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 17:23 RULE D20, written before any measurement of the candidates. Gap: hunter clear time (live 62.3 s pooled over PR-18/PR-19's 29 clears, model 55.9) with the in-reach share as its mechanism (live 27.9%, Wilson 95% 21-35%; model 43.4%; note 19). Candidates, each a Canary fact from monster.cpp getDistanceStep (note 20), off by default, on top of pace: K1 keeping_sight_like_canary (the distance-keeping branch gates on the projectile sight line, terrain.sight_blocks, instead of view range, and without a line the hunter follows the chase path); K2 stepping_away_like_canary (the away step follows getDistanceStep's table: orthogonal moves first with a seeded random tie, then the diagonal); K12 both. Score (100 held-out runs, the hunter table plus the hunter trace): (i) in-reach share inside 21-35%; (ii) clears inside the central 95% binomial range of k = 29 of 40 (PR-18 + PR-19), i.e. 57-85 of 100; (iii) hits per run within 2 SE of the pooled live mean, 22.15 (sd 8.78, n 40), i.e. 19.37-24.93; (iv) cyclops arms and hunting-ground kills unchanged (ranged-only change; checked by scripts/score.ps1). Tie: fewest changes, then the clear time closest to 62.3 s. Found on PR-18/PR-19 data, so never adopted in sample: a passing candidate only qualifies for a fresh pre-registered hunter block (PR-21) on a later day. If none passes, the gap stays open and the builders stay unused (deleted rather than left as dead options if they move nothing). [Edited before any candidate result was read: 62.0 corrected to 62.3 (arithmetic slip), and the bands for (ii) and (iii) computed.]")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_22 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 22u32, "2026-10-21T21:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 17:29 D20 K1 (keeping_sight_like_canary, scratch patch on a copy under target/; compiled C differs, so the patch applied) is identical to the base on every measure: 77 of 100 clears, 21.03 hits, 55.9 s, in-reach share 43.8% over all 100 runs (base 43.8%; the 43.4% in note 19 counted cleared runs only), arms unchanged (S 3.01). On the arena spot the projectile line between a hunter within distance 4 and the knight is evidently always clear, so the gate never fires. K1 fails (i) and moves nothing; by D20 it is not built as an option. K2 and K12 are scoring now (jobs tibia-d20-k2, tibia-d20-k12).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_23 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 23u32, "2026-10-21T21:36Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 17:36 D20 VERDICT (note 21, applied mechanically by scratchpad d20-judge.ps1; scratch patches on copies under target/, pace bundle, 100 held-out runs): no candidate passes, the gap stays open. base: reach 43.8%, 77 clears, 55.9 s. K1 (sight gate): identical to base, moves nothing. K2 (getDistanceStep's step table): reach 48.1% (fails i: band 21-35, live 27.9), 99 clears (fails ii: band 57-85), hits 24.92 (inside 19.37-24.93), potions 4.62, 54.0 s; arms unchanged (S 3.01). K12: reach 48.3%, 100 clears, hits 24.99 (just outside), 54.1 s. So the hypothesis behind K2, that the model's hunters back off diagonally at 3x step cost and get caught, is refuted: Canary's orthogonal-first table moves the model further from live, not closer. Nothing is built into src (the patches lived only in target/ copies). What remains for the hunter gap: the live hunters at distance 3 (19.5% vs 6.5%) are not explained by the step choice; untested leads are the hunters' think cadence when the knight closes (they move only on thinks in the model), Canary's A* re-path to maxTargetDist when they lose the line, and the knight's own chase speed and path (the model's knight follows at its step formula; live it is client-driven follow).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_24 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 24u32, "2026-10-21T21:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 17:54 Hunter chase gap, one lead closed by source reading (no measurement needed): think cadence is not it. Canary's goToFollowCreature sets hasFollowPath after a distance step (creature.cpp:1006), and onCreatureMove re-paths whenever the followed creature moves (creature.cpp:475-477), so a hunter backs off on every knight step. The model's distance-keeping branch already runs whenever now >= next_step_at with no think gate (the thinks_now gate at hunting_pack.spi:756 applies only to the chase branch). Remaining leads after D20 and this: the knight's own chase. Live, the knight follows by client chase mode (server-side Player follow, re-pathed asynchronously on each target move, WalkStartPolicy and the ImmediateWhenReady start); the model's knight steps toward its target at its step formula. Live, the hunters sit at distance 3 in 19.5% of samples against 6.5%, so the live knight closes more slowly. A trace of the knight's step timestamps while chasing a hunter (live: the probe's position samples; model: per-tick) would show it.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_25 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 25u32, "2026-10-21T21:55Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 17:55 Hunter chase, live side at 1 s resolution (the probe's 'seen at' trace, about one line per second; 2037 samples over the 40 PR-18/PR-19 runs), measured before the model's: nearest live hunter at distance 1: 26.5%, 2: 51.7%, 3: 17.5%, 4: 3.4%, 5+: 0.7% (confirms the 10-s status-line estimate of 27.9% in reach). The knight moves 1.28 tiles per second while the nearest hunter is 2 or more away (n 1461; 0 tiles 175, 1 tile 761, 2 tiles 477, 3+ 48), against a straight-step ceiling of 2 per second at 500 ms. Model side queued (scratch hunter-chase.ps1: the same two measures sampled every 10 ticks). Reading fixed now: if the model's knight covers clearly more than 1.28 tiles per second while chasing, the gap is the knight's chase (its follow path or start delay); if it matches, the gap is in how the hunters keep their distance.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_26 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 26u32, "2026-10-21T21:58Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 17:58 Hunter chase, model side (scratch hunter-chase trace, all4, sampled every 10 ticks, in-view samples only as live): knight moves 1.31 tiles per second while the nearest hunter is 2-7 away (n 2664) vs live 1.28 (n 1461), so by the reading in note 25 the knight's chase matches and the gap is in how the hunters keep their distance. Nearest-hunter distance: model 1: 46.1%, 2: 44.5%, 3: 4.6%, 4-7: 4.8% vs live 26.5 / 51.7 / 17.5 / 4.1. Already checked and matching or ruled out: targetDistance 4, runHealth 10, speed 105, the step table (D20 K2 moves the wrong way), the sight gate (K1 moves nothing), think cadence (note 24), WALK_TARGET_NEARBY_EXTRA_COST 2 and the diagonal cost 3 (same constants). One structural difference left: Canary's nearby slowdown is a counter (stepDuration, updated in getDistanceStep) that persists and applies to every step of a non-fleeing monster, while the model applies it only in its distance-keeping branch, but that would make live hunters slower, the wrong direction. Next measurement, live-only and cheap: the hunters' own step rate from the 1-s 'seen at' positions while the knight is adjacent vs 2-3 away (hunters can be told apart by their health % between samples), against the model's; that says whether live hunters out-walk the model's or start walking sooner.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_27 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 27u32, "2026-10-21T22:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:00 Hunter gait, the clearest signal so far (live: hunters matched between consecutive 1-s 'seen at' samples by nearest neighbour; model: hunter-gait trace, per slot every 10 ticks). Tiles moved in the next second by distance from the knight: live 0.86 (n 533) / 0.93 (1086) / 1.01 (612) at distance 1 / 2 / 3; model 0.95 (2139) / 0.66 (2587) / 0.44 (853). Model hunters at distance 2-3 walk 30-55% slower than live, which fits the in-reach excess (46% vs 26.5%) with the knight's chase speed matching. Source-backed candidate: Canary's nearby slowdown counter (Monster::stepDuration) changes on every getDistanceStep call, which runs on each move of the followed knight and on each think (onCreatureMove -> updateCreatureWalk; goToFollowCreature), so the x2 cost decays within a step or two of the knight leaving; the model's f.nearby changes only on the hunter's own distance-keeping steps, so the slowdown lingers at distance 2-3. Candidate for a rule written before scoring (D22): update the nearby counter on each knight step and each hunter think, as Canary does; score on the gait at distance 2-3, the in-reach share (21-35%), clears (57-85) and hits (19.37-24.93). In sample (found on PR-18/19), so it would need a fresh hunter block.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_28 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 28u32, "2026-10-21T22:02Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:02 RULE D22, written before any measurement of the candidate. Diagnosis (hunter-why trace, all4): model hunters at distance 2-3 wait on their step clock in 93.8% of ticks, 61.8% with the nearby slowdown on, and step in 4.2%; live hunters at 2-3 walk about 1 tile per second. Candidate K3 (one Canary fact, off by default, on top of pace): the nearby counter (Monster::stepDuration) is updated on each getDistanceStep call, i.e. on each move of the followed knight and each think, as well as the hunter's own step (creature.cpp onCreatureMove -> updateCreatureWalk -> goToFollowCreature; monster.cpp:1748-1755): +1 up to 2 when the knight is adjacent, -1 down to 0 otherwise. Score on 100 held-out runs (scratch patch, then score.ps1 plus traces): (i) in-reach share inside 21-35%; (ii) clears inside 57-85; (iii) hits per run inside 19.37-24.93; (iv) arms and hunting-ground kills unchanged; plus descriptive: the gait at distance 2-3 against live 0.93 / 1.01 tiles per second. In sample (found on PR-18/19), so passing only qualifies K3 for a fresh pre-registered hunter block (PR-21) on a later day; failing leaves the gap open and K3 unbuilt.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_29 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 29u32, "2026-10-21T22:09Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:09 D22 VERDICT (note 28, applied mechanically by d20-judge.ps1): K3 fails. Reach 43.7% (band 21-35), 78 clears, hits 21.09, 55.8 s, arms unchanged; gait at distance 1/2/3: 0.95 / 0.66 / 0.47 (base 0.95 / 0.66 / 0.44, live 0.86 / 0.93 / 1.01). Updating the nearby counter the Canary way barely moves anything, so the lingering slowdown is not the main cause either. Arithmetic: a speed-105 hunter steps 601 ms on arena ground, rounded to 650 (700 on the 100 ms tick); a diagonal step is about 2 s and is never slowed; the model's hunters at 2-3 step on 4.2% of ticks, about 2.4 s per step, which points to mostly diagonal steps (best_step tries the diagonal first). K2, which tries orthogonal moves first, made reach worse, so its gait is being measured now (job tibia-gait-k2) to see whether orthogonal steps make the hunters faster but walk them into the knight's path.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_30 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 30u32, "2026-10-21T22:10Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:10 Hunter gait under K2 (getDistanceStep's orthogonal-first table, scratch): tiles per 1 s at distance 1/2/3: 0.78 / 0.66 / 0.80 (base 0.95 / 0.66 / 0.44; live 0.86 / 0.93 / 1.01). So K2 moves the gait toward live at distances 1 and 3, as the diagonal-cost arithmetic predicted, but not at distance 2, where live hunters spend half their time (51.7%), and the in-reach share still rose (48.1%). Open question, stated precisely: why does a model hunter at distance 2 from the knight move only 0.66 tiles per second under every variant tried (base, K1, K2, K3), while live it moves 0.93? Distance 2 is where getDistanceStep's straight cases try the sideways moves (W/E with offsetx <= 0 etc.) that keep the distance; the model's best_step only accepts strictly improving tiles, and K2's table applies only while distance < targetDistance. Next session: split the distance-2 waiting ticks by cause (diagonal cost, slowdown, stuck, shot) under K2, before writing D23.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_31 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 31u32, "2026-10-21T22:13Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:13 Distance-2 step split (hunter-steps trace, hunters at Chebyshev 2 from the knight at the tick start). Base: one step per 3.35 s, diagonal 444 / slowed straight 116 / unslowed straight 247 of 807 steps (55% diagonal, each leaving a 2 s cooldown). K2 (getDistanceStep table): one step per 1.85 s, diagonal 148 / slowed 148 / unslowed straight 960 of 1256 (12% diagonal). Live hunters at 2 move about 0.93 tiles per second, roughly one step per second. So best_step's diagonal-first order is a real, source-backed difference that roughly halves the hunters' step rate at distance 2; K2 fixes that half. But stepping whenever allowed, K2's mix predicts about 0.9 s per step, and it observes 1.85 s, so something still holds K2's hunters back about half the time they could step, and K2's reach rose instead of falling (D20). Next session: per-tick causes of a K2 hunter at distance 2 not stepping while its clock allows (no improving or sideways tile free, the knight's tile, other hunters), then a D23 candidate that keeps K2's step table and fixes that cause.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_32 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 32u32, "2026-10-21T22:16Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:16 K2 distance-2 free/wait split (hunter-free trace): of 23233 hunter-ticks at distance 2, 94% wait on the step clock with 755 ms left on average, 5.4% step, 0.6% are free but stay. So K2's hunters are not blocked by terrain or crowding; their cooldowns are long. K2's step mix (76% straight at 700 ms on the 100 ms tick, 12% slowed 1400, 12% diagonal 2000) would leave about 580 ms on average for a tick sampled inside a cooldown; 755 ms says the cooldowns are longer than that mix. Known small contributor: the 100 ms tick rounds Canary's 650 ms (50 ms beat) up to 700 (+8%). Not yet explained: the rest. Next session: log each hunter step's cooldown length and kind directly (per-step, not per-tick), compare with the live step interval from the 1-s trace (about one tile per second at distance 2-3), then write D23. Hunter thread paused here for today.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_33 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 33u32, "2026-10-21T22:25Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:25 Per-step hunter cooldowns at distance 1-3 (hunter-cool trace, slain-slot resets filtered): base 4597 steps, mean 1233 ms: diagonal 29% at 1909, slowed straight 32% at 1300, plain straight 38% at 663; K2 5434 steps, mean 1094: diagonal 8% at 1950, slowed 52% at 1300, plain 40% at 652. K2 removes the diagonals, and then the nearby slowdown catches half its steps; K3 (Canary's counter updates) did nothing alone because the base's diagonal steps are never slowed. The two Canary facts interact and were only tested apart. RULE D23, written before measuring: candidate K23 = K2 + K3 (getDistanceStep's step table and stepDuration updated on each knight move and think, both from monster.cpp), off by default, on top of pace. Same score as D20/D22: (i) in-reach share 21-35%, (ii) clears 57-85, (iii) hits 19.37-24.93, (iv) arms and hunting-ground kills unchanged; descriptive: gait at distance 1/2/3 against live 0.86 / 0.93 / 1.01. In sample, so passing only qualifies K23 for a fresh hunter block (PR-21) on a later day.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_34 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 34u32, "2026-10-21T22:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:30 Live uncleared hunter runs, examined (11 of 40 in PR-18/PR-19): each killed 1 or 2 hunters, then the survivors were last seen at 4-24% health and were in view in only 15-46 of the run's 149 one-second samples. So a live failure is wounded hunters fleeing (runHealth 10, Canary getDistanceStep with flee = true plus A* to the view-port edge, getPathSearchParams maxTargetDist = MAP_MAX_VIEW_PORT_X) and staying out of sight. That is why D20-D23 keep failing the clears band in one direction: every candidate changed distance-keeping, and whenever the model's hunters keep their distance the Canary way (K2: 99 clears, K23: 96) they die before they can flee away. The clears criterion is decided by the flee/escape mechanics (the model: step_away while below run_health, and a reset to spawn at escape_distance 8), so the next hunter rule should pair the distance-keeping facts with Canary's flee: the flee step table (getDistanceStep flee branch), flee paths to the view-port edge, and no return to spawn.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_35 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 35u32, "2026-10-21T22:31Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:31 RULE D24, written before any measurement of the candidates. Source: summoned monsters (the arena's /m) have no spawn: Monster::isInSpawnRange returns true when spawnMonster has expired (monster.cpp:2414-2417) and nothing walks them home; with no player in view a monster goes idle (monster.cpp:369). So a wounded hunter that flees out of the knight's view stays there, idle, with its low health, which matches the live uncleared runs (note 34). The model instead resets an escaped foe to fresh_foe at its spawn tile with full health (hunting_pack.spi flee branch, escape_distance 8). Candidates (off by default, on top of pace): F = escaped foes in a camp without Canary spawns stay where they fled with their health and lose the knight as target (no reset); K23F = K2 + K3 + F. Score as D20/D22/D23: (i) in-reach share 21-35%, (ii) clears 57-85, (iii) hits 19.37-24.93, (iv) arms and hunting-ground kills unchanged (the hunting ground uses respawning_like_canary, which keeps its own spawn rule, and must stay unchanged); descriptive: gait at distance 1/2/3. Tie: fewest changes, then clear time closest to 62.3 s. In sample, so passing only qualifies the candidate for a fresh hunter block (PR-21) on a later day.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_36 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 36u32, "2026-10-21T22:32Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:32 D23 VERDICT (note 33, mechanical): K23 (K2 + K3) fails: reach 47.7% (band 21-35), 96 clears (band 57-85), hits 24.46 (inside), 53.9 s, arms unchanged; gait 0.77 / 0.67 / 0.81 at distance 1/2/3 (live 0.86 / 0.93 / 1.01). Like K2 it raises the clears far above the band, which note 34 traces to the flee/escape mechanics; D24 (note 35) tests that next: F alone and K23F (job tibia-d24).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_37 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 37u32, "2026-10-21T22:37Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:37 D24 F alone (stay where fled in summoned camps): identical to the base (77 clears, hits 21.03, arms unchanged), so the escape reset never fires on the arena spot. The base model's uncleared runs already fail the live way: 23 of 100, kills 1 (10) or 2 (13), and the knight has no target for 85% of the run (the survivors are out of view), against live 11 of 40 with kills 1 (9) or 2 (2) and a hunter in view in 15-46 of 149 samples. So the base gets the clear rate right for the right reason, and K2/K23 break it by keeping the hunters in view until they die (96-99 clears). Implication for the next hunter rule: a distance-keeping fix has to leave wounded hunters able to get out of view, i.e. it must pair K2's step table with Canary's flee (getDistanceStep flee branch plus the A* to the view-port edge, note 34), not with the escape reset. K23F is scoring now for completeness.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_38 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 38u32, "2026-10-21T22:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:40 Design for the next hunter candidate (D25, rule still to be written before scoring), from source: a fleeing Canary monster (health <= runHealth) calls getDistanceStep(flee = true), whose table adds the flee-only moves (sideways and, as a last resort, toward the player), and paths with getPathSearchParams' fleeing branch: maxTargetDist = MAP_MAX_VIEW_PORT_X = 11 (client view 8 + 3), keepDistance, no clear-sight requirement (monster.cpp:2683-2688, map_const.hpp:12-14). The model flees with terrain.step_away (strictly improving tiles only) and resets at escape_distance 8, which never fires on the arena spot (D24 F). Candidate pairing per note 37: K2's step table for distance-keeping plus Canary's flee (the flee branch of the same table, stepping until 11 tiles away, then idle where it stands), so wounded hunters can still leave the knight's 15x11 screen while healthy ones keep their distance the Canary way. Score as D20-D24 (reach 21-35%, clears 57-85, hits 19.37-24.93, arms unchanged; gait descriptive).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_39 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 39u32, "2026-10-21T22:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:41 D24 VERDICT (note 35, mechanical): no candidate passes. F alone is identical to the base (77 clears, hits 21.03; the escape reset never fires on the arena spot). K23F = K23 (96 clears, outside 57-85; hits 24.46), so F adds nothing to it either. Today's hunter chain D20-D24 is closed with every candidate rejected and nothing built into src; what it established is in notes 19-38: the knight's chase matches, model hunters walk slower at 2-3 tiles because of diagonal-first steps and the x2 slowdown, and the base fails clears the live way; the next candidate (D25, design in note 38) pairs Canary's step table with Canary's flee so wounded hunters still leave the screen.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_40 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 40u32, "2026-10-21T22:42Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:42 RULE D25, written before any measurement. Candidate K25 = K2 + K3 + Canary's flee step (scratch patch, off by default, on top of pace): while health < run_health the hunter steps by getDistanceStep's flee table (monster.cpp:1733-2222 with flee = true: the distance-keeping moves, then the flee-only sideways moves regardless of the offset sign, then the diagonals, then a step toward the player as a last resort), instead of terrain.step_away. Left out on purpose: Canary's A* fallback (getPathTo with keepDistance up to MAP_MAX_VIEW_PORT_X = 11) when the table gives no step; if K25 fails on clears, that fallback is the next candidate. Score as D20-D24: (i) reach 21-35%, (ii) clears 57-85, (iii) hits 19.37-24.93, (iv) arms and hunting-ground kills unchanged; gait descriptive. In sample, so passing only qualifies K25 for a fresh hunter block (PR-21).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_41 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 41u32, "2026-10-21T22:49Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:49 D25 VERDICT (note 40, mechanical): K25 (K2 + K3 + Canary's flee step table) fails: 96 clears (band 57-85), hits 24.45, arms unchanged, hunting-ground kills 13.18 (trolls also flee through the patched branch; within band). The flee table alone does not let wounded hunters leave the knight's view in the model, as D25 anticipated. The next candidate is the part D25 left out: Canary's A* flee path (getPathTo with the fleeing search params: keepDistance, maxTargetDist 11, no clear-sight requirement) when the table gives no step, i.e. a path search away from the knight rather than one greedy step. That needs a keep-distance path search in terrain (the model has route_between for approach paths), which is next session's work. Hunter-gap chain for today: D20-D25, every candidate rejected, nothing built; established facts in notes 19-40.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_42 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 42u32, "2026-10-21T22:51Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:51 Design detail for the A* flee candidate (next session; from reading terrain.spi, no compile): route_between (terrain.spi:2396) is a bit-parallel BFS over the arena region's open tiles that returns the first step from 'from' toward a target (GoEast/West/South/North, Arrived, NoRoute), already used by routed_step for approach paths. A Canary-like flee needs a target tile first: the open tile nearest the hunter by BFS (same region bitmap, taken tiles excluded) whose Chebyshev distance from the knight is at least MAP_MAX_VIEW_PORT_X = 11, else the farthest reachable one; then route_between toward it gives the step. Canary's keepDistance/fullPathSearch = false also stops the search early at the first qualifying tile. Cost: one extra BFS per fleeing hunter step (a handful per clear), cheap. Score it under a new rule (D26) on the same criteria as D20-D25, as K2 + K3 + table flee + path flee.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_43 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 43u32, "2026-10-21T22:52Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:52 CORRECTION to note 34 (and the motivation of D24/D25; their rejections stand): reading an uncleared live log in full (PR-19 run 2) shows the knight chased one hunter about 25 tiles east (1932 -> 1957,1354), killed it at 21.8 s, and from then on saw nothing ('attacking nothing', every 'seen at' line 'none') for the rest of the run. The 'survivors last seen at 4-24% health' in note 34 were the hunter being killed in its last seconds, not wounded survivors fleeing. The live failure is the one README already describes (PR-14 post-hoc): the knight chases a distance-keeping hunter far along the road, its companions fall out of view, and after the kill it stands with nothing in sight. So the clears criterion is about how far a chased hunter leads the knight from the others, which is distance-keeping again; K2/K23 raising clears to 96-99 means the Canary step table lets the model's knight catch its target sooner or nearer, which fits K2's higher reach share. The flee-path design (notes 38, 42) loses its motivation; the next hunter step is to measure, live and in the model, how far from the start the first kill happens and how many hunters are still in view at that moment.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_44 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 44u32, "2026-10-21T22:53Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 18:53 The first chase decides a live hunter run (PR-18/PR-19, the 'seen at' line just before the first 'Loot of a hunter'): uncleared runs make their first kill 22.5 tiles from the start tile on average (14, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 23, 25, 25, 26, 29) with 1.18 hunters in view just before it (essentially only the target); cleared runs 16.2 tiles (2 to 33) with 2.38 in view. So a run fails when the first target leads the knight far from its companions before dying. Model side next (after the build): the same two numbers per run from a trace (knight's Chebyshev distance from its start at the first kill; hunters within view range then), split by cleared. Any distance-keeping candidate (K2, K23) should be judged on this too: it raised clears to 96-99, so it should show shorter first chases or more hunters in view at the first kill.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_45 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 45u32, "2026-10-22T00:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:07 First-chase trace, model side (hunter-firstkill, read by note 44): base uncleared 23 runs, first kill 18.5 tiles from start with 1.26 hunters on screen (live 22.5 / 1.18, same pattern); base cleared 77, 14.5 tiles with 1.17 on screen vs live 16.2 / 2.38; K2: 99 clears, first kill only 5.2 tiles out with 2.78 on screen. So live, the companions follow the chase and are on screen at the first kill even 16 tiles from the start; the model's companions fall behind (base) or nobody leads the knight away (K2). Lead: the model's ranged monsters take chase steps only on think ticks (hunting_pack.spi: 'camp.ranged && stats.target_distance > 1 && thinks_now seed index now = false then p'), while Canary monsters walk their follow path continuously between thinks (eventWalk over listWalkDir) and re-path when the followed creature moves (onCreatureMove -> updateCreatureWalk). Hunting-ground join trace: the second cyclops arrives when the current fight is 8.6 s old in the model vs 10.2 s live (median 5, n 13: weak), in view 4.4 s vs 3.1 s before adjacency.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_46 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 46u32, "2026-10-22T00:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:07 RULE D26, written before any measurement: candidate C = ranged monsters chase continuously, as Canary's follow walk does (drop the thinks_now gate on the ranged chase branch; their step cost and the nearby slowdown unchanged), off by default, on top of pace. Score as D20-D25: (i) in-reach share 21-35%, (ii) clears 57-85, (iii) hits 19.37-24.93, (iv) arms and hunting-ground kills unchanged; descriptive: first-kill distance and hunters on screen (live cleared 16.2 / 2.38). In sample, so passing only qualifies C for a fresh hunter block (PR-21).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_47 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 47u32, "2026-10-22T00:09Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:09 Live reference for D26 (PR-18/19 1-s 'seen at' trace, written while D26 scores): companion hunters (every hunter except the nearest, matched between consecutive samples) change their distance to the knight by -0.089 tiles per second on average (n 1594; closer 29%, unchanged 47%, farther 24%) while the knight moves 0.89 tiles per second, i.e. live companions keep pace with the fight instead of falling behind. A model-side companion trace (same measure) is the next check for any chase candidate; D26 is read first on its rule's own criteria.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_48 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 48u32, "2026-10-22T00:12Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:12 D26 VERDICT (note 46, mechanical): C (ranged monsters chase continuously) fails: reach 44.8% (band 21-35), 97 clears (band 57-85); hits 22.71 per run (inside 19.37-24.93, closest to live 22.15 of any candidate today), arms unchanged, 53.5 s. Like every distance-keeping change today it pushes clears toward 100: once the model's hunters stay with the fight, the knight finishes them all. So the live 28% uncleared runs need something that separates a hunter from the group, which no single Canary movement fact tested today supplies; the first-kill and companion traces (running) say how C changes the first chase. Today's hunter chain D20-D26: seven candidates, all rejected in sample, nothing built.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_49 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 49u32, "2026-10-22T00:13Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:13 First-chase length decides clears (live vs base vs D26's C): first kill 20+ tiles from the start in 21 of 40 live runs (9 of them uncleared, 43%), 20 of 100 base runs (11 uncleared, 55%), 20 of 100 C runs (1 uncleared, 5%). C's typical chase matches live (cleared: 15.5 tiles, 1.93 hunters on screen vs live 16.2 / 2.38). Two separate gaps: (1) live first targets lead the knight on long chases about 2.6x as often as the model's (52% vs 20%), the part no candidate today changed; (2) on long chases live companions lose contact 43% of the time, base 55%, C 5%, so continuous chasing over-corrects. Next hunter rule should first explain (1): what makes a live target run 20+ tiles (its distance-keeping toward open road, the knight's ReachFirst choice of a far target, or the hunters' spread at the start); live logs give the target's path from the 'approach Hunter at' lines and the 1-s positions, cheap to read.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_50 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 50u32, "2026-10-22T00:13Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:13 Live long chases, first look (PR-18/19): the knight's first target is at the same two tiles whether the chase ends short or long (1930,1352 and 1933,1351 in 18 of 19 short and 20 of 21 long), so the knight's choice is not what makes a chase long. Long chases end far south (offset -5,+24 x4; -4,+29; -4,+32) or far east (+21,+4 x2; +25,+9 x2), so the target's flight path varies: a hunter keeping its distance sometimes runs down the road south or east and the knight follows. Next: the target's route in the model vs live (the model's hunters may be held by the arena's terrain bits or their path choice), via the 1-s positions of the target, for the next hunter rule.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_51 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 51u32, "2026-10-22T00:14Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:14 Companion trace, model side (read by note 47): change in a companion hunter's distance to the knight per 1 s: base +0.163 (n 3606, companions drift away), C +0.009 (n 4219, they hold), live -0.089 (n 1594, they close in slightly). So D26's continuous chase moves companions the right way and even stays a little short of live on this measure, yet clears overshoot (97 vs 72%); the clears gap therefore sits in how often a first chase becomes long (live 52% vs model 20%, note 49), not in how well companions keep up. C stays a good component for a future combined rule, paired with whatever makes live targets run 20+ tiles.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_52 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 52u32, "2026-10-22T00:14Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:14 RULE D27, written before measuring: candidate K23C = K2 + K3 + C (getDistanceStep's step table, stepDuration updated per call, and continuous chase between thinks: the three Canary movement facts that each moved one measure toward live today). Score as D20-D26: (i) reach 21-35%, (ii) clears 57-85, (iii) hits 19.37-24.93, (iv) arms and hunting-ground kills unchanged; descriptive: first-kill distance (live 20+ tiles in 52% of runs) and companions on screen. In sample, so passing only qualifies it for a fresh hunter block (PR-21).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_53 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 53u32, "2026-10-22T00:15Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:15 Live signature of a long first chase (PR-18/19 1-s trace, first approach to first kill): long chases (kill 20+ tiles out, n 21) last 25.5 s with the target adjacent only 24% of the time and the knight's net travel about 0.99 tiles per second, i.e. the target runs in a sustained line and the knight follows; short chases (n 19) last 18.5 s, target adjacent 32%, net travel 0.58 tiles per second, i.e. the fight circles near the start. The model-side equivalents (chase duration, adjacency, net travel) are cheap per-tick measures for the next session; together with notes 49-51 they define what a hunter rule must reproduce: a straight sustained flight about half the time, with companions that keep up only partly.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_54 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 54u32, "2026-10-22T00:18Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:18 Hunter-arena analog of the D21 bracket: at the first 'seen at' after 'policy starts' (40 PR-18/19 logs), hunters stand 0 tiles from the nearest summon-phase sighting 39 times, 1: 33, 2: 35, 3: 13 (mean 1.18), so they move more than cyclopses before the first sample, partly by keeping their distance from the arriving knight, and the summon listing covers only what the GM probe saw. If D21's opening rule is adopted, its hunter-arena effect should be scored as well (opening layout and first-chase length, notes 49-53).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_55 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 55u32, "2026-10-22T00:19Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:19 D27 VERDICT (note 52, mechanical): K23C (K2 + K3 + C) fails: 100 clears (band 57-85), hits 24.96 (just outside 19.37-24.93), arms unchanged; reach and first-chase numbers follow from its traces (descriptive). Combining the three facts that each moved one measure toward live drives clears to the ceiling. Today's hunter chain D20-D27: eight candidates, all rejected in sample, nothing built. The open question for the next session is unchanged and now precise: what makes about half of live first chases a straight 20+ tile flight (25.5 s, target adjacent 24%, net travel ~1 tile/s; notes 49-53), which no movement fact tested today reproduces.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_56 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 56u32, "2026-10-22T00:21Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:21 D27 first-kill trace (descriptive): K23C clears all 100 with the first kill only 5.2 tiles from the start and 2.85 hunters on screen, the same as K2 alone (5.2 / 2.78) and far from live (cleared 16.2 / 2.38). So the getDistanceStep table, as implemented in the scratch patch, makes the model's hunters hold their ground near the spawn instead of leading the knight away; whatever live hunters do to produce 20+ tile flights, the table in this form suppresses it. Checked at once by reading the patch: the K2 table replaces only the step in the distance < targetDistance branch, and at distance 4 the model still dances as Canary holds, so that is not the cause; why the table keeps hunters near the spawn stays open.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_57 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 57u32, "2026-10-22T00:22Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:22 Hypothesis for why the K2 table keeps hunters near the spawn (from reading the table, not yet measured): when the straight step away is blocked, getDistanceStep tries sideways moves that keep the distance (W/E with offsetx <= 0, N/S with offsety <= 0) before the diagonals, so a hunter slides around the knight instead of fleeing in a line; the base best_step accepts only strictly improving tiles, so it always runs away, which gives straight flights but in a slow diagonal gait. Live shows straight sustained flights in about half the runs (note 53), between the two. Testable next session with a per-step trace of the K2 hunter's move kind (straight away / sideways / diagonal) while distance < 4, against live, where sideways moves show as a 1-s position change that keeps the distance.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_58 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 58u32, "2026-10-22T00:22Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:22 Two last measures. (1) Live hunter moves within 3 tiles of the knight, per 1 s (n 2210): farther 31%, same distance but moved (sideways) 31%, still 12%, closer 27%. Sideways sliding is common live, so K2's sideways moves are live-like in themselves; note 57's hypothesis does not explain K2's short chases and stays open. (2) Model first-chase signature (chase-sig trace): base and C are identical (C changes only the companions): short chases 16.0 s, target adjacent 44%, net travel 0.86 tiles/s; long (n 20) 23.2 s, 47%, 0.95; live short 18.5 s, 32%, 0.58; long 25.5 s, 24%, 0.99. The model's knight is adjacent to its target almost twice as often during the first chase as live in both kinds, the same gap as the in-reach share (note 19), and its short chases travel further than live's (0.86 vs 0.58 tiles/s), so live short chases circle near the start more.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_59 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 59u32, "2026-10-22T00:25Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:25 Hunter move kinds within 3 tiles of the knight, per 1 s (hunter-movekinds trace; read against note 58's live 31 / 31 / 12 / 27 for farther / sideways / still / closer): base 35 / 28 / 7 / 30 (n 5779); K2 30 / 33 / 12 / 25 (n 6326). K2 matches live's per-second move mix almost exactly, yet its first chases are short (5.2 tiles) and it clears 99 of 100. So with Canary's step table the hunters' local behavior looks right, and the remaining hunter gap (long first chases, companions losing contact) lies in what the knight does: its pursuit or target choice (ReachFirst picks the nearest reachable; the live competitor's targeting and follow are client-driven). Next session's hunter work starts there: compare the knight's target switches and pursuit path, live vs model, with K2 + C as the hunter side.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_60 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 60u32, "2026-10-22T00:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:28 Knight target switches before the first kill: base 0 (100 of 100 runs without a switch), K2 0, live 0 (one engage line per run in all 40). Target commitment is ruled out: the model's knight, like the live one, follows its first target until it dies. State of the hunter gap at the end of 2026-10-21: with Canary's step table (K2) the hunters' per-second move mix matches live (note 59), targeting matches, the knight's chase speed matches (note 25), yet K2's first chases end 5.2 tiles out vs live 16.2 (cleared runs), and live first targets run 20+ tiles about half the time. Remaining candidates for the next session: the target's route (live long chases run south or east along open ground: compare with the arena's road/ground bits and the model's route choices), and the knight's pursuit start (follow walk from a stop, following_like_canary) in the first seconds, when the target decides its flight direction.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_61 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 61u32, "2026-10-22T00:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:30 Map edge ruled out for the hunter long-chase gap: only 3 of 40 live PR-18/19 runs took the knight beyond 30 tiles from the arena center (the model's arena_spot_radius), none of them uncleared, max 34; hunters were seen beyond 30 in 6 runs. (results/arena-region-r45.txt, the walkability dump at radius 45 listed as uncited in TIBIA note 19, is the reference for this and for the next route comparison, so it now has a use.)")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_62 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 62u32, "2026-10-22T00:33Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:33 KEY FINDING for the hunter gap (first-kill spot trace, base, 100 runs): all 20 of the model's long first chases end east (kill offsets from the start +20..+24, +4..+6), none south; live long chases split 10 south (-5,+24 x4; -4,+29; -4,+32; ...) and 11 east (+21,+4 x2; +25,+9 x2; ...), with 19 short (n 40). So the model is missing the southern flight route entirely, about half of live's long chases, which matches the 52% vs 20% long-chase gap (note 49). In results/arena-region-r45.txt (walkability, radius 45 around 1933,1350) the south route is the open ground west of the walled building (columns about -10..-6, rows +1..+34; the building's west wall at about column -5 from row +4 to +17). Next session, first: check the model's walkability bits and spawn-to-route geometry along that strip (terrain arena_spot_bits vs the dump, and where the model's hunters stand when the chase starts: live first targets at 1930,1352 and 1933,1351), then a rule (D28) for whatever separates the two. This replaces the pursuit/target leads of notes 59-60 as the first thing to check.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_63 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 63u32, "2026-10-22T00:42Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:42 follow-up to note 62, two diagnostic traces (base camp, 100 held-out runs each; scratch copies only, nothing in src): (1) terrain is not the cause: the model's arena_spot_bits decoded over rows +1..+36, columns -12..+30 match results/arena-region-r45.txt in all but 2 of 1,505 cells (row +22, columns +7..+8); the open south strip at columns -12..-6 exists in the model. (2) The flee tie-break is not the cause: terrain.best_step tries x+1 before x-1 when blocked (left = shifted 1 sy). Mirroring it for the hunter's step_away (W, both call sites) still gives 18 long first chases, all east (kills at (21,4) x9, (23,3) x5, (22,4) x3, (25,3) x1). (3) K2 (Canary's getDistanceStep table) gives 0 long first chases out of 100, which is why it clears 96. So the base model's long chases all come from one east pocket around (+21..+25,+3..+4). The ArenaSpot spawn trio (99,101 / 98,102 / 98,103) sits at the building's NW corner with its north wall one row south (row +4, columns -4..+4), so a fleeing hunter there has every south and diagonal step blocked. Next session, before writing D28: a per-tick trace of the long-chase target over its first 40 ticks, showing which branch moves it (flee step_away, spawn return, idle wander, or follow) and the tick where it turns east; and from the live logs, the first 10 positions of the 10 south-route targets. Scripts: scratchpad eval-bits.ps1, hunter-killspot-W.ps1.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_64 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 64u32, "2026-10-22T00:46Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:46 follow-up to note 63, cause of the model's long first chases found (per-tick path trace, base camp, 100 held-out runs; scratch copy, nothing in src). Of the long chases, 7 of 8 printed are the same trajectory tick for tick across different seeds (runs 4, 10, 12, 29, 33, 40, ...): the hunter spawned at (0,+2) from the knight's start steps to (1,4) by t10, the knight reaches (0,3) by t30 and lines up directly west on the same row, and from then on the hunter flees straight east along row +4 (just north of the building's north wall) with the knight adjacent behind it: x +2 at t30, +9 at t120, +19 at t200, killed at about (21,4) after 21-28 s. Cause: terrain.step_away only accepts steps that increase Chebyshev distance, and with sy = 0 its first choice (both = across) is straight east; nothing ever makes it leave the row, so the chase is one deterministic line into the east pocket. Live hunters leave that line: 10 of 21 live long chases end south (-5..-4,+24..+32), through the gaps in the wall row. So the gap is the deterministic, aligned flee step, plus seed-independence (the seed doesn't reach this path). D28 candidates for next session, to be written with the hunter score before measuring: (a) when the threat is aligned (sx = 0 or sy = 0), choose randomly among the distance-increasing steps, as Canary's getDistanceStep does with its random picks; (b) K2's table with only its random aligned picks, without its other changes (K2 as a whole kills every long chase: 0 of 100). Diagnostic to add: each candidate's long-chase share (live 52%) and south/east split (live 10/11). Scripts: scratchpad hunter-path.ps1 + read-path.ps1.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_65 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 65u32, "2026-10-22T00:46Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:46 D28 pre-registration (written before any D28 measurement; diagnostics in notes 62-64 only). Candidates on the all4 + pace camp (pins in EVENT-TIME note 40), hunter flee step only, knight unchanged: R = when the threat is aligned (sx = 0 or sy = 0) and more than one free step increases Chebyshev distance, pick uniformly among them with a draw keyed by (seed, tick, foe index, new purpose 21); otherwise the current best_step order. RK = K2's getDistanceStep table restricted to its random aligned picks. Score on the 100-run held-out block, as for D20-D27: pass = in-reach share 21-35%, clears 57-85 of 100, hits 19.37-24.93, arms and hunting-ground kills unchanged (|t| < 2 vs all4). Required diagnostics, reported but not gating: long first chase share (live 52%, n 40), south/east split of long chases (live 10/11), first chase seconds (live long 25.5 / short 18.5). Decision: build into src (off by default, builder named for the behavior) the passing candidate whose long-chase share is closest to 52%; tie-break, fewer changed lines; deadline end of the 2026-10-22 session; if none passes, record it and treat the aligned flee step as a known gap in the README fidelity table.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_66 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 66u32, "2026-10-22T00:51Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:51 D28-R diagnostic, run before the full score (scratch copy, killspot trace only, 100 held-out runs; this is not the D28 verdict, which needs the note-65 score): R (uniform pick among free distance-increasing steps when aligned, both step_away call sites in foe_turn) gives 0 long first chases and 100 of 100 runs cleared within 2,000 ticks, the same over-correction as K2. So any sidestep off the row lets the knight close the gap; the straight-line flee is the only thing that makes the model's chases long at all. Implication for D28: R almost surely fails the clears band (57-85). The live mix (52% long, half south) must come from something that keeps a fleeing hunter ahead even off the row: candidates are the hunter's step cost on diagonals vs the knight's (Canary's diagonal cost x3 on both sides vs our step_cost), or the knight's pursuit re-path delay when the target sidesteps (Canary re-paths on onCreatureMove, but its walk ticks lag). Next: a per-tick trace of R's first chase (gap per tick after a sidestep), compared with the live logs' gap after a direction change, before RK or any other D28 candidate is scored. Script: scratchpad hunter-killspot-R.ps1.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_67 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 67u32, "2026-10-22T00:53Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:53 D28 diagnostics, part 2 (killspot trace only, not the verdict): R's sidesteps are 2 of 3 diagonal, and geometry.step_cost charges diagonals x3, which alone could explain its over-correction. So R2 = R with straight sidesteps ((0,+-1) when the threat is on the row, (+-1,0) when it is on the column) instead of diagonals: 1 long first chase in 100, 100 of 100 cleared. Also over-corrected. So any flee step that does not increase distance lets the model's knight close in, whatever it costs. The only way the model produces long chases is the straight run, and live hunters turn and still stay ahead. The remaining candidates sit on the knight's side: its pursuit after the target turns (re-path delay, a step queued toward the old tile, or the cost of its own diagonal catch-up steps). First check next session, needing no compile: in the live logs, the knight-target gap over the 3 s after each change in the target's flee direction, then the same from a per-tick trace of R2. D28's candidates R and RK stay registered (note 65) but are expected to fail the clears band; a knight-side candidate needs its own pre-registration. Scripts: scratchpad hunter-killspot-R.ps1 / -R2.ps1.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_68 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 68u32, "2026-10-22T00:55Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 20:58 pointer for the note-67 live check: per-run live logs are %USERPROFILE%\\tibia-local\\arena-logs\\<yyyymmdd-hhmmss>-hunter-spiral-policy-<run>.log. Run numbers repeat across blocks (5 logs per run number), so pick each row's log by its results-TSV timestamp, within 5 s of the filename stamp, as scratchpad live-exposure.ps1 does; a bare '*-<run>.log' filter is ambiguous. The 40-run live set behind the 52% long / 10 south / 11 east split is most likely PR-18 + PR-19 (results\\arena-results-hunter-pr18.tsv + -pr19.tsv, 20 + 20 rows). Confirm by recomputing the 21 long / 19 short split from those 40 logs before the gap check. Sample lines read '[tibia-probe] seen at <kx>,<ky>: Hunter <hp>% <x>,<y>; ...', with the knight's tile, then each visible hunter's health and tile. Kills and the current target come from the periodic '[tibia-probe] ticks N ... kills K ... attacking Hunter <hp>% at x,y,z player at x,y,z' lines.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_69 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 69u32, "2026-10-22T02:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:07 path change for notes 62-63: the walkability dump is now generated at target/map/arena-region-r45.txt by apps/map/run.ps1 (CipSoft-map-derived, never published), and the live client scan lives at TIBIA_WORLD/scans/arena-spot-scan-r12.txt (TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE note 11).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_70 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 70u32, "2026-10-22T03:12Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:12 Live gap check (night N1a, no compile) and D29 pre-registration. Live, 40 PR-18+PR-19 first chases (21 long, 19 short; this confirms note 68's set), samples about 1 s apart: target adjacent (gap <= 1) 30%, gap >= 2 70%, gap >= 3 17%, mean 1.90. Target turns 106 (2.65 per run); after a turn the gap grows 1.78 -> 1.94 -> 2.03 -> 2.04 over 3 s. Model base trace (100 runs): gap <= 1 45%, >= 3 6%, mean 1.61, turns 13 (0.13 per run). Model R2 trace (straight sidesteps): turns 373 (3.73 per run, near live), but gap >= 3 only 6% and flat after turns (1.60 -> 1.67), with 1 long chase. So live targets turn often and still open leads the model's knight never allows. Knight-side fact on record: PC-4 measured the live knight's straight step at a median 558 ms (122 intervals, range 537-566), slower than the formula's 486 that the model's knight uses on grass; PC-4's decision text attributes the extra time to the probe's decision loop, which a chase goes through too. D29, written before any D29 run: candidates on the all4 + pace camp, knight chase steps only (player_approaches, both next_step_at sites): D29a, chase step = max(server step, 558 ms), the PC-4 median; D29b = D29a + R2 (aligned straight sidesteps, note 67); D29c = D29a + R (note 66). Score: the note-65 hunter score on the 100-run held-out block (in-reach 21-35%, clears 57-85, hits 19.37-24.93, arms and hunting-ground kills |t| < 2 vs all4). Required diagnostics: long-chase share (live 52%), south/east split (live 10/11), gap >= 3 share (live 17%), turns per run (live 2.65). Decision: build into src (off by default, named for the behavior) the passing candidate whose long-chase share is closest to 52%; tie-break, fewer changed lines; D28's R and RK are scored in the same pass as registered. If none passes by 06:00, N3 applies.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_71 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 71u32, "2026-10-22T03:43Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:43 No-compile measurement and D30 pre-registration. Knight movement while the first-chase target is 2+ tiles ahead, per ~1 s sample: live (40 PR-18+19 runs, 720 pairs) 0.93 tiles/s overall, 1.50 when moving, standing still in 38% of samples; model base (850 pairs) 1.30 overall, 1.31 when moving, still 1%. So the live knight is not slower per step; it stops for whole seconds mid-chase. That makes D29a (558 ms floor) unlikely to matter, since the model's moving pace is already slower than 558 ms per tile; it stays registered and is scored as written. Source: the live knight chases through the server's follow (competitor engage: chase mode + g_game.attack, apps/arena/competitor.spi 251-257), and Canary re-paths a follower only while it has a follow path (creature.cpp 472-477, onCreatureMove); otherwise it waits for onThink, which re-paths when walkUpdateTicks reaches 2000 or on forceUpdateFollowPath (creature.cpp 117-137). A knight standing adjacent has no path, so when the target steps away it stands until the next 2 s update. D30, written before any D30 run: F30 = in player_approaches' fresh-walk branch (the knight idle, the target out of reach), the knight's next step waits for its next follow update at phase + k x 2000 ms (phase per seed, uniform 0-1999, new rng purpose), instead of one step's cost; a knight already walking keeps re-pathing at once on target moves, as now. Candidates on the adopted bundle: F30, and F30 + R2. Score: note 65's hunter score; diagnostics: still share (live 38%), chase speed (live 0.93 tiles/s), gap >= 3 (17%), long-chase share (52%), turns per run (2.65). Decision as D29's (closest long-chase share among passers; tie-break, fewer changes). Scored after the D29 chain; N3's 06:00 stop covers D30 too.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_72 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 72u32, "2026-10-22T03:45Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:45 D30 support, measured before any D30 run (no compile): live knight stall lengths while the first-chase target is 2+ tiles ahead (same 40 runs, ~1 s samples): 177 stall episodes, 273 standing samples; 1 sample 110 (62%), 2 samples 43 (24%), 3 samples 19 (11%), 4 samples 5 (3%). 86% of stalls last 1-2 samples, which fits a follow update every 2 s with a uniform phase (expected stall 0-2 s). The longer tail fits blocked steps (Canary forces a re-path after a failed step) or a target that turns back. This is consistent with F30's mechanism, but the verdict is still the note-65 score.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_73 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 73u32, "2026-10-22T03:48Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:48 Scope of D30, measured before any D30 score (no compile): with the nearest wounded foe 2+ tiles away, the live knight is still in 100% of 1-s samples in PR-17's rules clears (20 logs), 89% in PR-20's retreat clears (moves are the competitor's own retreat steps, not follow), and 98% on the hunting ground (PR-15, 10 logs). The monsters come to the knight there, so follow-driven chasing is specific to the hunter arena. Prediction, written before the scores: F30 leaves the gated cyclops arms and the hunting-ground kills within their bands, and moves only the hunter-arena measures.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_74 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 74u32, "2026-10-22T03:50Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:50 Caveat for D30, written before any D30 score: within live, follow stalls are about as common in short first chases (19 runs: 6.5 stall seconds per run, 41% of 302 chase pairs) as in long ones (21 runs: 7.1 s, 36% of 418). Cleared 17 of 19 short vs 12 of 21 long. So stalls are a general feature of the live knight's chase, not what separates long chases from short ones. F30 may reproduce the stalls and the leads they open (gap >= 3) without moving the long-chase share to 52%. The note-65 score decides either way; the long share is a diagnostic.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_75 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 75u32, "2026-10-22T03:53Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:53 Where the south routes come from (no compile, 40 PR-18+19 runs). (1) The first target's spot when first wounded decides the long chase's direction: east-side targets (x 0..2, y 2..3; 17 runs) give 8 long-east, 0 south, 9 short; southwest targets (x -3..-5, y 4..14; 20 runs) give 10 long-south, 0 east, 10 short. (2) At the first sample after policy start (about 1 s in), live hunters stand at (-1,4) x39, (-1,3) x31, (-3,4) x27, (2,2) x13 (+ singles) from the knight's start. The model spawns them at (0,2), (-1,3), (-1,4) (ArenaSpot spawn points, Canary's /m ring scan order, manage_monster.lua createCreaturesAround). Two match. The third, nearest to the knight in the model, has stepped away live, to (-3,4) in about two thirds of runs or (2,2) in one third, before the policy's first act. (3) The knight targets the nearest hunter: live it is the (2,2) one (east chase) or (-1,3) (southwest, then south or short); in the model always (0,2), hence 20/20 east. Mechanism: the live knight is in the game and visible before its policy starts (login, then the policy-start delay that D21's +2.8 s term covers), and the ranged hunters keep their distance meanwhile; the model's knight acts at tick 0. Next: a rule (D31) for 'knight present but passive for P ms before the policy acts', with P taken from tonight's timeline lines (05:30 block, the same lines as D21's W), not fitted. It goes in after the block; it also bears on D21's builder design (note of 2026-10-21 17:53), which places the knight only after W, with no passive presence.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_76 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 76u32, "2026-10-22T03:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:54 Correction to note 75's mechanism, from the log stamps (40 runs): @t counts from client start; the login message arrives at a median 6.0 s (3.8-7.3), and the policy starts tens of ms later (e.g. 'last visit' @t+5970, boosted-creature texts @t+5986, then 'policy starts'). So the knight is not visible-but-passive for any real time. The hunters move earlier, between the summon and the knight's login, with no knight present: e.g. summoned and seen at 1931,1353 (-1,+4), first approached at 1930,1352 (-2,+3). That is exactly D21's mechanism (monsters idle-wander for W ms with no knight, then the knight is placed; rule of 2026-10-21 17:53). So D21's W also accounts for the hunter arena's first-target variety and the south routes. No separate D31: when D21 is scored with the measured W, its criterion (d) already scores the hunter arena (clears within band); the long-chase share and south/east split are added there as diagnostics.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_77 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 77u32, "2026-10-22T05:15Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 01:15 Interim scores under note 65 (adopted bundle, 100 held-out runs; d29-judge). D28: R fails (reach 46.2%, clears 100); RK fails (reach 43.9%; clears 85, hits 21.59, arms and kills hold). D29: P29 PASSES every criterion: reach 33.7% (band 21-35), clears 74 (57-85), hunter hits 23.66 (19.37-24.93), arms hold, hunting-ground kills hold. P29+R2 fails (reach 37.7%, clears 90, hits 25.94). P29+R still running. Correction to note 71: I wrote that the 558 ms floor 'is unlikely to matter'; it matters. Why is not yet traced; a guess, not checked: the floor slows the knight's approach steps that ran below 558 ms (straight steps on fast ground, the first step of each fresh walk), even though the average moving pace at gap >= 2 was 1.31 tiles/s. P29's path trace (queued) will show it. D29's verdict waits for P29+R; D30 (F30, F30+R2) is scored next.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_78 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 78u32, "2026-10-22T05:32Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 01:32 VERDICTS (notes 65, 70, 71; d29-judge on the adopted bundle, 100 held-out runs; band: reach 21-35%, clears 57-85, hits 19.37-24.93, arms and hunting-ground kills hold). D28 REJECTED: R (46.2%, 100 clears, 24.42) and RK (43.9%, 85, 21.59) fail on reach. D29: P29 PASSES (33.7%, 74, 23.66, arms and kills hold); P29+R2 fails (37.7%, 90, 25.94), P29+R fails on hits (34.0%, 76, 25.57). D30 REJECTED: F30 over-corrects (34.1%, 20 clears, 15.07 hits); F30+R2 fails (40.2%, 88, 27.59). F30's 20 clears suggest my stateless form (wait for the 2 s update whenever the knight was idle a tick) stalls far more often than Canary's 'has a follow path' state; a faithful version needs that state, so it stays a lead, not a retry tonight. Decision (D29): P29 is the only passer, so it is built into src off by default as chasing_at_the_live_probe_pace (chase step = max(server step, 558 ms), PC-4's median) once the last patch-based chain job has copied src. Rule N7: the 05:30 window runs PR-21 first (20 hunter runs; predictions 74 of 100 -> 11..18 of 20, hits 23.66), pre-registered in BENCHMARK and target/ledger before the window.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_79 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 79u32, "2026-10-22T05:35Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 01:35 P29 built into src, off by default (src/hunting_pack.spi): union chase_pace (ChasesAtTheServerPace, the default in all 3 camp constructors, or ChasesAtTheProbePace), live_chase_step_ms 558, builder chasing_at_the_live_probe_pace, chase_step_ms_at, and both player_approaches sites. The default arm reproduces the old step time exactly, so the pins should not move; a full build.ps1 is queued (tibia-p29-build) after the chain. Required diagnostics for P29 (note 70): long first chases 31 of 100 (base 20, live 52%), all 31 east (live 10 south / 11 east); target 3+ tiles ahead 11% of samples (base 6%, live 17%); knight still mid-chase 1% (live 38%). So P29 brings the hunter score into its bands and moves every shape measure toward live, but the south routes (D21, notes 75-76) and the follow stalls (a stateful F30, note 78) remain open. PR-21 tests P29's predictions in the 05:30 window (BENCHMARK PR-21; target/ledger PR-21-CLEARS and PR-21-HITS registered 05:33Z).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_80 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 80u32, "2026-10-22T08:02Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 04:02 P29 build check, partial and not yet green (tibia-p29-build2, 02:16-04:00, timed out at its 80-min cap on a saturated machine: several dotnet compilers, 2 GB free). Layout clean; 17 files ran: 15 green (hunt_sim 11/11, hunt, network, policy, lua and the small modules). gpu_sim 2 of 6 failed on a 300 s cell timeout. pack_sim 6 of 36 failed; the one detail printed is an expected-diagnostic test that got a dead compiler worker (GenServer call ended :normal) instead of its Unification failure, which is infrastructure, not P29. But the other five aren't detailed, so P29 isn't cleared yet. Unreached: rng, search, skills, standards, terrain, views, vitality, vocations, wire. Rerun queued as tibia-p29-build-rest2 (only those 11 files), guarded: it skips if it can't start by 04:40 and stops its own tree at 05:20, before the live block. If it isn't green by then, PR-21 still runs (it tests the live competitor, which P29 doesn't touch; the probes build from src at block start) and the check repeats after the block.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_81 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 81u32, "2026-10-22T09:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 05:28 P29 build check (src with chasing_at_the_live_probe_pace, off by default): pack_sim 36/36 and rng, search, skills, terrain, views, vitality, vocations, wire green (tibia-p29-build-rest2); gpu_sim 4/6, the two long cells (troll hunt + five cyclops clears, hunting ground routing) killed at the 300 s cell timeout while both heavy slots and other lanes' dotnet were busy. Rule: after the live block, rerun gpu_sim alone; green = build check closed; a timeout again on an idle machine = investigate as a P29 cost (the builder adds a branch per chase step).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_82 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 82u32, "2026-10-22T09:35Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 05:35 No-compile measurement (scratch stall-after-diagonal.ps1 on PR-18+19's 40 live logs; model-stall-origin.ps1 on the path traces), correcting the 'knight still 1% vs 38%' framing of notes 71/79. Notes 71/79 counted a 1-s pair as a stall when the gap was 2+ at its START; split by the gap one sample earlier, the live and model knights differ in two separate places. (1) The target steps away from an adjacent knight (gap <= 1 -> 2+): live knight still through the next second 141 of 144 (98%); model base 191/334 (57%), P29 278/406 (68%), F30 356/416 (86%). (2) Target already 2+ ahead: live still 73 of ~340 (21%); base 6/416 (1%), P29 6/392 (2%), F30 173/889 (19%). Diagonal steps are not the cause: live stalls start after a straight 1-s move 11% (24/228), after a diagonal one 5% (2/42). No 'Sorry, not possible' or 'not enough room' text in any of the 40 hunter logs, so failed steps (forceUpdateFollowPath) don't occur. Canary source (read only): the A* start node already adjacent counts as found (map.cpp 915-919), so hasFollowPath stays true at adjacency (note 19 holds); Player::goToFollowCreature skips for 2 s after a FAILED path (player.cpp 6030-6040, lastFailedFollow); a fresh walk from standing waits stepDuration x lastStepCost from the re-path, where lastStepCost is the cost of the knight's PREVIOUS step, 3 after a diagonal (creature.cpp 1493-1501, 443-451, RespectDelay; ImmediateWhenReady only for client single-step input, game.cpp 4141-4348). The model's fresh walk charges the NEXT step's cost (hunting_pack.spi player_approaches, the delays_a_fresh_walk branch). F30's (2) matches live, but F30 failed the score (note 78), so the stall shape alone does not fix the hunter score.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_83 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 83u32, "2026-10-22T09:36Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 05:36 RULE D31, written before any F31 measurement. Candidate F31 (one Canary fact, note 82; scratch flag F31 in d20-patch.ps1, sites checked on a copy, not compiled): the knight remembers whether its last step was diagonal (hunter field last_step_diagonal, set in hunter_steps), and a fresh walk from standing waits the chase step times that step's cost (x3 after a diagonal), from Canary's getEventStepTicks RespectDelay, instead of the next step's cost. Runs: F31 on the bundle as it stands after PR-21 (P29 included if PR-21 adopts it, else F31 and P29+F31 both). Score: note 65 on 100 held-out runs (reach 21-35%, clears 57-85, hits 19.37-24.93, cyclops arms and hunting-ground kills hold), via d29-run/d29-judge. Pass = every criterion. Several passers: take the one whose hits are nearest 22.15 (band midpoint); tie = fewer flags. A passer is built into src off by default with a builder, then a live pre-registration like PR-21. Required diagnostic, not a gate: model-stall-origin.ps1 (1) leaving-adjacency still share, prediction written now: rises from P29's 68% toward live 98%, and (2) stays near 2% (F31 changes only fresh walks from adjacency). Deadline: first compile slot after the 2026-10-22 night (not tonight: the live block holds 05:30-06:31 and a 100-run score takes about 40 min plus build).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_84 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 84u32, "2026-10-22T09:38Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 05:38 Correction to note 83, from a no-compile bound before any F31 run (scratch f31-reach.ps1 on the 100 ms path traces): in P29's traces the target leaves an adjacent knight 406 times; the knight's last step before it was diagonal in 94 (23%), and all 94 already stall through the next second (the model's post-step x3 wait covers them); after a straight last step 184 of 312 stall. F31 changes only fresh walks after a straight step whose next step is diagonal (x3 -> x1), so it can only LOWER the leave-adjacency still share, at most to the 68% ceiling it already has. Note 83's diagnostic prediction ('rises toward live 98%') is withdrawn and replaced: F31 lowers or keeps (1) and leaves (2) near 2%. The D31 gate (note-65 score) stands as written; F31 is a source-faithful fix, not a stall fix. What the live 98% implies, by arithmetic: with a fresh-walk wait of D after the target leaves, the chance the knight is still at the next 1-s sample is about min(1, D/1 s); 98% needs D >= ~1 s nearly always, while Canary's documented path (re-path on the target's move, then step after stepDuration x lastStepCost, 558 ms straight) gives ~56-68%, which is what the model shows. So something in the live chain adds ~0.5 s or more before the first step after adjacency; candidates not yet checked: the async pathfinder round trip (goToFollowCreature_async + dispatcher), the probe sampling the client view rather than the server, or the client's own chase/attack packets. Next step, no compile: the probe's 'seen at' cadence and any per-step timestamps in a PR-21 log (written tonight) to time the first knight step after the target leaves.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_85 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 85u32, "2026-10-22T09:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 05:40 Mechanism found for the live knight's stalls (source reading, no compile; answers note 84). The competitor turns chase mode OFF whenever its target is adjacent: engage (target_code = 2) (apps/arena/competitor.spi 292, 317), client.chasing sends DontChase (client.spi 107-109), and Canary's Player::setChaseMode(false) calls setFollowCreature(nullptr) plus stopWalk (player.cpp 6059-6080). So an adjacent live knight follows nothing, and the target's step away triggers no server re-path. The next probe tick (100 ms) sees target_code 2 and turns chase back on: setFollowCreature only sets isUpdatingPath (creature.cpp 1053-1056), serviced in the player's onThink, every 1000 ms at a fixed per-creature phase (creature.cpp 134-137, game.cpp 7429); the new path then starts with RespectDelay, one full step (stepDuration x lastStepCost) before the first move. First step after the target leaves = probe tick + U(0, 1 s) + ~558 ms; the chance the knight is still at the next 1-s sample is about 0.90 by that arithmetic (live 98%, model base 57%, P29 68%), and short re-adjacencies mid-chase produce part of the 21% 'already ahead' stalls. Differences from F30 (rejected): F30 waited for a 2 s grid on every idle tick and put the step itself on the grid; Canary waits for the next 1 s think, then a full step, and only after the knight stood adjacent.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_86 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 86u32, "2026-10-22T09:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 05:40 RULE D32, written before any F32 measurement (mechanism note 85). Candidate F32 (scratch flag F32 in d20-patch.ps1, sites checked on a copy, not compiled): a fresh walk from standing (the existing ApproachesAfterAFreshWalkDelay branch of player_approaches, i.e. the knight was idle at least one tick, in the hunter arena almost always adjacent with chase off) starts at the knight's next 1 s think, at a per-run phase in whole ticks (rng purpose 23), plus the existing chase step cost. Candidates, each on the bundle as it stands after PR-21: F32; P29+F32 (when PR-21 does not adopt P29). Score: note 65 on 100 held-out runs (reach 21-35%, clears 57-85, hits 19.37-24.93, arms and hunting-ground kills hold) via d29-run/d29-judge. Pass = every criterion; several passers: hits nearest 22.15, then fewer flags. Passer: built into src off by default with a builder, then a live pre-registration. Diagnostics, not gates, predictions written now: model-stall-origin.ps1 (1) leave-adjacency still share 85-95% (live 98%), (2) already-ahead still share up from 1-2% (live 21%), and the long-chase share (live 52%) reported. D31 (F31) stays registered and is scored in the same batch; if F32 passes and F31 fails, F31 is dropped; both pass: F32+F31 is scored once more before any build. Deadline: first compile slot after the 2026-10-22 night.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_87 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 87u32, "2026-10-22T09:44Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 05:44 PC-7 (live timing check of note 85's mechanism) is pre-registered in docs/BENCHMARK.md (PC-7, written 05:45): instrumentation, scenario, predictions and the decision that applies to D32 live there. Its measure waits for an eoie event-latency verb (requested from eoie 05:46).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_88 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 88u32, "2026-10-22T09:46Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 05:46 Support for note 85's second claim (no compile, same 40 live logs): of the 73 live 'already ahead' still seconds, the sample before was adjacent (gap <= 1) in 37, the knight was moving in 25, already still in 8, unknown 3. So about half continue a stall that began when the target left adjacency (chase off -> next 1 s think -> full step, often longer than one sample); the 25 after a moving second are not explained by the mechanism and stay open. PC-7's measure now names eoie's planned EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES latency commands (table logs --latency, table query --quantile/--share-at-least; eoie 05:47: lands after its pending releases).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_89 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 89u32, "2026-10-22T10:14Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 06:14 PR-21 RESULT (runs 05:30-06:09, all 20 counted, no crash, no death). Clears 19 of 20, outside the pre-registered 11..18 band (central 95% for p 0.74): PR-21-CLEARS FAILS. Hits 23.8 per run (CI 20.42-27.18) against 23.66, t 0.09: PR-21-HITS holds. By rule D29 + N7 (both must hold), chasing_at_the_live_probe_pace stays OFF by default; src unchanged. The live arena cleared far more often than P29 predicts (95% vs 74%) and more than the adopted bundle (77%); PR-18 and PR-19 cleared 14 and 15 of 20 (29 of 40, 72.5%), so live clears vary between blocks more than one binomial rate allows, or something in the live arena changed since 2026-10-21 (to check: the server and client builds, the datapack, the competitor's source snapshot). No compile overlapped any live run: main's four compile windows were granted 06:09:52.9-06:09:54.5, after PR-21's release at 06:09:52, and stopped about 06:10:25, before the D21 window (main, 06:14). eoie friction: experiment analyze reported reject_h0=no from a one-sided p_at_most while the registration's test was the two-sided band 11..18, so the band verdict was read by hand (EOIE request: band tests in experiment register/analyze). Next: the clear rate is now the open hunter-arena gap. D31/D32 slow the knight's chase, which pushes model clears DOWN, against this result; recorded before any D32 score.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_90 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 90u32, "2026-10-22T10:16Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 06:16 Lead for PR-21's 19/20 clears (no compile, observation only, no causal claim yet): the knight logged in later after client start in PR-21: 'Your last visit' text at a median @t+7.3 s (range 4.9-15.9) against PR-18 3.9 s (3.8-5.1) and PR-19 4.3 s (3.8-6.0). PR-21's logs also carry the summon-to-policy wall gap (the hunters' wander time before the knight acts): median 9.1 s (6.9-17.8); PR-18/19 logs predate that line. Hunters wander for longer before the knight arrives, which is D21's W mechanism (notes 75-76), so the hunter arena's clear rate may depend on the client's login delay, which varies with machine load (PR-21 ran right after a saturated night). The competitor's engage/chase code is unchanged since PR-19 (the staged apps/arena diff is a formatter re-lay plus retreat-only helpers; hunter runs use retreat-at 0). Test, to pre-register with D21's W: score the hunter arena at PR-21's wander times (W21 with per-run W from the 20 logs) vs PR-18/19's; if clears move toward 95% at the longer W, the clear gap is the opening, not the chase.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_91 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 91u32, "2026-10-22T10:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 06:17 RULE D33, written before any score (lead: note 90). Question: does the hunters' wander time before the knight acts explain the live hunter clear rate (PR-18/19 29 of 40, login ~4 s; PR-21 19 of 20, login ~7.3 s, summon-to-policy 9.1 s)? Runs: the hunter arena on the adopted bundle with scratch W21 (idling_before_the_knight, D20_FLAGS W21, d29-run) at D21_W = 4000 and 9100 ms, 100 held-out runs each, plus the existing W = 0 baseline (77 of 100). Bands (Wilson 95%): PR-18/19 29/40 -> 57.2%-83.9%; PR-21 19/20 -> 76.4%-99.1%. Verdict: the opening explains the gap when clears at W 4000 fall in the PR-18/19 band AND clears at W 9100 fall in the PR-21 band AND hits at both stay in 19.37-24.93. Then the next model step is per-run W from each live log's login delay (not a constant), pre-registered separately. Neither inside: the opening is not the explanation; W stays a cyclops-arm question only (D21). One inside: inconclusive, report the curve (clears at 0, 4000, 9100). Deadline: first compile slot after the night, in the same batch as d31-d32 (W21 jobs serialized after them).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_92 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 92u32, "2026-10-22T10:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 06:29 AMENDMENT to D33 (note 91), before any D33 run: W21's D21_W is the D21-defined W (summon-to-policy wall gap + 2800 ms, EVENT-TIME note 47, d21-w.ps1), and note 91's 4000/9100 used the raw gap without the offset. Corrected runs: D21_W = 11900 (PR-21: raw gap median 9.06 s + 2.8 s) and 8700 (PR-18/19: their logs lack the timeline line; estimated from their login text arriving ~3.2 s earlier than PR-21's, 4.1 vs 7.3 s median; an estimate, labelled as such in the verdict). Bands, hit band and verdict rule unchanged. For reference, tonight's cyclops calibration gave W 9700 (EVENT-TIME note 61).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_93 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 93u32, "2026-10-22T10:35Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 06:35 P29 build check CLOSED by note 81's rule: gpu_sim rerun alone (job tibia-gpu-sim-rerun, 06:29-06:35, after the live block) 6 of 6 green in 262 s; the two cells that hit the 300 s cell timeout overnight ran fresh and passed (memo_hits 4 = the four that had passed), WGSL compile 152 s and 179 s on the Quadro K1100M. With tibia-p29-build-rest2 (pack_sim 36/36, rng, search, skills, terrain, views, vitality, vocations, wire), every test file is green on src with the P29 builder off by default. The overnight failures were machine load, not P29.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_94 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 94u32, "2026-10-22T10:45Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 06:45 D33 free data point (from the D21 score job, W21 at W 9700, adopted bundle, 100 held-out runs): hunter clears 89 of 100, hits 23.35, against W 0's 77 and 21.03. 89% sits inside PR-21's band (76.4-99.1%) and above PR-18/19's (57.2-83.9%). So wander time before the knight acts does raise model clears, in the direction of PR-21; D33's two registered runs (W 8700, 11900; queued) decide it. Caveat for D33's reading: W21 also delays the knight on the hunting ground (kills 9.19 fail), so a D33 pass would call for a per-arena W, not the bundle-wide flag.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_95 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 95u32, "2026-10-22T10:48Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 06:48 D31 interim (rule note 83, amended 84): F31 alone FAILS on reach. Job tibia-d3132-F31 (06:32-06:45, 100 held-out runs, adopted bundle): in-reach share 46.7% (band 21-35), clears 79 (57-85), hits 20.70 (19.37-24.93), cyclops arms hold (t within 1.61), retreat steps 11.7, hunting-ground kills 13.19 hold. Reach is about where the bundle without it sits (43.8% in the 2026-10-21 base trace, which predates the pace adoption, so the comparison is approximate; P29 33.7%, W21 at 9700 45.8%), so F31 neither causes nor fixes the reach excess. P29B+F31 is running next in the same batch; D31's verdict is final when it lands (pass = every criterion; then hits nearest 22.15).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_96 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 96u32, "2026-10-22T10:50Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 06:50 D31 diagnostic for F31 (path trace job tibia-d3132-F31-path, model-stall-origin.ps1): target leaves an adjacent knight 325 times, knight still through the next second 189 (58%; base 57%, live 98%); already-ahead stalls 0 of 399 (live 21%). As note 84's corrected prediction said: F31 keeps (1) and leaves (2) near zero; the chase-mode mechanism (D32, F32) is the candidate for the stall gap.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_97 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 97u32, "2026-10-22T10:51Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 06:51 D31 VERDICT (rule note 83, amended 84): REJECTED, no candidate passes. F31: reach 46.7% (note 95). P29B+F31 (job tibia-d3132-P29B-F31, 06:32-06:50, 100 held-out runs): reach 37.1% (band 21-35) fails; clears 75, hits 22.22 (closest yet to the 22.15 midpoint), retreat steps 11.1, cyclops arms and hunting-ground kills (11.75) hold. F31 adds about 3 points of reach on either base (P29 alone 33.7% -> 37.1%), so the previous-step cost is a source fact the hunter score does not reward; it stays a scratch flag. D32 (F32, P29B+F32) and D33 (W 8700, 11900) continue in the same batch.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_98 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 98u32, "2026-10-22T10:52Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 06:52 D31 diagnostic for P29B+F31 (path rows hunter-path-P29B,F31-rows.tsv; the path runner keeps the comma in the name): leave-adjacency still 282 of 406 (69%; P29 alone 68%, live 98%), already-ahead stalls 0 (live 21%). Same as F31 alone: the previous-step cost leaves the stall shape where it was.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_99 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 99u32, "2026-10-22T10:56Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 06:56 D32 interim (rule note 86): F32 alone FAILS on clears. Job tibia-d3132-F32 (06:51-06:55, 100 held-out runs, adopted bundle): in-reach 31.5% (band 21-35, the first candidate tonight inside it without P29), clears 53 (57-85) fails, hits 20.83 (19.37-24.93), cyclops arms hold (t within 1.61), retreat steps 11.7, hunting-ground kills 12.72 hold. Direction as note 89 predicted before any D32 score: the chase-mode restart delay slows the knight and pushes clears down. P29B+F32 is next in the batch; D32's verdict is final when it lands.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_100 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 100u32, "2026-10-22T10:56Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 06:56 D32 diagnostics for F32 (path job tibia-d3132-F32-path, model-stall-origin.ps1), both inside note 86's written predictions: leave-adjacency still 387 of 427 (91%; predicted 85-95%, live 98%, every earlier model 57-69%); already-ahead stalls 122 of 871 (14%; predicted up from 1-2%, live 21%). So the chase-mode mechanism (note 85) reproduces the live stall shape, the first candidate to do so, yet the model's clears fall to 53 (live 72.5-95%). Stalls and clears are now in tension: with live-like stalls the model knight catches too few hunters, so something else in the live arena makes clearing easier (candidates already named: the opening/W, note 90 and D33; the hunters' own pace). Recorded before P29B+F32 and D33 land.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_101 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 101u32, "2026-10-22T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 07:00 D32 VERDICT (rule note 86): REJECTED, no candidate passes. F32 (note 99): reach 31.5% in band, clears 53 fail. P29B+F32 (job tibia-d3132-P29B-F32, 06:55-07:00): reach 26.6% in band, but clears 19 (57-85), hits 17.27 (19.37-24.93) and hunting-ground kills 10.99 fail; arms and retreat steps (11.1) hold. Both slow the knight's chase, and stacking P29's 558 ms floor on the chase-mode restart collapses clears. F32 stays a scratch flag: it is the only model of the live stall shape (note 100), so it is the base for D33's follow-up if the opening (W) restores clears. D33 (W 8700, 11900, on the bundle without F32) still runs.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_102 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 102u32, "2026-10-22T11:11Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 07:11 D33 VERDICT (rule note 91, amended 92): INCONCLUSIVE (one condition inside). W 8700: clears 89, outside PR-18/19's band 57.2-83.9% (hits 24.06 in band). W 11900: clears 87, inside PR-21's band 76.4-99.1% (hits 22.94 in band). The curve, as the rule asks: clears 77 at W 0, 89 at 8700, 89 at 9700 (D21 job), 87 at 11900; in-reach 44.9-46.3% throughout; hunting-ground kills fall to 9.6-10.0 under W21 (bundle-wide flag). Reading: any wander before the knight acts lifts model clears from 77 to about 88 and then saturates, so the login-delay difference between PR-18/19 (est. W 8700) and PR-21 (W 11900) cannot by itself produce 72.5% vs 95%. Next by the day protocol (TIBIA note 27): PR-22 decides which live rate the model should target before any further candidate is scored.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_103 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 103u32, "2026-10-22T11:13Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 07:13 Login delay vs clears across every live hunter block on disk (spiral-policy, no compile; descriptive, no causal claim): PR-13 5/10 clears, median login @t+4.4 s; PR-14 7/10, 8.5 s; PR-18 14/20, 3.9 s; PR-19 15/20, 4.3 s; PR-21 19/20, 7.3 s. Runs with login < 6 s 39 of 55 cleared (71%), >= 6 s 21 of 25 (84%, 19 of the 25 from PR-21). PR-14 had an even later login than PR-21 and cleared 7 of 10, so login delay alone does not track the rate (D33 found the same in the model: clears saturate once the hunters wander). PR-22 records login and summon-to-policy for every run; its rule (BENCHMARK) decides which live rate stands.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_104 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 104u32, "2026-10-22T12:14Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:14 Link to EVENT-TIME's D35 (notes 70-72 there), recorded before PR-22: the 2.5 s protected lead (cyclops and hunters random-step between login and the policy start because the knight is login-protected until its first attack) applies to the hunter arena too, which summons its hunters the same way. In D35's score job (W21 at W 2500, adopted bundle, 100 held-out runs) the hunter arena gave 85 clears and 24.37 hits, against 77 and 21.03 without the lead (in-reach share 43.4%, above the 21-35% band, like the bundle without P29 at about 44%). So the protected lead moves both hunter measures toward PR-21's live 95% clears and 23.8 hits. Which live clear rate the model should target is still PR-22's call (day protocol, TIBIA note 27); if PR-22 lands at 19 or more clears, D35's lead is the first candidate to score against that band, together with D33's finding that the opening saturates clears near 88.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_105 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 105u32, "2026-10-22T15:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 11:28 PR-22 result (BENCHMARK): 14 of 20 cleared, k <= 16, so PR-21 (19 of 20) was an outlier and the hunter target is PR-18/19 rate 0.725 (live without PR-21: 43 of 60). The adopted bundle predicts 77 of 100, consistent with it. Login delay does not explain clears here (cleared runs logged in after 5.47 s, uncleared 6.34 s). Hits taken 20.45 per run vs the bundle 21.03. Per TIBIA note 27, hunter-arena candidates may now be scored against 0.725.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_106 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 106u32, "2026-10-22T15:37Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 11:37 D37 RULE, written before any score. Basis: PR-22 set the hunter target at 0.725 (note 105); D32 named F32 (chase-mode restart, the only model of the live stall shape) as the base for a follow-up if the opening restores clears; D35 (EVENT-TIME note 72) showed the 2.5 s protected lead lifts clears 77 to 85. Candidate: W21 at W 2500 plus F32 on the adopted bundle, one score job (d21-score -W 2500 -Flags W21,F32, 100 held-out runs). Pass needs ALL of: hunter in-reach 21-35 percent, clears 57-85, hits 19.37-24.93, cyclops arms hold, hunting-ground kills hold (score d_kills), and D35s retreat criteria (b) Welch abs t <= 2 vs 11.05, (c1) 32.26-53.43 percent, (c2) 42-63 percent. Pass: pre-register a live block (PR-24) before adoption; nothing joins the bundle on the score alone. Any failure: REJECTED, F32 and W21 stay scratch flags, and the verdict names which criteria failed. Prediction written now: clears near 60 (F32 53 plus the lead), c1 near D35s 32, so c1 is the likeliest failure. Runs after the LIB-MIGRATION verify job.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_107 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 107u32, "2026-10-22T16:44Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:44 D37 VERDICT (rule note 106): REJECTED, 3 of 8 criteria fail. Job tibia-d37-w2500 (W21 at W 2500 plus F32 on the adopted bundle, 100 held-out runs). Pass: hunter in-reach 33.3 percent (21-35; computed from hunter-trace-W21F32 rows the d20-judge way), clears 79 (57-85), cyclops arms (t PR-11 1.19, PR-12 0.36, PR-16 0.97/0.17, PR-17 1.45/-1.20), (b) retreat steps 13.19 vs 11.05 Welch t 1.20, (c2) first pin 46 percent (42-63). FAIL: hunter hits 26.13 (19.37-24.93), hunting-ground kills 11.17 (score d_kills false), (c1) first retreat from 1933,1350 28 percent (32.26-53.43), the failure the rule predicted. Reading: the lead plus the chase-mode restart puts clears and in-reach where live is (the first candidate to hit both with the live stall shape) but over-counts hits by about 1.2 per run above the band top and under-counts hunting-ground kills, as W21 did before (D33: kills 9.6-10.0 at long W). F32 and W21 stay scratch flags; nothing is adopted.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_108 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 108u32, "2026-10-22T16:45Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:45 PAUSED with a mechanical resume condition. State: against PR-22s target (0.725) the adopted bundle holds clears (77) and hits (21.03); the open hunter gap is the in-reach share (bundle about 44 percent vs 21-35) with the live stall shape (leave-adjacency 98 percent live). Every source-backed candidate named so far is scored and rejected (P29, F31, F32, P29B+F32, W21 at 8700/11900, D35 W21 2500, D37 W21+F32). Resume when either holds: (1) eoie ships the sequences verb (EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES, latency kind) so PC-7s pre-registered measure runs on PR-22s 20 logs (knight first step after the target leaves), or (2) a new Canary source fact names a mechanism for the in-reach gap not yet scored. Either resume writes its rule here before any score.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_109 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 109u32, "2026-10-22T19:50Z", "tibia-lane", "RESUMED by condition (2) of note 108: a new Canary source fact names an unscored mechanism for the in-reach gap. monster.cpp:960 isFleeing is getHealth() <= runAwayHealth; the model flees on health < run_health at three sites (hunting_pack foe step, the fleeing foe holding fire, the fleeing network feature). So a hunter (runHealth 10, Canary hunter.lua) at exactly 10 health keeps fighting in the model and runs away in Canary, and so does a troll at exactly 15. Fleeing moves a hunter out of reach, so the model over-counts in-reach in the direction of the gap (bundle about 44 vs live 21-35 percent); size unknown. Fatal Hold (iobestiary.cpp:180) needs a charm and does not apply. D38 RULE, written before any score: candidate F34 (scratch d20-patch.ps1 -F34, all three sites to <=) on the adopted bundle, one score job (d21-score -W 0 -Flags F34, 100 held-out runs), D37 criteria unchanged: hunter in-reach 21-35 percent, clears 57-85, hits 19.37-24.93, cyclops arms hold, hunting-ground kills hold (d_kills), retreat (b) Welch abs t <= 2 vs 11.05, (c1) 32.26-53.43, (c2) 42-63. (A) All pass: pre-register a live block before adoption. (B) In-reach still fails but every criterion the adopted bundle passes today still passes: F34 joins the bundle as a source-exact fix (FIDELITY title: deterministic mechanics bit-exact), build.ps1 green first, and the in-reach gap stays named. (C) Otherwise REJECTED with the failing criterion named; a source-exact fix that breaks a passing criterion means a second mechanism hides behind it, which becomes the next named gap.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_110 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 110u32, "2026-10-22T20:01Z", "tibia-lane", "D38 VERDICT (rule note 109): outcome (B), F34 JOINS THE BUNDLE as a source-exact fix; the in-reach gap stays named. Job tibia-d38-f34 (15:52-15:59, 100 held-out runs, W 0): cyclops arms hold (t PR-11 rules -1.02, PR-12 retreat 0.80, PR-16 -0.63/1.61, PR-17 0.05/0.12), hunter clears 76 (57-85; bundle 77), hunter hits 20.93 (19.37-24.93; bundle 21.03), hunting-ground kills 13.18 (d_kills holds), retreat (b) 11.73 vs 11.05 Welch t 0.45 holds; failing exactly as the bundle does today: in-reach 43.8 percent (bundle 43.8; band 21-35), (c1) 0 percent and (c2) 25 percent (bundle 0 and 25, D21 baseline note). So the threshold fix moves no criterion: a hunter at exactly 10 health is rare enough not to show in the in-reach share, which rules this mechanism out as its cause. Applied to src/hunting_pack.spi at 16:0x (the three sites, < to <=); build check job tibia-f34-build (hunting_pack, pack_sim, hunt_sim) must be green before the change counts as adopted; a pinned number that moves is updated only if the test pins a model output, and named here.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_111 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 111u32, "2026-10-22T20:28Z", "tibia-lane", "D38 (B) NOT COMPLETED, F34 reverted from src (16:3x): the adoption condition build.ps1 green failed. Job tibia-f34-build (16:01-16:27; hunting_pack, pack_sim, hunt_sim): hunt_sim 11/11, pack_sim 30 passed 9 failed: golden_seed_7_troll_pack (6 vs 7), current_first_targeting_finishes_fleeing_trolls_over_twenty_seeds, mixed_camp_from_the_catalog_over_twenty_seeds, mixed_arena_camp_evolved_rules_dominate_the_tuned_cavebot_and_kind_aware_cyclops_rules_drink_less, on_the_hunting_ground_walking_pays_and_the_evolved_shuttle_edges_the_hand_route, the_random_control_stays_far_below_every_rule, the_live_wake_delay_brings_the_arena_clears_toward_the_live_potion_counts, mirroring_the_live_arena_predicts_rules_and_retreat_within_the_live_noise_at_two_and_three_cyclops, a_hunter_keeping_four_tiles_away_only_falls_where_walls_corner_it_and_walking_beside_the_knight_halves_its_pace. Several are exact pins (troll counts move when a troll at exactly 15 health flees), but some are property claims checked against live data, which the D37/D38 score does not cover; under the rule a source fact may not be adopted while it breaks them unexplained. F34 stays a scratch flag (d20-patch -F34). Next for this item: per failing test, classify pin vs claim from its body, and for each claim say whether F34 moves it outside its band; only then a re-adoption rule. Lesson for every (B)-style adoption: pack_sim is part of the criteria, run it inside the score job, not after it.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_112 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 112u32, "2026-10-22T20:34Z", "tibia-lane", "Superseded by note 113 (16:36): a first classification from run times alone guessed a timeout for mixed_arena_camp; the run own mismatch records show all 8 real failures are exact pins.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_113 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 113u32, "2026-10-22T20:36Z", "tibia-lane", "Correction to note 112 from the run own records (target/ci-metrics/pack_sim.out, spiral-tests mismatches): all 8 recorded failures are exact pins of model outputs at the first assert of each test, none a property band: golden_seed_7 kills 6 vs 7; current_first_targeting reach_kills 71 vs 75; mixed_camp_from_the_catalog 340 vs 328; mixed_arena_camp 38366 vs 38365 (616 s was its run time, not a timeout); on_the_hunting_ground (120, 104390, 108290, 114470, 119900, 119110) vs (120, 104920, 108270, 114470, 119950, 119280); the_live_wake_delay 3710300 vs 3708400; the_random_control (54420, 927, 0, -1145992, 7206, 0, 9353800) vs (54310, 915, 0, -1146134, ...); a_hunter_keeping_four_tiles (0, 173, 11, 82) vs (..., 86). The 9th, mirroring_the_live_arena_predicts, is cyclops-only (run_health 0, which F34 cannot change) and failed in 1.5 s with no mismatch: transient. Each test stops at its first assert, so the property checks after those pins never ran. D38B RULE, written before any rerun: re-apply F34 to src only while no tibia job that copies src is queued or running; refresh the moved pins with the Cells accept mode on pack_sim; adopt only if every accepted site is an exact model-output literal (no comparison, band or boolean property assert changes), then a plain rerun of pack_sim, hunt_sim and hunting_pack is fully green (properties included). Any changed property or red rerun: F34 stays a flag and the failing claim is named. Runs after main matrix end, on a box without heavy contention.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_114 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 114u32, "2026-10-22T21:40Z", "tibia-lane", "D38B in progress (17:4x): Cells accept refreshed 6 pins and 3 nested pins were set by hand, every change a numeric literal in place (scratch f34-review.ps1); plain rerun 1 (job tibia-f34-rerun): hunt_sim 11/11, pack_sim 38/39, the one failure mixed_arena second nested pin (only reachable once the first pin passed), its dominance property checked by hand with the new numbers (false, false, true, true: holds) and the pin refreshed. Plain rerun 2 (job tibia-f34-rerun2) STOPPED by me at 548 s under memory pressure (829 MB free beside main gates and eoie release F); not a verdict. src keeps F34 and the refreshed pins (backup in scratch f34-backup) until rerun 3 decides; nothing that copies src starts before that.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_115 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 115u32, "2026-10-22T21:45Z", "tibia-lane", "D38B VERDICT: F34 ADOPTED (rule note 113). src/hunting_pack.spi flees at health <= run_health at all three sites (Canary monster.cpp:960 isFleeing). Pins refreshed in src/pack_sim.spi, every change a numeric model-output literal in place (scratch f34-review.ps1): golden_seed_7 kills 7 -> 6; current_first_targeting reach 75/381 -> 71/393 and current 330/10 -> 303/20; mixed_camp_from_the_catalog 328/13 -> 340/1; mixed_arena first pin 38365 -> 38366 and second pin (190, 0, 38774), (135, 0, 37807), (67, 1, 37889) -> 38608, 37806, 37866; on_the_hunting_ground route tuple; the_random_control tuple; the_live_wake_delay 3708400 -> 3710300; a_hunter_keeping_four_tiles arena hits 86 -> 82. Six were rewritten by Cells accept, three nested tuples by hand (accept refuses nested tuples); every property assert (booleans, dominance, bands) is unchanged and passes. Plain reruns: hunt_sim 11/11 (job tibia-f34-rerun), pack_sim 39/39 (job tibia-f34-rerun3, 17:42-17:45; all 39 were memo hits, i.e. the identical programs had passed in rerun2 before I stopped it under memory pressure, and the memo key covers compiler, core and program text). Side effect named for TIBIA-PARTY: a troll at exactly 15 health now flees, so troll-only camps lose kills (330 -> 303 over 20 seeds in the current-first pin), in the direction of the open hunting-ground troll shortfall; D38's hunting-ground kills criterion still held (13.18 vs 13.19). The in-reach gap stays named; FIDELITY back to Paused with note 108's resume conditions.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_116 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 116u32, "2026-10-23T03:47Z", "tibia-lane", "Pointer, not a resume (23:4x): FIDELITY's open cyclops-opening gaps (in-reach 43.8 percent vs band 21-35, c1 0 and c2 25 percent, note 115) may share a cause with TIBIA-EVENT-TIME notes 100 and 108. In every model run the cyclops created on the walled-in tile 1931,1353 steps out before the policy starts, while live hunters in the same spot mostly stay. If tonight's cyclops probe runs (rule L3, EVENT-TIME note 109) show live cyclopses also staying, the model's opening differs in most runs, and these criteria join the D40 set for any no-retry candidate. The resume condition is unchanged: a Canary source line for the missing retry (rule L2).")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_117 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 117u32, "2026-10-23T04:10Z", "tibia-lane", "RETRACTED 00:1x, written in error: I read Monster::getDistanceStep (monster.cpp 1733-2090) as a new source fact and resumed this item with a rule D41 for a candidate F36, but the same mechanism was already scored as K2 (stepping_away_like_canary, getDistanceStep's orthogonal-first table with a seeded random tie) under rule D20 on 2026-10-21 (notes 21-23): it moved in-reach the wrong way (48.1 vs base 43.8 percent) and hunter clears to 99, so the diagonal-backing-off hypothesis was refuted then, and note 26 lists the step table among the mechanisms ruled out. No new fact, so resume condition (2) is not met; D41 and F36 are withdrawn unbuilt, and the item goes back to Paused. Lesson: search this item's notes for the function name before calling a source fact new.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_118 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 118u32, "2026-10-23T07:18Z", "tibia-lane", "Pointer, no resume (03:1x): rule L2 was applied on tonight 10 server-probe hunter runs (TIBIA-EVENT-TIME note 118). Live retries the boxed hunter in 8 of 10 runs, so the resume condition (a Canary source line for a missing retry) is not met and has lost its premise for probed runs. Rule L5 (EVENT-TIME note 117) decides next window whether PR-22 low mover count was load or the probe. Item stays Paused.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_119 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 119u32, "2026-10-23T07:45Z", "tibia-lane", "Pointer, no resume (03:4x): rule L3 applied (TIBIA-EVENT-TIME note 119). The live cyclops on 1931,1353 stays until the policy in 8 of 10 probe runs, while every model run moves it, so the model cyclops opening differs from live in most runs, as the note-115 pointer asked. The live cause reads as timing (the neighbour leaves late, median about 1500 ms, and the boxed cyclops then needs one think plus about 750 ms), not a missing retry. That is a model-side question for the next session: when the model cyclops slot 1 leaves, and its walk lag. Any candidate needs its own pre-written rule, scored on the D40 criteria. The resume condition (a source line for a missing retry) stays unmet.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_120 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 120u32, "2026-10-23T11:11Z", "tibia-lane", "Retirement grooming 07:1x: in scope, about 1 h left. Its open gaps now live in EVENT-TIME (L5 and the misses) and PARTY (P6); close this item with pointers once those close.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_121 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 121u32, "2026-10-23T20:06Z", "tibia-lane", "Finding 16:0x (tibia-lane, during S1's gpu_sim check): F34's adoption (D38B verdict, note 115) refreshed pack_sim and reran pack_sim and hunt_sim, but not src/gpu_sim.spi. gpu_sim pins the same seed-7 troll hunt as pack_sim's golden_seed_7 (kills 7 -> 6 under F34), so its oracle pin (7, 18, 0, 84, 0, 453000) and its WGSL twin have been red since 10-22 (today: actual (6, 18, 0, 84, 0, 453000)). uniform_camp uses Respawning, so today's S1 cannot move it. Being fixed in the S1 gpu_sim pass (TIBIA-PARTY notes 80-81): oracle pin by Cells accept, WGSL twin set to the same numbers, then a plain rerun in which the GPU must reproduce the oracle. Lesson for D38B: a pin refresh reruns every test file that simulates the changed path, found by grepping for the camp constructors, not just the files named in the rule.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_122 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 122u32, "2026-10-23T20:52Z", "tibia-lane", "F34's stale gpu_sim pin fixed (16:5x, note 121): the troll-hunt oracle pin is (6, 18, 0, 84, 0, 453000), set by Cells accept, and the WGSL twin's equality chain is set to kills 6; the plain rerun (job tibia-s1-gpu3) has both green, so the GPU twin reproduces the F34 oracle.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_123 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 123u32, "2026-10-24T03:09Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-23 23:09 grooming: Paused -> Blocked, with TIBIA-EVENT-TIME and TIBIA-PARTY added as dependencies (its old deps are Done). By TIBIA note 42 its remaining 0.5 h is the close with pointers once both close, so it waits on them, not on a user pause.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_124 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 124u32, "2026-10-28T10:32Z", "tibia-lane", "GROOM 10-28 06:4x: Blocked -> Active. Its 10-24 blockers are cleared: TIBIA-EVENT-TIME is Done, and TIBIA-PARTY's model work is closed (P12, note 107; PARTY is now Paused with only the ally gap open). What remains is the 0.5 h close with pointers, scheduled today right after the 08:30 P-EXEC1 block.")
inl note_of_tibia_fidelity_125 () = Note ("TIBIA-FIDELITY", 125u32, "2026-10-28T10:33Z", "tibia-lane", "CLOSE 10-28 06:5x, with pointers (per the 10-23 retirement note: close once EVENT-TIME and PARTY close). ADOPTED from this item and in the bundle: F34, monsters flee at health <= run_health at all three sites, source-exact to Canary monster.cpp:960 isFleeing (D38B verdict, note 115). pack_sim, hunt_sim and gpu_sim pins were refreshed to it; gpu_sim's oracle and WGSL twin were fixed in the S1 pass (note 121). The per-mechanic verdicts stay in this item's notes through D38B. OPEN GAPS AND WHERE THEY ENDED: (1) the cyclops opening (in-reach 43.8 percent against band 21-35, c1 0 and c2 25 percent, note 115) went to TIBIA-EVENT-TIME, which closed on 10-24 (its note 136). The server probe isn't the cause (PC-9); CPU load doesn't reproduce it (PC-11); the walled-in misses are follow-mode skipped steps (PC-10); hold-at-range H1 was rejected (L10 c). Known limit kept: the boxed hunter on 1931,1353 moves before the policy line live in 30 of 40 runs; the W37/W38 lead models stay scratch flags. (2) Party mechanics (P6, respawn clustering) went to TIBIA-PARTY, whose model work closed on P12 (note 107, saturation stands). Its only open gap, the ally potion share (note 98), parks with PARTY. Nothing else is open here.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 1u32, "2026-10-14T13:58Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-14 09:58 nExBot seeding prepared offline (read-only reading of nExBot 19632d4): HealBot.json in bot/nExBot/nExBot_configs/profile_{0,1}/ (same profile shape as vBot); the engine collapses HP and HP% into one hp threshold compared to floor(health*100/max) with <=, so heal below 160 at 515 max is hp% <= 30 (fires for health <= 159, exact); UnifiedStorage at nExBot_configs/characters/knight sample/UnifiedStorage.json (targetbot.enabled, selectedConfig arena, healbot.enabled), and vbot-arena.json is format-compatible (targeting/looting). The network sandbox is prepended to the runtime copy of _Loader.lua. Staged in run-policy-scenario.next.ps1 (-Bot nExBot) and run-arena.ps1 (competitor nexbot); swapped in after PR-1. Earlier note said 31%: that was wrong (floor semantics).")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 2u32, "2026-10-14T14:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-14 10:30 CORRECTION: every live spiral-* run since 2026-10-13 11:48 ran with vBot's HealBot and TargetBot active alongside our policy (the vBot probe enabled the client's bot setting for the character; nothing reset it; the policy probe never disabled it). Signature: server-reported potions our policy never drank in almost every spiral run (tibia-local/harness-check.ps1 scans all logs). All live policy comparisons and the live check of the simulator's clear prediction are therefore hybrids; PR-1 is void and stopped at 61 rows. Fix: run-policy-scenario writes the bot node per competitor and removes other bots' folders, the policy probe disables the bot before login, and run-arena records foreign potions/actions per run. Re-measurement: BENCHMARK PR-4. Server-mechanics fidelity rows and the 10-13 03:34 troll targeting run are unaffected.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 3u32, "2026-10-14T18:53Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-14 14:53 PR-4 (fixed harness, 30 runs each, 0 harness failures, 0 deaths): potions rules 11.53, retreat 12.90, vBot 14.00; retreat vs vBot difference -1.10 (CI -2.62 .. 0.42, t -1.45) no difference shown; retreat vs rules +1.37 (t 1.82) no difference shown, direction against retreat. Clear times 87.6 / 91.3 / 92.7 s match the simulator's 88.6 / 88.1; potions do not (predicted 13.23 rules, 9.09 retreat; t -3.69 and 6.44). The live retreat win and the 'simulator predicts live' claim from the hybrid runs are withdrawn.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 4u32, "2026-10-14T20:31Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-14 16:31 live random control (PR-5, probe-random-body.lua, 10 runs, clean): 94.1 potions per clear (simulator 90.5, holds), 102.7 s (simulator 117.0, gap: live auto-attack persists between random picks). Control far below rules 11.5, retreat 12.9, vBot 14.0.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 5u32, "2026-10-14T20:33Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-14 16:33 nExBot: 3/3 PR-3 check runs crash the client (TIBIA-NEXBOT-CRASH); PR-3 not run today.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 6u32, "2026-10-15T07:06Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 03:06 PR-8 (pre-registered replication, rules vs vBot, 30 each, clean): 12.90 vs 14.17 potions, difference -1.27 (CI -2.99 .. 0.46), t -1.47: no difference shown; PR-4's descriptive 11.53 for rules did not replicate. Both Spiral policies sit ~1.2 potions below vBot without significance at n=30.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 7u32, "2026-10-15T10:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 05:50: nExBot 4.0.4 is now a running baseline (three harness shims, BENCHMARK PR-3a). Pre-registered results on the clean harness: retreat vs vBot no difference shown (PR-4, t -1.45), rules vs vBot no difference shown (PR-8, t -1.47), retreat vs nExBot significant (PR-3a, 12.2 vs 14.6 potions, t -2.14, narrow). Left: OTCv8 bot module, scripted cavebot as a live competitor.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 8u32, "2026-10-15T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:00 grooming: open parts: OTCv8 bot module, scripted cavebot as a live competitor, and nExBot's license (see TIBIA-ARENA-RELEASE).")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 9u32, "2026-10-15T11:04Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 07:04 correction: nExBot's license is not a task (no interaction with public projects); open parts are the OTCv8 bot module and a scripted cavebot as a live competitor.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 10u32, "2026-10-15T16:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 12:54 PR-9 (pre-registered 08:13, 50 runs each): Spiral rules 12.46 vs vBot 14.66 potions per cyclops clear, diff -2.20 (CI -3.35..-1.05), Welch t -3.80: SIGNIFICANT, rules use fewer potions; same clear time (88.7 vs 87.9 s), 0 deaths, damage 2145 vs 2479 (the saving is damage avoided). First well-powered live win over an open-source baseline. One disclosed interim look at 10:37 (fixed n, no early stop).")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 11u32, "2026-10-15T18:14Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 14:14 PR-9 explained (post-hoc): the whole rules-vs-vBot difference is the knight's login protection. Canary ends it on the player's own move/autowalk/turn/say/item use/attack cancel, not on starting an attack; our probe keeps it, vBot ends it. Hits in the first 10 s: vBot 9.14, rules 1.58; after 10 s level (60.1 vs 60.4). So PR-9 measures a scenario artifact, not hunting skill. Harness fix next: end the protection for every competitor right after login (one native turn outside the parity gate), then re-test rules vs vBot (PR-11).")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 12u32, "2026-10-15T22:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 18:40 PR-11 (pre-registered 14:21; parity.lua now ends login protection for every competitor with one native turn after login): rules 14.42 vs vBot 14.78 potions, diff -0.36 (CI -1.58..0.86), t -0.58: NO DIFFERENCE SHOWN; hits 69.1 vs 69.2, first 10 s 9.6 vs 9.0, clear 88.3 vs 87.4 s, 0 deaths. Our rules policy and vBot are level; PR-9's win was the login-protection artifact. Next for a real win: a behavior that changes exposure live (the model's policy effects are too strong, PR-10), not thresholds.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 13u32, "2026-10-15T22:49Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-15 18:49 remaining harness asymmetry after the protection fix: parity.lua ends login protection at 0.3 s for everyone, but the policy probe starts deciding about 2.5 s after login while vBot acts at once (PR-11 first-10-s hits 9.6 vs 9.0, consistent). Does not change PR-11's no-difference verdict; a test for a win should start every competitor's logic at the same moment. Recorded in BENCHMARK.md Parity.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 14u32, "2026-10-17T20:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-17 16:28 paused (user). On resume: TIBIA-START-SYNC, then run the written PR-12 (fair nExBot re-test); the OTCv8 module and a scripted live cavebot stay open.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 15u32, "2026-10-18T19:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 15:30 resuming per the 10-17 note: TIBIA-START-SYNC is implemented (uncommitted, verification in today's live block), and PR-12 is amended in docs/BENCHMARK.md before any run: both competitors start at login + 2.5 s, and runs go in chunks across days into results/arena-results-cyclops-pr12.tsv, still with no interim looks. First chunk planned for today's live block after the start-sync and stray-server checks: alternating spiral-retreat and nexbot, cyclops pack v1, heal 160, level 30, axe 60, experience 675.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 16u32, "2026-10-18T19:42Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 15:42 PR-12 chunk 1 started at 15:42: run-arena -Runs 10 alternating spiral-retreat / nexbot (cyclops 3, level 30, axe 60, heal 160, retreat at 2, experience 675) into results/arena-results-cyclops-pr12.tsv, with synchronized starts (TIBIA-START-SYNC verified live 15:41). No looks at potions until all 40 per competitor are in.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 17u32, "2026-10-18T20:40Z", "tibia-lane", "PR-12 chunk 1 done 2026-10-18 16:40: 20 rows (10 spiral-retreat, 10 nexbot, alternating, synchronized starts at login+2.5 s) in results/arena-results-cyclops-pr12.tsv. Outcomes not inspected (no interim looks per the PR-12 amendment); more chunks on later days.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_18 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 18u32, "2026-10-20T06:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 02:03 PR-12 chunk-boundary check logged in BENCHMARK (arena sources changed only by the observational seen-at trace; competitor.spi under core v1 compiles to equivalent Lua). Chunks 2-4 slid to 07:00 or main's go (P0 queue).")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_19 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 19u32, "2026-10-20T10:18Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 06:18 PR-12 chunk 2 started (main's go at 06:17, x-chain11 ended); chunks 2-4 back to back into results/arena-results-cyclops-pr12.tsv, 20 rows before.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_20 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 20u32, "2026-10-20T11:04Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 07:04 PR-12 chunk 2 done 06:18-07:04, TSV rows 21-40 (20 per competitor to go). spiral-retreat run 10 ended without a clear (experience 0, 823 actions dropped): apply the crash rule before chunk 3. Chunks 3-4 wait for the user's go (7am time box ended).")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_21 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 21u32, "2026-10-20T13:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 09:28 PR-12 complete (chunks 3-4 08:19-09:25, rows 41-80, 40 per arm, 0 foreign rows). Pre-registered verdict: NO DIFFERENCE SHOWN; potions retreat 12.75 (sd 2.89) vs nExBot 13.93 (sd 2.70), diff -1.18, CI -2.42..0.07, Welch t -1.88. Secondary: clear 90.3 vs 89.2 s, deaths 0/0, cleared 39/40 vs 40/40, damage 2230 vs 2369, hits first 10 s 6.18 vs 9.75 and after 55.15 vs 57.65 (lab/tibia-start-sync/pr12-secondary.ps1), floor changes 0/0. BENCHMARK rows written.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_22 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 22u32, "2026-10-20T18:46Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 14:46 status: every baseline in scope has live results under parity: scripted cavebot/rules (PR-4, PR-8, PR-9, PR-11), vBot (PR-4, PR-8, PR-9, PR-11), nExBot (PR-3a, fair re-test PR-12: no difference shown), random control (PR-5); the retreat policy designed in the simulator against all of them. The one part not done is the headless protocol client, which has its own items (TIBIA-HEADLESS-CLIENT, TIBIA-GAME-HANDSHAKE, TIBIA-TCP-BYTES, P4). Paused at 95% until those are picked up.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_23 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 23u32, "2026-10-22T03:38Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:38 State refresh (retirement grooming): the 'on resume' steps of 2026-10-17 are done; TIBIA-START-SYNC closed and PR-12 ran with its verdict recorded (BENCHMARK PR-12, results/arena-results-cyclops-pr12.tsv). Left: the OTCv8 module and a scripted live cavebot. Paused by the user, so the resume condition is the user's go; that is the user's own pause, not one I parked.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_24 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 24u32, "2026-10-22T16:50Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:50 Moved from docs/PLAN.md (TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE, rule DC1), a scenario-pack rule: any scenario where the scripted cavebot already reaches the respawn cap on XP/h leaves the pack, since nothing can beat it there.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_25 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 25u32, "2026-10-23T11:11Z", "tibia-lane", "Retirement grooming 07:1x: outside the end estimate. Paused by the user; resuming is the user's call.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_26 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 26u32, "2026-10-31T22:50Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 18:1x tibia groom (pause re-decision, rule R3: never paused for silence). Reason: the user parked tibia (TIBIA n44, 10-30; TIBIA-PARK Done); the last 5% is live arena runs, which need the world up. Resume when: the user resumes tibia; first step: the side-by-side check of pz-in-traces on real hunter logs (TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE n27).")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_27 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 27u32, "2026-11-01T20:20Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 16:2x tibia, PAUSE RE-DECIDED (stays Paused). Reason unchanged from 10-31 n: the last 5% is live arena runs on a running world, and the user parked tibia's arena goal (TIBIA n44); today's harvest work does not touch the live layer. Resume when: the user resumes tibia (TIBIA's resume condition). First step then: the side-by-side check of pz-in-traces on real hunter logs (TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE n27), whose analysis script becomes a Spiral function under MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV first.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_28 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 28u32, "2026-11-02T13:08Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: Active again (TIBIA resumed by the user 11-02). The last 5% is live arena runs. First step is unchanged: the side-by-side check of pz-in-traces on real hunter logs.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_29 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 29u32, "2026-11-02T14:05Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByDefault. QUESTION: Resume the arena baselines, paused by the user? RULE (written before acting): Default: stay Paused. It respects the user's explicit pause, costs no CPU, and is trivially reversible. Resume trigger, pre-registered: TIBIA-AGENT M3 verdict recorded AND the box has no Active live window. On resume, each round fixes its run count before looking (n=20 per competitor, Welch t on potions/damage/clear), the lesson of the v7 peeking. nExBot's missing LICENSE matters only for ARENA-RELEASE (publishing = UserBoundary there). BACKUP/UNDO: No state change. OWNER: tibia. NEXT: Add the resume trigger and pre-registered n to the item, then leave it Paused. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_30 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 30u32, "2026-11-06T11:01Z", "tibia", "11-06 07:1x tibia: CLOSED at its deliverable, with the tail split off. Delivered: a frozen cyclops scenario pack with a parity layer (one 250 ms action gate, 15x11 view, the same character, supplies and reset), vBot and two Spiral policies measured on a clean live layer v4-v10. VERDICT: retreat-at-2 beats our rules policy (potions -19%, Welch t -3.89, n=23/24) and is level with vBot (pooled -8.2%, t -1.89, not significant). Split off: TIBIA-ARENA-NEXBOT (third competitor), TIBIA-ARENA-RANDOM-CONTROL (chance baseline). The headless protocol client stays TIBIA-HEADLESS-CLIENT (Paused). The ~150 h evaluation budget belongs to whichever round needs it.")
inl note_of_tibia_baselines_31 () = Note ("TIBIA-BASELINES", 31u32, "2026-11-06T12:06Z", "tibia", "11-06 08:05 tibia: CORRECTION to my 07:01 close note. The two tails I split off (TIBIA-ARENA-NEXBOT, TIBIA-ARENA-RANDOM-CONTROL) were already done: PR-3a (nExBot, 2026-10-15, retreat -2.35 potions, t -2.14) and PR-5 (live random control, 2026-10-14, 94.1 potions per clear) in tibia docs/BENCHMARK.md. This item's notes stopped at 10-14 08:37, and I read only those. Both are now closed as duplicates with that proof. The close itself stands, with more evidence than I quoted.")
inl note_of_kit_friction_2026_10_12_1 () = Note ("KIT-FRICTION-2026-10-12", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:35Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-FRICTION-2026-10-12 (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_1 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:35Z", "main-thread", "08:35 measured: samples/native_closure_union_rec's main.gleam builds for --target javascript as is; the only build blocker is the harness runner (tests/native-shims/gleam/src/spiral_run.gleam halt is @external(erlang) only). In a scratch copy, a 3-line spiral_exit.mjs (process.exit) plus @external(javascript, ...) runs it: JS exits 15, Erlang 70 (= C). Cause: spiral_wrap_unsigned/signed use int.bitwise_and/shift_left, which are 32-bit on JS, so 1 << 64 collapses the mask; exact 64-bit wrap on JS needs BigInt (same root as LUA-WIDE-INTEGERS). Next: add the JS halt to the shim, wrap via modulo for bits <= 32 on JS, and give a JS-target lane its Known rows for 64-bit.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_2 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 2u32, "2026-10-14T14:22Z", "main-thread", "10:22 harness runner now builds on both targets: tests/native-shims/gleam/src/spiral_exit.mjs (process.exit) plus @external(javascript, ...) on spiral_run.gleam's halt. The Erlang path is unchanged. Remaining gap: 64-bit wrap on JS (int.bitwise_* is 32-bit there).")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_3 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:48Z", "main-thread", "06:48 groom: Gleam 129 rows, 128 agree, 1 known, 1 build-fail (pack-1h oracle, Erlang target). The JS-target gap list is unchanged.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_4 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 4u32, "2026-10-15T11:04Z", "main-thread", "07:04 decision (user 'we need all of them sota'): raised to P2; every backend stays on the active rotation.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_5 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 5u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: the remaining JS gap (64-bit wrap) is the same root as LUA-WIDE-INTEGERS (32-bit bitwise ops, doubles past 2^53); separately lib convert.try_convert on Gleam now parses strings for every integer type (was only int), fixed in the tree.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_6 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:35Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:35 lap 1 grain: related progress today: lib Gleam string units are UTF-8 bytes everywhere (index_of, length', substring through bit_array), and bit_array exists on the JavaScript target too, so string helpers add no new Erlang-only externals; remaining JS gaps unchanged: spiral_halt, erlangMath, arrays/refs, 64-bit wrap Next: check whether gleam/bit_array.slice and byte_size behave identically on the JS target for non-ASCII (one gleam test on --target javascript)")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_7 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 7u32, "2026-10-19T13:34Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:34 lap 2 grain: verified the byte-unit string helpers are target-identical. lab/GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS/utf8-probe.ps1 builds a tiny project in spiral-bin/runs (gleam 1.19.2, gleam_stdlib) over h + e-acute + llo + space + U+1F600 and prints byte_size, slice(1,2) decoded, a 1-byte cut of the 2-byte char, a slice past the end, and string.length: erlang (scoop OTP first on PATH; the default PATH OTP fails, the kino-mix-erlang-path trap) and javascript both print 11 e-acute cut-rejected past-error 7. So lib's Gleam index_of/length'/substring through bit_array (UTF-8 bytes, 10-19) need no JS-specific code, and a cut UTF-8 sequence is detectable on both targets the same way (to_string Error), which is what spiral_halt's exit 3 needs. Remaining JS gaps unchanged: spiral_halt itself (an Erlang halt external; JS needs process.exit via an @external javascript twin), erlangMath (log, exp, tanh, sqrt, sin, cos: gleam_community_maths or float.* where present), arrays/refs. Next: write the @external(javascript) twin of spiral_halt as a .mjs snippet and check it exits 3 under node")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_8 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 8u32, "2026-10-19T13:39Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:39 drive slice: closes 2 of the 4 JS gaps as a compiler patch, proven on both Gleam targets by hand. lab/GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS/patch.py (3 anchored edits to codegenGleam, a shared section, anchor-checked on a copy): spiral_halt gets a second attribute @external(javascript, node:process, exit) next to the erlang halt one; Log and Sqrt go through new prelude helpers spiral_math_log/spiral_math_sqrt over float.logarithm/float.square_root (gleam_stdlib, both targets; Error panics like math:log raised badarith); Exp becomes float.exponential directly (stdlib has erlang and javascript externals). Tanh, Sin, Cos stay erlang-only (gleam_stdlib has no portable versions), and arrays/refs (process dictionary) remain the last JS gap. Hand proof, lab/GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS/prelude-probe.ps1 (gleam 1.19.2, stdlib 1.0.5): the exact emitted text prints 2.0 1.5 and exits 3 on erlang AND javascript. Matrix impact predicted from the committed outputs: 25 Gleam residual-only changes (23 outputs that hold spiral_halt gain the JS line; native_float_math_family and native_float_sqrt switch to float.*), native results unchanged. HEAVY QUEUE: apply patch.py with the next compiler pack and read those 25 Gleam rows in its review. Next: tanh/sin/cos for JS (a small .mjs is impossible in a single-file output; candidates: Taylor-free identities via float.exponential for tanh, keep sin/cos erlang-only and document)")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_9 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:39Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:39 decision on tanh/sin/cos for JS: keep them erlang-only. gleam_stdlib 1.0.5 has no portable sin, cos or tanh, and tanh from float.exponential ((1-e)/(1+e) with e = exp(-2|x|)) loses relative precision for small x (cancellation near e = 1, about 1e-6 relative at x = 1e-10), so its printed digits would disagree with the C oracle; an exact JS version needs Math.* through an .mjs FFI file, which a single-file Gleam output cannot carry. Same for arrays/refs (process dictionary and make_ref). So after the queued patch lands, the item's remaining scope is exactly: an optional companion .mjs emitted by the Gleam project builder (Cells builders, spiral CLI) for Math.sin/cos/tanh and a JS array store; no consumer needs it today (near_wallet uses none). Done criterion recorded: patch landed and the 25 Gleam rows reproduce; the .mjs companion becomes its own item only if a JS consumer appears")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_10 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 10u32, "2026-10-19T15:23Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:23 lap 5 grain: pack 7 readiness: on a scratch copy of today's spiral_compiler.fs the four helper-lane-2 compiler patches stack cleanly in order: GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS patch.py (3 edits), EBPF-C-PROFILE patch.py (2), OPENQASM3-BACKEND patch.py (2), SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT stack-frames.py (2); all exit 0, tree untouched. Recheck after the core switch if main edits the compiler.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_11 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 11u32, "2026-10-19T15:41Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:41 lap 6 grain: re-derived the patch's predicted residual impact from today's committed outputs: of 447 samples/**/main.gleam, 23 define spiral_halt and 2 use the erlang math externals for log/exp/sqrt (native_float_math_family, native_float_sqrt), 25 distinct files in all. That matches the queued row's 25 Gleam residual-only changes exactly, so pack 7's review can check the patch against an exact list: any Gleam residual change outside those 25 files is unintended.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_12 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 12u32, "2026-10-19T15:54Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:54 lap 7 grain: the doc half of the patch, so the AGENTS.md Gleam paragraph does not go stale when pack 7 lands. lab/GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS/agents-note.py rewrites its last sentence: spiral_halt now has an @external(javascript, node:process, exit) arm, log/exp/sqrt go through gleam/float, and only tanh/sin/cos (no portable gleam_stdlib versions; tanh through float.exponential loses precision near 0) plus arrays/refs stay Erlang-only. Dry run: the anchor matches once. Queued to ride pack 7 after patch.py.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_13 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 13u32, "2026-10-19T16:40Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:40 lap 8 grain: pack 7 state check: the Gleam codegen half is in the tree (floatResultMath and the node:process exit external, 4 hits in spiral_compiler.fs), but the AGENTS.md half is not: agents-note.py's anchor still matches once, so apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md still says spiral_halt and erlangMath are Erlang-only. Main is told to apply it together with the pack 7 review (the doc must land with the reviewed code). Done = review shows exactly the predicted 25 changed Gleam outputs, then agents-note --apply")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_14 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 14u32, "2026-10-19T17:16Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:16 lap 9 grain: both halves now in the tree: agents-note.py's anchor is gone (0 hits) and apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md carries the new sentence (Since pack 7 spiral_halt has a JavaScript implementation, log/exp/sqrt via gleam/float, tanh/sin/cos + arrays/refs stay Erlang-only); review single-flight-20261019-124451 changed exactly 25 Gleam residuals as predicted, DISAGREE 0. Accept single-flight-20261019-125623 is still running (baseline EXPECTED.tsv not yet rewritten). Next: Done when that accept lands (main records it)")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_15 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 15u32, "2026-10-19T17:41Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:41 lap 10 grain: the only JavaScript-target Gleam project in spiral (lib/spiral/near/wallet, the one gleam.toml with target = javascript) has no @external at all in near_wallet.gleam (erlang 0, javascript 0, no spiral_math_tanh/sin/cos), so the Erlang-only leftovers named in the new AGENTS.md sentence reach no JS build today. Done still waits only on accept single-flight-20261019-125623 (native tier running at 13:41; baseline not yet rewritten)")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_16 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 16u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:43 its pack 7 patch is in the accepted baseline (pack 7 ACCEPTED single-flight-20261019-125623 (7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451, C rows unchanged)); the item stays open for its remaining scope.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_17 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 17u32, "2026-10-19T17:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:46 Done (drive 11), evidence: pack 7 accepted as single-flight-20261019-125623 (main 13:43: 7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451); its Gleam residuals differ from the previous baseline run single-flight-20261019-113145 in exactly 25 rows, the predicted set; spiral_compiler.fs carries the JavaScript arms (spiral_halt via node:process exit, log/exp/sqrt through gleam/float); apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md states the new split (only tanh/sin/cos and arrays/refs stay Erlang-only); the only JavaScript-target Gleam project (lib/spiral/near/wallet) has no externals at all. The 3 other modified main.gleam files in git status (ebpf_neq, multiline_comment, portable_math_rounding) predate pack 7")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_18 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 18u32, "2026-10-19T17:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:47 correction: reopened (Active 60) right after my Done, because main's 13:43 note keeps the item open for its remaining scope, which the title still names: tanh, sin, cos and arrays/refs have no JavaScript implementation (Erlang-only externals). The 13:46 evidence holds for the pack 7 half only. Next: a portable tanh with C-oracle digits (the open question from lap 7: float.exponential loses relative precision near 0), sin/cos via a JS external (@external(javascript, Math...)), arrays/refs as JS externals; low priority while no JS-target project reaches them")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_19 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 19u32, "2026-10-19T17:56Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:56 lap 11 grain, options for the remaining sin/cos/tanh: gleam_community_maths v2.0.2 (hexdocs) exports sin, cos, tanh, exponential and natural_logarithm as pub fn (Float) -> Float (log returns Result), and ships its own Erlang and JavaScript externals, so the codegen could call maths.sin/cos/tanh on both targets; the cost is a hex dependency in every generated Gleam project that uses trig (gleam.toml of the native tier, Cells and lib projects), against today's dependency-free output. The other route is a per-program JS external, which needs an .mjs file next to the module (Gleam JS externals name a module path, and Math is a global, not a module). Decision deferred until a JS-target project needs trig (none does); recorded so the choice is ready")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_20 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 20u32, "2026-10-22T06:59Z", "main", "02:59 main: lab drained. patch.py (its spiral_halt anchor is gone; checked on a copy, since it writes without an apply flag) and agents-note.py (anchor consumed; tmp/AGENTS.md has the pack-7 Gleam prelude paragraph) have landed. prelude-probe.ps1 and utf8-probe.ps1 were the pack-7 probes whose results are in notes 13:46/13:47. Folder deleted. The open half (tanh, sin, cos, arrays/refs on the JavaScript target) has its options in note 13:56 and stays deferred until a JS-target project needs them; nothing for it was in lab.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_21 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 21u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap drive2 09:39 (subagent2): compiler patch: spiral_halt JS external + log/exp/sqrt via gleam/float; hand probe exits 3 with identical output on erlang and javascript; queued to ride the next pack (25 Gleam residual changes predicted) -> next: tanh for JS via float.exponential; sin/cos stay erlang-only (document) | lap 3 10:09 (subagent2): driven in drive phase 2 (patch queued, tanh/sin/cos decision) -> next: read the 25 Gleam rows after the pack | lap 4 10:32 (subagent2): waiting: patch rides next pack -> next: read 25 Gleam rows | lap drive11 13:46 (subagent2): Done: accept 125623 (DISAGREE 0, = review), Gleam residuals vs 113145 exactly the 25 predicted, codegen + AGENTS.md in tree, JS-target project has no externals -> next: lab/GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS can be promoted/deleted (user) | lap drive11b 13:47 (subagent2): reopened Active 60: main's 13:43 note keeps it open for tanh/sin/cos + arrays/refs JS implementations; the Done evidence covers pack 7's half only -> next: JS externals for sin/cos/tanh and arrays/refs (low priority) | lap 12 14:28 (subagent2): demand sized: of the committed Gleam outputs only native_float_math_family calls spiral_math_sin/cos/tanh (Erlang target, unaffected); log/exp/sqrt already go through float.* (portable branch, compiler 166832); in lib only math.spi and physics.spi use trig, and no Gleam-JS project imports them -> next: decide when a JS-target project needs trig (keep low priority) | lap 13 14:41 (subagent2): no demand change (lap 12 sizing holds: 0 Gleam-JS consumers of trig or halt) -> next: unblocks: the first JS-target project that needs trig/halt picks hex maths vs per-program .mjs | lap 14 15:03 (subagent2): no demand; low priority -> next: first JS-target project needing trig/halt | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): the lab gleam_runner ports the JS bundling path (esbuild + trunk) unchanged, so this item's codegen gaps are independent of the CLI split -> next: first JS-target demand | lap 19 18:56 (subagent2): unchanged (trig route decided on first JS-target demand) -> next: first JS-target project | lap 22 19:48 (subagent2): latest check of the recorded trig option: gleam_community_maths 2.0.2 (2026-04-20) is still the newest release, so the lap 11 option text stands -> next: first JS-target project needing trig")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_22 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 22u32, "2026-10-26T20:50Z", "main", "10-26 16:50 main: 10-26 16:5x main: demand re-check by the item's own rule (deferred until a JavaScript-target Gleam project needs trig or arrays/refs). Scan over every repo under C:\\home\\git: gleam.toml files with target = javascript, and .ps1/.yml build scripts invoking gleam build/run/test --target javascript. Result: one JS-target project, lib/spiral/near/wallet (near_wallet: lustre, modem, gleam_javascript), and no script invoking --target javascript. near_wallet's .spi and generated .gleam have 0 uses of sin, cos, tanh, spiral_math_*, am' arrays, array_base or refs. Demand is unchanged: 0 consumers of the gaps. The item stays deferred by rule. This matches the 10-19 lap's 'JS-target project has no externals', which was likely the same project.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_23 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 23u32, "2026-10-27T23:43Z", "main", "10-27 19:43 main: Groom 10-27 19:4x: status Active -> Planned. By its own rule the item is deferred until a JavaScript-target Gleam project needs trig or arrays/refs, and the 10-26 16:50 demand scan found 0 consumers (the only JS-target project, lib/spiral/near/wallet, uses none of the gaps). Active made it count as work in progress that nobody can advance. It returns to Active the day a demand scan finds a consumer; the scan is the same one as n.10-26 16:50.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_24 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 24u32, "2026-10-29T09:25Z", "main", "10-29 05:3x main: lib/spiral/lustre.spi (tests at :150, :166) and lib/spiral/near/wallet/src/near_wallet.spi (:60, :76) fail every test in the post-wipe lib gate at 'gleam build': 'Dependency resolution failed ... There's no compatible version of gleam_stdlib: lustre_ui requires gleam_community_maths >= 1.1.1 and < 2.0.0; gleam_community_maths requires gleam_stdlib >= 0.38.0 and < 1.0.0', against Cells' gleam_stdlib '>=1.0.5 and <2.0.0'. Their targets pin '-d lustre_ui=\\\"1.0.0-rc.1\\\"'. Hex today (read-only API): lustre_ui's latest is 1.0.0-rc.1 and its latest stable is 0.6.0, which needs gleam_stdlib ~> 0.34, so NO lustre_ui release resolves with gleam_stdlib 1.x. gleam_community_maths itself is at 2.0.2. Whatever cache made it pass before the wipe (spiral-bin\\cells-gleam-warm, lost), a fresh resolution cannot. Options: drop lustre_ui from these test cells (near/wallet's own gleam.toml locks lustre_ui 0.4.0 for its standalone build), or give these Gleam JavaScript targets their own gleam_stdlib below 1.0. The other Gleam lib tests pass.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_25 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 25u32, "2026-10-29T12:04Z", "main", "10-29 08:1x main: DECIDED and LANDED. lib/spiral/lustre.spi is migrated to lustre 5, and lustre_ui is dropped from the Cells test targets. Measured first, in a scratch gleam project with the cells' own constraints minus lustre_ui: Hex resolves lustre 5.7.1 with gleam_stdlib 1.0.5 and gleam_json 3.1.0. lustre_ui's upstream main does declare gleam_stdlib >= 1.0, but it is unreleased (still labelled 1.0.0-rc.1) and needs lustre >= 5.7, so no Hex release of lustre_ui fits Cells' stdlib 1.x.\nChanges, all through apply.ps1 (14 anchors), with main's scratchpad holding lustre5/apply.ps1 and the undo copies:\n- the attribute type: lustre/internals/vdom.Attribute -> lustre/attribute.Attribute;\n- style -> attribute.styles (lustre 5 split style(name, value) from styles(list));\n- new primitives class, role and attr, each one host call;\n- button is now html.button over (class \"lustre-ui-button\" :: role \"button\" :: attr \"tabindex\" \"0\" :: attributes), which keeps lustre_ui's markup, so its stylesheet still matches;\n- centre is a div with class lustre-ui-centre;\n- the 4 directives change to lustre=\">=5.7.0\" gleam_json=\">=2.0.0\";\n- expected HTML follows lustre 5's attribute order, class first.\nProof (job-1793274523797-main-lustre5-proof): on lib copies, the control arm fails 0 of 4, reproducing the Hex conflict, and the lustre5 arm passes 4 of 4 (lustre.spi tests and tests_2, near_wallet.spi tests and tests_2) with 0 type errors. Landed at 08:1x with the reader guard; the live files equalled the proof's control before the land and the proven copy after.\nFollow-up NOT done: the standalone near/wallet build (its own gleam.toml has lustre < 5, lustre_ui 0.4, modem, decipher, and the manifest locks stdlib 0.57) still matches its committed generated near_wallet.gleam. Regenerating near_wallet.gleam from the new lib needs that gleam.toml bumped to lustre 5.7 and gleam_stdlib 1.x, and its lustre_ui stylesheet link in index.html replaced. See NEAR-WALLET-LUSTRE5.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_26 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 26u32, "2026-10-30T02:06Z", "compiler", "10-29 22:0x compiler (groom): status Planned -> Paused at 60%. The work was started (25 notes, last 12:04) and then stopped, which 'Planned' at 60% misstated. The open follow-up is the near/wallet regeneration, tracked as NEAR-WALLET-LUSTRE5.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_27 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 27u32, "2026-10-31T20:01Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:01 compiler GROOM (user direction via /btw, 10-31 15:4x): the self-hosted rewrite (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) starts only after EVERY backend is SOTA: no memory problems, no Rc slowdowns, no union hacks, no recursion limits, no missing arithmetic; GADT bugs and conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'; edition 3 finishes first; Gleam is a top test target (abyssal codebases) with F#, Rust and Wasm; Cells as fast as F# projects. This item is now on that gate (priority -> P0, status -> Active).")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_28 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 28u32, "2026-10-31T20:36Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:36 compiler: PLAN to close it (gate item now, so no longer 'deferred until a JS project needs them'). The remaining Erlang-only operations (tanh, sin, cos; arrays and refs via the process dictionary / make_ref) have no portable gleam_stdlib versions, and a pure-Gleam sin/cos would drift from the C oracle's libm digits. Gleam's JavaScript externals need a module path, and the backend can already emit SEVERAL files per program (build_many returns a GeneratedFile list). So: codegenGleam writes main.gleam plus spiral_ffi.mjs only when an operation needs it, and each such function carries both externals: @external(erlang, 'math', 'sin') @external(javascript, './spiral_ffi.mjs', 'sin') (Math.sin/cos/tanh; arrays as JS Arrays and refs as {value} cells in the .mjs; the Erlang arms unchanged). Proof: a JS-target native lane for the Gleam rows that use them (gleam build --target javascript, then run), agreeing with C on the math and array samples, and lib/spiral/near/wallet building for JavaScript with an array. Estimate ~4 h (codegen + the extra native lane).")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_29 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 29u32, "2026-10-31T20:41Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:41 compiler: PLAN REFINED from the live codegenGleam (17695-17755) and package research. (1) Precedent in the tree: spiral_halt already pairs '@external(erlang, ...)' with '@external(javascript, 'node:process', 'exit')', and spiral_string_of_checked_bytes shows an Erlang external whose Gleam BODY serves as the fallback on other targets. So any operation with a JS-capable implementation needs no extra file. (2) MATH: gleam_community_maths supports both the Erlang and JavaScript targets (README; 2.x exposes maths.sin flat, 1.x under maths/elementary): https://hexdocs.pm/gleam_community_maths/ , https://preview.hex.pm/preview/gleam_community_maths/2.0.2 . To verify before adopting: that tanh exists, and that its JS digits match C's libm on the matrix's math samples (Erlang stays math:*, unchanged). If tanh is missing or the digits drift, write the JS arms in the generated .mjs instead. (3) ARRAYS/REFS: today spiralArrays/spiralRefs use 'gary' (Erlang arrays) plus the process dictionary (make_ref/put/get): Erlang-only. glearray works on both targets but is IMMUTABLE (https://hexdocs.pm/glearray/ , https://github.com/lunagl/glearray), and Spiral arrays are mutable with aliasing (a callee's write is visible to the caller), so a mutable cell is needed on JS, and Gleam has none without FFI. So arrays/refs need the generated spiral_ffi.mjs (a JS Array in a {value} cell, the same SpiralArray(key, size) surface) or a tiny companion Hex package; the first needs codegenGleam to emit two files (build's GeneratedFile list allows it). Next session: (a) the tanh/digits check of gleam_community_maths in a scratch gleam project (CPU-light), (b) the .mjs emission applier.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_30 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 30u32, "2026-10-31T21:39Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 17:39 compiler: (a) of n29 DONE, the math half is settled. Fetched gleam_community_maths 2.0.2 into a scratch gleam project and read its source: pub fn tanh/sin/cos exist (maths.gleam 1076/968/900); each carries @external(erlang, 'math', 'tanh'|'sin'|'cos'), EXACTLY the Erlang externals codegenGleam emits today (erlangMath, 17696), plus @external(javascript, '../maths.mjs', ...) whose bodies are plain Math.tanh/Math.sin/Math.cos. So switching codegenGleam's erlangMath to 'import gleam_community/maths' + maths.tanh/sin/cos changes NOTHING on the Erlang target (same BIFs, same digits) and gives the JavaScript target V8's Math.* (fdlibm-derived). Left for the math half: add gleam_community_maths to the native tier's and Cells' gleam project deps, and check JS digits against C on the matrix's tanh/sin/cos samples (an fdlibm vs msvcrt/glibc last-ulp difference would show as a stdout diff there). The arrays/refs half still needs the generated .mjs (n29 (b)).")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_31 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 31u32, "2026-11-01T03:41Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 23:41 compiler, cadence groom: TOP-3 CPU-LIGHT #1. Next concrete step: write the applier that emits spiral_ffi.mjs beside the Gleam output (mutable arrays as JS Array, refs as {v} objects) and maps tanh/sin/cos to gleam_community_maths 2.0.2 on the JavaScript target; drafting is CPU-light. NODE-RUNNABLE NEXT: the Gleam JS digits check (gleam run --target javascript on the arrays/refs and maths samples vs the Erlang target's digits) runs on the node, which has bun and gleam; queue it the moment the applier drafts.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_32 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 32u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): BATCHING: its node digits check joins the batched node job with the other drafted appliers (one candidate build, one matrix pass), so no draft waits for its own run.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_33 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 33u32, "2026-11-01T04:48Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:48 compiler: MATH HALF DRAFTED as a kit do program (Spiral-first: no new .ps1 applier). codegenGleam's erlangMath (an Erlang-only external per op) becomes portableMath: 'import gleam_community/maths' + maths.tanh/sin/cos. Per n30 these are the same math:* BIFs on Erlang (no digit change) and Math.* on JavaScript. The dep is added where Gleam projects are generated: the native tier's gleam.toml and Cells' @gleam_deps (targets.ex:346). Dry-applied on copies: 6 replacements. Bases: spiral_compiler.fs C0990B2D590CE965, gleam.toml 14C01FC8F480A476, targets.ex E74FF5BBA52F53E4. LANDING: the .fs part rides the next compiler pack via BACKEND-BATCH; the targets.ex part is a Cells land (reader guard) in the same window. PROOF in BACKEND-BATCH: the Gleam rows with tanh/sin/cos stay byte-identical on Erlang, and a JS-target build of the same rows agrees with C. ARRAYS/REFS half (generated spiral_ffi.mjs) is the next draft.\nPROGRAM (kit do <dir> -, files resolved by name from apps/compiler, apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/gleam, apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells):\nedit spiral_compiler.fs expect files 1 replacements 4\n  replace once <<<\n        let erlangMath (name : string) (arg : string) =\n            let decl = $\"@external(erlang, \\\"math\\\", \\\"{name}\\\")\\npub fn spiral_math_{name}(x: Float) -> Float\"\n            if not (externals.Contains decl) then externals.Add decl\n            $\"spiral_math_{name}({arg})\"\n  >>> with <<<\n        let portableMath (name : string) (arg : string) =\n            global' \"import gleam_community/maths\"\n            $\"maths.{name}({arg})\"\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                | Tanh, [x] -> erlangMath \"tanh\" (tup x)\n  >>> with <<<\n                | Tanh, [x] -> portableMath \"tanh\" (tup x)\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                | Sin, [x] -> erlangMath \"sin\" (tup x)\n  >>> with <<<\n                | Sin, [x] -> portableMath \"sin\" (tup x)\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                | Cos, [x] -> erlangMath \"cos\" (tup x)\n  >>> with <<<\n                | Cos, [x] -> portableMath \"cos\" (tup x)\n  >>>\nedit gleam.toml expect files 1 replacements 1\n  replace once <<<\ngary = \">= 1.0.0 and < 2.0.0\"\n  >>> with <<<\ngary = \">= 1.0.0 and < 2.0.0\"\ngleam_community_maths = \">= 2.0.2 and < 3.0.0\"\n  >>>\nedit targets.ex expect files 1 replacements 1\n  replace once <<<\n    {\"gary\", \">=1.1.0 and <2.0.0\"},\n  >>> with <<<\n    {\"gary\", \">=1.1.0 and <2.0.0\"},\n    {\"gleam_community_maths\", \">=2.0.2 and <3.0.0\"},\n  >>>")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_34 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 34u32, "2026-11-01T06:40Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:40 compiler, round 3: ARRAYS/REFS EMISSION ANCHORS (core C0990B2D590CE965). Dispatcher: 'build codegen backend ext' (35003-35006) wraps every single-file backend as [generated_file code ext]; Gleam's arm is at 35008 (build codegenGleam, Gleam, .gleam). codegenGleam (17643) assembles its text at 18359-18449 (globals, then externals at 18361, types, functions, main) and returns one string. PLAN without changing codegenGleam's type: (1) spiralArrays/spiralRefs (17723-17733) register their JS bodies in a jsFfi ResizeArray beside externals, and each Erlang-only decl gains its paired @external(javascript, './<stem>_ffi.mjs', '<fn>') line. Storage moves from gary to OTP's array module (array:new/get/set) and JS Arrays. (2) when jsFfi is non-empty, codegenGleam appends the marker line '//// spiral_ffi.mjs' plus the JS text after main. (3) the dispatcher's Gleam arm splits at the marker BEFORE UnusedGlobals.prune: the .gleam part is pruned as today, the rest becomes generated_file js '_ffi.mjs' (written as main_ffi.mjs by 34795's file + extension). (4) Cells targets.ex run_gleam and the native tier copy *_ffi.mjs beside main.gleam. The stem must be the output file's (main / main_real); build_many already hands 'file' to the lambda.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_35 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 35u32, "2026-11-01T07:24Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:23 compiler: math half SHIPPED in PACK-12 (gleam_community/maths on both targets; the Cells and native-tier deps landed with it). Remaining: arrays/refs via the generated _ffi.mjs (anchors in n34).")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_36 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 36u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:34 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: P0 gate, est 180m left (arrays/refs only), light, dep PACK-12 (math half installed). DEFINITION OF DONE: (a) samples using arrays/refs build and run on the Gleam JavaScript target ('gleam run --target javascript' in a copy of tmp/tests/native-shims/gleam with main.gleam + main_ffi.mjs) with exit codes equal to C for every Gleam-listed sample that uses arrays or refs (count them first from the matrix); (b) the Erlang target is byte-identical in stdout and exit across the Gleam list (predict: compile-status diffs 0; native agree); (c) gary leaves both gleam.toml/targets.ex dep lists. NEXT STEP: change codegenGleam (spiral_compiler.fs 17643) to return a record instead of one string (below), then the dispatcher arm at 35008. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN, REPLACING n34's marker-line idea (a '//// spiral_ffi.mjs' marker split by text is exactly the stringly design to avoid): codegenGleam returns '{ gleam : string; ffi : GleamFfi option }' with 'GleamFfi = { stem : string; functions : FfiFunction list }' and 'FfiFunction = { name : string; erlang : ErlangImpl; javascript : JsImpl }'. The two targets are a UNION 'FfiTarget = Erlang of module * fn | JavaScript of path * fn', rendered into @external lines by one function, so a function can't have one target without the other (the type forces both). The dispatcher pattern-matches the option and emits [generated_file gleam '.gleam'] @ (ffi |> Option.map render |> Option.toList). Storage is an opaque external type (no Option(a) sentinels for 'never set': the JS side throws, the Erlang side uses array:new with {default, undefined} plus a typed check).")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_37 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 37u32, "2026-11-01T19:15Z", "compiler", "11-01 15:15 compiler: ARRAYS/REFS HALF DRAFTED, with a simpler shape than n35's record plan and no stringly marker. PROGRAM: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS\\1-gleam-storage-ffi.kitdo (5 replacements on core F6F6879566C5A830 -> 81a865be25d81180; dry run clean; no `gary` left in the core). DESIGN: the storage externals are FIXED code, not per-program code, so nothing needs splitting. codegenGleam takes ffi_module (<output stem>_ffi) and returns (gleam text, GleamFfi), where GleamFfi = NoGleamFfi | GleamStorageFfi is set when spiralArrays or spiralRefs are used. The dispatcher's Gleam arm then also emits <stem>_ffi.erl and <stem>_ffi.mjs through the multi-file GeneratedFile list (the same mechanism as Python's _auto.py), so the output's writer names them. Gleam side: opaque types SpiralArrayStore(a) and SpiralRefCell(a); each external has paired @external(erlang, \"<stem>_ffi\", f) and @external(javascript, \"./<stem>_ffi.mjs\", f) attributes, so neither target can be missing one. Erlang: process dictionary + OTP array (array:new/get/set, fixed size); JS: Array.fill and {value} objects. The public helper API (spiral_array_create/_create_filled/_from_list/_to_list/_get/_set, .size, spiral_ref_new/_get/_set) is unchanged, so no codegen call site moves. The unused listToArray (the last gary/array import) is removed. LANDED NOW (harmless until the pack, since they only act when the files exist): scripts/test.ps1 copies <stem>_ffi.* into the lane project (single-row path) and the batch project (one copy, identical for every row), and bench-native.ps1 copies them for the bench project. Cells needs no copy change: with_cell_sources copies the whole src dir. AFTER THE PACK: drop gary from Cells @gleam_deps (targets.ex:352), from the native shim gleam.toml, and from apps/spiral spiral.spi:762. PROOF RUNNING: node job job-1793560465530-compiler-gleamffi builds the candidate, then compiles the 32 Gleam-listed samples that use arrays or refs (DoD a's count) with installed and candidate, and runs both on Erlang and JS (bun). Prediction: new-erl = old-erl 32/32, new-js = old-erl >= 30/32, old-js 0/32.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_38 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 38u32, "2026-11-01T20:33Z", "compiler", "11-01 16:40 compiler: PROVEN on the node (job-1793562431251-compiler-gleamffi4, candidate core 3B8D4591E4D376C1 = live core + program 1; host = live 98D282B5 + program 2). PROGRAM 2 is new: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS\\2-host-optional-siblings.kitdo, 6 replacements on Program.fs. It was needed because the first two runs failed. Run 1: the host copied only the main file to the requested output, so no _ffi files were written. Run 2: the BuildFile reply is every generated file joined with \"\\n\", and the host wrote that reply as main.gleam, so the Erlang FFI landed inside it ('Syntax error ... -module(main_ffi)'). Fix: for backends with OPTIONAL sibling outputs (Gleam: _ffi.erl, _ffi.mjs), the host reads the main file the core wrote, copies fresh siblings to the output's stem, deletes stale ones there, restores the input-side copies, and skips the source-unchanged cache. Fixed multi-file backends (C++, Python) are unchanged. RESULT over the 32 Gleam-listed array/ref samples. (a) JS target with the generated pair: exit = old Erlang exit on 31/32. The miss is mega_brzozowski_derivatives/runtime_bench, which panics with its own self-check 'brzozowski-bench-ends-with-zero-count' on JS: the known 64-bit wrap gap (32-bit int bitwise on JS), not the FFI. Old compiler on JS: 0/32 (gary). (b) Erlang: new exit = old exit on 32/32 (including 235, 42 and 5); stdout-only re-check on the 9 samples whose run produced output, old vs new: identical 9/9. The first hashes mixed in stderr compiler warnings; those were never program output. NEXT (pack time): ride the next compiler pack with DELPHI-TO-PASCAL step 1 and BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY's PrimToString arms (all stack cleanly), then drop gary from Cells @gleam_deps (targets.ex:352), from the native shim gleam.toml and from spiral.spi:762, then Done. The JS 64-bit wrap is a separate gap (n-earlier: same root as LUA-WIDE-INTEGERS) and is not part of this item's DoD.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_39 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 39u32, "2026-11-01T22:16Z", "compiler", "11-01 18:20 compiler: PACK15B INTEGRATION (node job-1793567591687, pack15b core A7F3592A598C96A1, which carries both programs plus PrimToString, DELPHI-CASE-INSENSITIVE-NAMES and DELPHI-TO-PASCAL step 1). This time stdout is hashed alone, with stderr dropped, over the 32 array/ref samples. new-erl = old-erl on exit AND stdout: 32/32. new-js = old-erl: 31/32 (runtime_bench, the known JS 64-bit gap). old-js: 0/32. Every new output carries the _ffi pair. pack15b's residual predict is running on the box; then main installs it.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_40 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 40u32, "2026-11-02T01:36Z", "compiler", "11-01 21:37 compiler: DoD (c) STAGED. %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS\\3-drop-gary-after-install.kitdo removes gary from Cells @gleam_deps (targets.ex), from the native shim gleam.toml and from apps/spiral/spiral.spi's Gleam dep list (3 replacements, dry run clean, 0 gary left in the three files). Census: nothing outside the compiler's own Gleam output imports gary (x, tibia, kit, fiscal, dice: 0 files). It lands only AFTER pack15c installs, since the installed compiler still emits 'import gary'. Apply from the spiral repo root with kit do, check that the removed lines left no blank entry inside spiral.spi's dep list, then rebuild the CLI through apps/spiral/build.ps1, whose run check now carries the JSON-protocol rows. Then this item is Done.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_41 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 41u32, "2026-11-02T04:49Z", "compiler", "11-02 00:49 compiler: DONE. pack15c (storage FFI + host optional siblings) installed by main 00:29-00:33 (box SpiralCompiler.dll 92820117717425C0 / Core 83CEDAA9DDED49EB, node CAE7CB4C16DA84FD). DoD (c) LANDED unstaged: gary removed from apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/targets.ex @gleam_deps, apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/gleam/gleam.toml and apps/spiral/spiral.spi (blank lines left by the program cleaned by hand). CLI rebuilt via apps/spiral/build.ps1 under a compile window: RUST-OK, 29 run-check rows as expected, spiral.exe shipped 00:45:54, 0 'gary' in spiral.rs. PROOF on the installed compiler: samples/array_create_reads_zero -> Gleam with main_ffi.erl/main_ffi.mjs in a gary-free shim project, gleam run exit 5 = C exit 5. Residual known: runtime_bench's 64-bit wrap on the JS target (31/32, n of 16:40) stays its own gap. Europa runs old core 7B234DAC until its Linux source build. Next step: none on this item; typefeatureset: GleamFfi closed union (NoGleamFfi | GleamStorageFfi) already.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_42 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 42u32, "2026-11-02T05:28Z", "compiler", "11-02 01:28 compiler: REOPENED for a pack15c host regression, reported by x (x-verify49 on the node: escript undef simframes_ffi:array_new/2). The core names the FFI module after the INPUT stem (spiral_compiler.fs:35230, ffi_module = <input stem>_ffi), but host Program.fs:667-673 copied the sidecar under the OUTPUT stem. So '--backend Gleam a.spi out/b.gleam' wrote out/b_ffi.erl containing -module(a_ffi), which the BEAM can't load (JS has the same mismatch: the import is ./a_ffi.mjs). FIX DRAFTED: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS\\4-ffi-sidecar-keeps-module-name.kitdo, 1 replacement on host 615BAAEDCA3FEC24: copy (and the stale-file delete) to <output dir>/<input stem><suffix>, so the file name equals the -module name and the JS import path. PROOF (box, Debug candidate = live core + host with program 4, A/B with the installed host as control; samples/array_create_reads_zero copied as frames.spi -> proj/src/main.gleam): installed wrote main_ffi.erl (-module(frames_ffi)), gleam run exit 1; candidate wrote frames_ffi.erl/.mjs, gleam run exit 5 = C exit 5. Rides pack15d with BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY program 3 and CELLS-SERVER-EXIT-TAIL. x's workaround (apps/sim/build.ps1 renames *_ffi.* to their -module name) becomes a no-op after install. Next step: hand pack15d to main with a passing install-pack -Programs dry run.")
inl note_of_gleam_js_target_gaps_43 () = Note ("GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS", 43u32, "2026-11-02T07:00Z", "compiler", "11-02 03:00 compiler: DONE again. pack15d installed by main 02:36 (box SpiralCompiler.dll 9489144496A935E6 / Core F16F6B2032F81F2D, which I verified, both equal to the candidate; node F014BE54FA782B8E; Europa source build core CB8092C4FEE7A01C). Program 4 (the sidecar keeps the input stem, so the file name equals the -module name) is live; the candidate repro (frames.spi -> main.gleam: frames_ffi.erl, gleam run 5 = C 5) ran on the same bytes. x's apps/sim/build.ps1 rename is now a no-op.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_1 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:38 touch: 16 #if SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL blocks remain in spiral_compiler.fs; with gpt5parallel rungs dropped its pairs only need to keep compiling, so next unification is CodegenFsharp per the 03:21 plan")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_2 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 2u32, "2026-10-14T21:24Z", "main-thread", "17:24 CodegenFsharp pair mapped (job tmp/rotation/cf.diff, single-flight vs gpt5parallel side, 54 hunks; gpt5parallel has 789 lines single-flight lacks, single-flight 97). (1) ~20 hunks are whitespace only (trailing blank lines). (2) ~25 mechanical hunks share directly with one rule: keep single-flight's text but gpt5parallel's id calls (UnionTagIdOps.text, LayoutFieldNameIdOps.matchesText; the single-flight CodegenAdapter versions are identity/equality, so move CodegenAdapter above CodegenFsharp); HopacExtensions.S.map -> Array.map; the memo tables use one TryGetValue/TryAdd shape on codegenMemoTable (Dictionary has TryAdd), with tag = dict.Count (gpt5parallel's F# emission is already sequential); FastRuntimeFormat vs sprintf: either is fine. (3) One ~300-line hunk is gpt5parallel's method/closure body lookup with recovery; it moves verbatim into hooks codegenFsharpMethodBody/codegenFsharpClosureBody in the CodegenAdapter pair. Gate: both cores build, and the single-flight -Suite all F# rows are residual-identical. Estimated 2-3 h; not started today (window too short to verify both cores).")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_3 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 3u32, "2026-10-15T01:15Z", "main-thread", "21:15 NEXT SESSION #4 (P0): CodegenFsharp per the 54-hunk map (job tmp/rotation/cf.diff): shared single-flight text plus gpt5parallel id calls, recovery lookups as CodegenAdapter hooks. Gate: both cores build and the F# rows are residual-identical. 2-3 h.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_4 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 4u32, "2026-10-15T04:49Z", "main-thread", "00:49 CodegenFsharp pair unified (tmp/rotation/cf-unify/unify_codegen_fsharp.ps1, 15 #if pairs left, core 186,572 -> 185,995 lines). Moved the CodegenAdapter pair above a shared CodegenFsharp; gpt5parallel's body-cell waits live in its adapter as codegenFsharpMethodBody/codegenFsharpClosureBody, single-flight's are dictionary reads. Shipping in pack-1g: review single-flight-20261015-002606 is identical to the pack-1f oracle on every cell (4,143 rows) and both cores build. gpt5parallel F#-only cross-core check running.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_5 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 5u32, "2026-10-15T05:14Z", "main-thread", "01:14 gpt5parallel side checked: F#-only suite gpt5parallel-20261015-005048 on the 1g build, 959 rows, all parity, 0 missing. Cross-core vs single-flight review 002606: 958/959 identical. The one difference, wgsl_record_loop (gpt keeps a let-bound struct around a self tail call), already exists in the gpt5parallel runs of 10-13 and 10-14 02:01. Versus the 10-14 02:01 gpt run: 6 differences, 3 load timeouts/errors there, and 3 string rows (multiline_triple_string, string_eq, string_utf8) from pack-1d's F# UTF-8 strings that now match single-flight. So the unification changes no output on either core.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_6 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 6u32, "2026-10-15T06:15Z", "main-thread", "02:15 CodegenFsharp unification installed in pack-1g and blessed: single-flight-20261015-015332 = review 002606 on all 4,143 rows, DISAGREE 0. Pairs left: 15. Next pair candidates: other codegen sections whose differences are only body-cell waits (same adapter-hook pattern).")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_7 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 7u32, "2026-10-15T07:06Z", "main-thread", "03:06 remaining 15 pairs sized after pack-1g (lines differing between sides): ServerHooks 11, GraphIdentity 40, CodegenAdapter 677 and BlockParsingGpt5parallel 255 are hook layers by design (per-core adapters), not duplication. Real duplication: main 64, Prepass 237, Infer 456 (2,464 vs 2,322 lines), SignalRSupervisor 3,945, HopacExtensions. PartEval and the Hopac* sections are gpt-only bodies against 2-line single-flight stubs. Next targets in order: main (64), Prepass (237), Infer (456), each with the CodegenFsharp pattern (shared single-flight text plus gpt hooks in an earlier gpt-only section) and an identity proof on both cores.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_8 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 8u32, "2026-10-15T07:45Z", "main-thread", "03:45 main pair unified (candidate pack-1h): shared single-flight main plus 8 hooks in the ServerHooks pair (mainTraceLevel, mainEntered, mainBeforeArguments, mainAnnounceServer, mainBeforeWebHost, mainBeforeRun, mainHostExitCode, mainRun); the gpt bootstrap sentinels move into its ServerHooks side. Both cores build; review single-flight-20261015-032242 = pack-1g oracle on every cell. Install after the renewal window.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_9 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 9u32, "2026-10-15T08:35Z", "main-thread", "04:35 main pair unification installed as pack-1h and blessed (single-flight-20261015-041405 = review 032242 on every cell, DISAGREE 0). Pairs left: 14. Next: Prepass (237 differing lines), then Infer (456).")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_10 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 10u32, "2026-10-15T09:02Z", "main-thread", "05:02 Prepass pair mapped (99 hunks ignoring whitespace; sides extracted to the job's tmp/rotation/prepass-pair). Plan: (1) Typed names: gpt uses TermNameId/TypeNameId with TermNameIdOps/TypeNameIdOps; single-flight already has identity aliases in IdentityAdapter (line ~6150), so gpt's text is identity on single-flight. (2) Pattern-ref memoization: gpt caches EPatternRef/TPatternRef by reference in eval, propagate, resolve and lower. In lower that cache ignores env, so it is a behaviour change for single-flight. Make it a hook (single-flight: direct call) in an earlier adapter pair, not shared text. (3) Dictionary vs ConcurrentDictionary in propagate/resolve/lower/typecase metavars: adopt ConcurrentDictionary (single-threaded use, same results) or a type hook. (4) HopacExtensions.A.map vs Array.map: hook. (5) Single-flight's cross-assembly factories (nominal_prepass, resolve_env_value, propagated_vars_env_with_vars): keep in shared text if referenced outside Prepass, else use gpt's inline records. Not started tonight: about 2 h plus a review and bless, which does not fit before 07:00 with the Tibia suite gate at 06:00.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_11 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 11u32, "2026-10-15T09:49Z", "main-thread", "05:49 NEXT SESSION #1: Prepass pair per the 05:02 mapping (pattern-ref memo as a hook, typed names via IdentityAdapter), then Infer (456 lines). Same proof as packs 1g/1h: both cores build, review equals the oracle on every cell, bless reproduces it.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_12 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 12u32, "2026-10-15T11:23Z", "main-thread", "07:23 Prepass unification written (tmp/rotation/prepass-unify/unify_prepass.ps1): shared = gpt5parallel's text with the 7 pattern-ref memo sites behind a new PrepassAdapter pair (single-flight: direct evaluation, gpt5parallel: memo/visit-once), the FError arm as a guarded hook arm (FilledTop has no FError in single-flight), and the PrepassNominal alias kept for single-flight PartEval; single-flight's split-assembly factories dropped (only used inside Prepass). Core 185,973 -> 184,569 lines on top of pack-1i; both-core build queued. Infer mapped next: 367 changed lines, 144 typed-ID, 223 structural (error cases carrying typed IDs, module-member conversions moduleMembersToTermMap/termMapToModuleMembers, a depth-limited type printer at 96, named vs anonymous NominalAux/PrototypeDef records, lit moved out). Estimated 3-4 h.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_13 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 13u32, "2026-10-15T12:18Z", "main-thread", "08:18 Infer mapped in full (diff in the job's tmp/rotation/infer-pair). Plan: shared = gpt5parallel's text (typed IDs, module-member helpers, depth-limited printer, ConcurrentDictionary are neutral or identity on single-flight) plus an InferAdapter pair with hooks for the error-path behaviour differences, so single-flight stays oracle-identical: (1) error rendering: single-flight's messages are what ExpectedErrors match (gpt5parallel already shows WRONG-ERROR on missing_core_negative); (2) ModuleMustBeImmediatelyApplied, reported by single-flight but commented out in gpt5parallel; (3) failed declarations: Promise failure (single-flight) vs FError recovery (gpt5parallel); (4) unused-type-variable list sorted in gpt5parallel; (5) CompilerError payload: string vs OperationalReasonId (type alias hook). Outside Infer only nominals_aux is shared, with structurally identical types. Order: after pack-1j; then PACKAGE-DEFAULTS-PER-PACKAGE on the unified Infer.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_14 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 14u32, "2026-10-15T13:05Z", "main-thread", "09:05 Infer unified (tmp/rotation/infer-unify/unify_infer.ps1): shared = gpt5parallel's text, an InferAdapter pair with 4 hooks (CompilerError payload type, its rendering, the module-index application check that gpt5parallel had commented out, failed declarations as failed promise vs FError recovery), FastRuntimeFormat replaced by sprintf (gpt-only module), and single-flight IdentityAdapter's identity termMapToModuleMembers/typeMapToModuleMembers dropped (the shared ones subsume them). On top of Prepass unification + pack-1i: both cores build (single-flight 202 s, gpt5parallel 599 s); core 182,256 lines, 13 pairs left. Error messages now use gpt5parallel's more precise text (e.g. Unbound term variable), so some ExpectedErrors entries will change; dry review running.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_15 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 15u32, "2026-10-15T14:06Z", "main-thread", "10:06 Prepass + Infer unifications reviewed in pack-1j (single-flight-20261015-094241): no residual change outside the intended Bend/Lua/new-backend rows; the only behaviour change is Infer's more precise error text (one ExpectedErrors entry updated to 'Unbound term variable: +.'). Pairs 15 -> 13. Install after the 12:45 Kino corpus check.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_16 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 16u32, "2026-10-15T15:49Z", "main-thread", "11:49 gpt5parallel side of the Prepass + Infer unifications checked: F#-only suite on the pack-1k gpt5parallel build (pack-c), 963 rows, 0 missing; cross-core vs single-flight review 105101: 962/963 identical, the one difference is the pre-existing wgsl_record_loop (since 10-13). Both cores are output-stable through the unification.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_17 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 17u32, "2026-10-15T18:26Z", "main-thread", "14:26 Prepass + Infer unifications installed in pack-1k (bless 140535). 13 pairs left; the rest are hook layers, gpt-only bodies, or SignalRSupervisor/PartEval (deep divergence that hopacsota replaces).")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_18 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 18u32, "2026-10-16T11:36Z", "main-thread", "07:36 main: recount with pair-drift.ps1's parser on the pack-1s tree: 8 core pairs, not 13. By design: BlockParsingGpt5parallel (layer, 256/3), InferAdapter (7/7), PrepassAdapter (13/6), CodegenAdapter (654/29), ServerHooks (63/14). The core difference itself: PartEval (104,779 gpt5parallel lines vs 2,968). Real candidates: GraphIdentity (37/21; gpt5parallel interns path ids, single-flight uses strings: unifying on interned ids changes single-flight Map key order, so it needs an output-order proof, not a mechanical move) and SignalRSupervisor (3,737/830). The item is near its floor; the next step is SignalRSupervisor's diff, then a decision on GraphIdentity.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_19 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 19u32, "2026-10-16T11:38Z", "main-thread", "07:38 main: SignalRSupervisor compared by top-level definitions: 23 shared names, 60 gpt5parallel-only (its parallel build orchestration), 11 single-flight-only. Unifying it means hooks for the gpt5parallel build machinery, which hopacsota (restarted from single-flight's evaluator) is replacing; decision: no further unification work on this lane; the remaining pairs retire with gpt5parallel. Item left Active at its floor for the record; hopacsota's G6 seam gate keeps single-flight changes seam-only.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_20 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 20u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night measurement (tree pack-1z, 186,430 lines): 9 two-sided pairs (PartEval 104,778 gpt5parallel vs 2,976 single-flight; SignalRSupervisor 3,736/829; CodegenAdapter 653/28; BlockParsingGpt5parallel 255/2; PartEvalSeamsHopacsota 131/19; GraphIdentity 36/20; ServerHooks 62/13; PrepassAdapter 12/5; InferAdapter 6/6) plus ~45k one-sided gpt5parallel lines. Single-flight is ~31.7k lines, gpt5parallel ~182k; edits happen twice only inside those 9 pairs.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_21 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 21u32, "2026-10-19T12:17Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:17 lap 1 grain: fresh count on the tree (186,825 lines): 14 #if SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL blocks, 2 negated, 1 SPIRAL_CORE_HOPACSOTA; per the 10-16 decision the remaining pairs retire with gpt5parallel (HOPACSOTA-P6 promotion), so no unification edits Next: re-count after the next compiler pack; close when gpt5parallel is removed")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_22 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 22u32, "2026-10-19T14:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:21 lap 2 grain: linked the retirement path as a dep: the remaining 14 SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL pairs leave with gpt5parallel at HOPACSOTA-P6 promotion, so P6 is this item's real exit criterion Next: -")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_23 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 23u32, "2026-10-19T18:40Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:40 lap 4 grain: recount from pair-drift -Table (14:3x): 8 pairs remain (BlockParsingGpt5parallel layer, InferAdapter 6/6 lines, PrepassAdapter 12/5, PartEval 104,783/2,979, CodegenAdapter 653/28, GraphIdentity 36/20, SignalRSupervisor 3,736/829, ServerHooks 62/13); InferAdapter is the smallest real pair, 6 lines a side Next: try unifying InferAdapter (6 vs 6 lines) as a hook, the cheapest proof that the adapter pattern works, in the next pack")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_24 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 24u32, "2026-10-19T18:40Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:40 correction to note 23: InferAdapter is not a unification candidate, it IS the hook (error reason type, reason text, module-application report, failed-declaration recovery differ per core and keep Infer itself shared); the same holds for PrepassAdapter, CodegenAdapter and ServerHooks, so the only pairs that still duplicate logic are PartEval (104,783 vs 2,979 lines), SignalRSupervisor and GraphIdentity, and those retire with gpt5parallel at HOPACSOTA-P6, as note 22 says. Next: none until P6.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_25 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 25u32, "2026-10-24T02:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main grooming: Paused by rule (Active, no note since 2026-10-19T18:40Z, no child item noted in 3 days). Resume when picked; progress kept at 60%.")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_26 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 26u32, "2026-10-31T11:35Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 07:35 compiler: SUPERSEDED by GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE (backlog groom). The user's 10-31 02:0x call retires gpt5parallel, and packs/apply-retire-gpt5parallel.ps1 collapses every SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL pair (core 190k -> 36k lines, projection identity proven), so no pair is left to unify and nothing is edited twice. Closes Done when PACK-10 is installed (deps now PACK-10).")
inl note_of_core_pair_unification_27 () = Note ("CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION", 27u32, "2026-10-31T19:07Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 14:59 compiler: DONE by retirement: PACK-10 is installed, the live core has 0 SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL blocks, pair-drift.ps1 is archived (20261031-145532). Nothing is edited twice any more.")
inl note_of_gadt_uninhabited_payload_runtime_1 () = Note ("GADT-UNINHABITED-PAYLOAD-RUNTIME", 1u32, "2026-11-02T11:30Z", "compiler", "11-02 07:35 compiler: the native row this item asked for now exists. samples/gadt_uninhabited_payload_runtime (the mega probe with an i32 self-check main) runs on the default Fsharp/C/Rust/Delphi: 4 ran, oracle agree 3, DISAGREE 0 (single-flight-20261102-072734). Companion samples/gadt_rank_matrix guards the 10-06 C/C++ GADT tag-numbering fix over three case orders, in the Fsharp,C,Rust,Delphi,TypeScript,Cpp,Python set: 7 ran, agree 6, DISAGREE 0. Both rows are \"new\" in the baseline until the next full -Suite all accept. The mega probe compiler_probe_gadt_runtime is byte-identical to samples/gadt_specialized_case_rank; it goes with the mega archive.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_1 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:24Z", "lane-a", "08:24 lane-a: spec gap found: one-call.md anchors are all one line, but compiler pack scripts use multi-line indented anchors. Drafted case 34 (+35 companion) with a <<< >>> block string, copy-to-scratch and hash: lane-a\\one-call\\case-34-multiline-anchors.md")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_2 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: evidence from tonight: compiler patches were content-anchored pwsh scripts (unify_codegen_fsharp.ps1, unify_main.ps1, r4_deferred.ps1) with multi-line anchors and throw-on-mismatch. These are the multi-line anchor cases the spec lacks.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_3 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:23Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:25 from the bundles (compiler alpha3006, samples alpha705, brzozowski alpha54, workspace alpha2891; details in BUNDLE-WISDOM note 7): OLD/NEW payloads from files or stdin (argv E2BIG at 497 KiB); an exact-N mode for repeated matches; row-local idempotency (the old apply-spec treated a row as done when its replacement text existed anywhere else in the file); refuse an empty write over a non-empty file without expected-old-hash (a source was wiped to 0 bytes); content-addressed inverse-patch receipts and restore --expect-hash; a text-only plan must not need the compiler; hash-checked append; --parents. Spec cases worth adding: the row-local idempotency trap and the empty-payload trap.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_4 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 4u32, "2026-10-17T20:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-17 16:28 evidence from a long agent day: most edits were anchored replacements with an expected count of 1, written as ad-hoc PowerShell helpers; failures came from line-ending changes (a WriteAllLines rewrite turned a whole LF file into CRLF), here-strings that dropped the final newline (gluing two lines), and anchors that matched zero times silently. Needed: preserve each file's line endings and encoding, refuse whole-file EOL changes unless asked, report per-anchor match counts.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_5 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 5u32, "2026-10-18T05:58Z", "claude", "(2026-10-18 01:58 -04:00, Claude fc1568a6) Evidence: the harness->matrix/live/cli_driver rename touched 5 repos, ~45 files, 251 regex replacements plus 4 file/folder moves. text-replace is one file and one literal pair per call (hundreds of calls), so it ran through a scratch Python plan (ordered regex rules per file glob, LF/CRLF kept, plain moves so nothing is staged, dry run first). The verb wants exactly that: a plan of (glob, exclude, ordered regex subs) + moves across roots, with a dry-run listing per file.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_6 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 6u32, "2026-10-19T00:33Z", "claude", "eoie do (EOIE-EDIT-BATCH surface) now covers edits + runs in one call (case 24 green on build 6). Edit failure leaves files untouched and starts no run; a rejected run statement is caught before edits are written. Checked by hand 20:32 (bad anchor -> exit 1, no run; unsupported statement -> exit 2, nothing written).")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_7 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 7u32, "2026-10-21T11:39Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:39 eoie team: multi-line anchors without escaping, decided by measurement (rule R4). eoie do statements accept raw blocks: <<< then a newline opens, the first line whose text starts with >>> closes, and text after >>> keeps tokenizing, so 'replace once <<< ... >>> with <<< ... >>>' works with no quote or backslash escaping. Two builds: (A) a typed Spiral tokenizer replacing the host tokenizer: do_program.rs 806 -> 1209 lines, over the 1000 gate; (B) the same block rule as a branch of the existing one-line tokenizer: no new census line, crate near 806. Chose B (A would need its own crate with an encoded token string across the boundary, which the census counts as drift). Scratch apply-block-strings.ps1, r28 order 15; notebook block_strings_carry_raw_lines_without_escapes (CRLF inside blocks becomes LF, an unclosed block is an unterminated string, an empty block is an empty string).")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_8 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 8u32, "2026-10-21T14:17Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 10:17 eoie team: progress 0 -> 55 from what ships (checked in source, eoie_do_edit): anchored replacements across files with expected counts (once|all|n times), dry-run, a failed anchor writes nothing and starts no run, edit then build in one call (case 24), before/after sha256 in the receipt, CRLF files keep CRLF in replacements (host.spi line 35), writes staged and applied together through temporaries; r28 adds raw <<< >>> blocks for multi-line anchors. Left: (1) apply_patch-style hunks, (2) a multi-root plan of (glob, excludes, ordered regex subs) plus moves with a per-file dry-run listing (the 10-18 rename evidence), (3) refuse an empty whole-file write over a non-empty file without an expected-old hash, (4) spec cases for the row-local idempotency and empty-payload traps.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_9 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 9u32, "2026-10-21T15:33Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 11:33 eoie team: left item 3 drafted for r31 (scratch apply-empty-write-guard.ps1): fs-write's typed proxy_write_action gains RejectEmptyPayload; replacing a non-empty file with an empty payload is refused unless --expect-hash is given, while creating an empty file or replacing an empty one stays allowed. Notebook an_empty_payload_never_wipes_a_nonempty_file. eoie do has no whole-file writes, so fs-write is the only path that needs it. Applies cleanly on scratch copies; compiles wait for tibia's live blocks.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_10 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 10u32, "2026-10-21T15:56Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 11:56 eoie team: r31 drafts cover left items 1, 3 and 4, and all are previewed and notebook-checked on r31-tree. (1) eoie do accepts patch <<< apply_patch hunks >>> inside an edit block. Each hunk becomes one replace-once rule (context plus removed lines as the anchor, context plus added lines as the replacement), so validation, all-or-nothing writes and hash receipts are shared. Stray lines such as *** Begin Patch, no-op hunks and anchorless pure insertions are refused. (3) fs-write refuses an empty payload over a non-empty file without --expect-hash, and eoie do refuses edits that would empty a file. (4) spec cases 35-37 plus notebooks; the row-local idempotency case already passes on the current binary. Generated sizes: do_program.rs 920, do_edit.rs 882, operations.rs 804. Left: item 2, a multi-root regex plan with moves. Progress stays 55 until r31 ships.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_11 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 11u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): raw <<< >>> blocks in eoie do, so multi-line anchors need no escaping.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_12 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 12u32, "2026-10-21T20:36Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:36 eoie team: excludes drafted for r34 (scratch apply-do-except.ps1; applies cleanly on r34-tree). 'edit src/**/*.spi except src/vendor/*,*_test.spi' keeps matching files out of a globbed edit, and the receipt's 'files n of m matched' counts after the exclusion. The model, parser, encoding and runner each change once; parser and CLI notebooks added. Moves remain: do_edit.rs and do_program.rs sit near 950 lines, so moves come with a crate split. Checks run after tibia's live block.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_13 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 13u32, "2026-10-21T20:39Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:39 eoie team: moves drafted for r34 (scratch apply-do-move.ps1; applies cleanly on r34-tree after the excludes slice). 'move <path> to <path>' joins the eoie do write family (append, copy): it refuses an existing target, creates parent folders, renames, and falls back to copy-and-remove for a file across volumes. Absolute paths reach other roots, so the 10-18 rename case (regex rules over several roots plus 4 moves) becomes one program per root, or one call with absolute globs. With r33's pattern rules and absolute globs and r34's excludes and moves, all four left items are drafted. Progress moves when r33 and r34 ship.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_14 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 14u32, "2026-10-22T02:21Z", "x", "2026-10-21 22:21 x: this item holds eoie/lab/EOIE-DO-EDIT (apply-do-except.ps1 and apply-do-move.ps1, named in notes 12-13). The folder name doesn't match the item ID, so the 22:19 lab inventory saw it as an orphan. Ranked under LAB-SESSION-SWEEP note 6 (tag lab-held, priority floor). Renaming the folder to lab/EOIE-EDIT-BATCH, or landing it, is the eoie team's call.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_15 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 15u32, "2026-10-22T02:24Z", "eoie", "22:24 eoie team: lab/EOIE-DO-EDIT renamed to lab/EOIE-EDIT-BATCH (its holder, per x's lab-held sweep); holds apply-do-except.ps1 and apply-do-move.ps1, both slices of r34 (applied from the scratch copies by apply-r34.ps1). The folder goes when r34 lands in the tree.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_16 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 16u32, "2026-10-22T10:41Z", "eoie", "06:41 lab drained by the folder's own exit condition (note of 10-21 22:24: 'the folder goes when r34 lands in the tree'): r34 is in the live tree since release ABC applied it (do write has except and MovePath), the folder was untracked, and the only other mention is an older lab-handoff.ps1 copy map (writes into the folder, never reads it). lab/EOIE-EDIT-BATCH deleted; the slices also live on in the night carry's r34 history.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_17 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 17u32, "2026-10-22T10:42Z", "eoie", "06:42 correction to the previous note: the night carry does not hold the r34 slices. The applied code is what lives on (the live src under eoie_do_write), and the two slice scripts remain only in this session's scratch folder, which next sessions do not see. Nothing depends on them once the tree is committed.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_18 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 18u32, "2026-10-22T22:27Z", "eoie-lane", "18:27 dogfood measurement from today's release train: 81 apply-*.ps1 slices were written or changed in this session today. 55 are plain anchored replacements with count checks (the Swapped helper: exact-once anchor, nothing written on mismatch), 19 also create crates or files, and 7 are regex rewrites with callbacks. Probe on the installed binary: 'eoie do <root>' with 'edit a.spi expect files 1 replacements 1 / replace once \"<multi-line old>\" with \"<multi-line new>\"' applies a 2-line anchor in 2 ms with sha256 before and after; a missing anchor fails with 'anchor not found ... (0 matches, expected 1) / nothing written', exit 1. So the 55 plain slices could each be one do program file (root = the tree to patch, which replaces the -Src parameter). Gaps found: (1) no whole-file write ('write new.spi \"...\"' is rejected as an unsupported statement), needed by the 19 crate-creating slices; (2) no regex-with-captures rewrite for the 7 heal slices. Both are this item's open scope (whole-file writes, apply_patch hunks). Habit change for the next session: write new plain slices as do programs, not PowerShell.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_19 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 19u32, "2026-10-22T22:29Z", "eoie-lane", "18:29 escape rule measured for do programs: in a do string, \\\" is a quote, \\\\ is one backslash, and \\t becomes a real TAB byte (probe: \"a\\tb\\\\c\" wrote a<TAB>b\\c). Spiral source holds literal \\t and \\n escapes inside string literals, so pasting Spiral text into a do replace changes it silently unless every backslash is doubled. A text slice that carries Spiral escapes either doubles them (checked by a byte compare against the intended text) or stays a PowerShell slice. The H claim slice keeps its PowerShell form for that reason; the host-home slice (no escapes) moved to job-host-home.do.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_20 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 20u32, "2026-10-23T18:04Z", "eoie", "14:05 eoie (corrected 14:0x): dogfood from 10-23. I wrote about 12 PowerShell slices today, all carrying Spiral escapes, and 6 of them create crates. Before proposing anything I misread the state: the live do usage already offers raw lines between <<< and a >>> line (no escape processing, so note 19's escape problem is solved) and 'replace pattern' with \\ groups (gap 2 solved). The one real gap left is gap 1, whole-file writes and creates: the write family has append (one quoted line), copy and move, no write or create. Proposal: 'write <path> <<< ... >>>' (refusing an existing path unless 'overwrite') in the write family, so a crate-creating slice becomes one program. Habit for the next session: plain slices as do programs with <<< >>> payloads; only regex-callback heals stay PowerShell.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_21 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 21u32, "2026-10-23T18:06Z", "eoie", "14:06 eoie: path for the whole-file write, read from the code. eoie_do/decode.spi routes by verb (has_write: append|copy|move -> Writes -> eoie_do_write, 785 generated lines, room to grow); the statements are parsed earlier by eoie_do_program_compiled (eoie_do_program, 988 lines), which is where <<< >>> raw blocks are captured for replace, and which rejects unknown verbs as 'unsupported statement'. A 'write <path> <<< ... >>>' therefore needs (1) the verb in the parser's verb union (GE2's heal-do-verbs makes those a reflection union) with raw-block capture extended from replace to write, which needs a crate boundary first because do_program is at the gate; (2) a WriteFile (path, text, overwrite) step in eoie_do_write's write_step union with lib file_system writes; (3) 'write' added to has_write. Order: after GE2, starting with the do_program split.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_22 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 22u32, "2026-10-23T22:13Z", "eoie", "18:13 eoie: gap 1 (whole-file writes) drafted as scratch do-write-file/apply-do-write-file.ps1; dry run on the post-O tree is clean and idempotent; typecheck waits for tibia's live block to end. Correction to my 14:06 note: no crate boundary in eoie_do_program is needed. Its tokenizer already turns any <<< ... >>> block into a quoted token wherever it appears, and write-family verbs reach eoie_do_write as generic query steps, so do_program (988 lines) is untouched. The slice: eoie_do_verbs gains WriteFileVerb (word write), picked up by reflection. eoie_do_write gains a typed write_mode (CreateOnly | Overwrite), a WriteFile step and file_write_parsed (write <file> [overwrite] <<< text >>>; refuses a quoted file name or a missing block). write_run goes through lib file_system (file_exists, directory_get_parent plus create_dir_all, write_all_text), not new host blobs, and refuses an existing file without overwrite, naming the fix. The receipt reads 'files N written bytes B', and the do help line in direct_verbs lists the statement. A grammar test covers create, overwrite with an empty block and both refusals. Absence check: write is not a read verb (0 hits; control 'append' 3 hits on the same glob).")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_23 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 23u32, "2026-10-23T23:32Z", "eoie", "19:32 eoie: dogfood proof that slices can be eoie do programs. Heal wave 1, written as a PowerShell slice tonight, was rewritten as one do program (scratch heal-wave-1/heal-wave-1.do: 4 edit blocks, 7 replace-once rules with <<< >>> raw payloads, so zero escaping, and expect files 1 replacements N on each block). Run with 'eoie do <tree> -' on an emulated post-N tree: ok files 4 replacements 7 in 15 ms, sha256 before and after per file, and all 4 files byte-identical to the PowerShell slice's result. A rerun refuses with 'anchor not found, nothing written', so it is fail-safe without hand-written guards. Plan, after tonight's train settles: fmt-rebase accepts .do slices (pipe the program into eoie do <work> -), and new plain slices are written as .do; crate-creating slices follow once release Q's do write installs. This removes the class of PowerShell slips I hit today (here-strings losing their newline, merged statements, backslash-quote).")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_24 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 24u32, "2026-10-23T23:33Z", "eoie", "19:33 eoie: probe on the installed binary: 'append f.txt <<< ... >>>' already appends a multi-line raw block (3 lines, 27 bytes, the newline before it added), so 'append a test block at a file's end' needs no new verb. The real remaining gap for one-call slices is the mix rule: eoie_do_write's grammar refuses a program that has both edit and write statements ('edit and write statements cannot share one program yet; run them as two calls'). Most slices edit some files and append a test or create a file, so today they would take 2 calls. Gap 3: let one program run its edit blocks, then its write statements, as one guarded batch, with a single receipt counting files, replacements, appended and written. That makes a typical slice one call.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_25 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 25u32, "2026-10-23T23:34Z", "eoie", "19:34 eoie: gap 3 placement, read from eoie_do (decode.spi and run.spi, 270 generated lines, so there is room; eoie_do_edit sits at 976 and can't grow). program_decoded already classifies the compiled records by family (Runs becomes Edits when has_edit). Plan: a compiled_program case Split of edit_records * write_records when the program holds edit blocks and write-family queries. program_run delegates edits to eoie_do_edit first and, only on exit 0, the writes to eoie_do_write, returning the first nonzero code. v1 prints two receipts; a merged receipt can come later. Needed first: a record encoder in eoie_do_records (records_encoded of a subset: the ok header plus chosen records with their 30/31 separators), replacing do_write's host-blob record_alone. eoie_do/host.spi's stdin_text blob is the one lib console.read_to_end replaces (EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN step 3).")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_26 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 26u32, "2026-10-23T23:35Z", "eoie", "19:35 eoie: train support drafted: scratch fmt-rebase/apply-do-slices.ps1 makes fmt-rebase run a slice whose name ends in .do as 'eoie do <stage work> -' (program on stdin), stopping the stage on a nonzero exit, while .ps1 slices run as before. Dry run on a copy of fmt-rebase.ps1 is idempotent and parses. Applied after tonight's train settles, since train scripts stay frozen while it runs; from then on, plain slices are written as .do.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_27 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 27u32, "2026-10-23T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 eoie: gap 3 drafted (scratch do-split/apply-do-split.ps1; dry run on the real post-P tree is clean and idempotent). eoie_do_records gains records_by_kinds, which keeps the ok header plus records whose kind is or isn't in a |-list, rejoined with the 30/31 separators (a host decoder, which is that package's rule). decode.spi: a program the family classifier calls Writes that also has edit records becomes Split of the edit set and the write set. The edit family, read from eoie_do_program compiled.spi, is edit, except, replace, pattern and show. My first draft filtered on 'edit' alone and would have sent the replace rules to the write crate; reading compiled.spi caught it before any compile. run.spi: Split runs eoie_do_edit on the edit set and, only on exit 0, eoie_do_write on the write set. Acceptance probe for its release: one program that edits a file, appends a test block and writes a new file, run once, then all three effects checked; and an edit whose anchor is missing must leave the append and write undone.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_28 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 28u32, "2026-10-23T23:54Z", "eoie", "19:54 eoie: second conversion: note-as-first as scratch note-as-first/note-as-first.do (2 replace-once rules) plus note-as-first-test.do (an append of the test block). On an emulated post-M tree, task_notes.spi is byte-identical to the PowerShell slice's result, and notes_cli.spi is equal except that do ends the file with a newline, the better form. Hazard found on the way, which argues for gap 3: run on a base that already had the change, the edit program refused correctly (files 0), but the separate append program still ran and duplicated the test block, while the PowerShell slice's 'already applied' guard skipped both. In one mixed program (do-split, release Q) a failed edit skips the writes, so the pair becomes fail-safe. Until Q installs, a .do slice that appends must be applied once per stage on a fresh raw copy, which is exactly what fmt-rebase does.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_29 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 29u32, "2026-10-24T00:00Z", "eoie", "20:00 eoie: third conversion probe, the file-creating part. log-sections' two new modules came from one do statement, copy <absolute scratch>/log-sections/blocks/section_*.spi to eoie_log_sequences (2 files, 13,035 bytes, 7 ms, both byte-identical to the drafts). So crate-creating slices are do programs today without waiting for write: copy the draft crate folder (its .spi files plus a pre-written Cargo.toml) into place, then edit blocks wire it in. Until do-split installs (release Q) that is 2 calls, copy then edits; after it, 1 call. The slice kinds I wrote this week, anchored edits, test appends and crate or file creation, are therefore all expressible as eoie do programs.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_30 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 30u32, "2026-10-24T00:18Z", "eoie", "20:18 gap 4 from tonight's lib-reuse heals: five heal programs (51 edit blocks) were generated by ad-hoc pwsh loops only because 'edit <glob> replace all' cannot say 'only where the file's package lists spiral-'; one generator forgot that check and targeted 2 packages without it (caught by an audit, reverted with an inverse program). Proposed grammar: edit <glob> where sibling <name> ~ <re> [expect files n replacements n], reusing the dirs-having matcher, so a lib swap is one statement and the precondition is part of the program instead of each generator. Also a 'skipped n (where)' count in the receipt so the excluded packages stay visible.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_31 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 31u32, "2026-10-24T00:38Z", "eoie", "20:38 gap 4 drafted as eoie do programs (scratch edit-where\\edit-where.do, 8 files 16 replacements, plus edit-where-scope.do appending the new module): header option 'where sibling <name> has line \"<text>\"' (exact trimmed line, no regex: lib has no portable matcher and package lists want exact lines). Model: union path_condition = AnyPath | SiblingHasLine name line, a field of edit_block. Parser branch + test, encoder 'where' record between except and the rules, decoder, files_run skips paths out of scope, do-split's kind list gains where. New module eoie_do_edit/scope.spi is lib only (sm'.last_index_of/substring/trim/lines, am'.any, file_system.file_exists/read_all_text, </>) with 2 tests; eoie_do_program and eoie_do_edit gain spiral-. Applies on the emulated post-train tree. Typecheck job-1792802262400 queued behind the post-block checks. Receipt skip count deferred: excluded files simply don't count toward expect.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_32 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 32u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): EDIT-1 (stdin and file payloads; raw <<< >>> blocks shipped, whole-file writes drafted as gap 1), EDIT-2 (empty-write guard, r31 RejectEmptyPayload), EDIT-3 (create, delete and move in the same program; rollback after a failed gate goes to EOIE-DO-UNDO), EDIT-4 (byte-exact payloads; CRLF kept already), EDIT-8 (append with an expected-tail hash). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_33 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 33u32, "2026-10-24T02:50Z", "eoie", "22:50 two write-family gaps hit while drafting a crate split: (1) 'copy src/Cargo.toml to <dir>' copies 0 files and fails, because a plain file path is not treated as a one-file glob (src/Cargo.* works); (2) 'append <new-dir>/<file> <<< >>>' fails with os error 3 when the folder doesn't exist, so a program that creates a new package needs a mkdir outside eoie. Both should just work: copy accepts a file path, and append/write create missing parent folders and report them in the receipt (folders N created).")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_34 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 34u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 300m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D6 at 60%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_35 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 35u32, "2026-10-25T14:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 10:29 tibia use case for one program mixing edit and write: a lib land that adds a module (6 files edited, 1 copied in) runs as land.do then copy.do ('edit and write statements cannot share one program yet'), so a failed copy needs the runner to undo the edits itself, and the undo program can't remove the copied file (no delete statement), the runner removes it by literal path. Wanted: one program for both, with the generated undo covering created files. Related bug: EOIE-DO-COPY-LITERAL.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_36 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 36u32, "2026-10-25T20:50Z", "eoie", "16:50 kit: gap hit 7 times today. Proving that a do program still applies to the current tree needs a copy of its targets plus a run against the copy, because dry-run exists only per edit block, not for a whole program. I wrote a scratch stand-in (clean\\do-dry.ps1: parse the edit/append/write targets, copy them to a temp root, run eoie do on the copy with the program on stdin, delete the copy). The real feature: a do --dry-run flag that applies the whole program in memory, prints the same per-file receipt (lines, sha256 before and after) and writes nothing. It runs safely during a tree lease, since it only reads. That would replace every per-draft dry-run script from today (standards, CI fixture, lint wiring, cases-find, 3 S stages).")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_37 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 37u32, "2026-10-27T17:04Z", "kit", "kit (fc1568a6): whole-program dry run drafted and staged for the next release train as step 5e of next-train.ps1 (scratch clean/edit-batch/dry-run.do, one do program over 5 files). 'kit do <root> --dry-run <program>|-' chains every edit block in memory through the same pending-writes table the real run uses, so block 2 sees block 1's result. It prints the same per-file rows with would-be sha256 values, ends with 'dry run, nothing written', and commits nothing. Design: the flag travels as the encoded program's header word ('dry' in place of 'ok'). Every consumer skips record 0, so kit_do_edit needs no second export. Edit programs with run statements preview the edits and report 'runs not started'. Read and fact programs run unchanged. Write, git, job and lab programs, and edit+write splits, are refused with exit 2, because they have no in-memory form yet. Size: do_edit.rs is 981 -> 992 lines after migrating 7 legacy concat5 sites to ++# and inlining 2 single-use macro wrappers; kit_do_edit now references the lib (spiral-). Test: edit_cli.spi a_dry_run_chains_every_edit_in_memory_and_writes_nothing, added to TRAIN's candidate notebooks. Left on this item: dry runs for write programs (an in-memory write table), then mixed edit+write programs with delete and undo. Proof: kit built from the probe crates on the node (mirror dryrun, cargo build -p kit-cli), smoke clean/edit-batch/dry-run-smoke.ps1 11/11 pass: a chained dry run leaves the file untouched; stdin works; a write program is refused (exit 2) and writes nothing; a read program runs; an edit+run program previews and starts no run; a failing anchor still exits 1; a missing program prints usage. Each refusal or preview has a positive control that applies without --dry-run.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_38 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 38u32, "2026-10-28T09:35Z", "eoie", "05:3x kit: 'kit do --dry-run' smoke on the installed binary: 11 checks, 0 failed (in-memory chained edits, file untouched, write and run programs refused, each with a positive control).")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_39 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 39u32, "2026-10-31T09:59Z", "kit", "10-31 06:0x kit groom (WIP limit, dangling rule 'no Active silent > 3 d'): Active -> Planned at 85%. No note for about 3 days, so this item isn't in progress. Planned keeps it in 'agile next --team kit' as ready work (Paused would park it out of the queue). Kit had 42 Active items, with Active meaning anything ever touched rather than being worked now. The progress and notes stay; whoever pulls it next sets it Active again.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_40 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 40u32, "2026-11-04T08:04Z", "kit", "DONE 11-04 04:5x (kit, closing with evidence): the last milestone shipped. The installed kit's `kit do` usage shows `--dry-run` (\"chains every edit in memory and writes nothing\"), program on stdin with `-`, and the edit statements with expected counts ([expect files <n> replacements <n>], replace once). Anchored replacements, whole-file writes, rehearsal and atomic receipts with hashes were in earlier milestones. Further edit-language work belongs to the KIT-ENTRY-X-S4 do-verb typing.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_batch_41 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", 41u32, "2026-11-06T11:22Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-EDIT-BATCH (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_table_query_1 () = Note ("KIT-TABLE-QUERY", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:22Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-TABLE-QUERY (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_repo_state_1 () = Note ("KIT-REPO-STATE", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:22Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-REPO-STATE (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:25Z", "lane-a", "08:25 lane-a: generator prototype lane-a\\one-call\\guide-from-spec.ps1: one-call.md -> guide section (33 intents, recorded-call counts, the exact eoie do program each) so the guide cannot drift from the spec; output agent-guide.generated.md. Real one lands with eoie do.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 2u32, "2026-10-14T15:16Z", "main-thread", "11:16 friction (main): 'agile set <root> ID progress 90' answered 'progress must be 0..100'. A non-numeric word in the progress slot reads as an out-of-range number; better: name the slot, show the word received, print the usage line.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: new friction for the guide: agile note text with embedded double quotes is split by PowerShell's native argument passing (received 6 operands). The guide should say to avoid inner double quotes or pass the text from a file.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 4u32, "2026-10-20T12:20Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 08:20 eoie lane: the 10-14 friction is fixed in source: agile set names the slot and echoes the word, e.g. agile set: progress must be a whole number 0..100, received '90%'; usage: agile set <root> <id> <progress> <Planned|Active|Blocked|Paused|Done> (same for status). The quoting friction is covered by eoie --args-from (10-18).")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 5u32, "2026-10-21T06:47Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:47 in-repo half drafted (scratch agent-guide + apply-guide.ps1, dry-run clean, compile pending): eoie guide <spec/one-call.md> [--out file.md] walks the spec and writes, per '## N.' intent, the first Habit line as 'Instead of:' and the command block as 'One call:'; expected/fixture blocks are left out, so the guide cannot drift from the spec. Routed as direct verb VerbGuide (its name comes from the reflected union). CLI notebook over a two-case mini spec. Inserting the guide into each client's AGENTS.md stays with the bootstrap work that needs the user's go; the adoption sweep is not started.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 6u32, "2026-10-21T11:47Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:47 eoie team, insertion decided under the user's 07:05 direction (decide myself, by evidence). No active repo has an AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md, and there is no global ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md or ~/.codex/AGENTS.md, so 'insertion' would mean creating global instruction files for every client: the widest-reaching form. Chosen instead (smallest and reversible): after r28 installs, 'eoie guide spec/one-call.md --out docs/one-call-guide.md' writes the guide into the eoie repo, and one Claude memory entry (the project memory all three lanes share) points to it with the top habit -> one-call rows. Global client config files stay untouched until a client is shown not to read the repo doc.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_7 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 7u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): eoie guide spec/one-call.md --out <file> (34 intents, checked 15:42); docs/one-call-guide.md is written at the r30 apply.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_8 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 8u32, "2026-10-21T20:41Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:41 eoie team: adoption metric defined before the first run (scratch adoption-sweep/adoption-sweep.ps1, a lab script once the next release publishes). Per client and day: shell_calls = Claude PowerShell/Bash tool_use and Codex shell/exec_command function_call entries; eoie_calls = those invoking the eoie executable; habit_calls = the rest that use the file and text cmdlets or python that eoie replaces (Get-Content, Select-String, Get-ChildItem, Set/Add-Content, ForEach-Object, Measure-Object, Copy/Remove/Move-Item, Test-Path, Start-Sleep, robocopy, findstr, python, [IO.File]); eoie_share = eoie/shell. Output is a TSV for eoie table query. Dedicated tools (Read, Grep, Edit) are not shell calls and are left out. OpenCode keeps no transcripts on this machine; zed is not parsed yet.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_9 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 9u32, "2026-10-21T20:41Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:41 eoie team: first adoption sweep (20 s over 1.2 GB of transcripts, last 7 days). Claude eoie_share per day: 10-15 0.116, 10-16 0.067, 10-17 0.211, 10-18 0.197, 10-19 0.310, 10-20 0.382, 10-21 0.518 (3443 shell calls today: 1785 eoie, 1557 habit). 7-day totals: 23804 shell, 6401 eoie, 15537 habit. Codex: no sessions in the window. So the habit share falls as verbs land, and half of today's shell calls are already one eoie call. The remaining habits are the next mining source (the same intent clustering as the 10-13 sweep). Left on the item: make the sweep recurring (an eoie verb or a scheduled job writing this TSV daily) and the shared memory pointer to docs/one-call-guide.md once the r30 apply writes it.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_10 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 10u32, "2026-10-21T20:46Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:46 eoie team: what today's 1557 habit calls are (Claude, all sessions, by cmdlet mention): Get-Content 1110, Select-String 1074, ForEach-Object 854, Get-ChildItem 469, Get-Date 345, Measure-Object 143, Copy-Item 136, Get-FileHash 114, Test-Path 85, Remove-Item 81. (A first count put 'git' at 2243, but that was the path C:\\\\home\\\\git in every command; real git calls were about 130: diff 35, status 27, apply 12, log 12.) So the largest remaining habits are reads and searches that eoie read/grep already cover: an adoption gap the guide addresses, not missing verbs. One concrete source of Get-Date: lanes prefix every agile note with local time because notes record only UTC 'at'. If agile note and show printed local time with its offset, that habit would go away; filing it as an EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES papercut.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_11 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 11u32, "2026-10-21T21:05Z", "claude", "17:05 r35 slice drafted: 'eoie adoption [--days N] [--out f]' (crate eoie_adoption: shell_call/agent_client unions, day_tally, classify notebook with 9 commands, offset line walk). Classification now needs a verb word after eoie (cd into the eoie folder no longer counts); patterns checked in .NET against this session's log (3748 commands). Applies after r34 per r35-plan.md.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_12 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 12u32, "2026-10-22T02:57Z", "eoie", "22:57 heal plan: the generated guide gets an eoie style contract section and eoie gets a generated AGENTS.md (none exists today in eoie or any sibling): eoie code older than the heal is not a style model; copy from lib/spiral functions, x boundary modules, tibia domain modules and the healed crates listed in standards/; the lib comes first and missing generic helpers go into lib/spiral.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_13 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 13u32, "2026-10-22T03:42Z", "eoie", "23:42 style contract drafted (scratch agents-contract/AGENTS.md, placed in eoie after release A publishes, then generated by eoie guide once the guide gains a contract section): eoie is generic; code here is not a style model unless listed in standards/healed.tsv; copy from lib/spiral first, then x's boundary modules, tibia's domain modules, healed eoie packages; model first (GADTs, existentials, result unions); host code only in the OS boundary package; missing generic helpers go into lib/spiral; every package references spiral-; touching legacy migrates it; Spiral test blocks; 1000-line crates. Points at eoie standards --ratchet and the edit-time hook.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_14 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 14u32, "2026-10-22T04:24Z", "eoie", "00:24 placed (before release ABC snapshots the tree): eoie/AGENTS.md style contract (generic project; legacy code is not a model unless listed healed; copy order lib/spiral > x boundary modules > tibia domain modules > healed eoie packages; host code only in the OS boundary; gaps go into lib/spiral; spiral- everywhere; touching legacy migrates it) and standards/healed.spi as Spiral data (union healed_package = Healed : string * string; first row eoie_tree_lease). Next: eoie guide generates the contract section so it can't drift from the rules the census checks.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_15 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 15u32, "2026-10-22T05:07Z", "eoie", "01:07 evidence the contract works at edit time: reading a file under C:\\home\\git\\eoie (compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1) made Claude Code attach eoie/AGENTS.md to the tool result automatically, so any agent opening eoie files now sees 'legacy code here is not a style model; copy from lib/spiral, x boundary modules, tibia domain modules, healed packages' right where it works, which is the countermeasure to imitating surrounding code.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_16 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 16u32, "2026-10-22T05:09Z", "eoie", "01:09 peer replies: x and tibia both declined adding an AGENTS.md on a peer suggestion (an agent-instructions file is the user's call; x asks the user in its 07:00 report). Tibia's point stands: rules belong in checks first (tibia's build refuses host calls in lua.spi; eoie standards reads per-package src/standards.spi rules), prose last. So eoie/AGENTS.md stays a pointer for the user to confirm or veto, and the enforceable rules go into eoie standards (census lib-first metrics in ABC, per-package standards.spi rules next) and the edit hook.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_17 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 17u32, "2026-10-22T09:33Z", "eoie", "05:33 decided by rule instead of asking (memory decide-autonomously, 10-22 version): eoie/AGENTS.md stays if (1) it names no external project, user or path, and (2) every rule it states points at an enforcing check that exists. Measured: 0 external names since the 04:01 generic-drift slice; it points at standards/healed.spi and standards/census.spi (both present), the eoie standards --ratchet verb and the PostToolUse style hook (installed). Both hold, so it stays. It is the edit-time half of the style contract: Claude Code attaches it when any file under eoie is read. Revisit when release D's standards rules replace healed.spi.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_18 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 18u32, "2026-10-22T19:57Z", "eoie-lane", "15:57 revisit (the 05:33 rule's trigger fired: AGENTS.md now points at per-package standards.spi rules, not healed.spi). Re-checking condition (2), every stated rule has an enforcing check: host code (HostFree + style hook host-macro patterns), lib reference (LibReferenced + hook spiproj check), legacy migration (census ratchet), Spiral test blocks (TestTargets), 1000 lines (topology gate), joined-string rows (census encoded-row), number tables (census number-arm, pipe-table) all hold. Missing: 'no empty-string success sentinels' and 'a missing helper goes into lib/spiral' (only the hook's host-macro advice). Rule written before measuring: a census probe empty-sentinel (pattern '= \\x22\\x22 then', a ratchet so it may not rise per package) is added only if at least 14 of 20 sampled live-tree hits (every k-th hit) are success or absence sentinels rather than ordinary empty-text checks; otherwise the AGENTS.md line is reworded to what the checks enforce. The lib-helper line stays as advice under the host-macro check (it is the hook's own message), so it counts as pointing at that check.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_19 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 19u32, "2026-10-22T19:59Z", "eoie-lane", "15:59 measured (287 hits of '= \\x22\\x22 then' in src/*.spi; every 14th hit sampled, 20 rows): 10 are absence or success sentinels (missing argument or option, rejection = empty, missing row or status), 10 are ordinary checks (accumulator joins, blank lines, empty joined lists). 10/20 is below the 14/20 bar, so by the rule no empty-sentinel probe is added and the AGENTS.md line is reworded to what the checks enforce: scratch agent-guide/apply-agents-checked-lines.ps1 (dry run on a copy: 1 line changed), applied once F's tree lease ends. New text: 'Model the domain first: unions, GADTs, existentials, records, result unions for outcomes. No joined-string rows or number tables: the census counts both per package and the ratchet refuses growth.' A narrower probe keyed on success-named bindings (problem/rejection/refusal = empty) would need its own item and a rule written before measuring; it is not adopted here.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_20 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 20u32, "2026-10-22T21:17Z", "eoie-lane", "17:17 applied: AGENTS.md's domain line now states what the census enforces (1 line changed).")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_21 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 21u32, "2026-10-23T10:26Z", "eoie", "06:26 adoption measured with the installed verb (eoie adoption, release F): eoie share of Claude shell calls per day 10-16 0.041, 10-17 0.141, 10-18 0.127, 10-19 0.258, 10-20 0.340, 10-21 0.481, 10-22 0.470, 10-23 (to 06:2x) 0.380; total 32,687 shell calls, 10,317 eoie, 19,447 habit (file/text cmdlets, python), share 0.316. The receipt's days=7 is the requested window (days_back, default 7) while the table also prints today, so 8 rows: correct, but the label reads like a row count; a clearer receipt would say days_back=7 rows=8 (eoie_adoption run.spi:83). Only the claude client appears, and that is right: ~/.codex/sessions and ~/.local/share/opencode hold 0 session files written in the last 8 days (checked 06:28, credential and account files skipped).")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_22 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 22u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): CLI-1 (help with signature, valid values, preconditions and an example, derived from dispatch; release self-check), CLI-3 (map a need to an existing verb). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_23 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 23u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 30m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 85%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_24 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 24u32, "2026-10-25T19:34Z", "eoie", "15:34 kit: adoption re-measured with the installed verb at 15:3x. eoie share of Claude shell calls per day:\n- 10-18 0.127, 10-19 0.258, 10-20 0.340, 10-21 0.481 (peak), 10-22 0.470, 10-23 0.406, 10-24 0.356, 10-25 0.296 so far.\n- 7-day total: 39,346 shell calls, of which 14,094 eoie and 21,874 habit; share 0.358.\n- The share has fallen 4 days running since the peak.\n- Likely causes, unmeasured: the freeze and the release work push sessions into PowerShell glue (waiters, probes, hash checks, scratch drafts). Today's session alone leaned on Get-Content tails, Get-CimInstance process probes and Select-String for things eoie has verbs for (job tail, read, search).\n- '--days-back 3' was ignored (receipt days=7). The flag ships with SLATE (kept stage adoption-days-back), so re-check it after the install.\n- Next, after the rename: the adoption verb should break habit calls down by cluster (as the 10-14 re-mine did by hand), so the guide targets the largest habit first.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_25 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 25u32, "2026-10-27T15:39Z", "kit", "11:4x kit: re-checked on the installed kit.exe: '--days-back 3' is still ignored (receipt days_back=7, 8 day rows), because the parser only knows '--days' (kit_adoption/run.spi adoption_run) and silently skips unknown options; '--days 3' works (days_back=3). The receipt and the guides say days_back, so the fix accepts --days-back as the spelling and keeps --days (clean/kit-rename/adoption-days-back.do, 1 replacement; compiles on the composed train, adoption.rs 661). It rides the next train (next-train.ps1 step 5b). Left after that: the habit-cluster breakdown (note 'Next, after the rename').")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_26 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 26u32, "2026-10-27T15:44Z", "kit", "11:4x kit: the habit-cluster breakdown exists: 'kit adoption --habits' ranks habit calls by cmdlet. Last 3 days on this box: foreach-object 8,607, get-content 6,973, select-string 5,497, get-childitem 3,739, [io.file] 2,789, test-path 1,582. So the guide's first targets are Get-Content (kit read) and Select-String (kit grep); my own lane is a large share of that today. Remaining here: the --days-back spelling fix (next-train step 5b), then close.")
inl note_of_kit_agent_guide_27 () = Note ("KIT-AGENT-GUIDE", 27u32, "2026-10-28T09:35Z", "eoie", "05:3x kit: verified on the installed TRAIN2c binary: 'kit adoption --days-back 3' now gives days_back=3 (shell_calls 17592, kit_calls 807, kit_share 0.046). The flag fix shipped. Done; the adoption share itself is tracked by the habit work.")
