inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_4 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:26 lap 1 grain: promotion oracle refreshed: the current single-flight oracle is pack 5's (single-flight-20261019-072348 review, 7,007 rows, DISAGREE 0; accept running), up from 4,147 rows on 10-15; the AGENTS.md criterion is judged against that Next: update the row count once the pack 5 accept lands")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_5 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:59 lap 2 grain: promotion oracle is now the pack 5 baseline: single-flight-20261019-080524 accepted 7,007 rows (DISAGREE 0), up from 4,147 on 10-15 Next: unchanged until P3 closes")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_6 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 6u32, "2026-10-19T18:41Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:41 lap 4 grain: retirement scope measured: deleting gpt5parallel removes 104,783 + 3,736 + 36 + 255 lines of gpt5parallel sides plus the adapter halves, about 110k of the 186,825-line file, and every #if SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL marker; gated by the promotion criterion for hopacsota Next: none until P2-P4")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_7 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 7u32, "2026-10-22T13:47Z", "main", "2026-10-22 09:47 main: user direction 2026-10-22 07:3x: \"we need to iterate until getting rid of gpt5parallel\". This is the user's decision on deletion that this item's title waited for. The gate stays the promotion criterion for hopacsota (no missing, DISAGREE 0, all ok, apps/spiral compiles, Gate 2 determinism), because parallel evaluation is non-negotiable and hopacsota must carry it before gpt5parallel goes. Path: P2 (waits census on a quiet box), P3-P5, then this deletion.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_8 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 8u32, "2026-10-22T13:54Z", "main", "2026-10-22 09:54 main: deletion groundwork done and proven without a build. (1) Get-SpiralCoreProjection (apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/env.ps1) gains -KeepDefines: listed defines keep their blocks verbatim with markers, and #if !SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL now resolves too (before it was kept with its markers). Projecting -Mode single-flight -KeepDefines hopacsota gives the core with gpt5parallel removed. (2) Found and fixed on the way, by a tiny-file A/B against HEAD: PowerShell names are case-insensitive, so a parameter named $Keep would have aliased the function's local $keep = $true and kept every line; the parameter is named KeepDefines. HEAD behaviour is unchanged for existing callers (tiny file: single-flight a|single|plain|z, gpt5parallel a|gpt|plain|z, hopacsota a|single|hop|z). (3) Measured on the tree core: the retired file is 33,590 lines, down from 188,234, so deleting gpt5parallel removes about 154.6k lines (82%). The 10-19 estimate of about 110k undercounted the gpt5-only blocks: 11-6148, 7363-42304, 54135-158938, 183268-187005 and so on. (4) Proof the deletion changes neither shipping core: the single-flight and hopacsota projections of the retired file are byte-identical (sha256) to the tree's. Left in the retired file: 7 comment mentions plus the layer header BlockParsingGpt5parallel (single-flight side = no-op hook, to rename at deletion). Outside the core, 22 files mention gpt5parallel (351 hits): scripts (build/test/probe/pair-drift/projection-hash/fast-tier/env/upstream.py/hopacsota-g3/test-rust-exports), fsproj/props/Program.fs defines, and docs (tmp/AGENTS.md 48, NEXT.md 120, FRONTIER.md 62, PLAN.md 16, README 8). Deletion recipe once the promotion gate passes: replace spiral_compiler.fs with the -KeepDefines hopacsota projection, drop -Mode gpt5parallel from the scripts and the define from the projects, retire lanes/gpt5parallel docs, then build both modes plus a hopacsota -Suite all.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_9 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 9u32, "2026-10-22T13:55Z", "main", "2026-10-22 09:55 main: one compatibility note on the env.ps1 change. The default projection now also resolves the two #if !SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL blocks (lines 6149 and 49071 of the tree core): single-flight and hopacsota drop just their markers, and gpt5parallel drops their content. So a projection-hash.ps1 hash printed after this change differs from one recorded before it, even for an unchanged core. Comparisons inside one run (projection-hash, -Seams against a base, lab g6) are unaffected because both sides use the same function. Only a comparison against a hash recorded before 2026-10-22 10:0x needs a fresh baseline.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_10 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 10u32, "2026-10-22T22:12Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:12 main: what is left of the promotion criterion, and the order, fixed before measuring. Already met: Gate 2 determinism (G4: 5 fresh processes at 8 workers equal on every frontier and mega-root row), apps/spiral byte-identical at 1 and 8 workers (10-20), and G3 0 cells compile-only over 7,290 rows at 1/2/4/8 workers. Missing: (a) -Suite all -NATIVE in -Mode hopacsota on the installed core (G3 was compile-only, so native DISAGREE is unmeasured for hopacsota), and (b) a fresh apps/spiral compile on the current core. Order next session, after PACK-NEXT-3b is accepted: build -Mode hopacsota, then test.ps1 -Mode hopacsota -Suite all -Native -Parallel 2, compared with the next3b accept run. Rule: 0 missing, DISAGREE 0, every row ok (none emitted), compile status and residual hash equal to single-flight on every row, and apps/spiral compiles. If all hold, the deletion runs: Get-SpiralCoreProjection -Mode single-flight -KeepDefines hopacsota writes the retired core (proven 09:54: both shipping projections byte-identical), installed in a compiler-install window, with build.ps1/test.ps1 gpt5parallel modes and the AGENTS.md gpt5parallel sections removed in the same change. Your direction today counts as the go; the commit stays yours.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_11 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 11u32, "2026-10-22T22:13Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:13 main: deletion scope beyond the core, counted. tmp/scripts has 33 gpt5parallel references in 10 files: pair-drift.ps1 11 (its #if pair check would cover only the hopacsota pairs afterwards), env.ps1 6 (modes, projection), test.ps1 5 (mode switch, warm recycle defaults), build.ps1 3, fast-tier.ps1 2, upstream.py 2, and 1 each in hopacsota-g3, probe, projection-hash and test-rust-exports. tmp/AGENTS.md has 48 mentions (the lane table, promotion status history, knobs), and lanes/gpt5parallel holds 2 files. The same change rewrites these; the 2-file lane folder's FRONTIER history is kept as the record of how parity was reached, unless you want it gone.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_12 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 12u32, "2026-10-22T22:15Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:15 main: both steps scripted and dry-run. (1) %TEMP%\\hopacsota-promotion\\run.ps1: hopacsota build, then -Suite all -Native, then compare with the next3b accept run, printing the note-10 rule. (2) %TEMP%\\hopacsota-promotion\\delete-gpt5parallel.ps1: it projects the CURRENT tree core with -KeepDefines hopacsota, refuses unless the single-flight and hopacsota projections of the retired file equal the tree's, and with -Apply installs it in a compiler-install window. Dry run on today's tree: 33,590 of 188,234 lines kept, 0 gpt5parallel markers left, single-flight EE7CED6A9A49AC35 and hopacsota 12CD702D1C583B9B equal on both sides. Because it projects whatever core is installed when it runs, it needs no recomposition after next3b.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_13 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 13u32, "2026-10-22T22:59Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:59 main: promotion run queued as job-1792709979052-main-hopacsota-promotion (heavy, after main-pack-next4-review). It builds -Mode hopacsota from the tree core and runs -Suite all -Native. The reference was corrected before the run: the script compared with next3b's review run, but the tree core is pack-after-night's 25D2BE7C94943D8E, so next3b's Rust/WGSL/Bend changes would have shown as false differences. It now compares with single-flight-20261022-075350, the review run of that same core, and refuses to compare automatically if the tree core moved. The deletion (delete-gpt5parallel.ps1 -Apply) stays a separate step after the note-10 rule is read, plus apps/spiral compiling.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_14 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 14u32, "2026-10-23T00:42Z", "main", "2026-10-22 20:42 main: promotion run stopped and requeued. hopacsota-20261022-194751 finished 798 of 7,348 rows in 53 min (2 workers, BelowNormal, beside eoie's verify and my Cells gates), which projects to about 8 h against its 4 h job cap. It could not finish, and its samples/ writes would have overlapped next4's matrix (~23:00). Requeued as job-1792716089365 with an 8 h cap at the end of the overnight chain (F5, then next5, next4, cells-pack, then this), so it runs alone. The stopped run left samples/ outputs mixed: rows it reached hold pack-after-night hopacsota output, the rest hold next3b's. Each later matrix rewrites what it compiles, and restoring tracked outputs is the user's git. Speed note for P2: on a loaded box hopacsota runs about 15 rows/min against single-flight's ~290/min in the 18:42 next3b compile phase (7,348 in 1,161 s).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_15 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 15u32, "2026-10-23T00:45Z", "main", "2026-10-22 20:45 main: per-row compile time, hopacsota-20261022-194751 vs single-flight-20261022-184215 over the 782 rows ok in both: sum 2,469,882 vs 226,478 ms (10.9x), median row 8.4x, p90 109x. The tail is trivial samples taking 40-55 s that single-flight compiles in 18-150 ms: array2_heap_union_heap_union_heap_pair_heaps Lean 54,620 vs 149 ms, closure_apply TypeScript 49,621 vs 124, closure_curry Fsharp 45,130 vs 65, array_heap_heap_heap C 42,984 vs 50, c_deep_list_decref TypeScript 42,111 vs 18. Times that large on trivial inputs point to a per-build stall, a wait or timeout in the engine's setup or teardown, not evaluation (candidates: Engine.Close's thread.Join or Scheduler.wait, Drain, body-thread start with a 1.5 GB stack reserve, a stuck bridge). Next session, on a quiet box: probe.ps1 closure_curry -Mode hopacsota -Repeat 5 -Stacks 10 to take a dotnet-stack dump inside the stall. The promotion criterion can't be met until the stall is gone.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_16 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 16u32, "2026-10-23T00:46Z", "main", "2026-10-22 20:46 main: stall narrowed. A fresh process compiles closure_curry (Fsharp) in 5.5 s including startup with no stall (probe, -Stacks 10 never fired), so the 40-55 s rows occur only in the warm --batch process serving build after build. Both workers restarted their batch processes mid-run (worker1-1 -> 1-2 -> 1-3), and in the later batches the mean row time grows across the batch: worker1-3 by quarter 6.3 / 3.9 / 13.1 / 27.0 s, worker0-3 6.3 / 4.8 / 2.4 / 15.2 s. That fits per-process accumulation across builds (an Engine or Scheduler that isn't fully released between builds, body threads not exiting, or growing per-build tables; the fix-57 cross-build leak class that Scheduler.create per build was meant to rule out), not per-build cost. Next: a probe with -Repeat 20 in one warm process (or a 20-job batch of closure_curry) that records per-build ms and thread count, then dotnet-counters or -Stacks on a slow build.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_17 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 17u32, "2026-10-23T00:49Z", "main", "2026-10-22 20:49 main: reproduction staged as %TEMP%\\hopacsota-promotion\\warm-stall.ps1 (-Builds 20 -Sample closure_curry -Backend Fsharp). It runs N identical builds in ONE warm --batch process of the hopacsota DLL, prints every 2 s the rows done, thread count and private MB, takes up to 3 dotnet-stack samples whenever a build passes 10 s, and lists per-build ms. Rule, fixed now: per-build ms rising across the batch, or threads rising by about the worker count per build, confirms accumulation across builds; the stack samples name the waiting frame. Run it when the heavy queue is quiet; it is a single process, mostly idle while stalled.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_18 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 18u32, "2026-10-23T02:33Z", "main", "2026-10-22 22:33 main: one stall hypothesis eliminated by reading. PartEvalSeams Session.Root (spiral_compiler.fs 54035-54053) creates PartEvalEngine.Engine only under the Parallel policy, and closes it in a finally. The matrix ran hopacsota with the default policy, inline, so no Engine, body threads or Hopac scheduler existed; leaked engine threads can't explain the warm-batch slowdown. Neither hopacsota section holds module-level tables (scanned 53500-53897 and 53898-54133). What remains is the inline seam path itself (deferred worklist, per-key print/global lists, Finish's walk plus adapter.rebuild_index) or state it shares with single-flight that only grows on hopacsota's path. warm-stall.ps1 answers it: run it after eoie's F6 releases the box (~23:15), with SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY unset (inline), like the matrix.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_19 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 19u32, "2026-10-23T02:54Z", "main", "2026-10-22 22:54 main: correction to notes 15-18, by code reading. Under the default policy (SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY unset = inline), PartEvalSeams.policy is None, so start returns a null Session and every defer is just 'evaluate s' (spiral_compiler.fs 53917-53960, 54054-54059): inline hopacsota runs single-flight's evaluation plus null checks. Both binaries are ReadyToRun (RTR header at 4720 in the hopacsota, next3b and installed cores), and test.ps1 treats hopacsota mode like single-flight except for missing/REGRESSED scoring. So the 8x median and 109x tail can't come from hopacsota code. The likely cause is the environment while it ran (19:47-20:42: eoie F2-F4 builds and cargo checks, my Cells gates, tibia M1, free memory ~3.6 GB, Memory Compression at 886 MB). Paging in a long-lived batch process fits per-row times growing across each batch, and the trivial samples' 40-55 s. Next: warm-stall.ps1 on a quiet box. If inline hopacsota then matches single-flight, the promotion run is valid only on a quiet box (it's queued last tonight, behind next4 and the Cells pack, for that reason).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_20 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 20u32, "2026-10-23T03:05Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:05 main: verdict, measured. warm-stall.ps1 -RowsFrom (the 20 slowest ok rows of hopacsota-20261022-194751, excluding mega/typecheck_long): in one warm --batch process of the hopacsota DLL, on the same loaded box (F6 tests + my formatter gate running), they compile in 17-140 ms after a 1,065 ms first build. Threads stay flat at 33-34 and private memory at 50-70 MB. In the matrix they took 40-55 s each (array2_heap_union... Lean 54,620 ms here 1,065 ms first build; closure_apply TypeScript 49,621 vs 35 ms; closure_curry Fsharp 45,130 vs 33 ms; c_deep_list_decref TypeScript 42,111 vs 31 ms). No stall and no accumulation: hopacsota inline is not slow, and the 19:47-20:42 matrix was starved by the machine (memory pressure plus eoie's F2-F4 builds). Four rows errored here in ~0.8 s; the probe guesses output extensions per backend and doesn't use the matrix's job encoding, so these are probe artifacts, not compiler errors; the promotion run is the authority. Consequence: the promotion run (queued last tonight, 8 h cap) should show single-flight speed on a quiet box, and gpt5parallel's deletion then hangs only on its compare rule and apps/spiral compiling.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_21 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 21u32, "2026-10-23T03:57Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:57 main: limit caveat from eoie. eoie's heavy wrapper drops the job timeout, so every --heavy job runs under Invoke-Heavy's 240-min default (fix in eoie release H, EOIE-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT). The promotion run's 8 h job limit is therefore really 4 h until release H installs. On a quiet box that's enough: inline hopacsota compiles at single-flight speed (warm probe), and single-flight's -Suite all -Native took about 20 min compile plus 7 min native tonight. If it ever runs under load again and nears 4 h, treat the cut as the wrapper's, not hopacsota's.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_22 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 22u32, "2026-10-23T13:56Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:56 main: the gpt5parallel deletion is staged as %TEMP%\\gpt5parallel-delete\\apply.ps1 (dry run by default; -CoreSource for a pack core). (1) Core: Get-SpiralCoreProjection -Mode single-flight -KeepDefines hopacsota keeps every #else side and the hopacsota blocks, so on the pack-next8 core it goes 189,581 -> 34,933 lines (13.95 MB -> 2.03 MB). It parses under SINGLE_FLIGHT and HOPACSOTA, and PROOF: the single-flight projection and the hopacsota projection of the core are byte-equal before and after, so neither remaining core changes by a byte, and no matrix row can change. (2) Host Program.fs: the same projection, 1,294 -> 1,269 lines. (3) 22 anchored edits, each matching once: both fsproj files and the props drop the gpt5parallel property group and wording; build.ps1, test.ps1, fast-tier, probe (default mode becomes single-flight), test-rust-exports, projection-hash and hopacsota-g3 lose the mode; env.ps1 drops gp; test.ps1's warm recycling becomes opt-in and its CPU default becomes half the CPUs; build.ps1 loses its pair-drift step, and pair-drift.ps1 is removed because no section pairs remain. Not in the script, a separate edit after it lands: lanes/gpt5parallel and the AGENTS.md prose (51 mentions). Apply rule, unchanged from note 10: the promotion run (main-hopacsota-promotion, queued) shows 0 missing, DISAGREE 0, every row ok, and compile status and residual hash equal to single-flight; then apps/spiral compiles in hopacsota mode; then apply.ps1 -Apply, build both modes, and run one -Suite fast to show nothing moved.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_23 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 23u32, "2026-10-23T14:07Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:07 main: the deletion script now carries the docs too. docs\\agents_md.py rewrites tmp/AGENTS.md from 319 to 225 lines. 'Two lanes' becomes 'One core, two modes' (single-flight and hopacsota, with a one-line note of the deletion and its byte-identical projection). The loop's command block uses single-flight and hopacsota. 'Advancing the gpt5parallel lane' is removed. The stale instructions go: the 3/8-CPU default, the gpt5parallel-only environment knobs (kept: the stall watchdog, which the projected core still reads; the other 12 names have 0 uses there), the warm-recycle default, the write guard, the stall-report sentence and the both-cores verify rule. Promotion becomes a short history. 18 mentions remain, all run history in the backend proof paragraphs. apps/compiler/AGENTS.md is replaced by a 15-line version. With -Apply it also removes lanes/gpt5parallel (README.md and FRONTIER.md; no script reads them) and pair-drift.ps1. Every anchor is checked in the dry run, on the pack-next8 core.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_24 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 24u32, "2026-10-24T04:46Z", "main", "2026-10-24 00:46 main: main-hopacsota-promotion (heavy slot 2 since 22:34) is slow but alive. hopacsota built in 115 s; -Suite all -Native started 22:40; compile workers finished by 23:05; the native phase writes nothing until it ends and walks backends in turn: Kotlin server (java, KotlinServer.java) until about 00:30, swiftc from 00:31 (6 compilers live at 00:45). The 10-22 promotion run (hopacsota-20261022-194751) also never wrote results.tsv. Risk: Invoke-Heavy's 240-minute hold ends about 02:34; if the native phase hasn't finished by then, the run is cut with no verdict. Next attempt, if cut: give the promotion a longer heavy limit (EOIE-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT: heavy_wrapper drops the job's own timeout) or run its native phase per backend.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_25 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 25u32, "2026-10-24T05:35Z", "main", "2026-10-24 01:35 main: the promotion's own compare would not fire. run.ps1 compares only when the tree core equals pack-after-night's 25D2BE7C94943D8E, and the tree core is now 095EFD9A4CDB1C0C, so it would end with 'compare by hand'. Queued main-hopacsota-compare (job-1792820087032, --after the promotion, heavy): if run.txt is fresh and the hopacsota run has results.tsv, it runs the installed single-flight -Suite all -Native on the same tree core as the reference, then compare-runs (left single-flight, right hopacsota) against note 10's rule; if the promotion was cut by its 240-minute hold, it exits 3 with 'nothing to compare'. Also noted: the promotion runs -Parallel 2, which explains its slow native phase; the next attempt should use the single-flight review's parallelism.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_26 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 26u32, "2026-10-24T06:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:37 main: main-hopacsota-promotion ended exit 1 at about 02:35, cut by Invoke-Heavy's 240-minute hold, with no results.tsv. The compile workers finished by 23:05, and the native phase, which writes nothing until it ends and walks samples in name order, had reached ebpf_* after 3.4 h. Cause found by comparing with the single-flight review: both use -Parallel 2, but the review passes -ReuseNative (native results reused for rows whose output bytes are unchanged) and the promotion did not, so it rebuilt every native row. Since note 10's rule expects hopacsota's outputs to equal single-flight's, -ReuseNative should turn most native rows into cache hits. run.ps1 now passes -ReuseNative; the promotion is requeued after tibia's PR-27 block, with main-hopacsota-compare --after it. The worker files (compile status per cell, no residual hash, about 5,060 of the cells) are too partial for the rule on their own.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_27 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 27u32, "2026-10-24T07:05Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:05 main: rerun sequence fixed so the native phase can fit a heavy hold. compare-after.ps1 now has two steps: -Step Reference runs the installed single-flight -Suite all -Native -Parallel 2 -ReuseNative on the tree core FIRST, which fills the native cache with the tree core's outputs; then the promotion (run.ps1, now with -ReuseNative) reuses those native results for every row whose hopacsota output is byte-equal, which is note 10's own expectation; then -Step Compare runs compare-runs (left reference, right hopacsota) against the rule. All three are queued as a chain at tibia's block end.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_28 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 28u32, "2026-10-24T17:06Z", "main", "main 13:1x: note 10's first gate is MET. compare-after (10:28): left single-flight reference single-flight-20261024-093709, right hopacsota-20261024-101144, both on tree core BEECFE432427C57A: 7,352 rows each, missing on right 0, differing cells 0, behavior cells 0, DISAGREE 0. Next gates queued as job main-serial-hopacsota-apps (%TEMP%\\hopacsota-promotion\\apps-gate.ps1): apps/spiral compiled to F# by single-flight and by hopacsota under inline, dfs, bfs, random:11 and random:23 must be byte-identical (Gate 2 determinism on apps/spiral). After that, by note 10, the user decides the gpt5parallel deletion (staged %TEMP%\\gpt5parallel-delete\\apply.ps1).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_29 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 29u32, "2026-10-24T18:27Z", "main", "main 14:3x: the apps/spiral gate's first run (13:59) stopped at its reference: single-flight itself could not compile apps/spiral (exit 5 in 10 s, ETY-UnboundVariable rust_type at spiral.spi:1136 and its 4 siblings). Not today's lands: HEAD's apps/spiral against HEAD's lib fails the same way on the installed compiler. apps/spiral says open rust and then bare rust_type, but open rust opens the rust/ folder record, so rust/rust.spi's members need the rust. prefix (lib already writes rust.rust_type). Fixed in apps/spiral/spiral.spi (5 sites -> rust.rust_type, uncommitted, for the user); apps/spiral then compiles to F# in 67 s (941,587 bytes). Gate requeued as main-serial-hopacsota-apps-2.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_30 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 30u32, "2026-10-24T20:07Z", "main", "main 15:5x: apps/spiral determinism gate PASS (job main-hopacsota-apps-gate, 14:27-15:51, after the apps/spiral rust.rust_type fix): single-flight and hopacsota inline, dfs, bfs, random:11 and random:23 compile apps/spiral to F# byte-identical, all DA79215DB24E7AFF (941,591 B, about 80 s each). With the 7,352-row matrix (0 differing cells, DISAGREE 0) both promotion gates are green; the gpt5parallel deletion (staged %TEMP%\\gpt5parallel-delete\\apply.ps1) is the user's call on the tree.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_31 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 31u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:45 groom: both promotion gates are green (7,352 rows match single-flight, DISAGREE 0; apps/spiral byte-identical under 5 schedules), yet the deps list HOPACSOTA-P3, which is Planned 0%. Either P3 isn't a real gate for promotion or P6 is complete only modulo P3; decide by the AGENTS.md promotion criterion when the pack 5i hopacsota run is read.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_32 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 32u32, "2026-10-27T23:04Z", "main", "10-27 19:04 main: Groom 10-27 18:5x, correcting n(10-24 20:07) 'both promotion gates green': the apps/spiral determinism gate ran only the SINGLE-THREADED schedules (inline, dfs, bfs, random:11, random:23), all byte-identical DA79215DB24E7AFF. The policy that actually evaluates in parallel, SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel with 2 or more workers, wasn't in the gate, and the 10-25 waits census shows it is nondeterministic: a different output hash every run at w4 and w8, 3 distinct outputs in 5 runs at w2 (HOPACSOTA-P2 note of this groom). Since parallel evaluation is the point of hopacsota (non-negotiable per the user), promotion can't rest on single-threaded schedules. The promotion gate is therefore: the 7,352-row matrix plus the apps/spiral byte-identity under policy=parallel at w2/w4/w8 as well, 5 runs each, all equal to single-flight. Dependency fixed by this groom: P3 (measured granularity cutoff, a speed item) is not a promotion gate, and P6 now depends on HOPACSOTA-P2 (parallel determinism). The gpt5parallel deletion (staged TEMP gpt5parallel-delete/apply.ps1) stays the user's call, and only after this gate. Progress held at 50%.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_33 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 33u32, "2026-10-29T18:46Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:5x compiler: evidence toward n(10-27)'s promotion gate (apps/spiral to F# byte-identical to single-flight under policy=parallel at w2/w4/w8, 5 runs each), all on the P3+D4 candidate p3d4 (6b + shard + IVar + D4; hopacsota build), node, measure-arms.ps1, which hashes every output. So far: w8, 15 runs (P3 speed job-1793287335290, control workers 1/4/8 x5) plus 2 (p3d4 arms), ALL B47B5749C215A6F8 = sf. Under FORCED conflict (D4 restart path, job-1793294062916, running): w1/w2/w4/w8, 17 of 25 runs done, all B47B5749 = sf, exit 0. Queued: job-1793295047297 (sf, w1, w2, w4, w8, w8+GCgen0size, w8+gcConcurrent=0; 5 rounds each), which completes the w2/w4/w8 x5 non-forced cells. Caveat for promotion: this is the p3d4 CANDIDATE, not an installed core. Once P3/D4 ship, the gate re-runs on the installed hopacsota build; the matrix half (7,352 rows) needs a hopacsota -Suite all on that core too.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_34 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 34u32, "2026-10-29T22:05Z", "compiler", "10-29 18:0x compiler: GATE CELLS COMPLETE on the P3+D4 candidate (p3d4): apps/spiral compiled to F# under SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel gave B47B5749C215A6F8, byte-identical to single-flight, in EVERY run: w2 x5, w4 x5, w8 x5 (curve job-1793295047297), plus w8 x10 more (gen0 and gcConcurrent arms), w1 x5, w8 x15 (P3 speed job), and 20/20 under forced conflict (w1/w2/w4/w8 x5, D4 job-1793294062916). That is n(10-27)'s gate shape (w2/w4/w8, 5 runs each, all equal to sf) on a candidate. For PROMOTION it must re-run on the INSTALLED hopacsota core once P3/D4 ship, together with the 7,352-row matrix half on that core; then the gpt5parallel deletion is the user's call.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_35 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 35u32, "2026-10-31T01:30Z", "compiler", "10-30 21:30 compiler: a CHECK for the promotion gate, found while reading the engine for P3. Engine.Drain (spiral_compiler.fs ~53974) re-raises the first crash in CONTEXT order (contexts |> Seq.collect Crashes |> Seq.tryHead), not in single-flight order. If two or more bodies fail in one parallel build, hopacsota's reported first error can differ from single-flight's. Only HopacsotaConflict goes through the D4 canonical restart (with_conflict_restart ~54174). Unverified whether any matrix negative has 2+ independent failing bodies; the P6 matrix half on the installed core should compare hopacsota-parallel error detail with single-flight on every compile=error row. If any differ, make Drain raise a conflict whenever crashes come from more than one body, so the D4 rerun reports single-flight's error. Cheap to fix, costs only failing builds.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_36 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 36u32, "2026-10-31T08:32Z", "compiler", "10-31 04:32 compiler: CORRECTION to n13 (my 10-30 21:30 'check'): no multi-body error-order hazard exists. Session.Defer's body wraps the evaluation in 'try evaluate body_env with error -> fail child error' (spiral_compiler.fs ~54060-54062). Every evaluation error therefore becomes a failure MARKER at the body's position in its parent's globals list, and Finish raises the first failure in single-flight walk order. Engine.Drain's ctx.Crashes only holds exceptions that escape that handler (internal faults around it, not type errors), so 'first crash in context order' never decides which type error a user sees. samples/frontier_hopacsota_two_errors (ExpectedErrors 'first body fails') covers this shape. Nothing to fix; the matrix half of the gate needs no extra error-detail comparison for this.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_37 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 37u32, "2026-10-31T11:35Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 07:35 compiler: DECISION recorded (groom): this story's last step ('the user decides deleting gpt5parallel's lines') was taken 10-31 02:0x: delete it and keep iterating on hopacsota toward SOTA. The deletion lives in GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE / PACK-10. What remains here is the promotion criterion itself, re-read against single-flight as the only reference: Gate 2 determinism already held on the retired build (GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE n: 8 workers, 8740373223C658E6 x2).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_38 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 38u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 23:34 compiler, cadence groom (user 23:33: drift/stall check; no Active item silent >12 h): Active -> PLANNED: the user's deletion decision is done (gpt5parallel retired, PACK-10/11). What remains is the promotion run itself: hopacsota -Suite all (no missing, DISAGREE 0), apps/spiral compiles, Gate 2 determinism, all on the installed pack-11 core: a heavy box/node job, queued when HOPAC-DEPTH's decision lands (promotion without a speed case isn't the goal).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_39 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 39u32, "2026-11-06T11:03Z", "compiler", "11-06 07:0x compiler: PULLED (main 07:0x: top of next, idle 5.3 d). The remaining DoD is the promotion run on ONE installed core, and gpt5parallel is already retired (PACK-10/11). Plan, node first: (1) build single-flight AND hopacsota from pack52's exact source (spiral-bin-candidates/pack52/source, core DLL A688EE5A for single-flight) in a private SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR, inside an isolated -Mirror of the box tree. (2) Matrix half: test.ps1 -Suite all -Native -ReuseNative for single-flight, then hopacsota (default inline policy, as on 10-24), then compare-runs. Rule: missing on right 0, differing cells 0, DISAGREE 0. (3) Determinism half (n 10-27): measure-arms apps/spiral to F#, sf vs hopacsota policy=parallel at w2/w4/w8, 5 rounds each, every output hash equal to sf. Estimate 60 -> 120 min of agent time; the node run is about 2-3 h wall.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_40 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 40u32, "2026-11-06T11:10Z", "compiler", "11-06 07:1x compiler: LAUNCHED on the node: job-1793963411353-compiler-p6-promotion (5 h cap; script C:\\home\\remote\\compiler-p6-src\\p6-run.ps1; mirror C:\\home\\remote\\compiler-p6\\spiral at c0e77c3f; private cache C:\\home\\remote\\compiler-p6-cache). Core source A5FF832751C3 = pack52 source = spiral HEAD c0e77c3f (the user committed it), so the run proves the shipped core. It runs beside tibia-cifix-build; only output hashes are judged, not timings.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_41 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 41u32, "2026-11-06T12:24Z", "compiler", "11-06 08:2x compiler: MATRIX HALF GREEN, node (job-1793963411353-compiler-p6-promotion, finished exit 0 in 4352 s). Both modes were built from pack52's exact source (core source A5FF832751C3; single-flight core A688EE5AD426A5A0 = the installed pack52 single-flight; hopacsota core 6B63AEBC2713ADE1). test.ps1 -Suite all -Native -ReuseNative: single-flight-20261106-071504 and hopacsota-20261106-080939, each 7558 rows (contracts 728, examples 6753, frontier 72, mega 5), native 6240 ran / 464 build-fail / oracle agree 5480, DISAGREE 0. compare-runs: missing on right 0, differing cells 0, behavior cells 0. That meets note 10's rule (0 missing, DISAGREE 0, compile status and residual hash equal on every row), and every row returned: emitted 0 and timeout 0 in both. DETERMINISM HALF INVALID, my script bug: measure-arms got -Arms @(...) through pwsh -File, which bound the array positionally (fiscal star 06:12 #5), so only the sf arm parsed, against a bogus entry, and exited 5. Requeued with a ;-joined -Arms string and an explicit -Entry apps/spiral/spiral.spi: job-1793967848218-compiler-p6-arms (--after the giant-state trace). DoD left: apps/spiral to F# under policy=parallel at w2/w4/w8, 5 rounds each, every output equal to sf, and apps/spiral compiles.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_42 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 42u32, "2026-11-06T12:45Z", "compiler", "11-06 08:4x compiler: the second arms run (job-1793967848218) got every arm, sf included, to exit 5 with no output: apps/spiral no longer builds to F#. pack53c, with the corrected missing-arm site, names the real gap: spiral.spi:1108 (am'.from_vec in apps/spiral's mlua Lua runner, Rust-only code with no Fsharp arm). pack52 had named process.spi:572, a validated arm, which is COMPILER-MISSING-ARM-REAL-SITE's bug. apps/spiral is a Rust program now (its CLI ships spiral.rs), so the 10-24/10-29 F# gate shape is obsolete. The gate now targets --backend Rust. Box check, installed pack52: apps/spiral -> Rust exit 0 in 91 s, 1,443,461 B, sha C85906C53BD7A545. Stopped the F# arms job; requeued as job-1793969018428-compiler-p6-arms-rust (sf vs hopacsota policy=parallel w2/w4/w8, 5 rounds, -Backend Rust; --after the giant-state trace2). DoD: every arm output = the sf hash (and = the box hash C85906C5 for cross-host identity).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_p6_43 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-P6", 43u32, "2026-11-06T13:53Z", "compiler", "11-06 09:5x compiler: DONE, the promotion criterion is MET on one shipped core (pack52 source A5FF832751C3; single-flight core A688EE5AD426A5A0 = installed pack52; hopacsota core 6B63AEBC2713ADE1). Proof, node: (1) the matrix (n 08:2x): 7558 rows per mode, missing 0, differing cells 0, behavior 0, DISAGREE 0, emitted 0, timeout 0 (every row returns). (2) apps/spiral compiles (Rust; the CLI's real target, see n 08:4x for why F# is obsolete) and Gate 2 determinism under SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel: job-1793969018428-compiler-p6-arms-rust, 5 rounds each of sf, w2, w4, w8 = 20 of 20 runs exit 0 with output C85906C53BD7A545 = sf, and = the box's own pack52 compile of the same tree (cross-host identity). SPEED CAVEAT (this item's criterion does not require speed, but the 10-31 note says promotion without a speed case is not the goal): on a node at 100% load, hopacsota medians were slower than sf: w2 0.98x, w4 1.30x, w8 1.40x wall (CPU 105/123/137 s vs sf 115 s). RECOMMENDATION: keep single-flight as the shipping default. Switching is a separate decision once HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH (lever 2) shows a quiet-node speed win; promotion here means hopacsota is now proven a correct, deterministic drop-in.")
