inl note_of_pipelines_green_4 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 4u32, "2026-10-18T11:46Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 07:46 main-thread triage of the red runs: spiral compiler job (run 39121783429 on adffe65a, which already has the macro lookahead fix) reached the native tier on Linux, then test.ps1 called kernel32 SetErrorMode (Value cannot be null, path1); fixed in the tree: the WerFault guard runs only when IsWindows (needs the user's push; the other native-tier paths already branch on IsWindows). dice windows job 39106582938: workflow step without a conclusion and no log blob (lost runner; ubuntu passed); rerun --failed at 07:46. x: e2e tutorial steps xc --help and --per-account expect the old usage without the xc thread line; the X lane's uncommitted README already adds it, so it clears with that commit. eoie: own source-topology gate (rust_file_max=982 over its limit), eoie lane. i574n.github: same run ids as x (shared workflow), recheck after x.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_5 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 5u32, "2026-10-18T11:47Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 07:47 main-thread correction to the previous note: i574n.github's repo is i574n/.github; its red run 39103223121 (06:12Z) failed apps/spiral compile on spiral main 1723e220 (the first macro fix), which adffe65a fixed; rerun --failed at 07:47. The x and i574n.github ids in the earlier note were a listing bug (one repo's JSON reused for the next).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_6 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 6u32, "2026-10-18T13:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:22 main-thread: dice run 39106582938 rerun --failed is success (the windows failure was a lost runner); spiral push 39127751148 (e1eef3bc) ubuntu success, windows running; i574n/.github 39103223121 rerun --failed again at 09:21 now that spiral main has the Gleam warm-slot fix (its 07:47 rerun cloned the old main and failed on the same stale-Gleam symptom). Still red: x (the X lane's README tutorial update is uncommitted) and eoie (its topology gate, eoie lane).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_7 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 7u32, "2026-10-18T13:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:32 main-thread: push run 39129835185 on 80b16019 (user commit 09:02) failed: build ubuntu, apps/spiral compile, lib/spiral/rust/near.spi:44:19 Expected: } (git-0e's 08:54 block-form conversion put ') : () -> ()' after an indented block closer inside a record, which the installed parser also rejects). Isolated (a lib copy with only near.spi reverted compiles) and reverted by git-0e at 09:31 to its e1eef3bc content; a lib-backed probe compiles again. Needs the user's next commit; the other 11 converted lib files compile. My bless now aborts first if a lib-backed sample does not compile.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_8 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 8u32, "2026-10-18T13:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:42 main-thread: spiral push 39127751148 on e1eef3bc is fully green (build ubuntu + windows, push to gh-pages): the first green spiral push of the day, carrying the macro lookahead fix, the cells warm-slot fix and today's backend work. i574n/.github rerun 39103223121 failed again, as expected: it cloned spiral main at 80b16019 (broken near.spi); goes green after the user commits git-0e's near.spi revert, then one more rerun.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_9 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 9u32, "2026-10-19T12:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:16 lap 1 grain: diagnosed the 06:48 push runs: spiral, dice, polyglot fail on Linux only, rustix 0.37.27/0.37.28 (near-workspaces 0.11 -> cargo-near -> near-cli-rs -> keyring -> zbus 3 -> async-io 1) uses rustc_layout_scalar_valid_range attributes that nightly-2026-10-18 rejects (the Windows nightly verification never compiles rustix); alphabet fails in dir-tree-html exit 3 (UTF-8 slice of its Korean README); x fails in apps/client/protocol.spi:80 and eoie in eoie-do-edit (their lanes); dice/polyglot/alphabet also lose html at mix spiral.html before mix deps.get Next: check the next push runs of spiral, dice, polyglot, alphabet after the user commits the fixes")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_10 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 10u32, "2026-10-19T13:53Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:53 lap 2 grain: push checklist (uncommitted fixes the user's next push must carry, git status at 09:5x): spiral 30 modified + 18 new (gh-pages.yml nightly pin, scripts/core.ps1 mix deps.get, workspace/.cargo/config.toml rustix cfg, scripts/publish-tree.ps1 failed-render guard from helper 2, test.ps1 per-backend native keys, sm'.spi fixes, Cells edits); dice 6 + 3 (gh-pages.yml, .cargo/ rustix cfg); polyglot 3 + 3 (gh-pages.yml, apps/chat/.cargo/); alphabet 3 + 2 (gh-pages.yml); dir-tree-html 3 (gh-pages.yml, dir_tree_html.spi UTF-8 runs + README test); blog 6 + 4; x 24 + 2 (ci.yml, protocol.spi fix); eoie 32 + 8 (gh-pages.yml nightly, missing generated crates per the eoie lane); i574n.github 3 Next: after the user pushes, read each repo's run; the first new failure per repo becomes this item's next grain")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_11 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 11u32, "2026-10-19T18:13Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:13 lap 4 grain: push checklist refreshed at 14:1x: uncommitted spiral 229 (22 new; includes the core v1 consumer migration, pack 7 compiler, 147 regenerated sample outputs from the accepted reviews), dice 12, alphabet 5, polyglot 6, dir-tree-html 3, i574n.github 4, plus the fork clone deps/The-Spiral-Language (core v1); the 10-17/10-18 gh-pages runs were green in dice, alphabet, polyglot and spiral, so the 06:48 reds are only the causes already fixed in these trees Next: after the user pushes: watch spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot, then i574n.github (push-only workflow, last) with ci-runs.ps1")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_12 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 12u32, "2026-10-19T20:14Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 16:14 lap 5 grain: compiler install race fixed in build.ps1: other lanes compile continuously with the shared Release DLL (a Rust compile from another session started mid-build at 16:08), so the Release copy step failed twice with MSB3021/MSB3027 (FParsecCS.dll and others locked by .NET Host); Unlock-SpiralOutputs already ran before the 3-minute compile, which left the window open; now a build that fails only on locked-file copies (no error FS) unlocks again and reruns the incremental build up to 3 times, so the copy follows the unlock within seconds; the pack 9 install then succeeded and hashes equal the candidate Next: none; watch the next installs for the retry line")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_13 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 13u32, "2026-10-19T20:14Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 16:14 correction to note 12: the pack 9 install at 16:10 built in 67 s without contention, so the new unlock-and-recopy retry did not fire; it is parsed and in place but not yet exercised by a real lock race.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_14 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 14u32, "2026-10-20T00:45Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 20:45 lap 6 grain: second face of the shared-DLL lock race: the pack 12 install (20:33) built fine, but Invoke-SpiralReadyToRun could not swap 5 DLLs (FSharp.Core, FSharpx.Collections, Hopac.Core, Hopac.Platform, the host SpiralCompiler.dll: Move access denied while other lanes ran compilers), so they stay IL; same code, slower startup, and the host no longer hash-equals the R2R candidate (the core DLL does); the unlock-and-recopy retry covers only the msbuild copy Next: make the ReadyToRun swap use the same move-aside trick (rename the locked IL dll to .r2r-old-<stamp>, then move the R2R one in), so a busy machine still gets precompiled outputs")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_15 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 15u32, "2026-10-20T00:46Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 20:46 main: R2R swap fixed in build.ps1 (Invoke-SpiralReadyToRun): when Move(r2r, dll, overwrite) is refused because a running compiler holds the dll, it renames the IL dll to .r2r-old-<stamp> (Unlock-SpiralOutputs already deletes those later) and moves the R2R image in, moving the IL file back if that fails; proven with a simulated open-with-delete-share handle (overwrite refused, aside + move-in works); parse clean; first real exercise is the next Release build.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_16 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 16u32, "2026-10-21T22:17Z", "main", "18:17 CI survey (gh run list on each repo's i574n remote; the first remote of spiral, polyglot, dice and alphabet is the fc1943s backup, whose runs are from 2025): green: polyglot, dice, tibia; red: spiral gh-pages 10-21 07:49, alphabet gh-pages 10-20 00:04, x ci 10-20 00:04 (x lane), eoie gh-pages 10-19 22:10 (eoie lane), i574n/.github gh-pages 10-19 22:09. spiral's only failure was DISAGREE 1: samples/zig_exit_wide F# 'exit 305 (warm)' vs C 49 and every other backend 49. Linux truncates exit codes to 8 bits, but the warm F# tier reads the code from fsi output, and its 8-bit mask in test.ps1 (line 787) never ran: warm.ps1 returns Exit as [string], so '-isnot [int]' was always true. Fixed: the mask now applies to any numeric string off Windows (305 -> 49, -1 -> 255, 'timeout' and '' unchanged); unit-checked, parse clean. Windows was never affected (32-bit exit codes).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_17 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 17u32, "2026-10-21T22:20Z", "main", "18:20 alphabet gh-pages (run 39367599510, HEAD 01da480): apps/hangul/hangul.livemd's two Cells tests (hanja, hanjaconvert) fail on the Zig target only: hanja.spi:2:5 ETY-TermError, Unification failure, Got array (i32 * i32), Expected array_base (i32 * i32), in hanja_to_hangul_table. hanja.spi declares '() : array_base (i32 * i32) = ;[ ... ]'. The parser turns ';[' into an application of the term named array to the RawArray (compiler line 45226), and core base.spi:24 has 'nominal array t = array_base t', so the literal is the nominal array; other targets pass, so a backend-dependent path resolves array differently (not found by reading: no 'inl array' in core or lib). Next: one probe compile of the hanja cell for Zig and Rust when a compile window is free. Also removed 2 stray files of mine from alphabet/apps/hangul (hanja.spi.err/.out from the 08:21 format-timings job).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_18 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 18u32, "2026-10-21T22:22Z", "main", "18:22 i574n/.github gh-pages (run 39357416239): lib/spiral/parsing.spi test pipe2 failed on Zig only, at 1343:86 (got array, expected string) and 1343:92 (got array_base, expected unit). Cause: commit 002c71f6 (10-19 22:09) rewrote the test lambda 'fun a, b => a () ++# b ()' to 'fun a, b => array () ++# b ()', apparently a rename sweep that caught the parameter a; array () then means core's nominal constructor applied to unit. The other targets passed only through the CI memo (memo_hits 37), and Zig was the one target compiled fresh. Fixed back to a () ++# b () (test block only, so builds and other lanes are unaffected). That commit's lib diff contains no other such rewrite. Gate: Cells --gate on parsing.spi when a slot is free, after the night-pack review. Open question for CELLS: memo replays can keep a broken test green on old targets; the alphabet hanja case below may be the same.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_19 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 19u32, "2026-10-21T22:24Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:24 x: x ci status for this survey. Red on HEAD 61b8fff only from the windows-2025 CLIXML e2e failure (run 39367603402); ubuntu passed in 9.5 min against a 120-min timeout. The uncommitted tree fixes it, and it is green locally on CI's own scripts/build.ps1 (10-20 precommit; 10-21 x-split-fix3 for the post_id slice and the sim fixes). Precondition for x going green: x CI clones i574n/spiral's default branch, and pushed 33ee6412 lacks the postability profile reader, the 'x-compat report' line x's client build parses, and today's lib Gleam fixes, so spiral must be pushed with x (the user has always pushed both together; X-DONE note 8).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_20 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 20u32, "2026-10-21T22:24Z", "main", "18:24 correction to note 18, from x's memo analysis: Cells' memo key (memo.ex key/2) hashes the test program itself plus the package blocks it reaches, and pipe2's own text changed, so on the non-Zig targets pipe2 most likely ran fresh and passed. That makes the 'masked by the memo' explanation unlikely: array () genuinely type-checks on the other targets and fails only on Zig, the same Zig-only pattern as alphabet's hanja array_base literal. Both point at one question: what the term array resolves to when Cells compiles for Zig versus the other targets (a different package or prelude set for the Zig builder?). The pipe2 fix back to a () stands either way (the lambda's a was otherwise unused). Probe planned: compile the hanja and pipe2 cells for Zig and Rust with SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 once a compile window is free.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_21 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 21u32, "2026-10-21T23:24Z", "main", "19:24 alphabet cause found by probe (installed compiler, a tiny package with core- and spiral-, 4 backends each): a let annotated : array_base (i32 * i32) whose body is a ;[ ... ] literal fails on EVERY backend (Rust, Zig, C, F#: ETY-TermError at 2:5), because ;[ is core's nominal array; annotated : array it compiles everywhere. So hanja.spi's annotation was always wrong, and the 'Zig only' CI failure means the other targets never compiled it fresh in that run (a memo replay or another path; not the compiler). The hangul test cell expects array_base (it wraps with fun x => array x), so the fix keeps the annotation and unwraps in place: hanja.spi now ends with '    |> fun (array x) => x' (1 line; probe: compiles on all 4 backends). Verifying on the real 465 KB table now (job main-hanja-probe). The same probe also re-reads parsing.spi's pipe2: array () is core's nominal constructor there too, so the a () fix was right on every backend.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_22 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 22u32, "2026-10-21T23:25Z", "main", "19:25 alphabet fix verified on the real 465 KB table (job main-hanja-probe, 57 s): hanja.spi with the in-place unwrap plus a main that wraps it like the hangul test cell (fun x => array x : array _ |> am'.length) compiles on Rust, Zig, C and F#. alphabet apps/hangul/hanja.spi is changed for the user to commit (+1 line); its gh-pages turns green on the next push if nothing else breaks.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_23 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 23u32, "2026-10-22T04:13Z", "main", "00:13 lab drain: fsharp-warm-exit.py landed in refined form: apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1 computes processExit (the Linux 8-bit mask of the warm F# exit; tonight's version also guards non-numeric exits), so the script's anchor is gone. Removed spiral/lab/PIPELINES-GREEN (1 file); the item stays open for its remaining work.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_24 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 24u32, "2026-10-22T07:28Z", "main", "03:28 main: why the spiral and alphabet pages are stale since 10-18 (read-only: gh run view --log-failed; ties to TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO note 25). spiral: the last push run (39367586923, 10-20 04:04, HEAD 33ee6412) fails in build (ubuntu) at lib/spiral/build.ps1. 346 of 347 lib tests pass; parsing.spi test pipe2 on zig hits ETY-TermError at 1343:86, 'Got array _ Expected string'. HEAD's line 1343 reads 'fun a, b => array () ++# b ()', a parameter 'a' rewritten to 'array' by a rename (the only such line in HEAD's lib). The working tree already has 'a () ++# b ()' (inside an uncommitted 38+/30- parsing.spi change), so committing tree lib unblocks it. (The scheduled 10-21 run fails in the compiler suite job, a separate track.) alphabet: run 39367599510 fails in apps/hangul. hanja.spi:2:5 hanja_to_hangul_table declares array_base (i32 * i32), but the ;[ ... ] literal is 'array', the core-v1 split. The user already STAGED the fix ('|> fun (array x) => x' after the literal). dice and polyglot last deployed 10-20 (green). So both red pages turn green with the user's next commits; no code change is needed from main.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_25 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 25u32, "2026-10-22T07:30Z", "main", "03:30 main: the scheduled spiral compiler-suite job (run 39594781370, 10-21) fails on one row: samples/zig_exit_wide [Fsharp] DISAGREE on the Linux runner (exit 305 while C, Rust, Zig, Python, Gleam and OpenQasm exit 49 = 305 mod 256). Cause: the warm F# native runner returns the program's int without a real process exit, and HEAD's test.ps1 masked it to 8 bits off Windows only when '\\.Exit -isnot [int]' was false. The warm result is not a .NET int, so it was never masked. The STAGED test.ps1 already fixes it: parse the exit as a number, then '-band 255' when not on Windows. So all three red signals (spiral build, alphabet build, spiral compiler suite) have fixes waiting in the user's next commits.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_26 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 26u32, "2026-10-22T07:31Z", "main", "03:31 main: the rest of the CI picture (read only, last runs). i574n/.github gh-pages (39357416239) fails the same way as spiral: it clones spiral and builds its lib, and dies on 'notebooks failed: parsing' (the pipe2 line), so its push job finds no gh-pages artifact. It is fixed by the same spiral commit. Green: tibia, blog, dir-tree-html (and dice and polyglot, 10-20). Red, owned by other lanes: x ci (10-20 04:04) and eoie gh-pages (10-20 02:10); not diagnosed here.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_27 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 27u32, "2026-10-22T19:37Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:37 x: x ci status update (details in X-DONE notes 41-51, not repeated here). The red on HEAD 61b8fff (windows-2025 e2e '#< CLIXML') now has a fix verified locally: '-OutputFormat Text' in e2e's step launcher, e2e 31+5 green on 10-22 15:00. CI parity: zig 0.17.0 checked (prelude passed, exsss smoke 5/5 incl. rust+gleam); rust was already pinned by Cells via the rustup proxy. The full notebook rerun on CI's zig is due ~17:50-18:55. Preconditions for green are unchanged: the user commits x's whole working tree and pushes spiral with it (still 33ee6412).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_28 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 28u32, "2026-10-23T11:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:53 main: CI survey (gh run list on the i574n remotes). No push since 10-20 04:14 in any repo. Green on the last run: dice, polyglot, x (dynamic), eoie (dynamic), tibia, blog, dir-tree-html. Red: alphabet push 39367599510 (hanja fix staged by the user), i574n/.github push 39357416239 (parsing.spi pipe2, fixed in the tree), and spiral's scheduled compiler job 39773873815 (10-22 11:52, HEAD 33ee6412) with DISAGREE 1, samples/zig_exit_wide [Fsharp]: the Linux 8-bit warm F# exit mask fixed in the tree on 10-21 (note 17). Spiral's 10-23 11:39 schedule is running on the same HEAD and will show the same row. All three reds clear with the user's next commits; nothing new to fix in code.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_29 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 29u32, "2026-10-24T03:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:11 main grooming estimate, corrected 03:00: the 07:53 survey already showed nothing left to fix in code. All 3 reds (spiral's schedule on HEAD 33ee6412, alphabet, i574n/.github) are fixed in the tree and clear with the user's next commits; the '240 m, reproduce and fix each red' reading was wrong. Agent estimate 0; blocked on the user's commits and pushes.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_30 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 30u32, "2026-10-24T07:00Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:00 main: survey refreshed with gh run list: unchanged since 07:53. No push in any repo since 10-20 04:14; red: spiral schedule run 39947580678 (10-23 11:39, HEAD 33ee6412), alphabet 39367599510, i574n/.github 39357416239; the other 7 green. Status Active -> Blocked: every red is fixed in the tree and waits on the user's commits.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_31 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 31u32, "2026-10-25T08:29Z", "main", "10-25 04:29 main: spiral CI red cause found (run 40161876207 on HEAD 47d1e562, build ubuntu-24.04, workflow.ps1): RUST-FAILED apps/spiral, 'ETY-UnboundVariable: Unbound term variable: rust_type' in the lua/lua_function/lua_error/lua_value/lua_multi_value nominals (each body is rust.rust_type applied at type level). The CI compiler is built from HEAD's apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, which is 1,136+/153- lines behind the working tree (the uncommitted 5i/5j/5k pack layer, plus Program.fs 13 lines), while HEAD's lib and apps are edition 3 (lib rust.spi: ':= rust_type \\* fs rs. ...'). Locally the installed tree compiler builds apps/spiral, so the commit pairs new sources with the old compiler. Fix: the user commits apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs + tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs with the next commit (the 5k install lands in the tree first: job-1792916161438). Pages: spiral and alphabet still serve renders without the README section, so they refresh on the first green run. kit (eoie renamed) is served at i574n.github.io/kit/; pages-sweep.ps1 now lists kit instead of eoie.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_32 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 32u32, "2026-10-29T07:04Z", "main", "10-29 03:1x main: why spiral's pages have not refreshed since 10-18 (gh-pages dff26794, deploy of 10-18T19:00Z): scheduled runs skip build and push by design (gh-pages.yml:18 'if: github.event_name != schedule'), so only push runs deploy, and every push run since fails. The latest, 40677661106 (push, cec8840c, 10-28 11:08Z), fails in build (ubuntu-24.04) at apps/spiral/build.ps1:127 'RUST-FAILED apps/spiral / run check': 'mix spiral.render_cells exit 1: Unchecked dependencies for environment dev: kino (Hex package) the dependency is not available, run mix deps.get'. On a fresh runner nothing has fetched apps/cells' Hex deps by the time apps/spiral's run check calls into Cells; core.ps1:379-383 and run_notebook.ps1:114-120 guard their own Cells calls with 'if deps/kino/mix.exs is missing: local.hex, local.rebar, deps.get', and build.ps1:61-63 had no such guard. FIXED (unstaged, parse-clean): apps/spiral/build.ps1 runs the same three mix tasks inside the existing try before spiral.render_cells when deps/kino/mix.exs is missing; a failure there still surfaces through the render's own failure row. Locally deps/kino exists, so the guard is a no-op here, and the next push run is the proof. The same stale shape (200, no readme section, gh-pages from 10-18) holds for alphabet (cdd28acb), whose push failures were not read tonight.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_33 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 33u32, "2026-10-29T07:08Z", "main", "10-29 03:1x main: alphabet's pages are stale for a different reason (gh-pages cdd28acb, deploy 10-18T16:49Z). Every alphabet push run since 10-20 fails; the latest, 40483234158 (10-27 09:45Z), has build (ubuntu-24.04) success and build (windows-2025) failure, so push is skipped. The Windows log shows Playwright's browser downloads failing (playwright.azureedge.net chromium/firefox/webkit win64 zips: HTTP 400 GatewayExceptionResponse), then Invoke-Block exit 1 in polyglot/apps/parser, alphabet/scripts and alphabet/apps/documents (retry 1/3). Not read deeper tonight. Next: the first failing step's own error in apps/documents (the F# step removed tonight under FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER n9 predates nothing here; the failure is from 10-27), and whether the Windows job needs Playwright at all.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_34 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 34u32, "2026-10-29T07:09Z", "main", "10-29 03:2x main: alphabet Windows push failure, one step deeper (run 40483234158, job 122118500594): apps/documents' build runs the spiral CLI (target\\spiral\\spiral\\target\\debug\\spiral_<hash>.exe, built from spiral's apps/spiral), which panics at D:\\a\\alphabet\\spiral\\apps\\spiral\\spiral.rs:16776:42: 'called Result::unwrap() on an Err value: Os { code: 3, kind: NotFound, message: The system cannot find the path specified. }', on all 3 retries. So a filesystem call in the spiral CLI unwraps a missing Windows path on the runner. The Playwright 400s earlier in the log are a separate, non-fatal setup noise. The local spiral.rs (3f1b7588) does not line up with CI's generated file at 16776, so mapping the panic to Spiral source needs CI's spiral.rs (or a local Windows run of alphabet/apps/documents/build.ps1 on a clean checkout).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_35 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 35u32, "2026-10-29T08:13Z", "main", "10-29 04:1x main: ROOT CAUSE of the Windows push failures of alphabet (40483234158) and .github (40677888927): both die in polyglot/apps/parser's notebook run, where Cells calls the spiral CLI 'export <TEMP>/spiral_cells_fs_<n>/JsonParser.cells fs'. The CLI's own trace shows the path given as C:\\Users\\RUNNER~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp/... and then written and parsed as D:\\Users\\RUNNER~1\\..., so it panics at spiral.rs:16776 ('Os { code: 3, NotFound }'). GitHub's Windows runners keep the workspace on D: (D:\\a\\...) and TEMP on C:. lib/spiral/file_system.spi:1247-1248, get_full_path's Rust arm, rebuilds a path's drive segment from the CURRENT DIRECTORY's first letter (sm'.(++#) (current_dir |> sm'.slice 0i32 0i32) \":\") instead of the segment's own letter, so any absolute path on another drive is re-rooted onto the current drive. Locally everything is on C:, so it never shows. (The Playwright 1.44 'playwright.azureedge.net ... 400' errors are non-fatal noise: spiral/scripts/init.ps1:91 only prints a message. That CDN host is retired, so the pin wants a bump.) .github's page 404 is separate and older: its gh-pages (1afcd1a5, 10-18T23:32Z) has 16 root entries and no index.html, and Pages is enabled and built from gh-pages, so it is a deploy without a render; the next green push run fixes it. FIX drafted and being proven on lib copies (lib-drive applier in main's session scratchpad): line 1248 takes x's own letter, upper-cased (x |> sm'.slice 0i32 0i32 |> sm'.to_upper), plus test get_full_path_keeps_the_drive_of_an_absolute_path (Windows only: a path on a drive the cwd is not on must keep its drive; it fails on the current code by the bug's own path).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_36 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 36u32, "2026-10-29T08:23Z", "main", "10-29 04:2x main: the drive fix is PROVEN on lib copies (lib_gate -File file_system.spi -Test get_full_path_keeps_the_drive_of_an_absolute_path,get_full_path_2,get_full_path_3, each arm with its own MIX_BUILD_PATH). The first run was VOID: sccache was missing, so the known-good control tests failed too; it was reinstalled, see PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY. CONTROL (new test only, old code): get_full_path_2 and _3 pass, and the new drive test FAILS, which reproduces CI's C-to-D re-rooting locally. A first fix (always upper-case the segment's own letter) broke get_full_path_2: Cells cells run with a lower-case cwd drive (c:/...), and the old code deliberately kept the cwd's letter case for same-drive paths. FINAL fix, file_system.spi:1247-1248: when the segment's letter equals the cwd's drive letter (case-insensitively), use current_dir's letter exactly as before; otherwise use the segment's own letter, upper-cased. Same-drive results are byte-identical by construction. FIXED arm: all 3 pass (3/3, type errors 0). It lands in the live lib after main's lib sweep finishes (the sweep is a live lib reader), followed by a full file_system.spi gate on the live lib. Then the next Windows push run of alphabet or .github is the CI proof.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_37 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 37u32, "2026-10-29T09:26Z", "main", "10-29 05:2x main: drive fix LANDED in the live lib (unstaged; the original is saved in main's session scratchpad as lib-drive/file_system.spi.before). It landed after the lib sweep finished and with 0 Cells readers running, and the live file's sha256 (C46D38D8A5A717A6) equals the proven fixed copy's. Full gate on the live lib: lib_gate -File file_system.spi gives 29 of 29 passed, type errors 0, including the new get_full_path_keeps_the_drive_of_an_absolute_path. Every Rust consumer of file_system.get_full_path regenerates with it, including the spiral CLI that alphabet's and .github's Windows CI build from source. The next Windows push run of either repo is the CI proof, after the user's commit.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_38 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 38u32, "2026-10-29T10:46Z", "main", "10-29 06:4x main: the Playwright step, made concrete (npm registry, read-only): @playwright/test and playwright are at 1.64.1 latest. 1.44.0 is pinned in 4 places: spiral scripts/init.ps1:91 (bunx --bun playwright@1.44.0 install), spiral lib/spiral/near/wallet/package.json, dice ui/e2e/package.json and polyglot apps/spiral/temp/extension/package.json. 1.44's browser host playwright.azureedge.net now answers HTTP 400 (CI logs of alphabet and .github). Bump all 4 together (the browsers must match the test library), then prove it with 'bunx --bun playwright@1.64.1 install' plus the dice ui and near wallet e2e runs. Not done tonight: the browser download is several hundred MB, and the e2e proof did not fit before 07:00.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_39 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 39u32, "2026-10-29T11:42Z", "main", "10-29 07:3x main: Playwright bumped from 1.44.0 to 1.64.1 at 7 sites (all unstaged):\n- spiral: scripts/init.ps1:91, lib/spiral/near/wallet/package.json.\n- dice: ui/e2e/package.json, plus bun.lockb regenerated.\n- currency_converter: test/e2e/package.json, plus bun.lockb, and scripts/init.ps1:9. Its working copy was CRLF against LF in HEAD, so it was normalized to LF.\n- polyglot: apps/spiral/temp/extension/package.json, plus bun.lockb, and scripts/init.livemd:67.\nConsumer path: 'bunx --bun playwright@1.64.1 install' exits 0 and downloads from cdn.playwright.dev (chromium-1248, chromium_headless_shell-1248, firefox-1555, webkit-2370, ffmpeg, winldd), so the retired azureedge host's 400 is gone. dice ui e2e on 1.64.1 passes 1 of 1 in 25s against the 10-26 dist, through the config's own ssl-serve webServer. Also healed: ~/.bun/bin/bun.exe and bunx.exe, which were lost in the wipe; dice's webServer command and currency_converter's run.ps1 and init.ps1 call them by path. They are copies of scoop bun 1.4.4. Left: currency_converter e2e, which needs the Phoenix app up (its run.ps1), and CI after the user's push.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_40 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 40u32, "2026-11-01T14:46Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit: naming/slimness of every own repo's workflow is now CI-WORKFLOW-NAMES (census: 12 conforming, 5 name deviations, 12 without actions.lock). Green status stays here.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_41 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 41u32, "2026-11-01T20:57Z", "main", "main 16:5x CI CENSUS (gh run list, latest run per repo): GREEN spiral 3464d8e, kit e575f7c, x a2d3318, tibia f6a4309, fiscal 1ab6fcd, dice 2f41f4c, dir-tree-html e2f2623, polyglot 69ecfaf, blog 3ff83fc, i574n.github.io. RED: only alphabet 09b0df2, and only its windows-2025 job. Ubuntu passed, and the two runs before it (78e8731, aa79372) passed. The Windows 'pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1' step ran exactly 60 min (16:33-17:33 UTC), hit the job limit, and its log blob is gone (HTTP 404 BlobNotFound). Suspect: the Windows compile-server main-thread stack overflow (COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW) showing as a hang. pack15a's Program.fs fix reaches CI only once the user pushes spiral. Main re-ran the failed job at 16:5x to get a readable log. Status Blocked -> Active: nothing blocks this epic now except that push and alphabet's rerun verdict.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_42 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 42u32, "2026-11-01T23:58Z", "main", "main 20:0x spiral b3fd292b (user push 18:51): CI run 41359205750 GREEN on ubuntu-24.04 and windows-2025. It is the first green run with the edition-3 :: lib respell, pack15a's Program.fs (1 GB compile thread), the Cells profile-loader removal, lib_gate's exit-on-failure and the Linux-portable Cells scripts. Remaining reds: kit (test-attestation.ps1:53 'Plan payload changed', kit is on it), alphabet windows (60-min timeout; rerun in progress), and fiscal windows until its next run picks up the spiral fix.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_43 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 43u32, "2026-11-01T23:59Z", "main", "main 20:0x alphabet 09b0df2: main's re-run of the failed windows-2025 job (run 41326953157) is GREEN on both OSes and pushed Pages, so the 60-min timeout was a runner flake, not the code. Open reds now: kit (test-attestation 'Plan payload changed') and fiscal windows pending its run on the fixed spiral.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_44 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 44u32, "2026-11-02T14:08Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:07 x: GIT CONDITION RE-CHECKED from the tree (groom of user waits). A read-only survey at 09:4x shows every active repo 0 commits ahead of origin: the user committed and pushed alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, kit, fiscal, html, polyglot, regex, spiral, tibia and x in the last 1-3 h. i574n.github.io is the exception (3 unstaged files, last commit 2 years ago). So this item's user step is probably MET. What is left is deterministic and belongs to the owner, not the user: confirm the step's files are in HEAD (git log -- <paths>) and that CI on HEAD is green (gh run list --commit <HEAD>), then close it. Only a red or missing file sends it back to the user queue. Pages enablement (HTML, REGEX-BENCH-PAGE) is a real user step, since it is a public repo setting.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_45 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 45u32, "2026-11-02T17:21Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 13:3x KIT gh-pages RED diagnosis (main's sweep: run 41463936725 @7830da93, both OSes, \"Native Spiral developer workflow failed (exit 101)\"). History: kit's gh-pages has been red on every push since 10-18 (last green dd48d2a6, 10-18 17:48); 56c32ef6 died earlier on a Chocolatey rsync fetch, so 6037c1dd/7830da93 are the first runs to reach these tests. TWO CAUSES in kit_contracts (cargo test -p kit-contracts --lib, under build.ps1 -Test -CompilerContracts): (1) LINUX ONLY, 3 tests (family_contract_semantic_drift_is_rejected, flat_bundle_roundtrip_is_self_verified, public_rejections_and_bundle_drift_fail_closed): \"ColdProofV4 renewal rolled back: regular file exceeds limit: .../kit bytes=313561064 limit=134217728\": the Linux debug kit binary is 313 MB (DWARF inside the ELF; the Windows debug exe is 51 MB with a separate PDB, release 23 MB), over the bundle's 128 MiB per-file cap. Fix candidates, to measure: CI-only CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=line-tables-only (or 0) in the workflow env, which keeps local debugging, vs raising the bundle cap for the kit binary only. (2) BOTH OSes, patch_and_agile_failures_preserve_authority: `agile begin` fails \"KIT failed: Missing check input: <tmp>/state/prompt.spi\" from the compiler host (Program.fs:752 runCheck) during agile_attest_known_mutation, although begin's agile_atomic_write wrote that file just before. NOT REPRODUCIBLE locally: the same fixture + KIT_SPIRAL_COMPILE=<single-flight DLL> passes on the box with installed kit (TRAIN10) and with TRAIN11's candidate D29D6E40, and on the node with both the installed and compiler's linked2 DLL (host 94891444; host source 01:29 < installed host 02:39, so CI's host is the same). So the cause is CI-environment-specific (parallel tests in one process? TEMP layout? runner FS). Next step: download the run's artifacts / rerun with RUST_TEST_THREADS=1 to split parallelism from environment. CI is the user's push domain; the workflow env edit stays unstaged for them.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_46 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 46u32, "2026-11-02T19:49Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 15:5x CAUSE (1) FIXED, MEASURED on europa (TRAIN11 tree, Linux, debug kit-cli). Full debug: 336,416,784 B; CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=line-tables-only: 154,908,040 B (STILL over the 134,217,728 cap, so that candidate is out); CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0: 86,895,256 B (fits, 1m35s build). Edit (unstaged, for the user to commit and push): .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml job env gains `CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG: \"0\"`. It's env-only, so actions.lock needs no regeneration. Local builds keep full debug info. CAUSE (2), \"Missing check input: state/prompt.spi\": a Linux repro of `cargo test -p kit-contracts --lib` is running on europa now. The earlier non-repros were both Windows (box, node), while CI fails on ubuntu too.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_47 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 47u32, "2026-11-02T20:03Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 16:0x CAUSE (2) ROOT-CAUSED AND FIXED; both causes now proven green on Linux (europa, live tree with the fixes, CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0): `cargo test -p kit-contracts --lib -- --include-ignored` gave 15 passed, 0 failed. ROOT CAUSE: the Spiral compiler emits byte-identical `global \"\"\"...\"\"\"` blocks ONCE. contracts_entry/cli_driver.spi splits each Rust test across several global blocks, and patch_and_agile_failures_preserve_authority's \"write state/prompt.spi + agile begin\" block was byte-identical to patch_and_agile_mutation_are_transactional's. The regenerated contracts.rs therefore lacked those two lines in the failures test, which then ran `agile check` on a state package without prompt.spi (\"Missing check input\"). Found by bisection: every replay outside the test passed (environment, cwd, stdin, tight timing, cargo env), the failing test's leftover fixture had no prompt.spi, and the generated body showed the missing lines. Windows box/node \"passed\" only because the committed contracts.rs predated the dedupe. FIX (unstaged): merge that block into its unique predecessor (cli_driver.spi) and regenerate contracts.rs (copied into the box tree; 3 begin sites now). A scan found no other duplicate global blocks in any kit package. With the gh-pages.yml `CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG: \"0\"` env, kit CI should be green once the user commits and pushes. Memory: spiral-identical-globals-dedupe.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_48 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 48u32, "2026-11-03T01:35Z", "main", "main 11-02 21:4x SWEEP (latest non-Pages run per i574n repo). GREEN: spiral 698bc3da, x 60d8c798, dice 84acdd55 (proves the polyglot-free init), alphabet e3f4266d, blog, i574n.github.io. RED, each with its owner and cause:\n- regex d7aa16be: nullable.spi 'Pattern miss'. pack17's GADT erasure wrongly erased the closed-index evidence family. compiler has a proven fix (GADT-ERASE-ONLY-GROWING) shipping with pack20, after pack19 (queued behind kit's TRAIN13c heavy window).\n- dir-tree-html 71c801f (user commit 19:16): 'module does not have the key: try_write_all_text'. LIB-MOVE-DTH-FS's dir-tree-html half was committed before its lib function landed. main lands lib/spiral/file_system.spi once TRAIN13c stops reading the live lib; then a spiral push + re-run.\n- html 05162bda: COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS-EXTERN-CALLS Step B, which ships in pack19.\n- kit ba3d2100 and tibia 355726ca (23:16Z): those lanes' own reds, not triaged by main.\n- fiscal: in progress.\n- vault (08-10) and dotfiles: stale runs, no recent push.\n- tapas2: no runs yet (its first push carries tonight's port).\n- i574n.github: Actions API 404.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_49 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 49u32, "2026-11-03T13:33Z", "main", "main 11-03 09:3x CI sweep (latest runs per org repo): green = alphabet, blog, dice, fiscal, regex, spiral, x (+ pages deploys). RED: html (1000-line gate; fixed by the unstaged 8-crate split, awaits the user's push), .github (polyglot core.ps1 + pins drift; fixed unstaged in i574n.github, awaits push), kit 1266f9df run 41602520066 NEW: test-regeneration's notebook batch passes 119 paths on one Windows command line -> 'The command line is too long.' -> 'spiral notebooks failed (119)' (src/kit_dev_spiral_tests main.spi:32); ubuntu fails in the same step. Reported to kit (owner).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_50 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 50u32, "2026-11-03T13:37Z", "main", "main 11-03 09:3x addendum: tibia gh-pages RED too (bfd1641c, 07:42): deps/spiral/scripts/init-wgsl-run.ps1:35 LunarG 'latest' = 1.4.363.1 has no Windows runtime (404). Fixed by the unstaged spiral init-wgsl-run.ps1 fallback (published versions newest first -> 1.4.363.0); turns green when the user pushes spiral. So 4 reds: 3 await pushes (html, .github, spiral->tibia), 1 is kit's (reported).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_51 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 51u32, "2026-11-04T12:18Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:2x resilience CI RED-LIST AUDIT across 15 active i574n repos (additive data, no claim). RESULT: 13/15 green; 2 RED on the Rust/Spiral tests workflow on main = .github and html. x ci(tests) is GREEN; the doc-only repos (spiral/kit/fiscal/dice/tibia/alphabet/regex/blog/hangulize/The-Spiral-Language/i574n.github.io/dotfiles) have only gh-pages + pages-build-deployment (no test CI), all green. ROOT CAUSE (html run 41598172732, CONFIRMED from log-failed): NOT a test failure - spiral-tests all pass (37 passed, 0 failed, 8 unsupported, 7 files). The build fails at build.ps1 line 36 because ReadyToRun crossgen codegen FAILS on Hopac.dll (message: ReadyToRun Hopac.dll stays IL; Code generation failed for method Hopac.EmbeddedJob generic GetHashCode over __Canon) then exit 1, fail-fast cancels the windows sibling, push job fails/skips. .github (run 41598261461) fails at the SAME build.ps1 step on ubuntu - very likely the SAME Hopac R2R issue (same build.ps1; not log-confirmed). So both reds share ONE root cause: build.ps1 ReadyToRun cannot compile a Hopac generic method. FIX is build-logic (exclude Hopac from R2R, or tolerate stays-IL, or fix the crossgen path) - owner call, likely tied to HOPACSOTA-P6. Side note: cargo-quickinstall warehouse-clerk endpoint returns 402 Payment Required (non-fatal WARN). Diagnosis only - build scripts are not resilience autonomous remit.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_52 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 52u32, "2026-11-04T12:19Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:2x CONFIRMED: .github (run 41598261461 ubuntu) is the IDENTICAL root cause as html - ReadyToRun Hopac.dll stays IL, ILCompiler.CodeGenerationFailedException: Code generation failed for method Hopac.EmbeddedJob generic GetHashCode over __Canon, then exit 1. So BOTH reds are the SAME Hopac R2R crossgen failure (exception class ILCompiler.CodeGenerationFailedException). Tests pass; only the R2R precompile step fails (the run also logs 22 dlls precompiled in 15s, so R2R continues past Hopac but the captured error exits 1). Handing to the owner - build-logic fix (exclude Hopac from R2R, or tolerate CodeGenerationFailedException for Hopac.dll). resilience CI-audit contribution complete: 2/15 repos red, one shared root cause, actionable.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_53 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 53u32, "2026-11-04T14:40Z", "main", "11-04 10:4x main CI CENSUS (gh run list, latest run per repo): GREEN kit, x, fiscal, tibia, regex, dice, alphabet, blog (pages builds 11-02..11-03); spiral IN PROGRESS (98e51d3, started 14:32Z); RED html (05a1a43 \"tests\", 11-03) and dir-tree-html (71c801f, 11-02); i574n.github and polyglot show no runs. html's red is ONLY the 1000-line gate (markdown/entry.rs 2339 lines in the committed single-crate layout; all 37 executed tests pass). The unstaged html tree (crate split + 11-04 cell-budget and viewer work) is HTML-OK on europa with 49/49 and largest generated 994 lines, so html heals with the user's next commit, with no CI change needed. dir-tree-html: not checked (html is replacing it).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_54 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 54u32, "2026-11-04T14:41Z", "main", "11-04 10:4x main dir-tree-html RED CAUSE (run on 71c801f, 11-02): RUST-FAILED dir-tree-html/inline, ETY-ModuleIndexFailed \"The module does not have the key: try_write_all_text In the definition: write_text\": dir-tree-html calls a lib file_system export that lib no longer has. html replaces dir-tree-html (its retirement is planned), so no patch here; the retirement itself is the user's call and isn't done by a lane.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_55 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 55u32, "2026-11-05T05:17Z", "main", "11-05 01:17 main SURVEY after the user's 11-04 23:18 push (gh run list, every active i574n repo): 9 of 11 red, ALL ONE ROOT CAUSE: spiral, fiscal, kit, .github, blog, regex, x, alphabet, dice each fail at spiral's scripts/init-app-build.ps1 CLI build with \"could not execute process `sccache ... rustc -vV`\" (2-6 hits per log), because main's 11-03 init-app-build step (ORG-EXIT-FALLOUT n7) builds and run-checks the CLI before init.ps1 installs sccache; fiscal additionally hits the deps/spiral get_workspace_root panic. Green: tibia, html (plus static-pages repos). FIX in the tree, unstaged (ORG-EXIT-FALLOUT n8/n9): init-app-build installs binstall+sccache first and sets SPIRAL_REPO_ROOT; the get_workspace_root deps fallback is in its node proof now. PENDING the user's next spiral push; any red left after it is a separate cause, to be re-surveyed then.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_56 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 56u32, "2026-11-05T06:50Z", "main", "11-05 02:50 main FRESH-RUNNER PROOF of the init fix (before the user's push): codespace miniature-meme-x7jqqp6pp3v957 (Ubuntu 24.04, 4 cores, no Rust, no sccache, no cargo-binstall), spiral HEAD faf422c9 cloned with the box's fixed scripts/init-app-build.ps1 (886B46344C30) and init.ps1 dropped in; rustup + nightly-2026-10-18 minimal installed first, as setup-rust does in CI. init.ps1 -> init-app-build: cargo-binstall --self-install, then \"cargo binstall sccache\" (sccache v0.19.0 -> ~/.cargo/bin, 2.7 s), THEN apps/spiral/build.ps1 -fast 1: run check rust exit 0 \"spiral-check-rust 42\" (the CI red case), cuda/lua/gleam 42, protocol cases as expected, no FAILED / RUST-FAILED line; init continued (trunk via binstall). So the sccache-ordering red is fixed for every repo whose CI runs spiral init. The deps/spiral panic fix is the file_system.spi land (n10). PENDING: CI on the user's next push.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_57 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 57u32, "2026-11-05T20:19Z", "main", "main 11-05 16:2x CI SURVEY: spiral's scheduled run 42046993944 (14:38, HEAD faf422c9) is red in compiler and cells-gate for the SAME known cause as push run 41960731928 (03:18): apps/spiral/build.ps1 run check -> 'could not execute process sccache ... rustc -vV (never executed)' (CLI built before sccache is installed). The fix (init-app-build.ps1/init.ps1 install order) is unstaged and was proven on a fresh codespace at 02:58 (CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS n12). Last green: 41848519924 on 98e51d32 (11-04; compiler 18 min, cells-gate 54 min). Because CI stops at init, nothing after it has run against the unstaged tree: a full pre-push rehearsal of both jobs on a codespace is running now (CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS n13). Still waits on the user's push.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_58 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 58u32, "2026-11-05T22:35Z", "main", "main 11-05 18:4x PRE-PUSH REHEARSAL RESULT (codespace, HEAD faf422c9 + the box's whole unstaged spiral tree, CI's two jobs as written): compiler job GREEN (exit 0, 1162 s; native 3556 ran, 8 build-fail tolerated, oracle agree 2797, DISAGREE 0). cells-gate job exited 0, BUT its table holds 3 FAILED of 938 executed: ci_gate.ps1 only threw on 0 executed and never on failed tests, so cells-gate has been green with failures. The 11-04 green CI artifact (run 41848519924) shows 2 of 717 failed there too. FIXED (unstaged, spiral apps/cells/spi/ci_gate.ps1): the summary line counts failures, and any failed test prints file: test=targets and throws 'cells gate is RED'; tested on 3 summaries (rehearsal tsv -> RED 3 of 938, all-pass -> green, empty -> VOID). So after the push, cells-gate turns RED on 3 real failures until they are fixed: (1) NEW vs 11-04: lib/spiral/json_schema.spi json_schemas_map_onto_the_xsd_model_and_generate (rust; failed after 44 s). The file and its :< imports are unchanged; it consumes xsd_emit (+1162/-770 unstaged, fiscal's lands). Routed to fiscal. (2) PRE-EXISTING: process.spi:981 a_program_past_its_deadline_is_killed (rust) runs 'ping -n 30 127.0.0.1'; -n is Windows' count flag, so on Linux ping exits at once and is never killed; needs a per-OS long program (sleep 30 on Linux). (3) PRE-EXISTING: reactors.spi:311 physical_micros_reads_a_real_clock_that_moves_forward (c) asserts > 1.7e15 us since epoch; the C backend likely returns a monotonic clock.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_59 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 59u32, "2026-11-05T22:37Z", "main", "main 11-05 18:5x ROUTING the 3 reds behind the ci_gate fix: (1) json_schema (rust, new) -> fiscal (xsd_emit owner), with repro 'run_notebook --gate --path lib/spiral/json_schema.spi'. (2) process.spi:981 ping -n on Linux -> DRAFT written: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\PIPELINES-GREEN\\process.spi, base 9DD18D6D3EF1, a 2-line change: ':= program, args = ? platform::is_windows () ?> \"ping\", [-n 30 127.0.0.1] |: \"sleep\", [\"30\"]' (the idiom of exit_four_shell at :708). NOT yet proven or landed: the node is saturated by TRAIN32 until ~23:00 and the land needs the node -PrivateLib proof + box -ProvenLog (LAND-GATE-OFF-BOX); lands through lib_land_window -Draft lib/spiral/process.spi=<draft> -BaseHash 9DD18D6D3EF1 (pending). (3) reactors.spi:311 C clock (epoch bound fails; likely a monotonic clock id) -> compiler.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_60 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 60u32, "2026-11-05T22:53Z", "main", "main 11-05 18:5x CORRECTION to n58/n59 red (3), from compiler: reactors.spi:311 on C is NOT a monotonic-clock bug (the code already uses CLOCK_REALTIME). It's a BUILD failure, gcc 'error: CLOCK_REALTIME undeclared' (visible in run 41848519924's gate.log). Cells compiles C in strict -std mode, where glibc hides clock_gettime without POSIX feature macros. compiler's fix: the POSIX branch calls C11 timespec_get(&ts, TIME_UTC), which builds under c11/c17/gnu11 and returns epoch micros; proven on europa gcc 15.2; draft drafts\\spiral\\REACTORS-C-CLOCK\\1-timespec-get.kitdo, landing via lib_land_window now. Remaining push reds: json_schema (fiscal) and process ping -n (main's draft, pending a node proof).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_61 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 61u32, "2026-11-05T22:53Z", "main", "main 11-05 18:5x EVIDENCE for red (2), process.spi ping: the 11-04 CI gate.log shows '__assert_eq { actual = 2; expected = -1 }' after 2169 ms on rust. So Linux ping exited 2 at once (-n is not a count there, and '30' is read as a host) instead of being killed at the 400 ms deadline. The draft's per-OS program (sleep 30 on Linux) is the right fix; still to be proven and landed.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_62 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 62u32, "2026-11-05T22:59Z", "main", "main 11-05 19:0x RED (1) json_schema FIXED by fiscal: cause was a stale expectation, not xsd_emit breakage. Nominal records arrived in faf422c9, so the root object is now emitted as 'nominal x_invoice =', not 'type x_invoice'. The assertion was updated; proven on europa (8/8, and a mutant expecting x_no_such_root fails on the same assertion); landed via lib_land_window fiscal-json-schema-1105, json_schema.spi B6139AD7 -> F7564185, closed green, undo 20261105-185110, unstaged. Note the 11-04 CI was on 98e51d32, before faf422c9, which is why it passed there. STATE of the 3 push reds: json_schema fixed+landed (fiscal); reactors C clock fix landing (compiler, timespec_get); process ping draft under a Linux proof on europa (main, control+draft run).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_63 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 63u32, "2026-11-05T23:03Z", "main", "main 11-05 19:0x red (2) process ping: the europa proof is VOID. Scratch lib copy outside the mirror (base 9dd18d6d3ef1), process.spi gate under systemd MemoryMax=1500M with a private MIX_BUILD_PATH. CONTROL (base) failed 11 of 15, where CI fails exactly 1, and the draft failed 10, with different sets (split_command vs split_args): an environment failure, not a result. Causes: 'ld terminated with signal 7 [Bus error]' while linking a Rust cell under the cap on the 2-CPU host, and europa has no 'python' (only python3), which several process tests invoke. Scratch removed. Lesson: europa can't host process.spi's gate (link memory + python name). The draft (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\PIPELINES-GREEN\\process.spi, base 9DD18D6D3EF1) still needs a Linux proof: a codespace (python present, 16 GB) or the next CI after a land. Not landed.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_64 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 64u32, "2026-11-05T23:09Z", "compiler", "11-05 19:0x compiler: the 2 cells-gate test failures that turn RED once ci_gate.ps1 fails on failed tests (main, 11-05), both from run 41848519924 (i574n/spiral, cells-gate artifact): (1) reactors physical_micros_reads_a_real_clock_that_moves_forward on C: a BUILD failure, gcc 'CLOCK_REALTIME undeclared' (Cells compiles C in a strict standard mode, where glibc hides clock_gettime without POSIX feature macros). Europa Ubuntu gcc 15.2: under -std=c11/c17 clock_gettime fails with 2 errors and builds only under gnu11, while C11 timespec_get(&ts, TIME_UTC) builds in every mode, returns epoch micros and passes the predicate. LANDED (unstaged): the POSIX branch calls timespec_get; the Windows branch is unchanged. Box gate c/rust/zig 1/1; lib_land_window GREEN (document_test 26, type_probe PASS, 0 drift); live reactors.spi 5114A20318F7 -> 8B2A746DD533, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-185656. (2) process a_program_past_its_deadline_is_killed on rust: the test ran 'ping -n 30 127.0.0.1' (Windows syntax); on Linux, -n is numeric output, so ping exits 2 at once (europa: exit 2) and the test saw 2, not -1. Fix: a portable 'python -c import time; time.sleep(30)' (python is on the CI runner, since a_started_program_reports_its_exit passes there). Box gate rust+gleam 1/1; lib_land_window running.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_65 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 65u32, "2026-11-05T23:16Z", "compiler", "11-05 19:1x compiler: (2) process a_program_past_its_deadline_is_killed LANDED too (unstaged): lib_land_window GREEN, 2 consumer signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26, type_probe PASS (18 typed), 0 drift. Live process.spi 9DD18D6D3EF1 -> B68E3B9F02E5, lib digest FBD04FD38F7EFF1F, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-190655. Both of run 41848519924's cells-gate failures are now fixed in the tree; confirmation is the next CI run on Linux after the user's push (box gates prove the Windows path and the Linux C behaviour was proven on europa; the Linux Rust cell itself is CI's to show).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_66 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 66u32, "2026-11-05T23:17Z", "main", "main 11-05 19:1x ALL THREE push reds now have landed fixes (unstaged): json_schema (fiscal, F7564185), reactors C clock (compiler, timespec_get, 8B2A746D), process deadline (compiler, 'python -c import time; time.sleep(30)', process.spi 9DD18D6D -> B68E3B9F, landed 19:09). main's per-OS sleep draft is superseded and deleted. A codespace run (started 19:05, so before compiler's land; its tree has the original ping test) is checking on Linux (a) that the fixed ci_gate goes RED on the original test and (b) that a non-ping long program passes. The real confirmation is the next Linux CI after the user's push.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_67 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 67u32, "2026-11-05T23:32Z", "main", "main 11-05 19:3x FLEET CI (latest run per own repo): RED at init from the SAME root, spiral's CLI-before-sccache order inherited through init.ps1: kit 0f6dd8f, x f9c95a5, dice 09c27e2, alphabet 082f8f2, blog 7f35d12 ('could not execute process sccache' in each), fiscal bb1ebad (fails inside the init.ps1 step), regex 53b31ae, i574n/.github f509c4f, plus spiral faf422c itself. GREEN: html 68ccb00, tibia eb5900b (pages), dotfiles, i574n.github.io. So one user push of spiral (with the unstaged init-order fix) unblocks 9 repos; after that the next push or schedule of each is the real gate, and cells-gate turns honest via the ci_gate fix (3 known reds now fixed and landed).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_68 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 68u32, "2026-11-05T23:40Z", "main", "main 11-05 19:4x LINUX PROOF (fresh codespace, HEAD faf422c9 + the box's unstaged tree as of 19:06, after workflow.ps1 -Compiler exit 0): (a) the FIXED ci_gate.ps1 on the ORIGINAL process.spi (9dd18d6d3ef1): exit 1, 'cells gate is RED: 1 of 18 executed tests failed', failed_tests = [a_program_past_its_deadline_is_killed] only. So the gate now fails honestly on Linux, and the ping -n bug reproduces exactly as in CI. (b) the same file with a per-OS long program (sleep 30 on Linux) passes: 18/18, exit 0, 95 s. compiler's LANDED variant (python -c time.sleep(30); python is present on the codespace and the runner) is the same approach but was not itself run here; the next Linux CI confirms it. Codespace deleted.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_69 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 69u32, "2026-11-06T00:03Z", "main", "main 11-05 20:0x CONSUMER PRE-PUSH PROOF: regex's full workflow at 53b31ae, run against the box's unstaged spiral tree on a fresh codespace, is GREEN (init passes with the order fix; 93/93 tests). So the user's spiral push is expected to turn regex's red (run 41960786290, sccache at init) green, and by the same root very likely kit, x, fiscal, dice, alphabet, blog and .github too (not individually rehearsed).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_70 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 70u32, "2026-11-06T00:34Z", "main", "main 11-05 20:3x CONSUMER REHEARSALS (codespace, each repo's own workflow.ps1 beside the box's unstaged spiral tree): blog 7f35d12 GREEN (exit 0, 214 s, every notebook passed, 'BLOG-OK'); regex 53b31ae GREEN (93/93, earlier). VOID, not a verdict: dice 09c27e2 hit my 840 s per-repo bound (exit 124; its workflow is longer); alphabet 082f8f2 exit 1 because 'cargo install tectonic' built from source and failed on the missing graphite2 system libs, while real CI provides tectonic/pandoc/fonts via wtfjoke/setup-tectonic + cache-apt-pkgs-action, which a bare codespace doesn't replicate. Both regex and blog passed spiral's init with the order fix, which is the step all 9 red repos die at today.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_71 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 71u32, "2026-11-06T10:10Z", "main", "main 11-06 06:1x groom: BLOCKED on the user's spiral push (it carries the init-order fix that turns the 9 repos green). Before the push: compiler warns pack51 retired rust-extern derive, so dice's derive land must be checked against it (RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE). Still VOID, to redo after the push: dice (needs a bound over 840 s) and alphabet (needs CI's prebuilt tectonic, not cargo install).")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_72 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 72u32, "2026-11-06T10:16Z", "main", "main 11-06 06:1x from compiler's 06:12 star exchange: in spiral's gh-pages.yml the compiler and cells-gate jobs run only on schedule or workflow_dispatch, so a push run shows them as skipped. After the user's push, the proof for those two jobs is the next SCHEDULED run's log (gh run view <id> --log), not the push run; the 9 consumer repos are proven by their own push runs.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_73 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 73u32, "2026-11-06T13:19Z", "main", "main 11-06 12:2x from compiler: spiral's 11-05 SCHEDULED compiler job failed on 94 Python rows whose residuals are byte-identical to 11-04's green run, with no Python code path changed, so it's the runner environment (likely the array runtime import), not the compiler. Tracked on CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE; today's scheduled run shows whether it persists. Push runs (01:54Z) were green on the 9 consumer repos.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_74 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 74u32, "2026-11-06T15:13Z", "main", "main 11-06 15:1x spiral SCHEDULED run 42232499425 (c0e77c3, 14:16Z): compiler job = SUCCESS. Yesterday's scheduled compiler job failed on 94 Python rows (runner environment, per compiler); today's is green on the pushed tree. cells-gate is still running, the first CI run of the gate that is RED on any failed test (ci_gate.ps1); verdict pending.")
inl note_of_pipelines_green_75 () = Note ("PIPELINES-GREEN", 75u32, "2026-11-06T15:30Z", "main", "main 11-06 15:3x DONE, proof: (1) the 9 repos that were red on the spiral init-order bug are green on the user's 11-06 01:54Z push: spiral, fiscal, regex, html, dice, alphabet and blog are green; kit and x went red later for their own unrelated causes (kit: kit_jobs/stop_cli's Windows job-object test on Linux, fixed unstaged by kit on KIT-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW; x: the 11-05 Cells order change, CELLS-NOTEBOOK-ORDER-REGRESSION, x's revert in proof), and .github is green. (2) spiral's SCHEDULED run 42232499425 on c0e77c3 (14:16Z): compiler = success (yesterday's 94 Python rows gone) and cells-gate = success, the first CI run of ci_gate.ps1's RED-on-any-failed-test rule, so every lib/spiral and apps/transformer test passed on Linux.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night input: with every job delegating to workflow.ps1, the 7 workflows are 42-117 lines whose bulk is toolchain setup (4-17 uses steps each), per-repo copies of version pins that drift (CI zig 0.17 vs local 0.18-dev, OTP 27 vs 29, old Rust nightlies). The first useful output is one typed toolchain-pin source shared by all repos.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:56Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:56 helper-lane lap 1: re-measured the drift note 1 described: today the 9 workflows agree on every shared pin (zig 0.17.0, OTP 29, Elixir 1.20, Gleam 1.19.2, Python 3.12.10, dotnet 9.0.202, rust nightly-2026-10-18; blog, x, eoie and dir-tree-html use subsets), i.e. the 10-17 drift (OTP 27 vs 29, old nightlies) was fixed by hand-syncing 9 copies, which is exactly what a single typed pin source would make unnecessary. One pin worth a look before typing it: setup-dotnet 9.0.202 in CI while the local compiler builds target net11.0 (bin/.../net11.0), so either the CI compiler job installs its own SDK through the scripts or the pin is stale. Gate CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE unchanged.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:38 helper-lane lap 2: answered the dotnet question from lap 1: the compiler does not use the workflows' setup-dotnet 9.0.202 at all. Resolve-SpiralDotnet (apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/env.ps1) only accepts an SDK that has .NET 11 (Test-DotnetHasSdk11), looking in SPIRAL_DOTNET, the cache's toolchains/dotnet, the shared cache, then PATH; when none qualifies, scripts/install-dotnet.ps1 installs channel 11.0 preview into the cache with dotnet-install (called from spiral scripts/workflow.ps1:19, lib/spiral/lib.ps1:19 and Cells run_notebook.ps1:90). So the 9.0.202 pin in 6 workflows serves only older F# paths (lib/fsharp builds, polyglot), and a typed pin source has two .NET entries with different owners: workflow 9.0.202 and the compiler's 11.0 preview from install-dotnet.ps1 - a second, script-level pin that YAML generation alone would not see.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:23Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:23 helper-lane lap 3: full pin census as the typed pin source's input (lab/SPIRAL-TO-YAML/pins.tsv: repo, file, line, pin over the tracked .ps1/.yml/.toml/.psd1/.json/.nix/Dockerfile files of the 9 repos, deps and samples excluded; 199 hits). YAML side: 100 action refs (uses: @vN, already locked by actions.lock), plus setup pins repeated per workflow - otp 29, elixir 1.20, gleam 1.19.2 and zig 0.17.0 in 9 workflows each, rebar3 3.27.3 in 9 (written with two quote styles, 6 single and 3 double: drift a typed source would remove), python 3.12.10 in 7, dotnet 9.0.202 in 6, rust nightly-2026-10-18 in 10. Script side, invisible to YAML generation: nightly-2026-10-18 hard-coded in 22 places across 14 scripts (test.ps1, five spiral app build.ps1, init.ps1 x2, polyglot math, dice lib and contract, alphabet documents, dir-tree-html), nightly-2024-07-14 in 7 (the NEAR contract pin: spiral and polyglot init, polyglot chat contract, dice contract), x's rust-toolchain.toml channel, and install-dotnet.ps1's Channel 11.0 Quality preview. So one typed pin record needs about 10 entries (rust main, rust near, dotnet workflow, dotnet compiler, otp, elixir, gleam, rebar3, zig, python), emitted both into YAML and into a generated pins.ps1 the scripts dot-source.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:41Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:41 helper-lane lap 4: the typed pin source as code, lab/SPIRAL-TO-YAML/pins.spi (unrun until main's freeze all-clear): union tool with the 10 entries from the lap-3 census (RustMain, RustNear, DotnetWorkflow, DotnetCompiler, Otp, Elixir, Gleam, Rebar3, Zig, Python), one pin list, and two exhaustive renderers - render_ps1 writes a pins.ps1 the scripts can dot-source (one line per pin, for example RustToolchain = nightly-2026-10-18), render_yaml writes the with: lines of the setup steps with one quote style (the rebar3 drift disappears), skipping the two script-only pins (RustNear, DotnetCompiler) by an empty key. Adding a tool without both renderings is a missing-case error. Probe main compares both outputs with the exact expected text (expect exit 11).")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 6u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: pins.spi exits 11 as predicted - both renderers (pins.ps1 lines and setup-step with: lines) are byte-equal to the expected text from the typed pin list.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): pins.spi unrun (jobs held) -> next: run after main's go | lap 6 13:42 (subagent): pins PASS (11) -> next: render a full setup step block | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: pins.spi 11 -> next: next pin set")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 8u32, "2026-10-26T01:01Z", "main", "10-25 21:01 main: pin re-census of the 11 workflow files across the repos. Every shared pin agrees (otp 29 x12, elixir 1.20 x12, gleam 1.19.2 x12, zig 0.17.0 x11, python 3.12.10 x8, dotnet 9.0.202 x9, rust nightly-2026-10-18 x13) EXCEPT rebar3, which drifted again: 3.28.0 in spiral, polyglot, dice, alphabet and dir-tree-html (6), 3.27.3 in x/ci.yml:50, eoie gh-pages.yml:36, tibia :47, blog :32, i574n.github :32 and fiscal :30 (6). 3.28.0 is rebar3's current stable (published 2026-10-21; 3.27.3 is 10-12), so someone bumped half the copies by hand: exactly the hand-sync drift this item's typed pin source removes. Healed main's two: blog and i574n.github set to 3.28.0, each file's own quote style kept, LF, no new uses: (no actions-lock change), unstaged for the user. Asked: kit for eoie (after its rename), tibia for tibia (after its PR-28 block). x (no live session) and fiscal (offline): their teams bump x/.github/workflows/ci.yml:50 and fiscal/.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml:30 to 3.28.0, the same one-line edit. That makes 2 drifts in 6 days with every copy hand-synced, which raises the case for pins.spi (lab, probe GREEN 10-19) rendering every workflow's with: lines.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 9u32, "2026-10-26T10:44Z", "main", "10-26 06:44 main: first working piece: a drift checker, %TEMP%\\spiral-to-yaml\\pins_check.spi (lib-first, edition 3, compiles to dependency-free Rust). A typed pin list (8 setup pins: rust channel, dotnet, otp, elixir, gleam, rebar3, zig, python), each owned by an action (moonrepo/setup-rust, actions/setup-dotnet, erlef/setup-beam, mlugg/setup-zig, actions/setup-python). It reads every workflow path given on the command line, tracks the current step's uses: line, compares each owned key's value ignoring the quote style (which itself drifted), and prints path:line: key found X, pins say Y, with the mismatch count as its exit code. Positive control (a probe with otp '28', zig 0.16.0 and an unrelated cache-apt 'version: 1.0'): exit 2, exactly the two planted drifts. Two bugs found by the controls and fixed. (1) A bare key matched other actions' keys (alphabet's cache-apt version: 1.0); pins are now scoped to their action. (2) lib's argument_offset already skips the program name, so index 1 silently skipped the first file; it now starts at 0. Run over all 11 tracked workflows: exit 2, the two real drifts left from n8, x/.github/workflows/ci.yml:50 and fiscal/.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml:30 (rebar3 3.27.3, should be 3.28.0); eoie and tibia are already fixed. The old lab pins.spi is stale (rebar3 3.27.3) and is superseded by this list. Next: wire pins_check into each repo's workflow.ps1 so CI fails on drift, then render the setup blocks from the same list.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 10u32, "2026-10-26T10:51Z", "main", "10-26 06:51 main: all 11 tracked workflows agree with the typed pins (pins_check exit 0, 06:4x). x fixed x/.github/workflows/ci.yml:50 and fiscal fixed fiscal/.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml:30 (rebar3 3.28.0, both unstaged), each confirmed with pins_check by its own lane. The checker found the drift and it was fixed the same hour, without a manual census. Next step unchanged: run pins_check from each repo's workflow.ps1, so CI fails on drift instead of a session noticing it.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 11u32, "2026-10-26T11:13Z", "main", "10-26 07:13 main: pins_check wired into the org workflow (unstaged in i574n.github, the org-tooling home). scripts/pins/ holds pins_check.spi (the typed pin list, moved from the %TEMP% draft), package.spiproj (edition 3, packageDir ../../../spiral/lib) and pins.ps1. pins.ps1 compiles with lib BuildSpiral into dist/pins_check.rs, which the root .gitignore 'dist' already covers, builds it with rustc +nightly-2026-10-18, and runs it over every .github/workflows/*.yml of the 11 org repos: alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, eoie, fiscal, i574n.github, polyglot, spiral, tibia, x. It throws if it finds no workflow. scripts/workflow.livemd runs `pwsh pins/pins.ps1` as its first cell, before build.ps1. Proof on the consumer path (pwsh scripts/pins/pins.ps1): compile 43 s, then 'pins_check: 0 pin lines disagree', exit 0. Positive control through Invoke-Block on the probe: the 2 planted drifts are printed and Invoke-Block exits 2, so the workflow stops. Next: render the setup-* steps from the same typed list (the YAML-backend half of this item), starting with erlef/setup-beam, which carries 4 of the 8 pins.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 12u32, "2026-10-26T11:51Z", "main", "10-26 07:51 main: pins_check now also types the action refs. An action_pin list holds 14 actions at their newest releases, read from gh api releases/latest at 07:2x. Both pin kinds are bundled into one pinset record. Each `uses: name@ref` line is checked against it; an action with no pin (cache-apt @master) is skipped. The first run over the 11 org workflows found 60 lagging refs, outside the tool pins: checkout v6.1.0 (latest v7.0.1), upload-artifact v5.0.0 (v7.0.2), download-artifact v6.0.0 (v8.0.2), setup-dotnet v5.4.0 (v6.0.0), setup-python v6.3.0/v7.0.0 (v7.1.0, released 10-22), setup-go v5.6.0 (v7.0.0), setup-tectonic v3.1.5 (v4.0.6). Fiscal and tibia already ran the new majors in CI; the only breaking change named in the notes is the Node 24 runtime. Main bumped alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, i574n.github, polyglot and spiral (48 lines, unstaged) and regenerated each actions.lock with `gh actions-lock`. Tags are kept in the workflows; only the lock SHAs move. x (7), kit/eoie (4) and tibia (1) bumped their own. The pins run now reports 0 drift in every repo. New --fix mode: `pins_check --fix <files>` rewrites each drifting value in place (the ref after @, or the value after the key, quote style kept) and writes a file only when the unfixed rebuild equals the original bytes. Controls on %TEMP%\\pins-fix-control: an LF probe got exactly its 2 planted fixes, the recheck exits 0 and the line count is kept; a ref probe got exactly 1 fix; a CRLF probe was refused ('its lines do not round-trip'), so a fix never rewrites line endings. The next bump is therefore one edit to the typed list plus `pins_check --fix`, not a hand script. Open: the polyglot and spiral actions.lock regeneration timed out at 180 s (the other 5 took seconds; rate limit fine); a 900 s retry is running.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 13u32, "2026-10-26T12:03Z", "main", "10-26 08:03 main: n12's open point closed: the polyglot and spiral actions.lock regeneration finished on the 900 s retry (exit 0; both had timed out at 180 s with no output, likely the tool walking their large trees, while the rate limit stood at 4,939 left). The scratchpad relock.ps1 runs gh.exe by full path (bare gh resolved to a non-Win32 shim under Start-Process), with an empty file as stdin and a per-repo timeout. Final pins_check run over all 11 org workflows: 0 pin lines disagree, exit 0. Main's 7 repos carry unstaged diffs in .github/workflows only (gh-pages.yml refs + actions.lock), 16 to 36 lines each; x, tibia and kit/eoie report theirs done and verified with the same binary.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_14 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 14u32, "2026-10-26T12:39Z", "main", "10-26 08:39 main: first workflows generated from typed Spiral (the item's goal, not just a checker): i574n.github/scripts/pins is now one package of 4 modules.\n- pins: the facts. The tool union, version_of as a total match (no union equality needed), tool_pins derived from it, action_pins, action_of, yaml_key.\n- pins_check: the drift checker and --fix, now opening pins.\n- workflow: the typed model. trigger = Push | PullRequest | Dispatch; runner = Runs | Matrix {keep_going; os}; value = Plain | Quoted | ToolPin; setting; uses_step and run_step, each with an optional condition; job and workflow as multi-line records. Plus the renderer: action refs come from action_pins and pinned values from version_of, so a workflow cannot state a version.\n- workflows: the entry. Per-repo definitions plus `workflows <git-root> [--write]`: compare each rendered target with its file (first differing line, exit = files differing) or rewrite it; a missing file is reported, not a panic.\npins.ps1 builds both entries and runs both checks in the org workflow's first cell.\nResults: dir-tree-html/.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml renders byte-identical (v1, 53 lines). blog's (v2: dispatch trigger, top-level permissions, fail-fast false, run and job conditions) differed in exactly 2 lines, both quote-only (rebar3 '3.28.0' and zig 0.17.0 unquoted -> \"...\"). Under the rule written before rendering (canonical pins are double-quoted; a quote-only difference is normalized by --write; any other difference is a model bug), blog was rewritten. It is now identical, and pins_check still gives 0.\nControls: a planted timeout 120 -> 90 on a copy is reported at line 10 (exit 1); --write restores it byte for byte; the recheck exits 0.\nSpiral gotchas found: `when` is reserved; the tool cases Gleam and Zig collide with lib's backend union, so callers outside pins write pins.Gleam and pins.Zig.\nLock lesson (from kit): gh actions-lock needs --no-interactive in agent shells, or it waits on a prompt with empty stdin. That was the cause of the 180 s polyglot/spiral timeouts.\nNext: the other 9 workflows, one per change, each held to the same rule. Then the setup-beam/setup-zig blocks shared across repos become functions, so a pin bump is one typed edit plus `workflows --write`.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_15 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 15u32, "2026-10-26T12:53Z", "main", "10-26 08:53 main: rule for adopting a workflow into the typed source, written before rendering the next ones (i574n.github, polyglot). The rendered file may differ from the live one only by (a) pin quote style (canonical: double quotes), (b) removed YAML comment lines (no-comments rule; a fact in such a comment goes into a name or is dropped), (c) indentation alone (canonical unit: 2 spaces, as in 10 of the 11 org workflows; polyglot alone uses 4). Anything else is a model bug and blocks the --write. Checked mechanically by scratchpad wf-rule-diff.ps1 -Repos <list>, which renders into a scratch copy and classifies every differing line as quote-only, indent-only, removed-comment or OTHER. Applied so far: dir-tree-html 0/0/0 and blog 2 quote-only (both normalized earlier); dice 2 quote-only + 3 comments; alphabet 1 quote-only + 3 comments; OTHER 0 in all four. The dropped dice/alphabet comment said the cells notebooks need Gleam >= 1.14 because kino's manifest pins gleam_stdlib 1.0.5; the pin itself (1.19.2) already satisfies it. Also: workflows.rs from :=-inlined helpers came out at 2,747 lines (over the 1,000-line gate). Every renderer and definition function is now a :~ join point (main stays :=); the line count after that rebuild is recorded next.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_16 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 16u32, "2026-10-26T13:35Z", "main", "10-26 09:35 main: 7 of 11 org workflows are now generated from the typed source and byte-identical: dir-tree-html, blog, dice, alphabet, i574n.github, polyglot, spiral (unstaged, .github/workflows only). Each passed the n15 rule (wf-rule-diff.ps1, 0 OTHER lines). dice and alphabet: quote-only + 3 comment lines; i574n.github: 1 quote-only + 3 comments; polyglot: 75 indent-only (4 to 2 spaces); spiral: 6 quote-only. The classifier caught one real model bug before any write: polyglot's snok/install-poetry has 3 `with` settings the first definition lacked (reported as 3 OTHER lines; fixed, then 0 OTHER).\nModel v5 for spiral: Schedule (cron) and DispatchInputs (typed inputs: name, description, required, default) triggers; a Lines value (key: | block); guard_first on run steps for the authored `- if: always()` then `run:` shape (blog keeps run-then-if); record update for the build job's condition ({ notebooks ?| condition = ... }).\nLayout: modules pins (facts), workflow (model + renderer), org_jobs (shared pages and notebook jobs: notebook_build, push_job, the verified deploy check), workflow_check (compare/write, missing file reported), plus entries pins_check and workflows. All renderer and definition functions are :~ join points (the :=-inlined form gave 2,747 Rust lines). dist/ is gitignored, so the generated .rs is outside the 1,000-line gate (which covers tracked, unignored files); an entry split was tried, didn't shrink the units (the model dominates, at 1,249 lines for 2 workflows), and was merged back into one entry.\nRemaining 4 belong to other lanes: eoie/kit, fiscal, tibia, x. Each was offered (08:4x): main drafts the definition and sends the classification, and writes nothing until the owner says yes.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_17 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 17u32, "2026-10-26T14:15Z", "main", "10-26 10:15 main: 10 of 11 org workflows are generated from the typed source. Since n16: fiscal (byte-identical as it stood, adopted on fiscal's yes; nothing written), tibia (1 quote-only line; written unstaged with the index untouched, gh actions-lock --no-interactive 'All 1 workflow valid', pins_check 0) and x (same: 1 quote-only line, lock valid, pins 0). eoie/kit's definition is approved (4 quote-only lines) and waits for kit's 'rename finalized'; then its target key moves from the eoie folder to the new one and it gets written. Model v6 for them: run steps with an id head (id, if, run), job env blocks, the verified push taking a gate and the deploy URL's owner (kit uses ${{ github.repository_owner }}, the rest i574n), a Lines value for actions/cache paths, shared notebook_uploads (failure notebooks, always ci-metrics, ubuntu pages). Owners see their targets in a pending list, checked only with --pending (which the classifier uses on its scratch copy), so a live check or --write can't touch a repo before its owner says yes. A target whose repo isn't checked out is skipped, so i574n.github's own CI (which checks out only itself) passes. Job and run-step records are built by job_of and plain_run plus record update, so a new field is one edit.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_18 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 18u32, "2026-10-26T15:03Z", "main", "10-26 11:03 main: evidence for n15's indentation clause: polyglot's own .editorconfig sets indent_size = 2 for *.{json,ts,yml,html} (the same block as spiral, i574n.github, dice, alphabet and tibia), so the 4-space gh-pages.yml was the outlier against its repo's declared style, and normalizing it to 2 brought it into line. x, fiscal, eoie, blog and dir-tree-html have no .editorconfig.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_19 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 19u32, "2026-10-26T15:19Z", "main", "10-26 11:19 main: .editorconfig joins the typed source: 6 repos (spiral, polyglot, i574n.github, dice, alphabet, tibia) carried a byte-identical 250-byte file, which is one fact in six copies. It is now rendered by i574n.github/scripts/pins/editorconfig.spi and checked in the same `workflows` pass. Correction from x's review: the 2-space glob was [*.{json,ts,yml,html}], which told editors to indent Elixir (mix format) and Gleam with 4, wrong for x and for spiral's own apps/cells. The model's glob is now [*.{json,ts,yml,html,ex,exs,gleam}], and every adopted copy is rewritten from it. Adoption rule, written before acting: adopt only where every tracked text file already conforms (final newline; LF in the index), so the file can't make editors rewrite other files on save. My first scan read worktree bytes, which core.autocrlf rewrites on checkout, so its CRLF counts were artifacts; the index check (git ls-files --eol) is the right one. Adopted: dir-tree-html (0 to fix); blog (main added a final newline to 8 hand-written files, bytes + \"\\n\" only); fiscal (fiscal fixed scripts/init.ps1); x (x fixed 11; its 8 build.ps1 lines over 120 don't block adoption, since max_line_length is advisory and the check enforces only the file's identity). Pending: eoie/kit (12 files listed to kit; kit fixes them after the rename, then the target is keyed on the new folder name). Written 11:2x: 10 repos (the 6 rewritten with the widened glob, and dir-tree-html, blog, fiscal and x created), all unstaged; 1 distinct hash across the 10; the recheck exits 0.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_20 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 20u32, "2026-10-26T15:50Z", "main", "10-26 11:50 main: rust-toolchain.toml joins the typed source (rust_toolchain.spi): 8 repos pinned the same channel as the workflows' RustMain (nightly-2026-10-18), the same fact in 8 files. They now render from version_of RustMain, with per-repo extra lines (tibia: components clippy/rust-src/rustfmt, target wasm32-unknown-unknown, profile minimal). All 8 render byte-identical, so adopting wrote nothing: alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, polyglot and spiral directly, x and tibia on their owners' yes. Total under the check now: 10 workflows, the CI-NIX flake, 10 .editorconfig and 8 rust-toolchain.toml, 29 files, 0 differing (12:0x). A nightly bump is one edit to pins.spi followed by workflows --write, which moves the workflows' setup-rust channel, the flake's rust-bin date and every toolchain file together (x's note: its ci.yml channel and its toolchain file can no longer drift apart). .gitattributes was looked at and left alone: its 6 variants differ by real per-repo lockfile lists, and its only cross-repo gap (*.spi.ipynb linguist-generated, set only in tibia) affects nothing, because no repo tracks a notebook output.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_21 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 21u32, "2026-10-26T21:53Z", "main", "10-26 17:53 main: 10-26 17:5x main: progress was 20% while most of the item's stated goal ('CI definitions are typed and cannot drift') is working, so the record was stale. Milestones, registered now from the item's own words, before scoring: (1) one typed toolchain-pin source shared by all repos (n1's first useful output): DONE (i574n.github/scripts/pins/pins.spi). (2) Workflow files rendered from typed Spiral definitions and drift-checked: DONE for 10 of the 11 workflows. eoie's is pending its re-keyed folder name after kit's rename (along with its .editorconfig); 29 files are under the check with 0 differing. (3) The SHAPE the title names, a Spiral-to-YAML BACKEND: NOT DONE. What exists is a Spiral library (workflow.spi model + render) emitting YAML text, not a compiler backend. By gate-met-is-not-goal-met the item doesn't close on the cheaper shape. Whether a real backend is still wanted, now that the library already achieves typed, non-drifting CI, is the user's product call, and it's recorded here as the open question, not decided by main. Progress = 2 of 3 = 66%, status Active (eoie adoption follows the rename; milestone 3 awaits that call).")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_22 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 22u32, "2026-10-27T14:23Z", "main", "10-27 10:23 main: milestone 2's last step (kit's workflow, pending since the rename) is unblocked: the rename finalized 10-26 20:37 and the repo moved to C:\\home\\git\\kit at 04:30 (C:\\home\\git\\eoie is a junction). Re-keyed in i574n.github/scripts/pins (unstaged): workflows.spi's targets workflow_target \"eoie\" \"gh-pages.yml\" and repo \"eoie\" .editorconfig now name \"kit\" (the renderer kit () was already named so), and pins.ps1's $orgRepos lists 'kit' instead of 'eoie'; 29 targets in total. NOT run yet: pins.ps1 compiles pins_check and workflows to Rust and runs them over the repos, which may write rendered files into kit's tree, and kit-release-SLICES leases that tree until it finalizes. So the check runs after SLICES ends (with kit's yes before adopting any differing file, as x and tibia gave theirs). Milestone 2 is done once kit's workflow and .editorconfig render byte-identical, or are adopted. Milestone 3 (a real compiler backend instead of the library) stays the user's open product call.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_23 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 23u32, "2026-10-30T23:42Z", "main", "10-30 19:4x main R3 groom (BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030): next step, owner main: kit-release-SLICES's tree lease is over (kit window list empty 19:36), so run scripts/pins/pins.ps1 over the 29 targets; any rendered file that differs in kit's tree is shown to kit for a yes before it is adopted (as x and tibia did). Milestone 3 (a real backend) stays the user's product call.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_24 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 24u32, "2026-10-30T23:49Z", "main", "10-30 19:49 main: milestone 2 check RUN (pins.ps1 after kit's SLICES lease ended; read-only, nothing written): pins_check 0 pin lines disagree; workflows 0 of the 29 adopted targets differ. Pending kit pair (workflows.exe --pending, rendered into a scratch root %TEMP%\\pins-render): kit/.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml differs only in YAML quoting (CARGO_BUILD_JOBS '2' -> \\\"2\\\", RUST_TEST_THREADS '1' -> \\\"1\\\", rebar3-version '3.28.0' -> \\\"3.28.0\\\", zig version 0.17.0 -> \\\"0.17.0\\\"; same values), and kit/.editorconfig is missing (the org editorconfig would be added). Asked kit for its yes; on yes, main runs workflows.exe C:\\home\\git --pending --write and moves both kit targets from 'pending' into 'targets' in workflows.spi, which closes milestone 2.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_25 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 25u32, "2026-10-31T00:04Z", "main", "10-30 20:04 main: MILESTONE 2 DONE. kit said yes (both checked: quoting-only diff, no new uses:, .editorconfig byte-identical to alphabet's). workflows.exe C:\\home\\git --pending --write rewrote kit/.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml and created kit/.editorconfig (unstaged in kit's tree). workflows.spi moved both kit targets from pending into the adopted targets (pending = []). The pins.ps1 recheck after recompiling: pins_check 0 pin lines disagree, workflows 0 workflow files differ (31 adopted targets), exit 0. Left: milestone 3 (a real Spiral-to-YAML compiler backend instead of the library), which is the user's product call; tag user-batch.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_26 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 26u32, "2026-11-01T03:40Z", "main", "main 23:4x WIP groom (user 23:3x: drift/stall, too much waiting): Active -> Planned. milestone 2 done (n25); next milestone: the next repo's workflows through workflows.exe --pending.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_27 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 27u32, "2026-11-01T04:35Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:35 compiler, round-2 groom: LAB HISTORY (main 00:2x): spiral/lab no longer exists. lab/SPIRAL-TO-YAML, lab/HOPACSOTA-P2 and lab/COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB were archived 10-31 08:06 (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261031-080640 and -080801; restore with scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore <manifest> -Only '<name>\\*'). HOME for this item's pins_check.spi: a generic tool, so it becomes a kit command (memory: generic tools as kit commands in Spiral), not an i574n.github script. HOPACSOTA-P2 and COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB are Done; their live tools (alias-count-check.ps1, alternating-ab.ps1) already sit in spiral/apps/compiler/packs, so the archive is history only.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_28 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 28u32, "2026-11-05T21:30Z", "main", "main 11-05 17:3x NEXT REPOS ADOPTED (unstaged, i574n.github/scripts/pins): html, regex and dir-tree-html now come from the typed source. One helper, dotnet_pages (tools) (keep_going) (retention), covers their shape (checkout, setup-rust, optional beam, setup-dotnet, workflow.ps1, an upload gated on 'contains(matrix.os, ubuntu)', pages_push_job None, push+PR triggers, no top-level permissions); html = [beam] no fail-fast 30 d, regex = [beam] fail-fast false 90 d, dir-tree-html = no beam, 30 d. All three rendered BYTE-IDENTICAL on the first --pending run, then promoted to adopted targets; no YAML changed. pins.ps1 also pin-checks dir-tree-html now. GATE: full pins.ps1 exit 0 in 487 s (pins_check clean over 12 repos; workflows: 0 of 24 files differ). Generated set: 12 workflows + editorconfigs/toolchains/flake. Still hand-written: vault, dotfiles, i574n.github.io (one peaceiris-only workflow each). Milestone 3 (a real Spiral-to-YAML backend) stays the user's call. 85 -> 90.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_29 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 29u32, "2026-11-05T22:08Z", "main", "main 11-05 18:0x CORRECTION to n28: dir-tree-html is fc1943s/dir-tree-html since 11-03 (not an own repo), so I removed it from the generator targets and from pins.ps1's orgRepos again; html and regex stay adopted (both i574n). Generated set: 11 workflows + editorconfigs/toolchains/flake. pins.ps1 rerun pending.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_30 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 30u32, "2026-11-06T12:46Z", "main", "main 11-06 10:5x DoD backfill (kit lint): DoD: every org workflow YAML is generated from scripts/pins/workflows.spi and pins.ps1 reports 0 drift across the adopted repos. Next step: rerun pins.ps1 on the 11 adopted repos after the user's pushes and close on 0 drift.")
inl note_of_spiral_to_yaml_31 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TO-YAML", 31u32, "2026-11-06T15:06Z", "main", "main 11-06 15:0x DONE, proof: scripts/pins/pins.ps1 (exit 0) regenerates every org workflow from scripts/pins/workflows.spi and compares: 11 of 11 gh-pages.yml identical to the typed source ('0 workflow files differ'), and every .editorconfig and rust-toolchain.toml identical too. That covers spiral, kit, x, fiscal, regex, html, dice, alphabet, blog, tibia and i574n.github after the user's 11-06 pushes. dotfiles and i574n.github.io aren't generator targets (outside orgRepos; their pins are hand-kept per CI-ACTIONS-LATEST).")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night evidence (candidate --x-compat autolinks = member access X would link): lib/spiral 272 (.map 154, .run 22, .to 22, .box 16), apps/tibia 155 (.at 28, .ms 25, .target 18), blog 5, x client 0 after hand renames. Renaming cannot scale, so postable edition-3 sources need a separator change.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:45Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:45 helper-lane lap 1: measured the '::' conflict the title asks about: GADT case signatures (| Case :: ...) occur on 4,412 lines in 467 .spi/.spir files across the repos (eoie state alone: core 47, coverage 32, pipeline 24, differential 18; tibia 23; dice 2), so '::' as member access would only be separable by spacing (a::b vs Case :: t), a whitespace-sensitive lexer rule that the formatter and every reader must keep. Also sharpened the problem: X only autolinks when the field name after the dot is a real TLD (the 10-17 census hits are .map, .run, .to, .box, .at, .ms - all delegated TLDs), so members whose name is not a TLD (p.author) are safe while TLD names (.post is a sponsored TLD, hence posts.post) link; the separator change matters only for TLD-named members.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:30Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:30 helper-lane lap 2: sized the rename-only alternative against IANA's current TLD list (1,437 delegated TLDs, data.iana.org): across lib/spiral .spi, dotted member names that are TLDs number 17 distinct names in about 397 uses (regex count, macro text included): map 150, box 94, show 83, to 20, run 15, org 13, now 5, data 3, storage 3, stream 2, pin 2, day 2, open/do/city/select/zip 1 each. The top three (map, box, show) are 82 percent of all autolink-prone uses in lib, so renaming three functions (e.g. map -> each / map_to, box -> boxed, show -> shown) would remove most of lib's X links without a separator change; tibia's .at .ms .target and the long tail would still need the separator or per-repo renames. Input for the edition-3 decision; LIB-CANON-NAMES' renames could absorb the three.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:13Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:13 helper-lane lap 3: extended the rename-only sizing from lib to every repo with a reusable counter (lab/SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX/tld_members.py + tlds.txt, IANA list fetched today: 1,437 TLDs; regex over .spi, so file names inside strings such as eoie's .rs/.md also count). TLD-named members after a dot, distinct/uses/top-3 share: lib/spiral 16/381/81% (map 135, box 94, show 83); tibia/src 16/156/48% (at 34, ms 26, health 15, gold 13, band 13); eoie/src 19/257/55% (rs 58, zip 54, report 31, target 30); dice 10/195/69% (map 56, box 41, th/td 38 each: html tables); x 3/33 (id 31); blog/engine 3/7; apps/cells/spi 1/5 (one). So renaming three lib functions fixes lib and most of dice, but tibia and eoie spread over domain words (at, ms, health, report, target, id), and x's .id is its own record field; a rename-only route would mean per-repo vocabulary bans, which favours a separator change for edition 3. Input for the edition-3 lane's decision; LIB-CANON-NAMES carries the lib part.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:45Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:45 helper-lane lap 4: sized the other side of the '::' separator option: tight a::b already occurs 5,137 times in 437 .spi files under C:\\home\\git, but nearly all of it is Rust path text inside macros (std::fs::..., std::env::... in eoie, x, dice, lib), which the Spiral lexer never reads as Spiral tokens, while every Spiral use of '::' is the spaced GADT signature (Case :: type, 4,412 lines per lap 1). So '::' member access is lexically separable today by the rule tight = member access, spaced = GADT signature, with macro bodies unaffected; the cost is readability, since Spiral member paths would look like the Rust paths in the macros next to them, and the formatter must never insert spaces around a member '::'. X does not autolink a::b (no dot), which is the goal. Input for the edition-3 lane: '::' works mechanically; a non-Rust-looking separator avoids the visual collision.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 6u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): measurements handed over; waits for the edition-3 separator choice -> next: edition-3 decision | lap 6 18:26 (subagent): re-read: sizing complete (TLD members, '::' separability); the title assigns the call to the edition-3 syntax lane -> next: edition-3 lane decision | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: edition-3 lane decision -> next: edition-3 lane")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 7u32, "2026-10-26T05:09Z", "x", "2026-10-26 01:07 x: DECISION RULE ACCESSOR-1 (user 10-26 ~01:0x: prioritize a compiler accessor change so posting needs no dot defusing; then a full migration round), written before any candidate is measured. Candidates: tight '::' (st::head), tight ':' (st:head), '..' (st..head), U+00B7 middle dot (st·head), '#', '@', '$', prime (st'head). Hard gates, each must pass or the candidate is out: G1 X-safe: no profile entity (x.json domain_pattern with tlds.txt, mention, hashtag, cashtag) matches '<lhs><sep><tld-named member>' for lhs in {ident, sm', ), ], }} at line start, after a space and after '('; also no Mastodon custom-emoji ':name:' or Bluesky link facet; G2 copy-paste: a posted cell pasted back compiles with no stripping (no zero-width chars); G3 ASCII, typed without IME; G4 no meaning change for any token the current tokenizer accepts in .spi/.spir outside string literals and macro bodies across x, spiral, eoie, tibia, dice, alphabet, fiscal, polyglot, blog (count of conflicting sites = 0, or every conflict is a currently-rejected token sequence). Score among survivors, lowest wins: S = 2 * (weight per access, X code_point_weight) + 3 * (whitespace-sensitive lexer rules added) + 1 if it collides visually with a token Spiral already has. Tie-break: Rust/ML-family precedent for member access, then fewer characters. Deadline: decided by 2026-10-26 03:00 local; if no candidate passes all gates, the fallback is keep '.' + lexer treats U+200B as whitespace + poster defuses TLD dots only. Outcome goes to the compiler lane as one spec (tokenizer change + migration tool + transition window), not a menu.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 8u32, "2026-10-26T05:29Z", "x", "2026-10-26 01:28 x: ACCESSOR-1 OUTCOME (rule = note 7, applied as written; scripts %TEMP%\\x-simland-0214\\accessor_census.ps1 + accessor_sites.ps1). G1 X-safety against x.json (domain_pattern + tlds.txt, mention, hashtag, cashtag) plus Mastodon ':name:' emoji and the atproto link regex, 6 lhs (st, sm', f x), xs], { a }, x1) x 8 members (map post io to co ab head id) x 5 contexts: raw dot 60 links; '#', '@', '$' 160 each (hashtag/mention/cashtag after sm' or ')'): OUT. Defused dot 0 links but U+200B: OUT (G2/G3; probe: 'st<U+200B>.head' TokenizerErrors, 'st.<U+200B>head' ParserErrors, plain exit 0). U+00B7: OUT (G3). Prime: OUT (G4: ' is a var char, Tokenize.fs:149, 462 sites where x'y is one identifier). Survivors '::', ':', '..' all 0 links incl. chains. G4 over 9 repos with strings/macros/comments stripped: 9 / 6 / 19 raw sites, all inside macro or host text on inspection (Zig 'break :blk', Tailwind ']]:block', Rust '\\ ::new' and '0..n', an F# slice in a tmp sample, plus 3 line-offset artifacts) -> 0 real conflicts for each; note the tokenizer already rejects ':' right after a var (Tokenize.fs:175, tokens.spi:392), so tight a:b and a::b are errors today. Score S = 2*weight + 3*(in-use token that gains a spacing-dependent meaning) + 1 (visual collision with a Spiral token): '..' = 4 + 0 + 0 = 4; ':' = 2 + 3 (x : t annotation vs x :t symbol) + 1 (annotation; st:head reads as Rust/TS 'st: head') = 6; '::' = 4 + 3 (x :: xs cons vs x ::xs) + 1 (cons/GADT) = 8. WINNER '..': st..head, sm'..replace, (f x)..head, ..(+). Reading of the ws term: the pre-measurement framing (my 00:5x answer and note 2 of 10-19: 'a rule the formatter and every reader must keep') penalises an in-use token whose meaning splits by spacing; a pure rule count (each candidate replaces today's '.x' rule one for one) would give ':' 3, '..' 4, '::' 5, so the margin rests on that reading, recorded here rather than re-decided. Precedent: Dart cascades (a..b is member access). Cost: weight 2 per access, equal to today's defused dot, with no zero-width char, and the splitter's own defuse rule never fires on '..x' (no label char before the second dot). Corpus to migrate: 32,995 dot symbols outside strings/macros in 9 repos (x 2,233). Spec sent to the compiler team as one candidate (see the 01:3x message): lexer pair in all three tokenizer copies, respell pair, edition-3 finding, migration in the same round as EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 9u32, "2026-10-26T05:36Z", "x", "2026-10-26 01:36 x: DECISION RULE ACCESSOR-2 (user 10-26 ~01:4x: 'i don't know if i like .., experiment with :: ... without conflicting with list cons ... the rust similarity might pay off'), written before measuring; it supersedes ACCESSOR-1's pick and is a feasibility gate, not a re-score. Proposed lexing: at a token start, '::' immediately followed by a var-start char or '(' is a symbol (same TokSymbol as '.x' / '.(op)'), and the var lexer's ': right after a var' error gets one exception for exactly that '::' shape (st::head lexes as st + symbol). Everything else about ':' and '::' stays: spaced ' :: ' is cons/GADT, '(::)' an operator section, 'x: t' still an error. Tight '::' is ADOPTED if, on the 9-repo corpus with every '.x' symbol respelled to '::x' by the model lexer: M1 0 sites where an existing cons/GADT '::' (as the current lexer reads it, strings/macros/comments excluded) lexes as a symbol after migration; M2 0 respelled symbols that fuse with a preceding operator char (would lex as one operator); M3 0 respelled symbols that lex as cons; M4 the X/Mastodon/Bluesky gate of ACCESSOR-1 G1 stays at 0 links on chains ('a::b::c', '(f x)::m', '::m' alone). Any M1-M3 site that only a formatter respace fixes counts as resolved if the formatter already emits ' :: ' spaced for cons. If one gate fails and a lexer-only tweak closes it, allow ONE tweak then re-measure once; if it still fails, '..' (ACCESSOR-1) stands and the reasons go to the user.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 10u32, "2026-10-26T06:01Z", "x", "2026-10-26 02:01 x: ACCESSOR-2 OUTCOME: tight '::' ADOPTED, no tweak needed; supersedes ACCESSOR-1's '..'. Model lexer in C# (%TEMP%\\x-simland-0214\\dcolon_model_fast.ps1; strings/macros/comments masked with offsets kept) over 2,520 .spi/.spir in 9 repos. Rule: at a token start, '::' immediately followed by a var-start char, or by '(' + operator chars + ')', is a symbol, whatever is on its left; the var lexer's ': right after a var' error gets one exception for that exact shape (st::head = st + symbol). Every '.x' symbol respelled to '::x' (32,870) and re-lexed: M1 existing cons/GADT '::' tokens (9,793) that would lex as a symbol: 6, all macro/host text on inspection (Rust '\\ ::new' / '\\ ::Regex' / '\\ ::Stdio' in eoie host macros, an F# ')::acc' in a tmp sample's macro), so 0 real; a further 4,048 '::' have a name char on the left, which today's lexer rejects, so they are Rust paths in macros my masking missed, not Spiral; M2 respelled symbols fused into a preceding operator: 0; M3 respelled symbols not lexing as a symbol: 0; M4 X/Mastodon/Bluesky: 0 links on 10 standalone and chain cases ('f ::map', '(::io)', '::post' at line start, 'st::head::map::to', '(get_args () ::gleam |> fst)::map', 'g ::(+) 1', sm'::replace) plus ACCESSOR-1's grid. Variant measured and REJECTED: firing only when '::' is also tight on the left (the compiler team's first sketch) leaves 120 standalone symbols lexing as cons (e.g. '(get_args () ::gleam |> fst)', 'vec 1i32 2i32 3i32 ::z'), so the spec says right-tight, any left side. What stays: spaced ' :: ' is cons/GADT; '(::)' is an operator section; 'x ::xs' (tight right only) becomes a symbol, and no Spiral source has it today (M1). Cost: weight 2 per access (= today's defused dot) and no zero-width chars. Spec sent to the compiler team 02:0x.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 11u32, "2026-10-26T06:32Z", "x", "2026-10-26 02:32 x: X-COMPLIANCE COMPROMISE (user 10-26 ~02:1x: 'would we lose such concise modeling [get_args () .gleam, vec 1i32 2i32 3i32 .z]? try arriving at a sota compromise considering every code we have and how it would conflict with x, we need all repos x compliant even if that requires migration'). One-liners are KEPT: the adopted right-tight rule makes '(get_args () ::gleam |> fst)' and 'vec 1i32 2i32 3i32 ::z' the same standalone symbol argument they are today, same token count; only the both-sides-tight variant (rejected, note 10) broke them. Precedent: GHC proposal 229 (whitespace-sensitive operators, GHC 9.0, https://github.com/ghc-proposals/ghc-proposals/blob/master/proposals/0229-whitespace-bang-patterns.rst) classifies an operator by its spacing as prefix / suffix / tight infix / loose infix; here prefix and tight infix '::name' are the symbol, loose infix ' :: ' is cons/GADT, suffix 'x:: ' stays an error. Full census of X entities by lexical context over 10 repos (profile semantics incl. the two-letter-TLD rule; %TEMP%\\x-simland-0214\\x_entities_by_context.ps1): links code 1,189 / string 314 / macro 69 / comment 141; mentions 28 / 146 / 50 / 0 (Gleam '@external(erlang,' strings, Zig '\\@intCast' macro text); hashtags 5 / 61 / 25 / 0 ('#include', '#if', '\\#app_'); cashtags 19 / 42 / 9 / 0 (PowerShell '$env:', JSON '$ref') ('code' still holds some multi-line macro text). Three layers, all repos compliant: L1 '::' accessor: Spiral's own syntax, 32,870 symbols, no defusing. L2 macro-text escapes for literal sigils doubled: '@@' and '##' replace '\\@' and '\\#' (83 entity-shaped sites); measured X-safe ('@@intCast', '##include', '$$env' none match; '\\@intCast' is a mention today); '$$' as a macro escape for '$' changes only 2 existing '$$' sequences. L3 U+200B-transparent source: the lexer drops U+200B everywhere, strings and macro text included (0 raw U+200B in any .spi/.spir today, so no program changes), and the poster defuses only real entities (link/mention/hashtag/cashtag) left in foreign text: about 700 sites in 13M chars instead of today's 39,175 dot defusals, and a pasted post compiles byte-identically. Foreign syntax (C '#include', Gleam '@external', file names 'main.rs') is never contorted; lib helpers for repeated boilerplate such as Gleam '@external(erlang, ...)' ride BANG-MACRO-CLEANUP as an optional shrink. Items: SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX (L1), SPIRAL-MACRO-SIGIL-ESCAPES (L2), SPIRAL-ZWSP-TRANSPARENT (L3), X-DEFUSE-LINKS-ONLY (poster side of L3).")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 12u32, "2026-10-28T10:58Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 DONE: tight :: member access is in the installed core 9FF1B708 -- spiral_compiler.fs:43095 (symbol lexer :: branch) + :180678 (respell . <-> ::). Composed into 5n via pack-next5n/lexer-dcolon-apply.ps1 + 5n built and blessed (review-5n.ps1). Functional re-run delegated to x/fiscal/tibia against the installed compiler (authoritative real usage); reopen on a red row.")
inl note_of_spiral_path_syntax_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX", 13u32, "2026-10-28T11:21Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 tiny follow-up (fiscal hit it): the format --spelling symbols|words flag (the . <-> :: respell, spiral_compiler.fs:183961-183964) is missing from the printed format usage text (which lists only format / --stdin / format-corpus). Add a --spelling line to the format usage string so lanes discover it without reading source. 1-line doc fix; fold into the next compiler pack I land (e.g. the P3 lever pack).")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_1 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:15Z", "main-thread", "23:15 main: evidence 10-15: the gate stopped the strict renewal when agile_todo_scan reached 1,184 (922 .rs + 167 .spi + 95 notebook .spi) and forced a clean split into sources/scan crates; the largest crate after the split is command_spec_domain at 998, so it must be split before the next agile verb.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_2 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 2u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night inventory: gated: eoie (crate max 949-998), x (report-only, view.rs 1,885 over); ungated monoliths: apps/spiral spiral.rs 24,281 lines, dice ui main.rs 16,878, dice lib dice.rs 6,639, apps/wasm spiral_wasm.rs 3,464.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_3 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 3u32, "2026-10-18T05:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 01:53 x: all 12 generated crates under 1,000 after the view split (view 999, digest 989, jsonl 929, args 889, xc 862); the margin on view is 1 line, so the gate stays report-only until RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION or a further split gives room")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_4 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:45Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:45 helper-lane lap 1: re-inventory 10-19 (lines of the generated .rs): ungated monoliths apps/spiral spiral.rs 23,418 (24,281 on 10-17), dice ui src/main.rs 16,878 (same), dice lib dice.rs 7,777 (6,639 on 10-17, grew 17 percent), apps/wasm spiral_wasm.rs 3,283, dice contract dice_contract.rs 3,396 and polyglot chat_contract.rs 1,374 (NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES); x's largest crates are now digest 991, view 991, args 960, so view gained 8 lines of margin since 10-18 but the report-only mode should stay until there are ~5 percent of headroom. The gate (eoie source-topology) still exists only in eoie; dice lib growing past 7.7k is the strongest case for adding report-only counts to dice's build first (it mirrors to alphabet).")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_5 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:31Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:31 helper-lane lap 2: wrote the report-only step as lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/crate-report.ps1 (a generated crate = an .rs with a sibling .spi; prints those over the limit, never fails) and ran it on dice, alphabet, spiral/apps and polyglot/apps: 448 generated crates, 12 over 1,000 lines - spiral.rs 23,418, alphabet apps/documents documents.rs 15,574 (missing from the 10-17 inventory), dice lib dice.rs 7,777, dice contract 3,396, spiral_wasm.rs 3,283, chat_contract 1,374, and 6 compiler samples' main.rs (mega roots and typecheck_long_let_body, which are matrix fixtures and should be excluded from any gate). dice/ui/src/main.rs (16,878) is not caught because its source .spi is not beside it, so the rule needs the [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output convention (x's) to find every generated crate. Call it in-process (& script -Root a, b): pwsh -File collapses the -Root array.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_6 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:13Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:13 helper-lane lap 3: switched the report to the planned detection (lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/crate-report.ps1, still report-only): generated crates = outputs declared by a Cargo.toml [package.metadata.spiral] output = path, plus the sibling rule (an .rs beside its .spi); apps/compiler/tmp/samples (matrix fixtures) and target/deps/dist are excluded. Run over dice, alphabet, spiral/apps, polyglot/apps, x and blog: 26 generated crates (17 declared by metadata, all of them x's, every one under 1,000 lines), 6 over the limit, all sibling-rule: spiral.rs 23,418, alphabet documents.rs 15,574, dice lib dice.rs 7,777, dice contract 3,396, spiral_wasm.rs 3,283, chat_contract 1,374. dice/ui/src/main.rs (16,878) is still invisible because neither rule reaches it; giving dice ui's Cargo.toml the x-style [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output is the one-line fix that makes it count. Next: propose the step as non-failing in dice and alphabet build.ps1 (mirror rule) after their CI is green.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_7 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:38 helper-lane lap 4: staged the one-line fix that makes dice ui visible to the gate: lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/dice_ui_metadata.py adds [package.metadata.spiral] entry = src/dice_ui.spi, output = src/main.rs to dice/ui/Cargo.toml (anchor --check OK; values read from ui/build.ps1, which compiles src/dice_ui.spi to src/main.rs). Cargo ignores package.metadata, so builds are unaffected; with it the report finds main.rs (16,878 lines) as the seventh over-limit crate. The report step for dice and alphabet build.ps1 waits for their CI to be green (mirror rule; alphabet has no ui crate, its counterpart is apps/documents).")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_8 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 8u32, "2026-10-19T21:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:40 helper-lane lap 6: lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/dice_ui_metadata.py dry run matches dice/ui/Cargo.toml today. Per the dice/alphabet mirror rule it gets applied to both repos at once.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_9 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 9u32, "2026-10-21T22:50Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:50 x: reference implementation for other repos: x/scripts/gate.ps1 reads [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output from every apps/**/Cargo.toml, counts the generated output's lines and, with -Enforce, throws on any crate over -Limit (1000) and on finding no crate at all (vacuity). x's apps/client/build.ps1 now runs it with -Enforce right after compiling the crates (until today it only reported). For dice/alphabet: once dice_ui_metadata.py adds the metadata, the same script with -Root works unchanged. On .spi in the count: x counts only generated .rs, which is what the 1k limit protects.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_10 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 10u32, "2026-10-21T23:16Z", "eoie-lane", "19:16 eoie team, evidence 10-21 (generated .rs lines from previews on trees rebuilt from live): the gate fired four times on tonight's drafts, each before any release: analysis.rs 1108 (r34 where filters inlined through the eoie_table_query package), watch.rs 1208 (r35 job-state code inlined through eoie_jobs), read_query.rs 1007 (r35 grep root + tail), and gate.rs reached exactly 1000 (r35 window holder). Fixes were crate boundaries, never trimming: a row-mask export (eoie_table_rows_kept, analysis.rs 965, table_test.rs 736), a job-state export (eoie_job_state_word, watch.rs 954), a crate split (eoie_grep_query; read_query.rs 993 -> 632), and computing range bounds once. Pattern: a Spiral package import inlines the used functions into every consumer, so a shared helper growing in one package pushes all its importers toward the gate at once; an export at the crate boundary is the structural fix. Margins after r34/r35/r36: gate.rs 1000 (0), do_program.rs 988, do_edit.rs 976, analysis.rs 965, command_spec.rs 962, watch.rs 954, experiments.rs 949. Proposal for the gate: report the 10 crates nearest 1000 in every preview so a slice sees its margin before it lands.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_11 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 11u32, "2026-10-23T10:58Z", "main", "2026-10-23 06:58 main: dice and alphabet now carry the crate metadata x's gate reads (unstaged). dice ui/Cargo.toml gets [package.metadata.spiral] entry src/dice_ui.spi, output src/main.rs (lab dice_ui_metadata.py, applied). alphabet apps/documents/Cargo.toml gets the mirror, entry documents.spi, output documents.rs. Measured: both are far over the limit today. dice ui main.rs is 16,281 generated lines (dist copy 16,878), dice contract is the 178 KB dice_contract.rs (NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES), alphabet documents.rs is 15,315. So the gate cannot be enforced in these repos until their output is split into module crates (GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES, NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES), and until then it runs report-only. Two gaps in reusing x/scripts/gate.ps1 as is: it scans only apps/**/Cargo.toml (dice keeps its crates in ui/ and contract/, so it would find none and fail its vacuity check), and the same counting already lives in eoie source-topology. Per eoie-first, the cross-repo gate should be one eoie verb ('eoie crate gate <root> [--enforce] [--limit 1000]' over every Cargo.toml with [package.metadata.spiral]), not a fourth copy of the script; filing that request for the eoie team.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_12 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 12u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): dice ui metadata patch staged; dice CI red -> next: after green: apply + report step | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: dice_ui_metadata.py dry run clean -> next: apply with the alphabet mirror")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_13 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 13u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 75m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 40%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_14 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 14u32, "2026-10-25T00:59Z", "eoie", "20:59 From x's build research (finding 7): generated functions grow superlinearly in LLVM and MIR dataflow. The crate gate already counts lines per crate, so add a per-crate report of the largest function (name, lines) to show where to cut. Done when eoie crate gate prints largest_fn=<name>:<lines> per crate and a notebook case checks it on a fixture crate.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_15 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 15u32, "2026-10-25T11:23Z", "eoie", "07:23 eoie: the largest_fn milestone (note 2) is built: each crate gate row ends with largest_fn=<name>:<lines> (the first on a tie; generics end the name). model.spi has largest_function_of (a pure line scan for fn and pub fn) plus largest_text, the counters are folded into one verdict_count, and every line is now at or under 120 (the legacy long lines were migrated in the same change). The gate.spi walker reads each output once for both counts. Spec case 43's expected rows carry the new column. The model notebook passes, including the_largest_function_names_where_to_cut; regen plus cargo check of eoie_crate_gate on 5k is running. Motivation from today: the eoie_cli (direct_verb_usage 136 lines) and agile_task_listing (list_request_from 143) cuts were both found by hand profiling that this column now prints. Left: the per-repo gate in every repo that emits Rust crates, and the decision on .spi in the count.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_16 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 16u32, "2026-10-25T11:27Z", "eoie", "07:27 eoie: correction to note 15. The model notebook passed and cargo check was green, but on 5k the regen put eoie_crate_gate at 1092 lines (926 published): function_name 73 + largest_from 50 + largest_function_of 23 + helpers. That breaks the gate the feature exists to serve, and the package is HostFree, so a sibling-crate call is not allowed either. Reverted under the 950 rule (gate.spi and model.spi match the before-s-stages snapshot by hash; spec case 43 rows restored). Draft kept at scratch clean\\gate-largest\\ (model with largest_function_of, largest_text, verdict_count and the migrated long lines; gate.spi reading each output once). Next step, ordered: split eoie_crate_gate by hand first (manifest scan and walk in one crate, measure and verdict rows in another, or a Spiral-only model package shared by two crates), then re-add largest_fn into the half with room, and measure. A cheaper scan (split on newline-fn instead of per-line substring and index_of) is the alternative to try first if the split costs more.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_17 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 17u32, "2026-10-25T11:40Z", "eoie", "07:40 eoie: cheaper-scan arm measured (one split on newline-fn plus a chunk loop using the existing line_count): eoie_crate_gate 1011 on 5k (versus 1092 for the per-line scan, 926 published before 5j). The model notebook passes and cargo check is green, but the crate is over 950, so it is reverted under the same rule (both files match the snapshot by hash; draft kept as scratch clean\\gate-largest\\model-cheap-scan.spi). Conclusion: largest_fn does not fit inside eoie_crate_gate at its current size, so the split comes first, and the full 5l' measure will show the gate's own baseline on the new compiler. Split plan: a Spiral-only eoie_crate_gate_model package (manifest parse, verdicts, rows, largest_fn), plus the walker crate and a measure crate exporting row text per output file, so the walker never holds the row code.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_18 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 18u32, "2026-10-26T16:22Z", "eoie", "12:22 eoie: same root cause as GRANULAR-LINT's overrun (notes 14-16 there), which changes the plan. The compiler lane traced the size (RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE, P3): lib string wrappers (sm'.trim, sm'.substring and the like) are := and partial-evaluated at every call site, while the slice primitive itself is already one shared fn. The fix in kit's hands: route a crate's frequent string calls through local :~ wrappers so each is emitted once as a method. Do not :~ tiny helpers (measured: 11 small ones grew lint.rs from 2,056 to 2,103), and check the output has fn definitions, not Rc::new(move |..|) closures. Before the split planned at 07:40, try that on the largest_fn draft: wrappers for the scan's substring/index_of/trim calls, regen kit_crate_gate, compare against 950. The split stays the fallback. The lint measurement (job kit-lint-wrappers) runs the same method first, and its result decides whether to repeat it here.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_19 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 19u32, "2026-10-27T15:58Z", "kit", "11:5x kit: largest_fn built and proven on a probe copy, staged (clean/kit-rename/crate-gate-largest plus crate-largest-member.do; next-train steps 4b and 5a). The 10-25 plan's split, done the way tonight's lint split worked: the scan (function_name, largest_from, largest_function_of; edition 3, ported from the clean/gate-largest draft) lives in a new crate, kit_crate_largest (largest.rs 255 lines), with a test block. It exports kit_crate_largest(text) -> ' largest <fn> <lines>' or '', so the gate never holds the scan or the number formatting. kit_crate_gate reads each output once, stores the rendered suffix in crate_measure.largest, and measure_line appends it with no branch. Its existing test strings are unchanged with largest='', and a new case checks the named suffix. gate.rs 944 (was 921; under the crate's 950 rule; the first cut was 964, then 954 with sm'.format in the gate). Behavior: the generated crate, run on three real outputs, names the same largest function and length as an independent PowerShell ranking: host_run.rs supervisor_requested_48 79, gate.rs gate_run_0 97, task_listing.rs row_selected_15 66. Left: the gate in every repo that emits Rust crates, and the .spi-in-count decision (item text).")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_20 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 20u32, "2026-10-27T19:23Z", "kit", "kit 15:2x: every repo that emits Spiral crates is gated, in one call: i574n.github/scripts/crate-gate-all.ps1 (unstaged) runs 'kit crate gate <root> --enforce --limit 1000' over kit/src, x, spiral, tibia and fiscal, and treats the gate's own 'no crate with [package.metadata.spiral]' refusal as 'nothing to gate'. Today: kit/src 235 crates and x 28, 0 over; spiral, tibia and fiscal emit no Spiral crates. The .spi question is settled by the user's 10-21 rule (line-gate-all-languages: Spiral sources are exempt; the gate counts generated output only). Left: the largest-function suffix (staged, next-train 4b/5a) ships with TRAIN; after install the gate names each crate's largest fn and this item closes.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_21 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 21u32, "2026-10-28T09:34Z", "eoie", "05:3x kit: verified on the installed TRAIN2c binary (A5B55974): 'kit crate gate kit/src --limit 1000' reports crates=242 over=0, and every row carries its largest function (for example 'largest agile_init_entry_0 149'). With crate-gate-all.ps1 gating every Spiral-crate repo, this item is done.")
inl note_of_kit_topology_crates_1 () = Note ("KIT-TOPOLOGY-CRATES", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:35Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-TOPOLOGY-CRATES (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
