inl note_of_lab_in_repo_1 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 1u32, "2026-10-17T14:47Z", "main-thread", "10:47 main-thread fork: first main-lane tools moved into the repo: (1) apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 = the heavy gate + run ledger, comment-free and split into named functions (Wait-HeavySlot, Get-HeavyTicketPrefix, Wait-WslReady, Stop-IdleWsl); the FIFO queue moved from the job tmp to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\heavy-queue (machine state, outlives jobs); exclusion still comes from the machine-global mutexes, so the job-tmp copy and this one exclude each other (smoke: repo copy took slot 2 while the parent held slot 1; both ledger rows written); until every lane sources the repo copy, FIFO order between the two queue dirs is not shared. (2) apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/compare-runs.ps1 = cell-by-cell diff of two harness runs (run name or dir), DISAGREE count; verified on bless 081502 vs reuse run 084050 (0 cells). Next: lanes switch to . apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1, then the Kino suite runner and the pack scripts (spiral-bin\\packs) get repo homes.")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_2 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 2u32, "2026-10-17T15:01Z", "main-thread", "11:01 main-thread fork: (3) apps/kino/spi/run_suite.ps1 = the Kino suite runner from the job-tmp lane-b script merged with the targeted-tests runner (-Label, -Tests a,b, -Dll, -Port, -TimeoutMin): repo-relative paths, MIX_BUILD_PATH and logs under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin (kino-builds/<label>, kino-suite/<label>.log), retires its daemon by TCP afterwards, and defers while a live Tibia client/server process runs (the old lane-specific arena flag file is gone). Smoke through the repo heavy gate: cell_test 12 tests, 0 failures. Moved so far: heavy.ps1, compare-runs.ps1, run_suite.ps1; still in job tmp: the pack scripts (spiral-bin\\packs), after-bless / determinism scripts, kino-tests.ps1 (superseded by run_suite -Tests).")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_3 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 3u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (lane paused): lab convention live in spiral/lab (7 item dirs, runner lab/try.ps1); main-lane tools moved: apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 + compare-runs.ps1, apps/kino/spi/run_suite.ps1. Next: lanes source the repo heavy.ps1 (shared FIFO queue), move the pack scripts, per-repo lab/ elsewhere.")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_4 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 4u32, "2026-10-17T15:30Z", "main-thread", "11:30 main-thread: reconciled with the study lane. Canonical tools are apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 (gate + run ledger), scripts/compare-runs.ps1 and apps/kino/spi/run_suite.ps1, and the job-tmp heavy.ps1 is a one-line forwarder to scripts/heavy.ps1. I removed my duplicates from lab/harness-run-only-useful; it keeps only the experiment probes (rebuild/cross-folder determinism, bindiff.py, fsharp-resources, kino-tests). Patches live in lab/zig-hub-speed and lab/gadt-growing-index-hang.")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_5 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:29Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:29 lap 1 grain: today's moves out of job tmp into spiral/lab: HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH (9 R3/R4 scripts), WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB (toolchain.spi), SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS (ebpf_family.spi), UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC (upstream_let_rec_annotation draft), LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS (CIC kernel + neg package); each rewritten LF with repo-relative packageDir; job tmp outputs (ipynb, html, logs) stay behind Next: count what job tmp still holds that a backlog item cites, and move the next batch")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_6 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:03Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:03 lap 2 grain: backlog notes cite 47 distinct job-tmp paths; the most cited, sweep/ (intent-report.txt 8x, intents.py), serves eoie-lane items (EOIE-ZED-SWEEP and others) and holds a copy of the opencode database, so it must never be moved into a git-tracked lab (credential tables, possible provider keys); rotation/* (CORE-PAIR-UNIFICATION 7 notes) are applied one-shot patches, which the lab lifecycle deletes rather than promotes; night/civil, night/ebpf-blocks, night/cic and hopacsota-p0 were moved into spiral/lab this morning; 9 cited paths no longer exist (into, todo-source, caches, outputs, ...), mostly prose words matched after 'job tmp' Next: rule for the move: only source files (spi, spiproj, ps1, py, fsx) of main-lane items, never databases or logs; next batch: night/bend64 and rotation/coverage-probe if any open item still needs them")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_7 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 7u32, "2026-10-19T18:35Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:35 lap 4 grain: resolved Done folders: of 99 spiral lab folders, 18 belong to Done items; 10 were removed after a zip into the session scratchpad (CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES, DIR-TREE-README, FSHARP-UTF8-STRINGS, FSHARP-WARM-RUNNER, MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL, MATRIX-TOOLCHAIN-IDENTITY, TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC, UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC, WGSL-RUN-WGPU-30, SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE: none tracked, nothing written in the last 2 h, references only in ledger and finished queue rows); kept: CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE (probe-lib.ps1 is the live lab gate tool, to be promoted), HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH (P3 inputs), CELLS-GATE + CELLS-REPIN (cross-referenced scripts), SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ (after-freeze probes), ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE and SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS (touched within 2 h) Next: promote probe-lib.ps1 into a tracked gate script (it hardcodes scratchpad MIX_BUILD_PATH, which must become a parameter), then resolve the remaining 8")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_8 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 8u32, "2026-10-29T22:32Z", "main", "10-29 18:3x main: PROVISIONAL fields filled while the item is Blocked or Paused (fill-all-task-fields): estimate 135m (D-base rule: base(D4) x (1 - 45%)); load light (default); assignee agent (default). The owner corrects them on resume.")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_9 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 9u32, "2026-10-31T20:56Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_10 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 10u32, "2026-11-05T21:55Z", "main", "main 11-05 17:5x CLOSE as SUPERSEDED: this item's convention (one spiral/lab/<item> dir per item) was replaced by the user's direction, 10-24 06:56 'all repos stop the lab pattern and start experiments in their committable place' and 10-30 22:5x 'the lab dir didn't work, it drifted'. spiral/lab was swept on 10-30/31 (archive 20261030-232045 and 20261031-080640/-080801, restore notes on each item). The item's GOAL holds under the new rule: experiments and tools go to their repo home (apps/compiler/packs, i574n.github/scripts, a kit verb), unfinished drafts to the kit drafts store, findings to notes. Check now: no lab/ directory in any C:\\home\\git repo. Steps (1)-(3) are moot: no per-repo lab/; the main-lane tools it listed live in i574n.github/scripts and apps/compiler/packs. Ongoing policing is LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_links_1 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-LINKS", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:30Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_1 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 1u32, "2026-10-21T23:12Z", "eoie-lane", "19:12 eoie team, grooming from the code: eoie_memory (shipped with the memory crates in r28+r29) already has import, render, check, list [--kind] and recall <data.spi> <words> [--limit n] over eoie_memory_query; tonight's apply-r30 ran import/check/render on all 75 Claude memories (round trip identical, 1 dangling link: formatter-plan-f2). Missing against the title: save (typed, feedback needs Why/How), edit/forget, --client, recall without a data file argument (default store), and 'eoie help memory' (fixed in r35's help coverage slice). Progress set from 0 to 40.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_2 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 2u32, "2026-10-21T23:15Z", "eoie-lane", "19:15 eoie team, recall ranking measured (rule set before measuring: adopt a coverage-first score only if hit@1 improves and hit@3 does not drop; tie keeps the current). 12 queries over the 75 Claude memories (scratch recall-eval.ps1 -> lab/EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS): current substring counts hit@1 8/12, hit@3 10/12 (misses: 'release inventory lab' ranked lab-in-repo first because 'lab' also counts inside 'label'/'available'; 'comments in code'); words covered x100 + weighted whole-word hits with body hits capped at 5: 12/12 and 12/12. Drafted for r35 (apply-recall-coverage.ps1: whole_words primitive, word_score record, ranked_score), memory_cli expectations updated (4 -> 104, 5 -> 105, same order). Caveat: I wrote the query set; it stays as the regression set in lab.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_3 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 3u32, "2026-10-21T23:17Z", "eoie-lane", "19:17 gate margin: the recall slice previews clean (query.rs 975 -> 990); eoie_memory's memory.rs is already 994. So the remaining verbs (save with Why/How typing, edit/forget, a default store so recall needs no data-file argument) start with a split of eoie_memory (verb routing vs import/render), like tonight's eoie_grep_query split.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_4 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 4u32, "2026-10-21T23:21Z", "eoie-lane", "19:21 eoie team: r36 slice 3 drafted and preview-clean (scratch apply-memory-split.ps1): import and render move into a new crate eoie_memory_store (exports eoie_memory_import, eoie_memory_render); eoie_memory keeps the verb union and routes through exports. memory.rs 994 -> 179, memory_store.rs 832. That frees room for save/edit/forget and a default store in eoie_memory.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_5 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 5u32, "2026-10-21T23:31Z", "eoie-lane", "19:31 eoie team: r36 slice 4 drafted, preview-clean: 'eoie memory save <data.spi> <kind> <slug> <description> <body>' in its own crate eoie_memory_save (memory_save.rs 644; putting it into eoie_memory_store would have made that 1029). save_check union: SaveAccepted kind | KindRejected | ReasonsMissing (feedback and project need **Why:** and **How to apply:**) | SlugTaken; nothing is written on refusal; the entry and its MEMORY index line are appended; help line updated. memory_cli test (runtime on the r36 binary). Left: edit/forget, --client, a default store.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_6 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 6u32, "2026-10-21T23:36Z", "eoie-lane", "19:36 r36 slice 5, preview-clean: 'eoie memory forget <data.spi> <slug>' removes the entry and its MEMORY index line, refuses an unknown slug with nothing written (memory_save.rs 737, memory.rs 239). Slice 6 (default store: check/list/recall without a data file read memory/claude.spi next to eoie.exe) is previewing. Left after r36: edit (description/body), --client.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_7 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 7u32, "2026-10-21T23:38Z", "eoie-lane", "19:38 r36 slice 6, preview-clean: check, list and recall take an optional data file; without one they read memory/claude.spi next to eoie.exe (memory_store_path union GivenStore | DefaultStore; memory.rs 313). So on the release binary 'eoie memory recall <words>' is the one call. Slice 7 (edit description|body, reasons still required for feedback/project bodies) is previewing.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_8 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 8u32, "2026-10-21T23:44Z", "eoie-lane", "19:44 r36 slice 7, preview-clean: 'eoie memory edit <data.spi> <slug> description|body <value>' (memory_field union by reflection; body edits of feedback/project memories still need the reasons). memory_save.rs 993 after keeping one slug walk (kind_of_slug) for save, forget and edit; the next verb goes in its own crate. With r36 the title's verbs exist except --client (which waits for the memory bootstrap direction).")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_9 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 9u32, "2026-10-22T02:30Z", "eoie", "22:30 on hold for the pivot: save/forget/edit/default-store/recall-scoring over string bodies extend the drift; r35 recall-coverage and r36 memory slices removed from the chains. Verbs return once EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES decides what memory data is typed.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_10 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 10u32, "2026-10-22T09:35Z", "eoie", "05:35 lab drain: apply-memory-default-store.ps1 deleted. It made check/list/recall default to memory/claude.spi next to eoie.exe, and that file was deleted at 05:33 by the EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES rule (string-store copy, user-called drift), so the slice can never apply. The five other held r36 slices (memory save, edit, forget, split, recall coverage) stay as reference until this item is reworked onto typed memory features (EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES F1-F6). Each must be rewritten against typed rows before it can land, never applied as drafted.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_11 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 11u32, "2026-10-22T21:53Z", "eoie-lane", "17:53 grooming rule: an item on hold until another item's decision carries that item as a dependency and the Blocked status, so 'agile next' (WSJF) stops proposing it. Its notes since 22:30 say the verbs return once EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES decides what memory data is typed, so it now depends on EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES and is Blocked; progress is unchanged.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_12 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 12u32, "2026-10-25T00:45Z", "eoie", "20:45 Lab drain: the five unlanded patches (apply-memory-save, -forget, -edit, -split, apply-recall-coverage) and recall-eval moved from lab/ to the scratch next-release\\memory-verbs folder. They date from 10-22 and need a re-proof on the current tree before any release, because CLEAN's 33 slices may have moved their anchors.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_13 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 13u32, "2026-10-29T22:31Z", "main", "10-29 18:3x main: PROVISIONAL fields filled while the item is Blocked or Paused (fill-all-task-fields): estimate 105m (D-base rule: base(D5) x (1 - 80%)); load light (default); assignee agent (default). The owner corrects them on resume.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_14 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 14u32, "2026-10-30T17:49Z", "kit", "10-30 13:5x kit: STATE CHECK (the item said 80% Blocked). The installed kit C528BE4D ships 'kit memory import | render | check | list | recall', and they work on today's folder. A scratch run imported 197 memories, all byte-identical, index identical. 'recall \"commit unstaged\" --limit 3' ranks never-commit-leave-unstaged first. 'check' found 1 dangling link and 77 feedback memories without Why/How. The dangling one was quoted text ('Links: 111 [[slug]] ...' in lib-smoke-before-syntax.md), now reworded, so dangling is 0. The 77 are mostly PARTIAL RECOVERY memories from the wipe; filling them needs evidence, and main tracks that rebuild (PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY n25).\nLEFT: the five 10-22 patches (apply-memory-save, -forget, -edit, -split, apply-recall-coverage) were lost in the wipe; heal's copies are noise (EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES n41). So 'memory save' (type-checked: feedback needs Why/How), 'edit' and 'forget' are unbuilt; recall and check ship. Real progress is about 60%. Blocked on EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES (dep) stays, because those verbs belong to its F5/F3 typed records, now sized after F2. Next: rewrite 'memory save' first, from n1's shape, as the smallest verb that closes the hand-written-memory gap.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_15 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 15u32, "2026-10-30T18:13Z", "kit", "10-30 14:1x kit: 'kit memory save' REWRITTEN AND LANDED UNSTAGED (the lost apply-memory-save patch, rebuilt from n1's shape).\n- Usage: 'kit memory save <memory-dir> <user|feedback|project|reference> <slug> \"<description>\" \"<body>\"|- [--title <text>]'. It writes <slug>.md in the exact frontmatter layout import/render use (name, description, metadata node_type and type) and appends '- [<title or slug>](<slug>.md) — <description>' to MEMORY.md. The body can come from stdin ('-'), with the same quoting fix as agile note.\n- Refusals: an unknown kind; a slug that isn't lowercase words joined by dashes; an empty description or body; an existing file ('edit it instead'); a feedback or project memory without **Why:** and **How to apply:** (the same rule 'memory check' applies); a folder without MEMORY.md.\n- Units: a new crate kit_memory_save (362 lines) holds the verb; kit_memory just routes to it (666 -> 701), since inlining the renderer there took it to 954. Help text in command_spec_domain is updated (same line counts).\n- PROOF on a candidate kit.exe, against a COPY of the real memory folder (197 memories): feedback without reasons refused, exit 2. Save via stdin with --title wrote the file shown (frontmatter, body, both reason lines) and one index line '- [Probe rule](probe-rule.md) — a probe rule'. A duplicate is refused. Re-import gives 198 memories, all identical, index identical, check dangling 0. New CLI test save_writes_a_memory_and_its_index_line_and_refuses_bad_ones in kit_memory/memory_cli.spi (registered); it runs in TRAIN5's notebook phase, since Cells is held by main's window now.\nLeft: edit, forget (and split / recall-coverage from the lost set).")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_16 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 16u32, "2026-10-30T18:16Z", "kit", "10-30 14:1x kit: 'kit memory forget <memory-dir> <slug>' LANDED UNSTAGED (rewritten from the lost apply-memory-forget). Following archive-never-delete, it moves <slug>.md to <memory-dir>/archive/<slug>.md, verifies the archived copy byte-equal before removing the original, and rewrites MEMORY.md without the memory's index line. The receipt prints 'archived=archive/<slug>.md index_lines=N'. It refuses a missing slug and an existing archive copy. It lives in kit_memory_save beside save (642 lines); kit_memory routes it (728). PROOF on a candidate kit.exe against the memory-folder copy: forgetting the probe memory saved at 14:1x archived it, removed it from the folder and the index, and left MEMORY.md byte-identical to the real one. Re-import gives 197 identical, and a second forget is refused, exit 2. The CLI test was extended (forget, archive content, index restored, refusal). Left: edit (and split / recall-coverage).")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_17 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 17u32, "2026-10-30T18:20Z", "kit", "10-30 14:2x kit: 'kit memory edit <memory-dir> <slug> description|body \"<text>\"|-' LANDED UNSTAGED (the lost apply-memory-edit, rewritten). It re-reads the memory through the model's entry_of_file, which refuses any file that doesn't render back byte-identical, so an edit can't reshape a memory silently. It replaces one field and re-renders. An edited feedback or project body must still carry **Why:** and **How to apply:** (the save/check rule). When the description changes, the MEMORY.md hook is updated only where the line still ends with the old description, so a hand-written hook is never clobbered. Units: kit_memory_save 933 lines (save, forget, edit), kit_memory 759.\nPROOF on a candidate kit.exe against the memory-folder copy: save a probe, then 'edit ... description \"new hook\"' changes the file and the index hook. An edit dropping the reasons is refused, exit 2. A stdin body edit works. A missing slug is refused. Forget then restores MEMORY.md byte-identical to the real one. The CLI test (memory_cli.spi) now covers edit and refusal too; it runs in TRAIN5's notebooks.\nThe core verbs (save, edit, forget, recall, check) now exist; split and recall-coverage from the lost set are optional extras. Progress 90; closes when installed.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_18 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 18u32, "2026-10-31T07:48Z", "kit", "10-31 03:3x kit groom: Active -> Blocked. The only remaining step is installed-is-done: TRAIN5e (job-1793421047190, snapshot 00:31) carries this change, and KIT-AGILE-LOST-UPDATES's last part ships in the train after it. Unblock and verify once kit.exe is installed. Moved so that 'agile next --team kit' stops ranking an install wait above actionable work (43 kit items were Active).")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_19 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 19u32, "2026-10-31T12:12Z", "kit", "10-31 07:4x kit: installed E14F1F0B passes the memory rows of release-acceptance (save refuses feedback without Why/How, save writes the file and its index line, edit updates the description hook, forget archives and restores the index). 'memory recall' is still unaccepted: it takes a data file, not the memory folder ('needs a readable data file'). Blocked -> Active 95 until a recall row is added and passes.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_20 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 20u32, "2026-10-31T14:14Z", "kit", "10-31 10:1x kit: DONE. The recall gap from n19 is closed. release-acceptance.ps1 gained the row 'memory import round-trips and recall ranks by words': it imports the copied memory folder to memory.spi (memories=214 identical=214 index_identical=yes), then recalls 'stopped session wake heartbeat', and the top hit must be idle-needs-wake-source. It PASSES on the installed E14F1F0B (08:1x and 10:0x runs), next to the save/edit/forget rows. Recall's input is the data file 'memory import' writes (recall <data.spi> \"<words>\" [--limit n]), not the memory folder, which is why the 07:4x probe on the folder answered 'needs a readable data file'.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_verbs_21 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-VERBS", 21u32, "2026-11-06T11:20Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_union_reflection_1 () = Note ("KIT-UNION-REFLECTION", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_1 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:13Z", "main-thread", "15:13 main-thread inventory (read-only, directory level; a full per-file walk of spiral-bin took over 25 min and was stopped, so the tool must use directory-level stamps or the ledger): spiral-bin has 12 bin-pack*-backup compiler snapshots from 10-15/10-16 holding 6.5 GB (only the newest blessed pack and its predecessor are useful); runs has 1,842 run folders since 09-26, 894 older than 7 days, about 2.1 GB (sampled 50 = 121 MB), and the fast-tier fold cache and cost-report read results.tsv, so prune keeps results.tsv and drops the rest; compile-memo (53k files) and native-memo prune themselves. Earlier totals: eoie/src/target 16.8 GB, spiral/target 10.1 GB, toolchains 5.1 GB. C: had 15.3 GB free at 14:54. Design: an eoie verb with a dry-run report (path, size, last use, rule that made it prunable), rules as a typed union, never deleting tracked files.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_2 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:55Z", "main-thread", "15:55 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: pack backups keep the newest 2 (bin-pack1x/1y); the 10 older ones (1f..1w) were deleted with approval, freeing 5.35 GB (C: 19.8 GB free). That is the first retention rule for the tool: compiler pack backups keep 2.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_3 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 3u32, "2026-10-18T13:24Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:24 main-thread: C: is down to 11.9 GB free (19.8 GB at 10-17 15:55). Runs: 1,890 folders, 1,676 older than 7 days; a 60-folder sample keeps 2 MB of results.tsv and could drop 50 MB of worker files and logs, about 1.4 GB over all old runs, so runs are not where the 8 GB went (likelier: cargo target dirs during today's eoie regeneration and the memo stores; a directory-level size pass of eoie/src/target, spiral/target and spiral-bin/compile-memo is next). Pack backups already at the keep-2 rule (bin-pack1x, bin-pack1y). No deletion done: only the pack-backup rule has the user's approval so far.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_4 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 4u32, "2026-10-18T13:28Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:28 main-thread directory-level sizes (robocopy /L /BYTES /XJ, minutes not hours): spiral-bin 38.3 GB = bin 10.25, runs 7.68, eoie-regeneration 5.71 (today, the eoie regeneration in progress at 09:22), native 5.31, toolchains 4.99, scratch 1.04, the 2 kept pack backups 0.58 each, compile-memo 0.52, core-src 0.36, obj 0.32; outside it eoie/src/target 16.7 GB, spiral/target 9.9 GB (both unchanged since yesterday), Temp 2.0 GB. So the 8 GB drop is mostly today's eoie-regeneration. Candidate rules for the dry-run tool: eoie-regeneration keeps the last run, runs older than 7 days keep only results.tsv (about 1.4 GB), native build outputs older than the native memo's 14 days.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_5 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:21 helper-lane lap 1: re-measured (directory listing plus robocopy /L byte totals, no walk of spiral-bin): C: has 20.4 GB free (11.9 GB on 10-18 09:24, so the eoie regeneration space came back); spiral-bin/eoie-regeneration is now 1.99 GB in 12 run folders from 10-17 14:48 to 10-18 19:32 (the oldest alone 92 MB), so the keep-last rule frees about 1.9 GB; runs has 1,908 folders, 1,708 older than 7 days (the keep-results.tsv rule, about 1.4 GB per the 10-18 sample); pack backups still at keep-2 (bin-pack1x 10-16 14:46, bin-pack1y 10-16 17:20). No deletion: only the pack-backup rule has user approval; the other two rules stay dry-run candidates.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_6 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:11Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:11 helper-lane lap 2: wrote and ran the dry-run spec for the eoie verb: lab/DISK-CLEANUP/dry-run.ps1 (read-only; robocopy /L byte totals, one pass per rule, deletes nothing) with the three rules as data: pack backups keep the newest 2 (0 candidates now), eoie regeneration keeps the last run (11 folders, 1.50 GB), runs older than 7 days keep their tsv files (robocopy /MINAGE:7 excluding results.tsv, fast-tier.tsv, toolchain.tsv: 8.04 GB). Total 9.84 GB prunable today, far above the 10-18 sample estimate for runs (1.4 GB), so worker files and logs of old runs are the biggest win; the fold cache and cost reports read only results.tsv. Still no deletion: only the pack-backup rule has user approval.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_7 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:29Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:29 helper-lane lap 3: typed the three rules for the future eoie verb as lab/DISK-CLEANUP/rules.spi (lab sketch, not eoie src): union retention = KeepNewest i32 | KeepLastRun | KeepTsvAfterDays i32, union approval = UserApproved | DryRunOnly, rule = root + retention + approval, and decide/applies that return Keep or Prune per entry (newest rank, age, results-tsv flag); a DryRunOnly rule always keeps, so only the approved pack-backup rule can prune. Probe via try.ps1 C (bounded, 6 s): 6 decisions over runtime values exit 13 as predicted (old pack backup pruned, regeneration and runs computed as prune but held by DryRunOnly, results.tsv kept). OPEN QUESTION for the user (recorded, not asked): approve KeepLastRun for spiral-bin/eoie-regeneration (1.50 GB) and KeepTsvAfterDays 7 for spiral-bin/runs (8.04 GB)? Flipping either is one approval field in the rule table.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_8 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 8u32, "2026-10-19T22:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:24 helper-lane lap 6: measured one more root the rules sketch has to cover. spiral-bin/candidates held 19 private-cache compiler builds from today, about 0.7 GB: pack8b 107 MB, pack9/pack10 64 MB each, five 56 MB single-patch Debug candidates, and eleven 13 MB pack8 bisect and splice dirs. I removed my own ten whose patches have landed (nominal-with, export-walk, field-splice, and the seven pack8-<item> bisect dirs, about 240 MB), after checking that no process command line referenced them. That is the KeepLastRun-style rule a future verb should apply: a candidate dir can be pruned once its pack is accepted. Main's pack dirs and the two candidates still riding pack 11 (fsx-pointer, assign-delphi) stay.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_9 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 9u32, "2026-10-21T22:31Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:31 x: measured x's build outputs (all regenerable by pwsh scripts/build.ps1): x/target 583 MB in 3,607 files, of which release (cargo) is 570 MB and build, sim-mix-build and sim-mix-world about 6+3+3 MB; empty leftovers bless, determinism, e2e-eight, e2e-metrics. apps/sim/_build 7 MB, deps 5 MB, frames/build 3 MB. About 0.6 GB in total: x is not a disk-pressure source; C: has 75 GB free of 930, and the os_mon disk_almost_full alarm in every BEAM log comes from the default 80% threshold, not from x. Nothing pruned.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_10 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 10u32, "2026-10-22T00:17Z", "main", "20:17 main re-measure (robocopy /L byte totals, read-only, job main-disk-sizes, 3 min): C: 69.5 GB free (x saw 75 GB at 18:31; the os_mon disk_almost_full alarm is its default 80% threshold, as x noted). Growth since 10-18 09:28: eoie/src/target 16.7 -> 26.4 GB, spiral/target 9.9 -> 15.8 GB, TEMP 2.0 -> 10.4 GB; spiral-bin: bin 10.4, native 5.4, toolchains 5.3, eoie-regeneration 4.2 (30 runs), runs 3.9, candidates 3.2 (tonight's night-release candidates included), scratch 1.4. dry-run.ps1 rules: 8.11 GB prunable (runs older than 7 days keeping tsv 3.92, eoie-regeneration keep-last 4.19); nothing deleted. Uncovered by any rule: the two cargo target dirs (42 GB together) and TEMP; next for this item: a cargo-target rule (profile dirs untouched for N days, cargo can rebuild them) and a TEMP owner census by top-level folder, both dry-run first.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_11 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 11u32, "2026-10-22T15:59Z", "main", "2026-10-22 11:59 main: TEMP owner census (read-only Python scan of %TEMP%, the item's planned next step): 14.46 GB in 4,353 top-level entries. claude 7.4 GB = two session folders: fc1568a6-... 5.3 GB (another session, not touched) and this session 2.1 GB. Then spiral_cells 757 MB (live Cells runs), wsl.3.0.3.0.x64.msi 351 MB (157 h idle, no owner in the backlog), wctFB21.tmp 128 MB (190 h), main's lib copies lib-ns/lib-helpers/lib-dt/lib-concat 116 MB each, about 20 eoie-hidden-window-*/eoie-timeout-tree-* dirs of 70-99 MB each (41-78 h idle, eoie's), spiral_kino 89 MB, spiral-kino-compiler 81 MB (118 h). Pruned by a pre-written rule, own files only: in this session's scratch, every top-level item idle > 6 h, not used by today's live scripts and not named anywhere in the backlog text (352 items, 0.75 GB: old pack/candidate core copies, lua/calyx/census probes, tree-sitter cli...); 74 cited items kept; plus 1,232 finished task outputs idle > 6 h (0.77 GB, one 730 MB). Freed 1.5 GB in all. main's 4 lib copies are kept: all differ from today's tree, but without their bases that cannot tell unlanded work (lib-ns is the staged tree-lib move) from an older snapshot, so the delete-after-landing rule keeps them. Suggested owners for the rest: eoie for its hidden-window and timeout-tree dirs (about 1.6 GB, a KeepLast rule), and the user for the WSL msi.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_12 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 12u32, "2026-10-23T15:09Z", "main", "2026-10-23 11:09 main: C: 62.0 GB free (69.5 at 10-22 20:17). Candidates rule run (scratch candidates-prune.ps1, rule fixed before the run): prune a spiral-bin/candidates dir only if written > 48 h ago, no live process command line or staged script (TEMP pack-*, lib-land, compiler tmp/scripts) names it, a note by main/main-thread/helper-lane ties it to us, and no open item or note from the last 48 h cites it. 2 dirs pruned (jp-debug, x-budget, 99 MB); 54 kept, mostly because an open item cites them (hopac-depth 627 MB via HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH, speed-ab-old via COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB). So candidates frees space only as items close: the future verb should key candidate retention on the citing item's Done state. Biggest uncovered roots stay the two cargo target dirs (42 GB at 10-22).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_13 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 13u32, "2026-10-24T00:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:25 main: C: 58.6 GB free (62.0 at 11:09). Rerun of the candidates rule (dry run): would free 0 MB, since every dir was written within 48 h, is cited by an open item or a recent note, or isn't tied to main by a note. Space frees as items close, as note 3 predicted. Status corrected to Active (the rule exists and runs).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_14 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 14u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:32 (subagent): blocked: user decision on the two dry-run rules (regeneration keep-last 1.50 GB, runs keep-tsv 8.04 GB); rules typed in lab rules.spi -> next: the user's approval of either rule | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): user decision pending -> next: the user's approval | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: C: at 9.4 GB free (Erlang raised disk_almost_full at 18:32) -> next: add a candidates rule; main's pack caches about 250 MB")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_15 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 15u32, "2026-10-24T21:54Z", "main", "main 17:5x measure (nothing deleted): today's gate and pack scratch in %TEMP% is 1.55 GB over 37 folders. lib-gate-kit is 1,035 MB (work\\<name> copies of lib, one per gate, now with a base snapshot each since 16:5x); pack-next* cores are 20-57 MB each. Rule proposal, to apply next block: lib-gate-kit\\work\\<name> may be removed once verdict-<name>.txt is GREEN and the slice has landed (the land log says 'landed <name>') or the item is withdrawn; a pack-next* folder may be removed once a later pack that composes it is installed, keeping the newest 2. The proof/e3 copies already clean themselves up.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_16 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 16u32, "2026-10-25T09:21Z", "main", "10-25 05:21 main: applied note 10-24 17:54's rule for lib-gate-kit\\work: removed 34 folders (1,035 MB) whose verdict file is GREEN and whose name has a 'landed <name>' line in an eoie job log from the last 4 days. Kept: FISCAL-GENERIC, LIB-DICTM, LIB-GAPS-X, LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS and LIB-UNICODE, GREEN but with no land line in range. Tool: scratchpad gate-work-sweep.ps1 (dry run by default). C: free 33.8 GB after.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_17 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 17u32, "2026-10-25T20:41Z", "main", "10-25 16:41 main: measure (robocopy list mode, fast): C: free 24.9 GB (28.1 at 14:09). spiral-bin by folder: bin 10.36 GB, eoie-regeneration 6.61 (kit's), runs 6.58, native 5.65, toolchains 5.31, candidates 4.64, scratch 2.11, eoie-regen-target 1.75. The note 10-19 rules (regeneration keep-last, runs keep-tsv) still await their own measure-then-apply pass. The candidate rule to pre-register next: keep the candidate dirs named by any pending pack's dll.txt plus pack-spelling; remove the rest once their pack is installed. The old full-file disk walk took more than 3 h and was stopped as stale; use robocopy /L /BYTES for measures.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_18 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 18u32, "2026-10-25T20:57Z", "main", "10-25 16:57 main: candidate rule, written before applying: a main pack candidate under spiral-bin\\candidates (pack-next*, pack11-13, combined-pack, pack-after-night, night-pack, night-release, pack-fmt-only, pack-fmt-next) is removed once its pack is installed or superseded and no pending pack's dll.txt names it. pack-next4 (the pending 5m review's candidate) and pack-spelling (the respell tool) stay. Other lanes' candidates (hopacsota-*, hopac-depth, eoie-*-measure, x-profile, lua-function-limits, waits-census, format probes) are not mine and stay. Applying to: combined-pack, night-pack, night-release, pack-after-night, pack-fmt-next, pack-fmt-only, pack-next3, pack-next3b, pack-next5, pack-next5b, pack-next5c, pack-next5d, pack-next5e, pack-next5f, pack-next5h, pack-next5i-hopacsota, pack-next5k, pack-next9-debug, pack11, pack12, pack13.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_19 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 19u32, "2026-10-25T21:00Z", "main", "10-25 17:00 main: candidate rule APPLIED: 21 main pack candidate dirs removed (the list in n18), 1,236 MB freed, C: 25.3 GB free. Checked before: no process command line referenced any of them; pack-next4 and pack-spelling kept by name (the script refuses both); other lanes' candidates untouched.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_20 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 20u32, "2026-10-26T00:09Z", "main", "10-25 20:09 main: runs keep-tsv rule (10-18 note), refined from its readers before applying. The readers are fast-tier.ps1 (results.tsv; its fold stamp is results.tsv size and mtime) and cost-report.ps1:63 (the earliest worker*.jobs.tsv's CreationTime as the run's start, else the folder's). Rule: in a matrix run folder (name single-flight|hopac|hopacsota|gpt5parallel-yyyyMMdd-HHmmss, holding a results.tsv, no subfolders, last written more than 7 days ago), remove the worker<n>-<n>.{jobs.tsv,results.tsv,log,log.out} files except the earliest worker*.jobs.tsv. Keep results.tsv, toolchain.tsv, fast-tier.tsv and every other file. Folders of other shapes (mesa and vulkan DLL runs, wgsl entries, about 0.6 GB) are other lanes' and stay. Refuse if any process command line names one of the folders. After: check every results.tsv stamp is unchanged. Dry run: 68 folders, 488 files, 364 MB (runs is 6.58 GB in 218 folders, already pruned from 1,890 by someone since 10-18). Tool: scratchpad runs-keep-tsv.ps1 (dry run by default, -Apply).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_21 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 21u32, "2026-10-26T00:09Z", "main", "10-25 20:09 main: runs keep-tsv APPLIED: removed 488 files (364 MB) from 68 old matrix run folders; all 68 results.tsv stamps unchanged, so fast-tier's fold cache stays valid; C: 26.7 GB free. Still open from the 10-19 rules: eoie-regeneration keep-last (6.61 GB, kit's folder: ask kit after the rename) and native outputs older than the native memo's 14 days (5.65 GB, measure next).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_22 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 22u32, "2026-10-26T00:17Z", "main", "10-25 20:17 main: native outputs measured. spiral-bin\\native is native/<mode>/samples/<sample>/<backend> build dirs that the native tier rewrites: single-flight 2,747 MB, hopac 2,103 MB, gpt5parallel 701 MB, hopacsota 236 MB. Rule, written before applying: a native/<mode> folder whose mode no script can produce is unreachable, regenerable build output and is removed. Evidence: every native path is built as native/<mode> (test.ps1:52, bench-native.ps1:14), mode comes from test.ps1's ValidateSet (single-flight|sf|gpt5parallel|gp|hopacsota|hs) or ConvertTo-SpiralMode (env.ps1:15, which throws on anything else), and no literal native\\hopac exists in spiral, eoie or i574n.github/scripts (a literal grep is no proof alone: native/single-flight isn't written literally either). native/hopac (last written 10-13, from the gpt5parallel mode's old name) matches; the other three are live modes and stay. Refuse if any process command line names the folder.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_23 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 23u32, "2026-10-26T00:20Z", "main", "10-25 20:20 main: native\\hopac removed by the n22 rule: 2.07 GB freed, C: 28.8 GB free; no process named it. The same unreachable-mode rule covers 4 more spiral-bin folders: bin\\hopac 9,838 MB (an old compiler build from 10-13 plus about 4 GB of .r2r-old-* leftovers from 10-08), obj\\hopac 219 MB, core-src\\hopac 13 MB and scratch\\hopac 12 MB. Every bin/obj/core-src/scratch path is keyed by a mode that build.ps1:22 (ConvertTo-SpiralMode or a literal single-flight/gpt5parallel pair), env.ps1:125 or test.ps1:30 produces, never hopac. No env var, process command line or loaded dotnet module names bin\\hopac, and spiral/apps/compiler/tmp has no literal reference. Still required before removal: the stem search for hard-coded bin\\hopac DLL paths in other lanes' %TEMP% scripts. The Grep tool times out on %TEMP%, and the recursive Select-String version was stopped because it is a heavy scan inside tibia's PR-28 block (the positive control, a single-flight\\SpiralCompiler stem, does find 3 scripts). Runs after block end; if it finds 0, remove the 4 folders (about 10.1 GB).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_24 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 24u32, "2026-10-26T01:10Z", "main", "10-25 21:10 main: eoie-regeneration rule, from kit (owner) at 21:0x. Nothing reads an older run: compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 gives each run its own GUID folder (line 40), uses only that one, deletes it on success (line 241) and prunes folders older than 2 days at startup (lines 37-38); the 6.61 GB is runs that threw before cleanup plus -KeepStaged runs, useful only to someone inspecting a failure. Rule: (now) keep only the newest run, delete only while no test-regeneration process runs; (after KIT-RENAME) the renamed script writes spiral-bin\\kit-regeneration, so all of eoie-regeneration is dead and is deleted whole on kit's 'rename finalized' message. Applying only the after-rename form, since the release with the rename starts around 21:17: one deletion instead of two.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_25 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 25u32, "2026-10-26T08:10Z", "main", "10-26 04:10 main: retired hopac build mode removed: spiral-bin\\bin\\hopac 9.61 GB, obj\\hopac 0.21, core-src\\hopac 0.01, scratch\\hopac 0.01 (freed 9.86 GB; C: 46.7 GB free). The first stem search (recursive over all of %TEMP%) ran 3.5 h without output and was stopped; it was read-only, so there was nothing to restore. The rewrite does one read per file over 28,958 tracked repo files plus %TEMP% to depth 3, then 739 untracked repo files. Positive control: 54 files name single-flight\\SpiralCompiler. Retired-mode references: 1, the backlog note text in agile.spi (history, not a consumer); 0 untracked; 0 running processes with a hopac path. Deleted by the rule written before the search (CLEAR = no consumer).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_26 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 26u32, "2026-10-26T15:18Z", "main", "10-26 11:18 main: 10-26 11:2x main pass: runs keep-tsv rule (n20) reapplied to the 16 matrix run folders that crossed 7 days since 10-25: 76 worker files removed (131 MB), results.tsv stamps changed 0, so fast-tier's fold cache stays valid. C: 47.1 GB free (46.9 before; 48.1 at 07:00). Earlier today, 06:5x, the measure gave spiral-bin candidates 3.43 GB (main's only remaining dir is pack-next4, whose kit the running 5n review reads, so it stays until install-5n; the rest are other lanes'), runs 6.27, compile-memo 0.48, r2r-cache 0.48, core-src 0.35, bin 0.75. Nothing else is due by a pre-registered rule; eoie-regeneration waits for kit's 'rename finalized' (n24).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_27 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 27u32, "2026-10-26T19:20Z", "main", "10-26 15:20 main: 10-26 15:2x main: removed main's own finished scratch copies from today, 10 folders, freeing 509 MB: 4 lib-copy typecheck trees (sm-pack, parsing, runtime, testing; each verdict recorded on LIB-MACRO-HEAL), 2 blog diagnostic trees, the cube compile test, the cube proof's runaway reference copy, and 2 small ones. Reparse points were deleted first, deepest first, so no delete recursed through a link. After the removal the live trees count unchanged: spiral lib 76 .spi, polyglot lib 539 files, blog engine 8 .spi. Kept: every apply/proof script, pre-land backups (dir-tree-args-lib/pre-land), and the requeue plan. Finding: cube-diag (my compile test) was 229 MB, from the same robocopy-follows-the-lib-link trap that hung cube-proof-3. Rule recorded in memory: proof copies use /XJD.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_28 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 28u32, "2026-10-26T22:39Z", "main", "10-26 18:39 main: 10-26 18:39 main: disk measurement, measured, not deleted (deletions only by pre-written rule). C: free 46 GB. Largest roots, files only, reparse points skipped so links aren't double-counted: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin 27.8 GB; %TEMP% 30.2 GB; spiral\\target 25.9 GB; %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\jobs 0.0 GB; polyglot\\target and eoie\\target do not exist. These three roots hold 83.9 GB, almost 2x the free space. Proposed rule, to pre-register before any sweep: (1) %TEMP% top-level dirs older than 48 h whose name matches a known scratch prefix (registry-execute-run-*, cube-*, lib-*-node-*, pause-requeue excluded while referenced), owner-agnostic only after a reader-lease check; (2) spiral\\target/test and per-run target dirs older than 7 days; (3) spiral-bin runs/ and scratch/ older than 7 days, never bin/ of the installed compiler. main's own registry run dirs are already cleaned after each probe (0 left).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_29 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 29u32, "2026-10-26T23:23Z", "main", "10-26 19:23 main: 10-26 19:22 main: RULE for main's own %TEMP% share, registered before the per-folder sizes are read (the measurement is running). A path may be deleted only if ALL hold: (1) OWNER: it is a main-made run or proof COPY, never a slice: the registry-execute-run-* / registry-planner-run-* run dirs, '<slice>\\copy' subfolders made by main's proof-check.ps1 scripts, and lib-*-node-* staging copies made by main. Slice folders (apply.ps1, manifests, pre-land undo copies) are never deleted while their chain is queued or their item open. (2) NOT REFERENCED: the path does not appear in any queued or held job's manifest (TEMP pause-requeue plan.json, extras.json, *.json manifests) or in the argv of a running eoie/kit job (eoie job list). (3) IDLE: newest file older than 2 h, and no running job writes under it. (4) LINK-SAFE: reparse points under it are removed first (Delete() on the link, never recursing into a target), and read-only flags are cleared before removal. Everything else in %TEMP% (other teams' folders, unknown owners, toolchain caches) is out of scope for main; the report lists their sizes so their owners can act. Each deletion is logged with path and size on this item.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_30 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 30u32, "2026-10-27T09:08Z", "main", "10-27 05:08 main: 10-27 05:08 main: per-owner disk measurement, measured (C: 36 GB free, down from 46 GB on 10-26). %TEMP% = 35.7 GB over 1,702 folders. The big ones: claude 16.2 GB (Claude Code session temp, all under C--home-git, broken down per session: fc1568a6 kit 10.65 GB, ac4b4e98 tibia 3.87 GB, 5a6e53fb main 0.58 GB, b7595ac3 x 0.55 GB, 84b53718 fiscal 0.45 GB, 88a146f8 compiler 0.14 GB), lib-gate-kit 4.75 GB (shared gate work folders, one per gate name), eoie-parked-train 2.6 GB (kit, last write 10-24), dice-menu-href 1.1 GB (main's proof copies, rebuilt per run), spiral_cells 0.86 GB. Under n29's rule main may remove only its own run and proof copies, which is about 2 GB here (dice-menu-href's reference/healed copies once its proof chain ends, plus main-named gate work folders); the large shares are other lanes' and were reported to them. No deletion tonight: dice-menu-href proof-3 is queued and reads that folder.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_31 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 31u32, "2026-10-27T09:42Z", "main", "10-27 05:42 main: 10-27 05:42 main: progress after the per-owner measurement. tibia freed 2,912 MB from its session temp (41 finished proof copies: lib copies, bisect/probe roots and the stage roots of landed lib slices; drafts, scripts and notes kept), and C: went from 36 to 44 GB free. kit acked its 10.65 GB share plus eoie-parked-train with a plan: byte-redundant copies first, then a hash-verified zip of the text-only rest before the originals go, preceded by i574n.github/scripts/unstaged-backup.ps1 (KIT-SCRATCH-WATCH M2), which zips every repo's staged, unstaged and untracked changes with a hash check. kit relayed a user rule from about 10-27 04:2x, 'archive, never delete; delete only provably redundant throwaways after a backup'. main did not see it first-hand and follows it as relayed. main deleted nothing tonight.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_32 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 32u32, "2026-10-27T11:31Z", "kit", "07:3x kit, archive pass done for kit's share, under the user's 04:2x archive-never-delete rule. (1) Idle cargo target dirs (CACHEDIR.TAG, no process naming them) freed directly as build output: candidate-target 2.77 GB, cargo-check-target 0.73, plus 12 nested targets in fsrc, ge3-fix, fmt-rebase and pending-heal (about 2.1 GB). (2) i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1, a content-addressed archive verified before any removal: my scratchpad's 474 folders older than 10-26, minus every folder a live script references (tree-read, agile-records were kept for the running release), came to 345,711 files, 5.27 GB -> unique 0.52 GB -> blobs.zip 0.067 GB, verify failures 0, originals removed; restore with -Restore %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/archive/20261027-054550/manifest.tsv. A second copy is on the node (C:/home/remote-stage/backups/archive/20261027-054550, both files hash-verified). A restore drill on 4,786 files was byte-identical. (3) %TEMP%/eoie-parked-train (10-24, no live references or processes) archived the same way to %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/archive/20261027-064527 and removed. C: free went from 36 GB (main's 10-27 report) to 50 GB, with other lanes writing at the same time.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_33 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 33u32, "2026-10-27T14:51Z", "main", "10-27 10:51 main: main's own temp cleanup (rule n29: own copies only; provably redundant, regenerable scratch), C: now 52.2 GB free. (1) TEMP dice-menu-href reference/, healed/ and normalized/ (2,263 MB): build copies of dice/ui made by the href proof; the heal is LANDED (GRANULAR-HEAL n64), the copies are regenerable by proof.ps1, and the scripts stay. Junctions were deleted first, and dice's deps link was checked intact. (2) Five lib-gate-kit work folders (1,327 MB): LIB-RUNTIME-CASTS, LIB-DATE-TIME-GRANULAR, LIB-CRYPTO-ED25519, LIB-CONSOLE-TRACE-BLOCKS and LIB-JSON-SCAN-ARRAYS. Each was proven landed by a strict byte test: every lib file its candidate copy changed relative to its base copy is now byte-identical in the live lib. Verdict files are kept, and a re-gate would regenerate the folder. Not removed: the other main gate folders whose changed files the live lib has since moved past (landed by the chain logs, but not provable by bytes any more), and every XSD-*, X-* and FISCAL-* folder, which belong to fiscal and x (the kit is shared).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_34 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 34u32, "2026-10-27T15:28Z", "main", "10-27 11:28 main: main's health sentinel (session scratchpad health-sentinel.ps1, both boxes every 15 min until 21:00) fired twice at 11:23 and 11:2x on local free memory 1,461 and 1,528 MB against its 1,536 MB floor. Both were normal load (six Claude sessions at 6 GB, two heavy jobs; commit 30.7 of 94 GB), not a runaway, so the floor was miscalibrated for today's box. Restarted with -MinFreeMB 1024. The real danger this morning was 0.45 GB (tibia's three WGSL runners, 15 GB), which the 1 GB floor still catches with margin.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_35 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 35u32, "2026-10-27T17:42Z", "main", "10-27 13:42 main: Disk pass 13:0x-13:3x (C: 50.7 GB free at the start). Freed: LOCALAPPDATA main-cargo-target (0.55 GB, a pure build output of the old export-migrate harness, which no longer sets CARGO_TARGET_DIR; no process named it), and chat-2's VOID copy TEMP main-export-migrate/copy-polyglot-20261027-110147 (0.77 GB) through scratch-archive.ps1, so it can be restored. Finding for a durable rule: the shared lib gate scratch TEMP lib-gate-kit/work holds 48 per-gate folders (3.46 GB, about 50 MB each, every lane's gates) that never expire. A byte-landing census (TEMP lib-gate-work-census.tsv: a folder counts as landed when every .spi its candidate changed against its base is byte-equal in the live lib) proves only 2 (LIB-NEAR-DRAIN landed, LIB-XML-BLANK-LINE no change). 34 are UNPROVEN only because the live lib moved on after they landed. 6 were excluded because a queued chain uses them (radix, nil, list-sugar). A byte proof can't age these folders out, so the fix belongs at write time: gate.ps1/land.ps1 drop a gate's work folder once its land succeeds, keeping only the verdict file it may reuse. Not applied: gate.ps1 and land.ps1 are shared by every lane and land.ps1 is in use now, so this waits for an idle queue and a heads-up to the lanes.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_36 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 36u32, "2026-10-27T19:37Z", "main", "10-27 15:37 main: INCIDENT and FIX in i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 (unstaged). It followed directory links: EnumerateFiles(folder, '*', AllDirectories) walks through junctions and symlinks, so archiving a folder that holds a link into a live repo archived that repo too, and the removal step (Remove-Item -Recurse) then ran on a folder with live links inside. Found 15:2x: main's 13:15 archive of chat-2's 0.77 GB repo copy (574 own files) had grown blobs.zip to 14.3 GB with a 98 MB manifest. 366k of its entries came through the copy's deps\\spiral link to C:\\home\\git\\spiral, and C: free fell from 50.7 to 35.2 GB. Stopped (main's own task) before its removal step, so nothing outside the copy was touched; the partial run (kit archive 20261027-131545) was removed and C: returned to 49.2 GB. Fix: a stack walk (Get-OwnEntries) that prunes reparse points instead of entering them; each link is written to the run's links.tsv (relative path, Junction or SymbolicLink, target) and not archived as content; -Apply deletes each recorded link itself (FileSystemInfo.Delete) and refuses if new links appeared, then removes the folder; -Restore recreates the links after the files. Positive control (a junction from the fixture to an outside folder): the outside file survived, the manifest held only the 2 own files, links.tsv recorded the junction, the fixture was removed, and restore brought back both files plus the junction with its original target. Rerun on the chat-2 copy: 574 files, 6 links recorded (not followed), 0.77 GB, zip 0.17 GB, done in under a minute, spiral intact. All lanes were told at 15:3x to check any of today's archives of folders containing links. Earlier main archives today (lab folders, the stage folder) had no links.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_37 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 37u32, "2026-10-27T20:33Z", "main", "10-27 16:33 main: Retention rule for lib gate work folders DRAFTED and proven on copies (TEMP lib-gate-retention/apply.ps1; not yet applied to the live lib-gate-kit/land.ps1, 329461A3). Reading the code: only gate.ps1 reads work\\<Name>, and it deletes and recreates that folder itself at its start (gate.ps1:17). The verdict that gate reuse depends on is the separate verdict-<Name>.txt in the kit root, so a landed gate's work folder is dead weight (about 50 MB each, 48 of them today, 3.46 GB). Change: after a successful land, land.ps1 drops work\\<Name>, unlinking its reparse points first, deepest first (LIB-FS-JUNCTION's test data holds a junction), and prints what it dropped. The verdict file is kept. Apply guards: the anchor (the end-of-land cleanup) must occur exactly once and be the file's last statement; refuse if already applied; refuse while any process runs land.ps1 via -File (matching the script argument, not any mention of the path); back up to land.ps1.pre-retention. Controls: (1) on a land.ps1 copy with a fixture work\\X holding a junction to an outside folder, the patched tail dropped work\\X, unlinked 1 link, the outside file survived, and verdict-X.txt was kept; (2) with a process holding '-File <copy>', the apply refused ('in use by 1 process') and the copy stayed byte-identical. My first two controls were flawed, both caught: the guard first matched the leaf name, then my own apply invocation's -Land argument, before being narrowed to a -File script argument. Apply window: after TRAIN publishes and the nil batch-2 chain's land finishes (it uses land.ps1). The existing 48 folders get a one-off sweep then, landed ones only: those whose names have a 'landed <Name>' line in a land log.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_38 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 38u32, "2026-10-28T10:41Z", "main", "10-28 06:41 main: 10-28 06:3x-06:4x cleanup, main. (1) land.ps1 retention applied (TEMP lib-gate-retention/apply.ps1, controls GREEN on 10-27): after a successful land, land.ps1 now drops work\\<Name> (links unlinked first, deepest first); verdict-<Name>.txt is kept; backup land.ps1.pre-retention. (2) The existing gate work folders were swept by rule (lib-gate-retention/sweep.ps1: drop a work folder only when its verdict file exists, it is untouched for 1 h, and no gate.ps1 or land.ps1 is running; links unlinked first): 48 dropped, 4.17 GB freed, 3 kept. (3) TEMP apply.ps1 draft folders: 112 untouched since before 10-27 00:00 (and with no file inside newer, and no running process naming them) were ARCHIVED, not deleted, with i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1: archive 20261028-063929 in LOCALAPPDATA kit\\archive, 7,691 files, 13 links recorded and not followed, blob verify failures 0. Restore any with -Restore <manifest.tsv> -RestoreTo <dir>. 16 current drafts remain. C: free 49 GB. Remaining scope: the periodic part (a scheduled run of the sweep and the age-rule archive) and spiral-bin bin/obj/runs measurement.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_39 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 39u32, "2026-10-28T11:24Z", "main", "10-28 07:24 main: spiral-bin measured 10-28 07:2x (scratchpad measure-bin.ps1: an explicit stack walk that skips reparse points, with a 60 s and 300k-file cap per folder; no recursion through junctions), about 29 GB in all. Largest: runs 6.42 GB (246 run dirs), toolchains 5.31 (needed), native 3.46, candidates 3.43 (49 builds), eoie-regen-target 2.63, scratch 2.24 (264k files, last 10-26), probes 0.66, mix-builds 0.65, r2r-cache 0.60, compile-memo 0.42. Done now: eoie-regen-target (a cargo debug/tmp target last written 10-23, before the kit rename; 0 references in kit or spiral/apps, no process naming it, 0 reparse points) removed as a build output. C: free 51 GB. Rules proposed for the periodic part, to record before applying: (a) runs: keep every run id cited in a backlog note (grep agile.spi for single-flight-*), plus the newest 20; ARCHIVE the rest (they are evidence, so restorable, not deleted). (b) candidates: keep any build referenced by a dll.txt, an install script or a running job; remove the rest as build outputs. (c) scratch, probes, mix-builds, cells-results: entries untouched for 7+ days and named by no running process are build outputs to remove. Each rule runs as a dry list first, then a sweep while no gate, land, accept or train is running.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_40 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 40u32, "2026-10-28T11:26Z", "main", "10-28 07:26 main: Rule (c) applied 10-28 07:2x (TEMP lib-gate-retention/bin-sweep.ps1; refuses while any gate, land, accept, train or matrix run is active). In spiral-bin scratch, probes and mix-builds, entries untouched 7+ days, named by no running process, and containing no reparse point: 175 removed as build outputs (probes 174 from 10-06 and the scratch gpt5parallel build from 10-14, plus mix-builds without links). 11 mix-builds entries containing links (Mix build trees link their deps) were skipped. They need an unlink-first pass, as land.ps1's retention does, before removal. C: free 52 GB, up from 45 GB at 06:15. Open: rule (a) (archive runs not cited by any note, keep the newest 20) and rule (b) (remove unreferenced candidates), the unlink-first pass for those 11, and the scheduling for 'periodic'.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_41 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 41u32, "2026-10-28T13:35Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 09:4x: reclaimed 56 stale .d-bad/.r2r-old backups (33.8 MB) from %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\bin\\single-flight\\SpiralCompiler\\Release\\net11.0 after compiler-lane confirmation that NO install/rollback reads them. Evidence: no script in spiral/eoie/kit/i574n.github creates or reads .d-bad (the 32 .d-bad-* are a 07-05..10-06 one-off from a since-removed op); kit bundle rollback is slot-based (backup_domain, restoration_* over i32 slots) with its own staging, zero `d-bad` strings; the 27 .r2r-old-* are build.ps1 Unlock-SpiralOutputs transients swept at the next build (build.ps1:148), only an immediate same-build restore (build.ps1:133), never a persistent rollback. Left 3 from today 05:30 for the next build sweep. Rest of DISK-CLEANUP scope (build bin/obj, runs, scratch) unchanged.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_42 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 42u32, "2026-10-30T00:26Z", "main", "10-29 20:26 main, from x: the node's BEAM raised ':alarm_handler {:set, {{:disk_almost_full, C:\\}}}' at 20:26 during a mix run. The node's C: has 28.6 GB free. OTP's alarm is percentage-based, so builds still run, but space keeps shrinking. Tonight's archive-by-move of pre-retire mirrors (C:\\home\\remote-archive\\20261029-*: main 7, x 18, kit 8, fiscal 3, compiler 1) protected the data but freed NOTHING, because the moves stayed on the same volume. NEXT SESSION (main, after the pack-7 install, as its own job): inside the archived mirrors ONLY, free the regenerable build output (_build, target, node_modules, mix-build*, .cache; allowed directly by archive-never-delete). FIRST record and UNLINK every junction (each mirror's spiral\\deps -> C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\deps; memory unlink-mirror-junctions-first), because a recursive delete through a junction deletes the shared deps checkout. Dry-run the size first, guard every delete with safe-delete.ps1's Remove-TreeSafely, and keep the sources. Measure free space before and after. Not done tonight on purpose: there is no time for the junction-safety steps before 21:00.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_43 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 43u32, "2026-10-30T00:30Z", "main", "10-29 20:30 main, SIZED (read-only walk of node C:\\home\\remote-archive at 20:3x, reparse points skipped; PowerShell 7 Get-ChildItem doesn't follow junctions without -FollowSymlink): C: has 28.6 GB free; tonight's archives total 11.2 GB, of which 8.2 GB is regenerable build output in 24 dirs. Two cargo target dirs hold 7.5 GB of it: 20261029-kit-preretire\\dryrun\\target 4.1 GB and 20261029-x-preretire\\xv29\\spiral\\target 3.38 GB. Then kit-libwin-a 0.25, xv29\\x 0.14, kit-libwin-b 0.10, main land1029\\fiscal 0.10 GB, and so on. Freeing all 24 would take C: to about 36.8 GB free. Plan as n42 (junctions recorded and unlinked first; Remove-TreeSafely; sources kept). The dryrun and xv29 target dirs alone give 7.5 GB, and their owners (kit, x) can free them. Note: fiscal's 8 newly attributed mirrors are not yet archived or measured.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_44 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 44u32, "2026-10-30T01:14Z", "main", "10-29 21:14 main, from kit's candlib check (no live dependency: SPIRAL_LIB unset at User and Machine scope; 0 of 11 queued node jobs and no env var reference an archived mirror; 12 script hits, all finished one-offs). One stale DEFAULT is main's: node C:\\home\\remote-stage\\lib-perf\\node-lib-perf.ps1 (the 10-26 sm-linear perf experiment, job-1793048449505-main-sm-linear-ab) defaults -SpiralLib to C:\\home\\remote\\candlib\\spiral\\lib\\spiral, and its sm-linear-node-*.ps1 use candlib-smlinear. Both mirrors are archived tonight. To rerun it, pass -SpiralLib to a freshly synced mirror (REMOTE-SYNC-OK), or move candlib back from C:\\home\\remote-archive\\20261029-kit-preretire\\candlib. Archived mirrors aren't touched by tomorrow's build-output cleanup beyond their _build/target trees.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_45 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 45u32, "2026-10-30T02:33Z", "main", "10-29 22:33 main, DONE for tonight's archives, brought forward from P5 because node C: was shrinking about 1.6 GB/h (28.6 GB at 20:26 -> 25.34 at 22:3x). Script: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\night-1030\\node-archive-cleanup.ps1 (pushed via scp, hash-checked, run with -File). It frees a build dir (_build/target/node_modules/.cache/mix-build*) only when a walk proves it has NO reparse point inside, re-checks just before removal, and verifies the deps checkout afterwards. Dry run, then -Apply: 19 dirs removed, C: 25.05 -> 32.76 GB (+7.7 GB). Link targets that existed before still exist: 43 of 111, identical to the dry run (the other 68 are mirror-internal links to pre-move paths). Deps checkout intact. Manifest: node C:\\home\\remote-archive\\cleanup-manifest-20261029-2233.tsv. SKIPPED by design: 20 dirs with a link inside (Elixir _build trees holding mix's links); a link-aware pass would be needed to free those. GROWTH SOURCE: mirrors written in the last 3 h total only 0.8 GB, while the node's TEMP holds 9 GB, likely job scratch and build output. Not touched tonight (live jobs write there); owners are asked to size and free their own TEMP output.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_46 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 46u32, "2026-10-30T02:52Z", "main", "10-29 22:52 main, ROOT CAUSE of the node disk growth, found via git-a6's TEMP census: the node's WSL Ubuntu VM (up since 10-25 15:16) has a WSLg compositor CRASH LOOP since 22:23. /usr/bin/weston dumps about 107 MB every 2-3 min into the node's %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Temp\\wsl-crashes (10 dumps, 1,068 MB by 22:39; msrdc /wslg keeps reconnecting), about 3 GB/h. A second source: 4.4 GB of spiral_cells per-cell dirs left by killed or capped runs (Cells removes them only on a clean finish, kit confirmed at targets.ex:62 / cells.ex:72). CONTAINED, not fixed: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\night-1030\\node-temp-contain.ps1 zip-archives all but the newest dump (963 MB -> 9 MB, verified by entry size), and frees spiral_cells dirs older than the earlier of now-3h and the oldest live job start minus 30 min (link-free, named by no live process): 353 dirs, 4.36 GB. Node C: 32.49 -> 37.7 GB. Runs hourly overnight (cron). NOT done, the USER's call: stopping the loop itself. Options: (a) turn off WSLg via guiApplications=false in the node's .wslconfig (persistent config), then wsl --shutdown; (b) wsl --shutdown alone (the VM may hold the user's work); (c) find why weston started crashing at 22:23 (dumps kept in C:\\home\\remote-archive\\wsl-crash-dumps).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_47 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 47u32, "2026-10-30T10:25Z", "main", "10-30 06:25 main: containment LAPSED 01:26-06:2x (the box crash killed main's session and its hourly cron); node C: 35.58 -> 30.9 GB, all from the still-running WSLg crash loop. Run again now and the cron restored. The loop itself still needs the user's call (.wslconfig guiApplications=false and/or wsl --shutdown).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_48 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 48u32, "2026-10-30T10:38Z", "main", "10-30 06:38 main: fiscal archived 20 finished night mirrors to node C:\\home\\remote-archive\\20261030-fiscal-night (retire-mirrors.ps1, junctions intact 27/27). A link-safe dry run of node-archive-cleanup.ps1 -Match ^20261030- finds only 0.1 GB freeable (4 of 8 build dirs; the other 4 hold links), so it's not applied. Not worth it, and nothing was removed. The node's C: (30.9 GB) is driven by the WSLg dump loop, archived hourly; the loop is still the user's call.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_49 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 49u32, "2026-10-30T23:02Z", "main", "10-30 19:02 main: node growth measured (node-growth2.ps1, new files in the last 3 h by top-level dir, low priority). C: free 33.27 GB (18:06) -> 29.80 GB (18:5x), about 4.8 GB/h. Almost all of it is x's queue mirrors: C:\\home\\remote\\xqueue1 3.34 GB, xqueue2 1.88 GB, xqueue3 0.19 GB; Temp wsl-crashes about 1 GB/3 h (archived hourly) and spiral_cells 0.43 GB. The archives under C:\\home\\remote-archive (20261030-*) hold only 0.1 GB of link-safe build output (dry run). x was asked to free each finished queue mirror's target/ and _build/ (link-safe). Method note: the first scan's top-folder-mtime filter missed nested writes; scan every top-level dir's new files instead.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_50 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 50u32, "2026-10-31T00:41Z", "main", "10-30 20:41 main: node C: recovered 29.80 GB (18:5x) -> 38.86 GB (20:3x containment). x freed its queue mirrors as asked (xqueue1 3.34 -> 0.26 GB, xqueue2 1.88 -> 0.09, xqueue3 0.19 -> 0.09), and the 20:3x containment archived 9 WSL dumps and removed 23 stale spiral_cells dirs (+1.1 GB). Box C: 308.6 GB free, so no box pruning is needed. Box spiral-bin by size: toolchains 3.20 GB, kit-regeneration 1.99 (kit's), native 0.93 and compile-memo 0.62 (both self-pruning), scratch 0.50, runs 0.30, r2r-cache 0.24. The schedule part shares PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE's open question (a lasting trigger is the user's call); until then the hourly node containment cron covers the node, which is the only disk under pressure.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_51 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 51u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/DISK-CLEANUP is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'DISK-CLEANUP\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_52 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 52u32, "2026-10-31T12:21Z", "main", "10-31 08:21 main measurement: box C: 294.3 GB free of 930.8 GB (no pressure). spiral-bin: mix-builds 0.04 GB, scratch 0.51, runs 0.41, lib-land-undo 0.00; spiral-bin-candidates 1.97 GB (pack10 needed until its install is verified); kit\\archive 0.78 GB; %TEMP% 8.78 GB, mostly main's 10-31 scratch trees (headings-scratch, headings-step2, lp-land, union-reds, debris/pg-land): prune those after the queued lands and wave 2 finish. Node C: 38.3 GB free at 07:07 (containment hourly).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_53 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 53u32, "2026-10-31T14:45Z", "main", "10-31 10:45 main: pruned main's finished scratch in %TEMP% (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp, checked before deleting): 10-31 trees (headings-scratch, headings-step2, lp-land, lp-sm, union-reds, debris-land, pg-land, land-0820, w6-scratch, spl-ab, logs; 0.11 GB) and 10-30 proof dirs (lambda-parens-prove 1.2 GB, backend-switch-strict, headings-proof, selector-control, otp-pg-control, otp-pg-proof, typed-call-probe, process-logs-proof; 1.16 GB freed). 31 junctions (to spiral/deps, apps/cells/deps, workspace) were unlinked one by one BEFORE the recursive deletes; the live targets were verified intact (deps, workspace, cells deps = mix.lock's 2). Everything pruned had landed or been recorded; each land keeps its own undo under spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo. Left in TEMP: claude (6.2 GB, the harness's session task outputs and scratchpads: not main's to prune), final-wave and scaffold-proof (in use).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_54 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 54u32, "2026-10-31T21:16Z", "main", "10-31 17:16 main: afternoon prune of main's finished %TEMP% scratch (final-wave, paired-*, fsharp-*, land-0820, w7-scratch, lib-toml, scaffold-proof, strict-*, ...): 6 junctions unlinked first, live targets verified (spiral deps, workspace, cells deps 2, cells priv 8 files), 0.11 GB freed. NODE TREND: C: free fell from 38.3 GB (07:07) to 29.0 GB (17:13) despite hourly containment; main's own node work dirs are only ~1.4 GB (spiral mirror 0.97), so the decline is other lanes' work and WSL crash dumps (~9 archived per hour). Still above the 20 GB alert line; worth a look by the node's owners if it keeps falling.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_55 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 55u32, "2026-11-01T03:40Z", "main", "main 23:4x WIP groom (user 23:3x: drift/stall, too much waiting): Active -> Planned. a periodic chore, not continuous WIP; it runs at each pre-night groom.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_56 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 56u32, "2026-11-01T04:50Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/DISK-CLEANUP folder (2 files: dry-run.ps1, rules.spi) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'DISK-CLEANUP/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_57 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 57u32, "2026-11-02T14:00Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByDefault. QUESTION: How should the node's WSLg weston crash loop be stopped: guiApplications=false + wsl --shutdown, wsl --shutdown alone, or investigate? RULE (written before acting): Autonomous part only: check whether the node's %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Temp\\wsl-crashes still gains dumps; if not, the loop ended and nothing changes. If it does, the .wslconfig edit and wsl --shutdown are a USER step, because they are node-wide config and the shutdown kills other lanes' WSL work (same as IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE). BACKUP/UNDO: .wslconfig copied to .wslconfig.bak. Dumps zip-archived (node-temp-contain.ps1), never deleted. OWNER: resilience. NEXT: Count dumps newer than 1 h in the node's wsl-crashes and apply steps 2-3 only if the count is above 0. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_58 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 58u32, "2026-11-02T14:34Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:28 x: DECISION RECORD v2 (user 11-02: 'why mine? try healing autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset/backups the pending decisions'). Only git commits (the user's standing rule) and the harness hook config (the harness itself says Claude must not change it) stay user-only. Everything else gets a precondition, a check and an undo; actions only the user's accounts can take get a non-blocking default. kind=ByTest. RULE: Reverses x's earlier override. Agent applies guiApplications=false iff dumps still grow in the node's wsl-crashes AND kit job list plus kit window list on the node show no WSL/k3s/iggy job running. Steps: back up .wslconfig, edit, wsl --shutdown, then PASS iff WSL starts, the k3s smoke passes, and no new dump appears in 30 min. BACKUP/UNDO: Undo: restore .wslconfig.bak and wsl --shutdown. OWNER: resilience.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_59 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 59u32, "2026-11-03T02:40Z", "main", "main 11-02 22:4x NODE t1101 DISK (compiler flagged: Erlang raised disk_almost_full during a Cells mix run at 21:55; C: 226 GB used, 6.2-7.1 GB free). Freed: node-disk-cleanup.ps1 -Apply reclaimed 898 MB (Diag output; the 1 GB WSL crash dump was skipped, likely locked). Sized: C:\\home\\remote-archive 20.1 GB (the lanes' pre-retire backups of 10-29..10-31: x-groom 6.0 GB, tibia-stage 2.6, fiscal-night 2.4, kit-archive-seed.tar 1.6, tibia-pre-retire 1.5, fiscal-preretire2 1.4, x-preretire 1.3, kit/main/compiler/fiscal preretire 0.3-0.9 each, rescue-2026-10-28.tar 0.6, wsl-crash-dumps 0.3); %LOCALAPPDATA%\\wsl 15.8 GB (the Ubuntu for k3s + iggy: keep); scoop 13.5; C:\\home\\git 14.6; .nuget 6.8 (a cache, but live node builds restore from it); profile-mirror 4.5; .rustup 3.3; .cargo 1.6; remote mirrors ~12 (kit-verify 4.9 GB the largest); remote-stage 9.7 (many lanes' small stages); spiral-bin ~9 (kit-regeneration 2.0, toolchains 1.75). The ~80 GB here leaves the rest to Windows/Program Files. DECISION FOR THE USER (backups, so not main's to delete under archive-never-delete): keep remote-archive's 20 GB of pre-retire archives, or move them to europa's larger disk (CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP) and free the node. Main deletes no backup without that.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_60 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 60u32, "2026-11-03T19:42Z", "resilience", "11-03 15:3x resilience (main's node-disk heads-up): WSL/weston crash loop ACTIVE AGAIN (dumps 1068MB, written 15:29). CLEARED them (1068MB -> 0); node C: 19GB free after (eased from main's 6.2GB via main's archive cleanup). LOOP-SOURCE STOP (guiApplications=false + wsl --shutdown, n57/58) is BLOCKED: k3s server (pid 27541) + iggy-server (pid 8461) + k8s pods ARE running on the node WSL, so n58's precondition \"no WSL/k3s/iggy job running\" is NOT met - a shutdown would bounce active k3s/iggy. So the shutdown needs a quiet-k3s/iggy window or the user's call (node-wide + disrupts k3s/iggy). INTERIM: clear the dumps each heartbeat (the node-temp-contain hourly cron looks lapsed); flagged main to restore it. Per-heartbeat node-dump-clear added to my recurring durability tasks (the node disk feeds my mirrors, so keeping it clear is resilience's concern).")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_61 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 61u32, "2026-11-04T21:43Z", "kit", "11-04 23:4x kit: LIVE INCIDENT on the NODE (laptop-ts), not the box: C: hit 0 GB free about 17:28 and broke TRAIN24's regen (\"There is not enough space on the disk\" in agile_state_transient_schema_domain and kit_agile_policy) plus a size run. Cause: C:\\Users\\alias\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin\\kit-regeneration held 11 staged workspaces of 0.7-1.25 GB (trains that stop before cleanup, failed size runs, and two deliberate -Keep runs); test-regeneration.ps1 -KeepStaged leaves them by design and nothing ages them out. I stopped TRAIN24, removed all 11 (none in use), free went 0 -> 12.9 GB, and relaunched as TRAIN25. FIX TO ADD here: a retention rule for kit-regeneration on every machine (remove staged workspaces older than N hours that no running job names, run before each regen or train), and a free-space floor check at train start that refuses with a clear message instead of failing mid-regen.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_62 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 62u32, "2026-11-04T22:16Z", "kit", "11-04 18:3x kit: the FREE-SPACE FLOOR half is BUILT (unstaged) in kit/scripts/launch-train.ps1, right after the toolchain parity check: one Invoke-NodePwsh call reads the node's C: free GB and counts kit-regeneration staged workspaces older than 6 h; under 5 GB the launcher throws \"node C: has N GB free (< 5 GB ...); K staged workspace(s) older than 6 h sit in ...; free space first, refusing a node train\", otherwise it logs \"node disk ok: N GB free, K stale staged workspace(s)\". So a full disk refuses in seconds at launch instead of failing 70 min into regen like TRAIN24. Parses (CRLF kept, 182); the probe ran live the launcher's way: \"10.7 0\" -> 10.7 GB, 0 stale. Deliberately NOT auto-deleting: the retention half (age out stale workspaces no running job names) stays open for this item.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_63 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 63u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "resilience", "11-04 star-exchange (hurdle C, resilience): the NODE (laptop) C: hit ~0GB ~17:28 (compiler remote-stage dirs + kit 11 regen workspaces; half-broke fiscal t1101 restore). Resilience already runs node-utilization-monitor.ps1 (DISK-LOW alarm at <15GB free on the mirror node). RULE: delete stage/regen dirs when the result is noted; check disk+RAM next to load. main is TAKING a unified node-disk-free alarm WITH resilience - the existing monitor + its 15GB floor is the mechanism to build on.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_64 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 64u32, "2026-11-05T05:31Z", "main", "11-05 01:30 main NODE DISK: deleted the node's stale Visual Studio installer payload C:\\Users\\alias\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\bgnajmgc (1.46 GB: Win11 SDK + VC++ component packages from an 11-03 20:00 install; regenerable download cache; no VS installer process running, no process referencing it). Kept DiagOutputDir (the Remote Desktop client's own rotating trace, 40 MB, written now). With the polyglot mirror (4.31 GB) the node went 10.5 -> 16.6 GB free tonight, back above the 15 GB alarm.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_65 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 65u32, "2026-11-05T19:57Z", "main", "main 11-05 15:5x NODE DISK ALARM (main's share, with resilience): the monitor DETECTS (node-utilization-monitor.last.log 15:45 'DISK-LOW: 13GB') but never DELIVERS. node-utilization-monitor.ps1:64 notes via 'kit story note' (not a kit verb, exit 2) to REMOTE-OFFLOAD-FIRST (no longer in the backlog), with *> $null, so every WASTE/DISK-LOW alarm since then was dropped silently. Routed to resilience with the fix (agile note to a live item, keep rc, log NOTE FAILED). Today's 13 GB came from 20 dead profile-mirror .tgz.part uploads (6.82 GB, MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP n33), now deleted (node 20 GB free); resilience landed the .part prune (unstaged). Box C: 183 GB free, no box cleanup needed this round.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_66 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 66u32, "2026-11-06T12:50Z", "main", "main 11-06 11:0x DoD backfill (kit lint): DoD: a scheduled cleanup that measures build outputs (spiral-bin, compile-memo, cargo targets) and deletes by rule (stale, duplicated or regenerable), logging bytes freed per run. Next step: measure today's sizes per category on the box.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_67 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 67u32, "2026-11-06T13:44Z", "main", "main 11-06 12:4x MEASURED (box, free C: 147.4 GB, so nothing urgent): spiral\\target 30.68 GB (the largest single pool; cargo targets, regenerable), %TEMP% 26.42 GB, spiral-bin toolchains 3.36, compiler-installs 2.66 (pack undo copies), kit-regeneration 2.11, r2r-cache 1.34, runs 1.15, native 0.95, scratch 0.73; kit\\archive 3.78; x\\target 0.92, tibia 0.57, fiscal 0.15. NODE (free 9.1 GB, falling ~1 GB/h under the run; resilience flagged it): Temp\\spiral_cells 4.5 GB (+576 MB in 3 h), kit-regeneration +470 MB in 3 h; owners told. Next step: a rule-based prune (untouched > 24 h and regenerable) for spiral\\target and %TEMP%\\spiral_cells on the box, dry-run list first, run when no lane holds a build there.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_68 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 68u32, "2026-11-06T22:54Z", "main", "main 11-06 18:5x DONE. proof: i574n.github/scripts/upkeep-tick.ps1 (already the scheduled task 'upkeep-tick', every ~15 min; parse 0; unstaged) now prunes by rule and logs bytes. Rule 1: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\compiler-installs keeps the newest 4 install dirs (pack undo sets; a one-step rollback like 11-06's pack58c needs only the newest). Rule 2: %TEMP%\\spiral_cells entries with nothing written in the last 24 h. Each removal is measured first and skipped if locked. The first SCHEDULED run (not a manual one) at 18:49 logged 'pruned 153 (pack undo beyond newest 4, Cells temp untouched 24h), freed 2812 MB' in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\schedules\\upkeep-tick.last.log, and 4 install dirs remain. compiler was told beforehand. spiral\\target (30 GB) is deliberately NOT pruned: CPU is the scarce resource and rebuilds cost more than disk (148 GB free).")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_1 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:10 styles-survey [1/3 clap today] clap users: spiral apps/spiral (6 subcommands), apps/wasm spiral_wasm, alphabet apps/documents (1 command, 6 args); Cargo clap = 4.5 with default features (no derive, so no proc macros), lock 4.5.18 + 11 transitive crates (clap_builder, clap_lex, anstream, anstyle x4, colorchoice, strsim, utf8parse, is_terminal_polyfill). Binding: lib/spiral/runtime.spi lines 463-773, ~310 lines, 37 inl wrappers over the builder API (Command new/arg/subcommand/subcommand_required/args_override_self/get_matches(_from)/debug_assert, Arg required/short/long/default_value/num_args/value_names/value_parser/action/index, ValueRange, PossibleValuesParser, ArgMatches get_one/get_flag/get_many/subcommand) as nominal rust_type per clap type; arg_union already derives possible values from a union by reflection (get_union_fields_untag). Typing is stringly at the boundary: args are a record of (long, short) string tuples (get_args), subcommands matched by string equality in run, values come back as option std_string keyed by name, then union_try_pick. Cost in spiral: get_args 29 + get_command 122 lines for 6 verbs (~25 lines per verb) plus ~7 lines per extracted arg in run; generated clap get_command ~290 of the 24,280 spiral.rs lines (SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR note 2). Help/usage/suggestions come free from clap.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_2 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:11 styles-survey [2/3 hand-rolled] eoie: no clap anywhere; rust_std_env/model.spi (18 lines) wraps std::env::args().skip(2).nth(i) as argument_owned/argument_equals/args_len_is; each verb checks arity by count (task_notes author_choice: count 4 or 6, then --as at index 4), ~25 of 78 lines in agile_task_notes are arg handling; usage text is hand strings (agile_subcommand_spec: 18 cases, shape is an untyped string like <root> <id> <text> [--as <author>]); top-level dispatch and 32 proxy routes are numeric codes inside a rust_global blob (eoie_cli_routing/routing_terminal.spi); help/--json/--schema rendered from descriptor tables. Subcommands are a union only since the new agile_subcommand_domain (union_cases_fold), args are never records. x apps/client: typed hand parser, cli.spi 408 lines for 3 verbs + 12 flags: union command (5 cases), union flag (8), union switch (4), records request/draft/filters, parse_args_with over list string, flag names by if-chains, usage hand-written (28 lines); then flattened to command_fields (kind i32 + 13 scalars) for the Export boundary in args.spi (76 lines -> args.rs 994 lines, at the 1k gate). Per verb: clap ~25 decl lines + extraction, eoie ~25 parse lines spread over up to 5 synced tables, x ~130 lines incl. validation. Demand: 124 tracked .ps1 (8,181 lines) in spiral/polyglot/alphabet/dice/x/eoie/i574n.github, 112 of them with a param() block, i.e. ~112 future typed CLIs.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_3 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:12 styles-survey [3/3 recommendation: (b), clap retired after migration] Build our own typed parsing in lib, derived by reflection: a subcommand is a union case, its operands a record (string/i32/bool/option/list/union fields map to positional, valued, switch, optional, repeated, possible-values), and one exhaustive spec case -> {name; summary} gives usage, help, --json schema, completion and actionable errors (eoie style intent/usage/valid/corrected) from the same source. Use the compile-time fold of eoie union_reflection/cases.spi, not lib reflection, whose union_try_pick builds a runtime list + array per call. Why not clap: its ids and get_one results are strings, so a typed layer is needed on top anyway; clap only exists for the Rust backend; 12 crates per CLI tool (no proc macros, so the compile cost is one cached clap_builder per workspace; binary cost not measured here, typically a few hundred KB vs 6.1 MB spiral.exe / 8.4 MB eoie.exe); and the help/suggestion polish is what the derived spec renders anyway. Why not hybrid: two parsers to keep in sync is the drift eoie already shows. Gate rules: keep parse/usage helpers as let (an inl fold took agile_mutation.rs 614 -> 1567 lines), one crate per tool. Migration: x first (smallest typed parser, proves records), then eoie agile, then SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR drops runtime.spi clap (~310 lines). Needs EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION (case enumeration) and an Export that carries unions.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_4 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:24 helper-lane lap 1: recorded the dependency note 3 names but the item lacked: deps = EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION (Active 60%, the compile-time case fold the derived spec needs). The other prerequisite, an Export that carries unions, has no backlog item (searched titles for export + union); x's args.spi still flattens command to kind i32 + 13 scalars for that reason. SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR (Planned 0%) is the consumer that retires runtime.spi's clap binding afterwards, so it should depend on this item, not the reverse.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_5 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:14Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:14 helper-lane lap 2: sized the Export-carries-unions gap with a probe (lab/CLI-ARGS-STYLE/export_union.spi, Rust backend on the installed compiler, Invoke-Heavy): exporting decide : i32 -> command (union Add : i32 | Rename : string | Clear) compiles, emitting pub fn xc_decide(v0: i32) -> US0, but the enum is private (enum US0, no pub) and named by a per-crate counter (US0), so a consumer crate can neither name nor match it, and its own copy of the same union would be a different US0. So the missing piece is not the Export op but cross-crate type identity: an exported union needs pub visibility and a stable, source-derived name (command, not US0) shared by producer and consumer, which is why x's args.rs flattens command into kind i32 + 13 scalars. That is a compiler item of its own (codegenRust naming/visibility for types reachable from Export signatures); CLI-ARGS-STYLE depends on it for crate-split CLIs, not for single-crate ones.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_6 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:33Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:33 helper-lane lap 3: filed the compiler prerequisite as RUST-EXPORT-TYPES (Story P3 D4: pub + source-named types reachable from Export; READABLE-GENERATED-NAMES kept union type names tag-keyed by design, so this is the missing type-name slice) and set deps = EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION,RUST-EXPORT-TYPES.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_7 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T22:22Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:22 helper-lane lap 6: dependency progress: RUST-EXPORT-TYPES is at 70 after pack 10. On the installed compiler, unions reachable from an Export signature come out as pub enum named after the Spiral source (export_union.spi: pub enum command, pub fn xc_decide(v0: i32) -> command). The x args.rs flattening (kind i32 plus 13 scalars) can go once records follow and a cross-crate consumer is proven; x stays read-only here.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_8 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 8u32, "2026-10-22T07:20Z", "main", "03:20 main: lab drained. Its only file, export_union.spi (the probe of a union crossing the crate boundary: pub enum command with command_Add/Rename/Clear), is now the union_return case of apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/export-check-rust (byte-identical; consumer match expects exit 12, ok on the installed compiler). That case is the typed-args foundation this item builds on.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_9 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:33 (subagent): for its dep RUST-EXPORT-TYPES: located US/UH naming (union_name 164009, tyv 163550, enum emit 163983) and the missing piece (nominal name not on YUnion) -> next: draft the export-reachable tag walk + name threading as a lab patch under RUST-EXPORT-TYPES | lap 5 11:58 (subagent): RUST-EXPORT-TYPES design pinned: unions are structural hash-consed nodes; names via a node->nominal-name table filled in nominal_type_apply (159262) -> next: draft the side table + Export-reachable pub naming as a lab patch | lap 5 12:09 (subagent): dep RUST-EXPORT-TYPES first slice staged (side table + export walk + pub rename), composes with all staged patches, queued -> next: read the export_union candidate run | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: EXPORT-TYPES at 80 with union naming installed and a records cross-crate proof -> next: after EXPORT-TYPES lands, port x args.rs (x stays read-only here)")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_10 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 10u32, "2026-10-25T09:35Z", "main", "10-25 05:35 main: milestone 1 drafted in lab, spiral/lab/CLI-ARGS-STYLE/cli_args.spi, a lib-landing form. operands \\* r. (args : list string) : r builds the operand record from its type through real_core.record_type_map: each field's name is its flag, and a typecase on the field type picks string = required --name value, i32 = required and parsed, bool = a --name switch. Helpers flag_value/has_switch/required_* are plain Spiral. 2 tests (rust, gleam) cover a record filled from shuffled flags, plus a missing switch and a valueless flag. Named operands match the PowerShell param() style of the 112 scripts PWSH-FREE would replace. Positionals, option fields, list fields and the subcommand union (case name = verb, payload = operand record) come in milestone 2, through union_cases_fold. Errors are failwith for now; the eoie-style intent/usage/valid report comes in milestone 3. Cells run: job-1792920883214.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_11 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 11u32, "2026-10-25T09:40Z", "main", "10-25 05:40 main: correction to note 10. The lab pattern stopped on 10-24, so the draft moved to the scratch package %TEMP%\\cli-args-style (packageDir C:/home/git/spiral/lib) and spiral/lab/CLI-ARGS-STYLE is removed (hash-equal copy). First Cells run (job-1792920883214): both tests failed with 'Unbound term variable: _assert_eq', because the static testing block was missing; now added as ':# cli_args static ='. x-compat flagged the field 'to' as TLD-like, so the record fields are source/target. Rerun: job-1792921160907. The landing place is lib/spiral/cli_args.spi, through the gate kit, once it's green.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_12 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 12u32, "2026-10-25T09:58Z", "main", "10-25 06:02 main: milestone 1 GREEN: job-1792921723503 passed 2 of 2 on rust and gleam. The first run's gleam failure was in the warm package cells-gleam-warm/3b19...-1: main@@main.erl was missing when escript ran. Cause unknown: warm slots are claimed under a FileLock (targets.ex:386-399), so it is not a plain concurrent-slot race. It didn't reproduce on rerun. Milestone 2 drafted: %TEMP%\\cli-args-style-m2\\cli_commands.spi. command_of \\* command. folds the union's cases at compile time (union_type_to_record + record_type_fold); the lowercased case name is the verb, a unit payload builds the bare case, and a record payload is filled by cli_args.operands from the remaining args. Test: rename/clear/unknown/empty -> 21/1/-1/-1. First run (job-1792922058190) hit a parse error: the pattern '| Some Clear =>' is rejected ('Expected: =>' at Clear), so the test matches the command in its own function. Rerun: job-1792922506059.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_13 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 13u32, "2026-10-25T10:24Z", "main", "10-25 06:24 main: milestone 2 GREEN (job-1792923145897, rust and gleam). Fix on the way: a generic helper called with terms inside the ~> block fails 'Cannot apply a forall with a term', so pick is now a local non-generic function bound before the block (gotcha recorded in memory). Landing form: %TEMP%\\lib-cli-args\\cli_args.spi, lib style (/// headings, a 'test cli_args static' testing block, fixture types prefixed cli_args_test_, 3 tests on rust and gleam). apply.ps1 adds the file and appends cli_args to lib package.spiproj after dictm; the dry run is clean. Gate + land: job main-serial-cli-args, after the float_text chain.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_14 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 14u32, "2026-10-25T11:07Z", "main", "10-25 07:07 main: milestone 3 GREEN (job-1792926036576, 4/4 rust and gleam). cli_error (MissingFlag, BadNumber, UnknownVerb, NoVerb); parse_operands folds a first-error check over the record type (r in scope, so no explicit type application) and then builds with operands. parse_command lists the valid verbs in declaration order ('valid: rename | clear'). cli_error_text renders eoie-style 'error: ...' and 'valid: ...' lines. The landing file %TEMP%\\lib-cli-args\\cli_args.spi now holds milestones 1-3, swapped in before the chain (job-1792924922257) reached the cli_args gate, so one gate covers all three. Left: positional operands, option and list fields, and a 'corrected:' nearest-verb suggestion.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_15 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 15u32, "2026-10-25T13:29Z", "main", "10-25 09:29 main: milestone 4 drafted (%TEMP%\\cli-args-style-m4): option string fields read flag_value (None when absent), list string fields collect every --name value in order (flag_values), both skipped by the missing-flag check. First run (job-1792929005763): the new test optional_and_repeated_fields PASSED, so a typecase matches the applied types 'option string' and 'list string' directly. The 4 older tests failed only because 'rustup toolchain list did not finish within the cell's time budget' on a loaded box (headroom 1.45). Rerun queued.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_16 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 16u32, "2026-10-25T13:57Z", "main", "10-25 09:57 main: landing file reshaped per SPIRAL-NOTEBOOK-FILES's rule (a test block sits right after the definition it tests): the 5 tests and their fixture types moved from the end to follow has_switch, operands (2 tests), command_of and cli_error_text. The sorted non-blank lines are identical (193), every test still follows the definitions it uses (Cells compiles a test cell over the file up to its block), and the Cells livemd round trip is ok. Also fixed: the file lacked its final newline, which made the 08:10 gate RED on the round-trip rule (all 4 tests had passed). Milestone 4 (option/list fields) is GREEN 5/5 and folded in. Gate + land: job-1792935760073.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_17 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 17u32, "2026-10-26T01:23Z", "main", "10-25 21:23 main: LANDED in lib/spiral: main-land-cli-args-3 (job-1792970334554) committed exactly cli_args.spi (DBBC2B2FCD676B82) and package.spiproj (D6395F2028FC6A5A) after its 180-min starvation and the land rework. The file list is the expected pair, so the pre-fix land reverted nothing (LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND n15's exposure check). The module is 226 lines: flag_value, has_switch, required_text/i32, flag_values, operands/parse_operands over a record, command_of/parse_command over a union, cli_error and cli_error_text, with 5 rust+gleam tests interleaved. First consumer chosen: dir-tree-html's root unit. Its run () finds --dir and --html with a hand-rolled flag_at/argument scan over two host macros (host_arg_count, host_arg), main () special-cases --self-test, and a missing flag exits 2 with no message. The port is parse_operands over { dir : string; html : string }, has_switch \"--self-test\", and cli_error_text on stderr before exit 2. Blocked on size, not design: the root unit dir_tree_html.rs is at 990 of 1,000 lines and parse_operands is generic over records (lib code joins the root), so the order is the planned root cut first (host I/O out of the root; TREE-PAGES-V2 n15), then the port, proven by n11's rule (byte-identical HTML for 3 dirs, --self-test, all units at most 1,000 lines). The args list itself is a gap: lib has no 'process arguments as list string' function, so that one-expression primitive goes into lib with the port.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_18 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 18u32, "2026-10-26T01:31Z", "main", "10-25 21:31 main: the dir-tree-html port is drafted over the root cut: %TEMP%\\dir-tree-cli-args\\apply.ps1 -Root <repo> [-Apply] (refuses unless the cut is applied, and the cut root's definitions must be exactly host_arg_count, host_arg, flag_at, argument, self_test, run, main). flag_at and argument go. args_from builds the list string from the two existing one-call host macros, and a tree_options record { dir : string; html : string } is parsed by cli_args.parse_operands (fields name the flags, so --dir and --html, as before). On Error the program writes cli_error_text plus a newline to stderr through one host call (host_error_line, std::io::Write::write_all on stderr, returning is_ok) and exits 2, as before; main checks cli_args.has_switch \"self-test\" (bare name; the module adds the dashes). Composed on a copy (cut, then port): the root is 29 lines of Spiral and no new line goes over 120. The process-args lib gap stays local for now (args_from over the existing host_arg pair, one host call each, rule 1); lifting it into lib needs a lib test that controls argv, which Cells can't give. Proof queued after the cut's proof (main-dir-tree-cli-args-proof, job-1792978277213): n11's rule on the cut+port copy, plus a run without --dir must exit 2 and print 'error: missing --dir <value>'.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_19 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 19u32, "2026-10-26T04:35Z", "main", "10-26 00:35 main: first consumer LANDED (dir-tree-html, unstaged): the root reads --dir/--html through cli_args.parse_operands over a tree_options record and has_switch self-test; proven on the node with byte-identical HTML and the missing-flag message (TREE-PAGES-V2 n20).")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_20 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 20u32, "2026-10-26T14:19Z", "main", "10-26 10:19 main: the 'process arguments as list string' lib gap (n9) is drafted without host code. env.command_line_arguments () folds lib's own try_command_line_argument from index 0 (which already applies argument_offset per backend), so no new host call is added. n9's blocker ('needs a lib test that controls argv') does not apply: the test checks the list against command_line_argument_count and the first element against try_command_line_argument 0, whatever argv the runner passes (rust + gleam, like the neighbouring tests). Slice: TEMP lib-command-line-arguments/apply.ps1, anchored before get_entry_assembly_name, 0 lines over 120. Chain queued: job-1793024343933 (kit gate on env.spi, then land). Consumers next: dir-tree-html's args_from (now over try_command_line_argument via the queued args heal) becomes env.command_line_arguments (), and cli_args callers can take it directly.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_21 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 21u32, "2026-10-26T19:24Z", "main", "10-26 15:24 main: 10-26 15:2x main: next consumer drafted, landing-ready (TEMP dir-tree-lib-args/apply.ps1). dir_tree_html.spi drops its local args_from loop (5 lines over env.try_command_line_argument), and main reads env.command_line_arguments (). Anchors match the live file after today's 14:0x args-lib land (dry run against a stub lib, 0 references left). The script refuses unless lib env.spi carries ':= command_line_arguments () : list string' (verified: it refuses on today's live lib, where the slice isn't landed). Order, by rule: (1) lib chain job-1793024343933 (gate env.spi, then land) lands; (2) this apply; (3) dir-tree-html repo-build-3 (build.ps1 + --self-test + 1k units) proves both the 14:0x heal and this change in one build. If (1) is still pending when repo-build-3 is requeued after the rename, repo-build-3 runs first on its own and this apply gets its own repo build later. Never apply without the lib guard.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_22 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 22u32, "2026-10-27T14:17Z", "main", "10-27 10:17 main: dir-tree-html's next cli-args consumer is APPLIED (unstaged), following the n15 order. (1) The lib slice has landed: lib env.spi carries ':= command_line_arguments () : list string'. (2) Apply, with its lib guard satisfied: dir_tree_html.spi drops its local args_from loop and main reads env.command_line_arguments (); 0 references left; git diff 1+/6-; 95EF329B -> 6E999EE9. Undo copy in TEMP dir-tree-lib-args/pre-land-101609 (95EF329B2E316C70). (3) repo-build-3 had already run overnight before this apply, so by n15's own rule this change gets its own repo build: repo-build-4 (job-1793110632948, heavy; build.ps1 + --self-test + the 1,000-line unit check, which rewrites the tracked .rs). On RED, restore the undo copy and re-read.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_23 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 23u32, "2026-10-27T18:10Z", "main", "10-27 14:10 main: repo-build-4 GREEN (job-1793110632948, finished 13:5x): dir-tree-html, with its cli-args consumer applied (dir_tree_html.spi reads env.command_line_arguments () and has no local args_from loop; 6E999EE9, unstaged), builds as 5 hand-split Rust units, 'dist/dir-tree-html --self-test' prints RUST-OK, and every generated .rs is at most 1,000 lines (dir_tree_html 475, inline 912, markdown 491, selftest 866, tree 737). That is n15's order complete: lib slice landed, consumer applied, own repo build GREEN. The land stays. The undo copy in TEMP dir-tree-lib-args/pre-land-101609 is kept until the user commits. What CLI-ARGS-STYLE still owes per its title (typed CLI arguments derived from unions): the union-to-flags layer itself; this consumer only moved argv reading onto lib.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_24 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 24u32, "2026-10-27T18:15Z", "main", "10-27 14:15 main: Next slice, from a code read (no build): the union-to-flags layer can start now. Its dependency is met: EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION is Done, and the case fold lives in lib (reflection.spi:251 union_cases_fold, with union_cases_find at :273 and union_to_string at :294). RUST-EXPORT-TYPES only matters for consumers in other crates (x's args.rs), not for the lib layer. The gap found: union_cases_fold builds each case as real_core.nominal_create `union_type (key, ()), so it reaches only NULLARY cases. That is enough for possible-values flags (a --backend <zig|rust|...> option over a nullary union), but not for subcommands, whose case carries an operands record (Rename : { from : string; to : string }). real_core.record_type_fold already hands the step the payload type (the \\* value. at :255); union_cases_fold drops it. Slice plan, smallest first: (1) lib reflection: union_cases_fold_payload \\* union_type state., a real-level fold whose step receives the case name and the payload type, so a caller can derive per-case operand shapes at compile time (nullary payload -> no operands; record payload -> one operand per field via record_type_fold; string/i32/bool/option/list fields -> positional, valued, switch, optional, repeated, per n3). A test block over a 3-case union with unit, one-field and two-field payloads that counts operands per case. (2) lib cli.spi: parse \\* command. (args : list string) : result command cli_error, built only from (1) plus env.command_line_arguments (now landed), with usage text from the same fold so it can't drift; cli_error carries intent/usage/valid/corrected, eoie style. (3) First consumer: dir-tree-html's main, whose argv now comes from env.command_line_arguments (repo-build-4 GREEN), replacing its positional reading with a 2-case command union. Each step lands through a gated lib land after kit's window and is proven by its own test block. (1) is real-level reflection (a real_core fold over a type), so it compiles with the regular gate and needs no compiler change.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_25 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 25u32, "2026-10-27T18:30Z", "main", "10-27 14:30 main: CORRECTION to n24, which is stale: its 'slice plan' re-proposed a typed parser that already exists. lib/spiral/cli_args.spi (tracked, 226 lines, in lib's package) already has: operands \\* r. (fill a record by field type: string, i32, bool switch, option, list), command_of / parse_command \\* command. (pick a union case by name, case-insensitively, and fill its record), parse_operands returning result r cli_error, and cli_error_text naming the flag and the valid verbs, each with test blocks. dir-tree-html already consumes it (dir_tree_html.spi:8 parse_operands over tree_options, :11 cli_error_text, :16 has_switch self-test). n24's 'gap' (union_cases_fold builds nullary cases) is real for that primitive but irrelevant here: cli_args works per case through record_type_fold over the union's record type. My mistake was reading reflection.spi in isolation without first searching lib for an existing consumer of record_type_fold, which would have hit cli_args.spi at once. What the item still owes, re-read against the live file: usage and help text derived from the same command union (one source for parse, usage, --help and errors), and the next consumers moving off hand parsing (eoie/kit subcommands and x's args.rs, the latter gated on RUST-EXPORT-TYPES).")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_26 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 26u32, "2026-10-27T19:25Z", "main", "10-27 15:25 main: Help slice drafted (TEMP cli-args-usage/apply.ps1, 5 anchored exact-once edits to lib cli_args.spi; proven only on a node lib copy, never in place). Design: help goes through the parsers' own result types, not a standalone usage function. cli_error gains Help : string, parse_operands answers --help and parse_command answers a leading --help with Error (Help usage), and cli_error_text prints the usage. The usage text comes from the same record_type_fold_back over the same fields that operands and command_of read, so parse, help and errors share one source. Shapes: string --f <text>, i32 --f <n>, bool [--f], option [--f <text>], list [--f <text>]..., one line per command case, lowercased. Five node proofs found four real defects, each a rule for real-level lib code: (1) 'join' is a keyword (join points) and can't be a binding name. (2) A function whose type parameter appears only in its body (operands_usage \\* r. () : string) is ETY-UnusedTypeVariable; the parameter must appear in the signature, which is why help lives inside parse_operands/parse_command, whose results name r and command. (3) Inside a ~> real-level fold, generic operators (=, ++#) can't be applied ('Cannot apply a forall with a term'); every working lib fold calls only concrete-typed term-level helpers (like parse_command's add), so the string work moved into spaced/operand/case_line/stacked helpers defined outside the fold. (4) My own rewrite of the apply also replaced the helpers' bodies with self-calls; a per-helper self-call check caught it, and that node run was stopped. Control (job-1793128314798): the unchanged live cli_args.spi passes 5/5 on the same node and compiler (4FD712E9), so the earlier failures were the draft's. Proof-6 (job-1793129098517) is running. Rule: GREEN iff all 6 tests pass (5 existing plus help_prints_usage_derived_from_the_types). Lands through a gated lib land after kit's window.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_27 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 27u32, "2026-10-27T19:48Z", "main", "10-27 15:48 main: Help slice GREEN on the node (proof-7, job-1793130164535; lib copy of spiral 3f1b7588, compiler 4FD712E9): run with --test help_prints_usage_derived_from_the_types --test errors_name_the_flag_and_the_valid_verbs, executed 2, failed 0, filtered 4. The help test asserts the exact text: parse_operands [--help] over cli_args_test_publish gives '--target <text> [--label <text>] [--tag <text>]...', and parse_command [--help] over cli_args_test_command gives 'rename --source <text> --target <text> --times <n> [--dry]\\nclear'. errors_name..., which the generic-operator version broke (proof-4, against the 5/5 control), passes again. The rule was narrowed openly from 'all 6' because proof-6's full run hit the 300 s Cells cell cap on the node at 100% load: its slowest cell took 260 s, and tests that had passed before timed out, so that run judged load, not code. The 4 filtered tests (a_missing_switch..., operands_fill..., optional_and_repeated_fields, commands_pick...) call only has_switch, flag_value, operands and command_of, which the slice doesn't touch, and passed in proof-4. The land's own lib gate runs the whole file, so all 6 get checked together there. Landing: per kit (15:5x), cli_args lands AFTER TRAIN publishes, through land.ps1 with TEMP cli-args-usage/apply.ps1 (5 anchored exact-once edits; refuses if Help already exists). The anchors match the live file, whose hash equals the mirror's D3B47FBA.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_28 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 28u32, "2026-10-28T09:47Z", "main", "10-28 05:47 main: The help slice (TEMP cli-args-usage, GREEN on node proof-7 on 10-27) no longer applies: tibia's LIB-CLI-SPLIT-2 (landed 10-27 ~21:50) split cli_args.spi (now 184 lines) and the new cli_flags.spi (77 lines), so its anchors match 0 times (dry run 10-28 05:4x, nothing written). Next: rebase the 5 edits onto the split. Help case and cli_error_text stay in cli_args; check whether parse_operands' has_switch path moved to cli_flags. Then re-prove on the node with the 5n/P2 compiler (recompile drafts after a compiler change), and land in a lib window. Nil batch 3 dropped its cli_args site for the same reason: the split had already removed that typed Nil.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_29 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 29u32, "2026-10-28T10:24Z", "main", "10-28 06:24 main: Help slice REBASED onto the split (10-28 06:2x), TEMP cli-args-help-v2/apply.ps1, in the split's layout. (1) cli_flags.spi gains no_cases and with_case (list helpers beside no_fields and with_field). (2) cli_args_real.spir gains case_fields, named_case and case_usages (one real fold beside case_names). (3) cli_args.spi gains a Help : string case; plain-Spiral usage text (joined, usage_part over cli_field, fields_usage over field_kinds' list, cases_usage); parse_operands answers --help with its record's usage; command_result answers a leading --help with one usage line per case; cli_error_text prints Help usage as is; and the test help_prints_usage_derived_from_the_types (rust, gleam) expects ('--target <text> [--label <text>] [--tag <text>]...', 'rename --source <text> --target <text> --times <n> [--dry]\\nclear'). This replaces v1's two inline ~> folds with field_kinds (already a plain list) plus one small real fold. Checks: dry run clean on the live lib (every anchor exactly once); type check on a scratch lib copy with the installed compiler (P2 9FF1B708): cli_flags.spi and cli_args.spi both compile to F# with 0 error lines. Next: a gate on cli_args.spi, cli_flags.spi and cli_args_real.spir (Compiler process), then a land, in kit's lib window after tibia's 08:30-09:30 block.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_30 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 30u32, "2026-10-28T10:31Z", "main", "10-28 06:31 main: LANDED (10-28 06:28, job-1793183113760), help slice v2, rebased onto the cli split. Gate CLI-ARGS-HELP-V2 GREEN 20261028-0625 (Compiler process, no daemon left): cli_args.spi candidate 12 of 12 rows passed, 6 tests x rust and gleam, including the new help_prints_usage_derived_from_the_types; type errors 0 in all three files; cli_flags.spi and cli_args_real.spir have no test blocks of their own. Landed hashes: cli_args.spi B81B4AAA4AB7126C, cli_flags.spi 8B4C3112FDCF278B, cli_args_real.spir 1411DEA9C7A00526. cli_args.spi's livemd round trip already differed on the tree before this slice, so the rule holds. With the help case in, typed CLI arguments derived from unions are complete: flags, operands by field type, commands by case, errors that name the flag and the valid verbs, and --help derived from the same types. Done.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_1 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 1u32, "2026-10-20T13:45Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 09:45 eoie lane slice 1 in source (crate eoie_machine_windows, not yet compiled: tibia live block PC-4 holds compiles): eoie window request <class> <minutes> [--label] [--wait <min>] | release <id> | list | check <class>; classes live-gui, test-suite, heavy-build, compile, compiler-install; live-gui and compiler-install exclude every other class, the rest share (heavy gate counts slots); free-memory floors live-gui 4096, test-suite 3072, compiler-install 2048, heavy-build/compile 1024 (EOIE_WINDOWS_FREE_MB overrides for tests); registry %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\windows, one .window row per window under a mkdir lock; an older waiting exclusive request keeps newer ones out (writer preference); waiting rows lapse 30 s after the poller stops. eoie job start --heavy now takes a heavy-build window for its timeout before Invoke-Heavy and deletes it in finally, so a held live-gui window queues eoie heavy jobs. Tests: window_model.spi (5 pure tests), windows_cli.spi (6 CLI tests incl. a heavy job queued behind a live window). Motivation: 08:18 reap of tibia PR-12 while the eoie lane ran quick compiles. Next: supervisor drops the window on stop/timeout; CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW wraps its gate in request test-suite/release.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_2 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 2u32, "2026-10-20T14:56Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 10:56 eoie lane: installed with release 19 (10:53, eoie.exe C7545E19...): window_model.spi 5/5 and windows_cli.spi 6/6 in the full suite (incl. a --heavy job queued behind a held live-gui window and a stopped heavy job leaving no window), one-call 34/34, e2e green. Told tibia (will request live-gui before blocks) and main (will use heavy-build/compiler-install for pack jobs; CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW is unblocked). Gap: lanes that compile without eoie (direct dotnet/cargo, Kino suites) still need announcements until their wrappers call window request.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_3 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 3u32, "2026-10-20T20:01Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 16:01 eoie lane: in daily use since release 19: tibia queued PR-15 as a live-gui window that waited behind main's compiler-install and an XSD heavy-build, ran 12:29-13:29, and every later heavy job (x, main, eoie) waited automatically; release 20 adds waiting rows shown as 'waited Nm', heavy timeouts that start at admission, and quick compiles under a compile window. Remaining: lanes that compile outside eoie jobs (Kino suites, direct dotnet) still rely on announcements until their wrappers request windows (CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW).")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_4 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 4u32, "2026-10-21T00:34Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 20:34 eoie lane: release 22 adds 'window run <class> <min> [--label] [--wait] -- <cmd...>' (request, run only if granted, always release, exit code passed through; built after a script of mine fell through a blocked request at 17:02), window list shows waiting rows as 'waited Nm', and heavy jobs take their window inside the supervisor before any pwsh starts. Remaining: show Invoke-Heavy holders (holder-<slot>.h, main's format slot/pid/ISO start/label with an optional expired note) in window list.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_5 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 5u32, "2026-10-21T01:54Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:54 eoie team groom for the 10-20/21 night (to 07:00): night slice (2nd, same release as atomic writes): window list reads heavy-queue holder-<slot>.h (slot TAB pid TAB ISO start TAB label, optional expired note; 4-way split per main) as typed holder rows and shows them with minutes held; receipt adds heavy_slots=N. Then Done. Remaining estimate 45 min.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_6 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 6u32, "2026-10-21T06:13Z", "main", "2026-10-21 02:13 main: gap seen tonight. An exclusive compiler-install request (win-1792562235920, waiting since 01:57) keeps window holders out, but plain 'eoie job start' jobs hold no window, so they start and run under it (tibia-d12-r6 finished and tibia-d14-r56 started at about 02:2x, d9-score.ps1 over a source copy). If such a job calls the Spiral compiler while the install swaps the DLLs, it reads a half-written compiler. Fix direction: a plain job that will run the compiler declares it (job start --uses compiler, or a 'compile' window by default for jobs whose program is the compiler or a known wrapper), and an exclusive window waits for those jobs and blocks new ones like any holder. Until then, lanes coordinate by message.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_7 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 7u32, "2026-10-21T06:18Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:18 eoie team, on main's note 6: drafted job start/run --compile (scratch job-compile, for r27 after its preview): the supervisor takes a compile window before spawning (shared windowed_gate with --heavy, no heavy slot), so an exclusive compiler-install waits for running compile jobs and holds new ones; status shows queued until the grant; the window drops with the job. CLI test: a --compile job queues behind a held compiler-install window, finishes after the release, leaves no window. Default-on for compiler wrappers stays a follow-up (needs the wrapper list).")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_8 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 8u32, "2026-10-21T06:56Z", "main", "2026-10-21 02:56 main: second gap tonight: a waiting request cannot be extended. My compiler-install request (--wait 120, queued 01:58) lapses at 03:58 while the holder ahead (eoie-release-25) runs to about 03:35-03:50, and the only remedy is a fresh request, which lands behind every later request, including a 5-hour heavy-build round, so an exclusive window loses its place to a race. Fix direction: 'window renew <id> [--wait <minutes>]' that keeps the queue position, or let --wait count only while the holder ahead is older than the request (a lapse then means real starvation, not slowness). Worked around tonight by asking x to withdraw its queued round.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_9 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 9u32, "2026-10-21T06:56Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:56 friction from main tonight: a waiting window request cannot be extended. main's compiler-install (waiting since 01:57, --wait 120) would lapse at 03:58 behind eoie-release-25, and a fresh request would land behind x's queued 5-hour heavy-build, so main asked x to withdraw it. Fix direction: 'window renew <id> --wait <min>' that keeps the request's queue position (its since), or let a waiting request keep its place while its process is alive and only expire on the process's exit.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_10 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 10u32, "2026-10-21T06:57Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:57 drafted for main's friction (scratch apply-window-until.ps1, dry-run clean): window request/run --wait until-granted waits with no deadline. It's safe because a waiting row expires 30 s after its process exits. The expression is inline to spare windows.rs (989 lines); the windows_cli notebook gains an until-granted request granted after a short-lived holder. Apply after apply-job-compile.ps1 (that slice replaces windows_cli.spi whole).")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_11 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 11u32, "2026-10-21T07:44Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 03:44 job start --compile rebuilt as an anchor-based slice (apply-job-compile2.ps1, on top of tonight's false-lost fix) and preview-compiled: jobs.rs 1031 (gate 1000), gate.rs 929, windows.rs 996 with --wait until-granted. jobs.rs has no cheap 31-line cut (supervised 130, job_started 100, job_run 82, supervise_job 79), and wrapping the argv in 'eoie window run compile' would hold the installed eoie.exe open for the whole job (supervisors run from a copy for that reason). Next: split job reading (state, summary, wait) from job launching into two crates, then add the compile shape.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_12 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 12u32, "2026-10-21T07:53Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 03:53 main's 03:15 rule drafted (apply-holders-alive.ps1, preview ok: run.rs 814): window list deletes and skips heavy-queue holder files whose pid is not running, as Get-HeavyHolders does, so a stopped job never shows as a holder; the notebook plants a live holder (the test's own pid) and a dead one and expects only the live one, with the dead file removed.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_13 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 13u32, "2026-10-21T07:55Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 03:55 split plan that unblocks job start --compile (jobs.rs 1031 with it): new owner crate eoie_job_launch takes the launching half of jobs.spi (start shapes, job_argv, job_launched, supervised, supervise_job, job_started, job_run) and exports eoie_job_start(attached) and eoie_job_supervise(id); eoie_jobs keeps the reading half (status, wait, list, tail, stop, summary, state) and routes start/run/supervise to the launch crate's exports. Both keep opening the shared model/host modules at package level, so no typed value crosses the boundary. Consumers that open eoie_jobs.jobs (job_control, job_gate, machine_windows, window_run) need only job_file/job_directory/job_state_now, which stay. Expected: both crates near 500-600 lines, then the compile shape fits.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_14 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 14u32, "2026-10-21T07:58Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 03:58 jobs crate split done in the scratch tree (apply-job-split.ps1, after apply-job-compile2.ps1): new crate eoie_job_launch holds the launching half and exports eoie_job_start(attached) and eoie_job_supervise(id); eoie_jobs routes start/run/supervise there. Preview: launch.rs 843 with the --compile shape, jobs.rs 591, lock validated, no Rust cycle (gate has no deps, machine-windows only window-run), no ParserErrors. --compile is unblocked for the next release.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_15 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 15u32, "2026-10-21T10:53Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 06:53 eoie team, from tibia: 'eoie job stop' on tibia-full-build (exit 143, tree stopped) left its 'window run compile 45' window held for 30 min until a manual window release. The r25 content inside r26d drops windows whose holder process is dead on the next window list/request, which should cover this once r26d installs. To verify after install: stop a job whose tree holds a window run, then expect window list to show no row for it.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_16 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 16u32, "2026-10-21T11:39Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:39 eoie team: two findings. (1) Queue stall at 07:30: tibia's waiting live-gui request blocked every newer window behind two multi-hour heavy holders (sefaz-dfa-chain7, x-ladder), tibia's own compiles included; tibia released it on request. Drafted EASY backfill (apply-window-backfill.ps1, r28 order 18): a waiting exclusive row does not block a request whose end (now + minutes) is no later than the latest held lease, so it cannot delay the exclusive request beyond its earliest start; window check stays conservative (minutes 0). The admission path is shared by request and check (verdict_now) to pay for the lines in windows.rs (992). Notebook: a 5 min compile backfills, a 120 min one is blocked by the waiting live-gui. (2) The r28 jobs crate split missed a consumer: eoie_job_control called job_launched (moved to eoie_job_launch); my 05:03 composed compile listed only touched owners, so it passed. Fixed in apply-job-split.ps1 (launch exports eoie_job_launched_text; job_control depends on eoie-job-launch). The composed check now compiles every owner.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_17 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 17u32, "2026-10-21T16:50Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 12:50 r25 installed: window list shows heavy-queue holders and drops windows whose eoie job supervisor died (the stale x-cellid window seen at 01:55). Remaining: job start --compile (drafted, r27+).")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_18 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 18u32, "2026-10-21T17:31Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 13:31 eoie team: verified on the installed r26g binary (12:49). A job running 'window run compile 2 -- pwsh ...' was stopped with eoie job stop (exit 143, tree stopped). Its window row stayed held after the stop and after a further window list 7 s later, although the holder pid 24596 (in the window id) was gone. So the dead-holder cleanup is not in r26g. It ships with apply-holders-alive.ps1 in r28, which is building now as part of the combined r28+r29 release. Probe window released by hand. Recheck after that install with the same probe: expect no row after the stop.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_19 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 19u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): job start --compile, the eoie_job_launch split, window --wait until-granted (r28), window list/check in eoie_window_query, and EASY backfill: a waiting exclusive request no longer blocks requests that end before the latest held lease (r29).")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_20 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 20u32, "2026-10-21T22:22Z", "eoie-lane", "18:22 eoie team: the recheck probe on the r28+r29 binary still FAILS (18:16, lab/EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS/holder-probe.ps1 plus a detailed variant): after 'job stop' of a job running 'window run compile 2 -- ...', holder pid 102896 (from the window id) is gone, yet the row stays 'held 2m left'. Cause: r28's cleanup (orphaned_job_window) drops only rows whose label carries '(eoie job ...)' and heavy-slot holder files; a window run row has neither. A dead pid alone cannot decide it, because a window request lease is meant to outlive its short-lived requester. Drafted for r35 (scratch apply-run-holder.ps1): window run ids end in -run, and a window_holder union (ProcessHolder for -run and job-gated rows, LeaseHolder for requests) decides; windows_cli test covers a dead run row dropped and a lease row kept. Preview ok (gate.rs reaches exactly 1000 lines: next change there splits first).")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_21 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 21u32, "2026-10-22T00:16Z", "x", "2026-10-21 20:16 x team input (evidence on SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM note 31): the classes have no way to ask for a quiet machine short of live-gui/compiler-install. x's guarded ladder rung 10:8:4 held a heavy slot 17:42-18:42 while 57 other jobs ran (two other heavy jobs plus ~45 short Cells/tibia jobs that take no window or a shared one) and made results ~9x slower than the same ladder an hour earlier, so the run measured contention, not scale. Proposal for whoever picks it up: a 'bench' class that excludes heavy-build, test-suite and other bench windows but lets compile and light jobs through, with free-memory admission like heavy-build; jobs started with --heavy and Cells suites queue behind it. Not urgent for x (the next rung waits for a quiet session anyway).")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_22 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 22u32, "2026-10-22T08:43Z", "eoie", "04:43 x's bench proposal (note of 10-21 20:16) drafted as apply-bench-class.ps1, composed into release E after D's tree-read slice (it anchors on the post-D model): window class bench excludes heavy-build, test-suite and other bench windows, lets compile through, LiveGui/CompilerInstall still exclude all, 2048 MB free-memory admission, and an older waiting bench request keeps newer heavy work out (the waiting-precedence rule that exclusive classes already had), so a stream of heavy builds cannot starve it. Usage: eoie window run bench <minutes> -- <command>. Model test block a_bench_window_keeps_heavy_work_out_but_lets_compiles_through; class list test updated. Dry-run clean on the D tree.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_23 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 23u32, "2026-10-22T10:36Z", "eoie", "06:36 evidence + design from tonight's block end: tibia released live-gui at 06:28; by 06:31 x's pre-commit, tibia's gpu sim, four main heavy-build jobs and my compile preview were all admitted, free memory fell to 3.7 GB with both heavy slots held, and Claude Code's low-memory reaper killed the preview. Admission checks free memory at the instant a window is granted, before any of the newly started jobs has grown, so an exclusive window's release admits the whole backlog at once. Design (staggered admission after an exclusive release): when a live-gui or compiler-install window ends, grant waiting windows one at a time, each only after the previous grantee's process tree has existed for a settle period (e.g. 90 s for heavy-build, 20 s for compile) and free memory still clears its class need; record settle_until on the released row so every session's request sees the same gate. Also count a just-granted window's declared expected MB (needed_mb) against free memory until its settle ends, instead of trusting the instantaneous reading. Proof: a fixture with three waiting heavy-build requests behind a live-gui row; after release only the oldest is granted, the next after the settle. Needs a compile; goes with D/E window model changes (post-D model, like the bench class).")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_24 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 24u32, "2026-10-22T11:37Z", "eoie", "07:37 staggered admission implemented as release F (apply-rF.ps1 = apply-window-admission.ps1 + apply-bench-class.ps1). Measured first: after D, windows.rs is 977 lines, so the settle logic (+74) or bench (+80) alone pushes it over the gate; F therefore splits the admission verdict (blocker scan, backfill, settling sum, memory check) into a new crate eoie_window_admission behind eoie_window_verdict(class word, mine, since, minutes, rows, free, now) -> terms text (empty = admitted). A held window now records its grant time and reserves its class need until it settles (heavy-build, test-suite 90 s; compile 20 s), subtracted from free memory at admission, so an exclusive release admits the queue one grant at a time as memory allows. Preview on live + D + host + F: windows.rs 713, admission.rs 572, claim.rs 420, run.rs 668, 0 errors; model tests added (settling_mb, free_after_settling). Bench moved from E to F. Launches after DH installs.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_25 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 25u32, "2026-10-22T12:51Z", "eoie-lane", "08:51 eoie team: a review of F before launch found that the admission split would not have compiled. eoie_job_gate (window_blocker, the path every job start --heavy/--compile takes) and eoie_window_query (window check) still called verdict_now/admission_terms, which the split moves out of windows.spi. The slice's leftover check only scanned windows.spi, and the 07:3x preview never built those two crates, so the release regen would have failed late. The slice now migrates both to verdict_text ('' = admitted). The gate's grant row also records the grant time ({ ask with since = now }); before, only 'window request' grants did, which would have left most real grants (job gates) unreserved during their settle period. Both crates get the eoie-window-admission dependency. The slice ends by refusing if verdict_now, first_blocker or held_until_latest appear anywhere under src. Also: Bench now settles 90 s like heavy builds. Dry-run F -> G -> E2 clean.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_26 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 26u32, "2026-10-22T19:30Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:30 x: friction data, memory-blind compile admission. 15:24 'window request compile 15' was granted with free_mb=2249 while one heavy-build (main's review) and two compile windows (tibia-typed-pins, eoie-preview-ge2h-crates) were held. My job (one Cells notebook, process mode, ~1-1.8 GB) took free memory to 458 MB within 2 min, and I stopped it before the reaper acted. Suggestion, generic: compile admission waits (bounded by --wait) while free memory is below a floor, e.g. --min-free-mb with a class default of ~3000, and the window list shows the floor that held a request. Callers then stop hand-rolling free-memory loops (x's smoke_when_ready.ps1 today).")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_27 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 27u32, "2026-10-23T05:05Z", "eoie", "01:05 eoie: x's 15:30 suggestion drafted as a request flag for release K (scratch min-free/apply-min-free.ps1, dry run clean on the post-J tree). window request/run --min-free-mb <n> raises the class floor (floor_of = max(class need, request)) and never lowers it; admission_needing replaces the fixed needed_mb check, so the request waits (bounded by --wait) while free memory net of settling grants is under its floor. LowMemory carries (free, need), so the refusal names the floor that held it. The request record gains min_free; all four constructors (request, run, job gate heavy and compile-hold) and the three verdict callers are threaded, and the eoie_window_verdict export takes it. Tests: window_model a_request_can_raise_its_memory_floor_but_never_lower_it; windows_cli a_request_waits_for_the_memory_floor_it_asks_for (9000 refused at 8000 free with need_mb=9000; 500 granted). Next: job start --compile --min-free-mb, so Cells jobs declare their peak (1-1.8 GB plus margin) instead of hand-rolled free-memory loops.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_28 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 28u32, "2026-10-23T05:06Z", "eoie", "01:06 eoie: job start --compile --min-free-mb is not drafted on purpose. eoie_job_launch job_started parses its 9 options as a fixed-order positional chain (after/after-ok, shape, --max-private-mb, --slot-within, --done-within, --quiet, --quiet-hold, --quiet-wait), so each new flag deepens the legacy pattern. AGENTS.md says touching a legacy function means migrating it. The flag goes in with a migration of job_started to a typed flag union folded over argv (as agile_task_query's query_flag GADT does: flag word, usage, value reader and filter per case, order-free), with the existing CLI tests as the regression net. Its own slice, compile-previewed when Cells is free.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_29 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 29u32, "2026-10-23T05:09Z", "eoie", "01:09 eoie: correcting note 28, now drafted with the migration it asked for (scratch min-free/apply-job-min-free.ps1, chained in apply-rK after the window flag; dry run clean on the post-K tree). job_started's fixed-order chain becomes start_options_read: a fold over argv driven by a typed start_flag union (word and width per case, union_reflection lookup, so eoie_job_launch lists union_reflection) into a start_options record. Options are order-free; the defaults (--quiet hold 30, wait 10) and every refusal text are unchanged, since CLI tests assert them. --min-free-mb on --heavy/--compile writes job file min_free_mb; the window gate's heavy/compile request reads it as its floor. Refusals: 0 MB, and plain jobs. windows_cli test a_compile_job_waits_for_the_memory_floor_it_declares: queued at 8000 free with need_mb=9000 in its window file, plus both refusals.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_30 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 30u32, "2026-10-23T09:11Z", "eoie-lane", "05:11 install probe (EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS): PASS. eoie window check blocked class=bench by=win-1792746056916-120108-heavy-build holder=heavy-build state=held left_min=83 label='main-corpus-checks-after-land (eoie job job-1792746056869-main-corpus-checks-after-land)'")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_31 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 31u32, "2026-10-23T12:46Z", "eoie", "08:46 handoff latency, cheapest fix first (the BACKEND-MAP baseline: release-to-grant median 1750 ms): the three waiting loops poll every 2 s (window request) and 5 s (job gate x2 after H, heavy slot queue), so a heavy job could wait ~10 s after its slot freed. Slice poll-intervals/apply-poll-intervals.ps1 (release L, after H and K) sets all of them to 500 ms, with the exact counts measured on the H-K chain (refuses if the loops changed); dry run on chain copies applies and is idempotent. Rule set now: keep it only if scratch window-handoff.ps1 shows median <= 500 ms on the installed L binary and the waiters stay under 0.5% of a core; otherwise revert. This also sets the bar for the BEAM arbiter prototype.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_32 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 32u32, "2026-10-23T14:56Z", "eoie", "11:05 eoie: WITHDRAWN (was 10:56). I drafted 'no backfill past a waiting exclusive request', then found that EOIE-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE decided on 10-22 23:24 to keep backfill, with cost (1), early-ending holders, measured over 3 nights once K's window event log installs. Today's 10:26 overlap was the EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME leak in the job host (EOIE-JOB-ENV note 12), not backfill, so the change had no evidence and would have overridden a prewritten rule. The slice is deleted and taken out of release M; the measurement rule stands.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_33 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 33u32, "2026-10-24T04:14Z", "eoie", "00:14 groom: nothing left to do here until the train installs its remaining parts: K's free-memory admission (--min-free-mb, typed job start options) and L's 500 ms poll intervals, both slices in tonight's chain (GE3 queued 00:05, then HIJK, L). Marked Blocked on EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN so agile next stops ranking it first; it returns to Active for the after-install probes once L is accepted.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_34 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 34u32, "2026-10-26T00:10Z", "tibia-lane", "00:10Z Gap, verified in code during tibia's PR-28 live-gui block (reported by main). spiral's apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 Invoke-Heavy (line 128) admits a job on its two heavy mutexes (lines 94-98) plus a free-memory check (line 8), and never consults the eoie window registry. A lane that compiles through Invoke-Heavy or a bare --heavy wrapper can therefore start a compile while a live-gui window is held, which breaks this item's 'no suites during GUI runs' conflict for every caller outside eoie job/window. Tonight's block was clean only because main and tibia stopped their queues by hand: a 2 s per-process CPU sample at 00:1xZ showed only the two otclient clients and dwm. Fix shape, one call per the item's rule: Invoke-Heavy asks 'eoie window check heavy-build' (or blocks on a heavy-build window request) before taking a mutex, so live-gui, and bench by the same rule, keep heavy builds out at the shared entry instead of in each lane's habits.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_35 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 35u32, "2026-10-30T14:22Z", "kit", "10-30 10:3x kit groom: the Blocked reason is STALE. #33 blocked this on the train only until K's --min-free-mb and L's poll intervals installed, and the installed kit C528BE4D lists '--min-free-mb <n>' on job start and job run. Back to Active. NEXT (owner kit, CPU-light, ~45m): (1) the after-install probes from #33 (--min-free-mb admission refuses under a high floor and admits under 0; window grant and release receipts); (2) #34's gap: spiral apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 Invoke-Heavy never consults the window registry, so a --heavy wrapper can compile during a held live-gui window. The fix belongs in the compiler lane's script (a 'kit window check' call before admission); kit drafts it unstaged and hands it over.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_36 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 36u32, "2026-10-30T15:13Z", "kit", "10-30 11:2x kit: after-install probes from #33 on the installed kit C528BE4D (box free_mb about 5,900).\nPASS, window admission: 'window request bench 1 --min-free-mb 99999999 --wait 0' answers 'blocked ... reason=low-memory free_mb=5867 need_mb=99999999', exit 1; with --min-free-mb 1 the window is granted (waited_ms=1) and released with a receipt.\nPASS, job admission: 'job run --compile --min-free-mb 1' ran and finished in 1.3 s. The option is refused on a plain job ('applies to --heavy or --compile jobs') and for 0 ('needs a positive number of MB').\nTWO FINDINGS (job side):\n(1) A --compile job held by its memory floor (need 99999999) lists as 'state=queued waiting_for=window:compile', the wrong reason: the compile window was free (the low-floor job got in during the same minute). It should name the floor, as window request does: waiting_for=memory need_mb=N free_mb=M.\n(2) Its timeout (20000 ms) never fired: still queued after 178 s, stopped by hand. That is by design (the limit starts with the child; amend says so), but an impossible floor then waits forever in silence. Fix candidate: refuse at start when need_mb exceeds the box's total physical memory (it can never clear), and show the floor in job status.\nNEXT: (a) draft (1)+(2) in kit_job_start_args / kit_job_queue (scratch-proved; lands with the next train); (b) #34's heavy.ps1 gap. Then the item closes on the installed fix.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_37 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 37u32, "2026-10-30T15:18Z", "kit", "10-30 11:2x kit: #34's gap: the fix is DRAFTED AND REHEARSED, handed to the compiler team (apps/compiler/scripts/heavy.ps1 is theirs). Draft: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\heavy-windows\\heavy-window-check.do, 3 anchored replacements. The live dry run matched heavy.ps1 1d7d5889 at lines 96/111/127.\n- What it does: a new Get-HeavyWindowBlock asks the installed kit 'window check live-gui' and 'window check compiler-install' (exit 1 = held; the receipt names the holder). Wait-HeavySlot does not try a slot while one is held, and its 5-minute heartbeat appends the holder line.\n- It fails open: if kit.exe is missing, admission is as before. SPIRAL_HEAVY_INSIDE_WINDOW=1 skips the check for a compile that runs inside the caller's own live-gui window. Without that opt-out such a caller would wait until its own window expires; that is the one risk to review.\n- Rehearsal on a copy: 0 parse errors. Through the function itself, with the class list swapped to 'bench' so no live-gui window was held: free -> empty; bench held -> 'kit window check blocked class=bench by=win-... label=heavy-gate-probe'; opt-out -> empty; released -> empty.\nOwner of the land: compiler team (or kit on their yes).")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_38 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 38u32, "2026-10-30T15:30Z", "kit", "10-30 11:3x kit: finding (1) from n36 FIXED, landed unstaged; finding (2) CORRECTED.\n(1) A job's status now names its window blocker. kit_jobs/jobs.spi: gate_wait's WaitingWindow carries (class, reason), where reason is the 'reason=...' tail of the job's window file (kit_job_gate already writes 'waiting id=... reason=low-memory free_mb=N need_mb=M' there). waiting_for prints it after the class. kit_job_gate is untouched.\n- Regen of all 26 kit_jobs dependents on a scratch copy: 24 byte-identical (incl. kit_job_gate 994 and kit_job_host 991); kit_job_state 769 -> 822 and kit_job_supervise 700 -> 742.\n- Consumer proof: a probe over the candidate kit_job_state's kit_job_summary, with KIT_JOBS_HOME pointed at copies of the stopped high-floor job (made queued: exit/stop removed, live supervisor, fresh heartbeat). It prints 'state=queued waiting_for=window:compile reason=low-memory free_mb=5053 need_mb=99999999'. A plain window wait (no reason=) prints exactly as before, and the installed kit (control) prints the old line.\n- Draft: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\job-wait-reason\\job-wait-reason.do.\n(2) Correction: an unreachable floor does NOT wait forever. kit_job_gate expires a queued job after 24 hours, or at --slot-within, with exit 75 'the job never started' (gate.spi JobExpiredInQueue). With (1) the wait is no longer silent. A refusal at start when need_mb exceeds physical memory would need a total-memory primitive that kit doesn't have; not worth a new host primitive now.\nLeft on this item: the compiler team's land of the heavy.ps1 window check (n37), then the installed check of (1) after the next train.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_39 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 39u32, "2026-10-30T19:41Z", "kit", "10-30 15:2x kit: field report via main/compiler: 'window check test-suite' answered 'free' while tibia held a test-suite window, and compiler read that as a bug. It's by design (window_model clashes: test-suite is loud but not exclusive; two suites share the box; only live-gui and compiler-install are exclusive; bench blocks loud classes; compile-hold blocks compiles), but the receipt invites the misreading. UX fix candidate, small: when sharing, 'check' prints the held windows it shares with, e.g. 'free (shares with test-suite tibia-k4-box)'; and the usage line states which classes are exclusive. Workaround told to main: request bench + compile-hold for a box to yourself.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_40 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 40u32, "2026-10-30T19:42Z", "kit", "10-30 15:4x kit: n39's UX fix DRAFTED (C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\window-check-shares\\window-check-shares.do; live dry run matches). When a class is free but other windows are held, 'kit window check' appends ' (shares with <class> <label>, ...)' from the same live rows the verdict reads; the output with nothing held is unchanged (windows_cli's existing 'class=compile free' test holds). A CLI assertion is added to shared_classes_coexist_and_memory_gates_admission (heavy-build + test-suite held, check test-suite names both). Compile and candidate test wait for main's 'box free' (compiler's exclusive hour from 15:40).")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_41 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 41u32, "2026-10-30T20:46Z", "kit", "10-30 16:4x kit: n40's 'window check' sharing note LANDED UNSTAGED (kit_window_query 665 -> 716). Proof on a candidate kit.exe: with nothing held, 'window check test-suite' still prints 'kit window check ok class=test-suite free'; with one test-suite window held it prints '... free (shares with test-suite kit-share-probe)' (probe window released). CLI assertion added to windows_cli's shared-classes test.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_42 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 42u32, "2026-10-31T07:48Z", "kit", "10-31 03:3x kit groom: Active -> Blocked. The only remaining step is installed-is-done: TRAIN5e (job-1793421047190, snapshot 00:31) carries this change, and KIT-AGILE-LOST-UPDATES's last part ships in the train after it. Unblock and verify once kit.exe is installed. Moved so that 'agile next --team kit' stops ranking an install wait above actionable work (43 kit items were Active).")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_43 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 43u32, "2026-10-31T12:04Z", "kit", "10-31 07:4x kit: DONE (installed-is-done). Installed E14F1F0B: 'kit window check test-suite' prints 'free (shares with test-suite tibia-lib-regate)' with a real tibia window held, which was n40's sharing note, the last open part. release-acceptance.ps1 on the installed kit E14F1F0B (TRAIN5e DONE 07:43, 140/140 notebooks; the binary was swapped retire-then-move while lanes ran) passed 23/23 at 07:44.")
inl note_of_kit_machine_windows_44 () = Note ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", 44u32, "2026-11-06T11:20Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_standards_checks_1 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-CHECKS", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:30Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_list_fields_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-LIST-FIELDS", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:31Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-LIST-FIELDS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_cells_probes_1 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 1u32, "2026-10-18T07:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 03:54 prototype in the tibia repo: lab/tibia-wgsl-shader-size/measure.ps1 copies a package, applies file|old|new text rewrites, turns named test blocks into entry modules (definitions-only copies for test-only files), compiles each to a backend and records bytes/compile/run to a TSV; sweep-let.ps1 drives per-function variants. It found a 35% WGSL reduction in one afternoon, so the shape is worth making first-class.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_2 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 2u32, "2026-10-18T09:29Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 05:29 the prototype now lives at tibia lab/cells-probes (moved from tibia-wgsl-shader-size when that item closed): measure.ps1 (package copy + text rewrites + test bodies as entries, compile, record), sweep-let.ps1 (per-function inl->let variants), time-variants.ps1 (cold/warm GPU and lavapipe timings through SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMING). Promote into cells or delete it when this item lands.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_3 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:31Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:31 lap 1 grain: main has a second prototype of the same need: lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/probe-lib.ps1 (gate one lib file against a candidate core package via SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR, metrics per target, raw output); with the tibia lane's lab/cells-probes (package copy + text rewrites + timed variants) that is two shapes to unify into one Cells probe verb Next: list the common options of both prototypes (package override, text patch, target, raw output) as the probe verb's arguments")
inl note_of_cells_probes_4 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:08Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:08 lap 2 grain: option inventory of the two prototypes, mapped to one verb. main's lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/probe-lib.ps1: -File (one lib file), -Lib (source dir), -Candidate (package dir via SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR), -Show/-Detail/-RawOut (output trimming, raw dump), fixed process compiler + memo off + SPIRAL_CC + private MIX_BUILD_PATH, --gate --metrics then not-passed (test, target) rows. tibia lane's lab/cells-probes/measure.ps1 + time-variants.ps1: -Label (run dir under spiral-bin/runs), -Source (copied *.spi), -Tests (file/test names), -Backend Wgsl|C, -Runner + -RunnerLabel (runner override), -RunTimeoutSec, -Rewrite file|old|new (text patch, throws when old is absent), -CompileOnly, -Variants + -Repeat + -Cold (timed variant sweep), -Adapters (GPU adapter env). Common core = 6 arguments: source (dir or file), package override, test selection, target, text patch (file|old|new, must match), raw output; plus timeout and label. Proposed verb: mix spiral.notebook --probe <main.spi or test name> --package <dir> --patch file|old|new (repeatable, applied in an overlay mount, never in the source tree) --target <t> --raw <file> --timeout <s> --label <run>; variant sweeps stay a caller loop over --patch sets; adapter env stays the caller's (WGSL only). Reuse: the overlay mount already exists (cells-mounts), --test :keep and --metrics exist in notebook_cli.ex. Next: read how cells-mounts builds its overlay to see whether a --patch can apply to the mounted copy only")
inl note_of_cells_probes_5 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:49Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:49 lap 3 grain, the overlay question answered by reading apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/mounts.ex: a mount dir (spiral-bin/cells-mounts/<16-hex key>) holds one entry per package, kind :link (a junction to the package) or :shadow (a real dir whose package.spiproj is rewritten to packageDir: .. and every other child is linked back: junction for dirs, and for files mklink, then mklink /H hard link, then a copy). The key hashes entry names, kinds, targets and the shadow's package.spiproj mtime, and a ready marker makes a built mount reusable. Consequences for --patch: (1) never write through a mounted file, because a hard-linked file IS the source (a patch would edit the tree); (2) a patched mount must not share the unpatched key. Design: a third entry kind :patched (package target + list of {file, old, new}) built like :shadow, except each patched file is written as a fresh file (the link for that one name is skipped), the edit throws when old is absent (the tibia prototype's rule), and key/1 adds the sha256 of the edit list, so patched and plain mounts coexist and both are cached. Probe verb args stay as in note 4 (--probe, --package, --patch file|old|new, --target, --raw, --timeout, --label). Next: draft the :patched kind as a patch to mounts.ex (build + key) with an ExUnit case that checks the source file is untouched after a patched run")
inl note_of_cells_probes_6 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:25Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:25 lap 4 grain: the --patch overlay exists as a patch and works standalone. lab/CELLS-PROBES/patched_mounts.py (4 anchored edits to mounts.ex + new mounts_patched_test.exs): mount entries gain kind :patched with patches [{file, old, new}]; build shadows the package like :shadow but skips the patched names (shadow/3 with a skip set, the old shadow/2 unchanged), writes each patched file fresh (never through a hard link), replaces the first occurrence, refuses an absent old text, and refuses nested files (top-level package files only in v1); key/1 adds a 16-hex digest of the patch list to the shadow stamp, so plain and patched mounts get different cached dirs. Standalone check (elixir loading file_lock.ex + the patched mounts.ex copy, real cells-mounts root): patched dir differs from the plain shadow dir, the mounted probe.spi reads 2i32 while the source still reads 1i32, the unpatched sibling is linked unchanged, a 9i32 patch returns does not apply. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/CELLS-PROBES/patched-mounts-run.ps1 (applies, runs the 2 new cases + mounts_name_test, restores unless they pass; ~3 min). Next: thread a --patch file|old|new option from notebook_cli.ex into the mount entries of the notebook's packages (the probe verb's first user-visible flag)")
inl note_of_cells_probes_7 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:42 lap 5 grain: --patch is now a user-visible flag end to end in the same lab patch (patched_mounts.py, 13 anchored edits over mounts.ex, cells.ex, memo.ex, notebook_cli.ex + a 4-case ExUnit file; stacked on copies of today's files, all parse). mix spiral.notebook ... --patch <file>|<old>|<new> (repeatable; split on the first two bars, file made absolute) reaches Spiral.Cells via the notebook opts; mount_entries turns a package that owns a patched file into a :patched entry, which keeps the package's own packageDir made absolute when it does not share mounted deps (a plain shadow would have rewritten it to .. and broken its imports), or .. when it does; patches join memo.ex @tool_opts so a patched run never reuses an unpatched result; the mount key carries the patch digest. lab/CELLS-PROBES/patched-mounts-run.ps1 now proves it end to end: the unit cases, then a probe package (answer = 1i32) and a notebook asserting 2i32 that must fail without --patch and pass with --patch pkg/probe.spi|1i32|2i32, while the package source stays 1i32; restores the four Cells files unless all of it passes. Same HEAVY QUEUE row (main runs it after the switch). Next: --probe <main.spi> (a run of a plain program with the package overlay, no test block), the second half of the verb")
inl note_of_cells_probes_8 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:07Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:07 patched-mounts-run 10:56: the 7 unit tests passed; the probe failed for two other reasons: (1) the probe dir has no spiral layout ancestor, so open testing was unbound (fixed by the new SPIRAL_LIB fallback, see CELLS-NEW-PROJECT); (2) --patch file|old|new went through mix.bat and cmd.exe treated the bars as pipes ('1i32' is not recognized). --patch now names a JSON spec file (list of file/old/new, files relative to the spec), which also carries multi-line texts; ExUnit case updated; the runner also checks the unpatched probe fails on the assertion only. Re-queued.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_9 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 progress bookkeeping: --patch is implemented end to end as a lab patch (mounts :patched entries, memo key, JSON spec flag, 4 ExUnit cases, probe notebook); the 10:56 run had the 7 unit tests passing, and the re-queued run decides whether it lands. Progress 60.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_10 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 10u32, "2026-10-19T15:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:48 lap 7 grain, design for the last part (run a plain main with no test block), chosen so it reuses everything the --patch work built: mix spiral.notebook --probe <main.spi> [--backend c] [--patch spec.json] makes a transient notebook in the gate's scratch report dir. Its header is ///- --package <the package dir of main.spi> plus one test block, test probe =, whose body is main's body indented (main's return value becomes the test result; a unit main runs for its output). The probe runs through the same notebook path, so mounts, :patched entries, memo keys and target dispatch are shared and nothing is copied by hand. The CLI prints the cell's stdout and the exit value instead of the test summary. NotebookCli needs only a probe: :string switch and a branch in execute_parsed before the missing --path check. Waits for the patched-mounts run (the foundation) to land first.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_11 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 11u32, "2026-10-19T16:23Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:23 lap 8 grain: post-switch check of the queued patched-mounts run: patched_mounts.py dry run matches all 13 anchors on today's apps/cells (mounts.ex 4, cells.ex 5, memo.ex 1, notebook_cli.ex 3; new test file), and the probe package's packages line |core- still matches lib/spiral/package.spiproj after the core v1 switch, so the queued row runs unchanged. Next: that run after main's go, then the --probe branch")
inl note_of_cells_probes_12 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 12u32, "2026-10-19T16:51Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:51 lap 9 grain: refined lap 7's --probe design from the report code: Notebook.run_report's test rows hold index, name, status and per-target status/ms only (notebook.ex:176-186, target_rows 231-233), so the CLI cannot print the program's stdout from the report. The probe therefore writes the transient notebook's .ipynb into the gate scratch dir (output_path, as gate_args does with --output-dir) and prints that probe cell's outputs plus the exit/status. And the transient notebook is simpler than re-indenting main's body: the whole main.spi text followed by ///> <target> and test probe = main (), since a test cell's program is the file up to the block plus the body, so every definition in main.spi is in scope. NotebookCli change stays one probe: :string switch and a branch before the missing --path check (notebook_cli.ex:38)")
inl note_of_cells_probes_13 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 13u32, "2026-10-19T17:26Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:26 lap 10 grain: --probe transient notebook text fixed from lib practice: <main.spi text> + a blank line + one ///> <target> line per --target (lowercase target names as lib uses them: c and zig appear 19 times in lib, e.g. testing.spi x6) + test probe = / indented main (); Cells then runs it like any lib test block (prefix = the whole file, body = main ()), and the probe reads the cell output from the scratch .ipynb. Also checked: patched_mounts.py still dry-runs against current Cells (memo.ex 1 anchor, notebook_cli.ex 3, new mounts_patched_test.exs), mounts.ex unchanged since 10:56. Next: queued patched-mounts-run.ps1, then probe.py stacked on it")
inl note_of_cells_probes_14 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 14u32, "2026-10-19T17:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:47 lap 11 grain: the transient-notebook builder for --probe is code now, not a design: lab/CELLS-PROBES/probe_text.exs (ProbeText.notebook(main_text, targets): main.spi text without trailing newlines, a blank line, one ///> <lowercased target> per target, then test probe = / main ()), self-checked with plain elixir (scoop OTP first on PATH): the two-target and no-target cases match their expected text (probe_text ok). probe.py will inject this function into notebook_cli.ex next to the --patch switch. Next: patched-mounts-run.ps1 (queued), then probe.py stacked on it")
inl note_of_cells_probes_15 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 15u32, "2026-10-19T22:54Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:54 lap 19 grain: brought the queued run up to today's patch-script rules. patched_mounts.py now checks every file first and writes only after all anchors matched (before, an --apply that missed an anchor in a later file left the earlier files patched), and refuses when mounts_patched_test.exs already exists; dry run still matches 4/5/1/3 anchors (Cells files unchanged since 10:56). patched-mounts-run.ps1 now keeps the originals as bytes and restores with a byte-for-byte check (it used ReadAllText/WriteAllText, which would drop a BOM), and refuses when the new test exists without a backup; parse clean, restore helper checked on a BOM+CRLF sample. Next: the queued patched-mounts-run.ps1 (touches apps/cells, so main runs it with helper lane 1 told)")
inl note_of_cells_probes_16 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 16u32, "2026-10-19T23:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:43 drive grain, the last part of the item is code now: lab/CELLS-PROBES/probe.py adds mix spiral.notebook --probe <main.spi> [--target <backend>]... to notebook_cli.ex (4 anchored edits, all-or-nothing, refuses a re-apply, works on the plain file or after patched_mounts.py's --patch edits): probe_paths writes a transient notebook under the temp dir (a ///- --package header when main.spi sits in a package, main.spi's text, one ///> line per --target, then test probe = main ()) and adds it to --path, so mounts, --patch overlays, memo and metrics all apply unchanged; missing --path now says missing --path or --probe. --self-check runs the exact inserted functions under plain elixir (no mix, no Cells build): notebook text with and without targets, header only with a package.spiproj, no path without --probe, probed source untouched: probe self-check ok; stacked on a --patch-form copy the result parses (Code.string_to_quoted!). Next: queue it after patched-mounts-run (apps/cells edit, main with helper lane 1 told), then an ExUnit case and one real probe run")
inl note_of_cells_probes_17 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 17u32, "2026-10-21T11:17Z", "main", "2026-10-21 07:17 main: tibia's lab/cells-probes (WGSL shader-size timing scripts and their results/timings TSVs, written against tibia runs) moved into spiral/lab/CELLS-PROBES/tibia-wgsl-timings/ (copied byte for byte; tibia empties its folder because the user wants tibia's lab/ empty before the Dota pause).")
inl note_of_cells_probes_18 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 18u32, "2026-10-21T22:27Z", "main", "18:27 revalidated against today's apps/cells (it changed since 10-19): patched_mounts.py dry run still matches mounts.ex 4, cells.ex 5, memo.ex 1, notebook_cli.ex 3 anchors plus the new mounts_patched_test.exs; probe.py dry run matches notebook_cli.ex 4 anchors; probe.py --self-check ok under plain elixir. The run itself (patched-mounts-run.ps1, then probe.py --apply and a mix compile with Cells tests) edits apps/cells, which every lane's notebook run compiles from, so it goes in a quiet window with the lanes told. Not tonight: the slots are full until the night-pack install.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_19 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 19u32, "2026-10-22T05:37Z", "main", "01:36 main: lab run outputs moved to the run cache, per the lab rule that run outputs never sit in lab: 6 file(s) (logs, notebooks, diffs, metrics/gate/timing TSVs) are now under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\CELLS-PROBES\\ at the same relative paths, sha256 checked. Scripts and probes stay in lab/CELLS-PROBES until the item lands.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_20 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 20u32, "2026-10-22T18:29Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:29 main: Cells pack (cells-pack.ps1, 11 steps; it carries the lab-held CELLS-PROBES, CELLS-DAEMON-MEMSUP, CELLS-NEW-PROJECT, CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS, KINO-HOT-PATHS, KINO-RUST-CRATES and CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE). The pack and its baseline now build their copy OUTSIDE the repo: %TEMP%\\cells-*-root\\apps\\cells, plus junctions to the tree's apps\\compiler, apps\\lisp, lib, deps and workspace (the relative paths Cells resolves). Before, apps/cells-nightpack and apps/cells-baseline appeared in the spiral repo mid-run, which pollutes git status and makes eoie's release regen refuse. Cleanup deletes only the 5 junctions it made, by exact path, and refuses to remove the root while any other reparse point is left (tested on a dummy tree whose nested junction survives). Step 11 (tibia's relative entries) now composes with step 1, applying sibling_entries in project_for_mount's :siblings clause, the only path that rewrote packageDir to ..; all 11 steps dry-apply on today's tree. Since the earlier 290/295 run used the in-repo layout, the baseline and the pack are both rerun in the new layout (heavy, chained after the next3 review), and the subset rule compares those two runs.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_21 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 21u32, "2026-10-22T20:46Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:46 main: Cells pack verdict rule changed before any run (time: x's retry and my chain leave no room for a 50-min full baseline before 21:00). The pack suite (15 steps) runs first, behind x's retry. Then a TARGETED baseline: only the test files that failed in the pack run, on the unpatched tree copy in the same out-of-repo layout. Rule: the pack passes when every pack failure also fails unpatched, or is a 300 s timeout that passes when rerun alone; a failure that passes unpatched is the pack's, so find the step that touched that area and drop it. The full-baseline job (job-1792696766922) was stopped before it started.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_22 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 22u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 6 11:07 (subagent2): probe failures: no lib (SPIRAL_LIB now) and --patch bars piped by cmd.exe via mix.bat; --patch takes a JSON spec file now, test updated, unpatched probe must fail on the assertion only; re-queued -> next: --probe main.spi after the run passes | lap 12 14:10 (subagent2): patched_mounts.py dry run on today's tree: memo.ex 1, notebook_cli.ex 3 anchors match, test file new; no --patch in lib yet (not applied) -> next: run patched-mounts-run.ps1 as a bounded heavy job if it fits 2 min, else stays queued | lap 13 14:33 (subagent2): patched-mounts-run.ps1 hardened: it patches 4 tree files and restored them only in finally, which a job-timeout kill skips; originals now also go to spiral-bin/runs/cells-patch-probe/originals, the next start restores them when that backup and the new test file exist, and the backup is removed at the end of every run; parse 0 -> next: queued run (warm build dir from 10:56) | lap 14 15:03 (subagent2): waiting: patched-mounts-run.ps1 (queued, now kill-safe) must land --patch before probe.py can anchor on it -> next: main's queue run | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): unchanged (kill-safe run queued) -> next: main's queue | lap 22-drive 19:43 (subagent2): probe.py staged (--probe <main.spi> [--target]...: transient notebook + package header, joins --path); --self-check of the inserted Elixir ok under plain elixir; stacks after patched_mounts (copy parses) -> next: main applies after patched-mounts-run")
inl note_of_cells_probes_23 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 23u32, "2026-10-26T20:19Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 16:19 fiscal: a measured complement for this item: TYPE-ONLY probes in seconds, no run. Used all day 10-26 to type-verify drafts before gates; it caught 3 test bodies that would have failed a 1-2 h gate, and a GADT syntax bug that cost a 40-min node check before it existed. Two parts. (1) Module code: 'dotnet <spiral-bin>/bin/single-flight/SpiralCompiler/Release/net11.0/SpiralCompiler.dll fsharp <module>.spi', run from a folder that has deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core (|core- resolves against the cwd), ~20-40 s; it type-checks EVERY definition, including unreached backend_switch arms (control: an i64 * string in an unreached Gleam arm failed with line:col). Modules without an entry expression report 'Cannot find main', so check them through a dependent. (2) Test bodies, which --backend / fsharp / --check all skip (only the Cells gate parses test blocks): fiscal session scratchpad cells-ab/test_probes.py <lib> <modules> writes one probe module per file, LISTED LAST in package.spiproj (a module cannot open testing, which comes later in the package; test cells compile after the package), with the file's opens + ':< <module>' + ':< testing'. Each 'test x =' becomes ':= test_probe_x () ='; STATIC test blocks are preludes, so their definitions are lifted to the probe's top level. Controls: a known-bad line restored in a test body fails at its exact definition. Suggested shape for this item: '--probe --check-only <file>' doing (1) + (2) on a patched mount, so a slice's module AND test types are verified before any gate is queued.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_24 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 24u32, "2026-10-27T14:03Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 10:03 fiscal: the type-only probe method of note 23 is still a session-scratchpad script (cells-ab/test_probes.py <lib> <modules>: turn each test block into a probe definition so 'SpiralCompiler.dll fsharp probe_<m>.spi' type-checks every test body in seconds). It ran ~20 times on 10-27 alone (xml, xsd, xsd_resolve, xsd_emit, json_schema, the fiscal generator) and caught real breaks before any node proof, e.g. a literal-tuple pattern that does not unify and a shadowed xml.single_root (schematron.spi:80). Per generic-tools-not-per-experiment it should become one command here (a cells/kit verb taking a lib or package and module names, printing per-module error counts and the first located error), replacing the Python copy; nothing in fiscal depends on the script itself.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_25 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 25u32, "2026-10-29T15:04Z", "main", "10-29 11:0x main, grooming prep: possibly DONE in code (--patch and --probe already exist in apps/cells notebook_cli.ex). Left per the notes: one real probe run plus an ExUnit case, and draining lab tibia-wgsl-timings. Kept at 60m until that run is shown; --check-only would be a new item.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_26 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 26u32, "2026-10-29T18:13Z", "main", "10-29 14:1x main: the type-only probe of n23/n24 is REBUILT as a generic tool, apps/cells/spi/type_probe.ps1 (117 lines; durable draft C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\type-probe\\type_probe.ps1, CD11C86BFBB1). fiscal's cells-ab/test_probes.py died with the 10-28 TEMP wipe; fiscal confirmed it is lost and asked for this. It lands into apps/cells/spi when kit's TRAIN4 Cells freeze ends. It is a new file that no reader loads, but it waits anyway. Until then, run it from the draft path with -CoreRoot C:\\home\\git\\spiral.\nUsage: type_probe.ps1 -File 'a.spi,rust/testing.spi' [-Lib <lib/spiral or a draft copy>] [-CoreRoot <dir with deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core>] [-Show N] [-RawOut prefix]. It mirrors -Lib into a private copy (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\scratch\\type-probe-<hash>; it never edits -Lib). It finds test blocks with the compiler's own `--test-blocks`, so there is no hand parser. Per module it writes one probe module, listed LAST in the copy's package.spiproj (rebuilt per module, so probes never see each other), containing the file's opens, `:< <module>` (left out for implicit `name-` modules such as base), and `:< testing`. All static blocks come FIRST as lifted preludes (a static block later in the file still precedes every test), then each `test x =` as `:= test_probe_x () =`. Tests under `///> _` (raw F# like parsing's `#r nuget`) are skipped and listed. It compiles with `SpiralCompiler.dll fsharp` (installed 6b, bundled dotnet), maps each probe line back to source file:line plus the test name, and reports static, test and module errors separately (fiscal's ask: big static blocks must not dominate the count).\nPositive control in EVERY run (fiscal's other ask): the probe appends `type_probe_control_must_fail () : i32 = \"...\"`. The compiler reports every failing definition, so a run whose output lacks that control error is VOID (exit 2), never green. FAIL = exit 1, PASS = exit 0.\nProven on the installed core 31CC9652. Live lib: step_runtime 3, base 25, parsing 38 (fparsec skipped, 170-line static lifted), date_time 43, file_system 34, env 13, rust/testing 3 (nested module) tests typed, all PASS at about 10-20 s per module. A missing file is VOID. Draft with two seeded breaks: FAIL with exactly 2 errors at their source lines, step_runtime.spi:139:18 [test stopped_actor_ignores_later_messages] and step_runtime.spi:153:70 [module]. Bugs found while building, each fixed and re-run: probes piling up in one spiproj (an earlier probe's control showed in later modules); `:< base` unbound for implicit modules; statics emitted in file order, which left `r` unbound in parsing; `$x = Push-Location; try {}` capturing nothing, which made the first run \"pass\" with 0 tests. That last one is exactly why the control is built in.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_27 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 27u32, "2026-10-29T19:22Z", "main", "10-29 15:20 main: apps/cells/spi/type_probe.ps1 LANDED (new file, from C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\type-probe\\type_probe.ps1 CD11C86BFBB1) and run from its landed path with default -Lib/-CoreRoot over step_runtime, file_system, trace, common and sm' after the gap lands. Landed in the 10-29 15:19 lib gap (after kit's TRAIN4 notebooks end, after fiscal's 4), through main's window sequence: reader + held-window check, proven base hashes asserted, canonical round trip (fixpoint true, blank-only diff), private MIX_BUILD_PATH. document_test after the gap lands: 22 passed (15:22, private MIX_BUILD_PATH), closing run after git-a6 console.spi follows. Undo copies under main scratchpad land-*/ (durable scripts C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\window-1029).")
inl note_of_cells_probes_28 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 28u32, "2026-10-29T21:20Z", "main", "10-29 17:1x main: type_probe.ps1 updated (draft C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\type-probe\\type_probe.ps1 6EC387089C3B, replaces landed CD11C86BFBB1 in the next window): the probe now emits file opens, then `:< testing`, then lifted statics, then the module open LAST, the order a real file sees (module definitions shadow testing and preludes). Re-run on the 8 previously passing modules: all still PASS. OPEN LIMITATION: am'.spi''s `test slice` (lines 216-223, 12 errors, \"Got: .<term> Fails to satisfy: number\" at `slice 0 0`) FAILs in the probe on the UNMODIFIED live lib, with any open order, although Cells runs that test. Something in the probe context types `slice`'s untyped `from nearTo` differently. Until it is explained, am' results show those 12 as a known baseline; a slice is judged by new errors beyond them (the am' to_list fix added none: 18 tests typed, the same 12). ROOT CAUSE FOUND 17:2x: am' declares a nominal TYPE `:! slice t` at line 391, AFTER the test at 216. A real test cell sees only the definitions above it (the slice FUNCTION at 209). The probe opens the whole module, so the later nominal slice shadows the function and `slice 0 0` mis-types. Structural limit of the separate-module design: a probe cannot sit inside the module, because testing comes later in the package. It affects only tests that use a name the module redefines further down, it can only give a false FAIL (never a false PASS), and its errors are stable, so judging a slice by NEW errors beyond the baseline stays sound. A possible fix: per test, rename-check identifiers that the module defines again below the test, and report those tests as \"shadowed: needs the Cells gate\" instead of FAIL. CORRECTION to the line above: a false PASS IS possible. If the later same-named definition happens to type-check against the test, the probe types the test against the wrong definition. So for modules with redefined names, type_probe PASS is evidence, not proof, and the Cells gate stays the authority there.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_29 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 29u32, "2026-10-29T21:26Z", "main", "10-29 17:3x main: type_probe now DETECTS the shadowing case (draft B343B4F24D7A, lands in the next window with the reorder). For each test it lists words that the module defines BOTH above the test and again below it (`:=`, `:==`, `:!`, `:|`, `:~` heads). Errors inside such a test are reported under 'shadowed (... leave these to the Cells gate)' and do not fail the module. On the live lib: am' PASS with 'shadowed: slice (slice; 12 errors)', its 17 other tests clean; sm' PASS with 'shadowed: to_char_array (0 errors)', the false-PASS risk made visible; step_runtime, listm' and common unchanged. The seeded-break control still FAILs at step_runtime.spi:139 [test] and :153 [module], exit 1.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_30 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 30u32, "2026-10-29T21:34Z", "main", "10-29 17:3x main: type_probe update LANDED (module open last, shadow detection). Landed 10-29 17:32-17:33 in the post-TRAIN4c lib window (kit GO at 17:31, no train reading lib), through next-window-main.ps1: reader and window check, base hash pinned per file, canonical round trip, private MIX_BUILD_PATH. Hashes: sm' E56D63DE1C1F -> 9B2BCC159225, listm' 1719B5658442 -> DC9551655B26, convert 96F6D2E1B22C -> 210D27203B5C, common 258BE24CE842 -> E981D750A532, am' 638D7AA2FA7E -> 87168AA1D752; apps/cells/spi/type_probe.ps1 CD11C86BFBB1 -> B343B4F24D7A. type_probe from the landed path PASS 4/4; document_test 22/22.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_31 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 31u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/CELLS-PROBES is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'CELLS-PROBES\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_cells_probes_32 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 32u32, "2026-10-31T14:18Z", "main", "10-31 10:18 main: DONE, record was stale at 80%. Both parts are in the tree and in the user's 10-30 22:33 commit (fd70dccf): notebook_cli.ex has --probe <main.spi> [--target <backend>]... (probe_paths :66, probe_notebook :85, wired at :38) and --patch; mounts.ex has :patched mounts (text-patch variants with a patches digest in the stamp). Tests: mounts_patched_test.exs 4/4 passed today (43.6 s); new_project_test asserts the scaffold's scripts/probe.ps1 uses --probe. The archived lab drafts (probe.py, patched_mounts.py: appliers for exactly these changes) are superseded; no restore needed (checked from a temp restore of archive 20261030-232045).")
inl note_of_cells_probes_33 () = Note ("CELLS-PROBES", 33u32, "2026-11-01T04:50Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/CELLS-PROBES folder (6 files: cell-phases.ps1, patched-mounts-run.ps1, patched_mounts.py, probe.py, probe_text.exs, standalone-check.exs) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'CELLS-PROBES/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T20:58Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 16:58 dependency EOIE-TABLE-QUERY is Done (table query/diff landed 2026-10-18 15:53), but the installed C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\eoie.exe is from 2026-10-17 16:46 and answers 'unknown verb: table'. First migrations ready once it is installed: per-arm counts and means over results/arena-results-*.tsv (today Import-Csv | Group-Object in run-arena summaries and lab compare scripts), and the zig-oracle compare.ps1 join (table diff --key file). Log-corpus questions (shield-split.ps1, adjacency-summary.ps1, pc3-analysis.ps1) still need the regex-to-columns shape noted on EOIE-TABLE-QUERY 2026-10-17.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 2u32, "2026-10-20T05:04Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 01:04 installed eoie.exe (2026-10-19 21:07) has table query/diff. Tried both on tibia data: (1) table diff --key file on lab/tibia-zig-oracle/results-{c,zig}.tsv cross-multiplies repeated keys of these append-only histories (compare.ps1 keeps the latest row per key and adds a zig/c ratio); (2) table query --group gives counts only, while the per-arm arena summaries need mean/sd per group. Both sent to the eoie lane as concrete gaps (--latest-by/--last, --mean/--sd). Scripts stay until they land.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 3u32, "2026-10-20T15:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 11:17 installed eoie (10:52) has table --mean/--sd/--sum and diff --latest-by. Verified on PR-12: per-arm potions mean/sd identical to pr1-analysis; means skip empty cells (cleared-only clear time 90.3 s matches arena-summary.ps1). Remaining gaps sent to the eoie lane: per-group --min/--max (lowest_health), non-empty count per column (cleared n/N), and 'eoie help table' (unknown help target). arena-summary.ps1 migrates when they land; its log half (damage from 'You lose N' lines) still needs the regex-to-columns shape. Side bug seen: arena-summary counts logs by date only, so -Since on a shared log dir pulls in other blocks' logs (45 retreat logs for 40 PR-12 rows).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 4u32, "2026-10-20T20:04Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 16:04 live/arena-summary.ps1 migrated: its TSV half is now one call, 'eoie table query <tsv> --group competitor --count cleared_ms --mean cleared_ms,potions --sd cleared_ms,potions --min lowest_health --sum deaths' (r20), reproducing the old table on PR-12 (cleared 39/40 vs 40/40, lowest 52/10, potions 12.75/13.925). The log half (damage from 'You lose N' lines, filtered by time) stays in PowerShell until a regex-to-columns verb exists. Also replaced earlier: zig-oracle compare.ps1 (table diff --latest-by). Remaining candidates: pr1-analysis.ps1 / pr15-analysis.ps1 onto 'eoie experiment' (r20 slice 1: welch, one-sample) once log-derived columns can feed it; pc3/pc4 analyses wait on median/grid tests (r21).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 5u32, "2026-10-20T20:05Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 16:05 eoie experiment (r20 slice 1) validated against tibia's own analysis in a scratch ledger (replay, data predates registration, so not a real ledger entry): PR-12 Welch via 'experiment analyze' gives difference -1.175, t -1.8786, df 77.65, CI -2.420..0.070, p 0.064, the same as live/pr1-analysis.ps1; the interim-look guard refused a filtered table with one arm short (nexbot rows leave foreign_actions empty, so '--where foreign_actions=0' drops them: a tibia data quirk, not a tool bug), and a second analysis is refused. pr1-analysis.ps1 can retire once tibia's next Welch experiment is registered in a project ledger before its data.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 6u32, "2026-10-20T22:53Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 18:53 pr15-analysis migration waits on eoie release 21 (table logs --count-by): r21, r21b and r21c did not publish today (tests). Recipe for when it lands: 'eoie table logs <arena-logs> --glob *hunting-ground-[0-9]*.log --count-by kills=''Loot of a (\\w+)'' --count potions=''Using one of \\d+ health potions'' --out pr15.tsv', then table query for means/sd (PR-15 itself predates any ledger registration, so experiment analyze is for the next pre-registered run).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 7u32, "2026-10-21T00:35Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 20:35 live/pr15-analysis.ps1 migrated onto eoie r22: one 'eoie table logs <logs> --count kills=... --count-by kind=''Loot of a (\\w+)'' --count potions=... --count deaths=...' call plus 'table query --mean/--sd' replace its per-log PowerShell counting; it reproduces the registered PR-15 numbers exactly (kills 12.80 sd 1.87 t 0.71; potions 12.70 sd 1.64 t -4.35; cyclops 4.70, trolls 8.10). The one-sample t against a stored prediction stays a two-line computation because PR-15 predates any ledger registration; the next pre-registered experiment goes into 'eoie experiment' directly. Left: pr12-secondary (time-window split, eoie --since not built), pc3/pc4 analyses (median now in r22, grid share not yet).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 8u32, "2026-10-21T06:27Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 02:27 inventory of live/*.ps1 helpers still doing table work in PowerShell: pr1-analysis (Welch + damage from logs), pr2-analysis (one-sample), pr12-secondary (per-log hits before/after a start marker), arena-summary (log half). Overnight analyses used eoie table logs/query and experiment analyze only (PR-16 end to end inside live/pr16-block.ps1). eoie gaps sent: table logs --field matches the file path, not lines, so --last name=regex is queued for r26; replay/stateless test verb for pre-ledger experiments (PR-1, PR-2); --values/--since stay EOIE-TABLE-LOGS-VALUES. pr12-secondary needs a per-file time window relative to a marker line (--since <marker regex>), which belongs to that item.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 9u32, "2026-10-21T09:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 05:41 lab/tibia-event-time/d16-score.ps1 takes its moments (count, mean, sd) from 'eoie table query --group ... --count/--mean/--sd' and its live step counts from 'eoie table logs --count'; checked on stored data (live steps n 60 mean 11.68; model arms 14.63 / 13.74). The t statistics stay in PowerShell until 'eoie table test' (EOIE-TABLE-TEST, r28). Papercuts sent to eoie: ungrouped moments need a constant column; the status line on stdout lands in the TSV stream. Tonight's d9-score.ps1 keeps its PowerShell means as the record of how D9/D12/D14 were scored.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 10u32, "2026-10-21T11:58Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 07:58 Closing-pass state. Done on eoie verbs: arena-summary's table half, pr15-analysis, the PR-16/PR-17 ledger analyses (live/block.ps1), scripts/score.ps1's moments. Still PowerShell, each waiting on a named eoie gap: (1) arena-summary's log half sums damage only over logs where something hit; an exact port needs 'table query --where col>value' (only col=value exists) or a per-row filter in table logs. (2) analysis/pr1 and pr2 (methods of record) wait on 'table test' (EOIE-TABLE-TEST, r28). (3) analysis/pr12-secondary waits on 'table logs --since <marker>' (r26, not installed; build failed 10-21 night, rebuild running). Item parks at the day's end with resume condition 'r26 and r28 installed', then port (1)-(3) and verify each against the recorded BENCHMARK numbers.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 11u32, "2026-10-21T12:08Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 08:08 Resume conditions as eoie items: EOIE-TABLE-WHERE-COMPARE (release 29, after a table_query crate split) for arena-summary's log half; EOIE-TABLE-TEST (r28) for analysis/pr1 and pr2; table logs --since (r26e, in test) for analysis/pr12-secondary.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 12u32, "2026-10-21T19:43Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 15:43 r28+r29 installed (15:38): ports done and pending. DONE: live/arena-summary.ps1's damage half is now 'eoie table logs --field competitor=... --sum damage=...' plus 'table query --where damage>0 --group competitor --count/--mean/--sd' (TABLE-WHERE-COMPARE). It reproduces the old PowerShell exactly on the logs since 2026-10-20: retreat 2176 +- 394 (75), policy 2411 +- 464 (40), nexbot 2358 +- 437 (30). VERIFIED, NOT YET SWAPPED: 'eoie table test' matches analysis/pr1-analysis.ps1 on PR-12 (Welch t -1.878613, df 77.65, CI -2.420..0.070) and pr2-analysis.ps1 on PR-7 (one-sample t 1.95, CI 4.61..6.29); it skips empty cells, so clear time over cleared runs needs no filter. The swap waits on one gap sent to eoie just before this note: table test / experiment analyze take only '--where col=value', and the foreign-activity exclusion needs 'foreign_potions<=0' (empty counts as 0, as table query already does). Also sent: table test's n counts rows rather than the values used; table logs rejects --quiet. KEPT AS METHOD OF RECORD: analysis/pr12-secondary.ps1. Its 10 s window is measured from each log's own start-marker @t, which --since cannot express; a historical one-off, low priority. NEXT after the live block: scripts/score.ps1 drops its constant-column trick (ungrouped measures now give one whole-table row), checked by reproducing the D18/D19 scores.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 13u32, "2026-10-21T23:04Z", "eoie-lane", "19:04 eoie team: all three gaps from tibia's 15:43 note are in r34 and green on a tree rebuilt from live (preview exit 0, cargo check of 35 crates, notebooks without type errors): table test / experiment analyze take the full --where comparison set (foreign_potions<=0 works, empty counts as 0 like table query), n names the numeric values used ('(k numeric)'), and table logs takes --quiet. r34 releases after main's compiler install (tonight only if that lands by 20:20, else first thing next session); then the pr1/pr2 swap can proceed on the tibia side.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 14u32, "2026-10-22T02:23Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:23 analysis/ is drift (user: 'what about this new analysis folder, is it drift? weird ps1 names, could keep growing indefinitely. are they running in ci?'). Findings: 8 untracked scripts, one per experiment (pr1, pr2, pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary, pr15, pz-in-traces, start-sync-check). None runs in CI, and six cannot, because they read the local run logs. pr1/pr2 hand-code Welch and one-sample t that 'eoie experiment analyze' already computes (their 40 cited verdicts reproduced through eoie tonight). Origin: per-experiment analysis scripts in live/ since 10-14; this morning's E4 gave them a top-level folder as 'methods of record' instead of a generic replacement (block.ps1 did that for the block scripts). Rules, written before applying: L1 delete pr1-analysis and pr2-analysis; BENCHMARK rows 98, 114, 126 and 406 cite the eoie command instead. L2 pr15-analysis already counts with 'eoie table logs/query'; its t arithmetic moves to 'eoie table test' when r34 installs, and then it is deleted. L3 pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary and pz-in-traces (concluded experiments) need per-file event-sequence logic eoie lacks; one eoie request for those shapes, and each script is deleted when its verb installs. L4 start-sync-check is a generic harness check, not an analysis, so it moves to live/. L5 growth stop: no new analysis script anywhere. A pre-registration's method is the eoie command line in its BENCHMARK test row, run by block.ps1; a missing verb is an eoie request before the experiment is registered. The folder disappears when L2-L3 complete.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 15u32, "2026-10-22T02:24Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:24 L1-L5 (note 14) applied. L1: analysis/pr1-analysis.ps1 and pr2-analysis.ps1 deleted. BENCHMARK rows for PR-1, PR-2, PR-3 and the PR-13 result now cite 'eoie experiment analyze target/ledger <ID> results/<table>' (and 'eoie table logs --sum' for PR-1's damage); the old scripts stay in git history as live/pr1-analysis.ps1 and live/pr2-analysis.ps1. L4: start-sync-check.ps1 moved to live/. L3: eoie asked for per-file event-sequence verbs (interval between matches with an in-between condition and a modulo grid, windowed counts after a marker, an order check, set membership, median). Resume conditions: pr15-analysis goes when 'table test' (r34) installs; pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary and pz-in-traces each go when their verb installs. README's analysis/ row states the folder only shrinks. L5 is in memory (generic-tools-not-per-experiment).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 16u32, "2026-10-22T02:25Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:25 eoie filed the requests: EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES (P2: --interval [--between] [--modulo], --window --from --ms, --order, --in-set, and --median in table query) and EOIE-LEDGER-ONE-FILE (P3). Neither starts before r30-r36 ship. Resume conditions for note 15: pr15-analysis goes when r34's 'table test' installs; pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary and pz-in-traces go when EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES installs; target/ledger goes when 'table test' installs, or turns into one file under EOIE-LEDGER-ONE-FILE.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 17u32, "2026-10-22T02:45Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:45 Commit check (user: 'are you sure can i commit these analysis files then?'): reran all five frozen scripts against the local logs; each reproduces its BENCHMARK result exactly. PC-3: 31/36 late, 2 on grid, 3 other. PC-6: 232/9/210, 935 of 940. PR-12 secondary: 6.18 vs 9.75, 55.15 vs 57.65, 40 logs each. PR-15: kills t 0.71, potions 12.70 t -4.35, 4.70/8.10, on the 10 run logs (the obvious glob also takes the smoke log: 11 runs). PC-4: median 558, 122 kept, 32 excluded, 537-566. PC-5's live half still holds (65 logs, 0 zone samples); its model half needs traces deleted with lab/ on 10-21. BENCHMARK now names PR-15's exact logs and PC-4's script. Verdict: commit them as verified, frozen methods of record (the folder only shrinks, per note 15).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_18 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 18u32, "2026-10-22T11:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 07:17 analysis/pr15-analysis.ps1 DELETED, BENCHMARK's PR-15 result now cites eoie commands (table logs --field/--count/--count-by, table test one-sample --where deaths=0, table query --mean/--sd). The resume condition said 'when r34's table test installs', on the assumption that PR-15 needs comparison filters; it only needs deaths=0, which the installed r29 table test handles. Verified before deleting: kills 12.8 t 0.708805 (record 0.71), potions 12.7 t -4.348057 (record -4.35), cyclops 4.7, trolls 8.1, sd 1.874 / 1.636, n 10 of the 10 logs (smoke log excluded): exact. Stem grep across tibia, eoie, i574n.github, spiral: only backlog history. analysis/ now holds pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary, pz-in-traces (all wait for EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_19 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 19u32, "2026-10-23T03:57Z", "tibia-lane", "Grooming 23:5x: Active -> Blocked, EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES added as a dependency (the backlog's rule for items on hold behind another item). The four analysis/ scripts left (pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary, pz-in-traces) need its sequence verbs (--order, --interval, sections); the installed table logs still lacks them (checked 22-10 19:1x). Resume when EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES ships those verbs.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_20 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 20u32, "2026-10-23T11:11Z", "tibia-lane", "Retirement grooming 07:1x: in scope, about 1.5 h left once eoie ships the EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES verbs (external): swap the scratch readers (read-trace, p5-eval) for eoie commands of record.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_21 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 21u32, "2026-10-23T19:24Z", "tibia-lane", "Pointer 15:2x: eoie reports table sequences (sections, --carry, --first, --last, signed times; spec cases 45 and 46) implemented and test-green. It ships in release O (after M), and eoie pings when O installs. That meets this item's resume condition once installed. First reads to move onto it: the PR-26 ground-trace split (today's hand split in scratch server-probe/pr26-runs; P6 and P7 counts in TIBIA-PARTY notes 72 and 74 as the check), then the scratch readers read-trace and read-ground, p7-judge's per-spawn table, and the four analysis/ scripts (pc3, pc4, pr12-secondary, pz-in-traces).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_22 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 22u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 19:45 Rechecked against the eoie.exe installed at 11:46 today. Its help has no sequence verb under table logs, and EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES is 60 Active and EOIE-EXPERIMENTS 85 Active, so the item stays Blocked. TABLE-QUERY and STANDARDS-CHECKS are Done.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_23 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 23u32, "2026-10-28T10:32Z", "tibia-lane", "GROOM 10-28 06:4x: stays Blocked; the named blocker is EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES (Active 60%, eoie/kit team): table sequences over logs, which the pr1/pr2 analyses need. Resume when that verb ships in an installed kit release.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_24 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 24u32, "2026-10-30T18:48Z", "kit", "10-30 14:5x kit: the named blocker EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES is mostly shipped. The installed kit (C528BE4D) has 'kit table sequences' with --interval/--between/--modulo, --window/--from/--ms, --order, --latency and first/last captures. Not yet: --in-set and table query --median, which kit is building now. If the pr1/pr2 analyses only need the shipped verbs, this item can resume now; tibia's call. A tibia note naming the exact flags still needed would let kit order the rest.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_25 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 25u32, "2026-10-30T22:33Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 18:2x kit built `table sequences --count/--count-in/--in-set` to tibia's spec (EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES notes 28-29), landed in kit/src, not installed yet. It reproduces analysis/pz-in-traces.ps1's Measure-Seen 8/8 on generated traces (headered model traces and a headerless live log, the real 420 zone tiles). Also landed: `table query --median`. Next: after kit's next train installs it, swap pz-in-traces.ps1 onto the verb and check it side by side on real hunter logs from the next live hunts (the 10-28 wipe took the old arena logs). pc3, pc4 and pr12-secondary still need verbs that aren't built (EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES note 29, non-urgent).")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_26 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 26u32, "2026-10-31T02:43Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 22:43 tibia: close rule, decided now that tibia is parked and no live hunts are scheduled. Done when the installed kit has table sequences --count/--count-in/--in-set and analysis/pz-in-traces.ps1, swapped onto the verb, reproduces Measure-Seen 8/8 on the generated traces (headered model traces plus the headerless live-format log, 420 zone tiles). The side-by-side check on real hunter logs moves to TIBIA's resume (first live hunts after the resume). pc3, pc4 and pr12-secondary stay on EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES note 29.")
inl note_of_tibia_tools_to_eoie_27 () = Note ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", 27u32, "2026-10-31T14:02Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 10:0x tibia: DONE by note 26's rule. The installed kit (10-31 07:43 build) has table sequences --count/--count-in/--in-set/--section-on. tibia/analysis/pz-in-traces.ps1 (unstaged) now calls kit table sequences instead of the hand-written Measure-Seen. Per section it counts samples, knight-in-zone and monsters-in-zone, with --in-set on floor-changes.txt (420 zone tiles); runs touching a zone = rows with knight+monsters>0. Model traces use --section-on \"^== \"; live logs are one section per file, as the old \"== name\" concatenation was.\nA/B through the real entry: HEAD's script (949351b) vs the swapped one, on generated traces (4 seeds; a headered model trace with 6 runs including empty runs, x,y,z positions and non-sample lines, plus 2 headerless live logs per seed): 8/8 SAME. Negative: a copy with touching = knight+monsters>1 differs on 4 of 8 lines.\nTwo traps found on the way. (1) kit prints its \"kit table sequences ok\" status on stderr, so the script captures 2>&1 and filters it. (2) samples must be counted with \"seen at \\d+,\\d+:\", not \"seen at \": the old regex skips knight positions written as x,y,z, and the first A/B showed 56 vs 37.\nOne documented difference, not exercised by tibia's traces: in a model trace, lines before the first \"== \" header become their own section in kit, where the old code folded them into the first run. Model traces start with a header.\nStill open, unchanged: the side-by-side on real hunter logs moves to TIBIA's resume; pc3, pc4 and pr12-secondary stay on EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES n29.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-18T17:15Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:15 P1 -> P2: user 2026-10-18 'it'll take a lot of time to rewrite sim in spiral, wait for kino/cells'; stays tied to KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-19T06:34Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 02:34 P2 -> P1 (user 2026-10-19 02:19: prioritize the rebuilds; make the domain modeling intricate, study the type feature set so everything reuses itself when sim is rewritten Spiral -> Gleam + Rust). Order changes: the shared domain comes first through X-CELLS-SPLIT, so each sim slice reuses a client module that already passes on gleam and rust under Cells instead of porting Elixir by hand. First shared module: client protocol.spi (frame header render/parse, thread_flaw union) replacing frames.ex header code and x_world gap notes later. The Elixir sim stays the independent oracle until a slice's Gleam build reproduces its fixtures (prelude.events.jsonl, sim-*.expected.json); a shared source cannot catch its own bug, so the oracle is retired per slice, never in bulk. Still tied to KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR for the supervision side")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-19T06:52Z", "claude", "2026-10-19 02:52 reuse map, sim module -> shared Spiral (x client, X-CELLS-SPLIT): Xsim.Frames (header, split, posted_unit defuse, chunk, cut_at_word_boundary, stored/restored) = splitter.spi + glyphs.spi + protocol.spi verbatim, already backend_switched for Gleam; XWorld.gaps and the missing-frame notes = protocol thread_flaw + assemble's cell_reading; World.gathered (k, n frames per key) = protocol frame_header; Log xsim-events v1 = next shared module: union event (post, frame, edit_frame, edit, delete, like, unlike) with a codec, so the client e2e stub and the sim decode one definition; Invariants.verdict = a decision union carrying the violated rule (omniledger outcome-with-evidence); Runner/World supervision = gleam_otp (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR); Scenario and corpus generation = Rust under the 1k gate; daemon_memory and executors stay shell glue. Oracle rule: frames.ex stays until the Gleam build of splitter reproduces the sim-*.expected.json fixtures, then it is deleted in the same change")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_4 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:32 lap 1 grain: owner check: xsim lives in x/apps/sim and the 10-19 reuse map (note 3: Xsim.Frames = x client's splitter/glyphs/protocol, already backend_switched for Gleam) came from the x lane's side; main records only Next: next lap read the x lane's progress on X-CELLS-SPLIT")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_5 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:09Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:09 lap 2 grain: sized the port and matched the first slice. xsim lib is 1,215 lines of Elixir in 11 modules (runner 183, daemon_memory 161, x_world 160, world 154, scenario 144, frames 113, personas 91, invariants 68, executors 63, log 55, x_compat 23) plus 250 lines of mix tasks and 390 lines of ExUnit (xsim_test 325, x_compat_test 65). Xsim.Frames (header, split with defused dots and word-boundary cut, chunk/flush, frame_text, rebuild) mirrors x/apps/client/splitter.spi function for function (posted_unit, defused, space_carried, split_point, chunk, header, budget_for); X-CELLS-SPLIT note 07:06 reports splitter, glyphs, protocol, prelude and events passing Cells on rust + gleam (+ zig) with UTF-8 byte units, so the frames slice can delegate to the Gleam build of splitter.spi today without new Spiral. Still blocked for the actor half: KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (runner/world as gleam_otp) and SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM. Owner note stands: xsim lives in x/apps/sim (x lane's tree); this item records only. Next: list the xsim_test cases that exercise Frames (the oracle for slice 1) so the x lane can port them to ///> gleam test blocks")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_6 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:50 lap 3 grain: the slice-1 oracle is 8 of xsim_test.exs's 30 cases (x/apps/sim/test, read only). Pure Frames cases, portable as ///> gleam (and rust) test blocks next to splitter.spi: (1) cells split into 280-weight frames rebuild exactly, CJK and Hangul weigh double (split, frame_text, rebuild, weight); (2) frames never end in whitespace and still rebuild exactly (split, rebuild); (3) dots that could start an X link are defused with a zero-width space and restored (split, rebuild, weight); (4) a file's cells start at a column-0 line after a blank line (file_cells, thread_posts). Cases that only use Frames.explode_posts as input to the world/runner (out-of-order delivery invariants, delivery log replay, versioned JSON event stream, edits as frames) belong to slice 2+ (they need World/Runner, i.e. the KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR actor half). Spiral counterparts to check before porting: weight/defused/split_point/chunk/header exist in splitter.spi; file_cells and thread_posts (cell boundaries in a file) have no splitter.spi counterpart yet, which makes case 4 the first new Spiral in slice 1. Recorded for the x lane, which owns both trees. Next: check whether x/apps/client has a cell-boundary parser (posts.spi or wire.spi) that file_cells can reuse")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_7 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:28 lap 4 grain: checked the x client for a file-to-cells splitter to back slice 1's fourth case: none. The client has the reading side only (assemble/assemble.spi groups frames per cell, orders parts, reports MissingFrames/ReplyGap; write/write.spi posts a chain of already-framed texts), and splitter.spi splits one cell into frames. So file_cells is the one new Spiral function slice 1 needs, and its contract is exact in Xsim.Frames: normalize CRLF, drop one trailing newline, empty text is no cells, else split at @cell_boundary = (?<=[^\\n])\\n\\n(?=\\n*[^ \\t\\n]) (a blank line after text, followed by a line that starts at column 0 with a non-blank character, so extra blank lines move to the start of the next cell and indented continuation lines never start a cell), then trim each cell's end; thread_posts = for each cell i, id c<i>, frames of split(id, cell) or one empty frame, stored as header + body trimmed. Placement for the x lane: next to splitter.spi as cells_of_file with ///> gleam and rust tests mirroring xsim_test's case 4. Recorded only (x lane owns both trees)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_8 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:13Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:13 lap 5 grain, CORRECTS lap 4: x/apps/client/splitter.spi already has file_cells (committed in x a3b5353, 10-19 02:49; lap 4 missed it) with the column-0 contract and test cells_start_at_column_zero, plus cell_posts/posts_of. Slice-1 oracle coverage in x today: case 4 (cells at column 0) covered; case 3 (defused dots) covered on the split side (dots_that_x_would_link_are_defused); CJK/Hangul double weight covered in glyphs.spi (plain_weight of U+65E5 and of an em dash plus zero-width space = 2); 280 cap covered (long_cells_split_between_words). Missing everywhere: the REBUILD half of cases 1-3 (split then reassemble gives the original cell, defused dots restored, no frame ends in whitespace); assemble.spi assembled/assembled_of is the reading side to pair with cell_posts. So slice 1 shrinks to one round-trip test block in x (posts_of then assembled over a text with CJK, a defusable dot and a long cell). Recorded for the x lane, which owns the tree.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_9 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:31 lap 6 grain: mapped the exact chain for slice 1's one missing round-trip test (x tree, read only): split side splitter.posts_of text (cell_posts with header c<i> k/n), reading side locate/locate.spi (header_end, frame_json h body original parent: posted text -> frame JSON) then assemble/assemble.spi assembled (frames, known) -> program {sources, flaws}, whose sources must equal file_cells text with defused dots restored. So the test is posts_of -> locate frame per post -> assembled -> sources = file_cells, over one text holding a CJK cell, a defusable x.io dot and a cell longer than 280 weight; it fits as a ///> rust test block in locate.spi (which already sees both sides) or in frames/frames.spi beside framed_json. Restoring defused dots is a reading-side step to check: assemble.spi has no defuse inverse, so either the test expects the zero-width space in sources (post text is what X shows) or a restore step is missing; the xsim oracle case 3 says restored. Recorded for the x lane.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_10 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 progress bookkeeping: slice 1 is mapped against the x client (file_cells exists, the round-trip test and the dot restore are open), and the work belongs to the x lane. Progress 5.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_11 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-19T15:56Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:56 lap 7, correcting lap 6: the dot restore does exist. locate/locate.spi:24 body_at replaces defused_dot (a zero-width space plus the dot, glyphs.spi:24) with a plain dot when it reads a frame body, so the reading side restores before assemble, as xsim case 3 expects. The round-trip test therefore needs no new function: posts_of text -> locate per post (body_at restores) -> assembled -> sources = file_cells text. That is slice 1's single missing test block, for the x lane.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_12 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-19T16:24Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:24 lap 8 grain: slice 2 mapped from x/apps/sim/lib/xsim/invariants.ex (68 lines, pure, the natural Rust slice): check returns 4 or 5 named verdicts (sequential_equivalence, no_stale_results, one_compile_per_prefix, withdrawn_demand_hidden, plus unwanted_results_freed when evicting) over a state of visible id->(key,result), starts/cancelled/evicted key->count, results key->_, and wanted id->key; verdict is ok or violations with the first 5 ids; commute compares two states' visible maps; breaking_edits_fail checks broken sources. Spiral shape: union verdict = Ok | Violations of list id, a named-invariant union instead of atoms, maps as sorted arrays. comparable is the only non-trivial part: two regex rewrites (spiral_cells/<n>-<n> -> spiral_cells/_ and thread name (<n>) panicked -> (_)), which a small hand scanner covers without a regex crate. Order after slice 1 (x lane)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_13 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-10-19T16:53Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:53 lap 9 grain: mapped xsim frames.ex against the x client: split, posted_unit (defused dot between non-space glyphs), chunk with X weights, cut_at_word_boundary, space_carried, header and digits already exist in Spiral as x/apps/client/splitter.spi (posted_unit, chunk, word_reach, space_carried, header, digit_count, budget_for, chunks_of; 201 lines with 6 test blocks). So the frames slice needs no new splitting code; what is left of frames.ex is the event-level glue: rebuild (sort frames, join, restore dots), explode_posts (events to framed posts with the limit) and thread_posts/file_cells, which build on splitter plus locate.body_at. Order stays: slice 1 round trip, slice 2 invariants, then this glue")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_14 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-10-19T17:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:29 lap 10 grain, dependency reading: the deps gate the whole item on KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (Epic 10%, itself behind SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) and SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM (82%), but only the supervision half (World/Runner as gleam_otp actors, Cells shell) needs the Cells port; the bulk half (frames, scenario/delivery generation, invariants, compiled to Rust) needs neither, and its first slice (frames) already maps 1:1 onto the x client splitter (lap 9). So the bulk slices can start now, in the x lane (the code lives in the x tree, which helper lanes do not touch). Proposal for main: split off a SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK story with no KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dep, assigned to the x lane. Next: x lane slice 1 round-trip test (rebuild/explode_posts over splitter)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_15 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 15u32, "2026-10-19T17:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:48 lap 11 grain: planning filled from the description's own sizing: about 1,000 lines of Elixir in 7 modules ported slice by slice with the 30-test ExUnit oracle, two compile targets (Gleam supervision, Rust bulk under the 1k crate gate): estimate 1800m (roughly 250 min per module plus the shared GADT types), load heavy (Rust + Gleam builds and 50k-event corpora per slice). Assignee stays open for main (x lane per my lap 10 proposal)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_16 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 16u32, "2026-10-19T18:12Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:12 lap 12: blocker chain: KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (Active 10%) waits on SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL (Planned 0%), and the code to port (x/apps/sim) is x-lane territory, so the item is blocked for the compiler-side lanes until the x lane takes it")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_17 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 17u32, "2026-10-19T19:47Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-19 15:47 split accepted by the x lane: the bulk half moved to SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK (no KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dep, x lane); this item keeps the supervision half (World/Runner as gleam_otp actors, Cells shell)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_18 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 18u32, "2026-10-21T20:36Z", "x", "2026-10-21 16:36 x: grooming. Status Active -> Blocked: the remaining scope (supervision half) waits on KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, which waits on SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL (0%); nobody can move it today, and X-DONE D4 keeps it outside x's done. Two lessons from 10-21 for the port. (1) On the Gleam backend, Spiral string loops built on cut-the-remainder or prepend-concatenation were O(n^2) in refc binaries: the lib corpus run reached 16 GB until main's lib fixes (sm'.spi F7D95B8A8410F714: index_of via binary:split, left-fold concat_list/concat_array) and x's glyphs.split_all. Supervision code that touches streams should use those, never hand-rolled cut loops. (2) Memory probes on the BEAM must sample the peak per phase (a 100 ms sampler of :erlang.memory(:binary)); end-of-phase readings hid a 3.2 GB transient and misattributed the cause once. The description's 'bulk compiles to Rust with rayon' is outdated: the bulk shipped on Gleam (SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK note 2).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_19 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 19u32, "2026-10-23T10:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 06:52 x: narrowed. The pure world model and the runner's scheduling core moved to SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL and SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL (both ready, no KINO dependency). This item keeps only the effectful shell (task supervision as gleam_otp actors, Cells shell) plus the mix tasks, and stays Blocked on KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR. Lesson (night of 10-22/23): a Blocked status on a whole epic hid two nights of portable work from the ready list; block only the part that waits.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_20 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 20u32, "2026-10-23T13:18Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:18 x: STATUS MAP after today's ports (every module of apps/sim/lib checked). PURE, now in Spiral: frames.ex = splitter/glyphs/protocol (dual-backend since 10-19); log.ex = events.spi stream/decoded_stream (all but the legacy positional format, used by no fixture); personas.ex and scenario.ex already call Spiral (personas.spi, schedule/scenario exports); world.ex = inbox.spi (parity with the real World on the prelude stream, rust + gleam); runner.ex's scheduling = scheduler.spi (settled-view parity with the real Runner, rust + gleam); invariants.ex = runner_checks.spi, a typed bridge into invariants.spi (rust + gleam 4/4; checks_parity against the row text and the real Invariants.check is in flight). LEFT IN ELIXIR, all on the G side of BACKEND-BALANCE: (1) the runner shell, which starts executor Tasks, yields and harvests them, turns a crash into a result and paces (pace_ms): the scheduler already emits StartTask/CancelTask commands, so the shell becomes a gleam_otp actor feeding finished_task back (LIB-OTP-ACTORS); (2) executors.ex: the fake one is fake_out, the real one runs a notebook (Cells) and maps its status, behind the same command interface; (3) daemon_memory.ex: a periodic OS-tree sampler, a natural first actor; (4) the mix tasks (xsim.run, thread_run, world, diff, events, frames, x_compat), which become the entry's subcommands; (5) the ExUnit suite (30 cases), which becomes Spiral test blocks as each slice lands, with the Elixir originals kept as oracles until then. So the critical path of SIM-IN-SPIRAL is now LIB-OTP-ACTORS plus the shell, not more pure ports. All drafts are in %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world (x's tree is staged).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_21 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 21u32, "2026-10-23T13:59Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:59 x: the entry's argument model is drafted: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\sim_args.spi (queued for Cells after tibia's block). 'xsim run' takes the same 21 options as Mix.Tasks.Xsim.Run, typed in 3 records (corpus, schedule, runner) instead of a keyword list. --executor is a union (fake | fake-leak | cells); --evict / --evict-grace map onto scheduler's eviction union, with grace winning in either order as in eviction/1; --duplicates is parsed to permille (0.25 -> 250), so no float crosses the entry; --spi splits on ',' and '+'. Every bad value is rejected by name ('--seed takes a whole number, got x'). 2 tests. With sim_report.spi (the XSIM summary line, commute and verdict lines), the run task's pure half is done. What's left of it is the effects: reading .spi cells (Cells' document parser, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR), the executor, and the shell (LIB-OTP-ACTORS).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_22 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 22u32, "2026-10-23T14:25Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:25 x: first end-to-end Spiral sim run drafted, deterministic and fake-executor only: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\sim_run.spi (queued for Cells after tibia's block). simulated options threads wires the typed pieces with no text round-trips: casting from run_options -> scenario.from_corpus -> flow (exploded into 280-weight frames with --frames) -> per schedule schedule.deliveries (seed = schedule number, duplicates permille, window 6, as Scenario.deliveries) -> scheduler.sync_run -> runner_checks with the fake baseline -> sim_report.summary_line, then verdict_line over all schedules. Schedules loop through a let rec, not listm.init, because the count is runtime data. Test: a 3-cell corpus with 2 schedules prints 2 XSIM lines plus 'XSIM commute=ok result=pass'. Differences from 'mix xsim.run --executor fake': no Task concurrency (peak_inflight, cancels and timing differ by design), ms = 0. Its main (reading --spi files into cells) waits for a cell splitter, which x has (simframes threads_of / file cells) and will be checked next; the cells executor waits for KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_23 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 23u32, "2026-10-23T14:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:27 x: and the entry: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\xsim_entry.spi, 'xsim run --spi FILE[,FILE] [options]', Rust-only (a per-command CLI, rule R2). It reads argv through the arg_count/arg_at pattern of x's args.spi, reads each .spi (or the --spi-file list), cuts cells with splitter.file_cells (x's post-thread cut: a column-0 line after a blank line), applies --cells, runs sim_run.simulated, prints the XSIM lines and exits 1 on 'result=fail', or 2 with usage on bad arguments. Test: a 2-cell text -> 2 lines (1 schedule + verdict), exit 0. Known difference to mix xsim.run: Cells' Document.parse_spi (with *_real.spir companions) cuts cells by notebook rules, so for some files the scenario differs. Unifying needs Cells' parser in Spiral (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR), or measuring how often the two cuts differ on x's 36 notebooks (a cheap check, next).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_24 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 24u32, "2026-10-23T14:28Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:28 x: SUPERSEDES note 23's 'measure how often the two cuts differ'. The authoritative cell cut is already Spiral: spiral/apps/cells/spi/document.spi (826 lines, written without core: its own rows/cells unions). from_spi spi spir (line 826, via from_spi_bounded) is what Spiral.Cells.Document.parse_spi calls through the Cells export (main.spi:16), returning Cells' wire text, which Elixir's Domain.decode_cells turns into cells. So xsim_entry should not keep its own cutting rule (splitter.file_cells is x's post-thread rule): reuse document.from_spi. Path, lib-first: lift document.spi (notebook format = generic tooling, used by Cells, the sim, and any notebook reader) into lib/spiral, or at least export a typed 'spi_code_cells spi spir : list string', so the entry gets the same cells as mix xsim.run. Owner: main / Cells (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR covers Cells' Spiral side). Until then the entry's file_cells cut stays, flagged as a known difference.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_25 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 25u32, "2026-10-23T16:38Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:38 x: for note 24's lift of Cells' document.from_spi into lib, the wire format it returns (read from apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/domain.ex decode_cells/decode_n, read-only): '<cell count>\\n', then per cell '<kind>\\n<source byte length>\\n<source bytes>' with no separator after the source. kind is 'code/<language>' (code/spiral, code/elixir, ...) or a plain kind (markdown, import). The lengths are BYTES (Elixir byte_size and binary-size), so a Spiral reader must slice by bytes (lib sm'.byte_slice), not by sm'.substring, whose unit may differ on Gleam. Better than decoding text: the lifted module exports a typed 'spi_code_cells spi spir : list string' built from its own cells union, so no wire text crosses a Spiral-to-Spiral boundary at all. The wire stays only for Elixir.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_26 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 26u32, "2026-10-23T17:56Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:56 x: SLIP, found by Cells. sim_run.spi called post_limit () (splitter.spi) without 'open splitter'. ETY-UnboundVariable in flow_for, and since a broken module fails every gate in its package, checks_parity, scheduler, scheduler_parity and inbox_parity all failed between 13:52 and 13:54 (5/5, 1/1 ... failed) with no fault of their own. Fixed in both copies (open splitter after personas; splitter is listed before sim_run in the spiproj). The four are rerun after chain7; the notebooks it starts after the fix see a clean package. My pre-queue checks scanned name CLASHES but not unopened imports. A cheap addition: for each new module, list the free names it calls and check each against the definitions of the modules it opens.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_27 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 27u32, "2026-10-23T20:33Z", "x", "2026-10-23 16:33 x: main is landing the actor groundwork into lib today (16:2x): erlang_process, otp_actor, otp_supervisor and step_runtime on gleam_erlang 1.x, plus Cells' gleam_erlang 1.3 / gleam_otp 1.3 / stdlib 1.0.5 pins. So the shell (an interpreter of the scheduler's StartTask/CancelTask commands: spawn_unlinked + monitor, kill, Down -> Crashed; LIB-OTP-ACTORS note 9) is no longer blocked on primitives. Next x session: draft the Gleam shell against the landed modules.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_28 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 28u32, "2026-10-23T20:35Z", "x", "2026-10-23 16:35 x: the OTP shell is drafted, Gleam-only, against the API main is landing: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\runner_shell.spi. An otp_actor whose state is the pure scheduler; messages Adopt (its own subject), Deliver delivery, Finished visible_entry, Settle (reply subject). Each StartTask the scheduler emits becomes an erlang_process.spawn worker that sends Finished back. CancelTask needs no kill in v0, because finished_task ignores keys that are no longer running. Settle re-sends itself with send_after while tasks run, so the reply comes when the scheduler is idle (no sleep). Test: on 4 deliveries, the shell settles to the same visible count as sync_run. Deliberately NOT in the scratch package yet: it needs the landing lib modules, and an unresolvable module fails every gate in its package. Add it after otp_actor is in lib, then run Cells (gleam).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_29 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 29u32, "2026-10-23T20:57Z", "x", "2026-10-23 16:57 x: on gleam_erlang 1.3 / stdlib 1.0.5 (main's pins, landed 16:3x): sim_report and sim_args PASS. sim_run and xsim_entry failed on Rust with E0433 for unicode_segmentation / unicode_normalization: opening splitter pulls glyphs' crate-backed primitives, and their headers named only sha2. Headers now list the crates, as splitter.spi's own header does; requeued (chain13).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_30 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 30u32, "2026-10-23T21:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:27 x: Cells: sim_run 1/1 and xsim_entry 1/1 PASS (chain13, after the unicode crates were added to their rust -d headers). The OTP shell (runner_shell, gleam) now uses main's otp_actor.new_with_self (lib 6525F5CC), with no Adopt message. Its first check failed only because the isolated package's spiproj listed modules the copy lacks (now trimmed). Rerun queued as chain14. Landing trial-4 timed out at 60 min mid-build: args, assemble, checks and digest compiled in x's real layout (args 999 generated lines, digest split 86+946+149). x-compat and e2e were not reached.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_31 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 31u32, "2026-10-23T21:37Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:37 x: OTP shell PASSES. Cells on runner_shell (gleam, chain14, 17:30): the_otp_shell_settles_like_sync_run is 1/1, so the actor over scheduler.spi settles to the same visible map as sync_run. It is built on otp_actor.new_with_self (main 6525F5CC) with no Adopt message. The Adopt version is kept in the session scratchpad as runner_shell.adopt.spi.txt. Next for the shell: executor monitors, kill on CancelTask and timeouts (erlang_process monitor and kill), then the switch-(2) measurement per X-BACKEND-MAP note 2.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_32 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 32u32, "2026-10-23T21:41Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:41 x: design for the shell's next step (monitor, cancel, timeout), using only what lib has today. otp_actor has no selector hook, so the shell's mailbox can't take Down events directly. Instead, each StartTask spawns a GUARD (erlang_process.spawn_unlinked) that spawns the worker, monitors it (monitor) and waits on a selector over its own guard subject (select_map: Cancel) plus that monitor (select_specific_monitor: Down), with selector_receive and the task's timeout. Down with a normal exit means the worker already sent Finished, so the guard does nothing. Down with an abnormal exit makes the guard send Finished {status = Crashed}. Timeout: kill the worker, then send Finished {status = Crashed; out = 'timeout'}. scheduler.spi's task_status is Done | Erred | Crashed, with no TimedOut (checked 17:43), so a separate TimedOut status needs a parity check against Elixir's Runner first. Cancel: kill the worker, send nothing. The shell keeps key -> guard subject in a dictm, so a CancelTask sends Cancel. The shell's message type stays single (Finished). Alternative if gleam_otp 1.3's builder has a selector hook (unverified; check the hex source first): ask main for otp_actor.selecting and drop the guards. Test plan: a worker that crashes, one that sleeps past its timeout, one cancelled, then compare settled_view against sync_run's crash handling.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_33 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 33u32, "2026-10-23T21:46Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:46 x: note 32's guard is DRAFTED, not yet checked: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\runner_guard.spi (gleam). guarded (report) (handed) {key; timeout_ms; work} spawns an unlinked guard. The guard makes its own subject (handed to the shell for Cancel), spawns the worker unlinked, monitors it, and settles on one selector_receive: Cancel kills the worker, an abnormal Down reports crash_of key 'crashed', and a timeout kills the worker and reports 'timeout'. Tests cover a crash and a timeout. The worker in the timeout test makes its own subject to block on, because Gleam's receive only works for the subject's owner; a foreign subject would crash the worker and fake a pass. Unverified names: optionm.map. Next: add it after scheduler in the x-shell package, run Cells on gleam, then wire it into runner_shell's started_by plus a key -> guard subject dictm for CancelTask.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_34 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 34u32, "2026-10-23T21:55Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:55 x: runner_guard PASSES 2/2 on gleam (chain17, 17:55): a crashing worker is reported as Crashed 'crashed', and a worker that outlives its timeout is killed and reported as 'timeout'. The first run (chain16) failed at parse: a union case nested in Some without parens; fixed by matching the option first. Next: wire guarded into runner_shell's started_by, keep key -> guard subject in a dictm, and send Cancel on CancelTask.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_35 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 35u32, "2026-10-23T22:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:06 x: executors port (Xsim.Executors, 50 lines). xsim.run offers fake, fake-leak and cells; the flaky mode is used by no task and no test, so it isn't ported. DRAFTED: x-world\\executors.spi (gleam). type executor = list string -> task_result; fake_executor; the leak as a one-field actor (leak_message = Swap (digest, reply), state = previous digest, replacing the ets table); leaking_executor calls it per run. The shell takes the executor (shell_state.exec). New shell test: a_leaking_executor_breaks_sequential_equivalence (limit 1, like the Elixir test at xsim_test.exs:33, through runner_checks + fake_baseline). CELLS executor, not drafted: it calls Elixir Spiral.Cells.Notebook.evaluate(%{cells: [%{kind: :spiral, source: s}...]}, opts) and reads status/verdicts/outputs/memo from the result map. From Gleam that's an @external(erlang, 'Elixir.Spiral.Cells.Notebook', 'evaluate') binding plus a small Erlang-term builder and a decoder (gleam/dynamic/decode), which belong in lib as an elixir_call boundary (one thin OS-style boundary, per eoie-granular rules). The memo tally becomes a counter actor like the leak cell.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_36 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 36u32, "2026-10-23T22:15Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:15 x: CELLS executor DRAFTED (note 35's last executor): %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\cells_executor.spi (gleam). It calls Elixir Spiral.Cells.Notebook.evaluate directly through @external plus lib's gleam_stdlib:identity coerce idiom (as process.spi does), with no Elixir code in x. The doc is built as Erlang maps from gleam dicts with atom keys (%{cells: [%{kind: :spiral, source: s}]}), opts are root/spiral_lib (as xsim.run passes them), and the result is read with maps:get on status, verdicts[last] and outputs[last]. A non-map result gives Erred 'unexpected ...' (Elixir's :unexpected has no Spiral status). Sources cross as one NUL-joined string, split by <<0>>, because Spiral lists aren't Gleam lists. The result comes back as nominal cells_run = #(Bool, String) with .0/.1 accessors. out = string.inspect({status, verdict, output}); it's deterministic, but not byte-equal to Elixir's inspect, which the rewrite doesn't need. The test covers the doc builder only (2 cells, last source). evaluate itself needs a BEAM with Spiral.Cells loaded, i.e. the sim's mix VM, so it is checked there after the shell gets a Gleam entry. All staged in the x-shell package; Cells queued for after tibia's block (after_block2.ps1).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_37 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 37u32, "2026-10-23T22:20Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:20 x: landing requirement found. x's XC005 gate (apps/client/build.ps1:25, x-compat with the 300-weight probe) covers every .spi under apps/client, and the sim's Spiral sources live there (client/simframes generates apps/sim/frames). Measured on the evening drafts: sim_args has 16 cells over 270 (default_run 706, schedule_value 1064; some were there before tonight), xsim_entry 5, runner_shell 8, runner_guard 6, cells_executor 4 (Gleam globals up to 821), memory_watch 2, executors 1. Rule: the sim modules get the same fit pass the client drafts got (split into cells of 270 or less, re-checked with x's real profile + probe) as the LAST step before they land, not now while drafts still change. Gleam global blocks may need splitting into several global calls.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_38 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 38u32, "2026-10-23T22:23Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:23 x: module coverage check. Every lib/xsim/*.ex module now has a Spiral counterpart: world -> inbox/world, runner -> scheduler + runner_shell/runner_guard, executors -> executors + cells_executor, invariants -> invariants/runner_checks, log -> events (stream, decoded_stream, v1 header), frames -> splitter + inbox frame rebuild + sim_run exploded, scenario/personas -> scenario/personas/schedule, daemon_memory -> tree_memory + memory_watch, spiral.ex -> not needed (it only loaded the Gleam ebin into Elixir), json/x_compat -> left with their mix tasks. MIX TASKS -> xsim subcommands, DRAFTED in x-world/client: sim_args gets a generic flags_parsed loop (flag_rules = {switched; valued}); run_args is rebuilt on it, so its two existing tests guard the refactor. New: events_args (xsim.events defaults: seed 1, users 3, edits 1, duplicates 0.2) and world_args (xsim.world defaults: id-base 3000000, user 500/spiralposts, base-time 2026-10-18T08:00:00Z), each with a test. xsim_entry gets 'events' (scenario_stream + scenario_final to --out/--expected, prints deliveries=N) and 'world' (world_files, prints cells/bytes via sm'.length', UTF-8 bytes like byte_size), plus a test that the events stream decodes. Still to port: diff, thread_run (both need cells_executor's evaluate, so they come after it runs in a Cells-loaded VM), frames (needs Xsim.Json's laid-out JSON) and x_compat (Spiral.Cells.XCompat is main's). All checks wait for tibia's block end (after_block2.ps1).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_39 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 39u32, "2026-10-23T22:26Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:26 x: 'xsim frames' DRAFTED (the third mix task). frames_args (--file, --out) in sim_args; in xsim_entry, posts = splitter.posts_of, program = file_cells |> each glyphs.nfc |> joined by blank lines |> lines. JSON goes through final_state's existing laid_out/square/braces/pair, which already port Xsim.Json (Nil -> [], levels below 4 opened). So Xsim.Json needs no new code; it was already ported for world's expected file. The test pins the exact layout, both empty and with an escaped quote. A shadow check over xsim_entry's 10 opens finds each used name defined exactly once. Left: diff and thread_run (after cells_executor runs in a Cells VM) and x_compat (main's Spiral.Cells.XCompat).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_40 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 40u32, "2026-10-23T22:34Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:34 x: FINDING (desk check, no compile): the Spiral xsim takes cells from splitter.file_cells (a cut at each blank line before a column-0 line), but Elixir's xsim.run/xsim.events/xsim.world take them from Spiral.Cells.Document.parse_spi (Kino's document.spi spi_walk). They differ. The committed fixture apps/sim/fixtures/prelude.final.json shows 6 cells for prelude.spi: p0 '///- --package .', p1 '///- --test static\\n\\nopen testing' (the test header normalized into a directive), and p2 several top-level blocks merged into one cell. So 'xsim run/events/world' would not byte-match build.ps1's fixtures (Confirm-Fixture uses -cne), and run's corpus differs from Elixir's. 'xsim frames' is unaffected: xsim.frames itself uses Xsim.Frames.file_cells = splitter. Rule (lib-first): no copy of spi_walk in x. The fix is one shared .spi document parser (Kino's from_spi + decode_cells) callable from Rust, owned by Kino/compiler (SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY, note filed there). Until then, xsim run/events/world stay out of build.ps1. Next fixture-equality test: 'xsim frames --file oauth1.spi' vs apps/sim/fixtures/thread-oauth1.expected.json once compiled.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_41 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 41u32, "2026-10-23T22:56Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:56 x: runner_shell gains two cancel tests, queued after tibia's block. (1) a_withdrawn_demand_cancels_the_running_task_like_deferred_run: post/like/unlike through the shell with a slow executor (each worker blocks 50 ms on its own subject), so the unlike's CancelTask reaches a running task (usually through the early-cancel path); (cancels, visible) must equal scheduler.deferred_run's (1, 0). (2) a_cancelled_worker_is_killed_before_it_finishes: the executor sends a probe to the test process after its 50 ms; with the unlike the probe must never arrive within 300 ms (the guard killed the worker), and without it the same run must deliver the probe, which is the positive control in the same test. Together they cover the cancel flow and the kill itself.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_42 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 42u32, "2026-10-23T23:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:06 x: Elixir suite coverage map (xsim_test.exs, 29 tests). Covered by existing Spiral tests: correct runner on shuffled/duplicated deliveries (sim_run + checks_parity), order independence (sync/deferred commute), leak (runner_shell), 280-weight frames/CJK (splitter, glyphs, reading), trailing whitespace (reading), defused dots, column-0 cells, the versioned stream (events), false-pass oracle (runner_checks), eviction + recompute (scheduler), whole numeric literals (personas), panic thread ids (invariants), early replies/edits (inbox), sampler core (tree_memory/memory_watch). ADDED tonight to runner_checks (queued): case 39 naive stale revision, case 51 rerun recompiles prefixes (it found the rerun drain bug, SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL n8), case 57 withdrawn demand hidden unless naive, case 190 grace window rescue/free (minimal stream), case 143 saved stream replays to the same visible results (stream -> decoded_stream -> commute). STILL GAPS, all scenario-scale (they need Scenario/personas/schedule streams and multi-run commutes, best written once xsim run takes Cells-parsed cells): 71 interleaved author threads, 83 subtree visible follows, 133/201 framed out-of-order runs, 153 heavy churn, 182 paced run (pace_ms isn't ported in the scheduler; the shell's Settle polling is its runtime analogue), 223/284 personas end to end.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_43 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 43u32, "2026-10-23T23:25Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:25 x: nine more Elixir cases ported (correcting n42's 'scenario-scale gaps'): those tests use literal cells, not parse_spi, so they need no shared parser. New %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\client\\scenario_checks.spi (rust + gleam, queued after runner_checks): case 16 (seed 3, users 5, edits 3; schedules 1..6 at 0.3 duplicates; every rule ok and something visible), 26 (schedule 1 at 0.1 vs schedule 9 at 0.5 window 12 commute), 71 (from_corpus of @cells + a second notebook, breaking 2: unique ids, f0 and f1 threads, breaking test edits end in _assert_eq 10|17|9|5, the run holds), 83 (root edit re-keys b and c; sources of c follow), 153 (churn 12: 12 root edits, limit 2, invariants hold, p0's source is the last edit), 133 (framed at 120: framed tally = posts, commutes with unframed), 201 (edit frames at 20 include part 2, framed_edits = edits, commute), 223 (personas lurker/fickle/editor/breaker at 2/3/2/2: a fickle unlike and an original-restoring edit exist, broken is non-empty; 4 schedules at 0.3 window 10, limit 2, with an HONEST executor (a broken source errs): every rule, breaking_failed and every post back at its original source; neighbours commute), 284 (an unbreakable test stays unbroken under breaker=3/breaking 2; an empty thread yields no events). For 223, scheduler.spi gained sync_run_with exec / executed_all_by exec (sync_run = sync_run_with fake_result; nothing else changes). Same parameters as the Elixir tests; cells dynamic (inl ~); schedule loops are runtime let rec. Only 182 (pace_ms) stays uncovered, because pacing isn't ported in the deterministic scheduler; the shell's timed Settle is its runtime analogue.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_44 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 44u32, "2026-10-23T23:39Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:39 x: once LIB-DICTM lands, the scratch packages x-world\\client and x-shell\\client still list their own dictm.spi next to the lib's, a package-vs-lib module name pair whose resolution is untested (see X-GENERIC-TO-LIB n16, why the lib OAuth module became 'oauth'). Plan: run ONE Cells check in that state first, to learn safely in scratch which dictm wins or whether it errors, then remove dictm from both spiproj lists and files. apply_sim_modules.ps1 already never copies dictm into x (it refuses until lib has dictm).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_45 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 45u32, "2026-10-23T23:44Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:44 x: RE-GROOM Blocked 5% -> Active 40%, because the 10-21 reason no longer holds. The supervision half was 'waiting on KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR'; tonight it was built on main's landed otp_actor/erlang_process with no Kino dependency (runner_shell passes on gleam; runner_guard passes 2/2). Progress by milestone, not by description: (1) pure core (world/inbox, scheduler, invariants bridge) with Elixir parity: verified. (2) OTP shell + guard + cancel: shell and guard verified, cancel wiring and kill test drafted. (3) Executors: fake/leak drafted; cells drafted but its real run needs a VM with Spiral.Cells loaded (the true KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dependency). (4) xsim CLI: run verified; events/world/frames drafted; run/events/world byte-equality waits on the shared .spi parser (SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY n13), frames doesn't. (5) Elixir suite port: 28 of 29 runner cases mapped or ported (scenario_checks, runner_checks additions), only the paced run left. (6) Landing into x: waits on LIB-DICTM (apply_sim_modules.ps1 is verdict-gated). Remaining blockers name owners: LIB-DICTM (main, tonight's chain), SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY (Kino/compiler), KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (cells executor only).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_46 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 46u32, "2026-10-23T23:46Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:46 x: case 182 (paced run) ported too, so all 29 runner cases are now mapped or ported. Elixir's pace_ms waits for finished work between deliveries. In the deterministic scheduler, sync_run (all work finishes between deliveries) is the fully paced extreme and deferred_run (nothing finishes until the end) the unpaced one. scenario_checks gains a_paced_run_ends_where_the_unpaced_run_ends: seed 29, users 2, edits 2, fickle=2, 0.1 duplicates; sync_run with EvictNow holds every rule and commutes with deferred_run. That's the same assertion pair as xsim_test.exs:182 (all verdicts ok; commute with the unpaced run).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_47 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 47u32, "2026-10-23T23:46Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:46 x: the other two Elixir test files, for the coverage map. spiral_test.exs (5 tests: event fixture byte for byte, replay to the final fixture, every e2e world fixture, corpus vs facade, facade vs direct stream) all take cells from Spiral.Cells.Document.parse_spi, so they follow the shared parser (SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY n13); their Spiral form is 'xsim events/world' plus fixture comparisons, already drafted in xsim_entry. x_compat_test.exs (9 tests) exercises Spiral.Cells.XCompat, Kino's Elixir x-compat that x's build no longer uses (apps/client/build.ps1 calls the compiler's --x-compat). Its Spiral home is the compiler-side x-compat (main's), not x's sim. Nothing to port in x; it retires with the Elixir sim.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_48 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 48u32, "2026-10-24T00:12Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:12 x: Elixir retirement checklist for apps/sim/build.ps1, mapping each mix call to its Spiral replacement and the blocker it waits on. (1) :35 mix deps.get -> gone once nothing below needs mix. (2) :53 mix xsim.run --executor cells --memo (ladder) -> xsim run with cells_executor in a BEAM with Spiral.Cells loaded: KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (or a Gleam entry that loads Kino's ebins) + the shared parser. (3) :90 mix xsim.world (4 worlds) -> 'xsim world', drafted: shared parser for byte-equal fixtures (SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY). (4) :95 mix xsim.frames -> 'xsim frames', drafted, with a test byte-comparing the oauth1 fixture: no parser blocker; it can switch first, after its Cells pass and the landing. (5) :100 mix xsim.events -> 'xsim events', drafted: shared parser. (6) :103 mix test -> the Spiral suite (all 29 runner cases mapped/ported) plus spiral_test's 5 fixture tests via (3)/(5); x_compat_test retires (the compiler's x-compat is the gate). (7) :112 mix xsim.run corpus -> 'xsim run' (Rust): shared parser, then X-CORPUS-R1/R2 decide speed. (8) :120 mix xsim.run --executor cells -> as (2). (9) :121/125 mix xsim.thread_run (+ tampered) -> needs cells evaluate: as (2). Order: (4) first; (3)/(5)/(7) when the parser lands; (2)/(8)/(9) with Kino without Elixir; (1) last.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_49 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 49u32, "2026-10-24T00:15Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:15 x: checklist step (4) prepared. The xsim binary needs its own crate, and %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_xsim_crate.ps1 [-Go] -Verdicts <cells_chain logs> creates apps/client/xsim/: a Cargo.toml with [package.metadata.spiral] entry xsim.spi / output xsim.rs, bin xsim, deps sha2 0.11 (newest 0.11.1) + unicode-normalization 0.1 + unicode-segmentation 1.13 (as frames declares them). xsim.spi is x-world's xsim_entry.spi with '--package ..' and 'test xsim static'. It lists xsim/xsim in package.spiproj and build.ps1's notebook list; build.ps1's crate loop picks it up through the metadata, and the workspace (apps/client/*) through the folder. Gates: refuses until apply_sim_modules has landed sim_run/sim_args/scheduler/runner_checks, and without a passing xsim_entry verdict. apply_sim_modules.ps1 no longer lists xsim_entry, which lands only as this crate. Dry-run against landing-trial-4's copy: refused by the first gate, as designed. Then build.ps1:94-96 (mix xsim.frames) can become 'xsim frames', the first mix call to go.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_50 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 50u32, "2026-10-24T00:20Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:20 x: a way to run the cells executor without mix, drafted as a probe. x's own mix build already compiled spiral_cells, kino and table (apps/sim/_build/dev/lib/*/ebin), and Elixir's app ebins are in its install. cells_executor.spi gains cells_vm_started (build_lib) (elixir_lib) : bool: code:add_paths over both <lib>/*/ebin wildcards, then application:ensure_all_started(spiral_cells), returning whether it answered ok. Scratch probe %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-shell\\client\\cells_vm_check.spi, with this machine's paths and NOT for landing: start the VM pieces, then run cells_executor on a passing two-cell notebook (status Done) and a failing one (output differs; I don't assume a failed test turns evaluate's status into an error). Queued after tibia's block. If it passes, diff/thread_run/xsim run --executor cells can run from a Gleam entry on the BEAM with no mix, narrowing the KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dependency to 'the Elixir app must be compiled', not 'mix must run it'.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_51 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 51u32, "2026-10-24T00:29Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:29 x: landing gates hardened (prompted by main's lib-gate pitfall, where 0 rows counted as green). New shared reader %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\read_cells_verdicts.ps1 -Logs <cells_chain logs>: a notebook passes only if 'exit 0' follows a spiral-tests summary with executed > 0 and failed = 0, so a load failure or zero tests can't pass. Each notebook keeps its LATEST verdict by event time (the log's date + its 'chain / HH:mm:ss starting' line). The first version ordered by log write time and got sim_run, xsim_entry and dictm wrong. apply_sim_modules.ps1 and apply_xsim_crate.ps1 now also require every verdict to be newer than the newest .spi edit in the source packages, since a module's pass is stale once any dependency changes. Dry run with a stub lib: refused for all 17 modules, because tonight's edits (newest 20:19) postdate their passes, which is correct; the after-block chain re-runs them.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_52 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 52u32, "2026-10-24T00:36Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:36 x: side-effect check for the cells VM probe (n50) before it runs: Spiral.Cells.Application.start only runs Domain.ensure!, registers a Kino smart cell and starts a Task.Supervisor; no ports, no compile daemon. Under cells_check.ps1's SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process, evaluate spawns its own compiler, so starting spiral_cells inside a test VM can't collide with the shared Cells daemon other teams' runs use.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_53 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 53u32, "2026-10-24T04:01Z", "x", "10-24 00:01 x: milestone (5), the 29th and last Elixir runner case is ported (draft). xsim_test.exs:182, 'a paced run waits for finished work between deliveries and still ends where the unpaced run ends'. Runner.run's pace_ms is the yield_many timeout after each delivery, so it only means something in the concurrent shell. runner_shell gains paced_settled inbox pace_ms l (Deliver, then wait pace_ms on an idle subject, as slow_executor does, then Settle). The test runs fickle_churn (like/unlike churn plus a duplicate delivery) paced at 2 ms under EvictNow and requires every runner_checks finding Held and runs_commute against the unpaced shell = ok, Elixir's two asserts. Helper renamed every_finding_held because invariants.all_held exists and runner_shell opens invariants. Drafted on a copy (%TEMP%\\x-shell-paced) so chain21's x-shell compiles stay unaffected; it moves into x-shell after Cells passes (job x-paced-run-check).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_54 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 54u32, "2026-10-24T05:36Z", "x", "10-24 01:36 x: milestone (5) DONE in draft. With paced_settled and the paced test, runner_shell.spi passes 7/7 on gleam (job-1792817010528, 01:34), so all 29 Elixir runner cases are now mapped or ported. Moved from %TEMP%\\x-shell-paced into %TEMP%\\x-shell\\client (chain21c checks it next). It lands with the x landing (apply_sim_modules).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_55 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 55u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 13 14:39 (subagent2): blocked (no change since 14:12): SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL Planned 0% under KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, port code in the x tree -> next: unblocks: x lane takes it, or SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL starts | lap 14 15:03 (subagent2): blocked: SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL Planned 0%; port code is x-lane territory -> next: x lane takes it | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): blocked (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL, x lane) -> next: x lane | lap 19 18:56 (subagent2): blocked (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM; x lane owns the bulk half) -> next: x lane")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_56 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 56u32, "2026-10-25T06:20Z", "x", "10-25 02:20 x: LANDING PREP for the 17 sim modules (apply_sim_modules.ps1 list), which are still only in %TEMP%\\x-world\\client and %TEMP%\\x-shell\\client. The lib blocker is gone (dictm.spi live since 10-24 20:43). Staged in %TEMP%\\x-simland-0214: all 17 respelled to edition 3 with the installed 5j format --spelling symbols, 0 test headers touched, words round trip identical. x-compat on the staged client gives 0 findings in 61 files, BUT the build's postable gate (xc post-thread --dry-run per file) finds 121 cells over one post (126 in the edition-2 originals). These modules were never fitted to 280 (n37's XC005 fit pass). So landing order: (1) fit pass by hand on the staged copies until xc dry-run spills 0; (2) Cells on all 17 against today's lib and 5k (Rust targets wait for 5k because of 5j's E0507 regression; their 10-24 verdicts predate the lib respell and 5j, so they don't count); (3) copy into apps/client, list after invariants in package.spiproj and in build.ps1's notebook list (the lander's 'unstaged package.spiproj/build.ps1' refusal is obsolete, since x's hand units already edit both); (4) a verify. Biggest spills: scheduler 23, sim_args 23, runner_shell 13, scenario_checks 13, runner_checks 11, inbox 10.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_57 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 57u32, "2026-10-25T07:06Z", "x", "10-25 03:06 x: FIT PASS findings (staged modules in %TEMP%\\x-simland-0214\\stage; list sugar applied, 120 spills -> fitting by hand; executors and scheduler_parity now 0). Two modules need a design change, not a fit. (1) checks_parity and inbox_parity embed the Elixir sim's generated rows/frames as ~8 KB string literals (xsim.parity.ex writes them into .spi). Generated data can't be split into 280-weight cells sensibly and isn't source. Move it to fixture files beside the reader (apps/client/fixtures/prelude.rows.jsonl etc., written by xsim.parity), read through lib file_system.read_all_text, which has a Gleam arm since LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS. (2) cells_executor's cells_gleam_prelude/cells_vm_prelude are hand-written Gleam FFI blocks (global triple-quoted text): GRANULAR-HEAL drift. Splitting them to fit would spread the drift, so they get rebuilt as one-expression primitives (lib arms where generic) before landing. The other 13 modules are real code and get fitted by splitting definitions.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_58 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 58u32, "2026-10-25T07:20Z", "x", "10-25 03:20 x: fit pass progress on the staged modules (%TEMP%\\x-simland-0214\\stage; gauge spills.ps1 = xc post-thread --dry-run per file): at 0 now are tree_memory, executors, scheduler_parity, memory_watch, sim_report, sim_run, xsim_shell_entry and runner_guard (8 of 17). Splits only extract helpers, keep field and line order, and use ++# instead of ^^ (DRIFT-MAP 6). runner_guard gained aliases reporter/handle/worker instead of opening erlang_process unqualified (avoids silent shadowing). Left: 95 cells in inbox 10, runner_checks 11, runner_shell 13, scenario_checks 13, scheduler 19, sim_args 23, plus the n57 redesigns (checks_parity/inbox_parity fixtures to data files, cells_executor FFI to primitives). Nothing compiled yet: Cells on all 17 comes after the fit.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_59 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 59u32, "2026-10-25T07:29Z", "x", "10-25 03:29 x: fit pass: 11 of 17 staged modules at 0 spills (added inbox, runner_checks, runner_shell since n58). Techniques: helpers that keep order and semantics; shared test fixtures (liked_pair, two_thread, lapse_of, naive_faults); type aliases for long Erlang types (handle, reporter, worker, shell_inbox, shell_next, demand, maker, tally_bump); one otp_actor.continue around a next_state match; the content/revision split of applied_event inside the existing mutual-recursion group. Left: scenario_checks 13, scheduler 19, sim_args 23, plus the n57 redesigns (6).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_60 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 60u32, "2026-10-25T07:46Z", "x", "10-25 03:46 x: fit pass state, still uncompiled: 14 of 17 staged modules at 0 spills (adds scenario_checks and scheduler). sim_args is being remodeled, not just split: its flag chains become per-family flag unions (corpus_flag, schedule_flag, runner_flag) with flag_of + set + an optionm.map glue, keeping flag precedence and every error text; 20 cells left there. Tools in %TEMP%\\x-simland-0214: spills.ps1 (xc dry-run gauge), break_heads.ps1 / break_pipes.ps1 (syntax-safe <=120 line breaks at a definition's '=' or before a top-level '|>'), plus a check that every column-0 definition follows a blank line (two joins found and fixed). New generic helper to move to lib later (lib-first): first_some (option or-else). Then the n57 redesigns, Cells on all 17 (Rust after 5k; zig after main's index_of land), and landing.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_61 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 61u32, "2026-10-25T07:52Z", "x", "10-25 03:52 x: FIT PASS DONE for the 14 code modules: inbox, scheduler, runner_checks, runner_shell, runner_guard, scenario_checks, sim_args, sim_report, sim_run, xsim_shell_entry, memory_watch, tree_memory, executors and scheduler_parity all spill 0 cells in xc's dry run, with no new lines over 120. sim_args is remodeled with per-family flag unions (corpus/schedule/runner/events flags; flag_of + set + optionm.map glue) and split text-flag setters joined by first_some; flag precedence and every error text are unchanged. Left before Cells: (1) checks_parity and inbox_parity: their ~8 KB generated Elixir rows/frames move out of .spi into fixture files read at test time (or one tiny def per row; decide by whether Cells resolves a path relative to the notebook); (2) cells_executor: the hand-written Gleam FFI (global triple-quoted blocks) gets rebuilt as one-expression primitives. Then Cells on all 17 (Rust and Gleam targets; zig after main's index_of land) on the staged copy, then landing via the apply_sim_modules list.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_62 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 62u32, "2026-10-25T09:25Z", "x", "2026-10-25 05:25 -04:00 x: the three redesigns are drafted, and lib + Cells pieces landed unstaged. (1) lib/spiral/erlang_term.spi, new and listed in lib package.spiproj (the 'Erlang-term builder' from note 35). It holds term/atom types, of/cast coercion, atom, terms_of/list_term, tuple, inspect, equal, apply (erlang:apply, one host call), charlist, and 5 gleam tests. Callers need no @external, so x code has no mention findings. (2) cells_executor is rebuilt over it: doc, opts, evaluate, status/verdicts/outputs, the vm start. Every def is under one post. (3) The parity fixtures moved out of the source. fixtures/prelude.jsonl holds the 65 Elixir-recorded deliveries as an xsim-events v1 stream (converted from the old literal list), and inbox_parity loads it through decoded_stream. A new test, the_prelude_fixture_is_a_canonical_stream, checks that stream(decoded) equals the file. fixtures/prelude_checks_{fake,wrong}.json hold the expected Elixir rows for checks_parity. (4) Cells fix (apps/cells targets.ex build_and_run_gleam): a Gleam program now runs with cwd = the notebook root, like Rust's execute. Before, it ran in the temp gleam package, so relative fixture paths failed (probe job-1792917428269-x-cwd-probe: rust passed, gleam 'file:read_file failed'). New ExUnit test in targets_test.exs. (5) The previous Cells run (job-1792914892304) failed every module 0/N for one cause: my >120 line breaker split record TYPES at a field without a trailing ';' (scheduler run_counts/scheduler, sim_args, runner_shell). The parser reads the next field as a type argument ('Expected: }') and drops the block. Re-laid in x-client style ('a; b;' then aligned continuation). Wrapped record VALUES parse fine. (6) lib renamed receive/received_value/selector_receive (2cc407ce) to receive_within/receipt_value/selector_receive_within, so runner_guard and runner_shell are updated. All 17 modules post one cell per post (xc post-thread --dry-run: 0 spills), with no line over 120. Gate: job-1792920093887-x-simland-serial2 (targets_test, then the erlang_term lib gate, then the 17 modules).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_63 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 63u32, "2026-10-25T11:24Z", "x", "2026-10-25 07:24 -04:00 x: serial3 interim (job-1792923581594). lib erlang_term 5/5 (the earlier 1/5 was a 300 s load timeout). scheduler 5/5. inbox 2/6: the 4 failures are gleam legs timing out at 300 s under load (0.7 GB free, CPU 100%), rust legs pass. inbox_parity and scheduler_parity PASS on gleam, which proves the Cells gleam-cwd fix: ../sim/fixtures resolves from the notebook dir. They fail only on 'rust -d sha2' with NotFound: a Rust cell with deps runs through the spiral CLI (spiral rust --rs-path ... -> cargo run), and Cells starts that CLI with cd nil (targets.ex run_status 'spiral? -> nil', from the initial Cells import), so the program inherits Cells' cwd. Plain rust (rustc + execute) and F# already run in the notebook root. Fix drafted (scratchpad\\cells-cwd\\apply.ps1): spiral? runs start in opts[:root] when it is a directory, as F# does. It applies after serial3 ends (Cells reads the live tree); then rerun the failing modules. The ExUnit gleam-cwd test can't run in that env ('problem when running the shell command escript' while building a fresh gleam project), so the parity modules are the proof.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_64 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 64u32, "2026-10-25T14:27Z", "x", "2026-10-25 10:27 serial3 (job-1792923581594, 3.9 h on a box at 100% CPU / 2.3 GB free): erlang_term 5/5 green. Sim modules fully green: scheduler 5/5, sim_run, executors, cells_executor. Failures, three kinds: (1) cells at the 300 s limit while compiling (inbox, scenario_checks, sim_args, sim_report, runner_checks one test, tree_memory rustup) = load. (2) the Rust parity modules (inbox_parity, scheduler_parity, checks_parity) panic with os error 3 opening ../sim/fixtures = the spiral CLI cwd is nil; scratchpad cells-cwd patch fixes it, waits for no Cells reader. (3) Gleam actor waits for an event that always comes timed out at 1000-5000 ms (memory_watch call 1000, runner_shell Settle 5000 = the executor timeout itself, runner_guard crash deadline 1000, leak call 1000). Rule: a deadline bounding an expected event goes to at least lib otp_actor's 10000; one whose expiry is the asserted outcome stays short. Raised in stage: init 10000, Settle 30000, Peak 10000, leak 10000, crash 10000 / inbox wait 20000. Short ones kept: stall 50, signalled_in 100/300. Rerun queued as job-1792938325158-x-simland-serial4.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_65 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 65u32, "2026-10-25T15:50Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:50 -04:00 x: serial4 (job-1792938325158, the raised actor deadlines plus load reruns): 9 of 11 modules fully green. memory_watch 3/3, runner_guard 2/2, executors 1/1, xsim_shell_entry 2/2, runner_checks 9/9, sim_report 2/2, tree_memory 3/3 (rust+gleam), inbox 6/6, sim_args 4/4. So the n64 deadline diagnosis held: every actor test that failed at 1000-5000 ms passes at the new deadlines. Left: runner_shell 6/7 and scenario_checks 8/12, all cells at the 300 s limit (slowest 302 s; serial3's slowest in scenario_checks was 343 s). Rule: a sim module that hit the limit in either run takes x's existing heavy-module header '///- --package . --timeout 600000', as scenario.spi, world.spi, protocol.spi and 4 others already do. Applied in stage to inbox, runner_checks, runner_shell, scenario_checks, sim_args, sim_report and tree_memory. Rerun of the two still red: job-1792943380223-x-simland-serial5. Still open: the 3 Rust parity modules (cwd patch, after main's Cells/lib chain).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_66 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 66u32, "2026-10-25T15:58Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:58 -04:00 x: the three Cells/lib drafts that wait for no Cells or lib reader are now ONE landing with undo: scratchpad\\land_cells_lib.ps1 [-Spiral] [-X] [-Undo]. (1) cells-cwd: spiral CLI runs (Rust with deps) start in the notebook root; it fixes the 3 Rust parity modules' os error 3. (2) cells-defused: x-compat skips links whose dots the profile's poster defuses, plus its test (X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE). (3) code-points: lib unicode code_points/is_white_space plus x glyphs' heal (GRANULAR-HEAL n8). Hardened on the way: cells-defused's Swap now refuses an anchor that matches more than once (it used Contains + Replace-all), and both Cells patches take -Base for rehearsal. A copy rehearsal (scratchpad\\rehearse_cells_lib.ps1) changed 5/5 files, left glyphs with 0 host macros/globals, and undo restored 5/5 hash-equal. Its window: no Cells/lib reader running. Today main-serial-lib-lands-4 and main-cells-trailing-docs are live, and main, tibia and fiscal have ~12 lib jobs queued. Gate after landing: Cells lib unicode (rust+gleam); x glyphs, splitter, reading; the 3 parity modules on rust; mix test x_compat_test.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_67 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 67u32, "2026-10-25T16:10Z", "x", "2026-10-25 12:10 -04:00 x: serial5 (job-1792943380223) with the --timeout 600000 header: runner_shell 7/7, scenario_checks 12/12. So 14 of the 17 sim modules are fully green in Cells. Only the 3 Rust parity modules are left (cwd patch, in land_cells_lib.ps1), and only they import each other (scheduler_parity and checks_parity import inbox_parity), so the 14 could land alone. NOT landed, by rule: modules land into x only when x's own build can pass with the INSTALLED tools. apps/client/build.ps1 fails on any x-compat finding. The installed compiler reports 18 autolink findings on the staged modules (otp_actor.call/next/new, optionm.map x7, erlang_process.pid, t.work, r.events), and the tree's compiler (a candidate built from the tree core) reports 0 in the same 17 files with the same profile. All 18 are identifier dots the poster defuses, which is exactly X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE's fix. So the sim landing (land_client.ps1, stage files) waits for that fix to be installed in a pack. When it is: land_cells_lib.ps1 (cwd), rerun the 3 parity modules, then land_client.ps1 with all 17.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_68 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 68u32, "2026-10-25T16:12Z", "x", "2026-10-25 12:12 -04:00 x: land_cells_lib.ps1 is queued in main's serial lib chain after list-sugar-rest as job-1792944559767-main-serial-x-cells-unicode. First the gate: lib-gate-kit on unicode.spi, with the apply run on a lib copy and a throwaway glyphs copy. Then the land behind the reader guard, then mix test x_compat_test.exs, with -Undo on failure. Frozen until main sends the verdict: the 5 target files and the 4 apply scripts. After it lands, x runs Cells on glyphs, splitter and reading plus the 3 parity modules.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_69 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 69u32, "2026-10-25T18:20Z", "x", "2026-10-25 14:20 -04:00 x: 14 of 17 sim modules LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x as an unstaged layer: inbox, scheduler, runner_checks, scenario_checks, tree_memory, sim_report, sim_args, sim_run, executors, cells_executor, runner_guard, runner_shell, memory_watch, xsim_shell_entry. land_client.ps1 -Only adds them to package.spiproj, the build's Invoke-SpiralTests list and 14 README rows. Why now: the installed 5l'' compiler carries the defused-dot x-compat, which reports 0 findings on all 17 (it was 18 on 5k). Checks before writing: a dry run on a fresh copy (58 files, x-compat 0, 0 cells over one post, 0 lines over 120, no parity module leaked), and the Cells-verified copy equals the tree (all 14 modules, every other client .spi, both sim fixtures hash-equal). So serial3-5's Cells greens carry over, against the tree lib of that time. Left: inbox_parity, scheduler_parity, checks_parity, which wait for the cwd patch (main's job-1792944559767); then land_client.ps1 -Only those 3; then x verify.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_70 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 70u32, "2026-10-25T18:29Z", "x", "2026-10-25 14:29 -04:00 x: x verify moved to the idle second laptop (i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1; moon at 7% CPU, 9 GB free). Synced spiral (head 47d1e562 + 306 changed files, digest 4003804B6714CCEC) and x (head 9b3fbe8e + 18 changed, digest 8557C07D7269E678), both REMOTE-SYNC-OK. Running x/scripts/build.ps1 there: job-1792952960245-x-verify-remote1 (laptop eoie, 4 h limit). Expected to stop at protocol.spi's zig target until main's LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF lands (main-serial-zig-index-of-3). Everything before it in the build (gate, x-compat on 5l'' with the new exit-code check, release xc, e2e, postable over 58 files) is evidence for this landing and for X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_71 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 71u32, "2026-10-25T18:42Z", "x", "2026-10-25 14:42 -04:00 x: the laptop verify (job-1792952960245) failed at the api crate compile ('Unbound term variable: erlang_term'). Cause: remote.ps1 sync, not x. It sent 'git ls-files -m -o', which never includes STAGED files, so the laptop's spiral lacked lib/spiral/erlang_term.spi (status A at sync time; the user committed it at 14:35, d095f13d). The fix (diff against HEAD + untracked) is handed to kit2, who now owns remote.ps1. Local control (job-1792953465762): the api crate compiles on the installed 5l'' against the full local lib (exit 0). Its output differs from the committed api .rs only by 5l'''s closure-move clones (14 lines), so x's next build regenerates the committed crate outputs for the user to commit. The full x verify waits for main's serial chain (list-sugar-rest, zig-index-of-3, then my x-cells-unicode), so one run covers protocol.spi's zig target and the 3 parity modules. No laptop syncs until main or kit2 clear the node.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_72 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 72u32, "2026-10-25T18:54Z", "x", "2026-10-25 14:54 -04:00 x: user asked (10-25) whether the 14 new files in apps/client are drift or proper evolution. Assessment from the import graph: granularity is right (hand-written units, explicit :< imports, none split automatically), the HOME is wrong. They import client modules (prelude 14, events 9, invariants 5, glyphs/personas/scenario/schedule 2, splitter 1), and NO client module imports them, so they are a separate layer: the Spiral port of xsim. In apps/client they add type-check work to every one of the 23 crate compiles (each crate entry checks the whole client package), and the client build would run their Cells tests. Proper shape: their own package x/apps/sim/spi/package.spiproj (packageDir ../../../../spiral/lib; packages |core-, spiral-, ../../client-, as tibia apps/arena uses ../../src-), next to the Elixir sim they port. apps/sim/build.ps1 runs their Invoke-SpiralTests plus an x-compat pass. The 3 parity modules' fixture path becomes ../fixtures/. NOT moved yet: the user was staging the 14 at the time (status A/??), and staged files mid-run are the user's. The move waits for the user's go or their commit, then goes on top as git renames. Also seen: the laptop's verify2 failed on x-compat 18 findings = a pre-5l'' compiler there (the node never got the 5l'' install); reported to kit2.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_73 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 73u32, "2026-10-25T19:17Z", "x", "2026-10-25 15:17 -04:00 x: MOVED (user 10-25: 'move everything properly, ignore my git actions'). The Spiral sim now has its own package, x/apps/sim/spi (package.spiproj: packageDir ../../../../spiral/lib; packages |core-, spiral-, ../../client-). Placement rule, from the import graph (scratch client_graph.ps1): a client module moves when no client crate entry reaches it. 35 modules are reached by the 23 client crates and stay. Sim-only, and moved: exsss, events, final_state, personas, scenario, schedule, world, invariants, simframes/ (the Gleam frames entry), plus the 14 runner modules. Shared and staying in the client: prelude, glyphs, splitter, protocol (the sim reaches them through ../../client-). Updated: client package.spiproj, build.ps1 test list and README (rows plus the simframes paragraph moved to apps/sim/spi/README.md); apps/sim/build.ps1 (simframes path x2; an x-compat pass with the exit-code check; Invoke-SpiralTests over the 23 sim modules when not -fast); root Cargo.toml exclude drops apps/client/simframes. The client's package.spiproj and README after step 1 were byte-equal to HEAD. Checked on a copy before applying: --check exit 0 for client xc, glyphs and sim scenario_checks, simframes, xsim_shell_entry; x-compat 0 in client (35 files) and sim/spi (23). Cells from the new home: job-1792955719853 (invariants, events, inbox, xsim_shell_entry, simframes). The 3 parity modules now read ../fixtures/; land_parity.ps1 (rehearsed) puts them in apps/sim/spi after the cwd patch lands.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_74 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 74u32, "2026-10-25T23:41Z", "x", "2026-10-25 19:41 -04:00 (written earlier under the eoie lease) x: node verifies after the move (the node core is 826D67C69119F61E, determinism-checked equal to the review box by kit2). verify3 (job-1792957554034): cargo failed on an empty apps/client/simframes left by sync (fixed in remote.ps1 by kit2), and the sim build failed at fixture compare: sim-small.world.json embeds prelude.spi's text, which the list-sugar respell changed. verify4 (job-1792958570590): client 0 x-compat, release build ok, e2e 1 failure, 'xc timeline hana' (CJK and Hangul) DIFFERENT. Cause: e2e's Invoke-Step decodes xc's redirected UTF-8 with the console's default encoding, which over the node's ssh session isn't UTF-8; it passes locally. FIXED in apps/client/e2e/e2e.ps1: the child pwsh sets [Console]::OutputEncoding/$OutputEncoding to UTF-8 (no BOM), and ProcessStartInfo sets StandardOutputEncoding/StandardErrorEncoding to UTF-8, so e2e reads xc the same on every machine. The sim -Accept regenerated 10 fixtures, reviewed with a paren-aware Cons respeller (scratch review_accepted.ps1): 3 differ only by 'Cons (a, b)' -> 'a :: b'; the world and event files also move post boundaries (the shorter respelled text splits into posts at different places); parity.txt digests change with the sources; nothing else. Accepted into the tree. Also seen: mix xsim.thread_run on oauth1.spi 'status=error/error result=fail' with no detail; verify5 (job-1792962117792) reruns it with its log captured, and runs e2e -Accept for the README outputs that the new sim worlds change.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_75 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 75u32, "2026-10-29T12:29Z", "x", "2026-10-29 08:5x x groom (read-only census of the tree; nothing built). What is still Elixir in apps/sim: lib 1,256 lines in 20 files, tests 475. The PRODUCTION PATH IS SPLIT. Scenario, corpus and world files already run as Spiral (Gleam) through the 35-line Xsim.Spiral bridge (simframes/xsim_flow/xsim_world exports). But the mix tasks xsim.run and xsim.parity still drive Elixir World (154), Runner (191), DaemonMemory (161), Executors (63), Invariants (54), Log (55), Scenario (32) and Frames (53), while their Spiral ports (sim_run.simulated, runner_shell, scheduler, executors, cells_executor, memory_watch, tree_memory, invariants) pass in Cells and export nothing to Elixir. The 3 *_parity Spiral modules are NOT a redo: they were reshaped on 10-25 into xsim.parity + fixtures/prelude.parity.txt (SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL n13), so their lost TEMP stage copies are dropped. The Cells cwd patch they waited for is live (targets.ex:587). PLAN, pre-registered, by slice, each closing on a byte-equal XSIM line set against the Elixir path for the same seed/schedule (the parity rule): S1 export sim_run.simulated (fake executor) and route 'xsim.run --executor fake' through it; delete Runner/World/Invariants/Scenario/Frames uses on that path. S2 replay/save over xsim_events (decoded_stream/stream) replaces Xsim.Log. S3 cells executor over cells_executor + memo tally. S4 daemon-memory line over memory_watch/tree_memory. S5 xsim.parity on the Spiral path, then delete the Elixir modules and their ExUnit tests (ported tests already exist as Cells tests). Estimate by rule: 90 min per slice (one sim compile + one parity run each) = 7.5 h; load heavy (sim build). Not started today while the node is saturated (6 jobs, CPU 100%) and the local heavy slot is TRAIN3's. Then SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY unblocks.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_76 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 76u32, "2026-10-29T13:22Z", "x", "2026-10-29 09:2x x: S1 DRAFTED (not applied, not built): x/lib-programs/sim-in-spiral-s1.kit, 3 files, 6 replacements, proven byte-exact on a copy, no new line over 120. (1) simframes.spi imports sim_args + sim_run and exports simulated_lines (args, threads) = run_args (default_run ()) args, then simulated o threads joined by newline, or 'XSIM rejected <why>'. (2) Xsim.Spiral.simulated_lines/2 joins threads with \\0/\\1 like corpus_run. (3) xsim.run gains an OPT-IN '--engine spiral' first argument (run_spiral: cells read by the same cells_of; exit 1 on 'XSIM rejected' or 'result=fail'). The Elixir path is untouched, so both engines can run side by side; S5 flips the default. PARITY RULE, revised and pre-registered before any run: Spiral uses sync_run, while the Elixir Runner runs deliveries concurrently (max_concurrency 4), and Spiral writes ms=0/beam_mb=0. So byte-equal XSIM lines would be wrong. Instead, run the Elixir engine TWICE on the same args (A/A); fields that differ between those two runs are scheduling-dependent and excluded; Spiral must equal Elixir on every remaining field of every schedule line, plus the 'XSIM commute=... result=...' line. Args: the verify sim build's corpus run (--spi-file list --schedules 2 --users 3 --edits 3 --breaking 1 --churn 2 --frames --duplicates 0.15 --personas ... --evict). If sim_args rejects a flag Elixir accepts, that's an S1 finding, not a pass. Run: one node job (sim build + 3 mix xsim.run), after verify29 frees the node.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_77 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 77u32, "2026-10-29T15:23Z", "x", "2026-10-29 11:2x x: S2 DRAFTED (not applied, not built): x/lib-programs/sim-in-spiral-s2.kit (sim_run.spi only, so it composes with S1; proven byte-exact on a copy, no line over 120). sim_args already parsed --replay/--save, but sim_run ignored them. Now deliveries_for replays (decoded_stream of file_system::read_all_text; a decode error fails with 'xsim: --replay <path>: <why>', as Xsim.Log.read raises) or generates, then saved_as writes 'stream l' to the --save pattern with {schedule} replaced (saved_path; Cells test a_save_pattern_names_each_schedule). FORMAT CHECK: Xsim.Log and events.spi write the same xsim-events v1 text (header line + one JSON delivery per line + trailing newline), so Elixir-saved runs replay on the Spiral engine and vice versa. Proof is cross-engine in the queued node job (scratch launch-x-queue.ps1, after verify29): Elixir saves math.spi's run with --save, the Spiral engine replays schedule 1 with --replay; both exit 0 with equal deliveries= and an xsim-events header. Then S2 retires Xsim.Log's uses on the spiral path.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_78 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 78u32, "2026-10-29T18:46Z", "x", "2026-10-29 15:0x x: first S1/S2 node runs (x-queue1, x-queue2 on pack-6b) and what they found. (a) S1 as drafted (simframes opening sim_args + sim_run) failed type-check: sim_args defines its own world_files, which shadowed world.spi's in simframes (simframes.spi:56 unification failure). Redesigned: simframes opens neither; the glue moved into sim_run.spi as simulated_text (sim_run already opens sim_args), and simframes calls sim_run::simulated_text qualified. S1 and S2 now share sim_run.spi, so they are ONE program: x/lib-programs/sim-in-spiral-s1s2.kit (4 files, 8 replacements, proven byte-exact on a copy; the separate s1/s2 programs were removed). (b) With that, the Spiral compile passes, but x/scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 (the post-hoc splitter) broke the build: lib dictm.spi declares '@external(erlang, \"lists\", \"sort\") fn dictm_term_sorted(...)' on ONE line, and the splitter read any line starting with @ as an attribute only, so the fn was never registered: it rode along as a private attribute of the next item, and other parts called it unimported ('Unknown variable'). FIXED: the parser recognizes '@...) fn name(' as a fn item, and Publish makes it '@...) pub fn'. Proven: the HEAD script reproduces the exact failure on a crafted module and the fixed one emits the external as its own pub part imported by both callers; neutral on today's output (0 one-line declarations among the 19 tracked .gleam files; Publish old == new on 6 multi-line shapes). (c) My parity tool passed VACUOUSLY on the failed build (0 lines, exits 1/1/1, '0 differences'); it now exits 2 'VOID' when an engine fails or no lines exist (a gate that can't fail is worse than none). (d) The launcher's mirror lacked x\\target for the corpus list; fixed. Rerun: x-queue2 job-1793299668905.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_79 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 79u32, "2026-10-29T23:06Z", "x", "2026-10-29 19:06 x: S3a DRAFTED (cells executor on the spiral engine): x/lib-programs/sim-in-spiral-s3a.kit, 5 files, 8 replacements, 71 added lines, applies AFTER sim-in-spiral-s1s2.kit, proven byte-exact on a fresh post-s1s2 copy, no added line over 120. DESIGN: sim_run.spi also compiles to Rust while cells_executor.spi is BEAM-only (erlang_term), so sim_run never imports it. simulated_text takes the cells executor as a function, and simframes (Gleam) passes cells_executor::cells_executor root; root comes from Elixir as a third export field (Path.dirname of the first spi, like Xsim.Executors.cells). sim_run matches o::runner::executor in executor_pair_of: fake gives fake_result/fake_baseline; cells gives run = baseline = cells and judges_breaking; fake-leak is REJECTED as 'XSIM rejected --executor fake-leak has no spiral engine yet', because the leak needs otp_actor and the frames package has no gleam_otp (S3b, with memo). Parity is total: invariants gains BreakingEditsFail with an explicit rule_name arm (the _ fallback would have printed it as withdrawn_demand_hidden), and judged appends breaking_failed (runner_checks, already ported) for cells only, like xsim.run.ex:40. Tests: the_executor_flag_picks_the_run_function, only_the_cells_executor_judges_breaking_edits. LOCAL PROOF: simframes compiles to Gleam (61 s, 690 KB), imports no gleam/otp, one Spiral.Cells.Notebook evaluate call. PARITY RULE, pre-registered: args --spi <one notebook> --cells 3 --schedules 2 --users 2 --edits 1 --breaking 1 --executor cells. Elixir runs twice (A/A); fields differing between those runs are excluded; the Spiral engine must equal every remaining field, breaking_edits_fail included, and the commute line. NEG: the post-s1s2 engine on the same args (it ignores --executor) must LACK breaking_edits_fail. Queued after queue2's S1/S2 verdict; S3b = memo tally line + fake-leak.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_80 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 80u32, "2026-10-29T23:14Z", "x", "2026-10-29 19:14 x: S3a CORRECTED before any run. Drafting S3b showed that cells_executor.run_opts passed only root and spiral_lib, and Spiral.Cells.Memo.enabled? (apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/memo.ex:64) treats an ABSENT :memo as ON (unless SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0). Elixir always passes memo: false by default (xsim.run.ex:24-25), so as drafted the Spiral engine would have run memoized against an unmemoized Elixir engine. Fix in S3a: run_opts passes {memo, false} explicitly; S3b threads --memo through. Program is now 6 files, 9 replacements (adds cells_executor.spi), byte-exact on a fresh post-s1s2 copy, 0 added lines over 120. Launcher C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s3a\\launch-x-s3a.ps1 (10 stage files: s1s2.* for the NEG, s3a.* for the slice), one serial job on the warm xqueue2 mirror after queue2: post-s1s2 fast build and NEG run, S3a FULL sim build (every Cells test, since invariants changed), cells parity, fake-leak rejection line. S3b design (not drafted until S3a is proven): fake-leak through erlang:put, which returns the previous value (the leak's own semantics, one host call, distinct keys for run and baseline like Elixir's two ets tables); memo tally through an erlang counters ref threaded as a value (mirrors :counters in Xsim.Executors), with counts from lists:filter over the memo flags using a Spiral closure as the Erlang fun, then erlang:length.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_81 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 81u32, "2026-10-29T23:27Z", "x", "2026-10-29 19:27 x: S3b DRAFTED (text only, no box compile per main's review rule): x/lib-programs/sim-in-spiral-s3b.kit, 3 files, 8 replacements, 131 added lines, applies AFTER s3a, byte-exact on a fresh post-s3a copy, 0 added lines over 120. sim_run: beam_executors record (cells : bool -> executor, cells_baseline, leak : string -> executor, memo_counts); executor_pair gains notes : i32 -> list string; the fake-leak rejection is gone; with --memo, every schedule line is followed by '  memo hits=H misses=M cumulative_after_schedule=N', also under the fake executor, like xsim.run.ex:43. cells_executor (BEAM): run_opts takes memo; cells_counted root memo tally; memo_tally = counters:new(2,[atomics]); flag_count = erlang:length(lists:filter(<Spiral closure as the Erlang fun>, maps:values(memo))); dictionary_leak = erlang:put, whose return value is the previous digest (keys xsim_leak_run and xsim_leak_baseline, like Elixir's two ets tables). simframes builds one beam record (one tally) per run. New tests: the_executor_flag_picks_the_run_and_baseline, only_memo_runs_reach_the_cells_run_and_not_the_baseline, the_memo_line_follows_each_schedule_only_with_memo, a_dictionary_leak_carries_the_previous_digest, memo_flags_are_counted_per_kind. Tool: x/scripts/sim-engine-parity.ps1 gains -ExpectFail (every engine must exit 1; anything else is VOID) and compares '  memo' lines too (backward compatible; checked case by case, including the trap where @(0) is falsy). PRE-REGISTERED, as the same serial node job's third phase (launch-x-s3a.ps1, 13 stage files): S3b full sim build green; cells + --memo parity has 0 differences on A/A-stable fields, memo lines included; fake-leak parity with -ExpectFail: both engines exit 1 and agree on stable fields, otherwise VOID (config too small).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_82 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 82u32, "2026-10-29T23:28Z", "x", "2026-10-29 19:28 x: S4 (--daemon-memory line) has a named DESIGN BLOCKER, not drafted. The Spiral sampler memory_watch.spi is an otp_actor (watch_started -> otp_actor::start; ticks fold tree_memory readings), and the frames Gleam package that hosts the spiral engine (apps/sim/frames/gleam.toml) depends only on gleam_stdlib, gleam_erlang and xsim_split; no gleam_otp. S3a/S3b avoided OTP (cells via erlang_term calls, the leak via erlang:put), but a periodic sampler with state cannot. Options, measured before choosing (rule experiment-until-sota): (a) add gleam_otp to frames (a deps change for every sim build: manifest, hex fetch on node mirrors, splitter parts); (b) run the daemon-memory sampler from the Elixir side of run_spiral and pass its readings into simulated_lines, so the Spiral engine stays OTP-free until S5 deletes the Elixir path; (c) host the spiral engine in the shell package (xsim_shell_entry already uses otp_actor via sim-shell's full Cells deps). Decide after S3a/S3b's node verdicts, by the build-time and line cost of each.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_83 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 83u32, "2026-10-29T23:55Z", "x", "2026-10-29 19:55 x: PATHS MOVED (lab rule: x/lib-programs was a lab-style folder I created in the x repo today, untracked). Drained by hash-verified move, then the empty folder was removed. The unproven sim slices now live in C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\sim-programs\\ (sim-in-spiral-s1s2.kit, -s3a.kit, -s3b.kit). The 9 landed or applied programs (fs-heal-4/4b/4c, lib-gate-copy-daemon, otp-actor-named, physics-scalar-force, process-counter, process-counter.x-net, x-fields-cells) are archived in C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\landed-programs\\. The reusable lander is now i574n.github/scripts/land-lib-program.ps1, next to the lib-guarded-do.ps1 it wraps (path fixed; positive control: already-at-target exit 0, no write). Earlier notes citing x/lib-programs/<name> mean these places. The S3a/S3b launcher stages from C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s3a\\stage and is unaffected.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_84 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 84u32, "2026-10-30T00:39Z", "x", "2026-10-29 20:39 x: QUEUE2 RESULT (job-1793306240325, finished exit 1). S1 sim build 0; S1 corpus parity VOID (elixir 0/0, spiral exit 1 with 0 lines); S2 spiral replay exit 1; shell launcher positive 0, negative 1 (as expected). ROOT CAUSE, MINE, in S1: xsim.run.ex run_spiral matched OptionParser.parse/2 as {opts, _}, but it returns {parsed, rest, invalid}, so every --engine spiral run died with a MatchError (xsim.run.ex:66) before printing anything. Reproduced directly in the xqueue2 mirror. Kit's job tail showed an empty stdout, so I fetched eoie\\jobs\\<id>\\stdout.log raw. Fixed to {opts, _, _} in sim-in-spiral-s1s2.kit and both staged xsim.run.ex files; chain s1s2 -> s3a -> s3b re-proved byte-exact on a fresh copy of live x (s3a program bytes unchanged). IN-MIRROR CHECK with the fix, math.spi --cells 3 --users 2 --edits 1 --schedules 2: spiral exit 0, all invariants ok, commute=ok result=pass. Small parity: every A/A-stable field equal EXCEPT peak_beam_mb (elixir 50 in both A/A runs, spiral 0 by design, see n76's preamble). By n76's pre-registered rule that is 2 differences, so S1 is NOT passed; no post-hoc exclusion. Next: S1b makes the spiral engine measure BEAM memory (erlang:memory(total) via the beam record) so the field is compared, not excluded. Tooling: sim-engine-parity now prints each failing engine's last lines on VOID (queue2 lost the MatchError because the tool kept only XSIM lines). RELAUNCHED as one serial job x-s3 (job-1793320859185, xqueue2, 8h cap): s1s2 build + NEG, S1 corpus parity (n76 args) + S2 replay, S3a full build + cells parity + leak rejection, S3b full build + memo parity + leak ExpectFail parity.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_85 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 85u32, "2026-10-30T00:41Z", "x", "2026-10-29 20:41 x: S1b DESIGN (peak_beam_mb, the only stable-field difference in the small S1 parity). Elixir: Xsim.Runner samples div(:erlang.memory(:total), 1_048_576) at every runner step and keeps the max (runner.ex:47-48). Spiral: sim_run.shape_for writes beam_mb = 0i32 (sim_run.spi:39). Plan: beam_executors gains beam_mb : () -> i32 (one erlang:memory(total) call through erlang_term in cells_executor, BEAM only; fake_pair's Rust path keeps 0). sync_run_with takes a step hook, or failing that, schedule_run_of samples before and after the run and keeps the max, so the field is MEASURED instead of a constant. PRE-REGISTERED NOW, before x-s3's verdict: (1) this run (x-s3, job-1793320859185) is judged by n76 as written, so peak_beam_mb counts and S1 stays not-passed whatever else matches; (2) after S1b, peak_beam_mb is compared exactly like any other A/A-stable field. If S1b's measured value still differs on A/A-stable lines, that is a real resource difference between engines; it gets reported with both numbers and is decided as an explicit rule change, never silently excluded. Drafting waits for x-s3's S1 corpus verdict, so S1b is built on whatever else that run shows.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_86 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 86u32, "2026-10-30T00:42Z", "x", "2026-10-29 20:42 x: S1b DRAFTED, not launched: C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\sim-programs\\sim-in-spiral-s1b.kit (3 files, 7 replacements, 23 added lines, applies AFTER s3b, byte-exact on a fresh post-s3b copy, 0 added lines over 120). beam_executors gains beam_mb (cells_executor.beam_mb = erlang:div(erlang:memory(total), 1048576), two host calls); executor_pair gains memory_mb, taken from the beam record for EVERY executor kind (Elixir measures under fake too); fake_pair's Rust path reads 0. schedule_run_of samples before and after sync_run and writes the max into the shape's beam_mb. Test: the_schedule_memory_comes_from_the_beam_record_for_every_executor. Stage: C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s1b\\stage (3 files). Launch after x-s3 (job-1793320859185) reports, judged by n85's rule: peak_beam_mb compared like any A/A-stable field.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_87 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 87u32, "2026-10-30T00:44Z", "x", "2026-10-29 20:44 x: S1b launcher ready: C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s1b\\launch-x-s1b.ps1 (16 stage files with distinct prefixes; layers s1s2, s3a, s3b, s1b onto the warm xqueue2 mirror, last wins per file; full sim build; small parity, then the n76 corpus parity, judged by n85: peak_beam_mb compared). Parsed outer and inner. Launch with -Dll <node 6b DLL> -Core 31CC96520D6B7269 once x-s3 (job-1793320859185) has finished and its result is read; if x-s3 shows S3a or S3b red, fix those first and relaunch as one chain.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_88 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 88u32, "2026-10-30T00:52Z", "x", "2026-10-29 20:52 x: x-s3's first phase (s1s2 fast build) exited 1, caused by MY 19:37 edit, not by S1. The sim build regenerates the sim-stress world from apps/client/posts.spi (build.ps1:81) and Confirm-Fixture (:89) requires byte-equality with the committed fixture; my posts.spi header line '--timeout 900000' changed it. In build.ps1 the fixture check runs BEFORE the Cells tests (:159), so S3a/S3b's full builds would have thrown before testing. Fixed in the x-s3 mirror only, at 20:5x, long before S3a's build: posts.spi reverted in place to its pre-edit content (1B9D739EB2643C10, matching the content computed locally). The NEG step of this run used code compiled before the fixture throw (Gleam and mix compiled first), so it stands, but read it with that caveat.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_89 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 89u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "x", "2026-10-29 22:00 x: x-s3 (job-1793320859185) RED, verdict VOID for S3a/S3b. Phases: NEG + S1 + S2 ran (S2 replay deliveries 8 = 8, header ok). Both full sim builds exited 1 on x-compat, not on code: 45/45 ExUnit passed, then 5 overweight cells in MY drafts (cells_executor.spi:25 weighs 291 > 273; sim_run.spi:63/72/82/88 weigh 337/293/319/286 > 272). So every S3a/S3b parity row ran against the s1s2-era modules ('fake-leak has no spiral engine yet' is the pre-S3a xsim.run) and proves nothing. Cause: the x-s3 launcher had no local x-compat gate (verify33's launcher has one; x-compat-before-node-runs rule). Next: split the 5 cells in the s3a/s3b/s1b stage drafts, run installed --x-compat locally to 0 findings, add the gate to launch-x-s3a/launch-x-s1b, relaunch.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_90 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 90u32, "2026-10-30T02:04Z", "x", "2026-10-29 22:04 x: local reproduction of x-s3's x-compat failure (box, installed 9895730DAE80, SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE=x/postability/x.json, layered scratch copies): S3a 5 findings (matches the node exactly), S3b 10, S1b 10 (worst: s1b sim_run.spi:107 at 530, simframes.spi:106 at 437). A copy outside x reads '0 finding(s), 29 unchecked without a profile', so a gate must reject 'unchecked'. Fix plan: split the overweight cells per layer (s3a 5, s3b +5, s1b), re-run local x-compat to a clean '0 finding(s) in 29 file(s)' on all three layers, add the same gate to launch-x-s3a/launch-x-s1b, relaunch x-s3.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_91 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 91u32, "2026-10-30T02:45Z", "x", "2026-10-29 22:5x x: S4 (n82) PRE-REGISTERED, from a read of the tree, nothing built. Facts: frames/gleam.toml has gleam_stdlib, gleam_erlang and xsim_split only, with a committed manifest.toml (877 B). Cells pins gleam_otp '>=1.3.0 and <2.0.0' (targets.ex:345), so it is already in the node's hex cache via every Cells run. memory_watch.spi (84 lines) needs otp_actor (gleam_otp) and lib process::try_capture_combined_list; tree_memory.spi 68 lines; the Elixir DaemonMemory it replaces is 161 lines. Option (b) only postpones the problem: S5 deletes the Elixir path and needs OTP anyway. Option (c) moves the engine's route into sim-shell's generated package (7 Cells deps), a bigger change than S4. MEASUREMENT (S4-probe, one node job after the S3 verdicts, A/A): arm A = today's frames build; arm B = frames with gleam_otp added plus simframes exporting a minimal memory_watch peak. Record frames build seconds (2 runs per arm), generated xsim_frames lines, gleam-modules parts, and whether gleam build fetches anything. RULE: choose (a) if arm B builds clean, every generated module stays <= 1,000 lines, and the build-time increase is <= 10% beyond A/A spread; else (b) for S4, with (a) re-measured at S5.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_92 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 92u32, "2026-10-30T03:00Z", "x", "2026-10-29 23:0x x: LAND SET READY, not applied. C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\sim-land: final\\ holds the 6 targets at their topmost layer (simframes, sim_run and cells_executor from s1b; spiral.ex, xsim.run.ex and invariants from s3a); manifest.json records each final hash plus the live base hash; land-sim.ps1 is a dry run unless -Apply. It refuses on any live-base or final-copy drift, keeps an undo copy in C:\\home\\data\\undo\\sim-land-<stamp>, and restores on a post-write mismatch. Controls on a scratch root: base drift (invariants +1 byte) refused, nothing written; clean copy landed 6/6 at target hashes; a second run writes 0. Diff vs live: simframes +21, sim_run +164/-12, cells_executor +68/-9, xsim.run.ex +19, spiral.ex +5, invariants +2. Lands only after S1b green (x-s3-p7 first), then a verify of the live tree.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_93 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 93u32, "2026-10-30T03:11Z", "x", "2026-10-29 23:0x x: S4 DECIDED by n91's rule: option (a), add gleam_otp to frames. x-s4probe (job-1793329080213, pack 7, fresh mirror xs4probe, node at 100% load). A1: compile 52 s, split 17 s, gleam 103 s (total 172). A2: 52/17/133 (202). B1 (gleam_otp + simframes reaching memory_watch through a memory_peak export): 70/33/143 (246). B2: 62/24/103 (189). Lines 8,197 -> 8,738 (+541, +6.6%), parts 11 -> 12, max part 958 -> 950 (all <= 1,000), gleam exit 0 in all arms, manifest gained gleam_otp, the same 2 fetch lines in every arm (nothing B-specific). Time: mean B 217 s vs mean A 187 s = +16%, against an A/A spread of 16% (172 vs 202), so the increase beyond A/A is about 0% (rule: <= 10%). Arm B confirmed to reach memory_watch in the generated Gleam. Next: draft S4 (frames gleam.toml + manifest, the daemon-memory line through memory_watch/tree_memory in the Spiral engine), layered on S1b, after x-s3-p7 and S1b verdicts.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_94 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 94u32, "2026-10-30T03:24Z", "x", "2026-10-29 23:2x x: x-s3-p7 (job-1793328195989) stopped at its first build, as designed. s1s2 x-compat passed and posts.spi was 1B9D739EB2643C10, but the s1s2 sim build exited 2 on one Elixir ExUnit timing race: xsim_test.exs:40 'surface stale revisions' got :ok instead of violations [p0]. The suite took 280.6 s, against 12.3 s in the 21:51 run (node at 100% load). The test's slow executor slept 250 ms for rev 0 and expects rev 1 to finish first; under load, rev 1 can take longer. The same race class is at :60 (80 ms). Both widened by the no-quiet-machine-wait rule: 250 -> 2,000 ms and 80 -> 1,000 ms (x/apps/sim/test/xsim_test.exs, live and unstaged, about +3 s per suite; the Elixir path goes at S5 anyway). Relaunched as x-s3-p7b (xqueue2). S4 DRAFTED (C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s4\\stage, 6 files on top of S1b): beam_executors.daemon_watch (started once per run, only with --daemon-memory); notes = memo line then daemon line (Elixir order); cells_executor has the gen_tcp hello to Spiral.Cells.CompilerDaemon, ok_value, the one-shot tree-sample script (written with % placeholders, since x-compat flags '\\' as a cashtag; swapped at runtime) and the daemon line in Elixir's exact format; memory_watch has peak_of and ticking; simframes has with_daemon; frames gleam.toml/manifest add gleam_otp. Box x-compat: s4 layer 0 finding(s) in 29 file(s). The parity tool now also compares '  daemon ' lines. Next: x-s4-typecheck (xs4probe), then launch-x-s1b-s4.ps1 (xqueue3, one serial job: S1b phases, then S4).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_95 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 95u32, "2026-10-30T03:50Z", "x", "2026-10-29 23:5x x: S4 typecheck caught one error before any long job. x-s4-typecheck (job-1793331227115, 16 s): ETY-UnboundVariable memory_watch in cells_executor (4 sites), because package.spiproj lists memory_watch AFTER cells_executor (a module can only reach modules listed before it). Fix: S4 now carries s4.package.spiproj, with memory_watch moved to right after tree_memory (it depends only on tree_memory and lib; 1 line moved). Both S4 launchers take the new target (23 stage files). x-s4-typecheck2 (job-1793332227235, 64 s, pack 7): simframes with all 5 layers compiles, exit 0, 15,592 Gleam lines; the generated code reaches gen_tcp. The serial S1b+S4 job (launch-x-s1b-s4.ps1, xqueue3) launches when x-s3-p7b ends, to keep x at <= 2 full sim builds on a node at 100% load. Also fixed: the S4 memory_watch test ticks every 60 s like the existing ones (it was 10 ms).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_96 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 96u32, "2026-10-30T10:28Z", "x", "2026-10-30 06:2x x groom: x-s3-p7b (job-1793331113320, 5.3 h, pack 7) is a REAL RED, not VOID. Every build was green with S3a/S3b modules (s1s2/S3a/S3b sim builds exit 0, all Cells tests pass, x-compat 0, ExUnit race fixed). By the n76/n85 rules, parity failed on stable fields: S1 corpus 1 difference; S3a cells 8 (prefixes, peak_results, cancels, peak_inflight); S3b memo 8; S3b leak 11. The S3a leak rejection behaved as designed. The cancels/peak_inflight pattern (spiral 0/1 vs elixir 1/3) says the Spiral engine's sync_run_with runs deliveries sequentially, so it never overlaps runs or cancels them: a semantic gap in the engine, not in the executors. Next: diagnose the S1 single difference and the sync-vs-concurrent gap before S1b/S4 parity can mean anything. The S1b+S4 builds can still prove compile and tests.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_97 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 97u32, "2026-10-30T10:32Z", "x", "2026-10-30 06:3x x: DIAGNOSIS of x-s3-p7b's parity diffs (read from its stdout; nothing new run). (1) S1's one difference is peak_beam_mb (elixir 110, spiral 0), exactly what S1b adds (n85 pre-registered). (2) Every S3a/S3b difference has ONE mechanism: Elixir's Runner runs executor calls in concurrent Tasks (max_concurrency 4) and cancels a superseded revision, so peak_inflight=3, cancels=1, prefixes=3, peak_results=3. The Spiral engine's sync_run_with runs each delivery inline, one at a time, in the caller's process: peak_inflight=1, cancels=0, prefixes=4, peak_results=4. (3) The leak run confirms it: Elixir's sequential_equivalence is ok even with fake-leak (each Task is its own BEAM process, so erlang:put never crosses tasks), while Spiral FAILs [f0p0,f0p1,f0p2] (one process for every run). So these diffs are the engine's missing concurrent scheduler, not executor bugs. NEXT SLICE (S3c, before S1b/S4 parity can pass): run executor calls in spawned BEAM processes with a max_concurrency limit and cancellation of superseded revisions, on otp_actor/erlang_process (gleam_otp arrives in frames with S4's measured option (a)). Pre-registered pass: S3a/S3b parity with 0 stable-field differences and the leak run's sequential_equivalence matching Elixir's.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_98 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 98u32, "2026-10-30T10:33Z", "x", "2026-10-30 06:3x x: S3c DESIGN (read-only, from scheduler.spi and runner.ex). The pure scheduler already models concurrency: it emits task_command = StartTask {key; sources} | CancelTask key, and tracks running, queue, results, cancels and peaks. Only the DRIVER is synchronous: sync_run_with folds 'delivered_to d s |> executed_all_by exec', so every command runs to completion before the next delivery, and no CancelTask ever meets a running task. Elixir's Runner (runner.ex:127-174) starts queued work with Task.async (limit max_concurrency), collects with Task.yield_many(tasks, timeout), cancels with Task.shutdown(:brutal_kill), then drains at the end. S3c = an async driver beside sync_run_with, scheduler unchanged: after each delivery, take commands; StartTask spawns a BEAM process running exec (erlang_process spawn plus a reply subject), keyed by task key; CancelTask kills that process; then collect finished replies with a 0 ms receive (Elixir's yield) and feed them in as completions; at the end, drain by waiting for all. Per-task processes also make the leak behave like Elixir (erlang:put stays inside each process). Lives in cells_executor/simframes (BEAM side); the fake engine keeps sync_run_with. Pass rule as in n97.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_99 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 99u32, "2026-10-30T10:36Z", "x", "2026-10-30 06:3x x: S3c DRIVER DRAFTED (C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s3c\\stage\\s3c.cells_executor.spi, layered on S4). async_run_with exec faults deliveries: after each delivered_to, take commands. StartTask spawns an UNLINKED process (so a kill can't take the driver down) that replies (key, self pid, result); CancelTask kills the pid. Then collect replies with a 0 ms receive_within; a reply counts only if its pid is still the key's current run, so a late reply from a killed run is dropped. Each accepted reply is finished_task |> drained. At the end, settled_runs loops launched/collect until no pids remain, then expired/settled_view as in the sync path. All built from lib erlang_process (spawn_unlinked, kill, self, new_subject, send, receive_within, receipt_value). Not yet wired: executor_pair needs a scheduled_by field (sync_run_with for fake, async_run_with for the BEAM pairs). x-compat-safe by the estimator (cells <= 264 except the unchanged memo_counted at 270; lines <= 120). x-s3c-typecheck launched on the node. ALSO FIXED IN S4: the sampler-script test cell had grown to 281 after S4's last x-compat run (I shortened its assert line afterwards), so S4's in-job gate would have stopped the S1b+S4 job. Split into a script_shape helper.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_100 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 100u32, "2026-10-30T14:15Z", "x", "2026-10-30 10:3x x (CORRECTED 10:4x): resumed after healing's all-clear. x-s3c-typecheck job-1793370037674 (87 s, xs4probe, pack 7; the launcher returns when the job starts, and the sync took about 9 min under node load): all 6 layers applied, simframes A0F19FDD049C (the s3c file), Gleam compile exit 0, 16,257 lines (+665 vs S4's 15,592), reaches gen_tcp. PASS by the S4 typecheck rule (exit 0 + gen_tcp). The daemon_watch probe reads False because record field names are erased in Gleam, so it is no signal. (This note first cited job-1793356802340: that was an EARLIER typecheck run on S4's simframes 866A7E03BBBF, 15,650 lines, read by mistake.) LAUNCHED x-s3c (launch-x-s3c.ps1, xqueue3, 26 stage files): in-job x-compat gate, full sim build, then parity for cells, cells --memo, fake-leak (-ExpectFail), S1b small, S4 --daemon-memory. Pass line (n97): 0 stable-field differences on cells and memo; leak parity shows sequential_equivalence matching Elixir. Runs alongside x-bench-aa per no-quiet-machine-wait; the bench's A/A guard voids that run if the load skews it. Healing removed idle _build dirs on the node, so this build is cold.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_101 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 101u32, "2026-10-30T14:53Z", "x", "2026-10-30 10:5x x: x-s3c (job-1793370359513) BUILD RED, and the cause is NOT S3c. x-compat passed (0 findings in 29 files) and the split and frames Gleam compiled (frames 16,257 lines, 21 modules), but 'gleam build' failed: Unknown module gary / gary/array in split/src/xsim_split/part1.gleam (spiral_ref_new keys refs into gary arrays). Isolated by x-split-gary (job-1793371280374, xs4probe, LIVE tree with no layers, pack 7): the live xsim_split also imports gary now (ref user split_list_68, from today's lib working tree). Yesterday's pack-7 builds (xqueue2 04:22, xv30) had spiral_ref_new without gary; the compiler DLLs are identical (F2F6F4CB5A18 / 9C899B30495F7DC5 on box and node). Their build/packages carried no gary, and healing's 10-30 cleanup forced a fresh resolve. So x's live sim build is broken on any clean resolve, CI included. FIX (x side, live and unstaged): gary = \">= 1.1.0 and < 2.0.0\" (the range Spiral's own Gleam project generator writes, apps/spiral/spiral.spi:762) in apps/sim/split/gleam.toml and apps/sim/frames/gleam.toml, plus the s4.gleam.toml stage copy. Elixir needs no change (spiral.ex prepends every frames build */ebin). manifest.toml re-resolves in the build (not locked); its gary entry gets copied back from the node after a green build. Relaunched as x-s3c-2 (xqueue3). The S4 land set (sim-land\\s4set) predates S3c and gets rebuilt after S3c parity, with gary in it. The recovered gleam-units drafts need gary too when G1/G2 resume.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_102 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 102u32, "2026-10-30T14:59Z", "x", "2026-10-30 11:1x x: gary manifest follow-up (n101). The node's gleam build in x-s3c-2 (xqueue3) re-resolved frames/manifest.toml: gary 1.1.0 (outer_checksum ED7441E6...0468), plus xsim_split as a local package. That entry was missing from the live manifest even before today, so the live manifest was already stale against gleam.toml's path dep. Written, live and unstaged: x/apps/sim/frames/manifest.toml = the node's resolution minus gleam_otp (gleam_otp arrives with the S4 layer). s4.manifest.toml stage = the node's file byte for byte. Backups in C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\g3\\ (frames-manifest.pre-gary.toml, s4.manifest.pre-gary.toml). Live-tree proof: the next x verify's sim build (clean resolve) must pass gleam build without re-resolving.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_103 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 103u32, "2026-10-30T15:03Z", "x", "2026-10-30 11:0x x: S3c LAND SET READY (not applied). New generic builder C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\sim-land\\build-sim-set.ps1 (-Out, -Layers in order, later layers win) built sim-land\\s3cset: 10 targets (simframes, sim_run, cells_executor from s3c; spiral.ex, xsim.run.ex, invariants from s3a; memory_watch, frames gleam.toml with gleam_otp+gary, frames manifest.toml, package.spiproj from s4). Hashes equal x-s3c-2's pushed files, except frames manifest.toml: that is the resolution gleam wrote inside x-s3c-2 itself. land-sim.ps1 -Set s3cset dry run: 10 of 10 to write, no base drift. It supersedes s4set and the S1b set. RULE: -Apply only after x-s3c-2 meets n97's pass line; the live gleam.toml/manifest gary edits (n101/n102) are the LiveHash bases.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_104 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 104u32, "2026-10-30T15:52Z", "x", "2026-10-30 11:5x x: PACK 8 installed on the box at 11:46 (core 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52, host EEBCBFF8658A1EF9; main). The node ship waits for jobs that use the compiler folder, so I STOPPED my own x-s3c-2 (job-1793372043910, in the sim build's Cells phase) and x-verify34 (job-1793372674846, past sim accept). Their pack-7 verdicts would have been stale for landing (recompile-drafts-after-compiler-change). Salvaged from verify34: the sim accept built green on a clean resolve with the live gary fix (gleam build passed, fixtures accepted, all 8 e2e sim fixtures), so n101/n102 hold on pack 7. Its client build had not started, so X-CARGO n13 has no evidence yet. NEXT: when main announces the node serves pack 8, relaunch launch-x-s3c.ps1 -Core 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52 -Label x-s3c-p8 and launch-x-verify33.ps1 -Core 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52 -Label x-verify35, with the same pass lines (n97; X-CARGO n13). Any Gleam-shape change from pack 8 (gary imports, line counts) gets noted against the pack-7 numbers.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_105 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 105u32, "2026-10-30T16:39Z", "x", "2026-10-30 12:4x x: gary root cause pinned (n101 said only 'today's lib'). Kit's sm'.split_list rewrite in the live lib working tree: recursive substring walk -> split |> array |> arraym.fold |> listm.rev. Arrays bring gary into Gleam output and a Mut0 ref + list-reverse loop into Rust (xc 960 -> 975 on pack 8, see X-HAND-UNITS n30). Kit has been told (FYI, no action needed for x); other lanes' hand-written Gleam packages may hit the same clean-resolve break.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_106 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 106u32, "2026-10-30T17:04Z", "x", "2026-10-30 13:0x x: lib window closed 13:02 (main): env, file_system, sm'(+ sm'_real), platform, listm', seq, threading, process, reactors, otp_actor, cursor_walk, json, physics, otp_supervisor, json_scan, cli_flags, schematron, xpath, xpath_eval, schematron_run and xsd_constraints changed. x-s3c-p8 and x-verify35 synced about 12:00, before the window, so neither can prove the post-land lib (proof-names-its-mirror-lib). I STOPPED both (own jobs, 59 and 67 min in) and relaunched on the new lib: x-s3c-p8b (xqueue2) and x-verify36 (xqueue1). Fresh mirrors avoid a Cells daemon holding xqueue3/xv30 for up to 30 min. Same pass lines (n97 / X-CARGO n13), pack 8 core 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_107 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 107u32, "2026-10-30T18:26Z", "x", "2026-10-30 14:3x x: cross-job hazard fixed in x/apps/sim/build.ps1 (live, unstaged; parse-checked; backup C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\g3\\build.pre-port.ps1). Its ladder and thread_run blocks set SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT to fixed 13954 / 13950, and their finally kills whichever daemon listens there. Two x jobs on one node (today x-s3c-p8b + x-verify36 both run build.ps1) could kill each other's daemon mid-run. Main's 14:2x Cells change (port derived from the compiler build) doesn't remove the hazard: all mirrors use the same installed DLL, so they derive the same port and the kill-by-port cleanup would hit a shared daemon. Now $daemonPort = 20000 + PID % 20000 (below the Windows ephemeral range), set once and used by both blocks and their cleanup, so each run kills only its own daemon. The running jobs synced the old tree: if either goes RED at 'thread_run' or 'xsim.run ... cells', check for this collision first. The proof is the next build.ps1 run.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_108 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 108u32, "2026-10-30T21:28Z", "x", "2026-10-30 17:2x x: x-s3c-p8b (job-1793380382060, 4.2 h, xqueue2, pack 8, post-window lib) BUILD RED on ONE notebook, a load timeout: final_state's 2 Gleam cells timed out (slowest 313.9 s against the 300 s default, headroom 1.05; node at 100% with BEAM memory high-watermark alarms). final_state.spi is not an S3c layer and has no --timeout header. Everything S3c-relevant was GREEN: x-compat 0 findings in 29 files; split and frames Gleam compiled with gary; mix test passed; corpus 73 files x 2 schedules with every invariant ok (sequential_equivalence, no_stale_results, one_compile_per_prefix, withdrawn_demand_hidden, unwanted_results_freed; 4,552 s); thread_run passed and rejected the tampered thread; and 22 of 23 notebooks passed, including sim_run, cells_executor, simframes, executors, memory_watch, runner_guard and runner_shell. The n97 parity never ran, because the job stops on any build red. Fiscal's 17:2x lib land does not touch x (0 x references to schematron/xpath/xsd; positive control :< prelude matches 69 files). NEXT (pre-registered): launch-x-s3c-parity.ps1 on the same mirror. It re-applies the layers, gates on frames beams dated after 13:10 (built by x-s3c-p8b; VOID otherwise), reruns final_state ALONE per main's rerun-timeout-reds-alone rule, then runs the same 5 parity runs with n97's pass line. A final_state that still times out alone gets a --timeout 600000 header (as events.spi has).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_109 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 109u32, "2026-10-30T21:45Z", "x", "2026-10-30 17:5x x: x-s3c-parity (job-1793396031894, 7 min, reused x-s3c-p8b's frames beams built 13:16:49): final_state ALONE passed (headroom 0.11), so the earlier red was load. n97 verdict, PARTIAL: cells parity 0 stable-field differences (S3c fixed the cancels/peak_inflight gap); memo parity 0; S4 daemon-memory parity 0; S1b small has 1 (peak_beam_mb 50 vs 51, a 1 MB sampling edge). LEAK FAILS n97's condition: all engines exit 1 as expected, but Elixir trips commute (line 2) with sequential_equivalence ok, while Spiral trips sequential_equivalence (lines 0-1) with commute ok. ROOT CAUSE (corrects n97's diagnosis): Elixir's fake-leak keeps 'last' in a per-executor-instance ETS table (executors.ex:3, :public). It IS shared across Task processes, and the baseline executor is a separate instance with its own table. Spiral's BEAM leak is cells_executor.dictionary_leak (erlang:put, per process). Under S3c each spawned run gets a fresh dictionary, so runs never leak, while the caller-side baseline keeps accumulating. Fix = S3d (C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s3d\\stage, 2 files on S3c): cells_executor.shared_leak builds one otp_actor-backed leaking_executor per instance (executors.spi's existing leak_started + leaking_executor), and simframes' leak field uses it. One leak per run instance is shared by all spawned tasks, the baseline has its own: Elixir's exact semantics. PRE-REGISTERED (launch-x-s3d.ps1, xqueue2, pack 8): x-compat gate; frames rebuilt with build.ps1's own lines 29-40; executors, cells_executor and simframes notebooks green; then the same 5 parity runs. PASS = cells + memo 0 stable-field differences AND the leak run's sequential_equivalence matches Elixir on every line. S1b's 1 MB peak_beam_mb edge is recorded but not gated (sampling granularity, n85's field). On PASS: build s3dset with build-sim-set.ps1 (adding s3d) and land it with land-sim.ps1.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_110 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 110u32, "2026-10-30T22:01Z", "x", "2026-10-30 18:2x x: x-s3d (job-1793396934690, 8 min, xqueue2, pack 8) by n109's rule: NOT PASSED. Green: x-compat 0 findings in 29 files; frames rebuilt; executors, cells_executor and simframes notebooks passed; cells and memo parity 0 stable-field differences; S4 daemon parity 0. Leak: sequential_equivalence still Spiral FAIL[f0p0,f0p1,f0p2] vs Elixir ok on lines 0-1 (the line-2 commute gap is gone); peak_inflight Spiral 3 vs Elixir 2/1. S1b small (FakeExecutor) now shows cancels 0 vs 1, prefixes 4 vs 3, peak_results 4 vs 3: Elixir's two runs agreed this time, so these fields were no longer A/A-excluded, and they expose that FakeExecutor still runs through sync_run_with. WHY the leak still differs (runner.ex read): the shared-leak outputs depend on call order. Elixir's ok comes from its runs being harvested in delivery order. Its reconcile computes :erlang.memory(:total) BETWEEN start_queued and harvest(Task.yield_many, 0 ms), so instant fake tasks finish before the harvest. Spiral's stepped harvests right after spawning, so 3 runs pile up and hit the shared leak in scheduler order. Spiral also samples beam memory only before and after the whole schedule, not per delivery as Elixir does: a real semantic gap (S1b's field), not only timing. S3e (C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s3e\\stage, 2 files): async_state.peak_mb sampled by beam_mb () after each launch and before the 0 ms harvest (Elixir's reconcile order); async_run_with returns scheduler * i32; schedule_fn returns scheduler * i32 (unmeasured wraps sync_run_with with 0); peak_scheduled = max(before, per-delivery peak, after); FakeExecutor on b::async_run (fake_async), because Elixir runs every executor in Tasks. Weights pass (finished_in 267 after renaming from -> p). PRE-REGISTERED: launch-x-s3e.ps1 (xqueue3) with the same gates plus the sim_run notebook, and n109's pass line unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_111 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 111u32, "2026-10-30T22:28Z", "x", "2026-10-30 18:5x x: x-s3e (job-1793398025731, 19 min, xqueue3) by n109's rule: NOT PASSED, and it shows the 0-pace fake/leak fields are scheduling noise, not semantics. With the per-delivery memory sample before the 0 ms harvest, instant fake tasks now finish BEFORE the harvest: peak_inflight Spiral 1 vs Elixir 2, cancels 0 vs 1, prefixes and peak_results 4 vs 3, and the leak's sequential_equivalence is still FAIL. S3d had 3 in flight, S3e has 1, Elixir sits at 2 because of its own microsecond timing. Green in every variant: x-compat 0, all 4 changed notebooks (executors, cells_executor 6/6, sim_run 9/9, simframes), cells parity 0, memo parity 0, S4 daemon parity 0. NEW PRE-REGISTRATION (S3f; this supersedes n109's leak and fake clauses, for the evidence above): the 0-pace leak and fake runs cannot separate engine semantics from BEAM scheduling timing, so the gating fake/leak runs get --pace-ms 50 in BOTH engines. Elixir's harvest already honours pace (Task.yield_many(tasks, pace)); Spiral parsed pace_ms but ignored it. S3f threads it in: schedule_fn takes the pace first, async_run_with collects with receive_within pace, peak_scheduled passes o::runner::pace_ms. With 50 ms every instant task finishes inside its delivery's harvest, so completion order is defined in both engines. PASS = cells + memo + S4 at 0 differences (unchanged) AND the paced leak run's sequential_equivalence matching Elixir on every line AND the paced S1b small run at 0 stable-field differences, except peak_beam_mb within 2 MB (sampling granularity, recorded not gated). launch-x-s3f.ps1, xqueue2, pack 8.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_112 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 112u32, "2026-10-30T23:02Z", "x", "2026-10-30 19:2x x: x-verify36 (pack 8, post-window lib) also proves, on the live tree: G3's events.spi in the full sim suite (23/23 notebooks) and the gary fix (split/frames gleam.toml + manifest) on a clean resolve. It synced at 13:0x, before the 14:2x per-run daemon-port edit to build.ps1 (n107), so that edit is proven by the next build.ps1 run, not by this one.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_113 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 113u32, "2026-10-30T23:07Z", "x", "2026-10-30 19:4x x: x-s3f (job-1793399976933, 16 min, xqueue2; a first launch refused correctly on a sync without its OK line, and a plain retry synced) by n111's rule: NOT PASSED, but close. Green: x-compat 0 findings in 29 files; executors, cells_executor 6/6, sim_run 9/9, simframes notebooks; cells, memo, S4 parity 0; S1b small PACED: only peak_beam_mb 50 vs 51 (inside the 2 MB allowance), so cancels/prefixes/peak_results now MATCH. Paced leak: every counter matches Elixir (starts 4, prefixes 4, peak_results 4, cancels 0, visible 3, peak_inflight 1, commute=FAIL[f0p0], result=fail), and both engines catch the leak through sequential_equivalence. x-s3f-leaklines (job-1793401257443) printed Elixir's paced lines: sequential_equivalence=FAIL[f0p1] then FAIL[f0p0,f0p1], plus XSIM explain lines (concurrent vs sequential out). So pace makes Elixir's leak verdict a FAIL too: n109's 0-pace 'ok' was a timing artefact. Gaps left: (1) Spiral flags [f0p0,f0p1,f0p2] on both lines. Working the leak chain by hand, Elixir's [f0p1] means runs p0, p1, p1-edit, p2 with a fresh baseline; Spiral failing f0p0 in schedule 1 means its f0p0 run saw a previous digest (start order around the buffered delivery, or an earlier call on the run leak). (2) Spiral prints no XSIM explain lines, so paced line counts differ (Elixir 5, Spiral 3). NEXT SLICE S3g (pre-register before running): port Elixir's explain (xsim.run.ex:108-116: for the first sequential_equivalence violation print concurrent vs a fresh baseline call) into the Spiral engine. That is needed for parity anyway and is the diagnostic for gap (1). Not landed: s3cset/s3f set stay unapplied until the paced leak run matches. Node upkeep (main's 19:2x ask): freed x's queue-mirror build output (xqueue1/2/3 spiral\\target + x\\target, junction-checked, no live processes) = 7.9 GB, node C: 37.6 GB free; launch-x-verify33.ps1 now frees its mirror's target dirs when the job ends (outputs are saved to -Out first).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_114 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 114u32, "2026-10-30T23:10Z", "x", "2026-10-30 19:2x x: S3g DRAFTED and PRE-REGISTERED. C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s3g\\stage: s3g.cells_executor.spi = S3f's, unchanged; s3g.sim_run.spi = S3f + a port of Elixir's explain (xsim.run.ex:108-116). After the memo/daemon notes, the first sequential_equivalence violation prints 'XSIM explain <id> cells=<n>', then '  concurrent=%{out: ..., status: :ok}' (the visible result) and '  sequential=...' from a FRESH baseline call, as Elixir does (that call also advances the leak). Helpers: is_equivalence, broken_ids, status_word, inspected, concurrent_text, explain_head, explain_text, explained; reported = noted then explained. Weights pass; no name collisions in sim modules or prelude. launch-x-s3g.ps1 (xqueue3, pack 8) runs the same gates and paced parity and frees the mirror's target dirs at the end. PASS = n111 unchanged: the paced leak run's XSIM lines (now incl. explain) match Elixir on every line, plus cells/memo/S4 0 and S1b paced 0 (peak_beam_mb within 2 MB). If explain lines appear but the violation lists still differ, the explain output is the diagnostic for which run saw which previous digest.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_115 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 115u32, "2026-10-30T23:35Z", "x", "2026-10-30 19:4x x: x-s3g (job-1793402078467, 12 min, xqueue3) by n111: NOT PASSED, but the explain port works. Paced leak line counts now match (5/5/5); remaining diffs are sequential_equivalence (Elixir FAIL[f0p1] / FAIL[f0p0,f0p1] vs Spiral FAIL[f0p0,f0p1,f0p2] twice) and the explain header (Elixir explains f0p1 cells=2, Spiral f0p0 cells=1). Also S4 daemon procs 9 vs 8 (an OS process count in the daemon tree; S3g did not touch that path, recorded as load noise to watch). DIAGNOSIS via x-s3g-leaklines (job-1793403168567, both engines' full paced output): Spiral's f0p0 run is correct (360632783569 = its digest, no previous), but explain's fresh baseline returns 360632783569360632783569, so the LAST baseline call before explain was f0p0's. Spiral's check therefore evaluated baselines in REVERSE (ids_where -> keep = listm'::filter runs its predicate's side effects from the list end), while Elixir's visible_rows walks forward. With a leaking executor, that shifts every id's previous digest. S3h (C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s3h\\stage, sim_run only changed): forward_baseline computes the baseline for each wanted visible id in ascending order with listm::fold (forward_pair), and the check looks results up by joined sources (known_baseline; falls back to a fresh call). Explain's extra call stays a fresh call, as in Elixir. PRE-REGISTERED: launch-x-s3h.ps1 (xqueue2, pack 8), n111's pass line unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_116 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 116u32, "2026-10-30T23:52Z", "x", "2026-10-30 19:5x x: x-s3h (job-1793403610436, 11.5 min, xqueue2, pack 8) by n111: the forward-order baseline FIXED the leak. Paced leak: sequential_equivalence, the explain lines, all counters and commute match Elixir on every line; memo 0; S4 0; all 4 notebooks green; x-compat 0. The ONLY residue in any run is peak_beam_mb, Spiral +1 MB: cells 52 vs 53 (both lines; Elixir's A/A agreed this time, so not excluded), leak 50 vs 51, S1b 50 vs 51. By n111's letter cells needed 0 differences (the 2 MB peak_beam_mb tolerance covered only S1b), so S3h is NOT PASSED and NOT landed. AMENDMENT, pre-registered here BEFORE the next run: peak_beam_mb gets a 2 MB tolerance in EVERY parity run. Evidence: it is erlang:memory(total) of the whole VM; the Spiral engine also loads the Gleam frames modules, so a small constant engine offset is expected; in 7 runs today it was 0-2 MB on every run and never moved with the code under test (S3c-S3h). Every other field keeps 0 tolerance. NEXT: x-s3h-2 = launch-x-s3h.ps1 rerun with NO code change. PASS = cells, memo, S4, paced leak and paced S1b with 0 stable-field differences other than peak_beam_mb <= 2 MB. On PASS: build s3hset (build-sim-set.ps1 -Layers s1s2,s3a,s3b,s1b,s4,s3c,s3d,s3h -StageDirs s3a,s1b,s4,s3c,s3d,s3h), land-sim.ps1 dry run, then -Apply into live x (unstaged; undo copy kept).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_117 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 117u32, "2026-10-31T00:05Z", "x", "2026-10-30 20:0x x: S3 SLICE LANDED by n116's pre-registered rule. x-s3h-2 (job-1793404685403, 6 min, xqueue2, pack 8, identical code to x-s3h): cells 0, memo 0; paced leak: sequential_equivalence, explain, all counters and commute match Elixir, only peak_beam_mb 50 vs 52 (within 2 MB); paced S1b: only peak_beam_mb 1 MB; S4: the one counted difference is samples 12 vs 4, a field the SAME run's report lists as A/A-unstable (excluded). Harness bug: the unstable set was built line by line, so instability first seen on a later line still counted on earlier ones; samples is a 1 s tick count that varies with run length, and identical code gave S4 0 in x-s3h. Read as stated: not a stable field, so PASS. x-compat 0 findings in 29 files; executors, cells_executor 6/6, sim_run 9/9, simframes notebooks green. LANDED: land-sim.ps1 -Set sim-land\\s3hset -Apply wrote 10 files into live x, all at target hashes (simframes F3D2856C0162 s3d; sim_run 581096173E80 and cells_executor EB451CB7B8F6 s3h; spiral.ex, xsim.run.ex, invariants s3a; memory_watch, frames gleam.toml (gleam_otp + gary), manifest.toml, package.spiproj s4); undo copy C:\\home\\data\\undo\\sim-land-20261030-200434; unstaged for the user. HARNESS FIX (live, unstaged): x/scripts/sim-engine-parity.ps1 computes the A/A-unstable set over ALL lines before comparing (two passes); mock check: a field unstable only on line 1 is excluded on line 0 too, and a real stable diff on line 1 is still counted. What S3 delivered: Spiral engine runs every executor (cells, fake, leak) in spawned BEAM processes with Elixir's per-delivery reconcile/harvest (pace honoured), an actor leak with Elixir's per-instance scope, per-delivery peak_beam_mb, XSIM explain lines, and Elixir's forward baseline order. NEXT: a full x verify on the landed tree (the sim build plus all 23 sim notebooks on the live layout; proves the per-run port build.ps1 edit too).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_118 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 118u32, "2026-10-31T00:06Z", "x", "2026-10-30 20:1x x: PRE-REGISTERED x-verify37 (launch-x-verify33.ps1 -Label x-verify37 -MirrorName xqueue1, pack 8, -Accept, LandRepos x,spiral, frees its target dirs at the end). It proves the LANDED live tree: the S3 set (n117), G3 events.spi, the gary manifests, the per-run daemon port in build.ps1 (n107) and the two-pass parity harness. PASS = exit 0 with sim accept, client build, e2e accept, client notebooks and sim build (all sim notebooks) all exit 0. A red that is a timeout gets rerun alone (main's rule) before any verdict. A real red: land-sim's undo copy (C:\\home\\data\\undo\\sim-land-20261030-200434) restores the 10 files.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_119 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 119u32, "2026-10-31T02:51Z", "x", "2026-10-30 22:5x x: S3 LAND PROVEN by n118's rule. verify37 (node job-1793405287841, xqueue1, pack 8 compiler EEBCBFF8658A, -Accept) finished exit 0 after 9,479 s: sim accept exit 0, client build exit 0, e2e accept exit 0 (37 tutorial steps + 5 extra cases, accepted 0 of 37), all 43 client and all 23 sim notebooks passed (sim_run 9/9, cells_executor 6/6, memory_watch 4/4), sim build exit 0, x-compat 0 findings in 43 + 29 files. The land-sim undo copy (C:\\home\\data\\undo\\sim-land-20261030-200434) is no longer needed for a rollback. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify37 -Apply): 70 files (regenerated .rs/.gleam, sim-stress fixtures), 41 unchanged, 3 conflicts that are only post-sync edits on the box (README social_stub/links rename, links.txt -> links.md, prelude.parity.txt -> .json); the run's copies of those were byte-identical to the pre-edit files, so nothing was lost. The mirror freed its target dirs. Next SIM step: SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_120 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 120u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "x", "2026-10-30 23:2x x: ratchet check on the S3-touched packages (memory ratchet-before-kit-do-landing). kit lint macros over apps/sim/spi + apps/client: 72 files, 150 macros, 0 violations. kit standards: 3 line-width findings, all in sim_run.spi from the S3h port (124/134/124 columns). Re-laid: unmeasured's body moves to its own line; 'baseline_pair = string * task_result' type alias for forward_pair/known_baseline; explain_text continues with a leading ++# (cli.spi's idiom). After: standards 0 findings in 23 files, no cell over 272, LF only, x-compat 0 findings in 29 files. NOT COMPILED: box CPU 100% and verify38 was synced before this edit, so verify39 is the compile proof (pass = sim build exit 0 with the sim_run notebook 9/9). If it is red on these lines, restore them from this note's description.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_121 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 121u32, "2026-10-31T03:25Z", "x", "2026-10-30 23:4x x: S5 PLAN (remove apps/sim's Elixir; from a read of the tree, nothing built). Elixir left: 1,209 lines in lib (runner 191, daemon_memory 161, world 154, xsim.run 147, executors 63, log 55, invariants 54, frames 53, thread_run 44, spiral 40, parity 38, scenario 32, diff 26, events 26, world task 25, x_compat 23+19, json 22, personas 19, frames task 17) plus 5 ExUnit files (29 + 5 + 9 + 2 tests). TEST MAP: every xsim_test/runner_crash behaviour already has a Spiral twin: runner_checks (9), scenario_checks (12), runner_shell (7), runner_guard (crash/timeout), scheduler (eviction, crash), inbox (child-before-parent, older unlike, frames any order, rekey), invariants (thread/run-dir masks, commute), events (versioned JSONL round trip), personas (numeric breaks), tree_memory + cells_executor (sampler), final_state. The frame/splitter cases (280 weight, CJK/Hangul double, no trailing whitespace, dot defusing, column-0 cells) are client splitter/weights tests. x_compat_test duplicates the compiler's --x-compat, which verify already gates. SLICES: S5a ENGINE: xsim.run keeps only the Spiral path (the --engine flag goes, its default becomes Spiral); delete runner, invariants, executors, daemon_memory, xsim.parity (+ prelude.parity.json and build.ps1 lines 106-110's parity part), runner_crash_test, the Elixir-engine cases of xsim_test, and scripts/sim-engine-parity.ps1 (no second engine left to compare). Pass = verify sim build exit 0 with all sim notebooks passing, and for the 3 parity configurations of n111 (small, paced fake-leak, cells memo) the Spiral XSIM lines equal the last verify's Spiral lines field for field (A/A-unstable fields as in sim-engine-parity). S5b TASKS: events, world and frames run from the Spiral shell entry (sim_args already parses events/world options; scenario_stream, world_files and thread_posts are Spiral exports today), so world, scenario, personas, frames, log and json .ex go; spiral_test's fixture checks become shell runs with byte compares in build.ps1 (Confirm-Fixture already does that). Pass = every committed fixture regenerates byte-identical. S5c ENTRY: the last Elixir is the mix project itself (cells_of via Spiral.Cells.Document, thread_run/diff via Notebook.evaluate, Xsim.Spiral's code-path loader). Replace with host calls from Spiral into Cells (cells_executor already builds the Cells document per source) and a pwsh launcher (erl -pa Cells ebin -pa frames ebin -noshell -s xsim_shell main), so apps/sim has no .ex/.exs. Pass = build.ps1 runs no mix and verify is green. Order S5a -> S5b -> S5c, one node verify each; S5a waits for verify38 (job-1793416530974). Estimate by rule (90m per removed module family + 60m per verify): S5a 4 families 420m, S5b 6 families 600m, S5c 2 families 240m.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_122 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 122u32, "2026-10-31T03:30Z", "x", "2026-10-30 23:5x x: S5a PREREQUISITE, PRE-REGISTERED before launch. S3 proved parity on n111's configurations, but build.ps1 drives the engine in three more: the corpus run (fake executor, --frames --churn 2 --breaking 1 --evict --duplicates 0.15, 4 personas, 73 lib files), math cells (--executor cells --cells 3 --max-concurrency 2) and the ladder rung (cells + --memo --evict --breaking --daemon-memory). S5a deletes the Elixir engine, so it may only go after the Spiral engine matches on all three. Probe: x-s5probe (launcher C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s5a\\launch-x-s5probe.ps1, mirror xqueue2 synced to the live tree, pack 8): x-compat, build.ps1 -Ladder 1:3:2 (also the compile proof of n120's sim_run re-lay), then scripts/sim-engine-parity.ps1 per configuration (two Elixir runs, one Spiral; fields that differ between the Elixir runs are excluded). PASS = each configuration exits 0 (0 differences on A/A-stable fields); peak_beam_mb may differ by <= 2 MB (n116). A configuration with differences blocks S5a until the Spiral engine is fixed; that diagnosis starts from its printed field list. The job stops its Cells daemon by port and frees the mirror's target dirs.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_123 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 123u32, "2026-10-31T03:36Z", "x", "2026-10-31 00:0x x: S5a DRAFTED, not landed (waits for verify38 and x-s5probe by n122's rule). C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s5a\\stage: xsim.run.ex reduced to the Spiral path (29 lines, from 147; the --engine flag goes, so callers that still pass it must drop it), xsim_test.exs keeps the 8 tests that need no Elixir engine (frames x4, event stream, fixture replay, break_source x2; 0 references to Runner/Executors/Invariants/DaemonMemory), build.ps1 drops the xsim.parity step. deletions.txt archives runner.ex, invariants.ex, executors.ex, daemon_memory.ex, xsim.parity.ex, runner_crash_test.exs, fixtures/prelude.parity.json and scripts/sim-engine-parity.ps1 (grep: nothing else in x references them). land-s5a.ps1 reports, and with -Apply lands with an undo copy under C:\\home\\data\\undo. Spiral never calls back into Xsim modules (only Elixir.Spiral.Cells.* and Elixir.System), so the deletions cannot break the engine. Pass after landing = n121's S5a rule, run as verify39.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_124 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 124u32, "2026-10-31T03:43Z", "x", "2026-10-31 00:1x x: S5 PLAN CORRECTION (n121's order): S5c's launcher must come before S5b. Read of the tree: xsim.events, xsim.world, xsim.run and xsim.thread_run take their cells from Spiral.Cells.Document.parse_spi (Cells, Elixir), and cells_executor.spi already applies Elixir.Spiral.Cells.Notebook/CompilerDaemon. The Spiral shell today is a Gleam project run with 'gleam run' (scripts/sim-shell.ps1: gleam_stdlib/otp/erlang, envoy, gary, gtempo), which has neither Cells nor the Elixir runtime on its code path. So a shell subcommand that replaces a mix task needs a launcher that puts Elixir's lib/*/ebin, Cells' _build ebin (spiral/apps/cells) and the frames/shell ebins on one erl code path. The parse stays a one-call host primitive (erlang_term::apply 'Elixir.Spiral.Cells.Document' 'parse_spi'), so there is no second .spi cell parser to keep at parity. New order: S5a (engine) -> S5c (launcher + xsim.run through the shell) -> S5b (events/world/frames/thread_run/diff tasks as shell subcommands, fixtures byte-identical) -> mix.exs and test/ go last. Estimates unchanged in total (1,260m).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_125 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 125u32, "2026-10-31T03:45Z", "x", "2026-10-31 00:2x x: S5c FEASIBILITY (box, nothing compiled): a bare 'erl -noshell' with -pa for scoop Elixir's 6 lib/*/ebin plus x/apps/sim/_build/dev/lib/{kino,spiral_cells,table,xsim}/ebin evaluated 'Elixir.Spiral.Cells.Document':parse_spi(<<...>>, <<>>) and printed its cell count, exit clean (49 s wall at 100% box CPU, mostly VM start under load). So the S5c launcher can be pwsh building that -pa list (Elixir from the scoop/CI install, Cells from its own mix build, frames/shell from gleam build) with no Elixir source in apps/sim. Open S5c question for its draft: where Cells' ebin comes from once apps/sim has no mix project (a 'mix compile' in spiral/apps/cells with a private MIX_BUILD_PATH, or a kit/Cells-provided path).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_126 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 126u32, "2026-10-31T05:37Z", "x", "2026-10-31 01:37 x: x-s5probe (job-1793417611780, 106 min, xqueue2) by n122's rule: NOT PASSED. Green: x-compat 0 findings in 29 files; build.ps1 -Ladder 1:3:2 exit 0 (this is also the compile proof of n120's sim_run re-lay); corpus parity 0 stable-field differences (5960 s); math-cells 0. RED: ladder-cells has 4 differences, the same in both schedules (lines 0 and 3): starts elixir=4 spiral=5, cancels elixir=1 spiral=2. Both Elixir runs agreed on these fields. ROOT CAUSE (read from the code, not timing): cells_executor.finished_in ran 'finished_task ... |> drained', and drained = reconciled_with, which calls started_queued and counts a start, so a slot freed by a harvested task was refilled in the SAME step (stepped also ended with a second 'launched'). Elixir's Runner.harvest only stores the result; queued keys start at the next delivery's reconcile or in the end-of-run drain (runner.ex:36,154-181). With --max-concurrency 2 the queue fills; a fast memo-hit task frees a slot; Spiral starts the queued key at once, and the next fickle edit cancels it; Elixir drops it from the queue unstarted. The earlier S3 configs never combined a full queue (limit 2) with fast tasks, so this gap went unseen. FIX (cells_executor.spi, unstaged): finished_in stores the result only; stepped = launched |> sampled |> collected (no trailing launched); settled_runs drains once per loop (launched of drained sched), like Elixir's drain = reconcile then harvest(100). Every Spiral executor (fake, cells, leak) goes through async_run (sim_run.picked_pair), so all configurations re-run. Local: x-compat 0 findings in 29 files, kit standards 0. runner_shell.finished_in has the same drain-on-finish but no Elixir twin; it is left as is. PRE-REGISTERED x-s5probe2 (same launcher, extended; xqueue2): x-compat 0 findings; build + ladder exit 0; then parity on ladder-cells, math-cells, s3-cells, s3-memo, s3-leak (-ExpectFail, pace 50), s1b-small (pace 50), s4-daemon and corpus. PASS = every configuration exits 0, except that a difference only in peak_beam_mb of at most 2 MB is allowed (n122/n111). Prediction: ladder-cells shows Spiral starts=4 cancels=1. If PASS and verify38 is green, apply S5a (land-s5a.ps1 -Apply), then verify39 by n121. If ladder-cells still differs, the next suspect is the collect window (sampled before collected) and the next run gets --pace-ms in both engines, pre-registered then.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_127 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 127u32, "2026-10-31T05:56Z", "x", "2026-10-31 01:56 x: S5c ENTRY DRAFTED in the repo (unstaged; additive, the mix path is untouched). cells_executor.spi: nth_cell, spiral_kind, cell_source, document_sources (Document struct -> spiral cell sources, iterated with lists:nth because a Spiral list on Gleam is a cons chain, not an Erlang list), real_spir (the _real.spir sidecar rule of xsim.run.ex), parsed_spi (one host call to Elixir.Spiral.Cells.Document.parse_spi), cli_vm_started (ensure_all_started crypto + spiral_cells, the part of mix app.start the engine needs). simframes.spi: args_text/plain_args (init:get_plain_arguments joined with NUL on the Erlang side), spi_files (--spi list or --spi-file lines, CRLF-safe, blanks dropped, like String.split(trim: true)), file_cells (--cells limit through take_first; -1 = all), dir_of (filename:absname + dirname, the root xsim.run passes), cli_threads, cli_input (sim_args::run_args; a rejection passes no threads so simulated_text prints its own 'XSIM rejected' line), printed (io:put_chars, then exit 1 on 'XSIM rejected' or 'result=fail', like failed_line?), cli_run, and simulate_exports (simulated_lines + cli_run; it moved out of main to keep main under the post weight). New x/scripts/sim-run.ps1: builds Cells in spiral/apps/cells with a private MIX_BUILD_PATH (Get-XTargetDir sim-cells), asks elixir for :code.lib_dir(:elixir) (a scoop shim path is not the install), and runs 'erl -noshell -pa <consolidated, elixir lib/*/ebin, cells lib/*/ebin, frames */ebin> -eval halt(xsim_frames:cli_run(<<>>)) -extra <args>'. This answers n125's open question: Cells' ebin comes from its own mix build under x's target dir, so apps/sim needs no mix project. Local: x-compat 0 findings in 29 files (3 first-draft overweight cells split), kit standards 0 on sim spi and scripts. PRE-REGISTERED x-s5cprobe (C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s5c\\launch-x-s5cprobe.ps1, xqueue3): x-compat 0; build.ps1 -Ladder 1:3:2 exit 0 (compiles the new exports); then per configuration two 'mix xsim.run --engine spiral' runs (an in-job A/A) and one sim-run.ps1 run. PASS = memo (small cells --memo) and fake-paced (small, --pace-ms 50): CLI exit and line count equal mix's, and 0 differences on fields that are stable across the two mix runs; rejected (no --spi): both exit 1 and the CLI prints 'XSIM rejected: missing --spi or --spi-file' (mix raises instead; the line form is the intended change). If PASS: S5c's remaining step is build.ps1 calling sim-run.ps1 instead of mix xsim.run, after S5a lands. If the CLI fails to start: read its tail (code path, app start) before touching the engine.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_128 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 128u32, "2026-10-31T06:08Z", "x", "2026-10-31 02:08 x: x-s5cprobe (job-1793426472705, 96 s) RED at compile, nothing compared: ETY-UnboundVariable 'term' in simframes.args_text (term is cells_executor's local alias). Fixed: erlang_term::term; and since simframes does not open sim_args, its RunParsed/RunRejected cases were moved behind a new sim_args.parsed_corpus : list string -> option corpus_options. RULE CORRECTION made BEFORE any rejected-case result existed (found by reading sim_run.spi:244, which prints 'XSIM rejected ' ++# why with no colon): the rejected clause now matches '^XSIM rejected:? missing --spi or --spi-file'. All other n127 clauses unchanged. Relaunching as x-s5cprobe2.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_129 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 129u32, "2026-10-31T06:30Z", "x", "2026-10-31 02:30 x: TWO RESULTS. (A) x-s5probe2 partial read (job-1793425214840, still running corpus): n126's PREDICTION FAILED. ladder-cells still has starts elixir=4 spiral=5 and cancels 1 vs 2 on lines 0 and 3. So the harvest-reconcile fix was not the cause (it stays: it matches runner.ex). Green with that fix: math-cells 0, s3-cells 0, s3-memo 0, s3-leak 0 (all exit 1 as expected), s4-daemon 0; s1b-small only peak_beam_mb 50 vs 51 (inside 2 MB). NEW DIAGNOSIS from runner.ex:154-156: Task.yield_many(tasks, pace) waits until ALL in-flight tasks reply or pace elapses, but Spiral's 'collected reply pace' waited for the FIRST reply only, then took the mailbox at 0 ms. At pace 0 both engines take only what has already arrived, so ladder-cells at pace 0 compares the delivery loop's speed with a memo-hit task's duration: Elixir's loop is fast enough that its two runs agree on 4/1, and the Gleam loop is slower, so tasks finish, slots free and one queued key starts and is superseded. FIX (cells_executor.spi): stepped harvests with 'harvested reply (deadline_after pace)', which receives until every pid has replied or the monotonic deadline passes (yield_many semantics); at pace 0 it is identical to the old collect, so s5probe2's corpus verdict holds for this code. (B) x-s5cprobe2 (job-1793427193163, 603 s): simframes with the S5c exports COMPILED (16,798 lines, 14 exports) and the ladder exit 0, but all 3 CLI configurations failed in the launcher before erl: elixir.bat passes -e 'IO.write(:code.lib_dir(:elixir))' through cmd, whose parser breaks on the parens (') was unexpected at this time'). FIX: sim-run.ps1 writes a one-line elixir-lib.exs and runs elixir on it (box: 6 ebins found); erl -eval with parens and -extra checked on the box (exit 3 for 3 plain args). PRE-REGISTERED, unchanged rules: x-s5cprobe3 = n127/n128 rules. x-s5probe3 (-Only, mirror xs4probe): ladder-paced (ladder-cells + --pace-ms 20000 in BOTH engines), ladder-cells, math-cells, s3-cells, s3-memo, s3-leak, s1b-small, s4-daemon. PASS for S5a = ladder-paced 0 stable-field differences, and every other configuration as in n126 EXCEPT ladder-cells at pace 0, which is now recorded but not gating, ONLY IF ladder-paced passes (this amendment is made after seeing (A); its basis is the yield_many reading above, and the paced run is its test). If ladder-paced differs: the gap is semantic and S5a stays blocked. Corpus is not re-run (identical code path at pace 0, s5probe2's verdict counts once it finishes).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_130 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 130u32, "2026-10-31T06:46Z", "x", "2026-10-31 02:46 x: x-s5cprobe3 (job-1793428440433, 635 s) by n127/n128: NOT PASSED BY THE LETTER, on one field only. The bare-erl CLI WORKS end to end: memo (cells --memo): exits 0/0/0, 5/5/5 lines, 0 differences (peak_beam_mb A/A-excluded); rejected: both exit 1, CLI prints 'XSIM rejected missing --spi or --spi-file'; fake-paced: exits 0/0/0, 3/3/3 lines, every behaviour field equal, but peak_beam_mb mix=51/50 vs cli=36/36. n127 gave no peak_beam_mb allowance for CLI-vs-mix, so this clause fails. Reading: peak_beam_mb is erlang:memory(total) of the whole VM, and the CLI VM does not hold mix, hex and the Elixir compiler (about 15 MB). Removing them is S5c's purpose, so this is a rule gap, not an engine difference. AMENDMENT, made AFTER seeing this result and flagged as such: CLI-vs-mix comparisons exclude peak_beam_mb (a VM-composition metric). Every other S5c clause passed; no rerun is needed, because a rerun cannot change the VM composition. S5c's launcher is proven; its build.ps1 step (C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s5c\\stage\\build.ps1, 3 mix xsim.run calls -> scripts/sim-run.ps1) lands after S5a. Fixture/peak note for verify39+: the sim build lines printed by the CLI will carry lower peak_beam_mb than earlier logs.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_131 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 131u32, "2026-10-31T06:53Z", "x", "2026-10-31 02:53 x: S5b DRAFT 1 (in the repo, unstaged, additive): the CLI dispatches subcommands. cli_text: 'events' -> sim_args.events_args -> events_written (cells from the .spi with an EMPTY _real.spir, as xsim.events does; --cells limit; staged_stream + staged_final written to --out/--expected; prints 'xsim.events <basename> deliveries=<non-blank lines - 1 header>', which is Xsim.Log.decode's count); 'world' -> sim_args.world_args -> world_written (staged_world, both files written, prints 'xsim.world <basename> cells=<n> bytes=<byte_size>'); anything else runs the engine as before. Rejections print 'XSIM rejected events|world: <why>' and exit 1 (mix raises). cells_executor: parsed_text shared by parsed_spi (with _real.spir) and plain_spi (without). The parsers already existed in sim_args with tests but had no runtime caller. Not yet ported: Xsim.Personas.parse's validation (an invalid persona raises in mix; build.ps1 passes only valid ones), frames (needs Xsim.Json's printer), diff, thread_run (Notebook.evaluate host calls). Local: x-compat 0 in 29 files, kit standards 0. PRE-REGISTERED x-s5bprobe (C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s5b\\launch-x-s5bprobe.ps1, xqueue3): x-compat 0; build -Ladder 1:3:2 exit 0; for build.ps1's 4 worlds (sim-small, sim-stress, sim-lost, sim-gap) and the prelude events stream, run mix xsim.<task> and sim-run.ps1 <task> with the same args. PASS = for all 5 cases both exit 0, both output files byte-identical (mix vs CLI), and the summary line identical; plus 'world --spi <file>' (no --out) exits 1 with 'XSIM rejected world: missing --out'. Equality with the committed fixture is printed but not gating (verify38 may re-accept fixtures).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_132 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 132u32, "2026-10-31T07:07Z", "x", "2026-10-31 03:07 x: x-s5probe3 (job-1793428696736, 22 min, xs4probe) by n129's rule: PASS for its part of the S5a gate. ladder-paced (--pace-ms 20000 in both engines): 0 stable-field differences, so with yield_many-style harvesting the engines agree on ladder-cells' starts and cancels, and n129's amendment applies: ladder-cells at pace 0 (still starts 5 vs 4, cancels 2 vs 1, now also daemon procs 8 vs 9) is recorded, not gating, as loop-speed timing. math-cells 0, s3-cells 0, s3-memo 0, s4-daemon 0; s3-leak (all exit 1 as expected) and s1b-small differ only in peak_beam_mb 51 vs 50 (inside 2 MB). verify38 is PASS (X-CLIENT n20). The S5a gate now waits only on x-s5probe2's corpus run (job-1793425214840, started about 02:12 local, ~100 min). If corpus shows 0 stable-field differences: land S5a (land-s5a.ps1 -Apply, undo copy under C:\\home\\data\\undo), then verify39 by n121's S5a rule. NOTE for S5a's landing: stage\\deletions.txt deletes scripts/sim-engine-parity.ps1, and with no Elixir engine left the paced-vs-unpaced comparison cannot be rerun, so this probe set is the final record of engine parity.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_133 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 133u32, "2026-10-31T07:23Z", "x", "2026-10-31 03:23 x: x-s5bprobe (job-1793429816539, 82 s) RED at build, nothing compared: gleam-modules refused xsim_frames/part22 at 1,267 lines (> 1,000). part22 is ONE function, closure109 (1,249 lines): Spiral inlines top-level functions into an exported lambda, so the single cli_text dispatcher merged the run, events and world paths into one closure (generated xsim_frames grew 16,798 -> 18,395 lines). FIX: no dispatcher in Spiral. Three exports, cli_run, cli_events and cli_world (command_args drops the subcommand word), each closure holding one path; sim-run.ps1 picks the export by the first argument (events|world -> cli_<word>, else cli_run). Lesson for S5b's remaining commands: one export per subcommand, never a Spiral-side switch. Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-s5bprobe2 with n131's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_134 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 134u32, "2026-10-31T07:42Z", "x", "2026-10-31 03:42 x: S5a LANDED (land-s5a.ps1 -Apply; undo copy C:\\home\\data\\undo\\s5a-land-20261031-033854). Gate met: verify38 PASS (X-CLIENT n20), x-s5probe2 corpus 0 stable-field differences (5403 s), x-s5probe3 ladder-paced 0 and the rest per n132. In the tree now: xsim.run.ex is the Spiral path only (30 lines; --engine gone, nothing left passes it), xsim_test.exs keeps its 8 engine-free tests, build.ps1 drops the xsim.parity step; archived via the undo copy: runner.ex, invariants.ex, executors.ex, daemon_memory.ex, xsim.parity.ex, runner_crash_test.exs, fixtures/prelude.parity.json, scripts/sim-engine-parity.ps1. RULE CORRECTION for verify39, made BEFORE launch: n121's clause 'Spiral XSIM lines equal the last verify's Spiral lines field for field' cannot be checked, because no verify or probe log holds full Spiral XSIM lines (sim-engine-parity printed only differences), and engine parity is already recorded by n126-n132. PRE-REGISTERED verify39 (launch-x-verify33.ps1 -Label x-verify39 -MirrorName xqueue1 -Core 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52; verify.ps1 -Part all -Accept): PASS = exit 0; 'x verify /' sim accept, client build before accepting, e2e accept, client build and sim build all exit 0 (sim build runs the corpus, math-cells and ladder engine runs through mix xsim.run, now Spiral-only, and gates on result=fail); all client and sim notebooks pass, including sim_run (n120's re-lay), cells_executor, simframes and sim_args (the S5b/S5c additions); x-compat 0 findings for client and sim; NO 'accepted apps/sim/fixtures/prelude.parity.json' line; mix test passes with the 8 kept tests; the three-way land dry run shows 0 conflicts and every accepted fixture unchanged. A timeout red is rerun alone. A red in a deleted module's consumer: restore from the undo copy and say so.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_135 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 135u32, "2026-10-31T07:55Z", "x", "2026-10-31 03:55 x: x-s5bprobe2 (job-1793432235105, 197 s): build exit 0 (the per-command exports fixed the part size), the rejected case is ok, but all 5 CLI cases died in PowerShell's binder before erl: '[Parameter(ValueFromRemainingArguments)]' makes sim-run.ps1 an advanced script with common parameters, so '--out' prefix-matches -OutVariable/-OutBuffer ('parameter name out is ambiguous'). --spi/--cells/--pace-ms match no common parameter, which is why x-s5cprobe3 passed. FIX: sim-run.ps1 reads $args (a plain script has no common parameters; box check: world --spi a --out b --expected c --ErrorAction x -Verbose --pace-ms 5 all reach the script verbatim). Mix references printed: sim-small cells=3 bytes=5301, sim-stress cells=17 bytes=155044, sim-lost cells=3 bytes=4763, sim-gap cells=6 bytes=6177, events deliveries=58. Same trap is latent in scripts/sim-shell.ps1 (same param form); not touched yet. Relaunching as x-s5bprobe3, n131's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_136 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 136u32, "2026-10-31T08:11Z", "x", "2026-10-31 04:11 x: x-s5bprobe3 (job-1793433704518, 277 s, xqueue3) by n131's rule: PASS. x-compat 0; build + ladder exit 0; sim-small, sim-stress, sim-lost, sim-gap (world) and prelude (events): mix and CLI both exit 0, both output files byte-identical between mix and CLI AND equal to the committed fixtures, summary lines identical (cells=3 bytes=5301; cells=17 bytes=155044; cells=3 bytes=4763; cells=6 bytes=6177; deliveries=58); 'world --spi' without --out exits 1 with 'XSIM rejected world: missing --out'. So xsim.events and xsim.world can leave once build.ps1 calls the CLI. For frames: OTP json:encode matches Elixir JSON.encode! on 8 edge strings (box A/B, Elixir 1.20.4 / OTP 29: quotes, backslash, \\t\\n\\r, \\u0007 \\u0000 \\u001F \\b \\f, CJK + emoji raw, U+2028/2029 raw, DEL, zero-width space), so the frames JSON can use it (with erlang:iolist_to_binary, not unicode:characters_to_binary, since json iodata holds bytes).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_137 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 137u32, "2026-10-31T08:19Z", "x", "2026-10-31 04:19 x: S5b DRAFT 2: 'frames' added as its own export cli_frames (sim-run.ps1 routes events|world|frames). frames_written: reads --file; posts = splitter.posts_of (the same function xsim_split exports as thread_posts); program = protocol.file_cells, each NFC-normalized (unicode:characters_to_nfc_binary), joined by blank lines, split on newlines ([] when empty); JSON laid out exactly as Xsim.Json at these depths ({ opened, 4-space indent; both lists opened at 8 spaces; [] when empty; keys posts < program sorted) with strings from OTP json:encode + iolist_to_binary; summary 'xsim.frames <basename> cells=<file_cells count> posts=<n>'. No app start (xsim.frames starts none). simframes' own file_cells(limit, spi) was renamed run_cells so it no longer shadows protocol.file_cells. Local x-compat 0, standards 0. PRE-REGISTERED x-s5bprobe4: n131's 6 checks unchanged, plus frames on apps/client/oauth1.spi: mix and CLI both exit 0, output byte-identical mix vs CLI, summary line identical; equality with apps/client/e2e/thread-oauth1.expected.json printed, not gating.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_138 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 138u32, "2026-10-31T08:49Z", "x", "2026-10-31 04:49 x: x-s5bprobe4 (job-1793435148389, 294 s) by n137: PASS. All of n131's checks again, plus frames on oauth1.spi: mix and CLI exit 0, output byte-identical between them AND equal to apps/client/e2e/thread-oauth1.expected.json, line 'xsim.frames oauth1.spi cells=17 posts=17' identical. S5b DRAFT 3: thread_run as its own export cli_thread_run (sim-run.ps1 routes it). sim_args.thread_args (--file, --program | --expected; same missing-option texts as the mix task). The program comes from --program, else the --expected JSON's program lines (OTP json:decode, joined by newlines, plus a trailing newline), as in program_text. cells_executor.evaluated_tests = Elixir.Spiral.Cells.Notebook.evaluate(doc, [path, root, plan: tests, spi: false, memo: false]); each test row is projected to 'name status [target=status,...]' (inspect of each field), never the whole map, so no run-varying field enters the comparison; result_ok = status :ok. The XSIM thread_run line keeps the mix format (file, cells, tests, same_cells, status=got/want, result), and agree = same_cells && same status && same rows && rows non-empty && want ok. KNOWN DIFFERENCE: the per-row diff lines under a failing line show the projected text rather than Elixir inspect of tuples; build.ps1 only matches 'same_cells=false .*result=fail', so nothing gates on them. Local x-compat 0, standards 0 (several first-draft cells split). PRE-REGISTERED x-s5bprobe5: n137's checks unchanged, plus thread_run as build.ps1 runs it. (a) --file oauth1.spi --expected thread-oauth1.expected.json: mix and CLI both exit 0 with identical XSIM thread_run lines. (b) the tampered program (the expected program + a failing test, built as build.ps1 builds it): both exit 1, identical XSIM thread_run lines, and the CLI line matches 'same_cells=false .*result=fail'.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_139 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 139u32, "2026-10-31T08:56Z", "x", "2026-10-31 04:56 x: x-s5bprobe5 (job-1793436789306, 32 s) RED at compile, nothing compared: simframes.paired_rows pattern 'a :: ra, b :: rb' parses as a :: (ra, b) :: rb (ETY-RecursiveMetavarsNotAllowed). Fixed to '(a :: ra), (b :: rb)', the form listm'.spi:270 uses. Relaunching as x-s5bprobe6, n138's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_140 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 140u32, "2026-10-31T09:06Z", "x", "2026-10-31 05:06 x: S5 FINAL STEP STAGED (not landed; waits for verify39 and x-s5bprobe6): C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\s5final\\stage\\build.ps1 = the live (post-S5a) build.ps1 with every mix call replaced (16 exact replacements, 0 'mix' left): mix deps.get -> $simRun = scripts/sim-run.ps1 (it builds Cells itself), 3 xsim.run -> sim-run, world/frames/events/thread_run (x2) -> sim-run subcommands, mix test dropped. TEST MAP CHECKED against the tree (n121's claim, now verified by name): xsim_test.exs's 8 -> reading.posted_cells_rebuild_exactly + glyphs.weights_follow_x (280-weight frames, CJK/Hangul double), reading.posted_frames_keep_no_trailing_space, splitter.dots_that_x_would_link_are_defused + defuse.restoring_undoes_both_defusings, splitter.cells_start_at_column_zero, events.streams_decode_back_to_themselves (+ foreign_streams_are_refused), final_state + runner_checks.a_saved_stream_replays_to_the_same_visible_results (+ build.ps1's Confirm-Fixture on prelude.final.json), personas.numbers_at_the_end_are_broken / numbers_after_a_dot_or_none_stay, scenario_checks.breakers_only_pick_tests_a_break_changes + personas_survive_an_empty_thread. spiral_test.exs: its 3 fixture checks are build.ps1's Confirm-Fixture on CLI output; its 2 'same deliveries' checks compare the Elixir facade with the direct exports, and the facade goes away. x_compat_test duplicates the compiler's --x-compat (verify gates it). xsim.diff and xsim.x_compat have no caller in x (grep of ps1/yml/md). CI keeps erlef/setup-beam (Cells is Elixir), so no workflow or actions.lock change. Final landing = staged build.ps1 + archive mix.exs, mix.lock, lib/ and test/ (undo copy), then verify40.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_141 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 141u32, "2026-10-31T09:11Z", "x", "2026-10-31 05:11 x: x-s5bprobe6 (job-1793437300048, 252 s): world x4, events, frames and the rejected case all ok again; both thread_run cases VOID by a PROBE bug: '@($t.Args)' is an array subexpression, not a splat, so native mix got '--file, ...' (unknown option) and the CLI got mangled args (cells=4). Probe fixed ([string[]] $targs; @targs). One REAL gap seen anyway: the CLI printed same_cells=True (sm'::obj_to_string of a bool on Gleam), Elixir prints true; fixed with bool_word. The CLI also reported status=error/error with the mangled args; to see the cause if it recurs, verdict_of now writes 'xsim thread_run / evaluate status <inspect>' to STDERR when the status is not :ok (stdout unchanged). Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-s5bprobe7, n138's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_142 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 142u32, "2026-10-31T09:29Z", "x", "2026-10-31 05:29 x: x-s5bprobe7 (job-1793438028153, 320 s): all world/events/frames/rejected cases ok; thread_run expected ok (mix and CLI exit 0, both 'XSIM thread_run file=oauth1.spi cells=4 tests=2 same_cells=true status=ok/ok result=pass'). thread_run tampered printed FAILED, but only because of the PROBE: its capture used PowerShell's case-insensitive -match '^XSIM thread_run ', which also caught the CLI's new stderr diagnostic \"xsim thread_run / evaluate status Error(zig failed (exit 1) ... __assert_eq { actual = 1; expected = 2 })\", which is the tampered test failing as intended. The CLI's own XSIM line is identical to mix's: 'XSIM thread_run file=oauth1.spi cells=5 tests=3 same_cells=false status=error/ok result=fail', both exit 1. Not claiming a pass on that reading: probe captures switched to -cmatch, rerunning as x-s5bprobe8 (job-1793438936892) with n138's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_143 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 143u32, "2026-10-31T09:37Z", "x", "2026-10-31 05:37 x: x-s5bprobe8 (job-1793438936892, 434 s) by n138's rule: PASS, 0 cases failed. World x4, events and frames: mix and CLI files byte-identical and equal to the committed fixtures, summary lines identical; thread_run expected: both exit 0, 'XSIM thread_run file=oauth1.spi cells=4 tests=2 same_cells=true status=ok/ok result=pass' identical; thread_run tampered: both exit 1, 'XSIM thread_run file=oauth1.spi cells=5 tests=3 same_cells=false status=error/ok result=fail' identical; the rejected case ok. So every mix task build.ps1 calls has a proven CLI twin (S5b done except diff/x_compat, which nothing calls). PROCESS CORRECTION: the kit-work-in-repo hook refused a new land script under C:\\home\\data (10-31 enforcement: C:\\home\\data\\<team>-drafts and \\undo are drift, not workspaces). So the final step will NOT go through a staging land script: once verify39 is green it is applied IN PLACE in x (build.ps1 with sim-run calls, verify.ps1 without its mix deps.get, apps/sim/.gitignore with only the frames/split build lines, and the mix project removed: mix.exs, lib/, test/; git is the undo for these tracked files), then verify40. The leftover C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\{s5a,s5b,s5c,s5final} folders and C:\\home\\data\\undo\\s5a-land-20261031-033854 get archived with i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 (dry run first) once their contents are in the repo.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_144 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 144u32, "2026-10-31T09:42Z", "x", "2026-10-31 05:42 x: verify39 (job-1793433004835, 107 min, xqueue1) by n134's rule: PASS. Exit 0; x verify / sim accept, client build before accepting, e2e accept, client build and sim build all exit 0; all 43 client and 23 sim notebooks passed (incl. sim_run = n120's re-lay, cells_executor, simframes, sim_args with the S5b/S5c code as of the 07:42Z sync); x-compat 0 in 43 and 29 files; the corpus ran through the Spiral-only xsim.run (files=73, 1642 s and 1423 s); 'xsim / thread_run rejects a tampered thread'; mix test with the 8 kept tests passed inside the sim build's Invoke-Block; NO prelude.parity.json accept line. Land dry run: 0 conflicts, every accepted fixture unchanged, 13 to land, all generated apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames*.gleam from the 07:42Z source, which is older than the live simframes.spi (frames/thread_run added since), so NOT landed: verify40 regenerates them from the current source. S5a is proven in the repo. Next: the S5 final step in place, then verify40.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_145 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 145u32, "2026-10-31T09:51Z", "x", "2026-10-31 05:51 x: S5 FINAL STEP APPLIED IN PLACE (unstaged; git is the undo). apps/sim/build.ps1: every mix call replaced by scripts/sim-run.ps1 (the same 16 exact replacements as the staged copy, re-run on the live file; result identical), mix deps.get and mix test gone; scripts/verify.ps1: its apps/sim 'mix deps.get' block removed; apps/sim/.gitignore keeps only /frames/build/ and /split/build/; removed: apps/sim/mix.exs and all of lib/ (15 .ex) and test/ (4 .exs), all tracked in git (git ls-files: 26 incl. S5a's; 0 untracked files lost), plus the ignored mix.lock. _build/ and deps/ (ignored build outputs) left for now. Docs: apps/client/README.md lines 260/761/818 now name scripts/sim-run.ps1 world|thread_run|frames instead of mix xsim.*; apps/sim/spi/README.md describes the bare-erl CLI, the one-export-per-subcommand reason and the Cells host calls instead of the Elixir glue. apps/sim now has no .ex/.exs (n121's S5c pass condition). PRE-REGISTERED verify40 (launch-x-verify33.ps1 -Label x-verify40 -MirrorName xqueue1 -Core 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52): PASS = exit 0; x verify / sim accept, client build before accepting, e2e accept, client build and sim build all exit 0; all client and sim notebooks pass; x-compat 0 for both; the 11 sim fixtures accepted (4 worlds x 2, thread-oauth1, prelude.events.jsonl, prelude.final.json) and the land dry run shows them unchanged with 0 conflicts (the regenerated frames gleam files are expected to land); 'xsim / thread_run rejects a tampered thread' and the corpus line present; the job's stdout shows no 'mix xsim' or 'mix test' (only Cells' own mix build inside sim-run). A timeout red is rerun alone; a CLI red is fixed in place, not by restoring mix.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_146 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 146u32, "2026-10-31T10:08Z", "x", "2026-10-31 06:08 x: DRIFT CLEANUP (after the kit-work-in-repo hook refusal). Archived with i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 (dry run, then -Apply; 0 blob verify failures): C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\{s1b, s3a, s3c, s3d, s3e, s3f, s3g, s3h, s4, s4probe, s5a, s5c, s5final} (13 folders, 65 files; all landed: S1-S4 by verify37/n119, S5a by verify39, S5c/S5 final in place) -> %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261031-060539 (restore: -Restore <that>\\manifest.tsv -RestoreTo <dir>); C:\\home\\data\\undo\\s5a-land-20261031-033854 (11 files, every one also in git) -> kit\\archive\\20261031-060646. LEFT in x-drafts (15 folders), each needing its own decision: s5b (probe launcher, kept until verify40 is green), launchers (launch-x-verify33.ps1 runs every x verify; its committable home would be x/scripts, but it carries node-machine paths, so that is a design question for an x node-verify script), tools (cell-weights.ps1 is superseded by the compiler's --x-compat and i574n.github/scripts/x-compat-census.ps1; groom/lost-paths are post-wipe one-shots), archive (the py/txt archive from 10-30), and older X slices (fs4, g3, g4, gleam-units, jsondyn, landed-programs, pre-split-1029-2210, sim-land, sim-programs, sim-queue2, superseded) to triage against the tree before archiving.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_147 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 147u32, "2026-10-31T10:15Z", "x", "2026-10-31 06:15 x: DRIFT CLEANUP part 2: archived C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\{sim-land, sim-queue2, sim-programs, landed-programs, pre-split-1029-2210, g3, fs4} (7 folders, 97 files, 0 verify failures) -> %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261031-061426; removed the empty superseded/. Reasons: sim-land and sim-queue2 held pre-S5 copies of the removed mix tasks and older .spi; sim-programs and landed-programs are kit programs of landed slices; pre-split is .bak files and old launchers; g3's G3 is live and n29 moved the resume rule to g4's launcher; fs4's file_system heal landed (live lib file newer; its kits were in landed-programs). x-drafts now holds 7 folders, all with a reason: g4 + jsondyn + gleam-units (X-HAND-UNITS held drafts, gated on GLEAM-INLINE-SINGLE-USE by n29/n30; their home is the repo once that rule lets them land), s5b (until verify40), launchers (launch-x-verify33.ps1, in use), tools, archive.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_148 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 148u32, "2026-10-31T11:34Z", "x", "2026-10-31 07:34 x: verify40 (job-1793440847072, 86 min) by n145's rule: NOT PASSED. Green: x-compat 0 in 43 and 29 files; sim accept exit 0 with all 11 fixtures written THROUGH THE CLI (summary lines identical to the mix references); client build before accepting, e2e accept and client build exit 0; all 43 client notebooks passed; the corpus engine run through sim-run (files=73, 1204 s) and math-cells passed. RED: the sim build's first thread_run printed 'XSIM thread_run file=oauth1.spi cells=4 tests=2 same_cells=true status=error/error result=fail', and the stderr diagnostic gives the cause: Notebook.evaluate returned Error(\"... 1st argument: the table identifier does not refer to an existing ETS table\"). The same command passed in x-s5bprobe8 (status=ok/ok). Differences: build.ps1 sets SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT and runs the math-cells CLI (which starts the warm compiler daemon) right before; the probe set no port. Every Cells ETS table goes through Spiral.Cells.Tables.ensure (an owner process per table), so a missing table means something evaluated where that owner never ran or has died. Also found: cli_vm_started ignored ensure_all_started's result; it now prints 'xsim / <app> did not start: <reason>' to stderr (x-compat 0, standards 0). DIAGNOSIS RUN (information only, scratchpad launcher, xqueue3): A = build.ps1's order with the port set (math-cells, then thread_run twice), B = daemon killed, no port env, thread_run twice; it prints each run's XSIM, start-failure and evaluate-status lines. Landing waits for a green verify (verify41).")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_149 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 149u32, "2026-10-31T11:42Z", "x", "2026-10-31 07:42 x: ROOT CAUSE of verify40's thread_run red, found with x-threadrepro (job-1793446620885, 109 s): it failed in ALL variants (port set after math-cells, twice; no port and no daemon, twice), always 'evaluate status Error(... does not refer to an existing ETS table)', and no app failed to start. So it is not the port and not the daemon's state. Cause: Spiral.Cells.CompilerClient.mix_env/0 (spiral/apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/compiler_client.ex:795) is 'if Code.ensure_loaded?(Mix), do: Mix.env() ...'; sim-run.ps1 put ALL of Elixir's lib/*/ebin on the code path, so Mix loads, but the :mix application never starts, and Mix.env() reads the missing Mix.State ETS table. mix_env is called only when the client SPAWNS a compiler daemon. x-s5bprobe8 passed because earlier mix runs in the same job had already started the warm daemon and the CLI only attached; verify40 and the repro had no mix left, so the CLI spawned it. FIX (x, scripts/sim-run.ps1): Elixir's mix/ebin is left off the code path, so Code.ensure_loaded?(Mix) is false and Cells uses its own 'dev' fallback, the same MIX_ENV the mix runs had (the CLI is now mix-free in fact, not just in name). Upstream note for the compiler/Cells team, not acted on: mix_env could check that the :mix application is running rather than that the module loads. Re-running the repro as x-threadrepro2, PREDICTION: A2, A3, B1, B2 all 'status=ok/ok result=pass'. Then verify41 with n145's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_150 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 150u32, "2026-10-31T11:49Z", "x", "2026-10-31 07:49 x: x-threadrepro2 (job-1793447070422, 183 s): n149's PREDICTION HELD. With Elixir's mix/ebin off sim-run's code path: A1 math-cells pass; A2 and A3 (port set, after math-cells) and B1 and B2 (daemon killed, no port env; B1 had to spawn its own daemon, the case that failed before) all print 'XSIM thread_run file=oauth1.spi cells=4 tests=2 same_cells=true status=ok/ok result=pass'. LAUNCHED verify41 (launch-x-verify33.ps1 -Label x-verify41 -MirrorName xqueue1 -Core 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52) under n145's rule, unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_151 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL", 151u32, "2026-10-31T15:21Z", "x", "2026-10-31 11:21 x: verify41 (job-1793447701381, 3 h 19 min under node load, xqueue1) by n145's rule: PASS. Exit 0; x verify / sim accept, client build before accepting, e2e accept, client build and sim build all exit 0; all 43 client and 23 sim notebooks passed; x-compat 0 in 43 and 29 files; the 11 sim fixtures accepted THROUGH THE CLI (4 worlds x 2, thread-oauth1, prelude.events.jsonl, prelude.final.json); corpus engine runs (files=73, 1317 s and 1278 s); 'xsim / thread_run rejects a tampered thread'; no 'mix xsim' or 'mix test' line anywhere (only Cells' own mix build inside sim-run). Land dry run: 0 conflicts, every fixture unchanged, 16 to land, all generated apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames*.gleam from the 07:49 sync, older than the live simframes.spi (diff and serve came later), so not landed; the next x verify regenerates them. DONE: apps/sim has no Elixir (mix.exs, lib/, test/ removed in place, unstaged), the engine, every former mix task and the build run through scripts/sim-run.ps1 on a bare erl. Follow-ups live elsewhere: SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY (the sim as the X API server), CELLS-MIX-ENV-GUARD (Cells' mix_env robustness), KIT-STANDARDS-ENTRY-SUBDIRS.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_note_edit_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-NOTE-EDIT", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:31Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-EDIT (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_1 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:32 main-thread: experiment script lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/measure.ps1 compiles each contract to Zig with the installed compiler, builds and runs the ok ones with zig ReleaseFast, and compares the compile verdict and exit with the accepted F# rows of 123705. Contract rows: 627 (413 compile errors, the negative contracts; 214 ok, which ran). Smoke on the first 6: 2 same, 4 where F# compiles but Zig refuses, all with the same cause: 'The return type of main in the Zig backend should be a 32-bit int (or unit)'. Contracts return a value (string, record) that F#'s tier treats as exit 0. Fixed in the tree, unbuilt: codegenZig's main accepts a single value of any type and discards it in spiralMain (return 0), matching F#; i32 and unit unchanged; tuples still rejected, with a clearer message. Next: build that, then run the full 627 to find the real feature gaps.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_2 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:31Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:31 helper-lane lap 1: unblocked the full run: the codegenZig main change (a single value of any type is discarded, return 0; tuples rejected with a clearer message) is committed in e0ffc80b (10-18 14:50, the old message string is gone from spiral_compiler.fs) and in the installed Release compiler (built 10-18 22:57), so the smoke's 4 F#-ok/Zig-error rows should now compile. The baseline run single-flight-20261018-123705 still has its results.tsv. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/measure.ps1 -Parallel 2 inside Invoke-Heavy (627 contract compiles to Zig + 214 zig builds, ~15 min), writes contracts-on-zig.tsv and the grouped zig gaps.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_3 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:38 main heavy queue ran lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/measure.ps1 -Parallel 2 (10:26-10:37, 669 s): all 627 contracts give the same compile verdict on Zig as on the accepted F# rows (0 F#-ok/Zig-error, 0 F#-error/Zig-ok); of the 214 that compile and run, exit codes match on 211 and differ on 3; table in lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/contracts-on-zig.tsv. So Zig can carry the contracts suite once the 3 exit differences are explained.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_4 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:46Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:46 helper-lane lap 3: explained main's 3 exit differences from contracts-on-zig.tsv against the 08:05 baseline: (1) contract_effectful_forall_bottom and (2) mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals are F#-text samples ($ macro bodies in F# syntax, failwith and 1L literals), so Zig build-fails them by construction (all_terminals is also a Known Fsharp row); (3) mega_brzozowski_derivatives/proof_suite is an F# timeout in the measure run only: the baseline's F# row exits 0, the same as Zig. So all 627 agree once those two are kept on F#. Decision (autonomous, per the user's 10-18 promote-Zig, F#-redundant direction): SWITCH, not added rows; this also drops about 214 dotnet fsi native runs (median 7.2 s) for zig build-exe (about 2 s). Staged lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/switch.py (anchors --check OK on today's files, LF): test.ps1 gains Get-ContractBackends (Zig unless listed), used by the contract_ and mega_ branches of the contracts suite and by smoke's contract samples; matrix.psd1 gains FsharpContracts with the two F#-text samples. Caveats for the review: ExpectedErrors pins are type-check texts, but the tsv stores only a truncated first error line, so the review run is what proves the 236 pins on Zig; and the removed Fsharp contract rows need the staged accept-prune (SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS) or they stay in EXPECTED.tsv.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_5 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 5u32, "2026-10-19T21:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:40 helper-lane lap 6: lab/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG/switch.py dry run still matches today's apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1 and tests/matrix.psd1 (anchors match). It is ready for a main pack slot, but test.ps1 is busy with main's probe-queue for about an hour, so it waits.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_6 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 6u32, "2026-10-22T06:38Z", "main", "02:38 main: switch.py's dry run still matches today's tmp/scripts/test.ps1 and tmp/tests/matrix.psd1 ('anchors match'). Slot: right AFTER the PACK-AFTER-NIGHT install, not inside its review, because moving 627 contract rows from Fsharp to Zig would hide compiler differences. Sequence: switch.py <tmp> --apply, then a single-flight accept run per the accept rules (reviewed rows; DISAGREE 0 on both cores; determinism check), where every moved row must keep its verdict (the 10-19 measure: all 627 agree once contract_effectful_forall_bottom and mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals stay on F#). Then delete the folder (measure.ps1 was the one-off experiment). Its output contracts-on-zig.tsv moved to the run cache (lab-outputs/CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG, sha256 checked).")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_7 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 7u32, "2026-10-22T22:11Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:11 main: staged in the post-install matrix bundle %TEMP%\\matrix-bundle\\bundle.ps1 (dry-run clean today), applied after PACK-NEXT-3b is accepted and installed; details and rule in ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY note 11.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_8 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 8u32, "2026-10-24T00:22Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:22 main: status fixed (staged / half landed). %TEMP%\\matrix-bundle\\bundle.ps1 is still dry-run clean on today's tree (all anchors match; Lean 3 pins). It applies after the next4/5f composite, which carries next3b, is reviewed and installed (scratch next-session.md step 7).")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_9 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:34 (subagent): blocked: switch.py + contracts review queued (matrix inputs are main's) -> next: read the review; accept with prune; Done | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): switch.py queued (matrix input) -> next: contracts review + accept with prune | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: switch.py dry run matches; matrix input, main's pack -> next: main's pack slot")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_10 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 10u32, "2026-10-27T08:17Z", "main", "10-27 04:17 main: 10-27 04:17 main groom: fresh status (stale since the 10-19 laps). The 10-22 post-install matrix bundle (TEMP matrix-bundle/bundle.ps1) was NEVER APPLIED, although its stated condition (apply after PACK-NEXT-3b is accepted and installed) has been met for days (5n and later packs installed since). A dry run now: all anchors still match, for ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY (Zig shims for the abi_external samples), CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG (contract rows move from Fsharp to Zig), and LEAN-BACKEND (pin v4.35.0-rc4 -> v4.35.0 stable, 3 pins in apps/cells/README.md). Not applied tonight on purpose: it changes which rows the matrix runs, so it must not land in the middle of the compiler lane's review or accept, and the Lean pin needs the stable toolchain installed first (otherwise every Lean row fails). Offered to the compiler lane, which runs the matrices; it lands right after their current review, then the next matrix run proves it.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_11 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 11u32, "2026-10-27T09:25Z", "main", "10-27 05:25 main: 10-27 05:25 main: Matrix-bundle review single-flight-20261027-042215 (-Suite all -Native -ReuseNative, 7,367 rows): DISAGREE 0 under both oracles; the one agree-fail is the long-known dynamic_array_bounds_negative Rust row (known vs C). This item's clause is GREEN: all 620 contract rows that moved from Fsharp to Zig have the SAME compile status as their Fsharp baseline row in EXPECTED.tsv, and the same stdout wherever they compile (620 of 620, 0 different). The 408 compile errors are the suite's expected rejections (e.g. contract_block_inside_phrasing: Unification failure, child flow vs child phrasing). Method trap, fixed in my own check: a first comparison filtered ids on ^samples/contract and silently compared only 331 rows; 289 contract-suite rows have other names. Still open: the bundle accept.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_12 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 12u32, "2026-10-27T23:20Z", "main", "10-27 19:20 main: Bundle accept GREEN (accept-6, job-1793136844465, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 17:34 to 19:1x; ran in slot 2 against the TRAIN-frozen lib). test.ps1 exit 0; 7,367 rows accepted into spiral-bin baseline EXPECTED.tsv, with toolchain.tsv recorded (SPIRAL_CC pinned to scoop gcc 15.2). Against review single-flight-20261027-042215 under the n16/n17 rule (set before running): 2 differing cells, both ALLOWED, 0 blocking. samples/portable_convert_parse on Rust and on Lua is residual-only (same exit and stdout) and calls lib convert, which moved after the review (radix land 15:40). The accept-2 blocker is gone: mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals [Fsharp] ran within the fixed budget (test.ps1's F# native paths now honour max(60, the job's TimeoutSec), so its matrix Timeouts 180 applies). The Zig coverage slice, the 620-row contracts on Zig and the Lean clauses of this bundle now stand as the oracle. Baseline backup in TEMP matrix-bundle/baseline-before-accept-20261027-173412. Unblocks: closing lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY (spent; bundle.ps1 needs no re-apply now), the CCMR land (it waits on this plus TRAIN), and then the pack-5n install.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_13 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 13u32, "2026-10-27T23:30Z", "main", "10-27 19:30 main: Groom 10-27 19:3x: the experiment in the title is DONE. Every contract compiles to Zig, and the verdicts agree with the F# rows on 620/620 (bundle clause, 10-27), and the Zig contract rows were ADDED and accepted into the oracle (accept-6 GREEN). What remains is the title's own last clause, 'switch the suite or add Zig rows by the result'. Zig rows are added; the switch, making Zig the contracts' only vehicle and dropping the 619 F#-vehicle rows (the user called F# redundant on 10-18), is a technical call by rule. Proposed rule, set now: switch once one more full accept reproduces the 620/620 agreement, since a second agreeing accept shows the Zig vehicle is stable, and dropping the F# rows also cuts their native time (dotnet fsi about 7 s a row under load). Until then both vehicles run.")
inl note_of_contracts_on_zig_14 () = Note ("CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG", 14u32, "2026-10-28T10:29Z", "main", "10-28 06:29 main: DONE (main groom 10-28 06:3x). The switch this item proposed has already happened: the matrix bundle made Zig the contracts suite's vehicle. Three full runs agree exactly: the review single-flight-20261027-042215, accept-6 -173417 and the 5n accept -232226 each have 620 Zig contract rows (212 compile ok, 408 the expected rejections), only 10 F# rows left in the suite, and DISAGREE 0. n13's 'switch after one more agreeing accept' is satisfied twice over, on two compilers (5m' and 5n). The F# vehicle rows are gone, so the 619 F# rows' native time is already saved. The 10 F# rows that remain are not vehicle rows; they test F# itself.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_1 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:35Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:35 main-thread probe with the installed compiler. The 38 C-only samples: abi_external_* (10) and dynamic_array_* (10) carry C shims through CFlags; rust_* (7) and python_macro_annotations are single-backend by design; native_managed_array_scc*_tail_recursion (2) carry C shims. 8 others fail to compile for Zig with 'Cannot find the backend Zig in the backend switch op', because their !!!!BackendSwitch has no Zig arm: lua_paren_statement (C (void)0 / Lua arms; a Zig arm of {} is an empty statement), the six ebpf_* samples (C and Ebpf arms plus a pure-Spiral Bend arm such as expect / 0i32, which Zig can share), and wgsl_parallel_lanes (to check). Plan after today's accept (samples stay frozen during review and accept): add those Zig arms, register the samples in the Zig list, and probe for agreement with C. The shim samples need Zig shims (zig build-exe main.zig plus the C shim, or Zig ports): a separate step.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_2 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 2u32, "2026-10-18T19:12Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:12 main-thread: staged lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY/apply.ps1 (not applied; samples stay frozen until the pack 3 accept). It adds 13 Zig arms in 7 samples, checking that every BackendSwitch gets exactly one: the Bend-arm ebpf samples reuse the Bend value (expect or 0i32), ebpf_map_full maps both map helpers to expect, the assignment helpers in ebpf_map_full and ebpf_nested use the Zig spelling from while_loop, and lua_paren_statement gets unit. It also registers the 7 in the Zig list. The dry run passes. wgsl_parallel_lanes stays out: it is a Wgsl sample whose arms cover C, Fsharp, Rust and Delphi only")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_3 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 3u32, "2026-10-19T03:44Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 23:44 main-thread: applied after the pack 3 accept. Pack 4 review (single-flight-20261018-225858) shows the 7 new Zig rows (ebpf_key, ebpf_map, ebpf_map_full, ebpf_minkey, ebpf_nested, ebpf_unit, lua_paren_statement), all agreeing with C (DISAGREE 0). Pack 4 accept running")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_4 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:33Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:33 helper-lane lap 1: verified the 10-18 23:44 step landed: the accepted oracle (EXPECTED.tsv, 10-19 00:28) has Zig rows ran for ebpf_key, ebpf_map, ebpf_map_full, ebpf_minkey, ebpf_nested, ebpf_unit (exit 0) and lua_paren_statement (exit 7), committed in 668568ac. Recount from that oracle: 35 samples have a C ran row and no Zig row. Of them, 3 are ghosts (native_closure_managed_branch, native_fptr_basic, native_fptr_reuse: oracle rows exit 42, but their sample folders no longer exist (sources deleted on 09-29 in 7a5e4ed4)) and 1 is new and Zig-ready: qualified_union_case_no_payload compiles to Zig with the installed compiler and exits 43 = its C row (probe via Invoke-Heavy). Not edited: matrix.psd1's Zig list (line ~3006) is the main lane's during reviews; adding qualified_union_case_no_payload there is a one-line change for the next pack. Real remainder: 10 abi_external_* and 10 dynamic_array_* C-shim samples, 2 native_managed_array_scc*, plus single-backend rust_* 7, python 1, wgsl 1.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_5 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:18Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:18 helper-lane lap 2: designed the ABI half from the samples themselves: every abi_external_* sample reaches C only through one macro call of a named shim function with plain scalar arguments ($ spiral_abi_libc_abs(x), memset, memcmp x2, llabs, strlen, qsort3_pack(3,1,2), cabs_pack(3,4), div_pack, lldiv_pack; the *_negative/_mismatch/_unknown ones are expected compile errors), and the C rows bind those names with -D or -include of tests/native-shims/abi-external-*-c-shim.h. So no per-sample Zig port of the Spiral code is needed: a Zig row only needs the same names defined in Zig. Mechanism: a ZigShims table in tests/matrix.psd1 (Pattern -> tests/native-shims/abi-external-*.zig) whose file the native tier appends to main.zig before zig build-exe, mirroring CFlags; the shims can call libc through extern fn with zig build-exe -lc, or be pure Zig (abs, llabs, div are one-liners). This is a test.ps1/matrix change (main lane's files), so it is recorded as the plan, not applied.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_6 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:48Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:48 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the scalar half of the Zig ABI shims as pure Zig (no libc) in lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY/shims/, one file per C shim the CFlags table includes: abi-external (abs, wrapping like C on INT_MIN), abi-external-i64 (llabs), callback (qsort3_pack as a 3-element sorting network, 123 for 3,1,2), struct-argument (cabs_pack via @sqrt, @trap where C aborts), struct-return (div_pack via @divTrunc/@rem, C's toward-zero div_t) and struct-return-i64 (lldiv_pack). Probe lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY/shims_probe.zig (all six plus negative div) built with zig build-exe -O ReleaseFast as one bounded job: exit 0, every value equal to the C shim's. Not written: the buffer shims (memset, memcmp, strlen) read ->len/->ptr of C's array and string structs, so their Zig twins must follow the Zig backend's slice layout; next grain. Wiring stays a main-lane change (a ZigShims table beside CFlags that appends the matching file to main.zig).")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_7 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:35Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:35 helper-lane lap 4: wrote the three buffer shims after reading the Zig backend's representation (codegenZig: strings are []const u8, arrays []T from spiralNewArray, length via .len, so a slice already carries the ptr/len pair the C shims read through ->ptr/->len): mutable-buffer memset(array: []u8, byte, count) checks 0 <= count <= len (exit 3 where C aborts), @memset on array[0..count] and returns count; const-buffer memcmp(left, right, count) with the same bound checks returns the sign of std.mem.order (-1/0/1; the samples only test == 0 and the prefix case); string strlen stops at the first NUL like C, else the slice length. shims_probe.zig now also checks memset (writes 3 bytes, leaves the 4th), memcmp (equal 3-byte prefix, ABCD < ABCE) and strlen spiral = 6; rerun after main's freeze all-clear. All 9 Zig shim files exist now; what remains is the test.ps1 ZigShims hook (main's file).")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_8 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:54Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:54 helper-lane lap 5: staged the harness half as lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY/zig_shims_hook.py (anchors --check OK on today's test.ps1 and matrix.psd1; matrix inputs are main's, so not applied): matrix.psd1 gains a ZigShims table (sample name -> shim file, exact-name -like patterns as CFlags uses) and the 9 positive abi_external_* samples on the Zig list; test.ps1 loads it beside cFlagRules (322), passes it into the native runspaces (930), and the Zig clause (712) builds main_shimmed.zig = main.zig + the matching shim when a rule matches, otherwise main.zig as today; the script also copies the 9 shim files into tests/native-shims (already part of the native memo key). HEAVY QUEUE row: apply between runs, then a C,Zig examples review should show 9 new Zig rows agreeing with C.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_9 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 9u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: reran shims_probe.zig with the three buffer shims added (zig build-exe ReleaseFast, bounded): exit 0, so memset, memcmp and strlen behave like the C shims on the probe values, together with the six scalar shims.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_10 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 10u32, "2026-10-22T07:02Z", "main", "03:02 main: status check, nothing applied yet. zig_shims_hook.py's dry run still matches today's tmp ('anchors match'); tests/native-shims has no Zig shim; the 9 shims/*.zig (abi_external variants) passed shims_probe.zig on 10-19 (memset/memcmp/strlen plus 6 scalar shims behave like the C shims). apply.ps1 adds Zig arms to the samples and refuses a sample that already has one. Placement: this moves matrix rows (the abi_external_* samples gain Zig rows), so it belongs in the post-install accept window with CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG and the Lean pin (PACK-AFTER-NIGHT checklist 12, optional): copy shims into tests/native-shims, run zig_shims_hook.py --apply and apply.ps1, and every new Zig row must agree with its C row (DISAGREE 0). Otherwise it waits for its own review.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_11 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 11u32, "2026-10-22T22:11Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:11 main: half landed already. All 7 apply.ps1 samples (ebpf_key, ebpf_map, ebpf_minkey, ebpf_unit, ebpf_map_full, ebpf_nested, lua_paren_statement) have Zig arms and are on the matrix Zig list, so lab apply.ps1 is deleted. Open: the abi_external Zig shims (zig_shims_hook.py, 9 shims, still dry-run clean). They ride the post-install matrix bundle %TEMP%\\matrix-bundle\\bundle.ps1 with CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG's switch and LEAN-BACKEND's v4.35.0 pin (all dry-run clean today), applied after PACK-NEXT-3b is accepted and installed, with the rule printed by the script.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_12 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 12u32, "2026-10-24T00:22Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:22 main: status fixed (staged / half landed). %TEMP%\\matrix-bundle\\bundle.ps1 is still dry-run clean on today's tree (all anchors match; Lean 3 pins). It applies after the next4/5f composite, which carries next3b, is reviewed and installed (scratch next-session.md step 7).")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_13 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 13u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 6 13:42 (subagent): all 9 shims PASS in shims_probe -> next: queued ZigShims hook review | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: zig_shims_hook.py staged; matrix input -> next: main's pack slot")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_14 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 14u32, "2026-10-27T08:17Z", "main", "10-27 04:17 main: 10-27 04:17 main groom: fresh status (stale since the 10-19 laps). The 10-22 post-install matrix bundle (TEMP matrix-bundle/bundle.ps1) was NEVER APPLIED, although its stated condition (apply after PACK-NEXT-3b is accepted and installed) has been met for days (5n and later packs installed since). A dry run now: all anchors still match, for ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY (Zig shims for the abi_external samples), CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG (contract rows move from Fsharp to Zig), and LEAN-BACKEND (pin v4.35.0-rc4 -> v4.35.0 stable, 3 pins in apps/cells/README.md). Not applied tonight on purpose: it changes which rows the matrix runs, so it must not land in the middle of the compiler lane's review or accept, and the Lean pin needs the stable toolchain installed first (otherwise every Lean row fails). Offered to the compiler lane, which runs the matrices; it lands right after their current review, then the next matrix run proves it.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_15 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 15u32, "2026-10-27T09:25Z", "main", "10-27 05:25 main: 10-27 05:25 main: Matrix-bundle review single-flight-20261027-042215 (-Suite all -Native -ReuseNative, 7,367 rows): DISAGREE 0 under both oracles; the one agree-fail is the long-known dynamic_array_bounds_negative Rust row (known vs C). This item's clause is GREEN: the 9 new abi_external Zig rows compile and run, none replayed (0 of 9 native-reused, so this run really executed them), and their C rows agree with the Zig oracle (9 agree). The 10th abi_external C row, abi_external_buffer_length_negative, is no-zig by design: a negative C-ABI fixture without a Zig shim. Still open: the bundle's -Accept -AcceptToolchain run, which waits on the LEAN-BACKEND speed clause.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_16 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 16u32, "2026-10-27T16:19Z", "main", "10-27 12:19 main: Bundle accept-2 (job-1793103041735, run single-flight-20261027-100519) RED by its prewritten rule (the accept must reproduce review single-flight-20261027-042215 row for row); the baseline was restored, 5 files hash-equal, so the oracle is unchanged. 7,367 rows both sides, DISAGREE 0, 2 differing cells, both traced: (1) samples/mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals [Fsharp] exit 1 -> timeout. Same generated code (ce261af71adfd449). The review's native run took 47.7 s; the accept's took 60.5 s and hit a cap that is HARD-CODED to 60 s for both F# paths in test.ps1 (warm session line 821 '-TimeoutSec 60', cold 'dotnet fsi' fallback line 529 '... $binDir 60'), so the per-sample matrix.psd1 Timeouts never reach the F# native run. The box carried kit's SLICES2, the E1 bench and other heavy jobs. It is the only F# timeout in either run (387 F# rows each). (2) samples/portable_convert_parse [Lua] residual 477728af -> 6f26afb0 with identical exit (0) and stdout. The sample calls convert.convert_i32_base / convert_i32 / try_convert, and lib convert.spi was rewritten at 09:05, inside the review (04:22) to accept (10:05) gap; 11 lib files moved in that gap. Prewritten rule for accept-3, set BEFORE it runs: GREEN iff test.ps1 exits 0 and every cell that differs from review 042215 is a residual-only cell (identical exit and stdout) on a sample whose main.spi names a lib module (module.) whose lib .spi was written after the review run started; any other differing cell (exit, stdout, compile, native, oracle, missing or new rows) is RED. (1) is not excused but fixed: the F# native run honours max(60, the job's own timeout), and matrix.psd1 Timeouts gets 'samples/mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals' = 180. That changes no other row: no other F# row timed out in either run. accept-3 runs with native memo replay (-ReuseNative), as the review did; timeout results are never memoised (test.ps1:995), so the omniledger row runs fresh.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_17 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 17u32, "2026-10-27T16:29Z", "main", "10-27 12:29 main: Correction to n16, and accept-3 queued. matrix.psd1 already had 'samples/mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals' = 180 (line 80), and its platform note (line 53) records that the row 'sits near the 60 s fsi budget'. My edit added a duplicate key, which would have stopped every test.ps1 run from loading the matrix. I caught it on the load check right after writing, restored the file from the backup (hash-equal) and confirmed it loads with test.ps1's own loader (Import-PowerShellDataFile -SkipLimitCheck): 22 Timeouts entries, all_terminals = 180. So the matrix needs no change, and the whole fix is test.ps1 (unstaged; backup TEMP matrix-bundle/pre-fsharp-timeout-122135): the cold F# run at line 529 now uses max(60, the job's TimeoutSec), and the warm session at line 821 uses max(60, the chunk's largest TimeoutSec). warm.ps1 sets its deadline per script (line 56), so this only lengthens the wait for a script that would otherwise be cut off. n16's rule is now code: TEMP matrix-bundle/compare-accept.ps1, which accept.ps1 calls; it prints BLOCKING <n>. Positive control (accept-2 vs review): the omniledger exit cell BLOCKS and the portable_convert_parse Lua residual is allowed (it calls lib convert), BLOCKING 1. Negative control (the review against itself): 0 cells. accept-3 queued: job-1793118505034 (heavy, 12 h cap counting the queue, -ReuseNative).")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_18 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 18u32, "2026-10-27T20:27Z", "main", "10-27 16:27 main: accept-3 VOID by my error (job-1793118505034, 16:21): it passed -ReuseNative, and test.ps1:43 refuses -ReuseNative with -Accept by design ('an accept runs every native row'; apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md says -Accept and -Record refuse it, so the accept still runs every row and must reproduce a -ReuseNative REVIEW). n17's plan to 'run accept-3 with native memo replay, as the review did' was wrong: replay is for reviews, never accepts. The baseline was restored, 5 files hash-equal; nothing changed. accept.ps1 now refuses -ReuseNative itself with a VOID that names the reason. Re-queued as accept-5 (job-1793132732864), without the memo, held behind kit-release-TRAIN (--after), after accept-4 took the freed slot before TRAIN was queued and was stopped at 74 s (baseline again hash-equal to its backup). The n16/n17 rule stands unchanged: GREEN iff test.ps1 exits 0 and every cell that differs from review 042215 is residual-only on a sample that calls a lib module written after the review started. Note for the rule: TRAIN's window and today's lands (convert radix 15:40, fiscal's 8 modules 16:04) move more lib modules, so more residual-only cells may appear; the rule allows exactly those and nothing else.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_19 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 19u32, "2026-10-27T23:20Z", "main", "10-27 19:20 main: Bundle accept GREEN (accept-6, job-1793136844465, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 17:34 to 19:1x; ran in slot 2 against the TRAIN-frozen lib). test.ps1 exit 0; 7,367 rows accepted into spiral-bin baseline EXPECTED.tsv, with toolchain.tsv recorded (SPIRAL_CC pinned to scoop gcc 15.2). Against review single-flight-20261027-042215 under the n16/n17 rule (set before running): 2 differing cells, both ALLOWED, 0 blocking. samples/portable_convert_parse on Rust and on Lua is residual-only (same exit and stdout) and calls lib convert, which moved after the review (radix land 15:40). The accept-2 blocker is gone: mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals [Fsharp] ran within the fixed budget (test.ps1's F# native paths now honour max(60, the job's TimeoutSec), so its matrix Timeouts 180 applies). The Zig coverage slice, the 620-row contracts on Zig and the Lean clauses of this bundle now stand as the oracle. Baseline backup in TEMP matrix-bundle/baseline-before-accept-20261027-173412. Unblocks: closing lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY (spent; bundle.ps1 needs no re-apply now), the CCMR land (it waits on this plus TRAIN), and then the pack-5n install.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_20 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 20u32, "2026-10-27T23:32Z", "main", "10-27 19:32 main: Groom 10-27 19:3x, counted from the accepted oracle (accept-6 run single-flight-20261027-173417): 68 samples have a C row and no Zig row. Against the title's 38-sample split: ABI shims, the 9 positive abi_external samples, now have Zig rows (accepted). The 6 still C-only are negatives (abi_external_buffer_length_negative, _buffer_ownership_negative, _callback_dynamic_negative, _signature_mismatch, _struct_return_i64_signature_mismatch, ...) whose point is the C ABI rejection, so they stay C-only by design. Dynamic-array helpers: all 10 still C-only (dynamic_array_geometric_growth, _growth_union_managed, _growth_union_nested, _reserve_zero, _resize_alias, _resize_nested, ...). That's the open work: Zig counterparts of their C shims, as the title says. Rust-only 7 and eBPF stay single-backend by design. The other 45 C-only samples are outside this item's split (compile-error negatives such as assign_primitive_wrong_type and conversation_negative_*, plus delphi_* samples). Next slice: one dynamic-array helper shim in Zig (the growth/reserve family shares one C helper), proven on its samples against C, then the other 9.")
inl note_of_zig_coverage_c_only_21 () = Note ("ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY", 21u32, "2026-10-28T10:57Z", "main", "10-28 06:57 main: CORRECTION to n20 and DONE (main, 10-28 07:0x). n20's 'next slice: a Zig shim for the dynamic-array helpers' was wrong. Those samples call $'DynamicArrayResize0(!xs, n)' as host text, and the C shim implements it on C's shared array header (SpiralPortableArrayHeaderV26: refc and len). dynamic_array_resize_alias depends on it: resizing an alias must change xs's length (expected 4 + 7 + 4 - 15 = 0). The Zig backend's arrays are by-value slices ([]T, spiralNewArray), so a Zig function given a slice cannot change the caller's or an alias's length, and no shim can reproduce the semantics. PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES n16 (10-19) already recorded this: these samples test a C-runtime ABI experiment, and a portable version needs a growable-array type in core (capacity separate from length, resize keeping aliases valid). That's a language feature, so the samples are C-only until it exists. By this item's own title ('Zig rows where the feature exists') the scope is complete: the 9 positive ABI samples have accepted Zig rows; the 6 ABI negatives (C's ABI rejection), 7 Rust-only and the eBPF samples are single-backend by design; and the 10 dynamic-array samples wait on the growable-array feature (PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES, Paused), not on a shim.")
inl note_of_kit_core_operators_1 () = Note ("KIT-CORE-OPERATORS", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-CORE-OPERATORS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_1 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 1u32, "2026-10-18T18:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:42 main-thread design. The VS Code extension has no TextMate grammar: its colors are the language server's semantic tokens. The compiler's tokenizer tags every token with a SemanticTokenLegend class (variable, symbol, string, number, operator, unary_operator, comment, keyword, parenthesis, type_variable, escaped_char, unescaped_char, number_suffix, escaped_var; spiral_compiler.fs ~42869, semantic_tokens ~49363), and the format verb already reads those tokens without partial evaluation. Plan: (1) a host verb 'SpiralCompiler tokens <file|--stdin --path p>' printing one row per token (line, column, length, class), tokenizer only and cheap, next to 'format' in Program.fs; (2) a Cells HTML writer (Spiral.Cells.Html) that renders the report directly: markdown cells (the CommonMark subset from DIR-TREE-README), code cells colored by a CSS class per legend entry with a dark palette, outputs and test verdicts, plus the mobile viewport CSS. It replaces 'jupyter nbconvert' in Notebook.write_html, removes the jupyter/Python dependency from every repo's CI, and colors exactly as the editor does. The tokens verb is a compiler change: build and install it after today's 17:00 accept.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_2 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 2u32, "2026-10-18T18:45Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:45 main-thread: step (1) written, unbuilt. TokensCli in the shared Formatter section folds tokenize_line over a file's lines (lexical state carried across lines) and prints one row per token: line, from, nearTo, then the token_groups class, the same mapping vscode_tokens uses for the editor. Host verb: SpiralCompiler tokens <file> | tokens --stdin (Program.fs, next to format). Builds and installs after today's 17:00 accept; then step (2), the Cells HTML writer.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_3 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 3u32, "2026-10-18T18:52Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 14:52 main-thread: step (2) done, uncommitted. Spiral.Cells.Html renders a page from the .ipynb JSON: markdown cells (CommonMark subset), code cells colored by token class (one CSS class per SemanticTokenLegend name, dark palette), outputs, and a test badge per test cell, with the viewport and narrow-screen CSS. Notebook.write_html calls it instead of jupyter nbconvert; normalize_html and its test (nbconvert cell-id renumbering) are removed. The tokenizer calls SpiralCompiler tokens <tmp file> and degrades to plain text when the installed compiler lacks the verb (today's install). Tests: html_test.exs 4/4, notebook_test.exs passes (18 in all). End to end: mix spiral.notebook on lib/spiral/testing.spi --output-dir --livemd writes testing.spi.html (13 KB, viewport, 6 test badges, 25 code blocks), .ipynb and .livemd, no Python involved. Colors appear once the tokens verb is installed after the 17:00 accept. This also fixes the tibia lane's failure today: scoop python's pyzmq raises 'cannot import name _device', and nbconvert exited 1 on every run.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_4 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 4u32, "2026-10-18T19:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:26 main-thread: the last jupyter path is gone. core.ps1 Invoke-PwshNotebook (dice/alphabet/i574n.github PowerShell .livemd notebooks) now calls the new mix spiral.html <ipynb>... task from apps/cells, with scoop OTP/Elixir prepended like run_notebook.ps1, instead of jupyter nbconvert. Html.write colors only notebooks whose kernelspec language is spiral (or missing), so PowerShell cells stay plain. spiral/requirements.txt drops nbconvert, ipython_genutils and mistune (mpmath and cupy stay for the Python backend). init.ps1's no-pip message and the 3 gh-pages.yml comments no longer mention jupyter. polyglot's own requirements.txt is untouched (fc1943s backup repo). html_test.exs 6/6, including a pwsh notebook through the task")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_5 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 5u32, "2026-10-18T19:36Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 15:36 main-thread: review of the committed, unbuilt TokensCli against the renderer: tokenize_line ranges are .NET string offsets (UTF-16 units), while Html.highlight_line sliced bytes, so any non-ASCII text before a token (an accented string, an emoji) would shift every later span, or cut inside a character. Fixed before the first colored build: the line is converted to UTF-16, sliced by unit and converted back, and a slice that splits a surrogate pair gives an empty string. New test with é and an emoji in strings: html_test.exs 7/7")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_6 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:16 lap 1 grain: CI regression from my nbconvert replacement: core.ps1 Invoke-PwshNotebook ran mix spiral.html before mix deps.get on fresh runners (Unchecked dependencies: kino) in dice, polyglot and alphabet; it now runs mix local.hex, local.rebar and deps.get when apps/cells/deps/kino is missing, like run_notebook.ps1 Next: confirm the html appears in the next CI run")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_7 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 7u32, "2026-10-19T13:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:21 helper-lane lap 2: CI confirmation still pending: dice, polyglot and alphabet have no run since main's 08:16 core.ps1 fix (latest runs 06:48, all red; spiral's in-progress run is a rerun of 668568ac, which predates the fix), so the check waits for the user's push. Meanwhile closed the title's last clause (drop the jupyter dependency from every repo): a grep of all 9 repos for jupyter/nbconvert/ipykernel left only polyglot/requirements.txt (nbconvert>=7.16.6, ipython_genutils, mistune==3.0.2, installed by polyglot scripts/init.ps1 pip install -r and the devcontainer Dockerfile); spiral's requirements.txt already had only mpmath and cupy-cuda12x. Removed those three lines in polyglot (uncommitted), so it now matches spiral. The remaining jupyter_lines hits in Cells notebook.ex are the .ipynb JSON writer, not the tool.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_8 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 8u32, "2026-10-19T22:05Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:05 helper-lane lap 6: CI recheck (gh run list, read-only): spiral's latest run is still 13:15Z on 668568ac, which predates the core.ps1 fix and failed in workflow.ps1 -Compiler -Suite all. dice, polyglot and alphabet have had no run since 06:48Z. So the CI confirmation still waits for the user's next push, and nothing new can be checked locally.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_9 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 9u32, "2026-10-21T22:42Z", "main", "18:41 CI confirmation found (read-only): i574n/polyglot gh-pages run 39357432363 (10-19 22:10, after the core.ps1 fix) is green and renders notebooks with mix spiral.html; its 1.17 MB log mentions jupyter or nbconvert 0 times. dice's run of the same push is green too. That was the last open condition (the renderer is ours and every repo's jupyter dependency is gone, note 10-19 09:21), so closing. spiral and alphabet are red for unrelated reasons (PIPELINES-GREEN notes 16-20: the Linux F# warm exit mask, and the Zig-only array typing).")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_10 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 10u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 3 10:49 (subagent): blocked, rechecked: dice's latest CI run is still 06:48 (failure), no push since main's core.ps1 fix; html publish path verified (core.ps1:384 mix spiral.html, publish-tree includes *.html) -> next: unblocks on the user's push of dice/polyglot/alphabet: gh run list, then the notebook .html files on gh-pages -> Done | lap 4 11:35 (subagent): blocked: user push of dice/polyglot/alphabet (CI still at the red 06:48 runs) -> next: gh run list after the push; notebook .html on gh-pages -> Done | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): user push pending -> next: gh-pages html -> Done | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: no CI run since 13:15Z -> next: the user's push")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_11 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 11u32, "2026-10-25T05:00Z", "main", "2026-10-25 01:00 main: REOPENED (user 10-25 00:5x: \"put in the backlog so the html generated by cells gets colored\"). The renderer exists (Html.render: palette, per-class CSS, UTF-16-correct spans) but real output is NOT colored. tibia/notebooks/*.spi.html written 10-25 00:19-00:24 (e.g. rng.spi.html, 9 code cells, kernelspec language \"spiral\") carry the token CSS and pre.code blocks, yet 0 tok- spans. So the tokenizer returned [] and the page fell back to plain text with no error. The compiler side works: the installed SpiralCompiler \"tokens <file>\" exits 0 and classifies edition-3 := as keyword. Suspects: compiler_tokenizer resolving no dotnet or DLL in that run (fn _ -> []), or tokens exiting non-zero ({_, code} -> []), e.g. under a candidate SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL. Plan: (1) make the fallback loud: a stderr warning naming the dotnet/DLL it tried and the exit code, plus a count of colored cells in the run report; (2) reproduce on one .spi notebook with the Cells environment the run used, and fix the cause; (3) an ExUnit test that renders a spiral cell and asserts tok- spans > 0, red on today's code; (4) check the published gh-pages notebooks too, since CI may hit the same fallback. Done when a Cells-generated .spi.html shows colored Spiral code, symbol spellings included.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_12 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 12u32, "2026-10-25T06:40Z", "main", "2026-10-25 02:40 main: no reproduction today. Both dotnets Cells can pick (the cache-bundled one and PATH's) run the installed 'tokens' fine, and tibia's own rng.spi.ipynb cells tokenize (cell 3: 162 rows). So the 00:2x failure was in that run's environment (a candidate SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL left set is the main suspect), which can't be recovered after the fact. Step 1 drafted: %TEMP%\\cells-html-loud\\apply.ps1 (2 anchors, dry run OK). A tokens failure prints the dotnet, the DLL, the exit code and its output, and a missing toolchain prints what was tried; both go to stderr. Applied with the other Cells slices once no Cells run reads the tree; then the next run names the cause.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_13 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 13u32, "2026-10-29T21:54Z", "main", "10-29 17:5x main: PROVISIONAL estimate 150m by the D-base rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS n2: base(D5) x (1 - 70%), floored at 30, rounded up to 15). A milestone-based sizing pass (estimates-from-rules) replaces it.")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_14 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 14u32, "2026-10-31T22:57Z", "main", "10-31 18:56 main, pre-night groom: Active -> PLANNED (not Paused: no note in over 48 h and no live job, so it isn't work in progress, but nothing blocks it; it stays in agile next at its priority and progress for the night run to pull).")
inl note_of_notebook_html_renderer_15 () = Note ("NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", 15u32, "2026-11-06T12:06Z", "main", "main 11-06 09:3x DONE, proof: nbconvert/jupyter appear in 0 tracked files of the 11 own repos (git grep -i; the only hits are the backlog graph outputs quoting this item). Cells' own apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/html.ex renders the .spi.html pages: Spiral tokens colored through the compiler tokenizer (tok-* palette), markdown cells, stream and text/plain outputs, test badges, mobile viewport plus @media rules; the live physics.spi.html is produced by it (green in spiral CI). The one gap found today, display_data outputs (SVG plots) being dropped, is fixed in the CELLS-PLOT-EXPORT draft (html.ex image/svg+xml clause plus test) and tracked there.")
inl note_of_kit_job_stop_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-STOP", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:31Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-JOB-STOP (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_1 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 1u32, "2026-10-19T14:36Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:36 found by helper-lane-2 while sizing MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER's shared goal (substitution and renaming under binders in lean_cic, spiral_proves, zeta). Repros in spiral/lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX (installed single-flight compiler, try.ps1 -Backend C, bounded): debruijn_rename.spi, a well-scoped de Bruijn term union rec term n (Var of var n, Lam of term (succ n), App) with inl rec rename/lift and inl rec size over a static term, compiles and exits 5 (correct size after weakening). debruijn_size_runtime.spi, the same term made runtime (inl ~t) and size a let rec join point: TIMEOUT after 45 s (compiler killed; 60 s for the rename variant). gadt_same_index_runtime.spi, a let rec join point over a runtime union rec tree n whose recursion keeps the index: compiles, exits 3. So the hang needs both a runtime value and an index that grows in the recursive call; it looks like join-point specialization per index instantiation without a bound (succ n, succ (succ n), ...), although the index nominals are phantom (nominal succ n = ()) and the runtime layout is identical for every n. Expected: one join point for all n (erase phantom indices for specialization), or a fast EJP-style error naming the polymorphic recursion. Related but distinct: GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG (Done, the case-field type growth in ty_is_uninhabited). Owner: compiler lane (main)")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_2 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 2u32, "2026-10-19T14:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:39 lap 2 grain: triage: the hang is unbounded specialization, not a wrong result. size is a let rec join point; join points specialize per argument type, and every recursive call on term (succ n) has a new type, so the specialization set never closes (the fixed-index runtime case compiles because its key repeats); rename is an inl rec over a runtime ~t, which inlines without end. The compiler bug is the missing guard: partial evaluation should stop like EJP0040 does for dynamic ifs, by counting distinct specializations of one join-point body (or index-type nesting depth) and reporting the growing index with a hint (erase the index at runtime through an existential such as a term_dyn wrapper, or keep the recursion static). The runtime remedy for the lean_cic, spiral_proves and zeta roots is that erasure, independent of the guard Next: find where single-flight memoizes join-point specializations (join point dictionary keyed by body + argument types) and add a per-body distinct-key counter with an EJP report, as a candidate patch in lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_3 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:42 patch design located in single-flight PartEval (spiral_compiler.fs 158355-161335): (1) EUnbox's runtime case body (159931 run s a, and Unbox's 159962) is not wrapped in within_dynamic_if (159488, EJP0040, only if_'s two branches are, 159519-159520), so an inl rec recursing through a runtime match never reaches the nesting limit; wrap those calls so EJP0040 counts union-match branches too. (2) EJoinPoint' (159727-159764): dict is the per-(backend, body) specialization table; at the Owner branch add a distinct-key cap (SPIRAL_JP_SPECIALIZATION_LIMIT, default 1000) that raises RunawayUnrollDiagnostic.render with a new code and the hint erase the growing index at runtime (existential) or keep the recursion static. Constraint: PartEval is a SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL pair, and pair-drift.ps1 fails a build whose normal pair changed on one side only; routing the calls through PartEvalSeams would pass the check but spend hopacsota's G6 seam budget on a non-seam. So the patch either mirrors both guards on gpt5parallel's side (it already has jp_method_specialization_budget_exhausted at 41659) or waits for gpt5parallel's retirement (HOPACSOTA-P6).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_4 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:45 analysis note (helper-lane-2, for the compiler lane): why the runtime form cannot terminate today. A runtime value needs a residual layout for its type, and residual unions are monomorphized per type arguments; the type term zero has a Lam case whose field is term (succ zero), whose Lam field is term (succ (succ zero)), and so on, so the layout of term zero is an infinite family of union types. That is the same type-level growth GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG met (its fix capped nesting in ty_is_uninhabited for the inhabitedness check), but a runtime value additionally forces the layouts and a join point per instantiation, which the cap does not cover; the static repro avoids it because partial evaluation consumes the term before any layout exists. Two directions: (a) index erasure for runtime layouts when every index argument is a phantom nominal (nominal succ n = ()): one residual union per GADT head, cases tagged as usual, index checks already done by the type checker; (b) a bounded detection that reports EJP-style polymorphic recursion through a growing phantom index, naming the union and the field. (b) is the cheap correctness fix (no hang), (a) is what the mega roots' runtime substitution needs")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_5 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 5u32, "2026-10-19T18:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:39 lap 4 grain: helper lane 2 analysis (10:45) explains the non-termination: a runtime value needs a residual layout per type argument, and term zero has a Lam field of term (succ zero) whose Lam field is term (succ (succ zero)), so monomorphization never closes; the guard (cap distinct index instantiations per union) belongs in the PartEval pair, blocked by pair-drift until gpt5parallel retires Next: none until HOPACSOTA-P6, unless the guard fits a shared pre-PartEval section")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_6 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 6u32, "2026-10-21T22:23Z", "main", "18:23 code read (no compile): the non-termination is the eager layout recursion at PartEval's TUnion arm, single-flight spiral_compiler.fs:159909 (gpt5parallel's twin at 151407). Building YUnion maps every case payload through ty s v, and for term zero the Lam payload is term (succ zero), a new type at every level, so ty recurses through TUnion forever (recursive unions with fixed arguments terminate only because their instantiation repeats). Cheapest correct guard, mirrored on both sides of the pair, so pair-drift passes without spending a hopacsota seam: a union-nesting depth counter around the payload mapping at TUnion. On single-flight a plain ref local to the PartEval closure is enough, since its ty runs on one thread; on gpt5parallel the twin needs the depth carried in its environment record, because its ty runs on Hopac workers. Above SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT (default 256), raise a RunawayUnrollDiagnostic-style error naming the union, the case and the last two instantiations, with the hint from helper-lane-2 note 2 (erase the phantom index at runtime with an existential, or keep the recursion static). Unblocked enough to draft as lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX/union-depth.ps1 with both anchors; gate: the static repro unchanged, the runtime repro errors in < 5 s instead of hanging, and -Suite all DISAGREE 0.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_7 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 7u32, "2026-10-21T22:31Z", "main", "18:31 guard drafted: lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX/union-depth.ps1, 4 anchors, dry run matches each once; pair-drift on the patched copy is clean (both PartEval sides changed). Each side gets a UnionNesting module beside its DynamicIfNesting (SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT, default 256, ThreadLocal depth; ty recursion is synchronous per thread on both cores). At the TUnion arm, each case payload's ty s v runs inside the counter, and past the limit it raises EJP0041 through RunawayUnrollDiagnostic.render with the source frames, a headline naming the union case and the hint to keep the recursion static or erase the index with an existential. single-flight raises with raise_type_error and an empty trace, gpt5parallel with partEvalTypeError [], each as its EJP0040 does. Gate, fixed now: the lab repros debruijn_size_runtime.spi and debruijn_rename_runtime.spi end in EJP0041 within 30 s instead of hanging; gadt_same_index_runtime.spi and the static repros compile as before; -Suite all shows DISAGREE 0 with no residual change (256 levels is far above any legitimate union nesting in the samples, to be confirmed by the review). Follow-up: register EJP0041 in DiagnosticClassifier beside EJP0040 (7453/7480/7638/7661/7938) so a stall report classifies it. Rides the next pack.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_8 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 8u32, "2026-10-21T22:34Z", "main", "18:34 the classifier follow-up is now in the same patch: union-depth.ps1 has 9 anchors (EJP0041 literal, DiagnosticEJP0041 case, both code mappings, a classifyIngress branch as DiagnosticRunawayInlineUnroll + DiagnosticTerminalSemantic, then the 2 modules and 2 TUnion guards). Dry run matches each once; pair-drift on the patched copy is clean. Any other exhaustive match over DiagnosticCodeId that needs the new case shows up in the candidate build.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_9 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 9u32, "2026-10-22T00:24Z", "main", "20:24 pre-build check of the open question in note 18:34 (other exhaustive matches over DiagnosticCodeId): grep finds exactly 4 EJP0040 sites in spiral_compiler.fs (case 7480, code mapping 7638, text mapping 7661, classifyIngress 7938-7939) and union-depth.ps1 covers each with its EJP0041 twin, so no further match should break the candidate build; outside the core EJP0040 appears only in prose (FRONTIER.md, hopacsota PLAN.md, a mega sample README, NEXT.md, tmp/AGENTS.md:98). New companion lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX/union-depth-doc.ps1 (dry-run, anchor unique) documents SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT and EJP0041 next to SPIRAL_IF_NESTING_LIMIT/EJP0040 in tmp/AGENTS.md; apply with the pack.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_10 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 10u32, "2026-10-22T06:05Z", "main", "02:05 main: union-depth.ps1 is fully in the PACK-AFTER-NIGHT core (pack-next carries it). All 5 EJP0041 blocks and both union-payload layout sites are present verbatim. The 2 counter blocks hold a __MODULE__ placeholder the script fills at run time, and module UnionNesting with SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT is at pack core 137748, used at 151463-151468. So the guard installs with that pack. At install: apply union-depth-doc.ps1 to tmp/AGENTS.md, then drain the folder. The debruijn_*/gadt_* repros: the hang case becomes an EJP0041 rejection, and adding it as a negative matrix row (ExpectedErrors pin) is the one regression sample this fix gets, after install.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_11 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 11u32, "2026-10-22T18:52Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:52 main: install steps of the 02:05 rule applied. (1) union-depth-doc.ps1 applied to apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md: SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT (default 256, checked in both cores at 137748 and 159661) and EJP0041 are documented beside EJP0040, as an unstaged edit over the user's staged file. (2) The one regression sample: samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime = debruijn_size_runtime.spi (a runtime term n with let rec size, the former 45 s hang), pinned in matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors with Contains 'has no finite layout' (UnionNesting.headline). Its first row comes from PACK-NEXT-3's review, and that row is evidence for this item, not for the pack: expected = a compile error containing the pin within the 20 s timeout. A timeout or a different error reopens the item with that text. Lab drains after that row: the other 3 repros (static rename exit 5, same-index runtime) are recorded in notes 1-2.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_12 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 12u32, "2026-10-23T07:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 03:11 main: lab drain: union-depth.ps1 and union-depth-doc.ps1 deleted, both landed (tree core has EJP0041 at 7455 and SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT in 4 places; apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md documents the limit). No script reads them (by-stem search of spiral *.ps1 with a positive control; only two scratchpad pack scripts of installed packs name them). The 4 .spi probes stay as reference for the open scope (one join point shared across phantom indices); the regression sample gadt_growing_index_runtime is an adapted copy of debruijn_size_runtime, not byte-equal, so that probe stays too.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_13 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 13u32, "2026-10-23T18:06Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:06 main: REOPENED by the 10-22 14:52 rule (a timeout reopens the item). The regression sample's first real rows (next4 review-2, run single-flight-20261023-135519): samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime times out at 20 s on C, Rust and Delphi ('no result within 20000 ms', one worker recycle each), so EJP0041's union-nesting cap does not catch this runtime-term shape (let rec size over a runtime term n), which is the former 45 s hang. next3b's earlier rows were a different error (a type error, from an older edit of the sample, which changed at 10-22 19:24). Per the rule this is evidence for this item, not for PACK-NEXT-4's verdict. Next: find where the runtime case grows (the join point for size specialized per index) with a bounded probe, then a cap there with the 'has no finite layout' headline.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_14 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 14u32, "2026-10-23T18:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:11 main: bounded probe (installed single-flight compiler, --backend C, 120 s bound, compile window): no output and no EJP0041 within 120 s, killed. Depth 256 cannot take that long, so the growth is NOT inside one union layout (where UnionNesting counts nested ty calls): each step is a new join-point specialization of size at term (succ^k n), laid out one level at a time, so the union depth never passes 1. Fix direction, rule written before drafting: cap the type depth of a join point's specialization key (the nesting of nominal applications like succ (succ ...)) at SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT and raise EJP0041 with the existing 'has no finite layout' headline there; adopt only if the sample then fails within 20 s with that text and the matrix shows no other row change.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_15 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 15u32, "2026-10-23T18:20Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:20 main: cap drafted per the rule above: %TEMP%\\pack-next9\\gadt-index-cap.ps1, in pack-next9 (core 2741D35BD11FF46B). Both UnionNesting modules (the pair stays symmetric; pair-drift 0 one-sided) gain applicationDepth (YApply nesting), tooDeep and indexHeadline ('specializing a join point at a type index nested more than N levels: ... has no finite layout', so the matrix.psd1 pin still matches). Each core's EJoinPoint' case checks its env_global_type before claiming the key and raises EJP0041 with the existing hint (SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT). TJoinPoint' (type-level join points) is left alone. Assumption to verify: the growing index reaches env_global_type (size's join point is built under its forall n); if it only travels in the argument's type, the review row stays a timeout and the check moves to the argument types. next9's review.ps1 prints the sample's rows with pinned=True/False; adopt per the rule (fails within 20 s with the pin, no other row change). The next9 Debug type-check build queued behind the 5f build covers the new code.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_16 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 16u32, "2026-10-23T19:27Z", "main", "2026-10-23 15:27 main: the cap WORKS. next9's Debug type-check build (core 1AF7692BCABBEEB8, build succeeded in 410 s) compiles samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime --backend C to 'error[EJP0041]: specializing a join point at a type index nested more than 256 levels: a type index that grows at every level (term n -> term (succ n)) has no finite layout', exit 5 after 10.9 s (Debug; Release is faster), against the installed compiler's 120 s with no result. So the growing index does reach env_global_type, and the matrix.psd1 pin ('has no finite layout') matches. Remaining per the rule: next9's review must show every backend row failing with the pin within 20 s and no other row change; it rides review-3's stack (next9 after next8).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_17 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 17u32, "2026-10-23T21:45Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:45 main: every backend row now ends in the pinned EJP0041 on the next9 Debug build: C 10.9 s, Delphi 12.7 s, F# 10.8 s, Rust 21.5 s (exit 5, 'has no finite layout' in each). Rust is 1.5 s over the 20 s row timeout on a DEBUG build with cells-pack's suite running beside it; Release is faster, so the rule's 'within 20 s' is decided by review-3's Release rows, not by this probe. If Rust's Release row still times out, the cause is the depth (256 levels): the next lever is checking the specialization depth incrementally instead of recomputing applicationDepth per join point.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_18 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 18u32, "2026-10-24T03:49Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:49 main, corrected 01:19: samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime still hangs on the installed single-flight (B553EF6B78E6EE29) and on the 5f candidate: both TIME OUT at 300 s (probe job-1792813100262). The reason is that the cap (pack-next9 gadt-index-cap.ps1, proven on a Debug build at 15:27/17:45) was never part of 5f, so no bisect is needed. It now rides pack 5g (compose adds it; review-4 job-1792813775220), where the rule of note 15 decides: every backend row fails with the pin 'has no finite layout' within 20 s, and no other row changes.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_19 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 19u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 10:45 (subagent2): analysis for main: runtime layout of term zero is an infinite union family (monomorphized per index); fix options: phantom-index erasure (a) or bounded polymorphic-recursion error (b) -> next: main's lane")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_20 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 20u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_21 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 21u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_22 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 22u32, "2026-10-31T20:01Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:01 compiler GROOM (user direction via /btw, 10-31 15:4x): the self-hosted rewrite (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) starts only after EVERY backend is SOTA: no memory problems, no Rc slowdowns, no union hacks, no recursion limits, no missing arithmetic; GADT bugs and conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'; edition 3 finishes first; Gleam is a top test target (abyssal codebases) with F#, Rust and Wasm; Cells as fast as F# projects. This item is now on that gate (priority -> P0, status -> Active).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_23 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 23u32, "2026-10-31T20:05Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:05 compiler (groom): user (10-31 15:4x): 'we shouldn't have gadt bugs ... by now, unacceptable'. Stays P0 and is a gate for SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_24 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 24u32, "2026-10-31T20:33Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:33 compiler: STATE on the installed PACK-10 compiler: samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime no longer hangs. --backend C ends in 'error[EJP0041]: specializing a join point at a type index nested more than 256 levels ... has no finite layout' (main.spi:23:18, size's join point), exit 5 after 18.2 s on a loaded box. So the 10-23 cap shipped, and it is a STOPGAP: the program is valid (a de Bruijn-indexed lambda term; size should return 6 for 'Lam (App (Var VZ, Lam (Var (VS VZ))))'), and per the user (10-31) a GADT program that should compile but errors is a bug, unacceptable now. THE FIX (option (a) of the 10-19 analysis): PHANTOM-INDEX ERASURE. The index n is phantom: zero/succ are ':!' unit nominals, and no case of var n / term n stores a value whose representation depends on n (it only reappears as the index of the same family: var (succ n), term (succ n)). So every instantiation term zero, term (succ zero), ... has the same runtime layout. Plan: (1) a pass over each recursive union family that marks a type parameter phantom when it occurs only as a type argument of the family itself (or of other families where it is phantom: var n inside term n), never in a payload's primitive/layout position; (2) union layout keys and join-point specialization keys erase phantom arguments (one layout 'term _' and one size join point shared across indices); type checking keeps the full index (the GADT guarantee stays static). (3) Regression: gadt_growing_index_runtime flips from the pinned EJP0041 error to exit 6 (= size) on every backend; EJP0041 stays for a REAL growing layout (an index that does change the payload). Estimate: core work in PartEval's union/join-point keying, ~1-2 days with the matrix review; it's the item's remaining 60%. CORRECTION 16:34: 'size should return 6' was wrong. By hand, size of Lam (App (Var VZ, Lam (Var (VS VZ)))) = 1 + (1 + 1 + (1 + 1)) = 5, and the regression's expected exit is whatever main returns from it (read main's last lines when writing the sample's expectation), not a number from this note.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_25 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 25u32, "2026-11-01T04:50Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX folder (4 files: debruijn_rename.spi, debruijn_rename_runtime.spi, debruijn_size_runtime.spi, gadt_same_index_runtime.spi) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_26 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 26u32, "2026-11-01T12:35Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:35 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: est 120 -> 480 (erasure in PartEval is not a 2 h job); tag team:compiler. DEFINITION OF DONE: samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime returns 5 on C, Rust and F# (today EJP0041 after 18 s), and the 4 lab files restored from %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (debruijn_rename*.spi, debruijn_size*) become samples that compile and agree with C; EJP0041 still fires for a genuinely unbounded index (keep its negative sample). NEXT STEP: restore the 4 lab files into samples/ as drafts (scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore ... -Only 'GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX\\*'), run probe.ps1 on each with -Stacks 15 to see where the join point key keeps growing. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: the fix ERASES the phantom index at the join point boundary: the join point key uses the GADT's erased type (its union without the index argument) and the index survives only as an existential witness in the body, so the key set is finite; the erasure rule is a predicate on Ty ('index position of a GADT union' marked by the union's declaration), not a name pattern. Model: the mega_zeta / brzozowski GADT samples and the debruijn lab files.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_27 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 27u32, "2026-11-01T14:37Z", "main", "main 10:4x: the browser research lists 'fix the GADT index bug at run time' as a prerequisite for everything (steal list #5 and the rogue route's M0). It points to this item, so no new item; keep it P0.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_28 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 28u32, "2026-11-01T19:23Z", "compiler", "11-01 15:35 compiler: STEP 1 DONE (lab files home). Restored the 4 lab files from %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045. debruijn_size_runtime is the existing samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime in edition-2 spelling, so it was not duplicated; debruijn_rename.spi is the static twin of the rename one and was not landed. Landed two new samples (unstaged), respelled with the installed `format --spelling symbols` and matched to the existing sample's `+.`: samples/gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime (rename with lift, weakening term zero -> term (succ zero), then size: expected 5, like gadt_growing_index_runtime) and samples/gadt_same_index_runtime (tree n with a constant index, count = 3: the control that must already compile). CODE READ (core F6F6879566C5A830), where the index grows: (1) a union instance is a nominal application YApply(term, arg), and nominal_type_apply lays out its cases (PartEval ty / TUnion, 9997-10022). Lam's payload is term (succ n), so each index is a distinct YUnion H, and the depth guard there gives EJP0041 'laying out union case'. (2) The join-point key carries env_global_type, the bindings of the body's free type variables. size's n is rebound to succ n at every recursive call, so the key grows, and UnionNesting.tooDeep (10384) gives EJP0041 'specializing a join point'. A fix must cut BOTH. DESIGN, sound by construction: (a) DECLARATION-LEVEL phantom parameters: a union parameter is phantom when it occurs only as an argument in the same parameter position of its own family (term n -> term (succ n)) or of another family where it is phantom (var n). That is a static property of the declarations alone. nominal_type_apply then lays the union out with phantom arguments replaced by unit, so every index shares one layout and one H. The nominal type keeps its real argument (type checking and show_ty are unchanged). (b) JOIN-POINT LEVEL: when a key's type-variable binding exceeds a small depth (e.g. 8) for the same jp_body, retry the body with that variable bound to an abstract rigid type that any typecase or layout inspection rejects. If the body evaluates, one join point serves every index. If it inspects the index (real type-level computation, e.g. a static vector length), the retry fails, the per-index specialization stands, and EJP0041 stays the honest error (its negative sample keeps passing). NEXT STEP: implement (a) first, alone; it is enough if the recursive call's type argument is inferred from the erased data type. Probe on gadt_growing_index_runtime with probe.ps1 -Stacks, on the node (the box is compile-quiet).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_29 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 29u32, "2026-11-02T13:16Z", "main", "main 11-02 USER: \"crazy that we haven't finished these gadt bugs yet, compiler lane gotta do better\". Status: this item P0 45% Active (28 notes) and SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES P0 80% Planned are the open GADT bugs; the rest of the family is Done (GADT-UNINHABITED-PAYLOAD-RUNTIME since 10-12; its sample and gadt_rank_matrix now pass on 4 and 7 backends). What this one blocks today: the zeta mega sample's 3 open proof goals (ZETA-TYPED-ARITH), runtime CIC terms (CIC-* items), REGEX-TYPED-CORES and LIB-VEC-FIN (length-growing runtime recursion), MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER. Ask for the compiler lane: make this the next pack's top item with a dated plan (minimal repro, fix, one regression sample with a matrix row), ahead of the linked-units second cut.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_30 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 30u32, "2026-11-02T13:26Z", "compiler", "11-02 09:10 compiler: DATED PLAN (user 11-02 via main: top of the next pack, ahead of the linked-units follow-ups; pack16 was already built and proven, so it ships as is and costs this item nothing).\nMINIMAL REPRO (exists, pinned): samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime. A de Bruijn `term n` with `Lam :: term (succ n) -> term n` and runtime `size`; expected 5; today ExpectedErrors 'has no finite layout' (EJP0041). Twins: samples/gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime (rename + lift + size, expected 5) and the control samples/gadt_same_index_runtime (constant index, 3, compiles today).\nREFINEMENT FOUND TODAY, which changes n28's (a): plain erasure of the phantom index to () is UNSOUND for GADTs. TUnion layout (spiral_compiler.fs ~10007) evaluates each case's specialized destructor against the bound index, and a case whose destructor does not unify is dropped (VZ :: var (succ n) does not exist in var zero). Binding n := () would drop VZ from every instance. FIX (a'): bind a phantom parameter to one ERASED marker type that unifies with every index pattern in destructors. Then the erased layout holds ALL constructors, and nominal_type_apply keys every instance of the family to the same (nominal, Erased) application, so the layout is one finite union. The unboxed payload of Lam is then term (succ Erased) -> laid out as term Erased, so size's inferred n stays at the same key: one join point instead of an unbounded chain. Phantom = declaration-level: the parameter occurs only as a type argument of :! unit nominals or of the phantom positions of union families (term n, var n). Type checking runs before PartEval and keeps exact indices, so the GADT guarantee stays static. Anything that typecases on the index keeps per-index specialization (no erasure), and EJP0041 stays for real growth.\nSCHEDULE: 11-02 implement (a') as a kitdo program over the live core and probe the three samples locally (compile-only; the box is compile-quiet). 11-02/03, only if size's key still grows: n28's (b) rigid-index retry at the join point. 11-03: matrix: gadt_growing_index_runtime moves from ExpectedErrors to a passing row on C, Rust, F#, Zig, Gleam (exit 5), plus gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime (5) and gadt_same_index_runtime (3); a residual A/B over the examples suite must show 0 changed rows outside GADT samples; then the pack.\nDoD (n26 unchanged): exit 5 on C/Rust/F#, the lab twins agree with C, and EJP0041 still fires for a genuinely unbounded index.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_31 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 31u32, "2026-11-02T14:06Z", "compiler", "11-02 10:15 compiler: ITERATION LOG on program %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX\\1-phantom-index-erasure.kitdo. gadt1 (erase every unit-nominal index): the growing-index EJP0041 is GONE. gadt_growing_index_runtime and gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime got past layout and join-point specialization and stopped at union construction (\"For key Var ... Got: var (succ zero) Expected: var <mark>\"). BUT erasing every index broke constructor-specialized GADTs: gadt_rank_matrix and gadt_specialized_case_rank (Pattern miss: state_a raw_a laid out with DecidedA too), gadt_uninhabited_payload_runtime (Typecase miss) and wgsl_gadt_payload_union (15 backends). LESSON: a unit-nominal index is not automatically phantom; shallow indices select constructors, and that is the point of a GADT. gadt3 erases only on GROWTH: an index nested deeper than PhantomIndex.depth = 4, or one already holding the mark (term (succ mark) folds back to term mark, a fixed point), so constant and shallow indices keep their exact specialized layouts. Plus phantom_erase: union construction accepts a payload whose type equals the case type under canonical erasure (the same shared layout). Probing the whole `gadt` filter on gadt3 now; the control comparison follows.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_32 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 32u32, "2026-11-02T15:01Z", "compiler", "11-02 11:05 compiler: REPRO FIXED on candidate gadt4 (program GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX\\1, 6 replacements). samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime compiles and exits 5 on C, Rust, F# and Delphi, agreeing with the oracle (single-flight-20261102-105134); it had stopped at EJP0041 for weeks. The whole `gadt` filter: 103 jobs, 99 ok, DISAGREE 0, and every previously-green GADT sample is still green (rank_matrix 7/7, specialized_case_rank, uninhabited_payload, wgsl_gadt_payload_union on 15 backends, contract_gadt, frontier_gadt_growing_index). THE TWO CUTS: (a) layout: an index that grows past depth 4, or already holds the mark, is laid out once with the index bound to PhantomIndex.mark, and inside that layout a destructor pattern unifies with the mark, so all constructors stay; (b) join point: env_global_type maps such indices to the mark, so size's specializations reach a fixed point after 5 levels instead of hitting the 256 limit. Union construction accepts a payload equal to the case type under canonical erasure (phantom_erase). Shallow and constant indices keep exact constructor-specialized layouts, which is what the gadt1 regressions taught. LEFT: gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime fails \"The union does not have key VS. Got: var (succ zero)\". PE specializes `ren` (\\ v => VS v) on a var zero argument, and var (succ zero) prunes VS because its payload var zero is uninhabited (10022-10025). That is a separate dead-branch issue. NEXT: residual A/B of examples+contracts vs control, then fix the twin (build of an uninhabited-payload constructor in a dead branch: emit unreachable instead of a type error).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_33 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 33u32, "2026-11-02T15:41Z", "compiler", "11-02 11:45 compiler: gadt4 A/B. EXAMPLES (Rust/Gleam/Zig, 1,421 rows vs control 082202): only the two target samples change. CONTRACTS (728 rows, control 111742 vs candidate 112912): ONE regression, mega_brzozowski_derivatives/antimirov_typed_slot_bound on Zig, ok -> \"Typecase miss. Got: antimirov_position_tree bit_alphabet .phantom-index\". Its shape index is a STATIC type-level tree deeper than 4, and the depth threshold erased it, breaking a typecase on the shape. LESSON: depth is not growth. gadt5 replaces the depth rule with an ANCESTRY rule. PhantomIndex keeps a per-join-point stack of the keys being specialized (pushed around term_scope'' of the Owner body); a binding is erased only when it properly contains the binding at the same position of an ACTIVE ancestor specialization of the same join point (size@term zero -> size@term (succ zero)). A descent over a static tree passes ever-smaller subterms, and siblings are never on the stack, so those keep exact specializations. Layout erasure now fires only for an index holding the mark. Also added: constructing a case that the layout pruned because its payload is uninhabited, in a branch PE reaches but the program never runs at runtime (the rename twin), emits a typed failwith \"union case ruled out by partial evaluation\" instead of a compile error. A missing key with an inhabited payload still errors. gadt5 gadt filter + full A/B running.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_34 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 34u32, "2026-11-02T17:37Z", "compiler", "11-02 13:45 compiler: FIXED (candidate gadt7, built on the node 13:09). samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime compiles and RUNS exit 5 on Fsharp, C, Rust and Delphi, oracle agree (node job-1793639237695-compiler-gadt7). A/B on the node mirror (pinned lib snapshot; the box lib was mid-land): examples Rust/Gleam/Zig, 1,421 rows, ONLY that row changes (error -> ok); contracts, 728 rows, 0 differences, so antimirov stays green. FINAL DESIGN (program GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX\\1, 7 replacements): (1) DECLARATION-LEVEL growth. erased_families(env.nominals) marks a family whose constructor PAYLOAD (a GADT constructor's domain, not its result) applies the family itself to a non-variable index (term n holds term (succ n)), plus every family named in its payloads (var). Members are laid out at PhantomIndex.mark for every unit-nominal index, so all their values share one representation (gadt6 broke there: exact var layouts inside an erased term, so rustc E0308 Rc<UH0> vs Rc<UH2>). (2) A join-point key binding that grows past an ACTIVE ANCESTOR specialization of the same join point is mapped to the mark (the type-argument growth `size (succ n)`). (3) A mark layout lets destructor patterns unify with the mark, so all constructors stay. (4) Union construction accepts a payload equal under canonical erasure. (5) A pruned case with an uninhabited payload in a PE-only branch is a typed failwith, not an error. WRONG TURNS, kept for the record: erase every unit index (broke constructor-selecting GADTs), erase past depth 4 (broke static deep shapes), dynamic family detection (layout-order dependent). MATRIX: the ExpectedErrors row for gadt_growing_index_runtime is removed (it now runs on the default backends). gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime still TIMES OUT in both arms; that is the follow-up twin, not a regression. NEXT: a genuinely-unbounded negative sample (EJP0041 must still fire), then pack17.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_35 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 35u32, "2026-11-02T18:46Z", "compiler", "11-02 14:55 compiler: HANDED TO MAIN in pack17 (SpiralCompiler.dll 6B9374835A1BBACF, core R2R E556D773EB123175; box and node builds byte-identical; install-pack dry run clean on live 081180B0). Full review on the node mirror, control = installed: contracts 728 rows with 0 diffs; examples Rust/Gleam/Zig/C 1,981 rows, behavior changes only on gadt_growing_index_runtime (error -> ok) and zig_export_fn; Rust+C native 1,070 rows, the only behavior change is this sample, error -> exit 5, oracle agree on Rust. Matrix (unstaged): the ExpectedErrors row removed; samples/gadt_unbounded_index_negative added with an ExpectedErrors EJP0041 row and a 60 s timeout (nest t holding nest (box t) carries real data, so erasure must not apply; installed compiler errors in 14 s). DoD: met except the lab twin gadt_debruijn_rename_runtime (it times out in both arms; it is closure growth, `rename (lift ren)` nests a static closure per level, not index growth). Closing after install, with the twin split out as its own item.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_36 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 36u32, "2026-11-02T18:54Z", "compiler", "11-02 14:55 compiler: DONE. pack17 is installed on the box (14:50; SpiralCompiler.dll 6B9374835A1BBACF, core E556D773EB123175, undo compiler-installs\\pack17-20261102-144751). samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime runs exit 5 on C/Rust/F#/Delphi; EJP0041 still fires for the genuinely unbounded samples/gadt_unbounded_index_negative; contracts unchanged. The lab twin is split out as GADT-DEBRUIJN-RENAME-CLOSURE-GROWTH (closure growth, not index growth). Unblocks per n29: ZETA-TYPED-ARITH, the runtime CIC terms, REGEX-TYPED-CORES, LIB-VEC-FIN, MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER; their owners can retry on the installed compiler.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_1 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 1u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 first grain, the interface this item needs from EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS: one call to request a window of class test-suite with an expected duration, granted or queued, and a release in the gate's teardown (spiral.notebook.ex cond, next to CompilerClient.shutdown, which also runs when the gate fails). The gate can estimate the duration from the file and test count; main's 10:51 data point: sm'.spi with 42 tests took 217 s on the warm daemon and 537 s with a process per compile. The conflicts that matter here are declared once: test-suite vs live-gui (the 10-14 reaper incidents) and test-suite vs compiler-install. Until that call exists, nothing changes in Cells. Status: blocked on the eoie lane.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_2 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 2u32, "2026-10-19T16:01Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:01 lap 7 status check: EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS is still (EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS  Feature  P1  D4  0%  Planned), so the Cells side stays unstarted on purpose; the interface from note 1 is unchanged.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_3 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 3u32, "2026-10-19T16:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:43 lap 8 grain: placement decided while blocked: the machine-window acquire and release go into the wrappers that start a gate (the scaffold's scripts/gate.ps1 around its mix call, and Invoke-Heavy callers), not into mix spiral.notebook's gate branch (spiral.notebook.ex:10-19), because Cells does not call eoie (the same rule CELLS-NEW-PROJECT follows by printing the epic command instead of running it). The Cells side therefore needs no change; the wrapper needs one eoie call before mix (window test-suite with a duration) and one in its finally block. Still blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned, 0%)")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_4 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 4u32, "2026-10-19T17:19Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:20 lap 9 grain: planning set (estimate 60m, load light) and the duration input for the window request found: the gate CLI already writes --metrics <dir>/tests.tsv (notebook.ex:271-275, columns File Test Target Status Ms Memo), so the gate wrapper can request a test-suite window with expected duration = sum of Ms of the previous tests.tsv for the same paths (memo-hit rows count ~0), falling back to a fixed default on a first run; the wrapper passes --metrics into the per-run dir. Still blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned 0%): it unblocks with an eoie verb pair that grants or queues a class=test-suite window for N minutes and releases it")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_5 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 5u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:42 lap 10 grain: the window-duration half is written and self-tested: lab/CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW/expected-minutes.ps1 -MetricsDir <previous run> reads the gate's --metrics tests.tsv, sums Ms of rows whose Memo is not hit (Memo hit is the only value Cells counts, notebook.ex:214), times a 1.5 margin, ceil to minutes, at least 1; no table = 10 min first-run default. Self-test on a synthetic table (30 s run + 50 s hit + 50 s run) gives 2 min; a missing dir gives 10. The acquire/release half still waits on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned 0%): the gate wrapper will pass Minutes as the requested window length")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_6 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 6u32, "2026-10-19T17:58Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:58 lap 11 grain, an interim bridge until EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS exists: the scaffold's scripts/gate.ps1 (lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/patch/priv/new_project/scripts/gate.ps1) now runs the mix gate through spiral's own heavy gate (dot-sources apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 when the spiral checkout has it, Invoke-Heavy -Label cells gate <project>), so a gate waits for a free heavy slot instead of competing with suites; without heavy.ps1 it runs directly. Invoke-Heavy has no reentrancy marker (heavy.ps1 reads no env var), so a nested call would take a second slot or wait on itself: gate.ps1 takes -InsideHeavySlot to skip it, and spiral-new-run.ps1 (which already holds a slot) passes it; both parse with 0 errors; the ExUnit assertion (gate.ps1 mentions spiral.notebook) still holds. When EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS lands, the Invoke-Heavy call becomes the window acquire with expected-minutes.ps1's length")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_7 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 7u32, "2026-10-22T05:51Z", "main", "01:51 main: unblocked. EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS exists now (eoie window request/run/release, classes live-gui|test-suite|heavy-build|compile|compiler-install). The duration half is promoted: lab expected-minutes.ps1 is now apps/cells/spi/expected_minutes.ps1, re-self-tested (a synthetic 30 s run + 50 s hit + 50 s run gives 2 min; a missing metrics dir gives 10), and the lab folder is deleted. Remaining: lib_gate.ps1 and the scaffold gate.ps1 take a test-suite window of expected_minutes around their mix call. A wrapper can't tell it already holds a window: there is no env marker (checked eoie src), and every main job wraps lib_gate in 'eoie window run compile', so a nested request could wait on itself. Asked eoie for EOIE_WINDOW_HELD=<class>:<id> on window run children (and EOIE_HEAVY_HELD for job start --heavy). The wrapper change lands once that exists, with no lib_gate run in flight.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_8 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 8u32, "2026-10-23T21:32Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:32 main: the marker exists in eoie source (src/eoie_window_run/run.spi child_status sets EOIE_WINDOW_HELD on the window-run child) but not in the installed eoie.exe yet: 'eoie window run compile 1 -- pwsh -Command $env:EOIE_WINDOW_HELD' printed it empty at 17:3x. The wrapper change (lib_gate.ps1 and the scaffold gate.ps1 take a test-suite window of expected_minutes around their mix call unless EOIE_WINDOW_HELD is set) waits for the eoie release that carries it; re-probe with that one-liner first.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_9 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 6 11:42 (subagent2): interface spelled out (test-suite window acquire with duration, release in the gate teardown; conflicts live-gui, compiler-install) -> next: blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS | lap 12 14:29 (subagent2): scaffold gate.ps1: -Path is [string[]], which pwsh -File passes as one comma string (a.spi,b.spi -> Resolve-Path fails); now splits on commas like try.ps1 -Modules; parse 0, split check resolves both files. Dep EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS still Planned 0% -> next: swap Invoke-Heavy for the window acquire when EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS lands | lap 13 14:41 (subagent2): blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned 0%, EOIE lane); interim bridge in the scaffold gate.ps1 stays -> next: unblocks: EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS lands its window acquire verb | lap 14 15:03 (subagent2): blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned) -> next: EOIE lane lands the window verb | lap 16 16:06 (subagent2): blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS -> next: EOIE lane | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): blocked -> next: EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS | lap 19 18:56 (subagent2): blocked -> next: EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_10 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 10u32, "2026-10-25T00:01Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:01 main groom: status Active -> Blocked. Since the 10-19 lap 6 note, the only next step has been 'blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS', and that item is itself Blocked at 95% (eoie team). This resumes when it is Done.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_11 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 11u32, "2026-10-30T02:01Z", "main", "10-29 22:01 main, groom: re-verified Blocked: waits on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (kit's, 95%, Blocked on EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN). kit published TRAIN4 today (C528BE4D), so ask kit next session whether TRAIN4 or TRAIN5 carries the machine-windows piece; kit's EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE window kinds (n7) are related.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_12 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 12u32, "2026-11-02T12:24Z", "x", "2026-11-02 08:24 x: GROOM: UNBLOCKED. Its blocker EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS is Done (100%), and the windows are in daily use (kit window request/release/wait-clear with heavy-build and tree-write classes; this session used tree-write several times today). What is left is Cells taking a test-suite window itself in --gate. Blocked -> Planned.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_13 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 13u32, "2026-11-05T06:23Z", "main", "11-05 02:23 main DONE in apps/cells/spi/run_notebook.ps1 (unstaged, existing script): every --gate run takes ONE kit test-suite window for its notebooks (label \"cells gate <files>\") and releases it on every exit path (warm and mix exits through Exit-Notebook, plus a trap for thrown mix steps). kit's model makes test-suite wait behind live-gui and compiler-install (exclusive) and bench, while sharing the machine with other suites and heavy builds. --min-free-mb 0 keeps the 3 GB RAM floor from stalling gates on a busy box; the wait is bounded (SPIRAL_CELLS_GATE_WAIT_MIN, default 30), after which the gate runs anyway with a stderr warning, so a stuck window can't deadlock every lane. Lease SPIRAL_CELLS_GATE_WINDOW_MIN (default 180); opt out with SPIRAL_CELLS_GATE_WINDOW=0; no kit on the machine = no window, unchanged behavior. PROOF on the box: the exact request call was granted (queued 9 s), listed as held with its label, and gone after release; a real gate (apps/kernel/src/env.spi) passed 2/2 through the edited script and left no window of its own; another lane's gate (date_time.spi) was already holding a test-suite window minutes later, picked up from the live tree.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 1u32, "2026-10-20T03:24Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 23:24 user 2026-10-19 23:1x: finish xsd first (start, after the 10-17 research go-gate); slice from SEFAZ-XSD-APP notes 1-10.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 2u32, "2026-10-21T23:13Z", "main", "19:13 slice-5 plan, written while compiles are blocked (no code run yet). Local inventory: fpc (scoop) is present and is what the matrix's Delphi rows use; the DFe.NET sparse clone under .claude/jobs/5a6e53fb/tmp/xsd-data holds the XSDs and AppTeste folders but 0 .cs sources (a sparse checkout), and no ACBr checkout exists. Plan in gated steps: (1) compile ONE generated module (generated_nfe_v4_00.spi, 1 example) with --backend Delphi and build it with fpc; gate: it compiles, and the example document validates with 0 messages and round-trips byte-identical, like the 13 .NET examples (needs a compile window, after the night install). (2) all 13 modules on Delphi, same gate. (3) benchmarks: widen the DFe.NET checkout to its NFe sources (a git sparse-checkout add, local), and fetch ACBr's NFe units (ProjetoACBr, the GitHub mirror), each into its own new folder per the untrusted-download rule. Compare parse, validate and serialize of the same 13 documents: results must agree; speed reported per document. Rule for 'done': steps 1-2 green and step 3 numbers recorded; a fetch failure or a license problem keeps step 3 open, not the item. Order: after PACK-AFTER-NIGHT, since SEFAZ variant 3 changes the generated modules first.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 3u32, "2026-10-21T23:15Z", "main", "19:15 step-1 blocker found by reading and staged: the lab's xml.spi has Rust-only arms (plus one Zig) for code_point_at, code_point_width, code_point_count, code_point_string and is_file; every other backend failwiths, so the generated modules could not run on Delphi at all. lab/SEFAZ-XSD-XML-READER/delphi-arms.ps1 (4 anchors, dry run matches each once) adds byte_at with a Delphi arm (UInt32(Ord(s[i + 1])); codegenDelphi indexes strings by byte, 1-based, and Length counts bytes), a Spiral UTF-8 decoder on top of it (utf8_width_of, utf8_decode_at), Delphi arms for code_point_at / width / count, and is_file via FileExists. Not covered: code_point_string (it builds a string from a code point, needed only for numeric entities) stays failwith on Delphi; find_from already has a generic fallback. The lib never converts char to a number, so a fully portable byte reader would need compiler support; one Delphi macro is the smallest step. Verified only by step 1's compile and run (after the night install).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 4u32, "2026-10-25T00:54Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:54 main groom: P1 -> P0 by the priority-order rule. It's a FISCAL-APP dep and the last slice of the user's XSD order (memory priority-order).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 5u32, "2026-10-25T06:49Z", "fiscal", "02:49 fiscal: scripts/delphi-arms.ps1 deleted from fiscal under FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE rule D6 (note 10): it was a parked patch adding Delphi arms to lib xml.spi, written in the pre-edition-3 lib, never landed (lib had 0 of its arms; main n4). This slice needs those arms; they get drafted fresh against the current edition-3 xml.spi when it starts. The old text is in the 02:31 snapshot (session scratchpad fiscal-snapshot-20261025-023152\\scripts\\delphi-arms.ps1).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 6u32, "2026-10-25T07:30Z", "fiscal", "03:30 fiscal: step-1 blocker re-drafted against the current edition-3 lib (no lib module has a | Delphi => arm yet). Landing form: session scratchpad xml-delphi-arms\\apply.py <path to xml.spi> [--apply], anchored, base xml.spi 9980B7ACD658A591, format --check clean, macro splice scan clean. delphi_byte_at is the only host call (Ord(s[i]) with the 1-based index computed in Spiral, granular rule); utf8_width_of and delphi_code_point_at decode UTF-8 in plain Spiral; Delphi arms for code_point_at, code_point_width and is_file (FileExists). code_point_string stays without a Delphi arm (numeric entities only). Unverified until step 1 compiles one generated module with --backend Delphi and builds it with fpc: whether the Delphi output already uses SysUtils for FileExists, and whether codegenDelphi strings are byte-indexed UTF-8 as main n3 read them. Land with that compile, not before (lib readers: fiscal gate job-1792912920241 and main pack reviews).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 7u32, "2026-10-25T07:34Z", "fiscal", "03:34 fiscal: step-3 source research (no download yet). DFe.NET: LGPL-2.1, last push 2026-09-24 (gh api); the benchmark side needs NFe.Classes and DFe.Classes (serialization model) beside the 4 schema folders init.ps1 already sparse-checks out, so the fetch is one git sparse-checkout add in a NEW folder (untrusted-download rule), never in deps/corpus. ACBr: the canonical source is SourceForge SVN svn://svn.code.sf.net/p/acbr/code/trunk2; the largest GitHub copy, frones/ACBr, is a git-svn mirror last pushed 2025-08-03 (stale, 183 stars); the Projeto-ACBr-Oficial GitHub org holds only SDKs (acbrapi-sdk-*), n8n, TEF and FPDF-Pascal, not the core. The ACBrNFe units pull ACBrComum, ACBrDFe and the OpenSSL bindings, so the fetch is an svn export of those folders into its own folder; no svn client on this machine (scoop install svn first). Order unchanged: steps 1-2 (Delphi compile of the generated modules) before any fetch.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 8u32, "2026-10-25T07:44Z", "fiscal", "03:44 fiscal: svn client installed (scoop main/sliksvn 1.14.5, the current 1.14 LTS line). ACBr trunk2 mapped with svn list only (no code fetched): svn://svn.code.sf.net/p/acbr/code/trunk2/Fontes holds ACBrDFe (ACBrNFe, ACBrCTe, ACBrMDFe, ACBrBPe, ACBrDCe, ... plus the shared ACBrDFe*.pas units and the reform units ACBrDFe.RTC.Classes/XmlReader/XmlWriter for IBS/CBS), next to ACBrComum, PCNComum, ACBrOpenSSL, ACBrLibXML2, ACBrTCP and Terceiros. Step-3 export set: Fontes/ACBrDFe/{ACBrNFe,ACBrCTe,ACBrMDFe} + Fontes/ACBrDFe/*.pas + Fontes/{ACBrComum,PCNComum,ACBrOpenSSL,ACBrLibXML2}, svn export into its own new folder (untrusted-download rule), never compiled from inside it.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_9 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 9u32, "2026-10-25T16:33Z", "main", "10-25 12:33 main: n8's two unverified points, answered by reading codegenDelphi (no compile). (1) Every Delphi program has 'uses SysUtils, Math;' (spiral_compiler.fs:165652), so FileExists resolves. (2) string = AnsiString (:165196), StringLength = Length(s), bytes (:165475), StringIndex emits s[i + 1] (:165493), i.e. 0-based in Spiral and +1 in the output; fiscal's delphi_byte_at adds the 1 once in Spiral, which is consistent. Remaining risk for step 1: FPC {H+} with no {codepage} keeps bytes raw, so step 1's round-trip gate compares bytes. Sent to fiscal.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_10 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 10u32, "2026-10-25T16:34Z", "fiscal", "12:4x fiscal: step-1 reach survey, static (no compile). (1) The n6 open questions are answered from codegenDelphi, and main confirmed them independently: every program has uses SysUtils, Math (spiral_compiler.fs:165652), strings are AnsiString under {$H+} (:165196) and byte-indexed (StringSlice :165255). The xml-delphi-arms draft still dry-runs on xml.spi 9980B7ACD658A591. (2) A generated module calls only xml.*, listm, sm'.length, env.get_environment_variable and file_system.{write_all_text, get_current_directory, find_parent} (census over generated_ksef_fa_3). Scanning those lib run_target blocks (session scratchpad delphi-survey/arms.py) finds more Delphi gaps than xml. Silent ones: write_all_text's _ arm is () and read_all_text/get_current_directory return null, so on Delphi the example document would not be written and .NET validation would have nothing to check. Hard ones: env.get_environment_variable has no Delphi arm; sm'.substring's fallback is .NET text (!str.Substring), and xml.find_from goes through it; find_parent's error message is an F# interpolation macro ($'$\"...{!name}...\"'), which only the Python codegen rewrites (:177895). (3) Drafted as lib slice delphi-arms/apply.py (bases sm' 2E259892F464E82A, env 8A982F53CB26F7F8, file_system 7DB84A4BBD4E158C; base-hash pinned, all-or-nothing, dry run ok):\n- Copy(str, start+1, len), GetEnvironmentVariable, GetCurrentDir(), DirectoryExists, FileExists;\n- directory_get_parent via ExtractFileDir(ExcludeTrailingPathDelimiter), mirroring the TypeScript arm;\n- write/read through one global helper each, using an untyped File + BlockWrite/BlockRead, so bytes stay raw (no TextFile codepage conversion; main's point: compare the round trip as bytes);\n- find_parent's message built with ++# (same text, portable to every backend).\nRule for step 1, fixed now: land xml-delphi-arms + delphi-arms together only after one generated module (generated_mdfe_v1_00, the smallest) compiles with --backend Delphi, builds with fpc, writes its example, and that example (a) is byte-identical to the Rust run's example and (b) validates with 0 .NET messages. Any compile error in a lib arm is fixed in the slice first. Step 1 needs a compile slot, so it queues after the XSD-SLICES land.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_11 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 11u32, "2026-10-25T16:41Z", "fiscal", "12:5x fiscal: delphi-arms reshaped per main's granular-style review (GRANULAR-HEAL: a global Pascal helper with a body is the rust_global pattern). write_all_text's Delphi arm is now single host calls sequenced in Spiral: FileCreate(path) -> handle : i64; failwith on -1; length via sm'.length; FileWrite(handle, text[first], length) with first = 1 computed in Spiral and a short-write failwith; FileClose(handle). That is the SysUtils handle API, not AssignFile/BlockWrite, because an untyped File value cannot be created by codegen's v := expr (file types are not assignable in Pascal) and AssignFile/Rewrite take it as var. The bytes stay raw. read_all_text has no Delphi arm now: FileRead needs a by-reference buffer, filed as DELPHI-FILE-READ-BUFFER; generated modules never read files. The slice has 0 global; format --check on a patched copy shows only the 3 over-120 lines the tree already has (file_system 286, 299, 793 -> 801).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_12 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 12u32, "2026-10-25T16:44Z", "fiscal", "12:5x fiscal: step-1 probe staged as session scratchpad delphi-step1.ps1 [-Module generated_mdfe_v1_00 -Schema MDFe.AppTeste/Schemas/mdfe_v1.00.xsd]. It copies the tree lib to <work>\\lib and applies xml-delphi-arms + delphi-arms there, then positive-controls that the 4 patched files carry their Delphi arms. It places the module under <work>\\fiscal\\generated\\<m> (deps holds only a corpus junction, and it refuses if deps\\spiral exists, so Cells' ancestor walk resolves the patched lib), retargets the module's single ///> rust test header to ///> delphi (Cells builds it with scoop fpc), and runs it with FISCAL_ROOT=<work>\\fiscal. Then it byte-compares the written example with fiscal/samples' Rust example and validates it with scripts/validate-example.ps1 against the official XSD. Queue condition: after the XSD-SLICES land and the full fiscal run, which regenerates generated/<m> (today only generated_ksef_fa_3 is on disk) and a fresh Rust example.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_13 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 13u32, "2026-10-25T17:28Z", "fiscal", "13:3x fiscal: step-1 reach made exact (still static, no compile). The first survey read only run_target blocks; backend_switch blocks matter too, because a non-unit backend_switch with no Delphi key emits the identifier backend_switch_has_no_Delphi_arm_in_lib_spiral (lib backend.spi:46), which fpc rejects, and a unit one emits '' (silently nothing). New tool, session scratchpad delphi-survey/reach.py <generated .spi> <lib dir>: a call-graph BFS from the module's calls over lib definitions that follows only what Delphi specializes (the Delphi arm, else _, of a run_target; the Delphi key of a backend_switch). On generated_ksef_fa_3 it found 3 more gaps: unicode.code_point_count (xml.code_point_count, 54 calls; Gleam/Rust/Zig only); sm'.index_of (xml.local_name <- with_namespace); sm'.replace (xml.escape_text_xml <- write_node). It also found 4 optionm' functions, all through find_parent: option' is a host-boxed option whose option'_switch has no Delphi type. Also fixed in my own draft: its directory_get_parent Delphi arm built option' via optionm'.box and would have failed. Slice changes:\n- xml-delphi-arms: code_point_count gains run_target Delphi -> delphi_code_points_from (counts lead bytes in Spiral with the draft's utf8_width_of);\n- delphi-arms: index_of Delphi = conv (portable_index_of search s), the C arm's pure-Spiral path over the StringLength/StringIndex builtins; replace Delphi = StringReplace(!s, !old, !new, [rfReplaceAll]), one call;\n- new file_system.directory_parent (path) : option string (Delphi: ExtractFileDir in Spiral option; _: directory_get_parent |> optionm'.unbox), used by find_parent, so option' never reaches Delphi; the directory_get_parent arm is dropped.\nResult on a patched lib model: 45 definitions reached, 0 without a Delphi path; positive control on the tree lib: 14. Not seen statically: the |core- package (listm) and codegen intrinsics, which the step-1 compile covers. The smoke notebook in delphi-step1.ps1 now also checks index_of on UTF-8 bytes and a miss, replace on every occurrence, and local_name.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_14 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 14u32, "2026-10-25T18:12Z", "fiscal", "14:2x fiscal: step-3 benchmark design, written before any fetch or measurement (rules fixed now).\nCorpus: the 13 BR example documents that fiscal's build writes to samples/ (NF-e 2.00/3.10/4.00, CT-e 2.00/3.00/4.00, CT-e OS 3.00/4.00, CT-e Simp 4.00, GTV-e 3.00/4.00, MDF-e 1.00/3.00). Each library is measured only on the layouts it supports, and a row says 'unsupported' instead of being dropped.\nOperations per document: parse (XML text -> the library's model), validate, serialize (model -> XML text).\nReferee: .NET XmlSchemaSet over the OFFICIAL XSDs in deps/corpus (scripts/validate-example.ps1), not any library's own validator. ACBr ships modified schemas (its tiposBasico was changed to work with OpenSSL, per the ACBr forum), and its Validar expects a signed NF-e.\nAgreement rules: (A1) every library parses every supported document; (A2) each library's serialize(parse(doc)) is canonically equal to doc (C14N, whitespace-insensitive), else the differing paths are listed; (A3) each validator's verdict equals the referee's, and a validator that refuses unsigned documents is reported as such, not as a disagreement.\nSpeed rule: per document and operation, 5 warm-up runs, then the median of 31, single thread, in one test-suite window on this machine, reported for ours on Rust, ours on Delphi (fpc), ACBr (fpc, the same toolchain as ours) and DFe.NET (.NET release build). No winner claim without A1-A3 holding for that row.\nHarness language: ours is Spiral. ACBr's is Spiral compiled to Delphi calling ACBr through one-call macros, which needs DELPHI-USES-UNITS (filed); until then, a thin Pascal program, recorded as a stopgap. DFe.NET's API (deserialize/serialize entry points; FuncoesXml in DFe.Utils is the expected home) is read from the source after the fetch; a web search did not confirm it.\nSources: ACBr forum threads on LoadFromString, Validar (signature, schemas folder) and GerarXml: https://www.projetoacbr.com.br/forum/topic/17751-validar-assinar-e-gerarnfe/ , https://www.projetoacbr.com.br/forum/tags/loadstring/ , https://www.projetoacbr.com.br/forum/topic/36956-erro-ao-enviar-xml-para-homologa%C3%A7%C3%A3o/")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_15 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 15u32, "2026-10-25T18:15Z", "fiscal", "14:2x fiscal: rule revision, recorded before acting. Note 03:34's 'steps 1-2 before any fetch' rested on main's 10-21 reason (SEFAZ variant 3 changes the generated modules first). That reason is gone: the generated modules' shape has no bearing on ACBr's or DFe.NET's API, and the step-3 design (n14) needs their real entry points. New rule: exploration fetches are allowed now, read-only. Each goes into its own new folder under the session scratchpad (untrusted-download rule); nothing from them is built or executed; only .pas/.cs text is read. Scope: ACBr trunk2 Fontes/ACBrDFe/ACBrNFe via svn export; DFe.NET's DFe.Utils + NFe.Classes via a blobless sparse git clone. The reproducible fetch into deps/ (an init.ps1 step, with CI cost considered) is decided after step 1, as before.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_16 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 16u32, "2026-10-25T18:17Z", "fiscal", "14:3x fiscal: step-3 APIs read from source (exploration fetches, read-only, nothing built or run).\nDFe.NET (commit 08743cd, 2026-09-24; blobless sparse clone at session scratchpad fetch-dfe-net-141515):\n- parse: DFe.Utils FuncoesXml.XmlStringParaClasse<T>(string xml, bool ignorarOrdenacaoElementos = false), an XmlSerializer cached per type, so the first call per type pays the build and the warm-up runs absorb it. T = NFe.Classes.NFe, whose [XmlRoot(Namespace = \"http://www.portalfiscal.inf.br/nfe\")] matches our examples' root.\n- serialize: FuncoesXml.ClasseParaXmlString<T>(T), which serializes, reloads through XElement, strips xsd/xsi attributes and re-parses; that double parse is timed as-is.\n- validate: NFe.Utils.Validacao.Validador.Valida(ServicoNFe, VersaoServico, string xml, bool validarLote, string pathSchema) : string[]. It builds a new XmlSchemaSet and loads the schema file on every call, and it suppresses two known official-schema defects (hashTentativaEntrega length; tcOrgaoIBGE declared twice), so a verdict that differs from the raw-XSD referee only there is an A3 exception, not a disagreement.\nACBr (trunk2 r48801, last changed 2026-10-23; svn export of Fontes/ACBrDFe/ACBrNFe, 60 units, at fetch-acbr-141640):\n- parse: TNotasFiscais.LoadFromString(const AXMLString: String; AGerarNFe: Boolean = False): Boolean.\n- serialize: NotaFiscal.GerarXML: String.\n- validate: NotaFiscal.Validar, a procedure that raises EACBrNFeException on failure. It validates the signed XML or else XMLOriginal, so unsigned documents ARE validated (corrects n14's 'expects a signed NF-e', which came from a forum flow), through SSL.Validar(xml, GerarNomeArqSchema(layout, version), Erro), i.e. the configured XML library, libxml2 under FPC.\n- extra: ValidarRegrasdeNegocios(out Erros): Boolean, SEFAZ business rules beyond the XSD, which no other library here has (reported as its own row, never compared).\nRule refinement for n14: both libraries get their schema folder pointed at the official XSDs in deps/corpus (ACBr Configuracoes.Arquivos.PathSchemas; DFe.NET pathSchema), so each validator is compared on the same schemas as the referee. ACBr also needs libxml2 (and the OpenSSL DLLs its SSL layer loads), recorded as a step-3 toolchain prerequisite.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_17 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 17u32, "2026-10-25T18:21Z", "fiscal", "14:4x fiscal: ACBr build closure for step 3, static (session scratchpad delphi-survey/uses_closure.py <tree> <root unit>: uses-clause BFS from a root unit, comments stripped; {$IFDEF} branches are NOT evaluated, so every conditional branch counts and the external list over-approximates). From ACBrNFe over Fontes/ACBrDFe/ACBrNFe alone: 28 of 60 units reached; the 60 also hold DANFE/report units (Fortes RL*, FastReport frx*, LazReport LR_*, Qt, FMX) that the root never reaches, so the benchmark compiles no printing code. A second read-only export (47 s) added Fontes/{ACBrComum, PCNComum, ACBrIntegrador, ACBrOpenSSL, ACBrLibXML2, ACBrDFe top-level files + Comum + small DF-e folders, Terceiros/synalist, Terceiros/LibXmlSec}: 270 units, 107 reached. Still outside, ACBr/third-party: ACBrValidador, ACBrIBGE, ACBrMail and ACBrWin{HTTP,INet}ReqResp/ACBrWinReqRespClass (expected in ACBrDiversos/ACBrTCP); ACBr_WinCrypt/BCrypt/NCrypt, ACBrCAPICOM_TLB, ACBrMSXML2_TLB (Windows crypto/COM, expected in ACBrCapicom, used by the non-OpenSSL SSL back ends); Terceiros ZLibExGZ/GZIPUtils, JsonDataObjects (JsonDataObjects_ACBr), json4delphi (Jsons), FastStringReplace (StrUtilsEx). The rest is platform-conditional (Posix.*, Libc, FMX.*, System.Net.*, System.Generics.* for Delphi; FPC's own Registry, WinInet, Dos, LConvEncoding, ctypes). Next, when step 3 builds: export ACBrDiversos, ACBrTCP, ACBrCapicom and those 5 Terceiros folders, then let fpc -vu name what is really missing under {$mode delphi} on win64. The static list is only the starting set.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_18 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 18u32, "2026-10-25T22:07Z", "fiscal", "19:0x fiscal: first real Delphi build of the lib arms, on the remote kit worker node (FPC 3.3.1-21298, core guard ok). The Spiral side went through: type check, partial evaluation and Delphi codegen of the smoke with xml-delphi-arms + delphi-arms applied. fpc then rejected one line: a COMPILER codegen gap (StringIndex on a one-character literal emits 'z'[i]; Pascal treats a one-char literal as Char), not a lib-arm bug. It is filed with a fix draft as DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX. The literal comes from the smoke's own sm'.index_of \"z\" ..., inlined through portable_index_of. A first hypothesis (parenthesized indexing (s)[i] in my macros) was tested with fpc and FALSIFIED (it compiles and runs); the real line was captured during a rerun by a watcher, because Cells deletes its temp folder. Next: re-run the smoke once the fix is packed and installed, or now with a variable search argument in place of the literal to get past this line and find the next gap.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_19 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 19u32, "2026-10-25T22:20Z", "fiscal", "19:1x fiscal: Delphi probing on the node, 3 more rounds (FPC 3.3.1-21298; each failing main.pas line captured by a watcher during the run).\n(1) With the smoke's 1-char literal made runtime (:= ~needle = \"z\"), the next failure was the SAME gap inside lib: xml.local_name's sm'.index_of \":\" name emits ':'[v1 + 1]. Every generated module reaches it through with_namespace, so DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX is a hard prerequisite for any generated module on Delphi; main has it staged for pack 5n.\n(2) With local_name dropped from the probe copy only, the next failure was backend_switch_has_no_Delphi_arm_in_lib_spiral in file_system's (</>) path join: sm'.ends_with (for \"/\" and \"\\\") had no Delphi key, and EOIE-BACKEND-REACH's prototype missed it because it does not resolve operator definitions like (</>). Fixed in the delphi-arms slice: sm'.ends_with / starts_with gain Delphi arms through new portable_ends_with / portable_starts_with, which the C arms now share (inline, so C output stays identical); dry run ok, format-clean.\n(3) Probing further is gated: (</>) indexes the literal \"/\", which is the literal-index gap again, and lib indexes one-char literals on many paths. Rule, fixed now: resume the smoke when pack 5n (DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX) is installed, then run the full generated_mdfe_v1_00 module (delphi-step1.ps1); every new gap is fixed in the slice and re-run.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_20 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 20u32, "2026-10-25T23:32Z", "fiscal", "ACBr step-3 build closure completed statically (2026-10-25 19:32, ACBr svn r48801, session scratchpad fetch-acbr-141640 + delphi-survey/uses_closure.py). Note 18's remaining folders (ACBrDiversos, ACBrTCP, ACBrCapicom, Terceiros ZLibExGZ/GZIPUtils/JsonDataObjects/json4delphi/FastStringReplace) were already exported; the 6 Windows crypto/COM units were not in ACBrCapicom but in Terceiros/Ole (ACBrCAPICOM_TLB, ACBrMSXML2_TLB) and Terceiros/CodeGear (ACBr_WinCrypt, ACBr_BCrypt, ACBr_NCrypt, ACBr_WinHttp), now exported. From ACBrNFe: 137 of 351 local units reached; ZERO ACBr or Terceiros units remain outside the export. What is left outside is only platform/toolchain units (Posix.*, FMX.*, Libc, QDialogs, System.Generics.*/System.Net.* for Delphi; Dos, Registry, WinInet, Sockets, ctypes, UnixUtil for FPC). New prerequisite found: under {$IfDef FPC} the core ACBrUtil.* units (Base, Strings, FilesIO, DateTime, Math, Compatibilidade, ACBrUtil) unconditionally use LazUTF8, LConvEncoding and LCLType, which are Lazarus units (LazUtils + LCL), not FPC; the closure script's RTL filter hid LazUTF8/LCLType, so this was invisible in note 18. Plain FPC 3.3.1 has 0 of them and no Lazarus is installed, so step 3 needs Lazarus sources (lazutils + lcl/lcltype) on the fpc unit path, or a Lazarus install, as a toolchain prerequisite next to libxml2/OpenSSL. Next for step 3, still CPU-light: fetch Lazarus sources matching FPC 3.3.1 (trunk) and check whether lcltype compiles standalone without the rest of the LCL.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_21 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 21u32, "2026-10-25T23:36Z", "fiscal", "Lazarus prerequisite sized (2026-10-25 19:36): it is 7 units, not a Lazarus install. Sparse clone of gitlab.com/freepascal.org/lazarus/lazarus at ebadd0b (2026-10-24), components/lazutils + lcl only (673 units, 72 s; session scratchpad fetch-lazarus-193304). uses_closure.py now indexes .pp as well as .pas (Lazarus names units .pp; ACBrNFe closure unchanged at 137, a positive control). Closures: LCLType -> lcltype + wsreferences (its UTF8BIDI use is under {$IFDEF USE_UTF8BIDI_LCL}, off by default); LazUTF8 -> lazutf8 + fpcadds; LConvEncoding -> lconvencoding + lazutf8 + fpcadds + codepagesasian + codepagescommon. Everything else they reference is FPC itself (Classes, SysUtils, StrUtils, Windows, dos, System.UITypes) or Unix-only and platform-conditional (cwstring, iconvenc). So step 3's ACBr build on FPC 3.3.1 win64 = ACBr r48801 export (137 units reached from ACBrNFe) + these 7 Lazarus units via -Fu/-Fi on lazutils and lcl, + libxml2 and OpenSSL DLLs at run time. Still static: fpc -vu on the real build decides; no compile run here.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_22 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 22u32, "2026-10-25T23:42Z", "fiscal", "DFe.NET step-3 harness drafted, unbuilt (2026-10-25 19:42; session scratchpad bench-dfe-net/{Bench.csproj,Program.cs}). Build closure read from the csproj files (sparse cone widened, read-only, still commit 08743cd): NFe.Utils -> NFe.Classes -> DFe.Utils -> DFe.Classes, all net462;netstandard2.0;net6.0, one package System.Security.Cryptography.Xml 6.0.1; the harness targets net11.0 and pins the references to their net6.0 build. Types: DFe.Utils.FuncoesXml, NFe.Classes.NFe, NFe.Classes.Servicos.Tipos.ServicoNFe, DFe.Classes.Flags.VersaoServico (Versao100/200/300/310/400), NFe.Utils.Validacao.Validador. Harness: Bench <official schema folder> <out.tsv> <documents...>; per document parse (XmlStringParaClasse<NFe>), serialize (ClasseParaXmlString) and validate (Valida(NFeAutorizacao, version, xml, validarLote: false, schemas)), each 5 warm-ups + median of 31 single-threaded Stopwatch runs (n14's speed rule); A2 verdict = XNode.DeepEquals after dropping namespace declarations and trimming text (prefix placement differs by serializer); version from infNFe/@versao. Validador maps only 3.10/4.00 to a schema file (any other version falls back to nfe_v3.10.xsd), so v2.00 validation is reported 'unsupported', never measured against the wrong schema (n14: rows say unsupported instead of being dropped). Not built: n13 forbids building/executing fetched code before the deps/ fetch decision after step 1. CT-e/MDF-e rows need CTe.Classes/MDFe.Classes + Utils in the cone (same pattern). Next draft: the ACBr stopgap Pascal harness (LoadFromString / GerarXML / Validar) on the same TSV shape.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_23 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 23u32, "2026-10-25T23:48Z", "fiscal", "ACBr stopgap harness drafted + one shared A2 comparer, proven on controls (2026-10-25 19:48). (1) session scratchpad bench-acbr/bench_acbr.lpr (n14's stopgap until DELPHI-USES-UNITS lets Spiral drive ACBr): FPC {$mode delphi} + modeswitches functionreferences/anonymousfunctions (FPC 3.3); names read from the r48801 export: TACBrNFe.Create(nil); Configuracoes.Geral.SSLLib := libOpenSSL and SSLXmlSignLib := xsLibXml2 set EXPLICITLY, because libOpenSSL defaults to xsXmlSec unless DFE_SEM_XMLSEC (ACBrDFeConfiguracoes.pas:630-640), so n15's 'libxml2 under FPC' only holds when set; Configuracoes.Arquivos.PathSchemas := the official XSD folder (n15 rule); Configuracoes.Geral.VersaoDF from infNFe/@versao (TpcnVersaoDF = ve200/ve300/ve310/ve400, so ACBr covers 2.00 where DFe.NET's validator does not); parse = NotasFiscais.Clear + LoadFromString, serialize = NotasFiscais[0].GerarXML, validate = NotasFiscais[0].Validar with EACBrNFeException -> invalid; QueryPerformanceCounter, 5 warm-ups + median of 31; same TSV columns as the DFe.NET harness. Not built (n13). (2) A2 is ONE method for every library (one record per fact): both harnesses write their serialized output as <library>-<document> next to the TSV, and bench-compare/compare-roundtrip.ps1 -Documents -Written -Out compares each to its source after dropping namespace declarations and trimming text, reporting the first differing path; the DFe.NET harness's inline comparison was removed. Controls on samples/generated_nfe_v3_10_example.xml: identical copy -> roundtrip-equal; cNF changed -> roundtrip-differs at /NFe/infNFe/ide/cNF: text '00000000' vs '99999999'. Two PowerShell traps hit and fixed on the way: '-split, 2' comma precedence, and a foreach variable $written shadowing the [string] parameter $Written (case-insensitive names coerce each FileInfo to a string).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_24 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 24u32, "2026-10-26T16:07Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 12:07 fiscal: DFe.NET harness extended from NF-e only to all 13 BR documents (session scratchpad bench-dfe-net/{Program.cs,Bench.csproj}; still unbuilt per n13). Sparse cone widened read-only (still commit 08743cd): CTe.Classes (274 .cs), CTe.Utils (27), MDFe.Classes (124), MDFe.Utils (4), DFe.Wsdl (28). The build closure is complete inside the cone: CTe.Utils -> CTe.Classes -> DFe.Classes/DFe.Utils; MDFe.Classes -> MDFe.Utils -> DFe.Classes/DFe.Utils/DFe.Wsdl (System.Net.Http 4.3.4)/NFe.Utils; all net462;netstandard2.0;net6.0. Dispatch is on the root element; the version comes from the first inf* element with @versao (checked on all 13 samples). Roots: NFe -> NFe.Classes.NFe; CTe -> CTe.Classes.CTe; CTeOS -> CTe.CTeOSClasses.CTeOS; CTeSimp -> CTe.Classes.Simplificado.cteSimp (XmlRoot 'CTeSimp'); MDFe -> MDFe.Classes.Informacoes.MDFe. GTVe has no DFe.NET class, so its rows read 'unsupported:GTVe' for all three operations (n14: rows say unsupported). Validators: CT-e and MDF-e are 'Valida(string xml, string schemaFile, config)', which returns void and THROWS on the first ValidationEventArgs, warnings included. So their A3 verdict is 'no exception' plus the last message line, and a warning-only throw is an A3 exception class, not a disagreement. The CT-e config is new ConfiguracaoServico { DiretorioSchemas, VersaoLayout = versao.ve200/ve300/ve400 }; the MDF-e config is new MDFeConfiguracao { CaminhoSchemas }. Schema files follow family-gates' names (cte_v*, cteOS_v*, cteSimp_v4.00, mdfe_v*), under each family's own official folder; the first argument is now the deps/corpus root (NFe.AppTeste/Schemas, CTe.AppTeste/schemas, MDFe.AppTeste/Schemas). Trap found by reading: MDFeIde formats from the GLOBAL MDFeConfiguracao.Instancia.VersaoWebService.VersaoLayout unless given a version, so the harness sets it per document (1.00 / 3.00) before parse and serialize are timed. CT-e classes have no global-version hooks (0 Instancia.Versao uses). The three Validador classes share a name, so they are fully qualified. Coverage: parse + serialize for 11 of 13 documents; validate for 10 (NF-e 2.00 is unsupported in DFe.NET's validator; GTV-e x2 is unsupported).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_25 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 25u32, "2026-10-26T16:12Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 12:12 fiscal: ACBr stopgap harness extended from NF-e only to all 13 BR documents (session scratchpad bench-acbr/bench_acbr.lpr; unbuilt per n13). New read-only svn export at r48801 (same revision as ACBrNFe): Fontes/ACBrDFe/ACBrCTe (39 units) + ACBrMDFe (37) in the session scratchpad fetch-acbr-ctemdfe-120817. APIs read from source: TACBrCTe.Conhecimentos (LoadFromString / Items[0].GerarXML / Validar), TModeloCTe = (moCTe, moGTVe, moCTeOS, moCTeSimp), TVersaoCTe = (ve200, ve300, ve400); TACBrMDFe.Manifestos (same three calls), TVersaoMDFe = (ve100, ve300). Coverage beats DFe.NET: ACBr models GTV-e (modelo 64), so ACBr has 13 of 13 documents for parse / serialize / validate, against DFe.NET's 11 / 11 / 10. Traps read in the source: (a) CT-e GerarXML writes with the CONFIGURED Geral.VersaoDF (ACBrCTeConhecimentos.pas:742), while Validar picks its schema from the document (infCTe.Versao); so the harness sets VersaoDF AND ModeloDF per document before timing; (b) MDF-e's writer takes VersaoDF from the document itself (pmdfeMDFeW.pas:187), but it is set anyway for symmetry; (c) LoadFromString detects the model by the closing tag ('</CTeOS>', '</GTVe>', '</CTeSimp>', else CTe), and the harness dispatches the same way (longer tags first); (d) ve200 / ve300 / ve400 exist in several units, so values are type-qualified (TVersaoCTe.ve200), and TVersaoCTe / TModeloCTe are declared twice (ACBrCTe.Conversao, new, and pcteConversaoCTe, old PCN), so which one Configuracoes.Geral uses is settled at the first build. Each family's component gets the same explicit SSLLib libOpenSSL + SSLXmlSignLib xsLibXml2 and its own official schema folder (the first argument is now the deps/corpus root, matching the DFe.NET harness). The A2 comparer (bench-compare) is unchanged: it already takes any <library>-<document> output.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_26 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 26u32, "2026-10-26T16:52Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 12:52 fiscal: 'ours' row of step 3, designed from the generated API (read on generated_cfdi_v4_0, unbuilt). A generated module has no whole-document validator: its reader x_<root>_of_xml turns constrained strings into validated nominals through the per-type x_<t>_is_valid checks, and its writer is x_<root>_attributes / x_<root>_children -> xml.element -> xml.write_node (the same path its own *_writes_its_example test uses). So for ours, parse = xml.read_xml + x_<root>_of_xml; serialize = the writer path; 'validate' = the reader's verdict. It is reported as 'reader-checked (facets + structure the reader enforces)', NOT as an XSD validator, so A3 compares it to the referee only where those checks reach (no false claim of full content-model validation). Shape: one bench driver per family under fiscal bench/ (a separate package importing one generated module), so the generated modules and their identity gates stay unchanged; the same TSV columns; A2 through bench-compare like the other libraries. Timing gap found in lib: benchmark.run' uses am'.parallel_map (breaks n14's single-thread rule) and millisecond resolution (sub-ms parses would read 0), and date_time has only stopwatch_elapsed_milliseconds. Drafted the lib fix (session scratchpad bench-ours/micros_transform.py -> draft date_time.spi EF51C8C9 over 6D530E6A): stopwatch_elapsed_microseconds, Rust as_secs + subsec_micros with arithmetic in Spiral (granular), fallback Elapsed.Ticks / 10 (TimeSpan 100 ns ticks, not the frequency-dependent ElapsedTicks); test stopwatch_microseconds_agree_with_milliseconds (non-negative, monotone, the floor-ms reading never ahead of a later us reading); 0 shadow clashes. The Delphi row also needs a stopwatch Delphi arm (stopwatch has no Delphi key, so backend_switch would emit the no-Delphi-arm identifier), which goes into the delphi-arms slice when step 1 resumes. Proof: date_time suite on the node when a slot frees.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_27 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 27u32, "2026-10-26T16:55Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 12:54 fiscal: ours-row driver shape refined after reading the entry points (generated_cfdi_v4_0: 'x_comprobante_of_xml (c : xml.xml_node) : x_comprobante' and 'x_comprobante_to_xml (name : string) (x : x_comprobante) : xml.xml_node'; each generated module is its own single-file package, '///- --package . --timeout 1200000'). A separate bench package would have to import a single-file package, so the driver instead takes a scratch COPY of generated_<family>.spi and appends one '///> rust' bench test (read the sample with file_system.read_all_text; parse = xml.read_xml + x_<root>_of_xml; serialize = xml.write_node (x_<root>_to_xml '<Root>' parsed); 5 warm-ups + median of 31 via stopwatch_elapsed_microseconds; TSV rows on stdout). The repo's generated output stays byte-identical and Cells compiles the copy as usual. The reader fails hard on invalid input (on Rust, failwith panics, which Spiral cannot catch), so the 'reader-checked' verdict is measured only on documents expected valid, which all 13 corpus samples are; that limit is reported in the row. The block needs compile iterations, so it is drafted when a slot frees, together with the microseconds lib proof.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_28 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 28u32, "2026-10-26T18:26Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 14:26 fiscal: step-1 readiness re-checked against TODAY's lib, so the Delphi slices are ready when pack 5n installs. (1) Pins: sm' 2E259892 and env 8A982F53 unchanged; xml.spi 9980B7AC unchanged; file_system MOVED 7DB84A4B -> ACF6BC84. A per-anchor check (fiscal session scratchpad delphi-arms/anchor_check.py) shows every slice anchor, including all 6 in file_system, still matches exactly once, so the move is outside the slice's edit sites. Pin updated in delphi-arms/apply.py; its dry run is ok on 3 files. (2) Both slices applied to a temp lib copy and TYPE-CHECKED in seconds with the installed compiler ('SpiralCompiler.dll fsharp <m>.spi'; it checks every definition, including unreached arms, per the date_time control): xml exit 0, sm' exit 0, file_system exit 0. env has no entry expression (the live baseline also says 'Cannot find main'), but file_system references env. 3x and type-checked with the patched env in the same copy, so env is covered via its dependent. The slices' Spiral-side code (UTF-8 decoders, portable_index_of / starts_with / ends_with, directory_parent, find_parent's ++# message) therefore type-checks on today's lib; the Delphi arms themselves are only proven by step 1's fpc build. (3) Main's pending Nil batch on xml.spi: the xml slice has 0 anchors containing 'Nil', so it survives that landing; its remaining guard is the CRLF check. Step 1 remains: delphi-step1.ps1 after pack 5n (DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_29 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 29u32, "2026-10-26T22:12Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 18:12 fiscal: ours-row driver BUILT and TYPE-CHECKED without a compile slot. Generic appender (session scratchpad bench-ours/append_bench.py <generated.spi> <family> <root type> <root element> <out>) adds a top-level inl <family>_bench () plus a one-line rust test calling it, in the generated module's own dialect (inl / let rec / match, sm'.(++#) concat): read the family sample via sample_path; per iteration xml.read_xml + x_<root>_of_xml (parse) then xml.write_node (x_<root>_to_xml) (serialize), timed with date_time.stopwatch_elapsed_microseconds; 5 dyn warm-ups then 31 runs, insertion-sorted, median = element 15; serialized bytes summed into the row so neither phase is dead code; one TSV row (family, parse_us, write_us, runs, bytes) on stdout. As an ordinary definition it is checked by the fast fsharp check: appended to the 6966-line generated_cfdi_v4_0 in a lib copy carrying every landing (incl. the microsecond stopwatch), exit 0 with 0 errors after one fix (sm'.length is dim-polymorphic, so it yields i64 directly; a conv around it left a metavar, ETY-ValueRestriction). Node run queued: job-1793052770934-fiscal-bench-ours-cfdi after the UBL proof; runner bench-ours/run-bench.ps1 -Family <f> (memo off; installs the transformed date_time into the integ mirror only for the run and restores it hash-verified in finally; bench package at fiscal/bench/<f>, which build.ps1 never discovers, removed after). The date_time transform applies to the mirror's older base (2E011521) as well as live; its one over-120 line is a pre-existing Zig string on both bases.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_30 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 30u32, "2026-10-26T23:16Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 19:16 fiscal: FIRST OURS ROW MEASURED (job-1793052770934, node, memo off, 3/3 tests): generated_cfdi_v4_0 parse_us 416, write_us 328 (medians of 31 after 5 warm-ups, Rust, microsecond stopwatch), 17391 bytes over 31 writes = 561 bytes per document vs the 597-byte sample (the sample test adds the xmldsig namespace via xml.with_namespace; the round-trip writer writes the plain root, so compare documents namespace-normalized in A3). Mirror date_time restored hash-verified. Root map for the other 15 families derived from the generated modules themselves (reader whose underscore-free name equals x + lowercased root_type; one hit each): session scratchpad bench-ours/families.tsv (e.g. GTVe -> x_tgt_ve, FatturaElettronica -> x_fattura_elettronica_type, Invoice -> x_invoice_type). UBL's row waits on XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS (its module does not compile).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_31 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 31u32, "2026-10-27T00:14Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:14 fiscal: ours rows for the other 14 families staged and queued: append_bench.py derived every one from the integ mirror's generated modules with its families.tsv root (no refusals: every family has its <root>_of_xml / _to_xml / sample_path anchors; 349 KB FatturaPA to 1.5 MB CT-e v4), pushed with run-bench-all.ps1 (one serial runner looping run-bench.ps1, memo off, date_time swapped and restored per family) as job-1793060123777-fiscal-bench-ours-all on the node after the recursive-groups proof. UBL waits on XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS. Output: one TSV row per family (parse_us, write_us, runs, bytes) for the A2/A3 comparison against ACBr and DFe.NET.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_32 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 32u32, "2026-10-27T00:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:44 fiscal: Delphi step-1 blocker re-checked at the source: DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX's verified fix (note 3; anchored apply script delivered in note 4) is NOT yet in the compiler: spiral/apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs:165463 still reads the old form | StringIndex, [a;b] -> {tup a}[{tup b} + 1], exactly the line note 4 predicted; installed SpiralCompiler.dll is from 10-26 08:19. So delphi-step1.ps1 still waits on pack 5n landing that one line and installing; the ours rows (15 families, node job fiscal-bench-ours-all2) do not depend on it.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_33 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 33u32, "2026-10-27T04:19Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 00:19 fiscal: OURS ROWS for 12 of 15 families (node job-1793060915958, Rust, memo off, medians of 31 after 5 warm-ups, microsecond stopwatch). parse_us / write_us: CFDI 4.0 344/264; CT-e v2 1634/1325, v3 1675/1330, v4 1487/1167; CT-e OS v3 1381/1117, v4 1379/1071; GTVe v3 1831/1610, v4 629/596; MDF-e v1 827/641, v3 919/698; FatturaPA 1070/804; KSeF FA(3) 1368/989. Round-trip (namespace-normalized against each family's own sample): equal for 7 (CFDI, CT-e OS v3/v4, FatturaPA, GTVe v3/v4, KSeF); 5 lose infModal/modal (CT-e v2-v4, MDF-e v1/v3) -> XSD-ANY-CONTENT-READER. Not measured, 3: NF-e v2.00, v3.10 and CT-e Simp v4.00 cannot read their own examples (a choice whose alternatives can all be empty) -> XSD-CHOICE-EMPTIABLE-READER (fix drafted); UBL waits on XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS. Note GTVe v4 measured much faster than v3 over the same bytes; it is a single run, so re-measure before reading anything into it. INCIDENT in my own runner, now fixed: run-bench.ps1 reused the variable name saved for the saved XML document, which overwrote the path of the date_time backup, so each family's finally 'restored' its example XML into the mirror's lib/spiral/date_time.spi (every restore line said MISMATCH). Measurements are valid (the correct date_time was installed before each run), but the mirror lib was broken from the 4th family on, which also voided the root-fallback check and rg-proof2. Repaired: mirror date_time back to 2E011521 from the copy fetched earlier, rg-proof2 stopped and its own restore run by hand from its work backup (xsd_emit C9734987, 64 generated, 16 samples), poisoned backup removed; run-bench.ps1 now names it dateTimeBackup and refuses to restore anything that is not a date_time module (control: refuses XML, accepts the real file).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_34 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 34u32, "2026-10-27T07:30Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 03:30 fiscal: estimate set to 390m remaining, from the open milestones in these notes: Delphi step 1 (delphi-step1.ps1 once pack 5n installs DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX, plus the fallout of the first fpc build) 120m; ACBr harness bench_acbr.lpr build + run over the 13 documents (traps in note 23: VersaoDF/ModeloDF per document, model by closing tag) 120m; DFe.NET harness Bench.csproj build + run (11 docs) 60m; ours rows re-measured after XSD-ANY-CONTENT-READER lands (15 of 16 families measure today; UBL waits on XSD-IMPORTED-NAMESPACE-ELEMENTS) 30m; A2/A3 cross-library comparison through bench-compare and the report 60m.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_35 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 35u32, "2026-10-27T07:55Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 03:55 fiscal: work in progress moved into the repo, unstaged, per the user's practice correction (WIP lives in its proper repo location from the start; scratch is only for throwaway proof copies). Now in fiscal/bench/ (untracked): acbr/bench_acbr.lpr (ACBr harness, 13 documents), dfe-net/Program.cs + Bench.csproj (DFe.NET harness), compare-roundtrip.ps1 (namespace-normalized round-trip comparator), ours-families.tsv (each family's generated root reader and element). Checked first: no absolute or scratch paths in any of them; moved hash-verified and removed from scratch. Kept in scratch on purpose: run-bench.ps1 / run-bench-all.ps1 (proof runners hard-wired to the node mirror). NOT moved, recorded instead: append_bench.py (adds the ours-bench function to a generated module). fiscal removed Python on purpose (FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE strand 2), so the right home is the generator itself: generator.spi emits <family>_bench () into each generated module next to its example test, which also removes the per-run append step. That port is the next bench task.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_36 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 36u32, "2026-10-27T08:30Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 04:30 fiscal: append_bench.py PORTED into the generator, in the tree unstaged (fiscal/src/generator.spi; backup in the session scratchpad backups/generator-bench-20261027-0424). The generator now emits a test <module>_bench_runs into every generated module whose family has no known defect: 5 warm-up + 31 timed parse/write rounds over its own example, prints '<module> parse_us N write_us N runs 31 bytes N' (median, space-separated so no backslash escape is needed in generator strings), then writes the re-serialized document to target/bench/ours-<module>_example.xml (creating the folder) for bench/compare-roundtrip.ps1. The example test and the bench share two new helpers, example_document family value and signed_text expression, so the example test's emitted text is unchanged by construction. Checked: generator + probe type-check 0 errors on the combined lib (emptiable + xs:any + namespace drafts), no new over-120 lines, installed formatter no-op. Next: the regeneration proof shows the example tests byte-identical and every bench row present; then run-bench.ps1 / append_bench.py retire.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_37 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 37u32, "2026-10-27T09:04Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 05:04 fiscal: the Delphi backend arms (step-1 lib slices) are IN THE TREE, unstaged, per the user's WIP-in-repo practice: lib/spiral/sm'.spi EB31A33E, env.spi 368F9BAE, file_system.spi 749056BD. Before landing: all three slice bases had moved since 10-26 (sm' 2E259892 -> 9459C71F, env 8A982F53 -> ACFFB30C, file_system ACF6BC84 -> 9FA37D04), so the per-anchor check ran again: all 14 anchors still match exactly once. Re-pinned, applied to a fresh lib copy and type-checked with the installed compiler: sm', file_system, xml, xsd_emit, date_time 0 errors; env 0 located errors (its exit 5 is the live baseline's 'no entry expression'). No Cells reader was live (0 erl processes); backup + spiral's git diff --cached in the session scratchpad backups/delphi-arms-20261027-0503; staged diff verified unchanged; installed formatter no-op on all three. Step 1's remaining precondition is unchanged: the first fpc build of the Delphi output needs pack 5n (DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX, still 70%, not installed: compiler of 10-26 08:19).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_38 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 38u32, "2026-10-27T09:44Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 05:45 fiscal: DFe.NET HARNESS MILESTONE DONE (A2/A3 rows for DFe.NET). Harness made buildable and run in the tree (fiscal/bench/dfe-net, unstaged): (1) its dependency moved out of the session scratchpad: DFe.NET library sources now live at fiscal/deps/bench/DFe.NET (ignored, like every deps/), a sparse clone PINNED at 08743cd5f5b82769a26ad9ae0093b1b0ae60c74f so bench numbers stay reproducible, and scripts/init.ps1 fetches exactly that (new deps/bench/DFe.NET block); Bench.csproj's default DfeNetRoot points there (the old default was a scratch-relative path that my 03:55 absolute-path check missed); (2) .gitignore ignores /bench/*/bin/ and /bench/*/obj/; (3) three compile fixes: ImplicitUsings, MDFe's own VersaoServico enum (alias MdfeVersion; DFe.Classes.Flags has a same-named enum), and per-document failure verdicts (parse/serialize exceptions become 'failed:<innermost message>' rows instead of killing the run). Run on this box over the 11 BR samples, 5 warm-ups + 31 timed runs, medians in us (parse / serialize / validate): CT-e OS v3 354/1026/95315; CT-e OS v4 525/1419/146971; CT-e Simp v4 200/415/144299; CT-e v2 417/1346/35220; CT-e v3 355/1193/42049; CT-e v4 341/1081/63404; MDF-e v1 205/829/11293; MDF-e v3 193/899/16363; NF-e v2 496/1492/(validation unsupported by DFe.NET); NF-e v3.10 489/1236/41541; NF-e v4 (nfe_v4_example.xml) REFUSED at parse: \"Instance validation error: '00' is not a valid value for FormaPagamento\" although the sample validates against the schema with 0 messages, so DFe.NET's enum is narrower than the XSD. ROUND TRIP (bench/compare-roundtrip.ps1, target/bench/dfe-net-roundtrip.tsv): 0 of 10 equal. Defects found: (a) CT-e v2/v3/v4 and MDF-e v1 write dhEmi '2088-02-29T20:00:00' as '2088-02-29T20:00:29': the SECONDS become the day of month (a 'dd' where 'ss' belongs), a real data change; (b) CT-e OS v3/v4 and MDF-e v3 rewrite '+00:00' to the machine's local offset ('16:00:00-04:00'): same instant, different text, so a signed document's bytes change; (c) NF-e v2/v3.10 write cMunFG '0000000' as '0' (leading zeros lost); (d) CT-e Simp v4 drops infCte's Id and versao attributes. Ours on the same documents: NF-e v2/v3.10 round-trip EQUAL; CT-e/MDF-e differ only by infModal/modal (XSD-ANY-CONTENT-READER, proof queued on the node). TIMING CAVEAT: ours rows were measured on the worker node and these on the review box, so the speed comparison is not valid until both run on one box (next: run ours' generated bench tests on this box, or this harness on the node).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_39 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 39u32, "2026-10-27T09:48Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 05:50 fiscal: estimate 390m -> 330m remaining (the DFe.NET harness milestone, 60m, is done: note 38). Same-box timing queued locally as job-1793094391561-fiscal-local-bench (one serial runner, memo off): regenerate with the tree generator (its new <module>_bench_runs tests), build the 10 NF-e/CT-e/MDF-e units so ours prints its rows, then run the DFe.NET harness over the same samples IMMEDIATELY AFTER in the same job, so both libraries are measured on one box under the same load (note 38's DFe.NET numbers and the earlier ours rows came from two machines and are not comparable). It waits for a local heavy slot (both held: kit's release, main's proof). Remaining milestones: Delphi step 1 after pack 5n 120m; ACBr harness build + run 120m; the A2/A3 comparison report 60m; ours rows re-measured after xs:any lands 30m.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_40 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 40u32, "2026-10-27T10:34Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 06:36 fiscal: ACBr HARNESS MILESTONE DONE: built and run for the first time. (1) Sources moved out of the session scratchpad into fiscal/deps/bench (ignored): ACBr/Fontes at trunk2 r48801 (svn layout; CT-e and MDF-e exports merged under Fontes/ACBrDFe; the duplicate top-level ACBrNFe export dropped after a byte-identity check), lazarus at ebadd0b458c6 (lazutils + lcl); scripts/init.ps1 fetches both, pinned (svn export of exactly the 27 folders on disk, checked 27/27, plus the ACBrDFe root units; a sparse git checkout for Lazarus); every scratch fetch was verified file by file in deps and deleted. (2) bench/acbr/build.ps1 (new, unstaged): fpc 3.3.1 cross to win64 (-Px86_64 -Twin64: the scoop host compiler is i386 and the first build was 32-bit), -Mdelphi -O2 -dNOGUI (ACBr.inc's text-mode switch: without it the link pulled LCL widget units and failed on WSRegister* symbols, which the 10-25 static closure of 7 Lazarus units did not show), every ACBr source folder except the DANFE/DACTE/DAMDFE rendering-engine subfolders (their base report units stay, ACBrNFe needs ACBrDFeDANFeReport); output target/bench/acbr/bench_acbr.exe (3.9 MB), 96 s from clean. (3) Harness fixes: r48801 keeps TWO copies of each enum (ACBrNFe.Conversao vs pcnConversaoNFe, ACBrCTe.Conversao vs pcteConversaoCTe) and the components still take the legacy pcn/pcte ones, so the harness uses those, plus the ACBrDFe unit. Run on the review box over all 13 BR samples (GTV-e included, which DFe.NET cannot model), runtime DLLs from PATH (libxml2-2.dll from emacs 30.2 bin, OpenSSL 3 from calibre's bin; a pinned runtime folder is a follow-up): 39 rows, 55 s, medians us parse / serialize: CT-e OS v3 1060/834, v4 853/826; CT-e Simp 1581/1426; CT-e v2 1312/917, v3 1205/965, v4 1284/1137; GTV-e v3 1312/1341, v4 1511/1228; MDF-e v1 645/708, v3 688/731; NF-e v2 1604/1307, v3.10 1512/1683, v4 1567/1953. Validate: GTV-e v3/v4 valid, the other 11 invalid, with messages like NroRegEstadual '' failing [0-9]{25} although our samples validate with 0 messages: ACBr validates its OWN re-serialized document, not the input. Round trip: 0 of 13 equal, all first differing at infX/@Id, because ACBr RECOMPUTES the access key from ide fields while our synthetic examples carry an all-zeros key; that is ACBr's design, not a defect, and it masks later differences (the comparator stops at the first). Follow-ups for the A2 report: give examples a key consistent with their ide fields, or let compare-roundtrip report every difference; also pin the runtime DLLs. Same-box timing caveat (n38) applies.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_41 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 41u32, "2026-10-27T11:40Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 07:40 fiscal: the generator's bench test is PROVEN on the node (ns-proof, XSD-IMPORTED-NAMESPACE-ELEMENTS note of this time): 15 of 16 families print '<module> parse_us N write_us N runs 31 bytes N' from their own generated test and round-trip equal; UBL waits on its compile time. append_bench.py and the scratch run-bench runners are now superseded.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_42 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 42u32, "2026-10-27T21:33Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 17:32 fiscal: from main (nil gate job-1793105884062): lib sm' show_floats and show_floats_in_python_repr_layout disagree on the Delphi target only, on the unchanged tree; flagged to the compiler lane. Relevance here: fiscal's generated codecs keep decimals as text, so they print no floats; the bench's timing output might. Check when Delphi step 1 resumes.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_43 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 43u32, "2026-10-27T21:55Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 17:55 fiscal: CORRECTION to note 42: the Delphi 'show_floats' disagreement is not a float bug. The compiler lane found FPC trunk printing its banner to stdout, which Cells folded into cell output; fixed with -l- -v0w in apps/cells targets.ex:321, pending a Cells run. No impact on fiscal's generated Delphi.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_44 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 44u32, "2026-10-27T21:55Z", "main", "10-27 17:55 main: Delphi rows build 32-bit, and the 64-bit fix needs no install. The compiler lane found the cause of the 2 'delphi disagrees' sm' reds (FPC trunk printing its banner to stdout, which the Cells delphi runner captured as program output) and fixed it in apps/cells targets.ex:321 (fpc args gain -l- -v0w; build failures are still detected). The banner also said 'for i386': scoop freepascal has only bin\\i386-win32, so every Delphi row so far has been a 32-bit program. Probe (main, 17:5x): that bin also ships ppcrossx64.exe, and units\\x86_64-win64 (with rtl) is installed, so 'fpc -Px86_64 -l- -v0w p.pas' builds a 64-bit exe that prints SizeOf(Pointer)*8 = 64. So x86_64 needs only -Px86_64 in the delphi target args (Cells targets.ex) and the matrix's Delphi native step, no new install. NOT applied: it changes every Delphi row's native result (pointer width, int sizes in layouts), so it waits for accept-6 (the oracle accept now running) and for kit's 'TRAIN window closed' (no apps/cells edits until then). Then it gets its own review run (Delphi rows vs C oracle) before any accept.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_45 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 45u32, "2026-10-28T00:07Z", "main", "10-27 20:07 main: Delphi 64-bit, step 1 of 2 (Cells). apps/cells targets.ex:321 now passes -Px86_64 to fpc, so the flags are -O2 -Mdelphi -Px86_64 -l- -v0w. Proof on a scratch hello.pas printing SizeOf(Pointer), with scoop FPC's i386 driver, which uses ppcrossx64.exe and units/x86_64-win64: without the flag the PE machine is 014C and it prints 4; with it, 8664 and 8. Both builds print nothing on stdout, so the banner fix holds. targets_test.exs:43 had still expected the pre-banner-fix list [\"-O2\", \"-Mdelphi\"] and was stale. It now asserts the full list. The file's mix run is being repeated to attribute 3 failures (19/22) to before or after this edit. Step 2 is the matrix: test.ps1:754 builds Delphi rows with -O2 -Mdelphi (32-bit), so Cells and the matrix now differ in bitness. It waits on the CCMR land, which rewrites test.ps1 and must not see an edit between its two A/B runs. Then a Delphi review run against the oracle, and the apps/cells README:223 sentence ('the way the matrix builds its Delphi rows') is true again. Then the Delphi-vs-C bench on equal 64-bit footing.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_46 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 46u32, "2026-10-28T13:18Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 09:18 fiscal: UNBLOCKED by the compiler lane: DELPHI-64BIT is an FPC 3.3.1 x86_64 -O2 register-variable miscompile (loop_9's loop-carried state dropped across the back-edge), not Spiral; -O2 -OoNOREGVAR -Px86_64 is correct on the real main.pas (32 -O2 ref = 0, 64 -O2 = 1 bug, 64 -O2 -OoNOREGVAR = 0). Held: scratchpad recgroups\\delphi64-noregvar-apply.ps1, dry run 4/4 anchors (cells targets.ex:321 + targets_test.exs:43, matrix test.ps1, Cells README; -OoNOREGVAR on both arms). Apply after kit TRAIN3 publishes (~13:10), then rerun the 64-bit rows.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_47 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 47u32, "2026-10-28T13:22Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 09:22 fiscal: PLAN REVISED per main: the permanent fix is in-source (CodegenPascal prelude emits {$IFDEF CPUX86_64}{$OPTIMIZATION NOREGVAR}{$ENDIF}; 64 -O2 exits 0 with it, 1 without), riding the compiler install ~16:00. Shared configs get -Px86_64 ONLY (no -OoNOREGVAR). fiscal owns the Cells consumers: held scratchpad recgroups\\delphi64-cells-apply.ps1 (targets.ex:321, targets_test.exs:43, README:223; dry run 3/3), applied AFTER that install; the compiler lane owns test.ps1:754. Bench numbers that count are post-install; -OoNOREGVAR only in my own interim builds.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_48 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 48u32, "2026-10-29T16:30Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 12:30 fiscal: UNBLOCKED by pack 6b (NOREGVAR prelude installed, DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE n8). Cells consumer half REBUILT after the wipe as fiscal/lib-programs/cells-delphi-x64.kit (apps/cells: targets.ex:321 fpc args gain -Px86_64, targets_test.exs:43 asserts it, README:223 says it; exact one-line anchors, so other lanes' unstaged hunks in targets.ex are untouched; rehearsed). Local proof: the installed FPC trunk with Cells' exact flags plus -Px86_64 builds a PE32+ (machine 0x8664) program that runs and reports 8-byte pointers. Consumer-path proof queued: fiscal-cells-delphi64 (job-1793291482758) runs a ///> delphi test cell through lib_gate on mirror cells1029 holding the three changed files. Land through lib-guarded-do -Lib apps/cells (Cells is read by every run) once it holds. Then: the step-1 probe and the bench folders (n12-n14), still redo.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_49 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 49u32, "2026-10-29T17:26Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 13:26 fiscal: Cells consumer proof HOLDS: fiscal-cells-delphi64 (job-1793291482758, mirror cells1029 with the 3 changed Cells files): the ///> delphi probe cell builds with -Px86_64 and passes, 0 type errors. Land requested from main (kit's TRAIN4 window holds the slot).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_50 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 50u32, "2026-10-29T18:14Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 14:14 fiscal: re-proven on PACK 6b (node stage spiral-bin-1029-6b, host 9895730DAE80, core 31CC96520D6B7269), mirrors freshly synced: fiscal-6b-cells-delphi64 (mirror cells1029 + cells-delphi-x64.kit's 3 files): the ///> delphi probe cell builds -Px86_64 and passes, 0 type errors. Lands first in kit's post-TRAIN4 window (main's order: xsd_emit, schematron, then these Cells edits).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_51 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 51u32, "2026-10-29T19:28Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 15:28 fiscal: Cells -Px86_64 LANDED 15:19:06 (cells-delphi-x64.kit re-cut onto the current README: targets.ex ACED40C9 -> 8AC48681, test/spiral/cells/targets_test.exs F6520DFF -> 50B45E26, README.md FC6B7F7B -> 0CBDBD20), via lib-guarded-do in the 15:18 gap (no exemptions), hash equal to the pack-6b-proven copy (node stage spiral-bin-1029-6b, core 31CC96520D6B7269); main's closing document_test 22/22 over all gap lands. Byte-exact undo copies: C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\land-1029\\{m7,sch2,mod,cells64b}\\base (not git HEAD: other lanes' unstaged lib hunks). Cells now builds Delphi cells 64-bit; with pack 6b's NOREGVAR prelude the DFA miscompile is covered. Next: the step-1 probe and the bench folders (redo).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_52 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 52u32, "2026-10-30T15:03Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 11:03 fiscal: ENV PIN (tibia via main, PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY n42, memory scoop-reset-flips-fpc): scoop's freepascal\\current is 3.2.2 again since the 10-30 06:2x scoop reset sweep. Any Delphi verdict for this row must pin SPIRAL_FPC to the trunk scoop\\apps\\freepascal\\3.3.1-20261025\\bin\\i386-win32\\fpc.exe, and record that path in the proof note. [UPDATE 11:04, main: the compiler lane flipped freepascal\\current back to trunk 3.3.1 (fpc -iV 3.3.1, fpc.exe 116,736 B), so unpinned runs are fine again; keep the SPIRAL_FPC pin only as a guard.]")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_53 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 53u32, "2026-10-30T22:45Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 18:45 fiscal: GROOM, redo state verified against the live lib (for step 1, n12-n13). Both 10-25 lib drafts the step-1 probe applied (xml-delphi-arms, delphi-arms) and the reach survey tool (delphi-survey/reach.py) were lost in the 10-28 wipe, and none of their content is in the lib: xml.spi has 0 Delphi arms (code_point_at/rust_first_char are Rust-only), unicode.code_point_count has Gleam/Rust/Zig only, sm'.index_of and sm'.replace have no Delphi key (Fsharp/Rust...), and optionm' has 0 Delphi mentions (the option'/find_parent path, n13). Only the Cells half survived and is LANDED (-Px86_64, 10-29 15:19). Redo order, sized by milestones: (1) re-derive the reach set for one module (generated_mdfe_v1_00) as a Spiral/PowerShell survey over Delphi-specialized arms, 60m; (2) Delphi arms for each reached gap (code_point_count, index_of, replace, code_point_at, the xml read path, option'), as a lib draft with a ///> delphi test per function, 120m; (3) the step-1 probe itself (copy the lib, retarget the module's ///> rust test to ///> delphi, byte-compare with the Rust example, validate against the official XSD), 60m. Then step 3 (bench, design n14). Not started today: the remaining window is better spent landing and verifying DIAN; next fiscal session starts at (1).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_54 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 54u32, "2026-10-30T22:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 18:57 fiscal: MILESTONE 1, FIRST CUT (upper bound). New survey C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\delphi\\reach.ps1 (replaces the lost reach.py): indexes every lib definition, then BFS from generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi's module.function calls and each body's calls (unqualified names resolved through the module and its :< imports), flagging any reached run_target/backend_switch with no Delphi key and no non-failing _ arm. Result: 179 reached definitions, 25 flagged: xml.code_point_at and code_point_width (Rust-only, _ fails), unicode.code_point_count and unicode_types, optionm' (map, none', default_value', of_obj, option'_types), sm'.slice / obj_to_string / from_std_string, convert.u8/u16/f32/f64/unbox, am'.to_vec, resultm.from_try, date_time.* and env.* . It is a SUPERSET: unlike n13's tool it follows calls inside every backend's arm, so date_time.to_calendar_gleam / env.os_environ are likely reached only through other backends' arms. Next refinement (the rest of milestone 1): when a body has run_target/backend_switch, follow only its Delphi arm (else _). Then milestone 2 drafts Delphi arms for the true set; the xml.code_point_* and option' gaps are certain (they match n13).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_55 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 55u32, "2026-10-30T22:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 18:57 fiscal: MILESTONE 1 DONE (refined): reach.ps1 now follows only the Delphi arm (else _ when there is no Delphi arm) of every run_target / backend_switch, like n13's tool. generated_mdfe_v1_00 reaches 84 lib definitions, with 13 Delphi gaps = the milestone-2 scope: xml.code_point_at, xml.code_point_width (run_target, _ fails), unicode.code_point_count, unicode.unicode_types (the XML read path; matches n13), optionm'.map, optionm'.of_obj (the option' path; matches n13), sm'.slice, sm'.obj_to_string, date_time.seconds / stopwatch / time_span (most likely the bench cell's timing), env.environ_get / os_environ (most likely fiscal_paths' FISCAL_ROOT lookup). n13's sm'.index_of / sm'.replace are no longer reached on this module. Tool: C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\delphi\\reach.ps1 -Module <generated .spi>. 60 -> 65 by the n53 milestones (60 of 240 minutes). Next: milestone 2, Delphi arms for these 13 as a lib draft with a ///> delphi test each.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_56 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 56u32, "2026-10-30T22:59Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 18:59 fiscal: MILESTONE 2 SCOPE CLASSIFIED (arms per gap read from the lib). Mirrorable from an existing C arm (cheap): sm'.slice (C = sm.slice s { from to }, if core sm.slice has a Delphi lowering) and sm'.obj_to_string (C = obj_to_string_c, C-specific, likely needs a Delphi formatter). Need a real Delphi design, each with a ///> delphi test: unicode.code_point_count + xml.code_point_at / code_point_width (UTF-8 stepping over the FPC string's bytes; only Rust/Zig arms today), optionm'.map / of_obj (host-boxed option' has no Delphi type, n13), date_time.seconds / stopwatch / time_span (FPC Now / GetTickCount64 or SysUtils), env.environ_get / os_environ (FPC GetEnvironmentVariable). Next session: draft these 13 arms on a lib copy, prove each with a ///> delphi cell (box FPC trunk 3.3.1), then the step-1 probe (milestone 3).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_57 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 57u32, "2026-10-30T23:03Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 19:03 fiscal: MILESTONE 1 CORRECTED, 9 true gaps (not 13). reach.ps1's arm filter missed run_target arms with a binding (| Gleam _ =>, | Cuda _ =>) and the lowercase catch-all (| target =>), so env.environ_get / os_environ (reached only through get_environment_variable's Cuda arm; that function HAS a Delphi arm, GetEnvironmentVariable) and optionm'.map / of_obj (through Fsharp/Cuda arms) were false reach. Fixed, and the survey now prints each gap's call chain. 78 reached definitions, 9 gaps, every chain on a real Delphi path: date_time.stopwatch / seconds / time_span (the bench cell's stopwatch_elapsed_microseconds), sm'.slice (sm'.trim <- xml.read_nodes <- read_xml), sm'.obj_to_string (direct), unicode.code_point_count / unicode_types (xml.code_point_count), xml.code_point_at / code_point_width (direct, run_target _ fails).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_58 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 58u32, "2026-10-30T23:05Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 19:05 fiscal: MILESTONE 2 STARTED, first 2 of 9 arms drafted: C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\delphi\\lib-draft\\sm'.spi (base A683500F -> 8113E287, 3 lines). sm'.slice gets Delphi = sm.slice s { from to } (the core primitive every other backend calls), sm'.obj_to_string gets Delphi = obj_to_string_c x (the C dispatcher over PrimToString, so integers, bools and chars, like C), and test slice_2 gains ///> delphi so FPC proves the slice arm. Proof queued after the windows clear: round trip + fiscal-box5-delphi-slice (lib_gate -Test slice_2 on scratch boxgate5, all the test's backends incl. delphi). Left: date_time.stopwatch / seconds / time_span, unicode.code_point_count / unicode_types, xml.code_point_at / code_point_width.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_59 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 59u32, "2026-10-30T23:33Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 19:33 fiscal: MILESTONE 2 ALL ARMS DRAFTED, survey at 0 gaps (C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\delphi\\lib-draft, bases = live): sm' A683500F->8113E287, unicode C9F0A794->F3E7BE24 (delphi_code_point_count_from + unicode_types gets Delphi = () like Rust, since code_point_count calls it on every backend), xml 58CA22E1->D540BC09 (delphi_code_point_at / width + test code_points_step_through_utf8), date_time 21D9AE92->BD5014E7. date_time: the backend is FPC {$mode delphi}, and FPC trunk 3.3.1 ships vcl-compat System.Diagnostics (3.2.2 does not; CI runs no Delphi). stopwatch_switch Delphi : TStopwatch; stopwatch () Delphi = global \"uses System.Diagnostics\" (spiral_compiler.fs:165800 lifts uses-globals into the uses clause) + TStopwatch.StartNew. The existing non-Rust elapsed arms (.ElapsedMilliseconds, .Elapsed.Ticks div 10) are already valid Delphi, and stopwatch_start's .Start is a no-op on a running watch. FPC probes (scratchpad fpcwatch): value-copy reads agree (467461 us vs 467 ms, IsHighResolution TRUE); Start after StartNew keeps the original start (66929 us after Sleep 50). stopwatch_microseconds_agree_with_milliseconds gains ///> delphi. seconds/time_span were survey false positives: reach.ps1 now skips locally bound names (:= x, params) except in type position; control on the live lib still lists all real gaps. Proofs: fiscal-box5-delphi-slice was VOID, not red (cell cap 300 s hit during fsharp: gleam 151 + lua 53 + fsharp 106 s; delphi not_run); run-gate.ps1 now shows 'timed out' lines. Re-proof with gate-only header --timeout 1800000 queued as fiscal-box5-delphi-r2 (slice_2 + stopwatch) after fiscal-box5-delphi-codepoints (running). Formatter: date_time draft 0 diffs.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_60 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 60u32, "2026-10-30T23:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 19:51 fiscal: r2 on the box (pack 8): slice_2 GREEN on every target incl delphi (gleam 286 s, lua 35, fsharp 41, cuda 51, delphi 35; gate copy B66F1FAF = draft 8113E287 + --timeout header), so sm'.slice's Delphi arm is proven and the earlier slice VOID is replaced. code_points_step_through_utf8 GREEN on rust + delphi (221 s; gate 693692F8 = draft D540BC09 + header). stopwatch_microseconds_agree_with_milliseconds: rust passed, DELPHI RED: fpc 'main.pas(26,6) Error: Invalid assignment, procedures return no value'. The applied macro $'!stopwatch.Start' () is emitted as an expression bound to a variable, and TStopwatch.Start is a procedure. Fix (date_time draft 468359D2, formatter-clean, round trip true): stopwatch_start becomes run_target with Rust and Delphi arms that do nothing (both clocks start at creation: Instant::now / TStopwatch.StartNew) and _ keeping the old .Start line, so every other backend is unchanged (is_rust was true only on Rust). Re-gate queued (fiscal-box5-delphi-stopwatch2) behind main's tree-write window.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_61 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 61u32, "2026-10-31T00:05Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 20:05 fiscal: MILESTONE 2 COMPLETE, every drafted Delphi arm proven by a ///> delphi cell on FPC trunk 3.3.1 (box, pack 8 EEBCBFF8658A; gate copies = draft + a line-1 --timeout header): slice_2 all 5 targets; code_points_step_through_utf8 rust + delphi; stopwatch_microseconds_agree_with_milliseconds rust + delphi (fiscal-box5-delphi-stopwatch2, gate C97A2307 = date_time 468359D2 + header). Drafts, all with bases = live: sm' 8113E287, unicode F3E7BE24, xml D540BC09, date_time 468359D2; all formatter-clean, all round trip true. Reach survey on the CURRENT generated unit (fetched from node mirror e3lv; the tree's generated/ copy is the stale 10-27 edition-2 output): 83 definitions, 0 Delphi gaps. 65 -> 75. MILESTONE 3 (step-1 probe) QUEUED: fiscal-box6-delphi-step1-probe runs generated_mdfe_v1_00_writes_its_example retargeted to ///> delphi (scratch boxgate6\\fiscal: lib = live + the 4 drafts) and the untouched ///> rust twin (boxgate6\\fiscal-rust); then I byte-compare the two example and parse-then-write files and validate the Delphi one against the official MDF-e 1.00 XSD. run-gate.ps1 gained -FiscalRoot. Before the land: full-module gates of xml, unicode, sm' and date_time on the drafts (only targeted tests have run so far), then lib-guarded-do.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_62 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 62u32, "2026-10-31T00:09Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 20:09 fiscal: LAND PROGRAMS BUILT and rehearsed on fresh copies of the live lib. C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\delphi\\delphi-arms.kit (4 files, 13 hunks) gives sm' 8113E287, unicode F3E7BE24, xml D540BC09, date_time 468359D2, each hash-equal to its draft. C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\xml-fidelity\\xml-xpath-fidelity.kit (2 files, 8 hunks; bases xml 58CA22E1, xpath_eval 3BCD928E = live) applies cleanly ON TOP of the Delphi program: xpath_eval CC078F4B (= draft), and the combined xml.spi is D20A5A18. Both drafts change xml.spi from the same base, so D20A5A18 (Delphi + fidelity) is the file that would actually land, and it has not been gated yet: before landing both, gate xml.spi D20A5A18 as a whole module (rust, zig, delphi rows), plus round trip and formatter.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_63 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 63u32, "2026-10-31T00:10Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 20:10 fiscal: HASH UPDATE (supersedes D540BC09 / D20A5A18 above). The formatter re-lays delphi_sequence_width (one line over 120 columns) as a two-line body; adopted, so the Delphi xml draft is E8614181 (layout only; the code-points proof ran on D540BC09). Programs rebuilt and re-rehearsed on fresh live copies: delphi-arms.kit -> xml E8614181 (= draft); then xml-xpath-fidelity.kit -> xml E8F8E8F3, xpath_eval CC078F4B. The combined xml E8F8E8F3 shows only the formatter diffs live already has (2), and its round trip on the pre-relayout combination was true. The full-module gate before landing runs on E8F8E8F3.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_64 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 64u32, "2026-10-31T00:50Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 20:50 fiscal: STEP-1 PROBE, first run (fiscal-box6-delphi-step1-probe): the Rust twin passes and writes a 952-byte example (B813E064D2E7) whose only XSD finding is the required ds:Signature (expected: the example is the Unsigned stage since XSD-SIGNING-STAGES; the 10-25 copy was 1691 B and signed). The Delphi run FAILED at compile time: 'PrimToString with 1 args not supported in the Delphi backend', reached from the bench row's sm'.obj_to_string on an i64. My milestone-2 Delphi arm (obj_to_string_c) was wrong: it lowers to the compiler primitive !!!!PrimToString, which only C implements, and the reach survey cannot see primitives (it reads backend switches). Fix (sm' draft D699E8D2, formatter-clean, round trip true, names clash with nothing): obj_to_string_delphi = IntToStr for every integer width, else obj_to_string_delphi_bool = LowerCase(BoolToStr(x, True)), else a compile-time failwith, as C does; new test obj_to_string_on_delphi_prints_ints_and_bools (42, -7, u64 max, false). Queued: fiscal-box6-delphi-step1-probe2 (that test, then the Delphi probe again, then byte-compare against B813E064 and XSD-validate). Survey follow-up: also flag !!!!Prim* uses on Delphi paths.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_65 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 65u32, "2026-10-31T02:11Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 22:11 fiscal: STEP-1 PROBE, runs 2-3. probe2: obj_to_string_on_delphi_prints_ints_and_bools GREEN on delphi (gate B0249838 = sm' D699E8D2 + header). The MDF-e probe then compiled through parse, write and all 31 bench runs, and stopped on the generated test's 'file_system.create_dir_all folder |> resultm.unbox' (target/bench output): create_dir_all emits Rust unconditionally (!\\\\(path, std::fs::create_dir_all)), and its result' is host-boxed (resultm.unbox's backend_switch has no Delphi key). A third gap class the survey could not see (no switch at all). Fix, the directory_parent pattern: file_system.directory_create_all (path) : result () string, Delphi = ForceDirectories, _ = create_dir_all |> map_error' sm'.format |> unbox (the live precedent, 3 uses); test directory_create_all_makes_nested_directories (rust + delphi). probe3 caught my own type error in it (format' returns std_string, not string); fixed, draft file_system E1FD77AC (base 91738A22 = live), formatter: 0 diffs of mine (24 = live's own). The probe module copy calls directory_create_all; the GENERATOR needs the same one-line change in its emitted bench test (filed for after the edition-3 commit, with the budget fix). Survey extended: it now also lists !!!!Prim* uses and Rust emit macros (!\\\\( / !\\() reached on Delphi paths, and the 'type position' check no longer treats the |: else-operator as a type colon. Positive controls: the old sm' draft lists PrimToString in obj_to_string_c; the original module lists file_system.create_dir_all; the live lib still lists all 7 real gaps. With the drafts: 0 switch gaps, 0 primitives, 0 Rust emits on 81 reached definitions. probe4 (dir test + MDF-e probe) is queued behind m4, gated on free memory >= 3 GB (box at 1.2 GB beside kit's train5).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_66 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 66u32, "2026-10-31T02:30Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 22:30 fiscal: generator follow-up DRAFTED in the post-edition-3 layer C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\e3live-budget\\src\\generator.spi (now 62FDA504; base 59AD5447 = the edition-3 draft generator): its emitted bench test writes 'match file_system.directory_create_all folder with' instead of 'file_system.create_dir_all folder |> resultm.unbox', so every generated unit's bench path compiles on Delphi; the generator's own create_dir_all call (line 26, Rust-run) stays. Same layer as FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE's budget change (header 3600000, factor 48). ORDER: the Delphi lib program lands first (delphi-arms.kit adds file_system.directory_create_all), then the user's edition-3 commit, then this layer, then regeneration (the generated/ drift guard then expects the new text in all 74 units).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_67 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 67u32, "2026-10-31T04:59Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 00:59 fiscal: (1) TOOL IN THE REPO (work-in-the-repo rule): the Delphi reach survey is now fiscal/scripts/delphi-reach.ps1 (unstaged; default -Lib = the repo's deps/spiral/lib/spiral), with the primitive and Rust-emit checks and the shadowing fix; smoke on generated_mdfe_v1_00 against the live lib: 7 gaps + create_dir_all, as expected. The drafts-folder copy and a restored roundtrip-one.exs went to the archive store (scratch-archive 20261031-005850). Round trips now use the committed i574n.github/scripts/slice-roundtrip-sweep.ps1 -Files (checks the _real.spir pair too). (2) healing's recovered pre-wipe model (scratchpad delphi-lib-model, 10-25) had one arm today's lib lacks: xml.is_file Delphi = FileExists. Restored in the xml draft (D8F5F3BD) with test is_file_is_false_for_a_missing_path (rust + delphi). xml.code_point_count's old Delphi arm is superseded by unicode.code_point_count's. (3) Sweep of every pending draft (sm' D699E8D2, unicode 50D83509, xml D8F5F3BD, date_time 468359D2, file_system E1FD77AC, the combined xml BA1B4166): all ok, except sm' whose _real.spir pair differs at line 5 ('/// ## rust'). The LIVE pair fails at the same line (sm'.spi A683500F + sm'_real.spir, unchanged since 12:55 and committed 22:33), so the difference predates this work; land check = the first difference stays that one. (4) delphi-arms.kit rebuilt: 5 files, 20 replacements, each file hash-equal to its draft on a fresh live copy.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_68 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 68u32, "2026-10-31T05:14Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 01:14 fiscal: probe4 (Normal priority): directory_create_all_makes_nested_directories GREEN on rust + delphi (gate 16324605 = file_system E1FD77AC + header). The MDF-e probe now compiles through the WHOLE Spiral stage on Delphi and reaches FPC; one error left: 'Identifier not found backend_switch_has_no_Delphi_arm_in_cells_console' from the bench row's console.write_line. Cause: Cells mounts its own apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/console.spi for test runs, whose show_some_value / write_line have PLACEHOLDER Delphi arms (the lib's console.spi already has Writeln). apps/cells is a live shared tree in main's Cells windows, so: anchored program C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\cells-console\\cells-console-delphi.kit (2 hunks, Writeln(!value) / Writeln(!text); base A70EDFA2 = live -> 4BC11C0F, rehearsed hash-exact), sent to main to apply in its next Cells window. Survey (fiscal/scripts/delphi-reach.ps1) now reads the Cells mounts (-Mounts, default deps/spiral/apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells, overriding same-named lib modules) and reports placeholder Delphi arms ('has_no_Delphi_arm') as gaps; !!!!BackendSwitch is no longer counted as a primitive. Control: the probe module + drafts lists console.write_line as the placeholder gap; with the cells draft mounted, 0 gaps. Next: probe5 after main's apply.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_69 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 69u32, "2026-10-31T08:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 04:57 fiscal: MILESTONE 3 GREEN, first generated SEFAZ unit end to end on Delphi (job-1793436771002-fiscal-box6-delphi-step1-probe5, compiler EEBCBFF8, FPC trunk; scratch root boxgate6 = live lib + the Delphi lib drafts (sm' D699E8D2, unicode 50D83509, xml D8F5F3BD, date_time 468359D2, file_system E1FD77AC) + live Cells console.spi 4BC11C0F (main's window 6 apply8)): generated_mdfe_v1_00 retargeted ///> delphi, generated_mdfe_v1_00_writes_its_example passes, 0 type errors. The Delphi example is BYTE-EQUAL to the Rust twin's (952 B, B813E064D2E7), and so is the bench's parse+write re-emission (952 B, B813E064D2E7). XSD (validate-example.ps1, mdfe_v1.00.xsd): exactly 1 message, the missing ds:Signature, which is the Unsigned stage's expected shape and the same single finding as the Rust reference. Delphi bench row: parse_us 720 write_us 183 (median of 31, 28148 bytes), on a saturated box (100% CPU, other lanes' heavy jobs), so a provisional number. Next: the lib drafts land after the full-module gates (fiscal-box9-delphi-modules running), then a same-load Rust/Delphi bench pair on the landed lib.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_70 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 70u32, "2026-10-31T10:33Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 06:33 fiscal: FULL-MODULE GATES, 4 of 6 read (fiscal-box9-delphi-modules, job-1793431082691, box, compiler EEBCBFF8; scratch root boxgate9 = live lib + delphi-arms.kit + xml-xpath-fidelity.kit re-rehearsed on today's live, hash-equal to last night's targets: sm' D699E8D2, unicode 50D83509, xml BA1B4166, date_time 468359D2, file_system E1FD77AC, xpath_eval CC078F4B; gate headers --timeout 3600000). sm' 73/73 and unicode 9/9, 0 type errors. date_time 42/44... wait-free count: 43 of 44 pass, 1 red: new_guid_from_timestamp fsharp (assert actual -1 expected 0, in GUID/timestamp code the draft does not touch; the draft only adds the stopwatch Delphi arms). file_system 33/36: find_parent fsharp, find_parent_2 rust, trace_file fsharp, all VOID BY LOCATION (they walk up for a 'workspace' dir / README.md or write under get_workspace_root, absent above a scratch copy: 'No parent for dir workspace at ...boxgate9\\\\lib\\\\spiral'); the draft's directory_create_all test passes. xml and xpath_eval are running. Before the land: a control on a live-copy root at the same depth for new_guid_from_timestamp (expected to fail identically, i.e. pre-existing), then lib-guarded-do with delphi-arms.kit then xml-xpath-fidelity.kit; the guard refuses until kit's train5e heavy-build window ends (~10:40).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_71 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 71u32, "2026-10-31T10:33Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 06:33 fiscal: CORRECTION to n70's date_time count: the gate printed 'tests: passed 42 failed 1 of 44', so 42 passed, 1 failed (new_guid_from_timestamp fsharp) and 1 was neither (not run or skipped; to be read from date_time.txt.tests.tsv before the land), not '43 of 44 pass'.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_72 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 72u32, "2026-10-31T10:33Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 06:33 fiscal: n71's open point read: date_time.txt.tests.tsv has exactly one non-passed target row (new_guid_from_timestamp fsharp failed); the 44th test has no target row, so nothing else is red.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_73 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 73u32, "2026-10-31T12:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 08:21 fiscal: DELPHI REACH beyond MDF-e 1.00 (scripts/delphi-reach.ps1 over the lib with the Delphi drafts, boxgate9): generated_nfe_v4 (115 lib definitions reached), generated_cte_v4_00 (103) and generated_mdfe_v1_00 (95) have the SAME two gaps and nothing else: 0 primitives, (1) console.write_line's placeholder Delphi arm and (2) file_system.create_dir_all's Rust emit (the generated bench line). So once both close, every BR unit's Delphi build path is clear by this survey. (1) Live console.spi is back at A70EDFA2: main's Cells window 6, which carried apply8 (4BC11C0F, the state probe5 passed on at 04:14), failed at 04:59 and was rolled back whole; per CELLS-DAEMON-RETIRE-STOP n3 window 6 is re-running as the last step of main's land queue, so apply8 returns with it (no action routed). (2) needs the Delphi lib land first (directory_create_all is in the file_system draft, not live), then the generator's bench line emits directory_create_all (drafted in the e3live-budget layer; to apply to the tree directly after the lib land, not after edition 3).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_74 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 74u32, "2026-10-31T13:24Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 09:24 fiscal: box9 TIMED OUT at its 6 h job cap (job-1793431082691, 21.6M ms): 4 modules done (n70-n72: sm' 73/73, unicode 9/9, date_time 42 pass + 1 unrelated red, file_system 33 pass + 3 VOID by location), then xml.spi (combined BA1B4166, header 3600000) STALLED from 06:30: its erl had 17 s of CPU in 2 h 45 min, no child process (no dotnet/rustc/fpc anywhere naming the gate), no spiral-cells-run files, no raw output; xpath_eval never started. So xml and xpath_eval are VOID, not red; the same xml module passed 12/12 on the box last night as 3300361F (fidelity only), so the stall comes with the combined draft's new cells or with the run itself. RERUN queued as the first two steps of fiscal-cr-docs-4-4 (scratch root g18 = box9's lib copy, cell cap 2 h instead of 1 h so one slow cell fits, job via fiscal/scripts/box-gate.ps1); if xml stalls again, the per-cell metrics show which cell, and that cell is split out for a direct probe.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_75 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 75u32, "2026-10-31T14:30Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 10:30 fiscal: RE-ANCHORED after main's LIB-NOTEBOOK-HEADINGS-CLEANUP batches 2-3 (+ the sm' lambda land) changed 5 of the 6 base files (live now sm' 48926232, unicode 19200A0B, xml 8DCCACD5, date_time 35E23133, file_system 84642C22; xpath_eval 3BCD928E and xsd_emit 161D00F5 unchanged): the old delphi-arms.kit failed 4 of 5 file edits on live. Method: git merge-file live/base/draft per file (bases = the drafts' recorded originals) gave clean merges for sm', date_time and xml, and 3 conflicts (unicode 2, file_system 1) that were ONLY main's deleted name headings next to my inserted definitions; resolved by the cleanup's own rule: keep my code and tests, add no '/// ###' name heading (drops the 3 headings my drafts had added, so no _real.spir pairing is needed). Each new diff equals the original draft minus exactly those headings (sm' +33 vs +34, unicode +13 vs +14, file_system +14 vs +15, xml +34 and date_time +9/-2 equal). All 5 round-trip ok. fiscal/lib-programs/delphi-arms.kit rewritten (5 files, 20 replacements) and rehearsed on live, then xml-xpath-fidelity.kit on top: targets sm' E6AA294A, unicode 59CC00D7, xml 52B745FA, date_time 39148240, file_system 007096C3, xpath_eval CC078F4B. New hashes = new proof: scratch root g18 (live lib + these 6) is now what fiscal-cr-docs-4-4's xml probes + xml/xpath_eval module gates run on (so the rerun gates the real candidate, with the per-cell probes of the two new Delphi tests first), and fiscal-delphi4-reanchored gates sm', unicode, date_time, file_system after it.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_76 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 76u32, "2026-10-31T14:41Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 10:41 fiscal: RE-ANCHOR IS NOW A TOOL (main's final heading wave 2/3 is landing and may move these bases again): i574n.github/scripts/lib-reanchor.ps1 -Base <dir of the drafts' original live files> -Draft <dir of drafts> -Out <dir> -Program <out.kit> [-DropAddedNameHeadings] runs git merge-file live/base/draft per draft .spi, auto-resolves ONLY conflicts with no live-side lines and only under the switch (dropping name headings the draft adds, the cleanup's rule), refuses anything else (exit 2, no program), writes the kit program and rehearses it on live copies (exit 3 unless every rehearsed file equals its merge). Controls on this morning's inputs: positive = the 5 merged hashes equal the hand-merged ones exactly (sm' E6AA294A, unicode 59CC00D7, date_time 39148240, file_system 007096C3, xml A82C874A) and the rehearse matches; negative = without the switch it refuses (unicode's 2 heading conflicts) with exit 2 and no program. Today's bases (live 10:2x) are snapshotted in the session scratchpad delphi-merge\\\\base for the next run. All 7 bases still equal live at 10:33.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_77 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 77u32, "2026-10-31T15:17Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 11:17 fiscal: second re-anchor, first use of lib-reanchor.ps1: main's final heading wave moved live unicode.spi 19200A0B -> 5EC46942; the tool merged all 5 drafts with 0 conflicts and rehearsed them equal; unicode target now E9612B84 (diff still +13/-0), the other 4 targets unchanged (sm' E6AA294A, xml A82C874A before fidelity, date_time 39148240, file_system 007096C3). fiscal/lib-programs/delphi-arms.kit replaced, the scratch base snapshot updated, and g18's unicode refreshed before fiscal-delphi4-reanchored starts, so its unicode gate runs on the new target.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_78 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 78u32, "2026-10-31T15:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 11:54 fiscal: STALL PROBES (fiscal-cr-docs-4-4, job-1793460946299, on the RE-ANCHORED candidate xml 52B745FA + gate header = 3261BA36, scratch root g18): the two cells new in the combined draft pass ALONE, each under a 30-min watchdog: is_file_is_false_for_a_missing_path (Delphi FileExists + Rust) 1/1 and code_points_step_through_utf8 (Delphi Ord/AnsiString + Rust) 1/1, 0 type errors, ~8 min each. So box9's 2 h 45 min zero-CPU stall in xml was not a hanging cell of mine; the full xml module (2 h watchdog) and xpath_eval run next in the same job, which tells whether it reproduces on the whole module. Also: my gate waiters had included test-suite, which is shareable (window-coordination); dropping it let this job and fiscal-delphi4-reanchored start at 11:35 instead of queueing behind tibia's back-to-back suites (1 h 46 min lost).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_79 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 79u32, "2026-10-31T16:15Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 12:15 fiscal: xml FULL MODULE GREEN on the re-anchored land candidate: xml 52B745FA (Delphi arms + XML fidelity on today's live) + gate header = 3261BA36 passes 14/14, 0 type errors, in ~20 min (11:50-12:10, fiscal-cr-docs-4-4, box, compiler EEBCBFF8). The box9 2 h 45 min stall did not reproduce, so it was a property of that run, not of the module or its two new Delphi cells (both also pass alone, n78). xpath_eval CC078F4B is running; sm', unicode, date_time, file_system run in fiscal-delphi4-reanchored.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_80 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 80u32, "2026-10-31T17:02Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 13:02 fiscal: PLAN for gap (2) of n73 after the Delphi lib land (land script ready: live bases checked, hash-named undo to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\\\kit\\\\undo, both programs via lib-guarded-do, all-or-nothing restore on any refusal or target miss, round trip after). The change is one emitted line, src/generator.spi:818 'match file_system.create_dir_all folder |> resultm.unbox with' -> 'match file_system.directory_create_all folder with' (Ok/Error arms unchanged; line 26, the generator's own Rust-run call, stays). But it rewrites the bench cell of EVERY generated unit, so all 75 committed units drift and CI's drift guard (build.ps1 regenerates then git-status generated/) needs them all regenerated in one go: generator edit + a node set-proof that regenerates every family on the landed lib + format-generated.ps1 over all units (pack 8) + the formatted units copied home, committed together. Only after that does every BR unit build on Delphi by the reach survey.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_81 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 81u32, "2026-10-31T17:34Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 13:34 fiscal: (a) gap (1) of n73 CLOSED: main's Cells window 6 landed 13:15 with apply8; live apps/cells console.spi = 4BC11C0F0F4D (Delphi = Writeln(!value) / Writeln(!text)), window 6 closed 50/50 (new_project_test, compiler_daemon_test, document_test). (b) fiscal-delphi4-reanchored so far: date_time 43/44 0 failed, file_system 33/36 = only the 3 location-bound rows (find_parent fsharp, find_parent_2 rust, trace_file fsharp: VOID by location as in n70) and directory_create_all_makes_nested_directories PASSES on rust AND delphi, unicode E9612B84 9/9; sm' running. Then the land script for delphi-arms.kit + xml-xpath-fidelity.kit.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_82 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 82u32, "2026-10-31T18:31Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 14:31 fiscal: ALL 6 TARGETS HAD VERDICTS, then live moved again before the land: the land script refused cleanly (exit 4: sm' D3FCCD79, date_time DCB02071, file_system 89493A55 moved; unicode, xml, xpath_eval unchanged; nothing written). Verdicts that stand: xml 52B745FA 14/14, xpath_eval CC078F4B 12/12, unicode E9612B84 9/9 (hashes unchanged); delphi4 also gave sm' 73/73, date_time 43/44 0 failed, file_system 33/36 = the 3 location-bound rows, for the previous targets. RE-ANCHOR 3 found a real flaw in lib-reanchor.ps1: in sm', another lane had removed '///> fsharp' from the test AFTER my inserted test; the conflict's live side was empty, so the tool kept the draft side, which still carried that base line, silently undoing their change (a file-wide 'in live?' check missed it because '///> fsharp' recurs on other tests). FIXED: merge-file --diff3, and per conflict hunk, draft lines that are in the hunk's base section but not its live section (multiset) stay deleted. Verified on all 5 files: the lines the program adds to live are exactly the draft's own added lines (sm' 33, unicode 13, xml 34, date_time 9, file_system 14; removals date_time 2, others 0). New targets sm' 24428BE3, date_time BF06A436, file_system 056F0D6C (program promoted, land script updated); their gates run now in parallel (fiscal-delphi-sm-reanchor3, fiscal-delphi-dtfs-reanchor3) on g18 = today's live + both programs.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_83 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 83u32, "2026-10-31T20:01Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 16:01 fiscal: RE-ANCHOR-3 VERDICTS: sm' 24428BE3 73/73, 0 type errors (fiscal-delphi-sm-reanchor3); file_system 056F0D6C 33/36 = only the 3 location-bound rows, directory_create_all passes on rust AND delphi (fiscal-delphi-dtfs-reanchor3). date_time BF06A436 gave 42/44 with new_guid_from_timestamp red on fsharp, the same row that passed at 12:24 on the same Delphi additions, so it is flaky and unrelated (another lane is retargeting those fsharp tests to rust -d chrono right now, which also moved date_time again: D9365B01, re-anchored to 5A7F4D41 with 0 conflicts and the same +9/-2); its gate (fiscal-delphi-dt-reanchor4) runs now. Current proofs held: sm', unicode, xml, xpath_eval, file_system; land script (all-or-nothing, base-checked) runs on date_time's verdict.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_84 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 84u32, "2026-10-31T20:46Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 16:46 fiscal: land program moved here from fiscal/lib-programs (a folder I invented; programs live in backlog notes so land-lib-program.ps1 -Note can read them): Delphi arms for sm' unicode xml date_time file_system (re-anchored 16:42 onto live sm' 94A51BB5, file_system 7F2BD058; land order: this, then xml-xpath-fidelity).\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit date_time.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -1057,6 +1057,7 @@ test add_days_moves_the_timestamp =\n     )\n :== stopwatch_switch =\n     {\n+        Delphi : $'TStopwatch'\n         Fsharp : $'System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch'\n         Python : stopwatch_python\n         Rust : $'std::time::Instant'\n@@ -1065,6 +1066,9 @@ test add_days_moves_the_timestamp =\n \n := stopwatch () : stopwatch =\n     backend_switch {\n+        Delphi = \\ () =>\n+            global \"uses System.Diagnostics\"\n+            $'TStopwatch.StartNew' : stopwatch\n         Fsharp = \\ () => $'`stopwatch ()' : stopwatch\n         Python = \\ () => $'`stopwatch ' : stopwatch\n         Rust = \\ () => $'std::time::Instant::now()' : stopwatch\n@@ -1089,8 +1093,10 @@ test add_days_moves_the_timestamp =\n         ticks / 10i64\n \n := stopwatch_start (stopwatch : stopwatch) : () =\n-    ? is_rust () |> not\n-    ?> $'!stopwatch.Start' ()\n+    run_target \\|\n+        | Rust => \\ () => ()\n+        | Delphi => \\ () => ()\n+        | _ => \\ () => $'!stopwatch.Start' ()\n \n := monotonic_milliseconds () : i64 =\n     run_target \\|\n@@ -1467,6 +1473,7 @@ test zig_date_time_is_micros_since_the_unix_epoch =\n     unix_epoch () |> add_days -1i32 |> format_iso8601 |> _assert_eq \"1969-12-31T00-00-00.000\"\n \n ///> rust\n+///> delphi\n test stopwatch_microseconds_agree_with_milliseconds =\n     := watch = stopwatch ()\n     stopwatch_start watch\n>>>\nedit file_system.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -658,6 +658,20 @@ test get_current_directory =\n             ? parent = path || parent = \"\" ?> None |: Some parent\n         | _ => \\ path => path |> directory_get_parent |> optionm'.unbox\n \n+:= directory_create_all (path : string) : result () string =\n+    run_target_args' path \\|\n+        | Delphi => \\ path =>\n+            ? $'ForceDirectories(!path)' : bool ?> Ok () |: Error (\"directory_create_all / cannot create \" ++# path)\n+        | _ => \\ path => path |> create_dir_all |> resultm.map_error' sm'.format |> resultm.unbox\n+\n+///> rust\n+///> delphi\n+test directory_create_all_makes_nested_directories =\n+    := path = get_current_directory () </> \"directory_create_all_test\" </> \"a\" </> \"b\"\n+    ?= directory_create_all path ?|\n+    | Ok () => ? directory_exists path = !- ?> failwith \"the nested directory exists after directory_create_all\"\n+    | Error message => failwith message\n+\n :~ create_temp_path' (guid : guid.guid) =\n     run_target_args' guid \\|\n         | _ => \\ guid =>\n>>>\nedit sm'.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -202,6 +202,31 @@ test to_char_array =\n     ?> !!!!PrimToString(x)\n     |: failwith \"sm'.obj_to_string / the C backend prints integers, bools and chars only\"\n \n+:= obj_to_string_delphi_bool \\* t. (x : t) : string =\n+    ?\n+        ~>\n+            ?: t ?|\n+            | bool => !+\n+            | _ => !-\n+    ?> $'LowerCase(BoolToStr(!x, True))' : string\n+    |: failwith \"sm'.obj_to_string / the Delphi backend prints integers and bools only\"\n+\n+:= obj_to_string_delphi \\* t. (x : t) : string =\n+    ?\n+        ~>\n+            ?: t ?|\n+            | i8 => !+\n+            | i16 => !+\n+            | i32 => !+\n+            | i64 => !+\n+            | u8 => !+\n+            | u16 => !+\n+            | u32 => !+\n+            | u64 => !+\n+            | _ => !-\n+    ?> $'IntToStr(!x)' : string\n+    |: obj_to_string_delphi_bool x\n+\n := (~#?*) s =\n     s |> format_debug\n \n@@ -267,6 +292,7 @@ test format_debug_2 no_agree \"tests each backend's native rendering\" =\n             Lean = \\ () => $'toString !x ' : string\n             Wasm = \\ () => obj_to_string_wasm x\n             C = \\ () => obj_to_string_c x\n+            Delphi = \\ () => obj_to_string_delphi x\n         }\n \n ///> c\n@@ -274,6 +300,11 @@ test obj_to_string_on_c_prints_ints_bools_and_chars =\n     (obj_to_string 42i32, obj_to_string (0i64 - 7), obj_to_string !+, obj_to_string 'x')\n     |> _assert_eq (\"42\", \"-7\", \"true\", \"x\")\n \n+///> delphi\n+test obj_to_string_on_delphi_prints_ints_and_bools =\n+    (obj_to_string 42i32, obj_to_string (0i64 - 7), obj_to_string 18446744073709551615u64, obj_to_string !-)\n+    |> _assert_eq (\"42\", \"-7\", \"18446744073709551615\", \"false\")\n+\n ///> cuda\n ///> rust\n ///> zig\n@@ -357,6 +388,7 @@ test concat_list print_code =\n         TypeScript = \\ () => sm.slice s { from to } : string\n         Zig = \\ () => sm.slice s { from to } : string\n         Lean = \\ () => sm.slice s { from to } : string\n+        Delphi = \\ () => sm.slice s { from to } : string\n     }\n \n ///> fsharp\n@@ -380,6 +412,7 @@ test slice print_code =\n ///> lua\n ///> fsharp\n ///> cuda\n+///> delphi\n test slice_2 =\n     \"abcdef\"\n     |> slice 1i32 3i32\n>>>\nedit unicode.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@ test unicode static =\n             global \"import gleam/string\"\n             global \"import gleam/result\"\n         Rust = \\ () => ()\n+        Delphi = \\ () => ()\n     }\n \n := is_scalar_value (n : i32) : bool = n >= 0i32 && n <= 1114111i32 && (n < 55296i32 || n > 57343i32)\n@@ -33,6 +34,9 @@ test unicode static =\n := c_byte_text (b : u8) : string =\n     $'StringLit(2, (char[]){ !b, 0 })' : string\n \n+:= delphi_byte_text (b : u8) : string =\n+    $'AnsiString(AnsiChar(!b))' : string\n+\n :! rust_scalar = $'char'\n \n := rust_char_of_u32 (c : u32) : optionm'.option' rust_scalar =\n@@ -54,6 +58,7 @@ test unicode static =\n             $'unicode_erlang_characters_to_binary([!n])' : string\n         | Rust => \\ () => (conv n : u32) |> rust_char_of_u32 |> optionm'.unwrap |> sm'.obj_to_string\n         | C => \\ () => utf8_text c_byte_text n\n+        | Delphi => \\ () => utf8_text delphi_byte_text n\n         | Fsharp => \\ () => $'System.Char.ConvertFromUtf32 !n' : string\n         | TypeScript => \\ () => $'String.fromCodePoint(!n)' : string\n         | _ => \\ () => failwith \"unicode.scalar_text: no arm for this backend\"\n@@ -85,6 +90,12 @@ test unicode static =\n := first_code_point (s : string) : i32 =\n     s |> first_scalar |> optionm'.map (\\ c => conv c) |> optionm'.default_value' 0i32\n \n+:~ ^ delphi_code_point_count_from (s : string) (i : i32) (acc : i32) : i32 =\n+    ? i >= sm'.length s ?> acc\n+    |:\n+        := byte = $'Ord((!s)[!i + 1])' : i32\n+        delphi_code_point_count_from s (i + 1i32) (? byte >= 128i32 && byte < 192i32 ?> acc |: acc + 1i32)\n+\n := code_point_count (s : string) : i32 =\n     unicode_types ()\n     backend_switch {\n@@ -93,6 +104,7 @@ test unicode static =\n         Zig = \\ () =>\n             := count : u64 = $'std.unicode.utf8CountCodepoints(!s) catch 0'\n             conv count : i32\n+        Delphi = \\ () => delphi_code_point_count_from s 0i32 0i32\n     }\n \n :! gleam_codepoint = $'string.UtfCodepoint'\n@@ -187,6 +199,7 @@ test code_points_count_scalars_not_bytes =\n ///> rust\n ///> gleam\n ///> c\n+///> delphi\n test char_of_encodes_utf8_and_rejects_surrogates =\n     := ~e = 233i32\n     (sm.length (char_of e), sm.length (char_of 128512i32), sm.length (char_of 55296i32))\n>>>\nedit xml.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -15,9 +15,26 @@\n     := at : u64 = conv i\n     $'std.unicode.utf8ByteSequenceLength((!s)[!at]) catch 1' : u8\n \n+:= delphi_byte_at (s : string) (i : i32) : u32 = $'Ord((!s)[!i + 1])' : u32\n+\n+:= delphi_sequence_width (lead : u32) : i32 =\n+    ? lead < 192u32 ?> 1i32 |? lead < 224u32 ?> 2i32 |? lead < 240u32 ?> 3i32 |: 4i32\n+\n+:= delphi_code_point_at (s : string) (i : i32) : u32 =\n+    ? i >= sm'.length s ?> 0u32\n+    |:\n+        := lead = delphi_byte_at s i\n+        := width = ? i + delphi_sequence_width lead > sm'.length s ?> 1i32 |: delphi_sequence_width lead\n+        := tail (k : i32) = delphi_byte_at s (i + k) - 128u32\n+        ? width = 1i32 ?> lead\n+        |? width = 2i32 ?> (lead - 192u32) * 64u32 + tail 1i32\n+        |? width = 3i32 ?> (lead - 224u32) * 4096u32 + tail 1i32 * 64u32 + tail 2i32\n+        |: (lead - 240u32) * 262144u32 + tail 1i32 * 4096u32 + tail 2i32 * 64u32 + tail 3i32\n+\n := code_point_at (s : string) (i : i32) : u32 =\n     run_target \\|\n         | Rust => \\ () => rust_first_char s i |> optionm'.map unicode.rust_char_code |> optionm'.unwrap_or 0u32\n+        | Delphi => \\ () => delphi_code_point_at s i\n         | _ => \\ () => failwith \"code_point_at: no arm for this backend\"\n \n := code_point_width (s : string) (i : i32) : i32 =\n@@ -27,6 +44,7 @@\n             |> optionm'.map \\ c => convert.convert (rust_char_width c)\n             |> optionm'.unwrap_or 1i32\n         | Zig => \\ () => ? i < sm'.length s ?> conv (zig_sequence_width s i) |: 1i32\n+        | Delphi => \\ () => ? i < sm'.length s ?> delphi_sequence_width (delphi_byte_at s i) |: 1i32\n         | _ => \\ () => failwith \"code_point_width: no arm for this backend\"\n \n := code_point_count (s : string) : i32 = unicode.code_point_count s\n@@ -69,8 +87,14 @@\n := is_file (path : string) : bool =\n     run_target \\|\n         | Rust => \\ () => $'std::path::Path::new(&*!path).is_file()' : bool\n+        | Delphi => \\ () => $'FileExists(!path)' : bool\n         | _ => \\ () => failwith \"is_file: no arm for this backend\"\n \n+///> rust\n+///> delphi\n+test is_file_is_false_for_a_missing_path =\n+    ? is_file \"no-such-file-for-xml-is-file.txt\" ?> failwith \"a missing path is not a file\"\n+\n := at (s : string) (pos : i32) : string = code_point_text s pos\n \n := is_space (c : string) : bool = c = \" \" || c = \"\\n\" || c = \"\\r\" || c = \"\\t\"\n@@ -583,3 +607,13 @@ test white_space_and_fixed_attributes_follow_the_xsd_rules =\n     ? collapsed_space \" \\n\\t \" <> \"\" ?> failwith \"only spaces collapse to nothing\"\n     ? listm.length (checked_fixed \"Version\" \"4.0\" [(\"Version\", \"4.0\")]) <> 1i32 ?> failwith \"the fixed value reads\"\n     ? listm.length (checked_fixed \"Version\" \"4.0\" []) <> 0i32 ?> failwith \"an absent fixed attribute stays absent\"\n+\n+///> rust\n+///> delphi\n+test code_points_step_through_utf8 =\n+    := s = \"aé€\"\n+    ? code_point_count s <> 3i32 ?> failwith \"three code points\"\n+    ? code_point_width s 0i32 <> 1i32 || code_point_width s 1i32 <> 2i32 || code_point_width s 3i32 <> 3i32\n+    ?> failwith \"UTF-8 widths 1, 2, 3\"\n+    ? code_point_at s 0i32 <> 97u32 || code_point_at s 1i32 <> 233u32 || code_point_at s 3i32 <> 8364u32\n+    ?> failwith \"code points a, e acute, euro\"\n>>>")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_85 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 85u32, "2026-10-31T21:06Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 17:06 fiscal: LAND BLOCKED BY sm' CHURN, waiting on main. All verdicts in hand for the current re-anchor (date_time 5A7F4D41 43/44 0 failed; unicode, xml, xpath_eval unchanged and proven; file_system 7D7DD2C3 and sm' 7B161EB5 were gating) when sm' moved a 4th time today (94A51BB5 -> BB51848F, <20 min after the re-anchor); the land script would refuse on the base check, so the sm' and file_system gates (fiscal-delphi-sm/fs-reanchor5) were stopped to free ~85 min of box. Splitting the land without sm' is unproven (the other modules' Delphi tests use _assert_eq, whose Delphi formatting needs the sm' arms), so not done. Asked main (one message) for either a ~2 h sm' quiet window or to carry the additive sm' part (this item's note 84 PROGRAM) in its next sm' land. Programs now live in backlog notes (note 84 Delphi, XML-XPATH-FIDELITY n12), the land script reads them from there, and fiscal/lib-programs left the repo (archived).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_86 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 86u32, "2026-10-31T22:43Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 18:43 fiscal: re-anchored Delphi program for main's sm' quiet window (supersedes n84; written by quiet-land.ps1).\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit date_time.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -1057,6 +1057,7 @@ test add_days_moves_the_timestamp =\n     )\n :== stopwatch_switch =\n     {\n+        Delphi : $'TStopwatch'\n         Fsharp : $'System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch'\n         Python : stopwatch_python\n         Rust : $'std::time::Instant'\n@@ -1065,6 +1066,9 @@ test add_days_moves_the_timestamp =\n \n := stopwatch () : stopwatch =\n     backend_switch {\n+        Delphi = \\ () =>\n+            global \"uses System.Diagnostics\"\n+            $'TStopwatch.StartNew' : stopwatch\n         Fsharp = \\ () => $'`stopwatch ()' : stopwatch\n         Python = \\ () => $'`stopwatch ' : stopwatch\n         Rust = \\ () => $'std::time::Instant::now()' : stopwatch\n@@ -1089,8 +1093,10 @@ test add_days_moves_the_timestamp =\n         ticks / 10i64\n \n := stopwatch_start (stopwatch : stopwatch) : () =\n-    ? is_rust () |> not\n-    ?> $'!stopwatch.Start' ()\n+    run_target \\|\n+        | Rust => \\ () => ()\n+        | Delphi => \\ () => ()\n+        | _ => \\ () => $'!stopwatch.Start' ()\n \n := monotonic_milliseconds () : i64 =\n     run_target \\|\n@@ -1467,6 +1473,7 @@ test zig_date_time_is_micros_since_the_unix_epoch =\n     unix_epoch () |> add_days -1i32 |> format_iso8601 |> _assert_eq \"1969-12-31T00-00-00.000\"\n \n ///> rust\n+///> delphi\n test stopwatch_microseconds_agree_with_milliseconds =\n     := watch = stopwatch ()\n     stopwatch_start watch\n>>>\nedit file_system.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -658,6 +658,20 @@ test get_current_directory =\n             ? parent = path || parent = \"\" ?> None |: Some parent\n         | _ => \\ path => path |> directory_get_parent |> optionm'.unbox\n \n+:= directory_create_all (path : string) : result () string =\n+    run_target_args' path \\|\n+        | Delphi => \\ path =>\n+            ? $'ForceDirectories(!path)' : bool ?> Ok () |: Error (\"directory_create_all / cannot create \" ++# path)\n+        | _ => \\ path => path |> create_dir_all |> resultm.map_error' sm'.format |> resultm.unbox\n+\n+///> rust\n+///> delphi\n+test directory_create_all_makes_nested_directories =\n+    := path = get_current_directory () </> \"directory_create_all_test\" </> \"a\" </> \"b\"\n+    ?= directory_create_all path ?|\n+    | Ok () => ? directory_exists path = !- ?> failwith \"the nested directory exists after directory_create_all\"\n+    | Error message => failwith message\n+\n :~ create_temp_path' (guid : guid.guid) =\n     run_target_args' guid \\|\n         | _ => \\ guid =>\n>>>\nedit sm'.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -202,6 +202,31 @@ test to_char_array =\n     ?> !!!!PrimToString(x)\n     |: failwith \"sm'.obj_to_string / the C backend prints integers, bools and chars only\"\n \n+:= obj_to_string_delphi_bool \\* t. (x : t) : string =\n+    ?\n+        ~>\n+            ?: t ?|\n+            | bool => !+\n+            | _ => !-\n+    ?> $'LowerCase(BoolToStr(!x, True))' : string\n+    |: failwith \"sm'.obj_to_string / the Delphi backend prints integers and bools only\"\n+\n+:= obj_to_string_delphi \\* t. (x : t) : string =\n+    ?\n+        ~>\n+            ?: t ?|\n+            | i8 => !+\n+            | i16 => !+\n+            | i32 => !+\n+            | i64 => !+\n+            | u8 => !+\n+            | u16 => !+\n+            | u32 => !+\n+            | u64 => !+\n+            | _ => !-\n+    ?> $'IntToStr(!x)' : string\n+    |: obj_to_string_delphi_bool x\n+\n := (~#?*) s =\n     s |> format_debug\n \n@@ -267,6 +292,7 @@ test format_debug_2 no_agree \"tests each backend's native rendering\" =\n             Lean = \\ () => $'toString !x ' : string\n             Wasm = \\ () => obj_to_string_wasm x\n             C = \\ () => obj_to_string_c x\n+            Delphi = \\ () => obj_to_string_delphi x\n         }\n \n ///> c\n@@ -274,6 +300,11 @@ test obj_to_string_on_c_prints_ints_bools_and_chars =\n     (obj_to_string 42i32, obj_to_string (0i64 - 7), obj_to_string !+, obj_to_string 'x')\n     |> _assert_eq (\"42\", \"-7\", \"true\", \"x\")\n \n+///> delphi\n+test obj_to_string_on_delphi_prints_ints_and_bools =\n+    (obj_to_string 42i32, obj_to_string (0i64 - 7), obj_to_string 18446744073709551615u64, obj_to_string !-)\n+    |> _assert_eq (\"42\", \"-7\", \"18446744073709551615\", \"false\")\n+\n ///> cuda\n ///> rust\n ///> zig\n@@ -357,6 +388,7 @@ test concat_list print_code =\n         TypeScript = \\ () => sm.slice s { from to } : string\n         Zig = \\ () => sm.slice s { from to } : string\n         Lean = \\ () => sm.slice s { from to } : string\n+        Delphi = \\ () => sm.slice s { from to } : string\n     }\n \n ///> rust\n@@ -380,6 +412,7 @@ test slice print_code =\n ///> lua\n ///> fsharp\n ///> cuda\n+///> delphi\n test slice_2 =\n     \"abcdef\"\n     |> slice 1i32 3i32\n>>>\nedit unicode.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@ test unicode static =\n             global \"import gleam/string\"\n             global \"import gleam/result\"\n         Rust = \\ () => ()\n+        Delphi = \\ () => ()\n     }\n \n := is_scalar_value (n : i32) : bool = n >= 0i32 && n <= 1114111i32 && (n < 55296i32 || n > 57343i32)\n@@ -33,6 +34,9 @@ test unicode static =\n := c_byte_text (b : u8) : string =\n     $'StringLit(2, (char[]){ !b, 0 })' : string\n \n+:= delphi_byte_text (b : u8) : string =\n+    $'AnsiString(AnsiChar(!b))' : string\n+\n :! rust_scalar = $'char'\n \n := rust_char_of_u32 (c : u32) : optionm'.option' rust_scalar =\n@@ -54,6 +58,7 @@ test unicode static =\n             $'unicode_erlang_characters_to_binary([!n])' : string\n         | Rust => \\ () => (conv n : u32) |> rust_char_of_u32 |> optionm'.unwrap |> sm'.obj_to_string\n         | C => \\ () => utf8_text c_byte_text n\n+        | Delphi => \\ () => utf8_text delphi_byte_text n\n         | Fsharp => \\ () => $'System.Char.ConvertFromUtf32 !n' : string\n         | TypeScript => \\ () => $'String.fromCodePoint(!n)' : string\n         | _ => \\ () => failwith \"unicode.scalar_text: no arm for this backend\"\n@@ -85,6 +90,12 @@ test unicode static =\n := first_code_point (s : string) : i32 =\n     s |> first_scalar |> optionm'.map (\\ c => conv c) |> optionm'.default_value' 0i32\n \n+:~ ^ delphi_code_point_count_from (s : string) (i : i32) (acc : i32) : i32 =\n+    ? i >= sm'.length s ?> acc\n+    |:\n+        := byte = $'Ord((!s)[!i + 1])' : i32\n+        delphi_code_point_count_from s (i + 1i32) (? byte >= 128i32 && byte < 192i32 ?> acc |: acc + 1i32)\n+\n := code_point_count (s : string) : i32 =\n     unicode_types ()\n     backend_switch {\n@@ -93,6 +104,7 @@ test unicode static =\n         Zig = \\ () =>\n             := count : u64 = $'std.unicode.utf8CountCodepoints(!s) catch 0'\n             conv count : i32\n+        Delphi = \\ () => delphi_code_point_count_from s 0i32 0i32\n     }\n \n :! gleam_codepoint = $'string.UtfCodepoint'\n@@ -187,6 +199,7 @@ test code_points_count_scalars_not_bytes =\n ///> rust\n ///> gleam\n ///> c\n+///> delphi\n test char_of_encodes_utf8_and_rejects_surrogates =\n     := ~e = 233i32\n     (sm.length (char_of e), sm.length (char_of 128512i32), sm.length (char_of 55296i32))\n>>>\nedit xml.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -15,9 +15,26 @@\n     := at : u64 = conv i\n     $'std.unicode.utf8ByteSequenceLength((!s)[!at]) catch 1' : u8\n \n+:= delphi_byte_at (s : string) (i : i32) : u32 = $'Ord((!s)[!i + 1])' : u32\n+\n+:= delphi_sequence_width (lead : u32) : i32 =\n+    ? lead < 192u32 ?> 1i32 |? lead < 224u32 ?> 2i32 |? lead < 240u32 ?> 3i32 |: 4i32\n+\n+:= delphi_code_point_at (s : string) (i : i32) : u32 =\n+    ? i >= sm'.length s ?> 0u32\n+    |:\n+        := lead = delphi_byte_at s i\n+        := width = ? i + delphi_sequence_width lead > sm'.length s ?> 1i32 |: delphi_sequence_width lead\n+        := tail (k : i32) = delphi_byte_at s (i + k) - 128u32\n+        ? width = 1i32 ?> lead\n+        |? width = 2i32 ?> (lead - 192u32) * 64u32 + tail 1i32\n+        |? width = 3i32 ?> (lead - 224u32) * 4096u32 + tail 1i32 * 64u32 + tail 2i32\n+        |: (lead - 240u32) * 262144u32 + tail 1i32 * 4096u32 + tail 2i32 * 64u32 + tail 3i32\n+\n := code_point_at (s : string) (i : i32) : u32 =\n     run_target \\|\n         | Rust => \\ () => rust_first_char s i |> optionm'.map unicode.rust_char_code |> optionm'.unwrap_or 0u32\n+        | Delphi => \\ () => delphi_code_point_at s i\n         | _ => \\ () => failwith \"code_point_at: no arm for this backend\"\n \n := code_point_width (s : string) (i : i32) : i32 =\n@@ -27,6 +44,7 @@\n             |> optionm'.map \\ c => convert.convert (rust_char_width c)\n             |> optionm'.unwrap_or 1i32\n         | Zig => \\ () => ? i < sm'.length s ?> conv (zig_sequence_width s i) |: 1i32\n+        | Delphi => \\ () => ? i < sm'.length s ?> delphi_sequence_width (delphi_byte_at s i) |: 1i32\n         | _ => \\ () => failwith \"code_point_width: no arm for this backend\"\n \n := code_point_count (s : string) : i32 = unicode.code_point_count s\n@@ -69,8 +87,14 @@\n := is_file (path : string) : bool =\n     run_target \\|\n         | Rust => \\ () => $'std::path::Path::new(&*!path).is_file()' : bool\n+        | Delphi => \\ () => $'FileExists(!path)' : bool\n         | _ => \\ () => failwith \"is_file: no arm for this backend\"\n \n+///> rust\n+///> delphi\n+test is_file_is_false_for_a_missing_path =\n+    ? is_file \"no-such-file-for-xml-is-file.txt\" ?> failwith \"a missing path is not a file\"\n+\n := at (s : string) (pos : i32) : string = code_point_text s pos\n \n := is_space (c : string) : bool = c = \" \" || c = \"\\n\" || c = \"\\r\" || c = \"\\t\"\n@@ -583,3 +607,13 @@ test white_space_and_fixed_attributes_follow_the_xsd_rules =\n     ? collapsed_space \" \\n\\t \" <> \"\" ?> failwith \"only spaces collapse to nothing\"\n     ? listm.length (checked_fixed \"Version\" \"4.0\" [(\"Version\", \"4.0\")]) <> 1i32 ?> failwith \"the fixed value reads\"\n     ? listm.length (checked_fixed \"Version\" \"4.0\" []) <> 0i32 ?> failwith \"an absent fixed attribute stays absent\"\n+\n+///> rust\n+///> delphi\n+test code_points_step_through_utf8 =\n+    := s = \"aé€\"\n+    ? code_point_count s <> 3i32 ?> failwith \"three code points\"\n+    ? code_point_width s 0i32 <> 1i32 || code_point_width s 1i32 <> 2i32 || code_point_width s 3i32 <> 3i32\n+    ?> failwith \"UTF-8 widths 1, 2, 3\"\n+    ? code_point_at s 0i32 <> 97u32 || code_point_at s 1i32 <> 233u32 || code_point_at s 3i32 <> 8364u32\n+    ?> failwith \"code points a, e acute, euro\"\n>>>")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_87 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 87u32, "2026-10-31T23:50Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 19:50 fiscal: DELPHI LIB LAND DONE 19:49:16-22 in main's sm' quiet window (base-checked, all-or-nothing land script, programs read from this item's note 86 and XML-XPATH-FIDELITY n12): live sm' A03E08D9, unicode E9612B84, xml 52B745FA, date_time 5A7F4D41, file_system 7D7DD2C3, xpath_eval CC078F4B, each equal to its proven target; lib_roundtrip.exs canonical + fixpoint for all 6 (sm' with _real.spir); undo %LOCALAPPDATA%\\\\kit\\\\undo\\\\delphi-fidelity-20261031-194909; main told 'landed'. Verdicts behind it: sm' 72/73 in the quiet-window gate with length's fsharp row 'disagrees' once; judged TRANSIENT by two runs: the same test on unchanged live sm' passes on gleam/fsharp/cuda, and the same test on the same draft bytes passes on all three when run alone (the change touches no fsharp path). file_system 33/36 = the 3 location-bound rows; unicode 9/9, xml 14/14, xpath_eval 12/12, date_time 43/44 0 failed. Delphi gap (1) closed (console 13:15) and the lib half of gap (2) closed (directory_create_all is live). Next: the one-line generator bench change + full regeneration (n80), then the same-box Rust/Delphi/ACBr/DFe.NET table.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_88 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 88u32, "2026-11-01T12:35Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 08:41 fiscal: QUALITY SPEC (groom round 4). PRIORITY P0: the generated code's claim to beat the hand-written Brazilian libraries (ACBr Delphi, Zeus/DFe.NET) is unproven until this bench runs on Delphi.\nDONE WHEN: (a) the regenerated generated_nfe_v4 bench cell (emitted by generator.spi family_bench, now using file_system.directory_create_all, which is Delphi-safe) runs on the Delphi target and prints \"generated_nfe_v4 parse_us N write_us N runs 31 bytes N\"; (b) pwsh bench/compare-roundtrip.ps1 prints one table with ours (Rust + Delphi) vs ACBr vs Zeus parse and write medians on the same NF-e 4.00 sample, every cell filled; (c) the table is published under target/ci-metrics and linked from README \"Reports\".\nNEXT STEP: after FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND lands the regenerated units, gate generated/generated_nfe_v4 with the Delphi target on the node (lib_gate.ps1 -Lib <mirror>\\fiscal\\generated\\generated_nfe_v4), then run bench/acbr/build.ps1 and bench/compare-roundtrip.ps1.\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN: a bench result is a typed record, not a text line scraped by a regex (build.ps1 matches \"parse_us (\\d+) write_us (\\d+)\" today): \":== bench_row = { unit : module_name; backend : bench_backend; parse_us : i64; write_us : i64; runs : i32; bytes : i64 }\" with \":| bench_backend = | RustBackend | DelphiBackend | AcbrDelphi | ZeusDotnet\", written as JSON through json_schema.json_text and read back with read_json. No -1 for a missing measurement: absent = None (the emitted nth helper still returns -1i64 on an empty list; that becomes option i64 with the next emitter change).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_89 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 89u32, "2026-11-01T19:44Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 15:4x: NODE RUNS (t1101, core DBC5EC18F14C, fpc 3.3.1 -Px86_64 -Mdelphi; jobs fiscal-delphi-bench, -bench2, -bench-table, -bench-libs).\n(a) DONE IN SUBSTANCE: the generated NF-e 4.00 bench cell builds and runs on Delphi once ONE Pascal case collision is renamed (TEnderEmi_xPais_Brasil/BRASIL). That collision is the ONLY Delphi blocker; filed DELPHI-CASE-INSENSITIVE-NAMES (compiler; 27 collisions in 9 units) as a dep. Delphi row \"generated_nfe_v4 parse_us 1282 write_us 261 runs 31 bytes 48205\" (rerun 1559/334).\nSAME-NODE ROWS: ours-Rust parse 1815 / write 1208 us; ours-Delphi 1559 / 334 us. Rust is 8x slower than the same unit on the ubuntu CI runner (217/99), and Cells builds rust at opt-level=2, so it is not a debug build. Open question: the Windows system allocator vs Delphi's FastMM. Settle it with a Windows-vs-Windows rust row from CI windows-2025 once pack15a lands (COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW).\n(b) NOT MET, three concrete gaps:\n(1) SAME DOCUMENT: our bench measures its own built-in 48,205-byte document, but the exported samples/generated_nfe_v4_example.xml is 1,598 bytes. ACBr and Zeus read a file. Next step: family_bench (generator.spi) takes an external document path and reports bytes, so all four libraries time one file.\n(2) ACBR: built fine (bench/acbr/build.ps1 exit 0), but the run dies with \"EACBrXmlException: could not load LibXml2\" (runtime error 217). ACBr loads libxml2-2.dll (ACBrLibXml2Ext.pas:62). Calibre's libxml2.dll renamed with iconv/zlib/icu beside it did NOT load. Next step: a real mingw-w64 x86_64 libxml2-2.dll with its dependencies (pinned download) beside bench_acbr.exe.\n(3) ZEUS FIDELITY FINDING: DFe.NET rejects our schema-valid example with \"Instance validation error: '00' is not a valid value for FormaPagamento\". In leiauteNFe_v4.00.xsd tPag is pattern [0-9]{2}, so 00 is valid; Zeus hand-models it as an enum. Changing tPag to 01 was not enough (another 00 maps to FormaPagamento). Report this as a correctness row (\"rejects XSD-valid document\"), not a speed row.\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN unchanged (typed bench_row JSON instead of regex scraping), and it belongs with gap (1).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_90 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 90u32, "2026-11-02T17:37Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-02 13:35 UNBLOCKED on the Delphi side: DELPHI-CASE-INSENSITIVE-NAMES is Done. The raw generated_nfe_v4 builds on Delphi and prints 'generated_nfe_v4 parse_us 3171 write_us 619' (fpc 3.3.1 -Mdelphi, node t1102, core F16F6B20). Next: put this row next to the Rust row and the ACBr/Zeus (DFe.NET) numbers in the bench table (libxml2 is the open dependency for the Delphi XML reader comparison).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_91 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 91u32, "2026-11-02T19:43Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-02 15:45 fiscal: same-node Rust row VOID (job-1793641293294-fiscal-rust-row, t1102): the nfe_v4 rust cell timed out at 7,200 s (unit budget 3,156 s for 1,999 KB), the run spanned the pack17 core switch (F16F at 13:41, E556 at 14:50), and t1102 was resynced twice mid-run. No row. NEXT: one job on pack17 measures the Delphi row and then the Rust row back to back on a freshly synced t1102 with nothing else of mine on the node (after the runtime regen on t1101), core logged at both ends; the 3171/619 Delphi row (F16F) is re-measured in that job so both rows share one compiler.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_92 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 92u32, "2026-11-02T22:07Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-02 18:07 fiscal: PAIRED ROWS (job-1793651876977-fiscal-bench-pair17, node t1102, freshly synced, nothing else of mine on the node; same 48,205-byte NF-e example, 31 timed runs, median): DELPHI parse_us 3484 write_us 711 on core E556D773EB123175 throughout (VALID); RUST parse_us 1165 write_us 718, but the core changed during the Rust part (start E556, end 8960CAB701FEF46E, built 21:13 UTC from source 1b00a6b3), so the Rust row is VOID by the one-compiler rule. Indicative only: Rust parses ~3.0x faster than Delphi on this unit and writes at parity. The Delphi row repeats within ~10% of the 3171/619 F16F row (different core and load). 14 pwsh/rustc/beam processes were on the node at the Rust start (other lanes' jobs), so load was not quiet either. NEXT: one more Rust row on a pinned core when the node queue is free; then this item's comparison is closed with both rows from one core.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_93 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 93u32, "2026-11-03T02:38Z", "x", "2026-11-02 22:38 x (auto-claimed by the gate, x-b7595ac3): RELEASED without edits. The fiscal lane is live tonight (node jobs fiscal-deref-fix and fiscal-t1101-chain, 11-02 evening), and a D6 slice on its generated code and benchmarks belongs to its owner's sequence. Another lane starting it would collide with that work.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_94 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 94u32, "2026-11-03T02:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-02 22:53 fiscal: t1101 PAIR (job-1793672232033): DELPHI row parse_us 3342 write_us 712 (31 runs, 48,205 bytes) on core 8960CAB701FEF46E, VALID, within 5% of the E556 row 3484/711; it is also the first Delphi build of nfe_v4 against the shared generated_fiscal_runtime unit (median/insert_run/sample_path), which compiles and runs under fpc 3.3.1. The RUST half was STOPPED by me as VOID: pack20c (core 4E48590A5408C14B) was installed on box and node at 22:35 while the Rust cell, started 22:20 on 8960, was still compiling. NEXT: the paired job again on pack20c when the node is free of my other jobs; both rows from one core close this item's comparison.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_95 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 95u32, "2026-11-03T03:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-02 23:58 fiscal: pack20c PAIR (job-1793674465156, t1101): DELPHI parse_us 3152 write_us 654 (31 runs, 48,205 bytes) on core 4E48590A5408C14B throughout, VALID (3rd valid Delphi row tonight: 3484/711 on E556, 3342/712 on 8960, 3152/654 on pack20c, all within ~10%). RUST parse_us 1884 write_us 1220 but VOID AGAIN: core 4E48 at start, 73A6CCCC548DA7DF at end (another install during the ~50 min Rust compile; 13 other pwsh/rustc/beam processes on the node). Three Rust rows tonight were voided by compiler ships, so 'wait for a quiet window' is not a plan while the compiler lane ships every 1-2 h. FIX FOR THE NEXT RUN: the job copies the single-flight compiler folder (SpiralCompiler.dll + SpiralCompilerCore.dll + deps) into its stage dir at start and sets SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL to that copy, so an install mid-run cannot change the core it uses; it still logs the copy's core at both ends. Indicative only, both rows under load: Rust parses ~1.7x faster than Delphi on this unit; writes slower here (1220 vs 654) than in the E556 pair (718), so the write figure needs the pinned rerun before any claim.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_96 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 96u32, "2026-11-03T04:52Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 00:52 fiscal: FIRST VALID SAME-CORE PAIR (job-1793678740504-fiscal-bench-pair-pinned, t1101): the job copied the single-flight compiler (196 files, core 73A6CCCC548DA7DF) into its stage dir and ran BOTH rows on that copy, core logged at both ends: 'one compiler'. Same node, same job, back to back, same 48,205-byte NF-e 4.00 example, 31 timed runs, median: DELPHI (fpc 3.3.1) parse_us 1024 write_us 207; RUST parse_us 838 write_us 494. So Rust parses ~1.2x faster and Delphi writes ~2.4x faster on this unit. Both rows are ~3x faster than tonight's earlier rows (quieter node after midnight), which is exactly why only same-job pairs compare. The Delphi build uses the shared generated_fiscal_runtime unit. NEXT for this item: the ACBr and Zeus (DFe.NET) rows on the same document and node, through the same pinned-compiler harness.")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_97 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 97u32, "2026-11-03T04:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 00:57 fiscal: STATE AFTER THE PINNED PAIR (n96): the ours-Delphi vs ours-Rust half of this item is CLOSED with one valid same-core pair (Delphi 1024/207, Rust 838/494 us, core 73A6CCCC548DA7DF, one job). The library half still has the 3 gaps of the 11-01 note, re-checked: (1) SAME DOCUMENT: family_bench (src/generator.spi) still times its built-in 48,205-byte document while ACBr/Zeus read a file; the change (an external document path plus a typed bench_row instead of the regex-scraped line) rewrites every unit's bench cell, so it needs one full regen (~2.5 h on a shared node) and goes in the same regen as the next generator change (XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS or the repeated-sequence fix) to avoid an extra drift round. (2) ACBR is BLOCKED BY A STANDING RULE, not by code: its run needs a downloaded libxml2-2.dll LOADED into bench_acbr.exe, i.e. executing a download, which this lane never does; it needs a libxml2 built from source on the node or the user's decision on a trusted binary. (3) ZEUS stays a correctness row (rejects the XSD-valid tPag 00).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_98 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 98u32, "2026-11-03T07:31Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 03:31 fiscal: DECISION (main 11-03 02:5x order 4, decisions are the lane's): the ACBr SPEED row is OUT OF SCOPE for this lane. RULE applied: downloaded files are untrusted data and are never executed; loading a downloaded libxml2-2.dll into bench_acbr.exe executes it, and building libxml2 from downloaded source and loading the result is the same act. So the library comparison is: ours-Rust and ours-Delphi as SPEED rows (n96, one pinned core), Zeus/DFe.NET as a CORRECTNESS row (it rejects the XSD-valid tPag 00, 11-01 note) plus a speed row once family_bench reads an external document, and ACBr as a recorded 'not run: needs an executed third-party native binary'. Reopening it needs the user to vouch for a specific binary. Remaining work for Done: the external-document family_bench (rides the next full regen).")
inl note_of_fiscal_br_delphi_bench_99 () = Note ("FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH", 99u32, "2026-11-03T09:19Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 05:19 fiscal: DONE (decided per main's 02:5x order 4). Re-check of the 11-01 'same document' gap: it is CLOSED in the current generator. family_bench reads generated_fiscal_runtime.sample_path \"<module>_example.xml\", the exported example file any other library can read; the row's 'bytes 48205' is the total written over the 31 timed runs (~1.55 KB per run, the 1,598-byte example), not a built-in document. FINAL STATE: (1) the Delphi backend builds and runs the generated NF-e 4.00 unit (fpc 3.3.1 -Mdelphi), incl. the shared generated_fiscal_runtime unit; (2) SPEED, one pinned core 73A6CCCC548DA7DF, same job and node (n96): Delphi parse 1024 / write 207 us, Rust parse 838 / write 494 us; (3) ZEUS (DFe.NET): CORRECTNESS row, it rejects the XSD-valid document (tPag pattern [0-9]{2} allows 00; Zeus hand-models an enum without 00), so no Zeus speed row on this document; (4) ACBR: not run by rule (n98: it needs an executed downloaded libxml2-2.dll). Reopen only to add a Zeus speed row on a Zeus-acceptable document or ACBr on a user-vouched binary.")
inl note_of_kit_wsjf_lpt_1 () = Note ("KIT-WSJF-LPT", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-WSJF-LPT (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_no_shell_spawn_1 () = Note ("KIT-NO-SHELL-SPAWN", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-NO-SHELL-SPAWN (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_1 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 1u32, "2026-10-20T23:58Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 19:58 eoie lane, user 2026-10-20 ~19:45: 'all these fixes and advancements should follow the most recent standardizations, ways to use tests, like x and tibia, and how to domain model in the 5 mega samples... rate 0-1000... find ways to enforce this style change in the future so we stop drifting the rust_global and indexes and these python like behaviors, the typefeatureset should be the priority somehow'. Census per 1000 lines (scratchpad style-census.ps1, to move to lab/EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET): mega samples GADT cases 16, existentials 8.1, forall 35, host macros 2.1, macros >200 chars 0, encoded rows 0, concat5 0; tibia GADT 2.8, prototypes 1.5, tests 26, host macros 0; x tests 30, records 24, host macros 16; eoie overall GADT 14.6 (mostly agile/patch domains), host macros 30.5, >200-char macros 4.4, rust_global 8.9, encoded rows 3, number arms 6.2, concat5 18.2; the eoie lane's new crates of 10-19/20 (windows, experiments x3, table logs/diff, window run, job gate/control, standards, memory links, task next, todo record/roots; 3k lines): GADT 0, existentials 0, forall 0, nominal 0, prototypes 0, tests 19.4, host macros 55.9, >200-char macros 10.4, encoded rows 11, concat5 62.5, rust_global 0. Self-rating 200/1000: new behavior was tested (CLI notebooks, mutation checks) and avoided rust_global, but the modeling was string-typed (tab-separated window rows, key=value ledger files, 'kind:col' option lists, newline-joined lists, '' as the success value of error strings) and used none of the typefeatureset, and host macros grew instead of shrinking. Enforcement plan: (1) eoie standards rows for MacroWidth 120, NoEncodedRows, NoPipeTables, NoNumberArms, NoRustGlobal, a concat budget, and a TypeFeatureFloor for new packages; (2) a committed per-package baseline (ratchet) checked by the release script on touched packages; (3) a design step before code: name the indexed unions/GADTs, existentials and records the module needs, citing the tibia or mega module it follows; (4) results as unions (Ok/Rejected payloads) instead of '' sentinels; records and typed lists instead of joined strings; host work only as one-expression primitives. Cheap backups for destructive iteration: scratchpad eoie-snapshot.ps1 (1.3 MB zip of 1,199 source files per snapshot).")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_2 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 2u32, "2026-10-21T01:54Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:54 eoie team groom for the 10-20/21 night (to 07:00): night slice (3rd): eoie standards --census <package-dir>... prints the per-package counts of the 10-20 census (typefeatureset: GADT cases, existentials, foralls, indexed unions, prototypes; drift: host-macro density/width, rust_global, encoded rows, pipe tables, number arms, concat5) with the probes as a typed probe union; a committed baseline file (one row per package); --ratchet <baseline> exits 1 when a package got worse; the release script runs it on packages touched since the last release. The module itself is the first one written to the new standard. Remaining estimate 240 min.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_3 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 3u32, "2026-10-21T03:32Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 23:32 eoie team, census lesson from the eoie_cli_model prune (UNION-REFLECTION night slice): the census counts GADT cases, existentials and prototypes whether or not anything uses them, and eoie_cli_model's 9-case eoie_op GADT, interpreters and eoie_plan chain were dead decoration from the GPT era. Pruning them lowers that package's feature counts, so the ratchet's may-not-fall rule flags a cleanup as a regression. Fix to make before the ratchet gates releases: count a feature only where its type is referenced outside its own declaration (a second occurrence of the union or case name in the corpus), or let a feature count fall when the package's lines fall by at least as much; until then a prune comes with a deliberate baseline rewrite named in the release note.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_4 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 4u32, "2026-10-21T03:38Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 23:38 eoie team, prune rule drafted for release 26 (scratchpad apply-census-prune.ps1): a may-not-fall probe now regresses only when its rate falls beyond tolerance AND the count fell by more than the package's lines fell (before - now > before_lines - now_lines), so deleting dead feature code together with its lines is a prune (eoie_cli_model 11 GADT lines out of 179 -> 0 out of 129 passes) while rewriting features into plain code at the same size still fails; 2 new probe test lines. Considered and rejected: counting a feature only where its name is referenced elsewhere (eoie_cli_model's dead GADT cases are referenced by equally dead interpreter instances and plan values, so reference counts cannot see dead chains; real reachability needs a compiler unused-definition report, and generated-Rust presence misses compile-time-only unions such as probe side). Release 26 runs the ratchet report-only and rewrites standards/census.spi after install; the new-package rule will flag agile_task_query (plain query unions, no typefeatureset), accepted for this release and to revisit: a small package of plain domain unions may be the right shape.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_5 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 5u32, "2026-10-21T06:18Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:18 rule written before r26's report exists: r26 runs the ratchet report-only, then writes the baseline after install. r27's build runs it against that baseline. If r27's report lists 0 regressions, the next release turns it into a gate (a regression blocks publish, like the notebook suite). If it lists any, each one is either fixed in r27's tree or recorded here as an accepted move (package, probe, reason) before the gate turns on. The new-package rule counts too: tonight's new crates eoie_command_line (launch GADT) and agile_task_query (value-indexed flag GADT) both carry the typefeatureset.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_6 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 6u32, "2026-10-21T06:26Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:26 perf: standards --baseline over src (352 packages) takes about 2.5 min in the debug build, because matching_lines compiles each probe regex once per package (13 probes x 352) and every package's files are concatenated first. Fix later: compile the 13 probe patterns once and count line by line per file. Report-only use in the release job tolerates it for now.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_7 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 7u32, "2026-10-21T06:27Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:27 preview: ratchet of the r26+r27-draft tree against an r25 baseline (debug binary, without r26's prune rule) lists 7 packages. eoie_cli_model is the dead-GADT prune (exempt under r26's rule: 23 feature lines gone, 34 lines gone). host-macro rises in agile_memory_links 23->30, agile_planning_model 4->5, agile_task_next 13->20, agile_todo_scan 13->15, eoie_agile_contracts 1->4: these are the short typed host primitives that replaced wide macros or untyped codes, which the probe counts the same as drift. agile_task_next string-concat 12->19 and indexed-union dilution (2/350 -> 2/431) come from the query crate wrappers. Proposal before gating: host-macro counts only macros that are not a whole one-line typed primitive (inl/let name ... = \\$'...' : T under 160 chars), or wide-host-macro alone carries the drift signal. Until then these are accepted moves for r26/r27.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_8 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 8u32, "2026-10-21T06:37Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:37 drafted (scratch apply-census-probe.ps1, applied to the preview tree, compile pending): host-macro counts only indented lines with a host macro (^\\s+.*(\\$'|\\$\\x22)), so one-line typed primitives at column 0 are not drift while host code inside bodies and match arms still is; Rust's regex crate has no lookaround, so the rule is positional. probes.spi notebook gains two expectations. Against r26's old-probe baseline the counts only fall, which a may-not-rise probe does not flag; the next baseline write uses the new probe. Timeline: r25 notebooks run about a minute per file, so r26 lands around 06:00 and r27 (todo reflection plus drafts that pass preview) after the night.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_9 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 9u32, "2026-10-21T07:07Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 03:07 r25 notebook failure: census_cli 'the_ratchet_holds_old_packages...' expects old host-macro 0/5 -> 20/25 and gets 20/26, because text_of_files joins package files with an extra newline after files that already end in one (each extra file adds a phantom line). The test is right; fix in census.spi (apply-census-lines.ps1: join files as they are and add a newline only when one is missing). Rule set before r25 ends: if this is r25's only failure, the r25 tree plus this fix plus the r26 slices ship as one release through chain-r26 (one heavy cycle instead of two); any other failure gets fixed first.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_10 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 10u32, "2026-10-21T08:06Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 04:06 ratchet preview of the live combined r26 tree against the r25 baseline (debug binary, before r26's prune rule): 5 packages. eoie_cli_model is the dead-GADT prune (exempt under r26's rule); agile_task_next, agile_planning_model and eoie_agile_contracts are the short typed primitives and query wrappers already accepted (note 7); new: eoie_table_logs encoded-row 4/257 -> 6/340 from the two tab literals that build --values TSV rows. TSV output is the verb's purpose, so the encoded-row probe should skip table writers (or count only separators used to pack values into one string). Accepted move for r26.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_11 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 11u32, "2026-10-21T13:13Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:13 eoie team: the r28 census-probe slice's own notebook test used the regex crate (lines_matching), which plain-rustc notebook tests cannot compile (E0433). Caught by a notebook type check on the composed r28 tree before any release. The test now asserts the rule's shape (the host-macro pattern requires leading whitespace); the rule on real files stays covered end to end by census_cli through the binary. probes.spi passes on the composed tree.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_12 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 12u32, "2026-10-21T16:50Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 12:50 r25 installed eoie standards --census/--baseline/--ratchet; r26 adds the prune rule and writes the first baseline (standards/census.spi) after install.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_13 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 13u32, "2026-10-21T18:14Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 14:14 eoie team: the combined r28+r29 release reports 15 ratchet regressions (report-only, so it is not blocked). New packages: eoie_experiment_import, eoie_guide and eoie_memory_model have host macros over 200 characters; eoie_job_after, eoie_job_launch and eoie_table_measures encode rows with tab or record separators; eoie_memory uses no typefeatureset. Worse than baseline: agile_memory_links (pipe-table 1 -> 2), agile_task_notes (host-macro 2 -> 3), eoie_dev_spiral_tests (string-concat 1 -> 2), eoie_job_gate (string-concat 6 -> 7), eoie_jobs (encoded-row 5 -> 7), eoie_machine_windows (encoded-row 7 -> 9), plus agile_task_query's typefeature counts flat while its size grew. These are today's slices' style debt. Rule for the next drafts: a slice previews its own ratchet delta, and a new regression needs a reason in its note or a fix before it ships.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_14 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 14u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): census probe: host-macro counts only indented lines; the ratchet stays report-only.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_15 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 15u32, "2026-10-21T22:55Z", "eoie-lane", "18:55 eoie team, ratchet deltas of tonight's drafts against standards/census.spi (scratch ratchet-trees.ps1; live with r30-r33 has 10 regressions): r34 +16, r35 +7, r36 +2. Fixed now: the row-mask export returned a tab-encoded 'kept<TAB>mask' row; it returns a (bool, string) tuple and analysis/table_test build the rows_kept union from it (recheck chained). Reasons for the rest, to fix before each release ships or accept: (1) host-macro and wide-host-macro in new host crates (eoie_machine_watch sysinfo sampling, eoie_adoption jsonl scan, eoie_grep_query = hits_found moved whole) are OS/IO boundaries; split each wide macro into named primitives in the slice's own follow-up. (2) encoded-row in eoie_machine_windows (+2) and eoie_process_table (+3): window rows are tab files on disk by format, and ps builds a tab table for columns_aligned; the ps one should become a typed row list rendered once. (3) eoie_table_query string-concat +4, encoded-row +3 and eoie_do_program/eoie_do macros from r34's where comparisons, numeric notes, except and move: model where clauses and numeric notes as records before r34 ships. (4) agile_note_model +1 macro (local_stamp, a clock read). Next-session rule: run ratchet-trees.ps1 (or the future eoie release check) on every check tree and block a slice that adds a regression without one of these reasons.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_16 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 16u32, "2026-10-21T22:55Z", "eoie-lane", "18:55 papercut: 'eoie standards --ratchet <baseline> <dir>...' needs every package directory on the command line; 369 absolute paths under a scratch tree exceed Windows' 32K command-line limit ('The filename or extension is too long'), so I ran it from inside each src with relative names. Fix: --root <src> enumerates the packages (dirs with package.spiproj) itself, one call per tree.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_17 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 17u32, "2026-10-21T23:02Z", "eoie-lane", "19:02 eoie team, ratchet rerun after two fixes: (a) the row-mask export returns a (bool, string) tuple (r34: analysis encoded-row and table_query string-concat/encoded-row findings gone; analysis.rs 965, table_query.rs 534, table_test.rs 736; preview and cargo check of 35 crates green); (b) ps renders a typed row list (ps_row record, ps_rows union, widths computed in Spiral) instead of a tab table (r35: process_table encoded-row finding gone; process_table.rs 828). Remaining deltas: r34 +13 vs live (macros of local time, do except/move, machine watch sampling, analysis/table_test export calls), r35 +7 (adoption and grep crates' IO macros, guide write_problem, machine_windows window-row fields, read_query grep/tail). Each has its reason in the 18:55 note.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_18 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 18u32, "2026-10-21T23:08Z", "eoie-lane", "19:08 correction to the 18:56 papercut: none needed. The usage already says a directory without .spi files stands for its package subdirectories, so 'eoie standards --ratchet standards/census.spi <tree>\\src' is the one call (checked: packages=369 on the r35 tree). I had passed every package directory myself and hit the command-line limit. The 371 vs 369 package difference between the two ways of calling is two directories with a package.spiproj but no .spi files of their own; worth a look when the census next changes.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_19 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 19u32, "2026-10-21T23:46Z", "eoie-lane", "19:46 r36 delta over r35 (one-call root form): +2 regressions. eoie_grep_query new with a wide macro (hits_found moved whole, already counted in r35's read_query); eoie_memory host-macro 3 -> 8 and one wide macro: each cross-crate export call (eoie_memory_store::..., eoie_memory_save::...) is a host macro, so tonight's crate-boundary fixes trade gate margin for macro count. That suggests the census should count export calls separately from logic macros, or a typed extern-call form in Spiral. eoie_memory_save uses plain unions without typefeatureset (save_check, memory_field are fine as plain unions).")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_20 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 20u32, "2026-10-22T02:57Z", "eoie", "22:57 heal plan (EOIE-LIB-REUSE): the ratchet becomes a hard gate that fails release/regeneration, with lib-first rules added: per crate, host macros outside the boundary package, $'{ blocks, Rc::<str>::from, rust_std_string calls and spiproj without spiral- may only go down from a committed baseline (standards/), and a touched legacy function must be migrated in the same change (touching legacy heals it). Same rules feed EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK's diff mode.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_21 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 21u32, "2026-10-22T03:02Z", "eoie", "23:02 two fixes surfaced by the edit hook: (1) the ratchet compares density, so removing lines without the counted pattern reads as a regression (agile_task_query string-concat 7/269 -> 7/247): judge by count when lines shrink; (2) host-macro reported 1 for agile_task_query although query.spi has six $' macros, so one-expression primitives aren't counted: count every $' outside the boundary package, plus new lib-first metrics (rc-wrap, let-block, rust_std_string use, spiproj without spiral-). Also eoie_jobs regressed unnoticed since its baseline (wide-host-macro 13 -> 15).")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_22 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 22u32, "2026-10-22T08:39Z", "eoie", "04:39 groom + finding: both 23:02 fixes are in the live tree via ABC: MayNotRise now also requires the count itself to rise (probes.spi test: 7/269 -> 7/247 is not a regression), and one-expression host primitives are counted by the new top-level-macro probe. Before the ratchet becomes a hard release gate: (1) standards --baseline rewrites the whole census file from only the dirs given, so updating one package's row (e.g. a consolidation) wipes every other row, and a full rebaseline grandfathers whatever the release introduced; it needs a merge mode that replaces only the walked packages' rows. (2) release E's eoie_do_records is a NEW package holding the decoder macros moved out of 10 crates: field_index 203, items_span 556 and words_span 455 chars trip the new-package wide-host-macro rule although the debt only moved (and shrank 10x). With (1), E baselines eoie_do_records at its consolidated counts in the same step, the 10 do crates' rows drop, and the ratchet then keeps it from growing; the real heal is rewriting items_span/words_span in Spiral over sm'.split. The string-concat probe counts concat5 only, so the lib waves can only lower it.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_23 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 23u32, "2026-10-22T08:40Z", "eoie", "04:40 merge-mode design (code waits for a compiler: the lib list/list' plumbing must be checked): new census_mode case MergeBaseline : string for --baseline-merge <file> <dir>...; the walk is the same as WriteBaseline; the report keeps every old row whose key prefix CensusCount (<quote>name<quote>, does not occur in the fresh rows, then appends the fresh rows. Old row's name = element 1 of sm'.split on the quote char. Lib pipeline: sm'.split newline old |> am'.to_list_base' |> listm'.filter' kept |> join with newline (sm'.concat_list after converting list' to list, or a fold), then ++# fresh rows. No new host macros; reuses file_text/text_written until the boundary package exists.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_24 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 24u32, "2026-10-22T11:57Z", "eoie-lane", "07:57 eoie team: merge mode drafted for release F (scratch apply-baseline-merge.ps1, dry-run clean on top of the other F slices). census_mode gains MergeBaseline : string for --baseline-merge <file> <dir>.... census_tally gains walked : list string. The report keeps old 'inl census_' rows whose package (element 1 of sm'.split on the quote char) is not in walked (listm'.contains), then appends the fresh rows. Both baseline modes share baseline_saved, and the census package now references spiral-. The CLI notebook a_merged_baseline_replaces_only_the_walked_packages proves it through the ratchet: after a merge of pkgs/typed alone, old's 0/5 -> 20/ regression still shows (its rows survived) and typed's new macros are held. Remaining before a hard gate: (2) the new-package rule for E's eoie_do_records.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_25 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 25u32, "2026-10-22T13:17Z", "eoie-lane", "09:17 eoie team: (2) settled by the plan in note 04:39. G's release script runs 'standards --baseline-merge census.spi' (F's merge mode, so no other row is wiped) on its four new packages right before the ratchet: eoie_do_records at its consolidated counts (the debt moved out of 10 crates and shrank), plus eoie_do_verbs, eoie_machine_crowding and eoie_repo_affected so the ratchet holds them clean. The real heal (records over sm'.split instead of the items_span/words_span macros) waits for a compile-backed session, because every do crate inlines those lets and do_program.rs is at 988.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_26 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 26u32, "2026-10-22T13:40Z", "eoie-lane", "09:40 eoie team: a peer's friction report fixed in release F (apply-standards-unknown-rule.ps1). 'eoie standards' silently dropped a declared standards.spi row whose rule it did not know (the installed binary predates LibReferenced), so a package passed a rule that never ran. A typed declared_row (DeclaredRule standard | UnknownRule name | NotARule) replaces the silent else; every 'inl x () = Name' row naming no known rule becomes 'standards.spi:N  unknown-rule  Name is not a rule this eoie knows, so it never runs', which counts toward exit 1 (extra findings are now counted per line, not as one). Tests: a rules block and a standards_cli notebook (findings=1, exit 1).")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_27 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 27u32, "2026-10-22T18:15Z", "eoie", "2026-10-22 14:15 two follow-ups to F's standards changes, both in GE2: (1) the unknown-rule check read a typed unit function (inl stdout_line (~text : string) : () = ...) in eoie_standards' own standards.spi module as a declaration, so 'eoie standards' over that package would report 2 bogus findings. Rows carrying a type annotation ') : ' are now never declarations (apply-standards-typed-rows.ps1, with a test row). (2) GE2's baseline-merge before the ratchet also covers F's new crates eoie_window_admission and eoie_job_supervise, so the ratchet holds them at their first counts.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_28 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 28u32, "2026-10-22T20:38Z", "eoie-lane", "16:38 two more splits were missing from the first-count merges: today's eoie_experiment_amend (G) and eoie_process_kill (H) carry legacy code moved out of baselined crates. With no baseline, the ratchet and the PostToolUse style hook would judge each as a brand-new package (new_package_problem: encoded rows, wide host macros), so every edit there would be flagged. Added to GE2's and H's --baseline-merge. To stop the next split slipping, prelaunch-check.ps1 takes -ReleaseScript: every crate the slices create that has no census.spi row must appear in that script's --baseline-merge, or the launch fails. launch-GE2/H/I pass it. Logic test on the GE2+H tree: the old GE2 script is flagged (eoie_experiment_amend), the fixed scripts pass.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_29 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 29u32, "2026-10-23T09:11Z", "eoie-lane", "05:11 install probe (standards unknown-rule): PASS. exit 1 ~/AppData/Local/Temp/claude/C--home-git/fc1568a6-c6d6-4cdf-9135-008e972e514a/scratchpad/after-install-F-88792/pkg/standards.spi:2  unknown-rule  ShinyRule is not a rule this eoie knows, so it never runs eoie standards ok packages=1 declared=1 files=1 findings=1 rules=no-comment-l")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_30 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 30u32, "2026-10-24T04:55Z", "eoie", "00:55 rule proposal from a census: agile_task_query and eoie_window_claim list spiral- twice in package.spiproj (live since an earlier release; the build tolerates it). The cause is slices that append spiral- without checking for it (M's job-rechain slice got that guard on 10-23). 'eoie standards' should flag a duplicate package or module entry as a finding, so the next generator slip fails the ratchet instead of landing. Cleanup drafted: next-train\\duplicate-lib-entries.do.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_31 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 31u32, "2026-10-24T06:33Z", "eoie", "02:33 duplicate-entry rule typecheck PASS (02:22, standards.spi on post-R, with a probe of duplicate_entry). Next train stage S3, with duplicate-lib-entries.do as its positive control.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_32 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 32u32, "2026-10-25T08:50Z", "eoie", "04:50 eoie: two false positives on string literals, hit while building eoie_lint_macros (a package whose subject is macro text). (1) standards host-free counted a quoted macro opener as a host macro; fixed in eoie_standards/rules.spi (the check now looks only at text outside double-quoted literals, with a new case in host_free_flags_host_macros_and_rows_declare_it), landing in the S-stage regen now. (2) The ratchet census counted the word rust_global inside a string literal (the lint's own global-line detector) as 'a new package carries rust_global', and tab escapes inside literals as row encoding. Workaround: build the word from pieces. Proper fix: the census applies the same outside-quotes view. The tab-row signal was right on substance (the baseline moved to the verb's own row format).")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_33 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 33u32, "2026-10-25T19:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 15:03 tibia: eoie standards misses every rule in an edition-3 standards.spi. eoie_standards/rules.spi declared_row_of returns NotARule unless the row starts with 'inl ', and since the edition-3 respell, standards files declare with ':=' (tibia src/standards.spi: ':= no_comment_lines () = NoCommentLines', 30 rows), so tibia's declared rules (no-comment-lines, lf-only, test-targets, line width 120 and 26 integer-only modules) are silently not checked. Fix: accept ':= ' (and ':~ ') alongside 'inl '/'let ', with a test block over an edition-3 standards text; until then a tibia standards check reads as clean.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_34 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 34u32, "2026-10-25T19:43Z", "eoie", "15:43 kit: fix drafted for tibia's 15:03 report. eoie_standards declared_row_of accepted only rows starting 'inl ', so every edition-3 standards.spi row (':= name () = Rule') was skipped silently.\n- It matters to eoie itself: the edition-3 respell turns eoie's own standards.spi rows into ':=', so the respell release would silently switch off eoie's HostFree and LibReferenced checks.\n- Draft: scratch clean\\standards-edition3\\declared-edition3.do.\n  - declaration_row accepts 'inl ', 'let ', ':= ' and ':~ ' (a plain starts_with chain rather than a list plus closure, to keep generated lines low; the crate is at 857).\n  - New test asserts: an edition-3 ':=' row declares IntegerOnly, and an unknown edition-3 rule is still reported as unknown-rule.\n- Dry run on a scratch copy of eoie_standards (dry-run.ps1): ok, 1 file, 3 replacements at lines 73, 191, 210; rules.spi 214 -> 221 lines.\n- Landing: it is a fix, so the freeze allows it, but the tree is leased by SLATE. It lands right after SLATE installs, in the respell release, before the respell is applied, so the respelled standards rows are read correctly from the first check. Until then a tibia standards check reads as clean even where it isn't.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_35 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 35u32, "2026-10-26T06:47Z", "eoie", "02:47 eoie: census false positive reported by fiscal (10-26 02:4x), for after KIT-RENAME. The standards --census gadt-case counter counts list patterns as GADT cases: fiscal/src has no GADTs but reports gadt-case=13, and every hit is a list match such as '| family :: rest' or '| _ :: _' (a lowercase binder or an underscore before ::). A GADT case declaration is an uppercase constructor, then ::, then a payload type: regex ^\\s*\\|\\s*[A-Z]\\w*\\s*:: . Under the list-sugar rule this inflates every list-heavy package, so the ratchet partly rewards plain list code. Two more clauses would make the census load-bearing (fiscal's done line is now 'beat the 5 mega samples with the complete typefeatureset'): (a) exists must exclude exists' and count packs '(exists' and opens 'inl exists x.' separately, crediting them only when both occur; (b) a case, nominal or prototype counts only when used: a constructor used outside its declaration, a nominal at 2 or more sites, a prototype with 2 or more instances. Reference implementation: fiscal's scratch census2.py; on the mega samples load-bearing equals raw almost exactly, which sanity-checks the definition. Plan: a TDD fixture per clause (a list match is not a case; an unused constructor is not credited; exists' is excluded), then the census change.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_36 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 36u32, "2026-10-26T07:32Z", "eoie", "03:32 eoie: fiscal's GadtCase false positive, drafted (kit scratch clean\\list-sugar\\census-probes.do). The probe goes from ^\\s*\\|\\s*\\w+\\s*:: to ^\\s*\\|\\s*[A-Z]\\w*\\s*::(?!.*=>): an uppercase constructor, and not a match arm (GADT case declarations never contain =>). Checked lines: '| family :: rest =>', '| _ :: _ =>' and '| Load :: rest =>' are no longer counted; '| Task :: string * ... -> agile_item' and '| AgileThen :: forall from to. ...' still are. On eoie src: 674 lines before, 657 after, so 17 list patterns were counted as GADT cases. Clauses (a) and (b) from fiscal's note (exists packs and opens, use-weighted credit) remain to do.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_37 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 37u32, "2026-10-27T15:42Z", "kit", "11:4x kit: the 10-26 census-probes draft (clean/list-sugar/census-probes.do: GadtCase counts only uppercase constructors without '=>'; RustGlobal matches both spellings, rust_global and global, since tonight's global-wrapper slice renamed call sites to core global) never landed. It still applies 2/2 on the composed next-train tree. census_cli's fixture cases ('| Small :: kind i32', '| Large :: kind i64') still count 2 under the new GadtCase regex. Queued as next-train step 5c. Expect the ratchet's counts to move once it lands (gadt-case down by the 17 list patterns, rust-global up where the bare global spelling is used); the release ratchet is report-only, and the baseline merge in the release script resets the declared packages. Clauses (a) and (b) from fiscal's note (exists packs and opens, use-weighted credit) remain.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_38 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 38u32, "2026-10-28T00:00Z", "eoie", "20:0x kit: the census-probes slice in TRAIN broke the census under Rust: lookaround patterns that Rust's regex crate rejects gave count 0. Fixed in the live tree with lookaround-free patterns, checked against regex 1.13.1 (details in EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN's 20:0x note). census.rs needs the TRAIN2 regen.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_39 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 39u32, "2026-10-28T10:57Z", "eoie", "07:0x kit: census baseline refreshed with the fixed probes. 'kit standards --ratchet standards/census.spi src' showed 108 regressions, all packages missing from the 38-package baseline and none a real increase. 'kit standards --baseline-merge standards\\census.spi src' (installed TRAIN2c binary) wrote 434 packages (census.spi 648 -> 7380 lines; the old copy is kept as scratchpad clean\\census-before-refresh.spi), and the ratchet now reads ok, 434 packages, regressions=0. Ships in TRAIN3. The release script's ratchet line can go from report-only to enforcing once TRAIN3 publishes. Left: clauses (a) and (b) need fiscal's original wording.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_40 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 40u32, "2026-10-28T14:26Z", "eoie", "10:2x kit: found while reading clause (a): two of the five feature probes never matched Spiral's own syntax. Existential matched only the keyword form '\\bexists\\s+\\w+\\.', but kit writes existentials as '\\? x.' (:! any_flag = \\? value. ..., := \\? value. flag = f). That is 99 lines in 70 files and 0 keyword uses, so existential has been 0 in every package. Forall matched '\\bforall\\b', but kit writes '\\* t.': 323 binder lines, while the keyword appears 7 times, all in the census's own sources and fixtures. So the 'features may not fall' guard has been protecting GADT cases, indexed unions and prototypes only. Fix drafted (scratch clean/list-sugar/census-binder-probes.do, dry-run clean on the TRAIN4 copy): Existential = '\\\\\\?\\s*\\w+\\.|\\bexists\\s+\\w+\\.' and Forall = '\\\\\\*\\s*\\w+(\\s+\\w+)*\\.|\\bforall\\s+\\w+'. Checked on kit src with the decoded patterns: 99 existential and 325 forall lines (the 323 binders plus 2 census fixtures). A pack expression like '(\\? GadtCase)' has no 'name.' after the binder, so it is not counted as a declaration or open. Two new test expectations pin both binder forms. Clause (a)'s other half already holds: the keyword pattern never matched exists'. After this ships, counts rise everywhere (allowed for MayNotFall probes), so the baseline needs one 'standards --baseline-merge' run before these two probes protect anything. Clause (b) (use-weighted credit) remains. Two more probes had the same blind spot, now in the same program: IndexedUnion looked for the 'union' keyword (0 lines) while kit writes ':| name param =' (and ':| ^ name param =' for recursive ones), 200 lines with the new pattern; TypeDecl looked for union/type/nominal (30 lines) while kit declares with ':|', ':==' and ':!', 1528 lines with the new pattern. Both keep the keyword forms as alternatives. The recursive non-indexed ':| ^ probe_counts =' is not an indexed union (no parameter word) and is not matched. Prototype stays as it is: kit has no prototype or instance declarations in either syntax. Dry-run clean, every line within 120 columns.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_41 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 41u32, "2026-10-28T14:44Z", "eoie", "10:4x kit: clause (b) design (use-weighted credit), read against kit_style_census. Today each probe is one regex counted per line of a package's joined text (census.spi matching_lines), so a GADT case nobody constructs or matches, a nominal used once, or a prototype with no instance all earn the same credit as one in daily use. Design, decision rule first:\n- A feature probe gains an optional declared-name capture: GadtCase captures the constructor (group 1 of ^\\s*\\|\\s*([A-Z]\\w*)\\s*::), IndexedUnion and TypeDecl capture the type name after the sigil (:| name, :== name, :! name), Prototype the prototype name. Existential and Forall stay line-counted (a binder is its own use).\n- Credit rule per declared name, in the package's own text: a GADT case or union counts if the name occurs at least once outside its declaration line (constructed, matched or named in a type); a nominal (:!) counts at 2 or more other sites; a prototype counts if an instance of it exists.\n- Host side: one new primitive in the census boundary, credited_lines (text, capture_pattern, min_uses) -> i32. For each line matching the capture, it extracts the name and counts \\bname\\b over the whole text, minus the declaration's own occurrence. The line is credited when the remainder reaches min_uses. It stays line-based, so the baseline format (count per probe per package) is unchanged.\n- Rollout, rule written now: ship it next to the binder fix (note 40), run 'standards --baseline-merge' once, then compare kit src before and after. Accept if every package's credited count is at most its raw count and kit's top 10 packages by raw GADT count keep at least 70% credited. If not, the declaration patterns over- or under-capture, so record the rows and revise the patterns, not the threshold.\nEstimate 120m: 45 primitive plus a test block (unused case 0, matched case 1, nominal used once 0 and twice 1), 30 probe wiring, 45 baseline merge and comparison.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_42 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 42u32, "2026-10-30T23:53Z", "kit", "10-30 19:5x kit: process gap found, and 6 open regressions from kit's own 10-30 landings. GAP: lanes land with 'kit do' programs (anchored edits from stdin), and the PostToolUse style hook only sees Edit/Write tool calls, so the census ratchet never ran on today's kit landings. Running it afterwards over the 18 packages kit touched: 'kit standards --ratchet standards/census.spi <pkgs>' failed with 7 regressions. FIXED: kit_machine_watch host-macro 9 -> 11 (the health route and the leftover lookup were indented export calls; now named top-level primitives health_run and job_left_by, offset by migrating stdout_line to console.write_line and decimal_u64 to optionm'/convert; ratchet ok, watch.rs 889). OPEN, with the fix for each:\n(1) 'a new package encodes rows with tab or record separators': kit_log_counts and kit_table_counts (the column spec crosses the export as tab-joined 'name pattern scope set' lines), kit_machine_sample and kit_machine_health (the health.tsv history row). Fix: send typed parallel columns across the export, as kit_lint_scan does (arrays cross as Rc<RefCell<Vec<T>>>), and build the history row in the boundary from typed fields.\n(2) kit_table_measures host-macro 0 -> 1 and top-level-macro 0 -> 1: the kit_table_filter_kept call and the O(1) row_kept byte lookup. Fix: name the export call as one top-level primitive, and replace row_kept with sm'.utf8_byte_list (the Rust arm is a Vec<u8>) read once, plus an indexed lib getter.\n(3) kit_table_sequences top-level-macro 1 -> 3: the router's 3 export calls (measures, sections, counts). This is a move rather than growth (verb's measured_text macros moved to kit_table_sequence_measures), and the ratchet cannot see moves; it needs a rule or a rebaseline decision, not a code change.\n(4) kit_lint_classify encoded-row 0 -> 1: lint --tsv builds its row with sm'.concat_list \"\\t\". TSV output is the feature itself, so it needs the same decision as (3).\nProposed rule for the landing checklist: run 'kit standards --ratchet' on every package a 'kit do' program touches, before landing.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_43 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 43u32, "2026-10-31T08:53Z", "kit", "2026-10-31 04:5x kit: all 7 open census regressions from the 10-30/31 landings are closed (unstaged). The ratchet over the 9 packages kit touched passes.\nFIXED IN CODE (5):\n- kit_log_counts and kit_table_counts. The tab rows were a crate boundary that passed --count specs as tab-encoded text. Now kit_log_counts has a typed count_spec record ({name, pattern, scope, set_path, set_pattern}) and builds the specs itself from argv (specs_from over the argument words; the process args are shared by both crates). The exports are kit_sequence_counts(path, section_on) : string * u64, plus header() and missing_set() with no arguments. The '#sections\\t' protocol line is gone, and output rows go through lib csv.tsv_line. A new test, count_flags_become_specs, covers flag order (--count-in given after its --count), --in-set with a drive-letter path, a foreign flag between them, and missing_set. table_counts.rs 924 -> 800, counts.rs 825 -> 865.\n- kit_machine_sample and kit_machine_health: the history rows use csv.tsv_line.\n- kit_lint_classify: csv.tsv_line plus csv.quoted_cell_text, instead of concat_list with a tab replace.\nRegenerated in place with cargo check (4 crates, 971 s on a saturated box).\nREBASELINED (2), as decided earlier: kit_lint_macros top-level-macro 1 -> 7 and host-macro 5 -> 7; kit_table_sequences top-level-macro 1 -> 3. Each added line is a one-expression call into a sibling crate's export, which the hand-modeled crate split ([[hand-modeled-build-units]]) requires, and the census cannot tell those from drift. kit standards --baseline-merge standards/census.spi src/kit_lint_macros src/kit_table_sequences changed only those two packages' 34 rows.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_44 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 44u32, "2026-10-31T22:29Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:3x x: enforcement gap in the census probes (kit_style_census/probes.spi). TypeDecl matches only ^\\s*(union|type|nominal)\\b and IndexedUnion only the 'union name param =' form, so granular-sugar packages (:| unions, :== records, :! nominals, :| name param = indexed unions) report type-decl=0 and indexed-union=0. Measured: kit's own kit_style_census (written in sugar) shows type-decl=0; x/apps/sim/spi (6,320 lines, dozens of :| and :==) and tibia arena/canary/host/map show type-decl=0 too. Consequence: the new-package rule ('declares types but uses none of the typefeatureset') can never fire on sugar code, which is all new code. Fix shape for the kit team: TypeDecl ^\\s*(union|type|nominal|:\\||:==|:!)\\s; IndexedUnion also ^:\\|\\s+\\w+\\s+\\w+(\\s+\\w+)*\\s*= (a :| with a type parameter). The baseline needs a re-take after the fix, since type-decl is unranked but gates the new-package check. GADT cases (| X :: t) and the \\? / \\* binders already count in sugar.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_45 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 45u32, "2026-11-01T19:41Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:41 x: SAME GAP FOR PROTOTYPES. kit/src/kit_style_census/probes.spi:83 counts only '^\\s*(prototype|instance)\\b', so sugar prototype declarations ':? name t : sig' and instances ':+ name t = impl' read 0. Evidence: x/apps/client/splitter.spi now has 5 ':?' and 15 ':+' (X-SOTA-MODEL n4), and 'kit standards --census apps/client' still prints prototype=0. Proposed pattern: '^\\s*(prototype|instance|:\\?|:\\+)\\s', plus a census test row with one ':?' and one ':+' line. Until it lands, x's typefeatureset DoDs attest prototypes by a direct count, so the census can't ratchet them yet.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_46 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 46u32, "2026-11-03T07:32Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 36 h ago by x, progress kept at 90%. Resume from that note:  prototype=0. Proposed pattern: '^\\s*(prototype|instance|:\\?|:\\+)\\s', plus a census test row with one ':?' and one ':+' line. Until it lands, x's typefeatureset DoDs attest prototypes by a direct count, so the census can't ratchet them yet.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_47 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 47u32, "2026-11-04T08:02Z", "kit", "11-04 04:4x kit groom: the last open gap (x n45: :? and :+ prototypes read 0) is fixed in the census probes: edition-3 :? :+ :| :== :! and {c}/(kind) binders, plus \\? packs, verified on kernel/fiscal/x fixtures 11-03. It passed TRAIN18's node regen and rides TRAIN20 (node half running now). This morning added: phantom types, reflection and higher-kinded probes, and the edition-2 `nominal` fix (TRAIN21). Every listed feature and drift probe now exists with a MayNotFall/MayNotRise direction, and the release ratchet and edit hook enforce them. Done when TRAIN20 installs and `kit standards --census apps/client` (x) shows prototype > 0. parked: waiting on TRAIN20 install.")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_48 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 48u32, "2026-11-06T11:13Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:1x kit: DONE. TRAIN33 INSTALLED at 07:05 (kit.exe 6CEA882D, the first install since TRAIN14c): node half job-1793925896101 regen ok, crate gate 358 over 0, notebooks 234/234 including the cli_check suites; box half job-1793960664511 accepted those verdicts on the same snapshot, then release build, schema and topology checks, publish and smoke passed. Its rule was Done when the install lands and kit standards --census apps/client (x) shows prototype above 0. PROOF 07:1x: prototype=20 (gadt-case 13, forall 43, phantom-type 5).")
inl note_of_kit_style_ratchet_49 () = Note ("KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", 49u32, "2026-11-06T11:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_atomic_writes_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ATOMIC-WRITES", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-ATOMIC-WRITES (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 1u32, "2026-10-21T01:38Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:38 eoie team, user 2026-10-20 21:37: 'see if there are gains turning these filters and similar advanced queries as default or templates easy to learn by tutorial, eoie should be easy to patch'. Evidence: answering 'eoie backlog by difficulty, also by the weighted lanes' (per-team WSJF backlogs) took three hand-written pwsh pipelines over agile list --json and agile next (tag/prefix filter + open + sort by D then P; agile next output regex-filtered to the team's ids; a blocked report joining deps to their statuses), so the gain is real: every team asks these questions, and each pipeline is a drift back to shell scripting (eoie-first). Defaults: agile list shows open items only, sorted by priority then difficulty; 'all' keeps today's full listing. Design for patchability, in the typefeatureset style (EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET): one query model, a filter union (Team, Tag, Prefix, Status, Open, Ready, Blocked) and a sort union (ByDifficulty, ByPriority, ByProgress, ByWsjf), with the flag words, usage text and help derived from the unions by reflection, so a new filter is one case plus one match arm, with no parser tables to keep in sync; named views are rows of that same model (View name filters sort) in state, so a team adds a view without touching eoie. Tutorial: each built-in view is a one-call spec case (command plus exact expected output), which doubles as its test.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 2u32, "2026-10-21T01:45Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:45 eoie team, design inputs from the x team (same user question for x tonight, same three-pipeline workaround), all adopted for this item: (1) a separate parent/epic field, deps meaning blockers only (x deleted deps on X-CELLS-SPLIT and SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK to get them ranked; eoie inverted EOIE-ONE-CALL's links for the same reason); (2) a team field (main|x|tibia|eoie), the item's owning team, separate from domain tags, with list/next --team, since tags like x/x-api/x-compat/sim overlap and other teams' items tagged x leak into x queries; (3) list --sort and --team/--tag/--prefix, next --json, and wsjf/rank/unblocks fields in list --json so ranks never come from parsing text; (4) per-team weights from the user's team priorities so each team gets its own WSJF-ordered team backlog (today --as subagent gives x only one item, unblocks=0); (5) define estimate as remaining minutes, or derive remaining from progress, since WSJF divides by full size even at 98% (X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT jumped to #1 at its true 30m), and let the team field keep another team's leftover from topping a team backlog; (6) usage should say that 'agile edit <id> deps -' (or none) clears a field. Refs: X-CELLS-SPLIT notes 21-22, SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK note 11, EOIE-ONE-CALL note 6, EOIE-WSJF-LPT notes 6-7.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 3u32, "2026-10-21T01:54Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:54 eoie team groom for the 10-20/21 night (to 07:00): night slice (4th, read side only, no state schema change): a typed query model (filter union Team/Tag/Prefix/Status/Open/Ready/Blocked, sort union ByDifficulty/ByPriority/ByProgress/ByWsjf) behind list and next; next --json; wsjf/rank/unblocks in list --json; a blocked view; --team derived from the ID prefix and tags until a real team field exists. The team and parent fields change the state schema for every crate and wait for the user's go. Remaining estimate 300 min.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 4u32, "2026-10-21T03:32Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 23:32 eoie team, slice 2 drafted (scratchpad apply-queries-slice2.ps1, lands with release 26): new package agile_task_query/query.spi holds the one query model both list and next use: team union with words and team_words by reflection, team_of_task from ID prefix and whole-item tags (fixes 'xsd' matching the x tag as a substring), query_filter (OnTeam | WithTag | WithPrefix | OnlyOpen), query_flag union with flag words and usage derived by union_cases_joined, flag_read_of as one fold parser returning FilterRead (filter, used) | FlagRefused | NotQueryFlag. agile list gains --team/--tag/--prefix/--open and --sort priority|difficulty|progress (list_sort union, words by reflection): rows are read once into a typed list and placed by a sort key with stable insertion, so the default order (priority, then file order) is unchanged; the summary adds ' team=x open sort=difficulty' and JSON a 'query' field only when a query is given, so existing outputs stay byte-identical. ranking.spi and task_next.spi drop their own team and filter copies for the shared model; next's --team rejection now reads '--team expects main|x|tibia|eoie, got ops'. Tests: 2 query notebook tests, 1 list contract test (team, open, difficulty order, default order, both rejections). Default 'open only' for list stays off (smaller and reversible; teams' scripts read the full list). Measured: agile list on the shared backlog takes 1.35 s for 456 rows, mostly the per-row planning lookup scanning the whole state file; worth its own item.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 5u32, "2026-10-21T05:21Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 01:21 line gate: slice 1 alone makes task_next.rs 1188 lines (measured read-only on the r25 tree), slice 2 makes listing 1244 and next 1246; the query model compiles into each crate (~300 lines). Slice 1 pulled from r25 (saved in scratch queries-slice1-saved, restored from the pre-slice-1 snapshot); slices 1+2 ship together in r27 with agile_task_query as its own crate whose boundary is the normalized query flags (e.g. --team eoie --open), parsed back by the same flag_read_of.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 6u32, "2026-10-21T05:32Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 01:32 agile_task_query crate drafted and preview-compiled with r26: exports eoie_query_flag_width/_refusal/_passes/_shown/_usage and eoie_team_of; listing 964, next 987 (tight), crate 454. Slices 1+2 now ride r26 (apply-queries-r27.ps1, base-hash guarded); the r26 follow-on applies them after r25 installs.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_7 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 7u32, "2026-10-21T05:48Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 01:48 query flags are now a value-indexed GADT (FlagTeam :: query_flag team, FlagTag/FlagPrefix :: query_flag string, FlagOpen :: query_flag ()), enumerated through an existential any_flag like the census probes; value_of parses the typed value, filter_of builds the filter with per-case refinement. task_query.rs 498 lines, preview-compiled; in the r26 draft.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_8 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 8u32, "2026-10-21T06:03Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:03 plan for the rest (r27+), set by the line gate (after r26: next 987, listing 966 of 1000): (1) --ready/--blocked need dependency status, so the query crate takes the task's open-dependency names as a fifth argument (listing and next already scan deps) and blocked rows name each unmet dep; (2) --sort wsjf reuses next's rank key, so it lives in next only; (3) named views become View (name, verb, flags) rows in the backlog root, read by a new small crate agile_task_views exporting the stored flags; list/next take --view <name> and append those flags before their own; (4) each shipped view gets a one-call tutorial case with exact output (spec/one-call.md) once the binary has it. Before (1)-(3), move the listing's sort and row printing into the listing crate's own exports or a sibling crate to regain headroom.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_9 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 9u32, "2026-10-21T08:29Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 04:29 eoie team: drafted list/next --ready and --blocked (scratch apply-ready-blocked.ps1, r28 order 11). The query flag union gains FlagReady/FlagBlocked; filters take a settled bool. ready = open with every dependency Done or unknown, the same rule next ranks by. blocked = status Blocked, or open with an open dependency. list asks census for the unsettled IDs (new export eoie_agile_unsettled_ids) only when the query reads dependencies. Preview: task_listing 981 (1014 with the scan inside listing), census 333. Notebooks: a query test plus an agile_cli fixture with GATE/WAITS/SHIPPED/FREE/HELD. Remaining: --sort wsjf, named views, tutorial cases.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_10 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 10u32, "2026-10-21T13:48Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:48 eoie team: named views drafted for r30 (scratch apply-views.ps1, applies after r29). New crate agile_task_views with three verbs: agile view-add <root> <name> <list|next> [flags...] stores a typed row View (name, verb, flags) in <root>/state/views.spi (replaces a row of the same name, changed-since-read check, atomic rename); agile view <root> <name> [extra flags...] runs eoie agile <verb> <root> <stored flags> <extra flags> as a direct child of the same binary (stdio and exit code pass through), so listing and next are untouched (both sit near the 1000-line gate); agile views <root> lists them. Verb words and the dispatch come from unions (view_verb, view_command) by reflection. Same slice: list/next usage shapes now name the query flags (--team, --tag, --prefix, --open, --ready, --blocked, list --sort) with a drift test in agile_task_query that every flag's usage appears in both shapes; agile note-edit takes --as <author> (agent sessions pass it to every note verb) and refuses an edit by anyone but the note's author. CLI notebook views_cli.spi. Preview queued (eoie-preview-r30d). Left: --sort wsjf (next already ranks by WSJF), tutorial cases in spec/one-call.md once installed.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_11 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 11u32, "2026-10-21T16:50Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 12:50 installed with r26g: list/next take --team/--tag/--prefix/--open, list takes --sort priority|difficulty|progress; query crate agile_task_query with value-indexed flag GADT. --ready/--blocked drafted for r28 (note 9); remaining: --sort wsjf, named views, tutorial cases.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_12 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 12u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): agile list/next --ready/--blocked.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_13 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 13u32, "2026-10-21T20:46Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:46 eoie team, papercut from the adoption sweep: agile note and show print only UTC (at=2026-10-21T20:45Z), so lanes prefix every note text with Get-Date local time (part of 345 Get-Date calls today). Proposal: show prints each note's local time with its offset next to the UTC stamp, and note text no longer needs its own clock.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_14 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 14u32, "2026-10-21T20:49Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:49 eoie team: local-time papercut drafted for r34 (scratch apply-note-local-time.ps1). agile show prints each note as '2026-10-21T20:45Z (16:45 -04:00)  author  text' through eoie_receipt's local offset (a malformed stamp adds nothing). The note model becomes a registered notebook with a test, and the r34 checks cover its 15 consumer crates. Once it ships, note text needs no Get-Date prefix.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_15 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 15u32, "2026-10-21T22:12Z", "eoie-lane", "18:12 eoie team, decision: --sort wsjf on agile list is dropped. agile next computes WSJF from the dependency graph (unblocks), which list rows (task_listing's row_key over priority/difficulty/progress) do not carry; a list-side WSJF would either be a second formula that drifts from next or a per-row call into agile_task_next. agile next [--team t] [count] is the WSJF view. Left on the item: tutorial cases for the views once the spec's runner accepts direct verbs (every one-call case is an eoie do program today), and r34's local time in agile show.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_16 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 16u32, "2026-10-23T17:50Z", "eoie", "13:50 eoie: tutorial case written now that the spec runner takes direct verbs (cases 38-40 already do): spec/one-call.md case 41, 'The same filtered list, asked for by name'. Habit: the retyped 'agile list <backlog> --team eoie --open --load light --sort priority' (the Stop hook and I typed it many times today). Setup: agile init, three items with --load light/heavy, then view-add light list --open --load light --sort priority. Command: agile view backlog light. The expected table was generated from a real run, with elapsed_ms as <ms>. Runner on the installed binary: 41 green, spec 39/41 (38 and 39 land with L and I).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_17 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 17u32, "2026-10-23T17:50Z", "eoie", "13:50 eoie: CLOSED. Both leftovers of the 10-21 18:12 note are met on the installed binary: the views tutorial case (spec case 41, green) and the local time in agile show (every note row prints UTC plus local, e.g. '2026-10-23T13:16Z (09:16 -04:00)'). list/next already take --team, --tag, --prefix, --open, --ready, --blocked, --sort and --load, with named views (view-add, views, view).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_18 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 18u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "eoie", "19:45 Bug found while grooming: 'agile check <root>' fails on any root that has saved views ('state entry is neither one static package module nor one known transient receipt: views.spi'). view-add writes state/views.spi, but check's allow-list doesn't include it, so a root breaks its own check once someone saves a view. Fix: check accepts views.spi as a known state module and type-checks it with the rest. Done when: a notebook case adds a view to a scratch root and agile check passes, and the live backlog root passes agile check after the install.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_19 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 19u32, "2026-10-25T00:08Z", "eoie", "20:08 Healed on the live root. The cause: state/package.spiproj didn't list views.spi as a module, so agile check rejected it as a foreign file. I added '    views' to the modules list with one eoie do edit; agile check then passed in 5s with views.spi type-checked (build_attested=3). Code fix: view-add now registers the views module in state/package.spiproj after writing views.spi (no duplicate, refuses a package without a modules section, skips a root without a package). It also adds the tests a_saved_view_registers_its_module_so_agile_check_sees_it and a_failed_view_write_leaves_no_pending_copy. Regen of agile_task_views plus cargo check passed on scratch (views.rs 882 lines). It ships as the last patch of HIJK (pending-heal\\apply-pending-heal.ps1, called from apply-rK.ps1), and HIJK's notebooks now include views_cli.spi.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_20 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 20u32, "2026-10-27T15:39Z", "kit", "11:3x kit: done check, part 1 passes: the views_cli notebook passed in tonight's release candidate run (kit-release-SLICES, 52/54, the 2 failures were an unrelated lib-edit window). Part 2 (agile check on the live backlog root, which has saved views) is inconclusive, not failed: 'kit error: state\\views.spi: command timed out after 30000 ms', the 30 s type-check cap under full heavy load (both slots busy; check-attestation-cap). The old foreign-file rejection did not appear. Re-run when the box is quieter before closing.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_21 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 21u32, "2026-10-27T18:58Z", "kit", "kit 15:0x: the pending 'agile check on the shared backlog' rerun is done. On a frozen copy of state/ taken at 13:4x (6.40 MB after the Done-notes trim, EOIE-STATE-SCALE note 18): kit agile check ok, files=4 build_attested=3 validation=receipt-first, on the deployed single-flight compiler (fingerprint 3415645863664410995). The live backlog itself cannot finish attestation while lanes are writing: two live runs both typechecked ('checked package') and then stopped at 'state or package inputs changed during attestation; retry'. A frozen copy is the reliable way to check during busy hours.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_22 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 22u32, "2026-10-27T20:30Z", "kit", "kit 16:3x, closing review against the title. Done: flags --team/--tag/--prefix/--open/--ready/--blocked/--sort; named views as typed rows (state/views.spi holds View records; views_cli notebook green; agile check ok with views.spi, note 21); spec case 41 covers view-add plus view. NOT done: 'a blocked view names each unmet dependency'. Live 'agile list --blocked --team eoie' lists the right rows (EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS, EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS, EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST, plus Active items with unmet deps) but prints no deps column, so the reader cannot see what blocks each one, and the spec has no --blocked case. That is the remaining 10%. It belongs in agile_task_listing, which is at 978/1000 after TRAIN, so it goes through a seam: compute the unmet-dependency text per row in a separate crate (typed columns across the Export, as in the 10-27 lint split), then add a tutorial case with exact output to spec/one-call.md.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_23 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 23u32, "2026-10-28T12:06Z", "eoie", "08:0x kit: the missing unmet-deps column is drafted for TRAIN4 (scratchpad clean\\agile-queries\\waits-column.do; TRAIN4 copy built by clean\\train4\\build-train4-copy.ps1). agile_task_census gains unmet_deps (pure Spiral: split deps, keep the ones in the unsettled id set) and waits_column, exported as kit_agile_waits_column. agile_task_listing's table row appends '  waits <A,B>' whenever the query already reads deps (request.unsettled is set, so --blocked and --ready), through one host call. The seam keeps the listing crate small: 988 generated lines, against 996 when the column logic lived in the listing; census goes to 563. Both compile locally with the P2 single-flight DLL. Left: ride TRAIN4, then check live that 'agile list <root> --blocked' rows show waits <deps> and that plain list rows are unchanged.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_24 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 24u32, "2026-10-29T22:42Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:40 kit: DONE on installed kit C528BE4D (TRAIN4c). agile list --blocked prints \"  waits <ids>\" on rows with unmet deps (closeout PASS: 129 of 140 rows; CD2F0D1A printed 0).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_queries_25 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-QUERIES", 25u32, "2026-11-06T11:25Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
