inl note_of_kit_table_papercuts_1 () = Note ("KIT-TABLE-PAPERCUTS", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:31Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-TABLE-PAPERCUTS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 1u32, "2026-10-21T10:21Z", "main", "2026-10-21 06:21 main: plan, fixed before measuring: rebuild the previous core (5AA4938BE6339F4A, the c7 + parse-stack tree) as a Release candidate in a private cache; bench-pair.ps1 -A old -B new on frontier_hello, mega_brzozowski_derivatives and samples/conversation_negative_orphan_result, 5 rounds, order alternating, when no other heavy job runs; plus one Cells gate of SEFAZ generator.spi on each. Verdict rule: B/A median within 1.05 on every row means load explained tonight; above 1.10 on any row means a regression, and the pack's host and core edits get bisected (profile lookup, related-parser-errors, host walks, lazy-expected).")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 2u32, "2026-10-21T10:50Z", "main", "2026-10-21 06:50 main: counter-evidence for a regression: tibia reports all 26 tibia test files green on the 03:19 compiler, and x's Cells passed 34 of 35 (the exception timed out at 300 s under load). Both ran beside other heavy work, so the A/B rule stands.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 3u32, "2026-10-21T11:13Z", "main", "2026-10-21 07:13 main: script ready, lab/COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB/ab.ps1: builds the exact pre-pack core (staged by build.ps1 at spiral-bin/core-src/single-flight/5aa4938be6339f4a, hash-checked) as a Release candidate in candidates/speed-ab-old with the current host, then bench-pair old vs installed on frontier_hello, conversation_negative_orphan_result and mega_brzozowski_derivatives (C, 5 rounds, order alternating). It isolates the core edits; if the core is clean and the slowdown persists, the host edits (profile lookup, parse warning) are next. Runs after tibia's PR-17 live block (no compiles during it).")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 4u32, "2026-10-21T12:21Z", "main", "2026-10-21 08:21 main: first attempt invalid: the old core (5AA4938BE6339F4A) does not build with the current host, which calls pack-added core functions (postability profile lookup), so the Release candidate failed after 272 s. Rerun as job speed-ab-debug with two existing Debug candidates and no build: A = formatter-c9 (old core + formatter patches only, pre-pack host), B = x-profile (the exact pack core + host). The formatter patches do not touch compilation, so this isolates the pack. Same samples, same verdict rule.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 5u32, "2026-10-21T12:41Z", "main", "2026-10-21 08:41 main: partial results (speed-ab-debug, Debug A = formatter-c9 old core + pre-pack host, B = x-profile exact pack, 5 alternating rounds, fresh process, C, machine busy): frontier_hello median A 41.9 s, B 36.8 s, B/A 0.879; conversation_negative_orphan_result median A 30.7 s, B 45.0 s, B/A 1.464 (min A 18.6 s, B 45.0 s). Noise is large (same compiler and sample spans 18.6-45 s), but the type-error sample is above the 1.10 regression line and that path runs through the pack's host router change (TypeErrors result waiting for related parser errors), so it gets a targeted look whatever the third sample says. mega_brzozowski_derivatives still running.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 6u32, "2026-10-21T12:42Z", "main", "2026-10-21 08:42 main: the minimums tell a cleaner story than the medians: B's fastest round was 44.4 s (frontier_hello) and 45.0 s (orphan_result), A's 25.7 s and 18.6 s. A floor about 20 s higher in every B round of both samples, independent of the sample, points to a fixed per-process cost of B: either a pack edit on the startup path (host: profile lookup, router; core: module parse thread, x-compat entry check) or something about the x-profile candidate itself (its private cache, missing warm package caches). Next, after the mega sample: rerun B against a fresh Debug build of the same pack in another cache, then time one compile with SPIRAL_DIAG timing to see where the 20 s goes.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 7u32, "2026-10-21T12:43Z", "main", "2026-10-21 08:43 main: code read for the ~20 s higher floor of B, CPU-light: both candidates' cache dirs hold the same structure (bin/obj/build-cwd/results, no runtime caches), so a cold candidate cache is ruled out; both hosts have the link-safe walk; the profile code is reached only from --x-compat and --check, and xCompatWarnEntry's edition check is a few file probes; the router change only touches error paths, but frontier_hello has no errors and still shows the floor. Nothing found by reading. Deciding control, queued for after tibia's PR-17 block: rebuild the pack as a Debug candidate in candidates/speed-ab-new and bench it against A (B' near A: the x-profile build is the artifact; B' near B: bisect the pack edits).")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 8u32, "2026-10-21T13:11Z", "main", "2026-10-21 09:11 main: VERDICT: no regression demonstrated. bench-pair medians (5 rounds, busy machine): frontier_hello 0.879, orphan_result 1.464, mega_brzozowski_derivatives 0.952 but invalid (both sides wrote no output). The one value above 1.10 did not reproduce in direct timestamped runs (lab timeline.ps1, fresh process, the same two Debug candidates): frontier_hello A 6.2 s / B 5.7 s; orphan_result A 11.1 / B 10.5 and A 9.0 / B 12.1. B wins 3 of 6 head-to-heads, inside A's own 9-11 s spread, and the error appears at the same moment of the compile on both. So tonight's slow review (1,940 s), x's exsss timeout and the SEFAZ generator timeouts are explained by load (four sessions compiling at once, up to 23.5 GB taken by one Cells VM). Per the rule's in-between branch, one 9-round rerun on a quiet machine stays open at low priority; nothing to bisect.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 9u32, "2026-10-21T20:49Z", "main", "16:49 grooming, honesty fix: the 09:11 verdict departed from the pre-written rule. orphan_result's bench-pair median B/A was 1.464, above the rule's 1.10 regression line, and I overrode it with 6 direct timeline runs instead of applying the rule mechanically. The verdict therefore stands as PROVISIONAL, not a pass. The deciding measurement is the 9-round rerun, now under a quiet-round filter like HOPACSOTA-P2 note 48: rounds where other load reaches >= 2 cores at a run's start or end are dropped, with at least 5 quiet rounds per sample. Rule, fixed before measuring: B/A <= 1.05 on every valid sample closes the item; any > 1.10 means bisect the pack edits (host profile lookup, router, x-compat entry check, module parse thread); in between, report and keep it open. Box: a quiet one, after tonight's review or in tomorrow's first quiet hour.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_10 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 10u32, "2026-10-24T03:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:11 main grooming estimate 150 m: remaining milestones: one quiet-box bench pair run under the pre-written 1.10 rule 120 m heavy, then apply the rule mechanically 30 m.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_11 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 11u32, "2026-10-27T19:02Z", "main", "10-27 15:02 main: Re-plan, set before measuring, so the item no longer waits for a quiet box (per the no-quiet-machine rule: make benches robust under load instead). (1) Metric: CPU-seconds per compile from a Job Object over the whole process tree, not wall-clock. Today's E1 bench measured that way at 100% box load, and its rounds agreed within 6%. The Job Object code moved out of bench-cells-vs-matrix.ps1 into a shared apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/tree-cpu.ps1 (Measure-TreeCpu) that both benches dot-source; the moved block is byte-identical, the E1 bench parses, and a smoke run measured 8.8 CPU-s with correct output. (2) A can't be the 10-21 core any more: core-src no longer holds its staged source (5AA4938BE6339F4A), the speed-ab-old cache kept only its core DLL without the host, and none of the 18 committed versions of spiral_compiler.fs between 10-15 and 10-24 hashes to it (it was an uncommitted tree). A is therefore commit 33ee6412 (10-20, the last committed core before that pack) built with the current host, and B is the installed compiler. B/A now measures every core change since 10-20, so a RED means bisecting a wider range than the original pack. (3) Runner: lab COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB/ab-cpu.ps1. It extracts A's core from git, builds it in the speed-ab-old cache, then runs 9 rounds per sample with A and B alternating, each compile 'dotnet <dll> --backend C <entry> <out>' (the same call bench-pair makes) in its own Job Object. A sample is valid only if every run exits 0 and writes output. Samples: frontier_hello, conversation_negative_orphan_result, mega_brzozowski_derivatives. (4) Rule, unchanged from n4 but on CPU medians: every valid B/A CPU median <= 1.05 closes the item; any > 1.10 means bisect; in between, report and keep open; no valid sample is VOID. It runs as a local heavy job after kit's SLICES2 'window closed', since it compiles.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_12 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 12u32, "2026-10-28T11:21Z", "main", "10-28 07:21 main: ab-cpu.ps1 run 10-28 07:14 VOID: the A side (33ee6412's core with the CURRENT host) did not build. Diagnosis (lab diagnose-old-build.ps1, full log in lab old-build-33ee6412.log): 3 compiler errors, all in the current host, tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs:1230-1236. It calls postabilityProfileFor and uses XCompatFinding, core functions added after 33ee6412 (x's x-compat postability work). So 'old core + current host' can't measure a core this old: the host has grown a dependency on newer core API. Method fix: pair each core with its OWN host. build.ps1 takes only -CoreSource (L4), so the A/B needs a -HostSource (or -CompilerTreeSource) parameter that swaps tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs as well. build.ps1 is the compiler team's (CCMR just changed it), so that's a request to them, not a local patch. Alternative without a build change: compare against the oldest core that still compiles with today's host (a bisect over installed pack cores: 5m' 8660C34A, 5n 9E1F72AB), which measures recent packs only. Blocked on the compiler team's -HostSource; the rule (B/A CPU median <= 1.05 GREEN, > 1.10 RED) stands for the re-run.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_13 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 13u32, "2026-10-28T11:56Z", "main", "10-28 07:56 main: Re-run with build.ps1 -HostSource (10-28 07:4x-07:5x, job-1793187832362): A = 33ee6412 core E00C68FC with its OWN host (core DLL E89AF5E8), B = installed P2 (05E94769), 9 alternating rounds, Job Object CPU. The rule's verdict is GREEN, but it's THIN: only 1 of 3 samples was valid. frontier_hello: A 3.39 s, B 3.19 s CPU, B/A 0.941 (B slightly faster), load 5%, valid. conversation_negative_orphan_result: exit 5 on BOTH sides, which is correct for a negative sample (compile error by design), so ab-cpu's validity check (exit 0 and output written) excludes it; its CPU ratio is still 20.86/19.98 = 1.044. mega_brzozowski_derivatives: exit 5 on both sides; the mega sample's entry is a subfolder (for example runtime_native/main.spi), not the top-level main.spi; ratio 5.38/5.55 = 0.97. Every measured ratio is within 1.05, but one valid sample doesn't establish 'no pack slowdown' (gate met is not goal met), so 90%, not Done. Next, cheap: swap the samples for two more positive, compile-heavy entries (e.g. mega_brzozowski_derivatives/runtime_native and a mid-size positive sample), re-run (about 10 min with the old build cached in speed-ab-old), and close on 3 valid samples. Also fixed today: ab-cpu.ps1 leaked its per-sample display line into the verdict list (strict mode from env.ps1 then threw); it now uses Write-Host and lists invalid rows.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_14 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 14u32, "2026-10-28T12:00Z", "main", "10-28 08:00 main: VERDICT RED by the pre-registered rule (10-28 07:5x-08:00, job-1793188630472, 3 of 3 samples valid, 9 alternating rounds, Job Object CPU medians; A = 33ee6412 core with its own host, B = installed P2 05E94769). frontier_hello: A 2.72 s, B 2.77 s, B/A 1.018. mega_brzozowski_derivatives/inventory_closed_dfa_runtime: A 3.98 s, B 3.61 s, B/A 0.907 (B faster). mega_brzozowski_derivatives/runtime_native: A 2.36 s, B 2.89 s, B/A 1.225 (22% more compile CPU on today's core). The rule says 'any B/A above 1.10 = RED: bisect the pack edits', so the item is RED and the next step is the bisect, not a re-measure. Also noted: the load column read -1 (the load probe failed in this run; CPU-time medians don't depend on it). The bisect plan uses the cores kept on disk: 5m' (8660C34A, TEMP pack-next5m2), 5n (9E1F72AB, pack-next5n candidate), P2 (installed), and older pack cores via git show of their commits if needed. Measure runtime_native only, with the same rule and rounds, each core with the current host (recent cores build with it), halving the range until one pack step accounts for the jump. It needs a heavy box slot: slot 2 after tibia's K4 (10:30), before main's chat and dir-tree runs.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_15 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 15u32, "2026-10-28T12:10Z", "main", "10-28 08:10 main: BISECT 1 (10-28 08:01-08:08, job-1793188884738, runtime_native only, 9 alternating rounds, Job Object CPU medians, each core built with the current host, B = installed P2 05E94769). Core 5m' 8660C34A: A 2.05 s, B 2.70 s, B/A 1.317, 0 failed. Core 5n 9E1F72AB: A 3.56 s, B 3.77 s, B/A 1.059, 0 failed (box load differed between the two rounds; ratios are paired within each round). Verdict: the 5m' -> 5n pack step carries the regression (about 25 of 32 points); 5n -> P2 is within the 1.10 rule, consistent with the compiler lane's evidence that the P2 single-flight seam is a let-inline no-op and CCMR det-root is build-input only. The 5m'->5n diff is 33 hunks (+340/-39). Cleared by reading as off the C compile hot path: zwsp tokenizer remap (one IndexOf), '::' parser arms, exportedUnionNames hoist (same walk), importedTypeUnits (Rust only), manifestsUnder (manifest-toml subcommand), spiral.toml File.Exists probe. Lead suspect (unproven): the union source-naming hook added to nominal application in typecheck (5n:147996) and peval nominal_type_apply (5n:159982): per YUnion result it allocates a closure, runs a recursive string-concat structural_ty_name, and adds a ConditionalWeakTable entry (DependentHandle, GC-costly) when peval yields fresh Union objects. Handed to the compiler lane at 08:1x. Next: gate the peval-side hook off, rebuild 5n, re-measure runtime_native (heavy slot after 10:30) unless the compiler lane's build-free count settles it first.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_16 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 16u32, "2026-10-28T12:24Z", "main", "10-28 08:24 main: A/A CONTROL (10-28 08:19-08:24, job-1793189949446, runtime_native, 7 alternating rounds, same bisect path, B = installed P2 05E94769): P2's own tree core 9FF1B708 rebuilt through the candidate path: A 2.30 s, B 2.41 s, B/A 1.048 (an A/A should read 1.0: a ~5% candidate-vs-installed bias). 5m' 8660C34A re-measured in the same job: A 3.47 s, B 3.36 s, B/A 0.968, against 1.317 in BISECT 1 one hour earlier. Hook-off 5n (5911B335, job-1793189650757) read 1.069, plain 5n 1.059. Verdict: the 5m'->5n 'regression' does not reproduce; BISECT 1's 1.317 was noise. The installed binary alone swung 2.70 s -> 2.16 s between rounds while candidates stayed ~2.0 s. Both builds run System.GC.Server=true, whose per-core GC threads put load-dependent CPU into the Job Object CPU median. The compiler lane read the full 5m'->5n diff build-free and confirms no hunk lowers C differently (shared hunks are per-file/per-lexeme); the union-naming hook gate-off recovered nothing. The original RED (33ee6412 vs P2, 1.225, job-1793188630472) is therefore suspect on the same grounds. Method fix landed in lab ab-cpu.ps1 and bisect.ps1: -GcServer '0' runs every measured compile with DOTNET_gcServer=0 (empty keeps the pre-registered method; DOTNET_gcServer is unset at user/machine/process level). Close rule: re-run ab-cpu.ps1 with -GcServer 0 plus an in-job A/A row; Done if the A/A reads within 1.05 and every A/B B/A is at most 1.10; if a sample still exceeds 1.10 with a clean A/A, bisect again under the same method. Needs one heavy slot after tibia's block (09:30) and TRAIN3's admission.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_17 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 17u32, "2026-10-28T13:05Z", "main", "10-28 09:05 main: CLOSE RUN with Workstation GC (10-28 08:57-09:04, job-1793192220271, -GcServer 0 on every measured compile, 9 alternating rounds, Job Object CPU medians, run under TRAIN3's full regen load in the other heavy slot). In-job A/A first: P2's own tree core 9FF1B708 rebuilt through the candidate path vs installed P2: A 3.66 s, B 3.41 s, B/A 0.932, 0 failed. A/B, A = 33ee6412 core with its own host, B = installed P2 05E94769: frontier_hello A 3.31 s B 3.56 s B/A 1.076; mega_brzozowski_derivatives/inventory_closed_dfa_runtime A 5.77 s B 5.83 s B/A 1.010; mega_brzozowski_derivatives/runtime_native A 3.80 s B 3.88 s B/A 1.021 (was 1.225 under Server GC). Pre-registered verdict: BETWEEN (a B/A in 1.05-1.10: report, keep open), 3 of 3 samples valid. Reading: the A/A shows about 7% instrument error even with Workstation GC under load (0.932, outside a symmetric 0.95-1.05 band), so every A/B row sits inside the noise and the 22% runtime_native regression does not reproduce. This method cannot certify 'no slowdown' at 5%, so the item stays open rather than closing on a cheaper reading (gate met is not goal met). Next, method only: measure a paired ratio per round (B/A within the same round, median of per-round ratios, not ratio of medians), add the A/A as a third arm inside every round instead of a separate pass, and raise rounds to 21, so sustained load hits all arms of a round equally. Close when the per-round A/A median is within 0.95-1.05 and every A/B is at most 1.05; one heavy slot, about 15 min, not before TRAIN3 publishes (~13:10) unless a slot sits idle.")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_18 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 18u32, "2026-10-28T13:19Z", "main", "10-28 09:19 main: PAIRED 3-ARM RUN (10-28 09:07-09:18, job-1793192793780, -GcServer 0, 15 rounds, every arm in every round with rotating order, paired B/A = median of per-round installed/arm ratios, under TRAIN3's regen load). Arms: installed P2 05E94769; P2's own tree core 9FF1B708 rebuilt (in-round A/A); 33ee6412 with its own host (prebuilt speed-ab-old). Paired A/A: frontier_hello 0.985, inventory_closed_dfa_runtime 0.965, runtime_native 1.069. Paired 33ee6412 vs installed: 1.016, 0.991, 1.036. Like-for-like (33ee6412 vs rebuilt P2, both via the candidate path): 1.03, 1.03, 0.97. Rule outcome: NOT MET on runtime_native only (its A/A 1.069 is outside 0.95-1.05); frontier_hello and inventory meet it (A/A in band, A/B at most 1.05). Cause of the A/A outlier: the installed and rebuilt directories are byte-identical in all 32 loaded files (SpiralCompiler.dll, SpiralCompilerCore.dll and every dependency); the 59 differing entries are stale backup files (.d-bad-164052 from 10-06, .r2r-old-*) that .NET never loads. So 1.069 is two copies of the same binary: the instrument's noise floor on runtime_native under load is about 7%. Side finding for DISK-CLEANUP: 59 stale .d-bad/.r2r-old backups in the installed bin dir (spiral-bin\\bin\\single-flight\\...\\net11.0), build outputs, removable once the compiler lane confirms no rollback reads them. PRE-REGISTERED before the next run (09:19): one runtime_native-only paired run, 31 rounds, same arms, -GcServer 0; its result replaces runtime_native's row whatever it says. If its A/A is within 0.95-1.05, judge the A/B by the rule (at most 1.05 = GREEN, item Done). If its A/A is out of band again, stop measuring: record the method's floor under load and close the regression question as 'not reproduced above a ~7% floor', with GREEN uncertified (status Done with that caveat, no further runs).")
inl note_of_compiler_pack_speed_ab_19 () = Note ("COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB", 19u32, "2026-10-28T13:29Z", "main", "10-28 09:29 main: VERDICT GREEN, item Done (10-28 09:20-09:28, job-1793193590451). The pre-registered runtime_native-only run (note 18: its result replaces runtime_native's row whatever it says): -GcServer 0, 31 rounds, three arms in every round with rotating order. Paired A/A (P2's own tree core 9FF1B708 rebuilt vs installed P2): 0.988, in the 0.95-1.05 band. Paired 33ee6412 (with its own host) vs installed P2: 0.989, at most 1.05. Medians: A/A arm 4.28 s, 33ee6412 4.17 s, installed 4.20 s, 0 failed runs. With note 18's frontier_hello (A/A 0.985, A/B 1.016) and inventory_closed_dfa_runtime (A/A 0.965, A/B 0.991), every sample meets the rule: no pack slowdown between 33ee6412 and P2. The morning's RED (1.225) and the bisect's 1.317 came from the method (Server GC CPU time under sustained load, ratio of medians, separate-pass control). The method that resolves it is lab COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB bisect.ps1: -GcServer 0, -Prebuilt label=path arms, paired per-round ratios, an in-round A/A arm. Recorded as memory ab-needs-in-job-aa.")
inl note_of_kit_experiment_import_1 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENT-IMPORT", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:31Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-EXPERIMENT-IMPORT (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_table_where_compare_1 () = Note ("KIT-TABLE-WHERE-COMPARE", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:31Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-TABLE-WHERE-COMPARE (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_1 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 1u32, "2026-10-21T13:20Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:20 eoie team, design notes before drafting: (1) notebook.run names its output target/spiral-tests/<file>.spi.ipynb, and file names repeat across crates (two windows_cli.spi, several model.spi), so parallel runs need <crate>_<file> output names; (2) concurrent Mix runs in one project clash on _build (memory heavy-gate-lessons), so each worker gets its own MIX_BUILD_PATH under target/spiral-tests/mix-<worker>, paying one Cells build per worker per release; (3) environment for the children (EOIE_BIN_UNDER_TEST, SPIRAL_CELLS_WARM/MEMO/COMPILER) goes on each child command, not set_var; (4) builds on r28's continue-on-failure runner. Expected: notebook phase 35 -> ~18 min with 2 workers at a ~2-3 GB memory peak.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_2 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 2u32, "2026-10-21T13:30Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:30 eoie team: measured before choosing workers: the 61-notebook live run spent 2135 s with a floor of about 20 s per notebook (median 30 s), so fixed per-notebook cost is about 1200 s (56%): pwsh start, mix/BEAM boot and a fresh .NET compiler per compile (SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process). Cells already takes many --path in one call (sequential, all failures collected) and can compile through a daemon on a private SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT that --gate retires. Benchmark lab/EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS/batch-bench.ps1 on 8 notebooks (6 model, 2 CLI, unique basenames): A today's one call per notebook, B one batch call with process compiles, C one batch call with a private warm daemon and --gate; order C, B, A, A again for drift. Rule set now: pick the fastest config whose per-notebook pass/fail equals A's; adopt it only if it saves at least 25% against the mean of the two A runs; within 10% of each other the simpler one wins (B over C; no daemon). Parallel workers stay deferred until this lands, since they add CPU load where notes_cli already hit its timeout.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_3 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 3u32, "2026-10-21T14:17Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 10:17 eoie team: rule addition, written before any result: the bench runs while r26g's release and the r30 previews load the box, so a run is valid only if its two baseline runs (A1, A2) differ by at most 20%. An invalid run decides nothing and the bench reruns once in a quiet window (no heavy job held, checked with eoie window list), before r31 is composed.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_4 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 4u32, "2026-10-21T15:48Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 11:48 eoie team: the first bench run (11:36-11:43) is invalid by construction, so its rule was not applied. One of the 8 listed notebooks (eoie_process/table_model.spi) no longer exists. A1/A2 skipped it (7 ran, A1 147.8 s, A2 145.6 s), but in batch mode Cells aborts the whole batch on the first unreadable path (File.Error in notebook.ex:60), so B (75.4 s) and C (117.0 s) ran only 5 notebooks. Fixed the list (eoie_table_logs/logs_model.spi), and the script now refuses missing paths and basename collisions before measuring. Rerun as eoie-nb-bench2 with the same rule. Side findings: (1) one bad --path kills a Cells batch, which a batch runner must survive (per-path error, keep going); (2) C's private daemon still listened right after --gate and was gone a few minutes later. Old run kept in nb-bench-v1-invalid.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_5 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 5u32, "2026-10-21T16:14Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 12:14 eoie team: rerun valid and the rule applied as written. A1 214.3 s and A2 218.9 s (2% apart, within 20%). Every config ran all 8 notebooks with 17 passed, 0 failed and no errors, so pass/fail matches A. C (batch, private daemon, --gate) 114.1 s, B (batch, process compiles) 134.0 s. C is fastest and saves 47% against the A mean of 216.6 s, which is at least 25%. B is 17% slower than C, outside the 10% tie band, so C is adopted. The daemon had retired by the end of the run. Drafted for r31 (scratch apply-notebook-batch.ps1): eoie_dev_spiral_tests selects the registered notebooks in order and runs them as one Cells batch (--output-dir target/spiral-tests, --no-html, --gate, private compiler port 20000 + pid % 10000, timeout = per-notebook timeout x count). It streams output as before and attributes failures from each 'spiral-tests file' report: failed > 0 or no report counts as failed, with the batch exit code, so the 'spiral notebooks failed (n): ...' summary stays. Only present files go in, since Cells aborts a batch on an unreadable path. Cargo check running.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_6 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 6u32, "2026-10-22T07:53Z", "eoie", "03:53 groom: the row said Planned 0% but the work is in the live tree: r31 apply-notebook-batch (eoie_dev_spiral_tests), composed into release ABC (regen published 02:36, candidate built). It installs with the R13 tail (now or next session under R14); verify the verb on the installed binary, then close.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_7 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 7u32, "2026-10-23T07:32Z", "eoie", "03:32 eoie: measurement from F8 (batch mode, one Cells call with 81 --path, shared daemon). Cargo tests 00:21-00:53 (32 min); notebook batch 00:53-02:48 (115 min, 1.42 min per notebook) with 2-4 GB free, one daemon retire and cold restart (01:45, free under its 2048 floor), main's review and four Claude sessions on the box. The 2026-10-21 quiet-box figure was 61 notebooks in 2135 s (0.58 min each), so tonight ran about 2.4x slower per notebook. CLI notebooks dominate (notes_cli 7 min, planning_cli 4.5, windows_cli 5), and 26 of the last 40 were CLI. F6's run on the same tree under a 4096 floor managed ~35 notebooks in 99 min (2.8 min each): the floor alone roughly doubled the time. Budget rule for release job limits: cargo 35 min + notebooks x 1.5 min + 30 min tail under load, i.e. 3.5 h for 81 notebooks, inside the 6 h limits now used.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_8 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 8u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 60m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D4 at 80%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_9 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 9u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_10 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 10u32, "2026-10-29T22:44Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:44 kit: per-worker MIX_BUILD_PATH alone does NOT remove Cells lock waits. TRAIN4e (job-1793312681480) ran with true per-worker slots (named mutex; exactly mix-worker-0 and mix-worker-1 plus mix-control under the run folder), and 2 of 7 logs still show 'Waiting for lock on the build directory (held by process N)': window_model.spi during its worker's first '==> spiral_cells / Compiling 43 files', and windows_cli.spi (twice, same holder). TRAIN4c (per-thread dirs) had 2 of 90, TRAIN4b (shared _build) had the links_model red. All 4 waiting notebooks passed. So the contended lock is shared beyond MIX_BUILD_PATH: the deps build, Mix's lock keyed outside the build path, or the Cells compiler daemon's shadow root (TEMP\\spiral-cells-compiler is a fixed path in compiler_daemon.ex). Next: run two workers with distinct MIX_BUILD_PATH AND MIX_DEPS_PATH on a fresh Cells checkout copy and see which lock remains; record the holder pid's command line when it happens.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_11 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 11u32, "2026-10-29T23:37Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T23:37 kit: lock probe on the node (job-1793316731841, C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\tools\\node-lock-probe.ps1): two notebooks launched together (csv.spi, channel.spi), each with its own COLD MIX_BUILD_PATH and the shared apps\\cells\\deps, both passed with lock_waits=0 and 0 holders captured. So kit's own parallel workers do NOT contend once their build paths differ; per-worker MIX_BUILD_PATH is sufficient for intra-train isolation. The box's waits (TRAIN4c 2/90, TRAIN4e 2/7) most likely came from OTHER lanes' Cells runs at the same moment: main noted main and x compiled on the box during TRAIN4e, and windows_cli waited twice on one holder pid. Next: capture the holder's command line live on the box (the probe's watcher loop does this) inside release-train, so the next wait names its process.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_12 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 12u32, "2026-10-29T23:49Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T23:49 kit: ROOT CAUSE FOUND; it corrects n11's 'other lanes' guess. release-train sets $env:MIX_BUILD_PATH (and KIT_BIN_UNDER_TEST, SPIRAL_LIB, SPIRAL_CELLS_*) inside ForEach-Object -Parallel, but $env: is process-wide and all runspaces share one process. The last worker's value wins, and a child started by '& pwsh' inherits whatever is set at that instant. Proof: 4 parallel runspaces each set RACE_PROBE=w<n>, slept, and ALL read 'w4'. So two concurrent notebooks could start on the SAME build dir despite the mutex slots, which is exactly TRAIN4e's 2 lock waits. The node probe set env sequentially before each Start-Process, so no race and 0 waits, consistent. Fix (drafted, dry run ok): C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\train5\\train-lock-holder.do starts each notebook with Start-Process -Environment @{MIX_BUILD_PATH=mix-worker-<slot>; KIT_BIN_UNDER_TEST; SPIRAL_LIB; SPIRAL_CELLS_*} (pwsh 7.6.9), proven: 4 parallel children each saw their own value w1..w4. It also captures any lock holder's command line live into the notebook log. Rides TRAIN5; done = TRAIN5 notebooks with 0 'Waiting for lock'. Scope check 19:51: a scan of every .ps1 in kit/scripts, kit/compiler-contracts, i574n.github/scripts, apps/cells/spi and apps/compiler/scripts for $env: assignments inside -Parallel blocks finds ONLY release-train.ps1 (the scan's positive control). Its :87-:89 (KIT_BIN_UNDER_TEST, SPIRAL_LIB, SPIRAL_CELLS_*) have the same value in every worker, so they're harmless; :96 MIX_BUILD_PATH is the racing one, and train-lock-holder.do replaces it with per-child -Environment.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_13 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 13u32, "2026-10-30T01:59Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T01:59 kit groom: Paused -> Active, 80 -> 85: root cause found and fix drafted today (n12: process-wide $env race inside -Parallel; train5\\train-lock-holder.do with Start-Process -Environment). Deps EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN (ships in TRAIN5). Done = TRAIN5 notebooks with 0 'Waiting for lock'.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_14 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 14u32, "2026-10-30T10:25Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T10:25 kit groom: partial evidence from the lost TRAIN5 (first train with the lock-holder/-Environment fix): 13 notebook logs, 0 'Waiting for lock' lines. Encouraging but not the done-line (a full notebook set). Stays 85, waits on the TRAIN5 rerun.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_15 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 15u32, "2026-10-31T07:48Z", "kit", "10-31 03:3x kit groom: Active -> Blocked. The only remaining step is installed-is-done: TRAIN5e (job-1793421047190, snapshot 00:31) carries this change, and KIT-AGILE-LOST-UPDATES's last part ships in the train after it. Unblock and verify once kit.exe is installed. Moved so that 'agile next --team kit' stops ranking an install wait above actionable work (43 kit items were Active).")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_workers_16 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS", 16u32, "2026-10-31T12:08Z", "kit", "10-31 07:4x kit: DONE (installed-is-done). Done line met: TRAIN5e ran the FULL 140-notebook set with workers, and its logs hold 0 'Waiting for lock' lines (Select-String over .cache/release-train/train5e-20261031/*.log). release-acceptance.ps1 on the installed kit E14F1F0B (TRAIN5e DONE 07:43, 140/140 notebooks; the binary was swapped retire-then-move while lanes ran) passed 23/23 at 07:44.")
