inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_1 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 1u32, "2026-10-22T00:44Z", "x", "2026-10-21 20:44 x: measured at filing: xsim_frames.gleam 9,093 lines, Gleam 1.19.2 installed = latest (gh release list). The 1.19.0 changelog has no explicit parallel-compilation feature (build tool: compact binary cache, compile-package --src-only, .app generation, --otp-app-names; compiler section: nothing on build speed), so parallelism has to come from our side: independent packages compiled concurrently, and measured, not assumed. Cross-package calls need exported types the way the Rust crates do (same seam as RUST-EXPORT-TYPES); the cut lines follow the shared domain modules the sim already reads (protocol, frames, scenario, invariants, x_world). Decision rule, written before measuring: keep the split if every package is <= 1,000 lines and sim -fast plus corpus wall are not slower than the single file by more than 10% on a quiet machine; parallel builds are kept only if they cut cold build wall by >= 20%.")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_2 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 2u32, "2026-10-22T00:46Z", "x", "2026-10-21 20:46 x: groundwork (text-only, no compile). simframes.spi is 109 lines and opens prelude, glyphs, protocol, splitter, schedule, invariants, world, scenario, personas, so the one Gleam file is the whole sim bulk inlined into one module. Of its 9,093 lines, 97 named functions: anonymous method* specializations hold 4,450 (49%), then encoded 280, fields_from 279, closure* 259, world_files 257, spiral_string_slice helpers 253, string_from 194, decoded 164, without_controls 143, digits_end/start 143/140, masked_cells 137, masked_threads 131. So a split by entry file alone will not reach 1,000 per package unless the specializations split with it: each package's entry must only reach its own slice (frames codec: encoded/decoded/fields_from/string_from; scenario + personas + schedule; invariants + masked_*; world: world_files), and the shared helpers (spiral_string_* , digits_*, without_controls) go into one base package the others depend on. First step next session: compile one slice entry (e.g. frames codec alone) and measure its line count before cutting the rest.")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_3 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 3u32, "2026-10-22T00:51Z", "x", "2026-10-21 20:51 x: first-step tool drafted: lab/SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES/slice_sizes.ps1 copies the client .spi tree and package.spiproj (packageDir made absolute) to a temp dir, writes one entry per export group of simframes.spi (splitter, stream, world, run, facade, invariant exports: main calls only that group) and compiles each to Gleam with the installed compiler, printing lines per slice. Not working yet: every slice compile exits 5 in the copy (a first run showed 'Module .../net/net.spi is not loaded' until the crate subfolders were copied too; the cause of the remaining exit 5 is unread). Next session: run it with -Keep in a compile window and read slice_*.gleam.err, likely the slice entry needs listing in the copied package.spiproj modules or a ///- --package header like the crate entries.")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_4 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 4u32, "2026-10-22T01:40Z", "x", "2026-10-21 21:40 x: lab rule: slice_sizes.ps1 pruned. Its method, for the Spiral version of the split: copy every apps/client .spi recursively (package.spiproj lists crate entries such as net/net, which must exist) plus package.spiproj with packageDir made absolute; per export group write an entry = simframes.spi up to 'inl main' plus a main calling only that group; compile with dotnet <single-flight SpiralCompiler.dll> --backend Gleam <entry> <out>; count lines. It exited 5 for every group with the error unread; first check whether the entry must be listed in the copied package.spiproj modules.")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_5 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 5u32, "2026-10-22T03:10Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:10 x: dependency layers of the sim closure (text-only; 12 client modules, 2,476 .spi lines): base = prelude 87, glyphs 187, exsss 110, protocol 158; events layer = events 365, final_state 119; splitter 218 (opens protocol); invariants 314 (opens events); scenario layer = personas 198, scenario 187, schedule 156 (opens nearly all); world 377 (opens 8). The compiler emits one self-contained program per entry, so a package per simframes export group (splitter, stream, world, run, facade, invariant) carries its own copy of the base helpers, as each Rust crate does (app_reply 116 lines in 4 crates). Expected sizes need the compile; the 9,093-line single file holds about 4,450 lines of anonymous specializations, so packages whose entries reach only a slice should fall well under 1,000. Wiring: the Elixir side calls the Gleam module by name (apps/sim/lib/xsim/spiral.ex), so each package's module name goes there too.")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_6 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 6u32, "2026-10-22T03:18Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:18 x: split implemented in source, compile pending (waits for rung 10:8:4 to finish, so the 2 GB floor isn't tripped by my own compiles). The earlier exit 5 had a found cause: subfolder entries must be listed in apps/client/package.spiproj ('simframes/' then 'simframes'), and the slice copies weren't. Layout, mirroring the Rust crates: apps/client/simexports.spi = simframes.spi minus its main (the 100 lines of helpers and export groups, a listed module). Six entries apps/client/sim{split,stream,world,corpus,raw,checks}/<name>.spi ('open simexports' plus a main calling one export group), listed in package.spiproj and excluded from the Cargo workspace. Six Gleam packages apps/sim/gleam/<name>/ (gleam.toml name xsim_<name>, src/xsim_<name>.gleam generated by BuildSpiral), plus apps/sim/gleam/gleam.toml as a root package with the six as path dependencies, so one build compiles gleam_stdlib once (Gleam has no workspaces; this is the Cargo-workspace equivalent). Xsim.Spiral.call routes each of the 15 exports to its package module (@package_of) and loads every package's ebin. apps/sim/build.ps1 compiles the six entries and builds the packages; .gitignore ignores gleam/build and gleam/*/build. Pending, in order: compile the six and read their line counts (the decision: every package <= 1,000, else cut further by function group); then gleam_bench (cold sequential vs parallel vs one root build, 2 rounds; parallel or root kept only at >= 20% faster cold); then sim -fast with byte-exact fixtures, the corpus wall per schedule, and mix test; then remove apps/sim/frames, simframes/ and its spiproj entry, update the client README paragraph and the gate's data allowlist.")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_7 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 7u32, "2026-10-22T03:23Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:23 x: measured, decision by the rule fixed in note 1 (\"keep the split if every package <= 1,000 lines\"): NOT MET, so the entry-level split is reverted. Six entries (one simframes export group each) compiled with the installed single-flight compiler, 13-19 s each. Gleam lines: split 846, stream 4,054, world 5,109, corpus 3,520, raw 4,035, checks 2,245. Total 19,809, i.e. 2.2x the single file's 9,093, because each self-contained entry carries its own copy of the scenario, events and world machinery. Profile of xsim_world.gleam (170 fns): 110 anonymous method specializations hold 3,090 lines (60%); 18 of them are 60+ lines (2,790 lines, the largest 439); several share one long parameter list (Int, Int, Uh10, Uh12, Uh13, Us11, Uh14, ...), likely the scenario state threaded through inlined folds. A single export (world_files) already needs about 5,000 lines, so no entry-level cut reaches 1,000. Only splitting one entry's output across several files does, which is compiler work: filed as GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES (main). The design stays recorded in notes 6-7 (simexports module, entries listed in package.spiproj, a root gleam.toml with path packages as the workspace equivalent, Xsim.Spiral routing table) for when the compiler can emit modules. Not run: gleam_bench (pointless without packages under the limit).")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_8 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 8u32, "2026-10-22T03:47Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:47 x: a working route that doesn't wait for the compiler. scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 post-processes the generated Gleam into modules of one package. Exports are read from the entry's !!!!Export names (15) and stay in the top module, so Elixir's apply(:xsim_frames, ...) is unchanged (Gleam compiles submodules into the same ebin as xsim_frames@partN). Types and constructors go to xsim_frames/types; functions are packed by call-graph SCCs in callee-first order into xsim_frames/part1..partN (import cycles impossible); each module imports only the names it uses, unqualified. Result on tonight's 9,093-line file: 12 modules, largest 957 lines (10 parts of 837-957 lines, top module 70, types 295). gleam build on 1.19.2: 0 errors; warnings 293 -> the compiler's own unused args/vars plus 5 unused-module false positives. Benchmark, 3 cold builds each (compile window, deps rebuilt each time): single file 12.57 / 15.71 / 11.55 s vs 12 modules 8.71 / 9.34 / 8.67 s, about 30% faster cold, likely erlc being superlinear on one 9k-line module plus per-module parallelism; warm one-file touch about equal (2.0-3.0 s). Wired into apps/sim/build.ps1 right after BuildSpiral. Adoption rule, written before running: keep it as x's interim build step only if every module <= 1,000 lines AND apps/sim/build.ps1 -fast 1 passes (byte-exact world/frames/events fixtures, mix test incl. spiral_test.exs, corpus commute). That run is queued as heavy job x-sim-fast-modules behind tibia's jobs. GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES (compiler) stays the proper home; this script is its executable spec.")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_9 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 9u32, "2026-10-22T03:50Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:50 x: behavioral equivalence checked without the heavy slot. An escript loaded each scratch build in its own VM (single-file xsim_frames vs the 12-module split, both from tonight's tree file), called all 15 exports with fixed inputs derived from apps/client/prelude.spi (file_cells, thread_posts, split_cell, scenario_stream/final, world_files, corpus_run, corpus_raw, cells_raw, rescheduled on the generated stream, break_source, checked/commute/breaking/masked_text), and printed sha256(term_to_binary(result)) for each, with Gleam panics reduced to their message so module names can't differ. 15/15 identical, including the large results: world_files 48,766 bytes, rescheduled 41,234, scenario_stream 34,653, corpus_run 31,585. The queued apps/sim/build.ps1 -fast 1 (byte-exact fixtures, mix test, corpus) remains the formal adoption gate per note 8.")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_10 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 10u32, "2026-10-22T04:21Z", "x", "2026-10-22 00:21 x: adoption run x-sim-fast-modules (apps/sim/build.ps1 -fast 1 with the gleam-modules step), interim: generated 9,078 lines (installed compiler) -> 12 modules, largest 957; gleam build ok; the four world fixtures, thread-oauth1 frames and the prelude events/final fixtures all byte-identical (Confirm-Fixture throws on any difference); mix test 43 passed (same count as the 10-20 pre-commit proof, so spiral_test.exs's byte-for-byte fixture checks ran against the split modules). Corpus run (2 schedules + commute) in progress; the adoption decision follows its result.")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_11 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 11u32, "2026-10-22T04:24Z", "x", "2026-10-22 00:24 x: determinism check (CPU-only): scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 run twice on the same 9,093-line input produced 12 files with identical sha256 per path, so rebuilds don't churn the committed modules (imports are sorted, and parts follow the source's function order through Tarjan).")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_12 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 12u32, "2026-10-22T04:41Z", "x", "2026-10-22 00:41 x: ADOPTED by the rule in note 8. x-sim-fast-modules (apps/sim/build.ps1 -fast 1 with the gleam-modules step) passed, exit 0, wall 46 min including queue time: 9,078 generated lines -> 12 modules (largest 957); gleam build ok; all world/frames/events fixtures byte-identical; mix test 43 passed; corpus 41 files, 2 schedules, every invariant ok on both (schedule 1 ms=42,057, schedule 2 ms=39,901, peak_beam_mb 74/83), commute=ok result=pass; corpus wall 636 s = 318 s per schedule (installed compiler without the night pack's slice fix, busy machine; the baseline 286 s is the same measure). Landed: scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 (exports read from the entry's !!!!Export names, deterministic, throws on any module over 1,000 lines), wired into apps/sim/build.ps1 after BuildSpiral; README scripts row; client README sentence. The Gleam is now \"packages like the Rust crates\" in the sense the 1,000-line rule needs (one Gleam package, modules of <=1,000 lines). The parallel-build benchmark (note 8) came out about 30% faster cold than the single file on Gleam 1.19.2, with no 1.19 parallel feature involved. The compiler-side version stays in GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES. Commit note for the user: the generated src/xsim_frames.gleam (65 lines) and src/xsim_frames/*.gleam (11 files) replace the staged 9,093-line file.")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_13 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 13u32, "2026-10-25T00:02Z", "x", "10-24 20:02 x: REOPENED (user 10-24 20:0x: 'why split into parts instead of proper parallelizable compilable packages with their toml? drifting from eoie's lessons'). Where the drift entered: note 12 (10-22 00:41). It closed this item with scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 (one package, part1..partN modules) and redefined the goal as 'packages in the sense the 1,000-line rule needs'. The gate replaced the intent. The title asks for packages, each with its own gleam.toml, wired as path deps and built in parallel, like x's Rust crates and eoie's 226 granular crates. The 10-22 package attempt (note 7) failed for a reason I didn't fix. It cut at export-group granularity, 6 coarse entries each inlining the whole scenario/events/world closure (2.2x). eoie's answer is finer granular crates plus 'let' join points (not inl) and exported nominal types (RUST-EXPORT-TYPES), not one package with post-hoc parts. The parts script stays only as the interim that keeps the gate green, and it is already costing: X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS is its import handling breaking on a lib change. The real work and its rule are in XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH.")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_14 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 14u32, "2026-10-28T10:31Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:29 x groom: deps set to XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH and assignee agent: its real work moved there (last note). Closes when the xsim_split package lands.")
inl note_of_sim_gleam_packages_15 () = Note ("SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES", 15u32, "2026-10-28T11:08Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it). Its real work, the xsim_split package, landed with XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_1 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-22T01:39Z", "x", "2026-10-21 21:39 x: filed for main from the 21:4x inventory (scratchpad lab-inventory.csv columns: repo, folder, files, tracked, KB, extensions, newest, item status). x did not touch spiral/lab except its own CELLS-POSTABILITY-PROFILE draft, which main promotes by applying it (apply.ps1 -Apply writes x_compat.ex, the test and its fixtures into apps/cells) or prunes.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_2 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-22T01:41Z", "main", "21:41 main inventory for session 2026-10-21 (rule relayed by x at 21:4x; read only, nothing deleted): 22 spiral/lab folders have files written since 07:00, 253 files. Planned disposition, not executed: CARRY once until PACK-AFTER-NIGHT / CELLS-PACK-NEXT install, then promote as the tree edit and prune the script: FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS (13), BEND-BACKEND (11), RUST-EXPORT-TYPES (4), SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE (3), GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX (2), SPIRAL-OP-ARG-NAME-WORDS (17, incl. the regression sample to promote into samples/), SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES (1), SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL (1), CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS (1), CELLS-DAEMON-MEMSUP (1), CELLS-PROBES (1), KINO-HOT-PATHS (3), CELLS-POSTABILITY-PROFILE (3, x's draft), SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR (1). CARRY once: SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS (44: family .spi files are the promotion candidates as test blocks, paused by the 2x rule; gate data stays as the measurement record), MACHINE-WATCH (3 PowerShell scripts; promotion means an eoie verb, EOIE-MACHINE-WATCH), HOPACSOTA-P2 (2), COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB (3), CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE (1, probe-lib.ps1 is a live tool), CI-OFFLOAD (1, waits for the user to paste). PRUNE candidates once confirmed: GLEAM-STRING-LINEAR bench outputs (108 files today; findings in its notes 1-13; keep patch-slice.ps1 until the night pack installs), SEFAZ-XSD-XML-READER's 29 files from today. spiral/lab is fully untracked (git status: ?? lab/), so a prune cannot be undone with git; prunes wait for a user confirmation of the rule, since it reached me through a peer.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_3 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-22T01:47Z", "main", "21:47 rule for tonight's session sweep, fixed before moving (user via x 21:5x: decide autonomously, back up in internal dirs; x's tested scheme: prune = move to <repo>/target/lab-attic/<date>/<ID>/, target/ is git-ignored in spiral (checked: .gitignore line 2) and core.ps1 only clears target/<profile>/deps|incremental|build). ATTIC now: files nothing reads any more whose finding is in the item note: SPIRAL-OP-ARG-NAME-WORDS bisect files not used by candidate.ps1 (bisect_a, b, c, c1, c2, c3, c5, op_arg_width, run-bisect, run-pair; findings in notes 2-3), GLEAM-STRING-LINEAR bench outputs (findings in notes 1-13; patch scripts stay, patch-balanced-concat is still staged), SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS whole folder (paused by its 2x rule; suite-time.ps1 reads gates-20261021 relatively, so it moves intact and returns with the folder). CARRY once, named: folders a queued script reads by path (pack-night: SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL; pack-next: BEND-BACKEND, FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS, GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX, RUST-EXPORT-TYPES, SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE, SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES; CELLS-PACK-NEXT: CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS, CELLS-DAEMON-MEMSUP, CELLS-PROBES, CELLS-POSTABILITY-PROFILE, KINO-HOT-PATHS; sefaz-table-job: SEFAZ-XSD-XML-READER, MACHINE-WATCH, CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE; morning-measure: COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB; op-arg proof: the rest of SPIRAL-OP-ARG-NAME-WORDS), tools named in tracked docs (FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS in lanes/formatter/PLAN.md, HOPACSOTA-P2 in lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md, probe-lib.ps1 used by every family and lib gate), and items waiting on the user (CI-OFFLOAD paste, SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR promote). Each move: sha256 of every file before, Move-Item, sha256 at the attic path, mismatch = move back and stop.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_4 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-22T01:48Z", "main", "21:48 session 2026-10-21 swept by the note 3 rule: lab: promoted 0, pruned 156, carried 97 (in 21 folders). The prunes are moves to spiral/target/lab-attic/2026-10-21/ (160 files with 4 older ones in the same folders; sha256 equal for every file after the move, git status unchanged since target/ is ignored; no running process read them). Moved: SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS whole (45), GLEAM-STRING-LINEAR/bench (105, 99 of them gleam build output), 10 SPIRAL-OP-ARG-NAME-WORDS bisect files. The carries are the folders named in note 3, each read by a queued pack or job script, a tracked doc or the user's pending decision; each leaves at its install or decision. Mover: scratchpad lab-attic-move.ps1 (hash check, auto move-back on mismatch). Not done tonight: the 19 folders under Done items (separate sweep).")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_5 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-22T01:52Z", "main", "21:52 REVERSED: the attic was drift (user via x: target folders get deleted often to free disk). All 160 files moved back from spiral/target/lab-attic/2026-10-21 to their original spiral/lab paths (sha256 equal for every file), and the attic folder is removed; ignore the attic path in the earlier note. Healing is finishing the items that hold these folders (user 21:5x).")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_6 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-22T02:04Z", "main", "22:04 reframed by the user (22:0x): lab is for minutes-long theory tests; drain it by redoing each holding item properly in its real place, from scratch, with the lab files as reference, deleting a folder only after its item has landed; this comes before new work. Map of all 107 spiral/lab folders to their items (every folder has one): Done 19 folders / 766 files, Active 53 / 630, Blocked 1 / 13, Planned 32 / 92, Paused 2 / 5. Drain order: (1) Done items first, cheapest: check that each folder's result is in the tree or in notes, promote what isn't (SEFAZ-XSD-XML-READER's xsd/xml as fixtures beside the reader), then delete; Done: SEFAZ-XSD-XML-READER 625, KINO-PREFIX-MODULE-UNBOUND 33, BACKLOG-LAPS 17, KINO-RUST-CRATES 16, CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE 12, SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION 11, CELLS-EXHAUSTIVE-TESTS 10, GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE 9, HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH 9, SPIRAL-LITERAL-DEFAULTS 5, HANGULIZE-UPSTREAM-RETEST 4, MACHINE-WATCH 3, SPIRAL-CORE-NEQ 3, CELLS-WGSL-TEST-STATUS 2, X-COMPAT-BLOCK-WEIGHT 2, ZIG-PRIMARY-ORACLE 2, COMPILER-MODULE-PARSE-STACK 1, X-COMPAT-PARAMETERIZE 1, CELLS-ETS-OWNER 1. (2) the two P0 packs (PACK-AFTER-NIGHT, CELLS-PACK-NEXT) and the night install, which land about 14 folders. (3) Active by size: GLEAM-STRING-LINEAR 112 (P2 70%), SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS 45 (P1 30%), UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA 29 (P1 90%), FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS 26 (P0 90%), SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES 25 (P2 80%), CELLS-EVERYWHERE 24 (P1 5%), SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR 24 (P1 70%), DATE-TIME-PORTABLE 23 (P2 80%), HOPACSOTA-P2 18 (P0 30%), SPIRAL-OP-ARG-NAME-WORDS 18 (P2 60%), COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS 18 (P3 50%), BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS 16 (P1 60%), CELLS-NEW-PROJECT 16 (P1 50%), KINO-HOT-PATHS 15 (P1 50%), RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE 15 (P1 85%). (4) Planned and Paused folders: mostly a script or two each; land or fold into the item note when the item is next worked.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_7 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-22T06:51Z", "main", "02:51 main: night progress, measured now: spiral/lab has 67 folders and 443 files, from 107 folders / 1,506 files at 22:04 and 86 folders at the start of this run's second half. Drained tonight by landing or verifying: SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL (Done), SEFAZ-XSD-XML-READER (Done; 625 files now in C:\\home\\git\\sefaz), SEFAZ-XSD-APP, DATE-TIME-PORTABLE, EBPF-C-PROFILE, CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW, BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS, CI-OFFLOAD (job wired into gh-pages.yml), TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO (verify step in 4 workflows), LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS, CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK, SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS, SAMPLES-OUT-OF-TMP, CONVO-IN-SPIRAL, LIB-REACTORS, PS1-TO-SPIRAL; GLEAM-STRING-LINEAR is down to the one patch it lands with. Tools went to apps/compiler/tmp/scripts, apps/compiler/tmp/formatter, apps/cells/spi and spiral/scripts; 76 run outputs went to the run cache (lab-outputs); the stale lab-held tag came off 20 main items. Waiting on PACK-AFTER-NIGHT's install: BEND-BACKEND, FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS, SPIRAL-OP-ARG-NAME-WORDS, SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE, SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION, RUST-EXPORT-TYPES, SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE, GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX (checklist notes 14-17 there). Waiting on CELLS-PACK-NEXT: CELLS-PROBES, CELLS-NEW-PROJECT, CELLS-DAEMON-MEMSUP, CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS, KINO-HOT-PATHS, KINO-RUST-CRATES, CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE. The rest are live experiments of Active or Planned items.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_8 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-22T07:14Z", "main", "03:14 main: follow-up to the output move. Checked every remaining lab script against the moved file names. One script READ a moved output (SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS suite-time.ps1, repointed) and three called a promoted tool by its old lab path (gate-string/tagged-union/tuple, repointed to lib_gate.ps1). Five scripts would have WRITTEN new outputs back into lab, so their output paths now default to the run cache (lab-outputs\\<ITEM>): CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG measure.ps1, RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE verify.ps1, KINO-RUST-CRATES e2e-run.ps1, NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS contract-run.ps1 and run.ps1. All parse clean. Old session-scratchpad scripts still name drained folders, but they are one-off history and nothing reruns them.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_9 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-23T07:21Z", "main", "2026-10-23 03:21 main: measured now: spiral/lab has 52 folders and 298 files (67 / 443 at 10-22 02:51). Tonight's drain was by verified landing only: GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX (union-depth.ps1, union-depth-doc.ps1), SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE (patch.py, expected-base.ps1), SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES (nominal_with_patch.py, retype-census.ps1, run.ps1, build-errors.ps1). Each was checked against the tree core, plus a by-stem search with a positive control. Held by queued work, not drainable yet: cells-pack reads CELLS-PROBES, CELLS-DAEMON-MEMSUP, CELLS-NEW-PROJECT, CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS, KINO-HOT-PATHS, KINO-RUST-CRATES and CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE. The post-install matrix bundle reads LEAN-BACKEND, CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG and ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY. next4 carries BEND-BACKEND's union-w64. The combined lib landing at tibia's block end closes COMPILER-NAMESPACE-COLLISIONS' scope; its 18 repro files go after that.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_10 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-23T21:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:30 main: measured: spiral/lab has 51 folders and 282 files (52/298 at 03:21). Today's landings (formatter-only install, lib batches: seq/listm', sm', gleam_erlang 1.x, erlang_process/otp_*/step_runtime) held no lab folder, so none drains by the landed-only rule. Next drains are tied to installs: lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX (4 probes) after next9's GADT cap installs (proven 10.9 s EJP0041 in a Debug build today); lab/SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS after its families run through apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test-blocks.ps1 (queued tonight, job-1792778604345) and move into their real place; lab/HOPACSOTA-P2 and HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH stay (measurement runners still cited by open items).")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_11 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-24T05:52Z", "main", "2026-10-24 01:52 main: measured 52 folders; none belongs to a Done item, so none drains by the landed-only rule tonight (all 52 map to Active, Paused or Planned items). Queued drains: after the Cells pack lands (cells-pack.ps1 -Target apps/cells at tibia's PR-27 block end), the 18 steps' sources in CELLS-PROBES, CELLS-DAEMON-MEMSUP, CELLS-NEW-PROJECT, CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS, KINO-HOT-PATHS and CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE become drainable, each checked against apps/cells first; after pack 5g installs, GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX (4 probes; the cap is in 5g).")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_12 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-24T06:30Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:3x main: drain list prepared for after the post-block Cells suite confirms the 02:23 landing (landed-only rule: only files the Cells pack APPLIED; measurement runners that open items still cite stay). CELLS-PROBES: patched_mounts.py, probe.py (keep cell-phases.ps1, patched-mounts-run.ps1, probe_text.exs, standalone-check.exs until CELLS-PROBES closes). CELLS-DAEMON-MEMSUP: bounded-memsup.ps1 (the folder then empties). CELLS-NEW-PROJECT: patch\\* (keep the run and gate scripts and pending\\). CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS: pool-probe-async.ps1. KINO-HOT-PATHS: server-apply.ps1 and the server modules it copies, if byte-equal to apps/cells (keep fixed-cost, in-vm-rounds, memo-cache, preload, warm-* runners). CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE: document_patch.py, patch.py, reference_test.exs. Each deletion is preceded by a by-stem search over the sibling repos and scratch scripts, with a positive control.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_13 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-10-24T21:52Z", "main", "main 17:5x inventory: spiral/lab is down to 52 folders and 286 files (107 folders / 1,506 files at the item's start). 21 folders are untouched since before 10-22; the oldest are QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER, HOPACSOTA-P5, GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX, GPU-RENTAL-READINESS, ML-LABS, SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX, LEAN-BACKEND and CRATE-GATE-HARDEN (all 10-19). Today DIB-MODERNIZE was drained: ports landed, folder moved to %TEMP%\\lab-attic after a ripgrep stem search. Next, per the session rule: for each of the 21, land-or-attic by item status (Done item -> attic after a stem search; Active item -> keep), oldest first.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_14 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-10-24T21:52Z", "main", "main 17:5x: applied the land-or-attic rule to the 7 oldest folders by item status: QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER (Planned 0%), HOPACSOTA-P5 (Planned 0%), GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX (Active 40%), ML-LABS (Planned 0%), SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX (Planned 5%), LEAN-BACKEND (Active 30%), CRATE-GATE-HARDEN (Active 40%). None is Done, so all 7 stay. The other 14 pre-10-22 folders are next; GPU-RENTAL-READINESS (Planned) also stays.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_15 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 15u32, "2026-10-24T21:53Z", "main", "main 17:5x: all 21 lab folders untouched since before 10-22 are now checked against their items' status: none is Done (Active: GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX, HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH, KINO-HOT-PATHS, LEAN-BACKEND, ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY, CRATE-GATE-HARDEN; Paused: SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT, TODO-AS-SOURCE; Planned: the rest). By the rule all stay; the sweep picks them up as each item closes. The sweep is at the steady state: 52 folders, each tied to an open item.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_16 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 16u32, "2026-10-25T00:01Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:01 main groom: two items closed in this grooming pass had lab folders: CELLS-DAEMON-MEMSUP (1 file) and KINO-RUST-CRATES (12 files). Their work is in the tree, so by land-or-attic both moved to %TEMP%\\lab-attic\\<ID>-20261024-2001, hashes equal, nothing deleted. spiral/lab: 52 -> 50 folders, each tied to an open item.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_17 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 17u32, "2026-10-25T08:05Z", "main", "10-25 04:05 main: re-swept spiral/lab, 50 folders. Every folder named by an item ID belongs to an open item (none Done after today's closes). The one name that is not an item, LIB-PROCESS-HEAL (5 files, newest 10-24 21:30), is the tibia team's lab for LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE (notes 2/6/7 name it), which is open, so it stays. Gap in the rule: a lab folder named after a slice instead of its item ID has no direct status lookup; name labs by item ID.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_18 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 18u32, "2026-10-25T21:05Z", "main", "10-25 17:05 main: re-swept spiral/lab, 51 folders: every folder named by an item ID belongs to an open item (Active 28, Planned 15, Paused 6; none Done after today's closes), so land-or-attic moves nothing. The two without an item are tibia's: LIB-PROCESS-HEAL (seen 04:05, LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE) and the new LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT (9 files), whose name says the same item. The rule gap from 04:05 still stands (name labs by item ID); tibia owns those two. lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN changed today: respelled to edition 3 plus the index-line types and status_probe.spi (its probe runs on the node, kit2 job-1792962100454).")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_19 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 19u32, "2026-10-26T08:13Z", "main", "10-26 04:13 main: re-swept spiral/lab, 51 folders: Active 30, Planned 13, Paused 6, none under a Done item, so land-or-attic moves nothing. The same two tibia folders have no item of their own (LIB-PROCESS-HEAL and LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT, both for LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE). lab/SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR's promote.ps1 and router_swap.py were retargeted today from spiral.livemd to spiral.spi (NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-RETIRE n2).")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_20 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 20u32, "2026-10-26T14:22Z", "main", "10-26 10:22 main: re-swept spiral/lab 10:2x: 51 folders, Active 31, Planned 12, Paused 6, none under a Done item, so land-or-attic moves nothing. The same two tibia folders (LIB-PROCESS-HEAL, LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT, both for LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE) still have no item of their own.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_21 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 21u32, "2026-10-26T19:07Z", "main", "10-26 15:07 main: 10-26 15:0x main: re-swept spiral/lab, 51 folders, status read through eoie agile show per folder: Active 31, Planned 12, Paused 6, none under a Done item, so land-or-attic moves nothing. The same two tibia folders (LIB-PROCESS-HEAL, LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT, both for LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE) have no item of their own. Method note: a first pass that regex-matched agile.spi's record text reported 28 folders as NO-ITEM, BLOG and PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN among them, which exist. The status must come from the tool, not a guess at the record format (the same 'one record per fact' reason).")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_22 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 22u32, "2026-10-26T22:54Z", "main", "10-26 18:53 main: 10-26 18:53 main: re-swept spiral/lab, status read through eoie agile show per folder: 51 folders, 306 files, Active 31, Planned 12, Paused 6, none under a Done item, so land-or-attic moves nothing. Checked because tibia reported LIB-PROCESS-HEAL landed at 17:26: its folder and LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT both belong to LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE, still Active at 90%, so both stay until that item closes (tibia's folders, tibia's drain). lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN changed tonight: the Nil-to-[] rewrite (114 sites, 13 files), store_lock_probe.spi and the read-only store lock; it drains when the item's executor lands in its real place (spiral/apps or a lib package; placement is a git-structure call for the user).")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_23 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 23u32, "2026-10-27T12:24Z", "main", "10-27 08:24 main: two lab folders drained by rule (land first, then archive and remove proven duplicates; the user's archive-never-delete rule as relayed 10-27). Both folders are untracked, so git is untouched. 13 files are archived in hash-verified zips under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\main-archive\\20261027-0822-lab-drained, then removed. KINO-HOT-PATHS: cells_server.ex (byte-identical to apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/server.ex), spiral.cells_server.ex (the tree's mix task is a line-superset, adding MemoryGuard.start) and server-apply.ps1 (its notebook routing is in the tree). The by-stem reference search found 0 references, and its positive control hit. The measurement runners stay; they're the item's open half. CELLS-NEW-PROJECT: the 9 patch files byte-identical to apps/cells, plus PROJECT_NAME.spi, whose tree copy is the same template after the 10-24 edition-3 respell (a pure syntax mapping: open/nominal/inl/forall/if to :</:!/:=/\\\\*/?). The patch/ folder is gone. Left in CELLS-NEW-PROJECT: its 5 lab scripts and pending/scripts/probe.ps1, not yet judged.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_24 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 24u32, "2026-10-27T13:00Z", "main", "10-27 09:00 main: lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT is fully drained and removed (untracked, so no git change). The last 6 files are archived in a hash-verified zip (main-archive 20261027-0822-lab-drained, CELLS-NEW-PROJECT-rest.zip). They were the 10-19 proof runners for the scaffold that landed that day (new-project.ps1, scaffold-gate.ps1, spiral-lib-env-check.ps1, spiral-new-run.ps1, standalone-check.exs; the last loads the lab patch file, removed earlier, so it no longer runs) and pending/scripts/probe.ps1, superseded by the tree's priv/new_project/scripts/probe.ps1 (10-19 19:43 vs the lab's 12:52, 16+/8- newer in the tree). By-stem search: references only inside the folder itself (the positive control) and in backlog notes. Earlier the same session: the 9 identical patch files and the pre-respell PROJECT_NAME.spi. Spiral lab folders: one fewer. What CELLS-NEW-PROJECT still owes, per its title, is the scaffold CONTENT decisions (n20), not lab files.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_25 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 25u32, "2026-10-27T13:32Z", "main", "10-27 09:32 main: lab landed-file census over all 50 spiral lab folders (289 files): every lab file hashed against the tree outside lab/, target and deps. Byte-identical copies found: ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY 9 of 11, QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER 2 of 6, and 1 each in RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION, LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS and LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT; 0 in the other 45, which are live drafts, design notes and probes, judged one by one under the session rule. ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY: its 9 Zig shims are in apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims (byte-identical). Its hook zig_shims_hook.py has its effect in the tree: the 9 abi_external samples are on matrix.psd1's Zig list, test.ps1 loads shims from tests/native-shims, and the 10-27 review ran 9 abi_external Zig rows agreeing with C. shims_probe.zig is a probe. So the folder is spent, BUT TEMP matrix-bundle/bundle.ps1:7 still calls the hook from this lab path, so it's archived and removed only after the bundle accept (job-1793103041735) is GREEN, when bundle.ps1 can't need a re-apply. Next by this census: QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER's 2 landed files.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_26 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 26u32, "2026-10-27T13:36Z", "main", "10-27 09:36 main: correction to n25's census: byte identity over-counts tiny boilerplate. QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER's 2 'landed' files are an 18-byte bare/package.spiproj and a 39-byte core-only/package.spiproj that happen to equal two unrelated samples' boilerplate spiproj (abi_external_struct_return_i64, ebpf_char). They are not landings. The folder is the epic's unlanded probes (QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER is Planned, 0%), so it stays. The single matches in RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION, LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS and LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT get the same check before any drain. Rule for this census from now on: a match counts as landed only if the file is over 200 bytes, or its tree twin sits at the path the lab folder's own apply or patch names. ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY's 9 shims pass that rule (each is at the native-shims path the bundle's hook writes).")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_27 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 27u32, "2026-10-27T14:04Z", "main", "10-27 10:04 main: lab/LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT archived and removed (untracked; 9 files in a hash-verified zip under main-archive 20261027-0822-lab-drained). Owner tibia confirmed (10-27 10:1x) that every draft landed under other names, checked by top-level definition name against the live lib: shared_command 13/13 in runtime.spi, process_split 18/18 in process.spi, split_misc 4/4 across date_time, erlang_process and platform, process_split_all (their union) 49/49, erlang_port 14/14. LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE and LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE-SPLIT are both Done. main's spot check agreed: process_split 15/15 and shared_command 13/13 by name (main's pattern counts fewer definition forms than tibia's). The 3 .log files were empty gate stubs (0, 7 and 0 bytes). spiral lab is now 49 folders.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_28 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 28u32, "2026-10-27T16:43Z", "main", "10-27 12:43 main: lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION drained to its one live probe (untracked, so no git change). Archived by scratch-archive.ps1 (kit archive 20261027-124241, blob verify 0 failures; restore with -Restore that manifest.tsv) and removed: or_join_patch.py (v1, dropped from pack 8 on 10-19 for the format_any regression), or_join_patch_v2.py and run.ps1 (v1's candidate runner). The v3 design superseded both patches and landed as OrPatternJoin in the core through pack 5g: spiral_compiler.fs:44537 typeDirected, 44546 trivial, 44551 lifted, which lift a non-trivial, non-type-directed or-pattern body into a function of its bound variables. The patches' anchor line no longer exists in the core, so they can't apply anyway. Also removed: or_pattern.spi, byte-identical (271 B, over the census's 200-byte floor) to the landed regression sample samples/or_pattern_bound_alternatives/main.spi. By-stem search (RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION, or_join_patch, lab\\or_pattern) over 971 scripts in the spiral, compiler-tmp, packs and i574n.github script folders and TEMP to depth 1: the only references are spent pack scripts (pack-next2/3 compose.ps1, next3/next3b review.ps1, next5g orjoin-check.ps1, all superseded or installed; current packs are 5n and 5o) and two old backlog exports. The positive control hit inside the folder. Kept: wildcard_pair.spi, the item's open case (a wildcard arm after two pair arms on a 3-case union), not yet measured. Spiral lab folder count is unchanged at 49.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_29 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 29u32, "2026-10-27T16:58Z", "main", "10-27 12:58 main: Lab sweep, pass by item status: each of the 49 spiral lab folders paired with its backlog item's state. Done items holding lab files: only LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE (5 files). Folders with no item: only LIB-PROCESS-HEAL (5 files). LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE (untracked): 5 of process_handle.spi's 7 top-level definitions are in lib by name, and started_switch landed as ':== started_switch' at process.spi:53 (a form the first name scan missed). But none of its 9 Gleam externals (spiral_process_await_logging, drain, get, kill_pid_tree, put, start_logging, started_pid, unlink, write_file) is in lib under that name; they probably landed in the granular form (gleam_started, erlang_port wrappers). Not provable by name, so the folder STAYS; owner tibia was asked (13:0x) to confirm, as for LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT. Its 3 logs are gate stubs ('ready', a directory path, and empty). LIB-PROCESS-HEAL: a live GRANULAR-HEAL draft (25 of 79 definitions in lib; the other 54 are the granular port, Rust command and Gleam dynamic wrappers), in tibia's queue per GRANULAR-HEAL (process.spi:185/204/217/232/246). It stays.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_30 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 30u32, "2026-10-27T17:26Z", "main", "10-27 13:26 main: lab/LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE archived and removed (untracked; kit archive 20261027-130455, 5 files, blob verify 0 failures). Owner tibia confirmed at 13:0x that nothing is unlanded, giving the granular landing site of each of the 9 Gleam externals in lib process.spi: write_file -> file_system.write_all_text :138 and append_all_text in gleam_waited :86; unlink -> erlang_port.unlink in port_for :23; get/put -> erlang_process.dictionary_get/put keyed by exit_key 'spiral_process_exit' :73; start_logging -> start_logging's Gleam arm via port_for + gleam_started :129; started_pid -> erlang_port.os_pid :147; await_logging -> await_exit_within with the memoised exit :154; drain -> the gleam_waited join point :79; kill_pid_tree -> kill_tree_of_pid's taskkill /F /T arm :39. The draft's tests (start, pid > 0, await, kill) are lib tests at process.spi:286-304. main spot-checked 4 sites in the live file, each within 3 lines of tibia's line (unlink :23, taskkill :36/:42, spiral_process_exit :74, os_pid :150). The draft's 3 logs were gate stubs. Spiral lab: 48 folders.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_31 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 31u32, "2026-10-27T17:42Z", "main", "10-27 13:42 main: Correction to n29: lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL was NOT a live draft. Owner tibia confirmed (13:3x) that it is fully landed and that the GRANULAR-HEAL queue entry process.spi:185/204/217/232/246 is done. Live process.spi:172-251 holds the healed capture family (exit_with, captured, not_run, rust_captured, gleam_collected, gleam_captured, captured_by, succeeded, required, words, capture_stdout(_list), capture_combined_list, try_capture_combined_list) and the draft's 4 tests. The draft's 54 names missing from lib were ad-hoc primitives that each moved to a granular home under a new name: port_* -> erlang_port, rust_command_*/rust_child_*/rust_file_create -> runtime.shared_command_*/shared_child_*/log_file_create, monotonic_ms/rust_now/rust_elapsed_seconds -> date_time.monotonic_milliseconds/stopwatch*, rust_pause -> threading.sleep, os_cmd -> platform.os_command_output, file_write -> file_system.write_all_text/append_all_text, and the dynamic/bits/gleam_* glue -> erlang_term; argument_separator was dropped on purpose (no callers). main spot-checked all six target modules for those names and 5 capture-family names in 172-251 (5/5). Archived by scratch-archive.ps1 and removed (untracked). Lesson for the census: a by-name check against lib can't see a draft whose primitives were re-homed under new names; the owner's mapping is the proof.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_32 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 32u32, "2026-10-27T17:49Z", "main", "10-27 13:49 main: Lab sweep of main-owned folders whose items are at 80% or more: 3 drained, all untracked, archived by scratch-archive.ps1 (kit archives 20261027-134855 and -134905, blob verify 0 failures), then removed. (1) BEND-BACKEND/union-w64.ps1, the folder's only file: all 6 of its edits are in spiral_compiler.fs. Edits 1-5 match verbatim. Edit 6 (the prelude's word projections) landed with a renamed parameter as 'def w64_hi(p: U32 & U32)'; a first scan cut its list at 6 definitions and missed it, so I checked the full text before calling anything a bug. In accept-2, every Bend i64/u64 row compiles, runs and exits 0. (2) CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE, all 3 files: patch.py (Cells: notebook.ex now has reference_target), document_patch.py (document.spi has --reference) and reference_test.exs (its test name is in apps/cells/test). (3) RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE/lift-pin.py only: dice/contract/build.ps1 carries nightly-2026-10-18, -Z build-std and target-cpu=mvp. Its near-mvp.ps1, near-sdk-bump.ps1 and verify.ps1 stay; they are the item's open NEAR half. By-stem search (737 scripts in the spiral, compiler-tmp, i574n.github and dice script folders plus TEMP to depth 1): the only references are spent pack scripts (cells-pack, pack-after-night, pack-next3/3b/4), and the positive control hit. Kept: COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB, whose 3 A/B runners are its open measurement. Spiral lab: 45 folders.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_33 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 33u32, "2026-10-27T23:25Z", "main", "10-27 19:25 main: lab/ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY archived and removed (untracked; kit archive 20261027-192504, 11 files, blob verify 0 failures, 0 links). Its hold condition from n25 is met: the bundle accept is GREEN (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417), so TEMP matrix-bundle/bundle.ps1 can no longer need the lab hook for a re-apply. Its 9 Zig shims live byte-identical in apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims, and the hook's effect is in matrix.psd1 and test.ps1 (n25). Spiral lab: 44 folders.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_34 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 34u32, "2026-10-28T11:12Z", "main", "10-28 07:12 main: Sweep 10-28 07:1x: matched each of the 44 spiral/lab folders to its item's status. Four folders belong to Done items. Three were spent and are archived (kit archive 20261028-071145, 6 files, blob verify 0 failures; restorable): SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX (tld_members.py plus a tlds.txt copy; x reads its own postability/tlds.txt), CRATE-GATE-HARDEN (crate-report.ps1, dice_ui_metadata.py) and CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG (measure.ps1, switch.py). A stem grep found 0 references in x, dice, spiral/apps and i574n.github/scripts. Kept: HOPACSOTA-P2 (Done, but its alias-count and determinism tools are what P3's done rule re-runs; drain when P3 closes). The other 40 belong to open items (16 Active, 11 Paused, 13 Planned). Each drains with its item. 41 lab folders now, none tracked by git.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_35 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 35u32, "2026-10-29T05:54Z", "main", "10-29 02:0x main: post-wipe sweep (kit agile list all --json, 885 items, matched by folder name): spiral/lab has 42 folders, none tracked by git: 16 Active, 14 Paused, 12 Planned, 2 Done, 0 without an item. New since 10-28: COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE (5 files, the mover, its rehearsals and the kit dual-path draft; its item's n29). Both Done folders are KEPT, each for a live reason: HOPACSOTA-P2 (its alias-count and determinism tools rerun for P3's done rule, as on 10-28), and COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB, whose bisect.ps1 (-GcServer 0, -Prebuilt arms, paired per-round ratios, in-round A/A) is the method HOPACSOTA-P3 note 2026-10-28T14:30Z names for its 8-worker speed table. It drains when P3 closes or when the method becomes a kit verb (generic-tools rule; EOIE-EXPERIMENTS). Nothing archived this pass. No lab file was lost in the wipe (spiral is outside the profile).")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_36 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 36u32, "2026-10-29T06:12Z", "main", "10-29 02:1x main: CORRECTION to n35: the 'new' COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE lab folder was main's own and broke the stop-the-lab rule. Its files moved to apps/compiler/packs/tmp-retire (re-proven there; COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE note 30). spiral/lab: 41 folders, none created since 10-28.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_37 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 37u32, "2026-10-29T17:46Z", "main", "10-29 13:5x main: post-wipe lab sweep 2 (kit agile list, 897 items, matched by folder name): spiral/lab has 41 folders, none created since 10-28, 0 without an item. Status: 2 Done, 16 Active, 13 Paused, 10 Planned. The two Done folders stay for the same live reasons as n36: HOPACSOTA-P2's alias-count/determinism tools for P3's done rule, and COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB's bisect.ps1 (the A/A + -GcServer 0 method that ab-needs-in-job-aa relies on). So land-or-attic moves nothing. The \"newest 12:29\" stamp on 46 lab files is the COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE mover rewriting their tmp/scripts paths to apps/compiler/scripts: all 46 appear in the mover's undo set by name, and the samples read `. C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\apps\\compiler\\scripts\\heavy.ps1`. They are rewrites, not new lab work.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_38 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 38u32, "2026-10-29T23:09Z", "main", "10-29 19:09 main, pass: spiral/lab has 41 folders plus 2 marker .txt files. NO NEW FOLDER since 10-21 (main's own COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE lab folder from this morning was moved to apps/compiler/packs/tmp-retire). None of the 41 has a git-tracked file. Only 2 have Done items, and neither drains yet because each has a live reader: (1) HOPACSOTA-P2, because apps/compiler/packs/apply-hopacsota-p2-alias-order.ps1's VERIFY step runs lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/alternating-ab.ps1 (not yet run). When the compiler team runs that applier, it should move alternating-ab.ps1 next to the applier in packs/; then the folder drains. (2) COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB, because the tmp-retire undo (packs/tmp-retire/callers.txt plus undo/files) covers 6 of its scripts. It drains when COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE is Done (after the user's commit). The 12:29 mtimes across lab are the tmp-retire caller rewrite, not new work. Two dangling references are historical only: PLAN.md:886 (a dated log line) and HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH/spawn-send.ps1:10 (a throw message for a precondition the core already meets) name lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/{engine,parallel}-slice.ps1, which no longer exist; nothing to fix.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_39 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 39u32, "2026-10-29T23:31Z", "main", "10-29 19:31 main: HOPACSOTA-P2 update. git-a6 moved alternating-ab.ps1 to apps/compiler/packs/ (hash-equal) and re-pointed apply-hopacsota-p2-alias-order.ps1:20. The folder still does NOT drain, for two reasons: (1) the applier's negative gate also runs lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/alias-count-check.ps1 (HOPACSOTA-P2 n61), and git-a6 was asked to move it as well; (2) packs/tmp-retire/undo/manifest.json and callers.txt cover 10 of its files, as for COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB, so both folders drain once COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE is Done. The PLAN.md:886 and HOPAC-DEPTH spawn-send.ps1:10 hits remain historical.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_40 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 40u32, "2026-10-30T14:54Z", "main", "10-30 10:54 main, pass (post-heal): spiral/lab still has 41 folders and 249 files; none changed since 10-29 19:38. HOPACSOTA-P2's blocker (1) from 19:31 is CLEARED: alias-count-check.ps1 is in apps/compiler/packs/ (gone from lab), and the applier's line 23 names the packs copy. Only blocker (2) is left for HOPACSOTA-P2 and COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB: packs/tmp-retire's undo set covers their files, so both drain when COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE is Done, which waits on the user's spiral commit (COMMIT A in commit-suggestion.md). Nothing else is drainable now; the other 39 folders belong to open items and drain at each item's own close.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_41 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 41u32, "2026-10-31T00:03Z", "main", "10-30 20:03 main pass: spiral/lab has 41 folders (no change since 10:54). Done items still own 2 folders (COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB, HOPACSOTA-P2), and both still wait on COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE (the user's spiral commit). New candidate: lab/SPIRAL-TO-YAML (pins.spi, the 10-19 first draft of i574n.github/scripts/pins/pins.spi; pins.tsv, a stale pin census). Milestone 2 closed 19:5x, but the folder does NOT drain yet: apps/compiler/packs/tmp-retire/callers.txt:57 lists lab/SPIRAL-TO-YAML/pins.tsv, the same blocker as the two above (a rg reference scan of C:\\home\\git finds only that and backlog notes). Note: the lab draft's RustNear and DotnetCompiler pins are not in the landed pins.spi (the archive keeps them if wanted). Archive it with the other two when COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE is Done.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_42 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 42u32, "2026-10-31T03:22Z", "main", "10-30 23:22 main: SWEPT on the user's word (relayed by kit, 22:5x: 'the lab dir didn't work, it drifted and we still didn't move the files properly'). The old rule, drain each folder only when its item closes, was the drift itself, so it is dropped. Done: 38 of 41 folders (225 untracked files) archived with scratch-archive.ps1 (archive 20261030-232045, verified; each item has a note with its restore command). Two had live references and now have real homes: CI-NIX's flake.nix is at spiral/flake.nix and pins renders it there (workflows.spi path updated; pins recheck running); SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT's 4 migration scripts are in i574n.github/scripts/spiral-independent/ (dice init.ps1 comment updated). KEPT: COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB, HOPACSOTA-P2 and SPIRAL-TO-YAML, because apps/compiler/packs/tmp-retire's undo set (callers.txt, manifest.json) names their files; they go when COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE closes (the user's spiral commit). The same pass emptied C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts down to tonight's active session folder: 14 folders plus loose files archived; reusable tools moved into repos (apps/cells/spi: lib_roundtrip.exs, lib_land_window.ps1, lib_consumer_signatures.tsv, daemon_collision_proof.ps1 + .exs, lambda_parens_prove.ps1; i574n.github/scripts: node-temp-contain.ps1, node-archive-cleanup.ps1, box-upkeep.ps1, safe-delete-test.ps1, scratch-archive-guard-test.ps1; the manifest-model compiler pack draft in apps/compiler/packs/manifest-model).")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_spiral_43 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL", 43u32, "2026-10-31T12:10Z", "main", "10-31 08:10 main, DONE: spiral/lab no longer exists. The last 3 folders (COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB 7 files, HOPACSOTA-P2 13, SPIRAL-TO-YAML 2) archived as 20261031-080801 (verified, 0 failures); the gate on COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE was dropped because the user committed spiral at 10-30 22:33 and cleanup is never gated on a release. Two loose 4-byte markers from old process.spi test runs (10-24/10-25, the debris LIB-PROCESS-TEST-DEBRIS fixed) archived as 20261031-080937. Restore any of them with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root <dir> -Restore %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\<id>\\manifest.tsv -RestoreTo <dir>.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_gate_1 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-GATE", 1u32, "2026-10-22T01:39Z", "x", "2026-10-21 21:39 x: proposal only; main owns ~/.claude/hooks/autonomous-gate.ps1 and nobody else edits it. Session start = the deadline file's creation time or the session's first transcript entry.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_gate_2 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-GATE", 2u32, "2026-10-22T01:41Z", "main", "21:41 main proposal only (no hook change; a peer cannot grant hook edits and the rule reached me through x): at the deadline pass, the gate would run eoie lab audit --since <deadline file creation time> once (bounded by the 30 s Stop timeout, so the audit must be a fast directory-time scan; no sleep or poll), and block the final stop once with the listed files and the required lab: line, then pass on the next stop if the last message contains a lab: line. Depends on EOIE-LAB-AUDIT existing; until then the gate could print the count from a Get-ChildItem LastWriteTime scan of the touched repos' lab folders, which took 261 ms for 253 files on spiral/lab (107 folders, warm file cache; measured 21:42).")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_gate_3 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-GATE", 3u32, "2026-10-29T22:32Z", "main", "10-29 18:3x main: PROVISIONAL fields filled while the item is Blocked or Paused (fill-all-task-fields): assignee agent (default). The owner corrects them on resume.")
inl note_of_lab_sweep_gate_4 () = Note ("LAB-SWEEP-GATE", 4u32, "2026-10-31T21:03Z", "main", "10-31 17:02 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK): DONE by supersession. A gate on lab sweeps has nothing left to gate: spiral/lab no longer exists (last folders archived 10-31 08:08-08:09, LAB-SWEEP-SPIRAL Done) and the memory rule is now 'never create lab/' (lab-in-repo).")
inl note_of_kit_generic_drift_1 () = Note ("KIT-GENERIC-DRIFT", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:26Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-GENERIC-DRIFT (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_1 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:24Z", "eoie", "22:24 requested by tibia: lets a repo drop per-experiment analysis scripts (tibia freezes four of them until these land; not urgent, experiments concluded). Fixtures must stay neutral (EOIE-GENERIC-DRIFT): no game log lines in tests.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_2 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 2u32, "2026-10-22T03:37Z", "eoie", "23:37 typed design drafted (scratch log-sequences/design.md, moves to lab after release A): --time name=<regex with ms capture> stamps lines; sequence_measure is a GADT indexed by result kind (Interval -> gaps with fold AsRows|Modulo ms, Window -> count over ms_range after a marker, Order -> verdict Before|After|Missing, InSet -> count against a set file); an existential any_measure lets mixed measures share the logs pipeline, each rendering its column via a prototype. Plus table query --median. Neutral fixtures only; built on lib/spiral after EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK, so it waits behind the boundary work.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_3 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 3u32, "2026-10-22T09:46Z", "eoie", "05:46 second request from the same consumer (event latency for a pre-registered probe check): folded into this item instead of a new verb. Design addition in lab/EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES/design.md: a Latency kind in the sequence_measure GADT (pairs each --latency match with the next --to match in the same file, optional --when on the from line, gaps from the shared --time stamp capture), plus table query --quantile p and --share-at-least t beside the planned --median. Gate note: eoie_table_logs.rs is 817 lines, so the sequence kinds may need their own crate. Needs compiles; next session after ABC/D/E.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_4 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 4u32, "2026-10-22T11:49Z", "eoie-lane", "07:49 eoie team: the pure half is drafted (scratch log-sequences/eoie_log_sequences, goes to lab/EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES when no lease holds the tree). It follows lease.spi's shape: nominal result phantoms gaps/counts/verdicts, a GADT sequence_measure result (Interval, Window, Order, Latency over named term records), and measured forall result. dispatching into one measure_outcome {rows : list measure_row; unpaired}. Runtime lists are core list with listm.rev (xsd_regex's idiom), and parsing uses sm'.split, am'.try_item and convert.try_convert. 5 test blocks with hand-checked expectations on a neutral request/reply/flush fixture. A primitive boundary exports eoie_sequence_interval/window/order/latency: (file, events text 'role TAB line TAB at_ms' per line) to rows 'file TAB line TAB value' plus '#unpaired TAB n'. Decisions: every measure yields rows (table query aggregates median/quantile/share); --when and --in-set are matching-side filters and stay in the logs crate; Order's row line is the first A's line (0 when absent). Integration plan for logs.spi (next session, needs compiles): a PerSequence table_shape like --values; events come from the lib's sm'.new_regex + regex_captures (named group ms for --time) instead of a new host macro, which migrates logs.spi's touched regex code to the lib in the same change.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_5 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 5u32, "2026-10-22T12:34Z", "eoie-lane", "08:34 eoie team: integration decided by the style contract's legacy rule (touching a legacy function means migrating it). Adding five flags to logs.spi's options_from would force migrating its rust_std_string parser in the same change, so the sequence measures get their own verb in the clean crate instead: eoie table sequences <file|dir>... [--glob g] --time name=<regex with an ms capture> (--interval name=A [--between B] [--modulo ms] | --window name=A --from M --ms lo..hi | --order name=A,B | --latency name=A --to B [--when W]) [--out f.tsv]. One measure per call, rows file/line/value like --values, routed by one elif in eoie_cli_model (VerbTable). Order: first preview the pure crate (proves list/listm.rev/sm'.split/convert idioms on Rust), then the verb with lib regex (sm'.new_regex + regex_captures) and the file walk.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_6 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 6u32, "2026-10-22T13:48Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 09:48 Consumer request (tibia, TIBIA-EVENT-TIME note 75): --interval keyed by a capture. Logs interleave several actors, e.g. 'monster <id> moved to x,y @login+<ms>' for three monsters in one file, and the measure is the gap between consecutive matches of the SAME id. Proposal: --interval name=A --key <capture name or group 2> (or a key=regex beside --time), giving gaps per key in file order; the rows carry file, key and gap, so table query --group key --median gap works. With --key absent, behavior is unchanged.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_7 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 7u32, "2026-10-22T14:03Z", "eoie-lane", "10:03 eoie team: verb half drafted (lab/EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES/eoie_log_sequences/verb_draft.spi; first compile pending, not in any release yet). measure_request (IntervalOf | WindowOf | OrderOf | LatencyOf) maps two user patterns to roles first/second. events_in walks a file's lines once, stamping each from a named (?<ms>...) group of the --time regex (lib sm'.new_regex plus regex_captures over mapm.get 'ms') and adding a role event per matching pattern. A new erasure union sequence_measure_any (AnyGaps | AnyCounts | AnyVerdicts) lets a runtime choice still dispatch to the GADT-typed measured. Lib-type plumbing is confined to matches_in, stamp_in and compiled, the parts most likely to need adjusting at first compile. Still to do: the argument reader, the file walk (read_dir_names as in repo affected), --out, and the VerbTable dispatch line.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_8 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 8u32, "2026-10-22T14:04Z", "eoie-lane", "10:04 eoie team: the verb draft is complete (lab verb_draft.spi): an args reader (--time, --interval/--window/--order/--latency name=pattern, --between/--from/--to, --ms lo..hi, --modulo, --out; an unknown option is refused), measure_of returning option measure_request (a two-role measure without its second pattern is refused), a file walk (dirs listed one level through lib read_dir_names, files kept as given), a TSV with header 'file line <name>' to stdout or --out through file_system.write_all_text, and the export eoie_table_sequences_run. Remaining to land: verb.spi joins the crate's modules after its first compile, one VerbTable elif in eoie_cli_model, the command spec usage and a CLI notebook on a neutral request/reply log. Goes in the release after H, once H has compiled the model.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_9 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 9u32, "2026-10-22T17:50Z", "eoie", "2026-10-22 13:50 previews (compile window, scratch tree): the H crate eoie_log_sequences compiles at 861 lines and now also exports eoie_sequence_events (log text plus time/first/second regexes in, role/line/ms rows out), with regex = 1.13.1 in its Cargo.toml. The table sequences verb (apply-table-sequences.ps1, release I) is its own crate calling the five exports: 892 lines, after three shrink rounds from 1523 (the GADT model specialized inside the verb crate, an 11-field argument record fold, inl helpers expanded per call site). It waits on lib console.write_error_line (main's second landing). lab/EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES/verb_draft.spi is superseded by table-sequences/eoie_table_sequences/verb.spi and goes once I lands.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_10 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 10u32, "2026-10-22T21:20Z", "tibia-lane", "Request from tibia (2026-10-22, two uses today, both done with a scratch loop instead): carry the last capture of one pattern onto each later match of another, per file. Shape: table logs <files> --values at=<pattern A with a capture> --carry pos=<pattern B with captures> writes one row per A match plus the most recent B capture before it (empty when none). Uses: the knight position line (seen at x,y:) at each Loot of a troll line, to place late kills; the latest @t+ms stamp at each seen-at line (seen-at lines carry no stamp), to bin sightings by time. --order and --window do not cover it (they need a time on both lines).")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_11 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 11u32, "2026-10-22T23:11Z", "tibia-lane", "Use case from tibia-lane 2026-10-22 19:1x (TIBIA-EVENT-TIME note 100): in 20 arena logs, for each file, the first @login+<ms> time of each of three monster ids (monster <id> moved to x,y @login+<ms>), then per file the gap between id 82 and id 81 first moves and whether 82 moved at all. table logs --count-by already gives the per-id counts (20, 6, 18); the per-file first-occurrence times and the gap need --order with a time capture (or --values grouped per file with a first-only flag). Done by hand today; expected rows: file, first_81_ms, first_82_ms, first_83_ms.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_12 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 12u32, "2026-10-23T03:21Z", "tibia-lane", "Second use case from tibia-lane (2026-10-22 23:3x): one log file holding many runs, each starting at a header line (lead-trace run <stamp> knight seen ...), with per-entity lines (<kind> <id> ... @knight+<ms>) and some lines that belong to the next section but come before its header (monster <id> ... starts at x,y @knight+-1). Needed: --section-on <regex> (rows per section instead of per file), optionally carrying lines marked by a capture (here @knight+-1) into the next section, then the --order / first-occurrence time per captured id within each section. Done tonight with a scratch reader (server-probe/read-trace.ps1); expected rows: section, id, first_time, first_value.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_13 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 13u32, "2026-10-23T06:33Z", "tibia-lane", "TDD case for note 12 (tibia-lane, 02:4x), with exact input and expected rows, verified by hand and by the scratch reader. Input file, 16 lines: lead-trace monster 81 Hunter starts at 1931,1352 @knight+-1 / lead-trace monster 82 Hunter starts at 1931,1353 @knight+-1 / lead-trace run 20261023-030000 knight seen at 1932,1349 @knight+0 / lead-trace think 81 at 1931,1352 @knight+600 / lead-trace think 82 at 1931,1353 @knight+300 / lead-trace monster 81 moved to 1930,1352 @knight+850 / lead-trace think 82 at 1931,1353 @knight+1300 / lead-trace think 82 at 1931,1353 @knight+2300 / lead-trace knight targets 82 @knight+2500 / lead-trace knight gone @knight+90000 / lead-trace monster 71 Hunter starts at 1931,1352 @knight+-1 / lead-trace monster 72 Hunter starts at 1931,1353 @knight+-1 / lead-trace run 20261023-030300 knight seen at 1932,1349 @knight+0 / lead-trace monster 71 moved to 1932,1352 @knight+700 / lead-trace knight targets 72 @knight+2500 / lead-trace knight gone @knight+90000 (the slashes separate lines). Sections start at the run header; lines stamped @knight+-1 belong to the next section. Expected first-move rows (section, id, first_time, first_value): 20261023-030000, 81, 850, 1930,1352 and 20261023-030300, 71, 700, 1932,1352. Expected start rows (section, id, value): 20261023-030000 has 81 at 1931,1352 and 82 at 1931,1353; 20261023-030300 has 71 at 1931,1352 and 72 at 1931,1353. Expected all-times rows for think lines (section, id, times): 20261023-030000, 81, 600; 20261023-030000, 82, 300,1300,2300. Expected first knight action per section: 2500 and 2500. The join the tibia question needs on top of that, the boxed id (start value 1931,1353) and its thinks after the neighbour's (start 1931,1352) first move, can stay a table query step: section 20261023-030000 gives 1300 and 2300 after 850, and section 20261023-030300 has no thinks logged. Scratch copy: server-probe/synthetic-trace4.log in the tibia session scratch.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_14 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 14u32, "2026-10-23T18:33Z", "eoie", "14:33 eoie: note 13's TDD case is now spec/one-call.md case 45 and fixes the design. table sequences <file> --section-on '<regex with (?<section>)>' --carry '<regex>' --time '<regex with (?<ms>)>' --first <name>='<regex with (?<id>) and (?<value>)>'. A section starts at each --section-on line; --carry lines before a header belong to the section that header opens; --first keeps the earliest match per section and id; columns are section, id, <name>_time, <name>_value; the receipt is 'eoie table sequences ok files=1 sections=2 rows=2'. Expected rows are tibia's first-move rows (81 850 1930,1352; 71 700 1932,1352). Red until the verb exists (table sequences itself lands with release I). Start, all-times and first-action rows (also in note 13) become follow-up cases once --first works.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_15 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 15u32, "2026-10-23T18:34Z", "eoie", "14:34 eoie: tibia confirmed case 45's syntax and gave a second real case: PR-26's ground trace (arena-logs/20261023-111503-hunting-ground-ground-trace.log, 10 runs in one file, split by hand at each 'ground-trace run <stamp> knight seen' line, with the @knight+-1 lines before a marker belonging to the FOLLOWING section, i.e. --carry). Creature ids repeat across sections (canary restarts each run), so rows key by (section, id), as case 45 does. Follow-up cases: (1) --last <name>='<regex>' next to --first, giving <name>_time/<name>_value of the last match per (section, id), so life = death - appear becomes a table query step; (2) the time capture accepts negative values (case 45's -?\\d+ already does). Fixture for a real-data case: tibia's hand split (10 files, tibia scratch server-probe/pr26-runs), which reproduces TIBIA-PARTY note 72's P6 counts (R 30, F 0, I 5 over 12 late clusters).")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_16 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 16u32, "2026-10-23T18:37Z", "eoie", "14:37 eoie: pure model for cases 45 and 46 drafted (scratch f6/src/eoie_section_sequences, HostFree, LibReferenced; typecheck queued behind main's matrix). Two stages, so regex matching stays in the IO layer as in eoie_log_sequences: (1) sections_of: classified lines (Header section | Carried | Plain) placed into sections, with carried lines joining the section the NEXT header opens, plus a '-' section before the first header; (2) rows_from: observations (section, column name, id, value, at_ms as i64, so @knight+-1 stays -1) folded into one row per (section, id) in first-seen order, First keeping the earliest and Last the latest match per column, and a column with no match printing '-'. Tests reproduce case 45's and case 46's TSV rows exactly. The existing sequence_model (event at_ms : u64) is left as is: sections are a separate measure, so interval, window, order and latency keep their types.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_17 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 17u32, "2026-10-23T18:49Z", "eoie", "14:49 eoie: IO side drafted as scratch log-sections/apply-log-sections.ps1 (anchored, idempotent, dry run on the post-L copy clean, second run is a no-op). eoie_log_sequences gains section_model (the pure model) and section_scan: regex scanning with named groups section/id/value/ms, a signed i64 ms parse, and an exported sections_text + sections_header. eoie_table_sequences/verb.spi gains --section-on/--carry/--first/--last as known flags; any number of --first/--last columns are collected in argument order; there is a sections branch with the same refusals as the measures; the receipt adds sections=N; and the usage line is extended. A CLI test adds a 2-run fixture with a carried line and a --last overwrite, plus the --time refusal. section_scan's tests replay cases 45 and 46 on the exact trace text. The notebook typecheck is running (compile window) now that main's matrix has ended. The slice is queued for the release after N.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_18 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 18u32, "2026-10-23T19:08Z", "eoie", "15:08 eoie: both new modules pass their notebooks (f6, 15:02): section_model 3/3 and section_scan 2/2. section_scan's tests run the real regex scan on the case 45/46 trace text and reproduce both expected tables exactly, including -1 times and '-' cells. Two fixes on the way: (1) multi-line 'listm.fold fun ...' with the initial state on its own line mis-parses, so the folds now use named step functions; (2) a Rust codegen ownership error, E0507 'cannot move out of v4, a captured variable in an FnMut closure': the shared empty-string literal was used both as the vec_fold' seed and as default_value' inside the closure. The closure now passes its own accumulator as the default. Test blocks that touch sm'.regex need '///> rust -d regex'. Slice log-sections/apply-log-sections.ps1 carries the fixed blocks.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_19 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 19u32, "2026-10-23T19:19Z", "eoie", "15:19 eoie: release vehicle set by rule (a slice ships in the earliest release whose gates can run; N's byte-identity gate waits on main's 5f formatter, and neither slice touches the formatter): release O = log-sections + note-as-first, chained right after M; N now rebases on O. Scripts (scratch): derive-o.ps1 writes launch-O, release-build-job-O (4 candidate notebooks: section_model, section_scan, sequences_cli, notes_cli; baseline merge eoie_log_sequences), after-install-O (sections table on a 2-run trace with carry, -1 times, --first and --last, and '-'; agile note --as on both sides; one-call spec >= 41; backlog reads). chain-next's order is GE2, HIJK, L, M, O, N. Positive controls on today's install: after-install-O scores 1/4 (the 3 new checks fail, as they must), and N's O-gate refuses (the first draft keyed on 'unknown option --first', which never shows on an eoie without table sequences; it now requires the probe's 'sections=1 rows=0' receipt). Typechecks: note-as-first task_notes PASS on m-on-l (15:11).")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_20 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 20u32, "2026-10-23T19:36Z", "eoie", "15:36 eoie: release blocker found and fixed before HIJK launches. Today's installed compiler emits Rust E0507 ('cannot move out of v4, a captured variable in an FnMut closure') for H's own stamp_in: a vec_fold' whose closure reuses the seed literal in default_value'. A probe test calling events_text on the untouched L-dry2 copy failed with it at 15:29. The same crate compiled at 861 lines on 10-22 13:50 (note 9), so this is a codegen regression since then, and HIJK's regen cargo check would have failed. Fix (same semantics; in that branch found is the empty string): default_value' found. Probe passes at 15:31. Applied to H's source (scratch log-sequences/eoie_log_sequences/sequences.spi and verb_draft.spi), the L-dry2, m-on-l and log-sections dry trees, and the 4 eoie lab reference copies (no tree lease held). A minimal repro (2 tests, literal and dynamic group name) went to main.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_21 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 21u32, "2026-10-24T06:23Z", "eoie", "02:23 root cause of the E0507 in stamp_in (RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE), from main: lib, not the compiler. optionm'.default_value's Rust arm is x.unwrap_or(value); in a vec_fold' closure whose default is the fold's seed literal, the deduplicated literal is captured by the FnMut closure and unwrap_or moves it. Main's fix (unwrap_or_else(|| value.clone()) plus a regression test with this shape in sm'.spi) lands after tibia's PR-27 block. After it lands, remove the workaround (passing the accumulator as the default) in H's stamp_in, the dry trees and the lab copies, and recompile to confirm.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_22 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 22u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 195m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D5 at 60%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_23 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 23u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_24 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 24u32, "2026-10-30T18:48Z", "kit", "10-30 14:5x kit groom (R2/R3, the step #23 asked for): checked on the installed kit C528BE4D. SHIPPED: 'table sequences <file|dir>... --time <regex with (?<ms>)>' with --interval name=A [--between B] [--modulo ms], --window name=A --from M --ms lo..hi, --order name=A,B, --latency name=A --to B, and section/carry/first/last captures. NOT shipped (absent from usage): --in-set name=<file>, and --median in table query (its aggregates are mean, sd, sum, min, max, count). Back to Active (owner kit) at 85%. Remaining: --median (kit_table_query, 708 lines: a sorted value list per measure) first, then --in-set (kit_table_sequences is 948 lines, so that one needs a unit split before it lands). This item blocks TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE, now told the sequences verbs exist.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_25 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 25u32, "2026-10-30T18:49Z", "kit", "10-30 14:5x kit: --median sized and STOPPED by the crate gate, not by design. MedianOf plugs into kit_table_query's aggregate union (measures are built by folding its cases), but kit_table_measures (921 lines) inlines that stats module; a sorted value list per measure would take it to about 970. --in-set is the same story (kit_table_sequences 948). Both wait on the unit splits planned in EOIE-HAND-CRATE-UNITS n18 (stats -> kit_table_stats; sequence captures -> own unit), scheduled after TRAIN5.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_26 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 26u32, "2026-10-30T21:46Z", "kit", "10-30 17:4x kit: --median LANDED unstaged in kit/src (candidate 403B3B8D, built from the live-equal scratch; not installed). 'table query --median col' prints median_<col>: the middle value, or the mean of the two middle values; empty for no values. Values are prepended per row (O(1)) and sorted once at output by a tail-recursive merge sort in kit_table_query/stats.spi (values_split/values_merged/values_sorted). Proof: 12/12 differential cases byte-identical to the installed kit (6 on a fixture, 6 on tibia's damage-cyclops.tsv); fixture median b=4 a=20; tibia nexbot median 2392 matches an independent PowerShell sort (n=30). Timing on a 100k-row synthetic table: installed 0.76 s, candidate 1.32 s, identical output. The first draft was O(n^2) (180 s at 100k: an insertion sort into the value list on every row, plus a per-row copy of the kept mask); both were fixed before landing. Remaining here: the stats.spi test blocks run after the 18:00-18:25 timing slot; then --in-set (needs the kit_table_sequences split first).")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_27 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 27u32, "2026-10-30T22:07Z", "kit", "10-30 18:0x kit: stats.spi test blocks 4/4 PASS in cells (rust target; the 18:03 run failed 4/4 only because the notebook tree lacked the rust_std_string and union_reflection packages, which my after-slot.ps1 did not copy; fixed). --median is complete in the tree, unstaged, awaiting the user's review and the next release train. Next on this item: --in-set, after the kit_table_sequences split (948 lines).")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_28 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 28u32, "2026-10-30T22:10Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 18:1x tibia, answering kit's --count/--in-set proposal (TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE consumer: tibia analysis/pz-in-traces.ps1). Corrections: (a) monsters = every ' x,y' AFTER the knight's 'seen at x,y:' prefix, matched globally. Kit's regex has no lookbehind, so a scoped global match is needed (a --count-in named rest group); ':.*? (?<value>...)' would count only the first monster. (b) The knight form is right. (c) runs touching = sections with knight>0 OR monsters>0 (needs an OR or a derived touched column); samples = all 'seen at' lines. (d) Default the section to the file name when there are no '== ' markers (live logs). (e) --in-set can take a value regex on floor-changes.txt ('^(?<value>\\d+,\\d+) protection-zone$'). Beyond the shipped verbs (kit's call): pc3 needs dt mod 2000 classification by the kinds of the events between; pc4 needs a same-as-previous-row test on a derived direction, then a median; pr12-secondary needs an anchor-relative 10 s window, per-file flags and an mtime file filter.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_29 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 29u32, "2026-10-30T22:32Z", "kit", "10-30 18:3x kit: --in-set LANDED unstaged, in the shape tibia asked for at 18:2x (pz-in-traces.ps1 as the reference). 'table sequences <files> [--section-on <re (?<section>)>] --count name=<re [(?<value>)]>... [--count-in name=<re (?<rest>)>] [--in-set name=<file>[:<re (?<value>)>]]' prints section + one count column per --count: every match on a line counts (scoped to the named rest when --count-in is given, since the regex crate has no lookbehind), and with --in-set only values listed in the set file count. The set regex picks values from the file (floor-changes.txt lines 'x,y protection-zone' with '^(?<value>\\d+,\\d+) protection-zone$'); a drive letter in the path is fine. Lines before the first header, or a whole file with no header, form a section named by the file. Runs touching a zone, tibia's OR question: a third --count touched=' (?<value>\\d+,\\d+)' --count-in touched='seen(?<rest> at .*)$' with the same set counts knight+monster positions in the zone, so '--where touched>0 --count section' answers it with no OR in table query. Code: new crates kit_log_counts (pure scan, HostFree, 825 lines, 3/3 Cells tests) and kit_table_counts (the verb, 924); kit_table_sequences routes --count there (verb 948 -> 976, in the risk band: the planned split is still due); usage and help updated. Proof on candidate 631DBEF4 (built from a scratch copy equal to live): pz-in-traces.ps1's own Measure-Seen vs kit on generated traces in competitor.spi's 'seen at x,y: ...' format with the real 420 zone tiles: 8/8 SAME (model traces with headers, a headerless live log, 4 seeds, including runs where 3/6 and 4/6 touch). The 7 existing sequence modes are byte-identical to the installed kit apart from the usage text. Refusals: stray --in-set name, missing set file, missing path (all exit 2). A CLI test case is in sequences_cli.spi; its notebook run comes after main's 18:40 slot. pc3, pc4 and pr12-secondary need more than these verbs (tibia's 18:2x list); not started, tibia called them non-urgent.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_30 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 30u32, "2026-10-31T00:22Z", "kit", "10-30 20:2x kit: the --count CLI case in kit_table_sequences/sequences_cli.spi passes in Cells: 4/4 on candidate E6EAE98D, with the 3 existing sequence tests (job kit-counts-cli-rerun2). The first two runs failed on my own test text: a missing closing paren, then ++# unbound in a module without sm'_operators; both fixed.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_31 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 31u32, "2026-10-31T07:48Z", "kit", "10-31 03:3x kit groom: Active -> Blocked. The only remaining step is installed-is-done: TRAIN5e (job-1793421047190, snapshot 00:31) carries this change, and KIT-AGILE-LOST-UPDATES's last part ships in the train after it. Unblock and verify once kit.exe is installed. Moved so that 'agile next --team kit' stops ranking an install wait above actionable work (43 kit items were Active).")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_32 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 32u32, "2026-10-31T12:12Z", "kit", "10-31 07:4x kit: installed E14F1F0B passes 'table query --median' and 'table sequences --count/--in-set' (release-acceptance 23/23). The typed count_spec refactor (n33 of EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET) is in the live tree and ships in the next train. Blocked -> Active 95; close after that train's sequences rows pass.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_33 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 33u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_34 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 34u32, "2026-11-06T11:32Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_log_sequences_35 () = Note ("KIT-LOG-SEQUENCES", 35u32, "2026-11-08T03:12Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 23:1x kit, CLOSE WITH EVIDENCE on the installed kit (TRAIN33 binary): over a 4-line log with @t+<ms> stamps (ticks at 100, 150, 260), kit table sequences <dir> --time '@t\\+(?<ms>\\d+)' --interval gap=tick printed the two gaps 50 and 110 with a receipt (files=1 rows=2), and --count ticks=tick printed run1.log 3 (sections=1 rows=1); table query --median works too. The typed count_spec refactor the 10-31 note waited for has shipped in every train since. One observation for later, not a blocker: the two calls together took over a minute on the busy box, slow for a 4-line file.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:56Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:56 Census verified: apps/arena/package.spiproj has packageDir ../.. only and 169 host macros (\\$' / \\$\\\"), apps/predict 3, src 0. x's form is 'packageDir: ../../../spiral/lib' + 'spiral-'; check which path holds in tibia's CI (init.ps1 links deps/spiral) before copying it. Known lib bug: am'.any fails on the Rust backend (SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY); arena is Lua and predict is C, so check per backend. Done when both packages reference the lib, every remaining host macro is one the lib can't express (each recorded as a lib gap or kept as a client binding in client.spi), the arena Lua probes regenerate with a live smoke clear, predict reproduces the registered predictions (scripts/predict.ps1), and build.ps1 is green. Part of the Tibia retirement scope, and src/ gets a pass for listm'/optionm'/math over local helpers.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 2u32, "2026-10-22T03:21Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:21 Census (130 macros matched; eoie's 172 also counts escaped forms). About 65 are lib-replaceable: 40 string.format/printf (-> sm' format with ++#? and ++#; format is lib-tested on lua and c), 5 string compares (Spiral = and <>), 3 lengths, 5 tostring/tonumber, 2 os.getenv (lib env), 2 os.time, 1 math.random, the lua.spi concat and find, and 9 Lua table helpers (am'/listm' or keep). About 65 are OTClient bindings (g_game, g_map, creature methods such as (!c):getPosition()), which stay in client.spi. Structure finding: spiproj package names are plain identifiers resolved under one packageDir, or under the compiler dir with '|' (spiral_compiler.fs 178106 and 178324), so no package can reach both the lib and tibia's own src- package. Siblings avoid this by having one package per repo with the lib as packageDir and their code as modules. Test queued (job tibia-libtest, scratch copy in target/libtest): src/package.spiproj gets packageDir lib plus spiral-, arena keeps packageDir ../.. plus src-; does the include re-export spiral- to arena? If yes, migrate in place. If no, the standard form is one root package.spiproj (packageDir deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib, modules src/ + apps/arena/ + apps/predict/) replacing the three per-folder packages. Output-equality risk: probe log lines are parsed by analyses and eoie regexes, so every format change must keep the text byte-identical (check with lua54 on the compiled probes, then the live smoke clear). Mapping table: session scratchpad lib-census.tsv.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 3u32, "2026-10-22T03:31Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:31 Structure tests (scratch copies in target/, two compile jobs). (1) target/libtest: src/package.spiproj with packageDir lib plus spiral- compiles (competitor.lua builds through it), but arena, which includes src-, does not see the lib's modules: 'open sm'' resolves to something without format or ++#, probably the old core's sm'. Includes are not transitive. (2) target/rootpkg: one root package.spiproj (packageDir lib; modules src/ ..., apps/arena/ ..., apps/predict/ predict). Arena modules resolve each other, but src modules are unbound from apps/arena and apps/predict (policy, hunting_pack, mirroring_the_current_arena). Module names resolve in a module's own folder and its ancestors; dice's app.spi sees state only because state sits in an ancestor folder. So no spiproj today gives arena both tibia's src and the lib. Decision (rule: a missing capability goes to the tool first, as with eoie verbs): (A) a compiler change, either an included package that re-exports its own packages, or a package entry with a relative path; requested from main. Rejected for now: (B) nesting apps/arena and apps/predict under src/ (moving tracked folders and rewriting build paths for one repo). Status: Blocked on that compiler capability. Unblocked parts: the census, the mapping table, and the output-equality check plan.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 4u32, "2026-10-22T03:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:34 main took the compiler route (b) into tonight's pack (PACK-AFTER-NIGHT): a package entry starting with '.' is a path relative to the spiproj's folder, e.g. apps/arena/package.spiproj gets packageDir ../../deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib with packages |core-, spiral-, ../../src-. A non-include entry is named after its last segment; regression sample package_relative_path. Install in the morning, after eoie's release. Resume condition: main's message that the installed compiler has it. Then arena and predict move to that spiproj form; the ~65 lib-replaceable macros go per the census (note 2), keeping log text byte-identical (lua54 on the compiled probes, then a live smoke clear); predict must reproduce the registered predictions exactly. No restructure.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 5u32, "2026-10-22T05:09Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 01:09 eoie suggested a root AGENTS.md carrying the lib-first rule (eoie has one since tonight). Decision: not for tibia now. No sibling has one (x, dice and spiral checked), tibia is retiring its prose docs (DOCS-TO-CODE), and tibia already enforces such rules in code (build.ps1 refuses host calls in lua.spi; eoie standards checks src/standards.spi). When this migration lands, the rule goes into that check instead: every package.spiproj lists spiral-, and host macros only in client.spi. Revisit if the user standardizes AGENTS.md across repos.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 6u32, "2026-10-22T12:18Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:18 Prepared for the resume (no edit yet; main: install after main-pack-review3, then 'installed, go'). Target spiproj for apps/arena (and apps/predict likewise): 'edition: 3', 'packageDir: ../../deps/spiral/lib' (tibia's deps/spiral is the symlink init.ps1 creates to the spiral checkout; deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib also exists but goes through polyglot), packages '|core-', 'spiral-', '../../src-' (main's relative entry), modules unchanged. x's apps/client uses the same shape with '../../../spiral/lib'. Timing rule: apps/arena edits happen only outside live blocks (run-arena rebuilds the probes when apps/arena or src is newer than target/arena, so an edit during a block would compile mid-block); during today's PR-22/PR-23 block the macro replacements are drafted in a scratch copy under target/libmig and landed after 'block end'.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 7u32, "2026-10-22T12:20Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:20 Draft started in the scratch copy target/libmig (apps/arena, apps/predict, src copied; the tree is untouched). 61 string.format sites in apps/arena: scratch libmig-format.ps1 rewrote the 39 single-line ones that use only %s/%d into lib concatenation (text ++# s ++#? n, with @@ unescaped to @), left 1 (%+3d in scan.spi), and the rest are multi-line macros or sit inside OTClient binding blobs in client.spi (review by hand). Kept as host macros, recorded as lib gaps when the census is final: float and width formats (adjacency %.3f, measure %.1f%% and %.2f, scan %+3d). To check at the first compile after main's install: whether the modules need an open for the lib operators (x's prelude), and that ++#? prints an i32 exactly like %d on Lua (byte-identical log text, verified with lua54 on the compiled probes, then a live smoke clear).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 8u32, "2026-10-22T12:22Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:22 Draft progress in target/libmig (no compile yet): besides the 50 format sites, the 5 empty-string compares are Spiral (text = empty, x <> empty), and lua.spi's joined, shown and contains are now defined through the lib (a ++# b; empty ++#? n; sm'.contains needle text, whose Lua branch emits the same string.find(s, value, 1, true) ~= nil). Call sites unchanged. Left for after the install, each needing a compile to verify: summon.spi's order split (sm'.split plus am'), random_between (lib random), matched_number (tonumber of string.match; likely a lib gap), and the Lua table helpers in lua.spi (cells, sample tables: am'/listm' or kept as the probe's runtime boundary).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 9u32, "2026-10-22T12:24Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:24 Draft: apps/predict's 3 host macros (tab-separated integer printf rows) become console.write_line of a ++#? / tab-joined line in target/libmig (the lib's C writer prints strings with printf of %s plus newline); apps/predict also needs the edition-3 spiproj with spiral-. src has no local helpers that duplicate lib names (abs, min, max, clamp, list folds checked), so its pass has nothing to change. Verification after main's install: predict must reproduce the registered predictions exactly (scripts/predict.ps1), which also checks that ++#? prints an i32 like %d on C.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 10u32, "2026-10-22T12:25Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:25 Draft complete up to the compile (target/libmig): apps/arena and apps/predict package.spiproj in the target form (edition 3, packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib, |core- spiral- ../../src-), and every module using ++# opens sm'_operators like x's cli.spi (12 files). Risk to check first at compile: edition 3 may change how the existing arena code parses (x's edition-3 lessons are about naming, not syntax, but unverified for tibia). Landing order after main's install and outside live blocks: compile the draft copy, lua54 byte check of every probe's log lines against the current probes on the same input, predict reproduces the registered predictions, then copy into the tree, build.ps1, a live smoke clear, and the standards check gains the rule (every package.spiproj lists spiral-, host macros only in client.spi and lua.spi's runtime boundary).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 11u32, "2026-10-22T12:27Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:27 Edition-3 risk checked (scratch edition3-check.ps1, compile job tibia-edition3-check2): the current apps/arena with only 'edition: 3' added compiles all nine probe entries (competitor, measure, nexbot, path, random, scan, steps, summon, vbot) to Lua byte-identical to target/arena, failed 0 of 9. So the edition switch is a no-op for tibia's arena code; the migration's remaining risks are the lib resolution (needs main's install) and ++#? matching %d.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 12u32, "2026-10-22T12:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:28 ++#? vs %d checked (scratch showint-check.ps1, a standalone package on deps/spiral/lib with spiral-, compile job tibia-showint-check): 0, 123, -45 and 2147483647 joined with tabs and an @t+ suffix print byte-identically to %d on both Lua (lua) and C (gcc). So the converted format sites and predict's rows keep their text. With the edition-3 check (note 11), the only open risk left is the package resolution itself, which needs main's install.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 13u32, "2026-10-22T12:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:30 Census final for the draft (target/libmig): apps/arena host macros 173 -> 112 and apps/predict 3 -> 0 (steps.spi's three expression-argument format sites done by hand). What stays: 85 OTClient bindings in client.spi (g_game, g_map, creature methods; the probe's boundary), 20 Lua runtime helpers in lua.spi (cells, sample tables, stamps, counts; candidates for am'/listm' only if the Lua output stays equivalent, decided at the compile), and 7 others: adjacency %.3f, measure %.1f%% / %.2f and its Lua summary function, scan %+3d (lib has pad_left, but no sign flag), summon's order split and table reads (sm'.split has no Lua branch, so it stays). Lib gaps to file when landing: fixed-decimal float formatting (no %.Nf equivalent in sm'), signed width formatting, sm'.split on Lua.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 14u32, "2026-10-22T13:31Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 09:31 Unblocked: main installed PACK-AFTER-NIGHT (single-flight core 6446A71F2DCD9FA3, equals the reviewed candidate, DISAGREE 0; compiler-install window released 09:30). First step is compile job tibia-libmig-compile on the draft (nine probes to Lua with a lua parse check, plus predict to C). After that, the PR-22 + PR-23 live block. The draft lands after block end.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 15u32, "2026-10-22T13:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 09:34 Draft compiles on the installed compiler (job tibia-libmig-compile, 90 s): all nine probes compiled to Lua and parse (lua loadfile), predict compiled to C; failed 0 of 10. So main's relative entry ../../src- resolves alongside spiral-. The probes grew 5 to 29 percent (competitor 152963 to 174413 bytes) because ++# lowers to one .. per piece through a Mut cell, against one string.format before. The client runs LuaJIT (otclient-src CMake: find_package LuaJIT), and local lua is 5.1.5 with the same number model, so the earlier ++#? check ran under the client's semantics. Edge cases, measured on lua 5.1: tostring and %d agree on 0, -0, 7, -45 and 2147483647. They differ only for non-integral values (2.5 gives 2.5 vs 2) and values above 2^31 (%d wraps to -2147483648, tostring prints the value). Integer division compiles to math.modf truncation and negation goes through spiral_wrap_signed, which normalizes -0, so i32 values stay integral. Landing order is unchanged and starts after block end.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 16u32, "2026-10-22T13:42Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 09:42 Lib gaps filed: LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C (sm'.split has no Lua or C arm; summon's order split stays a host macro until it lands) and LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL (no %.Nf equivalent; adjacency and measure keep three host macros). Signed width is not a lib gap: scan's %+3d is now sm'.pad_left 3 with an explicit + for dy >= 0 in the draft, so the draft has 6 others left, not 7; compile-check after block end. Landing prep, also in the draft: scripts/predict.ps1 rewrites apps/predict's packageDir to the absolute lib path in its target/predict work copy (../../deps does not exist from there), and apps/arena/standards.spi declares the four default rules plus LibReferenced (the installed eoie still drops LibReferenced silently; eoie will flag unknown rules next release). Verify script is scratch libmig-verify.ps1: predict before/after byte compare, format of the draft, eoie standards, then the 10 compiles.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 17u32, "2026-10-22T15:31Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 11:31 Landing rule, written before the verify run: the draft lands in the tree only when all of these hold in one libmig-verify job: predict output byte-identical before and after (same row count), format --check exit 0 on the draft src, apps/arena and apps/predict after the format write, eoie standards on both apps with no finding, and libmig-compile with 0 of 10 failed. Then build.ps1 on the tree must pass; if it fails, the 17 files are restored from the pre-landing copy in target/libmig-restore and the failure is recorded. A live smoke clear waits for the next live window; until it passes, progress stays below 90.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_18 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 18u32, "2026-10-22T15:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 11:41 Verify job tibia-libmig-verify (11:34-11:40) passed every note-17 criterion: predict 400 rows byte-identical before and after; format write then --check exit 0 on draft src, apps/arena, apps/predict; eoie standards 0 findings on both apps (installed eoie still drops LibReferenced silently); libmig-compile 0 of 10 failed, every probe parses. LANDED the 17 files into the tree at 11:41 (originals in target/libmig-restore, no Cells reader active). Job tibia-libmig-after-land runs scripts/build.ps1 and re-runs predict on the landed (formatted) tree against the before output; a failure restores the originals.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_19 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 19u32, "2026-10-22T16:15Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:15 Smoke criterion, written before the smoke run (refines note 17, which said smoke clear): one PR-22-style hunter run on the landed probes passes when the probe works end to end, not when it clears (a single run clears about 72.5 percent of the time at the PR-22 rate): the row is written with an experience value, the log has the in-game line, at least one strike and the PC-7 chase lines, an end or cleared summary, and no Lua error line. Pass moves the item to 90; Done after one ordinary block on the landed probes shows no row or parse anomaly.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_20 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 20u32, "2026-10-22T16:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:34 Landing side effect found before it bit: the old apps/predict spiproj (packageDir ../..) stayed valid in any work copy with src beside it; the landed one (packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib) does not, since a copy under target/predict has no deps there. In the repo only scripts/predict.ps1 copies apps/predict, and it carries the rewrite to an absolute lib path (landed). The four scratch score scripts D37 runs (score-d20 predict, hunter-trace-d20, hunter-killspot, d21-retreat) got the same one-line rewrite; duo-predict has it. The relative ../../src- entry resolves from the spiproj (verify copied predict to a work dir and matched 400 rows), so work copies still compile their own patched src.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_21 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 21u32, "2026-10-22T16:39Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:39 Build check FAILED and the rule applied: job tibia-libmig-after-land (11:41-12:36) ran scripts/build.ps1 on the landed tree: 26 of 27 test files green (every src file), but apps/arena/lua.spi failed all 5 of its lua tests; predict on the landed tree stayed byte-identical (400 rows). By note 17 the 17 files were RESTORED from target/libmig-restore (12:38); the user index is untouched (it holds pre-PC-7 versions staged earlier; the worktree is back to the pre-landing state). Diagnosis (from the diff, not yet run): the landing put open sm'_operators ABOVE the ///- --package line in lua.spi, and Cells reads flags per cell, so the file's first cell lost its package; all 5 tests failing, including ones that touch none of the 3 changed functions, points to a file-level cause; no passing src or lib file puts an open above its package line. Re-land rule, written before any rerun: fix the draft (open after the static prelude), then the re-land passes when scripts/build.ps1 -Files lua is green (arena build, format check, Cells on lua.spi) and predict stays identical; src needs no rerun (unchanged since its 12:34 green). If lua.spi still fails, no re-land today and the cause goes here first.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_22 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 22u32, "2026-10-22T16:51Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:51 Re-land (job tibia-libmig-reland, 12:41-12:50) FAILED again and restored itself by rule note 21; predict stayed identical. The note-21 diagnosis was WRONG: Cells names the real cause. It mounts the arena package as cells-mounts/<hash>/arena, where the migrated spiproj's relative packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib resolves to cells-mounts/deps/spiral/lib (Package ... is not loaded, likewise src). The old spiproj had packageDir ../.. with plain src-, which the mount carries. The open-line move stays (harmless, and the pattern every passing file follows), but it was not the cause. Next: find how sibling packages that reference lib pass under Cells mounts, then a single-file Cells run on the draft before any third landing.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_23 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 23u32, "2026-10-22T16:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:54 Cause traced in Cells source (spiral apps/cells mounts.ex): declared_dependencies expands every packages entry relative to packageDir (package_base), while the compiler resolves a relative entry like ../../src- from the spiproj dir. With packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib the src entry lands at tibia/deps/src (missing, silently skipped). The mounted copy keeps the relative packageDir, which points into cells-mounts/deps (deps is in the mount skip set). x/apps/client passes only because it has no local sibling package. Reported to main (Cells owner) with a request for a supported spiproj form for lib plus a local sibling. PAUSED. Resume when Cells mounts such a package (main's fix or a documented form), then: draft spiproj in that form, a single-file Cells run on the draft lua.spi, then the note-21 re-land check. The draft stays in target/libmig (open-line move and the TIBIA_ARENA_CHARACTER patch included). The character patch also went onto the restored tree for today's duo, using lua.joined.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_24 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 24u32, "2026-10-22T16:56Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:56 main confirmed the Cells bug; no supported form exists today. The fix is staged as Cells-pack step 11: path-like entries (./ or ../) resolve from the spiproj dir as the compiler does, and a shadowed mount rewrites them to sibling names; its test mirrors the arena layout. It lands with the next Cells pack after the full suite, not today. main withdrew the junction workaround (it would put a link inside spirals real lib folder). Resume condition, exact: the Cells pack containing step 11 is installed; then the note-21 re-land check runs unchanged on the draft.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_25 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 25u32, "2026-10-22T17:26Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:26 Draft gains summon.spi on lib (needs LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C landed): orders_of, order_count and order_at drop their three Lua macros for sm'.split then am'.choose dropping empty parts, am'.length and am'.index (1-based callers shift by one). Checked in lua 5.1 against the old string.gmatch [^;]+ on 5 inputs (default order, two orders, ;a;;b;, empty, ;;;): identical parts and counts. Lands with the rest of the migration after the Cells pack.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_26 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 26u32, "2026-10-22T17:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:28 Draft gains adjacency.spi's last host macro on lib (needs LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL landed): the %.3f report line is ++#? concatenation plus sm'.format_fixed 3 over an f64 rate computed as Lua did (hits * 1000 / max(1, adjacent_ms), double division in Lua 5.1), and format_fixed's Lua arm is string.format itself, so the text stays byte-identical by construction. measure.spi's two report lines (percent .1f and .2f with Lua-expression arguments) are next once format_fixed lands.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_27 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 27u32, "2026-10-22T17:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:30 Draft gains measure.spi's two report lines on lib (format_fixed now landed): the swings line (percent .1f landed share, .2f mean landed) and the taken line (.2f mean) are ++#/++#? concatenation plus sm'.format_fixed over f64 values computed the way the old Lua expressions did (100 * landed / swings, total / landed, taken_total / count, 0 when the divisor is 0). Byte-compared in lua 5.1 against the old string.format calls on 7 inputs, including zero swings and zero counts: identical. With summon (note 25) and adjacency (note 26), the arena draft no longer needs a host macro for formatting or splitting; it lands after the Cells pack (note 24).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_28 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 28u32, "2026-10-23T11:11Z", "tibia-lane", "Retirement grooming 07:1x: in scope, about 2 h left once main installs the Cells pack with step 11 (external dependency, not scheduled by tibia): the note-21 re-land check (one job), then a live smoke clear inside any tibia block.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_29 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 29u32, "2026-10-23T11:47Z", "tibia-lane", "Draft drift found and fixed (07:3x-07:5x), before the pack lands. The 10-22 draft in target/libmig predated the tree's later changes: the @t and near logging in competitor.spi (10-22 20:3x and today's formatter wrap), the knight-from-env patch in probe, observer, path and random, and today's src/live.spi tests. Landing it as it was would have silently reverted them. Fix: a 3-way merge per migrated file (base target/libmig-restore, the pre-landing originals of 10-22; ours the tree; theirs the draft) gave 13 of 16 files clean. The 3 conflicts (two lines in competitor.spi, bot_profile in probe.spi) were resolved by keeping both intents, the lib concatenation plus the new @t and near fields; ++#? prints an i32 exactly like %d (note 12), so the log text stays byte-identical. The formatter was run on the result. Compile check (job tibia-libmig-rebase-compile on a scratch copy of the tree with the rebased files overlaid): 9 probes to Lua, all parse, and predict to C; failed 0 of 10. target/libmig now holds the rebased files (the 10-22 versions are backed up in session scratch libmig-draft-1022-backup). The note-21 re-land check is unchanged and still waits for the Cells pack with step 11, which main queued after tibia's PR-26 block. Its byte comparison now needs fresh references from today's tree probes.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_30 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 30u32, "2026-10-23T11:56Z", "tibia-lane", "Rebased draft verified 08:0x by the note-17 criteria that run before the pack (job tibia-libmig-rebase-verify, on a scratch copy of today's tree with the rebased files overlaid): predict 400 rows byte-identical before and after; the format write, then --check exit 0; eoie standards 0 findings on apps/arena and apps/predict; libmig-compile failed 0 of 10. The first standards pass found 996 and 111 lf-only findings, CRLF from my merge step ([IO.File]::WriteAllLines); the 30 files in the check copy and in target/libmig were normalized to LF, after which both apps have 0 findings. Only the Cells part of the re-land check (scripts/build.ps1 -Files lua on the landed tree) and the live smoke clear remain, both after the Cells pack with step 11.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_31 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 31u32, "2026-10-24T03:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-23 23:07 grooming: main installed a formatter-only compiler pack (B553EF6B78E6EE29) at 22:27. The rebased draft in target/libmig was compiled and verified at 08:0x, before it, so the note-21 re-land check starts with a fresh compile of the draft on the new compiler. Still gated by the Cells sibling mount (checked 23:0x: no :siblings in spiral apps/cells).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_32 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 32u32, "2026-10-24T05:05Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 01:05 tibia: the P10 change was carried into the rebased draft by a 3-way merge (base: the pre-P10 tree in scratch p10-backup; ours: the tree with P10 landed; theirs: target/libmig). probe.spi merged clean with LF endings. The draft's client.spi equals the base and is not in the landed file set, so its copy is just the tree version. A re-merge after the land gave byte-identical results, and the draft's previous versions are in scratch libmig-pre-p10-backup. The fresh compile and verify on main's 22:27 compiler is queued: job tibia-libmig-overlay-verify, on a %TEMP% overlay of today's tree plus the 17 draft files. It reports tree files newer than the draft, compares predict before and after, runs the formatter write and check plus eoie standards, and runs libmig-compile. At land time, libmig-land.ps1's 10-22 08:18 drift cut must be replaced by the 3-way merge check, since probe.spi now postdates it.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_33 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 33u32, "2026-10-24T05:26Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 01:26 tibia: the rebased draft is verified on main's 22:27 compiler (job tibia-libmig-overlay-verify, exit 0, on a %TEMP% overlay of today's tree plus the 17 draft files). No tree file is newer than the draft. Predict gives 400 rows, byte-identical before and after. The formatter write and check exit 0. eoie standards finds 0 problems on apps/arena (15 files) and apps/predict. libmig-compile failed 0 of 10 (9 probes to Lua, all parse, plus predict to C). This closes the grooming note's fresh-compile step. What remains is unchanged: the Cells part of the re-land check, which waits for the Cells sibling mount, then a live smoke clear.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_34 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 34u32, "2026-10-24T05:48Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 01:48 tibia: the land path is ready for the Cells pack. Main's cells-pack.ps1 step 11 is the relative package entries fix (lib plus a local ../ sibling under Cells), and main lands the pack right after PR-27's live block (CELLS-PACK-NEXT note of 01:13). So this item and TIBIA-CELLS-APPS unblock tonight. Scratch libmig-land.ps1 got two fixes before it runs. (1) The drift guard was a 10-22 08:18 date cut that P10 now trips. It is now a manifest: scratch libmig-merged-tree.tsv holds the sha256 of the 17 tree files as they were for the last 3-way merge, and land refuses if any differs (re-merge first). -Check runs only the guard: the positive control passes 17 of 17, and a negative control with probe.spi altered refuses naming probe.spi. (2) Land used to back up the tree into target/libmig-restore, which is also the 10-22 merge base, so landing would have overwritten the base. Backups now go to scratch libmig-land-backup, and land refuses if a backup already exists. After the pack lands, scratch libmig-reland.ps1 runs the land, then scripts/build.ps1 -Files lua, then predict byte-identical, and restores on any failure.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_35 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 35u32, "2026-10-24T07:27Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 03:27 tibia: after the user's 03:2x feedback (stop adding hand-written Lua; memory no-hand-crafted-lua), here is what the rebased draft still carries. $'...' macros per file: client.spi 86 (the OTClient bindings), lua.spi 20, competitor.spi 1, and every other arena file 0. lua.* helper calls outside lua.spi, about 190: native_table 39 (the cell type), joined 38, cell_value 35, new_cell 20, set_cell 19, new_table 7, contains 6, amount_at 4, add_to 1, the sample family (sample, sample_count, sample_total, sample_median, sample_max, samples_joined, labeled_sample, labeled_summary) 13, matched_number 2, random_between 2, and the stamp family 3. Next slice, rule written before drafting: (S1) joined becomes ++# and contains becomes sm'.contains (44 sites, mechanical). (S2) The cell family becomes a Spiral record with a mut field (value : mut i32, read .value, write with <-), which already compiles to Lua: lib step_runtime's spawn test runs on lua with mut record fields. So no new Lua arm is needed, and lua.native_table and new_table leave the arena. (S3) The sample, stamp, matched_number and random families each go to a lib home with a Lua arm only where lib has none (statistics over an array, sm' matching, lib random), checked one family at a time. Acceptance per family: predict 400 rows byte-identical, the 9 probes compile and parse, the formatter check, and the arena's Cells tests from TIBIA-CELLS-APPS green on lua and c. The slice starts after tonight's libmig re-land and the CELLS-APPS land, so a red never mixes with theirs. client.spi's game-API bindings stay as the declared boundary.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_36 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 36u32, "2026-10-24T07:35Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 03:35 tibia: S3 design per family, written before drafting (S1 and S2 are proven on temp copies; scratch lua-retire-s1.ps1 and lua-retire-s2.ps1, 33 and 74 sites). Samples (sample, count, total, max, median, joined): a mut (list i32) holding newest first, with core listm folds for count, total and max, sm'.concat_list over the reversed list for joined, and the median from am'.sort over the array form. If am'.sort_by's backend_switch has no Lua key, the fix is a lib Lua key pointing at its existing pure insertion sort (no hand Lua). Labeled samples: a mut list of (label, value) pairs; the summary groups by label after sorting (strings order through core comp) and formats with ++#. Stamps: a mut list of (id, at, kind), newest first, read with listm'.try_find_ (the runtime form). Counts (add_to, amount_at, keys 0-3): a fixed array i32 of 4 with set and index. matched_number (2 sites in measure): a pure Spiral parse of the digits between the two phrases using sm' functions, which already run on Lua (lua.spi's own contains delegates to sm'.contains and its lua test passes). random_between (2 sites in random): tibia's src/rng.spi, the pure Spiral generator the simulator uses, seeded from client.now_ms, which replaces math.random and math.randomseed. When S3 is done, lua.spi holds only the native_table type for client.spi's OTClient slots, and the module can be folded into client.spi.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_37 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 37u32, "2026-10-24T08:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:07 tibia: re-land attempt after PR-27 (job tibia-after-block): REFUSED by its own acceptance and restored. libmig-land landed the 17 files, then scripts/build.ps1 -Files lua failed all 5 lua.spi tests: 'Package .../cells-mounts/deps/spiral/lib/spiral is not loaded'. Predict was byte-identical. The runner restored all 17 files and the tree is as before. CELLS-APPS was not attempted. Cause: main's Cells pack (step 11) now mounts the siblings (Cells mount 17b254bb61370c49 has arena, spiral and src junctions), but it mounts arena itself as a plain junction, so its spiproj's packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib resolves from the mount to cells-mounts/deps/spiral/lib. A package like this needs the shadow kind with packageDir rewritten to .. and ../../src- to src-. Reported to main with the evidence; it's main's call or mine to patch mounts.ex. Still blocked on that Cells fix; everything else for the land is green (verify note 33, manifest guard, predict identical).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_38 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 38u32, "2026-10-24T08:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:17 tibia: the Cells blocker is being fixed by tibia (main agreed 04:1x). Cause: mounts.ex share_mounted_deps? compared the raw spiproj entries (spiral, ../../src) with the mounted sibling names (spiral, src), so a package with a ../ sibling was never shadowed and kept its relative packageDir. The fix maps the entries through mount_name (one anchored edit), plus a test in mounts_relative_test.exs (shared with arena, spiral and src mounted; not shared without src). Scratch cells-mounts-fix.ps1 (eoie do slice and undo; the dry run against the live apps/cells previews 2 replacements and writes nothing) and cells-mounts-gate.ps1 (a cells-root copy, then run_suite on the 3 mounts test files), as heavy job tibia-cells-mounts-gate. The land goes outside main-cells-suite-tree's run, then after-block reruns.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_39 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 39u32, "2026-10-24T08:48Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:48 tibia: LANDED in lib (S3 groundwork, note 36). am'.sort_by's Lua key now points at its existing pure Spiral insertion sort instead of the F# Array.sortBy macro, which couldn't run on Lua, and sort_orders_on_every_backend gains lua. Gate GREEN at 03:52 (12 of 12, 0 type errors, 42 rows 0 red); kit land at 04:47 after waiting out one Cells run, sha256 50dc2df3 to f624a66c, byte-identical to the gated candidate. Undo program: scratch am-sort-lua-land\\undo.do (expect files 1 replacements 2). It's pure Spiral, one backend key, within GRANULAR-HEAL.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_40 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 40u32, "2026-10-24T09:31Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:31 tibia: the Cells mounts fix gate is GREEN (job tibia-cells-mounts-gate-2, compile class): run_suite on a cells-root copy with the fix applied, 10 of 10 across mounts_relative, mounts_patched and mounts_name, including the new 'a package with a relative sibling shares the mounted deps, so it is shadowed' (spiral-bin\\cells-suite\\mounts-fix.log). The land job tibia-cells-mounts-land is running and waiting for two clear samples (other lanes' Cells erl runs were in flight at 05:30); tibia-after-block-2 (libmig re-land, then CELLS-APPS) follows it after-ok. The land result is recorded when it applies.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_41 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 41u32, "2026-10-24T09:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:54 tibia: the Cells mounts fix LANDED at 05:51 (job tibia-cells-mounts-land, after-ok on the green gate). It waited about 30 min for clear samples (eoie's sm'.spi lib gate, then another lane's run, then my own file_exists gate), then applied: mounts.ex sha256 544b12b3 to 1e6ed95f, byte-identical to the gated copy, and the new mounts_relative test. Undo: scratch cells-mounts-fix\\undo.do. main was told. tibia-after-block-2 (libmig re-land, then CELLS-APPS) started right after, as chained.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_42 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 42u32, "2026-10-24T10:11Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 06:11 tibia: LANDED. apps/arena and apps/predict are on Spiral's lib (job tibia-after-block-2, libmig-reland PASSED at 06:10). The 17 draft files replaced the tree's after the manifest guard confirmed they matched the last 3-way merge (17 of 17). scripts/build.ps1 -Files lua is green: lua.spi 5 of 5 under Cells on the migrated package, which mounts correctly now that the Cells mounts fix is in (the same check failed at 04:0x with 'package not loaded'). scripts/predict.ps1 gives 400 rows, byte-identical to scratch predict-before.tsv. Originals: scratch libmig-land-backup (libmig-land.ps1 -Restore puts them back). Left on this item: the live smoke clear (the next live window) and the Lua-retire slices S1-S3 (S1 and S2 proven; the chain runs after the file_system Lua arm lands; S3 is designed). Restage for the user: the 17 libmig files (apps/arena: adjacency, competitor, lua, measure, observer, package.spiproj, path, probe, random, scoreboard, steps, summon, scan, standards; apps/predict: package.spiproj, predict.spi; scripts/predict.ps1).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_43 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 43u32, "2026-10-24T10:20Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 06:20 tibia: S3, first family drafted: scratch lua-retire-s3a.ps1 makes adjacency's per-count table (lua.new_table, add_to, amount_at) a Spiral array i32 of 4. It starts as ;[ 0i32; 0i32; 0i32; 0i32 ], adds through set and index on the slot capped at 3 in Spiral, and reads with index. Proven by replaying the chain on a temp copy of the CELLS-APPS candidates (gate-via-lib, then S1, then S2, then S3a): every anchor matches once, no line is over 120, and adjacency.spi has 0 lua.* calls left. It applies after lua-retire-chain.ps1, with an adjacency Cells run (rate_line, count_line and hit_line tests on lua and c) as acceptance. The other S3 families (samples, stamps, matched_number, random) follow note 36.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_44 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 44u32, "2026-10-24T16:52Z", "main", "main 12:5x: the Cells tree suite with the staged mounts.ex fix (main-cells-suite-tree-2, log spiral-bin\\cells-suite\\main-tree.log) passed 308/311, and none of the 3 reds touches mounts: (1, 2) DocumentTest lib round-trips (conversation.spi double blank line before persist; json_real.spir and routes_real.spir lack the canonical heading and ///- --real marker), both red at HEAD 0ec501fb too, fixed token-neutrally as LIB-CELLS-ROUNDTRIP (queued with a document_test rerun, job main-serial-cells-roundtrip); (3) CellsLiveTest \"a print with no value still runs\" hit a rustc \"expected expression, found $\" at 11:2x that does not reproduce now (console.write_line compiles, builds and prints on the live lib).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_45 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 45u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 19:45 Dry run of slice S3a (lua-retire-s3a.ps1) against HEAD 695c708 and against the working tree: all 4 anchors match once in both, and 3 lua.native_table fields remain in each (adjacent_ms, hits, last_tick, for the later slices). So whichever way the CELLS-APPS tree mismatch (CELLS-APPS note 20) is resolved, the Lua-retire chain doesn't need re-anchoring. It still waits on the RED file_exists arm.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_46 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 46u32, "2026-10-25T01:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 21:34 Lua file I/O lib slice in edition 3 (scratch lua-file-io-apply.ps1), replacing the RED lua-file-exists draft, whose root cause is corrected in TIBIA-PARTY note 90. It adds granular primitives in file_system.spi (:! lua_file, open, present, write, read_all, close, remove; one host call each, at most 120 chars), Lua arms for file_exists, write_all_text, read_all_text, append_all_text and file_delete with the logic in Spiral, and a cwd-independent ///> lua test that deletes, writes, checks existence, appends, reads back 'abc' and deletes. A 7-anchor eoie do program with undo, round-tripped byte-exact on a copy of the live edition-3 file. Gate: job-1792892069603-tibia-lib-lua-file-io-gate (heavy, kit gate with the fixed same-location baseline). It unblocks gate-via-lib and the Lua-retire S1-S3a slices here, and NO-HAND-LUA M1's trace-log append. LANDED 2026-10-24 22:07: gate-2 GREEN (job-1792892952996: 19 of 22 pass, 0 type errors; the 3 location reds are red on the same-location baseline too), applied by the kit land at 22:06 (job-1792893705330), lib file_system.spi 2A23F5F0 to 66D2992E, the gated candidate's hash. Main and x were told. gate-1 had failed only because the test sat above the definitions it calls, so it moved to the end of the file. Next: gate-via-lib.ps1 (anchors match 3 of 3 on the tree), then the Lua-retire S1, S2 and S3a slices.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_47 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 47u32, "2026-10-25T02:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 22:3x Landed in tibia as an unstaged layer over the user's staged 17 files (job-1792894637696-tibia-lib-lua-serial, exit 0). gate-via-lib: client.spi's hand-written g_resources.fileExists binding is gone, probe's gate check is now file_system.file_exists over the absolute gate path, and the harness passes that path. Lua-retire S1 (33 lua.joined/contains sites to sm' ++#), S2 (74 lua cell calls to mut cells) and S3a (adjacency counts to array i32) also landed. Checks: formatter clean, arena build LUA-OK for all 9 modules, lua.spi Cells 5 of 5. No file gained a dollar-quote macro or a lua. call; arena hand-written Lua went from 294 to 149 sites by count, and adjacency, observer, path, probe, scan, scoreboard and summon are at 0. git diff: 13 files, +129/-128. Left: the remaining S3 families (lua.sample_count, samples_joined, labeled_summary and the like in measure, steps, random, competitor; also GRANULAR-HEAL's lua.spi sites), about 75 min, and the smoke clear in the next live window, which is also the first live check of the gate path.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_48 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 48u32, "2026-10-25T12:08Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 08:08 S3 inventory (after S1/S2/S3a): 45 lua.* uses of 15 helpers in 5 files. Families: (1) samples, measure only: sample, sample_count, sample_median, sample_total, sample_max, samples_joined (11 uses), to become a Spiral accumulator (a list in a mut cell with count/median/total/max/joined), or lib statistics if a Lua-capable home exists; (2) labeled samples and stamps, steps only: labeled_sample, labeled_summary, stamp, stamp_at, stamp_kind (5), the same accumulator with labels; (3) random_between, random (2), to lib's RNG if it has a Lua arm, else a Lua arm there; (4) matched_number, measure (2), to lib string parsing; (5) native_table and new_table (24: client 11, competitor 2, measure 5, random 2, steps 4), creature tables keyed by id, to become a Spiral map. Family (5) also takes GRANULAR-HEAL's last 4 client.spi sites (instrument_game_calls, game_call_report, forget_dead, the monster list), which iterate those tables with pairs. Re-sized: about 2.5 h (was 75 min, which predated the client sites and family 5's count). Order by dependency: (1)+(2) first (measure and steps only), then (3) and (4), then (5) with the client sites, each a guarded slice with the arena build, lua Cells and, after pack 5l, competitor under the new Lua limits.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_49 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 49u32, "2026-10-25T13:18Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 09:18 S3 family 1 LANDED (job-1792932472660, unstaged): new apps/arena/samples.spi, a pure Spiral sample accumulator (mut list: sample, count, total, max from 0, median as sorted[floor((n+1)/2)] or 0, joined in insertion order), matching the hand Lua helpers exactly; measure.spi uses it for hits and taken (11 helper uses and 2 new_table). Proof: arena build LUA-OK for measure, formatter clean, Cells samples 3/3 (lua and c) and lua 5/5 with memo off. The first try restored itself on the formatter gate only (the renames lengthened a line); the slice now formats measure.spi itself. lua.spi's sample helpers have no callers left in measure; steps still uses labeled_sample/labeled_summary and the stamp helpers (family 2). Left: families 2 (steps), 3 (random_between), 4 (matched_number), 5 (native tables plus the 4 client sites), about 2 h.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_50 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 50u32, "2026-10-25T13:58Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 09:58 S3 families 2-4 in flight. (2) steps: labeled samples and per-key stamps added to samples.spi as Spiral association lists (labels ordered by core comp, since < is number-only; first try restored itself on that ETY constraint error), job-1792935902242. (3) random_between: lib common.random' had no Lua arm (fell to System.Random); slice random-lua-apply.ps1 adds lua_math_random (one host call) and a Lua arm with the bounds in Spiral (empty range returns min, like the Rust arm), plus a lua test; kit gate job-1792936186832 (LIB-RANDOM-LUA); tibia side s3-family3.ps1 (inclusive 0..3 and 1..8 become random' 0 4 and 1 9) waits on the lib land. (4) matched_number: new apps/arena/combat_text.spi (hit_amount, damage_taken over number_between, composed from sm'.index_of/substring/starts_with and convert.try_convert, all with Lua arms; tests mirror the Lua pattern incl. a later match after a non-fitting one), job-1792936644279 after family 2. Rule applied for (4): lib's only digit reader is xsd.digit_of inside the XSD domain module (main's P1 lane), so no lib move now; a generic sm' digit reader is a lib follow-up, not a tibia blocker.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_51 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 51u32, "2026-10-25T14:05Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 10:05 S3 family 2 LANDED (job-1792935902242, unstaged): steps.spi has 0 lua.* calls; labeled samples and per-key stamps live in samples.spi (Spiral association lists, labels ordered by core comp). lua.spi lost labeled_sample, labeled_summary, stamp, stamp_at, stamp_kind and their two tests. Proof: arena build LUA-OK (steps.lua 39957 bytes), formatter clean, Cells samples 6/6 and lua 3/3 with memo off.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_52 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 52u32, "2026-10-25T14:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 10:28 S3 family 4 LANDED (job-1792936644279, unstaged): new apps/arena/combat_text.spi (hit_amount, damage_taken over number_between; composed from lib sm'.index_of/substring/starts_with and convert.try_convert) replaces both lua.matched_number calls in measure.spi; lua.spi lost matched_number and its test half (contains keeps a test). Proof: arena build green (13 LUA-OK), formatter clean, Cells combat_text 3/3 and lua 3/3 with memo off. Left in S3: family 3 (random, waits on the lib gate LIB-RANDOM-LUA) and family 5 (native tables plus the 4 client sites, waits on pack 5l for the healed client).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_53 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 53u32, "2026-10-25T20:37Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 16:37 LIB-RANDOM-LUA gate RED in serial-b, but not from the slice: 3 retry_fn cells (rust, typescript, cuda) red on the candidate only, and 2 still red when run alone, all 300 s cell timeouts (compile 213 s of the cell for 4 targets at today's load); the slice's own random_draws test passed. Gate rule refined, written before the rerun: the retest copy carries a file-level ///- --timeout 900000 (the fiscal directive form) so a load timeout can't decide it, while the first pass and the baseline keep the normal budget. The random gate plus its lands (lib arm, family 3, dead lua.spi helpers) are requeued as the last steps of serial-c (job-1792953363791).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_54 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 54u32, "2026-10-26T09:33Z", "tibia-lane", "09:33Z 09:4xZ Correction to the TIBIA-PARK note 10 status: the random lands in tibia-serial-lib did NOT land. land.ps1's reader guard waited its full 90 minutes on 'a Cells run is in flight (erl pids 46708, 42528)': main's pack-5m2 lib notebook on date_time.spi, plus its Cells CompilerDaemon serve_app. Then it refused, so random-serial threw 'lib land failed' before family 3 and the dead lua.spi helpers. Nothing was written: common.spi still lacks lua_math_random, and lua.spi still holds random_between and the sample, counter and cell helpers. serial-lib went on to its process gate as designed. Both erl pids are gone now (checked 09:4xZ). Retry, packed into the queued tibia-serial-heals as its first step and guarded by 'common.spi has no := lua_math_random': random-serial.ps1 again (the lib arm through land.ps1 with the GREEN LIB-RANDOM-LUA verdict, then s3-family3 and s3-dead-helpers). This keeps the order before cells-apps, which touches the same arena files. Found while preparing lua.spi's edition-3 keywords: those wait on this land, because s3-dead-helpers deletes the inl lines at 21, 24 and 35 (sample_total, sample_max and add_to, all superseded by samples.spi with 0 lua.* callers), so the edition-3 lua.spi item mostly resolves itself.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_55 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 55u32, "2026-10-26T10:58Z", "tibia-lane", "10:58Z 10:5xZ Random lands, retry result (tibia-serial-heals first step): LIB-RANDOM-LUA LANDED in lib (common.spi after 5B3054397A77211E, undo kept). Tibia family 3 then wrote random.spi and lua.spi, failed the arena build and was restored by s3-land. Cause: measure failed with a type error at random.spi character 32. The slice wrote common.random' 0i32 4i32, but lib lists common as common- (unqualified names), so common. is unbound. Fix: s3-family3.ps1 now writes \random' 0i32 4i32 and \random' 1i32 9i32 (backup s3-family3-before-unqualify.ps1). Proof on a scratch copy (arena plus src, deps linked to the live spiral, which now has the lib arm): family 3 applies, and measure and random both compile to Lua and load in lua 5.1. Retry packed at the start of tibia-serial-gaps: family 3 if lua.spi still defines random_between, then dead helpers if lua.spi still has inl sample_total, both through s3-land (build gate plus restore). That now runs after cells-apps; cells-apps' merged random.spi still has both lua.random_between anchors and it doesn't touch lua.spi.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_56 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 56u32, "2026-10-26T16:37Z", "tibia-lane", "16:37Z S3 family 5 drafted (scratch family5: fam5-apply.ps1 and game_calls.spi), on top of the healed client (client-rebase/client-healed-on-cells.spi) and cells-apps' merged arena files. It retires lua.native_table and lua.new_table from arena entirely (0 references left), which closes GRANULAR-HEAL's last 4 client sites:\n- monsters_described: Spiral over the existing spectators/crowd_size/member primitives, live_monster, and target_line plus the spot. \"none\" when empty, else sm'.concat_list \"; \".\n- remember/forget_dead: engaged = mut (list (i32 * creature)) with listm'.filter (replace by id; count and drop dead). competitor and random hold client.engaged from client.no_engaged ().\n- creature slot (measure): creature_slot = mut (option creature) with the same six function names.\n- instrument_game_calls/game_call_report move out of client into a new game_calls module (after vbot, before nexbot, so it can use hooks and probe). The counters are Spiral mut cells. Each wrapper is hooks.replace_dispatch owner name pick, ONE branch-free adapter 'function(...) return (!pick)()(...) end' with the same shape as hooks' accepted replaceN adapters. Counting and the cancelFollow guard are Spiral picks (bumped cell native; ignoring when not following). The report composes new one-call primitives (inventory_item, item_present, item_id, hand slots, following_someone) with probe.chebyshev, and lists called names in a byte-sorted literal, which matches table.sort's order for these 15 names (first differences are case-consistent). game_calls carries 3 Lua tests: called order and format, the empty case, and that the literal is sorted (15 names).\nSemantics recorded: following_someone tests ~= nil where the original used truthiness (getFollowingCreature returns a creature or nil). The unconditional attack wrap and the conditional cancelAttack/cancelAttackAndFollow/name wraps keep their order, so the counting wrapper of cancelFollow still wraps the guard.\nAcceptance (written before any run): every arena program compiles to Lua and loads (LUA-OK), on the node first (tibia-fam5-lua: base vs fam5 on the composed tree), then at land in the arena build; Cells game_calls 3/3 and lua static with the memo off. Land order: after cells-apps and the client heal, i.e. after pack 5n installs.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_57 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 57u32, "2026-10-26T17:15Z", "tibia-lane", "17:15Z S3 family 5, node Lua check by its pre-registered acceptance: RED twice, then narrowed. Run 1 (tibia-fam5-lua): base 13/13 LUA-OK; fam5 11/13. nexbot was over 200 locals because instrument's 15 counting calls unrolled into main; competitor was over 60 upvalues. Making the heavy helpers join points (game_calls counting/instrument/report parts; client remember, forget_dead, monsters_described) fixed nexbot. Run 2 (tibia-fam5-lua2): 12/13, only competitor left. luac51 -l -l on the base build shows why: competitor's main callback (base line 2421, 1097 instructions) already holds exactly 60 upvalues, Lua 5.1's hard limit (top-level helpers, constructors and hoisted constants such as Us2_InReach). Any new top-level function referenced from inside that closure breaks it, whether inlined or a join point. LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS' pack 5n fix spills locals (200), not upvalues. Plan, decided by the granular rule rather than a compiler workaround: split competitor's giant callback into named top-level steps (it is also 9 cells over one post, POSTABLE), so that no closure captures 60 names. Family 5 lands on top of that split. Acceptance unchanged: all 13 arena programs LUA-OK on the composed tree.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_58 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 58u32, "2026-10-26T18:14Z", "tibia-lane", "18:14Z S3 family 5 meets its pre-registered acceptance on the node (tibia-split-full): on the composed tree (cells-apps merged arena + healed client), the competitor split alone gives 13/13 arena programs LUA-OK, and the split plus family 5 also gives 13/13. lua.native_table and lua.new_table are retired with 0 references left, which closes GRANULAR-HEAL's last 4 client sites. The land script family5/arena-f5-land.ps1 (guards: healed client and cells-apps competitor in the tree; applies the split, then family 5; gate: build.ps1 with arena LUA-OK >= 13 plus Cells on client, competitor, game_calls, lua, hooks, measure and random with the memo off; restores everything by hash on red and removes game_calls.spi) runs right after the client heal in tibia-serial-part's gaps parts, behind a hash guard. Negative control run: it refused with the tree untouched.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_59 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 59u32, "2026-10-27T12:28Z", "tibia-lane", "12:28Z 10-27 08:2x scope check plus two more migrations, in the tree unstaged. Both apps already reference lib (packageDir plus spiral-); predict has 0 host macros. Arena's remaining macros are client.spi and hooks.spi, which are the OTClient boundary (one-call primitives). Filtered for generic operations, the hand-rolled ones were: (1) env_is = os.getenv(name) == value, now env.get_environment_variable name = value (lib's Lua arm is os.getenv(var) or \"\"; unset still compares false); (2) lua_number plus tonumber, the nil test and the cast, the 3 macros behind env_i32, now optionm'.default_value fallback (convert.try_convert (sm'.trim ...)). The nominal and its 3 macros are gone, and every env_i32 variable is integer-valued. Also: fight_mode_text and player_speed now cast through host_text (the tostring left the macros; client.spi keeps exactly one tostring, inside host_text).\nProof: arena build, all 13 LUA-OK. A cost showed up: try_convert's Lua arm parses every integer target through i64, which on Lua 5.1 pulls in the spiral_w64 emulation (nexbot +34%, vbot +49%, random +23%). The fix goes into lib, not back into an app macro: LIB-LUA-NARROW-PARSE (scratch lib-lua-narrow-parse) gives i8..u32 targets a static narrow branch, a range check on Lua's double plus conv f64, with a new lua test (3, \" -12 \", 2147483648 to None, 1.5 to None, \"300\" as u8 to None). It is gating on the node now. After it lands, an arena rebuild should show the w64 code gone.\nStill open here: hooks.spi list_push (table.insert on host tables) and the client probe log (TibiaProbeLog global table) are host-state boundary code, not generic helpers. Left as they are.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_60 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 60u32, "2026-10-27T13:11Z", "tibia-lane", "13:11Z LIB-LUA-NARROW-PARSE LANDED 10-27 09:0x (live lib convert.spi, unstaged). It was gated GREEN on the node (3/3 tests, 9 rows, 0 red, compiler pinned, bases convert.spi=2D4AD23D59DC55A1), verdict imported with a base-hash check, and landed by the guarded land.ps1. The slice: narrow_parsed (static i8..u32 bounds, range check on Lua's double, then ~> conv f64 -> u) plus the static narrow branch in try_convert's Lua arm, so i64 parsing (and its spiral_w64 emulation) remains only for i64/u64 targets, with a new lua test (3, \" -12 \", 2147483648 to None, 1.5 to None, \"300\" as u8 to None). The first node run was RED: `conv` with type arguments must be `~> conv` with an annotated result, and a broken definition shows up as Unbound at its consumer. A 20-second local Lua probe (compile plus lua run, w64 absent) confirmed the fix before the rerun.\nConsumer proof (arena rebuild after the land, job-1793105740206): build exit 0 (all 13 programs load), spiral_w64 absent from every program. Against this morning's pre-migration Lua: competitor 188,725 to 161,255 B (-15%), measure 62,328 to 56,659 (-9%), nexbot 34,299 to 37,284 (+9%), random 49,815 to 52,782 (+6%), vbot 23,599 to 26,566 (+13%); the small growth is the integer-pattern check replacing a bare tonumber. This rebuild also covers the host-cast heal (fight_mode_text and player_speed through host_text).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_61 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 61u32, "2026-10-28T10:31Z", "tibia-lane", "GROOM 10-28 06:4x: DONE. Scope met per note 10-27 12:28Z: apps/arena and apps/predict both reference lib (packageDir plus spiral-), predict has 0 host macros, and arena's remaining macros (client.spi, hooks.spi) are the OTClient boundary, one-call primitives. The generic helpers it needed landed in lib (LIB-LUA-NARROW-PARSE 10-27 09:0x; env/convert/optionm use). The arena Lua built from it loads on 5n (LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS n11).")
inl note_of_kit_heal_lib_link_1 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:57Z", "eoie", "22:57 first step of EOIE-LIB-REUSE; pilot already passes under Cells (EOIE-LIB-REUSE note 2).")
inl note_of_kit_heal_lib_link_2 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 2u32, "2026-10-22T03:17Z", "eoie", "23:17 decision (R6, recorded before release C): the src/spiral link waits for a link-safe directory walk. eoie has 54 hand-rolled read_dir/walk sites in 39 files; census, standards, source topology, grep and the do verbs would all descend into a src/spiral junction and count or search lib files. Sequence: EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY gets one walk primitive that never follows junctions/symlinks, the scanners move onto it, then this link. Release C ships without it; the Cells-side pilot stays proven in lab/EOIE-LIB-REUSE.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_lib_link_3 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 3u32, "2026-10-22T03:40Z", "eoie", "23:40 redesign: no junction anywhere in eoie. test-regeneration already compiles in a staging copy (spiral-bin/eoie-regeneration/<guid>/eoie), and cargo builds the published .rs without needing Spiral packages, while Cells mounts link spiral next to each package. So the lib link is one staging step: after Copy-Workspace, copy <bundle>/../../../lib/spiral (top-level files + rust/) into the staged src/spiral; the live tree never holds the lib, scanners never see it, and Get-WorkspaceEntries' reparse-point refusal never fires. That removes the walk-primitive prerequisite noted at 23:17 (a link-safe walk is still wanted for EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY, using lib file_system.read_dir_names + path_buf_is_symlink). Proof running on a scratch copy (rL-tree): agile_task_query with the sm' pilot, regenerated through the patched staging, cargo check.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_lib_link_4 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 4u32, "2026-10-22T03:46Z", "eoie", "23:46 PROVEN on a scratch copy: test-regeneration with the lib staged as src/spiral (Spiral sources only: *.spi, *.spir, package.spiproj; target/node_modules/bench skipped) regenerated agile_task_query with the sm' pilot (24732 bytes) and cargo check passed; source topology with the staged lib stays ok (+1 Spiral-only package, limits ok). Shipping in release C as apply-lib-staging.ps1 (patches compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 + moves the pilot from lab/EOIE-LIB-REUSE into src/agile_task_query); C's candidate step adds next_cli and agile_cli. The boundary walk is no longer a prerequisite.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_lib_link_5 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 5u32, "2026-10-22T05:09Z", "eoie", "01:09 tibia mentions main is building a relative-package compiler feature (spiproj packages by relative path); when it lands it may replace the staging copy of lib/spiral with a declared relative package in each spiproj. Re-evaluate the staging step then.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_lib_link_6 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 6u32, "2026-10-22T05:37Z", "eoie", "01:37 proven in the real release: release ABC's regeneration staged lib/spiral as src/spiral and regenerated agile_task_query (sm' pilot) ok, 24732 bytes, identical to the scratch proof. Remaining for this item: ABC's cargo check, candidate notebooks (next_cli, agile_cli consume the query crate) and install.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_lib_link_7 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 7u32, "2026-10-22T16:56Z", "eoie", "2026-10-22 12:56 rule, written before measuring its effect: heal-crate's near-gate guard projects instead of reading today's size. A crate (or any direct consumer) is skipped when its widest generated .rs plus 3 lines per concat5 call in its .spi reaches 950 (a concat5 with n non-empty arguments becomes n-1 ++# bindings instead of one call, so at most +3 lines each). Trigger: eoie_experiments sits at 949 (one under the old 950 check) with 44 concat5 calls on the E2 list, so healing it could reach about 1080, past the 1000-line gate GE2's topology check enforces. A slice that anchors on healed text in a newly skipped crate fails its exact-once anchor; that crate's slice moves with it to after the codegen pack, and the wave never trims code to fit.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_lib_link_8 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 8u32, "2026-10-22T19:37Z", "eoie", "2026-10-22 15:37 first real compile of GE2+H (preview, 18 crates) found: (1) the argv heal turned inl argument helpers into wrappers of env.try_command_line_argument, so the lib call was expanded at every call site (process_table's pattern_from went 27 -> 94 lines; process_table 828 -> 1379). The argv, path-kind, integer-text and decimal-parse heals now emit let helpers; string-length keeps inl (same .len()). (2) heal-crate skipped every standards.spi as a rules declaration, which broke eoie_standards, whose standards.spi is the tool module. It now heals it as code when 'standards' is listed under modules, and never writes declarations over it. (3) eoie_do/run.spi lacked the eoie_do_model open after the program_family move (fixed in apply-do-verbs-crate). After crate splits for process_table (kill) and experiments (amend), every GE2+H crate previews under 1000 lines.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_lib_link_9 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 9u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 60m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 60%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_lib_link_10 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 10u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_lib_link_11 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 11u32, "2026-10-31T23:48Z", "kit", "10-31 19:5x kit, pause re-decision (main pre-night groom; R3 is now \"never auto-pause for silence\"): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the 10-28 silence groom (\"nothing queued\"), not by a blocker, and no note names an external condition. Back in agile next at its priority; its first step stays the one in the 10-28 note: check whether the remaining milestone shipped in a later train (installed kit 4983F9C0, TRAIN6) and close it with that evidence, else do the next milestone.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_lib_link_12 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 12u32, "2026-11-04T08:03Z", "kit", "DONE 11-04 04:4x (kit, closing with evidence per the 10-28 step): kit builds against lib/spiral through compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1, which stages <staged>/src/spiral from the Spiral checkout's lib/spiral (or a -FrozenLib copy, which trains use), so the live tree needs no src/spiral link and no ignore rule (no src/spiral exists). agile_task_query (package.spiproj references spiral-) passed TRAIN18's full node regen (231 owners published, cargo check) and the 1000-line crate gate (294/295; the one over was unrelated). AGENTS.md now requires every package to reference spiral-, and the lib-reuse pilot pattern is the standard shape.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_lib_link_13 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 13u32, "2026-11-06T11:26Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_heal_edit_hook_1 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:57Z", "eoie", "22:57 why: the harness tells agents to match surrounding code, and eoie's surrounding code is legacy (0/376 packages on the lib, 24.6 host macros/KLOC), so imitation is the drift; memory rules load at session start but the open file wins at edit time. The hook moves the rule to the moment of writing. Needs the user's approval: it edits ~/.claude/settings.json hooks (and the other clients' equivalents via EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS).")
inl note_of_kit_heal_edit_hook_2 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 2u32, "2026-10-22T03:02Z", "eoie", "23:02 decided and installed (user 23:0x: 'try getting more autonomous in the decisions'): ~/.claude/hooks/eoie-style-check.ps1 as a PostToolUse hook on Edit|Write|MultiEdit in ~/.claude/settings.json (backup in scratch settings.json.before-style-hook.*). Scope: files under eoie/src/<package>/ ending .spi/.spiproj. Checks the ADDED text only (Edit new_string, MultiEdit edits, Write content): $'{ blocks, Rc::<str>::from, rust_std_string, any new host macro; a .spiproj without spiral-; plus eoie standards --ratchet on the package. Reports via exit 2 (feedback to the editing agent, not a hard block). Tested: macro edit -> 2 findings, clean sm' edit -> 0, tibia path -> ignored, lib-less spiproj -> flagged; about 0.6 s per call. Takes effect for sessions started after 23:02. Interim: the regex checks live in the hook until eoie's census carries them (EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET), then the hook shrinks to the eoie call.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_edit_hook_3 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 3u32, "2026-10-22T07:39Z", "eoie", "03:39 hook made gate-aware (rule agreed with main 03:0x, EOIE-LIB-REUSE note 12): in a package whose committed .rs is at 950+ lines, rust_std_string.model.concat5 is allowed (each lib ++# costs one Rust binding until codegen inlines single-use bindings); any other rust_std_string use is still blocked; packages under 950 get the lib advice incl. ++# from sm'_operators. Same 950 threshold as heal-crate.ps1's skip. Probed with synthetic Edit payloads: listing concat5 exit 0, listing trim exit 2, do_tables concat5 exit 2. Backup in scratch eoie-style-check.ps1.before-near-gate-*. Left: other clients' equivalents (EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPT); drop the near-gate branch when main's codegen pack lands.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_edit_hook_4 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 4u32, "2026-10-23T10:06Z", "eoie", "06:06 groom: both remaining parts wait on other items, so they are now deps: dropping the near-gate concat5 branch waits on RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE (main's codegen pack, 25% Active), and the other clients' hooks wait on EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS. The Claude Code hook itself is installed and gate-aware; nothing else is left to do here before those land.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_edit_hook_5 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 5u32, "2026-10-29T01:35Z", "main", "10-28 21:4x main: the hook was lost in the profile wipe and is rebuilt from notes 2-3 plus KIT-RENAME 5/13/15 and kit AGENTS.md: ~/.claude/hooks/eoie-style-check.ps1, PostToolUse Edit|Write|MultiEdit (pwsh -File form). Root C:\\home\\git\\kit\\src, binary kit.exe, ratchet = kit standards --ratchet standards\\census.spi <package>. Boundary packages (host macros and Rc::<str>::from allowed): rust_std_*, *_host, kit_os_*, workspace_primitives. Pipe-tested 9/9: the 7 recorded cases (macro edit 2 findings, clean sm' 0, tibia ignored, lib-less spiproj flagged, listing concat5 exit 0 at 978 generated lines, listing trim exit 2, do_tables concat5 exit 2) plus a boundary-package macro (0) and a MultiEdit Rc (2); ~1.3 s per call. GRANULAR-LINT note 21's lint-macros wiring was an unlanded draft and is not included.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_edit_hook_6 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 6u32, "2026-10-29T22:32Z", "main", "10-29 18:3x main: PROVISIONAL fields filled while the item is Blocked or Paused (fill-all-task-fields): estimate 30m (D-base rule: base(D2) x (1 - 90%)). The owner corrects them on resume.")
inl note_of_kit_heal_edit_hook_7 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 7u32, "2026-10-31T00:37Z", "main", "10-30 20:37 main groom (BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 group C, 'confirm installed'): CONFIRMED. ~/.claude/settings.json runs the PostToolUse hook pwsh -File ~/.claude/hooks/eoie-style-check.ps1 (the 10-28 rebuild). The item is Blocked only on its deps RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE and EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS (group D: not dangling).")
inl note_of_kit_heal_edit_hook_8 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 8u32, "2026-11-01T12:44Z", "kit", "11-01 08:5x kit (Blocked rule). blocked by: RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE, EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS (its deps; the hook itself is installed and confirmed 10-30 by main).")
inl note_of_kit_heal_edit_hook_9 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 9u32, "2026-11-01T13:16Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit groom: CLOSED with evidence; remaining scope moved. Evidence: the PostToolUse edit-time hook is installed and confirmed (main 10-30: settings.json runs ~/.claude/hooks/kit-style-check.ps1), and it worked on 10-31/11-01: it refused kit's own edits with ratchet findings at least three times (agile_sprint's typed-feature density 179 -> 233 lines, kit_table_rows' tab-row encoder, a CRLF line), each fixed before landing. That is the item's purpose: the rule acts at the moment of writing. MOVED: the other clients' equivalent hooks -> EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS (that dep only covered them); the single-use inline-macro check -> RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE itself (it adds a rule the hook will run, not a precondition for the hook).")
inl note_of_kit_heal_edit_hook_10 () = Note ("KIT-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 10u32, "2026-11-06T11:26Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_1 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 1u32, "2026-10-22T07:03Z", "main", "03:03 main: filed from eoie's measurement (EOIE-LIB-REUSE). Chosen over (b), a lib n-ary concat, because it shrinks every generated Rust file, x's crates near the gate and GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES' pressure without callers changing. Scope: single-use bindings whose value is pure (no side-effecting macro, no allocation order that observers can see) and whose use is in the same block. Gate: its own pack after PACK-AFTER-NIGHT; full -Suite all review where only Rust residuals change, every Rust row keeps its native verdict, and a line-count table (before/after per sample and for eoie's crates) shows the drop. eoie keeps concat5 in crates within ~5% of the gate meanwhile.")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_2 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 2u32, "2026-10-22T07:42Z", "main", "03:42 main: patch drafted, %TEMP%\\rust-concat-fusion\\patch.ps1 (-Target; dry run by default; refuses if already applied); 4 anchors, each matching once on the tree core and the PACK-AFTER-NIGHT core. codegenRust sits in a shared section (CodegenRust, between #endif 163026 and #if 181941), so both cores get it and pair-drift stays clean. Design: fuseConcat rewrites a body BEFORE its liveness pass (method bodies, closure bodies and main), so live_binds and the clone/move choices see the fused code. A let is fused into the next statement only when (1) both are TyMacro of the Rust concat shape (first part 'Rc::<str>::from(format!(\"', which borrows its arguments); (2) the first let binds one variable whose use count over the whole body is exactly 1 (uses over-counted on purpose: binders inside unbox/while count too, and any op outside the known list makes the body opaque, so it is left unchanged); (3) the second macro names that variable exactly once, as a direct CMTerm(DV _). Fusion splices the first macro's parts in place of that term and cascades through a chain, so 'a ++# b ++# c ++# d' becomes one statement of nested format!. Only adjacent statements fuse, so no effect is reordered. Nested blocks (if, while, do, indent, unbox, int switch) are fused recursively. Gate (its own pack): -Suite all with DISAGREE 0 where only Rust residuals change; Rust line counts before/after for samples and eoie's 4 crates near the gate (eoie_agile_mutation 981, agile_todo_lines 986, agile_task_listing 979, eoie_memory_query 975); then tell eoie it can drop concat5.")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_3 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 3u32, "2026-10-23T10:55Z", "main", "2026-10-23 06:55 main: status groomed. The concat-fusion patch (fuseConcat, 4 sites) rode next3b into the next4 composite 67EBF80563E2239A (now in its matrix review) and is not yet in the tree. What remains is the review verdict, the install, and the line-count table for eoie's crates named in the title.")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_4 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 4u32, "2026-10-23T22:18Z", "eoie", "18:18 eoie: demand data for prioritizing. 15 eoie packages can't move off rust_std_string.concat5 onto lib ++# until this lands, because heal-crate projects their own or a consumer crate within 5% of the 1000-line gate: do_edit, do_facts, do_process, do_program, experiment_analysis, experiments, machine_watch, memory, memory_query, style_census, agile_todo_lines, plus agile_note_model, eoie_table_query, agile_todo_model and eoie_memory_model through consumers. Only 2 of 17 heal today. Each concat5 rewrite projects up to 3 extra generated lines; with single-use bindings inlined, that cost goes to zero and the wave proceeds without crate splits (EOIE-LIB-REUSE note 32).")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_5 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 5u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict: (3) KEPT Active: demand: 15 eoie packages wait on it (n.10-23 18:18); next compiler pack after P2.")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_6 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 6u32, "2026-10-29T07:13Z", "main", "10-29 03:2x main: status from the tree, not the old notes. fuseConcat (n1-n2's design) is in the core at all 4 sites (spiral_compiler.fs:164372 definition; uses at 165033, 165067, 165089). The tree core is the installed one: COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB n18 found the rebuilt P2 tree core byte-identical to the installed directory in all 32 loaded files, and tonight pair-drift shows the core equal to HEAD. So the concat fusion has SHIPPED. What remains is n2's line-count table for the eoie/kit crates named in the title (each concat5 to ++# move measured against the 1,000-line gate), which needs a kit regen: kit team. That is also all EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK's dependency on this item still waits on.")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_7 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 7u32, "2026-10-31T22:56Z", "main", "10-31 18:56 main, pre-night groom: Active -> PLANNED (not Paused: no note in over 48 h and no live job, so it isn't work in progress, but nothing blocks it; it stays in agile next at its priority and progress for the night run to pull).")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_8 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 8u32, "2026-11-01T13:17Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit groom: received from the closed EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK: this item's check becomes a rule that the installed edit-time hook (kit-style-check) runs, not a precondition for the hook. DoD addition: a single-use inline host macro added in an edit is reported by the hook (pipe test).")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_9 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 9u32, "2026-11-04T14:31Z", "compiler", "11-04 10:3x compiler (relaying tibia, no action asked): a typed function that folds a concatenation of literals, e.g. failwith (~> sm'_real::(++#*) name \": no arm for this backend\") called with \"picked\", emits only the folded literal on C, but Rust keeps both input literals as dead bindings: let v0: Rc<str> = { thread_local!{ static LIT ... from(\"picked\") } ... }; let v1 = ...; let v2 = ...from(\"picked: no arm for this backend\"). So each folded concat leaves 2 unused thread_local statics in generated Rust (dead pure lets that cacheRustStringLiterals turns into statics before dead-binding elision sees them). Repro: tibia scratch ...\\ac4b4e98-...\\scratchpad\\cuprobe\\lib\\spiral\\noarm_probe.spi (unsupported.spi beside it), outputs noarm4.c / noarm4.rust. Fits this item: drop unused literal bindings before or inside cacheRustStringLiterals.")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_10 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 10u32, "2026-11-06T15:53Z", "compiler", "11-06 11:5x compiler: STEP for tibia's 11-04 finding (dead literal statics). Repro read (tibia scratch cuprobe/lib/spiral/noarm4.rust): after the C-side fold of \"picked\" ++ \": no arm ...\", Rust keeps v0 = \"picked\" and v1 = \": no arm for this backend\" as thread_local statics that nothing reads; only v2 (the folded literal) is used. Cause: the Rust text pipeline (codegenRust ~15250: foldStringBindings ... import_rust_unions |> cacheRustStringLiterals) never drops the pure literal lets that folding left behind. FIX: kitdo 1-drop-dead-literal-lets.kitdo (kit drafts, 2 replacements, dry run ok): a text pass dropDeadRustLiterals, run right before cacheRustStringLiterals, drops `let vN: Rc<str> = Rc::<str>::from(\"...\");` when vN appears nowhere else in the same top-level fn. That is conservative (whole-fn word count) and safe, because a literal has no side effects. DoD for this step: noarm4 shape keeps only v2; Rust examples parity: residual changes only by removed dead literal lines, 0 behavior cells. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: none (text pass in the codegen pipeline).")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_11 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 11u32, "2026-11-06T18:17Z", "compiler", "11-06 14:2x compiler: candidate pack58 = live tree (now pack57, core 8F0094967068FC85) + tibia's RUST-ARRAY-2-WIDE-MERGE (reviewed, n8 there) + this item's 1-drop-dead-literal-lets.kitdo, building on the box, followed by the Rust byte comparison of every sample vs the installed core. Diffs attribute by shape: self-tail let-return fns turning into loops are RUST-ARRAY-2's, removed 'let vN: Rc<str> = Rc::<str>::from(...)' lines whose vN has no other use are this item's. The pass is safe under the group-arm number reuse found in RUST-GROUP-ARM-STRING-FOLD: it counts uses per fn segment, so reused arm numbers only over-count and keep more lines. next step: read every diff, then rustc-run the changed samples.")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_12 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 12u32, "2026-11-06T18:18Z", "compiler", "11-06 14:2x compiler: DoD: (1) the Rust byte comparison of every sample shows only removals of string-literal lets with no other use in their fn (plus RUST-ARRAY-2's own loop diffs in the same pack); (2) every changed sample still builds with rustc and returns the same exit code; (3) smoke at parity; (4) installed as pack58 and shipped by main.")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_13 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 13u32, "2026-11-06T18:51Z", "compiler", "11-06 14:5x compiler: PROVEN on candidate pack58 (core 97C2F2FF5915067B) with tibia's own 11-04 repro (ac4b4e98 scratch cuprobe/lib/spiral/noarm_probe.spi: run_target backend switch whose non-Lua arm is no_arm 'picked'): the installed pack57 still emits 3 thread_local LIT statics (v0 'picked' and v1 ': no arm for this backend' dead, v2 the folded message), pack58 emits only v2 (970 -> 709 bytes); both rustc 1.99.0 builds panic with the same message and exit 1, as the probe intends. Plain calls of no_arm_message / no_arm outside a backend switch already fold to one literal on pack57, so the dead lets come from the switch's arm evaluation. The sample corpus has NO instance (0 dead literal lets in all 550 pack57 Rust outputs, scanned per fn), so the corpus byte comparison (running) can only show that nothing else changes. next step: finish that comparison, then smoke + install pack58.")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_14 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 14u32, "2026-11-06T19:30Z", "compiler", "11-06 15:3x compiler: in candidate pack58b the dead-literal pass changes NO sample (the corpus has no instance; see n13) and removes the 2 dead statics from tibia's noarm_probe (pack58: 3 -> 1 statics). Rides pack58c next.")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_15 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 15u32, "2026-11-06T20:42Z", "compiler", "11-06 16:4x compiler: INSTALLED on the box as pack58c at 16:4x (core 1524D5D56E6A11D5, host 734CEF2EBF4E7DD4, equal to the reviewed candidate; reviewed source EF757B710BE39CFA); asked main to ship it to node and europa. Done when shipped.")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_16 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 16u32, "2026-11-06T20:52Z", "compiler", "11-06 16:5x compiler: DONE. pack58c SHIPPED to all three hosts (main 16:5x): box core 1524D5D56E6A11D5 (16:4x), node the same in place 16:49 (fresh launch exit 0), europa Linux build from source EF757B710BE39CFA (core ffe92f8ef12ec037, launch exit 0, 16:50). The dead literal statics of a folded no-arm message are gone on every host.")
inl note_of_rust_inline_single_use_17 () = Note ("RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", 17u32, "2026-11-06T21:21Z", "compiler", "11-06 17:3x compiler: REOPENED. pack58c was rolled back for TYPE-ARITH-3-FOLD's lib regression, so this item's program is no longer installed anywhere once main finishes the node/europa rollback. It re-rides pack58d (pack57 + RUST-ARRAY-2 x2 + this), gated with lib/spiral's sized_array type probe in addition to the Rust byte comparison and smoke.")
