inl note_of_pack_next_4_19 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 19u32, "2026-10-23T08:47Z", "main", "2026-10-23 04:47 main: composed after tibia's block end: tail-moves (TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES; F# parallelMoves equals the mirror's steps on 165/165 sample blocks) and gleam-helpers (GLEAM-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND; F# gleamUsedHelpers equals the mirror on 447/447 outputs) after census-per-statement. next4 core 3D8553145D246A26. Composite with the formatter (pack-next5d: then-habit, bare-join-head, chain-fill, shape-cache): 7FCC891349EB1608, parse-only clean under SINGLE_FLIGHT and HOPACSOTA. The chain (review -> cells-pack -> hopacsota promotion) starts on eoie's 'release install done' (F9, expected 05:20-05:40), as agreed with eoie.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_20 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 20u32, "2026-10-23T09:06Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:06 main: post-F9 steps armed as a background waiter (eoie job wait F9, then scratchpad after-f9.ps1). They run only if F9 ends finished with exit=0, in this order: (1) the next5d reformat on spiral and eoie working files, holding every staged .spi (574 in spiral, 133 in eoie, re-read at run time) and skipping files changed since the review copy (apply-format's hash check, which also skips eoie's fresh lib landings); (2) spiral rust-toolchain.toml (nightly-2026-10-18, channel only) if absent; (3) start-chain.ps1 -Core on composite 7FCC891349EB1608: next4 review -> cells-pack -> hopacsota promotion, output to a file per the job-start pipe rule.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_21 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 21u32, "2026-10-23T09:26Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:26 main: install script staged: %TEMP%\\pack-next4\\install.ps1 (from next3b's). It refuses unless the tree core is still pack-after-night 25D2BE7C94943D8E (next3b never installed; next4 carries its fixed parts) and the composite on disk is exactly the reviewed 7FCC891349EB1608. Under an exclusive compiler-install window it copies the composite into the tree, builds single-flight and hopacsota, and exits 0 only if the installed single-flight SpiralCompilerCore equals the reviewed candidate. Rule for -Apply, unchanged from the pack rules: the review's automatic gate PASS, DISAGREE 0 on both oracles, every per-backend text rule at 0 beyond its rewrite, and the hand-read of the formatter hunks.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_22 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 22u32, "2026-10-23T10:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 06:11 main: composite 67EBF80563E2239A review (job-1792748200944). Release candidate built in 263.9 s. raw.spi lays out like plain.spi (widest 96 = 96, 0 synthetic lines). Macro continuations kept 12 of 12. Formatter candidate gate automatic part PASS: user, lib, synthetic, dice and eoie corpora at 0 files differing beyond parentheses and whitespace; 33 flagged joins, 0 of them authored; 0 joined authored lines that fit. The matrix compare against next3b's run single-flight-20261022-184215 runs next; the per-backend text rules (locals_check.py) follow it.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_23 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 23u32, "2026-10-23T13:33Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:33 main: the 67EBF805 review (job-1792748200944) reaches its 240-min limit at about 09:36. The formatter gate and the build passed; the matrix was at 3,752 of 4,988 native rows (through the 'r' samples) at 6-13 rows/min, because HP's cargo regen, x's Cells and tibia's agents shared the box. No verdict yet. Plan: restore the samples from the review's own 'before' snapshot (pack-next4\\restore-samples.ps1: 4,987 outputs plus 12 candidate part files from RustModuleSplit), so the rerun snapshots the installed state. Then rerun with a 6 h limit as main-pack-next4-review-2 (job-1792762224345, after tibia-block-pr26). -ReuseNative replays the 3,752 finished rows from the native memo, so the rerun runs about 1,240 rows plus compiles. The install of 18AC8014 (install.ps1, formatter-only delta over 67EBF805) waits for that verdict.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_24 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 24u32, "2026-10-23T13:58Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:58 main: post-block chain made serial, by my memory rule that Cells gates and cargo beside a matrix cost row timeouts (heavy-gate-lessons, 10-21/10-22 recurrences). After tibia-block-pr26 it runs main-pack-next4-review-2 (6 h), then main-lib-land-gate (it first restores the samples from review-2's 'before' snapshot), then main-lib-land, main-cells-pack, main-dib-suite, main-rustls-check, main-am-any-gate, and main-hopacsota-promotion (8 h), each --after the previous one. The promotion's matrix never overlaps another matrix on the shared samples tree. Manual steps in between, by rule: install 18AC8014 (install.ps1) once review-2's verdict passes, then the next7 review (Gleam split and closure factory), then next8 (or-pattern v3).")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_25 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 25u32, "2026-10-23T18:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:57 main: review-2 VERDICT: FAIL by the pre-written rule (DISAGREE 0). Run single-flight-20261023-135519 vs next3b's 184215: 277 Gleam rows DISAGREE (oracle agree -> DISAGREE, exit 1). Every other rule holds: C beyond blank lines/fusion/linkage 0, Zig const-check 467/0 bad, Kotlin/Swift/TS/Lean beyond their locals rewrites 0, Lua 0, Wasm 0, formatter gate PASS; compile-status changes vs next3b are all explained (GADT sample error->timeout = GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX evidence; portable_math_rounding Lean/Gleam/Lua timeout->ok; record_with_expected_base error->ok, a new sample). Informational per the rule text: Rust beyond let mut 307 (snapshot predates next3b + RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE), Bend 9 (w64), Delphi 1 (native_managed_array_tail_recursion), Python closures keep their dels (pythonWithoutDels does not reach closure bodies: an incomplete drop, harmless, to fix with the Gleam fix). CAUSE of the Gleam failure (found and proven in fsi): GLEAM-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND's gleamUsedHelpers ends a helper at the next '\\n}\\n', but StringBuilder.AppendLine writes CRLF on Windows, so the externals block ends in '}\\r\\n' and the search for the unused spiral_wrap_unsigned ran on to main's closing brace: the 'helper' removed was everything through main, then spiral_wrap_signed went unused too; output = 'import gleam/int\\r\\n' (18 bytes; the sample's own repro with the next4 dll gives exactly that). Fix in %TEMP%\\pack-next6\\gleam-helpers.ps1: prune (code.Replace('\\r\\n', '\\n')); fsi on a CRLF case: 224 -> 19 bytes before, 224 -> 151 after (only the unused helper goes). Consequence: next4 (and the 5f composite on it) is not installed today; the user's formatter fix goes in as a formatter-only core instead (pack-fmt-only: the installed tree core with only 5f's Formatter section, delta Formatter-only, building now as job-1792781232696). next4 needs a recompose with the fix and a new review.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_26 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 26u32, "2026-10-23T19:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 15:51 main: after the formatter-only install (tree core now 19F5F752485517B1 = pack-after-night + 5f's Formatter section), pack-next4\\install.ps1's Base pin moved from 25D2BE7C to 19F5F752 so the reviewed 5f composite (5f 821195F8CC1C5E10 = next4 with the Gleam CRLF fix + the same 5f Formatter section) can install over it. review-3 (job-1792782626942, queued at the chain end) reviews that composite, records its candidate in pack-next5f\\dll.txt, and restores the samples tree after itself; install only on its pass.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_27 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 27u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:17 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 60 by the rule 'implemented and staged in a pack or queued gate, awaiting review'. Where: composed (next4 composite, Gleam CRLF fix included), decided by review-3 in chain B.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_28 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 28u32, "2026-10-24T23:58Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:58 main groom: none of this stack's passes is in the tree core yet (luaUsedHelpers, gleamUsedHelpers, zigConstLocals and bendUsedPrelude count 0 in apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, 2-3 each in pack-next5i). The install vehicle is pack 5i (next4 -> next7/8/9 -> 5h -> 5i; COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS has the remaining steps). 18 leaf items now depend on this one: LUA-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND, C-STATIC-FUNCTIONS, WASM-PRELUDE-FIELDS-ON-DEMAND, GLEAM-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND, TYPESCRIPT-CONST-LOCALS, ZIG-CONST-LOCALS, KOTLIN-SWIFT-VAL-AT-ASSIGNMENT, LEAN-LET-AT-ASSIGNMENT, BEND-PRUNE-IF-PARAMS, GLEAM-TIDY-OUTPUT, PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT, RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE, TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES, C-FUSE-DECLARATIONS, RUST-LET-IMMUTABLE, C-OUTPUT-BLANK-LINES, PYTHON-DROP-DEL, ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND. When 5i is installed and verified live, close them together, each with its pass's census rerun on the tree outputs.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_29 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 29u32, "2026-10-28T01:26Z", "main", "10-27 21:26 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (1) SHIPPED. its passes are in the tree core: luaUsedHelpers 2, gleamUsedHelpers 2, zigConstLocals 3, bendUsedPrelude 3 hits.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_1 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 1u32, "2026-10-22T20:54Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:54 main: measured with scratch rust_last_use.py, textually: a vN.clone() whose name does not occur again before the next fn. That over-counts clones inside loop bodies (the next iteration reuses the variable) and under-counts nothing, so it is an upper bound. Design: codegenRust's var (core 164154: v{i} if is_copy t || moves t, else v{i}.clone()) gets a per-method last-use table computed over the TypedBind array before emission. A use is last when no later statement in the same block, and no enclosing TyWhile or self-tail loop body, mentions the variable. Branches (TyIf, unbox arms) count as later for each other's siblings only when they follow textually, so a variable used in both arms is moved only at the last use in each arm. Rust's borrow checker is the proof oracle: a wrong move is a compile error (E0382), so the matrix catches every mistake as a build-fail. Rule: Rust residuals change only by dropped .clone() calls, every Rust row's native result is unchanged, DISAGREE 0, and the clone count is published before and after. The gain is refcount traffic (Rc<str>, Rc<RefCell<Vec>>) and deep union clones (#[derive(Clone)] enums with many Rc fields).")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_2 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T03:30Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:30 main: measured and implemented. Census (scratch rust_clone_census.py, tree main.rs): 2,396 vN.clone() calls; 1,562 (65.2%) are the variable's last textual use in a function with no loop, and 97 more are last uses in functions with loops (left alone, needs real liveness). Mirror rust_move_mirror.py removes 1,379 clones: last textual use, function without loop/while or a 'move' closure. rustc --emit=metadata on nightly-2026-10-18: the 12 largest and the 30 with the most removals (463 clones) all pass where their originals pass, so no 'cannot move out of a reference' errors in that sample. F# pass rustMoveOnLastUse equals the mirror on all 474 main.rs (fsi), appended after immutableRustLets (%TEMP%\\pack-next6\\rust-move.ps1). In PACK-NEXT-4, core F84F4569BC0035BE.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_3 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T05:26Z", "main", "2026-10-25 01:26 main: REGRESSION in 5j (found by eoie on eoie_log_sections, sections.rs:139): rustc E0507 'cannot move out of v2, a captured variable in an FnMut closure'. The cause is rustMoveOnLastUse's per-function guard. It skipped functions containing 'loop {', 'while ' or a line with '|' and 'move', but a plain iterator closure (v3.into_iter().fold(v4, |acc, x| { ... }), host text from am'.vec_fold') has no move, so the final textual use of the captured v2 inside the closure lost its .clone(), although the closure runs once per element. Fix (5k, %TEMP%\\pack-next5k\\rust-move-closures.ps1, anchor 1/1): a function holding any closure, |args| or || {, move or not, is skipped like a loop. The guard regex was checked on 8 generated-Rust shapes (4 closures true, 4 plain lines false); a bitwise a | b | c over-matches, which only skips an optimization. 5k core 3DC18876AFBE138E. Candidate build job-1792905955561; then eoie's real-crate check, the single-flight review, install. The matrix missed this because no sample moves a captured variable inside a non-move closure; add one when the review is green (memory regression-samples-allowed).")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_4 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 4u32, "2026-10-29T23:01Z", "compiler", "10-29 19:0x compiler: three findings from today bear on this pass (details on RUST-HOST-MOVE-ONLY n1-n4). (1) The 5k bail (skip a whole fn holding any closure) also fires on the compiler's OWN capture-free caches, 'LIT.with(|lit| lit.clone())' / CASE / CLOSURE, so any fn with a string literal loses the optimization. That caused x's E0599: a non-Clone ureq::RequestBuilder kept its .clone() once a literal entered the fn. Fixed for host move-only types by apply-rust-ureq-move-only.ps1 (pack 8). (2) Census of un-bailing those idioms only (build-free PowerShell port of this pass, fixed-point control 0/477): 206 of 477 committed main.rs would change, 1,642 more clones removed (draft apply-rust-postpass-idioms.ps1). It cannot reintroduce 5k's E0507: those closures capture no locals (thread_local caches). (3) A first bench of that change on mega_brzozowski runtime_bench Rust showed the move-heavier build SLOWER (median 7.2 s vs 2.9 s; A/A 1.38 under load, inconclusive). Possible mechanism: the last-use move hands the final Rc to the callee, which then runs the deep drop of a big structure in its hot path, where the clone used to defer it. So 'moving on last use is always a win' is unproven for this pass too. A clean re-bench is queued on the node (job-1793313397632), and fix 2 stays on hold until it reports. Also still open from n3: the matrix lacks a sample that moves a captured variable inside a non-move closure (the 5k regression shape).")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_5 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 5u32, "2026-10-31T20:02Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:01 compiler GROOM (user direction via /btw, 10-31 15:4x): the self-hosted rewrite (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) starts only after EVERY backend is SOTA: no memory problems, no Rc slowdowns, no union hacks, no recursion limits, no missing arithmetic; GADT bugs and conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'; edition 3 finishes first; Gleam is a top test target (abyssal codebases) with F#, Rust and Wasm; Cells as fast as F# projects. This item is now on that gate (priority -> P0).")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_6 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 6u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): NEXT CPU-LIGHT EDIT (user 00:2x: nail codegen that avoids Rc and clones, granular, ready for a multi-language std lib; Fable no longer forces .NET APIs): un-bail the compiler's own capture-free cache idioms (LIT/CASE/CLOSURE .with(|x| x.clone())) in the closure bail, so a fn with a string literal keeps move-on-last-use. The build-free census (n4) already sizes the win; write the applier and recount clones in samples/*/main.rs afterwards as this item's metric (63.1% of clones are last-use today).")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_7 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 7u32, "2026-11-01T04:44Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:44 compiler: METRIC BASELINE (build-free, apps/compiler/tmp/samples/*/main.rs as checked in, mixed cores): 476 samples, 29,362 lines, 2,778 .clone() = 94.6 per kloc, 861 Rc::new. Densest: rust_static_closure_chain 162 clones in 519 lines (312/kloc, the closure bail at work), then the native_cube_*_direct family at ~63-67 each. Once the un-bail applier lands, re-measure these same numbers on regenerated outputs; they become BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL rows (phase=Emit, measure=clones_per_kloc).")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_8 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 8u32, "2026-11-01T06:22Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:22 compiler, round 3: CORRECTION + NEW TARGET. The round-2 next-edit (un-bail the LIT/CASE/CLOSURE cache idioms) is ALREADY IN THE TREE: rustMoveOnLastUse (spiral_compiler.fs 13380) strips captureFreeCache before its bail test (13396-13397). So the remaining lever is the bail itself, which skips any fn with a loop or a closure. MEASURED (build-free, the committed samples/*/main.rs, which already went through the pass): 1,545 fns, 264 bailed (17%); 2,765 clones in fns, 643 of them (23%) inside bailed fns. Loop-only: 59 fns / 280 clones; closure-only: 205 fns / 363 clones; both: 0. The other 2,122 clones sit in fns the pass already saw, so they are not last uses (needed, or out of reach of the textual rule). NEXT EDITS, in order of safety: (1) LOOPS: brace-match each 'loop {' / 'while ... {' span and allow a cut when the last occurrence lies AFTER the span closes (it runs once), keeping in-loop last uses cloned. Bound: <= 280 clones. (2) CLOSURES: allow a cut only when the variable never appears inside any closure span in the fn (a moved var must not be captured or borrowed by a live closure: E0505/E0507). Bound: <= 363. Each edit is proven by rustc on every Rust row (BACKEND-BATCH), plus the clone recount: target bailed-fn clones 643 -> under 300.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_9 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 9u32, "2026-11-01T06:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:34 compiler: MEASURED BEFORE BUILDING, both n8 next-edits are NOT worth a pack. Build-free simulation of each rule on the committed samples/*/main.rs: (1) loop rule (cut a last use outside every loop span, braces counted outside string/char literals): 59 loop-only fns, 7 gain, 8 clones removed of 280. (2) closure rule (cut only vars that never appear inside a closure span): 205 closure-only fns, 2 gain, 9 clones removed of 363. Together ~17 of the 643 clones in bailed fns (2.6%): almost all of those clones sit INSIDE the loop or closure, where a move is wrong. So the textual pass is at its ceiling. The loop-rule program is drafted and dry-applied (3 replacements) but SHELVED. NEXT LEVER (to measure the same build-free way): the 2,122 clones in fns the pass already saw are non-last uses, i.e. reads of a value that's used again. The candidate is BORROWING: pass &T / &str for args the callee only reads (needs a per-method read-only census in codegenRust, not text). First step: count clones that feed a call argument whose callee never moves it. That's the next census.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_10 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 10u32, "2026-11-01T06:37Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:37 compiler: CLONE ANATOMY (build-free, the same 2,603 clone sites in samples/*/main.rs). The dominant shape is destructuring a layout's fields, 'let (mut v1, mut v2): (i32, Rc<str>) = (v0.l0.clone(), v0.l1.clone())' (158 + 64 + 48 lines in the top 3 forms), plus the cached-literal 'LIT.with(|lit| lit.clone())'. In the let-bound and destructuring sites the clone lands on: Copy types (i8..u64/f32/f64/bool/char/()) 268, Rc<..> 497, other 61. CONSEQUENCES: (1) the 268 Copy clones are free at runtime (rustc lowers them to a copy) but noise in the output: a TEXT-ONLY win, emit 'v0.l0' for Copy-typed fields (codegenRust knows each field's Ty, so it's exact, not textual). (2) The 497 Rc clones are refcount bumps; the lever there is reading a field by reference when the bound value is only read (borrow), which needs the per-method read-only census named in n9. (3) LIT clones are one Rc bump per literal use; an &'static str for literals that are only read would remove them, the same borrow question. Recommended order: (1) first (small, exact, cleaner output for the multi-language std lib), then the borrow census.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_11 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 11u32, "2026-11-01T06:38Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:38 compiler: EDIT (1) DRAFTED, one line: codegenRust's layout_index (13844) emits '<field>.l<i>' with no .clone() when the field's type is Copy (the existing is_copy at 13585: every YPrim except StringT), and '.clone()' otherwise. That covers the destructuring and let-bound field reads of n10 (<= 268 Copy clones, text only, same runtime). Dry-applied (1 replacement); it goes into the next stacked candidate. Proof: rustc on every Rust row + the clone recount (expect 2,603 -> about 2,335 sites).\nPROGRAM (kit do <apps/compiler> -):\nedit spiral_compiler.fs expect files 1 replacements 1\n  replace once <<<\n                x |> Array.map (fun (L(i',_)) -> $\"{field}.l{i'}.clone()\")\n  >>> with <<<\n                x |> Array.map (fun (L(i',t')) -> if is_copy t' then $\"{field}.l{i'}\" else $\"{field}.l{i'}.clone()\")\n  >>>")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_12 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 12u32, "2026-11-01T07:08Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:08 compiler: METRIC AFTER the Copy-field edit (pack12b outputs now in samples/*/main.rs for its 131 Rust P rows; other rows unchanged): 477 samples (+1, mutual_tail_rc_live), 29,431 lines, 2,510 .clone() = 85.3 per kloc, down from 2,778 / 94.6 (-268 clones, -9.8%). That's exactly n10's Copy-field count, so the edit removed every Copy clone it targeted and nothing else. Next lever is unchanged: the 497 Rc field clones (borrow census).")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_13 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 13u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:34 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: dep PACK-NEXT-4 (Done long ago) -> PACK-12 (the Copy-field edit shipped there); load heavy -> light (drafting is text, proof rides a batch); est 360m left (borrow census + edit). DEFINITION OF DONE: Rc field clones in samples/*/main.rs fall from 497 to under 250 (the census script in n10, rerun on regenerated outputs), with every Rust row still building under rustc -O and agreeing with C (predict: compile-status diffs 0; native 0 DIFFERS), measured as clones/kloc (today 85.3) in BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL rows. NEXT STEP: the borrow census: in codegenRust, for each emitted method, list params whose body only reads them (no move into a struct, no return, no capture by a move closure), and count the call sites that clone into those params (a pass over the TypedBind tree in codegenRust, not text). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: param passing becomes a UNION 'RustPass = ByValue | ByRef | ByRcClone' decided once per (method, param) from a read-only analysis and stored in a map keyed by the method's join point key, never re-derived from output text (today's rustMoveOnLastUse is a TEXT pass over generated Rust; the borrow rule must work on the typed tree so it can't misread strings or comments); 'is_copy' stays the Ty predicate (YPrim except StringT) that the Copy-field edit used.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_14 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 14u32, "2026-11-02T21:47Z", "compiler", "11-02 17:55 compiler: COORDINATION before the borrow census. tibia filed COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS (P1 D6, 11-02) for the SAME analysis on C: which join-point heap/array params a body only reads. One analysis, two consumers. PLAN: a shared `ParamEscape` pass over TypedBind (backend-independent, computed once per join-point key next to the method/closure memos), giving per (jp key, param index) a union `ParamUse = Borrowed | Escapes`. Escape = stored into a heap/mutable/array/union payload, captured by a closure, returned, or passed to a callee param that is itself Escapes (a greatest fixpoint over the jp call graph: start Borrowed, demote on evidence, so self tail loops keep Borrowed). Consumers: codegenRust maps Borrowed -> pass `&T` (or skip the Rc clone at the call site) = this item's ByRef; codegenC maps Borrowed -> no incref at the call and no decref at the callee's end = tibia's item. Split: tibia drafts the pass plus the C consumer (already in their queue, with my rule notes sent 11-02 17:30); this item takes the Rust consumer on top of it. DoD unchanged (Rc field clones 497 -> < 250, rustc -O on every row, C agree).")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_15 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 15u32, "2026-11-03T00:12Z", "compiler", "11-02 20:15 compiler: NEXT LEVER, a Rust consumer of tibia's ParamEscape (COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS 1-param-escape-c.kitdo: greatest fixpoint, Borrowed = a YArray/string param that never escapes). In Rust a Borrowed param can be &Rc<..>: the caller passes &vN and saves the Rc clone at the call, and the body reads through auto-deref. The same caller-side demotions apply: a self call reassigns parameters in the loop {} shape, so a Borrowed slot must receive a Borrowed param. BLOCKER found in codegenRust's method emission (14507-14531): mutual tail groups (tail_group, group.Length > 1) pack the params into a GroupN enum variant, so a &Rc param there needs a lifetime on the enum. Slice 1 therefore excludes every method in a tail group of 2 or more (they stay Escapes), plus the C slice's limits (arrays and strings only). Proof: rustc on every Rust row and a clone recount. Start after tibia's C timing settles the analysis.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_16 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 16u32, "2026-11-03T13:01Z", "compiler", "11-03 09:0x compiler: SLICE 1 DRAFTED, the Rust consumer of ParamEscape. Program RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE\\1-rust-borrowed-params.kitdo (14 replacements on pack24 live C1D78AAEB261EB74, all anchors unique, generated from the live text): (1) the ParamEscape section moves above codegenRust unchanged (its deps JoinPointKey, data_free_vars, DeadPureLetElision, codegenMethodKeyArgs all precede it); (2) codegenRust records each method tag's Borrowed indices at build time and rust_borrowed tag i also requires a tail group of 1 (mutual tail groups pack params into GroupN enums, excluded); (3) a Borrowed slot is mut vN: &T in the signature, the caller passes &vN, or vN when it is itself a borrowed param of the current method (current_borrowed, reset for closures since var tags repeat across functions); (4) self-loop reassignment of a Borrowed slot copies the reference (no clone; ParamEscape guarantees a Borrowed source); (5) rustMoveOnLastUse skips vars declared vN: & so a last-use clone never turns into a reference move. Node review compiler-rustborrow running: examples C,Rust -Native A/B vs pack24 plus the .clone() and borrowed-param census in samples main.rs.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_17 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 17u32, "2026-11-03T13:28Z", "compiler", "11-03 09:3x compiler: SLICE 1 REVIEWED on t1101 (candidate core 03E30078B1C87D2A): examples C,Rust -Native 1074 rows vs pack24: candidate Rust build-fail 0 (every Rust row passes rustc), DISAGREE 0; 19 Rust residuals changed; the 21 behavior cells are all three samples whose compile TIMED OUT on the control arm (portable_convert_parse, portable_math_rounding, routes_negative_extra_param; control 5 timeouts vs candidate 0) and that ran and agree on the candidate, i.e. load, not the change. EFFECT SMALL: 36 params become &T, .clone() over 499 samples main.rs 3827 -> 3801 (-0.7%). Cause: ParamEscape tracks only YArray and string params (its C consumer's scope); Rust clones are mostly Rc heap unions and heap/mutable layouts. SLICE 2: paramEscape takes the tracked predicate as a parameter (C keeps array|string), and the Rust consumer tracks every Rc-backed type (heap unions, heap and mutable layouts, arrays, strings), then recount. Ship decision after slice 2.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_18 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 18u32, "2026-11-03T14:08Z", "compiler", "11-03 10:1x compiler: SLICE 2 REVIEWED (program 2-rust-borrow-rc-types.kitdo, 5 replacements on top of 1: paramEscape takes a tracked predicate, C keeps array|string, Rust tracks every Rc type: arrays, strings, heap unions (match scrutinee &**vN when borrowed), Heap/HeapMutable layouts; candidate core 07B01308132ECDDF): examples C,Rust -Native 1074 rows vs pack24: 23 Rust residuals changed, 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE, Rust build-fail 0; C unchanged. Effect: 46 params become &T, .clone() 3827 -> 3794 (-0.9%). CLONE CENSUS (495 sample main.rs on the box, 4170 .clone()): var clones vN.clone() 2490 (60%), thread_local LIT/CASE/CLOSURE caches 1070 (26%), layout field reads .lK.clone() 401 (10%), index reads 209 (5%). So the DoD lever is not parameter passing but MOVES: the text pass rustMoveOnLastUse skips every function containing a loop, and var never consults codegenRust's own live_after liveness. NEXT SLICE: in var, emit a move when the bind's liveness says the var is dead afterwards (typed, inside loops too; self-loop back edges keep params live), retire the text pass, recount. Contracts C,Rust A/B of slices 1+2 running (job compiler-rustborrow2c) before a pack25 handover.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_19 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 19u32, "2026-11-03T14:17Z", "compiler", "11-03 10:2x compiler: slices 1+2 HANDED TO MAIN as pack25 (dry run reproduces source core 38A0B06D8D91276D from live pack24; R2R SpiralCompiler.dll 6B30D2F521660878, core 07B01308132ECDDF). Contracts C,Rust vs a PINNED pack24 copy (fiscal rule, core 21D5992E at both ends): 0 behavior cells, 1 Rust residual. Item stays Active for the DoD lever (liveness moves in loop functions).")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_20 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 20u32, "2026-11-03T14:25Z", "compiler", "11-03 10:2x compiler: SLICE 3 DRAFTED, the DoD lever. Program 3-rust-moves-by-liveness.kitdo (6 replacements on pack25 source 38A0B06D8D91276D): var moves (emits vN, no .clone()) when codegenRust's own live_after says vN is dead after the current bind, under four guards: never a borrowed &-param, never a var that occurs more than once in the same bind (direct_counts), never inside a while condition (re-evaluated every iteration; allow_moves off while emitting it), and only where the bind's liveness is KNOWN (a bind without a live_after entry, e.g. a group-loop body, keeps cloning; macro splicing keeps its existing semantics). var/args/show_w move below current_live, which they now read. Node review compiler-rustmove3 running: examples and contracts C,Rust -Native vs a pinned pack25 candidate copy, plus the clone count.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_21 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 21u32, "2026-11-03T14:49Z", "compiler", "11-03 10:4x compiler: SLICE 3 FIRST REVIEW (candidate core 48BF8C8EA91BF78E vs pinned pack25 candidate 07B01308, stable at both ends): .clone() over 499 samples main.rs 3405 -> 3117 (-8.5% on top of pack25), contracts clean, DISAGREE 0, but 8 Rust rows failed rustc E0382 (use after move). Cause, the same in every case read: one var twice in one data value, e.g. (v3, v3), Heap0 { l0: v0, l1: v0 }, UH0_Node(v1, v0, v0): direct_counts used data_free_vars, which DEDUPLICATES, so the count was 1 and both positions moved. FIX: count per position with data_term_vars. Rerun queued (job compiler-rustmove3b).")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_22 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 22u32, "2026-11-03T15:12Z", "compiler", "11-03 11:1x compiler: SLICE 3 GREEN (fixed program, candidate core 6179E0FCED666D6C vs pinned pack25 candidate 07B01308, stable at both ends): examples C,Rust -Native 1074 rows: 52 Rust residuals changed, Rust build-fail 0, 0 behavior cells, DISAGREE 0; contracts C,Rust: 4 Rust residuals, 0 behavior. .clone() 2914 -> 2642 (-9.3% on top of pack25; whole-folder counts include files from other suites, so the absolute numbers drift between reviews; the per-review A/B is the measure). Ships as pack26 after pack25 installs (its base is pack25 source 38A0B06D8D91276D).")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_23 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 23u32, "2026-11-03T16:26Z", "compiler", "11-03 12:2x compiler: HANDED TO MAIN as pack26 with COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS-EXTERN-CALLS 5 and LINKED-UNITS-ZIG 3 (dry run reproduces source core 5879473DB36BC3C2 from live pack25 38A0B06D; R2R SpiralCompiler.dll 6B30D2F521660878, core 7C49B7A6AFC05D2C). Final binary review vs pinned pack25: examples Rust 52 residuals, 0 behavior; examples Zig only control-arm timeouts; pack25 live on the box (core 07B01308).")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_24 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 24u32, "2026-11-03T16:28Z", "compiler", "11-03 12:3x compiler: pack25 (slices 1+2) INSTALLED on box (core 07B01308132ECDDF verified live), node (in-place swap) and europa (Linux build core 80CA8471A947D7E9), per main. pack26 (slice 3) install chain running.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_25 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 25u32, "2026-11-03T16:48Z", "compiler", "11-03 12:5x compiler: pack26 INSTALLED on box (core 7C49B7A6AFC05D2C verified live), node (in-place swap 12:37) and europa (Linux build core 61A05D193627F8D8), per main. Downstream data point (main): apps/lisp spl2spi.rs 61193 -> 60329 bytes under pack26, output byte-identical on all 3 samples.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_26 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 26u32, "2026-11-03T16:56Z", "compiler", "11-03 13:0x compiler: RUNTIME CHECK of slice 3 (n2's 10-29 concern: a move-heavier build looked slower on mega_brzozowski runtime_bench, deep drop moved into the hot path). Generated runtime_bench Rust on the node with pinned pack25 (core 07B01308) and live pack26 (7C49B7A6): .clone() 265 -> 136 (-49%), 87820 -> 86788 bytes. Bench on quiet europa (load 0.27), rustc -O each, 3 arms x 8 rotated rounds incl. an A/A copy of the pack25 binary: medians pack25 36 ms, pack26 32 ms, A/A 35 ms (ranges 34-43, 28-39, 34-38). No slowdown; pack26 at or below the A/A band. Caveat: on Linux this bench runs ~35 ms, so process start weighs heavily; the deep-drop concern did not show here.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_27 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 27u32, "2026-11-03T18:08Z", "compiler", "11-03 14:0x compiler: SLICE 4 (match by value) READY. Program RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE\\4-rust-match-by-value.kitdo (3 replacements; source 5879473D -> 7D5D4713; candidate core 9AB3D23AF34BAE12, host 6B30D2F521660878 unchanged). A stack-union TyUnionUnbox whose scrutinees are all dead after the match (same test as var moves: allow_moves, known, not live after, not borrowed, counted once) AND unused inside every arm (new unbox_arm_uses, filled by live_op) matches 'match vN' by value and binds every field by move; before, only unions with a moving field did, and they still cloned their non-moving fields. Heap unions unchanged. EVIDENCE (node, control = installed pack26 cache, core 7C49B7A6): examples Rust 515 rows: 120 residual cells, 0 DISAGREE (6 compile cells are control-side timeouts on the loaded node, 7 vs 1); native A/B on the 117 changed rows: rustc + run ok 117/117 on both arms, 0 behavior cells; apps/spiral/spiral.spi -> Rust: .clone() 3543 -> 3198 (-9.7%), arm rebind clones 551 -> 206, match & 439 -> 114, match by value 6 -> 331, bytes 1452341 -> 1447635; cargo check of both spiral.rs in a stage workspace: exit 0, 0 errors, 26 warnings each. Remaining clones: mostly var clones on live values and the Rc literal cache; heap-union field moves (Rc::try_unwrap) not attempted.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_28 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 28u32, "2026-11-03T20:39Z", "compiler", "11-03 16:3x compiler: TWO REGRESSIONS from pack25/26, both fixed and proven, kept as regression samples. (1) E0505, pack27 (INSTALLED 15:06 box, 15:18 node; europa regex 16/16): the text pass rustMoveOnLastUse cut the trailing .clone() of a var that the same call line borrows earlier (f(&v38, .., v38)); program 5-no-move-after-borrow-in-line keeps the clone when an earlier occurrence on the line is &/&mut/&*. Sample rust_borrow_and_move_same_arg. Hit regex certificate.spi and the x client. (2) E0507, pack28 (handed over 16:3x, install chain running): slice-3 liveness moved a var captured by a TEXTUAL closure (vec_fold' splices the lambda body between '|acc, x| {' macro lines). Program 6-no-moves-in-macro-closures freezes every statement of a body whose macro text opens a closure, so var moves and slice-4 by-value matches are off there, and splice clones stay exact (a first cut that skipped liveness also dropped splice clones and broke rust_macro_reuse_clone with E0382; caught by the native A/B). Sample rust_capture_in_macro_closure (Rust-only matrix row). Acceptance: kit_log_counts counts.rs:408 and kit_log_sections sections.rs:139 cargo check E0507 on pack27 -> exit 0 on pack28. LESSON (memory rust-move-packs-need-downstream-rustc): examples lacked both shapes; Rust move packs now need a downstream rustc A/B (regex certificate, apps/spiral cargo check, kit crates) before handover. Slice 4 rides in pack29 on top of these.")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_29 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 29u32, "2026-11-03T21:58Z", "compiler", "11-03 18:0x compiler: pack29 (slice 4 + LINKED-UNITS-ZIG 4/5 + NO-SUFFIX) accepted by main and live on the box (source 0EE5F81C). pack30 READY (handed over 17:5x): program 7-freeze-only-closure-regions narrows the E0507 freeze from the whole body to the brace-matched textual-closure region (opener macro through the macro that closes its braces; the whole body only when a region doesn't close in its block). apps/spiral .clone() 4398 -> 3201 (-27%), cargo check 0 errors; E0507 sample still fixed; certificate 16/16; examples 0 residual-changed rows; kit_log_counts/sections cargo check 0 errors. Clone path today: pack26 3543 (with the two bugs) -> pack28 4659 (hotfixes) -> pack29 4398 (slice 4) -> pack30 3201. Remaining: 1745 literal-cache clones (cheap Rc), arg clones of live vars, 41 heap-union matches (Rc::try_unwrap not attempted).")
inl note_of_rust_move_on_last_use_30 () = Note ("RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE", 30u32, "2026-11-03T23:03Z", "compiler", "11-03 19:0x compiler: pack29 (slice 4) and pack30 (freeze only closure regions) INSTALLED everywhere; main's downstream re-gates green on europa after each (pack30: regex 85/85 over 13 notebooks, html tree 5/5 + pages 5/5 + fast build HTML-OK, kernel 30/30 over 8 modules). Net on apps/spiral: .clone() 3543 (pack26) -> 3201 (pack30) with the two pack26 bugs fixed, 234 by-value matches. DoD: moves on last use plus by-value matches are live and proven downstream. Left as a separate lever: heap-union field moves (41 'match &*v', Rc::try_unwrap).")
inl note_of_kit_ci_parity_1 () = Note ("KIT-CI-PARITY", 1u32, "2026-10-22T22:00Z", "eoie-lane", "18:00 filed from x's candidate (EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES note, 14:28, memory ci-parity-toolchains) plus my own evidence today: eoie CI moved to nightly-2026-10-18 while this laptop's default is nightly-2025-05-09, so a local test-dev-workflow passed while CI was red. The cause (cargo's build-dir layout) only showed after pinning RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN to CI's channel (EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW). x saw the same with zig-dev 0.18 against 0.17.0. Pure model drafted (scratch f5/src/eoie_ci_parity_model: setup_action context per 'uses:' line, typed ci_tool keys, workflow_pins, then parity SameVersion | Differs ci local | MissingLocally; tests on the eoie workflow against today's laptop pins). Standards 0 findings; Cells preview after F (no compile beside F). Generic: no repo names in the code, any workflow that uses those actions.")
inl note_of_kit_ci_parity_2 () = Note ("KIT-CI-PARITY", 2u32, "2026-10-23T03:03Z", "eoie-lane", "23:03 from main (RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE): bare cargo calls in tracked eoie scripts (compiler-contracts/renew-cold-rebuild.ps1:54, test-dev-workflow.ps1:31, test-selected-build.ps1:14) resolve to the machine default nightly-2025-05-09 (rustc 1.88), while eoie CI pins nightly-2026-10-18, and no repo has a rust-toolchain.toml. That gap is what hid today's CI failure (EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW). Candidate fix: rust-toolchain.toml with channel = \"nightly-2026-10-18\" at the eoie root, so local and CI match. Rule, written before trying it: adopt it if a scratch copy builds eoie-cli and passes test-dev-workflow and the 9 F-candidate notebooks under the pinned toolchain with no new failures, and its debug build time stays within 1.5x of today's. Then the docs' 'Rust 1.88' mentions are updated in the same change. Not tonight: F6 holds the tree lease, and a pin changes every local build.")
inl note_of_kit_ci_parity_3 () = Note ("KIT-CI-PARITY", 3u32, "2026-10-23T14:01Z", "eoie", "10:01 verb drafted (scratch f6/src/eoie_ci_parity: model + parity + entry, HostFree LibReferenced). The model moved from hand-rolled pin lists to core list + listm'.try_find_/listm.map/fold (09:47 check pass on the old shape). Added: version_from_output per tool (rustup active toolchain without the host triple, gleam/rebar3 second word, zig/otp/elixir first line), pin_matches (patch releases match at a dot, never a dev build, Rust channels whole), probes (rustup show active-toolchain, zig or SPIRAL_ZIG, erl OTP_VERSION, elixir --short-version, gleam --version, rebar3 version; .cmd/.bat names tried), workflows joined with a uses: boundary, repeated pins dropped, refusal on no pins. The lib gap went into lib: process.try_capture_combined_list (Rust + Gleam arms, test) in a lib copy, slice ci-parity/apply-lib-try-capture.ps1 (baseline-guarded). Measured on this laptop vs eoie gh-pages.yml: rust nightly-2025-05-09 vs nightly-2026-10-18 differs, zig 0.18.0-dev.146 vs 0.17.0 differs, rebar3 3.28.0 vs 3.27.3 differs, otp 29.1.2, elixir 1.20.4 and gleam 1.19.2 same. Checks (notebooks for model + lib process, standards) are queued for after tibia's live block; then slice ci-parity/apply-ci-parity.ps1 joins the release after L.")
inl note_of_kit_ci_parity_4 () = Note ("KIT-CI-PARITY", 4u32, "2026-10-23T15:58Z", "eoie", "11:58 eoie: x's measured table (EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES note 33) folded in; I had drafted without reading it, now corrected. (1) Rust follows what a harness resolves, not rustc --version: the repo's rust-toolchain.toml or legacy rust-toolchain channel if that toolchain is installed (rustup toolchain list), else missing; no file means rustup's default. A toolchain file also pins CI when the workflow has no setup-rust channel. A Rust mismatch suggests only 'rustup toolchain install <channel>', never a direct-rustc override. (2) Python is a tool (setup-python python-version; python --version). (3) Every mismatched row prints a fix line: for zig, 'fix: SPIRAL_ZIG=<scoop zig matching the pin>'; for others, a 'candidate:' path, both found by scanning ~/scoop/apps/<tool>*/<version>/<tool>.exe with the same dot-boundary match. Tests added for toolchain-file parsing, installed lists, rust_local, the python pin and fix lines. Model and parity notebooks pass (11:52) on the f6 lib copy; ci parity remains in release M.")
inl note_of_kit_ci_parity_5 () = Note ("KIT-CI-PARITY", 5u32, "2026-10-23T16:41Z", "eoie", "12:41 eoie: lib gate on the lib-gaps copy (lib_gate.ps1, process compiler, own MIX_BUILD_PATH; job-1792770026831): am' 12/12, testing 8/8, env current_executable passes, sm' concat_array and growable tests pass; process.try_capture_combined_list passes on Rust, but its Gleam arm crashed with Erlang badarg on the third call (after a missing program and a failing exit both passed), the successful stderr_to_stdout capture, while the existing capture_combined_list passes the same case. eoie needs only Rust, so the Gleam arm and the test's gleam tag are dropped from the slice (lib already has Rust-only functions); a Gleam port is a follow-up. Reds in untouched tests (sm' format_fixed_avoids_ties zig, show_floats delphi/lean, range typescript; env get_environment_variable typescript) are being baselined on the tree lib (main has no recorded baseline; job-1792773558090), since the landing must not hide new reds.")
inl note_of_kit_ci_parity_6 () = Note ("KIT-CI-PARITY", 6u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): TOOL-2 (toolchain identity and probes per capability). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
