inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_1 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:20Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:20 x: found by a read-only review (no x edits tonight: the tree is staged for the user's commit). Candidates, with x's call sites (44 lines use them): keep -> listm'.filter, spread -> listm'.collect, first_where -> listm'.try_find, each -> listm.map. split_on/pieces (prelude.spi:40-45) and split_all (glyphs.spi:110-134, a hand-written Erlang binary:split binding for Gleam) -> sm'.split, which already covers Gleam, Rust, Zig, C, Lua and more, plus a list conversion. LATENT BUG in split_on: after a match it skips i + 1 characters, which is right only for 1-character separators (every caller passes 1 character today); it is also the cut-the-remainder O(n^2) shape that hurt Gleam on 10-21. Lib gaps that go into lib/spiral first: backend-neutral result combinators (then_ok/map_ok; resultm is Rust-shaped: anyhow_error, rc_try_unwrap), take_first, and digit parsing (count_of_text). Cautions: listm'.collect folds left with ++ (quadratic) where x's spread is a linear foldBack; try_find uses 'inl rec'. Benchmark Gleam notebook time before and after, keep each helper's test block, and run the full x notebook stage on CI's zig. Start only after the user's commit and a green CI.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_2 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T03:26Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:26 x: more string helpers with lib equivalents, same review (read-only). world.spi:264 padded width n = sm'.pad_left width '0' (sm'.obj_to_string n), exactly. splitter.spi:156's thousandths pad ('00'/'0'/'') = sm'.pad_left 3 '0'. wire.spi:115 trimmed_base (one trailing '/') ~ sm'.trim_end_suffix '/', which strips every trailing copy ('a//' gives 'a', x's gives 'a/'). For a base URL the lib behaviour is the better one, but it is a behaviour change, so add a test block for 'https://h//'. wire.spi:168 joined and xc.spi:141 joined_docs are already thin wrappers over sm'.concat_list; keep them only if the name says more than the call.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_3 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 3u32, "2026-10-23T03:31Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:31 x: one more lib gap, JSON string unescaping. events.spi:160-175 hand-decodes JSON escapes for the sim's event streams (its 6 users: world, scenario, schedule, personas, invariants, final_state). Each \\uXXXX becomes one char_of, so a UTF-16 surrogate pair (an emoji written ASCII-safe, e.g. \\ud83d\\ude00, which is Python json.dumps' default and e2e's stub is Python) decodes into two lone surrogates. lib/spiral/json_scan.spi:78-98 is weaker still: its unescape always advances two characters, so \\u is not decoded at all. Today's producers (Elixir Jason) write non-ASCII raw and e2e's world.json is pure ASCII, so nothing hits it yet. Order: add a JSON string decoder to the lib with \\uXXXX and surrogate pairs (test blocks: BMP, a pair, a lone high surrogate), then point events.spi at it. The client crates read X's API through serde_json, which already handles pairs.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_4 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 4u32, "2026-10-23T03:33Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:33 x: correction and measurement for note 3. The sim writes its logs with Elixir's built-in JSON module (apps/sim/lib/xsim/log.ex and json.ex: JSON.encode!), not Jason. Measured on elixir 1.20.4 / OTP 29: JSON.encode! of U+65E5, U+1F600 and U+2028 writes them raw (output code units 0022 65E5 D83D DE00 2028 0061 0022, no backslash-u). So the sim's own streams never contain escapes, and events.spi's surrogate gap stays latent until a producer writes ASCII-safe JSON.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_5 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 5u32, "2026-10-23T04:31Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:31 x: correction to note 1 plus a draft. resultm.map (resultm.spi:91) is a plain match on the core result, so it IS backend-neutral and replaces map_ok directly. Only a bind is missing (x's then_ok), so 'resultm is Rust-shaped' was too broad: anyhow_error and rc_try_unwrap are other functions in the same module. The draft at %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE.md lists 8 direct swaps (filter, try_find, exists', listm.map, resultm.map, pad_left x2, trim_end_suffix), 3 lib additions (resultm.bind, listm'.take, sm'.split_list returning a core list; am'.to_list_base' only reaches list'), keeps spread until collect stops being quadratic, and gives the order lib, then x, then the full pre-commit.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_6 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 6u32, "2026-10-23T11:08Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:08 x: grooming: linked to X-GENERIC-TO-LIB (the user's lib-reuse direction, 10-23). This item swaps x's helpers for lib functions that already exist; X-GENERIC-TO-LIB moves x's generic code into the lib. Do this one first, since it is smaller and its lib additions (bind, take, split_list) are the start of the other.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_7 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 7u32, "2026-10-23T14:08Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:08 x: the lib additions from notes 1 and 5 now exist as code: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\lib_gaps.spi (take, bind, split_list with the whole separator width, plus decimal_i32 for count_of_text), pure Spiral with 4 test blocks, queued for Cells rust + gleam after tibia's block, then handed to main for lib/spiral. X-GENERIC-TO-LIB notes 4-5 point here. Lesson recorded: read the related items before auditing. Today's re-audit repeated this item's findings.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_8 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 8u32, "2026-10-23T14:10Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:10 x: note 3's JSON gap now has code: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\json_text.spi (on top of the unicode.spi draft's char_of and code_point_count), queued for Cells rust + gleam after tibia's block. json_unescaped body decodes the short escapes (n t r b f, quote, backslash, slash) and \\uXXXX in either hex case. A high + low surrogate pair becomes ONE astral code point; a lone high or low surrogate becomes U+FFFD instead of a broken char (Rust's char::from_u32 has no surrogates, and the old path silently gave ''). Malformed \\u and a trailing backslash are kept as written. Reads near the end are guarded (substring past the end can panic on Rust). 5 tests: basic escapes, BMP both cases, a pair (3 code points for x+emoji+y), lone surrogates, broken hex. Order: lib json_scan.unescaped (which advances 2 characters per escape, so \\u is never decoded) and x's events.spi string_from both call it, after main lands unicode + json_text.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_9 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 9u32, "2026-10-23T14:12Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:12 x: CORRECTION to the swap list in notes 1 and 5. Reading lib listm'.spi: try_find (line 174) is a bare 'inl rec' loop with no runtime path, so on a runtime list (every x call site: posts, frames, wanted rows) the partial evaluator unrolls without end. That is the same failure mode as scheduler_parity's 300 s timeout this morning, derived from the code, not run. exists' (line 334, note the prime) has the var_is -> join-point path and is safe. So the safe swaps today are: each = listm.map (core, foldBack like x's own), keep = listm'.filter (foldBack), has = listm'.exists', map_ok = resultm.map. first_where -> try_find must wait for a lib fix that gives try_find exists'' runtime path. That fix is drafted as %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\find_runtime.spi (try_find_any, exists''s pattern verbatim, with a runtime-list test and a static-list test), queued for Cells rust + gleam after tibia's block, then handed to main.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_10 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 10u32, "2026-10-23T14:13Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:13 x: step 1 drafted as a no-rename delegation: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_prelude_delegation.ps1 turns prelude's each, keep, has and map_ok into one-line calls to listm.map, listm'.filter, listm'.exists' and resultm.map. Call sites stay unchanged, so the whole client is the test of 'same semantics'. Applied cleanly to a copy (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-prelude\\client); Cells on prelude, splitter and invariants (the heaviest users) is queued for after tibia's block. Step 2, after X-DONE: rename call sites helper by helper and delete the wrappers. first_where waits for the try_find runtime fix (note 9); take_first / then_ok / pieces for lib_gaps; spread for a linear collect, now drafted as lib_gaps.collect_linear (foldBack prepending each piece, as x's spread does, instead of collect's quadratic left fold of (++)), with an order test.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_11 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 11u32, "2026-10-23T14:22Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:22 x: CORRECTION to note 9. lib already has the runtime form: listm'.try_find_ (trailing underscore = let rec runtime variant; the plain name is the compile-time inl rec form). So first_where -> listm'.try_find_ can join step 1's delegation today. No lib fix is needed for it; LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS was reframed accordingly.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_12 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 12u32, "2026-10-23T15:57Z", "x", "2026-10-23 11:57 x: step 1 checked on prelude itself. Cells on the delegated prelude.spi (each/keep/first_where/has/map_ok -> listm.map, listm'.filter, listm'.try_find_, listm'.exists', resultm.map) PASSED (eoie job x-prelude-prelude, exit 0). splitter and invariants (the heavy users) were refused by a foreign Cells run and are re-queued.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_13 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 13u32, "2026-10-23T16:29Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:29 x: step 1 VERIFIED. With each/keep/first_where/has/map_ok delegating to listm.map, listm'.filter, listm'.try_find_, listm'.exists' and resultm.map, Cells passes prelude, splitter 7/7 and invariants 9/9 (rust + gleam, the heaviest helper users) on %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-prelude\\client. The lib functions behave exactly like x's own helpers there. apply_prelude_delegation.ps1 is ready to land after X-DONE.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_14 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 14u32, "2026-10-23T21:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:57 main: x handed off the draft json_text.spi (\\uXXXX unescape, surrogate pairs, lone -> U+FFFD; 5/5 PASS) in %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft for main to land. Landing rule: Cells gate on the tree-lib baseline subset rule, after the tonight chain; rebase over eoie's 17:56 lib-gaps land (sm', am', process, testing, env changed) first, since x's lib_gaps.spi also touches listm'/sm'.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_15 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 15u32, "2026-10-23T22:36Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:36 main: x's lib_gaps.spi is drafted for landing as %TEMP%\\lib-gaps-x\\apply.ps1 (anchored, -Lib; dry run clean on the tree after eoie's 17:56 lib-gaps land). The scan found no clash for take, bind, split_list, decimal_i32 or their helpers. listm'.collect: its body (fold (++) over the mapped lists, quadratic) becomes x's linear foldBack-append with the same order; anchored once. listm'.take, resultm.bind and sm'.split_list go at the ends of their files with x's tests. decimal_i32 lives in sm', not convert, because convert precedes sm' in the lib's module order and the function uses index_of/substring/length. resultm and listm' come before sm', so their tests compare values (Ok 2i32 and Error 'odd'; the list 1,10,2,20,3,30) instead of using sm' string helpers. No new line is over 120 characters. Gate: the generic %TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit\\gate.ps1 -Name LIB-GAPS-X -Files listm'.spi resultm.spi sm'.spi. It applies to a fresh tree-lib copy, gates each file, and on a red reruns that file on the unchanged tree lib. GREEN only if every candidate red (Test/Target) is red in the baseline too; for sm' that is show_floats and replace_regex, per eoie. Land: lib-gate-kit\\land.ps1 runs only on GREEN with no Cells erl in flight. Queued in requeue-after-block.ps1 (chain A) for tibia's 'block end'.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_16 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 16u32, "2026-10-23T22:42Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:42 main: json_text.spi is not landed as its own module. The lib's json decode already unescapes in json_scan, at runtime too (json_real emits runtime calls), so a second unescaper would be two records of one fact. x's algorithm and its 5 cases move into json_scan's missing \\u branch as JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES, which is blocked on one C probe after tibia's block for a code-point-to-text C arm.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_17 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 17u32, "2026-10-23T23:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:08 main: correction to note 15. x's take is a runtime let rec loop, so by the listm' convention it lands as listm'.take_ (plain names are the static inl rec forms). x's call sites need take -> take_. collect keeps its plain name: core fold and foldBack switch on var_is, so the linear foldBack body works on static and runtime lists alike.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_18 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 18u32, "2026-10-23T23:33Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:33 x: step 2 DRAFTED against main's final lib names (main 19:1x): %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_prelude_lib_step2.ps1 -Client <apps/client> -Lib <lib/spiral>. It applies after step 1 (apply_prelude_delegation.ps1) as a no-rename delegation, so call sites stay: spread -> listm'.collect (main's land makes it the same linear foldBack), take_first -> listm'.take_, then_ok -> resultm.bind, split_on -> sm'.split_list (whole-separator width, which fixes the i+1 bug; every x separator is 1 character, so no caller changes). It KEEPS count_of_text: its cap (refuse once n > 100000000, so about 1e9) differs from sm'.decimal_i32's int32 bound, which would widen CLI/API number parsing. Preconditions refuse with no writes until take_, split_list, bind and the linear collect exist in -Lib. Trialled on %TEMP%\\x-prelude2\\client (step-1 copy, packageDir pointed at %TEMP%\\x-libcopy2, which carries main's lib-gaps-x + x's landing patch): applied cleanly. Its notebooks (prelude, splitter, invariants, all with zig targets too) are at the end of the after-block queue. The lib bodies are pure Spiral over the primitives the old helpers used, so zig should hold.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_19 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 19u32, "2026-10-24T11:22Z", "x", "10-24 07:22 x: step 1 is in HEAD 6ef9c48, but 2 of its delegations failed x-compat as autolinks: 'each = listm.map f l' and 'map_ok = resultm.map f r' (.map is a TLD, so X would turn them into 23-char t.co links). At 07:05 both went back to their pre-delegation bodies (foldBack/Cons and then_ok (f >> Ok)), unstaged. Step 2 rule: a delegation whose qualified name ends in a TLD-like segment (.map, .app, .dev, .io ...) must be reached without the dotted spelling, e.g. a lib alias such as map_list/map_result or an 'open' plus the bare name. Run x-compat before the compile, not after.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_20 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 20u32, "2026-10-24T23:46Z", "x", "10-24 19:46 x: grooming. All 4 step-2 preconditions are now in the LIVE lib: listm'.take_ (listm'.spi:793), listm'.collect (:21), sm'.split_list (sm'.spi:2463, let rec, with test split_list_uses_the_whole_separator), resultm.bind (resultm.spi:538). So apply_prelude_lib_step2.ps1 is unblocked on the lib side. Order: re-anchor it on the real tree (07:05 restored the each/map_ok bodies, which step 2 doesn't touch), then x-compat first (n-latest rule: no TLD-like dotted names), Cells on prelude/splitter/invariants, and land after X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS' verify-landing5, so one verify covers both. main respells sm'/listm' to edition 3 next; if that lands first, recheck these 4 names before applying.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_21 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 21u32, "2026-10-27T11:54Z", "x", "2026-10-27 07:54 x: groomed to a light, pullable next step. Dry run of %TEMP%\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_prelude_lib_step2.ps1 on a copy of today's tree refuses 'listm'.spi has no take_ yet': take_ exists, but lib is now spelled in edition 3, so the draft's pre-respell precondition no longer matches, and its replacement text is old syntax too. Next (CPU-light): re-spell the draft to edition 3, re-anchor it on the live tree, keep the 10-24 rule (no TLD-like dotted names such as .map; check x-compat cells before any compile), and apply it into the tree unstaged per the WIP rule; X-WIP-VERIFY then proves it. Today also moved split_on onto lib's sm'.split_list (X-SPLIT-ON-NO-ARRAY). The dep on the X-DONE epic is dropped (an epic depends on its children, not the reverse).")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_22 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 22u32, "2026-10-27T11:59Z", "x", "2026-10-27 07:59 x: step 2 applied in the tree (unstaged), in edition 3: spread -> listm'.collect, take_first -> listm'.take_, then_ok -> resultm.bind, split_on -> sm'.split_list. The lib bodies are the same algorithms (collect = foldBack append, take_ = a :~ ^ taken join point, bind = the same match). None of collect, take_ or bind is a TLD (checked against x/postability/tlds.txt; .map is one, so each and map_ok keep their own bodies per the 10-24 rule). count_of_text stays (its cap differs from sm'.decimal_i32). The prelude tests (take_first, then_ok, keep/first_where) still cover the delegations. Proof: the next X-WIP-VERIFY.")
inl note_of_x_lib_first_prelude_23 () = Note ("X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE", 23u32, "2026-10-28T10:48Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:39 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_1 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:22Z", "tibia-lane", "Observed 2026-10-22 23:16 by tibia-lane: eoie window list showed win-1792725191752-128236-live-gui waiting (requested 23:13) and a newer win-1792725371521-11552-heavy-build HELD for a job still queued (x-run8-night). The held heavy window then blocks the older live request, so first-come ordering breaks for live blocks against heavy builds. Expected: the newer heavy-build stays waiting until the older live-gui request is granted and released. Reproduce with window request live-gui (waiting behind a held heavy-build), then window request heavy-build from another job; the second should show waiting.")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_2 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T03:24Z", "eoie", "23:24 eoie: reading of the 23:16 observation. This is the backfill rule, not a missing clash. eoie_window_admission/admission.spi:25 lets a newer request through when it ends no later than the latest-ending held window (held_until_latest), and window_model.spi:122 makes a waiting exclusive row block only when that backfill is false (windows_cli test a_short_window_backfills_while_an_exclusive_request_waits_on_long_holders). x-run8-night asked for a window that ended inside main's next5b (149 min left), so it could not delay the live block on the declared ends. Two real costs remain. (1) Holders that end early: if next5b ends early, the backfilled window keeps the live block waiting until its own end (EASY backfilling has the same trade-off, since it reserves on estimates). (2) A window HELD for a job still queued for a heavy slot occupies the reservation without running. Rule, before measuring: keep backfill; fix (2) by making a heavy job request its window only when its slot is granted (or count a queued job's window as Waiting). Fix (1) only if the window log shows a live block delayed by more than 15 min past a holder's actual release, measured over the next 3 nights from window files (start, until, release time).")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_3 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 3u32, "2026-10-23T03:26Z", "eoie", "23:26 eoie: cost (2) checked in the tree. eoie_job_gate job_window_gate runs the slot gate first (eoie_job_slot: ticket, first live ticket, free mutex) and only then windowed_gate HeavyBuild, so with F installed a heavy job requests its window at slot grant. main's next5b job folder shows it too: slot and window both written at 22:50:32. The 23:16 held-window-for-a-queued-job came from the installed 10-21 binary. Close condition for (2): after F installs, no window file whose job's slot file says waiting. (1) keeps its 3-night measurement rule.")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_4 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 4u32, "2026-10-23T03:46Z", "eoie", "23:46 eoie: tibia's second finding (23:35) confirmed in the tree. windows.spi requested rewrote its Waiting row every 2 s and eoie_job_gate gate_loop every 5 s, so 'window release' on a waiting request returned ok and the row came back on the next pass. Fixed in a draft for release H (scratch tree-read/apply-window-release-wait.ps1, chained into apply-rH after claim-live-rows; eoie_job_gate added to H's regen filter). Release on a waiting row leaves <id>.released. Both loops read it through a typed wait_step union in window_model (Granted | ReleasedWhileWaiting | WaitExpired | KeepWaiting; a release wins over a grant in the same pass; test block), remove their row and the mark, and end: request prints 'released while waiting' (exit 1); a job exits 75, never started. Expiry pruning leaves no mark, so a stalled requester is not taken for released. The whole GE2+H chain dry-runs clean on a src copy. Still to do: Cells check of window_model's test block and an e2e probe against H's candidate binary (background request --wait behind a planted live-gui row, release, expect 'released' within 3 s).")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_5 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 5u32, "2026-10-23T05:59Z", "eoie", "01:59 eoie: correction to note 2's measurement rule. It can't be measured as written: a release deletes the window file and nothing keeps window history, so 'a live block delayed by more than 15 min past a holder's actual release' leaves no trace. Prerequisite: an append-only window event log under the windows root, one line per grant, release (with 'while waiting'), expiry and orphan prune: time, id, class, label. lib/spiral file_system has no append (grep: none; positive control write_all_text: 12 hits), so a generic file_system.append_text goes into the lib first (Rust/F#/TS, test block), and windows.spi appends through it. The 3-night clock starts when that log is installed, not tonight.")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_6 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 6u32, "2026-10-23T06:03Z", "eoie", "02:03 eoie: event log drafted (scratch window-events/apply-window-events.ps1, chained last in apply-rK; dry run clean on the post-K tree). A typed window_event union (queued, granted, released, released-waiting, gave-up, pruned, dropped) with one word each and a test; event_line writes 'ms TAB event TAB row'; windows.spi logs at all seven places that change window state, and the job gate logs its grants. The log goes through lib file_system.append_all_text (scratch job-env/apply-lib-append.ps1, gated and landed with the env lib parts). windows_cli test every_grant_and_release_lands_in_the_window_event_log. Since it's TSV, the 3-night measurement is an eoie table query, not a script.")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_7 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 7u32, "2026-10-23T08:39Z", "eoie", "04:39 probe for close condition (2) written: scratch window-slot-order.ps1 (read-only; job folders started after -Since with both a slot and a window file; violation = window 'granted ...' while slot 'waiting ...'). Controls on a fixture home: a violating job -> exit 1 and named, a ready-slot job -> exit 0, a job older than -Since skipped. Baseline on the live home since the 10-21 15:38 install: 17 of 87 heavy jobs held a granted window while their slot waited (main, x, tibia and eoie jobs alike, incl. x-run8-night from the 23:16 observation), which is the installed binary's window-before-slot order counted from real data. Close rule for (2): after F9 installs, the probe run with -Since <install time> reports 0 once at least 3 heavy jobs have started on the new binary (GE2 is the first). Run points: report time 06:5x and GE2 start.")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_8 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 8u32, "2026-10-23T09:49Z", "eoie", "05:49 close rule for cost (2) met: window-slot-order.ps1 since the 05:06:57 install found 6 heavy/compile jobs with both files and 0 that held a granted window while their slot waited (baseline on the 10-21 binary: 17 of 87). Cost (1), early-ending holders under backfill, keeps its 3-night measurement, which starts when K's window event log installs; the item stays open for it.")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_9 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 9u32, "2026-10-23T13:10Z", "eoie", "09:09 validity check of the 17-of-87 baseline after EOIE-SLOT-FILE-SETTLED showed 28 settled jobs still saying 'waiting': those rows are jobs that settled WHILE waiting, so they are genuine window-before-slot holds, not phantoms. Example: release F (job-1792683021150) got its window granted at 11:30:21, and its slot file still said waiting at 11:33:40 when it was stopped (11:33:42); it held a granted window for 3+ minutes without a slot. The baseline stands.")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_10 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 10u32, "2026-10-23T22:04Z", "eoie", "18:04 eoie, corrected 18:10: a candidate instance of cost (1), an early-ending holder under backfill. 17:59-18:02: GE2's release held heavy-build with 228 min left, and main-dib-suite (heavy-build) and x-world-cells (compile) were granted as backfill inside GE2's window while tibia's live-gui request (win-1792792857265, 18:01) waited. At 18:02 I stopped GE2 to yield to tibia's time-boxed block. Measured result: both backfilled windows ended within about a minute (main-dib-suite finished exit 0), and the live-gui was granted at 18:03, so the cost was about 1 min, not the 61 min I first estimated from their declared lengths. Lesson for the measurement: use actual window ends, not declared minutes (the K event log records the real ones). The rule is unchanged.")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_11 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 11u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 30m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D2 at 60%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_12 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 12u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_13 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 13u32, "2026-10-31T23:49Z", "kit", "10-31 19:5x kit, pause re-decision (main pre-night groom; R3 is now \"never auto-pause for silence\"): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the 10-28 silence groom (\"nothing queued\"), not by a blocker, and no note names an external condition. Back in agile next at its priority; its first step stays the one in the 10-28 note: check whether the remaining milestone shipped in a later train (installed kit 4983F9C0, TRAIN6) and close it with that evidence, else do the next milestone.")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_14 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 14u32, "2026-11-04T20:25Z", "kit", "11-04 21:3x kit: the RULE already holds in code (kit_machine_windows/window_model.spi blocks: a Waiting row blocks when it is exclusive|quiet|compile-hold, older than the asker, clashes with the wanted class, and the asker does not backfill; LiveGui is exclusive and clashes with everything), but the test only proved it for a newer Compile. Added to an_older_exclusive_waiter_keeps_newcomers_out: `row_blocks HeavyBuild \"\" \"win-3\" 2000u64 !- waiting` (older waiting live-gui) -> true, \"a newer heavy build waits behind the live request\" (unstaged). Box notebook kit-window-live-tests; Done when it passes.")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_15 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 15u32, "2026-11-04T20:34Z", "kit", "11-04 21:5x kit: DONE. Box notebook kit-window-live-tests: window_model.spi 13/13 pass, including the new expectation in an_older_exclusive_waiter_keeps_newcomers_out that an older WAITING live-gui request blocks a newer heavy-build request (row_blocks HeavyBuild ... waiting = true), beside the existing compile case. The rule itself was already in blocks (exclusive waiting row, older, clashing, no backfill); this closes the test gap the title named.")
inl note_of_kit_window_live_queue_16 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE", 16u32, "2026-11-06T11:33Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_x_invariants_row_kind_1 () = Note ("X-INVARIANTS-ROW-KIND", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:28Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:28 x: found by a read-only review. invariants.spi:206-221 keeps the snapshot row kind as a string through read_row -> plain_row -> marked_row -> tally_row, each an if/elif chain on string literals with an else fallback. tally_row's final else is add_evicted, so ANY unrecognized kind (a typo, or a new row the Elixir side starts writing) is counted as an eviction, and the invariants that read evicted counts would judge wrong data without an error. Today the producer (apps/sim/lib/xsim/invariants.ex:19-40) emits exactly visible, broken, start, cancelled, evicted, evicting, wanted and result, all handled, so the bug is latent. Fix by domain modeling: union row_kind = Visible | Broken | Start | Cancelled | Evicted | Evicting | Wanted | Result, one parse function at the boundary returning option row_kind, an exhaustive match in read_row, and an unknown kind making the reading fail (result) rather than being folded in. Test blocks: each of the 8 kinds plus an unknown one. Start after the user's commit and a green CI (X-DONE).")
inl note_of_x_invariants_row_kind_2 () = Note ("X-INVARIANTS-ROW-KIND", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:15Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:15 x: a draft fix is at %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-INVARIANTS-ROW-KIND.md (not compiled). Domain: row_kind = Plain : plain_kind (Visible | Broken | Evicting | Wanted | Result) | Tally : tally_kind (Started | Cancelled | Evicted), the eight kinds invariants.ex writes. Literal-pattern parsers plain_kind_of / tally_kind_of / row_kind_of return option, so an unknown kind is None. Exhaustive plain_row / tallied / kind_row matches replace the plain_row / marked_row / tally_row if-chains. reading gains strays, and the three entry points return 'unknown_rows=<kinds>' when any row strayed, so the string comparison fails visibly. Two tests are included. Post weights checked with 'xc post-thread --dry-run': 19 posts, max 264, no cell split (the two-level union came from fitting kind_row into one post, and it is the better model anyway). Open: constructor name clashes (Result, Visible) with opened modules, and _assert_eq on an option of a union.")
inl note_of_x_invariants_row_kind_3 () = Note ("X-INVARIANTS-ROW-KIND", 3u32, "2026-10-23T04:45Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:45 x: the same shape in the client, lower risk. digest/digest.spi absorb_entry dispatches on the bundle entry's 'role' string (primary / lookup / users) with a final else that treats ANY other role as users. The roles are written by x itself (fetch.spi role 'primary', refs.spi entry 'lookup', xc.spi users_entry 'users'), so today a typo is the only way in. Same fix in passing: union bundle_role = Primary | Lookup | Users with an option-returning parser at absorb_entry and an exhaustive match; an unknown role becomes a visible failure line, not a users merge. Writers and reader should share one role_text function so the strings exist once.")
inl note_of_x_invariants_row_kind_4 () = Note ("X-INVARIANTS-ROW-KIND", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:11Z", "x", "2026-10-23 05:11 x: the draft now COMPILES, partly verified. %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_invariants_row_kind.ps1 applies it with asserted anchors. On a scratch copy (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck3, x untouched), simframes/simframes.spi, which opens invariants and exports checked_text, commute_text and breaking_text, compiled to Gleam in 27 s (9,256 lines) under an eoie compile window. 'unknown_rows=' and the kind literals are in the output, so the unions, parsers, exhaustive matches, strays and the three entry points were type-checked. A pre-run name check renamed Plain -> PlainRow (cli.spi:108 has Plain), Tally -> TallyRow, and tallied -> tally_into (final_state.spi:35); Broken was already BrokenId. NOT yet verified: gleam build of the split output, the sim's ExUnit fixtures (byte-exact) and the two new test blocks under Cells. Those come with the first pre-commit after applying. The digest roles (note 3) are not in this script.")
inl note_of_x_invariants_row_kind_5 () = Note ("X-INVARIANTS-ROW-KIND", 5u32, "2026-10-23T13:30Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:30 x: scope note. The typed bridge runner_checks.spi (SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL notes 4-5) hands invariants.spi a snapshot record directly, so the Spiral sim never parses row text. The row-kind union still matters as long as the Elixir runner sends text rows to checked_text (until SIM-IN-SPIRAL's shell lands), so the drafted apply_invariants_row_kind.ps1 stays worth landing. It's small, and checks_parity feeds the Elixir row text through the same parser, so a kind typo now fails loudly there too. Once the Elixir runner is gone, snapshot_read and the row union can be deleted with it, in the same change.")
inl note_of_x_invariants_row_kind_6 () = Note ("X-INVARIANTS-ROW-KIND", 6u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_invariants_row_kind_7 () = Note ("X-INVARIANTS-ROW-KIND", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_invariants_row_kind_8 () = Note ("X-INVARIANTS-ROW-KIND", 8u32, "2026-10-28T10:57Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_x_e2e_non_ascii_1 () = Note ("X-E2E-NON-ASCII", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:31Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:31 x: found by a read-only review. apps/client/e2e/world.json has 0 non-ASCII characters, so no e2e step sends or reads text that X weighs as 2 (CJK, Hangul) or as an astral or ZWJ emoji, although glyphs.spi's unit tests cover those weights. The stub is Python, and json.dumps escapes non-ASCII as \\uXXXX by default (surrogate pairs for emoji), so adding such text also tests xc's JSON decoding of escaped pairs (serde_json side) against what a strict ASCII-safe producer sends. Add posts with each class to world.json, plus one e2e case that posts and reads back a cell whose split point falls next to a double-weight character. Start after the user's commit and a green CI.")
inl note_of_x_e2e_non_ascii_2 () = Note ("X-E2E-NON-ASCII", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:19Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:19 x: precision on note 1. world.json itself is pure ASCII, but the four included sim worlds (sim-gap, sim-lost, sim-small, sim-stress .world.json) do contain non-ASCII: only U+200B (zero-width space, the splitter's link defusing), 232 in total, and thread-oauth1.expected.json has 20 more of them. So e2e does exercise one BMP non-ASCII character end to end, but no CJK, Hangul, combining marks or astral and ZWJ emoji, and no escaped \\u at all. The item stands as written. Draft for the world, next to the existing users (fields id, name, username, posts[id, created_at, text]): one user 'hana' with posts of CJK, Hangul, a combining accent, a ZWJ family emoji and one at exactly 280 weight with double-weight characters at the split point. Then add a README tutorial step 'xc timeline hana' (e2e replays the README) and accept the new outputs with e2e.ps1 -Accept after reviewing them.")
inl note_of_x_e2e_non_ascii_3 () = Note ("X-E2E-NON-ASCII", 3u32, "2026-10-23T04:26Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:26 x: a draft is at %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-E2E-NON-ASCII.md. It adds user 112 'hana' with posts 12001-12005: CJK, Hangul, combining accents (JSON-escaped on purpose), a ZWJ family + skin-tone emoji (escaped surrogate pairs) and a generated 140-CJK post of weight 280, plus a README step 'xc timeline hana' and a thread round-trip case with a double-weight split point. x's weights for those texts were measured with xc post-thread --dry-run: 26, 33, 18, 27, 280. The emoji value differs from twitter-text (16), which became X-EMOJI-WEIGHT.")
inl note_of_x_e2e_non_ascii_4 () = Note ("X-E2E-NON-ASCII", 4u32, "2026-10-23T12:35Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:35 x: scratch verification PASSED (no compile; release xc.exe + Python stub). Draft = %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\e2e_nonascii.ps1: adds user 112 hana to e2e/world.json (posts 12001-12005: CJK, Hangul, decomposed cafe+U+0301 / cre+U+0300, ZWJ family + thumbs-up with skin tone, 140 CJK chars) plus one tutorial step 'xc timeline hana' before the first timeline step. On a fresh copy the -Accept run and then a verify run each had 0 failures; README gains 12 lines. The accepted output keeps the combining marks exactly (0065 0301 / 0065 0300, no NFC) and shows the 140-char CJK post in full. The control copy (x unchanged) passes too. An earlier '4 failures' came from accepting into a copy missing its client .spi files, so it was an artifact of the copy, not an xc bug. Apply = the script's two edits on apps/client/e2e/world.json + README.md once the tree is unstaged, then e2e -Accept and review the README diff.")
inl note_of_x_e2e_non_ascii_5 () = Note ("X-E2E-NON-ASCII", 5u32, "2026-10-24T07:10Z", "x", "10-24 03:10 x review (CPU-free): composes with X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT. hana's 5 world posts (e2e_nonascii.ps1:13-17) are read through the timeline, while the stub's weighted check applies only to POST /2/tweets, so they never meet the 280 rule. Their measured oracle weights (x_weight.py, run 03:10): 26, 33, 18 (NFC composes the two combining accents), 16, and 280 for the 140-CJK post, which sits exactly at X's limit, so it would pass even if posted.")
inl note_of_x_e2e_non_ascii_6 () = Note ("X-E2E-NON-ASCII", 6u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_e2e_non_ascii_7 () = Note ("X-E2E-NON-ASCII", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_e2e_non_ascii_8 () = Note ("X-E2E-NON-ASCII", 8u32, "2026-10-28T10:31Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:29 x groom: deps re-pointed X-DONE -> X-WIP-VERIFY. Its Done condition named a 10-24 verify job that never went green; the e2e steps it needs run in every verify, so it closes on the first green one (now verify25, job-1793174907446), per X-WIP-VERIFY's closure rule.")
inl note_of_x_e2e_non_ascii_9 () = Note ("X-E2E-NON-ASCII", 9u32, "2026-10-28T11:03Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_x_sim_runner_crashes_1 () = Note ("X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:35Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:35 x: found by a read-only review. apps/sim/lib/xsim/runner.ex:134 starts each executor call with Task.async, which links the task to the runner. Nothing in apps/sim traps exits, uses Task.Supervisor.async_nolink or rescues (grep: no trap_exit, async_nolink, rescue or catch), so an executor that raises takes the runner and the mix task down with it. harvest's {task, {:exit, reason}} clause (runner.ex:159-161), which would record %{status: :crash, out: inspect(reason)}, can then never run, and no test covers a crash. It matters for the real-Cells executor (executors.ex:32-49 calls Spiral.Cells.Notebook.evaluate with no rescue): one internal Cells exception aborts a whole scale ladder instead of counting as one crashed key. Fix: Task.Supervisor.async_nolink under a supervisor started by the runner, or wrap the executor call in try/rescue/catch :exit returning the crash map. Add an ExUnit case with an executor that raises on one source and check that the run completes with exactly that key crashed. Minor, same file: drain/4 loops harvest(5) plus reconcile (including :erlang.memory) every 5 ms while tasks run (minutes under Cells). Cheap per loop, but a longer yield (or waiting on the first finished task) is cleaner on a CPU-scarce box. Elixir stays here until SIM-IN-SPIRAL's supervision half lands, so the fix is worth doing now.")
inl note_of_x_sim_runner_crashes_2 () = Note ("X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES", 2u32, "2026-10-23T03:36Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:36 x: confirmed by a probe on elixir 1.20.4 / OTP 29, not only by reading. A process that does Task.async(fn -> raise \"boom\" end) and then Task.yield_many([t], 1000), without trapping exits, dies before yield_many returns (the parent saw no reply within 3 s). So runner.ex's {:exit, reason} branch is unreachable for a raising executor, exactly as described.")
inl note_of_x_sim_runner_crashes_3 () = Note ("X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES", 3u32, "2026-10-23T04:21Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:21 x: a draft fix is at %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES.md (not applied). It guards the executor call inside the task (rescue + catch kind, reason -> %{status: :crash, out: ...}, the same shape harvest already writes), keeps harvest's :exit clause as a backstop, adds an ExUnit case (two liked roots, one raises, so 1 crash and 2 results) and raises drain's yield from 5 to 100 ms. The pattern was proven by a probe on elixir 1.20.4 / OTP 29: two Task.async tasks running the guarded function, one raising and one calling exit(:gone), both returned {:ok, %{status: :crash, ...}}, and the caller survived Task.yield_many. Task.async_nolink was considered and not chosen (it needs a supervisor lifecycle in a pure run/3).")
inl note_of_x_sim_runner_crashes_4 () = Note ("X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:17Z", "x", "2026-10-23 05:17 x: VERIFIED by test, red then green, on a scratch copy of apps/sim (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-simcheck, Cells path made absolute; x untouched). %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_sim_runner_crashes.ps1 applies the guard (anchors asserted: runner.ex:134 Task.async, :146 harvest, :171 drain yield 5 -> 100) and adds test/runner_crash_test.exs: a raising executor gives exactly one crashed key out of 2 results, and an exiting executor gives a crash result. With the fix: 'Result: 2 passed'. On the original runner.ex: 'Result: 0/2 passed', the RuntimeError boom killing the test process, i.e. the bug as filed. Both runs used mix test in an eoie compile window (deps compiled fresh: kino 50, spiral_cells 38, xsim 19 files). Remaining at apply time: the full sim mix test plus apps/sim/build.ps1 fixtures (byte-exact), and a measure of the drain yield change on a sim run.")
inl note_of_x_sim_runner_crashes_5 () = Note ("X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES", 5u32, "2026-10-23T11:08Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:08 x: grooming: this patches Elixir that SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL replaces, and the crash result is already folded into that item's domain. Keep it only as a verified stopgap (red-green tested, ~40 min to apply), because it protects sim runs until the rewrite lands. Drop it if SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL is applied first (user, 10-23: don't invest in Elixir code the rewrite throws away).")
inl note_of_x_sim_runner_crashes_6 () = Note ("X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES", 6u32, "2026-10-23T13:33Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:33 x: Spiral counterpart. In scheduler.spi (SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL) a crash is data: the shell reports a crashed executor as finished_task key {status = Crashed; out = reason}. It is stored and settled like any result, matching runner.ex's harvest of {:exit, reason} into %{status: :crash}. New test a_crashed_task_is_a_visible_crash_result (queued for after tibia's block) pins it: one start, and the visible result is the crash. So the Gleam shell needs only the monitor -> Crashed mapping (LIB-OTP-ACTORS note 9). The Elixir-side draft apply_sim_runner_crashes.ps1 stays for the oracle until then.")
inl note_of_x_sim_runner_crashes_7 () = Note ("X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES", 7u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_sim_runner_crashes_8 () = Note ("X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES", 8u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_sim_runner_crashes_9 () = Note ("X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES", 9u32, "2026-10-28T10:58Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_kit_job_amend_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-AMEND", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:36Z", "eoie", "23:36 eoie: friction tonight. F6 was going to run past its 4 h limit, and F7, queued behind it, had the same limit. No verb changes a queued job's limit, so I raised F7's timeout_ms from 14400000 to 21600000 by writing the job file before its child started (the old value is in timeout_ms.before-amend). eoie_job_supervise reads timeout_ms once at child start (supervise.spi:57), so it takes effect. That is a recorded bypass, not the way to do it: the verb should refuse once the child has started (pid file present), write a receipt, and recompute the heavy window's minutes.")
inl note_of_kit_job_amend_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-AMEND", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:50Z", "eoie", "00:50 eoie: verb drafted against the post-GE2+H tree (scratch job-amend/apply-job-amend.ps1, dry run clean). JobAmend command; eoie_job_control amend_verdict union (AmendAllowed ms | AmendRefused reason). Refused when the job is unknown, the flag isn't --timeout, the value is 0, the job is settled, or its pid file exists (child started: the supervisor already read the limit). Allowed: writes timeout_ms, and when the job has a heavy wrapper it rewrites its -MaxMinutes to match (needs EOIE-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT from H). Receipt 'eoie job amend ok id=.. timeout_ms=.. was=..'. stop_cli test a_queued_job_takes_a_new_limit_until_its_child_starts: amend a job queued --after a running one; refuse the running one, an unknown id and a wrong flag; then the queued job runs to exit 0. Release: J with --words.")
inl note_of_kit_job_amend_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-AMEND", 3u32, "2026-10-23T08:39Z", "eoie", "04:39 evidence for K's typed start-option fold (apply-job-min-free refuses -- labels): the live jobs home holds job-1792725331528---done-within, a heavy job whose label is the flag --done-within, because the fixed-order chain read a misplaced flag as the label. Same class as EOIE-AGILE-ROOT-FLAG (a flag in a positional slot).")
inl note_of_kit_job_amend_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-AMEND", 4u32, "2026-10-23T17:10Z", "eoie", "13:10 eoie: dogfood evidence 10-23: my queued eoie-chain-GE2 (30 min limit) had to start waiting on main's review job (hours), so I stopped it and started a copy with an 8 h limit and the same --after-ok, which cost a new id and a requeue. 'job amend <id> --timeout' (drafted for J) is that one call.")
inl note_of_kit_job_amend_5 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-AMEND", 5u32, "2026-10-28T10:31Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-23, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_kit_job_amend_6 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-AMEND", 6u32, "2026-10-31T23:49Z", "kit", "10-31 19:5x kit, pause re-decision (main pre-night groom; R3 is now \"never auto-pause for silence\"): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the 10-28 silence groom (\"nothing queued\"), not by a blocker, and no note names an external condition. Back in agile next at its priority; its first step stays the one in the 10-28 note: check whether the remaining milestone shipped in a later train (installed kit 4983F9C0, TRAIN6) and close it with that evidence, else do the next milestone.")
inl note_of_kit_job_amend_7 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-AMEND", 7u32, "2026-11-04T20:07Z", "kit", "11-04 20:4x kit: DONE, verified on the INSTALLED kit (TRAIN14c) against live jobs: `kit job amend <queued id> --timeout 7200000` -> \"kit job amend ok id=... timeout_ms=7200000 was=3600000\" and the job's timeout_ms file changed (then amended back to 3600000); a started job is refused (\"job ... has started; its supervisor read the limit when the child started\"); a non-number is refused (\"--timeout needs a positive number of milliseconds\"); an unknown id is refused. Every clause of the title holds.")
inl note_of_kit_job_amend_8 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-AMEND", 8u32, "2026-11-06T11:33Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-JOB-AMEND (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_python_drop_del_1 () = Note ("PYTHON-DROP-DEL", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:44Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:44 main: measured on the tree outputs. The backend emits 'del vN' after each variable's last use. CPython's refcounting frees those values anyway, so del only matters for freeing large arrays early, which is why array programs (cp./cuda, the same test PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT uses) keep theirs. Rule: when the program reaches no arrays, its function and main texts lose every 'del v..' line, and any block left empty gets 'pass'. Mirror python_del_mirror.py: 52,410 -> 42,060 lines. All 40 most-changed programs parse (ast) and run with the original exit and stdout. F# pythonWithoutDels equals the mirror on all 451 main.py (fsi); applied in the else branch of the cupy_prelude decision (%TEMP%\\pack-next6\\python-del.ps1). In PACK-NEXT-4 core F78B667128E027C2; its review checks main.py through locals_check.py's python_rule (cupy block removed and dels dropped when no arrays, unchanged otherwise).")
inl note_of_python_drop_del_2 () = Note ("PYTHON-DROP-DEL", 2u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:17 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 60 by the rule 'implemented and staged in a pack or queued gate, awaiting review'. Where: pack-next6 python-del, reviewed with its pack.")
inl note_of_python_drop_del_3 () = Note ("PYTHON-DROP-DEL", 3u32, "2026-10-28T01:29Z", "main", "10-27 21:29 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (1) SHIPPED. pythonWithoutDels (pack-next6 python-del.ps1) has 2 hits in the tree core.")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_1 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:48Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:48 x: checked read-only against the crates.io API (only crate names sent). Of x's direct dependencies in Cargo.lock: base64 0.23.1, hmac 0.13.0, jiff 0.2.38, serde_json 1.0.154, sha1 0.11.0, unicode-normalization 0.1.26 and unicode-segmentation 1.13.3 are the latest stable. ureq is locked at 3.4.2, and 3.5.0 was released 2026-10-21. Read its changelog first (memory latest-of-everything), then 'cargo update -p ureq', the full x pre-commit and e2e (ureq carries every X API call). Start after the user's commit and a green CI, so the lock change is not mixed into the verified tree.")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_2 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 2u32, "2026-10-23T03:49Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:49 x: P3 -> P2 after reading ureq's CHANGELOG (raw.githubusercontent.com/algesten/ureq/main/CHANGELOG.md). 3.4.3 is a security release ('Update TLS dependencies for security fixes', #1218), and x's lock is at 3.4.2. Every X API call (net.spi) goes over that TLS. 3.5.0 adds 'fix dependency advisories' (#1217), bounds TLS handshake I/O by the remaining global timeout (#1208; x sets timeout_global 30 s at net.spi:25/28, so a stalled handshake is now cut at 30 s), plus proxy changes (x uses no proxy). Its one 'technically breaking' entry is the EINTR retry under timeouts (#1205), harmless for x. Take 3.5.0. Separate latent issue found while reading net.spi:31/34: 'r.body_mut().read_to_string().unwrap_or_default()' turns a body-read error into an empty body with the real status code, and ureq's read_to_string has a size cap (10 MB default, as I recall; check), so an oversized or broken response reads as an empty 200. Map a read error to a distinct result (status 0 plus the error text, as call errors already do), with a negative test.")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_3 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 3u32, "2026-10-23T03:50Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:50 x: precise scope from ureq PR #1218 (GitHub API, read-only). 3.4.3 resolves 'all seven RustSec advisories reported against 3.4.2' by requiring rustls 0.23.46, rustls-webpki 0.103.16 and webpki-roots / webpki-root-certs 1.0.10. x's Cargo.lock has rustls 0.23.46 (ok), rustls-webpki 0.103.15 (below the fix) and webpki-roots 1.0.9 (below). So the security part is two semver-compatible patch bumps, 'cargo update -p rustls-webpki -p webpki-roots', independent of the ureq 3.5.0 bump. Do it first after the commit: then cargo build plus e2e (TLS is only exercised against the live API, e2e's stub is plain HTTP; one capped read-only live smoke, e.g. xc timeline, confirms the handshake).")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_4 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 4u32, "2026-10-23T03:53Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:53 x: Gleam/Hex side (hex.pm API, read-only). The sim's Hex deps are current (kino 0.19.1, table 0.1.2). The Gleam frames package (apps/sim/frames) is a major version behind and inconsistent with Cells: gleam.toml pins gleam_stdlib = \"0.57.0\" exactly and gleam_erlang >= 0.34 and < 1.0 (locked 0.34.0), while the latest are gleam_stdlib 1.0.5 and gleam_erlang 1.3.0. spiral/apps/cells resolves gleam_stdlib 1.0.5 (its manifest.toml), so x's Gleam notebook cells already run on stdlib 1.x, while the same splitter compiled into apps/sim/frames runs on 0.57.0. One generated code base is tested on two stdlib majors. The frames code imports only gleam/bit_array, int, list, order and string, and never gleam/erlang, so gleam_erlang can simply be dropped from gleam.toml. Fix: gleam_stdlib = \">= 1.0.5 and < 2.0.0\" (match Cells), remove gleam_erlang, regenerate manifest.toml, then run apps/sim/build.ps1 (gleam build plus the frames fixtures).")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_5 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 5u32, "2026-10-23T03:54Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:54 x: CI actions (GitHub releases API, read-only). ci.yml is current for erlef/setup-beam v1.24.1, mlugg/setup-zig v2.2.1, moonrepo/setup-rust v1.3.0 and peaceiris/actions-gh-pages v4.1.0. It is a major version behind on actions/checkout v6.1.0 -> v7.0.1, actions/upload-artifact v5.0.0 -> v7.0.2, actions/download-artifact v6.0.0 -> v8.0.2 and actions/setup-python v6.3.0 -> v7.1.0 (released 10-22). The tibia repo already runs checkout v7.0.1, download v8.0.2, upload v7.0.2 and setup-python v7.0.0, so its CI is the proof that the new majors work. Bump in one commit with 'gh actions-lock' regenerating actions.lock (memory actions-lock-on-workflow-edits), read each release's breaking notes first, and keep upload/download on mutually compatible majors (gh-pages artifact hand-off between the build and push jobs). The other i574n repos (alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, eoie, i574n.github, polyglot, spiral) share x's old set; main was told.")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_6 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 6u32, "2026-10-23T03:55Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:55 x: toolchain pins in ci.yml against upstream (GitHub releases, ziglang.org index, endoflife.date). Gleam 1.19.2, OTP 29 (29.1.2), Elixir 1.20 (1.20.4) and zig 0.17.0 are the latest stable. Python is behind: python-version 3.12.10, while the 3.12 line is at 3.12.15 and the newest maintained release is 3.15.1 (EOL 2031). Python runs only e2e's stub, and local e2e passes on 3.15.0, so set python-version to 3.15 (memory latest-of-everything). That also closes the local/CI Python gap from X-DONE note 52. alphabet, dice, spiral, polyglot and i574n.github pin 3.12.10 as well (main's call).")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_7 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 7u32, "2026-10-23T03:57Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:57 x: runner images (actions/runner-images README). windows-2025 is the newest Windows image (now windows-2025-vs2026), so x's Windows pin is current. An ubuntu-26.04 image is listed, but ubuntu-latest still maps to ubuntu-24.04, and the README's Beta section says beta images have no Actions SLA. Its badge only shows an image version, not a status. Rule: x stays on ubuntu-24.04 until GitHub moves ubuntu-latest to 26.04, then bumps with the siblings (main tracks the actions bump as CI-ACTIONS-LATEST and holds it until the user's staged gh-pages.yml commits land).")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_8 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 8u32, "2026-10-23T04:28Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:28 x: the ordered step list (TLS patch bumps first, then ureq 3.5.0, the Gleam frames constraint, the CI actions with siblings plus python 3.15, and runner images unchanged) is in %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-DEPS-LATEST-and-split-guards.md.")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_9 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 9u32, "2026-10-23T14:23Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:23 x: versions re-checked (crates.io API, read-only, crate names only). Unchanged: ureq 3.5.0 (2026-10-21) and rustls-webpki 0.103.16 (2026-10-20); its 0.104.0-alpha.8 is a pre-release, skip it. NEW: webpki-roots 1.0.11, released 2026-10-23 08:25 UTC (1.0.10 was 10-19). The note-3 step 'cargo update -p rustls-webpki -p webpki-roots' therefore lands 1.0.11, which still covers #1218's >= 1.0.10 requirement. After the update, check that Cargo.lock reads webpki-roots 1.0.11 and rustls-webpki 0.103.16.")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_10 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 10u32, "2026-10-23T22:39Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:39 x: steps 1, 2, 2b and 3 of note 8's plan are SCRIPTED (I first drafted them merged without reading notes 1-9; corrected to the plan's order at 18:4x). %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_deps_latest.ps1 -XRoot <x> -Step tls|ureq|rest|frames. tls = cargo update -p rustls-webpki -p webpki-roots (0.103.16, 1.0.11); ureq = workspace floor '3.4' -> '3.5' + cargo update -p ureq (3.5.0, ureq-proto 0.6.5); rest = NEW step 2b, the remaining semver-compatible updates (cc 1.10.0, crc32fast 1.5.3, ctutils 0.4.5, find-msvc-tools 0.1.15, libc 0.2.191); frames = gleam.toml gleam_stdlib '>= 1.0.5 and < 2.0.0' (hex.pm newest 1.0.5, 2026-08-06; Cells resolves the same), gleam_erlang removed (frames imports only bit_array/int/list/order/string), then gleam deps update. Trialled in order on landing-trial-4's x copy: each step moves only its own entries. Caveats: cargo resolved for the local Rust 1.101 nightly, so CI's pinned Rust must be at least as new; the OTP shell, once it lands in frames, re-adds gleam_erlang/gleam_otp at lib's 1.3 pins. Compile checks after tibia's block: cargo check -p xc_net (the only ureq user) and gleam build in apps/sim/frames (generated code vs stdlib 1.0) on that copy. The plan's live TLS call stays a step for whoever lands it (I make no X API calls).")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_11 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 11u32, "2026-10-24T05:06Z", "x", "10-24 01:06 x: VERIFIED (X-DONE n68 step 2, job-1792813103411-x-deps-builds, exit 0). On the landing-trial copy, cargo check -p xc_net builds ureq 3.5.0 (with ureq-proto 0.6.5) in 1m46s with no errors, and gleam build of apps/sim/frames passes on gleam_stdlib 1.0.5 (only unused-variable warnings). ureq 3.5.0 is still the newest release (cargo search, 23:08). Left: the user lands the deps step.")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_12 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 12u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_13 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 13u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_14 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 14u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "x", "10-24 19:45 x: follow-up, see X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS. Lib sm' (18:06 landing) now emits gleam/erlang/atom into frames, so the gleam_erlang this item removed comes back at lib's pin. The rest of this item stays verified.")
inl note_of_x_deps_latest_15 () = Note ("X-DEPS-LATEST", 15u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:29 x groom: DONE. Its last open point (gleam_erlang returning at lib's pin through sm' atom imports) is what X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS tracked, now Done: frames compiles and the sim passes 45/45 in every run of 10-27 (quick check job-1793112246184; verify17 to verify24 sim accepts). Nothing else left.")
inl note_of_kit_heavy_one_limit_1 () = Note ("KIT-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:56Z", "eoie", "23:56 eoie: found when F7 started (23:51): 'Invoke-Heavy: acquired slot 2 ... (held at most 240 min)'. eoie_machine_windows heavy_wrapper takes the job timeout and discards it (inl _ = timeout), so every --heavy job gets the gate's default MaxMinutes 240 (SPIRAL_HEAVY_MAX_MINUTES unset). The heavy-queue watchdog kills the job's whole process tree at that deadline, whatever eoie's limit says. F7 (limit 6 h) would have been killed at 03:52, possibly mid release build. Done tonight: stopped F7's own watchdog, pid 46596, verified as a child of F7's heavy wrapper 120228 with deadline 03:51:58; F7's eoie supervisor still enforces its 6 h limit. Fix drafted for release H: tree-read/apply-heavy-one-limit.ps1 (wrapper passes -MaxMinutes timeout/60000+1, the test's fake gate takes the parameter, and the heavy-job CLI test asserts '-MaxMinutes 1 {' and 'held at most 1 min'). Dry run on the GE2+H copy is clean; chained into apply-rH.")
inl note_of_kit_heavy_one_limit_2 () = Note ("KIT-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "eoie", "23:06 groom: the slice tree-read\\apply-heavy-one-limit.ps1 (run by HIJK's apply-rH) left heavy_wrapper's concat5 with 4 arguments; the Q typecheck caught it 22:41 in crowding.spi. Fixed on the slice (backup .before-arity-fix); the Q typecheck passes 10/10 since. Ships with HIJK.")
inl note_of_kit_heavy_one_limit_3 () = Note ("KIT-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", 3u32, "2026-10-24T11:23Z", "eoie", "07:23 groom: blocked on the train. The only work left is to install stage HIJK and run its after-install check, so 'agile next' must not hand this out as ready work. Back to Active when stage HIJK installs.")
inl note_of_kit_heavy_one_limit_4 () = Note ("KIT-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", 4u32, "2026-10-29T06:57Z", "main", "10-29 02:5x main: SHIPPED and checked after install. The installed kit.exe (10-28 05:25) passes the job timeout through. Both of main's --heavy jobs tonight, job-1793253922878-main-oracle-review-1 and job-1793254803476-main-oracle-review-1b, were started with timeout_ms=10800000 (180 min), and each printed 'Invoke-Heavy: acquired slot 1 after 0 s (held at most 181 min)'. That is n1's -MaxMinutes timeout/60000+1, not the gate's default 240, so the two limits are one. Done.")
inl note_of_kit_heavy_one_limit_5 () = Note ("KIT-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", 5u32, "2026-11-06T11:27Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_1 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:58Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:58 x: found by a read-only review. paging.spi:14-17 'paged' calls fetch with each next_token until the token is empty, with no guard. A cursor that repeats (the API returning a token already seen, or a cycle) makes xc thread and every paged read loop forever, spending paid X API reads on each request. The tests (paging.spi:24-38) cover two pages and an error, not a loop. Fix in the domain: keep the seen tokens; a repeat returns a typed error (e.g. CursorRepeated token) naming the page count; add a page cap (a named constant or a parameter, a few hundred for xc's thread reads) that returns CursorBudget. Tests: a pager that returns 't1' forever must stop with CursorRepeated after 2 fetches; a pager with an endless fresh token must stop at the cap. Also minor: 'listm.append acc [p.body]' is O(n^2) over pages; cons and reverse once.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_2 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:06 x: a draft fix is at %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-PAGING-GUARD.md (not compiled). It adds a cursor_halt union (CursorRepeated / CursorBudget), a walk state record, a 500-page budget and a 'walker' record so paging stays generic over the caller's error, plus 4 tests with hand traces. Its post weights were checked without a compiler via 'xc post-thread --dry-run' on paging_draft.spi: 22 posts, max weight 248, no cell split (the first version had one 301-weight definition and was split). Measured constraint for wire.spi: union failure (224 chars) and describe_failure (263) have no room for an inline-record case or one more arm, so the draft names a halted_cursor record and suggests passing whole records to the describers. Open at compile time: top-level 'let rec ... and ...' and mixed record punning.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_3 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-10-23T04:33Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:33 x: the three Spiral drafts (paging, token, invariants) pass x-compat with 0 findings ('mix xsim.x_compat' on the extracted .spi files, Cells' checker for hashtag, mention, cashtag and autolink hazards). Their post weights were checked before with xc post-thread --dry-run. Cost note: a fresh MIX_BUILD_PATH made mix compile kino, spiral_cells and xsim (about 107 .ex files) first; that ran outside any live block or compile window.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_4 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:05Z", "x", "2026-10-23 05:05 x: the draft now COMPILES, partly verified. %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_paging_guard.ps1 applies it to a client package (each anchor asserted exactly once). On a scratch copy (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck\\client, packageDir made absolute; x's tree untouched), the Spiral compiler built thread/thread.spi to Rust in 15 s (49 KB) under 'eoie window run compile' (a window granted at 4.1 GB free, then released). The cursor messages, the conversation resource and the 500 budget appear in the output, so the new paging walk, wire's CursorHalted / cursor_text and thread's halted_in were type-checked. Two fixes came from the run-up: the names stepped / finished / continued clashed with exsss.spi:54 and splitter.spi:104/109 and are now walk_step / walk_bodies / walk_on. NOT yet verified: the 4 new paging test blocks (Cells only) and cargo build of the generated crate. Both go in the first pre-commit after applying.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_5 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 5u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_6 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 6u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_7 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 7u32, "2026-10-28T10:59Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_1 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:00Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:00 x: found by a read-only review of token/token.spi. 'refreshed' reads the env file, then 'exchanged' posts the refresh token to X, which rotates it server-side (X's OAuth 2.0 refresh tokens are single-use), then 'saved' writes the file. write_text (line 20) is std::fs::write: it truncates and then writes, with no temp file and rename. On failure, line 72 returns only 'could not write <path>' (exit 4), and the new access and refresh tokens are dropped. After that, the file holds the now-invalid old token (if the open failed) or a truncated file (if the write failed midway), and only a fresh browser OAuth authorization recovers. Fix: write to <path>.tmp in the same folder, flush, then std::fs::rename over the original (rename replaces an existing target on Windows too). If that fails, write the new pair to <path>.renewed next to it and name that file in the exit-4 message, never print secrets to the console (memory x-api-credentials). Minor: split_on on '\\n' keeps '\\r' on CRLF env files and rewritten() rejoins with '\\n', so a CRLF file comes back mixed. Keep the file's line ending. Tests: a read-only target path keeps the old file intact and writes .renewed; a CRLF file round-trips.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_2 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:08Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:08 x: correction to note 1's CRLF point. key_line (token.spi:45-47) keeps a trailing '\\r' on the lines it replaces, so an existing CRLF file keeps its endings for the two token keys. Only a key that with_key APPENDS (the file had no X_OAUTH2_REFRESH_TOKEN line yet) gets a bare '\\n'. So it is minor: append with the file's own line ending. The lost-token issue stands unchanged.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_3 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 3u32, "2026-10-23T04:09Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:09 x: a draft fix is at %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE.md (not compiled). It adds boundary primitives synced_write (File::create + write_all + sync_all) and renamed (std::fs::rename, which replaces on Windows and Unix); 'replaced' writes <path>.tmp and renames it over; on failure, saved -> kept_aside writes <path>.renewed and names it, never printing secrets. Appended keys keep the file's line ending, and a test plan is included. Post weights checked with 'xc post-thread --dry-run': 11 posts, max 267, no cell split (the first version's kept_aside spilled and was split into set_aside / token_lost). Open at compile time: '&&' short-circuit on the Rust backend, and the '?' inside the closure of the one-expression primitive.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_4 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:07Z", "x", "2026-10-23 05:07 x: the draft now COMPILES, partly verified. %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_token_refresh_safe.ps1 applies it with asserted anchors; on a scratch copy (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck2, x untouched) the Spiral compiler built token/token.spi to Rust in 15 s (26 KB) under an eoie compile window. sync_all, std::fs::rename, the .renewed sidecar and the 'authorize again' message are in the output, so synced_write / renamed / file_replaced / kept_aside / line_end were type-checked. A pre-run name check renamed 'replaced' to 'file_replaced' (world.spi:113 has a 'replaced'; token.spi does not open world, but the clash was a trap). NOT yet verified: rustc on the generated code (the '?' inside the closure of synced_write) and a test of the failure path. Both go in the first pre-commit after applying.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_5 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 5u32, "2026-10-24T07:07Z", "x", "10-24 03:07 x review (CPU-free): the atomic replace holds on Windows. file_replaced = synced_write(path.tmp) (File::create + write_all + sync_all) and then std::fs::rename(tmp, path). On Windows, std's rename is MoveFileExW with MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING, and the temp file sits in the same directory, so it's a same-volume replace; a crash leaves either the old file or the complete new one. The known failure (the destination opened without FILE_SHARE_DELETE, e.g. by an editor) makes rename return Err, and the draft then keeps the renewed tokens in the aside file and prints where (line 34), so the rotated token is never lost. Nothing to change. Directory fsync doesn't apply on NTFS.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_6 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 6u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_7 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_8 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 8u32, "2026-10-28T11:00Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_kit_job_env_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-ENV", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-JOB-ENV (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_1 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:25Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:25 x: found while measuring X-E2E-NON-ASCII's draft texts. twitter-text's config/v3.json has emojiParsingEnabled: true and defaultWeight 200 (scale 100), and js/src/parseTweet.js:37-53 extracts emoji entities with twemoji-parser, adds defaultWeight ONCE per entity and skips its length. So a skin-tone thumbs-up and a 4-person ZWJ family each weigh 2. x's weight (glyphs.spi, per code point by v3 ranges) gives 4 and 11 (measured with xc post-thread --dry-run), and glyphs.spi's weights_follow_x asserts thumb = 4. No backlog note shows a live X measurement behind that 4. Effect: safe but not exact. x over-counts emoji, so posts split early (never over 280), and x-compat's one-post-per-definition check can flag emoji-rich definitions that X would accept. Fix: x already segments graphemes (unicode-segmentation, gleam string graphemes), so a grapheme cluster that contains an emoji code point weighs 2 in total. The tests become thumb = 2 and the ZWJ family = 2, and keep U+2014 + U+200B = 2. Before changing, check twemoji-parser's entity rules against grapheme clusters for flags (regional-indicator pairs) and keycaps, and ideally confirm one value against X's live counter when the user allows a write.")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_2 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:26Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:26 x: scope beyond x's glyphs.spi. The same per-code-point model lives in spiral/apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/x_compat.ex:112-113 (weight = sum of code_point_weight over the charlist, ranges from the profile) and in the compiler's XCompat overweight check (spiral_compiler.fs:183089: 'CJK and emoji weigh 2'). The profile x/postability/x.json describes weights only as code_point_weight ranges, so it cannot express 'an emoji entity weighs default_weight once'. Fix order: add an emoji-entity rule to the profile schema (e.g. post.emoji_entity_weight: 2, segmented by extended grapheme clusters), then x's glyphs.spi, Cells' x_compat.ex and the compiler read it. The Cells and compiler parts are main's (told 2026-10-23).")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_3 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 3u32, "2026-10-23T04:48Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:48 x: a design draft (steps, edge cases, tests; no code yet) is at %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-THREAD-RESUME-and-X-EMOJI-WEIGHT.md.")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_4 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 4u32, "2026-10-23T12:49Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:52 x: RULE, written before the code. A grapheme cluster is an emoji entity (weight 2 in total) when it has more than one code point and ONE of these holds: (a) its first code point is >= U+1F000 (pictographs, regional-indicator flag pairs, tag flags, ZWJ sequences, skin tones); (b) it contains U+FE0F and its first code point is in U+2000..U+2BFF or is U+00A9, U+00AE, U+3030, U+303D, U+3297 or U+3299 (text-default symbols made emoji by VS16); (c) it contains U+20E3 and starts with 0-9, # or * (keycaps). Every other cluster keeps the per-code-point weight, so the change can only LOWER a weight, and only for multi-code-point emoji clusters. Deliberately unchanged: a lone (c) or (R) without VS16 stays 1, and a letter + U+FE0F stays 3, because twemoji does not parse those as emoji and lowering them could produce a 281-weight post. Code shape: glyph_weight g = if emoji_entity g then 2 else plain_weight g, with two one-expression primitives (first_code_point, code_point_count) on both backends. VS16/keycap checks use sm'.index_of. splitter's posted_unit and budget_for switch to glyph_weight. Expected tests: thumb + skin tone 4 -> 2, the ZWJ family 11 -> 2, heart + VS16 4 -> 2, keycap 1 5 -> 2, JP flag 4 -> 2. Unchanged: decomposed e 2, a + VS16 3, CJK 2, U+2014 + ZWSP 2. Cells' x_compat.ex and the compiler's XCompat stay main's (note 2).")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_5 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 5u32, "2026-10-23T13:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:27 x: the glyphs half PASSES. Cells on scratch copy %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-emoji\\client (x tree + apply_emoji_weight.ps1): glyphs.spi 8/8 on rust + gleam, including emoji_entities_weigh_two (thumb + skin tone, ZWJ family, heart + VS16, keycap 1, JP flag all = 2) and other_clusters_keep_code_point_weights (decomposed e 2, a + VS16 3, CJK 2, lone (c) 1, a 1). first_code_point and code_point_count agree on both backends. splitter.spi (units use glyph_weight) is running. Landing still waits for main's x_compat.ex / XC005 side (note 2), so the checkers agree with x.")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_6 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 6u32, "2026-10-23T13:29Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:29 x: splitter.spi with glyph_weight units PASSES 7/7 (rust + gleam) on the same scratch copy, so the whole x-side draft is green. Emoji-free sources keep their weights by construction (glyph_weight only differs for multi-code-point emoji clusters), and no existing splitter test changed.")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_7 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 7u32, "2026-10-23T14:19Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:19 x: open question from note 4 CLOSED against twemoji-parser's source (src/lib/regex.js, read-only). Bare (c), (R) and TM are NOT entities: their only branch is '(?:[(c)(R)\\u2122\\u265f]\\ufe0f)', where FE0F is mandatory. Keycaps match with or without FE0F ('[#*0-9]\\ufe0f?\\u20e3'), which rule (c) already covers: it only needs U+20E3 in the cluster. Safety argument completed: the only emoji in x's weight-1 ranges (0-4351, 8192-8205, 8208-8223, 8242-8247) are (c) and (R), and those need FE0F, which rule (b) weighs 2. (Double-exclamation U+203C and U+2049 are 8252 and 8265, already weight 2.) So no lone code point weighs more on X than in x, and the draft can only remove over-counting, never cause a 281-weight post.")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_8 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 8u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_9 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_10 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 10u32, "2026-10-28T10:31Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:29 x groom: deps re-pointed X-DONE -> X-WIP-VERIFY. Its Done condition named a 10-24 verify job that never went green; the e2e steps it needs run in every verify, so it closes on the first green one (now verify25, job-1793174907446), per X-WIP-VERIFY's closure rule.")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_11 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 11u32, "2026-10-28T11:04Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_1 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:39Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:39 x: found by a read-only review. X API v2 lookups (/2/tweets?ids=, /2/users?ids=, /2/users/by?usernames=) take at most 100 items per request. (1) refs.spi:36 posts_by sends 'take_first 100 wanted': it respects the limit but silently drops every referenced post after the first 100 at each depth level, with no failure, note or count, so a large timeline shows references as missing with no reason. (2) users.spi:53-54 by_ids (called from xc.spi:138 with refs' 'unnamed' author list, which grows with the timeline) puts every ID into one /2/users?ids= request, so past 100 unnamed authors X rejects it and with_users returns the failure for the whole timeline run. (3) users.spi:23 'named' does the same for CLI usernames (only matters past 100 typed names). e2e's worlds stay far below 100, so nothing exercised it. Fix: one chunking helper (chunks of 100 over a list, results merged in order, the first failure kept) used by all three. Tests: 250 IDs -> 3 requests (100, 100, 50) against a counting fake fetch, and a refs level with 150 wanted posts fetches all 150. Cost note: more requests means more paid reads, which is the API's real price for the data.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_2 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:40Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:40 x: correction to note 1, point (3). CLI usernames cannot exceed the limit: cli.spi:265 rejects more than lookup_limit () = 100 accounts per run, and --per-account is validated to 5..100 (cli.spi:167, tested at :424). So the scope is (1) refs' silent truncation and (2) users by_ids with refs' unnamed authors, which the CLI cannot bound because the list comes from the API's referenced posts. The chunking helper can reuse lookup_limit () from cli.spi (move it to wire.spi, next to the paths, so all three share one constant).")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_3 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T04:41Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:41 x: a draft is at %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-LOOKUP-LIMITS.md (not compiled). It moves lookup_limit () to wire.spi and adds chunked/dropped plus all_got (the first failure wins, otherwise results in order). refs gets posts_chunks so every chunk is fetched and absorbed, users by_ids goes per chunk with merged found/unknown lists, and the tests are listed. Post weights via xc post-thread --dry-run: 5 posts, max 238, no split.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_4 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:21Z", "x", "2026-10-23 05:21 x: the refs half of the draft COMPILES. %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_lookup_limits_refs.ps1 (asserted anchors) adds after_first / chunked / all_got to wire.spi after 'type got r'. 'dropped' was renamed after_first because world.spi:105 has one. It replaces refs' posts_by (take_first 100) with posts_chunks (chunked (lookup_limit ()) + all_got; refs opens cli directly) and makes deepened take got (list fetched), with next_of_all / absorbed_all. On a scratch copy (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck5, x untouched), refs/refs.spi compiled to Rust in 15 s (47 KB) under an eoie compile window. Not done: the users by_ids half and the counting-fetch tests (chunks of 100/100/50, 150 wanted posts absorbed); both come with applying. Tonight's compile checks stopped here, at 2.4 GB free.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_5 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 5u32, "2026-10-23T12:07Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:07 x: the users half COMPILES too. %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_lookup_limits_users.ps1 (applied after the refs script) adds one primitive, wire.json_text (serde Value to text), and rewrites users.by_ids: ids_chunks (chunked by lookup_limit ()), one request per chunk, all_got, then merged_users joins every chunk's 'data' and 'errors' arrays into one document. by_ids' contract (one body for xc's users_entry) is unchanged, so xc.spi needs no edit, and users_of_doc keeps reading 'errors' for unknown ids. Both scripts applied in sequence to a scratch copy (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck6): users/users.spi compiled to Rust in 24 s, with the merge in the output. Still at apply time: tests with a counting fake fetch (250 ids -> 3 requests; 150 wanted posts all absorbed).")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_6 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 6u32, "2026-10-24T03:55Z", "x", "23:55 x: lib-first follow-up, drafted to apply AFTER this item's two drafts and AFTER lib part B (listm' skip_/chunks_) is live: programs-x-lookup-lib\\land.eoie (eoie do, 3 files, 4 replacements). It deletes wire.spi's after_first/chunked/all_got. refs.spi and users.spi call listm'.chunks_ and resultm.collect_list (part A) directly; these are the only 3 call sites of the 9 references. Semantics are the same for n > 0. For n <= 0, x's chunked never terminates (after_first 0 returns the list unchanged), while lib chunks_ returns [items]. That's latent today (lookup_limit is 100). collect_list is all_got's exact contract (first Error, otherwise the values in order). Dry-run fits the landing-trial tree. Check job-1792814090102 (--after-ok the part B lander) applies it to %TEMP%\\x-glyphs-check and gates wire, refs and users.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_7 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 7u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_8 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 8u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1). Not closable on that alone: the counting-fake-fetch tests promised in n4/n5 are not in HEAD (git grep finds no chunk test). Left: refs test (150 wanted posts, all absorbed) and users test (250 ids -> 3 requests of 100/100/50), written against lib listm'.chunks_ once lib part B lands (n6 program), so they test the final code.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_9 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 9u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "x", "10-24 19:44 x: UNBLOCKED: lib part B (listm' skip_/chunks_) is live since 17:58 (held-files runner, job-1792875049850). Next, CPU-light to draft: programs-x-lookup-lib (n6) on the real tree, then the two counting-fake-fetch tests against listm'.chunks_ (users: 250 ids -> 100/100/50 requests; refs: 150 wanted posts all absorbed). They gate together with X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS' verify-landing5.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_10 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 10u32, "2026-10-25T06:05Z", "x", "10-25 02:05 x: lib-first follow-up and the promised tests, written by hand in edition-3 spelling (the n6 eoie program anchored on edition-2 text, now stale). wire.spi drops after_first/chunked/all_got. refs.spi gets chunk_fetched (fetch : post_ids -> got r) = listm'.chunks_ (lookup_limit ()) |> each fetch |> resultm.collect_list, and posts_chunks calls it with get_raw. users.spi's ids_chunks uses listm'.chunks_ and by_ids uses resultm.collect_list. Semantics are identical for n > 0 (lib skip_/chunks_ are x's after_first/chunked moved to lib; collect_list is all_got with its accumulator inside). Tests: refs a_lookup_level_fetches_every_wanted_post (150 wanted posts through a counting fake fetch give request sizes [100; 50], all 150) and users user_ids_chunk_to_the_lookup_limit (250 ids give [100; 100; 50]). They run in verify-landing10's notebook stage (job-1792904826458), whose crate regen at 01:51 predates these 02:02 edits.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_11 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 11u32, "2026-10-28T11:01Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_1 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:42Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:42 x: found by a read-only review. Only post creation is priced (splitter.spi:152 post_price_thousandths = 15, shown by post-thread --dry-run). Reads have no count or estimate. 'xc timeline --following USERNAME --max-following all' reads one timeline per followed account, plus the reference and user lookups, so an account that follows thousands makes thousands of paid requests in one run, with nothing printed before or after. The default (20) is safe; 'all' is the risk. Fix: (1) every read command ends with one line 'N requests, M posts read' (count in transport, where get_raw is), (2) 'all' resolves to the real following count first and refuses above a named cap (e.g. 200) unless --max-following is given as a number. Price per read is left out on purpose: X's current read pricing must come from X's docs when this is done, not from memory.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_2 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:48Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:48 x: a draft is at %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-READ-BUDGET.md (not compiled). Every request passes xc_net::xc_send (transport.spi:11 -> net.spi sent_of -> exchange) or xc_post_form, so one AtomicU32 there counts a run's requests: counted () before exchange, an xc_requests export. xc prints '--- N API requests, M posts read' on stderr (keeps 'xc thread ID > file' clean). '--max-following all' is rejected above following_cap () = 200 unless a number confirms it. Tests: an e2e count for 'xc timeline alice' = 1 and the cli cap. Post weights: 5 posts, max 212. Open: how net.spi may declare the static under the style rules.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_3 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 3u32, "2026-10-23T09:19Z", "x", "2026-10-23 05:19 x: the net part of the draft COMPILES. It settles the open question of note 2: a top-level Rust static is declared the way glyphs.spi declares Gleam externals, through an inl calling global (request_counter ()). The increment stays one expression typed () via drop(XC_REQUESTS.fetch_add(...)). %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_read_budget_net.ps1 adds request_counted () to exchange and form_exchange (every request) and exports xc_requests. On a scratch copy (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck4, x untouched), net/net.spi compiled to Rust in 14 s: 'static XC_REQUESTS' appears exactly once (global emitted once despite 2 callers), fetch_add twice, and the xc_requests export once. The helper was renamed counted -> request_counted after the run (posts.spi:231 has a 'counted'; nothing opens net, so the compiled result stands). Still to draft at apply time: xc.spi's stderr cost line and the --max-following all cap.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_4 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 4u32, "2026-10-23T11:09Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:09 x: grooming: P3 -> P2. Unbounded paid reads are money loss, which X-DONE note 55's order puts in the top tier, next to X-PAGING-GUARD.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_5 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 5u32, "2026-10-23T12:12Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:12 x: the xc half COMPILES with the net half. %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_read_budget_xc.ps1 (after apply_read_budget_net.ps1) adds requests_made (calls xc_net::xc_requests) and cost_noted, which writes 'xc: N API requests, M posts shown' on stderr at the end of shown_timeline, and 'xc: N API requests' after a thread read. On a scratch copy (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck7) xc/xc.spi compiled to Rust in 20 s with both in the output. CORRECTION to note 2: e2e does compare stderr. e2e.ps1:37 builds each step's text from stderr lines then stdout lines, so the cost line changes every read step's expected output in the README tutorial. Applying it therefore means e2e.ps1 -Accept plus a reviewed README diff. Not drafted yet: the --max-following all cap in cli.spi.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_6 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 6u32, "2026-10-23T12:15Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:15 x: the --max-following all cap COMPILES too, so the item is fully drafted (counter, cost line, cap). %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_read_budget_cap.ps1 changes users.followed: with 'all' (cap < 0) it pages up to following_cap () + 1 = 201 accounts instead of the i32 max, and if more come back it refuses (exit 4) with '--max-following all would read more than 200 timelines; pass a number to confirm'. A numeric --max-following stays the explicit confirmation. No extra API call: the following list is paged anyway. Applied together with both lookup-limit scripts (all three touch users.spi at different anchors) on %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck8: users/users.spi compiled to Rust in 32 s with the message in the output.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_7 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 7u32, "2026-10-23T13:28Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:28 x: renames in the drafts, made after their compile checks: users' following_cap () -> following_read_limit () (cli.spi has a union named following_cap that users opens; types and terms don't collide, but it read as one name for two things), and xc.spi's requests_made -> api_requests_so_far (net.spi has its own requests_made, which xc reaches only as xc_net::xc_requests). Pure renames with no other change; recompile both at apply time. apply_all_check.ps1 confirms all 11 client drafts apply together cleanly.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_8 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 8u32, "2026-10-23T17:30Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:30 x: CORRECTION to note 6. The drafted cost line says 'M posts shown', but this item promises posts READ, and they differ: reference lookups return billed posts that never become timeline lines, and with X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1 (below 5, X still returns and bills 5 per account) the shown count would understate paid reads further. Draft apply_read_budget_posts_read.ps1 (after read_budget_xc and per_account_min_1): digest gains posts_read bundle, the sum over primary and lookup entries (not user entries) of each response's data plus includes.posts, i.e. what X returned. It is exported as xc_posts_read, and xc's line becomes 'xc: N API requests, R posts read, M shown'. Test: posts_read_counts_what_x_returned (primary_bundle -> 1). As before, the line goes to stderr, so the README read steps change at e2e -Accept.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_9 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 9u32, "2026-10-23T17:36Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:36 x: posts-read follow-up compiles: xc.spi with all 14 drafts applied (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-allcheck) builds to 831 lines of Rust carrying the xc_posts_read call and the 'posts read' text. The digest test is queued (self-gating chain10). It is also in land_all, after per_account_min_1.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_10 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 10u32, "2026-10-24T07:06Z", "x", "10-24 03:06 x review (CPU-free, during tibia's block): the request count stays exact under tonight's lookup work. net.spi counts at the transport: exchange (line 57) and form_exchange (71) each call request_counted once. Chunked by-ID lookups (X-LOOKUP-LIMITS, and its lib-first follow-up with listm'.chunks_) therefore count one paid request per 100-item chunk. patient/patient_bodied (24-28) only set a 30 s global timeout and http_status_as_error(false), with no retry loop, so ureq sends one HTTP request per exchange and no request goes uncounted. The timeline report's request number is a true upper bound of billed reads.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_11 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 11u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_12 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 12u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_13 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 13u32, "2026-10-24T21:02Z", "x", "10-24 17:02 x: verify3 e2e failures from this item: (1) extras 'xc timeline busy'/'gateway' now start with 'xc: 2 API requests, 0 posts read, 0 shown' before the 429/502 line, and e2e.ps1's hard-coded expectations predated the cost line. (2) The '--max-following all' check expected max_results=1000, but this item's bound reads following_read_limit()+1 = 201 accounts, so the stub's pages are 201 then 199&pagination_token=p2. Both are now expected in e2e.ps1 (unstaged). (3) Grammar: 'xc: 1 API requests' after xc thread 999. xc.spi cost_noted now goes through requests_counted (1 -> 'API request'). The README answers come from the next e2e -Accept and get reviewed by diff. Gate: job-1792875692136-x-verify-landing4.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_14 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 14u32, "2026-10-28T11:02Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it). Its README answers are unchanged by the accept (it rewrote only the thread and links.txt steps) and match on the plain pass.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_1 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:43Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:43 x: found by a read-only review. post-thread (cli.spi:74, write.spi) posts every post in sequence, each replying to the previous, with no pacing and no resume flag. On a failure mid-thread (e.g. a 429 from X's per-user posting limit on a large file; the README says xc's own source travels as a thread, about 1,644 posts for the whole client), it reports the IDs posted before the failure (write.spi:56, good) but cannot continue. The only path is delete-thread plus a full repost, paying again (.015 per post by splitter.spi's price) for the posts already made. Fix: 'xc post-thread --resume LAST_ID FILE' reads the thread back (the xc thread machinery), checks that the posted prefix equals the file's first k posts byte for byte, and continues at post k+1 replying to LAST_ID; it refuses on any mismatch. Separately, pace creates against X's documented per-user write limit (take the numbers from X's current docs at implementation time, not from memory) and stop cleanly with the resume command printed when the window is used up.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_2 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:48Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:48 x: a design draft (steps, edge cases, tests; no code yet) is at %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-THREAD-RESUME-and-X-EMOJI-WEIGHT.md.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_3 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 3u32, "2026-10-23T12:59Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:59 x: code draft of the pure half (not compiled yet; queued). %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_thread_resume_plan.ps1 adds to write.spi: union resume_plan (ResumeAfter {posted; remaining} | PostDiffers {at; planned; found} | MorePostedThanPlanned n), resume_from (compares found thread texts, oldest first, with the planned posts by exact equality; wire already unescapes on read and splitter already trims), resume_refusal, resumed_posting (starts chained from After LAST_ID with the line 'resumed after post k of n'), resume_written, plus 3 tests (half-posted resumes, a changed post 2 refuses, a longer thread or no thread refuses). Plumbing still to draft, in order: (1) cli: 'post-thread --resume LAST_ID FILE' -> command ResumeThread {last; path}; (2) args: kind 10, payload LAST_ID + newline + path; (3) thread.spi: a new export xc_thread_texts(base, bearer, id) giving the texts of the chain from its root down to id, oldest first, built from the same pages that xc_thread_ids reads; (4) xc.spi: run_resume_thread splits the file with framed, reads the found texts and passes {last, planned, found} as JSON to xc_write kind 10; (5) write.spi: thread_written dispatches kind 10 to resume_written. e2e test: a stub world with posts 1..3 of a known 6-post file, then '--resume <id 3>' posts 4..6 and a tampered post 2 is refused.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_4 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 4u32, "2026-10-23T13:12Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:12 x: DECISION, CLI shape: 'xc post-thread --resume ROOT_ID FILE' instead of LAST_ID. Reason: thread_bodies searches the conversation from the given post's created_at (search_of: start_time = whole_second created_at), so reading from LAST_ID would miss every earlier post. ROOT_ID is the first 'posted' line and what delete-thread already takes. The resume then finds the chain's end itself, so the user never has to work out which post came last. Plumbing drafted: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_thread_resume_plumbing.ps1, applied after apply_thread_resume_plan.ps1. It touches cli (ResumeThread (root, path), a usage line, a parse test), args (kind 10, payload root + newline + path), thread.spi (chain_of: the root is the post whose parent is outside the set; follow the single reply; refuse on a branch with 'the thread branches after post X'; a new export xc_thread_chain returns [{id, text}] oldest first; test a_thread_chain_runs_from_its_root_to_the_last_reply), xc.spi (run_resume_thread: file check, bearer check, xc_thread_chain, then xc_write kind 10 with {planned, found}) and write.spi (resume_of: last id = the last found id, then resume_written). Applied on scratch copy %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-resume\\client. Cells queued for write, thread and cli. xc.spi needs the staged crate build (compile_check.ps1). Still to do: the e2e step with a half-posted thread in the stub world.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_5 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 5u32, "2026-10-23T13:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:27 x: the drafted code PASSES Cells except xc.spi, which needs the staged crate build. On %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-resume\\client (x tree + apply_thread_resume_plan.ps1 + apply_thread_resume_plumbing.ps1): write.spi 5/5 (rust), thread.spi 2/2 (chain root -> last reply, branch refusal, empty), cli.spi 18/18 (rust + zig, with the --resume parse test). Left: xc.spi compile (compile_check.ps1 after the live block), then a release xc.exe and the e2e fixture %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\e2e_resume.ps1. That fixture is already built and inspected: posts 9101-9103 = the first 3 of checks.spi's 9 framed posts with conversation and reply refs, 9201-9202 with post 2 changed, and 2 tutorial steps before 'Renewing the OAuth 2.0 token'. Also: apply_all_check.ps1 in the drafts folder applies all 11 client drafts, in landing order, to one fresh copy: 11/11 apply, and 0 new cross-file duplicate names after renaming following_cap -> following_read_limit (users; cli has a type of that name) and xc's requests_made -> api_requests_so_far (net has a term of that name).")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_6 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 6u32, "2026-10-23T13:55Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:55 x: desk-review fix in apply_thread_resume_plumbing.ps1 (after the 2/2 Cells pass, so thread.spi is queued again after tibia's block). chain_of took the FIRST post whose parent is outside the found set as the root. An edited post breaks that: keep latest drops the old version, so a child that replied to the old id also looks like a root. The texts would then mismatch and refuse (safe), but with a misleading 'post k differs'. Now chain_of collects every outside-parented post: exactly one is the root, two or more refuse with ChainRoots ('post X replies to a post outside the thread's chain (an edited or deleted post?); --resume needs one unbroken chain'). The test gains a 'second root 5' case. apply_all_check still applies 11/11 with no new clashes.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_7 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 7u32, "2026-10-23T15:28Z", "x", "2026-10-23 11:28 x: Cells caught my note-6 change: thread.spi 1 of 2 failed (rust), actual 'second root 1', expected 'second root 5'. In [5; 3; 1; 2], post 5 (parent 9 missing) and post 1 (the real root) are both outside-parented, and ChainRoots named whichever came second in list order, the real root. Fixed so the refusal names the ORPHAN reply (a post whose parent is missing; orphan / stray_of), which is the post the user must look at. Re-queued as job x-resume-thread-2 on a freshly rebuilt x-resume copy; the queued xc compile reads the same copy.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_8 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 8u32, "2026-10-23T15:57Z", "x", "2026-10-23 11:57 x: xc.spi with both resume drafts COMPILES (eoie job x-resume-xc, BuildSpiral Rust, 14 s, 42,262 bytes). The output carries the xc_thread_chain call and the refusal texts, so the whole draft type-checks end to end: cli, args, thread, write, xc. Left: thread.spi's re-test after the orphan fix (re-queued in a self-gating chain, because the eoie-queued run was refused by a foreign Cells run), then a release build and the e2e fixture.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_9 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 9u32, "2026-10-23T16:29Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:29 x: thread.spi with the orphan fix PASSES 2/2 (rust; self-gating chain, 11:58). The whole draft is now verified on scratch: write 5/5, thread 2/2, cli 18/18, xc.spi compiles. Progress 50 -> 70 (verified on a copy; the rest is landing + a release xc + the e2e fixture apply_e2e_resume.ps1, both in land_all).")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_10 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 10u32, "2026-10-23T23:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:52 x: the pacing half of this item, decided and DRAFTED. X's per-user posting limits depend on the tier (from 17 posts per 24 h up to per-15-min windows), and net.spi returns no rate-limit headers, so an in-process sleep would be uninformed and could last a day. With --resume in place, pacing = stop cleanly and say exactly how to continue. %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_thread_resume_hint.ps1 -Client <apps/client> (after land_all's fit, anchored on post-fit write.spi + README): posting gains resumed : bool; stopped takes the posting and ends with '; continue with: xc post-thread --resume ROOT FILE' (ROOT = the first posted id) on a fresh thread, or '; continue with the same --resume command' on a resumed one, where the first new id isn't the root. resumed_line is split out to keep resumed_posting's cell at 228 (it was 282 > XC005's budget). Tests (429 failure): the fresh hint names root 1; the resumed hint says to repeat; no hint when nothing was posted. Touched cells weigh 259 or less. README's resume paragraph now says the error ends with the command. Validated on a fresh post-fit copy; the write.spi Cells check (on landing-trial-4's copy) is queued after tibia's block; joins land_all only after it passes.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_11 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 11u32, "2026-10-24T07:08Z", "x", "10-24 03:08 x review (CPU-free): the drafts match the 19:52 decision. Pacing = stop at the 429 and end with the exact 'xc post-thread --resume <root>' (apply_thread_resume_hint.ps1:67-90), and no draft has a fixed sleep or rate constant, which is right: X's per-user window can't be known locally. Follow-up idea, not in scope and not drafted: X's 429 carries x-rate-limit-reset (epoch seconds), so the hint could print 'resume after HH:MM'. net.spi's http_reply is (status, body) only (call_reply/send_reply drop headers), so that needs a third field read via r.headers().get(\"x-rate-limit-reset\") in both arms, plus a wire failure field. Small, but it touches every exchange's tuple; worth it only if threads hit the limit in practice.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_12 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 12u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_13 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 13u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_14 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 14u32, "2026-10-24T21:02Z", "x", "10-24 17:02 x: verify3 (job-1792840102746, 07:08) e2e FAILED on both resume steps: 9101 and 9201 printed 'no posted thread found before the resume point'. Root cause: thread.spi chain_json built the found list with wire.quoted_text, a test helper that wraps in quotes without escaping. Post texts carry newlines and quotes, so the resume payload was not valid JSON, parse_json gave nothing, planned and found were both empty, and resume_from 0 Nil Nil = ResumeAfter 0. The unit tests covered chain_listed but never chain_json. Reproduced at 16:5x with the release xc.exe against e2e/stub.py: xc thread 9101 reads the chain and --dry-run plans 9 posts, but --resume refuses. Fix (unstaged): chain_json uses wire.json_string (serde), the escaper that assemble/locate already use, plus test a_chain_keeps_multiline_quoted_texts (chain_json, then parse_json, gives back the text). Gate: job-1792875692136-x-verify-landing4.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_15 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 15u32, "2026-10-28T10:31Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:29 x groom: deps re-pointed X-DONE -> X-WIP-VERIFY. Its Done condition named a 10-24 verify job that never went green; the e2e steps it needs run in every verify, so it closes on the first green one (now verify25, job-1793174907446), per X-WIP-VERIFY's closure rule.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_16 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 16u32, "2026-10-28T11:05Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_lua_helpers_on_demand_1 () = Note ("LUA-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:48Z", "main", "2026-10-23 00:48 unused helpers are 4.3% of 1,321,108 Lua bytes over 448 tree samples (wrap_unsigned unused in 185, mul_mod32 in 167, wrap_signed in 19). Pass pack-next6\\lua-helpers.ps1 (luaUsedHelpers in codegenLua; helperLocals counted after pruning, so the 190-local definitions line can only gain room). Mirror lua_helpers_mirror.py; the F# port equals it on all 200 outputs with helpers (248 have none). In pack-next4, core 6D9273CFCCB09AEB parses under both defines; review rule in locals_check.py.")
inl note_of_lua_helpers_on_demand_2 () = Note ("LUA-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND", 2u32, "2026-10-27T23:07Z", "main", "10-27 19:07 main: Groom 10-27 19:0x: this change is LANDED and live; the 80% from 10-23 is stale. Evidence (read-only): the live core apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs contains the pass, and the committed sample outputs show its effect (LUA: luaUsedHelpers present, and only 1 of 40 sampled main.lua still defines wrap_unsigned, against 185 unused copies measured on 10-23; GLEAM: gleamUsedHelpers present; C-STATIC: generated C such as samples/closure_apply/main.c declares its functions static). It reached the core through pack-next4/next6, which were composed into the installed stacks. Closing condition, set now: Done when the running bundle accept (accept-6, job-1793136844465) is GREEN, since that accept blesses the current outputs as the oracle; if it's RED for unrelated reasons, close on the next GREEN accept.")
inl note_of_lua_helpers_on_demand_3 () = Note ("LUA-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND", 3u32, "2026-10-27T23:21Z", "main", "10-27 19:21 main: Closed by the condition set in n2: the bundle accept is GREEN (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 7,367 rows, 0 blocking cells), so the current outputs carrying this pass are the oracle.")
inl note_of_wasm_prelude_fields_on_demand_1 () = Note ("WASM-PRELUDE-FIELDS-ON-DEMAND", 1u32, "2026-10-23T05:01Z", "main", "2026-10-23 01:01 fd_write imported but never called in 423 of 444 tree outputs, prelude data unused in 437; mirror wasm_fields_mirror.py: 2,812,732 -> 2,714,570 bytes (3.5%). Rule: an import stays when proc_exit or reached; a prelude data segment at N stays when 'i32.const N' appears in the emitted globals, functions or reached helpers (addresses are only used as literals; user data starts at 72). wasmtime runs of 60 changed outputs (40 smallest, 20 largest) equal before and after (2 differ only in the error text path and backtrace offset, exit 1 both). Pass pack-next6\\wasm-fields.ps1 in pack-next4, core ECA129CF8D4A1EFE parses under both defines; review rule in locals_check.py.")
inl note_of_wasm_prelude_fields_on_demand_2 () = Note ("WASM-PRELUDE-FIELDS-ON-DEMAND", 2u32, "2026-10-29T07:11Z", "main", "10-29 03:2x main: SHIPPED; verified on the installed compiler's outputs. The pass is in the core (spiral_compiler.fs:171039-171045, prelude_field_needed: an import stays when it is proc_exit or reached; a data segment at N stays when i32.const N appears in the emitted text), and the installed compiler emits it. Tonight main's oracle run 1 rewrote every committed sample output byte-identically (git status samples: 0), so the committed .wat files are the installed compiler's output. Across all 444 tracked main.wat: fd_write is imported in 21 and called in the same 21, against n1's 'imported but never called in 423 of 444'; 23 prelude data segments (48 true, 56 false, 64 -) are kept and each has a use (total i32.const N minus the data line > 0), so 0 are kept unused. Done. (Main's 10-28 blocker 'oracle baseline lost' was wrong for this item.)")
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_1 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 1u32, "2026-10-23T05:09Z", "main", "2026-10-23 01:09 census: 431 temp copies in 111 of 411 Delphi outputs; the same two-loop pattern (all args into temps, then temps into params) sits at the 4 self-loop emitters in codegenDelphi, codegenZig, codegenLean and codegenWasm. P3 because the native compilers coalesce these moves (FPC least so), so it is mostly output size and readability. Review needs temp-name canonicalization (temp numbers come from decls.Count, so removing one renumbers later temps).")
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_2 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 2u32, "2026-10-23T07:02Z", "main", "2026-10-23 03:02 main: written for Delphi and Lean (the only backends with samples; Wasm has 0 such blocks in the tree samples, Zig is left alone because its review rule is a const-locals F# check that a second rewrite would entangle). Census: Delphi 115 blocks / 313 temp copies in 21 files, Lean 50 blocks / 187 copies in 33 files; exactly 1 block per backend has a real cycle (native_managed_array_tail_recursion: v1 <- v2, v2 <- v1), which keeps one temp. Patch pack-next6\\tail-moves.ps1: a shared ParallelMove union + parallelMoves (free move first in parameter order, else save the first pending target to a temp slot and redirect its readers) before CodegenDelphi, and the two self-loop emitters. Mirror tail_moves_mirror.py: Delphi 52,828 -> 52,120 lines (temp declarations dropped per function; the cycle reuses the parameter's own temp declaration, so the type matches), Lean 53,885 -> 53,619. Review rules wired: main.pas compared with temp names canonicalized by first occurrence (numbers come from decls.Count), main.lean as lean-let(moves(before)). Held for tibia's live block (no compiles of any size): the fsi check of parallelMoves against the mirror's step lists, composing into next4, the parse-only check, and fpc/lean runs of the changed programs.")
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_3 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 3u32, "2026-10-23T08:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 04:57 main: native proof after tibia's block end. Delphi: all 21 changed programs (mirror output vs tree output) build with fpc -O2 -Mdelphi as test.ps1 does, and run with equal exit and stdout, 0 broken; that includes native_managed_array_tail_recursion's real cycle. Lean: 6 programs (the cycle sample + 5) under lean +v4.35.0-rc4 --run, all equal. F# parallelMoves equals the mirror's steps on 165/165 blocks. Composed into next4 (3D8553145D246A26) and the composite 7FCC891349EB1608; the matrix review is the end-to-end check.")
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_4 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 4u32, "2026-10-23T14:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:23 main: tail-moves v2 is staged as %TEMP%\\pack-next8\\tail-moves-v2.ps1 and composed into pack-next8, which is now core D78DE6536D4AF893 (or-pattern v3 + hopacsota census + this). Eight self-tail emitters always saved every argument to a temp and then copied: Zig (tmpN = vI), Wasm (local.set tmp), WGSL (let spiral_t), Kotlin (val tN_I), Swift (let tN_I), OpenQASM and eBPF (statement temps), and TypeScript (a { let t0 ... } block for any call with 2 or more parameters). Each now goes through pack-next6's shared parallelMoves (MoveVar, SaveTemp, MoveTemp), so a temp is declared only where a parameter is overwritten while another move still reads it, and TypeScript opens its block only for such a cycle. C is excluded on purpose: its self_continue runs the refcount release between saving and assigning, so direct moves would decref the new values. Rust already destructures a tuple, Python assigns a tuple, and Lua recurses. Census of the tree outputs (tail_temp_census.py): temp saves that become direct moves, zig 437, ts 361, kt 198, swift 198, wgsl 41, qasm 25, ebpf 27 (0.3-0.9% of each backend's lines; the Wasm pattern didn't match, so its count is unknown). It parses under both defines with pair-drift clean. Review rule for next8: residual changes confined to self-tail blocks in these 8 backends plus or-pattern samples (v3); DISAGREE 0; every self-tail block's moves are a correct parallel assignment (native results equal).")
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_5 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 5u32, "2026-10-23T14:26Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:26 main: pack-next8 also gets ts-dead-break.ps1. TypeScript switch cases (int switch and union unbox) emitted 'break;' after a case body that already ended in return, continue tail or throw: 669 dead breaks over the 449 tree main.ts. A falls_through helper in the TypeScript codegen scope reads the last emitted line by walking back from the end of the StringBuilder, which is linear and avoids a whole-text ToString, and skips the break only after those three terminators. A body ending in a nested '}' keeps its break, which stays safe. It parses; the composed next8 core is printed by compose.ps1. Review rule: TypeScript residuals differ from the baseline only by dropped 'break;' lines and the self-tail moves; native results are unchanged.")
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_6 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 6u32, "2026-10-23T14:32Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:32 main: pack-next8's review is staged as %TEMP%\\pack-next8\\review.ps1 (parse, build, snapshot, -Suite all -Native -ReuseNative, rule check) with next8_rules.py as its output rule. Samples whose main.spi holds an or-pattern arm may change on any backend (v3). Everywhere else only these backends may change: zig, ts, kt, swift, wat, wgsl, qasm and ebpf, by removing temp saves, temp declarations and temp moves (plus TypeScript braces and dead breaks) and adding direct vN = vM moves; and lua, by removing 'local __v = { vN }' and its __v[1] reads. Any other change is a violation. Checked on a hand-made case: the TypeScript self-tail block made direct with its breaks dropped passes, and a stray literal change is flagged. Order: after the next7 review and its accept, so next8's snapshot is the next7 state; DISAGREE 0 and native results equal are required as usual.")
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_7 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 7u32, "2026-10-30T23:42Z", "main", "10-30 19:4x main R3 groom (BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030): PAUSED. The staged pack-next8 review (%TEMP%\\pack-next8\\review.ps1 + next8_rules.py, 10-23) is gone (TEMP lost in the 10-28 wipe; Test-Path false) and apps/compiler/packs holds no next8 applier, so nothing of it landed. Resume = the compiler team re-drafts pack-next8 as an applier in apps/compiler/packs against today's core (compiler-lane pivot: appliers there, main installs), keeping the v3 rule (only zig/ts/kt/swift/wat/wgsl/qasm/ebpf temp-move removals plus lua __v boxes may change). Tag compiler added.")
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_8 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 8u32, "2026-10-31T04:03Z", "compiler", "10-31 00:03 compiler: DONE, nothing to re-draft. Read from the tree, not the notes (decide-from-tree): the live core (4191A77FB3D3A335 = the installed pack-8 core) already resolves self-tail parameters with parallelMoves in every backend this item and the v3 rule name: Delphi (spiral_compiler.fs:165524), Zig (166254), Lean (166878), Wasm (170781), Wgsl (171530), Kotlin (172778), Swift (173556), OpenQasm (174077), Ebpf (174404) and TypeScript (174988); git log -S 'parallelMoves (Array.map2' puts it in commit d095f13d. The Lua half of the v3 rule is also in: codegenLua boxes scrutinees as 'local __v = { ... }' only for MULTI-scrutinee unboxes (169821-169823, single-scrutinee arm is '| [_] -> ()'), and the only 2 committed main.lua files with a box (wgsl_nullary_union 'local __v = { v16, v17 }', wgsl_payload_union '{ v1, v7 }') are two-scrutinee matches. Accepted with pack 8 (single-flight-20261030-120324), so n13's lost pack-next8 draft is superseded, not missing.")
inl note_of_c_static_functions_1 () = Note ("C-STATIC-FUNCTIONS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T05:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 01:51 generated C is always one translation unit (matrix, tibia predict.ps1, shims are -include headers that define only their own static helpers). 2,852 lines of 511 outputs gain static; prototypes of external functions (no definition in the file) and user globals are left alone, because the pass runs on fwd_dcls + types + functions only. gcc 15.2 -O2 A/B on 40 hashed-random samples: compile time -25% (198 s -> 149 s) and -16% with the order swapped (137 s -> 115 s), exe bytes -0.5%, 0 broken, 0 run differences; plus all 65 samples with CFlags rules, c-shim.h or prototypes: 65 same. F# pass equals the mirror on 511/511. In pack-next4 (core 366DF6250C393FFE, parses under both defines); review rule re-applies the same regex.")
inl note_of_c_static_functions_2 () = Note ("C-STATIC-FUNCTIONS", 2u32, "2026-10-27T23:08Z", "main", "10-27 19:08 main: Groom 10-27 19:0x: this change is LANDED and live; the 80% from 10-23 is stale. Evidence (read-only): the live core apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs contains the pass, and the committed sample outputs show its effect (LUA: luaUsedHelpers present, and only 1 of 40 sampled main.lua still defines wrap_unsigned, against 185 unused copies measured on 10-23; GLEAM: gleamUsedHelpers present; C-STATIC: generated C such as samples/closure_apply/main.c declares its functions static). It reached the core through pack-next4/next6, which were composed into the installed stacks. Closing condition, set now: Done when the running bundle accept (accept-6, job-1793136844465) is GREEN, since that accept blesses the current outputs as the oracle; if it's RED for unrelated reasons, close on the next GREEN accept.")
inl note_of_c_static_functions_3 () = Note ("C-STATIC-FUNCTIONS", 3u32, "2026-10-27T23:23Z", "main", "10-27 19:23 main: Closed by the condition set in n2: the bundle accept is GREEN (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 7,367 rows, 0 blocking cells), so the current outputs carrying this pass are the oracle.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_1 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 1u32, "2026-10-23T06:12Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:12 measured on generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi (FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS note 2). Shapes are parsed per block (layout -> Joined -> blockShapes over the block's own lines), so a per-file quadratic is not obvious from the code; each block may parse up to 4 candidate layouts (preferred, plain, habits, chain fill). Next: a scaling probe on the first quarter of the same file (about 140 s means linear with a large constant, about 35 s means quadratic in file size), then a sampled profile of one run.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_2 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 2u32, "2026-10-23T06:15Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:15 main: scaling probe: the first quarter of generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi (1,600 of 6,216 lines, 70 KB) took 169 s wall and 37 s CPU, against 555 s wall for the whole file, so time grows linearly (3.3x for 4x the lines). Wall time is mostly BelowNormal starvation on the loaded box (4.6x the CPU time). The real cost is a constant of about 23 ms of CPU per line, roughly 150 s of CPU for this module. Next: a sampled profile on a quiet box (dotnet-trace or a stopwatch build) to find which of flatten / blockShapes re-parses / layout candidates dominates.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_3 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 3u32, "2026-10-23T06:47Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:47 main: profiled (dotnet-trace, scratch tool path, on the quarter file): 94% of the worker thread is sameParse -> blockShapes -> shapeOf -> shapeSteps, which renders each parsed block to a shape string by F# reflection. Per node it ran isPosition (IsRecord + GetRecordFields + sort) and up to 3 GetUnionFields (foldedConcat, rawSeqHalves, then the node itself). Fix: fmt-fix\\shape-cache.ps1 classifies each runtime type once (ShapeKind in a ConcurrentDictionary: position, enum, union with PreComputeUnionTagReader + per-case PreComputeUnionReader, tuple/record precomputed readers, pair, array, enumerable) and the helpers match on it. The type-test order is unchanged. Equivalence and speed (fsi harness over the installed SpiralCompilerCore's own parse results: 60 lib/spiral files + the fiscal quarter, 2,252 blocks): 2,252 shapes byte-equal, rendering 378,818 ms -> 2,175 ms (174x). Composed into pack-next5d after chain-fill; composite 372640E632978A3E parses under both defines. The next4-composite review's formatter gate is the end-to-end check.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_4 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:46Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:46 main: first end-to-end number from the composite's formatter gate (same box, same run, user corpus 84 files): installed 87.5 s, candidate with the shape cache 55.8 s (-36% wall, 1.57x). Smaller than the 174x on shape rendering alone, because a whole run also pays process start, lexing, parsing and layout per file, and the user files are small. On the large generated module (where shapes were 94% of the profile) the gain should be close to that share; a timed rerun of generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi with the installed composite measures it.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_5 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 5u32, "2026-10-23T09:49Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:49 main: end-to-end on the large generated file, same box, same minute, BelowNormal, first 1,600 lines of the saved original generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi. Installed formatter: 100 s wall, 34.1 s CPU. Composite candidate (67EBF80563E2239A, shape cache + the reviewed habits): 10 s wall, 5.0 s CPU. That is 6.8x less CPU and 10x less wall time, with byte-identical output. Per line: 21 ms down to 3.1 ms of CPU, so the 300k generated lines drop from about 1.8 h to about 16 min of CPU, which makes FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS' format-after-generate step viable once the composite installs. (A first attempt timed a 3-line placeholder: the running fiscal family gate had replaced the live module, so that number was discarded.)")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_6 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 6u32, "2026-10-23T09:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:51 main: composite review's formatter gate (job-1792748200944, same run, same box), installed vs candidate. user 84 files: 87.5 s vs 55.8 s. lib 82 files: 274.5 s vs 44.5 s (6.2x). synthetic 10 files: 2.7 s vs 3.5 s (tiny files, process start dominates). Every corpus is at 0 files differing beyond parentheses and whitespace. The lib figure matches the 6.8x CPU on the generated module: larger files gain the most.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_7 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 7u32, "2026-10-30T14:17Z", "compiler", "10-30 10:1x compiler: DONE, measured on the installed compiler (core 9C899B30495F7DC5, the same hash on box and node; the shape cache is in the live core at spiral_compiler.fs:180175-180187). Node job job-1793369749168-compiler-fmt-full, format --check, SPIRAL_FORMAT_PARALLEL=1, generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi: the first quarter (101,098 B, 2,367 lines) took 2.0 s CPU / 3.9 s wall; the whole module (419,911 B, 9,467 lines; it has grown from the title's 287 KB) took 7.8 s CPU / 10.4 s wall. 4.0x the input for 3.9x the CPU: linear, as the title asks. Before the fix the module took 555 s. Both runs exit 0; the over-120 lines it lists are --check findings, not failures (layout stays FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS's concern). Script: scratchpad night\\fmt-full.ps1.")
inl note_of_gleam_helpers_on_demand_1 () = Note ("GLEAM-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND", 1u32, "2026-10-23T07:19Z", "main", "2026-10-23 03:19 census over 447 tree outputs: unused spiral_* helpers are 137,686 of 1,661,094 bytes (8.3%); wrap_unsigned unused in 167, array_from_list/to_list in 122, array_create in 84. Pass pack-next6\\gleam-helpers.ps1: gleamUsedHelpers runs on the assembled program after silenceUnusedBindings and drops a top-level 'pub fn spiral_*' (with its @external line, or its body up to the first column-0 '}') when nothing else names it, repeating to a fixed point. Types and imports stay (an unused import is a Gleam warning, not an error). Mirror gleam_helpers_mirror.py: 8.3% smaller, 265 outputs change; the review rule (squeeze(pruned(before)) = squeeze(after)) accepts all 265 mirror outputs. Not composed yet: tibia's live block bars compiles, so the fsi port check, the compose and the parse-only check run after its block end, with the gleam batch build in the review as the real proof.")
inl note_of_gleam_helpers_on_demand_2 () = Note ("GLEAM-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND", 2u32, "2026-10-27T23:08Z", "main", "10-27 19:08 main: Groom 10-27 19:0x: this change is LANDED and live; the 80% from 10-23 is stale. Evidence (read-only): the live core apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs contains the pass, and the committed sample outputs show its effect (LUA: luaUsedHelpers present, and only 1 of 40 sampled main.lua still defines wrap_unsigned, against 185 unused copies measured on 10-23; GLEAM: gleamUsedHelpers present; C-STATIC: generated C such as samples/closure_apply/main.c declares its functions static). It reached the core through pack-next4/next6, which were composed into the installed stacks. Closing condition, set now: Done when the running bundle accept (accept-6, job-1793136844465) is GREEN, since that accept blesses the current outputs as the oracle; if it's RED for unrelated reasons, close on the next GREEN accept.")
inl note_of_gleam_helpers_on_demand_3 () = Note ("GLEAM-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND", 3u32, "2026-10-27T23:22Z", "main", "10-27 19:22 main: Closed by the condition set in n2: the bundle accept is GREEN (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 7,367 rows, 0 blocking cells), so the current outputs carrying this pass are the oracle.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_root_flag_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ROOT-FLAG", 1u32, "2026-10-23T08:16Z", "eoie", "04:16 friction measured on myself: eoie agile next --team eoie (and --prefix EOIE) answered 'unexpected argument eoie' three times, because --team filled the <root> slot and its VALUE was reported; the usage list even names eoie as valid. Fix drafted in scratch agile-root-flag/apply-agile-root-flag.ps1 (src frozen until F9): agile_subcommand_domain gets root_place (RootLeads, RootAfterMode for migrate-tags and notes) and root_operand (RootNamed, RootFlagged via lib sm'.starts_with); agile_entry refuses RootFlagged with 'agile next takes <root> before any flag, got --team; usage: agile next <shape>' and takes the state lock on the RootNamed value, which also fixes migrate-tags and notes locking argument 1 (a mode word) instead of their root. Rule: ships only if agile_mutation.rs stays under 1000 lines (981 today, headroom 19) and next_cli plus the new domain test pass; over 1000 -> move the refusal into agile_subcommand_domain behind an export crate instead of trimming.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_root_flag_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ROOT-FLAG", 2u32, "2026-10-23T08:26Z", "eoie", "04:26 slice dry-run clean on a copy of the 3 packages (idempotent, LF kept) and chained into apply-rJ.ps1 after note-filter (agile-words only inserts --words after --prefix, so the usage prefix the test pins still holds); J's regen filter gains eoie_agile_mutation. Full-chain prelaunch (G, E2, 5 job-env slices, H, J, K with the GE2 and HIJK release scripts) clean at 04:26. next_cli is already an HIJK candidate. Left: the 1000-line rule check in HIJK's preview.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_root_flag_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ROOT-FLAG", 3u32, "2026-10-23T12:04Z", "eoie", "08:04 fixed before shipping: the slice's test block called expect, which subcommand.spi does not define, so it would have failed J's typecheck; the slice now adds the local expect helper. Verified: subcommand.spi passes on a post-GE2 tree with I and J applied.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_root_flag_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ROOT-FLAG", 4u32, "2026-10-23T12:36Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:36 x: a second case of the same friction, from x: 'agile note docs/backlog X-E2E-NON-ASCII --as x <text>' answered only 'usage: agile note <root> <id> <text> [--as <author>]'. --as filled the <text> slot, and the error didn't name the flag. Moving --as to the end worked. Suggestion for the same slice: accept --as at any position after <id>, or refuse with 'agile note takes <text> before --as'.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_root_flag_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ROOT-FLAG", 5u32, "2026-10-23T19:08Z", "eoie", "15:08 eoie: x's second case drafted as scratch note-as-first/apply-note-as-first.ps1. agile note takes --as on either side of the text: <root> <id> <text> --as <a> and <root> <id> --as <a> <text> both record the note. A note_request record (body, author choice) replaces the position-bound author_choice, and the arity router's 4|6 is unchanged. The empty-text check (now also for --as a with empty text) and the rule that an agent must name the author are unchanged, and the usage text is unchanged, so existing tests still pin it. A notes_cli test covers both orders plus the empty text. Dry run on the post-L copy is clean and idempotent. Queued for the release after N, next to the root-flag slice already in J.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_root_flag_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ROOT-FLAG", 6u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-23, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_root_flag_7 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ROOT-FLAG", 7u32, "2026-10-31T23:49Z", "kit", "10-31 19:5x kit, pause re-decision (main pre-night groom; R3 is now \"never auto-pause for silence\"): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the 10-28 silence groom (\"nothing queued\"), not by a blocker, and no note names an external condition. Back in agile next at its priority; its first step stays the one in the 10-28 note: check whether the remaining milestone shipped in a later train (installed kit 4983F9C0, TRAIN6) and close it with that evidence, else do the next milestone.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_root_flag_8 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ROOT-FLAG", 8u32, "2026-11-04T20:03Z", "kit", "11-04 20:3x kit: DONE, verified on the INSTALLED kit (TRAIN14c): `kit agile <verb> --open` for list, next, show, note and set each exits with \"kit error: agile <verb> takes <root> before any flag, got --open; usage: agile <verb> <root> ...\", i.e. it refuses the flag in the root slot, names it and prints the verb's usage (agile_subcommand_domain root_place + the shared message, test in agile_task_next/next_cli.spi). The verbs added since (lint, gate, cadence) use the default RootFirst place, so the same guard covers them once TRAIN23 installs; sprint is RootAfterMode by design (`agile sprint start <root>`). While here, the usage shapes the guard prints for lint and cadence were stale and now list --team / --since / --round (unstaged, europa --check ok).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_root_flag_9 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ROOT-FLAG", 9u32, "2026-11-06T11:33Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-ROOT-FLAG (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_progress_1 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T09:11Z", "eoie", "05:11 split from EOIE-MACHINE-WATCH (closed: its verb shipped in release F). Design, model and wiring are in that item's notes 10-12: scratch f5/src/eoie_notebook_progress (model.spi, progress.spi) and notebook-progress/apply-notebook-progress.ps1. Order: model typecheck (post-F preview), lib landing (last_write_ms), then its own release after HIJK with a --baseline-merge line.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_progress_2 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T09:58Z", "eoie", "05:58 model typechecks and passes its 3 test blocks (notebook check models2, 05:54), after adding the local expect every scratch model carries (LIB-TESTING-EXPECT tracks moving it into the lib). Both files formatted with the reviewed formatter. progress.spi joins the package modules once my lib batch (last_write_ms) lands.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_progress_3 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T10:46Z", "eoie", "06:46 full package typechecks against the live lib (model + progress.spi with file_system.last_write_ms; notebook check livelib). Found on the way: the scratch model tree's src/spiral was a copy of lib from 10-20, so every scratch model check since then ran on a 3-day-old lib (the new last_write_ms was invisible); it is now a junction to C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral, and notebook progress, accept-review and adoption-habits models all pass against the live lib.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_progress_4 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS", 4u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): JOB-5 (typed progress counts instead of sleep loops). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_progress_5 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS", 5u32, "2026-10-31T09:19Z", "kit", "2026-10-31 05:2x kit: RECORD WAS STALE. Since the wipe this item said 0% Planned, yet src/kit_notebook_progress (model.spi with 3 tests, progress.spi, export kit_notebook_batch_line) landed on 10-25. kit_machine_watch/run.spi calls it for every process row and prints '== notebook batches:'. The 10-23 notes still pointed at scratch drafts in f5/; nobody updated the item after the landing. GAP FIXED NOW (unstaged): release trains were invisible to it, because each train notebook runs alone with --output-path, not as a --output-dir batch. model.spi gains train_verdict (Pending/Passed/TimedOut/Failed) and train_progress, read from the train's own logs. progress.spi's train_line handles any '...\\scripts\\release-train.ps1 -Name <n>' process: it reads <root>/.cache/release-train/<n>/notebooks.txt and each notebook's log and prints 'release train <n>: D of T notebooks done, P pass, X timed out (a starved run, retried), F failed'. That replaces the hand loops lanes ran tonight to count train5e's .ipynb files. Two new test blocks (command parsing, verdict sorting with a timeout counted apart from a failure). Notebook and in-place regen are running.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_progress_6 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS", 6u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33): Active -> BLOCKED on the TRAIN7 install. The code is in the tree and verified on the node TRAIN7 candidates (923AAF5A/FB34FD11, notebooks green); the only remaining step is installing TRAIN7, then close with an acceptance run on the installed binary (WIP convention).")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_progress_7 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS", 7u32, "2026-11-01T12:44Z", "kit", "11-01 08:5x kit (Blocked rule). blocked by: EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN (the TRAIN7 install; the train line in kit_notebook_progress is verified on the node). Close with 'kit machine watch' showing a notebook batch line on the installed binary.")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_progress_8 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS", 8u32, "2026-11-01T13:17Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit: blocked by: KIT-TRAIN7 (the TRAIN7 install, now its own closable item split from EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN).")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_progress_9 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS", 9u32, "2026-11-01T19:35Z", "kit", "11-01 15:35 kit ACCEPTANCE on the INSTALLED TRAIN7 binary (kit.exe A25B8FBD): a Cells batch-mode run (run_notebook.ps1 --path x3 --output-dir, kit job job-1793561524824-kit-accept-batch) plus \"kit machine watch\" printed \"== notebook batches:\" and \"pid 29308 notebook batch: 0 of 3 written (3 share a report name, not counted), none yet after 2 min\". The three modules are all named model.spi, so their reports collide on model.spi.ipynb and are correctly not counted. One line per running batch, as specified. DONE. GAP found (not this item's scope): kit/scripts/notebook-batch.ps1 calls the runner once per module with --output-path, so its batches are never detected (the watch shows only the job line). It is fixed by construction when KIT-RUN-SPI replaces notebook-batch.ps1 (noted there).")
inl note_of_kit_notebook_progress_10 () = Note ("KIT-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS", 10u32, "2026-11-06T11:33Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_job_host_inherit_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_adoption_habits_1 () = Note ("KIT-ADOPTION-HABITS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:29Z", "eoie", "06:29 why: the adoption sweep says 19,447 of 32,687 shell calls in the last 8 days were habit calls, but not WHICH habits, so the guide cannot target the biggest ones. Today habit_named (eoie_adoption host.spi:69) is one host-macro blob that answers a bool over 18 words (get-content, select-string, get-childitem, set-content, add-content, out-file, foreach-object, measure-object, copy-item, remove-item, move-item, test-path, start-sleep, robocopy, findstr, python, python3, py.exe, plus [io.file]). Design: a typed habit union with one case per habit and its word (union_reflection for the lookup, like agile_task_query's flags), a per-habit tally in model.spi, and 'eoie adoption --habits' printing habit TAB calls TAB share, sorted by calls; the blob migrates into the union (AGENTS.md: touching legacy migrates it), word-boundary matching moves to lib sm' calls. Rule for the guide: its next section covers the habits that together make up 80% of habit calls in the --habits table, largest first. Tests: model test over synthetic commands, adoption CLI notebook with a fixture transcript.")
inl note_of_kit_adoption_habits_2 () = Note ("KIT-ADOPTION-HABITS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T10:37Z", "eoie", "06:37 pure model passes (scratch f5/src/eoie_adoption_habits_model, notebook check habits5): habit union with one word per case (19 cases incl. python3, py.exe and [io.file]), whole-word matching on lib code points (python3 is not python, my-get-contents.ps1 is not get-content), one count per habit per call, text table of the habits that occur in union order. Typecheck lessons recorded in memory (precedence of |> not with &&; annotate lambda params for record fields). Next: the --habits flag in eoie_adoption with the blob migrated into this union, after GE2.")
inl note_of_kit_adoption_habits_3 () = Note ("KIT-ADOPTION-HABITS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:57Z", "eoie", "07:57 wired: scratch adoption-habits/apply-adoption-habits.ps1 (on the raw chain after G's heal) adds habits.spi (the passing model + habit_any), threads a typed tally_mode (ByDay | ByHabit) through calls_tallied/files_tallied/client_tallied, prints 'habit TAB calls' largest first under --habits with receipt 'mode=habits days=n habits=k', switches classify to habits.habit_any and removes the 18-word host blob habit_named (migration; same word-boundary semantics). Full L dry chain applies; on that tree classify.spi (the migration's regression net) and habits.spi pass. First run hit the here-string trailing-newline trap twice (two lines glued); fixed. Ships in release L with an after-install probe; closes on PASS.")
inl note_of_kit_adoption_habits_4 () = Note ("KIT-ADOPTION-HABITS", 4u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-23, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_kit_adoption_habits_5 () = Note ("KIT-ADOPTION-HABITS", 5u32, "2026-10-31T23:49Z", "kit", "10-31 19:5x kit, pause re-decision (main pre-night groom; R3 is now \"never auto-pause for silence\"): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the 10-28 silence groom (\"nothing queued\"), not by a blocker, and no note names an external condition. Back in agile next at its priority; its first step stays the one in the 10-28 note: check whether the remaining milestone shipped in a later train (installed kit 4983F9C0, TRAIN6) and close it with that evidence, else do the next milestone.")
inl note_of_kit_adoption_habits_6 () = Note ("KIT-ADOPTION-HABITS", 6u32, "2026-11-04T20:19Z", "kit", "11-04 20:5x kit: NOT shipped in the installed kit: `kit adoption` and `kit adoption habits` both print per-day rows (client, day, shell_calls, kit_calls, habit_calls, kit_share), not habit calls per habit. The tree has a kit_adoption_habits package; check whether it is wired before resuming.")
inl note_of_kit_adoption_habits_7 () = Note ("KIT-ADOPTION-HABITS", 7u32, "2026-11-04T20:32Z", "kit", "11-04 21:4x kit: CORRECTION of my 20:5x note, and DONE: it IS shipped, as a flag, not a subcommand (I had tried `kit adoption habits`). `kit adoption --habits` on the INSTALLED kit prints \"habit<TAB>calls\" one row per habit, largest first: foreach-object 11412, get-content 10629, select-string 9774, get-childitem 5440, [io.file] 4711, test-path 2699, copy-item 2014, remove-item 1267, set-content 1242, ... (kit_adoption run.spi ByHabit mode over kit_adoption_habits.habit_counts). That is exactly \"lists habit calls per habit so the guide targets the largest\". Read for the guide: the top 3 shell habits (ForEach-Object, Get-Content, Select-String) are what kit read/grep/do should absorb first.")
inl note_of_kit_adoption_habits_8 () = Note ("KIT-ADOPTION-HABITS", 8u32, "2026-11-06T11:33Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-ADOPTION-HABITS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_1 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:41Z", "x", "2026-10-23 06:41 x: user request 2026-10-23 06:3x: 'extend sim so we reach full x feature parity, but only after all the elixir got rewritten in spiral'. Gate: deps SIM-IN-SPIRAL (the remaining Elixir half: World/Runner supervision, mix tasks); do not start while any apps/sim Elixir remains. Starting point for the inventory when it starts: today the sim models post, reply (parent), edit (revisions), delete, like/unlike, and frames (threads split into posts, edits of frames). xc also uses timelines per account with paging, following lists, users lookup by name and id, posts lookup by id with referenced posts (quote, reply, repost), conversation search for threads, OAuth 1.0a / 2.0 auth and token refresh, post and delete writes, and X's text rules (weights, autolinks, HTML escaping, trailing-whitespace trim), which e2e's Python stub covers today. First step: list each xc feature against the sim with a covered/missing column, then add the missing ones as sim slices in Spiral, each with its parity test against the stub's current behaviour, until e2e can run against the sim instead of the stub.")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_2 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 2u32, "2026-10-23T13:34Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:34 x: PARITY TARGET inventory (planning only; work still waits for SIM-IN-SPIRAL). x's model of the X API today is the e2e Python stub (apps/client/e2e/stub.py), so 'full x feature parity' means the sim serves at least what the stub serves. GET: /2/users/by (usernames), /2/users (ids), /2/tweets (id lookup, 100 per call), /2/tweets/:id, /2/tweets/search/recent (7 days) and /search/all (30 days, start_time), /2/users/:id/following (paged by next_token, following_page_size), /2/users/:id/tweets (timeline, exclude=replies,retweets). POST: /2/tweets (create, reply via reply.in_reply_to_tweet_id, assigns ids, conversation_id and replied_to refs), /2/oauth2/token (refresh rotation, client Basic auth). DELETE: /2/tweets/:id (own posts only). Cross-cutting: 4 auth contexts (app bearer, user bearer, OAuth 1.0a signature check, client Basic) and the 403 for app-only on writes; problem+json errors (400/401/403/404); edit_history_post_ids; HTML escaping of & < > on output; public_metrics; url entities with expanded/display/title; referenced posts and their expansions; a request log the e2e asserts on. GAP found while reading: the stub caps text at 280 Python characters (code points), not X's weighted count, so it accepts a 200-CJK post that X would refuse at weight 400. The sim should enforce the weighted rule (glyphs plain_weight + X-EMOJI-WEIGHT's entities) and return X's 400 problem. Its world model (inbox/scheduler) already has posts, edits, likes and deletes; users, following, timelines, search windows and auth are what remains to model.")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_3 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 3u32, "2026-10-24T11:21Z", "x", "10-24 07:21 x: estimate by rule, not by guess: 90m per X API endpoint family that the client calls and the sim does not serve (a sim handler, a world-model field, and one e2e row that runs the client against the sim). Code check on HEAD 6ef9c48: apps/client/wire.spi calls 7 families (/2/tweets lookup, /2/tweets create+delete, /2/tweets/search/all, /2/users lookup, /2/users/by, following, user timeline), and apps/sim mentions no /2/ path, so 7 x 90 = 630m. Load is medium: each family needs one sim compile plus one client e2e, not a matrix. Re-size after SIM-IN-SPIRAL lands, because the Spiral world may change the per-family cost.")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_4 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 4u32, "2026-10-31T03:22Z", "x", "2026-10-30 23:3x x: reference update (no start: SIM-IN-SPIRAL still has Elixir, per n1's gate). The parity target in n2 is now apps/client/e2e/stub.ps1 (stub.py was ported to PowerShell tonight at the user's request; the A/B gave the same 170 request rows, X-CLIENT n19). n2's GAP is already closed in the stub: create() refuses text by X's weighted count (x_weight: code-point weights, emoji = 2, URLs = 23, NFC) at > 280, so a 200-CJK post gets the 400 problem.")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_5 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 5u32, "2026-10-31T12:36Z", "x", "2026-10-31 08:36 x: FIRST STEP (n1's covered/missing census), read-only, done now that apps/sim has no Elixir in the tree (SIM-IN-SPIRAL n145; verify41 proving it). FINDING: the sim already covers the READ data model: its world files (apps/client/e2e/sim-*.world.json, from 'sim-run.ps1 world') carry users {id, name, username, posts} and posts {id, text, created_at, conversation_id, edit_history, metrics, refs {id, type}} (sim-stress: 276 posts, 274 with refs), the exact fields stub.ps1 serves; e2e/world.json pulls them in through its 'include' list. What the sim lacks is the SERVER and four bits of world. Per family (stub.ps1 handler -> sim): users/by (Get-UsersBy), users by ids (Get-UsersByIds), user timeline (Get-Timeline), post lookup and single post (Get-Lookup, Get-Single), search recent/all (Get-Search; needs now + created_at windows): DATA COVERED, handler missing. following (Get-Following): MISSING data (users' 'following' lists and following_page_size exist only in the hand-written world.json: carol, loner, mixed). create/delete (New-Post, Remove-Post): MISSING, needs a live mutable world (the sim's inbox/scheduler models post/edit/delete as events, but no world serves writes). Cross-cutting, MISSING: the 4 auth contexts (app/user bearer, OAuth 1.0a signature check, client Basic) and token refresh rotation (Get-Renewed), problem+json errors including the fault users (broke/busy/gateway, protected dave), HTML escaping and field rendering (Get-Rendered, Get-WithExpansions), the request log e2e asserts on. RE-SIZE by n3's rule: 7 families x 90m = 630m stands, plus ~120m for the cross-cutting auth/problem layer (one slice), so ~750m. SHAPE: the cheapest route is porting stub.ps1's handlers into a Spiral sim server, one family per slice, each proven by running e2e.ps1 against the sim with the same 153-170 request rows the stub gives (the stub stays the oracle until the last slice).")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_6 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 6u32, "2026-10-31T12:39Z", "x", "2026-10-31 08:39 x: SERVER DESIGN (read-only, from e2e.ps1 lines 12-64 and stub_http.ps1). Contract to keep: e2e starts '<server> world.json port.txt requests.jsonl', waits for the port file, and asserts on the JSONL request log ({method, path, query, authorized, context[, body]}). Sim side: 'scripts/sim-run.ps1 serve WORLD PORTFILE LOG' -> its own simframes export cli_serve (one export per path): OTP json:decode the world plus its include list; gen_tcp listen on 127.0.0.1 port 0 with {packet, http_bin} (BEAM parses the request line and headers natively; switch to raw for Content-Length bodies), write the port file, accept loop, reply 'Connection: close'. INCREMENTAL PROOF: until every family is ported, the sim server starts stub.ps1 on a second port and forwards the families it does not serve yet, writing the log row itself, so e2e.ps1 (switched by one env var, XSIM_SERVER=sim) runs the full 37-step tutorial against every partial server and must give the stub's request rows. Slices: (1) serve skeleton + forwarding + request log + users/by and users ids; (2) timeline + following (adds following lists to the sim world); (3) lookup + single + expansions/rendering/escaping; (4) search windows; (5) auth contexts + token refresh + problem+json and the fault users; (6) writes (create/delete on a live world); (7) drop the forwarding; stub.ps1 stays as the oracle in a parity check. Slice 1 filed as SIM-X-SERVE-USERS.")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_7 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 7u32, "2026-10-31T13:42Z", "x", "2026-10-31 09:42 x: SLICE RE-SCOPE after reading stub.ps1's handlers (lines 146-245): the user timeline renders posts through the same Get-Rendered/Get-WithExpansions code as lookup and single post (post.fields, expansions referenced_posts(.author_id), attachments.media_keys + media.fields, HTML escaping, edit_history_post_ids defaulting to [id], public_metrics zero-filled), so it moves into the rendering slice. New order: (1) users/by + users ids [SIM-X-SERVE-USERS, probing]; (2) following: max_results 1..1000 else 400 'Invalid Request', page = min(max_results, world.following_page_size), pagination_token 'p<start>', meta.result_count and next_token 'p<start+count>', data omitted on an empty page [SIM-X-SERVE-FOLLOWING]; (3) rendering + expansions, then timeline (max_results 5..100, the plain_status/status fault users, protected -> 200 with an Authorization Error entry, exclude replies/retweets by ref kind, start_time/end_time on the first 19 chars of created_at by ordinal compare, newest first by a stable sort, meta.result_count), post lookup and single post; (4) search recent/all windows from world.now; (5) auth contexts + refresh + problem+json; (6) writes; (7) drop forwarding.")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_8 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 8u32, "2026-10-31T18:25Z", "x", "2026-10-31 14:25 x: docs updated in x (unstaged): apps/sim/spi/README.md gains the sim_serve.spi row (what it answers and which auth it checks), the cli_diff/cli_serve exports, the diff subcommand in sim_args' row, a fixed intro (the Elixir lib/xsim is gone, no longer 'the port of'), and a paragraph on XSIM_SERVER=sim (sim-run.ps1 serve starts stub.ps1 beside it, relays the families not yet served byte for byte, writes the same log rows, and prints 'xsim serve / answered METHOD PATH' for its own answers); apps/client/README.md line 12 names the XSIM_SERVER=sim replay (prose, outside the tutorial's checked blocks).")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_9 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 9u32, "2026-10-31T21:31Z", "x", "2026-10-31 17:31 x: HANDOFF at the end of today's run. STATE: slices 1-6 Done (SIM-X-SERVE-USERS, -FOLLOWING, -RENDER, -SEARCH, -AUTH, -WRITES); in x-serveprobe14 the sim answered all 153 requests of the 37-step tutorial (e2e green, stub served none). Code, all unstaged in x: apps/sim/spi/sim_serve.spi (registered in spiral.toml), simframes cli_serve export, scripts/sim-run.ps1 'serve' (starts stub.ps1 beside it, still used as the fallback relay), apps/client/e2e/e2e.ps1 (XSIM_SERVER=sim, 300 s start wait, process-tree stop), scripts/e2e-parity.ps1 (stub vs sim request logs row for row), READMEs. Probe launchers live in this session's scratchpad (launch-x-serveprobe.ps1, launch-x-parityprobe.ps1; default core D1C49B8BDC3318F7). NEXT: (1) read x-parityprobe (job-1793481488711) by SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE n3; fix any differing row in sim_serve; (2) slice 7 = SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE: remove forwarded/relayed/drained, the stub port argument and sim-run's child stub, and the stale rule; unknown routes get the stub's 404 'no route'; keep e2e-parity as the oracle; (3) a full x verify (x-node-verify.ps1) to land the regenerated xsim_frames sources together with sim_serve, since every verify since 12:27 has been older than the live source; (4) decide with the user whether XSIM_SERVER=sim becomes e2e's default. Traps met today are in memory spiral-one-export-per-path (one export per path, :~ helpers, keywords, list/record ';', no shorthand in updates, nested alias updates, charlist vs binary, atom vs binary map keys).")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_10 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 10u32, "2026-10-31T21:34Z", "x", "2026-10-31 17:34 x: correction to n9's handoff: the probe launchers are now committed, not scratchpad-only: i574n.github/scripts/x-node-e2e.ps1 -Label <l> [-Mode sim|parity] [-MirrorName xqueue3] [-Core <16-hex>] builds sim + client on the node, then runs e2e.ps1 with XSIM_SERVER=sim (printing the sim's answered-line counts) or scripts/e2e-parity.ps1, with node paths derived as in x-node-verify.ps1 and failed launches printing their last lines. Parse-checked; not yet run through (the running x-parityprobe used the scratchpad launcher with the same command body).")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_11 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 11u32, "2026-10-31T21:50Z", "x", "2026-10-31 17:50 x: node fed (main's NODE-ALWAYS-FED request): PRE-REGISTERED verify43 through i574n.github/scripts/x-node-verify.ps1 (-Label x-verify43 -MirrorName xqueue1 -Core D1C49B8BDC3318F7). PASS = SIM-IN-SPIRAL n145's rule (exit 0, the five 'x verify /' steps exit 0, all notebooks, x-compat 0, the 11 fixtures unchanged in the land dry run with 0 conflicts, the thread_run tamper check, no mix xsim/mix test), on a tree that now carries sim_serve slices 1-6, e2e-parity.ps1 and the docs. If PASS: land its regenerated apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames*.gleam (-Apply), since no source edit follows this sync.")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_12 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 12u32, "2026-11-01T03:52Z", "x", "2026-10-31 23:52 x: verify43 (n11's rule) is VOID. I stopped it at 5 h 46 min because sim_serve.spi changed after it started (the overweight split for X-COMPAT-VIA-CELLS), so its regenerated frames sources would be older than the live source and can't land. verify44 (job-1793505156395, pack 11 core CA42B2E62EB69EA3) takes over with the same landing step.")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_13 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 13u32, "2026-11-01T08:55Z", "x", "2026-11-01 04:55 x: verify44 PASSED (X-COMPAT-VIA-CELLS n7), but its 128 outputs are HELD, not landed. The dry run lists 28 regenerated xsim_frames sources plus 1 CONFLICT (apps/client/README.md, changed here by the cell-budget rewording and by the run's accept). The frames sources were generated from verify44's tree, and the live sim sources have moved since: the user's 48bdcb2 (clock_ms, metrics, budget splits) plus unstaged raw-pair rows and the lean serve launch. Landing them would desync generated Gleam from its Spiral, the same hold as earlier verifies. verify45 (X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS' batch) regenerates from the current tree and lands with -Apply. This item closes then: 7/7 slices done, parity proven 4 times tonight (typed2, typed3, metrics1, lean1).")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_14 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 14u32, "2026-11-01T13:12Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:12 x: CLOSED. All 7 slices are done, parity was proven 4 times on 10-31/11-01 (typed2, typed3, metrics1, lean1: 153/153, 0 differing), and verify46 (X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS n7) regenerated and LANDED the frames sources from the current tree (59 files, 0 conflicts). The sim is now e2e's default X API server.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_1 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 06:52 x: split out of SIM-IN-SPIRAL after the 10-22/23 night retrospective. world.ex is a pure state machine (apply_delivery, drain, gathered, rekey, wanted_key, take_dirty) and has no Spiral counterpart (client world.spi is the posting side). Nothing in it needs gleam_otp actors or KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, so marking all of SIM-IN-SPIRAL Blocked hid this work from the ready list for two nights. Shape: unions for the 7 delivery kinds (log.ex @fields), a record world, exhaustive matches, the oracle being the current Elixir via the existing ExUnit and corpus-commute fixtures until the Spiral module replaces it. Until the user's commit lands, work in a scratch copy with an anchored apply script (memory verify-drafts-on-scratch-copies).")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_2 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:09Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:09 x: grooming, the user's 10-23 directions applied to the first rewrite step. (1) BACKEND-BALANCE: write it backend-neutral and test it on rust AND gleam; it runs inside the Gleam runner and on Rust for batch sweeps. (2) Lib first: build on listm' / sm' / resultm and LIB-PAR-MAP; anything generic it needs (a map/set over keys, a dedup set) goes into lib/spiral first, not into x. (3) No host blobs: pure Spiral domain (unions for the delivery kinds, records), with any Gleam boundary through granular primitives (LIB-OTP-ACTORS note 2), never global or rust_global code.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_3 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:16Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:16 x: plan and rules for today's run (until 18:00), written before any code. FACTS: world.ex needs a map (posts, keys, frames, demand, clock), a set (seen, dirty) and SHA-256 (rekey: first 16 hex chars of sha256(parent_key, 0, source)). lib/spiral has none of these backend-neutrally: mapm wraps Rust/F# HashMap/BTreeMap only, crypto's sha256 has F#/Python/Rust arms only, and there is no gleam/dict or gleam_crypto binding. STEPS: (1) draft lib modules in a lib scratch copy, granular per the user's 10-23 direction (one nominal per foreign type, one one-expression primitive per foreign function, no blobs). They are a persistent map (Rust std BTreeMap via persistent copy-on-insert or im-style; Gleam gleam/dict), a set on top of it, and a sha256-hex (Rust sha2 crate; Gleam gleam_crypto hash Sha256 + bit_array base16). Test blocks on rust + gleam, plus a fixed-vector sha test ('abc' -> ba7816bf...). (2) Port world.ex to a new module (name chosen after a clash scan) on top of them, with unions for the 7 delivery kinds and records, plus test blocks ported from xsim_test's World cases. (3) Compile-check both arms (rust crate entry, gleam via simframes-style entry) under eoie compile windows at >= 3.5 GB free. (4) Cells test runs only when no other Cells job is active. RULES: never touch x's staged tree or lib/spiral itself (scratch copies plus anchored apply scripts); hand the lib drafts to main for landing (main owns lib) and keep x's port on them; fallback if the map binding stalls past 13:00: an association-list map behind the same interface, swapped later. Progress is recorded here with timestamps.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_4 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-23T11:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:27 x: step 1 done for the map: LIB-DICTM drafted and passing on rust + gleam under Cells. Correction to note 3: SHA-256 is NOT a gap; lib crypto.hash_text is already backend-neutral (Gleam via an @external to erlang crypto:hash, plus Rust, Zig, F# and TS arms, fixed-vector tests). Next: the world.ex port on dictm + crypto.hash_text, before tibia's live block at about 09:40 (no compiles during it).")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_5 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-23T11:46Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:46 x: step 2 done in draft: world.ex is ported to Spiral as module 'inbox' (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\inbox.spi; scratch package %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\client = x client + dictm + inbox, x untouched). It reuses events.spi's delivery/event unions and splits state into small records (intake, graph, reactions, tallies) so each definition fits one post. Under Cells (process compiler, memo off, no other Cells job active): 6/6 test blocks pass on rust -d sha2 AND gleam (217 s). They cover dedup, a child before its parent waiting then landing, frames in any order rebuilding the post, an older unlike not undoing a newer like, an edit rekeying children, and keys_match_the_elixir_world: both backends give exactly Elixir's keys (022a6979e6dab7aa / 451526e1c47e6023, computed with the same :crypto.hash(:sha256, [parent_key, 0, source]) on elixir 1.20.4). Fixed on the way: 6 name clashes renamed (edited, frame_at, gathered, posted, step, wanted); x-compat autolinks on .social and .parts fields (now reactions / frame_parts); a tuple accumulator in a fold (see LIB-DICTM note 2). Next: a parity run against the Elixir World on real event streams, then hand dictm to main and the module to x's tree after the user's commit.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_6 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-23T11:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:52 x: PARITY with the Elixir World on a real event stream, both backends. %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\gen_parity.exs loads the actual apps/sim/lib/xsim/world.ex + frames.ex (Code.require_file), replays apps/sim/fixtures/prelude.events.jsonl (65 deliveries: 19 frame, 22 edit_frame, 20 like, 4 unlike) through Xsim.World and writes a Spiral test that rebuilds the same deliveries with typed constructors and asserts Elixir's results. Spiral's inbox gives exactly the same on rust -d sha2 and gleam (Cells, 96 s): tallies framed=6 edits=6 dups=14 buffered=0, all 6 post keys (p0=3f5400c8092236b1 ... p5=f86e168f9079f0b8) and the same 6 wanted keys. inbox gained frame_from (a frame with a parent) and edit_frame_at. The generated parity test stays scratch tooling (its fixture literal would not fit x-compat's one-post rule); the generator is the reusable part. Slip: this run overlapped eoie's notebook check by about a minute because my script printed the foreign runner instead of refusing; eoie was told, and the script now refuses (exit 75) while any other run_notebook is active.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_7 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-23T13:49Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:49 x: dependency recorded: LIB-DICTM. inbox.spi (and scheduler.spi on top of it) use dictm, and by lib-first dictm lands in lib/spiral (main's), not as a copy in x. LANDING PLAN once main lands dictm: inbox.spi, scheduler.spi, runner_checks.spi and the parity tests (inbox_parity, scheduler_parity, checks_parity) join apps/client's package next to invariants/world/scenario, where the sim's Spiral already lives (simframes exports). The parity tests' generators (gen_parity.exs, gen_runner_parity.exs, gen_checks_parity.exs) become mix tasks in apps/sim (xsim.parity), so the fixtures are regenerated from the Elixir oracle rather than hand-kept, until each Elixir module is retired. All sources are in %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_8 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-23T13:51Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:51 x: the generator mix task is drafted and checked: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\xsim.parity.ex (Mix.Tasks.Xsim.Parity, 'mix xsim.parity --events FILE --out DIR'). It replaces the three loose generators (gen_parity / gen_runner_parity / gen_checks_parity .exs). It reads the stream through the real Xsim.Log.decode, runs the real World, Runner (fake executor) and Invariants.check, and writes inbox_parity.spi, scheduler_parity.spi and checks_parity.spi with their headers, so no PowerShell assembly is left. Checked by loading the task source next to the real xsim modules (check_parity_task.exs, Elixir only) on prelude.events.jsonl: 65 deliveries, 6 posts, 6 visible. The fixture is byte-equal to the hand-verified inbox_parity.spi, and every final expectation equals the Cells-verified files. checks_parity keeps only the real Invariants.check test; the row-text copy test is dropped, because the real oracle supersedes it. Lands into apps/sim/lib/mix/tasks with the Spiral modules (note 7).")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_9 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-23T14:31Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:31 x: landing script for note 7's plan: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_sim_modules.ps1 [-Go]. It copies the 10 Spiral sim modules (inbox, inbox_parity, scheduler, scheduler_parity, runner_checks, checks_parity, tree_memory, sim_report, sim_args, sim_run) from x-world\\client into apps/client, lists them after invariants in package.spiproj (dependency order) and in build.ps1's Invoke-SpiralTests notebook list. Guards: it refuses (exit 75) until lib/spiral has dictm.spi (lib-first: no x copy of dictm) or while x has staged changes, and refuses if any module is missing or already present. Dry run now: refused on the dictm guard, as expected. xsim_entry is left out: as an entry it needs its own crate folder with Cargo.toml and a workspace entry, which come with the X-BACKEND-MAP decision, not this landing.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_10 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-23T19:25Z", "x", "2026-10-23 15:25 x: after the formatter pass (inbox 161 and scheduler 219 lines re-laid), the dictm no-host-callback rework, sorted Gleam keys and the sim_run import fix, everything passes again on rust + gleam: inbox 6/6, inbox_parity 1/1, scheduler 5/5 (with a_crashed_task_is_a_visible_crash_result and peak_results), scheduler_parity 1/1, checks_parity 2/2. Parity with the real Elixir World, Runner and Invariants holds on the formatted sources, so the formatter changed no meaning there. Remaining in chain9: tree_memory, sim_report, sim_args, sim_run, xsim_entry and the lib drafts.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_11 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-24T07:26Z", "x", "10-24 03:26 x: landing state of the 17 Spiral sim modules (apply_sim_modules.ps1). Its 'x has staged changes' refusal is replaced by an unstaged check on package.spiproj/build.ps1 only, per the new rule that work lands as an unstaged layer over the user's staged commit (memory land-drafts-in-the-repo). Dry run now refuses only on the real blocker: lib/spiral has no dictm.spi yet (main's main-lib-dictm-land-2, in main's lib chain, paused for tibia's PR-27 block). After dictm lands: run with -Verdicts <tonight's chain logs> (chain21c passed scheduler_parity, runner_checks, scenario_checks, runner_shell and executors; the rest are in requeue_after_block's chain21d), then -Go.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_12 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-25T10:24Z", "x", "2026-10-25 06:24 -04:00 x: xsim.parity LANDED (unstaged) in a new shape. It no longer writes .spi files with the fixture inlined (that broke the 280-weight cell rule: inbox_parity's literal list weighed 10,222). 'mix xsim.parity --events FILE --out FILE' writes one labelled line per expectation (tallies, keys, wanted, wanted_count, visible, visible_count, checks_fake, checks_wrong) from the real Xsim.World, Runner (fake executor) and Invariants.check. apps/sim/build.ps1 regenerates it after xsim.events and compares it with apps/sim/fixtures/prelude.parity.txt via Confirm-Fixture (-Accept rewrites). The Spiral parity modules hold no data: they read ../sim/fixtures/prelude.events.jsonl (the one recorded stream; one record per fact) and look up each expectation by label. The row-text test is dropped, as the draft said. A new test, the_prelude_fixture_is_a_canonical_stream, checks that Spiral's encoder re-emits the Elixir stream byte for byte. First run on the current stream: deliveries=61 posts=6 visible=6, dups=12 buffered=1. The old hand-kept expectations were for an older 65-delivery stream, the reason to generate them.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_13 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-10-27T12:41Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:41 x: groomed. LIB-DICTM is Done and lib/spiral/dictm.spi is live. The Spiral sim modules are already in the tree, in apps/sim/spi, not apps/client: inbox, scheduler, runner_checks, scenario_checks, tree_memory, sim_report, sim_args, sim_run, world, invariants and the rest are listed in apps/sim/spi/package.spiproj, and apps/sim/build.ps1's full pass runs their notebooks. The three *_parity modules were reshaped on 10-25 (xsim.parity writes fixtures/prelude.parity.txt, compared with Confirm-Fixture). So %TEMP%\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_sim_modules.ps1, which copies into apps/client, is obsolete; a dry run today refused on stale Cells verdicts and wrote nothing. What is left is a full sim build with every sim notebook green, which X-WIP-VERIFY's sim part runs (verify16b stopped at mix test under node load, before the notebooks).")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_14 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-10-28T10:39Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:39 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_1 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 06:52 x: split out of SIM-IN-SPIRAL with SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL. In runner.ex only start_queued (Task.async), harvest (Task.yield_many) and Task.shutdown touch processes; everything else is pure bookkeeping that can be modeled as 'state + event -> state + commands' (StartTask key, CancelTask key) in Spiral now. The effectful shell that executes those commands is what waits for KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (gleam_otp). Folds in X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES's crash result as a domain case.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_2 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:53Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:53 x: design and rules, written before code. Module 'scheduler' (name after a clash scan) on top of inbox. STATE (small records): the inbox; running (dict key -> owner ids) and the queue (list key); refs (key -> count), post_key (id -> key), owner (key -> id); results (key -> task_result); doomed (key -> deadline) with a clock; visible (id -> key, result); counters (starts, cancelled, evicted, cancels, rescues, recomputes, peaks). INPUTS: a delivery; a task finished (key, result); a task crashed (key, reason); the drain end. OUTPUTS: commands StartTask {key; sources} and CancelTask key, which a shell executes. The shell is Elixir Task today and gleam_otp later via LIB-OTP-ACTORS; it is not part of this item. Faults as values: cancel on/off, rerun_completed, evict Off | Now | Grace n, naive visibility, max_concurrency. Every runner.ex function maps 1:1 (retarget, release, acquire, doom, expire, rescued, evict, requeue_completed, start_queued, reconcile, settle). TESTS: a synchronous driver that runs StartTask at once with a fake executor (the same digest as Executors.fake: sha256 of the sources joined by NUL, first 12 hex), and a deferred driver that completes tasks only at drain, which exercises cancellation. PARITY RULE: compare against the Elixir Runner on the settled visible map (id -> key and result) and wanted keys, which are deterministic. Do not compare starts/cancels, which depend on Elixir task timing. Compile/test windows: before 09:40 or after tibia's block (about 12:10).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_3 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-23T12:03Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:03 x: drafted as module 'scheduler' (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\scheduler.spi, on top of inbox): every runner.ex function 1:1 as small pure functions over records (task_work, owner_map, life_cycle, run_counts), task_command StartTask/CancelTask as outputs, faults as values (cancel, rerun, eviction NoEviction/EvictNow/EvictAfter n, naive, limit), plus sync_run and deferred_run drivers and 4 test blocks (computed once, withdrawn demand cancels, same-source roots share one task, eviction then relike recomputes). A clash scan renamed command/settled/started (cli, invariants, refs). Type-checks and compiles to Rust (24 s, 304 KB) and Gleam (19 s, 140 KB) via a check entry. Cells runs are waiting for tibia's Cells run to finish (my script refused twice, as it should). Parity test generated from the real Elixir Runner (gen_runner_parity.exs, Code.require_file of frames/world/executors/runner, Executors.fake): on prelude.events.jsonl it settles 6 visible posts with keys and fake outputs (26 starts and 1 cancel there, which by the rule are not compared); scheduler_parity asserts the same settled map on both backends.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_4 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-23T13:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:06 x: PARITY PASSES. Cells (rust + gleam, process compiler, memo off): %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\client\\scheduler.spi 4/4 and scheduler_parity.spi 1/1. On the recorded prelude stream (65 deliveries), the Spiral scheduler's settled visible map equals the Elixir Runner's: 6 posts, each with the same source key and the same fake executor digest. Two fixes got there: (1) a test used {plain_faults () with ...}, a parse error, because record update needs a bound name, so it is now inl plain = plain_faults (); (2) sync_run and deferred_run now bind the delivery list dynamically (inl ~deliveries = l). Before that, a static 65-element fixture made the partial evaluator unroll the whole fold with every inl scheduler step inlined, and the cell hit Cells' 300 s timeout. Lesson for SIM-IN-SPIRAL: step functions over recorded streams take dynamic input. Next (in flight): runner_checks.spi, the typed bridge that replaces invariants.ex (scheduler state -> invariants.spi snapshot, rows sorted by id, no text rows), and checks_parity.spi, which compares the bridge's findings with (a) checked_text over the Elixir row text and (b) the real Xsim.Invariants.check, for a matching and a wrong baseline (expected 'sequential_equivalence=FAIL[p0,p1,p2,p3,p4]'). Both pass on gleam and fail on rust only because of the dictm fold bug (LIB-DICTM note 5).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_5 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-23T13:44Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:44 x: progress set to 60%, by the rule used for every x item today: verified on a scratch copy = 70%, and the last 30% is applying it in the real tree plus the full test run. This one sits 10 points lower until checks_parity passes on rust after the dictm fix (queued for after tibia's block, with the new crash-result test).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_6 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-23T13:57Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:57 x: xsim.run's pure half drafted as %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\sim_report.spi (queued for Cells after tibia's block). summary_line prints the same 'XSIM seed=.. schedule=.. ... peak_beam_mb=.. ms=.. <findings>' line as Mix.Tasks.Xsim.Run.summary, field for field in the same order, so xsim.diff and the logs keep working when the entry switches to Spiral. commuted_runs folds runs_commute over consecutive schedules (Enum.chunk_every(2, 1)), and verdict_line gives 'XSIM commute=.. result=pass|fail'. The scheduler gained run_counts.peak_results (max results size, updated in reconciled like runner.ex's reconcile). peak_beam_mb and ms come in through run_shape from the shell, since they are host measurements. Caught before compiling: 'x |> listm.append y' is y ++ x, so the fields are now joined by explicit nesting. 2 tests pin the line format and pass/fail.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_7 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-23T18:56Z", "x", "2026-10-23 14:56 x: checks_parity PASSES 2/2 on rust + gleam (self-gating chain9, 14:42, after the dictm no-host-callback fix and the formatter pass). On the recorded 65-delivery stream, runner_checks' findings equal both Elixir oracles on both backends: checked_text over the Elixir row text, and the real Xsim.Invariants.check, for a matching baseline and a wrong one ('sequential_equivalence=FAIL[p0,p1,p2,p3,p4]'). Scheduler parity passed earlier, so the runner core and its invariant bridge are verified on scratch. Progress 60 -> 70 (rule: verified on scratch; landing waits for LIB-DICTM, via apply_sim_modules.ps1).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_8 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-23T23:04Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:04 x: PORTING BUG found by desk check (mapping xsim_test.exs case 51 'forgetting finished results recompiles shared prefixes'), fixed in scratch, check queued after tibia's block. Elixir's Runner re-queues completed tasks (rerun_completed) only in the per-delivery reconcile; drain reconciles with rerun: false (runner.ex:166), and harvest doesn't reconcile at all. scheduler.spi's reconciled always read faults.rerun and is also called after every completion batch (executed_all, the crash test helper, runner_shell's Finished handler), so any run with rerun = true would re-queue forever and never settle. The parity tests all use plain faults, which is why it went unseen. Fix: reconciled_with (rerun) as the core, reconciled = per delivery (faults.rerun), drained = after completions (rerun false); executed_all, crash test and runner_shell use drained. With rerun false both are identical, so the parity results are unaffected (scheduler_parity rerun first in the queue). Regression tests added to runner_checks: rerunning_finished_results_recompiles_shared_prefixes (rerun -> one_compile_per_prefix FAIL, plain -> ok) and naive_visibility_without_cancelling_surfaces_a_stale_revision (case 39; a newest-first execution run stands in for Elixir's slow old revision: naive + no cancel -> no_stale_results FAIL[p0], plain -> ok).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_9 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-23T23:28Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:28 x: scheduler.spi gained an executor parameter for the synchronous runner: executed_by exec / executed_all_by exec / sync_run_with exec, with sync_run = sync_run_with fake_result (fake_result = the old inline {Done; fake_out}). Needed by the ported Elixir case 223 (an honest executor that errs on broken sources). Behavior for existing callers is unchanged; scheduler + scheduler_parity rerun first after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_10 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-24T00:37Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:37 x: scheduler.spi PASSES 5/5 on rust + gleam (chain20, 20:31-20:36) with both of tonight's changes: the rerun fix (reconciled_with / reconciled per delivery / drained after completions) and the executor parameter (executed_by / executed_all_by / sync_run_with). scheduler_parity (the Elixir oracle) is next in the chain; runner_checks then carries the regression tests for the rerun bug.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_11 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-24T00:40Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:40 x: scheduler_parity PASSES after tonight's changes (chain20, 20:36-20:39): the settled view still equals the real Elixir Runner's on the recorded 65-delivery stream. With rerun = false, reconciled_with is identical to the old reconciled, as predicted. runner_checks (the regression tests for the rerun bug) is next.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_12 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-24T01:02Z", "x", "2026-10-23 21:02 x: runner_checks PASSES 9/9 on rust + gleam (chain20, 20:44-20:5x): the 4 existing tests plus tonight's 5 runner-level ports of Elixir cases. Case 51, rerun recompiles shared prefixes, now settles and fails one_compile_per_prefix as Elixir does, which confirms the drain fix. Also case 39 naive stale revision, case 57 withdrawn demand, case 190 grace window and case 143 saved-stream replay. With scheduler 5/5 and scheduler_parity, the fixed core is verified on scratch. scenario_checks and everything after it in the queue didn't start before the 20:50 cutoff; re-run per X-DONE n68.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_13 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-10-24T03:51Z", "x", "23:51 x: chain21 (job-1792811662170) scheduler.spi: all 5 Rust cells timed out at 300 s. It passed 5/5 at 20:31. The box was saturated (CPU 100%, five heavy/compile windows across main, tibia and eoie), and main's listm-runtime land (23:05) changed listm'/seq runtime paths. RULE, written before the retry: retry_timeouts.ps1 (job x-chain21-retry, after chain21) reruns every notebook whose failures are all timeouts. Pass means contention. A timeout again means a regression, and I bisect against a pre-23:05 lib copy (x-libprog-roundtrip, 21:24, has the pre-tonight state) before anything that depends on scheduler lands.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_14 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-10-24T04:44Z", "x", "10-24 00:44 x: rule applied (n13). The timeout-only retry of scheduler.spi PASSED (job-1792813911883 attempt 2, 316 s, 00:43), so chain21's five 300 s timeouts were contention, not a regression from main's listm-runtime land. No bisect needed. The rest of chain21 is requeued as x-chain21c on the mutex-guarded compile-class gate.")
