inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_16 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 16u32, "2026-10-28T10:41Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:39 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_kit_crate_gate_1 () = Note ("KIT-CRATE-GATE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:58Z", "main", "2026-10-23 06:58 main: requested from CRATE-GATE-HARDEN. Reference behaviour is x/scripts/gate.ps1 (entry/output metadata, -Limit, -Enforce, vacuity). It needs repo-wide discovery, because dice keeps its crates in ui/ and contract/. eoie source-topology already counts .rs per crate dir, so the verb can extend it. First users: x (replaces its gate.ps1), dice and alphabet (report-only until their 15-16k-line monoliths are split).")
inl note_of_kit_crate_gate_2 () = Note ("KIT-CRATE-GATE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:38Z", "eoie", "07:38 drafted: scratch f5/src/eoie_crate_gate_model (model.spi pure: [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output parsing that stops at the next section, CRLF-safe line counts, within/OVER/no-output verdicts, enforce outcomes incl. the vacuity rule; gate.spi lib-only: repo-wide walk skipping dot folders, target, node_modules, _build, deps and lab, --limit via a code-point digit parser) typechecks and passes 2 test blocks against the live lib. Verb slice crate-gate/apply-crate-gate.ps1 adds VerbCrate (eoie crate gate <root> [--enforce] [--limit n]), usage, the spiral-tests registry entry (appended at the list tail, order-independent), eoie_cli dep and workspace member; prelaunch clean on a live copy. Ships in release L (candidate notebook + baseline merge added).")
inl note_of_kit_crate_gate_3 () = Note ("KIT-CRATE-GATE", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:42Z", "eoie", "07:42 main's ruling applied: under --enforce a crate with no generated output fails (GateFailedMissing n: 'n crate(s) have no generated output, so generation did not run or failed'), since enforcement runs right after generation; without --enforce it stays the 'no generated output yet' report. Order of refusals: vacuous, then over, then missing. Model test extended (enforce fails on missing; report-only passes), package passes against the live lib. dice ui (16,281) and alphabet documents (15,315) stay report-only until split.")
inl note_of_kit_crate_gate_4 () = Note ("KIT-CRATE-GATE", 4u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): CENSUS-2 (per-crate audit, thresholds from project policy, one count shared with topology). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_crate_gate_5 () = Note ("KIT-CRATE-GATE", 5u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_kit_crate_gate_6 () = Note ("KIT-CRATE-GATE", 6u32, "2026-10-31T23:48Z", "kit", "10-31 19:5x kit, pause re-decision (main pre-night groom; R3 is now \"never auto-pause for silence\"): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the 10-28 silence groom (\"nothing queued\"), not by a blocker, and no note names an external condition. Back in agile next at its priority; its first step stays the one in the 10-28 note: check whether the remaining milestone shipped in a later train (installed kit 4983F9C0, TRAIN6) and close it with that evidence, else do the next milestone.")
inl note_of_kit_crate_gate_7 () = Note ("KIT-CRATE-GATE", 7u32, "2026-11-04T20:18Z", "kit", "11-04 20:5x kit: DONE, verified on the INSTALLED kit (TRAIN14c): `kit crate gate <root> [--enforce] [--limit n]` reports one \"gate / <crate> <file> N lines ok|OVER largest <fn> M\" row per Cargo.toml with [package.metadata.spiral] found anywhere under the root (repo root C:\\home\\git\\kit -> 336 crates, not only src); with --enforce: an empty root refuses (\"no crate with [package.metadata.spiral] under the root, so nothing was measured\", exit 1), --limit 500 refuses on 182 over (exit 1), and crates without generated output refuse too (44 new unregenerated crates in today's tree, exit 1). Release trains already gate on it (TRAIN22 stopped there).")
inl note_of_kit_crate_gate_8 () = Note ("KIT-CRATE-GATE", 8u32, "2026-11-06T11:27Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-CRATE-GATE (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_1 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:00Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:00 x: BACKEND-BALANCE groundwork. Today lib/spiral has no parallel primitive and no rayon arm: eoie reaches rayon only through raw host-code blobs (eoie_do_read/host.spi:36, eoie_grep_query/search.spi:51, eoie_bundle_retention/retention_terminal.spi:7), which eoie's own style contract asks to remove. Shape: backend_switch with Rust = rayon par_iter over a Vec, results in input order; Gleam = one process per chunk (chunk count = schedulers), monitored, results collected in order, the first crash surfaced as a typed error; any other backend = the sequential map (same results, so tests agree everywhere). Test blocks on rust + gleam: order kept, a raising element is reported, an empty list works. Benchmark both arms on one CPU-bound workload (e.g. x's splitter over every client cell) against the sequential map.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_2 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:30 main: design read (code + probe). The Rust blocker is Send. Spiral's Rust output is Rc everywhere: strings are Rc<str>, closures are Rc<dyn Fn> built by a per-closure factory 'fn name(free vars) -> Rc<dyn Fn(..)>' (codegenRust closure, core ~164974). A closure with no free vars keeps one copy per thread in a thread_local. rayon needs Send + Sync, so today's eoie blobs convert to String/Vec inside host code. Probe (scratchpad par-probe/main.rs, rustc -O, std::thread::scope with 4 and 2 chunks): calling the FACTORY fn pointer inside each worker (fn() -> Rc<dyn Fn> is Send + Sync) type-checks and runs. Results come back in input order: i32 squares, Rc<str> strings deep-copied at the boundary as String, and empty input. Plan, decided by rule: (1) compiler, Rust only: a macro escape that renders a closure term as its factory call text (name plus captured args) when every free var is a Send type (primitive scalar or string-by-copy), and refuses at compile time otherwise. That puts soundness in the compiler instead of an unsafe Send wrapper, which would be UB once a capture is an Rc cloned on two threads. (2) lib par'.map/par'.each: Rust arm = rayon par_iter over chunks, each worker calls the factory once; element and result types limited to scalars and strings, copied at the boundary. Gleam arm = one process per chunk (chunks = schedulers_online), each sends back its index with list.map(chunk, f), collected in order under a monitor, so a crash surfaces as Error. Every other backend = the sequential map. (3) Test blocks rust + gleam: order kept, empty list, crash reported; then the benchmark from the first note. Step 1 is a pack item (codegenRust only, matrix review); the Gleam arm can draft now. Not started in lib/spiral: tibia's live block 09:40-12:30, and lib lands only in gaps.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_3 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:43Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:43 main: Gleam arm reworked onto the user's granular direction (LIB-OTP-ACTORS note 2): scratchpad par-lab/par'.spi is now pure Spiral over par-lab/erlang_process.spi primitives (new_subject, spawn_unlinked, monitor, select_map, select_specific_monitor, selector_receive_forever, exited_normally, schedulers_online). Typed union par_event = ChunkDone (index, values) | WorkerEnded exit_reason. One worker per chunk (chunk count = schedulers_online). It collects until every result and every Down has arrived, so no stray monitor message is left in the caller's mailbox, then concatenates in index order, and an abnormal Down fails with failwith. No global blob: the only globals are imports and one @external line per BIF. Tests: chunks_split_evenly, chunks_empty (all backends), map_keeps_order (gleam/rust/fsharp), map_empty, map_strings. Not compiled yet; it needs GLEAM-ERLANG-1X (the 1.x API names) and a compile gap. Arrays live in the process dictionary on Gleam, so items and captures must not hold arrays; this mirrors the Rust arm's Send rule.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_4 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 4u32, "2026-10-23T11:48Z", "tibia-lane", "Use case from tibia-lane (07:5x, TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP note 5: tibia's largest R1 win). (1) Trainer inner loop: network.evolve (src/network.spi 162-181) scores 64 offspring per generation, each fitness candidate independent; fitness = worth o camp (network_tactic w) first count (hunting_pack), and each call runs about 50 training clears. Shape: par'.map over offspring indices 0..63 giving i32 scores, then the existing sequential max (keeps the first best on ties, so the result stays bit-identical to the sequential evolve). The closure captures camp (a record of unions, i32 and nested arrays such as kinds and layout), o (a union) and the parent weights (array i32); it returns i32. (2) Held-out scoring: clear_ledger_of over 100 seeds is a fold of per-seed ledgers, so par'.map per seed plus a sequential sum. Today: single-threaded C, about 43-67 min per M1 arm on this 8-thread laptop. Constraints tibia needs: results in input order; deterministic (no shared RNG; tibia keys every roll by seed, tick, slot and purpose); captured values that are arrays of i32 and records of unions crossing the thread boundary; the C oracle and the Rust arm agreeing in Cells. A first consumer test: evolve on a 4-gene toy fitness, par versus sequential, bit-identical best genes.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_5 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 5u32, "2026-10-23T12:06Z", "main", "2026-10-23 08:06 main: Rust half drafted. (1) Compiler: %TEMP%\\pack-next6\\rust-closure-factory.ps1. Two anchors, each matching once on the next5e core, which parses under both defines. A macro term preceded by the text '__spiral_closure_factory ' renders as its closure's factory fn name (closure_names via closure_of_var, the same map the Export op uses for 'a closure without captures'). The marker text is stripped, and a captured or unknown closure is a codegen error naming the reason. (2) lib arm, par-lab/par'.spi map_on_threads: one expression on std::thread::scope with chunks = len.div_ceil(available_parallelism). Each worker calls (factory())(x.clone()) and the results are flattened in chunk order. It's std-only because Cells builds Rust cells with bare rustc (cells.ex rustc_args, no crates), so rayon can't link in test blocks; a rayon arm waits for a cargo-built consumer. rustc enforces Sync on items and Send on results, so Rc<str> items are refused at compile time (sound). Strings therefore stay gleam-only in the tests until a String boundary copy exists. The pack carrying (1) goes into the next codegen review (matrix: no residual changes, since no sample uses the marker), and lib par'.spi lands with GLEAM-ERLANG-1X in a gap.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_6 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 6u32, "2026-10-23T14:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:06 x: (1) CAVEAT for the Rust arm in note 5, from today's measurement (LIB-DICTM note 5): Spiral compiles a closure's record, tuple and unit parameters FLATTENED into separate Rust arguments, while map_on_threads calls (factory())(x.clone()) with one value. So par'.map over items that are tuples or records (tibia's use case passes offspring indices, fine; a (seed, schedule) pair would not be) fails with E0057 'this function takes N arguments but 1 was supplied'. Either restrict the item type to single-value types at the type level, or have the arm call through a Spiral-side adapter closure that takes the whole item as one i32 index and looks it up in an array captured by the factory. The index form also suits the 'no captures' factory rule, because the array becomes the map's own argument. UNVERIFIED: whether a closure RETURNING a record or tuple also changes shape on Rust. Today's evidence covers parameters only (dictm's folds returned lists), so probe a record-returning closure in the arm's first test. (2) x's first consumer, for the measured switch in X-BACKEND-MAP note 2: replaying N schedules of one corpus is N independent scheduler.sync_run calls. Item = schedule seed (i32), result = a scheduler record (or its XSIM summary line, a string), order kept, 2 to ~20 items of seconds each. The commute check over consecutive results stays sequential after the map.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_7 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 7u32, "2026-10-23T18:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:30 main: on rayon (BACKEND-BALANCE R1 names it): par'.map's Rust arm uses std::thread::scope with chunking, not rayon, because Cells compiles Rust cells with bare rustc and no crates, so a rayon arm could not be gated by the lib's own test blocks. A rayon variant fits cargo consumers only: a separate par'.map_rayon whose arm is '(*!native).par_iter().map(|x| (__spiral_closure_factory !fn())(x.clone())).collect()' (the closure-factory marker gives each worker its own closure, since Spiral closures are Rc and not Send), proven through a consumer's cargo build (x is the playground per BACKEND-BALANCE). Not drafted today; the std arm lands first with today's lib landing.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_8 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 8u32, "2026-10-23T20:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:37 main: par'.spi NOT landed: its Rust arm needs the __spiral_closure_factory marker that pack-next7's codegen implements (the installed compiler emits it verbatim: E0425 + a parse error), and F# also fails map_keeps_order; Gleam passes now. Kept in %TEMP%\\lib-land-1023\\lib\\spiral\\par'.spi (out of the land copy's package.spiproj). Next: land with next7's install, after an F# look.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_9 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 9u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:17 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 50. par' is drafted (lib-land-1023), and its Rust arm waits for pack-next7's closure factory (rust-closure-factory in pack-next6/7). It lands after that install.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_10 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 10u32, "2026-10-24T04:04Z", "x", "10-24 00:04 x: for main's 'F# look' (n 16:37): queued a Cells run of the draft par'.spi on a live-lib copy (%TEMP%\\x-par-fsharp, par' listed last) as job-1792814654580-x-par-fsharp-probe. Its F# arm is the plain listm.map fallback, so the failing map_keeps_order output should show whether F# trips on the module (Gleam-only union par_event over erlang_process types) or on the arm. The Rust failure there is the known missing closure-factory marker and isn't news. The result goes into this note's follow-up.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_11 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 11u32, "2026-10-24T05:36Z", "x", "10-24 01:36 x: F# look ANSWERED (n10's probe, rerun as job-1792817012060, 01:19). The draft par'.spi on a live-lib copy (par' listed last) ran 5 tests and failed 2: map_empty and map_keeps_order, both only on rust (the known missing __spiral_closure_factory marker). F# map_keeps_order PASSES on today's lib and compiler (single-flight B553EF6B78E6EE29), and gleam passes everything. So 16:37's F# failure doesn't reproduce; the only blocker left is pack-next7's closure factory for the Rust arm.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_12 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 12u32, "2026-10-24T05:40Z", "main", "2026-10-24 01:40 main: thanks to x's n11 (F# passes; only the Rust closure-factory marker is missing). pack-next6's rust-closure-factory.ps1 was in no composed stack (next4 took other next6 slices, not this one); it composes cleanly on 5g and is now in pack 5g's compose (core AEC3FF009A141AB8). It rides review-4 (job-1792813775220), and after the install par'.spi lands through the gate kit with its rust tests.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_13 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 13u32, "2026-10-26T09:44Z", "tibia-lane", "09:44Z 09:5xZ State check (tibia; GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT note 3, part 4, the Rust+rayon predict sweep, waits on this item). par'.spi never landed: lib/spiral has no par' and package.spiproj doesn't list it. The 10-24 05:40 plan was to land it through the gate kit after pack 5g's install. 5g and later installed, and the blocker is gone: the installed compiler has the closure-factory marker (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs:163852, rustClosureFactoryMarker = \"__spiral_closure_factory\"). Nobody picked the draft up afterwards.\nThe newest draft is %TEMP%\\lib-land-1023\\lib\\spiral\\par'.spi (4,682 bytes, 10-23 10:50; x-par-fsharp holds the same copy). It can't land as written:\n(1) Edition: inl, let rec, match, if/then and `open` throughout. Lib is edition 3 now, so it needs conversion to := / :~ ^ / ?= / ? ?> |: / :<.\n(2) Granular: the Gleam arm is already Spiral over erlang_process primitives. The Rust arm is not. map_on_threads is one host program: thread::scope, then chunks(...).map(|chunk| scope.spawn(move || chunk.iter().map(|x| (__spiral_closure_factory !fn())(x.clone())).collect())), then join and flat_map, with `as usize`/`as i32` casts. available_parallelism also casts in host text.\nPre-written rule for the Rust redesign: spawned closures borrow a scope lifetime Spiral can't name, so the scope-plus-spawn-plus-join triple counts as one host primitive taking a Spiral chunk list and the closure-factory fn. Chunking, chunk_size arithmetic and casts (through convert.unativeint and conv) move to Spiral, reusing par's own chunks. Before slicing, check with main's GRANULAR-LINT 'block' class that a scope primitive counts as one host expression; this is the same question as the trace and crypto cfg cases (GRANULAR-HEAL note 27).\nProof shape: map_keeps_order (gleam, rust, fsharp), map_empty (gleam, rust) and map_strings (gleam), plus a Rust consumer that sums squares over 100,000 items with both par'.map and listm.map: equal results and fewer wall-clock seconds on this box's cores.\nLib order: par' after erlang_process (lib module 111).\nThe tibia sweep measurement (GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT note 3, rule: byte-identical rows on 100 held-out seeds and at most 1/3 of C's wall time) starts once par' lands.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_14 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 14u32, "2026-10-26T09:53Z", "tibia-lane", "09:53Z 10:0xZ First par' land drafted: LIB-PAR-GLEAM, the Gleam arm in edition 3, and sequential listm.map on every other backend. x's 10-23 draft is converted to edition 3 (:| par_event, := and :~ ^ join points, ?= and ? ?> |:, list sugar, typed literals). The Gleam arm was already granular Spiral over erlang_process primitives, and all of them exist in today's lib: new_subject, spawn_unlinked, monitor, select_specific_monitor, down_reason, new_selector, select_map, selector_receive_forever, exited_normally, schedulers_online, send. One fix: listm.collect became listm'.collect. The Rust arm stays out until its rule (note 13) is decided, so Rust takes the sequential fallback. Tests: chunks_split_evenly, chunks_empty, map_keeps_order (gleam, fsharp), map_empty and map_strings (gleam). Placement: package.spiproj gains `par'` right after erlang_process. Formatter clean, 0 lines over 120.\nProof on the consumer path, against a lib copy with the slice applied: a probe program sums par'.map (\\ x => x * x) over 1..100.\n- Compiled to Gleam and run with `gleam run` inside a copy of Cells' warm Gleam package (gleam_erlang and gleam_otp 1.3), it prints 338350 through the process arm. The first run printed a stale cached module ('__assert_eq { expected = 3 }') because the copied source was older than the warm build; touching the source fixed it.\n- Compiled to F# and run with dotnet fsi, it prints 338350 (the sequential arm).\nGate on the remote kit worker node: job-1793008453212-tibia-par-gleam-gate (scratch lib-par-gleam/lib-par-gleam.ps1 plus par'.spi; spiproj base D6395F2028FC6A5A, identical on both boxes). The land is packed into tibia-serial-gaps. Undo: the spiproj undo.do, plus removing par'.spi by literal path.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_15 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 15u32, "2026-10-26T12:35Z", "tibia-lane", "12:35Z 12:4xZ LIB-PAR-RUST drafted under GRANULAR-LINT rule (8) as accepted (A1 scope class, A2 spiral_scope_ prefix, A3 tests on its backend). It stacks on LIB-PAR-GLEAM. One shim in global, spiral_scope_map<T: Sync + Clone, U: Send>(items: &[T], chunk_size: usize, make: fn() -> Rc<dyn Fn(T) -> U>) -> Vec<U>, whose body is one thread::scope, one spawning iterator map over items.chunks(chunk_size), where each worker calls make() once and maps f, and one joining flat_map. No arithmetic, casts or branches. In Spiral: rust_available_parallelism (one call, as usize), map_on_threads (workers via convert.convert, the length <= workers ? 1 : ceil-division chunk size, usize via convert.convert) and rust_scoped_map, whose single host expression passes __spiral_closure_factory !fn. The compiler emits the closure's capture-free factory function name there (spiral_compiler.fs: a capturing closure is a codegen error, 'A closure that crosses threads must capture nothing'). map gains | Rust => map_on_threads; map_keeps_order and map_empty gain ///> rust. Proof (scratch lib-par-rust/probe-run.ps1, a copy with par-gleam and par-rust applied): par'.map over 1..200000 squares (i64) sums to 2666686666700000, equal to listm.map's sum, on rustc -O; an empty list gives 0; the output holds exactly one spiral_scope_map and one thread::scope. Formatter clean, 0 lines over 120. Land: tibia-serial-gaps-lib, gate-then-land right after par-gleam lands (local gate, since par'.spi must exist). GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT part 4 (the predict sweep on Rust + par'.map against C, byte-identical rows at most 1/3 of the time) can start once it lands.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_16 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 16u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "main", "10-29 22:00 main, groom (WIP rule, 3 days with no note): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 50%. Last note 2026-10-26, before the 10-28 profile wipe interrupted every lane, and no live job. Resume when picked; it is not abandoned, and it keeps its priority P1.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_17 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 17u32, "2026-10-30T02:04Z", "main", "10-29 22:04 main, groom: RESUMABLE. Every dep is Done (GLEAM-ERLANG-1X), so nothing blocks it; it's Paused only for want of a picker (P1, 50%). Pick-up: the team that needs it (lane-roles), after the pack-7 install.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_18 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 18u32, "2026-10-30T02:05Z", "main", "10-29 22:05 main, groom follow-up: paused tonight by the WIP rule, but an ACTIVE item depends on it: BACKEND-BALANCE (Active). Resume it FIRST among the paused ones, since it gates live work.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_19 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 19u32, "2026-10-30T14:13Z", "main", "10-30 10:12 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R2/R4: deps Done, owner team main ('agile next --team main' hands it out), assignee agent (nothing in it is the user's). Resume when that team picks it up; the pack-7 install it waited on is done.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_20 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 20u32, "2026-10-30T14:24Z", "main", "10-30 10:24 main: DONE, verified on the live tree, not from notes. tibia's two drafts (the Gleam arm, then the Rust arm under GRANULAR-LINT rule 8) LANDED on 10-26 22:11 but were never recorded here: lib/spiral/par'.spi (5,554 B, sha256 5453657581BF, committed in spiral 3f1b7588) is listed in package.spiproj. Rust uses std thread::scope through the one spiral_scope_map shim and __spiral_closure_factory, not rayon (the accepted granular rule). Gleam uses erlang_process primitives; other backends fall back to sequential listm.map. Proof, through the consumer path: apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 -File par'.spi on the box at 10:2x, 'tests: passed 6 failed 0 of 6', type errors 0, discovered 6 = executed 6, failed_targets {}, slowest 51 s (the Rust cell). The tests are chunks_split_evenly, chunks_empty, map_workers_have_room_for_deep_recursion [rust], map_keeps_order [gleam fsharp rust], map_empty [gleam rust] and map_strings [gleam]. This unblocks Active BACKEND-BALANCE's LIB-PAR-MAP dep. Left elsewhere: GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT part 4 (the Rust predict sweep over par'.map) is that item's work, now unblocked.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:01Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:01 x (filed at the user's request, 2026-10-23: 'tibia and eoie we haven't thought about what could be gained there'; the tibia team decides and measures). Survey: 28 src modules plus 17 app files; targets today c 167, wgsl 28, lua 9, rust 2, zig 1 (C is the oracle, WGSL the GPU path, Lua the live OTClient entry). First classification by the rule. KEEP: (1) the rules and simulator modules on C, as the oracle and for speed, and gpu_sim on WGSL; (2) the Lua probes inside OTClient (the client's own language). RUST + LIB-PAR-MAP candidates: (3) batch work over independent seeds, policies or spots: hunt_sim / pack_sim sweeps, policy search, model-vs-live prediction tables (R1). A rayon fan-out over seeds on the Rust arm, measured against today's single-threaded C runs, could cut experiment wall time by about the core count. GLEAM candidates: (4) arena orchestration: start and supervise the game server, N OTClient clients with probes, and the competitor bots (vBot, NexBot), time out and restart them, and collect observer and scoreboard events as messages (G1, G2, G3, G4). A crash or hang of one client becomes a supervised restart instead of a lost block. (5) The OT Agent Arena as a public benchmark service that schedules runs across machines (G5, distribution). Measure first: how much live-block time goes to client hangs, restarts and manual cleanup today (the arena logs and PR items have the history), and the seeds/s of a batch sweep on C vs Rust + rayon.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:03Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:03 x: first measurement, from tibia (night of 10-22/23): 30 live runs with 0 crashes and 0 restarts. The only process losses were two low-memory reaps of waiter shells, and job-host jobs were unaffected. That weakens candidate (4) for now: supervision of clients (G3) pays only if hangs or restarts cost live-block time, and tonight they cost none. By the rule, (4) moves to Gleam only if a longer sample shows that cost, or if the benchmark-service need (5, G5 distribution) becomes real. (3), Rust + rayon for batch sweeps, is unaffected and is now the stronger first experiment.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:05Z", "tibia-lane", "tibia-lane 07:1x: the arena orchestration is in the retirement scope (TIBIA note 41), judged by dogfood value rather than by a measured hang rate. Inventory to map: live/*.ps1 (about 530 lines in 10 scripts; run-policy-scenario.ps1 188, run-arena.ps1 87, run-offscreen.ps1 67), the night-block chaining logic (scratch night-block.ps1), the crash-rerun rule from the pre-registrations, the server-probe lifecycle (-ServerProbe), and eoie window requests. The baseline measurement x suggested (hang and restart time per live block) still runs first, as the before number.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 4u32, "2026-10-23T11:11Z", "tibia-lane", "Retirement grooming 07:1x: in scope as filed, about 4 h: the hang and restart baseline over the next live blocks plus the classification. Implementing what the map chooses (rayon sweeps, an OTP arena) is sized by that classification and is not in this estimate.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 5u32, "2026-10-23T11:21Z", "tibia-lane", "Classification by BACKEND-BALANCE's rule (note 1), with before-numbers. tibia-lane 07:3x.\n(1) src rules and simulators (geometry, combat, hunting_pack, pack_sim, and the rest): KEEP. They are the C oracle, the WGSL GPU twins and the Lua live target, which the rule keeps (\"GPU work stays WGSL and C stays where the matrix needs it\").\n(2) apps/arena probes: KEEP Lua (they run inside OTClient's Lua host).\n(3) apps/map: KEEP Rust (R1 and R3: a one-shot parse of canary.otbm and appearances.dat).\n(4) apps/predict sweeps and the AGENT trainers (network.evolve, evolve_cem over population x training seeds; held-out scoring over 100 seeds): RUST plus rayon by R1, CPU-bound over independent seeds, through LIB-PAR-MAP. Before-number: single-threaded C; the M1 network.evolve arm took about 43 min (job tibia-agent-m1-trace-evolve, 05:11-05:53, beside main's heavy jobs) and about 67 min under heavier load (run 4); held-out scoring of one tactic takes about 50 s (job tibia-agent-clock: 4 tactics in 206 s with compile). The C build stays as the oracle for the same code (Cells agreement c = rust).\n(5) live/ orchestration (about 530 lines of PowerShell: run-policy-scenario, run-arena, run-offscreen, block, world): GLEAM by G1 (a block lives 1-3 h and holds state: window, server, DB, probe, results), G3 (it starts, times out and restarts OS children: canary.exe, mariadbd, OTClient on an off-screen desktop) and G2 (the duo runs two clients beside the server). Per x's note 2 (BEAM cold start 1-2.6 s), it would be a long-lived arena host that the CLI (block.ps1 or eoie) talks to, not a per-command tool. Before-numbers from the night block (results/*-leadtrace.tsv and *-pr23b.tsv, row-to-row minus time in game): orchestration overhead per run about 43 s on the fixed 300 s hunting-ground hunts (12 percent), about 61 s on cyclops arena runs, rough on hunter runs. 0 crashes, 0 hangs and 0 restarts in 30 runs; 2 waiter shells were reaped under low memory, outside the arena. The block's largest cost was about 4 h queued for a live-gui window behind heavy builds, which is eoie admission, not the arena. So the gain to measure after a switch is per-run overhead (a warm host instead of a cold restart per run) and the crash-rerun rule built in, not hang time.\n(6) Analysis readers (scratch read-trace.ps1, p5-eval.ps1): RUST via eoie verbs (R2; TOOLS-TO-EOIE, EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES).\nNext, as children of this item: (4) waits on LIB-PAR-MAP and is the bigger win for laptop time; (5) waits on LIB-OTP-ACTORS. Neither switch lands without its after-measurement.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 6u32, "2026-10-23T11:50Z", "tibia-lane", "Arena host design (07:5x, for (5) of note 5, on LIB-OTP-ACTORS primitives as main read them: gleam_erlang 1.3.0 and gleam_otp 1.3.0). Each responsibility of live/*.ps1, mapped:\n(1) arena_host, a static_supervisor (RestForOne): world first, then block. A world crash restarts the block; a block crash leaves the world up.\n(2) world, an actor that owns mariadbd and canary.exe as OS ports (today world.ps1 and run-policy-scenario.ps1 start them and stop canary by process name; here the port's own pid is the handle, which ends kill-by-name). Its messages are a typed union: Start, ResetCharacter (the DB reset SQL of run-policy-scenario), InstallProbe path, RemoveProbe, Restart (canary restarts per run today; whether a warm canary survives a DB reset is the after-measurement), Health.\n(3) offscreen_launch stays a Rust CLI by R3 (Win32 CreateDesktop and CreateProcess, today in run-offscreen.ps1), started by the run actor as a port and exited by it. It is the native boundary the rule names.\n(4) run, an actor started per run with restart Transient and restart_tolerance intensity 1. That is the pre-registered crash rule (\"a crash is rerun once, then counted missing\") as supervisor policy instead of a manual checklist step. It holds the timeout as a send_after timer value (today -TimeoutSeconds plus wall-clock caps), reads the client log, writes the results row, and keeps the probe trace (today the -ServerProbe finally block).\n(5) block, an actor that asks eoie for the live-gui window (eoie stays the Rust CLI by R2, called as a port) and sequences runs and stages (today block.ps1 and the hand-written night-block chaining), releasing the window on its own termination via a monitor, the guarantee PowerShell's finally gave only within one script.\n(6) The probe Lua (apps/arena) and the server probes stay as they are; the host only installs and collects them.\nTyped protocols as GADTs of request to reply per actor, once LIB-OTP-ACTORS lands its reply_to handle. The deterministic arm (actors as a step loop) gives Cells tests without BEAM: a run that times out is retried once and then counted missing; a world restart re-resets the character; a block that ends releases its window.\nBefore-numbers to beat (note 5): about 43 s of overhead per hunting-ground run, 0 hangs in 30 runs. After-numbers to measure: overhead per run with a warm world, and crash-rerun latency.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 7u32, "2026-10-23T12:17Z", "tibia-lane", "First host code LANDED 08:2x: apps/host (new package; packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib with |core- and spiral-, no sibling entry, so it mounts in Cells without step 11). block_plan.spi is the deterministic core of the run and block actors of note 6. It types run_outcome (Completed or Crashed, read from the experience field as run-arena writes it), run_fate (Counted, Rerun or Missing; fate_of is the pre-registered crash rule), progress over stages, and stage_step (InstallProbe, Runs, RemoveProbe, KeepTrace) with probed_stage, plain_stage, runs_of and probe_left_installed. Gate green from the tree (job tibia-host-gate2): scripts/build.ps1 -Files block_plan, 4 tests, 12 of 12 cells on c, lua and gleam; standards 0 findings. Tibia's first Spiral code tested on the Gleam backend. scripts/build.ps1 now gates apps/host (formatter check and Cells source list). Next in scratch: character.spi (level-derived stats, the reset statement and the canonical kit, each byte-identical to run-policy-scenario.ps1 lines 50-56, plus the duo config edit as a typed result), then the monster mix and summon orders (lines 30-34).")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 8u32, "2026-10-23T12:28Z", "tibia-lane", "08:3x. (1) apps/host/character.spi LANDED, gate green (job tibia-host-gate3: 4 tests, 11 of 11 cells). It holds the level-derived stats; the reset statement and the canonical kit, both byte-identical to run-policy-scenario.ps1 lines 55-56 (the kit's 609 characters compared to the script programmatically); and the duo config edit as a typed result (Raised text or LimitsMissing) instead of a throw. The duo test targets lua and gleam because sm'.replace has no C arm (known, CONVO-IN-SPIRAL note 29). (2) Row extraction stays in eoie: eoie table logs with Write-ArenaRow's patterns (--last experience, cleared_ms, lowest_health, actions_allowed, actions_dropped; --count potions and deaths; --sum gold) reproduces all 30 rows of last night's three result tables, 0 of 240 fields different. So the run actor writes rows by calling eoie (R2, the existing generic verb) instead of a Spiral regex port; Get-ForeignActivity's two columns are the only part not yet covered. (3) The comparison caught a false record of mine: PC-8's RESULT row said every run cleared, but the hunter runs cleared 7 of 10 (three hit the 200 s cap). Corrected in docs/BENCHMARK.md. (4) scenario.spi (monster mix, summon orders, BadOrder) is in Cells on lua and gleam. sm'.contains has no C arm, filed as LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 9u32, "2026-10-23T12:40Z", "tibia-lane", "08:4x. apps/host now has 4 modules, all derived from live/ PowerShell and checked against it. (1) scenario.spi LANDED, gate green (job tibia-host-gate4: 2 tests, 4 of 4 cells on lua and gleam): the monster mix (one kind with a count, or 'troll:2, cyclops:1'), summon orders byte-identical to run-policy-scenario.ps1 line 34, total count, and BadOrder naming the part when a count is not a number (the script throws mid-run). (2) Rows: eoie table logs plus arithmetic now reproduces every Write-ArenaRow column, the two Get-ForeignActivity columns included (0 of 30 rows different; three --count and four --last patterns). The arithmetic is apps/host/activity.spi (foreign_potions, own_actions, foreign_actions as an option, since PowerShell's null meant no allowed/dropped line); 3 of 3 cells on c, lua and gleam, LANDED, gate green (job tibia-host-gate5: 1 test, 3 of 3 cells). (3) So the run actor's logic exists as typed, tested Spiral for the crash rule, the stage plan, the character reset, the kit, the duo config, the mix, the orders and the row arithmetic; only process control (canary, mariadb, OTClient off-screen, eoie window calls) waits for LIB-OTP-ACTORS and the Rust offscreen_launch helper.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 10u32, "2026-10-23T13:47Z", "tibia-lane", "09:4x. Two more pieces of the arena host LANDED, gate green (job tibia-host-gate6: formatter clean, arena_host 4 of 4 tests, offscreen 3 of 3; eoie standards 0 findings). (1) apps/host/arena_host.spi: the host deterministic step loop of note 6, step : host -> host_event -> host * list host_command over block_plan crash rule. Four event-list traces as tests on lua and gleam: a clean block resets the character before every run and releases its window; a timed-out run is stopped, rerun once, then counted missing; a world crash restarts the world and resets the character before the rerun; a lost window ends the block and later events change nothing. (2) apps/offscreen: note 6 item 3, the off-screen launcher as a Spiral-to-Rust CLI (std only, plain rustc like apps/map; build.ps1 -Check builds target/offscreen/offscreen.exe and checks it). It replaces run-offscreen.ps1 inline C#: flags as a typed parse result (MissingExe, MissingValue, BadTimeout, UnknownFlag), run-offscreen.ps1 report lines byte for byte, and each Win32 call (CreateDesktopW, CreateProcessW, WaitForSingleObject, TerminateProcess, GetExitCodeProcess, CloseHandle) as its own small primitive. Native check: cmd.exe exit 7 reported, ping killed at 2 s with exit 124 and both lines, missing --exe exits 2. Not wired into live/ yet: run-offscreen.ps1 moves onto it after the block, with one probe run as the check (draft in scratch). Tibia requirements for the executor are posted on LIB-OTP-ACTORS (note 10). Left on this item: the Gleam executor (waits on LIB-OTP-ACTORS), the predict/trainer move to Rust (waits on LIB-PAR-MAP), and the after-measurements of note 5.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 11u32, "2026-10-23T15:28Z", "tibia-lane", "11:3x. live/run-offscreen.ps1 now runs on the Rust launcher (target/offscreen/offscreen.exe, built by apps/offscreen/build.ps1 if missing): same parameters and output lines, and the inline C# is gone. Checked after the block, without a live window: cmd.exe exit 5 reported; ping killed at 2 s with exit 124 and both lines; default desktop tibia-probe; -WorkingDirectory passed through; and the duo ally argument shape --user-dir=\"<path with a space>\" reaches the child intact, so the command line matches the old string-built one. The first real OTClient run on it is PC-10's first run (next live window); a failure there is a crash for the block's crash rule, and I restore the old script (scratch copy offscreen/run-offscreen.before.ps1) before rerunning.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 12u32, "2026-10-23T15:34Z", "tibia-lane", "11:4x. apps/host/arena_host.spi gained the deterministic executor of note 6, LANDED, gate green (job tibia-host-gate7: 6 of 6 tests on lua and gleam, formatter clean). A scripted world (Finishes outcome, TimesOut, CrashesWorld) answers each host command (StartWorld and RestartWorld give WorldReady, StartRun takes the next scripted reaction, and an empty script gives WindowLost), and simulated runs the host loop until no event is left, so a whole block runs closed-loop with no BEAM. Tests: a 3-run block with a timeout and a world crash gives the exact command trace expected by hand, and a world that stops answering ends the block. This is the deterministic arm LIB-OTP-ACTORS asks for (its note 10): the same step function, with the executor as the only per-backend part.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 13u32, "2026-10-23T20:38Z", "tibia-lane", "Blocker cleared 16:3x: main landed LIB-OTP-ACTORS' first modules in spiral lib (erlang_process, otp_actor, otp_supervisor, step_runtime, with process.spi and platform.spi on gleam_erlang 1.x; Cells pins gleam_erlang 1.3, gleam_otp 1.3, stdlib 1.0.5). The arena host's Gleam executor (note 6, requirements on LIB-OTP-ACTORS note 10) can now be built on them in the next session: the step function of apps/host/arena_host.spi as the actor's handle, its scripted executor as the deterministic arm (step_runtime), and an otp_actor executor that owns canary/mariadbd ports and target/offscreen/offscreen.exe runs. par' (LIB-PAR-MAP) is not landed yet: it needs a compiler pack. Also landed: sm'.spi C arms (LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM), so apps/host/scenario.spi's tests can add the c target.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 14u32, "2026-10-23T21:15Z", "tibia-lane", "17:1x: apps/host/arena_host.spi gains host_actor and host_trace: the arena host's step function as a step_runtime.actor (state: the run plan as a list, the host built inside the actor on its first message, and the trace; messages Event or Trace r, read back with call), the first consumer of main's LIB-OTP-ACTORS. One test runs the same event list through a real gleam_otp actor (step_runtime.otp_start, otp_actor.send and call) on gleam and through spawn, send and call on lua, and both give the identical command trace; Cells 7 of 7 on lua and gleam (job tibia-host-actor2), landing gate job tibia-host-gate8. The list plan works around Gleam arrays dying at the process boundary (LIB-OTP-ACTORS note 12); main landed otp_actor.new_with_init at 17:1x, so next session the plan can stay an array with the thunk form. Next for the executor: the OS-port side (canary and mariadbd ports, offscreen.exe runs) on erlang_process. Gate: tibia-host-gate8 failed only the actor test on gleam (callee did not send reply before timeout, the 1000 ms call, under load after main's 17:14 otp_actor landing); the rerun tibia-host-gate9 is 7 of 7 green with no memo. So the 1000 ms call can time out on a loaded laptop: next session either raises it or moves to new_with_init, so a CI run under load does not flake.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 15u32, "2026-10-23T22:03Z", "tibia-lane", "Dependency 18:1x: the executor's OS-port side (offscreen.exe per run, canary and mariadbd per block) needs a lib runtime-process Gleam arm (spawn_executable port with exit status), which KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR already plans as a lib gap (its 10-16 spike used a 30-line Erlang FFI); tibia's needs are recorded there as a second consumer. The pure host loop, its deterministic executor and the actor wrapper are landed; only the port arm waits. CORRECTED 2026-10-23 23:4x: the gap is smaller than written. lib/spiral/process.spi (main, landed 10-22) already has capture_status_within program args ms on rust and gleam: a spawn_executable port with exit_status, a deadline, and a tree kill (taskkill /T) past it, returning the exit code or -1. Run in a worker process (erlang_process.spawn) that sends the result to the host actor, it covers the per-run offscreen.exe call (its paths have no spaces, and offscreen.exe takes --directory, so the space-split args and the missing cwd option do not matter). What lib lacks is only a port handle for the per-run canary.exe and the block's mariadbd: open, await exit, and kill on demand from another process (the prelude has spiral_process_open_with and spiral_process_kill_tree, but no public start/stop pair).")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 16u32, "2026-10-24T03:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-23 23:30 Night item 4 of the 10-23 report: host_trace's gleam arm now calls otp_actor.call with 10000 ms instead of 1000 (apps/host/arena_host.spi 255), so a loaded CI laptop does not flake the actor test (gate8's one timeout); job tibia-night-full-build has arena_host 7 of 7 green on lua and gleam with it, on main's 22:27 compiler. The move to otp_actor.new_with_init is not made: the host is already built inside the actor on its first message (runs_of_plan), so the array never crosses the boundary, and new_with_init would only help if step_runtime.actor carried an init thunk (init : () -> s), a lib change to step_runtime and otp_start that every step_runtime user shares. Passed to main as information (step_runtime.otp_start evaluates spec.init in the caller, so any array state dies at the boundary for every user). BACKEND-MAP's in-scope part is the classification plus the landed host core, executor, actor wrapper and launcher; only the OS-port arm remains, waiting on KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR's runtime-process arm (note 15). UPDATE 23:4x: main took the finding as its own gap (otp_start never switched to new_with_init): its lib-step-init change gives step_runtime.actor an init thunk (init : () -> s), otp_start on otp_actor.new_with_init 10000, and every lib call timeout 10000 ms; gate job-1792812964392-main-lib-step-init-gate, land job-1792812965354-main-lib-step-init-land, which also flips arena_host.spi's init line to the thunk in the same land so the tree stays green. After it lands, tibia drops the workaround with scratch host-init-patch.ps1 (dry-run clean against main's flipped line): state host * string, the host opened by the init thunk inside the actor, then the arena_host gate.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 17u32, "2026-10-24T05:35Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 01:35 tibia: the dependency moved from the KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR epic to LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE, the one primitive note 15 names (start with a log, OS pid, await exit within a deadline, tree kill by pid). Its lab is green on rust and gleam, and its lib gate is queued after PR-27's live block (LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE note 2). Once it lands, the remaining OS-port arm is the block's world owner on BEAM: start mariadbd and canary with logs, await the world line, and kill both trees at block end or on a crash.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_18 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 18u32, "2026-10-24T08:59Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:59 tibia: the consumer is proven. world_port's 2 gleam tests pass against the healed process module (job tibia-world-port-cells, on a %TEMP% copy of apps/host with a lib copy holding the healed process.spi after file_system): a fake python server reports WorldReady, then WorldCrashed after world_stopped; and the owner restarts and releases its server. A plain compile of world_port as the entry exits 0 but checks nothing, because a module without main compiles the package's main (memory gotcha updated), so the Cells run is the real check. The land plan is one replace, not a delete then re-add: lib process.spi becomes the healed module, moved after file_system (scratch process-replace-apply.ps1, proven on a copy, kit gate tibia-lib-process-replace-gate on its 5 tests), and in the same session world_port.spi joins tibia apps/host (package.spiproj module list) as its first caller, so rule (4) holds for every new definition. The 6 uncalled definitions disappear in the replace.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_19 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 19u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 07:19 tibia: grooming re-size. The 240 min estimate covered the classification plus the host core, executor, actor wrapper and launcher, all done. The OS-port arm entered scope when LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's handle and the world_port owner were proven (note 18): joint land about 30 min, executor wiring (arena_host's StartWorld, RestartWorld and ReleaseWindow driving world_obeyed, events from world_event_within into the host actor) about 120 min. Estimate 390, progress 60 (from 90), still Blocked on LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's multi-file land.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_20 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 20u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 19:44 host-init done in the tree. apps/host/arena_host.spi (modified 09:43) has init = fun () => plan, None, '', matching lib step_runtime.spi line 11 'type actor s m = { init : () -> s; ... }' (lib modified 17:18). The note 16 pending patch is applied. Still unproven: no arena_host build since then, so the next host compile in a free window confirms it.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_21 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 21u32, "2026-10-25T14:50Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 10:50 host-init is proven, so note 20's open point is closed: target/ci-metrics/cells-arena_host/tests.tsv (written 2026-10-24 23:43, after arena_host.spi's last change at 23:31) has all 7 arena_host tests passed on lua and gleam, including the_host_step_runs_inside_an_actor_and_gives_the_same_trace_on_every_backend and a_scripted_world_drives_a_whole_block_through_the_executor. Still blocked only on LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE, whose anchored lib slice is in kit gate job-1792938470349; after it lands, the joint land wires the executor's world port to process.start_logging and await_exit_within.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_22 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 22u32, "2026-10-25T15:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:00 joint land queued behind the lib gate: job-1792940329591 (process-handle-serial.ps1) runs the kit land of LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE-SPLIT, then world-port-land.ps1, which adds apps/host/world_port.spi (the note 18 owner respelled to edition 3 with format --spelling symbols; database_answers dropped since nothing calls it) after arena_host in the host package and runs Cells world_port and arena_host with memo off, restoring both on red. Left after it: the executor wiring (StartWorld, RestartWorld and ReleaseWindow through world_obeyed, world_event_within feeding the host actor), about 120 min.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_23 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 23u32, "2026-10-25T15:02Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:02 Executor wiring design, written before drafting; it applies once world_port is in the tree (job-1792940329591). New apps/host/world_executor.spi after world_port, with the logic in Spiral over lib process and file_system. (1) executor record: world programs (world_port.world_programs), a database admin program plus args for running SQL, the run program (the offscreen client) plus its directory, run deadline, world-start deadline and block log path. (2) executed x current command : option world * list host_event. StartWorld and RestartWorld go through world_port.world_obeyed, then world_event_within is polled in 50 ms steps until WorldReady or WorldCrashed or the start deadline (deadline passed = WorldCrashed, which the host answers with RestartWorld). ReleaseWindow goes through world_obeyed with no event. ResetCharacter runs character.reset_sql through the database program with process.capture_status_within: exit 0 = no event, otherwise WorldCrashed. StartRun (stage, run) runs the client with capture_status_within under the run deadline: -1 = RunTimedOut, exit 0 = RunEnded (block_plan.outcome_of_experience of the experience line read from the run log), other codes = RunEnded Crashed. StopRun does nothing, because capture_status_within already killed the run tree at its deadline. Record fate appends fate_text to the block log. (3) driven h pending current: the simulated loop with executed in place of reacted, so the trace text and the host step stay the ones the 7 arena_host tests pin. (4) Acceptance: one gleam test over fake programs (python world printing the online line, a python run printing an experience line, a python database exiting 0) whose trace equals simulated's for the same reactions; a second where the fake run sleeps past its deadline and the trace shows stop, rerun. Lua is out: the executor runs on BEAM (BACKEND-BALANCE: supervision and ports).")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_24 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 24u32, "2026-10-25T15:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:07 Executor drafted to note 23's design: scratch world-port-draft/world_executor.spi (134 lines, edition 3): executed maps each host command to its program (world_obeyed plus a 50 ms start poll, reset_sql through the database program, the client run under capture_status_within, Record appending fate_text to the block log); driven mirrors simulated's FIFO queue; two gleam tests over python fakes (a 3-run block whose trace equals simulated's and whose block log reads counted x3; an always-sleeping run giving stop, rerun, stop, missing per stage). It lands as step 3 of job-1792940329591 (world-executor-land.ps1, Cells world_executor and world_port, restore on red).")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_25 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 25u32, "2026-10-26T12:19Z", "tibia-lane", "12:19Z 12:3xZ Unblocked, 90%. The joint land ran today in tibia-serial-lib (job-1792976722061): LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE-SPLIT in lib, then apps/host/world_port.spi on lib process handles (arena_host 7 of 7, world_port 2 of 2, memo off, formatter clean), then world_executor.spi from note 23's design (world_executor 2 of 2, world_port 2 of 2): host commands map to real programs through world_obeyed, the 50 ms start poll, reset_sql, the client run under capture_status_within, and the block log append. Left: (1) LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's process heal, re-anchored on the post-split base, packed as the first retry of tibia-serial-gaps; (2) the first live use of world_executor in the next live-gui window, which closes this item; that's the arena host driven by the executor instead of the scenario script. The 'Rust + rayon for batch sweeps' clause is measured on GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT part 4, waiting on LIB-PAR-MAP's Rust arm, not here.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_26 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 26u32, "2026-10-27T10:29Z", "tibia-lane", "10:29Z STATE 10-27 06:2x. Left (1) is DONE: LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's process heal landed tonight (attempt 3, after aligning the reader guard with main's filter: CompilerDaemon.serve_app and --path outside C:\\home\\git ignored, 600 min deadline), and the item is Done. The 'Rust + rayon for batch sweeps' clause is decided by measurement (GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT and BACKEND-BALANCE notes from 10-27): C seq 0.76-1.05 s, Rust par 1.17 s (ratio 1.554 against C, not adopted), Rust seq 6.09 s (ratio 5.8, codegen dominates). Sweeps stay on C, and the simulator stays native C/WGSL. Only (2) remains: the first live use of world_executor in the next live-gui window (arena host driven by the executor instead of the scenario script). It's a live block, run alone with no compiles. That closes the item.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_27 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 27u32, "2026-10-28T01:35Z", "tibia-lane", "01:3xZ Window requested from main for the last step, the first live world_executor run: 10-28 08:30-09:30 local, 60 min. That's after TRAIN2 (about 22:40 to 03:00-05:00) and kit's lib window, and before kit's next release, with nothing else compiling on the box. Plan: the executor starts the world, then 1 smoke hunt and 3 counted hunts at PR-28's pace (about 5.5 min per hunt), run alone. Main books it with kit and compiler.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_28 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 28u32, "2026-10-28T09:39Z", "tibia-lane", "09:39Z PRE-REGISTRATION P-EXEC1, the first live world_executor block (written 10-28 05:4x, before any live run; window booked for 08:30-09:30).\nQuestion: does the arena host, driven by world_executor through real programs, run a live block end to end?\nSetup:\n- Driver: scratch live-exec/world_live.spi, staged as a module beside a copy of apps/host. It's not in the tibia tree, so no gate can launch the world.\n- Programs: world = tibia-local/canary/canary.exe (log world.log, online line 'server online!', start cap 120 s); database = mariadb on 127.0.0.1:3317 db canary, running character.reset_sql for Knight Sample (level 8, axe 30, at 1932,1349); client = pwsh live/run-hunt.ps1, which runs live/run-policy-scenario.ps1 -ExternalWorld with its defaults (troll x3, competitor policy, parity prelude, kit SQL, 240 s hunt timeout) and writes 'experience N' to the run log; run cap 420 s.\n- Plan [1; 3]: stage 0 is 1 smoke hunt, stage 1 is 3 counted hunts.\n- Runner: live-exec/live-block.ps1 under kit window run live-gui 60, alone on the box, with arena Lua pre-built so nothing compiles in the window.\n- Positive control, done: the staged copy passes world_executor's own Cells gleam tests locally (2/2, fake programs).\n- New harness pieces (unstaged in tibia): run-policy-scenario.ps1 -ExternalWorld (skips world stop/start, DB start, level reset and spawn/config handling; refuses SpawnFile/Ally) and run-hunt.ps1.\nAcceptance, both must hold:\n(A1) The block completes with 0 missing and the world released. The ideal is the driver's own assertion: trace 'start-world | reset, run 0.0 | counted, reset, run 1.0 | counted, reset, run 1.1 | counted, reset, run 1.2 | counted, release' and block log counted x4. One crashed hunt rerun by design (an extra 'rerun' in the trace, still 0 missing) also passes A1.\n(A2) No canary process remains after the block.\nDescriptive only: experience per hunt from the hunt logs; wall time per hunt; world start time; whether troll leftovers carry over between hunts (the world is no longer restarted per hunt, unlike the scenario's own mode).\nDecision: if both hold, TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP's last step is done and the item closes. If either fails, name the failing step (start, reset, client, record or release) here, fix only that, and request a new window. No other changes ride on this block.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_29 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 29u32, "2026-10-28T12:41Z", "tibia-lane", "P-EXEC1 attempt 1 (08:33-08:34): ran, then FAILED A1 for a harness reason. kit admitted live-gui at 08:33:37 after a 3-minute low-memory wait (floor 4096 MB). The executor itself worked: start-world, canary online at 08:34:20, reset per run, release, no canary left (A2 true). But every client run returned at once: trace \"reset, run 0.0 | rerun, reset, run 0.0 | missing\" for all 4 runs, 0 counted, and no hunt log, because run-hunt.ps1 never started.\nRoot cause, proven: on Windows, a PowerShell host spawned from an Erlang port without the `hide` option exits 0 at once, without running its -Command/-File and without output. A console window is created for the child, and pwsh/powershell die in it. Probes (erl -eval with open_port spawn_executable):\n- python \"-c ... raise SystemExit(7)\" gives exit 7 with output; cmd \"/c echo hi & exit 5\" gives 5; pwsh -Command \"exit 7\" gives 0 and no output.\n- cmd /c pwsh, powershell.exe, and pwsh with every std handle redirected to files: all exit 0, nothing ran (a marker file is never written).\n- python under the port spawning pwsh with inherited handles, DEVNULL or fresh pipes: exit 7 and the marker is written. So it is neither the handles nor the environment block.\n- the same pwsh port with the `hide` option: exit 7, \"hello\". That is the fix.\nThe 05:38 world_executor check used python as its client, which is why it passed.\nFix: lib process.port_for prepends erlang_term.atom \"hide\" to the options (one site; capture_status_within, start_logging and the capture path all go through port_for; Erlang ignores hide outside Windows). For the block, the staged lib copy in scratch live-exec/run has that one-line fix (live lib untouched). Attempt 2 is queued under kit window run live-gui --wait until-granted, inside the booked hour (main agreed the block may slide and run past 09:30 by the same amount). The lib fix goes as its own gated slice (LIB-PORT-HIDE) with a Gleam row: capture_status_within \"pwsh\" -Command \"exit 7\" returns 7, and the negative without hide returns 0.\nPre-registration: A1/A2 are unchanged and read the same way. The deviation is harness only: the executor's port option, not the plan, the trace or the counting rule.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_30 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 30u32, "2026-10-28T12:56Z", "tibia-lane", "P-EXEC1 PASSED (attempt 2, window win-1793191323701, granted 08:46:42 after a 4.6-minute low-memory wait; the block ran 08:46:42-08:56:11, 9.3 min). Pre-registered reading (note 28, applied by scratch live-exec/read-pexec1.ps1 -Since 08:46):\n- Driver test world_executor_runs_a_live_block (gleam): passed, exact trace \"start-world | reset, run 0.0 | counted, reset, run 1.0 | counted, reset, run 1.1 | counted, reset, run 1.2 | counted, release\".\n- Block log: counted x4, missing 0.\n- A1 (block completed, 0 missing, world released): TRUE.\n- A2 (no canary left after the block): TRUE.\nDescriptive, not acceptance:\n- World start: canary online at 08:47:02, about 20 s after the window opened (the driver compile is included in the 560 s test time).\n- Hunts (start to log): 08:47:12-08:48:41, 08:48:41-08:51:42, 08:51:42-08:52:59, 08:53:00-08:56:08, so 1.3-3.1 min each against the 7 min cap.\n- Experience per hunt: +90, +0, +90, +0. Potions 0, 0, 0, 1; deaths 0. The alternation fits the pre-registered leftover question. The world is no longer restarted per hunt, so trolls killed in hunt n are missing (respawn not yet due) in hunt n+1, and that hunt earns 0. This is a property of the external-world mode, not an executor fault. If later work needs comparable per-hunt experience, the reset step should also clear and respawn the hunt area. That would be a new item, not part of this decision.\nOnly change from attempt 1: lib process.port_for passes `hide` (note 29; LIB-PORT-HIDE gates on the node for the 14:00 window, with a real-pwsh row on both spawn paths). Plan, counting rule, limits, client and world are as pre-registered.\nDECISION (as pre-registered): both A1 and A2 hold, so TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP's last step is done and the item closes. The tibia tree carries no driver; world_live.spi stays in scratch, by design.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_1 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:07Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:07 x: user direction 2026-10-23 07:0x: 'all projects should also reuse as much lib as possible, also moving generic code to there'. Candidates found in x (a quick lib grep found no equivalents; confirm each before moving): (1) oauth1.spi: RFC 3986 percent-encoding, the OAuth 1.0a signature base string and HMAC-SHA1 header. Generic HTTP auth, no lib equivalent. (2) glyphs.spi: grapheme segmentation and NFC on rust + gleam. Generic Unicode, while X's weight table stays in x. (3) paging.spi: the cursor walk with repeat and budget guards (X-PAGING-GUARD's walker), a generic paginated fetch. (4) X-LOOKUP-LIMITS' chunked / after_first / all_got: generic list and result helpers. (5) events.spi's JSON string unescape: the lib needs a full decoder (X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE note 3). (6) prelude's list/result helpers, already covered by X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE. Rule per move: the lib version lands first with lib test blocks on every backend x declares (rust, gleam, zig where used), and the lib corpus check runs (memory lib-smoke-before-syntax); then x switches to it in one change and deletes its copy. Lib edits are main's domain, so coordinate each landing with main and never edit lib while a Cells job reads it (memory cells-reads-live-tree).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_2 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 2u32, "2026-10-23T12:55Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:55 x: text primitives that are generic and belong in lib/spiral (main's domain; x keeps local copies until then). x's glyphs.spi has two-armed (Rust + Gleam) one-expression versions of char_of, graphemes (first_grapheme), plain code-point weight, nfc and blank, and the X-EMOJI-WEIGHT draft adds first_code_point and code_point_count. lib/spiral/xml.spi already has code_point_at, code_point_width, code_point_count and code_point_string, but Rust only (the other arms failwith) and inside the xml module. Suggested shape: one lib text module (graphemes, code points, nfc) with Rust + Gleam arms each. xml.spi would call it, and x's glyphs would drop its copies. Names already clash: x's draft and lib xml both define code_point_count, harmless only while x never opens xml.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_3 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 3u32, "2026-10-23T13:31Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:31 x: concrete draft for main: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\unicode.spi (next to dictm.spi, same scratch package). It has char_of, first_grapheme_bytes, graphemes, first_code_point, code_point_count and nfc: each a one-expression primitive with Rust (unicode-segmentation / unicode-normalization / chars) and Gleam (gleam/string, erlang unicode) arms, and no host splice calls a Spiral closure (LIB-DICTM note 5's rule). 3 tests: graphemes_hold_clusters, code_points_count_scalars_not_bytes, nfc_composes. Not run yet; queued for after tibia's live block. Once it lands in lib/spiral, x's glyphs.spi keeps only the X-specific parts (weights, emoji_entity, defused dots, blank/trim helpers), and xml.spi's Rust-only code_point_* can call it.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_4 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 4u32, "2026-10-23T14:03Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:03 x: SUPERSEDED: this duplicated X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE notes 1 and 5 (2026-10-22 23:20 and 2026-10-23 00:31), which own the prelude-to-lib audit, including the split_on bug and the gaps (bind, take, split_list). Their call is the more careful one on spread: keep it until listm'.collect stops being quadratic, rather than swapping it directly as this note first said. What is new here, on top of them: a 4th gap, the strict decimal parse 'text -> option i32' (count_of_text; convert.parsed_integer and convert.i32 don't fit). See note 5 for the code.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_5 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 5u32, "2026-10-23T14:04Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:04 x: code for X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE's lib additions (that item owns the plan): %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\lib_gaps.spi, pure Spiral, queued for Cells rust + gleam after tibia's block. take -> listm'.take; bind -> resultm.bind; split_list -> sm'.split_list (whole separator width, which fixes the split_on bug recorded there); decimal_i32 -> convert.decimal_i32 (digits only, exactly int32-bounded). 4 tests.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_6 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 6u32, "2026-10-23T21:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:57 main: x handed off the draft unicode.spi (char_of, graphemes, first_code_point, code_point_count, nfc; 3/3 PASS) in %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft for main to land. Landing rule: Cells gate on the tree-lib baseline subset rule, after the tonight chain; rebase over eoie's 17:56 lib-gaps land (sm', am', process, testing, env changed) first, since x's lib_gaps.spi also touches listm'/sm'.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_7 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 7u32, "2026-10-23T22:12Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:12 x: step drafted, client OS boundary through lib: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_os_boundary_lib.ps1. It applies AFTER land_all's fit step, anchored on post-fit text, because args.spi and xc.spi are hash-guarded by the fit. args: process_args uses env.command_line_argument / command_line_argument_count (drops arg_count/arg_at, no other users). xc: write_line -> console.write_line, write_error -> console.write_error_line (same println!/eprintln! arms), file_text -> file_system.read_all_text (callers check is_file first, so the panic-on-missing arm is fine), is_file -> file_system.file_exists (exists && is_file, the same as Path::is_file). Dry-applied to post-fit copies: every anchor found once, 0 std/println splices left in args+xc, touched cells weigh 241 and 71 (XC005 budget ~270). Stays as is: token.spi's read/write, which must be total (X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE); lib's are panicking, a gap already in EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY n17. Also the xc_* cross-crate calls, which are x's own glue. Compile check (args + xc on a fit copy) queued for after tibia's live block; it joins land_all only after passing.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_8 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 8u32, "2026-10-23T22:39Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:39 main: the landing draft of x's unicode.spi is %TEMP%\\lib-unicode\\lib\\spiral\\unicode.spi, with apply.ps1 (new module, listed last). Changes from x's draft: lib layout (/// # unicode, test unicode static, /// ### headings), and the x-world package line dropped. graphemes is now one arm per backend, linear: Rust UnicodeSegmentation::graphemes(..).rev().fold with Cons prepends; Gleam list.fold(list.reverse(string.to_graphemes(s))). x's version re-sliced the string once per grapheme, quadratic on Rust, and also needed first_grapheme_bytes, which is gone. Each backend arm sits inline in its backend_switch instead of rust_/gleam_ wrapper functions, and test helpers are prefixed unicode_. Test headers follow the lib convention ('///> rust -d unicode-segmentation', no versions, as in crypto.spi); the graphemes test also checks that the first cluster is 'a' (core listm has no head, so a local match). Gate: lib-gate-kit -Name LIB-UNICODE -Files unicode.spi, chain A in requeue-after-block.ps1 for tibia's 'block end'.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_9 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 9u32, "2026-10-23T22:48Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:48 x: candidate (1) DRAFTED for main: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\oauth1.spi, a lib-layout module (/// # oauth1, test oauth1 static, /// ### headings, arms inline in backend_switch, as main restyled unicode). Lib grep (positive control: crypto.hash_text found) shows no percent-encoder, HMAC, SHA-1 or base64 in lib. Primitives with Rust + Gleam arms: percent_encode (RFC 3986 unreserved), hmac_sha1_base64 and sha1_hex. Gleam uses OTP crypto:mac/hash + bit_array base64/base16, so no new hex dependency. Pure Spiral parts: parameter_string (sorted by comp/lt_is like am'), signature_base, signing_key, protocol_params, signature and authorization_header. Nonce and timestamp are parameters, so the module is pure and x keeps its clock/nonce source. Tests on rust + gleam: unreserved encoding (with UTF-8 é), Twitter's published signing example to its documented signature hCtSmYh+iHYCEqBWrE7C7hYmtUk=, and sha1('abc'). Both expected values were recomputed independently with .NET HMACSHA1/SHA1 at 19:0x. Listed in lib-draft, queued for after tibia's block. Once landed, x's oauth1.spi keeps only clock_seed/unix_seconds/fresh_nonce and calls lib's authorization_header (an apply draft follows the landing).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_10 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 10u32, "2026-10-23T22:49Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:49 x: candidate (3) DRAFTED for main: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\cursor_walk.spi (lib layout). It's the generic cursor-paginated fetch from x's paging.spi as it stands after X-PAGING-GUARD: cursor_walker e = {halted : cursor_halt -> e; budget; fetch : string -> result cursor_page e}, walked_from/all_pages, and it stops on a repeated next_token (CursorRepeated) or at the page budget (CursorBudget). It's one let rec with explicit matches, so it doesn't depend on resultm.bind from the unlanded lib_gaps draft; listm'.exists' does the repeat check. The 2 tests carry over x's 4 walker cases on rust + gleam (follow tokens, failed page, repeated cursor, budget). x keeps whole_second (X's search timestamp rule) and its 500-page budget; paging.spi then wraps lib's walker (an apply draft follows the landing). Queued after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_11 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 11u32, "2026-10-23T22:50Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:50 x: candidate (4) DRAFTED for main: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\list_more.spi. These are X-LOOKUP-LIMITS' generic helpers, meant to land as listm'.skip_ (x's after_first), listm'.chunks_ (x's chunked; n <= 0 yields one chunk instead of looping forever) and resultm.collect_list (x's all_got: Ok list in order, or the first Error). The names were renamed at 19:1x to main's rule that runtime let rec list loops take a trailing underscore. Lib grep (positive control: listm'.try_find_ found) shows only am' array versions (am'.skip, am'.vec_chunks), nothing for lists. Kept separate from lib_gaps.spi because main is rebasing that draft over eoie's 17:56 lib-gaps landing. 2 tests on rust + gleam. Queued after tibia's block. Once landed, wire.spi's three helpers are deleted and call sites renamed (an apply draft follows the landing).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_12 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 12u32, "2026-10-23T23:19Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:19 main: unicode draft revised for one record per fact. lib/spiral/xml.spi already has Rust-only code_point_count and code_point_string (plus code_point_at, code_point_width and code_point_text, which xpath_eval, xsd_regex, json_schema and xsd_emit use). unicode is now listed right before xml in the lib modules, and xml.code_point_count and code_point_string delegate to unicode.code_point_count and unicode.char_of (conv). Their names stay for the callers, Rust behaviour is identical (from_u32 -> '' when invalid), and they gain Gleam arms. The gate covers unicode, xml, xpath_eval and xsd_regex.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_13 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 13u32, "2026-10-23T23:20Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:20 x: per main's 19:1x placement review, the oauth1/cursor_walk/list_more drafts are replaced by ONE landing patch: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\landing\\apply_x_lib_drafts.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. It adds crypto.sha1_hex/hmac_sha1_base64 (generic crypto in its existing home), sm'.percent_encode (beside encode_uri_component), and listm'.skip_/chunks_ APPENDED at the end of listm'.spi after main's take_ (Spiral resolves top-down; main's LIB-GAPS-X land rewrites collect and appends take_, so it lands first, and a precondition refuses with no writes until take_ exists). Also resultm.collect_list (anchored with its inl line, since unwrap_err is a prefix of unwrap_err'), and new modules cursor_walk + oauth1 (oauth1 calls crypto.* and sm'.percent_encode). Lines are at most 120 with \\ continuations. Proof copy %TEMP%\\x-libcopy2 built in tree order: mine alone refused (crypto.spi hash unchanged), then main's %TEMP%\\lib-gaps-x\\apply.ps1 -Apply, then mine cleanly (listm' take_ 766 < skip_ 791 < chunks_ 797). Cells after tibia's block: x-libcopy2\\check\\x_lib_check.spi + oauth1.spi + cursor_walk.spi in the copy. Loose drafts lib-draft\\oauth1/cursor_walk/list_more.spi are superseded (kept as reference until main lands).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_14 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 14u32, "2026-10-23T23:35Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:35 x: x-side follow-up for candidate (4) DRAFTED: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_wire_lib_helpers.ps1 -Client <apps/client> -Lib <lib/spiral>. It applies after land_all's fit step, anchored on post-fit wire.spi. It's a no-rename delegation: after_first -> listm'.skip_, chunked -> listm'.chunks_, all_got -> resultm.collect_list (the accumulator contract kept: Ok (rev acc ++ l); callers in refs.spi/users.spi pass Nil). One deliberate difference: x's chunked looped forever on n <= 0, lib's chunks_ returns one chunk (callers pass lookup_limit () = 100). It refuses until the lib has skip_/chunks_/collect_list. Trialled: applied to a post-fit wire.spi copy against %TEMP%\\x-libcopy2; refused against the live lib. Compile check runs once main lands, on the real lib.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_15 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 15u32, "2026-10-23T23:36Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:36 x: paging.spi -> lib cursor_walk DRAFTED at 19:4x: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_paging_cursor_walk.ps1 -Client <apps/client> -Lib <lib/spiral>, applied after X-PAGING-GUARD's draft. It replaces paging's walker block (located by its boundaries, verified by its SHA-256, so a drifted text refuses) with aliases: type page = cursor_page, pager e = string -> result cursor_page e, page_budget, and an all_pages that calls cursor_walk.all_pages. whole_second and paging's tests stay; walk_with uses cursor_walk.walked_from with a literal trail. Only thread.spi and wire.spi name the types (step-1 grep: thread 14/17/31, wire 58/403-408); both get 'open cursor_walk' BEFORE 'open paging'. Order matters: lib's all_pages takes a walker record, and Spiral lets the later open win silently, so paging's all_pages must come later. Also renamed lib cursor_walk's test helpers with the module prefix (cursor_walk_halt_text, cursor_walk_two_pages), since wire defines its own cursor_halt_text. Trialled on copies against %TEMP%\\x-libcopy2: applied cleanly; the only line over 120 in paging is the pre-existing 'broken' test helper. Refuses until lib has cursor_walk.spi. Cells on paging/wire/thread run once main lands.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_16 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 16u32, "2026-10-23T23:39Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:39 x: the lib module in the landing patch is renamed oauth1 -> oauth (functions and types unchanged). x has its own apps/client/oauth1.spi, and no x module shares a name with a lib module today, so how Spiral resolves a package-vs-lib module name clash is untested. A lib oauth1 could break x's build the moment main lands it, before x migrates. Rebuilt x-libcopy2 in tree order (main's lib-gaps-x, then the patch): clean; the queue now checks x-libcopy2\\lib\\spiral\\oauth.spi. When x migrates, its oauth1.spi keeps the clock/nonce source and calls oauth.authorization_header.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_17 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 17u32, "2026-10-24T00:41Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:41 main: unicode's module position moved before json_scan (lib package.spiproj line 42), not before xml (97). json_scan's \\u branch (JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES) will call unicode.char_of, and xml's delegation still works because xml comes later. unicode needs only sm'_operators (line 26) and core listm/sm, so the move is safe. The apply's dry run is clean; its gate runs in chain A.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_18 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 18u32, "2026-10-24T00:46Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:46 main: unicode.char_of moved to the run_target form, the JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES prerequisite: Gleam, Rust, C (the probed ({ }) UTF-8 encoder), Fsharp (ConvertFromUtf32 with the surrogate check) and TypeScript (String.fromCodePoint, written without '!(' since ! starts macro interpolation), plus '| _ => failwith', so every backend compiles it. Risk to watch: no lib function yet uses '| C =>' in a run_target match (0 of 31 Gleam/C/TS case uses are C), though the backend union has a C case. The unicode gate compiles the C test, so if C does not resolve the gate is red and the land is skipped.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_19 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 19u32, "2026-10-24T01:11Z", "x", "2026-10-23 21:11 x: CORRECTION by user direction (21:0x): \"why we're delegating to main lib work? makes no sense, one of my goals of inventing eoie is the atomic batched patches, so all lanes can go ham, also with automatic backups etc\". The earlier note here saying \"Lib edits are main's domain, so coordinate each landing with main\" was my own unexamined assumption, not a rule. From now on x lands its own lib changes through eoie do atomic batched edits (expect counts, all-or-nothing, sha256 per file) plus the lib Cells gate. Coordination with other lanes is about order and live-tree reads only, not ownership. x takes over landing of its own drafts: the x landing patch (crypto/sm'/listm'/resultm + oauth + cursor_walk) and LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS's 7 batches. Main keeps LIB-GAPS-X, which x's patch builds on (take_).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_20 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 20u32, "2026-10-24T01:12Z", "x", "2026-10-23 21:12 x: landing split, agreed with main. dictm, unicode and try_find' stay in main's chain A, already gated and rebased (no re-owning work in motion). x lands its own work as eoie do programs, one per change, each followed by the lib Cells gate on the touched modules. Part A, independent: LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS batches 1-7, crypto sha1_hex/hmac_sha1_base64, sm' percent_encode, resultm collect_list, new oauth + cursor_walk. Part B: listm' skip_/chunks_, after LIB-GAPS-X's take_ is in the tree. Constraint: lib has to be quiet (no Cells reader); main's baseline/cells-pack/dib reruns read it for hours, so part A lands in the first quiet window of 20+ min.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_21 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 21u32, "2026-10-24T01:36Z", "x", "10-23 21:18-21:22 incident (as of 21:36): a control run of land_lib_part_a.ps1 applied part A to the live lib, because the runner had no target param and its reader check only matched run_notebook.ps1. Reverted with reverse eoie do programs; restore round-trip proven (0 differing); main confirms no reader was hit. Runner rebuilt: mandatory -Target, -Live required and must match; ONE eoie do batch (33 replacements, 7 files, 38 ms) plus generated undo.eoie; the guard checks eoie window claim (tree-read leases), running lib/cells eoie jobs, erl/beam/Spiral-dotnet, live-gui windows and lib command lines, then again before applying. Controls on a scratch probe all failed for the tested reason (no -Live -> 64; dummy reader, tree-read lease -> 75; broken anchor -> nothing written, 0/1138 changed). Order vs main-lib-gaps-x: commutes (main appends bind/split_list/take_, rewrites listm collect; part A anchors mid-file). Queued: job-1792805715663-x-lib-part-a-land (after main-float-wire-gate), then job-1792805730458-x-lib-part-a-gate (--after-ok --heavy). Part B (listm skip_/chunks_) waits for main-lib-gaps-x-land (take_).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_22 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 22u32, "2026-10-24T01:39Z", "x", "Part B ready as one eoie do program (%TEMP%\\x-world\\lib-draft\\programs-part-b\\land.eoie): it inserts skip_/chunks_ + test right after main's take_ definition, anchored on 'taken n l'. The anchor encodes the dependency: on the current lib it refuses (nothing written), and on a scratch lib with main's lib-gaps-x applied it lands at listm' lines 774/780. Land it through the guarded runner after job-1792801901862-main-lib-gaps-x-land is ok, then gate listm' with Cells.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_23 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 23u32, "2026-10-24T02:29Z", "x", "Part A LANDED in live lib 22:06:07 (as of 22:29): one eoie do batch, 7 files, 33 replacements, plus new oauth.spi/cursor_walk.spi. job-1792807012027-x-part-a-lander landed it on attempt 13; earlier attempts were refused while main fmt-only-gate, erl and Spiral dotnet readers were active, and the apply-time re-check caught an erl that started mid-run. Guard fixes on the way: own job excluded via the supervisor pid in the eoie job folder (it had flagged itself at first); dotnet build tools (MSBuild/fsc) no longer count as readers; SpiralCompiler.exe added. Gate job-1792807567547 ran on compiler B553EF6B78E6EE29 (slot at 22:20:39, after the 22:18 install): x_lib_check 2/2; gleam_os_check 8/9, the failure being the notebook's Rust test missing -d regex (normalize_path's regex dependency predates this change); notebook fixed, recheck job-1792808951052. Undo: programs-landing\\batch\\undo.eoie plus removing the 2 new files.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_24 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 24u32, "2026-10-24T02:35Z", "x", "Part B armed (22:35): generic land_lib_program.ps1 (mandatory -Program/-Target, -Live must match, guard dot-sourced from land_guard.ps1, rehearsal on a fresh copy, guard re-check before apply), plus programs-part-b\\undo.eoie. Scratch tests on a copy taken after part A landed: without take_ it rejects and writes nothing; -Live on a non-live target gives 64; with gaps-x applied it lands; undo restores byte for byte; dot-sourced guard names a planted reader (75). Launch when main confirms take_ is live: eoie job start x-part-b-lander 14400000 pwsh -NoProfile -File land_when_quiet.ps1 -Program programs-part-b\\land.eoie -GateNotebook C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral\\listm'.spi -Label x-lib-part-b.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_25 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 25u32, "2026-10-24T03:02Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:02 main: main's gaps-x lib slice LANDED in live lib/spiral (job-1792810371034-main-lib-gaps-x-land-2, exit 0): listm' take_ (line 766) and linear collect, resultm.bind (545), sm'.split_list (2400, let rec), sm'.decimal_i32 (2425). Gate job-1792801901497 was GREEN on the post-install compiler (B553EF6B78E6EE29), on a lib copy taken at 22:24:53 that already held x's Part A: every candidate red was also red on the unchanged lib. x's Part B (skip_/chunks_) is unblocked; x was told at 23:00.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_26 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 26u32, "2026-10-24T03:45Z", "x", "23:45 x: x-side switch for candidate (2), now that main's lib unicode.spi is live (20:45). One eoie do program, %TEMP%\\x-world\\lib-draft\\programs-x-glyphs-unicode\\land.eoie (anchors generated from the staged file's text). It makes glyphs.char_of, graphemes and nfc one-line delegations to unicode.char_of, unicode.graphemes and unicode.nfc, and drops x's rust_/gleam_ wrappers plus the per-grapheme re-slicing walk (quadratic on Rust; the lib's version is linear). Names stay, so the 8 modules that open glyphs don't change. The lib arms are character for character the same as x's (char_of, nfc) or the same segmentation (graphemes, extended). Dry-run fits both the staged tree and the landing-trial tree (after apply_emoji_weight; its anchors are disjoint). Cells glyphs + splitter on %TEMP%\\x-glyphs-check: job-1792813502665. The x tree lands only when the user does. Left for this item after that: oauth1/paging/lookup switch to lib oauth, cursor_walk and chunks_ (apply_paging_cursor_walk is drafted; chunks_ waits for part B).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_27 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 27u32, "2026-10-24T03:48Z", "x", "23:48 x: x-side switch for candidate (1). programs-x-oauth1-lib\\land.eoie (generated from the staged text) makes x's oauth1.spi a thin layer over lib oauth. type oauth1 = oauth.oauth1_credentials (same fields), pair = oauth.oauth1_pair, percent = sm'.percent_encode (the same Rust arm character for character, plus a Gleam arm), fresh_nonce = crypto.sha1_hex (clock_seed ()), oauth_header = oauth.authorization_header (same protocol-param order). It keeps x's clock_seed and unix_seconds. It drops x's own hmac/base64/sorting/base-string code and the digest_bytes nominal: outside callers (transport, wire, net, token, write) use only percent, unix_seconds, fresh_nonce, oauth_header, oauth1 and pair, and wire's 'quoted' is its own JSON helper. Tests: x's percent test is the same fact as lib's percent_encode_keeps_only_unreserved, so it's dropped; the published-example checks live in lib oauth; x keeps a_fresh_nonce_is_forty_hex_digits. Dry-run fits the staged tree. Cells oauth1 + wire on %TEMP%\\x-glyphs-check (which also has the glyphs switch): job-1792813707329.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_28 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 28u32, "2026-10-24T05:00Z", "x", "10-24 01:00 x: oauth1 switch, first Cells run: a_fresh_nonce_is_forty_hex_digits failed on Rust, E0433 'cannot find crate sha1'. fresh_nonce now goes through crypto.sha1_hex, so the test needs '///> rust -d sha1' (lib oauth's own tests declare -d hmac sha1 base64). This was a directive bug in my draft; the code was fine. Fixed in programs-x-oauth1-lib\\land.eoie and the check copy, and the program still fits the staged tree. Recheck queued after the running wire check.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_29 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 29u32, "2026-10-24T05:04Z", "x", "10-24 01:04 x: oauth1 switch, what each check covers. Only net.spi:85 (signed_header, exported as xc_oauth1_header) calls oauth_header, and no Cells test does. wire.spi uses only the oauth1 type. So the delegation's Rust path is proven by net's cargo build, not by Cells. That build compiles the same calls against the same pins: lib crypto.hmac_sha1_base64 / sha1_hex use x's exact APIs (<hmac::Hmac<sha1::Sha1> as KeyInit>::new_from_slice, Mac::update/finalize, base64 Engine::encode STANDARD; lifted from x), and net's Cargo.toml already pins hmac 0.13, sha1 0.11 and base64 0.23. Add 'cargo check -p xc_net' on the check copy to this item's gate before landing.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_30 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 30u32, "2026-10-24T05:06Z", "x", "10-24 01:06 x: glyphs -> lib unicode delegation, glyphs.spi 8/8 PASS (job-1792817008488, rust + gleam) on %TEMP%\\x-glyphs-check. splitter.spi (the heavy glyphs user) is running next.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_31 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 31u32, "2026-10-24T05:36Z", "x", "10-24 01:36 x: glyphs -> unicode delegation VERIFIED: glyphs 8/8 and splitter 7/7 (rust + gleam, job-1792817008488). oauth1 delegation: wire 23/23 with exit 0. Its 4 failed_targets entries (zig x3 and 'rust -d serde_json' on the negative tests) match every earlier wire run back to the staged tree (10-22 09:23), so they're pre-existing and not from the delegation. oauth1.spi's own recheck (sha1 directive) is queued.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_32 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 32u32, "2026-10-24T06:08Z", "x", "10-24 02:08 x: oauth1 -> lib delegation VERIFIED in Cells. The oauth1.spi recheck with '///> rust -d sha1' passes (job-1792818046562), and wire 23/23 matches its baseline. Both x-side switches (glyphs -> unicode, oauth1 -> oauth/crypto/sm') are now ready-to-land programs for the user's x landing; oauth1's landing gate also takes 'cargo check -p xc_net' (n29). Left for this item: paging -> cursor_walk (apply_paging_cursor_walk, drafted) and lookup -> chunks_ (waits for part B).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_33 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 33u32, "2026-10-24T06:16Z", "x", "10-24 02:16 x: paging -> lib cursor_walk, now against the LIVE lib (cursor_walk landed in part A, 22:06). apply_paging_cursor_walk.ps1 -Lib C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral applies cleanly to a copy of the landing-trial client (%TEMP%\\x-paging-check; the post-X-PAGING-GUARD walker hash matched). Cells paging + wire + thread\\thread queued: job-1792822553404. This one has its own copy, so the lookup check's wire edits on x-glyphs-check stay independent.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_34 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 34u32, "2026-10-27T11:54Z", "x", "2026-10-27 07:54 x: groomed to a light, pullable next step. Dry run of %TEMP%\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_paging_cursor_walk.ps1 on a copy of today's tree refuses 'walker block not found': paging.spi was rewritten since 10-23 (X-PAGING-GUARD, the edition-3 respell). Next (CPU-light): re-anchor the paging -> lib cursor_walk draft on the live paging.spi in edition-3 spelling and apply it into the tree unstaged; the lookup -> chunks_ part follows the same way. X-WIP-VERIFY proves them. The X-DONE dep is dropped (epic-on-child, backwards).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_35 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 35u32, "2026-10-27T11:58Z", "x", "2026-10-27 07:58 x: paging -> lib cursor_walk applied in the tree (unstaged), re-spelled to edition 3 from the 10-23 draft's intent. paging.spi drops its own walker (walk, walk_step, halt_at, walked_from/next_of/walk_on, start_walk, cursor_stop, cursor_halt) and keeps page = cursor_page, pager e, page_budget and its two-argument all_pages over cursor_walk.all_pages, plus whole_second; its tests run through lib's walked_from. wire.spi and thread.spi open cursor_walk before paging, so cursor_halt, cursor_stop and the CursorRepeated/CursorBudget constructors come from lib and paging's all_pages still wins. Left: lookup -> chunks_. Proof: the next X-WIP-VERIFY.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_36 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 36u32, "2026-10-27T12:00Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:00 x: lookup -> chunks_ was already landed: refs.spi chunk_fetched and users.spi ids_chunks both call listm'.chunks_ (lookup_limit ()), with user_ids_chunk_to_the_lookup_limit testing 100/100/50. All four moves are in the tree now: glyphs -> unicode, oauth1 -> lib oauth/crypto, lookup -> chunks_, and paging -> cursor_walk (today). Only the verify of today's paging move is left.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_37 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 37u32, "2026-10-28T10:42Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:39 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_kit_next_fits_now_1 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_start_under_shell_1 () = Note ("KIT-START-UNDER-SHELL", 1u32, "2026-10-23T12:50Z", "eoie", "08:50 requested by main (CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT): one generic verb that starts a long-lived process with a live parent, so Cells' CompilerDaemon (and any other daemon) stops running parentless without a second Win32 launcher. Shape: eoie process start-under-shell <program> [args...]: CreateProcessW with PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_PARENT_PROCESS = the user shell (GetShellWindow -> explorer.exe), handle inheritance off, NORMAL priority, TEMP cwd, detached; prints pid=<n> parent=<shell pid> and exits 0; falls back to a plain detached start (reported as parent=none) only when there is no shell window. Reuses the launch primitive HP ships for the job host (one primitive in the eoie_os boundary package per EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY's 08:32 scope), so the host and this verb share code. Test: start a ping under the shell, read its parent (explorer.exe), stop it. Priority P2, after HP-tail and the release chain.")
inl note_of_kit_start_under_shell_2 () = Note ("KIT-START-UNDER-SHELL", 2u32, "2026-10-23T13:04Z", "eoie", "09:04 drafted for release L: new OS boundary crate eoie_os_launch. launch.spi emits the launch function once through Spiral global (top-level Rust: CreateProcessW with the shell as parent attribute, the same fallbacks as the job host, returns pid and parent; unix keeps a Command in its own process group) plus a one-expression primitive; run.spi is lib-only (env args, console). Verb: VerbProcess -> 'eoie process start-under-shell <program> [args...]' printing 'pid=<n> parent=<shell pid> program=...'. The Rust compiled standalone and started ping under explorer.exe (reported parent 8628 = actual parent explorer.exe); both Spiral files parse with the 5e formatter, which left the global Rust byte-identical. Prelaunch clean after two checker fixes (a triple-quoted global block and a formatter-wrapped macro were read as code); the improved checker still passes GE2's chain. after-install-L probes it (parent must be explorer.exe). Next: the job host calls this function instead of its own copy.")
inl note_of_kit_start_under_shell_3 () = Note ("KIT-START-UNDER-SHELL", 3u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "eoie", "23:06 groom: user direction 10-23 22:1x is that eoie processes belong to the session that started them, not explorer (EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT). This verb stays for daemons that must outlive any session, but should gain a start-under-owner form on the same eoie_parented_start primitive (owner pid instead of the shell window), and callers should pick it unless outliving the session is the point.")
inl note_of_kit_start_under_shell_4 () = Note ("KIT-START-UNDER-SHELL", 4u32, "2026-10-24T04:34Z", "eoie", "00:34 found and fixed a certain stage-L build failure before L launched. launch.spi put its Rust in a top-level 'global' block of a non-entry module, which codegen drops: the regenerated os_launch.rs was 4,623 bytes with no eoie_shell_parented_start*, so run.spi's call failed E0425 under real cargo (the 09:04 check compiled the Rust by hand, outside Spiral). Fix: start-under-shell\\eoie_os_launch\\launch.spi now puts the global inside shell_parented_started's body (move-global-into-body.ps1; Rust byte-identical; backup .before-global-move). Verified: regen + cargo check of eoie-os-launch + eoie-job-host on the emulated post-R tree, PASS 00:33, os_launch.rs 13,334 bytes. This edits stage L's source while the train runs (GE3 queued); justified because L would otherwise fail deterministically, and the change was emulated and compiled before L launches.")
inl note_of_kit_start_under_shell_5 () = Note ("KIT-START-UNDER-SHELL", 5u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_kit_start_under_shell_6 () = Note ("KIT-START-UNDER-SHELL", 6u32, "2026-10-31T00:22Z", "kit", "10-30 20:0x kit: REGRESSION FIXED in the tree (unstaged; install rides the next train, which needs the user's OK). The user reported visible erl windows appearing since main's 14:09 Cells change (relayed by compiler): the Cells compiler daemon now always starts via 'kit process start-under-shell'. Cause: kit_os_launch/launch.spi started the program with DETACHED_PROCESS (flags 0x228 / 0x0100_0228). A detached cmd.exe has no console, so the console program it starts (erl.exe) gets a new, visible console window. Fix: CREATE_NO_WINDOW instead (0x0800_0220 / 0x0900_0220; the new-process-group and breakaway bits kept). cmd then gets a hidden console, and its children inherit it. Proof, 2 runs each of 'process start-under-shell cmd /c ping ...': installed kit C528BE4D shows 1 visible console window per run, candidate 6E4F7AB5 shows 0 (test processes mine, stopped). Mitigation until the install: SPIRAL_CELLS_SHELL_LAUNCHER (process-level, set in lib_gate.ps1) can point at C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\launch-no-window\\kit-nowindow-6E4F7AB5.exe; told compiler. os_launch.rs regenerated with pack 8 (32 lines of codegen drift since 10-28, expected).")
inl note_of_kit_start_under_shell_7 () = Note ("KIT-START-UNDER-SHELL", 7u32, "2026-10-31T11:58Z", "kit", "10-31 07:4x kit: DONE (installed-is-done). release-acceptance.ps1 on the installed kit E14F1F0B (TRAIN5e DONE 07:43, 140/140 notebooks; the binary was swapped retire-then-move while lanes ran) passed 23/23 at 07:44. Row: 'start-under-shell opens no visible console' (0 windows; the old installed kit opened 1).")
inl note_of_kit_start_under_shell_8 () = Note ("KIT-START-UNDER-SHELL", 8u32, "2026-11-06T11:33Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_1 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T13:34Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:34 x: found while inventorying the stub for SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY. stub.py create() checks len(text) > 280 in code points, so a 200-CJK post (X weight 400) is accepted, and e2e can never catch an overweight frame. The stub's weight must be an independent implementation (Python, from twitter-text v3's ranges and emoji entities), not a call into x's glyphs, so it stays an oracle. Test: one tutorial or e2e-only step posting a text that is 141 CJK characters gets the 400. Land after X-EMOJI-WEIGHT, so both sides count emoji the same way.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_2 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 2u32, "2026-10-23T13:43Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:43 x: drafted and verified red/green (no compile: release xc.exe + the Python stub). Oracle: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\x_weight.py, an algorithm independent of x's glyphs: NFC first as twitter-text does, then regex-matched emoji entities (flag pairs, keycaps, pictograph or VS16-symbol sequences with skin tones, tags and ZWJ joins) at 2 each, then per-code-point v3 ranges. Its self-test agrees on 14 of 14 cases (thumb + tone 2, ZWJ family 2, heart + VS16 2, keycap 2, JP flag 2, decomposed e 1 after NFC, (c) 1, a + VS16 3, 140 CJK 280, 141 CJK 282). apply_stub_weighted_limit.ps1 inlines it into stub.py (the stub runs with python -I, so no sibling import), changes create() to x_weight(text) > 280 with the same 400 message, and adds a README step after 'posted 9001': xc post ('日' * 141); $LASTEXITCODE. GREEN (patched stub): the step answers 'xc: X API answered 400 for /2/tweets: Invalid Request: text must be 1 to 280 characters' and 4. RED (same README, original stub): 'posted 9002' and 0. Both copies: accept and verify have 0 failures, so no other step changed and the refused post uses no id. Note: x's counts are >= the oracle's for every case (x doesn't NFC; it counts a decomposed e as 2), so the stub never refuses a post x's splitter produced. Harness: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\e2e_stub_check.ps1.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_3 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 3u32, "2026-10-23T16:39Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:39 x: known simplification in the drafted oracle (x_weight.py): X weighs every URL as 23 (t.co, twitter-text's transformedURLLength), but the oracle counts a URL's real characters. So it is STRICTER than X for URLs longer than 23 and laxer for shorter ones. No tutorial step hits it today: framed thread posts defuse dots (no links form), and e2e's single posts carry no URLs. If a future step posts a link, add twitter-text's URL rule to the oracle first (its validUrl regex is large; a scheme-and-host subset covers x's cases), or the stub will refuse posts X accepts.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_4 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 4u32, "2026-10-24T04:07Z", "x", "10-24 00:07 x: n3's known gap CLOSED for scheme URLs. x_weight.py gains twitter-text's transformedURLLength rule: each http(s):// URL weighs 23 whatever its length, and trailing . , ! ? ; : ) ] ' quote stay outside it. Emoji and code-point weights apply to the text between URLs (plain_weight). Seven new self-test cases make 21/21. Positive control: the old rule weighs the same three URL cases 61, 11 and 16, the new one 27, 23 and 25. Still NOT covered: bare domains ('example.com' weighs 11 here, 23 on X, since twitter-text links them through its TLD list). x's own posts defuse dots (defused_dot), so only a human-typed bare domain would hit it. apply_stub_weighted_limit.ps1 inlines the file up to its __main__ block. A simulated inline into the staged stub.py compiles (py_compile 0), and the new names (plain_weight, URL, TRANSFORMED_URL_LENGTH) don't clash: stub.py's 'url' is a lowercase local.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_5 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 5u32, "2026-10-24T07:10Z", "x", "10-24 03:10 x: n4 CORRECTED by a cross-check against x's own weighting (X-EMOJI-WEIGHT's Spiral cases agree with the oracle on every shared case). The 00:05 URL regex matched DEFUSED links: x writes dots in posts as ZWSP + '.' (defused_dot), Python's \\s doesn't match U+200B, so 'https://x<ZWSP>.co/abc' was weighed 23, while X doesn't link it (a host containing U+200B isn't a valid domain) and counts its characters. The regex now requires a real host (label(.label)+ over [a-z0-9-], case-insensitive) before an optional path. Two new defused cases make 23/23. Positive control: URL.search finds 'https://x.co/abc' but not its defused form. The simulated inline into the staged stub.py still compiles.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_6 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 6u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_7 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_8 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 8u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 02:0x x: e2e evidence; Done waits on a fully green verify. Weighted stub limit in HEAD since 6ef9c48; the README step xc post ('<CJK>' * 141) matched its expected 400 refusal ('same', 1 request) in node verify job-1792988648856-x-verify-remote9 among 36/36 tutorial steps (the node log prints the CJK as '?', an encoding artifact of the log only).")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_9 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 9u32, "2026-10-28T10:53Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_kit_job_rechain_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RECHAIN", 1u32, "2026-10-23T14:13Z", "eoie", "10:13 from main (10:1x): re-chaining queued jobs costs a stop and a fresh start per job, plus relaunching every job behind it; done 7-8 times on 10-23 (around HP, tibia's block, serializing a chain), each time retyping program and args and re-reading new ids. Builds on job start --after/--after-ok (EOIE-JOBS note 31). Rule: every verb one call (EOIE-ONE-CALL). Shape to decide when drafting: re-point by editing the queued job's after file under the jobs home vs stop+restart with the same command record; the first keeps the id, which is what callers hold.")
inl note_of_kit_job_rechain_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RECHAIN", 2u32, "2026-10-23T14:34Z", "eoie", "10:34 eoie: part 1 drafted, scratch job-rechain/apply-job-rechain.ps1 (dry run clean on copies). eoie job after <queued-id> <predecessor-id> [--ok] rewrites the waiting job's after file in place, so the id main holds stays valid. Refusals in a fixed order: unknown job, unknown predecessor, self, not started with --after, no longer waiting, and a cycle (the predecessor's own after chain, walked up to 64 steps, leads back to the job). The waiter (eoie_job_after) re-read nothing before: it read the after file once. It now re-reads every 250 ms beat, and the ok-mode decision uses the predecessor that actually settled; the file is migrated off its two host macros onto lib split/format (AGENTS.md: touching a legacy function migrates it). Model: typed facts record -> rechain_outcome union (HostFree crate eoie_job_rechain, model tests). CLI test in stop_cli: re-point a job from a 20 s predecessor to a 0.5 s one with --ok, finishes within 15 s while the old predecessor still runs, plus the cycle, not-chained, unknown and settled refusals. Part 2 (job chain) not drafted yet. Checks wait for tibia's block.")
inl note_of_kit_job_rechain_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RECHAIN", 3u32, "2026-10-23T14:38Z", "eoie", "10:38 eoie: part 2 drafted in the same slice. eoie job chain [--ok] [<after-id>] -- <label> <timeout-ms> <program> [args...] -- ... parses into a typed chain_plan (head: optional --ok and starting job id; links split at --, each needs label, timeout, program) and starts each link as job start --after(-ok) <previous id> through the running binary (lib gap env.current_executable added to lib-gaps), so the launch logic stays in one place and --heavy/--compile inside a link keep working. It prints each start receipt, then 'eoie job chain ok jobs=N ids=a,b', and stops at the first link that does not start, naming the ids started before it. Model test covers head, links, short link, refusals and id parsing; CLI test chains two jobs with --ok and waits on the second. Checks queued with the other f6 drafts for after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_kit_job_rechain_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RECHAIN", 4u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): JOB-2 (chains that stop on the first failing prerequisite; per-stage receipts go to EOIE-BUILD-PHASES). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_job_rechain_5 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RECHAIN", 5u32, "2026-10-26T06:58Z", "eoie", "02:58 eoie: stop_cli's job-ordering tests flake under load. In the RESUME release (02:5x, box saturated, 20 of 53 notebooks after 82 min) after_repoints_a_waiting_job_and_refuses_cycles_and_settled_jobs failed, although it passed in the SLATE and FASTRENAME candidate runs. All four of these tests fail on the installed binary (oldbinary7 run): chain_starts_each_job_after_the_previous_one, after_repoints_a_waiting_job_and_refuses_cycles_and_settled_jobs, after_waits_for_the_named_job_and_after_ok_skips_when_it_failed, a_queued_job_takes_a_new_limit_until_its_child_starts. So by the release gate's pre-registered rule (a row only for a test that also fails on the installed binary) all four are now known-failure rows, added before the gate step reads the table, so a load flake can't fail an otherwise good release. Owed: make them deterministic. They assert on job-state transitions within fixed waits, so under contention a supervisor starts late. Wait on the state, not on a clock, then remove the rows.")
inl note_of_kit_job_rechain_6 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RECHAIN", 6u32, "2026-10-26T08:08Z", "eoie", "04:08 eoie: the stop_cli timing flakes (note 5), drafted deterministic: kit scratch clean\\kit-rename\\stop-cli-deterministic.do, 5 replacements, test-only notebook (not a crate module, so no regen). Each change only removes a race; no assertion changes. (1) started_tree: the sleeper's job timeout 60 s to 600 s (it is always stopped at the end of its test) and the child-beat wait 20 s to 60 s, so a_queued_job_takes_a_new_limit_until_its_child_starts no longer loses its running predecessor to a timeout before amend runs. (2) after_waits_for_the_named_job...: first sleeps 30 s instead of 8 s (second must still be queued at the first status check, which several job calls can delay under load) with a 120 s timeout, and the wait on second gets 120 s. (3) after_repoints...: slow sleeps 600 s with a 600 s timeout (stopped at the end), so 'slow is still running' holds at any load, and the wait on the repointed job gets 60 s. chain_starts_each_job_after_the_previous_one is not a timing flake (the id that chain prints is unknown to job wait), so its row stays until that bug is fixed. Lands with the post-rename batch (clean\\respell\\land-post-rename.ps1); then a stop_cli run on the loaded node is the proof, and the three timing rows leave the known-failures table.")
inl note_of_kit_job_rechain_7 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RECHAIN", 7u32, "2026-10-26T08:12Z", "eoie", "04:12 eoie: chain_starts_each_job_after_the_previous_one is a TEST HARNESS bug, not a product bug. Direct run of the candidate kit.exe on the worker node with a scratch jobs home: job chain --ok with two links printed both receipts and 'kit job chain ok jobs=2 ids=job-...-one,job-...-two'; both job wait calls returned state=finished exit=0. The test takes the second id with word_after chained ',' and kit_cli_check/cli.spi's word_after splits the rest only at a space (char 32). The ids are the last thing on the line, so the id kept the trailing newline, and job wait looked up 'job-...-two' plus a line ending: unknown job id (invisible in the printed message, and consistent rather than flaky). Every other caller (id=, at=, elapsed_ms=, supervisor_pid=) reads a value followed by a space, which is why only this test failed. Fix drafted: kit scratch clean\\kit-rename\\word-after-whitespace.do (split at any whitespace); kit_cli_check is a notebook-only harness package (no Cargo.toml), so no regen. With it and stop-cli-deterministic.do, all four stop_cli known-failure rows can leave once a node run confirms.")
inl note_of_kit_job_rechain_8 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RECHAIN", 8u32, "2026-10-30T14:22Z", "kit", "10-30 10:3x kit groom (R3, silent 4.3 d): the product ships and both test fixes are in the live tree. Installed kit C528BE4D prints 'job chain [--ok] [--serial] ...' and 'job after <queued-id> <predecessor-id> [--ok]'. Harness fix word-after-whitespace.do (survived the wipe in clean\\kit-rename) is applied: kit_cli_check/cli.spi splits on char::is_whitespace. stop-cli-deterministic.do is applied too: stop_cli.spi:93 sleeper 600000 ms, :95 child-beat wait 60000. NEXT (owner kit, event = TRAIN5 rerun, which runs the CLI tests): close when chain_starts_each_job_after_the_previous_one and the stop_cli tests pass in that train's candidate; on a failure, record the first failing assertion here.")
inl note_of_kit_job_rechain_9 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RECHAIN", 9u32, "2026-10-31T12:42Z", "kit", "10-31 08:4x kit: DONE, and the record was stale: the installed kit (E14F1F0B; the verb predates it) lists 'job after <queued-id> <predecessor-id> [--ok]'. LIVE PROOF: job A (sleep 40) and job B (sleep 5) were started, and queued job Q was started --after A. 'kit job after Q B' answered 'ok ... after=B after_mode=any previous=A'. Q finished (exit 0, printed 'ran') at 17.7 s while A was still running at 36.8 s, so Q followed B, not A. Label, timeout and command were kept, as the usage says.")
inl note_of_kit_job_rechain_10 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RECHAIN", 10u32, "2026-11-06T11:34Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_1 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 1u32, "2026-10-23T14:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:30 main: found reading samples/native_closure_union_rec/main.c. UH0 * UH0_Nil() { malloc; tag = 1; refc = 1 }, so every list end and every payload-free case pays a malloc and a free through UHDecref0. A census of the tree main.c files finds 46 nullary heap-union constructors in 19 files. The design must keep the decref path branch-light: (a) a static object per nullary case with an immortal refcount, where UHDecref0 never frees an object whose refc is above a sentinel bound, so the static's refc starts at INT32_MAX / 2 and increments wrap safely below the sentinel; or (b) the tag lives in the pointer for nullary cases (a tagged-pointer null per case), which changes every switch on x->tag. Rule, written before measuring: pick (a) unless the benchmark shows decref overhead above 2% on a refcount-heavy sample (bench-pair -Native on mega_brzozowski_derivatives runtime_native and native_closure_union_rec, 5 rounds, QuietCores 2); adopt only if allocation-heavy samples speed up by 5% or more and every C row stays bit-identical in exit and stdout. Same question for Zig, which allocates per box.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_2 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 2u32, "2026-10-23T14:58Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:58 main: option (a) drafted, %TEMP%\\pack-next9\\c-nullary-singleton.ps1 (1 anchor in union_tmpl's heap constructor), composed as pack-next9 on next8 (3957189B) -> core 86448170CAD44C75, hopacsota lint G1/G2 pass. A nullary UH case emits 'static UHi x = { .refc = INT32_MAX/2, .tag = t }; return &x;'. No decref sentinel is needed: C refcounts are single-threaded and balanced, so the static's count cannot reach 0; the code read found no refc == 1 reuse path and free only inside UHDecref. Pending (CPU, after tibia's block): fsc --parseonly, then the pre-written rule from the 10:30 note (bench-pair -Native on mega_brzozowski_derivatives and native_closure_union_rec, 5 rounds, QuietCores 2; adopt on >=5% on allocation-heavy samples with every C row bit-identical). Zig question still open.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_3 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 3u32, "2026-10-23T23:42Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:42 main: mirror survey across backends on samples/native_closure_union_rec. Rust already shares nullary cases (a thread_local Rc, cloned), and F#, Lean, Gleam and Python use plain constructors or values. Swift's case is a struct, so no allocation. Kotlin (class UH0_Nil instantiated per use) and Lua (function Uh0_Nil() return { tag = ... } end, a new table per call) allocate per nullary case like C did. Mirror drafted as pack-next10 on next9: %TEMP%\\pack-next10\\nullary-mirrors.ps1 has 4 anchors, each matching once on the tree and next9. Kotlin: object instead of class, and the zero and box sites name the object without (). Lua: the heap case becomes NAME = (function() local shared = {...} return function() return shared end end)(), so no new chunk-level local, staying under Lua 5.1's 200-local limit, and the call sites are unchanged. Lua stack unions (Us) and TypeScript are left as they are: whether anything mutates or compares those tables or objects by identity is unverified. Review rule (next10_rules.py, checked with positive and negative controls): a changed main.kt or main.lua must equal its snapshot with exactly these rewrites applied, no other file changes, and native results and DISAGREE 0 as baseline. Compose and review wait for the next9 review.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_4 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 4u32, "2026-10-23T23:48Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:48 main: Wasm added to the pack-next10 mirror. Its bump allocator never frees, so a nullary case allocated per use (alloc 8, store the tag) was also an unbounded leak in loops. Now each distinct tag gets one 8-byte static data segment [tag][pad], interned like string literals at data_end before heap_start is computed. Objects with the same tag are byte-identical and immutable, so tag alone is the key across unions. Boxing a nullary case returns (i32.const at). The patch has 6 anchors (Kotlin 3, Lua 1, Wasm 2), each matching once on the tree. The patched F# was read on a scratch copy: the arm is if/else with the allocation path under else, and nullary_object sits after intern. The rule for main.wat is that allocation calls must not increase, with the matrix's native check (DISAGREE 0) deciding.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_5 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 5u32, "2026-10-23T23:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:51 main: Delphi added to pack-next10 (7 anchors now). Its nullary heap constructor did Result := TUH0.Create per call, and none of the 412 generated main.pas files frees anything, so every Nil leaked an object. Now: var <case>_shared: TUHn = nil; plus a constructor that creates it on first use and returns it. FPC/Delphi allow an initialized global var between routines. Stack unions (TUS, records) are unchanged. next10_rules.py predicts the main.pas rewrite exactly and was checked on the real sample text (1 file within the rule, 0 violations). Order: next10's review compares against the installed outputs, so it runs only after next9 is reviewed and installed; review.ps1 and compose.ps1 are in %TEMP%\\pack-next10.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_6 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 6u32, "2026-10-24T00:00Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:00 main: TypeScript added to pack-next10 (8 anchors). It was held back on an identity risk, now settled by a code read: TS's EQ lowering (a === b, core 174081) serves primitives only. Union equality reaches codegen as a multi-scrutinee unbox comparing tags (v{a}.tag === v{b}.tag at 173971), so no union object is ever compared by identity. A nullary case now emits const <case>_shared: U = { tag: i }; and the function returns it; the types are readonly already. next10_rules.py predicts the main.ts rewrite and was checked on the real sample (1 file within the rule). Python is skipped on purpose: a fieldless NamedTuple per call is cheap, and Python is the slowest target anyway. Swift's nullary case is a struct (no allocation), and Rust already shares a thread_local Rc.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_7 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 7u32, "2026-10-29T17:52Z", "compiler", "10-29 13:5x compiler: SHIPPED, but the item still showed 0% Planned. Committed C output at HEAD, samples/native_closure_union_rec/main.c:47-50: 'UH0 * UH0_Nil() { static UH0 x = { .refc = 1073741823, .tag = 1 }; return &x; }' (1073741823 = INT32_MAX/2, written as a literal), i.e. option (a) from n1/n2. The next10 mirrors are in the core as well (Kotlin object, Delphi '<case>_shared' first-use global at spiral_compiler.fs:165680, plus the Lua/Wasm/TS rewrites per n3-n6). Bit-identity half of the n1 rule: met by every accepted review since (DISAGREE 0). NOT met: the speed half. No note records the pre-registered bench (bench-pair -Native on mega_brzozowski_derivatives runtime_native and native_closure_union_rec, 5 rounds, QuietCores 2; adopt only at >=5% on allocation-heavy samples). Remaining = that bench, run after the fact: installed core vs the same core with the singleton anchor reverted. If it is under 5%, the decision goes back to main (the Delphi/Wasm halves fix unbounded leaks regardless, so they stay). Progress 90.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_8 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 8u32, "2026-10-29T18:27Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:3x compiler: the pre-registered bench, run after the fact (node, gcc 15.2, matrix C flags -std=c11 -O2 -fwrapv -w; committed HEAD main.c = arm S, the static singleton; arm M = the same file with each nullary constructor rewritten back to the old malloc form, i.e. the pre-change output byte for byte; arm Saa = an A/A copy of S; 15 rotated rounds x 15 runs, verdict = median of per-round ratios). brz runtime_native (6 nullary ctors): S 15.75 ms, A/A 0.924, M/S 1.095. native_closure_union_rec (1 ctor): S 15.81 ms, A/A 1.194, M/S 1.167. Exits and stdout identical in every arm. A first 5-round run under heavier node load gave A/A 1.04/1.19 and M/S 0.78/1.36 (opposite directions). VERDICT: INCONCLUSIVE, not a win or a loss. Both samples the rule names run ~15 ms, mostly process start, so the A/A spread (up to 19%) exceeds any allocation effect and the >=5% threshold cannot be decided on them. What would decide it: an allocation-heavy micro-sample (build and drop millions of list ends) with in-process timing. Not worth a pack slot on its own: the change is correct (every accepted review since, DISAGREE 0) and its Delphi/Wasm halves fix unbounded leaks. Recommendation: keep it shipped, close on correctness, and drop the speed claim from the title's motivation. Main decides. Bench script: compiler scratchpad nullary-bench/bench.ps1.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_9 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 9u32, "2026-10-29T18:33Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:3x compiler: DONE on correctness (main agreed 14:3x). The change shipped (committed C output uses the static singleton; every accepted review since has DISAGREE 0), and the Delphi/Wasm mirrors fix unbounded leaks. The n1 speed claim is withdrawn, not refuted: the two samples the rule names run ~15 ms each, mostly process start, and the A/A spread measured in the same job (0.924 and 1.194) is wider than any >=5% effect could be, so they cannot carry the claim (n8). A future speed claim needs an allocation-heavy sample with in-process timing.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_1 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 1u32, "2026-10-23T16:22Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:22 x: user request 2026-10-23 ~11:58: 'make --per-account have a minimum of 1 instead of 5'. Today cli.spi validates 5..100 (usage line :81 '5..100 (default 5)'; the test at :422 per_account_below_the_api_minimum_is_rejected expects 'rejected: --per-account expects a number from 5 to 100, got 4'), because X's GET /2/users/:id/tweets takes max_results 5..100, and args.spi:38 passes per_account straight through as max_results. DESIGN: (1) cli accepts 1..100, with the usage line and rejection text saying 1 to 100; (2) the request asks for max(5, N) (a wire-side constant named for the API floor); (3) after fetching, each account's posts are cut to its newest N before merging, so --limit and --per-author keep their meaning; (4) tests: the cli test pins 1 accepted and 0 rejected, and a unit test checks the per-account cut (N=1 out of 5 fetched keeps the newest). e2e gets one tutorial step, 'xc timeline --per-account 1 alice bob', showing one post each. COST NOTE for the README line: below 5, X still bills the 5 posts it returns, so --per-account 1 trims what is shown but doesn't cut paid reads (X-READ-BUDGET's cost line would show it). Lands after X-DONE with the other client drafts; the draft and verification go on a scratch copy while the tree is staged.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_2 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 2u32, "2026-10-23T16:30Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:30 x: drafted as %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_per_account_min_1.ps1 (in land_all and apply_all_check: 12 of 12 drafts apply together, no new name clashes). cli: range 1..100, the usage line says 'posts shown per account, 1..100 (default 5; X sends at least 5)', and the test now rejects 0 and accepts 1 ('accounts alice per=1 ...'). wire: requested_posts n = max(timeline_floor () = 5, n) in posts_path, plus a test that n=1 asks for max_results=5. fetch: each primary entry carries 'keep' = N, and refs are taken only from the kept posts, so a smaller N also means fewer reference lookups. digest: kept_posts cuts each account's primary posts to the first N (X and the e2e stub both return newest first; the stub sorts by created_at descending), and a test checks keep '1' -> 1 of 2, '' -> all. Negative control already in hand: today's stub answers 400 to max_results < 5, so without the floor the e2e step would fail. Cells on cli, wire, fetch, digest queued (self-gating chain8).")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_3 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 3u32, "2026-10-23T17:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:27 x: Cells on the scratch copy (%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-permin\\client, x tree + this draft): cli.spi 18/18 PASS, including per_account_takes_one_and_rejects_zero ('0' rejected as 1 to 100, '1' accepted as per=1), and wire.spi 23/23 PASS, including posts_below_the_api_floor_ask_for_five (max_results=5 for N=1). fetch and digest (the keep cut) are next in the same chain.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_4 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 4u32, "2026-10-23T17:36Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:36 x: VERIFIED on the scratch copy: fetch.spi PASS and digest.spi 2/2 PASS (keep_cuts_each_account_to_its_newest: keep '1' gives 1 of 2, '' gives all), alongside cli 18/18 and wire 23/23. Also compiled with all 14 drafts applied together (xc.spi -> 831 lines of Rust, under the 1,000-line gate). Left: landing after the user's commit, then e2e -Accept for the README step (covered by the queued x-landing-trial).")
