inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_7 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_8 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 8u32, "2026-10-26T08:46Z", "x", "2026-10-26 01:4x x: e2e evidence; Done still waits on a fully green verify (the written criterion), since verify9's client build stopped at glyphs and may not have reached cli/wire/digest's tests. Code in HEAD since 6ef9c48 (cli 1..100 with per_account_takes_one, wire floor max_results=5, digest keep cut). The README tutorial step 'xc timeline --per-account 1 alice ''@bob''' matched its expected output (3 requests) in node verify job-1792988648856-x-verify-remote9, among 36/36 tutorial steps; that job's exit 1 came from the node's stale spiral runner (glyphs rust -d cells, oauth1 thread_run), not x.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_9 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 9u32, "2026-10-27T00:22Z", "x", "2026-10-26 20:22 x: full x verify pre-staged for when main's Zig index_of fix lands (main-serial-zig-index-of-5, gate step running in slot 1 since 18:44; main will message on land). The script is %TEMP%\\x-verify-full\\verify11.ps1, the same shape as node verify10 (mix deps.get, then apps/client/build.ps1 and apps/sim/build.ps1), plus SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL pinned to the hash-verified 10/26 08:19 compiler and its hash printed first. On the land: sync spiral and x to mirror xv11, start it, and close X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1, X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE and X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT on exit 0 (actions-lock runs locally, since the node verifies skip it). If main installs 5n first, re-stage the pinned compiler from the new install.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_10 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 10u32, "2026-10-27T00:52Z", "x", "2026-10-26 20:52 x: main's Zig index_of gate (main-gate-zig-index-of-2) ended RED at 20:23 and the land was refused. Per main, that is the box failing to run tests, not the slice: the unchanged baseline ran 0 of 60 and free memory fell to 768 MB. It is requeued as main-serial-zig-index-of-6, after kit's rename finalize. This item and X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE and X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT stay blocked on it. verify11.ps1 (note 9) starts on main's land message.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_11 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 11u32, "2026-10-27T07:20Z", "x", "2026-10-27 03:20 x: UNBLOCKED. Main's Zig index_of fix LANDED in lib, unstaged (chain main-serial-zig-index-of-6, gate and land exit 0 under the fixed runner). The whole-repo verify is ready but NOT started: it arrived after the standing autonomous window ended (10-26 21:00), so it waits for the next session. To run: sync spiral and x to node mirror xv11, push %TEMP%\\x-verify-full\\verify11.ps1 to C:\\home\\remote-stage\\xv11, start it (compiler pinned; re-stage the pinned DLL if 5n is installed by then), and run actions-lock locally. On exit 0, close this item, X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE and X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_12 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 12u32, "2026-10-28T10:43Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:39 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_in_host_1 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 1u32, "2026-10-23T16:30Z", "eoie", "12:30 eoie, from the user's 12:2x question ('a shit ton of eoie processes, is our product stable?'). Census 12:25 (scratch eoie-process-census.ps1): 26 eoie processes = 2 hosts (the pre-install one draining) + 18 supervisors + 6 job waits; 12 of the 18 supervisors serve QUEUED jobs (main's 9-job serial chain, my GE2 chain, a raw gate run, a typecheck), each polling its after file every 250 ms or its gate every 5 s. Measured over 10 s: all 25 used 672 ms CPU (0.84% of the box) and 161 MB. No leak: every waiter and supervisor targets a live job, every after-chain points at a live job, and the only dead parent is the draining host. Design: the host already runs; it can hold the queue (after-waits and gate polls in one loop) and spawn a job's supervisor at grant, so idle queued jobs cost no process. Rule before building: adopt it if a 12-job queue drops eoie processes by 12 and CPU by at least half, with the stop_cli, after and windows CLI tests unchanged.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_in_host_2 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 2u32, "2026-10-23T17:05Z", "eoie", "13:05 eoie: decision model drafted and passing (scratch f6/src/eoie_job_queue, HostFree, LibReferenced): a queued_job (stop request, NoChain or Chained predecessor, mode, predecessor state) maps to KeepWaiting | StartSupervisor | SettleStopped | SettleSkipped detail, with the same rules and skip detail as eoie_job_after's after_outcome_of today ('--after-ok: <id> ended exit N'). The host's loop would fold this over its queued job files every beat and spawn a supervisor only on StartSupervisor; a serial chain behind a running job costs 0 processes (test). Gate waits (slot and window) stay in the supervisor for now, since they need a live process holding the ticket. Next: the host loop (eoie_job_host host_run), after release M.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_in_host_3 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 3u32, "2026-10-23T17:12Z", "eoie", "13:12 eoie: host design, read from eoie_job_host/host_run.spi (host_served drains request files and starts a supervisor per request; supervisor_requested waits 5 s for the supervisor pid, then falls back to a detached start). (1) On a request whose job has an after file, the host evaluates eoie_job_queue's step_of from the job files: KeepWaiting writes shell-host/held/<id> and starts nothing; StartSupervisor starts the supervisor as today; SettleStopped and SettleSkipped write the job's exit line exactly as eoie_job_after does (143 stopped, 125 skipped with the same detail). (2) Each 500 ms beat the host re-evaluates only shell-host/held/ (no scan of the whole jobs root), so the held set lives in files and survives a host restart or upgrade; the host is not idle while held/ is non-empty. (3) supervisor_requested treats a held marker as taken and returns 'held', so job start prints supervisor_pid=held and never falls back to a detached supervisor for a held job. (4) job stop needs no change: its stop file is read by step_of. eoie_job_after stays for the fallback path and older hosts. Tests: a three-job serial chain shows one supervisor process at a time; stop of a held job settles 143; --after-ok skip detail unchanged; a host restart re-holds from held/. Risk: host_run is what HP fixed today, so this lands only after GE2's acceptance and with HP's 6 checks rerun.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_in_host_4 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 4u32, "2026-10-23T20:25Z", "eoie", "16:25 eoie: host loop drafted and typechecked; it ships as release P (after O, before N), its own release because it changes the host every job depends on. Slice: scratch queued-in-host/apply-queued-in-host.ps1 + blocks/queue.spi. eoie_job_host gains queue: step_of over a queued_job read from the job files (after = predecessor TAB mode; predecessor state via job_state_now, so a missing predecessor reads as lost, exactly like eoie_job_after). host_served: a request whose predecessor is live is recorded as <jobs>/held/<id> (its executable) instead of starting a supervisor. Each 500 ms beat, held_served re-evaluates held/: KeepWaiting writes the job's heartbeat_ms, which keeps readers from declaring it lost after 15 s; StartSupervisor starts it; a stop writes 143 and an --after-ok on a failed predecessor writes 125, with eoie_job_after's exact details. Held jobs count as live, so the host does not idle-exit. supervisor_awaited returns SupervisorHeld 'held' (or 'settled'), so job start prints supervisor_pid=held. Notebooks on the post-O tree pass (16:13 and 16:17): queue (4 model tests plus a read-only IO probe), host_run (probe reaching host_run and supervisor_requested), host_model. after-install-P on today's eoie: the held check fails as it must (supervisor_pid=131976, 1 supervisor); run-after-head, skip detail ('--after-ok: <id> ended finished exit 3'), stop 143 ('stopped on request while waiting for <id>') and HP 6/6 pass, and P must keep them passing. Today's details match the model's strings exactly. The train order is now GE2, HIJK, L, M, O, P, N.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_in_host_5 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 5u32, "2026-10-23T21:05Z", "eoie", "17:05 eoie: review of P against every verb that touches a queued job, read in the post-O tree. job stop writes the stop file and waits 15 s for a settle (eoie_job_control control.spi:47-53), and the host settles a held job within one 500 ms beat, so it works unchanged. job amend refuses only once a pid file exists, and a held job has none, so the new limit is read when its supervisor starts. job after (M) rewrites the after file, which the host re-reads every beat. job wait and status read state, and the beaten heartbeat keeps the job queued. Known risk, accepted for P with a follow-up before P installs: if the host process dies while jobs are held, nobody beats them and the first reader marks them lost after 15 s, whereas today each queued job's own supervisor would outlive a host crash. Follow-up slice: job status, list and wait call the existing host-start path (supervisor_requested's HostMissing branch) when held/ is non-empty and host.pid is dead, so a reader revives the queue instead of burying it. P's acceptance gains a probe: kill the isolated-home host with a held job, run job status, and the job must still finish.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_in_host_6 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 6u32, "2026-10-23T21:07Z", "eoie", "17:07 eoie: enforced mechanically: launch-P refuses until queue.spi carries the revival follow-up (marker held_revived), and P moved to the end of the train (GE2, HIJK, L, M, O, N, P; N rebases on O again, P on N), so P's refusal ends the train after N instead of stranding N. P can't reach install tonight in any case (GE2's regen alone runs past 18:00).")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_in_host_7 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 7u32, "2026-10-23T21:26Z", "eoie", "17:26 eoie: the follow-up from note 5 is now in P's slice, so launch-P's gate (held_revived) passes. (1) A held job is never declared lost by silence: in job_state_now's lost branch, a job with a held/<id> file reads Live JobPending, since it has no process that could be lost. This removes the 15 s race in which a reader would bury a held job before a revived host beats it. (2) eoie_job_host exports eoie_job_host_revive (held_revived: idle | running | revived | error). It starts a host through the existing detached path when held/ is non-empty and host.pid is dead, and job status and job wait call it first (eoie_jobs already depends on the eoie-job-host crate, so no cycle). Checks on a tree rebuilt as post-M plus O plus P (17:12 and 17:23): host_run and queue notebooks pass, and the jobs.spi probe reaches job_status and job_waited with only the standalone link error (eoie_job_host crate). after-install-P gains 'held: survives a host crash': kill the isolated home's host while a job is held, wait 20 s (past the old silence limit), run job status, and the job must finish exit 0. A slip caught in my own slice: a here-string without its trailing newline merged two statements into one line (it parsed, but the second swap never ran). Found by checking the dry-run output, not the parse.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_in_host_8 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 8u32, "2026-10-24T09:20Z", "eoie", "05:1x eoie, user asked again ('damn we have a lot of eoie processes up, is this drift or intended?'), the second time after 10-23 12:2x. Census: 61 eoie processes = 1 host + 56 supervisors + 3 caller waiters + 1 window run. 8 jobs run; 52 are queued, 34 on --after (release P's held queue removes these) and 18 on a slot or window gate (P keeps those in the supervisor). Each idle supervisor costs ~7 MB plus a ~7.5 MB conhost (stage R removes the conhost): ~800 MB in all on a memory-tight box. Priority raised P2 -> P0 because the user repeated the request; the gate-wait remainder is EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_in_host_9 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:22Z", "eoie", "07:2x groom: estimate set from what remains, not from the item text. Release P carries the held queue in tonight's train, behind GE3 -> HIJK -> L -> M -> O -> N -> Q. Left: P's install and its after-install check (a queued --after job shows no supervisor; job stop, amend and after on a held job keep their receipts), about 30 min of check time once P lands.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_in_host_10 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 10u32, "2026-10-24T11:23Z", "eoie", "07:23 groom: blocked on the train. The only work left is to install release P and run its after-install check, so 'agile next' must not hand this out as ready work. Back to Active when release P installs.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_in_host_11 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 11u32, "2026-10-30T14:11Z", "kit", "10-30 10:1x kit: after-install check PASSED on the installed kit C528BE4D. A queued --after job is held by the host (supervisor_pid=held, 0 kit processes for it); job amend and job stop on the held job both return receipts. This was the only remaining work (07:2x groom note), so the item closes.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_in_host_12 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 12u32, "2026-11-06T11:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_doctor_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS-DOCTOR", 1u32, "2026-10-23T16:30Z", "eoie", "12:30 eoie: today's checks were hand-rolled scripts (scratch eoie-process-census.ps1 for the user's 12:2x stability question; the detached 'window check' and 'cmd /c set' probes that found the EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME leak at 10:4x). By the generic-tools rule each method must be an eoie command, not a scratch script. Typed findings: DeadParent pid | SettledButAlive job | WaitOnSettled job | ChainToMissing job | OrphanWindow id | HostEnvDiffers names (the detached environment probe; today it would have shown EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME, CARGO_BUILD_JOBS and a missing EOIE_LEASE_POLICY). The census today: 0 findings except the draining host. Spec examples: today's 10:4x leak (HostEnvDiffers EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME) and 12:25 census.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_doctor_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS-DOCTOR", 2u32, "2026-10-23T16:38Z", "eoie", "12:38 eoie: pure model drafted and passing (scratch f6/src/eoie_jobs_doctor, HostFree, LibReferenced). role_of reads a command line into JobHost | JobSupervisor job | JobWaiter job | WindowHolder | OtherCall. process_findings: a dead-parent host counts only when it serves no job (IdleOrphanHost), because a host draining after an install is by design; a supervisor of a settled job is SettledButSupervised, and a waiter on a settled or missing job is WaitOnSettled/WaitOnMissing. chain_findings: ChainToMissing. drift compares a detached job's environment with the caller's over the steering prefixes (EOIE_, SPIRAL_, CARGO_, RUSTUP_; EOIE_JOB_* excepted) and reads today's leak as +EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME +CARGO_BUILD_JOBS -EOIE_LEASE_POLICY. Tests: the 12:25 census (draining host, no finding), an idle orphan host, stale waiters, roles, chains, drift. Next: the verb crate, with snapshots through eoie_machine_watch's sysinfo rows, job state from the job files, and the detached env probe as a 'cmd /c set' job.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_doctor_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS-DOCTOR", 3u32, "2026-10-23T16:59Z", "eoie", "12:59 eoie: verb drafted as 'eoie job doctor [--env]' (scratch job-doctor/apply-job-doctor.ps1; crate eoie_jobs_doctor: model + check + entry). check.spi reads the eoie processes through eoie_machine_watch's sysinfo snapshot (role and parent liveness), job standing from the job files, and the after-chains from every job folder. --env runs the same 'cmd /c set' (or 'env') attached and as a detached job and diffs the variable names, which avoids parsing the NUL-separated snapshot. Notebooks: model and check pass on f6 with the real legacy packages copied in. The slice's anchors are order-independent with job-rechain (both orders tried: union tail JobStop, JobAfter, JobChain, JobDoctor, JobUnknown); job-rechain's case anchor was made order-independent for this. In release M: 8 candidate notebooks, baseline merge, and acceptance 'job doctor --env' must print ok, which proves the env-leak class fixed end to end once GE2's caller environment is in.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_doctor_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS-DOCTOR", 4u32, "2026-10-23T17:25Z", "eoie", "13:25 eoie: companion slice from the same question (scratch waiting-for/apply-waiting-for.ps1, in release M): job status/list/wait print waiting_for=after:<id> | window:<class> | heavy-slot after state=queued. A queued job no longer looks the same whether it is stuck or correctly waiting on its predecessor. gate_queued became a typed gate_wait union over the same job files (lib sm' calls instead of rust_std_string); the stop_cli after test asserts the predecessor. eoie_jobs model notebook and the doctor's check notebook pass with it; all four M slices applied together on a src copy (union and routes consistent). after-install-M asserts 'state=queued waiting_for=after:<slow id>' for a chained job.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_doctor_5 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS-DOCTOR", 5u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): TOOL-4 (helper and daemon lifecycle: stale compiler servers reaped by identity, with a leak receipt). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_doctor_6 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS-DOCTOR", 6u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "eoie", "23:06 groom: add the unwatched-jobs check from the 22:14-22:45 audit (EOIE-JOBS note 49). A live job is unwatched when no job wait/run process waits on it or on any successor reachable through after links. Print the sample time. Also flag supervisors whose conhost exists (until EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE ships) and hosts anchored outside their owner session (until EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT).")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_doctor_7 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS-DOCTOR", 7u32, "2026-10-24T05:59Z", "eoie", "01:59 case from main (01:5x): a Spiral compiler server (dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --server-tcp ...spiral-cells-<erlpid>-<n>.sock --stall-seconds 120) outlived its parent and its erl. The pid in the socket name no longer existed, and it sat at 0 CPU for 25+ min despite --stall-seconds 120 (it later exited by itself). Doctor should flag compiler servers whose socket's erl pid is dead and that have idled past their own stall limit, suggest 'quit' on the socket, and attribute them by start time against the job registry.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_doctor_8 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS-DOCTOR", 8u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 60m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 50%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_doctor_9 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS-DOCTOR", 9u32, "2026-10-27T23:20Z", "eoie", "19:2x eoie: audit of the live doctor against the title. 'kit job doctor' ships, and today it reads 'ok processes=6 findings=0'. It covers idle orphan hosts, settled-but-supervised, waits on settled or missing jobs, chains to missing jobs, and --env drift. Window rows of dead holders don't need a doctor finding: kit_machine_windows prunes them on every window operation (live_rows -> orphaned_window: a ProcessHolder whose pid in the row id is dead gets WindowPruned). So the open gap is note 7's orphaned Cells compiler servers. Draft: scratchpad clean\\jobs-doctor\\orphan-servers.do, 7/7 on package copies. The model gains server_fact {pid, owner, owner_alive} and the finding OrphanCompilerServer (pid, owner). serves_cells means a command with --server-tcp and spiral-cells-; cells_owner_of reads the erl pid from the socket name spiral-cells-<erlpid>-<n>.sock. server_findings ignores an owner that doesn't parse. The finding line is 'orphan-compiler-server pid=.. owner=.. (the erl named in its socket is gone; send quit on the socket or stop the pid)'. It has a rust test block. check.spi scans every process row, not only kit*. Node compile (job-1793143001848-kit-doctor-regen, box DLL 4FD712E9E357): it typechecks, but kit_jobs_doctor goes from 887 to 1091 generated lines, over the 1,000-line gate. The growth comes from the inlined word_after, split and try_convert (servers_from is 89 lines) plus the new finding case. The control crates are byte-identical. Next: split the server scan into its own crate (hand-modeled unit, kit_jobs_doctor_servers exporting the findings text), so the doctor crate only calls it; or parse the owner with a lighter lib primitive. Both go in the release after TRAIN.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_doctor_10 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS-DOCTOR", 10u32, "2026-10-27T23:35Z", "eoie", "19:3x eoie: split done and proven on the node (job-1793143757656, box DLL 4FD712E9E357). The new crate kit_jobs_doctor_servers (HostFree + LibReferenced standards; packages spiral-, kit_machine_watch, kit_jobs_doctor_model; sysinfo dep) holds the process scan and exports kit_job_doctor_server_report : () -> i32 * string. doctor_run takes it the way it takes env_drift, prints the lines, and counts them in findings=. Generated sizes: kit_jobs_doctor 887 -> 917, kit_jobs_doctor_servers 502, kit_jobs_doctor_env byte-identical. The model keeps server_fact, OrphanCompilerServer, serves_cells, cells_owner_of, server_findings and the rust test block. Land as: scratchpad clean\\jobs-doctor\\stage (manifest.tsv, 5 new files) plus orphan-servers.do (11 edits: model, check, entry, the doctor's Cargo dep, the workspace member). Release after TRAIN, then run 'kit job doctor' against a real orphaned server: start Cells, kill its erl, expect orphan-compiler-server.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_doctor_11 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS-DOCTOR", 11u32, "2026-10-29T15:38Z", "kit", "10-29 11:3x kit: shipped in TRAIN3 (kit.exe CD2F0D1A, 11:35) with the kit_jobs_doctor_servers crate; live: 'kit job doctor' -> 'ok processes=9 findings=0'. That is a smoke check (the old binary also printed ok), and the crate's own notebooks passed in the candidate run (kit_jobs_doctor_servers/servers.spi, kit_jobs_doctor/check.spi). Done.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_doctor_12 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS-DOCTOR", 12u32, "2026-11-06T11:27Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_1 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T18:21Z", "x", "2026-10-23 14:21 x: decision on eoie's question (EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS note 4 follow-up): x declares NO LineWidth override. 120 is the formatter's own limit (memory formatter-keep-breaks: it re-lays only what exceeds 120), so x's 136 over-wide client lines are unformatted code, not a different standard. ORDER: after land_all.ps1 -Go, because the 14 drafts are anchored on today's exact text and formatting first would break their anchors (the reformat-peer-repos lesson, applied to my own patch chain). Steps: SpiralCompiler format on each .spi (copy first, read 3 files by hand), byte-compare the generated Rust and Gleam before and after (tibia did that for 15 outputs), then Cells and eoie standards (expect 0 line-width findings, and 0 test-targets findings once eoie's release M ships).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_2 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T00:43Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:43 x: scope update from the 10-23 evening drafts, so the formatting pass covers everything that lands. Post-land scripts touch apps/client args/args.spi, xc/xc.spi, write/write.spi (+ README), wire.spi, thread/thread.spi, paging.spi, prelude.spi. Lib-dependent delegations touch the same files. apply_sim_modules adds 17 sim modules (inbox ... xsim_shell_entry) and apply_xsim_crate adds xsim/xsim.spi. Order: format only after all of them land and pass, then x-compat (XC005) on the formatted cells, because re-laying can move cell weights. Rules from memory formatter-keep-breaks: never join authored lines, re-lay only lines over 120, and apply to unstaged files only.")
inl note_of_x_format_client_3 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 3u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "x", "23:08 x grooming: main installed a formatter-only compiler pack at 22:18 (B553EF6B78E6EE29, single-flight): no parens around join heads, 'then' placement follows each file's habit, deep-indent room max 40. Run this item with that compiler (or newer), and compare against a format of the same tree with it, not against earlier 10-23 formats. Still waits for the user to land x's queued drafts.")
inl note_of_x_format_client_4 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 4u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "x", "10-24 19:45 x: grooming: the condition 'after the drafts land' is MET (HEAD 6ef9c48, user commit 05:20). What's left is the unstaged fix layer: x-compat fixes, chain_json, e2e expectations, plural, and the frames imports. Start when verify-landing5 passes and the user commits that layer. Its anchors are the last chain this pass could break. Use main's newest formatter pack (n3).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_5 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 5u32, "2026-10-25T03:53Z", "x", "10-24 23:53 x: measured input for this item. The installed formatter (10:08 build) on x's edition-3 text changes 40 of 44 files and makes 28 cells overweight, mostly records exploded to one field per line and long signatures re-laid with deep indentation. So the format pass here must come with per-cell splits until x-compat and postable are both 0, plus a check that no authored line is joined (0 files lost lines in the measured run).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_6 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 6u32, "2026-10-25T23:41Z", "x", "2026-10-25 19:41 -04:00 (written earlier under the eoie lease) x: MEASURED on a copy (scratch x-simland-0214\\format_measure.ps1; installed 5l'' formatter over apps/client and the new apps/sim/spi). Before: client 35 files / 4,619 lines, 133 lines over 120, 0 cells over one post, x-compat 0; sim/spi 23 files / 4,550 lines, 65 over 120, 0 spilled, x-compat 0. The formatter changes 51 of 58 files. After: client 4,991 lines, 1 over 120, 21 cells over one post (x-compat 21 overweight); sim/spi 4,752 lines, 0 over 120, 8 spilled (x-compat 8). So formatting fixes 197 long lines and creates 29 overweight definitions in 17 files: wire 5, glyphs 3, fields 2, posts 2, assemble 2, personas 2, scenario 2, world 2, and 1 each in api, digest, fetch, locate, refs, token, xc, runner_shell, schedule. Plan, by the formatter-keeps-breaks rule (re-lay only >120): (1) land the formatter result file by file; (2) split each of the 29 by hand into named helpers, as the batch dry-run patch did, gated on x-compat 0 + dry-run 0 spilled + --check per package; (3) glyphs.spi waits for main's job-1792944559767 (it lands the glyphs heal), and the sim/spi files go last, after the parity landing. Size: 29 splits at ~5-10 min each, 3-5 h, light load. || 2026-10-25 17:19 -04:00 x: classified the 29 (scratch classify_overweight.ps1): 5 are RECORD LAYOUT (fields command_fields + filter_fields, assemble.frame_of, posts.at, personas.acted_in), where the formatter puts one field per line (EDITION3-280-SPLIT n2). Those need a formatter record-fill layout (fields filled within 120 columns), which is compiler work x now owns, not hand splits. 24 are code: 16 split by hand on formatted copies (%TEMP%\\fmt-bisect client, %TEMP%\\fmt-measure sim), each with a named helper: wire x5 (post_extras, included_posts, edit_history_in, root_fields, ghost_error), api classified_export, assemble frame_members, digest primary_entry, fetch fetch_filters, locate frame_members, posts loud_posts, refs refs_of + refs_args (the generated Rust is byte-identical, pub fn xc_refs unchanged), token with_tokens, xc credentials_hint, and in sim personas fresh_cast, runner_shell withdrawn_demand, scenario rooted_post + merged_posts, schedule corpus_pacing, world metrics_pair + child_cell. Every split file: x-compat clean except its record-layout def, 0 lines over 120, --check exit 0 (one wrong guess, ghost_error typed json instead of string, was caught by --check and bisected). The 3 glyphs defs wait for job-1792944559767, and gleam_weight goes away with that heal. Tooling gap seen: '--check' rejection prints only 'Package typecheck rejected' with no error, so finding the failing definition took a per-edit bisect; worth an eoie or compiler item (print the first type errors). Landing: after node verify5b, copy the 46 formatted files that carry no record-layout def into the tree and verify again; fields, assemble, posts, personas and glyphs wait for the record-fill layout. || 2026-10-25 17:45 -04:00 x: RECORD FILL drafted and measured (scratchpad\\record-fill\\apply.ps1, compiler formatter). layoutFields packs consecutive simple fields onto one line, separated by '; ', within the width; a field the author started on a new line still starts one (authoredBreaks), so no authored line is joined; fields whose body needs its own layout keep their lines; the formatter's parse guard still falls back to as-written if a layout changes meaning. Proof 2 on the node (job-1792964325355, candidate = tree core + patch, installed vs candidate on the same x copy): overweight client 28 -> 22, sim 8 -> 5, only 12 files differ. Fixed: assemble.frame_of, fields.filter_fields, posts.at, personas.acted_in. NOT fixed: fields.command_fields, a record TYPE: type definitions go through a separate path (3 sites near spiral_compiler.fs:181804, 181811, 181891) that maps each field to its own line. The patch now routes those 3 through layoutFields as well; proof 3 = job-1792964722843. With all 5 records fixed, the 16 hand splits plus the record fill leave only glyphs (main's landing) before x formats cleanly. || 2026-10-25 17:57 -04:00 x: DECIDED by main on FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS (rules on record, user 10-20 n1): record fill as a default is rejected (the user corpus template is one field per line at +4, no ';', 224/0), and record TYPES are one field per line by design. Record VALUES keep their authored breaks. So x fixes it at the source: the 4 over-120 record values (fields.filter_fields, assemble.frame_of, posts.at, personas.acted_in) are re-authored as 2 lines within 120 (scratch rewrap.ps1, applied to the tree; x-compat 0 in client and sim/spi). The installed formatter keeps those lines; it only normalizes brace spacing and the +4 continuation, and none is overweight after formatting. Left: fields.command_fields, a 15-field record TYPE, 376 > 273 after formatting by design. It needs a structural change: split it into two record types or nest a sub-record (it carries the CLI parse fields; check its consumers: fields.spi helpers, args, the xc_args export). That's its own small design step before fields.spi can be formatted. The record-fill patch stays as evidence only (4 of 5 fit). || 2026-10-25 18:07 -04:00 x: command_fields SPLIT, applied to the tree (scratch cfsplit.ps1): command_fields = {kind; payload; selection : selection_fields; window : window_fields}, with selection_fields (following, usernames, max_results, limit, per_author, cap) and window_fields (since, until, no_replies, no_reposts, depth, hints, json), written in the canonical one-field-per-line form; blank, source_fields, counts_fields, filter_fields and output_fields go through per-sub-record helpers (record updates on a bound variable); 2 args tests now read f.selection.usernames, f.window.since and f.window.no_replies/no_reposts. Because Spiral exports flatten a record into its leaves in declaration order, the boundary is unchanged: pub fn xc_parse_os_args and xc_timeline_fields keep the same flat 15-tuple (byte-identical signatures), and the xc crate's generated Rust is byte-identical. Checked: --check exit 0; fields.spi 0 lines over 120; formatted with the installed formatter, 0 overweight and 0 long lines; x-compat client 0. So all 5 record definitions are now within 280 after formatting. Landing the format: re-run the formatter on the tree files, then apply the 16 code splits as anchored edits against the formatted text (the scratch copies are older than the tree for fields, posts, assemble and personas). || 2026-10-25 19:37 -04:00 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x (unstaged): the batch dry-run patch (POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS M1) and the formatting of 57 files (installed 5l'' formatter, glyphs.spi skipped) plus 23 anchored hand splits (scratch format_land.ps1, rehearsed stacked twice on copies). After landing: x-compat 0 in apps/client (35 files) and apps/sim/spi (23); --check exit 0 for xc and scenario_checks; the only lines over 120 are glyphs.spi's 9. verify7b on the node (pre-format tree) showed e2e 36/36 + 5 extra after accepting the one stale tutorial output (xc thread 3000001, the respelled prelude), postable 1301 posts / 35 files / 0 spilled, and the client notebooks green up to protocol.spi's known zig failure (LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF). The README accept is copied into the tree (3 lines, the respelled prelude in the thread output). The formatting changes prelude/posts text again, which the sim worlds embed, so verify8 (job-1792971438120) runs the sim build -Accept, then e2e -Accept, then both full builds; the accepted fixture and README diffs come back for review before they enter the tree. Node gaps reported to kit2 and fixed on the way: rename-folded moves, empty dirs, mirror deps, the stale compiler; the node's spiral rust runner is missing, so Cells rust-with-deps cells are 'unsupported' there (oauth1 thread_run).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_7 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 7u32, "2026-10-26T04:23Z", "x", "2026-10-26 00:23 -04:00 x: verify8 on the node (job-1792971438120, after the format + batch landing): sim -Accept regenerated 11 fixtures; e2e -Accept updated 3 of 36 tutorial outputs (the new 'post-thread --dry-run FILE...' usage line in xc --help and in the --per-account usage refusal, plus the thread output of the formatted prelude); then e2e 36/36 + 5 extra; postable 1325 posts / 35 files / 0 spilled; x-compat 0. Reviewed and copied into the tree: the README (only those 3 tutorial blocks beyond my earlier move edits) and the fixtures, which are the formatted sources as the sim renders them (the sim-small program matches the formatted prelude line for line after the notebook header rewrite). Two failures, both fixed or explained. (1) mix test: ':xsim_frames.split_cell/3 is undefined'. The formatter wrapped !!!!Export( so the name sits on the next line, and scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 found exports with a per-line regex, so it dropped split_cell from xsim_frames' kept surface (14 of 15). FIXED: it matches '!!!!Export\\(\\s*\"(\\w+)\"' over the whole file (15 of 15); nothing else in x or lib parses exports per line. (2) Cells glyphs rust -d cells: the node's spiral runner rejected --rs-path (stale 2025-03-13 build). It is being rebuilt as node upkeep (job-1792988618221), with verify9 chained after it (job-1792988648856: full client + sim builds in a fresh mirror). Expected result: green up to protocol.spi's known zig failure (LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_8 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 8u32, "2026-10-26T09:52Z", "x", "2026-10-26 05:52 x: glyphs.spi, the last file, FORMATTED and landed unstaged now that its heal is live (X-CELLS-UNICODE, 05:27): installed formatter, then glyphs_split.ps1 (the two weight-table tests become lists mapped through glyph_weight). Checks on a client copy: 0 lines over 120, x-compat 0 findings, compiles on Rust and Gleam. All x .spi (apps/client + apps/sim/spi) now have 0 lines over 120. Backup: scratch x-simland-0214\\glyphs-before-format-20261026-055150.spi. Done when the next full verify runs glyphs' 8 notebook tests green.")
inl note_of_x_format_client_9 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 9u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:29 x groom: DONE by its own condition (the next full verify runs glyphs' notebook tests green): verify24 (job-1793167893768) printed notebook glyphs passed. Since then glyphs.spi changed only its header timeout.")
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_1 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T19:38Z", "main", "2026-10-23 15:38 main: filed from eoie's report with its repro (session scratch fc1568a6\\f6\\src\\fold_capture_repro\\repro.spi: two rust -d regex tests, named_group/literal_group, both E0507). Hypothesis to test first: the seed '' and the '' inside the closure share one CSE'd variable, which the FnMut closure captures and then moves into optionm'.default_value' instead of cloning. Bisect against the cores installed since 10-22 13:50 (pack-after-night 25D2BE7C is the tree core now); the fix gets the one regression sample (the literal_group shape).")
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_2 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T21:35Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:35 main: narrowing by code read (no build): (1) the E0507 closure is not a Spiral closure. codegenRust emits those as Rc<dyn Fn> with captured variables kept live (closure: live_binds (fun i -> captured.Contains i)), so the FnMut must be the Rust closure written inside lib am'.vec_fold''s macro arm, where a spliced variable is opaque to liveness. (2) live_binds itself is old (git log -S: 76f3989c, 10-06), so it is not the regression. The window is 10-22 13:50 to now, i.e. the pack-after-night install (tree core 25D2BE7C, now 19F5F752 with only the formatter changed since). Bisect next round: compile eoie's repro with the next3 and pack-after-night candidate DLLs (both still in spiral-bin/candidates) and diff the vec_fold' call site's Rust; a plain listm.fold version of the shape compiles fine (scratch fnmut probe), so the macro arm is the place.")
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_3 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T06:23Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:2x main: ROOT CAUSE found, and it is lib, not the compiler. A probe package with eoie's literal_group shape, compiled to Rust by night-release (10-21), pack-after-night and the installed compiler, gives byte-identical output (scratch fnmut-bisect), so no compiler change in the window caused it. The generated fold closure reads `let mut v14: Rc<str> = v13.unwrap_or(v3);`: v3 is the fold's seed literal (the inner \"\" default is the same CSE'd value), captured by the FnMut closure, and lib optionm'.default_value''s Rust arm `!x.unwrap_or(!value)` moves it (E0507). Note 1's CSE hypothesis holds. Fix (%TEMP%\\lib-default-clone\\apply.ps1, dry run clean): the Rust arm becomes `!x.unwrap_or_else(|| !value.clone())` (a lazy clone; every Spiral Rust value is Clone), plus the regression test default_value_inside_a_fold_closure_keeps_the_captured_seed in sm'.spi (rust -d regex, eoie's shape). Gate and land after tibia's PR-27 block. It likely surfaced on 10-22 because a lib change began CSE-sharing the literal, not because the arm changed.")
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_4 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 4u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_5 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 5u32, "2026-10-31T20:02Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:01 compiler GROOM (user direction via /btw, 10-31 15:4x): the self-hosted rewrite (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) starts only after EVERY backend is SOTA: no memory problems, no Rc slowdowns, no union hacks, no recursion limits, no missing arithmetic; GADT bugs and conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'; edition 3 finishes first; Gleam is a top test target (abyssal codebases) with F#, Rust and Wasm; Cells as fast as F# projects. This item is now on that gate (priority -> P0, status -> Active).")
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_6 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 6u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): NEXT STEP: one probe on the installed pack-11 core (seconds) to see whether E0507 still reproduces; if so, the edit is: inside an FnMut body, a captured variable that's moved gets .clone() (or the seed literal is rebuilt per call). That's a codegenRust text edit, drafted while the probe result is pending.")
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_7 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 7u32, "2026-11-01T18:51Z", "compiler", "11-01 14:55 compiler: DONE (evidence from the tree, then a run). The root cause from main's n3 (lib optionm'.default_value' moving the captured seed into unwrap_or) is fixed in lib: the Rust arm clones first (`:= value = rust.clone value` then `!x.unwrap_or(!value)`; committed in HEAD, and today's respell layer spells it rust::clone). The regression test `default_value_inside_a_fold_closure_keeps_the_captured_seed` (sm'.spi, ///> rust -d regex; eoie's literal_group shape, a vec_fold' closure whose seed \"\" is also the default) is in every node mirror. RUN: today's lib sweep on node mirror t1101, control arm (job-1793540254707-compiler-libord-seq, seq-control.tsv): sm'.spi 73 discovered / 73 passed / 0 failed, optionm'.spi 9/9. The E0507 shape compiles and passes on Rust. The n4 'codegenRust clone in FnMut bodies' edit is NOT needed: the move came from a lib macro arm, which codegen cannot see into, so the lib-side clone is the right layer. Closing.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_1 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 1u32, "2026-10-23T22:19Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:19 main: the Cells live test 'a print with no value still runs' fails on the tree and in every pack (CELLS-PACK-NEXT notes 11, 12, 16): rustc rejects SpiralKinoShow(&$0). Root of the literal: inlineRustEmits (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs:163964 splitArgs) maps an args text of '()' to no arguments, and the $k evaluator (164012) keeps m.Value when k >= args.Length, so a unit argument leaves $0 as written. Fix A (compiler): splitArgs returns ['()'] for '()', so $0 becomes () (valid Rust; other $k unchanged). Fix A alone does not pass the test: Cells' split_output (apps/cells/spi/cell.spi:226) would read 'SPIRAL_CELLS_VALUE:()' as a value, and the test expects none. Open question B: why does a unit cell reach show_value_main at all? cells.ex compile/12 tries Cell.unit_result? (append 0i32) before value_result? (show_value_main), and unit_pair accepts Got () / Expected i32 with padding skipped. So either the error text no longer matches (restore_type_lines?), or the wrap already emits a show_value main. Rule: B is decided by one probe run after tibia's block (that cell's compile output with attempt numbers). If unit_result? misses, fix its match. In both cases also land A, since any emit snippet given a unit argument hits it.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_2 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 2u32, "2026-10-23T22:24Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:24 main: Fix A staged in pack-next9. Patch: %TEMP%\\pack-next9\\rust-emit-unit-arg.ps1 (splitArgs '()' -> ['()']; anchors once on both the tree and next8). compose.ps1 runs it after gadt-index-cap, and next9 now composes to 5124A93187EEF889 (lint G1/G2 clean). Regression sample samples/rust_emit_unit_arg is staged in %TEMP%\\pack-next9\\samples, not in the tree, so review-3 (next4 composite, no fix) never sees it. next9's review.ps1 copies it in after the snapshot (remove it if next9 is rejected) and prints its row: Rust must pass with 42, i.e. a unit's size_of_val is 0. next9_rules.py now accepts a main.rs change only when the old text with $0 replaced by () equals the new.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_3 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 3u32, "2026-10-24T23:57Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:57 main groom: still the only non-lib red in the Cells suite on the tree (cells-suite-tree-2 11:55). Fix A rides in pack 5i (5h is built on pack-next9). Done once 5i is installed and the Cells live test passes on the tree.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_4 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 4u32, "2026-10-25T23:38Z", "compiler", "Root cause found. The core emits `__spiral_emit_rust <args> <code>` placeholders; the HOST post-processes them\nin `rewriteRustEmitExpr` (apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs:416-491, called at :590 for coreWrites Rust),\nsubstituting `$0,$1,...` in the snippet payload with the args (loop at :484-485).\n\nBug: `splitArgs` (:440-462) has `if value = \"()\" then []` (:442). A UNIT argument renders as Rust `()`, so the\nargs group is `()`, which splitArgs treats as ZERO args -> the substitution loop `for argIndex = args.Length-1 downto 0`\nnever runs -> `$0` stays LITERAL in the emitted Rust -> rustc rejects `$0`. (Cells symptom: \"a print... keeps its literal $0\".)\n\nFix (one line): :442 `if value = \"()\" then []` -> `if value = \"()\" then [\"()\"]`.\nSafe in both cases:\n- unit arg, payload has $0  -> $0 replaced with `()` (correct Rust unit). FIXES IT.\n- genuine no-args, payload has no $0 -> args=[\"()\"] but Replace(\"$0\",\"()\") at :485 is a no-op. HARMLESS.\n\nVerify (needs a compile, deferred under 100% CPU): add/compile a sample with a Rust emit snippet taking a unit\nargument that references $0 (e.g. `$\"do_thing($0)\"  ()`), build -Backend Rust -Native, confirm rustc exits clean and\nthe output has `()` not `$0`. Then -Suite examples -Native stays green (the change only affects the \"()\" args branch).\nCommit the sample's main.rs. Seam: anchored apply + composite hash into pack 5n.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_5 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 5u32, "2026-10-26T05:42Z", "compiler", "compiler lane: a plain `emit ()` unit arg does NOT reproduce the bug on the node single-flight compiler. TDD: added `inl unit_case : i32 = emit () ($'\"{ let _u = $0; 0i32 }\"')` to a copy of rust_emit_tuple_args, compiled -Mode single-flight -Backend Rust on the node (exit 0); generated main.rs = `let v10: i32 = { let _u = (); 0i32 };` -- $0 -> () correctly, NO literal $0, NO __spiral_emit_rust marker anywhere (all three tuple emits also clean). So the core substitutes emit args (incl. a unit arg) directly; the host post-pass rewriteRustEmitExpr (Program.fs:416-491, splitArgs(\"()\")->[] at :442) is NOT exercised by a normal `emit` call. The item real trigger is the specific Cells \"print\" scenario that LEAVES a __spiral_emit_rust marker for the host to rewrite with a unit arg -- need that exact shape to reproduce. The splitArgs(\"()\")->[\"()\"] fix stays correct for the host-rewrite path but is UNVERIFIED until that trigger is found. Next: locate the Cells print/emit that leaves the marker (kept at 55%).")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_6 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 6u32, "2026-10-26T16:47Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (open question from note 5 CLOSED: the Cells marker trigger is located + the host-vs-core distinction explained). Trigger: apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/console.spi.\n- Line 1-2: `:= (~!\\\\) ... = $'__spiral_emit_rust !args !code '` -- `!\\\\` is a MACRO ($'...'), so it emits the LITERAL text `__spiral_emit_rust <args> <code>` into the Rust output; the CORE does not substitute it.\n- Line 4-16: `show_value value` calls `!\\\\(value, <snippet>)` where the snippet (line 7-15) contains `(&SpiralKinoShow(&$0)).spiral_cells_show()` -- i.e. it references $0, and $0 is `value`.\nSo `show_value value` -> emitted marker `__spiral_emit_rust <value-text> <snippet with $0>`, left for the HOST's rewriteRustEmitExpr (Program.fs:416-491) to substitute $0 with the args.\n\nWHY note 5's plain `emit ()` did NOT reproduce but THIS does: a normal core `emit (args)(code)` intrinsic is substituted by the CORE (so a unit arg -> $0 becomes () correctly, no marker survives). Cells' `!\\\\` is a $'__spiral_emit_rust ...' MACRO -> the core emits the marker VERBATIM and the HOST post-pass does the $0 substitution. The host path is `splitArgs` (:440-462) with the `if value = \"()\" then []` bug (:442): a UNIT value renders args group `()`, splitArgs returns [] -> the $0 substitution loop never runs -> literal `$0` survives -> rustc rejects `SpiralKinoShow(&$0)`. That is EXACTLY the \"a print with no value\" Cells live test (note 1: rustc rejects SpiralKinoShow(&$0)).\n\nSo: Fix A (host splitArgs \"()\"->[\"()\"], already staged in pack 5i per note 3) is the CORRECT fix for this host-rewrite path; now its trigger + mechanism are pinned. Repro shape = a Cells CELL whose value is unit (e.g. last expr is `console.write_line \"x\"` / a bare `()` -> show_value gets a unit value). It is a CELLS-HARNESS repro (the marker is host-rewritten only in the Cells compile path), NOT a standalone `--backend Rust` sample -- consistent with note 1's gate being the Cells live test 'a print with no value still runs', not a matrix sample. Item open question (note 5 \"locate the Cells print/emit that leaves the marker\") = CLOSED. Remaining: 5i install + the Cells live test passes (both build/harness-bound). Open question B from note 1 (why a unit cell reaches show_value_main at all, vs unit_result? appending 0i32) is a separate cells.ex compile/12 ordering question, still needs the probe run.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_7 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 7u32, "2026-10-27T01:00Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (open question B CLOSED by reading the Cells host). Q: why does a unit cell reach show_value_main vs unit_result? appending 0i32? A: it normally DOESN'T. In apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells.ex compile/13 the recovery cond on a first-attempt compile error of a generated_main cell checks Cell.unit_result? (line ~632-634, retry appending 0i32) BEFORE value_result? (line ~636-638, retry via show_value_main). They are ORDERED, not competing, so a true unit generated_main cell gets 0i32 appended and never reaches show_value_main. The $0 bug is ORTHOGONAL to that routing: show_value DEFAULTS true (cells.ex ~549 `show_value = not prepared.no_value`), so console.spi's SpiralKinoShow show-snippet (!\\(value, ...$0...)) is emitted for any non-:no_value cell, and when that cell's RUNTIME value is unit the host splitArgs(\"()\")->[] drops $0 (the Program.fs:440-462 bug) -> literal $0 survives -> rustc rejects SpiralKinoShow(&$0). The compile-retry 0i32 logic only protects generated_main cells caught by unit_result?, NOT every unit value reaching the show-snippet. So pack 5i's splitArgs \"()\"->[\"()\"] is confirmed at the RIGHT layer (host show-path, covers all unit-reaching-show cases). Open question B resolved; remaining = 5i install + the Cells live test \"a print with no value\" passes (build/harness-bound).")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_8 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 8u32, "2026-10-28T11:45Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28: confirmed the splitArgs fix is NOT in the installed 9FF1B708 host -- Program.fs:442 still reads `if value = \"()\" then []` (unfixed). So pack 5i (:442 -> [\"()\"]) hasnt shipped; it is a clean one-liner land-candidate to fold into the next compiler pack (e.g. the P3 lever pack). Remaining = land it + the Cells \"print with no value\" live test (harness-bound). Fully diagnosed (n5-7: host rewriteRustEmitExpr path, Cells !\\\\ macro leaves the __spiral_emit_rust marker); build-free surface exhausted.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_9 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 9u32, "2026-10-29T06:22Z", "main", "10-29 02:3x main: the applier lost with pack 5i and the compiler lane's TEMP is REBUILT: apps/compiler/packs/apply-rust-emit-unit-arg.ps1 -HostSource <Program.fs> [-Apply] (untracked, with the other appliers). It has one exact anchor, the two lines `if value = \"()\" then []` / `else` in rewriteRustEmitExpr's splitArgs (apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs:442, still unfixed in the live host), and changes the result to [\"()\"]. Dry run on the live host: 1/1 anchor matches exactly once; applied to a scratch copy, the diff is exactly that one line. Harmless-case check by reading :463-488: the args are used only by the $k substitution loop (args.Length-1 downto 0, payload.Replace(\"$k\", arg)), and borrow \"()\" returns \"()\", so a snippet without $0 is unchanged and a unit argument now turns $0 into (). Left for an install slot: this is a HOST change, so the host DLL hash moves (today A4E480202BD40D80; the core hash is unaffected); then the Cells live test 'a print with no value still runs'. Still open from n1: whether Cells' split_output (apps/cells/spi/cell.spi) then reads 'SPIRAL_CELLS_VALUE:()' as a value; the live test answers it, and if it does, split_output treats a \"()\" value as none.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_10 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 10u32, "2026-10-29T18:07Z", "compiler", "10-29 14:1x compiler: COMPILER HALF PROVEN, n1's open question B ANSWERED, the Cells fix drafted and proven. Node, installed 6b compiler (host 9895730DAE807B70, core 31CC96520D6B7269, the splitArgs '()' -> ['()'] host fix shipped in 6b, Program.fs:442), mirror pack7b at cec8840c, CellsLiveTest 'a print with no value still runs': rustc now compiles the show snippet (3.3 s) and the cell exits 0 with stdout 'Hello from Spiral!', so the literal $0 is gone. The test still FAILED, on value: \"()\" (expected nil): with $0 substituted, console.show_value formats the unit with {:?} and prints SPIRAL_CELLS_VALUE:(), and split_output (apps/cells/spi/cell.spi:233-238) takes any such line as a value. So n1's B is 'yes, it reads () as a value'.\nFix at the right layer, not split_output: a unit has nothing to show on any backend (F# would print (), Python None). apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/console.spi: the existing body becomes show_some_value, and show_value is a type-case on t, using the idiom of the same file's Lua arm: ~> ?: t ?| | () => () | _ => show_some_value value. Proof (job-1793296589793, the mirror's console.spi swapped in and restored by hash afterwards): the whole cells_live_test.exs passes 4/4 (compiles and runs a cell; a print with no value still runs; a type error is a SpiralError; imports a package and runs it = \"42\"), so value-returning cells are unaffected. The draft is formatter-canonical (format --check exit 0; formatting it is a no-op). Hashes: live 03A7768198A9DB54 -> new A70EDFA2F9652A79. Draft: compiler scratchpad cells-unit/console.new.spi.\nLand: after TRAIN4's 'window closed', in the compiler slot (no Cells runs during it), with the reader guard (live must still be 03A7768198A9DB54). Then Done.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_11 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 11u32, "2026-10-29T19:24Z", "compiler", "10-29 15:2x compiler: DONE. apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/console.spi landed in the lib/Cells window after main's 5 lands, with the reader guard: 03A7768198A9DB54 -> A70EDFA2F9652A79, equal to the proven draft, +7/-1 (show_value type-cases unit -> () and delegates everything else to show_some_value). Undo copy: C:\\home\\data\\pack7-review\\cells-unit\\console.undo.spi. Box confirmation: main's closing document_test passed 22/22 including this file (15:23); node proof, cells_live_test 4/4 including 'a print with no value still runs' (n10). Compiler half: 6b's host splitArgs fix.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_1 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T22:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:27 x: found while building Gleam components for BACKEND-BALANCE. The sim's OTP shell, memory_watch and a future Gleam xsim entry (needed for xsim diff/thread_run, which call Cells' evaluate on the BEAM) all hit these five functions having no Gleam arm (Rust/F#/Zig/TS only). memory_watch works around it with an injected thunk. Previously only a mention in X-BACKEND-MAP n9, which main read as already tracked; this item is the record. x will draft the arms in x-world/lib-draft for main to land, as with today's dictm/unicode handoff.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_2 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T22:30Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:30 x: arms DRAFTED as an anchored patch, %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_os_arms.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. env: count = list.length(init:get_plain_arguments()), item i = charlist.to_string of the i-th, or '' past the end (Rust returns unwrap_or_default ''). file_system: read_all_text = file:read_file + bit_array.to_string, with let assert (panics on error like Rust's unwrap); write_all_text = file:write_file, asserting ok; file_exists = filelib:is_regular (an existing regular file, the same as Rust's exists && is_file). Helpers are env_gleam_argv () and file_system_gleam_files () (global blocks, as process.spi does). Dry-applied to a lib copy: 5 arms, every anchor unique. Proof set up on a PATCHED lib copy (%TEMP%\\x-libcopy\\lib, real folder, no junction), check package %TEMP%\\x-libcopy\\check: write/exists/read round trip + argv. The same test is also run on the LIVE lib (%TEMP%\\x-libcopy\\control) as the positive control, which must fail at the missing Gleam arm. Both queued after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_3 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T23:30Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:30 x: fixed before any run. The draft put file_system_gleam_files () before read_all_text (~634), but file_exists (572) and write_all_text (611) call it too, and Spiral resolves top-down, so those arms would not have compiled. The helper now goes before file_exists, anchored with its let line, since file_exists is a prefix of file_exists_content. The proof copy %TEMP%\\x-libcopy was rebuilt from the live lib and re-applied: helper at 571, users at 612/654/678; no new lines over 120 in env.spi or file_system.spi. The check + live-lib control still run after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_4 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 4u32, "2026-10-24T00:02Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:02 x: one more missing Gleam arm, same theme (found while making memory_watch incremental): sm'.last_index_of has Zig/Rust arms and an F#-style default (.LastIndexOf), so on Gleam it fails. Draft %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_last_index.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral> adds a Gleam arm returning a BYTE offset, like sm'.index_of's Gleam arm (bit_array.byte_size of the head) and Rust's rfind. It uses Erlang string:find(S, Search, trailing): total bytes minus the suffix's, -1 on nomatch, and the length for an empty search. That's also right for overlapping patterns ('aa' in 'aaa' = 1), where binary:matches' non-overlapping scan would give 0. Applied to the OS-arms proof copy %TEMP%\\x-libcopy; the check package gains last_index_of_gives_the_last_byte_offset_on_the_beam (abcb/b = 3, aaa/aa = 1, z = -1, '' = 4), and the live-lib control must fail it.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_5 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 5u32, "2026-10-24T00:05Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:05 x: CENSUS of lib functions with backend arms but no Gleam arm (core modules, Rust-only signatures filtered out): 50 with portable signatures (string/i32/bool/list), mostly file_system (25), env (6), sm' (5: line_count, nonempty_line_count, line_at, nonempty_line_at, last_index_of), console colors, date_time, process.try_capture_combined_list. A BEAM component that calls one fails at runtime. Second batch DRAFTED: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_os_arms2.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral> adds Gleam arms to get_file_name (filename:basename), directory_exists (filelib:is_dir), file_delete (file:delete, errors ignored like Rust's traced path) and append_all_text (file:write_file with [append], asserting ok like Rust's unwrap). It has its own helper before get_file_name (line 414), because those functions come before the first batch's helper. Skipped get_extension on purpose: its backends already disagree (Rust gives 'spi' and panics without an extension; F# gives '.spi'), so a Gleam arm would have to pick a contract; that needs a decision first. Applied to %TEMP%\\x-libcopy after batch 1 + last_index_of: helper 414, users 457/534/827/1386, no new lines over 120. Check test paths_append_and_delete_on_the_beam added (live-lib control must fail).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_6 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 6u32, "2026-10-24T00:07Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:07 x: batch 4 DRAFTED: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_line_arms.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. It adds Gleam arms to sm'.line_count, nonempty_line_count, line_at and nonempty_line_at, whose only arm was Rust's .lines(), with the same semantics: split on '\\n', strip one trailing '\\r' per line, no final empty line after a trailing newline, 0 lines for ''. The helper lines_gleam () sits before line_count (line_count has no heading, so the anchor is its inl line). Applied on %TEMP%\\x-libcopy after batches 1-3 (helper 683, users from 712). Check test line_helpers_follow_rust_lines_on_the_beam: 'a\\r\\nb\\n' has 2 lines and line 1 = 'b'; '' has 0; nonempty in 'a\\n\\nb' = 2 and line 1 = 'b'.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_7 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 7u32, "2026-10-24T00:08Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:08 x: batch 5 DRAFTED: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_try_capture.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. It gives process.try_capture_combined_list a Gleam arm built from process.spi's own port helpers: a missing program gives None (os:find_executable is checked first, because open_port with 'false' would crash, while Rust's output() returns Err), a non-zero exit gives None, and otherwise stdout + stderr (stderr_to_stdout). One known difference: invalid UTF-8 output gives None on Gleam where Rust gives lossy text. Risk stated: the arm returns a Gleam #(Bool, String) typed as Spiral bool * string, which assumes Spiral pairs are native Gleam tuples; the check settles it (if not, switch to a nominal + accessors like x's cells_run). Use case: memory_watch's sampler can run a sampling command per tick on the BEAM. Applied on %TEMP%\\x-libcopy; check test: a missing program is None.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_8 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 8u32, "2026-10-24T00:10Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:10 x: batch 6 DRAFTED: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_stat_arms.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. get_current_directory gets file:get_cwd (asserting ok like Rust's unwrap), and last_write_ms gets file:read_file_info with posix time, mtime x 1000 and 0 when missing (Rust's unwrap_or(0)). Two differences, recorded: Erlang's cwd uses forward slashes on Windows ('c:/...') where Rust prints backslashes, and the mtime is whole seconds where Rust has milliseconds; both are fine for change detection, not for path equality across backends. Skipped on purpose: get_full_path (Rust canonicalize adds Windows' \\\\?\\ prefix; the contract needs a decision) and get_temp_path (OS-specific env-var rules). Helper file_system_gleam_stat () sits before get_current_directory (494; users 540, 1396). Check test: cwd non-empty, mtime > 0 for a fresh file, 0 for a missing one. Applied on %TEMP%\\x-libcopy with batches 1-5.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_9 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 9u32, "2026-10-24T00:13Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:13 main: get_extension contract decided by rule (x asked). The arms disagree (Rust Path::extension -> 'spi' and a panic when there is none through optionm'.unwrap; F# Path.GetExtension -> '.spi' or ''), and the function has 0 callers in lib/spiral, eoie/src and x/apps, so it can be fixed without breaking anyone. Rule: total (no panic) and the majority of standard libraries (Python os.path.splitext, Node path.extname, Go filepath.Ext and .NET all return the leading dot, and '' when there is no extension; Rust is the outlier). Contract: '.spi', or '' for no extension. So the Rust arm becomes extension().map_or('', |e| '.' + e) and the Gleam arm follows the same contract, with a test over 'a/b.spi' -> '.spi', 'a/b' -> '' and 'a.b/c' -> '' (a dot in a directory name only).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_10 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 10u32, "2026-10-24T00:15Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:15 x: get_extension folded into batch 2, per main's 20:1x decision (it has no callers in lib, eoie or x; Python, Node, Go and .NET all give the leading dot or ''). The Rust arm becomes extension().map_or('', |e| '.' + e) instead of unwrap (which panicked); the Gleam arm is filename:extension of the basename with one leading dot stripped, because Erlang gives '.bashrc' for a dotfile where Rust gives None. Test on rust + gleam: a/b.spi -> .spi, a/b -> '', a.b/c -> '', .bashrc -> ''. %TEMP%\\x-libcopy rebuilt from the live lib with all six batches in order: all applied, no new lines over 120. That makes 18 functions covered; get_full_path and get_temp_path still need a contract decision.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_11 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 11u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:17 x: get_extension's F# arm aligned too (main's follow-up): an explicit Fsharp arm returns empty for a dotfile (the file name starts with a dot and has no later dot; IndexOf with a string, ordinal), else Path.GetExtension, which by default gives .bashrc. The string overload avoids a quoted char literal, which would close the Spiral macro string early. The test targets rust, gleam and fsharp, so the three arms are pinned together. Proof copy rebuilt with all six batches; no new long lines.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_12 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 12u32, "2026-10-24T00:22Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:22 x: analysis for the two undecided contracts (main decides; evidence from this machine). Both have callers only inside file_system.spi (6 and 8 hits, mostly its own tests), so a contract change is cheap, as with get_extension. (1) get_temp_path: .NET GetTempPath returns WITH a trailing separator (C:\\Users\\...\\Temp\\); Rust std::env::temp_dir follows GetTempPath2 on Windows and TMPDIR or /tmp elsewhere. Proposal: return the directory without a trailing separator on every backend (trim it in the F# arm), and for Gleam look up os:getenv TMPDIR, then TEMP, then TMP, falling back to /tmp. (2) get_full_path: today the Rust arm canonicalizes existing paths, which requires existence, resolves symlinks and adds Windows' \\\\?\\ prefix, and normalizes lexically only for missing paths; .NET GetFullPath is purely lexical (x/../y -> <cwd>\\y, no filesystem access). Proposal: purely lexical everywhere (absolute against cwd, . and .. removed, no symlink resolution, no \\\\?\\), i.e. the .NET behavior, the same for existing and missing paths. Gleam: filename:absname, then fold the segments to drop . and resolve .. . Either way, a test pinning x/../y and a missing path on all arms.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_13 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 13u32, "2026-10-24T00:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:23 main: get_temp_path and get_full_path decided by the get_extension rule (total; the majority of standard libraries; callers only inside file_system.spi). (1) get_temp_path: no trailing separator on every backend. Python tempfile.gettempdir, Node os.tmpdir and Go os.TempDir omit it; .NET GetTempPath is the outlier. Lookup order on Gleam: TMPDIR, TEMP, TMP, then /tmp (Python's order), so the F# arm trims the trailing separator. (2) get_full_path: purely lexical everywhere (absolute against cwd, . and .. removed, no symlink resolution, no \\\\?\\ prefix), as Python os.path.abspath, Node path.resolve, Go filepath.Abs and .NET GetFullPath are. Rust's canonicalize is the outlier and fails on missing paths. Test: x/../y and a missing path, on rust, gleam and fsharp.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_14 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 14u32, "2026-10-24T00:26Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:26 x: batch 7 DRAFTED per main's 20:2x decisions: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_path_arms.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. (1) get_temp_path: no trailing separator on every backend (new pure without_trailing_separator, keeping a lone root); Rust and F# trim, and Gleam looks up TMPDIR, TEMP, TMP (empty counts as unset), then /tmp (Python's order). (2) get_full_path: purely lexical. The Rust arm drops its canonicalize branch and always takes its existing lexical branch (extracted programmatically from the live text and dedented, refusing if the arm's shape drifted). </> is PathBuf::join, so absolute inputs still replace cwd. The Gleam arm is filename:absname, then a fold dropping . and empty segments and resolving .. ; F# keeps GetFullPath (already lexical). Recorded difference: Gleam joins with / on Windows, like get_current_directory. Test on rust + gleam + fsharp: x/../y ends in y with no .., a missing path ends in its name, and the temp path has no trailing separator. %TEMP%\\x-libcopy rebuilt with all 7 batches in order: all applied; helpers precede users (env 408 < temp 435; paths 450 < full_path 1091); no new lines over 120. 20 functions covered now.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_15 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 15u32, "2026-10-24T01:11Z", "x", "2026-10-23 21:11 x: CORRECTION by user direction (21:0x): \"why we're delegating to main lib work? makes no sense, one of my goals of inventing eoie is the atomic batched patches, so all lanes can go ham, also with automatic backups etc\". The earlier note here saying \"Lib edits are main's domain, so coordinate each landing with main\" was my own unexamined assumption, not a rule. From now on x lands its own lib changes through eoie do atomic batched edits (expect counts, all-or-nothing, sha256 per file) plus the lib Cells gate. Coordination with other lanes is about order and live-tree reads only, not ownership. x takes over landing of its own drafts: the x landing patch (crypto/sm'/listm'/resultm + oauth + cursor_walk) and LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS's 7 batches. Main keeps LIB-GAPS-X, which x's patch builds on (take_).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_16 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 16u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "x", "23:06 x: batches 1-7 are LIVE in lib/spiral since 22:06:07 (inside x part A's single eoie do batch; see X-GENERIC-TO-LIB n23). Gate on the new compiler: oauth 2/2 and cursor_walk 2/2 pass (the first oauth timeout was contention); x_lib_check 2/2; gleam_os_check 8/9. The open failure is the Gleam target of full_path_is_lexical_and_temp_path_has_no_trailing_separator: gleam build stops at 'Unknown module gleam/regexp'. Only sm'.replace_regex imports gleam/regexp (its Gleam arm), reached through file_system.normalize_path, and statically neither new Gleam arm calls it. Bisecting with two one-call probes (%TEMP%\\x-gleam-regexp-probe, job-1792811193870-x-gleam-regexp-bisect). Rules: if the probe that fails (get_full_path or get_temp_path) shows the regexp import, fix that arm with an atomic patch; if neither fails, it's the combined notebook, so add the gleam regexp dependency to the test.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_17 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 17u32, "2026-10-24T03:35Z", "x", "23:35 x: bisect DONE. probe_temp_path passes on Gleam; probe_full_path fails with the regexp import. Cause, by four compile probes in %TEMP%\\x-gleam-regexp-probe: the dead Rust arm of get_full_path calls the top-level let functions get_current_directory and normalize_path. Their Gleam-side globals leak into Gleam builds (normalize_path brings sm'.replace_regex's gleam/regexp), while an inl callee in a dead arm doesn't leak. Compiler finding sent to main. Lib fix (programs-normalize-prefix\\land.eoie + undo): normalize_path strips the literal \\\\?\\ prefix with starts_with + substring instead of replace_regex, plus a test normalize_path_drops_the_verbatim_prefix (rust, no -d regex). On a patched copy the probe no longer imports regexp. RULE, written before the Cells results: land when (a) gleam_os_check on the patched copy is 9/9, (b) the new test passes, and (c) file_system.spi's failing tests on the patched copy are a subset of those on an unpatched copy with the same layout (x-normalize-base). Otherwise don't land, and fix first.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_18 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 18u32, "2026-10-24T04:09Z", "x", "10-24 00:09 x: side finding from the bisect. lib sm'.replace_regex's own test runs on '///> gleam' with no dependency directive, but its Gleam arm imports gleam/regexp, which Cells' Gleam build doesn't include by default (probe_full_path's failure). Red/green probes queued, job-1792814947532 (%TEMP%\\x-regexp-directive-probe): the same assertion bare vs '///> gleam -d gleam_regexp=\">= 1.1.0 and < 2.0.0\"' (base.spi's -d form). RULE, before results: bare fails on regexp and directive passes -> land the directive on sm'.spi's replace_regex test via the guarded runner, gated on that test. Both pass -> Cells already provides it, so re-examine tonight's failure. Directive fails -> the -d syntax is wrong; read Cells' directive parser before touching lib.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_19 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 19u32, "2026-10-24T04:59Z", "x", "10-24 00:59 x: ORDERING. main's %TEMP%\\lib-fs-primitives\\apply.ps1 (replace_checked, is_within and find_on_path, appended to file_system.spi; is_within calls normalize_path) waits for the normalize_path land. When x-lib-normalize lands (decide3 job-1792817005040 -> lander), send main a one-liner. main takes the file_system.spi:889 repo-coupled cwd test fix itself (not folded into mine, so my program stays byte-identical to the proof).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_20 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 20u32, "2026-10-24T06:08Z", "x", "10-24 02:08 x: n18's rule applied. The bare probe fails on Gleam with 'Unknown module gleam/regexp' and the same assertion with '///> gleam -d gleam_regexp=\">= 1.1.0 and < 2.0.0\"' passes, so landing the directive on sm'.spi's replace_regex test. It goes via the guarded lander (programs-regexp-directive\\land.eoie + undo, dry-run fits the live lib, job x-regexp-directive-lander), gated on sm'.spi through the mutex + 3.5 GB floor gate. (probe job job-1792817005684-x-regexp-directive-probe3)")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_21 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 21u32, "2026-10-24T06:19Z", "x", "10-24 02:19 x: proof3 result. gleam_os_check is 9/9 on the patched copy (rule a). The patched file_system.spi ran 21 tests and failed 3: find_parent (fsharp), find_parent_2 (rust) and trace_file (fsharp). The new normalize_path_drops_the_verbatim_prefix test is not among them. PREDICTION, written before the baseline result: all three fail the same way on the unpatched twin. find_parent/_2 walk up from the source or current directory for a 'workspace' folder and README.md, which don't exist above a %TEMP% lib copy, and trace_file writes under get_workspace_root's layout search. None of them reaches the \\\\?\\ prefix branch. If the baseline differs, the decision job's rule (c) refuses the land, and I investigate before any retry.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_22 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 22u32, "2026-10-24T08:47Z", "x", "10-24 04:47 x: TAKING the file_system.spi GRANULAR-HEAL slice at main's request (7,928 violating chars). It's drafted on a lib copy with the normalize_path patch already applied (%TEMP%\\x-fs-heal\\lib), so its anchors match the tree after the normalize land, and it lands after it. The globals with bodies: env (temp_dir_from), paths (lexical_full_path, dotted_extension, append_text), stat (cwd, mtime_ms), files (read_text, write_text), plus walk_dir_filter's poll (Rust), file_exists, and Rust's MAIN_SEPARATOR cast, symlink cfg blocks and mtime. FS-1 (programs-fs-heal-1, 4 replacements) moves the 3 gleam_paths bodies into Spiral over the externs, which stay. gleam_segment_step plus a listm.fold over sm'.split_list of absname gives lexical_full_path; basename, a Spiral leading-dot strip and the extension extern give dotted_extension; append goes through gleam_written + coerce + gleam_atoms_equal, and failwith keeps the old let-assert behavior. Check: gleam_os_check on the copy, job-1792831611429.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_23 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 23u32, "2026-10-24T08:54Z", "x", "10-24 04:54 x: FS-1 VERIFIED. gleam_os_check on the healed copy passes 9/9 on rust, gleam and fsharp (job-1792831611429), covering full path, extension and append. FS-2 (programs-fs-heal-2, 10 replacements) is applied to the copy: the shared nominals (gleam_dynamic, gleam_atom, gleam_charlist) move above gleam_env; temp_dir_from becomes a Spiral let rec over one-call getenv/coerce/charlist primitives; cwd and mtime_ms go through resultm.result' + unbox, with the posix-option tuple written as ##( in the macro and * 1000 in Spiral; read_text and write_text go through result' + unbox and the ok-atom check. Its check is job-1792832046429.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_24 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 24u32, "2026-10-24T09:01Z", "x", "10-24 05:01 x: FS-2 VERIFIED. gleam_os_check on the healed copy passes 9/9 on rust, gleam and fsharp (job-1792832046429), covering temp path, cwd, mtime and the file round trip. With FS-1 + FS-2 every Gleam global in file_system.spi holds only externs and imports (rule 3): the bodies of temp_dir_from, lexical_full_path, dotted_extension, append_text, cwd, mtime_ms, read_text and write_text are now Spiral over one-call primitives. Ready to land in order after the normalize land (anchored on the post-normalize text): programs-normalize-prefix, then programs-fs-heal-1, then programs-fs-heal-2. Left for FS-3: the F# macros (interpolated messages, REPL let-exports), Rust walk_dir_filter's async filter, the MAIN_SEPARATOR cast, the symlink cfg blocks, and the mtime/append chains.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_25 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 25u32, "2026-10-24T09:02Z", "x", "10-24 05:02 x: land chain queued, all guarded (reader guard + rehearsal on a fresh copy + gleam_os_check on the live lib): normalize lander job-1792828314873 (waits for eoie GE3's live-lib phases) -> --after-ok job-1792832494176-x-fs-heal-1-lander -> --after-ok job-1792832495716-x-fs-heal-2-lander. Each lander's guard also waits for the previous slice's own gate, which reads the lib, so every slice lands on a verified base. Message main at the normalize land (their fs-primitives append follows).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_26 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 26u32, "2026-10-24T09:09Z", "x", "10-24 05:09 x: FS-3 under main's rule readings (GRANULAR-HEAL n5): symlinks use cfg-gated helper fns (same name and signature, one-call bodies); F# REPL export lines are host declarations, so they stay; walk_dir_filter is x's (later, over main's async primitives). FS-3a (programs-fs-heal-3): Rust last_write_ms = metadata/modified/duration_since bridges + ms in Spiral (0 on any error); Rust append_all_text = OpenOptions open + write_all bridges, with failwith keeping the old unwrap panics. Check job-1792832705648. FS-3b (programs-fs-heal-4, dry run 6/6): directory/file_create_symbolic_link Rust arms call spiral_fs_symlink_dir/_file, cfg-gated globals for windows (symlink_dir/symlink_file) and unix (symlink), behind a rust_link_done bridge + failwith; and the all-string F# interpolations become Spiral ++# (find_parent's message, new_file_uri, trace_file's file name with sm'.obj_to_string guid). Left: read_link's mixed-type messages (ints and exceptions; they need the lib formatter, so not a mechanical swap), trace_file's %A, and walk_dir_filter.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_27 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 27u32, "2026-10-24T09:37Z", "x", "10-24 05:37 x: FS-3a VERIFIED (job-1792832705648). The whole file_system.spi on the healed copy fails exactly the 3 layout tests, the same set as the unpatched baseline (find_parent, find_parent_2, trace_file); gleam_os_check passes 9/9. Its lander, job-1792834592721, is chained --after-ok the FS-2 lander. FS-3b (programs-fs-heal-4: symlink cfg helpers + string messages) is applied to the copy; its check is job-1792834585180 (same two notebooks, same baseline rule).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_28 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 28u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "x", "10-24 19:44 x: held-files runner (job-1792875049850, exit 0) LANDED normalize-prefix and fs-heal-1..3 on live file_system.spi (18:28; gate %TEMP%\\x-libcopy\\control\\gleam_os_check.spi passed after each). fs-heal-4 was applied only to the copy %TEMP%\\x-fs-heal-4-recheck-10241828\\spiral: file_system.spi failed 21/21 tests, 0 passed, on every target (rust, fsharp, gleam, zig). That's a whole-file failure, not the baseline subset (find_parent, find_parent_2, trace_file), so FS-4 stays unlanded. Next (CPU-light first): diff the copy's file_system.spi against live and read the first compile error in that run's per-target output. 21/21 points at a parse/type error the program introduces, not test semantics. main got 'file_system.spi done for this round' for its edition-3 respelling, so FS-4 will be redrafted in edition-3 spelling after main's pass.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_29 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 29u32, "2026-10-24T23:46Z", "x", "10-24 19:46 x: main acked (EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION n11): listm'/sm' are released from the edition-3 hold, and file_system.spi stays held until tibia frees it. So FS-4 waits on both its 21/21 diagnosis and tibia's release, and gets redrafted in edition-3 spelling then.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_30 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 30u32, "2026-10-29T15:47Z", "x", "2026-10-29 11:5x x: fs-heal-4 REDONE after the wipe (its 10-24 copy was in TEMP) as x/lib-programs/fs-heal-4.kit (file_system.spi, 4 replacements, proven byte-exact on a copy, 0 new lines over 120; base 9C90242EE8514168 -> target F1495496B0021AED). (1) directory_create_symbolic_link and file_create_symbolic_link: the Rust arms no longer carry '#[cfg] ... .unwrap()' in macro text. Each declares cfg-gated helper fns of one name and signature in a global (windows symlink_dir/symlink_file, unix symlink; each body is one call + is_ok; closing triple quote aligned to the content, per memory rust-global-closing-quote) and keeps the panic semantics through Spiral failwith when the call fails. (2) new_file_uri: the F# interpolation 'file:///{!path}' becomes the Spiral concatenation 'file:///' ++# path. (3) trace_file's file_name: an F# interpolation becomes assembly_name ++# '_' ++# sm'.obj_to_string guid ++# '.txt'. Left as n26 said: read_link's mixed-type messages and trace_file's %A. OVERLAP: none with tibia's LIB-FS-HEAL-P (junction, read-only), main's DELETE-ROOT-GUARD (directory_delete, remove_dir_all) or kit's merged-lib (adds read_text_or_empty); main confirmed from tibia's rescued stage. But kit's insert lands inside hunk 1's leading context, so the program is regenerated on the post-land text (scratch fs4/gen-fs-heal-4.ps1). GATE (not yet run; after verify29 frees the node): whole-file A/B, base vs candidate, verdict = identical failure sets (n27's baseline already failed find_parent, find_parent_2, trace_file), plus a NEG arm ('file://' in new_file_uri, whose two Rust tests must go red).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_31 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 31u32, "2026-10-29T18:47Z", "x", "2026-10-29 15:0x x: the A/B on base B5339FEF is VOID, not red. Arms: base 30/34 passed, candidate 33/34, NEG 29/34. NEG failed new_file_uri and new_file_uri_2 on the assertion, as designed (the control works). But every other failure in every arm was 'Spiral cell timed out after 300s' (slowest cell: base 545 s, candidate 323 s) on a node running 7+ jobs: base failed main's 4 delete-guard Rust tests, the candidate failed file_exists_on_gleam, NEG failed 3 Gleam tests, so the sets differ only by which cell hit the budget. Rule kept (identical failure sets) and not loosened. The rerun gives ALL arms the same gate-only header '///- --timeout 900000' (iggy.spi's precedent), and runs ONCE, on main's post-TRAIN4 file_system land (it replaces the delete_target_is_protected block, which fs-heal-4 does not touch), via the launcher that regenerates the candidate from the live file.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_32 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 32u32, "2026-10-29T19:45Z", "x", "2026-10-29 15:5x x groom (WIPE-REDO-LEDGER n41): blockers for fs-heal-4, which are not backlog items, so no deps entry: (a) the node A/B gate on the rebased base 633EE8887B75 (base/cand/neg, all arms with the 900 s cell budget; launched 15:2x, results pending), then (b) the next lib window after TRAIN4c publishes. Verdict rule unchanged: identical failure sets for base and candidate, NEG fails new_file_uri/new_file_uri_2 on the assertion.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_33 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 33u32, "2026-10-29T19:58Z", "x", "2026-10-29 16:2x x: fs-heal-4 GATED GREEN on base 633EE8887B75 (main's post-gap file_system), pack-6b, every arm with the gate-only 900 s cell budget. Base job-1793301920545 passed 34/34; candidate job-1793302026852 passed 34/34, so the failure sets are identical (both empty); NEG job-1793302359479 fails exactly new_file_uri and new_file_uri_2 (Rust, assertion path), 32/34. The earlier void A/B is resolved: the 300 s budget, not the change, caused those failures. Program x/lib-programs/fs-heal-4.kit regenerated on the live file by the launcher's derivation guard: base 633EE8887B75923A -> target 1F37E39FF3595D80. LAND by hand in the next lib window (after TRAIN4c publishes): pwsh -File x/lib-programs/land-lib-program.ps1 -Program x/lib-programs/fs-heal-4.kit -File file_system.spi -Base 633EE8887B75923A -Target 1F37E39FF3595D80 (re-run gen-fs-heal-4.ps1 first if file_system moved again).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_34 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 34u32, "2026-10-29T21:32Z", "x", "2026-10-29 17:4x x: FOLLOW-UP registered from main's review of fs-heal-4.kit (non-blocking; it lands as gated). (1) wasm32 and other non-windows/non-unix targets: the new cfg'd helper globals leave spiral_fs_symlink_dir/_file UNDEFINED there, where the old cfg'd macro expressions compiled away, so a wasm build that reaches directory_/file_create_symbolic_link would fail. Add '#[cfg(not(any(windows, unix)))] fn ...(target: &str, path: &str) -> bool { false }' (only reachable code is emitted; no wasm consumer calls these today). (2) Diagnostics: is_ok() + failwith(path) drops the io::Error text that unwrap() printed; return the error string (empty on success) and put it in the failwith message. Both go through their own gate (A/B + NEG), as a separate program after fs-heal-4 lands.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_35 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 35u32, "2026-10-29T21:34Z", "x", "2026-10-29 17:34 x: fs-heal-4 LANDED 17:34:27 in main's lib window (after main's 'main lands done'), one guarded try, no exemptions: file_system.spi 633EE8887B75923A -> 1F37E39FF3595D80, exactly the gated target (A/B base 34/34 = candidate 34/34, NEG fails new_file_uri x2). Undo: C:\\home\\data\\undo\\fs-heal-4. Main's closing document_test (the lib round-trip gate) follows. Open: the fs-heal-4b follow-up (x/lib-programs/fs-heal-4b-other-targets.kit, base 1F37E39F -> 0577F852336BCC6A, the cfg(not(any(windows, unix))) fallback helpers; drafted and proven on a copy, gate next) and the io::Error-text follow-up (design pending, one-call host rule).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_36 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 36u32, "2026-10-29T21:40Z", "x", "2026-10-29 17:3x x: fs-heal-4b (other-target fallback) PROVEN THROUGH THE BUG'S OWN PATH: a 3-line Spiral probe calling file_system::directory_create_symbolic_link, BuildSpiral -> Rust on pack-6b, then cargo check (pinned nightly). With live lib (fs-heal-4, 1F37E39F): windows exit 0, wasm32-unknown-unknown exit 101 'E0425 cannot find function spiral_fs_symlink_dir' (main's review concern reproduced). With fs-heal-4b applied (0577F852336BCC6A): windows exit 0, wasm32 exit 0 (3 cfg'd helpers per name). Windows regression gate (the whole file_system notebook, 900 s budget) on the node next; base 34/34 is known from the fs-heal-4 A/B. Lands in the next lib window after TRAIN4d.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_37 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 37u32, "2026-10-29T21:59Z", "x", "2026-10-29 18:1x x: fs-heal-4b GATED GREEN. Windows regression gate (node job-1793310043453, pack-6b, the whole file_system notebook with the 900 s budget): 34/34 passed, 0 type errors, the same as fs-heal-4's base and candidate (34/34). With the wasm32 proof through the bug's own path (n36), it lands FIRST in window 3 (after 'TRAIN4 published', ~19:45), then main rebases last_write_ms onto it. Command: pwsh -File x/lib-programs/land-lib-program.ps1 -Program x/lib-programs/fs-heal-4b-other-targets.kit -File file_system.spi -Base 1F37E39FF3595D80 -Target 0577F852336BCC6A.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_38 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 38u32, "2026-10-29T22:38Z", "x", "2026-10-29 18:37 x: fs-heal-4b LANDED 18:37:15 in window 3 (first, by main's order, right after TRAIN4 published): file_system.spi 1F37E39FF3595D80 -> 0577F852336BCC6A, the gated target (Windows notebook 34/34; wasm32 cargo check fails before and passes after, through the bug's own path; x reachability 0 crates). Undo: C:\\home\\data\\undo\\fs-heal-4b-other-targets. fs-heal-4 and its other-target fallback are both in lib. Remaining in this item: the io::Error-text follow-up (main's review note 2; design under the one-call host rule) and the n26 leftovers (read_link's mixed-type messages, trace_file's %A).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_39 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 39u32, "2026-10-29T22:40Z", "x", "2026-10-29 18:4x x: DESIGN for main's review note 2 (keep the OS reason when a symlink fails), following file_system's own pattern (create_dir_all returns resultm.result' () stream.io_error from one call; file_delete formats it with resultm.map_error' sm'.format |> resultm.unbox). Helpers: each cfg'd spiral_fs_symlink_dir/_file returns std::io::Result<()> as ONE call (windows symlink_dir/symlink_file, unix symlink); the other-targets fallback returns Err(std::io::ErrorKind::Unsupported.into()). Spiral: := linked : resultm.result' () stream.io_error = $'spiral_fs_symlink_dir(&*!target, &*!path)', then ?= linked |> resultm.map_error' sm'.format |> resultm.unbox ?| Ok () => () | Error why => failwith ('directory_create_symbolic_link / ' ++# path ++# ': ' ++# why). Same for the file variant. Gate as for 4b: the Windows notebook A/B plus the wasm32 cargo-check probe; NEG = a nonexistent target dir must fail with the OS text in the message. Drafted AFTER window 3 closes, on the post-window base (main's last_write_ms lands on 0577F852 now).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_40 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 40u32, "2026-10-29T22:54Z", "x", "2026-10-29 18:54 x: fs-heal-4c DRAFTED on main's post-window-3 base. Program x/lib-programs/fs-heal-4c-io-error-text.kit takes file_system.spi 8661852BC5D5E573 to 81140E3E5A74B5F4 (2 replacements, n39's design verbatim, sm'.format as at :1074). Local probe (scratchpad fs4cprobe, installed 6b DLL 9895730DAE80), pre-registered: base panics 'directory_create_symbolic_link / failed: <path>' (no reason); cand panics '... <path>: Os { code: 3, kind: NotFound, message: The system cannot find the path specified. }'; cand wasm32 cargo check exit 0. NEG deviation: a dangling TARGET does not fail on Windows (symlinks may dangle), so the failing case is a link whose PARENT is missing. Probe Rust grows 2011 to 3997 bytes (result plumbing + show_real); failure path only, 0 x crates reach it. Node Cells A/B queued (x-fs4c-gate-base/cand, which also carry the physics rename). Lands in main's next window via land-lib-program -Base 8661852BC5D5E573 -Target 81140E3E5A74B5F4.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_41 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 41u32, "2026-10-29T22:56Z", "x", "2026-10-29 18:56 x: n26 leftovers RESOLVED as no-change, with evidence. read_link's messages use the compiler's portable interpolation, not host logic: generated spiral.rs:1290/1314/1335 show one format! call with Display holes per message (F# renders the same construct as an interpolated string), which is one host call by the granular rule. A ++# sm'.format rewrite would route ints and io errors through show_real, grow the output and risk changing the text. trace_file's %A sits in an F#-only arm, where the host formatter IS the one-call form. Main queued the remaining work for next session's first window, after fiscal's 3 programs: physics-scalar-force.kit, then fs-heal-4c-io-error-text.kit, landed by x and gated on the node Cells A/B. The item closes at the fs-4c land.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_42 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 42u32, "2026-10-29T23:34Z", "x", "2026-10-29 19:34 x: fs-heal-4c GATE GREEN. node Cells A/B on pack-6b, same 900 s header on both arms: base job-1793314685520 (file_system 34/34, physics 43/43, 0 type errors) vs candidate job-1793314938313 (file_system 34/34, physics 43/43, 0 type errors); identical (empty) failure sets. Gated bytes bound to the arm files: base BA57252523F8C437 / C71EC3BDB0114892, candidate 650F33A377EA66D8 / 254B1994434F27B4 (header + target), matched locally. With the pre-registered probe (base panics without the OS reason; candidate carries Os code 3 NotFound; wasm32 check passes), fs-4c (8661852BC5D5E573 -> 81140E3E5A74B5F4) is ready. It lands by hand in the first lib window after the pack-7 review, after fiscal's three programs and the physics rename.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_43 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 43u32, "2026-10-29T23:50Z", "x", "2026-10-29 19:50 x: fs-heal-4c LANDED 19:50:23 in window 4 (3rd, after fiscal's 4 programs and the physics rename, by main's order): file_system.spi 8661852BC5D5E573 -> 81140E3E5A74B5F4, the node-gated target (A/B 34/34 on both arms; pre-registered probe: the OS reason is now carried). One guarded try, no live lib reader. Undo: C:\\home\\data\\undo\\fs-heal-4c-io-error-text. Every part of this item is now in lib (fs-heal-4, 4b, 4c; n26 resolved as no-change). Closes Done when main announces window 4 closed with document_test and type_probe green.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_44 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 44u32, "2026-10-29T23:52Z", "x", "2026-10-29 19:52 x: DONE. Main closed window 4 all green: document_test 22 passed; type_probe 6/6 PASS (file_system 34 tests typed, physics 43, 0 errors, controls ok). Live file_system 81140E3E5A74B5F4. The item's whole scope is in lib: Gleam arms for env and file_system plus sm'.last_index_of (earlier), fs-heal-4 (symlink helpers, new_file_uri, trace_file name), 4b (other-target fallback), 4c (OS reason carried), n26 leftovers resolved as no-change with evidence (n41).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_note_stdin_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 1u32, "2026-10-23T22:31Z", "eoie", "evidence 2026-10-23: three agile note calls from PowerShell failed with 'received 6 operands' because an escaped quote inside a double-quoted argument split the text (pwsh does not treat backslash-quote as an escape), and one more failed in Invoke-Expression; each needed a rewrite into a here-string. Shape: agile note <root> <id> - [--as <author>] reads stdin to EOF, refuses an empty text as today, and the receipt is unchanged; same for note-edit <root> <id> <n> -. A note text that is literally a dash stays possible by quoting it differently, since a lone dash as text has no real use. Test: notes_cli pipes a text with quotes, backslashes and newlines and shows it back byte for byte.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_note_stdin_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 2u32, "2026-10-23T22:31Z", "eoie", "18:31 eoie: prerequisite found: lib/spiral has no function that reads the process's own stdin (grep for read_to_end, stdin and read_line across lib: only runtime.spi's child-process stdin writers). eoie do <root> - already reads stdin through a legacy host blob in eoie_do. By lib-first, step 1 is lib console.read_to_end () : string (backend_switch: Rust std::io::read_to_string, plus F#/Gleam arms where they exist, with a lib test), landed in my next lib batch after main lands x's lib_gaps. Step 2 is the agile note/note-edit dash (agile_task_notes and agile_note_edit), and step 3 moves eoie_do's blob onto it.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_note_stdin_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 3u32, "2026-10-23T22:33Z", "eoie", "18:33 eoie: step 1 drafted as scratch lib-gaps-2/apply-console-read-to-end.ps1. It inserts lib console.read_to_end () : string before console's write section: a Rust arm through std::io::Read into sm'.std_string and sm'.from_std_string, plus F# Console.In.ReadToEnd and Python sys.stdin.read arms. Its test only typechecks the call behind a runtime-false branch, since a test process's stdin may be a terminal. Dry run on a copy is clean and idempotent. Lib gate after tibia's block; landing after main's next lib land (x's lib_gaps), with main told.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_note_stdin_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 4u32, "2026-10-23T22:35Z", "eoie", "18:35 eoie: step 2 drafted as scratch note-stdin/apply-note-stdin.ps1, on top of release O's note-as-first. note_text_of maps a lone dash to console.read_to_end, trimmed like an argument, at all 4 body sites of note_request_of (refusing if that count changes); note-edit's body gets the same. A guard refuses until lib console.read_to_end exists (control: on the live lib it refused). Dry run on post-O copies applies and is idempotent. Ships in the first release after the lib function lands; its acceptance probe pipes a note with quotes, backslashes and newlines and reads it back with agile show.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_note_stdin_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 5u32, "2026-10-23T23:19Z", "eoie", "19:19 eoie: fourth case today: a note quoting a pwsh loop wrote 'while (\\$true)' inside a double-quoted argument, pwsh expanded $true, and the backlog stored while (\\\\True) until a note-edit fixed it. The fix was the same each time: route the text through a literal here-string. A stdin dash makes that the default path.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_note_stdin_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 6u32, "2026-10-30T15:35Z", "kit", "10-30 11:3x kit: STEP 2 LANDED UNSTAGED. Step 1 (lib console.read_to_end) was already live, so this is the dash.\n- Change: agile note and note-edit read their text from stdin when it is a lone '-'. A one-line note_text_of in agile_task_notes/task_notes.spi (at all 4 body sites of note_request_of) and in agile_note_edit/note_edit.spi maps '-' to console.read_to_end, trimmed as before. Both drafts were lost in the wipe and rewritten from n3/n4: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\note-stdin\\note-stdin.do.\n- Sizes: task_notes 966 -> 970, note_edit 874 -> 899. No other package imports either.\n- ACCEPTANCE (the probe from n4), run through a probe binary over the candidate crates' kit_agile_note_run / kit_agile_note_edit_run, on a scratch backlog:\n  - A here-string with \"double\" and 'single' quotes, a backslash-quote, C:\\path\\, while ($true) { $x } and a second line was piped to 'agile note <root> PROBE - --as kit'. 'agile show' returns it byte for byte, the newline included.\n  - A plain argument still works.\n  - An empty stdin is refused: 'the note text is empty', exit 2.\n  - 'note-edit <root> PROBE 2 -' replaced note 2 from stdin, with receipt replaced=\"plain argument\".\nLEFT: installed-is-done (next train), then step 3 (move kit do's own stdin blob onto console.read_to_end) as a cleanup.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_note_stdin_7 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 7u32, "2026-10-30T18:16Z", "kit", "10-30 14:1x kit: TRAP until TRAIN5 installs this. On the installed kit C528BE4D, 'note-edit <root> <id> <n> - --expect-prefix ...' stores a literal '-' as the note: it hit kit's own EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS n16 at 14:16 (921 -> 1 byte), which was restored at once from the same text (now 930 bytes). --expect-prefix guards the OLD text, so it doesn't catch this. Until the installed kit's usage line mentions '-', pass note text as an argument (a here-string variable).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_note_stdin_8 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 8u32, "2026-10-31T07:48Z", "kit", "10-31 03:3x kit groom: Active -> Blocked. The only remaining step is installed-is-done: TRAIN5e (job-1793421047190, snapshot 00:31) carries this change, and KIT-AGILE-LOST-UPDATES's last part ships in the train after it. Unblock and verify once kit.exe is installed. Moved so that 'agile next --team kit' stops ranking an install wait above actionable work (43 kit items were Active).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_note_stdin_9 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 9u32, "2026-10-31T11:56Z", "kit", "10-31 07:4x kit: DONE (installed-is-done). release-acceptance.ps1 on the installed kit E14F1F0B (TRAIN5e DONE 07:43, 140/140 notebooks; the binary was swapped retire-then-move while lanes ran) passed 23/23 at 07:44. Row: 'agile note reads stdin' (the probe text came back from stdin, 45 bytes). Step 3 (moving kit do's own stdin blob onto console.read_to_end) is cleanup, not user-facing; it stays with EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_note_stdin_10 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN", 10u32, "2026-11-06T11:34Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_1 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 1u32, "2026-10-23T22:41Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:41 main: found while landing x's json_text.spi (X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE). json.decode (json.spi:8) runs json_real.decode in real code, and that emits runtime calls into json_scan (json_scan.spi:78-98 unescaped/unescape_from). unescaped maps n t r quote backslash slash and keeps any other escape as its two source characters, and unescape_from always advances 2. json's tests run on C and Rust (json.spi:2-3). Decision (one record per fact): x's json_text is not landed as a second unescaper; its algorithm (hex4, surrogate pairs joined, a lone surrogate or broken pair -> U+FFFD, broken hex kept) becomes json_scan's \\u branch. unescaped returns (piece, width) so unescape_from advances 6 or 12. That needs a code-point-to-text function with C, Rust and Gleam arms (sm' has none; core sm has only index/length/slice). The Rust arm is char::from_u32 with unwrap_or U+FFFD, and the Gleam arm string.utf_codepoint, both as in the unicode draft. The C arm needs the C backend's string construction convention. Blocker: one small C probe after tibia's 'block end' to read how a C string is built from bytes in generated C. Then draft the arm, gate json.spi (C+Rust) plus the new tests via lib-gate-kit, and land. Regression tests: x's five cases as json decode tests.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_2 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 2u32, "2026-10-23T23:19Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:19 main: correction to note 1: lib/spiral/xml.spi:24 already has code_point_string (u32 -> text), Rust only, and after LIB-UNICODE lands it delegates to unicode.char_of (Rust + Gleam). The missing piece is a C arm for unicode.char_of, which still needs the C probe. json_scan comes before xml in the module order, so its \\u branch calls unicode.char_of, which is listed before xml and after sm'. json_scan is listed after sm' too, so unicode must also come before json_scan: re-check the module order at landing.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_3 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 3u32, "2026-10-23T23:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:23 main: C string convention read from samples/managed_string_builder/main.c: String is Array0 { int refc; uint32_t len; char ptr[] } with len counting the trailing NUL, built by StringLit(len, ptr), which copies. A C arm for char_of can therefore UTF-8-encode into a local char[5] and return StringLit(k + 1, buf) inside a GCC statement expression; no lib C arm uses ({ }) yet, so the probe tests exactly that. Probe drafted: scratch c-char-of-probe (main.spi with the C macro; run.ps1 compiles with the installed compiler --backend C, then gcc -std=gnu11 -O2). Expected exit 6 (e-acute 2 bytes + U+1F600 4 bytes + lone surrogate U+D800 0 bytes, matching Rust's from_u32 None -> ''). It runs after tibia's block. Exit 6 means the arm goes into unicode.char_of as C = fun () => (wrapped under 120); anything else means read the generated main.c first.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_4 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 4u32, "2026-10-24T00:40Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:40 main: the C probe passed (job-1792802067040, exit 6: e-acute 2 bytes, U+1F600 4 bytes, lone surrogate 0), so the ({ }) statement expression with StringLit works on the C backend. The C arm is now in the unicode landing draft (%TEMP%\\lib-unicode, unicode.char_of C = ..., every line under 120), with a new test char_of_encodes_utf8_and_rejects_surrogates on rust, gleam and c (2, 4, 0 bytes). Its gate (main-lib-unicode-gate, chain A) now also compiles C. Next: json_scan's \\u branch calling unicode.char_of, which needs unicode listed before json_scan in the lib modules.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_5 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 5u32, "2026-10-24T00:43Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:43 main: json_scan side drafted (%TEMP%\\lib-json-unicode\\json_scan_u.spi.draft, plain Spiral in json_scan's own style): hex_digit over digit_of; hex4; unicode_escape (a valid pair -> one code point, width 11; a lone or broken surrogate -> U+FFFD, width 5; broken hex keeps the 2 source chars as today); unescaped now returns (piece, width) and also maps \\b and \\f; unescape_from advances 1 + width. Prerequisite found while drafting: every program using json.decode compiles this branch, so unicode.char_of must compile on every backend JSON runs on. Before json_scan calls it, char_of moves to the run_target form with Rust, Gleam, C, F# (System.Char.ConvertFromUtf32 with the surrogate check) and TypeScript (String.fromCodePoint) arms and a default '| _ => failwith', as xml's code-point functions do. Otherwise F#/TS/Zig users of json decode stop compiling. Order: LIB-UNICODE lands, then the char_of arms, then this json_scan patch with x's 5 cases as json tests on c and rust.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_6 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 6u32, "2026-10-24T00:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:51 main: status 0 Planned -> 40 Active: the C probe passed, the run_target char_of with every arm is in the gated unicode draft, and the json_scan \\u side is drafted; it lands after LIB-UNICODE.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_7 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 7u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: lib unicode slice LANDED in live lib/spiral at 23:01 (job main-lib-unicode-land, exit 0) after a GREEN gate (xsd_regex 4/4 and every file rows red 0): unicode.spi listed before json_scan, xml code_point_count and code_point_string delegate to it, char_of with Gleam/Rust/C/F#/TS arms. Next milestone: the json_scan_u draft (hex4, unicode_escape with surrogate pairs, lone surrogate to U+FFFD) through the same gate kit.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_8 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 8u32, "2026-10-24T04:02Z", "main", "2026-10-24 00:02 main: json_scan_u slice ready as %TEMP%\\lib-json-unicode\\apply.ps1 (dry run clean on the live lib): hex_digit, hex4, unicode_escape (surrogate pair = width 11, lone surrogate = U+FFFD width 5, invalid hex kept as written), unescaped returning (piece, width) plus \\b and \\f, unescape_from advancing by width, a testing header, and 2 tests on rust/gleam/c (BMP char + surrogate pair + lone surrogate + \\n; malformed \\u12zz kept). No caller outside json_scan uses unescaped (grep of lib, apps, x, tibia, eoie, fiscal). Queued gate then land at the end of main's lib chain. Side finding for EOIE-PRIMITIVES-TO-LIB: eoie has 3 private JSON unescapers (agile_note_model, agile_task_inspection, eoie_memory_model) that could use lib json_scan once this lands.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_9 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 9u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict: (3) KEPT Active: its slice %TEMP%\\lib-json-unicode\\apply.ps1 is drafted; re-dry-run on the current lib and gate it in main's post-TRAIN2 lib lands.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_10 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 10u32, "2026-10-28T09:47Z", "main", "10-28 05:47 main: Groom 10-28 05:4x: this item's slice has LANDED, as a newer implementation than main's 10-24 draft (TEMP lib-json-unicode, now superseded; its anchors match 0 times). lib/spiral/json_scan.spi (written 10-27 19:53, by another team) has is_high_surrogate, is_low_surrogate, surrogate_pair (code point 65536 + ..., width 11), scalar_text (a lone low surrogate gives U+FFFD, 65533) and unicode_escape, dispatched from the \\u branch at L129, plus the test unicode_escapes_decoded (L240). Set to 95%: the shape asked for is in the tree, but main hasn't seen that test pass. It closes on the next lib gate that runs json_scan's tests GREEN.")
inl note_of_json_unicode_escapes_11 () = Note ("JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES", 11u32, "2026-10-29T08:00Z", "main", "10-29 03:5x main: closed by the lib gate n2 asked for. lib_gate.ps1 -File json_scan.spi on the live lib (compiler A4E480202BD40D80): tests passed 2, failed 0, type errors 0. Per target: parse_string_decodes_unicode_escapes and parse_string_keeps_a_malformed_unicode_escape both pass on rust, gleam and c (6 of 6 rows). Done.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_settle_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SETTLE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T00:24Z", "eoie", "20:24 found in an audit: a waiter queued with job start from a fresh shell got an empty path variable, pwsh exited 64 in 694 ms ('\\post-block-waiter.ps1' not recognized), the receipt said 'job start ok', and the planned post-block checks would never have run (found 25 min later only because the audit listed running jobs). Proposal: job start waits a settle window (default 2 s, --settle-ms n, 0 disables) for the supervisor's first state; if the job finished nonzero inside it, print 'job start failed id=... exit=n stderr=<first line>' and exit with the job's code; if it is still running or finished 0, print the ok receipt with settled_ms. Spec case: a program that exits 3 at once yields exit 3 and the stderr line; a sleeper yields ok.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_settle_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SETTLE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "eoie", "23:06 groom: draft job-start-settle\\job-start-settle.do exists (settle loop over the typed job_state, SettleHealthy/SettleFailed, --settle-ms flag, usage text). Known defect before it can be applied: in the Launched branch the old stdout_line receipt and 'if attached' line must be indented under the new '| SettleHealthy =>' arm; the draft leaves them at the outer level. Fix, then apply on a post-train copy and typecheck.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_settle_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SETTLE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T03:35Z", "eoie", "23:35 draft typecheck PASS (23:34, drafts-typecheck.ps1 with job-start-settle\\spec.json on the post-train tree, current compiler): eoie_job_launch launch.spi including a probe that reaches start_settle_of, and eoie_jobs model.spi with the new usage text. Two fixes on the way: the SettleHealthy arm indentation, and a nested pattern that needed parentheses (Settled (JobLost)). Ready to ride the train after P, or to join a later stage.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_settle_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SETTLE", 4u32, "2026-10-24T05:36Z", "eoie", "01:36 follow-up agreed with main (EOIE-JOB-EXPECT): one receipt for every early settle, 'eoie job start failed id=<id> state=<state_word> exit=<n> detail=\"<settled_detail>\"', using model.spi's vocabulary so job status/list and the settle receipt agree. R's SettleFailed widens from (exit, stderr line) to (word, exit, detail) read from the exit record. Lands in the next train with job-expect (a missed expectation then needs no special case: its exit record is nonzero). Tonight's R ships as tested.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_settle_5 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SETTLE", 5u32, "2026-10-27T22:53Z", "eoie", "18:5x eoie: state check on the installed kit. The settle ships: --settle-ms defaults to 2000, and 'kit job start settle-probe2 60000 cmd /c exit 7' printed 'kit job start failed id=... exit=7 stderr=' and exited 7 after 1.9 s. A pwsh -Command job that failed took 8.6 s to settle under load, so start said ok. That's expected: the window is 2 s. Not done: note 4's agreed single receipt. The live receipt still says exit=/stderr=, and stopped or timed-out jobs that settle inside the window are reported as ok (the match falls through to SettleHealthy). Draft: scratchpad clean\\job-settle\\settle-receipt.do, 4/4 on package copies. SettleFailed now carries job_settled * string, every Settled case reports, and Live alone is healthy. The receipt becomes 'kit job start failed id=<id> state=<settled_word> exit=<settled_exit>' plus ' detail=\"...\"' when there is one, the same shape as job status. The detail is the first stderr line for a finished job and state_detail otherwise; lost keeps its sentence. Exit codes come from settled_exit, so spawn-failed moves from 127 to 125, as job status reports it. The usage text in kit_jobs/model.spi follows. Nothing parses the old 'stderr=' receipt (grep of kit/src and i574n.github/scripts). Left: compile kit_job_launch on the node, then ride the release after TRAIN, then a CLI check that a failed start prints state=finished exit=7.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_settle_6 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SETTLE", 6u32, "2026-10-27T23:35Z", "eoie", "19:3x eoie: node regen (job-1793142539742): kit_job_launch goes 601 -> 714 lines with the one-shape receipt; the settled_word/settled_exit/state_detail functions now get emitted into that crate. kit_jobs and kit_job_start_args differ only by the usage text. 25 other job and window crates that reach kit_jobs regenerate byte-identical. Ready for the release after TRAIN.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_settle_7 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SETTLE", 7u32, "2026-10-29T15:37Z", "kit", "10-29 11:3x kit: INSTALLED and verified live. TRAIN3 published 11:35, kit.exe CD2F0D1A (was A5B55974); closeout script C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\train3-closeout.ps1, 7/7 pass. The same script was run on A5B55974 first as a control, and this check FAILED there. 'kit job start settle-probe 60000 cmd /c exit 7' exits 7 with the one-shape receipt 'kit job start failed id=... state=finished exit=7'; the old binary printed 'exit=7 stderr=' (note 5's open half). Side effect, handled: stop_cli's memory-cap test predated the settle and now passes --settle-ms 0. Done.")
inl note_of_kit_job_start_settle_8 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-START-SETTLE", 8u32, "2026-11-06T11:27Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-JOB-START-SETTLE (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_supervisor_no_console_1 () = Note ("KIT-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_lib_generic_audit_1 () = Note ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:44Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:44 main (user 2026-10-23 21:4x: 'also gotta check later if all lib code is really generic, not having products details etc'). Method: grep lib/spiral for product, repo and app names (dice, alphabet, tibia, x, nearblocks, near wallet and leptos ui components, fiscal/SEFAZ, eoie, user paths) and read each hit in place. Verdict per module: generic, generalize (name the generic shape), or move (name the target repo and its consumers). Done when every module has a verdict and every move or generalize has an item.")
inl note_of_lib_generic_audit_2 () = Note ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T04:50Z", "main", "2026-10-24 00:50 main: audit pass 1 over 107 lib .spi/.spir files, by name scan (tibia, otclient, arena, dice, alphabet, polyglot, fiscal, sefaz, nfe, twitter, oauth1, xsim, hangul, dota, eoie, i574n, fc1943s, near_wallet, nearblocks, sigenius, gerenciador), absolute-path scan (C:/Users, /home, AppData) and team words (lane, team main/x/tibia/eoie). Findings: (1) bench/parsing/bench_kit.spi benchmark command lines carry the checkout path c:/home/git/spiral (5) and a user path ~/... (1). (2) xsd.spi test schema uses an NFe id pattern. (3) file_system.spi:889 test asserts the current directory is named spiral or polyglot (a repo coupling). Kept as generic: oauth.spi's api.twitter.com URL is the documented OAuth 1.0a signing test vector; near/wallet is a reusable integration package for an external platform, not product code. No team or lane names in lib. Slice %TEMP%\\lib-generic-audit\\apply.ps1 fixes (1) and (2) with neutral text (c:/repo/..., user, ID[0-9]{44}); queued gate (xsd.spi) then land at the end of main's chain. (3) waits for x's normalize_path land on file_system.spi to avoid a concurrent edit; then the test should assert a property that holds in any checkout (current directory exists and is absolute).")
inl note_of_lib_generic_audit_3 () = Note ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", 3u32, "2026-10-24T05:00Z", "main", "2026-10-24 01:00 main: finding (3) folded into %TEMP%\\lib-fs-primitives\\apply.ps1 (x asked main to take it, so x's normalize_path program stays byte-identical to its proof). Both find_parent tests were coupled: fsharp find_parent asserted the parent is named spiral or polyglot, and rust find_parent_2 asserted spiral. Now both assert only that the workspace dir and README.md share one parent (am'.distinct length 1), and rust asserts a non-empty name. Lands after x's normalize_path land, with the fs primitives.")
inl note_of_lib_generic_audit_4 () = Note ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", 4u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl note_of_lib_generic_audit_5 () = Note ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", 5u32, "2026-10-31T21:00Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_lib_generic_audit_6 () = Note ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", 6u32, "2026-11-01T22:59Z", "main", "11-01 19:0x main (lib-reshape generic-code sweep, read-only). Eight lib moves filed from a sweep of kit, x, tibia, fiscal, dir-tree-html, dice and alphabet (.spi/.spir, generated and deps excluded), each with exact sites, lib target, typed shape and DoD in its first note: LIB-MOVE-UNIX-CLOCK (date_time), LIB-MOVE-CIVIL-DATE (date_time), LIB-MOVE-JSON-QUOTE (json; also folds lib's own 3 partial quoters), LIB-MOVE-READ-TEXT (file_system), LIB-MOVE-X-SIM-ERLANG (erlang_term/erlang_file/erlang_map/erlang_process), LIB-MOVE-CLI-PRELUDE (env/console/convert), LIB-MOVE-TEXT-HELPERS (sm'; also lib xsd_regex's private padded/count_text), LIB-MOVE-DTH-FS (file_system). Lib-internal duplicates found on the way (same audit scope): 5 private epoch reads (guid, trace, reactors, file_system, date_time zig) and 3 JSON quoters (json, json_schema, conversation), both covered by the items above. fiscal (src/ only; generated/ excluded), dice and alphabet had no host bindings or helpers duplicating lib.")
inl note_of_lib_generic_audit_7 () = Note ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", 7u32, "2026-11-02T22:13Z", "main", "main 11-02 18:1x AUDIT COMPLETE. (1) INTO lib: done in n6, with 8 LIB-MOVE-* items filed (2 Active, 6 Planned), each with sites and DoD. (2) OUT of lib (product/app/user specifics in lib/spiral): a scan for tibia, fiscal, verifactu, postab, tweet, dice, hangul, alphabet, kit_, eoie, mechahaze, near_, otclient, canary, x_compat, i574n, fc1943s found NO product code. The hits are substrings ('dice' in 'indices'), the NEAR binding modules (a generic chain SDK), physics (PHYSICS-OUT-OF-LIB, filed), and test fixtures only: xpath_eval.spi:1427-1428 (an XPath user-function test named for the codice fiscale), xsd_emit.spi:3318 (an assertion message citing a VeriFactu shape), iggy.spi:86/97/227/228 (the arbitrary tree name 'eoie' inside byte-frozen wire fixtures; renaming it means re-freezing the hex for no gain), file_system.spi:1946 (a temp folder name). Nothing to move. The follow-ups live in those 9 items.")
inl note_of_kit_consolidated_intake_1 () = Note ("KIT-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:28Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T02:35Z", "eoie", "22:45 user 22:1x: 'when i asked for a parent to eoie processes, i thought they would be naturally born from our claude process or the pwsh that we're using (bad, slow startup) instead of being forced to spawn from explorer'. Drift traced: EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT turned 'spin up with a parent' into the metric '0 dead parents' and met it with explorer, because the job host is one machine-wide singleton no session can own; memory then recorded explorer as the fix. Live trace 22:14: 52 processes anchored under explorer (the host, 20 supervisors, their job programs and conhosts), none under the session that started them.\nRule, written before drafting: a job's processes belong to the session that started it.\n(1) Owner = walk the calling eoie process's ancestors, skipping shells (cmd, pwsh, powershell, sh, bash, conhost) and eoie itself; the first other process is the owner, identified by pid + creation time. Generic names only (eoie is generic): the skip list is a typed union of launcher kinds, not a product name.\n(2) One host per owner: jobs/hosts/<owner-pid>-<owner-start-ms>/ {pid, requests/}; the host is created with PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_PARENT_PROCESS = the owner (OpenProcess 0x0080). The tree reads owner -> eoie host -> supervisors (DETACHED_PROCESS, EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE) -> job programs.\n(3) Owner exits: the host removes its requests dir (HomeGone), writes owner-ended, takes no new jobs, lets running supervisors finish, then exits; queued jobs it holds are settled as a typed OwnerEnded case (visible in job status and to waiters), never silently dropped.\n(4) Fallback only when the owner can't be opened: explorer, named in the start receipt (parent=fallback-shell).\n(5) Machine-wide coordination (windows, heavy slots, after links across owners) stays file-based and unchanged.\nDone = a live trace shows every eoie process under its owner session, 0 explorer anchors and 0 dead parents while owners live, plus a notebook test of the owner walk (shell skipping, pid reuse via creation time) and an after-install probe that starts a job from a nested shell and finds the host under the calling session.")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T02:58Z", "eoie", "22:58 draft owner-parent\\owner-parent.do (24 replacements over eoie_os_launch launch.spi, host_model, queue, host_run) applies on the post-train tree after supervisor-no-console. Typecheck 22:53: host_model PASS, including the new tests (owner walk skips shells and nested eoie, case-insensitive, pid+start home name, draining when the owner ends); host_run is link-only (E0433 eoie_os_launch). eoie_os_launch run.spi shows E0425, but an unmodified baseline shows the same E0425 (osl-baseline 22:57): module notebooks don't compile 'global' blocks. So the new Rust (eoie_parented_start, eoie_ancestry_text, eoie_process_started_ms) is NOT verified yet and needs the regen + cargo preview before release. Release order: after the train, after EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE; after-install probe: a job started from a nested shell finds its host under the calling session.")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 3u32, "2026-10-24T04:18Z", "eoie", "00:18 acceptance probe written: owner-parent\\after-install-owner.ps1. It derives the expected owner the way the code will (walk this shell's ancestors, skip launchers and eoie), starts a job in an isolated jobs home, and checks (1) one per-owner home named <owner-pid>-..., (2) the host's parent is the owner, (3) the job's supervisor is the host's child. Negative control on today's binary: 0/3, each for the right reason (expected owner claude.exe 51360 found; no hosts home; supervisor under an explorer-parented host). A cargo check of eoie-os-launch + eoie-job-host with the draft applied on the post-R tree is running (job-1792815425568); it compiles the new Windows code that notebooks skip.")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 4u32, "2026-10-24T04:34Z", "eoie", "00:34 the new Windows code compiles: regen + cargo check of eoie-os-launch and eoie-job-host with owner-parent.do on the post-R tree PASS (00:33, warnings only). It took the stage-L global fix (EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL note 4) for codegen to emit the launch Rust at all. Remaining: a release stage after R, then after-install-owner.ps1 (negative control 0/3 recorded).")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_5 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 5u32, "2026-10-24T08:21Z", "eoie", "04:2x eoie: under GRANULAR-HEAL (P0, user 10-24 via main), this draft cannot ship as written. owner-parent.do adds ~70 lines of Rust logic to eoie_os_launch's global block (eoie_process_started_ms, eoie_ancestry_text), and it edits the legacy eoie_shell_parented_start, which per AGENTS.md means migrating it in the same change. My audit of my own draft text (main's macro-rule regexes, scratch granular-audit.ps1) flags its parented_start call too (long, flow, cast). Rule, written before rework: S1 ships owner-parent only once the whole eoie_os_launch boundary is granular. (1) The global block keeps declarations only: repr(C) StartupInfo/StartupInfoEx/ProcessInfo/ToolhelpEntry, const empty values and named constants, and unsafe extern blocks whose items are declared safe fn (Rust 2024), so call sites need no unsafe blocks. (2) Each Win32 call is one named primitive of at most 120 chars, with pointers and out-cells passed as RefCell/Cell as_ptr primitives and no as-casts (convert in Spiral). (3) In Spiral: argument quoting, the flags x parent retry ladder (a union list), the shell-window fallback, FILETIME to unix ms, the toolhelp table and the ancestry walk. Unstable std raw_attribute (rustc 1.88 nightly, feature windows_process_extensions_raw_attribute) is not an option. Consumers call the Spiral functions through the package instead of eoie_os_launch:: Rust paths. Rework drafting starts now in scratch owner-parent-granular/.")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_6 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 6u32, "2026-10-24T11:17Z", "eoie", "07:1x groom: the granular rework is drafted (owner-parent-granular/: win32.spi boundary, launch.spi logic, run.spi exports; apply-os-launch-granular.ps1 runs inside S1 after owner-parent.do). The first typecheck caught 'usize' (no such Spiral type) and an unescaped cfg bang, both fixed; the retypecheck is in runner eoie-serial-checks-2. launch-S1 refuses until lib env.process_id and env.command_line_arguments_from land (gates: arguments_from PASS, process_id regate queued).")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_7 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 7u32, "2026-10-25T10:02Z", "eoie", "06:02 eoie: the 4 lib gate drafts are respelled for edition 3 (:~ ^ for a recursive let, :! for nominal, \\\\ lambdas, ? ?> |: conditionals, !+ for true; scratch lib-gaps-3\\env-arguments-and-pid.do and char-code-and-stdin.do, both dry-run clean on the live lib) and typechecked on a real-folder lib copy. env.command_line_arguments_from passes. env.process_id passes, after its test was parenthesized: '(process_id () > 0u64) |> _assert_eq !+'; without parentheses the comparison bound after the pipe and the test failed silently, with no Rust error. console.read_to_end passes. In env only two pre-existing F# environment tests fail. sm'.char_code/of_char: its Rust test failed on the installed 5j with E0507 (5j's move-on-last-use bug, as did 3 other sm' Rust tests), so it is being re-run on the 5k candidate. Next: land all four through one eoie do program plus a lib gate job once heavy capacity allows, then S1 (this item) can go after SLATE.")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_8 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 8u32, "2026-10-25T11:18Z", "eoie", "07:18 eoie: all 4 edition-3 lib drafts pass on a real-folder lib copy with the 5k candidate: env.command_line_arguments_from, env.process_id, console.read_to_end, and sm'.char_code/of_char (one-test probe, since the full sm' notebook timed out under machine load). Bug found on the way: of_char's Rust arm (!input).to_string() returned 65 for 'A', because a Spiral char reaches Rust as a numeric code unit. Fixed with char::from(!input).to_string(), still one expression; the draft is updated (scratch lib-gaps-3\\char-code-and-stdin.do). Landing waits until after SLATE: their consumers (S1 here, and EOIE-GAP-INTAKE) are post-SLATE work, and a lib edit now would land under main's 5l' review and the SLATE full measure, both of which read the tree lib (cells-reads-live-tree).")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_9 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 9u32, "2026-10-27T14:46Z", "kit", "10:4x kit: state re-read. (1) None of the four lib drafts ever landed (env.command_line_arguments_from, env.process_id, console.read_to_end, sm'.char_code/of_char are all absent from lib/spiral). Their drafts (lib-gaps-3/*.do, edition 3, proven 10-25 on a 5k lib copy) had been archived with the old scratch folders and are restored to the kit lane scratchpad's clean/restored/lib-gaps-3. They land through lib-gate-kit land.ps1 in a quiet window, not during a release that reads the live lib. (2) The kit-side owner-parent.do (24 replacements) is restored to clean/restored/owner-parent, with after-install-owner.ps1 (the 3-check acceptance probe). Renamed eoie_ to kit_ and dry-run on a fresh copy of the current tree: 3 of 4 edits fail on edition-2 anchors (union host_step, let held_recorded, let host_home_directory), because the tree was respelled to edition 3 after the draft. Next: rebase the 24 replacements to edition-3 anchors (kit_os_launch, kit_job_host host_model, queue, host_run), then prove on a probe copy with the acceptance probe against a candidate build. Progress stays at 60%.")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_10 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 10u32, "2026-10-27T15:16Z", "kit", "11:1x kit: kit-side rebase done on a probe copy and staged (clean/kit-rename/job-owner-parent, 7 files with base hashes, applied by staged-apply.ps1 after the SLICES2 release). It is not a 1:1 port of the 10-24 draft. (1) The edition-2 anchors were rewritten for edition 3; the recursive union is spelled ':| ^ ancestry'. (2) host_run had moved on since the draft (queue_decide through the kit_job_queue crate), so the host_run section was written against the current code. (3) A straight port put host_run.rs at 1,340 lines, so the owner model (ancestry parse, launcher kinds, nearest-owner walk, home name) moved into kit_os_launch as a new granular module, owner.spi, with its test block. That package already produces the ancestry text (kit_ancestry_text) and process start time (kit_process_started_ms). It exports two scalar calls, kit_caller_owner () -> (home, pid, started_ms) and kit_owner_present (pid, started) -> bool. host_run carries the owner as two u64 (pid, start); a held job's owner home is read from the job's own host-home file, which the requester already writes, so no new files appear in the held folder. Sizes: host_run.rs 977 (was 731), os_launch.rs 590 (was 178). Behavior kept from the draft: one host home per owner (hosts/<pid>-<start>), the host started as the owner's child through kit_parented_start (falling back to a detached start), HostDraining when the owner ends (running children finish, held jobs of that owner are stopped with 'its owner session ended before the job started'), and SupervisorHeld reporting host_home. The launcher check now matches 'kit' (it was 'eoie'). Not yet proven: the 3-check acceptance probe (restored after-install-owner.ps1) against a candidate build; that runs in the release after the stage lands.")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_11 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 11u32, "2026-10-27T21:10Z", "kit", "kit 17:1x: the owner-parent stage rides TRAIN (next-train step 4, applied live 15:48), and its acceptance check is wired into post-install-TRAIN.ps1: scratch clean/respell/owner-parent-check.ps1, ported from the 10-26 after-install-owner.ps1 to kit names (KIT_JOBS_HOME, -Kit, skipping kit* in the owner walk). It runs one ping job in a fixture jobs home and asserts (1) exactly one host home named <owner pid>-<start>, (2) the host process's parent is the owner, (3) the supervisor's parent is that host. Negative control on today's installed kit.exe: 0/3. No per-owner home is created, and the supervisor's parent is the shared host (71316), not this session's claude.exe (102168, correctly found as the owner). So the check fails through the bug's own path, and after install it must be 3/3. The four lib drafts (clean/restored/lib-gaps-3) re-verified at 15:06 on today's lib; they land after the TRAIN window closes (TRAIN does not depend on them).")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_12 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 12u32, "2026-10-28T09:35Z", "eoie", "05:3x kit: owner-parent check on the installed TRAIN2c binary is 3/3. The home is keyed to the owner (claude.exe 102168), the host's parent is that owner, and the supervisor's parent is the host. Left: the 4 lib drafts land in today's lib window.")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_13 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 13u32, "2026-10-28T09:46Z", "eoie", "05:4x kit: this item's lib programs landed live in today's lib window. Gates are running on the node (P2 compiler pinned): see EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN's 05:4x note. The kit-side half follows in the next release.")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_14 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 14u32, "2026-10-28T11:55Z", "eoie", "07:5x kit: Done. The kit half shipped in TRAIN2c (kit.exe A5B55974) and is verified live: owner-parent check 3/3 (post-install job-1793179869223). The 4 lib drafts landed live in today's lib window, 05:36-05:44 (char-code-and-stdin: sm'.char_code/of_char and console.read_to_end; env-arguments-and-pid: env.command_line_arguments_from and env.process_id). Their gates: console 3/3 on the node; env 9/9 locally (the 3 node env reds are typescript and fsharp toolchain gaps there); sm' char_code/of_char not among the sm' failures. Lib window record: EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN note 175.")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_15 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 15u32, "2026-11-02T11:51Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 07:5x REGRESSION + FIX. On 11-01 21:21 kit removed CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB from kit_os_launch's launcher (tries 0x0900_0220 dropped) for start-under-shell's 'belongs to its session' fix; the same launcher started job hosts and supervisors, and TRAIN10 shipped it. Effect on the node: over ssh, when no host is alive, kit job start put the host or supervisor INSIDE the ssh session's job object, and Windows killed it when the ssh command returned: every job longer than ~2 s was 'lost' exit 125 (compiler's apos-b, regex-bench9/9b, from ~07:15 when the last live eoie host idle-exited). Reproduced with an isolated KIT_JOBS_HOME: lost at +2189 ms. FIX (unstaged, src/kit_os_launch/launch.spi): kit_launch(..., breakaway) body; kit_parented_start = no breakaway (start-under-shell, Cells daemons: die with their session, unchanged); new kit_job_launch = breakaway first, then without (job host and supervisor via kit_shell_started/kit_owner_started), so a host still has its owner as parent but drains on owner end instead of being killed with the session's job. PROOF: node candidate 1F774B05 (job-1793619870121-kit-breakaway, regen 2 owners) ran the same ssh repro: finished exit 0 in 8058 ms, host alive after the session. Ships in TRAIN11 (job-1793620210905, launched 07:50). RULE: a parent pid and a job object are separate; the 'session owns its processes' rule is about the parent; job hosts must break away from short-lived shells' job objects.")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_16 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 16u32, "2026-11-02T13:22Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 09:1x HOOK STALL HEALED (tooling, not this item's code, but the same train day). TRAIN11's regen leased 375 dirs as 375 files; ~/.claude/hooks/kit-tree-lease.ps1 did Get-Content + Get-Process per lease on every Edit/Write/PowerShell call, exceeded its 45 s timeout on the saturated box, and the shell-string hook form counts a timeout as a block: ~40 min with no commands. FIX: (1) hook fast path: a command with no write verb (after heredoc / null-redirect stripping) or an edit with no target exits before reading leases; one [Diagnostics.Process]::GetProcesses() snapshot; ReadAllLines; multi-root leases (many root= lines); (2) settings.json: exec form (cmd.exe + args, same if-exist shortcut) with onFailure continue; (3) compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 writes ONE regeneration-<pid>.lease with all roots. TESTS: tree-lease-hook-test.ps1 15/15 OK; multi-root probe 3/3; with the 375 live leases: read-only 6.4 s, live edit allowed 7.2 s, snapshot edit and snapshot write both still blocked (exit 2), 10-15 s (pwsh start on a saturated box is the floor).")
inl note_of_kit_job_owner_parent_17 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", 17u32, "2026-11-06T11:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_ge2_crate_splits_1 () = Note ("KIT-GE2-CRATE-SPLITS", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-GE2-CRATE-SPLITS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T03:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:37 main: repro from x in %TEMP%\\x-gleam-regexp-probe (BuildSpiral -Backend Gleam). probe_arms: the dead Rust arm calls an inl that emits global import, no leak. probe_vc: the dead Rust arm calls the let file_system.get_current_directory, which leaks gleam/erlang/charlist plus file_system_gleam_stat's helpers. probe_vd: the let file_system.normalize_path leaks gleam/option and gleam/regexp (not in Cells' Gleam deps, so gleam build fails). probe_va leaks all three; probe_vb (trivial Rust arm) leaks none. The generated get_full_path_0 body is clean, so it looks like a let's specialization (with its globals) memoized while a dead arm was evaluated speculatively. Milestones: locate where top-level let specializations register globals during backend_switch / run_target arm evaluation 90 m; make globals belong to the emitted specialization only (collect per join point, keep the ones reached from the live program) 120 m; minimal regression sample from probe_vd 30 m; pack review 60 m. x's lib side (normalize_path without regex) is a separate workaround that x lands.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T04:32Z", "main", "2026-10-24 00:32 main: cause and fix. In the BackendSwitch op, a non-current arm is validated in an env rebuilt as {s with seq; cse; backend; i} that kept s.globals, so everything the dead arm recorded went into the live scope's globals list. That includes the bsv and key markers of let callees specialized there and the globals of their own nested arm validations. Single-flight registers a bsv marker via key_body first, and the rebuild keeps its child list. Fix (slice %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\dead-arm-globals.ps1, 3 sites: 2 parallel-core, 1 single-flight): the validation env gets globals=ResizeArray(), so the validated arm's globals are discarded; the bsv marker stays, with no children, and a join point first specialized inside a dead arm keeps its own child list under its key marker, so a later live KeyHit still brings its globals. Regression sample (staged; review-4 installs it into tmp/samples before its run): gleam_dead_arm_let_globals, core only, a let gleam_marked whose Gleam arm emits global 'import spiral_dead_arm_marker', called only from picked's Rust arm. Positive control on the installed compiler (job main-dead-arm-positive-control-8): the Gleam output contains the marker import, so gleam build would fail on an unknown module; the Rust output does not. Expected after the fix: Gleam builds and exits 6. In pack 5g with the Rust self-rebind clone fix, Bend balanced if-heads and segment-head-relaid; review job main-pack-next5g-review-4.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 3u32, "2026-10-24T09:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 05:37 main: pack 5g review-4 (core 64902F6956ECA45B; run single-flight-20261024-043256 vs 20261022-184215) is NOT acceptable. The automatic part passed (formatter corpus, DISAGREE 0; Bend build-fail -> ran across many rows; record_with_expected_base and portable_math_rounding fixed), but 5 Gleam rows regress from native ran to build-fail with \"Unknown module\" in 10-48 ms: mega_brzozowski_derivatives indexed_dfa_state_cardinality, inventory_closed_dfa_runtime, runtime_bench, runtime_native, and mega_omniledger_erp_kernel runtime_native. 447 Gleam residuals changed and 440 still run, so a few large programs lose an import or module reference; the dead-arm-globals slice is the prime suspect (it changes which globals the validation env keeps). Probe queued: main-pack5g-gleam-probe (job-1792834615254) compiles indexed_dfa_state_cardinality on Gleam with the installed and candidate compilers and diffs their imports; the sample folder is restored from a snapshot afterwards.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 4u32, "2026-10-24T09:39Z", "main", "2026-10-24 05:39 main: hypothesis, written before the probe result: the globals index is per list (GlobalsIndex.get s.globals), so de-duplication does not leak between the arm env and the live env. What does leak is evaluation memoized by join points and type join points: a join point (or type join point) first evaluated while validating a NON-current arm records its body once, its global emissions go to the discarded list, and later live uses hit the cache without replaying those globals. Before the slice, the leak into the live list masked this. Fix options, in order of preference: (a) cache entries keep the globals their first evaluation emitted and replay them into the caller env on a hit; (b) non-current arm validation uses a separate join point cache. The probe decides whether an import is what goes missing.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 5u32, "2026-10-24T09:52Z", "main", "2026-10-24 05:52 main: probe result (main-pack5g-gleam-probe, outputs in %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\gleam-probe): installed main.gleam has 1 import (gleam/int) and builds; the candidate main.gleam has 3 imports (main_types, main_part1, main_part2) and LOSES `import gleam/int`, so the build stops with Unknown module. The global is emitted while something is first evaluated in a context whose globals are now discarded, and later live uses do not re-emit it: the memo hypothesis of note 4. Decision by the review rule: pack 5g is not accepted as is. Next (compiler, main): find where the join point / type join point caches store their first evaluation (the top-level join_point_method / join_point_closure dictionaries, PartEvalSeams), record the globals that evaluation emitted with the cache entry and replay them into the caller env on a hit. Positive controls: this sample on Gleam (must import gleam/int again) and x's gleam_dead_arm_let_globals (must keep the dead arm's globals out). Then re-run review-4 for 5g' (5g + the replay).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 6u32, "2026-10-24T09:57Z", "main", "2026-10-24 05:57 main: mechanism found by reading the 5g core (BackendSwitch, ~line 1611xx in %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\spiral_compiler.fs). Validating a non-current arm (say Fsharp on a Gleam build) reaches nested backend_switches whose GLEAM arm is then validated as non-current with backend=Gleam. Join points evaluated there fill the (Gleam, body) method cache (JpBodyOwnerIdentity includes backend'), and their `global \"import gleam/int\"` lands in the slice's throwaway list. The live Gleam compile later hits that cache entry and never re-emits the import, hence Unknown module. Before the slice, the leak into the live list hid this. Fix design (one rule, no replay machinery): a validation env keeps the LIVE root globals when the arm's backend equals the build's target backend, and a throwaway list otherwise. Implementation: a ThreadLocal root (backend, globals) recorded when RustGapTrace.validating goes 0 -> 1, then `globals = if backend = root backend then root globals else ResizeArray()` in the 3 validation envs (single-flight + 2 parallel). Positive controls: gleam_dead_arm_let_globals (Rust/C target, so the Gleam marker stays out) and mega_brzozowski indexed_dfa_state_cardinality on Gleam (gleam/int is back). The cost is a possible unused target-language import, which Gleam only warns about.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 7u32, "2026-10-24T15:26Z", "main", "main 11:3x: implemented note 6 as pack 5h, plus a second leak found while reading the parallel core. (a) note 6: each peval keeps validation_root_globals (set when its root LangEnv is built); a backend_switch validation env uses the live root globals when its arm backend = env.backend (the target) and a fresh list otherwise, so a nested target-backend arm validated under a dead arm (the 5g Gleam case) emits import gleam/int into the live program. (b) parallel cores add a global only if s.globals_seen.TryAdd succeeds (spiral_compiler.fs ~128580); 5g swapped globals to a throwaway list but kept the LIVE globals_seen, so a global first emitted in a dead-arm validation was marked seen and then skipped by the live emission. 5h gives non-target validation envs their own (globals, globals_lock, globals_seen) triple. Slice %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\dead-arm-root-globals.ps1 replaces dead-arm-globals.ps1; %TEMP%\\pack-next5h\\compose.ps1 composes it on pack-next9 (core 02815A83BA3AEA8B). Queued job main-serial-pack5h: parse both defines, build candidate, positive control gleam_dead_arm_let_globals on Rust and C (marker must stay out), decisive mega_brzozowski Gleam probe (import gleam/int must be back). Full matrix review only after both pass.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 8u32, "2026-10-24T15:42Z", "main", "main 12:0x: correction to note 7: the first 5h compose (02815A83BA3AEA8B) was corrupted (the slice's Swap helper printed its status into the returned text, so line 1 of the core held status lines; the queued parse check would have refused it). Fixed (status to Out-Host) and recomposed before the build took its slot: pack-next5h core F3DFDD955559E904, line 1 #nowarn 40, validation_root_globals at 7 sites (2 declarations, 2 root sets, 3 validation envs).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 9u32, "2026-10-24T16:41Z", "main", "main 12:4x: pack 5h build+probe (12:13): candidate built (306 s); positive control gleam_dead_arm_let_globals keeps the dead Gleam arm's marker out of Rust and C; the mega Gleam row still reported build-fail Unknown module, BUT the cause is the matrix harness, not the core: 5h's output for that row is split (main.gleam + main_types/main_part1/main_part2.gleam, the pack-next7 Gleam module split), main_part1 now DOES import gleam/int (the note-6 fix works), and the 4 files build and run in a scratch Gleam project. test.ps1's Gleam native tier copied only main.gleam: the batch renames it spiral_row_NNNN and the per-row path copies it alone, so main_types was unknown. Fixed in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1 (uncommitted, for the user): Gleam rows with .gleam parts skip the batch (every split row has a module named main_types) and the per-row path clears stale main_*.gleam parts and copies the row's parts beside main.gleam. 5g's 5 Gleam mega build-fails were probably the same harness gap on top of the real lost-import bug. Re-probe queued (main-serial-pack5h-probe-2); then the full matrix review of 5h.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_10 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 10u32, "2026-10-24T20:07Z", "main", "main 15:5x: re-probe with the test.ps1 Gleam split-part fix (main-pack5h-gleam-probe-2 rerun 15:38-15:42): mega_brzozowski indexed_dfa_state_cardinality on Gleam builds and runs exit 0 with BOTH the installed and the 5h candidate (DISAGREE 0); the runner's exit 1 is the probe script's robocopy restore code. Before the full review I found 5h would revert a tree change: pack-next9's base predates the 10-24 09:25 fmt-next install (arm-lambda-opener, FORMATTER-LAMBDA-NEXT-LINE); the tree's other later install (pack-fmt-only) only spliced 5f's Formatter section, which next9 already holds. Pack 5i (%TEMP%\\pack-next5i\\compose.ps1) = 5h + arm-lambda-opener + the pack-spelling slices (format --spelling verb, tight-first respelling); its Formatter section equals the tree's except 5h's own segment-head-relaid. Full review queued: main-serial-pack5i-review (%TEMP%\\pack-next4\\review-5i.ps1: build, formatter gate, matrix -Suite all -Native, output rules). Acceptance by the review rule, then install.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_11 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 11u32, "2026-10-24T21:41Z", "main", "main 17:4x: pack 5i review done (main-serial-pack5i-review, 16:06-17:13, exit 0). Formatter candidate gate, automatic part: PASS. Matrix run single-flight-20261024-164057: native 6,833 ran, 9 build-fail, oracle agree 6,106, DISAGREE 0. Against the 10-22 reference run single-flight-20261022-184215: 115 behavior cells changed (not residual), DISAGREE on the right 0. Visible so far: Bend rows going from build-fail to ran and agreeing (array_i64, array_i64_at1, array_u64, closure_pair_i64, ...), and gadt_growing_index_runtime C/F# oracle WRONG-ERROR -> blank (the tree's 10-23 GADT type-check hang; noted for 5g, not caused by this pack). The output-rule text checks compare against an older snapshot and are informational per the rule text. The candidate is recorded in %TEMP%\\pack-next5i\\dll.txt. By the accept rule, install only after every one of the 115 cells is reviewed and explained (compare-runs -Show all), then the determinism check: next block's first job. 5i also carries the format --spelling verb, so installing it lets the edition-3 apply use the installed compiler.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_12 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 12u32, "2026-10-24T21:43Z", "main", "main 17:4x: all 115 changed cells of the 5i review are reviewed and explained, with no REGRESSED or DISAGREE. (1) Bend: 12 samples build-fail -> ran, oracle agree (balanced if-heads), including native_closure_union_rec exit 70 and printf_portable with stdout. (2) Rust: the 5 mega runtime rows (brzozowski indexed_dfa_state_cardinality, inventory_closed_dfa_runtime, runtime_bench, runtime_native; omniledger runtime_native) and typecheck_long_let_body (exit 236) go build-fail -> ran, agree (self-rebind clones; this is the BACKENDS-SOTA 'Rust 3 of 8 mega' gap). (3) record_with_expected_base: compile error -> ok on F#, C, Rust and Delphi, agree (C: no-zig). (4) portable_math_rounding: Lean, Gleam and Lua compile timeout -> ok, agree. (5) gleam_dead_arm_let_globals: new rows (the regression sample). (6) gadt_growing_index_runtime: oracle WRONG-ERROR -> blank on 4 backends, the tree's 10-23 type-check hang (5g review-4 note), not this pack. Still needed before install: the hopacsota-side run of 5i (both cores DISAGREE 0) and the determinism check, then install at a block start when no gate reads the installed compiler (fiscal cte-os-3, the edition-3 proof and the lib gates are running now).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_13 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 13u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:45 groom: 75%. Pack 5i (5h + arm-lambda-opener + spelling verb) single-flight review exit 0, DISAGREE 0, 115 cells explained. Left: read the hopacsota-side run (main-serial-pack5i-hopacsota), run the determinism check, install at a block start.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_14 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 14u32, "2026-10-25T00:06Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:06 main: the install vehicle is now pack 5j = 5i + no-gleam-parts.ps1 (see GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES): 5i's Gleam module split drops '.{...}' imports from parts. The slice changes only the '.gleam' branch of build. So the 5j review rule: build 5j, rerun the Gleam rows whose 5i run wrote main.spiral-parts, and require each to agree with 5i's stdout as one file; every other row's output is unchanged by construction. Then the determinism check and install as before. The running 5i hopacsota review stays valid for every non-Gleam-split row.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_15 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 15u32, "2026-10-25T00:18Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:21 main (corrected; x found the error): 5j removes both output splits (Rust and Gleam; see GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES). Install-step rules: (1) Compose and build 5j, then rerun the 6 samples whose committed outputs are split: mega_brzozowski_derivatives/{indexed_dfa_state_cardinality, inventory_closed_dfa_runtime, runtime_bench, runtime_native}, mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/runtime_native, typecheck_long_let_body. Each Rust and Gleam row must agree with 5i's stdout as one file. Re-accept those rows by the accept rule (single-flight review, every changed row explained, DISAGREE 0, an accept run that reproduces the review). (2) The 29 split files are TRACKED (user commit 0ec501fb, 10-24 05:02 'tests'), not untracked as the first version of this note said: 6 main.spiral-parts, 6 main_part1.rs, the Gleam parts and main_types.gleam (git ls-files lists exactly these). By the samples rule (one committed copy of each output, the compiler's output replacing the previous one), they become stale once 5j writes single files. After the accept run, delete exactly those 29 from the working tree and leave the deletions unstaged for the user. (3) At install, apply host-no-parts.ps1 and revert test.ps1's parts hunks (Get-OutputParts, the compile-memo .parts dir, partsKey, the main_*.gleam copy) back to HEAD's behavior. Get-OutputParts also caught 451 Python main_auto.py files (the 'Python + Cuda' multiFile output), which HEAD never collected; that is MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO's question, not 5j's. (4) Message x, tibia and eoie before touching the core or the host. The install waits for eoie CLEAN's 'lib-free:' (note 16).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_16 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 16u32, "2026-10-25T00:19Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:19 main: install timing rule from eoie. CLEAN's regen, candidate notebooks and spiral tests all run on the installed SpiralCompiler.dll and finish before CLEAN prints 'lib-free:'. So the 5j install waits for that line, the same hold as the edition-3 land. A compiler-install window taken before CLEAN launches makes CLEAN wait instead. 5j isn't built or reviewed yet, so the window isn't taken: the install happens after lib-free.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_17 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 17u32, "2026-10-25T04:40Z", "main", "2026-10-25 00:40 main: 5j REVIEW GREEN (job-1792900139397, 00:29, exit 0). Composite 8E893BAAE47D1D2A (5i plus no-output-parts, test-header-words and test-symbol-lexer; lint G1/G2 clean); reviewed SpiralCompilerCore BA0949F4EE7399DE. DISAGREE 0; native results equal next3 row for row; formatter gate PASS. Every text-check count equals 5i's except Gleam 'beyond locals' 29 -> 23: the 6 split samples are single files again, as planned. The candidate is recorded in pack-next5j\\dll.txt. install-5j.ps1 dry run is OK: base tree core BEECFE432427C57A (the 09:25 install), and the post-build hash check against the reviewed core is the determinism check. The host-no-parts slice and the test.ps1 revert stay out of this install, because they'd change the reviewed build; they go in the next pack. Waiting on x, tibia and eoie for a quiet window, then: install, accept run, then delete the 29 tracked part files.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_18 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 18u32, "2026-10-25T05:06Z", "main", "2026-10-25 01:06 main: 5j INSTALLED (job-1792904479028, window win-1792904480020; tree core 8E893BAAE47D1D2A, single-flight core 2CC30D887CF85F24, hopacsota core A2C4D1FAAB168E4A). The file-hash check said 'differs from the reviewed BA0949F4EE7399DE' (exit 3). The analysis: both DLLs are 12,128,256 bytes, with 53 differing bytes in 7 runs, all inside the PDB identity records (debug directory stamps @184776, the CodeView PDB GUID @987592, PdbChecksum @987736). The MVID is IDENTICAL (4bf3840d-2864-620b-bdb6-1c29a7c317ea), and in a deterministic build that's the content hash of metadata and IL, so the installed code is the reviewed code. Likely cause: a source path in the PDB (candidate cache vs tree). install-5j.ps1 now accepts an equal MVID with PDB-only differences. Next: tell the lanes, then the accept run (6 single-file samples), then delete the 29 tracked part files (left unstaged for the user).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_19 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 19u32, "2026-10-25T07:18Z", "main", "10-25 03:18 main: 5j ACCEPT done (job-1792904859558, run single-flight-20261025-010749): native 6833 ran, DISAGREE 0, 7352 rows. Matches review single-flight-20261025-000845 row for row except 1 cell: mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals Fsharp exit, review 1 vs accept timeout (a load timeout on the F# tier). Both the review and the 10-20 baseline say 1, so EXPECTED.tsv keeps the reviewed 1 (raw accept kept as EXPECTED.tsv.accept-5j-raw; new hash 77512DAEA5CC1050). Part files: only 6 tracked main_part1.rs were left (no main.rs references them, and git grep finds no consumer outside samples, with a positive control), so I deleted them, unstaged (copies in %TEMP%\\pack-next5j\\deleted-parts). The '29' in n18 counted Gleam parts too, and those are no longer tracked.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_20 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 20u32, "2026-10-25T08:22Z", "main", "10-25 04:22 main: 5k REVIEW done (job-1792908855610, run single-flight-20261025-032812, core 3DC18876AFBE138E, reviewed SpiralCompilerCore 75272FE5C7F5443F). Formatter gate PASS on user/lib/synthetic/dice/eoie (0 files differ). Against today's 5j accept (single-flight-20261025-010749), row for row: 7352 rows, 0 missing, 209 differing cells = 208 Rust residuals (the closure guard: functions that hold a closure keep the clone instead of moving, as designed for E0507) + the F# mega_omniledger all_terminals load-timeout cell flipping back to the baseline's 1; 0 behavior cells otherwise, DISAGREE 0. Install queued: job-1792916161438 (install-5k.ps1 = install-5j.ps1 with base 8E893BAAE47D1D2A, composite 3DC18876AFBE138E, MVID-equal acceptance). Then: tell eoie (holds SLATE/publishes on 5k), x, tibia.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_21 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 21u32, "2026-10-25T10:34Z", "main", "10-25 06:34 main: PACK 5l composed: %TEMP%\\pack-next5l\\spiral_compiler.fs = the 5k core 3DC18876 + test-header-symbol (stage 3, FE738C3B) + tibia's LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS pair (lua-do-blocks + lua-spill-locals, 375E1C9A) + tibia's C-PRIM-TO-STRING (!!!!PrimToString for C) = composite 04823272F73F534A. Each slice applied with all its anchors matching once, in sequence, from pack-local copies. Review: job-1792924381126 (review-5l.ps1), after the 5k install: the pack-next4 matrix review, then the lib Lua check. The check runs the 12 lib files that have ///> lua tests on the candidate and reruns red files on the installed compiler; only candidate-only reds fail. After install: gate tibia's lib-obj-to-string-c.ps1 (sm'.obj_to_string C arm), which needs PrimToString.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_22 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 22u32, "2026-10-25T11:02Z", "main", "10-25 07:02 main: the 5k install (job-1792916161438) REFUSED by its own base check: the tree core is 17D970FE7D26D27B, not 5j's 8E893BAA. x's x-compat change (xCompatDefusedAt/xCompatPostedLink, xCompatTopLevelBlocks rewritten; 38+/11-, written 05:50, outside codegen) was installed into the tree meanwhile. That skipped the 5l review (after-ok). Decision: no separate 5k rebase plus re-review. 5l is recomposed on the tree core: 17D970FE + the 5k closure guard (4B67A297) + stage 3 + LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS + C-PRIM-TO-STRING = 5l' 0C48169521E5384F. Its diff from the first 5l is exactly x's delta. One review (job-1792926015134) and one install then cover 5k and 5l.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_23 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 23u32, "2026-10-25T14:30Z", "main", "10-25 10:30 main: 5l' REVIEW (run single-flight-20261025-081136, composite 0C48169521E5384F) vs today's 5j accept: 7352 rows, DISAGREE 0. 442 differing cells: Lua residual 229, Rust residual 208 (the 5k guard), the F# all_terminals load-timeout cell, and ONE behavior regression: samples/mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/runtime_native Lua ran/agree -> build-fail 'main.lua:3196: main function has more than 200 local variables' (LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS: the do-block temporaries land in main's frame and the spill doesn't cover main). Not installed. Decision (keep SLATE unblocked, tibia's pair returns when fixed): 5l'' = tree 17D970FE + 5k guard + stage 3 + C-PRIM-TO-STRING = 34519B923366CCEC; review job-1792938510698 (lib Lua check skipped, since Lua codegen is unchanged). Stopped the 5l' review during its lib Lua check (samples already restored; only logs written after that). tibia and kit told.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_24 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 24u32, "2026-10-25T14:52Z", "main", "10-25 10:52 main: pack 5m staged (%TEMP%\\pack-next5m). tibia's fixed LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS pair: the do-blocks emit bare 'local vN' at chunk level; spillChunkLocals counted only 'local x =' lines, so chunk main never hit its trigger. Fix: count bare declarations, and drop a spilled bare declaration after the rename. tibia's chunk-spill-check reproduces 'more than 200 local variables' with the old regex and runs clean with the fix. The pair dry-runs clean on the 5l'' composite. 5m = installed 5l'' core + the pair + HOPACSOTA-P2's published-ready.ps1 (5/5 on 5l'). Review requirements: the omniledger runtime_native Lua row back to ran/agree, and the lib Lua check (12 files) on the candidate.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_25 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 25u32, "2026-10-25T17:50Z", "main", "10-25 13:50 main: 5l'' REVIEW done (job-1792938510698, run single-flight-20261025-123908, composite 34519B923366CCEC, reviewed SpiralCompilerCore CDD68DC3EE829A67): DISAGREE 0. Outputs changed: Rust 208 beyond let mut, the same 208 the 5k review attributed to the closure guard (n20). C, Zig, Python, Kotlin, Swift, TS, Lean, Gleam, Bend, Lua, Wasm and Pascal changed 0 beyond their known rewrites. So every changed row is explained. Install queued: job-1792950541607 (install-5k.ps1 -Base 17D970FE7D26D27B -Composite 34519B923366CCEC -CoreDir pack-next5l -Wait until-granted). Fixes to the install script, found while queueing: (1) its 120-minute window wait would expire behind x's 250-minute compile window, so it now takes -Wait; (2) it checked the base core only BEFORE the wait, so an edit by another lane during a long wait would have been overwritten; it now re-checks after the grant and refuses if the core moved. x and kit told.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_26 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 26u32, "2026-10-25T18:20Z", "main", "10-25 14:20 main: 5l'' INSTALLED (job-1792950541607, window granted after x released its compile window and kit's SLATE gate passed). Tree core 34519B923366CCEC; single-flight SpiralCompilerCore CDD68DC3EE829A67 = the reviewed candidate; MVID 7fd00bff-ba14-fc03-1b62-5a896ccd06ed (kit's SLATE trigger); hopacsota core 7CECE03C8FB4BB12. kit, x and tibia told. Next: pack 5m composed over 34519B92: tibia's fixed LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS pair, HOPACSOTA published-ready, the citation-comment strip (strip-doc-citations.ps1). Gate: the omniledger runtime_native Lua row ran/agree, plus the lib Lua check. Per memory recompile-drafts-after-compiler-change, the queued lib gates and heals now run on the new compiler; their verdicts already target it.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_27 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 27u32, "2026-10-25T19:06Z", "main", "10-25 15:06 main: pack 5m COMPOSED: %TEMP%\\pack-next5m\\spiral_compiler.fs A29F241986A441F3 = installed 5l'' core 34519B92 + HOPACSOTA published-ready (lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/published-ready.ps1, all 6 anchors matched once) + tibia's LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS pair (lua-function-limits.ps1) + strip-doc-citations (24 blocks, 86 comment lines). pair-drift: 0 pairs touched on one side only. Review script review-5m.ps1 re-enables the lib Lua check (every lib .spi with a ///> lua target, candidate vs installed) and reads the omniledger runtime_native Lua row (5l'': ran/agree; it must stay ran/agree). Toolchain change, rule written before the run: fiscal moved this machine's fpc to trunk 3.3.1-21298 today, so Delphi native rows rerun on a new fpc and test.ps1 will print a toolchain diff. A Delphi row that differs from the 5j accept is attributed to the pack only if it also differs from a same-fpc rerun of the installed 5l'' compiler (test.ps1 -Backend Delphi with the installed DLL). Otherwise it's the toolchain, and the next accept takes -AcceptToolchain after its own review. Rows of every other backend gate as before (DISAGREE 0, every changed residual explained).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_28 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 28u32, "2026-10-25T22:09Z", "main", "10-25 18:09 main: pack 5n staging opened (%TEMP%\\pack-next5n), to compose over whatever 5m installs. Staged so far: fiscal's DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX (delphi-literal-index.py B780414AA124; a StringIndex on a one-character literal emitted 'z'[i + 1], a Pascal Char constant that cannot be indexed, so the build broke; the new arm wraps literal operands in AnsiString(...), and variable operands keep their text, so existing Delphi rows should stay byte-identical) and DELPHI-USES-UNITS (delphi-uses-units.py 1990FA92D7BA; a global 'uses X' folds into the one uses clause). Each brings one regression sample when packed (a one-char-literal index; a StrUtils call). Compiler-lane slices join 5n per the lane split (seam: anchored apply script + composite hash on a stated base).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_29 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 29u32, "2026-10-25T22:28Z", "main", "10-25 18:28 main: pack 5n regression samples written (per regression-samples-allowed: one minimal sample per fixed compiler bug), staged in %TEMP%\\pack-next5n\\samples. delphi_literal_index: StringIndex on the literal z at a dynamic index; exits 42 when the char is z, so C (the oracle) and Delphi must both give 42. Before the fix, fpc fails with a Char-constant index. delphi_uses_units: a Delphi BackendSwitch arm declares global 'uses StrUtils;' and calls IfThen(ReverseString(text) = 'cba', 7, 1); the C arm returns 7, so both exit 7 iff the uses fold works and StrUtils resolves. Priority raised by fiscal's evidence: lib's xml.local_name (index_of a colon) and file_system's (</>) index one-char literals too, so every generated fiscal module fails on Delphi until 5n installs. Composition: both samples join the C and Delphi lists in tests/matrix.psd1 when 5n is composed over 5m's install.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_30 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 30u32, "2026-10-25T23:22Z", "main", "10-25 19:22 main: x's GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES joins pack 5n (agreed: not landed into the tree under the queued 5m review). Staged: gleam-export-types-apply_core.ps1 854E59552659; gleam-export-types-apply_host_cells.ps1 C80163877429. Core part (apply_core.ps1 -Target): structural names for generic union instantiations on both PartEval sides, Gleam producer names, the Gleam consumer pass, and the Rust consumer pass with exported tag/Default impls. It throws on any anchor not matched exactly once. Host+Cells part (apply_host_cells.ps1 -Spiral): Program.fs import-types header read, the Cells directives arm and its test; it lands in 5n's install window right before the host build, so core and host can't disagree. x's proofs: Gleam consumer job-1792931443568, Rust consumer job-1792940361755, same-core neutrality job-1792952173917 (257 entries identical). 5n composition order over the 5m-installed core: fiscal's delphi-literal-index, delphi-uses-units, then x's apply_core; samples delphi_literal_index and delphi_uses_units join the C and Delphi matrix lists.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_31 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 31u32, "2026-10-25T23:25Z", "main", "10-25 19:25 main: pack 5n DRY-COMPOSED on a copy of the 5m composite (A29F241986A441F3), so 5n composes without re-anchoring if 5m installs unchanged. In order: delphi-literal-index.py exit 0, delphi-uses-units.py exit 0, then x's apply_core.ps1 (run from x's folder, since it loads helpers from its own directory) exit 0 (all core layers incl. the Rust consumer imports). Dry 5n composite 2CCD56F5D125E961; pair-drift 0 pairs touched on one side only. If 5m's review is RED and 5m is recomposed, rerun this dry composition on the new base.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_32 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 32u32, "2026-10-26T04:47Z", "main", "10-26 00:47 main: pack 5n gains the COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO fix (compiler lane, node-validated 10-26 00:4x): build.ps1 det-root plus PDB path /_/, and Directory.Build.props obj-shared to det-root. Build 2 passes G1 (no C:\\Users strings) and the decompressed-metadata check (no machine-varying user path; only FSharp.Core's constant dependency paths remain, identical on every machine), so G4 holds by construction. G3 (node core == box core) needs no extra box build: 5n's review already builds the box single-flight core from the composite, so G3 is that build's SpiralCompilerCore.dll hash against the node's build of the same composite. Composition order over the 5m-installed core, updated: fiscal's delphi-literal-index and delphi-uses-units, x's gleam apply_core, then the repro apply-script (build-infra files only, no core text, so no pair-drift interaction). After install: blessed single-flight DLLs go to the node by hash, which unblocks the node lib-gate trial without a node build.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_33 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 33u32, "2026-10-26T04:58Z", "main", "10-26 00:58 main: pack 5m review (job-1792976664435, run single-flight-20261025-213330) is NOT installable, by its pre-registered rule. (1) The rule's one hard requirement failed: mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/runtime_native on Lua is still native=build-fail ('main.lua:3196: main function has more than 200 local variables'), with revision=unchanged and native-reused: the candidate's main.lua is byte-identical to the regressed output, so the fixed LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS pair did not change this program, although the composite holds its bare-declaration counting (spillChunkLocals at line 170014). Evidence sent to tibia (pair owner). (2) The lib Lua check's 2 candidate-only reds in trace.spi (trace_2@rust, test_trace_level@typescript) are NOT regressions: 'Spiral cell timed out after 300s; the process tree was killed' at 00:11 under box load, i.e. a timeout, not a verdict. Everything else held: DISAGREE 0; C, Zig and Python outputs unchanged beyond the known rules. Decision (precedent: 5l dropped the same pair to unblock SLATE): recompose without the pair. 5m' = installed 5l'' core 34519B92 + HOPACSOTA published-ready (CD8B0869857578F4) + strip-doc-citations (24 blocks, 86 comment lines) = %TEMP%\\pack-next5m2\\spiral_compiler.fs 8660C34A11F33259; pair-drift: 8 pairs, 0 touched on one side. Review main-pack5m2-review (job-1792990625364, 12 h cap): the same review script, plus one rerun for any lib-check cell that hits its time budget. Install rule unchanged: DISAGREE 0, no candidate-only reds after the rerun, output changes explained by published-ready (the strip is comment-only). Pack 5n then composes over 5m': fiscal's two Delphi slices, x's gleam apply_core, and the compiler lane's repro fix, staged as %TEMP%\\pack-next5n\\repro-build.ps1 (64B02255) and repro-Directory.Build.props (52BB618A); both base files in the tree match the lane's base hashes, so they drop in as whole files.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_34 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 34u32, "2026-10-26T05:05Z", "main", "10-26 01:05 main: pack 5n's repro set is now 5 whole-file drop-ins (compiler lane's helper refactor: one Get-SpiralDetRoot in env.ps1), all verified against the lane's hashes (tree base and edited file, first 16 hex), staged as %TEMP%\\pack-next5n\\repro-*: env.ps1 E1B7CF36 -> 5EDB3F87 (adds Get-SpiralDetRoot), build.ps1 ACD5D825 -> 45C91E28 (stages core-src under the det root; PathMap /_/), test.ps1 43F1D9F9 -> 526EF552 (:62 stale-jsonl cleanup walks the det root), rebuild-determinism.ps1 F83FDD94 -> 2C21D0F0 (:10 touch walks the det root), Directory.Build.props 1551399A -> 52BB618A (obj-shared under the det root). The earlier inline build.ps1 (64B02255) is withdrawn. Review impact: test.ps1's native-memo key is a signature of its native functions (Build-And-Run, Invoke-Native, Get-CFlags, the native tier block, read through the AST), and the :62 edit is outside them, so native rows still replay; 5n's review must still show 0 native rows rerun only because of test.ps1.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_35 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 35u32, "2026-10-26T06:29Z", "main", "10-26 02:29 main: pack 5n contents so far, all on the 5m' base 8660C34A11F33259 once its review installs: fiscal x2, x apply_core, the 5 repro files (env.ps1 5EDB3F87, build.ps1 45C91E28, test.ps1 526EF552, rebuild-determinism.ps1 2C21D0F0, Directory.Build.props 52BB618A), tibia's Lua pair plus the CRLF fix through %TEMP%\\tibia-luafix\\spill-lf-apply.ps1 (dry run on a 5m' copy gives BC8145A1DAA91953; LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS n10), and the compiler lane's DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX one-liner (codegenDelphi StringIndex wraps only a literal operand as string('z')[i + 1]; its anchor matches exactly once in 5m', at line 165463). The Delphi regression sample (samples/delphi_string_literal_index, matrix.psd1 entry and outputs) comes from the compiler lane. 5n's accept adds a -Suite examples -Native Delphi no-regression pass to the full matrix, and omniledger must come back ran/agree.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_36 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 36u32, "2026-10-26T06:52Z", "main", "10-26 02:52 main: correction to n35: 5n takes fiscal's DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX fix (%TEMP%\\pack-next5n\\delphi-literal-index.py, a new StringIndex arm matching DLit (LitString _) and emitting AnsiString('z')[i + 1], with samples\\delphi_literal_index), not the compiler lane's StartsWith \"'\" one-liner. Rule: match the literal by AST node rather than by emitted text, and AnsiString doesn't depend on {$H+} (FPC's default-mode string is a 255-byte ShortString). Also new in 5n: EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML's core slice %TEMP%\\pack-toml\\spiral-toml.ps1 (drafted 10-24, never packed). All 5 anchors match exactly once on the 5m' composite (dry run 8660C34A -> 7E7AB06E alone). It is the manifests class of LIB-EDITION3-ROUND (P0, 10-26), so 5n's review adds its residual-identity proof: convert one consumer package and compile it byte-identical with spiral.toml vs package.spiproj.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_37 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 37u32, "2026-10-26T07:01Z", "main", "10-26 03:01 main: 5n candidate composed ahead of the 5m' verdict by %TEMP%\\pack-next5n\\compose.ps1 (each step must change the hash, any failure stops): base 5m' 8660C34A11F33259 -> fiscal delphi-literal-index B651BEC4 -> fiscal delphi-uses-units 16FED6E0 -> x gleam-export-types core (x's structural-names\\apply_core.ps1, 4 layers) 5F08C8C8 -> tibia spill-lf 5C439A56 -> main spiral-toml FD534D87D8EC2FF6, at %TEMP%\\pack-next5n\\candidate\\spiral_compiler.fs. Rest of the pack: the 5 repro files, x's apply_host_cells, samples delphi_literal_index and delphi_uses_units. Correction to n36: the generated Delphi program already sets {$mode delphi}{$H+}, so string is AnsiString there too; fiscal's arm wins only on the AST-node rule. If 5m' is not installed as-is, compose.ps1 -Base re-runs on whatever base installs.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_38 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 38u32, "2026-10-26T07:50Z", "main", "10-26 03:50 main: 5n recomposed with the compiler lane's three edition-3 lexer items after spiral-toml: lexer-dcolon (SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX '::': symbol lexer, var-lexer exception, respell pair .x <-> ::x), macro-sigil-respell (respell() InsideMacro carve-out for \\@ <-> @@, \\# <-> ##, \\$ <-> $$; no lexer change, since @@/## already lex the same), lexer-zwsp (SPIRAL-ZWSP-TRANSPARENT: strip with column remap in tokenize_line). Chain from 5m' 8660C34A: ... spiral-toml FD534D87 -> 8E0B1670 -> B976C0A5 -> 9E1F72ABFD095D26, the same final hash the compiler lane reported. They are not build-verified yet (both machines saturated), so 5n's review is the build gate, and the compiler lane posts an independent single-flight green when the node drains. Queue order: after kit's rename is finalized (kit asked all lanes to queue nothing during the release) and after the 5m' review installs.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_39 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 39u32, "2026-10-26T09:08Z", "main", "10-26 05:08 main: pack 5m' review (job-1792990625364, run single-flight-20261026-015252 vs 20261022-184215) meets the install rule: matrix DISAGREE 0; formatter gate automatic part PASS (0 files differ beyond parentheses/whitespace in user, lib, synthetic, dice and eoie); lib checks no candidate-only reds (file_system's one red, file_exists_on_gleam..., is red on the installed core too; trace.spi's cell timeout passed on its one retry); Lua check 12 files, 0 candidate-only reds. Every behavior difference is explained: build-fail -> ran on Bend rows and on mega_brzozowski indexed_dfa_state_cardinality Rust; gleam_dead_arm_let_globals new rows; gadt_growing_index_runtime goes from WRONG-ERROR to the intended EJP0041 diagnostic with parity. The 207 'Rust changed beyond let mut' text rows are informational by the rule (the snapshot holds older pack outputs), and native results equal the prior run row for row. Note: omniledger runtime_native Lua shows native=ran oracle=agree even though 5m' dropped the Lua pair; 5n (which carries the pair plus tibia's CRLF fix) checks that row again. Install queued: job-1793005633424-main-pack5m2-install (%TEMP%\\pack-next5m2\\install-5m2.ps1: base 5l'' 34519B92 verified, composite 8660C34A, reviewed SpiralCompilerCore 022C3338, compiler-install window, single-flight + hopacsota builds, then a hash or MVID equality check against the reviewed candidate). 5n's compose already sits on 8660C34A, so no recompose is needed once this installs.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_40 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 40u32, "2026-10-26T09:22Z", "main", "10-26 05:22 main: 5n packaging finished (05:2x). (1) x's GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES host + Cells step (structural-names\\apply_host_cells.ps1: host Program.fs ///- --import-types read; Cells directives.spi arm + directives_test.exs) composes after the --check host patch: both applied in order on copies (Program.fs CB1670A2 -> 27676BD3 -> B24847FC). install-5n.ps1 runs it inside the compiler-install window after a Cells reader gap, guarded by the two Cells files' recorded hashes. (2) fiscal's samples are in the tree (unstaged): apps/compiler/tmp/samples/delphi_literal_index (default backend set, as gleam_dead_arm_let_globals showed in 5m') and delphi_uses_units, which has only C and Delphi arms and so gets a new matrix.psd1 group 'C,Delphi' (parse-checked); otherwise F# and Rust would fail spuriously for lack of an arm. The 5n review (job-1793005854886) runs both as new rows.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_41 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 41u32, "2026-10-26T12:27Z", "main", "10-26 08:27 main: 5m' INSTALLED 08:2x (job-1793014939081-main-pack5m2-install-2): the single-flight core 022C3338096B4051 equals the reviewed candidate, so no re-review is needed; hopacsota was rebuilt (core A1715886B0F081AC); the compiler-install window was released. The first attempt (job-1793005633424) was stopped at 07:4x without writing anything: as a --heavy job it held heavy slot 2 for 67 min while its window request waited behind tibia's compile window. It was requeued without --heavy, since the exclusive window serializes it; tibia yielded slices-c at 08:1x so it could run. Next: the 5n review (job-1793014942979, --after-ok) on the installed compiler, then install-5n.ps1 -Apply in its own announced window.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_42 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 42u32, "2026-10-26T16:36Z", "main", "10-26 12:36 main: 5n review (job-1793014942979, 09:5x-12:1x) passed on its own rule: 'review exit 0'. DISAGREE 0; native results equal to next3 row for row; C, Zig, Python, Lua, Wasm, Delphi, Lean, Kotlin, Swift and TypeScript outputs change 0 beyond their named rewrites. Rust's 208 'changed beyond let mut' lines are informational by the review's own rule text (the comparison snapshot holds pack-after-night outputs, so the next3b Rust fusions show; Rust DISAGREE 0, native row for row, no new build-fail). Lib: 13 files candidate green; file_system's file_exists_on_gleam row is red on the installed compiler too (not candidate-only). The reviewed candidate is recorded for install (spiral-bin candidates/pack-next4 single-flight SpiralCompiler.dll). The job's exit 2 came only from main's spiral.toml identity step: it resolved core relative to the shell's working directory (then the backlog folder), 'Package .../docs/backlog/deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core is not loaded', because the step didn't set SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR. That's a proof bug, not a 5n fault; rerun standalone as TEMP pack-next5n/toml-proof.ps1 (package dir set explicitly, VOID if the reference doesn't compile). Install order agreed with kit (12:1x): 5n installs only after kit's rename release (FOLLOWUP's install, pause and finalize), because that release's proofs were produced on core CDD68DC3; install-5n.ps1's dry-run guards all pass except the dll.txt check, which the review has now satisfied.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_43 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 43u32, "2026-10-26T16:37Z", "main", "10-26 12:37 main: spiral.toml identity rerun GREEN (TEMP pack-next5n/toml-proof.ps1, 12:3x, reviewed candidate pack-next4 single-flight, core package dir set explicitly): the candidate writes spiral.toml via 'format --manifest toml', and dir_tree_html.spi's Rust residual is byte-identical (23,757 bytes) with spiral.toml in place of package.spiproj. 5n is fully reviewed. install-5n.ps1 dry run 12:3x: every guard passes, with composite 9E1F72ABFD095D26 and reviewed SpiralCompilerCore 295C72E12D55860A. Waiting only on kit's rename release (order (b)), then 'install-5n.ps1 -Apply' queued without --heavy, since its exclusive window serializes it.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_44 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 44u32, "2026-10-26T17:04Z", "main", "10-26 13:04 main: install-5n's Cells guard widened on evidence, 12:5x: fiscal's Cells fidelity land (job-1793033954641) inserts one line in apps/cells/spi/directives.spi (a '--name' arm after '--symbol'), FFE782851366145F -> DB5E79558E2BC5C2; directives_test.exs is unchanged at 4E2868DA52D05801. Simulated on a scratch tree, the insert reproduces fiscal's hash exactly, and x's apply_host_cells.ps1 (which install-5n runs inside its window) applies cleanly over it, exit 0. The guard now accepts either proven hash ('FFE782851366145F|DB5E79558E2BC5C2'), so the order between fiscal's land and the install no longer matters; the dry run still passes. Install order unchanged: after kit's rename release (FOLLOWUP job-1793034001350, first in the heavy queue).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_45 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 45u32, "2026-10-26T17:44Z", "main", "10-26 13:44 main: Rename-pause plan for main, 13:4x (kit: finalize-RENAME's state move refuses while ANY eoie job supervisor is alive, queued ones included). Two scripts, both dry-run tested.\npause-plan.ps1 snapshots main's 15 jobs: argv (argv.inner for --heavy jobs), cwd, timeout and label. For serial chains it also computes the steps still to run, from the CURRENT run's segment of the manifest log; the runner appends across reruns of a reused manifest, which first made zig-index-of-5 read as 0 steps left. With -Stop it stops jobs waiting in the heavy queue or gating a scratch copy, and holds any job in its land step.\npause-requeue.ps1 requeues with kit.exe after 'rename finalized': from each job's recorded cwd, oldest first (heavy queue is FIFO), only the remaining steps. It refuses without kit.exe.\nHeld at planning time: job-1793020861797 (unicode-3), whose land waits on the reader guard. Its polyglot proof step is planned for requeue.\nOrder: kit's pause text, then pause-plan -Stop, then report the supervisor count to kit. After 'rename finalized': pause-requeue -Apply, then install-5n -Apply.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_46 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 46u32, "2026-10-26T22:01Z", "main", "10-26 18:01 main: 10-26 18:01 main: NODE COMPILER REFRESH rule (x's finding: the node's shared single-flight DLL is 10/25's 499AFC18E49F, while this box has 10/26 08:19's 4FD712E9E357; Ensure-SpiralCompiler builds only when the DLL is missing, so syncing spiral never refreshes it, and node codegen verdicts carry the 10/25 base). Owner: main (machine upkeep). Rule, written now: ONE refresh, right after this box's 5n install, from that installed single-flight output, (1) hash-verified on both ends, (2) only while the node has 0 running jobs, (3) the old DLL folder kept as a dated backup, (4) node-status noted with the new hash. Not now: a refresh to 08:19 would mean a second swap within hours, and swapping under running jobs (fiscal-ubl-proof, kit-requeue3-runner) mixes compiler versions inside them. Interim: per-job SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL overrides (x staged a hash-verified 08:19 copy at C:\\home\\remote-stage\\spiral-bin-1026-0819\\net11.0).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_47 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 47u32, "2026-10-26T22:25Z", "main", "10-26 18:25 main: 10-26 18:25 main: CORRECTION to n46 (from x's recheck). The evidence that node codegen verdicts carry a different base was wrong. x's proof 7 compiled on the node with a hash-identical copy of this box's 10/26 08:19 DLL (4FD712E9E357) and emitted the same extra .clone() calls as the 10/25 node DLL. The clone-free .rs in x's tree came from an older compiler (commit 0959354, 10/25). The compiler lane classes those clones as status-quo &str-to-Rc copies. What still holds: the node's shared DLL is an older build (499AFC18E49F), but no output diff shows that it matters. The one-refresh-after-5n rule stands as routine upkeep, not as a correctness fix, and node verdicts don't need a stale-base caveat on this evidence.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_48 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 48u32, "2026-10-27T13:09Z", "main", "10-27 09:09 main: pack 5n readiness, re-checked against today's tree (10-27 09:1x). (1) CORE: the tree core is still 5n's base 8660C34A11F33259 (last written 10-26 00:55). compose.ps1 on it reproduces the composite exactly: all 8 applies (fiscal delphi-literal-index and delphi-uses-units, x gleam-export-types, tibia spill-lf, main spiral-toml, compiler lexer-dcolon, macro-sigil-respell and lexer-zwsp) match once each, giving 9E1F72ABFD095D26, the hash install-5n.ps1 expects. Review: job-1793014942979 (10-26) DISAGREE 0; the toml proof passed standalone at 12:3x. The install hold of 10-26 (after kit's rename release) is long satisfied (finalized 10-26 20:37). (2) BUILD SCRIPTS: 4 of the 5 repro-* bases still match. test.ps1 moved (43F1D9F9 at commit d095f13d -> 6AD031FF, tonight's bundle edits since committed), so 5n's whole-file repro-test.ps1 would have silently reverted them. A 3-way merge (base d095f13d, ours 5n, theirs tree) is clean, 3E2691408C0984C8, and adds only 5n's one line, Join-Path (Get-SpiralDetRoot) 'core-src'. That line shows 5n's repro-* files ARE CCMR, which the compiler team holds behind G2 with its newer, deduped apply-ccmr-det-root.ps1. Asked the compiler team to pick: land CCMR through 5n, or strip repro-* from 5n (core untouched) and land CCMR later. Leaning strip. (3) ORDER: bundle accept (job-1793103041735) -> kit-release-SLICES finished (it regenerates on the installed compiler) -> install-5n -> 5n accept. Zig Export is not in 5n's composite; it follows as its own pack. fiscal's P0 Delphi step 1 waits on this install.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_49 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 49u32, "2026-10-27T13:15Z", "main", "10-27 09:15 main: 5n repro-* STRIPPED by the compiler team's decision (route b, 10-27 09:1x): the five whole-file build-script replacements are the CCMR det-root change, which the compiler team lands later as one piece via apps/compiler/packs/apply-ccmr-det-root.ps1 (det-root plus the Get-FileSha256 dedup) behind G2 (the per-job SPIRAL_DET_ROOT versus staged-.fs path conflict) and G3 (box-vs-node core-sha equality). Landing them through 5n would have landed the determinism change unproven. install-5n.ps1 now has an empty $repro map (backup install-5n.ps1.bak-with-repro-* beside it; the repro-* files stay in the folder as record, not installed). The core composite, the Cells files and the other lanes' slices are untouched by the strip; all remaining guards MATCH today's tree (core base 8660C34A, composite 9E1F72AB, directives.spi DB5E7955, directives_test.exs 4E2868DA), and the installer parses clean. Install order unchanged: bundle accept -> kit-release-SLICES finalize (kit asked to signal) -> install-5n -> 5n accept.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_50 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 50u32, "2026-10-28T02:56Z", "main", "10-27 22:56 main: 5n installed and cleared 10-27 22:49. Installed core 7A6C1445 differs from the reviewed 295C72E1 by CCMR build metadata only: same-tree A/B GREEN, frontier,smoke,examples -Native, 6,637 = 6,637 rows, 0 differing, 0 missing (runs single-flight-20261027-214556 and -221917). The baseline still holds accept-6's 5m' oracle, so 5n needs its own full accept. Rule, written before it runs (10-27 23:0x). Run: after kit's 'TRAIN2 window closed', test.ps1 -Mode single-flight -Suite all -Native -Accept with SPIRAL_CC pinned to scoop gcc 15.2, through matrix-bundle accept.ps1 (baseline backup first; it refuses -ReuseNative). Compare against the 5n review run single-flight-20261026-101636. GREEN iff test.ps1 exits 0 and every differing cell is one of: (a) residual-only (same exit and stdout) on a sample that calls a lib module written since 10-26 10:16; (b) a Delphi row whose oracle verdict is agree, since the bitness changed to -Px86_64 at 21:4x; (c) samples/delphi_literal_index [Delphi] going from build-fail to compile ok, exit 42, oracle agree (the 5n fix). Anything else is BLOCKING: restore the baseline backup, record the cells, no re-run until each is explained. Lengths: about 1 h 45 min; it must end by 08:15 (tibia's live block from 08:30), so start by 06:15 or else at 09:30. The compiler team checks (c) in the results.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_51 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 51u32, "2026-10-28T03:00Z", "main", "10-27 23:00 main: SUPERSEDES the n50 rule, before anything ran. Reason, measured with comparer controls: against the 10-26 5n review the comparison gives 1,251 blocking cells. 56 lib modules moved since then, the review mixed 5m'-era rows, and the row sets differ, so that rule could only pass by loosening it until it meant nothing. Revised rule. Reference: tonight's installed-5n A/B run single-flight-20261027-214556 (the same compiler 7A6C1445, the same tree, Delphi already -Px86_64, lib frozen for TRAIN2 since 22:51). GREEN iff (1) test.ps1 -Mode single-flight -Suite all -Native -Accept exits 0, where its own bad-row check fails on any DISAGREE, including the 730 contracts and mega rows outside the A/B's suites; and (2) each of the reference's 6,637 rows is unchanged in the accept, except residual-only cells on samples calling a lib module written since the reference started (compare-accept.ps1 -IgnoreNewRows). Otherwise RED and the baseline is restored from the backup (accept.ps1). Controls run at 22:59: reference vs the reviewed A/B run gives BLOCKING 0 (positive), and reference vs accept-6 (5m') gives BLOCKING 5 with 0 new-row cells (negative: the comparer sees a compiler change and ignores only the extra rows). Timing: it runs right after main's sample regen, during TRAIN2 (lib frozen, as accept-6 ran during TRAIN), so the morning stays free for kit's lib window and tibia's 08:30 block. delphi_literal_index is checked in the results by the compiler team.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_52 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 52u32, "2026-10-28T05:05Z", "main", "10-28 01:05 main: 5n ACCEPTED GREEN under the n51 rule. Run single-flight-20261027-232226 (job-1793157737180, 23:22 to about 00:16; it ran in slot 1 during TRAIN2 with lib frozen). test.ps1 -Mode single-flight -Suite all -Native -Accept exited 0: 7,367 rows, DISAGREE 0. Against the reference single-flight-20261027-214556: 6,637 rows compared, 0 differing cells, 0 blocking. Baseline EXPECTED.tsv rewritten 10-28 00:19, with the backup in TEMP matrix-bundle/baseline-before-accept-*. Key rows: samples/delphi_literal_index [Delphi] compile ok, ran, exit 42, oracle agree (the 5n fix, 32-bit); mega_brzozowski_derivatives/inventory_closed_dfa_runtime [Delphi] exit 0, agree (32-bit; the 64-bit failure is DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE). The installed compiler is 5n (core 9E1F72AB, core DLL 7A6C1445) with its own oracle. Pack chain done: composed, reviewed (10-26), installed (10-27 21:43), same-tree A/B (22:49) and accepted (00:16). Not part of this pack: the CCMR det-root/SourceLink build files (landed by the compiler team, 21:50).")
inl note_of_kit_note_edit_expect_1 () = Note ("KIT-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T04:14Z", "main", "2026-10-24 00:14 main: hit tonight. Correcting a grooming note, main ran note-edit FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL 9 meaning the newest note (26), and silently replaced a 10-22 note about Delphi build outputs. Recovered byte-identical only because the user had staged agile.spi (git show :docs/backlog/state/agile.spi). The receipt printed at=2026-10-22T06:59Z, which would have shown the mistake if it had been checked first. Milestones: optional --expect-at and --expect-prefix guards in note_request_of 30 m; the receipt shows the old text head 15 m; one-call tutorial case (edit the newest note by --last) 30 m; release 15 m. A --last shorthand (edit the newest note) would remove the counting entirely.")
inl note_of_kit_note_edit_expect_2 () = Note ("KIT-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T05:03Z", "main", "2026-10-24 01:03 main: draft ready as scratch note-edit-expect\\apply.ps1 (dry run on live eoie src: 5 anchors x1). note_guard union NoGuard | ExpectedAt | ExpectedPrefix plus a note_edit_options record; guard_problem is checked before the author check and before any write; a recursive flag parser takes --as, --expect-at and --expect-prefix in any order, replacing the legacy positional --as check; the usage shape is updated. Handed to the eoie team for their train's preview and typecheck (the tree is leased during releases).")
inl note_of_kit_note_edit_expect_3 () = Note ("KIT-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT", 3u32, "2026-10-25T13:57Z", "eoie", "09:57 eoie: main's draft is landed in the live tree and ships in SLATE (the parked note-edit-expect-rebased stage passed the S-stage rule: its crates measured at or under 950 once eoie_cli was fixed; it is in the 5l' full measure, all 234 crates at or under 1000 and cargo check green in one workspace). It adds the note_guard union (NoGuard | ExpectedAt | ExpectedPrefix), --expect-at <ISO> and --expect-prefix <text> parsed in any order beside --as, and the guard checked before the author check and before any write. Left: after install, a CLI run: note-edit with a wrong --expect-at is refused with nothing written, a right one edits, and --expect-prefix behaves the same.")
inl note_of_kit_note_edit_expect_4 () = Note ("KIT-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT", 4u32, "2026-10-27T22:19Z", "eoie", "18:1x CLI acceptance on the installed kit (scratch root from agile init, one item with two notes). A wrong --expect-at was refused (exit 2, 'note 1 was written at ..., not 2020-01-01T00:00Z'). A wrong --expect-prefix was refused (exit 2, 'does not start with the expected text'). The state file hash was unchanged after both refusals. A right --expect-prefix and a right --expect-at each edited the note (hash changed, show --json carries the new text). The flags work in any order beside --as. Still missing from the title: the receipt prints only text_bytes=a->b, not the replaced text's first 80 chars. Drafted, both verified on scratch copies. (1) lib sm'.clip (width) (s): char-safe, at most width chars including '...'. It has a Rust arm (chars().take), an F# arm, and a length/slice fallback, plus a fsharp+rust test block. lib's slice is byte-based on Rust and exits 3 mid-character, so ellipsis can't serve notes with non-ASCII text. A rustc probe printed abc|abc...|é...|x→... and exited 0. Draft: scratchpad clean\\lib-clip\\lib-clip.do (1/1 on a lib copy). (2) kit-clip.do (8/8 on package copies): the receipt gains replaced=\"<first 80 chars>\". The three identical host-macro 'clipped' helpers (kit_memory_model, agile_task_next, agile_task_listing) are removed in favour of sm'.clip at their 4 call sites. Order: lib-clip in the next lib window (after the TRAIN window closes, with a lib gate), then kit-clip in the release after TRAIN. task_listing.rs is at 978 lines, so regen it and check the crate gate.")
inl note_of_kit_note_edit_expect_5 () = Note ("KIT-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT", 5u32, "2026-10-27T23:35Z", "eoie", "19:3x eoie: node regen of the drafts (job-1793142539742 and job-1793144013979, box DLL 4FD712E9E357). agile_note_edit goes 845 -> 855 lines for the replaced= receipt. The clip consumers (agile_task_listing, agile_task_next, kit_memory, kit_memory_query) regenerate byte-identical, because sm'.clip's Rust arm is the same expression as the retired host copies. Nothing is over 1000.")
inl note_of_kit_note_edit_expect_6 () = Note ("KIT-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT", 6u32, "2026-10-28T09:46Z", "eoie", "05:4x kit: this item's lib programs landed live in today's lib window. Gates are running on the node (P2 compiler pinned): see EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN's 05:4x note. The kit-side half follows in the next release.")
inl note_of_kit_note_edit_expect_7 () = Note ("KIT-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT", 7u32, "2026-10-30T14:22Z", "kit", "10-30 10:2x kit: VERIFIED LIVE on the installed kit C528BE4D, scratch backlog (agile init, add PROBE with note 1 'first note'). 'note-edit PROBE 1 rewritten --expect-prefix nope' is refused: 'note 1 does not start with the expected text', exit 2. With '--expect-prefix first' the edit is applied, and the receipt prints replaced=\"first note\". The kit-side half the #6 note waited on has shipped, so the item closes.")
inl note_of_kit_note_edit_expect_8 () = Note ("KIT-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT", 8u32, "2026-11-06T11:29Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_python_nullary_shared_1 () = Note ("PYTHON-NULLARY-SHARED", 1u32, "2026-10-24T06:42Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:5x main: drafted and composed into pack 5g. %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\python-nullary-shared.ps1 (3 anchors, the construction one scoped to module CodegenPython, because the CUDA backend's Python host has the same TyUnionBox text). Every nullary heap/stack union case gets `<Case>_shared = <Case>()` right after its NamedTuple class, and a nullary construction emits `<Case>_shared` instead of `<Case>()`. Nullness is read from the case's payload array (as next9 does), not from the argument text. NamedTuple values are immutable, so sharing is safe, and `case <Case>():` patterns still match the class. Rides review-4 (job requeued after tibia's block); the matrix's Python rows are its behaviour check, and main.py diffs should change only at nullary constructions.")
inl note_of_python_nullary_shared_2 () = Note ("PYTHON-NULLARY-SHARED", 2u32, "2026-10-29T17:50Z", "compiler", "10-29 13:5x compiler: DONE, found stale at 0% Planned. The pack-5g change shipped before the wipe and is committed: the core emits '<Case>_shared = <Case>()' after each nullary case's NamedTuple (spiral_compiler.fs:178298, 178308, both heap and stack unions) and builds nullary cases through pythonConstruct -> <Case>_shared (:178163). It entered the tree in commit d095f13d (2026-10-25, git log -S on the emit text), and 113 committed samples/**/main.py at HEAD carry the shared form (git grep at HEAD), so the accepted oracle's Python rows include it. Nothing left to do.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_1 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T07:08Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:08 main: user direction 2026-10-24 03:0x: \"we started ruining lib's code stuffing a lot of code inside macros, same way eoie drifted with rust_global, unhealable drift that we're still dealing with there. look at async.start_child_rust geez, prioritize healing ALL of this\". Census (scratch macro-census.ps1 over 107 lib .spi/.spir files): macro text is 193,899 of 856,114 definition chars (22.6 %); 962 definitions use a macro; 69 hold LOGIC inside a macro (3+ statements or 2+ control keywords), 59,479 chars (list in scratch macro-logic.tsv). Largest: sm'_real.format_real 12,866 (171 lines), sm'_real rec 4,109, runtime.execute_with_options_rust 2,276, leptos svg loading render 2,168, async.start_child_rust 2,072, date_time.time_span_format 1,908, trace.trace 1,505, unicode.char_of 1,365, trace.init_trace_state 1,184, sm'.index_of 1,141, common.random' 1,114, base.try_finally 1,102, convert.convert_i32_base 1,072, guid.new_raw_guid 1,045, resultm.unwrap_err 996, base.try_unit 989, resultm.get 968, file_system.walk_dir_filter 952, sm'.split 948, async.catch_rust 905. Not counted as logic but in scope: Gleam helper preludes kept as global blobs (process.process_gleam_prelude 5,839, file_system.file_system_gleam_paths 1,550). Out of scope: F# export `main` stubs (one let per exported name) and one-call backend arms ($'Async.Sleep').\nRULE, written before any heal: (1) host code stays only as a named primitive whose macro is ONE host call or expression, at most 120 chars, and does no control flow; (2) everything else (statements, loops, matches, panic and timeout handling, helper preludes) is Spiral over lib, with the primitives as its only host contact; (3) order: async.start_child_rust first (user-named), then by logic chars descending, a file's defs together when they share primitives; (4) acceptance per heal: every test the def had passes on every backend it had, the generated output for its tests is reviewed (no behavior change), the lib Cells gate is green, and no new macro exceeds rule (1); (5) a new lib def with logic in a macro is a review failure from now on. Progress = healed logic chars / 59,479, plus the 2 preludes.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_2 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-24T07:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:37 main: under GRANULAR-HEAL; rule extended by the user's cast remark (env.command_line_argument_count Rust arm (std::env::args().count() as i32) - 1): no as-casts and no arithmetic in macro text either. Rule (6): authorship is irrelevant (user 03:3x: don't keep old code just because I wrote it). Census now 715 sites, 60,477 chars; top files sm'_real.spir 44/15,447, file_system 55/7,928, sm' 69/6,033, trace 27/5,649, date_time 43/2,373, base 48/2,331. start_child_rust: 0 callers in every repo since the 10-23 unused removal took start_child/start_child_timeout, so rule (4) deletes it; catch_rust and run_synchronously (used by catch' and start) are healed first.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_3 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-24T08:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:17 tibia: process.spi's heal moved to tibia, prelude included, by main's handover (04:0x), and it absorbs LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's slice. Lab spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL/process_healed.spi is the whole module rewritten under this item's rule. The Gleam global keeps only imports and @external declarations (coerce, open_port, element, find_executable, halt, port_info, port_close, unlink, os cmd, monotonic_time, get, put, file write_file). Every host contact is a named primitive of one expression: nominals dynamic, port, bits, gleam_strings, port_options, selector, received, plus rust_command, rust_output, rust_try_output, rust_file, rust_instant, started. The verbs follow main's suggested shape (port_open, port_unlink, port_os_pid, port_selector, selector_receive, file_write, os_cmd, rust_command_new and friends, rust_command_spawn, rust_child_exited and rust_child_code). The logic is Spiral: the argv filter, the collect and drain loops, the exit cache, the deadline loop, and kill_tree_of_pid choosing taskkill or kill through platform.is_windows. It carries the module's 6 existing tests plus the handle's 3, on the same backends. Lab run 2 is queued after run 1's two parse and type errors (a != splice and an array type in words_of).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_4 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-24T08:32Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:32 tibia: rule (4) agreed with main (04:2x). Slice (a) removes lib process.spi: all 6 of its definitions have 0 callers in every repo. Scratch process-delete-apply.ps1: one eoie do edit drops process from package.spiproj, both files are backed up for undo, and the file is removed by literal path. Its guard refuses if any lib code outside macro text still calls process.*; the first runs flagged 15 lines, all host text (TypeScript process.stdout and argv, Zig std.process, Gleam process.*), and the strip now covers single-line and unterminated multi-line macros. Proven on a lib copy: process.spi removed, spiproj lists runtime then file_system. Kit gate job tibia-lib-process-delete-gate (gates runtime.spi to show the package still loads); the land goes after the Cells mounts fix. The lab is trimmed to what lands later with world_port: capture_status_within (now taking list args) plus the handle, 5 tests, with no unused definitions left (two cleanup rounds; a removal that ate 4 adjacent nominals was caught and restored). Lab run 4 is compiling.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_5 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-24T08:40Z", "main", "2026-10-24 04:40 main: heal slices drafted and queued as gate-kit gate+land pairs (land waits for Cells erl, live-gui windows and now running eoie-release-* trains, which read the live lib): (1) LIB-UNICODE-GRANULAR %TEMP%\\lib-unicode-granular (char_of validity + UTF-8 encode in Spiral, one host call per arm; new width test) job-1792829476802; (2) LIB-ENV-ARGV %TEMP%\\lib-env-argv-granular (host_argument_count/host_argument one-call arms, offsets and +/- in Spiral, Gleam fn body gone) job-1792829682792; (3) LIB-ASYNC-GRANULAR %TEMP%\\lib-async-granular (poll/context/waker/panic-hook primitives; run_synchronously via rust.loop, catch_rust via a capture-style poll_fn, ignore/sleep via new_future_move; start_child_rust deleted by rule 4; threading.sleep cast -> conv; 3 new Rust tests) job-1792830257981; (4) RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE %TEMP%\\lib-default-clone (default_value' Rust clones via rust.clone; Lua/Python/TS branch in Spiral over one-op is_none') job-1792830472144; (5) LIB-FLOAT-NATIVE %TEMP%\\lib-float-native (format_real: 11 float arms -> one float_native.shortest call; new module float_native.spi: is_nan/is_infinite/native shortest one-calls, precision search for Lua/C/Delphi and scaling search for Lean in Spiral; composes the float-wire slice; gated on all 10 float-wire files, superseding float-wire gates 2-4) job-1792831183650. Withdrawn: lib-otp-single (Rust mailbox blobs), dictm land (x rebuilt it granular, 0 violations, pending its Cells check), reactor-actor Gleam clock arm.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_6 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-24T10:27Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:27 main: more slices drafted and queued: LIB-SM-LINES-GRANULAR (line_count/line_at/nonempty_* over split/ends_with/slice, last_index_of and percent_encode in Spiral; Gleam fn bodies gone), LIB-SM-INDEX-GRANULAR (index_of on 6 backends, substring casts; composes sm-lines), LIB-BASE-TRY-GRANULAR (one home for the Rust panic primitives; catch_unwind_rust, try_unit, try_finally in Spiral; async rebased onto it), LIB-CRYPTO-GRANULAR (Gleam fn bodies inlined as call expressions; hmac block as primitives), LIB-TRACE-GRANULAR (TS clock, NEAR chunk cast), LIB-XML-GRANULAR (code_point_at/width; composes unicode). The queue is collapsed into serial runners (eoie's process-pile request): main-serial-old-lib-chain, -heal-gates, -heal-lands (dependency order), -other-heavies, -xml; manifests and logs are scratch serial-*.json/.log. Census correction: my first census read only tracked files; with untracked lib modules it is 738 sites / 69,262 chars (new: process.spi 7,304, which tibia is replacing; unicode 600; xml 149; erlang_process/otp_actor/otp_supervisor are thunks around one call, allowed by rule 2, except erlang_process's ProcessDown/PortDown case bridge). Left for later, each needing a platform test first: trace's Rust local clock and NEAR log helper, env.environment_restored's Rust block, sm's Rust lines/nonempty_lines closures.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_7 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-24T10:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:37 main: next lib target by size (not started; plan written for the next pass). guid.spi: (1) new_raw_guid's Zig and Rust arms do the v4 bit layout and hex formatting in host text. Move it to Spiral as guid_v4_text (a b : u64) : string, with the masks, shifts and version/variant bits in Spiral bit ops and hex text from a Spiral digit loop, reusing sm's hex_digit once LIB-SM-LINES lands. (2) Per backend, only random_u64 stays host. Zig keeps `var spiral_random_state` as a declaration, the xorshift step moves to Spiral over a state get/set primitive pair, and the OS seed becomes a rule-3c platform helper. Rust uses RandomState hasher primitives (new/build_hasher, write_u128, finish), each one call, with the SystemTime nanos through unwrap_or_default. Tests: guid's rust/zig/fsharp/gleam rows plus a format check (8-4-4-4-12, version 4, variant 8-b).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_8 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-24T10:41Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:41 main: guid plan of note 7 drafted after all (%TEMP%\\lib-guid-granular): guid_v4_text and guid_hex in Spiral; Rust random through one-call hasher primitives (rust_unix_nanos, build_hasher, write_u128/u64, finish); Zig keeps zig_random_u64 for now (its xorshift/seed declaration is the remaining guid site). Serial runner main-serial-guid (gate on guid.spi, then land).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_9 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-10-24T15:41Z", "main", "main 12:0x: process.spi (user 06:56 list: the multiline block) heal A queued as job main-serial-process-a (gate files process.spi,runtime.spi). The Rust arms now compose the user's own runtime.spi Command lego (new_process_command, process_command_args, setters, spawn, output/status accessors) plus new one-call pieces in runtime.spi: Stdio::null, Command::output, try_wait, kill, wait, exit; process_command_stdout/stderr now use their stdio argument (they ignored it and always piped). Argument filtering, success, UTF-8 decoding and the deadline loop (core loop.while' over try_wait and date_time.stopwatch, no raw-brace injection) are Spiral. Probe on a lib copy: Rust compile + rustc + run gives (true, 2 a b, warned, None, None, 0, -1) = every test expectation. Census note: macro-rule.ps1 missed these arms (it does not follow \\ continuations), so its lib count is low. Heal B next: the 165-line Gleam program in process_gleam_prelude becomes one-call @external primitives with the port/selector logic in Spiral over erlang_process (needs module order: process after erlang_process, or a new erlang_port module).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_10 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 10u32, "2026-10-24T15:47Z", "main", "main 12:1x: correction to note 9: process slice A is withdrawn and its job stopped before it took a slot (no live change). process.spi's heal was handed to the tibia team at 04:0x (note 3) and rule (4) already decided it: 0 callers outside process.spi in every repo, so today's module is deleted (tibia-lib-process-delete-gate) and the GREEN granular module in spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL lands with its first caller (TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP). I missed note 3 because I searched the backlog for the file name and not the item tree. The slice A runtime pieces (Stdio::null, Command::output, try_wait, kill, wait, exit) stay as a draft in %TEMP%\\lib-process-granular-a for whoever lands the lab module's Rust half.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_11 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 11u32, "2026-10-24T16:11Z", "main", "main 12:4x: run-the-binary probes ahead of the queued heal gates (CPU minutes instead of gate slots). base-try (queued in main-serial-heal-gates) had 2 hidden failures, both fixed in %TEMP%\\lib-base-try-granular before its slot: (1) Rust: catch_unwind_rust returned rust_caught (std::thread::Result, not Clone) out of rust_quietly's if, and Spiral clones branch results, so rustc E0599 x4; the conversion now happens inside the quiet thunk. (2) Lua: the healed arms ended in $'nil' : t, a bare nil statement Lua rejects; restored the original IIFE (function() return nil end)() (one expression, so within the rule). Probe results: Rust (Some 7, Some -1, true, true) with 0 stderr lines; Lua (true, true). sm-lines (RED 11:55: lines code used split before its definition, and arraym.length) fixed and requeued as main-serial-sm-lines-2 after Rust+Gleam probes matched every expectation.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_12 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 12u32, "2026-10-24T16:18Z", "main", "main 12:2x: sm-index (queued in main-serial-heal-gates) would have thrown at apply: its lines/nonempty_lines anchor was the pre-format text, but LIB-FORMAT-IDLE (landed 08:55) wrapped nonempty_lines' macro with a \\ continuation; and moving lines/nonempty_lines below the healed helpers stranded the test lines_follow_rust_line_rules above them. The apply now cuts the span by position (inl lines .. that test, checked by content markers) and moves the test with the definitions. Composed with sm-lines on one fresh copy: order split 1389, line_parts 1455, lines 1482, test 1500, line_count 1509. Probe: Rust ((2, 2, -1, 1), (bcd, ), 3, 2, a, b, 0) and Gleam ((2, 2, -1, 1), bcd, ) match every expectation; Lua has no substring arm on the tree either (gap, not a regression: the test runs on gleam/rust/fsharp only).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_13 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 13u32, "2026-10-24T17:43Z", "main", "main 13:4x: date_time.spi heal 1 (rule (4) order after sm') queued as job main-serial-date-time-span: hours/minutes/seconds/milliseconds Python, Rust and TypeScript arms are one host call each (seconds/microseconds field, num_hours..num_milliseconds, the ms number) with the /, % and casts in Spiral (conv; annotated : i32 per arm since backend_switch record arms do not unify). Probes per accessor, each in its own program: TypeScript (node) 10/11/12/13 after widening the ms arm to i64 (conv of a 3.4e10 ms span to i32 wrapped to -355); Rust through a chrono cargo crate built --offline 10/11/12/13. Python not run locally (its generated main imports a main_auto harness module), covered by the gate's cuda target. Unified unicode landed 12:51 (GREEN 12:48, live = draft).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_14 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 14u32, "2026-10-24T18:08Z", "main", "main 14:0x: date_time heal 2 queued (job main-serial-date-time-span-2): Rust tick math as one-call num_seconds/subsec_nanos with the scaling in Spiral (rust_time_delta_ticks, used by total_seconds and days), add_days' cast through conv, stopwatch_elapsed_milliseconds from as_secs/subsec_millis (no u128 as i64), F# to_timestamp and time_span_of_micros arithmetic in Spiral. Two new tests (spans_convert_to_seconds_and_days, add_days_moves_the_timestamp; F# and Rust): the first exposed a PRE-EXISTING bug, total_seconds truncated to whole seconds (n / 10000000 in i64 before the f64 conversion: 1.5 s gave 1.0 on Rust and F#); fixed as (conv n : f64) / 10000000f64; its one caller converts to i32 anyway. Probes per body: Rust (chrono 0.4.45 cargo crate, --offline) and F# (dotnet fsi) give (1.5, 1.0), 1792152000000000, (1791892800000000, 0); the stopwatch reads 25..5000 ms after a 30 ms sleep. Lua and Python/Cuda arms left for a slice that can run them.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_15 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 15u32, "2026-10-24T18:18Z", "main", "main 14:1x: unicode heal LANDED 12:51 (GREEN 12:48). xml heal run 5 RED 13:57 was my own cross-slice break: the unicode heal changed rust_char_code's argument to the new rust_scalar nominal (Spiral char is u8 in Rust), and the xml slice still annotated option' char. Fixed (rust_scalar in the xml helpers and its unicode-presence check), plus a Zig fix found by the probe: convert.convert i32 -> convert.unativeint is identity on Zig (try_convert has no Zig mapping for the nominal), so the index stayed i32; the Zig helper now indexes with conv to u64 (Zig coerces u64 to usize). Probe: Rust (1, 5) and (2, 1); Zig (2, 1). code_point_count has no Zig arm on the tree either (same error on the baseline; the xml Zig tests do not call it). Requeued as main-serial-xml-6. Lib gap noted: convert.convert to unativeint on Zig.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_16 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 16u32, "2026-10-24T18:42Z", "main", "main 14:4x: the post-land document_test (main-document, 14:25) is still 15/17: today's lands broke the Cells livemd round trip of two more files, which the lib gate never checked: async.spi (the async heal's primitives block ends without the blank line before /// ### ignore) and the sm'.spi / sm'_real.spir pair (a missing blank line before /// ### (~#?) and a .spir heading sequence that no longer mirrors the .spi's format_fixed_digits). Fixes: lib-async-roundtrip queued (token-neutral blank line); the sm' pair waits until the 4 pending sm'.spi slices land (canonicalizing it now would move their anchors). The gate kit now runs %TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit\\roundtrip.exs on every touched .spi/.spir (candidate and tree): a file that round-trips on the tree and not on the candidate turns the verdict RED. Positive control: tree async.spi differs, fixed copy ok, json.spi ok.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_17 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 17u32, "2026-10-24T20:28Z", "main", "main 16:3x, correction to note 16 for the sm' pair: not a regression from today's lands. HEAD fails the same document_test; the lib-pair loop stops at its first failure, so sm' surfaced only after json/routes landed. Cause: sm'_real.spir kept 7 empty mirror headings with no .spi counterpart (index_of_char, is_white_space, builder_append_line, char_is_alphanumeric, os_string_as_ref, base64_decode, utf8_decode; their .spi sections were removed before HEAD). apps/cells/spi/document.spi interleave walks the .spir headings and, when one is absent from the rest of the .spi, appends the remaining .spir cells at the end, so the canonical form was not even a fixed point. Slice %TEMP%\\lib-sm-roundtrip\\apply.ps1: drop only empty unmatched .spir headings (refuses if one has content), then write Cells' canonical pair (a fixed point; code lines unchanged, checked; the installed formatter leaves it unchanged; idempotent). Queued: main-serial-sm-roundtrip (gate + land). Rule for later pairs: every .spir heading needs a .spi twin, or the interleave appends the rest.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_18 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 18u32, "2026-10-24T21:49Z", "main", "main 17:5x: LIB-SM-ROUNDTRIP landed (gate GREEN 17:15, land 17:47). Live check: the tree's sm'.spi / sm'_real.spir pair is a Cells canonical fixed point and round-trips byte for byte through livemd (both hashes equal), which is what the document_test lib-pair loop asserts. The next pair in that loop, if any, is the next thing to look at in the post-land document_test.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_19 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 19u32, "2026-10-25T11:39Z", "x", "2026-10-25 07:39 -04:00 x: two observations from x's code_points draft (X-GRANULAR-HEAL n10), both in lib/spiral/unicode.spi after the 10-24 unicode heal. (1) first_scalar's Gleam arm is still one macro chaining four host calls: option.from_result(result.map(list.first(string.to_utf_codepoints(s)), string.utf_codepoint_to_int)). With x's draft primitives gleam_codepoints and gleam_codepoint_code, it becomes Spiral over a host-list head bridge. (2) grapheme_list's Rust arm is a graphemes(..).map(..).collect() chain inside Rc::new; x's rust_scalars uses the same chars().collect() shape, so whichever rule decides that one (iterator chain = one host expression or not) applies to both. x lands code_points and is_white_space into unicode.spi with its gate; it doesn't touch first_scalar or grapheme_list.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_20 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 20u32, "2026-10-25T15:26Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:26 tibia takes date_time.spi's time_span_format, its largest host-text logic (rule 4: chars descending). Its TypeScript and Rust arms (two 6-line host programs computing days, hours, minutes, seconds and 100 ns ticks with sign, c format and hh/mm/ss substitution) become one Spiral time_span_ticks_text over i64 ticks, with one primitive per backend: rust_time_span_nanoseconds (num_nanoseconds) and typescript_time_span_milliseconds (the number itself); ticks = nanoseconds / 100 or milliseconds * 10000 in Spiral. Equivalent for whole milliseconds, which is all TypeScript's Date subtraction gives. New test time_span_format_custom_and_negative (rust, typescript; F# excluded because .NET rejects hh-mm-ss as a custom format) pins hh-mm-ss substitution and a negative c format. Slice scratch time-span-heal-apply.ps1 (2 replacements, round trip byte-exact); kit gate job-1792941907856 on date_time, land job-1792941929670 after it. Its anchors don't overlap LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's monotonic_milliseconds insertion, so either can land first.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_21 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 21u32, "2026-10-25T15:32Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:32 date_time.spi second slice, rule (2) casts: the Rust arms of min_value, max_value, unix_epoch, date_time_milliseconds and date_time_utc chained chrono calls with 'as u32' casts inside host text. They become Spiral over five primitives (naive_date_rust; rust_naive_date year month day; rust_date_at, rust_date_at_milli and rust_date_at_nano, one chrono call each) with the casts as conv in Spiral; 0 'as u32' left. New ///> rust -d chrono test calendar_constructors_agree_on_rust (date_time_utc and date_time_milliseconds at 1970-01-01 equal unix_epoch; min_value and max_value run). Slice scratch date-casts-heal-apply.ps1 (7 replacements, round trip byte-exact; anchors don't overlap the time_span or monotonic slices). Gate then land in one job: job-1792942299778 (kit gate-then-land.ps1, the reusable runner from now on).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_22 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 22u32, "2026-10-25T18:14Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 14:14 Both date_time gates went RED for kit reasons, and the cells show what each slice actually did. (1) Kit bug: gate.ps1 piped lib_gate into Select-Object -First 16, which stops the upstream script once 16 lines match, so with many reds lib_gate died before copying tests.tsv (the metrics dirs were left in %TEMP%); fixed by capturing the whole lib_gate output to <raw>.gate.log before filtering. (2) The surviving tests.tsv: time_span slice: time_span_format and _2 'failed' only on typescript, as 300 s cell timeouts (compile 190-209 s of each cell under load), while _3 and the new custom-and-negative test passed on rust and typescript; date-casts slice: its new test had a real type error ('=' needs primitive types; date_time is a backend_switch host type), now rewritten to compare chrono %f text for utc, milliseconds 250, unix_epoch, min_value and max_value. The other reds (unix_epoch cuda, date_time_milliseconds fsharp/cuda, to_timestamp*, hours, minutes) flip between the two runs, i.e. load. New gate rule, written before the next run: a candidate-only red counts only if it is red again when its test runs alone (lib_gate.ps1 gained an optional -Test list for --test; additive). Both slices now run in serial-b (job-1792945250381) after the process steps.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_23 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 23u32, "2026-10-25T20:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 16:02 The two date_time slices and LIB-OBJ-TO-STRING-C now gate on the remote kit worker node (kit2's go at 15:3x; node core 826D67C69119F61E built from the tree source; mirror tibia-gates, REMOTE-SYNC-OK digest FE6BB515A8B9E970; kit in C:\\home\\remote-stage\\tibia-kit). The first node run failed in mix because a mirror worktree has no apps/cells/deps (reported to kit2); after mix deps.get in the mirror the rerun is past mix. The local kit gate now takes -Tree, -Gate and -Candidate, and reuses a GREEN verdict newer than its apply script, so when the node verdicts are fetched into the local kit, serial-b's time_span and date-casts steps and serial-c's obj-to-string-c step go straight to their lands instead of re-gating.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_24 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 24u32, "2026-10-25T20:51Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 16:51 Node offload result: not usable for lib gates yet. After mix deps.get the Cells run starts, but LIB-OBJ-TO-STRING-C had 204/204 target rows red or not run (c, fsharp, gleam, lua, rust, zig failing; cuda, typescript, delphi, lean, python not run) and the run ends in a mix dependency error: the node lacks the per-backend toolchains and deps the lib tests target. Reported to kit2 with a readiness check (one small lib file gated on node vs review box, equal rows). Node RED verdicts are ignored (the local kit only reuses GREEN); the two date_time slices and obj-to-string-c stay in the local serials (serial-b steps time-span and date-casts; serial-c obj-to-string-c).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_25 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 25u32, "2026-10-25T21:12Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 17:12 Node rerun, read for signal (verdicts not used): both date_time slices show 51 baseline reds from the node's environment and exactly one candidate-only red each, the slice's NEW rust test, reported 'rust -d chrono: unavailable on this machine (spiral rust runner not found)'; the node baseline marks 18 rust targets unsupported. So beyond environment gaps, nothing in either slice fails where the node can run it; the time_span slice's typescript arm, which only timed out locally under load, ran there without a candidate-only red. Kit note: a new test that is unavailable (not failed) on a target should count as not-run, not red; the local gate keeps its rule until a real run shows the case. Local gates in serial-b remain the deciding ones.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_26 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 26u32, "2026-10-25T22:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 18:41 Node retry after the rust provisioning (17:57-18:2x): no change. The date_time baseline still marks rust -d chrono 18, cuda 21 and lua 12 targets unsupported in a remote.ps1 session, so the slices' new rust tests read 'unavailable' and the verdicts stay unusable. Reported to the compiler lane as a PATH-visibility gap. The date_time heals stay in tibia-serial-heals (job-1792967843150) locally.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_27 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 27u32, "2026-10-26T07:54Z", "tibia-lane", "07:54Z 07:5xZ Correction to note 26: the remote kit worker node now gates lib for real. Gaps gates of job-1792998526851 (Cells tests.tsv read on the node, not just the verdict line):\n- file_system candidate: 28 rows over fsharp, gleam, lua, rust, rust -d chrono, rust -d regex, rust -d sha2 regex and zig. 24 passed, 4 failed, the same 4 as the 27-row baseline. The new path_join_native_uses_the_platform_separator ran on rust and passed. Rust, chrono and lua no longer read 'unavailable'.\n- env (exit_with): 23 of 23 passed over cuda, fsharp, gleam, lua, rust and typescript.\n- am' (create_base, set_base): 42 of 42 passed over cuda, fsharp, lua, python, rust, typescript and zig.\nThese touched files are byte-identical on both boxes (file_system 2D2FE2B3, env 8A982F53, am' 6D5FD000), so the three GREEN verdicts were imported into the local gate kit, and tibia-serial-gaps goes straight to the anchored lands.\nRule for the next node gates: read the candidate tests.tsv. A new test whose row reads unavailable or not run voids the verdict, even when it is GREEN.\nHeal gates on the node need a lib base with the slices they stack on. Job-1792998723460's REDs (process, time_span, date-casts, obj-to-string-c) were all 'did not apply to the copy', never test reds.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_28 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 28u32, "2026-10-26T08:22Z", "tibia-lane", "08:22Z 08:2xZ resultm.spi slice LIB-RESULT-DEBUG (tibia; nobody had claimed resultm).\nBefore: get's and unwrap_err's Rust arms each inlined the same 6-line host program in one macro: a SpiralDebug struct, two traits and two impls doing autoref specialization (Debug output, or the type name for types without Debug), plus the format!.\nAfter: one :~ rust_debug_message (prefix) (x). Its global \"\"\"...\"\"\" holds only the declarations, which rule 3 allows: the struct, both traits and both impls, written once, in base.spi's SpiralUse form. Its body is one host expression: Rc::<str>::from(format!(\"{}{}\", prefix, (&SpiralDebug(&x)).show())). Both arms become `Rust = \\ () => rust_debug_message \"resultm.get / Error x: \" x` (and the unwrap_err text).\n- Why not sm'.format_debug: resultm is module 12 and sm' is module 26, so neither format_debug nor ++# is reachable, and !!!!StaticStringConcat is compile-time only (PartEval.fs:2611).\n- New test rust_debug_message_shows_debug_values (rust): (\"x: 5\", \"s: \\\"a\\\"\").\nProof on the consumer path (scratch lib-result-debug/probe-run.ps1). The same programs are compiled to Rust against the tree lib and against a patched copy, built with rustc and run.\n- resultm.get on Error \"boom\" panics with `resultm.get / Error x: \"boom\"` on both libs.\n- resultm.get on an Error holding a closure (no Debug) panics with `resultm.get / Error x: &alloc::rcs::rc::Rc<dyn core::ops::function::Fn(i32) -> i32>` on both libs, byte-identical. That proves the type-name fallback.\n- rust_debug_message prints `x: 5` and `s: \"a\"`.\nFormatter clean, 0 lines over 120, 1 SpiralDebug declaration instead of 2. resultm.spi base B5AEA6562895C673 is identical on this box and on the remote kit worker node's mirror.\nGate on the node: job-1793002971950-tibia-result-debug-gate (generic lib-gates-job.ps1, which prints every non-passed tests.tsv row). The land step is packed into the queued tibia-serial-gaps, which reuses a GREEN node verdict once it is fetched.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_29 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 29u32, "2026-10-26T08:39Z", "tibia-lane", "08:39Z 08:3xZ Second resultm slice, LIB-TRY-LUA (base.spi and resultm.spi). resultm.from_try kept its own try in four host arms: Lua as 12 host statements (locals, pcall, if/else), Python as a try/except statement block, TypeScript as an IIFE, and F#. That duplicated base.try, the granular single home from main's LIB-BASE-TRY-GRANULAR.\n- from_try becomes Spiral over base.try: `try (\\ () => Ok (fn ()) : result t exn) caught |> optionm.value`, where caught wraps ex_fn's exn in Some (Error ...). All four from_try host arms are gone. Side effect: Rust gains from_try through catch_unwind_rust. Before, Rust had no arm, and a Rust consumer failed to build with 'backend_switch: this lib/spiral function has no Rust arm yet'.\n- base.try's Lua arm called fn without catching. It now uses one-call primitives: lua_pcall ({pcall(fn)}, a :! lua_attempt), lua_attempt_ok ([1]), lua_attempt_value ([2] : t) and lua_attempt_error ([2] : exn). The branching is a Spiral if. On Lua this changes the two lib callers of try, and both intend to catch: testing._assert_fn and common.retry_fn (whose retry loop never retried on Lua, because a failure escaped the try).\n- New tests: base try_catches_failures_and_keeps_values and resultm from_try_keeps_values_and_catches_failures, both on lua, fsharp, cuda, rust and typescript.\nStem check: from_try's only callers are convert (F# and Python arms) and file_system (F#), so no repo reaches the deleted Lua or TS arms. Positive control: the same Grep finds resultm.get in dice.\nProof (scratch lib-try-lua/probe-run.ps1): programs compiled against the tree lib and against a copy patched with LIB-RESULT-DEBUG plus this slice, which composes the two resultm slices. Run on Lua (lua 5.1), Rust (rustc) and TypeScript (node).\n- Tree: from_try prints '5 | caught' on Lua and TS, and fails to build on Rust (no arm). base.try prints '5 | caught' on Rust and TS, and on Lua dies with 'probe.lua:26: boom'.\n- Patched: '5 | caught' for from_try and base.try on all three backends.\nFormatter clean on both files, 0 lines over 120. The bases are identical on the remote kit worker node: base 4E28EB421DBB5F8F, resultm B5AEA6562895C673, common D658CC64AF3DA35A.\nGate on the node: job-1793003995132-tibia-try-lua-gate (files base, resultm, common), after the result-debug gate. Lands go into tibia-serial-gaps in the proven order: result-debug, then try-lua.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_30 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 30u32, "2026-10-26T08:50Z", "tibia-lane", "08:50Z 08:4xZ Third resultm slice, LIB-RESULT-UNBOX. It deletes dead arms and heals nothing: rule 4 applied at arm level.\n- unbox's Lua arm (8 host statements: locals, nil test, if/else) and its Python arm (the same as a statement block) never run. result'_union () is !+ on Lua and Python, so unbox takes `$'!x ' : result t u` there, and so does box, whose Lua and Python Ok and Error lines are also unreachable.\n- Found by proof: the Lua probe's generated code for unbox was the identity (local v22 = v21) on the tree, and no 'local r = nil' was emitted.\n- The slice removes those 2 blocks and 4 lines. Its apply refuses unless result'_union still reads !+ for both Lua and Python.\nProof (scratch lib-result-unbox/probe-run.ps1): one copy patched with all three resultm slices (result-debug, try-lua, result-unbox), which also proves the anchors compose. On Lua, box (Ok 5), then map''' (+1), then unbox prints 'ok 6', and a nil result' prints 'nil', byte-identical to the tree. Formatter clean.\nLeft in resultm unbox: the F#, Rust and Gleam arms are single host match/case expressions. Bridging them with is_ok plus value primitives would clone the payload on Rust's hot path. That trade-off gets its own decision rule before any slice.\nGate on the node: job-1793004636955-tibia-result-unbox-gate. The land is packed into tibia-serial-gaps after try-lua.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_31 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 31u32, "2026-10-26T08:56Z", "tibia-lane", "08:56Z 08:5xZ listm'.spi slice LIB-LIST-CASTS, rule 2 (no as-casts in host text). The Rust arms of fold', fold_back', filter' and distinct' each held `(!list).len() as i32` and `(!list)[!i as usize].clone()`. They now use two one-call primitives: rust_list'_length, which is `($'(!list).len()' : convert.unativeint) |> convert.convert`, the env.spi:150 shape, and rust_list'_item, which converts i to convert.unativeint in Spiral and then indexes `(!list)[!index].clone()`. convert is module 13 and listm' module 14, so convert is reachable.\n- The apply refuses if the length-cast text appears anywhere besides the four loops.\n- Proof (scratch lib-list-casts/probe-run.ps1): one Rust program runs fold', fold_back', filter' and distinct' over box [3; 1; 3; 2], against the tree and a patched copy (rustc, then run). Output is identical on both: Cons(20,30,10,30) | Cons(4,2,4,3) | Cons(2,3,3) | Cons(2,1,3). The generated Rust goes from 6 'len() as i32' casts to 0.\n- Formatter clean. Base 289CECC552F64E5E is identical on the remote kit worker node.\n- Zig's matching @as/@intCast arms stay as they are, because of the noted gap: convert to unativeint is identity on Zig.\nGate on the node: job-1793005022670-tibia-list-casts-gate. The land is packed into tibia-serial-gaps.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_32 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 32u32, "2026-10-26T09:09Z", "tibia-lane", "09:09Z 09:0xZ testing.spi slice LIB-THROWS-TRY. This is the third copy of try in lib. testing._throws had an is_rust special case over catch_unwind_rust plus F#, Python (statement block), TypeScript (IIFE) and Zig arms. It is now Spiral over base.try: `try (\\ () => fn (); none) caught |> optionm.value`, where caught ex = Some (Some ex). base.try already routes Rust through catch_unwind_rust, so Rust behaves as before. Lua and Gleam gain _throws: before, a Lua consumer failed at run time with 'backend.backend_switch / record_type_try_find'.\nConsumers by stem: dice lib tests calculate_dice_count_5/6/7 (rust), sm' format_exception (fsharp, cuda, rust, typescript), listm' item_2 (fsharp).\nProof (scratch lib-throws-try/probe-run.ps1): _throws on failwith \"test\", then sm'.format_exception, and _throws on a thunk that doesn't throw.\n- Rust: 'test | none' on both tree and patched.\n- TypeScript: 'Error: test | none' on both.\n- Lua, with LIB-TRY-LUA applied first (the land order): 'probe.lua:16: test | none'. The tree fails at run time; this slice alone lets the error escape, because base.try's Lua arm only catches after try-lua.\nFormatter clean; testing.spi base BF2AADFDACD09E2C is identical on the node.\nGate on the node: job-1793005792320-tibia-throws-try-gate (testing and listm'; sm' gates on the node are pending the lib_gate diagnosis, job-1793004142496). The land is packed into tibia-serial-gaps after try-lua.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_33 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 33u32, "2026-10-26T09:22Z", "tibia-lane", "09:22Z 09:2xZ seq.spi slice LIB-SEQ-LENGTH (rule 2, cast). seq.length''s Rust arm $'(!items.len() as `int)' becomes ($'!items.len()' : convert.unativeint) |> convert.convert : int. The annotation is needed: without it, the conversion target inside the backend_switch is an unbound metavar (ETY-ValueRestriction at seq.spi:345). Proof (scratch lib-seq-length/probe-run.ps1): seq.of_list' over box [3; 1; 3; 2; 7], then length', prints 5 with the tree lib and with the patched lib (Rust, rustc); host 'len() as' casts drop from 1 to 0. Formatter clean; seq.spi base 2AAA221CA673F880 is identical on the node. Gate on the node: job-1793006586197-tibia-seq-length-gate. The land is packed into tibia-serial-gaps.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_34 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 34u32, "2026-10-26T10:00Z", "tibia-lane", "10:00Z 10:0xZ testing.spi slice LIB-CONTAINS-CHAR (rule 2, cast). __assert_contains' Rust arm for a char needle read $'!a.contains(!b as char)'. Spiral char is u8 on Rust, so that was a u8 to char cast in host text. It is now a one-call primitive, rust_char_of_byte (c : char) : unicode.rust_scalar = $'char::from(!c)' (From<u8>, no cast), and the arm reads := scalar = rust_char_of_byte ($'!b ' : char) followed by $'!a.contains(!scalar)'. The primitive lives in testing.spi to stay clear of x's unicode work. Proof (scratch lib-contains-char/probe-run.ps1, with LIB-THROWS-TRY applied first, which composes both testing.spi slices): a Rust program runs _throws over _assert_contains 'r' and then 'z' on \"troll\" and prints 'false | true' on both tree and patched. 'as char' in the generated Rust drops from 3 to 1; the one left comes from sm'_real's format_debug char arm, which is main's file. Formatter clean, 0 lines over 120. Gate on the node: job-1793008875316-tibia-contains-char-gate. The land is packed into tibia-serial-gaps after throws-try. The testing.spi Zig loops (for, break) at 252-253 remain; they need a Zig slice length/index primitive pair, a follow-up.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_35 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 35u32, "2026-10-26T10:48Z", "tibia-lane", "10:48Z 10:4xZ Correction to note 29 (LIB-TRY-LUA): its first node gate was RED, correctly.\n- What failed: common's retry_fn and retry_fn_2 failed on fsharp, again when run alone, and passed on the baseline. resultm's map/typescript red passed alone, so that one was load.\n- Reproduced locally (scratch lib-try-lua/retry-repro.ps1, an F# consumer of retry_fn): the patched lib fails to compile. On F#, retry_fn's trace calls file_system.trace_file, which calls resultm.from_try with a thunk whose t is unconstrained outside host text.\n- Cause 1: routing F#'s from_try through base.try gave 'Pattern miss' in convert's `from_try ... |> resultm.ok`. The partial evaluator can't match through base.try's host try/with macro.\n- Cause 2: shared local helpers (caught, through_try) at from_try's top level gave 'Got: i64, Expected: result time_span exn' for a date_time caller.\n- Fix to the cut: only the Lua arm changes, written inline in the arm. The 12 host statements become `try (\\ () => Ok (fn ()) : result t exn) caught |> optionm.value`, with base.try's new pcall primitives underneath. F#, Python, TypeScript and Zig keep their original arms. F#'s is already one host expression; Python's and TypeScript's try blocks wait for a rule on try-as-a-statement hosts, and Python has no expression try. Rust stays without a from_try arm, as on the tree, so the new test drops its rust marker.\n- Re-proof: the F# retry_fn consumer compiles and runs like the tree. from_try and base.try print '5 | caught' on Lua and TypeScript, and base.try also on Rust. LIB-THROWS-TRY on top, now including F#, prints 'System.Exception: test | none' on both libs.\n- Re-gate on the node: job-1793011562381-tibia-try-lua-gate2, with files base, resultm, common and convert (convert added as from_try's F# and Python consumer).\nAlso gated on the node: LIB-RESULT-UNBOX GREEN (11 of 11) and LIB-LIST-CASTS GREEN (45 of 45).\nGate-kit fix found by base checks: lib files changed after their node gates ran: listm'.spi (289CECC5 to C6FA3415, 06:07) and file_system.spi (2D2FE2B3 to ACF6BC84). gate.ps1 used to reuse any GREEN verdict newer than its apply script without looking at the base.\n- Verdicts may now carry a `bases file=hash;...` line, and gate.ps1 reuses a verdict only while every listed base matches (tested: matching base reuses, a forged hash re-gates with a stated reason, no bases line behaves as before).\n- The stale LIB-PATH-JOIN-NATIVE import was removed. LIB-LIST-CASTS wasn't imported. Both re-gate locally in tibia-serial-gaps on the real base (both still apply cleanly). The other imports carry bases lines.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_36 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 36u32, "2026-10-26T11:06Z", "tibia-lane", "11:06Z 11:0xZ Pre-land check of the whole tibia-serial-gaps lib stack, after listm'.spi and file_system.spi moved under it (scratch gaps-compose-check.ps1). (1) Each of the 12 packed slice scripts dry-runs cleanly on today's live lib: path-join-native, exit-with, array-base, result-debug, try-lua (revised), result-unbox, list-casts, throws-try, seq-length, par-gleam, contains-char and obj-to-string-c. (2) The 11 gaps slices, applied in land order to one lib copy, all apply, which proves the resultm and testing slices compose. (3) One consumer exercises every touched module: listm'.fold', seq.length', par'.map, resultm.from_try then ok, testing._throws, file_system.path_join_native and env.exit_with. It prints '12 3 14 a\\b | tried 4 | thrown', exit 0, on F# (dotnet fsi) and on Rust (rustc, with the from_try call swapped for Some, since from_try has no Rust arm on the tree either and fails there with 'no Rust arm' plus E0614). Coordination from main's 07:00 relay (both nodes until 21:00): the probes above ran here, CPU-light in a few minutes; heavy lib gates keep going to the remote kit worker node one at a time, and tree-writing lands stay local.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_37 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 37u32, "2026-10-26T11:19Z", "tibia-lane", "11:19Z 11:2xZ date_time.spi slice LIB-DATE-CASTS2 (rule 2: casts and arithmetic out of host text), on the base left by today's time-span and date-casts lands (6D530E6A98F37ED9).\n(1) time_span x, Rust arm: '(chrono::TimeDelta::microseconds((!x as i64) / 10) + chrono::TimeDelta::nanoseconds(((!x as i64) % 10) * 100))' becomes rust_time_delta_of_ticks (conv x). That is Spiral over three one-call primitives, rust_time_delta_microseconds, rust_time_delta_nanoseconds and rust_time_delta_sum, with /10, %10 and *100 in Spiral.\n(2) get_utc_offset: the offset chain stays one host expression returning i32; 'as i64' becomes conv and TimeDelta::seconds its own call.\n(3) guid_sub_micro_digit: timestamp_subsec_nanos as u32, and '/ 100 % 10 as i64' becomes Spiral arithmetic plus conv.\nProof (scratch lib-date-casts2/probe-run.ps1): a cargo crate on chrono 0.4.45. The cached 0.4.23 predates TimeDelta, so it was pinned >= 0.4.40. The same Spiral program, built against the tree and the patched lib, prints identical output: ticks round-trip 15000037 and -15000037 (the negative case exercises truncating / and %), and guid_sub_micro_digit 0. Host 'as i64' in the generated Rust drops from 5 to 3. Formatter clean; the one over-120 line in date_time.spi (153, a Zig line) is already on the tree.\nGate on the remote kit worker node: job-1793013639091-tibia-date-casts2-gate. Its mirror's date_time.spi was synced to the live file first (hash-verified 6D530E6A98F37ED9). If the node again runs 0 date_time tests, the packed tibia-serial-gaps step gates locally.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_38 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 38u32, "2026-10-26T11:32Z", "tibia-lane", "11:32Z 11:3xZ date_time.spi slice LIB-DATE-LUA-OR (rule 1, no and/or fallbacks in Lua host text): 6 sites.\n- os.date: the date and time fallbacks become one-call os.date macros typed optionm'.option' string, then optionm'.default_value' \" (whose Lua arm already does the nil test in Spiral). The year-below-1000 format choice moves into a Spiral ?, and gsub stays one host call.\n- os.time: min_value, max_value, unix_epoch, date_time_milliseconds and date_time_utc each become a one-call os.time typed optionm'.option' date_time (or int), then default_value' with the old fallback: -62135596800, 253402300799 and 0 as date_time literals, and 0i32. 446 and 488 were the same line, so their anchors include the line after.\nProof (scratch lib-date-lua-or/probe-run.ps1, on a copy with LIB-DATE-CASTS2 applied first, so the two date_time slices compose): lua 5.1 prints '-62135596800 253402300799 14400 | 1793012889 1793012889 | 10-26-26 07:08:09 AM | 2026 | 12-31-69 11:04:05 PM' on both tree and patched. That covers min (the year-1 fallback fires on Windows), max, epoch, both built dates, the default and explicit formats, and the year-999 two-digit path. Host ' or ' on os.time/os.date lines in the generated Lua drops from 12 to 0. Formatter clean; new lines at most 120.\nGate on the node: job-1793014240142-tibia-date-lua-or-gate, after date-casts2's. Lands are packed in tibia-serial-gaps in that order.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_39 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 39u32, "2026-10-26T11:58Z", "tibia-lane", "11:58Z 11:5xZ My mistake on the remote kit worker node's gate mirror (tibia-gaps), found and fixed. At 11:1xZ I copied only the live date_time.spi into the mirror's lib, so LIB-DATE-CASTS2 would gate on its real base. That file already uses erlang_term (today's process split), and the mirror's package.spiproj and process modules were still pre-split. Every Cells run on the mirror that loaded date_time then failed to type-check: 'date_time.spi:1133 ETY-UnboundVariable: erlang_term' in monotonic_milliseconds.\nAffected:\n- LIB-PAR-GLEAM's RED: void, every test cell failed to compile on that error.\n- LIB-CONTAINS-CHAR's GREEN: void; its baseline and candidate were both broken, which is why gleam and fsharp rows read failed or not run.\n- the running LIB-TRY-LUA re-gate (try-lua-gate2): stopped, process tree stopped.\nFix: the whole live lib/spiral (1,147 files, without target, _build, .cache and node_modules) was zipped, uploaded, expanded and robocopy /MIR'd over the mirror's lib. The node now matches this box on package.spiproj 7193C5EF, date_time 6D530E6A, process 72A16CF8 and erlang_term B2BA83E4. The queued date-casts2 and date-lua-or gates now run on the consistent mirror. Requeued after them: try-lua-gate3 (job-1793015876485), contains-char-gate2 (job-1793015919659) and par-gleam-gate2.\nRule from now on: a node gate mirror is refreshed as a whole lib snapshot, never one file, and only between gates. Every bases line still names the files a verdict depends on.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_40 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 40u32, "2026-10-26T12:35Z", "tibia-lane", "12:35Z 12:4xZ First two shims under GRANULAR-LINT rule (8). (1) LIB-SHIM-SHA256 (crypto.spi): hash_text's Zig arm was one 247-char blk (an out-param digest, then hash, bytesToHex and dupe), the file's only line over 120. It becomes zig_sha256_hex, whose one host expression calls global spiral_out_sha256_hex: a var digest, then calls only. The arm is Zig => \\ () => zig_sha256_hex input. New ///> zig test zig_sha256_hex_matches_known_digests ('' and '\\n'). Proof (scratch lib-shim-sha256/probe-run.ps1): crypto.hash_text compiled to Zig against the tree and the patched lib, run with zig run, prints e3b0c442...b855 and 01ba4719...546b on both. Lines over 120 in crypto.spi go from 1 to 0. Gate on the node: job-1793017761158-tibia-shim-sha256-gate; land packed in tibia-serial-gaps-lib. (2) LIB-PAR-RUST (spiral_scope_map), see LIB-PAR-MAP note 15. Left: (3) spiral_cfg_local_fields for trace.spi:328. Its proof needs a wasm32 compile against near_sdk besides the Windows run, so it goes through a cargo crate and comes next.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_41 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 41u32, "2026-10-26T12:42Z", "tibia-lane", "12:42Z 12:5xZ Third shim under GRANULAR-LINT rule (8): LIB-SHIM-CLOCK (trace.spi:328, the 1,214-char Rust clock line). Two cfg-class shims in one global, each defined under every cfg so all targets link. spiral_cfg_local_clock() -> Option<[i64; 3]> returns hour, minute and second: GetLocalTime on Windows, localtime_r on unix (secs through c_long::try_from, fields through i64::from: calls, no casts), None on wasm32 and the fallback. spiral_cfg_epoch_nanos() -> u64: near_sdk block_timestamp on wasm32, SystemTime as_nanos through u64::try_from elsewhere. Spiral side: rust_local_clock and rust_epoch_nanos (one host expression each), three one-call field readers, and local_seconds_of_day, which is h*3600 + m*60 + s via conv, or the epoch fallback nanos / 1e9 % 86400 (equal to the old wasm (secs/3600)%24 split for the time of day). trace's Rust arm becomes := seconds_of_day : u64 = local_seconds_of_day (). New ///> rust test local_seconds_of_day_is_within_a_day. Proof (scratch lib-shim-clock/probe-run.ps1): the shim text, extracted from the patched trace.spi with a near_sdk stub module, type-checks (rustc --emit=metadata) on x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu and wasm32-unknown-unknown. A Rust consumer of local_seconds_of_day prints 31233, equal to PowerShell's local time of day at that second (diff 0). Formatter clean; lines over 120 in trace.spi go from 3 to 2 (the two left are pre-existing). Gate on the node: job-1793018635336-tibia-shim-clock-gate (trace base 52B2D34F matches); land packed in tibia-serial-gaps-lib. With LIB-SHIM-SHA256 and LIB-PAR-RUST this closes the three sites GRANULAR-LINT rule (8) was accepted for.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_42 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 42u32, "2026-10-26T13:32Z", "tibia-lane", "13:32Z 13:2xZ (1) LIB-DATE-CASTS2's node gate is GREEN but deliberately not imported. Its red rows are red on the node baseline too: rust -d chrono to_timestamp, timestamp_round_trip, hours, minutes and seconds, and gleam's guid round trips. Those are exactly the time_span paths the slice touches. The node's chrono runner fails them either way, so the verdict says nothing about the change (the weak-GREEN rule in the remote-node memory). tibia-serial-gaps-lib re-gates it locally on a working chrono; the local cargo probe on chrono 0.4.45 already showed identical tick round trips. LIB-DATE-LUA-OR gets the same treatment unless its lua rows run on the node. (2) Style fix from the list-sugar rule (updated 10-26: never Nil, use [] and | [] =>): LIB-PAR-GLEAM's par'.spi had 8 Nil and now has 0 (backup par'-with-nil.spi). It re-proved: formatter clean, compiles to Gleam and F#, gleam run prints 338350. The node copy was updated before par-gleam-gate2 started. A grep of my other packed slice scripts finds no Nil or 'Cons (' in code.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_43 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 43u32, "2026-10-26T15:59Z", "main", "10-26 11:59 main: lib census 11:5x (scratchpad macro-rule.ps1 over lib/spiral: 684 flagged; TEMP macro-rule-lib-1026.tsv), triaged by hand on the reliable classes (52 casts, 78 arith; most of the 540 'flow' are the known false positives). date_time.spi (16) is tibia's, in flight (LIB-DATE-CASTS2 / LIB-DATE-LUA-OR), as are crypto, trace and par' (rule (8) shims). Not violations: convert.spi's `(!x as u8)`, `as u16/u64/f32/f64` and round_ties_even are the conversion primitives themselves (the cast is that module's job); base.spi 50-56 `1 + 1` are test literals; file_system's 'import * as' rows are JS module syntax, and MAIN_SEPARATOR as u8 is one host constant. Real and unclaimed: listm'.spi's Rust arms (4x `(!list).len() as i32`, 4x `(!list)[!i as usize].clone()`) and Lua arms (6x `[!i + 1]`, Lua's 1-based index). Slice LIB-LISTM-INDEX-CASTS (TEMP lib-listm-index-casts/apply.ps1) adds three one-call primitives before rev': rust_list_length (len() as convert.unativeint, then convert.convert in Spiral), rust_list_item (index converted in Spiral, then [!at].clone()) and lua_list_item (i + 1 in Spiral, then !list[!at]). It moves all 14 sites, leaves 0 host casts or index arithmetic in those arms and 0 lines over 120. Zig arms (`@intCast(!i ...)`) stay until CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT, since convert.convert from unativeint emits an uncast assignment on Zig. Typechecks on a lib copy, and the negative control (a string as rust_list_item's index) is rejected, so the check sees the generic definitions. Chain queued: job-1793030321888 (gate on listm'.spi, rust + lua among its rows, then land). Remaining real convert.spi sites, next: from_str_radix's `(!base) as u32` and the Delphi StrToInt(##36 + !x) prefixes.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_44 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 44u32, "2026-10-26T16:01Z", "main", "10-26 12:01 main: LIB-CONVERT-RADIX drafted and queued (TEMP lib-convert-radix/apply.ps1): convert_i32_base's Rust arm cast '(!base) as u32' in host text. Now ':= radix : u32 = conv base' in Spiral, and the host call takes !radix. One anchored edit, dry run clean, 0 casts left in that arm. convert_i32_base backs the int-from-string conversions, and convert.spi's try_convert test runs on rust, so the gate exercises it. Left in the same function, written down: the Delphi arm's StrToInt(##36 + !x) prefixes ('$'/'%'/'&' + x concatenated in host text) need a run-time concat that convert.spi can't reach (sm'_operators comes later in the module order), so they wait for a Delphi concat primitive placed before convert.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_45 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 45u32, "2026-10-26T16:03Z", "main", "10-26 12:03 main: LIB-AM-TRY-ITEM drafted and queued (TEMP lib-am-try-item/apply.ps1). am'.spi:724: try_item's Rust arm was '(if !i < 0 { None } else { !a.borrow().get(!i as usize).cloned() })', flow plus a cast in host text. The function already had a pure-Spiral in_bounds_item (i >= 0 && i < length a, then index i a), used by the Zig and Lean arms, so the Rust arm becomes 'Rust = in_bounds_item'. That leaves no host code in it, and one implementation for three backends. try_item had no test; the slice adds try_item_is_none_out_of_bounds (rust, zig, fsharp): indices -1, 0, 1, 2 over [10; 20] give [-1; 10; 20; -1] with None coded -1. Option equality is avoided, since union equality is unproven here. Dry run clean, 0 lines over 120. Other am'.spi slices (main's json_scan length_base, tibia's LIB-ARRAY-BASE) edit other anchors, and the land kit's base check re-gates a moved base.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_46 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 46u32, "2026-10-26T16:07Z", "main", "10-26 12:07 main: LIB-SM-FORMAT-PRECISION drafted (TEMP lib-sm-format-precision/apply.ps1): sm'.format_fixed's Rust arm cast '!decimals as usize' in host text; now ':= precision : convert.unativeint = convert.convert decimals' in Spiral, and the host format! takes !precision. Rust isn't routed through format_fixed_digits (as C, Gleam and Zig are), because format_fixed_avoids_ties pins Rust's rounding. Packed into the queued LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF chain (job-1793027588823, its gate not yet started, waiting for a slot): that slice's apply.ps1 now also runs the format step, dry or -Apply, right after its own edit. One whole-file sm'.spi gate (about 2 h under today's load, and noisy) then covers both, instead of two. Proven on a scratch copy of sm'.spi: both edits present, anchors unique. The pre-pack script is kept as apply.before-pack.ps1.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_47 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 47u32, "2026-10-26T17:12Z", "main", "10-26 13:12 main: sm' pack extended and checked (12:5x-13:1x). The packed LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF apply now carries three sm'.spi edits: the Zig index_of/last_index_of arms; format_fixed's precision (conv in Spiral); and strip_prefix, whose '(!prefix) as char' becomes the infallible conversion call char::from(!prefix), Spiral's char being a byte on Rust, with the result typed as the function's option' (rust.ref str). The lib-copy typecheck is GREEN (exit 0) and the control is rejected (exit 5), so the check sees the edit. That control annotates strip_prefix's host call as i32 against the declared option' return. The first control (convert.convert applied to a string literal) was accepted, because try_convert parses strings, so it proved nothing and was replaced, not counted. The first check run was VOID ('Attestation timed out after 30000 ms', exit 3, load). The chain job-1793027588823 gates and lands all three; its gate hasn't started yet (it waits for a slot, behind kit's FOLLOWUP).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_48 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 48u32, "2026-10-26T17:14Z", "main", "10-26 13:14 main: LIB-PARSING-CONCAT-CASTS drafted, checked and queued (TEMP lib-parsing-concat-casts/apply.ps1). parsing.concat_rev's Rust fast path (4+ pieces) cast three times in host text: 'vec![0u8; !total as usize]' and '[!start as usize..!end as usize]'. All three conversions now happen in Spiral (size, from, upto : convert.unativeint via convert.convert), and the host text keeps only the buffer calls. New ///> rust test concat_rev_keeps_byte_order_past_three_pieces: [\"d\"; \"c\"; \"é\"; \"a\"] gives \"aécd\". A multi-byte piece past three pieces reaches the fast path and checks the byte-length offsets, which matters because the path ends in from_utf8_unchecked. It uses a literal \"é\" because unicode comes after parsing in the module order. Lib-copy typecheck GREEN; control rejected (a string returned where the fold carries the i32 offset, exit 5). Chain job-1793034801882: gate on parsing.spi, then land. With this, the lib census's cheap provable sites are all drafted; what remains needs a new primitive or test first (runtime.spi:51 capacity vector, runtime.spi:638 arg_index on F#).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_49 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 49u32, "2026-10-26T17:24Z", "main", "10-26 13:24 main: LIB-RUNTIME-CASTS drafted, checked and queued (TEMP lib-runtime-casts/apply.ps1). I corrected my own note 48: the two runtime.spi sites didn't need a new primitive, because they are the same cast pattern. (1) split_args: Vec::with_capacity(!n as usize) becomes a Spiral 'capacity : convert.unativeint = convert.convert n'; the host text keeps the one allocation call. Its existing ///> rust test split_args covers it. (2) arg_index: the whole is_rust split (Rust 'as usize', F# type-cast text) collapses into 'index : convert.unativeint = convert.convert value' on every backend. Lib-copy typecheck GREEN; control rejected (index bound as i32, exit 5). Chain job-1793035301036. Census rerun 13:2x: every lib host-text cast is now drafted, a convert primitive body, or owned elsewhere. testing.spi:236 '!b as char' is next (char::from, like strip_prefix). date_time.spi:619 is left to fiscal, which has a pending date_time.spi draft. xml.spi:71 is in fiscal's layout land. sm'.spi:1180 is a whole host block (char_indices/map/unwrap_or) and needs its own rewrite with a test.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_50 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 50u32, "2026-10-26T17:27Z", "main", "10-26 13:27 main: sm'.builder_insert Rust arm (sm'.spi:1180-1181) is a whole host block: clone, char_indices().nth(!i as usize), map, unwrap_or(len), insert_str. The plan, by the granular rule: lift the Spiral helpers that already exist into sm', namely xml.code_point_width (xml.spi:23) and xpath_eval's private byte_offset (xpath_eval.spi:301), which walks code points to a byte offset clamped at the length. Then compute 'at' in Spiral, so the host text keeps one call: clone + insert_str at !at (a unativeint from convert.convert). Add ///> rust to test builder_insert (\"ab\" |> insert 1 \"gh\" = \"aghb\"), plus a multi-byte case (\"éb\" |> insert 1 \"x\" = \"éxb\") and a past-end clamp case. On hold by a pre-written condition, not by guess: xml.spi and xpath_eval.spi are both in fiscal's pending landings (LIB-TARGET-GAP layout; S2d node check job-1793034540282), and moving their helpers now would stale those anchors. Resume when fiscal reports both landed, then re-point xml/xpath_eval callers at the sm' helper in the same slice. Module order allows it: sm' comes before xml and xpath_eval.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_51 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 51u32, "2026-10-26T17:28Z", "main", "10-26 13:28 main: LIB-TESTING-CHAR-CAST queued (chain job-1793035684942; TEMP lib-testing-char-cast/apply.ps1). testing.__assert_contains' Rust char arm '!a.contains(!b as char)' becomes '!a.contains(char::from(!b))', the same lossless byte-to-char conversion as sm'.strip_prefix. The lib-copy typecheck is VOID as proof: its control (the arm annotated i32 instead of bool) was ACCEPTED, exit 0. The arm sits inside a '?: t ?| char =>' type-case of a generic function, and untaken type-case arms aren't checked until instantiation. That is unlike plain generic bodies, which are (sm'/parsing/runtime controls rejected). So the proof moves to execution: the slice adds '///> rust' to test assert_contains_2 (\"abcd\" |> _assert_contains 'b'), which instantiates exactly that arm. The rule for accepting the GREEN, written before the run: the gate log must show assert_contains_2 passed on rust. A GREEN without that row is VOID.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_52 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 52u32, "2026-10-27T02:50Z", "tibia-lane", "02:50Z Status of tibia's slices from notes 28-33, after tibia-serial-gaps-lib5 (job-1793062501895, requeued after the kit rename):\n- Landed, gated GREEN: LIB-RESULT-DEBUG (earlier, in gaps-lib4), LIB-RESULT-UNBOX, LIB-THROWS-TRY and LIB-SEQ-LENGTH.\n- LIB-LIST-CASTS: superseded, nothing to land. Its 'Rust loop 1' anchor matched 0 because listm'.spi changed at 20:29 local, where main's LIB-LISTM-INDEX-CASTS replaced every '(!list).len() as i32' and '[!i as usize].clone()' with rust_list_length plus an index-with-conversion primitive (the same heal under different names).\n- LIB-TRY-LUA: gate GREEN. The land got its clear samples, but the apply-time re-check saw a Cells run appear (erl 70016) and refused safely, with no write. Requeued as tibia-lib-try-lua-land2 (job-1793069335405) at the end of the local chain, reusing the GREEN verdict.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_53 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 53u32, "2026-10-27T06:56Z", "tibia-lane", "06:56Z LIB-TRY-LUA LANDED (tibia-lib-try-lua-land3, about 03:00 local, reusing its GREEN verdict).\nFiles: base.spi 2D3CA4A6D144D776 (lua_pcall present) and resultm.spi 33094A9EC47E8A65; the live hashes equal the land's.\nAttempts 1 and 2 expired the land's 90-minute reader wait. The reader that never left was the long-lived Cells compiler daemon (CompilerDaemon.serve_app), which tibia's guard counted and main's lib-gate-kit guard ignores. tibia's process-handle-kit land.ps1 now uses main's filter: it skips the daemon and any --path outside C:\\home\\git\\, waits up to 600 min, and also skips notebooks whose .spi paths all sit in %TEMP% or spiral-bin\\runs. Attempt 3 landed about 15 min after starting.\nAll of tibia's slices from notes 28-33 are now landed or superseded (LIB-LIST-CASTS by main's LIB-LISTM-INDEX-CASTS).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_54 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 54u32, "2026-10-29T13:35Z", "main", "10-29 09:3x main: CENSUS for slicing. kit lint macros lib/spiral (kit.exe A5B55974, 908 ms): 114 files, 3,719 macros, 513 violations; 499 rows parsed into main scratchpad lib-macro-census.tsv (file, line, def, kind). By kind: flow 214, block 148, arith 115, cast 21, long 1. 65 rows are 'global' blocks (host globals: a separate decision, not logic-in-macro), leaving 434 definition-level sites.\nTop files, by non-global count:\n- base 48 (arith 19, flow 28, cast 1); sm' 43 (flow 28, block 7, arith 5, cast 3); file_system 31 (block 13, flow 14, arith 2, cast 1, long 1); date_time 29 (arith 15, block 6, flow 8); convert 21 (cast 7, flow 6, arith 4, block 4).\n- common 15, leptos/leptos 13, resultm 13, trace 13 (all flow), bench/parsing/fparsec 12, listm' 12, leptos/svg/loading 12, leptos/ui/toggle 10, rust/near 10, runtime 9, am' 9, backend 8, optionm' 7, platform 7, async 6.\nSlice plan, one file per slice, each proven on a lib copy with a Rust or host row plus a negative, and landed with the reader guard:\n(1) arith-only rows first: the cheapest, pure Spiral arithmetic replacing host operators (base 19, date_time 15, backend 7, toggle 5, optionm' 4);\n(2) flow rows next, per file: trace 13 and resultm 10 are self-contained;\n(3) block rows last, needing a primitive split, as tibia did for env and file_system;\n(4) leptos/*, bench/fparsec and rust/near are product-facing: after the core files.\nOwners: whoever needs the file, per lanes-land-lib-directly. tibia's heals covered env and file_system, and its rescued node stages are in C:\\home\\data\\rescue-2026-10-28\\tibia-lib-stages. CPU note: Rust-cell proofs are full rustc builds, so run slices outside kit's train windows.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_55 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 55u32, "2026-10-29T13:37Z", "main", "10-29 09:4x main: CORRECTION to n54, so slices aren't sized from an inflated count. 20 of the 115 'arith' rows are host COMMENTS read as operators. All of backend.spi's 7 are fallback-arm comments ('Nil // backend.backend_switch / ...', '() /* backend_switch: no Rust arm */', 'None ## ...'). Many of the other 95 are single-operator primitives that are already one host call ('!a == !b' at base:292-300) or deliberate host probes (base:50-56, '1 + 1' style). So slice (1), arith-first, is mostly NOT logic-in-macro. Re-rank: flow rows first (trace 13, resultm 10, common 13), then real block splits. The linter fix is filed on GRANULAR-LINT.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_56 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 56u32, "2026-10-29T15:22Z", "main", "10-29 11:2x main, slice reading for resultm (10 flow rows in the census): (a) from_try's 4 sites (F# try/with, Python try/except, the TS try/catch IIFE, plus a binding helper) are the HOST EXCEPTION BOUNDARY. try/catch cannot be expressed in Spiral, so they are irreducible one-construct primitives and should be EXEMPT from the lint (another GRANULAR-LINT class next to n23's comment and single-operator cases). (b) unbox's 5 sites (Gleam case, F# match x2, Rust match x2) branch INSIDE host text. That is a real heal: per backend, three one-call primitives (host_result_is_ok x : bool, host_result_ok_value x : t, host_result_error_value x : u), then Spiral '? host_result_is_ok x ?> ok (host_result_ok_value x) |: error (host_result_error_value x)'. The unit-Ok arms ('Ok () ->') stay one shape via ok (). Proof: resultm's existing unbox and from tests on every backend that has an arm (gleam, fsharp, rust), plus a negative (an Error value round-trips as Error). Its Rust and F# cells are full builds, so run it outside train windows. (c) The two Zig allocPrint rows (get, unwrap_err) are the 'block' kind: error-message formatting that is one host call each, so likely lint-exempt as well. Net: resultm's real slice is unbox only, 5 sites, about 60 min plus the proof.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_57 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 57u32, "2026-10-29T16:54Z", "main", "10-29 12:5x main: CORRECTION to n56, after reading resultm.unbox in full. unbox is NOT a heal: it is the HOST-TO-SPIRAL TYPE BRIDGE. One host match on the backend's native Result (Gleam case, F# match, Rust match; the unit-Ok arms differ only in the pattern) dispatches straight into Spiral constructors passed in as closures (!ok and !error, from ~~ ok and ~~ error). No logic lives in the host text. Splitting it into is_ok, ok_value and error_value primitives would be WORSE: Rust unwrap needs E: Debug, Gleam needs let-assert blocks, and it costs 3 host round trips instead of 1. Decision: EXEMPT, with a GRANULAR-LINT class next to n23's comment and single-operator cases and n56's host exception boundary: 'host union bridge: one match on a host Result/Option whose arms call Spiral constructor closures'. That leaves resultm with 0 real slices; the epic's honest size keeps shrinking as the lint's false-positive classes are separated out.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_58 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 58u32, "2026-10-29T18:43Z", "main", "10-29 14:4x main, slice reading for trace (13 flow rows in the census), read in full:\nREAL heals (3 slices). (A) init_trace_state's Lua, Python and TypeScript arms each fold the lazy-init `if absent then assign` into ONE host string. The F# arm of the same function already has the healed shape (a Spiral `?` over a one-op condition primitive plus a one-op assignment primitive, inside `\\ () => ... |> exec_unit`). (C) Zig's spiralClock global does OS branching plus HH:MM:SS formatting in Zig text, while Rust already does the same job in Spiral (local_seconds_of_day over field primitives, plus clock_two_digits). Zig should take the Rust shape. (B) The Rust spiral_trace_near_log global holds the 15000-char chunking and the 16000-byte budget truncation loop in Rust text; Spiral already computes chunks_len next to it.\nEXEMPT (GRANULAR-LINT classes): the Rust cfg-per-platform clock shims (a host platform boundary, one call each); the Gleam `let trace_state = fn ...` shadowing (a host scoping idiom: Gleam has no mutable global, so the arm rebinds the name inside the generated function); the OnceLock and Zig spiralHold helpers (one call each).\nSlice A DRAFTED AND PROVEN: macro-heal/apply-trace-init.ps1, 2 anchored edits on trace.spi base 16BCF8E2B4D4 (durable copy in C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\macro-heal). Proof on a fresh lib copy: type_probe trace.spi PASS (3 tests typed, control ok). A runtime probe main (critical on; Debug then Verbose off; Verbose then Verbose on) compiled with the installed 6b on the Lua and TypeScript backends returns 0 on both the healed and the baseline lib. The SABOTAGE control (Lua condition forced true, so init re-creates state on every call) returns 2 as predicted, so the probe detects a non-lazy init. The healed Python emits `v0 = TraceState.trace_state is None` / `if v0: ...` (inspected; running the Python output needs the backend's _auto companion module). The healed code also inlines new_trace_state inside the branch instead of passing it to the host as a closure. Landing in the post-TRAIN4-notebooks gap through land-anchored.ps1 (canonical round trip, base hash pinned, undo copy). Next: slice C (Zig clock to the Rust shape), then B.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_59 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 59u32, "2026-10-29T18:54Z", "main", "10-29 14:5x main: trace slice C DRAFTED AND PROVEN (macro-heal/apply-trace-zig-clock.ps1; combined with A in apply-trace-heal.ps1; on a fresh copy it reproduces the proven draft byte for byte). New Spiral helper clock_text_of_seconds (u64 -> \"HH:MM:SS\", hours % 24). Zig's host global shrinks from \"OS clock read + allocPrint formatting\" to spiralClockSeconds(), the same statements minus the format line, so only the OS-branch platform boundary stays in Zig text. The Rust arm drops its private two_digits copy and calls the shared helper (its value is under 86400, so % 24 changes nothing). Proof with the installed 6b: the helper on Zig AND Rust prints exactly 12:34:56 / 01:02:03 / 00:00:00 for 45296, 20000 days + 3723 s, and 0 (the second input exercises % 24 on an epoch-sized value, the Linux branch's case). Rust end-to-end `trace Info` prints a current HH:MM:SS line on both the baseline and the healed lib. type_probe trace.spi PASS. Zig end-to-end is NOT provable today on EITHER lib: a pre-existing Zig backend bug (an empty-array prepend indexed in a dead branch fails the zig build, so any Zig program calling trace fails to build) is filed as ZIG-EMPTY-ARRAY-INDEX (P1). A+C land together in the post-TRAIN4-notebooks gap (land-anchored.ps1, base 16BCF8E2B4D4). Slice B (the Rust near-log chunking loop) needs a wasm32 + near_sdk build to prove and stays queued for a non-train window.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_60 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 60u32, "2026-10-29T19:05Z", "main", "10-29 15:0x main, slice reading for common (13 flow rows). REAL heal, drafted and proven: random' Zig arm computed the bounded draw (i128 span, modulo, intCast) inside one Zig `blk:` string. New pure Spiral helper random_in_span (x : u64) (lo hi : i64) : i64, now shared by native_random_between (the Rust path, previously the same arithmetic inline) and the Zig arm, which keeps only guid.zig_random_u64 () as host and narrows with convert.numeric_conv (convert.convert emitted a bare i64->i32 assignment on Zig, a build error; numeric_conv is what convert's own Zig arms use). Semantics note: the Zig arm now takes the Rust path's i64 span, as already accepted there, instead of i128. Proof with the installed 6b: a probe of 200 draws in [3,7) plus random' 5 5, random' 5 6 and a coverage mask over 400 draws in [0,4) prints exactly \"0 5 5 15\" on Zig and Rust for both the baseline and the healed lib. The mask must be 15, so a span bug that always returns lo would print 1 (control by construction). type_probe common.spi PASS (7 tests). Applier macro-heal/apply-common-random.ps1 on base BD2F7F52AAFF, landing in the post-TRAIN4-notebooks gap. Remaining common rows, classified: TypeScript random' (float Math.random to an int range, with a bigint typecase) is a real heal, next; the Python Disposable class (6 lines) is a host type definition (exempt: class syntax); dispose per backend, the yield/return wrappers and the Rust process counter global are one-call primitives (exempt).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_61 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 61u32, "2026-10-29T19:13Z", "main", "10-29 15:1x main: the TypeScript random' slice is BLOCKED on convert. A draft routing TS through random_in_span (with a one-call typescript_random_u53 = BigInt(Math.floor(Math.random() * 2^53))) compiled, but ran into `throw new Error(\"backend_switch: this lib/spiral function has no TypeScript arm yet\")`: convert.i64, convert.u64 and convert.i32 have NO TypeScript arm, and numeric_conv on TS would leave a bigint where an i32 is expected (`1i32 <<< x` then mixes BigInt and number). The TS slice therefore needs TS arms for convert's integer conversions first (a lib-wide TS change, for example i64 = BigInt.asIntN(64, BigInt(x)) and i32 = Number(BigInt.asIntN(32, BigInt(x))), following u8/u16's existing TS arms) plus a census of TS callers that currently throw. The TS edit was dropped from this slice. The landing applier stays the 2-edit Zig+Rust version, and with it all four backends (Zig, Rust, TypeScript, Lua) print the probe's expected \"0 5 5 15\".")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_62 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 62u32, "2026-10-29T19:21Z", "main", "10-29 15:2x main: census refresh (flow-bearing macro lines per lib file, after today's lands): sm' 13, base 12, bench/parsing/fparsec 11, parsing 11, resultm 10, convert 8, trace 6, platform 5, optionm' 4, async 3, file_system 3. Classified, read in full: convert's 8 are integer-parse primitives, one host call each (Gleam int.parse/base_parse into a tuple = host union bridge as in resultm.unbox; C strtol/strtoll; Swift optional-chained Int32 parse; Lua string.match; Lean spiralParseInt?), so all EXEMPT except the TS `(!ok ? BigInt(!x) : 0n)` ternary, a small heal (Spiral `? ok ?> ... |: 0i64`) that rides with the TS convert-arms prerequisite from n61. platform's 5 are C preprocessor #if/#endif lines and one F# pipe into a mutable (a host platform boundary, EXEMPT). resultm's 10 are exempt (n56/n57). The lint-count drop expected from today's lands is trace -3 (Lua/Python/TS init) and common -2 (Zig/TS random' flow, Zig healed). The real remaining heal mass is in sm' (13; next, now that sm' landed un-static), parsing's fparsec F#, and trace slice B (Rust near-log loop).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_63 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 63u32, "2026-10-29T19:22Z", "main", "10-29 15:20 main: slices LANDED. trace.spi 16BCF8E2B4D4 -> 2BA48C032DC1 (A: Lua/Python/TS lazy-init in the F# shape; C: clock_text_of_seconds, Zig host reduced to the OS clock read). common.spi BD2F7F52AAFF -> 258BE24CE842 (random_in_span shared by Rust and Zig). Landed in the 10-29 15:19 lib gap (after kit's TRAIN4 notebooks end, after fiscal's 4), through main's window sequence: reader + held-window check, proven base hashes asserted, canonical round trip (fixpoint true, blank-only diff), private MIX_BUILD_PATH. document_test after the gap lands: 22 passed (15:22, private MIX_BUILD_PATH), closing run after git-a6 console.spi follows. Undo copies under main scratchpad land-*/ (durable scripts C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\window-1029).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_64 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 64u32, "2026-10-29T19:22Z", "main", "10-29 15:2x main: correction to n62. common's expected lint drop is -1, not -2: only the Zig random' arm was healed, and the TS arm waits on the convert TS-arm prerequisite (n61).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_65 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 65u32, "2026-10-29T19:27Z", "main", "10-29 15:2x main: sm' slice DRAFTED AND PROVEN on the post-gap base E56D63DE1C1F (macro-heal/apply-sm-first-line.ps1). first_line and after_first_line's F# arms folded IndexOf plus an if/else plus Substring into one host string. Each now takes one IndexOf primitive, Spiral `? at < 0i32 ?> ... |: ...`, and one Substring primitive, and the `+ 1` moves into Spiral. Proof: the test block's 6 cases (including the UTF-16 \"é1\" case kit fixed on 10-28) compiled with the installed 6b `fsharp` and run in dotnet fsi print exactly \"é1|b~c|only|||\" on both baseline and healed lib. A seeded off-by-one (rest = at + 0) prints \"é1|~b~c|only||~|\", so the probe detects it. type_probe sm'.spi PASS (69 tests). Queued for the next lib window (after kit's \"TRAIN4 published\"), next-window-main.ps1, base-hash pinned. Remaining sm' rows: ellipsis_end Rust+F# (a char-count/truncate heal; Rust counts chars, so it needs a char-length primitive), split_nth F# (bounds check over a Split array), and String.forall digits (host lambda; judge after GRANULAR-LINT's classes). The Zig optional captures and bool-to-string are EXEMPT.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_66 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 66u32, "2026-10-29T19:32Z", "main", "10-29 15:3x main: sm' clip slice DRAFTED AND PROVEN (macro-heal/apply-sm-clip.ps1; combined with first_line in apply-sm-heal.ps1, base E56D63DE1C1F; on a fresh copy it reproduces the proven draft byte for byte). clip's Rust arm held count, compare, take, saturating_sub and append in one `{ ... }` block, and its F# arm held an if/else with Substring and max. Each arm now has a one-call char-count primitive (Rust chars().count(), F# Length) and a one-call take primitive (Rust chars().take collect, F# Substring). The comparison, the floor-at-0 keep width and the \"...\" append are in Spiral. Proof: 7 cases (short text, an 8-char cut, the multi-byte \"héllo wörld\" at widths 6 and 7, the width-3 keep-0 boundary, width 2, an exact fit) compiled with the installed 6b give an identical line on F# (fsi) and Rust (rustc) for baseline and healed. type_probe sm' PASS (69). Behaviour note: for a NEGATIVE width the old Rust arm returned the input (its usize cast wraps) while F# returned \"...\"; both now return \"...\" (the F# answer). Queued with the first_line slice in next-window-main.ps1.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_67 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 67u32, "2026-10-29T19:34Z", "main", "10-29 15:3x main: sm' split_nth slice DRAFTED AND PROVEN (macro-heal/apply-sm-split-nth.ps1, folded into apply-sm-heal.ps1 with first_line and clip; on a fresh copy it reproduces the proven draft byte for byte). The F# arm folded Split, a bounds check and indexing into one let/if string. It now has one Split primitive typed `array string`, and Spiral does `? index >= 0i32 && index < am'.length parts ?> am'.index index parts |: \"\"`. Proof: the record-separator row (\"a\", \"bé\", \"c\" split on 30u8) at indexes 0, 1, 2, 3 and -1, plus an empty input, gives \"a|bé|c|||\" in dotnet fsi on both baseline and healed. type_probe sm' PASS (69). sm' now has 3 healed slices queued for the next window (first_line pair, clip, split_nth). Left: String.forall digits (a host lambda; decide after GRANULAR-LINT's classes) and the exempt Zig optional captures and bool-to-string.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_68 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 68u32, "2026-10-29T19:35Z", "main", "10-29 15:3x main: smaller files classified, read in full. EXEMPT: optionm' 178-193 (lambdas that the census regex reads as flow, through `=>`) and 236 (TS `??`, one op); async 221-222 (F# mutable capture plus an option match into failwith) and 313 (Rust Poll match), both host union bridges; seq 400 (an F# sequence expression) and 403 (a Python comprehension, one construct each); float_native (strtof/strtod); file_system 811 (a Zig error-union capture) and 908 (F# try/with, the host exception boundary). REAL but deferred: file_system 1859 (a TS existsSync ? statSync.mtimeMs : 0 ternary) waits until x's fs-heal-4 lands on file_system 633EE888, to avoid a second rebase for x. testing 252-253 (Zig for-else contains loops) is real but BLOCKED: `a` is the generic u, narrowed only by a compile-time typecase, so a Spiral loop over am'.length/index cannot type it without an array-typed bridge. Revisit with GRANULAR-LINT's \"typecased host search\" class. listm' 545/549 (Python for-loops building lists) are probably performance arms: measure against the generic path before healing (experiment-until-sota). With sm' (3 queued) and trace/common (landed), the census's real heal set outside parsing's F# fparsec block is now: trace B (needs a wasm32 + near_sdk build), sm' String.forall, file_system 1859, testing 252-253, listm' 545/549.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_69 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 69u32, "2026-10-29T19:43Z", "main", "10-29 15:4x main: listm' fold' Python slice DRAFTED AND PROVEN, AND IT FIXES A BUG. The Python arm built a Python list with a quadratic `r = [fn(x)] + r` loop over rev' list, then re-consed it from a fresh []. With a non-empty init the live lib returns the WRONG order: fold' (x*10) [1;2] (box [3;4;5]) gives 2,1,50,40,30 on Python, against 50,40,30,1,2 on Rust and per F#'s List.fold arm (measured with the installed 6b). Every current caller passes [] (dictm, sm'), which is why no test saw it. The heal gives Python the Rust/TS/Zig shape: a len() primitive, an index primitive, and a Spiral tail join point consing onto init. Proof: Python (python 3.12, SPIRAL_CUDA=0) and Rust both print \"50,40,30,1,2, 5000\"; the 5000-item fold also shows the join point does not hit Python's recursion limit. Added regression test fold_keeps_init_after_the_folded_items (python, rust, fsharp). type_probe listm' PASS (29). Applier macro-heal/apply-listm-fold-python.ps1 on base 1719B5658442, queued in next-window-main.ps1. Side finding for anyone running generated Python by hand: the Python + Cuda backend imports <input-name>_auto, so name the output after the input file.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_70 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 70u32, "2026-10-29T19:43Z", "main", "10-29 15:4x main: addendum to n69. fold_back' on Python is `rev' |> fold'`, so the same fix makes it correct too: fold_back' fn (box [3;4;5]) [1;2] now gives 30,40,50,1,2, matching F#'s List.foldBack arm. Before the fix it was wrong for any non-empty init. MEASURED 15:4x (run-fold.ps1, installed 6b): baseline Python fold_back' gives 2,1,30,40,50; healed Python and Rust (both libs) give 30,40,50,1,2.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_71 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 71u32, "2026-10-29T19:46Z", "main", "10-29 15:4x main: sm' all_digits classified EXEMPT, a new GRANULAR-LINT class \"single host combinator over a string\" (Rust `!text.is_empty() && text.bytes().all(is_ascii_digit)`, F# `text <> \"\" && String.forall (...)`). Each is one host call with an inline predicate. A Spiral loop over core sm.index/length would be O(n^2) on Rust (char-indexed nth) and would turn the pinned byte-level ASCII semantics (the test rejects \"١٢\") into per-backend char semantics. Separately, all_digits has NO arm for other backends (failwith); a generic Spiral arm for those is a FEATURE, filed only if a caller needs it. sm' heal set is therefore complete at 3 slices (first_line pair, clip, split_nth), queued.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_72 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 72u32, "2026-10-29T19:51Z", "main", "10-29 15:5x main: the n61 prerequisite is DONE, and the TS random' slice is now drafted and proven. (1) convert.i32 / i64 / u64 gain TypeScript arms (macro-heal/apply-convert-ts-arms.ps1, base 96F6D2E1B22C), following u8/u16's BigInt.asIntN/asUintN convention, with one improvement: float sources go through Math.trunc first (BigInt(3.7) throws). Before this, any TS program reaching them threw \"backend_switch: this lib/spiral function has no TypeScript arm yet\" at run time. A probe of 11 conversions (i64 of 5, -7, 3.7, -3.7; i32 of -2i64, 4294967297i64 -> 1, 9.99, 2147483648i64 -> -2147483648; u64 of -1i64 -> 18446744073709551615, 12, 2.5) gives the IDENTICAL line on bun and on Rust. The baseline TS build throws. (2) common random's TS arm now goes through random_in_span with a one-call typescript_random_u53 draw (apply-common-random-ts.ps1, base 258BE24CE842). The coverage probe prints \"0 5 5 15\" on Zig, Rust, TypeScript and Lua. type_probe convert (3 tests) and common (7) PASS. Both are queued in next-window-main.ps1, convert first.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_73 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 73u32, "2026-10-29T19:55Z", "main", "10-29 15:5x main: SECOND Python list bug found by the same audit of listm' Python arms: cons' did `tail.insert(0, head)`, MUTATING the shared tail in place, while list' is a value on every other backend (Rust clones before insert). Measured with the installed 6b: with xs = box [2], a = cons' 1 xs and b = cons' 9 xs, the live lib prints a = b = xs = 9,1,2 on Python, against a = 1,2, b = 9,2, xs = 2 on Rust and TypeScript. Fix: Python arm = `([head] + tail)` (a new list; the O(n) copy matches Rust's clone), plus regression test cons_leaves_the_shared_tail_unchanged (python, rust, typescript; asserts all three lists through fold_back'). On a fresh copy, with the fold' heal applied too: cons probe OK on Python, Rust and TS; fold probe still OK; type_probe listm' PASS (30). Composed with fold' in apply-listm-heal.ps1, base 1719B5658442, queued. Other listm' Python arms read: empty' [], rev' list(reversed()), filter'/map' builtins (one call each, exempt); distinct uses set + sort(key=index), order-preserving and correct but O(n^2) (perf, not a bug).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_74 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 74u32, "2026-10-29T20:01Z", "main", "10-29 16:0x main: the cons' mutation bug is on LUA too (`table.insert(tail, 1, head)`). Measured: the live lib prints a = b = xs = 9,1,2 on Lua, the same as Python. Fixed in the same applier (apply-listm-cons-python.ps1, now 3 edits). The Lua arm builds a new table in Spiral with the Zig arm's shape: arraym.init (len + 1) with head at 0 and tail[i] (1-based) after. It avoids Lua 5.2's table.unpack, so it stays Lua 5.1 (WoW) safe. The regression test now also lists lua. Fresh-copy proof: the cons probe is OK on Python, Rust, TypeScript and Lua; the fold probe is still OK; type_probe listm' PASS (30). A first draft that called lua_list_item failed in type_probe as Unbound: Spiral resolves top-level defs in order, and lua_list_item is defined after cons'. The probe caught that in 15 s, before any gate. Safety check: every cons' call site (lib listm' box, rev', filter', map', distinct; none in tibia, x or fiscal src) uses the RETURNED list through a fold or an assignment, so no caller depends on the old mutation. Cost is unchanged: the old front table.insert / list.insert(0) also shifted O(n) per cons.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_75 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 75u32, "2026-10-29T20:11Z", "main", "10-29 16:1x main: FOURTH and biggest lib bug, on LUA (tibia's target, and the WoW addons' next): listm' list' operations had their iteration direction flipped against Rust. Measured on the live lib with the installed 6b, xs = box [3;1;3;2;1;4]: rev' xs returns 3,1,3,2,1,4 (IDENTITY); filter' (>1) returns 4,2,3,3 (reversed); map (*2) returns 8,2,4,6,2,6 (reversed); distinct' returns 4,2,1,3 (reversed). Python, Rust and TypeScript give 4,1,2,3,1,3 / 3,3,2,4 / 6,2,6,4,2,8 / 3,1,2,4. Lua fold' also walked down, so it did not reverse; in fold_back' (= rev' then fold') the two errors cancelled, which is why unbox, and so every lib caller (am', seq, sm', crypto, benchmark, par' all go through unbox), stayed correct and no test saw it. Fix (macro-heal/apply-listm-lua-order.ps1, 6 edits): rev' and fold' walk up; filter' and map walk down (like Rust's filter'); distinct' keeps its first-occurrence walk up and reverses once at the end; new regression test list_operations_keep_their_order (lua, python, rust, typescript). Fresh copy with all three listm' appliers: the arms probe is OK on Lua, Python, Rust and TS; the fold and cons probes are OK on Lua; type_probe PASS (31). No direct callers of rev'/filter'/map/distinct'/fold' exist in lib, tibia, x or fiscal, so no current output changes except those functions themselves. Composed in apply-listm-heal.ps1 (3 appliers), base 1719B5658442, queued for the next window.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_76 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 76u32, "2026-10-29T20:20Z", "main", "10-29 16:1x main: trace slice B (the Rust spiral_trace_near_log global) DEFERRED, with reasons. Its loop mixes CHAR-based chunking (chars().chunks(15000)) with a BYTE-based budget (16000 - LOGGED, s.len()) and char-boundary truncation (is_char_boundary walk-back plus \" [truncated]\"). A faithful Spiral port needs new primitives for char-range take, byte length and boundary-safe byte truncation, and runs only on wasm32 with near_sdk (provable: wasm32-unknown-unknown is installed and near-sdk 5.11.0 is cached in CARGO_HOME = scoop persist rustup\\.cargo). That is high regression risk for one wasm-only logging path. Revisit if NEAR logging changes, or when those primitives exist for another reason.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_77 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 77u32, "2026-10-29T20:33Z", "main", "10-29 16:3x main: FIFTH lib bug, LUA again, found by reading Lua pattern use. sm'.builder_replace's Lua arm was `string.gsub(sb, old, new)` UNESCAPED, so `old` is a Lua pattern (\".\" matches any char, \"+\" is a quantifier) and a \"%\" in `new` is an error; gsub's second return value also leaked. sm'.replace (line 1488) already escaped correctly. Measured on the live lib (installed 6b): builder_replace on Lua returns \"abef|\" and then breaks on the \".\" -> \"%\" case, while TS gives abef|a%b%c|xxb|abc. Fix: the Lua arm uses replace's escaping, ((sb):gsub(((old):gsub(\"%p\",\"%%%0\")), ((new):gsub(\"%%\",\"%%%%\")))), with the outer parentheses dropping the count. New test builder_replace_treats_text_literally (lua, typescript); the old test never listed lua. Fresh copy with all sm' slices: Lua and TS both print abef|a%b%c|xxb|abc; type_probe sm' PASS (70). Folded into apply-sm-heal.ps1 (now 4 appliers), queued. Other Lua pattern sites read and found SAFE: sm'.index_of and contains use string.find(..., 1, true) (plain); convert's integer check and date_time's zero strip use fixed literal patterns.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_78 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 78u32, "2026-10-29T20:34Z", "main", "10-29 16:3x main: reading-audit leftovers, NOT measured. (a) am'.to_list_base' ALIASES its source on Lua and Python (`convert.to`) and on Zig (`!items`), while Rust (clone), TS ([...items]) and F# (Array.toList) COPY. A list' obtained from an array therefore changes if that array is later mutated in place, on those three backends only. It is a hazard only for callers that mutate after converting; no such caller was looked for. Measure with a probe (mutate after to_list') before deciding copy versus documented aliasing. (b) The Lua string audit is complete: ends_with/starts_with use sub(-#v)/sub(1, #v) correctly (a longer value compares false), substring maps 0-based start/len to inclusive 1-based from/until correctly, and the find calls are plain. (c) seq, mapm, dictm and arraym have no Lua arms; am' has 4 (index, to_list_base', a sort routed to insertion_sort, a fallback routed to fsharp).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_79 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 79u32, "2026-10-29T21:19Z", "main", "10-29 17:1x main: SIXTH lib bug, measured (it was n78's unmeasured hazard): am'.to_list_base' ALIASED its source on Lua and Python. A probe mutating the array after to_list' (set source 0 9) printed the snapshot as 9,2,3 on Lua and Python, and 1,2,3 on Rust and TypeScript. The same probe showed am'.sort_by correct and STABLE on all four backends (Lua's insertion_sort included). Fix (macro-heal/apply-am-to-list-copy.ps1, base 638D7AA2FA7E): the Lua arm copies through arraym.init over the 1-based table, the Python arm is list(items); new test to_list_snapshots_the_array (lua, python, rust, typescript). Fresh-copy proof: snapshot 1,2,3 on all four. Zig still aliases (the `!items` slice); not measured, left as is. ALSO folded into the listm' land: git-a6's root cause for ZIG-EMPTY-ARRAY-INDEX. empty''s Zig arm `&[_]t{}` emitted a comptime-known empty slice, and cons''s Zig arm indexes it. Fix: `spiralNewArray(t, 0)` (macro-heal/apply-listm-zig-empty.ps1, 4th listm' applier). Proof: the Zig trace clock probe now builds and prints \"17:17:11 1i clock-probe\" end to end. That is also the first full Zig proof of trace slice C. The next window now lands 5 lib files (sm', listm', convert, common, am') plus the type_probe update; a dry apply of all 5 appliers on a fresh live copy changed exactly those 5 files.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_80 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 80u32, "2026-10-29T21:34Z", "main", "10-29 17:3x main: LANDED: sm' heals (first_line pair, clip, split_nth, builder_replace Lua escaping); listm' (Python fold'/cons', Lua list order, Zig empty'); convert TS arms; common random' TS; am' to_list' copy on Lua/Python. All six lib bug fixes and their regression tests are in. Landed 10-29 17:32-17:33 in the post-TRAIN4c lib window (kit GO at 17:31, no train reading lib), through next-window-main.ps1: reader and window check, base hash pinned per file, canonical round trip, private MIX_BUILD_PATH. Hashes: sm' E56D63DE1C1F -> 9B2BCC159225, listm' 1719B5658442 -> DC9551655B26, convert 96F6D2E1B22C -> 210D27203B5C, common 258BE24CE842 -> E981D750A532, am' 638D7AA2FA7E -> 87168AA1D752; apps/cells/spi/type_probe.ps1 CD11C86BFBB1 -> B343B4F24D7A. type_probe from the landed path PASS 4/4; document_test 22/22.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_81 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 81u32, "2026-10-29T21:44Z", "main", "10-29 17:4x main: SEVENTH lib bug, TypeScript, found while healing file_system.last_write_ms (the deferred file_system 1859 row, unblocked once x landed). The TS arm returned a JS NUMBER for a u64, while u64 is a bigint on TS. A probe doing `last_write_ms written + 1u64` on the live lib (file_system 1F37E39FF359) throws \"TypeError: Invalid mix of BigInt and other type in addition\" under bun; F# is fine. Heal (macro-heal/apply-fs-last-write.ps1): F# and TS arms each take an exists primitive plus a ms primitive with Spiral `? ... ?> ... |: 0u64`, and TS wraps BigInt(Math.trunc(mtimeMs)). Proof (lw-base vs lw-draft copies, installed 6b): baseline TS BAD (the TypeError), baseline F# OK; healed TS and F# both print \"pos 0 adds\" (written file > 0, missing = 0, +1 works); type_probe file_system PASS (34). QUEUED for the next lib window, which is next session because TRAIN4d holds the freeze until its publish: window3-main.ps1 (durable C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\window-1029), base 1F37E39FF359.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_82 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 82u32, "2026-10-29T21:51Z", "main", "10-29 17:5x main: CONSUMER-PATH PROOF of window 2 (proof-must-run-consumer-path). Every probe re-run against a fresh copy of the LANDED lib (sm' 9B2BCC159225, listm' DC9551655B26, am' 87168AA1D752, file_system 1F37E39F): listm' arms (Python, Rust, TS, Lua), cons sharing (Python, Rust, TS, Lua), fold/fold_back (Python, Lua, Rust), am' sort/aliasing (Rust, Lua, Python, TS), builder_replace (Lua, TS), convert TS arms (TS, Rust), random' (Zig, Rust, TS, Lua), and Zig trace end to end (\"17:50:07 1i clock-probe\"). Result: 24 OK, 0 BAD. Log: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1029\\consumer-proof.log.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_83 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 83u32, "2026-10-29T21:59Z", "main", "10-29 18:0x main: base.spi's 12 flow rows, read in full; ALL EXEMPT. 163-170 (base.capture's F# fun-thunk capture into a mutable plus an option match into failwith) are a host union bridge, as in async.new_task. 563/569 (match a host Choice1Of2/Choice2Of2 or Ok/Err into Spiral constructor closures) are a host union bridge. 726/739/828/850 (try/with, try/catch IIFE) are the host exception boundary. 814 is already healed (Spiral `?` over a one-op result.is_error primitive). Census status after today: every module with flow rows has been read and classified except parsing's F# fparsec block. The remaining REAL heals are trace B (deferred, wasm-only), testing's Zig contains (blocked on a typecase bridge), and file_system last_write_ms (drafted and proven, queued for window 3).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_84 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 84u32, "2026-10-29T22:00Z", "main", "10-29 18:0x main: parsing's fparsec_2 static block (170 lines) read: it is an FParsec BINDING layer. Each def is one host call to an FParsec combinator or type alias (pchar, pstring, >>., .>>, >>=, |>>, anyChar, ...), so it is EXEMPT as class 13, \"host library binding\", added to GRANULAR-LINT's list. CENSUS READING COMPLETE: every lib module with flow-bearing macro rows has been read and classified. The real heal set was trace A+C, common random' (Zig, TS), sm' (first_line pair, clip, split_nth, builder_replace Lua), listm' fold'/cons'/Lua order/Zig empty', convert TS arms, am' to_list' and file_system last_write_ms. All but last_write_ms are LANDED (window 2); last_write_ms is queued for window 3. Open: trace B (deferred), testing Zig contains (blocked), and the per-backend missing-arm census (SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD n5), which is a separate, measured-candidates list.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_85 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 85u32, "2026-10-29T22:13Z", "main", "10-29 18:1x main: testing's Zig contains row is UNBLOCKED (n68 said blocked on a typecase bridge, which was wrong). listm's Zig arms already loop over host slices in Spiral through host-text primitives (`!a.len`, `!a[i]`), which need no typed-array bridge. Drafted (macro-heal/apply-testing-zig-contains.ps1, base 44FC11FB2CDA): the Zig arm is restructured as a static `? (~> ?: u ?| string => !+ | _ => !-)`; the string search stays one call (indexOf / indexOfScalar); the array search is a Spiral `scan` join point over a len primitive and an index primitive, with equality as one-op primitives (std.mem.eql for strings, == otherwise). The two for-else host loops are gone. NOT YET PROVEN: the probe (run-testing-contains.ps1: string and int arrays and a string-contains-char case must pass; contains 9 in [1;2;3] must FAIL, so a scan that always says true cannot pass) needs Zig compiles, so it runs once the box is free after TRAIN4e publishes, then lands in window 3 if green.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_86 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 86u32, "2026-10-29T22:19Z", "main", "10-29 18:2x main: REVERTED clip's Rust-arm heal (sm' 9B2BCC159225 -> BA70B7B225DA, 18:2x, guarded, document_test exit 0). The F# arm stays healed. Cause: kit TRAIN4d's new early crate gate refused kit_memory_query at 1032 > 1000 lines. kit's snapshot diff: +9 generated Rust lines per clip call site (agile_note_edit 874->883, agile_task_listing 988->997, agile_task_next 954->963, kit_memory 666->684, kit_memory_import 776->794, kit_memory_query 996->1032), because moving clip's branch into Spiral INLINES it at every Rust call site. The restored arm is byte-identical to the pre-window one; the Rust and F# probe line is identical. LESSON (attribute-generated-lines): a Rust-arm heal of a widely called lib function must be measured for generated-line growth in its consumers BEFORE it lands. One-block host code is the compact form for hot Rust call sites. If the Rust heal is wanted, retry it as a single non-inlined Spiral helper (a :~ join point called from the arm) and measure consumer line counts first. apply-sm-clip.ps1 now keeps the original Rust text (F#-only heal), so a re-apply cannot reintroduce it.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_87 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 87u32, "2026-10-29T22:21Z", "main", "10-29 18:2x main: consumer size check of the window-1 Rust-arm heals (trace clock, common native_random_between). x measured a clean A/B on the same compiler (pack-6b): verify31 (synced 14:5x, before window 1) vs verify32 (after). All 29 x crates have IDENTICAL generated line counts except net 725 -> 737, x's own net.spi restructure, not lib. Highest crates unchanged: xc 960, digest 954, refs 896, all close to x's 1000 gate. kit's TRAIN4d diff also showed no kit crate moved for them. So only the clip Rust heal grew consumers, and it is reverted. x has not yet measured the clip revert BA70B7B2; its next verify does.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_88 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 88u32, "2026-10-29T22:46Z", "main", "10-29 18:4x main: WINDOW 3 LANDED (18:44-18:46, after kit published TRAIN4 at 18:35 and x landed fs-heal-4b at 18:37). Each slice was re-proven on a fresh copy of the LIVE lib right before its land. file_system 0577F852 -> 8661852BC5D5: last_write_ms F#/TS, bug 7 fixed (TS and F# print \"pos 0 adds\"). testing 44FC11FB -> 20C1C824AF64: Zig contains as a Spiral scan; baseline and healed agree, and the negative case fails correctly with exit 1. sm' BA70B7B2 -> 2196F9C70148: char_code/of_char Lua arms with a UTF-8 decoder in Spiral (Lua prints 65,233,8364,é). The new test is lua-only, because the Rust backend represents chars as bytes and rejects non-ASCII char literals at compile time, and a 4-byte literal (an emoji) is not a Spiral char at all. type_probe 3/3; document_test 22/22. No Rust arm changed (told to kit). The census's real heals are all landed except trace B (deferred). Log: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1029\\window3-run.log.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_89 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 89u32, "2026-10-30T00:12Z", "main", "10-29 20:12 main, REMAINING SCOPE re-derived. (1) The $'...' control-flow census is SETTLED. 91 lines were classified: 3 CANDIDATE, all healed (window 3), plus 1 already healed; the other 87 are host-boundary classes that stay macros by design (union bridge 16+2, lambda arrow 13, thunk opener 10, exception boundary 7, c pointer arrow 6, host type text 5, platform boundary 4, library binding 4, zig capture 3, gleam rebinding 3, one-call parse 3, host combinator 3, one-construct ternary 3, one-op ternary 2, comprehension 2, one-op operator 1). (2) NEW, the title's second half, 'helper preludes kept as global blobs', which the census never covered: lib has 252 global blobs (22,744 chars). 182 are imports only (legit); 47 contain control-flow keywords. Largest: trace.spi:316 and :250 (wasm32, 1678+1543 chars = trace B, deferred), guid.spi:54 (Zig random state, 968), rust/near.spi:28 (wasm32), convert.spi:84 (a Lean parseInt def, 15 flow tokens), file_system.spi:469/758 (Erlang @external FFI), file_system.spi:926/953 (cfg windows), par'.spi:63 (Rust spiral_scope_map), common.spi:178 (process counter), leptos.spi:499, resultm.spi:71. Census file: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1029\\global-blob-census.tsv (File, Line, Chars, Lines, Flow, Head). NEXT, a CPU-light step: classify the 47 with the same classes plus 'ffi declaration'. Most look like FFI or cfg shims, so expect a handful of real candidates. Each real heal gets a consumer line-count check BEFORE it lands (the clip lesson, n-2).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_90 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 90u32, "2026-10-30T00:13Z", "main", "10-29 20:13 main, global-blob CLASSIFICATION done (all 47 control-flow blobs; C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1029\\global-blob-classes.tsv): ffi declaration 18, host type text 7, platform boundary 6 (incl. trace B x2 and a syscall clock), exception boundary 3, host closure cache 3, host concurrency/state/stub 3, CANDIDATE 7. The 7 candidates, where logic in host text could be Spiral over lib: convert.spi:84 (a Lean parseInt def, 15 flow tokens); guid.spi:54 (Zig PRNG state and step); crypto.spi:176 (Zig sha256 hex formatting); trace.spi:387 (seconds arithmetic over GetLocalTime fields); file_system.spi:469, :605, :758 (Gleam wrappers with logic after an @external; classified from size and flow only, so CONFIRM by reading the bodies before drafting). With the 3 healed $' candidates, the epic's real heal set is about 10 sites, not 69 definitions. Proposed re-scope, the owner's call: progress about 60% once these 7 are drafted; each heal gets a consumer line-count check before landing (the clip lesson), and trace B stays deferred (wasm-only).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_91 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 91u32, "2026-10-30T00:17Z", "main", "10-29 20:17 main, CORRECTION to n90 after reading all 7 candidate bodies: only 2 are real. file_system.spi:469, :605 and :758 are pure FFI declaration lists (stacked @external lines with bodiless fn signatures). The census counted the 'fn' keyword as control flow, so they were false positives, and the census method is noted for reuse: exclude bodiless fn signatures. crypto.spi:176 is a library binding (one std sha256 call plus bytesToHex). trace.spi:387 is a platform boundary (GetLocalTime/clock_gettime plus 1-line arithmetic). REAL: (1) convert.spi:84, Lean spiralDigit and a foldl base-N parse in host text; Spiral over lib could do it, given a Lean char_code arm. (2) guid.spi:54, PARTIAL: the xorshift64 step is pure arithmetic and Spiral-expressible, but the seeding (QueryPerformanceCounter/clock_gettime) stays a platform boundary. So the epic's real heal set is 3 landed + 2 left + trace B (deferred). Classes are updated in global-blob-classes.tsv. Suggest re-scoping the epic to about 80% once these 2 are drafted, the owner's call.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_92 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 92u32, "2026-10-30T00:40Z", "main", "10-29 20:40 main, OWN ERROR found by git-a6: two of main's window-2 regression tests in listm'.spi NEVER RAN on any backend, because each sat above definitions it uses, and a Cells test sees only the definitions above it. cons_leaves_the_shared_tail_unchanged@473 uses box@481 and fold_back'@595 (ETY-UnboundVariable); list_operations_keep_their_order@712 uses unbox@718 and distinct'@723 (Lua red, the rest not_run). The fixes themselves are sound: a whole-module Lua probe of the same body passes. Main proved them only with probes, which open the whole module, so the probe was not the consumer path. Fix: git-a6's widened applier moves both tests below their definitions and adds ///> zig (gate job-1793320740902; it lands with land-anchored.ps1). SCOPE CHECK by main (static, no compile; C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1029\\test-order-check.ps1) over ALL 41 test blocks added to lib today: exactly these 2 have defined-below references (positive control = git-a6's finding); the other 39 are clean. ALSO: lib_gate.ps1 exits 0 even on 'tests: passed 0 failed 1', so verdicts must parse the tests: line (git-a6 rewrote memory lib-gate-needs-test-rows). Main's 20:06 sm' close parsed it (72/72).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_93 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 93u32, "2026-10-30T00:42Z", "main", "10-29 20:42 main: the static test-order check, widened to EVERY test block in lib (712 tests; C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1029\\test-order-check-all.ps1), finds exactly the 2 listm' tests git-a6 is fixing. No other lib test uses a same-file definition below it. The first pass also flagged 4 FALSE positives, each a checker flaw now fixed: convert's i32/u8 were type annotations; date_time's milliseconds was a local binding; resultm's Ok is a union constructor, which matched the function ok because PowerShell hashtables are case-insensitive (now an Ordinal dictionary). After the fix: 2 findings, positive control kept. Suggest a kit lint (EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET or GRANULAR-LINT) that runs this per test block, since Cells' above-only scoping makes such tests silently not_run.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_94 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 94u32, "2026-10-30T00:53Z", "main", "10-29 20:53 main: CLOSED OUT the listm' test placement. git-a6 landed at 20:50 (DC9551655B26 -> 220ACFE33776, land-anchored.ps1, round-trip fixpoint; gate job-1793321172171: fixed 31/31, control 30/31 red only on the cons zig row; document_test exit 0; type_probe 31 tests typed). Once list_operations_keep_their_order ran for real, it exposed an EIGHTH cross-backend bug: list'_types (listm'.spi:418) had only Gleam and Rust arms, and backend.spi's missing-arm fallback is a runtime print on Lua only, so every Lua map/distinct' printed a stray line. Fixed in the same land with 'Lua = \\ () => ()'; a probe shows 0 stray lines. git-a6's census: 103 lib backend_switch blocks lack a Lua arm, nearly all Rust-only helpers, and listm' was the only live instance of this unit-prelude shape. Checker idea for kit: flag a unit prelude without a Lua arm that is called inside a function that has one (pairs with test-order-check-all.ps1).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_95 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 95u32, "2026-10-30T02:24Z", "main", "10-29 22:24 main: the last 2 'real' candidates (n91) checked against what a heal would COST, and both are NOT heals. (1) convert.spi:84 (Lean spiralParseIntBase/spiralDigit): a Spiral version over sm' would need Lean arms that sm' lacks for trim, substring, length, index and char_code (5 of the 6 ops; only starts_with has one; sm' has 12 Lean arms in total). It would replace one 15-line helper with 5 new Lean macros, MORE host text. The helper fills a real Lean stdlib gap (no base-N integer parse): reclassified as a host primitive. (2) guid.spi:54 (the Zig xorshift step): the state is a host global (var spiral_random_state), so a Spiral step needs a macro to read it and one to write it back, no smaller than the 4 lines it replaces, while the seeding stays a platform boundary. Reclassified as host state. RESULT: the epic's real heal set is EXHAUSTED: 3 landed in window 3 (the clip F#, the Lua char arms and the TS last_write had already landed in windows 2-3), plus the census's 87 + 45 sites classified as legitimate host boundaries, and 8 cross-backend bugs found and fixed along the way. Only trace B remains (wasm-only, deferred). Progress 60 -> 90. Closes when trace B is either healed or explicitly dropped (the owner's call: wasm isn't a current target).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_96 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 96u32, "2026-10-30T15:08Z", "main", "10-30 11:08 main: CLOSED, Done at its real scope (n95's close rule: trace B healed or explicitly taken out). Trace B is taken out of this epic as its own item, LIB-MACRO-HEAL-TRACE-B (P3, D4, 120m, load medium, team main): its proof needs a wasm32 + near_sdk build that no lane runs today, so it waits for a NEAR consumer and doesn't hold this P0 open. What the epic delivered, per n95: the real heal set is exhausted. 3 heals landed in window 3 (after the clip F#, the Lua char arms and TS last_write in windows 2-3), the census's 87 + 45 sites were classified as legitimate host boundaries (convert.spi:84 Lean base-N parse = host primitive; guid.spi:54 Zig xorshift = host state; each with its cost reasoning), and 8 cross-backend bugs were found and fixed with regression tests (Python fold'/cons', Lua list order, am' to_list' copy, Lua builder_replace, the TS convert arms, TS last_write_ms, listm' Lua list'_types). The follow-up lint ideas (test-order-check-all.ps1 per test block; a unit prelude without a Lua arm inside a function that has one) belong to kit's GRANULAR-LINT and EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_97 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 97u32, "2026-10-30T15:18Z", "main", "10-30 11:18 main: CORRECTION to n96. The 'heal set exhausted' claim missed one deferred candidate: sm'.clip's Rust arm (sm'.spi:754), reverted on purpose in n86 (consumer line growth), with a retry path (a non-inlined join point). Kit's GRANULAR-LINT port found it as the lib's only remaining CANDIDATE (GRANULAR-LINT n31). It's now its own item, LIB-MACRO-HEAL-CLIP-RUST (P3), like trace B, so this epic stays Done at its scope with both leftovers tracked separately.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_1 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T07:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:37 main: prototype is the scratchpad macro-rule.ps1 classifier (regexes for flow/cast/arith; tracked files via git ls-files; build dirs skipped). Port it to Spiral in eoie as a generic verb (no project names), drop the -> false positive by skipping type-position macros, then baseline each repo with the GRANULAR-HEAL census.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_2 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T10:35Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:35 main: lessons for the verb, from the prototype: (1) count untracked files too. New agent modules are untracked until the user commits, and my tracked-only census missed 23 lib sites / 8.8k chars (process.spi, unicode, xml, the otp modules). (2) Do not walk untracked trees with git ls-files --others in repos whose large caches are not gitignored (eoie's 5.5 GB .cache took over 30 min); take the package manifests' module lists, or skip known cache dirs before listing. (3) The flow regex has false positives on type text (Rc<dyn Fn()>, -> in signatures), data literals ((char[]){ !b, 0 }) and allowed thunks; classify by AST position (type vs term macro) and by the rule 2/3 exceptions instead of regexes. (4) Report per definition and per added line of a diff, so a slice's check covers only what it adds (slice-macro-check.ps1 in scratch does that for apply scripts).")
inl note_of_granular_lint_3 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 3u32, "2026-10-24T21:10Z", "main", "main 17:2x: draft one-call case for the verb, in eoie/spec/one-call.md form, offered to the eoie team to adopt (their spec file; nothing edited there). Fixture lib/sample.spi lines: (1) inl ok (x : i32) : i32 = $'!x'  (2) inl widen (x : i32) : i64 = $'!x as i64'  (3) inl pick (b : bool) : i32 = $'if !b { 1 } else { 0 }'  (4) inl sum (a : i32) (b : i32) : i32 = $'!a + !b'  (5) nominal callback = $'Rc<dyn Fn()>'. Command: eoie lint macros . lib. Expected, one row per violation, then the receipt: 'lib/sample.spi:2  widen  cast   $'!x as i64'' / 'lib/sample.spi:3  pick   flow   $'if !b { 1 } else { 0 }'' / 'lib/sample.spi:4  sum    arith  $'!a + !b'' / 'failed  files 1  macros 5  violations 3  new 3  <elapsed>  <clock>'. Line 5 is the positive control for lesson 3: -> and dyn in a TYPE-position macro (nominal body) is not flow. Companion case: the same command with --baseline lint/macros.tsv listing rows 2 and 3 prints only row 4 and 'failed ... violations 3  new 1'; with all three listed it ends 'ok ... new 0'. Classes from the prototype (scratch macro-rule.ps1): long = text over 120 chars, flow = if/else/match/for/while/loop/let/return/fn/=>/->/; or a braced statement in a TERM macro, cast = 'as T', arith = an operator between splices or literals, block = a triple-quoted global/macro; rule 2/3 exceptions (named one-expression boundary primitives) come from the package standards.spi, not from regexes.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_4 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 4u32, "2026-10-25T07:46Z", "eoie", "03:46 eoie: verb core built TDD in eoie/src/eoie_lint_macros (HostFree, LibReferenced, standards 0 findings). model.spi holds unions macro_place (term/type) and macro_class (long/block/flow/cast/arith), records host_macro, violation, known_violation and lint_tally, and the pure classifier. lint.spi walks the tree (skipping target, node_modules, _build, deps, bin, obj and dot-dirs; untracked files count, per lesson 1, and no git listing, per lesson 2) and builds the report. Spec cases 48/49 adopted into eoie/spec/one-call.md. Both notebooks pass: main's 5-line fixture gives exactly the 3 expected rows and the receipt head; type-position macros (nominal, after : or ->) are not flow, per lesson 3; string literals and a deref are clean; and a temp-tree e2e run gives new 3, then new 1 against a baseline. Change from main's draft: the baseline is the verb's own output (eoie lint macros . lib > lint/macros.txt, keyed by file+name+class, line numbers ignored), because the style ratchet refuses tab-encoded rows in a new package. Left: a crate plus a CLI route (lint verb, help line), after SLATE; standards.spi exemptions for named boundary primitives (rule 2/3) need a declaration in eoie_standards first; per-diff reporting (lesson 4); wiring into the PostToolUse check. Finding for eoie_standards: host-free counts \\$' inside string literals as a host macro, a false positive that forced fixtures through a marker.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_5 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 5u32, "2026-10-25T08:07Z", "eoie", "04:06 eoie: lesson 4 done: report only what a change adds. A file named on the command line counts whatever its extension, and a .diff or .patch contributes only its added lines (+, not the +++ header; context and removed lines are skipped), so a slice check is 'eoie lint macros . change.diff'. Test a_diff_counts_only_its_added_lines passes, and both notebooks and standards are green (0 findings). Spec case 48's note documents it. CLI wiring is drafted (scratch clean\\lint-verb\\apply-lint-verb.ps1, all 4 anchors present once): a crate with entry.spi exporting eoie_lint_macros_run, VerbLint with its help line, the eoie_cli_model route and the eoie_cli dependency. It applies right after SLATE.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_6 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 6u32, "2026-10-25T19:48Z", "eoie", "15:48 kit: lint CLI wiring draft re-verified against the live tree (scratch clean\\lint-verb\\dry-run.ps1, on a copy of the 7 files it touches).\n- All anchors matched exactly once. Result: the eoie_lint_macros crate files (entry.spi export eoie_lint_macros_run, package.spiproj, Cargo.toml), VerbLint plus its help line in direct_verbs.spi, the lint_run runner and route in eoie_cli_model, the eoie_cli dependency, and the workspace member.\n- cargo metadata exits 101 on the partial copy, which is expected; on the full tree it must exit 0.\n- apply-lint-verb.ps1 now checks the tree lease only when -Src is the live tree, so dry runs on copies work during a release.\n- Sequencing: the wiring is a feature, so it lands after KIT-RENAME, and its anchors name eoie_* (eoie_crate_gate, eoie_mirror, eoie_cli_model).\n  Rule: run kit-rename.ps1's replacement rules over the draft script itself before applying it, then rerun this dry run on the renamed tree. Every anchor must again match exactly once.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_7 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 7u32, "2026-10-26T02:08Z", "main", "10-25 22:08 main: three refinements to the verb's 'block' class, from today's GRANULAR-HEAL census (n12-n17), as fixture cases for eoie/spec/one-call.md form (eoie team's file; nothing edited there; eoie source frozen until KIT-RENAME). With the naive rule (any '{' at the start of a macro body) lib reported 36 blocks; with these three, 23 real ones, and leptos' 5 'blocks' all vanish. (A) unit adapter, NOT a violation: a brace body holding exactly one statement and no let/if/for/while/match/loop keyword, e.g. $'{ leptos::prelude::Effect::new(move |_| (!fn)()); }' : (). The braces turn a returned handle into the () the macro is typed as; a bare call would not on Rust, whose unit-macro emission isn't specified as a statement (Zig's is, since pack-1w). Also '{ let _ = f(&$0); }' (a discard: one let of _). Fixture: inl fire (f : () -> ()) : () = $'{ spawn(!f); }' gives 0 violations. (B) format-string literal, NOT a block: a '{' inside a host string literal, e.g. tag_text tag props $'\"{ !children }\"', where the macro body is the Rust string \"{ children }\". Rule: strip host string literals (\"...\", with escapes) before looking for braces. Fixture: inl t (c : string) : string = $'\"{ !c }\"' gives 0. (C) new class target_cfg, reported apart from block: a brace body whose statements carry #[cfg(...)] attributes (near.spi's 9 wasm32/native pairs, trace's 2). They are not mechanically healable (Spiral can't select on rustc's target; that is a backend-key design gap), so the baseline should list them separately and the 'NEW violations fail' gate should apply to them on their own terms. Fixture: inl h () : u64 = $'{ #[cfg(a)] let v = f(); #[cfg(not(a))] let v = 1u64; v }' gives 1 target_cfg and 0 block. Census numbers to check the verb against, today's tree: lib 23 block + 11 target_cfg-ish + 13 adapters; spiral/apps 5; polyglot 3 (dist/ excluded); alphabet 1; dir-tree-html 1; dice 0.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_8 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 8u32, "2026-10-26T11:21Z", "eoie", "07:21 eoie: the wiring draft is redone for the renamed, respelled tree, per note rule (rename rules over the draft, then the dry run on the renamed tree). Scratch clean\\lint-verb\\apply-lint-verb-kit.ps1 replaces apply-lint-verb.ps1. Names are kit_* (crate kit_lint_macros, export kit_lint_macros_run, kit_cli dep, workspace member). The generated entry.spi is edition 3 (':< lint', ':= main', a backslash lambda), and the entry module is appended to the existing package.spiproj instead of overwriting it (the live one already lists kit_receipt). Anchors updated: the union now ends before ':== verb_help =', the help entry is a multi-line record (new VerbLint record laid out the same way, every line within 120 columns, checked), and the runner line is ':= mirror_run'. Refuses if lint.spi lacks ':~ lint_run () : i32'. Dry run on a partial copy (dry-run-kit.ps1): every anchor matched once, all five checks true, and a second run says 'already wired' (cargo metadata 101 is expected on a partial copy). Node proof queued: job-1793013737837-kit-lint-verb (applier, then regen of kit_lint_macros plus the crates that inline command_spec_domain or kit_cli_model: kit_cli, kit_command_help, kit_legacy_operations, command_spec_domain; debug build; smoke 'kit lint macros <src> kit_lint_macros'; notebooks lint.spi and command_spec_domain model.spi, strict). Found on the way: kit_lint_macros/lint.spi has a test block but is not in the release test phase's 111 notebooks, so its tests never run in a release. To fix when the verb lands: register it with the dev test registry.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_9 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 9u32, "2026-10-26T12:21Z", "tibia-lane", "12:21Z 12:3xZ tibia proposal for the 'block' class: three open sites (GRANULAR-HEAL note 27, LIB-PAR-MAP note 13) need host code that is neither one host expression nor expressible in Spiral:\n- trace.spi:328, the Rust local clock under 4 cfg branches;\n- crypto.spi:177, Zig sha256 into an out-param digest;\n- par' Rust map_on_threads, std::thread::scope with spawned closures borrowing the scope.\nProposed rule (8), host shims, as fixtures for one-call.md:\n(a) A shim is a host function declared in `global`, allowed only when the construct needs a host-only feature: cfg or conditional compilation, an out-parameter or `var` buffer the host forces, or a lifetime- or scope-bound closure (thread::scope, borrow-scoped iterators).\n(b) A shim body uses only that feature plus calls. No arithmetic, no casts, no branches beyond the cfg/feature selection itself, no loops. Each cfg arm is one call or field read, at most 120 chars per line.\n(c) Its inputs and outputs are raw host values. Everything else stays in Spiral at the call site: unit conversion, h/m/s splitting, chunk sizing and the conv casts.\n(d) Each shim gets one named Spiral primitive that calls it in one host expression. No other Spiral code names the shim.\nWorked shapes:\n- trace: one cfg'd shim per platform, each returning a raw local-time field tuple (wasm block_timestamp secs; windows GetLocalTime [u16; 8]; unix localtime_r [i32; 16]; fallback SystemTime secs), plus Spiral seconds-of-day math over conv.\n- crypto: `fn spiral_sha256(input) -> [32]u8 { var d: [32]u8 = undefined; Sha256.hash(input, &d, .{}); return d; }`; bytesToHex stays its own call.\n- par': `fn spiral_scoped_map<T, U>(chunks, f) -> Vec<Vec<U>>` doing scope, spawn and join only; chunking and flattening in Spiral (par'.chunks, listm'.collect).\nLint check: a global block may declare fn only when tagged with its allowance class: cfg, out-param or scope.\nThis is a proposal for main's GRANULAR-LINT decision; tibia drafts the three slices once a rule is recorded.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_10 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 10u32, "2026-10-26T12:22Z", "main", "10-26 08:22 main: DECISION on tibia's rule (8), host shims (note 12:21Z): ACCEPTED, with 3 amendments. Checked against the pre-written granular rules: a macro is one host call; casts, arithmetic and flow stay in Spiral at any cost; the rules govern style, not authorship. (8) only admits host code for constructs Spiral cannot express (conditional compilation, an out-parameter the host forces, a closure bound to a host scope). Everything else stays at the Spiral call site, so it extends the rules without loosening them. Amendments: (A1) scope class: spawning per chunk needs iteration, which (b) as written forbids. The spawn and the join may each be one iterator map over the chunks, because that iteration is the scope feature itself. No other closure body logic is allowed: the spawned closure only calls the Spiral-provided f. (A2) the allowance class lives in the shim's name, not a comment (no-comments rule): spiral_cfg_<name>, spiral_out_<name>, spiral_scope_<name>. The lint check reads the prefix, and a global fn without one of the three prefixes is a violation. (A3) proof per shim: its single Spiral primitive (rule d) gets a test block on the shim's backend (///> rust for trace and par', ///> zig for crypto), and one-call.md gets a negative fixture: a spiral_cfg_ shim whose arm has arithmetic ('secs / 60') is flagged as arith, and one with two calls chained in an arm passes only if each arm stays a single call or field read. Consequence for the three sites: trace.spi:328 gets spiral_cfg_local_fields per platform, returning raw tuples, with the seconds-of-day math in Spiral; crypto.spi:177 gets spiral_out_sha256 with bytesToHex as its own call; par' gets spiral_scope_map, with chunking and flattening in Spiral. Tibia drafts the three slices; each goes through the gate kit with its backend's tests.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_11 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 11u32, "2026-10-26T13:07Z", "eoie", "09:06 eoie: classifier refinement from the granular migration: a macro whose whole body is one call into another kit crate's export (\\$'other_crate::kit_export(&*!a, ...)' : t) is the hand-modeled build-unit seam, not a violation. Report it as its own class (export_seam), never as flow or block. Fixture: := f (~a : string) : string = \\$'agile_title_tails::kit_agile_title_kept_end(&*!a)' : string gives 0 violations and 1 export_seam.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_12 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 12u32, "2026-10-26T14:59Z", "eoie", "10:59 eoie: node proof of the redone wiring (job kit-lint-verb, runner step 4): the applier wired it (Cargo.lock refresh 0), regen exit 0, debug build 0, smoke 'kit lint macros <src> kit_lint_macros' gives 'ok files 4 macros 2 violations 0 new 0', and lint.spi plus command_spec_domain model.spi notebooks pass 2/2. Not landable as is: the generated kit_lint_macros/lint.rs is 2,056 lines against the 1,000-line gate, so the release topology check would refuse it (the other touched crates are 843-911). Next, a hand-modeled split at a crate boundary: a kit_lint_macros_model crate generating the classifier and baseline matching with an export (the report as typed rows), and kit_lint_macros keeping the file walk and CLI and calling that export, each under 1,000. Size the split from lint.rs's function list before drafting.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_13 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 13u32, "2026-10-26T15:10Z", "eoie", "11:10 eoie: split sized from the node-generated lint.rs (2,056 lines, 79 functions). Source: model.spi 351 lines, lint.spi 110; generated, about 300 lines come from lint.spi (lint_run 78, lint_report 40, the walk) and about 1,700 from model.spi (operator_between 96, line_macros 91, definition_name 81, global_macro 81, term_class 75, known_of 57, macros_from 52, words_of 51, the rest smaller). So the model alone is over the gate and needs two crates of its own. Cut by concern: (1) kit_lint_macros_scan (macro discovery: line_macros, macros_from, definition_name, global_macro, words_of); (2) kit_lint_macros_classify (term_class, operator_between, the class union and the violation row); (3) kit_lint_macros (walk, baseline known_of, report, CLI), joined by exports carrying typed rows. Each about 600-800 generated lines by these numbers; confirm with a regen before the wiring draft is rebased onto it.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_14 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 14u32, "2026-10-26T15:12Z", "eoie", "11:12 eoie: design tension in the split, found reading model.spi's structure before drafting. The natural cut (scan: lines 9-153, macro discovery and the diff filter; classify: 156-288, words, term_class, class_of, file_violations, baseline, rows) passes list host_macro and list violation between stages. A crate boundary is an export, and exports carry primitives, strings and tuples, so the lists would cross as joined-string rows, which the census counts as drift (no joined-string rows). Options to measure before choosing, rule set now: (a) cut where the payload is already a primitive, e.g. scan one file to a count tuple plus row text only at the report end, measured by generated lines per crate; (b) keep one crate and cut generated size by healing its own bloat (79 functions, 13 of them over 50 lines; a regen with the lint pass's own fixes may drop it under 1,000); (c) wait for a compiler export of typed rows. Take (b) first: it adds no seam, and the lint verb should not need a dispensation from the rule it enforces.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_15 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 15u32, "2026-10-26T15:32Z", "eoie", "11:32 eoie: option (b) measured and refuted. Hypothesis: 11 small helpers declared with := (inlined at each call site: word_letter, last_letter, first_letter, flow_word, punctuation, definition_word, global_line, declaration_body, macro_open, interpolated_open, hosted) explained the bloat, and :~ (one generated function each) would shrink lint.rs. Node regen with lint-join-points.do on the wired lint1 mirror: lint.rs 2,103 lines against 2,056 before (+47), with build, smoke and notebooks unaffected. So inlining is not the cost; the generated size comes from each string operation's expansion (sm'.trim and sm'.substring each become 10-20 Rust lines with slice bounds and Rc copies, visible in operator_between's 96 lines). Consequences: (1) the lint crate cannot get under 1,000 by declaration tweaks; (2) the remaining options are the export split (a), with its joined-row tension, or compiler-side string codegen, i.e. one Rust helper per lib string op instead of an inline expansion, which would shrink every kit crate. (2) belongs to the compiler lane; I'll send them the measurement. Until then the verb waits; its wiring is proven.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_16 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 16u32, "2026-10-26T15:39Z", "eoie", "11:39 eoie: compiler lane's read of the join-point miss (RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE, P3): string_slice is already one shared fn. The bloat is lib wrappers like sm'.trim and sm'.substring being := and partial-evaluated at every call site. A :~ on a large, often-called wrapper becomes one method (a top-level fn called per use), while on tiny helpers the call costs more than inlining, or the result becomes a boxed closure (Rc<dyn Fn> plus thread_local), the heavy path. That is why 11 small helpers grew the crate. Next measurement, set now: route lint's own trim/substring uses through two local :~ wrappers (a method each), regen, check that lint.rs has 'fn' definitions and no 'Rc::new(move |' closures for them, and compare lines.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_17 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 17u32, "2026-10-26T20:11Z", "kit", "16:11 kit: the wrapper measurement is done (job-1793035682149-kit-lint-wrappers2, mirror lint4, checked 1207 C9520BC87568C658; the first try, lint3, was void because its mirror sync failed). Lint's own trim, substring, starts_with and replace uses were routed through four local :~ wrappers (begins, cut, swapped, trimmed). Regen exit 0, debug build 0, both lint notebooks pass (lint.spi, command_spec_domain model). Shape check (scratch clean\\lint-verb\\wrapper-shape.ps1): each wrapper is one fn definition called 6 to 25 times, as intended, not a boxed closure. Size: lint.rs 2,005 lines, against 2,056 with no change and 2,103 with join points. So declaration-level tactics are exhausted: 51 lines, about 2.5%, against the 1,005 still to go. Decision by the 11:32 rule: the crate must be split into hand-modeled build units (option a, the export split: the lint model, the rule tables and the CLI entry as separate packages along the import graph), not tuned further. The lib-wide :~ change for sm'.trim and substring (RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE) would give at most what these local wrappers gave inside this crate, at the cost of a full lib oracle gate, so kit does not take it for lint. Also found: the smoke 'kit lint macros <src> kit_lint_macros' printed the top-level usage and exited 2, so the verb's CLI route is not wired by apply-lint-verb-kit.ps1. That goes with the split.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_18 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 18u32, "2026-10-26T20:26Z", "kit", "16:26 kit: split plan, written before any measurement. Library packages compile into their consumer's crate, so a split only shrinks lint.rs when it makes separate crates joined by Exports (the way kit_agile_mutation calls kit_handoff_render::handoff_render_owned). The cut must keep the interface narrow and must not cross the boundary as joined-string rows (the census bans them). model.spi (351 lines) has three layers along its own import order: (1) scanning, lines 9-153: macro_place/origin/class, host_macro, line_macros, macros_from, file_macros, global_line, declaration_body, the diff reader added_from/added_text; (2) classification, 156-235: outside_quotes, words_of, operator_between, arith_in, term_class, class_of, file_violations; (3) baseline and rendering, 241-289: known_violation, baseline_of, known, name_width, violation_row, tally_head. lint.spi (110) is the walk and the CLI entry. Candidate cuts: A, two crates. kit_lint_file (layers 1-3) exports one call per file, taking file, text and the baseline text and returning that file's rendered report text plus three counts (logic, known, new) as separate scalar exports; kit_lint_macros keeps the walk, the totals and the entry. B, three crates: as A, but layer 3 stays with the entry, and kit_lint_file returns per-violation scalars by index (count, then name/line/class at i), which is chattier but keeps rendering in one place. Rule: take the cut with the fewest crates in which every generated file is at or under 950 lines, the lint notebook and the smoke ('kit lint macros <src> kit_lint_macros') pass, and the report text is byte-identical to the unsplit binary on src. Rendered report text is output, not a data encoding, so A's boundary is allowed. The smoke failure (top-level usage, exit 2) is fixed in the same change by routing 'lint' in the CLI dispatch. Starts after the rename lands (the tree is held), measured on node mirrors.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_19 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 19u32, "2026-10-26T20:29Z", "kit", "16:29 kit: refinement of cut A from reading lint_report (lint.spi:46-66). Row alignment is global (width = name_width over every file's fresh violations), so a per-file call cannot render rows on its own. Cut A's kit_lint_file therefore exports scalars and text in two passes: macros_in(text) : i32, found_in(file, text) : i32, fresh_in(file, text, baseline) : i32, width_in(file, text, baseline) : i32, then rows_at(file, text, baseline, width) : string, the file's rendered rows at the given width joined by newlines (display text, not a tab or record-separator encoding). kit_lint_macros walks the files once for counts and the maximum width, then once more for rows, so the output stays byte-identical. Cost: about 5 parses per file instead of 1, so the rule adds a timing check: lint over src may take at most 2x the unsplit binary, otherwise go to cut B (the parsed model kept on the entry side, only scanning moved). Lint's own usage text already exists (lint.spi:86); the smoke's top-level usage means the CLI dispatch lacks a 'lint' route, which this change adds.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_20 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 20u32, "2026-10-27T13:39Z", "kit", "09:4x kit: the lint split is built, proven and staged. Cut A as planned (16:26) was measured first and failed its own rule: the per-file layer alone generated 1,560 lines (flat, 84 items, no hot spot), so per-file exports could not fit. The cut that fits sends typed parallel columns across the Export (arrays cross as Rc<RefCell<Vec<T>>>; no joined-string encoding). Layout: kit_lint_model (source package, model.spi plus code mappings for place, origin and class; each crate emits only what it reaches); kit_lint_scan (kit_lint_scan(text) returns line, name, text, closed, place and origin columns in one call per file, plus added_text, row and tally rendering: 946 lines); kit_lint_classify (kit_lint_class per macro, plus the baseline check kit_lint_known: 891); kit_lint_macros (the walk, the glue and the kit_lint_run export: 844), against 2,005 to 2,056 unsplit. Arrays are filled with create/set (arraym.fromList returns a different array type). Byte identity: the split and unsplit binaries (same compiler, entries exported the same way) give identical output over the kit src tree (959 lines; 792 files, 1,720 macros, 958 violations) and over lib/spiral (516 lines), and identical output with --baseline (new 306 of 515). Timing over the lib, 3 rounds each: unsplit 1,955/1,321/2,784 ms, split 1,182/2,025/973 ms, inside the 2x rule. The lint CLI route was missing (the 16:11 smoke printed top-level usage); it now exists: VerbLint plus kit_cli_model to kit_lint_macros::kit_lint_run (kit.rs 970). The pure sample test moved to kit_lint_model. Stage: clean/kit-rename/cli-ceremonies-evidence-lint (23 rows including one delete of kit_lint_macros/model.spi, Cargo wiring for 5 new crates), applied by staged-apply.ps1 after kit-release-SLICES. Next after it lands: the per-repo baseline files and the PostToolUse hook wiring.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_21 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 21u32, "2026-10-27T19:05Z", "kit", "kit 15:1x: the hook wiring is drafted and waits for TRAIN, which ships the public 'lint macros' verb (today's binary answers 'unknown verb: lint'). Scratch clean/granular-lint: eoie-style-check.next.ps1 lints every edited .spi under kit/src and lib/spiral with 'kit lint macros <root> <file> --baseline <rows>', and only fresh violations block (exit 2, rows quoted). Outside kit/src the hook stops there; inside it, the existing pattern and ratchet checks still run, and lint rows join their findings. The baselines live in kit/standards (lint-macros.rows, lint-macros-lib.rows), so nothing is written into the spiral repo. activate.ps1, after install: writes both baselines from the installed verb, proves them (one file must fail with an empty baseline, exit 1, and pass with the real one, exit 0), pipe-tests the new hook with a real payload, then swaps it in and keeps a .bak. The lib gate's kit acceptance is the other half of the wiring, still open.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_22 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 22u32, "2026-10-28T09:36Z", "eoie", "05:3x kit: 'kit lint macros' is installed (TRAIN2c). The activate.ps1 dry run gives baselines of kit/src 952 rows (800 files, 1668 macros) and lib/spiral 513 rows (114 files, 3695 macros). I'm holding the hook swap: x's first run found that a string literal containing a dollar sign is misread as a macro opener (KIT-LINT-DOLLAR-LITERAL), and the live hook would flag that pattern in new text across lanes. Activation goes after that fix ships: rerun activate.ps1 -Go, which writes fresh baselines, proves them with a positive control and swaps the hook with a .bak.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_23 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 23u32, "2026-10-29T13:37Z", "main", "10-29 09:4x main: false-positive class in 'kit lint macros' (kit.exe A5B55974). Host comment markers inside macro text are classified as arithmetic: '//' (C/C++/TS/F#), '/* ... */', '##' and '-- ' after the expression. On lib/spiral, 20 of 115 arith rows are this, among them all 7 in backend.spi's backend_switch fallback arms. Second class: a macro that IS one binary operator on two splices ('!a == !b', '!a === !b') is already a one-host-call primitive by GRANULAR-HEAL rule (1), so it should not be flagged. Fix: strip host comments by backend syntax before classifying, and exempt <splice> <op> <splice> forms. Repro: kit lint macros C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral, then backend.spi:10-36.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_24 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 24u32, "2026-10-29T16:54Z", "main", "10-29 12:5x main: third exemption class for kit lint macros (after n23's host comments and single-operator splices): HOST UNION BRIDGE, a macro that is one match/case on a host-native Result or Option whose arms only call Spiral constructor closures (!ok x, !error e). resultm.unbox (5 rows) and box are the examples. Also the HOST EXCEPTION BOUNDARY (try/with, try/except, try/catch wrapping one call), e.g. resultm.from_try (4 rows). Exempting these three classes would shrink lib/spiral's 434 definition-level rows toward the real logic-in-macro set.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_25 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 25u32, "2026-10-29T19:46Z", "main", "10-29 15:4x main: exemption classes found while healing (LIB-MACRO-HEAL n56-n71), to teach the lint so the census stops counting them: (1) host comment lines (n54); (2) single-operator primitives; (3) host exception boundary (try/with, try/except, try/catch); (4) host union bridge: one match on a host Result/Option/Poll whose arms call Spiral constructors, or an int.parse case into a tuple; (5) host platform boundary: cfg/#if/comptime os-tag selection; (6) host scoping idiom (Gleam let-rebinding of a global name); (7) one-call hold/cache helpers (OnceLock, Zig static slot); (8) host type definitions (a Python class body); (9) host sequence and comprehension constructs (F# seq yield, Python list comprehension); (10) single host combinator over a string (bytes().all, String.forall); (11) Zig optional or error-union captures; (12) lambdas that the regex reads as flow because of `=>`. Real-heal markers by contrast: a branch or loop whose arms do Spiral-expressible work (lazy-init check plus assign, OS read plus formatting, span arithmetic, bounds check plus index).")
inl note_of_granular_lint_26 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 26u32, "2026-10-29T22:00Z", "main", "10-29 18:0x main: class 13 for the list in n25, \"host library binding\": one host call that binds an external library function, operator or type (parsing's FParsec layer: pchar, pstring, >>., .>>, >>=, |>>). With this, LIB-MACRO-HEAL's census is fully classified (LIB-MACRO-HEAL n84).")
inl note_of_granular_lint_27 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 27u32, "2026-10-29T22:11Z", "main", "10-29 18:1x main: the exemption classes are now an EXECUTABLE, VALIDATED rule set for kit to port: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1029\\macro-class-census.ps1 (one regex per class, first match wins; rows in macro-class-census.tsv beside it). Run over the live lib after window 2: 91 flow-bearing macro lines -> 3 CANDIDATES, which are exactly the hand-classified open real heals (file_system.spi:1869 last_write_ms, queued; testing.spi:252/253 Zig for-loops, blocked). None of today's landed heals reappear. Classes with counts: union bridge 16, lambda arrow 13, thunk opener 10, exception boundary 7, C pointer arrow 6, zig capture 6 (if/while/catch/orelse only; `for` loops stay candidates), host type text 5, platform boundary 4, library binding 4, gleam rebinding block 3, one-call parse 3, one-construct ternary 3, one-op ternary 2, sequence/comprehension 2, host combinator 2, healed already 1, one-op host operator 1. Rule refinements that mattered: zig capture must exclude `for (...) |x|` loops (real logic); `->` needs C-pointer and type-text classes, or it floods; multi-line host matches need a header rule. When kit ports these into `kit lint macros`, use this run as the positive control: the same 3 candidates must come out.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_28 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 28u32, "2026-10-29T22:13Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:13 kit: port design for main's rule set (main-drafts\\session-1029\\macro-class-census.ps1, n27), read against kit_lint_model/model.spi. (1) Where it goes: kit_lint_model's term_class sets MacroFlow on any flow word or =>/->/;. Add a union flow_exemption with one case per main class (22: ExceptionBoundary, UnionBridge, PlatformBoundary, ScopingIdiom, TypeDefinition, SequenceComprehension, HostCombinator, CPointerArrow, HostTypeText, ThunkOpener, OneConstructTernary, OneCallParse, OneOpHostOperator, GleamRebindingBlock, HealedAlready, ZigCapture, UnionBridgeHeader, LambdaArrow, LibraryBinding, OneOpTernary, Comment, FsharpPipeMutate). exemption_of (text) : option flow_exemption tries them in main's order, first match wins; term_class keeps MacroFlow only when exemption_of = None. (2) No regex: lib's only regex is sm'.new_regex, a Rust-only legacy binding over std_string (!\\\\ macros), which kit's style contract forbids. Every rule is a keyword/substring/prefix test that words_of + sm'.contains/starts_with express as a typed predicate. The ternary and capture rules need small structural checks (e.g. a Zig capture is if/while/catch/orelse followed by |name|, and per n27 'for (...) |x|' stays a candidate). (3) Tests: one sample line per exemption class, plus main's 3 hand-classified heals as must-flag rows. (4) Positive control: 'kit lint macros C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral' prints exactly 3 flow rows (file_system.spi:1869, testing.spi:252, testing.spi:253), and main's census.ps1 on the same lib hash agrees row for row. (5) Size: kit_lint_model is a model package; check the consumer crates (kit_lint_classify, kit_lint_macros) stay under 1000 lines with the new crate gate.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_29 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 29u32, "2026-10-30T02:15Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T02:15 kit: port design refinement (adds to n28). kit_lint_model's host_macro keeps only the macro text (model.spi:148/152, a substring from the macro start to its closing quote), not the source line. Several of main's classes match context OUTSIDE the macro: scoping idiom (a 'Gleam = \\ () =>' prefix), healed already (a '? $'...' : bool ?>' wrapper), one-construct ternary, and lambda arrow ('fun x ->' before the macro). So the port adds a line_text field to host_macro (filled in line_macros, model.spi:149/153, from the line already in hand), and exemption_of takes (macro text, line text). The macro-only classes (exception boundary, union bridge, platform boundary, c pointer arrow, host type text, zig capture, comment...) keep reading text; the context classes read line_text. kit_lint_classify's export kit_lint_class(text, closed, place, origin) gains a line argument; its one caller is kit_lint_macros/lint.spi:44. Sizes to check at port time: kit_lint_classify and kit_lint_macros under the crate gate.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_30 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 30u32, "2026-10-30T14:39Z", "kit", "10-30 10:4x kit: PORT PLAN, refined after measuring, written BEFORE any code (pass line pre-registered here).\nMEASURED on the live lib (114 files): (1) Main's census (a scratch copy of macro-class-census.ps1) now finds 89 flow lines and 1 CANDIDATE, sm'.spi:754 (a '{ let text = ...; if ... else ... }' block). The 3 candidates in n27 were healed or moved since 10-29 (testing.spi:252/253 no longer hold for-loops), so n28's 'exactly 3' control is stale. (2) The installed 'kit lint macros' reports 209 flow rows; only 59 of them are flow by main's definition. The other 150 come from kit's term_class treating ';', let, return, loop and fn as flow (a variable named !fn, an array type '[`el; 10]', a 'let _x = ...' binding). Kit also misses 31 of main's flow lines, because main tests the whole line (ternary '? ... :', a Spiral '\\ x =>' between two macros). Exemptions alone cannot reach parity: the flow test itself must be main's.\nDESIGN:\n- New package and crate kit_lint_flow (HostFree, LibReferenced). flow_line (line) is main's $flow test without regex: the region between the line's first $' and its last quote holds a flow word at word boundaries (if, else, match, for, while, case, switch), '=>', '->', or '? ' followed later by ':'. flow_exemption is a union of main's 22 classes, each a list of typed pattern alternatives over a small piece union (Text, a run of letters/blanks/any/none-of/one-of with a minimum, Maybe text, Edge, End), matched with backtracking, first class in main's order wins. The crate exports kit_lint_flow (line) -> i32: -1 not flow, 0 flow candidate, n = exemption n.\n- kit_lint_model.term_class takes the flow decision as a bool instead of its own flow test; cast and arith keep their rules and order (flow, cast, arith). kit_lint_classify's kit_lint_class gains the bool. kit_lint_macros/lint.spi splits the file text into lines once and asks kit_lint_flow per macro line. kit_lint_scan is untouched (965 lines, near the gate).\n- Why a separate crate: kit_lint_classify is 891 lines and inlines the model, so the matcher must not be inlined there. The model only names kit_lint_flow in its test blocks.\nPASS LINE (per line, since main's unit is the line and kit's is the macro): on the same lib tree, (a) every main CANDIDATE line is a kit violation row of some class (sm'.spi:754 is over 120 chars, so kit reports it as long, which precedes flow); (b) every kit flow row's line is a main CANDIDATE line; (c) one unit test per exemption class (one sample line each), plus must-flag rows for the current candidate and for the healed 10-29 shapes (a Zig 'for (...) |x|' loop, a JS ternary over a call); (d) kit_lint_classify, kit_lint_macros and kit_lint_flow each regenerate under 1000 lines. Any other outcome: record the first disagreeing line here before changing a rule.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_31 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 31u32, "2026-10-30T15:17Z", "kit", "10-30 11:2x kit: PORT LANDED UNSTAGED in kit, and the pass line pre-registered in n30 is MET.\nWHAT CHANGED:\n- New package kit_lint_pattern (no crate): a typed pattern piece union (Text, Texts, Words, Run of letters with a minimum, Maybe, Edge, Start, End) and a backtracking matcher over the line's UTF-8 bytes (sm'.utf8_get_bytes). There is no byte slicing, so a multi-byte character can't panic at a char boundary; there's a test for it.\n- New crates:\n  - kit_lint_flow (284 generated lines): main's $flow test. The region between the line's first $' and its last quote holds an edge-bounded flow word, '=>', '->', or '? ' followed later by ':'. Lines are lowered first, since PowerShell -match is case-insensitive.\n  - kit_lint_flow_constructs (881): 11 host constructs.\n  - kit_lint_flow_idioms (898): 11 one-construct idioms.\n  - Why three: rule data costs about 2 generated lines per piece and the matcher about 300 per crate, so one or two crates could not stay under the gate (measured 1529, then 1331, then 1020/898).\n- Wiring: kit_lint_model.term_class takes the flow decision as a bool (cast and arith unchanged, same order), and flow_word is deleted. kit_lint_classify's export gains the bool (891 -> 792 lines). kit_lint_macros/lint.spi asks the three crates per macro line (858 -> 910). kit_lint_scan and kit_lint_shadows regenerate byte-identical. Cargo.lock gains only the 3 packages (cargo update --workspace: 15 added lines, nothing else moved).\nPASS LINE (n30), on the live lib, in a scratch workspace build of kit_lint_run:\n(a) main's census now has 1 CANDIDATE, sm'.spi:754, and kit reports it (class long, which precedes flow).\n(b) Kit flow rows that are not main candidates: 0.\n(c) Spiral tests through Cells: pattern 1/1, flow 1/1, constructs 1/1, idioms 2/2, model 8/8 (incl. the new flow_is_the_census_flow_test_minus_its_exempt_shapes).\n(d) All generated crates under 1000 (max 910).\nLib totals: 509 violations -> 293; flow 209 -> 0; arith 118 -> 123 (5 former 'flow' rows are real arithmetic). The spec/one-call.md lint case prints the same row and receipt on the probe and on the installed kit.\nFINDING: in main's order, FsharpPipeMutate (#22) is shadowed by LambdaArrow (#18): '\\bfun \\w+ ->' matches every '|> fun x -> _...' line, so the census can never report that class. Exemption is unaffected (it is any match). The idioms test now checks each sample against its own class's rule.\nDrafts: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\lint-flow (packages, wire-lint-flow.do, idioms-own-rule-test.do, run-lint-notebooks.ps1).\nLEFT FOR THE TITLE: installed-is-done (the next train installs this); JSON/TSV output with the exemption class per row; wiring into the PostToolUse style check and the lib gate's kit acceptance. Progress 70 -> 80.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_32 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 32u32, "2026-10-30T15:20Z", "kit", "10-30 11:1x kit, follow-up to n31 (main left the order to kit): FsharpPipeMutate now comes before LambdaArrow in kit_lint_flow_idioms, so the specific class is the one reported. A new assertion checks that idiom_of on the pipe-mutate sample is FsharpPipeMutate. Regenerated at 898 lines and landed unstaged; idioms notebook 2/2 pass. Draft: kit-drafts\\lint-flow\\idioms-specific-first.do.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_33 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 33u32, "2026-10-30T16:13Z", "kit", "10-30 12:1x kit: TSV output DRAFTED, deliberately NOT LANDED. Draft C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\lint-flow\\lint-tsv.do: 'kit lint macros ... --tsv' prints a header (file, line, name, class, text) and one tab-separated row per new violation (tabs in macro text become spaces), with the receipt line last as in 'agile list --json'. The row text is built in kit_lint_classify behind a new kit_lint_tsv_row export (872 lines), and the help line now shows [--tsv]; the dry run on the live tree matches. Why it waits: under pack 8, kit_lint_macros goes 910 -> 962 even after moving the row rendering out and mapping once (first cut 1012, then 977), and kit's own rule (EOIE-HAND-CRATE-UNITS n16) keeps crates out of the 950+ band. Nothing consumes TSV yet. Unblock: split the file walker (walk, spi_files, relative_to, skipped_dir, about 140 generated lines) into its own crate kit_lint_walk, exporting the path list as a string array. kit_lint_shadows reuses spi_files by Spiral inlining today, so it moves to the export too. Then land the TSV draft. JSON is not started.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_34 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 34u32, "2026-10-30T19:07Z", "kit", "10-30 14:5x kit: TSV LANDED UNSTAGED, unblocked by the first unit split from EOIE-HAND-CRATE-UNITS n18.\n- Split: kit_lint_macros' file walker (skipped_dir, entry_names, spi_files, relative_to, moved verbatim) is a new crate kit_lint_walk (211 lines) exporting kit_lint_walk_files. lint.spi reads the path list through two one-line primitives. kit_lint_shadows inlines the same Spiral functions from the new package (it used to reach into kit_lint_macros), so its walk can't drift. kit_lint_macros 910 -> 855.\n- TSV: then the n33 draft went on unchanged (anchors still matched): 'kit lint macros ... --tsv' prints a header (file, line, name, class, text) and one tab row per new violation; kit_lint_classify builds the row (kit_lint_tsv_row). kit_lint_macros ends at 907.\n- Proof, candidate kit.exe:\n  - lint rows on lib byte-identical to the candidate before the split (289 rows, same receipt);\n  - --tsv gives 289 rows, all with 5 fields;\n  - 'lint shadows' receipts identical to the installed kit on kit/src (854 files), tibia (65) and lib (114).\nJSON output not started; the hook and lib-gate wiring remain.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_35 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 35u32, "2026-10-30T23:54Z", "kit", "10-30 19:5x kit: hook wiring REDRAFTED and the baseline is fixed (the 10-27 draft and activate.ps1 were lost in the 10-28 wipe). (1) Bug fixed in kit_lint_model known_of (unstaged): a row with an empty name (an unnamed top-level type macro, e.g. kit_do_facts/host.spi:3) was parsed with its class word as the name, so those rows never matched their own baseline: the installed kit's self-check reports 'new 5' on kit/src and 'new 14' on lib. A name that is a class word followed by a non-class word now means an unnamed row; a macro literally named like a class keeps its name. Test added; candidate 4008B427 self-checks 'new 0' on both trees. kit_lint_classify 872 -> 933. (2) Baselines are in kit/standards (unstaged): lint-macros.rows (907 rows) and lint-macros-lib.rows (290), generated by the candidate, because the shipped binary must generate the baseline it is checked against (the installed kit classifies differently: 920/507). (3) Hook draft C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\lint-hook\\eoie-style-check.next.ps1: after the pattern checks, an edited .spi under kit/src runs 'kit lint macros <src root without the trailing slash> <relative path> --baseline standards\\lint-macros.rows', and fresh rows become findings. The trailing slash matters: with it, rows come out as absolute paths and nothing matches the baseline. Negative control on an unchanged file: about 3.4 s per call, with the lint part about 0.37 s. ACTIVATION: after the next train installs (1), main swaps the hook (hooks are main's), keeping a .bak. Open: lib/spiral edits (the hook still exits for paths outside kit/src); also the 27 s whole-tree run with a baseline, because known_in reparses the baseline once per violation (a single-file call is fine).")
inl note_of_granular_lint_36 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 36u32, "2026-10-31T00:29Z", "kit", "10-30 20:2x kit: kit_lint_model test blocks pass in Cells, 8/8, including the new unnamed-row baseline case (job kit-lint-model-tests2). The first run failed only because the notebook tree lacked kit_lint_pattern.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_37 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 37u32, "2026-10-31T01:35Z", "kit", "10-30 21:3x kit: whole-tree lint with a baseline: 31.5 s -> 4.0 s on kit/src, 3.4 s -> 1.3 s on lib (candidate 05DC0C41, same verdicts, and the rows without a baseline are byte-identical on both trees). Cause: known_in reparsed the whole 130 KB baseline for each of 907 violations. Now known_text splits the baseline once on '\\n<file>:' and parses only that file's rows; a longer file name ending in the same text cannot match. The kit_lint_known export also moved from kit_lint_classify to kit_lint_walk, because the faster lookup would have taken classify from 933 to 961; now classify 619, walk 651, macros 907 (its call changed only its crate path). kit_lint_model Cells 8/8 including the new known_text cases (job kit-lint-model-tests3). Draft C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\lint-baseline-unnamed\\known-text.do; lands after TRAIN5 releases the tree lease and ships in the following train.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_38 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 38u32, "2026-11-01T01:18Z", "kit", "10-31 21:2x kit: lint baselines are typed Spiral rows now (DATA-ROWS-TYPED). 'kit lint macros <paths> --baseline <file.spi>' reads a baseline module of 'inl lint_known_<n> () = LintKnown (file, name, class)' rows (line numbers are not part of a row's identity, as before); '--write-baseline <file.spi>' writes one: with --baseline given it converts that accepted state exactly, without one it writes the current findings. The reader splits on the row prefix, not on lines, so a formatter-wrapped row still reads back. kit/standards now holds lint-macros.spi (909 rows: the 907 old rows plus the 2 cross-crate calls added tonight, agile_task_notes note_added -> agile_note_segments and kit_lint_shadows open_rows -> kit_lint_opens, the sanctioned crate-boundary shape) and lint-macros-lib.spi (290 rows); the .rows files are archived (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261031-210429). PROOF: node candidate 81741E5A (job-1793495163724-kit-node-lint3, kit_lint_model/model.spi 9/9 incl. the wrapped-row case); on the box with that candidate: kit src vs lint-macros.spi new 0; a copy with its 8 long rows wrapped the formatter's way (949 lines) new 0; lib vs lint-macros-lib.spi new 5 = the same 5 the old .rows gave, so the conversion is exact. LIB DRIFT, for the lib owners (not accepted by kit): process.spi:84 split_args_regex_case and :110 split_args_path_input flagged arith (false positives: test data strings containing '-', a lint precision gap worth a fix in kit_lint_classify), erlang_process.spi:332 broadcast long, and two new Delphi arms since 19:09, unicode.spi:96 and xml.spi:18 'Ord((!s)[!i + 1])' arith (real: index arithmetic inside the macro; GRANULAR-HEAL rule says compute i + 1 in Spiral and pass it in). Ships with TRAIN7.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_39 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 39u32, "2026-11-01T03:38Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom, NEXT CONCRETE STEP (CPU-light, top-3): fix the arith false positive in kit_lint_classify: a '-' (or '+') that sits inside a quoted string literal within the macro body is data, not arithmetic (lib process.spi:84/:110 split_args_* test strings). Add a classifier test with those two macro texts expecting no arith and with unicode.spi:96 'Ord((!s)[!i + 1])' still arith; then node notebook kit_lint_model + candidate (full owner list).")
inl note_of_granular_lint_40 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 40u32, "2026-11-01T06:56Z", "kit", "11-01 02:5x kit (round 3): classifier false positive FIXED in the tree (unstaged), kit_lint_model. term_class checks arithmetic and ==/!= on the macro body OUTSIDE string literals: outside_strings splits on quotes, re-joins escaped quotes (odd backslash run), keeps the even segments, and turns each literal into a 'literal' operand token, so an operator between literals still reads as arith. Fixes lib process.spi:84/:110 split_args_* (dashes inside test strings), while unicode.spi:96 'Ord((!s)[!i + 1])' stays arith. Test minus_signs_inside_string_literals_are_not_arithmetic. PROOF: node job-1793515823795-kit-node-r3l, kit_lint_model/model.spi 10/10 (first run: a blank placeholder left '+' without a left operand). Ships with TRAIN7; the lib baseline then shrinks by those two rows.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_41 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 41u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: 'kit lint macros C:\\home\\git\\kit\\src --baseline standards\\lint-macros.spi' prints new 0 on the installed binary after TRAIN7; on lib the two process.spi split_args_* false positives are gone (new count drops by 2 against the same baseline); every lane's edit-time hook reports a new host macro in the text just added (pipe test). NEXT STEP: ship TRAIN7 (typed baselines, string-literal fix), then rebaseline lib with --write-baseline after the lib owners rule on the 3 remaining rows. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN (in code): macro_class closed union (MacroLong | MacroFlow | MacroCast | MacroArith | MacroBlock), host_macro record with origin/place unions, baselines as typed LintKnown rows; remaining stringly part: classification still scans macro text with string splits (outside_strings, operator_between); next refactor: tokenize the macro body once into a token union (Ident | Op of op | Literal | Punct) and classify over tokens.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_42 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 42u32, "2026-11-02T13:16Z", "main", "main 11-02: the probe misses multi-line host blocks. kit standards --census reported wide-host-macro=1 for ALL of lib/spiral (35,956 lines), yet json.quote's Rust arm alone is a 4-line `\\`-continued $'...' block. A direct count (macro-blocks.ps1 in drafts\\i574n.github\\GROOM-1102-MAIN): kit/src 146 files, 550 continued host-string lines, 30 single-line statement blocks; lib/spiral 39 files, 71 continued, 142 statement blocks; x, fiscal and tibia src/apps 0 so far. WHO: kit and lib (lib is shared: its recent statement blocks need attribution by blame). DoD addition: the lint counts `\\`-continued host strings and host strings containing `;`/for/while/match/let mut as violations, matching these counts.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_43 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 43u32, "2026-11-02T14:59Z", "main", "main 11-02 lesson from the star exchange (kit #7): measure generated size per variant before calling a shape granular. In json.quote, pure-Spiral char-by-char cost ~+230 generated Rust lines while a global fn plus a one-line call cost +16. So the granular rule is 'logic as typed Spiral, host text as one call into a global fn', not 'no host text'. JSON-QUOTE-GRANULAR is being done that way.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_44 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 44u32, "2026-11-02T17:45Z", "main", "main 11-02 13:4x FULL macro-block census (macro-blocks.ps1; continued = a host string ending in a backslash continuation; statement = a host string holding ; / for / while / match / let mut / {..;}), the answer to the user's \"detect which lane is still playing dumb\": kit/src 550 continued in 146 files + 30 statement (still growing: kit_jobs/host.spi 18 and kit_grep_query/search.spi 10 touched 11-02 00:49-02:37, kit_style_census/census.spi 8 at 06:59); lib/spiral 71 continued in 39 files + 142 statement (recent: file_system.spi 10+1 and date_time.spi 6 at 02:44, sm'.spi 6+10 and base.spi 4 statement at 07:27 incl. the Kotlin arms, json.spi 1 = kit's 03:10 quote arm, xml.spi 2); dice 24 in 7 files (mtime 11-01 22:02 = main's R1b2 consumer edit, not new drift); regex 1 statement (bench host shim); x, fiscal, tibia, apps/cells, graphs, polyglot/lib 0. File mtime says touched, not who added: attribute lib blocks by blame before assigning. kit is the lane still adding them.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_45 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 45u32, "2026-11-03T04:16Z", "kit", "11-03 00:1x kit (gate-claimed): kit tree went from 15 NEW lint violations to 3, all unstaged.\n(a) kit_os_search: 5 casts now go through convert.unativeint (sibling idiom from kit_do_walk/kit_lint_macros/kit_machine_sample); digits is pure Spiral.\n(b) kit_os_files.entry_names has no host code left: lib file_system.read_dir_names, with resultm.unbox' for the error path.\n(c) kit_os_machine is one-call primitives: sysinfo handles as Rc nominals, MB/GB division and disk search in Spiral, and NonZeroUsize::get instead of a cast closure.\n(d) Lint fix in kit_lint_model: edition-3 `:!` nominals are type text (rule 1 exemption) and a definition word; test added.\n(e) standards/lint-macros.spi: dropped the 17 stale kit_grep_query/search.spi rows (that host code moved into kit_os_search) and added 3 sanctioned rows: the kit_os_search runtime global (main 11-02: host text as one call into a global fn; precedent row 577 kit_os_launch global) and the two agile_claim_api cross-crate calls (same shape as rows 908/909). 909 -> 895 rows.\nRemaining new: kit_os_search :! flow (fixed in tree, ships with the next train) and 2 casts in resilience's unstaged BEAM-GUARD work (told them the idiom).\nCensus: kit_os_machine top-level-macro 2->12 and kit_os_search host-macro 10->13 are the accepted price under rule (6); baseline-merge after the europa proof.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_46 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 46u32, "2026-11-03T05:05Z", "kit", "11-03 01:0x kit: DoD wiring done (unstaged). Statement rule landed.\n(1) Statement rule (main 11-02 DoD): kit_lint_model.term_class marks a `;` outside string literals and outside [...] as block. The census HostStatement probe matches it, minus false positives: array repeats `[x; n]`/`vec![0u8; n]` and `;` inside escaped strings are not statements. Continued (unclosed) macros were already block.\n(2) Pipeline fix: dropped the naive quote-strip (unquoted_body) for macro_body plus the escape-aware outside_strings. The cast check now uses code outside literals. This finally clears lib process.spi split_args_*, which the 11-01 note called fixed but the old pipeline still flagged. It also surfaces string concatenations like `\"<\" + !tag + \">\"` as arith, as the model's own documented intent says ('an operator between literals still reads as arith'); that is existing drift, so it gets baselined, not counted as new.\n(3) PostToolUse: ~/.claude/hooks/kit-style-check.ps1 runs `kit lint macros <package>` (~50 ms) and reports rows missing from standards/lint-macros.spi, keyed package/file+name+class, whose macro text is in the added text. KIT_STYLE_ROOT/KIT_STYLE_KIT seams; hooks-test.ps1 has 4 cases (reported, baselined quiet, outside-added quiet, missing kit fails open); 0 failed.\n(4) Acceptance: release-acceptance.ps1 row 'lint macros on kit: no new rows past baseline' (new 0).\n(5) Baseline: kit rows added for the 7 pre-existing statement macros (cli_check fixtures, launch scratch_removed, queue held_released, argument_file rejected, rules floating), now 902 rows. Europa candidate: kit new 0.\nProofs: kit_lint_model 10/10; lint owners regen + cargo check; rank 999 lines; cli_check 2/2; grep_cli 7/7.\nREMAINING: (a) ships with TRAIN14b, which is waiting on resilience's BEAM-GUARD build. (b) After install, rebaseline lint-macros-lib.spi on the box lib tree: append rows the new binary reports and the old one did not, keyed by file+name+class (europa's lib mirror is a different tree, 123 vs 127 files). (c) Lib owners heal the statement rows: leptos dispatch blocks, listm'.cons', near.vector_extend, rust.fix_closure, console.write (Kotlin run {...}).")
inl note_of_granular_lint_47 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 47u32, "2026-11-03T05:46Z", "kit", "11-03 01:4x kit: REMAINING (b) DONE before install. Lib baseline rebaselined by a same-tree A/A: the box lib tree (128 .spi) was copied to europa; the old binary (AF229BEC) ran on the box and the candidate on the copy; findings were compared by file+name+class, which carry no line numbers.\nstandards/lint-macros-lib.spi went 290 -> 266 rows. Removed: rows for keys only the old binary flagged (old false positives such as leptos/svg render dashes inside strings, bench_kit cases, base exn/use). Added: 29 keys only the new binary flags in existing code (statement blocks, string concatenation arith).\nOne correction: file_system.spi read_bytes was UNBASELINED drift under the old binary (arith) and is reclassified as block. It must stay reported, so its block key is NOT baselined.\nCheck: old binary + old baseline = new 10; candidate + new baseline = new 8 (10 minus the two process.spi split_args false positives). The same 8 real lib rows remain: erlang_process broadcast, sm' index_of, json global, unicode ^, xml pascal_byte_at, convert parse_f64, env get_environment_variable, file_system read_bytes.\nKit: the 7 statement rows baselined earlier were never flagged by the old binary, so this is detection of existing code, not absorbed drift.\nLEFT: ships with TRAIN14b; lib owners heal the 8 real rows and the statement blocks (GRANULAR-HEAL slices).")
inl note_of_granular_lint_48 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 48u32, "2026-11-03T05:48Z", "kit", "11-03 01:5x kit: Blocked at 95%. RESUME when TRAIN14b installs (job-1793682628186-kit-TRAIN14b): confirm `kit lint macros C:\\home\\git\\kit\\src --baseline standards\\lint-macros.spi` = new 0 and lib = new 8 on the installed binary, then mark Done. The 8 real lib rows are GRANULAR-HEAL slices for the lib owners, not this item.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_49 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 49u32, "2026-11-03T08:01Z", "kit", "11-03 03:5x kit PAUSE (main: the 21:00 run was armed early by mistake; run paused). STATE unchanged from n48 (Blocked 95%): confirm kit new 0 / lib new 8 on the installed binary. WAITS ON: TRAIN14c.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_50 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 50u32, "2026-11-03T10:09Z", "kit", "11-03 06:1x kit: TRAIN14c INSTALLED (kit.exe 5B9082C1; 58/58 notebooks; crate gate 284/284 under 1000). Confirmed on the installed binary: kit lint new 0 (961 files, 892 violations, all baselined). lib new 10 = the 8 known rows + 2 NEW from tibia's unstaged lib/spiral/file_system.spi (c_read_into arith, mmap global long; tibia told). DoD met (classification, per-repo baselines, JSON/TSV out, PostToolUse hook, release-acceptance row; baselines only shrink). Done.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-25T07:11Z", "main", "10-25 03:11 main: 25 of 48 macro sites healed in the repo (escape-free strings + path splices -> plain ++#), 23 left: CSS/TeX escape lines 51-82, crowbook --single quoting 89/103, Rust block 283, git ls-tree/hash-object 357/364, exports/args/exit 739-746. Proof job-1792911393939-main-alphabet-heal-build (build.ps1 -SkipNotebook: Rust compile, cargo, run on README + nota with exact nota.hangul.md). Dice mirror: dice has no documents.spi, nothing to mirror.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-25T11:01Z", "main", "10-25 07:01 main: heal2 GREEN (job-1792912923374). The 17 escape-heavy sites (CSS/TeX args, the crowbook --single quoting, git ls-tree/hash-object) are plain Spiral strings with ++#. Proof: every folded literal in documents.rs is unchanged (202 before, 208 after; 0 missing, 0 unexpected beyond the 6 allowed concatenation pieces), no format! remains at the healed sites, and RUST-OK passes with the exact nota.hangul.md check. 5 macro lines remain. The Fsharp exports (739/740) and the Rust argv/exit (743/746) are one host call each, so granular style keeps them. The real one left is the Rust directory-walk block at 283 (find .md files, skip .hangul.md), which becomes a file_system call in Spiral.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-25T11:57Z", "main", "10-25 07:57 main: heal3 (the Rust directory walk at line 283 as plain Spiral: read_dir_names + directory_exists + </> + am'.new_vec) was RED (job-1792926763461): documents.main logged result_len = 0, so no README/nota outputs were produced. heal3 restored documents.spi and documents.rs (macro lines back to 5). directory_exists is right (exists && is_dir), and relative_path strips dist_dir then maps \\ to /, so the separator mix alone shouldn't zero the list. Probe job-1792929390206 checks the building blocks on a temp dir (read_dir_names count, directory_exists on dir and file, ends_with) to find which one returns the unexpected value.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-25T18:33Z", "main", "10-25 14:33 main: heal3's RED (result_len 0) root cause found, and it's in lib, not in the walk. walk-probe-2 (job-1792943650492) ran heal3's exact walk on a temp tree: the list has 2 files, but am'.new_vec turns it into a vec of length 0 (actual (2, 0), expected (2, 2)). new_vec's Rust arm pushed into the vec inside a listm.iter closure. For a literal list that iteration unrolls at compile time, which is the only case its existing test covers; for a runtime list the closure pushes into a captured copy. Fix staged as LIB-NEW-VEC-RUNTIME (%TEMP%\\lib-new-vec-runtime\\apply.ps1): fold vec_push over the list, plus a test whose list comes from a runtime recursion. Gate + land queued: job-1792953140278, after the lib chain. Then heal3 reruns unchanged; its rule stays the alphabet RUST-OK run check with the exact nota.hangul.md.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-26T10:06Z", "tibia-lane", "10:06Z 10:1xZ tibia lane: the documents.spi walk and exit heal (GRANULAR-HEAL notes 06:35Z/06:48Z; original and healed walk proven identical on a fixture: 6 files, and 1 with the filter) is packed into tibia-serial-gaps. It applies with documents-heal-apply.ps1 -Apply right after the lib lands. The step is guarded: env.spi must have exit_with and file_system.spi must have path_join_native (both node GREEN, landing in the same job). It lands as an unstaged working-tree change in alphabet; documents.spi is LF and clean in git, so only the healed spans change. Follow-up after the land: the documents app's cargo build on the remote kit worker node.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_6 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-26T19:28Z", "main", "10-26 15:28 main: 10-26 15:2x main: alphabet apps/documents argv slice drafted (TEMP alphabet-lib-args/apply.ps1), the CLI-ARGS-STYLE consumer twin of dir-tree-html's. main's Rust arm built argv as one host iterator chain (std::env::args().skip(1).map(...).collect into Rc<RefCell<Vec>>). It becomes ':= args : array_base string = env.command_line_arguments () |> listm'.box |> listm'.to_array'' (the list-to-array idiom lib runtime.split_args uses). env.command_line_arguments starts at the first user argument (try_command_line_argument applies each backend's argument_offset), matching skip(1). Anchored, count 1, 0 host argv code left. The new line is 105 chars; the file's one line over 120 (292, 127 chars) predates this change and is left as authored. Guarded: refuses unless lib env.spi carries command_line_arguments. Its exit line (758) waits on LIB-EXIT-WITH, as before. Proof after the lib chain job-1793024343933 lands: alphabet apps/documents/build.ps1 (the consumer build) on the applied tree, with restore-on-RED like perf-fix-run. dice: no counterpart (dice has no host argv chain; census 13:3x).")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_7 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "main", "10-29 22:00 main, groom (WIP rule, 3 days with no note): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 85%. Last note 2026-10-26, before the 10-28 profile wipe interrupted every lane, and no live job. Resume when picked; it is not abandoned, and it keeps its priority P1.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_8 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-31T20:54Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_9 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-11-01T21:22Z", "main", "main 17:2x LAST HEALABLE SITE DONE (unstaged): documents.spi:755's Rust exit macro ('if !exit_code != 0 { std::process::exit(...) }') is now '? exit_code <> 0i32 ?> env.exit_with exit_code |: ()', the lib primitive that landed for this. The walk and argv heals had already landed. Remaining macro lines: only the two F# entry exports (750/751, one host call each, which granular style keeps). PROOF: box build compiles and runs with exit 0, and nota.hangul.md equals the expected Hangul text exactly. The run check still fails on missing README.md.html / *.hangul.md.pdf, but the HEAD baseline (my edit reverted, same job pattern) fails identically: the box has NO crowbook (a wipe casualty; tectonic is present). Main is installing crowbook via alphabet's own init line (cargo binstall --git crowdagger/crowbook --locked) and re-running the proof. documents.rs is 15,273 lines, far over the 1k gate; that's a separate split job.")
inl note_of_alphabet_granular_heal_10 () = Note ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", 10u32, "2026-11-01T22:24Z", "main", "main 18:2x DONE. With crowbook installed on the box (v0.17.1, alphabet's own init line, 16 min build), the full documents proof is GREEN: RUST-OK, exit 0, all 16 outputs (README/nota .md, .hangul.md, .html, .epub, .pdf), and nota.hangul.md exact. The heal leaves only the two F# entry exports as host lines (one host call each, kept by granular style). Unstaged: alphabet/apps/documents/documents.spi (one line) plus the regenerated documents.rs.")
inl note_of_polyglot_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-25T07:48Z", "main", "10-25 03:48 main: classified, nothing edited. chat_contract.spi: 3 type aliases (12/152/171) and 3 one-call sites (81 panic_str, 108 get.cloned, 182 get.cloned) are already granular. The 5 multi-statement Rust blocks (39 alias validation, 65 CID varint encoding, 122/131 alias-map update, 161 account lookup) are the real heal. Proof: a wasm32 build plus chat_contract_tests, which need the NEAR sandbox and so run on Linux (CI); WSL waits for the workflow per the standing rule, so this item pairs with a CI run. math.spi: 9 complex operators (10-85) are one host call each, so they stay. The heal candidates are the 3 println! traces (96, 254, 263) -> console.write_line with format_debug (same {:?} text, if format_debug's Rust arm is format!(\"{:?}\")), plus the exit at 555. The pyo3 glue (137-162, 274-284) stays as host calls; 276-284 opens a closure across macros, which is the real design smell. Proof: the math build runs the Rust+pyo3 tests, which needs a Python env.")
inl note_of_polyglot_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-26T10:05Z", "tibia-lane", "10:05Z 10:1xZ polyglot math.spi exit site (note 1's 'exit at 555'), tibia lane. main's $'if !code \\!= 0 { std::process::exit(!code) }' (host if plus exit) becomes ? code <> 0 ?> env.exit_with code, over LIB-EXIT-WITH (node GREEN, queued to land in tibia-serial-gaps). polyglot's deps/spiral is a symlink to C:\\home\\git\\spiral, so the land is visible at once. Proof (scratch polyglot-exit/probe-run.ps1): math.spi compiled to Rust against the tree lib and against a copy with exit_with. The generated math.rs differs only at the exit site: 'if v41 != 0 { std::process::exit(v41) };' becomes a Spiral-side 'let mut v42: bool = v41 != 0i32; if v42 { std::process::exit(v41); () }', where the exit now comes from lib's arm, already proven to exit with its code and run nothing after it. Apply: scratch polyglot-exit/polyglot-exit-apply.ps1, an anchored single replace that refuses on 0 or more than 1 match. It is packed at the end of tibia-serial-gaps, guarded on env.spi having exit_with, and lands as an unstaged working-tree change in polyglot (the file is LF and clean in git). The println! traces (96, 254, 263) stay: format_debug renders tuples without parentheses, unlike {:?}, so moving them would change trace text, and the pyo3 test run needs a Python env. Follow-up after the land: polyglot math build.ps1 (Rust plus pyo3 tests) on CI or the node.")
inl note_of_polyglot_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-26T13:53Z", "tibia-lane", "13:53Z 14:0xZ tibia: generate_cid heal drafted, proven and offered to main (POLYGLOT-GRANULAR-HEAL n1 listed '65 CID varint encoding' among the 5 multi-statement Rust blocks). The block (varint buffer, hasher new/update/finalize, iterator chain, concat, multibase) becomes the spiral_out_varint_u64 global shim (GRANULAR-LINT rule 8, out class: a buffer plus calls), plus sha256_digest (one call, <sha2::Sha256 as sha2::Digest>::digest(..).to_vec()) and multibase_base32_lower (one call). The [0x12, 32] ++ digest and [1] ++ codec ++ multihash concatenations move to Spiral via am'.new_vec and am'.vec_extend. Proof (scratch polyglot-cid/probe-run.ps1): the old and healed functions in one module, compiled to Rust and built by cargo (multibase =0.9.1, sha2 0.10, unsigned-varint 0.8), print identical CIDs for [], [104;105] and [0;255;7;85]. The [] case equals the generate_cid test's bafkreihdwdcefgh4dqkjv67uzcmw7ojee6xedzdetojuzjevtenxquvyku. Apply: scratch polyglot-cid/cid-heal-apply.ps1 (anchored, refuses on rerun, dry-run ok on base 1826A02274991F07). It lands with main's is_valid_alias heal or on main's word.")
inl note_of_polyglot_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-30T16:13Z", "tibia-lane", "10-30 12:1x tibia wipe-redo audit: both tibia heals for this item are in polyglot's tree and survived the wipe (the repo is outside the profile). lib/math/math.spi:561 is `? code <> 0 ?> env.exit_with code` (note 1's exit site). apps/chat/contract/chat_contract.spi:63-89 holds the generate_cid heal (spiral_out_varint_u64 shim, sha256_digest, a test), unstaged in polyglot. Progress 10% predates both; re-scoring is main's call (team main).")
inl note_of_polyglot_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-11-03T08:52Z", "main", "main 11-03 04:2x FOLDED, closing: polyglot left the org 11-03 and its code is not kept, so healing it in place is moot. The two heals travel with their code: chat_contract.spi's generate_cid heal (lines 63-89, unstaged in the local polyglot) moves with the contract to i574n.github/apps/chat under NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES, and lib/math/math.spi's exit site moves under ZETA-NUMERIC-CORE (the zeta lib is written to the granular rules from the start). Removed from GRANULAR-HEAL's deps.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T07:50Z", "x", "10-24 03:50 x: started, rule from GRANULAR-HEAL n1 applied mechanically. Own census (lib-draft\\macro_census.ps1: length > 120, 'as' cast, arithmetic, flow; ';' inside string literals gives false positives): staged tree 21 sites / 2,443 chars. Tonight's landing in x's working tree already removed 591 chars: the glyphs -> unicode and oauth1 -> lib delegations dropped x's char_of/graphemes/nfc wrappers and the hmac/sha1/base64/percent macros. Now 18 / 1,852. Batch 1, programs-x-heal-1\\land.eoie (3 files, 7 replacements, dry-run fits x's tree): glyphs first_code_point/code_point_count -> lib unicode (2 cast sites gone); net status_of = ($'!r.0' : u16) |> conv and requests_made = ($'...load(Relaxed)' : u32) |> conv; wire number_of = ($'..as_i64().unwrap_or(0)' : i64) |> conv, count_of = map_or(0, Vec::len) typed convert.unativeint |> convert (no closure), item converts i to unativeint before the call (no cast in the macro), unescaped becomes 3 sm'.replace calls (no macro). On those 3 files: 13/1,406 -> 7/1,027. Cells glyphs + wire + net on %TEMP%\\x-heal-check: job-1792828153670. It lands into x after the landing verify (so the verify reads a stable tree), then gets verified again. Next: glyphs plain_weight/blank (they need a code-point list: a lib unicode.code_points primitive per backend, then the weight ranges in Spiral), net call_reply/send_reply (the match on the ureq Result becomes a result bridge per constructor), wire's replied_to search (Spiral over json helpers), token synced_write, oauth1 clock_seed, locate.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-24T07:53Z", "x", "10-24 03:53 x: batch 2 (programs-x-heal-2, net/net.spi, applies after batch 1). The 200-char match macros in call_reply/send_reply become a bridge in rule (2)'s shape: nominals ureq_response / ureq_failure / ureq_outcome, one-call primitives called / sent_with / outcome_ok (is_ok) / outcome_response (unwrap) / outcome_failure (unwrap_err) / response_status (u16 |> conv) / response_body / failure_text, and reply_of in Spiral (if/else, with r used once per branch after the is_ok borrow, so Rust's moves hold). resultm.unbox was not used because Spiral unions derive Clone and ureq's Response isn't Clone. http_reply is now a Spiral i32 * string, so status_of/body_of are pattern bindings, not macros. net.spi: 6 sites -> 1 (the jiff timestamp map_or left). No Cells test reaches this path, so the check is a regenerate of the net crate plus cargo check -p xc_net on %TEMP%\\x-heal-check: job-1792828390876.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-24T08:00Z", "x", "10-24 04:00 x: batches 1+2 COMPILE on Rust. Regenerating the net crate and running cargo check -p xc_net on %TEMP%\\x-heal-check (job-1792828507946, 2m56s) exits 0 with the ureq result bridge, http_reply as a Spiral tuple, and the conv/convert.convert casts. The first try failed: bare `convert` in wire.spi resolves to lib's convert MODULE (ETY-ModuleMustBeImmediatelyApplied / ExpectedSymbolAsModuleKey), so batch 1 now writes convert.convert (memory spiral-macro-gotchas). There are 2 existing warnings, both from request_counted's drop(...) of a Copy u32. Next heal for it: ($'XC_REQUESTS.fetch_add(1, std::sync::atomic::Ordering::Relaxed)' : u32) |> ignore, which removes the drop and the warning. Still pending: the Cells check of batch 1 (glyphs, wire and net notebooks, i.e. the gleam paths), job-1792828153670.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-24T08:11Z", "x", "10-24 04:11 x: batches 1-3 APPLIED to C:\\home\\git\\x (working tree, unstaged) at 04:10. Batch 3 (programs-x-heal-3): oauth1 clock_seed/unix_seconds as dependency-free std::time one-call primitives (system_now, since_epoch, whole_seconds, sub_micros, process_id) with the micros arithmetic in Spiral (lib date_time would have pulled in chrono, which x's crates don't depend on); token synced_write as create/write_all/sync_all one-call primitives with the order and short-circuit in Spiral (the file's own file_text bridge style); net request_counted as fetch_add typed u32 |> ignore (drops the drop(Copy) warning). The crate check (cargo check -p xc_net -p xc_token on the healed copy) exits 0. x census: 6 sites / 795 chars, from 21 / 2,443 on the staged tree. Left: glyphs plain_weight (rust + gleam) and gleam_blank, which wait for main's unicode heal + code_points; net's jiff format_timestamp map_or; wire's replied_to search; locate's flat_map. The queued x-verify-landing pre-commit will verify the healed tree.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-24T08:16Z", "x", "10-24 04:16 x: batch 4 APPLIED to x (programs-x-heal-4). wire replied_to_in is a Spiral scan over count_of/item/at_key, with no macro left; net local_minute bridges the Option<jiff::Timestamp> (instant_found/instant_value) plus one-call system_zone/zoned_in/minute_text, with the fallback in Spiral; locate's posts_of_pages became page_posts : list json (collect id + spread over field \"data\"), and its only caller maps framed_of over the list, so the rebuilt JSON array is gone. Crate check of net, locate and token exits 0, and the regenerated net.rs/locate.rs carry no old macro text. x census is now only the 3 glyphs sites, which wait for main's unicode heal and then code_points.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_6 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-24T08:19Z", "x", "10-24 04:19 x: batch 1 Cells (job-1792828153670): wire 23/23 (failed_targets same as baseline), net 2/2, glyphs 6/8. The 2 glyphs failures (graphemes_hold_clusters, a_line_break_pair_is_one_grapheme) are both gleam and both 300 s cell timeouts (cell started 03:53 with main's chain, eoie's GE3 and tibia's gate running). Not caused by the heal: batch 1 doesn't touch graphemes, lib unicode.spi is unchanged since 20:45, and the same glyphs + lib unicode passed 8/8 at 01:22. Rule (timeout-only -> rerun once; a pass means contention, a repeat timeout is a real defect): retry job-1792829946596.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_7 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-24T08:25Z", "x", "10-24 04:25 x: rule applied (n6): the glyphs timeout-only retry PASSED 8/8 (job-1792829946596, 304 s), so the two gleam grapheme timeouts were contention. Heal batches 1-4 are verified: Cells glyphs 8/8, wire 23/23, net 2/2, plus crate checks for net, token and locate.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_8 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-24T11:22Z", "x", "10-24 07:22 x: census on HEAD 6ef9c48: 3 sites left (glyphs plain_weight rust+gleam arms, gleam_blank), down from the title's 32. All 3 wait on main's lib unicode heal plus code_points. One side effect fixed: the heal's wire.replied_to_in loop (replacing the serde macro) went 19 over x-compat's post weight, so it was hoisted at 07:05 into a top-level first_reply_from (unstaged) and x-compat is at 0. Gate: job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_9 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-10-24T23:46Z", "x", "10-24 19:46 x: gate moved. verify3 failed e2e (fixed: chain_json, expectations), and verify4 stopped at the sim frames build (X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS) before x-compat ran. So first_reply_from's hoist is proven only by the 07:05 local x-compat (0 in 36 files). The gate for it is now verify-landing5. The 3 remaining sites still wait on main's unicode heal; main is respelling sm'/listm' to edition 3 now, so check for that heal after the respelling.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_10 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 10u32, "2026-10-25T11:34Z", "x", "2026-10-25 07:34 -04:00 x: the last 3 sites are DRAFTED; the lane that needs a lib primitive lands it (no wait on main). Patch: scratchpad\\code-points\\apply.ps1 (-Lib/-Glyphs; a dry run on copies applies cleanly). lib unicode gets code_points (gleam: string.to_utf_codepoints + utf_codepoint_to_int as one-call primitives; rust: chars().collect() into list' plus rust_char_code; order via listm'.rev'/fold' in Spiral, as graphemes does) and is_white_space (the Unicode White_Space set in Spiral, the set Rust's trim uses and that x_spaced spelled in Gleam), each with a rust+gleam test. x glyphs: rust_weight/gleam_weight/x_light become light + code_point_weight + plain_weight over unicode.code_points, and rust_blank/gleam_blank/x_white/x_spaced become one blank over unicode.is_white_space: one Spiral rule for both backends, no macro left. Also checked: the 17 sim modules about to land carry 0 host macros (positive control: the original cells_executor had 18). Applies after serial3 releases the live tree; gate: Cells lib unicode + x glyphs, splitter, reading (the weight users) on rust and gleam.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_11 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 11u32, "2026-10-28T00:00Z", "x", "2026-10-27 20:00 x: re-census while the lint verb waits for kit's next release (TRAIN ended unpublished, so 'kit lint macros' isn't installed). The 10-25 draft is in: lib unicode.code_points/is_white_space exist and glyphs.spi's plain_weight and blank use them. Heuristic over every x client macro (host text with control flow, closures, 2+ chained calls or statements): 9 flagged. 8 are one host API spelled as a builder or accessor chain (wire field get-or-default, ureq post/delete builders and their config, body read, strftime), which is one host call. 1 carried logic: wire.spi replying cloned a record and mutated its 'reply' field in host statements. Healed: reply_refs builds {root, parent} and reply_record builds the whole record in one json! construction (same keys and order, so the request body is unchanged), and reply_write composes them. Real x-compat 0 in 42 files, no line over 120. net.spi patient/patient_bodied share host text but not types (bodiless vs bodied builder); merging needs an unconstrained generic, so they stay. Proof: the verify after verify22, which synced before this edit, plus a regen of wire.rs.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_12 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 12u32, "2026-10-28T09:28Z", "x", "2026-10-28 05:28 x: the real lint is installed (kit TRAIN2, published 05:25) and replaces my heuristic census. 'kit lint macros' over x: sim spi 24 files, 2 macros, 0 violations; client 43 files, 156 macros, 11 violations. Healed 4 genuine 'block' rows, each one host call wrapped with a backslash continuation only to fit 120 characters: chain.thread_pages, jsonl.selected_lines and view.selected_lines (macro on its own line, unchanged text), and net.basic_auth (the macro bound to 'encoded' on one line, using base64::prelude::BASE64_STANDARD, the same engine as general_purpose::STANDARD in base64 0.23.1). After: 7 rows. 6 are a lint false positive, a string literal \"$\" (the cashtag sigil) read as a $\"...\" macro opener (defuse.spi 155, 162, 173, 196, 204; preview.spi 24), reported to kit with the repro. 1 is net.spi:80, a one-line global declaring the XC_REQUESTS atomic counter, which is state, not logic, so it stays. Real x-compat 0 in 43 files, no line over 120. These 4 heals post-date verify25's sync, so the verify after verify25 proves them (behaviour-neutral: same host calls, same encoder).")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_13 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 13u32, "2026-10-29T17:13Z", "x", "2026-10-29 13:1x x: CLOSED. Closes by X-WIP-VERIFY n15 (verify27 clause) as amended by n17: verify29 carries the same 4 lint heals plus the wire reply_record heal on the respelled tree. verify29 (node job-1793273389666, compiler A4E480202BD4 pinned, mirror xv29 synced 10-29 07:27 with the respelled tree, the lint heals and the defuse links-only change) is GREEN on every X-WIP-VERIFY n6 clause: exit 0 after 20,493 s; sim accept 45/45; client gate 29 crates 0 over 1,000 and 149 files 0 over 1,000; x-compat 0 findings in 43 client + 29 sim files; all 42 client and all 23 sim notebooks passed; plain e2e 153 stub requests, 0 FAILED (the accept rewrote 1 of 37 answers, the respell inside the prelude-text fixture, reviewed: only . -> :: plus the splitter re-chunking it causes); sim build exit 0 with corpus commute=ok over 73 files; the tampered thread rejected (same_cells=false). Outputs landed three-way 10-29 13:1x: 20 files, 0 conflicts (node-proof -Land x-verify29 -Apply).")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_14 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 14u32, "2026-10-29T19:17Z", "x", "2026-10-29 15:1x x: RE-PROOF PENDING (per main's sweep 5 and memory recompile-drafts-after-compiler-change). This item closed on verify29, which ran on the pre-6b compiler A4E480202BD4. Pack-6b (core 31CC96520D6B7269, DLL 9895730DAE80) is now installed on both boxes. The first 6b run, verify30, was RED for reasons outside this item (fields.spi's new test cell overweight; ExUnit 60 s load timeouts), both fixed. verify31 (node job-1793299160741, 6b) is this item's re-proof: if any of its clauses fails there, I reopen the item with the cause.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_15 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 15u32, "2026-10-30T02:01Z", "x", "2026-10-30T02:02Z groom: Done 100 -> Active 95 so the state matches the previous note's RE-PROOF PENDING: closed on verify29 (pre-6b A4E480202BD4) and not yet re-proven on installed pack-6b. verify32 (live, xv30) was synced after the 15:19 heals; verify33 (-Accept) is the planned re-proof. Back to Done 100 on the first green 6b verify, with its job id.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_16 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 16u32, "2026-10-30T10:27Z", "x", "2026-10-30 06:2x x groom: CLOSED with evidence. verify33 (job-1793328097444, pack 7 core 9C899B30495F7DC5, -Accept, LandRepos x,spiral) finished exit 0: client build, sim build, e2e accept and sim accept all exit 0; 66 notebooks passed.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T07:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 03:40 tibia: census by GRANULAR-HEAL rule 1 as written (one host call or expression, at most 120 chars, no casts, no arithmetic, no flow: if/elseif/for/while/local/function/return, and/or/not fallbacks, ;). Over every tibia .spi in the tree it finds more than the umbrella's 5 sites, because Lua (function() ... end)() blocks and x or fallback count as flow. Per file: client.spi 22, lua.spi 10, offscreen.spi 9 (Rust Win32 blocks), and the other arena files 1-4 each (adjacency 1, competitor 2, measure 3, observer 3, path 2, random 2, scoreboard 1, steps 4, summon 1). Plan, rule-first, ordered by what's already in flight: (a) the non-client arena files: the LIB-MIGRATION rebased draft already has 0 macros there and re-lands tonight after PR-27; competitor's last one ($'(!text) == \"\"') goes in slice S1 (note 35). (b) lua.spi's 10 are retired as pure Spiral by LIB-MIGRATION S2 (mut cells) and S3 (lists, arrays, src/rng), with no macro left but the native_table type. (c) client.spi's 22 OTClient bindings: each becomes one-call primitives (one getter per host value, nil bridged to option by one primitive per case) plus Spiral logic, the biggest first (instrument_game_calls 1101 chars, the inventory report 809, the monster list 388, footing_at 269, the attack lines 227 and 152, login 198, bot settings 189 and 156, position hooks 156, chase 126, protection zone 125), each with a lua Cells test from TIBIA-CELLS-APPS. (d) offscreen.spi's 9: Rust Win32 calls as one-call primitives with the struct and loop logic in Spiral (rule 3 keeps extern declarations). Estimate by milestones: (c) 22 x 12 min, about 4.5 h; (d) 9 x 15 min, about 2.3 h; (a) and (b) are counted in LIB-MIGRATION. Total about 420 min, replacing the 45 min first guess.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-24T08:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:34 tibia: rule (4) census for client.spi: all 73 definitions have callers in apps/arena, apps/host or src (enable_client_bot is used inside client.spi), so nothing is deleted and (c) is a real rewrite. The pattern, set on the largest testable site, footing_at (269 chars): five one-call primitives, map_tile x y z = g_map.getTile({ x = !x, y = !y, z = !z }), tile_present = (!t) ~= nil, tile_walkable = (!t):isWalkable(), tile_floor_change = (!t):hasFloorChange(), tile_walkable_through = (!t):isWalkable(true); then a pure Spiral footing_of present walkable floor_change walkable_through : footing (the if-chain of Unknown/Free/Ramp/Occupied/Blocked), with footing_at s = footing_of over the primitives. footing_of replaces CELLS-APPS's footing_of_code split (the code numbers existed only to cross the macro boundary), and the client Cells test moves to footing_of's 5 cases. The other 21 sites follow the same shape: one getter primitive per host value, nil bridged by a ~= nil primitive, and the logic and string building in Spiral. Verification needs the Cells mounts fix (the arena package can't mount until it lands), so (c) starts after it.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-24T08:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:41 tibia: (d) offscreen.spi design, drafted in scratch offscreen-heal/win32-section.spi and NOT landed. Rule 3 keeps one declaration global: unsafe extern 'system' blocks with safe fn items (Rust 2024), handles typed i64 so no as-casts remain, and the two #[repr(C)] structs with pointer fields as usize filled through .addr(), so #[derive(Default)] works. Out-params go through Cell::as_ptr, and the cwd fallback is Spiral over file_system.directory_get_parent. Two blockers found while drafting. (1) offscreen's 3 Cells tests cover only parsing and report text, and nothing exercises the Win32 calls, so a heal could compile and still break every live run. A Windows-only behaviour test comes first (create and close a desktop; start cmd /c exit 3 hidden, wait, read exit 3), gated off on Linux. (2) Pointer lifetime: a pointer taken in one primitive and used by a later call can dangle if Spiral's Rust codegen passes the Vec<u16> as a clone. Rule for this file: every pointer is derived inside the same host expression as the call that uses it (CreateProcessW gets its wide strings and its inline StartupInfo literal in one expression, with short parameter names to stay under 120 chars). Start (d) after the behaviour test exists.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-24T08:55Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:55 tibia: (d)'s first blocker has a draft: scratch offscreen-heal/win32-behaviour-test.spi (one rust test, a_hidden_desktop_runs_a_program_and_reports_its_exit_code). It creates the 'spiral-offscreen-test' desktop, starts cmd.exe /c exit 3 on WinSta0\\spiral-offscreen-test with start_on, and checks wait_for returns 0 within 10 s and exit_status is 3, then closes the process and the desktop. Off Windows it passes trivially through platform.is_windows, since CI runs Linux. It lands first, against today's (unhealed) offscreen.spi, and must pass there before any heal; then the granular rewrite must keep it green. It needs a compile slot and a window that's free of live-gui (it creates a desktop), so it waits for a quiet moment after tonight's queue.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-24T09:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:03 tibia: (d)'s safety net is in place. The Win32 behaviour test passes against today's unhealed offscreen.spi (job tibia-offscreen-behaviour, 4 of 4 with the 3 existing tests): a hidden desktop is created, cmd.exe /c exit 3 runs on it, wait_for returns 0 and exit_status returns 3. The pointer-lifetime worry from note 3 is answered by inlining: every primitive is an inl, so macro text is substituted at its call site with the caller's own variable (wide_start application is application.as_ptr() on the same live Vec), so no clone happens; a pointer can dangle only across a let function or a closure, and the section has none. The long macros are shortened (create_process_at takes a null *const u8 parameter instead of repeating std::ptr::null()). The healed copy (scratch offscreen-heal/offscreen-healed.spi: the Win32 section replaced, plus the behaviour test) scans 0 macro violations, with the declarations as rule 3 allows, and runs as job tibia-offscreen-healed.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-24T09:10Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:10 tibia: (d) LANDED. apps/offscreen/offscreen.spi's Win32 section is granular (job tibia-offscreen-land, exit 0): one declaration global (unsafe extern 'system' with safe fn items typed i64, and the two repr(C) structs with usize pointer fields and Default), 0 macro violations, every call a one-expression primitive, and the cwd fallback and branching in Spiral. Acceptance before the land: the healed copy passed 4 of 4 under Cells (job tibia-offscreen-healed), including the new Win32 behaviour test (hidden desktop, cmd /c exit 3, wait 0, exit 3), which also passed on the unhealed file (job tibia-offscreen-behaviour). The land script checked the source against the base hash, backed up the source and offscreen.exe (scratch offscreen-heal\\offscreen-backup-20261024-050832), ran the formatter check, rebuilt target/offscreen/offscreen.exe (25,379 bytes of Rust), and smoke-ran live/run-offscreen.ps1 on cmd.exe /c exit 0: pid 70784, exit 0 on desktop tibia-probe. Left: (c) client.spi's 22 sites (after the Cells mounts fix), while (a) and (b) ride LIB-MIGRATION. Restage for the user: apps/offscreen/offscreen.spi.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-24T09:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:34 tibia: (c) first slice drafted: scratch client-heal-footing.ps1 heals footing_at, the largest client.spi site with a pure part (269 chars of Lua flow). Five one-call primitives (tile_at, tile_present, tile_walkable, tile_floor_change, tile_walkable_through_creatures) plus a pure footing_of over the four facts, replacing CELLS-APPS's footing_of_code, with the client test moved to footing_of's 5 cases (Free, Ramp, Occupied, Blocked, Unknown). Proven on a copy of the CELLS-APPS candidate client.spi: both anchors match once, footing_of_code is gone, and no line is over 120. It applies after the CELLS-APPS land (tibia-after-block-2), then a client Cells run on lua and c.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-24T09:38Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:38 tibia: (c) second slice drafted: scratch client-heal-batch2.ps1 heals player_zone (zone-check primitives plus Spiral branching), attacking_short and attacking_described (creature_present plus the existing name, health and spot getters, and spot_text and target_line built with ++#; client.spi now opens sm'_operators), and chasing (ChaseOpponent, DontChase, get and set chase mode primitives, compared in Spiral). Applied after client-heal-footing.ps1 on a copy of the CELLS-APPS candidate: every anchor matches once, no line is over 120, and client.spi's violating macros drop from 22 to 17. Both slices run after the CELLS-APPS land, then a client Cells run on lua and c, plus the arena build and its LUA-OK parse check. Next by size: instrument_game_calls (1101 chars; it wraps g_game functions to count calls, the hardest to split) and the inventory report (809).")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-10-24T09:43Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:43 tibia: (c) third slice drafted: scratch client-heal-batch3.ps1 heals the and/or fallback sites. env_text and env_i32 are now Spiral over lib env.get_environment_variable, plus a tonumber primitive (lib's own os.getenv or \"\" fallback belongs to LIB-MACRO-HEAL). player_present is player_exists && game_online. positioned_monster and live_monster moved below the creature getters and compose creature_is_monster, creature_dead and creature_positioned. parity_summary, mute (g_sounds present, disable, stop) and write_log_line (probe log missing, start, text, with the trailing newline added in Spiral) are healed too. Applied after the footing and batch2 slices on a copy of the CELLS-APPS candidate: every anchor matches once, no line is over 120, and client.spi's violating macros go from 22 to 9. Order constraint: env_text needs lib env, which the arena package reaches only after the libmig re-land (packageDir lib, spiral-), so the order is libmig, then CELLS-APPS, then the three client slices, then the client Cells run and the arena build. Left: 9 sites, mostly multi-statement blocks (instrument_game_calls, the inventory report, the monster list, forget_dead, login, the position hooks, the bot settings).")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 10u32, "2026-10-25T06:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 02:30 The client slices are re-derived for the staged base and edition 3: footing's anchor is now the staged inline footing_at (no footing test without CELLS-APPS), and batch2's sm'_operators header goes before the first nominal. Method: apply to the words original (respell-repo's original copy), then format --spelling symbols. Control check: respelling the unhealed original reproduces the tree byte for byte. Result: client.spi violating macros go from 22 to 9, no line over 120, lua. helper calls 11 to 11; the file is in scratch heal-e3. Found and fixed in batch2 and batch3: PowerShell here-strings drop the final newline, so five edits joined lines (gotcha in memory; the first land's parse error came from this, not from 5j). The land (job-1792909058769, restored itself) is blocked by LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS: with inl composites competitor.lua exceeds 200 locals, and with let composites it exceeds 60 upvalues. It resumes when that lands, from the let variant (scratch client-let.spi), since functions are the better granular shape. Remaining after that: the 9 multi-statement sites.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 11u32, "2026-10-25T10:06Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 06:06 Batch 4 drafted (scratch client-heal-batch4.ps1, edits on the edition-3 healed client-let.spi; result client-let-b4.spi). step: 8 one-call direction constants plus walk_direction, chosen by a Spiral if-chain over a local 'toward' predicate (negative literals written 0 - 1). disable_client_bot and enable_client_bot: bot_settings_node, presence check, empty node, mark disabled/enabled, store and save primitives composed in Spiral (current_bot_settings). on_position_change: the one-expression curried adapter 'function(c, n, o) (!f)(c)(n)(o) end' plus two single connect calls. Type-checked on the scratch tibia root (scan and path compile against it); no line over 120. Census 9 to 6, where one of the 6 is that adapter, flagged only for the word function and the same branch-free shape as hooks.spi's arity adapters. Real sites left: instrument_game_calls, the inventory report, the monster list, forget_dead (pairs over lua.native_table, which belongs to LIB-MIGRATION's S3 family) and the login connect with its onLoginError adapter. Lands with batches 1-3 once LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS is installed.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 12u32, "2026-10-25T12:06Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 08:06 Batch 5 drafted (scratch client-heal-batch5.ps1; result client-let-b5.spi): log_in's connect becomes login_error_handler and text_message_handler (one-expression adapters with the tostring conversions) plus connect_game_events (one connect call with a table constructor). Type-checked on the scratch tibia root (scan and summon compile), no line over 120. With batches 1-5, client.spi goes from 22 violating sites to 4. All 4 iterate lua.native_table counters with pairs/sort (instrument_game_calls, game_call_report, forget_dead, the monster list), so they move to TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION's S3 family, where lua.native_table itself retires. GRANULAR-HEAL's own client.spi part is drafted in full and lands (client-let-b5.spi over client.spi, guarded with arena build plus lua Cells) once pack 5l with LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS installs; competitor needs those limits either way.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 13u32, "2026-10-26T04:36Z", "tibia-lane", "04:36Z Granular census of tibia today (scratch granular-census.ps1, main's n12-n17 rule, multi-line macros included): 31 flagged sites, all in apps/arena (client 22, lua 5, hooks 4 accepted arity adapters); src, canary, offscreen and predict are clean. The client heal (batches 1-5) is rebased onto cells-apps' merged client.spi, because cells-apps lands first in the heals retry. A 3-way merge (base = the tree, ours = client-let-b5, theirs = cells-apps merged) gave 2 conflicts, resolved by notes 7 and 10: the package header plus sm'_operators, and the heal's granular footing (5 one-call tile primitives plus pure footing_of) instead of footing_of_code. cells-apps' footing test is rewritten to footing_of's 5 fact cases, pinning the same Free/Ramp/Occupied/Blocked/Unknown mapping. Result (scratch client-rebase/client-healed-on-cells.spi): the census goes from 22 to 7 (3 one-expression callback adapters plus the 4 native-table sites of S3 family 5); formatter clean; no line over 120. Queued: a client-heal step at the end of tibia-serial-heals, after the cells-apps retry. Its control is the cells-apps merged client, so it refuses unless cells-apps landed, and the arena build restores it until pack 5m's LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS pair is installed. The node type-check offload (scan, path, summon, observer and steps compiled against the healed client in mirror tibia-granular) is staged; it was blocked when the node went unreachable at about 00:3x local.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 14u32, "2026-10-27T09:06Z", "tibia-lane", "09:06Z STATE CHECK 05:0x (granular census on the live tree, scratch granular-census.ps1 -Detail): tibia is down to 9 flagged macros in 2 files. lua.spi is at 0 (retired), offscreen at 0, src and predict clean. client.spi is at 3 (the heal's plan said 4), so the client heal of batches 1-5 is IN THE TREE: it arrived with tonight's arena-f5 composition, and a 3-way merge of the scratch client-healed-on-cells.spi against the tree shows its blocks byte-identical on both sides. That scratch copy is superseded, not parked WIP. The installed SpiralCompilerCore.dll (10-26 08:19) carries LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS' spillChunkLocals, so the old \"lands once 5l installs\" condition is met.\nAll 9 remaining are Lua arity adapters, the shape this item accepts: client 331 (on_position_change, 3-ary curried), 385 and 388 (login and text-message handlers), and hooks 22, 25, 28, 30, 33 and 107 (hook wrappers, a no-op function, getSpectators, dofile with unpack). Open follow-up, small: client 385/388 apply tostring inside the adapter. Per the user's \"even casts go through Spiral's type system\", the adapter should pass the raw lua value and Spiral should convert it with a one-call primitive. Drafted after the running tibia gate, since every build.ps1 builds the arena first.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 15u32, "2026-10-27T09:20Z", "tibia-lane", "09:20Z CASTS HEALED in client.spi, in the tree, unstaged (05:1x). login_error_handler and text_message_handler no longer cast inside their Lua adapters. A host nominal host_value plus a one-call primitive host_text ($'tostring(!v)' : string) do the conversion in a typed Spiral closure (typed), and the adapters are now pure arity forwarders: function(message) (!typed)(message) end and function(mode, text) (!typed)(mode)(text) end.\nProof on the consumer path (job-1793092132219, apps/arena/build.ps1): build exit 0, all 13 probes LUA-COMPILED and LUA-OK (loadfile parse). Diff against the pre-edit Lua from minutes earlier: the probes without log_in (bot_dofile, bot_env, bot_prelude, parity) are byte-identical; the rest change only as intended (tostring moves into generated locals, the adapters lose it) plus local renumbering.\nCensus: client.spi drops to 1 flagged site (331, on_position_change, a pure arity adapter). Tibia total: 7, all arity adapters (client 1, hooks 6). Open: a live login run is the runtime check, done at the next live block (no live run tonight; live runs go alone).")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 16u32, "2026-10-27T09:21Z", "tibia-lane", "09:21Z CORRECTION to n15: the census count does not drop. The census flags the word function, and the adapters keep it, so client.spi stays at 3 flagged sites and tibia at 9 (rerun after the edit). What changed is their class: all 9 are now pure arity adapters with no cast or other logic inside, the shape this item accepts. n15's \"drops to 1\" and \"total 7\" were wrong.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 17u32, "2026-10-28T10:31Z", "tibia-lane", "GROOM 10-28 06:4x: DONE. Notes 15-16: all 9 remaining flagged sites are pure arity adapters with no cast or logic, the shape this item accepts. Both deps are Done: TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION (just now) and LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS (10-28 03:0x, 5n).")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_1 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T09:14Z", "eoie", "05:1x eoie, measured on the user's question ('a lot of eoie processes up, drift or intended?'): 61 eoie processes = 1 job host + 56 job supervisors + 3 caller-side waiters + 1 window run. Only 8 jobs are running; 47 are queued (main 36, x 11, eoie 5, tibia 3, counting running) and each queued job holds a resident supervisor (~7 MB) plus its hidden conhost (~7.5 MB): 48 idle supervisors, ~800 MB together on a memory-tight machine. Cause: job start spawns the supervisor at once, and the supervisor itself waits for --after, the slot and the window (job chaining 10-21, note 31 on EOIE-JOBS). It was a convenient implementation, never a design goal, and it drifted as queues grew to 40+ jobs. Rule (acceptance written before work): a queued job is only a record in the jobs home; the per-owner host starts its supervisor when the job becomes runnable (predecessor settled, slot and window granted). Done = with N queued and R running jobs, the eoie process count is hosts + R supervisors (+ caller waiters), measured by job doctor; queue order, --after/--after-ok semantics, timeouts counted from start and 'job stop' of a queued job all keep their current receipts. Pairs with EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE (stage R: no conhost per supervisor) and EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT (S1: hosts under the owner session, not explorer).")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_2 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T09:21Z", "eoie", "05:2x eoie: narrowed after a search I should have run first. EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST already holds --after-chained jobs without a process (release P, tonight's train; queue.spi step_of). This item is now only the gate waits: the host decides slot and window admission itself (the window store and heavy slots are files it can read on each 500 ms beat) and starts the supervisor holding the granted lease, so no process waits for a gate. The acceptance in note 1 is unchanged.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_3 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 3u32, "2026-10-24T10:06Z", "eoie", "06:0x eoie: user follow-up ('i thought you were going to heal the processes when i asked, help me here'). Healed what can be healed live: my 9 queued checks are now one serial runner (job-1792836186900-eoie-serial-checks; eoie processes 74 -> 66), and main (40 queued), x (4) and tibia (3) were asked to requeue serial chains the same way. Main fixed the starvation source the same hour (Invoke-Heavy runs heavy at Normal). Gap found in eoie: 'job chain' (one-call case 42) starts N jobs with N resident supervisors, which is the pattern that piles up processes. Until P lands, a chain should be ONE job that runs its links in order, each under its own window. Add to this item: 'job chain --serial' (or the chain default) runs the links inside one supervisor, keeping per-link receipts.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_4 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 4u32, "2026-10-24T10:17Z", "eoie", "06:1x eoie: live heal measured. Every lane collapsed its queued chains into serial runner jobs: eoie 9 -> 1, main 40 -> 4, tibia stopped 1 independent gate, x had no serial chain. eoie processes 74 -> 28, supervisor conhosts 56 -> 22, free memory 1.3 -> 1.7 GB. The runner pattern (one job, steps in a foreach, each under its own window run) is the interim rule until release P, stage R and this item land.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_5 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 5u32, "2026-10-24T10:31Z", "eoie", "06:3x eoie: drafted 'job chain --serial', the one-call form of the runner pattern every lane used by hand tonight. Scratch: job-chain-serial/job-chain-serial.do, 11 edits, dry run clean on post-R. Model (eoie_job_rechain/model.spi): a typed chain_head {mode, first, serial, runner} parsed by a fold over --ok/--serial/--run-serial/<after-id>. links_timeout sums the link limits (one job's limit; a non-numeric limit refuses). serial_start_arguments builds ONE 'job start [--after...] <first>-serial <sum> eoie job chain --run-serial [--ok] -- <links>'. run_arguments makes each link 'job run <link>' (in-process supervision, so each link keeps its own id and receipts). continues_after: --ok stops at the first failure, plain continues. Runner (chain.spi): serial_ran runs the links in order and ends 'eoie job chain ok serial ran=N failed=M' or 'stopped serial ... skipped=K'. Usage updated in both places. A test block covers the start arguments, the sum, the refusals and the stop rule. Typecheck appended to runner eoie-serial-checks-2. Effect: a waiting chain costs 1 supervisor instead of N, today, without the host changes.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_6 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 6u32, "2026-10-24T11:18Z", "eoie", "07:1x groom: 'job chain --serial' moved to its own ready item EOIE-JOB-CHAIN-SERIAL, since it doesn't wait on the host held queue. This item keeps only the gate waits (slot and window) held by the host.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_7 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 7u32, "2026-10-25T11:28Z", "eoie", "07:28 eoie design, written before code, grounded in eoie_job_host/queue.spi (step_of over queued_job.wait: NoChain or Chained(id, mode, PredecessorLive|PredecessorSettled) gives KeepWaiting / StartSupervisor / SettleSkipped / SettleStopped; host_run's held_from loop beats held files and starts supervisors through eoie_job_queue_decide). (1) Model: queued_job gains a gate field, union start_gate = NoGate | HeavySlot | CompileWindow : u64 (minutes) | HeavyWindow : u64, read from the job record that job start already writes (shape, slot_minutes, min_free). (2) step_of becomes chain first, then gate: a settled-or-absent chain with an ungranted gate is KeepWaiting, so no supervisor exists while a job waits on a slot or window. (3) Grant without a race: the host claims the window or slot itself (the same admission verdict eoie_window_query uses; heavy also needs a free heavy slot), then starts the supervisor with the claim id in an env var; the supervisor adopts the claim instead of requesting again, and releases it on exit as today. If adoption fails (a stale claim), the supervisor requests normally, which only costs a wait. (4) Order: held jobs are granted in queue order per class, so a later small compile cannot starve an earlier heavy job (the current per-supervisor polling has no global order). (5) Acceptance, unchanged from note 1: with N queued and R running, eoie processes = hosts + R supervisors (+ caller waiters) per job doctor; receipts for queue order, --after/--after-ok, timeout from start, and job stop of a queued job unchanged; plus a new test: two queued heavy jobs and one free slot give exactly one supervisor. Lands after S1 (owner-parent hosts), which lands after SLATE.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_8 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 8u32, "2026-10-25T20:18Z", "eoie", "16:18 kit: baseline before SLATE (live count while SLATE runs). 22 job supervisor processes are alive, 42 MB working set in total: 13 for queued jobs (16 MB) and 9 for running jobs (26 MB). So 59% of supervisors exist only to wait for a slot or an after-link. This item's goal is 13 -> 0 (the host holds queued jobs with no process). Re-count after the change with the same query: supervisors by job status.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_9 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 9u32, "2026-10-26T08:40Z", "eoie", "04:40 eoie: evidence from the release candidate: every job queued with --heavy --after-ok in the flag-order check got 'supervisor_pid=held' in its receipt, so a gate-waiting job holds no process of its own; the host holds it. Worth a dedicated post-install count: N queued jobs, 0 supervisors among them.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_10 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 10u32, "2026-10-26T10:35Z", "eoie", "06:35 eoie: the post-install count note 1 asked for now exists and passes on the release candidate. Scratch clean\\respell\\queued-no-process-check.ps1, run on the worker node against the current tree's debug kit.exe with a fixture jobs home: a 60 s predecessor, then 3 jobs queued with --after-ok behind it. (1) all 3 receipts say supervisor_pid=held; (2) no process on the machine has any of the 3 queued job ids on its command line (Win32_Process scan). 2/2 pass, then every job is stopped. A missing binary reports VOID (exit 2), not a failure. Wired into post-install-RENAME.ps1 as a required step. Scope: this proves jobs waiting on a predecessor hold no process; jobs waiting on a heavy slot go through the same host path but need a held slot to queue, which post-install's flag-order check covers only by receipt.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_11 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 11u32, "2026-10-27T00:54Z", "kit", "20:54 kit: verified on the INSTALLED kit.exe (C0F2D2F3C66BFD43, published 20:26:42) by post-install-RENAME after the finalize: queued-no-process 2/2 (3 queued receipts say supervisor_pid=held; no process names a queued job id). Status left at 30%, because the item has scope beyond this count.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_12 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 12u32, "2026-10-27T14:26Z", "kit", "10:4x kit: live measurement of the remaining scope on the installed kit.exe (10-26 20:26). 6 queued jobs on this box: 5 wait for a heavy slot and all 5 still hold a 'job supervise' process, sitting in kit_job_slot's slot_loop; 4 of them also have a pwsh naming their id. The 1 job waiting on --after holds no kit process (the one pwsh naming it is a lane's own watcher). So --after waits are done (note 2, and verified at 20:54), and heavy-slot waits are not. Design for the rest, written before building: the job host already polls --after jobs; it gains a second held state, HeldForSlot. For a --heavy job the host creates the job's ticket itself (the owner pid is the host's own pid, which is long-lived, so first_live_ticket keeps honoring it), polls 'first live ticket is mine and slot_free' on its existing tick, and only then starts the supervisor with SPIRAL_HEAVY_TICKET set. The supervisor's slot_gate adopts an existing ticket named in its env instead of creating one, and the heavy wrapper's Invoke-Heavy already adopts it. The window class (--compile) follows the same shape through kit_job_gate. Done check: queue 2 heavy jobs behind a held slot; their receipts say supervisor_pid=held, and no process names their ids until the slot frees. Stop, slot_by_ms expiry and --front must keep working in the held state. Size: kit_job_host's crate, the slot crate (448 after --front) and the gate crate get measured first. It builds on the job-front-ticket stage (same slot.spi), so it starts after that stage lands.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_13 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 13u32, "2026-10-27T17:21Z", "kit", "kit (fc1568a6): HeldForSlot drafted as one do program (scratch clean/held-slot/held-slot.do), built on the job-front-ticket stage, with no change to kit_job_host (host_run stays at 991). The decision moved where the host already asks: kit_job_queue's queue_decide routes StartSupervisor through a new kit_job_slot export, held_step. For a job with heavy-wrapper.ps1, held_step creates the job's ticket once (honoring --front), owned by the host's pid and holding the job id, and records it in the job's slot_ticket file. It returns 'hold' until that ticket is the first live one and slot_free, then stamps the handoff time into the ticket and returns 'start'. slot_by_ms expiry settles a held job with exit 75 (expired-in-queue) and removes its ticket. A job settled while held (stop, skip) drops its ticket through a second export, ticket_dropped. The supervisor's slot_gate adopts a handed ticket by renaming it to its own pid with the same ticks, so it keeps its queue place and dies with the supervisor as before. A sweep removes a host-owned first ticket whose job is no longer held and whose handoff is older than 60 s (a spawn that never came). Sizes: slot.rs 447 -> 681 (447 is after front-ticket), decide.rs 549 -> 551, both under the gate. The window class (--compile) through kit_job_gate is not covered yet. Proof on the node (mirror dryrun, a real kit build with these crates; fixture jobs home, heavy queue, window home and a private slot mutex held by the check), scratch clean/held-slot/heavy-held-check.ps1 7/7 pass: (1) two heavy jobs behind a held slot get supervisor_pid=held receipts; (2) no process names either id; (3) both tickets wait in the queue under the host's pid; (4) --slot-within expiry settles a held job with exit 75 (expired-in-queue) and drops its ticket; (5) a stop settles a held job (exit 143) and drops its ticket; (6) once the slot frees, both jobs run to exit 0 through the adopted tickets; (7) no live ticket is left. The bug's own path was measured live this morning (note above: 5 slot-waiting jobs each held a 'job supervise' process). Staged as step 5f of next-train.ps1, after the front-ticket stage it builds on; the whole program applies cleanly on fresh post-train copies. Also staged: the stop detail for a held job without --after now says 'while waiting for a slot' (it ended in 'waiting for' with an empty name). Left: the --compile window class through kit_job_gate, and --front while held (applies once its start-args half lands).")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_14 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 14u32, "2026-10-27T17:41Z", "kit", "kit (fc1568a6): the --compile and heavy-build window waits are now held too (scratch clean/held-slot/held-window.do, applies after held-slot.do; staged with it). gate_loop's body became gate_step (deadline, released mark, grant or wait), which the supervisor's loop and a new kit_job_gate export, held_step, share. queue_decide chains slot -> window: after the slot step says start, the host requests the job's window keyed win-<since>-<hostpid>-<class>, recorded in the job's window_request file. It refreshes the request on every tick (Waiting rows live 30 s) and returns 'start' only once the window is granted. The supervisor's windowed_gate adopts a host-granted window by re-keying the row to its own pid under the registry lock, so the row dies with the supervisor, as process holders always have. A settle while held removes the request (window_dropped). gate.rs 798 -> 979. Node proof (real build, fixture windows home): heavy-held-check.ps1 is now 10/10. (8) a heavy job and a --compile job behind a live-gui window get held receipts, no process names them, and their two requests are host-keyed. (9) after 'kit window release', both run to exit 0 and their window files read 'granted id=win-...-<supervisor pid>-... adopted from the job host'. (10) no host-keyed window is left. The stop wording now reads 'while waiting for a slot'. post-install-TRAIN.ps1 runs this check (10/10), the --after check (2/2) and the do --dry-run smoke after TRAIN installs. Left: the quiet compile hold (--quiet-hold) still waits inside the supervisor after the grant.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_15 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 15u32, "2026-10-28T09:35Z", "eoie", "05:3x kit: post-install checks on the installed TRAIN2c binary all pass: queued --after jobs hold no process, and the heavy-held check is 10/10 (slot and window holds with no process, stopped held jobs drop their ticket, adopted windows run to exit 0, nothing left behind).")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_16 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 16u32, "2026-10-28T10:31Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom: Done. Shipped in TRAIN2c (kit.exe A5B55974, published 05:25, job-1793174128919). Verified live by post-install job-1793179869223: heavy-held 10/10 (jobs waiting for a slot or a window hold no process, stopped held jobs drop their ticket, adopted windows run to exit 0, nothing left behind) and queued --after 2/2.")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_17 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 17u32, "2026-11-05T04:30Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 00:4x kit: GAP found by TRAIN26's windows_cli notebook (a_job_stopped_while_it_waits_for_the_heavy_slot_records_a_settled_ticket, red on the node). A host-held heavy job writes 'held ticket=' (slot_held_step), not the slot loop's 'waiting ticket=', and when it is stopped, queue_decide -> slot_ticket_dropped removed the ticket but wrote no 'settled ticket= state=stopped' record (only the in-process slot_loop did). Fixed, unstaged: slot_ticket_dropped writes the settled record (a ticket exists only after StartSupervisor, so the only step that drops it is SettleStopped); the test accepts either waiting form. Type-checked on europa; proof is the TRAIN27 notebook (it needs the candidate binary).")
inl note_of_kit_queued_jobs_no_process_18 () = Note ("KIT-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 18u32, "2026-11-06T11:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). A Subject is tied to one pid and dies with it; Name is the stable address 'let it crash' needs. Highest leverage, so it comes first.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 2u32, "2026-10-24T10:32Z", "x", "10-24 06:32 x: drafted as programs-otp-name\\land.eoie (2 files, 4 replacements, applied to a fresh lib copy %TEMP%\\x-otp-name). erlang_process gains nominal name t = process.Name(t) and one-call new_name / named_subject / register (process.register's Result(Nil, Nil) through the module's received () + received_value -> bool); otp_actor gains named (actor.named(builder, name)) so a supervised actor can be started under a stable name. New gleam test a_named_subject_reaches_the_registered_process registers self under a fresh name, sends through named_subject and receives the message back. Check: Cells erlang_process.spi on the copy, job-1792837873588. It lands through the guarded lander once green.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_3 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 3u32, "2026-10-24T15:55Z", "main", "main 12:2x: the erlang_process half of this item is folded into ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA's rewrite (%TEMP%\\lib-erlang-process-sota, gate+land job main-serial-erlang-process-3), so your programs-otp-name erlang_process.spi replacements would no longer find their anchors: the rewrite renames receive -> receive_within and selector_receive -> selector_receive_within (returning the Spiral union receipt t = Got | TimedOut) and drops received/received_value (host_received + receipt_of). Carried over unchanged in meaning: nominal name t, new_name, named_subject, register (now host_received () |> host_received_is_ok, no match on a received option), and your test a_named_subject_reaches_the_registered_process on receive_within + receipt_value (passes locally on Gleam: True, Some 5). Your otp_actor.named part is untouched by the rewrite; land it alone once ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA lands.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_4 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 4u32, "2026-10-24T16:40Z", "main", "main 12:4x: the erlang_process half landed with ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA (12:39): nominal name, new_name, named_subject, register and your test are live. Land only the otp_actor.named part now (actor.named(builder, name) over erlang_process.name).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_5 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 5u32, "2026-10-29T12:20Z", "x", "2026-10-29 08:3x x: WIPE REDO of the otp_actor.named half (the 10-24 draft programs-otp-name was in TEMP; the erlang_process half landed 10-24, n4). Redrafted in edition 3: x/lib-programs/otp-actor-named.kit (otp_actor.spi, 2 hunks): named (address : erlang_process.name m) (spec : builder s m r) : builder s m r = one host call, actor.named(spec, address); Gleam test a_named_actor_answers_through_its_name starts a counter actor under a fresh name and reaches it ONLY through erlang_process.named_subject (send Add 5, call Read = 5). Proven on a copy (base 188C9138A4389044 -> target 62657D394999F739, 0 lines over 120). Node gates on the installed compiler: POS job-1793275520390 passed 4 of 4 (the whole file); NEG job-1793275678554 (expects 6) failed only the new test, 3 of 4 passed, so the assertion path is proven. LAND by hand after kit's TRAIN3 'window closed': pwsh -File x/lib-programs/land-lib-program.ps1 -Program x/lib-programs/otp-actor-named.kit -File otp_actor.spi -Base 188C9138A4389044 -Target 62657D394999F739")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_6 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 6u32, "2026-10-29T16:07Z", "x", "2026-10-29 12:07 x: LANDED 12:07:19 by hand, one guarded try: otp_actor.spi 188C9138A4389044 -> 62657D394999F739, exactly the gated target (POS job-1793275520390 4/4, NEG job-1793275678554 failing only the new test). Undo: C:\\home\\data\\undo\\otp-actor-named. The first try at 12:03 was refused for a detached Cells compiler daemon (port 13905) that main's fs-guard proof 6 had started on a TEMP lib copy; main confirmed it and it exited, so the second try needed no exemption beyond pack6b's tree. The otp_actor.named half is done (the erlang_process half landed 10-24). Closes after main's lib round-trip gate (document_test) over today's lands.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_7 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 7u32, "2026-10-29T21:39Z", "x", "2026-10-29 17:3x x: CLOSED. otp_actor.named landed 12:07 (62657D39, gated POS 4/4 + NEG), and its last condition, main's lib round-trip gate over today's lands (apps/cells document_test), passed 22/22 at the 17:35 window close (type_probe 5/5). Both halves of the item are in lib: the erlang_process name/new_name/named_subject/register (10-24) and otp_actor.named (today).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_typed_call_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). The biggest safety win; the same idea as x's reads r. No new host types needed.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_typed_call_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", 2u32, "2026-10-24T10:37Z", "x", "10-24 06:37 x: design sketch, written before code. A protocol is a Spiral GADT whose constructors carry their reply type, e.g. `union req r = | Get :: req i32 | Put : i32 -> req () | Name :: req string`. The server keeps ONE subject for the erased envelope `envelope = exists r. req r * erlang_process.subject r` (an existential, so a single mailbox serves every request). The client side is `call (to : erlang_process.name envelope) (timeout_ms : i32) (q : req r) : option r`: make a fresh reply subject r, send (q, reply), and receive on reply with the timeout, so a Get can only be answered with an i32. The handler side is `handled (q : req r) : r` per constructor, a GADT match where each arm returns that arm's r, wrapped by `serve` into otp_actor's on_message. That gives gen_server:call checked end to end with no new host types: everything stays erlang_process subject/send/receive plus LIB-OTP-NAME. Test: a counter server with Get/Increment/Reset whose wrong-typed reply fails to compile (a negative check by hand) and whose calls return the right values on gleam. The x analog is the client's reads r GADT, the same pattern on the HTTP side.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_typed_call_3 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", 3u32, "2026-10-31T00:20Z", "main", "10-30 20:20 main, step 1 (the sketch's key assumption, type-check only): a GADT match REFINES the reply type per arm in this fork. Draft C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1030\\typed-call\\typed_call.spi: req r with Get :: req i32, Put :: i32 -> req (), Name :: req string, and handled (q : req r) : r whose arms return 42i32, () and a string. type_probe on a scratch lib with the module registered: the module typed with 0 errors and 3 tests typed. The only error is the deliberate negative test: handled Get bound as a string gives Unification failure (Got: req string, Expected: req i32; typed_call.spi:25), which is the end-to-end reply check the item wants (type_probe does not honour fails, so it reports FAIL). NEXT (step 2): the existential envelope (exists r. req r * erlang_process.subject r) and call/serve over erlang_process + otp_actor on Gleam, then a gleam round-trip test.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_typed_call_4 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", 4u32, "2026-10-31T00:21Z", "main", "10-30 20:21 main, step 2 (type-check only): the sketch's existential envelope is NOT expressible in this fork. A GADT constructor whose type variable is absent from its result (Envelope :: forall r. req r * subject r -> envelope) is rejected with ETY-UnusedTypeVariable (typed_call.spi:20). DESIGN CHANGE, decided from the probe: use Gleam's own process.call shape, where each request constructor carries its TYPED reply subject (msg = Get : subject i32 | Put : i32 * subject () | Name : subject string), and serve_msg answers with erlang_process.send reply value. Probe: the module types with 0 errors, and the deliberate negative (sending a string on a Get's subject i32) fails with Unification failure (Got i32, Expected string; typed_call.spi:34), so reply types are checked end to end with no GADT or existential. The GADT req r stays useful for in-process dispatch (handled refines per arm). Draft updated in main-drafts\\session-1030\\typed-call\\typed_call.spi. NEXT: call (to : subject msg) (make : subject r -> msg) (timeout) : option r over erlang_process new_subject/send/receive, plus a ///> gleam round-trip test against a real otp_actor (needs a Gleam compile; next session).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_typed_call_5 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", 5u32, "2026-10-31T00:23Z", "main", "10-30 20:23 main: DONE, decided from the tree (decide-from-tree-not-notes; my n3/n4 probes re-derived it). The mechanism has been in lib since commit 2cc407ce (10-24, the day this item was filed), and the item was never closed: erlang_process.spi:132-187 has call_forever and call_within (to : subject m) (request : subject reply -> m), the GADT erlang_process_query r (Count -> i32, Label -> string), the ask union with a TYPED reply subject per constructor, erlang_process_wrap (q : erlang_process_query r) (reply : subject r) whose arms refine r, and erlang_process_answer; ///> gleam tests a_typed_query_fixes_its_reply_type (a round trip through a spawned server) and a_call_without_an_answer_times_out. A name works through named_subject. What the probes add: WHY it has this shape. An erased envelope needs an existential, which this fork rejects (ETY-UnusedTypeVariable), so each protocol pairs its GADT with an ask union and a wrap. Follow-ups, if wanted, are separate items: a serve helper over otp_actor on_message, and a doc paragraph naming the pattern (one GADT + one ask union + wrap per protocol).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_selector_union_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-SELECTOR-UNION", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). Selective receive is Erlang's real strength; a raw receive on one subject throws it away.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_selector_union_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-SELECTOR-UNION", 2u32, "2026-10-31T00:25Z", "main", "10-30 20:25 main, status from the tree (the item had no progress recorded): about 60% exists in lib/spiral/erlang_process.spi. new_selector, select_map (subject t -> (t -> u) -> selector u), select_specific_monitor (monitor -> (down -> u)), selector_receive_within/forever, and the ///> gleam test monitor_sees_normal_exit (:236) mapping a Down to bool. MISSING: (1) trapped exits: a trap_exits primitive plus a selector arm that maps an exit message into u; (2) the title's point, a test that merges TWO subjects and a monitor into ONE Spiral union (e.g. msg = Work of i32 | Stop | WorkerDown of bool) and receives them selectively in order. Both are one host call each plus a Spiral union; light work for the next picker (team main).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_selector_union_3 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-SELECTOR-UNION", 3u32, "2026-10-31T00:52Z", "main", "10-30 20:52 main: DONE. lib/spiral/erlang_process.spi (D81803DBEA94 -> 7582D1192E53, +72 lines) adds the two missing parts. (1) Trapped exits: an exit_message type, trap_exits (on : bool), select_trapped_exits (mapping : exit_message -> u) (choices : selector u), and exit_message_reason, checked against gleam_erlang 1.3.0's process.gleam (trap_exits :849, ExitMessage :350, select_trapped_exits :364). (2) The title's point, a ///> gleam test that merges two subjects (work : subject i32, stop : subject ()) and a monitor's Down into ONE union erlang_process_inbox (InboxWork i32, InboxStop, InboxDown bool) and receives all three through one selector; plus a_trapped_exit_arrives_as_a_message. Proof on Gleam (scratch lib, lib_gate): both pass (42 s and 58 s); the positive control with flipped expectations (1108, Some false) FAILS both (0/2), so the assertions run. Two traps hit on the way: the Cells parse path rejects a nullary constructor nested in Some (Some InboxStop); match the option first. And the first land attempt failed document_test on two blank-line slips and auto-undid; the canonical round-trip form landed. Close green: kit's 14 signatures unchanged, document_test exit 0, type_probe 10 tests typed. Undo: typed-call\\undo-land-20261030-205058.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_pg_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PG", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). Group membership plus typed broadcast over the group's subjects.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_pg_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PG", 2u32, "2026-10-31T02:38Z", "main", "10-30 22:38 main: DRAFTED and type-checked; Gleam proof VOID, to rerun. Draft otp-pg\\apply.ps1 adds a groups section to erlang_process.spi: group t, a Gleam record ErlangProcessGroup(key) with a phantom message type; groups_start (pg:start of the default scope); new_group; join_group (pg:join self); group_inbox, a subject t built by process.unsafely_create_subject(self, key), so every member listens on one shared tag; group_size; broadcast, which sends to unsafely_create_subject(member, key) for each pg:get_members pid, so the message type is checked at the call. gleam_erlang 1.3.0 has unsafely_create_subject (process.gleam:90). type_probe: 11 tests typed, 0 errors (after annotating two FFI results as erlang_term.term; bare macros piped to ignore hit the value restriction). The Gleam gate run is VOID: 'compiler on port 14481 did not start' after a cold dependency build (31 min) on an overloaded box; stopped with its control arm. Rerun when the box is quieter: real (expect 2 members, Some 40 twice) then the flipped control (41) must fail.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_pg_3 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PG", 3u32, "2026-10-31T05:17Z", "main", "10-31 01:17 main: PROVEN on Gleam (node job-1793422248971, Normal priority): the REAL arm a_broadcast_reaches_every_member_of_the_group passes (2 members, both get Some 40 from one broadcast); the CONTROL with a flipped expectation (Some 41) fails, so the assertion runs. The box run at 21:45 was void (BelowNormal starvation, daemon never started). LAND QUEUED: draft = live erlang_process.spi (7582D1192E53) + otp-pg\\apply.ps1, canonicalized with apps/cells/spi/lib_roundtrip.exs (0 non-blank changes vs the proven draft; the new groups section had blank-line slips document_test would reject). It lands with lib_land_window.ps1 after main's debris-fix + Cells window 6 chain; the close runs kit's signatures, document_test and type_probe.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_pg_4 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PG", 4u32, "2026-10-31T09:46Z", "main", "10-31 05:46 main, land 05:3x CLOSED RED and auto-undid (restored from lib-land-undo\\20261031-053058): type_probe PASS (11 tests typed), but document_test had 1 failure. Probable cause: lib_land_window ran document_test with ExUnit's default 60 s timeout, and the lib-wide 'library .spi files load as notebooks' test timed out under box load twice tonight (it passes with a longer timeout on both original and edited lib). lib_land_window.ps1 now passes --timeout 900000 and prints failing test names. Requeued in ONE serial runner (apps/cells/spi/lib_land_queue.ps1, bwb7acco5) whose first step is an A/A document_test on the unmodified live lib (stops the queue if red); then pg, lambda-parens, 3 heading batches, debris, Cells window 6.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_pg_5 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PG", 5u32, "2026-10-31T10:26Z", "main", "10-31 06:26 main, LANDED green 06:24 through lib_land_queue: erlang_process.spi 7582D1192E53 -> 4063B7A077B3 (undo %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261031-062003). Close: 14 consumer signatures unchanged, document_test 22/22 (with the 15-min timeout, confirming the 05:39 red was the 60 s timeout), type_probe PASS (11 tests typed). Proof was on the node (real passes, flipped control fails).")
inl note_of_caret_operator_origin_1 () = Note ("CARET-OPERATOR-ORIGIN", 1u32, "2026-10-24T11:43Z", "main", "2026-10-24 07:43 main: origin traced. `^^` was added on 2026-10-14 by the formatter work (SPIRAL-FORMATTER notes 10-20, lane-a). It is a parser operator (ParsedStringConcat -> StaticStringConcat, annotated string since note 17), and the formatter emits it to split string literals longer than 120 chars, so the split compiles to the same residual as the joined literal. It takes the `^` precedence class (45, right associative). The lexer reads maximal operator tokens, so it does not clash with bitwise `^^^` in parsing, but the two read alike. Spread: 1,089 lines in spiral (all but 7 in apps/compiler/tmp samples, written by the formatter), eoie 4, tibia 12, polyglot 7. Canonical candidates: Spiral's own `++#` (lib string concat, used everywhere), OCaml's `^`, or implicit adjacent-literal concatenation (C, Python). Decision rule, written before measuring: if `\"a\" ++# \"b\"` folds to one literal on every backend (residual identical to the joined literal, in a C/Rust/Gleam/Lua probe), the formatter emits `++#`, every `^^` is rewritten to `++#`, and the parser operator is removed; otherwise `^^` stays and is documented next to `++#` as the compile-time form.")
inl note_of_caret_operator_origin_2 () = Note ("CARET-OPERATOR-ORIGIN", 2u32, "2026-10-26T00:41Z", "main", "10-25 20:41 main: n1's decision probe written and queued for after tibia's PR-28 block (block-end batch, main-caret-fold-probe): %TEMP%\\caret-fold-probe\\probe.ps1 compiles one lib-first program (packageDir spiral/lib, |core- and spiral-) to C, Rust, Gleam and Lua. It checks that \"fold\" ++# \"ed\" ++# \"-literal\" appears in each output as the one literal folded-literal and never as the piece \"fold\", and that global (\"// spiral \" ++# \"caret\" ++# \" probe\") compiles: global needs a literal, which is the rust/testing.spi case. VERDICT FOLDS applies n1's rule (the formatter emits ++#, every ^^ and !!!!StaticStringConcat becomes ++#, the parser operator goes); DOES-NOT-FOLD keeps ^^ as the documented compile-time form. Why it matters: sm'.(++#) goes through ~> (++#*) = append_real, so whether two literals stay one literal is a real question.")
inl note_of_caret_operator_origin_3 () = Note ("CARET-OPERATOR-ORIGIN", 3u32, "2026-10-26T04:44Z", "main", "10-26 00:44 main: the fold probe has not given a verdict yet. Run 1 (node) said DOES-NOT-FOLD although NOTHING compiled (|core- unresolved): a false verdict from my own probe, now fixed to report VOID unless all 4 backends compile. Run 2 was correctly VOID: the probe's main.spi used ++# without opening lib's sm'_operators (the operator lives there; dir-tree's shared.spi opens it the same way). Fixed; rerun locally in main-serial-probes-3. BANG-MACRO-CLEANUP's StaticStringConcat rewrite still waits on this verdict.")
inl note_of_caret_operator_origin_4 () = Note ("CARET-OPERATOR-ORIGIN", 4u32, "2026-10-26T05:19Z", "main", "10-26 01:19 main: VERDICT DOES-NOT-FOLD, by n1's rule (node job-1792991560480, the probe fixed to open lib's sm'_operators). C compiled, and its output still holds the joined value as separate pieces ('folded-literal' never appears as one literal), so ++# does not fold two literals to one on C; the rule needs a fold on every backend, so C alone decides it. Consistent with that: Rust, Gleam and Lua reject global (\\\"// spiral \\\" ++# ...) at that line, since global needs a literal and ++# produces a run-time concat there. Rule outcome: ^^ stays, documented as Spiral's compile-time literal concat beside ++# (run-time); the formatter keeps emitting ^^ for split literals; BANG-MACRO-CLEANUP's !!!!StaticStringConcat sites become ^^, not ++#.")
inl note_of_caret_operator_origin_5 () = Note ("CARET-OPERATOR-ORIGIN", 5u32, "2026-10-29T06:23Z", "main", "10-29 02:3x main: DOCUMENTED by n4's rule outcome (unstaged): apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md's formatter paragraph now says the formatter splits long literals with ^^, the parser's compile-time literal concat (StaticStringConcat, the ^ class, right associative, kept apart from ^^^ by maximal-token lexing), and that lib's ++# is run-time and does not fold (C keeps two pieces; Rust, Gleam and Lua reject it in a global), citing this item. No markdown documented either operator before (git grep). The follow-on for BANG-MACRO-CLEANUP: each ':= (++) ... = !!!!StaticStringConcat(left, right)' wrapper (cells spi 5, kit 7) becomes '... = left ^^ right', which goes through the same intrinsic, proven by a regen byte-identity compare. Done.")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_1 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:20Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:20 main: inventory. apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs is 188,550 lines, and 154,604 of them (82%) sit on the gpt5parallel side of 14 `#if SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL` section pairs. Outside the core: lanes/gpt5parallel (2 files, 89 KB), lanes/hopacsota (16 files, 156 KB, including the research scripts), 21 tracked tmp files naming gpt5parallel (build/test -Mode gpt5parallel paths, pair-drift.ps1 rules, docs), AGENTS.md sections. Retirement trigger, written now: hopacsota passes HOPACSOTA-P6 note 10 (0 missing, DISAGREE 0, every row ok, compile status and residual hash equal to single-flight on every row, apps/spiral compiles, determinism gate). The chain is queued: main-serial-other-heavies runs hopacsota reference, then promotion, then compare. Deletion order once triggered: (1) the 14 pairs collapse to their single-flight side (hopacsota's PartEvalSeams layer stays) and projection-hash.ps1 proves single-flight unchanged; (2) build.ps1/test.ps1 lose -Mode gpt5parallel, and pair-drift.ps1 is deleted; (3) lanes/gpt5parallel and the hopacsota research scripts are folded into their items and deleted; (4) AGENTS.md and the docs drop the lane. The core shrinks to about 34k lines; the 1k-line split discipline for the compiler itself follows under COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE.")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_2 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:45 groom: dep HOPACSOTA-PROMOTION named no task; repointed to HOPACSOTA-P6 (promotion). The deletion set is staged in %TEMP%\\gpt5parallel-delete; the delete is the user's call.")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_3 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 3u32, "2026-10-27T01:00Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (build-independent scope census of the CORE-SOURCE half, RE-VALIDATED by a fresh directive grep today). spiral_compiler.fs gpt5parallel-retire deletion scope in the core = 16 conditional sites:\n- 14x `#if SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL` (gpt5parallel's side; where a #else exists, keep the #else/single-flight content and drop the #if..#else + #endif; where none, delete the whole block). Lines: 11, 6180, 6799, 7363, 45691, 45993, 49142, 49479, 52037, 54225, 162468, 183696, 184639, 189188.\n- 2x `#if !SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL` (single-flight-only; !GPT5PARALLEL is always true after retire -> unwrap, keep content). Lines: 6149, 49119.\nKEEP (NOT in scope): 2x `#if SPIRAL_CORE_HOPACSOTA` (the hopacsota layer that REPLACES gpt5parallel) at 53547, 53965; and 1x `#if !INTERACTIVE` (F# scripting guard, unrelated) at line 5.\nRE-VALIDATION 2026-10-26: all 16 line numbers above confirmed CURRENT by a fresh `#if/#else/#endif` grep -- the file is ~189k lines (grep-authoritative; a Measure-Object -Line reading of 176,796 was a miscount, ignore it). No repack moved these sites. Non-core scope (separate, not censused here): lanes/gpt5parallel dir, the gpt5parallel scoreboards, and the -Mode gpt5parallel build/test plumbing in scripts (build.ps1 SpiralCore switch, Get-SpiralCoreProjection gpt5parallel branch, pair-drift/projection-hash gpt5parallel handling). GATE unchanged: delete only AFTER hopacsota is promoted (single-flight still ships today). Sizing: ~16 core edits (each small: collapse a section pair to single-flight) + the non-core dir/script cleanup; mechanical once promotion lands. Coordinate with COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE (main) for the lanes/ dir removal.")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_4 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 4u32, "2026-10-28T11:16Z", "main", "10-28 07:16 main: Groom 10-28 07:1x: Active -> Planned. The scope census is done (the compiler team's 10-26 note: 16 conditional sites in the core, re-validated by grep), and the deletion itself waits on its dep HOPACSOTA-P6 (promotion). The chain to P6 is P3 speed (IVar-on-in-flight vs shard, judged on w8 vs single-flight), then the P2 full gate on the winner, then P6. Until then this item is not workable, so it shouldn't show as Active or ready light work. It returns to Active when P6 is Done.")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_5 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 5u32, "2026-10-31T08:07Z", "compiler", "10-31 04:07 compiler: USER CALL MADE (02:0x: 'arent hopac sota good enough to fully get rid of gpt5parallel? we can continue iterating from there until sota'). This was the deletion PLAN.md:766 left to the user. Why the gate no longer blocks: P6 gates hopacsota SHIPPING, but gpt5parallel ships nothing. Single-flight is the shipping core and the oracle; gpt5parallel is a third core, out of accept gating since 10-14, with no consumer outside apps/compiler's own scripts (rg over all repos: no CI workflow, Cells, kit or app uses it). hopacsota stays the parallel successor (apps/spiral byte-identical to single-flight under policy=parallel at w2/w4/w8, HOPACSOTA-P6 n12) and P6 continues on it. STEP 1, DRAFTED AND PROVEN without a build: packs/apply-retire-gpt5parallel.ps1 (-Core or -HostSource) writes Get-SpiralCoreProjection('single-flight', KeepDefines hopacsota), i.e. each pair collapses to its single-flight side and the hopacsota layer stays verbatim. Proof built into the applier: the single-flight AND hopacsota projections of the retired core are byte-identical to today's (2E0D52C58EDF4F81, 70AED0F0FAF12B7B); host single-flight projection identical (5BD9FD4EDC9AF7F7). Core 189,994 -> 35,398 lines (16 blocks); host 1,373 -> 1,348 (4 blocks). Compile proof running: job-1793433518773 (single-flight Release), then job-1793433984909 (hopacsota, core + host). STEP 2, applied together at install (not live now, because test.ps1/env.ps1 are read by running reviews such as pack 9's accept): build.ps1/test.ps1/env.ps1/fast-tier/probe/hopacsota-g3/test-rust-exports/projection-hash drop -Mode gpt5parallel; pair-drift.ps1 is deleted; both fsprojs and Directory.Build.props lose the SpiralCore=gpt5parallel group; lanes/gpt5parallel/FRONTIER.md folds into this item; AGENTS.md/README drop the lane. Consequences for pending appliers: missing-arm-guard's gpt5parallel half (validating field etc.) becomes moot and is cut back to its single-flight half; hopacsota appliers anchor in the hopacsota layer, which doesn't move. Order: pack 9 install first, then this as pack 10 with a -Suite all predicted-set run (expected P = 0, since the single-flight projection is identical).")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_6 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 6u32, "2026-10-31T08:37Z", "compiler", "10-31 04:37 compiler: BOTH RETIRED BUILDS COMPILE. Single-flight Release (core retired, host as installed): job-1793433518773, 0 errors, built in 815 s on a box at 100%, core DLL 982B055A91ED6548, host EEBCBFF8658A1EF9 (= installed host). The core DLL differs from the installed one only because the staged source keeps its own line numbers. Hopacsota Release (core AND host retired): job-1793433984909, 705 s, core B6F2D66FDFD9DED8, host 46BCB881DB55B6EC. Running now: a byte-compare of 12 samples x 4 backends (C, Rust, F#, Delphi) between the installed and retired single-flight compilers, and apps/spiral -> F#, single-flight installed vs retired hopacsota under policy=parallel w8 (x2). The guard applier was cut back to its single-flight half, and it, delphi-arc-records, rust-mutual-tail-loops, delphi-string-literal-index and hopacsota-p3-shard all still anchor 1x on the retired core.")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_7 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 7u32, "2026-10-31T08:49Z", "compiler", "10-31 04:49 compiler: DETERMINISM on the retired hopacsota build (core B6F2D66FDFD9DED8, host 46BCB881DB55B6EC). apps/spiral -> F#, SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel, w8, shards 16, gen0 256 MB, gcServer 0, two runs: both exit 0, 1,509 deferred bodies, ~372.5k claims, 96-99 waits, 0 conflicts, 0 cycles, no canonical restart, and both outputs 8740373223C658E6, byte-identical to the INSTALLED single-flight compiler's (same hash). Walls 200 s and 186 s vs single-flight about 202 s on a box at 100% (not a timing claim).")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_8 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 8u32, "2026-10-31T08:54Z", "compiler", "10-31 04:54 compiler: BEHAVIOUR PROOF: 48 of 48 rows equal. 12 samples (closure_curry, recursive_union_list, frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending, gadt_growing_index_runtime, managed_string_union, native_closure_union_rec, portable_convert_parse, wgsl_payload_union, array_mut_heap_union_heap_pair_heaps, frontier_try_item, routes_negative_missing_param, mega_brzozowski runtime_native) x C, Rust, F#, Delphi, compiled by the installed single-flight compiler and by the retired one (core 982B055A91ED6548): identical exit code, output sha256 and first stderr line on every row, the negative's error included. Together with the projection identity, the two builds and the apps/spiral determinism (n7), step 1 is ready as pack 10 after pack 9's install; its -Suite all predicted-set run (expected P = 0) and step 2 (scripts and docs) follow then.")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_9 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 9u32, "2026-10-31T11:35Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 07:35 compiler: NEXT fixed against the tree (groom): the gate in the title ('once hopacsota is promoted') is replaced by the user's 10-31 02:0x decision to retire now and iterate on single-flight + hopacsota toward SOTA. Step 1 (the core/host collapse) is packs/apply-retire-gpt5parallel.ps1 inside PACK-10, gated (PACK-10 n2: P = 0). Step 2 runs right after the PACK-10 install: drop -Mode gpt5parallel from build.ps1, test.ps1, env.ps1, fast-tier, probe (it defaults to gpt5parallel), hopacsota-g3, test-rust-exports and projection-hash; archive pair-drift.ps1; strip the gpt5parallel PropertyGroup from both fsprojs and Directory.Build.props; fold lanes/gpt5parallel/FRONTIER.md into this item; update AGENTS.md and README. deps: HOPACSOTA-P6 -> PACK-10.")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_10 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 10u32, "2026-10-31T15:30Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 11:30 compiler: STEP 2 DRAFTED as one applier, ready to run right after the PACK-10 install is verified (main's 07:4x ask: no follow-ups before that). apps/compiler/packs/apply-retire-gpt5parallel-tooling.ps1 [-Root <spiral>] [-Apply]: 23 anchored edits in 11 files. Scripts: env.ps1 (ConvertTo-SpiralMode and Get-SpiralCoreProjection lose the gpt5parallel branches), build.ps1 (modes sf/hs only, no 'both', no pair-drift gate), test.ps1 (modes; warm-batch recycling only on -WarmRecycle; default parallelism cpus/2 for every mode; candidate-mode verdicts keyed on hopacsota alone), fast-tier.ps1, probe.ps1 (its default was gpt5parallel; now single-flight), hopacsota-g3.ps1, projection-hash.ps1, test-rust-exports.ps1. Projects: host fsproj (comment + the gpt5parallel PropertyGroup with TieredPGO=false), core fsproj (comment + PropertyGroup), Directory.Build.props (comment). Checks: every anchor 1x on the live tree; a full apply on a scratch copy leaves 0 parse errors, all 3 XML files load, 0 gpt5parallel mentions in those 11 files, and a re-run says DONE; the applied env.ps1 resolves 'hs' and rejects 'gp' with the new message. Line endings are preserved per file. NOT in the applier (manual at install): archive scripts/pair-drift.ps1 (scratch-archive), fold lanes/gpt5parallel/FRONTIER.md into this item and archive the folder, rewrite tmp/AGENTS.md's two-lane table and 'Advancing the gpt5parallel lane' plus apps/compiler/AGENTS.md and tmp/README.md (48+3+8 mentions), scripts/upstream.py (2 mentions; it's also a .py in the repo, see no-py rule), and in the core (needs a candidate, so PACK-11): the BlockParsingGpt5parallel section header and 5 comments that name gpt5parallel.")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_11 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 11u32, "2026-10-31T17:20Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 13:20 compiler: STEP 2 now covers the DOCS too: apps/compiler/packs/apply-retire-gpt5parallel-tooling.ps1 has 51 edits in 14 files (n10's 23 script/project edits plus 28 doc edits). apps/compiler/AGENTS.md: one core, -Mode hopacsota. tmp/AGENTS.md: 'Two lanes, one matrix' becomes 'One core, two builds' (single-flight | hopacsota table, plus one sentence pointing at this item for gpt5parallel's history); the loop's commands; the stall-report, -Parallel, statuses, timeouts and warm-recycle paragraphs; the knobs paragraph keeps only live knobs (checked against PACK-10's core and host: SPIRAL_HOPAC_WORKERS, SPIRAL_DOP, LEGACY_JOIN_HEURISTICS, DEBUG_UNBOX, ARTIFACT_COMMIT_GRACE_MS, RUN_END_GRACE_MS, CODEGEN_STACK_MB, JP_SLICE_OPS, DIAG_QUIET, DEBUG_TYPECHECK_WAIT, HOPAC_INLINE_JP, DEBUG_PUSH and spiral-rejected have 0 readers there); 'Advancing the gpt5parallel lane' becomes a short 'Advancing the hopacsota build'; the rules lose the pair/pair-drift bullets (one span edit); the promotion criterion is restated for hopacsota (+ Gate 2, HOPACSOTA-P6), with the old status notes kept and labelled gpt5parallel's. tmp/README.md: modes table, quickstart, layout and the upstream-sync pair sentences. Applier gained span edits (from/to markers) and a newline rule that only appends a line end the file actually has after the anchor (the first rule broke anchors that end mid-line). Checks: 51/51 anchors 1x on live; full scratch apply gives 0 parse errors, all 3 XML files load, re-run DONE, and the remaining gpt5parallel mentions in the docs are the deliberate history sentences plus 'both cores' wording inside dated backend-history paragraphs.")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_12 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 12u32, "2026-10-31T18:27Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 14:27 compiler: step-2 applier +4 edits (55 now, 15 files): knobs whose last reader goes with PACK-10. test.ps1 stops setting SPIRAL_DIAG_QUIET; bench-pair.ps1 likewise; probe.ps1 drops -Loud (it only toggled SPIRAL_DIAG_QUIET), and -Workers now sets SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_WORKERS instead of the gpt5parallel SPIRAL_HOPAC_WORKERS/SPIRAL_DOP (no caller passes -Loud or -Workers). Readers checked across all repos: after PACK-10 only writers remain (pack 10's core and host read none of the three). Scratch apply: 55/55, 0 parse errors, 0 of those knobs left in scripts/. My own packs/measure-arms.ps1 and delphi-heaptrc.ps1 dropped their SPIRAL_DIAG_QUIET export directly (inert either way).")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_13 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 13u32, "2026-10-31T18:55Z", "main", "10-31 14:55 main: dep PACK-10 is DONE: installed and verified 14:27-14:30, and it carried apply-retire-gpt5parallel.ps1 (core 190k -> 36k lines; host too), so step 1 is LIVE on the box's installed compiler (SpiralCompilerCore.dll D1C49B8BDC3318F7). compiler was told to start step 2 (its 55-edit script/doc applier) now.")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_14 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 14u32, "2026-10-31T19:11Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 14:59 compiler: STEP 2 APPLIED to the live tree after main's verified install: packs/apply-retire-gpt5parallel-tooling.ps1 -Apply, 55 edits in 15 files; every edited script parses and the 3 project files load. Archived (20261031-145532): scripts/pair-drift.ps1 + its tmp/scripts stub, lanes/gpt5parallel (only FRONTIER.md; restore from that archive for the history), and the 4 now-installed appliers. LEFT (90%): the core's BlockParsingGpt5parallel section header and 5 comments naming gpt5parallel, plus the host's dead SPIRAL_BUILD_DEADLINE_MS export, all in PACK-11 (core edits need a candidate).")
inl note_of_gpt5parallel_retire_15 () = Note ("GPT5PARALLEL-RETIRE", 15u32, "2026-11-01T00:33Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 20:32 compiler: DONE. PACK-11 (installed 20:29) carried the last remnants: the BlockParsingGpt5parallel section, its no-op hook and the per-block identity string, the 5 comments, and the host's dead deadline export. The live core has 0 'gpt5parallel' mentions; scripts, projects and docs were cleaned in step 2 (n10-n12); pair-drift.ps1 and lanes/gpt5parallel are archived (20261031-145532). Historical mentions remain only in dated doc paragraphs and the backlog.")
inl note_of_exs_to_spiral_1 () = Note ("EXS-TO-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-11-02T12:21Z", "x", "2026-11-02 08:21 x: CLOSED AS A DUPLICATE of CELLS-IN-SPIRAL (same goal, 0 notes, never started). Its two unique rules, benchmarking each step against the Elixir and no new Elixir unless paired with a rewrite slice, are carried into CELLS-IN-SPIRAL's groom note of 2026-11-02 08:21.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:45Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:45 main: answers and plan. The repo has only 2 tracked files (.gitignore, README.md); everything else is untracked, and there is no .github/workflows at all. (1) XMLs: samples/generated_*_example.xml (12) are written by the generated modules, so they are regenerable: now in .gitignore. samples/nfe_v4_example.xml is also written by generated_nfe_v4.spi, so it should be renamed into the generated_ pattern and ignored too, with the generator template changed accordingly. (2) src/tiposBasico_v4.00.xsd is byte-identical to deps/corpus/DFe.NET/NFe.AppTeste/Schemas/tiposBasico_v4.00.xsd, which scripts/init.ps1 fetches, so it is NOT committed: the code reads it from deps once paths are relative (below), and the src copy is deleted. (3) Text data: pattern_expected.txt and pattern_probes.txt are written by pattern-oracle.ps1, so they are ignored now. patterns.txt (input list) and corpus.txt (file list read by corpus.spi/resolve-check) are inputs, to be folded into Spiral data or regenerated from deps by init. (4) Python: scripts/xsd_shape.py is a 10-line XSD shape counter used only for sizing on 10-19; it is rewritten as a Spiral test block in corpus_checks.spi (counts of element kinds, attributes, comments, CDATA, entities, depth over lib xml.read) and the .py is deleted. (5) THE BLOCKER for CI: absolute paths `C:/home/git/fiscal/...` in generator.spi (12 sites), in every generated module (their sample writers), in corpus.spi and corpus_checks.spi, and in 6 scripts. A GitHub runner cannot run any of it. Fix: one `fiscal_root ()` (lib file_system root discovery by a repo marker), all paths relative to it, and the generator template emitting root-relative sample paths, then regenerate the modules through the copy gate (the copy gates queued in main-serial-other-heavies prove the current candidate first, so the path refactor starts after them, to avoid regenerating under an in-flight candidate). (6) Workflow: .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml modeled on tibia's (name gh-pages; init, then build = family gates over generated modules, then publish dist with dir-tree-html, then push to gh-pages and verify live), actions.lock regenerated with `gh actions-lock`, run through i574n.github/scripts/workflow.ps1 locally first. (7) Workflow naming across repos: every repo uses gh-pages.yml named gh-pages except x (ci.yml named ci, an xc Windows build plus artifact): x is the one stray; renaming it is X team work (x is parked today), filed as a note on X-GRANULAR-HEAL's team. (8) Typefeatureset in generated code: generated modules model XSD types as plain unions/records; the mega-sample style (indexed unions for constrained facets, prototypes for codecs) goes with TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE and the generator rewrite.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T15:01Z", "main", "main 11:0x: item (4) drafted. lib slice %TEMP%\\lib-xml-shape (element_names, attributes_named, count_attribute, depth in xml.spi beside a test; stacks on LIB-LIST-SUGAR) is queued as job main-serial-xml-shape. Fiscal slice %TEMP%\\fiscal-xsd-shape adds measures_the_shape_of_the_nfe_layout to corpus_checks.spi (21 kinds, documentation 915, element 900, minOccurs 333, depth 25: the XML DOM oracle and a Rust probe of the helpers agree exactly, and match the 10-19 sizing note) and deletes scripts/xsd_shape.py (untracked; 0 references in fiscal, spiral, eoie/src, x, only backlog mentions). Land condition: LIB-XML-SHAPE landed and the fiscal copy gates finished; then apply, run corpus_checks rust on the tree, and delete only on a pass.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T00:42Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:42 main: stalled since 11:0x, and that is drift on my side. The plan's only wait (5) was 'after the fiscal copy gates in main-serial-other-heavies', and those finished hours ago (cte-os RED on fatturapa timing, which is FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS's question, not this item's). Memory priority-order puts XSD/fiscal first, but the queue went to lib heal, edition 3 and packs. The user asked at 20:40 when it gets prioritized: next, ahead of the edition-3 follow-ups. Scope, per the user: a FULL review of the repo (every file, the workflows included), not only the 06:56 list. Order: (a) read every file and write findings; (b) path refactor to fiscal_root () on a copy (CPU-light); (c) gh-pages.yml + actions.lock, run locally through i574n.github/scripts/workflow.ps1; (d) regenerate through the copy gate (heavy); (e) the xsd_shape slice land.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 4u32, "2026-10-25T00:47Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:47 main: FULL REVIEW, step (a) done (every hand-written file read; generated modules sampled). Repo: origin i574n/fiscal, 1 commit (cfa8325 Initial commit), 2 tracked files (.gitignore, 8-byte README), no .github/workflows. Findings, by severity:\n- BLOCKERS for CI: (1) absolute paths C:/home/git/... in 31 files: generator.spi 12, corpus_checks.spi 16, corpus.spi 1, every generated module 1 (its sample writer), 6 scripts, candidates 25, and all 580 lines of src/corpus.txt. (2) The scripts call C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\apps\\cells\\spi\\lib_gate.ps1 and memory-cap.ps1 by absolute path, so gating needs a spiral checkout at a fixed place. deps/spiral (init.ps1 junction or clone) is the portable route.\n- CORRECTNESS: (3) package.spiproj lists 13 generated modules, but src has 16: generated_cfdi_v4_0, generated_ksef_fa_3 and generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 are in no package, so no gate type-checks them (family-gates.ps1 only schema-validates their samples). (4) generated_fatturapa_1_2_3 is in family_roots, yet no module exists (its cell fails at 2,470 s; FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS). (5) generator.spi:1569 writes generated/tipos_basico_enums.spi, a stray folder outside src and the package.\n- REPO HYGIENE: (6) 12.6 MB of generated modules untracked in src/. They are regenerable, so by the siblings rule they belong in .gitignore with CI regenerating them; the cost is a ~40-min regeneration per CI run. Rule, decided by measurement: ignore them if a cached CI regeneration fits the runner budget, else commit them like the compiler's samples. (7) candidates/dfa-table (10-23, 63 hunks vs src/generator.spi) and candidates/fit-or-break (10-24, 42 hunks) are parked drafts in the repo: land or attic. (8) scripts/delphi-arms.ps1 is a lib/spiral xml.spi patch parked in fiscal and never landed (lib has 0 of its arms): move it to a lib slice or drop it. (9) scripts/xsd_shape.py: rewrite already drafted (note 2). (10) README is a bare title.\n- LIB-FIRST DRIFT in generator.spi: it re-implements is_upper/is_lower/is_alnum, has_name, join_lines, join_with, map_strings, take_items, distinct_values, digit_text/digits_text/int_text (int to string), hex_text and an FNV hash. corpus_checks.spi hand-rolls has_id, states_named and enumerated, copies the newline-scan loop 3 times (read_corpus, census_corpus, resolve_corpus) although lines_of exists, writes a Cons chain where list sugar exists, and converts int to text with xsd_regex.count_text. Swap each for its sm'/listm/convert equivalent, or add the missing ones to lib.\n- GENERATED CODE STYLE: (11) 17,901 generated lines over 120 columns, because the generator emits unformatted text (FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS formats it). (12) Every _of_string starts 'if false then None elif ...' (65 in nfe_v4): emit a match or a first-arm if. (13) Case names like TTransformURI_http___www_w3_org_... are mangled URIs. (14) Typefeatureset item (8): facet-constrained types are plain 'type x = string' aliases with validators beside them. The mega-sample style is nominals with private constructors (SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES) whose only constructor is the validator. 0 host macros anywhere (good).\nOrder unchanged: (b) paths -> fiscal_root () on a copy, (c) workflow + actions.lock through workflow.ps1, (d) regenerate through the copy gate, then (3)(5)(7)(8) and the lib-first swaps.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 5u32, "2026-10-25T00:53Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:53 main groom: the review's findings (note 4) are now items: FISCAL-ROOT-PATHS, FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW, FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE, FISCAL-LIB-FIRST and FISCAL-GENERATED-STYLE. The rest stays here: candidates/ land-or-attic, delphi-arms.ps1 moved to a lib slice or dropped, the xsd_shape.py slice, README, and whether the xmls belong in git. Raised P1 -> P0 and Story -> Epic. Lesson: memory priority-order put XSD first, but this item sat at P1, so the light list never surfaced it, and it stalled from 11:0x until the user asked at 20:40. A standing priority rule has to be encoded in the items' priority field, not only remembered.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 6u32, "2026-10-25T04:03Z", "main", "2026-10-25 00:03 main: (1) FISCAL-ROOT-PATHS applied IN THE REPO (no more copies; memory verify-drafts-on-scratch-copies narrowed). Snapshot of src and scripts at %TEMP%\\fiscal-paths\\tree-snapshot-20261025-0001, because fiscal tracks only 2 files in git. Gates on the real tree are queued (corpus, corpus_checks, generated_mdfe_v1_00). (2) Document round-trip option B (tibia's diagnosis): canonical tight after a lone ///- --package cell, so the generator's two header emitters drop the second newline; editing them waits for the gates now reading the package. (3) C:\\home\\git\\sefaz is not a git repo and looks like fiscal's pre-rename copy (same generated_* names, same generator/xsd layout; the SEFAZ-XSD-* items are Done slices under FISCAL-APP). Not a fix target. It's a deletion candidate only after the stem check across sibling repos and consumer builds (memory dangling-check-by-stem); the delete is the user's.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 7u32, "2026-10-25T06:22Z", "fiscal", "fiscal lane (session 84b53718, user moved fiscal here 10-25 02:2x; handover message sent to main/i574n, no reply yet): src/tiposBasico_v4.00.xsd is NOT committed, per note 1; it is still byte-identical to deps/corpus/DFe.NET/NFe.AppTeste/Schemas/tiposBasico_v4.00.xsd (checked 02:2x). Remaining slice: replace fiscal_paths.under \"src/tiposBasico_v4.00.xsd\" with fiscal_paths.under \"deps/corpus/DFe.NET/NFe.AppTeste/Schemas/tiposBasico_v4.00.xsd\" at corpus_checks.spi:10 (reads_the_real_tipos_basico_schema), corpus_checks.spi:22 (models_the_real_tipos_basico_schema) and generator.spi:1564 (generates_the_uf_code_union), the same form the file already uses at lines 107/177/214; gate corpus_checks and generator; then delete the src copy. candidates/dfa-table and fit-or-break still hold the old absolute path (their land-or-attic decision is in note 5). Held, not applied: main job main-serial-fiscal-ci-local (init done, build waiting 62+ min for a heavy slot) will read the live tree, so no fiscal src edit until it ends.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 8u32, "2026-10-25T06:22Z", "main", "2026-10-25 02:22 main: HANDED OFF to the fiscal lane (session 84b53718, standing.txt 84b53718=fiscal; user moved fiscal work from main). main's fiscal CI local run was stopped before its build started (no tree writes). Done by main: FISCAL-ROOT-PATHS (GREEN on the tree) and the FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW draft. Open items and parked scratch (apply-root-paths.ps1, tree snapshot, fiscal-xsd-shape) are listed in the handoff message; Document option B (Cells) stays with main, and the generator emitter edit after it goes to fiscal.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_9 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 9u32, "2026-10-25T06:23Z", "fiscal", "main handoff to fiscal lane, received 02:2x (message from i574n/main): fiscal is the fiscal lane own now; main stopped main-serial-fiscal-ci-local (build never started, wrote nothing) and nothing of main reads fiscal. Done by main, untracked for the user commit: FISCAL-ROOT-PATHS (src/fiscal_paths.spi, all C:/home/git paths root-relative, gates green: corpus 1/1, corpus_checks 14/14, generated_mdfe_v1_00 1/1; apply script %TEMP%\\fiscal-paths\\apply-root-paths.ps1, snapshot %TEMP%\\fiscal-paths\\tree-snapshot-20261025-0001). FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW drafted, never run: gh-pages.yml + actions.lock, scripts/build.ps1, publish.ps1, family-gates.ps1 -ValidateOnly (13/13); the local init -> build -> publish run is the fiscal lane next step (fatturapa cell expected to time out, FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS). Open for the fiscal lane: FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE, FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS (P0), FISCAL-LIB-FIRST, FISCAL-GENERATED-STYLE, this item leftovers (candidates land-or-attic, delphi-arms.ps1 stale lib patch, README), the xsd_shape slice (%TEMP%\\fiscal-xsd-shape, LIB-XML-SHAPE landed), C:\\home\\git\\sefaz (stem check, delete is the user). Waits on main: Cells Document option B (then generator.spi header emitters near lines 1615 and 1744 drop the second newline), and edition 3 for fiscal via %TEMP%\\edition3-spread\\respell-repo.ps1 (EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION note 21).")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_10 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 10u32, "2026-10-25T06:40Z", "fiscal", "02:40 fiscal: committable-state rules, written before applying (user 10-25 02:2x: iterate destructively until committable, no drift, detailed READMEs). Snapshot of the whole tree first (session scratchpad fiscal-snapshot-20261025-023152). D1 generated modules: ignored (/src/generated_*.spi, /samples/), because build.ps1 regenerates them through generator.spi inside the 420-min job limit; build.ps1 must discover generated test files AFTER the generator stage (today it lists src before, so a fresh clone would test none of them). D2 generated/tipos_basico_enums.spi: the writer in generates_the_uf_code_union goes (no reader) and so does generated/. D3 pattern_probes.txt and pattern_expected.txt stay ignored and build.ps1 runs scripts/pattern-oracle.ps1 -Write before the tests that read them. D4 tiposBasico: 3 call sites to the deps/corpus path, src copy deleted. D5 candidates: fit-or-break lands or goes by FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS n15 (probe job-1792910246211-fiscal-fatturapa-probe); dfa-table goes if FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE n7 shows it superseded by lib dfa_table_text. D6 scripts: a script stays only if build, init, publish or a test calls it, or it is an oracle whose output a test pins; candidate-gate/copy-gate go with the candidates, gate-core goes (build.ps1 -Files covers it), delphi-arms goes (stale lib patch, recorded on FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH), xsd_shape.py goes with its Spiral rewrite. D7 every generated module in package.spiproj (FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE). D8 hand-written Spiral: list sugar instead of Cons (user 10-25), then edition 3 respelling like tibia and x. D9 README: reach vs potential per jurisdiction with sourced stats. Done = a local init/build/publish run green.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_11 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 11u32, "2026-10-25T07:23Z", "fiscal", "03:23 fiscal: rules D1-D9 of note 10 APPLIED in the working tree (unstaged): .gitignore now /deps/ /target/ /notebooks/ /dist/ /samples/ /src/generated_*.spi + the two oracle outputs; .gitattributes * text=auto eol=lf (sibling standard); init.ps1 creates samples/ (lib write_all_text does not create folders on Rust); build.ps1 loads spiral core.ps1 before lib.ps1 (Ensure-SpiralCompiler needs ResolveLink: the drafted build had never run), runs pattern-oracle -Write first, then generator.spi, and only then lists the other test files, so a fresh clone tests the modules the generator just wrote; tiposBasico call sites read deps/corpus and src/tiposBasico_v4.00.xsd is deleted; the stray generated/tipos_basico_enums.spi writer and folder are gone; candidates/ (dfa-table superseded by lib dfa_table_text, fit-or-break superseded by lib xsd_emit) and scripts candidate-gate, copy-gate, gate-core, delphi-arms are deleted; package.spiproj lists cfdi, ksef and ubl (FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE); measures_the_shape_of_the_nfe_layout is in corpus_checks (xsd_shape.py goes once it passes); hand-written Spiral is list sugar (x converter + a multi-line pass, 221 sites) and edition-3 symbols (main respell-repo.ps1 GREEN, round trip clean), format --check clean; generator.spi is a thin client of lib xsd_emit (1,741 -> 211 lines); corpus.spi gains manifest_files over xsd_regex.lines_of and the three newline loops are list recursions; has_id -> listm.contains. README rewritten: what is generated, reach vs potential with sourced World Bank/official stats, build, layout, limits. Proof run queued: job-1792912920241-fiscal-ci-local (init, build, publish; heavy).")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_12 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 12u32, "2026-10-25T11:50Z", "fiscal", "07:50 fiscal: the first local CI run (job-1792912920241, 03:44-07:44) is RED on every file and was cut by the 240-min heavy-slot cap: generator 7/7 timed out (1200 s cells), corpus 300 s timeout, corpus_checks and every generated module so far OutOfMemoryException in the compiler. Cause: every test file carries ///- --package ., and Cells type-checks every module of that package in every cell; with the 16 real generated modules (13.7 MB) in src/package.spiproj that is OOM. main's gates were green only because family-gates.ps1 first overwrote each generated module with a one-line placeholder; build.ps1 and CI had no such step, so they could never pass, and my D7 (adding cfdi/ksef/ubl) made it worse. Rule, written before the fix: build units by hand (hand-modeled-build-units). src/package.spiproj lists only the 4 hand-written modules; the generator writes each module to generated/<module>/<module>.spi beside a one-module package.spiproj (packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib, |core-, spiral-), so a cell type-checks one module; each module carries a sample_path helper (FISCAL_ROOT, else walk up to src/generator.spi) instead of fiscal_paths; /generated/ is ignored; build.ps1 lists src/*.spi and generated/*/*.spi; family-gates.ps1 loses its placeholder branch. Green = every file passes in one build.ps1 run under the 240-min cap; if the cap is the only failure, split build.ps1 into per-unit jobs.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_13 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 13u32, "2026-10-25T12:40Z", "fiscal", "08:40 fiscal: smoke (job-1792930374147, test-suite window) step results: generator --test generates_ksef_fa_3 PASSED in 664 s and wrote generated/generated_ksef_fa_3/ (one-module package, 10,098 lines, option-B header), so the build-units writer works; corpus.spi still timed out at 300 s in the 4-module package, so its cause is the budget, not the package: corpus.spi and corpus_checks.spi had no --timeout header (Cells default 300 s) while each parses the 580-schema corpus. Both now carry --timeout 1200000 like generator.spi (a gate budget, note-13 kind). The ksef module step is running.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_14 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 14u32, "2026-10-25T12:53Z", "fiscal", "08:53 fiscal: smoke results acted on. (1) generated_ksef_fa_3 fails to type-check from a lib emitter defect, now XSD-NAMESPACE-NAMES (P0): tns:TAdres and etd:TAdres share a name. Rule: a family with a known emitter defect stays generated and gated, but its example test is pinned as Cells `fails \"<error>\"` through a typed family_root.known_defect : option string (ksef = Some \"Unification failure\"), so the build stays green while the defect exists and turns red the moment the fix lands, which is the reminder to drop the pin. No skip list. (2) FatturaPA next: generates_fatturapa_1_2_3 alone on a scratch copy with a 7,200 s budget, now in the 4-module package (its old timeouts type-checked all 16 modules per cell): finishes under 1,200 s -> nothing to change; under 7,200 s -> raise generator.spi's budget to 1.5x the measured time or split the emission per FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS n18; never -> stack dump.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_15 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 15u32, "2026-10-25T14:09Z", "fiscal", "10:09 fiscal: two more no-drift edits in the tree (unstaged). (1) Test placement per SPIRAL-NOTEBOOK-FILES: main's interleaver moved 7 trailing tests (corpus 1, corpus_checks 1, generator 5) right after the last definition each uses; exact line multiset kept, format --check clean. (2) List sugar in fiscal's own emitted text: the NF-e self-check and family_example_check no longer emit Cons (0 Cons left in fiscal src). Both ride in the full local run after the FatturaPA probe.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_16 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 16u32, "2026-10-25T16:58Z", "fiscal", "12:5x fiscal (watchdog): kit gate job-1792943656306 healthy (54 min; heartbeat 12:49): xsd_regex 6/6, xsd_resolve 3/3, xsd 6/6; xsd_emit candidate rows 5 red 2 (the two family tests' cell timeouts again), so the kit runs the tree baseline and its one timeout retry by its own rule. The post-land sequence is ONE serial job (serial-runner rule): job-1792947423678-fiscal-post-land (session scratchpad post-land-run.ps1), after job-1792945636355-fiscal-oracle-271b. Steps, each gated by a rule written before the result: (1) the 271b stdout total is executed 5, passed 5, failed 0, else no land; (2) kit land.ps1 -Name XSD-SLICES (waits for a GREEN verdict and its reader guard; throws otherwise), then prints the 4 tree hashes; (3) fiscal-post-land/apply.py (oracle over 271 patterns, KSeF known_defect None, gitignore and README); (4) scripts/build.ps1 in its own test-suite window (240 min), printing every Cells row with its ms, which gives FatturaPA's new generation time for FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS n21; (5) delphi-step1.ps1 in its own window (FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH n12). xsd_shape.py is deleted only after (4) shows measures_the_shape_of_the_nfe_layout passed.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_17 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 17u32, "2026-10-25T19:06Z", "fiscal", "14:5x fiscal (user 14:4x: stale ignored files, the py file): (1) scripts/xsd_shape.py deleted (untracked; 0 references in fiscal, positive-controlled grep); its Spiral replacement measures_the_shape_of_the_nfe_layout runs in the build in flight. (2) Stale build products: build.ps1 now clears generated/, samples/, notebooks/ and target/ci-metrics at the start of every FULL run (not on -Files subsets, which would lose modules they do not regenerate) and recreates samples/ itself, so no product of an older run can survive or be validated again. Not deleted by hand now: the full build in flight (job-1792950895715) writes exactly those folders. After it ends, anything it did not rewrite is deleted (files older than its start). (3) Tracked-but-ignored: src/patterns.txt is a build product since the post-land patch (pattern-oracle.ps1 derives it from deps), but it is still in the index; removing it from git is the user's commit (git rm --cached src/patterns.txt), so it is not staged here.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_18 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 18u32, "2026-10-25T20:09Z", "fiscal", "16:0x fiscal: first full build on the landed lib (job-1792950895715, stopped by me at 16:0x after diagnosis) found 3 defects, all in fiscal's own files, fixed in the tree:\n(A) every generated module failed to type-check: fiscal's sample_helper emitted `root ++# \"/samples/\" ++# name`, but generated modules open no sm'_operators (that was their only ++#). It now emits the qualified sm'.(++#), as generated code already does with sm'.length.\n(B) corpus_checks cfdi_catalogs_become_minimal_dfas: EJP0040 (more than 1000 dynamic if branches) from MY FISCAL-LIB-FIRST edit: listm'.try_find is an inline recursion, unrolled over the runtime catalog rows. It is now listm'.try_find_, the join-point variant, as the compiler's help says. The two cen_* failures in the same file were cells killed by the compiler process dying after that (compiler connection failed: :noproc), not code.\n(C) generator generates_the_uf_code_union still asserted the OLD enumeration text (elif s = \"35\" then Some TCodUfIBGE_35); the landed xsd_emit emits match arms by design (FISCAL-GENERATED-STYLE), so the expectation is now `    | \"35\" => Some TCodUfIBGE_35`.\nResults from that run that stand: generator 6/7 passed, including generates_ksef_fa_3 with known_defect None (XSD-NAMESPACE-NAMES works on the real KSeF tree) and generates_fatturapa_1_2_3 in 743 s (was 6,368 s: the cubic DFA build is gone; generation went from about 96 min to about 2). Rule n21 applied: generator.spi budget 9,600,000 -> 1,400,000 ms (1.5x the file's slowest cell, UBL 913 s, above the 1,200 s floor). Requeued as job-1792958908945-fiscal-post-build (build, then delphi-step1, then the XSD-EMIT-BUDGET slice).")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_19 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 19u32, "2026-10-25T21:20Z", "fiscal", "18:1x fiscal: build job-1792958908945 so far: generator 7/7 (FatturaPA cell 457 s, KSeF un-pinned), corpus_checks 15/15 (the 271-pattern oracle, and measures_the_shape_of_the_nfe_layout, the Spiral replacement for the deleted scripts/xsd_shape.py), corpus 1/1. The generated modules are compiling now (generated_cfdi_v4_0 past 240 s instead of failing fast), the first evidence that fix A (qualified sm'.(++#) in sample_path) holds.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_20 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 20u32, "2026-10-27T06:38Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 02:38 fiscal: strand-by-strand state of this epic, checked against the trees. (1) XML and tiposBasico in git: DONE by construction: fiscal tracks 0 *.xml and no src/tiposBasico_v4.00.xsd (it is no longer on disk); samples/, generated/, notebooks/, target/, dist/, deps/ and the three src pattern text files are ignored as build outputs (.gitignore, 9 lines), matching standardize-with-siblings (CI outputs are not committed). (2) py rewrite: DONE: 0 tracked and 0 on-disk *.py outside deps (xsd_shape.py's replacement is corpus_checks' measures_the_shape_of_the_nfe_layout, note of 10-25). (3) gh actions: fiscal has .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml + actions.lock, the same naming as spiral; the workflow's content is FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW (its own item). (4) workflow naming across repos, audited against spiral's convention (gh-pages.yml, plus actions.lock whenever a workflow has uses:), i574n-owned repos only (origin remotes; fc1943s repos are backups, upstream forks keep upstream's files). Conforming: blog, dir-tree-html, eoie, fiscal, i574n.github, tibia (+ spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot, fork-spiral-patch). STRAYS, not changed by me because renaming CI workflows and generating locks (gh actions-lock, network) is repo structure owned by each repo's lane and the user: x: ci.yml instead of gh-pages.yml (x lane); tictactoe: extra dist.yml and no actions.lock; no actions.lock: azeroth, dotfiles, i574n.github.io, tapas2; cargo-outdated: upstream's ci/ci-pr/deploy/lint kept on purpose (fork). Remaining for this epic: FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW, plus the stray fixes above once their owners agree.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_21 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 21u32, "2026-10-27T14:02Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 10:02 fiscal: SCRATCH SWEEP (user 10-27 03:2x: WIP lives in its repo, scratch only for throwaway proof copies), second pass after the 03:55 bench move. Inventory of the session scratchpad's 68 folders with top-level sources: (1) transforms and proof runners of LANDED work (r5, r5b, r6x, r7, r8, r9, s1a, s1b, s2, s2d, gf0-gf2, ge, f1, xsd-slices, xsd-remodel, xsd-namespace-names, xsd-emit-style, xsd-regex-repeat, examples, rtsweep, cells-guard, cells-name, gsplit, fiscal-post-land, recgroups' applied transforms, landings, identity, integ, integ2, g1build, kit-staged-land): throwaway once landed, as the rule allows; (2) snapshot copies of tree files (wip-any, wip-ns, delphi-fmt, release-dry, list-sugar-025911, fmt-075531, cons-check, nil-xc-regen, xmlrt, *-check folders): backups/proof copies; (3) superseded: bench-ours append_bench.py, micros_transform.py, run-bench*.ps1 (the generator's own bench replaced them, proven 10-27); (4) genuinely PENDING, decided one by one: edition3/generator_toml_transform.py waits on the compiler reading spiral.toml (applying it now breaks every build; a draft with an unmet precondition waits, per the rule); generator-v2/families.spi (10-25) is STALE against today's generator (source union, families, bench port, documents, budgets), so FISCAL-VERSIONED-FAMILIES re-derives it on the live tree from notes 82-83 of FISCAL-GENERIC instead of moving a non-compiling file into fiscal/src; (5) methods I reuse (cells-ab/test_probes.py: turn test blocks into probe definitions for type-checking; typefeature-census census.py; edition3 keyword_census.py; delphi-survey uses_closure.py): under generic-tools-not-per-experiment they belong as kit commands, filed as a follow-up rather than moved as Python into fiscal (which has no Python by design).")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_22 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 22u32, "2026-10-28T10:44Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 06:44 fiscal: SCRATCH SWEEP APPLIED (from the 10-27 inventory note): the 44 folders it classed as throwaway (transforms + proof runners of landed work, snapshot copies, superseded bench scripts) archived via scratch-archive.ps1 -Apply: archive 20261028-064245, 1773 files, 0 blob verify failures, 0 links; restore with -Restore %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261028-064245\\manifest.tsv. Kept: notes, recgroups (set-proof launchers referenced by FISCAL-GENERIC), cells-ab/typefeature/edition3/delphi-survey methods, pending drafts, backups, bench-acbr/dfe-net/compare (FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH). Scratchpad 100 -> 56 folders. Next: the commit summary of fiscal's unstaged tree for the user.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_23 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 23u32, "2026-10-28T10:49Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 06:49 fiscal: COMMIT SUMMARY for the user (fiscal tree, nothing staged; git is the user's): suggested commits: (1) corpus: scripts/init.ps1 (+611: ZATCA SDK data + rebuilt .sch, SUNAT homologation, GST example + normalization, Greek BOM-free, UBL-TR code page 857, DFe.NET pinned 56ad2820, Remove-EmptySigningSlot) + new scripts/schxslt-to-schematron.ps1; (2) checks: scripts/validate-example.ps1 (path fix), new scripts/validate-manifests.ps1, scripts/family-gates.ps1 (GST JSON gate), src/corpus_checks.spi (ZATCA + ZATCA-CEN census); (3) generator: src/generator.spi (UBL business documents, real-document checks XML + JSON, count-type fix). (4) bench/ours-families.tsv = FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH WIP, commit with the bench. All type-check; node proofs green except 1 Greek document (XSD-ALL-GROUPS).")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_24 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 24u32, "2026-10-28T12:39Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 08:39 fiscal: ADDENDUM to the 3 suggested commits (all unstaged, user commits): (4) 'CFDI 4.0 stamped fixture': scripts/init.ps1 pins CfdiUtils@f2a03dc2 cfdi40-valid.xml into corpus/mx/stamped + mx-cfdi-comprobante manifest; scripts/validate-manifests.ps1 mx row (1/1 schema-valid). (5) 'corpus pattern pin 336': src/corpus_checks.spi 271->336 in the two pattern-count tests (corpus grew; node census proves every pattern parses and has a DFA). Held lib program, not for commit: lib-programs/xsd-any-complements-writer.kit. CORRECTION: the earlier 3 suggested commits are already in, as user commit 96cac20 07:08 'tests' (7 files, +1341); only (4) and (5) above remain uncommitted, plus bench/ours-families.tsv.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_25 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 25u32, "2026-10-28T13:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 09:58 fiscal: ADDENDUM 2 (uncommitted, user commits): (6) 'UBL-JSON manifests + catPagos': scripts/init.ps1 writes ubl-<kind>-json-documents.txt (invoice 22 LHDN JSON) and pins SAT catPagos.xsd. Still held, not for commit: lib-programs/ubl-json-transcoder.kit, xsd-any-content-indirection.kit, xsd-any-complements-writer.kit.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_26 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 26u32, "2026-10-28T14:15Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 10:15 fiscal: ADDENDUM 3 (uncommitted): (7) 'format generated units': new scripts/format-generated.ps1 + build.ps1 calls it after the generator (CI builds now format every generated unit; cached in target/format-cache, so unchanged units cost ~0).")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_27 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 27u32, "2026-10-29T12:06Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 08:06 fiscal: ADDENDUM 4 (uncommitted, user commits): (8) 'UBL-JSON documents in the generator': src/generator.spi ubl_json_documents + document_round_trip + module_package dependency list + test; (9) 'list sugar in fiscal src': src/{corpus,corpus_checks,generator}.spi Nil -> []; (10) lib-programs: xsd-staged-type-names.kit (value-restriction fix, proven fix-ss2), new generator-complements.kit (held). Landed lib programs of 10-29 (list sugar, complements writer, UBL-JSON transcoder) stay in lib-programs as the record.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_28 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 28u32, "2026-10-29T13:12Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 09:12 fiscal: ADDENDUM 5 (uncommitted, user commits): fiscal/lib-programs gained held programs xsd-any-content-indirection (proven fix-ac2), xpath-castable-for, xpath-following-axes, schematron-abstract-extends, xsd-staged-type-emission (M2), generator-complements (fiscal tree); fiscal/src/corpus_checks.spi gained regional_rules_name_every_test_that_does_not_parse (names every unparsed schematron test per region). All lib lands wait for kit's 'TRAIN3 notebooks end' (09:07 start).")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_29 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 29u32, "2026-10-29T16:03Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 12:03 fiscal: ADDENDUM 6 (uncommitted, user commits): (11) src/corpus_checks.spi gains ubl_tr_main_schematron_runs_its_extended_rules (applied corpus-ubltr-main-census.kit; needs today's landed schematron/xpath); (12) src/generator.spi: json_round_trip, an order-free JSON check (stable typed round trip + same element/text/attribute count) replacing the order-sensitive first_difference for UBL-JSON documents. New held programs: xsd-staged-writers (M4, revised), xsd-staged-examples (M5), xsd-staged-units (M6 lib), generator-signed-families (M6 generator, rebased onto the tree's JSON fix). Landed lib programs of 10-29 (8 at 11:37-11:58 plus 3 at 07:28) stay in lib-programs as the record.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_30 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 30u32, "2026-10-29T22:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 18:53 fiscal: ADDENDUM 7 (uncommitted, user commits): new held lib programs in fiscal/lib-programs, all rehearsed hash-equal on fresh copies and round-tripped spi->livemd->spi as byte-identical fixpoints: schematron-pattern-lets-shared (schematron_run 8C0F45B9 -> B9D3079F, HOLDS 3/3 pack 6b), xpath-path-step-flattens (xpath_eval BB243652 -> 38FCB953, HOLDS 7/7), xpath-floor-pictures-errors (stacked: xpath_eval -> BFFB32F6 HOLDS 7/7, xpath C266A60D -> BD24C126 HOLDS 4/4), schematron-unnamed-asserts (schematron DD29523E -> C36C6296, proof fiscal-6b-idfb2-proof). NOT LANDED tonight: git-a6's pack-7 review holds a 5 h heavy-build window over lib (main 18:5x: no exemption). Main's plan: they land FIRST in next session's first window, after the review and before kit's physics rename. Lander: scratchpad land-1029/lander10.ps1 (copy in C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\land-1029), undo bases in C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\land-1029\\{letscope,seqflat,zfn}\\base. src/corpus_checks.spi (D1EFC449) gains first_failures (rule ids in the Peppol/ZATCA/UBL-TR evaluation failures).")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_31 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 31u32, "2026-10-29T23:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 19:58 fiscal: LANDED in window 4 (main's order, fiscal first) via lander10 / lib-guarded-do, reader check on, base pins matched, drafts canonical: 19:49:30 schematron-pattern-lets-shared (schematron_run 8C0F45B9 -> B9D3079F), 19:49:32 xpath-path-step-flattens (xpath_eval BB243652 -> 38FCB953), 19:49:33 xpath-floor-pictures-errors (xpath_eval -> BFFB32F6, xpath C266A60D -> BD24C126), 19:49:34 schematron-unnamed-asserts (schematron DD29523E -> C36C6296). Live at land: sm' 2196F9C70148, testing 20C1C824AF64, file_system 8661852BC5D5 (x then landed file_system 81140E3E5A74 + physics). Main's closing gate: document_test 22 passed; type_probe PASS on all 4 files. fiscal's node gate: fiscal-6b-gate4-{schematron_run,schematron,xpath,xpath_eval} on mirror gate4 (post-land tree, file_system 81140E3E). Still held: xpath-double-cast (proven, round trip running), xsd-staged-element-signatures (M6, proving).")
