inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_35 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 35u32, "2026-10-30T10:35Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 06:35 fiscal: ADDENDUM 9, commit inventory at the end of the night run (uncommitted; you commit). FISCAL repo: M scripts/build.ps1 (passes -Dotnet to the formatter), M scripts/init.ps1, M scripts/validate-manifests.ps1, M src/corpus.spi, M src/corpus_checks.spi (78DD9F9C: evaluated_on (), first_failures, UBL-TR invoice + other-document pins, ZATCA/Peppol diagnostics), M src/generator.spi (CB7E07C7: json_round_trip size-drift diagnostic with the paren-pattern fix, signed families, Nil -> []), ?? scripts/format-generated.ps1 (-Dotnet), ?? lib-programs/ (every 10-29 program, landed and held), ?? bench/ours-families.tsv; 6 files changed, 514+ / 71-. SPIRAL repo, landed by fiscal through the guard, unstaged: lib/spiral/schematron_run.spi B9D3079F, xpath_eval.spi 34646246, xpath.spi 382A551C, schematron.spi C36C6296, xsd_emit.spi 161D00F5; apps/cells lib/spiral/cells.ex 1CE33272, cells/memo.ex BABC5167, cells/mounts.ex 85B259B5, cells/notebook.ex 11296BE2, cells/notebook_cli.ex AAA3A024, cells/x_compat.ex 91E1417B, new cells/manifest.ex D73B3524, new test/spiral/cells/manifest_test.exs 91BC7680. These commits gate closing ZATCA, Peppol, UBL-TR and CL-DTE, and the edition-3 apply.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_36 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 36u32, "2026-10-30T14:16Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 10:16 fiscal: lib-programs/ MOVED out of the fiscal repo (main agreed 10-30): 28 .kit programs, 150373 bytes, to C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\lib-programs, hash-checked 28/28 identical. They are applied kit do programs that duplicate the landed spiral/fiscal diffs, so they don't belong in git. Notes citing fiscal/lib-programs/<name>.kit now resolve under that folder. Commit inventory = ADDENDUM 9 minus lib-programs/.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_37 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 37u32, "2026-10-30T19:17Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 15:17 fiscal: ADDENDUM 10, current commit inventory (replaces ADDENDUM 9's list; you commit, nothing is staged by me). FISCAL repo (unchanged since 06:35 apart from lib-programs/ leaving): M scripts/build.ps1, M scripts/init.ps1, M scripts/validate-manifests.ps1, M src/corpus.spi, M src/corpus_checks.spi (78DD9F9C), M src/generator.spi (CB7E07C7), ?? scripts/format-generated.ps1, ?? bench/ours-families.tsv; 6 tracked files, +514/-71. lib-programs/ is NOT in the repo any more (C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\lib-programs). SPIRAL repo, lib files fiscal landed (each also carries earlier unstaged lands): lib/spiral/xsd_emit.spi 161D00F5 (M6 10-29), xsd.spi 147F773B (xs:all 10-29), schematron.spi 59A7D2CA, schematron_run.spi 1E8B9B6C, xpath.spi 3EAD89A2, xpath_eval.spi 40180DBF, xsd_constraints.spi E1CE6C63 (today's DIAN land 12:55: compiled-schematron reader, current(), except/intersect, prefix wildcards). apps/cells: M lib/spiral/cells.ex, M lib/spiral/cells/mounts.ex (+ memo/notebook/notebook_cli/x_compat from 10-29 23:54), ?? lib/spiral/cells/manifest.ex, ?? test/spiral/cells/manifest_test.exs (spiral.toml support). The spiral repo has 1273 unstaged paths overall from all lanes; these are fiscal's. AFTER your fiscal commit: the edition-3 draft (FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND n27, proven) gets applied as a new unstaged layer (src/*.spi respelled + package.spiproj edition 3 + new src/spiral.toml). The live src still equals the draft's bases (checked 15:1x: generator CB7E07C7, corpus_checks 78DD9F9C, corpus 8B45E3CB, fiscal_paths 62FE941B).")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_38 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 38u32, "2026-10-31T01:02Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 21:02 fiscal: USER DECISION (10-30 ~20:55): the generated .spi units (the GADT-typed readers/writers) are tracked in the fiscal repo, with their relevant compiled backends, to show and benchmark them; TSVs stay out of git but are generated and shown on the gh-pages dir-tree-html page; drift must not pollute the repo. This replaces my earlier 'do not commit generated/*.spi'. DONE in the tree (unstaged): .gitignore drops /generated/ and adds *.tsv (bench/ours-families.tsv leaves the commit inventory). scripts/build.ps1 gains a drift guard right after generation + format-generated and before the unit tests: git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all -- generated; any line is a build problem ('commit the regenerated generated/'), so CI fails whenever the committed units differ from what the generator writes (eol is safe: .gitattributes '* text=auto eol=lf'). TSVs: build writes target/ci-metrics/*.tsv, and publish.ps1 -> spiral publish-tree.ps1 -Include *.tsv publishes untracked matches (git ls-files --others, no exclude-standard), so they reach the dir-tree-html page without being tracked. SIZE: 74 units, 48.9 MB of .spi (largest: the three UBL units, 3.0 MB each); git deltas/compression keep a regeneration commit far smaller, and the content only moves when the generator does (deterministic: 148/148 identical across the edition-3 respell). POPULATING: the tree's generated/ is the stale 10-27 output; it gets the e3lv mirror's copy (same generator, synced 10:32 from the tree's 06:29 generator.spi) once fiscal-p7-e3lv-set-grptcr finishes rewriting it. COMPILED BACKENDS, not done yet, by rule: dice's ratio is 12 KB .spi -> 510 KB .rs (42x), so all 74 units on every backend would be GBs and would drift on every compiler bump (spiral slides with main). Plan: a showcase set = the bench units (MDF-e 1.00 first, then NF-e 4.00), on the bench's backends (Rust; Delphi .pas once the Delphi arms land; F# for the DFe.NET comparison), written next to the unit by build.ps1 like dice's lib/build.ps1 (BuildSpiral), under the same drift guard; sizes measured from one real compile before adding more units.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_39 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 39u32, "2026-10-31T01:47Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 21:47 fiscal: generated/ POPULATED in the fiscal tree (unstaged, for your commit): 74 units, 148 files (each unit's .spi + package.spiproj), 52.5 MB, copied from node mirror e3lv after fiscal-p7-e3lv-set-grptcr finished rewriting it (21:42); two successive copies were hash-identical, nothing else in the folders, no machine paths (C:\\home, remote\\e3lv, runner paths: 0 hits), no CRLF. The 10-27 stale copy is replaced. Commit set for this decision: .gitignore, scripts/build.ps1 (drift guard), scripts/format-generated.ps1 (untracked, build.ps1 already calls it), generated/. The first CI run after the commit proves the copy: the drift guard fails if the CI generator writes anything different.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_40 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 40u32, "2026-11-01T03:39Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 23:35 fiscal: CLOSING (groom 10-31 night): the rule is settled and enforced: commit generated/ (the user's showcase choice) plus every non-regenerable file; TSVs, samples, notebooks, target/ and canon/ are gitignored and built + published by CI (dir-tree-html); build.ps1's drift guard fails CI when generated/ differs from a regeneration.")
inl note_of_fiscal_commit_hygiene_41 () = Note ("FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE", 41u32, "2026-11-01T04:40Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 02:12 fiscal: LAB HISTORY (main's groom round 2): fiscal never had a lab/ folder. git log --all shows no lab/ path in fiscal, and none named FISCAL/XSD/XML in spiral, i574n.github or kit; none on disk. Its lab-like places were fiscal/lib-programs/*.kit (an invented extension), archived 10-31 (kit archive 20261031-165057) with every program moved into backlog notes (FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH n84/n86, XML-XPATH-FIDELITY n12, XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE n5, FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND n29), and C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts, drained and removed for DATA-DIR-HOME. Drafts now live in the session scratchpad only while being verified, then land in the repo or a note.")
inl note_of_kit_registry_giant_line_1 () = Note ("KIT-REGISTRY-GIANT-LINE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T11:17Z", "eoie", "07:2x groom: tonight's next-train stage S5 (contracts slice D) already edits eoie_dev_spiral_tests/registry.spi (contract_registered () plus a test), so the giant-line fix goes into that same change: re-lay the nested SpiralTestNotebook chain one entry per line, or better make it a list of records so adding a notebook stops needing a paren at the end. First step: find what writes registry.spi (a generator or hand edits by slices; tonight's slices A-D append entries by string replace, which explains the shape).")
inl note_of_kit_registry_giant_line_2 () = Note ("KIT-REGISTRY-GIANT-LINE", 2u32, "2026-10-30T15:42Z", "kit", "10-30 11:4x kit: DONE. The giant line was already fixed by an earlier change: kit_dev_spiral_tests/registry.spi is a list of { package; relative } records, one per line (the 'better' shape in n1). Measured now: 122 lines, longest 103, 0 over 120, 112 entries, no duplicate paths, every registered file exists. Adding a notebook is a one-line insert before ']', so no generator writes nested parens any more. Done in the same change: the 5 test modules from today's GRANULAR-LINT port were missing from the registry (so 'kit dev spiral tests' and CI never ran them). They are registered now (kit_lint_model, kit_lint_pattern, kit_lint_flow, kit_lint_flow_constructs, kit_lint_flow_idioms); spiral_tests.rs regenerated 647 -> 661. Landed unstaged; draft C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\registry-lint\\registry-lint.do.")
inl note_of_kit_registry_giant_line_3 () = Note ("KIT-REGISTRY-GIANT-LINE", 3u32, "2026-11-06T11:29Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-REGISTRY-GIANT-LINE (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_1 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 1u32, "2026-10-25T00:02Z", "x", "10-24 20:02 x: P2 -> P1, the next x work after the interim fix (user 20:0x). DECISION RULE, written before measuring. Candidates: (A) today's one package with post-hoc parts (baseline). (B) eoie-style granular packages: one Spiral entry + gleam.toml per domain module (prelude/glyphs base, protocol, splitter, events, invariants, personas, scenario, schedule, world, frames codec), with cross-package calls through exported nominal types (Gleam has the same need RUST-EXPORT-TYPES solved for Rust) and 'let' join points where inlining duplicates closures. A root gleam.toml with path deps plays the workspace. (C) B plus packages built concurrently. Measure on a quiet machine, 3 cold + 3 warm runs each: cold/warm build wall, a one-module touch rebuild (the incremental case packages should win), total generated lines, largest file, and sim -fast + corpus wall per schedule. Pick: the package layout (B or C) wins if every file is <= 1,000 lines with no post-processing, outputs are byte-identical (15 export hashes plus fixtures, the note-9 method), and the touch rebuild or cold wall beats A by >= 20%. If B/C only tie A on speed, still take B/C, since the user's stated goal is packages and the parts script is a known breakage point; record the numbers. A stays only if B can't be expressed without compiler work, and then that compiler gap is filed for main with this evidence. First CPU-light step: list which nominal types cross each proposed package seam (from the .spi opens) and check whether the Gleam backend emits pub named types for Export signatures, as Rust now does.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_2 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 2u32, "2026-10-25T00:07Z", "x", "10-24 20:07 x: TYPE-SEAM CENSUS (step 1, text only). Scope: the sim closure's 13 .spi files; a type counts as crossing when a module that opens its home mentions it. 82 types; 27 cross a module seam: 17 records, 5 unions, 2 nominals, 3 aliases. The hub is events (11 of the 27: unions event, revision, content, reaction; records rooted, social, sourced, delivery, frame_part), used by final_state, personas, scenario and schedule. Next are scenario (scenario, casting, flow), personas (union persona; acted, cast), protocol (nominals post_id, cell_id; post_ids), final_state (tally, cell_state, replay), exsss, and the facade asks (run_ask, world_ask, world, losses). How the Gleam backend emits types today, from apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames/types.gleam: 59 pub types named by a per-program counter (Uh0.., Us0..), not after their Spiral source, and all 15 exports are String -> String. That's the state Rust was in before RUST-EXPORT-TYPES. Two packages would each mint their own Uh3 for 'event', so only text crosses a package boundary today. A cross-package call is a host call to the other package's exported fn, the way xc.spi calls xc_net::xc_requests. Proposed cut, from the open graph: base (prelude, glyphs, exsss, protocol), events (+ final_state), splitter, invariants, cast (personas, scenario, schedule), world, and the facade. Compiler need, for main: Gleam Export signatures carry the 5 unions and 2 nominals as pub types named after their Spiral source; records crossing a seam need named records too (17), or a fixed positional layout that every package agrees on. Until then, layout B can be measured only with text seams. Run it that way as B0, an honest lower bound that pays parse cost at each seam, and record the cost per seam.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_3 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 3u32, "2026-10-25T00:09Z", "x", "10-24 20:09 x: RULE AMENDED before any measurement (main 20:1x; nothing measured yet, so the rule stays pre-registered). (1) Census correction: the 2 nominals (post_id, cell_id) are evaluated away before codegen and cross as their primitive, so the compiler seam is the 5 unions plus the crossing records. Records get stable names by Rust's exported_record_names rule, not a positional layout. (2) Compiler work is not a disqualifier. GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES (main, P1, 240m) is the bounded Gleam codegen half of RUST-EXPORT-TYPES, since unionSourceNames already exists for every backend. New rule: measure A (parts) and B0/C0 (packages with text seams). Packages are chosen if every file is <= 1,000 lines with no post-processing, outputs are byte-identical, and B0 or C0 is no more than 10% slower than A on cold build, touch rebuild and sim/corpus wall. B0 is the upper bound on the cost of the typed version, which drops the parse at each seam. If they win, GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES lands and B is re-measured typed to confirm it is <= B0. If B0/C0 is more than 10% slower, packages are still chosen when the typed version is projected to close the gap: B0 minus its measured seam-parse time must come within 10% of A. A survives only if even that projection loses.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_4 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 4u32, "2026-10-25T00:13Z", "x", "10-24 20:13 x: SCOPE CHANGE (user 20:1x). Packages vs parts is decided: packages, cut at codegen, and parts are drift. The note-1/3 rule's 'A survives' branch is void. A stays only as the baseline the numbers are reported against. The bench now picks the best package granularity and build technique (B, B finer per X-PARALLEL-CENSUS, C parallel, plus whatever the research ranks), by cold, touch and sim/corpus wall. Parent: X-PARALLEL-BUILD.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_5 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 5u32, "2026-10-25T00:27Z", "x", "10-24 20:27 x: research (X-PARALLEL-BUILD n2). Gleam 1.19 compiles packages sequentially, and BEAM-compiles one package's modules in parallel (gleam@@compile.erl worker pool). Candidates, rule unchanged otherwise: P = packages per domain unit (as noted earlier), M = one package whose modules map 1:1 to Spiral source modules, emitted at codegen (SPIRAL-SHARED-SPECIALIZATIONS), and A = the post-hoc parts baseline. Prediction (recorded so it can be wrong): M beats P on cold build, P only ties on touch rebuild. Measure both; packages are kept only where they win.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_6 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 6u32, "2026-10-25T00:31Z", "x", "10-24 20:31 x: RULE CORRECTED before any measurement; this note supersedes the n3 tie-break. n3 said 'packages win within 10% of A', which could pick the shape the user named while losing the parallelism they named; main flagged it at 20:3x, and gleam's compile_dependencies() is a plain sequential loop (main verified). The user's goal (10-21, 10-24) is splits at codegen that build in parallel. Packages were the means. New rule: among the codegen-level candidates (M: modules per Spiral source module in one package; P: packages per domain unit; P+M), the fastest on cold build plus touch rebuild wins, with byte-identical outputs and every file <= 1,000 lines. A within 5% counts as a tie, and a tie goes to fewer gleam.toml files (fewer sequential barriers and resolutions). A (post-hoc parts) is a baseline only. The user is told of this tension directly.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_7 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 7u32, "2026-10-25T00:40Z", "x", "10-24 20:38 x: all candidates are HAND-MODELED (user 20:3x). M = one package, the sim's slices authored as separate Spiral entries, each compiled to its own Gleam module and calling the others through exports. P = the same slices as path packages. No candidate is emitted by the compiler or a script. The n6 rule is otherwise unchanged; per the research, M is expected to win for Gleam.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_8 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 8u32, "2026-10-25T02:44Z", "x", "10-24 22:44 x: GLEAM PROBES MEASURED (job-1792896048419, scratch copy, live lib), one entry per hand unit with the event-stream text as the seam: g_split 1159 (defused 328), g_masked 524, g_rows (checked/commute/breaking) 2692, g_raw (cells/corpus -> stream) 2768, g_paced (stream -> deliveries) 3168, g_final (stream -> final state) 2373, g_world (stream -> world files) 4899. Findings: (1) text seams don't work for Gleam: each consumer re-decodes the stream, and the event codec alone (string_from 347, fields_from 279, decoded 164, encoded 280, without_controls 143) is over 1,100 lines, so no unit that reads the stream can be <= 1,000. (2) Gleam inflates single functions: defused is 200 lines in Rust and 328 in Gleam. (3) Codegen compaction (single-expression arms without braces, let-then-return inlined) is estimated at only ~15% (g_split 1159 -> ~1000, g_world 4899 -> ~4120). So M (one package, modules per source slice) needs TYPED CROSS-MODULE CALLS: the codec unit exports decode/encode with named types, and consumers import them, so shared code is emitted once instead of per unit. That's GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES plus a consumer half (an entry declares it uses another unit's exports, and codegen emits 'import xsim_events.{type Event, decoded}' instead of re-declaring). Until then scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 stays as the interim; it is the only post-hoc splitter left.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_9 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 9u32, "2026-10-25T05:09Z", "x", "10-25 01:09 x: re-measured the 7 Gleam probes on the installed pack 5j (18 codegen passes, dead helpers dropped): g_split 1143, g_rows 2676, g_masked 508, g_raw 2765, g_paced 3165, g_final 2367, g_world 4893, i.e. -6..-16 lines each. n8's conclusion stands: Gleam hand units need typed cross-module imports (GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES n6 design) before scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 can go.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_10 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 10u32, "2026-10-27T13:46Z", "x", "2026-10-27 09:46 x: DESIGN for the package split, taken from today's Rust crate rounds (frames split, requests crate), CPU-free. simframes.spi already exports only primitive boundaries (strings, i32, bool, and tuples of them; structured data as text with char 0/1 separators), so each export group can become its own Gleam package with its own gleam.toml, and none needs GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES. Cut along the existing export groups:\n(1) xsim_split: split_cell, file_cells, thread_posts (splitter + glyphs + defuse + tlds; pure text).\n(2) xsim_checks: checked_text, commute_text, breaking_text, masked_text (invariants).\n(3) xsim_scenario: scenario_stream, scenario_final (scenario, personas, schedule, events, final_state).\n(4) xsim_world: world_files (world).\n(5) xsim_corpus: corpus_run, corpus_raw, cells_raw, rescheduled, break_source.\nThe 10-22 attempt failed at 2.2x because each coarse entry compiled the whole scenario/events/world closure into itself. Today's fix for the same problem in Rust: a package that needs another's code calls that package's export through a one-expression host call (Gleam: $'xsim_split.split_cell(!a, !b, !c)' plus a path dependency in gleam.toml, the shape of x's $'xc_chunks::xc_cell_chunks(...)'), so splitter code exists once, in xsim_split. Shared helpers inside a package stay :~ join points, not := (attribute-generated-lines memory). Order of work: (a) xsim_split first, since world and corpus call split_cell and it is the cleanest boundary; (b) measure lines per package with the pinned compiler (each <= 1000 and the total <= today's 11,469 x 1.1); (c) cold and warm gleam build per package, in parallel vs the single package. Pre-register (b) and (c)'s thresholds before any build; the build and measuring are the heavy part, this design is the light part.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_11 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 11u32, "2026-10-27T14:03Z", "x", "2026-10-27 10:03 x: design step 2 (still CPU-free), from reading the sim's build and call graph. Loading: Xsim.Spiral.call prepends every frames/build/dev/erlang/*/ebin, so a Gleam path dependency (xsim_split = { path = \"../split\" } in frames/gleam.toml) is built by frames' 'gleam build' into that folder and loads automatically; Xsim.Spiral.call gains a module argument (split_cell, file_cells and thread_posts -> :xsim_split). Where splitter code enters xsim_frames: exactly two points, (1) schedule.spi parts_of (via framed_post, the scenario/corpus flow) and (2) world.spi chunks_for (posted_cell and revised_with). Both call splitter's split_cell directly, so moving the exports alone would leave a second full copy of the splitter inside xsim_frames and break the total-lines bound. Fix: thread a chunker : cell_id -> i32 -> string -> list string parameter from the export wrappers in simframes.spi down to those two points. simframes passes a one-expression host call into xsim_split; the notebook tests pass splitter's split_cell (the spiral-macro-gotchas pattern: a module takes the cross-package function as a parameter, the entry passes the primitive, tests pass a local stand-in). A chunker FIELD in the world record would touch fewer functions, but world values are compared and printed in tests and fixtures, and a function field breaks both, so thread a parameter instead. RULE for the step (a+b) round, written before drafting: accept if, with the pinned compiler, (1) xsim_split's generated Gleam and every xsim_frames module are each <= 1000 lines after gleam-modules, (2) xsim_frames no longer contains defused_dots_by or split_units (grep), (3) total generated sim Gleam is <= 12,616 lines (today's 11,469 x 1.1), and (4) the sim build passes with fixtures byte-identical to the same tree's pre-split accept, mix test, and the notebooks green. Measure cold and warm gleam build of both packages only after (1)-(4) pass.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_12 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 12u32, "2026-10-27T14:23Z", "x", "2026-10-27 10:23 x: step (a+b) DRAFTED in the tree (unstaged), measured under note 11's rule. New Gleam package apps/sim/split (gleam.toml xsim_split, built from the new entry apps/sim/spi/xsim_split/xsim_split.spi, which exports split_cell and file_cells/thread_posts for Elixir plus split_cell_joined, the chunks as one text joined with char 0, for package-to-package use, since each Gleam package has its own list type). frames/gleam.toml gains xsim_split as a path dependency, sim build.ps1 compiles and module-splits xsim_split before frames, and the sim .gitignore covers /split/build/. The splitter is reached only through a chunker (cell_id -> i32 -> string -> list string, declared in splitter.spi): schedule threads it through parts_of, framed_post/edit, revised_in, exploded_one and exploded, and staging and run_ask carry a cut field; world threads it through chunks_for, posted_cell, revised_with, edited_cell, laid_down and materialized, and world_ask carries cut. simframes passes remote_split (one host call to xsim_split.split_cell_joined, split back on char 0) and no longer exports the splitter. The notebook tests (scenario_checks, sim_run, world's tests) pass splitter's split_cell. Xsim.Spiral.call takes a module, and Xsim.Frames calls :xsim_split. Every cell <= 272, lines <= 120. Quick node check: apps/sim/build.ps1 -fast 1 -Accept (job-1793111039900, mirror xsplitpkg1).")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_13 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 13u32, "2026-10-27T15:02Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:02 x: step (a+b) MEASURED (quick node check job-1793112246184, sim build.ps1 -fast 1 -Accept, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) against note 11's pre-registered rule, which passes on all four clauses. (1) Every module <= 1000 lines: xsim_split splits into 6 modules (largest 960) and xsim_frames into 13 (largest 959). (2) No splitter functions in xsim_frames: 0 grep hits for defused_dots_by|split_units across frames/src. (3) Total sim Gleam <= 12,616: 12,437 file lines (frames 9,347 + split 3,090; the generated counts are 8,926 + 2,952 = 11,878). (4) Sim build green: ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, sim x-compat 0 in 24 files, and every fixture re-accepted. Three earlier checks failed on my own mistake, a second `:== chunker` alias in splitter.spi (the later declaration silently wins); renamed to cell_chunker. The rule closes here; what remains is landing, which goes through verify18 (job-1793113365827, the whole tree with the fixed accept order, sim before e2e), since the split changes the sim fixtures and generated Gleam the tutorial reads.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_14 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 14u32, "2026-10-28T10:31Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:29 x groom: deps replaced X-PARALLEL-CENSUS,GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES -> X-WIP-VERIFY. The split met its rule (note 13) without either; what is left is landing the generated Gleam from a green verify.")
inl note_of_xsim_gleam_split_bench_15 () = Note ("XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", 15u32, "2026-10-28T11:07Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it). The generated Gleam landed: frames 13 modules with 0 splitter functions, xsim_split 6 modules (staged from mirror xv20, since verify -Out lacked *.gleam, now fixed).")
inl note_of_kit_job_chain_serial_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-CHAIN-SERIAL", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-JOB-CHAIN-SERIAL (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_matrix_split_parts_memo_1 () = Note ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", 1u32, "2026-10-24T12:52Z", "main", "2026-10-24 08:52 main: fixed in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1 (Get-OutputParts: <stem>_*.<ext> plus <stem>.spiral-parts are stored under <memo>.parts, replayed with the main output, and hashed into the native memo key; parse-checked). Verification queued (main-serial-memo-parts: two -ReuseCompile runs of mega_brzozowski_derivatives on Rust -Native; round 2 must replay the compile and still run every row). Then the next accept run should turn the 5 Rust mega build-fails into ran/agree rows.")
inl note_of_matrix_split_parts_memo_2 () = Note ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", 2u32, "2026-10-24T23:46Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:46 groom: 70%. The uncommitted test.ps1 edit copies split parts in the native tier (found on the Gleam row, COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS), and in the pack 5i single-flight review the 5 Rust mega rows now run and agree. Left: confirm the native memo key covers main.spiral-parts (change one part, check a -ReuseNative rerun rebuilds), then Done.")
inl note_of_matrix_split_parts_memo_3 () = Note ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", 3u32, "2026-10-25T00:21Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:21 main: the split-parts half becomes moot with pack 5j, which removes the output splits. The real remaining gap is multiFile outputs: the host writes several files for 'Cpp + Cuda' (.corelib.hpp, .hpp, .cpp, .cu) and 'Python + Cuda' (_auto.py, .py) (Program.fs:493-496). At HEAD, test.ps1's compile memo stores and replays only the main output and keys the native memo on it alone. My parts code (Get-OutputParts) caught the 451 main_auto.py files only by accident, through its '<stem>_*' pattern, and 5j reverts it. Rule: the memo stores, replays and keys exactly the host's multiFileSuffixes for the row's backend, nothing found by pattern. Done when a -ReuseCompile replay of a Python + Cuda row restores main_auto.py byte-identically and a changed main_auto.py changes the native key.")
inl note_of_matrix_split_parts_memo_4 () = Note ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", 4u32, "2026-10-25T07:55Z", "main", "10-25 03:55 main: drafted per n3's rule: %TEMP%\\matrix-multifile-memo\\apply.ps1 (3 anchors, 1 each). Get-OutputParts takes the row's CoreBackend and returns exactly the host's multiFileSuffixes (Program.fs:493: Cpp + Cuda .corelib.hpp/.hpp/.cpp/.cu, Python + Cuda _auto.py/.py) minus the main output, so nothing is found by pattern. The compile-memo store and Get-NativeMemoKey pass CoreBackend; the native tier's Gleam filter (line 912) is left as written, so the native-code signature, and with it every native memo entry, survive. Python keys don't change, since main_auto.py was already hashed. Probe: Python + Cuda -> main_auto.py only (a planted main_other.py is no longer picked), Rust -> none. A patched copy parses with 0 errors. Land: job main-land-multifile-memo after the 5k review (test.ps1 reader). Done-check left: a -ReuseCompile replay of a Python + Cuda row restores main_auto.py byte-identical.")
inl note_of_matrix_split_parts_memo_5 () = Note ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", 5u32, "2026-10-25T08:44Z", "main", "10-25 04:44 main: LANDED in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1 (job-1792914904841, after the 5k review released it; 8+/8-, uncommitted). It supersedes the 'test.ps1 parts revert' planned for the next pack, which is the same code. Done-check left, riding on the next matrix run: a -ReuseCompile replay of a Python + Cuda row restores main_auto.py byte for byte.")
inl note_of_matrix_split_parts_memo_6 () = Note ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", 6u32, "2026-10-25T09:15Z", "main", "10-25 05:15 main: done-check attempt on samples/typecheck_long_let_body (Python + Cuda), two runs of test.ps1 -Filter ... -Backend Python -ReuseCompile: single-flight-20261025-044648, then -045543 with main_auto.py deleted between them. INCONCLUSIVE, with two findings. (1) Run 2 replayed 0 and compiled again (same residual c9c54fc07ac29d57), so the memo key changed between the runs. The key covers every .spi under the bundle, lib and core dir, and other lanes keep editing the live tree, so a replay check needs a quiet tree or a pinned copy (WorkspaceRoot). (2) The fresh compile in run 2 rewrote main.py but did NOT recreate the deleted main_auto.py. Restored from git, hash E10B3C2C as before. The core writes both multi-file outputs at coreBase (Program.fs:537-541) only when it writes at all. A deleted sibling output not coming back suggests the core or server skips writes whose text it believes unchanged. Next: reproduce on a quiet tree (delete main_auto.py, compile once outside test.ps1 via probe.ps1 -Backend Python); if it doesn't come back, it's a host bug, filed under this item.")
inl note_of_matrix_split_parts_memo_7 () = Note ("MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO", 7u32, "2026-10-29T08:00Z", "main", "10-29 03:5x main: ROOT CAUSE + FIX + PROOF on a quiet tree. Why the 10-25 done-check replayed 0: Python + Cuda rows were NEVER memoized. test.ps1 runs under Set-StrictMode 3.0 (from env.ps1). Get-OutputParts returns @(...), but PowerShell unrolls a one-element array returned from a function to the bare FileInfo, so for Python's single part (main_auto.py) `$parts.Count` threw 'The property Count cannot be found on this object'. The store loop's catch { } swallowed it before writing <key>.json, while C++/CUDA rows (3 parts, still an array) stored fine (36 .parts dirs, 0 holding main_auto.py). Found with an instrumented one-row copy of test.ps1 (removed after): MEMO-STORE-FAILED for samples/typecheck_long_let_body|Python, status=ok, core=Python + Cuda. FIX (unstaged, one token): test.ps1:340 `$parts = @(Get-OutputParts $job.Output $job.CoreBackend)`. PROOF: run A (-Filter typecheck_long_let_body -Backend Python -ReuseCompile) stores the entry, and its .parts holds main_auto.py byte-equal to the sample's. Run B, with main_auto.py deleted, gives 'compile memo: 1 jobs replayed, 0 to compile' and restores main_auto.py byte-identically; samples dirty 0 afterwards. The fix is outside the native-memo signature (Build-And-Run etc.) and outside the compile-memo key, so no memo entry is invalidated. Also found: the compile-memo key covers every SPIRAL_* variable except SPIRAL_NATIVE_PARALLEL, SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR and SPIRAL_CELLS_*, so SPIRAL_CC, SPIRAL_PYTHON and SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR (which also adds a source root, test.ps1:262) all change keys between shells; compare replays only between identical environments. Done.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_p0_weight_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:04Z", "eoie", "Rule written before measuring: the P0 weight is the smallest power of two w with w*6000/480 >= 256*6000/30, which gives w = 4096 (16x P1). P1..P5 keep their 4x steps (256, 64, 16, 4, 1). Live check on the shared backlog, run before and after with agile next 20: accept only if every ready P0 of 480 min or less ranks above every ready P1 that has no unblocks, and the order among same-priority items is unchanged. Change: priority_weight in agile_task_next/ranking.spi plus its test. The int overflow guard in rank_key already clamps to i32::MAX.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_p0_weight_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", 2u32, "2026-10-27T22:04Z", "eoie", "Draft and preview against the 10-24 rule (2026-10-27 18:0x). The draft is scratchpad clean\\agile-weight\\p0-weight.do: priority_weight becomes a pure Spiral match (P0 4096, P1 256, P2 64, P3 16, P4 4, otherwise 1; the host shift macro is gone). It adds a test for the ask itself (a lone 480-min P0 >= a lone 30-min P1), and the expected orders become GATE BIG-P0 SMALL-P2 KIT-SIDE in ranking.spi and GATE BIG OPS-SIDE SMALL in next_cli.spi. It applied 4/4 with kit do on a scratch package copy. Preview: p0-weight-sim.ps1 replays the ranking from agile list --json on the live backlog. It reproduces the shipped wsjf for 202 of 232 ready items, so it is a preview, not the binding check. The rule's binding check is still agile next 20 before and after on the real binary; the before snapshot is next-before-1810.json. The preview predicts the rule REJECTS the change on both clauses. Clause 1: 4 ready 480-min P0s (XSD-FACET-TYPES, XSD-DECODE-RESULT, FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE, BANG-MACRO-CLEANUP; key 51200-54400) rank under EOIE-DO-APPEND-PARENTS, a 20-min P1 (76800). The 10-24 weight derivation covered a 30-min P1, but the clause says every P1, and estimates go down to 15 min, or below that when set explicitly. Clause 2 can't hold for any P0 weight change: unblocks add the waiter's priority weight, so every item a P0 waits on gains weight and moves within its own priority (the preview shows P0 and P1 orders changing). Outcome by the rule: not adopted, and not queued for a release. I did not fish for a passing weight after seeing the preview. Blocker: the acceptance rule needs a revision written from the ask alone, before the next measurement. Possible shapes: clause 1 as the ask states it (480-min P0 vs 30-min P1, which holds by arithmetic at 4096); clause 2 limited to items with no P0 waiter. Then run that one revised rule against the binary once, and stop.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_p0_weight_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", 3u32, "2026-10-28T01:34Z", "main", "10-27 21:34 main: Revised acceptance rule (main, owner of AGILE-FIELDS-REVIEW; written 10-27 21:3x from the ask, before the binding run). Disclosure: kit's preview showed why the 10-24 rule can't pass, so this revision is not blind to that diagnosis. It narrows each clause back to what the ask states and does not tune any number to the data. Clause 1, the ask itself: on agile next with the real binary, every ready P0 with an estimate of 480 min or less ranks above every ready P1 with an estimate of 30 min or more that unblocks nothing. P1s under 30 min are outside the ask, which compared a 480-min P0 with a 30-min P1. Clause 2, no collateral reordering: among ready items that no P0 waits on, directly or transitively, the relative order within each priority is the same before and after. Items a P0 waits on are exempt, since gaining the waiter's weight is the intended effect. Procedure: one run of agile next (full ready list, not top 20) before and after on the shipped binary. Adopt on both clauses passing; otherwise not adopted and closed as measured. No further weight or rule revision.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_p0_weight_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", 4u32, "2026-10-28T01:38Z", "eoie", "21:3x kit: owner's revised rule (main, AGILE-FIELDS-REVIEW) accepted as the binding rule. Clause 1: every ready P0 at 480 min or less ranks above every ready P1 at 30 min or more that unblocks nothing. Clause 2: same-priority order is unchanged among items no P0 waits on, directly or transitively. One before/after run of the full ready list on the shipped binary; adopt if both pass, else close as measured, no further revision. The draft is in the live tree and rides TRAIN2. priority_weight is a :~ pure-Spiral match: as := it inlined into 2 sites (agile_task_next 983 lines), as :~ 961. The ranking.spi notebook passes 5/5 with the new expectations; next_cli.spi runs in TRAIN2's candidate notebooks. A/B setup: the pre-change installed binary is copied to scratchpad clean\\agile-weight\\kit-before-p0.exe, so after TRAIN2 installs, both binaries run 'agile next <root> 1000 --json' on the same backlog state, and the two clauses are checked mechanically on those two outputs.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_p0_weight_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", 5u32, "2026-10-28T09:29Z", "eoie", "05:2x kit: binding check by the owner's rule (note 4): CLOSED AS MEASURED, not adopted. It ran once, as the rule specifies. Both binaries ran 'agile next <root> 1000 --json' on the same backlog state: the saved pre-change binary (kit-before-p0.exe, C0F2D2F3) and the TRAIN2c binary that shipped the change (A5B55974). 218 ready items each; 72 items have a P0 waiting on them, directly or transitively. The check is scratchpad clean\\agile-weight\\p0-binding-check.ps1, run on next-before.json, next-after.json and tasks-at-check.json. Clause 1 fails with 2 violations: FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE and BANG-MACRO-CLEANUP (P0, 480 min, ranks 27 and 28) sit below DELPHI-STRING-LITERAL-INDEX (P1, 30 min, rank 25). The keys tie exactly (4096*6000/480 = 256*6000/30 = 51200), the tie keeps list order, and the clause asks for 'above'. Clause 2 fails for P0: among items no P0 waits on, the P0 order changed, because unblocks add to the weight, so (4096+u)/m and (1024+u)/m don't preserve order between P0s with different unblocks. As the rule says, no further revision. The change is reverted in the live tree with the inverse program p0-weight-revert.do: git diff of ranking.spi and next_cli.spi is now empty, so both match HEAD again. The revert ships with the next release's regen of agile_task_next. Until then, the installed binary carries the 16x P0 weight.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_p0_weight_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT", 6u32, "2026-11-06T11:29Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-P0-WEIGHT (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_stale_active_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:04Z", "eoie", "Rule written before measuring: list gains --stale <days>, a query filter (one FlagStale case in agile_task_query) that keeps Active items whose newest note, or whose creation if there is none, is older than <days>. Rows add a 'quiet=<n>d' column. Shipped as the named view 'stale' = list Active --stale 3 --sort priority, with a one-call spec case. Acceptance: on the live backlog (201 Active) the view's count matches a recount over agile list --json plus the notes.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_stale_active_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE", 2u32, "2026-10-31T08:30Z", "kit", "10-31 04:2x kit: MEASURED BEFORE BUILDING (the user asked to diagnose management drift since the wipe). The stale-Active view as specified would show nothing today. Over the live backlog's 124 Active items, NONE has a newest note older than 3 days: everything was touched after the 10-28 wipe. The real signal is WIP size. Active per team: kit 43, main 31, compiler 22, fiscal 18, x 6, untagged 3, eoie 1. Many of kit's were 85-95% done and waited only on an install, yet 'agile next' still ranked them first (KIT-AGILE-LOST-UPDATES at P0 sat at #1 while its code was done). Fix applied: 7 install-waiting kit items went Active -> Blocked with a note each, the convention the five other install-waiting items already used. SCOPE CHANGE for this item: keep --stale <days> as specified, and add a per-team WIP count with an install-wait hint, i.e. Active items whose newest note says landed/unstaged/waits on train, so they are proposed for Blocked. Measured with i574n.github/scripts/dangling-census.ps1 plus an inline JSON grouping; dangling-census also found 21 DEPS-DONE items, 5 of them kit's (EOIE-IDIOMATIC-CORE, EOIE-GAP-INTAKE, EOIE-AGILE-REQUESTED, EOIE-TABLE-JSONL, EOIE-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_stale_active_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE", 3u32, "2026-10-31T11:27Z", "kit", "2026-10-31 07:2x kit, main's all-lanes hygiene request (processes, jobs, 10-min backlog box). kit has no tracking item, so this record sits on its WIP item. (1) PROCESSES: every process descended from kit's session (claude pid 12908) is live work: TRAIN5e's release build (job host 8552, supervisor 23228, release-train 15676, build.ps1, kit-dev, cargo/rustc) and its background 'kit job wait'. No leftover shells and no orphans, so nothing stopped. Earlier tonight kit's only duplicate (an unstaged-backup run started at 03:26 while the 03:10 one was alive) was stopped at once. (2) JOBS: box, 2 live: TRAIN5e job-1793441998142 (release phase since 07:05, 140/140 notebooks green) and the queued job-1793442110046 resume-gate3 behind it (it exits 0 on DONE). Windows: only the train's own heavy-build window and heavy-slot-1. Node (remote.ps1 node job list): no kit jobs. Nothing to stop. (3) BACKLOG, done in tonight's grooms plus this box: kit Active 51 -> 35; 8 install-waits -> Blocked with notes (EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS, EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS, EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH, KIT-AGILE-LOST-UPDATES, EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-STDIN, EOIE-NOTEBOOK-WORKERS, EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES, EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS); 7 items silent about 3 days -> Planned (EOIE-EDIT-BATCH, EOIE-USES-LIB, EOIE-EXPERIMENTS, EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD, EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS, EOIE-JOB-CELLS-GATE, EOIE-STATE-SCALE); KIT-EVIDENCE-REFRESH Done (dry-run on a real row); EOIE-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS's stale 0% Planned corrected to 70% Active (its code landed 10-25); EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS vs EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS checked as an overlap, not a duplicate (linked in a note); CLAUDE-HOOKS-IN-REPO filed for main. Field audit: 0 open non-Epic kit items lack estimate, load or assignee. NEXT when TRAIN5e installs: release-acceptance.ps1 on the installed binary, then close the 8 Blocked install-waits.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_stale_active_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE", 4u32, "2026-10-31T16:06Z", "kit", "10-31 12:1x kit: placement constraint, measured before building. 'agile list' rows already carry each item's last note (task_listing.spi:197 last_note_in), so --stale <days> belongs in the LISTING, not in agile_task_query's filter (whose callers, the listing and agile_task_next, would both need a new last-note argument). But agile_task_listing's generated crate is at 988/1000 lines (task_listing.rs), so the filter plus an ISO-minute date parse (days_from_civil over '2026-10-30T23:42Z') can't go inline. Shape: a new pure crate agile_task_staleness exporting stale_days(last_note, now_unix_ms) : i32 (-1 when there is no note) with a test block (leap year, month ends, an empty note), and the listing calls it once per row only when --stale is given, through one host call (same pattern as agile_planning_filters). Value check (n2): today's 124 Active items have no note older than 3 days, so the per-team WIP count matters more; i574n.github/scripts/dangling-census.ps1 already gives it, so this stays at its current rank.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_stale_active_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE", 5u32, "2026-11-01T12:03Z", "kit", "11-01 08:0x kit groom (pre-merge inventory, main is bringing a batch of new items): MERGED into KIT-AGILE-CADENCE (P0, Active 50%), whose typed model already answers this ask (stale_active now hours, node-proven 11-01). Carried over: this item's placement constraint (n4: list rows already carry each item's last note via task_listing last_note_in, so --stale <hours> belongs in the LISTING verb, not a separate pass). Closed here; continue on KIT-AGILE-CADENCE.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_stale_active_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE", 6u32, "2026-11-06T11:29Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_fields_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:04Z", "eoie", "Rule written before measuring: agile check prints one 'eoie agile check warn id=<ID> missing=estimate,load' line per open item and adds 'unfilled=<n>' to its ok line. The exit code stays 0, since a warning never fails check; --strict turns warnings into exit 1 for the Stop hook. Done items are never warned. Acceptance: the live count equals agile list --json filtered by hand (35 Planned without an estimate on 10-24 08:59).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_fields_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Grooming rule for estimates, written before applying: an open non-Epic item with no estimate gets a provisional remaining estimate of base(D) x (1 - progress), floored at 30 and rounded up to 15 minutes. base: D1 30, D2 60, D3 120, D4 240, D5 480, D6 720, D7 1200, D8 1800. Epics stay unestimated because they roll up from their leaves (EOIE-AGILE-LPT-LEAVES). This is a placeholder for the milestone-based sizing in estimates-from-rules, so the next sizing pass replaces it. Each provisional estimate gets a note saying it is one.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_fields_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", 3u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "eoie", "19:44 Load rule, written before applying and following the existing eoie convention: an item is light when its own work is drafting plus scratch notebook checks and it reaches users through the shared release train, which carries the heavy load. It is medium when its own verification repeatedly runs commands or builds (bench). It is heavy only when it holds a heavy slot itself. Applied to 14 eoie items that had no load: EOIE-BENCH-VERB is medium, the rest are light.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_fields_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", 4u32, "2026-10-29T13:12Z", "kit", "10-29 09:1x kit: measured on state/agile.spi (read-only regex over Task and Planning rows): 893 tasks, 704 Planning rows, 248 open (Planned/Active) non-Epic items, 19 missing a field. 18 lack only the estimate and 1 has no Planning row at all; load and assignee are complete everywhere. The 19 (difficulty, gap): BLOG(D5,est), CELLS-NEW-PROJECT(D3,est), CELLS-PROBES(D4,est), CI-OFFLOAD(D5,est), CODESPACES-BUTTON-ALL-REPOS(D3,est), DOTA-FIRST-SCENARIO(D3,est), DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD(D3,est), EDITION3-280-SPLIT(D6,est), FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL(D4,est), KINO-HOT-PATHS(D6,est), LIB-REACTORS(D6,est), LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS(D5,est), NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES(D3,est), NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS(D3,est), NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE(D3,est), NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER(D5,no Planning row), SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR(D7,est), SPIRAL-UI(D7,est), UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST(D6,est). They span other teams, so they go to main's 16:00 grooming with note 2's provisional rule (base(D) x (1 - progress), floor 30, rounded up to 15). The check: no warning exists in agile check today (planning fields are typed in agile_planning_model: Unestimated, LoadUnset, Unassigned). Design: a pure model function, open non-Epic task + its planning -> list of missing field names, with a test; 'agile check' prints one 'warn missing <fields> id=<ID>' line per item and doesn't change the exit code (it's a warning). The first live run must print exactly these 19 unless groomed first. Code after TRAIN3 releases the tree lease.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_fields_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", 5u32, "2026-10-29T16:55Z", "kit", "10-29 12:5x kit: drafted for TRAIN5, uncompiled, in C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\planning-gaps. A new crate agile_planning_gaps (gaps.spi + entry.spi) reuses the two existing parsers instead of a third: agile_task_next's tasks_from/backlog_task (which now carries kind, from LPT-LEAVES) and agile_planning_model's planning_of (unplanned () when there is no Planning row, so an item without a row reports all three fields). For every open (Planned/Active) non-Epic item it prints 'kit agile check warn missing=<estimate,load,assignee> id=<ID>'; it changes no exit code. The test uses rows in the exact on-disk Task/Planning format: an unestimated Story is listed; an item without a row lists all 3 fields; a planned item, an Epic and a Done item are not listed. Cargo deps are those that agile_task_next's parse path reaches through host calls (agile-planning-fields and others). wire-check.do (dry-run clean, 3 replacements) prints the text in check_runtime.spi before 'kit agile check ok', adds the dependency to kit_agile_state and the workspace member. Expected first live output: the 19 gaps from note 4, unless groomed at 16:00.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_fields_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", 6u32, "2026-10-29T19:23Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T19:23 kit: agile_planning_gaps crate proven at pack-6b. Node job job-1793301727556-kit-gaps-compile-6b ran under remote.ps1 -RequireCore 31CC96520D6B7269 (L6 guard ok). node-regen-compare now resolves the node's installed compiler and printed core 31CC96520D6B7269: agile_planning_gaps 640 lines, agile_note_listing 489, 0 over 1000, 0 compile failures, 0 missing backend arms. This replaces the 17:29Z proof, which ran on the staged pre-6b DLL A4E480202BD4. Wiring (wire-check.do) is still dry-run only; it lands in TRAIN5 with cargo check + notebook.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_fields_7 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", 7u32, "2026-10-30T01:59Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T01:59 kit groom: 0% Planned -> 50% Active: the agile_planning_gaps crate is built and proven on the node at core 31CC9652 (n6: 640 lines, 0 failures, 0 missing arms) and the wiring (wire-check.do) is dry-run green; both ride TRAIN5 (deps EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN). Done = 'kit agile check' prints the planning gaps on the installed binary.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_fields_8 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", 8u32, "2026-10-31T09:43Z", "kit", "10-31 05:4x kit groom: Active -> Blocked (install wait). The wiring is in the live tree (agile_state_runtime/check_runtime.spi:88 calls agile_planning_gaps::kit_agile_planning_gaps_text before 'kit agile check ok') and in TRAIN5e's 00:31 snapshot. The gaps notebook passed earlier (job kit-nb-gaps). TRAIN5e resumed at 05:35 after its 5 h cap. Done = 'kit agile check' on the installed binary prints the warn lines.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_fields_9 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", 9u32, "2026-10-31T12:10Z", "kit", "10-31 07:4x kit: DONE (installed-is-done). Installed E14F1F0B. Positive control on a scratch backlog: 'kit agile check' printed 'warn missing=estimate,load,assignee id=PROBE-GAP' and nothing for the fully planned PROBE-FULL. On the live backlog it prints 0 warn lines, which is correct, because every open item has its fields after tonight's grooms.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_check_fields_10 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS", 10u32, "2026-11-06T11:29Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_lpt_leaves_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-LPT-LEAVES", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:04Z", "eoie", "Ask 2 measured on 10-24 09:0x. Paused and Blocked are already excluded: agile_status_open is true only for Planned and Active, and TIBIA, the #1 row, is Active. The team filter already exists (--team main gives 126 ready instead of 218), so the view 'subagent-main' now stores it. What is left: epics top LPT by size (TIBIA 9600m, an Epic). Rule written before measuring: in the Subagent role, an Epic is not a candidate. In the MainAgent role nothing changes. Test: the sample backlog gains an Epic, and the subagent order skips it.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_lpt_leaves_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-LPT-LEAVES", 2u32, "2026-10-29T13:00Z", "kit", "10-29 09:0x kit: drafted (not compiled) as C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\lpt-leaves\\lpt-leaves.do, following note 1's rule: in the Subagent role an Epic is not a candidate; MainAgent is unchanged. The Task tuple's field 2 is the kind (checked on agile.spi: 59 Epic, 88 Feature, 364 Story, 382 TaskKind). Changes: backlog_task gains kind (task_next.spi reads field 2); a new role_takes (role) (task) says Subagent takes everything except an Epic; candidate takes the role, so ranked_from passes it; task_at defaults kind to Story, so existing tests keep their shape; a new test subagents_take_leaf_work_and_never_an_epic puts an Active 9600 m Epic on top of the sample backlog and expects the Subagent order to stay 'BIG-P0 GATE SMALL-P2', while MainAgent still takes it. Only agile_task_next constructs backlog_task. The task_next.spi anchor matches once in the live tree and once in merged-m5's version, so it applies before or after the m5 restore. Applies after TRAIN3 releases the tree lease; module notebook ranking.spi, then rides TRAIN4.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_lpt_leaves_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-LPT-LEAVES", 3u32, "2026-10-29T14:52Z", "kit", "10-29 10:5x kit: the draft compiles. A node compare (job-1793285455384, P2 DLL pinned, -RequireCore 05E94769A86A9B9C) put the lpt-leaves.do edits on a live-src copy: agile_task_next goes 933 -> 954 generated lines (under the 1000 gate), 0 compile failures; the agile_note_listing control is identical. Not yet run: the new test block (subagents_take_leaf_work_and_never_an_epic) and cargo check. Both run when it's applied after TRAIN3 (module notebook ranking.spi).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_lpt_leaves_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-LPT-LEAVES", 4u32, "2026-10-29T22:41Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:40 kit: DONE on installed kit C528BE4D (TRAIN4c). agile next --as subagent returns 0 of the 59 epics (closeout PASS; CD2F0D1A returned 16); the ranking.spi notebook (subagents_take_leaf_work_and_never_an_epic) is green.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_lpt_leaves_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-LPT-LEAVES", 5u32, "2026-11-06T11:29Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-LPT-LEAVES (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_convert_zig_unativeint_1 () = Note ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T18:32Z", "main", "Found 2026-10-24 14:1x by the xml heal probe (main.zig: expected type 'usize', found 'i32' at (v0)[v8] where v8 = convert.convert i). Workaround in the xml slice: index with conv to u64 (Zig coerces u64 to usize). Milestones: a Zig test of convert.convert i32 -> unativeint indexing a string 20 m; the Zig arm using the backend conversion for number targets 25 m; matrix/Cells check 15 m.")
inl note_of_convert_zig_unativeint_2 () = Note ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", 2u32, "2026-10-25T15:07Z", "main", "10-25 11:07 main: cause read: convert.to is a bare retag ('$'!x '') on every backend except Rust, and numeric_conv handles only primitive sources, so unativeint (a :! nominal, usize on Zig) falls through to the retag in both directions, and Zig rejects the uncast assignment (seen again today in LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF gate 2). Slice %TEMP%\\lib-convert-zig-native\\apply.ps1 (dry run 1/1): try_convert's Zig arm computes native = t or u is unativeint (compile-time typecase) and emits '@intCast(x)' then; Zig takes the result type from the typed destination, and every other conversion is unchanged. Test a_native_index_converts_both_ways (rust, zig): i32 -> unativeint -> i32. Gate + land: job-1792940867731-main-serial-convert-zig (after the zig index_of chain).")
inl note_of_convert_zig_unativeint_3 () = Note ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", 3u32, "2026-10-30T01:59Z", "main", "10-29 21:59 main, groom (WIP rule, as in the 10-23 and 10-27 grooms): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 60%. No note since 10-25 and no live job; main carries 69 Actives, which is not real WIP. Resume when picked; if its pass already shipped in an installed core, close it Done with that evidence instead. Compiler/lib work: per lane-roles, the team that needs it (likely the compiler team, git-a6) resumes it.")
inl note_of_convert_zig_unativeint_4 () = Note ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", 4u32, "2026-10-31T21:33Z", "main", "10-31 17:33 main, read from the tree (lib/spiral/convert.spi), the fix is now exact: try_convert's Zig arm (convert.spi:339-344) handles strings, then falls through to 'x |> to |> Some' (:344), and 'to' is a type-level retag that emits NO conversion, so an i32 retagged as unativeint stays i32 in the generated Zig where usize is needed. The lib already has the right tool: numeric_conv (convert.spi:60-76) routes a numeric source through core 'conv t u x' (a real emitted conversion) and only retags otherwise; convert's own Zig arms use it (:439-518, e.g. 'numeric_conv x : u8'). FIX: in :344, when BOTH t and u are numeric, use 'numeric_conv x |> Some : _ u' instead of 'to'; and add nativeint/unativeint to numeric_conv's source list (:63-74, today i8..u64 and f32/f64 only) so a unativeint source converts too. PROOF: a '///> zig' test converting 3i32 to unativeint and back, plus the existing convert tests on zig; land with lib_land_window (close: signatures, document_test, type_probe). ~45 min.")
inl note_of_convert_zig_unativeint_5 () = Note ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", 5u32, "2026-11-01T06:21Z", "main", "main 02:3x DRAFT READY, corrects n4's plan. unativeint is a ':!' nominal over a backend_switch type (convert.spi:5-19; usize on Zig/Rust), and core 'conv' converts primitives only, so adding it to numeric_conv's source list (n4) would fail to compile. The draft (main scratchpad apply15-zig-unativeint.ps1, applied to a scratch lib under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\scratch\\zig-unativeint, formatter --check clean): try_convert's Zig arm (:339-344) keeps the string path; when t or u is unativeint it emits Some (\\$'@intCast(!x)' : u) (Zig infers the target from the typed binding); otherwise 'x |> numeric_conv |> Some', so numeric sources really convert instead of the bare 'to' retag. Test added before ':= convert': ///> zig, ///> rust, unativeint_round_trip_casts_on_native_backends (3i32 -> unativeint -> i32). PROOF PENDING: the box had 563 MB free with compiler's pack12a heavy build waiting, so no box compile now. Next: lib_gate -File convert.spi on the scratch lib (zig + rust rows plus the existing tests) when memory allows, then lib_land_window.")
inl note_of_convert_zig_unativeint_6 () = Note ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", 6u32, "2026-11-01T06:53Z", "main", "main 02:5x: node proof 1 (job-1793514783221) compiled the draft and failed with type errors: macro text reads '@intCast' as a type splice ('Unbound type variable: intCast'), which also took down the file's other 3 tests. Fixed in the draft as '@@intCast' (the compiler's own hint; lib elsewhere writes '\\@intCast' in convert.spi's base-parse arm). Re-pushed to the node scratch lib and requeued as job-1793515945856 (proof2), with the LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED reruns chained after it (one at a time: the first two reruns started together and both died replacing the node's shared lib-gate priv link, which is Administrators-owned from 10-25 and points into the stale shared checkout; see NODE-SHARED-CHECKOUT-STALE).")
inl note_of_convert_zig_unativeint_7 () = Note ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", 7u32, "2026-11-01T12:38Z", "main", "main 08:3x PROVEN on the node (job-1793536456739, proof3): convert.spi with the draft passes 4 of 4 tests (try_convert, try_convert_2, try_convert_reads_narrow_integers_natively, and the new unativeint_round_trip_casts_on_native_backends on zig + rust), 0 type errors. Fixes on the way: '@@intCast' (proof1's type-splice error), and the test calls '3i32 |> try_convert |> optionm.value', because 'convert 3i32' inside a test cell resolves 'convert' as the module name (ETY-ExpectedSymbolAsModuleKey, proof2). The final draft is the main scratchpad apply15-zig-unativeint.ps1 (formatter-clean). NEXT: land on the live lib with lib_land_window (close = signatures + document_test + type_probe) after the groom round.")
inl note_of_convert_zig_unativeint_8 () = Note ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", 8u32, "2026-11-01T12:42Z", "main", "main 08:4x LANDED and closed green (lib_land_window): convert.spi 36C910747663 (base 33B8A857CF74); 14 consumer signatures unchanged, document_test 22/22, type_probe PASS (4 tests typed, 0 errors). try_convert's Zig arm casts unativeint with @@intCast and routes numeric sources through numeric_conv instead of a bare retag; the new zig+rust round-trip test passes. Undo: lib-land-undo\\20261101-083924. Done.")
inl note_of_convert_zig_unativeint_9 () = Note ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", 9u32, "2026-11-01T15:18Z", "main", "main 11:1x REOPENED: the 08:39 land broke the spiral CLI build in CI (apps/spiral/build.ps1:27 'Cannot convert i64 to the following type: time_span'; tibia's CI on 8c8f6c3, root cause by compiler). Cause: backend_switch type-checks every arm on every backend, so the Zig arm's numeric_conv (used whenever t is numeric) ran on Rust builds too, and apps/spiral converts i64 to the nominal time_span, which core conv can't target. The land's gates missed it because the 14 consumer signatures and document_test don't cover apps/spiral. FIX (compiler is landing it under ZIG-EXPECTED-SEMICOLON-REGRESSION): numeric_conv only when both t and u are primitive numerics, @@intCast when either side is unativeint, otherwise the old 'to' retag; and add apps/spiral to lib_consumer_signatures. LESSON for lib lands: a backend_switch arm change is a change on every backend; run the consumer builds (apps/spiral, x, tibia, fiscal) or their signatures, not only lib's own tests.")
inl note_of_convert_zig_unativeint_10 () = Note ("CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT", 10u32, "2026-11-01T15:37Z", "main", "main 11:3x CLOSED again, with the full damage on record. compiler's guard landed 11:34 (convert.spi 36C9107476637EBF -> 9035FB80D406E22D): numeric_conv converts only when both t and u are primitive numerics, otherwise it retags; the @@intCast unativeint arm is unchanged; convert 4/4 including the round-trip test. Damage the 08:39 land caused and its gates missed: the spiral CLI build (apps/spiral, CI red for tibia) and date_time.spi 33 of 44 tests failing on the live lib (43/43 after the guard). Why the gates missed it: a consumer signature row can't catch it, because try_convert's signature never changed; it breaks only when a consumer specializes it. compiler added a 'consumers' close step to lib_land_window.ps1 (-Consumer, default apps/spiral/spiral.spi) that compiles the consumer on Rust against the just-landed lib and undoes the land on failure. CI goes green after the user pushes spiral (convert.spi + lib_land_window.ps1).")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_1 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 1u32, "2026-10-24T20:57Z", "main", "main 17:0x design, from the parser at spiral_compiler.fs ~178521 (config: an FParsec record of name, version, moduleDir, modules, packageDir, packages, default-int, default-float, edition, crates). Schema: edition = 3; name = 'x'; version = '...'; moduleDir; packageDir = '../../../spiral/lib'; packages = ['|core-', 'spiral-'] (the spiproj spellings kept as strings, so | = compiler dir and a trailing - = include stay one rule); default-int, default-float; crates = [...]; modules = a TOML multi-line literal string holding the existing indentation hierarchy (dir/, -, *, *- flags), parsed by the same file_hierarchy parser, so module order and flags need no new encoding. Reader: a small TOML subset parser (key = string | int | string array | multi-line literal) that maps onto RawSchema, with ranges shifted to the toml line/column so diagnostics point into spiral.toml. Lookup: the 3 package.spiproj sites (~182587, ~183219 spiproj_suffix, ~183564) try spiral.toml first. Milestones: TOML subset reader + RawSchema mapping 90 m; lookup at the 3 sites + both-file conflict error 30 m; converter verb 'format --manifest toml <dirs>' (writes spiral.toml, deletes nothing; the user removes the spiproj) 45 m; proof: every converted package compiles to byte-identical residuals 30 m; pack review 45 m. Upstream: core packages keep package.spiproj; mixing is per directory, like editions.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_2 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 2u32, "2026-10-24T21:04Z", "main", "main 17:1x: milestones 1-2 drafted as core slice %TEMP%\\pack-toml\\spiral-toml.ps1 (4 anchors, each 1 match on the tree core BEECFE432427C57A): tomlManifestText + isTomlManifest before config, and config translates TOML text before parsing; spiproj_suffix prefers spiral.toml, so discovery, reads, diagnostics URIs and watchers follow; both edition probes (formatter editionOf, xCompatPackageEditionThree) read spiral.toml with edition = 2|3. fsc --parseonly passes under SINGLE_FLIGHT and HOPACSOTA. Converter draft %TEMP%\\pack-toml\\to-toml.ps1 (spiproj -> toml: strings, ints, packages/crates arrays, modules as a ''' literal tree). Translator check in fsi (%TEMP%\\pack-toml\\test\\check.fsx) on 4 real manifests (x apps package, lib, 2 eoie src packages): each TOML is detected and translates back to its spiproj text (blank lines ignored), and no spiproj is detected as TOML. Known v1 gap: diagnostics line numbers inside spiral.toml shift where a one-line array expands; a range map is milestone 1b. Next: typecheck/build with the next pack (after 5i's review), then the converter as the compiler verb, then a residual-identity proof per converted package.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_3 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 3u32, "2026-10-24T21:31Z", "main", "main 17:3x: milestone 3 drafted in the same slice (5th anchor, 1 match): 'format --manifest toml <dirs>' writes a spiral.toml beside every package.spiproj under the dirs (generated dirs and links skipped). It keeps an existing spiral.toml, never deletes the spiproj, and writes only after a read-back check: tomlManifestText of the new file must give the spiproj's significant lines, with inline list items such as 'packages: core-' moved below their key, as the spiproj parser treats them. Exit 2 lists refusals (a value holding a quote). Measured in fsi with the slice's own functions over every tracked package.spiproj in 9 repos: 1,643 manifests, 1,643 read back, 0 differ, 0 refused. The first pass had 238 differing, all inline lists that the first converter dropped (fixed). Positive control: dropping one module line from a TOML makes it read back false. fsc --parseonly passes under both defines. Next: build it with the pack after 5i, then convert one consumer (x's apps package), then the residual-identity proof, then the rest.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_4 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 4u32, "2026-10-27T00:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:49 fiscal (recording for main, whose backlog writes are leased during its 21:00 wrap): the converter draft %TEMP%/pack-toml/to-toml.ps1 wrote CRLF (line 29 [IO.File]::WriteAllLines uses the OS newline), which land.ps1 would refuse in an LF tree. Found by fiscal comparing it with a generator-emitted spiral.toml (113 vs 108 bytes, equal after CRLF -> LF). Main fixed it 10-26 ~20:48: WriteAllText with LF joins, a final LF, UTF-8 without BOM. Re-verified by fiscal on generated_cfdi_v4_0/package.spiproj: 108 bytes, 0 CR, no BOM, byte-identical to fiscal's generator_toml_transform output. Open: the F# 'format --manifest toml' path is not drafted yet and must follow the same LF rule.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_5 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 5u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "main", "10-29 22:00 main, groom (WIP rule, 3 days with no note): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 45%. Last note 2026-10-27, before the 10-28 profile wipe interrupted every lane, and no live job. Resume when picked; it is not abandoned, and it keeps its priority P0.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_6 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 6u32, "2026-10-30T02:05Z", "main", "10-29 22:05 main, groom follow-up: paused tonight by the WIP rule, but an ACTIVE item depends on it: FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND (fiscal, Active). Resume it FIRST among the paused ones, since it gates live work.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_7 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 7u32, "2026-10-30T02:05Z", "main", "10-29 22:05 main: HANDED TO FISCAL (lane-roles: the lane that needs it drives it). FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND can start right after next session's first window, and its first step is spiral.toml, so this item will soon gate live fiscal work. fiscal sets assignee/team and starts by reading its dep EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION (main's, 65%, Paused); if spiral.toml can't move without that, main and fiscal decide who drives it.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_8 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 8u32, "2026-10-30T02:06Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 22:06 fiscal: TAKEN OVER by fiscal (main 10-29 22:0x: 'fiscal takes EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML'; fiscal is the lane that needs it next, for FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND). Team -> fiscal, Paused -> Planned at 45% (no work tonight). Starts after next session's first lib window. Dependency EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION stays main's; fiscal reads it first and tells main if spiral.toml can't move without it.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_9 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 9u32, "2026-10-30T02:06Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 22:06 fiscal: DEPENDENCY CYCLE REMOVED. EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML depended on EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION, which lists the per-repo rounds as its deps (FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND among them), and FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND depends on EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML: TOML -> MIGRATION -> ROUND -> TOML. The migration is the umbrella that finishes after every round; spiral.toml is a prerequisite those rounds use (the compiler reads spiral.toml first). The edge pointed backwards, so it's dropped (deps now none). The design input from SPIRAL3-EDITIONS note 10 (spiral.toml picked) is already decided, and milestones 1-3 are drafted (core slice + converter, notes of 10-24), so nothing upstream gates it. EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION stays main's.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_10 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 10u32, "2026-10-30T02:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 22:21 fiscal: STATE RE-READ (main's %TEMP%/pack-toml drafts were lost in the 10-28 wipe; absent from rescue-2026-10-28 and the node profile-mirror). Milestones 1-3 are nonetheless LANDED in apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs: tomlManifestText (l.179757), isTomlManifest + config translation (l.179805-179813), spiproj_suffix preferring spiral.toml (l.184645), and the converter manifestToml behind 'format --manifest toml <dirs>' (l.184009-184041: keeps an existing spiral.toml, never deletes the spiproj, writes only after a read-back check). They are in the pack-6b DLL too: a NODE DRY RUN (scratch copy, toolchain dotnet rc.3 at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\toolchains\\dotnet) converted fiscal's src/package.spiproj into a 129-byte LF spiral.toml, exit 0, read-back passed. Findings: (a) the output carries no 'edition = 3' line when the spiproj has no edition key; (b) generated dirs are skipped by design, and fiscal's generator emits a package.spiproj per generated module (module_package), so the generator must emit spiral.toml itself. Remaining here: prove a spiral.toml-only package compiles (spiproj removed) on node, and decide the edition-key rule; per-repo migration happens in each round. Progress 45 -> 75.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_11 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 11u32, "2026-10-30T02:35Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 22:35 fiscal: CELLS GAP FOUND (fiscal-6b-toml-only-proof, job-1793327138360, mirror tomlp: fiscal src with spiral.toml only, spiproj moved aside): the compiler side is fine (type errors 0), but Cells refuses the package: '** (Mix) .../fiscal/src/corpus.spi: Spiral package was not found at .../fiscal/src' (cells.ex:1114-1115, package_project? at l.1138), so the test shows not_run. Census: 'package.spiproj' is hardcoded at ~25 sites in apps/cells/lib across 6 modules: cells.ex (753, 835, 1075, 1114, 1138), memo.ex (290, 348, 582), mounts.ex (62, 114-115, 163-171, 285, 300, 318, 366), notebook.ex (1260-1274), notebook_cli.ex (75), x_compat.ex (228); mix/tasks/spiral.new.ex scaffolds a spiproj. mounts.ex 62/163 READ AND REWRITE the manifest text (packageDir for shadow/patched mounts), and memo.ex hashes it. So spiral.toml support in Cells needs (1) one manifest_path(dir) resolver preferring spiral.toml, used at every site, (2) a TOML-aware packageDir rewrite in mounts.ex (or rewriting via the compiler's tomlManifestText equivalent), (3) memo keys over whichever manifest exists. Until then a converted package can't be gated through Cells, so per-repo migration must keep the spiproj beside the toml (the converter already does). Progress 75 -> 60 (scope grew).")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_12 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 12u32, "2026-10-30T02:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 22:51 fiscal: CELLS DESIGN (from reading mounts.ex 62-71, 161-176, 298-330 and the n11 census): every Cells reader parses spiproj-format text (Regex '^packageDir:', package_entries, 'crates:' blocks, local_package_names), and shadow mounts rewrite it (project_for_mount) into the link. Least invasive support: (1) Spiral.Cells.Manifest.text(dir) returns spiproj-format text, read from package.spiproj if present, else translated from spiral.toml by an Elixir port of the compiler's tomlManifestText (spiral_compiler.fs:179757, ~45 lines: key = 'x' -> 'key: x', ['a','b'] -> 'key:' + indented items, ''' blocks -> indented lines), so every existing parser keeps working unchanged; (2) Manifest.exists?(dir) replaces File.regular?(...package.spiproj) at the existence checks (cells.ex 1114/1138, notebook.ex 1260-1274, mounts.ex 366, notebook_cli.ex 75); (3) shadow keeps writing package.spiproj into the link (the compiler reads either; spiproj_suffix prefers toml only when present) and skips BOTH manifest names when linking (mounts.ex 171, 285); (4) memo keys hash Manifest.text, so a toml edit invalidates memos the same way. Test: the toml-only fiscal src (tomlp) gates corpus.spi green. Box compiles of cells wait for compile-quiet to end; node-testable meanwhile.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_13 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 13u32, "2026-10-30T03:02Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 23:02 fiscal: CELLS SUPPORT DRAFTED + PROVEN on the toml-only package. Draft (scratch cells-toml, backup C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\cells-toml): new apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/manifest.ex (Spiral.Cells.Manifest: names/0, path/1 preferring spiral.toml like the compiler's spiproj_suffix, exists?/1, read/1 + read!/1 returning spiproj-format text, from_toml/1 = Elixir port of tomlManifestText), plus 22 call sites in cells.ex (source_mtime, mount existence, package_project?), memo.ex (3 reads), mounts.ex (share_mounted_deps?, shadow read and skip-both-names, mtime stamps, source?, declared_dependencies/crates, dependency existence), notebook.ex (3 lib-root checks), notebook_cli.ex (probe header), x_compat.ex (nearest_project now returns the dir, and the edition check reads Manifest text, so a toml 'edition = 3' counts). Left on purpose: the core package check, the cell's own generated spiproj, the shadow link's written spiproj, the scaffold doc. PROOF: fiscal-p7-toml-cells-proof (job-1793329018212, pack 7, mirror tomlp: fiscal src with spiral.toml only): corpus.spi 1/1 passed, type errors 0 (before the draft: 'Spiral package was not found'). Regression on a spiproj package (lib schematron.spi) running: fiscal-p7-toml-cells-regress.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_14 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 14u32, "2026-10-30T03:11Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 23:11 fiscal: CELLS REGRESSION HOLDS: fiscal-p7-toml-cells-regress (job-1793329027753, pack 7, mirror tomlp with the draft Cells): lib/spiral/schematron.spi, a spiproj package, 3/3 passed, type errors 0. So the draft serves both manifest kinds. Held program fiscal/lib-programs/cells-spiral-toml-manifest.kit (root apps/cells: writes lib/spiral/cells/manifest.ex + test/spiral/cells/manifest_test.exs, patches 6 modules with 17 hunks), rehearsed hash-equal on a fresh copy of apps/cells (0 mismatches over 8 files). Cells unit tests (manifest + mounts x3 + memo x2 + x_compat + notebook_spiral_lib) running: fiscal-p7-cells-toml-unit.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_15 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 15u32, "2026-10-30T03:38Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 23:38 fiscal: CELLS UNIT TESTS on the draft (fiscal-p7-cells-toml-unit3, job-1793330297738, node mirror tomlp): 45/48 passed, including the 3 new ManifestTest cases. The 3 failures are all Spiral.Cells.MemoTest cases timing out at ExUnit's 60 s limit (memo_test.exs:30, :179, :261; each compiles a project), on a node saturated by set-ublj, the M6 proof and other lanes. That's the same 60 s load-timeout pattern x hit in verify30, but memo.ex is one of the patched modules, so it is NOT yet cleared: A/A control queued, memo_test.exs on unpatched Cells (p7dbl) then patched (tomlp) back to back: fiscal-p7-memo-AA-base -> fiscal-p7-memo-AB-toml. Pass on both = load; fail only on tomlp = a regression in the draft.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_16 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 16u32, "2026-10-30T03:43Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 23:43 fiscal: A/A CLEARS THE CELLS DRAFT: memo_test.exs passes 26/26 on unpatched Cells (fiscal-p7-memo-AA-base, p7dbl, job-1793331533673) AND 26/26 on the draft (fiscal-p7-memo-AB-toml, tomlp, job-1793331541594), so the 3 timeouts in the 8-file run were node load, not the draft. Held program cells-spiral-toml-manifest.kit is PROVEN: toml-only package gates green, spiproj package gates green, Cells unit tests green (ManifestTest 3/3 + mounts/memo/x_compat/notebook lib). Needs a window that touches apps/cells (offered to main).")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_17 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 17u32, "2026-10-30T03:55Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 23:55 fiscal: CELLS PROGRAM LANDED in main's Cells window (23:54, after the lib close; Cells reader check clean; positive control by main: the old Cells failed the toml-only gate with 'Spiral package was not found'). All 8 at target: cells.ex 1CE33272, memo.ex BABC5167, mounts.ex 85B259B5, notebook.ex 11296BE2, notebook_cli.ex AAA3A024, x_compat.ex 91E1417B, new manifest.ex D73B3524, new manifest_test.exs 91BC7680. Undo: fiscal-drafts/cells-toml/undo-20261029-235413. The lander exited 1 because the guard's dry pass did not neutralize 'write' (only 'edit' gets dry-run), so the writes happened in the dry pass and the apply pass refused them as existing; the end state is byte-exact. Guard fixed in fiscal's supervisor copy (dry pass strips write blocks), and main told. Main's close: mix test + document_test + lib_gate (schematron spiproj, toml-only corpus.spi with FISCAL_ROOT).")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_18 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 18u32, "2026-10-30T04:05Z", "main", "10-30 00:05 main: CELLS WINDOW closed GREEN. fiscal's cells-spiral-toml-manifest.kit landed 8 files in apps/cells at their rehearsed targets (undo: C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\cells-toml\\undo-20261029-235413). BEFORE/AFTER control: the spiral.toml-only package (fiscal src, no package.spiproj) FAILED on the old Cells with 'Spiral package was not found', read from the gate's -RawOut (lib_gate's printed summary hides it), and PASSES 1/1 on the new Cells. spiproj lib package schematron 3/3 (unaffected); document_test 22. mix test 322/324: the 2 failures (PostabilityProfileTest lines 24/32) are pre-existing x.json fixture drift, independent of this diff (line 24 calls rewrites with no path), routed to x. CORRECTION (10-30 00:29, x): the 2 PostabilityProfileTest failures were NOT fixture drift. The fixture is byte-identical to x/postability/x.json (CD95B0C1D176); the TEST's premise was stale. x's profile has a posting.defused_dot rule that the built-in default deliberately lacks (x_compat_test.exs:53), so 'x's profile == default' can't hold. x rewrote the tests (test-only, postability_profile_test.exs, unstaged): postability + x_compat 8/8. They were still independent of fiscal's Cells change.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_19 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 19u32, "2026-10-30T04:06Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 00:06 fiscal: CELLS WINDOW CLOSED GREEN (main, 00:0x): before/after proof on the box: the toml-only corpus.spi gate went from 'Spiral package was not found' (old Cells) to 1/1 passed (new Cells); spiproj schematron 3/3; document_test 22; mix test 322/324, the 2 failures being x's pre-existing postability-profile fixture drift, independent of this diff (routed to x). Cells now reads spiral.toml (prefers it, like the compiler's spiproj_suffix). The compiler side (reader, discovery, converter 'format --manifest toml') was already landed. Remaining for this story: per-repo migration (each repo's edition-3 round runs the converter; the converter keeps the spiproj, so packages stay readable either way) and the edition-key rule (the converter writes no 'edition = 3' when the spiproj has none). Staging package archived (junctions unlinked first, targets verified intact): fiscal-drafts/archive/20261030-toml-only-cells-gate. Progress 80 -> 90.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_20 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 20u32, "2026-10-30T04:24Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 00:24 fiscal: EDITION-KEY RULE SETTLED, no converter change needed: the converter carries the edition when the spiproj has one. Node check (pack 7) on a copy of fiscal's edition-3 spiproj ('edition: 3' first line): 'format --manifest toml' wrote a spiral.toml starting 'edition = 3', with the same packageDir/packages/modules. Rule for every round: add 'edition: 3' to the spiproj (x's pattern), then convert. All design questions here are answered; the only remaining work is each repo running the converter in its own edition-3 round (fiscal's FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND, x/tibia/eoie/lib rounds). Progress 90 -> 95; closes when the per-repo conversions are done or are moved to the rounds.")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_21 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 21u32, "2026-10-31T01:35Z", "compiler", "10-30 21:35 compiler: CLOSED by the item's own rule (n20: 'closes when the per-repo conversions are done or are moved to the rounds'; R4, the rule answers it). Checked: every edition-3 round names the converter step (X-EDITION3-RESPELL, TIBIA-EDITION3-ROUND, FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND, EOIE-EDITION3-ROUND, LIB-EDITION3-ROUND all mention spiral.toml / format --manifest toml). On disk: tibia 7 spiral.toml for 7 package.spiproj (converted); fiscal 0/33 (its round is open at 95%); eoie/lib rounds Planned. ONE GAP, routed to x: X-EDITION3-RESPELL is Done, and its 10-26 reopening planned 'spiral.toml for its 2 package.spiproj', but C:\\home\\git\\x has 0 spiral.toml and 2 package.spiproj today (noted on that item, x told). The compiler, Cells and converter sides are all landed and proven (n17-n20).")
inl note_of_edition3_spiral_toml_22 () = Note ("EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML", 22u32, "2026-10-31T02:00Z", "main", "10-30 22:00 main: the modules encoding chosen in n1 (the spiproj tree DSL inside a ''' string) is under review in EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL (user question). The per-repo conversions are mechanical and keep the spiproj, so they are reversible, but converting the remaining manifests is cheapest after that item decides the schema.")
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_1 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "x", "10-24 19:44 x: found by job-1792875692136-x-verify-landing4 (exit 1 at apps/sim/build.ps1 -fast 1 -Accept, before e2e ran). lib sm'.spi:737 spiral_sm_find_trailing (landed 18:06 by programs-regexp-directive) takes direction: atom.Atom and returns Dynamic. Two gaps: (1) apps/sim/frames/gleam.toml has only gleam_stdlib, so 'import gleam/erlang/atom' finds no package; add gleam_erlang (newest 1.x, check release first). (2) gleam-modules.ps1's split put spiral_sm_find_trailing in part1.gleam, whose imports carry gleam/erlang/atom but not gleam/dynamic.{type Dynamic}; the split must copy every import a moved function uses. Fix both in x, then rerun verify_x_after_e2e_fix.ps1 as verify-landing5. Blocks X-THREAD-RESUME, X-READ-BUDGET and X-LOOKUP-LIMITS closing (their e2e never ran).")
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_2 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "x", "10-24 19:45 x: cause of gap (1): X-DEPS-LATEST's frames step (n10, in HEAD 6ef9c48) removed gleam_erlang from apps/sim/frames/gleam.toml. That was right then, since frames imported only bit_array/int/list/order/string. n10 also predicted lib code would bring it back at lib's 1.3 pins, and sm'.find_trailing's atom arm now does. So: re-add gleam_erlang at the version lib/Cells resolve (read spiral/apps/cells manifest.toml, don't guess), then gleam deps update. Keep gleam_stdlib >= 1.0.5 < 2.0.0.")
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_3 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 3u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "x", "10-24 19:45 x: version source found. Cells declares gleam_erlang '>=1.3.0 and <2.0.0' in spiral/apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/targets.ex:344, per test, not in Cells' manifest.toml. Its manifest resolves only gleam_stdlib 1.0.5. So frames gets gleam_erlang = '>= 1.3.0 and < 2.0.0' to match, and the Cells gate on sm' passing never covered frames' own toml. Positive control after the fix: gleam build in apps/sim/frames must compile part1.gleam's spiral_sm_find_trailing.")
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_4 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 4u32, "2026-10-25T00:02Z", "x", "10-24 20:02 x: scope note (user 20:0x). This is the interim fix that unblocks verify-landing5 (gleam_erlang dep plus the parts script's import copy). It is not the direction. The direction is SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES (reopened) and XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH (P1): real packages that make the parts script unnecessary.")
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_5 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 5u32, "2026-10-25T01:12Z", "x", "10-24 21:12 x: APPLIED (unstaged): apps/sim/frames/gleam.toml gains gleam_erlang = '>= 1.3.0 and < 2.0.0' (permanent). scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 Get-UsedImports now splits 'path.{names}', so the alias is the last path segment and an import is kept when the text uses 'alias.' or any unqualified name, 'type ' stripped ('Dynamic' from 'gleam/dynamic.{type Dynamic}'). Unit-checked on both import shapes. That fix dies with the script (X-PARALLEL-BUILD). Gate: job-1792890731500-x-verify-landing5 (heavy, after main's edition-3 pass2; sim fixtures -Accept, then client regen, gate, x-compat, release, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_6 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 6u32, "2026-10-25T01:40Z", "x", "10-24 21:40 x: verify-landing5 (job-1792890731500) proved the stopgap: gleam build compiled, sim fixtures accepted (prelude.events/final: only prelude.spi's own source text changed, as expected). It then failed mix test 44/45: 'results assigned to whoever asked, without cancelling, surface stale revisions'. Root cause, by elimination: (a) the Spiral invariant is correct, since hand-built rows give no_stale_results=FAIL[p0]; (b) keys are sha256 of the source, so ordering is lib-independent; (c) the test's slow executor slept 80 ms against the runner's 100 ms harvest window, so on a 100%-CPU box both results landed in one batch and the stale one never surfaced. Fix: sleep 250 ms (apps/sim/test/xsim_test.exs), 3/3 pass, and the second assert (cancel on -> :ok) still guards the other direction. Rerun inside job-1792892406819-x-verify-landing6.")
inl note_of_x_sim_frames_gleam_imports_7 () = Note ("X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", 7u32, "2026-10-28T00:14Z", "x", "2026-10-27 20:14 x: closed on today's evidence. The 10-24 break (a lib sm' landing broke apps/sim/frames' Gleam build) is gone: quick check 4 (job-1793112246184) printed 'GLEAM-COMPILED x/apps/sim/frames' (8,926 generated lines, 13 modules) and ExUnit 45/45, and every sim accept since (verify17, 19, 20, 21b) built frames and passed 45/45. Nothing was left to do on this item; it was never closed.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_1 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 1u32, "2026-10-25T00:09Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:09 main: filed from x's type-seam census (XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH note 2): 27 of 82 sim types cross a seam (17 records, 5 unions, 2 nominals, 3 aliases), and the hub is events. Mechanism, read in the tree core: RUST-EXPORT-TYPES already fills unionSourceNames (union node -> Spiral name), shared by every backend, and codegenRust walks each Export signature (spiral_compiler.fs 164055-164077: exported_union_names, sanitized, index suffix on collision). The Gleam slice is codegen only: the same walk in codegenGleam, then pub type <source name> for those unions in types.gleam, with internal types keeping Uh/Us. Nominals over primitives (post_id, cell_id) are evaluated away before codegen, so they cross as their primitive and need nothing. Records crossing a seam need a stable name too: take Rust's exported_record_names rule, not a positional layout. Proof: an export_union regression sample with a two-package Gleam consumer that pattern-matches the union, as the Rust union consumer crate did (exit 12). Estimate 240 m from the Rust slice minus its evaluator half (the table exists). Starts only if XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH picks packages.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_2 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 2u32, "2026-10-25T00:09Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:09 main: x amended the bench rule before measuring (XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH note 3). This item counts as cost, not as a disqualifier. Packages win if B0/C0 is within 10% of A, or if B0 minus its measured seam-parse time is. If packages win, this item lands and B is re-measured typed, which must come out <= B0. Seam scope confirmed as 5 unions plus the crossing records; the nominals cross as primitives. Numbers will come after X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS and verify-landing5.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_3 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 3u32, "2026-10-25T00:13Z", "x", "10-24 20:13 x: TEAM main -> x (user 20:1x: 'compiler work is for each lane, not main, where did you take that?'). The drift was mine: I filed this 'for main' from a stale lane-roles memory, which is now fixed. The dependency on the bench's packages-vs-parts outcome is dropped. The user decided packages, built at codegen, so this item starts as part of X-PARALLEL-BUILD. main's design notes (n1, n2) are the plan: codegenGleam walks Export signatures like codegenRust 164055-164077, emits pub types named by source for unions, and records follow exported_record_names. Proof: a two-package Gleam consumer that pattern-matches an exported union.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_4 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 4u32, "2026-10-25T00:40Z", "x", "10-24 20:38 x: scope (user 20:3x). This is the enabler for HAND-modeled Gleam units (X-HAND-UNITS): types reachable from a hand-written !!!!Export get source names, so sibling modules can share them. It does no splitting. x does all of it itself (draft, gate, pack, install), telling the other lanes only before an install.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_5 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 5u32, "2026-10-25T02:44Z", "x", "10-24 22:44 x: SCOPE GREW from measurement (XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH n8). Producer-side names aren't enough for Gleam. Hand units must CALL each other with typed values, or every unit re-emits the 1,100-line event codec. Needed: (a) the producer emits pub types named after their Spiral source for its exports (the original scope); (b) the consumer: an entry declares a dependency on another unit's exports, and codegen emits 'import <module>.{type T, f}' and calls f, with no local re-declaration of T or of f's body. That is how Rust's x crates should share types too, instead of tuples-only. Design first (CPU-free): how the consumer names the producer (a package.spiproj unit list?), and how Spiral types the foreign f (from the producer's entry signature).")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_6 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 6u32, "2026-10-25T02:59Z", "x", "10-24 22:59 x: DESIGN from reading the core (CPU-free). Producer side today (Rust): codegenRust walks the Export signatures (spiral_compiler.fs 164055-164077), names each union from unionSourceNames (52672), then rename_exported_unions (164849) regex-renames UH<n>/US<n> to the source name, case names included (UH0_Cons -> event_Cons), and makes the enum pub. Gleam reuse: the same walk in codegenGleam plus a rename of Uh<n>/Us<n> (and their CapitalCase constructors Uh<n>Cons...) to CapitalCase source names (Event, EventCons). Consumer side (new): an entry header directive, e.g. '///- --import-types <unit>:<union>,<union>' (the host already parses ///- options like --package/--timeout), makes codegen rename its local copy of those unions the same way, DROP the local definition, and emit 'use <crate>::{...}' (Rust) or 'import <module>.{type Event, EventCons, ...}' (Gleam). Calls cross through the existing host-call macro typed with the Spiral type. Correctness rests on both sides generating the same layout from the same Spiral type: case order and payload types come from the union definition, so they match. The open problem is generic instantiations: 'list event' vs 'list string' are distinct union nodes with the same source name 'list', and today's naming is first-come with an index suffix, which differs per unit. Needed: structural names for instantiations (list_event, result_post_failure). Proof plan: a two-entry regression sample per backend (producer exports a union, consumer imports it and pattern-matches), then x's Gleam codec unit. Estimate up from 240m: 1-2 days with matrix runs.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_7 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 7u32, "2026-10-25T05:57Z", "x", "10-25 01:57 x: design detail from codegenGleam (spiral_compiler.fs 168240; program assembly at 168959-168964). Gleam types are already emitted 'pub type' (168962), so the producer side is only the rename: a post-assembly pass like Rust's rename_exported_unions mapping Uh<n>/Us<n> and their constructors (generatedCaseName from 'Uh<tag>' + case free vars) to CapitalCase source names, regex-anchored as \\bUh<n>(?=[A-Z_]|\\b). Consumer side: after its own rename, drop the matching 'pub type <Name> {...}' block and prepend 'import <module>.{type <Name>, <Ctor>...}'; constructors must be imported to be used unqualified. Watch silenceUnusedBindings (168965): it runs per 'pub fn' chunk, so the import line goes before the first chunk.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_8 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 8u32, "2026-10-25T09:37Z", "x", "2026-10-25 05:37 -04:00 x: the open instantiation-naming problem, read in the core (CPU-free). Today a generic instantiation gets NO source name, not a first-come one. single-flight nominal_type_apply (PartEval, spiral_compiler.fs ~159967) notes a name only in the bare YNominal branch (noteUnionSourceName, the only call site). The YApply branch evaluates the applied body into a fresh YUnion without noting anything, so 'list event' stays Uh<n> on every backend. gpt5parallel's twin (~147962) flattens the application spine in a loop and notes no names at all, not even bare nominals. Design: one shared helper, structuralTyName (in Prepass, next to unionSourceNames at 52723): YNominal n -> n.node.name; YApply(f, a) -> name f + '_' + name a; YPrim -> its keyword (i32, string, ...); YUnion with a noted source name -> that name; YPair -> name a + '_' + name b; anything else -> 't'. Both cores call noteUnionSourceName (structuralTyName x) when an application yields a YUnion: single-flight at the end of its YApply branch, gpt5parallel after its spine loop (both sides of the PartEval pair, as pair-drift requires). Names then depend only on the type expression, so 'list event' is list_event in every unit and the per-unit index suffix only breaks real collisions. Gate: an export sample where two entries each export 'list event' and print the emitted type name; both must say list_event. Then the two-package Gleam consumer proof from note 6.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_9 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 9u32, "2026-10-25T09:51Z", "x", "2026-10-25 05:51 -04:00 x: structural names DRAFTED as an anchored patch, deliberately not in the tree: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--home-git\\b7595ac3-963d-4714-be58-0267aa270947\\scratchpad\\structural-names\\apply.ps1 ([-Reverse]; round-trip byte-identical on a copy; pair-drift clean with it applied). It adds noteUnionSourceNameOnce (shared Prepass; computes the name only for an unnamed node) and structural_ty_name on both PartEval sides; single-flight notes in the YApply branch, gpt5parallel after its spine loop. Why not in the tree yet: no matrix sample uses !!!!Export, so -Suite all can't see it. The consumers that can change are the 654 Export sites (eoie and x crates): a generic union in an export signature goes from UH<n> to a pub source name (list_string, ...), which renames the Rust enum and its cases. Proof gate before landing: (1) a candidate with the patch compiles x's crates and eoie's export entries; (2) every generated .rs diff vs today is a rename only (no other token changes); (3) no macro text in those sources names UH<n>/US<n> literally (grep); (4) the new two-entry export sample gives list_event on both. Then it lands with the Gleam consumer side.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_10 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 10u32, "2026-10-25T10:29Z", "x", "2026-10-25 06:29 -04:00 x: structural-names gate step 3 MET: no .spi under eoie/src, x/apps or spiral/lib names a generated union (UH|US|Uh|Us + digits) in macro text: 0 hits. Positive control: the same pattern finds 83 in x/apps/client/cells/cells.rs. So a rename can't break hand-written host text; steps 1, 2 and 4 (candidate compile of eoie and x export entries, rename-only .rs diff, the two-entry sample) are still to run.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_11 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 11u32, "2026-10-25T11:19Z", "x", "2026-10-25 07:19 -04:00 x: structural-names proof (job-1792924355141, private Debug candidate with the patch). Steps 1+2 for x: all 22 x crates are token-identical to the installed compiler's output (renamed 0, other 0), so no x export carries a generic union. Step 4 MET: in the two-entry sample (scratchpad\\structural-names\\sample), producer (i32 -> list event) and consumer (list event -> i32) both emit 'pub enum list_event' with cases list_event_Nil/list_event_Cons; the installed compiler emits a private UH0. eoie's 234 crate entries are running (job-1792927142876-x-structural-names-eoie): each is compiled with the candidate and compared to the committed .rs; where they differ, the installed compiler compiles it too and the token diff must be renames of UH/US names only (pub tokens ignored). The patch lands when that reports 0 other changes.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_12 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 12u32, "2026-10-25T11:56Z", "x", "2026-10-25 07:55 -04:00 x: PRODUCER half for Gleam drafted as a second layer, scratchpad\\structural-names\\apply_gleam_producer.ps1 (applies over apply.ps1; dry run on a copy clean, pair-drift 0 one-sided). Rust's Export-signature walk is hoisted unchanged into a shared helper, exportedUnionNames env x (CodegenUtils, before the CodegenAdapter pair), so codegenRust and codegenGleam name exported unions by one rule. codegenGleam gets renameExportedUnions as the last pass: Uh<tag>/Us<tag> -> UpperCamel source name (list_event -> ListEvent), regex \\b<generated>(?![0-9]) so Uh3 never hits Uh30, constructors following (Uh3Cons -> ListEventCons, Uh3i0 -> ListEventi0); Gleam types are already 'pub type'. Proof queued: job-1792929315191-x-gleam-producer-names (producer and consumer samples must emit 'pub type ListEvent' on Gleam; Rust outputs byte-identical to the structural-only candidate, which proves the hoist is neutral). Consumer half (n6's --import-types directive: drop the local definition, emit import) is next.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_13 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 13u32, "2026-10-25T12:03Z", "x", "2026-10-25 08:03 -04:00 x: Gleam PRODUCER half PROVEN (job-1792929315191, a private Debug candidate with apply.ps1 + apply_gleam_producer.ps1). Both sample entries emit 'pub type ListEvent { ListEventNil  ListEventCons(Event, ListEvent) }' with 0 leftover Uh/Us names (9 ListEvent refs each, signatures included), and Event keeps its source name. Rust outputs are byte-identical to the structural-only candidate, so hoisting the walk into exportedUnionNames is neutral. Both patches still wait on the eoie rename-only check (job-1792927142876), then land together. Next is the consumer half.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_14 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 14u32, "2026-10-25T12:06Z", "x", "2026-10-25 08:06 -04:00 x: CONSUMER transport rule, written before implementing. The consumer names its imports in its own source, not in build config: hand units are modeled in Spiral (hand-modeled-build-units), so a unit has to describe its own seams, and BuildSpiral/build.ps1 stay one command per entry. Candidates: (a) a ///- header option '--import-types <unit>:<type>,<type>' read by the host; (b) an env var per invocation; (c) a Spiral-level declaration. Pick (a), because the host already reads ///- options and the header is what a reader of the unit sees first. Reject (b): config outside the source. Reject (c) for now: it needs parser and type-system work, so it is deferred unless (a) fails. Codegen effect: for each listed source name, the local union noted with that name (unionSourceNames, whether or not the consumer exports it) is renamed like the producer's, its 'pub type' block is dropped, and 'import <unit>.{type T, C1, C2...}' (Gleam) or 'use <crate>::{T}' (Rust) is prepended. Gate: the two-entry sample with consumer.spi declaring '--import-types producer:list_event,event' must gleam build as one package and run tally (produced 5) = 5, and the Rust pair builds as two crates the same way.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_15 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 15u32, "2026-10-25T12:07Z", "x", "2026-10-25 08:07 -04:00 x: CORRECTION to n6 and n14. The compiler does NOT parse ///- header options: neither the host (Program.fs) nor the core matches '///-' or '///>'. Only Cells reads them (apps/cells directives.ex: --package, --timeout, --test). n6's 'the host already parses ///- options' was wrong, and n14's choice of (a) rested on it. The rule's reason still holds (seams declared in the unit's own source, one build command per entry), so (a) stays, with its real cost added: the host reads the entry file's leading ///- lines before compiling, picks out --import-types <unit>:<type>,..., and hands codegen the list through one core entry point (e.g. a CodegenUtils 'importedUnionNames' table set per build), the same way SPIRAL_DEFAULT_INT reaches startupParse. Cells passes those lines through untouched (it ignores unknown ///- options; check directives.ex before landing). That is roughly 2 h more than n6's 'codegen only'.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_16 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 16u32, "2026-10-25T12:10Z", "x", "2026-10-25 08:10 -04:00 x: CORRECTION to n15's last claim. Cells does NOT ignore unknown ///- options: apps/cells/spi/directives.spi pair's final arm 'Token (flag, _) => ParseBad (text.err ''flag'' flag)' rejects them with 'unknown kernel flag'. So option (a) touches three places: the host reads the header, codegen applies the table, and Cells' directive parser gets an arm that accepts and ignores --import-types and its value (as it does --symbol), with a test that a header carrying it still parses. Checked by reading the code, not assumed.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_17 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 17u32, "2026-10-25T12:32Z", "x", "2026-10-25 08:32 -04:00 x: CONSUMER half, first proof run (job-1792930546726): the codegen pass works. consumer.gleam drops both local type blocks and imports them, but with one 'import producer.{...}' line per type. Gleam then keeps only the first, so 'The custom type variant constructor EventLiked is not in scope' (gleam run exit 1). Fix: group the imports per unit into one line ('import producer.{type ListEvent, ListEventNil, ListEventCons, type Event, EventLiked, EventGone}'). Rerun: job-1792931443568-x-gleam-consumer-imports-2. The patch is scratchpad\\structural-names\\apply_gleam_consumer.ps1 (core importUnions + importedTypeUnits, host applyEntryImports, Cells directives arm). The proof sets SPIRAL_IMPORT_TYPES directly, because build.ps1 compiles the host from the live tree; the host's header read gets its own check once landed.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_18 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 18u32, "2026-10-25T12:45Z", "x", "2026-10-25 08:45 -04:00 x: CONSUMER half PROVEN (job-1792931443568). With imports grouped per unit, consumer.gleam starts 'import producer.{type Event, EventLiked, EventGone, type ListEvent, ListEventNil, ListEventCons}' and declares no types. producer.gleam + consumer.gleam + a run.gleam build as ONE gleam package (offline, deps from apps/sim/frames), and consumer.consumed(producer.produced(5)) prints 5. So hand units share typed values across modules, with no text seam and no re-emitted codec. Left before landing: (1) the eoie rename-only check: 83 of 234 entries identical, 151 couldn't compile in my harness ('Package C:/home/git/eoie/src/spiral is not loaded': eoie's packages need the src/spiral link that eoie's regen sets up), so it reruns on a scratch copy with that link; (2) the host's header read checked once landed; (3) the Rust consumer half (use <crate>::{T}), the same pass for codegenRust.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_19 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 19u32, "2026-10-25T14:39Z", "x", "2026-10-25 10:39 -04:00 x: RUST consumer half DRAFTED, scratchpad\\structural-names\\apply_rust_consumer.ps1, layer 4 over apply.ps1 + both Gleam layers. Dry run on a copy clean; pair-drift 0 one-sided. Rust's orphan rules shape it: a crate may not add inherent or Default impls to another crate's type, so the consumer drops the whole local definition (derive line, enum, 'impl T { fn tag }', 'impl Default for T') and 'use <unit>::{T, ...}' goes after 'use std::rc::Rc;', one line per unit. The producer then owns those impls for every exported union: it emits its tag impl and its Default impl even when the producer itself never uses them, and 'fn tag' becomes 'pub fn tag'. That makes the producer layer ADDITIVE, not rename-only, so the x-crate and eoie neutrality check gets a fourth allowed diff class (added impl blocks for exported unions plus pub on tag) once layer 4 lands. Gate: the two-entry sample as a 3-crate cargo workspace (producer lib, consumer lib depending on it, a run bin) prints 5. Proof job-1792939045373-x-rust-consumer-imports.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_20 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 20u32, "2026-10-25T15:01Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:01 -04:00 x: Rust consumer proof 2 (job-1792939287925) FAILED, cause found. The consumer pass and 'pub fn tag' never matched: the Rust pipeline builds text with StringBuilder.AppendLine, which writes CRLF on Windows, and only the host turns it into LF on write (Program.fs:868). So every pattern ending in a bare \\n missed: the impl and enum drops, the 'use std::rc::Rc;' anchor, and the tag rewrite. The existing rename survives only because '\\nenum ' is still a substring of CRLF text. The Gleam pipeline builds with bare \\n, which is why the Gleam layer worked. Fix: every layer-4 pattern takes \\r?\\n, checked by scratchpad\\structural-names\\regex_check.ps1 on the real consumer output with CRLF put back (both local types dropped, 'use producer::{event, list_event};' inserted, 1 pub tag). Lesson for codegen passes: match Rust text with \\r?\\n; the Gleam text has bare \\n. Proof 3: job-1792940361755-x-rust-consumer-imports-3. Also: apply_gleam_consumer.ps1 gains an opt-in -DirectivesTest path that adds the n16 Cells test ('--import-types belongs to the compiler, so Cells reads past it and its value', in directives_test.exs). It is opt-in because the proof scripts call the patch without that path and must never write to the live tree.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_21 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 21u32, "2026-10-25T15:08Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:08 -04:00 x: RUST consumer half PROVEN (job-1792940361755, a private Debug candidate with all 4 layers). consumer lib.rs declares no enum and no impl and starts 'use producer::{event, list_event};'. producer lib.rs owns 'impl event', 'impl list_event' (both 'pub fn tag') and both Default impls. A 3-crate cargo workspace (producer lib; consumer lib depending on it; run bin calling consumer::consumed(producer::produced(5))) builds offline and prints 5. So hand units share typed values across crates on Rust too, by the same --import-types seam as Gleam. Left before landing all 4 layers together: (1) the eoie rename-only check for layer 1 (job-1792932468478, running); (2) a neutrality pass for layer 4 over x's 22 crates and eoie's entries, where the allowed diffs are renames, added tag/Default impl blocks for exported unions, and 'pub' on tag; (3) the host's ///- --import-types header read, checked once landed (the proofs set SPIRAL_IMPORT_TYPES directly); (4) the Cells directives test via apply_gleam_consumer.ps1 -DirectivesTest. Progress 60 -> 70: both halves proven, only checks and landing left.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_22 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 22u32, "2026-10-25T15:38Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:38 -04:00 x: structural-names gate steps 1+2 MET for eoie (job-1792932468478, a scratch copy of eoie src with the src/spiral link to the tree lib): the remaining 151 entries are byte-identical to the committed .rs (same 151, rename-only 0, other 0, no output 0). With the first run's 83 identical, all 234 eoie entries are unchanged and all 22 x crates are token-identical, while the two-entry sample shows the rename (list_event), so layer 1 changes only code that exports a generic union, which no shipped crate does yet. Remaining before landing layers 1-4 together: the layer-4 x neutrality pass (job-1792941002251, added tag/Default impl blocks plus pub on tag allowed), the same pass over eoie's entries, then the land with pair-drift and the host header-read check.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_23 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 23u32, "2026-10-25T15:47Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:47 -04:00 x: layer-4 neutrality MET for x (job-1792941002251): all 22 x crates are identical between the layer-1 and layer-4 candidates once 'pub fn tag' is normalized, with 0 added impl blocks, 0 other additions and 0 removals, because no x crate exports a union. Positive control in the same run: sample/producer.spi goes from 0 to 4 top-level impl blocks (tag + Default for event and list_event), so the check sees the additions. eoie's pass is running (job-1792942810301, layer 4 vs the committed .rs). Landing is scripted and rehearsed: scratchpad\\structural-names\\land_export_types.ps1 [-Base] [-Undo] applies the 4 layers + the Cells arm + the directives test with a backup and a hash-checked undo. A copy rehearsal applied cleanly, pair-drift was 0 one-sided, and undo restored all 4 files hash-equal. It runs on the tree once eoie's pass is clean and no job reads the compiler tree (pack reviews build from it); then a Debug build from the tree, the two-language sample again through the host's ///- header read, and Cells directives_test.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_24 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 24u32, "2026-10-25T18:00Z", "x", "2026-10-25 14:00 -04:00 x: the layer-4 eoie pass (job-1792942810301) was STOPPED at 182/234 entries to release its compile window for main's 5l'' install (job-1792950541607), which was waiting on it. Its partial result is not evidence about layer 4. 161 entries differ from the committed .rs, almost all as 'let mut' -> 'let' and other codegen-pass changes: the tree core moved between the layer-1 candidate (cache, 07:1x, identical to the committed .rs) and the layer-4 candidate (cache-rust, 10:41). With mut ignored, 139 still differ, so it is base-core drift, not my layers. Method fix: compare a BASE candidate and a LAYER-4 candidate built from the SAME core snapshot, not the committed .rs. That is the x pass's method (layer-1 vs layer-4 DLLs, clean), except both must share a base. Rerun after 5l'' installs and the core is pinned: build base and base+4 layers from 17D970FE (or its successor), then run scratchpad\\structural-names\\layer4_eoie_verdicts.ps1 between the two output sets. Landing waits for that.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_25 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 25u32, "2026-10-25T23:41Z", "x", "2026-10-25 19:41 -04:00 (written earlier under the eoie lease) x: layer-4 neutrality MET on the same core (job-1792952173917): a candidate = tree core 34519B92 + all 4 layers vs the installed 5l'' (built from that core), over every x crate and every eoie entry (eoie scratch copy). Same 257, other changes 0, missing 0; the only difference is the positive control, sample/producer.spi: 17 renamed tokens (UH<n> -> list_event and cases) + 4 impl blocks (tag + Default for event and list_event). So the layers change nothing in shipped code (no shipped crate exports a union) and the method is now sound: the earlier committed-.rs comparison mixed in base-core drift (let mut, clones). All proof gates are met: Gleam consumer (2 modules, prints 5), Rust consumer (3 crates, prints 5), structural names in eoie/x, layer-4 neutrality. Landing: land_export_types.ps1 (hash-checked -Undo, rehearsed) touches spiral_compiler.fs, host Program.fs, Cells directives.spi + directives_test.exs; asked main at 19:0x whether to land before or after main-pack5m-review (queued; reviews build from the tree core) or take it into pack5m. Progress 70 -> 85 by rule: only the landing and its post-land checks (Debug build from the tree, the sample through the host's ///- header read, mix test directives_test) remain. || 2026-10-25 19:21 -04:00 x: main's decision: not into the tree now (main-pack5m-review composes over 34519B92 and its install refuses if the core moves); the core layers go into PACK 5n, composed right after 5m installs, and the host + Cells parts land in 5n's install window before the host build, so core and host never disagree. Split for that (rehearsed: byte-identical to the full landing on all 4 files, pair-drift 0 one-sided): scratchpad\\structural-names\\apply_core.ps1 -Target <core> and apply_host_cells.ps1 -Spiral <root>; apply_gleam_consumer.ps1 gained -SkipCore and empty host/Cells defaults (no default path touches the tree).")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_26 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 26u32, "2026-10-28T12:36Z", "x", "2026-10-28 08:36 x: FOUND LANDED. Every layer of this item is in today's core source (sha256 9ff1b708..., which the installed compiler A4E480202BD4 is built from): structural names (noteUnionSourceNameOnce and structural_ty_name at both nominal-application sites), Gleam producer naming (exportedUnionNames, gleamTypeName), the Gleam and Rust consumer passes (importedTypeUnits, importUnions, import_rust_unions), the Cells --import-types arm in apps/cells/spi/directives.spi plus its test, and the host. git: the core identifiers arrive in spiral commit cec8840c (2026-10-28 07:06), i.e. composed into a pack after the 10-25 proofs (neutrality on every x crate and eoie entry, positive control sample/producer.spi). That is why my 10-25 apply scripts' anchors all miss today: they are applied, not drifted. Remaining before Done (installed-is-done): a LIVE check that the installed compiler names the producer sample's union after its source (list_event, not a counter name) on Gleam and Rust: node job-1793190999228 (installed DLL, scratch package with lib). Then X-HAND-UNITS (deps on this) becomes pullable.")
inl note_of_gleam_export_types_27 () = Note ("GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES", 27u32, "2026-10-28T12:52Z", "x", "2026-10-28 08:52 x: DONE, verified live on the INSTALLED compiler (staged A4E480202BD4, node job-1793190999228, a scratch package with lib): the 10-25 positive control sample/producer.spi compiles with its union named after its Spiral source on both backends (Gleam: 9 source-named occurrences, 0 counter-named Uh/US names, 2 pub types; Rust: 25 source-named, 0 counter-named, 2 pub types). The layers landed in spiral cec8840c (note 26), and the 10-25 neutrality proofs cover shipped code. X-HAND-UNITS' Gleam half is now pullable.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_1 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 1u32, "2026-10-25T00:27Z", "x", "10-24 20:27 x: x/Cargo.toml [profile.release] codegen-units = 1, lto = true (fat), opt-level = 3; .cargo/config.toml sets only +crt-static. e2e and the pre-commit build release, so every verify pays for serial fat LTO. RULE, set before measuring: 3 cold and 3 touch builds of the 16 crates per setting, quiet machine, cargo build --timings saved. Take thin LTO + codegen-units 16 if cold or touch wall drops >= 15% and the e2e wall and xc timeline replay don't slow by > 5%. Take rust-lld (linker = 'rust-lld' for x86_64-pc-windows-msvc) if the link phase drops >= 20% and e2e passes; it isn't benchmarked against link.exe upstream (rust#71520), so our measurement decides. Leave dev as is unless the pre-commit uses dev. Sources: Cargo profiles docs, perf book, rust 1.90 lld post.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_2 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 2u32, "2026-10-25T00:40Z", "x", "10-24 20:38 x: focus (user 20:3x: 'find the sota combinations to make it faster while iterating with it'). The loop that matters is edit one .spi -> regenerate one crate -> build -> e2e, so the touch rebuild is the primary measure and cold is secondary. Combos to measure together, not one at a time: dev vs release for the e2e loop (does e2e need release?), thin vs fat LTO, codegen-units 16/256, rust-lld vs link.exe, debug line-tables-only, incremental on/off, and nightly -Zthreads=8 for dev. Read cargo build --timings for the critical crate. The n1 rule applies per combo.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_3 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 3u32, "2026-10-29T13:00Z", "x", "2026-10-29 09:3x x: UNBLOCKED (dep X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS is Done). The harness is drafted, not run: x/scripts/cargo-profile-bench.ps1. The arms are set with cargo --config profile.release.* overrides (Cargo.toml untouched), each in its own --target-dir: base (fat LTO, cgu 1, today), base-aa (the in-job A/A arm), thin16 (thin LTO + cgu 16), thin16-lld (+ rust-lld, present in the pinned nightly), nolto16 (LTO off + cgu 16; answers n2's 'does e2e need release-grade LTO'). One cold build per arm, then -Rounds touch rounds (mtime-touch apps/client/xc/xc.rs, the edit-one-crate loop n2 names as primary). Every arm runs every round, order rotating so each arm takes each position once, rows in rows.tsv. Verdict = median of per-round arm/base ratios, trusted only if base-aa/base is within 1.05 (memory ab-needs-in-job-aa; this replaces n1's 'quiet machine', which never comes, per no-quiet-machine-wait). n1's adoption rule is unchanged: >= 15% touch-wall drop, and e2e wall + xc replay not > 5% slower (measured after, for the winner only). Runs on the node after verify29 releases it; heavy (5 cold + 25 touch builds).")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_4 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 4u32, "2026-10-29T18:14Z", "x", "2026-10-29 14:2x x: first bench run (node x-queue1, job-1793294826836, pack-6b, node at 97-100% CPU) - DATA, NOT A VERDICT. Cold builds, one each: base (fat LTO, cgu 1) 525 s; base-aa 640 s; thin16 266 s; thin16-lld 309 s; nolto16 416 s. The in-job A/A reads 640/525 = 1.22, far outside the 1.05 trust bound (memory ab-needs-in-job-aa), so no cold ratio is trusted; cold is secondary by n2 anyway. One touch sample landed before the harness died: base-aa touch (mtime of xc.rs) 508 s, i.e. a one-crate edit under today's profile costs nearly a full cold build, consistent with fat LTO redoing whole-program codegen on every link. That is the loop n2 calls primary, and exactly what thin16/nolto16 target. HARNESS BUG, fixed: PowerShell variables are case-insensitive, so the touch hashtable $touch WAS the [string] $Touch parameter; assigning @{} coerced it to a string and the first indexing threw. Renamed to $touchSeconds (5 sites). Rerun: the 5 touch rounds, after queue 2, as its own node job.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_5 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 5u32, "2026-10-29T19:47Z", "x", "2026-10-29 16:0x x: BENCH VERDICT (node job-1793298157963, pack-6b, 5 rotated rounds, node under load). The in-job A/A base-aa/base median is 1.038, inside the 1.05 bound, so the verdict is TRUSTED. Touch rebuild (mtime of xc.rs, the edit-one-crate loop n2 calls primary), median ratio vs base (fat LTO, cgu 1, ~190-280 s per touch): thin16 (thin LTO + cgu 16) 0.547; thin16-lld 0.586 (rust-lld SLOWER than link.exe here, so REJECTED by n1's link rule); nolto16 (LTO off + cgu 16) 0.062, i.e. ~12 s per touch vs ~220 s (16x). Per n1, a setting is adopted only if touch wall drops >= 15% (thin16 and nolto16 both do) AND e2e wall + xc timeline replay are not > 5% slower. That second half is NOT measured yet, so nothing is adopted. Next: build xc with each winner and run e2e (153 stub requests) and a timeline replay with an A/A, then pick. Expected shape: nolto16 for the verify/dev loop if e2e holds, fat or thin for the published binary.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_6 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 6u32, "2026-10-29T20:02Z", "x", "2026-10-29 16:3x x: runtime half launched as its own tool, x/scripts/cargo-profile-runtime.ps1 (node job-1793304218824). It takes the xc.exe each bench arm already built (x-cargo-bench\\target-<arm>\\release, built 15:24-15:35 on pack-6b), and runs the real e2e (apps/client/e2e/e2e.ps1: the 37-step tutorial, sim threads, Mastodon/Bluesky stubs; 153 stub requests) per arm, 5 rotated rounds, arms base, base-aa (A/A), thin16, nolto16. It records wall time and exit per run; any failed e2e disqualifies its arm. Verdict by n1: adopt an arm only if its e2e median ratio vs base is <= 1.05 (not > 5% slower) and the A/A reads within 1.05. e2e wall is the closest x has to 'xc timeline replay' (it replays tutorial timelines and threads against the stub).")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_7 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 7u32, "2026-10-29T21:30Z", "x", "2026-10-29 17:3x x: runtime half (job-1793304218824) is UNTRUSTED, so still no adoption. e2e wall medians vs base: base-aa 0.857 (A/A, an identical binary), thin16 0.837, nolto16 0.979. The A/A is 14% off, far outside the 1.05 bound: e2e wall on the loaded node swung 88-487 s for the SAME arm, because it is dominated by stub I/O, waits and node load, not by xc's CPU work, so it cannot resolve a 5% effect. ESTABLISHED: all 20 e2e runs passed (0 failures), so thin16 and nolto16 binaries are functionally correct on the full tutorial, sim threads and the Mastodon/Bluesky stubs. Next instrument, pre-registered: per arm, the PROCESS CPU TIME (TotalProcessorTime, robust under load, unlike wall) of a CPU-bound xc command, post-thread --dry-run over a large .spi (the splitter, weights and defuse path, no network), 7 rotated rounds with base-aa; adopt if arm/base CPU median <= 1.05 and A/A within 1.05. Touch-build verdict (n5, trusted) stands: nolto16 0.062, thin16 0.547.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_8 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 8u32, "2026-10-29T22:07Z", "x", "2026-10-29 18:2x x: VERDICT + ADOPTED by n1's pre-registered rule. Runtime instrument (x/scripts/cargo-profile-cpu.ps1, node job-1793309621871, pack-6b binaries from the bench): PROCESS CPU TIME of 'xc post-thread --dry-run apps/client/wire.spi' (splitter + weights + defuse, offline), 7 rotated rounds x 5 runs, 0 failures. A/A base-aa/base 1.005 (TRUSTED). thin16/base 1.028 (+2.8%, within 5%); nolto16/base 1.099 (+9.9%, over 5%). With the trusted touch verdict (n5): thin16 touch 0.547 (-45%) and CPU +2.8% passes BOTH clauses -> ADOPTED. nolto16 touch 0.062 (-94%) but CPU +9.9% -> REJECTED for release. thin16-lld rejected (n5). Applied (tree, unstaged): x/Cargo.toml [profile.release] codegen-units 1 -> 16, lto true -> \"thin\" (opt-level 3, panic abort and strip unchanged; cargo parses it). e2e correctness with thin16 was already shown (20/20 runs green, n7). OPEN, not decided by this rule (it is about the release binary): a separate verify/dev profile with LTO off would cut the edit-build loop ~16x at a ~10% CPU cost, which matters only if the loop does not ship the binary. That is a new question for a new rule. Closes on the next x verify building with the new profile.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_9 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 9u32, "2026-10-29T23:11Z", "x", "2026-10-29 19:11 x: verify32 (job-1793303169424) does NOT close this item. Its node mirror xv30 Cargo.toml (synced 13:41) still has codegen-units = 1 / lto = true, so it predates thin16's adoption; read on the node, not assumed. Old-profile reference from verify32 under today's node load (CPU 100%): two release builds at 5m48s and 5m02s. Closing condition unchanged: the next verify built from the current tree (thin16, codegen-units = 16 / lto = thin) must be green with the same e2e accept, and its release times are recorded next to these two.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_10 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 10u32, "2026-10-29T23:19Z", "x", "2026-10-29 19:19 x: cargo hygiene from verify32's log. The only cargo::unused_dependencies warning (2x, one per release build) is unicode-normalization in apps/client/entities/Cargo.toml; the generated entities.rs never names unicode_normalization (only weights.rs does). Removed it from that Cargo.toml and from xc_entities' list in Cargo.lock by hand (no x script or workflow uses --locked/--frozen; 4 crates still list the package, so the [[package]] entry stays). Hand-maintained file: nothing in x/scripts generates client Cargo.toml. The Cells test header '///> rust -d ... unicode-normalization=0.1' stays, because the test build compiles more than the crate. Proof rides on the next verify: green, and 0 unused-dependency warnings. Unstaged for the user's commit.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_11 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 11u32, "2026-10-30T10:28Z", "x", "2026-10-30 06:2x x groom: verify33 GREEN on the thin16 profile (verify33 (job-1793328097444, pack 7 core 9C899B30495F7DC5, -Accept, LandRepos x,spiral) finished exit 0: client build, sim build, e2e accept and sim accept all exit 0; 66 notebooks passed). The release-time readout from its stdout is the last step before closing.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_12 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 12u32, "2026-10-30T10:29Z", "x", "2026-10-30 06:3x x: n9's condition MET. verify33 (job-1793328097444) was built from the xv30 mirror with codegen-units = 16 / lto = 'thin' (read on the node), green with the same e2e accept. Release times next to verify32's old-profile 5m48s / 5m02s (both under 100% node load): thin16 4m28s (client build before accepting) and 2m47s (client build), about -23% and -45%. n10 (0 unused-dependency warnings) NOT yet met: the entities warning is gone, but the same lint now flags apps/client/posted/Cargo.toml:18 unicode-normalization (2 per log; posted.rs never names unicode_normalization, only weights does). Removed it from posted/Cargo.toml and from xc_posted's list in Cargo.lock (backups in C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\pre-split-1029-2210; 3 crates still list the package). 95: closes on the next green verify with 0 unused-dependency warnings. Unstaged for the user's commit.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_13 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 13u32, "2026-10-30T15:00Z", "x", "2026-10-30 11:1x x: PRE-REGISTERED verify34 (launch-x-verify33.ps1 -Label x-verify34, xv30 mirror, pack 7, -Accept, LandRepos x,spiral). It is one run carrying three proofs from the live tree: (1) this item's n10: CLOSE at 100 Done if the job exits 0 and its stdout+stderr have 0 lines matching (?i)unused.{0,40}(dependenc|crate) (posted/Cargo.toml without unicode-normalization); (2) SIM-IN-SPIRAL n101/n102: the sim build passes gleam build on a clean resolve with gary in split/frames gleam.toml and manifest; (3) X-HAND-UNITS n26: G3's events.spi passes the whole sim notebook list. Any red names its own item; nothing closes on a red run.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_14 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 14u32, "2026-10-30T15:52Z", "x", "2026-10-30 11:5x x: verify34 stopped for the pack-8 node ship (see SIM-IN-SPIRAL n104). The client build never ran, so n13 rolls over unchanged to x-verify35 on pack 8.")
inl note_of_x_cargo_build_profile_15 () = Note ("X-CARGO-BUILD-PROFILE", 15u32, "2026-10-30T23:01Z", "x", "2026-10-30 19:2x x: CLOSED by n13's pre-registered rule. x-verify36 (job-1793379985899, 5.8 h, xqueue1, pack 8 core 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52, post-13:02-window lib, -Accept, LandRepos x,spiral) exit 0: sim accept, client build before accepting, e2e accept, client build (all 43 notebooks passed), sim build (all 23 notebooks passed) all exit 0; outputs in C:\\home\\remote-stage\\xv36-out (95 files). 0 lines matching (?i)unused.{0,40}(dependenc|crate) in stdout + stderr. The pattern's positive control: verify33's stderr has 2 lines 'warning: unused dependency `unicode-normalization`' (posted/Cargo.toml:18), matched by the same pattern. So removing unicode-normalization from posted/Cargo.toml + Cargo.lock (live, unstaged) cleared the lint. The thin16 release profile has held since n9 (-23%/-45% release builds). Unstaged for the user's commit: Cargo.toml (workspace profile), apps/client/posted/Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock.")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 1u32, "2026-10-25T05:15Z", "main", "2026-10-25 01:15 main: DRAFTED in the repo (untracked, for the user's commit). .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml follows tibia's: ubuntu-24.04 and windows-2025, Rust nightly-2026-10-18, BEAM (OTP 29, Elixir 1.20, Gleam 1.19.2), dotnet 9.0.202; init, build, publish; artifacts; then a push job with _deploy.txt and a live-deploy check. actions.lock comes from gh actions-lock (7 actions pinned by commit). New scripts/build.ps1, after tibia's: run_notebook per src file with tests (generator first, because its tests regenerate the modules), per-test metrics, a step summary, fail on anything not green, then family-gates.ps1 -ValidateOnly (new switch) to validate every example document against its XSD. New scripts/publish.ps1: deps/spiral publish-tree. CI portability fixes: family-gates used backslash paths and \\%USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp (missing on Linux), and lib_gate.ps1 is a local tool (scoop paths, LOCALAPPDATA), so CI runs run_notebook instead. copy-gate.ps1's anchor follows the new param line. Validate-only already passes 13/13 documents. A finding: the cfdi, ubl, ksef and fatturapa examples are never written (their modules aren't in the package), so they've never been validated (FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE). Local run of the workflow's steps queued: job-1792905316501-main-serial-fiscal-ci-local (init, build, publish).")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 2u32, "2026-10-25T12:05Z", "fiscal", "08:05 fiscal: first local run of the drafted workflow steps (init/build/publish, job-1792912920241, 03:44-07:44) was RED and showed two defects fixed since: build.ps1 never loaded spiral core.ps1 (Ensure-SpiralCompiler needs ResolveLink) and listed test files before the generator wrote them; and one 4+16-module package made every cell OOM or time out (FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE n12: generated modules are now one-module build units under generated/). Pending: the smoke run job-1792929474834-fiscal-smoke (corpus, generator ksef, the ksef unit), then the full local run; if one build.ps1 run cannot fit the 240-min heavy cap locally or the 420-min CI job, the workflow gets one matrix leg per build-unit group (build.ps1 -Files already selects).")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 3u32, "2026-10-25T22:25Z", "fiscal", "19:2x fiscal: CI shape rule, written before the numbers (from job-1792958908945's per-file seconds; so far generator 1,794 s, corpus_checks 1,280 s, corpus about 60 s, generated modules several minutes each). Let T = the build's total measured file time on this machine.\n(a) T <= 0.6 x 420 min (252 min): keep today's single job per OS (headroom for slower hosted runners and Windows).\n(b) T > 252 min: split into legs.\n- leg 1 runs the pattern oracle and the generator, and uploads generated/ + src/pattern_*.txt + src/patterns.txt as an artifact;\n- legs 2..N each download it and run a group of build units (corpus, corpus_checks, the generated modules), assigned longest-first (LPT) to the fewest legs that keep each leg <= 150 min, using the measured per-file seconds;\n- a final job downloads every leg's samples/ and target/ci-metrics, runs family-gates.ps1 (the .NET example validation) and publish.ps1, and feeds the existing push job.\nbuild.ps1 already takes -Files, so a leg is build.ps1 -Files <its units>, plus a switch to skip the clear-products step so a leg keeps the downloaded generated/ (the clear runs only when -Files is empty, so -Files legs already keep it; no new switch needed).\n(c) Either way, both OS legs stay (ubuntu-24.04, windows-2025), and actions.lock is regenerated (gh actions-lock) if any new uses: appears (memory actions-lock-on-workflow-edits).\nApplied when job-1792958908945 reports its rows.")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 4u32, "2026-10-27T06:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 02:57 fiscal: the CI-shape rule (note 3) needs T, the build's total per-file time. Merged every per-file table on the node mirror (latest measurement per unit): 16 of ~33 units measured, 170 min (top: generated_nfe_v2_00 1,360 s, generated_ksef_fa_3 1,320 s, generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 1,186 s now that it compiles, generated_mdfe_v1_00 850 s, nfe_v2 certificates 798 s, generator 630 s). The missing units are mostly the CT-e family (overwritten by later labels); projecting them from comparable units puts T well over 252 min, so rule (b) looks likely, but the rule says measure, so no decision yet. Queued the measurement: job-1793084333885-fiscal-full-build (node, after the emptiable proof), a full build.ps1 run with no -Files on the integ mirror (synced to the live tree), memo off, 2 jobs, writing files-full.tsv with every unit; it prints T and the per-unit seconds the LPT leg assignment needs. Note: the node is a 16 GB box like this one, so hosted runners may differ; the rule's 0.6 factor is the headroom for that.")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 5u32, "2026-10-30T02:01Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 22:01 fiscal: GROOM: dependency FISCAL-ROOT-PATHS is Done, so the only open input was T. MEASURED by job-1793084333885-fiscal-full-build (node, 10-27 03:48-05:00, 2 jobs, memo off): 33 units, every one exit 0, sum of per-unit times T = 51 min (slowest corpus_checks 697 s); job wall 121 min, and its exit 1 came from a step after the units, still to be read. The 10-27 projection 'T well over 252 min' was wrong by about 5x. Next session: apply note 3's CI-shape rule to T = 51 min, then read why the run exited 1. Its mirror 'integ' is archived at C:\\home\\remote-archive\\20261029-fiscal-preretire2\\integ; the job log is on the node. [CORRECTION 2026-10-29 22:57: the job printed its own summary: \"units 35, T = 91 min of file time (rule: <= 252 keeps one job per OS, > 252 splits into legs)\". My 51 min came from a regex that matched only 33 unit lines. With T = 91 min, the rule says ONE CI JOB PER OS, which decides the CI shape. The exit 1 was not the format step (format-generated.ps1 did not exist on 10-27); it was \"build.ps1 / 1 problems: generated_ubl_invoice_2_1: failed 1\", the same unit whose UBL-JSON round trip fails today (FISCAL-MY-MYINVOIS n16-19).]")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 6u32, "2026-10-30T03:01Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 23:01 fiscal: STATE RE-READ: the workflow EXISTS and is committed (fiscal 70f4b5b: .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml + actions.lock covering all 7 'uses:' with pinned versions). It is modeled on tibia: one build job per OS (ubuntu-24.04, windows-2025), init.ps1 + build.ps1 (no legs), publish on ubuntu, artifacts (notebooks on failure, ci-metrics, gh-pages), and a push job with peaceiris + a live-deploy check (_deploy.txt SHA + readme/viewport marks). That is exactly the shape the measured T = 91 min selects (rule: <= 252 = one job per OS), so no split is needed. Remaining: (1) the local run through i574n.github/scripts/workflow.ps1 the title asks for; (2) a green build.ps1, blocked on generated_ubl_invoice_2_1's UBL-JSON round trip (FISCAL-MY-MYINVOIS) and helped by the format-step dotnet fix (FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS n27). Planned 0% -> Active 60%.")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 7u32, "2026-10-30T15:36Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 11:36 fiscal: init portability finding (for the workflow's init step): scripts/init.ps1:135 fetches facturae.gob.es, which fails from Windows 10 (TLS 1.3-only server; Schannel on 19045 can't negotiate it) and from the node's Python (FNMT root not trusted). Windows 11 / Server 2022 and Ubuntu runners are fine, so CI is unaffected, but the abort skips every later corpus fetch. Candidate hardening (tree, your call to commit): fetch each corpus independently, so one failed source doesn't skip the rest, and name the failed source at the end. The node workaround (seed from the box) is in memory node-cannot-fetch-facturae.")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 8u32, "2026-10-31T22:19Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 18:19 fiscal: CI COVERAGE PASS (user 10-31: 'make sure everything is downloaded in gh actions, ran/converted and available through dir-tree-html with reports/benchs'). (1) DOWNLOADS: init.ps1 now also fetches the OpenPeppol BIS 3.0.20 structure files with all 16 included parts (guard on the part folder). (2) RUN/CONVERT: build.ps1 regenerates canon/en16931-terms.json via scripts/en16931-terms.ps1 and the drift guard now covers canon/ as well as generated/. (3) REPORTS: build.ps1 writes target/ci-metrics/examples.tsv (Example, Bytes, Messages, SignaturePending, First message) from family-gates.ps1 and target/ci-metrics/bench.tsv (Unit, ParseUs, WriteUs, Runs, Bytes, Os; the medians every generated unit's bench cell prints, read from the run outputs and the notebooks) and adds the bench table to the job summary. (4) PUBLISH: publish.ps1 includes *.json (canon inventory, the GST JSON sample) besides *.xml/*.txt/*.tsv, so dir-tree-html shows the reports. (5) FIX: since signing stages (10-29) every signed family writes an UNSIGNED example, which the XSD reports as one 'incomplete content ... expected Signature (xmldsig)' error, and build.ps1 counted that as a failed example (CI red on correct output). validate-example.ps1 now reports that one case as 'signature pending N' and keeps every other message as a validation message (verified on the CR Factura 4.4 sample: 'validation messages 2; signature pending 1', the 2 being a real defect, see FISCAL-CR-HACIENDA). All scripts parse; the next CI run on push exercises them end to end.")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_9 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 9u32, "2026-11-01T02:50Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 22:50 fiscal: CORRECTION (user 10-31: 'do we need canon/en16931-terms.json to be committed? ain't this drift?'): no. It is derived entirely from the downloaded structure files (deps/, ignored) by scripts/en16931-terms.ps1, like the TSVs the user keeps out of git. Now: .gitignore ignores /canon/; build.ps1 still regenerates it every run but the drift guard covers generated/ only again (generated/ stays committed by the user's showcase choice); publish.ps1's *.json include still publishes it, because publish-tree lists ignored files too (git ls-files --others without --exclude-standard, the same path that publishes the ignored samples/*.xml). Rule for fiscal: commit only what cannot be regenerated, plus the generated/ units the user chose to show; every other derived artifact is built in CI and shown through dir-tree-html.")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_10 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 10u32, "2026-11-01T03:25Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 23:25 fiscal: CI WAS RED ON EVERY PUSH since at least 10-25 (runs 40224734143 ... 41267206321, a8b62b9) and the page was never browsable. ROOT CAUSES, fixed in the tree (unstaged): (1) init.ps1 cloned spiral but never ran spiral's scripts/init-app-build.ps1, so deps/spiral/deps/The-Spiral-Language was missing and every Cells run died ('Package .../The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core is not loaded'); the generator failed in 5-11 s, so all 162 generated files 'differed' and the examples were never written. init.ps1 now runs init-app-build.ps1 -fast when the fork checkout is missing (the log showed it being cloned only later, inside publish.ps1). (2) pattern-oracle.ps1 wrote '\\\\\\patterns.txt' etc.; on Linux that makes files whose names contain a backslash, and publish-tree failed copying 'src\\\\pattern_expected.txt'; all 5 paths now use Join-Path (no other fiscal script has such paths). (3) GitHub Pages was NOT ENABLED for i574n/fiscal (siblings serve gh-pages /): enabled via the API (source gh-pages /, https://i574n.github.io/fiscal/); gh-pages currently holds a8b62b9's partial deploy without index.html (publish failed before dir-tree-html), so it serves 404 until the next green publish. PAGE CONTENT: README gained a Reports section linking target/ci-metrics/bench.tsv, examples.tsv, tests.tsv, files.tsv, samples/ and canon/en16931-terms.json. VERIFY LOOP: a watcher is armed for the next push-triggered run (no push requested; the user's next push is the check), and memory verify-ci-after-user-push makes it standing for this lane.")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_11 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 11u32, "2026-11-01T04:40Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 02:12 fiscal: LAST 5 RUNS (gh run list): 41268995292 dynamic 10-31 23:23 success (Pages build); 41267206321 push a8b62b9 10-31 22:56 FAILURE; 41240069901 push 196b088 10-31 16:41 FAILURE; 41160607934 push 85274a9 10-30 22:34 FAILURE; 40677823853 push 96cac20 10-28 07:10 FAILURE (each 12 min). Every red has the same causes on both OS jobs: build.ps1 fails after 1.8-2.6 min because each Cells file stops with PackageErrors (deps/spiral/apps/cells/spi/package.spiproj: core not loaded, the Spiral fork missing in CI). The \"generated: 162 files differ\" problem follows from that: build.ps1 clears generated/ and the generator never runs. Then publish fails, and deploy \"verify the deploy is live\" times out at 6.0 min because Pages was not enabled. FIXES in place: init.ps1 runs spiral's init-app-build (fork), pattern-oracle uses Join-Path (Linux backslash names), Pages enabled via the API on 10-31; the first two are unstaged and run on the user's next push (watcher armed). SLOW STEPS today: init 1.7-2.1 min, toolchain setup about 1.2 min, the 6 min deploy-verify timeout; the real build time is unknown until the first green run. NEXT: on the next push, read every step's duration and file any step over 5 min.")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_12 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 12u32, "2026-11-01T06:48Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 02:49 fiscal: USER PUSH 3536d8e (02:36, \"tests\": all tonight's fiscal changes) and spiral e0461f15 (same minute, includes the landed Canton xsd_emit) -> run 41281571029 in progress (watcher armed). EXPECTED RED on the drift guard (\"generated: N files differ\"): the commit carries generator changes (bench line directory_create_all, samples-folder creation in writes_its_example, the EnvioDTE prefix, the family_root.extras refactor, which emits identical text) but not regenerated units, and CI generates with spiral main, which now emits Canton/Distrito 01. This is not a code defect; the remedy is committing regenerated units. The truest source is the CI run's own generated/ (if an upload-artifact step carries it), because it is byte for byte what the guard compares against; otherwise the queued node e3 regen (t1101, landed xsd_emit), after checking its lib matches spiral main. Also for the user: git rm --cached src/corpus.txt (now ignored and written by pattern-oracle -Write).")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_13 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 13u32, "2026-11-01T09:07Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 05:10 fiscal: RUN 41281571029 (push 3536d8e) READ. FIRST RUN THAT GOT PAST CELLS: the init fork fix works on both OSes and every test file ran (ubuntu about 2.3 h, windows about 2.4 h). ubuntu problems: (a) generated: 44 files differ (expected; the generator changed and the units were not regenerated in the commit); (b) corpus_checks en16931_rules_parse_completely \"281 fatal assertions, read 283\": a SECOND stale 1.3.16 pin in the same test (XML count of validation-1.3.17: 981 asserts, 283 fatal, 698 warning). FIXED in the tree (unstaged): 281 -> 283. windows: the same two plus 7 \"compiler socket failed: :closed\" on the biggest units (cfdi_pagos/cfdi_v4_0 certificates, cte_os/cte_simp/cte_v4, gtve_v4, nfe_v4), spread 07:30-08:16, then 5 examples \"not written\", which counts as \"5 documents do not validate\". The same units pass on ubuntu, so this is a Windows-runner compiler-daemon fault, not fiscal code. MITIGATION (unstaged, build.ps1 Invoke-TestFile): one retry, logged \"compiler socket closed; retrying once\", when a file's output has that line. A transient crash will pass on the retry; if memory is the cause it fails twice, and the log will show which. GENERATED: the run's gh-pages artifact is NOT a source for the regenerated units (its generated/ equals the committed tree byte for byte; publish copies the committed files). The source is the node e3 regen on t1101, whose lib was checked file by file against spiral e0461f15 (the commit CI clones): only env.spi differed, now aligned. Bring its units home -> the user commits -> drift 0.")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_14 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 14u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 08:39 fiscal: QUALITY SPEC (groom round 4). PRIORITY P0: the user verifies through CI and the published page; it has been red since 10-25.\nDONE WHEN: the first push run after the regenerated units are committed concludes success on both build jobs and the deploy job (\"verify the deploy is live\" passes), and https://i574n.github.io/fiscal/ serves target/ci-metrics/examples.tsv (at least 32 rows, every Messages = 0), bench.tsv (one row per measured unit) and tests.tsv. Windows: no file needs the new \"compiler socket closed; retrying once\" twice; if one does, file it with the unit name and peak memory.\nNEXT STEP: the watcher (background bugpz7sn7) is armed for the next push. On red: gh run view <id> --log-failed | strip ANSI | find \"build.ps1 / N problems\"; fix the root cause in the tree, unstaged; never ask the user to push.\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN: the CI scripts are PowerShell glue, so no model change here. The data they print moves into typed Spiral rows under PS1-TO-SPIRAL: the examples table (port 2: (module, sample, schema, preload, resolve) rows from family_roots) and the metrics TSVs as records written with json_schema.json_text (landed 11-01), so a column cannot be misnamed or dropped by a regex.")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_15 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 15u32, "2026-11-01T17:20Z", "fiscal", "CI 11-01: push f6abb23 (run 41314140694) brought ubuntu GREEN and windows down to 7 units, all one compiler defect: 0xC00000FD stack overflow in SpiralCompilerCore.dll on the rust target (reproduced on the node; filed COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW). Windows has failed on every push since >= 10-25; this is the first run where every src test and the generated drift check pass there. scripts/build.ps1 (unstaged): the retry-once is removed (all 7 crashed again on retry) and replaced by a Windows crash readout from the Application event log (module, exception code, offset). Upstream DFe.NET leiauteConfRecebto_v1.00.xsd redeclares TCOrgaoIBGE from tiposBasico_v1.03 (Windows-only XmlSchemaSet error, non-fatal, validation still runs). Done rule unchanged: both OS jobs green.")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_16 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 16u32, "2026-11-01T21:13Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 17:1x: the 3 held-back CR rows are now in scripts/family-gates.ps1 $resolved (unstaged): cr_factura_4_4, cr_factura_exportacion_4_4, cr_tiquete_4_4 (14 rows, was 11). Evidence: the samples CI published in run 41314140694 (gh-pages artifact, units regenerated with the Canton lib fix) validate with validate-example.ps1 -Resolve -Preload xmldsig: \"validation messages 0; signature pending 1\" for all three. They were held back only for the Canton/Distrito 00 defect, fixed by the lib land and the f6abb23 regeneration.")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_17 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 17u32, "2026-11-01T21:42Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 17:41: family-gates coverage 35 -> 40 of 44 published samples (scripts/family-gates.ps1, unstaged). The single UBL Invoice block is now a loop over the 6 UBL 2.1 documents (Invoice, CreditNote, DebitNote, DespatchAdvice, ReceiptAdvice, ApplicationResponse; -Resolve + UBL xmldsig preload; a missing sample now reports \"not written\" like the resolved rows). PROOF: the real family-gates.ps1 ran end to end on CI run 41314140694's published samples (temp root: samples copy + deps junction): 40 rows, 0 bad, all 9 new rows (5 UBL + 3 CR) present with 0 validation messages. Still uncovered: cl_envio, es_verifactu, hu_nav (XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES) and pt_saft (XSD 1.1 asserts).")
inl note_of_fiscal_ci_workflow_18 () = Note ("FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW", 18u32, "2026-11-02T00:13Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 20:1x: DONE. CI run 41351043040 (push b2fab25) is green on ubuntu-24.04 AND windows-2025 (\"all 86 test files green and every example validates\"), and the push job published. PAGE JUDGMENT (https://i574n.github.io/fiscal/): present: target/ci-metrics bench.tsv / examples.tsv / files.tsv / tests.tsv and per-unit cells tests, README, canon/en16931-terms.json, 44 samples, generated units, corpus manifests. Gaps, filed as FISCAL-PAGES-REPORTS: reports are raw .tsv only (no rendered HTML views); one OS's metrics only (no windows-labelled files); no commit id or build time; no notebooks.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 1u32, "2026-10-25T12:06Z", "fiscal", "08:06 fiscal: resolved by construction under the build-units split (FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE n12), not by listing modules in src/package.spiproj (that listing made every cell OOM): write_generated_module writes every generated module, cfdi, ksef and ubl included, into generated/<module>/ beside its own one-module package.spiproj, and build.ps1 runs every generated/*/*.spi, so no module can be left ungated. fatturapa gets its unit the first time its generation finishes (FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS rule). Done when the full local run shows every unit green.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 2u32, "2026-10-26T06:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 02:51 fiscal: rechecked against x's 10-26 round note ('3 generated modules missing from src/package.spiproj'): that is the pre-split state; src/package.spiproj correctly lists only fiscal_paths, corpus, corpus_checks, generator, and every generated module is its own unit under generated/<module>/ (n1). The done evidence is the full run now on the worker node (job-1792994170131-fiscal-g1-node-build, every generated/*/*.spi incl. cfdi, ksef, ubl, fatturapa): mark Done when it shows every unit green.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 3u32, "2026-10-27T00:59Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 20:59 fiscal: re-checked against its own done rule ('the full run shows every unit green'). (1) The stray generated/tipos_basico_enums.spi is gone: the live generator.spi has 0 mentions, and the file exists in neither the tree nor the node integ mirror. (2) Every non-UBL unit is green on the landed release bytes: the resume1/2 36-file run plus today's proofs (corpus_checks 15/15, every family's example test, 16 samples at 0 validation messages; the transfer proof showed the landed emitter regenerates those units byte-identically). (3) The ONE unit that cannot be green is generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 main: it has never compiled, because its types are cyclic and the emitter never emitted recursive groups (24 ETY-UnboundVariable diagnostics, all forward references or cascades, no parse errors). That is exactly XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS (draft type-checked, node proof job-1793059146977 running, landing wired into job-1793062371516-fiscal-land-post behind that proof's rule). Dependency added; close this item when the full run with recursive groups shows UBL main green.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 4u32, "2026-10-27T06:24Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 02:24 fiscal: its blocker XSD-RECURSIVE-TYPE-GROUPS landed (UBL main compiles; its example test passes in rg-proof4). Remaining for 'every unit green' in a full run: none of the units fails to compile any more; close when the next full run on the live tree shows every unit green.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 5u32, "2026-10-27T06:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 02:57 fiscal: the closing full run is queued: job-1793084333885-fiscal-full-build (full build.ps1 on the integ mirror synced to the live tree, every unit, memo off). Close this item if every unit is green.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 6u32, "2026-10-27T09:02Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 05:05 fiscal: FULL MEASURED BUILD DONE (job-1793084333885-fiscal-full-build, node, build.ps1 with no -Files from a cleared tree, memo off, 2 cell jobs): 35 units, 91 min wall, T = 91 min of file time. CI-SHAPE RULE (pre-registered: <= 252 keeps one job per OS, > 252 splits into legs) applied mechanically: T = 91 <= 252, so ONE JOB PER OS, no legs. NOT closable yet: 34 of 35 units green; generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 is red because its only test cell (the example test, rust) TIMED OUT at the module's 1,200 s budget ('Spiral cell timed out after 1200s; the process tree was killed'); its first cell compiled in 39 s, so the time goes to the rust test cell. That is speed, not a wrong result (the same module produced its example in the recursive-groups proof). Largest units: UBL 1,219 s (capped), generator 1,162 s, corpus_checks 697 s, nfe_v4 244 s, KSeF 200 s; every other unit is under 170 s. The capped UBL time is inside T, so its real cost is higher, but T stays far under 252. Next: measure the UBL cell's phases alone (target vs compile) in a run of its own, then decide between a higher UBL budget and splitting UBL's module into build units (FISCAL-GENERIC's unit shape).")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 7u32, "2026-10-27T12:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 08:51 fiscal: UBL MEASURED ALONE (job-1793101531887-fiscal-ubl-time, node, budget raised for the run only): generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 (3,045 KB, 56,090 lines) PASSES its test (1/1, 0 memo hits) when given time. Cell 2 took 2,852 s: rustc 2,626 s (92%), Spiral compile 172 s, patch 19 s, run 6 s; cell 0 186 s. So the dominant phase is rustc on the generated Rust, not Spiral. FIX (in the tree, fiscal/src/generator.spi): write_generated_module sets each module's cell budget to max(1,200 s, 1.3 s per KB of generated Spiral) (cell_budget_ms), calibrated on this run (0.94 s/KB on a saturated node, +40% headroom): UBL gets ~3,960 s, modules under ~920 KB keep 1,200 s. Type-checked. The structural lever is less generated Rust: XSD-FIELD-DESCRIPTORS and FISCAL-VERSIONED-FAMILIES. Mirror ublt removed after the verdict; the node's stale worktree registrations pruned (spiral 51 -> 21, fiscal 7 -> 5).")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 8u32, "2026-10-27T13:32Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 09:25 fiscal: from cj-proof (job-1793100366145, exit 1, 4 broken claims, mirror cj removed after reading): (a) the lib-test claims for xml/xsd_emit/json_schema got 0 ROWS, i.e. lib_gate never ran its tests there (environment, VOID, not red); my filter hid its output, so the next proof logs lib_gate whole; (b) regeneration, the UBL repeated-choice readers (x_transform_type, x_key_info_type through xml.claimed_children) and the CFDI prefix fix all HOLD; CFDI, CT-e v4 and KSeF pass every test; (c) NF-e v3.10's bench cell TIMED OUT at 1,200 s (its example cell took 1,183 s, headroom 0.99) on a saturated node: load and budget, not logic. Two generator fixes, in the tree (fiscal/src/generator.spi, type-checked, formatter no-op): (1) ONE CELL: every rust test cell recompiles the whole generated module, and the bench port had added a cell per family (~1.5x rustc per unit); the bench body now runs INSIDE the example test's cell (no _bench_runs test any more), after the example write and the real-document checks (whose trailing match is bound as 'inl _ =' so the composition parses); (2) cell budget = max(2,400 s, 1.6 s per KB of generated Spiral) (was max(1,200, 1.3/KB)): rustc is not linear in module size and the node runs saturated. NEW FEATURE, real documents: family_root.documents lists real documents per family (UBL: the 7 Peppol BIS 3.0.20 invoice examples + CEN TC434 ubl-tc434-example1..10); the example cell reads each with the typed reader, writes it back and compares with the new lib xml.first_difference (local names, attributes as sets ignoring xmlns/xmlns:*/xsi:*, children in order, trimmed leaf text; returns the first difference's path, e.g. '/r/a: missing attribute k=1'), failing with the count and the first failing document. lib xml.spi gains first_difference (+ elements_of, content_attributes, has_attribute_pair, missing_attribute) with test first_difference_ignores_prefixes_and_names_the_first_lost_content; a helper I first named single_root shadowed xml.single_root (schematron.spi:80 broke) and was renamed into a test-local root_of: xml, schematron, xsd_emit, json_schema, xsd_constraints all 0 errors. Proof: all-proof (prepared, on the reused geo mirror after geo-proof2).")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_9 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 9u32, "2026-10-30T02:03Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 22:03 fiscal: GROOM, evidence after the 09:25 note: job-1793084333885-fiscal-full-build (node, 10-27 03:48-05:00, full build.ps1, no -Files, memo off) gated 33 units and every one exited 0 (generator 9/9, corpus_checks 15/15, every generated_* module and its _certificates unit), so each generated module was gated in one full run. The job's own exit 1 came from a step after the units, not yet read (shared with FISCAL-CI-WORKFLOW n5). Since then signing stages (M6) changed the signed families, so MDF-e/CL need the M6 land before this row can close. Progress stays 85. [CORRECTION 2026-10-29 22:57: the full build had 35 units, not 33 (my regex missed 2), and generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 FAILED 1 test (\"build.ps1 / 1 problems: generated_ubl_invoice_2_1: failed 1\"), so \"every unit exited 0\" is wrong: 34 of 35 were green, the exception being the UBL-JSON round trip tracked on FISCAL-MY-MYINVOIS.]")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_10 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 10u32, "2026-10-30T15:26Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 11:26 fiscal: GATE GAP FOUND, budget not robust under a shared node. Every generated unit's cell budget comes from generator.spi cell_budget_ms = max(2400000, bytes/10*16), i.e. 1.6 ms per byte. generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 (3.19 MB) ran 2852 s on a quiet node, ~0.9 ms/byte, so headroom is only ~1.8x, and fiscal-p7-set-ublj2 (10-30) was killed at its 5108672 ms budget under node contention (VOID; FISCAL-MY-MYINVOIS n22). Same pattern in generator.spi's own header (--timeout 1400000): 10 of 22 rows died at exactly 1400 s in fiscal-p7-e3b-generator. Rule (memory no-quiet-machine-wait): budgets must hold under the shared node, not a quiet one. Plan: factor 16 -> 48 (~5x quiet headroom on the largest unit), and the generator header 1400000 -> 3600000. Ordered AFTER FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND, because the factor rewrites the --timeout header of every generated unit and would void that round's generated-identical gate mid-proof. The factor-48 effect is being measured now, mirror-only, by fiscal-p7-set-ublj3.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_11 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 11u32, "2026-10-30T16:24Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 12:24 fiscal: BUDGET MECHANICS FINDING, for the fix: Cells records each cell's budget as the cell's parsed timeout (notebook.ex:591, opts[:timeout] || the cell's timeout || default), and the spiral-tests row reports it as timeout_ms. Observed on 10-30: in corpus_checks.spi, whose FILE HEADER is ///- --package . --timeout 1200000, a test-level ///- --timeout 3600000 before ///> rust was NOT applied (row timeout_ms 1200000). In schematron_run.spi, whose header has no --timeout, the same test-level form WAS applied (900000). So a header --timeout wins over a per-test one. Fix design follows from that: raise the header, don't add per-test lines. Done in the DIAN/RO layer: corpus_checks header 1200000 -> 3600000 (C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\e3live-dian\\src\\corpus_checks.spi 0C6ADC10). The generator.spi header (1400000) and the generated units' cell_budget_ms get the same treatment after FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_12 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 12u32, "2026-10-30T23:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 19:51 fiscal: the budget fix is DRAFTED, ready for after FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND: C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\e3live-budget\\src\\generator.spi (base 59AD5447 = the edition-3 draft generator): cell_budget_ms factor 16 -> 48 (largest unit, 3.19 MB: about 15.3M ms, about 4.25 h; well inside i32) and the generator's own header --timeout 1400000 -> 3600000. No test pins the arithmetic (grep of e3live src). Evidence the factor holds: fiscal-p7-set-ublj3 ran the generated UBL invoice unit to a verdict under node load with the factor-48 header mirror-only. Apply after the user's edition-3 commit, then a set-proof on the largest units.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_13 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 13u32, "2026-10-31T02:30Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 22:30 fiscal: generator follow-up DRAFTED in the post-edition-3 layer C:\\home\\data\\fiscal-drafts\\e3live-budget\\src\\generator.spi (now 62FDA504; base 59AD5447 = the edition-3 draft generator): its emitted bench test writes 'match file_system.directory_create_all folder with' instead of 'file_system.create_dir_all folder |> resultm.unbox', so every generated unit's bench path compiles on Delphi; the generator's own create_dir_all call (line 26, Rust-run) stays. Same layer as FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE's budget change (header 3600000, factor 48). ORDER: the Delphi lib program lands first (delphi-arms.kit adds file_system.directory_create_all), then the user's edition-3 commit, then this layer, then regeneration (the generated/ drift guard then expects the new text in all 74 units).")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_14 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 14u32, "2026-11-01T03:40Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 23:40 fiscal: MOVED (groom 10-31 night). The Delphi lib landed tonight (directory_create_all is live), so the generator bench-line change from the archived e3live-budget layer can go into the tree now: src/generator.spi:818 'match file_system.create_dir_all folder |> resultm.unbox with' -> 'match file_system.directory_create_all folder with'. NEXT: the edit (CPU-light), then ONE full regeneration on the node (it rewrites every unit's bench cell) + bring-generated-home, committed together (drift guard).")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_15 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 15u32, "2026-11-01T03:44Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 23:52 fiscal: NEXT NODE-RUNNABLE (groom): once the bench-line edit at src/generator.spi:818 is in the tree, run a full regeneration on the node (fresh mirror, set-proof-c.ps1, build.ps1 without -Files). Pass = generated/ matches after bring-generated-home, and bench.tsv covers every unit. It queues behind job-1793494454747 (t1031u UBL drift, which reports on XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE).")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_16 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 16u32, "2026-11-01T03:59Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 00:10 fiscal: in the tree (unstaged): (1) src/generator.spi bench line now uses file_system.directory_create_all folder (Delphi-safe); (2) both emitted writes_its_example tests now run file_system.directory_create_all (sample_path \"\") |> ignore before writing, so a unit run outside build.ps1 no longer panics on a missing samples/ (the node UBL gate hit this). Both change every unit's text: they need ONE full regeneration (node) + bring-generated-home, committed together for the drift guard. Shared with the EDITION3-ROUND respell proof.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_17 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 17u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 08:37 fiscal: QUALITY SPEC (groom round 4). PRIORITY P0: CI is red on \"generated: 44 files differ\" until the regenerated units are committed.\nDONE WHEN: CI on the next push prints \"build.ps1 / generated / matches the committed copy\" on ubuntu-24.04 and windows-2025, and every generated unit file is green (\"build.ps1 / all N test files green\").\nNEXT STEP: when node job-1793506463392 (t1101) finishes, read its \"e3-regen /\" lines (node job tail). Expected: \"changed beyond the bench + samples-folder lines\" lists only generated_cl_envio_dte_v10 and the CR units with Ubicacion. Then scp C:/home/remote/t1101/fiscal/generated to a scratch folder and run scripts/bring-generated-home.ps1 -From <scratch> -Pattern '^generated_' -Compiler <pack-10 dll>, review git diff --stat generated/, and leave it unstaged for the user.\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN (generator side of coverage): the per-unit test set stops being string concatenation chosen by module-name tests. family_root.extras (union family_extra, done 11-01) is step 1. Step 2: \":| unit_check = | WritesExample | KeepsRawStringsOut | ReadsDocuments | Bench | ModelTypeChecks\", a list derived once from family_root, with one emitter per case through a prototype (\":+ check_text unit_check\"), so a family cannot get a check by accident of its name. Module names become a nominal \":! module_name = string\" built from the family, so \"generated_...\" literals are not compared anywhere. MODEL: the walker prototypes in xsd_emit (\":+ ... exampling\").")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_18 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 18u32, "2026-11-01T13:16Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 09:32 fiscal: UNITS HOME (unstaged): the 44 regenerated files from node t1101 (lib = spiral e0461f15 file for file, formatter pack 10) copied into fiscal/generated: git diff --stat = 44 files, 120 insertions, 68 deletions, 0 untracked. That is exactly CI's \"generated: 44 files differ\". Content: bench line + samples-folder line + size-derived timeout header in every unit, plus cl_envio xmlns:SiiDte and 6 CR Canton/Distrito 01. With src/corpus_checks.spi (283 + 24 pins) and scripts/build.ps1 (Windows socket retry), the user's next commit should turn the drift guard green.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_19 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 19u32, "2026-11-01T21:59Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 18:0x: two of three done conditions MET on CI run 41314140694 (push f6abb23) and 41327004564 (6d102ac): \"build.ps1 / generated / matches the committed copy\" on ubuntu-24.04 AND windows-2025, and every generated unit green on ubuntu. Remaining: 7 generated units on windows-2025 (nfe_v4, cte/cte_os/cte_simp/gtve v4_00, cfdi_v4_0 + cfdi_pagos_2_0 certificates) crash the compile server with a stack overflow: COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW, fixed by the compiler lane (server compiles on a 1 GB thread, proven 7/7 on the node) and shipping as pack15a. It needs the user's spiral push to reach CI. Dep added; close on the first windows-green run.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_20 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 20u32, "2026-11-02T00:10Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 20:1x: DONE. CI run 41351043040 (b2fab25) prints \"build.ps1 / generated / matches the committed copy\" and \"all 86 test files green and every example validates\" on ubuntu-24.04 AND windows-2025. The last blocker, COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW, is fixed in spiral main 73e4d863.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_21 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 21u32, "2026-11-02T14:07Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:07 x: GIT CONDITION RE-CHECKED from the tree (groom of user waits). A read-only survey at 09:4x shows every active repo 0 commits ahead of origin: the user committed and pushed alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, kit, fiscal, html, polyglot, regex, spiral, tibia and x in the last 1-3 h. i574n.github.io is the exception (3 unstaged files, last commit 2 years ago). So this item's user step is probably MET. What is left is deterministic and belongs to the owner, not the user: confirm the step's files are in HEAD (git log -- <paths>) and that CI on HEAD is green (gh run list --commit <HEAD>), then close it. Only a red or missing file sends it back to the user queue. Pages enablement (HTML, REGEX-BENCH-PAGE) is a real user step, since it is a public repo setting.")
inl note_of_fiscal_package_coverage_22 () = Note ("FISCAL-PACKAGE-COVERAGE", 22u32, "2026-11-02T15:01Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-02 11:01 DONE (star-exchange groom). All 81 generated/<family> units carry test blocks, and build.ps1's Select-TestFiles gates every one. CI c8830d7 (run 41366612730) was green on ubuntu-24.04 + windows-2025 with every test file executed. The 11-01 close was lost (agile status lost update); this re-closes it. x's 10:07 git-condition note is answered: the files are in HEAD.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generated_style_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERATED-STYLE", 1u32, "2026-10-25T07:28Z", "fiscal", "03:28 fiscal: the emitter is lib xsd_emit now (fiscal generator.spi is its thin client), so this item lands in lib. Draft, landing form: session scratchpad xsd-emit-style\\apply.py <path to xsd_emit.spi> [--apply], anchored, base xsd_emit 7FDAE457DCCC540F; format --check clean on a copy. (1) _of_string emits match s with / | \"v\" => Some Case / | _ => None instead of if false then None / elif / else None. (2) case_name names a URI value by its fragment, else its last path segment (TTransformURI_enveloped_signature instead of TTransformURI_http___www_w3_org_2000_09_xmldsig_enveloped_signature); unused_case still breaks collisions. (3) the last Cons pattern in xsd_emit (union_text example_part) becomes _ :: _. Tests: names_follow_spiral_conventions gains the two URI cases; new enumerations_parse_through_one_match. fiscal generates_the_uf_code_union then expects | \"35\" => Some TCodUfIBGE_35. Land condition: after job-1792912920241-fiscal-ci-local ends and no other lane reads lib xsd_emit (main pack reviews compile lib programs); then lib gate on xsd_emit + json_schema and a fiscal build rerun. Not in this draft: unforgeable nominals for facet types (changes of_xml failure semantics, needs its own rule) and the 120-column fit (already in xsd_emit fitted_*).")
inl note_of_fiscal_generated_style_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERATED-STYLE", 2u32, "2026-10-25T14:00Z", "fiscal", "10:00 fiscal: slice extended at main's handoff (list sugar, user 10-25 rule): xsd-emit-style\\apply.py now also converts the 5 emitted-string Cons sites of xsd_emit (944, 1097, 1269, 1391 and repeated_cons, which becomes repeated_items + repeated_list emitting [v0; v1] and Nil for zero), so generated modules use list sugar too; new xsd-emit-style\\apply-xsd.py converts xsd.spi's 4 nested test-pattern Cons (one-element matches become | Complex (...) :: Nil =>, the tuple-slot cons keeps its parens). On copies: 0 Cons left in xsd_emit and xsd, format --check clean. fiscal generator.spi's own emitted strings converted in the tree (0 Cons left). All lib slices (xsd, xsd_emit, xsd_resolve) gate together in lib-gate-xsd.ps1 behind the FatturaPA probe.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generated_style_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERATED-STYLE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T18:01Z", "fiscal", "13:59 fiscal: XSD-SLICES LANDED in the tree lib by job-1792950895715 (kit land.ps1 + the staged apply): xsd_regex 528907A279636CC1, xsd_emit 5C364F8DA3287371, xsd_resolve 170A91102936C86C, xsd 5CF595CEF83FE12C, byte-identical to the GREEN candidate (the kit's post-land format was a no-op). The lib now has: counted RxRepeat + XSD block escapes (xsd_regex); match-based enumerations, URI case names and list sugar in emitted code (xsd_emit); namespace-qualified type names with collision tags (xsd_resolve); parenthesized tuple-slot list patterns (xsd); tests interleaved. The same job then applied fiscal-post-land (271-pattern oracle derived from deps, KSeF known_defect None, gitignore and README) and is waiting for a test-suite window for the full fiscal build, which checks KSeF's example: it must now pass with no pin.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generated_style_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERATED-STYLE", 4u32, "2026-10-28T10:36Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 06:31 fiscal: PAUSED with its dep FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS. Next step: after the format check, census the emitted _of_string openings in one regenerated module.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generated_style_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERATED-STYLE", 5u32, "2026-10-31T21:09Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 17:09 fiscal: RE-DECIDED: resume as Planned. Its blocker FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS is Done (format-generated.ps1 runs in build.ps1 before the drift guard; generated/ is committed formatted). Next step unchanged from 10-28: census the emitted _of_string openings in one regenerated, formatted module (e.g. generated_nfe_v4 0D73AB909800 in the tree).")
inl note_of_fiscal_generated_style_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERATED-STYLE", 6u32, "2026-11-01T14:38Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 10:49 fiscal: QUALITY SPEC (round 5). DONE WHEN: grep over generated/ counts 0 \"_of_string\" bodies opening with \"if false then None elif\" (65 in nfe_v4 alone at filing); the units stay formatter-stable (format-generated --check 0 failed) and every unit gate stays green (behaviour unchanged). NEXT STEP: after FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND, change the emitter of enumeration of_string in xsd_emit (variant_arms :152-159 builds the if/elif chain) to emit a match, regenerate. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: an enumeration's of_string is a match from the literal to the union case generated from the value set (a total table, no if-chains, no fall-through None sentinel arm except the final unknown-value case); with XSD-CODEC-PROTOTYPES it is the \"codec\" instance of that union, not a free function.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generated_style_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERATED-STYLE", 7u32, "2026-11-02T15:04Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-02 11:01 DONE by its DONE WHEN (n of 11-01 10:49): grep over generated/ counts 0 'if false then None elif' bodies (was 65 in nfe_v4 alone) and 0 mangled '_http___' union cases. format-generated: 81 formatted, 0 failed (CI run 41427219939). Every unit gate is green in that run; its only red is generated drift, tracked on XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES n13.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_1 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 1u32, "2026-10-25T00:53Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:53 main: PLAN, written before measuring (step 1 is a CPU-light inventory; nothing is switched until its row is measured).\nScope excludes apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs and tmp/compiler/host (the compiler). Inventory, per repo under C:\\home\\git (spiral, eoie, x, tibia, dice, alphabet, polyglot, fiscal, blog, dir-tree-html, i574n.github):\n(a) tracked .fs/.fsx/.fsi/.fsproj files (git ls-files by extension), e.g. apps/compiler/tmp/formatter/*.fsx (Format.fsx, Convert.fsx with its sync-core dll/), lib/spiral/spiral_runtime.fs;\n(b) F# as a runtime target: '///> fsharp' test targets in .spi files, '| Fsharp =>' arms that are the only arm of a lib function, the matrix F# native tier (FSHARP-NATIVE-TIER / FSHARP-WARM-RUNNER), and the contracts suite's vehicle (CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG);\n(c) .NET as a tool: dotnet fsi / dotnet run / FSI scripts called from .ps1 or CI, Cells' F# builder (fsi_chain.ex, fsi_session.ex), PowerShell scripts that load .NET types for logic (Xml schema validation in fiscal validate-example.ps1, regex oracles in pattern-oracle.ps1);\n(d) CI: workflow steps that install dotnet only for (a)-(c).\nOutput: one row per site with its repo, path, category, what it does, its users, and measured compile+run time on F# against the best existing replacement (Rust or Zig through Cells, or a lib function).\nDecision rule per row, written now: switch when the replacement is at least 1.5x faster end to end (compile + run, median of 3, quiet box per bench-pair) with identical output; keep F# where it is the only arm, or where the replacement would be hand-crafted host text (GRANULAR-HEAL rules); .NET used as an oracle (XmlSchemaSet, .NET Regex) stays until a lib equivalent agrees on the whole corpus. Each switch becomes its own item.\nRelated, not duplicated: FSX-FROM-SPI (generate the compiler's .fsx), FPARSEC-RETIRE, CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG, PWSH-FREE, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_2 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 2u32, "2026-10-25T03:52Z", "eoie", "23:52 eoie rows (eoie team; inventory by note 1's method). (a) Tracked F# files: none. git ls-files *.fs, *.fsx, *.fsi and *.fsproj in eoie returns nothing. (b) F# as a runtime target: none. 0 '///> fsharp' test targets and 0 '| Fsharp =>' arms in eoie's .spi; every eoie crate compiles to Rust only. (c) .NET as a tool, three sites: [c1] compiler invocation through dotnet in compiler-contracts/test-attestation.ps1, renew-cold-rebuild.ps1 and renew-strict-evidence.ps1 (Resolve-SpiralDotnet, then dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll), plus test-regeneration.ps1 and build.ps1 --compiler. That is the compiler itself, out of scope, and it moves when the compiler does. Users: eoie regen, attestation and release. [c2] PowerShell as a .NET host for logic: compiler-contracts/test-*.ps1 and build.ps1 use [regex]::Match/Replace and [IO.File] to parse receipts and check generated Rust. This is scripting, not F#, and belongs to PWSH-FREE; the contracts suite's vehicle is CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG. Nothing here is an oracle needing agreement on a corpus. [c3] eoie release and train job scripts (pwsh, session-scoped): same class as c2; EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN's train-as-verbs moves them into eoie commands. (d) CI: gh-pages.yml runs pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1 and publish.ps1; there is no setup-dotnet step and nothing installs dotnet for F#. Result: eoie has no F# site to time against a replacement, so the 1.5x switch rule has no row to apply to. Its .NET reliance is the compiler (out of scope) and PowerShell (PWSH-FREE).")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_3 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 3u32, "2026-10-25T03:53Z", "x", "10-24 23:53 x: INVENTORY x (git grep over tracked files, read-only). (a) Tracked .fs/.fsx/.fsi/.fsproj: NONE. (b) F# as a runtime target: NONE. No .spi has a ///> fsharp target, and there are no Fsharp arms (no '| Fsharp =>' and no backend_switch 'Fsharp ='). x's notebooks target rust/zig/gleam only. (c) .NET as a tool: every hit is either the Spiral compiler (out of scope) or PowerShell itself. Compiler rows, out of scope, listed for completeness: apps/client/build.ps1:23 (dotnet SpiralCompiler --x-compat, the postability gate; users: x build, CI); apps/sim/build.ps1:16-19 (resolves dotnet + the single-flight compiler dll for BuildSpiral Gleam; users: sim build, CI); scripts/gate.ps1:14-23 (dotnet compiler --backend Rust for the -Compiler candidate regen; users: candidate reviews). PowerShell [regex] for logic, minor and PWSH-FREE's scope rather than F#: apps/client/e2e/e2e.ps1:64 (parses the README tutorial into steps; users: e2e, CI), and scripts/gleam-modules.ps1:60/100/106/120 (call-graph and import scan; it retires with X-PARALLEL-BUILD, so don't measure it). (d) CI: .github/workflows/ci.yml has no dotnet step of its own; dotnet comes in through spiral's scripts/init-app-build.ps1 (called by x's scripts/init.ps1) for the compiler. So x has no F#/.NET site to switch; x's .NET dependency is entirely the compiler's.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_4 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 4u32, "2026-10-25T03:53Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 23:53 tibia rows (CPU-light inventory, no measurement yet). (a) Tracked .fs/.fsx/.fsi/.fsproj: 0. (b) F# as a runtime target: 0. No '///> fsharp' test target and no 'Fsharp' arm in src/ or apps/; the target reasons are c oracle, wgsl GPU, lua live client, rust for the 2 golden seeds plus apps/map and apps/offscreen. So tibia never uses Cells' F# builder. (c) .NET as a tool: 1. The compiler via dotnet (SpiralCompiler.dll net11.0) in apps/offscreen/build.ps1:14, apps/map/run.ps1:15 and 77 (format), scripts/predict.ps1:34 (--backend C), live/run-policy-scenario.ps1:18 (--backend Lua). That's the compiler itself, out of scope. 2. PowerShell .NET types for logic, all [regex] parsing of logs and dumps: analysis/pz-in-traces.ps1, pr12-secondary.ps1 and pc4-analysis.ps1 (already queued for eoie table verbs under TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE); apps/map/run.ps1:38-57 (dump and scan parsing); live/run-arena.ps1:40 (gold sum); live/run-policy-scenario.ps1:93 and 138-145 (rewrites the bot's Lua and config text, which goes away with TIBIA-NO-HAND-LUA M5); scripts/score.ps1:2 (OrderedDictionary parameter only). These belong to PWSH-FREE and eoie verbs rather than an F# switch. (d) CI: .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml:49 installs actions/setup-dotnet 9.0.202, yet the compiler runs on net11.0 from spiral-bin toolchains (Ensure-SpiralCompiler). So this step looks vestigial; the check is a CI run without it. Decision per this item's rule: no tibia site needs an F#-to-Rust/Zig switch; the CI dotnet 9 step is a removal candidate (its own row, checked by one CI run).")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_5 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 5u32, "2026-10-25T03:53Z", "main", "2026-10-24 23:53 main: inventory progress. eoie (its note) and x (note 3) have no F# sites; their only .NET is the compiler call and PowerShell's own .NET types. tibia's rows are coming. Still to inventory: spiral (outside the compiler), dice, alphabet, polyglot, fiscal, blog, dir-tree-html, i574n.github. Known so far: apps/compiler/tmp/formatter/*.fsx (Format.fsx, Convert.fsx), lib/spiral/spiral_runtime.fs, the matrix F# tier, and my edition-3 land checks, which compiled apps/spiral to F# (dropped; memory no-fsharp-residual-checks).")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_6 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 6u32, "2026-10-25T03:56Z", "main", "2026-10-24 23:56 main: inventory for main's repos (git ls-files / git grep, compiler core and host excluded). Ranked by likely payoff. (1) 214 '///> fsharp' test targets in .spi files, each run through dotnet fsi in Cells: lib sm' 40, date_time 37, parsing 25, base 20, am' 10, file_system 8, reflection 7, plus apps/compiler/spi/tokens.spi 13. Measure each file's F# target against its Rust/Zig target under the 1.5x rule; this is the biggest lib-gate lever. (2) 60 '| Fsharp =>' arms in lib (file_system 31, sm' 4, env/runtime/threading 3, am'/base/console 2): first find which are the ONLY real arm of their function; those block retiring F# targets. (3) Hand-written F# outside spiral: dice lib/dice.fsx and lib/fsharp/dice_fsharp.fs; alphabet apps/documents/documents.fsx and apps/hangul/*.fs (5 files); polyglot lib/fsharp/*.fs (Async, AsyncSeq, Common, CommonFSharp, FileSystem), apps/parser/*.fs, apps/spiral/temp/cube (cube.fsx plus its fsi build.ps1). These are likely Fable-era remnants: run the stem check (memory dangling-check-by-stem) before calling any of them dead. (4) Tooling: apps/compiler/tmp/formatter (8 files: Format.fsx, Convert.fsx, ...) and tmp/lanes (6) are compiler-adjacent dev scripts on fsi, where FSX-FROM-SPI applies; apps/cells fsi_session.ex and targets.ex are the F# cell runner. (5) 576 tracked samples main.fsx files are the matrix F# tier's outputs, which is F# as a backend under test, in scope only if the F# backend itself is retired. (6) CI: setup-dotnet in spiral, dice, alphabet, polyglot, dir-tree-html and i574n.github workflows, for the compiler (out of scope) plus any (1)-(4) users. fiscal and blog have no F#.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_7 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 7u32, "2026-10-25T07:23Z", "main", "10-25 03:23 main: row: alphabet/apps/documents/build.ps1:21 compiles documents.spi to F# (documents.fsx, 629 KB tracked) before the Rust build on every run, including CI. The fsx has no consumer in alphabet: grep finds only build.ps1 and the file itself. CI's setup-dotnet stays either way, because the compiler is a .NET DLL. Cost: one extra full compile per build. Candidate: drop the F# step and the tracked fsx together, mirrored in dice if it has the same step.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_8 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 8u32, "2026-10-25T11:46Z", "main", "10-25 07:46 main: row: apps/spiral's F# project path (persist_code_project writing <name>.fsproj + dotnet publish in publish_project) has no caller in any repo. It is being deleted as dead code under APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL (job-1792928699219, byte-identical spiral.rs proof). The F# cell magic (Fsharp blocks in export) is a separate, live path.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_9 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 9u32, "2026-10-29T06:02Z", "main", "10-29 02:1x main: row n7 (alphabet documents) RESOLVED by removal, not a switch (unstaged): apps/documents/build.ps1 no longer compiles documents.spi to F# before the Rust build, and the generated, tracked documents.fsx (12,0xx lines) is removed. Evidence that it was dead: git grep -F 'documents.fsx' finds 0 references in alphabet, dice, polyglot, spiral, x and kit (positive control: 'documents.spi' hits 2 in alphabet), and 0 .fsx references in alphabet/.github. History: commit 3302cc3 (10-06) only ported the Supervisor-era '--build-file documents.spi documents.fsx' step onto BuildSpiral -Backend Fsharp; F# was the product in that era, and Rust is the product now. Its throw 'FSHARP-FAILED' was an F# identity gate, which the no-fsharp-residual-checks rule rules out. Saves one full compile per build, CI included; build.ps1 parses clean. Not run end to end (cargo build plus the 900 s run): the first CI run, or the next local build, is the check. dice has no documents app (alphabet-mirrors-dice: nothing to mirror).")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_10 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 10u32, "2026-10-30T14:33Z", "main", "10-30 10:33 main: n6 step (2) DONE, a static census on the live lib (read-only; arms counted per definition from '| X =>' and backend_switch 'X = \\' lines; '_' fallbacks ignored). Positive control: it finds file_system.create_directory, the known F#-only def from backend-switch-idiom. 28 lib definitions have F# as their ONLY real arm, so on Rust they compile to compile_error! and they block retiring F# targets. By file: file_system 12 (file_info, directory_info + name/full_name/attributes, file_attributes_reparse_point, file_attributes_has_flag, create_directory, directory_get_files, write_all_text_async, file_info_link_target, trace_file); am' 6 (average, parallel_map, map', sort, sort_descending, transpose); console 2 (reset_color, set_foreground_color); networking 2 (new_tcp_client, client_connect_async); runtime 2 (process_id, current_process_kill); sm' 2 (split_string, join'); async 1 (value_task_as_task); guid 1 (to_string). TRIAGE RULE, fixed before the next step: a def whose TYPE is a .NET type (file_info, directory_info, value_task_as_task, the tcp client) is F#-by-nature and stays F#-only, documented. Every other def gets a Rust arm, or a redirect to an existing portable one (create_directory -> create_dir_all; parallel_map -> par'.map, which landed with LIB-PAR-MAP; sort/sort_descending/transpose/average/map' as plain Spiral over listm). NEXT, owner main, CPU-light: the consumer count for those ~20 portable candidates (grep the repos), to order them by use. Then step (1), the 214 '///> fsharp' targets measured under the 1.5x rule, which is CPU-bound and waits for a quiet box after PACK-8.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_11 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 11u32, "2026-10-30T14:35Z", "main", "10-30 10:35 main: consumer count for the ~19 portable F#-only defs (git grep -P over the .spi files of 10 repos, deps/target excluded). Qualified external uses, plus unqualified uses inside the def's own module. Positive control: kit has 2 uses of file_system.create_dir_all. An earlier pass used spiral as the control, saw 0, and was discarded, because spiral has no qualified caller of it. Ranked: guid.to_string 8 (spiral 7, dir-tree-html 1); runtime.current_process_kill 3; am'.transpose 2; console.reset_color 2; then 1 each: file_system.trace_file (+3 in-module, and it calls create_directory), sm'.join' (+2), sm'.split_string, am'.sort_descending, am'.average, am'.parallel_map, console.set_foreground_color. 0 external uses: am'.sort (2 in-module), create_directory (2 in-module, via trace_file), write_all_text_async, file_info_link_target, file_attributes_has_flag, directory_get_files, runtime.process_id, am'.map' (0 anywhere: a dead-code candidate, to be checked by stem before removing). ORDER for the Rust arms: guid.to_string first (most used; a Rust Uuid/format arm), then current_process_kill (std::process::exit or a kill of self), transpose, reset_color/set_foreground_color (ANSI escapes cover Rust, Zig and C at once), then the 1-use defs. Each one lands as a small guarded lib do with a '///> rust' test row. Lib work, owner main, in the next lib window after PACK-8.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_12 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 12u32, "2026-10-30T14:36Z", "main", "10-30 10:36 main: CORRECTION to n10/n11. The census wrongly ignored every '_' arm, but most '_' arms are REAL portable bodies. am'.average/sort_descending/transpose fold in Spiral, and guid.to_string's '_ => !x' already serves Rust (its to_string_is_lowercase_text test runs on rust). Re-counted, with a '_' arm a fallback only when its body is failwith, 'no arm', compile_error or a 'null ()' stub. Controls: create_directory is IN the set (the known Rust compile_error case); am'.average is OUT (real '_' body). F#-ONLY = 15 defs: file_system 11 (file_info, directory_info, directory_info_name/full_name/attributes, file_attributes_reparse_point, file_attributes_has_flag, create_directory, directory_get_files, write_all_text_async, file_info_link_target), networking 2 (new_tcp_client, client_connect_async), async 1 (value_task_as_task), runtime 1 (process_id). By n10's triage rule, 11 have a .NET TYPE in their signature (file_info, directory_info, the attributes, value_task, the tcp client), so they stay F#-only. Only 4 are real candidates: runtime.process_id (0 external uses; Rust std::process::id()), directory_get_files (0 external; std::fs::read_dir), write_all_text_async (0 external), and create_directory (used only by trace_file in-module; it returns the .NET directory_info, so the fix is trace_file calling create_dir_all, not a Rust arm). n11's ranking is VOID, since guid/transpose/console/sm'/am' already have portable arms. The real Rust lever is n6 step (1), the 214 '///> fsharp' test targets (CPU-bound, after PACK-8), and the 4 small heals above in a lib window.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_13 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 13u32, "2026-10-30T14:37Z", "main", "10-30 10:37 main: n12 follow-up. NO lib heal is needed for the 4 candidates. trace_file is F#-only by design: its '_' arm is a deliberate no-op, and its body uses env.get_entry_assembly_name and the async run, so its create_directory call never reaches Rust. directory_get_files, write_all_text_async and process_id have 0 external and 0 non-F# consumers. So the lib's 15 F#-only defs block NO Rust consumer today, and step (2) is CLOSED with no edits. Left: step (1), the 214 '///> fsharp' test targets. Whether each one can retire is a measurement, F# against Rust/Zig under the 1.5x rule, which is CPU-bound and runs on the box after the PACK-8 review and install, or on a quiet node. Step (3) is hand-written F# outside spiral: dice lib/dice.fsx and lib/fsharp/dice_fsharp.fs, and alphabet apps/hangul/*.fs, per n6.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_14 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 14u32, "2026-10-30T14:38Z", "main", "10-30 10:38 main: step (3) triaged (read-only). (a) dice lib/dice.fsx (326 KB, tracked) is GENERATED: lib/build.ps1:27 compiles dice.spi to F#, publishes it and asserts the run, as ONE of four asserted product backends (F#, Rust, TypeScript, Python+Cuda; lines 27-75). Unlike alphabet's documents.fsx (removed 10-29, 0 consumers), the F# row here is a deliberate multi-backend product check, so it STAYS. Dropping it is a product call, and no rule answers it. (b) dice lib/fsharp/dice_fsharp.fs (3 KB, hand-written over polyglot Common.fs) is a live FIXTURE: spiral apps/cells/test/spiral/document_test.exs and apps/cells/README.md name it, so it STAYS. (c) alphabet apps/hangul/*.fs (hangul.fs 16 KB, hanja.fs 466 KB, cp949_from/to.fs 716 KB each) is hand-written F#. Its move to Spiral is the existing item HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL (team main, lib by need), so it isn't duplicated here. So only step (1) is left: the 214 '///> fsharp' test-target measurement (CPU-bound, after PACK-8).")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_15 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 15u32, "2026-10-31T14:53Z", "main", "10-31 10:53 main: step (1) MEASUREMENT RUNNING on the node: job-1793458299079-main-fsharp-measure (8 h cap). The 27 lib files that carry '///> fsharp' test targets (223 sites today: am', base, benchmark, common, console, convert, crypto, csv, date_time, env, file_system, float_text, guid, listm', math, optionm', par', parsing, platform, process, reflection, resultm, seq, sm', testing, threading, trace) each run through lib_gate on a copy of the node mirror's lib; --gate implies --no-memo, so every row is a real run. Rows (File, Test, Target, Status, Ms, Memo) collect in C:\\home\\remote\\main-manifest\\fsharp-measure-rows.tsv, progress in fsharp-measure-log.txt. Rule to apply when it ends: per test, ratio = F# ms / best of Rust and Zig ms; under the 1.5x rule a test's F# target retires when the native target passes and is within 1.5x of F#. Note: wall time includes each target's compile, so the ratio compares compile+run, the cost a gate actually pays.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_16 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 16u32, "2026-10-31T16:11Z", "main", "10-31 12:11 main: step (1) MEASURED (node job-1793458299079, 10:51-11:58, 27 files, 1,025 rows, 0 memo, 2 failed: file_system's 2 known reds). 212 tests carry an F# target (211 pass). 133 also have a passing Rust/Zig target: 119 are within the 1.5x rule (native ms <= 1.5 x F# ms; median native/F# 0.72, q1 0.56, q3 0.99) and 14 are slower (max 3.21x: process split_args/split_command, convert try_convert_2, csv x2, sm' listed_compares_trimmed_parts; wall time includes each target's compile, so these are rustc-compile-heavy tests). 79 have no passing Rust/Zig target: 52 pass on another backend (gleam/ts/...), 27 are F#-only (benchmark x2, common new_disposable_2/3, date_time's guid/timestamp family, file_system find_parent, ...). DECIDED by the pre-registered rule: retire the F# target on the 119 tests (they cost 1877 s of F# per full gate); keep it on the 14 slow ones and the 79 without a native target. Per-test table: %TEMP%\\fsharp-measure\\per-test.csv. NEXT: a generic tool that removes one '///> fsharp' line above named test blocks, then land by file batches through lib_land_queue (each close types and runs the remaining targets).")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_17 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 17u32, "2026-10-31T16:28Z", "main", "10-31 12:28 main: RETIRE LAND QUEUED. New generic tool apps/cells/spi/drop_test_target.ps1 (-Lib -Target -Tests 'file|test,...' -OutDir; case-insensitive test names; removes the target line from the '///' group directly above each named test block, reports NOT FOUND per file). On the 119-test retire set: removed 119 of 119 in 19 files; every diff is ONLY '-///> fsharp' lines; canonical round trip 19/19, 0 non-blank differences. Queued as land-queue-fsharp.json (3 batches: 7/7/5 files, bases = live hashes) behind the runner that ends with Cells window 6 (waits on its process exit). Each close: signatures, document_test, type_probe. The tests keep their passing Rust/Zig targets, so no test loses its native run.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_18 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 18u32, "2026-10-31T16:50Z", "main", "10-31 12:50 main: NEXT SLICE queued on the node: job-1793465288949-main-add-rust (after strict-blast). The 78 tests whose F# target passes but that have no passing Rust/Zig target (parsing 25, date_time 16, sm' 16, math 4, file_system 3, am'/benchmark/common/listm' 2 each, base/optionm'/reflection/resultm/seq/threading 1) get a '///> rust' line on a copy of the node mirror's lib (drop_test_target.ps1 gained -Add; checked locally: math 4/4 + resultm 1/1 inserted, each diff a single '+///> rust' line) and their 15 files are gated. Rows in C:\\home\\remote\\main-manifest\\add-rust-rows.tsv. Then the same 1.5x rule: Rust passes within 1.5x of F# -> add rust and retire F# for that test; Rust fails -> the test stays F# (true F# dependency or a Rust backend gap worth its own item).")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_19 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 19u32, "2026-10-31T17:50Z", "main", "10-31 13:50 main: RETIRE LANDED green, 3 batches (13:15-13:4x; undos lib-land-undo\\20261031-131551, ...-132410, and batch 3's), 19 files, every live file = its draft. lib/spiral '///> fsharp' test targets 223 -> 104 (119 retired: each of those tests keeps a Rust/Zig target that passed within 1.5x of F#), saving ~1,877 s of F# per full lib gate. Left: the 104 (14 slower natively, 78 without a Rust/Zig target: the add-rust node measurement job-1793465288949 is queued, 27 F#-only among them, plus the rest), and dice's lib/dice.fsx F# product row (a product call, n12).")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_20 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 20u32, "2026-10-31T18:18Z", "main", "10-31 14:18 main: ADD-RUST RESULT (node job-1793465288949, 15 files): of the 78 F#-passing tests without a native target, a new '///> rust' target PASSED on 39 (24 within 1.5x of F#) and FAILED on 37 (date_time 16, parsing 8, file_system 3, common 2, sm' 2, listm'/optionm'/reflection/resultm/seq/threading 1 each: true F# dependencies or Rust backend gaps, worth a per-file look), 2 had no Rust row (benchmark). SWAP for the 24 within 1.5x: add rust + drop fsharp. 17 outside tibia's x-compat files are LANDING now (parsing 11, math 4, am' 1, base 1; each diff exactly its +rust/-fsharp lines; canonical 4/4). The other 7 (sm' 6, listm' 1) wait: main promised tibia no lands on env/listm'/otp_actor/process/file_system/sm' until 16:30 or its go. Per-test tables: %TEMP%\\fsharp-measure\\add-rust-per-test.csv.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_21 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 21u32, "2026-10-31T18:24Z", "main", "10-31 14:24 main: SWAP LANDED green 14:22 (17 tests: parsing 11, math 4, am' 1, base 1; math 02A23CCEECCB, parsing 70971EC1A1DF; undo lib-land-undo\\20261031-141837). lib '///> fsharp' test targets: 223 (morning) -> 104 -> 87. RUST FAILURE CAUSES, read from the node's raw gate output: date_time 16 = missing crate, rustc E0433 'cannot find crate chrono' (the experiment added plain '///> rust'; date_time's other tests already use 'rust -d chrono'): RERUN queued as node job-1793471022910-main-add-chrono with target 'rust -d chrono'; parsing 8 = genuine F# dependency (the generated code carries an F# '#r nuget: FParsec' line and F# interpolated strings: FParsec-based tests); file_system 3 = genuine .NET dependency (System_IO_DirectoryInfo); common/sm'/listm'/optionm'/reflection/resultm/seq/threading 1-2 each still to read.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_22 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 22u32, "2026-10-31T18:26Z", "main", "10-31 14:26 main: remaining Rust failures classified from the node raw output. MISSING RUST ARM ('backend_switch: this lib/spiral function has no Rust arm yet'): sm' format_debug + format_debug_2, optionm' of_obj, resultm from_try_keeps_values_and_catches_failures, seq new_seq. F#-ONLY MACRO emitted as Rust (rustc syntax error at a '.'): common new_disposable_2/_3, threading new_disposable_token (IDisposable). RUST PANICS AT RUN TIME (Rust and F# disagree on the same test: possible backend bugs): listm' item_2 (main.rs:246), reflection get_functions_types (main.rs:198). So of the 37: 16 date_time = missing chrono dep (rerun queued), 8 parsing = FParsec, 3 file_system = .NET IO, 3 = IDisposable macros, 5 = missing Rust arms, 2 = runtime panics.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_23 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 23u32, "2026-10-31T18:27Z", "main", "10-31 14:27 main: the 2 Rust RUN-TIME PANICS are NOT backend bugs: both tests hard-code F#-specific expected strings. listm' item_2 expects 'System.Exception: Option does not have a value.' (.NET exception formatting; Rust correctly gives 'Option does not have a value.'), and reflection get_functions_types expects F# type names 'int32' where Rust correctly reports 'i32'. The behaviour agrees across backends; only the assertions are F#-shaped. To port: make the expected value backend-aware (a backend_switch in the test, or compare the message without the .NET type prefix and type names through a per-backend table). Low priority: 2 tests.")
inl note_of_fsharp_outside_compiler_24 () = Note ("FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER", 24u32, "2026-10-31T18:51Z", "main", "10-31 14:51 main: date_time rerun with 'rust -d chrono' (node job-1793471022910): 12 of 16 pass (8 within 1.5x), 4 fail = the GUID family (date_time_from_guid_2/_3, timestamp_from_guid_2, timestamp_guid_from_timestamp_2: .NET Guid). The 8 swaps (add 'rust -d chrono', drop fsharp; diff exactly 8+/8-, canonical) land after EDITION3's Cells window. After it: lib F# targets 87 -> 79.")
