inl note_of_x_tutorial_defuse_step_2 () = Note ("X-TUTORIAL-DEFUSE-STEP", 2u32, "2026-10-27T12:25Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:25 x: drafted in the tree (unstaged). apps/client/e2e/links.txt holds one line ('go x.com and ask @bob; listm.foldBack and x.io stay plain'), and the README tutorial gains a step after the checks.spi dry run, 'xc post-thread --dry-run e2e/links.txt', with a placeholder expected block. The next X-WIP-VERIFY's e2e -Accept writes the real output. Review by hand: a ZWSP before .com and after @, none in listm.foldBack or x.io, weight and price lines as for one post.")
inl note_of_x_tutorial_defuse_step_3 () = Note ("X-TUTORIAL-DEFUSE-STEP", 3u32, "2026-10-28T10:33Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:33 x: HAND REVIEW PASSED (its stated condition) on verify25's e2e accept (job-1793174907446, node mirror xv20 README, ZWSP rendered as <ZWSP>): the step prints '--- post 1 of 1, weight 66', 'c1 1/1', the line 'go x<ZWSP>.com and ask @<ZWSP>bob; listm.foldBack and x.io stay plain', and '--- 1 posts, $0.015 to create'. Every criterion holds: a ZWSP before .com in x.com and after @ in @bob; none in listm.foldBack; x.io plain (a two-letter TLD after a one-letter label, per the links-only rule); weight and price lines as for one post. Closes when this accepted README lands from verify25 through node-proof -Land (the X-WIP-VERIFY rule).")
inl note_of_x_tutorial_defuse_step_4 () = Note ("X-TUTORIAL-DEFUSE-STEP", 4u32, "2026-10-28T11:06Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it). The hand-reviewed step (note 3) is in the landed README.")
inl note_of_x_site_e2e_review_1 () = Note ("X-SITE-E2E-REVIEW", 1u32, "2026-10-27T12:05Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:05 x: stage D added a Bluesky stub path; nobody has read it against the requests crate yet.")
inl note_of_x_site_e2e_review_2 () = Note ("X-SITE-E2E-REVIEW", 2u32, "2026-10-27T12:20Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:20 x: reviewed e2e.ps1 and social_stub.py. Covered: Mastodon post-thread (statuses accepted, no ZWSP in any status, no more posts than X's split, no X-stub requests); Bluesky post-thread (exactly one createSession, every createRecord accepted, no ZWSP, at most X's post count, every record replying to its parent and the thread root, no X-stub requests). Gap closed in the tree (unstaged): a refused Bluesky sign-in must exit 4, create no record, name the stub's 'Invalid identifier or password' and never echo the password, and its output joins the secret-leak check. Still uncovered: xc delete and delete-thread under XC_SITE (the stub has DELETE /api/v1/statuses/:id and deleteRecord), and --dry-run per site. Both need a read of how xc routes delete per site before drafting.")
inl note_of_x_site_e2e_review_3 () = Note ("X-SITE-E2E-REVIEW", 3u32, "2026-10-27T14:06Z", "x", "2026-10-27 10:06 x: verify17 (job-1793108152156) caught a bug in MY new e2e check, not in xc: 'a refused Bluesky sign-in does not say why' read $denied.Raw, which e2e's Invoke-Step fills from stdout only, while xc prints failures to stderr, which goes into .Errors and .Text. Fixed in the tree: the refused sign-in and the delete-thread refusal checks read .Text. The single post/delete check keeps .Raw (its 'posted <uri>' line is stdout). verify17's other client finding is the expected placeholder in the new links.txt tutorial step; accept rewrites it, and I review it from verify17's saved README.")
inl note_of_x_site_e2e_review_4 () = Note ("X-SITE-E2E-REVIEW", 4u32, "2026-10-28T10:52Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_x_posted_margin_1 () = Note ("X-POSTED-MARGIN", 1u32, "2026-10-27T13:44Z", "x", "2026-10-27 09:44 x: ranked today's regen (posted.rs 899 lines, 50 fns, pinned compiler): flat, the largest is letters_after 40, then suffix_ok 36, label_before 35, tag_has_letter 30, posted_named 29; it sits at Spiral's per-arm floor, so no := inlining lever is left. The natural seam is semantic: entities (tags, mention/hashtag/cashtag_after, entities_from, suffix_ok, tag_has_letter: about 250 lines) vs dots (run_of, pieces_from, defused_run, tld_ok, label_before, letters_after). The draft is CPU-light: a sigils crate exporting xc_defused_entities, called by posted after the dots; measure only if posted must grow.")
inl note_of_x_posted_margin_2 () = Note ("X-POSTED-MARGIN", 2u32, "2026-10-27T15:09Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:09 x: groomed to Paused, so the light view lists only pullable work. Resume condition (from this item's own analysis): a touched crate's regen nears 900 generated lines, or a change has to grow it. Checked against every regen until then.")
inl note_of_x_posted_margin_3 () = Note ("X-POSTED-MARGIN", 3u32, "2026-10-28T06:01Z", "x", "2026-10-28 02:01 x: RESUMED by its own condition. verify23 (job-1793152313075) gate: 'posted 1034 lines OVER 1000' (was 899). Attribution of the regenerated posted.rs: tonight's RT:@ rule (rt_before 50, rt_letters 34 with one_of inlined twice) plus the bigger mention_after/mention_quiet account for most of +135, on a crate that was already at Spiral's per-arm floor. Fix: this item's planned seam. A new crate xc_entities (apps/client/entities: entities.spi exports xc_defused_entities, string -> string, over defuse's defused_entities) does the entity-sigil pass, and posted calls it through one host function, as it already does for the TLD check (xc_domains). splitter's posted_text/posted_by take the sigil pass as a 'tagged : string -> string' argument beside 'known'; posted_on (local, sim and notebooks) passes defused_entities, posted_named passes entities_remotely. Registered in posted/Cargo.toml, package.spiproj, build.ps1's notebook list and the client README crate table; Cargo.lock via cargo metadata --offline (xc_entities entry plus posted's dependency); the workspace glob apps/client/* covers the crate. Real x-compat 0 in 43 files, no new line over 120. Measuring: node regen job-1793167383150 (posted and entities line counts), then verify24.")
inl note_of_x_posted_margin_4 () = Note ("X-POSTED-MARGIN", 4u32, "2026-10-28T06:10Z", "x", "2026-10-28 02:10 x: measured (node regen job-1793167383150, 386 s, pinned compiler): posted 555 (from 1034), entities 638, regen failed 0, every other crate unchanged (xc 991, digest 954, refs 896, frames 449). Both crates now have room for future sigil rules. Closes on verify24 (job-1793167893768, manifest x-verify24 with 212 files), which carries the split.")
inl note_of_x_posted_margin_5 () = Note ("X-POSTED-MARGIN", 5u32, "2026-10-28T11:06Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_cells_lib_snapshot_1 () = Note ("CELLS-LIB-SNAPSHOT", 1u32, "2026-10-27T14:00Z", "main", "evidence (10-27): main's Cells-vs-matrix benchmark, analysed by x in SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS n31. All 23 rows that flipped (round 3: tuple x3; round 5: array x7, closure x10, ebpf x3) were real type errors from lib files OTHER lanes edited mid-round: xsd_emit.spi written 08:36, then schematron's import xml.spi written 09:15. Cells mounts the live lib (the first ancestor holding lib/spiral wins; SPIRAL_LIB is only a fallback, notebook.ex find_spiral_lib), so one run can see two different libs. Not timeouts, locks or OOM, so a longer timeout or a retry doesn't fix it. Second finding: the families files import nothing from lib (their package needs only samples/core), yet they failed on lib-only files. So Cells type-checks the whole ancestor lib/spiral (1,146 files) for every cell even when the file uses only lib's testing module. That is both the fragility and part of the compile cost (79% of Cells' time in the benchmark).\nWANTED: (1) per run, snapshot (or hash-pin) the mounted lib at start, compile every cell against it, and report any lib file that changes during the run in the run's summary; (2) mount only what a file reaches (its package's imports plus testing for test blocks), not the whole ancestor lib, measured by the benchmark harness (bench-cells-vs-matrix.ps1) as its own experiment. Workaround in the benchmark today: each round copies lib/spiral, samples/core and the families into a round tree, so the snapshot is the ancestor Cells finds.")
inl note_of_cells_lib_snapshot_2 () = Note ("CELLS-LIB-SNAPSHOT", 2u32, "2026-10-27T14:12Z", "main", "10-27 10:12 main: second victim of the same mid-run lib read, so this item now has two incidents. kit-release-SLICES' first candidate notebook (kit_process_table/ps_cli.spi, started about 09:12) failed with a type error in the LIB, 'schematron.spi:80:25 read_schematron: Got list xml_node, Expected string', because lib/spiral/xml.spi was written at 09:15:40 (+100 lines) mid-read. The release refuses to publish over an unlisted failure, so it reruns (about 2 h of regen, build and notebooks). The write did not come from a land job (the only lib land near then was tibia's gated convert.spi at 09:05); it was a direct live edit that bypassed land.ps1's reader guard, likely fiscal's xs:any work (asked). Two lessons: (1) the reader guard only protects readers when every writer goes through it, so direct lib edits must stop (all lanes reminded, with a freeze during kit's announced rerun window); (2) the readers shouldn't depend on that discipline at all: a per-run lib snapshot (this item) makes Cells runs and release notebooks immune to any write, gated or not.")
inl note_of_cells_lib_snapshot_3 () = Note ("CELLS-LIB-SNAPSHOT", 3u32, "2026-10-27T14:19Z", "main", "10-27 10:19 main: design, from reading the code (no change made yet; Cells reads its own source live, so the change goes in as an anchored apply in a gap). SINGLE resolution point: notebook.ex:577 in execute/4, 'lib = opts[:spiral_lib] || (opts[:path] && find_spiral_lib(opts[:path])) || \"-\"'. It runs once per notebook run (one file), so every cell of one file already shares one lib PATH but reads its CONTENT live, and a lib_gate or test-blocks run over many files resolves it again per file. Plan in three steps, each measured with bench-cells-vs-matrix.ps1 as an experiment: (1) a gate-level snapshot: lib_gate.ps1 and test-blocks.ps1 copy the resolved lib/spiral once per run (excluding target, _build, .cache, node_modules; about 11 MB), set SPIRAL_CELLS_LIB=<copy> plus SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR (needed so the copy's bare |core- resolves; found while building the E1 harness), and notebook.ex:577 prefers an explicit SPIRAL_CELLS_LIB over the ancestor walk (today SPIRAL_LIB is only the fallback). Every file of the run then sees one lib. (2) Report mid-run drift: hash the live lib at the start and the end of the run and print the files that changed, so a run that raced a writer says so instead of flipping rows silently. (3) Mount only what a file reaches: the families import nothing but testing, yet type-check the whole lib (the second finding in n1). That's the compile-cost half, and it needs the compiler's package loading to accept a module subset; it's adjacent to the compiler team's E2 shared-prefix design, so coordinate there. RISK to measure in (1): compile caches keyed by source path (compile memo, daemon caches) may miss when the lib path changes per run; the bench measures that directly.")
inl note_of_cells_lib_snapshot_4 () = Note ("CELLS-LIB-SNAPSHOT", 4u32, "2026-10-27T15:39Z", "main", "10-27 11:39 main: Step 1 PROVEN on the node, land waiting for a Cells-quiet window. Draft: TEMP cells-lib-snapshot/apply.ps1 (anchored: the notebook.ex:577 resolution line exactly once, find_spiral_lib/1's header exactly once; refuses if SPIRAL_CELLS_LIB or resolve_lib already exist, or if the test file exists). Change: execute/4's lib line becomes 'lib = resolve_lib(opts)', and a new public resolve_lib/1 (beside find_spiral_lib) checks, in order: opts[:spiral_lib], then spiral_lib_from_env(SPIRAL_CELLS_LIB) (the existing helper, so a folder without package.spiproj is ignored), then the ancestor walk, then \"-\". Behaviour is unchanged unless SPIRAL_CELLS_LIB is set. New test module test/spiral/cells/notebook_lib_snapshot_test.exs (async: false; restores the env var on exit) with 2 behavioural tests: the snapshot wins over the ancestor lib, and an explicit spiral_lib still wins while a package-less snapshot is ignored. Proof (job-1793115484386, mainpost mirror spiral 3f1b7588, mix test --trace): the 3 existing NotebookSpiralLibTest tests and the 2 new ones passed, 'Result: 5 passed'. The first run (job-1793115200688) printed VOID only because my parser expected '5 tests, 0 failures'; this Cells ExUnit prints 'Result: N passed', and the verbose rerun showed all 5 by name. Land condition: Cells reads its own source live and recompiles on change, so apply.ps1 -Apply on apps/cells runs only when no Cells job is live (kit SLICES2 notebooks, the E1 bench and the radix gate are all queued or running now). Step 2 (gates pass a per-run snapshot via SPIRAL_CELLS_LIB) follows the land.")
inl note_of_cells_lib_snapshot_5 () = Note ("CELLS-LIB-SNAPSHOT", 5u32, "2026-10-28T11:05Z", "main", "10-28 07:05 main: LANDED in the live apps/cells (10-28 07:0x, unstaged), in a box gap with no Cells runs (0 erl before and after). TEMP cells-lib-snapshot/apply.ps1: execute/4 now resolves the lib through resolve_lib/1, where opts[:spiral_lib], then an explicit SPIRAL_CELLS_LIB that holds a package, then the ancestor walk from --path, then '-'. That lets a caller pin the lib a Cells run reads (a snapshot or a mirror) instead of whatever lib/spiral the first ancestor holds. The new test module notebook_lib_snapshot_test.exs (async: false, 2 behavioural tests) plus the existing notebook_report_test.exs: mix test, 35 passed, 0 failed, 52 s (MIX_BUILD_PATH per run, process-mode compiler, no daemon left). This was proven 5/5 on the node on 10-27; today it passes in the live tree. Use: SPIRAL_CELLS_LIB=<snapshot>\\spiral pins a run's lib, which makes gates and accepts independent of live lib lands.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_1 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 1u32, "2026-10-27T15:10Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:09 x: found 2026-10-27 at a cost of three node checks. I added `:== chunker = string -> i32 -> string -> list string` at splitter.spi:163; splitter already had `:== chunker = i32 -> string -> list string` at line 211, which silently won, and every other module saw a type error far from the cause. Census of every top-level name in lib/spiral and x/apps (179 .spi files), per namespace (types: :== :! :|; terms: := :~): 1 type and 7 term names are defined twice in one module, and every one is deliberate ML shadowing whose first definition is used before the second (leptos for/show/option', iter enumerate, sm' to_char_array, file_system file_type, digest primary_entry; runtime arg_action is used as a type on 631). So the precise check, a top-level definition shadowed in the same module before any use, has zero hits on today's corpus and catches exactly this mistake. Plan: a text-level kit verb over .spi files (top-level definitions per namespace, then a use between the two lines), with a positive control on a two-line sample, wired into x's x-compat pass (and any other project's). Later, the same as a compiler warning, if the compiler team wants it.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_2 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 2u32, "2026-10-27T15:18Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:18 x: rule decided before drafting (the advisor's point: \"shadowed before any use\" misses the variant where the new alias is used once in between; importers still see only the later one). Types (:== :! :|): ANY second declaration of a name in one module is a finding. Terms (:= :~): a definition shadowed before any use in its module is a finding (deliberate overload-style shadowing, as in leptos for/show/option' and iter enumerate, uses the first one in between). Expected hits on today's corpus (pre-registered): 1 type finding, lib file_system.spi file_type (:! at 312 wrapping std::fs::FileType, shadowed by :| File | Directory at 979, so importers' file_system.file_type is the union and the Rust wrapper is unreachable outside lines 313-978; a real hazard, fix = rename the union, e.g. entry_kind, in its own lib land); 0 term findings. Positive control: the literal pair from splitter.spi (:== chunker at 163 and at 211), which must be flagged. Home: a kit verb (memories: a method is a kit command; kit is generic), wired into x's build beside --x-compat.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_3 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 3u32, "2026-10-27T16:06Z", "x", "2026-10-27 12:06 x: re-sized after reading kit's wiring (read-only, during kit's SLICES2 window): a verb means the command model in src/command_spec_domain/model.spi (command_spec.rs follows it), a dispatch arm in src/kit_legacy_operations/operations.rs next to spi-map's (kit_source_map_run_family), the check itself in Spiral, and a kit release to install it. So 180m and medium, sequenced after kit's current release; the kit team may prefer to fold it into a release slice.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_4 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 4u32, "2026-10-28T11:15Z", "x", "2026-10-28 07:15 x: HANDED OVER to kit (as kit asked at 06:5x: a sibling verb 'kit lint shadows <root> [<path>...] [--baseline <rows>]', drafted outside kit/src during TRAIN3; kit verifies on a copy and lands it in TRAIN4, about 16:00). Stage: scratchpad kit-lint-shadows\\src\\kit_lint_shadows\\ (new package: shadows.spi, the pure core with test blocks; shadows_run.spi, the CLI, with '///> _' above main exporting kit_lint_shadows_run; package.spiproj referencing spiral-, kit_receipt and kit_lint_macros; standards.spi declaring HostFree and LibReferenced; Cargo.toml with entry shadows_run.spi). The run module reuses kit_lint_macros.lint's spi_files, relative_to, argument, paths_from and value_after by qualified name instead of duplicating them. Wiring: scratchpad kit-lint-shadows\\wiring.do, one kit do program (root: the kit repo), 4 files and 5 replacements: the workspace member in src/Cargo.toml, the kit_cli dependency, kit_cli_model/model.spi (lint_shadows_run plus lint_word, and VerbLint branching on argument 1 = 'shadows'), and a second usage line in command_spec_domain/direct_verbs.spi. Proven: the core's 5 tests pass on the node (job-1793185294448, rust): a second type alias flagged (the splitter.spi positive control, ':== chunker' at 1 and 4); a term used before its redefinition is not flagged; a term shadowed before any use is flagged; a use inside the redefinition's own signature counts (the runtime.spi arg_action case); qualified (m.f) and partial (ff) names are not uses. wiring.do applied cleanly to scratch copies of the 4 files (exit 0, 5 replacements; the only line over 120 is src/Cargo.toml's existing one-line members list). Expected on today's corpus: 0 rows over lib/spiral and x/apps (the census's only real hit, file_system file_type, was fixed by LIB-FILE-TYPE-SHADOW; every other duplicate is used before its redefinition). Not yet run: the full kit build and the run module against real directories, which kit's copy verification covers; Cargo.lock regenerates in kit's build.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_5 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 5u32, "2026-10-28T12:13Z", "x", "2026-10-28 08:13 x: REVISED per kit's TRAIN4 regen (job-1793187641077: one package generated 1285 lines, and kit_cli went 971 -> 1013 from the dispatch inlining). Now two crates: kit_lint_shadows_core (pure scan plus tests, exporting parallel arrays like kit_lint_scan; 716 generated lines measured on the node, job-1793188798481, tests 5/5) and kit_lint_shadows (CLI, one host call to the core). Dispatch moved into kit_lint_macros' run entry (routed: argument 1 'shadows' -> kit_lint_shadows::kit_lint_shadows_run), so kit_cli and kit_cli_model are untouched; wiring.do is 4 files and 4 replacements, proven on copies of kit's current files. Handed back to kit before the TRAIN4 freeze (~15:50).")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_6 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 6u32, "2026-10-28T13:09Z", "x", "2026-10-28 09:09 x: kit's node regen-compare of the revised split is CLEAN (kit 08:58): 8 crates, 0 compile failures, 0 over 1000 lines. It rides TRAIN4 (TRAIN3 runs now; lib land window ~13:10). Closes when TRAIN4 publishes and 'kit lint shadows' runs on the installed kit: expected 0 rows on lib/spiral and x/apps.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_7 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 7u32, "2026-10-29T22:45Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:45 kit: closing condition, x/apps half MET on installed kit C528BE4D: 'kit lint shadows C:\\home\\git\\x\\apps' -> ok, files 72, shadows 0, 327 ms. Positive control on the same binary: a temp module defining chunker twice before its use -> 'm.spi:3 chunker shadowed-unused first at line 1', failed, exit 1. The lib/spiral half runs after main's lib window 3 closes (x's fs-heal-4b lands in it); then this item closes.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_8 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 8u32, "2026-10-29T22:47Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:47 kit: lib/spiral half run after main's lib window 3 (lib file_system 8661852BC5D5, sm' 2196F9C70148), installed kit C528BE4D: 'kit lint shadows C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral' -> failed, files 114, shadows 1: 'physics.spi:748 force type-twice first at line 30'. Real double definition: ':== force = f64' at :30, USED at :32 (velocity_cf ... list force), then redefined as ':| force =' (ExternalForce | InternalForce) at :748, chapter style. The item's title rule is 'shadowed before any use'; the term rule (shadowed-unused) honors use, but the type rule (type-twice) flags any second type definition. So x's '0 rows on lib' condition is NOT met. The decision is x's: (a) type-twice should also skip a first definition that was used (then physics.spi passes), or (b) keep it strict and rename one force in lib (e.g. scalar_force at :30). The item stays Active at 85% until x picks.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_9 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 9u32, "2026-10-29T22:48Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:48 kit: correction to n8. type-twice is deliberately strict even after a use: the item's own first test (a_second_type_alias_is_a_finding: chunker used at :3, redefined at :4 -> 'chunker 1 4 type-twice') encodes the original incident, where the second :== silently won for later uses. So physics.spi's force (:30 f64 alias used at :32, :748 union) is a TRUE finding, and option (a) is withdrawn. Remaining step: a lib land renaming one force (e.g. the :30 alias to scalar_force plus its uses before :748); then 'kit lint shadows lib/spiral' must print 0 rows and this item closes. x was told.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_10 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 10u32, "2026-10-29T22:49Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:49 kit: lib fix drafted: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\lib-land\\physics-scalar-force\\physics-scalar-force.do renames the physics.spi f64 alias force -> scalar_force at its 5 type uses (:30 :32 :126 :135 :142; the string literals :153 :689 stay). Base sha256 bb24137c0fe1; dry run on live lib ok; scratch-copy proof: kit lint shadows 1 row -> 0. Handed to main for the next lib window (the compile/document_test gate is the lander's). Closes when 'kit lint shadows lib/spiral' prints 0 rows on the landed lib.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_11 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 11u32, "2026-10-29T22:51Z", "main", "10-29 18:5x main: kit's lib fix (C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\lib-land\\physics-scalar-force\\physics-scalar-force.do: rename the f64 alias `force` to scalar_force at physics.spi:30/32/126/135/142, so the union `force` at :748 no longer shadows it) is PROVEN on a lib copy: 5 replacements, bb24137c0fe1 -> b1a4e84872ad, type_probe physics PASS (43 tests, 0 errors); a pure rename, so no generated line count moves. The live land was REFUSED by lib-guarded-do because git-a6's pack-7 review holds a heavy-build window (job-1793314021578, about 5 h, 13 lib-reading samples). That refusal is correct. QUEUED FIRST for next session's first lib window, after the review finishes; then kit re-runs `kit lint shadows lib/spiral` (expected 0 rows) and closes this item.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_12 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 12u32, "2026-10-29T22:52Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:52 kit: NEXT-WINDOW HANDOFF. main proved physics-scalar-force.do on a copy (5 replacements at :30/:32/:126/:135/:142, bb24137c0fe1 -> b1a4e84872ad; type_probe physics PASS, 43 tests typed, 0 errors; a pure rename, so generated line counts can't move). lib-guarded-do correctly REFUSED the live land at 18:5x: git-a6's pack-7 review holds a heavy-build window whose 13 samples read lib. Live physics.spi is unchanged (bb24137c0fe1). Whoever opens next session's first lib window: land C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\lib-land\\physics-scalar-force\\physics-scalar-force.do FIRST (main queued it), then run 'kit lint shadows C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral' (installed kit C528BE4D or later) and expect 0 rows; then this item closes (x's other half, x/apps, is already 0).")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_13 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 13u32, "2026-10-29T22:54Z", "x", "2026-10-29 18:54 x: picked (b) after kit's correction (type-twice is strict by design; the first test is this exact pattern). Lib fix drafted: x/lib-programs/physics-scalar-force.kit renames physics.spi :30 f64 alias force to scalar_force plus uses :32 :126 :135 :142, BB24137C0FE1E374 to B1A4E84872ADD194. Proof: kit lint shadows {base,cand} gives 1 row on base only; type_probe cand PASS 43 typed, base's shadowed position_ft_2 note gone; node Cells gate queued. Closes after the land plus kit's 0-row closing lint.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_14 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 14u32, "2026-10-29T22:54Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:54 kit: x decided (b): the lint stays strict and the fix is the lib rename. x drafted the same rename independently (x/lib-programs/physics-scalar-force.kit), with the identical result hash bb24137c0fe1 -> b1a4e84872ad, its own lint and type_probe proofs, and a node Cells gate queued. Land exactly ONE of the two programs in next session's first window (the second would refuse on missing anchors); main and x settle which file. The closing step is unchanged (n12).")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_15 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 15u32, "2026-10-29T23:34Z", "x", "2026-10-29 19:34 x: physics rename GATE GREEN in the same job pair. node Cells A/B on pack-6b, same 900 s header on both arms: base job-1793314685520 (file_system 34/34, physics 43/43, 0 type errors) vs candidate job-1793314938313 (file_system 34/34, physics 43/43, 0 type errors); identical (empty) failure sets. Gated bytes bound to the arm files: base BA57252523F8C437 / C71EC3BDB0114892, candidate 650F33A377EA66D8 / 254B1994434F27B4 (header + target), matched locally. physics-scalar-force.kit (BB24137C0FE1E374 -> B1A4E84872ADD194) lands in the first lib window after the pack-7 review, after fiscal's programs; then kit runs the 0-row closing lint.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_16 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 16u32, "2026-10-29T23:50Z", "x", "2026-10-29 19:50 x: physics rename LANDED 19:50:13 in window 4 (2nd, by main's order): physics.spi BB24137C0FE1E374 -> B1A4E84872ADD194, the node-gated target (43/43 on both arms). One guarded try, no live lib reader. Undo: C:\\home\\data\\undo\\physics-scalar-force. Kit has been pinged for the closing 'kit lint shadows lib/spiral' (expect 0 rows), which closes this item.")
inl note_of_spi_shadow_before_use_17 () = Note ("SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE", 17u32, "2026-10-29T23:52Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T23:52 kit: DONE. x's closing condition holds on installed kit C528BE4D after main's window 4 (closed 19:5x, document_test 22 passed): 'kit lint shadows C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral' -> ok, files 114, shadows 0 (physics.spi now B1A4E84872AD: x's scalar_force rename landed 19:50:13); 'kit lint shadows C:\\home\\git\\x\\apps' -> ok, files 72, shadows 0. Positive control (n7): a temp module defining chunker twice before use -> 'shadowed-unused', exit 1. The lint shipped in TRAIN4 (installed 18:35:54). The type-twice rule stays strict by design (its first test encodes the original chunker incident).")
inl note_of_org_node_proof_land_1 () = Note ("ORG-NODE-PROOF-LAND", 1u32, "2026-10-27T15:16Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:16 x: filed 2026-10-27 from today's landings. node-proof.ps1 syncs the tree to a mirror, and verify -Out brings back every .rs/.json/.txt/README under apps; landing then needs a base: which files did the run change, and which did I edit locally after the sync? Today that was done by hand (verify17/18, README and build.ps1 edited after sync), and verify -Out copies every README.md, so a blind copy would revert later edits. Plan: node-proof writes a hash manifest of the synced files at sync time (local, beside the job id); a land step copies an output only where the tree still matches the manifest and the output differs, and lists every file changed on both sides as a conflict for hand review. Positive control: edit one README after a sync and see it listed, not overwritten.")
inl note_of_org_node_proof_land_2 () = Note ("ORG-NODE-PROOF-LAND", 2u32, "2026-10-27T15:58Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:58 x: done in i574n.github/scripts/node-proof.ps1 (unstaged). (1) node-proof ... -LandRepos x writes a sync manifest right after the OK-gated syncs: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\node-proof\\<label>.json, sha256 of every file git ls-files -co --exclude-standard lists (x: 210 files, about 3 s; spiral's 10,887 files take 67 s, so only repos named in -LandRepos are hashed). -WriteManifest -Label l -LandRepos r writes one by hand when the tree is known to equal the synced base (done for x-verify19: no x file changed after 11:46, before its sync). (2) node-proof -Land <label> -From <remote output folder> -Repo x [-Apply] fetches the folder to %TEMP%\\node-proof-land\\<label>, then per file: same as the tree = unchanged; tree still equal to the manifest's base = land (copied only with -Apply); otherwise CONFLICT with the fetched path for hand review. Report-only by default, which keeps remote.ps1's rule (fetch to scratch, never straight into a tree) and makes the landing three-way. Positive control: verify17's output against a copy of the x-verify19 manifest with one base hash altered (as if edited locally since the sync) gave 'CONFLICT apps/client/e2e/sim-small.world.json', 13 to land, 33 unchanged, and the tree untouched. First real use: verify19's landing.")
inl note_of_org_node_proof_land_3 () = Note ("ORG-NODE-PROOF-LAND", 3u32, "2026-10-28T10:56Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it). First real use: the report found 0 conflicts and named net.rs as stale-by-source, which was held back.")
inl note_of_x_e2e_says_helper_1 () = Note ("X-E2E-SAYS-HELPER", 1u32, "2026-10-27T15:17Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:16 x: filed 2026-10-27. verify17 went red partly because an e2e check read .Raw (stdout only) for a message xc prints to stderr. Each check chooses .Raw or .Text by hand. Plan: one e2e.ps1 helper, Assert-Says $step $pattern $label, that searches stdout and stderr together and names which stream matched, used by every 'says why' check (refused sign-in, X-only refusals, the missing session); .Raw stays only for byte-exact tutorial output.")
inl note_of_x_e2e_says_helper_2 () = Note ("X-E2E-SAYS-HELPER", 2u32, "2026-10-27T15:39Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:39 x: done differently than filed. Invoke-Step's .Text already joins stderr and stdout, and both says-why checks use it, so an Assert-Says wrapper would add a name and no protection. The trap was the property name: .Raw did not say it was stdout only. Renamed in e2e.ps1 (the only reader): Raw -> Stdout and Errors -> Stderr (11 lines, 0 left), so a check like $denied.Stdout.Contains('why') now reads as wrong at a glance; byte-exact program and posted-id parsing keep Stdout on purpose. Behavior-neutral; closes on verify19's e2e.")
inl note_of_x_e2e_says_helper_3 () = Note ("X-E2E-SAYS-HELPER", 3u32, "2026-10-28T10:55Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:50 x: CLOSED by X-WIP-VERIFY's pre-registered rule. verify25 (node job-1793174907446, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357) GREEN on every clause of X-WIP-VERIFY note 6: exit 0; sim accept and plain sim build exit 0 (ExUnit 45/45, corpus commute ok, thread_run pass, tamper rejected with same_cells=false); client gate 29 crates 0 over 1000; x-compat 0 in 43 client and 24 sim files; all 42 client and 23 sim notebooks passed, 0 failing totals; plain-pass e2e 37 of 37 tutorial steps same, 0 FAILED, 153 stub requests. Outputs landed three-way (node-proof -Land x-verify25: 21 files plus 19 Gleam modules, 0 conflicts; net.rs held back because its source changed after the sync, so verify26 lands it).")
inl note_of_lib_file_type_shadow_1 () = Note ("LIB-FILE-TYPE-SHADOW", 1u32, "2026-10-27T15:22Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:22 x: found by the SPI-SHADOW-BEFORE-USE census. lib file_system.spi declares :! file_type (std::fs::FileType) at 312 and :| file_type = File | Directory at 979, so every importer's file_system.file_type is the union and the Rust wrapper cannot be named outside lines 313-978. Its functions still work (dir_entry_file_type and file_type_is_dir are typed at definition; alphabet documents.spi:307-312 uses them that way). Fix: rename the union and its one consumer find_parent to entry_kind (File | Directory), a file_system-only edit; grep across repos found no other user of the union. Land through land.ps1 with the lib gate, outside kit's notebook window.")
inl note_of_lib_file_type_shadow_2 () = Note ("LIB-FILE-TYPE-SHADOW", 2u32, "2026-10-27T19:47Z", "x", "2026-10-27 15:47 x: drafted and dry-run, waiting for kit's land slot before TRAIN (asked 15:4x). The rename is only two lines: find_parent takes its kind untyped, and File/Directory keep their constructor names. Program (scratchpad file-type-shadow.kit): edit lib/spiral/file_system.spi expect files 1 replacements 2: ':| file_type =' -> ':| entry_kind =' and its own heading (the line-978 '/// ### file_type' anchored to the renamed declaration; the line-311 heading of the std::fs::FileType nominal stays). Dry run: lines 978-979, 2 replacements, nothing written. The undo program is the same records reversed. No .spir sibling (only file_system_operators.spi), and entry_kind is unused in the module. Gate after apply: the lib Cells gate on file_system.spi, as a separate job.")
inl note_of_lib_file_type_shadow_3 () = Note ("LIB-FILE-TYPE-SHADOW", 3u32, "2026-10-27T20:20Z", "x", "2026-10-27 16:20 x: HELD until kit's 'TRAIN window closed' (kit's cut-off message at 16:1x; TRAIN runs 5-7 h once it gets a heavy slot, likely tonight). Nothing landed: all 21 guarded attempts 15:52-16:15 were refused by live lib readers (exit 3), the waiter is stopped, and lib file_system.spi:979 still reads ':| file_type ='. Ready to land on that message with lib-guarded-do.ps1 -Program <scratchpad>\\file-type-shadow.lib.kit (root lib\\spiral; dry-run clean; undo program beside it). Resume condition: kit's 'TRAIN window closed'.")
inl note_of_lib_file_type_shadow_4 () = Note ("LIB-FILE-TYPE-SHADOW", 4u32, "2026-10-27T23:54Z", "x", "2026-10-27 19:54 x: LANDED at 19:51 after kit's 'TRAIN window closed', through the fixed lib-guarded-do.ps1 (its first live use: one application, exit 0): file_system.spi 2 replacements at lines 978-979, sha256 e6b5c9ce -> 287cc0fe. Gate as a separate job on a node mirror synced after the land (job-1793145315742, mirror xlibgate): file_system.spi's notebook tests. Undo program in the scratchpad (file-type-shadow.undo.kit) if the gate goes red.")
inl note_of_lib_file_type_shadow_5 () = Note ("LIB-FILE-TYPE-SHADOW", 5u32, "2026-10-28T01:05Z", "x", "2026-10-27 21:05 x: gate 1 (job-1793145315742, 883 s): 25/26; the one failure is file_exists_on_gleam_tells_files_from_missing_paths (file_exists 'gleam.toml' false), a cwd-relative test that does not touch the renamed union. The first base control (job-1793146450777: same harness, rename reverted on the mirror) is VOID: 20/26 failed in 2756 s, all cell timeouts with the node at 100% cpu. Rerun as an A/B in one job (job-1793149601802): landed vs reverted on the same mirror, both with a 900 s cell budget in a ///- header on the mirror's copy only. Rule, written before it reports: the land stands iff the landed arm fails no test that the reverted arm passes; otherwise undo with file-type-shadow.undo.kit through lib-guarded-do.")
inl note_of_lib_file_type_shadow_6 () = Note ("LIB-FILE-TYPE-SHADOW", 6u32, "2026-10-28T02:09Z", "x", "2026-10-27 22:09 x: A/B (job-1793149601802): the REVERTED arm gave 25/26, failing only the cwd-relative file_exists_on_gleam test, the same failure as gate 1 with the rename in. The LANDED arm is VOID: all 16 of its failures are Cells compiler-daemon errors on the shared default port 13905 (13 lock_timeout, 2 did not start, 1 'started with a different build', i.e. another job's daemon from a different compiler held the port), with 0 assertions, 0 type errors and 0 timeouts; it ran first, while fiscal's set-proof Cells jobs were starting. Rerun of the landed arm alone with SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT=13977 (private, like the sim build's 13950): job-1793153439819. The note 5 rule stands: the land stays iff that arm fails nothing the reverted arm passed.")
inl note_of_lib_file_type_shadow_7 () = Note ("LIB-FILE-TYPE-SHADOW", 7u32, "2026-10-28T02:25Z", "x", "2026-10-27 22:25 x: DONE by note 5's rule. Landed arm rerun on a private daemon port (job-1793153439819, SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT 13977): 26/27, and its one failure, file_exists_on_gleam_tells_files_from_missing_paths, also fails in the reverted arm (25/26, job-1793149601802) and in gate 1. The landed arm fails nothing the reverted arm passes, so the land stays. That test resolves the relative 'gleam.toml' against the runner's working directory, so it fails on a direct run_notebook.ps1 launch from the node job's home dir with or without this change; it is a property of the gate's launch directory, outside this item. lib file_system.spi now has ':| entry_kind = File | Directory' (lines 978-979), and the std::fs::FileType nominal file_type can be named by importers again.")
inl note_of_xsd_regex_reject_invalid_1 () = Note ("XSD-REGEX-REJECT-INVALID", 1u32, "2026-10-27T18:41Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 14:41 fiscal: found by all-proof (GST unit: example Stcd '?!00$0'). xsd_regex.parse_atom recurses on '(' with no error channel, so '(?!0+$)' reads as literals: an invalid XSD pattern becomes a different valid one and the validator checks the wrong language. Fix: compile_pattern/dfa_of_pattern return None for a quantifier with no atom ('(?', '|?', leading ?*+{) and the generator fails loudly on a None pattern, naming the type.")
inl note_of_xsd_regex_reject_invalid_2 () = Note ("XSD-REGEX-REJECT-INVALID", 2u32, "2026-10-27T18:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 14:58 fiscal: DRAFTED as notes/regex-reject-invalid.kit (program 6, after the 5 held): xsd_regex.is_xsd_pattern (a quantifier needs an atom and cannot stack; escapes incl. \\p{..}, classes incl. subtraction); compile_pattern returns None for invalid patterns; the emitter's runtime fallback failwiths naming the pattern. Census first (PS port, 14/14 controls): 0 of 331 corpus patterns rejected. Tests folded into patterns_match_like_xsd. Type-checked + fiscal generator.")
inl note_of_xsd_regex_reject_invalid_3 () = Note ("XSD-REGEX-REJECT-INVALID", 3u32, "2026-10-27T19:23Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 15:23 fiscal: latent gap: escape_ranges has no \\S \\D \\W (or \\i \\c \\I \\C); they fall through to the literal letter. Corpus census (case-sensitive, controls ok): 0 uses in 331 xs:pattern values and 31 matches() calls. Fix later: negated ranges, or reject until supported.")
inl note_of_xsd_regex_reject_invalid_4 () = Note ("XSD-REGEX-REJECT-INVALID", 4u32, "2026-10-27T19:55Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 15:55 fiscal: program 6 (fiscal/lib-programs/regex-reject-invalid.kit) now also FIXES \\S \\D \\W: atoms read as negated classes, inside a class via complement_ranges (sorted gaps over 0..10FFFF); \\i \\c \\I \\C are rejected until supported (0 corpus uses). Scanner renamed invalid_from. 4 new asserts. 7-program set re-proven on the live lib at 15:4x: applies in order, 11 modules type-check. Landing waits on a running lib gate.")
inl note_of_xsd_regex_reject_invalid_5 () = Note ("XSD-REGEX-REJECT-INVALID", 5u32, "2026-10-27T20:13Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 16:13 fiscal: this item's lib changes are LANDED (16:04; see FISCAL-GENERIC note of the same time). Runtime proof: TRAIN + set-proof.")
inl note_of_xsd_regex_reject_invalid_6 () = Note ("XSD-REGEX-REJECT-INVALID", 6u32, "2026-10-28T10:34Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 06:31 fiscal: DONE: landed 10-27 16:04; node set-proof 10-27/28, compiler 4FD712E9E357: xsd_regex lib gate 8/8 incl patterns_match_like_xsd (job-1793150142816).")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_1 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 1u32, "2026-10-27T21:22Z", "kit", "kit 17:2x: found while proving KIT-AGILE-SEAL-DONE on the node. The Spiral compile of agile_note_seal passed, but cargo failed twice: 'System.String.Join (v1, v0)' from sm'.join', then 'v1 |> Array.map v2' from am'.map'. Routed around for now: sm'.concat_array has a Rust arm, and the membership check no longer maps. Census method: functions in am'/sm'/listm'/seq whose body is .NET text (System., Array., Seq., List. or |>) with no run_target, backend_switch or Rust arm. am' has 6, sm' has join' (obj_to_string_fsharp and gleam_byte_size are backend-specific by name); listm' and seq have none. A compile-time guard would be better than per-function fixes: a Rust build that reaches a body with no Rust arm should fail in Spiral, not in cargo.")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_2 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 2u32, "2026-10-27T21:28Z", "kit", "kit 17:3x: first fix drafted, staged for the lib window after TRAIN: scratch clean/lib-dotnet/join-rust-arm.do (sm'.spi, 1 replacement). join' becomes run_target with an Fsharp arm keeping System.String.Join and a fallback to concat_array, which already has a Rust arm and a generic fold for the others. It gets a lib test block, join'_joins_with_the_separator_on_every_backend (fsharp and rust). Checked on a copy of today's lib: the program applies 1/1. A probe package calling sm'.join' compiles to 21 lines of Rust with 0 .NET text, the call becoming Rc::<str>::from(... .join(sep)). Still owed: the lib gate run, then the six am' bodies. am'.map' needs a real Rust arm (an iterator map over the borrowed Vec, calling the closure) or a generic Spiral body built with create/set; average, distinct, skip_while, parallel_map and transpose need the same shape decision.")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_3 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 3u32, "2026-10-27T21:30Z", "kit", "kit 17:3x: am'.map' added to the same staged program (clean/lib-dotnet/join-rust-arm.do, now 2 files). map' becomes run_target with Fsharp keeping Array.map and every other backend using core arraym.map, which map_base already relies on. Proof on a copy of today's lib: both edits apply. A probe calling ;[a;b;c] |> am'.map' (x ++# '!') |> sm'.join' ', ' compiles to 40 lines of Rust with 0 .NET text, rustc builds it, and it prints 'a!, b!, c!'. Left: average, distinct, skip_while, parallel_map and transpose, then the lib gate for the program, landing in the lib window after TRAIN. The F# arms keep their exact old bodies, so F# output is unchanged by construction.")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_4 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 4u32, "2026-10-27T21:31Z", "kit", "kit 17:3x, correction to note 1's census: am'.distinct and am'.skip_while already dispatch with '? is_rust () ?>' (a form my regex did not recognize), so they are not gaps. The real list was 5: sm'.join', am'.map' and am'.parallel_map (all three now drafted in join-rust-arm.do; parallel_map falls back to sequential arraym.map off .NET, which gives the same result), plus am'.average and am'.transpose, which are still open (average needs a numeric fold and division per type class; transpose works on array_base of array_base). parallel_map applies 1/1 on the lib copy too.")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_5 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 5u32, "2026-10-27T21:33Z", "kit", "kit 17:3x: the two left are low priority. am'.average and am'.transpose have one user each in the whole C:\\home\\git tree, lib benchmark.spi, which runs the .NET path. So the three staged fixes (join', map', parallel_map) cover every Rust-reachable gap found; average needs a {number} fold and division, and transpose an array_base-of-array_base Rust arm, when a Rust caller appears. The compile-time guard (a Rust build reaching a body with no Rust arm fails in Spiral, not cargo) is still the durable fix and belongs to the compiler lane's codegen.")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_6 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 6u32, "2026-10-28T00:32Z", "eoie", "20:3x kit: am'.average gets a Rust arm too. Draft: scratchpad clean\\lib-dotnet\\average-rust-arm.do; on non-F# backends it computes arraym.fold (+) 0 divided by conv (length a), and the F# arm keeps Array.average. A rustc probe prints 2.5 for [1.0; 2.0; 4.5] f64 and 5 for [2; 4; 9] i32 (integer division, as sum's generic 0 implies); no Array.average text is left in the .rs. That leaves am'.transpose (array_base of array_base; its types need a careful arraym.init version) and the compile-time guard against a .NET-only body reaching Rust. Lands with join-rust-arm.do in the next lib window, with a lib gate.")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_7 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 7u32, "2026-10-28T00:35Z", "eoie", "20:3x kit: am'.transpose gets a Rust arm as well (scratchpad clean\\lib-dotnet\\transpose-rust-arm.do). On non-F# backends it builds the result with arraym.init width, each column an arraym.init height reading index c of row r, unwrapped to array_base with the lib's '\\ (array x : array _) => x' idiom; an empty matrix gives an empty result. The F# arm keeps Array.transpose. A rustc probe on [[1;2;3];[4;5;6]] prints 3x2, last row 3,6, and no Array.transpose text is left. All 5 Rust-reachable bodies from today's census now have drafts (join', map', parallel_map, average, transpose). Left: land them in the next lib window with a lib gate (fsharp+rust test blocks still to add for average/transpose), then the compile-time guard so a .NET-only body can't silently emit F# into Rust again.")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_8 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 8u32, "2026-10-28T00:36Z", "eoie", "20:3x kit: fsharp+rust test blocks were added to average-rust-arm.do and transpose-rust-arm.do. They haven't run yet: test blocks are proven only by a notebook run or the lib gate. All five lib drafts (average, transpose, join-rust-arm, lib-clip, lib-text) apply together on the live lib as of 20:36, after tonight's other lib lands.")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_9 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 9u32, "2026-10-28T09:46Z", "eoie", "05:4x kit: this item's lib programs landed live in today's lib window. Gates are running on the node (P2 compiler pinned): see EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN's 05:4x note. The kit-side half follows in the next release.")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_10 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 10u32, "2026-10-28T11:19Z", "eoie", "07:1x kit: fix-forward of the two undone lib programs, green in local lib gates on a full lib copy (lib_gate.ps1 -Compiler process). (1) average-rust-arm.do: the test now uses f64 literals (1.0f64 ... 2.5f64; the bare literals defaulted to i32 under F#). average and transpose pass, 2/2. (2) lib-clip.do: three fixes. On F#, the untaken fallback arm got folded at compile time on a literal argument and hit a UTF-8 slice error, so the fallback now starts with dyn s. first_line and after_first_line get F# arms (IndexOf plus Substring), because the fallback mixed index_of's UTF-16 index with slice's UTF-8 bytes on F# ('é1' came back 'é'). The Rust clip arm borrows (&*!s) instead of moving, which fixes E0382 in the test context. clip, first_line and decimal/char pass, 3/3 on fsharp and rust. Both re-land in the 14:00 land window; kit-clip and kit-lines follow in TRAIN4 and need a fresh regen-compare, since clip's Rust text changed.")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_11 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 11u32, "2026-10-29T06:26Z", "main", "10-29 02:2x main: post-wipe state of the five drafts, read in the live lib (no lib edit yet): LANDED with Rust-capable arms: sm'.join' (run_target, Fsharp keeps System.String.Join, fallback concat_array), am'.map' and am'.parallel_map (Fsharp keeps Array.map / Array.Parallel.map, others arraym.map), am'.transpose (am'.spi:722, arraym.init columns, with test transpose_swaps_rows_and_columns). NOT landed: am'.average (am'.spi:525 is still the .NET-only body Array.average). n9's fix-forward draft (average-rust-arm.do, with f64 test literals) was in kit's TEMP and was lost in the wipe before the 14:00 re-land. REBUILT from n6 and n9 as a main draft (session scratchpad lib-average/apply.ps1, dry run: anchor x1): run_target with the Fsharp arm keeping its exact old body, and '| _ => \\ () => arraym.fold (+) 0 a / conv (length a)' (sum's fold, so i32 divides as integers, as n6 measured), plus test average_divides_the_sum_by_the_count (fsharp, rust: [1.0f64; 2.0f64; 4.5f64] gives 2.5f64). It lands after main's oracle accept run (the compile memo keys on every lib .spi, so the review and accept runs keep identical inputs), with a lib_gate run of the new test as the proof. Still open after that: the compile-time guard (a Rust build that reaches a body with no Rust arm fails in Spiral, not in cargo), for the compiler lane.")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_12 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 12u32, "2026-10-29T08:00Z", "main", "10-29 04:0x main: am'.average LANDED in the live lib (unstaged; the original is saved in main's session scratchpad as lib-average/am'.spi.before). Proof first, on a full lib copy: lib_gate -File am'.spi -Test average_divides_the_sum_by_the_count,transpose_swaps_rows_and_columns, tests passed 2 failed 0, type errors 0, all 4 rows pass (average fsharp + rust, transpose fsharp + rust). Landed only with no Cells reader running, and the live file's sha256 (638D7AA2FA7E0DF7) equals the gated copy's. All 5 Rust-reachable .NET-only bodies from n1's census now have Rust arms. Left: the compile-time guard (a Rust build reaching a body with no Rust arm fails in Spiral, not cargo), compiler lane.")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_13 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 13u32, "2026-10-29T17:53Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T17:53 re-proof at pack-6b: the 8 landed lib tests pass on core 31CC96520D6B7269 (node job job-1793295746436-kit-libland-gates-6b, remote.ps1 -RequireCore 31CC96520D6B7269, runner C:\\home\\remote-stage\\kit-libland-gates.ps1 via kit job --compile, exit 0 in 670 s): tests 2/13 + 1/34 + 5/70 passed, 0 failed, type errors 0 in all three suites. Main's closeout condition (sweep 4) is met.")
inl note_of_lib_dotnet_only_bodies_14 () = Note ("LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES", 14u32, "2026-10-29T17:55Z", "main", "10-29 13:5x main: CLOSED. The lib half is complete: all 5 Rust-reachable .NET-only bodies have Rust-capable arms (n10, n11), and kit re-proved the 8 landed tests on the installed core 31CC96520D6B7269 (n13, job-1793295746436-kit-libland-gates-6b: 2/13 + 1/34 + 5/70 passed, 0 failed, 0 type errors), which was sweep 4's closeout condition. The compile-time guard named in n11 is now its own compiler item, SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD (P2), together with today's sibling shape, a backend_switch missing its Rust arm (kit TRAIN4 13:21).")
inl note_of_delphi_cells_banner_capture_1 () = Note ("DELPHI-CELLS-BANNER-CAPTURE", 1u32, "2026-10-27T21:56Z", "compiler", "Filed 10-27 (compiler lane), from main's flag + diagnosis. The Cells delphi runner captures FPC trunk's stdout\nbanner as the program's output.\n\nMECHANISM: apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/targets.ex run_delphi (:326) runs the compiler (run_program -> `compiled`,\nwhich carries fpc's stdout) then the exe (run_status -> `ran`), and cell_exit(compiled, ran) COMBINES them. FPC trunk\n(3.3.1, i386) now prints its \"Free Pascal Compiler version ... Copyright ...\" banner to STDOUT, so it leaks into the\ncell output. Other backends don't show it (gcc/rustc are silent). Symptom: sm'.spi show_floats +\nshow_floats_in_python_repr_layout \"delphi disagrees\" (the evalue is the banner, even float-normalised).\n\nIMMEDIATE FIX (APPLIED, unstaged): targets.ex:321 fpc args -> [\"-O2\",\"-Mdelphi\",\"-l-\",\"-v0w\"]. Verified build-free on\nthe box: -l- -v0w => EMPTY stdout on a successful compile (banner gone), while a failing compile still prints\n\"Error: Identifier not found\" + \"Fatal\" exit 1 (build-failure detection preserved).\nTIMING CAVEAT (main 17:5x): apps/cells is read live by Cells, and kit's TRAIN runs 66 notebooks through it, so this\nsource edit can force a Mix recompile. It STANDS unless kit asks to hold/revert until the TRAIN window closes. VERIFY\n(build-bound) = a Cells run of sm'.spi -> both tests green.\n\nLONG-TERM (main: better shape, option b): split build output from run stdout in cell_exit so NO compiler stdout ever\nleaks into the cell output (covers warnings too, and dcc32 if ever used), instead of silencing fpc per-flag. Bigger\nchange touching all backends' output handling -- do it as this item's real fix after the flag stopgap is confirmed.\n\nRELATED (not this item): scoop freepascal is i386-only (bin\\i386-win32, no x86_64-win64) -> Delphi rows build 32-bit\n(\"for i386\"). main owns the x86_64 FPC trunk install (after accept-6; switching 32->64 mid-accept would move Delphi\nnative results under the running oracle), then the Delphi rows get their own review run.")
inl note_of_delphi_cells_banner_capture_2 () = Note ("DELPHI-CELLS-BANNER-CAPTURE", 2u32, "2026-10-27T22:00Z", "compiler", "Correction (main 17:5x): the x86_64 switch needs NO install -- scoop freepascal bin\\i386-win32 already ships ppcrossx64.exe and units\\x86_64-win64 (with rtl). It is just -Px86_64 in the delphi target args and the matrix Delphi native step. Still mains to apply after accept-6 + TRAIN window closed, with its own Delphi-vs-C review run (pointer width changes native results). Authoritative record: FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH n44. Supersedes this items earlier install an x86_64 FPC note.")
inl note_of_delphi_cells_banner_capture_3 () = Note ("DELPHI-CELLS-BANNER-CAPTURE", 3u32, "2026-10-27T22:03Z", "compiler", "Option-b design (the long-term fix), build-free analysis complete + verified SAFE. LEAK: cell_exit (targets.ex:606-607) folds the build step stdout (compiled.output) into the success output via join_output(compiled, ran). On SUCCESS that build stdout is noise; it is only wanted on FAILURE, which already surfaces via run_program {:error} (short-circuits before cell_exit). SAFE across ALL 3 cell_exit callers: :217 already passes %{output: \"\"} (empty, confirms intent); :299 run_cc (gcc) is silent on success so compiled.output is empty -> dropping is a no-op, C cells unchanged; :332 run_delphi (fpc) is the ONLY caller with non-empty build stdout (the trunk banner). FIX: cell_exit returns %{exit_status: ran.exit_status, output: ran.output} (drop compiled.output). Covers the banner, build WARNINGS, and dcc32 uniformly -> the -l- -v0w flag stopgap at :321 becomes removable. VERIFY (build-bound): a Cells run shows only the Delphi banner gone, every other backend cell byte-identical. Deferred to post-TRAIN per main; simpler + more robust than the per-flag stopgap.")
inl note_of_delphi_cells_banner_capture_4 () = Note ("DELPHI-CELLS-BANNER-CAPTURE", 4u32, "2026-10-29T05:55Z", "main", "10-29 02:0x main: OPTION B LANDED (unstaged; it was deferred only for kit's TRAIN window, which the wipe ended). apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/targets.ex: cell_exit(ran) now returns only the run's exit status and stdout; the build step's stdout no longer joins the cell output on success. Its 3 callers: run_delphi (fpc), run_cc (gcc, whose binding becomes _compiled) and promote_spiral (cpp, which already passed \"\"). join_output had cell_exit as its only caller and is removed. A failed build still surfaces via run_program's {:error} with the compiler output, and a Delphi build that produces no exe still returns its compiler output. The -l- -v0w flags stay: failure output stays concise, and they cost nothing now. Gap the stopgap left: -v0w still PRINTS WARNINGS on a successful build. Probe on trunk 3.3.1 (i386): a program with an uninitialized local builds with exit 0 and stdout 'main.pas(5,19) Warning: Local variable \"x\" does not seem to be initialized', which leaked into the cell output. Option b also covers MSVC cl, which echoes the source name on success. New test in apps/cells/test/spiral/cells/targets_test.exs, 'a Delphi cell's output is the program's stdout, never the build's warnings' (that program through Targets.run %{tool: :delphi}; @tag skip when no fpc is found). mix test of targets_test.exs: 23 passed, no compile warnings. NEGATIVE CONTROL: with the old joining cell_exit spliced back (source restored by hash afterwards), the new test fails with stdout \"main.pas(5,19) Warning: ...\\nok\". CONSUMER PATH: lib_gate.ps1 -File sm'.spi -Test show_floats,show_floats_in_python_repr_layout: passed 2, failed 0, 20 of 20 target rows passed (gleam, lua, fsharp, cuda, rust, typescript, c, zig, delphi, lean), compiler A4E480202BD40D80. The other backends are byte-identical by construction: gcc runs with -w, so its build stdout is empty on success, and promote_spiral already passed \"\". Trap for the next runner: `cmd /c start /b /wait mix.bat ...` returns exit 0 even when tests fail, so read the 'Result:' line. Done; the x86_64 switch is DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE.")
inl note_of_kit_unrun_test_blocks_1 () = Note ("KIT-UNRUN-TEST-BLOCKS", 1u32, "2026-10-28T00:00Z", "eoie", "2026-10-27 20:0x kit: found when TRAIN went red (job-1793137570729, 19:50). Test blocks in a module whose package modules aren't visible to a standalone notebook run never execute anywhere: generated crates carry no #[test] for :# blocks, and the notebook runner treats the file as main.spi without its package. TRAIN's candidate list added 12 notebooks; 5 failed for exactly this reason. evidence, workspace_primitives/files and kit_job_slot gave ETY-UnboundModule/UnboundVariable on their own package modules. ceremonies and crate_largest passed their test (executed 1, passed 1), then failed rustc with 'main function not found'. Census of kit/src: 9 modules with :# blocks and no ///- header, 14 test blocks (toolchain 4, kit_job_slot 2, kit_table_query/wheres 2, coverage_prune_model, ceremonies, crate_largest, evidence, os_launch/owner, 1 each). toolchain and owner pass anyway because they read only the lib. Fix: give each module the notebook prelude kit_jobs_doctor/check.spi uses ('///- --package .' plus ':# <module> static =' with its opens), run each notebook, and add kit standards / style-ratchet coverage that refuses a :# block in a module that can't run. Until then the release candidate list leaves these 5 out (make-TRAIN.ps1, 61 rows).")
inl note_of_kit_unrun_test_blocks_2 () = Note ("KIT-UNRUN-TEST-BLOCKS", 2u32, "2026-10-28T00:15Z", "eoie", "20:1x kit: preludes landed in the live tree (scratchpad clean\\unrun-tests\\preludes.do: '///- --package .' plus ':# <module> static =' opening sm'_operators) on slot, wheres, ceremonies, crate_largest, evidence and workspace_primitives/files. Test blocks generate no Rust (--backend skips them), so crates don't change; the TRAIN2 regen confirms that anyway. Notebook run job-1793146062366: every test now executes and passes: slot 2/2, wheres 2/2, ceremonies 1/1, crate_largest 1/1, evidence 1/1, files 1/1, so 8 test blocks run for the first time. wheres and files pass outright and are now in the release candidate list (make-TRAIN.ps1, 63 rows). slot, ceremonies, crate_largest and evidence still exit 1, after their tests, because the notebook also compiles the module's main for Zig: 'Export with 2 args not supported in the Zig backend'. Two ways out: move main and the exports to an entry.spi, as kit_jobs_doctor does, or have Cells skip main on backends a module doesn't target (Cells/compiler lane). Left: that decision, the remaining 3 modules (toolchain, owner and coverage_prune_model already run or run without a prelude; verify), and the standards check that refuses an unrunnable :# block.")
inl note_of_kit_unrun_test_blocks_3 () = Note ("KIT-UNRUN-TEST-BLOCKS", 3u32, "2026-10-28T00:25Z", "eoie", "20:2x kit: the 4 'main on Zig' notebooks pass now. main pointed to the known fix (CELLS-EVERYWHERE n16, SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR n35): a file's trailing Export main gets built as a cell on the default target (Zig) unless '///> _' sits directly above it. I tried '///> rust' on the static prelude first (job-1793146660992, still red) and reverted it. main-untargeted.do puts '///> _' above main in slot, evidence, ceremonies and crate_largest; run job-1793146772343 passes all 4 (slot, evidence, ceremonies, crate_largest). So all 6 modules from tonight run green and are in the release candidate list (make-TRAIN.ps1, 67 rows). Left: coverage_prune_model (core only, no main, likely runnable as-is; run it), toolchain and owner (already pass without a prelude), and the standards check that refuses a :# block in a module that can't run, plus a check for a trailing Export main with no '///> _'.")
inl note_of_kit_unrun_test_blocks_4 () = Note ("KIT-UNRUN-TEST-BLOCKS", 4u32, "2026-10-28T00:27Z", "eoie", "20:2x kit: coverage_prune_model passes as-is (job-1793147180621, executed 1, passed 1; core-only package, no main). All 9 modules from the census now run their 14 test blocks: 6 needed fixes tonight, and toolchain, owner and coverage_prune_model already ran. Left: the standards check (a :# block in a module that can't run as a notebook, and a trailing Export main without '///> _'), so the gap can't come back silently.")
inl note_of_kit_unrun_test_blocks_5 () = Note ("KIT-UNRUN-TEST-BLOCKS", 5u32, "2026-10-28T00:30Z", "eoie", "20:3x kit: I tried a first check script and dropped it, because its rule isn't the real Cells rule. It flagged a trailing Export main without '///> _' in kit_machine_crowding/crowding.spi, kit_do_verbs/verbs.spi, kit_log_sequences and kit_bundle_manifest, but crowding and verbs passed TRAIN's notebook run with exactly that layout (crowding's Export even takes 2 args). It also flagged owner, toolchain and coverage_prune_model for lacking a prelude, and all three pass. So the condition that sends main to Zig is narrower than 'untargeted trailing main'. The standards check needs the exact rule from Cells' memo.ex/default_builders first (main's 20:2x reading: memo roots every block that defines main, and untargeted blocks take the static prelude's targets, else default_target, else Zig). Next step: read that path and write the check from it, with tonight's 4 reds and crowding/verbs as positive and negative controls.")
inl note_of_kit_unrun_test_blocks_6 () = Note ("KIT-UNRUN-TEST-BLOCKS", 6u32, "2026-10-28T00:40Z", "eoie", "20:4x kit: the Cells rule, from reading the code. memo.ex unreached_definitions roots every block that isn't a plain definition, plus any block that defines main. notebook.ex default_builders gives untargeted cells the static prelude's own ///> commands, else default_target or SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET, else ///> zig. So a rooted main without its own ///> line builds on Zig unless the prelude cell carries ///> commands; '///> _' above main opts it out. Open: why crowding.spi and verbs.spi pass with a bare main. My '///> rust' line above ':# slot static =' didn't count as the prelude's commands, so the prelude cell's source likely starts at ':#'. Either their Exports compile on Zig or their main is reached differently; check that before writing the standards rule. Also checked: the census crate compiles with the probe fix (977 lines, same as the tree), using a bundle with the lib at src\\spiral. A direct compile from kit/src fails 'Package C:/home/git/kit/src/spiral is not loaded', so kit compiles need that layout.")
inl note_of_kit_unrun_test_blocks_7 () = Note ("KIT-UNRUN-TEST-BLOCKS", 7u32, "2026-10-28T11:24Z", "eoie", "07:3x kit: the exact rule, found by comparing files. crowding.spi and verbs.spi pass with a bare main because their main comes BEFORE the test blocks; the 4 reds had a TRAILING main (after the last :# test), which Cells builds as its own cell on the default Zig target. kit_log_sequences/sequences.spi has a trailing bare main and still passes because its static prelude carries '///> _' (no default targets). Rule: in a headed .spi with test blocks, a main after the last test block needs '///> _' directly above it, or a '///> _' on the static prelude. Interim check: i574n.github/scripts/notebook-main-check.ps1 -Src <dir>, which finds 0 in kit/src today; its positive control (slot.spi with the '///> _' line removed) flags slot.spi:186. Next: make it a kit standards rule in TRAIN4 (kit_standards rules.spi), with that control as the test.")
inl note_of_kit_unrun_test_blocks_8 () = Note ("KIT-UNRUN-TEST-BLOCKS", 8u32, "2026-10-28T11:34Z", "eoie", "07:3x kit: the rule is now a kit standards check, in the live tree, riding TRAIN3 (scratchpad clean\\unrun-tests\\trailing-main-rule.do). Under TestTargets, file_scan tracks seen_test and header_untargeted, and a ':= main () : i32 =' line after a test block, without '///> _' directly above it or an untargeted prelude, yields 'a main after a test block builds on the default Zig target; put ///> _ above it'. New test a_main_after_a_test_block_needs_an_untargeted_marker covers trailing (flagged), marked, leading and untargeted-prelude (not flagged). The kit_standards/rules.spi notebook passes (job-1793186898937).")
inl note_of_kit_unrun_test_blocks_9 () = Note ("KIT-UNRUN-TEST-BLOCKS", 9u32, "2026-10-29T13:29Z", "kit", "10-29 09:2x kit: found in TRAIN3's candidate run (job-1793275863025): agile_note_seal/seal.spi is red 0/2 TYPE-ERROR, 'ETY-UnboundModule' on every open (rust_std_fs, agile_task_text, agile_note_model, workspace_primitives) and then unbound byte_slice/byte_len. Cause: the file has test blocks (a_note_line_names_its_owner_and_binding, sealing_moves_only_done_notes_and_partitions_the_lines) but no notebook header ('///- --package .' plus a ':# seal static =' prelude repeating the opens), so Cells compiles it as its own root with no package in scope. These tests have never run. The crate itself is fine: the regen phase compiled agile_note_seal. It surfaced now because release-train.ps1 selects every .spi with test blocks in a changed package. A scan of all 73 selected notebooks finds this as the only headerless one. Fix drafted: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\train3-reds\\seal-notebook-header.do (adds the header and the static prelude, kit_jobs/model.spi's shape). Plan: once the notebook phase ends, apply it, prove it codegen-neutral (dev.ps1 -CheckOnly -Package agile_note_seal leaves seal.rs byte-equal), then rerun the reds with release-train.ps1 -SkipRegen -Only before the install. Gap in this item's rule: kit standards catches a main after a test block, but not a module whose test blocks have no notebook header at all; add that as a second finding.")
inl note_of_kit_unrun_test_blocks_10 () = Note ("KIT-UNRUN-TEST-BLOCKS", 10u32, "2026-10-29T13:45Z", "kit", "10-29 09:4x kit: the second finding is drafted (not compiled): C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\train3-reds\\standards-headerless-tests.do (7 replacements in kit_standards/rules.spi). file_scan gains notebook_header (line 1 starts with ///-). Under TestTargets, the first ':# x =' or 'test x =' block (not static) in a file without that header yields one finding: 'a test block in a module without a notebook header never runs; start the file with ///- --package . and a :# <module> static = prelude'. New test a_test_block_needs_a_notebook_header_to_run: a headerless file gets exactly 1 finding at its first test; a headed file and a file without tests get none. The existing widths_crlf_floats_and_test_targets_are_flagged test is adjusted because its headerless text now earns the new finding at line 5; its old 'no test-targets finding at line 5' check becomes 'no missing-target finding at line 5'. Census of all kit/src: 6 headerless modules with test blocks, so 6 sets of tests that have never run: agile_note_seal/seal.spi (red in TRAIN3; header draft ready), agile_todo_model/model.spi, coverage_prune_model/model.spi, kit_dev_spiral_tests/failures.spi, kit_os_launch/owner.spi, workspace_primitives/toolchain.spi. Order: give all 6 headers and run their notebooks first (fix any red tests they reveal), then land the rule, so 'kit standards' stays green. Cross-reference: main's LIB-TESTS-NOT-RUN covers the same class in lib.")
inl note_of_kit_unrun_test_blocks_11 () = Note ("KIT-UNRUN-TEST-BLOCKS", 11u32, "2026-10-29T14:20Z", "kit", "10-29 10:2x kit: headers for the 5 other modules are drafted, and the dry run applies (5 files, 5 replacements): C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\train3-reds\\headerless-modules.do. A pattern explains 2 of them: agile_todo_model/model.spi and kit_dev_spiral_tests/failures.spi HAD '///- --package .', but a ':< sm'_operators' line sits ABOVE it (line 1), so the header is no longer on line 1 and Cells ignores it. That's most likely a past codemod that prepended its import to every file, the ++# / sm'_operators migration being the likeliest. seal.spi and kit_os_launch/owner.spi start with the same import and no header at all. Fix shapes: files with imports get the header on line 1 plus a ':# <module> static =' prelude repeating their opens (the model.spi convention); files without imports (coverage_prune_model/model.spi, workspace_primitives/toolchain.spi) get the bare header line, like the 40 *_cli.spi notebooks. Applies after the TRAIN3 rerun, together with the standards rule, which now also stops a prepended import from silently disabling a notebook again. Then run the 5 notebooks; any red they reveal is a real, previously hidden failure.")
inl note_of_kit_unrun_test_blocks_12 () = Note ("KIT-UNRUN-TEST-BLOCKS", 12u32, "2026-10-29T15:55Z", "kit", "10-29 11:5x kit: the 5 header fixes are APPLIED in kit/src and proven. Regen of their crate owners (kit_dev_spiral_tests, kit_os_launch, in job-1793288690204) shows them codegen-neutral (4 unchanged owners); the other 3 are module-only packages. Their notebooks, run for the FIRST time (release-train -NotebooksOnly, candidate 8CD04145): agile_todo_model/model.spi, coverage_prune_model/model.spi, kit_dev_spiral_tests/failures.spi, kit_os_launch/owner.spi and workspace_primitives/toolchain.spi all PASS, so no hidden failures. seal.spi went green in TRAIN3 (plus '///> _' above its main). Rule 1 (trailing main) is installed in CD2F0D1A and fires live (TRAIN3 closeout). Left: rule 2 (headerless test module), drafted as standards-headerless-tests.do, which shares kit_standards/rules.spi with the m5 restore, so it applies after that restore and rides TRAIN4.")
inl note_of_kit_unrun_test_blocks_13 () = Note ("KIT-UNRUN-TEST-BLOCKS", 13u32, "2026-10-29T22:40Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T22:40 kit: DONE on installed kit C528BE4D (TRAIN4c). Standards refuses a test block in a module without a notebook header (closeout rule 2 PASS; the old binary prints nothing). The headerless modules (the 9 plus merge.spi, which TRAIN4b found) ran green in TRAIN4c's notebooks; a scan of every changed kit .spi finds no other headerless test module.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_1 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 1u32, "2026-10-28T02:11Z", "main", "10-27 22:11 main: Filed 10-27 21:5x from x's node finding: a lib gate on the default Cells daemon port 13905 failed 16 of 26 tests around 21:0x (13 :lock_timeout, 2 'did not start', 1 'compiler on port 13905 started with a different build', meaning another job's daemon from a different compiler held the port). Jobs that share one machine share the one default port, so a parallel job contends for it, and a job started after a compiler install meets the previous build's daemon. Today's mitigations are per job: SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT (x 13950/13977) or SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process with SPIRAL_CELLS_SERVER=0 (fiscal's set-proofs). Kit's TRAIN2 preflight now waits until nothing listens on 139xx (EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN n170). Fix: (1) the default port is derived from the compiler build (e.g. 13905 + a hash of the SpiralCompilerCore MVID, mod a range), so two builds never share a daemon and a post-install run gets a fresh one; (2) a per-job port when several Cells runs on one machine are expected (kit jobs could export one from the job id). Done when: two Cells gates on different compilers, started together on the node, both pass with 0 daemon errors (positive control), and two on the same build either share cleanly or get distinct ports. Tag eoie as well if the fix lands in kit's notebook runner.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_2 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 2u32, "2026-10-28T02:18Z", "main", "10-27 22:18 main: Second casualty, same night: tibia's src-postland node run failed pack_sim, hunt_sim, creatures and gpu_sim on :lock_timeout (shared 13905), and is re-queued as job-1793152392179. tibia's 6 RED negatives since 18:21 were re-read and all fail on real assertions with 0 daemon errors, so its 18 lands stand. Mitigations now in use: tibia takes ports 13983-13989, each held by a named mutex Global\\tibia-cells-port-N for the job's life, with SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process as the fallback; x uses 13950 and 13977; fiscal uses process mode. So three lanes hand-allocate ports. That's the case for the build- and job-derived default, which would retire all three schemes.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_3 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 3u32, "2026-10-28T09:44Z", "main", "10-28 05:44 main: Third case, on the box (10-28 05:37-06:0x): tibia's local Cells positive control set SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT=13985 by hand, without its own mutex scheme. Its CompilerDaemon (erl 121932) outlived the run, orphaned (grandparent gone), and as a live lib reader it held off kit's two sm'.spi lands in the lib window until tibia stopped it. Ownership was unclear from the outside: neither the command line nor the log names the owner, and kit first attributed it to main. Fix requirements this adds: (1) a daemon started by a run dies with that run, or has an idle timeout of minutes (it idled from 05:41 on); (2) the daemon's log or command line names the owner (team, job id or parent pid), so an orphan can be attributed and stopped without guessing. Kit's jobs-doctor draft is to flag orphaned daemons.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_4 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 4u32, "2026-10-28T11:04Z", "main", "10-28 07:04 main: Design draft (main, 10-28 07:1x), from reading apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/compiler_daemon.ex. What exists: port/0 is SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT else a fixed @default_port 13905 (L6, L65-81); an idle retire with a 30 min default (SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_IDLE_MIN, L9-19) plus a 5 min pressure-idle rule below 4,096 MB free; an owner field (SPIRAL_CELLS_DAEMON_OWNER, L112) that no caller sets, so it reads 'unrecorded' (that's why tonight's orphan took 3 lanes to attribute); and a boot stamp of the Cells CODE (module md5, L126-128). The 'different build' check compares that Cells stamp; it does not cover the compiler DLL. So two jobs on different COMPILERS but the same Cells code share 13905 and the same banner. Proposal, three small changes. (1) Default port = 13905 + (fnv1a(compiler core DLL sha256 hex ++ Cells boot stamp) mod 64) when SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT is unset: clients and daemons compute the same value from the same inputs, two compiler builds land on different ports, and a post-install run gets a fresh daemon by construction. The compiler hash comes from Toolchain.compiler_dll(opts), the same DLL the daemon serves. Add a collision fallback: if the derived port answers with a different banner, refuse loudly, never share. (2) The banner carries the compiler core hash too (spiral-cells, version, pid, cells stamp, compiler stamp), and the client refuses a daemon whose compiler stamp differs, instead of using it. (3) Owner defaults to something attributable when SPIRAL_CELLS_DAEMON_OWNER is unset: KIT_JOB_ID or EOIE_JOB_ID if present, else the client's parent pid and executable name; `mix spiral.compiler_daemon --list` already prints owner. Optional (4): lower the default idle to 10 min; the pool restarts warm in seconds, and a 30 min idle kept tonight's orphan holding the lib. Done rule (from n1): two Cells gates on different compilers started together on one machine both pass with 0 daemon errors, and --list shows two daemons on two ports, each with an owner. This is apps/cells work (Elixir), about 2 h plus its tests; it needs a window with no Cells runs on the box to land.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_5 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 5u32, "2026-10-30T14:40Z", "main", "10-30 10:40 main: re-read against the live tree, then DRAFTED. Since n4, part (3) has half-landed: compiler_client.daemon_owner now records 'os_pid N argv ...' (and --list prints it), but it has no job id. Draft: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1030\\cells-daemon-port\\apply.ps1 (anchored, 4 edits; dry run on the live tree 4/4 anchors match exactly once, nothing written). (1) CompilerDaemon.port() with no env = build_port(Toolchain.compiler_dll([])) = 13905 + 8 x phash2({path, size, mtime}, 12). The DLL's size and mtime change on every install, so a post-install run gets a fresh daemon, while two jobs on the SAME build still share one (wanted: a warm pool). It avoids hashing a large DLL on every port() call. The range is 13905..13993 plus the pool, inside 139xx, so kit's TRAIN2 preflight ('nothing listens on 139xx') still covers it, and lib_gate's copy-gate range 14000-14999 isn't touched. A missing DLL maps to 13905. (2) The owner gains 'job <KIT_JOB_ID|EOIE_JOB_ID>' when set, so an orphan names its job (the 10-28 box orphan took 3 lanes to attribute). (3) Tests: build_port is stable for the same file, lands on the 13905+8k grid, moves across 24 mtimes, and gives 13905 for a missing DLL; the port() default test now expects build_port. NOT in this draft (n4 part 2): a compiler stamp in the banner. The client's stamp check compares only the Cells code, so two builds that hash to the same slot (1 in 12) still share a daemon; that's the follow-up. RISK, recorded before landing: a derived slot can equal a hand-picked lane port (tibia 13983-13989 = slot 10 gives 13985; x 13950/13977 sit between grid points), so the lanes should DROP their hand ports when this lands (their env overrides keep working until then). LAND: a Cells window (no Cells runs on the box), then mix test test/spiral/cells/compiler_daemon_test.exs plus the full mix test, then broadcast to x, tibia and fiscal. Owner main, after the PACK-8 review and install.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_6 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 6u32, "2026-10-30T15:14Z", "main", "10-30 11:14 main: the apply script now also carries CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT's starter edits (7 anchors in all, dry run 7/7; scratch-copy parse OK on all 3 files). Land both in one Cells window after PACK-8.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_7 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 7u32, "2026-10-30T18:17Z", "main", "10-30 14:17 main: LANDED in apps/cells (unstaged), main's Cells window 14:09-14:2x (win-1793383787468, released). cells-daemon-port\\cells-window.ps1 -Apply took undo copies (cells-daemon-port\\undo-20261030-140956), applied the 7 anchored edits, then: mix test compiler_daemon_test.exs 22 passed; FULL mix test 327 passed, 1 excluded, 0 failures. Hashes: compiler_daemon.ex E785F6A18692, compiler_client.ex A3BD7AFA5166, compiler_daemon_test.exs F86C8336F823. Live proof on the consumer path: lib_gate -File guid.spi with no SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT set gave 2/2, and its daemon came up on the build-derived port 13945 (= 13905 + 8 x 5; cells-13945.start.cmd), not 13905. Open from n4: part (2), a compiler stamp in the banner (a 1-in-12 slot collision between two builds still shares a daemon). Lanes can now drop their hand-picked ports (x 13950/13977, tibia 13983-13989, fiscal process mode); told them.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_8 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 8u32, "2026-10-30T18:18Z", "main", "10-30 14:18 main: design for the remaining part (2), read from the code before drafting (decide-from-tree-not-notes). What exists: the daemon tracks dll_stamp(dll) of the compiler it serves and restarts its compiler session when that DLL changes (compiler_daemon.ex:539 compiler_current?, :649 dll_stamp), but it always serves Toolchain.compiler_dll(state.toolchain), i.e. the DLL in the DAEMON's environment, not the client's. Remaining gap: two builds whose slots collide (1 in 12) reach one daemon, and the second client gets the first build's compiler. The NAIVE fix, mixing dll_stamp into code_stamp/boot_stamp, is WRONG: on a stamp mismatch the client calls replace(port, pid) (compiler_client.ex:141), so two concurrent jobs on colliding builds would keep killing each other's daemon. RIGHT fix: (a) the banner and status line carry the daemon's dll_stamp as a separate field (the Cells code stamp stays as is); (b) when the client's own dll_stamp differs from the daemon's, it does NOT replace: it probes the NEXT slot (port + 8, wrapping inside the 12-slot grid) and ensures a daemon there, and replace stays only for a Cells-code mismatch on its own slot; (c) a test with two fake DLLs forced onto one slot asserts two daemons on two ports and no replace. About 45 min plus a Cells window. Progress stays 85%.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_9 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 9u32, "2026-10-30T18:27Z", "main", "10-30 14:27 main: GAP in what landed at 14:09, found by x (SIM-IN-SPIRAL n107). build_port hashes ONLY the compiler DLL's path, size and mtime, but n4's design hashed the DLL AND the Cells identity. Consequences: (1) every node mirror uses the same installed DLL, so every mirror gets the SAME port; a mirror with different Cells code then meets another mirror's daemon, the client's code-stamp check calls replace(port, pid), and the jobs fight, which is the pre-change bug in a new place. (2) Scripts that stop 'their' daemon by port in a finally (x apps/sim/build.ps1) kill another job's live daemon. x moved to an explicit per-run port (20000 + PID % 20000). FIX (small; next Cells window): build_port(dll) hashes {Path.expand(dll), size, mtime, @cells_root}, so each mirror (its own apps/cells path) gets its own slot, while jobs in the same checkout on the same build still share a warm daemon. Plus a test: two cells roots give different ports. Daemon cleanup should go by pid or owner (compiler_client's retire path or '--list' owner), never by port: noted for every lane. The title also says 'per job', so per-job isolation stays opt-in through SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT (x's per-run port) for jobs that must not share. Progress 85 -> 75.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_10 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 10u32, "2026-10-30T18:28Z", "main", "10-30 14:28 main: the n9 fix is DRAFTED: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1030\\cells-daemon-port\\apply2-cells-root.ps1 (2 anchored edits; dry run on the live tree 2/2, nothing written; applied to a scratch copy, both files parse). build_port(dll, cells_root) defaults cells_root to Path.expand of ../../.. from __DIR__ and hashes {dll path, size, mtime, cells root}, so each node mirror gets its own slot. The path is computed inline because @cells_root is defined at line ~989, BELOW build_port, and an attribute read before its definition is nil. The test adds: 24 cells roots give more than one port, and the same root gives a stable port. LAND in the next Cells window with the same procedure as cells-window.ps1 (undo, compiler_daemon_test, full mix test). Lanes checked: tibia keeps explicit ports and stops nothing by port; fiscal stays in process mode and stops nothing by port; x moved to a per-run explicit port; kit was warned.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_11 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 11u32, "2026-10-30T18:52Z", "main", "10-30 14:52 main: n9's fix LANDED in Cells window 2 (14:47-14:5x, win-1793386058482, released). The build_port hash now includes the cells root. compiler_daemon_test 22 passed; full mix test 327 passed, 1 excluded. Hashes: compiler_daemon.ex 071C1BF1DBF7, compiler_daemon_test.exs 55356DAEBBEC; undo in cells-daemon-port\\undo-* (second folder). Live proof: lib_gate guid.spi 2/2, daemon on 13921 (was 13945 before the root was hashed), so the checkout now selects the slot and node mirrors get separate daemons. Left: n8 part (2), probing the next slot when the client's dll_stamp differs from the daemon's (instead of replace). Progress 75 -> 85.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_12 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 12u32, "2026-10-30T22:23Z", "main", "10-30 18:23 main: part (2) DRAFTED, plus a REGRESSION FIX found while drafting. apply3-slot-probe.ps1 (cells-daemon-port\\; 4 anchored edits, dry run 4/4 on the live tree; applied to a scratch copy, all .ex/.exs parse and run_notebook.ps1 has 0 parse errors). (a) The daemon banner gains a 5th field, served_dll_tag ('<mtime>-<size>' of the DLL it serves); old clients parse [pid, stamp | _], so it's compatible. (b) With no env port, the client's base port = pick_slot over the 12-slot grid from build_port: the first slot that's down, legacy or serving its own DLL; a slot serving ANOTHER build is skipped, never replaced. Cached per VM in persistent_term. (c) REGRESSION from 14:09's landing: run_notebook.ps1:72 still probed 13905 for the warm daemon when no env port is set, so every notebook took the cold mix path (slower; results unaffected). Fix: the client writes spiral-bin\\cells-port-<first 16 hex of sha256(lowercased, forward-slash cells root)>.txt and run_notebook reads it (fallback 13905). Key parity across Elixir and pwsh checked: 215003CF759F40A9 for c:/home/git/spiral/apps/cells. Tests added: slot_port wrap; pick_slot with fake probes (other build -> next slot, own build or legacy -> same slot, all taken -> first); root_key normalization; banner 5 fields. LAND: a ~6-min Cells window right after compiler's 18:40 split slot.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_13 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 13u32, "2026-10-30T23:08Z", "main", "10-30 19:08 main: part (2) and the run_notebook warm-port fix LANDED (Cells window 3, 18:45-18:51, auto-started by kit window wait-clear after compiler's timing slot): full mix test 330 passed, 1 excluded (+3 new tests); compiler_daemon.ex 84D4D830, compiler_client.ex 82F3DB28, compiler_daemon_test.exs 5B9F497E, run_notebook.ps1 E2A28FAE. LIVE: a gate with no env port wrote spiral-bin\\cells-port-215003CF759F40A9.txt = 13921, which is the key run_notebook.ps1 computes and the daemon's actual port. All three parts of the item are now in: per build, per checkout, and probing the next slot instead of replacing. Left: none required; a two-build collision test on real DLLs would be the extra proof. Progress 85 -> 95.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_14 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 14u32, "2026-10-30T23:38Z", "main", "10-30 19:3x main: REAL two-build collision proof, and a GAP it found. Method: copied the live compiler release dir and stepped the copy's DLL mtime until build_port(copy) == build_port(live) = 13921 (offset +6 s; next slot 13929); held a live-build daemon on 13921 (owner 'main collision-proof 10-30', retired after), then ran lib_gate guid.spi with SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL=<copy>. RESULT: 2/2; the client skipped 13921 and started its own daemon on 13929; the held daemon was NOT replaced (same pid). GAP: the held daemon's log shows a 'compile open main.spi' at 19:20:05 from the copy run: run_notebook.ps1's warm path sends 'notebook' to the port in the root-keyed port file, and the daemon compiles with ITS OWN DLL, so a run on another build silently compiled on the wrong compiler; the copy run also rewrote the port file to 13929. FIX DRAFTED: cells-daemon-port\\apply4-dll-guard.ps1 (9 anchors, dry run 9/9): the warm request carries 'dll' and notebook_reply answers 'stale' (cold fallback) when dll_tag(dll) != served_dll_tag; the warm-port file is keyed by root AND dll tag (cells-port-<root16>-<mtime>-<size>.txt; run_notebook computes the tag from Get-SpiralCompilerDll or SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL). Scratch copy: run_notebook.ps1 0 parse errors; warm_notebook_test 4 passed (new stale-build test), compiler_daemon_test 25 passed (warm_port_file key test). Land queued: land4-when-clear.ps1 waits for the other lanes' live Cells runs, then a tree-write window, full mix test, undo on red.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_15 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 15u32, "2026-10-31T00:01Z", "main", "10-30 20:01 main: n14's guard LANDED (Cells window 4, win-1793404076544, 19:47 until the full mix test ended; released): compiler_daemon_test + warm_notebook_test 29 passed, FULL mix test 331 passed, 1 excluded. Hashes: compiler_daemon.ex F150F5CA4318, compiler_client.ex 0BE1088C172F, compiler_daemon_test.exs 5BB8B4F6A58C, warm_notebook_test.exs 97246FFCC9E9, run_notebook.ps1 D7ADAC3D526F, priv/new_project/editorconfig 4F71105F64A5 (CELLS-NEW-PROJECT gap (a), packed into the same window). Undo: cells-daemon-port\\undo-20261030-194757. Next: repeat the two-build proof on the landed code (the held daemon's log must show NO compile from the copy run). ALSO a regression of MY 14:09 landing, reported by compiler from the user: daemon starts went through 'kit process start-under-shell' by default (find_executable kit fallback + lib_gate default), and kit's cmd.exe is created DETACHED_PROCESS, so erl allocates a new VISIBLE console. Fix landing now (Cells window 5, apply5-launcher-optin.ps1): unset/''/'none' -> the hidden Start-Process branch; lib_gate no longer defaults the launcher. kit has the CREATE_NO_WINDOW fix; the shell-parent default returns only after that is installed and verified hidden.")
inl note_of_cells_daemon_port_per_build_16 () = Note ("CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD", 16u32, "2026-10-31T00:12Z", "main", "10-30 20:12 main: DONE. (1) Cells window 5 LANDED (20:00:46, win-1793404845438, released): the daemon shell launcher is opt-in again; unset/''/'none' select the hidden Start-Process branch, and lib_gate.ps1 no longer defaults it to kit.exe. Full mix test 331 passed, 1 excluded. compiler_client.ex 1212147E18C5, compiler_daemon_test.exs E613C147660C, lib_gate.ps1 62CE6CBF94F2. LIVE CHECK: every erl started after 20:08 has no visible window. Two visible ones were MY OWN pre-fix gates (owner via the daemon status verb: the LAMBDA-PARENS base.spi edit arm and the strict-blast strict arm, whose environments still had the old default); I hid them with ShowWindow(SW_HIDE), without stopping the runs. (2) The two-build proof on the landed code, as a reusable script cells-daemon-port\\collide-proof.ps1: a DLL copy forced to the live build's slot 13921 (mtime +6 s) ran lib_gate guid.spi 2/2 on the next slot 13929 (its per-DLL warm-port file names 13929), and the held live-build daemon compiled 0 files for it (the 19:20 run: 1) and kept its pid. VERDICT: PROVEN. Goal met: per build, per checkout, next-slot probing, and the warm path refuses another build. The shell-parent default (CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT) returns once kit's CREATE_NO_WINDOW launcher is installed and shown hidden.")
inl note_of_lib_fs_gleam_cwd_test_1 () = Note ("LIB-FS-GLEAM-CWD-TEST", 1u32, "2026-10-28T02:27Z", "x", "2026-10-27 22:27 x: filed after it cost two explanations tonight (LIB-FILE-TYPE-SHADOW notes 5-7). lib file_system.spi's test file_exists_on_gleam_tells_files_from_missing_paths asserts file_exists 'gleam.toml', a path relative to the Gleam run's working directory, so it is red whenever the gate starts anywhere without a gleam.toml (a direct run_notebook.ps1 launch from a node job's home dir, and per CI-OFFLOAD's 10-25 baseline and COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS, on the installed compiler too). Every file_system gate shows one 'expected' red that each reader must recognize, which hides a new red there. Fix: make the test create its own file and check it (the Rust test near append_all_text writes root </> 'a.txt' first), or assert on a path the test owns; keep the missing-path half as is. Land through lib-guarded-do outside kit windows; gate on a private SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT.")
inl note_of_lib_fs_gleam_cwd_test_2 () = Note ("LIB-FS-GLEAM-CWD-TEST", 2u32, "2026-10-28T02:31Z", "x", "2026-10-27 22:31 x: drafted and proven on a scratch copy (scratchpad fs-cwd-test.lib.kit, one kit do program, 2 replacements). The old block goes, leaving one blank line before /// ### directory_delete. The new test sits after file_delete, so it can use write_all_text and file_delete, both with Gleam arms: it writes spiral-file-exists-probe.txt, asserts it exists, deletes it, and asserts (written, exists after delete, missing path) = (true, false, false). It owns its file, so the result no longer depends on the working directory. No new line over 120 (the file's 3 long lines predate this). kit do escapes learned: in a replacement, backslash-n is a newline and backslash-quote a quote; in a pattern, backslash-escaped regex metacharacters come out doubled, so match | and + with '.' instead. Landing queued behind main's heavy-build window (5n A/B, 137 min left at 22:3x) by a retry loop until 01:30; gate after it lands, on a private SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT.")
inl note_of_lib_fs_gleam_cwd_test_3 () = Note ("LIB-FS-GLEAM-CWD-TEST", 3u32, "2026-10-28T02:55Z", "x", "2026-10-27 22:55 x: LANDED 22:51:20 through lib-guarded-do (try 11, once main's 5n window had released), sha256 7ac1f7bc -> dce66aee, the same bytes as the scratch-copy result I reviewed. Gate on a node mirror with a private daemon port (job-1793156178571). kit's TRAIN2 window starts now (ETA ~05:00): no lib writes from x until it closes. If the gate goes red, the revert (the reverse program) waits for the window too; kit and main were told the write is final and test-only.")
inl note_of_lib_fs_gleam_cwd_test_4 () = Note ("LIB-FS-GLEAM-CWD-TEST", 4u32, "2026-10-28T03:39Z", "x", "2026-10-27 23:39 x: DONE. Gate on a node mirror with a private daemon port (job-1793156178571, 2576 s, the same direct run_notebook.ps1 launch from the job's home dir that failed the old test every time): 0 failed; file_exists_on_gleam_tells_files_from_missing_paths passed with its own probe file. This is the first file_system gate with no baseline red since at least 10-25 (CI-OFFLOAD's baseline), so any red there now is a real one. Note for whoever reads that gate's log: the job printed no spiral-tests total line (Select-String MatchInfo objects inside the staged script didn't render), so the verdict was read from the notebook's per-test statuses.")
inl note_of_delphi_64bit_dfa_disagree_1 () = Note ("DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE", 1u32, "2026-10-28T03:22Z", "main", "10-27 23:22 main: Found 10-27 23:1x by main's 5n sample regen (run single-flight-20261027-225158): with the Delphi rows built 64-bit (fpc -Px86_64, applied 21:4x for FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH), samples/mega_brzozowski_derivatives/inventory_closed_dfa_runtime [Delphi] exits 1 while every other backend (C, Rust, Zig, Wasm, Kotlin, Swift, Lean, Bend, Gleam, Lua, Python, TypeScript, F#) exits 0 with the same empty stdout. As 32-bit the same main.pas exits 0 (accept-6 agree). Repro, from the CURRENT 5n output: scratchpad delphi64-probe.ps1 (copies the sample's main.pas, builds it with scoop FPC's i386 driver as 32-bit and as 64-bit via ppcrossx64, runs both): 32-bit exit 0, 64-bit exit 1. The exit code is SpiralMain's result (main.pas:2626 Halt(SpiralMain)), so one of the program's own checks computes differently with 64-bit words. A census of the 74 KB main.pas finds none of the usual suspects (0 shl/shr, 0 Ptr/NativeInt, 0 Pointer(), 0 Cardinal/LongWord, 0 integer casts; one SizeOf, on the output buffer). Next: instrument SpiralMain's checks (print each check's value in both builds and diff), or bisect by trimming the inventory to the first failing check. Likely areas: integer overflow or wrap semantics (Delphi Integer stays 32-bit on x64, so i64 or u64 ops may map to a type whose behaviour changes), set or enum layout, record alignment. Decision: -Px86_64 is reverted in both test.ps1:754 and apps/cells targets.ex:321 (plus its test and README:223) until this is fixed, so the oracle stays clean. Re-apply with TEMP delphi64-matrix/apply.ps1 and the Cells edit once this sample agrees as 64-bit.")
inl note_of_delphi_64bit_dfa_disagree_2 () = Note ("DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE", 2u32, "2026-10-28T12:24Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 build-free read of inventory_closed_dfa main.pas CONFIRMS mains census: the ONLY 64-bit-sensitive tokens are function SpiralMain: LongInt (:2451, 32-bit on both Delphi targets), the benign SizeOf(SpiralOutputBuffer) (:2625, text buffer), and Halt(SpiralMain) (:2626). NO Int64/UInt64/QWord/NativeInt/set-of/Pointer anywhere -> the body is all Integer/LongInt (32-bit, consistent across 32/64). So the 32/64 divergence is NOT an explicit 64-bit type/size diff; it is subtle -- FPC x64 codegen, a 32/64 RTL/library behavior diff, or UB that differs by build. Build-free surface EXHAUSTED. Only path = main empirical instrument: add per-check Writeln across SpiralMain (2452-2623), build 32-bit (fpc) + 64-bit (ppcrossx64), diff the printed values -> the first divergent check. BUILD-BOUND (node + FPC). Next: get/recreate delphi64-probe.ps1, instrument, run on the node.")
inl note_of_delphi_64bit_dfa_disagree_3 () = Note ("DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE", 3u32, "2026-10-28T12:36Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 LOCALIZED via instrumented node probe (job-...706980): the 64-bit divergence is v60 := loop_8(v59, v20) (main.pas:2588). 32-bit loop_8 -> True (then v70=1 v72tag=2 v75=1 -> exit 0); 64-bit loop_8 -> FALSE (the probe showed 64-bit skipped the entire v60-true branch, printing only PROBEv75 0 -> exit 1). loop_8 (1250-1294) = a while-loop over the input list: per cons, v5 := loop_9(v4,v2); case v5.tag [0: v11:=accepts_10(v0,v2); 1: v11:=(accepts_10 = False); 2: v11:=False]; if v11 continue else Result:=False; tag0 -> True. So loop_8 returns False because some iteration v11 is False on 64-bit but True on 32-bit. Divergence is in loop_9 (picks the v5.tag arm) or accepts_10 (the accepts check) -- pure union/DFA, no explicit 64-bit construct. NEXT probe: instrument loop_8 per-iteration (v5.tag + v11) -> first divergent iteration -> loop_9 vs accepts_10, then to root. Deeper suspects: regex_equal_30 / regex_compare_28 / canonical_derivative (DFA equality), or an FPC x64 codegen bug. Probe is light (52s, node).")
inl note_of_delphi_64bit_dfa_disagree_4 () = Note ("DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE", 4u32, "2026-10-28T13:23Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 ROOT CAUSE + FIX (main-blessed). loop_9 is miscompiled by FPC 3.3.1 x86_64 -O2 register-variable allocator: the loop-carried LongInt state v0 (v0:=v27; v1:=v7; Continue in `while True`) is dropped across the back-edge on the single-symbol path. Proof (scratchpad delphi64-probe-loop9.ps1): at loop_8 iter-3 the element is one symbol, identical on both targets (sym=1 v0in=1), yet L9 end v0=1 on 32-bit (->Rejected, correct) vs 0 on 64-bit (->Accepted, wrong). So loop_9 itself miscompiles; NOT an upstream input bug. Param-copy-to-locals does NOT fix it (tested loop_9w). -O2 pass-disable sweep: -OoNOREGVAR and -OoNOPEEPHOLE both fix; all others still bug. Real main.pas exit matrix (0=correct): 32-O2=0; 64-O2=1 BUG; 64-O2-OoNOREGVAR=0; 64-O2-OoNOPEEPHOLE=0; 64-O1=0.\nFIX (blessed by main): CodegenPascal prelude emits {$IFDEF CPUX86_64}{$OPTIMIZATION NOREGVAR}{$ENDIF} right after {$mode delphi}{$H+} (spiral_compiler.fs ~165736) so every generated .pas is correct at -O2/x86_64 in-source, no per-consumer flag. In-source directive PROVEN: 64-O2 plain -Mdelphi -Px86_64 WITH directive -> exit 0; WITHOUT -> exit 1 (negative via the bug own path); 32-O2 WITH -> exit 0 (guard keeps regvar on 32-bit). Anchored-apply scratchpad/apply-delphi64-noregvar.ps1 dry-run clean vs live core; ships on the next install with RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG.\nPOST-INSTALL (main split, both AFTER the install only -- before it a 64-bit row DISAGREEs): compiler adds -Px86_64 only to compiler-matrix test.ps1:754; fiscal adds -Px86_64 to Cells targets.ex:321/targets_test.exs/README:223; -OoNOREGVAR stays in fiscal own bench builds until install. COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE (20:00-21:00) moves test.ps1 -- coordinate anchors if the edit lands before 20:00. Regression sample (one per bug) authored at the install slot (needs a compiler run); plan + faithful standalone repro (delphi64-repro/repro.pas) in scratchpad. Per no-public-project-interaction: no upstream FPC report.")
inl note_of_delphi_64bit_dfa_disagree_5 () = Note ("DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE", 5u32, "2026-10-28T22:48Z", "main", "10-28 18:5x main: the compiler lane's unlanded apply scripts for the NOREGVAR prelude (and RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG) lived in its scratchpad and were lost in the profile wipe; rebuild them from this item's notes. Also: scoop now installs FreePascal 3.2.2, while the miscompile was found on trunk 3.3.1, which is gone; re-check the 32/64 repro on whichever FPC the matrix uses before landing.")
inl note_of_delphi_64bit_dfa_disagree_6 () = Note ("DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE", 6u32, "2026-10-29T01:31Z", "main", "CORRECTION to note 5: FPC is trunk again (3.3.1-21298-g814ece0205, the same bytes as before the wipe; see PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY), so Delphi rows run on the compiler this item was found on. The lost apply scripts are still lost.")
inl note_of_delphi_64bit_dfa_disagree_7 () = Note ("DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE", 7u32, "2026-10-29T06:03Z", "main", "10-29 02:1x main: lost apply script REBUILT and the fix RE-PROVEN on tonight's trunk (FPC 3.3.1-21298-g814ece0205, ppcrossx64 for 64-bit). (1) apps/compiler/packs/apply-delphi64-noregvar.ps1 (untracked, like the other appliers; -Core <file> [-Apply]). One exact anchor in CodegenPascal's prelude (spiral_compiler.fs:165736, a shared section, so it covers both cores) adds .AppendLine(\"{$IFDEF CPUX86_64}{$OPTIMIZATION NOREGVAR}{$ENDIF}\") after {$mode delphi}{$H+}. Dry run on the live core: 1/1 anchor matches exactly once; applied to a scratch copy, the diff is exactly that one line, and pair-drift -Source <copy> -Tracked: 8 pairs, 0 touched. (2) Exit matrix on the committed inventory_closed_dfa_runtime/main.pas, PE machine checked: 32-O2 x86 exit 0; 64-O2 x64 exit 1 (the bug); 64-O2 with the in-source directive x64 exit 0; 32-O2 with the directive x86 exit 0 (the guard keeps regvar on 32-bit); 64-O2 -OoNOREGVAR flag x64 exit 0. (3) THE POST-INSTALL 64-BIT REVIEW, PRE-RUN BUILD-FREE: the committed samples/**/main.pas are today's residuals, and the patched compiler emits exactly them plus the one directive line, so the sweep inserted the line textually. All 413 tracked Delphi residuals, each built as x86 plain, x64 plain and x64 + directive (matrix flags -O2 -Mdelphi, run from the sample dir, exit code + stdout sha256), 205 s at 3 workers: 0 build-fail, 0 timeout; x64 plain disagrees with x86 on exactly 1 sample (this item's: exit 1 vs 0, same empty stdout), so the regvar miscompile hits only this loop shape; x64 + NOREGVAR disagrees with x86 on 0 of 413. So once the prelude is installed, -Px86_64 can be switched on with 0 expected Delphi disagreements. Regression check: this sample's own Delphi row under -Px86_64 is the bug's own path (exit 1 without the line, 0 with it), so no new sample is needed (one per bug, and this one exists). Left for an install slot (heavy, compiler-owned oracle): apply, build both modes, -Suite all -Native review (only Delphi residual text should move, every Delphi row by the one line), -Accept, then -Px86_64 in test.ps1's Delphi native step (moves with COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE; re-anchor if the move lands first) and in Cells targets.ex:321, its test and README:223.")
inl note_of_delphi_64bit_dfa_disagree_8 () = Note ("DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE", 8u32, "2026-10-29T16:24Z", "main", "10-29 12:2x main: the NOREGVAR prelude is INSTALLED (pack 6b; see CI-OFFLOAD for the install note). The installed compiler emits {$IFDEF CPUX86_64}{$OPTIMIZATION NOREGVAR}{$ENDIF} in every .pas (the closure_pair output is checked). The post-install split from n5 can run now: compiler adds -Px86_64 to the matrix test.ps1 Delphi rows, and fiscal adds it to Cells targets.ex:321, targets_test.exs and README:223. The regression sample (one per bug) gets authored at the next compiler run.")
inl note_of_delphi_64bit_dfa_disagree_9 () = Note ("DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE", 9u32, "2026-10-29T17:32Z", "compiler", "10-29 13:4x compiler: matrix side DONE. apps/compiler/scripts/test.ps1:754 Delphi native step now passes '-Px86_64' (after -Mdelphi). Proof before the edit: scoop fpc shim with no flag gives PE machine 0x014C, SizeOf(Pointer)=4; with -Px86_64 it gives 0x8664 and 8 (ppcrossx64 present after the wipe recovery). Bug's own path with the exact row flags (-O2 -Mdelphi -Px86_64) on the working-tree inventory_closed_dfa_runtime/main.pas (6b output, 1 directive line): exit 0, stdout ''; the same file with the directive line removed: exit 1 (the miscompile still reproduces, so the check can see it). Full confirmation = the pack 7 full review (no -Reuse, so no cached 32-bit exe is credited), which now runs every Delphi row 64-bit; expected 0 Delphi disagreements per main's 413-residual pre-run. Left: fiscal's Cells part (targets.ex:321, targets_test.exs, README:223), then Done.")
inl note_of_delphi_64bit_dfa_disagree_10 () = Note ("DELPHI-64BIT-DFA-DISAGREE", 10u32, "2026-10-30T14:29Z", "main", "10-30 10:29 main: DONE, both parts verified on the tree. (1) Matrix: the pack-7 full review (run single-flight-20261029-184709, no -Reuse, Delphi rows built with -Px86_64 since test.ps1:754 on 10-29 13:4x) has samples/mega_brzozowski_derivatives/inventory_closed_dfa_runtime [Delphi] ok / ran / exit 0 / agree (results.tsv), and the pack-7 baseline row reads exit 0. The bug's own sample now passes at 64-bit through the installed NOREGVAR prelude (pack 6b). (2) Cells (fiscal's part): targets.ex:321 builds fpc with [-Px86_64, -O2, -Mdelphi, -l-, -v0w], targets_test.exs:43 asserts exactly that, and README:223 documents it. All 3 files are unstaged for the user's commit (listed in commit-suggestion.md's apps/cells group).")
inl note_of_kit_lint_dollar_literal_1 () = Note ("KIT-LINT-DOLLAR-LITERAL", 1u32, "2026-10-28T09:30Z", "eoie", "kit lint macros reads the string literal \"$\" as the start of a $\"...\" interpolation macro: the scanner pairs the closing quote of \"$\" with the next quote. x hit it on its first run after TRAIN2 (x 05:2x): x/apps/client/defuse.spi:155 gives \"cash_quiet block $\")\" for prev = \"$\", and the same happens at defuse.spi 162, 173, 196, 204 and preview/preview.spi:24 (\"dollars arith\"). That is 6 of x's 7 remaining rows; the 7th, net.spi:80 (a one-line global \"static ...\"), is a genuine block. Fix: a $ opens a macro only outside string literals. Scan quotes left to right, track whether the position is inside a literal, then match $' and $\" openers. Regression test: a line with prev = \"$\" and a \"$\" ++# concat must yield 0 rows, and a real $\"...\" must still be flagged.")
inl note_of_kit_lint_dollar_literal_2 () = Note ("KIT-LINT-DOLLAR-LITERAL", 2u32, "2026-10-28T10:54Z", "eoie", "06:5x kit: fixed in the live kit tree (scratchpad clean\\lint-dollar\\dollar-literal.do). A new inside_literal (prefix) counts unescaped double quotes before a candidate opener (escaped quotes and backslashes are stripped first); an odd count means the dollar sign sits inside a string literal, and line_macros skips past it. New test a_dollar_sign_inside_a_string_literal_opens_no_macro covers x's prev = \"$\" case, a dollar before a concat, a real macro after a dollar literal (still counted), and escaped quotes. The kit_lint_model notebook passes (job-1793184688660). Ships in TRAIN3.")
inl note_of_kit_lint_dollar_literal_3 () = Note ("KIT-LINT-DOLLAR-LITERAL", 3u32, "2026-10-29T15:36Z", "kit", "10-29 11:3x kit: INSTALLED and verified live. TRAIN3 published 11:35, kit.exe CD2F0D1A (was A5B55974); closeout script C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\train3-closeout.ps1, 7/7 pass. The same script was run on A5B55974 first as a control, and this check FAILED there. Probe: a .spi whose string literal holds $'5': 'kit lint macros' says 'macros 1' on the old binary (the bug) and 'macros 0  violations 0' on CD2F0D1A. Done.")
inl note_of_lib_process_counter_1 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER", 1u32, "2026-10-28T09:31Z", "x", "2026-10-28 05:31 x: kit ruled net.spi:80 a correct 'block' hit (KIT-LINT-DOLLAR-LITERAL handles the other 6 rows). The site is x's paid-request counter: a global 'static XC_REQUESTS: AtomicU32', fetch_add in request_counted (called by both request paths) and load in requests_made (exported as xc_requests for the cost line). lib has no counter primitive and uses the same pattern itself (common.spi:135 SPIRAL_NATIVE_RNG: AtomicU64, trace.spi:242 LOGGED: AtomicUsize), so the lint will flag lib too. Proposal: one lib primitive for named process-wide counters (e.g. counter_add name n and counter_value name, one host call each, Rust over a single static map or per-call-site statics generated by the backend), used by x's net.spi and by lib's own two sites. Design first (backend arms: Rust, Gleam via an ETS or atomics counter, F#), then the lib land, then x.")
inl note_of_lib_process_counter_2 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER", 2u32, "2026-10-28T10:34Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:34 x: DESIGN (read-only pass, before any draft). Census of the three statics: (1) x net.spi XC_REQUESTS: a true counter, low frequency (one add per paid HTTP request, one read for the cost line). (2) lib common.spi:135 SPIRAL_NATIVE_RNG: NOT a counter but a u64 state cell with load/store on every random draw, a hot path. (3) lib trace.spi:242 LOGGED: lives inside a host-function block (the wasm32 NEAR logger), a different lint class, and is LIB-MACRO-HEAL's domain, not this item's. Decision: the primitive serves (1), and (2) stays as a lib primitive (it IS the primitive layer; a map lookup per draw would cost every simulation; the lib lint baseline should accept it with that reason). API: process_counter_add (name : string) (n : u64) : u64 (returns the new total) and process_counter_value (name : string) : u64, one host call each. Rust arm: one lib static, OnceLock<Mutex<HashMap<String, u64>>> (std only; contention irrelevant at x's rate). Gleam/BEAM arm: persistent_term keyed by name holding an Erlang counters ref (counters:new(1, [atomics]); add/get), created on first use under a guard. F# arm: a static ConcurrentDictionary<string, int64> with AddOrUpdate. Tests (rust, gleam): two names stay independent; adds accumulate; an unknown name reads 0. x then replaces request_counter, request_counted and requests_made with the primitive under the name \"xc_requests\", and its global goes. Sizing: lib draft 60 min, gate on a node mirror with a private Cells port, land in a lib window, then the x change rides the next verify. Load light.")
inl note_of_lib_process_counter_3 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER", 3u32, "2026-10-28T11:53Z", "x", "2026-10-28 07:53 x: DRAFTED (Rust-first by design note 2: the BEAM already has an actor counter, otp_actor counter_step, so the Gleam arm fails with a message naming it instead of adding persistent_term host logic). lib common.spi gains process_counter_add (name : string) (n : u64) : u64, one run_target primitive: its Rust arm calls one host function declared once in a triple-quoted global (a Mutex<Option<HashMap<String, u64>>> behind spiral_process_counter_add); every other backend fails with 'Rust only; on the BEAM use an otp_actor counter'. process_counter_value name = process_counter_add name 0. Rust test process_counters_add_up_per_name: two names stay independent, adds accumulate, an unknown name reads 0. Insertion text: scratchpad process-counter\\insert.spi, inserted before /// ### random' (anchor unique). Gate: node job-1793188354077 (common.spi with the insert on mirror xcounter, installed compiler, private Cells port 13980). The x side is ready as process-counter\\x-net.do: net.spi's request_counter global plus its two helpers become two one-line calls (common::process_counter_add/value 'xc_requests'), proven on a scratch copy. Order: lib land in the 14:00-16:00 window through lib-guarded-do, then x-net.do, then the next x verify.")
inl note_of_lib_process_counter_4 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER", 4u32, "2026-10-28T12:19Z", "x", "2026-10-28 08:19 x: land program ready: scratchpad process-counter\\counter.lib.kit (root lib\\spiral; one replacement before the unique '/// ### random' anchor), built mechanically from insert.spi and proven BYTE-EXACT against the intended file on a copy (0 lines over 120 before and after). Lands through lib-guarded-do in the 14:00-16:00 window, if gate job-1793188354077 is green; then process-counter\\x-net.do into x and the next x verify.")
inl note_of_lib_process_counter_5 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER", 5u32, "2026-10-28T14:07Z", "x", "2026-10-28 10:07 x: gate attempts so far measured nothing: nb1 (whole common.spi notebook) hit its 1 h cap; nb2 (the insert alone, private daemon port 13981) never started its daemon; nb3 (process mode) hit its 30 min cap with no output under the node's current load (verify27, verify28 and other teams' jobs). The insert is unproven, not failed. Next attempt after verify27/28 finish, the insert alone in process mode with a 2 h cap; the lib land waits for a green gate.")
inl note_of_lib_process_counter_6 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER", 6u32, "2026-10-29T11:44Z", "x", "2026-10-29 07:40 x: REBUILT after the 10-28 TEMP wipe (the scratch process-counter drafts were lost), in a committable place: x/lib-programs/process-counter.kit (root lib/spiral, one patch hunk at common.spi line 166, before '/// ### random''). Recovered byte-exact from the node gate mirror xcounter, whose common.spi = live + this insert + a gate-only timeout directive. One change from n3: the non-Rust failwith message is shortened to 'process_counter_add: Rust only; on the BEAM use an otp_actor counter', because the recovered line was 128 chars (n4's '0 over 120' was wrong). Proven on a scratch copy: hash equals the target C573D2C4F944976E, 0 lines over 120; live common.spi A1317C0B2E3A. x side: x/lib-programs/process-counter.x-net.kit (net.spi: the request_counter global + 2 helpers become 2 one-line calls on 'xc_requests'), proven on a copy (live 48466BA966E8). GATE HOLE found and fixed: the first gate pair (jobs 1793273825295 pos / 1793273996293 neg) ran ZERO tests (the fresh mirror's apps/cells lacked mix deps, kino), yet lib_gate.ps1 printed 'type errors: 0' and exited 0 for both. lib_gate.ps1 now exits 2 with 'no spiral-tests total, so nothing ran'. Gate2 runs mix deps.get first: POS job-1793274289844, NEG job-1793274362909 (asserts 6, must fail; it also runs the pre-deps gate as the control of the fix). Land via lib-guarded-do when POS passes 1/1 and NEG fails 1; then x-net, then the next x verify.")
inl note_of_lib_process_counter_7 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER", 7u32, "2026-10-29T12:09Z", "x", "2026-10-29 08:12 x: GATED GREEN after a real fix. Gate2 (jobs 1793274289844/1793274362909, deps fetched) failed BOTH ways with rustc E0428: the triple-quoted Rust global inside the run_target arm was emitted once per call site (5 copies), because its closing triple quote sat at the global's column. Aligning the closing quote with the content (the crypto.spi/base.spi form) emits it once: POS job-1793274765836 passed 1 of 1 (filtered 6), NEG job-1793275238055 failed on the assertion itself ('actual (5, 1, 0), expected (6, 1, 0)'). The lib_gate fix is proven through its own failing path: the pre-deps control exits 2, 'nothing ran'. Reported to the compiler lane. Program updated: x/lib-programs/process-counter.kit, target BD2F7F52AAFF5F54 (base A1317C0B2E3A), proven on a copy, 0 lines over 120; undo copy C:\\home\\data\\undo\\lib-process-counter. LAND: by hand, one guarded try (x/lib-programs/land-process-counter.ps1, which also applies process-counter.x-net.kit after the hash check), after kit's TRAIN3 'window closed'. An auto-retry job was started and stopped before it landed anything (train-guard-gap).")
inl note_of_lib_process_counter_8 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER", 8u32, "2026-10-29T12:20Z", "x", "2026-10-29 08:3x x: lander generalized (one single-shot tool for every lib program; it keeps an undo copy and restores by hash if the landed hash isn't the target). Command after TRAIN3: pwsh -File x/lib-programs/land-lib-program.ps1 -Program x/lib-programs/process-counter.kit -File common.spi -Base A1317C0B2E3A5C8F -Target BD2F7F52AAFF5F54 -FollowProgram x/lib-programs/process-counter.x-net.kit -FollowFile x/apps/client/net/net.spi -FollowBase 48466BA966E8151E")
inl note_of_lib_process_counter_9 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER", 9u32, "2026-10-29T15:40Z", "x", "2026-10-29 11:4x x: ORDER fix before landing. The x half (process-counter.x-net.kit) rewrites apps/client/net/net.spi, and net.rs is one of verify29's outputs (generated from TODAY's net.spi). If the x half applied before verify29's three-way land, that land would install a net.rs built from the old source beside the new net.spi (the X-WIP-VERIFY n14 'source changed after the sync' case). So: (1) the lib half (common.spi) lands alone in main's order, without -FollowProgram; (2) after verify29's outputs land, apply process-counter.x-net.kit to net.spi; (3) the next x verify regenerates net.rs from it and proves xc_requests still counts (e2e prints 'N requests to the stub'). Checked: x has 0 of 224 manifest files changed since verify29's 07:28 sync, so its land will be conflict-free.")
inl note_of_lib_process_counter_10 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER", 10u32, "2026-10-29T16:06Z", "x", "2026-10-29 12:0x x: LIB HALF LANDED 12:02:52 by hand, one guarded try (x/lib-programs/land-lib-program.ps1, kit bin CD2F0D1A): common.spi A1317C0B2E3A5C8F -> BD2F7F52AAFF5F54, exactly the gated target (POS job-1793274765836 1/1, NEG job-1793275238055 on the assertion). Undo copy: C:\\home\\data\\undo\\process-counter. The guard used all of today's rules on live processes: a TEMP-copy reader skipped, the pack6b-review window and its process tree exempted (git-a6: native phase, no lib reads), and no other reader. Remaining: (2) process-counter.x-net.kit onto net.spi AFTER verify29's three-way land (n9 ordering), (3) the next x verify regenerates net.rs and proves xc_requests still counts. Main's document_test (the lib round-trip gate) runs after kit's merged-lib.")
inl note_of_lib_process_counter_11 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER", 11u32, "2026-10-30T00:20Z", "x", "2026-10-29 20:20 x: PATH MAP (x/lib-programs drained 19:5x by hash-verified move; the folder no longer exists). Old x/lib-programs/<name> resolves as follows. LANDED/APPLIED, archived in C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\landed-programs\\: process-counter.kit, process-counter.x-net.kit, otp-actor-named.kit, fs-heal-4.kit, fs-heal-4b-other-targets.kit, fs-heal-4c-io-error-text.kit, lib-gate-copy-daemon.kit, physics-scalar-force.kit, x-fields-cells.kit. UNPROVEN DRAFTS, in C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\sim-programs\\: sim-in-spiral-s1s2.kit, sim-in-spiral-s3a.kit, sim-in-spiral-s3b.kit. TOOL: land-lib-program.ps1 now lives in repo i574n.github at scripts/land-lib-program.ps1 (untracked, for the user's commit), next to the lib-guarded-do.ps1 it calls; positive control after the move: already-at-target exit 0, no write.")
inl note_of_lib_process_counter_12 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER", 12u32, "2026-10-30T10:28Z", "x", "2026-10-30 06:2x x groom: CLOSED with evidence. verify33 (job-1793328097444, pack 7 core 9C899B30495F7DC5, -Accept, LandRepos x,spiral) finished exit 0: client build, sim build, e2e accept and sim accept all exit 0; 66 notebooks passed.")
inl note_of_xsd_all_groups_1 () = Note ("XSD-ALL-GROUPS", 1u32, "2026-10-28T10:38Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 06:38 fiscal: found by set-proof setd (job-1793178481148, BOM-free corpus): generated_gr_mydata_2_0_2 reads 1 of 2 real documents back; request-doc-with-delivery-lifecycle loses /InvoicesDoc/invoice/deliveryLifecycle/deliveryEvents/transportDetails/location/longitude. LocationType is an xs:all (longitude, latitude); lib xsd has no xs:all particle (grep of xsd*.spi: 0). Corpus users: gr/mydata TransportTypes, pt/saft saftpt1.04_01. Plan: model all as unordered required children (min/max 0..1 each); reader claims each child by name in any order; writer emits declaration order; test with a reordered document.")
inl note_of_xsd_all_groups_2 () = Note ("XSD-ALL-GROUPS", 2u32, "2026-10-28T11:06Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 07:05 fiscal: DRAFT ready, lands in the 14:00-16:00 window (after kit's fix-forward): fiscal/lib-programs/xsd-all-groups.kit: particle_of reads xs:all as a Sequence of its elements (readers claim children by name via first_child/children_named, so any document order reads; writers emit declaration order, valid for xs:all) + test all_groups_keep_their_elements. Before the fix, xs:all content was DROPPED entirely (particle_of returned None). Type-checks (xsd, probe, xsd_emit, xsd_resolve, json_schema, fiscal generator + corpus_checks); applies fresh = draft. Node: xsd.spi gate HOLDS 9/9 incl the new test (job-1793185188686). Greece + Portugal regeneration proof running on mirror setall with the overlay.")
inl note_of_xsd_all_groups_3 () = Note ("XSD-ALL-GROUPS", 3u32, "2026-10-28T15:07Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 11:07 fiscal: setall (job-1793186085227) VOID, not red: 13 of 21 generator cells timed out at the 1,400 s package-line budget on a node at 100% (12 jobs), so the GR/PT units were never built. Replaced by a narrow proof on the same mirror (xsd.spi overlay 147F773B, budget raised to 5,400,000 in the stage copy only): generator cells generates_mydata_2_0_2 (job-1793199986948) and generates_saft_pt_1_04_01 (job-1793200015262), each --after-ok its unit gate (job-1793200006172, job-1793200029875). Land only on all four green.")
inl note_of_xsd_all_groups_4 () = Note ("XSD-ALL-GROUPS", 4u32, "2026-10-29T11:47Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 07:47 fiscal: generator cells HOLD (gen-mydata job-1793199986948, gen-saft job-1793200015262); both unit gates were VOID: my proof script hashed generated_mydata_2_0_2 / generated_saft_pt_1_04_01, but the units are named generated_gr_mydata_2_0_2 / generated_pt_saft_1_04_01. Rerun with the real names: fiscal-all-unit2-gr job-1793273742074, fiscal-all-unit2-pt job-1793273745610 (setall mirror, A4E48020). Land on both green.")
inl note_of_xsd_all_groups_5 () = Note ("XSD-ALL-GROUPS", 5u32, "2026-10-29T12:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 08:20 fiscal: land NOT done yet: lib-guarded-do refused at 07:52 and 08:00 (another lane's formatter candidate gate, then the compiler pack6 review's heavy-build window). Waiting for kit's 'TRAIN3 published' (lib-guarded-do runs the kit tree's kit.exe, whose swap a running copy would block). Then xsd-all-groups.kit: xsd 1D437DBF -> 147F773B.")
inl note_of_xsd_all_groups_6 () = Note ("XSD-ALL-GROUPS", 6u32, "2026-10-29T16:01Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 12:01 fiscal: LANDED 11:37:52: xsd-all-groups.kit, xsd 1D437DBF -> 147F773B, via lib-guarded-do (git-a6's pack6b window exempted at its native phase; no live reader), hash equal to the node-proven copy; post-land mirror post1029 reproduces the proof mirrors' hashes exactly; main's document_test (spi -> livemd -> spi) GREEN over it; full live-tree gates queued as the fiscal-post-* chain. Undo = git HEAD.")
inl note_of_xsd_all_groups_7 () = Note ("XSD-ALL-GROUPS", 7u32, "2026-10-30T16:15Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 12:15 fiscal: PROGRESS 60 -> 90 by milestone: landed 10-29 11:37 (xsd 147F773B, still the live hash), lib tests and document_test green. The last milestone is the real-document proof: the Greek myDATA document that lost location/longitude without xs:all must round-trip. Queued as fiscal-p7-e3lv-set-grptcr (job-1793376912165). Closes on that HOLDS.")
inl note_of_xsd_all_groups_8 () = Note ("XSD-ALL-GROUPS", 8u32, "2026-10-31T03:02Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 23:00 fiscal: CLOSED, last milestone met: the real-document proof HOLDS (fiscal-p7-e3lv-set-grptcr (node job-1793376912165, mirror e3lv = live lib + edition-3 draft + box corpus, pack 7 F2F6F4CB5A18; generator 22/22, 0 memo hits)). generated_gr_mydata_2_0_2 1/1; the Greek myDATA document whose xs:all LocationType lost longitude now round-trips unchanged through the generated reader/writer (xsd 147F773B, landed 10-29). 90 -> 100, Done.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_1 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 1u32, "2026-10-28T11:14Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 07:14 fiscal: DESIGN (no code). Census of corpus schemas: required ds:Signature refs in BR NF-e 38, CT-e 25, MDF-e 4, CL DTE 2, CR v4.4 9 (all 9 documents). Today a reader needs the signature, so an unsigned document (what fiscal's own writer produces, and the 7 hacienda-cr cases) cannot be read. Plan, GADT-first: a stage index on documents whose type references a required Signature: signature_slot s = Pending : signature_slot unsigned | Present : xml_node -> signature_slot signed; the record field becomes signature_slot s; T_of_xml_unsigned accepts the element absent, T_of_xml (signed) requires it; T_to_xml writes Present only; examples build T unsigned. Rejected: minOccurs 0 (loses the signed-carries-a-signature guarantee in the type).")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_2 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 2u32, "2026-10-28T12:43Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 08:43 fiscal: DESIGN PROVEN on A4E48020 (node --check): nominal unsigned/signed; union stage s = Unsigned/Signed :: string -> stage <tag> (versioned witness idiom); type x_doc s = { ..; signature : signature_slot s }; reader forall s. (witness : stage s); writer takes x_doc signed, matches only Present. Controls fail as wanted: writer on unsigned doc and Present in an unsigned doc are unification errors. Refined: (1) family config lists staged elements (xmldsig Signature now; later PAC stamps/protocols), not a hard-coded ds:Signature; (2) the index propagates to every type that transitively holds a staged type (TEnviNFe holds TNFe).")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_3 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 3u32, "2026-10-28T14:39Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 10:39 fiscal: MILESTONES (pre-registered): M1 staged_type_names (which types carry the stage index), M2 type emission with the index, M3 readers take the stage witness, M4 writer takes signed only, M5 examples unsigned, M6 BR NF-e/CT-e/MDF-e + CR regen: CR's 7 unsigned hacienda cases read. Facts: ref elements keep their QName text (ds:Signature) since tag names are not qualified, so staged = local names of ref-only elements, per family.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_4 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 4u32, "2026-10-28T14:39Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 10:39 fiscal: M1 DRAFTED, held as lib-programs/xsd-staged-type-names.kit (xsd_emit, +144, 2 hunks: after has_name + 1 test at the end; independent of the other xsd_emit programs; draft %TEMP%\\tc-ss-draft). Direct = content REQUIRES a staged ref (min >= 1 on the path; a choice only if every branch requires it); closure = any field (optional/repeated too), inline type or base that mentions a staged type, to a fixpoint. Test covers direct, container, inline root, optional (not staged), plain. Proof job-1793198427541 fiscal-fix-ss (after fix-ac).")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_5 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 5u32, "2026-10-28T14:47Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 10:47 fiscal: M2 SHAPE PROVEN (node --check, A4E48020): ONE shared slot per unit, union staged s a = Pending :: forall a. string -> staged unsigned a | Present :: forall a. a -> staged signed a; staged types take s (type x_doc s = { ..; signature : staged s x_signature }), containers pass it (x_batch s = { docs : list (x_doc s) }); reader helper staged_of forall s a. (witness : stage s) (read : () -> a); writer present (x : staged signed a). Controls fail as wanted: signed writer on an unsigned doc, and an unsigned batch holding a signed doc.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_6 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 6u32, "2026-10-29T11:47Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 07:47 fiscal: M1 proof fix-ss (job-1793198427541) was RED: 6 ETY-ValueRestriction at grow_staged (xsd_emit:688): 'listm.length next = listm.length names' leaves the generic length result type unsolved (lib annotates it the same way, listm':840). Fixed in lib-programs/xsd-staged-type-names.kit: (listm.length next : i32). fix-ss2 (job-1793273962189, live xsd_emit + M1 = 20136A82, mirror header raised): the test passes, 0 type errors in the whole file. M1 lands after the emit gate verdict; M2 drafting next.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_7 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 7u32, "2026-10-29T12:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 08:20 fiscal: M1 lands with XSD-ALL-GROUPS after TRAIN3 (xsd_emit 841B97D2 -> 20136A82). M2 design: emit_record has no staged list, so M2 either threads the staged names through emit_named_type/emit_record or post-processes the emitted items; either way it stays unwired from generated_units until M3/M4, so generated units keep compiling between milestones.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_8 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 8u32, "2026-10-29T13:12Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 09:12 fiscal: M2 DRAFTED, held as fiscal/lib-programs/xsd-staged-type-emission.kit (stacks on indirection + M1: xsd_emit E88E120D -> F87980B0; rehearsed): staging_prelude (nominal unsigned/signed + union staged s a = Pending :: forall a. string -> staged unsigned a | Present :: forall a. a -> staged signed a, the n5 shape), staged_items post-pass over emitted items: a staged type's header becomes 'type x s =', fields of staged types pass s (x, option (x), list (x)), the required signature field becomes 'staged s string' (ref-only elements emit string). Not wired into generated_units until M3/M4, so generated units keep compiling. Test staged_types_take_the_stage_and_pass_it_on (M1's schema). Proof on fresh mirror m2x1029: jobs 1793279563913 (M2 test) and 1793279583021 (M1 test on the same file). Known gap for M3: a choice union holding a staged type is not staged yet.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_9 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 9u32, "2026-10-29T13:44Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 09:44 fiscal: M2 PROVEN on fresh mirror m2x1029 (live xsd_emit + indirection + M1 + M2 = F87980B0, + mirror header = 3000D720, A4E48020): staged_types_take_the_stage_and_pass_it_on passes, M1's test still passes, 0 type errors in the whole file (fiscal-m2-* jobs). Lands after the indirection and M1 (post-TRAIN3). Next: M3, readers take the stage witness.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_10 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 10u32, "2026-10-29T13:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 09:54 fiscal: M3 SHAPE PROVEN by a probe in the generated units' exact shape (fiscal-m3-probe job-1793281953956, mirror m2x1029, A4E48020, rust, 0 type errors): union stage s = Unsigned :: stage unsigned | Signed :: stage signed; inl staged_of forall s a. (witness : stage s) (read : () -> a) : staged s a matches the witness (Pending / Present (read ())); readers in the generated 'let rec ... and let ...' group take 'forall s. (witness : stage s)' and pass the witness to the readers of the staged types they hold (x_batch -> x_doc); a function over 'x_doc signed' matches only Present and the test on the unsigned read matches only Pending, with no impossible arms needed. So readers of unsigned documents never touch the signature, and signed readers must produce one. M3 build plan: a reading instance that carries the staged names (record_texts adds the witness to staged types' _of_children/_of_xml and returns 'ty s'; element_part passes the witness to staged children and wraps the signature in staged_of), the other reading instances delegate, and it stays unwired until M4/M5.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_11 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 11u32, "2026-10-29T14:11Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 10:11 fiscal: M3 DRAFTED, held as fiscal/lib-programs/xsd-staged-readers.kit (stacks on M2: xsd_emit F87980B0 -> 7A9F19FF; rehearsed): staging_prelude gains union stage s (Unsigned/Signed) and staged_of; a staged_reading family (StagedReading (staged, signatures)) whose record_texts gives staged types 'forall s. (witness : stage s)' readers returning 'ty s', whose element_part wraps a required signature in staged_of witness and passes the witness to staged children, and whose other 12 prototypes delegate to Reading; module_parts_with / walk_texts_with take the reading family (module_parts = module_parts_with Reading, so every generated unit is unchanged). Test staged_readers_take_the_witness_and_stage_the_signature. Mirror m2x1029: names_follow_spiral_conventions passes and the whole file has 0 type errors (job-1793283015882); the M3 and M2 tests are running.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_12 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 12u32, "2026-10-29T14:29Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 10:29 fiscal: M4 DRAFTED, held as fiscal/lib-programs/xsd-staged-writers.kit (stacks on M3: xsd_emit 7A9F19FF -> E96F45C2; rehearsed): prelude gains signed_value (matches Present only); a staged_writing family whose record_texts gives staged types writers over 'ty signed' (_children/_attributes/_to_xml), whose element_part writes the required signature from (signed_value x.signature), and whose witness prototypes return none for staged owners (their _field witness helpers would name ty without its stage), so staged types take the concat path; the other prototypes delegate to Writing. module_parts_by (walk) now carries the body; module_parts_with reader and module_parts_staged staged signatures (StagedReading + StagedWriting) both go through it, and module_parts = module_parts_with Reading leaves every generated unit unchanged. Test staged_writers_take_signed_documents_only. Proof on mirror m4x1029 (E96F45C2 + header = 2B9A8753), chained after fiscal-set-ublj to keep fiscal at <= 2 node jobs (x's verify29): M4 test, M3 test, complements writer test. Still open for M5/M6: examples build unsigned values, wiring into generated_units per family (BR NF-e/CT-e/MDF-e + CR), and choice unions holding staged types.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_13 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 13u32, "2026-10-29T14:39Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 10:39 fiscal: re SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS (host --check exits 0 on parse errors): n2 and n5's design proofs were --check only, so they did not prove the syntax. Both shapes are now re-proven by a real build: fiscal-m3-probe (job-1793281953956) is a Cells rust run of a superset (nominal unsigned/signed, union staged s a, union stage s, staged_of, staged record types, staged readers), 1/1 passed, 0 type errors. No fiscal land today rests on --check: every gate is a Cells build + run (fix-proof-d / set-proof-c).")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_14 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 14u32, "2026-10-29T14:41Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 10:41 fiscal: M3 PROVEN on mirror m2x1029 (live + indirection + M1 + M2 + M3 = 7A9F19FF, + mirror header = 383FC5A9, A4E48020, Cells rust): staged_readers_take_the_witness_and_stage_the_signature passes, M2's staged_types_take_the_stage_and_pass_it_on still passes, names_follow_spiral_conventions passes, 0 type errors in the whole file (fiscal-m3-* jobs). M3 lands after M2.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_15 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 15u32, "2026-10-29T14:45Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 10:45 fiscal: M5 DRAFTED, held as fiscal/lib-programs/xsd-staged-examples.kit (stacks on M4: xsd_emit E96F45C2 -> E76BD66A; rehearsed): a staged_exampling family whose record_texts gives staged types '_example (k : i32) : ty unsigned', whose element_part sets the required signature to Pending (a staged child's own example is already unsigned, so containers need nothing more), and whose other prototypes delegate to Exampling; staged_walk_texts now uses StagedReading + StagedWriting + StagedExampling. Test staged_examples_are_unsigned. Proof after M4's chain (fiscal <= 2 node jobs until verify29 reports). Remaining for M6: wire module_parts_staged into generated_units for families with required signatures (BR NF-e/CT-e/MDF-e, CL DTE, CR v4.4), give unit document checks a stage (read unsigned unless the document carries a signature), and stage choice unions that hold staged types.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_16 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 16u32, "2026-10-29T15:09Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 11:09 fiscal: DECISION, M4 REVISED before landing (n3 pre-registered 'M4 writer takes signed only'). Why: an XML-DSig enveloped signature is computed over the document serialized WITHOUT its Signature element, so fiscal must write unsigned documents in order to sign them; a signed-only writer would also break every unit's _writes_its_example check now that examples are unsigned (M5). The guarantee that matters is on the read side and M3 gives it: a Signed read requires the signature, an Unsigned read never touches it. New M4 (fiscal/lib-programs/xsd-staged-writers.kit, M3 7A9F19FF -> 6DA69F57): staged writers are 'forall s.' over 'ty s', and the signature is written through prelude staged_list (Pending -> no element, Present v -> the element); signed_value stays for callers that hold a signed document. Test renamed staged_writers_write_any_stage_and_only_a_present_signature. M5 rebased onto it (6DA69F57 -> 21E27223; its test hunk was re-anchored by hand after the M4 test changed). The old queued M4 jobs were stopped before running. New proof chain on mirror m4x1029 (21E27223 + header = CF70840D), one job at a time after fiscal-set-ublj: M5, M4, M3 and the complements writer test (jobs 1793286618627, 1793286620722, 1793286624299, 1793286627888).")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_17 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 17u32, "2026-10-29T15:13Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 11:13 fiscal: M6 lib half DRAFTED, held as fiscal/lib-programs/xsd-staged-units.kit (stacks on M5: xsd_emit 21E27223 -> F49F611A; rehearsed): units_of_parts (any_content, prelude, parts) is the shared tail; generated_units_with = units_of_parts any_content '' (module_parts schema), so existing units are byte-identical; new generated_units_signed signatures any_content schema stages a schema with required signatures (module_parts_staged + the M2 type pass + staging_prelude) and falls back to generated_units_with when nothing is staged. Test a_family_with_required_signatures_generates_staged_units (staged prelude/types/readers present; an unsigned schema's unit equal to generated_units). Mirror m4x1029 now holds M6 (F49F611A + header = B3FF1D04); the queued M5/M4/M3/complements chain runs on it, then fiscal-m6-staged_units (job-1793286904819). M6 generator half (fiscal): signatures_of family for BR NF-e/CT-e/MDF-e, CL DTE, CR v4.4, generated_units_signed in write_family_modules, and document checks reading signed real documents with the Signed witness (unsigned CR cases with Unsigned).")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_18 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 18u32, "2026-10-29T15:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 11:21 fiscal: M6 generator half DRAFTED, held as fiscal/lib-programs/generator-signed-families.kit (stacks on generator-complements: fiscal/src/generator.spi FF920D9F -> 7CCC1E85; rehearsed): signatures_of gives ['Signature'] to BR NF-e/CT-e/MDF-e/GTVe, CL and CR families (an explicit prefix list, so no other schema's ref named Signature gets staged); write_family_modules generates every family through generated_units_signed (unstaged schemas are byte-identical), and when the root type is staged, the real-document checks read with the Signed witness and the bench re-reads its written example with Unsigned (examples are unsigned since M5); document_round_trip, document_checks and family_bench take the witness text ('' for unstaged roots, so their output is unchanged). Apply order in the fiscal tree: generator-complements.kit, then generator-signed-families.kit, after the lib chain M1..M6 has landed. Proof then: set-proof on the BR/CL/CR units.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_19 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 19u32, "2026-10-29T16:01Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 12:01 fiscal: M1, M2 and M3 LANDED 11:38:56-11:58:43 (xsd_emit CF7BBF4D -> E88E120D -> F87980B0 -> 7A9F19FF), via lib-guarded-do (git-a6's pack6b window exempted at its native phase; no live reader), hash equal to the node-proven copy; post-land mirror post1029 reproduces the proof mirrors' hashes exactly; main's document_test (spi -> livemd -> spi) GREEN over it; full live-tree gates queued as the fiscal-post-* chain. Undo = git HEAD. M4-M6 lib programs wait on the fiscal-m45/m6 chain; nothing is wired into generated_units until M6 + the generator half land, so every generated unit is unchanged.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_20 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 20u32, "2026-10-29T16:04Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 12:04 fiscal: M4 (revised) and M5 PROVEN on mirror m4x1029 holding the M6 file (F49F611A + header = B3FF1D04, A4E48020): staged_examples_are_unsigned, staged_writers_write_any_stage_and_only_a_present_signature, staged_readers_take_the_witness_and_stage_the_signature and complements_read_and_write_typed_cases_and_keep_unknown_elements_whole all pass; 0 type errors in the whole file (M6 code included). M6's own test fiscal-m6-staged_units is running; on its pass, lander6 lands M4 -> M5 -> M6 (xsd_emit 7A9F19FF -> 6DA69F57 -> 21E27223 -> F49F611A).")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_21 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 21u32, "2026-10-29T17:26Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 13:26 fiscal: LIVE-TREE GATE HOLDS for the whole chain: fiscal-post-xsd_emit (mirror post1029, live xsd_emit F49F611A = M1..M6 + mirror header = B3FF1D04, A4E48020): 23/23 tests, 0 type errors. The lib half of signing stages is DONE (M1-M6). Remaining: apply generator-complements.kit + generator-signed-families.kit to the fiscal tree (after the CFDI operator fix lands), then set-proof the BR/CL/CR units.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_22 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 22u32, "2026-10-29T19:28Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 15:28 fiscal: generator half APPLIED to the fiscal tree (generator-signed-families.kit, after generator-complements; tree now 84A609E1): BR NF-e/CT-e/MDF-e/GTVe, CL and CR families generate through generated_units_signed, real-document checks read with Signed, and the bench re-reads its example with Unsigned. Proof: fiscal-6b-set-signed (MDF-e 3.00 + CL Envio DTE among its units).")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_23 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 23u32, "2026-10-30T16:15Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 12:15 fiscal: PROGRESS 30 -> 80 by milestone, with weights fixed before counting: lib 40, generator 20, family proofs 40. Lib M1-M6 DONE (live-tree gate 23/23, 10-29 13:26) = 40. Generator half applied to the tree (generator-signed-families, 10-29 15:28) = 20. Proofs: CL DTE + Envio DTE and MDF-e 3.00 GREEN on the live lib with M6 (fiscal-p7-set-cl-mdfe, 10-30) = 20 of 40. Open: CR (generated_cr_factura_4_4 in fiscal-p7-e3lv-set-grptcr, job-1793376912165) and BR NF-e/CT-e (a set-proof on their units after it). [Queued 12:16: BR proof fiscal-p7-e3lv-set-brsigned (job-1793376988214-fiscal-p7-e3lv-set-brsigned), units ^generated_(nfe_v4|cte_v4_00)$ (the NF-e unit is named generated_nfe_v4, without _00; a first queue with nfe_v4_00 was stopped before it ran), after grptcr.]")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_24 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 24u32, "2026-10-31T03:04Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 23:00 fiscal: BUG FOUND + FIX DRAFTED. fiscal-p7-e3lv-set-grptcr broke generated_cr_factura_4_4: signature typed 'staged s (list ...)' but read and written as a plain list. Cause: staged_signatures (type side) stages every signature field by name, while element_part staged_reading / staged_writing stage only when is_required_once; CR 4.4 declares ds:Signature minOccurs=1 maxOccurs=5. Rule now shared by both sides, matching particle_requires_staged (line 575): a signature is staged iff occurs.min >= 1. Draft xsd_emit 4D56FBA1 (base 161D00F5 = live): new is_staged_signature; the reader wraps any required signature read (once or list) in staged_of witness; the writer keeps staged_list for exactly-once and writes listm.concat (staged_list (fun vs => listm.map (fun v => ...) vs) x.f) for a required list. Test a_required_signature_list_is_staged_as_one_slot (CR's 1..5 shape + a single-signature control). Round trip true; formatter: only the lib-wide bracket spacing the neighbouring tests also get. Proof queued (fiscal-box7-signature-list-stages, behind main's tree-write window): a CONTROL first (live code + the new test, which must fail through the bug's own path), then the whole xsd_emit module on the draft. Then: regenerate CR (and mdfe_v3_00 / cl_envio_dte_v10 in signed2) on a lib with the draft.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_25 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 25u32, "2026-10-31T03:10Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 23:10 fiscal: SCOPE of the list fix (read from today's generated units in the tree, generator = e3lv's): CR 4.4 is the only required-LIST signature (minOccurs=1 maxOccurs=5). mdfe_v3_00 has 'signature : option (x_signature_type)' (minOccurs=0, unstaged) and 'signature : staged s x_signature_type' (required once); cl_dte_v10 / cl_envio_dte_v10 have required-once staged signatures only. So their 10-29 breaks in fiscal-6b-set-signed (mdfe: unification against staged s {signed_info...}; cl: ETY-KindError at 5572) are NOT this list bug; node job fiscal-p7-e3lv-set-signed2 (after brsigned) shows whether they still break on today's lib. Separately: generated_nfe_v4's signature is 'x_signature_type' (not staged at all) although its root is a signed document; checked when signed2 and brsigned report.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_26 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 26u32, "2026-10-31T03:15Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 23:15 fiscal: FINDING, NF-e 4.00 is outside the staging. generated_nfe_v4 is written by the generator's older direct path (write_generated_module 'generated_nfe_v4' (text ++# sample_helper () ++# self_check)), not through family/signatures_of, so signatures_of's 'generated_nfe_' prefix never reaches it: x_tn_fe has 'signature : x_signature_type' (required, unstaged; leiauteNFe_v4.00.xsd:6683 ref ds:Signature), while the protocol wrapper's is option. Effect: an unsigned NF-e 4.00 (the document a signer receives) cannot be built or written by the generated code, unlike nfe_v2_00 / nfe_v3_10 / every CT-e, MDF-e, GTV-e, CL and CR family. Fix direction: move NF-e 4.00 onto the family path (its own family entry with root TNFe), then its example and documents use the Unsigned / Signed witnesses like the others; size it after the CR list fix lands, since both change the same staged readers.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_27 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 27u32, "2026-10-31T07:06Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 03:06 fiscal: LIST FIX PROVEN on the box (job-1793425243907-fiscal-box7-signature-list-stages2, compiler EEBCBFF8, Cells rust): CONTROL = live xsd_emit CDA88705 (161D00F5 + the new test + gate header) failed a_required_signature_list_is_staged_as_one_slot through the bug's own path ('the reader stages the whole list', main.rs:31432), so the test detects the defect; DRAFT 102EEE65 (4D56FBA1 + header) passed the whole module, 25/25 tests, 0 type errors. Round trip of 4D56FBA1: ok (slice-roundtrip-sweep, positive control holds). Land program fiscal-drafts/xsd-stages/xsd-stages.kit (4 hunks) rehearsed on a copy of live: 161D00F5 -> 4D56FBA1 hash-equal. Land queued (land-lib-program, undo by hash) behind kit's train5e heavy-build window, which counts as a lib reader. Then: regenerate CR 4.4 on the landed lib.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_28 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 28u32, "2026-10-31T07:49Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 03:49 fiscal: NF-e 4.00 MOVED ONTO THE FAMILY PATH, in the fiscal tree (unstaged): src/generator.spi drops the hand-built test generates_the_nfe_v4_model (generate_module + write_generated_module, unstaged signature) and adds nfe_v4_00 () : family_root (module generated_nfe_v4, nfe_v4.00.xsd, NFe/TNFe), so signatures_of's generated_nfe_ prefix now stages it like v2.00/v3.10; its unit-specific runtime checks (TCodUfIBGE round trip, CNPJ validator, TEnderEmi read/write) move into the unit through unit_checks_of (same per-module hook shape as complements_of) and run beside the family's writes_its_example / bench / keeps_raw_strings_out; new generator test generates_nfe_v4_with_a_staged_signature asserts 'type x_tn_fe s =', 'signature : staged s x_signature_type' and an unsigned example. The sample is now samples/generated_nfe_v4_example.xml (validate-example.ps1 default and family-gates.ps1 updated; no workflow names it). family_roots stays at 12, so generates_every_family_version is untouched. Proof queued as fiscal-box10-nfe4-family (scratch root boxgate10 = live lib + xsd_emit 4D56FBA1, my src): generator test, then the regenerated unit's whole test set, then XSD validation of the unsigned sample (expected: only the missing ds:Signature). Regenerated generated/generated_nfe_v4 enters the tree after the proof (drift guard).")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_29 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 29u32, "2026-10-31T09:29Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 05:29 fiscal: FAMILY PROOFS READ (node, e3lv lib, set-proof, 0 broken claims each): fiscal-p7-e3lv-set-brsigned (job-1793376988214, finished 10-30 23:53): generator 22/22, generated_cte_v4_00 2/2 (staged family path, Signed real-document reads + Unsigned example) and generated_nfe_v4 1/1 (the OLD direct path, unstaged; its move onto the family path is n28, proof fiscal-box10-nfe4-family running). fiscal-p7-e3lv-set-signed2 (job-1793403473254, finished 10-31 02:23): generated_cl_envio_dte_v10 2/2 and generated_mdfe_v3_00 2/2, so n25's 10-29 breaks are gone on today's lib. Progress per n23's weights (lib 40, generator 20, family proofs 40): proofs now CL DTE + Envio DTE, MDF-e 3.00, CT-e 4.00 = 30 of 40, open CR 4.4 (list fix, landing when the live lib has no reader) and NF-e 4.00 (n28) = 80 -> 90.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_30 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 30u32, "2026-10-31T09:37Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 05:37 fiscal: NF-e 4.00 STAGED, PROVEN (fiscal-box10-nfe4-family, job-1793432980506, box, compiler EEBCBFF8; scratch root boxgate10 = live lib + xsd_emit 4D56FBA1 + the tree's src with n28): generates_nfe_v4_with_a_staged_signature passes (0 type errors); the regenerated unit generated_nfe_v4 (4682ED118018, 1,972,656 B) passes 3/3 (writes_its_example with the bench cell, generated_nfe_model_type_checks carried over through unit_checks_of, keeps_raw_strings_out), 0 type errors; its unsigned sample (1598 B) gets exactly ONE XSD message from validate-example.ps1 against nfe_v4.00.xsd, NFe incomplete expecting infNFeSupl (optional) or Signature, i.e. only the missing signature, like MDF-e. Unit shape vs the old direct path: same 255 types, 251 validators, 1216 and-lets, 88 let-recs; unions +staged +stage -x_tn_fe_field -x_t_envi_n_fe_field -x_t_nfe_proc_field (the staged owners' field witnesses, dropped by design since M4: staged owners take the concat writer); 38,347 -> 29,395 lines. The proven unit is now in the tree's generated/generated_nfe_v4 (unstaged, for the user's commit with src/generator.spi; the drift guard then matches). Proofs per n23's weights: CL, MDF-e 3.00, CT-e 4.00, NF-e 4.00 = 35 of 40; open: CR 4.4, which waits on the xsd_emit list-fix land (live has had another lane's lib reader at every try since 03:04) and then a CR regeneration. 90 -> 95.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_31 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 31u32, "2026-10-31T10:43Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 06:43 fiscal: CR 4.4 REGENERATED on the list fix (fiscal-box13-cr-factura, job-1793440924432, boxgate10 = live lib + xsd_emit 4D56FBA1): generates_hacienda_factura_4_4 passes and the unit now stages the whole list on the READ side (staged_of witness (fun () => listm.map ... children_named ds:Signature)) and the WRITE side (listm.concat (staged_list ...)), but the unit is RED: 2 type errors at line 5468, the EXAMPLE: 'signature = [x_signature_type_example (k + 0i32)]' where the type is staged s (list ...). Third site: staged_exampling's element_part still staged only is_required_once. FIX in the draft: it now uses is_staged_signature like the reader and writer (Pending is forall a, so it fits staged unsigned (list ...)); a_required_signature_list_is_staged_as_one_slot gains 'no signature = [' in the emitted functions. Draft 4D56FBA1 -> AE0A917F (round trip ok; land program xsd-stages.kit rebuilt, 5 hunks, rehearsed on live 161D00F5 -> AE0A917F hash-equal). The 4D56FBA1 land loop was stopped before it ever landed (every try refused by live readers; live still 161D00F5), so the land is the combined AE0A917F. Proof chain queued as ONE serial job fiscal-stages3-example-cr-ubl (scratch roots under TEMP, so lib-guarded-do never counts them as live readers): control (AE0A917F with only the example rule reverted) must fail at the new assertion; whole xsd_emit module on AE0A917F; CR Factura regenerated + its unit; then generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 regenerated with the new size_drift (FISCAL-MY-MYINVOIS n25/n26) for the tree's drift guard.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_32 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 32u32, "2026-10-31T12:46Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 08:46 fiscal: CORRECTION to n30. (1) The unit I copied into the tree at 05:3x was RAW generator output; the committed generated/ is FORMATTED (build.ps1 runs format-generated.ps1 before its drift check), so CI would have flagged it. Now formatted with the repo's format-generated.ps1 and pack 8 EEBCBFF8 (verified as the committed layout's formatter: committed generated_mdfe_v1_00 is a format --check fixpoint under it): generated_nfe_v4 0D73AB909800, 38,384 lines, diff vs HEAD +143/-106 (the real staging change; n30's '38,347 -> 29,395 lines' compared raw against formatted and is void). (2) The family path also writes generated_nfe_v4_certificates (new, formatted AB41CF4A35C5, 19,429 lines), which the old direct path never had; it is now in the tree too and is being gated (fiscal-box17-nfe4-certificates, queued for a free slot). The unit-shape comparison in n30 (types, validators, and-lets, the three dropped _field witness unions) is unaffected by layout.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_33 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 33u32, "2026-10-31T13:06Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 09:06 fiscal: CR 4.4 PROVEN, the last family proof (fiscal-stages3-example-cr-ubl, job-1793444866796, box, compiler EEBCBFF8; scratch roots under TEMP): (1) CONTROL (AE0A917F with only the example rule reverted) fails a_required_signature_list_is_staged_as_one_slot at the new assertion 'the example leaves the whole signature list pending', so the test sees the third site; (2) the WHOLE xsd_emit module on AE0A917F passes 25/25, 0 type errors; (3) generates_hacienda_factura_4_4 on it, and the regenerated generated_cr_factura_4_4 passes 2/2, 0 type errors, its example now 'signature = Pending \\n unsigned example\\'. The unit is in the tree FORMATTED (format-generated.ps1, pack 8): diff vs HEAD +7/-3, exactly the three sites (reader staged_of over the list, writer listm.concat over staged_list, example Pending); its _certificates unit is byte-identical to HEAD. Family proofs per n23 = 40/40 (CL, MDF-e 3.00, CT-e 4.00, NF-e 4.00, CR 4.4). LAND armed (fiscal/lib-programs/xsd-stages.kit, live 161D00F5 -> AE0A917F, land-lib-program retrying every 2 min until no live reader; kit's heavy-build window holds it now). The item closes on that land; the other 8 CR v4.4 documents then regenerate in one batch.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_34 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 34u32, "2026-10-31T13:49Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 09:49 fiscal: generated_nfe_v4_certificates PROVEN (fiscal-box17-nfe4-certificates, job-1793454061842, box, compiler EEBCBFF8; the FORMATTED tree file AB41CF4A35C5 in scratch root g17 on boxgate10's lib): generated_nfe_v4_certificates_hold passes 1/1, 0 type errors. So both NF-e 4.00 family units in the tree (generated_nfe_v4 0D73AB909800 + its certificates companion) are proven as committed.")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_35 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 35u32, "2026-10-31T16:56Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 12:50 fiscal: NF-e 4.00 family re-proven on a second machine: generated_nfe_v4 3/3 in node job-1793448496635-fiscal-p8-t1031-set-events-cfdi-ubl (mirror t1031 = box spiral fd70dccf + today's fiscal tree, corpus seeded from the box, pack 8 EEBCBFF8658A; generator all tests pass, 0 memo hits).")
inl note_of_xsd_signing_stages_36 () = Note ("XSD-SIGNING-STAGES", 36u32, "2026-10-31T18:17Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 14:17 fiscal: CLOSING. The list fix LANDED 14:13:53: live lib/spiral/xsd_emit.spi 161D00F5 -> AE0A917F90E02A6A, hash-equal to the proven draft (control red at the example assertion, whole module 25/25, CR Factura 4.4 regenerated 2/2; n33), via land-lib-program + lib-guarded-do (fiscal/lib-programs/xsd-stages.kit, 5 replacements; no live lib reader at apply time, my own scratch gates exempt by ancestry), round trip of the landed file ok, undo at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\\\kit\\\\undo\\\\xsd-stages. Done criteria per n23 met: lib M1-M6 + the list fix (reader, writer and example share is_staged_signature), generator half applied, family proofs 40/40 (CL DTE/Envio, MDF-e 3.00, CT-e 4.00, NF-e 4.00 with its certificates, CR 4.4). Follow-ups live elsewhere: the other 8 CR v4.4 documents (FISCAL-CR-HACIENDA, regenerating now), NF-e events (FISCAL-LIFECYCLE-EVENTS).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_d4_restart_1 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-D4-RESTART", 1u32, "2026-10-28T14:43Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 (split from HOPACSOTA-P3 note 21 at main's call to track D4 as its own row). Today PartEvalEngine Check() (spiral_compiler.fs:53976-53978) RAISES HopacsotaConflict when conflicts+cycles>0 (\"the canonical-order restart (D4) is not built yet, rerun with SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=inline\"). D4 turns that conflict into a DETERMINISTIC RESTART: on a foreign claim of an abandoned key (or a wrong D16.3 return-type hint), abandon the speculative claims and re-run the affected evaluation in canonical (single-flight depth-first) order, yielding SF-hash-equal output. Blocks D16.3 (HOPACSOTA-P3) and MEGA-PARALLEL lever B. Estimate 300m FROM MILESTONES: restart mechanism (abandon speculative claims + canonical re-run) 180m; POSITIVE-CONTROL test (fault injected through the REAL path: force a wrong hint / conflicting claim, REQUIRE a restart then SF-hash-equal output, NOT a raise -- proves the nack fully discards speculative work) 60m; determinism gate under forced conflicts (w1/w2/w4/w8 x5 = SF hash, DISAGREE 0) 60m. Sum = 300m. BLOCKER: a build slot (both heavy slots busy today). deps: none.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_d4_restart_2 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-D4-RESTART", 2u32, "2026-10-29T13:51Z", "compiler", "10-29 09:51 compiler: D4 IMPLEMENTED as a whole-build canonical restart: apps/compiler/packs/apply-hopacsota-d4-restart.ps1, 7 anchored edits. On a HopacsotaConflict (Engine.Check: foreign claims of abandoned keys or wait-for cycles, incl. a cycle raised on a body thread and re-raised at Drain), PartEvalSeams.with_conflict_restart reruns peval FROM SCRATCH with inline_restart = true. peval's tables (join_point_method/closure, ...) are created per call, so no speculative or pending entry survives. start_for returns no session, so every defer evaluates inline = canonical single-flight order, single-flight-hash-equal by construction (P2's inline proof). Plumbing: PartEvalTopEnv.inline_restart on both pair sides; the gpt5parallel driver passes false (it has its own pevalWithInlineRestart); the single-flight/hopacsota driver and single-flight peval change only through PartEvalSeams calls (D10 seam rule). The no-op seams make start_for/with_conflict_restart pass-throughs, so single-flight builds are unchanged. A ThreadLocal or mutable flag was rejected (G2 bans ThreadLocal; overlapping builds would race). Positive control: SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_FORCE_CONFLICT=1 makes Check raise through the real path, and the Check message no longer says D4 is missing. Scratch: 7/7 anchors; pair-drift 0 one-sided; G1/G2 clean; composes with the P3 shard + IVar appliers (lint and pair-drift clean on all three). PROOF RUNNING on the node (job-1793281990840-compiler-p3d4-proof, mirror pack7check): builds single-flight + hopacsota from the same source (shard + IVar + D4), then measure-arms on apps/spiral/spiral.spi, 2 rounds, arms sf (reference) / w1 / w8 shards16 / w8 shards1 (= IVar alone) / w8 FORCE_CONFLICT. DONE RULE for this row: the forced arm prints the D4 restart line AND its output hash is SAME as sf; every other arm SAME as sf (determinism under load does not depend on wall time). Wall times from that node are not P3 evidence (oversubscribed); P3's speed table runs on a quiet slot.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_d4_restart_3 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-D4-RESTART", 3u32, "2026-10-29T14:40Z", "compiler", "10-29 10:40 compiler: D4 PROVEN on the real path (node job-1793282628758-compiler-p3d4-proof-2; single-flight + hopacsota built from ONE source = HEAD + P3 shard + P3 IVar + D4 core + D4 host; apps/spiral/spiral.spi, --backend Fsharp, gcServer=0). Round 1, every arm exit 0 with output B47B5749C215A6F8 (the same hash as P2's installed single-flight output, HOPACSOTA-P2 n74): sf, w1, w8 shards16, w8 shards1, and w8 SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_FORCE_CONFLICT=1. The forced arm's stderr shows the engine stats line, then 'hopacsota parallel conflict: 0 foreign claims of abandoned keys, 0 wait-for cycles; restarting the build in canonical single-flight order (D4)', and its output is byte-equal to sf, so the conflict path discards the speculative attempt and the inline rerun reproduces single-flight. Its wall (417 s vs 271 s for w8) is the parallel attempt plus the inline rerun, as designed. A first proof run failed to build (FS0764: the host Program.fs:377 builds a PartEvalTopEnv) and was fixed by apply-hopacsota-d4-restart-host.ps1. Round 2 is running. Left for 100%: n1's full gate under forced conflicts (w1/w2/w4/w8 x5 = sf hash) on a quieter node.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_d4_restart_4 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-D4-RESTART", 4u32, "2026-10-29T15:01Z", "compiler", "10-29 11:01 compiler: round 2 done (job-1793282628758): 10 of 10 runs across 2 rounds output B47B5749C215A6F8 = sf, including the forced-conflict arm both times (restart line printed, output byte-equal). D4's restart is deterministic under load. Remaining for 100%: n1's gate at w1/w2/w4/w8 x5 under FORCE_CONFLICT on a quieter slot.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_d4_restart_5 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-D4-RESTART", 5u32, "2026-10-29T19:16Z", "compiler", "10-29 15:2x compiler: GATE MET, 100%. Forced-conflict matrix job-1793294062916 (node, p3d4 build = 6b + P3 shard/IVar + this item's core and host appliers; entry apps/spiral/spiral.spi to F#; measure-arms 5 rotated rounds; SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel, SHARDS=16, FORCE_CONFLICT=1): w1, w2, w4 and w8, 5 runs each, 20 of 20 exit 0 with output B47B5749C215A6F8, byte-identical to single-flight (sf 5/5 B47B5749). Medians: sf 130 s wall / 123 s CPU; w1force 188 s CPU, w2force 205, w4force 230, w8force 269. The forced arms cost more than unforced w8 (~180 s CPU, P3 runs) and w1 (102 s): that is the abandoned parallel attempt plus the inline canonical-order rerun, so the restart path really ran in every one. The stats' conflicts=0 counts natural conflicts only (FORCE_CONFLICT raises at Engine.Check regardless; earlier proof: 10/10 = sf plus the restart message). Wall times are noisy (load 95-100%, other node jobs ran concurrently) and are not part of this gate. Ships with PACK-7 (apply-hopacsota-d4-restart + apply-hopacsota-d4-restart-host); the single-flight side is a no-op seam (pair-drift 0 one-sided).")
inl note_of_c_shim_reserve_realloc_alias_1 () = Note ("C-SHIM-RESERVE-REALLOC-ALIAS", 1u32, "2026-10-29T07:08Z", "main", "10-29 03:1x main: found by diffing main's post-wipe oracle review (single-flight-20261029-022008, Windows) against CI run 40691709422 (Linux, same commit cec8840c). samples/dynamic_array_reserve_zero [C] exits 134 on CI and 0 locally; it is the only C row that differs that way, and its sibling dynamic_array_* rows exit 0 on both. Mechanism: tests/native-shims/dynamic-array-reserve-zero-c-shim.h:9-20 grows the array with realloc((raw), header + elements) and reassigns only the macro's local raw. The sample takes inl ~alias = xs before reading the capacity, so when realloc moves the block, alias and every refcounted holder point into freed memory: glibc's heap checks abort at the later free (134 = SIGABRT), while msvcrt grows small blocks in place and the Windows row passes by luck. It is the only shim that reallocs the handle (git grep 'realloc((raw)' in native-shims: 1 hit); geometric-growth and the resize shims only track capacity. Options for the compiler team: (a) give the shim a stable header (create allocates the requested capacity up front, so reserve never moves the block, and capacity is bookkeeping as in the geometric-growth shim), or (b) keep the realloc and abort when it returns a different pointer while refc > 1, making the alias hazard explicit. Done when the row exits the same on Windows and Linux (CI's compiler-oracle artifact against a local run, compare-runs.ps1) with the sample's own check unchanged.")
inl note_of_c_shim_reserve_realloc_alias_2 () = Note ("C-SHIM-RESERVE-REALLOC-ALIAS", 2u32, "2026-10-29T11:55Z", "main", "10-29 07:5x main: the fix is drafted and proven on a scratch copy (main scratchpad c-shim-reserve/shim.new.h). It is NOT landed yet, because git-a6's pack-6 review reads apps/compiler/tmp live until ~08:30.\nThe mechanism is worse than an alias: DynamicArrayReserve0 runs inside method0(Array0 * v0), which takes the handle BY VALUE, so the macro's '(raw) = realloc(...)' never reaches the caller. Any move leaves xs AND alias dangling, so a Reserve that can move the block is never correct here.\nFix, option (a) in shim-local form. The shim wraps malloc with a fixed slack (#define malloc -> malloc(size + 256)) and reserves only within the real allocation (_msize / malloc_usable_size, the SPIRAL_ALLOCATED_ELEMS idiom from dynamic-array-resize-union-managed). It aborts when a request exceeds the block, and it never reallocs. Resize and Capacity are unchanged, and so is the sample.\nProof, with gcc 15.2 -O2 on Windows:\n- Old shim exits 0 (the luck case), and gcc warns -Wstringop-overflow 'writing ... into a region of size 0' at its Resize0 memset, so gcc itself sees the overflow.\n- New shim exits 0, and the only warning left is the generated code's unused ArrayLit0.\n- Positive control through the bug's own path: a header forcing realloc to move the block and poison the old one. The OLD shim then dies with 0xC0000374 (heap corruption, the Windows face of CI's glibc 134), and the NEW shim exits 0.\nDone when a CI run shows samples/dynamic_array_reserve_zero [C] at exit 0 on Linux, like Windows. Land: copy shim.new.h over tests/native-shims/dynamic-array-reserve-zero-c-shim.h after the pack-6 review, then let the next CI run or matrix row prove it.")
inl note_of_c_shim_reserve_realloc_alias_3 () = Note ("C-SHIM-RESERVE-REALLOC-ALIAS", 3u32, "2026-10-29T13:12Z", "main", "10-29 09:1x main: LANDED (unstaged). tests/native-shims/dynamic-array-reserve-zero-c-shim.h is now the proven shim.new.h (n2): malloc gets a fixed slack, Reserve0 reserves only within _msize/malloc_usable_size and aborts past it, and it never reallocs. Landed at 09:1x right after git-a6's pack-6 review job timed out (exit 124), so no test.ps1 or review was reading the shim. The live file was hash-equal to the proven original first, and equal to the proven new text after. The original is in main scratchpad c-shim-reserve/shim.orig.h. Remaining proof: the next matrix run or CI row of samples/dynamic_array_reserve_zero [C], exit 0 on Linux like Windows (the item's done rule).")
inl note_of_c_shim_reserve_realloc_alias_4 () = Note ("C-SHIM-RESERVE-REALLOC-ALIAS", 4u32, "2026-10-29T16:24Z", "main", "10-29 12:2x main: proven on Windows inside the pack-6b review: samples/dynamic_array_reserve_zero [C] exit 0 at parity, with the landed shim. The Linux row still needs a CI run after the user pushes; that is the item's done rule.")
inl note_of_c_shim_reserve_realloc_alias_5 () = Note ("C-SHIM-RESERVE-REALLOC-ALIAS", 5u32, "2026-10-30T14:16Z", "compiler", "10-30 10:1x compiler, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R1: the item's only remaining step is the Linux CI row after the user pushes (main 10-29 12:2x). The Windows proof is done (pack-6b review: dynamic_array_reserve_zero [C] exit 0 at parity). So assignee -> user, tag user-batch; grooms do not re-verify it. Unblocks on the user's push; then any compiler-team agent reads the CI row and closes it.")
inl note_of_c_shim_reserve_realloc_alias_6 () = Note ("C-SHIM-RESERVE-REALLOC-ALIAS", 6u32, "2026-11-02T14:07Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:07 x: GIT CONDITION RE-CHECKED from the tree (groom of user waits). A read-only survey at 09:4x shows every active repo 0 commits ahead of origin: the user committed and pushed alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, kit, fiscal, html, polyglot, regex, spiral, tibia and x in the last 1-3 h. i574n.github.io is the exception (3 unstaged files, last commit 2 years ago). So this item's user step is probably MET. What is left is deterministic and belongs to the owner, not the user: confirm the step's files are in HEAD (git log -- <paths>) and that CI on HEAD is green (gh run list --commit <HEAD>), then close it. Only a red or missing file sends it back to the user queue. Pages enablement (HTML, REGEX-BENCH-PAGE) is a real user step, since it is a public repo setting.")
inl note_of_c_shim_reserve_realloc_alias_7 () = Note ("C-SHIM-RESERVE-REALLOC-ALIAS", 7u32, "2026-11-06T10:13Z", "compiler", "11-06 06:1x compiler GROOM: DONE. The done rule was the Linux CI row after the user push. spiral pushed 11-02 (x 11-02 10:07). The compiler and cells-gate jobs run only on schedule/dispatch (gh-pages.yml:64, :109), and the scheduled run 41848519924 (11-04 14:32, head 98e51d32, ubuntu-24.04) has compiler=success, cells-gate=success, and the log rows \"native samples/dynamic_array_reserve_zero C: ran exit 0\" in both jobs.")
inl note_of_lib_package_dot_listed_twice_1 () = Note ("LIB-PACKAGE-DOT-LISTED-TWICE", 1u32, "2026-10-29T09:25Z", "main", "10-29 05:3x main: found by the first post-wipe full lib gate (PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY n19). The six lib files with test blocks that open with '///- --package .' (decimal, dictm, float_text, json_schema, schematron, schematron_run; xpath and xpath_eval carry the header but have no test blocks) fail EVERY test in 8-9 s with Cells' 'package spiral is listed twice' (apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells.ex:1112, mount_entries), while all 65 other lib files pass. The headers came on 10-24 (0ec501fb); the duplicate check dates from 10-17 (59b477ba). Ruled out by experiment, each reverted by hash afterwards: deduplicating mount_packages' targets by Path.expand, and then case-insensitively on Windows, changed nothing (still 0 of 6). So the two 'spiral' entries are two DIFFERENT directories that share Mounts.mount_name (the directory base name), as in XSD-NAMESPACE-NAMES n4, where a 'baseline\\spiral' beside 'lib\\spiral' collided the same way. Inside lib, '--package .' names lib itself, and Cells already brings in another directory named spiral for a lib notebook. Next step: log the two {target, name} pairs in mount_packages for decimal.spi. Then either (a) drop the header from these lib files, if no other consumer path needs it (check what the 10-24 header was for), or (b) make mount names unique, so different directories with the same base name don't collide. Proof: lib_gate on the six files passes, and the Cells mix tests stay green (targets_test 23 passed; document_test 20/22, unchanged).")
inl note_of_lib_package_dot_listed_twice_2 () = Note ("LIB-PACKAGE-DOT-LISTED-TWICE", 2u32, "2026-10-29T09:28Z", "main", "10-29 05:3x main: DIAGNOSED. Cells' error now names both directories (apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells.ex:1111-1112: 'package spiral is listed twice: <existing> and <new>'; unstaged; targets_test 23 passed and document_test 20/22, both unchanged, 0 warnings). For decimal.spi it reports 'c:/home/git/spiral/deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib/spiral and c:/home/git/spiral/lib/spiral'. That is the SAME directory reached two ways: spiral's deps\\polyglot links to polyglot, whose deps\\spiral links back to C:\\home\\git\\spiral. Cells' automatic lib lookup takes the link route (the lookup order also lists deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib/spiral, like lib_gate.ps1's root walk), and '--package .' takes the direct one. Path.expand keeps both spellings, which is why main's two dedupe experiments (string, then case-insensitive) changed nothing. Fix options, sharpened: (a) compare packages by their link-resolved path (resolve each component's links before comparing; neither Path.expand nor Erlang has a realpath), or (b) make the automatic lib lookup prefer the direct lib/spiral spelling when the notebook sits inside it, or (c) drop the '--package .' headers from these 6 lib files. (b) is the smallest change with no effect on other notebooks.")
inl note_of_lib_package_dot_listed_twice_3 () = Note ("LIB-PACKAGE-DOT-LISTED-TWICE", 3u32, "2026-10-29T09:29Z", "main", "10-29 05:3x main: the resolver behind it, for option (b). apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/notebook.ex find_spiral_lib (:1251-1261) walks the notebook's ancestors, and in each ancestor tries @lib_rels (:29-35) in order: deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib/spiral FIRST, then deps/spiral/lib/spiral, then lib/spiral. For a notebook inside spiral's own lib, the walk reaches C:\\home\\git\\spiral and returns the link-chain spelling before it tries the direct lib/spiral. Smallest fix: when an ancestor itself is a lib root (it ends in /lib/spiral and holds package.spiproj, i.e. the notebook lives inside that lib), return it before the @lib_rels lookup. Physically nothing changes (same directory), but it re-spells lib resolution for every lib notebook, so the proof is a full lib sweep (lib_sweep.ps1, about 71 min on this box) with 0 new failures, plus the 6 header files passing. Not landed tonight: too little time left before 07:00 for that proof, and the resolver is shared by every lane.")
inl note_of_lib_package_dot_listed_twice_4 () = Note ("LIB-PACKAGE-DOT-LISTED-TWICE", 4u32, "2026-10-29T09:32Z", "main", "10-29 05:3x main: option (b) is being PROVEN, not assumed. Draft applied (unstaged, with a hash backup): find_spiral_lib checks lib_root_itself(dir) before the @lib_rels lookup (an ancestor that ends in /lib/spiral and holds package.spiproj is returned as-is). Job main-lib-root-sweep runs the full lib_sweep.ps1 into lib-sweep-20261029-libroot.tsv and compares each file with the 05:16 sweep. It KEEPS the change only if no file loses a passing test or gains a type error, at least one of the 6 header files now passes, and all 73 rows exist; otherwise it restores notebook.ex by hash. Result by about 06:45.")
inl note_of_lib_package_dot_listed_twice_5 () = Note ("LIB-PACKAGE-DOT-LISTED-TWICE", 5u32, "2026-10-29T10:40Z", "main", "10-29 06:4x main: PROVEN and KEPT (unstaged; the original is saved in main's session scratchpad as lib-root/notebook.ex.before). With lib_root_itself in find_spiral_lib, the full lib sweep (job main-lib-root-sweep, lib-sweep-20261029-libroot.tsv, 70 min) has 73 files, 516 tests, 439 passed and 4 failed (it was 421/21 at 05:16). Better: decimal 0->3, dictm 0->5, float_text 0->1, json_schema 0->5, schematron 0->1, schematron_run 0->2 (6 of 6 header files fully pass), and file_system 28->29 (tonight's drive test). Worse: none (no file lost a passing test or gained a type error). The 4 left are lustre.spi and near_wallet.spi, the lustre_ui/gleam_stdlib Hex conflict (GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS n24). Done.")
inl note_of_lib_package_dot_listed_twice_6 () = Note ("LIB-PACKAGE-DOT-LISTED-TWICE", 6u32, "2026-10-29T10:43Z", "main", "10-29 06:4x main: the Cells mix tests after the lib-root change (the sweep proved only the lib gates): targets_test 23 passed, notebook_spiral_lib_test (lib resolution) 3 passed, document_test 20/22 (the same two existing round-trip reds, NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-RETIRE n8), 0 compile warnings.")
inl note_of_wipe_redo_ledger_1 () = Note ("WIPE-REDO-LEDGER", 1u32, "2026-10-29T11:43Z", "main", "10-29 07:4x main, from the user at 07:4x: \"do multiple readings in the entire history of the lanes later so we try having a better grip into what can be recovered etc, coordinate with the lanes when you're at it. until 9pm we gotta leave the damage behind, getting to a state of move-forward only, like we were. then coordinate backlog groomings to know what will need to be redone\".\nSources:\n(1) Each live lane's own context. Lanes that were running at the wipe still hold pre-wipe knowledge, and this is the richest source.\n(2) The 6 post-wipe transcripts in ~/.claude/projects/C--home-git, whose compaction summaries carry pre-wipe history.\n(3) The node: C:\\home\\remote, C:\\home\\remote-stage, and its untouched %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit job logs.\n(4) The backlog plus done-notes.spi, and git reflogs and stashes.\n(5) C:\\home\\data\\rescue-2026-10-28: loss-map.md, and missing-paths-cited-in-backlog.tsv with 510 rows.\nLedger row format, one row per lost thing: LOST <what> | WAS <path or place> | SOURCE <node:path | transcript | backlog:ID nN | regenerate:<how> | gone> | STATE <recovered | recoverable | redo | dropped> | ITEM <backlog ID>.\nReadings: pass 1 at 08:xx (each lane on its own history, main across the transcripts and the missing-paths list), pass 2 at ~14:00, a final pass at ~19:00. Grooming at ~16:00 and ~20:00: every redo row gets an item tagged wipe-redo with all fields filled. Done when every row is recovered, dropped, or has a groomed item, and no lane has a known-lost dependency that isn't ledgered.")
inl note_of_wipe_redo_ledger_2 () = Note ("WIPE-REDO-LEDGER", 2u32, "2026-10-29T11:49Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 07:49 fiscal: PASS 1, fiscal's own losses (read from fiscal's context, its backlog notes and its node jobs; nothing secret involved). Every fiscal land payload lived in the repo (fiscal/lib-programs/*.kit) and survived; what died were working drafts and launchers in the session scratchpad/%TEMP%.\nLOST UBL-JSON transcoder working draft (+ json_schema 54CFD0B2) | WAS %TEMP%\\tc-uj-draft | SOURCE fiscal/lib-programs/ubl-json-transcoder.kit + node:C:\\home\\remote\\uj3\\spiral\\lib\\spiral\\json_schema.spi | STATE recovered (landed 10-29 07:28:51, hash = proven copy) | ITEM FISCAL-MY-MYINVOIS\nLOST complements writer + any-content indirection lib drafts | WAS %TEMP% draft dirs | SOURCE fiscal/lib-programs/xsd-any-complements-writer.kit, xsd-any-content-indirection.kit | STATE recovered (writer landed 07:28:50; indirection proof fix-ac2 queued) | ITEM XSD-ANY-COMPLEMENTS\nLOST complements generator half (cfdi_tfd_1_1 + cfdi_pagos_2_0, complements_of CFDI) | WAS TEMP\\claude\\C--home-git\\84b53718\\scratchpad\\complements\\src\\generator.spi | SOURCE backlog:XSD-ANY-COMPLEMENTS n10 n11 | STATE redo | ITEM XSD-ANY-COMPLEMENTS\nLOST ubl_json_documents generator draft (UBL Invoice unit reads 22 JSON + 155 XML) | WAS session scratchpad (generator.spi copy) | SOURCE backlog:FISCAL-MY-MYINVOIS n9 n11 | STATE redo | ITEM FISCAL-MY-MYINVOIS\nLOST edition-3 respell draft of the fiscal tree | WAS TEMP\\claude\\C--home-git\\84b53718\\scratchpad\\edition3\\src | SOURCE regenerate:SpiralCompiler format --spelling symbols on the fiscal tree (proof ge3e = gbase2, 0/70 units differ) | STATE recoverable | ITEM FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND\nLOST landing schedule + job ids of uj3/fix-ss/census/fix-ac (decisions only in TEMP) | WAS TEMP\\claude\\C--home-git\\84b53718\\scratchpad\\notes\\landing-schedule-1028.md | SOURCE node:kit job list (all fiscal-* jobs and outputs survive) | STATE recovered (verdicts read and noted 10-29) | ITEM FISCAL-GENERIC\nLOST set-proof launcher (launch-set-proof.ps1; set-proof.ps1 beside it) | WAS TEMP\\claude\\C--home-git\\84b53718\\scratchpad\\recgroups\\ | SOURCE node:C:\\home\\remote-stage\\recgroups\\set-proof.ps1, set-proof-c.ps1, compare-generated.ps1, fix-proof-d.ps1 (all survive); regenerate:the local launcher wraps remote.ps1 sync + job start | STATE redo | ITEM FISCAL-GENERIC\nLOST Delphi Cells -Px86_64 applier (targets.ex:321, targets_test.exs:43, README:223; dry run 3/3) | WAS TEMP\\claude\\C--home-git\\84b53718\\scratchpad\\recgroups\\delphi64-cells-apply.ps1 | SOURCE backlog:FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH n47 | STATE redo (after the NOREGVAR prelude install; main rebuilt the compiler-side applier) | ITEM FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH\nLOST Delphi interim -OoNOREGVAR applier | WAS TEMP\\claude\\C--home-git\\84b53718\\scratchpad\\recgroups\\delphi64-noregvar-apply.ps1 | SOURCE backlog:FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH n46 | STATE dropped (superseded by n47's in-source prelude fix) | ITEM FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH\nLOST Delphi step-1 probe | WAS TEMP\\claude\\C--home-git\\84b53718\\scratchpad\\delphi-step1.ps1 | SOURCE backlog:FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH n12 n13 | STATE redo | ITEM FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH\nLOST bench folders bench-acbr, dfe-net, compare (bench scripts + svn/git exports) | WAS TEMP\\claude\\C--home-git\\84b53718\\scratchpad\\bench-acbr, \\dfe-net, \\compare | SOURCE backlog:FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH n7 n8 n14 (sources, export set, rules); svn 1.14.5 back via scoop | STATE redo | ITEM FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH\nLOST method folders cells-ab, typefeature, delphi-survey | WAS TEMP\\claude\\C--home-git\\84b53718\\scratchpad\\cells-ab, \\typefeature, \\delphi-survey | SOURCE backlog notes hold their results | STATE dropped (results recorded; a reused method becomes a kit command) | ITEM FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE\nLOST scratch sweep archive 20261028-064245 (1773 throwaway files of landed work) | WAS %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261028-064245 | SOURCE gone | STATE dropped (classed throwaway in FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE n22) | ITEM FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE\nLOST fiscal's local kit job history | WAS %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\jobs | SOURCE gone (node-side history intact) | STATE dropped | ITEM FISCAL-GENERIC")
inl note_of_wipe_redo_ledger_3 () = Note ("WIPE-REDO-LEDGER", 3u32, "2026-10-29T11:52Z", "kit", "10-29 07:5x kit, pass 1 batch 1 (kit lane: release trains, renameproof programs, kit state). Method: three-way SHA1 of every .spi/.spiproj/.toml in node:remote-stage\\train{3,4}-bundle\\{base,cand} against the live kit tree; kit do --dry-run plus a new-text presence check for each renameproof .do; node %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit listing.\nLOST TRAIN3 apply programs (8: kit-text, settle-receipt, doctor servers crate, seal, argument-count/after-flag/at, window-watch-to-lib) and train3 staging | WAS %TEMP% scratchpad clean\\train3 | SOURCE live tree: all 45 TRAIN3 files are byte-equal to node:remote-stage\\train3-bundle\\cand (the release had applied them in place); kit_jobs/model.spi differs only by today's env guard (+43 lines) | STATE recovered | ITEM EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN\nLOST TRAIN3 run record and per-notebook results (job-1793192300314, exit 1, 67/69 candidate notebooks; reds ps_cli kill_with_mine_refuses and stop_cli a_job_over_its_memory_cap) | WAS %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\jobs | SOURCE gone (the summary survives only in tibia's 10-28 message) | STATE redo: retry TRAIN3 from the live tree, reproduce both reds first, and name table_cli/table_test_cli in the report (EOIE-TABLE-EMPTY-CELLS) | ITEM EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN\nLOST TRAIN4 kit programs (kit-clip, kit-lines, waits-column, kit-part-at, rename-retry, text-replace-cas) | WAS scratchpad clean\\train4 + build-train4-copy.ps1 | SOURCE node:remote-stage\\train4-bundle\\cand: 18 files whose live copy equals base, so copying cand reproduces the programs exactly | STATE recoverable | ITEM EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN (also EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES n23, EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS n6/n8, EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION n77/n78)\nLOST TRAIN4 lib half (fixed lib-clip, sm'.split_nth) | WAS scratch lib copy | SOURCE node:remote-stage\\train4-bundle\\cand\\src\\spiral\\sm'.spi and am'.spi; both DIVERGED from the live lib (later lands plus main's 07:39 blank-line normalization), so recover by diffing cand against base and re-applying onto live | STATE recoverable | ITEM LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES, EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION\nLOST renameproof programs, 21 .do of 10-25/26 | WAS kit lane scratch | SOURCE node:remote-stage\\renameproof\\scripts | STATE recovered (10 fully landed in the live tree: argument-counts-to-lib, drop-unused-std-string, kit-named-length, list-planning-index-granular, path-predicates-to-lib, route-arity, sequences-static-skip, stop-cli-deterministic, string-builders-to-lib, word-after-whitespace); dropped (10 superseded, their anchors no longer exist: declared-edition3, heal-arith, heal-expect, heal-title-notes, lint-join-points, list-planning-index (replaced by -granular), subcommand-find-b/-c (variants), table-csv-quoted plus its revert); dropped (heal-task-rename: still applies, but it would fold the agile_rename_rewrite crate into agile_task_rename and no note asks for that) | ITEM KIT-RENAME\nLOST pre-wipe kit job history, windows, events and tree leases | WAS %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\{jobs,windows,tree-leases,supervisor} | SOURCE gone; node %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit holds only node-local state (tree-leases 60, supervisor 14, state-backups 1, todo-cache 1, newest 10-27) | STATE dropped (history only; the job store is live again since 10-28 ~20:18, job-1793228918503) | ITEM PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY\nLOST kit archive manifests and unstaged/state backups (archive\\20261027-054550, -064527, 20261028-064245; backups\\unstaged; state-backups\\agile-20261027-133935) | WAS %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit | SOURCE gone | STATE dropped (the repos they backed up are intact; DISK-CLEANUP n32 and FISCAL-COMMIT-HYGIENE n22 cite them as receipts only) | ITEM DISK-CLEANUP\nLOST snapshot 20261028-085735-before-release-TRAIN.zip | WAS %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit | SOURCE gone | STATE dropped (the tree it protected is intact and fully accounted for above) | ITEM EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN\nLOST todo-cache (KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS warm cache) | WAS %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\todo-cache | SOURCE regenerate: the next 'kit agile todos' run rebuilds it | STATE recovered on first use | ITEM KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS\nLOST shell environment in pre-wipe and old agent shells: 11 User variables missing (KIT_LEASE_POLICY, MIMALLOC_DISABLE_REDIRECT, SPIRAL_PYTHON, UV_*, BUN_*) | WAS process env | SOURCE regenerate: import the User env before kit calls; without KIT_LEASE_POLICY=renewing every mutating 'kit agile' verb fails 'lease authority blocked ... should_wrap=1' (seen 08:0x) | STATE recovered (per call) | ITEM PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY\nAlso done in this batch: view-add wipe-redo (list all --tag wipe-redo), for the 16:00 and 20:00 groomings. Next batch: kit drafts from 10-28 that lived only in scratch (TODO-markers M5, kit_do_commit split, tree-write, remote status M1, lib-set-env and the census lib slices, x's lint shadows), each checked against the node mirrors before it's called redo.")
inl note_of_wipe_redo_ledger_4 () = Note ("WIPE-REDO-LEDGER", 4u32, "2026-10-29T11:55Z", "compiler", "10-29 08:0x compiler (session 88a146f8 = kit2 on 10-25, compiler lane 10-27/28), PASS 1. Sources read: my 208 backlog notes (126 distinct file references, each checked for existence), the missing-paths TSV (2 rows cite my notes), the node (C:\\home\\remote mirrors + eoie job logs), and the live packs/lab dirs. My pre-wipe transcript and compaction summaries are gone (file recreated 10-28 15:09Z), so the backlog is the record.\nLOST compiler-lane transcript + context | WAS ~/.claude/projects/C--home-git/88a146f8-*.jsonl | SOURCE backlog:208 kit2/compiler notes | STATE dropped (facts live in the notes) | ITEM PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY\nLOST P3 shard prototype applier + runner | WAS compiler TEMP scratchpad | SOURCE node:C:\\home\\remote\\scripts\\apply-hopacsota-p3-shard.ps1, hopacsota-p3-shard-proto.ps1 | STATE recovered (fetched to session scratch; superseded by packs/apply-hopacsota-p3-shard.ps1, HOPACSOTA-P3 n22: the old one shards by the structural hash of the whole id, which races on the shared per-body Dictionaries) | ITEM HOPACSOTA-P3\nLOST P3 shard prototype RESULT (never noted) | WAS node job-1793189883882-hopacsota-p3-shard-proto | SOURCE node:%LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\jobs | STATE recovered, verdict INCONCLUSIVE: node oversubscribed (SF wall 285-290 s vs 26.5 s clean; w1 548-752 s); w8 284.7 s; w8 bridges 100,935 (~unchanged vs 103k); cut before w8-b by the wipe -> redo the measurement on a quiet slot with the n22 applier | ITEM HOPACSOTA-P3\nLOST P3 IVar-variant applier + runner + result | WAS TEMP | SOURCE node:C:\\home\\remote\\scripts\\apply-hopacsota-p3-ivar.ps1, hopacsota-p3-ivar-proto.ps1, job-1793187175380 (build 1 FS0001, build 2 ok, measure from 07:42) | STATE recoverable (scripts fetched; measure output to read in pass 2) | ITEM HOPACSOTA-P3\nLOST P2 determinism gate + P3 contention/trace probes | WAS TEMP | SOURCE node:C:\\home\\remote\\scripts\\hopacs