inl note_of_sim_x_serve_render_1 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", 1u32, "2026-10-31T14:17Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: slice 3 of SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY (its n7). Port stub.ps1's Get-Rendered (id, HTML-escaped text, edit_history_post_ids defaulting to [id]; conversation_id, created_at, author_id, public_metrics zero-filled over the metric names, referenced_posts, entities.urls, attachments.media_keys, each only when asked in post.fields) and Get-WithExpansions (referenced_posts expansion with included posts and referenced_posts.author_id users, attachments.media_keys with media.fields; includes object assembled as the stub does), then serve GET /2/users/:id/tweets (timeline: max_results 5..100, plain_status/status fault users, protected -> 200 errors entry, exclude replies/retweets by ref kind, start_time/end_time on created_at's first 19 chars by ordinal compare, newest first by a stable sort, meta.result_count), GET /2/tweets (lookup by ids) and GET /2/tweets/:id. Posts carry author_id from their user, as the stub's postsById copy does. Make every multi-line helper ':~' from the start (the one-path size lesson, SIM-X-SERVE-FOLLOWING n3). Proof: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim, 153 rows, and 'answered' lines for the three paths.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_render_2 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", 2u32, "2026-10-31T14:19Z", "x", "2026-10-31 10:19 x: exact shapes read from stub.ps1 for this slice. Metric names (line 142): bookmark_count, impression_count, like_count, quote_count, reply_count, repost_count (zero when absent). Posts get author_id = their user's id (line 60). includes: posts / users / media, each a map keyed by id in FIRST-insertion order, emitted as its values and only when non-empty; 'includes' only when any is non-empty; data only when non-empty. Lookup (243): found ids rendered with expansions; unknown ids -> errors [{value, detail 'Could not find post with ids: [<id>].', title 'Not Found Error'}] (no resource_type). Single (251): unknown -> 200 {errors: [{value, detail 'Could not find post with id: [<id>].', title 'Not Found Error', resource_type 'post', parameter 'id'}]}; found -> {data: rendered with post.fields, no expansions}. Timeline sort (Sort-Stable, 126): ordinal compare on created_at, descending, ties keep input order.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_render_3 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", 3u32, "2026-10-31T15:05Z", "x", "2026-10-31 11:05 x: DRAFTED in sim_serve.spi (unstaged): the world indexes posts by id with author_id from their user (with_posts); rendering (base id/escaped text/edit_history_post_ids, then conversation_id, created_at, author_id, public_metrics zero-filled over the 6 names, referenced_posts, entities.urls, attachments.media_keys, each only when asked), expansions (referenced_posts with included posts and referenced_posts.author_id users, attachments.media_keys with media.fields; includes posts/users/media first-insertion-unique; includes and data only when non-empty); GET /2/users/:id/tweets (5..100 check, plain_status/status/protected fault users, exclude replies/retweets by ref kind, start/end_time on created_at's first 19 chars by ordinal compare, newest first by a stable insertion sort, meta.result_count), GET /2/tweets (lookup + errors for unknown ids) and GET /2/tweets/:id (data or the 200 errors entry). Routing is one api_route union (UsersBy, UsersByIds, Following, Timeline, Lookup, Single, Elsewhere); search and writes still forward. The status-line reason table now mirrors stub_http.ps1. Every multi-line helper is ':~'. Local: x-compat 0 in 30 files, kit standards 0. PRE-REGISTERED x-serveprobe5: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK ... 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests, and the sim's answered lines include at least one GET /2/users/<id>/tweets, one GET /2/tweets and one GET /2/tweets/<id> (a path the tutorial never calls is reported, not failed, with the tutorial step that would cover it).")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_render_4 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", 4u32, "2026-10-31T15:28Z", "x", "2026-10-31 11:28 x: x-serveprobe5 (job-1793459559419, 29 s) RED at parse, nothing run: (1) 'when' is a Spiral keyword, so the helper named when broke every cell that used it (renamed gated); (2) a list literal split over lines needs ';' at the end of each continued line, like a record type (the status-reason tables). Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe6, n3's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_render_5 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", 5u32, "2026-10-31T16:06Z", "x", "2026-10-31 12:06 x: x-serveprobe6 (job-1793460783753, 20 min) by n3's rule: NOT PASSED. Green: sim and client builds; the sim answered every new path (28 x GET /2/users/<id>/tweets, 13 x GET /2/tweets/<id>, 2 x GET /2/tweets, plus 19 users/by, 3 users, 6 following). RED: e2e 6 failures, 123 requests instead of 153, all on 'xc thread 9003', 'xc delete-thread 9003', 'xc thread 9018 did not read back the posted oauth1.spi (0 vs 1276 chars)' and 'delete-thread deleted 0 of 17'. CAUSE: 9003 and 9018 are not in world.json; earlier tutorial steps CREATE them through POST /2/tweets, which is still forwarded, so they exist only in the stub's live world, and the sim answered 'not found' from its static world. FIX (until slice 6 brings writes into the sim): api_world.stale turns true after any forwarded non-GET request, and from then on the sim forwards post reads (timeline, lookup, single post) to the stub; users and following stay answered by the sim, since writes never change them. The serve loop now threads the updated world through each request (replied returns the reply and the next world). Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe7, n3's rule unchanged (the answered counts for post reads will drop to the steps before the first write).")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_render_6 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-RENDER", 6u32, "2026-10-31T16:37Z", "x", "2026-10-31 12:37 x: x-serveprobe7 (job-1793463270388, 20 min, xqueue3) by n3's rule: PASS. Sim and client builds exit 0; e2e.ps1 with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK x/apps/client / e2e: 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests (the stub's count). Answered by the sim: 25 x GET /2/users/<id>/tweets, 2 x GET /2/tweets, 2 x GET /2/tweets/<id> (post reads before the first forwarded write; after it they go to the stub, by the stale rule), plus 19 users/by, 3 users, 6 following. DONE (code in x, unstaged).")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_search_1 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", 1u32, "2026-10-31T16:42Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: slice 4 of SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY (its n7). Port stub.ps1 Get-Search: GET /2/tweets/search/recent (7-day window) and /2/tweets/search/all (30 days, start_time wins over the window), windows computed from world.now (Get-WindowStart: now minus N days, 'yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss'), query terms as the stub matches them (read Get-Search first), rendering and expansions reused from slice 3, the same stale rule (forward once a write was forwarded, since searches see posted threads). Every multi-line helper ':~'. Proof: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim, 153 rows, and answered lines for both search paths.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_search_2 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", 2u32, "2026-10-31T16:44Z", "x", "2026-10-31 12:44 x: exact semantics read from stub.ps1 Get-Search (line ~258). oldest = world.now minus Days ('yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss'; 7 recent, 30 all); since = first 19 chars of start_time; if since is set and (search/all, or since > oldest by ordinal compare) then oldest = since. query: split on whitespace, tokens with ':' become key:value (last wins); from:<handle> resolves to that user's id via the lowercase handle (else the raw text); conversation_id:<id> is REQUIRED (none -> nothing matches). found = every post (users in world order, then their posts) with conversation_id (default its id) equal, author matching when from is given, created_at[0..19] >= oldest by ordinal compare; sorted descending by the key (id length zero-padded to 10, '|', id), stable. max_results default 10 (no range check here), next_token default 0 is a plain index; page = slice. Body: data = page rendered with post.fields only (NO expansions) when non-empty; meta {result_count, newest_id + oldest_id when the page is non-empty, next_token = string(start + size) while more remain}.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_search_3 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", 3u32, "2026-10-31T16:59Z", "x", "2026-10-31 12:59 x: DRAFTED in sim_serve.spi (unstaged): GET /2/tweets/search/recent and /search/all answered by the sim (routes SearchRecent/SearchAll, both covered by the stale rule like every post read). Window from world.now through OTP calendar (rfc3339 to system seconds, minus 7 or 30 days, back to rfc3339 with offset Z, first 19 chars); start_time replaces it for search/all, or for recent when later; query terms whitespace-split, key:value, last wins; from: resolves a handle to its id; no conversation_id -> empty result; matching posts sorted descending by (zero-padded id length, '|', id) with a stable insertion sort; next_token as a plain index; data rendered with post.fields only; meta result_count, newest_id/oldest_id on a non-empty page, next_token while more remain. Local x-compat 0 in 30 files, kit standards 0. PRE-REGISTERED x-serveprobe8: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK ... 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests, and at least one answered GET /2/tweets/search/(recent|all). If every search in the tutorial comes after the first write, the count is 0 by the stale rule: then NOT PASSED as a proof (e2e still recorded), and the next step is a direct sim-only check that searches a world thread before any write.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_search_4 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", 4u32, "2026-10-31T17:09Z", "x", "2026-10-31 13:09 x: x-serveprobe8 (job-1793466266839, 26 s) RED at compile, nothing run: sm'::length is numeric-polymorphic, so feeding it straight to obj_to_string left an unbound metavar (ETY-ValueRestriction; annotated ': i32'), and listm::last does not exist (replaced by a fold from the first element). Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe9, n3's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_search_5 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", 5u32, "2026-10-31T17:55Z", "x", "2026-10-31 13:55 x: x-serveprobe9 (job-1793466918057, 33 min, node shared with verify42) VOID, not a verdict on search: builds exit 0, but e2e died before the tutorial (0 request rows, no port file; the launcher's line filter dropped e2e's own error). A direct 'sim-run.ps1 serve' on the same mirror started and wrote its port, so the cause is start time: e2e.ps1 gave the X server 60 s, and the sim server (mix check of the Cells build, VM start, world load, child stub) takes longer on a loaded node. FIX: e2e.ps1 waits 300 s for the server when XSIM_SERVER=sim (60 s for the stub, unchanged); the probe launcher now also prints e2e's failure lines. Since slices 5 and 6 are now drafted on top, the next probe covers 4-6 together (x-serveprobe10, rule on SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES n3); a red is bisected by slice.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_search_6 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH", 6u32, "2026-10-31T21:06Z", "x", "2026-10-31 17:06 x: x-serveprobe14 (job-1793480014001, 9 min, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES n3's combined rule: PASS. e2e.ps1 with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK x/apps/client / e2e: 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests. Answered by the sim: 13 x GET /2/tweets/search/all, 3 x POST /2/oauth2/token, 37 x POST /2/tweets, 29 x DELETE /2/tweets/<id>, 13 x GET /2/tweets/<id> (the post reads after writes), plus 28 timeline, 2 lookup, 6 following, 19 users/by, 3 users: 153 in all, i.e. the sim answered EVERY request of the tutorial and the stub served none. Slice 4''s clause (search answered): met. DONE.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_auth_1 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-AUTH", 1u32, "2026-10-31T17:18Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: slice 5 of SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY (its n7), semantics read from stub.ps1 (lines 69-100, 303-318, 340-347). (a) OAuth 1.0a (Test-OAuth1): parse 'OAuth k=\"v\", ...' (unescape keys and values), require oauth_consumer_key and oauth_token equal to world.oauth1; params = every field but oauth_signature; signature = base64(HMAC-SHA1(key = pct(consumer_secret) '&' pct(token_secret), base = METHOD '&' pct(url) '&' pct(join('&', sorted 'k=v' with k and v pct-encoded, ordinal sort on k then v)))), url = 'http://' + Host + path; pct = RFC 3986 unreserved-only (EscapeDataString) -> uri_string:quote(Bin, \"\") on OTP 25+; HMAC via crypto:mac(hmac, sha, Key, Data), base64:encode. Startup self-test on the published example (expected 'Ls93hJiZbQ3akF3HF3x1Bz8/zU4=') like the stub. (b) client Basic = 'Basic ' + base64(client_id ':' client_secret) of world.oauth2, valid only on /2/oauth2/token. (c) token refresh (Get-Renewed, STATEFUL): form grant_type=refresh_token and refresh_token equal to the LAST issued one (starts at world.oauth2.refresh_token); else 400 {error invalid_request, error_description 'Value passed for the token was invalid.'}; on success issue refresh_token-N and access_token-N (N = number issued so far), body {token_type bearer, expires_in 7200, access_token, scope 'tweet.read tweet.write users.read offline.access', refresh_token}. The world record carries the issued list, threaded through the serve loop like stale. (d) app context on a non-GET (non-token) path -> 403 Unsupported Authentication with the stub's exact detail. Scope for this slice: answer OAuth1-signed reads and the token endpoint in the sim; writes stay forwarded until slice 6 (so the stale rule stays). Every multi-line helper ':~'. Proof: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim, 153 rows, answered lines for POST /2/oauth2/token and for at least one oauth1-context read.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_auth_2 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-AUTH", 2u32, "2026-10-31T17:19Z", "x", "2026-10-31 13:19 x: primitives CHECKED on the box (erl, OTP 29): uri_string:quote(Bin, \"\") for RFC 3986 encoding, lists:sort on {K, V} binary tuples (ordinal on K then V, as the stub's Comparison), crypto:mac(hmac, sha, Key, Base) and base64:encode reproduce the published example signature 'Ls93hJiZbQ3akF3HF3x1Bz8/zU4=' exactly (true). So the Spiral port is plumbing over these four host calls. Drafting starts once SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH's probe (x-serveprobe9) passes, so unproven slices do not stack.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_auth_3 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-AUTH", 3u32, "2026-10-31T17:33Z", "x", "2026-10-31 13:33 x: DRAFTED in sim_serve.spi (unstaged), launch held until SIM-X-SERVE-SEARCH's x-serveprobe9 gives its verdict. auth_kind now classifies app / user (bearer), client (Basic = base64(client_id:client_secret) of world.oauth2, only on /2/oauth2/token) and oauth1 (header parsed and unescaped, consumer key + token must equal world.oauth1, HMAC-SHA1 over the RFC 3986-encoded, lists:sort-ed params with base METHOD&pct(http://Host+path)&pct(params), key pct(consumer_secret)&pct(token_secret)); nothing is forwarded for its auth any more (unknown auth -> the stub's 401). POST /2/oauth2/token is answered by the sim with the stub's branches (no auth 401, client -> refresh rotation, app 403 Unsupported Authentication with the exact detail, user/oauth1 404 'no route'); the issued refresh tokens (newest first, starting with world.oauth2.refresh_token) live in the world record and thread through the serve loop; a refresh needs grant_type=refresh_token and the LAST issued token, and issues refresh_token-N / access_token-N with N = tokens issued so far. POST log rows carry the raw body like the stub's. Startup self-test: the published OAuth example must give 'Ls93hJiZbQ3akF3HF3x1Bz8/zU4=' or serve exits 3. Writes other than the token endpoint stay forwarded (stale rule unchanged). Local x-compat 0 in 30 files, kit standards 0. PRE-REGISTERED x-serveprobe10 (after slice 4's verdict): e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK ... 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests, at least one 'answered POST /2/oauth2/token', and every answered family of slices 1-4 still present.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_auth_4 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-AUTH", 4u32, "2026-10-31T21:07Z", "x", "2026-10-31 17:06 x: x-serveprobe14 (job-1793480014001, 9 min, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES n3's combined rule: PASS. e2e.ps1 with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK x/apps/client / e2e: 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests. Answered by the sim: 13 x GET /2/tweets/search/all, 3 x POST /2/oauth2/token, 37 x POST /2/tweets, 29 x DELETE /2/tweets/<id>, 13 x GET /2/tweets/<id> (the post reads after writes), plus 28 timeline, 2 lookup, 6 following, 19 users/by, 3 users: 153 in all, i.e. the sim answered EVERY request of the tutorial and the stub served none. Slice 5''s clause (token endpoint answered, refresh rotation): met; OAuth 1.0a-signed requests were answered by the sim too (no request went to the stub). DONE.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_1 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 1u32, "2026-10-31T17:35Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: slice 6 of SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY (its n7), semantics read from stub.ps1 New-Post / Remove-Post (lines 317-338). POST /2/tweets: body JSON {text, reply.in_reply_to_tweet_id}; text trimmed at the END only; empty or X weight > 280 -> 400 'Invalid Request' / 'text must be 1 to 280 characters' (weight from the client's glyphs module, the same weighting xc splits with; sim_serve may open it since the sim package includes the client sources); unknown parent -> 400 'in_reply_to_tweet_id <p> does not exist'; ids from 9001 upward; created set records it; conversation_id = the parent's conversation_id (else the parent id) or its own id; refs [{type replied_to, id parent}] when replying; created_at = world.now + (size of the created set AFTER adding) seconds as 'yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss.000Z'; author_id = world.me; 201 {data {id, text HTML-escaped, edit_history_post_ids [id]}}. DELETE /2/tweets/:id: only ids in the created set, else 404 'Not Found Error' / 'Could not find post with id: [<id>].'; removes it from created and posts; 200 {data {deleted: true}}. Context (stub dispatch order): no auth 401; POST with client -> refresh (slice 5); any other non-GET without user/oauth1 context -> 403 Unsupported Authentication; then POST /2/tweets, DELETE /2/tweets/:id, else 404 'no route'. With writes in the sim the stale rule and the forwarding of writes both go (slice 7 then drops forwarding entirely). Every multi-line helper ':~'. Proof: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim, 153 rows, answered POST /2/tweets and DELETE /2/tweets/<id>, and post reads answered after the first write (the stale rule gone).")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_2 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 2u32, "2026-10-31T17:46Z", "x", "2026-10-31 13:46 x: DRAFTED in sim_serve.spi (unstaged), launch held behind slices 4 and 5. The world record now groups write state (write_state {me, created set, next_id from 9001}) and uses an index alias for its dicts (the type cell was over the post weight). POST /2/tweets: JSON body, text end-trimmed, empty or splitter::x_weight > 280 (xc's own weighting) -> 400 'text must be 1 to 280 characters'; unknown parent -> 400 'in_reply_to_tweet_id <p> does not exist'; new id = next_id; created_at = now + created-set size seconds as yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss.000Z; conversation_id from the parent (else its own id); refs [replied_to parent]; author_id world.me; 201 with escaped text and edit_history_post_ids [id]. DELETE /2/tweets/:id: only created ids, else 404 'Could not find post with id: [<id>].'; 200 {data {deleted true}}. One change dispatcher in the stub's order (401 no auth; client POST -> refresh; non-user/oauth1 -> 403; create; delete; 404 'no route'); token_reply is folded into it. served_here now answers EVERY non-GET request in the sim, so no write is forwarded and the stale rule never fires (post reads after writes are the sim's own). Local x-compat 0 in 30 files, standards 0. The tree now stacks slices 4, 5 and 6, all unproven: probes run in order (4, then 5, then 6), and a red is bisected by slice. PRE-REGISTERED (for its turn): e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 37+5 steps, 153 requests, answered POST /2/tweets and DELETE /2/tweets/<id>, and post reads answered after the first write.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_3 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 3u32, "2026-10-31T17:56Z", "x", "2026-10-31 13:56 x: PRE-REGISTERED x-serveprobe10, covering slices 4 (search), 5 (auth) and 6 (writes) together: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK x/apps/client / e2e: 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests; answered by the sim: at least one GET /2/tweets/search/(recent|all) [slice 4], at least one POST /2/oauth2/token [slice 5], at least one POST /2/tweets and one DELETE /2/tweets/<id> [slice 6]; and post reads after writes now answered by the sim, shown by GET /2/tweets/<id> answered at least 13 times (probe 6's count when the sim answered every single-post read, against 2 under the stale rule in probe 7). Each slice item is closed only by its own clauses.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_4 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 4u32, "2026-10-31T18:06Z", "x", "2026-10-31 14:06 x: x-serveprobe10 (job-1793469660520, 51 s) RED at compile, nothing run: (1) field shorthand ('writes;') is accepted in record LITERALS but not in record UPDATES ({ w ?| ... }): ETY-TermError at the two update sites, now 'writes = writes'; (2) gated was defined after its first use in log_row (top-down resolution), moved up. Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe11 with n3's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_5 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 5u32, "2026-10-31T18:17Z", "x", "2026-10-31 14:17 x: x-serveprobe11 (job-1793470275422, 38 s) RED at compile, nothing run: ETY-TermError on assigning a nested record update ({ b::writes ?| me = ... }) to the write_state field; nested updates of an alias-typed record field do not unify here. FIX: every write_state value is built by writes_of (me) (created) (next_id) : write_state, a literal with a declared type; the 3 nested updates (world_auth, counted, dropped) are gone. Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe12, n3's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_6 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 6u32, "2026-10-31T19:03Z", "x", "2026-10-31 15:03 x: x-serveprobe12 (job-1793470966961, 26 min) RED at server start, e2e not run: builds exit 0 (slices 4-6 compile), but sim-run serve crashed during boot ('the X API stub exited before writing its port (exit code 1)'); a direct run on the mirror gave the cause: badarg in uri_string:quote, whose Safe argument must be a CHARLIST, while Spiral's str sends a binary (the OAuth self-test runs at startup). Fixed with erlang_term::charlist. The same audit found string:pad_leading does not exist (search's id order would have crashed): now string:pad(digits, 10, leading, 48) + iolist_to_binary. Both checked on the box (erl): quote gives a%20b%2Bc%2F~, pad gives 0000000004, and the rfc3339 window math gives 2026-10-11T12:00:00Z for now minus 7 days. Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe13, n3's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_7 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 7u32, "2026-10-31T20:00Z", "x", "2026-10-31 16:00 x: x-serveprobe13's launch failed 8 times (two launcher runs) AFTER clean syncs: the node-proof core guard refused 'node core D1C49B8BDC3318F7 does not match required 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52'. A new compiler pack was installed at 14:30 on BOTH box and node (core D1C49B8BDC3318F7, source 5b4655ec4bf5990b, build-identity built 18:30Z); verify42 started earlier on the old core and is unaffected. Relaunching with -Core D1C49B8BDC3318F7; n3's rule unchanged, and this run is also the first proof of slices 1-6 on the new pack (local x-compat runs since 14:30 already used it, 0 findings).")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_8 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 8u32, "2026-10-31T20:46Z", "x", "2026-10-31 16:46 x: x-serveprobe13 (job-1793476993862, 25 min, new core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by n3's rule: NOT PASSED, but every family is now answered by the sim: 37 x POST /2/tweets, 3 x DELETE /2/tweets/<id>, 3 x POST /2/oauth2/token, 9 x GET /2/tweets/search/all, 13 x GET /2/tweets/<id>, 2 x GET /2/tweets, 28 timeline, 6 following, 19 users/by, 3 users (so slices 4 and 5 serve; slice 6 writes are accepted). RED: e2e 6 failures, 123 requests: 'xc thread 9003' (and delete-thread 9003, the 9018 read-back, delete-thread 0 of 17) exit 4 right after GET /2/tweets/9003. CAUSE, found by replaying the run's logged writes against a fresh sim on the node: a post the sim CREATED comes back with every field empty ({id:\"\", text:\"\", author_id:\"\", ...}), because created posts were built with map_of, whose keys are ATOMS (fine for responses, which json:encode writes the same way), while every lookup (field) uses BINARY keys like the JSON-loaded posts. FIX: stored_map (binary keys) for created posts and their replied_to refs. Local x-compat 0, standards 0. Relaunching as x-serveprobe14, n3's rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_writes_9 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES", 9u32, "2026-10-31T21:09Z", "x", "2026-10-31 17:06 x: x-serveprobe14 (job-1793480014001, 9 min, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by SIM-X-SERVE-WRITES n3's combined rule: PASS. e2e.ps1 with XSIM_SERVER=sim exit 0, 'RUST-OK x/apps/client / e2e: 37 tutorial steps and 5 extra cases', 153 requests. Answered by the sim: 13 x GET /2/tweets/search/all, 3 x POST /2/oauth2/token, 37 x POST /2/tweets, 29 x DELETE /2/tweets/<id>, 13 x GET /2/tweets/<id> (the post reads after writes), plus 28 timeline, 2 lookup, 6 following, 19 users/by, 3 users: 153 in all, i.e. the sim answered EVERY request of the tutorial and the stub served none. Slice 6''s clauses (creates, deletes, >= 13 single-post reads after writes): met. DONE.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_standalone_1 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", 1u32, "2026-10-31T18:25Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: slice 7 of SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY (its n7). Once slices 4-6 pass, the sim answers every family stub.ps1 serves, so: (1) remove the forwarding (sim_serve's forwarded/relayed/drained, the stub port argument, sim-run.ps1's child stub.ps1) and the stale rule (api_world.stale, after_forward, post_route), with any unknown route answered by the stub's 404 problem 'no route <path>' as the stub does; (2) keep stub.ps1 as the ORACLE, not a fallback: a parity check (a scripts/ step or an e2e option) runs the tutorial once against each server and compares the two request logs row for row (method, path, query, authorized, context, body) plus each step's client output; (3) decide with the user-facing docs whether XSIM_SERVER=sim becomes the default e2e server. Proof: e2e with XSIM_SERVER=sim passes with no stub process started, and the parity check shows 0 differing rows. Every multi-line helper ':~'.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_standalone_2 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", 2u32, "2026-10-31T18:30Z", "x", "2026-10-31 14:30 x: the oracle check is WRITTEN (unstaged): x/scripts/e2e-parity.ps1 -Exe <xc.exe> [-WorkDir] runs e2e.ps1 twice (stub, then XSIM_SERVER=sim), compares the two requests.jsonl logs row for row on method, path, query, authorized, context and body, prints up to 10 differing rows, and exits 1 on any difference or a failed e2e run. Parse-checked (0 errors). First real use: right after slices 4-6 pass, as one node job (it needs a built xc.exe, so it rides on a client build). Slice 7 then removes the forwarding and keeps this check as the stub's oracle role.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_standalone_3 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", 3u32, "2026-10-31T21:12Z", "x", "2026-10-31 17:12 x: PRE-REGISTERED x-parityprobe (scratchpad launcher, xqueue3, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7): sim and client builds exit 0, then scripts/e2e-parity.ps1 runs the tutorial against stub.ps1 and against the sim. PASS = both e2e runs exit 0 and the two request logs have 0 differing rows on method, path, query, authorized, context and body. Rows that differ are listed (first 10) and each becomes a fix in sim_serve before slice 7 removes the forwarding.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_standalone_4 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", 4u32, "2026-10-31T21:36Z", "x", "2026-10-31 17:36 x: x-parityprobe (job-1793481488711, 15 min, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by n3's rule: PASS. Sim and client builds exit 0; scripts/e2e-parity.ps1: 'exits stub 0 sim 0; rows stub 153 sim 153', '0 differing row(s)' on method, path, query, authorized, context and body. So the sim's request log equals the stub's row for row over the whole 37-step tutorial (and in x-serveprobe14 the sim answered all 153 itself). LEFT for this item: the removal step (forwarding, stub port argument, sim-run's child stub, the stale rule; unknown routes get the stub's 404 'no route'), then the same parity check again with no stub process behind the sim.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_standalone_5 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", 5u32, "2026-10-31T22:30Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:3x x: slice 7 code done, together with the typed-caller slice (TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE n6), so one node proof covers both. sim_serve.spi no longer forwards. Gone: relayed, forwarded, drained, after_forward, relay_here, served_here, post_route, the stale world field, the stub_port argument through replied/handled/served/listening/started/serve, and the dead token_reply. An unknown route now gets the sim's own 404 'no route PATH' (routed's Elsewhere arm). scripts/sim-run.ps1 no longer starts stub.ps1 for serve; it checks the 3 arguments and runs erl. README paragraph rewritten. e2e-parity stays the oracle. Pass rule, registered before the run: node job x-parity-typed1 (x-node-e2e -Mode parity) gives both e2e exits 0, stub rows = sim rows = 153, 0 differing rows, and the sim build passes gleam-modules' 1,000-line part limit.")
inl note_of_sim_x_serve_standalone_6 () = Note ("SIM-X-SERVE-STANDALONE", 6u32, "2026-10-31T23:19Z", "x", "2026-10-31 19:19 x: x-parity-typed2 (job-1793486075070, 44 min, xqueue3, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by n5's rule: PASS. Sim build exit 0, so the parts stay under gleam-modules' 1,000-line limit with five forall instantiations each of caller_reply, answer and change_reply. Client build exit 0. e2e-parity: 'exits stub 0 sim 0; rows stub 153 sim 153', '0 differing row(s)'. The sim now serves the whole tutorial with no relay and no stub child. Done. Unstaged files: x/apps/sim/spi/sim_serve.spi, x/scripts/sim-run.ps1, x/apps/sim/spi/README.md.")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_1 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 1u32, "2026-10-31T19:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 15:54 fiscal: FOUND via FISCAL-MY-MYINVOIS's localized drift (n30). UBL-UnqualifiedDataTypes-2.1 AmountType is <simpleContent><restriction base='ccts-cct:AmountType'> re-declaring currencyID use=required; CCTS_CCT AmountType extends xsd:decimal with currencyID optional + currencyCodeListVersionID optional. XSD rule: in a restriction, a re-declared attribute REPLACES the base's same-named one. xsd_emit treats restriction like extension: the derived record keeps base (x_2_amount_type, whose own reader takes attribute_of currencyID) and adds its own required currency_id; generated_ubl_invoice_2_1.spi: reader 27521 required_attribute currencyID + 27742 attribute_of currencyID (both fill from the same XML attribute), writer 46423 listm.concat [ x_2_amount_type_attributes x.base; [ (currencyID, x.currency_id) ] ] with 46729 also emitting currencyID from the base -> every UDT amount writes currencyID TWICE: the 22 extra nodes in all 22 MyInvois UBL-JSON documents (408 -> 430 at .../Amount/here). Fix: in the restriction case, drop from the base's attribute part every attribute the restriction re-declares (writer), and read the re-declared one once; regression test with a 2-level restriction re-declaring an attribute. Then regenerate the UBL units (drift guard) and re-run reads_its_documents (expect 0 drift).")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_2 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 2u32, "2026-10-31T19:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 15:58 fiscal: DESIGN (read from the lib; nothing drafted yet). xsd.spi derived_of (lines ~262-274) builds the SAME Derived { name; base; content; attributes } for xs:extension and xs:restriction, so the model drops which one it was. The fix does not need it back: an extension may not re-declare an attribute its base already has (invalid XSD), so 'a name the derived type declares wins over the base's' is right for both. EMITTER-ONLY fix in xsd_emit: where the writer composes base_attributes (xsd_emit.spi:1519-1522, '<base>_attributes x.base') with the derived type's own attribute parts, wrap the base call as listm.filter (fun (n, _) => <n not in the derived's attribute names>) (<base>_attributes x.base) whenever the derived declares any attribute (names from d.attributes; QName text as written, e.g. currencyID). The reader may keep filling both fields from the one XML attribute (harmless, same value). Regression test: a 2-level simpleContent restriction re-declaring an optional base attribute as required must write that attribute exactly once (count it in the emitted _attributes body, plus a unit round trip). Then regenerate generated_ubl_* (bring-generated-home.ps1) and re-run MyInvois reads_its_documents expecting 0 drift (the 408 -> 430 case).")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_3 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 3u32, "2026-10-31T20:19Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 16:19 fiscal: DRAFTED (n2's design), program fiscal/lib-programs/xsd-restriction-attributes.kit (xsd_emit live AE0A917F -> B1BF0D77, 3 replacements, rehearsed hash-equal, round trip ok after adding the final newline the appended test had dropped): (1) attribute_names_of collects a derived type's attribute names (tag_text a.name); (2) without_redeclared rewrites the writer's base part '<t>_attributes x.base' into listm'.filter (fun (n, _) => n <> ... ) (...) excluding those names, other families' prefixes pass unchanged (they never end in that text); (3) the one call site, record_walk's Derived arm, wraps derived_prefix with it; (4) test a_restriction_redeclaring_an_attribute_writes_it_once (TBase extends xs:decimal with cur + ver optional; TAmount restricts TBase re-declaring cur required): the writer must filter cur from the base part and still write ver. Proof running: fiscal-restriction-attrs = CONTROL (live + the test only, must fail at the filter assertion) then the whole xsd_emit module on the draft. Then: land, regenerate generated_ubl_* (bring-generated-home.ps1), MyInvois reads_its_documents expecting 0 drift.")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_4 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 4u32, "2026-10-31T20:38Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 16:38 fiscal: land program for xsd_emit live AE0A917F -> B1BF0D77 (moved here from fiscal/lib-programs, which leaves the repo; land with land-lib-program.ps1 -Note XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE/<this note> -File xsd_emit.spi -Base AE0A917F90E02A6A -Target <B1BF0D77 16-hex>).\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit xsd_emit.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -1083,6 +1083,22 @@\n     | _ :: rest => choice_cases choice_name rest acc\n     | [] => acc\n \n+:~ ^ attribute_names_of \\* form. (attributes : list (xsd attribute_decl form)) (acc : list string) : list string =\n+    ?= attributes ?|\n+    | (Attribute a) :: rest => attribute_names_of rest (tag_text a.name :: acc)\n+    | [] => acc\n+\n+:= without_redeclared \\* form. (attributes : list (xsd attribute_decl form)) (prefix : walked) : walked =\n+    ?= attribute_names_of attributes [] ?|\n+    | [] => prefix\n+    | names =>\n+        := kept = join_with \" && \" (listm.map (\\ n => \"n <> \\\"\" ++# n ++# \"\\\"\") names) \"\"\n+        := filter (part : string) : string =\n+            ? sm'.ends_with \"_attributes x.base\" part\n+            ?> \"listm'.filter (fun (n, _) => \" ++# kept ++# \") (\" ++# part ++# \")\"\n+            |: part\n+        { prefix ?| attribute_parts = listm.map filter prefix.attribute_parts }\n+\n :& :~ collect_attributes \\* form.\n     (owner : string) (attributes : list (xsd attribute_decl form)) (acc : collected)\n     : collected\n@@ -2073,7 +2089,8 @@\n     (family : f) (kinds : list (string * type_kind)) (name : string) (item : xsd type_def form) : list walk_text =\n     ?= item ?|\n     | Complex c => record_walk family kinds name c.content c.attributes (nothing_walked ())\n-    | Derived d => record_walk family kinds name d.content d.attributes (derived_prefix family kinds d.base)\n+    | Derived d =>\n+        record_walk family kinds name d.content d.attributes (without_redeclared d.attributes (derived_prefix family kinds d.base))\n     | Simple _ => []\n \n :& :~ record_walk\n@@ -3469,3 +3486,19 @@ test a_family_with_required_signatures_generates_staged_units =\n     := unchanged, _ = generated_units_signed [\"Signature\"] (untyped_any_content ()) plain\n     := before, _ = generated_units plain\n     ? unchanged <> before ?> failwith \"a family without required signatures generates exactly what it did before\"\n+\n+///> rust\n+test a_restriction_redeclaring_an_attribute_writes_it_once =\n+    := schema =\n+        read_schema (\n+            \"<xs:schema><xs:complexType name=\\\"TBase\\\"><xs:simpleContent><xs:extension base=\\\"xs:decimal\\\">\"\n+            ++# \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"cur\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\" use=\\\"optional\\\"/>\"\n+            ++# \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"ver\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\" use=\\\"optional\\\"/></xs:extension></xs:simpleContent>\"\n+            ++# \"</xs:complexType><xs:complexType name=\\\"TAmount\\\"><xs:simpleContent><xs:restriction base=\\\"TBase\\\">\"\n+            ++# \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"cur\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\" use=\\\"required\\\"/></xs:restriction></xs:simpleContent>\"\n+            ++# \"</xs:complexType></xs:schema>\"\n+        )\n+    := functions = join_lines (module_parts schema).functions \"\"\n+    ? sm'.contains \"listm'.filter (fun (n, _) => n <> \\\"cur\\\") (\" functions = !-\n+    ?> failwith \"the base's attributes leave out the one the restriction re-declares\"\n+    ? sm'.contains \"\\\"ver\\\"\" functions = !- ?> failwith \"the base's other attributes are still written\"\n>>>")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_5 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 5u32, "2026-10-31T20:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 16:58 fiscal: CORRECTED program (supersedes n4): the first draft failed to type-check because its helpers sat BEFORE ':== walked' and inside the ':& collect_attributes' group (splitting it: 'Unbound term variable: collect_attributes', 'Unbound type variable: walked'); moved just above ':~ ^ named_type_walk' (after walked, outside any group). xsd_emit live AE0A917F -> 3872CC94.\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit xsd_emit.spi\npatch <<<\n@@ -2053,6 +2053,22 @@\n             type_ref = type_ref\n         }\n \n+:~ ^ attribute_names_of \\* form. (attributes : list (xsd attribute_decl form)) (acc : list string) : list string =\n+    ?= attributes ?|\n+    | (Attribute a) :: rest => attribute_names_of rest (tag_text a.name :: acc)\n+    | [] => acc\n+\n+:= without_redeclared \\* form. (attributes : list (xsd attribute_decl form)) (prefix : walked) : walked =\n+    ?= attribute_names_of attributes [] ?|\n+    | [] => prefix\n+    | names =>\n+        := kept = join_with \" && \" (listm.map (\\ n => \"n <> \\\"\" ++# n ++# \"\\\"\") names) \"\"\n+        := filter (part : string) : string =\n+            ? sm'.ends_with \"_attributes x.base\" part\n+            ?> \"listm'.filter (fun (n, _) => \" ++# kept ++# \") (\" ++# part ++# \")\"\n+            |: part\n+        { prefix ?| attribute_parts = listm.map filter prefix.attribute_parts }\n+\n :~ ^ named_type_walk\n     \\* form f {\n         derived_prefix;\n@@ -2073,7 +2089,8 @@\n     (family : f) (kinds : list (string * type_kind)) (name : string) (item : xsd type_def form) : list walk_text =\n     ?= item ?|\n     | Complex c => record_walk family kinds name c.content c.attributes (nothing_walked ())\n-    | Derived d => record_walk family kinds name d.content d.attributes (derived_prefix family kinds d.base)\n+    | Derived d =>\n+        record_walk family kinds name d.content d.attributes (without_redeclared d.attributes (derived_prefix family kinds d.base))\n     | Simple _ => []\n \n :& :~ record_walk\n@@ -3469,3 +3486,19 @@ test a_family_with_required_signatures_generates_staged_units =\n     := unchanged, _ = generated_units_signed [\"Signature\"] (untyped_any_content ()) plain\n     := before, _ = generated_units plain\n     ? unchanged <> before ?> failwith \"a family without required signatures generates exactly what it did before\"\n+\n+///> rust\n+test a_restriction_redeclaring_an_attribute_writes_it_once =\n+    := schema =\n+        read_schema (\n+            \"<xs:schema><xs:complexType name=\\\"TBase\\\"><xs:simpleContent><xs:extension base=\\\"xs:decimal\\\">\"\n+            ++# \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"cur\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\" use=\\\"optional\\\"/>\"\n+            ++# \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"ver\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\" use=\\\"optional\\\"/></xs:extension></xs:simpleContent>\"\n+            ++# \"</xs:complexType><xs:complexType name=\\\"TAmount\\\"><xs:simpleContent><xs:restriction base=\\\"TBase\\\">\"\n+            ++# \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"cur\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\" use=\\\"required\\\"/></xs:restriction></xs:simpleContent>\"\n+            ++# \"</xs:complexType></xs:schema>\"\n+        )\n+    := functions = join_lines (module_parts schema).functions \"\"\n+    ? sm'.contains \"listm'.filter (fun (n, _) => n <> \\\"cur\\\") (\" functions = !-\n+    ?> failwith \"the base's attributes leave out the one the restriction re-declares\"\n+    ? sm'.contains \"\\\"ver\\\"\" functions = !- ?> failwith \"the base's other attributes are still written\"\n>>>")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_6 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 6u32, "2026-10-31T23:09Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 19:09 fiscal: LANDED 18:59:49 from this item's note 5 via land-lib-program.ps1 -Note (the first note-sourced land): live xsd_emit AE0A917F -> 3872CC94DCBD3505, hash-equal to the proven draft (control red at the filter assertion; whole module 26/26, 0 type errors), undo at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\\\kit\\\\undo\\\\XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE-n5; round trip of the landed file: canonical true, fixpoint true (spiral apps/cells/spi/lib_roundtrip.exs; slice-roundtrip-sweep.ps1 is retired). Next, already queued: regenerate EVERY family on the landed lib (regen-all, box) and bring the changed units home formatted (drift guard), then re-run MyInvois reads_its_documents on the node expecting 0 drift. 40 -> 80.")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_7 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 7u32, "2026-11-01T00:46Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 20:46 fiscal: REGENERATED every family on the landed lib (fiscal-regen-all-restriction, box: all 23 generator tests pass) and brought all 81 units home formatted (bring-generated-home.ps1, format-generated 1108 s, 0 failed). Blast radius, exactly: the 6 UBL units (+47/-? each: e.g. x_unqualified_data_types_2_amount_type_attributes now 'listm'.filter (fun (n, _) => n <> \\currencyID\\) (x_2_amount_type_attributes x.base)', plus the Id of the timestamp types) and generated_ksef_fa_3 (18 filters, e.g. rodzajAdresu on TAdres); every other unit byte-identical. Also brought home: the 6 NF-e event units (the generator change was committed without them). Compile proof of the emitted filter: the formatted KSeF unit (0FD0BC55795D) is gating now; the UBL invoice drift re-run (expect 0 drift on the 22 MyInvois JSON documents) goes to the node after the set-all job frees t1031.")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_8 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 8u32, "2026-11-01T00:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 20:54 fiscal: COMPILE PROOF of the emitted filter: the formatted generated_ksef_fa_3 (0FD0BC55795D, the tree file; 18 listm'.filter base parts) passes 2/2 (writes_its_example + keeps_raw_strings_out), 0 type errors (fiscal-ksef-filter-proof, box). Zero-drift proof queued on the node: fiscal-p8-t1031u-ubl-drift (job-1793494454747) on a new mirror t1031u (spiral fd70dccf with xsd_emit 3872CC94, the fiscal tree with the formatted UBL units; corpus copied on the node from today's t1031, the same box tar) runs generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 with its cell cap raised mirror-only to 18,000,000 ms; expect 'real documents do not read back' to be gone (22 MyInvois JSON documents). On that, this item and FISCAL-MY-MYINVOIS's drift close. 80 -> 90.")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_9 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 9u32, "2026-11-01T03:59Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 00:10 fiscal: node UBL drift run job-1793494454747 gave NO VERDICT. The 2.8 h rust build passed, then generated_ubl_invoice_2_1_writes_its_example panicked with OS error 3 (path not found) on write_all_text: the t1031u mirror has no samples/ folder (gitignored; build.ps1 makes it, and the direct cell gate does not). Created the folder and re-queued the same gate as job-1793505518611-fiscal-p8-t1031u-ubl-drift2. Close on its 0-drift verdict.")
inl note_of_xsd_restriction_attribute_override_10 () = Note ("XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE", 10u32, "2026-11-01T07:08Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 03:10 fiscal: ZERO-DRIFT VERDICT: node job-1793505518611-fiscal-p8-t1031u-ubl-drift2 (t1031u: spiral with xsd_emit 3872CC94, the fiscal tree with the formatted UBL units, samples/ created) ran generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 over its 155 XML + 22 JSON real documents: 1/1 passed, 0 failed, headroom 0.62, the example written, and 0 drift lines in the log (no \"real documents do not read back\", \"does not keep every element\" or \"not stable\"). The UBL AmountType restriction no longer writes currencyID twice, so the MyInvois UBL-JSON round trip holds. CLOSED by the item's rule.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_1 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 1u32, "2026-10-31T20:07Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:07 compiler: filed from the self-hosting groom (user: conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'). MEASURED on the installed compiler (PACK-10), sample with packages |core-: ':= ~c = 'b' / := ~d = 'a' / ? c > d ...' fails on C, Rust AND F# with 'Got: char | Fails to satisfy: number' (so it's the language's constraint, not one backend); the same program with '=' compiles. Every lexer in the self-hosted compiler needs 'a' <= c <= 'z' style tests. Design options: (a) a comparable/ordered constraint that both number and char satisfy, with each backend's codegen comparing chars as code points (C: int32_t code point, Rust char, F# char, Gleam/Lua/Wasm ints); (b) char -> i32 / i32 -> char conversions as portable ops and compare the ints (a lib-level workaround, weaker). Also UNVERIFIED and to check first: whether char <-> integer conversion compiles on every backend (my probe's conv spelling was wrong, so it only produced a parse error). Done when ordering and conversion compile and agree with C on every backend in the matrix lists, with one regression sample.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_2 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 2u32, "2026-10-31T20:21Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:21 compiler: ROOT CAUSES MEASURED (installed PACK-10, packages |core-, --backend C). (1) ORDERING is only the core library's constraint: core.spi:15-21 declares (<) (<=) (>) (>=) as 'forall t {number}', and char isn't a number; the raw primitive '!!!!LT(d, c)' on two chars COMPILES to C (exit 0), so the backend support exists. Fix direction: an ordering constraint that char satisfies (or char-specific comparison functions over !!!!LT/LTE/GT/GTE), then agree-with-C rows on every backend. (2) CONVERSION is a COMPILER gap in partial evaluation, not the library: '!!!!Conv(i32, c)' on a char -> 'TracedError: Cannot convert char to the following type: i32', and '!!!!Conv(char, 99i32)' -> 'Cannot convert the literal to the following type: char' (core.spi:100's conv also requires {number} on both sides). Fix direction: PartEval's Conv accepts char <-> integer (code point semantics), plus each backend's TyConv for char (C int32_t, Rust 'as u32'/char::from_u32, F# int/char, Gleam/Lua/Wasm ints). Order: the Conv fix first (it's a core change for the next pack), then the ordering constraint.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_3 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 3u32, "2026-10-31T20:27Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:27 compiler: DEEPER GAP FOUND, a semantics decision before any code. char's runtime representation differs by backend (prim* mappings in the live core): ONE BYTE on C/C++ ('char', 25134/26003), Rust ('u8', 13133), Zig ('u8', 15101), Delphi ('AnsiChar', 14576); a UTF-16 code unit on F# ('char', 12554); a Unicode scalar value on Lean ('Char', 15832); a one-character STRING on Gleam and Lua (17610, 18506); a 32-bit int on WGSL, Kotlin, Swift, OpenQASM, eBPF (20480..23454). So 'char -> i32' is not one operation until char is: 'λ' or any non-ASCII char already means different things per backend (a lexer over UTF-8 sees bytes on C/Rust but code units on F#). PROPOSAL (needs the user's or main's decision, since it's a language-semantics change): char = a Unicode scalar value (u21 range, 'i32' payload) on every backend, with string indexing staying byte-based as today (the C oracle's UTF-8). Then Conv char <-> integer = scalar value, the ordering constraint compares scalar values, and the 17 TyConv emitters that need a char case are: Fsharp 12885, Rust 14011, Delphi 14856, Zig 15583, Lean 16127, Bend 17274+, Gleam 18069, Lua 19180, Wasm 20048, Wgsl 20818, Calyx 21321, Kotlin 22034, Swift 22812, OpenQasm 23332, Ebpf 23657, TypeScript 24356, Python 27517 (C/C++ cast generically). The alternative (char = byte everywhere, matching the C oracle) is simpler for the byte backends but breaks F#/Lean/Gleam's char literals beyond ASCII. Estimate after the decision: ~6 h (PartEval Conv + LitChar folding, 17 emitters, a sample per direction, matrix rows on every backend).")
inl note_of_char_ordering_4 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 4u32, "2026-10-31T20:38Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:38 compiler: CONTEXT from PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES n27-n28 (main, 10-30): main decided to leave CharT's per-backend representation as it is ('the first real consumer sets the shape'), and code points already have a lib path: sm'.char_code (sm'.spi ~1131) with F#, Python, Rust, TypeScript and Lua arms (Lua decodes UTF-8 itself), utf8_get_bytes (Rust, else .NET) and utf8_byte_list (Gleam, Rust). Its rule was 'add an arm when a caller on that backend needs it'. The user's 10-31 direction ('no conversion gaps, unacceptable by now') overrides that rule for the self-hosting gate, so: EITHER the compiler-level fix of n3 (char = a Unicode scalar value everywhere, Conv char <-> integer in PartEval, ordering constraint), OR complete the lib path on every backend (char_code + its inverse + ordering helpers, with the missing Gleam, Delphi, C and Zig arms). The compiler-level fix removes the per-backend divergence at its root and is what the gate asks for; the lib path keeps divergent CharT and only papers over it. Recommendation: compiler-level, decided with main (it overturns main's 10-30 call).")
inl note_of_char_ordering_5 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 5u32, "2026-10-31T20:42Z", "compiler", "DECISION (main, 10-31 10-31 16:42, relayed to compiler): char = a Unicode scalar value on every backend. This supersedes main's 10-30 PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES n27-n28 call: the user's 10-31 self-hosting direction ('no conversion gaps, unacceptable') is newer, and CHAR-ORDERING's measurements show today's per-backend CharT is exactly such a gap. SCOPE (next compiler pack): Conv char <-> integer in PartEval (+ LitChar folding), an ordering constraint chars satisfy, and a char case in the 17 TyConv emitters, with CharT's representation moved to a scalar value on the byte backends (C/C++/Rust/Zig/Delphi) and the string backends (Gleam/Lua). CONDITION: string APIs that index or slice today (byte offsets on the native backends, the C oracle's UTF-8) keep an EXPLICIT meaning: either they stay byte/UTF-8 based and are named so, or they move to scalar indexing in the same pack, never silently. GATE: lib/spiral plus the consumer corpus (tibia, kit, fiscal) with the candidate DLL (packs/lib-gate-dll.ps1), so a char that used to be a byte can't silently change a consumer's string code; plus every backend's char samples agreeing with C.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_6 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 6u32, "2026-10-31T20:44Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:44 compiler: SCOPE ADDITION found while drafting the PartEval half: the compiler's own literal is 'LitChar of char' (spiral_compiler.fs:932), a .NET char = one UTF-16 code unit, so a char literal ABOVE U+FFFF (any astral scalar, e.g. an emoji) can't even be represented today, and folding Conv to char can't produce one. 'char = a Unicode scalar value' therefore also means LitChar carries a scalar (System.Text.Rune, or an int validated to 0..0x10FFFF minus surrogates); that touches all 47 LitChar references (the lexer's char literal, litXxx emitters per backend, Conv folding, equality folding). PLAN ORDER for the pack: (1) LitChar -> Rune (mechanical, every backend's literal printer: C/Zig/Rust/Delphi print the scalar value or a UTF-8 escape per backend; F# 'char' can't hold astral values either, so F#'s CharT becomes Rune/int too); (2) CharT runtime representation = scalar on all backends; (3) Conv char <-> integer in PartEval and the 17 TyConv emitters; (4) the ordering constraint; (5) string index/slice APIs kept byte-based and named so (main's condition), or moved to scalar in the same pack; (6) gate = lib + tibia + kit + fiscal on the candidate DLL plus a char sample per direction on every backend. Estimate revised 480m -> 900m.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_7 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 7u32, "2026-10-31T21:41Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 17:41 compiler: STEP (1) SIZED, the LitChar -> scalar checklist from the live core (47 references; literalStableText 28035 goes away with PACK-11, leaving 46). TYPE: 'LitChar of char' (932, beside globalIdStableText 949). LEXER/PLUMBING: char_quoted 1317 (parse the literal into a scalar; astral values need surrogate-pair or \\\\u{...} handling), token_groups 994, show_lit 1022, lit 4515, lit_to_primitive_type 9321. PARTIAL EVALUATION (8): peval 10224, 10922, 10978, 11267, 11293, 11319, 11345, 11372 (equality/compare folding and Conv, which step (3) extends). FORMATTER (2): FormatterCheck 30021, FormatterFormat 33163 (print the literal back as written). LITERAL PRINTERS, one per backend: litFsharp 12528, litRust 13107, litDelphi 14556, litZig 15082, litLean 15815, codegenBend 17071, litGleam 17583 (+ codegenGleam 18164-18167), litLua 18479, codegenWasm 19855, codegenWgsl 20587, codegenCalyx 21072, codegenKotlin 21851, codegenSwift 22620, codegenOpenQasm 23173, codegenEbpf 23488, litTypeScript 23852, CodegenC 25158, CodegenCpp 26395, CodegenPython 27155. Each printer emits the scalar in its target's form (C/Zig: integer code point; Rust: char literal or from_u32; F#: needs Rune/int since F# char is UTF-16; Gleam/Lua: the scalar's UTF-8 string while CharT is still a string there, an int after step (2)).")
inl note_of_char_ordering_8 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 8u32, "2026-11-01T03:41Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 23:41 compiler, cadence groom: TOP-3 CPU-LIGHT #3. Next concrete step: LitChar checklist n7 step 1, change LitChar of char to a scalar (Rune) in the core AST and list every match site it breaks (a grep census, no build), so the 17 TyConv emitters and the PartEval Conv char<->int land in one applier.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_9 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 9u32, "2026-11-01T06:13Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:13 compiler, round 3: STEP 1 CENSUS DONE (build-free, spiral_compiler.fs on core C0990B2D590CE965). 'LitChar of char' (Tokenize 932) has 40 match sites in 18 sections, plus 110 CharT sites. Per section: Tokenize 5 (932 decl, 949 show, 994 semantic tokens), Infer 1 (4513 -> TyPrim CharT), PartEval 9 (9319 type of; 10236 equality fold; 10935 to DSymbol; 10991 STRING INDEX = 'bytes.[b] |> char |> LitChar', the byte-based index the decision must keep explicit or move to scalars; 11280/11306/11332/11358/11385 the comparison folds), Formatter 2, and one emit site each in CodegenFsharp 12540, Rust 13119, Delphi 14568, Zig 15094, Lean 15827, Bend 17083, Lua 18491, Wasm 19867, TypeScript 23864, C 25170, Cpp 26407, Python 27167, plus Gleam 5 and Wgsl 6. FINDING: PartEval already FOLDS literal char comparisons (11280-11385), so 'ordering fails on every backend' comes from the type side (no ordering constraint on CharT in Infer) and the runtime path, not from literal folding. PLAN for the applier: (a) LitChar of char -> LitChar of System.Text.Rune (scalar) at 932, with show/token sites; (b) the 9 PartEval sites compare Rune values; 10991 keeps bytes and returns a byte-valued Rune, explicit; (c) each emitter's one site prints the scalar for its CharT (C/Rust/Zig/Delphi: u32 or one byte per today's CharT; F# Rune; Lean Char; Gleam/Lua String); (d) Infer: add the ordering constraint for CharT. About 40 + 18 edits, all text, as one kit do program.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_10 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 10u32, "2026-11-01T06:19Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:19 compiler, round 3: ROOT CAUSE FOUND and STEP (d) DRAFTED. Why char ordering fails: the core prelude declares (<) (<=) (>) (>=) with {number} (apps/compiler/tmp/samples/core/operators.spi:19-28; upstream's corebase/core.spi does the same), and CNumber (Infer 5505) excludes CharT. PartEval itself already accepts char LT both folded (11280) and runtime (11286, any equal primitive type). FIX (upstream-compatible: {number} keeps its meaning, so upstream code still compiles): a new constraint 'ord' = the numeric prims + CharT. Two less obvious edits it needs: (1) a forall var declared {number}/{int}/{sint}/{uint}/{float} must satisfy {ord} too, or the 18 lib functions with '\\* t {number}' that call '<' (float_native is_positive, am' average, file_system loops...) break with ForallVarConstraintError (5526 checks IsSubsetOf), so the check uses the implied set; (2) literal defaulting (6664) tests 'constraints = {number}', and '1 < 2' would now carry {number; ord} and stop defaulting to int, so it compares after removing ord. Dry-applied: 11 replacements (7 core, 4 operators.spi), and every Constraint match is covered (constraint_name, constraint_kind, the body check, the name table). STILL NEEDED for runtime char '<' (step c): emitters whose CharT isn't natively ordered: Gleam (String has no '<': use string.compare) and Wgsl/Calyx subsets; C/Rust/Zig/F#/Lua/Python/TS compare natively. Gate = lib + tibia + kit + fiscal (the decision's gate), via BACKEND-BATCH.\nPROGRAM (kit do <dir holding spiral_compiler.fs and core/operators.spi> -):\nedit spiral_compiler.fs expect files 1 replacements 7\n  replace once <<<\n        | CNumber\n        | CPrim\n        | CSymbol\n  >>> with <<<\n        | CNumber\n        | COrd\n        | CPrim\n        | CSymbol\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n        | CFloat -> \"float\" | CNumber -> \"number\" | CPrim -> \"prim\"\n  >>> with <<<\n        | CFloat -> \"float\" | CNumber -> \"number\" | COrd -> \"ord\" | CPrim -> \"prim\"\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n        | CSInt | CUInt | CInt | CFloat | CNumber | CPrim | CSymbol | CRecord -> KindType\n  >>> with <<<\n        | CSInt | CUInt | CInt | CFloat | CNumber | COrd | CPrim | CSymbol | CRecord -> KindType\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                    | CNumber, TyPrim (UInt8T | UInt16T | UInt32T | UInt64T | Int8T | Int16T | Int32T | Int64T | Float32T | Float64T)\n                    | CPrim, TyPrim _\n  >>> with <<<\n                    | CNumber, TyPrim (UInt8T | UInt16T | UInt32T | UInt64T | Int8T | Int16T | Int32T | Int64T | Float32T | Float64T)\n                    | COrd, TyPrim (UInt8T | UInt16T | UInt32T | UInt64T | Int8T | Int16T | Int32T | Int64T | Float32T | Float64T | CharT)\n                    | CPrim, TyPrim _\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                | TyVar (b,_) -> if con.IsSubsetOf b.constraints = false then [ForallVarConstraintError(TypeNameIdOps.create b.name,con,b.constraints)] else []\n  >>> with <<<\n                | TyVar (b,_) ->\n                    let implied = if Set.exists (function CNumber | CInt | CSInt | CUInt | CFloat -> true | _ -> false) b.constraints then Set.add COrd b.constraints else b.constraints\n                    if con.IsSubsetOf implied = false then [ForallVarConstraintError(TypeNameIdOps.create b.name,con,b.constraints)] else []\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                        if m.constraints = Set.singleton CNumber then default_numeric \"int\" |> Option.iter (fun t -> link.Value <- Some t)\n                        elif m.constraints = Set.singleton CFloat then default_numeric \"float\" |> Option.iter (fun t -> link.Value <- Some t)\n  >>> with <<<\n                        let numeric = Set.remove COrd m.constraints\n                        if numeric = Set.singleton CNumber then default_numeric \"int\" |> Option.iter (fun t -> link.Value <- Some t)\n                        elif numeric = Set.singleton CFloat then default_numeric \"float\" |> Option.iter (fun t -> link.Value <- Some t)\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                \"number\", CNumber\n                \"prim\", CPrim\n  >>> with <<<\n                \"number\", CNumber\n                \"ord\", COrd\n                \"prim\", CPrim\n  >>>\nedit operators.spi expect files 1 replacements 4\n  replace once <<<\n:= (<) \\* t {number}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>> with <<<\n:= (<) \\* t {ord}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n:= (<=) \\* t {number}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>> with <<<\n:= (<=) \\* t {ord}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n:= (>) \\* t {number}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>> with <<<\n:= (>) \\* t {ord}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n:= (>=) \\* t {number}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>> with <<<\n:= (>=) \\* t {ord}. (a : t) (b : t) : bool =\n  >>>")
inl note_of_char_ordering_11 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 11u32, "2026-11-01T06:20Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:20 compiler: CORRECTION to step (c) above: Gleam ALREADY orders chars. codegenGleam 18176-18179 send LT/LTE/GT/GTE on StringT|CharT (and LitChar literals) to string.compare, which is UTF-8 byte order and therefore code-point order. Every other emitter's CharT is natively ordered (bytes / UTF-16 / Lean Char / int32 / Lua strings), so once the ord constraint lands, char ordering should run end to end on the existing emitters. Remaining for the full item: the scalar move (LitChar -> Rune, steps a-c of n9) and the Conv char<->int, both separate from ordering. So ordering can ship first, alone, in PACK-12 (after BACKEND-BATCH's lib+tibia+kit+fiscal gate), with a sample: 'a' < 'b' and a runtime char compare.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_12 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 12u32, "2026-11-01T06:21Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:21 compiler: SAMPLE DRAFTED, held OUT of the tree on purpose: the installed compiler rejects it today ({number} on <), and C/Rust rows are default-on, so adding it now would turn every lane's accept run red until the pack lands. It enters samples/char_ordering together with the ord program in PACK-12. Installed formatter 'format --check' = exit 0. Expected: exit 0 on every backend that runs it; the nested ifs return 1-4 to show which comparison failed. package.spiproj = 'packages: / core- / modules: / main'. TEXT (samples/char_ordering/main.spi):\n:= main () : i32 =\n    := ~a = 'a'\n    := ~b = 'b'\n    ? a < b ?>\n        ? b > a ?>\n            ? a <= a ?>\n                ? 'x' >= 'w' ?> 0i32 |: 4i32\n            |: 3i32\n        |: 2i32\n    |: 1i32")
inl note_of_char_ordering_13 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 13u32, "2026-11-01T07:22Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:22 compiler: pack12b INSTALLED 03:14 (main), so the COrd constraint, the {number}=>ord implication and the defaulting change are live. TWO CORES, so the switch is two steps: (1) SAMPLES core (apps/compiler/tmp/samples/core/operators.spi, packages 'core-') affects only matrix samples. Its A/B is running on the node in my mirror ordsw (job-1793517757741): arm A = full -Suite all on the installed pack12b, then the 4 operators switch to {ord} + samples/char_ordering added in the MIRROR only, then arm B; it reports compile-status, residual and verdict diffs plus the char_ordering rows. The live tree is untouched until it's green. (2) LIB core = '|core-' = deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core/core.spi:15-21 (our fork of upstream, commit cc1b12ac), used by lib, tibia, kit and fiscal. Switching it gives the whole lib char ordering, but that fork's core.spi then needs our compiler: upstream's compiler has no 'ord'. Our compiler still accepts upstream's {number} core, so backward compatibility holds; forward compatibility (upstream compiling our fork's core) doesn't. That's the user's 'keep upstream core compatibility in mind' line, so step (2) is flagged for a decision and gated on lib+tibia+kit+fiscal. FOLLOW-UP for PACK-13: an unresolved metavar with ONLY {ord} isn't defaulted (6664 now strips ord and needs {number}). Before, (<) imposed {number}, which defaulted to int; add 'or constraints = {ord} -> int' to keep that.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_14 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 14u32, "2026-11-01T07:41Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:41 compiler: STEP (1) LANDED 03:40, SAMPLES CORE: apps/compiler/tmp/samples/core/operators.spi (<) (<=) (>) (>=) now {ord} (09D3BCD4CD2F6D11 -> 1CBF689E0842CDF4), plus samples/char_ordering (main.spi + package.spiproj), all unstaged. PROOF (node mirror ordsw, installed pack12b): two full -Suite all runs, A = {number} (single-flight-20261101-032241) and B = {ord} + sample (-033255). 7,405 shared rows: compile-status diffs 0, residual diffs 0, timeouts 0. char_ordering compiles on C, Delphi, F# and Rust in B (the old core would reject it, so B really used {ord}). Natively on the node: C exit 0, Rust exit 0, and the C output compares chars at runtime ('bool v2 = v0 < v1;'). STEP (2), the LIB core (deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core/core.spi:15-21), is NOT done: it needs the user's call on forward upstream compatibility (upstream's compiler can't read {ord}), then the lib+tibia+kit+fiscal gate. Until then lib code can't use < on chars.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_15 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 15u32, "2026-11-01T11:51Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 07:51 compiler: DECIDED (user 11-01: 'decide this autonomously'): the LIB core switches to {ord}. REASON, measured: our fork's core (deps/The-Spiral-Language, origin i574n, vs mrakgr/master) already diverges in 29 files (+1172/-704) and already uses ops upstream's compiler doesn't know (LayoutToHeapRefs, LayoutToStackRefs, UnsafeBackendSwitch; git grep on mrakgr/master *.fs). So forward compatibility (upstream compiling our core) was already gone, and {ord} costs nothing new there. The direction that matters stays intact: our compiler still accepts upstream's {number} core and upstream code, since COrd is additive and {number} implies ord. The lib-helper alternative (char.lt etc.) would leave '<' on chars a type error in lib, against the 'conversion gaps unacceptable' gate. PLAN: (a) PACK-13's ord-defaulting fix FIRST, so an {ord}-only metavar still defaults to int, which matches today's behaviour exactly; (b) switch core.spi:15-21 in a copy and gate lib + tibia + kit + fiscal on that copy (never through the node mirror's deps junction, which points at the shared, stale node checkout); (c) land in deps/The-Spiral-Language (commit left to the user, unstaged).")
inl note_of_char_ordering_16 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 16u32, "2026-11-01T11:56Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 07:56 compiler: LIB-CORE GATE RUNNING on the node, both arms in parallel on fresh mirrors (libord-a/-b, synced 07:5x, digest E1E8FFC64F1E7AE1), installed pack12b, never via the mirrors' deps junction: lib_sweep.ps1 -Candidate <core copy> over all 73 lib test files. Control = box's live core copy (core.spi 6631368E734BE060), job-1793534174603; ord = the same with the 4 operators on {ord} (6892459D72F539D6, program core-ord.kitdo), job-1793534183205. Outputs: node C:\\home\\remote\\libord-cores\\sweep-{control,ord}.tsv. READING: per file, passed/failed/type_errors must be equal across arms. Any new type error in ord (esp. ValueRestriction from an {ord}-only metavar) means PACK-13's defaulting fix ships before the switch. tibia/kit/fiscal come after a clean lib sweep.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_17 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 17u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:34 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: P0 (self-hosting gate: 'conversion gaps unacceptable'), est 600m left, load medium (node sweeps), dep PACK-13 (ord defaulting, installed). DEFINITION OF DONE, all three: (a) lib core switched: the node sweeps sweep-control.tsv vs sweep-ord.tsv (C:\\home\\remote\\libord-cores) show equal passed/failed/type_errors per file over all 73 lib test files, then core.spi:15-21 lands in deps/The-Spiral-Language (unstaged); (b) a lib test 'char_ordering_runs' in lib/spiral/sm'.spi ('a' < 'b', a runtime char compare) is green on rust, gleam, c, zig and fsharp via apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 -File sm'.spi -Test char_ordering_runs; (c) Conv char<->int: !!!!Conv(char -> i32) and back compile on all 17 TyConv emitters, proven by a sample samples/char_scalar_conv (exit 0 on every backend list it joins, agreeing with C). NEXT STEP: when job-1793534174603/-183205 finish (~10:15), diff the two TSVs (Import-Csv, join on file). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: (1) the constraint is a union case COrd in Infer's Constraint (done), never a string tag; (2) the scalar is a TYPE: LitChar of System.Text.Rune in the core (not an int with -1 for 'invalid'; invalid scalars can't be constructed); (3) lib keeps byte-based and scalar-based string offsets apart with PHANTOM nominals, ':! byte_offset = i32' and ':! scalar_offset = i32', so sm'.index takes byte_offset and a new sm'.char_at takes scalar_offset: mixing them is a type error, not a runtime bug; (4) Conv emitters dispatch on a (source CharT, target IntT) pair in a union, not on strings. Model: lib/spiral/unicode.spi (already has unicode_types) and sm'.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_18 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 18u32, "2026-11-01T13:20Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 09:20 compiler: LIB-CORE SWEEP VERDICT = VOID, so no land. Both node sweeps finished (control 83 min, ord 72 min), but 62 of 73 control rows and 61 of 73 ord rows have NO 'tests:' line (unreadable, the lib-gate-needs-test-rows rule: an empty row is never green). The node was loaded (x verify46, fiscal e3-regen, Cells orphans). Readable rows only: control passed 64 / failed 6 / type errors 0; ord passed 149 / failed 0 / type errors 0. In every file readable in both arms (am', erlang_port, erlang_process), ord is equal or better (am' control 12/6 vs ord 18/0, a load failure in control), and NO readable ord row has a new failure or type error. Encouraging, not a gate. NEXT: rerun both arms SEQUENTIALLY in one job when the node is quieter (or on the box in a held window), with lib_sweep reading -RawOut so empty rows can be diagnosed; land only on 73/73 readable rows with equal counts.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_19 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 19u32, "2026-11-01T13:37Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 09:37 compiler: LIB-CORE A/B RERUN started, SEQUENTIAL on an idle node (0 other jobs at start), installed pack14a (node digest 0AF644516C09E959, includes PACK-13's ord defaulting): job-1793540254707, fresh mirror libord-seq (digest E910E536DDABA732). Control core 6631368E734BE060 (= live) then ord core 6892459D72F539D6 -> node C:\\home\\remote\\libord-cores\\seq-{control,ord}.tsv. LAND RULE unchanged: 73/73 readable rows in both arms with equal passed/failed/type_errors per file.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_20 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 20u32, "2026-11-01T14:43Z", "compiler", "11-01 10:43 compiler: sequential A/B job-1793540254707 still in the CONTROL arm after 66 min (started 09:37:35, peak 5.0 GB private); no rows read yet. Land rule unchanged. The groom round did not touch the node.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_21 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 21u32, "2026-11-01T17:43Z", "compiler", "11-01 13:45 compiler: SWEEP DONE, LAND RULE MET. job-1793540254707-compiler-libord-seq: control 09:37-11:52 (73 rows, 0 unreadable), ord 11:52-13:40 (73 rows, 0 unreadable). Per-file comparison of seq-control.tsv vs seq-ord.tsv: 0 of 73 files differ in passed, failed or type_errors. Totals are identical: 627 passed / 61 failed / 0 type errors in both (the 61 failures are the same per file, so the {ord} change is invisible there, as predicted). Arm bases verified on the node: control core.spi 6631368E734BE060 (= live), ord 6892459D72F539D6. LANDING: background shell bfer85gyh waits for the heavy-build window (kit-TRAIN7c compiles with core.spi) and the tree-write window to clear. It then applies scratchpad libord\\core-ord.kitdo under a tree-write window, but only if live core.spi still hashes 6631368E... and the result hashes 6892459D... (the swept bytes); an undo copy goes beside the script. The change is unstaged in deps/The-Spiral-Language. NEXT after the land: gate tibia, kit and fiscal on the new core.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_22 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 22u32, "2026-11-01T19:37Z", "compiler", "11-01 15:35 compiler: LANDED 15:26. deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core/core.spi 6631368E734BE060 -> 6892459D72F539D6 (unstaged in that repo): <=, <, >, >= are now `forall t {ord}` instead of {number}. Landed under tree-write window win-1793561195545, after the heavy-build window (kit-TRAIN7c) cleared, and only after re-checking that the live file still had the swept base hash and the program reproduced the swept ord bytes. Undo copy: session scratchpad libord\\core.spi.undo-6631368E734BE060. CONSUMER GATE RUNNING: `--check` on every tibia (8) and fiscal (82) package with the installed compiler and the new core, under a compile window (scratchpad libord\\consumer-check.ps1, parallel 4). A failure gets the old core as control on that package only, so only differences count. kit's gate is its next release train (it reads core from the same package dir). DONE when tibia and fiscal show no new --check failure and kit's next train has no red that traces to comparisons.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_23 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 23u32, "2026-11-01T20:11Z", "compiler", "11-01 16:12 compiler: CONSUMER GATE GREEN for tibia and fiscal. On the new core 6892459D72F539D6 with the installed compiler, all 90 packages pass: 8 tibia + 82 fiscal; by mode: check 68, compile 22. Packages that hit --check's 30 s cap (exit 3) were re-proven with a full --backend Rust compile, which has no cap. The first attempt (15:3x) was void: my script did not set SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR, so |core- did not resolve and every package was rejected before any type check. No old-core control was needed, since nothing failed. LEFT: kit's next release train on this core (it reads the same core package dir); close when it shows no comparison-related red.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_24 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 24u32, "2026-11-03T07:30Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). Parked to Planned (35 h). Next: compare the DoD with the 11-01 16:12 consumer gate (90/90 packages green) and close it, or name the one missing proof.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_25 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 25u32, "2026-11-03T10:39Z", "compiler", "11-03 06:4x compiler: close check. DoD clause 1 met (11-01 16:12: tibia 8 + fiscal 82 packages, --check green on the ord core). Clause 2 (kit's next train has no red tracing to comparisons) asked of kit directly; Done on its answer.")
inl note_of_char_ordering_26 () = Note ("CHAR-ORDERING", 26u32, "2026-11-03T10:40Z", "compiler", "11-03 06:5x compiler: DONE. DoD clause 2 met per kit: the result logs of all eleven kit trains since the 11-01 15:26 ord core change (TRAIN8, 8s, 9, 10, 11, 13, 13b, 13c, 14, 14b, 14c in kit/.cache/release-train) have 0 hits for {ord}, \"ord constraint\" or ConstraintError; the reds in that window had other causes (TRAIN14 a missing Cargo dep, TRAIN14b a crate-path call in a notebook cell), and TRAIN14c installed green 58/58. Clause 1 was met 11-01 16:12 (tibia 8 + fiscal 82 packages green).")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_1 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 1u32, "2026-10-31T20:10Z", "main", "10-31 16:10 main. PROVEN EQUIVALENT on the node with the installed pack-10 compiler (node = box D1C49B8BDC3318F7; job-1793473912315): 'format --manifest toml --derive' converts the lib to a 126-byte spiral.toml (packages = ['|core-'], prelude = [am'_prototypes, backend, base, iter_prototypes, sm'_prototypes, trace, common], double-quoted) in place of the 1,896-byte module tree; --check over all 110 lib .spi gives the SAME exit under both manifests (110/110, 0 differ); Cells gates under both pending. NOT LANDED because removing package.spiproj breaks tools that read it directly: a grep of C:\\home\\git finds 40+ files naming package.spiproj, among them apps/cells/spi/type_probe.ps1 (requires it in -Lib; used by every lib land's close), Cells manifest.ex/mounts.ex/cells.ex/domain_compiler.ex, compiler scripts env.ps1/test.ps1/try.ps1/test-rust-exports.ps1, kit standards/contracts/bundle/affected/agile-lease code and release-acceptance.ps1/test-regeneration.ps1, tibia scripts/predict.ps1, fiscal src/generator.spi, i574n.github bang-export-rewrite.ps1. PLAN: (1) per tool, resolve the manifest the way Cells' Manifest.path does (spiral.toml preferred, package.spiproj fallback), each team for its own files; (2) then add the lib spiral.toml and archive package.spiproj in one lib land.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_2 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 2u32, "2026-10-31T20:47Z", "main", "10-31 16:47 main: NODE PROOF COMPLETE (job-1793473912315, installed pack-10 compiler D1C49B8BDC3318F7): --check over 110 lib files same exit 110/110; Cells gates identical under package.spiproj and the derived spiral.toml (package.spiproj removed): base 25/26 vs 25/26, listm' 32/32 vs 32/32, process 15/15 vs 15/15, type errors 0 everywhere. The modules-free lib manifest is EQUIVALENT for the compiler and for Cells; what remains is the tool migration in n1's inventory.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_3 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "main", "main 08:4x SCOPE + DoD (compiler flagged this as under-specified in round 4, and it was). SCOPE: only lib/spiral's own manifest moves, from package.spiproj (hand-kept module tree) to spiral.toml with a prelude list and the module order derived by the installed compiler's --resolve-manifest (pack 10; Cells manifest.ex already reads it, EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL Done). x/x_real pairs: until SINGLE-SPI-EXTENSION lands, real modules keep the legacy '*' marker in the derived order; after it, the ///- --real header decides and '*' becomes a warned alias. No other repo's manifest is in scope. THE '40+ TOOLS' FIGURE WAS NEVER COUNTED: a grep (08:4x) finds exactly one script hard-coding lib's package.spiproj, apps/cells/spi/type_probe.ps1, which edits it in its copy to register probe modules. The rest read any package's manifest generically (kit package_inputs/lease, Cells mounts.ex/cells.ex/domain_compiler.ex, the compiler) and must accept spiral.toml. Cells does; kit's package_inputs needs checking. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: manifest = record { dialect; edition; modules : union Listed of module_name list | Derived of prelude : module_name list } with module_name a nominal, and the derived order a value computed once by the compiler, not re-parsed text. DoD: (1) lib/spiral/spiral.toml present and package.spiproj gone; (2) --check over all lib files gives the same exit per file as before (node proof 16:47 had 110/110); (3) a full node lib sweep matches the pre-move sweep verdict per test row (tonight's baseline: 1,236 passed rows); (4) document_test 22/22 and type_probe PASS on a changed file, with type_probe adding probe modules through the toml; (5) kit agile lease's package inputs hash the toml. NEXT STEP: make type_probe.ps1 register probe modules in spiral.toml (its only lib-manifest edit), then check kit's package_inputs.spi against a toml-only lib copy. FIELDS: estimate 360 (unchanged), load light, owner main.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_4 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 4u32, "2026-11-01T14:34Z", "main", "main 10:3x, scope widened by the user (11-01: 'get the conclusion for the best version that'll replace the multiline modules ... (you, without my approval please) then migrate everything, test then delete the spiprojs'). DECISION, main's: spiral.toml with a prelude list only and the module order derived by the compiler (--resolve-manifest, pack 10); no multi-line modules string; the dialect implicit when spiral.toml is present; package.spiproj deleted repo by repo after its gates pass. lib/spiral goes first (this item's DoD n3), then every repo via PACKAGE-MODEL-STANDARD's per-lane list.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_5 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 5u32, "2026-11-02T14:59Z", "main", "main 11-02 lesson from the star exchange (fiscal #1): a lib change that feeds a code generator must regenerate EVERYTHING the generator owns, then run the drift gate, before the land closes. Regenerating only the units in focus left 7 committed files stale and turned fiscal CI red. Applies to SPIPROJ-RETIRE too: fiscal's generator writes package.spiproj into all 81 units.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_6 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 6u32, "2026-11-02T22:51Z", "tibia", "11-02 18:5x tibia, n1 step (1) for tibia's own tools DONE (unstaged): tibia/scripts/predict.ps1 was the only tibia script reading a manifest (it hard-required apps/predict/package.spiproj and src/package.spiproj). It now goes through Set-Manifests: patches packageDir in whichever of spiral.toml / package.spiproj exists (throws only when neither does), and adds -Module to a modules list only where one exists (a prelude-only spiral.toml needs none). Proof on the node mirror (tibia synced d4ad52ad, job predict-toml-1, -Module lanes): compiled and printed lane 0-3 = -1000121800, -1000138000, -1000600000, -1000600000, identical to the pre-change run. live/paths.ps1 only lists .spiproj among source extensions (a hash input), which is fine either way. Tibia's 9 packages still carry both manifests with modules lists; they move to prelude-only and drop package.spiproj under PACKAGE-MODEL-STANDARD once lib's land is done.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_7 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 7u32, "2026-11-03T02:06Z", "compiler", "11-02 22:1x compiler: n1 step (1) for the compiler/Cells tools DONE (unstaged). (a) apps/cells/spi/type_probe.ps1, the one script hard-coding lib's manifest and run in every lib land's close: it takes spiral.toml or package.spiproj in -Lib. Under toml the prelude list stands in for the trailing-dash modules, and since the modules are derived the probe adds no manifest line; it deletes each probe file after its compile so one probe's deliberate control error never reaches the next. Box A/B on metrics.spi: package.spiproj lib PASS, 6 tests typed, control ok; a copy with a prelude-only spiral.toml gives the SAME verdict. (b) apps/compiler/scripts/lib-usage-census.ps1 Get-LibModules reads spiral.toml (modules = the .spi/.spir files, flat = in prelude) and falls back to package.spiproj. CORRECTED 22:5x: format <lib> --manifest toml --derive on a full copy of today's live lib succeeds (exit 0; writes lib/spiral.toml prelude = array_prototypes, backend, base, iter_prototypes, text_prototypes, trace, common, plus bench/parsing/spiral.toml). The earlier refusal came from a root-only zip copy that dropped the subfolder modules; there is no drift.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_8 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 8u32, "2026-11-04T07:32Z", "compiler", "11-04 02:4x compiler: TOOL INVENTORY DONE for the spiral repo and every repo under C:\\home\\git. A grep for code that opens LIB's manifest by path (spiral/package.spiproj, lib/spiral/package*, Join-Path $Lib 'package.spiproj') finds no tool; the only hits are backlog notes. The 33 spiral files that mention package.spiproj write or read their OWN package manifests: test.ps1 linked cases and sample dirs, try.ps1 and lisp build.ps1 (generated app manifests), bench.ps1 (an app that reaches lib through spiral-, which the compiler resolves for either manifest), env.ps1 (core/package.spiproj), Cells domain_compiler.ex (apps/cells/spi), zed (editor project actions), and Cells manifest.ex (which already reads spiral.toml, EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL). type_probe.ps1 and lib-usage-census.ps1 were migrated 11-02; tibia's predict.ps1 on 11-02. Remaining is the switch itself: replace lib/spiral/package.spiproj with the derived prelude-only spiral.toml (format <lib> --manifest toml --derive) and remove package.spiproj, gated by a full lib_gate over every lib file plus Cells and kit regen. lib_land_window already takes package.spiproj as a draft (new modules); with a prelude-only toml, new modules need no registration. It needs a window with no train running (TRAIN19 regens on lib), so the timing is main's call. RE-PROVEN 03:4x on pack33 (box, scratch copy of today's live lib): format <lib> --manifest toml --derive wrote lib/spiral/spiral.toml (packages ['|core-'], prelude array_prototypes, backend, base, iter_prototypes, text_prototypes, trace, common) and bench/parsing/spiral.toml. With both package.spiproj files removed, lib_gate passes nameof_heal.spi (1/1) and convert.spi's widening test (rust/zig/c/pascal), 0 type errors. The switch is ready whenever main opens a no-train window.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_9 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 9u32, "2026-11-04T08:15Z", "main", "11-04 04:1x main: SWITCH PREPARED, waiting for the post-TRAIN20 window (kit holds TRAIN21 for pack34 + this). Dry run on europa AND on the node (both pack33): format <lib> --manifest toml --derive exits 0 and writes lib/spiral.toml (packages = ['|core-'], prelude = array_prototypes, backend, base, iter_prototypes, text_prototypes, trace, common) plus bench/parsing/spiral.toml; the root manifest's 149 hand-kept module lines become a 7-module prelude. A toml-only scratch lib (both package.spiproj removed) sits on the node at C:\\home\\remote-stage\\main-toml-lib\\spiral\\lib for kit's early one-package regen. kit made its regen and train scripts accept spiral.toml (unstaged; TRAIN21 is the full proof). PLAN for the window: run the derive on live lib through lib_land_window (spiral.toml as a new file, package.spiproj removed), close gate = full lib_gate over every lib file plus Cells document_test and kit regen on the node; then TRAIN21.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_10 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 10u32, "2026-11-04T08:49Z", "main", "11-04 04:49 main SWITCHED. lib_land_window (tree-write window) landed lib/spiral/spiral.toml (prelude-only: array_prototypes, backend, base, iter_prototypes, text_prototypes, trace, common; 248E51A0) and bench/parsing/spiral.toml (B035DE1D), and removed both package.spiproj files (hash-guarded 35B16EE6/25C21F6C, kept in the undo dir). Closed green on pack34: 0 consumer-used signatures changed, apps/spiral/spiral.spi compiles, document_test 22/22, type_probe and respell_drift ok. Lib digest 418D97F7F74649CF; undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261104-044527. Before the switch: kit's 5-owner regen on a toml-only scratch lib had identical sizes. Full proof = kit's TRAIN21 (regen over all owners on pack34 + toml lib), launching now. Lanes told: new lib modules need no manifest edit.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_11 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 11u32, "2026-11-04T11:04Z", "tibia", "11-04 07:0x tibia: dropped the plain tibia tag (it made the stop gate list this item as tibia work). tibia's follow-up, moving its 9 packages to prelude-only, is tracked on PACKAGE-MODEL-STANDARD (tibia note 05:0x: inventory plus the standalone-programs risk).")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_12 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 12u32, "2026-11-04T13:52Z", "main", "11-04 09:5x main FIELD EVIDENCE for the toml-only lib: a brand-new module (fin_vector.spi, LIB-VEC-FIN) landed through lib_land_window with NO manifest edit, and Cells, type_probe (\"static lifted: fin_vector\") and the consumer check all found it from the file alone, on the node proof and on the box. The same held for x's doc_blocks/doc_render/list_sort earlier. Still waiting on TRAIN21 as the full regen proof.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_13 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 13u32, "2026-11-04T16:51Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 12:50 fiscal: FISCAL SHARE inventory (no edits yet). (1) fiscal/src keeps BOTH spiral.toml and package.spiproj with the same hand-kept 14-module list (both modified unstaged, in sync: +families document p2p posting reports profiles sunat); src holds exactly those 14 .spi files, so a prelude-only toml would derive the same set. (2) The generator writes a package.spiproj per generated unit (generator.spi:26, module_package) and reads it back (generator.spi:558) -- 69 units + generated_fiscal_runtime, each 'packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib, packages |core- spiral- ../generated_fiscal_runtime-, modules <unit>'. ORDER: (2) changes every generated unit's manifest, so it waits for the joint decode land (generator draft 28372016 is frozen until landed) and then goes in as one generator change proven by a full regen (69 units, the manifest diff only) + a node Cells gate that finds each unit from spiral.toml. (1) is a two-file change (drop package.spiproj, drop the modules list) proven by fiscal's own Cells gate on the node; it waits for the node (busy with the decode gate) and for TRAIN21 confirming toml-only discovery outside lib. README.md:145 names the per-unit package.spiproj and changes with (2).")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_14 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 14u32, "2026-11-04T16:55Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 12:55 fiscal: probe INCONCLUSIVE, do not cite. On europa pack27 I compiled one generated unit (generated_cii_d16b) three ways: its package.spiproj, a prelude-only spiral.toml (no modules list), and a negative control toml WITHOUT ../generated_fiscal_runtime-. All three exit 0 with byte-identical 33447-byte .rs, so the negative control did not bite: a plain compile emits only what an entry point reaches, and the unit uses the runtime (sample_path) only from its test/sample sections, so the manifest's package list was never exercised. The real check stays the node Cells gate on the toml-only units (tests compile there), after the decode land. Probe tree removed.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_15 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 15u32, "2026-11-05T04:27Z", "fiscal", "11-05 00:4x local, fiscal share (1) DONE, in place and unstaged: fiscal/src is toml-only. src/package.spiproj is deleted, and src/spiral.toml keeps edition, packageDir and packages with no modules list. Backups are in the fiscal session scratchpad (spiral.toml.before-toml-only, package.spiproj.before-toml-only). PROOF, europa pack27 dll 21E08222, live box src tgz 4062512A, compiler --check of canon, reports and generator (log ~/remote-stage/fiscal-check/toml-probe-1793852457.log): (a) control with both manifests: 0 diagnostics x3; (b) toml-only: 0 diagnostics x3, identical; (c) negative, toml-only minus fiscal_paths.spi: exit 5 with 41 diagnostics (ETY-UnboundVariable fiscal_paths), so discovery really comes from the files. --check type-checks every top-level def, so this avoids the 11-04 compile probe's reachability gap. Cells side: apps/cells manifest.ex/manifest.spi already try spiral.toml BEFORE package.spiproj, so fiscal Cells runs were already toml-driven and the spiproj was dead weight. Still open: (2) the per-unit package.spiproj that generator.spi:26/558 writes and reads; it goes in with the generator after the decode land. The first fiscal CI run after the user commits this is the end-to-end check (build.ps1 run_notebook).")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_16 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 16u32, "2026-11-05T06:14Z", "fiscal", "FISCAL SHARE (2) DRAFTED + PROVEN, 11-05 02:1x local: drafts\\fiscal\\LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION\\src\\generator.spi 2A9BE918 (base .next6 CB407C1C). module_package dependencies writes a spiral.toml with no modules list: packageDir = '../../deps/spiral/lib'; packages = ['|core-', 'spiral-', '../<dependency unit>-' ...]. write_unit_package (still the single place) deletes a stale package.spiproj and writes spiral.toml. The CFDI read-back test now reads spiral.toml, expects the complement units in it, no modules key, and no package.spiproj left. PROOF europa pack27, dec-gen decode lib (reference xsd_emit hash 5B274B48 checked before and after): generator --check + bodies exit 0. A generated unit (cii_d16b, its test bodies converted to defs so the 9 generated_fiscal_runtime uses are type-checked) with the toml manifest exactly as the draft writes it: 0 diagnostics. NEGATIVE, the same toml without ../generated_fiscal_runtime-: exit 5 with 9 x ETY-UnboundVariable generated_fiscal_runtime. So units resolve their dependencies from the toml packages list; this replaces the inconclusive 11-04 compile probe. A first run of this check at 02:11 was VOID: my earlier scratch trees had written drafts THROUGH dec-gen's absolute lib symlink (memory scratch-tree-copies-keep-symlinks); restored and re-proven. LANDS in the .next6 + families window instead of .next6 (it replaces CB407C1C); the later .next7 / facet generator layers take the same disjoint patch. Every unit's manifest changes in that regen (83 package.spiproj -> spiral.toml).")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_17 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 17u32, "2026-11-05T06:59Z", "fiscal", "Layer carry, 11-05 03:0x local: the toml-manifest patch (.next6 -> 2A9BE918, 25 changed lines) applies cleanly (patch dry-run, then apply) to the two later generator layers, written as NEW files so the proven originals stay: drafts\\fiscal\\GRANULAR-GENERATOR\\src\\generator.next7t.spi AA09ECE1 (= .next7 sentinel + toml) and drafts\\spiral\\XSD-FACET-TYPES\\fiscal\\src\\generator.toml.spi A9622AB3 (= the CE32E90E facet companion + toml). Each has the sentinel match and (the facet one) the defect_check; 2 package.spiproj references remain (the stale-file delete and the one-manifest test); max 1 blank line. PENDING: their --check reruns, plus the identity regens, on the node after its nfe_v4 gate (europa is starved).")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_18 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 18u32, "2026-11-05T07:31Z", "fiscal", "TYPE-CHECKS PASS, 11-05 03:30 local, europa pack27 (deps trees built with cp -rL; reference dec-gen xsd_emit 5B274B48 checked before and after; MemAvailable gate >= 2100 MB). Generator --check + bodies exit 0 for all three: (1) repo_path layer, generator C54163F3 + families 53B36EFD over the decode lib; (2) generator.next7t AA09ECE1 (sentinel + toml) + families F1CF3DDF over the sentinel lib D00BE390; (3) facet companion generator.toml A9622AB3 + families 8472AD8B over the sentinel lib. The identity regens (sentinel vs facet, repo_path vs toml base) stay PENDING: both europa attempts went VOID (memcg kill exit 137; rustc timeout exit 124 after 2402 s while the VM was starved). They move to the node after its nfe_v4 gate.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_19 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 19u32, "2026-11-05T12:01Z", "fiscal", "GENERATOR + FAMILIES WINDOW LANDED in fiscal src, 11-05 08:0x local, in place and unstaged (backups generator.before-granular-land-385DD926.spi and families.before-granular-land-B59F8A10.spi in the session scratchpad): src/generator.spi 385DD926 -> 40DE71DF and src/families.spi B59F8A10 -> 99A8339B, in one window. That brings .next6 (decode + GRANULAR split + generates_cii_d16b back), the toml manifests, repo_path, the row split, documents as data, and bench_parts / write_family_parts; identity proven node-side (0 differing). generated/: all 82 unit manifests converted package.spiproj -> spiral.toml exactly as module_package writes them (dependencies in order; CFDI lists runtime, tfd, pagos), and generated_cii_d16b added (C3C111BE, formatter-stable) with its toml. README line 145 now names the per-unit spiral.toml. fiscal working tree: 222 changed paths. PENDING: the CI run after the user commits (generator tests regenerate units and diff; the generator's own CFDI test now checks spiral.toml).")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_20 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 20u32, "2026-11-05T23:38Z", "fiscal", "11-05 19:3x local fiscal: fiscal's share is complete in the working tree. src has only spiral.toml, and all 83 generated units carry spiral.toml (the facet window placed today keeps them). The 2 remaining package.spiproj references, generator.spi's stale-manifest delete and its 'a unit keeps one manifest' test, must stay until the user commits the converted units: HEAD's generated/ still has package.spiproj per unit, and a fresh CI checkout relies on that delete. They can go in the first change after that commit.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_21 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 21u32, "2026-11-06T08:24Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-06 (for SPIPROJ-RETIRE census): fiscal reads NO package.spiproj; src + all 83 generated units have spiral.toml. The 2 remaining textual references are guards in src/generator.spi: :27 deletes a stale unit package.spiproj on regen, :684 fails if a unit keeps both manifests. Both can go once no lane's tree has a spiproj; they never read its content.")
inl note_of_lib_spiral_toml_migration_22 () = Note ("LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION", 22u32, "2026-11-06T13:22Z", "main", "main 11-06 12:3x DONE, proof: lib/spiral holds spiral.toml and no package.spiproj (Test-Path false/true); git grep for 'lib/spiral/package.spiproj' finds 0 references in the 11 own repos (backlog excluded); dep EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL is Done; spiral's push CI at c0e77c3 (01:54Z) is green with the lib on spiral.toml, and every lib notebook gated there. The fiscal generator's 2 package.spiproj guards are fiscal's (they delete or refuse a stale unit manifest and never read lib's) and go with SPIPROJ-RETIRE.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 1u32, "2026-10-31T23:14Z", "tibia-lane", "10-31 19:1x tibia: PULLED and running as job-1793488430712-tibia-lib-consumer-regate on the node, cap 4 h. Mirror tibia-gates: tibia synced (REMOTE-SYNC-OK head 6859e568, digest 5F4BD53B; includes the box's unstaged tibia edits: the 34 lambda-parens sites, the postable gate and pz-in-traces), polyglot synced (head d01f61ec), and the mirror's tibia\\deps\\spiral and \\polyglot junctioned to the MIRROR copies, so tibia builds against the post-land spiral (digest 070CB919, the same lib the post-land gate passed). The runner is tibia\\scripts\\build.ps1 -Label postland: layout, postable, then every test file. Compare with K4 (TIBIA-EDITION3-ROUND n17: 56 files GREEN on 10-30).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T02:58Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 00:xx tibia: VERDICT RED, a consumer regression in the lib, not in tibia's lands. job-1793488430712 (221 min, node mirror tibia-gates): build.ps1 reports 32 of 56 test files not fully green. 24 of those have only unsupported targets on the node (no failures); 8 files have real failures: activity 2, the host actor-trace test file 7, character 6, map 3, scan 1, scenario 3, world_executor and world_port 2. ROOT (148 ETY errors, 197 Unbound): ETY-ModuleIndexFailed \"The module does not have the key: started\" at apps/host/world_port.spi:16 (type process.started), cascading into tibia's own types world, maybe_world, stepped and drive. process.spi still defines `:! started` directly under `:== started_switch` (the same adjacency as on 10-30 fd70dccf, when K4 passed); the difference is that main's headings wave removed the `/// ### started` heading above that cell. Hypothesis: the Cells module index keys the cell by heading or first definition, so `started` is no longer exported. 26 lib sites share the shape (async, base pair/choice2'/exn, channel sender/receiver, common disposable, convert, date_time, am' and more). Reported to main (owner of the headings tool and Cells) with the evidence. Separate: scan's C row hits \"a lib backend_switch with no arm for the C backend\" (an arm gap, owner to identify). tibia's 6 lands do not touch these regions (their post-land gate passed, POSTABLE n159). Next: rerun this job once main fixes or refutes the cause.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 3u32, "2026-11-01T03:11Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 tibia: n2's ROOT CAUSE IS RETRACTED. main's box probe (pack-11 compiler, live process.spi BE258A03) shows no ETY for process.started. On the box, tibia's build.ps1 -Files world_port is 2/2 GREEN (\"all 1 test files green\"). The node red came from the node's pre-pack-11 compiler: the job started about 19:14, and pack 11 reached the node at 20:29. The headings hypothesis was wrong: the compiler ignores comments, so a heading cannot change the module index. Lesson: before calling a node red a lib regression, check the node compiler digest (main named CC1F29DA5A8718F1, the pack 11 folder) and reproduce on the box. Next: resync the mirror, confirm the node compiler digest, rerun the full consumer build. Status back to Active.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 4u32, "2026-11-01T03:43Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 tibia: this is the NODE-RUNNABLE item. Rerun in flight: job-1793503032785-tibia-lib-consumer-regate2. Node compiler DFC86B91 = the box's (pack 11, active since 20:29); mirrors re-synced (spiral 587b3541 digest 67699CFF, tibia digest B237F3A9, polyglot); tibia deps junctions point to the mirror copies.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 5u32, "2026-11-01T04:59Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 02:xx tibia: ROOT CAUSE FOUND for both node reds (supersedes n2's hypothesis and n3's compiler explanation). regate2 reproduced the same 148 ETY errors with the node on the box's compiler (DFC86B91), so the compiler was not the cause. Cells resolved lib through tibia\\deps\\polyglot\\deps\\spiral\\lib\\spiral (Cells mount a675b8d7..., its spiral junction). In the node mirror, polyglot's deps linked to the node's SHARED polyglot deps, whose spiral is the node's shared C:\\home\\git\\spiral checkout: process.spi F75DF957 from 10-25. On the box that link goes to live spiral. So the node typed tibia against a 10-25 lib; today's lib is not regressed (box: world_port 2/2). FIX in the runner (stage consumer2.ps1): the mirror's polyglot\\deps becomes a real folder (spiral -> mirror spiral, every other entry -> shared), tibia\\deps\\spiral and \\polyglot -> mirror copies, all links unlinked before each sync (git clean must never meet a junction), and the hash of each route's process.spi is printed before the build. Rerun in flight on the respelled tree: job tibia-respell-full-build.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 6u32, "2026-11-01T07:45Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 03:45 tibia, node RESPELL FULL BUILD 2 (job-1793510072271): mirror tibia-gates synced from head 6859e568 with REMOTE-SYNC-OK; both lib routes on process.spi BE258A03. Exit 1 after 144 min, 30 of 56 files not green, in TWO classes. (A) MY BUG, from a respell cell split: apps/host/character.spi:119-120 put a '^^' continuation inside '( ... )' at column 5, left of the paren contents, so the parser closed early ('Expected: )'). Every host importer failed with it: activity, block_plan, character, scenario, world_executor, world_port (c 17, lua 11, gleam 5 failed; the rest not_run). Fixed with the file's own idiom: an unparenthesised ^^ fixture expected_kit_tail_rows. Local proof: build.ps1 -Files character,world_port gave 6/6 + 2/2. The split had passed the cell-budget check, but I had never run that file's tests, which is the lesson. (B) NODE ENV, not respell: 29 wgsl tests unsupported in about 20 files, because the mirror has no target/wgsl-run runner (build outputs aren't synced). Every other file is green on the respelled tree, including the new test-discovery regex. RELAUNCHED as job-1793519129551-tibia-respell-full-build3 at 03:4x on head b1a02bcb (REMOTE-SYNC-OK). Its runner now streams: the '| Select-Object -Last 80' trim was removed from consumer2.ps1, which in build 2 had dropped every early file's details.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 7u32, "2026-11-01T07:57Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 03:57 tibia: CI on the user's 02:36 commit b1a02bcb is RED on both platforms (gh-pages run 41281548884): 8 of 55 files, the same 8 on Windows and Linux. 7 are host importers of character.spi failing on its paren split (character.spi:120:5 'Expected: )'): activity, arena_host, block_plan, character, scenario, world_executor, world_port. The 8th is apps/suite, committed mid-iteration with the nested-update type error (suite.spi:68). BOTH ARE FIXED IN THE TREE, unstaged: character green locally (6/6, plus world_port 2/2), suite 7/7. CI's wgsl runner works (0 unsupported), so the node's 29 wgsl rows are node-only. The previous red run (6859e568, 10-31) was the tibia/postability read, fixed since. Next CI goes green once the user commits the tree; node build 3 (job-1793519129551) proves all 56 files first.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 8u32, "2026-11-01T09:07Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 05:07 tibia, node BUILD 3 VERDICT (job-1793519129551, mirror tibia-gates on head b1a02bcb + tree, REMOTE-SYNC-OK, lib routes on process.spi BE258A03, streaming runner, 73 min): 57 files, 0 failures from the respell or the character fix. 33 fully green; 23 differ only by the 29 wgsl rows that are unsupported on the node (no runner there; CI runs them). 1 REAL FAILURE: world_executor gleam, a_block_runs_through_real_programs_and_matches_the_simulated_trace. Its trace held a stray 'rerun' that sat at a different position on the node than locally: a SHARED FILE RACE that I introduced. My drift heal moved both tests' logs to fixed temp names (tibia-world-executor-block.log), and Cells runs the two tests concurrently, so each test read the other's lines. FIX: fake_log tag kind gives every test its own run and block log ('counted', 'sleeping'). Local: 2/2. So the respelled tree is green apart from the node's wgsl gap; three of my own regressions were caught and fixed (character paren, suite nested update, world_executor shared temp log), all unstaged, waiting for the user's commit.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 9u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 08:36 tibia QUALITY PASS. DEFINITION OF DONE, the remaining 10%: the gh-pages run on the user's next tibia commit (which includes character.spi, world_executor.spi, suite.spi and build.ps1) is green on both build (windows-2025) and build (ubuntu-24.04), i.e. 'build.ps1 / all N test files green' in both logs. NEXT STEP: after the user commits, 'gh run list --limit 1' then 'gh run view <id> --log-failed'; if green, set 100 Done here with the run id; if red, the failing file's detail lines go in a note. No typefeatureset work (proof item).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 10u32, "2026-11-01T13:42Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 09:42 tibia: the 3 changed Spiral files RE-PROVEN on the box's pack14a compiler (installed 09:31; it fixes pack 12's Rust E0425 'st = GroupN::' regression). build.ps1 -Files suite,character,world_executor: suite 7/7 (lua + rust), character 6/6 (c, lua, gleam), world_executor 2/2 (gleam), memo_hits 0 on all three, so nothing was replayed from pack12b. Tonight's node logs (builds 2 and 3) contain no E0425, so no tibia rows were VOIDed. The unstaged set is unchanged and still waits for the user's commit, then CI.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 11u32, "2026-11-01T14:47Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 10:47 tibia: CI on the user's 10:01 commit 8c8f6c3 (gh-pages run 41314129444). build (ubuntu-24.04) is RED, 4 of 55 files, and NONE are tonight's fixes: the 8 files red on b1a02bcb are all green now. The 4 are combat_text (5 unsupported), game_calls (3), ground_trace (1) and samples (1), all in apps/arena and apps/canary, unpinned tests that default to ZIG. The detail line is 'ground_trace.spi: zig failed (exit 1) / main.zig:160:25: error: expected ';' after statement'. These files are unchanged since b1a02bcb, whose Linux run passed them with the same respelled source, so this is a ZIG CODEGEN REGRESSION in the compiler CI builds from spiral main, routed to compiler. windows-2025 is still running at 10:47. The item stays at 90 until both jobs are green. UPDATE 11:0x: build (windows-2025) finished RED with the SAME 4 files and the same 'main.zig:160:25: error: expected ';' after statement'. So both platforms fail only on the Zig codegen regression, and all 51 other files are green on both, including every file touched tonight. The DoD's 'all N test files green' now waits only on the compiler fix. CORRECTION (compiler, 11:1x): my 'Zig codegen regression' attribution was WRONG. Root cause: in both OS jobs, spiral's apps/spiral/build.ps1 threw at line 27 compiling the spiral CLI ('TracedError: Cannot convert i64 to the following type: time_span', lib/spiral/convert.spi:75 via try_convert's Zig arm), after today's 08:42 lib land CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT routed try_convert through numeric_conv for nominal targets. With no spiral tool, Cells marked those cells tool_missing, which shows as 'unsupported'. The main.zig:160 line is a long-standing red herring (ground_trace's unpinned main pulls a Lua-only macro into a default-Zig build; the box prints it with pack11 too while passing 2/2). Compiler is landing the fix and tracking it on ZIG-EXPECTED-SEMICOLON-REGRESSION. My error: I read the first per-file detail line instead of the job's earlier setup steps, and the 'unsupported' status (not 'failed') should have pointed me at a missing tool.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_consumer_regate_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE", 12u32, "2026-11-01T17:14Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 DoD met: CI run 41326996580 on 7d47bad (suite.spi retype + devcontainer) completed success on build (windows-2025), build (ubuntu-24.04) and push. The spiral CLI build, broken by CONVERT-ZIG-UNATIVEINT on run 41314129444, is fixed upstream, and the 4 files that went unsupported there now run. Closing.")
inl note_of_cells_census_1 () = Note ("CELLS-CENSUS", 1u32, "2026-10-31T22:02Z", "x", "2026-10-31 x: slice 0 of CELLS-IN-SPIRAL, read-only (CPU-light). For each of the 44 lib .ex files and 13 mix tasks: class (a) pure logic, (b) process/OTP, (c) Kino/Livebook-facing, (d) mix task; what already lives in apps/cells/spi (Domain exports); its callers inside Cells and outside (every repo and lane: grep Spiral.Cells.X and :spiral_cells); the test files that pin it; and a parity oracle for its slice (e.g. Document: every tracked .spi in every repo parsed by both implementations; compiler_client: the daemon line protocol replayed). Output: an ordered slice list with estimates by rule (lines per class), recorded as notes on the epic; the first slice filed with its pre-registered rule. Also: how Livebook loads smart cells (behaviour callbacks, module naming) to choose the aliasing trick, proven by a one-module Gleam smart cell loaded in a Livebook-less Kino test.")
inl note_of_cells_census_2 () = Note ("CELLS-CENSUS", 2u32, "2026-10-31T22:29Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:3x x: census of spiral/apps/cells/lib (44 .ex files, 11,413 lines; marks: Proc = GenServer/Task/spawn/Port/gen_tcp/Supervisor/receive/System.cmd, Kino, Domain.):\n- a pure (19 files, 3,572 lines): Memo 643, Builders 486, FsiChain 277, Display 251, XCompat 235, Html 232, Document 208 (17 Domain. calls, already half Spiral), NotebookCli 188, Toolchain 174, Domain 170, Accept 162, Manifest 130, SourceBlocks 91, Directives 66, FileLock 66, SourceMap 65, Cell 44, NewProject 42, Lisp 42.\n- b process (11 files, 5,727): Notebook 1,452, CompilerDaemon 1,184 (62 marks), Targets 860, CompilerClient 825 (40), Mounts 442, Runner 387 (29), CompilerPool 250, Slots 124, Server 88, MemoryGuard 82, Tables 33.\n- c kino-facing (5 files, 1,856): Spiral.Cells 1,166, FsiSession 367, SmartCell 230, Result 77, Application 16.\n- d mix tasks (9 files, 258): Notebook, Targets, Edit, RenderCells, XCompat, New, CompilerDaemon, Html, CellsServer.\nBackend map by BACKEND-BALANCE's rule (memory backend-balance, written before this census):\n- b: Gleam. CompilerDaemon (G1 state for hours, G3 owns OS children, G4 messages), CompilerPool and Slots (G2, G3), Runner (G2 parallel runs, G3 deadlines), MemoryGuard (G3), Server (G1, G4). Targets and CompilerClient run inside that BEAM node and talk to it by messages (G4), so they go Gleam too. Mounts is a tie: it keeps its current runtime (BEAM).\n- a: backend-neutral Spiral domain whose test blocks run on rust AND gleam (the rule's tie clause). Memo's hashing is the only R1 candidate, and it moves only after a measurement.\n- c: Gleam with Elixir-named modules (aliasing or beam rename), because Kino/Livebook load Elixir.Spiral.Cells.* by name.\n- d: no mix. Each task is a per-command CLI, which is R2 (BEAM cold start measured 15-50x a Rust CLI), so it becomes a thin Rust CLI that talks to the long-lived Gleam server (CellsServer). This is the iggy split (IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE, LIB-IGGY-CLIENT): Rust on the per-call data path, Gleam for supervision and state, joined by ONE Spiral GADT message family with byte-identical Rust and Gleam codecs, the same way lib/spiral/iggy.spi frames job events.\nTypefeatureset design for the slices (user 10-31, TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE n6): the daemon protocol is a request GADT indexed by its reply type, like x's wire.spi (reads r / writes r), so a client can't misread a reply. Targets are a GADT over target kind, so a test block's target and its builder agree by type. The memo key carries phantom indices for source digest and compiler core, so a stale core can't hit the cache. Notebook evaluation moves through typestate phantoms (parsed, planned, ran). Kino-facing values cross the Elixir boundary as an existential any_cell.\nSlice order stays parse-first: Document, the a-pure Directives/Cell/SourceBlocks/SourceMap, then Memo, then the daemon protocol GADT with CompilerClient. Main owns compiler_client/compiler_daemon/targets/x_compat items (window 6 hashes in main's 10-31 message), so a slice that rewrites one of them is announced to main first.")
inl note_of_cells_census_3 () = Note ("CELLS-CENSUS", 3u32, "2026-10-31T22:48Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:48 x: CELLS-CENSUS finished (callers, tests, oracles, aliasing proof).\nKino aliasing PROVEN on the node (gleam 1.19.2). Pass rule written before the run: Elixir prints hi! and Spiral.CellsProbe. Setup: a two-module Gleam project (cells_probe/inner.shout; cells_probe/kino_entry.eval calling inner). Gleam 1.19 no longer keeps .erl artefacts (only .abstr and .beam), so the rename runs at BEAM level. beam_lib:chunks(Beam, [abstract_code]) gives raw_abstract_v1 forms. Replace {attribute, L, module, _} with 'Elixir.Spiral.CellsProbe', then compile:forms(Forms, [binary, debug_info]), and write Elixir.Spiral.CellsProbe.beam. Then elixir -pa <alias> -pa <gleam ebin> probe.exs printed 'hi!' and 'Spiral.CellsProbe': KINO-ALIAS-OK. Rule for the c slices: each Kino-facing module is a Gleam FACADE at the top of the call graph (nothing in Gleam calls it), renamed to its Elixir.Spiral.Cells.* name after gleam build. Its callbacks (Kino.SmartCell init/handle_connect/to_attrs/to_source) are plain exports, and calls out to Kino go through @external(erlang, \"Elixir.Kino...\", ...).\nOutside callers of Spiral.Cells.* (ripgrep, excluding apps/cells, deps, _build, target, tmp):\n- alphabet/apps/hangul/hangul.livemd uses Spiral.Cells.SmartCell and Spiral.Cells.eval (4 cells each): the Kino contract to keep.\n- x/apps/sim/spi/cells_executor.spi calls Notebook (3), CompilerDaemon (1) and Document (1). That is why sim-run.ps1 still compiles Cells with mix, and those three slices free x's sim of Elixir.\n- kit/src/kit_machine_watch_model/model.spi:188 names Spiral.Cells.CompilerDaemon. When the daemon moves to Gleam its process name changes, so the kit team is told in that slice.\nTests (46 .exs files) by slice:\n- Directives: directives_test.\n- Cell: cell_test.\n- Document: document_test (611).\n- Builders/Targets: builders_test, targets_test, targets_test_name_test, notebook_targets_test.\n- Memo: memo_test, memo_deps_test, memo_tool_identity_test.\n- Daemon: compiler_daemon_test plus the idle, retire-race and status tests, warm_notebook_test.\n- Pool, Slots, Runner, Tables: compiler_pool_test, slots_test, runner_test, tables_test.\n- Mounts: mounts_name, mounts_patched, mounts_relative.\n- Notebook: notebook_test plus the report, metrics, lib_snapshot, spiral_lib, gate_args, livemd_target and timeout_headroom tests.\n- Kino: smart_cell_test, display_test, fsi_session_test, unit_first_test.\n- Other: accept, html, lisp, manifest, new_project, x_compat, postability_profile.\nOracle per slice:\n- a-pure: the slice's .exs tests pass unchanged against the Spiral-backed module, AND the old and new export give byte-equal output over every tracked .spi (Directives prepare, Document parse/to_spi round trip, Memo keys).\n- b: their .exs tests, plus x's ladder-cells engine parity and a CELLS-GATE corpus run with equal pass, fail and memo-hit counts.\n- c: smart_cell_test plus hangul.livemd evaluated headless.\n- d: each CLI's stdout and exit equal the mix task's on the same arguments.\nThe probe script was a one-shot. Its method is above, and the file is archived (no new .ps1 under CELLS-IN-SPIRAL, per main's 10-31 request).")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_1 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 1u32, "2026-10-31T22:39Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:4x x: slice 1 of CELLS-IN-SPIRAL, the precondition for the typefeatureset migration (CELLS-IN-SPIRAL n2). apps/cells/spi (text, cell, directives, document, notebook, targets, main; 2,157 lines) references only core-, so it hand-rolls tokens, lines and text helpers and can't use option, list, resultm, listm or sm'. Applier i574n.github/scripts/x-cells-lib-ref.ps1 -Cells <apps/cells> [-Apply] adds 'packageDir: ../../../lib' and 'spiral-' (the shape of apps/compiler/spi/package.spiproj). Dry run checked against today's tree. No lib module shares a name with the seven Cells modules. Pass rule, registered before the run: on a node scratch copy (node_cells_window_proof.ps1 with this applier), mix compile regenerates domain.gleam and the full mix test result equals the same tree's without the applier. Failures that pass alone on --failed reruns are load reds (CELLS-SUITE-FLAKY-UNDER-LOAD). Then land through cells_land_window.ps1 with a train-hold. package.spiproj is not on main's window-6 list, so no hand-over is needed.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_2 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 2u32, "2026-10-31T22:50Z", "x", "2026-10-31 18:50 x: no new .ps1 under CELLS-IN-SPIRAL (main's 10-31 request), so the applier lives here and its file is archived. At land time, write it to a temp file for cells_land_window.ps1 -Applier, the same way land-lib-program.ps1 handles note-stored programs. APPLIER (-Cells <apps/cells> [-Apply]):\nparam([Parameter(Mandatory)] [string] $Cells, [switch] $Apply)\n$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'\n$project = Join-Path $Cells 'spi/package.spiproj'\n$before = [IO.File]::ReadAllText($project)\nif ($before -match '(?m)^\\s+spiral-\\s*$') { 'x-cells-lib-ref / already references spiral-'; exit 0 }\nif ($before -notmatch '(?m)^packages:\\r?\\n\\s+\\|core-\\r?\\n') { throw \"x-cells-lib-ref / unexpected package.spiproj shape in $project\" }\n$after = \"packageDir: ../../../lib`n\" + ($before -replace '(?m)^(\\s+)\\|core-(\\r?\\n)', '$1|core-$2$1spiral-$2')\nif (!$Apply) { 'x-cells-lib-ref / dry run, would write:'; $after; exit 0 }\n[IO.File]::WriteAllText($project, $after)\n\"x-cells-lib-ref / applied to $project\"")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_3 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 3u32, "2026-10-31T23:28Z", "x", "2026-10-31 19:28 x: proof x-cells-spi-lib1 started (job-1793489269930, xqueue3, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7). One job runs the full mix test on the synced tree (baseline), applies n2's edit inline, runs the full mix test again, then restores package.spiproj. The pass rule in n1 is unchanged: same result as the baseline, and failures that pass alone on --failed reruns count as load reds.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_4 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 4u32, "2026-11-01T00:56Z", "x", "2026-10-31 20:56 x: x-cells-spi-lib1 (job-1793489269930, 78 min, xqueue3, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7) by n1's rule. Compile with spiral- gives exit 0, domain.gleam regenerated (1,057,403 bytes), so the lib resolves and no module name collides. The full-run totals are cut off in the tail. After the two --failed rounds, 8 fail on each side.\n- Shared (7): LispTest x2, DocumentTest x3 (dice and alphabet are missing from a spiral-only mirror, as in CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES n2), FsiSessionTest per-cell interaction, TargetsTest gleam build without a kept lock.\n- Baseline only: CellsTest 'a missing pinned Rust toolchain is installed once' (cells_test.exs:698).\n- With spiral- only: TargetsTest 'gleam cells with the same manifest run in a warm package' (targets_test.exs:321).\nVerdict by the rule as written: NOT MET (the sets differ by one test each). Reading: both differing tests are toolchain and warm-slot plumbing that the Spiral domain package doesn't reach, and the node ran 3 jobs then, so they look like load reds (CELLS-SUITE-FLAKY-UNDER-LOAD). Discriminating rule, written before running: run both tests ALONE on the now-idle mirror in both states (base, then with spiral-). If each test gives the same outcome in both states, the lib reference changes nothing observable and the slice may land under a train-hold. If a test passes in base and fails with spiral-, it doesn't land and that test is investigated.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_5 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 5u32, "2026-11-01T01:05Z", "x", "2026-10-31 21:05 x: discriminating run by n4's rule. The first attempt measured nothing: [IO.File] with a relative path resolved against the process cwd, so the state never switched (memory ps-dotnet-relative-paths). Rerun with absolute paths, mirror idle, then package.spiproj restored and checked:\n- base: cells_test.exs:698 (Rust toolchain) passes; targets_test.exs:321 (gleam warm package) FAILS.\n- with spiral-: :698 FAILS; :321 passes.\nBy n4 AS WRITTEN: NOT MET, since :698 passed in base and failed with spiral- here, so the slice does not land on this evidence. Reading: the full run showed the exact inverse (:698 failed in base, :321 failed with spiral-). Each test has now failed once in each state, the signature of flakes independent of the package reference. That reading was formed after the results, so it decides nothing.\nNew rule, written before running: three interleaved rounds (base, lib, base, lib, base, lib), both tests alone each round, idle mirror. LAND iff no test fails ONLY in the lib state across the 3+3 runs (failures in both states, or in neither, mean a flake or a pass). If :698 fails in all 3 lib runs and passes in all 3 base runs (or :321 does), it doesn't land and that test is read for a lib-dependent path. Flakes in :698 and :321 also go to CELLS-SUITE-FLAKY-UNDER-LOAD.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_6 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 6u32, "2026-11-01T01:24Z", "x", "2026-10-31 21:24 x: 3-round interleaved run by n5's rule. Each round printed package.spiproj's first line, so the state switch is attested (base 'packages:', lib 'packageDir: ../../../lib'), and it was restored after.\n- targets_test.exs:321 failed in 6 of 6 runs (3 base, 3 lib).\n- cells_test.exs:698 failed in base rounds 1 and 2 and lib round 1, so 2 of 3 base and 1 of 3 lib.\nNo test fails ONLY in the lib state: PASS. apps/cells/spi referencing lib/spiral changes no observed test outcome, the compile is clean, and domain.gleam regenerates.\nLand plan: through cells_land_window.ps1 with the n2 applier text written to a temp file, a train-hold around it, and kit window list checked for a held Cells tree-write. Not during tonight's night run, which reads apps/cells live, so the next morning window. Landing is a 2-line package.spiproj change; CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES lands in the same window.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_7 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 7u32, "2026-11-01T06:54Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:54 x: landing is now ONE window with CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES, so its plan isn't duplicated here: CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES n5 holds the runbook and the applier, which also writes this item's package.spiproj change (packageDir ../../../lib + spiral-, the same shape n6 proved). Verified tonight with no live writes: the anchors are found exactly once and the output equals the proven shapes. The combined state (lib reference + typed drafts) gets its first proof in that window's test run. This item closes on that landing.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_8 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 8u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:36 x: ITEM UPGRADE (round 4). Fields: tags gain team:x; 90 / medium / agent stand (medium because the land window runs the Cells suite). DEFINITION OF DONE: package.spiproj carries 'packageDir: ../../../lib' + 'spiral-' in the live tree, landed through CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES n5's window, which closes green. Artifact: the landed package.spiproj hash. Evidence so far: n6 (3-round interleaved check, no test fails only with the lib). NEXT STEP: the same morning window. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: this slice adds no types; it's the precondition that lets later slices use lib option, result, list, listm and sm' in place of the package's hand-rolled tokens/lines/text and its 0/1 i32 flags. The first consumer is the directives v2 follow-up: tokens become 'list string' and flags.packages become 'list string', with no tab joins.")
inl note_of_cells_spi_lib_9 () = Note ("CELLS-SPI-LIB", 9u32, "2026-11-01T19:52Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:50 x: LANDED and closed green (cells_land_window x-cells-c1c2, 15:47, train-hold x-cells-c1c2 taken and released, window win-1793562439067). The pre-land baseline on the same live tree (dice and alphabet present) was 45 passed, 0 failed across directives_test, cell_test, notebook_test and document_test. After landing: 45 passed. n2's rule (equal to the pre-land result) is met. Landed hashes: package.spiproj 73D9D8FAA3E9, cell.spi 1C2358BB8E63, notebook.spi 74F7478DFDD2, directives.spi F00ABD35C3D6. The live domain.gleam was regenerated at 15:47:48 (0 NoToken, 5 MainFromExpression). Undo: spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\cells-20261101-154718. Unstaged in spiral for the user's commit: apps/cells/spi/{package.spiproj,cell.spi,notebook.spi,directives.spi}. The appliers are archived (one-shot). DONE.")
inl note_of_spl2spi_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("SPL2SPI-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-31T22:58Z", "main", "10-31 18:58 main, created in the pre-night groom so the first PS1-TO-SPIRAL step is pullable (the epic is assignee=pair). SOURCE: apps/lisp/spl2spi.ps1 (~120 lines: an S-expression reader, then printers for types, patterns, expressions and the nominal/type/union/inl top forms). ORACLE ready: the 3 samples' outputs (membership, membership_short, membership_wrong) were byte-identical between the .py and the .ps1 on 10-31, so the Spiral version must reproduce the same .spi bytes for the same .spl. DONE WHEN: build.ps1 calls the Spiral program, the 3 samples are byte-identical to today's outputs, RUST-OK for membership/membership_short and the rejection for membership_wrong, and spl2spi.ps1 is archived. No new .ps1 (spiral-first-tooling).")
inl note_of_spl2spi_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("SPL2SPI-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-11-03T16:31Z", "main", "main 11-03 12:4x PORTED (unstaged, spiral apps/lisp): spl2spi.spi (edition 3, 0 kit findings) with typed tokens (token = Open|Close|Word|Quoted), a typed tree (sexp = Atom|Text|Items, rec), typed reader outcomes (parsed = Parsed|Unclosed; read = Forms|Unbalanced) instead of exceptions, and one printer per ps1 function (format_type/pattern/expr with a :& group for if/match/ctor, union/inl/nominal/type tops); 4 rust tests (inl signature+body, union forall + nested parens, match arms, comments + unbalanced in both directions) + package.spiproj. ORACLE: the Spiral binary's output is BYTE-IDENTICAL to spl2spi.ps1 on all 3 samples (membership 66A6B69D, membership_short 46F0F4B2, membership_wrong 5671E9EE), first run. build.ps1 now builds spl2spi from Spiral (BuildSpiral + rustc) and uses it. ALSO FIXED: Cells' lib/spiral/cells/lisp.ex still shelled out to python apps/lisp/spl2spi.py, deleted on 10-31, so ///! lisp cells (and lisp_test.exs) could not work; it now runs target/build/lisp/spl2spi/spl2spi[.exe] and names the build step when it is missing. PENDING: europa notebook gate + full lisp build.ps1 (RUST-OK x2, membership_wrong rejected), then delete spl2spi.ps1.")
inl note_of_spl2spi_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("SPL2SPI-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-11-03T16:45Z", "main", "main 11-03 12:4x DONE. Europa: spl2spi notebook 4/4 (the program's main moved to the file's end and marked to skip the notebook run, since a main cell defaults to Zig and env args have no Zig arm; memory notebook-main-default-zig). apps/lisp build.ps1 builds spl2spi from Spiral and passes: RUST-OK membership, RUST-OK membership_short, membership_wrong rejected (ETY-TermError, as designed). Byte identity re-proven under pack26 too (spl2spi.rs 61193 -> 60329 bytes, outputs unchanged). spl2spi.ps1 DELETED (unstaged D; git history keeps it; git grep finds no reference). First PS1-TO-SPIRAL slice done.")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_1 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 1u32, "2026-10-31T23:29Z", "kit", "10-31 19:3x kit, from the user's 'what about these .rows and tsv files? also look like drift, try standardizing better, heal their origin'. STANDARD: data a repo keeps is typed Spiral rows (a .spi module of union constructors read by a typed parser, like docs/backlog/state/agile.spi); run outputs and metrics go to target/ or a scratchpad, never tracked. ORIGIN: each text table was invented by a pwsh writer with no typed home, the same drift as PS1-TO-SPIRAL. INVENTORY AND DECISIONS: (1) kit/standards/lint-macros.rows (907+2) and lint-macros-lib.rows (290), written by lint macros' old text baseline -> kit, NOW: 'kit lint macros <paths> --baseline <old.rows> --write-baseline <new.spi>' converts the accepted state exactly (rows become 'inl lint_known_<n> () = LintKnown (file, name, class)'), and '--baseline <x.spi>' reads it back typed; the .rows get archived once the candidate shows new 0 on both. (2) infra/a/iggy-client/fixtures.tsv: duplicate of wire.rs mod fixtures -> archived 10-31; the 10 pairs become lib iggy.spi test data under LIB-IGGY-CLIENT (kit). (3) tibia/results/*.tsv, 61 tracked arena run outputs appended by live/run-arena.ps1, read by arena-summary.ps1 and scripts/score.ps1 -> tibia: results become a typed results module (one constructor per run row) written by the Spiral arena runner when tibia's PS1-TO-SPIRAL port lands; tibia is parked, so no change before it resumes. (4) spiral/apps/cells/spi/lib_consumer_signatures.tsv (untracked, 14 hand-written rows, read by main's lib_land_window.ps1) -> main: becomes typed rows inside the kit command that replaces lib_land_window (PS1-TO-SPIRAL step 2). (5) tibia/scripts/build.ps1 metrics (files/tests/wgsl/machine .tsv) go under target/, which is already right. GUARD: ~/.claude/hooks/kit-work-in-repo.ps1 now advises on any new .tsv/.rows/.csv under C:\\home\\git (and on a new .ps1; a new .py is denied).")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_2 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 2u32, "2026-11-01T01:18Z", "kit", "10-31 21:2x kit: part (1) DONE in the tree (ships with TRAIN7): kit lint baselines are typed .spi (GRANULAR-LINT n-latest has the proof); .rows archived. Part (2) DONE: iggy fixtures landed as lib iggy.spi frozen_bytes_1/_2 (LIB-IGGY-CLIENT n21, B438384D08C9E048). Parts (3) tibia results and (4) main's lib_consumer_signatures wait on their PS1-TO-SPIRAL ports, owners named in n1. Guard hook in place.")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_3 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 3u32, "2026-11-01T03:43Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 tibia NEXT STEP for part (3) (CPU-light): list tibia's tracked text tables (results/*.tsv, .rows and .txt data), sort them into kept data (-> typed Spiral rows in a .spi module of union constructors with a typed reader) and run outputs (-> target/, untracked), and write the conversion for the kept ones; their pwsh writers move with PS1-TO-SPIRAL n17.")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_4 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 4u32, "2026-11-01T04:31Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 tibia part (3) INVENTORY: 65 tracked text tables in tibia/results. 61 arena-results-*.tsv are per-run live arena outcomes (pr1-pr28, pc3-pc11, calibration and diagnostic runs). They are KEPT DATA, not regenerable, because the live world runs are history, so they become typed rows: one results/arena_runs.spi of union constructors (run id, scenario, policy, per-hunt fields) with a typed reader, converted once, the .tsv archived. arena-region-r45.txt is GENERATED by apps/map/run.ps1 (compared against the committed copy), so it becomes typed rows emitted by the map program (terrain.spi already uses generated typed definitions). arena-spot-scan-r12.txt, path-check-pc1.txt and pc2.txt are one-off live scans: typed rows in the same results module. Writers: live/arena-summary.ps1 and score.ps1 move with their PS1-TO-SPIRAL ports.")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_5 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 5u32, "2026-11-01T06:03Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 02:2x tibia part (3) DESIGN, measured: tibia/results holds 61 .tsv in 3 schemas plus 4 .txt. Arena-run rows: 1,075 in 45 files, whose schema grew over time (42 files have 15 columns: timestamp, competitor, run, monster, count, experience, cleared_ms, lowest_health, actions_allowed, actions_dropped, potions, gold, deaths, foreign_potions, foreign_actions; 2 files have 13; 1 file has 10). Arm-step rows: 284 in 16 *-secondary files (file, arm, steps, taken, potions). TYPED FORM: results/arena_runs.spi with ':| result_row = | ArenaRun : <source, at, competitor, run, monster, count, experience, cleared_ms, lowest_health, allowed, dropped> * option potions/gold/deaths/foreign_potions/foreign_actions | ArmStep : source * log name * arm * steps * taken * potions'. One small definition per row in agile.spi style (inl arena_run_<n> () = ArenaRun (...)), about 150 characters each, so every row fits the cell budget; per-file chunk lists of at most about 10 row references, so no cell holds a 1,359-element list. The schema growth is expressed as option fields, not as 3 types. The arm rows'' absolute log paths (~/tibia-local/arena-logs/...) are stored as the log file name only, since the folder prefix is constant and machine-specific. CONVERSION: one exact pass with a typed reader test that recounts rows per source file against the .tsv line counts (1,075 + 284), then the .tsv are archived. It runs as one complete step (never a partial conversion that leaves two formats). Its writer, live/arena-summary.ps1, emits typed rows after its PS1-TO-SPIRAL port. Next hour''s first task.")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_6 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 6u32, "2026-11-01T07:47Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 03:47 tibia part (3): the conversion is HELD before any write, because the reader census shows a gap my n5 design missed. The arena .tsv are the INPUT of kit's experiment tooling: docs/BENCHMARK.md's pre-registered analyses (PR-1, PR-2, PR-3, PR-3a, PR-5..PR-7, PR-12, PC-3..PC-8; about 25 citations) run 'eoie experiment analyze target/ledger <ID> results/arena-results-*.tsv' and 'kit table query/diff' on them. A typed .spi module would break every cited command, and with it the reproducibility of registered results. Their writer live/arena-summary.ps1 is idle (last results 10-25, live runs paused, no other references). apps/map/run.ps1 reads the 4 .txt (region dump and spot scan), which are map-tool outputs (run outputs, so target/ candidates; separate from the rows). GENERIC FIX, not tibia-specific: 'kit table' (query, diff) and 'kit experiment analyze' should accept a typed row module (a .spi union-constructor module like agile.spi) as well as .tsv, selected by the file extension. Then part (3) converts in one pass, BENCHMARK citations change from .tsv to the .spi with an unchanged analysis output (an A/B of 'experiment analyze' on PR-1 and PR-12, .tsv vs .spi, must print identical numbers), and the writer is guarded. Until kit reads typed rows, converting only moves the drift. Owner of the reader half: kit. Tibia keeps the converter half ready (n5 schema: ArenaRun with option fields, ArmStep with log basename).")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_7 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 7u32, "2026-11-01T07:51Z", "kit", "11-01 04:0x kit: part (3) tibia results UNBLOCK PLAN (tibia n6: the 45 arena .tsv are inputs of 'experiment analyze' and 'kit table query|diff', cited ~25 times in tibia's BENCHMARK.md pre-registrations, so converting before kit reads the new form would break them). Typed codec DONE in the tree (unstaged): new granular HostFree+LibReferenced package src/kit_table_rows. table = { columns : list string; rows : list (list string) }; rows_spi_of_table / table_of_rows_spi over a module shaped '///- --no-respell' + 'union table_row = | Columns :: list string -> table_row | Row :: list string -> table_row' + 'inl columns () = Columns [ ... ]' + 'inl row_<n> () = Row [ ... ]' (cells quoted, backslash and quote escaped, empty cells kept, parsed by a scanner, not by splitting). The census refused a first version that also joined tabs ('a new package encodes rows with tab or record separators'), so the tab split/join stays in the legacy table package that already reads TSV. Test a_table_round_trips_through_a_typed_row_module. PROOF: node job-1793519377345-kit-node-table-rows 1/1, first try. NEXT (rides the post-TRAIN7 candidate build): table query/diff and experiment analyze accept a .spi row module by extension; 'kit table to-spi <tsv> [out.spi]'; CLI test = tibia's acceptance check (experiment analyze PR-1 and PR-12 print identical numbers from .tsv and .spi). Shape sent to tibia; tibia waits for kit's generator instead of writing its own.")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_8 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 8u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: (a) kit/standards holds only .spi baselines (done); (b) 'kit table to-spi results/x.tsv' generates the row module and 'kit experiment analyze' on tibia PR-1 and PR-12 prints identical numbers from .tsv and .spi (CLI test); (c) zero tracked .tsv in tibia/results after tibia's conversion; (d) the edit-time hook advises on new .tsv/.rows/.csv (done). NEXT STEP: .spi input in kit table query/diff and experiment analyze via kit_table_rows.table_of_rows_spi at the read boundary, then to-spi. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: table is a record (columns : list string; rows : list (list string)) because tables are arbitrary; the typed part is the boundary: an existential typed_table (\\? row. schema row * list row) for tables whose schema kit knows (arena results get a record row), so analysis code reads typed fields; cells are never tab-joined inside new packages (census rule).")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_9 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 9u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 08:34 tibia QUALITY PASS on part (3). This SUPERSEDES n5's option-field model, where None would mean both 'column did not exist yet' and 'value missing'. STORAGE is kit's generic module (n-kit: table_row = Columns of list string | Row of list string), generated by 'kit table to-spi'. TYPING lives in a tibia reader on top of it. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: (1) a GADT indexed by SCHEMA VERSION: phantom tags v10, v13, v15 and ':| arena_row v = | Base : base_run -> arena_row v10 | Economy : base_run * economy -> arena_row v13 | Foreign : base_run * economy * foreign -> arena_row v15'. Potions, gold and deaths exist only from v13, foreign_* only in v15, so code holding an arena_row v15 can read foreign_actions without an option; (2) an EXISTENTIAL 'some_arena_row = exists v. arena_row v' for one source file's mixed list, with a per-version 'base_of' prototype so all 1,075 rows answer common queries; (3) closed unions for the observed value sets, taken from the data, not open strings: competitor = | SpiralRetreat | SpiralPolicy | Vbot | Nexbot | RandomControl | ... and monster = | Cyclops | ...; the decoder REJECTS an unknown name; (4) nominal units ':! ms = i32', ':! hp = i32' for cleared_ms and lowest_health; (5) arm-step rows as arm_step = { log : log_name (nominal, basename only); arm : competitor; steps; taken; potions }; (6) decode failures as a typed union row_rejection = | WrongWidth of i32 * i32 | NotANumber of column | UnknownCompetitor of string, never -1 or an empty string. Model: kit's agile.spi typed reader plus lib json_schema's decode-to-union. DEFINITION OF DONE: tibia/results/arena_rows.spi decodes every generated module with 0 rejections; a recount test asserts decoded rows per source equal the .tsv data lines (1,075 arena + 284 arm, per file); 'kit experiment analyze' on PR-1 and PR-12 prints identical numbers from .tsv and .spi (kit's CLI test); BENCHMARK.md cites .spi paths; the .tsv are archived. NEXT STEP, not blocked: write the reader against kit's declared shape now, with 3 inline sample rows (one per schema, copied from cyclops-pr1, the 13-column file and the 10-column file) and 2 rejection tests, pinned lua + rust. The bulk conversion waits for 'kit table to-spi'.")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_10 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 10u32, "2026-11-01T14:42Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 10:42 tibia, round 5: part (3) NOT finished this round, for a checked reason. The installed kit (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe, built 10-31 18:57) still has only 'table query|diff'; 'kit table to-spi' is not there yet (kit ships it with the candidate after TRAIN7). Converting 1,359 rows without it would mean a new conversion script, which spiral-first-tooling forbids, and would create a second format before the readers exist. RISK RETIRED meanwhile: the n9 typefeatureset plan's GADT and existential forms are already proven in tibia's own src/catalog.spi (':| kind k = | TrollKind :: kind troll_kind', ':! entry = \\? k. spawn k', unpacked by ':= \\? k. s = e'), so the version-indexed arena_row v GADT follows a working in-repo pattern rather than new syntax. TRIGGER: kit's 'converter installed' message; then the same session runs to-spi over results/*.tsv, writes src/arena_rows.spi (the n9 reader), runs the recount test, rewrites the BENCHMARK citations and archives the .tsv, as one step.")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_11 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 11u32, "2026-11-01T14:42Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit (round 5): two more tab-row writers found while measuring orphan logging: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\machine-watch\\health.tsv and quiet.tsv (written by the machine watch). They move to typed .spi rows together with the proc_event log of KIT-ORPHAN-SIM, so the watch keeps a single writer. Migration discipline (user list item 8): main-thread light items + kit jobs, no subagents.")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_12 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 12u32, "2026-11-01T14:45Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit: raised P1 -> P0 (user round 5 item 8: prioritize tsv/ps1/py/txt migrations). Children: MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV (61 files), machine-watch health/quiet rows (with KIT-ORPHAN-SIM). Siblings: MIGRATE-SPIRAL-PY-CLASSIFY, MIGRATE-TXT-FILES, PS1-TO-SPIRAL.")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_13 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 13u32, "2026-11-01T21:22Z", "kit", "11-01 17:22 kit: SLIP OWNED (asked by tibia): the to-spi verb was not in TRAIN7 (only the kit_table_rows codec was proven). It is now wired, unstaged: kit table to-spi <file.tsv> <out.spi> in kit_table_query (TableToSpi, module tospi.spi, reusing the crate's TSV cell reader; output = kit_table_rows' typed module), plus a CLI test in table_cli.spi. Ships in TRAIN8 tonight (node proof, then box train); the installed hash will be noted here. Unblocks MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV.")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_14 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 14u32, "2026-11-02T02:57Z", "kit", "11-01 22:57 kit: kit table to-spi SHIPPED in TRAIN8, installed kit.exe 7C559D83 (22:55). Acceptance on tibia's results/arena-results-cyclops-leadtrace.tsv with --open table_rows: rows=10 columns=15; header ///- --no-respell plus open table_rows (no own union); inl columns, inl row_N, and the inl rows () : list table_row index. MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV is unblocked.")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_15 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 15u32, "2026-11-03T01:26Z", "tibia", "11-02 21:2x tibia, part (3) progress: tibia's two map text tables are typed rows now (TIBIA-MAP-GRIDS-TYPED n2-n3, unstaged): results/rows/arena_region_r45.spi and results/rows/arena_spot_scan_r12.spi (table_rows Table literals), a Spiral agreement check (results/map_grids.spi, 2/2 memo-off on the node) replacing run.ps1's PowerShell loop, and run.ps1 comparing the live dump with the .spi row module. results/arena-region-r45.txt and results/arena-spot-scan-r12.txt are deleted from the worktree. Earlier the same day: path-check-pc1/pc2 .txt became .md. What remains of tibia's text tables: the 61 results/*.tsv (MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV, waits on kit's TRAIN13 for the .spi readers in table query/diff and experiment analyze; TRAIN13 is on its third attempt, 13c).")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_16 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 16u32, "2026-11-03T03:53Z", "tibia", "11-02 23:5x tibia: part (3) DONE in the tree (MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV n-latest): tibia/results holds no .tsv any more (61 deleted, unstaged), every reader and writer goes through kit's .spi table I/O (TRAIN13c), BENCHMARK cites results/rows/*.spi, and the two map .txt tables were converted earlier (TIBIA-MAP-GRIDS-TYPED). tibia has no repo text tables left; target/ keeps only regenerable scratch .tsv.")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_17 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 17u32, "2026-11-03T17:37Z", "main", "main 11-03 13:4x ORG CENSUS (git ls-files *.tsv *.rows *.csv, deps/ excluded, fixtures INCLUDED) over alphabet, blog, dice, fiscal, kit, regex, spiral, tibia, x, vault, html, i574n.github, dotfiles: ZERO tracked text tables in any repo. With tibia's part (3) done, the goal 'repo data as typed Spiral rows' holds org-wide as of today; owners (kit, tibia) can close unless a remaining part covers untracked/generated scratch (target/ is excluded by design).")
inl note_of_data_rows_typed_18 () = Note ("DATA-ROWS-TYPED", 18u32, "2026-11-04T04:52Z", "kit", "DONE 11-04 00:5x (kit, agreed with tibia): the title scope is REPO data. Tibia part (3) is done (MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV); kit table to-spi shipped in TRAIN8; main's 11-03 13:4x org census found zero tracked text tables in any repo. The one open thread, machine-watch health.tsv/quiet.tsv under %LOCALAPPDATA%, is local runtime state, not repo data. It moves to KIT-ORPHAN-SIM (which reads those rows) as its own typed-rows step.")
inl note_of_cells_typed_directives_1 () = Note ("CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES", 1u32, "2026-10-31T23:36Z", "x", "2026-10-31 19:36 x: the first typefeatureset migration of the Cells Spiral domain (CELLS-IN-SPIRAL n2). It needs no lib, so it doesn't wait on CELLS-SPI-LIB. directives.spi had 9 positional i32/string state parameters, a parsed = Parsed of i32 * i32 * string | ParseBad of string, and error kinds as strings. The draft types it:\n- directive_error = ShebangLine | UnknownDirective | UnknownFlag | BadTimeout | UnknownBackend\n- a generic outcome t = Ready t | Refused directive_error\n- timeout = NoTimeout | TimeoutMs (it was -1 as a sentinel)\n- main_source = OwnMain | MainFromExpression | MainFromDefinitionsOnly (it was cell.wrap's 0/1/2)\n- backend_line = RustCell | SkippedCell (it was the \"skip\" string)\n- records flags, prepared and scan_state, and packages kept as tokens (it was a tab-joined string).\nThe joined 'ok\\n...' and 'error\\n...' text now exists only in encoded, which serves the prepare export while directives.ex still decodes it. encoded goes away in the slice that removes that Elixir caller. Same bytes by construction (encoded mirrors finish's layout and text.err's kinds).\nPass rule, registered before the run: proof x-cells-typed-directives1 (job-1793489744264, new spiral-only mirror xcells2, core D1C49B8BDC3318F7; the draft is pushed through node-proof -Push) gives mix compile exit 0 with domain.gleam regenerated, and directives_test.exs, cell_test.exs and document_test.exs all pass unchanged. Those tests assert prepare's wire bytes through Spiral.Cells.Directives. Follow-up, recorded and not started: cell.wrap returns main_source itself (notebook.spi:233 is its other caller), which removes main_of.")
inl note_of_cells_typed_directives_2 () = Note ("CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES", 2u32, "2026-10-31T23:53Z", "x", "2026-10-31 19:53 x: x-cells-typed-directives1 by n1's rule.\n- First attempt: compile FAILED. Line 127, '| Ready SkippedCell =>': a nested nullary constructor is not a valid pattern argument (Expected: =>), so scanned was left out and prepare saw it unbound. Fixed with a helper, backend_seen s b. Same trap family as the parenthesized cons patterns in memory spiral-one-export-per-path.\n- Rerun in the same mirror (xcells2): mix compile exit 0 (domain.gleam regenerated). directives_test and cell_test pass. document_test has 3 failures: the export over the dice and alphabet notebooks, dice's .spi round trip, alphabet's documents/hangul round trip.\n- Verdict by the rule AS WRITTEN: NOT MET.\n- Reading: the 3 are environmental. document_test reads @dice = ../../../../../dice and ../alphabet, which a spiral-only mirror doesn't have. The original directives.spi in the same mirror fails the same 3 tests, same names, exit 2. So the typed draft changes no observed result. That baseline ran after the typed run, so this is a reading, not a pass.\n- Rule for the landing run, written now: the full mix test with dice and alphabet present (the live tree's siblings, through cells_land_window.ps1) equals the pre-land result of the same tree. Land under a train-hold, not tonight, because the night run reads apps/cells live (cells-reads-live-tree).\n- directives.spi is not on main's window-6 hash list, so no hand-over is needed.\nDRAFT (replaces apps/cells/spi/directives.spi; the applier at land time copies this text):\n:= (++) (left : string) (right : string) : string = left ^^ right\n\n:| ^ tokens =\n    | NoToken\n    | Token : string * tokens\n\n:~ ^ rev_tokens (xs : tokens) (acc : tokens) : tokens =\n    ?= xs ?|\n    | NoToken => acc\n    | Token (s, rest) => rev_tokens rest (Token (s, acc))\n\n:~ ^ take_token (src : string) (i : i32) : i32 =\n    ? i >= text.length src ?> i\n    |:\n        := ch = text.index src i\n        ? ch = ' ' ?> i\n        |? ch = '\\t' ?> i\n        |: take_token src (i + 1i32)\n\n:~ ^ skip_ws (src : string) (i : i32) : i32 =\n    ? i >= text.length src ?> i\n    |:\n        := ch = text.index src i\n        ? ch = ' ' ?> skip_ws src (i + 1i32)\n        |? ch = '\\t' ?> skip_ws src (i + 1i32)\n        |: i\n\n:~ ^ tokens_from (src : string) (i : i32) (acc : tokens) : tokens =\n    := j = skip_ws src i\n    ? j >= text.length src ?> rev_tokens acc NoToken\n    |:\n        := k = take_token src j\n        tokens_from src k (Token (text.slice_len src j (k - j), acc))\n\n:~ ^ joined_tab (xs : tokens) : string =\n    ?= xs ?|\n    | NoToken => \"\"\n    | Token (s, NoToken) => s\n    | Token (s, rest) => s ++ \"\\t\" ++ joined_tab rest\n\n:| directive_error =\n    | ShebangLine\n    | UnknownDirective : string\n    | UnknownFlag : string\n    | BadTimeout : string\n    | UnknownBackend : string\n\n:| outcome t =\n    | Ready : t\n    | Refused : directive_error\n\n:| timeout =\n    | NoTimeout\n    | TimeoutMs : i32\n\n:| main_source =\n    | OwnMain\n    | MainFromExpression\n    | MainFromDefinitionsOnly\n\n:| backend_line =\n    | RustCell\n    | SkippedCell\n\n:== flags = { print_code : bool; timeout : timeout; packages : tokens }\n\n:== prepared = { code : string; flags : flags; main : main_source; skip : bool }\n\n:== scan_state = { kept : string; flags : flags; seen : bool; started : bool; skip : bool }\n\n:= no_flags () : flags = { print_code = !-; timeout = NoTimeout; packages = NoToken }\n\n:= positive_timeout (ms : string) : outcome timeout =\n    := n = text.parse_i32 ms\n    ? n > 0i32 ?> Ready (TimeoutMs n) |: Refused (BadTimeout ms)\n\n:~ ^ merged (xs : tokens) (f : flags) : outcome flags =\n    ?= xs ?|\n    | NoToken => Ready f\n    | Token (\"--print-code\", rest) => merged rest { f ?| print_code = !+ }\n    | Token (\"--timeout\", Token (ms, rest)) =>\n        ?= positive_timeout ms ?|\n        | Ready t => merged rest { f ?| timeout = t }\n        | Refused e => Refused e\n    | Token (\"--package\", Token (path, rest)) => merged rest { f ?| packages = Token (path, f::packages) }\n    | Token (\"--test\", Token (\"static\", rest)) => merged rest f\n    | Token (\"--test\", rest) => merged rest f\n    | Token (\"--symbol\", rest) => merged rest f\n    | Token (\"--import-types\", Token (_, rest)) => merged rest f\n    | Token (\"--name\", Token (_, rest)) => merged rest f\n    | Token (flag, _) => Refused (UnknownFlag flag)\n\n:= backend_of (rest : string) : outcome backend_line =\n    := trimmed = text.trim rest\n    ? trimmed = \"_\" ?> Ready SkippedCell\n    |? trimmed = \"rust\" || text.starts_with trimmed \"rust \" ?> Ready RustCell\n    |: Refused (UnknownBackend trimmed)\n\n:= main_of (generated : i32) : main_source =\n    ? generated = 0i32 ?> OwnMain |? generated = 1i32 ?> MainFromExpression |: MainFromDefinitionsOnly\n\n:= finished (s : scan_state) : outcome prepared =\n    ? text.contains s::kept \"\\n#!\" || text.starts_with s::kept \"#!\" ?> Refused ShebangLine\n    |:\n        := wrapped, generated = cell.wrap s::kept\n        Ready { code = wrapped; flags = s::flags; main = main_of generated; skip = s::skip }\n\n:= kept_line (s : scan_state) (line : string) : scan_state =\n    { s ?| kept = (? s::started ?> s::kept ++ \"\\n\" ++ line |: line); started = !+ }\n\n:= flags_line (s : scan_state) (line : string) : outcome flags =\n    merged (tokens_from (text.drop line (text.length \"///- \")) 0i32 NoToken) s::flags\n\n:= backend_seen (s : scan_state) (b : backend_line) : scan_state =\n    ?= b ?| SkippedCell => { s ?| skip = !+ } | RustCell => s\n\n:~ ^ scanned (src : string) (i : i32) (s : scan_state) : outcome prepared =\n    ?= text.next_line src i ?|\n    | LineDone => finished s\n    | LineGot (line, next) =>\n        ? text.starts_with line \"////\" ?> scanned src next s\n        |? text.starts_with line \"///- \" ?>\n            ? s::seen ?> scanned src next s\n            |:\n                ?= flags_line s line ?|\n                | Ready f => scanned src next { s ?| flags = f; seen = !+ }\n                | Refused e => Refused e\n        |? text.starts_with line \"///> \" ?>\n            ?= backend_of (text.drop line (text.length \"///> \")) ?|\n            | Ready b => scanned src next (backend_seen s b)\n            | Refused e => Refused e\n        |? text.starts_with line \"///\" ?> Refused (UnknownDirective line)\n        |: scanned src next (kept_line s line)\n\n:= flag_text (b : bool) : string = ? b ?> \"1\" |: \"0\"\n\n:= timeout_text (t : timeout) : string = ?= t ?| NoTimeout => \"-\" | TimeoutMs n => text.show_i32 n\n\n:= main_text (m : main_source) : string =\n    ?= m ?| OwnMain => \"0\" | MainFromExpression => \"1\" | MainFromDefinitionsOnly => \"2\"\n\n:= error_text (e : directive_error) : string =\n    ?= e ?|\n    | ShebangLine => text.err \"dib\" \"\"\n    | UnknownDirective line => text.err \"directive\" line\n    | UnknownFlag flag => text.err \"flag\" flag\n    | BadTimeout ms => text.err \"timeout\" ms\n    | UnknownBackend backend => text.err \"backend\" backend\n\n:= prepared_text (p : prepared) : string =\n    := f = p::flags\n    := head = \"ok\\n\" ++ flag_text f::print_code ++ \"\\n\" ++ timeout_text f::timeout ++ \"\\n\" ++ main_text p::main\n    head ++ \"\\n\" ++ joined_tab (rev_tokens f::packages NoToken) ++ \"\\n\" ++ flag_text p::skip ++ \"\\n\" ++ p::code\n\n:= encoded (o : outcome prepared) : string = ?= o ?| Ready p => prepared_text p | Refused e => error_text e\n\n:= initial () : scan_state = { kept = \"\"; flags = no_flags (); seen = !-; started = !-; skip = !- }\n\n:= prepare (code : string) : string = encoded (scanned (text.replace code \"\\r\\n\" \"\\n\") 0i32 (initial ()))")
inl note_of_cells_typed_directives_3 () = Note ("CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES", 3u32, "2026-11-01T03:38Z", "x", "2026-10-31 23:37 x: NEXT STEP (CPU-light, no compile): cell.wrap returns main_source (OwnMain | MainFromExpression | MainFromDefinitionsOnly) instead of the i32 0/1/2. Move the main_source union into cell.spi, update notebook.spi:233 (its other caller) and drop directives' main_of. Draft as a second DRAFT block in this item. Then a NODE-RUNNABLE proof in xcells2 (scp the drafts, mix compile, directives_test + cell_test + notebook_test; document_test's 3 dice/alphabet reds are the known environment baseline, n2). Landing stays the morning window with a train-hold.")
inl note_of_cells_typed_directives_4 () = Note ("CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES", 4u32, "2026-11-01T04:37Z", "x", "2026-11-01 00:37 x: n3's step DONE and proven. cell.wrap returns main_source; the 0/1/2 i32 is gone.\nProof: xcells2 mirror, pack 11 core CA42B2E62EB69EA3, mix compile exit 0. directives_test, cell_test and notebook_test pass. document_test fails only the 3 known dice/alphabet tests (environment baseline, n2). Rule met: PASS.\nEdits (land together in the morning window, train-hold, cells_land_window):\n- cell.spi: before ':= wrap', add\n    :| main_source = | OwnMain | MainFromExpression | MainFromDefinitionsOnly   (one case per line, as in the file's style)\n    := from_expression (m : main_source) : bool = ?= m ?| MainFromExpression => !+ | _ => !-\n    := main_text (m : main_source) : string = ?= m ?| OwnMain => \"0\" | MainFromExpression => \"1\" | MainFromDefinitionsOnly => \"2\"\n  and wrap's signature becomes 'string * main_source', with the arms returning OwnMain, MainFromDefinitionsOnly and MainFromExpression in place of 0i32, 2i32 and 1i32.\n- notebook.spi:234: '? generated = 1i32' becomes '? cell.from_expression generated'.\n- directives.spi: the full draft below supersedes n2's. main is a cell.main_source, main_of is gone, and the encoder uses cell.main_text.\nCross-module note: the Cells package reaches another module's union through helpers (cell.from_expression, cell.main_text) and its type as cell.main_source. No caller matches another module's constructors.\nDRAFT directives.spi (v2):\n:= (++) (left : string) (right : string) : string = left ^^ right\n\n:| ^ tokens =\n    | NoToken\n    | Token : string * tokens\n\n:~ ^ rev_tokens (xs : tokens) (acc : tokens) : tokens =\n    ?= xs ?|\n    | NoToken => acc\n    | Token (s, rest) => rev_tokens rest (Token (s, acc))\n\n:~ ^ take_token (src : string) (i : i32) : i32 =\n    ? i >= text.length src ?> i\n    |:\n        := ch = text.index src i\n        ? ch = ' ' ?> i\n        |? ch = '\\t' ?> i\n        |: take_token src (i + 1i32)\n\n:~ ^ skip_ws (src : string) (i : i32) : i32 =\n    ? i >= text.length src ?> i\n    |:\n        := ch = text.index src i\n        ? ch = ' ' ?> skip_ws src (i + 1i32)\n        |? ch = '\\t' ?> skip_ws src (i + 1i32)\n        |: i\n\n:~ ^ tokens_from (src : string) (i : i32) (acc : tokens) : tokens =\n    := j = skip_ws src i\n    ? j >= text.length src ?> rev_tokens acc NoToken\n    |:\n        := k = take_token src j\n        tokens_from src k (Token (text.slice_len src j (k - j), acc))\n\n:~ ^ joined_tab (xs : tokens) : string =\n    ?= xs ?|\n    | NoToken => \"\"\n    | Token (s, NoToken) => s\n    | Token (s, rest) => s ++ \"\\t\" ++ joined_tab rest\n\n:| directive_error =\n    | ShebangLine\n    | UnknownDirective : string\n    | UnknownFlag : string\n    | BadTimeout : string\n    | UnknownBackend : string\n\n:| outcome t =\n    | Ready : t\n    | Refused : directive_error\n\n:| timeout =\n    | NoTimeout\n    | TimeoutMs : i32\n\n:| backend_line =\n    | RustCell\n    | SkippedCell\n\n:== flags = { print_code : bool; timeout : timeout; packages : tokens }\n\n:== prepared = { code : string; flags : flags; main : cell.main_source; skip : bool }\n\n:== scan_state = { kept : string; flags : flags; seen : bool; started : bool; skip : bool }\n\n:= no_flags () : flags = { print_code = !-; timeout = NoTimeout; packages = NoToken }\n\n:= positive_timeout (ms : string) : outcome timeout =\n    := n = text.parse_i32 ms\n    ? n > 0i32 ?> Ready (TimeoutMs n) |: Refused (BadTimeout ms)\n\n:~ ^ merged (xs : tokens) (f : flags) : outcome flags =\n    ?= xs ?|\n    | NoToken => Ready f\n    | Token (\"--print-code\", rest) => merged rest { f ?| print_code = !+ }\n    | Token (\"--timeout\", Token (ms, rest)) =>\n        ?= positive_timeout ms ?|\n        | Ready t => merged rest { f ?| timeout = t }\n        | Refused e => Refused e\n    | Token (\"--package\", Token (path, rest)) => merged rest { f ?| packages = Token (path, f::packages) }\n    | Token (\"--test\", Token (\"static\", rest)) => merged rest f\n    | Token (\"--test\", rest) => merged rest f\n    | Token (\"--symbol\", rest) => merged rest f\n    | Token (\"--import-types\", Token (_, rest)) => merged rest f\n    | Token (\"--name\", Token (_, rest)) => merged rest f\n    | Token (flag, _) => Refused (UnknownFlag flag)\n\n:= backend_of (rest : string) : outcome backend_line =\n    := trimmed = text.trim rest\n    ? trimmed = \"_\" ?> Ready SkippedCell\n    |? trimmed = \"rust\" || text.starts_with trimmed \"rust \" ?> Ready RustCell\n    |: Refused (UnknownBackend trimmed)\n\n:= finished (s : scan_state) : outcome prepared =\n    ? text.contains s::kept \"\\n#!\" || text.starts_with s::kept \"#!\" ?> Refused ShebangLine\n    |:\n        := wrapped, generated = cell.wrap s::kept\n        Ready { code = wrapped; flags = s::flags; main = generated; skip = s::skip }\n\n:= kept_line (s : scan_state) (line : string) : scan_state =\n    { s ?| kept = (? s::started ?> s::kept ++ \"\\n\" ++ line |: line); started = !+ }\n\n:= flags_line (s : scan_state) (line : string) : outcome flags =\n    merged (tokens_from (text.drop line (text.length \"///- \")) 0i32 NoToken) s::flags\n\n:= backend_seen (s : scan_state) (b : backend_line) : scan_state =\n    ?= b ?| SkippedCell => { s ?| skip = !+ } | RustCell => s\n\n:~ ^ scanned (src : string) (i : i32) (s : scan_state) : outcome prepared =\n    ?= text.next_line src i ?|\n    | LineDone => finished s\n    | LineGot (line, next) =>\n        ? text.starts_with line \"////\" ?> scanned src next s\n        |? text.starts_with line \"///- \" ?>\n            ? s::seen ?> scanned src next s\n            |:\n                ?= flags_line s line ?|\n                | Ready f => scanned src next { s ?| flags = f; seen = !+ }\n                | Refused e => Refused e\n        |? text.starts_with line \"///> \" ?>\n            ?= backend_of (text.drop line (text.length \"///> \")) ?|\n            | Ready b => scanned src next (backend_seen s b)\n            | Refused e => Refused e\n        |? text.starts_with line \"///\" ?> Refused (UnknownDirective line)\n        |: scanned src next (kept_line s line)\n\n:= flag_text (b : bool) : string = ? b ?> \"1\" |: \"0\"\n\n:= timeout_text (t : timeout) : string = ?= t ?| NoTimeout => \"-\" | TimeoutMs n => text.show_i32 n\n\n:= error_text (e : directive_error) : string =\n    ?= e ?|\n    | ShebangLine => text.err \"dib\" \"\"\n    | UnknownDirective line => text.err \"directive\" line\n    | UnknownFlag flag => text.err \"flag\" flag\n    | BadTimeout ms => text.err \"timeout\" ms\n    | UnknownBackend backend => text.err \"backend\" backend\n\n:= prepared_text (p : prepared) : string =\n    := f = p::flags\n    := head = \"ok\\n\" ++ flag_text f::print_code ++ \"\\n\" ++ timeout_text f::timeout ++ \"\\n\" ++ cell.main_text p::main\n    head ++ \"\\n\" ++ joined_tab (rev_tokens f::packages NoToken) ++ \"\\n\" ++ flag_text p::skip ++ \"\\n\" ++ p::code\n\n:= encoded (o : outcome prepared) : string = ?= o ?| Ready p => prepared_text p | Refused e => error_text e\n\n:= initial () : scan_state = { kept = \"\"; flags = no_flags (); seen = !-; started = !-; skip = !- }\n\n:= prepare (code : string) : string = encoded (scanned (text.replace code \"\\r\\n\" \"\\n\") 0i32 (initial ()))")
inl note_of_cells_typed_directives_5 () = Note ("CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES", 5u32, "2026-11-01T06:32Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:32 x: MORNING LAND RUNBOOK. One window lands CELLS-SPI-LIB, CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES and the cell.wrap change together.\n(1) Write the APPLIER below to a temp dir, with this item's n4 'DRAFT directives.spi (v2)' saved beside it as directives.spi (the applier reads -Drafts, default its own folder).\n(2) kit window list: no Cells tree-write held. Then C:\\home\\git\\kit\\scripts\\train-hold.ps1 -Holder x-cells-typed -Minutes 60.\n(3) pwsh spiral/apps/cells/spi/cells_land_window.ps1 -Applier <temp>\\apply-cells-typed.ps1 -Files spi/package.spiproj,spi/cell.spi,spi/notebook.spi,spi/directives.spi -Tests test/spiral/cells/directives_test.exs,test/spiral/cells/cell_test.exs,test/spiral/notebook_test.exs,test/spiral/document_test.exs -Label x-cells-typed\n(4) train-hold.ps1 -Release -Holder x-cells-typed. Notes on all three items with the landed hashes.\nVerified tonight with NO live writes: the applier's anchors are found exactly once in today's tree. Its -Apply output on a scratch copy of spi/ is byte-IDENTICAL to the node-proven drafts of cell.spi, notebook.spi and directives.spi (xcells2, PASS n4), and package.spiproj matches CELLS-SPI-LIB's proven shape (PASS n6). Combined state is unproven: the drafts were proven without spiral- and spiral- without the drafts, so the land window's test run is the combined proof. On a red, cells_land_window restores from its undo copy. Pass rule, written now: the four test files pass (dice and alphabet exist on the box, so document_test's 3 node-only reds must pass here); any red that passes alone on --failed is a load red.\nAPPLIER:\nparam([Parameter(Mandatory)] [string] $Cells, [switch] $Apply, [string] $Drafts = $PSScriptRoot)\n$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'\n$spi = Join-Path (Resolve-Path $Cells).Path 'spi'\nfunction Read-Lf([string] $p) { [IO.File]::ReadAllText($p).Replace(\"`r`n\", \"`n\") }\nfunction Swap([string] $text, [string] $old, [string] $new, [string] $what) {\n    $at = $text.IndexOf($old)\n    if ($at -lt 0 -or $text.IndexOf($old, $at + 1) -ge 0) { throw \"apply-cells-typed / anchor not found exactly once: $what\" }\n    $text.Substring(0, $at) + $new + $text.Substring($at + $old.Length)\n}\n$out = [ordered]@{}\n$project = Read-Lf (Join-Path $spi 'package.spiproj')\nif ($project -notmatch '(?m)^\\s+spiral-\\s*$') {\n    if ($project -notmatch '(?m)^packages:\\n\\s+\\|core-\\n') { throw 'apply-cells-typed / unexpected package.spiproj shape' }\n    $out['package.spiproj'] = \"packageDir: ../../../lib`n\" + ($project -replace '(?m)^(\\s+)\\|core-\\n', \"`$1|core-`n`$1spiral-`n\")\n}\n$cell = Read-Lf (Join-Path $spi 'cell.spi')\n$wrapOld = \":= wrap (code : string) : string * i32 =`n\"\n$union = \":| main_source =`n    | OwnMain`n    | MainFromExpression`n    | MainFromDefinitionsOnly`n`n\"\n$helpers = \":= from_expression (m : main_source) : bool = ?= m ?| MainFromExpression => !+ | _ => !-`n`n:= main_text (m : main_source) : string =`n    ?= m ?| OwnMain => `\"0`\" | MainFromExpression => `\"1`\" | MainFromDefinitionsOnly => `\"2`\"`n`n\"\n$cell = Swap $cell $wrapOld ($union + $helpers + \":= wrap (code : string) : string * main_source =`n\") 'cell.wrap signature'\n$cell = Swap $cell \"main_line (nth_line xs block) ?> text.ensure_nl code, 0i32\" \"main_line (nth_line xs block) ?> text.ensure_nl code, OwnMain\" 'wrap own-main arm'\n$cell = Swap $cell \"`\"inl main () : i32 = 0i32\\n`\", 2i32\" \"`\"inl main () : i32 = 0i32\\n`\", MainFromDefinitionsOnly\" 'wrap definitions-only arm'\n$cell = Swap $cell \"(join_lines (map_main xs 0i32 at)), 1i32\" \"(join_lines (map_main xs 0i32 at)), MainFromExpression\" 'wrap expression arm'\n$out['cell.spi'] = $cell\n$notebook = Read-Lf (Join-Path $spi 'notebook.spi')\n$out['notebook.spi'] = Swap $notebook \"    := wrapped, generated = cell.wrap body`n    ? generated = 1i32`n\" \"    := wrapped, generated = cell.wrap body`n    ? cell.from_expression generated`n\" 'notebook real_program'\n$out['directives.spi'] = Read-Lf (Join-Path $Drafts 'directives.spi')\nforeach ($name in $out.Keys) {\n    $target = Join-Path $spi $name\n    if ($Apply) { [IO.File]::WriteAllText($target, $out[$name]) }\n    \"apply-cells-typed / $(if ($Apply) { 'wrote' } else { 'would write' }) $name ($($out[$name].Length) chars)\"\n}")
inl note_of_cells_typed_directives_6 () = Note ("CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES", 6u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:34 x: ITEM UPGRADE (round 4). Fields: tags gain team:x; 120 / light / agent stand. The dep CELLS-CENSUS is Done.\nDEFINITION OF DONE (verifiable): cells_land_window.ps1 (runbook n5) lands spi/package.spiproj, cell.spi, notebook.spi and directives.spi and closes GREEN on the box: directives_test, cell_test, notebook_test and document_test pass (dice and alphabet are present on the box, so document_test's 3 node-only reds must pass). Artifact: the 4 landed hashes printed by the window and noted on this item and CELLS-SPI-LIB.\nNEXT CONCRETE STEP: the morning window, following n5's steps (1)-(4) exactly. The applier text is in n5 and directives v2 in n4.\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN (written in n1, refined here, so reviewers can judge it):\n- directive_error union (5 cases) in place of the string kinds 'dib/directive/flag/timeout/backend'.\n- Generic 'outcome t' (Ready t | Refused directive_error), so every parser stage returns a typed result, never a joined 'error\\n...' row.\n- timeout = NoTimeout | TimeoutMs i32, replacing the -1 sentinel.\n- main_source union, moved into cell.spi and returned by cell.wrap, replacing the 0/1/2 i32.\n- backend_line = RustCell | SkippedCell, replacing the \"skip\" string.\n- Records flags / prepared / scan_state, replacing 9 positional i32/string parameters.\nWHY NO GADT INDEX HERE, said honestly: directives are parsed from RUNTIME text, and a Spiral GADT index can't be chosen from runtime data without an existential pack, which Spiral refuses ('Cannot dyn an existential into a runtime var', x-parity-typed1). So unions plus a generic result is the correct model for a parser, and indices belong on STATIC paths. Cells' GADT candidates are the daemon request -> reply GADT and the target -> builder GADT (CELLS-IN-SPIRAL n2), not this slice.\nWhat still stays stringly, on purpose: encoded (the 'ok\\n...' wire) until directives.ex, its Elixir caller, is ported. It's deleted in that slice.")
inl note_of_cells_typed_directives_7 () = Note ("CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES", 7u32, "2026-11-01T17:01Z", "x", "2026-11-01 13:01 x: the draft moved to the kit drafts store under the 11-01 13:0x rule (drafts leave TEMP and the repos). It now lives at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES\\: apply-cells-typed.ps1, cell.spi, notebook.spi, directives.spi, plus the discriminate*.ps1 probes from 10-31. In n5's runbook, read the -Applier path as that folder's apply-cells-typed.ps1. Nothing else in n5 changes. x has no untracked drafts: the tree is clean at bcbca2d.")
inl note_of_cells_typed_directives_8 () = Note ("CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES", 8u32, "2026-11-01T18:45Z", "x", "2026-11-01 14:45 x: NODE PRE-PROOF of the combined state (lib reference + typed directives + typed cell.wrap), while the box is in kit's held heavy window (TRAIN7c, until ~18:30). The 14:5x dry run on the live tree finds every anchor exactly once. Live directives.spi last changed 10-30 (fd70dccf), before the 11-01 00:34 draft, so the wholesale overwrite reverts nothing. Run: node mirror xqueue3 synced to the box tree, stage = applier + directives.spi, node_cells_window_proof.ps1 -Applier apply-cells-typed.ps1 (full mix test, then 2 --failed rounds).\nPASS RULE, written before the run: PASS iff (a) the apply prints 4 'wrote' lines, (b) no test in directives_test.exs, cell_test.exs or notebook_test.exs is left failing after the --failed rounds, and (c) every remaining failure is in CELLS-SPI-LIB n4's known spiral-only-mirror set (LispTest x2, DocumentTest x3 dice/alphabet, FsiSessionTest per-cell, TargetsTest gleam lock, the cells_test:698 / targets_test:321 flakes). Any other remaining failure: NOT MET, and that test is read before the window. A PASS turns the box window into a confirmation run, not a first proof.\nQUESTIONS: does typed cell.wrap (main_source union) keep the Elixir reader of the generated flag working? Do the typed directives parse every fixture? PREDICTION: PASS, with only n4's known set remaining.")
inl note_of_cells_typed_directives_9 () = Note ("CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES", 9u32, "2026-11-01T18:55Z", "x", "2026-11-01 14:55 x: x-cells-typed-pre1 (job-1793558987011, node, core DBC5EC18F14C354C, mirror xqueue3 at spiral 504e1e07, 202 s) by n8's rule: PASS.\n(a) The proof script prints only the applier's last line ('wrote directives.spi'). Read after the writer: all 4 files have mtime 14:49:48, package.spiproj starts 'packageDir: ../../../lib', cell.spi and notebook.spi carry MainFromExpression/from_expression, and src/spiral_cells/domain.gleam was regenerated at 14:50:00 with MainFromExpression in it. So the suite ran the typed code, not a stale domain.\n(b) Every directives_test, cell_test and notebook_test passed.\n(c) Full run 329/335 (1 excluded). The 6 left after 2 --failed rounds are exactly n4's known spiral-only-mirror set: LispTest x2, DocumentTest x3 (dice/alphabet), FsiSessionTest per-cell. The :698 and :321 flakes didn't fail this time.\nAnswers: the typed cell.wrap keeps every consumer working (only notebook.spi and directives.spi call it, and no Elixir code does), and the typed directives parse every fixture. The prediction held. The mirror's 4 files are restored with git checkout.\nNEXT: the box land window runs as a confirmation (n5 runbook, -Applier = the store path from n7) once kit's held heavy window (TRAIN7c) ends and kit window list shows no Cells tree-write.")
inl note_of_cells_typed_directives_10 () = Note ("CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES", 10u32, "2026-11-01T19:44Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:44 x: RUNBOOK UPDATE (supersedes n5's -Applier path and n7's). The window lands C1+C2 together with ONE applier: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\CELLS-IN-SPIRAL-C2\\apply-c1c2.ps1. It calls this item's apply-cells-typed.ps1 with -Drafts set to the C2 folder, so directives.spi is C2's lib-list version (5,094 chars), and the other 3 files are unchanged. Dry run on the live tree at 15:4x: all 4 anchors found, exit 0. Command: train-hold, then cells_land_window.ps1 -Applier <that path> -Files spi/package.spiproj,spi/cell.spi,spi/notebook.spi,spi/directives.spi -Tests test/spiral/cells/directives_test.exs,test/spiral/cells/cell_test.exs,test/spiral/notebook_test.exs,test/spiral/document_test.exs -Label x-cells-c1c2. Node evidence: n9 (C1) and CELLS-IN-SPIRAL n9 (C1+C2). Window: after kit's TRAIN7c heavy window ends.")
inl note_of_cells_typed_directives_11 () = Note ("CELLS-TYPED-DIRECTIVES", 11u32, "2026-11-01T19:50Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:50 x: LANDED and closed green (cells_land_window x-cells-c1c2, 15:47, train-hold x-cells-c1c2 taken and released, window win-1793562439067). The pre-land baseline on the same live tree (dice and alphabet present) was 45 passed, 0 failed across directives_test, cell_test, notebook_test and document_test. After landing: 45 passed. n2's rule (equal to the pre-land result) is met. Landed hashes: package.spiproj 73D9D8FAA3E9, cell.spi 1C2358BB8E63, notebook.spi 74F7478DFDD2, directives.spi F00ABD35C3D6. The live domain.gleam was regenerated at 15:47:48 (0 NoToken, 5 MainFromExpression). Undo: spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\cells-20261101-154718. Unstaged in spiral for the user's commit: apps/cells/spi/{package.spiproj,cell.spi,notebook.spi,directives.spi}. The appliers are archived (one-shot). DONE.")
inl note_of_x_compat_via_cells_1 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VIA-CELLS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T03:37Z", "x", "2026-10-31 23:37 x: x's step (2) of POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE n9 (user 23:2x: spiral is X-compatible generically through Cells, and no repo references x). Today x's three call sites run the compiler's --x-compat: apps/client/build.ps1:23, apps/sim/build.ps1:112 and scripts/node-run.ps1:30 (verify's pre-check). NEXT STEP (CPU-light, no compile): replace each with 'mix spiral.x_compat --check <tree>' run in spiral/apps/cells with x's private MIX_BUILD_PATH (Get-XTargetDir sim-cells, the build sim-run.ps1 already makes). Exit 1 only on overweight, per main's n9. Drop the PostabilityProfile/SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE plumbing from x's build path, since Cells' built-in defaults apply. NODE-RUNNABLE PROOF after the edits: verify44 through x/scripts/node-run.ps1 -Mode verify. Pass rule, written now: exit 0, and the pre-check prints Cells' report with 0 overweight in apps/client and apps/sim/spi. main measured parity with the compiler under x's profile at 2/2 overweight on x/apps, so any other count means the two checks disagree; stop and report it to main. This unblocks the compiler retiring --x-compat (n9 step 3).")
inl note_of_x_compat_via_cells_2 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VIA-CELLS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T03:52Z", "x", "2026-10-31 23:52 x: edits DONE (unstaged):\n- x/scripts/common.ps1 gains Assert-CellsXCompat <path> <label>. It runs 'mix spiral.x_compat --check' in spiral/apps/cells with x's private MIX_BUILD_PATH (target/build/sim-cells), after mix deps.get, and resolves the path before Push-Location (memory ps-dotnet-relative-paths). It keeps x's stricter gate: the report must say 0 findings, not only 0 overweight.\n- apps/client/build.ps1 and apps/sim/build.ps1 call it in place of the compiler's --x-compat, and sim's now-unused second lib.ps1 load is gone.\n- x/scripts/node-run.ps1's verify pre-check uses the same helper.\n- CI: only the 'build' job runs these, and it has setup-beam with Elixir 1.20. xc-windows (-Xc) never reaches x-compat.\nBox check (no windows held, 42 s): apps/client 0 findings in 43 files. apps/sim/spi showed 2 OVERWEIGHT, sim_serve.spi:888 (365) and :905 (466). Both were MINE, from tonight's typed-caller slice: x-parity-typed2 never ran the x-compat step, and the parity in your n9 counted exactly these 2. Split: change_reply plus user_write, and replied into bearer_reply, keyed_reply and signed_reply with a 'bearer' predicate (caller_reply renamed reply_as). Recheck: 0 findings in 30 files, exit 0.\nverify43 STOPPED, superseded (sim_serve changed after it started, so its frames outputs couldn't land). Pack 11's core CA42B2E62EB69EA3 was installed 20:29 on box and node, and the D1C4 pin failed '| verifying)', so I re-pinned.\nNODE proof: verify44 (job-1793505156395, xqueue1, core CA42B2E62EB69EA3, -Accept, LandRepos x,spiral). This is the first x proof on pack 11. Pass rule (this item's n1): exit 0, and Cells' pre-check reports 0 findings in both trees. It also re-proves sim_serve's split through e2e and the sim build. If it passes, land its regenerated outputs with -Apply.")
inl note_of_x_compat_via_cells_3 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VIA-CELLS", 3u32, "2026-11-01T04:31Z", "x", "2026-11-01 00:30 x: HOLD per main (00:2x). The checker is being renamed generically to Spiral.Cells.CellBudget, mix spiral.cell_budget --check, finding kind over_budget. x's swap is already in the tree, unstaged: one helper, Assert-CellsXCompat in x/scripts/common.ps1, so the rename is a one-line change there plus the helper's name. Apps/sim's XCompat calls (x_weight, segments, shortened_url_weight) wait for main's exact renames. verify44 runs on the current name (it exists until the rename lands). x-parity-typed3 PASSED (job-1793505324963, pack 11 CA42B2E62EB69EA3: builds exit 0 including the Cells check, 153 = 153 rows, 0 differing). That also re-proves sim_serve's overweight split.")
inl note_of_x_compat_via_cells_4 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VIA-CELLS", 4u32, "2026-11-01T04:49Z", "x", "2026-11-01 00:49 x: HOLD lifted. main's CELL-BUDGET-NAMING landed 00:45, and x applied its exact map (unstaged): the helper is now Assert-CellBudget in x/scripts/common.ps1 running 'mix spiral.cell_budget --check' and requiring 'cell-budget report: 0 finding(s)'; the error tags are CELL-BUDGET; the callers are apps/client/build.ps1, apps/sim/build.ps1 and node-run.ps1's verify pre-check; and apps/client/README.md:791 is reworded. No old name is left in x's scripts or docs. x's sim calls no Cells module (it uses its own splitter::x_weight), so the module renames don't touch it. Box check after the rename, with no window held (91 s): apps/client 0 findings in 43 files, apps/sim/spi 0 in 30, matching main's 73. verify44 runs on a mirror synced BEFORE the rename, so it exercises the old task name. That still proves the build path, and the new name gets its node proof at verify45. The item closes when verify44 passes (n1's rule) and verify45 shows the cell-budget line.")
inl note_of_x_compat_via_cells_5 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VIA-CELLS", 5u32, "2026-11-01T05:12Z", "x", "2026-11-01 01:12 x: respell trap fixed (tibia's finding, relayed by main). apps/sim/build.ps1:114's lib-corpus filter now matches '^(test|:#) ' instead of '^test ', so it still finds test blocks after 'format --spelling symbols' rewrites test to :#. Checked on today's lib: new filter 73 files, old filter 73, equal before the respell, as expected. x's scripts and Spiral sources have no other test-keyword filter. It lands in the same unstaged batch, proven by verify45.")
inl note_of_x_compat_via_cells_6 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VIA-CELLS", 6u32, "2026-11-01T06:51Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:51 x: first NODE run of the renamed helper. x-parity-metrics1 (job-1793513794793, xqueue3, pack 11, mirror synced after main's 00:45 rename): the sim build and the client build both exit 0. Each calls Assert-CellBudget (mix spiral.cell_budget --check, requiring 'cell-budget report: 0 finding(s)'), so the new names work on the node in both trees. verify44 (old name, running) remains n1's formal proof; verify45 repeats this with the respell, the list-sugar heal and the guard (BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS n4).")
inl note_of_x_compat_via_cells_7 () = Note ("X-COMPAT-VIA-CELLS", 7u32, "2026-11-01T08:55Z", "x", "2026-11-01 04:55 x: verify44 (job-1793505156395, 4 h 57 min, xqueue1, pack 11 core CA42B2E62EB69EA3, compiler DFC86B91968D, -Accept) by n1's rule: PASS. Exit 0. The five 'x verify /' steps exit 0 (sim accept, client build before accepting, e2e accept, client build, sim build). All 43 client and 23 sim notebooks pass. The Cells pre-check gives 0 findings in apps/client (43 files) and apps/sim/spi (30), run as 'mix spiral.x_compat --check' since the mirror predated the rename. The new cell_budget name already passed on the node in both builds of x-parity-metrics1 and x-parity-lean1 (n6). CLOSED.")
inl note_of_lib_sweep_1031_red_1 () = Note ("LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED", 1u32, "2026-11-01T03:42Z", "main", "main 23:4x: next step, CPU-light: fetch the raw outputs from the node (C:\\home\\remote\\main-manifest\\lib-sweep\\raw\\file_system.spi.*, process.spi.*, rust_testing.spi.*) with remote.ps1 node fetch and read each failure message before any box run. process.spi was landed today (LIB-PROCESS-TEST-DEBRIS), so check that first. Full per-file timings are in the main session scratchpad lib-sweep-result\\lib-sweep.txt; xsd_emit took 43 min, the slowest file.")
inl note_of_lib_sweep_1031_red_2 () = Note ("LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED", 2u32, "2026-11-01T03:45Z", "main", "main 23:4x TRIAGE from the node's raw outputs (fetched to the main scratchpad lib-sweep-result\\raw\\raw). (1) file_system find_parent/find_parent_2: NOT a code regression; the test isn't hermetic. It looks for an ancestor dir named 'workspace' and fails with 'No parent for dir workspace at ...\\lib-sweep\\lib\\spiral (until c:\\)' because the sweep ran on a copy of lib outside the spiral tree (on the box, spiral\\workspace happens to exist). Fix: the test builds its own tmp tree with a workspace dir. (2) process split_args/split_command rust: failed at 137 s and 146 s, then the file's cell hit the 300 s timeout (headroom 0.95) and typescript was not_run, while the node ran 4 other lanes' jobs. Likely load or timeout, not proven; rerun alone on the node before touching code. (3) rust/testing run_tests_2/_3/_log (rust with encoding_rs): cargo builds of 167-296 s against a 300 s limit (headroom 0.99); the captured error is cut off. Same class as (2); rerun alone, and if it repeats, read the full cargo error. Next: fix (1) in lib (CPU-light, one test), and queue (2)+(3) as one node rerun when the node has a free slot.")
inl note_of_lib_sweep_1031_red_3 () = Note ("LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED", 3u32, "2026-11-01T04:49Z", "main", "main 00:5x CORRECTION to n2 (1): find_parent and find_parent_2 deliberately test the real layout. They walk up from get_source_directory / get_current_directory for a 'workspace' dir and a README.md and assert the parent is named 'spiral' or 'polyglot'. So they're right to fail when lib runs outside a spiral tree, and making them hermetic would change what they test. The cause is main's sweep: lib-sweep.ps1 robocopied lib to C:\\home\\remote\\main-manifest\\lib-sweep\\lib\\spiral, outside the mirror's spiral tree. Fix the sweep, not lib: run each file against the mirror's own spiral\\lib\\spiral (the lib_gate -Lib copy goes to a scratch spiral tree that keeps workspace\\ and README.md around it). Remaining real triage: (2) process split_args/split_command and (3) rust/testing, both at the 300 s limit on a 4-job node; rerun alone.")
inl note_of_lib_sweep_1031_red_4 () = Note ("LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED", 4u32, "2026-11-01T23:41Z", "main", "main 19:4x CROSS-OS LIB SWEEP (post-:: respell tree, same sources). LINUX (codespace, Ubuntu 24.04, 4 cores, idle): 77 files, 647 passed, 3 failed (erlang_map 1, process 1, reactors 1), 0 type errors. Results: kit drafts store CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS\\cs-lib-sweep.tsv. WINDOWS (node, saturated by 5-8 lane jobs, still running): 38 of ~77 files so far, 271 passed, 14 failed: listm' 12, float_native 1, math 1. Linux passes all three, so they are Windows-only or load-induced. Pass counts also differ on base (23 vs 25), date_time (26 vs 43) and file_system (20 vs 36): per-OS test sets, which the comparison must key per test, not per file. NEXT: main's node job main-listm-windows-detail runs listm' alone to get the error; erlang_map/process/reactors get Linux-only detail from a cloud run when its slot is free. File per-cause items once known.")
inl note_of_lib_sweep_1031_red_5 () = Note ("LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED", 5u32, "2026-11-02T00:10Z", "main", "main 20:1x DISCRIMINATED: listm' run ALONE on the node (job-1793576504128, a single lib_gate) passes 35/35 with 0 type errors. So the Windows sweep's 12 listm' failures (and probably float_native's 1 and math's 1) were LOAD REDS from the saturated node (7+ concurrent lane jobs), not Windows bugs. The Linux sweep's 3 reds (erlang_map 1, process 1, reactors 1, on an idle 4-core codespace) are the real candidates. NEXT: rerun those 3 alone on europa when its slot is free (one-job rule) to get their errors, then file per-cause items. Lesson, already on MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS: an over-admitted node turns a full sweep into noise; sweeps need a reserved slot or an idle machine.")
inl note_of_lib_sweep_1031_red_6 () = Note ("LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED", 6u32, "2026-11-02T00:59Z", "main", "main 21:0x the Windows node sweep ended at 41 of ~77 files (282 passed, 14 failed): it hit its 4 h job limit on the saturated node, ~6 min per file vs ~37 s per file on the idle 4-core codespace. Its 14 failures are float_native 1, listm' 12, math 1; listm' alone is 35/35 (load reds). The Linux-only reds (erlang_map, process, reactors) are queued on europa (main's chain). Lesson for MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS/NODE-RUN-VALUE: a full sweep on a shared saturated node is ~10x slower and noisy; run sweeps on an idle codespace (quota) or a reserved node slot.")
inl note_of_lib_sweep_1031_red_7 () = Note ("LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED", 7u32, "2026-11-05T05:30Z", "main", "11-05 01:30 main TRIAGE INPUT (Linux, europa, spiral head faf422c9 + box tree): file_system.spi with tonight's LIB-WORKSPACE-ROOT-DEPS draft, in a -Mirror copy at MemoryMax 2200M: 43 discovered, 21 passed, 6 FAILED, 16 unsupported. Failed: path_join_without_a_native_arm, get_current_directory, read_bytes_reads_a_written_text_file, map_f32s_views_floats_after_an_8_byte_header, read_f32s_reads_little_endian_floats_at_an_offset, read_prefix_reads_the_first_bytes. None touches the drafted code (deps_workspace_root/get_workspace_root), so they are presumed pre-existing; the europa control run of the unmodified file was killed twice: a GLOBAL OOM (2200M cap, neighbours' jobs) and its own 1500M memcg cap (the compiler alone holds ~1.1 GB). LESSON: file_system.spi's notebook gate needs ~2 GB and fits europa only when the VM is otherwise idle. The node job main-ws-proof runs control + draft on Windows in one window. NEXT: the same control on europa at 2200M in a quiet window to read the 6 Linux error texts, then fix or file per cause. The 10-31 find_parent / find_parent_2 reds are not in tonight's Linux failures.")
inl note_of_lib_sweep_1031_red_8 () = Note ("LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED", 8u32, "2026-11-05T06:24Z", "main", "11-05 02:23 main WINDOWS evidence (node job main-ws-proof, one window, node mirror main-proven = box tree): UNMODIFIED file_system.spi (A981A5951BF8) 40/42; failed path_join_without_a_native_arm and get_extension_keeps_the_dot_and_skips_dotfiles. With the LIB-WORKSPACE-ROOT-DEPS draft, 43/43 (both of those passed), so on Windows the two are flaky, not deterministic; the node's compiler was swapped pack38 -> pack40 at 02:05, possibly between the two gates, so those flips are inconclusive. path_join_without_a_native_arm also failed on Linux (europa), making it the strongest real-red candidate of the six Linux failures.")
inl note_of_lib_sweep_1031_red_9 () = Note ("LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED", 9u32, "2026-11-05T06:27Z", "main", "11-05 02:27 main CORRECTION to the previous note: the two Windows control \"failures\" are CELL TIMEOUTS, so VOID, not reds. The node control log says failed_targets {get_extension_keeps_the_dot_and_skips_dotfiles: [gleam], path_join_without_a_native_arm: [zig]} with \"Spiral cell timed out after 300s; the process tree was killed\", headroom 1.23 (slowest cell 368861 ms vs timeout_ms 300000), on a node at 100% CPU under TRAIN27. So there is NO deterministic Windows red in file_system.spi. The 6 Linux failures came from europa while it was loaded (it thrashed minutes later), so they too may be timeouts; their texts were never captured. ACTIONS: (1) rerun file_system.spi's gate on a quiet machine and read the error text of anything still failing; (2) its slowest cell sits near 300 s even uncontended, so give the slow Zig/Gleam blocks (or the file) a ///- --timeout header so a busy machine doesn't turn them into VOIDs (the lib owner's call; file_system.spi is shared lib).")
inl note_of_lib_sweep_1031_red_10 () = Note ("LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED", 10u32, "2026-11-05T06:58Z", "main", "11-05 02:58 main SETTLED on a QUIET machine (codespace, Ubuntu 24.04, 4 idle cores, spiral HEAD faf422c9 + fixed init): file_system.spi UNMODIFIED (A981A5951BF8) 41/41 passed (42 discovered, 1 unsupported on that image), headroom 0.09; the version landed tonight (AB1B2AFC5F06) 42/42, headroom 0.08. So all eight file_system \"failures\" seen tonight (6 on europa Linux, 2 on the node) were load artifacts / cell timeouts on overloaded machines; there is no real red in file_system.spi on either OS. The 10-31 find_parent/find_parent_2 reds are not reproduced either. Lesson: a red from a loaded shared machine needs a quiet rerun before it becomes an item; a 4-core codespace is the cheapest quiet machine.")
inl note_of_lib_sweep_1031_red_11 () = Note ("LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED", 11u32, "2026-11-05T07:15Z", "main", "11-05 03:15 main DONE, every 10-31 red triaged on today's tree: (1) file_system.spi find_parent/find_parent_2: not reproduced; tonight's 8 failures were load artifacts (41/41 and 42/42 on a quiet codespace, n9). (2) process.spi split_args/split_command: GREEN, 18/18 on the node (Windows, mirror main-sweep = box tree, headroom 0.61); fixed by the 11-03 process.spi rework (GRANULAR-SPLIT-PROCESS-FSHARP). (3) rust/testing.spi run_tests_2/_3/_log: the file moved to lib/spiral/rust_testing.spi in the 11-02 flattening; it is GREEN, 3/3 + 1 static on the node with pack41 (core 887F89D1821E70AA), headroom 0.55. No item needed for any of them.")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_1 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:26Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 01:35 fiscal: FOUND by validating the node t1031 samples against their XSDs (while extending family-gates.ps1). Two writer namespace defects. Both emitted documents fail their schemas and neither family is in family-gates yet. (1) CL EnvioDTE: SetDTE declares <xs:element ref=\"SiiDte:DTE\">; the writer emits the QName text SiiDte:DTE as the element name with no xmlns:SiiDte declaration, so the document is not even well-formed (\"SiiDte is an undeclared prefix\"). DTE is in the default namespace http://www.sii.cl/SiiDte, so it must be written <DTE>: a ref must resolve its prefix through the schema's namespace map, not copy it. (2) ES VeriFactu: Cabecera (an element of the SuministroLR namespace) has type sf:CabeceraType from SuministroInformacion.xsd (elementFormDefault qualified), so its children (ObligadoEmision ...) must be sf-qualified, and likewise the children of sf:RegistroAlta (IDVersion, IDFactura ...). The writer leaves them in the default namespace (\"element Cabecera has invalid child element ObligadoEmision\"). FIX in xsd_emit: qualify a local element by the namespace of the schema that declares it (honouring that schema's elementFormDefault), and resolve ref prefixes. DONE = both examples validate (validate-example.ps1 -Resolve; CL without the xmldsig preload) + rows added to family-gates.ps1 $resolved + typed round trip still holds.")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_2 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 2u32, "2026-11-01T04:27Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 01:45 fiscal: CODE POINTERS (read, not drafted). (a) xsd.spi:230 particle_of stores an Element's name as the raw name, or else the raw ref attribute, so a ref keeps its schema prefix (SiiDte:DTE) and nothing resolves it later. (b) xsd_resolve.spi:102 qualify_reference already resolves TYPE references through the schema's xmlns map into {uri}local, and :197 renames definitions to {uri}name (renamed_definition (qualified target_namespace)). Element tag names get neither treatment. PLAN: during resolution, rename prefixed Element names with the same namespace map: same namespace as the document root -> the bare local name; another namespace -> carry the uri so the writer qualifies it (the path imported top-level elements like ds:Signature already take). For (2), a type's namespace is recoverable from its {uri}name, so the reader/writer for a Complex type from another namespace can qualify its local elements when that schema is elementFormDefault qualified (record the form at merge, since merge drops it today). Tests: a two-schema fixture (an import with qualified form plus a same-namespace ref), asserting the written names, then a typed round trip. Lib land (xsd.spi + xsd_resolve.spi + xsd_emit.spi), then regenerate cl_envio + es_verifactu.")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_3 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 3u32, "2026-11-01T05:03Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 03:35 fiscal: MAPPED (Explore pass over xsd.spi / xsd_resolve.spi / xsd_emit.spi / generator.spi) and FIX (1) APPLIED.\nHow names flow today: Element tag names are never renamed during resolution (xsd_resolve renamed: the Element arm touches only type_ref/inline). The raw name=/ref= text reaches the writer as the literal tag (xsd_emit value_node/occurs_part :1486-1499). Readers match local names only (xml.spi children_named/count_named), so prefixes never matter when reading. Root prefixes come from generator.spi prefix_declarations/declared_prefixes, which declared xmlns:p only for prefixes used in ref=\"p: whose uri is not the target, xsd or xmldsig namespace.\n(1) CL EnvioDTE: SiiDte maps to the TARGET namespace, so it was skipped and <SiiDte:DTE> went undeclared. FIX (fiscal src/generator.spi, unstaged): the target is no longer excluded, and the now-unused target parameter is removed. A scan of every root schema (static and dynamic roots: DFe.NET, CR, UBL maindoc) shows only EnvioDTE_v10 has a target-mapped ref prefix, so only generated_cl_envio_dte_v10 changes. SIMULATED on the node sample (xmlns:SiiDte added): well-formed and namespace-valid; one error left, which is defect (3).\n(3) NEW: builtin_example writes \"ID1\" for EVERY xs:ID, so SetDTE ID and Documento ID collide (\"ID1 is already used as an ID\"). FIX belongs in xsd_emit's example values (a per-site counter or the owner name, e.g. ID_SetDTE), lib land like the Canton fix.\n(2) VeriFactu PLAN (lib): xsd lib never reads elementFormDefault; merge keeps one targetNamespace. In xsd_resolve qualify_schema (:196, per file before merge, where text, target and xmlns map are in scope; load_tree :258), when the file is qualified and its target is not the root's, rename its local element tags to p:local with the file's own prefix, carry the (p, uri) pairs through merge, and have generator prefix_declarations emit them on the root. Do not use {uri}local tags (xml.local_name splits at the first colon). Inline type names derived from prefixed tags (xsd_emit :2042, :2172) need xml.local_name or they move.\nFix (1) is packed into the edition-3 regen overlay (t1101 generator 367522F1), so that proof's unexpected-change list must contain exactly generated_cl_envio_dte_v10 (the root's xmlns:SiiDte).")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_4 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 4u32, "2026-11-01T06:44Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 02:48 fiscal: DEFECT (3) located. xsd_emit.spi value_example (:1536) emits a constant literal for a builtin KindText reference, escape_text (builtin_example reference), so every xs:ID site gets \"ID1\" (builtin_example :836). It receives only (kind, t, reference, variant); the variant expression (k + n) restarts per example function, so it cannot make IDs unique. FIX PLAN: thread the owning type name (the record walk knows it) into value_example for ID references and emit \"ID_<owner>_<field>\" (an NCName, unique per declaration site), with a test: two records each with an xs:ID attribute produce distinct example IDs. The CL EnvioDTE example then validates (simulated: the prefix fix leaves this as the only error).")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_5 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 5u32, "2026-11-01T06:46Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 02:49 fiscal: DEFECT (3) DESIGN refined: the example value of an attribute is chosen in the walker prototype instance \":+ attribute_step exampling = \\ _ acc a\" (xsd_emit :1985-1998, value_example a.kind a.t (reference_text r) \"k\"), which sees the attribute (name \"ID\" at both sites) but not its owner. Only record_texts (:1978) learns the owning type ty, after the parts are text. So a unique ID needs the owner threaded through the attribute_step prototype (all instances: reading, writing, staged, exampling), or record_texts passing ty into the parts it builds. Prefer threading the owner (a typed parameter, no text post-processing). Size: one prototype signature + its instances + the record walk call; then emit \"ID_\" ++ owner for builtin xs:ID references.")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_6 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 6u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 08:36 fiscal: QUALITY SPEC (groom round 4). PRIORITY P0: two of the four held-back CI examples (cl_envio_dte_v10, es_verifactu) fail their XSD only because of this; XSD is the lane's top priority.\nDONE WHEN: (a) one node gate on a lib copy runs xsd.spi, xsd_resolve.spi and xsd_emit.spi with 0 failed, including 3 new tests: a_same_namespace_ref_writes_its_local_name, a_qualified_import_writes_its_prefix, example_ids_are_unique_per_owner; (b) after regeneration, scripts/validate-example.ps1 -Resolve gives \"validation messages 0\" for samples/generated_cl_envio_dte_v10_example.xml (no xmldsig preload) and generated_es_verifactu_example.xml; (c) both rows are added to scripts/family-gates.ps1 $resolved and CI's target/ci-metrics/examples.tsv shows Messages 0 for them.\nNEXT STEP: copy lib/spiral/xsd.spi, xsd_resolve.spi and xsd_emit.spi to a scratch lib. In xsd_resolve.spi qualify_schema (:196), add a tag pass beside renamed_definition/renamed_reference (:109-127), run per file before merge (:16), where the target namespace and declared_namespaces (:85) are in scope.\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN: element names stop being colon strings. Add a phantom-indexed name like the existing type_reference form / definition_name form: \":! element_name form = string\" built only by the resolver. A raw ref text lives in xsd ... written; qualify_schema is the only function from written to qualified and yields a union \":| qualified_name = | Local : tag_name | Prefixed : string * namespace_uri * tag_name\". So an unresolved \"SiiDte:DTE\" cannot reach the emitter (type error), and xml.local_name splitting at ':' disappears from emitter code. elementFormDefault becomes \":| element_form = | QualifiedForm | UnqualifiedForm\", read once at load (no \"qualified\" string compares per element; generator.spi:462 does one today). Defect 3: thread the owner into the walker prototype attribute_step (:1985) as a typed definition_name; an xs:ID example becomes \"ID_\" ++ owner, not the literal \"ID1\". No sentinels; missing prefixes are option, not \"\". MODEL: xsd.spi''s own phantom forms (:28-46).")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_7 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 7u32, "2026-11-01T20:24Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 16:2x: ALL THREE PARTS DRAFTED against the :: lib (live bases: xsd_resolve BA5D4CDD2F813875, xsd_emit 3CC9CE2461F2BAB4; xsd.spi unchanged), with gates running on Europa (Linux pre-check, compiler core 7B234DAC2373). The landing verdict will be a node gate with the box compiler (DBC5EC18F14C). Drafts: fiscal session scratchpad xsd-ids/ (bases kept beside them as *.base.spi).\nDEFECT 3 (xsd_emit 3127EB334396C256): smaller than the planned prototype threading. attribute_view gains \"owner : string\", filled where attributes_walk builds the view (it already had owner), so every attribute_step instance sees it with no signature change. The exampling instance writes \"ID_<type_name owner>\" for a builtin xs:ID attribute (a valid NCName; type_name prefixes x_). New test example_ids_are_unique_per_owner (SetDTE and Documento each with an xs:ID attribute: distinct IDs, no \"ID1\").\nFIX 1+2 (xsd_resolve 3275B7837B933997): a per-file tag pass (tag_schema with tag_scope) runs before qualify_schema, inside load_tree, which now carries a document_scope (root namespace + document prefixes). Rule: a ref p:N resolves p through the FILE's xmlns map; if that lands in the root namespace, write N; otherwise write <root prefix>:N. A local element of an elementFormDefault=\"qualified\" file outside the root namespace becomes <root prefix>:N. Top-level element names are untouched. Only DOCUMENT prefixes are used: the generator's declared_prefixes rule (xmlns:p bound on the root, not XMLSchema/xmldsig, and ref=\"p: in the root text) is mirrored in the lib as declared_document_prefixes, so a tag can never get an undeclared prefix. That keeps the xmldsig subtree bare, as before. Readers are unaffected (xml children_named/count_named compare local_name on BOTH sides; field names use local_name). Public API unchanged (load_schema_tree_with, qualify_schema, finalize_names). New tests: a_same_namespace_ref_writes_its_local_name (SiiDte:DTE -> DTE) and a_qualified_import_writes_its_prefix (sf:ObligadoEmision; bare without a document prefix; bare when unqualified).\nPREDICTED REGEN DIFF: only generated_cl_envio_dte_v10 (SiiDte:DTE -> DTE) and generated_es_verifactu (sf: children); UBL and DFe.NET refs already use the root's own prefixes. FOLLOW-UP: the generator should call the lib's declared_document_prefixes instead of its own copy (one source).")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_8 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 8u32, "2026-11-01T21:40Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 17:3x: THIRD INSTANCE found, HU NAV invoiceData 3.0. The CI sample (run 41314140694) fails validation with \"element supplierTaxNumber in namespace .../OSA/3.0/data has invalid child element taxpayerId in namespace .../OSA/3.0/data\" (validate-example with -Preload common.xsd,invoiceBase.xsd; 2 messages). taxpayerId belongs to the imported base schema (qualified). The drafted tag pass will NOT fix it as written: the root uses base: only in type= attributes, never in ref=, so under the generator-equivalent declared_document_prefixes rule (ref=\"p: required) the document never declares base. FOLLOW-UP DESIGN: document prefixes = every root xmlns binding except XMLSchema/xmldsig (drop the ref= condition), changed in lockstep in the lib and the generator (the generator then calls the lib function, one source). That changes every unit whose root binds unused prefixes (extra harmless xmlns on the root), so it needs its own regen proof. Coverage today: 35 of 44 CI samples are validated by family-gates. Uncovered: cl_envio + es_verifactu (this item), hu_nav (this follow-up), pt_saft (XSD 1.1 xs:assert, unsupported by .NET XmlSchemaSet; needs an assert-stripped copy made at CI time or an XSD 1.1 validator), and 5 UBL documents, which validate and are added to family-gates now.")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_9 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 9u32, "2026-11-02T00:30Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 20:3x REGEN READING (land gate round 2, node t1101, SPIRAL_CELLS_LIB = staged lib): xsd 9/9, xsd_resolve 6/6 (incl. both new tests), xsd_emit 28/28 (24 in round 1 + 4 VOID rows rerun; rustc temp-dir os error 3 from parallel Cells jobs). cl_envio: IDs now owner-unique (ID_x_envio_dte_set_dte, ID_x_dte_def_type_choice_documento_documento; no \"ID1\"), and children_named \"SiiDte:DTE\" is gone. es_verifactu: 2196 changed lines, because qualified tags now carry sf: AND identifiers derived from tags carry it too (x_persona_fisica_juridica_type_choice_sf_nif, cases ..._sf_NIF_sf_NIF; field names stay local, choice_nif). DECISION: not a regression. It is the established convention: generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 already has 8764 prefix-derived identifiers (InvoiceType_field_cbc_UBLVersionID ...). Whether identifiers should drop prefixes is a separate, codebase-wide style question for FISCAL-GENERATED-STYLE, because changing it would rename every UBL unit.")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_10 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 10u32, "2026-11-02T00:42Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 20:4x: GATE GREEN (node t1101, box compiler DBC5EC18F14C: xsd 9/9, xsd_resolve 6/6, xsd_emit 28/28, cl_envio + es_verifactu regenerated, examples written and validated 0 messages by the hardened validator). Land program for XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES (defect 3 + fix 1/2), drafted on live bases xsd_resolve BA5D4CDD2F813875 and xsd_emit 3CC9CE2461F2BAB4; targets xsd_resolve 3275B7837B933997 and xsd_emit 3127EB334396C256.\r\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\r\nedit xsd_resolve.spi\r\npatch <<<\r\n@@ -197,6 +197,92 @@\r\n     := definitions = renamed_definition (qualified (namespace_text schema::target_namespace))\r\n     rename_schema definitions (renamed_reference (qualify_reference namespaces)) schema\r\n \r\n+:== tag_scope =\r\n+    {\r\n+        root_namespace : string\r\n+        document_prefixes : list (string * string)\r\n+        file_namespace : string\r\n+        file_namespaces : list (string * string)\r\n+        qualified_locals : bool\r\n+    }\r\n+\r\n+:~ ^ declared_document_prefixes\r\n+    (text : string) (attributes : list (string * string)) (acc : list (string * string))\r\n+    : list (string * string)\r\n+    =\r\n+    ?= attributes ?|\r\n+    | (key, value) :: rest =>\r\n+        := known = value = \"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema\" || value = \"http://www.w3.org/2000/09/xmldsig#\"\r\n+        := prefix = ? sm'::starts_with \"xmlns:\" key ?> sm'::substring 6i32 (sm'::length key - 6i32) key |: \"\"\r\n+        ? prefix <> \"\" && known = !- && sm'::contains (\"ref=\\\"\" ++# prefix ++# \":\") text\r\n+        ?> declared_document_prefixes text rest ((prefix, value) :: acc)\r\n+        |: declared_document_prefixes text rest acc\r\n+    | [] => acc\r\n+\r\n+:= schema_attributes (text : string) : list (string * string) =\r\n+    ?= first_named \"schema\" (xml::read_xml text) ?|\r\n+    | Some (attributes, _) => attributes\r\n+    | None => []\r\n+\r\n+:~ ^ prefix_for (uri : string) (prefixes : list (string * string)) : option string =\r\n+    ?= prefixes ?|\r\n+    | (prefix, value) :: rest => ? value = uri ?> Some prefix |: prefix_for uri rest\r\n+    | [] => None\r\n+\r\n+:= tag_in (scope : tag_scope) (uri : string) (local : string) (fallback : string) : string =\r\n+    ? uri = scope::root_namespace ?> local\r\n+    |:\r\n+        ?= prefix_for uri scope::document_prefixes ?|\r\n+        | Some prefix => prefix ++# \":\" ++# local\r\n+        | None => fallback\r\n+\r\n+:= scoped_tag (scope : tag_scope) (name : string) : string =\r\n+    ? sm'::contains \":\" name ?>\r\n+        ?= namespace_for (prefix_of name) scope::file_namespaces ?|\r\n+        | Some uri => tag_in scope uri (xml::local_name name) name\r\n+        | None => name\r\n+    |? scope::qualified_locals ?> tag_in scope scope::file_namespace name name\r\n+    |: name\r\n+\r\n+:~ ^ tagged \\* k a. (scope : tag_scope) (item : xsd k a) : xsd k a =\r\n+    ?= item ?|\r\n+    | Simple s => Simple s\r\n+    | Complex c => Complex { c ?| content = tagged scope c::content }\r\n+    | Derived d => Derived { d ?| content = tagged scope d::content }\r\n+    | Sequence s => Sequence { s ?| items = tagged_all scope s::items [] }\r\n+    | Choice c => Choice { c ?| items = tagged_all scope c::items [] }\r\n+    | Element e =>\r\n+        Element { e ?| name = tag_name (scoped_tag scope (tag_text e::name)); inline = tagged_inline scope e::inline }\r\n+    | AnyContent () => AnyContent ()\r\n+    | Empty () => Empty ()\r\n+    | Attribute a => Attribute a\r\n+\r\n+:& :~ tagged_all \\* k a. (scope : tag_scope) (items : list (xsd k a)) (acc : list (xsd k a)) : list (xsd k a) =\r\n+    ?= items ?|\r\n+    | item :: rest => tagged_all scope rest (tagged scope item :: acc)\r\n+    | [] => listm::rev acc\r\n+\r\n+:& :~ tagged_inline \\* a. (scope : tag_scope) (inline : option (xsd type_def a)) : option (xsd type_def a) =\r\n+    ?= inline ?|\r\n+    | Some inner => Some (tagged scope inner)\r\n+    | None => None\r\n+\r\n+:~ ^ tagged_top \\* a.\r\n+    (scope : tag_scope) (items : list (xsd particle a)) (acc : list (xsd particle a))\r\n+    : list (xsd particle a)\r\n+    =\r\n+    ?= items ?|\r\n+    | (Element e) :: rest => tagged_top scope rest (Element { e ?| inline = tagged_inline scope e::inline } :: acc)\r\n+    | item :: rest => tagged_top scope rest (item :: acc)\r\n+    | [] => listm::rev acc\r\n+\r\n+:= tag_schema \\* a. (scope : tag_scope) (schema : xsd_schema a) : xsd_schema a =\r\n+    {\r\n+        schema ?|\r\n+            complex_types = tagged_all scope schema::complex_types []\r\n+            elements = tagged_top scope schema::elements []\r\n+    }\r\n+\r\n := qualified_local (name : string) : string =\r\n     := close = sm'::index_of \"}\" name\r\n     ? sm'::starts_with \"{\" name && close > 0i32\r\n@@ -250,18 +336,48 @@\r\n         | None => catalogued_namespaces catalog rest acc\r\n     | [] => acc\r\n \r\n-:~ ^ load_tree (catalog : schema_catalog) (path : string) (tree : loaded_tree qualified) : loaded_tree qualified =\r\n+:== document_scope = { root_namespace : string; document_prefixes : list (string * string) }\r\n+\r\n+:= attribute_text (key : string) (attributes : list (string * string)) : string =\r\n+    ?= xml::attribute_of key attributes ?|\r\n+    | Some value => value\r\n+    | None => \"\"\r\n+\r\n+:= document_scope_of (text : string) : document_scope =\r\n+    := attributes = schema_attributes text\r\n+    {\r\n+        root_namespace = attribute_text \"targetNamespace\" attributes\r\n+        document_prefixes = declared_document_prefixes text attributes []\r\n+    }\r\n+\r\n+:= file_scope (document : document_scope) (attributes : list (string * string)) : tag_scope =\r\n+    {\r\n+        root_namespace = document::root_namespace\r\n+        document_prefixes = document::document_prefixes\r\n+        file_namespace = attribute_text \"targetNamespace\" attributes\r\n+        file_namespaces = declared_namespaces attributes []\r\n+        qualified_locals = attribute_text \"elementFormDefault\" attributes = \"qualified\"\r\n+    }\r\n+\r\n+:~ ^ load_tree\r\n+    (catalog : schema_catalog) (document : document_scope) (path : string) (tree : loaded_tree qualified)\r\n+    : loaded_tree qualified\r\n+    =\r\n     ? has_string path tree::paths ?> tree\r\n     |? xml::is_file path = !- ?> { tree ?| missing = (path :: tree::missing) }\r\n     |:\r\n         := text = xml::read_text_replacing_invalid_utf8 path\r\n-        := schema = text |> read_schema |> qualify_schema (schema_namespaces text)\r\n+        := attributes = schema_attributes text\r\n+        := schema =\r\n+            text |> read_schema |> tag_schema (file_scope document attributes)\r\n+            |> qualify_schema (declared_namespaces attributes [])\r\n         := loaded = { tree ?| paths = (path :: tree::paths); schema = merge schema tree::schema }\r\n         := locations = listm::append schema::includes (catalogued_namespaces catalog schema::namespace_imports [])\r\n-        load_includes catalog (directory_of path) locations loaded\r\n+        load_includes catalog document (directory_of path) locations loaded\r\n \r\n :& :~ load_includes\r\n     (catalog : schema_catalog)\r\n+    (document : document_scope)\r\n     (directory : string)\r\n     (locations : list string)\r\n     (tree : loaded_tree qualified)\r\n@@ -269,8 +385,9 @@\r\n     =\r\n     ?= locations ?|\r\n     | location :: rest =>\r\n-        := tree = ? location = \"\" ?> tree |: load_tree catalog (resolved_location catalog directory location) tree\r\n-        load_includes catalog directory rest tree\r\n+        := next = resolved_location catalog directory location\r\n+        := tree = ? location = \"\" ?> tree |: load_tree catalog document next tree\r\n+        load_includes catalog document directory rest tree\r\n     | [] => tree\r\n \r\n := load_schema_tree_with (catalog : schema_catalog) (path : string) : loaded_tree final =\r\n@@ -283,7 +400,10 @@\r\n             complex_types = []\r\n             elements = []\r\n         }\r\n-    := tree = load_tree catalog path { paths = []; missing = []; schema = empty }\r\n+    := document =\r\n+        ? xml::is_file path ?> document_scope_of (xml::read_text_replacing_invalid_utf8 path)\r\n+        |: { root_namespace = \"\"; document_prefixes = [] }\r\n+    := tree = load_tree catalog document path { paths = []; missing = []; schema = empty }\r\n     { tree ?| schema = finalize_names tree::schema }\r\n \r\n := load_schema_tree (path : string) : loaded_tree final = load_schema_tree_with [] path\r\n@@ -315,6 +435,66 @@\r\n     | Some inner => references_of inner acc\r\n     | None => acc\r\n \r\n+:~ ^ tags_of \\* k form. (item : xsd k form) (acc : list string) : list string =\r\n+    ?= item ?|\r\n+    | Simple _ => acc\r\n+    | Complex c => tags_of c::content acc\r\n+    | Derived d => tags_of d::content acc\r\n+    | Sequence s => tags_all s::items acc\r\n+    | Choice c => tags_all c::items acc\r\n+    | Element e => inline_tags e::inline (tag_text e::name :: acc)\r\n+    | AnyContent () => acc\r\n+    | Empty () => acc\r\n+    | Attribute _ => acc\r\n+\r\n+:& :~ tags_all \\* k form. (items : list (xsd k form)) (acc : list string) : list string =\r\n+    ?= items ?|\r\n+    | item :: rest => tags_all rest (tags_of item acc)\r\n+    | [] => acc\r\n+\r\n+:& :~ inline_tags \\* form. (inline : option (xsd type_def form)) (acc : list string) : list string =\r\n+    ?= inline ?|\r\n+    | Some inner => tags_of inner acc\r\n+    | None => acc\r\n+\r\n+///> rust\r\n+:# a_same_namespace_ref_writes_its_local_name =\r\n+    := text =\r\n+        \"<xs:schema xmlns:xs=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema\\\" xmlns:SiiDte=\\\"urn:sii\\\" targetNamespace=\\\"urn:sii\\\">\"\r\n+        ++# \"<xs:element name=\\\"DTE\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\"/><xs:complexType name=\\\"SetDTE\\\"><xs:sequence>\"\r\n+        ++# \"<xs:element ref=\\\"SiiDte:DTE\\\"/></xs:sequence></xs:complexType></xs:schema>\"\r\n+    := schema = tag_schema (file_scope (document_scope_of text) (schema_attributes text)) (read_schema text)\r\n+    := tags = tags_all schema::complex_types []\r\n+    ? has_string \"DTE\" tags = !- || has_string \"SiiDte:DTE\" tags\r\n+    ?> failwith \"a ref into the root namespace writes the bare local name\"\r\n+\r\n+///> rust\r\n+:# a_qualified_import_writes_its_prefix =\r\n+    := xs = \"xmlns:xs=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema\\\"\"\r\n+    := root_text =\r\n+        \"<xs:schema \" ++# xs ++# \" xmlns:sfLR=\\\"urn:lr\\\" xmlns:sf=\\\"urn:sf\\\" targetNamespace=\\\"urn:lr\\\"\"\r\n+        ++# \" elementFormDefault=\\\"qualified\\\"><xs:element name=\\\"Cabecera\\\" type=\\\"sf:CabeceraType\\\"/>\"\r\n+        ++# \"<xs:complexType name=\\\"R\\\"><xs:sequence><xs:element ref=\\\"sf:RegistroAlta\\\"/>\"\r\n+        ++# \"<xs:element name=\\\"Own\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\"/></xs:sequence></xs:complexType></xs:schema>\"\r\n+    := imported form =\r\n+        \"<xs:schema \" ++# xs ++# \" xmlns:sf=\\\"urn:sf\\\" targetNamespace=\\\"urn:sf\\\"\" ++# form\r\n+        ++# \"><xs:complexType name=\\\"CabeceraType\\\"><xs:sequence>\"\r\n+        ++# \"<xs:element name=\\\"ObligadoEmision\\\" type=\\\"xs:string\\\"/></xs:sequence></xs:complexType></xs:schema>\"\r\n+    := document = document_scope_of root_text\r\n+    := tags_in scope text =\r\n+        := schema = tag_schema (file_scope scope (schema_attributes text)) (read_schema text)\r\n+        tags_all schema::complex_types []\r\n+    := qualified_text = imported \" elementFormDefault=\\\"qualified\\\"\"\r\n+    ? has_string \"sf:ObligadoEmision\" (tags_in document qualified_text) = !-\r\n+    ?> failwith \"a local element of a qualified imported schema takes the root's prefix\"\r\n+    := root_tags = tags_in document root_text\r\n+    ? has_string \"sf:RegistroAlta\" root_tags = !- || has_string \"Own\" root_tags = !-\r\n+    ?> failwith \"the root keeps a foreign ref's prefix and writes its own locals bare\"\r\n+    ? has_string \"ObligadoEmision\" (tags_in { document ?| document_prefixes = [] } qualified_text) = !-\r\n+    ?> failwith \"without a declared document prefix the name stays bare, never an undeclared prefix\"\r\n+    ? has_string \"ObligadoEmision\" (tags_in document (imported \"\")) = !-\r\n+    ?> failwith \"an unqualified schema writes its locals bare\"\r\n+\r\n ///> rust\r\n :# colliding_local_names_keep_both_namespaces =\r\n     := xs = \"xmlns:xs=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema\\\"\"\r\n>>>\r\nedit xsd_emit.spi\r\npatch <<<\r\n@@ -1640,6 +1640,7 @@\r\n \r\n :== attribute_view form =\r\n     {\r\n+        owner : string\r\n         name : string\r\n         required : bool\r\n         fixed : option string\r\n@@ -1993,7 +1994,10 @@\r\n                 ? a::inline ?> a::t ++# \"_example k\"\r\n                 |:\r\n                     ?= a::type_ref ?|\r\n-                    | Some r => value_example a::kind a::t (reference_text r) \"k\"\r\n+                    | Some r =>\r\n+                        ? is_builtin_reference (reference_text r) && xml::local_name (reference_text r) = \"ID\"\r\n+                        ?> \"\\\"ID_\" ++# type_name a::owner ++# \"\\\"\"\r\n+                        |: value_example a::kind a::t (reference_text r) \"k\"\r\n                     | None => value_example a::kind a::t \"\" \"k\"\r\n     push (field_assignment (field_name a::name) value) [] acc\r\n :+ element_arm exampling = \\ _ kinds choice_name v =>\r\n@@ -2283,6 +2287,7 @@\r\n         := inline = ?= a::inline ?| Some _ => !+ | None => !-\r\n         := view =\r\n             {\r\n+                owner = owner\r\n                 name = tag_text a::name\r\n                 required = a::required\r\n                 fixed = a::fixed\r\n@@ -3173,6 +3178,23 @@\r\n     ? simple_example \"xs:positiveInteger\" (Pattern \"[0-9]{1,3}\" :: []) <> \"1\"\r\n     ?> failwith \"a positiveInteger pattern that takes 1 keeps 1\"\r\n \r\n+///> rust\r\n+:# example_ids_are_unique_per_owner =\r\n+    := schema =\r\n+        read_schema (\r\n+            \"<xs:schema><xs:complexType name=\\\"SetDTE\\\"><xs:sequence/>\"\r\n+            ^^ \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"ID\\\" type=\\\"xs:ID\\\" use=\\\"required\\\"/></xs:complexType>\"\r\n+            ^^ \"<xs:complexType name=\\\"Documento\\\"><xs:sequence/>\"\r\n+            ^^ \"<xs:attribute name=\\\"ID\\\" type=\\\"xs:ID\\\" use=\\\"required\\\"/></xs:complexType></xs:schema>\"\r\n+        )\r\n+    := generated = generate_module (xsd_resolve::finalize_names (xsd_resolve::qualify_schema [] schema))\r\n+    ? sm'::contains (\"\\\"ID_\" ++# type_name \"SetDTE\" ++# \"\\\"\") generated = !-\r\n+    ?> failwith \"the SetDTE example ID names its owner\"\r\n+    ? sm'::contains (\"\\\"ID_\" ++# type_name \"Documento\" ++# \"\\\"\") generated = !-\r\n+    ?> failwith \"the Documento example ID names its owner\"\r\n+    ? sm'::contains \"\\\"ID1\\\"\" generated\r\n+    ?> failwith \"no two xs:ID sites share the constant ID1\"\r\n+\r\n ///> rust\r\n :# coded_types_share_the_text_prototype =\r\n     := coded = union_text \"TUf\" [ \"SP\"; \"RJ\" ] \"\"\r\n>>>")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_11 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 11u32, "2026-11-02T02:03Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 22:02:35 LANDED (lib-guarded-do, no live reader; TRAIN8s snapshot readers exempted by window and ancestry): live xsd_resolve 3275B7837B933997 and xsd_emit 3127EB334396C256, exactly the gated targets (program n10, 9 replacements). Undo: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\undo\\XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES-n10 holds the xsd_resolve base; the xsd_emit base (3CC9CE2461F2BAB4) is in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES\\xsd_emit.base.spi. Fiscal side unstaged: the 2 regenerated units + the cl_envio and verifactu family-gates rows. Commit ORDER: user spiral push first, then fiscal. REMAINING to close: CI examples.tsv shows cl_envio + es_verifactu Messages 0 after both pushes. HU NAV (document prefixes beyond ref=) stays a follow-up.")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_12 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 12u32, "2026-11-02T11:50Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-02 07:51 DECIDED (user 11-02: \"those things can be decided autonomously\"), un-parking the prefix style question: generated identifiers KEEP their namespace prefixes (e.g. verifactu sf_*). RULE, fixed first: generated names must be collision-free across every namespace in a closure and stable across regens. Dropping the prefixes brings back exactly the cross-namespace collisions this item fixes, and the landed lib (xsd_resolve 3275B7837B933997) already writes them. No code change. Close condition unchanged: CI examples.tsv shows cl_envio + es_verifactu at Messages 0 after the spiral push and the fiscal commit.")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_13 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 13u32, "2026-11-02T12:27Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-02 08:3x CI evidence. The spiral push a8dc6b62 (10:09Z, xsd_resolve 3275B7837B933997 / xsd_emit 3127EB334396C256) landed before fiscal ddac819 (run 41427219939). GOAL MET on both OSes: generated_cl_envio_dte_v10_example.xml shows validation messages 0 (signature pending 2) and generated_es_verifactu_example.xml shows 0; cl_dte and all 6 UBL examples are also at 0. Every test file passed (generator 23/23, corpus_checks 26/26, canon 1/1). RED anyway: build.ps1's drift gate found \"7 files differ from the committed copy\" (generated_cl_dte_v10 + the 6 generated_ubl_*_2_1 units). My land regenerated only cl_envio + verifactu, but the per-owner xs:ID examples (ID_<owner>) and the prefix scoping reach those 7 too. 9093af3 (LICENSE only) will fail the same way. FIX in progress: a local full regen with a lib byte-equal to spiral main (5 xsd/xml files checked); the local drift must be exactly CI's 7 files (A/A), then they are left unstaged for the user's commit. 95%: closes on the first green fiscal run after that commit.")
inl note_of_xsd_cross_namespace_names_14 () = Note ("XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES", 14u32, "2026-11-03T09:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 05:57 fiscal: DONE: its closing condition (the first green fiscal run with the regenerated units committed; cl_envio and verifactu at 0 validation messages) is met by CI run 41598162815 (push 741c715 with spiral 98e51d32), ubuntu-24.04 AND windows-2025: \"build.ps1 / generated / matches the committed copy\", generated_fiscal_runtime 3/3, generated_pt_saft_1_04_01 2/2, \"all 87 test files green and every example validates\".")
inl note_of_tibia_wipe_damage_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-WIPE-DAMAGE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:30Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 tibia retro. LOST: (1) every tibia memory (tibia-dogfood-project, tibia-other-session, tibia-log-stamps, no-hand-crafted-lua, wow-after-tibia) came back only as PARTIAL rebuilds from backlog notes: the rules survive, many of the user's original wordings and reasons do not. (2) Session history: pre-wipe transcripts gone; this session works from compaction summaries rebuilt from the backlog. (3) %TEMP% drafts: the edition-3 scripts (ed3-apply, trim, identity-after; redone 10-30), the 16 lib-slice drafts (rescued from the node''s remote-stage, re-merged 3-way and re-gated 10-30, landed 10-30), and the DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS prototype (the one true loss: no copy anywhere; healing''s 10-31 census confirmed it). (4) MariaDB world data: restored 10-29 from rescue dumps (47 tables); tibia-local rebuilt 10-30 (canary, otclient, mariadb-data). (5) Old arena hunter logs, so pz-in-traces'' real-log side-by-side moved to TIBIA''s resume. REWORK: about 1.5 lane-days (10-29 recovery and MariaDB, 10-30 regen, regate and world rebuild). WEAKER PRACTICES (the felt part): the TEMP-draft habit is what made the loss total; after it the lane drifted the other way (C:\\\\home\\\\data drafts, a lab dir, tools left in the scratchpad) until the user called it out 10-30; memories without their reasons get applied too literally or too loosely; 10-31 had avoidable false leads (a node red blamed on lib before checking the node compiler; a gate proven on the box with an untracked input). HEAL TASKS FILED: TIBIA-MEMORY-REASONS (re-derive the 5 tibia memories'' lost reasons from backlog quotes), TIBIA-HARVEST (extract value before the pivot), DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS stays the redo of the lost prototype (40%, re-planned under TIBIA-HARVEST''s lessons). Practices now in memory as guards: work-in-the-repo, test-outputs-to-temp, postability-generic-no-x, lib-landing-under-churn.")
inl note_of_tibia_wipe_damage_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-WIPE-DAMAGE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:02Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 08:02 tibia groom: CLOSED. The item's scope (what the wipe cost tibia, plus the heal tasks it implies) is complete in n1: losses, rework (about 1.5 lane-days), weaker practices and their memory guards. The heal work lives in its own items: TIBIA-MEMORY-REASONS (Planned), TIBIA-HARVEST (Active), DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS (the redo of the one true loss).")
inl note_of_tibia_memory_reasons_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-MEMORY-REASONS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 08:36 tibia QUALITY PASS. No code, so no typefeatureset plan applies. DEFINITION OF DONE: each of the 5 memory files (tibia-dogfood-project, tibia-other-session, tibia-log-stamps, no-hand-crafted-lua, wow-after-tibia) has a **Why:** line quoting the user's own words with the backlog note that holds them (item id plus note number), or an explicit 'reason not recoverable: searched X, Y' line; MEMORY.md's line for each drops 'PARTIAL'. NEXT STEP: for each memory, git grep the pre-wipe agile.spi for its rule's key phrase (e.g. 'hand-crafted Lua', 'log stamp') limited to user-quote notes ('user 10-'), read the 3 nearest notes, and copy the quote. CPU: none. 90m.")
inl note_of_tibia_memory_reasons_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-MEMORY-REASONS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T20:25Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 16:3x tibia, DONE against n1's DoD. no-hand-crafted-lua: Why quotes the user 10-24 ('stop adding hand-written Lua', TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION n35), already present. wow-after-tibia: Why quotes the user 10-25 (WOW-LUA-SANDBOX n1), already present. tibia-log-stamps: an evidence rule, not a user rule; its Why lists the measuring notes (TIBIA-PARTY n44..n99, TIBIA-FIDELITY n25..n76). tibia-dogfood-project: Why now quotes TIBIA's title (user 2026-10-12), n3 (10-17 pause for tooling), n7 (10-18 'not paused ... good enough to start Dota 2', paraphrase) and TIBIA-HARVEST n1 (11-01 00:2x, verbatim); 10-12 founding words marked not recoverable (TIBIA's first note is 10-15); its stale 'don't start without go' became harvest-proceeds and arena-waits. tibia-other-session: founding quote marked not recoverable (searched TIBIA notes and citations), pointing to the 10-30 06:5x lift quote. profile-wipe memory gained a dated line: none of the five is PARTIAL now.")
inl note_of_tibia_harvest_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-HARVEST", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:40Z", "main", "main 00:4x: raised to P0 (user 11-01 00:2x: 'unpark tibia enough so we extract the most value we can there before pivoting to things like dota2 or wow addons (make sure the wipe didn't messed up our plans here)'). Checked: the plan survived the wipe. WOW-LUA-SANDBOX is next (memory wow-after-tibia quotes the user's 10-25 words), the Dota items are parked at P4, and DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS is cross-linked as prior art. tibia owns the harvest; its round-2 groom reply names the slices.")
inl note_of_tibia_harvest_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-HARVEST", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:02Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 02:1x tibia: (1) LESSONS AND TEMPLATES written onto WOW-LUA-SANDBOX n7 (the two-module split with its host-name gate, event hooks, the per-entry Lua build gate with a 5.1 load check, 5.1 limits, the headless fakes pattern, trace stamps analysed with kit verbs, and the day-one guards). DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS needs no copy: it is paused as superseded by the WoW pivot (main, 10-31 17:02) and its restart path is its own notes. (2) LIB CANDIDATES among tibia/src (none has a lib counterpart by name): rng.spi (61 lines: counter-based keyed RNG, mix32 lowbias plus roll_key {seed, tick, entity, purpose}; agrees across C, Rust and WGSL); quantity.spi (53: phantom-typed amounts with unit tags); views.spi (112: lane views); search.spi (173: genome search with clamp_between and the nudge_key fixture); network.spi (271: fixed-point MLP, one = 256); geometry.spi (304: grid positions, directions, Chebyshev distance); wire.spi (104: XTEA key and cipher, a crypto.spi neighbour). Game-specific, stays in tibia: blessings, catalog, coins, combat, cooldowns, creatures, damage, experience, hunt, hunt_sim, hunting_pack, pack_sim, policy, readiness, skills, terrain, vitality, vocations; standards.spi is kit-standards config. NEXT: file one lib-landing item per candidate, starting with rng (smallest, already cross-backend proven), and check each against lib's style contract (granular, test blocks per backend) before drafting.")
inl note_of_tibia_harvest_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-HARVEST", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:04Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 08:04 tibia LANE SNAPSHOT (pre-merge groom, before main's external-conversation items arrive; match each new item against this list and merge, don't duplicate). TIBIA-OWN, in WSJF order: (1) TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE 90: respell proven on node build 3; done once the user commits the 4 unstaged files (character.spi, world_executor.spi, suite.spi, build.ps1) and CI goes green. (2) TIBIA-HARVEST 50 (this item, P0): lessons and templates into WOW-LUA-SANDBOX are done; next, the lib candidates (rng -> LIB-KEYED-RNG, then quantity, views, search, network, geometry, wire). (3) PS1-TO-SPIRAL, tibia share (3 ports: build.ps1, predict.ps1, live/run-policy-scenario.ps1): build.ps1 slices 1-3 green in apps/suite; the rest waits on KIT-RUN-SPI (kit). (4) DATA-ROWS-TYPED part 3: HELD for kit's 'kit table to-spi' and .spi readers (kit accepted, ships after TRAIN7); then one conversion pass plus the BENCHMARK citation rewrite. (5) TIBIA-RUN-METRICS 66: 2 of 3 metrics live; hunts/s needs the sweep's hunt-count row plus a node run. (6) LIB-XCOMPAT-TIBIA-DEFS 70: Rust identity probe (n9); dep LIB-XCOMPAT-TIBIA-TESTS. (7) LIB-KEYED-RNG (P2, 120m), (8) TIBIA-MEMORY-REASONS (P2, 90m). PAUSED (11, each with a Reason and Resume condition from the 10-31 night groom): the game and arena line (TIBIA, BASELINES, PARTY, ARENA-RELEASE, GPU-RENTAL, STORAGE-BOUNDARY, 2048-ANCHOR) and the headless-client chain (HEADLESS-CLIENT, GAME-HANDSHAKE, TCP-BYTES, BIGNUM-MODEXP). All resume only if tibia is un-pivoted; the pivot target is WOW-LUA-SANDBOX, then DOTA. SHARED, where tibia only contributes (owner): POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS (x drives the census port now), BACKEND-BALANCE (x), GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT (compiler), GRANULAR-HEAL (epic), CELLS-EVERYWHERE and LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION (main), ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME (resilience), POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE (compiler/main; tibia's proof part done). Tibia's share is DONE on CELL-BUDGET-NAMING, DATA-DIR-HOME and BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS. CLOSED this groom: TIBIA-WIPE-DAMAGE, and TIBIA-CI-OFFSCREEN-LINUX earlier tonight. Cross-lane waits: KIT-RUN-SPI and kit table to-spi (kit); the user's commit (CI). ALSO TIBIA-TOUCHED, untagged: POLYGLOT-GRANULAR-HEAL (main's): tibia's 2 heals (math.spi:561 exit site, chat_contract.spi:63-89 generate_cid) sit unstaged in polyglot since 10-30, waiting on main's re-score and the user's commit; WOW-LUA-SANDBOX (Planned 0, the pivot target; harvest lessons in its n7).")
inl note_of_tibia_harvest_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-HARVEST", 4u32, "2026-11-01T12:35Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 08:35 tibia QUALITY PASS. DEFINITION OF DONE, verifiable: each of the 7 lib candidates ends in exactly one of LANDED (a lib module with backend-pinned test blocks, gated by lib_program_gate.ps1 on the node with a -Negative mutant, and tibia importing it with build.ps1 green on the node) or REJECTED (a note here with the reason, e.g. too game-specific). Plus: WOW-LUA-SANDBOX holds the lessons and templates (done, n7). The item reaches 100 when the 7 verdicts are written. TYPEFEATURESET SHAPE PER CANDIDATE, decided before code: rng -> LIB-KEYED-RNG (nominal seed/tick/entity, a phantom-indexed purpose key, and a purposed prototype; plan on that item n1). quantity -> phantom-UNIT quantities 'quantity u' (u = gold | hp | ms) with add only within one unit and explicit conversions, replacing tibia's bare i32 money and health. search -> HKT-style generic climb: evolve over any genome type g with a prototype 'genome_space g' (mutate, clamp), instead of the 3-field genome record plus a separate vector copy (search.spi carries the climb code twice today). geometry -> nominal position and a direction union, no (dx, dy) int pairs. wire -> a GADT codec 'field t = | U8 : field u8 | U16 : field u16 | Str : field string | Seq : field a * field b -> field (a * b)' so encode and decode are derived from one description (Canary protocol parsing). views and network -> decide from the importer graph; the default is REJECTED as game-specific unless WOW or DOTA needs them. Model modules: lib sm' prototypes, json_schema decode-to-union, tibia search.spi's nominal vector. NEXT STEP: LIB-KEYED-RNG (next step on that item), then quantity: list its 6 importers (git grep 'quantity::' src apps) and draft lib/spiral/quantity.spi with the unit phantom and 3 tests. CORRECTION (read src/quantity.spi:5-16): quantity ALREADY has phantom units, ':! amount unit = i32' with unit tags hitpoints, milliseconds, tiles and gold_coins, and plus/value_of generic only within one unit. So its harvest is a MOVE, not a retype, and quantity.spi is itself the phantom-unit model the other candidates (DATA-ROWS-TYPED's ms/hp, TIBIA-RUN-METRICS' tenths) should reuse instead of new ':! ms = i32' wrappers. Importer count verified: 6. DECIDED (importer graph, 08:38): views -> HARVEST with a retype. It holds generic fixed-size containers (band a = 6-tuple, duo a = pair; importers hunting_pack and pack_sim), but duo_at/duo_with index by i32 with every i <> 0 meaning the second element, a weak index. Lib form: an index union per container (duo_index = | First | Second; band_index = | B0 | ... | B5), so out-of-range indexes cannot be written; a size-indexed vector GADT is the stretch option. network -> REJECTED for lib: a hard-coded policy-net shape (one = 256, inputs 8, hidden 6, outputs 3; importers competitor, hunting_pack, pack_sim) is game-specific. It moves to WOW/DOTA only if they reuse the policy net. 7 verdicts: rng, quantity, views, search, geometry, wire harvest; network rejected.")
inl note_of_tibia_harvest_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-HARVEST", 5u32, "2026-11-01T14:37Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 10:37 tibia SOTA TYPEFEATURESET TARGET for tibia's business logic (combat, scenarios, arena, GPU model): what carries into WoW and how it looks at its peak. MEASURED TODAY (git grep over src + apps): 0 prototypes anywhere; 1 typestate phantom (vitality::vitality vitality::alive); 1 GADT plus existential (catalog: kind k indexed by troll_kind | goblin_kind | cyclops_kind, entry = exists k. spawn k); quantity's phantom units exist but combat and hunt_sim keep times as bare i32 (knight_state.next_step_at, foe_state.strikes_at); sentinel pairs in competitor.spi (sighting = { found : bool; index : i32 }, threat likewise); -1 sentinels in 6 arena files (random 6, combat_text 4, competitor 2, samples, steps, ground_trace); stringly scenario orders (order = { name : string; count }); gpu_sim declares 0 types and duplicates hunt_sim's step logic in WGSL form. PEAK SHAPE: (1) PROTOTYPES as the spine: 'combatant c' (attack_of, defense_of, health_of, step) instanced by the knight and each creature kind, so combat math is written once over any c; 'lanes f' (map, fold, index) as the HKT-style container prototype with a CPU array instance and a WGSL storage-buffer instance, so ONE step definition compiles to C/Rust and to WGSL and gpu_sim stops being a second copy. (2) GADTS: catalog's kind k extends to 'ability k' (a cyclops throw exists only at kind cyclops_kind) and arena_host's host_event/host_command become a typestate 'host s' (s = idle | world_up | running | crashed) whose transitions are total functions, so illegal sequences (StartRun before WorldReady) don't type-check. (3) EXISTENTIALS: mixed foe packs as exists k. foe k (the entry pattern), scenario mixes as exists k. order k with order k = { kind : kind k; count }, which removes order.name strings. (4) PHANTOM UNITS AND TYPESTATE everywhere: every time field becomes 'amount milliseconds', every health field 'amount hitpoints'; cooldowns become 'timer ready | timer cooling' like vitality alive | dead. (5) NO SENTINELS: sighting and threat become option foe_index; the arena -1s become option or a named union. (6) REFLECTION via a 'describe' prototype: a scenario's typed record renders its BENCHMARK.md spec text, so registration text cannot drift from the code. CARRIES INTO WOW: the combatant prototype (WoW combat-log modelling), quantity units, typestate vitality and timers, the host-typestate pattern (addon state machines), keyed RNG and search (policy tuning). STAYS: the Canary protocol, the map tool, the arena harness. MEGA SAMPLES: not the bar. They use no prototypes, so they show GADT and existential syntax but not the prototype spine; the bar is the shape above. DEFINITION OF DONE for 'tibia at SOTA' (census by git grep over src + apps): at least 1 prototype per domain (combat, scenario, arena, gpu); 0 '-1' sentinels and 0 found/index pairs; 0 bare-i32 time or health fields in records; gpu_sim and hunt_sim share one step through 'lanes'; build.ps1 green on the node. Because tibia is pivoting, it is applied ONLY to code that moves to lib or WoW (harvest order on n4), never as a tibia-only rewrite. NEXT STEP: the 'combatant' prototype in lib with combat.spi's swing math as its first instance, at LIB-KEYED-RNG's landing.")
inl note_of_tibia_harvest_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-HARVEST", 6u32, "2026-11-01T18:18Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 14:2x tibia, JUNK CENSUS for main's narrowed drafts rule (git ls-files outside deps; the kit drafts store has no tibia folder; untracked tree clean). Every file decided: (1) LAND TYPED, then archive: results/*.tsv (61) plus results/*.txt (4: path-check-pc1/pc2 cited by docs/BENCHMARK.md, arena-spot-scan-r12 written by apps/map/run.ps1, arena-region-r45 cited only by 2 backlog notes) and analysis/*.ps1 (4: pc3-analysis, pc4-analysis, pr12-secondary, pz-in-traces, all cited by BENCHMARK.md; they compute the numbers MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV's DoD must reproduce). One execution item: MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV (scope extended there); DATA-ROWS-TYPED part 3 points at it. The Pages-readable form (main's incoming HTML item) renders from the typed rows. (2) BEYOND GLUE, ported under PS1-TO-SPIRAL's tibia share: scripts/build.ps1 11.8K (suite.spi slices 1-5 moving its logic; slice 5, the budget report decoder, is in test now), live/run-policy-scenario.ps1 16.5K, apps/map/run.ps1 7.1K, live/run-arena.ps1 6.6K, scripts/score.ps1 5.9K, live/setup.ps1 5.7K, live/block.ps1 3.1K. (3) KEEP AS GLUE (under 2.5K, call one tool each): apps/arena|canary|offscreen/build.ps1, live/arena-check|arena-summary|paths|run-hunt|run-offscreen|run-probe|start-sync-check|world.ps1, scripts/init|predict|publish|workflow|tibia-mariadb-dump.ps1. (4) KEEP: README.md, docs/BENCHMARK.md (its .tsv citations get rewritten in MIGRATE-TIBIA-TSV), .devcontainer/devcontainer.json, live/vbot-arena.json (config).")
inl note_of_tibia_harvest_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-HARVEST", 7u32, "2026-11-01T20:03Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 16:1x tibia, VERDICTS for the 7 lib candidates (DoD n4: each LANDED or REJECTED). (1) rng: LANDED as lib keyed_rng.spi 15:12 (nominal seed/tick/entity, prototype purpose_code, sub_key; c/rust/lua node gate + wgsl box green); tibia's 6 importers migrated, consumer build on the node running (LIB-KEYED-RNG). (2) quantity: lib half proven as units.spi (phantom 'amount unit'), land queued; importers next (LIB-UNITS-AMOUNT). (3) views: LAND, as LIB-FIXED-VIEWS: fixed-arity containers (one/duo/trio/lanes/band) with HKT prototypes 'mapped f a' and 'counted f a'; lib has no functor-style prototype (grep ':? (mapped|fmap|map)' over lib: none), and this is the typefeatureset showcase the WoW port wants. (4) wire: LAND, as LIB-WIRE-CHECKSUMS: XTEA encrypt/decrypt plus Adler-32 are protocol-generic; lib crypto.spi has only sha256 and hash helpers; tibia has 0 importers left (src/wire.spi is reference code), so the move costs no migration. (5) geometry: REJECTED: position carries Tibia's z floor, and step/straight_step encode Tibia's 8-direction stepping and diagonal rules; WoW uses continuous coordinates, so the generic part is only chebyshev_distance (3 lines, not worth a module). (6) search: REJECTED: a hill-climb hard-wired to a 3-gene genome record; a generic version would take the genome as a type parameter with a mutation prototype, which is a new design, not a move (re-file if WoW tuning needs it). (7) network: REJECTED: the fixed-point MLP's input features and output actions are tibia unions (feature, output) and the sizes are constants (8/6/3); generic only after a typed-size rewrite. Progress: 2 landed or landing, 2 to land, 3 rejected.")
inl note_of_tibia_harvest_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-HARVEST", 8u32, "2026-11-01T23:23Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 19:2x tibia: 7 of 7 verdicts written. LANDED in lib today: keyed_rng (Done), units (Done), wire (Done), fixed_views (90%, waits on the next green push). REJECTED with reasons (n7): geometry, search, network. Also landed as harvest by-products: lib case_labels (derived case<->label names, interim for SPIRAL-NAMEOF), and tooling fixes in spiral apps/cells/spi (lib_land_window lands new files; type_probe skips fails-tests). DONE when LIB-FIXED-VIEWS closes.")
inl note_of_tibia_harvest_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-HARVEST", 9u32, "2026-11-02T01:19Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 21:2x tibia, DONE: 7 of 7 verdicts final. LANDED in lib with tibia importing them green in CI: keyed_rng (LIB-KEYED-RNG), units (LIB-UNITS-AMOUNT), wire (LIB-WIRE-CHECKSUMS), fixed_views (LIB-FIXED-VIEWS, CI run 41366570407). REJECTED with reasons (n7): geometry, search, network. By-products: lib case_labels (derived case<->label names, the interim for SPIRAL-NAMEOF), and spiral apps/cells/spi fixes (lib_land_window lands new files; type_probe skips fails-tests). WOW-LUA-SANDBOX holds the lessons and templates (n7 there).")
inl note_of_tibia_run_metrics_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-RUN-METRICS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T06:42Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 02:42 tibia: 2 of 3 metrics LIVE in scripts/build.ps1, written as Prometheus text to target/ci-metrics/tibia[-label].prom on every run: tibia_cell_budget_findings{kind} (parsed from the cell-budget report line; tonight autolink 9, mention 17, over_budget 0), tibia_file_seconds{file} + tibia_file_exit_code{file}, and the per-backend split as tibia_test_ms{file,test,target,status,memo} (memo label added because memo hits replay the cached run's ms; offscreen rerun shows memo=hit on all 7). Verified twice locally: offscreen 129.9 s, then offscreen + suite. LEFT: tibia_sim_hunts_per_second{backend}, which needs hunting_pack to print hunts and elapsed per backend (no throughput line exists today: git grep finds none); next is a typed throughput row in the hunting_pack sweep test, read by the same writer. The writer moves into apps/suite/suite.spi with the build.ps1 port (PS1-TO-SPIRAL n28).")
inl note_of_tibia_run_metrics_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-RUN-METRICS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T07:57Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 03:57 tibia, design for the THIRD metric tibia_sim_hunts_per_second{backend}: the sweep's hunt count is known only inside the program (training evaluations over 6 generations x 6 offspring x training_block, plus 2 held-out blocks), and scripts/predict.ps1 is C-only (dotnet compile, gcc -O2, run). Shape: apps/predict/sweep.spi prints a final typed row 'hunts<TAB>N', counting each ledger_on block it evaluates, so the program owns its count. The runner times only the exe (excluding compile) and emits prom_line 'tibia_sim_hunts_per_second' with backend=c, using suite.spi's prom writers. Rust and wgsl follow when the sweep runs on them through the same Spiral module (the 10-27 5.8x C-over-Rust figure becomes a ratio of two series). It is NOT CPU-light: one sweep per backend is minutes of a full core, so it runs on the node, not the box. Next step: the hunts row in sweep.spi with a test that the count equals 6*6*training + 2*held_out.")
inl note_of_tibia_run_metrics_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-RUN-METRICS", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 08:34 tibia QUALITY PASS. SELF-CRITIQUE: tonight's writers in apps/suite/suite.spi are STRINGLY. prom_line (name : string) (labels : string) (value : string) accepts any name with any label set, so a typo'd metric or a missing label compiles. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN (it replaces them before the third metric lands): (1) a GADT of series indexed by its LABEL RECORD: ':| series l = | CellBudgetFindings : series budget_label | FileSeconds : series file_label | FileExitCode : series file_label | TestMs : series test_label | SimHuntsPerSecond : series backend_label', so writing a sample needs exactly that series' label record; (2) closed unions for the label values: budget_kind = | OverBudget | Autolink | Mention; backend = | C | Rust | Lua | Gleam | Wgsl | Zig; test_status = | Passed | Failed | Unsupported | Skipped | NotRun; memo = | Hit | Miss. Strings remain only for file and test NAMES, which are open sets; (3) an existential 'sample = exists l. series l * l * metric_value' for the heterogeneous .prom body; (4) metric_value = | Tenths of i32 | Count of i32 (no f64, for byte-identical lua/rust output, proven tonight); (5) one prototype 'series_name' plus 'label_pairs' per label record (the reflection-like part), and the renderer is total. Model: lib json_schema's typed writer; reflection via prototypes as in sm'. DEFINITION OF DONE: build.ps1 -Files suite is green with tests that render one sample of EACH series from typed values (5 tests, lua + rust); a node run of the predict sweep adds tibia_sim_hunts_per_second{backend=c} and {backend=rust} to target/ci-metrics/tibia.prom; the ratio of the two is recorded here next to the 10-27 figure of 5.8x. NEXT STEP: in apps/suite/suite.spi, replace prom_label/prom_line with the series GADT plus budget_kind/backend/test_status/memo, and port the 4 writer tests to it (CPU: one 3-minute local Cells run). ESTIMATE: 120m stays (the GADT is 60m, the hunts row plus a node run 60m).")
inl note_of_tibia_run_metrics_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-RUN-METRICS", 4u32, "2026-11-01T23:44Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 19:4x tibia, THIRD METRIC step 1 of 2 (unstaged): apps/predict/sweep.spi now prints a final typed row 'hunts<TAB>N'. N is derived from the same values the sweep runs on, not hard-coded: sweep_hunts plan = training_count * (1 + trials) + 2 * held_out_count, where trials = 1 + generations * offspring. That's search::evolve's real evaluation count: one fitness call for the start genome plus one per child per generation, with no parent re-evaluation (src/search.spi:57-69), plus kills_floor's training block and the two held-out ledgers = 4,000 hunts for the 6x6 plan. The plan moved into sweep_plan () so the count and the run share one record. Test a_sweep_counts_every_hunt_it_runs (c) pins 4000; sweep.spi gained a test header; format clean; budget 0. Step 2: the runner times only the compiled exe and writes tibia_sim_hunts_per_second{backend} through suite.spi's sample union (a new SimHuntsPerSecond case), c first, rust and wgsl as the sweep runs there; one node run per backend (minutes of a core each).")
inl note_of_tibia_run_metrics_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-RUN-METRICS", 5u32, "2026-11-02T15:03Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia groom: not touched in more than 12 h, so Planned (WIP cap). Step 2 (runner glue plus one node run per backend) is unchanged. Node jobs go through remote.ps1 laptop job start until kit TRAIN11 reaches the node.")
inl note_of_tibia_run_metrics_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-RUN-METRICS", 6u32, "2026-11-03T00:53Z", "tibia", "11-02 20:4x tibia, step 2 MEASURED for backend=c (node job sweep-rate-1, tibia synced to the tibia-gates3 mirror, scripts/predict.ps1 -Module sweep, gcc -O2 as predict.ps1 builds it, node at 100% load): the sweep prints its own `hunts 4000` row (sweep_hunts plan, the 6x6 plan), and timing the compiled exe alone (compile excluded) gives 226,913 / 205,559 / 196,607 ms = 17.6 / 19.5 / 20.3 hunts/s. suite.spi already carries the typed path (sim_run { target; hunts; ms }, hunts_per_second, sim_rate_line, the SimHuntsPerSecond sample case). Left: the runner glue that turns one timed run into a SimHuntsPerSecond sample in the .prom (today the timing lives in a node scratch script, not in the repo), then rust and wgsl series. Numbers on a loaded laptop node are indicative only; the published value needs a quiet box.")
inl note_of_tibia_run_metrics_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-RUN-METRICS", 7u32, "2026-11-03T05:09Z", "tibia", "11-03 01:0x tibia, step 2 RUNNER GLUE IN THE REPO (unstaged): scripts/predict.ps1 times only the compiled exe (a Stopwatch around the run, compile and gcc excluded) and, when the program prints its own 'hunts<TAB>N' row, writes target/ci-metrics/sim-<Label>.prom with '# TYPE tibia_sim_hunts_per_second gauge' and tibia_sim_hunts_per_second{backend=c} floor(N*1000/ms). That is the same integer formula as apps/suite/suite.spi's hunts_per_second, whose fixture (4000 hunts in 2000 ms = 2000) the glue reproduces. It follows build.ps1's existing inline .prom pattern. END-TO-END on the node (job chain2-1, tibia-gates3 mirror, -Module sweep): 'predict / sweep-rate / 4000 hunts in 68125 ms = 58 hunts/s', and the .prom holds the c series = 58. That is 3x the 11-02 figure (17.6-20.3 hunts/s at 100% node load), which confirms that a loaded node's numbers are indicative only. Left: rust and wgsl series (one node run per backend), and a quiet-box published value.")
inl note_of_tibia_run_metrics_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-RUN-METRICS", 8u32, "2026-11-03T08:00Z", "tibia", "11-03 03:5x tibia, STATE AT PAUSE (main's correction: the 21:00 run has not started). scripts/predict.ps1 (unstaged) gained -Backend c|rust (Rust: --backend Rust plus rustc --edition 2024 -O, the offscreen build.ps1 way); the .prom backend label follows it. RUNNING, left to finish (not killed): node job job-1793691703545-tibia-sweep-pair-1 runs the sweep as C, Rust, C (the second C is the A/A control); first C row: 4000 hunts in 65,758 ms = 60 hunts/s. NEXT STEP when the run resumes: read that job's stdout (job wait job-1793691703545-tibia-sweep-pair-1), record the Rust rate and the C/Rust ratio against the A/A spread (10-27 figure: C 5.8x Rust). Then the wgsl series, which needs a GPU entry for the sweep. Waits on: nothing but the job.")
inl note_of_tibia_run_metrics_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-RUN-METRICS", 9u32, "2026-11-03T08:02Z", "tibia", "11-03 04:1x tibia, SWEEP PAIR RESULT (node job job-1793691703545-tibia-sweep-pair-1, finished exit 0, tibia digest 01ED058AC420E72D, 4000 hunts each): C 65,758 ms = 60 hunts/s; Rust 426,495 ms = 9 hunts/s; C again (A/A control) 195,938 ms = 20 hunts/s. The Rust series works end to end: predict.ps1 -Backend rust builds and runs the sweep, and the .prom carries tibia_sim_hunts_per_second{backend=rust}. But the A/A control FAILED: the same C program took 3.0x longer in the second run, so node load changed during the job and the C/Rust ratio is only bounded, 2.2x-6.5x (it brackets the 10-27 figure of 5.8x and confirms nothing). Rule applied: no ratio is claimed from a job whose A/A spread exceeds the effect. NEXT STEP when the run resumes: repeat C,Rust,C on a quiet machine (europa when its load is under 1, or the box under a bench window), and accept only if the two C runs agree within 5%.")
inl note_of_tibia_run_metrics_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-RUN-METRICS", 10u32, "2026-11-03T09:05Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:0x tibia, QUIET C/RUST RATIO MEASURED, A/A PASSED. The node-generated sweep sources (sweep-c predict.c and sweep-rust predict.rs from tibia digest 01ED058AC420E72D, the 6x6 plan, 4000 hunts) were built on europa (gcc -O2 -lm; rustc 1.101.0-nightly --edition 2024 -O) and run C, Rust, C with the load average logged per row: C 48,506 ms (load 1.01), Rust 126,358 ms (1.21), C 46,913 ms (1.25). The A/A spread is 3.3%, inside the 5% rule (the node pair's 3.0x spread failed it). RESULT: C 83.8 hunts/s, Rust 31.7 hunts/s, C/Rust = 2.65x (126,358 / 47,710). The 10-27 figure was 5.8x, so the Rust gap more than halved since then, which matches the compiler lane's Rust hot-loop work (COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP) and tonight's allocation cut in network.spi. Series values for the .prom: backend c 83, rust 31 (europa, quiet). Left: the wgsl series (the sweep needs a GPU entry) and a CI run of predict.ps1 -Backend rust.")
inl note_of_tibia_run_metrics_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-RUN-METRICS", 11u32, "2026-11-03T14:16Z", "tibia", "11-03 10:2x tibia, CI RUN OF THE SIM SERIES WIRED (unstaged): tibia CI never ran predict.ps1, and predict.ps1 could not run on Linux (dotnet and compiler paths were Join-Path LOCALAPPDATA). (1) scripts/predict.ps1 resolves the spiral-bin cache like spiral apps/zed/test.ps1: SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR, else LOCALAPPDATA/spiral-bin, else HOME/.cache/spiral-bin (the Ubuntu CI log shows the bundled dotnet and SpiralCompiler.dll exactly there). (2) .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml gains step sim-rate after long-gpu, ubuntu only, if init succeeded: predict.ps1 -Label ci-c -Backend c and -Label ci-rust -Backend rust with -Out to runner.temp, so target/ci-metrics/sim-ci-c.prom and sim-ci-rust.prom ride the existing ci-metrics artifact. No new actions (actions.lock untouched). Checks: both files parse, the cache fallback picks HOME/.cache/spiral-bin with LOCALAPPDATA unset. NOT yet run end to end on Linux: europa is busy with the timed WGSL 10-lane run; do it when europa is idle, then the first CI run after the user push is the proof. CI rates are noisy-runner numbers (a trend, not a benchmark; the quiet europa pair stays the reference: C 83.8, Rust 31.7).")
inl note_of_tibia_run_metrics_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-RUN-METRICS", 12u32, "2026-11-03T15:05Z", "tibia", "11-03 11:2x tibia, CI SIM STEP PROVEN ON LINUX (europa, pwsh 7.6.9, LOCALAPPDATA unset, tibia-shaped tree with deps/spiral): first run showed the default predict module prints no hunts line, so no .prom; the step now passes -Module sweep (apps/predict/sweep.spi prints hunts). Exact CI commands: predict.ps1 -Module sweep -Label ci-c -Backend c: 4000 hunts in 49,873 ms = 80 hunts/s, exit 0; -Label ci-rust -Backend rust: 128,226 ms = 31 hunts/s, exit 0; target/ci-metrics/sim-ci-c.prom and sim-ci-rust.prom written (tibia_sim_hunts_per_second backend c 80, rust 31), and the C and Rust sweep rows are byte-identical. Matches the quiet pair (83.8 / 31.7). CI cost about 3 min of runs plus two compiles. Proof left: the first CI run after the user push.")
inl note_of_tibia_run_metrics_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-RUN-METRICS", 13u32, "2026-11-04T04:16Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:16 tibia GROOM: DONE. The three metrics are exported and the CI sim step is proven on Linux (europa, 11-03 11:2x: c 80 and rust 31 hunts/s, .prom written). Linux CI proof joins the post-commit CI heal (user 11-04: no commit asks; when the user commits, each lane checks and heals CI).")
inl note_of_tibia_ci_offscreen_linux_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-CI-OFFSCREEN-LINUX", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:31Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 tibia: CI review of the last 5 runs (gh run list -R i574n/tibia). 3 pages deploys succeeded. 2 'tests' runs FAILED: (a) 41240095363 (10-31 20:41), both OSes about 1 min into build.ps1: the postable step had no profile because tibia/postability was untracked (tibia''s 10-30 mistake, TIBIA n50). FIXED tonight, pending commit: the step is now Cells'' generic check with no x (POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE n12). (b) 41160539750 (10-31 02:33), ubuntu only, 21 min in: this item (offscreen links -luser32). SLOW STEPS: init.ps1 12-13 min on windows-2025 (6 on ubuntu); the short build 21-27 min. Both are candidates for the TIBIA-RUN-METRICS series.")
inl note_of_tibia_ci_offscreen_linux_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-CI-OFFSCREEN-LINUX", 2u32, "2026-11-01T05:16Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 02:xx tibia: FIX in tibia (unstaged): apps/offscreen/offscreen.spi's CloseDesktop extern block now has #[cfg_attr(windows, link(name = \"user32\"))] instead of an unconditional #[link(name = \"user32\")], so non-Windows builds no longer ask the linker for user32 (nothing calls the Win32 externs on Linux). Windows proof on the box, through build.ps1 -Files offscreen on the respelled tree: discovered 7, passed 7, failed 0. The Linux proof is the next tibia CI run, after the user commits; a hand Linux run is not allowed (memory: no WSL until the workflow passes).")
inl note_of_tibia_ci_offscreen_linux_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-CI-OFFSCREEN-LINUX", 3u32, "2026-11-01T07:57Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 03:57 tibia: LINUX PROOF MET. gh-pages run 41281548884 on the user's commit b1a02bcb: 'build.ps1 / offscreen / discovered 7 executed 7 passed 7 failed 0' on BOTH build (ubuntu-24.04) and build (windows-2025). The #[cfg_attr(windows, link(name = \"user32\"))] gating builds and runs on Linux. The run itself is red for other reasons (TIBIA-LIB-CONSUMER-REGATE n-latest); offscreen is not among them. Done.")
inl note_of_x_wipe_damage_1 () = Note ("X-WIPE-DAMAGE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:30Z", "x", "2026-11-01 00:30 x: what the 10-28 wipe cost x, from evidence (memory profile-wipe-2026-10-28, wipe-redo tags, x memories).\nLOST:\n(1) ~/.secrets/x-api.env, so X-CLIENT is Blocked at 98% on the user re-issuing credentials. The live-API path has been unproven since.\n(2) Every x memory came back partial. x-api-credentials and x-compat-naming were rebuilt 10-30 from backlog citations, original wording lost.\n(3) TEMP and C:\\home\\data drafts: X-HAND-UNITS's g4/jsondyn/gleam-units drafts survived only because they were in C:\\home\\data, later archived (20261031-093702). Anything only in TEMP (launchers, probe outputs) is gone.\n(4) Pre-wipe transcripts, so the 'why' behind several x decisions lives only in notes.\nREWORK: not measured, so I won't invent hours. The x items tagged wipe-redo carry 3,660 min of estimates: X-HAND-UNITS 3000, X-BACKEND-SWITCHES 240, POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS 240, LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER 120, X-WIP-VERIFY 60. Done since: X-WIP-VERIFY, LIB-PROCESS-COUNTER.\nPRACTICES THAT FEEL WEAKER (tonight's own slips, 10-31):\n(a) A proof that skipped a gate. x-parity-typed2 never ran x-compat, so 2 overweight definitions reached the user's commit e81ac5cf and turned CI red (run 41267182382).\n(b) A probe that measured nothing: [IO.File] with a relative path (memory ps-dotnet-relative-paths).\n(c) Peer-flagged drift: two new .ps1 files and two drafts, folded into notes and archived.\nHEAL and MAKE-UP tasks:\n- H1, filed as part of this item and DONE: node-run.ps1 sim and parity modes build through apps/*/build.ps1, which now run the same Cells check as verify (Assert-CellsXCompat), so a parity proof can't skip it again. x-parity-typed3 is its first proof (PASS, 00:3x).\n- H2: x-api.env re-issue stays the user's step (X-CLIENT). Nothing for x.\n- M1: X-BACKEND-SWITCHES (wipe-redo, 0%) gets its pre-registered measurements back on the node queue after verify44.\n- M2: POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS (45%) resumes as the 'xc postable census' port (PS1-TO-SPIRAL port 1).")
inl note_of_x_wipe_damage_2 () = Note ("X-WIPE-DAMAGE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:17Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:17 x: CLOSED. The item's deliverable, the damage record (n1) and heal H1, is done. H1 (parity proofs run the Cells budget check) was proven by x-parity-typed3 (00:3x) and exercised again by x-parity-metrics1. The rest lives on its own items, so this record doesn't duplicate them: H2 = X-CLIENT (user step), M1 = X-BACKEND-SWITCHES, M2 = POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS through PS1-TO-SPIRAL n20 port 1. The practice slips named in n1 are addressed by memories ps-dotnet-relative-paths and spiral-one-export-per-path (nested nullary pattern) and by the Cells budget gate in every proof mode.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_1 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:30Z", "x", "2026-11-01 00:30 x: three numbers x exports per run, typed rows (a Spiral record per sample, printed as Prometheus text exposition next to the verify log):\n(1) x_sim_frames_per_second{backend=\"gleam\",ladder=\"1:1:1\"}: frames replayed per second by xsim_frames:cli_run on the sim build's ladder. It is the sim's throughput and the BEAM-side cost x pays.\n(2) x_e2e_wall_seconds{server=\"sim\"|\"stub\"} plus x_e2e_parity_differing_rows (must be 0) for the same 153-request tutorial. Sim against stub wall time is x's BEAM-versus-pwsh overhead, measured on identical traffic.\n(3) x_gleam_part_lines_max{package=\"xsim_frames\"|\"xsim_*\"}: the largest generated Gleam part against gleam-modules' 1,000-line gate. It is the headroom that decides when X-HAND-UNITS resumes (its n33 rule: within 100 lines).\nRule before building it: the emitter is Spiral (sim_report and the frames CLI already print stats), not a new .ps1. node-run.ps1 only collects the lines. Baseline = first 3 runs on pack 11; a regression is a move beyond 10% of the baseline's median.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_2 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T04:31Z", "x", "2026-11-01 00:31 x: dir-tree-html (i574n.github.io/x, via spiral/scripts/publish-tree.ps1) shows the file tree and the README only. It could also show run state beside the files: the latest run's three metric rows, the e2e parity verdict (153/153, 0 differing), and the per-notebook test verdicts that Cells already writes as .ipynb metadata. Shape: publish.ps1 copies verify's typed metric rows and the notebook verdict summary into dist, and publish-tree renders a status line per directory. The renderer is spiral's (main), so the request goes to main; x supplies the rows.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_3 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:05Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:05 x: 2 of 3 metrics EMIT (unstaged, parse-checked, first node run at the next parity/verify).\n(3) scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 prints 'x_gleam_part_lines_max{package=\"<module>\",limit=\"1000\"} N' beside its 'largest N lines' line, for xsim_split and xsim_frames in every sim build. node-run.ps1 sim and parity modes print the build log's x_ rows.\n(2) scripts/e2e-parity.ps1 times each server's e2e and prints 'x_e2e_wall_seconds{server=\"stub\"|\"sim\"} N' and 'x_e2e_parity_differing_rows N'. node-run.ps1's parity filter passes the x_ rows.\nHonest deviation from n1's rule ('the emitter is Spiral'): both live in EXISTING glue that already holds the numbers (gleam-modules measures the parts, e2e-parity runs both servers), so no new script and no new Spiral surface for numbers PowerShell computes. They move with their hosts: gleam-modules to the kit verb, e2e-parity retires with the stub (PS1-TO-SPIRAL n20).\n(1) BLOCKED by a FINDING: the Spiral sim never measures time. sim_run.spi:39's shape_for hardcodes ms = 0i32, so every XSIM line prints ms=0. The Elixir engine measured wall ms; the port dropped it. Fix design (Spiral, typed): executor_pair gains clock_ms : () -> i32. cells_executor gives erlang monotonic_time (its now_ms already exists) and the fake executors give a counter, so tests stay deterministic. measured_shape fills ms from two reads around peak_scheduled. Then x_sim_deliveries_per_second{seed,schedule} = deliveries * 1000 / max(ms, 1), integer to stay identical across rust and gleam. That needs a sim compile, so it rides verify45.\nBaseline rule unchanged: the first 3 runs on pack 11, then a regression is a move beyond 10% of their median.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_4 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 4u32, "2026-11-01T06:17Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:17 x: metric (1) IMPLEMENTED (unstaged); this is n3's design. The sim now measures time again.\n- sim_run.spi: beam_executors and executor_pair gain clock_ms : () -> i32. idle_beam and fake_pair read 0, so test blocks stay deterministic on rust and gleam. 'timed' wraps scheduled_by between two clock reads. peak_scheduled returns a typed 'measured' record { sched; mb; took }, and measured_shape fills ms from it, replacing shape_for's ms = 0.\n- cells_executor.spi: clock_ms = erlang:monotonic_time(millisecond). simframes wires it through a new with_meters step.\n- sim_report.spi: a phantom-unit metric type (:! per_second = (); :! metric u = metric_row), 'exposition \\* u.' printing the Prometheus row, and deliveries_rate : metric per_second = deliveries * 1000 / max(ms, 1), integer. Each schedule's outcome line carries the row after its XSIM line (inside the same string, so the line count test holds). New test the_delivery_rate_is_an_integer_per_second_row expects 285 for 2 deliveries in 7 ms.\n- The cell budget caught 3 new over-budget definitions on the way (idle_beam 284, peak_scheduled 349, with_vm 284). Split them: named no_reading/no_counts/unwatched, timed, with_meters. Now 0 findings in 30 files.\nProof: x-parity-metrics1 (job-1793513794793, xqueue3, pack 11). Pass rule: sim build exit 0 (sim notebooks incl. the new test, cell_budget names), client build exit 0, parity 153 = 153 and 0 differing, and the x_ rows (x_gleam_part_lines_max x2, x_e2e_wall_seconds x2, x_e2e_parity_differing_rows 0) printed.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_5 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 5u32, "2026-11-01T06:46Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:46 x: FIRST LIVE ROWS (x-parity-metrics1, sim build exit 0 with clock_ms and the new rate test; parity half still running): x_gleam_part_lines_max xsim_split 945 and xsim_frames 954 (gate 1000). x_sim_deliveries_per_second seed 1 schedule 1 = 5, schedule 2 = 0. FINDING: the rate truncates. Integer deliveries * 1000 / ms reads 0 when a short ladder delivers few events over seconds of real Cells compiles, so the row hides the signal. Fix, decided now: emit the raw pair x_sim_run_ms{seed,schedule} and x_sim_deliveries_total{seed,schedule}, both integers and exact on every backend, and let the reader (Prometheus rate, or the baseline script) divide. A phantom unit per row (:! milliseconds, :! count) keeps the types honest. The rate function goes away, so there's no float formatting across backends.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_6 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 6u32, "2026-11-01T06:48Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:48 x: n5's fix APPLIED (unstaged). sim_report.spi: the phantom units are now ':! milliseconds' and ':! count', with run_ms : metric milliseconds and deliveries_total : metric count, plus metric_rows printing both. deliveries_rate and per_second are removed, so nothing truncates and nothing is float. The test is now a_run_reports_its_milliseconds_and_deliveries_as_exact_rows: shape_of 2 gives x_sim_run_ms{seed=\"1\",schedule=\"1\"} 7 and x_sim_deliveries_total{...} 2. sim_run's outcome line carries metric_rows. NOT yet budget-checked or compiled: the compiler's pack12a holds the box heavy-build window (02:47, 105 min), so its first check is the next node run (verify45 or a parity run). x-parity-metrics1, still running, proves the previous shape.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_7 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 7u32, "2026-11-01T06:57Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:57 x: x-parity-metrics1 (job-1793513794793, 41 min, xqueue3, pack 11, tree = the user's commit 48bdcb2) by n4's rule: PASS. Sim build exit 0 (clock_ms, the budget splits and the old rate test). Client build exit 0 (cell_budget names). e2e-parity 'exits stub 0 sim 0; rows stub 153 sim 153', 0 differing. All x_ rows printed.\nFIRST BASELINE ROWS (run 1 of 3):\n- x_gleam_part_lines_max xsim_split 945, xsim_frames 954 (gate 1000; X-HAND-UNITS fired)\n- x_e2e_wall_seconds stub 133, sim 367, so the sim-served tutorial is 2.76x the stub's wall time on identical traffic\n- x_e2e_parity_differing_rows 0\n- x_sim_deliveries_per_second 5 and 0 (the truncation n5 found; the raw-pair rows replace them in the tree)\nREADING the 234 s gap, not yet a finding: sim-run.ps1 pays mix deps.get + mix compile of Cells + an elixir lib probe + an erl boot before 'serve' listens, and e2e.ps1 waits up to 300 s for its port. The serve path needs none of Cells' mix build (sim_serve uses only OTP). Hypothesis to measure: a serve-only launch that skips the mix steps brings sim within 1.5x of stub. Rule, written now: same tree, parity mode, adopt iff the sim wall time falls >= 40% and the parity rows stay 153 = 153 with 0 differing.\nThis also gates PS1-TO-SPIRAL n20 port 2: retiring stub.ps1 would make e2e slower by ~234 s per run until the serve launch is lean.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_8 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 8u32, "2026-11-01T07:25Z", "x", "2026-11-01 03:25 x: x-parity-lean1 (job-1793516385831, 25 min, xqueue3, pack 11) by n7's rule: PASS. Sim build exit 0 (first compile of the raw-pair rows and the guarded sim-run.ps1). Client build exit 0. Parity 153 = 153, 0 differing. Sim e2e 105 s vs the 367 s before the lean serve launch: -71%, past the >= 40% bar. The sim-served tutorial is now FASTER than the stub (124 s) on identical traffic.\nRows (run 2 of 3 for the baseline):\n- x_sim_run_ms schedule 1 = 2351, schedule 2 = 354\n- x_sim_deliveries_total 20 / 19\n- x_gleam_part_lines_max 945 / 954\n- x_e2e_wall_seconds stub 124, sim 105; differing rows 0\nThe raw-pair fix works: the old rate row read 5 and 0, and the exact rows show 20 deliveries in 2.35 s, then 19 in 0.35 s (the first schedule pays the cold Cells start).\nChange (unstaged): scripts/sim-run.ps1 picks the entry first, and for 'serve' skips the Cells mix deps.get/compile, the elixir lib probe and the Elixir and Cells -pa paths. sim_serve needs only OTP and the frames ebins.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_9 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 9u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:34 x: ITEM UPGRADE (round 4). Fields: tags gain team:x and typefeatures; 180 / light / agent stand. P2 is justified: it measures, it doesn't change product behaviour, but X-HAND-UNITS' and X-BACKEND-SWITCHES' decisions now read its rows.\nDEFINITION OF DONE (verifiable):\n(1) Every node-run parity/sim run prints these rows: x_gleam_part_lines_max{package} for xsim_split and xsim_frames, x_e2e_wall_seconds{server=stub|sim}, x_e2e_parity_differing_rows, and per schedule x_sim_run_ms{seed,schedule} plus x_sim_deliveries_total{seed,schedule}.\n(2) THREE baseline runs on one core (pack 12, 3445FAA05588C96E) recorded on this item with each row's median. Have: metrics1 and lean1 were pack 11 (the rows' shape changed since), so the baseline restarts with verify46 and 2 parity runs.\n(3) The regression rule is applied once: a row beyond 10% of its median is reported on the item.\nArtifact: the medians table in a note. Command: pwsh x/scripts/node-run.ps1 -Mode parity -Core <core> -Label x-parity-baseNN.\nNEXT CONCRETE STEP: once verify46 lands, launch x-parity-base1 and base2 on xqueue3 (the node is free of other x jobs then) and record the medians. Then close.\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN (as built, to keep): rows are 'metric u' (':! metric u = metric_row'), with the unit as a phantom index (':! milliseconds', ':! count'). Constructors fix the unit (run_ms : metric milliseconds, deliveries_total : metric count), and the printer is '\\* u.' generic, so a reader can't add milliseconds to counts by type. Integers only: no float printing to diverge between rust and gleam, and no derived rate (the 10-31 rate row truncated to 0, n5). The PowerShell-side rows (gleam-modules, e2e-parity) are glue that already held the numbers. They move with their hosts (GLEAM-PARTS-EMIT, the stub retirement), and the Spiral replacements reuse 'metric u'.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_10 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 10u32, "2026-11-01T13:23Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:23 x: PRE-REGISTERED (main's heads-up). Installed compiler pack12b/pack13a has a Rust codegen regression: a mutual-tail group loop can emit 'st = GroupN::M..(..)' in a function without 'st', and rustc fails with E0425. Rule for x's proofs from now on: a client Rust crate failing with E0425 on that signature makes the run VOID (compiler's bug, fix pack14a), not an x failure, and the run is repeated on the fixed pack. x-parity-base1 is pinned to 3445FAA05588C96E (the pack verify46 built every client crate green on), so it's not exposed unless that pin is stale. Box core at this note: 61D2D7AE0E946086.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_11 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 11u32, "2026-11-01T13:26Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:26 x: BASELINE run 1 of 3 (pack 12, 3445FAA05588C96E), x-parity-base1 (job-1793538879510, 9.8 min, xqueue3): PASS (builds exit 0, parity 153/153, 0 differing).\nRows:\n- x_gleam_part_lines_max xsim_split 945, xsim_frames 954\n- x_sim_run_ms schedule 1 = 1087, schedule 2 = 95\n- x_sim_deliveries_total 20 / 19\n- x_e2e_wall_seconds stub 43, sim 42\n- x_e2e_parity_differing_rows 0\nNote: the e2e times are much lower than lean1's (124/105) on an emptier node (no verify46 alongside), which is exactly why the median of 3 on one core is the baseline and not one run. This run is ALSO the final stub-vs-sim parity record PS1-TO-SPIRAL n38 wanted before archiving the stub. Runs 2 and 3: next idle node slots.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_12 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 12u32, "2026-11-01T13:39Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:39 x: BASELINE RESET, decided now. The node's compiler core changed at 09:33:02 to DBC5EC18F14C354C (base2's launch at 09:34 failed the 3445FAA pin with '| verifying)'), and main announced a one-shot node ship at 10:23 after compiler's A/B. Run 1 (x-parity-base1, 3445FAA) can't share a core with runs 2 and 3, so it stays recorded as a single pack-12 reading (n of 09:2x), outside the median. Rule: the 3-run baseline starts on the FIRST node core that holds after 10:23 (read it with the SpiralCompilerCore.dll hash, box and node equal), runs back to back on xqueue3 with node-run.ps1 -Mode parity -Core <that core>, and the median per row is taken from those 3. No x node run launches before the ship settles, so none gets cut in half.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_13 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 13u32, "2026-11-01T14:52Z", "x", "2026-11-01 10:52 x: BASELINE run 1 of 3 on pack 14a (DBC5EC18F14C354C), x-parity-base2 (job-1793543579604, 17.3 min, xqueue3, alongside compiler-libord-seq): PASS (builds exit 0, parity 153/153, 0 differing). Rows: x_gleam_part_lines_max split 945 / frames 954; x_sim_run_ms schedule 1 = 2513, schedule 2 = 275; x_sim_deliveries_total 20 / 19; x_e2e_wall_seconds stub 82, sim 108; differing rows 0. Run 2 of 3 (x-parity-base3, job-1793544756790) is running back to back.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_14 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 14u32, "2026-11-01T15:14Z", "x", "2026-11-01 11:14 x: BASELINE run 2 of 3 on pack 14a, x-parity-base3 (job-1793544756790, 19.7 min, xqueue3): PASS (builds exit 0, parity 153/153, 0 differing). Rows: x_gleam_part_lines_max 945 / 954; x_sim_run_ms schedule 1 = 1996, schedule 2 = 269; x_sim_deliveries_total 20 / 19; x_e2e_wall_seconds stub 164, sim 110; differing rows 0. Run 3 (x-parity-base4) is launching back to back.")
inl note_of_x_run_metrics_15 () = Note ("X-RUN-METRICS", 15u32, "2026-11-01T15:46Z", "x", "2026-11-01 11:46 x: BASELINE COMPLETE (pack 14a, core DBC5EC18F14C354C). Run 3 of 3: x-parity-base4 (job-1793546048389, 31 min) PASS (builds exit 0, 153/153, 0 differing). MEDIANS of base2/base3/base4:\n- x_gleam_part_lines_max: split 945, frames 954 (identical in all 3 runs)\n- x_sim_run_ms: schedule 1 = 2513 (2513, 1996, 6553), schedule 2 = 275 (275, 269, 296)\n- x_sim_deliveries_total: 20 / 19 (identical)\n- x_e2e_wall_seconds: stub 164 (82, 164, 190), sim 110 (108, 110, 207)\n- x_e2e_parity_differing_rows: 0\nREGRESSION RULE APPLIED ONCE (the DoD's clause 3), to run 3 against the medians. Beyond 10%: x_sim_run_ms schedule 1 6553 (+161%), x_e2e_wall_seconds stub 190 (+16%), sim 207 (+88%). Reported here.\nREADING, and a rule REVISION for the wall-time rows: on a shared node (compiler jobs alongside) the wall-time rows swing 2-3x between back-to-back runs on one core, so a cross-run 10% band flags load, not regressions. Revision: the 10% cross-run band applies only to DETERMINISTIC rows (part lines, deliveries totals, differing rows), and those must match EXACTLY, since they were identical across all 3 runs. Time rows (run_ms, e2e wall) are compared only WITHIN one job against an in-job A/A arm (memory ab-needs-in-job-aa), never across runs.\nDoD met: (1) rows printed every run, (2) 3 runs on one core with medians recorded, (3) rule applied once with its result. CLOSED. Its consumers: X-HAND-UNITS (part lines), X-BACKEND-SWITCHES (run_ms, in-job), PS1-TO-SPIRAL S1 (now unblocked, since the baseline no longer needs parity mode).")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s1_1 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S1", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:33Z", "kit", "11-01 00:3x kit, slice of EOIE-SCRUM-XP (user 00:2x via main: 'speed up the agile->scrum/xp migration'; slices of an hour or less). Spec: EOIE-SCRUM-XP M3 DESIGN note (10-30 13:5x) and the 10-22 typestate draft.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s1_2 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S1", 2u32, "2026-11-01T05:02Z", "kit", "11-01 01:0x kit: DONE in the tree (unstaged). New granular HostFree+LibReferenced package src/agile_sprint (standards 0 findings, census row, ratchet ok). Typestate as the house idiom (phantom states sprint_planned/running/closed, GADT sprint_op from to + sprint_plan composition, sprint_lifecycle typechecked at compile time); data: sprint_terms, candidate (task, minutes, standing ItemOpen|ItemDone|ItemUnknown), sprint_item (fate Committed|Dropped), sprint (status SprintOpen|SprintClosed), start_outcome = Started | AnotherSprintOpen | ItemNotOpen | ItemUnestimated, sprint_review (done, carried, dropped minutes, goal_met), velocity = mean done minutes of the last n reviews. Tests: a_second_open_sprint_and_bad_items_are_refused, closing_reports_done_carried_and_dropped_minutes_and_velocity. PROOF: node job-1793508804444-kit-node-sprint-cadence, agile_sprint/model.spi 2/2. (First run failed to parse: a binding named 'open', a Spiral keyword; renamed.) Ships with the S2 crate; S2 next.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_1 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:33Z", "kit", "11-01 00:3x kit, slice of EOIE-SCRUM-XP (user 00:2x via main: 'speed up the agile->scrum/xp migration'; slices of an hour or less). Spec: EOIE-SCRUM-XP M3 DESIGN note (10-30 13:5x) and the 10-22 typestate draft.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_2 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:35Z", "kit", "11-01 02:3x kit (round 3): the row codec is DONE in the tree (unstaged), in a new granular HostFree+LibReferenced package src/agile_sprint_rows (kept out of agile_sprint because the census ratchet measures typed-feature density per package, and 54 plain codec lines diluted the sprint model's GADT/forall/existential share 179 -> 233 lines; one concern per package). Rows in the state's own shape: 'inl sprint_<id> () = Sprint (id, goal, start, finish, SprintOpen|SprintClosed)' and 'inl sprint_item_<id>_<task> () = SprintItem (id, task, <n>i32, Committed|Dropped)'; sprints_of reads them back from the whole state text, ignoring every other row. Test sprint_rows_round_trip_through_the_state_text (open and closed sprint with a committed and a dropped item; a Task row in between). PROOF: node job-1793514672111-kit-node-r3f pass (first run: minutes parsed from the whole tail read 0; fixed to the field between the first two commas). LEFT for S2: append these rows through kit's state writer (append-only, like note segments) and the byte-identical regression of the old agile verbs with no sprint rows present; that needs the agile crates, so it rides the next node candidate build.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_3 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: (a) 'kit backlog sprint start' on a fixture appends exactly one Sprint row plus one SprintItem row per item and nothing else (diff of state before/after = only those lines); (b) every existing agile verb's output on the live-shaped fixture is byte-identical with and without zero sprint rows present (regression test); (c) agile_sprint_rows round trip stays green. NEXT STEP: an append path in kit_agile_mutation that writes sprint rows like note segments (append, CAS), using agile_sprint_rows.sprint_rows. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: sprint lifecycle is a GADT over phantom states (sprint_op from to in src/agile_sprint, compile-time checked); sprint data are records and closed unions (sprint_status, item_fate, item_standing); still stringly and to fix in S2: sprint id and task id are raw strings and minutes a bare i32: make nominal sprint_id, task_id and minutes so a task id cannot be passed as a sprint id and a negative estimate is unrepresentable by construction (minutes_of returns option, not 0).")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_4 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 4u32, "2026-11-01T18:15Z", "kit", "11-01 14:15 kit: EMACS-STEALS folded into the DoD (user 14:0x via main): lease -> actual-minutes rows (org-clock: kit agile begin/wrap writes ActualMinutes (item, minutes, lane) rows, append-only) and typed StateChange rows (item, from, to, at, author, reason) whose reason is REQUIRED (an empty reason is refused, like org's logged state changes). Model: a state_change record with a status union for from/to, so an illegal transition is a type error (reuse agile_sprint's typestate op GADT). Queue: S2 -> S8 are kit's P0 slice work, built typefeatureset-first on the typed-crate architecture as it lands (KIT-TYPED-CRATES).")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_5 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 5u32, "2026-11-01T20:09Z", "kit", "11-01 16:09 kit: EMACS-STEALS half of S2 is modeled and proven (unstaged). New HostFree package agile_activity: task_status + a closed move union (Start/Shelve/Pause/Block/Finish/Resume/Unblock/Reopen) with ends and move_of (an illegal from->to is unrepresentable as a move); reason, built only by reason_of (a blank reason is refused); a state_change record built only by changed (outcomes Changed | Refused NoReason | Refused IllegalMove); actual_minutes from lease start/end (rounded up), plus actual_total. Node 2/2 and Linux 2/2. New package agile_activity_rows: the StateChange/ActualMinutes typed .spi row codec (change_row, actual_row, changes_of, actuals_of; quotes folded, other rows ignored), Linux 1/1. DESIGN NOTE: the first draft used a GADT plus an existential (any_move) and failed with 'Cannot dyn an existential into a runtime var'. Spiral existentials are compile-time only, so runtime-read statuses get a validated closed union instead. NEXT: append these rows in the agile state via the note-segment append path (no Task row rewrite) from kit agile set/begin/wrap, with the byte-identity regression test when no activity rows exist. That is the verb half and rides the typed-crate pattern (KIT-TYPED-CRATES).")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_6 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 6u32, "2026-11-01T20:48Z", "kit", "11-01 16:48 kit DESIGN for the verb half (read from the code): the append mechanism already exists. agile_note_segments.note_appended (export kit_agile_note_appended state_dir row stamp) appends ANY row text to the month segment notes_YYYYMM.spi and registers it in state/package.spiproj with replace_file_unchanged CAS, without rewriting Task rows (KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS=1 path). The real work is the SCHEMA: agile check typechecks the state modules, so StateChange/ActualMinutes/SprintItem rows need their constructors declared in the state schema, a versioned migration (agile_planning_migration shape) that every lane's backlog gets. Plan: (1) an activity segment kind activity_YYYYMM.spi (not notes_), with a schema module activity_schema.spi declaring the activity row union from agile_activity_rows' codec, added by a one-shot migration verb and refused by agile check when rows exist without it; (2) set/begin/wrap append StateChange/ActualMinutes through the generalized appender (segment kind as a typed union NotesSegment | ActivitySegment); (3) the byte-identity regression: with no activity rows, every existing verb output and the state bytes are unchanged. This touches the shared live state, so it lands only after a node proof on a COPY of the live backlog plus a CLI acceptance, never straight on the live tree.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_7 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 7u32, "2026-11-01T22:59Z", "kit", "11-01 18:59 kit: (1) the schema half is modeled (unstaged), in agile_activity_rows: activity_schema_text () emits the activity_row union (StateChange 6 strings, ActualMinutes string*string*i32*string) for an activity_schema.spi state module; schema_verdict_of rows declared = NoActivityRows | SchemaPresent | SchemaMissing n is the typed refusal agile check will apply. (2) The hand-written status_word/status_of_word case<->string maps are replaced by lib reflection.union_to_string / union_try_pick (main 17:4x quality slip 2); the codec round-trip test proves the reflection parse. Linux worker: agile_activity 4/4, agile_activity_rows 2/2. Remaining for DoD: the migration verb writing activity_schema.spi plus the manifest entry, set/begin/wrap appending rows, and the byte-identity regression on a copy of the live backlog.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_8 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 8u32, "2026-11-03T02:34Z", "x", "2026-11-02 22:34 x (auto-claimed by the gate, x-b7595ac3): RELEASED without edits. What's left (n-1) is a migration verb that writes activity_schema.spi into every lane's backlog state, set/begin/wrap appending StateChange/ActualMinutes rows through the note-segment appender, and the byte-identity regression on a copy of the live backlog. All of it changes kit's agile verbs and the shared state schema and ships in a kit TRAIN, so it's a kit-lane change: another lane can't land it without kit's window and train. Resume condition: kit takes it at its next train. x keeps working its Cells and lib queue.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_9 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 9u32, "2026-11-03T02:35Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps (auto-claimed by gate): RELEASED + Blocked, same as x (n8) and same as REMOTE-EXEC-S6. Remaining work (migration verb writing activity_schema.spi into every backlog, set/begin/wrap appending StateChange/ActualMinutes via the note-segment appender, byte-identity regression on a live-backlog copy) CHANGES kit's agile verbs + the shared state schema and ships in a kit TRAIN - a kit-lane change, not landable by another lane. The --any gate keeps auto-claiming this Active P0 to idle non-kit lanes (x, x-dumps) who can only bounce. RESUME: kit takes it at its next train/window; un-block then. PATTERN: kit's train-series P0s (REMOTE-EXEC-Sx, SCRUM-XP-Sx) should be Blocked-until-train or excluded from --any for non-kit lanes, else every idle lane thrashes them.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_10 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 10u32, "2026-11-03T10:09Z", "kit", "11-03 06:1x kit: TRAIN14c INSTALLED (kit.exe 5B9082C1; 58/58 notebooks; crate gate 284/284 under 1000). Unblocked: the train it waited on has installed. Resume from the last kit note.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_11 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 11u32, "2026-11-03T12:51Z", "kit", "11-03 09:0x kit: parked as Planned (WIP cap) behind KIT-CATCH-UP, the user's priority today (11-03 04:0x: \"groom ... so kit stops falling back to the drifty project\"). RESUME at exactly: (1) the one-shot migration verb writing state/activity_schema.spi + the manifest entry (agile_activity_rows.activity_schema_text, schema_verdict_of in agile check); (2) set/begin/wrap append StateChange/ActualMinutes through the note-segment appender with a typed segment kind (NotesSegment | ActivitySegment); (3) a byte-identity regression on a COPY of the live backlog (every verb output + state bytes unchanged when no activity rows exist). Proof on a copy only; never on the live state first. Write the new verbs in the x shape (typed command union), not string dispatch.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_12 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 12u32, "2026-11-04T08:36Z", "kit", "11-04 04:5x RESUMED step (1): `kit agile migrate-activity preview|apply <root>` (new linked crate kit_agile_activity_migration; AgileMigrateActivity in agile_subcommand_domain with root after the mode; routed in kit_agile_mutation as a write, so the agile lock covers it). apply writes state/activity_schema.spi from agile_activity_rows.activity_schema_text (only if missing) and adds `activity_schema` as the FIRST module of state/package.spiproj (define-before-use), idempotently via modules_with_schema; preview only reports would-write/would-add; every step reports kept/written/added. Test the_schema_module_is_added_once_and_modes_read_back covers the insert, idempotence, the bare-project case and the modes. europa --check exit 0 for the crate, subcommand.spi and agile_mutation.spi. NOT run against any live backlog. Next: (2) set/begin/wrap append StateChange/ActualMinutes rows; (3) a byte-identity regression on a COPY of a backlog.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_13 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 13u32, "2026-11-04T08:38Z", "kit", "11-04 05:0x: the SPRINT rows half of the title. agile_sprint_rows gains sprints_append (header `///- --no-respell` on first use, then the sprint's Sprint/SprintItem rows; earlier text never rewritten). Test appending_a_sprint_never_rewrites_existing_rows: the first text is an exact prefix of the second, and sprints_of reads both sprints back in order. With the rows in their own state file (state/sprints.spi, note-segment style), Task rows and old verbs stay byte-identical while no sprint exists. europa --check exit 0 (as-is and bodies); box notebook queued. Remaining: the sprint schema line in the migration (same shape as activity_schema; one migrate-activity apply adds both modules) and the byte-identity regression on a backlog COPY once TRAIN21 installs the verbs.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_14 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 14u32, "2026-11-04T08:42Z", "kit", "11-04 05:1x: the sprints file is SELF-CONTAINED, like the backlog's views.spi and claims.spi: each state module declares its own union, and a later module cannot see another's constructors (verified: a sprints module without its schema fails ETY-UnboundVariable Sprint). sprints_schema_text generates the unions sprint_status_row and item_fate_row from the agile_sprint model by reflection (so it can't drift), plus sprint_row (Sprint/SprintItem); sprints_appended writes that schema on first use, then appends rows only. A sample self-contained sprints.spi (schema + Sprint + SprintItem rows) passes --check on europa; rows.spi exit 0 as-is and bodies. Earlier pass: box notebook 1/1 before this change; re-run queued. So no migration module is needed for sprints: adding `sprints` to state/package.spiproj on first append is the only manifest step (left to the S3 `sprint start` verb). The activity rows (migrate-activity) are the separate schema-module path.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_15 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 15u32, "2026-11-04T08:51Z", "kit", "11-04 05:3x: code complete (append-only sprint rows with self-contained schema, migrate-activity verb), every piece type-checked and box-notebook green. The remaining DoD item, the byte-identity regression (old verbs' output and state bytes unchanged on a backlog COPY with no sprint rows), needs the installed binary. parked: waiting on TRAIN21 install.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_16 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 16u32, "2026-11-06T11:14Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:1x kit: unblocked. Its only remaining DoD step, the byte-identity regression on a backlog copy, needed an installed binary, and TRAIN33 installed at 07:05. Planned until picked.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s2_17 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S2", 17u32, "2026-11-06T11:44Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit: DONE. The byte-identity regression, run on two copies of the pre-rename backlog (no Sprint or SprintItem rows; the 9 Sprint mentions are note text), with the previous installed kit 5B9082C1 (retired copy) against the TRAIN33 kit 6CEA882D. Read verbs list, show KIT-RENAME, next --team kit and wip gave byte-identical output (elapsed_ms and the root path normalized; 271191, 70374, 1589 and 236 bytes). Write verbs agile note and agile set, run in the same minute, left byte-identical state (agile.spi sha C530DDEE6F88 on both).")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s3_1 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S3", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:33Z", "kit", "11-01 00:3x kit, slice of EOIE-SCRUM-XP (user 00:2x via main: 'speed up the agile->scrum/xp migration'; slices of an hour or less). Spec: EOIE-SCRUM-XP M3 DESIGN note (10-30 13:5x) and the 10-22 typestate draft.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s3_2 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S3", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:37Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: 'kit backlog sprint start <root> S-1 7 \"ship the sprint verbs\" --items A,B' appends the Sprint row and one SprintItem per item (state diff = only those lines) and prints the committed minutes; it exits 2 with a named reason for a second open sprint (AnotherSprintOpen S-0), a Done or unknown item (ItemNotOpen X) and an unestimated item (ItemUnestimated X); 4 CLI tests, node green. NEXT STEP: the verb in kit_agile_routes' backlog ceremony (M1 routing exists) calling agile_sprint.started over candidates built from the task rows (status -> item_standing, estimate -> minutes), writing via S2's append path. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: start_outcome is already a closed union (Started | AnotherSprintOpen | ItemNotOpen | ItemUnestimated) so each refusal is a pattern with its own exit text, never a string compare; candidate.standing a closed union; days as a nominal (1..30 smart constructor), dates derived, never typed by hand.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s3_3 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S3", 3u32, "2026-11-01T18:15Z", "kit", "11-01 14:15 kit: EMACS-STEALS fold: an over-estimate note (org effort warnings). When ActualMinutes sum > estimate x 1.5, kit writes one typed EstimateExceeded note row (item, estimate, actual) once per item and surfaces it in standup.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s3_4 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S3", 4u32, "2026-11-01T20:34Z", "kit", "11-01 16:34 kit: the over-estimate rule is modeled and proven (unstaged), in src/agile_activity beside actual_minutes. estimate_verdict = WithinEstimate | EstimateExceeded (estimate, actual) | AlreadyNoted; estimate_verdict_of estimate actual noted: exactly 1.5x is within, past 1.5x is exceeded, it is noted once per item, and an item without an estimate never warns. Test an_overrun_past_one_and_a_half_estimates_is_noted_once: Linux worker 3/3 for the package. NEXT (verb half): after each ActualMinutes append, sum actual_total for the item, run estimate_verdict_of with noted = an EstimateExceeded note row already exists, and append one typed EstimateExceeded row; standup (S8) surfaces it.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s3_5 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S3", 5u32, "2026-11-03T07:33Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 35 h ago by kit, progress kept at 40%. Resume from that note: ackage. NEXT (verb half): after each ActualMinutes append, sum actual_total for the item, run estimate_verdict_of with noted = an EstimateExceeded note row already exists, and append one typed EstimateExceeded row; standup (S8) surfaces it.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s3_6 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S3", 6u32, "2026-11-04T08:51Z", "kit", "11-04 05:3x kit: `kit backlog sprint start <root> <id> <days> <goal> [--items A,B]` BUILT (unstaged). New linked crate kit_agile_sprint (typed sprint_verb union, SprintStart). It reads state/agile.spi and state/sprints.spi, builds candidates through two new direct linkable exports (agile_task_census kit_agile_task_status_of, agile_planning_fields kit_agile_planning_minutes_of; the old lambda exports are kept for their host callers), and calls the existing model `started`, which refuses another open sprint, an unknown or Done item and an unestimated item. On Started it appends via agile_sprint_rows.sprints_appended (self-contained schema on first use) and adds `sprints` to state/package.spiproj once. Dates come from civil_date (days since epoch -> YYYY-MM-DD; tests 1970-01-01, 2026-11-04 and leap day 2000-02-29). Wiring: AgileSprint (root after the mode), routed in kit_agile_mutation as a write (agile lock), and `sprint` added to the backlog ceremony's verbs. europa --check exit 0 for the crate and the 5 touched files. Box notebook and node size run going; then TRAIN21. CLI test against a backlog COPY after install.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s3_7 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S3", 7u32, "2026-11-04T08:58Z", "kit", "11-04 05:4x: PARKED out of TRAIN21 for size. Node size run: kit_agile_sprint 1390 lines (box notebook 2/2 pass, europa --check exit 0), and kit_agile_mutation reached 996 with the route. Crate and wiring are in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\SCRUM-XP-S3-sprint-start-1104 (crate + wired/ copies). The census and planning direct exports stay in the tree (574 / 817 lines). Next: split so the agile_sprint model + agile_sprint_rows parsing live in one linked unit (sprint_started_rows: inputs as a tuple of strings, output as an array_base of one {code; message; sprints_text} row), with the CLI crate keeping only argv and file I/O. And give kit_agile_mutation headroom first (996): e.g. move agile_entry's lock wrapper or the route table into a unit.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s3_8 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S3", 8u32, "2026-11-06T08:19Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 04:1x kit groom: parked Active to Planned, untouched since 11-04 (over 24 h; WIP cap: Active means touched within 48 h, and kit held 42 Active). Progress kept. Resume order unchanged; the next installable train (TRAIN33, link lost, result unread) carries no change to this item unless its last note says so.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s3_9 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S3", 9u32, "2026-11-06T13:29Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 09:4x kit: DONE. The split crates (kit_agile_sprint CLI + linked kit_agile_sprint_state) and the AgileSprint route are in the tree and in the TRAIN33 install (kit.exe 6CEA882D). DoD checked on scratch copies of the backlog with the installed kit: (1) kit agile sprint start <copy> S-PROBE-1 7 probe goal --items SCRUM-XP-S3,SCRUM-XP-S4 printed ok id=S-PROBE-1 items=2 finish=2026-11-13, wrote typed rows to state/sprints.spi (SprintOpen, Committed) and listed sprints in state/package.spiproj; (2) a second start refused: sprint S-PROBE-1 is still open; (3) an unknown item and a Done item refused: item ... is unknown or Done; (4) an unestimated item refused: item KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR has no estimate; no sprints.spi was written on any refusal. The CLI test ran in TRAIN33 notebooks (234/234).")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s4_1 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S4", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:33Z", "kit", "11-01 00:3x kit, slice of EOIE-SCRUM-XP (user 00:2x via main: 'speed up the agile->scrum/xp migration'; slices of an hour or less). Spec: EOIE-SCRUM-XP M3 DESIGN note (10-30 13:5x) and the 10-22 typestate draft.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s4_2 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S4", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:38Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: 'kit backlog sprint add <root> S-1 C' appends one SprintItem (Committed) and 'sprint drop <root> S-1 A' appends a Dropped row for A without deleting the Committed one (state diff = one appended line each); add refuses ItemNotOpen/ItemUnestimated and a closed sprint; 3 CLI tests. NEXT STEP: two verbs beside sprint start using agile_sprint_rows.sprint_item_row and the S2 append path. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: ChangeItems is already the GADT op sprint_op sprint_running sprint_running, so add/drop only typecheck on a running sprint value; item_fate = Committed | Dropped (closed union), the review reads the last fate per task, never a status string.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s4_3 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S4", 3u32, "2026-11-04T09:21Z", "kit", "11-04 06:3x kit: `kit backlog sprint add|drop <root> <id> <item>` BUILT (unstaged, not wired yet; europa pack34 + toml lib --check exit 0 for all 3 files, as-is and test bodies). Append-only: add appends a Committed SprintItem row (the sprint must be open; the item must be open and estimated, via the model's first_refusal); drop appends a Dropped row for an item already in the sprint, so the dropped row is kept as history. agile_sprint_rows.items_of now lets the LAST row per task win, in place (test a_later_item_row_replaces_the_earlier_one_for_that_task), so a drop replaces rather than duplicates. The state unit gained sprint_item_appended. Wiring + node size run together with S3/S5.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s4_4 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S4", 4u32, "2026-11-06T08:19Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 04:1x kit groom: parked Active to Planned, untouched since 11-04 (over 24 h; WIP cap: Active means touched within 48 h, and kit held 42 Active). Progress kept. Resume order unchanged; the next installable train (TRAIN33, link lost, result unread) carries no change to this item unless its last note says so.")
inl note_of_scrum_xp_s4_5 () = Note ("SCRUM-XP-S4", 5u32, "2026-11-06T13:34Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 09:5x kit, dogfood with the TRAIN33 kit (6CEA882D) on a scratch backlog copy: DONE. sprint add <copy> S-PROBE-1 SCRUM-XP-S5 printed ok, sprint drop <copy> S-PROBE-1 SCRUM-XP-S4 printed ok, and sprint show then listed SCRUM-XP-S4 as dropped, so the dropped SprintItem row stays for the review. The CLI test ran in TRAIN33 notebooks.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_1 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): OWNERSHIP: compiler owns the row model and the per-backend numbers; kit owns collection (KIT-EXEC-METRICS, main's naming). MODEL: one row per (sample, backend, phase in Spiral|Build|Run) with core hash, machine, wall_ms, cpu_ms, peak_rss_bytes, output_bytes, exit, agrees_with_oracle. Units are phantom types (ms, bytes) so a ms can't be added to a byte count. The Prometheus names are spiral_backend_seconds{backend,sample,phase}, spiral_backend_peak_bytes{...} and spiral_backend_agree{...}. QUESTIONS IT ANSWERS: (1) time drain = sum of wall by backend over a day of matrix + cells runs; (2) Rust vs Gleam = Build+Run wall per sample size class; (3) Zig vs C on every axis = per-sample Zig/C ratios for build, run, rss and size, with the gap list (which axis loses, which sample) as the step-by-step improvement queue; (4) Lua/Wasm = run-wall rank among fast-startup backends. FIRST CPU-LIGHT STEP: write the row type in Spiral (lib-first, granular) and an importer from today's results.tsv/rows.tsv columns, so the past matrix runs become the first data.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_2 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 2u32, "2026-11-01T07:09Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:09 compiler: first rows for the model, measured build-free today: (1) lib test targets per backend (rust 427, gleam 205, cuda 141, ts 117, fsharp 72, lua 65, zig 27, c 22, delphi 10, python 9, lean/wasm/kotlin/swift 0; 746 tests): coverage = share of tests per backend; (2) Rust emit quality: clones per kloc 94.6 -> 85.3 after pack12b's Copy-field edit (2,778 -> 2,510 clones in 476-477 samples); (3) matrix volume per backend in one full run: Zig 1,088 rows, C 552, Rust 508, Lean 458, Kotlin 452, Python/Swift 451, TS 449, Lua 448, Gleam 447, Delphi 446, Wasm 444, Bend 420, Fsharp 396. The row model must carry suite (contracts vs examples) to separate expected negatives from gaps.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_3 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:34 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS ok (P0, 300m, light, team:compiler). DEFINITION OF DONE: lib/spiral/metrics.spi with tests green on rust and gleam (lib_gate.ps1 -File metrics.spi); an importer program that reads a matrix results.tsv into typed rows and prints Prometheus text, whose output for run single-flight-20261101-080900 has exactly 7,409 'spiral_backend_compile_seconds' samples and is parsed back by a test (round trip). NEXT STEP: write metrics.spi's types (below) and one test that builds a row and renders one exposition line. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: units are PHANTOMS: ':! quantity u = f64' with nominal unit tags 'ms', 'bytes', 'count', so adding ms to bytes doesn't type check; the backend is a UNION (C | Rust | Zig | Gleam | ...; mirror lib's 'union backend', never a string); phase is a UNION (Spiral | Build | Run | Emit | Oracle); a metric is a GADT indexed by its unit: 'compile_seconds : metric ms', 'peak_bytes : metric bytes', 'agree : metric count', so a sample's value type follows from the metric; Prometheus rendering is a PROTOTYPE 'exposition t' with instances per metric (no reflection over field names); the row's suite is a union (examples | contracts | frontier | mega), which separates expected negatives from gaps (the n2 lesson). Model: lib/spiral/date_time.spi's time_span nominal for units, am' for arrays of rows.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_4 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 4u32, "2026-11-02T21:33Z", "compiler", "11-02 17:40 compiler: SLICE 1 GREEN, land pending. Draft: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL\\metrics.spi. Phantom units (`:! ms/bytes/count = ()`, `:! quantity u = f64`); unions backend_name (C, Rust, Zig, Gleam, Fsharp, Lua, Wasm, Pascal) and suite (Examples, Contracts, Frontier, Mega); a GADT `metric u` indexed by its unit (CompileSeconds/NativeSeconds : metric ms, PeakBytes : metric bytes, OracleAgree : metric count), so a sample's value type follows from its metric; `sample u` record; `exposition` renders one Prometheus line. Node lib gate on a mirror copy: 1/1 passed (rust + gleam targets), 0 type errors, executed > 0. LAND: `lib_land_window.ps1 -Draft \"metrics.spi=<draft path>\" -Label ... -Apply` once kit-TRAIN13b releases the heavy window (held at 17:38; I don't land into a held window). NEXT SLICES: the exposition prototype per metric (the plan's no-reflection rule); the results.tsv importer (typed rows -> samples) with the DoD round-trip test (7,409 compile_seconds samples for single-flight-20261101-080900).")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_5 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 5u32, "2026-11-02T21:48Z", "compiler", "11-02 17:50 compiler: SLICE 2 GREEN (node lib gate on a mirror copy: 3/3 passed on rust + gleam, 0 type errors). Same draft: backend_of / suite_of (string -> union, None for unknown), compile_sample (one results.tsv row -> option (sample ms), seconds = compile_ms / 1000), rendered. Tests: the real first row of single-flight-20261101-080900 renders `spiral_backend_compile_seconds{backend=\"c\",suite=\"examples\",id=\"samples/abi_external\"} 0.794`; an unknown backend row yields none. That run has exactly 7,409 rows, all with compile_ms, matching the DoD count. NEXT SLICE: an importer main (file_system read, lines, compile_sample per row, count + print) plus the round-trip test; then land (still waiting on kit-TRAIN13b's heavy window).")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_6 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 6u32, "2026-11-02T22:54Z", "compiler", "11-02 18:50 compiler: slice 3 green on node t1101, lib_gate 4/4 (rust+gleam), 0 type errors: results_exposition text = arraym::init (sm'::line_count text) (line_at) as array string |> am'::choose compile_sample |> am'::map' exposition; the test keeps 2 of 3 rows (unknown backend dropped). First try used sm'::lines, which is Rust-only (Gleam got panic as ... then .size on it: Unknown type for record access); see SM-LINES-PORTABLE. Draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL\\metrics.spi. Land via lib_land_window once kit-TRAIN13b frees the heavy window (also needs a metrics line at the end of lib package.spiproj modules).")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_7 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 7u32, "2026-11-03T00:33Z", "compiler", "11-02 20:3x compiler: landing queued in the same box job as SM-LINES-PORTABLE (job-1793665902376-compiler-lib-land-smlines-metrics): metrics.spi as a new lib file (base new) and package.spiproj D17DC89255957311 with '    metrics' appended after iggy. It applies when kit-TRAIN13c releases the heavy window; then a document_test row check.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_8 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 8u32, "2026-11-03T01:31Z", "compiler", "11-02 21:3x compiler: slice 4 green on node t1101, lib_gate 6/6 (rust + gleam), 0 type errors. Program BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL\\4-native-and-oracle.kitdo (2 replacements on lib metrics.spi 85075BB36A0B89E4 -> 2974F7FBB2EAB095): results_rows factored out, native_sample (NativeSeconds from native_ms on native=ran rows), oracle_sample (OracleAgree 1/0 from agree/DISAGREE, other verdicts skipped), results_oracle_samples; tests native_sample_reads_ran_rows and oracle_samples_skip_rows_without_a_verdict. Landing queued: box job job-1793669481543-compiler-lib-land-metrics4.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_9 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 9u32, "2026-11-03T02:21Z", "compiler", "11-02 22:2x compiler: slices 4 and 5 LANDED (unstaged, both closed green: document_test 22 passed, consumers compile). Lib metrics.spi now CBB772589AA5DE13. Slice 4: native_sample, oracle_sample, results_oracle_samples (land undo 20261102-213129). Slice 5: results_file_exposition (path), i.e. read_all_text then results_exposition, with a temp-file round-trip test on rust + gleam (node gate 7/7; land undo 20261102-221635). The model now reads a real results.tsv file into Prometheus lines for compile seconds, native seconds and oracle agreement per backend and suite. Next: a node runner that writes the .prom text for a run directory (consumer), then the clones_per_kloc emit measure from RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE n7.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_10 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 10u32, "2026-11-03T05:03Z", "compiler", "11-03 01:0x compiler: slice 6 green on node t1101 (lib_gate 10/10 rust + gleam, 0 type errors) after one fix round (a multi-line lambda argument to fold did not parse -> named step heavier; choose result needed an array f64 annotation, value restriction). Program BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL\\6-backend-totals.kitdo (1 replacement, lib metrics.spi CBB772589AA5DE13 -> C244036D7A779DF7): backends (), sample_value, backend_total (sum per backend), heavier/heaviest (the backend draining the most time, None when no rows), total_line, results_totals (spiral_backend_compile_seconds_total{backend=...} per backend present) and results_heaviest; tests for totals, heaviest and the empty case. This answers the first question of the story (which backend drains most of our time) from a results.tsv. Landing queued: box job job-1793682173099-compiler-lib-land-metrics6.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_11 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 11u32, "2026-11-03T05:17Z", "compiler", "11-03 01:1x compiler: slice 6 LANDED (unstaged, closed green: 0 consumer signatures changed, apps/spiral/spiral.spi compiles, document_test 22 passed, type_probe 10 typed tests 0 errors). Lib metrics.spi now C244036D7A779DF7; land undo 20261103-011051. Left on the story: the Rust-vs-Gleam sweet spot and Zig-beats-C questions (pairwise per-sample comparison over two backends), then a run over a real examples results.tsv.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_12 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 12u32, "2026-11-03T05:39Z", "compiler", "11-03 01:3x compiler: FIRST REAL RUN. A Spiral main (metrics::results_totals + results_heaviest over a file, compiled to Rust with the installed pack22 against the box lib) read 8 merged t1101 results.tsv files from tonight (2106 lines: examples C, Cpp, Gleam, Lua, OpenQasm, Python A/B arms). Model: c 115.056 s, gleam 79.860 s, lua 171.164 s, heaviest = lua; a PowerShell sum over the same rows gives the same three numbers. GAPS it exposed: (1) Python (70.4 s), Cpp (15.0 s) and OpenQasm (79.1 s) rows are dropped because backend_name lacks them -> slice 7 (7-more-backends.kitdo, metrics C244036D7A779DF7 -> 0F83CC7888E5E9B5, node gate compiler-metrics7 running); (2) heaviest took ~15 s on 2106 rows in Rust (backend_total rescans every sample per comparison, plus array clones): a one-pass total per backend is the next slice; (3) totals print binary-float noise (115.05600000000017), fine for Prometheus but worth rounding to ms.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_13 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 13u32, "2026-11-03T05:42Z", "compiler", "11-03 01:4x compiler: slice 7 green on t1101 (lib_gate 11/11, 0 type errors): BackendPython, BackendCpp, BackendOpenQasm in backend_name, labels, backend_of and backends (), plus test results_totals_cover_python_cpp_and_openqasm. Landing queued: box job job-1793684486358-compiler-lib-land-metrics7 (base C244036D7A779DF7 -> 0F83CC7888E5E9B5).")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_14 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 14u32, "2026-11-03T05:51Z", "compiler", "11-03 01:5x compiler: slice 7 LANDED (closed green: 0 consumer signatures changed, document_test 22 passed, type_probe 11 typed tests; metrics.spi 0F83CC7888E5E9B5; undo 20261103-014141). SLICE 8 (8-rows-one-split.kitdo, 0F83CC78 -> D02D8C363CE25887): results_rows = sm'::lines text instead of arraym::init line_count (line_at i), which re-split the whole text per row (O(n^2)). Real run on t1101, same 2106 lines: all six backends now (c 115.056, gleam 79.860, lua 171.164, python 70.412, cpp 14.982, openqasm 79.052 s, each equal to the PowerShell sum), heaviest lua, model 78 ms end to end (was ~40 s, ~15 s of it in heaviest). Gate: 11/11 rust + gleam against the box lib; against the node mirror lib gleam fails because the mirror sm' predates portable lines (expected, not this change). Landing queued: job-1793685021077-compiler-lib-land-metrics8.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_15 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 15u32, "2026-11-03T05:58Z", "compiler", "11-03 01:5x compiler: slice 8 LANDED (closed green: 0 consumer signatures changed, document_test 22 passed, type_probe 11 typed tests; metrics.spi D02D8C363CE25887; undo 20261103-015036). Left: the Rust-vs-Gleam and Zig-vs-C pairwise questions (per-sample comparison over two backends) and rounding totals to ms.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_16 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 16u32, "2026-11-03T07:59Z", "compiler", "11-03 04:0x compiler: PAUSED (run not started). State: slice 9 (9-head-to-head.kitdo, metrics D02D8C363CE25887 -> 784FB4491FA12E50: value_for, beats, head_to_head a b -> wins, shared samples; results_head_to_head; test head_to_head_counts_shared_samples_only) is GREEN on t1101 against the box lib tree (12/12 rust + gleam, 0 type errors), NOT landed. Next step: queue its land (lib_land_queue, base D02D8C363CE25887), then run results_head_to_head Rust Gleam and Zig C over a fresh examples results.tsv for the two open questions; round totals to ms. Waits on: the run starting.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_17 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 17u32, "2026-11-03T09:34Z", "compiler", "11-03 05:3x compiler: slice 9 LANDED (closed green: document_test 22 passed, type_probe 12 typed tests; metrics.spi 784FB4491FA12E50; undo 20261103-052241). Next: run results_head_to_head Rust Gleam and Zig C over a fresh examples results.tsv once main has synced the node mirror.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_18 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 18u32, "2026-11-03T10:27Z", "compiler", "11-03 06:3x compiler: FIRST ANSWERS from the landed model (slice 9 head_to_head, Rust build of a lib-tree main) over real t1101 examples runs today on installed pack23/24 (Rust 515 rows, Gleam 441, a fresh C,Zig run 559/470): ZIG BEATS C on compile time for 330 of 468 shared samples (71%), medians 151 ms vs 194 ms. RUST VS GLEAM is close: Rust faster on 242 of 441 (55%), but Gleam's median is lower (222 ms vs 236 ms), so Rust's wins are narrow and its losses wide. Heaviest total: rust. Every model count equals a PowerShell per-sample comparison of the same rows. Lua vs Python needs a fresh Lua examples run (the picked run held 1 row). Open: native (run-time) head-to-head per pair, and rounding totals to ms.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_19 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 19u32, "2026-11-03T10:38Z", "compiler", "11-03 06:4x compiler: slice 10 LANDED (box lib gate 13/13 rust + gleam, 0 type errors, since node t1101 was unreachable by LAN name for a while; closed green: document_test 22 passed, type_probe 13 typed tests; metrics.spi 447B9EF6E21BCFC7; undo 20261103-063445): results_native_head_to_head a b text compares native_ms over ran rows only, test native_head_to_head_counts_ran_rows_only. Real native run on t1101 queued (job compiler-h2hn: examples C,Zig,Rust,Gleam -Native on pack24, then compile and native head-to-heads for Rust/Gleam, Zig/C, Rust/C with a PowerShell cross-check).")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_20 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 20u32, "2026-11-03T11:13Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:1x compiler: COMPILE + NATIVE-TIER HEAD-TO-HEADS on one t1101 run (examples C,Zig,Rust,Gleam -Native on installed pack24, 1985 rows; native ran C 521, Gleam 440, Rust 491, Zig 470); every model count equals the PowerShell cross-check. COMPILE (Spiral): Zig faster than C on 329/468 (medians 148 vs 191 ms), Rust faster than C on 353/514, Rust vs Gleam 216/441 (even). NATIVE_MS is the whole native tier per row (toolchain build + run), so it measures the toolchain more than the program: Rust beats Gleam on 420/440 (2.7 s vs 9.4 s median, mostly gleam build), C beats Zig (1.1 vs 1.6 s; Zig wins 69/468) and Rust (1.1 vs 2.7 s; Rust wins 46/490). Pure run time needs a run-only column from test.ps1 (native_ms splits build vs run); that is the next model input. Lua/Python still need a fresh Lua run.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_21 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 21u32, "2026-11-03T12:17Z", "compiler", "11-03 08:1x compiler: slice 11 (RunSeconds metric, run_sample over test.ps1's run_ms column = the last native process alone, i.e. the built binary's run for C/Rust/Zig, gleam run for Gleam; results_run_head_to_head; test run_head_to_head_reads_the_run_column) green on t1101 against the box lib tree 14/14, round-trip canonical; landing queued (job compiler-lib-land-metrics11, base 447B9EF6E21BCFC7 -> 31BA091E590AE009). RUN-TIME answers on the 06:36 -Native run (pack24), model = PowerShell exactly: Zig faster than C on 179/468 (medians 107 vs 82 ms), Rust faster than C on 168/490 (131 vs 81 ms), Rust faster than Zig on 199/453 (129 vs 108 ms), Rust vs Gleam 440/440 (gleam run includes its build). CAVEAT: examples are tiny, so run medians mostly measure process start (Rust and Zig start main on a 1 GB-stack thread); the hot-loop question needs the mega samples or tibia's transformer, not examples.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_22 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 22u32, "2026-11-03T12:50Z", "compiler", "11-03 08:5x compiler: slice 11 LANDED 08:19 (closed green: document_test 22 passed, type_probe 14; metrics.spi 31BA091E590AE009; undo 20261103-081503). Slice 12 (the nameof heal of suite_label/suite_of through reflection) green 14/14, landing queued.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_23 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 23u32, "2026-11-03T13:14Z", "compiler", "11-03 09:0x compiler: slice 12 LANDED (closed green: document_test 22 passed, type_probe 14; metrics.spi 301D4B755DC3415C; undo 20261103-085256): suite_label and suite_of now go through reflection::case_name / union_cases_find (SPIRAL-NAMEOF-HEAL's first rewrite).")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_24 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 24u32, "2026-11-04T00:28Z", "compiler", "11-03 20:3x compiler: QUESTION 4 (when Lua or Wasm is the best fast backend), first answer from run_ms of the last full -Native run that has both (single-flight-20261030-163636, examples, native ran). Medians: C 35 ms (500 rows), Zig 36 (464), Rust 38 (470), Lua 86 (437), Wasm 159 (434), TypeScript 300 (437), Python 300 (439), Gleam 1508 (434, gleam run). Head to head: Lua faster than Wasm on 336/431, than TypeScript on 422/434, than Python on 430/434; Wasm faster than TypeScript on 277/430; Lua beats C on 20/437, Wasm beats C on 6/433. So among the non-native targets Lua (lua5.1) is the fastest by start-to-exit, then Wasm (wasmtime), and neither competes with C/Zig/Rust. CAVEAT, as slice 11's: examples are tiny, so these medians mostly measure interpreter or runtime start (lua5.1 ~80 ms, wasmtime ~150 ms, node ~300 ms); the hot-loop answer needs the mega samples (runtime_bench) on europa. Computed with PowerShell over results.tsv; the Spiral model (results_run_head_to_head, slice 11) reads the same column.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_25 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 25u32, "2026-11-04T00:32Z", "compiler", "11-03 20:3x compiler: QUESTION 4 HOT-LOOP ANSWER (node, compiler core 24EEB518 = pack31, mega_brzozowski_derivatives/runtime_bench; 3 runs each, every run exit 0): Zig 71 ms median (87/71/70), Wasm 114 (300 first, then 112/114: wasmtime caches the compiled module), C 210 (197/215/210, gcc -O2 without the matrix CFlags), Lua 7563 (7330/7588/7563). So for hot loops WASM is the fast non-native backend: 66x faster than Lua and here even ahead of C, while Lua is 36x slower than C. The examples-only answer (n24: Lua 86 vs Wasm 159 ms medians) was start-up, not compute. My prediction (C/Zig 1-2 s, Wasm 2-4x C) was wrong on both. A plausible reason Wasm beats C is its never-freeing bump allocator against C's refcounting, but that is not measured. DoD for Q4 met for this sample; a second compute sample (tibia's transformer) would confirm.")
inl note_of_backend_metrics_model_26 () = Note ("BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL", 26u32, "2026-11-04T05:32Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:3x compiler: DONE. The typed model is landed in lib metrics.spi (phantom units ms/bytes/count, backend_name and suite unions, a GADT metric u indexed by unit; slices 1-11: totals, head_to_head, run_head_to_head over run_ms). All four questions of the title have a measured answer from it, each cross-checked against a plain per-sample comparison of the same rows. (1) Most time: Rust (heaviest total compile, n 11-03 06:27). (2) Rust vs Gleam: close; Rust is faster on 55% of 441 shared samples but Gleam's median is lower (222 vs 236 ms), so Rust's wins are narrow and its losses wide. (3) Zig vs C: Zig compiles faster on 71% of 468 shared samples (151 vs 194 ms medians); run-time pairs are via results_run_head_to_head (slice 11). (4) Fast non-native backend: Wasm, start-up apart. Hot loop (runtime_bench, node, pack31): Zig 71 ms, Wasm 114, C 210, Lua 7563. Examples-only medians (Lua 86 vs Wasm 159 ms) measure start-up, not compute. Follow-ups, not blocking: a second compute sample (tibia's transformer) to confirm Q4's ranking, and DOTNET-ORACLE-COST, now unblocked.")
inl note_of_dotnet_oracle_cost_1 () = Note ("DOTNET-ORACLE-COST", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): (user 11-01 00:2x). MEASURE, per pack and per day: the dotnet build time of SpiralCompiler(+Core) per candidate; the share of matrix wall spent in Fsharp rows; dotnet RAM peaks (GC pause dominates compiles); lib bodies that exist only for .NET APIs (Fsharp arms with no Rust/Gleam counterpart: count them by backend_switch census); upstream-core compatibility churn. Report as BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL rows (phase=Oracle). Feeds SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL's decision on when the oracle moves to the Rust build.")
inl note_of_dotnet_oracle_cost_2 () = Note ("DOTNET-ORACLE-COST", 2u32, "2026-11-04T05:44Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:4x compiler: FIRST MEASUREMENTS (CPU-light, from existing data).\n(1) dotnet build per candidate: node (moon) today 282 s, 340 s and 436 s (gadtx, linkwarn2, pack34; TRAIN18 shared the node), 283 s on 11-03 (monorule). So about 5-7 min of node CPU per reviewed program, plus the same again for every combined pack.\n(2) F# rows in the matrix: 404 of 7,439 rows (5.4%) are 8.0% of a fresh compile pass (11-01 18:22: 6.0 of 74.7 min); on the last full -Native run (10-30 16:36) F# is 3.1% of native wall (4.2 of 133.4 min). The heavy backends are Zig (18-37% of compile), Rust and Gleam native. Keeping the F# rows is cheap.\n(3) .NET-only lib bodies (census of all 253 lib backend_switch blocks; an arm counts if it is not a failwith stub): 150 have a real F# arm, and only 9 have a real F# arm with no real Rust or Gleam arm. Those are exceptions (base try_unit x2, try_finally, resultm from_try), async (do, return_await'), base yield_from, rust fsharp_global and trace init_trace_state. That is the semantic gap a Rust or Gleam oracle has to close.\n(4) RAM peaks are not in results.tsv. Per memory gc-pause-dominates-compiles, GC pauses dominate compile wall. A measured peak needs kit's collection (KIT-EXEC-METRICS).\n(5) Also seen: frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any take 37-40 s to compile on F# against the 20 s examples cap, so they flip to timeout under load (pack34 review: control 4 timeouts, candidate 0, identical residuals on a 180 s box rerun).\nReading: the oracle's own cost is the .NET compiler build (minutes per candidate) and its GC-bound compiles, not the F# rows. Next: the per-pack build time from install-pack logs, and the upstream-core churn count.")
inl note_of_dotnet_oracle_cost_3 () = Note ("DOTNET-ORACLE-COST", 3u32, "2026-11-04T11:53Z", "compiler", "11-04 07:5x compiler: PER-CANDIDATE BUILD TIME (the n-last 'next' item). install-pack.ps1 writes no log, so the source is the node review logs, which stamp every step: the gap from the last 'apply' to 'candidate core <hash>' is one .NET compiler build (core + host, Release, single-flight, private SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR). Today on the node with 3-4 concurrent jobs: bendsafe3 5.9 min, splitu7 6.9 min, splitu4 8.4 min (splitu6r's 10.2 includes its first oracle cut, so it is an upper bound). So every candidate pays about 6-8.5 min of build before any check runs; a typical day today had 9 candidate builds across node and box (pack36, shape x2, Bend x2, and the oracle/review jobs), i.e. roughly 60-75 machine-minutes of pure F# compile for the oracle. That and the GC-bound compiles (n-earlier) are the oracle's cost; the F# rows themselves are not. Next: the upstream-core churn count, then the item can be summarized.")
inl note_of_dotnet_oracle_cost_4 () = Note ("DOTNET-ORACLE-COST", 4u32, "2026-11-04T11:54Z", "compiler", "11-04 07:5x compiler: UPSTREAM-CORE CHURN (deps/The-Spiral-Language at f4e54a0e, 2026-11-01): commits touching 'VS Code Plugin/core' = 2 in 30 days, 2 in 90, 2 in 365, and both are our fork's own 'tests' commits (cc1b12ac 10-19, eea8e980 11-01). The last upstream (mrakgr) change to core was merged 2024-03-30 ('The old core lib should now work with exists as well'). So upstream core churn is effectively zero, and keeping the .NET compiler as oracle carries no upstream-sync cost. SUMMARY of the metric so far: (1) per-candidate .NET build 6-8.5 min on the node under load, ~60-75 machine-min/day today; (2) GC-bound compiles (frontier_format_any_union / frontier_join_format_any 37-40 s vs the 20 s cap, flipping to timeout under load); (3) F# rows themselves cheap; (4) upstream sync ~0. The dominant cost is (1), so the lever is fewer full rebuilds (incremental host/core builds, or reusing one candidate build across several reviews), not dropping the oracle.")
inl note_of_dotnet_oracle_cost_5 () = Note ("DOTNET-ORACLE-COST", 5u32, "2026-11-04T13:02Z", "compiler", "11-04 09:0x compiler: LAST METRIC + VERDICT. (5) .NET-only lib bodies (backend_switch census over lib/spiral *.spi/*.spir, bench/target/tests excluded, brace-matched blocks): 159 switches have an Fsharp arm; 24 lack a Rust arm, 16 lack a Gleam arm, and 9 have NEITHER (async.spi:354, :364; base.spi:162, :739, :768, :816; resultm.spi:29; rust.spi:21; one more), mostly Fsharp+Python pairs. So moving the oracle off .NET would orphan 9 lib bodies, and 24/16 more need a Rust/Gleam arm before those backends cover what F# does. VERDICT on the cost of keeping .NET as the oracle: (a) per-candidate compiler build 6-8.5 min on a loaded node, ~60-75 machine-min/day today, the dominant cost; (b) GC-bound compiles flip two frontier rows to timeout under load (37-40 s vs the 20 s cap); (c) F# matrix rows themselves are cheap; (d) upstream core churn ~0 (last upstream core change 2024-03-30); (e) 9 lib bodies are .NET-only. Recommendation for SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL: keep the oracle; cut (a) by building one candidate per review batch and reusing it across reviews (today's runs rebuilt overlapping candidates 9 times), and file the 9 .NET-only bodies as the porting list. Done as a metric.")
inl note_of_compiler_wipe_damage_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): (main 00:2x, sibling of TIBIA-WIPE-DAMAGE). KNOWN COSTS: compiler-lane memories came back only partly (profile-wipe-2026-10-28 lists which); the lab drafts were archived later rather than lost; the toolchain pins (SPIRAL_CC gcc 15.2, FPC 3.3.1) and the candidate bin cache had to be rebuilt; the transcripts before 10-28 are gone, so old decisions survive only as backlog notes. NEXT CONCRETE STEP: read WIPE-REDO-LEDGER's compiler rows and every compiler memory marked PARTIAL; for each one, file a heal task (re-derive it from the tree) or mark it covered by a backlog note. Make-up: any compiler item whose last note predates 10-28 and cites a lost file gets its next step rewritten from the tree.")
inl note_of_compiler_wipe_damage_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:42Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:42 compiler, round 3: STEP 1 DONE, read from WIPE-REDO-LEDGER's compiler rows (n4, n5, batch 3, 10-29 12:38 and 14:37) against today's tree and item states. COST of the 10-28 wipe to the compiler lane: (a) the lane transcript (dropped; facts live in ~210 notes); (b) TEMP drafts: P3 shard/IVar runners and results (results INCONCLUSIVE on an oversubscribed node; P3 was re-benched twice on 10-31 with an A/A arm and NOT ADOPTED, so that make-up is complete), OpenQasm tanh + Bend float drafts (redone 10-29), host --check diagnostics patch (redone, and it exposed a false green), GLEAM-COMPACT-OUTPUT compact.do (redone), op-census tooling (dropped: method in memory op-census-method, rerun when needed), CONTRACT-REJECTION negatives (deferred, item Done since); (c) 5 patched candidate cores and 3 pack folders (dropped: shipped in HEAD, reproducible from appliers); (d) lane docs for FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS (recoverable via git lanes/formatter/PLAN.md@0ec501fb). Also found during recovery: the node's installed compiler was 4 days stale (since fixed). HEAL left: none blocking. MAKE-UP left, as tasks: (1) apps/compiler/packs still holds apply-hopacsota-p3-shard.ps1 and apply-hopacsota-p3-ivar.ps1, applier drafts of a verdict that's in (NOT ADOPTED), so archive them with scratch-archive (packs lifecycle: rejected leaves the repo); (2) FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS's lane docs return via git show when that item unblocks. Estimated total loss: ~1 day of compiler-lane work, all redone or superseded by 10-31.")
inl note_of_compiler_wipe_damage_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:43Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:43 compiler: CORRECTION to n2's make-up (1). The 10-31 NOT ADOPTED verdict covered P3's deferred validation (p3defer, whose applier was archived 10-31 18:50), NOT the shard and IVar variants. apply-hopacsota-p3-shard.ps1 / -ivar.ps1 are still open drafts on HOPACSOTA-P3 whose wall-time results were lost to an oversubscribed node, so they stay in packs. Real make-up (1): ONE quiet-node A/B with an A/A arm for shard+IVar (build-candidate -Mode hopacsota with both appliers, measure-arms.ps1 -Trace), queued when HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH's design decision says whether caller-thread claims replace them. Nothing to archive.")
inl note_of_compiler_wipe_damage_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE", 4u32, "2026-11-03T01:00Z", "compiler", "11-02 21:0x compiler: CLOSING the retro. Costs are enumerated (n2), heals: none blocking, and the memory heals are done (COMPILER-MEMORY-REASONS closed 11-02 after the PARTIAL re-audit, e.g. backend-targets-2026-10-07 re-audit line). The only remaining make-up, one quiet-node A/B with an A/A arm for the P3 shard + IVar appliers, belongs to HOPACSOTA-P3 and waits on HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH's design decision, so it is tracked there, not here.")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_1 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:35Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:34 compiler, round-2 groom (user 00:2x: backend codegen is CPU-light text work, write it while runs batch): ROOT CAUSE (read from lib/spiral/env.spi:343-352 + file_system.spi:1467-1481): environment_snapshot_usable demands that every critical name CURRENTLY set is in the snapshot. The test sets LOCALAPPDATA=surrogate, then restores 'before'. On Windows 'before' has LOCALAPPDATA, so it passes. On Linux LOCALAPPDATA was unset in 'before' but is set now, so the guard calls the snapshot truncated, and the env is left with the surrogate. FIX OPTIONS: (a) the guard compares against a critical-name list captured in the snapshot itself, not the live env; or (b) a restore that only removes vars the caller added is allowed (names absent from the snapshot that aren't in the guard's original-env list). Prefer (a): it keeps the anti-truncation intent. Owner of the guard is DELETE-ROOT-GUARD (kit's last note): compiler files and can land it, but asks that lane first because lib is under churn. Windows windows-2025 job was cancelled by fail-fast, so it says nothing.")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_2 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-11-01T04:36Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:36 compiler: LAST 5 i574n/spiral runs (gh-pages workflow): 10-29 13:00 green, 10-30 12:55 green, 10-31 02:33 RED, 10-31 12:29 green, 11-01 02:53 RED. Both reds come from this one test (ubuntu build), so this is a standing Linux red, not a flake. SLOW STEPS: build job 'workflow.ps1' ~33-36 min per OS; cells-gate ci_gate.ps1 26 min plus workflow -Compiler 14 min; compiler -Suite all 16 min. Cells-gate at 41 min is the longest job: a target for BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL rows (cells as fast as F# projects).")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_3 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-11-01T04:41Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:41 compiler: main 00:5x: compiler lands it (kit is on SCRUM-XP-S1). FIX CHOSEN, smaller than (a): environment_snapshot_usable's Linux critical list becomes the Linux-native names [PATH; HOME]. The old Linux list was the Windows list plus HOME; those names are never set on a normal Linux box, so they guarded nothing there except, wrongly, the case where a caller ADDED one (this test's LOCALAPPDATA surrogate) and then restored the snapshot taken before. The Windows list is unchanged, so behaviour on the box and the node is identical. Base env.spi C66DDE65E676F77F. Gate: node mirror envguard (digest 15054FDE8EAA8359), lib_program_gate -Control on env.spi and file_system.spi (jobs job-1793508093582 and job-1793508098130). The Linux proof is the next CI run after the user's push (no hand WSL, per the rule).\nPROGRAM (kit do <lib> -):\nedit env.spi expect files 1 replacements 1\n  replace once <<<\n        |: [ \"PATH\"; \"SystemRoot\"; \"TEMP\"; \"TMP\"; \"USERPROFILE\"; \"LOCALAPPDATA\"; \"APPDATA\"; \"HOME\" ]\n  >>> with <<<\n        |: [ \"PATH\"; \"HOME\" ]\n  >>>")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_4 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-11-01T05:05Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 01:05 compiler: GATES (node mirror envguard, lib copies at process.spi BE258A03 = live). env.spi: 14/15, failed try_get_environment_variable_is_none_when_unset on TypeScript only. file_system.spi: 33/36, surrogate test PASSED; failed find_parent (fsharp), find_parent_2 (rust) and directory_delete_removes_a_scratch_tree (rust). find_parent needs a 'workspace' parent dir that gate copies don't have (a copy artifact). Slowest cell 277 s of 300 s (node loaded). VERDICT PENDING: none of the failing tests touches environment_snapshot_usable, so these look pre-existing; unmodified-copy controls are running to prove it (job-1793509330599 env TS test, job-1793509504725 file_system full). Land only if each control fails the same set.")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_5 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 5u32, "2026-11-01T05:21Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 01:21 compiler: CONTROLS (unmodified copies, same mirror): env TS test PASSED (1/1 run); file_system 34/36, failing only find_parent + find_parent_2 (the copy-artifact pair). So the positive's extra reds, try_get_environment_variable_is_none_when_unset (TypeScript) and directory_delete_removes_a_scratch_tree (Rust), did NOT fail in control. The edit only changes the non-Windows branch, which never runs on the Windows node, so a real regression from it isn't possible there. Load flake is the likely cause (positive's slowest cell was 277 s of 300 s). Not landing on a guess: a targeted rerun of exactly those two tests plus the surrogate test on the edited copy is running (job-1793510469595). LAND RULE: green on the rerun means land; red again means compare the raw causes before anything else.")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_6 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 6u32, "2026-11-01T05:33Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 01:33 compiler: RERUN GREEN on the edited copy (job-1793510469595): env.spi try_get_environment_variable_is_none_when_unset 1/1; file_system.spi directory_delete_removes_a_scratch_tree + directory_delete_refuses_a_protected_surrogate_root 2/2. So the positive's 2 extra reds were load flakes, and the edit is proven no-regression on Windows. LAND attempt 01:3x in a tree-write window was REFUSED by lib-guarded-do: 2 live lib readers, a Cells 'mix spiral.x_compat C:\\home\\git\\fiscal' (pids 1976/22384, running since 00:40, not compiler's). Not exempting another lane's reader: a background step waits for those pids to exit and lands then (same note/n3 program, base C66DDE65E676F77F, target 59D341CE372E87E3).")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_7 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 7u32, "2026-11-01T06:24Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:24 compiler: LANDED 02:24 (tree-write window, land-lib-program -Note CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD/3): lib/spiral/env.spi C66DDE65E676F77F -> 59D341CE372E87E3, one line (environment_snapshot_usable's non-Windows critical list = [PATH; HOME]), unstaged. Undo: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\undo\\CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD-n3. Readers: two refusals first (a Cells 'spiral.x_compat fiscal' check, then tibia's 'spiral.cell_budget --check'); waited them out, never exempted or stopped them. Exempted only my own pack12a heavy-build window, which builds the compiler from a private candidate source and never reads lib/spiral. Left: the Linux proof is the next CI run after the user's push (the ubuntu build should go green on file_system); Done when that run is green.")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_8 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 8u32, "2026-11-03T07:34Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). Blocked on the user's push: the only step left is the next CI run after it, then Done.")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_9 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 9u32, "2026-11-06T08:25Z", "compiler", "11-06 04:2x compiler GROOM, from main star #5: before the user pushes, the compiler lib fixes that only CI on Linux can confirm (reactors timespec_get, the process deadline test via python -c) can be rehearsed on a codespace (clone HEAD plus an overlay of the unstaged files). Until then, they stay PENDING on Linux.")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_10 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 10u32, "2026-11-06T10:17Z", "compiler", "11-06 06:1x compiler GROOM: DONE. The fix is in pushed spiral: 98e51d32:lib/spiral/env.spi has the non-Windows critical list [ \"PATH\"; \"HOME\" ]. The scheduled CI run 41848519924 (11-04 14:32, ubuntu-24.04, the compiler and cells-gate jobs run only on schedule/dispatch) has cells-gate=success, and its artifact tests.tsv shows \"file_system directory_delete_refuses_a_protected_surrogate_root rust passed\" plus directory_delete_removes_a_scratch_tree passed. cells-gate.tsv: file_system.spi 23 executed, 0 failed; env.spi 16 executed, 0 failed. The DELETE-ROOT-GUARD dep is a later hardening, not this red.")
inl note_of_ci_linux_env_restore_guard_11 () = Note ("CI-LINUX-ENV-RESTORE-GUARD", 11u32, "2026-11-06T10:18Z", "compiler", "11-06 06:1x compiler, precision on the dep: DELETE-ROOT-GUARD (team:resilience, Planned 85) is the wider every-recursive-delete rule. This item was only the Linux red in its lib test, which CI now passes. The dep edge was reversed (fiscal 06:12 #6: the parent depends on the child), so it does not hold this item open.")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_1 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:36Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ROUND-2 WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER (resilience). Round 1 was WIPE-REDO-LEDGER (Done 10-29, recovered/recoverable/redo). This is the residual damage: what is still thinner than before the 10-28 wipe, across all lanes, with an owner per row.\n\nLANES FILED: TIBIA-WIPE-DAMAGE (Planned, note 1 full), X-WIPE-DAMAGE (Active 50%, note 1 full). STILL OWED a <LANE>-WIPE-DAMAGE item: main, kit, fiscal, compiler. Requesting each file one (or say \"no residual\") so this ledger is complete.\n\nCROSS-LANE LOSSES ONLY RESILIENCE SEES:\n(A) Memory \"lost reasons\" is FLEET-WIDE, not just tibia/x. ~60 memories were rebuilt 10-29/30 from backlog notes; the rules survived but many Why lines are reconstructions, not the user's original wording. Only tibia filed a re-derive task (TIBIA-MEMORY-REASONS). HEAL: each lane re-derives the Why of its partially-rebuilt memories from pre-wipe backlog quotes. OWNER: each lane for its own memories; resilience audits which memories carry a reconstructed vs original Why and hands each lane its slice (filing MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT under resilience).\n(B) MEASURED practice erosion, the CPU-light-while-blocked habit. Resilience cadence forensics (PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY, scratchpad heal\\cadence.py) comparing backlog notes 10-25..27 vs 10-29..31: raw cadence held (notes/day 718->739) but the SHARE spent waiting rose - wait-notes/day +29% (194->250), wait/note 0.27->0.34, prog:wait 2.25->1.85. The cluster (backlog-always-has-cpu-light, parallel-while-waiting, no-sleep-polling, no-quiet-machine-wait, tokens-abundant-cpu-scarce) is well-written; the gap is APPLICATION. CURE: backlog light-item DEPTH - keep >=3 ready load=light items with a concrete next step on top of each team's queue so `agile next --load light` always returns real work. OWNER: main (backlog depth) with each lane feeding its queue; resilience re-measures cadence weekly. Memories already strengthened to HARD DEFAULT (done).\n(C) CONFIRMED true data losses (no copy on box or node): DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS prototype (tibia's, now the redo, owner tibia) and the 10-21 eoie/memory/claude.spi memory snapshot (resilience node + pagefile search confirmed gone 10-31; ACCEPT, owner none; prevention = profile-mirror now carries the memory dir, PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE Done). No other irreplaceable artifact found so far.\n(D) Shared slip pattern on 10-31, visible only across lanes: proofs that skip a gate or measure nothing - x's parity proof skipped x-compat and turned CI red (run 41267182382); tibia proved a gate on an untracked input; x's probe used a relative [IO.File] path. Guards already in memory (proof-must-run-consumer-path, check-scripts-by-data-flow, gate-met-is-not-goal-met, ps-dotnet-relative-paths). OWNER: all lanes; no new task, the guards are the fix.\n\nPENDING ROWS (this turn): lab-code forensics (every lab/ path in git history + archives, whether it survives in its proper place) and the C:\\home non-git census (per-folder owner lane) land as notes 2 and 3; any lost lab code or unbacked irreplaceable folder becomes a row here with an owner.")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_2 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 2u32, "2026-11-01T04:45Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 LAB-CODE FORENSICS (resilience, WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER ask 2). Swept lab/ paths across all 44 project repos + 12 heavy upstream forks (git history --diff-filter=A + name-only, none skipped), plus %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive, C:\\home\\data, and the node C:\\home\\remote-archive. TSV: scratchpad heal\\lab-forensics.tsv (55 rows).\nFINDING: 55 distinct lab folders; ALL 55 exist today in their proper non-lab home; 0 genuinely lost. The 10-28 wipe cost NO lab code. 4 were committed-then-deleted (eoie/kit EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE->kit src/kit_adoption, EOIE-MACHINE-WATCH->src/kit_machine_watch; tibia potion-fidelity->apps/arena, cells-probes->apps/arena/measure.spi). The other 51 were never committed - they lived only in the spiral node working tree (HEAD d095f13d) and were captured in the 10-29 node-retire rescue extract (C:\\home\\data\\node-checkout-only-2026-10-29-extract\\lab); their landed forms are live in lib/spiral/process.spi + erlang_process.spi and across spiral apps/. No lab/ dir exists in any current working tree - the lab-in-repo discipline held.\nARCHIVES: kit\\archive has 40+ snapshots, none containing lab/. Node remote-archive = repo checkouts (no lab/).\nTWO ROWS NEED GROOMING (code safe, but no task id): lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL (process_healed.spi) and lab/LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT (process_split.spi, erlang_port.spi, shared_command.spi) were folded into LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE / the consolidated lib/spiral/process.spi. OWNER: main/lib - either re-register the two names or note them explicitly subsumed, so the backlog shows where that code went.\nCAVEAT: the 48 non-LIB-PROCESS archive folders carry live task ids and are confirmed present in the rescue extract, but were not file-diffed one-by-one against their landing spot; spot-checks (LEAN-BACKEND, KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM, ZIG-COVERAGE, UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES, MEGA-SAMPLES) all resolved to live files. Confidence: high that nothing is lost, medium that every archived variant exactly equals its landed form.")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_3 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 3u32, "2026-11-01T04:59Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 C:\\home NON-GIT CENSUS (resilience, WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER ask 3). 13 top-level dirs beside git. Sizes via robocopy /l tally; data drained to 3 subdirs. TSV basis in scratchpad heal\\ (home-census).\nPER-OWNER ROLLUP (folder | size | newest | holds | reproducible):\nCOMPILER (clean or archive its build outputs, ~372 MB, all 10-27/28, all reproducible): ccmr-alt 14MB, ccmr-box 145MB (apps+bincache+binRa/Rb+logs), ccmr-land 14MB, ccmr-land-bin 46MB, g3a-bin-alt 46MB, g3a-bin-box 46MB (bin/obj/results, gen3 arith), p2-cond3 61MB (cache+p2-core.fs+run logs, pack2).\nMAIN/SPIRAL: spiral-cache 171MB (10-29, build cache, reproducible); data\\main-drafts 0MB (drained 11-01).\nKIT/NODE: remote-queue 0.1MB (10-25, drained).\nRESILIENCE (mine): recovery 20.3 GB (10-30, wipe-20261028 staging - the biggest REAL consumer on the box); data\\rescue-2026-10-28 543MB (10-30, the wipe rescue dump); data\\node-checkout-only-2026-10-29-extract 5MB (holds the ONLY copy of uncommitted spiral lab code, per ask-2 forensics).\nSTALE/UNOWNED: alias 0MB (2023); tmp ~164 GB (2023, NOT a junction - a real split 7z set tmp.7z.001.. ; the single biggest reclaimable blob on the box).\nRESILIENCE FLAGS (actionable):\n(1) IRREPLACEABLE + possibly UNBACKED: data\\rescue-2026-10-28 (543MB) and data\\node-checkout-only extract (5MB, sole copy of uncommitted spiral lab code) are recovery SOURCES. The profile mirror covers the user profile, NOT C:\\home\\data - so if these are box-only they are a single point of failure. ACTION (resilience): confirm each is on the node; if not, archive to the node remote-archive. Filed as DATA-RECOVERY-SOURCES-BACKUP.\n(2) SPACE: tmp ~164 GB + recovery 20.3 GB = ~184 GB reclaimable. tmp is a 2023 split archive (verify contents, then archive-off-box or discard - user/machine-upkeep decision, archive-never-delete so NOT deleted now). recovery\\wipe-20261028 likely overlaps rescue-2026-10-28 + the node mirror; resilience to diff and trim the redundant part.\n(3) COMPILER build outputs (~372MB) and spiral-cache (171MB) are reproducible - owner lanes can clean freely; low risk, listed so they are not mistaken for data.\nNET: C:\\home non-git is ~185 GB, but ~184 GB of that is two stale/redundant blobs (tmp, recovery); the irreplaceable part is only ~550 MB (the two data recovery sources), which is the part that actually needs protecting.")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_4 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 4u32, "2026-11-01T05:03Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ROLL-UP of the four remaining lanes (main relayed they filed; all 6 lanes now have a <LANE>-WIPE-DAMAGE item). Supersedes note-1's \"4 lanes still owe\".\nMAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE (2 notes): AUTHORITATIVE memory count - 185 memory files, only 2 verbatim; 72+ rebuilt as index-line+practice (\"original wording lost\"); 13 INFERRED with confidence lines. This is how the spiral-first, lab, and codespaces-quota rules slipped. Main's wait share peaked 0.42-0.43/note on 10-28/29 (corroborates my fleet cadence). NEW CONCRETE CONFIG LOSS: the gh CLI token re-created post-wipe lost its codespace+user scopes, silently blocking CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS; heal is a USER step (gh auth refresh -s codespace,user). Make-up: MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP (every round), WIP cap, HEAVY-HOLD-DEADLOCK.\nKIT-WIPE-DAMAGE: NEW cross-lane theme = EVIDENCE loss. %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit was wiped: every pre-10-28 job receipt/log is gone, so Done items that cite a job id have no surviving evidence; records also drifted from the tree (EOIE-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS said 0% though its package landed 10-25). kit owns the fix: (a) evidence census of pre-10-28 Done items, (b) record-vs-tree census, (c) memory fidelity re-derive, (d) ASK ON RESILIENCE: fold kit's job+archive store into the hourly profile mirror + rehearse one restore.\nFISCAL-WIPE-DAMAGE (90%): 7 TEMP-only artefacts lost, all recovered/rebuilt/landed except the edition-3 respell (re-derived 11-01, node proof queued = the only open make-up, FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND). ~7 h rework + ~1 day calendar drift. Clean retro; nothing outstanding for the ledger.\nCOMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE: memories partial, lab drafts archived (not lost - confirms my ask-2), toolchain pins (SPIRAL_CC gcc 15.2, FPC 3.3.1) + candidate bin cache rebuilt. Make-up: re-derive each PARTIAL compiler memory from the tree, rewrite the next step of any item citing a lost file.\nRECONCILIATION with my cross-lane rows: (A) memory \"lost reasons\" is UNIVERSAL and now quantified (185/2/72/13) - MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT owns the cross-lane sweep, each lane checks its own 10 (main's split); scope updated on that task. (B) BACKLOG-LIGHT-DEPTH taken by main, pairs with MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP. (C) lab code = 0 lost, compiler agrees. (D) proof/gate slips guarded. NEW rows this pass: EVIDENCE loss (kit owns its censuses); the gh-token scope loss (USER action, surfacing now); kit's job/archive-store mirror coverage (resilience + kit - same backup-boundary gap the census exposed, folding into the mirror-coverage review).\nLEDGER STATUS: all 6 lanes rolled up; every residual loss has an owner. Remaining resilience execution: rescue-2026-10-28 archive to node (in flight), mirror-coverage review (kit store + C:\\home\\data recovery sources).")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_5 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 5u32, "2026-11-01T06:08Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:1x MIRROR-COVERAGE REVIEW (resilience, round-3; kit ask d + the census backup-boundary). Read against the live script i574n.github\\scripts\\profile-mirror.ps1 wholeTrees, not from notes.\nCURRENT COVERAGE (verified): the hourly mirror DOES cover kit\\jobs (4.2MB), kit\\backups (120MB), kit\\windows, kit\\config-baseline, plus .claude\\projects (memory+transcripts), hooks, autonomous, fiscal-undo, tibia-mariadb-dumps, and curated single files. So kit's JOB STORE is already protected going forward - the pre-10-28 receipt loss predates the mirror's existence, it is not an open gap. Correction to my note-3/memory: the mirror is NOT blind to kit\\jobs.\nREAL GAPS: (1) kit\\archive = 1.5 GB, NOT mirrored. (2) C:\\home\\data recovery sources, NOT tree-mirrored (only rescue-2026-10-28\\loss-map.md as a single file).\nDESIGN RULE (the finding): the hot mirror runs ~every 50 min and keeps 48 copies, re-tarring its whole set each run. It must hold only SMALL, CHURNING, irreplaceable state. Putting kit\\archive (1.5GB) in it would push ~75 GB onto the node and slow every run; a 543MB recovery dump in it would bloat each archive ~5x. So LARGE append-only stores and ONE-TIME recovery artifacts need a SEPARATE off-box copy: an append-only store (kit\\archive) gets a daily incremental (robocopy /mir of just that dir), a one-time artifact gets a one-shot archive (rescue-2026-10-28 done today). kit\\backups (120MB) is the hot mirror's biggest item and a candidate to move to the same daily lane to keep each run lean.\nACTION: filed KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR (resilience) - add a daily robocopy /mir of kit\\archive to the node as a scheduled task (Priority 5), separate from the hot mirror. Closes kit ask d's archive half; jobs half already done.")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_6 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 6u32, "2026-11-01T06:09Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:1x CLOSING SYNTHESIS (resilience) - ledger rollup complete, every residual 10-28 wipe loss has an owner and a status. The ledger's job (roll up all lanes + cross-lane losses, each -> an owned heal/make-up task) is done; execution lives on the child tasks, not here.\nLOSS -> OWNER -> STATUS:\n1. Memory fidelity (185 files, 2 verbatim, 72+ wording-lost, 13 INFERRED) -> MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT (resilience audit + per-lane slices: main 7, tibia 3, kit 2, compiler 1). IN PROGRESS: 2 of 3 LOW-confidence re-derived (decision-rounds CORRECTED - was backwards; fork-identity VERIFIED+upgraded), kit's 2 handed to kit.\n2. CPU-light-while-blocked erosion (measured: wait/note 0.27->0.34) -> BACKLOG-LIGHT-DEPTH + MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP (main). OWNED.\n3. True data losses: DOTA-LUA-BINDINGS prototype -> tibia redo; 10-21 claude.spi snapshot -> ACCEPT (no copy; mirror now carries memory). CLOSED.\n4. Lab code -> forensics: 0 lost, all 55 folders live in place. CLOSED.\n5. Proof/gate slips (10-31) -> guards already in memory. CLOSED.\n6. Evidence loss (kit pre-10-28 job receipts) -> kit evidence+record censuses; kit\\jobs mirrored forward. OWNED (kit).\n7. gh token lost codespace/user scopes -> USER action (gh auth refresh -s codespace,user). SURFACED.\n8. Backup boundary: rescue-2026-10-28 (543MB) was box-only -> archived to node, byte-verified (DATA-RECOVERY-SOURCES-BACKUP DONE); kit\\archive 1.5GB gap -> KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR (resilience). CLOSED + OWNED.\n9. Space: tmp ~164GB (2023 7z) + recovery 20.3GB -> user/machine-upkeep keep-or-drop decision (archive-never-delete; untouched). SURFACED.\n10. Lane damage items: all 6 filed (main/kit/fiscal/compiler/tibia/x); fiscal 90% (FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND open), others have make-up tasks. ROLLED UP.\nMETRIC this ledger added: memory-fidelity tiers (T0 verbatim 2 / T3 INFERRED 13 / T2 re-derived-with-quote ~70 / T1 rest) - risk concentrates in T3. NEXT: lanes work their MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT slices; resilience builds KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR.")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_7 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 7u32, "2026-11-01T14:36Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ~10:4x resilience (git-51), ROUND-5 CLOSING VERDICT (user ask: \"a last assurance we're set\" — every wipe loss has an item that says how to heal/move-forward, even if not yet executed). Read each lane's <LANE>-WIPE-DAMAGE item + state.\n\nPER-LANE VERDICT (set = heal path captured as task/notes):\n- tibia:     SET. TIBIA-WIPE-DAMAGE Done 100% (retro complete; DOTA-LUA redo owned).\n- x:         SET. X-WIPE-DAMAGE Done 100% (credentials/memories/drafts retro closed).\n- resilience: SET. WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER Done; DATA-RECOVERY-SOURCES-BACKUP Done; all cross-lane rows (A memory-reasons, B cadence depth, C data losses, D proof slips) owned.\n- fiscal:    SET. FISCAL-WIPE-DAMAGE Active 90%; sole open make-up = FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND (node proof queued). Retro clean.\n- compiler:  SET (plan captured, exec 80%). COMPILER-WIPE-DAMAGE Active; heal = re-derive each PARTIAL compiler memory from the tree + rewrite the next step of any item citing a lost file. Toolchain pins + bin cache already rebuilt.\n- kit:       SET except EXECUTION. KIT-WIPE-DAMAGE Planned 0% but notes 1-2 fully specify 4 make-up tasks (a evidence census, b record-vs-tree census, c memory-fidelity re-derive, d store mirror — d CLOSED by resilience) with a typed DoD. Plan complete; 0% executed.\n- main:      SET except EXECUTION. MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE Planned 0% but notes 1-2 full (185 memories / 2 verbatim / 13 INFERRED; make-up = MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP, WIP cap, HEAVY-HOLD-DEADLOCK). Plan complete; 0% executed.\n\nLOSSES WITH NO ITEM (the \"anything with no item\" ask):\n1) Two subsumed lab names lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL + lab/LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT: CODE SAFE (live in lib/spiral/process.spi, confirmed note 2) but NO backlog row shows where it went. OWNER main/lib: re-register the two ids as subsumed or add a pointer note. The ONLY true backlog gap.\n2) gh CLI token scopes (codespace,user) lost post-wipe: a USER step (gh auth refresh -s codespace,user), documented MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE note 2 — not a lane gap.\n3) 10-21 eoie/memory/claude.spi snapshot: truly gone, ACCEPTED unrecoverable (node+pagefile search confirmed); prevention in place (mirror now carries the memory dir). No heal possible — an accepted loss, not an open gap.\n\nBOTTOM LINE: all 6 lanes are SET — every wipe loss is captured as a task with a known heal path. Two caveats worth the user's eye: (i) kit's and main's retros are fully SPECIFIED but 0% EXECUTED (the tasks exist, the work hasn't started); (ii) one real backlog gap — the two subsumed lab-name pointers (main/lib). Everything else is Done, Active-with-plan, user-action, or accepted-unrecoverable. The wipe can stop being an open worry: the move-forward path is fully enumerated.")
inl note_of_wipe_damage_ledger_8 () = Note ("WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER", 8u32, "2026-11-01T14:45Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ~10:4x resilience (git-51), CORRECTION to note 7's \"only true gap\": it is ALREADY CLOSED. main's LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE note 1 (02:2x) records lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL and lab/LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT as subsumed into LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE and lib/spiral/process.spi. So there is NO open backlog gap from the lab-code forensics -- every lab folder has a home AND a backlog row showing where it went. REVISED BOTTOM LINE: all 6 lanes SET, zero no-item losses; the only caveats are kit+main retros specified-but-0%-executed, the gh-token user step, and the accepted-unrecoverable 10-21 snapshot. The wipe ledger is fully reconciled.")
inl note_of_backend_batch_1 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:35Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 00:35 compiler, round-2 groom: (user 00:2x: 'going days waiting for jobs instead of finishing low cpu work'). RULE: drafts are written CPU-light. When 2+ drafts are ready (or the node is idle), apply them all to one candidate (build-candidate.ps1), run predict-residuals.ps1 once over the union of their backends on the node, then split the verdict per applier. Each verdict lands as BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL rows. This is the compiler's NODE-RUNNABLE item.")
inl note_of_backend_batch_2 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:12Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:12 compiler: STARTED (round 3). Candidate pack12a = live core C0990B2D590CE965 + apply-rust-mutual-tail-loops.ps1 (3 anchors, re-dry-run OK on the stacked copy) + kit do programs no-respell (KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD n2) and the Gleam portable math .fs part (GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS n33). The --x-compat retirement is NOT in it (it waits on x/tibia). Box job job-1793513508799-compiler-pack12a-build, --heavy --min-free-mb 3000 (box at 1.5 GB free, so it queues until memory frees). build-candidate.ps1 gained -Programs (kit do programs applied to the candidate's source copy after the appliers): Spiral-first, so new drafts don't need .ps1 appliers. NEXT on the node, once it's built: predict-residuals vs the installed PACK-11 run. Pre-registered reading: P = Rust rows with a mutual tail group + Gleam rows that use tanh/sin/cos (import changes only; Erlang stdout identical) + the new mutual_tail_rc_live; formatter --spelling unchanged except on '///- --no-respell' files.")
inl note_of_backend_batch_3 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:30Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:30 compiler: pack12a BUILT (job-1793513508799, queued until memory freed, then 340 s build): candidate core 97E844B52FDA7E82 source -> SpiralCompilerCore.dll 1326B40E2ABB8F1D, SpiralCompiler.dll DFC86B91968D447D (host unchanged = PACK-11's). Warnings: the known FS0046/FS0026/FS0760 classes only. PREDICT queued (job-1793514612752, --heavy): predict-residuals -Candidate pack12a -InstalledRun single-flight-20261031-154548 (PACK-11's run), reading pre-registered in n2.")
inl note_of_backend_batch_4 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 4u32, "2026-11-01T06:51Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:51 compiler: PREDICT DONE (job-1793514664798, 781 s compile-only; run single-flight-20261101-023127 vs PACK-11's): P = 4, matching n2's pre-registered reading: the 3 mutual-tail Rust rows (native_managed_scc_tail_recursion, frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending, native_recursive_tail_recursion) + native_float_math_family|Gleam (the maths import). Compile-status diffs 0. 1 timeout row (conversation_negative_orphan_result|Fsharp, 20 s cap under load; rerun alone before reading). +4 new rows = the new sample, which FAILED: my mutual_tail_rc_live used '=' and '-', which the samples' core doesn't define (only '=.' and '-.'; n9's text had been proven in a scratch package with another core). FIXED at 02:52 (4 operators), then re-proven: installed C ok and Rust ok; pack12a Rust emits 1 'fn group_0'; gcc -O2 (C) exit 0, rustc -O (candidate) exit 0. The rows go green in the next run. NEXT: native half for the 4 P rows (rustc vs C; Gleam Erlang stdout byte-equal), then hand main pack12a + evidence (PACK-12).")
inl note_of_backend_batch_5 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 5u32, "2026-11-01T06:52Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:52 compiler: NATIVE HALF, Rust P rows: all 3 (native_managed_scc_tail_recursion, frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending, native_recursive_tail_recursion) hold pack12a's output (1 'fn group_N' each), rustc -O vs gcc 15.2 -O2: exit 0/0 and stdout identical 3/3. Left before handing main the candidate: (a) the Gleam row native_float_math_family, Erlang stdout byte-equal installed vs candidate (needs gleam_community_maths fetched into the native shim project; node or box); (b) rerun conversation_negative_orphan_result|Fsharp alone (20 s timeout under load); (c) the mutual_tail_rc_live rows in a run (fixed after this pass). Then PACK-12 hand-off: pack12a = core 1326B40E2ABB8F1D + host DFC86B91968D447D.")
inl note_of_backend_batch_6 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 6u32, "2026-11-01T06:53Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:53 compiler: (b) DONE. conversation_negative_orphan_result|Fsharp rerun ALONE with pack12a: 13.9 s, FatalError ETY type error, the same verdict as the installed run (18.1 s there). The predict's timeout was load against the 20 s cap, not a candidate change, but this negative sample sits close to the cap on both compilers: worth a Timeouts entry in matrix.psd1 (matrix owner).")
inl note_of_backend_batch_7 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 7u32, "2026-11-01T06:54Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:54 compiler: (a) DONE. native_float_math_family|Gleam on the Erlang target, as a gleam project = native shim deps + gleam_community_maths 2.0.2 (fetched from hex): installed output (math:* externals) exit 0, pack12a output (import gleam_community/maths) exit 0, stdout identical (empty: the sample reports through its exit code, which C also gives 0). So every P row agrees natively. Left for the PACK-12 hand-off: the mutual_tail_rc_live rows in a full run, plus adding gleam_community_maths to the native shim/Cells deps in the same land (the targets.ex/gleam.toml parts of GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS n33).")
inl note_of_backend_batch_8 () = Note ("BACKEND-BATCH", 8u32, "2026-11-01T07:24Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:23 compiler: DONE as a first batch: pack12a then pack12b, one build each, one predict each, native halves, installed as PACK-12. The batching rule stands for the next drafts (PACK-13).")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_1 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:36Z", "kit", "11-01 00:3x kit: design in PS1-TO-SPIRAL (kit note, 11-01 00:3x). NEXT CONCRETE STEP (CPU-light): typed model for the run cache key (file hash + package closure hashes + compiler hash) and the test selection (all | named | list) with tests; then the verb over the installed compiler in process mode; first consumer: notebook-batch.ps1 -> kit run --test.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_2 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:33Z", "kit", "11-01 02:3x kit (round 3): typed model DONE in the tree (unstaged): new granular HostFree+LibReferenced package src/kit_run_model. run_key = { file_hash; closure_hashes; compiler_hash }; cache_name = compiler-file-closure_digest, where the closure digest is a sum of per-hash 32-bit FNV-1a values (order-independent without sorting, overflow-free as in lib xsd_emit's fnv); test_selection = AllTests | NamedTests | ListTests; run_request = RunProgram (file, args) | RunTests (file, selection) | RunRefused reason, parsed from 'kit run <file.spi> [--test a,b | --test | --list] [-- args]' (only .spi runs). Tests the_cache_name_ignores_closure_order_and_follows_the_compiler, arguments_become_a_typed_run_request. PROOF: node job-1793514472507-kit-node-r3e, kit_run_model/model.spi 2/2 (two earlier tries failed: string '<=' has no comparison constraint; listm.toArray/am do not exist in this lib). NEXT: the verb over the installed compiler in process mode with the run cache; first consumer notebook-batch -> kit run --test.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_3 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: 'kit run hello.spi -- a b' prints its args and exits with its main's code; a second identical run is an exe start (< 1 s, cache hit printed); 'kit run notebook.spi --test t1' runs only t1 and exits 1 on failure; 'kit run --list' prints the test names; scripts/notebook-batch.ps1 replaced by kit run calls; CLI tests, node green. NEXT STEP: the verb in a new crate kit_run over the installed compiler in process mode, cache dir %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\run-cache\\<cache_name>. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: run_request and test_selection are closed unions (RunProgram | RunTests | RunRefused; AllTests | NamedTests | ListTests); still weak: run_key holds three raw string hashes that could be swapped; fix with phantom-tagged hash (hash file_kind, hash closure_kind, hash compiler_kind) so cache_name cannot mix them, and the digest as a nominal.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_4 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 4u32, "2026-11-01T14:39Z", "kit", "11-01 10:3x kit (round 5, user Q: did we advance on replacing pwsh with Spiral without losing ergonomics?). HONEST STATE: kit run file.spi runs ZERO real scripts today; there is no verb. Done: the typed model only (src/kit_run_model, node 2/2): run_key {file_hash; closure_hashes; compiler_hash}, order-independent FNV closure digest, test_selection AllTests|Named|Listed. Not done, in order: (1) the verb crate kit_run_cli: parse kit run <file> [--test n] [-- args]; (2) build through the installed compiler in process mode to a cached exe under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\run-cache\\<cache_name>.exe; (3) exec with args, propagate the exit code; (4) --list/--test over the notebook test cells. ERGONOMICS GAPS vs pwsh, measured by what our 10 most-used scripts need: argv (have via lib), env read/write (lib env, partial), spawn with pipes and exit codes (workspace primitives, partial: WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB 10%), globbing and path joins (lib fs, partial), here-strings/format (sm format!, have), JSON (lib json, have), ssh/scp (none; stays pwsh glue). Cold-start budget: first run = one compile (~20-60 s on the box), later runs exe start < 1 s, which beats pwsh startup (~400 ms with profile) only once cached. FIRST REAL CONSUMER stays scripts/notebook-batch.ps1 -> kit run --test. Progress stays 30% (model only); it moves when hello.spi runs end to end on the node.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_5 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 5u32, "2026-11-01T19:38Z", "kit", "11-01 15:38 kit: the replacement of scripts/notebook-batch.ps1 by kit run --test should emit the batch-mode command line (run_notebook.ps1 --path ... --output-dir) or its own progress rows, so kit machine watch shows its progress. Today notebook-batch.ps1's per-module --output-path runs are invisible to the watch (EOIE-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS acceptance, 11-01). Also made portable today: notebook-batch.ps1 joins paths with multi-segment Join-Path, and user-env.ps1 returns early on non-Windows, both found on the Linux worker.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_6 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 6u32, "2026-11-03T07:32Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 36 h ago by kit, progress kept at 30%. Resume from that note: uns are invisible to the watch (EOIE-NOTEBOOK-PROGRESS acceptance, 11-01). Also made portable today: notebook-batch.ps1 joins paths with multi-segment Join-Path, and user-env.ps1 returns early on non-Windows, both found on the Linux worker.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_7 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 7u32, "2026-11-04T14:05Z", "kit", "11-04 11:2x kit: `kit run <file.spi> [-- args]` BUILT (unstaged, routed as top-level VerbRun). PIPELINE PROVEN BY HAND on the box first: a script compiled with `dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --backend Rust x.spi x.rs` (72 s under load), then `rustc -O --edition 2021 x.rs -o x.exe` (7 s), ran with 3 args, exit 0. KEY FINDING: with no SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR the compiler resolves `spiral-` and `|core-` as SIBLINGS of the package folder, so staging <cache>/pkgs/<stem>/ with `spiral` and `core` junctions beside it is all the setup needed. NEW crate kit_run_cli (run.spi): setup from SPIRAL_LIB / SPIRAL_CORE / SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL (typed SetupMissing names the missing one; kit names no machine path), cache = %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/run-cache/<cache_name of kit_run_model.run_key: file fnv, lib+core, compiler fnv>, a second run reuses the exe, compile/rustc failures name their step and last line, the exit code passes through. Uses the model's request_of (RunProgram / RunTests / RunRefused). Tests: stems, paths with and without .exe, manifest, missing setup. europa pack34 --check exit 0 (run with bodies, direct_verbs, cli model, cli main). LIMITS: dependency-free scripts only (plain rustc; scripts using crates need the cargo path later); output is captured and printed after exit (no streaming/stdin; a stdio-inheriting primitive is a boundary-package follow-up); --test answers \"not wired yet\".")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_8 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 8u32, "2026-11-04T14:28Z", "kit", "11-04 11:5x kit: two more pieces, PROVEN in box notebooks (unstaged). (1) Output now STREAMS: NEW boundary package kit_os_run (run_os.spi) with one-expression primitives status_of (std Command with inherited stdio over the array's borrowed Rc<str> args) and exit_code_of (code, 127 when it could not start); kit run executes the cached exe through it, so stdout/stderr/stdin are live and the exit code is the program's own. Test runs `rustc --version` through it: pass. (2) `kit run <file> --list` prints the file's test names (from its `:# name` lines, static preludes skipped) plus `kit run list ok tests=N`, no build needed. run.spi 1/1 pass (2 rounds). Still open: --test (wire through Cells), and crate dependencies (plain rustc today).")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_9 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 9u32, "2026-11-04T15:01Z", "kit", "11-04 12:5x kit: `kit run <file> --test [a,b]` WIRED (unstaged): it runs the file's tests through the Cells runner named by SPIRAL_CELLS_RUNNER (run_notebook.ps1 --path <file> --output-path <run-cache>/<stem>-tests.ipynb --no-html, plus one `--test <name>` per picked name, which Cells' notebook_cli already accepts as a keep option and reports under \"filtered\"), via the streaming primitive, so progress shows live and the runner's exit code is returned. Missing SPIRAL_CELLS_RUNNER refuses with its name. Test checks the runner args (two filters -> 12 args). europa --check exit 0 (with bodies). With this, every verb in the item title exists: run, --list, --test, -- args. Remaining before Done: crate dependencies for scripts (cargo path instead of plain rustc) and one end-to-end use after TRAIN22 installs.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_10 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 10u32, "2026-11-04T19:28Z", "kit", "11-04 21:2x kit: TRAIN22 RED at the crate gate on kit_run_cli run.rs 1067 (run_run 240). FIXED (unstaged): the build half (paths, manifest, cache key, links, compile, rustc) moved to NEW linked crate kit_run_build (export kit_run_built_exe built_exe ((lib, core, compiler, cache_root, file)) -> (exe, failure)); kit_run_cli keeps setup, test discovery and the runner. Node size (kept run): kit_run_cli 750, kit_run_build 443. Box notebooks: kit-splits-tests.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_11 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 11u32, "2026-11-04T21:13Z", "kit", "11-04 23:1x kit: TRAIN23 RED in regen cargo check, NOT on code: my new crate kit_run_build generated its output as build.rs, and Cargo treats a build.rs in a crate root as a BUILD SCRIPT (\"error[E0601]: main function not found in crate build_script_build\"). Renamed the module to builder (builder.spi -> builder.rs; package.spiproj, Cargo entry/output/path, kit_run_cli's two calls now kit_run_build.builder.*). europa --check ok for both; no other crate outputs build.rs. TRAIN24 launched 17:1x (job-1793826626159-kit-TRAIN24-node) with the fix.")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_12 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 12u32, "2026-11-05T00:14Z", "kit", "11-04 20:1x kit: the split + rename is PROVEN in a full train: TRAIN26's regen phase (every owner, cargo check, publish) ended ok at 20:09:20 with kit_run_build.builder in the tree, and the crate gate passed (337 crates, 0 over; kit_run_cli 750 / kit_run_build 443 had been node-sized). TRAIN26 is now in candidate build -> notebooks -> release; the item closes when the installed kit runs `kit run <file.spi>` (after 21:00, pending).")
inl note_of_kit_run_spi_13 () = Note ("KIT-RUN-SPI", 13u32, "2026-11-06T11:12Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:1x kit: DONE. TRAIN33 INSTALLED at 07:05 (kit.exe 6CEA882D, the first install since TRAIN14c): node half job-1793925896101 regen ok, crate gate 358 over 0, notebooks 234/234 including the cli_check suites; box half job-1793960664511 accepted those verdicts on the same snapshot, then release build, schema and topology checks, publish and smoke passed. PROOF on the box with the installed binary: kit run on a one-line hello.spi compiled to Rust and printed kit run ok, exit 0 in 23 s. Follow-up: kit run needs SPIRAL_LIB, SPIRAL_CORE and SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL set by hand; defaulting them from the installed toolchain is a small usability item.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_1 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 1u32, "2026-11-01T05:03Z", "resilience", "11-01 SCOPE from main's roll-up: the memory fidelity loss is quantified fleet-wide - 185 files, only 2 verbatim, 72+ rebuilt as index-line+practice (\"wording lost\"), 13 INFERRED with confidence lines (MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE n1). Split (main's): resilience runs the cross-lane audit (which files carry a reconstructed vs original Why, flag INFERRED/wording-lost), each lane re-derives the Why of its own ~10 from the backlog notes that quote the user. kit (c) and compiler both also want their memories re-derived - same task.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_2 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:02Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:0x AUDIT RESULT (resilience, round-3). The 13 INFERRED memories identified and assigned per owner lane for re-derivation; each already lists a \"basis\" citation set to mine the user's words from.\nMETRIC ADDED - memory fidelity tiers (of 185 files): T0 verbatim survived = 2; T3 INFERRED (rule invented from title+indirect evidence, no user quote) = 13 (listed below); T2 \"wording lost\" but re-derived WITH a backlog quote/citation = the bulk of the ~70 rebuilt (acceptable); T1 verbatim-quote-in-Why = the rest. Risk is concentrated in T3; within T3, rank by the self-declared confidence.\nTHE 13 INFERRED, by owner lane (confidence in parens):\n- main/direction: bold-native-goals (med), decision-rounds-one-at-a-time (LOW), decisions-are-not-go (med), fork-identity-and-ownership (LOW), inner-jev-autonomy (med/low), landing-formats-staged-files (med), workspace-folder-stays (high).\n- tibia/cells: cells-erl-memory-guard (high/med), cells-rust-cell-recompiles (med), cells-test-block-structure (med).\n- compiler: backend-targets-2026-10-07 (med).\n- kit/node: kit-rename-call-path (LOW), node-proof-skips-regen (med).\nSLICE HAND-OFF (main's split, each lane re-derives its own from the cited notes, drops/keeps the INFERRED tag): main 7, tibia 3, kit 2, compiler 1. Resilience re-derives the 3 LOW-confidence ones this round (most likely wrong) regardless of owner and flags each owner to confirm: decision-rounds-one-at-a-time, fork-identity-and-ownership, kit-rename-call-path.\nNOTE: most \"original wording lost\" memories OUTSIDE this 13 already carry a backlog citation in their Why (T2) and do not need re-derivation - they need only a spot-check that the cited note actually supports the rule. The 13 T3 files are the real work.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_3 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:05Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:0x RE-DERIVATION progress (resilience). Of the 3 LOW-confidence INFERRED memories:\n(1) decision-rounds-one-at-a-time: CORRECTED - the rule was backwards. \"One per round, never stack\" was contradicted by the only recorded rounds (10-14 batched 4 items + Q1-Q3). Rewrote to match evidence (default batch related decisions, recommendation on each; kept the supported core); flagged main to confirm asking-style + slug with the user.\n(2) fork-identity-and-ownership: VERIFIED + UPGRADED - not a lost user quote at all but a fleet-derived operating rule; confirmed against LAB-IN-REPO n3 (a \"main-thread fork: HANDOFF\" note) + sibling rules. Removed the misleading \"INFERRED, correct when user restates\" footer; substance confidence now high; no user confirmation needed.\n(3) kit-rename-call-path: HANDED TO KIT (its slice). It is a narrow kit-technical memory (job run vs job start+wait) verifiable against the kit tree/README, which kit knows better; re-deriving it myself risks a wrong detail. Kit re-derives it with node-proof-skips-regen.\nLESSON for the rest of the 13: two failure shapes seen - (a) a rule INVENTED backwards from a slug (decision-rounds) = dangerous, must re-derive from evidence; (b) a sound rule mis-tagged as a \"lost user quote\" when it was always a derived operating rule (fork-identity) = fix the FRAMING, don't hunt a quote that never existed. Each owner lane should sort its slice into (a) vs (b) first.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_4 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 4u32, "2026-11-01T06:11Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:1x RESOLUTIONS (resilience, round-3):\n- decision-rounds: RESOLVED. Main confirmed with the user 02:0x: \"Batch, with recs\" - so the re-derivation was correct, the old INFERRED \"one per round\" was backwards. Main renamed the memory to decision-rounds-batched.md (old archived to kit\\archive\\memory-decision-rounds-one-at-a-time-20261101.md; MEMORY.md + decisions-are-not-go relinked), kept my evidence, dropped the leftover \"cut to the single decision\" how-to. My superseded edits to the old file are moot (file archived by main).\n- kit slice: RESOLVED by kit. Both kit memories were shape (b) (sound derived rules mis-tagged as lost user quotes); INFERRED tags dropped, provenance added. kit-rename-call-path verified against the installed tool (\"kit help job\" carries the run/start rule verbatim; live probe job-1793513273531: \"job wait <id> 1000\" exits 3 while running, 0 after) and fixed a wrong attribution (help text, not README). node-proof-skips-regen sourced to IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE L9 + KIT-RENAME manifest fast path; kit noted a GAP (the train does not enforce the regen guards) now going into TRAIN7 (EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN n207).\nAUDIT STATUS: of the 13 INFERRED - resilience 3 LOW done (decision-rounds corrected+confirmed, fork-identity verified+upgraded, kit-rename handed off), kit 2 done. REMAINING: main's 6 (bold-native-goals, decisions-are-not-go, inner-jev-autonomy, landing-formats-staged-files, workspace-folder-stays; decision-rounds done), tibia's 3 (cells-*), compiler's 1 (backend-targets). The (a)/(b) sort is the method each lane uses. 5 of 13 resolved.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_5 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 5u32, "2026-11-01T06:13Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:2x T2 SPOT-CHECK (resilience, round-3; the audit's other half). Checked 6 fleet-governing \"wording lost / rebuilt-from-citation\" feedback memories that are NOT in the 13 INFERRED: land-drafts-in-the-repo, use-full-timebox, retry-after-memory-kills, no-quiet-machine-wait, tokens-abundant-cpu-scarce, accept-autonomously. RESULT: 6/6 CLEAN - each states a rule that matches its cited evidence, and each carries a direct user/evidence quote of the rule (e.g. land-drafts SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL n11; no-quiet-machine user \"cpu is idle, we're working poorly\"; accept-autonomously RENAME-BLESS n3 \"do the renames autonomously\"; retry EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN n20 R7 verbatim).\nCONCLUSION (de-scopes the fleet's work): the fidelity risk was ENTIRELY in the 13 T3 INFERRED (no quote) memories. T2 (rebuilt WITH a citation) memories are reliable because they carry the actual evidence - they do NOT need re-derivation, only the light spot-check done here. So each lane should RE-DERIVE its T3 slice (the 13) but only SPOT-CHECK its T2, not rewrite ~70 good memories. The decision-rounds failure signature to hunt: T3 + no quote + a Why that contradicts itself; that is the cheap filter, not \"was it rebuilt\".\nMETRIC: fidelity tiers of 185 - T0 verbatim 2, T3 INFERRED 13 (5 resolved), T2 cited ~70 (6/6 sampled clean), T1 the rest. Audit method proven; remaining T3 re-derivations are per-lane (main 6, tibia 3, compiler 1).")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_6 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 6u32, "2026-11-01T06:14Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:2x METHOD REFINEMENT for the pending slices (resilience). A systemic framing bug seen across the rebuilt memories (fork-identity, anchors-stale-across-respell, and both kit memories): a rule the FLEET derived from its own operating experience gets tagged \"original wording lost\" or \"INFERRED, correct when the user restates it\" - implying a lost USER utterance that never existed. That tag is wrong for a derived rule and sends a lane hunting a quote it will never find.\nSO, each lane, before re-deriving its T3 slice, sorts each into:\n- SHAPE U (a real user utterance): the rule IS something the user said. Find the quote in backlog notes (authored \"user\") and put it in the Why. If the quote contradicts the rule (decision-rounds), the rule is wrong - rewrite to the evidence and flag the owner.\n- SHAPE D (a derived operating rule): no user ever phrased it; the fleet learned it from behaviour. Do NOT hunt a user quote. Verify it against the tree/notes that show the behaviour, cite those, drop the INFERRED/\"user restates\" tag, and state plainly \"derived operating rule, no user wording to recover\" (as done for fork-identity; kit did the same for its 2).\nMost of the 13 T3 are SHAPE D (operating rules); only the genuinely user-voiced ones (decision-rounds was one, and it was backwards) are SHAPE U. This is the cheap sort that makes the remaining 10 slices fast.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_7 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 7u32, "2026-11-01T06:22Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:3x FLEET STATUS: T3 INFERRED re-derivation 11/13 resolved. Resilience 3 (decision-rounds CORRECTED->batched+confirmed, fork-identity VERIFIED SHAPE-D, kit-rename handed off). kit 2 (both SHAPE-D, verified vs installed tool). main 6 (02:2x: all SHAPE-D operating rules, no self-contradiction, no user quote exists - left INFERRED by design, to correct only if the user restates; main will not ask - open ones go to its end-of-run report per the user \"give me decisions only when i ask\"). REMAINING: tibia 3 (cells-erl-memory-guard, cells-rust-cell-recompiles, cells-test-block-structure) - tibia's slice, all expected SHAPE-D (technical, verifiable vs apps/cells). T2 (~70 cited) de-scoped: spot-checked 10/10 clean, do NOT rewrite. Audit method + U/D sort proven and handed to all lanes; resilience portion complete.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_8 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 8u32, "2026-11-01T12:37Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 08:3x QUALITY PASS (resilience, round-4). FIELDS: estimate 90m, load light, assignee agent, team resilience.\nDEFINITION OF DONE (verifiable): all 13 T3 INFERRED memories sorted SHAPE-U/SHAPE-D and resolved — SHAPE-U either carries a dated user quote in its Why or is corrected to the evidence; SHAPE-D has its INFERRED tag dropped with a provenance line citing the tree/notes it was derived from. Check: `grep -rl \"INFERRED 2026-10-30\" ~/.claude/.../memory` returns 0 once all lanes finish. T2 (~70 cited) left as-is (spot-check 10/10 clean, de-scoped). Artifact: mem_sweep clean + the 13 files each showing a dated re-derivation footer.\nNEXT CONCRETE STEP: tibia's 3 (cells-erl-memory-guard, cells-rust-cell-recompiles, cells-test-block-structure) are the only unresolved T3 — ping tibia if not done by next sweep; resilience owns none of the remaining 3, so this item is DONE from resilience's side at 90% pending tibia.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_9 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 9u32, "2026-11-01T14:42Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ~10:4x resilience (git-51), ROUND-5 ask 2 (user: \"read all my past user inputs, make sure everything is accounted for\"). This audit is the SIBLING of MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT: that one asks \"is each memory's WHY faithful to the user's words\"; THIS asks \"is each user DIRECTIVE captured as an item or a memory at all\". Extending this task to own both halves.\nSOURCES (pre-wipe transcripts are GONE, profile-wipe-2026-10-28): (1) backlog notes that quote the user (grep the state for \"user \" / \"user:\" / quoted directives), (2) done-notes citing a user ask, (3) the CURRENT session transcripts ~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl (the only verbatim user words that survive). Pre-10-28 user design talks survive ONLY as backlog-note paraphrase -- that loss is already recorded (MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE n1) and is unrecoverable, so the audit's ceiling is \"every directive that still has a textual trace is covered\", not \"every directive ever given\".\nMETHOD (the detector, not hand-reading): enumerate candidate directives = user-turn lines in the jsonl transcripts + backlog \"user <date>:\" quotes; for each, require a matching item (agile list --json title/notes) OR a memory (kit agile links --memory). The residual = directives with neither -> file an item or a memory. This is a typed set-difference, runnable repeatedly.\nALREADY COVERED (spot-checked this session): spiral-first-tooling, typefeatureset-default-all-code, decision-rounds-batched, wip-cap, granular-stats, no new .py/.ps1 -- each has BOTH a memory and an item. Standing directions becoming items (not memory-only) is MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE n1 heal (2).\nGAP / NEXT STEP (bounded, not completed this round): the jsonl set-difference pass has NOT been run end-to-end -- it is the concrete next step. Known candidate-with-no-dedicated-item from this session: the browser/Chrome direction the user will export \"after the stabilization round\" (MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE n1 (3)) -- a FUTURE input, correctly deferred, flagged so it is not forgotten. No OTHER uncovered directive surfaced in the spot-check, but the full pass is owed.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_10 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 10u32, "2026-11-01T14:48Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ~10:4x resilience (git-51), ROUND-5 ask 2: RAN the jsonl set-difference pass (bounded). Scope this round: the CURRENT session transcript (614162e5, 26 MB, 13,761 lines) parsed turn-by-turn; user-type turns filtered to directive sentences, cross-checked against the 180-file memory index + known items. The 7 large prior-session jsonls (5a6e53fb 133MB, fc1568a6 78MB, 88a146f8 69MB, 84b53718 65MB, b7595ac3 52MB, ac4b4e98 31MB, + ec368ac8) remain -- the SAME parser over all of them is the next step (each ~1-2 min; batchable).\nCAVEAT on method: the naive turn filter also catches peer cross-session relays and system-reminders nested inside user-type turns; a clean pass must exclude from=/<system-reminder>/<cross-session-message> wrappers (refined next run). Signal was still separable by eye this round.\nCOVERAGE (current-session directives -> all have an item or memory): \"assurance the wipe is recovered\" -> WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER (this round); \"read all past inputs\" -> this item; \"groomings lazy / typefeatureset sloppy\" -> typefeatureset-default-all-code + round-4 quality notes; \"granular stats / what's left behind\" -> GRANULAR-STATS; \"iterate autonomously, CPU-light while waiting\" -> backlog-always-has-cpu-light / no-quiet-machine-wait / idle-needs-wake-source; \"no new scheduled-task registrations without a user yes\" -> machine-upkeep-myself; \"didn''t lose that code, backups, repurpose/groom\" -> WIPE-REDO-LEDGER + lab forensics.\nONE CANDIDATE GAP (verify, don''t assume from a noisy extract): user \"we gotta lose the fear of parallelizing, we have automatic backups i hope\" -- a DISPOSITION directive (parallelize more boldly) premised on backups EXISTING. No memory captures \"parallelize boldly; durable backups are what make it safe\", and it tensions with max-three-agents (a CAP). It is half-covered by my durability work (off-box copies now exist, backup-mirror-coverage-boundary) but the user''s own worry (\"i hope\") is the point: the premise (backups exist) is now TRUE and should be stated back as a memory so the boldness is licensed. Proposing a feedback memory \"parallelize-boldly-backups-hold\" and reconciling it with max-three-agents (bold within the agent cap). NEXT: run the parser over the 7 prior jsonls with the refined wrapper-exclusion, then file any residual.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_11 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 11u32, "2026-11-01T14:52Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 ~10:5x resilience (git-51): note-10 candidate gap CONFIRMED + RESOLVED (main verified). The verbatim user message exists in this heal session''s jsonl (614162e5, line 11335, role user, no timestamp): \"did you give main the task to heal? why haven''t we done that ourselves? we gotta lose the fear of parallelizing, we have automatic backups i hope\". The only hit in main''s own jsonl was my message quoting it. CONTEXT CORRECTION to my paraphrase: it is about lanes doing work THEMSELVES in parallel instead of handing it to main -- NOT about subagent count -- so it does NOT conflict with max-three-agents. Main filed memory parallelize-dont-hand-off (verbatim quote + the user''s 11-01 restatement on destructive migrations, cross-linked to max-three-agents). Holding it as \"verify, don''t file from a noisy paraphrase\" was correct: the real quote reframed it. This directive is now item+memory covered. Residual audit scope unchanged: the 7 prior-session jsonls with the refined wrapper-exclusion parser.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_12 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 12u32, "2026-11-01T20:29Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 16:4x BOUNDED AUDIT PASS (resilience, autonomous run) — prior-session jsonl ec368ac8 (personal/X-audio lane, 9 genuine user turns after excluding tool_result/system-reminder/cross-session wrappers). METHOD proven: ConvertFrom-Json per line, filter type=user, strip wrapper turns, regex for directive language (from now on|always|never|make sure|don't|stop|we gotta|prefer|instead of|avoid), dedupe snippets. COVERAGE: the directives found map to EXISTING memories/items — 'be sota, best open-source text-to-audio or post-train' + 'biggest audio per account capping 24h' -> x-account-audio-project; 'unmetered vm / europa disk' -> CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP + REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER; 'Don''t post status tables; work, then stop' -> autonomous-stop-gate; 'backlog items to keep iterating' -> backlog-always-has-cpu-light-work; 'parallelizing instead of waiting, unblock yourself' -> parallelize-dont-hand-off. CANDIDATE GAP (flag, do NOT file — not resilience's lane, and per the verify-don''t-file-from-paraphrase discipline): 'respect [rate] limits; never bypass access controls' (X-scraping/API ethics context) — the personal/x lane should verify whether this access-control rule is captured (x-api-credentials or a sibling) or is a genuine uncaptured durable rule. REMAINING: the 6 larger prior-session jsonls (5a6e53fb 139MB, fc1568a6 82MB, 88a146f8 74MB, 84b53718 68MB, b7595ac3 54MB, ac4b4e98 31MB) — same parser, run when a longer CPU-light window is free; this small session validated the parser + exclusion filter end to end.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_13 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 13u32, "2026-11-01T20:30Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 16:5x AUDIT PASS 2 (resilience) — ac4b4e98 (36MB). Genuine user directive found: 'we should stop this working-in-tmp-dirs-to-move-to-the-repo-later after some unknown criteria' — CAPTURED by the drafts-store direction (land-drafts-in-the-repo / kit drafts store with explicit graduate-to-commit criteria) + work-in-the-repo. No new uncaptured gap. METHOD REFINEMENT (important for the full pass): the type=user turns include MY OWN cron/heartbeat prompts and <task-notification> text, which the directive regex false-positives on ('never end a turn idle', 'tibia lines never seen'). The full-pass parser must also exclude lines whose user text is an autonomous-heartbeat prompt or a task-notification echo (match 'autonomous-iteration heartbeat|<task-notification>|Stop hook feedback'), not just the <system-reminder>/tool_result/cross-session wrappers. Two of 7 prior jsonls now sampled (ec368ac8, ac4b4e98); both clean but for the one flagged access-controls candidate (n12). Remaining 5 (the 50-139MB ones) when a longer window is free.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_14 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 14u32, "2026-11-01T20:32Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 16:5x AUDIT PASS 3 (resilience) — b7595ac3 (56MB). Clean. Directive 'i''m trying to avoid py files in the repo, if this is really important rewrite it in another language' = the faithful ORIGIN quote of memory no-py-txt-in-repos (+ spiral-first-tooling, org-tooling-in-dot-github) — Why fidelity confirmed. The recurring 'don''t start now, just establish the comm' is a session-restart behavioral line, not a durable rule. TALLY: 3 of 7 prior jsonls sampled (ec368ac8, ac4b4e98, b7595ac3), all clean; one candidate gap outstanding (access-controls, n12, for personal/x to verify). Memory Why-fidelity is holding across the sample. Remaining: 5a6e53fb 139MB, fc1568a6 82MB, 88a146f8 74MB, 84b53718 68MB (the heavy four) when a longer CPU-light window opens.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_15 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 15u32, "2026-11-01T20:36Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 17:0x AUDIT PASS 4 (resilience) — 84b53718 (70MB, 64 directive-turns -> 5 distinct). Clean. 'make sure its gh actions are passing, are we verifying that every time i decide to push' = faithful origin of verify-ci-after-user-push; 'everything downloaded in gh actions, available through dir-tree-html with reports/benchs' -> same (CI artifacts + dir-tree-html judgment). 'i don''t even remember asking for such extension to exist in kit' = a scope reaction (don''t add unrequested features), not a durable rule to file. TALLY: 4 of 7 prior jsonls sampled (ec368ac8, ac4b4e98, b7595ac3, 84b53718), all clean; one candidate gap outstanding (access-controls, n12). Memory Why-fidelity confirmed across the sample. Remaining heavy three: 5a6e53fb 139MB, fc1568a6 82MB, 88a146f8 74MB.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_16 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 16u32, "2026-11-01T20:56Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 17:1x AUDIT PASSES 5-7 (resilience) — fc1568a6 82MB, 88a146f8 74MB, 5a6e53fb 139MB. ALL 7 PRIOR JSONLS NOW SWEPT. Strong coverage: 'back up then leave unstaged since my last manual commit', 'no acks; never commit or stage' -> never-commit-leave-unstaged + messaging-budget; 'avoiding these oks, do backups then do what you think best' -> accept-autonomously/decide-autonomously; 'i don''t like py files in the repos' (again) -> no-py-txt-in-repos; 'use installed bin\\\\kit.exe not the scratchpad copy' -> kit-binary-installed-path; 'Never stop another lane''s process; respect held windows' -> stop-only-own-processes + window-coordination; 'always low cpu work while waiting' -> backlog-always-has-cpu-light.\nCANDIDATE GAPS surfaced (flag for the OWNING lane to verify vs memory; NOT filing — cross-lane + verify-don''t-file-from-paraphrase): (1) 'must never copy secrets' [in tsv/CI context] — security; is this captured beyond the userEmail rule? (2) 'Do not re-sync the mirror between regen and build' — node-proof/candidate-build workflow (kit lane; relates to node-proof-skips-regen) — NOTE I complied in my own proofs. (3) 'Don''t post backlog notes while the box freeze is on (each rewrites the ~8MB agile.spi on C:)' — operational I/O rule (relates to agile-status-lost-updates / box-freeze); no freeze now but worth a memory. (4) 'zero downtime deployments' — deployment goal (x/main). (5) access-controls (n12). METHOD note: type=user turns include own cron/heartbeat + task-notifications; the exclusion filter ('autonomous-iteration heartbeat|task-notification|completed (exit|one line per point') is now proven over all 7. DELIVERABLE: the prior-session transcript sweep for faithful-vs-captured directives is COMPLETE; residual = the 5 flagged candidates routed to owning lanes. Original pre-wipe transcripts remain gone (only these 7 post-wipe survive); this is the recoverable universe.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_17 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 17u32, "2026-11-01T23:40Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 19:4x gap RESOLVED (resilience). Candidate gap n16 'no backlog notes during box freeze' CONFIRMED real (verbatim user quote in transcript 5a6e53fb) and WRITTEN as memory no-agile-writes-during-box-freeze (each agile note rewrites the ~11MB agile.spi; hold writes while a quiet/bench/live window is held; write-side sibling of window-coordination). So of the 5 flagged candidate gaps: export + nameof + this one are now memories (3 written tonight); REMAINING for the owning lanes to verify: 'never bypass access controls' (x/personal, X-scraping context) and 'zero-downtime deployments' (x/main) and 'do not re-sync the mirror between regen and build' (kit, node-proof workflow - relates to node-proof-skips-regen). mem_sweep clean after the write.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_18 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 18u32, "2026-11-02T17:56Z", "resilience", "11-02 13:5x resilience MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT first-pass scan (288 memories). Classification by body markers: ~132 original Why, ~149 reconstructed/enriched Why (carry a 'Recovered from citations' / 'Enriched from citing notes' / 'rebuilt 2026-' footer = rebuilt post-10-28-wipe from backlog notes, so the Why is a RECONSTRUCTION to trust less than an original), 7 with no explicit Why. The 7 no-Why are all REFERENCE-type (europa-vm, box-gpu-cuda-limit, erlang-port-hide-console-apps, ps-stub-server-traps, claude-code-git-bash-path, backlog-file-size-limit(self-marked UNVERIFIED), audiobooks-lost) - facts/traps that legitimately don't need a **Why:**, NOT gaps. So: no genuine missing-Why gaps; the real flag-set is the ~149 reconstructed Whys. Caveat: regex classification is approximate (enrichment-footer heuristic). REMAINING: spot-check a sample of the reconstructed-Why memories against source (kit archive / original shares) for accuracy, per tibia's lesson 'read the archived source, not notes written from memory'. Method + landscape captured; the spot-check is the audit's substantive remainder.")
inl note_of_memory_reasons_audit_19 () = Note ("MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT", 19u32, "2026-11-02T19:49Z", "resilience", "11-02 15:48 resilience: COMPLETE (audit + cross-lane hand-off). Audit done: 233 files tiered (T0 verbatim 2; T3 INFERRED 13; rest T1 quote / T2 re-derived-with-citation), see note 2 for the 13 by owner + confidence + basis. Hand-off done: filed MAIN-MEMORY-REASONS(7), COMPILER-MEMORY-REASONS(1), KIT-MEMORY-REASONS(2), each team-tagged with its INFERRED memories + confidence + basis-citation pointer + the re-derive-from-pre-wipe-quotes instruction; tibia already had TIBIA-MEMORY-REASONS (Done). Fixed one stale slug in the hand-off (decision-rounds-one-at-a-time -> decision-rounds-batched, renamed since the 11-01 audit). Each lane now owns its re-derivation in its own item; resilience audit+hand-off half is done.")
inl note_of_cell_budget_naming_1 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:37Z", "main", "main 00:3x: decided names, told x and tibia to hold their call-site swaps (x keeps them behind Assert-CellsXCompat in x/scripts/common.ps1; tibia wires its gate to spiral.cell_budget). apps/sim uses x_weight, segments, shortened_url_weight; keep equivalents under CellBudget and send x the exact map. Next: the applier on the scratch tree, then a cells_land_window land, then the compiler retires --x-compat (POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE step 3).")
inl note_of_cell_budget_naming_2 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", 2u32, "2026-11-01T04:46Z", "main", "main 00:45 LANDED green (15/15: cell_budget, cell_budget_profile, warm_notebook, new_project tests). Spiral.Cells.XCompat -> Spiral.Cells.CellBudget (cell_budget.ex), mix spiral.x_compat -> spiral.cell_budget (exit 1 only on over_budget), overweight -> over_budget, x_weight -> cell_weight, shortened_url_weight -> link_weight, run flag --x-compat -> --cell-budget and line prefix 'cell-budget '; hints say 'a renderer' instead of X; the 280 is one constant (@weight_limit in CellBudget.Profile; the profile loader falls back to it). README section 'X compatibility' -> 'Cell budget'. x and tibia have the exact map. Left (20%): x's and tibia's gates switch (theirs), the compiler retires --x-compat (POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE step 3), the profile loader goes after that, and the CELLS-IN-SPIRAL port (M1, main).")
inl note_of_cell_budget_naming_3 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:03Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 08:02 tibia groom: tibia's share is DONE. scripts/build.ps1 calls mix spiral.cell_budget, prints 'build.ps1 / cell budget / ...', and has no x_compat, postable or post names left (verified on every run tonight; also runs under the erl memory guard). Nothing in tibia blocks the remaining 20%.")
inl note_of_cell_budget_naming_4 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", 4u32, "2026-11-01T19:55Z", "x", "2026-11-01 15:55 x: x's SHARE IS DONE (audited 15:5x, git grep over x). Gate: x/scripts/common.ps1 Assert-CellBudget runs 'mix spiral.cell_budget --check' and requires 'cell-budget report: 0 finding(s)', under the erl memory guard. x has no x_compat, XCompat, x-compat, overweight or shortened_url_weight names left. Its 'x_weight' (splitter.spi:237) is X's own counting rule inside x, the X-only layer the item moves to x. The 280 lives once in x (splitter.spi post_limit, the on_x cell_limit instance), and the sim reads it from there (sim_serve too_heavy, unstaged, proven by x-platform-proto2). What's left is not x's: the compiler retires --x-compat (POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE step 3), and main drops the profile loader.")
inl note_of_cell_budget_naming_5 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", 5u32, "2026-11-01T20:40Z", "main", "main 16:4x MAIN'S SHARE DONE (unstaged in spiral/apps/cells): the profile loader is gone. CellBudget.profile_for/nearest_profile/cached_profile/load_profile (61 lines, including the SPIRAL_POSTABILITY_PROFILE override and the walk up to the nearest postability/*.json) and the now-unused defused_regex are removed. mix spiral.cell_budget and notebook_cli use default_profile() directly, so Cells checks every repo with the built-in profile and never reads x's postability/x.json (x keeps that file as its own X data, read by x's tooling only). cell_budget_profile_test.exs is deleted (it tested only the loader; git history keeps it); cell_budget_test's custom-profile assertions became one built-in-profile autolink test. mix test cell_budget_test.exs: 5 passed (box, own MIX_BUILD_PATH). Left: compiler retires --x-compat (POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE step 3), then this closes.")
inl note_of_cell_budget_naming_6 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-NAMING", 6u32, "2026-11-02T21:09Z", "x", "2026-11-02 17:09 x: CLOSED. The last condition (the compiler retires --x-compat, POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE step 3) is MET and checked from the binary, not the announcement: the installed pack17 compiler (core E556D773EB123175) answers 'dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --x-compat' with its usage text and exit 2, and --x-compat is not among its options. The last outside caller, i574n.github/scripts/x-compat-census.ps1, is archived (no longer in the tree). x's share (n4) and main's share (n5: profile loader removed) were already done.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_1 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:37Z", "main", "main 00:3x census (tracked .spi, outside deps/backlog/samples): spiral 77 (mostly Gleam Nil in macros), x 285, kit 97, tibia 21, tictactoe 3, fiscal 0. Next: the compiler formatter gets a list-sugar rule (the type is known after parse_block only for patterns, so start with the pattern position '| Nil =>' and the literal argument position), then x and kit run it.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_2 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:24Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:24 x: x's share measured today (tracked + untracked .spi, excluding target and frames): 236 lines with Nil in 38 files (main's 285 census counted occurrences, not lines), 0 'Cons (', 0 Nil inside macros or /// lines, and 0 user unions with a case named Nil. So every hit is list Nil. 4 sit in tuple pattern slots and take parens per memory list-sugar-not-cons (the comma trap): cli.spi:270 '| Some h, Nil =>', cli.spi:272 '| None, Nil =>', schedule.spi:70 '| Nil, _ =>', schedule.spi:71 '| _, Nil =>' become '([])'. Sites include tonight's sim_serve.spi, x's own new drift.\nPLAN: x heals its share by HAND-RUN rewrite (token-boundary \\bNil\\b -> [], the 4 tuple slots -> ([])), no committed tool. It rides X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS' batch: both edit client sources the sim fixtures embed, so both wait for verify44 to land, then one verify45 -Accept proves them together with zero-difference outputs (memory: the sugar is syntax only, so regenerated outputs must not change beyond fixtures carrying the edited source text). The formatter rule that stops regression stays compiler/main's (n1).")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_3 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:26Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:26 x: CORRECTION to n2's count. PowerShell -match and Select-String are case-INSENSITIVE by default, so 'nil' atoms and strings (\"nil\", erlang_term::atom \"nil\") inflated it. Dry run, case-sensitive and skipping string literals, /// lines and $' / $\" macros: 230 lines in 38 files change, 4 of them tuple-slot patterns (the four named in n2). Samples: '| Nil =>' -> '| [] =>', 'next :: Nil' -> 'next :: []'.\nMETHOD for step (2) (hand-run, not a committed tool). Per line: skip /// lines and lines holding $' or $\" macro text. Walk the characters, toggling inside \"...\" strings (backslash escapes the next char; 'x' char literals are passed through). Outside strings apply -creplace '\\bNil\\b','[]'. Then on lines matching the pattern-tuple shape '\\|\\s*[^=]*(\\[\\]\\s*,|,\\s*\\[\\])[^=]*=>', wrap those [] in parens: ([]). Apply with [IO.File] on RESOLVED absolute paths (memory ps-dotnet-relative-paths), LF kept.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_4 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-11-01T12:03Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:03 x groom: progress 0 -> 40 and Planned -> Active. x's share (230 lines, 38 files) is APPLIED in the tree (X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS n4) and rides verify46. Open after that: kit's share (~97) and the formatter rule (compiler/main, n1).")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_5 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 5u32, "2026-11-01T13:13Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:12 x: x's share PROVEN and LANDED. verify46 (X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS n7) passed on the healed tree, and its outputs are landed. x has 0 list Nil left (case-sensitive scanner). Open: kit's share (~97) and the formatter rule that prevents regression (compiler/main).")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_6 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 6u32, "2026-11-01T20:26Z", "x", "2026-11-01 16:26 x: x's REGRESSION GUARD landed (unstaged): x/scripts/gate.ps1, which apps/client/build.ps1 runs with -Enforce on every build and in CI, now fails when any tracked or untracked x .spi has a line matching (case-sensitive) '\\bNil\\b|\\bCons \\(' outside a host macro ($' or $\"). Proven on the box before landing, since it's a CI-gating edit and the read-only gate needs no compile. Live x tree: '73 Spiral files, 0 with list Nil/Cons outside host macros', exit 0. Negative case, a scratch git tree with one ':= xs () : list i32 = Nil' line and one $'Nil' macro line: exit 1, 'list sugar drifted back in 1 file(s): apps/c/a.spi' (the macro line is exempt). So x can't regress. Open, not x's: kit's ~97 lines and the generic formatter/lint rule (compiler/main, n1). This guard is x's stopgap until then, and it retires when that rule lands.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_7 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 7u32, "2026-11-01T22:39Z", "compiler", "11-01 18:36 compiler: GUARD DRAFTED as a formatter lint, not a rewrite. The formatter's token rewriter (FormatterConvert, the --spelling path) only accepts a line whose relexed tokens equal the original's, so Nil -> [] (one token becomes two) can't pass that guard; a safe rewrite needs a per-mapping guard. PROGRAM: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD\\1-format-list-sugar-lint.kitdo (3 replacements, FormatterCli.runOnThisStack; dry run clean on core F6F6879566C5A830). 'format <paths> --list-sugar' lexes each file and prints 'file:line:col: list-nil (write [] for the empty list)' for every TokVar Nil and 'list-cons (write head :: tail)' for every TokVar Cons outside macros (lexeme.InsideMacro, so Gleam's Nil inside $' ... ' is exempt). It exits 1 when it finds any. Opt-in, so today's --check users (kit has its ~97 hits still to heal) don't go red. Gates add --list-sugar once their repo is clean, and x's hand-run method (n3) stays the healer. Rides the pack after pack15b; proof = a probe file with '| Nil =>', 'x :: Nil' and a Gleam $'Nil' macro gives exactly 2 findings and exit 1, and lib/spiral gives 0 findings.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_8 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 8u32, "2026-11-01T23:18Z", "compiler", "11-01 19:19 compiler: PROVEN on the pack15c candidate (node job-1793574178127, core 6292FBD7632BB398). The probe (| Nil => / 1i32 :: Nil / Gleam $'Nil') gives exactly 2 findings, probe.spi:3:7 and :6:19 list-nil, with the macro exempt, and exit 1. The run also exposed a flag bug, now fixed in the program (5 replacements): FormatterCli.positional treats an unknown --flag as a key/value pair, so --list-sugar was read as a path ('vanished: --list-sugar'), and before a path it would have swallowed that path. --list-sugar is now registered as a boolean flag in both positional lists. Next: rerun the probe plus lib/spiral (expect 0 findings) on the next candidate build.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_9 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 9u32, "2026-11-02T00:29Z", "compiler", "11-01 20:30 compiler: FINAL PROGRAM PROVEN on the box pack15c build (core 83CEDAA9DDED49EB). Probe: 1 finding, exit 1, no 'vanished' (the flag fix works). lib/spiral: the first build reported 7, of which 5 were reflection.spi's type-applied `(Nil (string * t))` in real code, which [] cannot express. The lint now skips a Nil followed by a backtick type argument. The second build reports exactly 2 real drifts: xml.spi:336 '| Cons _ =>' and xpath_eval.spi:924 '(_ :: Cons _)', both meaning 'non-empty', i.e. '_ :: _'. They are lib heals for the next lib land (after lib-reshape's R1b+R2), not compiler work. Ships in pack15c (handed to main 20:2x). DONE when pack15c is installed and both lib sites are healed, so 'format --check --list-sugar lib/spiral' exits 0 and can join the lib gate.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_10 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 10u32, "2026-11-02T05:29Z", "x", "2026-11-02 01:29 x: BOTH LIB HEALS PROVEN (x-lib-sugar1, Europa on pack15c; rule written before the run: both files exit 0 with 0 failed, and 0 lint findings). xml.spi:336 '| Cons _ =>' -> '| _ :: _ =>' passes 14/14. xpath_eval.spi:924 '(_ :: Cons _)' -> '(_ :: _ :: _)' passes 12/12. CORRECTION to compiler n9: '_ :: Cons _' matches 2 OR MORE items (head plus a non-empty tail), so '_ :: _' would have widened unique_located to single-item steps. 'format --check --list-sugar' over all 122 lib .spi files: 0 findings. Drafts are in drafts\\spiral\\LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD. The land is held: xml.spi moved under compiler's DELPHI-TO-PASCAL step 2 (delphi_* -> pascal_*, live FA14058BE85A, base 9AE0E2DB4DA7; lib_land_window refused, as it should). Next: rebase xml.spi on the live file once compiler's tree-write window clears, re-prove, then land with erlang_map. This closes when 'format --check --list-sugar lib/spiral' exits 0 on the live tree.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_11 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 11u32, "2026-11-02T09:15Z", "x", "2026-11-02 05:15 x: CLOSED. Rule from n10: 'format --check --list-sugar lib/spiral' has 0 list-sugar findings on the live tree. Run at 05:13 on the box core F16F6B2032F81F2D (pack15d): 0 list-nil/list-cons lines. The other 68 output lines are general formatting the item doesn't cover (45 over-120, 23 would-change), which is why the exit code is 1. POSITIVE CONTROL in the same run: a probe with ':= xs () : list i32 = Nil' and '| Nil =>' gives exactly 2 list-nil findings, so the zero is real. Both lib heals are live, unstaged. xml.spi (C2F46E8ABD06) is my x-lib-sugar2 draft plus the compiler's '///> pascal' lines. xpath_eval.spi equals the draft 734F0463FCBA ('(_ :: _ :: _)'). The guard has three parts: x gate.ps1 -Enforce (n6), the compiler formatter lint (n7-n9), and lib at 0.")
inl note_of_list_sugar_drift_guard_12 () = Note ("LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD", 12u32, "2026-11-02T10:56Z", "compiler", "11-02 07:05 compiler: CLOSING. DoD met: pack15c (the lint) is installed, and both lib sites are healed (xml.spi C2F46E8ABD06F5D9, xpath_eval.spi 734F0463FCBA2995; node gate 14/14 and 12/12, the same as the control). CENSUS with the installed `format --check --list-sugar`: lib 0; x 0 (73 .spi); kit 95 findings in 19 files under src/ (94 list-nil, 1 list-cons at kit_table_sections/sections.spi:82:11), 0 in compiler-contracts/infra/standards. Kit owns its heal; told kit the count. Census method note: running the lint on the kit root walks .cache/release-tree (one lib snapshot per train) and took 4.6 CPU-hours without finishing; scope it to src/. Output: scratchpad sugarland\\lint-kit-src.txt.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_1 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:37Z", "main", "main 00:3x: first pass at this groom; the count trend goes on this item each round.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_2 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 2u32, "2026-11-01T04:39Z", "main", "main 00:3x round 1: open items 401; ownerless 4 (FISCAL-APP, FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE and TIBIA-2048-ANCHOR carry the lane in the id but no tag, WORKFLOW-END-TO-END is ownerless and 185 h stale); open with no note for 48 h: 80. Deps on Blocked items hide items from 'agile next' (CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS sat 8 days behind a user-blocked badge item): next round, also list open items whose every dep is Blocked.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_3 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 3u32, "2026-11-01T04:51Z", "main", "main 00:5x round 2 check, 'open items whose deps are all Blocked or Paused' (hidden from agile next): 6. Fixed PS1-TO-SPIRAL (P0, behind PIPELINES-GREEN; now behind KIT-RUN-SPI). Left with a reason: X-PARALLEL-BUILD (x's census), NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE and CI-K3S (really need green pipelines), XSD-FIELD-DESCRIPTORS (fiscal's paused prototypes), UPSTREAM-FORK-START (user-blocked upstream post). Ask kit: 'agile next --hidden' should list these every time (KIT-AGILE-CADENCE).")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_4 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 4u32, "2026-11-01T20:58Z", "main", "main 16:5x ROUND 3 (computed from agile list --json; deps split on commas): of 496 open items, 4 were hidden from agile next because every dep was Blocked or Paused, and 0 deps dangle. Fixed all 4: PIPELINES-GREEN was Blocked with no deps and every repo is green except alphabet's Windows job, so it is Active again, which unhides CI-K3S and NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE. PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH's dep on TREE-PAGES-V2 was main's own mistake (the -Include works with the current renderer), dep removed. UPSTREAM-FORK-START stays behind UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA, a user-blocked upstream post, on purpose. Also fixed K3S-EVERYWHERE -> CI-K3S (machines don't wait on Actions compliance). The census is a one-liner now; it belongs in kit as 'agile next --hidden' (KIT-AGILE-CADENCE).")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_5 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 5u32, "2026-11-02T23:55Z", "main", "main 11-02 19:5x ROUND 4 (computed from agile list --json; deps split on commas): 667 open. (a) 0 without a team tag. (b) 12 hidden from agile next: 6 EOIE-* items (JOBS, READ-QUERY, EDIT-BATCH, ONE-CALL-SPEC, SPIRAL-TESTS, ZED-SWEEP) had the literal dep 'none', which kit reads as a missing item: CLEARED. The other 6 are hidden on purpose (the WoW chain + TIBIA-GAME-HANDSHAKE behind the user-paused tibia/WoW work; UPSTREAM-POST-2 and UPSTREAM-FORK-START behind the user-gated upstream PR). (c) 8 dangling deps: the 6 'none' above + 2 GLUED ids missing a comma, both fixed: CELLS-IN-SPIRAL 'X-SIM-CELLS-SPIRALCELLS-TYPED-UNITS' -> X-SIM-CELLS-SPIRAL,CELLS-TYPED-UNITS, and CELLS-PORT-NOTEBOOK 'CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPEDCELLS-TYPED-UNITS' -> CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED,CELLS-TYPED-UNITS. (d) 5 Active silent > 48 h: main's BANG-MACRO-CLEANUP (94 h), RUNNER-STALE (73 h), CELLS-RUST-TEST-BATCH (49 h) -> Planned with a note; EOIE-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT retagged team:eoie -> team:kit (eoie was kit's old name); KIT-STANDARDS-ENTRY-SUBDIRS (52 h) left to kit. Trend vs round 3: hidden 4 -> 12 found (all fixed or intentional), dangling 0 -> 8 (all fixed). FOR kit (KIT-AGILE-CADENCE): agile edit/add should refuse 'none' as a dep and refuse a dep id that does not exist.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_6 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 6u32, "2026-11-03T05:03Z", "main", "main 11-03 01:0x ROUND 5 (computed from agile list --json; deps split on commas): 660 open (Active 158, Planned 452, Paused 29, Blocked 21). (a) 0 dangling deps. (b) 11 hidden from agile next, all on purpose with a resume condition in the blocker's last note: HEAVY-HOLD-DEADLOCK <- EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE and REMOTE-EXEC-S3/S4 <- REMOTE-EXEC-S2 (both Blocked by kit until the TRAIN14 install); SCRUM-XP-S3 <- SCRUM-XP-S2 (kit-train-only work, released by x-dumps); X-DUMPS-TTS <- X-DUMPS-TRANSLATE (user budget for the paid translation); UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL and UPSTREAM-FORK-START <- UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA (user-gated public PR); the WoW chain and TIBIA-GAME-HANDSHAKE (user-paused). (c) Active and silent > 48 h: 4 (round 4: 5). EOIE-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT 61 h, KIT-STANDARDS-ENTRY-SUBDIRS 58 h, SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD 52 h belong to kit/compiler; main's FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER 53 h gets a next step (its note). Trend: hidden 6 -> 4 -> 12 -> 11, dangling 0 -> 0 -> 8 -> 0, silent Active 5 -> 4.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_7 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 7u32, "2026-11-03T14:58Z", "main", "main 11-03 11:0x ROUND 6 (agile list --open --json): 611 open in the list view (Active 115, Planned 496; Paused/Blocked not in --open). (a) items with no team:/lane tag: 0. (b) Active and silent > 48 h: 2 (round 5: 4). LEAN-BACKEND (main, 54 h): lab file ruled out (its pin bump is already applied), next step + owners written (n30). FISCAL-APP (team:fiscal, 48 h): fiscal's epic, its children carry the activity; left to fiscal. Trend: silent Active 5 -> 4 -> 2; untagged 0. Also closed today: HURDLES-1103 (all 8 lanes, every open hurdle owned), MIRROR-RESYNC-1103, HTML-LINT-HEAL.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_8 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 8u32, "2026-11-03T18:36Z", "tibia", "11-03 14:4x tibia, sweep of the TIBIA-TAGGED slice (claimed by the gate as tibia-ac4b4e98): 40 open tibia-tagged items; 2 stale (last note older than 48 h) and both without a team tag: CELLS-EVERYWHERE and BACKEND-BALANCE. Each now has an owner (team:main) and a next step in a note; CELLS-EVERYWHERE also got tibia measured share (129/137 cell files). The other 38 have notes within 48 h. Count for this slice: 2 orphans -> 0. The untagged non-tibia remainder is main.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_9 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 9u32, "2026-11-03T18:55Z", "main", "main 11-03 15:0x ROUND 7: open incl. Blocked/Paused 663 (Active 125, Planned 483, Blocked 24, Paused 31). Active silent > 48 h: 1 (FISCAL-APP, fiscal's epic; round 6: 2, round 5: 4). NEW CHECK, main's Blocked/Paused (23): each has a reason, though 14 keep it in an earlier note rather than the last one (procedural last notes: parent-epic or provisional-field notes). Re-decided TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO (its resume condition was met: sweep rerun, 9/10 pages OK, now blocked on html's push only). Blocked today with explicit RESUME WHEN on the user's push: HTML-CLI, HTML-CI-RELEASE, ORG-EXIT-FALLOUT. Trend: silent Active 5 -> 4 -> 2 -> 1.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_10 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 10u32, "2026-11-03T21:13Z", "tibia", "11-03 17:1x tibia, second gate claim, WHOLE-BACKLOG SLICE: open items 606; without a team tag 250, of which 171 stale (last note older than 48 h). All 171 already named an owner through a plain lane tag (kit 89, main 60, compiler 22) but were invisible to agile next --team <lane>, which matches only team: tags (the gap main recorded 11-01). Each now has team:<its first lane tag> appended with every existing tag kept (edit tags replaces, so the full list was passed; one item read back first: kino,main -> kino,main,team:main): team:kit 89, team:main 60 (KINO-SOTA first, then 59), team:compiler 22; 170+1 edits ok, 0 failures. COUNT TREND: untagged open 250 -> 79; stale untagged 171 -> 0. Next steps stay with each owning lane (their agile next now lists them). The remaining untagged open items have notes within 48 h.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_11 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 11u32, "2026-11-03T21:40Z", "main", "main 11-03 17:4x ROUND 8: open 661; Active silent > 48 h: 3 (FISCAL-APP, NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-RETIRE, HOME-STRAY-FOLDERS); items with no team/lane tag: 1. Unchanged since round 7 except today's new items (all tagged at creation: GRANULAR-SPLIT-*, REGEX-STAGED-TABLES, HTML-NODE-RECORDS (Done)). Trend silent Active: 5 -> 4 -> 2 -> 1 -> 3.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_12 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 12u32, "2026-11-03T23:18Z", "resilience-614162e5", "GROOM 1 (11-03 19:10, resilience-614162e5) - BASELINE for the count trend.\nCOUNTS (open=654 of 1429; Done 775 excluded): no-team-tag ORPHANS=108; STALE (last note >48h)=256; union=270.\nROOT CAUSE of orphans: a tag-convention SPLIT. 546 open items use canonical team:X; 108 carry a BARE lane tag (main/kit/x/compiler/fiscal/tibia) with NO team: prefix, so `kit agile next --team X` routing cannot see them - they have a de-facto owner but are unroutable. `migrate-tags preview` = tasks=0 (it does not canonicalize bare->team:, so no auto-fix).\nNOT mechanically fixable: many orphans are MULTI-lane (e.g. GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT [gleam,rust,concurrency,x,tibia,kit,main]; TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT [...,main,fiscal]) - ownership is a decision per item, not a rewrite. `edit tags` REPLACES the whole list, so each fix must set the full canonical list.\nSTALE by team: kit 115, main 72, compiler 30, fiscal 7, tibia 7, x 6, eoie 2, resilience 2. kit+main = 187/256 (73%) - those two lanes carry almost all the stale-note debt; they should re-note or close.\nORPHAN by priority: P0 ~25, P1 ~55, P2 ~15, P3-5 ~13.\nACTION PATH (for main, over grooms, to drive orphan->0): canonicalize bare lane tags to team:X, resolving multi-lane ambiguity by picking the primary owner; start with P0 single-lane (PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY->team:main, PS1-TO-SPIRAL->team:main, etc.). Not auto-batched here: owner choice needs judgment + edit-replaces is error-prone at scale.\nTREND: groom 1 baseline = orphan 108 / stale 256. Next groom compares; target is both shrinking.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_13 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 13u32, "2026-11-04T00:39Z", "tibia", "11-03 20:4x tibia, re-check after the BSOD (claimed again by the gate): open 595, untagged 79, stale untagged 0 (was 171 before the 17:1x round). Trend holds; nothing to assign.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_14 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 14u32, "2026-11-04T01:14Z", "x", "2026-11-03 21:14 x: x's CORNER of the sweep done (claimed for x by the gate). Open x-tagged items: 57; with no note in 48 h: 15, now 0 unaddressed. Each has an owner and a next step: 1 Blocked -> Paused with a resume condition (X-PARALLEL-CENSUS); 1 Blocked confirmed with its blocker named (X-CLIENT: the user's push); 4 Paused confirmed with reason + condition (X-FAILURE-TEXT-DEDUP, X-ON-NEAR, X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES, X-NEAR-PROVENANCE); 3 owned by team:compiler (x a consumer: RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES, GLEAM-INLINE-SINGLE-USE, GLEAM-PARTS-EMIT); 5 heavy x items with next steps queued behind the node (X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED, X-BACKEND-SWITCHES, X-PARALLEL-BUILD, X-HAND-UNITS, X-LAMBDA-PARENS); 1 CPU-light share taken now (LIB-MOVE-CIVIL-DATE's world.spi). Releasing; the rest of the backlog is main's.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_15 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 15u32, "2026-11-04T01:16Z", "resilience-614162e5", "GROOM 2 (21:15, ~2h after groom 1). TREND vs groom 1 baseline (108 orphan / 256 stale):\n- orphan (no team: tag) = 108, FLAT. Zero change in 2h confirms the bare-lane-tag convention split is STRUCTURAL and will NOT self-resolve - it only moves via the deliberate canonicalize-bare-tags-to-team: pass from groom 1's action path (owner decisions, multi-lane items need a primary-owner call). This is the sole lever on the orphan number.\n- stale (>48h) = 249, down 7 from 256 (roughly flat): ~2h of items aging past the sliding 48h cutoff offset by ~2h of fresh notes (post-crash fleet activity + my BSOD-incident notes).\n- stale by team: kit 115 (flat), main 80 (UP from 72 - main's own stale debt is the ONLY growing segment; main should re-note/close its 80), compiler 28 (-2), fiscal 7, tibia 7, eoie 2.\n- fleet actively filing post-crash: new items since groom 1 incl LIB-MOVE-CIVIL-DATE, X-BACKEND-SWITCHES, X-PARALLEL-BUILD, plus BOX-BSOD-1103 (mine).\nNET: counts stable across the crash+restart; orphan-canonicalization and main's rising stale debt are the two levers. No per-item triage this groom (box fragile post-BSOD, ~45min to run-end); trend recorded, lease released.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_16 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 16u32, "2026-11-04T01:21Z", "resilience", "GROOM 2 ACTION (not just trend): canonicalized resilience's OWN single-lane bare-tag orphans to team:resilience - CLAUDE-TREE-PRIORITY-BOOST and MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST (both clearly durability/machine-upkeep = my lane). Orphan count 108 -> 106. Left ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME untouched (tags scripts,languages,cleanup,main,kit,tibia,resilience = genuinely multi-lane; primary-owner is main's call, not a unilateral resilience claim).\nPATTERN that actually moves the flat 108 without a risky bulk rewrite: EACH LANE canonicalizes its OWN single-lane bare-tag orphans to team:X (safe, deterministic, lead-by-example); the multi-lane orphans are the residue that needs a per-item owner decision (main/the owning lanes). I did resilience's share; kit (its bare-kit orphans), compiler, x, main should do theirs. edit tags REPLACES the list - pass the full set with team:X prepended (as done here).")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_17 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 17u32, "2026-11-04T01:24Z", "x", "2026-11-03 21:24 x: second half for x's corner (re-claimed by the gate): open items without any team: tag = 106 of 651. By their project tag: main 53, kit 22, compiler 15, x 12 (9 of them x-only), x-dumps 3 (XDUMPS-BACKUP-TRANSFER, XDUMPS-TYPED-SPI, XDUMPS-UNRECOVERABLE-INDEX), tibia 1. x tagged its 9 with team:x (tags kept): X-DONE, X-CLIENT, NEAR-FEED-PAGED-VIEW, X-PARALLEL-CENSUS, CELLS-IN-SPIRAL, X-REPO-TEMPLATE, X-ON-NEAR, X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES, X-NEAR-PROVENANCE. The other 97 are for main, kit, compiler, x-dumps and tibia to tag; the per-lane counts above are their lists. Untagged trend: 106 -> 97 after x's share. Releasing.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_18 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 18u32, "2026-11-04T01:27Z", "kit", "11-03 21:3x kit (gate-assigned; kit ran the measuring half, main owns the follow-up). Counts:\n- 651 open rows; 0 with no lane word. 97 lack a 'team:' prefix but carry a bare lane word ('main', 'kit', 'compiler' ...), and agile next --team already resolves those, so relabelling would be churn.\n- 154 Active/Blocked; 15 with a last note older than 48 h, none kit's:\n  - main: FISCAL-APP is fiscal's; NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT and UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL (Blocked since 10-28/29); FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS; CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS; LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED; PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH; REGEX-SYNTAX, REGEX-DERIVATIVES, REGEX-ANTIMIROV, REGEX-XSD; HTML-MARKDOWN, HTML-PARITY;\n  - compiler: RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE, EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC.\nEach needs an owner's next step or a close. The 4 REGEX-* items go silent together (last notes 11-01 22:31 to 23:18), which looks like one parked lane rather than 4 forgotten items. Main to triage.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_19 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 19u32, "2026-11-04T01:31Z", "x", "2026-11-03 21:31 x: third gate claim. The 3 XDUMPS items with only a project tag (XDUMPS-BACKUP-TRANSFER, XDUMPS-TYPED-SPI, XDUMPS-UNRECOVERABLE-INDEX; no main/kit/compiler tag, so no lane's list would pick them up) got team:x-dumps, the existing team tag (8 items already). Untagged open: 106 -> 97 -> 94. The remaining 94 carry a lane's project tag (main 53, kit 22, compiler 15, x 3 shared with other lanes, tibia 1): each lane's own pass. x's corner of this sweep is complete; releasing.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_20 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 20u32, "2026-11-04T01:41Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps (auto-claimed by the --any gate) RELEASED - this is MAIN-owned by its own definition (\"Main owns the CPU-light work no lane owns\"). Ironically it is the item whose job is to TAG orphan/untagged items, and it is itself untagged so --any kept handing it (and its siblings) to idle lanes. Concrete orphans for this sweep to tag to kit/compiler, released by x-dumps in the last hour because they are Spiral-code standards not X archival: GRANULAR-STATS and TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT. Tagging those (and giving MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP itself team:main) stops the --any mis-handing to x-dumps.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_21 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 21u32, "2026-11-04T01:46Z", "compiler", "11-03 21:5x compiler (claimed for me by the stop gate at the end of the run; released). Compiler-slice data point: kit agile list <root> all --team compiler --json gives 300 tasks, 100 open (Active/Planned/Blocked), but every task's last_note field is EMPTY in the JSON, so an 'older than 48 h' filter can't be computed from one list call and flags all 100. The sweep needs either last_note.at filled in list --json (kit fix, one field) or 100 agile show calls. Compiler items touched today with a written next step (not orphans): PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES, BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL, PREDICT-PER-BACKEND, CELLS-TYPED-TARGETS, RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES (pack33 ready, install 11-04), LINKED-UNITS-DERIVE-OUT, LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-RECORDS, KIT-TRAIN-PIN-COMPILER (kit), SPIRAL-NAMEOF-HEAL (cells daemon site is x's).")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_22 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 22u32, "2026-11-04T01:59Z", "resilience", "CLOSING TREND (21:58): orphan 108 (groom1/2, structurally flat) -> 106 (my 2 resilience canonicalizations) -> 94 now. The extra 12 were canonicalized by OTHER lanes - the per-lane \"canonicalize your own single-lane bare-tag orphans to team:X\" pattern (note 16) is being ADOPTED fleet-wide. So the flat 108 broke once the fix was demonstrated + recorded, not before: lead-by-example worked. stale steady at 249. Lease released for the run's 22:00 stop. NEXT GROOM: continue tracking orphan down as lanes finish their shares; the residue will be the genuinely multi-lane items (e.g. ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME) needing per-item owner decisions.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_23 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 23u32, "2026-11-04T04:17Z", "main", "11-04 00:1x main groom (user ask: 10 min, all lanes): moved 36 of main's Active items untouched for 12 h or more to Planned, keeping their percents (WIP cap). PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY is now Blocked on user-only steps. Active for main: 58 down to 21.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_24 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 24u32, "2026-11-04T04:22Z", "main", "11-04 00:2x main CORRECTION to my 00:1x groom note. The user moved the stale window from 12 h to 48 h (relayed by kit 00:2x: \"at least 48h would be better\"). I put the 27 items with a note inside 48 h back to Active, percents unchanged. Only 9 stay Planned (no note since 11-02T04Z or earlier): REGEX-ANTIMIROV, REGEX-DERIVATIVES, REGEX-XSD, REGEX-SYNTAX, CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS, PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH, LIB-SWEEP-1031-RED, HTML-PARITY, HTML-MARKDOWN. PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY is Blocked (user-only remainder). Active for main: 58 down to 48.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_25 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 25u32, "2026-11-04T05:02Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:0x resilience GROOM (stop-gate claimed). ORPHAN SWEEP big reduction: live orphans (non-Done, no team: tag) 93 -> 40 -> 24 (run baseline was 108). (1) Canonicalized all 53 bare-main single-lane orphans to team:main - main's own 00:1x grooming moved statuses not tags, so these sat un-canonicalized; 53/53 ok via a retry-on-CAS loop. (2) Assigned primary owners to all 16 multi-lane orphans: 11 compiler-domain -> team:compiler (EBPF-C-PROFILE, HOPACSOTA, HOPACSOTA-P3, RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION, EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION, EDITION3-TEST-SYMBOL, FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS, SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES, MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER, CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE, LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION); GRANULAR-STATS -> team:kit; cross-lane coordination items ORG-SCRIPTS-HOME, CODESPACES-BUTTON-ALL-REPOS, GRANULAR-HEAL, GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT -> team:main (work no single lane owns = this item charter). REMAINING 24 = single-lane shares of actively-grooming lanes: kit 18, compiler 5, tibia 1 - left for them per the per-lane canonicalization pattern (note 16). STALE (>48h) NOT swept: list all --json leaves last_note EMPTY (compiler finding) so a 48h filter needs per-team calls; and now every orphan is routed to a team, stale grooming is distributed to each lane via the user 48h rule. NEXT GROOM: nudge kit/compiler/tibia to canonicalize their 24; then the sweep is structurally clean. Trend 108 -> 24.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_26 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 26u32, "2026-11-04T06:00Z", "main", "11-04 02:0x main SWEEP: 1444 tasks; Active with no note in 48 h: 0 (every lane groomed at 00:1x under the new 48 h rule). Open items with no team tag: 24 -> 0. Each got an owner by its existing tags, old tags kept (edit tags replaces the list): team:kit for 17 (EOIE-IDIOMATIC-CORE, EOIE-ZED-SWEEP, EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL, EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP, TODO-AS-SOURCE, EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION, EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET, EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY, EOIE-PAGES-VERIFY, EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW, EOIE-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA, KIT-RENAME, EOIE-HAND-CRATE-UNITS, IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE, KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS, KIT-STANDARDS-ENTRY-SUBDIRS, KIT-VERBS-SCOPE); team:compiler for 5 (RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE, EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC, C-SHIM-RESERVE-REALLOC-ALIAS, SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD, SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST); team:tibia for TIBIA-1-0-DOD; team:resilience for DELETE-ROOT-GUARD (recursive-delete safety is durability work). Trend: untagged 24 -> 0, stale Active 0.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_27 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 27u32, "2026-11-04T06:58Z", "resilience", "11-04 02:5x resilience ORPHAN SWEEP COMPLETE: live orphans = 0 (trend 108 -> 24 -> 0). After my main-53 canonicalization + 16 multi-lane primary-owner assignments, the other lanes finished their single-lane shares (kit 18 + compiler 5 + tibia 1) - the per-lane lead-by-example pattern (note 16) worked fleet-wide. EVERY live backlog item now carries a team: tag = routable to an owner. The orphan dimension of this recurring sweep is at target (0); the stale (>48h) dimension is each lane via the user 48h rule. RECURRING: re-check orphan count each groom; a new bare-lane-tag item should be canonicalized to team:X by its lane on creation (or swept here).")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_28 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 28u32, "2026-11-04T07:00Z", "resilience", "11-04 03:0x resilience STALE DIMENSION also at target: fleet stale-Active (>48h) = 0 across all 8 teams (149 Active total: main 46, kit 25, fiscal 24, compiler 16, tibia 14, x 10, resilience 8, x-dumps 6). Trend: stale ~249-256 (earlier run) -> 0 now - the user 48h rule + lane grooming cleared the stale-Active backlog. BOTH sweep dimensions now at target: orphans=0 (all routable to a team) + stale-Active=0 (all noted <48h). Backlog hygiene excellent. METHOD NOTE: measure stale via PER-TEAM list --json (last_note is populated there); list ALL --json returns empty last_note so would false-flag all 149 as stale. RECURRING: re-measure both each groom.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_29 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 29u32, "2026-11-04T11:56Z", "main", "11-04 09:1x main SWEEP (box): parentless erl/beam/dotnet/pwsh/cargo/rustc/zig/lean = 1, pid 17768, the scheduled profile-mirror.ps1 (kit schedule) in its scp upload since 07:20. A Task Scheduler launch has no live parent by design, and it is resilience's mirror lane, so it is NOT an orphan and was left running. main's stopped box kernel gate (07:2x) left no children (checked by its private MIX_BUILD_PATH). europa: main's scratch dirs (main-toml-dry, main-scope-draft) removed. NODE: job host up since 00:28 with 6 supervised jobs; new starts fail under 100% CPU (\"supervisor never recorded its pid\"), reported to kit by main with tibia's evidence.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_30 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 30u32, "2026-11-04T16:18Z", "main", "11-04 12:2x main ROUND (agile list --json census): open 630 (Active 132); no team tag 0 (was 4 on 11-01); Active untouched 48 h: 5, all main's (TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL, HTML-REPO, BLOG, MIRROR-FRESHNESS-GUARD, YED-DIAGRAMS) -> Planned at their progress with a note; hidden behind Blocked/Paused deps: 15. Fixed 2 wrong deps: HTML-PARITY no longer waits on HTML-CLI (that is Blocked only on the user's push; parity needs no CI), CELLS-MOUNT-CLEANUP-HARDEN now follows CELLS-PORT-RUNNER-MOUNTS (x's 11-02 groom absorbed it there) instead of the unrelated PROFILE-WIPE-RECOVERY. Left on purpose: UPSTREAM-* (user-blocked post), WOW-* (wow after tibia), TIBIA-GAME-HANDSHAKE (tibia's paused client), x/x-dumps/kit chains whose blockers carry their own notes. Census trap: deps \"-\" is the no-deps placeholder, not a dangling dep (20 false hits). TREND: ownerless 4 -> 0; hidden 4 (11-01) -> 15 (more paused lanes).")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_31 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 31u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "main", "11-04 22:2x main STAR EXCHANGE (all 8 lanes, user request) merged into 7 themes, broadcast to every lane: A silent failures hid real causes (tail logs, Select -Last, OOM 137/143, Cells timeouts read as reds, xargs+apostrophe, vacuous negative controls) -> full logs to files, 0/timeout/no-output = VOID; B lib lands fail on the notebook round trip (x, tibia, main) -> round-trip every draft before its proof (LIB-LAND-FAILURE-DIAGNOSTICS); C shared machines ran out (node C: 0 GB from stage/regen dirs; europa RAM OOM killing k3s pods; two compile windows racing swaps) -> delete dirs when noted, disk+RAM checks, systemd memory caps, per-file restores, swap mutex (NODE-ALWAYS-FED); D proofs proving less than they seem (identity vs correctness, idealized guard shapes, layout before parity, size every inliner, probe across backends, control+draft in one window); E script traps (parse-gated launches, heredoc escapes, hand-typed clocks, scheduled-task full paths, kit job wait without pipes, self-matching kill patterns, module names build/main/lib); F clocks (box date is UTC, local UTC-4: never date -u); G trains (TRAIN21-26 red; installs stalled behind trains). Asks routed: kit 5, compiler 2, main 3 (gate blocked state on AUTONOMOUS-STOP-GATE, disk alarm on NODE-ALWAYS-FED, land diagnostics), lib owners 1 (natural merge in list_sort), all node users 2 (sweep remote-stage, audit scheduled tasks), user 1 (Cloud GPU capacity).")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_32 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 32u32, "2026-11-05T14:23Z", "main", "11-05 10:23 main ORPHAN SWEEP of the node's C:\\home\\remote-stage (x reported 12.3 GB free and a BEAM disk_almost_full at 09:54): deleted every stage dir with no write in 48 h AND no running process or running/queued kit job naming it (junctions unlinked first, never followed): 7.37 GB, ~90 dirs from 10-12..11-02 (lib-gate-*, gadt6/7, gleamext*, fresh-1102, charcmp, identity, ...). Kept: backups (owner-sensitive, excluded by name), all dirs touched in the last 48 h, all referenced ones (fiscal-facet*, xqueue3, metrics, ...). Plus main's own 10 superseded main-ship-pack* stages (587 MB; pack49's kept as the undo set). Node C: 12.3 -> 20.5 GB free. Script: scratchpad stage-sweep.ps1 (dry run by default, -Apply to delete); a recurring version of this belongs with the 15 GB disk alarm.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_33 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 33u32, "2026-11-05T19:50Z", "main", "main 11-05 15:5x ROUND: backlog open 594 (Active 119), no team tag 0; Active untouched 48 h = 3, all main's (LEAN-BACKEND 30, POLYGLOT-RETIRE 70, DIALECT-IDENTITY 30) -> Planned with notes. POLYGLOT-RETIRE's delete gate is wider than recorded: fc1943s.github scripts dot-source ../../polyglot/scripts/core.ps1 and The-Spiral-Language fsproj:56 falls back to polyglot (its n29). Box: 1 parentless process = the scheduled profile-mirror (not an orphan). NODE DISK: 20.5 GB free at 10:23 -> 13.2 at 15:4x. Measuring by CreationTime as well as mtime (tar/-p copies keep old mtimes) traced it to C:\\home\\profile-mirror: 20 dead .tgz.part uploads (6.82 GB), each cut at the 1800 s scp bound. Deleted after checking (none written in 2 h, no recent scp/sshd): node 13.2 -> 20.0 GB. Root cause routed to resilience with profile-mirror.ps1:124 (the .part delete is a 15 s ssh during saturation) and :131/:154 (retention lists *.tgz only). 20 timeouts vs 18 finished uploads since 11-03.")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_34 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 34u32, "2026-11-05T21:31Z", "main", "main 11-05 17:3x EUROPA: /root/.cache/spiral-bin/compiler-installs held 30 *-src-before undo copies (pack15c..pack52, 1.7 GB), written by main's pack-chain before each europa build. Kept the newest 5 (pack48-52) as undo points and deleted 25 after checking: all match pack*-src-before, no systemd unit or script references them, no process uses them. europa / avail 24.5 -> 26.0 GB, dir 294 MB. europa load 0.3, 74% disk. pack-chain should prune these itself like the node ship stages (next pack-chain edit).")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_35 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 35u32, "2026-11-06T00:36Z", "main", "main 11-05 20:4x END-OF-RUN SWEEP, main's own leftovers on every machine: codespaces 0 (all 7 of today's deleted by their drivers). europa: no /root/main-* scratch; compiler-installs holds exactly the 5 kept undo copies (pack48-52), and pack-chain now prunes to 5 itself. Node: removed main's 11-04 stages main-mix-build, -2, -3, -4 and main-toml-lib (each checked: no process naming it, no reparse points inside); kept main-ship-pack52-20261105-150829 (the current pack's undo set); my probe scripts in C:\\home are gone; node free 15 GB at 20:4x under 5 lanes' jobs. Box: no main processes left besides this session; scratch scripts worth keeping are in the kit drafts store (REC-MARKER-HEAL, MD-LINE-LIMIT-120, CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS).")
inl note_of_main_orphan_sweep_36 () = Note ("MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP", 36u32, "2026-11-06T10:08Z", "main", "main 11-06 06:0x groom (close-ratio pass): DONE. The 11-05 20:4x sweep left nothing of main's on any machine: 0 codespaces, europa holds exactly the 5 kept undo copies and pack-chain prunes to 5 by itself, node stages removed. The last 5% was the recurring check, which is now the end-of-run step in the run memory, not an open item.")
inl note_of_fiscal_wipe_damage_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-WIPE-DAMAGE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:39Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 02:10 fiscal: ACCOUNTING (sources: WIPE-REDO-LEDGER pass 1 of 10-29, the profile-wipe memory, fiscal's notes). LOST 7 working artefacts, all in %TEMP%/scratchpad (land payloads were in the repo and survived): (1) UBL-JSON transcoder draft: recovered, landed 10-29 07:28; (2) complements writer + any-content indirection drafts: recovered, landed; (3) ubl_json_documents generator draft: rebuilt 10-29 08:06, proven 22/22 (set-ublj); (4) edition-3 respell draft: rebuilt, then archived stale 10-31, re-derived 11-01 00:40 (FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND, node proof job-1793506463392 queued); (5) landing schedule + job ids: recovered from the node job list; (6) set-proof launcher: no longer needed (remote.ps1 node job start + node-side set-proof-c.ps1, which survived); (7) Delphi Cells -Px86_64 applier: rebuilt by main, the Delphi land is done. MEMORIES: every fiscal-relevant memory came back only partially and was rebuilt from backlog citations by 10-30 (verify-drafts-on-scratch-copies, window-coordination, land-drafts-in-the-repo, job-timeout-counts-queue-wait ...). REWORK estimate about 7 h of lane time (respell x2 about 3 h, ubl_json_documents about 2 h, schedule/launcher about 1 h, waits on re-proofs about 1 h), plus about 1 day of calendar drift on the edition-3 round. WEAKER PRACTICES the wipe exposed: drafts kept only in TEMP (now: drafts land in the repo or in backlog notes, never TEMP-only); an invented repo folder for programs (lib-programs/*.kit, removed 10-31; programs now live in notes); delete targets not guarded against a TEMP fallback (temp-root-falls-back-to-profile). OPEN MAKE-UP: only FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND's node proof. Done when it lands.")
inl note_of_fiscal_wipe_damage_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-WIPE-DAMAGE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T23:19Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 19:2x: DONE. The only open make-up, FISCAL-EDITION3-ROUND, closed today (all three gates met on CI run 41314140694). Practice check against the lesson \"drafts never TEMP-only\": the XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES lib drafts were scratchpad-only during the afternoon. They are now copied, hash-checked, to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\XSD-CROSS-NAMESPACE-NAMES (base + draft for xsd_resolve and xsd_emit, plus land-note.txt). Lib drafts cannot sit unstaged at their final path, because that would land them without the base guard.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeatureset_metrics_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:39Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 02:10 fiscal: BASELINE (apps/cells/spi/model_metrics.ps1, line-weighted over model files; TSVs in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\model-metrics-{fiscal-src,fiscal-generated,mega,tibia}-20261101.tsv; lib from model-metrics-lib-20261031.tsv). fiscal src (5 files, 1,228 code lines): M1 ret-annot 97.8 | M2 bare-param 0 | M3 GADT-case 1.6 | M4 indexed-union 1.8 | M5 existentials/kl 0 | M6 wild-arm 8.4 | M7 string-dispatch/kl 6.5 | M8 sentinel/kl 4.1 | M9 bool-opt/def 0.1 | M10 mut/kl 0 | M11 types/100defs 11.6. tibia (27 files, 7,085): 98.1 | 0 | 9.1 | 5.3 | 0.7 | 10.1 | 0 | 0 | 0.1 | 0 | 13.7. 5 mega samples (45,069): 98.6 | 0 | 86.7 | 72.3 | 30.8 | 2.5 | 0 | 0.2 | 0 | 0.2 | 36.4. Handcrafted lib: listm' M1 45 M2 52 M6 20 M11 4; base M1 83 M2 15 M6 13 M11 27. READING: fiscal annotates as well as everyone (M1), but its modeling is far below the mega samples and below tibia (GADT cases 1.6 vs 9.1 vs 86.7, no existentials, types/100defs 11.6), and it is the only one with stringly dispatch (M7 6.5) and -1 sentinels (M8 4.1). GENERATED units (44 files, 953,676 lines) are not measurable yet: their inl/let heads do not match the tool's ':=/:~' head pattern (defs read 0, M9 1747 is noise), so the tool needs the word spelling (or they get measured after the edition-3 respell of the emitter). TARGETS for the next pass: M7 -> 0 (family/region kinds as unions instead of strings: region_failures \"ubl_tr\"/\"dian\"/\"ro_cius\"), M8 -> 0 (region_failures returns -1i32 for an unreadable document -> option/result), M3/M11 toward tibia first (an indexed family_root by document kind), measured again after each change.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeatureset_metrics_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:09Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 02:20 fiscal: PASS 1 DONE in the tree (unstaged). fiscal src measured again (model_metrics -> %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\model-metrics-fiscal-src-20261101c.tsv): M7 string-dispatch/kl 6.5 -> 3.9, M8 sentinel/kl 4.1 -> 0.8, M11 types/100defs 11.6 -> 13.4 (M3 1.6 unchanged). CHANGES: (a) corpus_checks: region_failures, sample_failures and states_named return option i32 instead of -1i32 (callers compare = Some count / <> Some n); examples_fatal and examples_fatal_in collect a new union fatal_check = FatalFindings : i32 | NotOneRoot (fatal_text, with_finding) instead of i32 with -1 for an unreadable document; corpus_checks M8 0, M7 0. (b) generator: new union family_extra = Signed | CfdiComplements | NfeModelChecks and field family_root.extras on all 20 literals (7 Signed = exactly the old name-prefix set nfe/cte/mdfe/gtve/cl/cr; cfdi_v4_0 CfdiComplements; nfe_v4 NfeModelChecks); complements_of, signatures_of and unit_checks_of test has_extra instead of comparing module_name strings; starts_with_any removed. LEFT: generator.spi emitted text \"| [] => -1i64\" in the bench nth helper (changes every unit, so it goes with the next emitter change); the remaining M7 hits are = \"\" emptiness tests and constant-URI membership, false positives of the textual metric (tool tweak suggested to main: exclude = \"\"). PROOF: both files are in the queued edition-3 regen overlay (t1101 generator E187DAD2, corpus_checks 6071E31B); generated output must stay identical except cl_envio (the prefix fix).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeatureset_metrics_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 08:45 fiscal: QUALITY SPEC (groom round 4). DONE WHEN: pwsh spiral/apps/cells/spi/model_metrics.ps1 -Path C:\\home\\git\\fiscal\\src -Tsv <report> gives, line-weighted over the 5+ model files: M8 = 0; M7 <= 1.0 once the tool stops counting = \"\" emptiness tests (otherwise only those hits remain, listed); M3 GADT-case >= 9.1 and M11 types/100defs >= 13.7 (tibia''s numbers); each pass gated green with 0 generated drift.\nNEXT STEP (pass 2, after the e3 round): the generator''s family_root becomes indexed by its schema source. Today family.source : schema_source plus is_json_family / bench_measurable branches (generator.spi) decide per call which emitters apply.\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN: \":| ^ family_root src = | XsdFamily :: { ...; schema_path : string; known_defect : option string } -> family_root xsd_source | JsonFamily :: { ...; json_schema : string } -> family_root json_source\" with phantom \":! xsd_source = ()\" / \":! json_source = ()\". family_roots () returns \"list (exists s. family_root s)\" (an existential), and the emitters are prototypes per source (\":+ unit_checks family_root\"), so an XSD-only emitter cannot be applied to a JSON family. known_defect : option string becomes \":| defect = | NoDefect | KnownDefect : string\". MODEL: xsd.spi \"xsd sort form\" GADT; mega_zeta_structural_laboratory for existentials over indexed families.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeatureset_metrics_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS", 4u32, "2026-11-01T21:08Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-01 17:0x: model_metrics (spiral/apps/cells/spi/model_metrics.ps1 -Path <comma list> -Tsv) on fiscal src after today's typed canon. Columns: lines / defs / M3 GADT-case % / M4 indexed-union % / M7 string-dispatch per kloc / M8 sentinel per kloc / M9 bool-opt per def / M11 types per 100 defs.\ngenerator 939/59/20/0/3.2/1.1/0.08/5. canon 181/20/0/0/0/0/0.15/15. corpus_checks 222/29/0/0/0/0/0.14/21. corpus 39/4/0/0/25.6/0/0/25. fiscal_paths 12/2/0/0/83.3/0/0/0. lifecycle 68/7/29/33/0/0/0.29/100.\nReading: generator is the hot spot (lowest type density, 5 per 100 defs, and the only -1 sentinels). The per-family literal blocks (FISCAL-FAMILY-MANIFEST) and the bench -1 nth (FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH plan) are the next two moves. fiscal_paths and corpus have high M7 on tiny files: string-keyed env and path lookups, a single \"if string = ...\" each, so their per-kloc rate overstates. canon went from string id + bool group to two unions with M7=0 and M8=0.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeatureset_metrics_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS", 5u32, "2026-11-03T07:28Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 03:28 fiscal: GROOM (main 11-03 02:5x order 3): kept Active, not a duplicate of TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT (that is the cross-repo ratchet; this is fiscal's own M7/M8/M3/M11 targets). Tonight's movement is recorded there (n11 scoreboard 18 -> 23 types/100 defs, sentinels 0.8 -> 0, string fields 32 -> 8 %; n12 string-dispatch triage; n14 event_code). NEXT STEP: re-measure with model_metrics.ps1 on the committed tree after the user's commit, and drive the 9 remaining M7 hits (n12 list) down via the deferred lib round.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeatureset_metrics_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS", 6u32, "2026-11-03T19:19Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 15:19 fiscal: RE-MEASURED on today's tree (model_metrics.ps1 over the 12 fiscal src files, 2,047 lines, 206 defs; report in the session scratchpad model-metrics-fiscal-20261103.tsv). DoD: M8 sentinels 0 (MET); M3 GADT-case 21.3 % line-weighted, 22.5 % def-weighted (MET, target >= 9.1; document 100, p2p 100, families 50, lifecycle 29); M11 types/100 defs 27.9 line-weighted, 31.6 def-weighted (MET, target >= 13.7; was 13.4 on 11-01); M7 string dispatch 4.88/kl (NOT met as counted). M7 TRIAGE of all 11 regex hits: 7 are emptiness tests (canon:205, corpus:27, families:176, fiscal_paths:7, generator:515 (the deferred lib certificates sentinel), reports:157/158), 1 is a test assertion (lifecycle:149), 3 are real boundary parses of external text: canon:75 the XSD cardinality 'n', generator:69 two constant schema URIs, reports:52 build.ps1's 'not written' marker in examples.tsv. Without emptiness and test hits: 3 / 2.047 kl = 1.47/kl. Remaining moves: generator:515 with the lib round (string * option string), and reports:52 if build.ps1 ever writes the example verdict as its own column.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeatureset_metrics_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS", 7u32, "2026-11-04T05:20Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 01:20 fiscal: M7 move reports:52 DONE (in place; backups of reports.spi 182588EB and build.ps1 CEDAD279 in the session scratchpad): build.ps1 no longer smuggles a 'not written' marker into examples.tsv's free-text First column; it writes an explicit trailing Written column (1/0), and reports reads it as a bool (written_of: an integer, 0 = not written; 1 or a missing column = written, so older TSVs still read) and verdict_of takes (written : bool) (messages) -- no string-literal dispatch left on that path ('not written' remains only as the display label of NotWritten). Test tsv_rows_read_into_their_typed_columns extended (written_of 0/1/missing). PROVEN on europa pack27: --check exit 0 (8 s) and bodies exit 0 (11 s); all 3 reports bodies to Rust exit 0 incl. writes_the_ci_reports (reports.md written). Now reports 80F1F990, build.ps1 7FE74D33. M7 real boundary hits left: canon:75 (XSD cardinality 'n', the schema's own text format: legitimate), generator:69 (two constant schema URIs), generator:515 (the lib-round certificates sentinel) -> 2 movable, both in generator.spi, which waits for the CII/Facturae land.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeatureset_metrics_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS", 8u32, "2026-11-04T14:36Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 10:36 fiscal: M7 (string dispatch) 31 -> 11 (12.1 -> 4.24 /kl, model_metrics over fiscal src, 2,593 lines; M8 stays 0). sunat.spi (in place, unstaged): catalog 07 parsing no longer re-states the 18 codes in string-equality chains; taxed_of / exempt_of / unaffected_of = listm'::try_find_ over per-group case lists (taxed_cases / exempt_cases / unaffected_cases) compared through the exhaustive printer affectation_code, so each code is written ONCE (the try_find_ idiom canon.spi already uses). profiles.spi: exhaustive myinvois_version_text printer + myinvois_version_of via try_find_. PROVEN on europa pack27 with fiscal tests converted to lib ':#' form (a first run without the conversion was VACUOUS: zero test bodies ran, exposed by mutants passing): sunat 9 tests pass in Rust and the mutant (GravadoIvap dropped from taxed_cases) FAILS with 'every catalog 07 code parses and prints back'; profiles 4 tests pass and the mutant (V11 printed as 1.2) FAILS with 'MyInvois 1.1 has no customization'. The 11 left are boundary text checks, not dispatch: empty-string tests (path, from_env, declared, built, sha, certificates), canon's cardinality n, generator's two namespace URIs, sunat's 3-name line predicate. Rewriting those to dodge the regex would game the metric, so M7 counts as at its floor for real dispatch.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeatureset_metrics_9 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS", 9u32, "2026-11-04T21:30Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 17:30 fiscal: M7 movable hit #1 moved (generator, the 'two constant schema URIs'): is_declared_prefix no longer compares value = 'http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema' || value = '...xmldsig#'; it asks listm'::contains value (built_in_namespaces ()), a one-literal data def. Draft drafts\\fiscal\\GRANULAR-GENERATOR\\src\\generator.next6.spi CB407C1C (on the proven .next5, which it replaces), PROVEN on europa pack27: compile/rustc/generate, 69 units 0 differing vs the decode reference; GRANULAR with kit's ruling-aware counter 2/92 = 2.2%. Left movable: the lib-round certificates sentinel (certificates <> '' in write_certificates_unit) -- that '' comes from lib xsd_emit's generated_units_signed returning string * string, so moving it means an option return in lib (a lib change, after the land). M7 for fiscal src then = the boundary checks only.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeatureset_metrics_10 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS", 10u32, "2026-11-05T05:44Z", "fiscal", "CERTIFICATES SENTINEL MOVED (the last movable M7 hit, n of 11-04 17:30), 11-05 01:4x local. lib: drafts\\spiral\\FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS\\lib\\spiral\\xsd_emit.spi D00BE390, base xsd_emit.base-4E831159 = the XSD-FACET-TYPES draft. certificate_unit_text returns option string (None when nothing is certified, no longer \"\"), so units_of_parts / generated_units_with / staged_units / generated_units_signed / generated_units return string * option string. The two lib tests that read the certificates text now match on the option, with None an explicit failure where a pattern must certify. New test a_schema_without_patterns_has_no_certificates_unit. fiscal: drafts\\fiscal\\GRANULAR-GENERATOR\\src\\generator.next7.spi = .next6 + write_certificates_unit (certificates : option string) matching Some/None, its only consumer. model_metrics: generator M7 2 -> 0 per kl (.next6 492 lines -> .next7 493), M8 0 -> 0. Lib round trip canonical/fixpoint true. LANDS WITH: the lib sentinel draft and .next7 in ONE window (API change), after XSD-FACET-TYPES. PENDING (europa, load-gated, log sentinel-run-1793856809.log): lib --check + bodies, plus an identity proof of two full regens in one tree, facet lib + .next6 vs sentinel lib + .next7, which must be byte-identical; then a Rust run of the 3 certificate tests with a mutant ([] => Some \"\").")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeatureset_metrics_11 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS", 11u32, "2026-11-05T06:05Z", "fiscal", "Sentinel proof, 11-05 02:0x local, europa pack27: lib draft D00BE390 --check module + bodies exit 0 (base-decode). The 2-regen IDENTITY proof (facet lib + .next6 vs sentinel lib + .next7) is VOID: the first regen's rustc was OOM-killed (exit 137 after 1455 s; the same step took 196 s an hour earlier) while europa ran at load 36-50 under another lane's compiler and kernel reclaim threads. I stopped my run and removed its tree. Rerun when europa is calm, or on the node after the decode re-gate. The facet-companion generator draft (on .next7) type-checks with the sentinel lib (exit 0, bodies 0), so write_certificates_unit (option string) matches the lib API. Live src generator 5DB916E8 + sentinel lib gives the expected Unification failure (option string vs string) at write_family_modules, which shows the lib draft and .next7 can only land in one window.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeatureset_metrics_12 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS", 12u32, "2026-11-05T09:43Z", "fiscal", "IDENTITY REGENS QUEUED on the node, 11-05 05:5x local: job-1793871773349-fiscal-identity runs after job-1793868130086 (nfe_v4) with a 7200000 ms cap counted from its own start (kept independent of nfe_v4's 3 h cell per widen-queued-job-amend-timeout). Script C:\\home\\remote-stage\\node-identity.ps1 176DAFF6, inputs in fiscal-identity-in, hash-verified on the node. Four arms, each in its own tree (core from VS Code Plugin\\core, a lib copy with xml B6EED09B + xml_codec 5694B36A + the arm's xsd_emit, fiscal src from the box, the corpus as a read-only junction; junctions removed as links before any recursive delete, and corpus file count plus mirror xsd_emit hash checked start vs end). (1) facet 4E831159 + .next6 vs sentinel D00BE390 + .next7, the certificates sentinel identity, after two europa VOIDs; (2) toml base 2A9BE918 + families F1CF3DDF vs FISCAL-GRANULAR-280 generator 40DE71DF + families 99A8339B, the repo_path / row-split / documents-as-data identity. Each arm compiles the generator plus the harness main, rustc -O, runs, and hashes every generated file. PENDING.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typefeatureset_metrics_13 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPEFEATURESET-METRICS", 13u32, "2026-11-05T11:29Z", "fiscal", "SENTINEL IDENTITY PROVEN, 11-05 07:28 local, node job-1793871773349: arm facet (xsd_emit 4E831159 + generator .next6 CB407C1C + families F1CF3DDF) vs arm sentinel (xsd_emit D00BE390 + .next7 D6F663A7 + the same families), each a full regen (compile, rustc -O, run exit 36 = family count). 138 files each (69 units + manifests), 0 DIFFERING. The certificates option string (None instead of the \"\" sentinel) is output-neutral. With the lib --check, the Rust-run certificate tests, a mutant ([] => Some \"\") and a round trip, the sentinel move is proven; it lands with the facet window (lib D00BE390 together with generator .next7 / .next7t). M7 for fiscal src: generator 2 -> 0 per kl.")
