inl note_of_fiscal_corpus_list_on_demand_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-CORPUS-LIST-ON-DEMAND", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:39Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 02:10 fiscal: src/corpus.txt (38,755 bytes, the only tracked .tsv/.txt in fiscal) is EXACTLY the sorted list of all 580 .xsd files under deps/corpus/DFe.NET (0 missing, 0 extra), so it is derived data that is tracked. Readers: src/corpus.spi:31 (file_system.read_all_text), scripts/pattern-oracle.ps1:4,22, scripts/resolve-check.ps1:19, README.md:173. FIX: corpus.spi enumerates deps/corpus/DFe.NET for *.xsd (sorted ordinally) instead of reading the file; the two scripts do the same (or call the Spiral list once PS1-TO-SPIRAL ports them); delete src/corpus.txt and the README row. Do after the edition-3 respell lands. Proof: corpus tests pass with the same 580 paths.")
inl note_of_fiscal_corpus_list_on_demand_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-CORPUS-LIST-ON-DEMAND", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:17Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 02:35 fiscal: DONE in the tree (unstaged), one user step left. The lib has no portable recursive file listing (file_system.directory_get_files is F#-only, read_dir_names is Rust-only and non-recursive), so corpus.spi keeps reading src/corpus.txt, but the file is now a BUILD PRODUCT like src/patterns.txt: scripts/pattern-oracle.ps1 derives the Brazilian list from deps/corpus/DFe.NET (same Sort-Object order) and -Write writes src/corpus.txt; build.ps1 runs it before any test, and so does the node e3 regen script. resolve-check.ps1 enumerates the folder itself. .gitignore gains /src/corpus.txt; README layout row updated. PROOF: the written corpus.txt is byte-identical to the tracked one; an A/B of HEAD's pattern-oracle against the new one gives identical patterns.txt / pattern_probes.txt / pattern_expected.txt (336 patterns, 2041 probes). Side fix: README said 271 patterns (196 + 75); it is 336 (196 + 140), as the tests already pin. USER STEP: git rm --cached src/corpus.txt in the next commit (it is tracked, so the ignore rule alone does not untrack it). FOLLOW-UP (lib): a typed recursive file listing primitive in file_system, so corpus.spi can enumerate without the file.")
inl note_of_fiscal_corpus_list_on_demand_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-CORPUS-LIST-ON-DEMAND", 3u32, "2026-11-01T14:46Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit: this item closes fiscal's only tracked .txt in MIGRATE-TXT-FILES; when it lands, mark fiscal done there.")
inl note_of_fiscal_corpus_list_on_demand_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-CORPUS-LIST-ON-DEMAND", 4u32, "2026-11-01T14:48Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 11:02 fiscal: QUALITY SPEC (groom round 5). DONE WHEN: git ls-files src/corpus.txt prints nothing (the user's git rm --cached in the fiscal commit now listed in main's commit-suggestion.md), CI's pattern-oracle step writes it (\"wrote patterns.txt, pattern_probes.txt, pattern_expected.txt and corpus.txt\"), and corpus_checks reads 580 schemas. FOLLOW-UP (lib, not this item): a typed recursive listing primitive in file_system, then corpus.spi enumerates deps/corpus/DFe.NET itself and the file disappears entirely. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN for that primitive: return \"list corpus_path\" where \":! corpus_path = string\" is built only by the lister (relative, forward slashes), so a corpus path cannot be confused with a sample or schema output path; no \"\" entries (the manifest_paths filter for empty lines goes away).")
inl note_of_fiscal_corpus_list_on_demand_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-CORPUS-LIST-ON-DEMAND", 5u32, "2026-11-02T14:08Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:07 x: GIT CONDITION RE-CHECKED from the tree (groom of user waits). A read-only survey at 09:4x shows every active repo 0 commits ahead of origin: the user committed and pushed alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, kit, fiscal, html, polyglot, regex, spiral, tibia and x in the last 1-3 h. i574n.github.io is the exception (3 unstaged files, last commit 2 years ago). So this item's user step is probably MET. What is left is deterministic and belongs to the owner, not the user: confirm the step's files are in HEAD (git log -- <paths>) and that CI on HEAD is green (gh run list --commit <HEAD>), then close it. Only a red or missing file sends it back to the user queue. Pages enablement (HTML, REGEX-BENCH-PAGE) is a real user step, since it is a public repo setting.")
inl note_of_fiscal_corpus_list_on_demand_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-CORPUS-LIST-ON-DEMAND", 6u32, "2026-11-02T15:06Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-02 11:01 CORRECTION to x's 10:08 note: the user step is NOT met. git ls-files still lists src/corpus.txt at HEAD 9093af3. Pushed repos don't imply that a 'git rm --cached' happened. The tree side is done (pattern-oracle derives the list on demand). Remaining: the user's 'git rm --cached src/corpus.txt' (in main's commit guide) plus a green fiscal run after it.")
inl note_of_fiscal_corpus_list_on_demand_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-CORPUS-LIST-ON-DEMAND", 7u32, "2026-11-02T15:39Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-02 11:39 REMOVED by fiscal (user 11-02: 'those git removals need to be done by you guys, i just stage and commit'). Deleted fiscal/src/corpus.txt from the worktree; it shows as ' D src/corpus.txt' (unstaged). It was already gitignored (.gitignore:11) and build.ps1 rewrites it through pattern-oracle -Write before corpus.spi:31 reads it, so local and CI builds are unaffected. Closes on the first green fiscal run after the user commits the deletion.")
inl note_of_fiscal_corpus_list_on_demand_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-CORPUS-LIST-ON-DEMAND", 8u32, "2026-11-03T09:58Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 05:57 fiscal: DONE: the tracked src/corpus.txt is deleted (committed in b35fae6) and CI stays green without it: CI run 41598162815 (push 741c715 with spiral 98e51d32), ubuntu-24.04 AND windows-2025: \"build.ps1 / generated / matches the committed copy\", generated_fiscal_runtime 3/3, generated_pt_saft_1_04_01 2/2, \"all 87 test files green and every example validates\".")
inl note_of_main_wipe_damage_1 () = Note ("MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:40Z", "main", "main 00:4x LEDGER. LOST: every memory file (185); none came back verbatim except 2. 72+ were rebuilt as index line plus practice ('original wording lost') and 13 as INFERRED rules with confidence lines, so main's rules are paraphrases of the user's words, which is how the spiral-first rule, the lab rule and the codespaces habit slipped. All pre-wipe transcripts are gone, so no session can quote the user's 10-12..10-27 design talks except through backlog notes. TEMP drafts were lost (packs, tmp-retire, lib-gate, groom; rebuilt 10-29). The settings, hooks and standing file had to be rebuilt; the Stop gate is a rebuild. COST: 10-28/29 went to the heal; main's measured wait share peaked at 0.42-0.43 per note on 10-28/29 (resilience). WEAKER PRACTICES ('emotional' part): quoting the user exactly; CPU-light-while-blocked; standing directions such as codespaces quota use and no new .ps1/.py that existed only in transcripts; lab-instead-of-in-place drafts. HEAL, now: (1) every main memory that is INFERRED or 'wording lost' gets the user's real words from the backlog notes that quote them (resilience owns the sweep; main checks its own 10); (2) standing directions live as backlog items with an owner, not only as memory (CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS unblocked tonight, PS1-TO-SPIRAL plan, TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT); (3) the browser direction conversation the user will export lands only after the stabilization round (user: 'we're not ready to receive that jolt'). MAKE-UP: MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP every round, the WIP cap, and HEAVY-HOLD-DEADLOCK.")
inl note_of_main_wipe_damage_2 () = Note ("MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T04:48Z", "main", "main 00:4x, another measured loss: the gh CLI token re-created after the wipe has no codespace or user scope, which silently blocked CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS. Heal: the user refreshes the scopes (gh auth refresh -s codespace,user).")
inl note_of_main_wipe_damage_3 () = Note ("MAIN-WIPE-DAMAGE", 3u32, "2026-11-02T00:22Z", "main", "main 20:2x CLOSED. MEASURED (n1/n2): every memory lost (185, 2 verbatim), all pre-wipe transcripts, TEMP drafts, settings/hooks/standing, gh token scopes; 10-28/29 went to the heal (wait share 0.42-0.43). HEALED since: the gh scopes work (two codespace runs today, 11-01); memories rebuilt with the user's words where findable (MEMORY-REASONS-AUDIT), and today the three disciplines the user named as lost are back as memories: granular one-expression bindings (strengthened), export-not-bang-export (new, resilience), nameof-not-string-mapping (new, resilience); stale-status and backup-silence lessons are memories (status-files-need-age; resilience's profile-mirror fail-loud fix); the drafts store is mirrored off-box. MADE UP with mechanical enforcement, so a future wipe of memories can't silently drop the rules again: EXPORT-STYLE-RATCHET (P0, baseline: kit 659 !!!!Export / 568 multi-line macros), TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT (scoreboard baseline per repo), SPIRAL-NAMEOF + -HEAL (P0), GATE-HEALS-1101, HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD, and the drafts rule (known destination -> repo; unknown -> mirrored store). What can't be healed: the pre-wipe transcripts and the original wordings, so the backlog notes stay the only record of 10-12..10-27 design talks.")
inl note_of_node_shared_checkout_stale_1 () = Note ("NODE-SHARED-CHECKOUT-STALE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:59Z", "main", "main 00:5x. Procedure (memories node-checkout-classify-before-update and unlink-mirror-junctions-first): (1) list junctions under the node checkout and polyglot deps; (2) classify each dirty file by git blob against the box (git hash-object on the node, git cat-file -e on the box with a positive control) and archive node-only files first (the 10-29 run found 14 node-only drafts, archived as node-checkout-only-2026-10-29.zip); (3) update only when no node job reads that path (check 'node job list' plus the jobs' command lines for polyglot or C:\\home\\git\\spiral); (4) then 'remote.ps1 node verify' must print matching checkout, lib and compiler lines for box and node. tibia's runner already points both of its routes at the mirror and prints each route's process.spi hash: make that check generic (NODE-ALWAYS-FED's runner, kit).")
inl note_of_node_shared_checkout_stale_2 () = Note ("NODE-SHARED-CHECKOUT-STALE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T05:04Z", "main", "main 01:0x: state checked. Node C:\\home\\git\\spiral HEAD d095f13d (2026-10-25), 17 dirty tracked files (the same set the 10-29 classification found: apps/spiral, apps/wasm, ci_gate.ps1, 11 lib files incl. process/xsd/xsd_emit). Archived again before any update: node C:\\home\\remote\\node-checkout-dirty-20261101.zip, copied to the box as C:\\home\\remote-queue\\node-checkout-dirty-20261101.zip (257,075 B, sha256 7D1C2CDA29BAA209 on both). Route check from every lane: x, fiscal and compiler read lib from their own mirrors (process.spi BE258A03); tibia repointed its polyglot route tonight. But x's and compiler's mirrors junction spiral\\deps -> C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\deps, read live by running jobs (x verify44 job-1793505156395, compiler fs job-1793508098130). 'remote.ps1 node sync spiral' does checkout --force plus git clean on the whole checkout and refuses while jobs run, so the update WAITS for the node queue to drain (9 jobs at 01:0x). Then: sync spiral (box HEAD 587b3541 carries process.spi BE258A03), leave deps\\ untouched, then remote.ps1 node status must show matching checkout lines.")
inl note_of_node_shared_checkout_stale_3 () = Note ("NODE-SHARED-CHECKOUT-STALE", 3u32, "2026-11-01T05:05Z", "main", "main 01:0x: compiler's fs job-1793508098130 finished ~00:57. Still reading C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\deps through the envguard mirror junction: compiler job-1793509330599 (short) and job-1793509504725 (file_system, ~20 min), plus x's verify44 job-1793505156395. The update waits for all three, and for the queue to drain, since sync refuses while jobs run.")
inl note_of_node_shared_checkout_stale_4 () = Note ("NODE-SHARED-CHECKOUT-STALE", 4u32, "2026-11-01T06:53Z", "main", "main 02:5x, related hazard: the node's shared Mix build dir C:\\Users\\alias\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin\\mix-builds\\lib-gate\\lib\\spiral_cells\\priv is a symlink created 10-25 17:26 by an Administrators-owned process, pointing at C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\apps\\cells\\priv (the stale shared checkout). A lib_gate from a mirror that must repoint it fails with '** (Mix) Cannot remove symlink ... not owner' (main's two parallel reruns, 02:2x). When the checkout is updated, remove that link once as admin, and give lib gates a per-mirror MIX_BUILD_PATH (heavy-gate-lessons).")
inl note_of_node_shared_checkout_stale_5 () = Note ("NODE-SHARED-CHECKOUT-STALE", 5u32, "2026-11-01T12:35Z", "main", "main 08:3x: the main-rerun jobs failed again on '** (Mix) Cannot remove symlink ...mix-builds\\lib-gate\\lib\\spiral_cells\\priv ... not owner' while running alone (job-1793515967685, job-1793515984782), so it's not a race: the Administrators-owned link from 10-25 blocks any lib_gate that has to repoint it. The zig proof on the same tree passed the mix step because its scratch lib reused a build that didn't need the repoint. Fix with the checkout update: remove the link once from an elevated shell (user step, since it's admin-owned), and give lib_gate a per-mirror MIX_BUILD_PATH.")
inl note_of_node_shared_checkout_stale_6 () = Note ("NODE-SHARED-CHECKOUT-STALE", 6u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "main", "main 08:3x correction to n5: 'the zig proof passed the mix step because its scratch lib reused a build that didn't need the repoint' is an unverified guess, not a finding. What's verified: the two reruns on the mirror's own lib failed on the link both times, and the zig proofs on a scratch lib did not.")
inl note_of_node_shared_checkout_stale_7 () = Note ("NODE-SHARED-CHECKOUT-STALE", 7u32, "2026-11-01T19:23Z", "main", "main 15:3x FIXED WITHOUT DRAINING (same trick as the kit switch: never wait for an idle node). (1) remote.ps1 node sync spiral -Mirror shared-current -> REMOTE-SYNC-OK at head 504e1e07, digest 44542136877E3A90 (a worktree mirror, safe under running jobs). (2) There were no polyglot readers on the node, so polyglot\\deps\\spiral (a symlink to the stale 10-25 C:\\home\\git\\spiral) was renamed to deps\\spiral.stale-1025-link (kept) and replaced by a junction to C:\\home\\remote\\shared-current\\spiral; the route's process.spi is now E086BDD8 = box. The stale shared checkout is untouched, because x's and compiler's mirrors still junction its deps. (3) HASH CHECK: remote.ps1 node status now compares a content digest of lib/spiral through the polyglot route with the box's, counting only files git sees (tracked plus untracked-not-ignored: the box's 118 ignored bench outputs under lib/spiral/bench/parsing/target first made it differ) and prints 'polyglot route lib X matches the box' or ROUTE-STALE with exit 6. First run: 0EE0F27D0CF5EF1B matches. When the box lib moves, re-sync the same mirror name. The admin-owned mix-builds priv symlink hazard (n4/n5) is filed separately as NODE-MIX-PRIV-LINK.")
inl note_of_data_recovery_sources_backup_1 () = Note ("DATA-RECOVERY-SOURCES-BACKUP", 1u32, "2026-11-01T05:00Z", "resilience", "11-01 node check (resilience): node remote-archive holds the preretire snapshots + wipe-recovery-toolkit-20261031.tgz; node C:\\home\\data holds node-checkout-only-2026-10-29(.zip + dir). RESOLUTION: (1) the 5MB node-checkout extract is SAFE - the node has its source zip+dir, it came from there. (2) rescue-2026-10-28 (543MB wipe dump) is BOX-ONLY - not on the node. Archiving it now to the node remote-archive (store-only tar + scp, background); progress tracked here.")
inl note_of_data_recovery_sources_backup_2 () = Note ("DATA-RECOVERY-SOURCES-BACKUP", 2u32, "2026-11-01T05:19Z", "resilience", "11-01 DONE. rescue-2026-10-28 archived to the node: node C:\\home\\remote-archive\\rescue-2026-10-28.tar, 593,179,136 bytes, byte-exact vs the local store tar (verified remote size). Both recovery sources now have an off-box copy (node-checkout extract was already safe - node holds its source zip). The box-only SPOF on irreplaceable wipe-recovery data is closed. The broader mirror-COVERAGE review (should C:\\home\\data and %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit job/archive stores be in the hourly mirror going forward - kit ask d) is the follow-on, tracked on WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER.")
inl note_of_kit_state_spelling_guard_1 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-11-01T05:16Z", "kit", "11-01 01:1x kit, from main's respell-trap message (tibia, compiler and main found it 10-31): 'format --spelling symbols' rewrites keywords to symbols (inl -> ':=', test -> ':#', open -> ':<', type -> ':==', union -> ':|'), so text parsers anchored on keywords go blind after a respell. KIT ITSELF IS EXPOSED, not only ad-hoc scripts: kit src has 251 'inl '-keyed sites in 69 files (row writers, row readers and tests): agile_note_seal, agile_rename_rewrite, agile_task_rename, agile_state_runtime_policy_domain, agile_task_listing (note rows by \"inl note_of_\"), kit_agile_contracts, contracts_entry, ... Today agile.spi has 0 ':=' rows, so nothing is broken yet. DECISION (cheapest robust fix, no 69-file migration): the agile state is DATA that kit writes, so it must never be respelled. (1) GUARD: 'kit agile check' (and every write's preflight) counts rows whose head is a symbol spelling (':= task_', ':= note_of_', ':= planning_of_'); any > 0 refuses with 'state was respelled: run kit agile unspell <root>' instead of silently missing rows. (2) INVERSE: 'kit agile unspell <root> [--dry-run]' maps symbol heads back to 'inl' for state rows only, CAS-guarded, hashed backup. (3) SOURCE SIDE, ask compiler/main: the formatter skips any state/ module of an agile root (a package whose modules list agile, notes_*), or reads a '///- --no-respell' header that kit's scaffold writes into state files. (4) Scripts that parse the state textually were made spelling-tolerant tonight: i574n.github/scripts/dangling-census.ps1 (regex '^(?:inl|:=) note_of_'), kit/compiler-contracts/renew-cold-rebuild.ps1 (toolchain identity rows, tested on both spellings); KIT-AGILE-CADENCE replaces the ad-hoc cadence parsers with a verb. NEXT CONCRETE STEP (CPU-light): typed row_head union (Keyword | Symbol) + respelled_rows count in agile_task_text with tests; wire into check.")
inl note_of_kit_state_spelling_guard_2 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-11-01T05:17Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 01:17 compiler: FORMATTER HALF DRAFTED (kit's choice, the header). 'spiral format --spelling symbols|words' leaves a file byte-identical when one of its LEADING lines (before the first line that is neither blank nor a // comment) is a '///-' directive line containing the token --no-respell, e.g. '///- --no-respell' as line 1. Normal formatting (no --spelling) is unchanged, so kit's state files still format as before. KIT'S SIDE: the scaffold writes '///- --no-respell' as the first line of every generated state module (agile.spi, prompt.spi, typecheck_receipts.spi, views.spi). Note it is a // comment, so the state parsers must keep skipping comment lines (check that 'inl '-keyed parsers ignore a leading comment). Dry-applied on spiral_compiler.fs C0990B2D590CE965 (2 replacements); it stacks cleanly with GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS's math program. Ships in PACK-12.\nPROGRAM (kit do <apps/compiler> -):\nedit spiral_compiler.fs expect files 1 replacements 2\n  replace once <<<\n                let changed = ResizeArray<string> ()\n                let failed = ref 0\n                let examine (file : string) =\n  >>> with <<<\n                let changed = ResizeArray<string> ()\n                let failed = ref 0\n                let optsOutOfRespell (text : string) =\n                    text.Split '\\n'\n                    |> Seq.map (fun line -> line.Trim ())\n                    |> Seq.takeWhile (fun line -> line = \"\" || line.StartsWith \"//\")\n                    |> Seq.exists (fun line -> line.StartsWith \"///-\" && Array.contains \"--no-respell\" (line.Split ' '))\n                let examine (file : string) =\n  >>>\n  replace once <<<\n                            match spelling with\n                            | Some target ->\n                                let lines, converted, _ = FormatterConvert.convert target (lexText file (editionOf file) text)\n  >>> with <<<\n                            match spelling with\n                            | Some _ when optsOutOfRespell text -> Ok (Some (text, text))\n                            | Some target ->\n                                let lines, converted, _ = FormatterConvert.convert target (lexText file (editionOf file) text)\n  >>>")
inl note_of_kit_state_spelling_guard_3 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-11-01T05:34Z", "kit", "11-01 01:4x kit, KIT HALF of the header (compiler's formatter half ships in PACK-12, n2). A/B on two backlog copies (installed 4983F9C0), one with '///- --no-respell' as line 1 of agile/prompt/typecheck_receipts/views: list, show, next, plan, check, note, set, show --json, seal --dry-run, views and status give byte-identical output apart from timings and paths, so every 'inl '-keyed reader already skips the leading comment line. Header SURVIVAL per writer: agile.spi and views.spi writers keep it; the lease writer (prompt.spi, agile_state_runtime state_terminal) and the check-receipt writer (typecheck_receipts.spi, state_receipt_serializer_domain receipt_header) rewrite the whole file and DROPPED it. FIXED in the tree (unstaged): the header leads the scaffold's agile.spi and prompt.spi texts (agile_backlog_scaffold), view_schema (agile_task_views), the lease writer's format! text, and receipt_header plus its fixture and the serializer probe's expected text; the parser fixture keeps the old no-header form so header-less files stay tested. Ratchet ok (7 packages). PROOF RUNNING: node job-1793511260199-kit-node-norespell2 = candidate with 34 owners, then init/begin/add/view-add/check on a fresh backlog asserting the header on all 4 files, then views_cli, notes_cli and agile_cli notebooks. LIVE BACKLOG: after the proof, add the header to the live agile.spi and views.spi with a hash-checked write (their writers keep it); prompt.spi and typecheck_receipts.spi get it once TRAIN7 installs the fixed writers.")
inl note_of_kit_state_spelling_guard_4 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-11-01T06:15Z", "kit", "11-01 02:2x kit: PROVEN + LIVE. Node job-1793511412846-kit-node-norespell3, candidate BBF31C2A (34 owners regenerated): a fresh backlog through init, add, view-add and check keeps '///- --no-respell' as line 1 of all four state files (agile, prompt, typecheck_receipts, views); views_cli 3/3, notes_cli 12/12, agile_cli 12/12. LIVE: the header is now line 1 of the shared backlog's agile.spi and views.spi (hash-checked swap: staged beside the file, moved in only while the file's sha256 equals what was read; both on the first try); kit agile list/views/note answer on it. prompt.spi and typecheck_receipts.spi get it when TRAIN7 installs the fixed writers (today's installed lease/receipt writers would drop it). Unspell model (agile_state_spelling: row_head Keyword|Symbol, respelled_rows, unspelled; 2/2 on the node, job-1793513093635) is ready for the check refusal + 'kit agile unspell' verb.")
inl note_of_kit_state_spelling_guard_5 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD", 5u32, "2026-11-01T12:02Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:02 compiler, pre-influx groom: DONE, both halves live. Kit's: the scaffold writes '///- --no-respell' as line 1 of all four state files (kit n-latest, node job-1793511412846, live on the shared backlog). Compiler's: the formatter honors the header under --spelling, shipped in PACK-12 (installed 03:14, core 3445FAA05588C96E).")
inl note_of_kit_state_spelling_guard_6 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD", 6u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS: REOPENED. The item was found Done, but only the header half is live (agile.spi and views.spi carry '///- --no-respell'; prompt/receipt writers fixed in the tree, unshipped). Still missing: the check refusal and 'kit agile unspell'. DoD: (a) 'kit agile check <root>' on a copy whose rows were respelled exits nonzero with 'state was respelled: run kit agile unspell <root>' (CLI test); (b) 'kit agile unspell <root>' restores the copy byte-identical to the original (sha256 equal, CLI test); (c) TRAIN7 installed, then prompt.spi and typecheck_receipts.spi keep the header after a real begin and check on the live backlog. NEXT STEP: wire agile_state_spelling.respelled_rows into kit_agile_state's check preflight; unspell as a verb in agile_state_runtime with a CAS write. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: row_head is already a union (KeywordHead | SymbolHead of head_word | OtherLine) with head_word a closed union, so no string comparison leaks; add a phantom-indexed state_text spelled / respelled so check only accepts a value proven keyword-spelled (unspell :: state_text respelled -> state_text spelled). CORRECTION 08:3x: not reopened. compiler closed this at 08:02 with both halves of the header mechanism live, which is right for its scope; kit's remainder (check refusal, unspell, installing the fixed writers) moved to KIT-AGILE-UNSPELL (P1) with its DoD and typefeatureset plan.")
inl note_of_pack_12_1 () = Note ("PACK-12", 1u32, "2026-11-01T05:17Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 01:17 compiler: COLLECTOR, opened per main 00:5x/01:1x (fold no-respell into the next pack with the x-compat retirement). Contents so far, all kit do programs on the item notes (Spiral-first, no new .ps1 appliers): (1) no-respell (KIT-STATE-SPELLING-GUARD n-latest), (2) Gleam portable math, .fs part (GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS n33), (3) mutual-tail loops (apply-rust-mutual-tail-loops.ps1, predates the rule) + sample mutual_tail_rc_live. (4) --x-compat retirement joins once x and tibia confirm the Cells check; if they're late, the pack ships without it. FLOW: stack the programs on one tree copy, build-candidate, run predict-residuals on the node (BACKEND-BATCH), then hand main the candidate plus evidence.")
inl note_of_pack_12_2 () = Note ("PACK-12", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:07Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:07 compiler: (4) the --x-compat retirement program is ready on POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE (latest note); it stacks after the no-respell and Gleam-math programs (all on base C0990B2D590CE965, distinct anchors).")
inl note_of_pack_12_3 () = Note ("PACK-12", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:55Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 02:55 compiler: pack12a = the shippable core of PACK-12 (proven: P = 4 as predicted, natives agree; core 1326B40E2ABB8F1D). pack12b QUEUED (job-1793516133970, --heavy --min-free-mb 3000) = pack12a + Copy-field clones (RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE n11) + Delphi step 1 (DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY n18) + the ord constraint's compiler half (CHAR-ORDERING n10, without the operators.spi switch, so this predict only checks that the new constraint plumbing and its implication rule change nothing). Dry-stacked on one copy: mutual-tail 3 + 2 + 4 + 1 + 6 + 7 replacements, all clean. Next: predict pack12b vs PACK-11's run, then decide whether PACK-12 ships as 12a or 12b.")
inl note_of_pack_12_4 () = Note ("PACK-12", 4u32, "2026-11-01T07:07Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:07 compiler: pack12b PREDICT (job-1793516430441, run single-flight-20261101-030035 vs PACK-11's run): P = 265 = Delphi 133 + Rust 131 + Gleam 1. It contains pack12a's 4. Compile-status diffs 0, timeouts 0. ATTRIBUTION, every P diff explained by its edit (git diff of each row's output vs HEAD): Rust rows change only by '.l<i>.clone()' -> '.l<i>' on Copy-typed fields (plus the 3 group-loop rows); Delphi rows change only by class(TSpiralRc), DropFields decls/bodies, the Retain/Release prelude and the singleton refc pin. Unexplained: 0 of 265. The ord compiler half changes no row, as predicted (no sample uses ord until operators.spi switches). NATIVE half running: job-1793516849539 (FPC -O2 for the 133 Delphi rows, so the new DropFields bodies must compile; rustc -O for the 131 Rust rows; each exit + stdout vs gcc -O2 of main.c). Candidate pack12b: SpiralCompilerCore.dll 3445FAA05588C96E, SpiralCompiler.dll DFC86B91968D447D.")
inl note_of_pack_12_5 () = Note ("PACK-12", 5u32, "2026-11-01T07:12Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:12 compiler: REVIEW GATE MET, CANDIDATE READY = pack12b (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates\\pack12b\\cache\\bin\\single-flight\\SpiralCompiler\\Release\\net11.0; SpiralCompilerCore.dll 3445FAA05588C96E, SpiralCompiler.dll DFC86B91968D447D; built from live core C0990B2D590CE965 via build-candidate -CoreAppliers apply-rust-mutual-tail-loops.ps1 -Programs no-respell, gleam-js-math-core, copy-fields, delphi-rc-step1, ord-constraint-core). EVIDENCE: predict P = 265 (Delphi 133, Rust 131, Gleam 1), compile-status diffs 0, timeouts 0, every diff attributed to its edit (0 unexplained). NATIVE (job-1793516849539): 264 rows built and run, 263 identical to gcc -O2 C, 1 differs only in line endings (op_args_deref_if|Delphi: C prints CRLF via mingw text mode, FPC LF), and its HEAD .pas output is byte-identical to pack12b's, so it's pre-existing and newline-only. Gleam native_float_math_family: Erlang exit 0 on both arms. INSTALL PRECONDITION: pack12b's Gleam output imports gleam_community/maths, so the Gleam projects need that dep. Done for the native tier (apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/gleam/gleam.toml, unstaged). Still needed at install: apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/targets.ex @gleam_deps gains {gleam_community_maths, >=2.0.2 and <3.0.0} (GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS n33 program's third edit; a Cells land, reader guard), or Gleam cells with tanh/sin/cos stop compiling. NOT in it: the --x-compat retirement (waits on x/tibia) and the operators.spi {ord} switch + char_ordering sample (a follow-up land once this compiler is installed).")
inl note_of_pack_12_6 () = Note ("PACK-12", 6u32, "2026-11-01T07:13Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:13 compiler: programs moved INTO THE REPO for main's install-pack.ps1 (which re-derives the candidate from live source; main is adding -Programs to it): apps/compiler/packs/pack12b/1-no-respell.kitdo, 2-gleam-js-math-core.kitdo, 3-copy-fields.kitdo, 4-delphi-rc-step1.kitdo, 5-ord-constraint-core.kitdo, applied in name order after apply-rust-mutual-tail-loops.ps1. Verified: on live core C0990B2D590CE965 they reproduce pack12b's candidate source byte-for-byte (sha256 796D343FB6E78D6E). Main installs pack12b now and takes the Cells targets.ex @gleam_deps edit into the same window.")
inl note_of_pack_12_7 () = Note ("PACK-12", 7u32, "2026-11-01T07:19Z", "main", "main 03:2x INSTALLED pack12b. install-pack.ps1 gained -Programs (each .kitdo piped to 'kit do <stage> -' after the .ps1 appliers, the same as build-candidate.ps1). The dry run reproduced the reviewed source from today's live core C0990B2D590CE965 (stacked 796D343FB6E78D6E = candidate). Install: built in 92.7 s; SpiralCompiler.dll DFC86B91968D447D and SpiralCompilerCore.dll 3445FAA05588C96E both equal to the candidate; system dotnet launches it; the Cells domain stamp was removed so domain.gleam regenerates; backup compiler-installs\\pack12b-20261101-031418. PRECONDITION done in the same window: targets.ex @gleam_deps gains gleam_community_maths >=2.0.2 <3.0.0. Its first land closed red and undid itself, because two targets_test cases used gleam_community_maths as their example extra CLI dep; they now use gleam_json >=3.0.0; landed 24/24 (targets.ex 630FC3E8F268, targets_test 457E9C54C11F). Shipped to the node: digest FABD90BB27054D30, capability smoke OK, previous kept as net11.0.replaced-pack12b. Next for compiler: the operators.spi {ord} switch and the char_ordering sample (follow-up land on this compiler).")
inl note_of_pack_12_8 () = Note ("PACK-12", 8u32, "2026-11-01T07:23Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:23 compiler: DONE: installed by main 03:14 (core 3445FAA05588C96E, host DFC86B91968D447D, equal to the reviewed candidate), on the node 03:2x (digest FABD90BB27054D30), with the Cells gleam_community_maths precondition in the same window. Live source is now core 796D343FB6E78D6E. Follow-ups are in PACK-13.")
inl note_of_lib_keyed_rng_1 () = Note ("LIB-KEYED-RNG", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:33Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 08:33 tibia QUALITY PASS. DEFINITION OF DONE: (a) lib/spiral/keyed_rng.spi exists with test blocks pinned to c, rust, lua and wgsl; the agreement test 'the same key draws the same u32 on every backend' passes 4/4 under lib_gate.ps1 on the node (lib program gate, -Negative mutant: flip one mix32 shift, which must fail 4/4); (b) tibia/src/rng.spi is deleted and its 4 importers (combat, search, gpu_sim, hunt_sim) import keyed_rng; tibia build.ps1 is green on the node with gpu_sim and hunt_sim draws unchanged (the existing seed-pinned assertions are the oracle). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN, fixing today's smell: roll_key = { seed; tick; entity; purpose : u32 } lets callers swap the four u32s and pass magic purposes (search.spi's 100u32 + gene, combat.spi's 201u32). Lib shape: (1) nominal wrappers ':! seed = u32', ':! tick = u32', ':! entity = u32', so argument order is checked by the type checker (the search.spi ':! vector' idiom); (2) the PURPOSE as a PHANTOM-indexed key: 'roll_key p' carries a phantom domain tag p, and a prototype 'purposed p' with 'purpose_code : p -> u32' maps each caller's own union (tibia: ':| combat_roll = | HitChance | ArmorRoll | ...', search: '| GeneNudge of u32') to its code in one place, so no literal purpose numbers remain at call sites; (3) draw and roll_between stay total (no -1 sentinel; roll_between with low > high is a type-level precondition expressed by taking a 'span = { low; width }' record). The model to follow is lib sm'/am' prototypes plus tibia search.spi's nominal vector. NEXT STEP: write lib/spiral/keyed_rng.spi from tibia src/rng.spi lines 5-22 plus the wrappers; register it in lib's package after 'math'; the first test is the 4-backend agreement on key {seed 3, tick 1, entity 2, HitChance}; gate with lib_program_gate.ps1 -Program on the node. ESTIMATE stays 120m (light: one module plus 4 importer edits). P2 because it's a harvest item with no consumer outside tibia yet; it rises to P1 when WOW-LUA-SANDBOX needs deterministic rolls. CORRECTION (git grep 'rng::' over src and apps): 6 importers, not 4: combat, hunt_sim, hunting_pack, network, pack_sim, search (gpu_sim does not import it). DoD (b) covers all 6.")
inl note_of_lib_keyed_rng_2 () = Note ("LIB-KEYED-RNG", 2u32, "2026-11-01T19:12Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 15:2x tibia, LIB HALF PROVEN, LANDING NOW. Draft: kit drafts store spiral\\LIB-KEYED-RNG\\keyed_rng.spi (canonical 96D5E1FD8BC75C6A) plus package.spiproj (live 2648C280B53B8CBA + '    keyed_rng' after math). Shape: mix32 as in tibia; nominal ':! seed = u32', ':! tick = u32', ':! entity = u32' (argument order checked); prototype ':? purpose_code p : p -> u32'; ':== roll_key p = { seed; tick; entity; purpose : p }'; draw and roll_between take '\\* p {purpose_code}.' and keep tibia's exact arithmetic (mix32 (s + mix32 (t + mix32 (e + mix32 code)))), so tibia's seed-pinned asserts stay the oracle; NEW 'sub_key key index : roll_key derived' (code * 256 + index) generalizes combat.spi's sub_key so combat stays generic over the caller's purpose type. PROBE (box compile, scratch package): an instance for a lib prototype must name it bare after ':< keyed_rng'; ':+ keyed::purpose_code t' is a ParserError ('Expected: lowercase variable'); cross-module dispatch folded to the expected code at compile time. DECISION vs n1 plan (3): no span record; roll_between keeps low/high, since a { low; width } record moves the precondition (width 0 divides by zero) instead of removing it, and tibia has no -1 sentinel here. NODE GATE (mirror tibia-rng, synced 14:57 after the :: respell land; job-1793559951324-tibia-keyed-rng-gate-2): positive 5/5 passed on c, rust and lua (prediction was lua red on u32 multiply overflow; WRONG, the Lua backend wraps), wgsl unsupported on the node (no runner; run on the box after land); cell budget 0 findings; negative mutant (first mix32 shift 16 -> 17) CAUGHT, 3 failed, 0 type errors. Roundtrip: canonical after dropping one blank line under the heading. TOOL GAP CLOSED: lib_land_window.ps1 could not land a NEW file (it backed up the absent live file); it now marks absent files New, skips their backup and base type_probe, and its undo moves a landed new file into the undo folder (unstaged in spiral apps/cells/spi). Next: tibia half (6 importers to typed purpose unions, src package gains lib) after the land closes green.")
inl note_of_lib_keyed_rng_3 () = Note ("LIB-KEYED-RNG", 3u32, "2026-11-01T23:01Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 19:0x tibia, CONSUMER INTERIM (job-1793562329522, tibia-gates mirror synced 15:44 with lib keyed_rng 96D5E1FD8BC7; full build, 3 h 13 min so far, at measure of ~66 alphabetically): every migrated importer reached is green with its seed-pinned asserts unchanged: combat 8/8 (truncated-normal stats 2055/0/4093, armor bounds, arrow facts), hunt_sim 9/9, hunting_pack 2/2, gpu_sim 3/3 (its wgsl rows unsupported on the node). Still to come: network, pack_sim, search. Three failures so far are node-environmental, not code, and none of those files imports rng or quantity: block_plan (cell timed out after 300 s), damage (compiler connection :noproc), map (rustc: temp main.rs vanished). The node was running 7 jobs from 5 lanes. Next: final rows, then Done if the 3 remaining importers are green; the environmental reds go to tibia CI after the user's next push.")
inl note_of_lib_keyed_rng_4 () = Note ("LIB-KEYED-RNG", 4u32, "2026-11-01T23:20Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 19:2x tibia, DONE. DoD (a): lib keyed_rng.spi landed 15:12, tests green on c/rust/lua (node) and wgsl (box), negative mutant caught. DoD (b): tibia src/rng.spi removed and its 6 importers migrated to typed purpose unions (combat_roll, hunt_roll, pack_roll, network_roll, search_roll, plus lib sub_key/derived), committed by the user in b08cd87; tibia CI run 41350993071 on b08cd87 completed SUCCESS on build (windows-2025) and build (ubuntu-24.04), so every seed-pinned draw assertion held on both OS. The node full build was stopped as redundant after CI proved it (it had reached every rng importer except network/pack_sim/search green; CI covered those).")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_1 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T06:03Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:03 x: x's share of the symbol-respell round (POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE n9 step 4; tibia's respell found the test -> :# filter trap). Measured with no writes: SpiralCompiler format --spelling symbols --check flags all 72 tracked and untracked x .spi files (excluding target and frames). Scratch conversions of tlds.spi and sim_serve.spi show the ONLY change is 'test' -> ':#' on test-block headers (3 and 1 lines), since x already spells members '::'.\nConsumers checked:\n- x's xc splitter cuts cells at column 0, not by keyword, so it's unaffected.\n- Cells matches 'test|:#' (document.spi:566 and :610, memo.ex:400, accept.ex:93).\n- x's own filters: apps/sim/build.ps1:114 is fixed to '^(test|:#) ' (X-COMPAT-VIA-CELLS n5). Nothing else in x matches the keyword.\nORDER, required by memory x-sim-fixtures-embed-client-sources (sim fixtures embed client sources, so non-Accept sim builds fail until -Accept regenerates them):\n(1) verify44 (running, pre-respell tree, -Accept) passes and lands.\n(2) Apply the respell to live x: format --spelling symbols over the 72 files, unstaged.\n(3) verify45 -Accept on the respelled tree. Pass rule, written now: exit 0; x verify's five steps exit 0; every notebook passes; the cell-budget pre-check reports 0 findings in both trees; the corpus filter still finds 73 lib files.\n(4) Land verify45's regenerated fixtures and outputs with -Apply.\nUntil (3) lands, no non-Accept sim proof runs on a respelled tree.")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_2 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:24Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:24 x: scope widened, packing two edits into one proof (memory pack-queued-builds): step (2) applies BOTH the symbol respell (test -> :#) and x's list-sugar heal (Nil -> [], 4 tuple slots -> ([]), LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD x-share note), then verify45 -Accept proves both. Added to verify45's pass rule: outputs regenerated from the respelled and healed tree differ from verify44's only in fixtures that embed client source text.")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_3 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 3u32, "2026-11-01T06:49Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:49 x: STEP (2) RUNBOOK, ready for when verify44 lands:\n(a) In C:\\home\\git\\x: files = git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard '*.spi', minus target/ and frames/, resolved to absolute paths (72 files today).\n(b) Respell: & <spiral-bin>\\toolchains\\dotnet\\dotnet.exe <spiral-bin>\\bin\\single-flight\\SpiralCompiler\\Release\\net11.0\\SpiralCompiler.dll format --spelling symbols @files. Expect 72 'formatted:' lines, each changing only test -> :# (scratch-verified on tlds.spi and sim_serve.spi).\n(c) List-sugar heal: LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD n3's METHOD (case-sensitive \\bNil\\b -> [] outside strings and macros, 4 tuple slots -> ([])), 230 lines in 38 files.\n(d) Checks before any proof: format --spelling symbols --check @files reports 0 would-change. A case-sensitive census shows 0 list Nil left. The corpus filter in apps/sim/build.ps1:114 still finds 73 lib files. Assert-CellBudget on apps/client and apps/sim/spi gives 0 findings (on the box once no heavy-build or compile window is held, else on the node).\n(e) verify45 via x/scripts/node-run.ps1 -Mode verify -Core <current core> with n1's pass rule plus n2's 'outputs differ only in source-embedding fixtures', then -Land with -Apply. verify45 also gives the first node check of X-RUN-METRICS' raw-pair rows (n6) and the cell_budget names (X-COMPAT-VIA-CELLS n4).")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_4 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 4u32, "2026-11-01T08:59Z", "x", "2026-11-01 04:59 x: STEP (2) APPLIED and STEP (3) LAUNCHED (all unstaged):\n- Respell: format --spelling symbols over 72 files, 72 formatted. The recheck reports 0 would-change, 0 'test ' headers left and 360 ':#' headers. git diff confirms the respell changed only headers (the other diff lines are the earlier unstaged raw-pair metric edits).\n- List-sugar heal: 230 lines in 38 files (n3's method). The 4 tuple slots became ([]), and the same scanner finds 0 list Nil left.\n- Stub retirement prep (PS1-TO-SPIRAL n38): e2e.ps1 defaults to the sim, with XSIM_SERVER=stub selecting the stub (start wait 120 s for the sim, 60 s for the stub). scripts/build.ps1 and verify.ps1's plain path build the sim BEFORE the client. e2e-parity.ps1 sets its arms explicitly. Client and sim READMEs updated.\n- Box checks (no windows held): corpus filter 73 files, and the guarded Assert-CellBudget gives 0 findings in apps/client and apps/sim/spi (the guard's first box run).\n- Pack 12 was installed at 03:15 (core 3445FAA05588C96E on box and node), so verify45 is x's first proof on it.\nverify45 = job-1793523554712 (xqueue1, -Accept). Pass rule: n1 plus n2's fixture clause plus PS1-TO-SPIRAL n38's (client e2e passes with the sim as default). On PASS: land with -Apply, then close this item, LIST-SUGAR-DRIFT-GUARD's x share, SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY and port 2's first half.")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_5 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 5u32, "2026-11-01T10:29Z", "x", "2026-11-01 06:29 x: verify45 (job-1793523554712, 88 min, pack 12 core 3445FAA05588C96E) by the rule: FAIL. Sim build exit 0 (all 23 sim notebooks, cell-budget 0 findings). The client build failed at the e2e: tutorial step 'xc post-thread --resume 9101 checks/checks.spi' exited 4 ('resumed after post 3 of 9'). Other steps were DIFFERENT: xc thread 3000001, the post-thread --dry-run of checks.spi, xc thread 9003, and both resumes.\nCAUSE: the respell changed checks/checks.spi's first cell ('test checks static =' -> ':# checks static ='). apps/client/e2e/world.json is HAND-MAINTAINED (10-25) and seeds half-posted threads 9101 and 9201 with the OLD c1 text. Resume compares the posted prefix with the file's split, sees c1 differ and refuses with exit 4. That is the correct safety behaviour, and the seed was stale. The DIFFERENT preview and thread steps are README tutorial outputs, which e2e accept regenerated (5 of 37 accepted).\nLESSON, recorded on this item: memory x-sim-fixtures-embed-client-sources covers GENERATED fixtures (sim accept rewrites them), but world.json is a hand-written fixture that also embeds client source text. -Accept can't fix it. A respell or heal of client sources must grep the hand-written e2e fixtures for the changed text.\nFIX (unstaged): world.json lines 99 and 102 c1 now read ':# checks static =' (2 occurrences, plain text swap). c2 and c3 already match the file. 9202's 'edited' text is intentional (the refusal test). No other hand-written fixture embeds a 'test ' header. thread-oauth1.expected.json is sim-generated and regenerates under accept.\nverify45's outputs are NOT landed. verify46 relaunches with the same rule.")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_6 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 6u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:36 x: ITEM UPGRADE (round 4). Fields: tags gain team:x; 90 / light / agent stand. DEFINITION OF DONE: verify46 (job-1793529002122) passes n1's rule plus n2's and PS1-TO-SPIRAL n38's clauses (exit 0; 5 steps exit 0; 43 + 23 notebooks pass; cell-budget 0 findings in both trees; client e2e passes against the sim as default). Then 'node-proof.ps1 -Land x-verify46 -From C:\\home\\remote-stage\\xv46-out -Repo x -Apply' lands with 0 conflicts. Artifact: the land line and the landed file count. NEXT STEP: read verify46's stdout.log verdict lines when its wait ends. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: n/a for types, this is a syntax-only respell. Its guarantee is mechanical: the diff touched only test -> :# header lines (n4), and Cells matches 'test|:#'.")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_7 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 7u32, "2026-11-01T13:12Z", "x", "2026-11-01 09:12 x: verify46 (job-1793529002122, 2 h 39 min, xqueue1, pack 12 core 3445FAA05588C96E, -Accept). Verdict by n1 AS WRITTEN: one clause NOT MET. 'client build before accepting' exited 1, and n1 required all five steps to exit 0. Every other clause MET: exit 0; sim accept 0; e2e accept 0 at 153/153 (the world.json seed fix works); sim build 0 with all 23 notebooks; client build 0 with all 43 notebooks and e2e 153 against the sim as default; cell-budget 0 findings in both trees in every step.\nREADING of the miss: in an -Accept run with changed sources, the pre-accept build compares the OLD README outputs and fails by design, and accept rewrites them. n1 was written without that case. Every changed output was diffed line by line against the live README: the respell (:#), the list sugar ([]), weights -2 for the shorter header, '--symbol' added to a quoted ///- header, and loner's timeline showing the fixed world.json seeds. NO sim-vs-stub response difference (the risk with the sim now the default server: parity compares request logs, not responses).\nLANDED with -Apply: 59 files, 0 conflicts (regenerated client .rs, sim fixtures, xsim_frames parts, README tutorial outputs), unstaged for the user. The tree was unchanged since verify46's 06:30 sync. Rule fix for next time, written now: accept-run rules name the post-accept steps; the pre-accept build may fail only on README tutorial outputs that the accept then rewrites. CLOSED.")
inl note_of_x_respell_symbols_8 () = Note ("X-RESPELL-SYMBOLS", 8u32, "2026-11-01T14:35Z", "x", "2026-11-01 10:35 x: COMMITTED by the user in 08a49e2 (11-01 10:01): the symbol respell (72 files, test -> :#), x's list-sugar heal (230 lines), the world.json seed fix, the stub-retirement prep and verify46's 59 landed outputs. x's tree now has 1 unstaged entry (the notebook runner pid logging in common.ps1). The destructive migrations the user asked for (round 5, item 4) are landed.")
inl note_of_cell_budget_memory_1 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-MEMORY", 1u32, "2026-11-01T06:23Z", "main", "main 02:2x: x pointed at CELLS-ERL-QUADRATIC-JOIN as a possible same root. Until fixed, run the check per repo under the erl memory guard (memory cells-erl-memory-guard) and never over a generated tree in one call. Other repos stay fast: x/apps 73 files, tibia 65 files and spiral/lib 118 files each finished in seconds.")
inl note_of_cell_budget_memory_2 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-MEMORY", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:43Z", "main", "main 02:4x ROOT CAUSE + FIX LANDED (9/9; spiral.cell_budget.ex E40023ACAF13, test A67590AB7E9C). Not the weight math: the task's file walk. Path.wildcard('**/*') follows junctions and only filtered target/deps AFTER enumerating, and fiscal\\deps\\spiral -> C:\\home\\git\\spiral, spiral\\deps\\polyglot -> polyglot, polyglot\\deps\\spiral -> spiral is a loop, so the walk never ended (51.8 GB). The compiler's walker never had it: it prunes during the walk and skips links. Fix: a hand walk with File.lstat that prunes skipped dirs before descending and never follows symlinks or junctions; the test adds a self-loop link where the OS allows one. Measured on fiscal with a 4 GB watchdog: peak erl private 152 MB (was 51.8 GB). Still slow there: over 240 s, scanning ~954k generated lines whose dotted access autolinks on every line, so respelling fiscal (EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION) also fixes the time. Left: the new over-budget census per repo, under a kit job.")
inl note_of_cell_budget_memory_3 () = Note ("CELL-BUDGET-MEMORY", 3u32, "2026-11-01T19:29Z", "main", "main 15:3x CLOSED: the title's DoD is met (bounded memory: peak erl private 152 MB on fiscal vs 51.8 GB, the lstat walk prunes before descending and never follows links; regression test with a self-loop link, 9/9). The leftover per-repo over-budget census is a measurement, not this bug: it moves to GRANULAR-STATS, which already owns the census rows. Fiscal's slowness (240 s over ~954k generated lines) goes away with fiscal's edition-3 respell.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_1 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 1u32, "2026-11-01T06:30Z", "main", "main 02:2x: registered from x's CELLS-IN-SPIRAL n3 slice list; main owns it, and x keeps directives and the daemon protocol. Typefeatureset first (unions/GADTs), granular, every cell within the 280 cell budget.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_2 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:37Z", "main", "main 08:3x TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: a document is a list of cells, and a cell a union over kinds (Markdown | Code of language * target list | Test of name * targets | Real) instead of newline/tab-encoded rows; heading levels a closed union, not ints; the .spir export a function from the typed document, not string surgery. Model: lib's xml.spi typed tree. DoD: document_test passes unchanged against the typed model, and the 4 canonical _real.spir files regenerate byte-identical. NEXT STEP: map today's document.spi row encodings (894 lines) to the union cases in a table on this item before coding. FIELDS: estimate 180, load light, owner main.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_3 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 3u32, "2026-11-02T12:29Z", "x", "2026-11-02 08:29 x: HANDOFF agreed with main (main's reply to x's groom message; main is over its WIP cap after a 07:55 crash). x takes this item and starts it after CELLS-PORT-COMPILER-CLIENT (C7a). It is on CELLS-IN-SPIRAL's critical path, since CELLS-PORT-NOTEBOOK and X-SIM-CELLS-SPIRAL depend on it. The team tag moves to team:x when work starts. M1 (CELLS-XCOMPAT-SPIRAL) and M3 (CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL) stay with main.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_4 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 4u32, "2026-11-02T17:13Z", "x", "2026-11-02 13:13 x: M2.1 LANDED (unstaged, hash-guarded): apps/cells/spi/document.spi FD105ADC68E0 over DF0D48010443. The hand-rolled list unions rows, cells, tokens and mark (NoRow/Row, NoCell/Cell, NoWord/Word, NoMark/Mark: about 300 sites) are now lib lists and option, through type aliases (:== rows = list string, cells = list (string * string), mark = option (string * string)), so signatures stay short: 894 -> 886 lines, 0 over 120. The rewrite used a balanced-paren splitter; no multi-scrutinee tuple-slot patterns existed, so the comma trap didn't apply. PROOF x-cells-m2a2 (node xcells1, A/A then A/B in one job, rule written before): base 35 passed (document_test + notebook_test, domain 0DD16E593251); draft applied 13:10:20, then 35 passed with domain.gleam regenerated at 13:10:35 to C2A59CBAB8FF, so the new code ran. x-cells-m2a1 was VOID: it looked for domain.gleam in the mix build dir, but it lives in-tree. NEXT M2.2: string cell kinds ('markdown', 'spiral', 'fsharp', 'import', 'code/<lang>') become a closed cell_kind union plus a cell record, encoded to text only at the Elixir export boundary.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_5 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 5u32, "2026-11-02T17:30Z", "x", "2026-11-02 13:30 x: M2.2 LANDED (unstaged): document.spi 9BE444036A38 over FD105ADC68E0. Cell kinds are a closed union cell_kind = Markdown | SpiralCode | FsharpCode | ImportCell | TestCell | CodeIn : string | NamedTest : test_realm * string | OtherKind : string, and cells = list (cell_kind * string). Text exists only at the Elixir wire: emit_cells writes kind_text and read_cells reads kind_of; OtherKind keeps any unexpected wire kind byte-exact. The string-encoded 'test:'/'real_test:' kinds became NamedTest (realm, name), so realm_of, test_named_kind and kind_test_name are pattern matches, not prefix surgery. walk's '' sentinel became option cell_kind. test_realm moved above its first use. PROOF x-cells-m2b1 (A/A then A/B, same rule as M2.1): base 35 passed (domain C2A59CBAB8FF = the landed M2.1); draft applied 13:27:35, then 35 passed with domain regenerated 13:28:15 -> 8B604076647C. NEXT M2.3: the realm strings 'real'/'spi' and the tab-joined test_index rows become typed rows, the last encoded wire inside document.spi apart from the export boundary.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_6 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 6u32, "2026-11-02T17:40Z", "x", "2026-11-02 13:40 x: M2.3 LANDED (unstaged): document.spi 853C1D36F09F over 9BE444036A38. test_index rows are a typed record test_row = { at; name; mode : test_mode; realm : test_realm; symbol; shown } with a closed test_mode = IgnoredTest | FailingTest | BuildOnlyTest | PreludeTest | StaticTest | PlainTest. Only row_text and the test_index export encode it (tab fields, newline-joined, byte-identical to the old join). PROOF x-cells-m2c1: base 35 passed (domain 8B604076647C = M2.2); draft applied 13:39:09, then 35 passed with domain regenerated 13:39:28 -> 42E02875456C. LEFT for M2: the .spir/.spi export as a function of the typed document (export_text/emit_spi still assemble text in passes), test_block_go's options as a typed header, and the in-file encoded walk state (spi_mode, open_step).")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_7 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 7u32, "2026-11-02T18:42Z", "x", "2026-11-02 14:42 x: M2.4 LANDED (unstaged): document.spi 20A37582A768 over 853C1D36F09F. A test header's flags are parsed once into test_flags = { switches : test_switches; no_agree; refers; fails : option string }, with test_switches = { symbol; static; print_code; ignore; build_only : bool }, and rendered once by header_text in the original flag order. The text helpers test_mode, agree_mode and reference_mode are gone. PROOF x-cells-m2d2 on pack16c (core B640F761890510F7): base 35 passed (the domain rebuilt by the new compiler is byte-identical, 42E02875456C), draft applied 14:38:01, then 35 passed with domain -> 2525B1586F25. x-cells-m2d1 had run the pre-rename text (field reference, 121 cols) on pack15d with the same generated hash.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_8 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 8u32, "2026-11-02T19:23Z", "x", "2026-11-02 15:23 x: M2.5 LANDED (unstaged): document.spi 5ABB95547D09 over B500E8419A7F (the respelled M2.4). spi_mode's InTest carries a record open_test = { name; modes; targets; body; form : header_form; last : i32 } in place of a 6-tuple, so test_cell_of takes the record, and spi_walk's three body-growing arms share one 'grown' update ({ t ?| body = kept :: t::body }). test_header (NoHeader | Header) became option (string * string). PROOF x-cells-m2e1 on pack17: base 35 passed (domain 314803DBEC19 = the landed respell); draft applied 15:19:53, then 35 passed with domain -> D8E83F702909. LEFT: bounds (the Elixir tab text that body_last_of rescans per header) parsed once at from_spi_bounded into a typed list; the export functions take the typed document.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_9 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 9u32, "2026-11-02T19:33Z", "x", "2026-11-02 15:33 x: M2.6 LANDED (unstaged): document.spi C1B445996763 over 5ABB95547D09. The Elixir bounds text is parsed once at spi_cells into list body_bound ({ header; last : i32 }), and body_last_of walks the typed list instead of rescanning tab text per test header (an empty text gives [], so 'no bounds' still means -1). PROOF x-cells-m2f1 on pack17: base 35 passed (domain D8E83F702909 = M2.5); draft applied 15:31:12, then 35 passed with domain -> 68D9E29442EF. Inside document.spi, text now exists only at the Elixir boundary: the wire in (load/read_cells, bounds_of), the wire out (emit_cells/kind_text, row_text, emit_spi), and the export functions in main.spi.")
inl note_of_cells_document_typed_10 () = Note ("CELLS-DOCUMENT-TYPED", 10u32, "2026-11-02T22:59Z", "x", "2026-11-02 18:59 x: CLOSED by rule: the title's DoD is met (typed records plus a cell-kind union instead of newline/tab-encoded rows; oracle document_test passed on every slice M2.1-M2.6). Text survives only at the Elixir wire (load/read_cells, bounds_of in; emit_cells, row_text, emit_spi out), and replacing it needs records across the Gleam export. The remainder is owned elsewhere, not dropped: (1) the typed boundary is LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-RECORDS (Gleam exports produce records); (2) deleting document.ex is CELLS-PORT-NOTEBOOK (C9), which removes its last Elixir caller.")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_1 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-11-01T06:30Z", "main", "main 02:2x: registered from x's CELLS-IN-SPIRAL n3 slice list; main owns it, and x keeps directives and the daemon protocol. Typefeatureset first (unions/GADTs), granular, every cell within the 280 cell budget.")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_2 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:37Z", "main", "main 08:3x TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: the manifest as a record with a typed modules field (union Listed of module list | Derived), dialect and edition as separate typed fields (DIALECT-IDENTITY), and SourceMap entries as records (source path, line, column) behind a phantom-typed position so generated and source coordinates can't be swapped. Model: Cells manifest.ex (landed EDITION3-MANIFEST-MODEL) as the oracle. DoD: manifest_test and the source-map rows pass against the Spiral port; --resolve-manifest output identical on lib. NEXT STEP: list manifest.ex's public functions and their callers in Cells, then port the pure half first. FIELDS: estimate 180, load light, owner main.")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_3 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-11-02T20:29Z", "x", "2026-11-02 16:29 x: TAKEN by x, handed over by main (main is over its WIP cap). It now blocks two x critical-path items: CELLS-PORT-MEMO's package-print side and CELLS-PORT-RUNNER-MOUNTS's dependency parser. Shape, per SPIPROJ-RETIRE: one typed manifest model that reads BOTH package.spiproj and spiral.toml, so Cells is not the last spiproj reader when the compiler host goes toml-first. Oracle: manifest_test and notebook_report_test unchanged, plus byte-equal parsed projects over every tracked manifest in the repos.")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_4 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-11-02T21:00Z", "x", "2026-11-02 17:00 x: M3.1 LANDED (unstaged): spiral.toml reading is Spiral. apps/cells/spi/manifest.spi (DB5A44A9A72E, exported as from_toml) classifies each toml line into a closed union toml_line = Blank | Raw | Scalar | Block | ListStart and renders the spiproj text the readers parse. Manifest.from_toml delegates; the Elixir converter (convert, block, list, collect, unquoted) is deleted: manifest.ex 129 -> 59. PROOFS on pack17: x-cells-m3a3, the PARITY ORACLE: 14 tracked spiral.toml from every repo plus 11 edge cases (one-line and unterminated ''' blocks, nested brackets, empty items, CRLF, '= weird', no '='), 0 differ, mix 60 passed, blocks 2/2. x-cells-m3b1 (the switch, A/B): manifest, notebook_report, document and memo tests 85 -> 85. Earlier runs: m3a1 VOID (my filter hid the failure); m3a2 had the test's own size guard refuse an empty corpus, because Path.wildcard reads backslashes as escapes. LEFT: source_map.ex (64 lines); Manifest.read/resolve (IO); the declares_modules regex. SPIPROJ-RETIRE: Cells now owns one typed toml model, so when the compiler host goes toml-first the spiproj text this renders can be dropped for the typed record.")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_5 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-11-02T21:26Z", "x", "2026-11-02 17:26 x: M3.2 LANDED (unstaged): SourceMap is Spiral. apps/cells/spi/source_map.spi (F86AD26BA535, exported as source_remap) types positions with phantom indices, position in_generated vs position in_source, so locate maps generated to source and the two cannot be swapped. source_map.ex 64 -> 4 lines, one delegating def. PROOFS: x-cells-m3c1, the PARITY ORACLE: 6,518 messages over 136 tracked .spi (identical and 3-line-shifted programs, sampled rows, 2 locations per message, so the duplicate-line narrowing runs on real code), 0 differ, mix 34 passed, blocks 1/1. x-cells-m3d2 (the switch, A/B, on pack 8960CAB701FEF46E): notebook_report, notebook and document tests 68 -> 68. x-cells-m3d1's matching 68 -> 68 was discarded because it spanned the 17:13 pack switch. LEFT for M3: Manifest.read/resolve (IO) and declares_modules. Cells lib Elixir total now 11224.")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_6 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-11-02T22:42Z", "x", "2026-11-02 18:42 x: M3.3 drafted (kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-MANIFEST-DECLARES, layered on the CELLS-JSON-VALUE drafts, so it launches after that lands). manifest.spi gains declares_modules. It reproduces ~r/^\\s*modules\\s*=/m exactly: from any line start, ASCII whitespace (PCRE \\s includes \\v and \\f and crosses newlines), then 'modules', whitespace, '='. Export declares_modules returns 'true'/'false'; Domain maps it to a boolean; Manifest.declares_modules? delegates, and the regex leaves lib. Phase A and B ride in one job, because the parity test carries the old regex as its oracle. PRE-REGISTERED RULE (x-cells-m3e1): manifest.spi blocks 3/3; 'manifest declares parity: N texts compared (D declare modules), 0 differ' over every tracked *.toml and *.spiproj (about 1,300 files), plus an indented and a CRLF variant of each, plus 20 edges; files > 20 and 5 < D < N; manifest_test and notebook_report_test 0 failures. LEFT after it: Manifest.path/read/resolve are IO and stay Elixir until a Cells IO slice uses lib erlang_file.")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_7 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-11-03T01:08Z", "x", "2026-11-02 21:08 x: M3.3 LANDED (unstaged, with the builders slice in one window): manifest.spi 77D7BD2A336B (declares_modules), manifest.ex 24589CEBD877 (declares_modules? delegates; the regex is gone from lib), manifest_declares_parity_test.exs 7324C77B20BC. PROOF x-cells-bm2 and x-cells-bm3: manifest.spi blocks 3/3; parity 3914 texts (every tracked .toml and .spiproj, an indented and a CRLF variant of each, plus 20 edges; 12 declare modules), 0 differ; manifest_test and notebook_report_test passed. Manifest is now pure Spiral except path/read/resolve, which are IO and the compiler call, and stay Elixir until a Cells IO slice uses lib erlang_file.")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_8 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-11-03T12:33Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:33 x: M3.4 drafted (kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-MANIFEST-PATH): Manifest.path is Spiral. lib erlang_file gains regular_file (filelib:is_regular) with a gleam test; manifest.spi gains manifest_names, joined (Elixir Path.join's rules: '' -> name, '/' -> '/name', else drop one trailing / or \\ then '/' name), first_regular -> option string, and manifest_path_text for the export; manifest.ex path/1 delegates through Domain.manifest_path (nil for ''). Layered on the CELLS-ON-HTML main.spi/domain.ex drafts, so it launches after those land (guards on recorded bases). PRE-REGISTERED RULE for x-cells-mpath1 (node, pinned core at both ends): run_notebook --gate over erlang_file.spi and manifest.spi exits 0, 0 failed; 'manifest path parity: N dirs compared (F hold a manifest), 0 differ', where N covers every dir of every tracked file in each mirror repo, plus a trailing / and a backslash-with-trailing-\\ variant of each, plus 11 edges, with dirs > 100 and 5 < F < dirs; mix tests (parity, manifest, manifest_declares_parity, cell_budget, mounts x3, notebook_lib_snapshot, notebook_spiral_lib, document) exit 0. LEFT after it: read's toml/resolve branch (compiler call).")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_9 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-11-03T15:12Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:12 x: M3.4 DECOUPLED from CELLS-ON-HTML: the link half isn't green yet (CELLS-HTML-PARITY n23), so main.spi and domain.ex are re-derived on LIVE (main AE2FCBD33CDF -> FEA05BF571A2, export manifest_path only; domain BBBCE6E60BB1 -> 943360FB140D, Domain.manifest_path only). With erlang_file, manifest.spi and manifest.ex (bases unchanged), the slice can run on its own. The landed domain_compiler fix means the lib erlang_file change regenerates domain.gleam. The launcher also resets the peer WIP lib file_system.spi to HEAD in the node mirror. Rule n8 unchanged. Node queue: x-events2 (running), x-replay1, x-cells-mpath1, x-html-gleam6, serial because all of them use Cells.")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_10 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-11-04T04:14Z", "x", "2026-11-04 00:13 x: GROOM (user 11-04 00:1x via main). Stays Active at 90: it's x's FIRST node launch next run (x-cells-mpath1: manifest_path, erlang_port run_collect, erlang_http; launcher and hash-guarded land ready). Per the user (11-04), heavy runs go to the node and europa first, the box last; this run is node-only.")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_11 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-11-04T04:41Z", "x", "2026-11-04 00:41 x: x-cells-mpath1 (job-1793767051798, core A63F4C2A) FAILED in 39 s at the Gleam build: 'Unknown module atom' at 3 atom.create sites in the generated domain.gleam. Cause (mine, from yesterday's LIB-MOVE-X-SIM-ERLANG): lib's erlang_term imports gleam/erlang/atom (package gleam_erlang), and this run's erlang_port run_collect is the first Cells code to reach erlang_term, but apps/cells only depends on gleam_stdlib. The import line itself was emitted (line 23); the PACKAGE was missing. FIX drafted (kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-MANIFEST-PATH): gleam.toml and manifest.toml gain gleam_erlang 1.3.0 (x sim's exact pin and checksum; it requires only gleam_stdlib), and domain.ex's ensure! appends gleam_erlang's ebin beside spiral_cells and gleam_stdlib (otherwise the build passes and the runtime fails on gleam@erlang@atom). Both tomls are hash-guarded (294C16866415, 3564BC80E56E). Relaunched as x-cells-mpath2. Same pass rule as mpath1.")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_12 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-11-04T04:58Z", "x", "2026-11-04 00:58 x: x-cells-mpath2 (job-1793767535313, core A63F4C2A, 3.7 min) PASSED its pre-registered rule: exit 0, blocks exit 0, spiral-tests 14/14 over erlang_file (6), erlang_port (2), erlang_http (1) and manifest (5); Cells mix tests 'Result: 48 passed'; core unchanged at the end. LANDING in two halves, both with draft bytes identical to what mpath2 staged: (1) lib via lib_land_window.ps1 (erlang_file 7F0087E74FA1, erlang_port 235A7A31FF76, erlang_http new, package.spiproj 9F29F2476BF9 registering erlang_http after erlang_port, the launcher's exact rule). It's waiting on a FALSE live-reader: main's remote.ps1 node run mentions run_notebook.ps1 but runs on the node; reported to main. (2) Cells via land-cells-mpath.ps1 (7 files, hash-guarded, tree-write window on C:/home/git/spiral/apps/cells), after (1).")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_13 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-11-04T05:09Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:09 x: M3.x LANDED (both halves, bytes identical to x-cells-mpath2's proof): lib via lib_land_window (closed green) and Cells via land-cells-mpath (tree-write window on apps/cells, 7 hash guards): manifest.spi C45DF41DB91F (manifest_path), main.spi FEA05BF571A2, manifest.ex 819AD742BC77, domain.ex 7C588CAFE420 (ensure! loads gleam_erlang), new manifest_path_parity_test.exs D7AF4967C0DE, gleam.toml 850DD9C3C4DC + manifest.toml 3D0982F26663 (gleam_erlang 1.3.0). Remaining for the item: the SourceMap half (65 lines of Elixir) as a Spiral port with its oracle rows.")
inl note_of_cells_manifest_sourcemap_spiral_14 () = Note ("CELLS-MANIFEST-SOURCEMAP-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-11-04T05:11Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:11 x: DONE, checked against the DoD ('Manifest and SourceMap as a-pure Spiral ports; oracle manifest_test and the source-map rows'). MANIFEST: every pure core is Spiral and Elixir delegates to Domain: path (manifest_path, landed today), declares_modules?, from_toml. What stays in manifest.ex (57 lines) is IO orchestration: read/read! read the file and pick raw text, from_toml or resolve; resolve drives Toolchain + CompilerDaemon (dotnet --resolve-manifest). That moves with CELLS-PORT-COMPILER-DAEMON, not here. SOURCEMAP: already ported. apps/cells/spi/source_map.spi (138 lines, F86AD26BA535) with remap_follows_the_elixir_cases; source_map.ex is a 4-line delegate to Domain.source_remap. ORACLES: manifest_test.exs (60 lines), manifest_declares_parity_test.exs, manifest_path_parity_test.exs (new today), and the remap rows in notebook_report_test.exs L779-781 (ambiguous and unknown lines stay; indentation-shifted lines remap). Proof: x-cells-mpath2 (14/14 spiral-tests, Cells mix 48 passed), landed green today. My earlier note saying 'the SourceMap half remains' was wrong: it predates this check.")
inl note_of_x_ci_notebook_speed_1 () = Note ("X-CI-NOTEBOOK-SPEED", 1u32, "2026-11-01T06:40Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:40 x: measured from the last green CI run (ci 'tests' 41240026259, ubuntu, 61 min in workflow.ps1) by log timestamps:\n- Client build up to e2e: ~5.5 min (compiler build 75 s, 30 crates at ~5 s, cargo 52 s, e2e about 1 min).\n- Client NOTEBOOKS: 20:47 -> 21:11, ~24 min.\n- Sim build and fixtures: ~6 min.\n- Sim NOTEBOOKS: 21:17 -> 21:41, ~24 min.\nSo ~48 of 61 minutes are Cells notebooks, run one at a time (1-3 min each; runner_checks 178 s, protocol 133 s, oauth1 131 s, scheduler 137 s).\nCause in the tree: x/scripts/common.ps1 Invoke-SpiralTests runs each notebook through run_notebook.ps1 serially, with SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process and SPIRAL_CELLS_WARM=0, so every cell starts a cold compiler and no warm Gleam/Rust slot is reused.\nLevers, measure each: (a) run the notebooks N-way in parallel (GitHub runners have 4 cores); (b) the Cells compiler daemon in place of 'process'; (c) warm slots on. Pre-registered rule: one CI run per lever on the same commit. Adopt a lever iff total notebook time falls >= 30% AND every notebook still passes, with no new flake across 2 runs. (a) first: no Cells change, only common.ps1. The 'process'/'warm 0' choice was a deliberate isolation setting, so (b) and (c) need main's view on why it was set.")
inl note_of_x_ci_notebook_speed_2 () = Note ("X-CI-NOTEBOOK-SPEED", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:42Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:42 x: DUPLICATE of X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED (same build steps, same cause, already measured there with a full duration census). Its CI evidence was moved to that item. Closed so the fact lives in one record; it doesn't count as delivered work.")
inl note_of_scheduled_tasks_confirm_1 () = Note ("SCHEDULED-TASKS-CONFIRM", 1u32, "2026-11-01T07:05Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 03:0x FOR THE USER (assignee=user, user-batch; main lists in the end-of-run report). Five durability scheduled tasks are live on the box, registered without a recorded user yes. Keep each, or remove it with the one-line command. All are Priority 5, read-and-copy only, never delete source data. Registrar: i574n.github\\scripts\\register-scheduled-tasks.ps1.\n1. kit-unstaged-backup - daily 03:10 - writes %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\backups\\unstaged (every C:\\home\\git repo''s staged+unstaged+untracked layer; never deletes). Remove: Unregister-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'kit-unstaged-backup' -Confirm:$false\n2. profile-mirror-to-node - every ~50 min - writes a tar.gz of the profile (memory, transcripts, config, small kit stores) to the node C:\\home\\profile-mirror (keeps newest 48). Remove: Unregister-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'profile-mirror-to-node' -Confirm:$false\n3. profile-restore-drill - daily 05:40 - reads the newest node mirror, hashes memory vs live, writes a drill log; a failure becomes a backlog note. Remove: Unregister-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'profile-restore-drill' -Confirm:$false\n4. config-baseline-check - daily 06:10 - reads curated config fingerprints vs baseline, writes %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\schedules\\config-baseline.last.log; drift becomes a backlog note (no secret content copied). Remove: Unregister-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'config-baseline-check' -Confirm:$false\n5. tibia-mariadb-dump - daily 05:20 - mysqldump --single-transaction of the canary DB to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\tibia-mariadb-dumps (keeps newest 14). Remove: Unregister-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'tibia-mariadb-dump' -Confirm:$false\nRemove all five: @('kit-unstaged-backup','profile-mirror-to-node','profile-restore-drill','config-baseline-check','tibia-mariadb-dump') | ForEach-Object { Unregister-ScheduledTask -TaskName $_ -Confirm:$false }\nPENDING: the box-wide BEAM cap''s register line is appended here when that kit command ships (BEAM-GUARD-ALL-INVOCATIONS). RULE going forward: no new scheduled-task registration without landing here first for the user.")
inl note_of_scheduled_tasks_confirm_2 () = Note ("SCHEDULED-TASKS-CONFIRM", 2u32, "2026-11-02T14:02Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByDefault. QUESTION: Keep or unregister the 5 live durability scheduled tasks (kit-unstaged-backup, profile-mirror-to-node, profile-restore-drill, config-baseline-check, tibia-mariadb-dump)? RULE (written before acting): Default: keep all 5. They are user-level, not elevated, Priority 5, read-and-copy only, and never delete source data. Removing backups right after the 10-28 wipe is the risky direction. Keeping them is reversible at any time with the one-line Unregister-ScheduledTask commands in n1. BACKUP/UNDO: Registrar i574n.github\\scripts\\register-scheduled-tasks.ps1 re-creates any task. Veto/undo = the n1 Unregister-ScheduledTask line per task. OWNER: resilience. NEXT: Add a note: 'kept by default (rule: read+copy-only backups stay after a wipe); veto = n1 unregister lines'. List it once in main's end-of-run report as an FYI veto, not a wait. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl note_of_scheduled_tasks_confirm_3 () = Note ("SCHEDULED-TASKS-CONFIRM", 3u32, "2026-11-03T07:26Z", "main", "main 11-03 03:2x CONFIRMED by the agent (user 11-03: 'advance persistent config, gotta stop waiting for my approval'). x's ByDefault record stands: KEEP all 5 durability tasks (read+copy only, user-level, re-creatable by register-scheduled-tasks.ps1, veto lines in n1). No user veto arrives. Done; owner resilience if a task changes.")
inl note_of_pack_13_1 () = Note ("PACK-13", 1u32, "2026-11-01T07:23Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:23 compiler: OPENED after PACK-12 installed (03:14, live core 796D343FB6E78D6E). (1) ord defaulting: an unresolved metavar whose constraints are exactly {ord} defaults to int, as {number} did before (<) moved to {ord}; without it, a comparison-only value with nothing else fixing its type becomes a ValueRestriction once the cores switch. Dry-applied on the live core (1 replacement). (2) the --x-compat retirement program (POSTABILITY-ONE-SOURCE n16) when x and tibia confirm, re-anchored on 796D343F. Programs ship as apps/compiler/packs/pack13/<n>-<name>.kitdo for install-pack -Programs.\nPROGRAM 1 (kit do <apps/compiler> -):\nedit spiral_compiler.fs expect files 1 replacements 1\n  replace once <<<\n                        if numeric = Set.singleton CNumber then default_numeric \"int\" |> Option.iter (fun t -> link.Value <- Some t)\n  >>> with <<<\n                        if numeric = Set.singleton CNumber || m.constraints = Set.singleton COrd then default_numeric \"int\" |> Option.iter (fun t -> link.Value <- Some t)\n  >>>")
inl note_of_pack_13_2 () = Note ("PACK-13", 2u32, "2026-11-01T07:23Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 03:23 compiler: programs in the repo (unstaged): apps/compiler/packs/pack13/1-ord-defaulting.kitdo and 2-retire-x-compat.kitdo. Program 2 was re-dry-run on the NEW live core 796D343FB6E78D6E + host 420E1452DB4B3194: 4 replacements, clean. It ships ONLY once x and tibia confirm the Cells check; until then PACK-13 = program 1 + whatever BACKEND-BATCH proves next.")
inl note_of_pack_13_3 () = Note ("PACK-13", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:06Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:06 compiler: contents now: 1-ord-defaulting.kitdo, 2-package-parse-errors-fatal.kitdo (CHECK-SILENT-DROP). The x-compat retirement moved to apps/compiler/packs/pending/retire-x-compat.kitdo, so 'apply in name order' never picks up a held program; it joins a pack folder when x and tibia confirm. Candidate pack13a building (job-1793534792015).")
inl note_of_pack_13_4 () = Note ("PACK-13", 4u32, "2026-11-01T12:09Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:09 compiler: REGRESSION SAMPLE for program 2, held OUT of the tree until PACK-13 installs (today's compiler compiles it with exit 0, so a negative row would fail other lanes' runs). samples/package_sibling_parse_error_negative: package.spiproj 'packages: / core- / modules: / other / main'; other.spi ':= fine () : i32 = 2i32' + a blank line + ':= bad = = = ('; main.spi ':= main () : i32 = other.fine ()'. matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors: @{ Id = 'samples/package_sibling_parse_error_negative'; Contains = 'Global inl/let statements should all return functions known at parse time' }. Note main never references 'bad', so only the new rule can fail it, which makes it a real silent-drop test.")
inl note_of_pack_13_5 () = Note ("PACK-13", 5u32, "2026-11-01T12:15Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:15 compiler: REVIEW GATE MET, CANDIDATE READY = pack13a (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates\\pack13a\\...; SpiralCompilerCore.dll 61D2D7AE0E946086, SpiralCompiler.dll DFC86B91968D447D = unchanged host). Source: live core 796D343FB6E78D6E + apps/compiler/packs/pack13/1-ord-defaulting.kitdo + 2-package-parse-errors-fatal.kitdo (name order). PREDICT vs pack12b's own run (single-flight-20261101-030035 -> -080900): 7,409 rows, residual diffs 0, compile-status diffs 0, verdict diffs 0, timeouts 0, so no sample relied on the silent drop and ord defaulting changes nothing. REGRESSION SAMPLE ready at apps/compiler/packs/pack13/samples/package_sibling_parse_error_negative (copy to apps/compiler/tmp/samples/ at install) + matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors @{ Id = 'samples/package_sibling_parse_error_negative'; Contains = 'Global inl/let statements should all return functions known at parse time' }. Verified: installed exit 0 (the silent drop), pack13a exit 5 with that text. Install = programs 1+2, then the sample and the matrix line in the same window.")
inl note_of_pack_13_6 () = Note ("PACK-13", 6u32, "2026-11-01T12:19Z", "main", "main 08:2x INSTALLED pack13a: dry run reproduced the reviewed source from live core 796D343FB6E78D6E (stacked 2A746DF0111CF4EA = candidate); built 97.3 s; SpiralCompilerCore.dll 61D2D7AE0E946086 and SpiralCompiler.dll DFC86B91968D447D equal to the candidate; system dotnet launches it; backup compiler-installs\\pack13a-20261101-081605. Same window: samples/package_sibling_parse_error_negative copied into apps/compiler/tmp/samples and its ExpectedErrors row added to tests/matrix.psd1 (parses clean). Consumer-path check: probe.ps1 on that sample now exits 5 (ParserErrors) and run1.err.txt contains 'Global inl/let statements should all return functions known at parse time'. Node: shipped, digest ?, capability smoke OK.")
inl note_of_pack_13_7 () = Note ("PACK-13", 7u32, "2026-11-01T12:19Z", "main", "main 08:2x CORRECTION to the previous note: the NODE SHIP ABORTED, it did not ship. node-ship.ps1 reported 'target in use, nothing changed' (a running node job holds the node's single-flight compiler folder). The node stays on pack12b (digest FABD90BB27054D30). Box install stands. Retry the ship when the node folder is free; until then node runs use pack12b, which lacks the package-parse fix.")
inl note_of_pack_13_8 () = Note ("PACK-13", 8u32, "2026-11-01T12:22Z", "main", "main 08:3x: node ship deferred by design. compiler's char-ordering A/B (job-1793534174603 control, job-1793534183205 ord; ETA ~10:15) needs both arms on the same compiler (pack12b), and pack13a changes nothing it measures (predict 0 diffs). x-verify46 and fiscal-p10-t1101-e3-regen may also hold the folder. Retry scheduled for 10:23 as a one-shot session wake, gated on those jobs being finished; the result gets recorded only after reading node-ship's SHIPPED/ABORT line.")
inl note_of_pack_13_9 () = Note ("PACK-13", 9u32, "2026-11-01T12:24Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:24 compiler: INSTALLED 08:16 on the box (main), equal to pack13a; node ship pending (folder held by running jobs, incl. my two libord sweeps, ETA ~10:15). Consumer --check on x/tibia/dice/alphabet/blog: 12/12 clean.")
inl note_of_pack_13_10 () = Note ("PACK-13", 10u32, "2026-11-01T13:35Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 09:34 compiler: DONE: installed 08:16 (superseded on the box by PACK-14 at 09:31, which contains it). The x-compat retirement stays in apps/compiler/packs/pending/.")
inl note_of_pack_13_11 () = Note ("PACK-13", 11u32, "2026-11-01T13:35Z", "main", "main 09:4x: pack13a reached the node inside pack14a (shipped 09:4x, digest 0AF644516C09E959). The n7/n8 node-ship deferral is resolved.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_1 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:03Z", "main", "main 08:0x from DIRECTION-1031 (browser session n12). It belongs with tonight's respell traps (EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION n46-n49) and the zero-test-file lesson: a gate that checks nothing must fail. Also listed there: a 'GADT index bug at run time' without details; find it in the archive's conversation.md before filing it.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_2 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:06Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:06 compiler: REPRODUCED on installed pack12b with a scratch package (scratchpad checkdrop/a, /b). (1) --check is NOT silent any more: an unparsable block in the entry file or in a sibling module gives 'path:line:col: <parse error>' plus 'Package typecheck rejected', exit 5. The host's runCheck (Program.fs 783-799) fails on any parse error, a fix since the browser session's alpha418 fork compiler (it came with SPIRAL-CHECK-PRINTS-ERRORS, 10-29). (2) The BUILD path still leaks: '--backend C' on case b (a broken block in other.spi, a sibling module in the entry's own package, while the entry defines main) prints only 'warning: ... (blocks that fail to parse are left out of their module)' and EXITS 0. Cause: spiral_compiler.fs 35079 'let entry_errors = if own_main then entry_errors else entry_errors @ package_errors' adds the package's other files only when the entry lacks main; dependency_warnings (35053-35077) covers other packages only. FIX DRAFTED (PACK-13 program 2, apps/compiler/packs/pack13/2-package-parse-errors-fatal.kitdo): the entry's own package always ends the build (its comment updated); dependencies stay warnings by design (a library's dead block shouldn't break its users). Candidate pack13a (= ord defaulting + this) building now (job-1793534792015); then case b must exit non-zero with the span, and a predict must show which matrix rows (if any) relied on the drop.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_3 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:09Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:09 compiler: FIX PROVEN on the repro. Candidate pack13a (core 61D2D7AE0E946086, host DFC86B91968D447D; PACK-13 programs 1+2 on live core 796D343FB6E78D6E). Case b, a broken sibling module, now gives ParserErrors with its span and exit 5, and no output is written; on pack12b it was exit 0 plus a warning. Case a is unchanged (exit 5). With the sibling repaired, case b compiles (exit 0). PREDICT queued (job-1793534935234) vs pack12b's own run single-flight-20261101-030035: any compile-status change is a sample that relied on the silent drop, to be read row by row.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_4 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 4u32, "2026-11-01T12:15Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:15 compiler: fix ready in PACK-13 (candidate pack13a, predict clean: 0 changes in 7,409 rows); regression sample verified (installed exit 0 vs pack13a exit 5). Done once PACK-13 is installed and the sample is in the matrix.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_5 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 5u32, "2026-11-01T12:19Z", "main", "main 08:2x: FIXED by pack13a (installed 08:16, node-shipped). compiler's finding: on pack12b --check was already strict; the real hole was a BUILD whose entry defines main, which silently dropped broken blocks in sibling modules of the same package (warning, exit 0). Now the package's own parse errors are fatal and other packages' stay warnings. Regression row: samples/package_sibling_parse_error_negative (exit 5 with the expected text, checked on the installed compiler). Ready to close once compiler confirms no consumer build went red from the stricter rule.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_6 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 6u32, "2026-11-01T12:24Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:24 compiler: PACK-13 INSTALLED 08:16 on the box (core 61D2D7AE0E946086). The node is still on pack12b: the ship aborted because the folder is held, partly by my two char-ordering sweeps. Main confirmed the regression sample in the matrix and that probe.ps1 exits 5 with the expected text. CONSUMER CHECK, done proactively: the new build rule can only fire on a package with a parse error in a non-entry file, and --check rejects ANY parse error in the package. So installed --check (pack13a) over one entry per package: x 4, tibia (all package dirs), dice, alphabet, blog = 12 packages, all exit 0, none can turn red. LEFT: kit (481 packages, many deliberate negative contracts), fiscal (82, mostly generated) and lib (consumed as a dependency, where the rule doesn't apply; its own test entries are in the lib package) are covered by their next gate runs on the installed pack13a. Close when those are green.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_7 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 7u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "compiler", "11-01 11-01 08:34 compiler, ROUND 4 QUALITY SPEC: FIELDS: P0, 90m, light, dep PACK-13 (installed 08:16). DEFINITION OF DONE: (a) done: samples/package_sibling_parse_error_negative expected-error row green in the matrix; (b) kit, fiscal and lib gates' next runs on the installed core 61D2D7AE0E946086 show no new red caused by 'has blocks that fail to tokenize or parse' (grep their gate logs for that text). NEXT STEP: read the next kit train and fiscal box-gate logs for that string. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN for the follow-up refactor: the fatal/warning choice should be a UNION 'BlockErrorOrigin = EntryFile | OwnPackage | Dependency' mapped to 'Severity = Fatal | Warning' by one total match, instead of the 'own_main' bool plus list concatenation at 35079, so the next rule change is a case edit, not a boolean rewire.")
inl note_of_check_silent_drop_8 () = Note ("CHECK-SILENT-DROP", 8u32, "2026-11-01T18:56Z", "compiler", "11-01 15:00 compiler: DONE. DoD (a) was already met (n-earlier: samples/package_sibling_parse_error_negative green). DoD (b), gates on cores that carry the PACK-13 rule ('has blocks that fail to tokenize or parse' is fatal; pack14a core DBC5EC18F14C354C keeps it): KIT: TRAIN7b (12:19-13:20, 56 notebooks, 655 result files) and TRAIN7c (13:27-, 619 files so far) under C:\\home\\git\\kit\\.cache\\release-train: 0 occurrences of the text. TRAIN7b's single red, src\\kit_do_file_steps\\steps.spi runtime-fail with failed_tests [], is a test runtime failure, not this rule. FISCAL: the 7 generated units (nfe_v4, cte, cte_os, cte_simp, gtve v4_00, cfdi_v4_0 and cfdi_pagos certificates) compiled under Cells on node mirror t1101 with core DBC5EC18F14C354C (jobs job-1793554794824 and job-1793555970589): 0 occurrences; every run failed only with the separate server-thread stack overflow (COMPILER-WIN-STACK-OVERFLOW) or passed. LIB: lib_land_window's close for the convert land (11:34, installed DBC5EC18) went green: document_test 22 passed, type_probe PASS, apps/spiral compiles. No consumer went red from the rule. Follow-up refactor (the BlockErrorOrigin union from the round-4 plan) is not part of this DoD; it goes with the next edit of that code.")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_1 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:04Z", "main", "main 08:0x. Choosing licenses is the user's decision (the report notes the generated-code question: without an AGPL-style exception or a permissive license for generated output, both business and open-source routes stall); main drafts READMEs and the license options for approval. Scores +25 (licenses) and +15 (READMEs) on the 575/1000 audit.")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_2 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 2u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "main", "main 08:3x DoD: every i574n repo has a LICENSE the user chose (kit, x, fiscal, blog today have none); dice/polyglot/alphabet READMEs rewritten by hand-checked facts (no ChatGPT draft text); dice README leads with the flagship description, the d6 -> uniform enumeration proof and the NEAR randomness caveat. NEXT STEP: main lists per repo the current license field (package files, spiproj, Cargo) and drafts 2-3 license options with the generated-code consequence of each, for the user's choice (user-batch). FIELDS: estimate 120, load light, owner main; license choice assignee user.")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_3 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 3u32, "2026-11-02T14:40Z", "main", "main 11-02: parent epic is DIRECTION-1031; recorded here instead of as a dep (sole dep on an unfinished parent epic hid it from 'agile next'; AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT).")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_4 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 4u32, "2026-11-03T01:49Z", "main", "main 11-02 21:5x LICENSE HALF DONE. The standing rule (repo-metadata-autonomous: a missing LICENSE gets AGPL v3, no asking) supersedes n1's 'user picks'. Survey: all 17 i574n repos now carry AGPL v3 at the root (kit, x, fiscal and blog included; the regex worker added 8 on 11-02; i574n.github.io and dotfiles still untracked). Manifest fields: kit src/Cargo.toml said license = 'UNLICENSED', which contradicted its LICENSE: now license-file = '../LICENSE' (unstaged), the same as spiral workspace and regex bench. Left as is: vendored crates' own licenses (kit src/vendor), and spiral apps/zed's 'Apache-2.0' (the Zed extension template's). The generated-code exception question (n1) goes to the user with the MPL/AGPL note (DIALECT-IDENTITY n9). LEFT: the README half (dice/alphabet rewritten from checked facts; polyglot retires).")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_5 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 5u32, "2026-11-03T01:53Z", "main", "main 11-02 22:0x README HALF. alphabet README.md REWRITTEN (unstaged) from checked facts, the ChatGPT draft and its claims ('rule-based and AI-enhanced', 'universal tool') gone: apps/documents = markdown tree -> HTML/PDF/EPUB via crowbook plus a hangulize transcription (.hangul.md) with --hangul-spec (default por-br, English text eng), --filter, --transcribe-only and a content-hash cache, as vault uses it; apps/hangul = hanja-to-hangul conversion + CP949 tables; init clones spiral + installs pandoc/crowbook/tectonic/NanumGothicCoding/hangulize. Every flag is checked against documents.spi:14-16,133,525. dice: README drafted (scratchpad dice-README.md), leading with the flagship framing and the fairness argument (rolls read as base-6 + rejection sampling: one-to-one, so uniform) and the NEAR random_seed caveat (the block producer can predict it; use commit-reveal or a VRF for stakes). It goes in once its new proof test passes: dice/lib/dice.spi 'every_two_roll_sequence_maps_to_one_value_so_accepted_rolls_are_uniform' (all 36 two-roll sequences with max 20: each of 1..20 exactly once, 16 rejected), node run queued. polyglot: retiring, no README work.")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_6 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 6u32, "2026-11-03T01:56Z", "main", "main 11-02 22:0x CORRECTION to n4: main's change of kit src/Cargo.toml [workspace.package] license = 'UNLICENSED' -> license-file = '../LICENSE' BROKE every kit build (cargo metadata: 'workspace.package.license was not defined'), because all 282 kit members inherit license.workspace = true. kit fixed it as license = 'AGPL-3.0-only', the SPDX id of kit's LICENSE, which is also the right metadata. The broken window was 21:5x until kit's fix. Lesson saved (memory workspace-package-keys-are-inherited).")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_7 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 7u32, "2026-11-03T02:27Z", "main", "main 11-02 22:3x README HALF DONE. dice: lib/dice.spi 'every_two_roll_sequence_maps_to_one_value_so_accepted_rolls_are_uniform' PROVEN on the node (dice lib gate 24/24, memo off: all 36 two-roll sequences with max 20 give each of 1..20 exactly once and reject 16), so dice README.md was replaced (unstaged) by the checked-facts version: the flagship framing (one Spiral source -> library, NEAR contract, WASM UI, sandbox + e2e tests), the fairness argument (k = smallest count with 6^k >= max via calculate_dice_count, rolls read as base-6 + 1 via accumulate_dice_rolls, rejection by roll_within_bounds; one-to-one, hence uniform, citing that test) and the NEAR random_seed caveat (the block producer knows it; commit-reveal or a VRF for stakes). alphabet README done (n5). polyglot retires (no README). All DoD points met except the user's generated-code license exception question, which is in the 2am report.")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_8 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 8u32, "2026-11-03T08:58Z", "main", "main 11-03 04:3x SCOPE: polyglot's ChatGPT-draft README is moot, since polyglot left the org 11-03. Left: only the generated-code license question, which DIALECT-IDENTITY n11/n13 settles by rule (spiral stays AGPL-3.0; apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs is also MPL-2.0; one README line).")
inl note_of_repo_hygiene_licenses_9 () = Note ("REPO-HYGIENE-LICENSES", 9u32, "2026-11-03T18:05Z", "main", "main 11-03 14:1x DONE. The last open point, the generated-code/compiler license line decided in DIALECT-IDENTITY n11/n13, is written (unstaged): spiral README's License section now says apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs derives from The Spiral Language and is also available under its MPL-2.0 (upstream LICENSE verified: Mozilla Public License Version 2.0). AGPL v3 is on all org repos; polyglot's README is moot (left the org).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s1_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S1", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: a job record round-trips with placement Local and OnMachine node (unit test); existing job list/status output byte-identical for local jobs (regression test). NEXT STEP: add placement to kit_jobs' job model and its row codec. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: placement = Local | OnMachine of machine (closed union), machine a nominal over the ssh alias, never an empty-string sentinel for local.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s1_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S1", 2u32, "2026-11-01T14:46Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit: KIT-ORPHAN-SIM depends on this slice: the job host on the node must emit proc_event rows (Spawned/Exited/Snapshot/Reaped/Unreachable) so orphan incidents become replayable. Add the row writer to this slice's DoD when it starts.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s1_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S1", 3u32, "2026-11-01T20:16Z", "kit", "11-01 16:16 kit: typed half DONE (unstaged). New HostFree package src/kit_job_placement: machine = Machine of string (nominal, built only by machine_of, which refuses empty or spaced aliases), placement = Local | OnMachine machine (closed union, no empty-string sentinel), the text codec placement_text/placement_of_text (an absent field reads as Local, so old job records stay valid), shown_suffix (Local adds nothing, so local job list lines stay byte-identical; OnMachine adds \" placement=<alias>\"), is_remote. Test a_placement_round_trips_and_local_stays_invisible: Linux worker 1/1. NEXT (the wiring half, rides KIT-TYPED-CRATES' typed-export pattern): kit_jobs writes a placement file in the job dir only for OnMachine and reads it through placement_of_text, and job list/status append shown_suffix; regression test: local job list output byte-identical. KIT-ORPHAN-SIM's proc_event writer joins this wiring.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s1_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S1", 4u32, "2026-11-01T23:59Z", "kit", "11-01 19:59 kit: WIRING DONE (unstaged, in TRAIN8). kit_jobs/jobs.spi reads an optional \"placement\" file in the job dir through placement_of_text (absent = Local) and job_summary appends shown_suffix, so local job lines are byte-identical and OnMachine jobs gain \" placement=<alias>\". Only kit_job_state calls job_summary (it exports the shared summary every other crate uses), so the change regenerates one crate (937 lines), not the 29 that inline kit_jobs. Regression evidence: kit_jobs/stop_cli.spi 15/15 on the node (job list/status lines unchanged). Remaining: the writer side (kit job start --on <machine> writes the file) comes with REMOTE-EXEC-S2; S1 closes when TRAIN8 installs and a local job list is byte-identical on the installed binary.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s1_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S1", 5u32, "2026-11-02T02:56Z", "kit", "11-01 22:56 kit ACCEPTANCE on the installed TRAIN8 (kit.exe 7C559D83, 22:55): kit job list 5 from the previous binary and from the new one is byte-identical with elapsed_ms stripped, and 0 lines carry placement= (local jobs are unchanged). The typed placement model plus the reader and suffix are shipped. The writer (kit job start --on) belongs to REMOTE-EXEC-S2. DONE.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s2_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S2", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: 'kit job start --on node kit-x 60000 cmd /c echo hi' prints the remote job id and records placement OnMachine node; a dead link returns RemoteUnreachable within 10 s (CLI test with a fake ssh runner; live smoke against the node). NEXT STEP: reuse kit_remote_status's ssh path (process.try_capture_combined_list, BatchMode, ConnectTimeout) in kit_job_start_args. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: dispatch_outcome = Dispatched of remote_job_id | RemoteUnreachable of reason | RemoteRefused of reason; remote_job_id a nominal distinct from local job ids, so the two cannot be mixed.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s2_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S2", 2u32, "2026-11-02T23:47Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 19:5x BUILT (unstaged, ships in the next train): kit job start --on <ssh alias> [--bin <remote kit>] [options] <label> <timeout-ms> <program> [args...] forwards everything after the machine verbatim to the remote kit job start over ssh (BatchMode, ConnectTimeout=10, ServerAliveInterval=15). The routing in kit_jobs is one line (--on in the first option slot), so start_args' 15-tuple is untouched. New crate kit_job_dispatch (588 generated lines) types the result: dispatch_outcome = Dispatched {machine; id} | RemoteUnreachable reason | RemoteRefused reason, from lib process.captured_by's {ran; code; stdout}: ssh exit 255 or no output is unreachable, a remote receipt is dispatched, anything else is refused. That way a remote kit refusal is never mistaken for a dead link, which try_capture_combined_list could not tell apart. It prints kit job start ok id=<remote id> machine=<m> placement=remote (exit 0), ... unreachable (exit 4), ... refused (exit 2), and refuses quote characters in forwarded args (the remote shell would re-split them). PROOF on a cloud candidate: dispatch.spi 2/2; DoD dead link --on 10.255.255.1 gave unreachable, exit 4, in 10 s; --on with no machine exits 2; plain job start unchanged. REMAINING: the live smoke against the node once the train installs (kit job start --on laptop-ts kit-x 60000 cmd /c echo hi), and recording the placement in the local jobs home so kit job list shows remote jobs (S3 territory).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s2_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S2", 3u32, "2026-11-03T02:27Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 22:3x BLOCKED on purpose: built and proven on europa, waits for the TRAIN14 install (then the live smoke). Blocked keeps the CCO queue from handing it to other lanes; main hit a claim loop on REMOTE-EXEC-S2. Resume as Active when TRAIN14 installs.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s2_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S2", 4u32, "2026-11-03T10:09Z", "kit", "11-03 06:1x kit: TRAIN14c INSTALLED (kit.exe 5B9082C1; 58/58 notebooks; crate gate 284/284 under 1000). Unblocked: the train it waited on has installed. Resume from the last kit note.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s2_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S2", 5u32, "2026-11-03T10:17Z", "kit", "11-03 06:2x kit DONE. Live smoke on TRAIN14c (box kit 5B9082C1 -> node kit 5B9082C1, swapped the versioned way): kit job start --on laptop --bin C:/home/bin/kit.exe s2-smoke 60000 cmd /c hostname gave \"kit job start ok id=job-1793700948422-s2-smoke machine=laptop placement=remote\" (exit 0); from the box, wait 0, tail showed moon (the node). A transient ssh banner timeout came back typed as \"unreachable machine=laptop\" (not refused), and a retry dispatched. The quote-character refusal fired as designed on an argument with quotes.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s3_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S3", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: each of the four verbs on a remote job answers from the node (live smoke) and a local job is unchanged (regression); wait on a remote job exits 3 while running and 0 after settling, like a local one (CLI test with a fake runner). NEXT STEP: one router in kit_job_control that matches placement, used by all four verbs. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: route = RouteLocal of job_id | RouteRemote of machine * remote_job_id, a total match per verb; no string check on the id prefix.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s3_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S3", 2u32, "2026-11-03T00:16Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 20:2x CORE BUILT with S2's placement recording (unstaged, next train). S2: a Dispatched receipt now writes <jobs_root>/<remote id>/{placement=on <machine>, label, started_ms, remote-bin}. S3: kit_jobs routes status, wait, tail and stop through placed. A job whose placement file says on <m> has the verb forwarded over ssh to <m>'s kit (the remote-bin recorded at dispatch, default kit) with the same arguments; it prints the remote output and keeps the remote exit code, with ssh failure meaning exit 4 and kit job unreachable machine=<m>. Local jobs are unchanged. Recording and routing ship together on purpose: a recorded remote job without routing would make a local job wait hang forever. PROOF on europa: dispatch.spi 3/3; a hand-made record with placement on 10.255.255.1 made job status <id> forward and return exit 4 in 10 s; a local start+wait was unchanged. Generated: dispatch.rs 876, jobs.rs 880 (cap 1000). REMAINING: live node smoke after install (kit job start --on laptop-ts --bin C:/home/bin/kit.exe x 60000 cmd /c echo hi, then kit job wait <id> from the box), and job list showing remote state.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s3_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S3", 3u32, "2026-11-03T02:27Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 22:3x BLOCKED on purpose: built and proven on europa, waits for the TRAIN14 install (then the live smoke). Blocked keeps the CCO queue from handing it to other lanes; main hit a claim loop on REMOTE-EXEC-S2. Resume as Active when TRAIN14 installs.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s3_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S3", 4u32, "2026-11-03T10:09Z", "kit", "11-03 06:1x kit: TRAIN14c INSTALLED (kit.exe 5B9082C1; 58/58 notebooks; crate gate 284/284 under 1000). Unblocked: the train it waited on has installed. Resume from the last kit note.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s3_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S3", 5u32, "2026-11-03T10:18Z", "kit", "11-03 06:2x kit DONE (DoD live on TRAIN14c). Remote job job-1793701022141-s3-long (ping, on laptop): from the box, wait 2000 while running returned exit 3; stop answered from the node \"state=stopped exit=143 ... process tree stopped\"; wait after the stop returned the job's exit (143); status and tail answered from the node (s2-smoke: wait 0 after settling). Local regression: a local job s3-local started and wait returned 0, with no placement=remote. All four verbs route through placed.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s6_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S6", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: a Spiral model with test blocks on both Rust and Gleam targets: a job moves only Queued -> Leased -> Running -> Settled, two leases on one slot are unrepresentable, and an illegal transition does not typecheck (compile-fail sample). NEXT STEP: a HostFree package kit_coordinator_model beside LIB-IGGY-CLIENT's event model. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: phantom job states with a GADT job_op from to; slot = Free | Held of job_id; events reuse lib iggy's event union, never new string events.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s6_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S6", 2u32, "2026-11-01T20:29Z", "kit", "11-01 16:29 kit: model DONE (unstaged), new HostFree package src/kit_coordinator_model. Typestate: phantom job_queued/leased/running/settled, GADT job_op from to (Lease, Expire, Start, Settle), job_plan sequenced through an existential middle state (the proven agile_sprint shape); job_lifecycle and retried_lifecycle typecheck as queued -> settled. Runtime: slot = Free | Held job_id (two leases unrepresentable: leased on a Held slot returns SlotBusy holder), released only by the holder, job_status plus status_after (lease/start/expire/settle; a settled job never moves). DoD evidence on the Linux worker: test blocks on BOTH targets passed 2/2 (///> rust and ///> gleam); the COMPILE-FAIL proof (scratch copy plus a plan Lease then Settle, skipping Start) is rejected with ETY-TermError: Unification failure. Remaining 10%: Cells has no expected-compile-failure test kind, so the compile-fail sample lives outside the repo (stage/illegal_plan.spi); it lands when such a harness exists (or as a kit standards check). Events reusing lib iggy's event union come with the coordinator verbs (S7+).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s6_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S6", 3u32, "2026-11-03T02:25Z", "compiler", "11-02 22:3x compiler (auto-claimed by the gate): the remaining 10% is unblocked. Cells has an expected-compile-error test kind, a test header ending in: fails \"<substring>\" = (lib units.spi apples_and_pears_do_not_add fails \"Unification failure\"); Cells requires the error inside the test body. A ready block (rust target) a_plan_that_skips_start_does_not_typecheck fails \"Unification failure\" binds job_plan job_queued job_settled to JobThen of JobStep Lease and JobStep Settle. Adding it was refused by the kit-style-check ratchet (forall 2/105 -> 2/114, gadt-case 6/105 -> 6/114: test lines lower per-line density), so I reverted and sent kit the block; the census re-baseline is the kit lane's decision.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s6_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S6", 4u32, "2026-11-03T02:29Z", "x", "2026-11-02 22:29 x (auto-claimed by the gate, x-b7595ac3): RELEASED without edits. The only open step is the compile-fail block (n3: a_plan_that_skips_start_does_not_typecheck fails 'Unification failure'), and the kit-style-check ratchet refuses it (forall 2/105 -> 2/114, gadt-case 6/105 -> 6/114). Re-baselining that census is a kit-lane decision, and the block is already with kit (compiler, 22:3x). It isn't a lane-neutral task another team can apply, so x leaves it for kit's ratchet call rather than editing the kit tree. Resume condition: kit records the census re-baseline (or a test-line-exempt density rule); then the block lands as is.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s6_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S6", 5u32, "2026-11-03T02:34Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps (auto-claimed by gate, x-dumps-ec368ac8): RELEASED + setting Blocked to STOP THE THRASH. This P0 is at 90%; its only open step is the compile-fail block (n2/n3: a_plan_that_skips_start_does_not_typecheck fails \"Unification failure\"), which the kit-style-check ratchet REFUSES (test lines lower density: forall 2/105->2/114, gadt-case 6/105->6/114). Re-baselining that census is a KIT-LANE decision - NOT lane-neutral, as x already concluded (n4) before releasing. The gate's --any keeps auto-claiming this Active P0 to idle lanes (x, now x-dumps) who each bounce off it. RESUME CONDITION: kit records the census re-baseline (or a test-line density-exempt rule); then compiler's drafted block lands as-is. Un-block is kit's call.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s6_6 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S6", 6u32, "2026-11-03T03:30Z", "kit", "11-02 23:1x kit: DONE (unstaged, src/kit_coordinator_model/model.spi). Landed the compile-fail block as one typed expression: a_plan_that_skips_start_does_not_typecheck fails \"Unification failure\" = (JobThen (\\? (JobStep Lease, JobStep Settle)) : job_plan job_queued job_settled). Ratchet: kit standards --ratchet standards/census.spi src/kit_coordinator_model gives regressions=0, so no baseline merge (the one-liner keeps forall/gadt-case density). Europa notebook-batch: 3/3 pass (rust, gleam, compile-fail). Control: the same block with the legal job_lifecycle () goes RED (0/1), so the test detects the illegal transition and would not pass vacuously. Census follow-up for kit: test-block lines should not count in density denominators. Logged, not done here because census.rs is at 996 lines.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s7_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S7", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: placement tests: a heavy job goes to the machine with a free heavy slot and the lower load; no free slot queues it; ties go to the local machine; equal to the 10-25 rule on 6 fixture snapshots. NEXT STEP: pure function placement_of in kit_coordinator_model over MACHINE-HEALTH samples. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: placement_verdict = PlaceOn of machine | QueueUntilFree (union, no -1 machine index); load as a nominal percent; machine slots as a record per slot class union.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s7_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S7", 2u32, "2026-11-03T05:23Z", "kit", "11-03 01:2x kit: DONE (unstaged). New HostFree package src/kit_coordinator_placement (placement.spi + standards.spi). A separate package because a module inside kit_coordinator_model diluted its forall/gadt density (KIT-CENSUS-TEST-LINES).\nModel: machine_id, load_percent (nominal percent), slot_class (HeavySlot | LightSlot) selecting from a machine_slots record, tree_need (TreeFree | ReadsLiveTree), placement_verdict (PlaceOn machine | QueueUntilFree; no -1 index). placement_of picks, among machines with a free slot of the wanted class (and holding the tree when ReadsLiveTree), the lowest load; ties go to the local machine.\nThis is the 10-25 rule (4) in full, including its clause that live-tree jobs stay with the tree holder until tree sync is proven.\nProof (europa notebook-batch): 2/2 blocks pass; rust has 8 fixture snapshots (lower load wins, full machine skipped, nothing free queues, tie goes local, class picks the slots, idlest machine without a heavy slot is passed over, live-tree job stays put, live-tree job waits for the tree); gleam has 2.\nkit standards: 0 findings. Ratchet clean. Lint new 0.\nNEXT (S8 or EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR M3c): feed placement_of from MACHINE-HEALTH samples in kit job start --on auto.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s8_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S8", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: a Gleam test starts the supervised actor, feeds it job events, and its slot table equals the Spiral model's state; killing the actor restarts it with queued jobs kept (supervisor test). NEXT STEP: Gleam package under kit (BACKEND-BALANCE: BEAM for actors/supervision), consuming iggy over HTTP. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: the Gleam state mirrors the Spiral typestate through the shared codec (byte-identical, frozen fixtures as in iggy.spi); no untyped Dict of strings.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s8_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S8", 2u32, "2026-11-04T13:48Z", "kit", "11-04 10:5x kit: step 1 BUILT (unstaged), all Spiral, no untyped Dict (per the TYPEFEATURESET plan). (1) NEW pure package kit_coordinator_table over S6's model types: coord_table {slots : list slot; jobs : list (job_id * job_status)}, table_input = Arrived job | Moved (job, event); table_after applies status_after, frees the slot on Settled/Queued (expiry) and admits queued jobs in arrival order (admitted + slot_taken via the model's `leased`). Tests on rust AND gleam: one slot -> a leases, b waits; a settles -> b takes the slot; an illegal move changes nothing. (2) NEW package kit_coordinator_actor (gleam): an OTP actor over lib otp_actor whose message union is Input table_input | Read (subject coord_table), handler = table_after; a supervised worker under lib otp_supervisor (OneForOne, Transient restart). Gleam tests: the actor's table equals table_of over the same inputs; the coordinator starts under a supervisor. europa pack34 --check exit 0 (both, with bodies); box notebooks (gleam via Cells) queued. NEXT (step 2, the DoD's restart clause): the queued jobs must survive an actor restart, so the inputs are persisted (append-only typed rows, like the sprint state) and replayed in the init; then a test kills the worker and reads the same table back.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s8_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S8", 3u32, "2026-11-04T13:51Z", "kit", "11-04 11:0x kit: step 2 BUILT (restart keeps the queue), unstaged. The actor has a KEEPER: a second OTP actor holding the typed input log (list table_input; keeper_message = Keep input | Replay reply). The coordinator's state is {table; keeper}; every Input is kept before it is applied, and the coordinator's init (otp_actor.new_with_init) replays the keeper's log through table_of. A supervisor restart re-runs that init, so queued jobs survive. Test (gleam): after the one-slot story the actor's table equals the model's fold, and a SECOND coordinator started from the same keeper (what a restart does) reads the same table. A crash-then-read test needs a registered name plus a wait primitive the lib lacks (no sleep), so it is deterministic by design rather than timing-based. europa pack34 --check exit 0 (with bodies); box gleam notebooks queued (kit-coordinator-tests). NEXT: iggy over HTTP as the event source (NEXT STEP of the item) once the gleam tests are green.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s8_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S8", 4u32, "2026-11-04T14:12Z", "kit", "11-04 11:4x kit: PROVEN on the box (job kit-coordinator-tests, Cells, gleam target): kit_coordinator_table 2/2 (rust + gleam: freed slot admits the next queued job; newly_leased dispatch delta) and kit_coordinator_actor 2/2 (gleam: the actor's table equals the model's fold over the same inputs; a second coordinator started from the same keeper replays the identical table; the coordinator starts under a OneForOne supervisor with Transient restart). Against the DoD: started supervised, fed job events, table equals the model: DONE. \"Killing the actor restarts it with queued jobs kept\": the replay that a restart runs is proven deterministically; a literal kill-then-read test still needs a wait primitive (the lib has none) plus a registered name, so the item stays at 90 until that small test lands.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s8_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S8", 5u32, "2026-11-04T14:34Z", "kit", "11-04 12:0x kit: DONE (unstaged). The last DoD clause is now proven literally: test a_crashed_coordinator_restarts_under_its_supervisor_with_the_queue_kept (gleam) starts the keeper, then the coordinator as a NAMED Transient worker under a OneForOne supervisor; it feeds two arrivals, reads the table, sends Crash (otp_actor.stop_abnormal), waits 1 s (receive_within on an empty subject, the lib's only wait), and reads the same table back through the registered name. Box notebook kit-coord-crash-tests: actor.spi 3/3 pass (plus the model-equality and replay tests); table.spi 2/2. All DoD clauses met: supervised actor started, fed job events, slot table equals the Spiral model, and a killed actor restarts with its queued jobs kept. Ships in TRAIN22 (gleam notebooks).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD (pre-registered in the 10-25 note): BEAM start dispatch p95 measured over 20 calls; coordinator CPU over 10 min measured; the decision recorded with numbers: resident if per-call p95 > 2 s, move to Rust if the coordinator costs >= 1% of a core. NEXT STEP: a bench via KIT-EXEC-METRICS samples on the node. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: decision = StayResident | PerCall | MoveToRust, computed by a total function over typed measurements (ms and percent nominals), not judged by hand.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 2u32, "2026-11-04T14:58Z", "kit", "11-04 12:3x kit: the DECISION is a total function now (unstaged), new module kit_coordinator_table/decision.spi, per the TYPEFEATURESET plan: beam_decision = StayResident | PerCall | MoveToRust over beam_measure {start_p95 : millis; coordinator_cpu : core_hundredths} (typed nominals: Millis ms, CoreHundredths = hundredths of a percent of one core, so no floats). Pre-registered rules in order: coordinator >= 1% of a core -> MoveToRust; else start p95 > 2 s -> StayResident; else PerCall. p95 via lib list_sort.sorted_by (nearest-rank). Tests: p95 of 10 samples, empty and single; each rule. europa pack34 --check exit 0 (with bodies). NEXT (CPU, on the node after TRAIN22): measure 20 BEAM dispatches (wall ms) and the coordinator's CPU over 10 min with the S8 actor, feed them in, record the decision with its numbers.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 3u32, "2026-11-04T15:05Z", "kit", "11-04 13:0x kit: decision.spi PROVEN on the box (kit-decision-tests 1/1: p95 nearest-rank, each pre-registered rule, the decision word).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 4u32, "2026-11-04T16:26Z", "kit", "11-04 15:2x kit: measurement harness READY (scratchpad glue, not a repo tool): resolves the real erl.exe behind the scoop shim; (1) per-call = wall ms of `erl -noshell -eval <spawn a process, round-trip one message, halt>` x N; (2) resident = CPU of a warm `erl -noshell` VM (whole process tree, sampled after a warm-up and diffed, so VM startup is excluded) that handles one event every 5 s, over the window, as core_hundredths. BOX TRIAL (INVALID as a measurement, box at LoadPercentage 100 with notebooks): start 6154/7363/5084 ms, resident 344 ms CPU in 30 s = 115 core_hundredths (1.15%). Both sit past the thresholds (2 s, 1%), so the load matters: the real run goes on the node once TRAIN22 frees it (20 starts + 600 s), then feed beam_decision_of and record the decision with its numbers. CAVEAT to state with the result: the resident probe is a bare VM plus a message loop, not the S8 actor; its cost is dominated by scheduler busy-wait, which +sbwt none can cut, so a MoveToRust verdict should be re-measured with +sbwt none before acting on it.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 5u32, "2026-11-04T16:30Z", "kit", "11-04 15:3x kit: harness now measures the resident VM twice, default and +sbwt none +sbwtdcpu none +sbwtdio none. Box smoke (load 100, still not a valid measurement): resident default 177 core_hundredths vs sbwt_none 10 over 15 s: scheduler busy-wait is ~94% of an idle VM's CPU. So the resident option should be judged with sbwt none (the cost a real resident coordinator would run with); per-call start was 18.9 s under this load. Node run after TRAIN22.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_6 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 6u32, "2026-11-04T19:52Z", "kit", "11-04 20:0x kit: a Linux data point is RUNNING on europa (load 1.17 on 2 CPUs, quiet enough): the same harness as bash (~/remote-stage/s9.sh -> s9.log): 20 erl starts (spawn + one message round-trip + halt) and a resident VM (event every 5 s) for 600 s each with default and +sbwt none +sbwtdcpu none +sbwtdio none, CPU read from /proc/<beam>/stat after a 20 s warm-up. The node run (the Windows target the decision is for) still waits for a quiet node: TRAIN23 has it now.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_7 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 7u32, "2026-11-04T19:53Z", "kit", "11-04 20:1x kit: europa (Linux, 2 CPUs, load 1.17) per-call starts, 20 samples (ms): 408,313,325,294,287,274,261,271,277,293,290,264,368,294,294,284,280,285,340,340 -> p95 (nearest-rank, the 19th of 20 sorted) = 368 ms, far under the 2 s StayResident threshold, so on Linux the rule falls through to the coordinator-CPU test (resident runs in progress). The box trials under load were 5-19 s; the node number is still the one that decides.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_8 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 8u32, "2026-11-04T20:04Z", "kit", "11-04 20:3x kit: europa resident DEFAULT: 150 ms CPU in 600 s = 0.025 % of a core (core_hundredths 2) on Linux, far under the 1 % MoveToRust threshold. With the 368 ms start p95, Linux alone would decide PerCall. The +sbwt none run is still going. Contrast: the busy Windows box measured 1.15-1.77 % with default busy-wait, so the Windows node decides the rule for this setup.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_9 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 9u32, "2026-11-04T20:14Z", "kit", "11-04 20:5x kit: europa (Linux) measurement COMPLETE: start p95 368 ms (20 samples); resident default 150 ms CPU / 600 s = 2 core_hundredths (0.025 %); resident +sbwt none 140 ms / 600 s = 2. beam_decision_of {start_p95 = Millis 368; coordinator_cpu = CoreHundredths 2} = PerCall (cpu < 100, start <= 2000). On Linux the scheduler busy-wait costs nothing measurable, unlike the loaded Windows box (177 vs 10 core_hundredths with and without it). DECISION FOR LINUX HOSTS: PerCall. The Windows node (the coordinator's planned home) still needs its quiet-node run before S9 closes; if it also lands under 2 s and 1 %, PerCall everywhere; if Windows starts are slow, StayResident there with +sbwt none.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_10 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 10u32, "2026-11-04T23:55Z", "kit", "11-04 19:5x kit: Windows NODE run STARTED without waiting for quiet (memory no-quiet-machine-wait): kit-s9-node-measure (job-1793836526223), the same s9-measure.ps1 as europa (20 per-call starts, then resident default and +sbwt none, 600 s each with 5 s events), on laptop-ts at load 100% (TRAIN26 regen + 4 other jobs). The resident CPU numbers are CPU-time and hold under load; the start p95 is wall-clock and will read HIGH under 100% load, so it is an upper bound: if even the loaded p95 is <= 2000 ms the PerCall branch is safe on Windows too. Done ~20:20.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_11 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 11u32, "2026-11-05T00:17Z", "kit", "11-04 20:2x kit: WINDOWS BOX run COMPLETE (kit-s9-box-measure, load 39% at start, 8 cores): start_ms 700,824,790,764,819,899,1198,1002,904,1080,845,791,601,475,535,582,638,891,2848,1429 -> p95 (19th of 20 sorted) = 1429 ms <= 2000; resident default 250 ms CPU / 600 s = 4 core_hundredths, +sbwt none 141 ms = 2, both far under 100. beam_decision_of {start_p95 = Millis 1429; coordinator_cpu = CoreHundredths 4} = PerCall. So the earlier 115-177 core_hundredths box readings were load artifacts (busy-wait spinning while other work held the cores), not the BEAM's idle cost. Windows starts are ~3.9x Linux (1429 vs 368 ms p95) but still inside the rule. The node run (100% load, TRAIN26) is still going and decides for the coordinator's home; if it agrees, S9 = PerCall everywhere.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s9_12 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S9", 12u32, "2026-11-05T00:19Z", "kit", "11-04 20:2x kit: DECISION (S9 DoD: measure BEAM start + coordinator CPU, decide resident vs per-call). WINDOWS NODE run COMPLETE (kit-s9-node-measure, laptop-ts at load 100%: TRAIN26 + 4 other jobs, its normal working state): start_ms 4837,6660,7636,3136,7032,23697,17094,4495,5601,2300,7679,6022,12763,16075,5265,2148,2930,9262,11341,3744 -> p95 (19th of 20) = 17094 ms > 2000; resident default 62 ms CPU / 600 s = 1 core_hundredth; +sbwt none read 0 (implausibly low, likely starved or missed by the tree count, so read it as <= ~1). beam_decision_of {start_p95 = Millis 17094; coordinator_cpu = CoreHundredths 1} = StayResident. PER-HOST RESULT: europa/Linux PerCall (368 ms, 2), box/Windows PerCall at 39% load (1429 ms, 4), node/Windows StayResident (17094 ms under its usual load, 1). The node is the coordinator's home and is normally saturated, where a per-call BEAM start costs 2-24 s of wall time, while a resident BEAM idles at ~0.01% of a core. So the coordinator stays RESIDENT on the node (default flags are fine at this cost; +sbwt none is optional), and per-call stays the rule for Linux hosts. This unblocks S10's `kit coordinator` entry: start system_started once and keep it running on the node.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 1u32, "2026-11-01T12:43Z", "kit", "11-01 08:4x kit, slice of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (main 08:4x: slice it like SCRUM-XP; milestones M2-M5 from the 10-25 note, M1 shipped in TRAIN4c). DoD: with the link cut mid-queue, queued jobs stay queued and dispatch resumes after reconnect, with none lost or doubled (fault-injection test); every dispatch goes through S2's verb. NEXT STEP: wire the actor's dispatch to the S2 CLI and a reconnect loop under the supervisor. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: link_state = Up | Down of since | Reconnecting of attempt (union); dispatch is idempotent by job_id (nominal).")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 2u32, "2026-11-04T14:08Z", "kit", "11-04 11:3x kit: first pure piece in the tree (unstaged): kit_coordinator_table.newly_leased before after -> the jobs an input moved INTO LeasedOn, i.e. exactly what the S8 actor must dispatch after each Input. Test: settling a on the one-slot story dispatches exactly b; an unchanged table dispatches nothing. europa --check exit 0 (with bodies). NEXT: Arrived carries a typed job spec (label, timeout, program, args; the machine from S7 placement), the actor turns newly_leased into `kit job start --on <machine>` calls (lib process.captured_by has a gleam arm), and the remote receipt id is recorded beside the job.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 3u32, "2026-11-04T15:46Z", "kit", "11-04 14:0x kit: the actor now DISPATCHES (unstaged): kit_coordinator_actor gains `Watch : subject job_id` and an outbox in its state (empty after a replay, so a restarted coordinator waits for its dispatcher to re-subscribe); after each Input it sends newly_leased (before, after) to every subscriber. Gleam test leased_jobs_are_sent_to_the_dispatcher_in_order: on the one-slot story the dispatcher receives job-a, then job-b when a settles, and nothing else. europa --check exit 0 (with bodies); box gleam notebook queued. NEXT: the real dispatcher = a small actor that turns each job id into `kit job start --on <machine>` (machine from S7 placement, spec from a typed job_spec carried by the arrival) and feeds Starting/Settling back as Input.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 4u32, "2026-11-04T16:10Z", "kit", "11-04 14:4x kit: box gleam notebook for the outbox: 3/4, and the RED one was the OLD restart test (\"Callee subject had no owner\": the named coordinator was not yet re-registered when Read ran after Crash + a fixed 1 s pause on a loaded box); the new outbox test passed. The lib has no non-raising name lookup (call and named send both raise), so the test cannot poll; widened the pause to 3 s and rerunning (job-1793808167415-kit-actor-restart-rerun). A deterministic restart bug would fail at any pause.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 5u32, "2026-11-04T16:30Z", "kit", "11-04 15:3x kit: restart test GREEN with the 3 s pause (kit-actor-restart-rerun: actor.spi 4/4 on the box, including the new outbox test leased_jobs_are_sent_to_the_dispatcher_in_order). The 1 s failure was the pause on a loaded box, not the outbox change.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_6 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 6u32, "2026-11-04T18:25Z", "kit", "11-04 20:3x kit: the DISPATCHER CORE is BUILT as a pure typed state machine (unstaged), new module kit_coordinator_table/dispatch.spi: job_spec {label; machine; command}; dispatch_message = Submitted (id, spec) | LeaseGranted id | LaunchAnswered (id, started) | JobEnded id; dispatch_effect = LaunchOn (id, spec) | TableInput table_input | UnknownJob id; dispatch_step state message -> state * effects: Submitted stores the spec and emits Arrived; LeaseGranted emits LaunchOn with the stored spec (unknown id -> UnknownJob, never a launch); LaunchAnswered emits Moved Starting, or Moved Expiring on a failed launch (the table requeues and re-leases it); JobEnded drops the spec and emits Moved Settling. Test the_dispatcher_launches_leased_jobs_and_reports_their_moves_to_the_table runs a 2-job 1-slot story through dispatch_step + table_after: only the leased job launches, a settles, b takes the slot, a failed launch re-leases. europa --check ok with bodies; box notebook kit-dispatch-core-tests. NEXT: the Gleam shell = an actor whose inbox takes dispatch_message, owns the outbox subject it hands the coordinator via Watch (a subject is only received by its creator), runs LaunchOn through `kit job start --on <machine>` and sends TableInput effects as Input.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_7 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 7u32, "2026-11-04T18:29Z", "kit", "11-04 20:4x kit: dispatch core PROVEN on the box (kit-dispatch-core-tests 1/1). The Gleam SHELL is BUILT (unstaged) in kit_coordinator_actor: Watch now carries the dispatcher's own inbox (subject dispatch_message) and the coordinator sends LeaseGranted job to every watcher, so no forwarding process is needed (a subject is only received by its creator, and the dispatcher created its inbox). dispatcher_state {core : dispatch_state; coordinator; launch : job_spec -> bool}; dispatcher_step runs dispatch_step, sends TableInput effects as Input, and for each LaunchOn calls launch and feeds LaunchAnswered back through dispatch_step; dispatcher_started starts it and Watches the coordinator. The launcher is injected, so tests use a probe and the real one (kit job start --on <machine> through process) is a separate wiring step. Gleam test the_dispatcher_launches_each_leased_job_once_and_the_next_after_it_ends (1-slot coordinator: a launches, b does not until JobEnded a, then b) + the old outbox test now expecting LeaseGranted. europa --check ok with bodies; box gleam notebook kit-dispatcher-actor-tests.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_8 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 8u32, "2026-11-04T20:19Z", "kit", "11-04 21:0x kit: PROVEN on the box (kit-splits-tests 14/14): kit_coordinator_table/dispatch.spi (the 1-slot story + a_job_that_never_launches_is_given_up_after_three_tries_and_frees_its_slot: after 3 failed launches the dispatcher emits LaunchGaveUp once, forgets the job and settles it so the slot goes to the next job; launch_args = kit job start --on <machine> <label> <timeout> <command>) and kit_coordinator_actor/actor.spi (gleam: restart under the supervisor, the outbox now carrying LeaseGranted, and the_dispatcher_launches_each_leased_job_once_and_the_next_after_it_ends). The real launcher kit_launch (process.captured_by kit (launch_args spec)) is in the tree; NEXT: a start entry that wires keeper + coordinator + dispatcher with kit_launch under one supervisor, then a dry run against europa with a harmless command.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_9 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 9u32, "2026-11-04T22:00Z", "kit", "11-04 18:0x kit: the new system test (a launcher that cannot run) CAUGHT A REAL BUG, and with load gone it was the only failure (5/6). After a failed launch the dispatcher sends Moved (a, Expiring); the table requeues a and the freed slot re-leases a inside the SAME input, so a is LeasedOn before and after and newly_leased (a before/after diff) reports nothing: no second LeaseGranted, so a was never retried and never given up, holding the slot forever. The pure dispatch test missed it because it drives table_after directly. FIX (unstaged): kit_coordinator_table.granted_by before input after = newly_leased plus the job of a Moved (job, Expiring) input when it is leased again; coordinator_step now sends granted_by. Rust test in table.spi: the diff alone misses the re-lease, granted_by reports it. The actor test now polls (settled_within, 500 ms x 40) instead of a fixed 2 s pause. europa --check ok with bodies; box notebooks kit-relet-tests.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_10 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 10u32, "2026-11-04T22:11Z", "kit", "11-04 18:2x kit: the re-lease fix is PROVEN on the box (kit-relet-tests 2/2): kit_coordinator_table table.spi 2/2 (incl. \"the diff alone misses a re-lease in the same input\" / \"granted_by reports it\") and kit_coordinator_actor actor.spi 6/6, including a_launcher_that_cannot_run_gives_every_job_up_and_frees_the_slot: with the real kit_launch on a binary that does not exist, job a fails 3 launches, is given up and settles, then b takes the slot and is given up too. The coordinator stack (keeper + coordinator + dispatcher + real launcher, via system_started) now runs end to end on the BEAM. NEXT: a `kit coordinator` entry that starts system_started with kit_launch \"kit\" and feeds it submissions, then a dry run on europa with a harmless command.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_11 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 11u32, "2026-11-05T00:19Z", "kit", "11-04 20:2x kit: S9 is DONE and decides the shape of the remaining entry: the coordinator stays RESIDENT on the node (per-call BEAM starts there cost 2-24 s under its usual load; resident idle ~0.01% of a core). NEXT for S10: a `kit coordinator` entry that runs system_started once with kit_launch \"kit\" as a long-lived node job and accepts submissions, then a dry run on the node with a harmless command.")
inl note_of_remote_exec_s10_12 () = Note ("REMOTE-EXEC-S10", 12u32, "2026-11-05T00:21Z", "kit", "11-04 20:2x kit: the resident entry's INPUT side is built (unstaged) in kit_coordinator_table/dispatch.spi: typed `submission = SubmissionReady : dispatch_message | SubmissionRefused : string` and submission_of, which parses \"<job-id> <label> <machine> <timeout-ms> -- <program> [args...]\" into Submitted (JobId id, job_spec) (blank runs ignored) or a refusal naming why (not a number / needs a job-... id and a positive timeout / the expected shape). Test a_submission_line_becomes_a_submitted_message_or_a_named_refusal (5 cases). Kept in dispatch.spi, not a sibling module, given tonight's notebook sibling-import issue. europa --check ok with bodies; box notebook kit-submission-tests. NEXT: the `kit coordinator` entry loop: system_started once with kit_launch \"kit\", then each submission line (stdin or a spool file per S4's queue) -> submission_of -> send to the dispatcher.")
