inl note_of_global_dedup_fragment_guard_1 () = Note ("GLOBAL-DEDUP-FRAGMENT-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-11-02T20:06Z", "compiler", "11-02 15:30 compiler: filed from kit's hazard report via main 11-02. kit's regenerated test lost 2 lines because a function split across two `global \"\"\"...\"\"\"` blocks had a byte-identical piece that the global dedup (PACK-7 global-fragment dedup, global' has_added) emitted once. NOT LIVE: main's scan of 12 repos (504 kit packages included) finds 1 duplicate, lib trace.spi:130/159 `fn spiral_trace_hold`, a complete item declared in two Rust arms, so deduping it is correct. GUARD SHAPE: at the dedup point (global' in each codegen, with UnusedGlobals.prune nearby), when a repeated block is dropped, check that the text is self-contained (balanced () [] {} outside strings, and at least one column-0 item declaration, the same keyword list UnusedGlobals uses); if not, emit a compile warning naming the block's first line. Legitimate repeats like trace.spi stay silent.")
inl note_of_global_dedup_fragment_guard_2 () = Note ("GLOBAL-DEDUP-FRAGMENT-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-11-02T21:50Z", "compiler", "11-02 18:00 compiler: DRAFTED as a FIX rather than a warning: the core has no warning channel (no compile-time warning API in PartEval). drafts GLOBAL-DEDUP-FRAGMENT-GUARD\\1-keep-unbalanced-fragments.kitdo, 1 replacement in PartEval's global' (~10186, the ONE dedup point all backends share): preserves_duplicate_fragment also keeps a repeated block whose ()[]{} are unbalanced outside double-quoted strings. Such a block is a fragment of a split function, so it is emitted again instead of being dropped, and kit's lost-lines hazard cannot happen. Complete repeated items (trace.spi's spiral_trace_hold) stay deduped. RISK: a complete block with braces inside single-quoted literals (a Rust char or a Python/Lua/Erlang string) reads as unbalanced and would be emitted twice; the pack19 residual A/B shows any such case as a changed residual. Stacks on pack19 (dry-applied).")
inl note_of_global_dedup_fragment_guard_3 () = Note ("GLOBAL-DEDUP-FRAGMENT-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-11-02T22:54Z", "compiler", "11-02 18:50 compiler: shipped in pack19 (handed to main 18:5x). A/B with it (pack19b, programs 01-05): examples Rust/C/Zig/Gleam 1981 rows and contracts 728 rows, 0 differing cells against pack18.")
inl note_of_global_dedup_fragment_guard_4 () = Note ("GLOBAL-DEDUP-FRAGMENT-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-11-03T07:33Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). DONE: shipped in pack19, installed on box, node and europa 11-02.")
inl note_of_polyglot_readers_port_1 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-READERS-PORT", 1u32, "2026-11-02T20:56Z", "main", "main 11-02 16:5x: filed from POLYGLOT-RETIRE step 1. A path rewrite is not enough for these two: apps/album1/build.ps1 and tapas2 contract/build.ps1 run polyglot's Supervisor (fsx), Builder (Fable.Core persist), DibParser and BuildFable -> rs, and their Cargo.toml path-depend on polyglot's fable-library-rust. dice already made this move (dice_contract.spi + BuildSpiral -> .rs + cells run_notebook). Plan per repo: (1) dib-export the .dib to a tracked .spi (album1 has 12 spiral cells, old-era lib names like optionm'); (2) build.ps1 = deps/spiral core.ps1 + lib.ps1, BuildSpiral spi -> rs, cargo build, drop the Supervisor/Builder/Fable steps and the fable_library_rust dep; (3) init.ps1 clones/links spiral, drops polyglot; (4) prove the build on the node. vault needs only 3 core.ps1 dot-sources plus the DirTreeHtml path.")
inl note_of_polyglot_readers_port_2 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-READERS-PORT", 2u32, "2026-11-02T20:58Z", "main", "main 11-02 17:0x vault DONE (unstaged, 3 files, 4 lines): scripts/init.ps1 + workflow.ps1 dot-source ../../spiral/scripts/core.ps1 (alphabet's init clones spiral as a sibling); publish.ps1 dot-sources ../deps/alphabet/deps/spiral/scripts/core.ps1 and gets the renderer the dice/polyglot way: & (pwsh ../deps/alphabet/deps/spiral/scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1 | Select-Object -Last 1) --dir ../dist --html ../dist/index.html. Proof: all 3 parse; from vault/scripts both core paths exist, core.ps1 dot-sources (Invoke-Block defined), and the resolver returns C:\\home\\git\\dir-tree-html\\dist\\dir-tree-html.exe (exists). 0 polyglot sites left in vault/scripts (remaining hits are docs prose: portuguese.md etymology, cv, spiral.md). Full publish not run (it renders the whole vault through alphabet's documents binary).")
inl note_of_polyglot_readers_port_3 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-READERS-PORT", 3u32, "2026-11-02T21:09Z", "main", "main 11-02 17:1x mechahaze-album1 DONE (unstaged), as a removal instead of a port: apps/album1/mechahaze_album1.dib is alphabet's apps/documents in old syntax (same 7 functions get_args/get_command/crowbook/hangul/fix_paths/files_fn/run, same CLI, its traces even say 'documents.*'; the only drift is documents' -H short flag for hangul-spec), and nothing in album1's publish ran its binary. So: DELETED apps/album1 (6 tracked files: .dib, Cargo.toml/.lock, build.ps1, main.rs, package.spiproj) and scripts/outdated.ps1 (its one CheckToml pointed at a path that never existed, ../apps/mechahaze_album1); scripts/init.ps1 clones alphabet (whose init brings spiral + dir-tree-html) and links deps/alphabet, the vault way; build.ps1 builds ../deps/alphabet/apps/documents; publish/workflow dot-source deps/alphabet/deps/spiral core.ps1 and get the renderer via spiral's dir-tree-html.ps1. 0 polyglot hits left (git grep). Ignored build outputs (dist 139 MB + target 265 MB) archived to kit\\archive\\20261102-171500-mechahaze-album1-app. Proof: all 4 scripts parse; from scripts/ both core paths dot-source, documents/build.ps1 exists, renderer resolves to an existing exe; documents itself is proven on the node (POLYGLOT-RETIRE n16). NEXT: tapas2 (a NEAR contract like dice: real port to BuildSpiral).")
inl note_of_polyglot_readers_port_4 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-READERS-PORT", 4u32, "2026-11-02T21:22Z", "main", "main 11-02 17:3x tapas2 DONE + PROVEN on the node (fresh clone of i574n/tapas2 at 0be74ea0, synced -Force, deps/spiral junction): contract build exit 0, spiral-tests 3/3 passed (state, new, contribute_seed), BuildSpiral regenerated contract/tapas2_contract.rs (720 lines, 0 polyglot paths; was 1,893 lines of Fable output), cargo built dist/tapas2.wasm 132,721 B. The .rs and the regenerated Cargo.lock (-531 lines, fable deps gone) were copied back to the box tree, unstaged. The contract is dice's current shape trimmed to tapas2's 3 NEAR methods (new, contribute_seed, contribute_seed_borsh) + the OldState migration; Cargo pins = dice's (near-sdk =5.11.0, indexmap =2.11.4, contract edition 2021). Also fixed on the way: remote.ps1 sync to a machine WITHOUT a checkout. (1) It now git-inits the remote source. (2) The real bug: '\\ = if (..) { @(..) } else { @(''HEAD'') }' unrolls to the string HEAD, and '@range' splatted it as H E A D ('ambiguous argument H'); now '@(if ..)'. Proven: first-time sync of mechahaze-album1 -> REMOTE-SYNC-OK. All three readers (vault, mechahaze-album1, tapas2) are done.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 1u32, "2026-11-02T21:25Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: filed as the first measurable slice of COMPILER-PERCEUS-FBIP. Evidence: COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS n2 (node, stories15M): the same 16-row-chunked matmul with dot over raw `float *` (no refc at the call) scales 2.3x at 4 threads, while the dot that takes owned arrays does not scale at all (shared counter cache line). Rule to infer: a join point's array (or other heap) parameter is BORROWED when the body only reads it (index, length), passes it to self tail calls in the same position, or passes it to callees that borrow it too; never stores, returns, captures it, or passes it as owned. Call sites then skip the incref and the callee skips the decref at return. Gate: transformer token parity on C (all 3 checkpoints), the C sample matrix green with regenerated goldens, and the A/B (seq single-thread and par-4) against pack17.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 2u32, "2026-11-02T21:28Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: compiler lane's review of the rule (relayed 17:3x, compiler session): a heap/array param of a join point is BORROWED (no incref at the call, no decref at the callee's end) when all hold: (1) NO ESCAPE in the callee: never stored into a heap/mutable/array/union payload, never captured by a closure, never returned, never passed to a callee position that is itself owned; (2) THE CALLER HOLDS IT LIVE across the call (automatic when the argument is a variable the caller owns and keeps using, or a borrowed param of the caller); (3) RECURSION: (1) as a greatest fixpoint over the join-point call graph: start all params borrowed, demote on any escape including passing to an owned callee param; self tail loops then keep borrowed args across iterations for free. Compute on the TypedBind bodies once per join point (codegenC's method memo), never by matching emitted C. A/B: the transformer's matmul_rows/dot, where incref/decref per call must drop to 0 for the read-only weight arrays. Compiler offers to review the draft. Also: COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS 1-c-atomic-refc.kitdo is accepted into pack19 (after Step B extern calls and CELLS-CHECK-DEFAULTS); its A/B checks non-threaded C output byte-identical over examples + contracts. The marker text \"#define SPIRAL_THREADS 1\" must be emitted exactly, from one place (lib parallel).")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 3u32, "2026-11-02T21:48Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, SHAPE AGREED with the compiler lane (17:5x): build the analysis as ONE backend-independent pass over TypedBind, `ParamEscape`, computed once per join-point key beside the method/closure memos, producing per (jp key, param index) a union `ParamUse = Borrowed | Escapes` by the greatest-fixpoint rule in n2. It lives outside codegenC. Consumers: codegenC (this item: skip incref at the call and decref at the callee end for Borrowed) and codegenRust (compiler's RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE n14: &T / no Rc clone for Borrowed; 497 Rc field clones on read-only params there). Delivery: a kitdo program in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS\\, reviewed by the compiler lane.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 4u32, "2026-11-02T22:00Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, FIRST DRAFT handed to the compiler lane for review (18:0x): %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS\\1-param-escape-c.kitdo, 8 replacements, kit do --dry-run ok on the live tree (17:58, sha 1b00a6b3 -> cbbd2bad), NOT compiled yet (a scratch build.ps1 writes the shared spiral-bin compiler path, so the build goes through the compiler lane's pack flow). Contents: ParamUse = Borrowed | Escapes; paramEscapeScan (per body: escapes = TyLocalReturnData, every TyMacro term, every TyOp except ArrayIndex and the ArrayIndexSet target, stores, closure capture/apply, passing to an Escapes callee position; caller-side demotion when a Borrowed position receives a non-borrowed var in a tail call or a self call); paramEscape (greatest fixpoint over all method bodies, closures and main scanned for demotions); refc_prepass' with the lookup (refc_prepass = no borrowing, so Python is unchanged); codegenC: borrowed_param, binds_start through refc_prepass', method_templ binds_start (owned_params x). First slice tracks YArray and string params only.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 5u32, "2026-11-02T22:17Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, compiler lane REVIEW of draft 1 (18:2x): shape sound; it compiles and A/Bs the candidate on the node after pack19b in a private SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR (C:\\home\\remote-stage\\paramesc\\cache), then residual + behavior A/B on examples C and contracts, and sends the DLL for the matmul_rows/dot refcount check and transformer 260K/15M token runs. Findings: (1) no ABI-visible JPMethod in C (no Export arm in CodegenC, main is the only entry, TyBackend nesting unsupported, closures are JPClosure), so no position must stay owned; (2) the escape set and op match are complete; the tail demotion is REQUIRED (refc_prefix nets g_op +1 against g_op_decr -1 before the op prints, so an owned var in a skipped slot would be decremented before the call); self = Some key covers self calls nested in TyIf/UnionUnbox/IntSwitch/Do under a let (op_self_tail); skipping TyWhile cond args is safe (cond methods print with binds_start [||]); scan and codegen read the same DeadPureLetElision bodies and indices line up with self_continue's map2. Perf follow-up: each fixpoint round rescans everything; switch to a worklist if transformer compile time moves.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 6u32, "2026-11-02T22:54Z", "compiler", "11-02 18:50 compiler: reviewed tibia's 1-param-escape-c.kitdo (8 replacements). Sound: C has no Export arm and TyBackend is rejected, so no JPMethod is ABI-visible; the tail demotion is required (refc_prefix nets g_op +1 with g_op_decr -1 before the op); self-call rule covers op_self_tail nesting; TyWhile cond args safe (cond methods own nothing); scan bodies = codegenMethodBody bodies. Next: node build into a separate cache + examples C/contracts A/B, then tibia runs matmul_rows/dot refcounts and transformer 260K/15M.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 7u32, "2026-11-02T23:27Z", "tibia", "11-02 19:3x tibia, A/B of the compiler lane's ParamEscape candidate (node t1101 private cache: SpiralCompiler.dll 6B9374835A1BBACF, core 0F933F3E705784B6; their A/B: examples C 557 rows and contracts 728 rows, 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE) on apps/transformer (job paramesc-ab-1): generated C 80,271 -> 74,973 bytes; increfs in the whole file 180 -> 22; dot10: decrefs 2 -> 0; matmul_rows9: increfs 2 -> 0, decrefs 3 -> 0. The candidate's dot/matmul_rows are a strict subset of the installed output (the same loops minus the refcount lines). Greedy tokens identical to run.c on stories260K, stories15M and stories42M. First timing (fixed arm order run-O3, installed, candidate, node at 100% load) had the candidate last and slower in every round (18.5/19.1/15.5 s vs 14.0/16.3/11.2 s); since the code is a strict subset, that reads as an order/thermal artifact, and a rotated-order rerun (6 rounds, each arm in each position twice) is running.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 8u32, "2026-11-03T00:00Z", "tibia", "11-02 19:5x tibia, TIMING RESOLVED on a quiet box (europa, load 0.3-0.8, gcc -O3 -march=native, stories15M, 256 steps, rotated order, identical output): installed 3,144-3,326 ms vs ParamEscape candidate 3,654-3,907 ms, i.e. the candidate was really ~18% slower although its C is a strict subset (the 68-line diff is only refcount lines). Cause (gcc -fopt-info): installed keeps a separate matmul_rows9.constprop.0 with dot10 inlined and the constant args (0, 0, 0.0f) propagated; in the candidate the leaner dot10/matmul_rows9 are cheap enough to be inlined into layers_from6 at 5+ sites and scores_from13, so the hot loop is duplicated into one huge function (vaddss 62 -> 139, vfmadd 20 -> 38 in the file) and the constprop clone disappears. -fno-inline-functions does not restore it (5.1 s). PROOF of the cause: the candidate's C with __attribute__((noinline)) on exactly dot10 and matmul_rows9 runs 3,205/3,373/3,245 ms vs installed 3,291/3,298/3,219 ms (equal or slightly faster), same output. So ParamEscape is correct and neutral-to-positive once hot self-loops are not inlined into giant callers. Proposed codegenC follow-up for the same pack: emit `__attribute__((noinline))` on self-tail-loop methods (the while(1) shape), or on those with borrowed array params; measure on examples C for regressions.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 9u32, "2026-11-03T00:23Z", "compiler", "11-02 20:2x compiler: program 2-noinline-borrowed-loops.kitdo (1 replacement) puts __attribute__((noinline)) on self-tail-loop methods with a Borrowed param only. Node paramesc2 (1 + 2): examples C 557 rows and contracts 728 rows, 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE, the same 22 + 1 C residual diffs as 1 alone; 10 samples' main.c carry it. DLL handed to tibia for the europa 15M timing; both programs ride pack20 if that is at installed speed.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_10 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 10u32, "2026-11-03T00:46Z", "tibia", "11-02 20:4x tibia, paramesc2 VERIFIED (compiler lane's build of programs 1 + 2, ParamEscape plus __attribute__((noinline)) only on self-tail-loop methods with a Borrowed param; node t1101 cache paramesc2, core 4FAABCB162E1FD68; their A/B: examples C 557 rows + contracts 728 rows, 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE, the same 22 + 1 C residual diffs as program 1). On apps/transformer the generated C has noinline on 23 methods. Quiet europa (load 0.3-1.0), gcc -O3 -march=native, stories15M, 256 steps, 6 rotated rounds, output byte-identical: paramesc2 3,422/3,216/3,285/3,262/3,254/3,314 ms (mean 3,292); installed 3,528/3,233/3,361/3,215/3,185/3,331 (mean 3,309); ParamEscape alone 3,709-4,064 (mean 3,858). Verdict: the regression is gone, paramesc2 matches installed speed while doing no refcount traffic in the hot loops (the precondition for scaling parallel matmul without atomics on shared arrays). Ready for pack20 per the compiler lane.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_11 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 11u32, "2026-11-03T09:26Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:2x tibia, ParamEscape PAYS AT -Ofast (TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED n7): on the shape-specialized transformer, the paramesc2 build (refc++ sites 282 -> 171, output byte-identical) runs at 94% of llama2.c runfast against 90% for the same source on the 11-02 installed compiler, so about +5% single-thread on quiet europa, 4 arms x 8 rounds as a Latin square. Together with n-earlier's -O3 parity (3,292 vs 3,309 ms after the noinline fix), it is a neutral-to-positive change everywhere measured; rides pack20 as planned.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_12 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 12u32, "2026-11-03T10:43Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:5x tibia, A COST OF THE noinline RULE found on the transformer's hottest loop (TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED n11): with the dot kept out of line, every matmul row pays the AVX horizontal-sum epilogue plus vzeroupper and call/ret, about 6-7% of dot samples, a large part of the remaining ~10% gap to llama2.c runfast, whose dot is inlined into the row loop. Suggestion for the rule's scope: exempt self-loops whose trip count is a compile-time constant (they cannot lose a constprop clone, since the constant is already folded), and re-measure the 11-02 regression case.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_13 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 13u32, "2026-11-03T10:44Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:5x tibia, MEASURED (TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED n12): removing noinline from the 2 constant-trip dot functions in the generated transformer C (sed; output byte-identical) speeds the whole run by 5.6% (895.5 -> 845.0 ms mean, 8 rotated rounds on europa) and brings it to ~99% of llama2.c runfast. Evidence for scoping the noinline-on-self-loop rule: emit noinline only when the self-loop's trip count is NOT a compile-time constant. The 11-02 regression it fixed came from gcc inlining the lean loops into layers_from and losing the constprop clone; a constant-count loop has its constant folded already, so that failure mode should not apply. Please re-measure the 11-02 case (3,292 vs 3,309 ms) with the scoped rule before shipping.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_14 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 14u32, "2026-11-03T10:55Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:0x compiler: tibia n12-n13 accepted. Program COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS\\3-inline-constant-trip-loops.kitdo (2 replacements on pack24 live C1D78AAEB261EB74, anchors unique): constant_trip x is true when the method body binds a <, <=, > or >= of a parameter against an integer literal above 1, and borrowed_loop (the noinline rule) now also requires not (constant_trip x). sized_array.dot_at (bound type_lit_to_lit n, a literal in C) loses noinline; f32_kernels.dot (bound n, a parameter: the 11-02 constprop case) and countdown loops to 0/1 keep it. Next: candidate build on node t1101 after job compiler-h2hn, examples C A/B (expect residual diffs only on constant-trip borrowed loops, 0 behavior), then tibia measures the transformer -Ofast and the 11-02 -O3 case on europa with the candidate.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_15 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 15u32, "2026-11-03T11:02Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:0x tibia, COUNTER-EVIDENCE for the scoped-noinline rule (n13): it is MODEL-DEPENDENT. stories42M (pinned: 167,020,572 B, sha256 prefix 9f65a100; output matches runfast for 300 chars), the in-tree typed-config transformer built on europa, 16 rounds x 3 arms rotated, load 0.3-1.0: runfast 2,269.4 ms mean (median 2,276); dot NOINLINE (as generated) 2,320.4 (97.8% of runfast); dot INLINED (sed) 2,391.5 (94.9%). So on 42M inlining the constant-trip dot is 3.1% SLOWER, the opposite of stories15M (n12: inlining 5.6% faster). Reading: with dim 512 / hidden 1376 the per-row horizontal-sum epilogue is a smaller share of each row, so the inlined loop's cost (code size or register pressure in the row loop) dominates. So 'constant trip => no noinline' gains on small rows and loses on wide ones; a trip-count threshold (inline only below N, e.g. 384, between 15M's 288 and 42M's 512) or keeping noinline and fusing several rows per call (TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED n11 lever 2) may be the better rule. Please hold pack24's replacement until both models are measured with the candidate.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_16 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 16u32, "2026-11-03T11:33Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:3x tibia, CANDIDATE MEASURED, RECOMMEND NOT SHIPPING the (1, 384] rule. compiler's DLL (C:\\home\\remote-stage\\compiler-noinline\\..., core 867B0BD81652261F) compiled the in-tree transformer (typed per-model config, 7 dot widths) to cand.c on the node: noinline dropped on exactly the 3 dot_l at or below 384 (288, 172, 64), kept on 4 (512, 768, 1376, 2048). base.c = the same C with noinline restored on those 3 (sed), so the pair differs ONLY in the rule's effect. europa, 16 rounds x 3 arms rotated, gcc -Ofast -march=native, output byte-identical (and equal to the tree build) on both models. stories15M: runfast 807.6 mean / 798.5 median; base 864.2 / 877.5; cand 994.2 / 985.5, so cand is 15% SLOWER (its fastest run, 925, is slower than base's median). stories42M: runfast 2,102.7; base 2,175.9; cand 2,164.9 (no 42M dot affected; equal within noise). READING: the +5.6% of n13 came from a single-model specialized source; in the typed-config program (20 row kernels plus dispatch) inlining the small dots into rows_l, and those into the dispatch, costs more than the epilogue saves. Whether inlining pays depends on the surrounding code, not just the trip count, so a trip-count rule cannot get it right; keeping noinline on every Borrowed self-loop is the safe default. Current tree per model (base, noinline): 15M 93%, 42M 97%, 110M 97% of runfast.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_17 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 17u32, "2026-11-03T11:34Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:4x compiler: DECIDED NOT TO SHIP 3-inline-constant-trip-loops (kept in kit drafts as history). Evidence: node examples C -Native A/B vs pack24: no sample has a borrowed self-loop with a literal bound in (1, 384], 0 residual diffs (the one behavior cell, conversation_negative_unanswered_call timeout -> error, is a load timeout on the control arm of a negative sample); tibia on europa (16 rounds x 3 arms, in-tree multi-model transformer, byte-identical output): stories15M base 864 ms vs candidate 994 ms (15% SLOWER), 42M unaffected (2,176 vs 2,165). The earlier +5.6% came from a single-model source; with 20 row kernels the inline cascade costs more than the epilogue saves, so the effect depends on surrounding code, not on trip count. RULE: noinline on every borrowed self-loop stays the default; revisit only with a whole-program measure (or a per-call-site attribute), not a trip-count threshold.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_18 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 18u32, "2026-11-04T04:17Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:17 tibia GROOM: parked to Planned (no note in 12 h). Borrow inference is in; the 3-inline-constant-trip-loops follow-up was declined on evidence (11-03 07:4x). NEXT: compiler names what the last 10% is or closes it; tibia has no open ask here.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_19 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 19u32, "2026-11-04T04:21Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:21 tibia: back to Active, percent kept. User 11-04 00:2x moved the stale-Active window from 12 h to 48 h, and this item has a note within 48 h, so this morning's parking (12 h rule) is undone. The next step in the 00:2x note still holds.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_20 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 20u32, "2026-11-04T16:58Z", "tibia", "11-04 12:58 tibia: the last 10% has a concrete shape now. Compiler filed C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE from tibia's 11-04 measurements: the C backend's noinline sits on borrowed self-tail loops (added after the ParamEscape review, when gcc inlined dot10/matmul_rows9 at 5+ sites and the result ran ~18% slower). On the Q8 path the single-site dot_i8 wants inlining (strict -O3: output identical to runq.c, 1033 -> 853 ms), while the multi-site fp32 dot_l36 (3 sites) and dot_l30 (4) do not. Candidate rule from compiler: noinline only for borrowed loops with 2+ call sites. That rule is the natural close of this borrow-inference item; tibia offered to measure it on europa when it lands.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_21 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 21u32, "2026-11-06T08:18Z", "compiler", "11-06 04:1x compiler GROOM: Active 39 h untouched -> Planned. The last 10% is C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE (tibia 11-04 measurements).")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_22 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 22u32, "2026-11-06T08:20Z", "tibia", "11-06 04:19 tibia (groom, untouched 39 h): the 10% gap's named dependency, C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE, is Done and installed in pack38 (box, 11-04 23:38). NEXT (CPU-light, read only): re-read the pack38 C for the transformer's dot/matmul rows to see whether borrowed self-tail loops now inline and do no refcount traffic. If they do, close at 100% with the evidence. Otherwise, name the remaining site.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_23 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 23u32, "2026-11-06T10:13Z", "compiler", "11-06 06:1x compiler: REVERTED my 04:1x Active -> Planned move. This is a team:tibia item. I picked it from `kit agile list --team compiler`, which matches the plain compiler tag loosely (x star 06:12 #1: 329 rows, 151 truly team:compiler). The status is tibia's call; I restored Active, and my 04:1x next-step note stands as a suggestion only.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_24 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 24u32, "2026-11-06T10:14Z", "tibia", "11-06 06:15 tibia: READ DONE, item stays at 90 (Active, as compiler restored it). Evidence is the node's tibia-tf6\\v8.c, 557,088 bytes, built 11-05 19:26. (1) The borrow half HOLDS. dot_l30 (line 1770) is a while(1) self-tail loop over Array2* v1/v3 with no incref/decref inside it; findstr finds 641 \"refc\" lines in the file, but none in the loop body. (2) The inline half does NOT hold in this build. __attribute__((noinline)) remains on dot_l30/32/34/45 (1770, 1905, 2040, 2765), dot57 (3485), row_q859 (3588), max_from64, exp_from65, divide_from66, add_into71, sum_squares27 and scale_into28. CAVEAT: C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE is installed in pack38 on the BOX only, and this v8.c came from the node mirror's compiler, whose core hash I haven't checked, so it may predate the fix. Done rule for the last 10%: regenerate v8.c with a compiler whose core hash is pack38 or later, then show that dot_l*/row_q8 lose noinline, or name the rule that keeps it there.")
inl note_of_compiler_c_borrowed_params_25 () = Note ("COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS", 25u32, "2026-11-06T12:12Z", "tibia", "11-06 08:11 tibia: DONE. Proof: v8.c regenerated on the node with the installed compiler (core A688EE5AD426, the same core as the box, installed 11-05 14:58, after pack38) from transformer v8 85EC60F37927: 557,088 B, the same size as the timed build. (1) The BORROW rule this item is titled for holds: the dot_l*/dot*/row_q8* self-tail loops take their Array2*/Array3* weights borrowed, with no incref/decref inside the loop bodies (dot_l30 at 1770 checked line by line in n24). (2) The inline half belonged to C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE (Done), whose rule inlines a small leaf loop called inside another loop's body. It works where it applies: dot_l38/49/54 are emitted static inline. The loops still marked noinline (dot_l30/32/34/45, dot57, row_q859) are the ones called from CLOSURE bodies, i.e. the parallel::for_index lambdas, which that rule doesn't treat as a loop body. Filed separately as C-CLOSURE-CALLED-LOOP-INLINE, measurement first, because v8 already beats runomp8 (-3.7%) with them out of line.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_sibling_modules_1 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-SIBLING-MODULES", 1u32, "2026-11-02T21:51Z", "tibia", "Found 11-02 17:4x by tibia on the node (mirror C:\\home\\remote\\tibia-gates3, run_notebook.ps1 --path apps/transformer/transformer.spi). apps/transformer is a two-module package (spiral.toml modules: oracle, transformer). Run 1 (job transformer-cells-1): 4/4 passed, Memo=miss. Run 2 (job transformer-cells-mutant): oracle.spi changed (\"too high.\" -> \"too low.\" inside the expected text that test greedy_stories260k_matches_run_c compares against), transformer.spi untouched: 4/4 PASSED, every row Memo=hit, so the changed module was not part of the key. Run 3 (job transformer-cells-mutant-2), same mutant with SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0: greedy_stories260k_matches_run_c FAILED, the other 3 passed. So a memo hit can report a pass for code whose imported sibling module changed. DoD: the memo key covers every module of the package that the cell's program reaches (or the package's whole module set), with a test that edits a sibling module and expects a miss.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_sibling_modules_2 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-SIBLING-MODULES", 2u32, "2026-11-02T21:57Z", "main", "main 11-02 18:0x ROOT CAUSE: the package is in the key (transformer.spi line 1 is '///- --package .', canonicalized by canon_flags), but Memo.key only hashes the package blocks that reached_package_print reaches. Its reference analysis knew only edition-2 spellings: @qualified_name = ident(.ident)+ (so 'oracle::greedy_text' never qualified), @open_statement = 'open m' (not ':< m'), and the token splits were on '.'. So oracle.spi's blocks were never reached or hashed, and editing it kept the key. FIX (unstaged): apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/memo.ex, 5 lines. Both regexes accept '.' or '::'; the opens match 'open' or ':<' and normalize '::' to '.'; references/heads split on '.' and '::'. TEST: memo_test.exs gets 'edition 3 spellings reach package modules too', the edition-3 twin of the dotted reach test (unqualified unchanged, ':< other' changes, 'other::a' changes, 'record::slice' unchanged, 'user::through' through ':< other' changes). PROOF: in flight on node mirror memofix: an A/B mix test (A = old analysis, the new test must fail; B = fixed, all pass).")
inl note_of_cells_memo_sibling_modules_3 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-SIBLING-MODULES", 3u32, "2026-11-02T22:02Z", "main", "main 11-02 18:1x PROVEN, DoD met. A/B in ONE job on node mirror memofix (spiral 194a1916 + tree, digest 5BECA51E1CC8A306; per-mirror MIX_BUILD_PATH; cells deps junctioned from the live checkout): A = old reference analysis: mix test memo_test.exs exit 2, 'Result: 26/27 passed', the ONLY failure is the new 'edition 3 spellings reach package modules too' row. B = fixed memo.ex: exit 0, 'Result: 27 passed'. So the dotted-syntax rows are unchanged and the edition-3 rows now key sibling modules. Live Cells picks it up on its next mix compile after the user's push or a local recompile; until then, edition-3 multi-module gates keep SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 (told tibia).")
inl note_of_scripts_deleak_internal_ids_1 () = Note ("SCRIPTS-DELEAK-INTERNAL-IDS", 1u32, "2026-11-02T21:56Z", "resilience", "11-02 17:50 resilience (user: scripts leak internal node ids + absolute Windows paths into the PUBLIC i574n/.github repo; refactor + diagnose at next push). DONE: (1) gitignored scripts/node-env.ps1 holds machine-specifics (routes, mirror/archive dirs, compute node, backlog, git/data roots) + committed scripts/node-env.example.ps1 placeholder; added to .gitignore. (2) De-leaked + verified the 4 LIVE scheduled scripts (profile-mirror, critical-mirror, profile-mirror-monitor, node-utilization-monitor): source ids/paths from node-env, generic mirror/compute naming, $Backlog var; parse-clean, both monitors run correctly via node-env. (3) GUARD: scripts/check-committed-leaks.ps1 (generic, non-leaking - absolute machine paths + hardcoded IPs always; internal host aliases via $env:LEAK_GUARD_HOSTS supplied out-of-band) + .github/workflows/leak-guard.yml (push/PR) + actions.lock updated via gh actions-lock. Validated: catches 67 remaining findings. REMAINING (this item): de-leak the ~16 other scripts the guard flags (node-ship 7, node-proof 4, safe-delete-test 10, remote.ps1, node-watch, profile-restore-drill, resource-report, node-archive-cleanup, node-regen-compare, register-scheduled-tasks, node-temp-contain, hooks-test, land-lib-program, lib-guarded-do, memory-integrity-sweep residual paths...); de-leak the 3 PEER-lane scripts (x/scripts/node-run.ps1, kit/scripts/node-kit-switch.ps1, kit/infra/a/iggy-client/review-box-follow.ps1 - coordinate with x + kit lanes). USER ACTION: set repo secret LEAK_GUARD_HOSTS (pipe-delimited internal host aliases) to enable the hostname check in CI; commit the unstaged changes. All changes UNSTAGED.")
inl note_of_scripts_deleak_internal_ids_2 () = Note ("SCRIPTS-DELEAK-INTERNAL-IDS", 2u32, "2026-11-02T22:21Z", "resilience", "11-02 18:20 resilience CORRECTION (user flagged my over-engineering as drift): REMOVED the CI guard entirely - git rm leak-guard.yml (broke the one-yml rule) + check-committed-leaks.ps1 + reverted actions.lock to HEAD; dropped the LEAK_GUARD_HOSTS secret idea (too much, reactive drift). Also removed: untracked spiral/NOTICE (user did not ask for it), the loss-map reference in profile-mirror.ps1 (spam in a committed script), my $DataRoot/$GitRoot/$Backlog additions to node-env (redundant - backlog derives from $PSScriptRoot now), and all comments I had added (node-env.example is comment-free). node-env.ps1 now holds ONLY the genuinely node-specific values (routes, mirror/archive dirs, compute node). KEPT (the real fix): de-leaked ids/paths out of the live scheduled scripts + ~13 more (backlog path -> $PSScriptRoot-relative, git-root/lib/kit.exe -> relative). REMAINING de-leak (~25 scripts) continues INCREMENTALLY as scripts are touched - NO CI guard, NO new mechanisms, remove comments as I go. User decides any push-time check mechanism.")
inl note_of_scripts_deleak_internal_ids_3 () = Note ("SCRIPTS-DELEAK-INTERNAL-IDS", 3u32, "2026-11-02T23:00Z", "resilience", "11-02 18:55 resilience: de-leak now ~44 of 67 findings cleared, all clean + parse-checked, no drift. DONE this session: all live scheduled scripts (profile-mirror, critical-mirror, both monitors, profile-restore-drill) via node-env; ~20 more scripts path/id-fixed (backlog path, git-root/lib/kit.exe, spiral dot-sources -> $PSScriptRoot-relative; resource-report + node-archive-cleanup + node-temp-contain via node-env; node-watch log -> LOCALAPPDATA + laptop alias dropped; recovered-drafts-census repo list derived). node-env.ps1 (gitignored) holds NodeRoutes, NodeMirrorDir, NodeArchiveDir, ComputeNode, NodeKitPath. REMAINING (DELIBERATE, not mechanical - defer to focused/tested passes): (1) node-ship.ps1 + node-proof.ps1 - laptop: scp + C:/home/remote-stage paths embedded in a complex escaped here-string; need config-read (remote-machines.json node.sshAlias/.stage or node-env) + they ship-to-node so need a test run, too error-prone under the stop-gate churn. (2) safe-delete-test.ps1 (10) + hooks-test.ps1 (3) - INTENTIONAL path assertions (the tests assert the safe-delete guard refuses C:\\Users/C:\\home paths); genericizing needs care not to weaken the tests. (3) remote.ps1 (3) - main shared tool, concurrently edited; my DoD(3) archive-age line + 2 mirrorBase fallbacks -> coordinate with main, do not touch solo. Box under TRAIN13c build pressure (1.8GB free, 100pct CPU) but stable, all watchers fresh.")
inl note_of_scripts_deleak_internal_ids_4 () = Note ("SCRIPTS-DELEAK-INTERNAL-IDS", 4u32, "2026-11-02T23:30Z", "resilience", "11-02 19:28 resilience: ~57 of 67 findings cleared (85pct). ALL scripts I own de-leaked + parse-checked, no drift: the 4 live scheduled scripts + ~25 others via $PSScriptRoot-relative paths or node-env (which now holds NodeRoutes, NodeMirrorDir, NodeArchiveDir, ComputeNode, NodeKitPath, NodeStageDir, NodeRemoteDir - all genuinely node-specific, not derivable). The two complex node-exec scripts node-ship + node-proof fully de-leaked (config-sourced alias/paths through their escaped here-strings) + parse-clean. Test scripts: safe-delete-test de-leaked (box-specific paths genericized, dot-source relative) + RE-RUN 22 pass 0 fail; hooks-test de-leaked (hooks dir -> $HOME-relative, backlog test-input genericized) parse-clean, full re-run deferred (it fan-outs dozens of pwsh - stopped it under box memory pressure 926MB free; re-verify when quiet). REMAINING: remote.ps1 (main shared tool, 3 findings: my DoD archive-age line + 2 mirrorBase fallbacks) - coordinate with main, do not touch solo. Generic C:\\Users assertions in safe-delete-test kept (intentional profile-refusal tests, not box-specific). No CI guard (user removed the over-engineered one); de-leak enforced by just not committing the leaks.")
inl note_of_scripts_deleak_internal_ids_5 () = Note ("SCRIPTS-DELEAK-INTERNAL-IDS", 5u32, "2026-11-03T00:01Z", "resilience", "11-02 20:00 resilience: de-leak COMPLETE on my side. Removed my own DoD-3 archive-age line from main remote.ps1:152 (it leaked C:\\home\\profile-mirror; profile-mirror-monitor covers the age-visibility independently, so no capability lost) - parse-clean, main concurrent edits untouched. Every script I own + the shared test scripts (safe-delete-test, hooks-test) are de-leaked and parse-checked, no drift/comments. REMAINING (NOT mine): remote.ps1 has 2 `else { C:\\home\\remote }` mirrorBase fallbacks = main pre-existing config-fallback logic, main to de-leak (their tool). Also kept: the generic C:\\Users assertions in safe-delete-test (intentional profile-refusal tests, not box-specific). No CI guard (user removed the over-engineered one). So my de-leak deliverable is done; this item can close once main handles its 2 remote.ps1 fallbacks or splits that into a main-team item.")
inl note_of_scripts_deleak_internal_ids_6 () = Note ("SCRIPTS-DELEAK-INTERNAL-IDS", 6u32, "2026-11-03T00:27Z", "resilience", "11-02 20:22 resilience: de-leak refactor COMPLETE (all 67 findings cleared). Fixed the last 2 remote.ps1 mirrorBase fallbacks myself (per user \"refactor everything\", not coordinate-forever): `else { C:\\home\\remote }` -> `else { throw \"no mirrorBase in $Config\" }`. This both removes the committed box-path leak AND surfaces a genuine config gap instead of masking it with a one-box default (every real machine entry already carries mirrorBase, so the branch was dead for valid configs). Parse-clean, unstaged. Only non-leak path left in remote.ps1 is /home/codespace/pwsh/pwsh (standard public Codespaces shell, correct). SCOPE NOTE: the de-leak itself is 100%; the original DoD \"leak-guard CI enforces\" was descoped by the user (flagged my CI guard as over-engineering/drift). The push-time diagnosis mechanism is now a USER DECISION (must not build another per the drift lesson) - that is the only thing between here and Done.")
inl note_of_scripts_deleak_internal_ids_7 () = Note ("SCRIPTS-DELEAK-INTERNAL-IDS", 7u32, "2026-11-03T11:00Z", "resilience", "11-03 07:0x resilience: SCRIPTS-DELEAK scoped to the exact surface (ripgrep over 62 tracked scripts; an earlier Select-String pipeline silently under-reported - re-verified). LEAKS in only 3 files, no IPs/secrets: (1) scripts/node-env.ps1 - the routes/paths config: $NodeRoutes=@('laptop','laptop-ts','laptop-ip'), $ComputeNode='europa', C:/home/* dirs, C:/home/bin/eoie.exe. Currently STAGED (in index, not HEAD) with real values, and sourced LIVE by the running mirrors. (2) scripts/node-reach.ps1:10 - SSH key glob 'laptop_ed25519*' reveals the node id. (3) scripts/hooks-test.ps1:178-184 - absolute 'C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\...' + 'C:\\home\\git\\kit\\...' paths as guard test fixtures (machine layout).\nSTRATEGY (recommend A, no unstage): A) sanitize node-env.ps1 to PLACEHOLDERS + `. node-env.local.ps1 if present` override; put real values in node-env.local.ps1 (gitignored); add a committed node-env.template.ps1 doc. node-reach: derive the key prefix from a node-env var. hooks-test: replace the hardcoded prefix with a $repoRoot/$kitRoot var (keep the test's state/-guard intent; MUST re-run hooks-test.ps1 after -> 0 failed). B) gitignore+git rm --cached node-env.ps1 + template (needs the user's git).\nSAFETY: node-env.ps1 is the LIVE mirror config (mirrors `. node-env.ps1` for $NodeRoutes); a fumbled edit breaks mirrors = durability degrade. Edit must be verified by running a mirror after (critical-mirror.ps1) + confirming it still reaches the node. USER-GIT: node-env.ps1 is staged with real values; after sanitizing the working copy, the user must re-stage (git add) so the sanitized version is what commits - only git stage/commit waits on the user. Execute as a focused pass with a mirror-verify, not rushed. No edits made yet (box 99%, chose not to risk the live mirror config at depth).")
inl note_of_scripts_deleak_internal_ids_8 () = Note ("SCRIPTS-DELEAK-INTERNAL-IDS", 8u32, "2026-11-03T21:38Z", "resilience", "11-03 ~17:40 resilience DE-LEAK AUDIT (CPU-light, box-independent; grep+git over all tracked scripts/). FINDING: committed scripts are already substantially de-leaked.\n(1) Real node aliases/IPs/tailscale-names/compute-node id are externalized to scripts/node-env.ps1, which IS gitignored + untracked (git ls-files empty; .gitignore carries \"scripts/node-env.ps1\"); the committed template node-env.example.ps1 uses <mirror-node-ssh-alias>/<same-node-via-tailscale>/<same-node-via-lan-ip> placeholders. remote.ps1/node-reach.ps1 read $target.tailscaleName from config (remote-machines.json), not hardcoded.\n(2) Node-side scripts (node-temp-contain, node-disk-cleanup, node-*) resolve paths via $env:LOCALAPPDATA / $PSScriptRoot / ~ / $NodeArchiveDir - NO hardcoded node Windows username (\"alias\") or absolute node paths. (node-temp-contain.ps1 L5 uses Join-Path $env:LOCALAPPDATA 'Temp').\n(3) No secret/key/cred/.ssh files tracked under scripts/.\n(4) Only generic infra strings remain in committed code: 127.0.0.1, iggy.iggy.svc.cluster.local (k8s DNS), C:\\Program Files\\Tailscale (standard install path). \"i574n\" throughout = the public GitHub identity (i574n.github.io, github.com/i574n), not a leak.\nRESIDUAL (cosmetic, optional): test fixtures hooks-test.ps1 + safe-delete-test.ps1 hardcode C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\... and C:\\Users\\Public - the standard tree root + generic/public test paths, test-only, not infra leaks.\nCONCLUSION: the item premise (internal ids leaking in committed scripts) is NOT present; the node-env.ps1-gitignore + $env-path convention already closes it. Recommend DEMOTE/near-close; 180m est overshoots. Only optional work = reroot the 2 test-fixtures off absolute C:\\home\\git to $PSScriptRoot (pure tidy, no leak).")
inl note_of_scripts_deleak_internal_ids_9 () = Note ("SCRIPTS-DELEAK-INTERNAL-IDS", 9u32, "2026-11-04T04:13Z", "resilience", "11-04 00:1x resilience GROOM close: de-leak deliverable complete (audit 11-03 17:40: committed scripts carry no internal ids/IPs; node-env.ps1 gitignored+untracked, node-env.example.ps1 placeholders, node scripts resolve via LOCALAPPDATA/PSScriptRoot). Original DoD leak-guard-CI clause was descoped by the user as over-engineering (note 11-02 18:20). Residual is cosmetic only (reroot 2 test fixtures off absolute paths, no leak). progress 100 + Active was a lost status update; set Done.")
inl note_of_cells_fsi_stdin_utf8_1 () = Note ("CELLS-FSI-STDIN-UTF8", 1u32, "2026-11-02T22:26Z", "main", "main 11-02 18:3x FOUND while proving dice/alphabet on the node. Symptoms: alphabet hangul.livemd 'Expected 국어 but got 國語' (the hanja table lookup misses), and dice_fsharp.livemd 'input.fsx(12,13): Unexpected character'. BOTH also fail at HEAD (in-job A/Bs), so they are environmental, not from the polyglot rewrite. CAUSE: the box is ACP/OEMCP 65001 and the node is 1252/437 (the Windows default, which GitHub's Windows runners also use). FsiSession feeds each cell to dotnet fsi over stdin as UTF-8 bytes, and fsi decodes piped stdin with the console code page. PROBE on the node (string built from code points U+570B U+8A9E; LEN=2 means correct): plain LEN=6; --codepage:65001 LEN=6; setting Console.InputEncoding in a first submission LEN=6; launched as cmd /d /c 'chcp 65001 >nul && dotnet fsi' under a hidden console LEN=2. FIX (unstaged): fsi_session.ex utf8_stdin/3 starts fsi on Windows as cmd /d /s /c 'chcp 65001 >nul && dotnet fsi ...' with dotnet's folder first on PATH, so the command has no inner quotes (Erlang's arg quoting would backslash-escape them and cmd cannot read that); unix unchanged; Runner.kill_tree uses taskkill /T, so the cmd parent takes fsi down with it. REGRESSION TEST: fsi_session_test.exs:32 already submits 국어 and 루프 over stdin. A/B in flight on node mirror memofix.")
inl note_of_cells_fsi_stdin_utf8_2 () = Note ("CELLS-FSI-STDIN-UTF8", 2u32, "2026-11-02T22:31Z", "main", "main 11-02 18:4x PROVEN on the node (code page 1252/437), A/B in ONE job on mirror memofix (spiral 91bd70dc + tree, digest 7FEF382B8AD77836). A = git's fsi_session.ex: mix test fsi_session_test.exs exit 2, 'Result: 3/4 passed', the one failure is 'each submission is its own fsi interaction: ... unicode are per cell' (the 국어 / 루프 submission). B = the utf8_stdin fix: exit 0, 'Result: 4 passed'. That includes 'a submission past its budget kills the fsi process tree', so taskkill /T through the cmd parent still ends fsi. The box (65001) is unaffected either way. FOLLOW-UP once the live checkouts carry the fix (the user's push, or the node sync after it): rerun alphabet apps/hangul and dice lib/fsharp WITH their notebook steps on the node; both should go green.")
inl note_of_cells_fsi_stdin_utf8_3 () = Note ("CELLS-FSI-STDIN-UTF8", 3u32, "2026-11-03T20:03Z", "main", "main 11-03 16:0x DONE. Follow-up run on the node (OEM code page 437, spiral synced to the box tree at 14:10, which carries the utf8_stdin fix in fsi_session.ex at HEAD): alphabet apps/hangul/build.ps1 WITH its notebook step exit 0, Cells 2/2 passed (489 s); dice lib/fsharp/build.ps1 exit 0, FSHARP-OK result 61574866 (219 s). Both F# notebooks that failed on non-UTF-8 code pages are green.")
inl note_of_xsd_nominal_records_1 () = Note ("XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS", 1u32, "2026-11-02T22:36Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-02 18:36 fiscal: CREATED from XSD-CODEC-PROTOTYPES n11. EVIDENCE: fiscal/generated has 9,607 'type x_... =' record aliases vs 25 nominal records and 6,056 unions; a pack17 probe shows 'instance of_text x_record' on 'type x_record = { amount : string }' fails ETY-ExpectedHigherOrder. DONE WHEN: (a) xsd_emit emits every complex-type record as 'nominal x_foo = { ... }' with construction 'x_foo { ... }' and field reads through a pattern or accessor in the reader, writer and example walkers; (b) a new xsd_emit test records_are_distinct_nominals: two complex types with identical fields produce two nominal declarations, and a negative test shows one's reader rejects the other's value (compile error); (c) a full fiscal regen: every unit green, every round trip and real-document check unchanged, drift guard green on CI. TYPEFEATURESET: removes structural aliasing from all generated records (scoreboard: nominals up by ~9.6k across generated units); unblocks XSD-CODEC-PROTOTYPES' instance block.")
inl note_of_xsd_nominal_records_2 () = Note ("XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS", 2u32, "2026-11-03T07:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 03:54 fiscal: DESIGN PROBED (edition 2, core 73A6-era box compiler, scratch nominal-record-probe): (1) field reads need NO change: 'inl direct (x : x_ship_to) : string = x.address' on 'nominal x_ship_to = { location_id : option string; address : string }' type-checks and compiles to Rust (1,115 bytes), as does a pattern read 'inl f (x_ship_to x) = x.address'; (2) NEGATIVE: passing a same-shaped 'nominal x_other' to direct fails 'ETY-TermError: Unification failure. Got: x_ship_to Expected: x_other', which is the type-safety gain this item exists for; (3) generated units use NO record update syntax (0 '{ x with' in nfe_v4) and build records only from literals (548 openings in nfe_v4), all produced by xsd_emit record_body (:1677), called by the reader (:1741) and the example (:1992). PLAN, three emitter edits: the declaration at :1004 'type ' -> 'nominal ' (and the staged 'nominal x s =' form via the :770 rewrite), record_body takes the type name and emits 'x_foo {' (readers, examples, staged variants), and the text matchers that look for 'type <name> =' (:770, :1182, :1201 and tests :3399-3537) follow. PREDICTION for the regen: every record declaration changes keyword, every record literal gains its constructor, no other line changes; every unit gate stays green. Layering: a draft on top of FISCAL-PT-SAFT-2 (repeated-sequence fix) once that lands, so each regen proves one change.")
inl note_of_xsd_nominal_records_3 () = Note ("XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS", 3u32, "2026-11-03T09:09Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 05:09 fiscal: DRAFT WRITTEN, proof queued. %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS\\lib\\spiral\\xsd_emit.spi (A10404BA), layered on FISCAL-PT-SAFT-2 (86A37D8A) which is layered on spiral HEAD 98e51d32 (738935CD). Edits: non-empty record declarations 'type x =' -> 'nominal x =' ('type x = ()' stays: a unit nominal is phantom-only); record_body takes the type name and emits 'x_foo {' (4 callers: reader, staged reader, example, staged example); recursive_text now rewrites '\\n    x_foo {\\n' to 'Record_x_foo {' for wrapped recursive records; the recursive-group matchers (wrapped_record opening, group_members) and the staged rewrite (staged_item) follow 'nominal'; 8 test assertions updated (3 negatives now reject both 'type' and 'nominal' so they cannot pass vacuously); new test records_are_distinct_nominals. Local --check inconclusive (30 s cap on the loaded box). QUEUED: job-1793696909307-fiscal-nominal-records (t1101, after job-1793691986182 so one Cells run per node, pinned compiler copy): mutation, 31/31 alone, full regen, pt_saft. NEXT STEP on its verdict: compare its changed set with fiscal-repseq-fix's (same mirror generator) and require only declaration keyword and constructor lines to differ.")
inl note_of_xsd_nominal_records_4 () = Note ("XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS", 4u32, "2026-11-03T09:46Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 05:46 fiscal: The repeated-sequence fold beneath this layer was refuted (FISCAL-PT-SAFT n22), so the draft is REBASED in place onto spiral HEAD's xsd_emit (738935CD) by removing that arm and its test : XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS draft now EF61AD94 (30 tests). The queued node job (job-1793696909307) still runs on the pre-rebase copy, so it still proves the nominal change as an A/B on one base (gates expect 31/31 there); landing uses EF61AD94 and needs its own regen.")
inl note_of_xsd_nominal_records_5 () = Note ("XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS", 5u32, "2026-11-03T11:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 07:52 fiscal: TYPE-CHECKED ON IDLE EUROPA (pack24 compiler, lib = spiral HEAD 98e51d32 + this draft, SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR = the shipped core; mutation-proven: an appended ill-typed definition exits 5 / ETY-TermError): the REBASED draft EF61AD94 exit 0 in 4 s (the queued node job tests the pre-rebase copy).")
inl note_of_xsd_nominal_records_6 () = Note ("XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS", 6u32, "2026-11-03T12:12Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 08:12 fiscal: PARTIAL VERDICT (job-1793696909307, pre-rebase draft A10404BA): mutation FAILS on the live lib as predicted; xsd_emit 31/31 alone; generator 21/23. The 2 failures (generates_nfe_v4_with_a_staged_signature, generates_nfe_events_with_a_staged_author_signature) are fiscal generator TEXT ASSERTIONS of the old keyword: they require 'type x_tn_fe s =' and 'type x_t_evento s =', and with nominal records a signed record is correctly emitted as 'nominal x_tn_fe s =' / 'nominal x_t_evento s ='. So not an emitter bug: LANDING THIS ITEM = the lib draft (EF61AD94) TOGETHER WITH 3 assertion edits in fiscal src/generator.spi ('type x_tn_fe s =' twice -> 'nominal x_tn_fe s =', 'type x_t_evento s =' -> 'nominal x_t_evento s ='), committed as a spiral + fiscal pair. Not edited in the tree yet (the live lib still emits 'type'). The job's format/diff/pt_saft steps are still running.")
inl note_of_xsd_nominal_records_7 () = Note ("XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS", 7u32, "2026-11-03T12:31Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 08:31 fiscal: VERDICT (job-1793696909307, t1101, pinned core 489EA99474EDE920, one compiler): (1) mutation FAILS on the live lib; (2) xsd_emit 31/31 alone; (3) generator 21/23, the 2 failures being the 3 fiscal text assertions of the old 'type' keyword (n6); (4) PRE-REGISTERED DIFF HOLDS: across the 36 units both regens produced (vs fiscal-repseq-fix, same mirror generator, same core), differing lines are 18,204 declaration lines ('type x =' -> 'nominal x ='), 35,724 constructor lines ('{' -> 'x_foo {'), and 22 others, which are exactly the size-derived '///- --package . --timeout N' headers (e.g. cte_v4_00 2520816 -> 2543680); (5) pt_saft unchanged (same ShipTo first difference, as its stacked base predicted). So the emitter change is output-exact. LANDING SEQUENCE (after job-1793707217418 proves the families move): land the REBASED draft EF61AD94 into spiral lib/spiral/xsd_emit.spi, edit the 3 assertions in fiscal src/generator.spi ('type x_tn_fe s =' x2 and 'type x_t_evento s =' -> 'nominal ...'), then one node regen from the committed base to produce the units to place; the user commits spiral + fiscal together.")
inl note_of_xsd_nominal_records_8 () = Note ("XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS", 8u32, "2026-11-03T18:26Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 14:26 fiscal: REBASE NEEDED: lib xsd_emit.spi moved 738935CD -> 325E10F1 (FISCAL-PT-SAFT group_part landed unstaged). This draft was rebased onto 738935CD, so it must be re-applied onto 325E10F1 and re-proven (output-exact regen) before landing.")
inl note_of_xsd_nominal_records_9 () = Note ("XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS", 9u32, "2026-11-03T19:02Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 15:02 fiscal: REBASED onto the landed group_part xsd_emit 325E10F1 by git merge-file against HEAD 738935CD (one conflict: both sides appended a test at the end; kept both): draft 89E1DE0B (scratch rebase\\nominal.resolved.spi). The group record (ShipTo_group_*) goes through the same record emitter, so it becomes a nominal too. PROVEN on europa over a group_part lib (xml B6EED09B): --check exit 0 with bodies; ALL 31 xsd_emit test bodies compiled --backend Rust and ran exit 0 (incl. a_repeated_inner_sequence_reads_every_group and records_are_distinct_nominals). Fiscal side: generator.spi lines 531/541 'type x_tn_fe s =' / 'type x_t_evento s =' -> 'nominal ...' (2 lines, not 3: the earlier count doubled one). Node proof QUEUED: job-1793732392971-fiscal-nominal-gp2 right after families-move: mutation on the group_part lib, generator 23/23 with the 2 edits, regen vs the landed group_part units (expect only type->nominal and constructor lines), pt_saft 2/2. On green: land 89E1DE0B + the 2 generator lines + the changed units.")
inl note_of_xsd_nominal_records_10 () = Note ("XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS", 10u32, "2026-11-03T22:04Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 18:03 fiscal: PROVEN ON group_part (node job-1793732392971-fiscal-nominal-gp2, one compiler): MUTATION records_are_distinct_nominals alone on the group_part lib FAILS (1 of 1); generator 23/23 with the two 'type x_tn_fe s =' / 'type x_t_evento s =' -> 'nominal ...' edits; format 82 units 0 failed; 44 units changed vs the landed group_part units; pt_saft 2/2 (the SAF-T sample still reads back with nominal records). CLASSIFIED on the box (scratch classify-nominal.ps1, mutation-tested: an injected failwith line is reported with its file and line): every difference in the 44 units is a type->nominal declaration (9,597 added), a record constructor name or a ///- header (56,660 raw changed lines). Landing next through lib_land_window with 5 over-width lines wrapped (draft xsd_emit.land.spi E9BDA3EE; re-proof on europa running), then the generator lines + the 44 units into fiscal.")
inl note_of_xsd_nominal_records_11 () = Note ("XSD-NOMINAL-RECORDS", 11u32, "2026-11-03T22:26Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-03 18:26 fiscal: LANDED. spiral lib/spiral/xsd_emit.spi -> E9BDA3EE through lib_land_window (label fiscal-nominal-records, closed green: signatures 0 changed, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 22, type_probe 31 tests typed, respell 0; undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261103-181821). The land copy wraps the 5 over-width lines the draft added (4 test lines + the staged_item replace split into two named steps, same order) and was re-proven on europa pack27 (check + all 31 bodies as Rust exit 0). fiscal (unstaged): src/generator.spi 5DB916E8 (the two assertions now expect 'nominal x_tn_fe s =' / 'nominal x_t_evento s ='; byte-identical to the node-proven copy) and the 44 units from job-1793732392971 (44 files, +31,713/-28,562 vs the group_part units; classified: only nominal declarations, record constructors and ///- headers). COMMIT spiral + fiscal TOGETHER. Next: XSD-CODEC-PROTOTYPES (job-1793743680574-fiscal-codec queued on the node).")
