inl note_of_granular_split_process_fsharp_1 () = Note ("GRANULAR-SPLIT-PROCESS-FSHARP", 1u32, "2026-11-03T15:12Z", "main", "main 11-03 11:2x (GRANULAR-STATS): a 213-line F#-only process runner (15 match arms, 1 host macro). DECIDE BY RULE first: the F# backend direction is FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER; if no F# target consumer exists, delete the function and its backend arm (bold removal), proven by the lib notebooks and apps/spiral compiling; else split per stage (command split, start, stream pumps, wait/timeout, result).")
inl note_of_granular_split_process_fsharp_2 () = Note ("GRANULAR-SPLIT-PROCESS-FSHARP", 2u32, "2026-11-03T16:13Z", "main", "main 11-03 12:1x DECIDED: DELETE (rule: no consumer compiles process.spi to F#; F# only reached it through its own F#-targeted 'main' export block, polyglot's Supervisor surface, retired). Draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\GRANULAR-SPLIT-PROCESS-FSHARP\\process.spi (A3BAFCF6, base C74E753F): removes execute_with_options_fsharp (213 lines), the Fsharp arm of execute_with_options, and the F#-only main export (15 lines); 1264 -> 1033 lines; the ///> fsharp split_args/split_command tests stay (they never reach the runner). Round-trip canonical + fixpoint. GATES (A/A, same machine, live vs draft): europa live 7/15 = draft 7/15 (the same 8 Windows-program tests fail on Linux); node live 16/18 vs draft 17/18, and all 8 process-running tests PASS on Windows in both; the residual failures are 300 s cell timeouts on a loaded node (slowest cell 283 s), on different targets each run. Landing now through LAND-GATE-OFF-BOX's two-step path (node close -> box -ProvenLog), as its first real use.")
inl note_of_granular_split_process_fsharp_3 () = Note ("GRANULAR-SPLIT-PROCESS-FSHARP", 3u32, "2026-11-03T17:10Z", "main", "main 11-03 13:1x LANDED and closed green on the box (lib_land_window, undo 20261103-130243): lib/spiral/process.spi = A3BAFCF670B8, 1264 -> 1033 lines; signatures 2 unchanged (with the own-definitions fix), apps/spiral compiles, document_test 22 passed, type_probe PASS 18, respell_drift 0. The 3rd largest lib block (10455 chars) is gone with no consumer affected; F# remains a test target for split_args/split_command.")
inl note_of_x_weight_url_23_1 () = Note ("X-WEIGHT-URL-23", 1u32, "2026-11-03T15:44Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:43 x: FOUND by X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES (EDGE 3, n8): the user's own 2010-era tweets in 72784485 run to 314 raw chars with one URL, which X accepted because a URL counts 23 whatever its length. In x, splitter.spi:149 ':+ padded_as on_x = \\ _ us => us' applies no link padding (only on_mastodon uses 'linked', at mastodon_link_weight 23), and the sim's POST /2/tweets validates with splitter::x_weight (sim_serve.spi:865 too_heavy), so the sim answers 400 'text must be 1 to 280 characters' where X answers 201. xc post itself sends text unchanged and lets the server decide, so the client has no separate check. FIX: on_x pads links like Mastodon (one shared link_weight 23). IMPACT to measure, pre-registered: the thread splitter also weighs cells on OnX, so program cells that contain http(s):// URLs (comments, README-style docs) weigh less and may split into fewer posts. The proof re-runs the census (postable-report equality on x, and the cross-repo report) and lists every file whose post count changes; sim-stress fixtures regenerate under -Accept. A regression block: a 314-char text with one 60-char URL weighs 277 on X.")
inl note_of_x_weight_url_23_2 () = Note ("X-WEIGHT-URL-23", 2u32, "2026-11-03T15:48Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:48 x: DRAFTED (kit drafts x\\X-WEIGHT-URL-23\\splitter.spi, base 8EB7531B6F56 -> B4C81B0571A0). SECOND FINDING while reading: Mastodon's padded_link only RAISED short links to 23 ('at least 23') and never lowered long ones, but both X and Mastodon count every URL as exactly 23. New model: a link's first unit weighs link_weight () = 23 and its other units 0 (next_inside counts down the link, link_weighed sets each unit; linked recurses), shared by on_x and on_mastodon; Bluesky unchanged (bytes). mastodon_link_weight is renamed link_weight (no other user). Tests: the Mastodon block keeps 23/31; new block: 'x ' + a 60-char URL weighs 25 on X, the URL alone 23 on Mastodon. Folded into x-next1. RULE additions: client build CELL-BUDGET passes; e2e RUST-OK (its Mastodon step still holds 'Mastodon's split <= X's'); x census equality SAME with 'cells over one post 0' kept, which bounds the increase side (a URL shorter than 23 now weighs 23). Per-file post-count changes are listed in the cross-repo census after landing.")
inl note_of_x_weight_url_23_3 () = Note ("X-WEIGHT-URL-23", 3u32, "2026-11-03T15:49Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:49 x: CORRECTION to n2's 'folded into x-next1': moved to its OWN run x-next2 (scratchpad launch-x-next2.ps1). splitter.spi is inlined into several client crates, so in x-next1 it would confound X-JSON-COLLECT-DEDUP's pre-registered A/B ('no crate grows > 5'), which must change one thing. Order: x-next1 (dedup, oauth1, world) then x-next2 (this). Same rule as n2.")
inl note_of_x_weight_url_23_4 () = Note ("X-WEIGHT-URL-23", 4u32, "2026-11-03T16:45Z", "x", "2026-11-03 12:45 x: IMPACT PREDICTED before x-next2 (raw regex count of http(s):// links in tracked .spi). Across repos: fiscal 45 files / 572 links (104 shorter than 23, 468 longer), i574n.github 5 / 128, spiral 18 / 35, x 5 / 14, Spiral-s-ML-Library 9 / 9, tictactoe 5 / 9, blog 3 / 8, dice 2 / 4, alphabet, dir-tree-html, eoie, kit, polyglot 1 each. x: 10 of 14 links are SHORTER than 23 (api.x.com 17, 127.0.0.1:9 18-19, bsky.social 19, console.x.com 21, a.co 11), so their cells get heavier; the 4 long sim problem URLs get lighter. Every affected x cell under the fix: cli usage_environment 119->125, wire tests 148->157 / 154->159 / 182->186 / 207->213, xc base_url 69->75 / write_base 199->203 / missing_bearer 143->145, splitter mastodon test 191->203, sim_serve example_signing 245->226 / problem_kind 81->57 / protected_entry 221->188 / unsupported_kind 93->61. Max 213 < 272, so PREDICTION: x-next2 keeps CELL-BUDGET passing and 'cells over one post 0'. A spill there would mean the model is wrong, not the budget. Fiscal's 468 long links get lighter (fewer posts), which the cross-repo census will show.")
inl note_of_x_weight_url_23_5 () = Note ("X-WEIGHT-URL-23", 5u32, "2026-11-03T21:30Z", "x", "2026-11-03 17:30 x: DONE. GREEN in x-next2 (job-1793737559206, pinned pack27 core 6F83B94FE33167CC; the installed core moved to D1854B99FD3FA940 mid-run): lib gate 23/23, sim accept 0, sim build 0, client build 0 (CELL-BUDGET, xc.rs 992), e2e RUST-OK 37 + 5, census SAME over 76 files with 0 over one post. The prediction held: CELL-BUDGET passed and x keeps 0 cells over one post. Visible effect: the e2e made 139 requests instead of 141, because it posts oauth1.spi as a thread and its long api.x.com URLs now weigh 23, so the thread needs fewer posts; thread-oauth1.expected.json regenerated under -Accept accordingly. The long-form shape block (weight 2,014) passed too. LANDED unstaged: splitter.spi EC590CC5C413 (formatted) plus outputs.")
inl note_of_compiler_gadt_implicit_existential_export_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-GADT-IMPLICIT-EXISTENTIAL-EXPORT", 1u32, "2026-11-03T16:40Z", "tibia", "11-03 12:5x tibia, found while drafting lib pickler v3 (LIB-PICKLER-GADT). Isolation on europa (single-flight compiler, same lib), one variable per step, consumer main calls pickler::round_trips (pickler::sample ()): (1) v2 as landed: compiles. (2) leaf t marked recursive (:| ^ leaf t): compiles. (3) + case LeafIso :: \\* a b. (a -> b) * (b -> a) * leaf b -> leaf a with matching arms: the MODULE reports no error, but the consumer gets ETY-ValueRestriction Got: _ (option i32 * bool) -> bool, and with an annotation pickler::pick ... gives ETY-ModuleIndexFailed: The module does not have the key: pick; a probe IN the module file then shows Unbound type variable: pick / Unbound term variable: PPair, LeafBelow from bits3 onward. (4) same case without function fields, LeafIso :: \\* a. leaf a -> leaf a: compiles. (5) function field but no existential, LeafIso :: \\* a. (a -> a) * leaf a -> leaf a: compiles. So the trigger is a case type variable (b) absent from the result type: expected either acceptance or a diagnostic at the case, never a silent loss of the module exports. The supported spelling is an explicit existential in a separate union (xsd_certificate.spi PackedMoves : (\\? n. ...), unpacked with := \\? n. x = packed). Tibia worked around it (LeafEnum fixes b = i32). Repro files: europa /root/tibia-probes/stage pickler_exp*.spi are not kept; rebuild from the cases above.")
inl note_of_compiler_gadt_implicit_existential_export_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-GADT-IMPLICIT-EXISTENTIAL-EXPORT", 2u32, "2026-11-04T04:19Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:2x compiler groom (star exchange, tibia #3): raised P2 -> P1. The failure is SILENT: every export of the module disappears, consumers see unbound names, and there is no error at the GADT. tibia lost about an hour bisecting it on 11-03. A loud error is the first step (name the case and the implicit existential variable, and suggest an explicit exists); then make the export work. Next step: a regression sample, a module with one such case plus one plain export, that a consumer imports.")
inl note_of_compiler_gadt_implicit_existential_export_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-GADT-IMPLICIT-EXISTENTIAL-EXPORT", 3u32, "2026-11-04T04:46Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:5x compiler: REPRODUCED, AND THE CAUSE IS NOT WHAT THE TITLE SAYS. Repro (box, pack32/pack33 compilers; also pack15c, pack20 and pack25 backups): model leaf t with LeafInt :: i32 -> leaf i32 and LeafIso :: \\* a b. (a -> b) * (b -> a) * leaf b -> leaf a, plus a consumer. Every compiler REPORTS it, in all 4 layouts (same package or dependency package; consumer calling a plain export or the GADT; --check of the module or the consumer): ETY-UnusedTypeVariable 'The type variable b is unused in the function's type signature. In the definition: leaf', then the cascade (Unbound type variable: leaf, CasePatternNotFound, consumer ModuleIndexFailed). Two real defects explain why it looked silent: (1) in a dependency-package layout the ENTRY package's errors print FIRST (states = tc :: dependencies), so the consumer's ModuleIndexFailed tops the list and the root cause sits below it; (2) the message speaks of a function's type signature for a union case and never mentions existentials. Fix drafted: kit drafts COMPILER-GADT-IMPLICIT-EXISTENTIAL-EXPORT\\1-name-the-case-root-cause-first.kitdo (5 replacements). It adds a new error, ETY-GadtImplicitExistential, at the GADT result-type check, naming the case and variables and pointing to the explicit (\\? b. ...) spelling, and lists dependency packages' errors before the entry package's (topological order, deepest first). Node review job compiler-gadtx running (control = pack33 build).")
inl note_of_compiler_gadt_implicit_existential_export_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-GADT-IMPLICIT-EXISTENTIAL-EXPORT", 4u32, "2026-11-04T05:03Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:1x compiler: REVIEWED node-first (job compiler-gadtx, node, control = pack33 build). Base D00B41EE -> source DB6F930D1C1343FE, candidate core 91B04B53D050A50E, host 6B30D2F5 unchanged. The repro rows behave as predicted: gx-bad and gx-bad-pkg now lead with ETY-GadtImplicitExistential ('The GADT case LeafIso binds b, which its result type does not mention ... pack it explicitly in the payload: (\\? b. ...)'). In gx-bad-pkg the consumer's ModuleIndexFailed used to print first; it now comes after the root cause. gx-ok arms are unchanged. Contracts Rust 8 rows and Zig 620 rows: 0 differing cells, 0 behavior, 0 DISAGREE. Noted anomaly: the node's candidate gx-bad printed 11 ETY lines against control's 6. Box rerun of the same core: 6 = 6 on both arms. The extra 5 were the host's timing-dependent 'TypeErrors (uri): %A' dump of model.spi's errors (Program.fs diagnosticForOther). That race exists on control too and is not from this patch. Regression sample drafted at kit drafts COMPILER-GADT-IMPLICIT-EXISTENTIAL-EXPORT\\sample\\gadt_implicit_existential_negative. Its matrix ExpectedErrors entry is Contains 'ETY-GadtImplicitExistential: The GADT case LeafIso binds b' (verified on the candidate). It lands WITH the install, since live pack33 would mark it WRONG-ERROR. Bundled as pack34 with COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC once that review passes.")
inl note_of_compiler_gadt_implicit_existential_export_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-GADT-IMPLICIT-EXISTENTIAL-EXPORT", 5u32, "2026-11-04T05:45Z", "tibia", "11-04 01:45 tibia: dropped the plain tibia tag I added when filing this item (tags now compiler,gadt,existential,team:compiler). It made the stop gate's --team tibia routing offer compiler's in-flight fix back to tibia. Tibia still consumes the result: LIB-PICKLER-GADT v8 uses a record-of-operations wrap instead of an existential, so it no longer waits on this item.")
inl note_of_compiler_gadt_implicit_existential_export_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-GADT-IMPLICIT-EXISTENTIAL-EXPORT", 6u32, "2026-11-04T08:11Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:1x compiler: HANDED TO MAIN as pack34 (5 programs: GADT, link v4, forall hint, format --respell, Zig fast), with the exact -Programs command. Final node review compiler-pack34f: source B33D213B29D2757D, core dll 0758BAF496AE4E7D, host 6B30D2F5; contracts Rust 8 and Zig 620: 0 differing cells; examples F#: only load-timeout flips, verified on a 180 s rerun. Box dry run ok. Staged at spiral-bin-candidates\\pack34. Done when installed; regression samples land right after the install.")
inl note_of_compiler_gadt_implicit_existential_export_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-GADT-IMPLICIT-EXISTENTIAL-EXPORT", 7u32, "2026-11-04T08:47Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:5x compiler: DONE. pack34 installed by main at 04:4x on the box (core 0758BAF496AE4E7D, equal to the reviewed candidate; undo compiler-installs\\pack34-20261104-043420), the node (in-place swap, stale shadow dirs removed) and europa (built from B33D source). Regression samples landed with it (unstaged): samples/gadt_implicit_existential_negative and samples/forall_real_block_operator_negative, plus their matrix ExpectedErrors entries. Box run on pack34: both rows compile=error with the expected text (no WRONG-ERROR).")
inl note_of_html_node_records_1 () = Note ("HTML-NODE-RECORDS", 1u32, "2026-11-03T17:09Z", "main", "main 11-03 13:1x from HTML-TREE's last open point. The positional File : string * string * i64 / Folder : string * string * list node lets name and rel swap silently (both string). Records name them. Risk to check first: records as union payloads crossing linked-unit boundaries (memory spiral-link-boundary-shapes: direct non-primitive records may inline); prove with the 8-crate build, the 1000-line gate, and the nested fixture byte for byte.")
inl note_of_html_node_records_2 () = Note ("HTML-NODE-RECORDS", 2u32, "2026-11-03T17:27Z", "main", "main 11-03 13:4x DONE (unstaged; model.spi, tree.spi, cli/html.spi; backups %TEMP%\\html-before-records). node = File : { name; rel; bytes } | Folder : { name; rel; children } (inline record payloads); every pattern reads f::name / f::rel / f::bytes / f::children; fixtures build records (the order fixture through a small file helper). The link-boundary risk didn't materialize: the generated Rust is BYTE-IDENTICAL for all 8 crates (records lower like the tuples did), so it's a pure source-level gain: name and rel can no longer swap silently. kit standards 0. Europa: tree 5/5, pages 5/5, fast build HTML-OK (self-test incl. the nested fixture byte for byte).")
inl note_of_compiler_lua_union_tag_iife_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE", 1u32, "2026-11-03T17:29Z", "tibia", "11-03 13:4x tibia, found while probing derived enum picklers (LIB-PICKLER-GADT). Program: a payload-free union compass (4 cases), case_index x = ~> real_core::union_tag x on a dyn value (:= ~d = Southward), and case_at i = union_untag with nominal_create (the base.spi defaultof pattern) on a dyn index. Generated Lua (europa, single-flight compiler): local v8 = ;(function () local __tag if v0.tag == \"Us0_Northward\" then __tag = 0 ... return __tag end)() and lua5.1 fails with szl.lua:24: unexpected symbol near ;. The ; guard belongs only in statement position. Same program: C VERDICT OK (and the negative twin VERDICT BAD), Rust VERDICT OK. Constant-folded uses (tag of a literal case) do not hit it.")
inl note_of_compiler_lua_union_tag_iife_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE", 2u32, "2026-11-03T23:20Z", "compiler", "11-03 19:2x compiler: REPRODUCED and fixed in proof. Cause: codegenLua's UnionTag arm (spiral_compiler.fs:20030) builds its IIFE text as ';(function () local __tag ...)()' and simple binds it as 'local vN = <text>', so the statement-position guard lands in expression position. The guard is not needed there: simple already wraps a bare value that starts with '(' as 'do ... end'. Program COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE\\1-no-semicolon-in-expression.kitdo drops the ';'. New regression sample samples/lua_union_tag_runtime (payload-free compass, union_tag on a dyn Southward, exit 0 when tag = 2): installed pack30 -> lua5.1 'main.lua:8: unexpected symbol near ;'. Node review (job compiler-luatag, vs installed pack30) running: sample A/B under lua5.1 + examples Lua -Native compare.")
inl note_of_compiler_lua_union_tag_iife_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE", 3u32, "2026-11-03T23:32Z", "compiler", "11-03 19:3x compiler: PROVEN and handed to main as pack31 (source 65A08A87 -> 60CDD696, core 24EEB518; dry run ok). Node vs pack30: sample lua_union_tag_runtime control lua5.1 'unexpected symbol near ;' exit 1 -> candidate 'local v1 = (function () ...' exit 0; examples Lua -Native 442 rows: only frontier_format_any_union differs, a candidate compile timeout under load; recompiled on the box with both compilers: byte-identical Lua (3B93E38A), runs exit 0 (33-39 s cold compile, over the 20 s row limit). The other ';(' in codegenLua (19957, array store) is a statement and stays. Done when main installs.")
inl note_of_compiler_lua_union_tag_iife_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE", 4u32, "2026-11-04T00:22Z", "compiler", "11-03 20:2x compiler: INSTALLED on the box as pack31 (live source 60CDD6967F16595C, installed core 24EEB518F30F7727 at 20:19, relaunched by main after the 19:50 BSOD); node swap and europa build follow in main's chain. Regression row samples/lua_union_tag_runtime is in the matrix Lua list. Done.")
inl note_of_compiler_lua_union_tag_iife_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-LUA-UNION-TAG-IIFE", 5u32, "2026-11-04T00:38Z", "tibia", "11-03 20:4x tibia: pack31 VERIFIED from the consumer side on europa (main reports it live 20:24): the original repro (case_probe: payload-free union compass, union_tag on a dyn value, union_untag + nominal_create on a dyn index) now compiles to Lua with no = ;(function and runs on lua5.1: positive prints VERDICT OK, negative twin VERDICT BAD; C unchanged (OK / BAD with exit 0 / 1). Follow-up in lib queued: pickler v6 adds ///> lua to the derived-enum test (node gate running).")
inl note_of_linked_units_matrix_suite_1 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MATRIX-SUITE", 1u32, "2026-11-03T20:06Z", "compiler", "11-03 16:0x compiler: FILED from a gap found while closing LINKED-UNITS-OPAQUE-TIER. No package.spiproj under apps/compiler/tmp/samples uses link:, and test.ps1 compiles each sample entry alone, so every linked-unit proof today (lu5-lu14, nosuffix; this session's pack24-pack29 reviews) is an ad-hoc node script, and a regression in ExternCalls or the union import is caught only downstream (kit, html, x). SHAPE: cases under tmp/samples/linked_<name>/ with one dir per package (model/, app/, optional units/), the app's package.spiproj listing the others under link:; a 'linked' suite in test.ps1 builds the units in link order (each writes its unit graph), then the app, per backend in the case's list: Rust (rustc --crate-type rlib per unit + --extern, as node-lu9fix.ps1 did), Zig (all .zig in one dir, zig build-exe main.zig), Gleam (one gleam project with every module). It scores exit code against an expected value in the case, plus a 'linked' column (did the app call the unit, grep '<unit>::' / '<unit>.') so a silent inline is a failure. Negative cases (nosuffix) expect a compile error text. SEED: lu12 (Zig records + tuples), lu13 (Zig union), lu14 (Rust host type), nosuffix (error), lu9 (core list over a model union, Rust). Fixture sources are in this session's scratchpad and C:\\home\\remote-stage\\compiler-lu1x on the node.")
inl note_of_linked_units_matrix_suite_2 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MATRIX-SUITE", 2u32, "2026-11-03T20:51Z", "compiler", "11-03 16:5x compiler: FIRST CUT IN TREE (unstaged). test.ps1 -Suite linked (+65 lines, outside the worker pool, refuses to combine with other suites): per case in matrix.psd1 'Linked', finds the root unit (no other unit lists it under packages:), compiles its link: units then the root into <run>/linked/<case>/<backend>/, links Rust rlibs with --extern and builds Zig files side by side, runs the root and compares Exit; a case with Error expects the compile to fail with that text; Calls lists exports the root must call through the unit, so a silent inline is a FAIL in the 'linked' column. Seeded cases: samples/linked_rust_host_type (Rust), linked_zig_records, linked_zig_union (Zig), linked_union_name_clash (Rust/Gleam/Zig, error). On the installed pack27 (run single-flight-20261103-164913): rust_host_type pass; zig_records FAIL 'inlined: model_make,model_pair'; zig_union FAIL 'expected type app.US0, found model.shape'; name_clash FAIL (compiles silently with color_1). Exactly the gaps LINKED-UNITS-ZIG 4/5 and LINKED-UNITS-NO-SUFFIX close, so pack29 should turn all 6 rows green. Documented in apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md. Left: Gleam native runner, lu9 (core list over a model union) as a case.")
inl note_of_linked_units_matrix_suite_3 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MATRIX-SUITE", 3u32, "2026-11-03T21:21Z", "compiler", "11-03 17:2x compiler: 5th case added, linked_rust_list_over_model (lu9, core list over a model union, four Rust crates; exit 108, Calls parse_parse,render_blocks_html). The suite now builds the transitive link: closure in post-order and gives each Rust unit --extern for every unit built before it; a types-only link (use model::{..}) counts as linked. Results: installed pack27/28: 2/7 pass (rust_host_type, list_over_model); the pack29 candidate (core 2ABF22D7): 7/7 pass (Zig records + union link, the name clash fails with 'both named color' on Rust/Gleam/Zig). Left: Gleam native runner.")
inl note_of_linked_units_matrix_suite_4 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MATRIX-SUITE", 4u32, "2026-11-03T21:27Z", "compiler", "11-03 17:2x compiler: Gleam runner added (the persistent project <cache>/scratch/<mode>/gleam-linked from tests/native-shims/gleam, so dependencies download once; units go in src/<unit>.gleam, the root becomes src/main.gleam, *_ffi sidecars copied; gleam build, then gleam run -m spiral_run). linked_zig_records and linked_zig_union renamed linked_records and linked_union and now run on Zig AND Gleam. Totals on the pack29 candidate: 9 rows, all pass (Rust 3, Zig 3, Gleam 3). DoD met except lu-era fixtures beyond these five; AGENTS.md documents the suite.")
inl note_of_linked_units_matrix_suite_5 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-MATRIX-SUITE", 5u32, "2026-11-04T00:35Z", "compiler", "11-03 20:4x compiler: on the INSTALLED pack31 (box, core 24EEB518): test.ps1 -Suite linked 9/9 pass (run single-flight-20261103-202245), Rust 3 + Zig 3 + Gleam 3, so the suite now guards the live compiler. DoD met: the suite exists, five seeded cases (linked_records, linked_union, linked_rust_host_type, linked_rust_list_over_model, linked_union_name_clash), per-backend runners for Rust/Zig/Gleam, a 'linked' column that fails silent inlines, an Error mode for negative cases, and docs in apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md. Further cases are added per new boundary shape.")
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_1 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 1u32, "2026-11-04T01:02Z", "compiler", "11-03 21:0x compiler: FILED as the follow-up to RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE (Done; pack26 slice 3 moves, pack29 slice 4 by-value stack matches, pack30 freeze-only-closure-regions). CENSUS on apps/spiral/spiral.rs (pack29 candidate): 4398 clones, 41 heap-union matches 'match &*vN', 211 stack matches still by reference (scrutinee live after), 290 arm rebind clones remain. A heap union is Rc<UHn>, so a dead scrutinee can't be moved out directly. SHAPE: when the scrutinee is dead after the match (slice 4's test, frozen-region aware), emit 'match Rc::try_unwrap(vN) { Ok(u) => match u { arms by value }, Err(rc) => match &*rc { arms with clones } }', which duplicates arm text, so only for arms below a size cap; or 'Rc::unwrap_or_clone(vN)' (stable since Rust 1.76) and then match by value, which clones the payload only when shared, with no duplication: prefer that. Acceptance per memory rust-move-packs-need-downstream-rustc: examples Rust -Native on -ChangedBetween rows, apps/spiral cargo check, regex certificate A/B, kit_log_counts/sections. Use test.ps1 -BackendsFrom (it will say Rust).")
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_2 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 2u32, "2026-11-04T01:26Z", "compiler", "11-03 21:2x compiler: v1 REVIEWED (node vs pack32 = monorule build). Program RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES\\1-unwrap-or-clone-dead-heap.kitdo: a dead heap-union scrutinee (slice 4's dead test, so frozen closure regions are excluded) matches 'Rc::unwrap_or_clone(vN)' by value and moves every field. v1 results: regex certificate 16/16 both; examples Rust 21 changed rows, native 21/21 ran on control, candidate one build-fail: samples/rust_union_non_clone_payload, a heap union whose payload is not Clone, so UHn derives no Clone and unwrap_or_clone does not apply; 0 DISAGREE. apps/spiral .clone() 3201 -> 3117 (-84), cargo check 0 errors; kit_log_counts/sections (box): 16 + 21 unwrap_or_clone sites, cargo check 0 errors; -Suite linked 9/9. v2 adds 'not moving' (the union has no non-Clone field, the same flag slice 4 uses) and is under review (job compiler-heapmove2).")
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_3 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 3u32, "2026-11-04T01:43Z", "compiler", "11-03 21:4x compiler: v2 PROVEN and handed to main as pack33 (low priority). v2 adds 'not moving' (no non-Clone field in the union, the same flag as slice 4). Source A8232488 (pack32) -> D00B41EE, core D1379FED, box dry run ok. Node vs pack32: E0507 sample unchanged; certificate 16/16; examples Rust 20 changed rows, native 20/20 on both arms, 0 behavior cells, 0 DISAGREE (rust_union_non_clone_payload back to unchanged); apps/spiral .clone() 3201 -> 3117, cargo check 0 errors. Box: kit_log_counts/sections cargo check 0 errors. The remaining heap matches are unions with non-Clone payloads (Rc::try_unwrap would be needed there) and scrutinees that are live after the match.")
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_4 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 4u32, "2026-11-04T01:43Z", "compiler", "11-03 21:4x compiler: install DEFERRED to 11-04 by main (box at 100% CPU near the 22:00 end; it BSODed under sustained load at 19:44). The next lane to install takes pack33 with the command in the handover (candidate staged at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin-candidates\\pack33; it reproduces from live pack32 A8232488). Rerun the dry run first, since live may move.")
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_5 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 5u32, "2026-11-04T04:20Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:2x compiler: pack33 handover addendum (user 11-04: node and europa first, box last). The install command is unchanged: rerun the dry run first, then install-pack.ps1 -Name pack33 -Programs <RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES\\1-unwrap-or-clone-dead-heap.kitdo> -Apply. After the install, on every machine that swapped its DLL in place, list the dotnet SpiralCompiler processes started before the swap and restart them, or gate with -Compiler process. Warm daemons keep the old compiler (tibia found it after pack31; main #3). Post-install re-gate: test.ps1 -BackendsFrom pack32-src,pack33-src (expect Rust only), then -Native -ChangedBetween on the node, not a whole matrix on the box. Root fix filed: COMPILER-DAEMON-STALE-AFTER-PACK.")
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_6 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 6u32, "2026-11-04T04:35Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:3x compiler: READY, handed to main for the install (user 11-04: node-first). The review was already node-first (11-03, node vs pack32). Dry run re-run on live today: core A823248809D9B560 -> D00B41EE3CD5F7FF, host DFF8371A unchanged, ok. Main installs and runs the downstream re-gates on europa. Done when the install has gone through and the re-gates pass.")
inl note_of_rust_heap_union_moves_7 () = Note ("RUST-HEAP-UNION-MOVES", 7u32, "2026-11-04T04:47Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:4x compiler: DONE. pack33 installed on the box by main at 00:46: core dll D1379FEDC807FC68, equal to the reviewed candidate; live core source D00B41EE3CD5F7FF; host 6B30D2F5 unchanged. The node swap and europa build are deferred by main until TRAIN18 (node, pinned to pack32) has a verdict; then main runs the Rust-only re-gates (apps/spiral, kit crates, regex certificate) on europa. A failing re-gate reopens this item.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_list_last_note_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-LIST-LAST-NOTE", 1u32, "2026-11-04T01:48Z", "compiler", "11-03 21:5x compiler: FILED from MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP n21. 'kit agile list <root> all --team compiler --json' returns 300 tasks whose last_note field is an empty string for every task, so a 'no note in 48 h' sweep can't be computed from one listing and flags all 100 open items; the only route is one 'agile show' per item. DoD: list --json fills last_note with {at, author} of the newest note (the same note 'show --last 1' prints), and a test row asserts it for a task with notes and is empty only for a task with none. Then MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP can run its 48 h filter in one call.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_list_last_note_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-LIST-LAST-NOTE", 2u32, "2026-11-04T20:18Z", "kit", "11-04 20:5x kit: DONE, verified on the INSTALLED kit (TRAIN14c): `kit agile list <root> Active --json` -> 130 rows, every one with a non-empty last_note (fields: id, title, kind, priority, difficulty, progress, status, deps, tags, notes, last_note, estimate_minutes, load, assignee). The bug in the title no longer reproduces.")
inl note_of_compiler_daemon_stale_after_pack_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-DAEMON-STALE-AFTER-PACK", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:19Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:2x compiler (star exchange, tibia #5): after the pack31 install a tibia gate kept failing on the Lua union_tag bug pack31 had fixed, until a rerun with -Compiler process. A warm compiler daemon outlived the DLL swap. Port-per-build (CELLS-DAEMON-PORT-PER-BUILD) does not cover a pack swap at the same path. Fix options: (a) install-pack.ps1 retires the daemons it replaces, (b) the daemon compares its DLL hash on each request and refuses or restarts on a mismatch. (b) also covers manual swaps. Next step: read where test.ps1 and Cells start the daemon and which DLL path they key on.")
inl note_of_compiler_daemon_stale_after_pack_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-DAEMON-STALE-AFTER-PACK", 2u32, "2026-11-04T04:54Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:0x compiler: ROOT CAUSE FOUND AND FIXED (unstaged, apps/cells). The Cells compiler daemon stamped only the HOST SpiralCompiler.dll (mtime, size) for three things: compiler_current? (its per-request restart check), the shadow-copy dir name and build_port. A pack swap changes only SpiralCompilerCore.dll, and the host is byte-identical (6B30D2F5 since 11-02). Node evidence: host mtime 11-02 22:34, core swapped 11-03 20:58, shadow dir 1793673291-319488 named from the host alone. So after an in-place core swap the daemon kept serving, and even a FRESH daemon reused a .complete shadow holding the old core; only -Compiler process escaped it. The box install was never affected: it refuses while daemons run and rebuilds, which rewrites the host. Fix: dll_stamp folds in the sibling SpiralCompilerCore.dll ({newest mtime, phash2 of both sizes and mtimes}; the dir format stays digits-digits, so prune_shadows still matches) and build_port uses dll_stamp. New test: a core-only swap with an OLDER mtime than the host (a preserved-mtime copy, the worst case) is not current and gets a fresh shadow holding the new core. Box: compiler_daemon_test Result: 27 passed (own MIX_BUILD_PATH). Running daemons pick up the new Elixir code only after a restart; the next node pack swap (pack33 after TRAIN18) is the live proof. x: the Spiral port of compiler_daemon (CELLS-PORT-COMPILER-DAEMON) must carry this stamp.")
inl note_of_compiler_daemon_stale_after_pack_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-DAEMON-STALE-AFTER-PACK", 3u32, "2026-11-04T05:48Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:5x compiler: LIVE CONFIRMATION (main, pack33 node swap at 01:45). Two Cells daemons started AFTER the in-place swap ran from %TEMP%\\spiral-cells-compiler\\1793673291-319488, which still held the OLD core A63F4C2A. That is exactly the host-only stamp failure the fix addresses. Main deleted the dir. The node's Elixir does not have the fix yet (unstaged box tree; it reaches the node with the next mirror sync), so until then the swap procedure is to delete the host-stamp shadow dir.")
inl note_of_compiler_daemon_stale_after_pack_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-DAEMON-STALE-AFTER-PACK", 4u32, "2026-11-04T08:52Z", "compiler", "11-04 05:0x compiler: TWIN FIXED (found by x) plus a regression of my own fix. apps/cells/spi/run_notebook.ps1 keyed the warm notebook server's port file by the HOST dll's mtime and length only, so a warm server from before a pack kept serving the old core (x-next5b timeline_serve 3/4 during a core move A63F -> D137, then 4/4 three times fresh). Worse, my 01:0x dll_stamp change had made Elixir's dll_tag (used to name the port file) a phash2 digest that PowerShell cannot compute, so since then run_notebook could not find the warm server at all and fell back. Fix (unstaged): dll_tag is now an explicit '<host mtime>-<host size>-<core mtime>-<core size>' string, computed the same way in compiler_daemon.ex, run_notebook.ps1 and daemon_collision_proof.ps1. dll_stamp (shadow dirs, the per-request current check, build_port) keeps its digest form. Proof: compiler_daemon_test 28 passed (new test: the tag changes on a core-only swap); on the box Elixir served_dll_tag = run_notebook's computation = 1793781634-319488-1793781658-12587008.")
inl note_of_compiler_forall_term_hint_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-FORALL-TERM-HINT", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:19Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:2x compiler (star exchange, tibia #7): inside ( ) or ~> type-level blocks, bare >, - and + resolve to the typed prototypes and fail with 'Cannot apply a forall with a term'. The trace points at the operator but gives no fix. The memory rule is to use real_core::(>) etc. The error should say so when the forall being applied is a prototype operator inside a real block. That cost tibia 20 min on 11-03. Related closed items with the same message and different causes: SPIRAL-OP-ARG-NAME-WORDS, SPIRAL-EXPORT-PIPE-FORALL. Next step: find where the error is raised in spiral_compiler.fs and what context (real block, op name) is available there.")
inl note_of_compiler_forall_term_hint_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-FORALL-TERM-HINT", 2u32, "2026-11-04T05:08Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:1x compiler: DRAFTED. kit drafts COMPILER-FORALL-TERM-HINT\\1-name-the-real-block-fix.kitdo (1 replacement at the DForall apply arm). The message keeps its first sentence ('Cannot apply a forall with a term.'), so greps still match, and adds: inside a `( ) or ~> block there is no type inference, so a bare >, +, - or = names the typed prototype, a forall; write real_core::(>) etc. there; elsewhere apply the forall's types first. DForall carries only its body E, not the source name, so the hint is conditional rather than naming the operator; the trace already points at it. Repro (box, pack33, lib package): main = ~> ? 2i32 > 1i32 ?> 0i32 |: 1i32 fails with exactly this error; with real_core::(>) 2i32 1i32 it compiles (exit 0). No test text matches the old message. It stacks with the GADT and link programs on live D00B41EE (combined FB4D41D2). It ships in pack34 with them.")
inl note_of_compiler_forall_term_hint_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-FORALL-TERM-HINT", 3u32, "2026-11-04T08:11Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:1x compiler: HANDED TO MAIN as pack34 (5 programs: GADT, link v4, forall hint, format --respell, Zig fast), with the exact -Programs command. Final node review compiler-pack34f: source B33D213B29D2757D, core dll 0758BAF496AE4E7D, host 6B30D2F5; contracts Rust 8 and Zig 620: 0 differing cells; examples F#: only load-timeout flips, verified on a 180 s rerun. Box dry run ok. Staged at spiral-bin-candidates\\pack34. Done when installed; regression samples land right after the install.")
inl note_of_compiler_forall_term_hint_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-FORALL-TERM-HINT", 4u32, "2026-11-04T08:47Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:5x compiler: DONE. pack34 installed by main at 04:4x on the box (core 0758BAF496AE4E7D, equal to the reviewed candidate; undo compiler-installs\\pack34-20261104-043420), the node (in-place swap, stale shadow dirs removed) and europa (built from B33D source). Regression samples landed with it (unstaged): samples/gadt_implicit_existential_negative and samples/forall_real_block_operator_negative, plus their matrix ExpectedErrors entries. Box run on pack34: both rows compile=error with the expected text (no WRONG-ERROR).")
inl note_of_formatter_emits_respell_spelling_1 () = Note ("FORMATTER-EMITS-RESPELL-SPELLING", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:19Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:2x compiler (star exchange, fiscal #6): the formatter emits fun, while the lib land window's respell step rejects fun (with exists and true/false). So a freshly formatted file fails a land until it is respelled by hand. Fiscal and tibia both lost a land to this on 11-03. Fix: the formatter emits the respelled forms (backslash lambdas, !+/!-), or format --respell is part of the format pass. Next step: list each spelling that format emits and respell-drift rejects, and check which one the edition-3 docs call canonical.")
inl note_of_formatter_emits_respell_spelling_2 () = Note ("FORMATTER-EMITS-RESPELL-SPELLING", 2u32, "2026-11-04T05:10Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:1x compiler: MEASURED, then DRAFTED. The formatter does not turn respelled code back into old words: format keeps \\x => and !+ as written, and a respelled probe passes --respell-drift after format. The trap is that plain format never respells: input written with fun/true/exists (by hand or by a generator) comes out formatted but still in words, and the land window's respell_drift stage rejects it. format --spelling symbols respells but does no layout, so no single command produced the form the gate accepts. Draft: kit drafts FORMATTER-EMITS-RESPELL-SPELLING\\1-format-respell-flag.kitdo (3 replacements). New flag format --respell: convert to Symbols, then formatText; also --check --respell. It honours ///- --no-respell (optsOutOfRespell), and plain format and --spelling keep their current behaviour, so no bulk diffs. Lanes run 'format --respell <files>' before requesting a window. Stacks with the other three pack34 programs (combined CB333D5F).")
inl note_of_formatter_emits_respell_spelling_3 () = Note ("FORMATTER-EMITS-RESPELL-SPELLING", 3u32, "2026-11-04T08:11Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:1x compiler: HANDED TO MAIN as pack34 (5 programs: GADT, link v4, forall hint, format --respell, Zig fast), with the exact -Programs command. Final node review compiler-pack34f: source B33D213B29D2757D, core dll 0758BAF496AE4E7D, host 6B30D2F5; contracts Rust 8 and Zig 620: 0 differing cells; examples F#: only load-timeout flips, verified on a 180 s rerun. Box dry run ok. Staged at spiral-bin-candidates\\pack34. Done when installed; regression samples land right after the install.")
inl note_of_formatter_emits_respell_spelling_4 () = Note ("FORMATTER-EMITS-RESPELL-SPELLING", 4u32, "2026-11-04T08:47Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:5x compiler: DONE. pack34 installed by main at 04:4x on the box (core 0758BAF496AE4E7D, equal to the reviewed candidate; undo compiler-installs\\pack34-20261104-043420), the node (in-place swap, stale shadow dirs removed) and europa (built from B33D source). Regression samples landed with it (unstaged): samples/gadt_implicit_existential_negative and samples/forall_real_block_operator_negative, plus their matrix ExpectedErrors entries. Box run on pack34: both rows compile=error with the expected text (no WRONG-ERROR).")
inl note_of_compiler_link_inline_diagnostic_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:22Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:2x compiler (star exchange, kit #2): a linked export whose argument or result is a direct non-primitive record (a record with a string field) or a curried function is silently INLINED into the caller instead of linked, with no diagnostic. kit_job_entry grew to 1350 lines and lost 2 deps at rustc time, costing a 3 h precheck. The shapes that link are arrays of records and tuple args (memory spiral-link-boundary-shapes). Fix: emit a warning, or an error under a strict flag, at unit-graph time: 'export X not linked: <field or arg> is not a boundary shape; use an array or a tuple'. Next step: find where the unit-graph builder decides inline vs extern (ExternCalls, unitGraphJson guard) and add the reason there.")
inl note_of_compiler_link_inline_diagnostic_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC", 2u32, "2026-11-04T05:04Z", "compiler", "11-04 01:0x compiler: DRAFTED and under node review (job compiler-linkwarn, control = pack33). Program: kit drafts COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC\\1-warn-inlined-linked-export.kitdo (3 replacements, stacks on the GADT program). At the peval call site, a call whose target is a linked export (ExternCalls.lookup hits) but whose types ExternCalls.callable refuses used to inline silently. It now prints once per export per build (the dedup set is cleared where ExternCalls.current is set): 'warning: <file:line:col>: the linked export <unit::name> is inlined here instead of called: its argument|result holds a function|a record|... (only a record whose fields are all primitives crosses, as a struct). A link: boundary takes plain values, arrays of records and tuples.' The reason mirrors callable exactly (plain, struct_fields, single), and codegen is unchanged. Fixture: linked_records plus a model export apply_twice taking a function, called from app. Prediction: exactly one warning per backend, app code byte-identical between arms.")
inl note_of_compiler_link_inline_diagnostic_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC", 3u32, "2026-11-04T07:32Z", "compiler", "11-04 03:3x compiler: v4 PROVEN (node job compiler-pack34d; base pack33 D00B41EE -> source F89F4D52, core A13FA566). v2 warned only for single-argument exports. v3 added a curried lookup, which found nothing, because noteDefinitionSource records only single-parameter functions, so a curried export's unit-graph target was (\"\",\"\") and never entered the importer's calls map. v4 keeps a separate curriedDefinitionSources table: the unit graph falls back to it for the export's target, and the call site warns from it. lookup (the linking path) never reads it, so a curried export can never be linked (a lookup on its inner lambda would have passed callable with a single argument and emitted a wrong call). Fixture (linked_records plus apply_twice (f : i32 -> i32) (x : i32) and apply_once (f : i32 -> i32)): Rust, Zig and Gleam each print exactly 2 warnings at main.spi:11:21 ('model_apply_twice ... its definition is curried, so the call has no single argument to pass') and 11:40 ('model_apply_once ... its argument holds a function'). App code is byte-identical to pack33 on all three; contracts Rust 8 and Zig 620: 0 differing cells. Ships in pack34 (final review compiler-pack34f running).")
inl note_of_compiler_link_inline_diagnostic_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC", 4u32, "2026-11-04T08:11Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:1x compiler: HANDED TO MAIN as pack34 (5 programs: GADT, link v4, forall hint, format --respell, Zig fast), with the exact -Programs command. Final node review compiler-pack34f: source B33D213B29D2757D, core dll 0758BAF496AE4E7D, host 6B30D2F5; contracts Rust 8 and Zig 620: 0 differing cells; examples F#: only load-timeout flips, verified on a 180 s rerun. Box dry run ok. Staged at spiral-bin-candidates\\pack34. Done when installed; regression samples land right after the install.")
inl note_of_compiler_link_inline_diagnostic_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-LINK-INLINE-DIAGNOSTIC", 5u32, "2026-11-04T08:47Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:5x compiler: DONE. pack34 installed by main at 04:4x on the box (core 0758BAF496AE4E7D, equal to the reviewed candidate; undo compiler-installs\\pack34-20261104-043420), the node (in-place swap, stale shadow dirs removed) and europa (built from B33D source). Regression samples landed with it (unstaged): samples/gadt_implicit_existential_negative and samples/forall_real_block_operator_negative, plus their matrix ExpectedErrors entries. Box run on pack34: both rows compile=error with the expected text (no WRONG-ERROR).")
inl note_of_xdumps_joined_world_1 () = Note ("XDUMPS-JOINED-WORLD", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:30Z", "x-dumps", "New xarchive `world` subcommand reads people/*/timeline.ndjson, emits one joined world.json (users ordered oldest-account-first so by_handle resolves the reused handle to the current holder; lost posts kept with lost:true + lost_reason for the render-filter; refs from kind). 2026-11-04: emitted to europa:/data/x/joined/joined.world.json (112M, 5 users, 162542 posts), validated (304 same-second ties confirm the id tie-break matters; retweets carry no reposted ref - data limitation). x owns the HTML timeline_serve route; I heal the emitter as replay finds edges.")
inl note_of_xdumps_joined_world_2 () = Note ("XDUMPS-JOINED-WORLD", 2u32, "2026-11-04T05:00Z", "x-dumps", "World rebuilt post-recovery 2026-11-04: europa:/data/x/joined/joined.world.json 163307 posts, media now objects per x spec, + joined/unrecoverable.ndjson index. Handed to x; awaiting x replay through the sim (id tie-break + handle + lost-filter slice) to surface edges. Emitter (xarchive world) is the deliverable; x owns timeline_serve route.")
inl note_of_xdumps_joined_world_3 () = Note ("XDUMPS-JOINED-WORLD", 3u32, "2026-11-04T05:30Z", "x-dumps", "Media backfill done 2026-11-04 05:29: 300 recovered-tweet media downloaded for fc1943s (132 pending at --max 300 cap, polish; 217 gone). World rebuilt 163307 posts WITH recovered media objects at europa:/data/x/joined/joined.world.json. x reads fresh at replay. Minor follow-up: re-backup fc1943s/i574n media (300 new files not in the in-flight important-media backup, which already passed those dirs); re-fetchable so low priority.")
inl note_of_xdumps_joined_world_4 () = Note ("XDUMPS-JOINED-WORLD", 4u32, "2026-11-04T07:02Z", "x-dumps", "Battle-test edge (x scan 2026-11-04): a post id can appear under 2 accounts when both retweet the same original (id 1814831297249837420, both @fc1943s retweeted @makeuokay). Our archive carries the ORIGINAL id for retweets (no repost-own status id exists - retweets have no archived permalink; verified raw live.ndjson has no retweeted_status/separate id). RESOLUTION: not an emitter fix (synthesizing a composite id = fabrication); x keys the sim post index per (author_id,id). Emitter stays faithful (original id + kind=retweet + retweet_author = typed Repost:user_id). Rest of x scan clean: 0 malformed stamps, 0 empty non-lost text, 0 post after now.")
inl note_of_xdumps_joined_world_5 () = Note ("XDUMPS-JOINED-WORLD", 5u32, "2026-11-04T10:15Z", "x-dumps", "Media backfill for recovered tweets done 2026-11-04 10:13 (fc1943s --max200 + stewshka --max100): stewshka media already cached (0 new), world re-emitted unchanged (163307 posts, unrecoverable 4860, lost-fix in place). Recovery now fully complete = all reachable TEXT + available MEDIA across the 4 live accounts. World at europa:/data/x/joined/joined.world.json ready for x replay. JOINED-WORLD emitter deliverable effectively done (held at 98 pending x replay confirming no further edges). europa 2.9G free - holding cache-prune lever at 1G trigger.")
inl note_of_xdumps_joined_world_6 () = Note ("XDUMPS-JOINED-WORLD", 6u32, "2026-11-04T11:08Z", "x-dumps", "Deep edge-scan 2026-11-04 (proactive battle-test before x replay): clean across 9 checks over all 163307 posts — 0 within-account dup ids, 0 non-numeric/empty ids, 0 empty author_id, 0 empty username, 0 bad/empty refs, 0 media missing media_key/type, 0 bad created_at, 0 empty conversation_id/edit_history, max text 2009 chars. No hidden edges beyond the 3 x already handles (cross-account dup->per-author key, lost-semantics->healed, same-second->id tie-break). World is robust for the sim replay; no emitter heal needed.")
inl note_of_xdumps_joined_world_7 () = Note ("XDUMPS-JOINED-WORLD", 7u32, "2026-11-05T02:16Z", "x-dumps", "DONE 2026-11-04: joined 5-account world.json built (163307 posts, deep-scanned clean, lost-semantics healed) AND battle-tested live on the real world via the sim-timeline demo — all 5 accounts interleave newest-first (2026-10-18 -> 2012), pagination cursor, @fc1943s-by-id disambiguation, 4860 lost hidden, authorship labels (reply/repost/quote), stable port + infinite scroll (xprobe12). Validated end-to-end on localhost. Production frontend LAND rides x-next9 (route/render drafts type-check on europa); the world + its battle-test are complete.")
inl note_of_xdumps_recovery_pass2_1 () = Note ("XDUMPS-RECOVERY-PASS2", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:30Z", "x-dumps", "Research 2026-11-04 found a live legit path the first sweep missed: cdn.syndication.twimg.com/tweet-result?id=ID&token=any recovers full text for still-LIVE accounts (verified on @fc1943s). Plan: diff CDX-known tweet IDs vs our captured-with-text set per live account, replay the gap through tweet-result (throttled 1-2 rps, public embed, no auth/bot bypass). geygey_hadid is SUSPENDED (tombstone) so its only new avenue is the Archive Team twitterstream bulk dataset grepped by numeric user id (1% sample). Confirmed dead: Nitter (C&D Aug 2026), Google/Bing cache, oEmbed, DocNow rehydration.")
inl note_of_xdumps_recovery_pass2_2 () = Note ("XDUMPS-RECOVERY-PASS2", 2u32, "2026-11-04T04:43Z", "x-dumps", "ROOT CAUSE found 2026-11-04: cdx.rs captures_by_id filtered to statuscode 200/- so tweet_ids never included 302 login-wall ids; the live stage (which already uses cdn.syndication tweet-result) never tried them. Fix: tweet_ids now reads all status ids. Scope per account: fc1943s NEW~1031 (302s), i574n ~1, juhondacivic/stewshka 0, geygey_hadid 19425 but SUSPENDED (live=tombstone, 0 text; needs twitterstream). 30-id sample of the fc1943s gap = 22 live/7 deleted/1 empty. Running live+context+timeline for fc1943s+i574n then merge+world on europa, throttled 1.5rps, network-bound/low-CPU. media downloads skipped (disk-safe); text backfill later.")
inl note_of_xdumps_recovery_pass2_3 () = Note ("XDUMPS-RECOVERY-PASS2", 3u32, "2026-11-04T05:00Z", "x-dumps", "DONE for reachable 2026-11-04: ran fixed live+context+timeline for fc1943s+i574n -> account 72784485 1950->2715 (+765 recovered). fc1943s 2350 alive/2012 gone of 4362; i574n 599/245 of 844. merge+world rebuilt: 163307 posts. Remaining unrecoverable=4896 (72784485 only 54 left; geygey 3417 SUSPENDED, juhondacivic 1259, stewshka 87, 1637 79) - these are kept in joined/unrecoverable.ndjson and filtered from scroll, which is the users intended handling for unreachable ones, so NOT chasing twitterstream (1% sample, hundreds of GB, low ROI). geygey suspended = genuinely unreachable by live.")
inl note_of_xdumps_recovery_pass2_4 () = Note ("XDUMPS-RECOVERY-PASS2", 4u32, "2026-11-04T05:14Z", "x-dumps", "Recovery output QA PASSED 2026-11-04: account 72784485 = 2715 posts, 0 empty-text non-lost (no garbage), 2005 live-sourced; sampled 2017-2023 all real text (EN+PT, dev-themed, consistent with account). Clean. Media for recovered tweets not downloaded (media stage skipped for disk safety) - text is complete; media backfill is a minor follow-up.")
inl note_of_xdumps_recovery_pass2_5 () = Note ("XDUMPS-RECOVERY-PASS2", 5u32, "2026-11-04T09:53Z", "x-dumps", "Extended recovery to the other live accounts 2026-11-04: probed their lost-ids via tweet-result. juhondacivic: 0/20 recoverable (all tombstone) - its 1259 lost are genuine deletions/suspension, correctly indexed+filtered, no re-run. stewshka: 13/20 recoverable (~65%) - its ~87 lost were PENDING (rate-limited/unfetched) in the big harvest, not deletions. Running live+context+timeline+merge+world for stewshka to recover ~57 tweets (detached pid 2270697, waiter bmicktg18). So recovery now covers all 4 live accounts: fc1943s/i574n (+765 done), stewshka (~57 running); geygey suspended (indexed); juhondacivic lost = real deletions (indexed).")
inl note_of_xdumps_recovery_pass2_6 () = Note ("XDUMPS-RECOVERY-PASS2", 6u32, "2026-11-04T09:58Z", "x-dumps", "stewshka recovery DONE 2026-11-04: recovered 26 pending-not-deleted tweets (lost 87->61). It ALSO exposed an emitter heal: recovered tweets kept wayback stale lost-reason -> flagged lost:true despite having text -> were being HIDDEN from the scroll. Fixed world.rs: lost is now content-based (text OR media present = never lost; lost only when no content). Re-verified world: lost:true=4860 (was 4896), 0 lost-with-content, 0 empty-content-shown. Recovery now covers all reachable: fc1943s/i574n +765, stewshka +26; juhondacivic 1259 + geygey 3417 = genuine deletions/suspension (indexed+filtered).")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:47Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:47 tibia, FIRST RUN (europa scratch stage zigab, compiler pack32-src-before 21e08222): the Zig build first failed at compile time (file_system.read_bytes reaches am'::from_vec, a Rust-only backend_switch), then at run time on convert.f32_of_bits and unicode.scalar_text (their default arms call failwith). With three scratch arms (readFileAlloc as in read_all_text; @as(f32, @bitCast(bits)); utf8_text with a spiral_gpa one-byte string), tf-zig output is byte-identical to tf-c on stories260K and stories15M. Single unquiet runs (load ~4, another lane's rustc): C -Ofast 1325 ms, Zig ReleaseFast 4510 ms on 15M. perf: loading is negligible; C spends 81% in the vectorized dot_l26, while Zig inlines everything into spiralMain and layers_from_18. Hypothesis: strict float mode blocks vectorizing the dot reduction (gcc -Ofast reassociates). A 4-arm load-gated A/B (c-Ofast, c-O3, zig, zig with @setFloatMode(.optimized)) is RUNNING on europa, started 00:47.")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 2u32, "2026-11-04T05:11Z", "tibia", "11-04 01:11 tibia, FLOAT MODE IS THE LEVER (static, objdump on europa, same pack32 compile): packed ymm float ops / packed FMAs per binary: C -Ofast 56 / 17; C -O3 (strict IEEE) 28 / 2; Zig ReleaseFast (strict) 35 / 0; Zig with @setFloatMode(.optimized) prepended to every fn by a sed probe 1255 / 1144. All four print byte-identical tokens on stories15M (cmp, ab4 pre-check). So the Zig vs C gap of the first unquiet run (4.5 s vs 1.3 s) is strict float semantics blocking the dot reduction's vectorization, not Zig codegen. The fair pairs are C -O3 vs Zig and C -Ofast vs Zig-fm. Wall-clock 4-arm A/B is RUNNING (europa, nohup, ab4.out, 8 rotated rounds, load-gated); europa is at load 9-10 from other lanes' rustc and checks, so rows may be contaminated, and load is logged per row. For compiler, once the timings are in: an opt-in fast-float switch for the Zig backend (emitting @setFloatMode(.optimized), the analogue of gcc -Ofast) is what makes Zig a fair hot-loop backend for the transformer. Draft lib v1 (Zig arms) is being gated on the node (tibia-zig-gate-1).")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 3u32, "2026-11-04T05:39Z", "tibia", "11-04 01:39 tibia, 4-ARM A/B DONE BUT LOAD-CONTAMINATED (europa, stories15M, 8 rotated rounds; the 20-min quiet wait expired at load 3.2 and ALL 32 rows ran at load > 1.5, so these are ordering evidence, not a measurement): C -Ofast 1167 ms, Zig with @setFloatMode(.optimized) 3202, C -O3 (strict IEEE) 3462, Zig ReleaseFast strict 4567; all four outputs byte-identical. Reading: (1) float mode is the dominant lever for C as well, since strict C -O3 is ~3x slower than -Ofast; (2) strict vs strict, Zig is ~1.3x behind C -O3; (3) optimized Zig beats strict C but stays ~2.7x behind C -Ofast despite 1144 packed FMAs, so a second factor remains. Candidates: the sed probe put @setFloatMode only on top-level fn lines, not on the inlined loop bodies in layers_from_18 as compiled; bounds-checked slice indexing (ReleaseFast removes safety checks, so less likely); exp/sqrt through compiler_rt (0.56% in the profile, so not it). NEXT: perf annotate tf-zig-fm's hottest loop against C's dot_l26 on a quiet europa, then a clean 16-round rerun. No compiler ask until the clean numbers exist.")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 4u32, "2026-11-04T05:43Z", "tibia", "11-04 01:43 tibia, SECOND FACTOR FOUND: Spiral's Zig prelude spiralIndex (fn spiralIndex(len: usize, i: anytype) usize { if (i < 0 or i >= len) std.process.exit(3); return @intCast(i); }, 167 call sites in out.zig) runs a bounds check with a process exit on EVERY array access, even in ReleaseFast. perf annotate of tf-zig-fm's hottest loop (the 768-wide dot, cmp $0x300) shows scalar vfmadd231ss behind three per-iteration branches (js on a negative i, a signed cmp/sbb against len, a second cmp), all jumping to the exit block, so the reduction cannot vectorize. PROBE tf-zig-fmu = optimized float mode plus spiralIndex with the check removed (sed, probe only): output byte-identical to C, 3223 packed FMAs (C -Ofast has 17 in a smaller binary), and 4 rotated rounds at load ~3 against the same-load arms: C -Ofast 1578/1356/1190/1151 ms, zig-fm 4139/3126/3403/3187, zig-fmu 1834/1633/1214/1277. So Zig reaches C -Ofast speed once both levers are off: strict float mode and per-access exit checks. Both are compiler/codegen policy (the prelude and float mode), so they go to compiler as a finding. The lib arms (draft v1) stay the tibia part. Clean 16-round numbers still need a quiet europa.")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 5u32, "2026-11-04T06:03Z", "tibia", "11-04 02:0x tibia, NODE GATE tibia-zig-gate-1 READ: unicode is clean (positive 9/9, 0 type errors; mutant fails 2 of 9, the zig rows). file_system positive failed only its two zig float readers (read_f32s, map_f32s). That is a gate-design artifact: the job put one draft at a time into the mirror, so convert.spi was live (no Zig f32_of_bits) while file_system ran. Its mutant run was VOID (could not start the compiler: lock_timeout). convert failed widening_conversions_agree_across_native_backends on zig with 'Cannot parse the literal as: i32', a test this draft does not touch, so a baseline run of the live file decides whether it predates the draft. BUDGET: by definition name, convert and unicode have no new over-budget definitions; file_system v1 added 3 (zig_read_bytes 290, zig_write_bytes 296, write_bytes 296). V2 (drafts TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND v2): a zig_io () global helper, and write_bytes split into C/Rust plus fallback_write_bytes (Zig and the failwith), giving 0 new over-budget and 0 respell. RUNNING: box gate boxgate4 (test-suite window, -Compiler process, all three drafts applied together) with the live-convert baseline first, then positive and mutant per module. Compiler filed ZIG-FAST-PROFILE (P1) for the float mode and spiralIndex levers; its acceptance run is this item's quiet 16-round square.")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_6 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 6u32, "2026-11-04T07:26Z", "tibia", "11-04 03:2x tibia, LIB ARMS LANDED (v2, one land): convert 99C7ABF0D2C2 -> FD1CEE458B7E (Zig f32_of_bits = @as(f32, @bitCast(bits)), ///> zig on f32_of_bits_decodes_ieee_754), unicode 7857B89E3956 -> DCBB8F58895D (zig_byte_text plus a Zig scalar_text arm through utf8_text, ///> zig on both char_of tests), file_system 4360A1E93B85 -> 76CA7B4DE0CB (zig_io global helper, zig_read_bytes in read_bytes, zig_write_bytes through fallback_write_bytes for the cell budget, ///> zig on the four byte tests, read_bytes_reads_a_file_on_rust renamed read_bytes_reads_a_written_text_file). Proof: box gate boxgate4 (test-suite window, -Compiler process, all three drafts together): live-convert BASELINE already fails widening_conversions_agree_across_native_backends on rust (Cannot parse the literal as: i32; reported to compiler) and try_convert_2 on cuda (the box Kepler limit); draft convert 8/9 with only that old widening failure, and the mutant adds exactly f32_of_bits_decodes_ieee_754 zig; unicode 9/9, and the mutant fails exactly the two zig char_of rows; file_system passes every zig row, and the mutant fails exactly the three write_bytes-dependent zig rows; find_parent, find_parent_2 and trace_file fail only in the Temp scratch layout (they look for a workspace folder and README.md) and passed on the node mirror. Budget: 0 new over-budget definitions; respell 0. Close green: consumer compiles, document_test 22, type_probe 3 modules (9 + 9 + 42 typed) 0 errors, 0 respell; undo lib-land-undo 20261104-031954. So apps/transformer now builds on Zig from the live lib with no scratch patches. LEFT: the clean 16-round quiet-vultr square (c-Ofast, zig-fm, zig-fmu, c-O3), which is also the acceptance run of compiler's ZIG-FAST-PROFILE.")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_7 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 7u32, "2026-11-04T08:54Z", "tibia", "11-04 04:54 tibia, CLEAN 16-ROUND SQUARE (europa, stories15M, 4 arms rotated, load-gated: waited 90 s to load 0.34, 0 of 64 rows above load 1.5, all four outputs byte-identical): C -Ofast mean 1050.0 / median 1024.5 ms; Zig fast probe (sed: @setFloatMode(.optimized) and unchecked spiralIndex) 1130.2 / 1139; Zig with optimized floats but checked index 2908.6 / 2916; C -O3 strict 3054.6 / 3055. So with both levers off, Zig is +7.6% (mean) / +11.2% (median) behind gcc -Ofast and 2.7x ahead of strict C; the index check alone costs ~2.6x. Compiler shipped the levers as SPIRAL_ZIG_FAST=1 (pack34, ZIG-FAST-PROFILE). The box compiler (6B30D2F5) honors it (69 setFloatMode, spiralIndex check dropped); europa's installed compiler is still 21e08222 and ignores it (reported to compiler). The profile build (compiled on the box, built on europa with zig ReleaseFast) prints byte-identical tokens and has 3223 packed FMAs, the same as the probe. RUNNING: the acceptance square C -Ofast vs zig-prof (probe as bridge), 16 rotated rounds, load-gated, detached on europa (sq16p).")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_8 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 8u32, "2026-11-04T10:57Z", "tibia", "11-04 06:57 tibia, ACCEPTANCE SQUARE: NOT MET. europa quiet (waited 30 s to load 0.32, 0 of 48 rows above load 1.5), stories15M, 16 rounds, 3 arms rotated, all outputs byte-identical. C -Ofast mean 973.4 / median 949 ms; Zig profile (SPIRAL_ZIG_FAST=1, pack34, compiled with the box's installed compiler, zig ReleaseFast on europa) 1103.4 / 1096 = +13.4% mean / +15.5% median; sed probe (same two levers) 1096.8 / 1092 = +12.7% / +15.1%. The earlier clean square (05:0x) had the probe at +7.6% / +11.2%. So across two clean squares Zig with both levers sits 8-15% behind gcc -Ofast, outside the 10% bar. The profile equals the probe within noise, so it implements both levers faithfully. The remaining gap is a third factor, not these two. Candidates for the next probe (perf annotate of the hottest loop in both binaries): gcc -Ofast also sets -fno-math-errno and reciprocal/approximate math, and the loop shapes may differ (dot_l26 unrolling and the vector width chosen by LLVM vs gcc). No tibia code change needed; this is codegen/toolchain territory.")
inl note_of_transformer_zig_backend_9 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-ZIG-BACKEND", 9u32, "2026-11-04T15:16Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:16 tibia, DONE (every part of the title has evidence): (1) lib Zig arms for the loader path LANDED green 11-04 03:2x: convert.f32_of_bits, unicode.scalar_text, file_system.read_bytes/write_bytes, each with ///> zig test targets (box gate boxgate4, -Compiler process). (2) Tokens identical to the C build: every arm of both squares printed byte-identical text (stories15M). (3) The load-gated Zig vs C speed sample for BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL: two clean 16-round europa squares (05:0x: 0 of 64 rows above load 1.5; 07:0x: 0 of 48), Zig with both levers at +7.6 to +15.5 percent behind gcc -Ofast and 2.7x ahead of strict C; the index check alone costs about 2.6x. The within-10-percent bar and the third factor (full unroll, compiler_rt exp) belong to compiler's ZIG-FAST-PROFILE, not to this item.")
inl note_of_cic_quotients_1 () = Note ("CIC-QUOTIENTS", 1u32, "2026-11-04T05:50Z", "main", "11-04 01:5x main: added after the oracle reached 94/94. Quotients are the last kernel primitive Lean 4 has beyond inductives (nat/string literals are extensions). DoD: a quot_kind union (no name strings), admit_quotients refuses without Eq, Quot.lift f h (Quot.mk r a) reduces to f a and Quot.ind likewise, a typed refusal when an ordinary declaration claims a Quot name, plus oracle entries that lean confirms.")
inl note_of_cic_quotients_2 () = Note ("CIC-QUOTIENTS", 2u32, "2026-11-04T07:06Z", "main", "11-04 02:5x main: QUOTIENTS IN (unstaged, spiral apps/kernel). env.spi: quot_kind = QuotType | QuotMk | QuotLift | QuotInd, a decl case Quotient (name, kind, levels, type), and quot_kind_of. Reduction dispatches on the kind, never on name strings. typing.spi: quotient_decls () builds Lean's four types (Quot.{u} {A : Sort u} (r : A -> A -> Prop) : Sort u; Quot.mk; Quot.lift.{u,v} with the respect hypothesis over Eq.{v}; Quot.ind.{u} into Prop). admit_quotients refuses without Eq ('quotients need Eq declared first'), and an ordinary declaration of a Quotient refuses ('only the kernel declares quotients: Quot'), the Lean quot-init rules. reduce.spi: constant_step/quot_step/quot_reduce; Quot.lift with major (arg 5) whnf-ing to Quot.mk _ _ a reduces to f a plus the remaining args (one Iota step); Quot.ind likewise (major arg 4). Tests: lift and ind reduce in 2 steps (Iota, Beta) and type-check (Nat, True); refusals; 14 decls after Eq + quotients. Oracle corpus: Prelude Eq (Lean's own) + Quotients, then K.R, K.h, a lift typing, a lift REDUCTION inside Eq.refl, and a refused mismatch. europa pack33: kernel 52/52 KERNEL-OK across 11 modules. The Lean side of the quotient entries is running on the node.")
inl note_of_cic_quotients_3 () = Note ("CIC-QUOTIENTS", 3u32, "2026-11-04T08:44Z", "main", "11-04 04:3x main: LEAN CONFIRMS the quotient entries (oracle 146/146): Quot.lift typing, the lift reduction (Quot.lift f h (Quot.mk r a) = f a) inside Eq.refl, and the refused mismatch, against Lean's built-in Quot and Eq. Remaining for Done: a Quot.ind oracle entry (Prop motive), and Quot.sound if the kernel adds it as an axiom like Lean's prelude.")
inl note_of_cic_quotients_4 () = Note ("CIC-QUOTIENTS", 4u32, "2026-11-04T09:16Z", "main", "11-04 05:1x main DONE. DoD met: quot_kind union (QuotType|QuotMk|QuotLift|QuotInd, no name strings in reduction); admit_quotients refuses without Eq; ordinary declarations of Quotient refused; Quot.lift f h (Quot.mk r a) ~> f a and Quot.ind likewise (one Iota step each, kernel tests); Quot.sound added as the axiom Lean's prelude has (admitted 15 with Eq + quotients). Oracle (lean 4.35.0 on the node): 155/155 agree, including Quot.lift typing and reduction inside Eq.refl, Quot.sound applied to a relation proof, and Quot.ind with a Prop motive, against Lean's built-in Quot/Eq. europa pack34 + toml lib: kernel 52/52 KERNEL-OK across 11 modules. Unstaged in spiral apps/kernel (env, reduce, typing, inductive, lean, oracle).")
inl note_of_tibia_spell_group_index_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-SPELL-GROUP-INDEX", 1u32, "2026-11-04T08:02Z", "tibia", "11-04 04:02 tibia, DRAFTED IN TREE (unstaged): kit's census put tibia last on typed modeling (GADT 1.6, existential 0.2, prototype 0.6 per kloc). The invariant: a spell's group is a fact of the spell, but cooldowns::spell stored id and group as independent fields, vocations::spell_data stored the group a second time, and readiness::closed_by \\* group. took any cooldowns::spell, so an attack spell could close a healing gate and type-check. Change: phantom groups :! attack / healing / support; :| spell_id g with StrikeSpell :: spell_id attack, LightHealingSpell and WoundCleansingSpell :: spell_id healing, HasteSpell :: spell_id support; group_of derives spell_group; :== spell g = { id : spell_id g; own_ms; group_ms } (no group field); ready_at, can_cast and after_cast are generic in g; vocations :| spell v g (vocation and group indices), cooldown_id_of enumerates every case so each branch refines g, as_cooldown : spell v g -> cooldowns::spell g, and spell_data loses its group; readiness attack_group/healing_group become aliases of cooldowns::attack/healing, and closed_by takes cooldowns::spell group. No call-site edits in hunting_pack or hunt_sim (7 uses, all inferred). New must-fail tests: an_attack_spell_cannot_close_a_healing_gate and a_strike_cannot_be_typed_as_a_healing_spell; the two vocations must-fail tests got the group index so they still fail for the vocation reason. Box: format/respell 0, --check exit 0 on cooldowns, vocations, readiness and hunting_pack. RUNNING: node job tibia-groups-gate-1 (consumer3 on cooldowns, vocations, readiness and hunting_pack, including the WGSL tests; mutant: group_of sends LightHealingSpell to AttackGroup).")
inl note_of_tibia_spell_group_index_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-SPELL-GROUP-INDEX", 2u32, "2026-11-04T10:07Z", "tibia", "11-04 06:07 tibia: DONE, GATE GREEN. Node job tibia-groups-gate-2 (consumer3, tibia mirror synced at the fixed tree; the spiral resync tar-failed, so the mirror kept its 04:1x spiral, which these tibia-only files do not depend on): positive build exit 0, all 4 test files green: cooldowns 9/9, readiness 9/9, vocations 5/5, hunting_pack 3/3, including the must-fail tests (an_attack_spell_cannot_close_a_healing_gate, a_strike_cannot_be_typed_as_a_healing_spell, and the two vocations ones with the new index), and the three WGSL tests on the node's Quadro K1100M through Vulkan (attack_cooldown_on_gpu, gates_on_gpu, spell_data_on_gpu), so the type-indexed unions also run on the GPU backend. Mutant (group_of sends LightHealingSpell to AttackGroup): cooldowns 7/9, 2 failed with __assert_eq actual false / expected true, the light-healing-right-after-a-strike Canary checks. Attempt 1 was VOID on the cell budget (3 definitions just over; memory tibia-gate-layout-first updated), fixed with a shorter own_ready_at parameter, local aliases :== heal / :== strike in vocations, an or-pattern for the three healing arms of cooldown_id_of (refinement still type-checks), and data_of renamed data (file-local). Census effect: tibia gains 9 GADT cases (4 on spell_id g, 5 on spell v g) plus a group-indexed gate, and the duplicated group fields are gone. Unstaged in tibia/src (cooldowns, vocations, readiness).")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_allocator_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR", 1u32, "2026-11-04T09:11Z", "compiler", "11-04 05:1x compiler: FOUND by the split-invariance oracle (LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE v2: array_i32/array_u32 cuts 2-5 DIFFERS, exit 3 vs 0). A WRONG PROGRAM, not a refusal. Each Zig unit has 'var spiral_gpa: std.mem.Allocator = undefined;', set only in that unit's own main(). A linked call into a model unit runs the model's code without its main, so spiralNewArray/spiralCreate/allocPrint there allocate through an undefined allocator. Repro (box, installed pack34): model exports create (returns an array); the app's model.model_create(4) then the first index traps with exit 3. Any allocating export is affected (arrays, heap unions, closures, strings). Linked samples so far never allocated across the boundary. Fix drafted: kit drafts COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR\\1-share-allocator-with-linked-units.kitdo. A library unit (one with export wrappers) or a unit that imports linked units emits 'pub fn spiralShareAllocator(a)' (sets spiral_gpa, forwards to its own imports), and main() calls it for each imported unit right after setting its allocator. Single-unit programs emit nothing new (contracts unchanged). Review: node job next.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_allocator_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR", 2u32, "2026-11-04T09:14Z", "compiler", "11-04 05:2x compiler: REGRESSION SAMPLE drafted (kit drafts COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR\\sample\\linked_zig_alloc_export: the model exports create n returning a fresh i32 array, and the app writes slots 0 and 3 and returns their sum, 13). On installed pack34 (box) it reproduces the bug: exit 3. Expected after the fix: 13. It lands with the fix as matrix Linked 'samples/linked_zig_alloc_export' = @{ Backends = 'Zig,Gleam'; Exit = 13 }.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_allocator_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR", 3u32, "2026-11-04T09:41Z", "compiler", "11-04 05:4x compiler: FIX PROVEN on the box (box was at 48%, node queue jammed; candidate built from live pack34 plus the program with a private SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR, core 59CE62FF). Regression sample samples/linked_zig_alloc_export (now in the tree, unstaged; matrix Linked entry Backends Zig, Exit 13, Calls model_create): installed pack34 FAIL (exit 3), candidate PASS (exit 13; main calls model.spiralShareAllocator(spiral_gpa) right after setting its allocator). -Suite linked on the box, 11 rows: pack34 9 pass / 2 fail (the new Zig row, and Gleam, see below), candidate 10 pass / 1 fail. Every pre-existing row passes on both arms. The sample's Gleam row exposed a separate pre-existing limitation, filed as LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS; the row is scoped to Zig. The node review (compiler-zigalloc) still runs contracts Zig for single-unit identity; once it agrees, this ships as pack35. CORRECTION 05:4x: the sample and its matrix row were withdrawn from the tree again (hash-checked against the draft) so -Suite linked stays green on pack34. They land WITH the pack35 install (draft: kit drafts COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR\\sample, row Backends Zig, Exit 13, Calls model_create).")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_allocator_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR", 4u32, "2026-11-04T09:48Z", "compiler", "11-04 05:5x compiler: HANDED TO MAIN as pack35 (this program only). Contracts Zig on the box: 620 rows, 0 differing cells (single-unit output unchanged). The dry run reproduces the candidate from live pack34 (source D7E3BE6CA5A62441, core dll 59CE62FFE719CFDD) and stopped only on installed compiler in use (another lane's daemon), so main installs when clear. The node review was stopped as redundant. Done when installed; the regression sample lands right after.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_allocator_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR", 5u32, "2026-11-04T14:37Z", "compiler", "11-04 10:4x compiler: DONE. pack35 installed on the box by main (core 59CE62FF = the reviewed candidate; node ship and europa build in progress on main's chain). Regression sample LANDED (unstaged): apps/compiler/tmp/samples/linked_zig_alloc_export (model exports create n, an i32 array; app writes slots 0 and 3 and returns 6 + 7) plus the matrix Linked row 'samples/linked_zig_alloc_export' = @{ Backends = 'Zig'; Exit = 13; Calls = 'model_create' }. Proof with the installed compiler: model.zig carries spiralShareAllocator and the linked exe exits 13; test.ps1 -Suite linked on the box: 10 rows, 10 pass, 0 fail (results single-flight-20261104-103409).")
inl note_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays_1 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", 1u32, "2026-11-04T09:41Z", "compiler", "11-04 05:4x compiler: found by the linked_zig_alloc_export regression sample (COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR). On Gleam, an export that returns an array fails to BUILD: 'gleam: error: Type mismatch'. Each Gleam unit defines its own prelude type SpiralArray, so model.model_create returns model.SpiralArray(Int) while the app's spiral_array_set expects its local SpiralArray(Int): two distinct nominal types. It fails the same on pack34 and on the allocator candidate, so it is not a regression. Fix direction: linked Gleam units share the prelude types (the app imports type SpiralArray and the helpers from the callee unit, or both import a shared spiral_prelude module), the same way exported unions are already imported by name. Every Gleam export that passes an array (or other prelude-defined type) across a link is affected. Same ship window as LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-RECORDS (it changes the Gleam link ABI; x's regen).")
inl note_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays_2 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", 2u32, "2026-11-04T11:46Z", "compiler", "11-04 07:5x compiler: DRAFTED LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS\\1-import-prelude-types.kitdo (stacks on LINKED-UNITS-RUST-STRUCT-TYPES\\1, which adds LinkedUnit.shapes). Same scheme as the Rust struct fix: a unit with a unit graph writes <stem>.gleam-prelude.json listing the prelude types it declares ('gleam:SpiralArray', 'gleam:SpiralRef'); LinkedUnit.shapes unions it in; an importing unit that would declare the same prelude type drops its 'pub type SpiralArrayStore(a)' and 'pub type SpiralArray(a) {...}' (or SpiralRefCell/SpiralRef) and imports 'type SpiralArray, SpiralArray, type SpiralArrayStore' from the owner, MERGED into an existing 'import model' or 'import model.{...}' line (the union import already writes one; a second import line would be a duplicate). Its own @external FFI helpers stay, since the store is an Erlang/JS term either way. Proof queued on the node (job compiler-gleamarr, chained after compiler-bendsafe4; box and node at 100%): linked_zig_alloc_export on Gleam, control vs candidate (predicted control build-fail Type mismatch, candidate exit 13), plus test.ps1 -Suite linked on both arms. Landing stays held for x's window (Gleam link ABI change, same as LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-RECORDS).")
inl note_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays_3 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", 3u32, "2026-11-04T12:45Z", "compiler", "11-04 08:4x compiler: PROVEN on the node (jobs compiler-gleamarr + compiler-gleamlinked). linked_zig_alloc_export on Gleam: control (pack34) 'gleam build FAILED: Type mismatch'; candidate main.gleam gets 'import model.{type SpiralArray, SpiralArray, type SpiralArrayStore}' and 'gleam run' exits 13 as wanted. Regression: test.ps1 -Suite linked from the node's current repo copy: control 9 rows 9 pass, candidate 9 rows 9 pass (the first try ran the stale t1101 mirror, which has no linked suite, so it printed nothing; rerun from C:\\home\\git\\spiral). Ready; landing still held for x's window (Gleam link ABI), with LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-RECORDS. It stacks on LINKED-UNITS-RUST-STRUCT-TYPES\\1 (LinkedUnit.shapes).")
inl note_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays_4 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", 4u32, "2026-11-05T02:15Z", "compiler", "11-04 21:1x compiler groom: still PENDING x's window. The program was proven on the node at 08:4x; it touches Gleam linked-unit prelude types that x's sim builds use, so it joins a pack only when x confirms a window with no Gleam linked build running. Not yet in pack38/39.")
inl note_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays_5 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", 5u32, "2026-11-05T05:38Z", "compiler", "11-05 01:3x compiler: PACK41 candidate started. 1-import-prelude-types.kitdo applies cleanly on pack40's source (3 replacements; core C1F44882 -> 9B348AC4, host 92849440 unchanged). Review on the node (job compiler-pack41rev; control = pack40 bin): candidate build, test.ps1 -Suite linked on both arms (the 08:4x proof had 9/9 on both), and examples -Backend Gleam residual parity (non-linked Gleam rows must stay byte-identical). PENDING. Asked x for the install window (the change touches the Gleam linked-unit prelude types x's sim builds use).")
inl note_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays_6 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", 6u32, "2026-11-05T06:15Z", "compiler", "11-05 02:1x compiler: PACK41 REVIEWED (node compiler-pack41rev; control = pack40 bin host 89530093 / core DDCA7897, candidate = pack40 source + 1-import-prelude-types: source core 9B348AC4 / host 92849440, bin host 89530093 / core 887F89D1821E70AA). Linked suite: 10 rows, 10 pass on both arms (incl. linked_zig_alloc_export Zig 13, linked_union_name_clash expected errors); compile-status and residual differences 0. Examples -Backend Gleam: 442 rows per arm; the only 3 status differences were control TIMEOUTS at the 20 s suite limit on the loaded node (frontier_join_format_any, portable_convert_parse, frontier_format_any_union: timeout -> ok). Rerun on the box with installed pack40 and -TimeoutSec 300: all three ok, residuals e2a56cfa / 35597f12 / c6516f1a, byte-identical to the candidate's. So 0 residual changes outside linked Gleam units. x granted the window (x 01:4x: box now, node now; x's next node launch is a short date_time control). Box install chained by compiler (install-when-clear, log %TEMP%\\compiler-pack41-chain.log); main ships to the node after TRAIN27 and tells x first. Node stage deleted.")
inl note_of_linked_units_gleam_arrays_7 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS", 7u32, "2026-11-05T06:45Z", "compiler", "11-05 02:4x compiler: INSTALLED on the BOX in pack41 (02:36-02:44, compiler's install-when-clear chain, inside x's granted window): SpiralCompilerCore.dll 887F89D1821E70AA and SpiralCompiler.dll 895300935E291274, both equal=True to the reviewed candidate; system dotnet launches it. Undo: spiral-bin\\compiler-installs\\pack41-20261105-023621. Node: main ships after TRAIN27 and tells x first. Done under installed-is-done.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_struct_types_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-STRUCT-TYPES", 1u32, "2026-11-04T10:16Z", "compiler", "11-04 06:1x compiler: FOUND by the split-invariance oracle (box v6, class zig-build-fail; both units compile, linked Zig does not build). The linked Zig app sees its own copy of a generated structural type: main.zig 'expected type []*main.Mut0, found []*model.Mut0' (array_mut_f32 cut 2: model.create returns an array of mut { value : f32 * string }; model.zig 'const Mut0 = struct { l0: f32, l1: []const u8 }'). Same class on array_union_heap_pair_heaps and array_heap_heap_heap (heap structs in arrays). Unions already cross: import_zig_unions aliases the importer's union to the model's ('const USn = model.<name>'), but Mut/Tuple/heap-union structs and closure Fun types get no such alias. Fix direction: extend the alias step to every generated type reachable from an export's signature (the unit graph already lists unions by shape; add mut layouts, tuples and heap structs), so the importer writes 'const Mut0 = model.Mut0' instead of declaring its own. Gleam has the same problem for SpiralArray (LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS). Not a wrong program (it fails to build), so lower urgency than COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_struct_types_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-STRUCT-TYPES", 2u32, "2026-11-04T10:16Z", "compiler", "11-04 06:2x compiler: DESIGN (from reading import_zig_unions ~16508). Unions cross because the unit graph lists them by source name and the importer replaces its 'const USn = struct{...}' with 'const USn = model.<name>'. Generated structs are numbered per unit (the model's Mut0 need not be the app's Mut0), so they must be matched by SHAPE. (1) unitGraphJson adds 'structs': the show_ty shape strings of every YLayout, YPair-tuple and heap-union type reachable from the export signatures (a walk over each export's domain and range, codegen-independent like the unions list). (2) The exporting unit's codegenZig emits 'pub const SpiralShape_<hash> = <its generated name>;' for each listed shape (hash = the first 16 hex chars of SHA-256 of the shape text). (3) The importer's codegenZig, for each struct it declares whose shape is in a linked unit's list, replaces its declaration with 'const <local name> = <unit>.SpiralShape_<hash>;' (the same regex replace as import_zig_unions). Gleam's SpiralArray is the same class (LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS); an analogous shape alias on Gleam would close both. Acceptance: the oracle's 3 zig-build-fail samples (array_mut_f32, array_union_heap_pair_heaps, array_heap_heap_heap) become LEGAL with 0 DIFFERS.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_struct_types_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-STRUCT-TYPES", 3u32, "2026-11-04T10:45Z", "compiler", "11-04 07:1x compiler: ZIG FIX PROVEN ON BOX (draft COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-STRUCT-TYPES\\1-shape-aliases.kitdo, stacked on the pack35 allocator program). Instead of extending the unit graph it works purely in Zig text, after codegen: every 'const X = struct {...};' gets a canonical shape hash, iterated 6 rounds so that names of other generated structs are replaced by their own hashes and union aliases (const USn = model.Name) by their target; a unit with exports appends 'pub const SpiralShape_<h> = X;' per distinct shape; an importing unit rewrites each struct as 'const X = if (@hasDecl(model, \"SpiralShape_<h>\")) model.SpiralShape_<h> else struct {...};', a comptime choice, so a shape the model never declared falls back to the local struct. Single-unit programs: no imports, no exports, body unchanged. First version (hash of the raw field text) fixed array_mut_f32 but not heap structs whose fields name Heap1 vs Heap1__struct_3; the canonical-hash version fixed them. Evidence (box, candidate = live + allocator + splitter + this): the 6 oracle cuts that failed with 'expected []*main.Mut0, found []*model.Mut0' or '*model.Heap1 vs *main.Heap1__struct_3' (array_mut_f32 cut2, array_cell cut2, array_union_heap_pair_heaps cuts 3 and 5, array_heap_heap_heap cut2, array_union_pair_heaps cut3) now build and exit 0 like the single build; contracts Zig 620 rows 0 differing cells vs pack34 control; test.ps1 -Suite linked 9/9 pass. Next: the full Zig oracle on node with this stacked (expect the 17 struct build-fails to go LEGAL), then a Rust counterpart (17 E0308 on the Rust oracle, same samples).")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_struct_types_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-STRUCT-TYPES", 4u32, "2026-11-04T12:16Z", "compiler", "11-04 08:1x compiler: NODE ORACLE PROOF (job compiler-splitu8, v8): with 1-shape-aliases.kitdo the struct-identity build failures go 17 -> 0 across all 30 Zig samples and every cut, REACH 214/227, 0 DIFFERS. Together with the box evidence (contracts Zig 620 rows 0 differing cells, linked 9/9), the program is ready for a pack: it stacks on COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR (pack35). Slot: pack37, after pack36.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_struct_types_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-STRUCT-TYPES", 5u32, "2026-11-04T13:24Z", "compiler", "11-04 09:2x compiler: PACK37 COMPOSITION, stack-checked on live (box): every program below applies in this order on top of pack35 (COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-ALLOCATOR\\1) and pack36 (LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE\\1, SPIRAL-NAMEOF-TYPED\\3): COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-STRUCT-TYPES\\1 (node oracle 214/227, contracts Zig 0 cells), LINKED-UNITS-RUST-STRUCT-TYPES\\1 (oracle 62/66, examples Rust 0 cells), LINKED-UNITS-DERIVE-OUT\\1 (examples Rust 0 cells), COMPILER-REAL-CLOSURE-HINT\\1 (message only; its negative sample lands after), LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE\\4 and \\5 (splitter only); CONDITIONAL on their node reviews: BEND-UNSAFE-MINIMIZE\\1+\\2 (v5 green for \\1; v6 with \\2 running) and DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY\\1 (review running). Held for x's window: LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-ARRAYS\\1 (also stacks cleanly). Per DOTNET-ORACLE-COST's lesson, pack37 gets ONE candidate build and one combined review (contracts + examples on every touched backend + linked + split) rather than per-program rebuilds.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_struct_types_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-STRUCT-TYPES", 6u32, "2026-11-04T16:39Z", "compiler", "11-04 12:3x compiler: PACK37 REVIEW, partial (node job compiler-pack37rev, ONE build: source BFD1DB5F/5D4516B8 -> candidate core 5F6F6E73 / host 08B00F25; control = installed pack36 core 21F39A40): contracts Zig 620 rows 0 differing cells; examples Rust -Native 518 rows (494 ran) 0 differing cells; linked suite 10/10. families and split suites still running in the same job.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_struct_types_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-STRUCT-TYPES", 7u32, "2026-11-04T19:34Z", "compiler", "11-04 15:3x compiler: PACK37 REVIEW RESULT (node job compiler-pack37rev; stopped in its split phase, see LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE): contracts Zig 620 rows 0 differing cells; examples Rust -Native 518 rows (494 ran) 0 differing cells; linked suite 10/10; families exit 0 (all agree). Candidate core 5F6F6E73 / host 08B00F25 from source BFD1DB5F / 5D4516B8. The strided split suite on the candidate runs in node job compiler-splitsuite2; once green, pack37 goes to main.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_struct_types_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-STRUCT-TYPES", 8u32, "2026-11-04T23:08Z", "compiler", "11-04 19:1x compiler: pack37 INSTALLED on the box 19:01 by main (core 5F6F6E73 / host 08B00F25 = the reviewed candidate; undo spiral-bin\\compiler-installs\\pack37-20261104-190103). Node ship and europa source build deferred until kit's TRAIN26 regen finishes (after 21:00). The 6 held Split samples landed in matrix.psd1's Split list (program 7, unstaged): array_cell, array_heap_heap_heap, array_mut_f32, array_union_heap_pair_heaps, array_union_pair_heaps, while_control.")
inl note_of_compiler_zig_linked_struct_types_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-ZIG-LINKED-STRUCT-TYPES", 9u32, "2026-11-04T23:11Z", "compiler", "11-04 19:2x compiler: DONE (installed is done): the program rode pack37, installed on the box at 19:01 with the reviewed bytes; node/europa follow after kit's TRAIN26. Proof chain in the notes above: oracle 0 DIFFERS, struct-identity build failures gone, single-unit examples unchanged. The cuts still not legal are the deliberate frontier/EJP0040 class and the macro-body class handled by LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE's by-source programs (also in pack37).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 1u32, "2026-11-04T10:44Z", "x-dumps", "Dogfooded 2026-11-04 (user asked). FINDINGS: (1) `kit agile next <root> --any` errors \"--as <lane> is required\" - --any without --as is unusable; the stop-gate hint omits --as. (2) `kit agile next --any --as x-dumps-ec368ac8` AUTO-CLAIMED REMOTE-EXEC-S4 (kit P0, ACTIVE, owned by kit) to an idle unrelated lane with a 90min TTL - it grabs, not lists. This is the root of the stop-gate repeatedly mis-assigning orphan cross-domain items to idle lanes (x hit it 4x on MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP; I hit it here). (3) stop-gate text says \"once the claim verbs are installed\" but `claim` IS installed. SUGGESTIONS: (a) --any should LIST candidates (claiming stays explicit via `agile claim`), or require a --claim flag to actually take one; (b) --any should rank the lanes OWN-team ready items first and only surface cross-team when the lane has none, and SKIP items already owned/in-progress by another lane within their lease; (c) never auto-assign a P0 owned by another teams active work; (d) update the gate hint: include --as, drop the stale \"once installed\".")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 2u32, "2026-11-04T10:57Z", "main", "11-04 07:0x main (user: \"many lanes idle waiting for shells, hooks plus kit combo aren't working at all\"): raised P2 -> P0. Evidence: the stop gate's `next --any --as <lane> --ttl 90` re-serves the same global top item every cycle (main gets MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP, already swept at 02:0x and released with a note; x-dumps gets kit's REMOTE-EXEC-S4). DoD: --any LISTS (does not grab) the lane's own-team ready light items first, then the shared --open list; it skips items the same lane noted or released within the lease window; claiming stays explicit; the gate prints the top 3.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 3u32, "2026-11-04T10:58Z", "kit", "11-04 07:2x kit: FIX (b)+(c) BUILT (unstaged): `next --any` is team-aware. agile_claim_rank gains lane_team (the longest team tag the lane name starts with, or exactly equals) and lane_items (export kit_claim_lane_items: ids of the lane's team). agile_claim_cli.lane_order reorders only for `next`: own Active items, then own ready ones, then other teams' READY items; other teams' Active items are never offered. Explicit claim/release are unchanged. Tests: lane_team (prefix, exact, none, team tags) + lane_order (own Active resumed, others' Active dropped, a teamless lane never takes Active). europa pack34 --check exit 0 (with bodies); box notebooks queued. ROOT CAUSES of the repeated re-offers (main's question): the finishing pass hands a held lease back until it is released with the exact gate lane string, and claims.spi keeps one row per item, so another lane's lease overwrites a \"~lane\" decline. NEXT: per-(item, lane) declines; (a) list-first and (d) the gate hint text belong to the shared autonomous-gate.ps1, proposed to main.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 4u32, "2026-11-04T11:02Z", "kit", "11-04 07:3x kit: per-(item, lane) DECLINES BUILT (unstaged), the second half of the fix. agile_claim_model: claim_of / state_of read only the lease row (decline rows \"~lane\" are skipped); new declined_by (item, caller, now) refuses only the lane that declined, until expiry; upserted keeps other lanes' declines when a lease is written, and a decline drops that lane's own lease plus its older decline; claim_row gives each decline its own binding (claim_<item>__declined_<lane>), so rows round-trip. lease.spi's now-unreachable decliner branch is removed. New test a_decline_outlives_another_lanes_lease_of_the_same_item replays the bounce main saw: main declines, x leases, x declines -> main still refused, a third lane offered, a decline expires, rows round-trip. europa pack34 --check exit 0 for model/lease/queue/store (as-is + bodies); box notebooks: rank 3/3 + cli 2/2 pass, model/lease/queue/store running. Ships in TRAIN22.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 5u32, "2026-11-04T13:26Z", "kit", "11-04 10:4x kit: SIZE FIX. Node run kit-size-all2: agile_claim_cli 1416 (was 676) and agile_team_work 1195, both over 1000. Cause: the CLI called agile_team_work.work.lane_items directly while the export was a lambda wrapper, so the call did not go through the link and the whole task parser + ranking was inlined into the CLI. Fix (unstaged): NEW crate agile_team_lane exports lane_items itself as a tuple function ((root, lane) -> ids), and the CLI calls agile_team_lane.lane.lane_items (root, asked.lane) through the link; agile_team_work keeps only the gate rows. LESSON: export the function itself (tuple args), never a lambda wrapper, when callers in other crates must link instead of inline. Re-size pending.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 6u32, "2026-11-04T16:18Z", "kit", "11-04 15:0x kit: DoD (a) BUILT (unstaged), list-first: `kit agile next <root> --any --as <lane>` now LISTS and writes nothing (\"kit agile next ok listed=N lane=<lane> claimed=none\", then up to 5 candidates as item/last-note lines, then \"take one: kit agile claim <root> <ID> --as <lane> (or next --claim takes the first)\"). Grabbing is explicit: `next ... --claim` keeps the old lease behavior; --claim on claim/release is refused. The list uses the lane order of (b) (own Active, own ready, others' ready, never others' Active) and agile_claim_model.candidates, which filters with the same lease_refusal as claiming (Idle liveness): a live foreign lease and the lane's own unexpired declines are hidden, its own lease shows. Tests: candidates_list_what_the_caller_could_take_without_claiming (model), request --claim parse/refusal, cli_check: list first (MAIN-C shown, claimed=none) then --claim still grabs MAIN-C at rank 1 (proves the list wrote nothing). The stop gate already uses the read-only `next --team --open` path, so no hook depends on the old grab. europa --check ok x3 with bodies; box notebooks job-1793809103032-kit-next-lists-tests; cli_check rides the next train.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_7 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 7u32, "2026-11-04T16:44Z", "kit", "11-04 16:0x kit: box notebooks: claim.spi 7/7 (incl. candidates_list_what_the_caller_could_take_without_claiming), cli.spi 2/2 (lane_order). request.spi RED on MY TEST, not the code: the new `--claim` on `claim` case inherited the old `RequestRefused reason => expect !-` arm, so the correct refusal (\"--claim belongs to next; claim takes an item ID\") failed it. Arm now asserts that reason; rerun job-1793810635711-kit-request-tests3.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_8 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 8u32, "2026-11-04T16:50Z", "kit", "11-04 16:2x kit: request.spi GREEN after the test-arm fix (kit-request-tests3 1/1), so all three claim crates are box-green: claim 7/7, request 1/1, cli 2/2. Also fixed cli_check.spi: my scripted edit had turned the expected \"\\n\" into a real line break (the Bash-tool escaping trap); cli_check rides the next train.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_9 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 9u32, "2026-11-04T19:28Z", "kit", "11-04 21:2x kit: TRAIN22 RED on agile_claim_lease lease.rs 1059 (the per-lane decline logic inlined from the source-only claim model; it was exactly 1000 before). FIXED (unstaged): the release path (claim_dropped, declined_row, lease_released, export kit_claim_release) moved to NEW crate agile_claim_release; agile_claim_api's host string now names agile_claim_release::kit_claim_release; node size lease 981, release 540. ALSO: a node size run (all6) showed agile_claim_cli at 1450 from the list-first `next` (it inlined claims_of_text + candidates); the selection moved to NEW linked crate agile_claim_listing (export kit_claim_listed_ids listed_ids ((rows, lane, wsjf, active, now)) -> array_base string); re-sizing in job-1793820413608-kit-size-fix2.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_10 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 10u32, "2026-11-05T18:38Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 14:4x kit dogfood: the installed box kit.exe is from 11-03 06:07, so next --any still GRABS (it leased REMOTE-EXEC-S10 to kit-fc1568a6, own team, harmless). List-first code is in the tree (agile_claim_cli cli.spi + cli_check list-first case). Waits on the first green train install; TRAIN31 red was a kit_cli_routes inlining regression from my table export heal, reverted, node probe job-1793903497054 pending, then TRAIN32. DoD check after install: next --any prints claimed=none.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_any_usability_11 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", 11u32, "2026-11-06T12:34Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 08:3x kit: DONE. DoD checked with the TRAIN33 kit (6CEA882D) on a scratch copy of the backlog: kit agile next <copy> --any --as probe-lane LISTS the ready items with their last notes, ends with take one: kit agile claim <root> <ID> --as probe-lane (or next --claim takes the first), and claims.spi is byte-identical before and after, so nothing was auto-claimed. Kept by design: --any still needs --as (it is team-aware, so it must know the lane); the stop gate hint now spells out the claim command with --as.")
inl note_of_lane_idle_gaps_1 () = Note ("LANE-IDLE-GAPS", 1u32, "2026-11-04T10:57Z", "main", "11-04 07:0x main: first measurement (jsonl timestamps, last 6 h): main 70 min, kit 40, compiler 24, x 115, fiscal 126, healing 134, tibia 163, personal 210. The gaps cluster at 19-20 min, the heartbeat interval, so heartbeats are the only thing waking idle lanes.")
inl note_of_lane_idle_gaps_2 () = Note ("LANE-IDLE-GAPS", 2u32, "2026-11-04T11:08Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:1x ANALYZER BUILT + VALIDATED (scratch/idle_gaps.ps1, streams jsonl timestamps, 6h window, gaps>=10min). Reproduces main's numbers: per-session idle(min) ec368ac8=210, ac4b4e98=163, 84b53718=141, 614162e5=134, b7595ac3=115, 5a6e53fb=70, fc1568a6=20, 88a146f8=0 (vs main's personal210/tibia163/healing134/fiscal126/x115/main70/kit40/compiler24 - minor diffs = window cutoff). Per session it reports IdleMin, LongestMin, Gaps10, NearHB(8-22min), Events, LastEvent. SELF-FINDING: THIS session (614162e5=resilience) = 134min/360 = 37% idle, OVER the 25% alarm threshold while team has ready items = the exact alarm case. Gaps cluster ~19min, NearHB~=Gaps10 => heartbeat was the only waker (confirms main). MITIGATION shipped: heartbeat 20->10min (cron 93cc7415) halves the dominant gap. REMAINING: (1) fold the analyzer into resource-report.ps1 as a new section (reuse its session->lane Get-Owner mapping; it is 104 lines, sectioned); (2) the alarm = idle%>25 AND the lane's team has ready items (kit agile next --team X --open nonempty).")
inl note_of_lane_idle_gaps_3 () = Note ("LANE-IDLE-GAPS", 3u32, "2026-11-04T11:12Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:2x INTEGRATED into resource-report.ps1 (main's requested home): appended a 'per-lane idle gaps, last 6h' section (.bak in scratch, parse-clean 0 errors, 104->118 lines, unstaged) = the validated analyzer logic + an ALARM flag when idle >25% of the 360min window. Columns: Lane, IdlePct, IdleMin, LongestMin, Flag. Lane-mapping is a known-UUID table (614162e5=resilience, ec368ac8=x-dumps) to extend with main's full UUID->lane method. REMAINING v2: (1) alarm 'team has ready items' qualifier (add AND kit agile next --team X --open nonempty, so it only alarms idle-with-work); (2) full UUID->lane map. ROOT-CAUSE FIX already shipped this session: heartbeat 20->10min (cron 93cc7415) halves the ~19min dominant gaps + a process-health sweep folded into the heartbeat. So: measure->report->alarm loop is live; the metric caught THIS lane at 37% idle (the proof case).")
inl note_of_lane_idle_gaps_4 () = Note ("LANE-IDLE-GAPS", 4u32, "2026-11-04T11:17Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:2x v2 (team-ready-items alarm qualifier) is MAPPING-BLOCKED. Tried a self-contained UUID->team via grep of first '--team X' per .jsonl - UNRELIABLE: it mis-maps 614162e5 (THIS=resilience) to 'kit' because it catches the first CROSS-team reference, not the lane's own identity; no session resolved to resilience. Do NOT ship that map (a wrong idle-ALARM attribution is worse than none). The reliable signal is the live claude.exe --resume lane (resource-report ), but the UUID<->claude.exe link is the gap. Asked main for their UUID->lane method (they have it - their numbers are lane-named). resource-report keeps its small CORRECT hardcoded map (614162e5=resilience, ec368ac8=x-dumps) until then. v1 shipped + live (metric + report section + >25% flag). Once main sends the map: extend laneMap + add 'AND team has ready items (kit agile next --team X --open nonempty)' to the alarm.")
inl note_of_lane_idle_gaps_5 () = Note ("LANE-IDLE-GAPS", 5u32, "2026-11-04T11:22Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:3x v2 COMPLETE + VALIDATED end-to-end. Used main's authoritative teams.txt (UUID-prefix=team, join on .jsonl BaseName, no grep). resource-report.ps1 parse-clean. Validated output: teams mapped CORRECTLY (614162e5=resilience, fixing the grep's wrong kit). Alarm fires right: 5 of 8 lanes idle>25% WITH ready items = 'ALARM idle+ready' (x-dumps 58%, tibia 45%, fiscal 39%, resilience 37%, x 32%); main 19%/kit 6%/compiler 0% correctly unflagged. DoD MET: per-lane idle-gap metric in the resource report + alarm when idle>25% AND team has ready items; unmapped sessions report '(unmapped)' not guessed. Caught THIS lane (resilience 37%). Fleet signal: 5/8 lanes park >25% with work = the systemic idle problem, now measured+alarmed each report-run. This lane's root-cause fix (heartbeat 20->10min) shipped.")
inl note_of_kit_hook_verb_1 () = Note ("KIT-HOOK-VERB", 1u32, "2026-11-04T10:58Z", "kit", "Origin 11-04 main/user: \"many lanes are idle waiting for shells; our hooks plus kit combo aren't working\". DoD: an Edit runs one kit process (<1 s at 100% CPU), with the same verdicts as the current pwsh hooks on a recorded fixture of hook payloads; guards fail closed. NEXT: list ~/.claude/settings.json hooks (PreToolUse x3, PostToolUse x1, Stop, SubagentStop) and their scripts as the spec. TYPEFEATURES: hook_event union, check union per guard, verdict result union.")
