inl note_of_kit_train_stale_pin_1 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-STALE-PIN", 1u32, "2026-11-06T22:17Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 18:2x kit: FOUND LIVE: TRAIN35 relaunched at 17:27 on pack57 but its node half pinned core 1524D5D5 (pack58c) because release-train.ps1 copies the compiler into .cache/release-train/<Name>/compiler only when that folder is missing, and the aborted 16:52 launch had pinned pack58c there. Two compilers in one verdict, so it was stopped and relaunched as TRAIN36 (pinned 8F009496 pack57, confirmed in its log). DoD: a fresh run whose pinned core differs from the live core re-pins and says so; a -Resume run with a differing pin refuses (Stop-Train) instead of mixing; proven by the next train log line. Draft (parse 0) at drafts/kit/TRAIN-STALE-PIN-1106/release-train.ps1; apply after TRAIN36 ends (its box half reads the tree copy when it starts).")
inl note_of_kit_train_stale_pin_2 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-STALE-PIN", 2u32, "2026-11-07T00:23Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 20:3x kit: APPLIED (unstaged) to scripts/release-train.ps1 (base matched the tree; the copy before is archived beside the draft; parse 0), safe now because TRAIN36 is red and its box half will not read the file. Deterministic proof: a harness runs the new pin block verbatim with temp live/pinned compiler folders: fresh launch with a stale pin -> re-pinned with a Say line; fresh launch with a matching pin -> kept; -Resume with a stale pin -> Stop-Train refuses. Live proof is the next train log (compiler pinned line equals the parity line). Progress 80.")
inl note_of_kit_train_stale_pin_3 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-STALE-PIN", 3u32, "2026-11-07T00:23Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "NEXT STEP: launch the next train and read its node-half compiler pinned line against the launch parity line; equal closes the item. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: not applicable, the change is pwsh launcher glue (no Spiral model); the pin state is a 3-way outcome (fresh, kept, refused) that a later kit-native train runner would model as a union.")
inl note_of_kit_train_stale_pin_4 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-STALE-PIN", 4u32, "2026-11-07T18:13Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit: TRAIN37 launched 14:12 on pack61 (box and node core E3F33F3766EE6EDA) is the live proof: its node-half compiler pinned line must equal the parity line (fresh train name, and the re-pin code is in release-train). NEXT STEP: read that line when the node half starts.")
inl note_of_kit_train_stale_pin_5 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-STALE-PIN", 5u32, "2026-11-07T18:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit: DONE, live proof. TRAIN37 (launched 14:12, fresh name, release-train with the re-pin code): launch parity core E3F33F3766EE6EDA on box and node, and the node half logged at 14:19:24 compiler pinned at .cache/release-train/TRAIN37/compiler (host 734CEF2EBF4E7DD4 core E3F33F3766EE6EDA), equal to the parity line. The 3-path harness proof (fresh stale pin re-pinned, matching pin kept, -Resume with a stale pin refused) stands from 11-06.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_wpdis_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-WPDIS", 1u32, "2026-11-06T23:34Z", "tibia", "11-06 19:3x tibia: from PR-33's RESULT. DoD: per-step normalization needs every hunt's prefix weight at each step bucket (TIBIA-IS-PER-DECISION-DESIGN (3)-(4)): a bounded per-hunt record of (bucket, log ratio) at bucket boundaries, built without sharing a mutable array across pack copies (copy-on-write or a fixed-size immutable record per bucket), the estimator in logprob with golden tests (equal policies = plain per-bucket sums; end-only rewards = self-normalized path IS), then a pre-registered rerun of PR-33's targets. NEXT: decide the bucket width from think timing (about one think per 2 s). TypeFeatures: buckets and rewards as records, not parallel arrays.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_wpdis_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-WPDIS", 2u32, "2026-11-06T23:41Z", "tibia", "11-06 19:4x tibia: ESTIMATOR in place (tibia src/logprob.spi B89D388232BA, unstaged): bucket_row { bucket; hunt : logged_hunt } and wpdis rows buckets = the sum over buckets of is_estimate's self-normalized mean on that bucket's rows, so each step is normalized by the prefix weights of every hunt at that step (a bucket with no hunt on path adds 0). Every hunt must contribute one row per bucket (reward 0 where nothing happened). Golden tests on c and rust: equal policies give the plain mean of hunt totals (30), one bucket reproduces is_estimate (200), a hunt that leaves the path in bucket 1 keeps its bucket-0 reward (70). Box: cell budget 0, --check passes. QUEUED: node job-1794008471034-tibia-wpdis-cells (logprob), with a waiter. NEXT (logging, still open): build each hunt's rows by RE-SIMULATING the seed to every bucket boundary (simulate camp t seed T_b), which is pure and needs no array shared across pack copies: reward of bucket b = potions at T_b minus potions at T_(b-1), ratio = target_log vs log_path at T_b; 10 buckets cost 10 sims per seed. Then a pre-registered rerun of PR-33's targets.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_wpdis_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-WPDIS", 3u32, "2026-11-06T23:42Z", "tibia", "11-06 19:4x tibia: NEXT, made precise before stopping for the day: the re-simulated bucket rows must stop at each seed's CLEAR, or they count potions drunk after the camp is cleared and stop matching PR-31/33's fresh potions-per-clear. clear_run returns elapsed ms while simulate takes ticks, so the first step is a pure ms-to-tick mapping (or a simulate variant that stops at all_killed), with a test that the last bucket's cumulative potions equal clear_run's tally on one seed; only then the rows, and a PR-34 pre-registration (WPDIS vs fresh at epsilon 90/50/0) before any run.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_wpdis_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-WPDIS", 4u32, "2026-11-06T23:57Z", "tibia", "11-06 19:5x tibia: ESTIMATOR PROVEN: node job-1794008471034-tibia-wpdis-cells, logprob 14/14 on c and rust (log read in full), incl. the 3 WPDIS goldens (equal policies = plain mean of totals 30; one bucket = self-normalized path IS 200; a hunt leaving the path keeps its earlier reward 70) on the first run. LOGGING CODE in place, not yet on the node (tibia src/hunting_pack.spi, unstaged): no ms-to-tick mapping needed after all, because clear_run's limit is in ticks and it stops at the clear, so snapshot run seed (b x k) is the state capped at the clear; bucket_ticks (6000 split evenly), logged_of, bucket_row_of (reward = difference of consecutive snapshots, ratio from the later tally), row_at, fill_rows, bucket_rows; test (c) bucket_rewards_add_up_to_the_cleared_hunt (wpdis over 4 buckets of one equal-target hunt = its cleared potions x 1000). Box: cell budget 0, --check passes, max line 120. NEXT: node Cells for hunting_pack, then a PR-34 pre-registration (WPDIS potions vs fresh at epsilon 90/50/0) before any run.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_wpdis_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-WPDIS", 5u32, "2026-11-07T00:11Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:1x tibia: Blocked on: node job-1794009481504-tibia-buckets-cells (hunting_pack AF5E2EDDD6D4: bucket_rewards_add_up_to_the_cleared_hunt), with a waiter. Resume when: it finishes; then pre-register PR-34 (WPDIS potions vs fresh, epsilon 90/50/0) before any run.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_wpdis_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-WPDIS", 6u32, "2026-11-07T00:14Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:1x tibia: bucket rows PROVEN: node job-1794009481504-tibia-buckets-cells, hunting_pack 15/15 on c (log read in full), incl. bucket_rewards_add_up_to_the_cleared_hunt. Code complete for the estimator and the rows; what remains is the measurement: PR-34 pre-registration (WPDIS potions vs fresh, epsilon 90/50/0) and its run. Resume: write PR-34 first.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_wpdis_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-WPDIS", 7u32, "2026-11-07T00:14Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:1x tibia: PR-34 pre-registered in tibia/docs/BENCHMARK.md above PR-33 (unstaged), before any WPDIS measurement: soft_troll 100 scored for targets 90/50/0, 10 buckets of 600 ticks, held-out 1001-1100; prediction 90 within 10 pct, 50 within 50 pct, 0 under 1 potion; decision rule written. NEXT: wpdis rows in is_softened (bucket_rows potions_milli over seed_run_of troll_spot (scored eps) 1001 100, logprob::wpdis rows 10 / 100 per hunt), --check, then the node run.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_wpdis_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-WPDIS", 8u32, "2026-11-07T00:14Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:1x tibia: CORRECTION to the previous note: logprob::wpdis rows 10 is already the per-hunt estimate (the sum over buckets of per-bucket self-normalized means), so it must NOT be divided by the hunt count; compare it directly with the fresh potions_milli mean.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_wpdis_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-WPDIS", 9u32, "2026-11-07T00:21Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:2x tibia: PR-34 program in place (tibia apps/predict/is_softened.spi 8E8B13136582, unstaged): wpdis_mean eps = logprob::wpdis (bucket_rows potions_milli (seed_run_of troll_spot (scored eps) 1001 100) 10) 10, printed as wpdis rows beside the PR-31/33 rows; --check passes, cell budget 0, max line 120. RUNNING: node job-1794010851240-tibia-pr34-run (tree 22B02C8F3504), with a waiter. Blocked on: that job. Resume when: it finishes; write PR-34's RESULT by its pre-registered decision.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_wpdis_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-WPDIS", 10u32, "2026-11-07T00:26Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:2x tibia: PR-34 RESULT written (BENCHMARK.md, unstaged). Node job-1794010851240-tibia-pr34-run (248 s, log read in full): WPDIS potions x 1000 vs fresh: eps 90 17,039 vs 17,760 (-4.0 pct, holds), eps 50 16,723 vs 9,860 (+69.6 pct, FAILS the 50 pct bar), eps 0 0 vs 0 (holds). Per-bucket normalization cut eps 50 from +165 (path) and +1,188 (per-decision) to +70. Gap: the program does not print per-bucket ess, which the decision rule asks for. DONE as code and measurement (estimator, rows, program, result); proof: job-1794008471034-tibia-wpdis-cells (logprob 14/14), job-1794009481504-tibia-buckets-cells (hunting_pack 15/15), the PR-34 log. Follow-up filed: TIBIA-IS-SHORT-HORIZON.")
inl note_of_link_split_corpus_implicit_1 () = Note ("LINK-SPLIT-CORPUS-IMPLICIT", 1u32, "2026-11-06T23:39Z", "compiler", "11-06 19:4x compiler: split from LINK-SPLIT-CORPUS. lib/spiral (spiral.toml has only packages + prelude) and kit/src (empty modules:) declare no module order, so the manifest-based corpus split can't cut them. DoD: the compiler reports a package's resolved module order (e.g. a --list-modules flag printing the order it typechecks them in), the split-corpus harness uses it when the manifest has no list, and lib/spiral plus kit/src run on the node with 0 fail. typefeatureset plan: none; a host flag plus harness glue. next step: find where proj_validate resolves implicit modules and expose that order.")
inl note_of_link_split_corpus_implicit_2 () = Note ("LINK-SPLIT-CORPUS-IMPLICIT", 2u32, "2026-11-06T23:43Z", "compiler", "11-06 19:5x compiler: no compiler change needed. The host already has '--resolve-manifest <dir>' (Program.fs ~1403, withDerivedModules), which prints the package's DERIVED module list in spiproj form: lib/spiral yields 160 lines ('array_prototypes-', 'backend-', ...; the trailing '-' is part of the entry, the file is the name without it). The split-corpus harness now calls it when the manifest lists no modules, and strips the '-' only for the file lookup. kit/src derives an empty list (its sources aren't modules of that package), so it is skipped with a named line. lib/spiral re-added to SplitCorpus and running on the box.")
inl note_of_link_split_corpus_implicit_3 () = Note ("LINK-SPLIT-CORPUS-IMPLICIT", 3u32, "2026-11-07T11:40Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: DONE in effect: lib/spiral runs through --resolve-manifest's derived order (157 module lines, glob entries like sm'_real* expanded) and passes on pack59d with the rest of the corpus (58 cuts, 0 fail). kit/src derives no modules and is skipped with a named line (its sources are not modules of that package).")
inl note_of_tibia_cfr_brain_trace_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-CFR-BRAIN-TRACE", 1u32, "2026-11-06T23:48Z", "tibia", "11-06 19:5x tibia: from PR-32's RESULT. Suspects, in order: (1) the brain never reaches learned_heals/learned_plan (an earlier dispatch arm or tactic field wins, e.g. the footing StepAwayLikeTheLiveProbe or the order ReachFirst deciding before the brain); (2) the drawn action is constant (a table row read at the wrong index, or arena_state_of clamping every think into one state); (3) engage needs strike_ready/reach so Melee never turns into a swing. DoD: a printed trace (a scratch predict program, not in the repo manifest) naming which suspect holds, a fix with a unit test, and PR-32 rerun as a pre-registered PR-34. NEXT: write the trace program.")
inl note_of_tibia_cfr_brain_trace_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-CFR-BRAIN-TRACE", 2u32, "2026-11-07T00:11Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:1x tibia: trace program written (tibia apps/predict/cfr_trace.spi, unstaged; scratch entry outside the manifest): one held-out seed (1001) of cfr_tactic 3 on lanes.spi's spot, printing every 2 s the abstract state (hp, attackers, ready), the drawn action and kills; --check passes, cell budget 0. The dispatch code read (target_turn -> after_heal_plan -> learned_plan -> cfr_plan) is wired as designed, so both readings in PR-32's RESULT stay open. Blocked on: node job-1794010075650-tibia-cfr-trace (predict.ps1 -Module cfr_trace), with a waiter. Resume when: it finishes; read the trace and name which suspect holds.")
inl note_of_tibia_cfr_brain_trace_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-CFR-BRAIN-TRACE", 3u32, "2026-11-07T00:13Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:1x tibia: DIAGNOSED (node job-1794010075650-tibia-cfr-trace, predict.ps1 -Module cfr_trace, 252 s, log read in full; DIAGNOSIS row under PR-32 in BENCHMARK.md). The brain's draws DO reach play (suspects 1 and 2 ruled out; dispatch and table indexing work). Suspect 3's neighbour holds: the abstract game makes Retreat dominant whenever an attacker is adjacent. From tick 20 the trace draws retreat at every state with attackers >= 1, even at hp 515 against one attacker; kills stay 0; hp falls while stepping away; death near tick 630; respawn at 515 / 0 attackers draws melee only while nothing is adjacent. Cause: Retreat costs 40 tempo, 0 damage, and REMOVES an attacker for good (attackers - 1), cheaper than one Melee step (52 damage, no progress until Low); on the live spot stepping away does not remove the cyclops. This also explains PR-30 = PR-32 to the digit (same always-retreat policy in both games). Proof: the trace log. Fix filed as TIBIA-CFR-RETREAT-MODEL.")
inl note_of_auto_export_2b_polymorphic_1 () = Note ("AUTO-EXPORT-2B-POLYMORPHIC", 1u32, "2026-11-06T23:58Z", "compiler", "11-06 19:5x compiler: FOUND by the split-corpus oracle on lib/spiral (6 of 7 cuts fail): AUTO-EXPORT-2's synthesize builds f__uncurried for ANY curried definition with annotated parameters, including implicitly polymorphic ones like sm' (++#?) (x : 'a): the synthesized wrapper is not generalized, so it fails 'Unification failure. Got: forall 'a. string -> 'a -> string, Expected: string -> 'a -> string. In the definition: ++#?__uncurried'. Only packages with auto-export: monomorphic are affected (today: the compiler samples only; main's regex trial was reverted), so nothing live breaks, but any real unit with a polymorphic curried definition would fail to type check. FIX: AUTO-EXPORT-2-CURRIED\\3-skip-polymorphic.kitdo (1 replacement, dry run ok on pack59's source): skip synthesis when a parameter or the result annotation contains a metavar, wildcard, forall, exists, typecase or term/macro type. Regression guard: samples/linked_rust_auto_export_modules gains first (a : 'x) (b : 'x) : 'x, used by the app (must compile and inline; exit stays 34). DoD: pack59b (pack59 + this) passes linked incl. that sample and the lib/spiral corpus cuts; ships with pack59 after kit's TRAIN36. typefeatureset plan: the rule is exactly the existing 'no forall' candidate rule, applied to implicit polymorphism too.")
inl note_of_auto_export_2b_polymorphic_2 () = Note ("AUTO-EXPORT-2B-POLYMORPHIC", 2u32, "2026-11-06T23:58Z", "compiler", "11-06 19:5x compiler: next step: pack59b's linked suite and the lib/spiral corpus rerun (running on the box).")
inl note_of_auto_export_2b_polymorphic_3 () = Note ("AUTO-EXPORT-2B-POLYMORPHIC", 3u32, "2026-11-07T00:17Z", "compiler", "11-06 20:2x compiler: pack59b proved half of it: lib's ++#? (its real shape is (b : _), a WILDCARD annotation) is skipped, and the sample guard now spells exactly that (first (a : i32) (b : _) : i32): installed pack57 fails its --check with the unification error, pack59b passes. The lib corpus then surfaced a SECOND synthesis bug: date_time's format (format : string) (date_time : date_time) : string names a parameter like the function, so the synthesized body 'format format date_time' applied the string ('Got: string, Expected: string -> date_time -> string. In the definition: format__uncurried'). The program now also renames the wrapper's parameters to fresh auto_export_arg<i> names (2 replacements, dry run ok). Candidate pack59c building, then the lib/spiral corpus cuts and the linked suite.")
inl note_of_auto_export_2b_polymorphic_4 () = Note ("AUTO-EXPORT-2B-POLYMORPHIC", 4u32, "2026-11-07T00:35Z", "compiler", "11-06 20:3x compiler: pack59c (core 3C07DA7F94347417 = pack57 + pack58b's 3 + AE3 + this program with both fixes): linked 18/18 (the 2-module sample with the (b : _) guard passes, exit 34), and the lib/spiral corpus went from 6 of 7 cuts failing to 1. The last one is a THIRD synthesis case: wasm's set_inner_html (html : string) (el : html_element) has NO result annotation, so its result is generalized ('a) and the wrapper fails ('Got forall 'a. string -> html_element -> 'a'). The program now also skips synthesis when the result is unannotated (same as AUTO-EXPORT-1's 'annotated' rule; dry run ok, 2 replacements). NOT built or installed tonight (the run ends 21:00): next session, build pack59d = pack58b-tree + AE3 + this, then the lib corpus (expect 7/7), linked, smoke, the Rust byte comparison vs pack58b and the lib type probes, then install and ask main to ship. pack57's live auto-export keeps this latent bug until then; no live package uses auto-export.")
inl note_of_auto_export_2b_polymorphic_5 () = Note ("AUTO-EXPORT-2B-POLYMORPHIC", 5u32, "2026-11-07T01:07Z", "compiler", "11-06 21:0x compiler: pack59d (core AAD93F1E9CB86319 = live pack58b tree + AUTO-EXPORT-3 + this program with all 3 synthesis fixes): full split-corpus suite 58 cuts over 7 roots (lib/spiral now included via --resolve-manifest), 3 fail, all in spiral/apps/cells/spi at cuts 12, 14 and 16 of 17. Those cuts PASSED on pack57 (run single-flight-20261106-183547), so they are new under AE3/this program: a fourth synthesis or export case in Cells' later modules. next step (next session): read one failing Cells cut's --check error (run single-flight-20261106-204110), fix, rebuild, then the full gate. NOT installed.")
inl note_of_auto_export_2b_polymorphic_6 () = Note ("AUTO-EXPORT-2B-POLYMORPHIC", 6u32, "2026-11-07T11:10Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: CORRECTION to n5. The 3 Cells cut failures under pack59d were NOT a fourth synthesis case. They were a live-tree race: apps/cells/spi/run_result.spi was created at 20:48:03, during that 20:41 corpus run (a peer was editing Cells), so the cut copied its files before the module existed while the manifest it read already listed it ('Unbound term variable: run_result'). Rerun on pack59d (11-07): Cells 8 cuts, 0 fail. So pack59d's corpus is 58 cuts over 7 roots, lib/spiral included, 0 real failures. Running now: linked, lib type probes, smoke, Rust byte comparison vs pack58b. Then install and main ships.")
inl note_of_auto_export_2b_polymorphic_7 () = Note ("AUTO-EXPORT-2B-POLYMORPHIC", 7u32, "2026-11-07T11:40Z", "compiler", "11-07 07:3x compiler: INSTALLED on the box as pack59d (core AAD93F1E9CB86319, host 734CEF2EBF4E7DD4, tree 04123021555F0DE9). Gate: linked 18/18, split-corpus 58 cuts over 7 packages (lib included) 0 fail, lib type probes pass, smoke 159 parity / DISAGREE 0, Rust byte comparison vs pack58b 550/550 identical. Asked main to ship to node and europa; Done when shipped.")
inl note_of_auto_export_2b_polymorphic_8 () = Note ("AUTO-EXPORT-2B-POLYMORPHIC", 8u32, "2026-11-07T11:44Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: DONE. pack59d SHIPPED (main 07:4x): box core AAD93F1E9CB86319 (07:32), node the same in place 07:41 (fresh launch exit 0), europa Linux build from 04123021555F0DE9 / 9452B1A2EBD761EB (core 9eddee63e40fd6ac, launch exit 0); main's sized_array probe passes under it.")
inl note_of_lib_date_time_flaky_1 () = Note ("LIB-DATE-TIME-FLAKY", 1u32, "2026-11-07T00:11Z", "main", "Found 11-06 20:0x (europa, two back-to-back gates of lib/spiral/date_time.spi, identical tree, pack57): run 1 failed guid_from_date_time_3/4, zig_date_time_guid_writes_seven_contiguous_fraction_digits, date_time_guid_fraction_digits_are_contiguous, civil_of_days_names_known_dates; run 2 failed _2/_3, the same zig and fraction tests, civil_of_days_names_known_dates, civil_of_days_inverts_days_from_civil, date_time_guid_round_trips... Varying failures on an unchanged tree = clock/seed/now-dependent assertions or backend precision (zig fraction digits). DoD: each test is deterministic (fixed instants, no now()), green 3/3 consecutive runs on Linux and the box. Next step: read the logs at europa /home/remote/stage/main-timings-{1,2}.log for the assertion values, then pin the inputs. TypeFeatures: none; test determinism.")
inl note_of_lib_date_time_flaky_2 () = Note ("LIB-DATE-TIME-FLAKY", 2u32, "2026-11-07T00:12Z", "main", "main 11-06 20:1x INVALID, closing: not flaky tests. The gate log (europa main-timings-1.log) is one line, 'FatalError: Compiler error while building main: OutOfMemoryException', so the failing cells were the ones whose compile hit the 1400M MemoryMax cap that main set, and which ones failed varied with memory pressure. Same class as the 11-06 kernel gate OOM. No date_time defect is shown. Lesson: read the gate log before naming a test flaky.")
inl note_of_tibia_cfr_retreat_model_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-CFR-RETREAT-MODEL", 1u32, "2026-11-07T00:14Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:1x tibia: from TIBIA-CFR-BRAIN-TRACE. Cause: Retreat costs 40 tempo, 0 damage, and REMOVES an attacker for good (attackers - 1), cheaper than one Melee step (52 damage, no progress until Low); on the live spot stepping away does not remove the cyclops. This also explains PR-30 = PR-32 to the digit (same always-retreat policy in both games). DoD: next_state keeps attackers on Retreat (only a kill lowers them), a unit test that a full-hp knight against one Fresh attacker puts most weight on Melee at depth 3, the trace rerun showing swings and kills, then PR-35 pre-registered and run against retreat2. NEXT: change next_state and the pressure test (the_tree_steps_away_more_under_pressure may need restating).")
inl note_of_tibia_cfr_retreat_model_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-CFR-RETREAT-MODEL", 2u32, "2026-11-07T00:14Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:1x tibia: CAUTION before coding: keeping the attacker on Retreat is necessary but not sufficient at depth 3. One attacker, Fresh target, 3 steps: Melee 52 + 52 + (52 - 20 loot) = 136 with the attacker killed on the last step, Retreat 40 x 3 = 120 with the attacker still there. Retreat stays cheaper because the kill's benefit (no attacker afterwards) falls past the horizon. So the fix needs one of: a deeper tree (depth >= 4, test cost grows again), a terminal value for remaining attackers (e.g. expected damage of the rest of the clear per attacker), or recalibrated buckets (fewer melee steps per kill from measured damage). Recommended: the terminal value, since it keeps depth 3 and states the real cost of leaving a cyclops alive.")
inl note_of_tibia_cfr_retreat_model_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-CFR-RETREAT-MODEL", 3u32, "2026-11-07T00:22Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:2x tibia: PLAN, concrete, with its cost on the existing tests. (1) next_state: attackers fall only on a kill (drop \"k = 2 ||\" from the attackers line); Retreat keeps its damage relief (hitters = pressed - 1) and its 40 tempo. (2) Terminal value for attackers left alive: a separate `horizon_value (s) = attackers x open_attacker_cost` with open_attacker_cost about 300 gp (to calibrate: measured damage-plus-potions per cyclops kill on the spot from pack_sim clear ledgers), used ONLY by the decision paths (tree_knight and strategy_table call values_at with this base) so values_at 0 = zeros stays for the hand-enumerated goldens (one_step 140-148, two_steps 230-242, exact-hp 280-292), which keep their meaning as pure-cost games. (3) Tests: full-hp knight vs one Fresh attacker puts most weight on Melee at depth 3 under the horizon value (expected from 136 vs 120 + 300); pressure_raises_the_step_away_share and the_tree_steps_away_more_under_pressure re-read (Retreat now only buys one step of relief, so the pressure effect should shrink but keep its sign at 3 attackers: 52 saved vs 40 tempo); trace rerun shows swings and kills. (4) Then PR-35 pre-registered (CFR vs retreat2 on the fixed game). Not started tonight: changing the terminal is a design step that needs its own golden derivations, not a last-half-hour edit.")
inl note_of_tibia_cfr_retreat_model_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-CFR-RETREAT-MODEL", 4u32, "2026-11-07T19:00Z", "tibia", "11-07 16:4x tibia: CODE in place (tibia src/arena_cfr.spi 04580E30E651, unstaged), checked against an EXACT ORACLE written first (scratch cfr-oracle/oracle.py, outside the repos: the abstract game re-implemented and every 3x2 zero-sum matrix game solved in closed form by the camp's lower envelope; it reproduces the three old goldens exactly, 144 / 236 / 286). Changes: (1) next_state lowers attackers only on a kill (Retreat keeps its attacker); (2) values_from base generalizes the recursion, values_at keeps the zero base (pure-cost goldens), and decision_values = values_from horizon_values (300 per attacker left alive) feeds tree_knight and strategy_table. Oracle results: OLD rules: a full-hp knight vs one Fresh attacker retreats at every depth (exactly the PR-32 trace); NEW rule alone: still retreats (40 tempo < 52 damage per step), so the rule change is necessary but not sufficient; NEW rule + horizon 300: Melee at depth 3 (weight 1.0). Tests: two_steps_match_the_exact_game_value moves from 236 to the oracle''s 288 (range 282-294); one-step 144 and low-hp 286 unchanged; NEW c test with_a_horizon_cost_a_healthy_knight_fights_one_attacker_instead_of_stepping_away (Melee >= 900 at depth 3). Box: cell budget 0, max line 120, predict -CheckOnly passes. PR-35 pre-registered (BENCHMARK.md). QUEUED on the node behind the quiet A/B and PR-35-IS: job-1794077943855-tibia-retreat-cells (arena_cfr + hunting_pack) then job-1794077968283-tibia-pr35-run (tree A61FA7E597C1), with waiters.")
inl note_of_tibia_cfr_retreat_model_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-CFR-RETREAT-MODEL", 5u32, "2026-11-07T19:02Z", "tibia", "BLOCKER: node jobs job-1794077943855-tibia-retreat-cells (proof for the arena_cfr and hunting_pack cells) and job-1794077968283-tibia-pr35-run (PR-35 measurement), queued behind job-1794077143695-tibia-pr35is-run. NEXT STEP: read both job logs in full; if the cells are green, write the PR-35 RESULT row using its pre-registered decision rule, then set the item to Done or reopen it.")
inl note_of_tibia_cfr_retreat_model_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-CFR-RETREAT-MODEL", 6u32, "2026-11-07T20:13Z", "tibia", "11-07 16:1x tibia: DONE, both jobs read in full. Proof: job-1794077943855-tibia-retreat-cells exit 0. arena_cfr 15/15 tests (26 rows across c and rust, including two_steps_match_the_exact_game_value at the oracle's 288 and the new with_a_horizon_cost_a_healthy_knight_fights_one_attacker_instead_of_stepping_away), hunting_pack 15/15. Measurement: PR-35 (job-1794077968283-tibia-pr35-run, 374 s) HOLDS by its pre-registered rule. cfr clears 63 of 100 (PR-30 and PR-32: 0), deaths 126 -> 64; retreat2 is unchanged to the digit (-150,600, 38 deaths, 100 clears), so the shift belongs to the brain alone. RESULT row in tibia docs/BENCHMARK.md (unstaged). The fix the exact oracle predicted (Retreat keeps its attacker, plus 300 per attacker left at the horizon) is what made the brain fight.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_short_horizon_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-SHORT-HORIZON", 1u32, "2026-11-07T00:27Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:2x tibia: from PR-34's RESULT (+69.6 pct at eps 50 over 10 buckets). DoD: logprob gains per-bucket ess output (is_estimate's ess per bucket), is_softened prints it per target, a horizon parameter (first n buckets) with a golden test that n = all buckets equals wpdis, then PR-35-IS pre-registered and run. NEXT: per-bucket ess in logprob.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_short_horizon_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-SHORT-HORIZON", 2u32, "2026-11-07T00:33Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:3x tibia: STEP 1 in place (tibia src/logprob.spi 3A7D784362D0, unstaged): wpdis_upto rows horizon (sum over the first horizon buckets) and bucket_ess rows b (is_estimate's ess for one bucket); golden tests c and rust: horizon 1 scores 15 and horizon 2 equals wpdis; two equal hunts in a bucket give ess 2.0 (131072). Box: cell budget 0, max line 120, --check passes the src package. Blocked on: node job-1794011620685-tibia-horizon-cells (logprob), with a waiter. Resume when: it finishes; then is_softened prints per-bucket ess and horizon-3 rows, pre-registered as PR-35-IS before the run.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_short_horizon_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-SHORT-HORIZON", 3u32, "2026-11-07T00:38Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:3x tibia: STEP 1 PROVEN: node job-1794011620685-tibia-horizon-cells, logprob 16/16 on c and rust (log read in full), incl. a_short_horizon_scores_only_its_first_buckets and a_bucket_of_two_equal_hunts_has_an_ess_of_two. NEXT: is_softened prints per-bucket ess and horizon-3 rows; pre-register PR-35-IS before the run.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_short_horizon_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-SHORT-HORIZON", 4u32, "2026-11-07T00:39Z", "tibia", "11-06 20:4x tibia: PR-35-IS pre-registered in tibia/docs/BENCHMARK.md above PR-34 (unstaged), before the horizon program exists: first 3 buckets (1,800 ticks) of PR-34's rows against fresh potions over a clear run capped at 1,800 ticks (same window both sides); print bucket_ess for buckets 0-2; prediction 90 within 10 pct, 50 within 25 pct with bucket-2 ess >= 10, 0 under 1 potion; decision written (fail with low ess -> a behavior closer to the target, eps 75). NEXT: is_softened horizon rows (wpdis_upto rows 3, capped fresh via clear_run ... 1800) and the run.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_short_horizon_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-SHORT-HORIZON", 5u32, "2026-11-07T15:53Z", "tibia", "11-07 11:5x tibia groom round 2 (stale Active, no note in 12 h): Planned. Not touched since 11-06 20:39; its next step is concrete (is_softened horizon rows: wpdis_upto rows 3 plus fresh capped at 1,800 ticks; PR-35-IS is pre-registered). Pull it as the next CPU-light IS step.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_short_horizon_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-SHORT-HORIZON", 6u32, "2026-11-07T18:46Z", "tibia", "11-07 16:0x tibia: PROGRAM in place (tibia apps/predict/is_softened.spi 673767636889, unstaged): horizon rows per target epsilon 90/50/0 = WPDIS over buckets 0-2 (wpdis_upto (bucket_rows potions_milli ... 10) 3, i.e. the first 1,800 ticks) against fresh potions from clear runs capped at 1,800 ticks (capped_sum, same window), error per mille, and bucket_ess for buckets 0, 1, 2, as PR-35-IS pre-registered. Checked with the new predict.ps1 -CheckOnly (both packages pass), cell budget 0, max line 120. QUEUED: node job-1794077143695-tibia-pr35is-run (tree 5B79A8900E03) behind the quiet RUST-ARRAY-3-AB run so its timings stay clean, with a waiter. Blocked on: that job. Resume when: it finishes; write PR-35-IS's RESULT.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_short_horizon_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-SHORT-HORIZON", 7u32, "2026-11-07T19:54Z", "tibia", "11-07 15:5x tibia: PR-35-IS MEASURED and read in full (node job-1794077143695-tibia-pr35is-run, exit 0; RESULT row in tibia docs/BENCHMARK.md, unstaged). VERDICT by the pre-registered rule: \"fails at 50 with ess under 10\". Target 90: WPDIS_3 17,039 vs capped fresh 17,760, -4.0 pct (within 10). Target 50: 16,723 vs 9,860, +69.6 pct (outside 25); bucket-2 ess 65,531 q16, i.e. about 1.0 effective hunt of 100. Target 0: 0 vs 0. POST-HOC, the reason the horizon changed nothing: the cap never bound. The capped fresh means equal the uncapped ones exactly, and the bucket-1 and bucket-2 ess are identical, so every hunt has ended by bucket 1 and WPDIS_3 = PR-34's 10-bucket WPDIS. PR-34's premise (that the late buckets rest on few hunts) was wrong; the epsilon-50 weight collapses onto one hunt from the start. DONE: the code (wpdis_upto, bucket_ess, horizon rows) works and the question is answered; the estimator line ends here. The next step is the rule's own: a behavior closer to the target (epsilon 75), filed as TIBIA-IS-BEHAVIOR-75.")
inl note_of_transformer_typed_simd_dot_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:04Z", "tibia", "11-07 tibia: the FIRST typed lever, picked because Mojo's published lead is mostly single-thread (154 vs 105 tok/s, 1.47x, from SIMD width plus independent accumulators against the FMA dependency chain, TRANSFORMER-MOJO-STUDY), while our threads already match OpenMP. Why types: the row length is a type-level literal in sized arrays, so the chunking is decided by partial evaluation with zero runtime cost; that is Mojo's nelts parameter expressed as a type, with a compile-time check that lanes divide the row (core type_lit_to_lit, no TYPE-ARITH fold needed for literal dims). Lib-only: no host macros and no compiler change (COMPILER-SIMD-CODEGEN stays the later, explicit-vector step). DoD: lib function with golden tests on c and rust (equal to the scalar dot within the float tolerance the logits gate allows, since reordered sums differ in the last bits), the transformer using it in matmul, parity at the logits tolerance, and a node A/B (v8 vs v9) plus the codespace table from TRANSFORMER-MOJO-BASELINE. Split into <= 120 min steps when started: (1) lib dot + tests, (2) transformer switch + parity, (3) A/B. NEXT: step 1 in lib/spiral (round-trip the draft first).")
inl note_of_transformer_typed_simd_dot_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT", 2u32, "2026-11-07T18:36Z", "tibia", "11-07 15:5x tibia: STEP 1 drafted (kit store drafts\\spiral\\TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT\\pkg\\f32_lanes.spi, new lib module): dot4 keeps 4 independent accumulators in a record partial { a0; a1; a2; a3 }, steps 4 terms per call over a span { a; ao; b; bo } record (Spiral records flatten at partial evaluation, so no runtime cost), and finishes the n mod 4 remainder by continuing f32_kernels::dot from n4 with the combined sum (a0 + a1) + (a2 + a3). DEVIATION from the DoD, recorded on purpose: the lane count is FIXED at 4 for step 1; lanes as a type-level literal (16 like Mojo's nelts) needs a phantom lane parameter and is only worth adding if the measured gain from 4 justifies it (step 2 decides). Tests (c, rust): on small-integer ramps every product is exact, so dot4 must EQUAL the scalar dot (n 16 -> 2040, 7 -> 252, 3 -> 38, hand-computed as sum k(k+4)); on a 288-wide ramp with fractions it stays within 0.1 pct. Box: --check passes, cell budget 0. NEXT: Cells run of the tests, then land, then the transformer switch (step 2) and the node A/B.")
inl note_of_transformer_typed_simd_dot_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT", 3u32, "2026-11-07T19:17Z", "tibia", "11-07 15:1x tibia: STEP 1 LANDED: spiral/lib/spiral/f32_lanes.spi 5368857D1EF8 (lib_land_window green: round trip, document_test, type probe 1/1, respell_drift 0; lib digest D7987E9E658D688E; undo 20261107-145325; unstaged). STEP 2 FINDING, no timing yet, deterministic. The hot path is NOT f32_kernels::matmul, which is only the fallback for unknown models; it is transformer.spi's typed dot_l (width a `len n` literal) called from rows_l, rows_l_fused, and the q8 path. On the node, gcc -Ofast -march=native -S of the v8 C shows dot_l30 (width 768) compiles to ONE `vfmadd231ps mem, ymm4, ymm1` chain: a single 8-wide accumulator, so FMA latency (~4 cycles) bounds the loop, not FMA throughput (2 per cycle). Adding -funroll-loops -fvariable-expansion-in-unroller gives 2 accumulator registers. This is exactly the Mojo lever (nelts x several accumulators), and it also explains the 11-03 refutation: a 4-scalar-accumulator dot still packs into one ymm, so step 1's dot4 alone cannot help. PRE-REGISTERED v9 probe (scratch variant of the v8 source, node C:\\Users\\alias\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\tibia-tf6, not the repo): dot_l keeps 32 scalar accumulators as 4 records of 8 (lane8 x 4) over a row_pair record, then finishes with the scalar remainder; every model row width (288, 512, 768, 1376, 2048) is a multiple of 32. Gates, in order: (G1, asm) the v9 dot loop must use >= 3 distinct ymm FMA accumulators, else the Spiral spelling does not reach SLP, VERDICT VOID-design, and I respell; (G2, parity) the 15M greedy text equals v3's, or if the reordered sums flip a token, the first differing position is recorded and the logits gate (1e-4) decides; (G3, timing) single-thread 15M and 42M, v8 vs v9, interleaved, quiet-gated like tf9-sweep: a lever if v9's median is >= 5 pct faster with non-overlapping ranges; under 3 pct means refuted, and 3-5 pct is unresolved and needs 16 rounds. G1 and G2 run now, while the node is busy; G3 waits for a quiet window.")
inl note_of_transformer_typed_simd_dot_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT", 4u32, "2026-11-07T20:00Z", "tibia", "11-07 16:0x tibia: v9 PROBE READ IN FULL (node job-1794079162891-tibia-tf9-probe, exit 0; compiler core E3F33F3766EE, v9 86BA27447902). G1 (asm) passes on its letter: the 32-accumulator loop (quads3230, noinline, 16 call sites; every dot_l is now inlined into its caller) has 4 vfmadd231ps on 4 distinct ymm accumulators. G2 (parity) passes: greedy text byte-identical to v3 for 15M (817 chars) and 42M (749) at OMP 1 and 8. BUT the indicative timings on a node at 100 pct load, which are not G3, show v9 SLOWER in 6 of 6 interleaved pairs (15M t1: v8 22.6/10.6/7.5 s vs v9 29.9/34.8/26.4; 42M: v8 33.1/10.9/26.5 vs v9 58.5/64.6/130.6). Asm cause, read before any rerun: SLP builds each ymm operand from 8 scalar loads. Per 32 elements the loop has 48 vmovss + 48 vinsertps + 12 vinsertf128 against 4 FMAs, and each operand's base pointer is reloaded from the stack, because gcc strength-reduced every one of the 64 int32 sums v1 + (v4 + k) into its own induction pointer and could no longer see that they are adjacent. The generated C is clean (v0->ptr[v1 + v4 + k], no checks), so this is an SLP limit, not a codegen bug. G1 was under-specified: it should also have required contiguous vector loads. v9 is REJECTED as a design. v10 (pre-registered under the same gates, with G1 now also requiring vmovups loads and no vinsertps in the loop): 4 accumulators over the 4 contiguous QUARTERS of the row, acc_k += a[j + k*q] * b[j + k*q] for j < q = width/4, so that gcc's LOOP vectorizer (not SLP) sees 4 stride-1 streams and should emit 4 ymm chains with vmovups. Every row width (288, 512, 768, 1376, 2048) divides into quarters that are multiples of 8. Running: job-1794081578930-tibia-tf10-probe.")
inl note_of_transformer_typed_simd_dot_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT", 5u32, "2026-11-07T22:16Z", "tibia", "11-07 18:2x tibia: G3 DEVIATION, recorded before any G3 timing. The node has not been quiet once this evening (the gated Rust A/B ahead of tibia-tf10-g3 logged 6 straight 20-minute waits at calm 0), so G3 MOVES TO EUROPA, which goes quiet between other lanes' runs. Same rule as pre-registered (v10 at least 5 pct faster in median with non-overlapping ranges = lever; under 3 pct = refuted; 3-5 pct = unresolved), with three changes made now, before any look: 16 rounds (the noise memory's minimum), an A/A arm V8B (the same v8 binary again) to set the noise band, and arm order rotated by round. Builds on europa with gcc 15.2.0 -Ofast -march=native -fopenmp, OMP_NUM_THREADS=1: v8.c BDD375D03DF8 and v10.c 9CDCEB12D660 (both generated on the node by core E3F33F3766EE). Models: stories15M and stories42M (hashes match the node's). Per-run gate: /proc/stat idle at least 90 pct over three 2 s windows. Parity: v10 text equals v8's for both models, else VOID. It starts after the Mojo single-thread sweep on the same host, so the two gated runs never overlap. tibia-tf10-g3 on the node stays queued as a backup; whichever is quiet first decides, and the other is cancelled.")
inl note_of_transformer_typed_simd_dot_6 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT", 6u32, "2026-11-07T22:45Z", "tibia", "11-07 18:5x tibia: G3 MEASURED on europa, VERDICT REFUTED for v10, by the pre-registered rule (scratch tf9/g3-vultr.sh + g3_verdict.py; 16 of 16 quiet-gated rounds per model, 0 skips; binaries v8 15fc0b0cd515, v10 8fd7e146ce83; text parity v10 == v8 on both models). stories15M: V8 median 1136.0 ms (989-1218), V10 1277.5 (1099-1408), A/A V8B 1114.5; noise band 42.0 ms (3.7 pct); v10 is 12.5 pct SLOWER and won 0 of 16 pairs. stories42M: V8 2645.5 (2502-2848), V10 3838.5 (3710-4066), V8B 2614.5; noise band 114.5 ms (4.3 pct); v10 is 45.1 pct SLOWER, and its fastest run is slower than v8's slowest. The quarter-accumulator dot (4 ymm chains, clean vmovups) loses badly on a bandwidth-bound loop: 4 streams 1/4 row apart per call plus a runtime trip count and a noinline call per row. v11 (the same quarters with a literal trip count, width k / 4) is still running on the node as job-1794089050666-tibia-tf11-probe (asm + parity only; its timing would also need europa). Consistent with the bandwidth arithmetic (v8 moves about 7 GB/s on both models) and with the single-thread Mojo row (MOJO-BASELINE n4: v8 within noise of llama2.mojo -j 1). NEXT STEP: read the v11 asm. If it also gathers or keeps the per-row call, close this item as REFUTED for multi-accumulator dot spellings, with step 1's lib dot4 kept as a tested utility and the lane-count type parameter never added (no measured gain to justify it).")
inl note_of_transformer_typed_simd_dot_7 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT", 7u32, "2026-11-07T22:48Z", "tibia", "11-07 19:0x tibia: v11 probe read in full (node job-1794089050666-tibia-tf11-probe, core 65DEE79CF2CF). G1: the quarter loop is now specialized per row width (quarters30/32/34/45, literal trip width/4), with 4 ymm accumulators and vmovups loads in the main loop (14 vinsertps remain in the epilogue), still called non-inlined per row (23 call sites). G2: text identical to v3 on 15M and 42M at OMP 1 and 8. The node timings are useless (100 pct load; the same v8 binary ranged 4.7-22.2 s). G3 for v11 is RUNNING on europa under v10's exact rule and script (tf9/g3b-vultr.sh, V10 arm = tf-v11 built from v11.c), so the item closes on measured evidence for both spellings.")
inl note_of_transformer_typed_simd_dot_8 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT", 8u32, "2026-11-07T23:12Z", "tibia", "11-07 19:1x tibia: DONE, NEGATIVE RESULT, measured. v11 G3 on europa (tf9/g3b-vultr.sh, the same rule and script as v10; the arm labelled V10 in its output is tf-v11 660caae6da14 built from v11.c 9F9591851252; 16 of 16 quiet rounds per model, 0 skips, text parity on both models). stories15M: v8 1053.0 ms (933-1181), v11 1089.5 (966-1246), A/A 1094.5; band 84 ms (8.0 pct); v11 -3.5 pct, 7 of 16 wins: REFUTED (under 3 pct faster; in fact inside the noise). stories42M: v8 2600.5 (2349-2826), v11 3578.0 (2915-4045), A/A 2664.0; band 114 ms (4.4 pct); v11 -37.6 pct, 0 of 16: REFUTED. SUMMARY of the lever: v8's single-thread dot is one ymm FMA chain, but the loop is memory-bandwidth-bound (about 7 GB/s on 15M and 42M alike), so more accumulators cannot pay. v9's 32 scalar accumulators gathered every operand through vinsertps (SLP limit); v10's 4 quarter streams vectorized cleanly but lost 12.5 / 45 pct; v11 with literal quarter trips recovered 15M to parity and still lost 38 pct on 42M. Single-threaded, v8 is already within noise of llama2.mojo -j 1 (MOJO-BASELINE n4). KEPT: lib f32_lanes.spi (dot4, landed and tested) as a utility. NOT DONE, on purpose: the lane-count type parameter and the transformer switch, since there is no measured gain to justify either. Scratch variants and probes: tf9/ (v9, v10, v11 .spi and .c, tfv-node.ps1, g3*-vultr.sh, verdict scripts). Memory transformer-speed-levers updated.")
inl note_of_compiler_memo_design_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-MEMO-DESIGN", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:04Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: filed from the user 11-07 via main direction (old-log mining for memo/cache ideas; main sends the hits, compiler owns the design). DoD: a design note listing each candidate cache (key, value, invalidation, expected win measured against LARGE-INPUT-SCALING / COMPILER-LOAD-ALL), the 2 best split into implementation items. next step: wait for main's hits; meanwhile list today's caches in spiral_compiler.fs (CompilationCache, memo tables). typefeatureset plan: cache keys as typed records (path, content hash), never string concatenation.")
inl note_of_compiler_memo_design_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-MEMO-DESIGN", 2u32, "2026-11-07T11:15Z", "main", "main 11-07 OLD-LOG HITS (the user's point 3; transcripts start 10-28, so pre-wipe ideas survive only in backlog notes; spiral/kit commit messages carry none). EXISTS: an in-process whole-output compile cache (tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs:348, sourceGraphFingerprint :380; nothing persisted, nothing per phase); on-disk matrix and native memos (test.ps1:541, keyed by DLL hash + source tree + SPIRAL_* + backend); the Cells result memo (KINO-RESULT-MEMO family; math.spi 201 s cold -> 6.2 s memoized; sm' 225 s -> 9 s with 38/38 hits); per-compile memos (Utils.memoize, codegenMemoTable, EPatternMemo, hash-consing). NUMBERS: a cold lib-importing compile is 5.7-6.0 s, of which about 75% is re-reading lib (QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER n1-n8); a warm daemon took the sm' gate 537 -> 217 s; --batch amortizes to about 31 ms per row; GC is 93.5 of 197 s (single-flight) and 178 of 298 s (hopacsota 8 workers), and a 256 MB gen0 cuts it to 14-22 s; hopacsota at 4 workers runs at 0.68x of 1. UNBUILT, in order of value: (1) QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER (Epic P1, 0%): persistent fill-once cells, starting with lib parse+typecheck keyed by the lib .spi hashes that Cells already computes ('hard to retrofit', 10-31); (2) HOPACSOTA n15 R5: the never-run near-SOTA rule (GC < 15% of wall, every phase's scaling exponent <= 1.1), which answers the user's 11-01 single-flight question; (3) a token-level memo key (whitespace edits stop invalidating); (4) a possible missing backend in the join_point_type memo key (HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH n18). User quotes: 10-12 'each cell run should be instant, memoized by the compiler and kino'; 10-13 'incremental in the Hopac model, not salsa-style'. Upstream 8134d983 (2024-07-06, Infer.fs): 'Tried memoization and realized it wouldn't work. Nevermind.'")
inl note_of_compiler_memo_design_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-MEMO-DESIGN", 3u32, "2026-11-07T11:16Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: CHECKED main's flag (HOPACSOTA-RESEARCH n18, 'backend missing from the join_point_type memo key'). Read at the live tree: join_point_method / join_point_closure / join_point_type are created INSIDE peval (spiral_compiler.fs ~9935-9937), and a build runs peval for one backend, so the tables cannot be shared between builds for different backends. The remaining exposure is narrower: within ONE build, BackendSwitch validates the non-current backends' arms (backend_switch_validated, keyed by (backend, arm) ~9941), but a TYPE join point (key = body + env_global_type, ~10218) evaluated during that validation is cached without the backend. If a type-level computation depends on the backend (a backend_switch inside a type join point), the result cached while validating arm B could be reused for the current backend A. Status: plausible, not reproduced. next step for this item: a probe sample with a backend_switch inside a type-level function called from two arms; if it reproduces, key the type join point by the validating backend during arm validation. Design work on main's top lever (QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER: persistent lib parse + typecheck keyed by lib .spi hashes, about 75% of a cold lib-importing compile) follows the LARGE-INPUT-SCALING table.")
inl note_of_compiler_memo_design_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-MEMO-DESIGN", 4u32, "2026-11-07T19:08Z", "compiler", "11-07 15:2x compiler: DESIGN v1.\n(0) n18 CLOSED, not reachable: validating a non-current arm sets s.backend to that arm (spiral_compiler.fs ~11203), and a type join point (~10218) is keyed by (body, env_global_type) without the backend, BUT BackendSwitch requires every arm's type to equal the current arm's (~11177), so any type a switch computes is backend-independent by construction. Only UnsafeBackendSwitch skips that check, and the core already documents it as never for type-level computation (~11216). No probe needed.\n(1) CANDIDATES (key -> value; invalidation; measured or expected win):\n a. RESIDENT WARM COMPILER FOR EVERY HEAVY CLIENT. key: (core DLL hash, client root) -> a live server-tcp process holding parsed+inferred lib/package states (Hopac fill-once streams, the model the user asked for: 'incremental in the Hopac model, not salsa-style'). Invalidation: the existing revision store + manifest reopen. WIN measured: sm' gate 537 -> 217 s; a cold lib-importing compile is 5.7-6.0 s with ~75% re-reading lib, while a warm compile of the same pays ~0. Cells already does this; kit regen (83 units), fiscal lib_land_window consumer gates, test.ps1 linked/split suites and node-proof jobs start a cold process per compile.\n b. PERSISTED lib parse+typecheck (QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER). key: (core hash, DefaultEnv, file content hash, dependency closure hashes) -> serialized InferResult/TopEnv. Blocker: those values hold refs, Hopac promises and closures, so persistence means a stable typed IR of TopEnv (an export format). Expected win as (a) but across process restarts. Hard; behind (a), which captures most of the win without serialization.\n c. WHITESPACE-INSENSITIVE BLOCK MEMO. key: a block's token kinds+texts (no ranges) -> parse/infer reuse with a range shift. Win: editor and formatter-respell edits stop re-inferring unchanged blocks (respells touch hundreds of files; x's census + document_test round trips re-infer everything). Moderate, inside the daemon only.\n d. CROSS-BACKEND peval reuse (Rust+Zig of the same entry): REJECTED, peval tables are per backend by design (join points emit backend code) and BackendSwitch validation is already memoized per (backend, arm).\n e. GC budget (gen0 256 MB): already applied by test.ps1 and the host's re-exec for long-lived modes; extend to the warm daemon in (a).\n(2) SPLIT: COMPILER-WARM-CLIENTS (a, P1) and BLOCK-MEMO-TOKEN-KEY (c, P2). (b) stays QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER, behind (a)'s measured result. LARGE-INPUT-SCALING's table is the measuring stick for all three.")
inl note_of_compiler_memo_design_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-MEMO-DESIGN", 5u32, "2026-11-07T19:11Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: DONE, proof: the DoD's design note is note 4 (each candidate cache with key, value, invalidation and its measured or expected win, n18 closed by reading the code), and the 2 best are split into COMPILER-WARM-CLIENTS and BLOCK-MEMO-TOKEN-KEY with their own DoD and next step.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_1 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:04Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: filed from the user 11-07 via main question (require test cells to name a language?). DoD: decide with x; if yes, a test block with no ///> target and no prelude target is a named error from the directive parser (not a silent default), with the Cells side changed by x in the same window. next step: ask x for the Cells-side view and count today's blocks that rely on a default.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_2 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 2u32, "2026-11-07T11:07Z", "x", "2026-11-07 07:07 x (Cells side): Cells TODAY defaults a block with no ///> line in this order (notebook.ex 694-705): the file's static-prelude targets, then the :default_target option or SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET (an empty string means none), then a hardcoded ///> zig. Such steps are mark_untargeted, and mix spiral.targets --check lists them (use it for the count). The Cells change, in the same window as the parser: drop the zig fallback and the env default, and a block with no target becomes a named error. Open question for the user: does the explicit file prelude still count? x recommends keeping it. Related: CELLS-SKIP-MARKER-DECIDE (no default makes the explicit no-run marker matter more).")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_3 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 3u32, "2026-11-07T11:10Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: x's view (11-07): (1) Cells does apply defaults today, in order (notebook.ex 694-705 default_builders): the file's static prelude builders, then the :default_target option / SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET (empty = no targets), then a HARDCODED ///> zig fallback (the true -> arm). 'mix spiral.targets --check' already lists untargeted test blocks, which is the census tool. (2) x changes Cells in the same window: the zig arm becomes a named error ('test <name> names no target: add ///> <lang>') and the env default goes. DECISION FOR THE USER: may a file's static prelude still supply targets for its blocks? x and compiler recommend keeping prelude inheritance (it is explicit in the file) and removing only the implicit zig and env fallbacks. next step: the census via mix spiral.targets --check, then agree the error name with x.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_4 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 4u32, "2026-11-07T11:10Z", "x", "2026-11-07 07:10 x: PENDING THE USER'S DECISION on whether an explicit file-level prelude may still supply targets. main, x and compiler all recommend YES (it's written in the file, so it's explicit), but that is our recommendation, not the user's answer. Agreed with compiler: code NoTestTarget, message 'test <name> names no target: add ///> <lang> to it or to the file's static prelude', the same text on both sides. Cells side, x, once the user answers: remove the SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET / :default_target fallback and the hardcoded ///> zig arm in notebook.ex default_builders. NO CODE CHANGE until then; compiler first takes the census with mix spiral.targets --check.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_5 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 5u32, "2026-11-07T11:17Z", "x", "2026-11-07 07:17 x: USER DECIDED (11-07, in x's session): 'nah let's keep everything explicit, no env vars prelude etc. maybe after rewriting the compiler in spiral we think about bringing it back.' So NO inheritance of any kind: no SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET / :default_target, no hardcoded zig, and NO file static-prelude targets. Every test cell names its own ///> target. This overrides the prelude recommendation in n3/n4. Error text with compiler, updated: code NoTestTarget, 'test <name> names no target: add a ///> <lang> line to it'. Cells side, x: default_builders returns no inherited builders, and a target-less cell is the named error. Migration: compiler's census (mix spiral.targets --check plus prelude-inheriting cells) lists the cells that need their own ///> line before the switch. Revisit inheritance after the compiler is rewritten in Spiral.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_6 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 6u32, "2026-11-07T11:22Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: USER DECISION (in x's session, relayed by x; x's n5): 'nah let's keep everything explicit, no env vars prelude etc. maybe after rewriting the compiler in spiral we think about bringing it back.' So NO inheritance of any kind: no SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET, no hardcoded ///> zig, and no static-prelude targets; every test cell names its own ///> line. Shared error: code NoTestTarget, text 'test <name> names no target: add a ///> <lang> line to it'. Also decided (CELLS-SKIP-MARKER-DECIDE n3): ///> _ retires, with Cells inferring no-run cells by shape; the parser keeps accepting ///> _ until the respell removes its 276 uses. DoD now: (1) census of target-less blocks AND prelude-only blocks (running, box), (2) a respell giving every prelude-only block its own ///> lines, (3) the parser/directive error, and x's Cells change, in the same window.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_7 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 7u32, "2026-11-07T12:02Z", "x", "2026-11-07 08:02 x: COUPLING with CELLS-SKIP-MARKER-DECIDE. The skip inference's rule R1 ('a file with any test cell never runs its program entry') counts static tests and static preludes. 16 files that are marked today have NO test except one static prelude: tibia predict cfr_arena/cfr_trace/gpu_plan/is_softened/lanes/lanes_gpu, arena nexbot/vbot, kit_job_slot, kit_lint_shadows shadows_run, x client events/timeline, xsim_checks/events/flow/world. If this item deletes static preludes because they no longer supply a default target, those files lose R1 coverage and their mains (live tibia programs) would RUN in the gate. So keep their prelude as an opens-only static cell, or give each a real test, in the same window.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_8 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 8u32, "2026-11-07T13:11Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: CENSUS DONE (box, rg-scoped over all of C:\\home\\git: 632 .spi files with test blocks; a block's own targets = the ///> lines directly above its :# header; a prelude = a ':# name static' block). 2,307 test blocks already name their own ///> target. 326 blocks in 81 files get targets ONLY from their file's static prelude (x 67 files, spiral 14): each needs the prelude's ///> lines copied above it. 177 blocks in 24 files have NO target at all and run on today's implicit zig/env default (spiral 16 files, blog 8): each needs an explicit ///> (zig, to keep today's behavior). Respell split by owner: x's 67 files to x; spiral's 30 to compiler; blog's 8 to its owner (main to route). The NoTestTarget error lands only after every respell, so no file goes red. next step (compiler): a scratch respell script over spiral's 30 files (copy the prelude's ///> lines above each prelude-only block; add ///> zig to target-less blocks), byte-checked with Cells on a sample of them.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_9 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 9u32, "2026-11-07T13:11Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: typefeatureset plan: none; a directive rule plus a mechanical respell, no new data shapes. (The census is n8; my message to x said n7.)")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_10 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 10u32, "2026-11-07T13:16Z", "x", "2026-11-07 09:16 x: x's share of the target respell: MEASURED, NOT APPLIED. My scan finds 68 x files and 283 prelude-only blocks (compiler counted 67; the extra file was probably edited after the census). Applied as a mechanical copy of the prelude's ///> lines, then reverted byte-exact (68/68 hashes restored): it breaks x's cell budget in 54 test cells (sim 26, client 28). x posts every line of a cell, and preludes like '///> rust -d unicode-segmentation=1.13 unicode-normalization=0.1' + '///> gleam' add about 70 weighted chars to each block. With BARE targets ('///> rust', '///> gleam'; -d lists dropped) only 18 cells go over (sim 12, client 6), small enough to split. But bare targets need the crate deps declared once per package: Cells already merges a package's declared crates (Mounts.declared_crates, read from the 'crates:' section of a --package manifest), yet Manifest.from_toml drops crates and x's manifests are spiral.toml. So the bare route needs a small Cells change first: a crates table in spiral.toml carried through from_toml (manifest.spi), and the x manifests (both toml and spiproj, see x-dual-manifests) declaring serde_json, sha2, unicode-*, base64, jiff. OPTIONS for compiler/main: (A) that Cells change, then bare targets + 18 splits [x recommends: explicit, short, dependencies stated once where they belong]; (B) mechanical copy + 54 splits; (C) exempt directive lines from the budget [rejected: x posts them, so they do cost characters].")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_11 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 11u32, "2026-11-07T13:16Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: DECIDED with x: option A. Crates are declared once per package manifest (crates: in spiproj, crates in spiral.toml) and every test cell gets a BARE target line (///> rust, ///> gleam, no -d), instead of copying long preludes like '///> rust -d unicode-segmentation=1.13 ...' onto every block. x measured that the mechanical copy breaks the cell budget in 54 x cells, while bare targets leave 18 over (splittable). The compiler already accepts and ignores crates: in spiproj (spiral_compiler.fs ~30363), and the toml<->spiproj translation carries it (~34808, ~34853), so no compiler pack is needed. Work: x does Cells manifest.spi (from_toml keeps crates), x's manifests and x's 68 files; compiler does spiral's 30 files the same way; blog's 8 are routed by main. NoTestTarget lands after all three.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_12 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 12u32, "2026-11-07T13:27Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: SPIRAL'S SHARE APPLIED (unstaged): 30 files, 190 test blocks, by scratch target-respell.ps1. Prelude-only blocks copy their file's static-prelude targets BARE (lib routes / reactors / json / conversation get ///> c + ///> rust; the 11 Cells files get ///> gleam); target-less blocks get ///> zig, which keeps today's behavior because CI runs lib notebooks through ci_gate.ps1 -> run_notebook.ps1 with SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET unset (only Cells' own Elixir test_helper.exs sets it, to rust, for its unit tests). No -d crate lists were involved. Verified: a rescan finds 0 untargeted blocks in spiral; in all 30 files the diff adds only '///> ' lines (2 files also carry other lanes' unstaged ///> _ retirement edits, parsing.spi and builders.spi; both edits survived the concurrent write); no BOMs. Left before NoTestTarget: blog's 8 files (main routes them).")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_13 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 13u32, "2026-11-07T13:39Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: ALL RESPELLS IN (unstaged): spiral 30 files / 190 blocks (compiler), x 68 / 283 (x, crates in manifests), blog 8 files / 31 cells (main, ///> zig; blog's runner sets no default target, gate proof running). The compiler side is EMPTY: spiral_compiler.fs and the host never read ///> (the host's --test-blocks returns block boundaries only), so target directives are parsed entirely in Cells and NoTestTarget lands wholly in x's Cells change, with no compiler pack. Remaining for Done: x's NoTestTarget change, after main's blog proof.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_14 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 14u32, "2026-11-07T15:22Z", "x", "2026-11-07 11:22 x: NoTestTarget IS IN the Cells tree (unstaged). A test step without its own ///> now fails with 'NoTestTarget: test <name> names no target: add a ///> <lang> line to it', whatever the static prelude, SPIRAL_CELLS_DEFAULT_TARGET or default_target: say. implicit_default? is removed and with_builders lost its implicit? parameter. untargeted_tests (mix spiral.targets) lists every test without its own target. README updated. Cells fixtures that relied on the suite's rust default now name ///> rust (notebook_test 10, notebook_report_test 18, accept_test 5, display_test 1); located.spi expects 6:9. NODE: full Cells suite 388/394, and the 6 reds are the known VOID mirror gaps. ORG CENSUS (box, cache-free walk of C:\\home\\git, skipping .cache dirs + polyglot): 5548 .spi/.livemd files, 0 untargeted outside Cells' own tmp/ test fixtures, and 0 retired markers. It found dice/lib/dice.spi with 21 untargeted 'test x =' blocks that compiler's census (which counted only ':#' headers) missed; they got ///> zig (today's default; nobody sets the env var). x: 68 files / 283 blocks with bare targets plus crates in both manifests; 18 budget splits. STILL INHERITED (deliberate, follow-up): non-test steps (code cells, the whole-file check) still use prelude/env/zig defaults, and inherit_deps still gives a test's own ///> rust the prelude's -d crates. Each needs its own census before removal.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_15 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 15u32, "2026-11-07T15:23Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: DONE. x landed NoTestTarget in the Cells tree with the agreed text ('test <name> names no target: add a ///> <lang> line to it'). x's cache-free org census before leaving it in (5,548 files) found 0 untargeted tests outside Cells' tmp fixtures. It also caught a file MY census missed: dice/lib/dice.spi, 21 blocks written in the 'test name =' header form, while my census matched only ':#' headers; x gave them ///> zig. Lesson: a test-block census must match both header forms (':# name' and 'test name'). Respells: spiral 30 files / 190 (compiler), x 68 / 283 (x), blog 8 / 31 (main), dice 1 / 21 (x). The remaining inheritance (non-test steps' default targets, inherit_deps) is x's CELLS-NONTEST-TARGETS-EXPLICIT.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_16 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 16u32, "2026-11-07T15:23Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: proof: x's cache-free org census (5,548 files) with NoTestTarget active finds 0 untargeted tests outside Cells' tmp fixtures; compiler's rescan of spiral finds 0 untargeted ':#' blocks; x's node verify job-1794057862441-x-verify-targets1 covers x's respell.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_17 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 17u32, "2026-11-07T16:59Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: USER REVIEW + CLEANUP (user: 'the required comments for tests was done sloppily, duplicated items ... putting a language in a test that has only opens got weird'). Two lanes respelled the same files with different models: per-test ///> lines (compiler/x/main) and then a per-file line-1 header (x), so 600 files named their language twice and 509 headers sat on opens-only static preludes. USER DECISIONS (asked in compiler's session): (1) PER-TEST TARGETS ONLY: every test names its own ///>, no file header, and no static prelude carries a target; (2) a file's whole-file run uses the UNION of its own tests' explicit targets, a file with no tests gets no whole-file run until it has one, and the ~391 test-free self-check files each get one explicit test cell (x implements both, drops NoFileTarget, keeps NoTestTarget). CLEANUP APPLIED (unstaged, scratch target-cleanup.ps1): 737 files in 13 repos, 888 ///> lines removed (kit 292, spiral 139, fiscal 101, x 82, tibia 75, regex 16, html 11, blog 9, alphabet 6, i574n.github 2, dice 2, tapas2 1, polyglot 1); also 3 duplicate '///> cuda' lines that HEAD already had (am'.spi choose and try_pick, optionm'.spi of_obj). proof: over all 759 files with test blocks (both ':# name' and 'test name' header forms), 0 targets on static preludes, 0 file-level headers, 0 duplicate target lines within a test, and a rerun changes nothing; the only tests without an own target are 6 'fails' (compile-error) tests that never run.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_18 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 18u32, "2026-11-07T17:02Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: CORRECTION to n17's scope. The cleanup covered the 759 files that CONTAIN test blocks. x's line-1 header also went into test-free files, and 1175 .spi files fleet-wide still start with ///> (kit 918, spiral 75, tibia 73, tictactoe 49, i574n.github 23, dice 14, html 9, dir-tree-html 7, alphabet 2, fiscal 2, regex 2, blog 1); kit spotted it. Under the user's rule (b) a test-free file gets no whole-file run, so all of them go, in this order: (1) x gives the ~391 self-check files their explicit test cell (so their checks keep running); (2) the remaining line-1 headers are stripped with the same rule (x, who holds the list; compiler's target-cleanup.ps1 if x prefers); (3) kit strips the line it gained in the backlog's live data files (docs/backlog/state/*.spi incl. agile.spi, archive/done-notes.spi) through its own writer, since a script edit would race kit agile writes. Done when a fleet-wide scan finds 0 files starting with ///> whose first code line is not a test.")
inl note_of_cells_target_required_19 () = Note ("CELLS-TARGET-REQUIRED", 19u32, "2026-11-07T17:07Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: USER CORRECTIONS (compiler's session): (1) 'the main is for code generation, why we're confounding with checks?': a file with only a main means code generation only. No test cells are added to test-free files (the ~391 plan is withdrawn); they only lose x's line-1 header. (2) 'why did we add crates to the spiral toml? shouldn't this be explicit in each test?': option A is REVERTED. The crates entries come back out of x's manifests, and each test's own line names the crates it uses (///> rust -d ...); the cell-budget cost is accepted, split a cell where needed. (3) Asked and confirmed: TESTS ONLY. Cells runs only test cells, each in its own ///> languages; a file's main is never compiled or run by Cells, only by the builds that generate code from it. The 'union of test targets' whole-file run (n17's decision 2) and the plan_tests whole-file check are DROPPED. The model is now: per-test targets only (NoTestTarget), no headers, no targets on static preludes, per-test crates, no whole-file step. Compiler's earlier option-A call was wrong; x implements the Cells side plus a census of any coverage that only the whole-file check gave.")
inl note_of_kit_eoie_dump_groom_1 () = Note ("KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:05Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "user 11-07 via main point 10. main is sweeping the disk for the old agile_eoie dump and will hand it over. DoD: every item in the dump is matched to a current item (same intent), imported as a new Planned item with all fields, or recorded as dropped with a reason, in one review list; nothing is imported twice. MILESTONES: parse the dump and match by title words 60 m; review list and imports 60 m. NEXT STEP: wait for main handoff of the dump path. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: match verdict union (Same, Import, Drop) with the reason as a record field.")
inl note_of_kit_eoie_dump_groom_2 () = Note ("KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM", 2u32, "2026-11-07T12:53Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit: handoff from main received. SOURCE: %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/drafts/kit/KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM (agile_eoie.md 72,904 B, EOIE consolidated pendencies dated 2026-10-22, recovered from the user work PC Downloads/eoie-consolidated; plus README, REPORT, universal_prompt_blocks.md, prompt-blocks/). Shape: 135 entries in 20 sections (46 P0, 36 P1, 53 P2; 100 Open and 35 Partial as of eoie a831bad), plus an overlap table with the old eoie backlog and appendices of container-era, resolved and product-specific drops. METHOD: each entry gets one verdict (Covered by an open item, Done in kit today with the verb as evidence, Import as a new item of 240 m or less, Drop with a reason) in a review list; the matching runs read-only now; imports carry the source path and entry IDs in their first note.")
inl note_of_kit_eoie_dump_groom_3 () = Note ("KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM", 3u32, "2026-11-07T13:04Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit: GROOM DONE. Review list (one verdict per entry, all 135 counted exactly once) archived at drafts/kit/KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM/review-20261107.json. Finding: main had groomed this same dump on 10-24 as KIT-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE (Done; about 20 notes, 14 new KIT items, bundle/container work parked in KIT-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA, Paused), so this pass re-checked that mapping against kit today. Verdicts: Covered 67 (30 open items, each got one note naming its entries and the source path), Done 27 (verb or source as evidence, 3 run live: grep . root, grep into head exits 0, job doctor), Drop 40 (all bundle-era, target KIT-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA), Import 1 (CENSUS-3 -> KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY, P2 120 m: jobs now run on Linux workers). Re-pointed from Done targets to open ones: AGILE-3 -> KIT-INIT-LEASE-FILE, CARGO-5 -> WARM-MIRROR-CACHES, FIXT-3 -> DELEG-JOB-PROFILE, KIT-NEXT-FITS-NOW entries -> KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION, EDIT-8 -> KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS (do append still lacks a tail-hash precondition). Mostly-done caveats: TABLE-2 has no N-way join, CLI-1 help examples unconfirmed, JOB-1 lease inheritance left to KIT-LEASE, TOOL-1 named profiles still open in KIT-MEMORY-FEATURES, EDIT-2 empty overwrite via do write unchecked. Done list: EDIT-1 (kit do <root> -); EDIT-2 (proxy fs-write; kit do edit); EDIT-4 (kit do (<<< >>> raw lines)); FS-2 (kit do write); FS-3 (kit grep <file-root>); READ-1 (kit grep . <needle>); READ-3 (kit grep | head); READ-5 (kit env check; agile lease; do append {clock}); READ-6 (kit standards); PROC-2 (kit job tail); PROC-5 (kit job stop; kit kill --tree); PROC-6 (kit job start); PROC-8 (proxy run --cwd --env --unset); JOB-1 (kit job start|status|wait|tail|stop); JOB-5 (kit job wait; do wait; table sequences; machine watch); EVID-3 (kit evidence refresh); EVID-7 (kit mirror check); TOOL-2 (kit ci parity; kit env check); TOOL-4 (kit job doctor); CENSUS-2 (kit crate gate --enforce --limit); TABLE-2 (kit table query --group --sum; table diff --key); TABLE-4 (kit table logs --values/--count-by --out); BNDL-10 (proxy hash-tree; proxy parallel-manifest); AGILE-7+CLI-5 (KIT-GENERIC-DRIFT (Done)); CLI-1+...")
inl note_of_kit_eoie_dump_groom_4 () = Note ("KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM", 4u32, "2026-11-07T14:15Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Done, part 1 of 3. EDIT-1 · File-backed payloads for writes · P0 · auth, ota, node, ssam, brz, scp, ge2e · Open / `proxy fs-write` carries the content in one argv cell, which fails with E2BIG/ARG_MAX on large files (171 KB to 1 MB was observed). Accept the payload from stdin, from a file (`--from-file`) or as chunks, with the same `--expect-hash` / `--replace-existing` / `--create-or-replace` preconditions. Also add `--old-file/--new-file` to `text-replace`, and a hash-checked copy from an arbitrary source file, so large or quote-heavy content never goes through argv. [done in kit today: kit do <root> -] || EDIT-2 · Reject accidental empty or truncating writes · P0 · sqlt, node, brz, ge2e · Partial / Writes have replaced nonempty sources with zero bytes when an upstream generator failed and still reported success. Refuse an empty payload over a nonempty target unless `--allow-empty` is given, print old and new sizes, and make every batch check its upstream status before it writes. *Now:* `fs-write --expect-nonempty` and `--replace-existing` exist (ge2e confirms they fail closed), but both are opt-in. [done in kit today: proxy fs-write; kit do edit] || EDIT-4 · Byte-exact payload semantics and line endings · P0 · ota, ge2e, brz, scp · Partial / Escape handling differed between surfaces: `text-replace` kept `\\n` literally, `literal-payload` unescaped `\\t`/`\\n` inside Rust source, and plan-IR batching rewrote `b' '` as `b32` while reporting success. Define one payload contract (raw bytes, or an explicit escape mode), preview the bytes in dry runs, and verify byte equality after the write. Exact matches also failed on CRLF vs LF: offer `--eol preserve|auto|lf|crlf` and keep each file's style when writing. *Now:* `do edit` documents `\\n \\t \\' \\\\` escapes, and LF anchors match CRLF files. [done in kit today: kit do (<<< >>> raw lines)] || FS-2 · Directory creation · P0 · sqlt, ssam, brz, erp, ge2e · Partial / Add `fs-mkdir` (parents, root-guarded, with a receipt) and a run option that pre-creates declared output directories. *Now:* `fs-write` creates parent directories; there is no standalone mkdir. [done in kit today: kit do write] || FS-3 · Single-path operations stay single-path · P1 · ota, scp, erp · Partial / Hashing, removing or copying one file under a broad root failed because the generic root scan walked unrelated siblings (a symlink next to the target). Validate the lexical target without traversing the rest of the root, and let hash/size work on any artifact path. *Now:* the old injection of `eoie/state` receipts into application roots is gone from the source; check the sibling-traversal case. [done in kit today: kit grep <file-root>] || READ-1 · Root sentinel and absolute roots · P1 · brz, ge2e, scp · Open / `fs-search`, `parallel-search`, `parallel-manifest` and `hash-tree` reject `.` as the relative scope, and `parallel-search` rejects an absolute root. Accept an explicit root sentinel and canonical absolute paths while staying sandboxed. [done in kit today: kit grep . <needle>] || READ-3 · Closed pipes are not crashes · P1 · ssam, brz, node · Open / Piping `fs-search` / `fs-read` / status output into `head` made the CLI panic with a broken pipe. Treat EPIPE as early termination, and add `--limit/--offset` pagination. *Now:* child-process streaming handles BrokenPipe, but no SIGPIPE/EPIPE handling was found on the CLI's own output path (Rust's `println!` panics there by default). [done in kit today: kit grep | head] || READ-5 · Clock, executable discovery, elapsed view · P2 · brz, ota · Open / Add a clock reading with a receipt, a bounded `which` that reports the resolved path, and an elapsed/remaining view of the active lease, so agents don't shell out to `date` / `command -v`. [done in kit today: kit env check; agile lease; do append {clock}] || READ-6 · Pattern assertions and forbidden-token scans · P2 · scp, ota · Open / Declarative assertions such as 'zero occurrences of the old marker' or 'exactly N call sites', and a scan for forbidden external-control tokens in product files (lane names, bundle version numbers), each with a receipt. [done in kit today: kit standards] || PROC-2 · Readable streams, surfaced · P0 · sqlt, ota, erp, ssam, scp · Partial / Persist both streams even on timeout (incrementally, with drained streaming so a chatty child cannot stall on backpressure), print the stderr path and a short excerpt in the console result, let `slice` follow it, surface the first diagnostic in summaries, and add `receipt-summary` / `receipt-tail` so a 300 KB stdout doesn't flood the caller. *Now:* the `command-capture` receipt file holds both stdout and stderr text after a normal exit; nothing is written if the call is killed. [done in kit today: kit job tail] || PROC-5 · Process-tree ownership on timeout · P1 · brz, ssam, sbin, ota · Partial / Timed-out wrappers left compiler children alive (one consumed 3.8 cores and made later benchmarks look 4× slower), and descendants holding pipes kept a capture from completing after its child exited. Every capture must own a process group or job object, kill and reap the whole tree, and report what it could not control; a process query by owner, cwd and target tells which transaction a running build belongs to. *Now:* jobs stop the tree and count survivors; `ps --tree` / `kill --tree` exist. `command-capture` and `proxy run` both go through the shared streaming runner, which starts the child in its own process group (Unix) or Job Object (Windows) and kills that group on timeout, but they report no survivor count. [done in kit today: kit job stop; kit kill --tree]")
inl note_of_kit_eoie_dump_groom_5 () = Note ("KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM", 5u32, "2026-11-07T14:15Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Done, part 2 of 3. PROC-6 · Receipt-first launch and commit-aware timeouts · P0 · sbin, ota · Open / Write a `running` receipt before spawning (command, source fingerprint, target directory), update a heartbeat while the child lives, and close it as `interrupted` if the call is killed, so a killed supervisor still leaves a typed record. On timeout, re-read the child's own transactional receipts before declaring failure (children had already committed when the wrapper reported a timeout). [done in kit today: kit job start] || PROC-8 · One capture contract · P2 · node, ota · Partial / Plain `command-capture` takes no cwd and inherits a fixed variable list, while `command-capture-env` takes cwd and one `;`-separated env cell; Cargo run from the wrong cwd silently used the registry instead of the vendored config. Unify capture around explicit `--cwd`, repeatable `--env`/`--unset`, an env file, typed argument vectors without a shell round-trip, platform env-key semantics and input-file preconditions. *Now:* `proxy run --cwd --env --unset` and `json:[...]` matrix cells exist. [done in kit today: proxy run --cwd --env --unset] || JOB-1 · Lease-bound detached jobs · P0 · ota, sbin, ge2e, node, erp, scp · Partial / Start/status/stop/tail/wait for long builds, suites and services, with fully detached stdio (inherited descriptors kept tool calls open), persisted PID, start clock, streams, readiness probe and final exit; estimate a job's budget before starting it and refuse anything that cannot finish before the cutoff. *Now:* `eoie job start|status|wait|list|tail`, with wall-clock timeout and tree stop, reaper-proof. Still missing: inheriting the active lease (refuse new jobs past the semantic cutoff, stop before the hard deadline), explicit `job stop`, readiness probes for services, and a typed `yielded_by_closeout` state. [done in kit today: kit job start|status|wait|tail|stop] || JOB-5 · Polling with typed progress · P2 · sbin, ge2e · Partial / An `await-receipt` / checkpoint poll that returns attempted / passed / skipped / failed / committed counts plus lease remaining, instead of shell sleep loops. Digest a long run's progress log into per-phase quantiles and the slowest items, and assert that the run's input count matches the requested selection (a probe ignored its selected files and ran over the whole root). *Now:* `job wait` and `do wait <file> ~ re` exist. [done in kit today: kit job wait; do wait; table sequences; machine watch] || EVID-3 · Evidence refresh, audit and custody · P2 · brz · Open / `evidence-ref-audit` (fail closeout on hash/size drift), `evidence-adopt`/`refresh` that keeps semantic refs and adds missing historical rows, and an atomic custody refresh. `release-closeout` validated before refreshing the references it had just changed; compute one future-state plan and apply it atomically. [done in kit today: kit evidence refresh] || EVID-7 · Mirror and derived-file drift gates · P1 · ota, ssam, ge2e · Open / Assert that mirrored or generated files still match their source (`assert-mirror <owner> <mirror>`), or regenerate them from the canonical source with a hash receipt as a declared gate preflight. Write paired canonical and current outputs in one transaction, so aliases cannot drift apart. [done in kit today: kit mirror check] || TOOL-2 · Toolchain adoption with probes · P1 · ssam, brz, ge2e, scp · Partial / Record a candidate toolchain's identities, run a required probe matrix (rows can require current-pass and historical-fail), and mark compatibility; locate the pinned toolchain from identity, not absolute session paths; report health per capability (SDK usable / compiler attested / smoke failing) with history; ship a machine-readable executable index in tool bundles (self-contained vs needs a runtime). *Now:* `proxy toolchain`, `portable-toolchain`, `binary-install` exist. [done in kit today: kit ci parity; kit env check] || TOOL-4 · Helper and daemon lifecycle · P2 · ssam, brz · Open / Bind helper/daemon identity to binary hash, SDK and profile; reap stale daemons; avoid process-global fixed sockets; restore a hashed runtime closure; and own lifecycle cleanup by toolchain identity, with a typed leak receipt. [done in kit today: kit job doctor] || CENSUS-2 · Per-crate structural audit · P1 · ge2e, ssam, brz, ota · Partial / Cargo-metadata-derived per-crate aggregate LOC (manifest + sources), max file LOC, totals, layout violations (only `Cargo.toml` + `src/`) and ceiling violations as canonical receipts, honoring custom `[lib]`/`[[bin]]` paths, with a report-only mode that still writes the receipt when a ceiling is violated, and thresholds taken from the project's policy (the built-in gate allowed about 2,000 lines per crate where the project required 1,000). `source-stats` and `source-topology` disagreed (empty `cargo_crate_max` with an aggregate-scale line count; 36 crates vs 0): unify them. *Now:* `eoie lines` covers per-file counts. [done in kit today: kit crate gate --enforce --limit] || TABLE-2 · Joins and aggregation · P2 · ota, ge2e · Partial / Join two tables by key (including very large key sets), group and summarize (weighted sums, sums for ratings), compare a previous and a current table by key and metric to rank deficits and trends, emit the unmatched queue, and work across snapshots with a receipt. *Now:* group and count exist. [done in kit today: kit table query --group --sum; table diff --key] || TABLE-4 · Log-to-inventory reducer · P2 · ge2e · Open / Extract keys from logs with a regex into an inventory table with a receipt. [done in kit today: kit table logs --values/--count-by --out]")
inl note_of_kit_eoie_dump_groom_6 () = Note ("KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM", 6u32, "2026-11-07T14:15Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Done, part 3 of 3. BNDL-10 · Whole-root SHA-256 manifest · P1 · brz, ge2e, scp, ota · Partial / Materialize and verify a self-excluding file manifest with a declared schema and fingerprint (SHA-256 or FNV), declarative include patterns and a parity check after writing. `parallel-manifest apply` wrote a readiness program instead of a manifest and nearly replaced `MANIFEST.tsv`: give the two jobs distinct names and refuse to write a non-TSV payload to a manifest path. *Now:* `parallel-manifest` and `hash-tree` exist (see READ-1). [done in kit today: proxy hash-tree; proxy parallel-manifest] || AGILE-7 · Never overwrite a foreign schema · P0 · ota · Open / `write-reports` replaced a project's own ratings and agile tables with EOIE-internal dimensions; the project had to restore them by hand. Merge into the existing schema or refuse when the target file does not belong to the writer. [done in kit today: KIT-GENERIC-DRIFT (Done)] || CLI-5 · EOIE's own gates are profile-aware · P0 · ota, sqlt, scp, erp, sbin · Open / `release-preflight`, `source-continuity`, `bundle-hygiene`, `round-gauge`, `crate-split-plan`, `zip-mode-audit`, `rating-delta-gate`, `staged-binary-provenance-gate`, `verify-tool` and the absorption index assumed EOIE's own layout (`eoie-core`, multi-workspace roots, C++ references) and reported false blockers on application workspaces. Derive required gates from a workspace profile or manifest, and report checks that do not apply as `not_applicable` instead of `blocked`. [done in kit today: KIT-GENERIC-DRIFT (Done)] || CLI-1 · Help parity and self-consistency · P0 · node, sbin, ssam, brz, erp, ge2e, ota · Partial / Every advertised capability has help with its exact signature, accepted enum values (`text-replace` modes, backend names), argument grammar (the `agile begin` title rule of 3 to 7 words, `fs-list`'s second argument), preconditions (destination must exist or be absent; `command-capture` uses the root as cwd) and a minimal example, derived from the dispatch schema. A release proves that source, help and dispatch agree (a packaged binary lacked commands present in its source), and `workspace-state` accepts the same roots `status` discovers. Old positional forms (`proxy run <cwd> <program>`) get a translation to the current form. *Now:* per-capability usage lines exist; examples, preconditions and the self-check do not. [done in kit today: kit help [verb|--json|--schema]; kit guide] || CLI-3 · Discover existing primitives first · P2 · ota · Open / Projects rebuilt tools the bundle already had. An inventory command that maps a need to existing capabilities would help, and the same machine-readable capability list would let a launcher pick the most capable of several installed EOIE builds instead of agents comparing two builds' help output by hand. Low priority. [done in kit today: kit help [verb|--json|--schema]; kit guide]")
inl note_of_fiscal_regex_dup_map_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-REGEX-DUP-MAP", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:06Z", "fiscal", "11-07 fiscal (main's relay of the user's 11-07 direction: 'everything should reuse from everything somehow', even via a temporary polyglot-style ouroboros; main owns regex). FOUND by reading both sides: lib xsd_regex (1052 lines) and the regex repo implement the SAME layers separately, logic not lines: (a) Brzozowski engine: ordering + smart constructors + nullability + derive (xsd_regex rx_compare/seq/alt/star/nullability_of/derive) vs regex syntax/nullable/derivative; (b) the XSD dialect parser (escapes, classes, \\p{Is<Block>}, quantifiers) vs regex src/xsd.spi; (c) DFA / table / example construction vs regex dfa/minimal/table. The regex repo adds laws, certificates, lazy/partial DFAs and an agreement oracle on top. Constraint: regex depends on spiral lib (packageDir ../deps/spiral/lib), so lib cannot import regex; xsd_regex's consumers are in lib (xsd_emit 31 refs, xpath_eval 15) plus 19 generated units and fiscal corpus/corpus_checks. DoD: a layer-by-layer map (each xsd_regex definition -> its regex counterpart or 'none', lines per layer), the consumer API census (which xsd_regex names each consumer uses), and a proposal sent to main. PROPOSAL to measure: regex's core engine (charset, syntax, nullable, derivative, dfa, minimal, xsd parser) becomes the canonical copy IN lib (REGEX-SPIRAL-DELETION's option ii), xsd_regex shrinks to an adapter over it with its API unchanged, and the regex repo keeps its research layer (laws, certificates, agreement, lazy, bench) importing that lib core. Ouroboros meanwhile: regex's agreement.spi imports lib xsd_regex and compares both engines on fiscal's pinned pattern corpus until the switch. TYPEFEATURES: none new (a map). Related: REGEX-XSD (parity with lib xsd_regex), REGEX-SPIRAL-DELETION phase B, FISCAL-REGEX-LIB-ADAPTER.")
inl note_of_fiscal_regex_dup_map_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-REGEX-DUP-MAP", 2u32, "2026-11-07T11:07Z", "fiscal", "11-07 main DECIDED option (ii) as regex's owner: regex's core engine (charset, syntax, nullable, derivative, dfa, minimal, the XSD parser) becomes the canonical copy IN lib; xsd_regex an adapter with its API unchanged; regex keeps laws/certificates/agreement/lazy/bench on top of the lib core. Gate before the switch: regex agreement.spi vs lib xsd_regex on fiscal's pinned corpus (the ouroboros). main takes the regex side once this map lists the definition pairs.")
inl note_of_fiscal_regex_dup_map_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-REGEX-DUP-MAP", 3u32, "2026-11-07T11:08Z", "fiscal", "NEXT: dump every top-level definition with its signature on both sides (lib xsd_regex; regex src charset/syntax/nullable/derivative/dfa/minimal/xsd/table/language), pair them by role, and list the consumer API (what xsd_emit, xpath_eval, generated units and fiscal corpus call).")
inl note_of_fiscal_regex_dup_map_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-REGEX-DUP-MAP", 4u32, "2026-11-07T11:10Z", "fiscal", "11-07 MAP DONE (full text: kit drafts fiscal\\FISCAL-REGEX-DUP-MAP\\map.txt). lib xsd_regex 5963371C vs regex src, by layer: (1) term + ordering: rx <-> syntax re (re is a superset: adds Not / And / an upper type), rx_compare family <-> order_re family, ranges_compare <-> charset order_ranges; (2) char sets: list (u32*u32) <-> char_set normal (in_ranges, insert_range, gaps_from, complement_ranges <-> contains_in, normalize, gaps, complement); (3) smart constructors: seq / alt (leaves, insert, sort) / star / counted <-> syntax seq / alt_leaves / alt_of / star / repeat; (4) nullability with evidence: SAME design and names (accepts_empty, rejects_empty, nullability_of, accept_reason, reject_reason, nullability_text); (5) derivatives: derive_by (as_written | similar) <-> derivative derive; matches_from / pattern_matches <-> derive_word + matches; longest_match has NO counterpart; (6) XSD parser: compile_pattern + invalid_from <-> parse_xsd with typed refusals; parse_alt/seq/atom/quant, class_items, escape_*, block_range/property_* <-> alternation/branch/atom/quantify, class_*, escape*, block union (regex 7 named blocks, lib 4); class_end/escape_end/brace_end have none; (7) examples: example_with family <-> agreement shortest_accepted (different method; byte-identical examples required); (8) DFA: interval_starts/range_points, build_states, dfa_of_pattern_within (4096 cap) <-> dfa points_of/classes_of, build_rows, build_within (same 4096 cap); dfa_of_values (value-set DFA) <-> none directly (minimal.spi moore); (9) emitted string tables: dfa_table_text + table_matches stay in the adapter byte-for-byte (19 generated units, 61 calls) <-> table.spi in-memory table; (10) oracle/census (all_disagreements, census_of, ...) is research, proposed to move to regex agreement / fiscal. CONSUMER API: generated units table_matches only; xsd_emit dfa_state, dfa_move, pattern_matches, dfa_table_text, example_with, repeated, table_matches, compile_pattern, dfa_of_pattern(_within), dfa_of_values, is_xsd_pattern, rx; xpath_eval rx, class_end, compile_pattern, escape_end, longest_match, brace_end, matches_from, nullable; fiscal corpus_checks the oracle layer. GAPS the canonical core needs first: longest_match, syntax-skipping positions, block list parity, value-set DFA, example_with's choice rule. Sent to main.\nDoD: met (layer-by-layer map, consumer API census, proposal decided by main as option ii).\nPROOF: green, read from live sources (lib xsd_regex 5963371C, regex src at today's tree); map.txt in the drafts store.")
inl note_of_cells_elixir_ratchet_1 () = Note ("CELLS-ELIXIR-RATCHET", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:06Z", "x", "2026-11-07 07:06 x: user 11-07 via main: 'we are still writing Elixir code'. MEASURED from git: apps/cells/lib .ex went 10,944 lines/45 files (faf422c9, 11-04) -> 11,123 (c0e77c3f, 11-05) -> 11,341/46 (0dd57883, 11-07), +397 in 3 days, while spi went 4,300 -> 4,318 (+18). The new file is x's own temp_sweep.ex (11-06). DIAGNOSIS: (a) every IO-touching fix lands in .ex because the typed process layer (CELLS-TYPED-UNITS) doesn't exist yet, so IO has no Spiral home; (b) a Spiral domain function costs 4 edits (spi module, main.spi export, domain.ex wrapper, the Elixir caller) plus a Gleam rebuild, against 1 Elixir edit; (c) no gate measures it, so the drift is invisible. DoD: (1) cells_land_window computes .ex lines and files for the base commit (derived from git, never stored) and for the result, and refuses growth unless the label is a CELLS-PORT-* or CELLS-ELIXIR-HEAL item; (2) a test with a fixture tree; (3) the drift prints as one line in the close report.")
inl note_of_cells_elixir_ratchet_2 () = Note ("CELLS-ELIXIR-RATCHET", 2u32, "2026-11-07T18:23Z", "x", "2026-11-07 14:23 x: IMPLEMENTED (unstaged), cells_land_window.ps1 C4756A46BB99. After the applier runs, it measures the .ex lines of the landed files (before = the undo copy, 0 for a created file; after = the result) and prints one line, 'elixir drift: .ex lines B -> A (delta), new .ex files N'. A land that grows .ex lines or creates a .ex file is refused and undone unless its label is CELLS-PORT-* or CELLS-ELIXIR-HEAL*. DESIGN NOTE vs the DoD's 'base commit': I measure the land's own delta, not tree-vs-HEAD, so another lane's unstaged Elixir WIP can never fail or excuse this land. DoD (2): a -RatchetSelfTest parameter set runs 6 fixture cases (shrink, grow, new file, port and heal exemptions, no .ex): 6/6 ok, parse 0. Context: the tree's apps/cells/lib .ex is 11,292 lines / 46 files vs HEAD 11,319 / 46 (today's skip/default removals shrank it). Next: the first real land through it reports its drift line.")
inl note_of_cells_elixir_ratchet_3 () = Note ("CELLS-ELIXIR-RATCHET", 3u32, "2026-11-08T00:08Z", "x-b7595ac3", "2026-11-07 20:07 x: DoD (3) met. The first real land through the ratcheted window (CELLS-TESTS-ONLY-1107) printed 'elixir drift: .ex lines 5249 -> 5249 (0), new .ex files 0' on both the node window and the box close. It reads 0 because the changes were already in the tree and the applier was a no-op; measured against HEAD, the same land is +52/-110 .ex lines (net -58) across 9 files. Lesson: a no-op applier makes the window's drift line meaningless, so the next lands should apply from a draft tree (slice 2 of CELLS-PORT-RESULT-UNION is staged that way). The window runs the applier twice (main had to make theirs idempotent); that's fine for draft copies.")
inl note_of_cells_elixir_ratchet_4 () = Note ("CELLS-ELIXIR-RATCHET", 4u32, "2026-11-08T00:09Z", "x-b7595ac3", "2026-11-07 20:1x x: Evidence: cells_land_window.ps1 C4756A46BB99 (spi\\, landed in CELLS-TESTS-ONLY-1107). -RatchetSelfTest 6/6 (shrink, grow, new file, port and heal exemptions, no .ex); the node window x-cells-landwin and the box close both printed 'elixir drift: .ex lines 5249 -> 5249 (0), new .ex files 0', box undo cells-20261107-200705.")
inl note_of_cells_elixir_heal_1106_1 () = Note ("CELLS-ELIXIR-HEAL-1106", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:06Z", "x", "2026-11-07 07:06 x: heal half of the user's 11-07 point 1. The pure decisions in the +397 lines move into spi modules with string exports (the CELLS-PORT-RESULT-UNION pattern, landed 11-06): which run dirs are older than the cutoff, how verdict rows count (timed_out vs failed), and the stable source-stats ordering for the mount key. The file IO stays in Elixir until CELLS-TYPED-UNITS. DoD: .ex lines below 11,123 (the 11-05 base), all cells tests unchanged.")
inl note_of_cells_elixir_heal_1106_2 () = Note ("CELLS-ELIXIR-HEAL-1106", 2u32, "2026-11-08T01:06Z", "x-b7595ac3", "2026-11-07 21:05 x: STEP 1 LANDED on the box (unstaged): Notebook.counts (22 lines of status counting) now calls Spiral (new spi\\verdicts.spi ED5E94118282: counts_wire over tab rows status/memo/no_agree; 5 gleam tests). Evidence: node window x-heal-win (job-1794099098789, xcells3, pack61b) closed green, 54 passed (notebook_timeout, notebook_report incl. memo_hits and no_agree_reasons, notebook); box closed by -ProvenLog, 7 files byte for byte, undo cells-20261107-210529. Drift: .ex 1962 -> 1965 (+3) under this exempt label. counts lost 9 lines, but the two Domain wrappers (verdict_counts, void_text) cost 10 and the CLI 2: the per-export glue cost the ratchet diagnosis named. NOT MET: the DoD target. apps/cells/lib .ex is 11,266 vs the 11,123 base; temp_sweep.ex is mostly IO (ls/stat/rm_rf), so porting it adds glue. NEXT STEP: cut the per-wrapper cost (one generic Domain.call(name, args) instead of a 4-line def per export would save about 4 lines x 40 wrappers), then port the mount source digest.")
inl note_of_cells_elixir_heal_1106_3 () = Note ("CELLS-ELIXIR-HEAL-1106", 3u32, "2026-11-08T01:57Z", "x-b7595ac3", "2026-11-07 21:56 x: DoD MET. apps/cells/lib .ex is now 11,104 lines in 46 files, below the 11,123 base (11-05). Step 2: Spiral.Cells.Domain's 36 pass-through wrappers (each 'def f(args) do ensure!(); :spiral_cells@domain.f(args) end') collapse into one @passthrough [name: arity] list and one generated def, via Macro.generate_arguments; the 8 wrappers that convert a result stay hand-written. domain.ex 303 -> 141 (-162); names and arities are unchanged, so every caller and capture still resolves. Evidence: node window x-heal2-win (job-1794100336114, xcells3, pack61b) ran 97/100; the 3 reds are document_test's mirror gaps (alphabet, dice, the mixed-F# export), so it undid. The box window then closed GREEN, 17 passed (notebook_timeout, manifest, cell_budget, result_union): domain.ex 8FF3E2955ED3, undo cells-20261107-215436. This also cuts the glue cost of every future port to one list entry.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_tag_one_source_1 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-TAG-ONE-SOURCE", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:06Z", "x", "2026-11-07 07:06 x: user 11-07 point 5 ('hardcoded numbers, typefeatureset?'). JUDGMENT after reading iggy.spi (49 numeric-literal lines): most literals are honest encoding constants (u32/u64 byte shifts 24/16/8, 4-byte length prefixes), and naming them would add nothing. The antipattern is the TAGS: body_tag maps each event_body case to a u8, and body_at maps the same u8s back, as two hand tables that must agree. Today only the frozen_bytes tests catch drift. Do NOT derive tags from case order (reflection union_cases_fold), because a reorder would silently change the protocol. FIX: one typed table of (tag, case) pairs used by both directions; encode is a lookup by case name (reflection::case_name), decode a lookup by tag; a test proves the table covers every case exactly once. kit builds the org-wide detector; this is the iggy heal.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_tag_one_source_2 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-TAG-ONE-SOURCE", 2u32, "2026-11-07T15:55Z", "x", "2026-11-07 11:54 x groom: DoD: iggy's tag table has one source (lib iggy.spi); the x topics/sim_iggy drafts consume it with no duplicated literal numbers, landed via lib_land_window with a base-vs-draft control. Next: diff the tag literals in kit drafts x\\X-SOTA-MODEL topics.spi/sim_iggy.spi against lib iggy.spi and list each duplicate. TypeFeatures: a closed union for the tag names, or nameof over the existing union, instead of numeric literals.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_tag_one_source_3 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-TAG-ONE-SOURCE", 3u32, "2026-11-07T18:50Z", "x", "2026-11-07 14:50 x: DRAFT in the kit store, drafts\\spiral\\LIB-IGGY-TAG-ONE-SOURCE\\iggy.spi, base lib 8D78ECFBD635. body_tag = real_core::union_tag (declaration order is the one source; the frozen wire bytes are unchanged), body_at = reader_at over one ordered list of the 10 readers (no numeric literals left outside tests), plus a new test pinning every case's tag 0..9. Gate 1 (job-1794075493223-x-iggy1, node xlib2, pack61): base control 7/7; draft 4/8, failing only on GLEAM ('error: Unknown module', from am'::try_item over an array of closures); the new tag test passed. Fix: list + recursive reader_at, no array. Draft DFA41438240E, re-gate pending once node load drops (100% now). Also to fix before landing: the draft's Document round trip differed by 1 line (294 vs 295), so make the source canonical.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_tag_one_source_4 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-TAG-ONE-SOURCE", 4u32, "2026-11-07T20:05Z", "x-b7595ac3", "2026-11-07 16:05 x: draft made canonical: a blank line was missing between the tag-order test and the next ///> rust line; the Document round trip is now 300 vs 300 lines, 0 differing (box, local mix run). Draft hash 12E34AF4E5CC. Next: lib gate (control + draft) on the node once load allows; node and box were both at 100% at 16:00.")
inl note_of_lib_iggy_tag_one_source_5 () = Note ("LIB-IGGY-TAG-ONE-SOURCE", 5u32, "2026-11-08T00:42Z", "x-b7595ac3", "2026-11-07 20:42 x: LANDED in lib (unstaged). Evidence: lib_gate on the node (job-1794097449320 x-iggy-gate, xlib2, pack61b 65DEE79CF2CF8B9F): control 7/7, draft 8/8 (the new test pins tags 0..9), 0 type errors each. The node lib_land_window (x-iggy-land3) passed every iggy check (round trip, 0 consumer signatures changed, apps/spiral compiles, type probe PASS, 0 respell drift) but undid on document_test 23/26: the 3 failures are mirror-only gaps (alphabet and dice repos absent, no runner exe), the same as the Cells gate's. The box lib_land_window then closed GREEN with document_test 26/26: iggy.spi 8D78ECFBD635 -> 12E34AF4E5CC, lib digest 55A730E9E1DBAA36, undo 20261107-203922. Wire bytes unchanged: body_tag = real_core::union_tag (declaration order is the one source), and body_at decodes through reader_at over one ordered list of readers.")
inl note_of_conduct_backends_list_1 () = Note ("CONDUCT-BACKENDS-LIST", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:13Z", "main", "User 11-07: create a task to add all the other languages we target for backends in the code of conduct text, keeping the tone. Caution from 11-06 (MD-LINE-LIMIT-120): i574n.github CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md is the Contributor Covenant verbatim, kept so GitHub recognizes it, so the addition goes in an org-specific section above or below the Covenant body, never inside it. DoD: the backends (Rust, Gleam, F#, Zig, C, CUDA/Python, Lua, TypeScript, Pascal, WGSL, Lean, Kotlin, ...; take the list from the compiler backend_switch keys) named in the conduct file in the existing voice; GitHub community profile still recognizes it. Next step: list the backends from the compiler, then draft the paragraph.")
inl note_of_conduct_backends_list_2 () = Note ("CONDUCT-BACKENDS-LIST", 2u32, "2026-11-07T21:11Z", "main", "main 11-07 17:3x DONE (unstaged, i574n.github/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md). The file is the custom 'Draconian Code of Conduct' with per-language sections, NOT the Contributor Covenant the 11-07 caution assumed, so the additions go in as sections in its own voice.\n- Added 10 sections between Rust and Spiral for the compiler backends the file lacked: Gleam, Zig, C, Lua, Pascal, WGSL, Lean, Kotlin, Swift, eBPF (the backend list is from the compiler's `| \"<Backend>\"` cases: C, Ebpf, Fsharp, Gleam, Kotlin, Lean, Lua, Pascal, Python, Rust, Swift, TypeScript, Wgsl, Zig). Each is two paragraphs of grand praise, then the flaw, then the case for generating rather than hand-writing.\n- TOC entries added; C links to #c-1 because C# already owns #c.\n- The Spiral section's outdated 'TypeScript and Rust through F#'s Fable' now lists the native backends.")
inl note_of_conduct_backends_list_3 () = Note ("CONDUCT-BACKENDS-LIST", 3u32, "2026-11-07T21:16Z", "main", "proof: the 10 headings exist once each and their TOC anchors resolve (C -> #c-1, since C# owns #c); the Spiral sentence lists the 14 native backends. A docs-only change.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_grad_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD", 1u32, "2026-11-07T14:50Z", "main", "Child of TRANSFORMER-TRAIN. TritCritNet's C++ had hand-written backprop through attention and FFN plus layernorm (no rmsnorm or RoPE); read it for structure, test everything fresh. DoD: per-op gradient checks (relative error under 1e-4 in fp32 on random inputs), whole-model gradients equal to PyTorch autograd on the smallest llama2.c config within tolerance on a fixed batch, as test blocks on Rust and C. The gradient engine is lib code shared with TIBIA-GRADIENT-POLICY and the ML-LABS Tensor Lab, not transformer-private.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_grad_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD", 2u32, "2026-11-07T14:56Z", "tibia", "11-07 tibia groom (lint markers): child of TRANSFORMER-TRAIN. Next step: split into steps of 120 min or less when started: (1) backward for matmul and rmsnorm with finite-difference golden tests in lib; (2) softmax, attention and SwiGLU backward; (3) a PyTorch autograd cross-check script kept outside the repos (no .py in repos), its numbers pinned as goldens. TypeFeatures: each backward shares its forward's sized types (the gradient of a sized m n tensor is a sized m n tensor), so shape errors are compile errors.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_grad_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD", 3u32, "2026-11-07T18:11Z", "tibia", "11-07 14:5x tibia: STEP 1 GREEN in draft, landing in progress. New lib module lib/spiral/f32_grads.spi (draft FC831119F4B7 in kit store drafts\\spiral\\TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD\\lib): matmul_backward (dx += W^T dout, dw += dout x^T, accumulating so shared inputs sum their paths) and rmsnorm_backward (dw_i += dout_i s x_i; dx_k += s w_k dout_k - (s^3/n) x_k sum_i dout_i w_i x_i, s = 1/sqrt(mean x^2 + 1e-5), the same epsilon as f32_kernels.rmsnorm), on f32_kernels' floats and helpers. Tests (c and rust) via Cells on a scratch package (box, 370 s): matmul gradients equal central differences (h 0.01) within 0.01 + 1 pct, the input gradient equals the hand-computed W^T dout (0.25, 4.5, -6.375), rmsnorm gradients equal central differences (h 0.001) for x and w: 3/3 passed on both backends. lib_land_window dry run: the draft round-trips as document_test requires; the -Apply land is running. Next: steps 2 (softmax, attention, SwiGLU backward) and 3 (the PyTorch cross-check script kept outside the repos).")
inl note_of_transformer_train_grad_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD", 4u32, "2026-11-07T18:12Z", "tibia", "11-07 15:1x tibia: the lib land was UNDONE cleanly (lib_land_window, label transformer-train-grad; undo dir 20261107-140316): round trip ok, type probe PASS (3 tests typed), respell drift 0, but the close failed on document_test exit 2. The one failing test is NOT this module: \"dice's Spiral sources ... write back byte for byte\" (document_test.exs:527) expects `sixth_power_sequence` defined at top level in dice, which dice no longer has. Reported to main. Blocked on: that dice document_test red. Resume when: document_test passes again; then rerun the same -Apply (draft FC831119F4B7 unchanged).")
inl note_of_transformer_train_grad_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD", 5u32, "2026-11-07T18:26Z", "tibia", "11-07 15:3x tibia: STEP 1 LANDED in lib, closed green (lib_land_window retry after main fixed the dice document_test): lib/spiral/f32_grads.spi FC831119F4B7 (new file, unstaged in spiral), document_test 26 passed, type probe PASS (3 tests typed), respell drift 0, lib digest 221BB64A90BD30B0, undo lib-land-undo\\20261107-141814. STEP 2 drafted (kit store pkg\\f32_grads.spi): softmax_backward (dx += y (dy - <dy, y>)) and swiglu_backward (db += dh silu(a); da += dh b silu'(a), silu' = s (1 + a (1 - s))), with central-difference tests; the three loops split into step + loop so the cell budget is 0 (the landed step-1 headers were multi-line and over budget, fixed in this draft). Cells run of all 5 tests on the box is in progress; then a land with -BaseHash FC831119F4B7.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_grad_6 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD", 6u32, "2026-11-07T19:02Z", "tibia", "11-07 tibia: STEP 2 LANDED in lib: spiral/lib/spiral/f32_grads.spi 341EEAC426C2 (unstaged; lib_land_window undo 20261107-143927). It adds matmul_backward (dx += W^T dout, dW += dout x^T, via f32_kernels::axpy), rmsnorm_backward (s = 1/rms, c = s^3/n * sum(dout*w*x); dx_i += s*w_i*dout_i - c*x_i; dw_i += dout_i*s*x_i), softmax_backward (dx += y*(dy - <dy,y>)), and swiglu_backward (db += dh*silu(a); da += dh*b*silu'(a)). Five tests are green on c and rust: each backward against central finite differences (h = 0.01 / 0.001, tolerance 0.01 + 1% relative), plus an exact transposed-product golden for dx. NEXT STEP: step 2b is attention backward (scores = QK^T/sqrt(d), causal mask, softmax_backward reused, then dV = P^T dO, dP = dO V^T, dQ/dK from dS), with a finite-difference test on a 3-token x 2-dim head. Step 3 is an outside-the-repos PyTorch cross-check of a full block's gradients on fixed weights.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_grad_7 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD", 7u32, "2026-11-07T20:47Z", "tibia", "11-07 16:4x tibia: STEP 2b LANDED: attention backward added to spiral/lib/spiral/f32_grads.spi, now 54A72E792BC0 (was 341EEAC426C2; lib_land_window green: round trip, document_test 26 passed, type probe 7 tests typed / 0 errors, respell_drift 0; lib digest 55DC50F3A77198A0; undo 20261107-160724; unstaged). New: records head {q; k; v; d}, grads {dq; dk; dv} and query_row {p; dp; inner; i}; the forward pass head_probs / attention (causal, single head, scores scaled by 1/sqrt d, softmax over j <= i, o_i = sum_j p_ij v_j); and attention_backward (dV_j += p_ij dO_i; dP_ij = dO_i . v_j; dS_ij = p_ij (dP_ij - <dP_i, p_i>); dQ_i += dS_ij k_j / sqrt d; dK_j += dS_ij q_i / sqrt d). Two new tests, green on c and rust (node job-1794080386667-tibia-grads2-cells, 14/14 rows with the earlier five): causal_attention_backward_matches_central_differences (3 tokens x 2 dims, every q/k/v entry, h = 0.001), and one_token_attention_passes_the_output_gradient_straight_to_its_value (exact: dv = dout, dq = dk = 0, since p = 1 makes dS vanish). Two box faults on the way, both fixed: one definition over the cell budget (split into score_grad and query_key_step), and a missing final newline that failed the round trip. NEXT STEP: step 3, an outside-the-repos PyTorch cross-check of one full block's gradients (rmsnorm -> attention -> rmsnorm -> SwiGLU MLP) on fixed weights, using these lib ops.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_grad_8 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD", 8u32, "2026-11-07T21:02Z", "tibia", "11-07 17:1x tibia: STEP 3 DONE, the one-block PyTorch cross-check MATCHES. A Spiral program built only from the landed lib ops (f32_grads 54A72E792BC0 plus f32_kernels) runs one llama-style block: RMSNorm, then q/k/v projections, causal single-head attention, Wo plus residual, RMSNorm, SwiGLU MLP (W1/W3 -> silu * gate -> W2) plus residual. 3 tokens, dim 4, hidden 6; weights from the integer formula ((37 i + 11 seed) mod 17 - 8) / 10 (RMSNorm weights offset by 1); loss = sum(dout * y). It prints the loss and all 156 gradients (dx, wa, wq, wk, wv, wo, wf, w1, w3, w2). Compiled with the C backend (node pack61b, core 65DEE79CF2CF, gcc -O2, no fast-math; job-1794084759600-tibia-block-grads, 157 lines, grads.tsv A14E27177FD4). The independent side is PyTorch 2.15.0+cpu autograd on the same block (scratch block_torch.py outside the repos, run on europa in ~/venvs/torch). RESULT: 157 of 157 values compared, loss -0.58075285 vs -0.580753, worst error 0.002 of the tolerance (abs 1e-4 + rel 1e-3, so about 2e-7 absolute), 0 over tolerance; 153 of 156 gradients are larger than 1e-3, so the check is not vacuous. Scratch block program: kit drafts TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD\\block\\block.spi. NEXT STEP (step 4, at most 120 min): lib backward for the three ops still missing (embedding row scatter-add, RoPE as the inverse rotation, and cross-entropy as softmax minus one-hot), with finite-difference tests on c and rust, landed. Then step 5: whole-model gradients on stories260K against PyTorch on a fixed batch.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_grad_9 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD", 9u32, "2026-11-07T21:54Z", "tibia", "11-07 17:5x tibia: STEP 5a DONE, the whole-model PyTorch reference, outside the repos (scratch model_torch.py; europa ~/tibia-block, venv ~/venvs/torch with torch 2.15.0+cpu). It reads llama2.c's legacy checkpoint (7-int header; skips freq_cis) and runs the full forward: embedding, then per layer RMSNorm, q/k/v, RoPE on q (dim) and k (kv_dim) with llama2.c's interleaved pairs and i % head_size frequencies, causal multi-head attention with GQA (head -> kv head = head / (heads / kv_heads)), Wo + residual, RMSNorm, SwiGLU + residual; then final RMSNorm, shared classifier, summed cross-entropy. It dumps per-tensor gradient fingerprints (sum, sum of squares, and 16 entries at index 7919 k mod size), 199 rows. Pinned run: stories260K.bin (sha256 B0A507E7...2696, matching oracle.spi's pin; dim 64, hidden 172, 5 layers, 8 heads / 4 kv heads, head size 8, vocab 512, shared classifier), tokens 1,403,407,261,378,410,274,384,259 (8 predictions); loss 50.8682938; model_ref.tsv on europa. NEXT STEP 5b (at most 120 min): a Spiral program (scratch, like block.spi) doing the same forward with saved activations and the backward through the lib ops (embed_backward, rope_backward, attention_backward per head with GQA accumulation into the shared kv head, cross_entropy_backward), printing the same fingerprints; then 5c compares. Step 4's lib ops wait on their rust rerun (job-1794087613207-tibia-grads3b-cells; c was 10/10 and the rust rows were VOID at the 300 s cell cap).")
inl note_of_transformer_train_grad_10 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD", 10u32, "2026-11-07T22:37Z", "tibia", "11-07 18:4x tibia: STEP 5b+5c DONE, WHOLE-MODEL GRADIENTS MATCH PYTORCH on stories260K. Spiral program (scratch kit drafts TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD\\model\\model.spi 4694640CC98A, built only from lib ops: f32_kernels plus f32_grads with the step-4 additions, draft 7862EA5B6C2D). It maps the checkpoint, runs the full forward (embedding; 5 layers of RMSNorm, q/k/v, RoPE, 8-head causal attention over 4 kv heads (GQA), Wo + residual, RMSNorm, SwiGLU + residual; final RMSNorm; shared classifier; summed cross-entropy over 8 predictions), stashes each layer's input, and backpropagates layer by layer (per-head attention_backward with the dK/dV of a shared kv head accumulated over its 2 query heads; rope_backward on dq and dk; cross_entropy_backward; matmul_backward into the shared embedding for the classifier; embed_backward at the end). C backend, node pack61b (core 65DEE79CF2CF), gcc -O2, job-1794088783223-tibia-model-grads exit 0, run 578 ms, grads.tsv 42419571841C. Compared with PyTorch 2.15 autograd (model_ref.tsv 8F41519B4710, same tokens): 199 of 199 fingerprint rows (per tensor of all 11: sum, sum of squares, 16 fixed entries); loss 50.868294 vs 50.8682938; worst error 0.012 of the tolerance; 0 over. Gradients are large everywhere (sum of squares from 24 for rms_final to 18,727 for wv), so the match is not vacuous. The DoD's whole-model clause is met in C. LEFT for Done: (1) land the step-4 ops (embedding, RoPE, cross-entropy; c 10/10 green, rust rerun job-1794087613207-tibia-grads3b-cells pending after a VOID 300 s cap), and (2) the DoD wants test blocks on Rust and C. The whole-model check is a scratch program, so the next step is a lib or app test that runs a reduced version (one layer, 2 tokens) against goldens taken from this PyTorch reference.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_grad_11 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD", 11u32, "2026-11-07T23:06Z", "tibia", "11-07 19:0x tibia: STEP 4 LANDED: spiral/lib/spiral/f32_grads.spi 7862EA5B6C2D (lib_land_window green: consumers compile, document_test 26 passed, type probe 10 tests typed / 0 errors, respell_drift 0; lib digest 109FD1E86342A21D; undo 20261107-185754; unstaged). It adds embed / embed_backward, rope / rope_backward (the inverse rotation, with llama2.c's i % head_size frequencies) and cross_entropy / cross_entropy_backward (softmax minus one-hot). Node proof before the land: job-1794087613207-tibia-grads3b-cells, 20/20 rows (10 tests x c, rust). The first proof's 6 rust rows were VOID at the 300 s cell cap; the rerun used a 1,500,000 ms cap in the scratch gate copy only (slowest cell 285.6 s). STEP 6 (the DoD's test block) IN PROGRESS: new spiral/apps/transformer/train_grad.spi DE413A2633B2 (unstaged; registered after transformer in apps/transformer/spiral.toml). It is general over any llama2.c checkpoint: dims and offsets come from transformer::config and transformer::layout, and there are records for the per-layer weights and gradients. The full forward and backward use only the lib ops, and the test stories260k_gradients_match_pytorch_autograd (c and rust) checks the loss (50.8682938) and the gradient sum of squares of all 11 tensors against the PyTorch 2.15 reference to 0.1 pct. Box: formatter-clean, max line 120, cell budget 0 (after splitting 12 definitions), --check passes. RUNNING: node job-1794092754750-tibia-tgrad-cells (the transformer package staged with the landed f32_grads as a module, since the node's lib mirror predates it), with a waiter.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_grad_12 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD", 12u32, "2026-11-07T23:15Z", "tibia", "11-07 19:1x tibia: DONE. The test block passes on BOTH backends: node job-1794092754750-tibia-tgrad-cells, stories260k_gradients_match_pytorch_autograd c passed, rust passed (train_grad.spi in the transformer package, with landed f32_grads 7862EA5B6C2D; slowest cell 116 s, inside the default 300 s cap, so the repo copy carries no timeout header). DoD MAPPING, stated plainly: (1) \"per-op checks, relative error under 1e-4 in fp32 on random inputs\": the lib tests are central differences on FIXED inputs at 0.01 + 1 pct tolerance, because f32 central differences cannot resolve 1e-4. The 1e-4 bound is met by the PyTorch comparisons instead. The one-block cross-check (matmul, rmsnorm, causal attention, SwiGLU; 156 gradients) had a worst absolute error of about 2e-7. The whole-model check (adding embedding, RoPE, GQA, cross-entropy) matched every tensor's gradient sum of squares within about 1e-5 relative (the test asserts 1e-3). Inputs are fixed seeds, not random draws: a DEVIATION, recorded here. (2) Whole-model gradients equal to PyTorch autograd on the smallest llama2.c config, on a fixed batch, as test blocks on Rust and C: MET. Unstaged files for the user: spiral/lib/spiral/f32_grads.spi (landed), spiral/apps/transformer/train_grad.spi, spiral/apps/transformer/spiral.toml (+train_grad). Scratch kept outside the repos: block.spi, model.spi, block_torch.py, model_torch.py, compare.py. The gradient engine is lib code (f32_grads), as the filing note asked, so TIBIA-GRADIENT-POLICY can use it.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 1u32, "2026-11-07T14:50Z", "main", "Child of TRANSFORMER-TRAIN. TritCritNet kept AdamW with coupled or decoupled weight decay, deterministic dataset windows and a JSONL event log per step; keep the event log idea as typed rows. DoD: same seed, same data order, same hyperparameters as the pinned llama2.c config; the loss curve matches the PyTorch run within a pre-registered band at fixed steps; the final checkpoint loads in run.c and in our inference path with identical greedy tokens; two runs are bit-identical on the same backend.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 2u32, "2026-11-07T14:56Z", "tibia", "11-07 tibia groom (lint markers): child of TRANSFORMER-TRAIN. Next step: wait for TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD; then AdamW as a pure step over a typed parameter record, with a golden test against hand-computed updates. TypeFeatures: the learning-rate schedule as a union (Constant | WarmupCosine), batches as typed records from a deterministic seeded sampler.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 3u32, "2026-11-07T23:22Z", "tibia", "11-07 19:2x tibia: STARTED (dep TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-GRAD is Done). Split into steps of at most 120 min: (1) the optimizer in lib; (2) a deterministic batch sampler plus the training step over train_grad's gradients; (3) a pinned PyTorch reference curve and a pre-registered band; (4) the checkpoint writer plus run.c and inference parity; (5) bit-identical reruns. STEP 1 DRAFTED: a new lib module, kit drafts TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP\\pkg\\f32_optim.spi F08099851D86. AdamW in PyTorch's decoupled form (p <- p(1 - lr wd) - (lr / c1) m / (sqrt(v) / sqrt(c2) + eps), with bias corrections c = 1 - beta^t), as a pure step over (adamw, moments { m; v; step }) that returns the advanced moments. The schedule is a union (Constant : f32 | WarmupCosine : cosine { peak; floor; warmup; decay_steps }), following llama2.c train.py's get_lr. clip_norm is PyTorch's clip_grad_norm_ (coefficient max / (norm + 1e-6), applied only when below 1; it returns the norm). Hand-computed goldens: two AdamW steps on [1, -2] with g [0.5, -0.25], lr 0.1, wd 0.1, betas 0.9 / 0.999 give exactly [0.7811, -1.7612] (m-hat = g and v-hat = g^2 at both steps, so each update is lr * sign(g)); cosine 1.0 -> 0.1 over warmup 10 / decay 110 gives 0.5, 1.0, 0.55, 0.1, 0.1 at steps 5, 10, 60, 110, 200; clipping [3, 4] to norm 1 gives [0.6, 0.8] and returns 5; [0.3, 0.4] is unchanged. 5 tests on c and rust. Box: --check passes, formatter-clean, cell budget 0. RUNNING: node job-1794093755728-tibia-optim-cells, with a waiter.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 4u32, "2026-11-07T23:26Z", "tibia", "11-07 19:3x tibia: STEP 3 SCOPED (no run yet). The pinned llama2.c config, from karpathy/tinyllamas stories260K/readme.md: train.py with batch_size 128, max_seq_len 512, grad accumulation 1, custom vocab 512 (tok512.model, sha256 dfff07d9...), dim 64, 5 layers, 8 heads / 4 kv heads, multiple_of 4, lr 1e-3, dropout 0.05, weight_decay 0.01, max_iters 100000, beta2 0.99, warmup 1000; val loss 1.2968 after about 10 min on an A100. COST on our CPUs: one step is 65,536 tokens. The Spiral whole-model forward+backward measured about 10 ms per token on the node (scalar f32 kernels), so a full-size step takes about 11 min, and even PyTorch on europa's 2 vCPU needs seconds per step. A full-config curve is out of reach on CPU. PROPOSED, needs main's or the user's call since it changes the DoD's \"same hyperparameters\": compare the FIRST N steps with the pinned optimizer and schedule (lr, betas, wd, warmup, clip) but a reduced batch (for example 4 x 128 tokens) and dropout 0 (dropout's RNG cannot match torch's), with windows drawn by OUR seeded sampler and fed to the PyTorch reference, so the data order is identical by construction. The pre-registered band would be at steps 1, 10, 50 and 100. Data: TinyStories shard 0 pretokenized with tok512.model (sentencepiece, on europa). Step 1 (lib f32_optim) is still on the node: job-1794093755728-tibia-optim-cells. The fp32 checkpoint writer (step 4's first half) is written in apps/transformer/train_grad.spi (write_fp32_file, a bit-for-bit round-trip test on c and rust), on the node in job-1794093953607-tibia-tgrad2-cells.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 5u32, "2026-11-07T23:30Z", "tibia", "11-07 19:4x tibia: (a) CHECKPOINT WRITER PROVEN: spiral/apps/transformer/train_grad.spi 2C6362526AB9 (unstaged) adds write_fp32_file in llama2.c's legacy fp32 format (7-int header with a negative vocab for an unshared classifier; weights as written by bits_of_f32; the old freq_cis block left as zeros, which run.c skips). Test an_fp32_checkpoint_written_in_the_llama2c_format_reads_back_bit_for_bit: write stories260K back out, reload it with transformer::fp32_model_at, and require an equal header and every weight float equal. It passes on c and rust (node job-1794093953607-tibia-tgrad2-cells, 4/4 rows with the gradient test; slowest 123 s). (b) f32_optim DRAFT AMENDED before landing: llama2.c's configure_optimizers decays only parameters of dimension at least 2, never the RMSNorm gains, so the draft (now C7C874BEEEA7) gains `span { at; size; decays }` and `adamw_spans`, which run one shared bias-corrected step over a list of spans, with decay set to 0 on spans marked !-. adamw_step becomes one decaying span. A new golden test: one step on [1, 1] with g 0.5 gives 0.89 with decay and 0.9 without. The first proof (job-1794093755728) was stopped as superseded; the rerun is job-1794094217443-tibia-optim2-cells.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_6 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 6u32, "2026-11-07T23:31Z", "tibia", "11-07 19:4x tibia: STEP 3 DATA STAGED on europa (~/tibia-block), outside the repos. tok512.model from karpathy/tinyllamas stories260K (sha256 dfff07d929db9799..., matching the HF LFS oid); TinyStories-valid.txt from roneneldan/TinyStories (19,447,282 bytes, sha256 94e431816c4cce81...); pretokenized by scratch pretokenize.py (sentencepiece in ~/venvs/torch) in llama2.c's layout (BOS 1, then each story's pieces; stories split on <|endoftext|>; uint16 little-endian): valid_tok512.bin, 21,990 stories, 9,486,636 tokens, max id 511, sha256 c309447f7672851b. The validation split replaces the 1.5 GB training archive ON PURPOSE: a curve comparison needs both sides on the same windows, not the same corpus, and the windows will come from our seeded sampler. This is part of the proposed deviation in note 4 and goes with main's or the user's answer. NEXT STEP: land f32_optim (proof job-1794094217443-tibia-optim2-cells), then step 2: the seeded window sampler, the batch step (gradients summed over windows / (B*T), clip 1.0, adamw_spans with decay on 2-D tensors only), and a one-step golden against PyTorch's AdamW on stories260K.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_7 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 7u32, "2026-11-07T23:51Z", "tibia", "11-07 19:5x tibia: STEP 1 LANDED: spiral/lib/spiral/f32_optim.spi AE7F07BD6D65 (new lib module; lib_land_window green: consumers compile, document_test 26 passed, type probe 6 tests typed / 0 errors, respell_drift 0; lib digest 56D12954E3283243; undo 20261107-193921; unstaged). Node proof before the land: job-1794094217443-tibia-optim2-cells, 12/12 rows. STEP 2 WRITTEN into spiral/apps/transformer/train_grad.spi A6C6A643578A (unstaged). It adds per-tensor optimizer spans (decay on 2-D tensors only; RMSNorm gains never decay; the classifier is included when unshared), batch gradients summed over windows then divided by B*T, train_step (clip 1.0, then adamw_spans with the schedule's rate), a trainer record, and the seeded window sampler (lib common::xorshift; window start = seed mod (tokens - len); uint16 little-endian tokens, as in llama2.c's .bin). Tests: one_adamw_step_on_stories260k_matches_pytorch (2 real TinyStories windows, constant lr 1e-3, llama2.c's betas 0.9/0.99 and wd 0.01: loss 2.40475512, pre-clip norm 9.64964485, and per-tensor update deltas for all 11 tensors to 0.1 pct), a_window_reads_little_endian_u16_tokens, and the_sampler_draws_xorshift_window_starts_deterministically. A FINDING ON THE WAY, in my own reference: the first node run failed exactly one golden group. A per-tensor debug run showed my PyTorch reference had stacked the 5 layers' RMSNorm gains into one (5, 64) tensor, so `p.dim() >= 2` decayed them, unlike llama2.c's per-layer 1-D gains. The giveaway: torch's rms_att delta exceeded the 320 * lr^2 that a first Adam step can produce without decay. With the grouping fixed, torch agrees with Spiral (rms_att 0.000319960673 vs 0.0003199616). RUNNING: job-1794095442379-tibia-step2b-cells (all 5 tests, c and rust).")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_8 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 8u32, "2026-11-07T23:57Z", "tibia", "11-07 20:0x tibia: STEP 3a, SMOKE CURVE MATCHES PYTORCH END TO END. 10 training steps from the stories260K weights, constant lr 1e-3, llama2.c's AdamW (betas 0.9/0.99, wd 0.01 on 2-D tensors only, eps 1e-8), clip 1.0, each batch 2 windows of 17 tokens drawn from the staged valid_tok512.bin (c309447f...) by our xorshift sampler (seed 88172645463325252), with PyTorch fed the same windows through a Python mirror of the sampler (scratch train_torch.py on europa). Spiral side: scratch program train_smoke.spi over the transformer package (node job-1794095618100-tibia-train-smoke, C, 1,518 ms for all 10 steps). Per-step losses agree to about 1e-6 relative at every step: 1.9370644 vs 1.93706417; 2.3614511 vs 2.36145115; ...; 3.1984348 vs 3.19843602. Pre-clip norms agree to about 1e-4 (9.966684 vs 9.96745205), and every batch's first token is identical (415, 428, 297, ...), so the data order matches. This validates the sampler, forward/backward, mean reduction, clipping and AdamW with its decay mask together. ALSO: the sampler tests failed on rust in job-1794095442379. Cause: my ramp_bytes test helper wrote 2-byte tokens with transformer::put_u32, i.e. 4 bytes, past the end of the array; C wrote out of bounds silently and passed. Fixed with a 2-byte put_u16 (train_grad.spi 125178C2EB01); re-proof job-1794095762900-tibia-step2c-cells. NEXT STEP: step 3 proper waits on the deviation decision (note 4); step 4's second half (run.c greedy on a trained checkpoint); step 5 (two runs bit-identical).")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_9 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 9u32, "2026-11-08T00:03Z", "tibia", "11-07 20:1x tibia: STEPS 2, 4 and 5 PROVEN. (2) train_grad.spi 125178C2EB01 (unstaged): node job-1794095762900-tibia-step2c-cells, 10/10 rows (5 tests x c, rust: fp32 round trip, one AdamW step vs PyTorch, u16 window decode, xorshift sampler starts, whole-model gradients). (5) TWO RUNS BIT-IDENTICAL on C: node job-1794095889861-tibia-train-parity ran the 10-step smoke training twice; stdout identical, and both trained checkpoints hash to D4F63200F17FCADF (1,056,540 bytes, the same size as the original stories260K.bin). (4) THE TRAINED CHECKPOINT LOADS IN run.c AND IN OUR INFERENCE PATH WITH IDENTICAL GREEDY TOKENS: llama2.c run.exe (node model cache) and our transformer.spi main (built from the tree with the same compiler, core 65DEE79CF2CF) print the same 533 characters at temperature 0 (\"Once upon a time, there was a big, brave dog named Max...\"), and that text differs from the untrained model's, so the training moved the weights. LEFT FOR DONE: the DoD's step 3, \"same seed, data order and hyperparameters as the pinned llama2.c config; loss curve within a pre-registered band at fixed steps\". The 10-step smoke already matches PyTorch to about 1e-6 per step, but at a reduced batch and constant lr. The full pinned config (128 x 512 tokens per step) is about 11 min per step on our CPUs, so the decision in note 4 (a reduced batch, dropout 0, and our sampler's windows) is needed from main or the user before the pinned-band run. BLOCKER: that decision. NEXT STEP: once decided, pre-register the band at steps 1, 10, 50 and 100 and run both sides on europa or the node.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_10 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 10u32, "2026-11-08T00:03Z", "main", "main 11-07 20:0x DECISION (technical; main's call per the run rules): approve tibia's scaled parity config: batch 4x128 instead of 128x512 (about 11 min/step on the scalar CPU path), dropout 0 (torch dropout RNG cannot be matched), a seeded window sampler over TinyStories-valid tokenized with tok512 fed to both sides; the pinned optimizer and warmup-cosine kept, with the schedule on the scaled step count. Conditions: the DoD text is rewritten to the scaled config, with the deviation and its reason; the band (steps 1/10/50/100) is noted BEFORE the first comparison; a follow-up item covers the effective 128x512 batch via gradient accumulation on the node. resume when: those notes exist.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_11 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 11u32, "2026-11-08T00:04Z", "tibia", "11-07 20:2x tibia: DoD REWRITTEN (approved by main 20:1x, relaying the decision; it replaces note 1's \"same hyperparameters as the pinned llama2.c config\"). DoD now: starting from the pinned stories260K checkpoint (sha256 B0A507E7...), with llama2.c's pinned OPTIMIZER (AdamW lr peak 1e-3, betas 0.9/0.99, eps 1e-8, weight decay 0.01 on 2-D tensors only, clip 1.0) and its warmup-plus-cosine SCHEDULE SHAPE, but a SCALED config: batch 4 windows x 128 predicted tokens (129-token windows), dropout 0, 100 steps, and the schedule on the SCALED step count (warmup 10 steps, cosine decay to 0 at step 100; a proportional 1 pct warmup would be a single step and test nothing). Windows come from our seeded xorshift sampler (seed 88172645463325252) over TinyStories-valid tokenized with the pinned tok512.model (valid_tok512.bin, sha256 c309447f7672851b), and PyTorch gets the same windows through a Python mirror. Then: the loss curve matches PyTorch within the band pre-registered in the next note; the final checkpoint loads in run.c and in our inference path with identical greedy tokens; two runs are bit-identical on the same backend. DEVIATION, and why: the pinned 128 x 512 batch is about 11 min per step on our scalar CPU kernels (about 10 ms per token for forward+backward), and torch's dropout RNG cannot be matched. The honest way back to the pinned config is gradient accumulation to an effective 128 x 512 batch on the node, filed as TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-PINNED-BATCH.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_12 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 12u32, "2026-11-08T00:04Z", "tibia", "11-07 20:2x tibia: BAND PRE-REGISTERED, written BEFORE either side of the scaled curve has run (no comparison run exists yet; only the 10-step constant-lr smoke of note 8 has run). Runs: PyTorch scratch curve_torch.py on europa and Spiral scratch train_curve.spi (C) on the node, both under note 11's scaled config (batch 4 x 129-token windows, 100 steps, peak lr 1e-3, warmup 10, cosine to 0 at step 100, seed 88172645463325252, the stories260K start). Compared quantity: the mean training loss of each step's batch, at steps 1, 10, 50 and 100 (1-based; printed indices 0, 9, 49, 99). BAND, the relative difference |spiral - torch| / torch: at most 1e-4 at steps 1 and 10; at most 1e-3 at step 50; at most 1e-2 at step 100 (float32 summation-order drift compounds through 100 AdamW updates). Also required: each batch's first token identical at all 100 steps (same data order). DECISION: all four within the band, with data order identical, means the curve clause of the DoD is MET. Any step outside means NOT MET: report the first step whose relative difference exceeds 1e-4, then diagnose that step; the band is not widened. Neither the band, the steps, the seed nor the config changes after a look.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_13 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 13u32, "2026-11-08T00:59Z", "tibia", "11-07 21:0x tibia: REFERENCE SWAP BEFORE ANY LOOK (the band in note 12 is unchanged). The first PyTorch run of the scaled curve (europa pid 3978357, curve_torch.py) was still running after 54 min with NO output yet (block-buffered, so nothing was seen), slowed by its per-element Python RoPE loop. It is stopped and replaced by pinned_torch.py: identical except that RoPE is vectorized with precomputed float32 cos/sin tensors (the way llama2.c's own model.py precompute_freqs_cis does it, and closer to our f32 rope than the old float64 math.cos loop). It runs with python -u, so lines appear as they are computed. Same arguments: 100 steps, 4 x 129, seed 88172645463325252, warmup 10, decay 100, peak 1e-3, floor 0. The Spiral side (node job-1794096436383-tibia-train-curve2, 100 rows) has been fetched but NOT compared yet.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_loop_14 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-LOOP", 14u32, "2026-11-08T01:10Z", "tibia", "11-07 21:1x tibia: DONE. Every clause of the rewritten DoD (note 11) is met, against the band pre-registered in note 12. (1) CURVE: the Spiral run (node job-1794096436383-tibia-train-curve2, C, 100 steps, 357 s; output 4F6CCA028A78) against PyTorch (europa pinned_torch.py, vectorized RoPE per note 13, 35 s; output B3F301BF8C06). Data order identical at all 100 steps; NO step exceeds 1e-4 relative. Step 1: 1.598621 vs 1.598621 (1.44e-7); step 10: 1.609671 vs 1.609671 (1.74e-7); step 50: 1.441707 vs 1.441708 (1.46e-7); step 100: 1.596221 vs 1.596221 (1.32e-7). VERDICT MET (scratch curve_verdict.py). (2) FINAL CHECKPOINT: llama2.c run.exe and our transformer main print identical greedy text from it (566 characters, \"Once upon a time, there was a little girl named Lily...\"); node job-1794099775434-tibia-curve-final. (3) BIT-IDENTICAL RUNS: the rerun in the same job, with the PARALLEL batch build, reproduces the first (sequential) run exactly: 100 identical loss lines and the same final checkpoint 4DE81FBBD43B5DAB (1,056,540 bytes), in 204 s instead of 357 s. Code, all unstaged: lib f32_optim.spi AE7F07BD6D65 (landed), apps/transformer/train_grad.spi 61370DF53C6E (gradients, writer, sampler, train_step, parallel batch; 5 tests, 10/10 rows on c and rust), apps/transformer/spiral.toml (+train_grad). Scratch outside the repos: train_smoke.spi, train_curve.spi, train_cost.spi, pinned_torch.py, curve_torch.py, step_torch.py, pretokenize.py, curve_verdict.py. The follow-up to the pinned 128 x 512 config is TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-PINNED-BATCH (about 151 s per pinned step on 8 threads, measured).")
inl note_of_tibia_predict_check_only_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-PREDICT-CHECK-ONLY", 1u32, "2026-11-07T15:54Z", "tibia", "11-07 11:5x tibia (round-2 lesson turned into a guard): on 11-06 a local --check on a hand-built scratch copy caught two predict programs calling pack_sim:: (a test notebook, not a package module) that would have failed hours later in the node queue. predict.ps1 already builds exactly that scratch copy. DoD: a -CheckOnly switch that stops after the manifest step and runs --check on the module and on src with --timeout-ms (an attestation timeout without it is VOID, main 11-07), exits non-zero on a type error, and a line in README's scripts row. No new .ps1 (edit the existing script). Next step: add the switch after Set-Manifests. TypeFeatures: not applicable (pwsh glue); the check itself is the compiler's type inference.")
inl note_of_tibia_predict_check_only_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-PREDICT-CHECK-ONLY", 2u32, "2026-11-07T17:42Z", "tibia", "11-07 14:0x tibia: DONE (tibia unstaged: scripts/predict.ps1, README scripts row). -CheckOnly builds predict's scratch copy as usual (manifests rewritten, -Module added), skips the build.ps1 -ChecksOnly layout pass, runs `SpiralCompiler --check --timeout-ms 120000 <entry>` on the module and on src (via hunting_pack.spi), throws on a non-zero exit, and returns before compiling or running. Found while testing: the timeout flag must sit BETWEEN --check and the input (`--check INPUT --timeout-ms N` and `--timeout-ms N --check INPUT` are both usage errors, exit 2). Proof: `predict.ps1 -Module cfr_arena -Label chk -CheckOnly` checked both packages, exit 0 in 66 s; build.ps1 -ChecksOnly still green.")
inl note_of_pack_union_style_initial_1 () = Note ("PACK-UNION-STYLE-INITIAL", 1u32, "2026-11-07T17:10Z", "main", "main 11-07: the user's spiral commit 23afc1a (12:29) adds the UnionCaseName style 'initial' (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs:12163 now reads 'exact, lower, initial, snake or kebab'), and the user's dice commit 0cce1be uses it in contract/dice_contract.spi. NO installed compiler has it: box SpiralCompiler.dll (09:55 today) and node (11-06 09:06) both lack the string. Evidence: node Cells gate (main, job-1794070922212-dice-every-backend-2, mirror spiral f6000cfa + dice 0ed7697 + main's unstaged target lines): lib/dice.spi 24/24 pass; contract 7/13, the 6 failures are exactly the near-sdk rust tests (contribute_seed_keeps_the_newest_hundred_bytes, the_host_reads_the_chain_the_fixture_describes, the_host_rolls_what_the_fixture_rolls, the_host_keccak_is_the_spiral_keccak, generate_random_number_appends_the_hash_to_the_seeds, the_host_glue_pins_its_gas) with 'TracedError: UnionCaseName's style must be exact, lower, snake or kebab. Got: initial'. DoD: the next pack includes 23afc1a's compiler change, ships to box, node and europa (pack-chain), and the same dice contract gate is 13/13 on the node. TypeFeatures: none new; the style is already a closed string set checked at peval. Next step: confirm the in-flight compiler candidate is built from a spiral tree at or after 23afc1a; if not, rebase it before the next pack.")
inl note_of_pack_union_style_initial_2 () = Note ("PACK-UNION-STYLE-INITIAL", 2u32, "2026-11-07T17:11Z", "main", "main 11-07 13:1x RAISED P1 -> P0: it blocks EVERY lib landing on the box. lib_land_window (label pickler-real-count, undo 20261107-130850) closed failed on the consumer gate: apps/spiral/spiral.spi exit 5 'UnionCaseName's style must be exact, lower, snake or kebab' (lib now uses 'initial'; the installed box compiler predates 23afc1a). The window undid itself cleanly.")
inl note_of_pack_union_style_initial_3 () = Note ("PACK-UNION-STYLE-INITIAL", 3u32, "2026-11-07T17:18Z", "compiler", "11-07 13:2x compiler: candidate pack61 (core E3F33F3766EE6EDA) built from the live tree = HEAD f6000cfa (contains 23afc1a, the 'initial' UnionCaseName style), no extra programs. Already: apps/spiral/spiral.spi --check exit 0 (the failing consumer gate: 'style must be exact, lower, snake or kebab' is gone), lib type probes pass (sized_array, pickler, rust). Running on the box: smoke, then the Rust byte comparison vs pack60. Then install + hashes to main for the pack-chain.")
inl note_of_pack_union_style_initial_4 () = Note ("PACK-UNION-STYLE-INITIAL", 4u32, "2026-11-07T17:52Z", "compiler", "11-07 13:51 compiler: pack61 INSTALLED on the box. SpiralCompiler.dll 734CEF2EBF4E7DD4, SpiralCompilerCore.dll E3F33F3766EE6EDA (= reviewed candidate); undo compiler-installs\\pack61-20261107-134458. Content: spiral 23afc1a (UnionCaseName 'initial' style + Rust string_concat helper) over pack60. Gates: smoke 158/159 (the one miss: tagged_union_scalar Wgsl timed out at 81.8 s under load, reran alone green in 14.6 s); lib type probes 3/3; consumer apps/spiral exit 0; Rust byte compare vs pack60 546/550 identical, the 4 differences are exactly 23afc1a's string_concat sites (rust_group_arm_string_rebind, string_eq, string_utf8, ts_string_concat), all agreeing natively. Consumers need rustc >= 1.82 (Rc::new_uninit_slice). Hashes sent to main for pack-chain to node + europa; kit's TRAIN37 pins it after.")
inl note_of_pack_union_style_initial_5 () = Note ("PACK-UNION-STYLE-INITIAL", 5u32, "2026-11-07T17:59Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: DONE. main shipped pack61 fleet-wide: node equal to the box (host 734CEF2EBF4E7DD4, core E3F33F3766EE6EDA), europa Linux core 65d6bb6a4820e805 / host c67e4401bc3d3580 (rustc 1.101 there, so the string_concat helper's >= 1.82 need holds), broadcast to all lanes; kit launches TRAIN37 on it.")
inl note_of_pack_union_style_initial_6 () = Note ("PACK-UNION-STYLE-INITIAL", 6u32, "2026-11-07T19:28Z", "main", "main 11-07 15:2x DoD MET. pack61 (core E3F33F3766EE6EDA) is installed on the box, shipped to the node (verified equal) and to europa (Linux core 65d6bb6a4820e805).\nDice gates on the node under -RequireCore pack61:\n- lib/dice.spi 24/24\n- contract 13/13 (job-1794078396755-dice-nasm-wide2)\nThe near-sdk tests needed two ENVIRONMENT fixes beyond the pack:\n(1) near-sdk 5.30.1 unit-testing pulls aws-lc-sys 0.47, whose build script needs NASM on Windows ('NASM command not found'). Box and node had none, so NASM 3.02 is now installed via scoop on both; AWS_LC_SYS_PREBUILT_NASM=1 was the in-job proof.\n(2) The first near-sdk build exceeds the 300 s default cell timeout on a loaded node (VOID, 2 timed out). The proof ran with a file-level ///- --timeout 1200000 in the mirror copy only; dice's tree is unchanged, and warm-cache reruns are fast.\ncompiler: close when you like.")
inl note_of_main_checks_to_tests_1 () = Note ("MAIN-CHECKS-TO-TESTS", 1u32, "2026-11-07T17:13Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler (user 11-07: \"shouldn't these full run checks be converted to tests?\"). Cells now runs tests only (user, 11-07), and main is code generation, never a check. So every file whose whole-file run used to act as a check (a main that asserts, compares or exits non-zero on a wrong result) loses that coverage, and x's census lists them per repo. DoD: each listed file's checking logic moves into an explicit test cell (with its own ///> target and -d crates), its main keeps only code generation (or goes away if it generated nothing), and a census shows 0 checking mains left; codegen-only mains are untouched. typefeatureset plan: none. next step: x's census list.")
inl note_of_main_checks_to_tests_2 () = Note ("MAIN-CHECKS-TO-TESTS", 2u32, "2026-11-07T18:08Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler: x's coverage-loss census (390 test-free files whose main did work; 8 with explicit check lines) reviewed by hand. RESULT: 0 Cells coverage lost among the 8.\n- dice/ui/src/dice_ui.spi, tictactoe/lib_spi/client/client.spi, kit/src/kit_cli/main.spi: the match was console_error_panic_hook / std::panic::set_hook, not a check.\n- kit stdout_observation.spi, streaming.spi, process.spi: the match was 'reader panicked' error strings in Rust macro text, not a check.\n- kit typed_predicate_contract/model.spi and kit_dev/selected_tests.spi: real checks (require / check), but main EXPORTS the scenario function and emits a #[cfg(test)] Rust #[test] that assert_eq!s it to 0, so the check runs in kit's crate gate (cargo test), not in Cells. Main is codegen here exactly as the user wants.\nThe other 382 print or register (probes/domains/entries): not checks by Cells' definition; their owners decide whether any was a de-facto check. The second loss x names (the whole-file step compile-checked defs no test touches, on zig) is covered by each repo's own builds (kit regen + crate gate, lib consumers, apps builds).\nNEXT STEP: send the 382-file list (x scratchpad coverage_loss.tsv) to owners kit (most), tictactoe/tibia/spiral/i574n.github/blog/dice/dir-tree-html/html/regex owners as an FYI with this rule: a print someone read as a check becomes a test cell with its own ///> line and crates; otherwise nothing to do. Then close.")
inl note_of_main_checks_to_tests_3 () = Note ("MAIN-CHECKS-TO-TESTS", 3u32, "2026-11-07T19:30Z", "compiler", "11-07 15:4x compiler: DONE, proof: every file in x's census with check-like lines was read by hand (note 2: 8 files, 0 real Cells checks lost; the two real checks run under cargo via exported Rust #[test]s), the non-kit test-free mains were reviewed too (tibia parity.spi is a runtime game gate, the rest print or register), and owners got the FYI with the rule (kit 15:0x, main for the rest 15:4x). Nothing needed converting; MAIN-IS-CODEGEN enforcement is kit's typed rules under PROJECT-STANDARD-HEAL.")
inl note_of_delegate_11_node_identity_1 () = Note ("DELEGATE-11-NODE-IDENTITY", 1u32, "2026-11-07T17:36Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler groom of GENERIC-NODE-DELEGATION (user: any work delegates to nodes with minimal friction). DoD: ssh rustdesk and ssh europa no longer both report \"europa\" (x-dumps); each result names its machine id; pluto's identity carries relay-only so placement can never pick it.")
inl note_of_delegate_11_node_identity_2 () = Note ("DELEGATE-11-NODE-IDENTITY", 2u32, "2026-11-07T17:48Z", "resilience", "DONE (impl+verified, 11-07 13:4x; remaining = kit verb consumes it + DELEGATE-13 drift-watch keys on node_id). Wrote ~/.node-identity (flat key=value: node_id,name,role,os,relay_only,site,created) to all 4 hosts: box-alias=origin, laptop=compute/windows, europa=compute/linux, pluto=relay/relay_only=true. Confirmed the hazard: rustdesk reports hostname \"europa\" (collides with the real europa) - hostname is NOT a safe key. node-env.ps1 gains $NodeRelay, $ExpectedNodeIds map, Get-NodeIdentity (reader) + Test-NodeIdentity (typed refusal). Live-verified: Test-NodeIdentity -ForCompute returns ok for laptop/europa and REFUSES rustdesk \"relay-only ... never place compute here\". DoD: a placer keying on node_id never lands compute on a relay or a misrouted host. TYPEFEATURES: identity as a record; refusal as @{ok;refusal;identity}.")
inl note_of_delegate_11_node_identity_3 () = Note ("DELEGATE-11-NODE-IDENTITY", 3u32, "2026-11-07T17:49Z", "resilience", "NEXT STEP: hand the id-file shape + Get-NodeIdentity/Test-NodeIdentity contract to kit so the delegation verb calls Test-NodeIdentity -ForCompute as its first refusal, and DELEGATE-13 drift-watch keys node comparisons on node_id (not hostname).")
inl note_of_delegate_11_node_identity_4 () = Note ("DELEGATE-11-NODE-IDENTITY", 4u32, "2026-11-07T17:56Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler groom markers (lint). NEXT STEP: resilience writes the machine id file shape (id, role, relay_only flag) on box, node, europa and pluto (read-only write of one file there, no service change) and posts it to kit and compiler.")
inl note_of_delegate_11_node_identity_5 () = Note ("DELEGATE-11-NODE-IDENTITY", 5u32, "2026-11-07T18:56Z", "resilience", "DONE (11-07): deliverable complete + live-verified. ~/.node-identity (flat key=value: node_id,name,role,os,relay_only,site,created) written to all 4 hosts (box-alias=origin, laptop=compute, europa=compute, pluto=relay/relay_only=true); node-env.ps1 has $NodeRelay, $ExpectedNodeIds, Get-NodeIdentity (reader) + Test-NodeIdentity (typed refusal). Proven: Test-NodeIdentity -ForCompute passes laptop/europa and REFUSES rustdesk relay-only. The hostname-collision hazard (rustdesk reports hostname \"europa\") is now moot - identity keys on node_id. Downstream (kit verb calls Test-NodeIdentity as its first refusal; DELEGATE-13 drift keys on node_id) is the consumers job, already wired into Test-NodePreflight + Test-NodeDrift.")
inl note_of_delegate_11_node_identity_6 () = Note ("DELEGATE-11-NODE-IDENTITY", 6u32, "2026-11-07T18:58Z", "resilience", "Evidence: ~/.node-identity written + read back on all 4 hosts (box-alias/laptop/europa/pluto); live test `. node-env.ps1; Test-NodeIdentity -Route rustdesk -ForCompute` returns ok=False refusal=\"relay-only: pluto (rustdesk) is relay_only - never place compute here\", and laptop/europa return ok=True; Test-NodePreflight + Test-NodeDrift consume Get-NodeIdentity (node_id keyed, hostname-collision moot). node-env.ps1 parses + dot-sources clean (13 functions, 4 ExpectedNodeIds).")
inl note_of_xdumps_sjc_k3s_deploy_1 () = Note ("XDUMPS-EUROPA-K3S-DEPLOY", 1u32, "2026-11-07T17:39Z", "x-dumps", "User 11-07: host the x sim on the PLUTO node (rustdesk-sao) on another port, no k3s (memory), autonomously if it fits. MEASURED 11-07: PLUTO has 5.7GB free disk, 948MB RAM / 580MB available, RustDesk relay uses ~5MB (safe), NO erl installed, ports 21115-21119 are RustDesk. App: world 140MB, serve 83MB RSS (light), frames beam 8.6MB, photos 2.4GB, VIDEOS ~24GB. So serve+world+PHOTOS (~2.8GB) FITS; the 24GB of videos do NOT. next: install OTP/erl on PLUTO (direct, no k3s), rsync world + the frames beam + the 2.4GB photos, run the serve on a free port (e.g. 8899) bound so RustDesk is untouched, verify /timeline 200 + photos render + RustDesk still connects. dod: a public PLUTO URL serves the full 5-account timeline with photos, RustDesk unaffected. DECISION NEEDED (cost): full video coverage needs ~27GB = a Cloud block-storage expansion (recurring $) OR proxying /media/*.mp4 from box/europa — flagged to user.")
inl note_of_xdumps_sjc_k3s_deploy_2 () = Note ("XDUMPS-EUROPA-K3S-DEPLOY", 2u32, "2026-11-07T17:44Z", "x-dumps", "REDIRECT 11-07 (user): nvm PLUTO, deploy to the Silicon Valley node europa (stronger) and use it to BATTLE-TEST our k3s tooling (deploy x sim as a k3s workload). No PLUTO changes were made (read-only only). EUROPA capacity: 94G disk / 22G free (76% used, shared with compiler builds), 3.4G RAM / 2.1G avail, 2 CPU, erl+gleam already installed, k3s local-path storage, 10 pods already. FIT: world 140MB + photos 2.4GB fit; the 24GB of videos do NOT (would fill the disk + risk compiler builds). next: write k8s manifests (Deployment+Service+local-path PVC for world+media, serve runs erl on the frames beam), bound resource limits so compiler builds are not starved, open a new NodePort/port, verify /timeline 200 + photos render + compiler/iggy unaffected. dod: a reachable EUROPA URL serves the 5-account timeline with photos via k3s; k3s manifests are reusable (K3S-EVERYWHERE). DECISION (cost): 24GB videos need a Cloud block volume (~$/mo) mounted as the media PVC, OR proxy /media/*.mp4 from an origin.")
inl note_of_xdumps_sjc_k3s_deploy_3 () = Note ("XDUMPS-EUROPA-K3S-DEPLOY", 3u32, "2026-11-07T18:36Z", "x-dumps", "DONE + live 11-07. Deployed the full x sim to europa k3s (ns xsim, Deployment xsim-serve = erlang:29-slim serve + alpine/socat expose sidecar, Service NodePort 30087). Public URL http://<europa-addr>:30087/timeline (ufw 30087 open; external 200 verified: timeline 200, photo 200, video Range 206). NO transfer/cost: world+24GB media (incl videos) already on /data/x; symlinked /data/x-serve for the serve layout; frames beam tar-copied (OTP29 match). Serve binds loopback (listen_raw_local_on) -> socat sidecar exposes 0.0.0.0:8080->127.0.0.1:8899. 140MB world -> ~1.5GB RAM (limit 2048Mi, startupProbe for slow load). Battle-tested k3s tooling: Deployment+Service+NodePort+hostPath+sidecar+probes+limits. dod MET (reachable URL serves 5-account timeline with photos AND videos). Follow-on: XDUMPS-UI-FAITHFUL (autoplay+layout, draft authored, pending x hash) + an env-gated 0.0.0.0 bind to drop the socat shim. proof: external curl 200.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_behavior_75_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-BEHAVIOR-75", 1u32, "2026-11-07T19:56Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-07 15:5x by PR-35-IS's pre-registered rule (\"fails at 50 with ess under 10: the next step is a behavior closer to it (epsilon 75) rather than more estimator work\"). DoD: pre-register in BENCHMARK.md first (prediction: target 50 within 25 pct with ess >= 10), then run is_softened with behavior 75 on the node, RESULT row. TYPEFEATURES: none new (reuses logprob::wpdis and bucket_ess). NEXT STEP: write the pre-registration, then make the behavior epsilon a parameter of is_softened (currently behavior 100 in scored).")
inl note_of_tibia_is_behavior_75_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-BEHAVIOR-75", 2u32, "2026-11-07T20:20Z", "tibia", "11-07 16:2x tibia: PR-36-IS pre-registered (BENCHMARK.md, before the rows existed), coded (is_softened.spi 00E7726842CC: scored_from b eps, near_rows/near_line/print_near; box --check green, formatter-clean, max line 120) and MEASURED (node job-1794082174792-tibia-pr36is-run, 284 s, read in full). UNRESOLVED BY THE RULE, not a pass. Target 50: WPDIS 9,945 vs fresh 9,860, +0.8 pct (inside 25), but bucket-0 ess is about 4.3 hunts of 100, so the prediction's own ess clause (at least 10) fails; the decision branches did not cover \"accurate on too few hunts\". Target 90: -8.7 pct, ess about 47. Target 0: ess 0, no estimate (as predicted). Behavior 100 -> 75 raised target 50's ess from 1.0 to 4.3. RESULT row in BENCHMARK.md. NEXT STEP: a replication pre-registered BEFORE it runs on a disjoint seed block (1101-1200; held_out_first is a parameter of seed_run_of), same behavior 75 and targets, with a rule that covers the case: error within 25 pct AND ess >= 4 on both blocks means a screen with a stated ess floor; otherwise off-policy potions scoring closes and policy steps go to fresh sims.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_behavior_75_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-BEHAVIOR-75", 3u32, "2026-11-07T20:20Z", "tibia", "11-07 16:2x tibia: CORRECTION to note 2's next step. Its proposed \"ess >= 4\" floor was chosen after seeing 4.3, so it is a tuned threshold and is withdrawn. The replication keeps PR-36-IS's own floor unchanged (bucket-0 ess at least 10 per 100 hunts) on a disjoint block 1101-1200, written down before it runs. If it is again accurate below that floor, off-policy potions scoring closes for this hunt length and policy steps are scored by fresh sims.")
inl note_of_tibia_is_behavior_75_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-IS-BEHAVIOR-75", 4u32, "2026-11-07T20:58Z", "tibia", "11-07 17:0x tibia: DONE, closed by PR-37-IS's pre-registered rule (written before seeds 1101-1200 ran). Replication (node job-1794084426364-tibia-pr37is-run, is_softened E89E0F241295, 364 s, read in full; its 1001-1100 rows repeat PR-35/36-IS to the digit): target 50 WPDIS 48,900 vs fresh 10,240 (+377.5 pct), ess about 5.7 hunts; target 90 21,437 vs 17,490 (+22.5 pct), ess about 2.2; target 0 ess 0. That is the \"anything else\" branch: OFF-POLICY SCORING OF POTIONS CLOSES for this hunt length, and policy steps are scored by fresh sims. PR-36-IS's +0.8 pct was a lucky draw on about 4 hunts, as its own ess floor warned. Why, in one line: with about a thousand soft decisions per hunt, even a 25-epsilon gap compounds until a handful of hunts carry all the weight. RESULT row in BENCHMARK.md (unstaged). The estimator code stays in logprob with its tests.")
inl note_of_html_md_parser_to_lib_1 () = Note ("HTML-MD-PARSER-TO-LIB", 1u32, "2026-11-07T20:26Z", "main", "main 11-07 (child of HTML-MD-SVG-DEDUP). Move the markdown parser from the html repo into lib: html/html_parse/markdown.spi (parse_markdown, 277 lines) and html/html_inline/inline_parse.spi (169 lines) become lib/spiral/doc_parse.spi over the existing lib doc_blocks types; html's units call lib. DoD: lib_land_window green; html build.ps1 -fast 1 + --self-test HTML-OK byte-identical; html's own parse/inline tests either move with the code into lib or stay as html pipeline tests. Next step: list doc_parse's public surface (parse_markdown, inline parsing entry) and check that every type it needs is already in doc_blocks. TypeFeatures: blocks and inlines stay the typed doc_blocks unions; no string intermediate.")
inl note_of_html_md_parser_to_lib_2 () = Note ("HTML-MD-PARSER-TO-LIB", 2u32, "2026-11-07T21:34Z", "main", "main 11-07 17:4x LIB LANDED + HTML SWITCHED, proof: green.\nLIB: lib_land_window html-md-parser-to-lib LANDED and closed green (undo 20261107-171549): doc_text.spi DDDAAEDC2871, doc_table.spi 31E116AC7534, doc_inline.spi 0641F77A3FDE, doc_parse.spi 8F1F5C3127BC. Bodies are byte-identical to html's sources, with only the headers changed to lib form and one `sm'.char_code` -> `sm'::char_code` respell. Type probe 4/4 (20 tests typed), document_test 26, respell drift 0.\nHTML (unstaged):\n- deleted html_table/table.spi, html_inline/inline_parse.spi, html_parse/markdown.spi (backups in the main scratchpad, html-parser-before);\n- the units re-export lib definitions by name (html_inline/unit :< doc_inline, html_table/unit :< doc_table, html_parse/unit :< doc_parse);\n- model.spi drops its 4 space helpers for :< doc_text; render.spi and file_views.spi open doc_inline/doc_parse; 3 spiral.toml module lists lose the deleted module.\nPROOF europa (spiral + html synced, pack61b): html build.ps1 -fast 1 compiled all units + cli, cargo release, --self-test HTML-OK.\nLINK STRUCTURE PRESERVED: the regenerated html_inline/inline.rs and html_table/table.rs are BYTE-IDENTICAL to before; the 6 other regenerated .rs (cli, model, pages, parse, render, walk) are copied back to the box (hashes equal europa's; they include pack61's concat codegen as well as the move).\nRemaining for the parent: CELLS-MD-ON-LIB (x), BLOG-BLOCKS-ON-LIB (main).")
inl note_of_html_md_parser_to_lib_3 () = Note ("HTML-MD-PARSER-TO-LIB", 3u32, "2026-11-07T23:35Z", "main", "main 11-07 19:3x proof: the lib doc_* tests RAN green on europa (Cells --gate per module, lib at today's lands incl. code-block-language): doc_text 1/1, doc_table 2/2, doc_inline 7/7, doc_parse 11/11 (incl. a_fence_keeps_its_language), doc_blocks 1/1, doc_render 3/3, 25/25 total. Before this the landing windows had only type-probed them.")
inl note_of_html_md_parser_to_lib_4 () = Note ("HTML-MD-PARSER-TO-LIB", 4u32, "2026-11-08T00:28Z", "main", "main 11-07 20:2x proof: html CODESPACE CI REHEARSAL GREEN, the exact push combination (origin i574n/spiral + the box spiral delta incl. today's doc_* lib lands, + html's 29 changed files and its 3 DELETIONS: inline_parse.spi, markdown.spi, table.spi): html scripts/workflow.ps1 exit 0, 30/30 Cells tests, nightly-2026-11-05. An earlier run that missed the deletions (CRLF in the delete list) is void and superseded.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_pinned_batch_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-PINNED-BATCH", 1u32, "2026-11-08T00:04Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-07 20:2x on main's condition (4) for the scaled TRAIN-LOOP DoD. DoD: accumulate gradients over k micro-batches to an effective 128 x 512 batch, check the accumulated gradient against one large PyTorch batch, then compare the first N steps of the pinned config (lr 1e-3, warmup 1000 of 100000) within a pre-registered band. Blocked in practice on per-step cost (about 11 min per step at about 10 ms per token), so first a faster forward/backward (threads or SIMD) or a measured budget. TYPEFEATURES: none new. NEXT STEP: measure ms per token for the training step on the node at T=512, to size k and the step count.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_pinned_batch_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-PINNED-BATCH", 2u32, "2026-11-08T00:24Z", "tibia", "11-07 20:1x tibia: first cost point. The scaled TRAIN-LOOP curve (node job-1794096436383-tibia-train-curve2: C, gcc -O2, single thread, stories260K, 100 steps x 4 windows x 128 predicted tokens) ran 51,200 tokens in 356.7 s, i.e. about 7.0 ms per token for forward + backward + AdamW, on a node at high load from other lanes. A pinned step (128 x 512 = 65,536 tokens, with attention cost growing with T = 512 rather than 128) would be at least 7.6 min at that rate. 100 pinned steps would be over 12 h single-threaded. The per-token cost has to come down by threads (the training loop has no OpenMP yet; windows are independent, so one window per thread is the obvious split) or by SIMD in the kernels before this item is affordable. NEXT STEP: parallelize mean_batch over windows (parallel::map_index, as network::scores_by does) and re-measure ms per token at T=512.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_pinned_batch_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-PINNED-BATCH", 3u32, "2026-11-08T00:34Z", "tibia", "11-07 20:4x tibia: STEP 1 DONE, the batch's windows now compute in parallel. In spiral/apps/transformer/train_grad.spi 61370DF53C6E (unstaged), summed maps gradients over the windows with parallel::map_index (OpenMP on C, sequential elsewhere) and then sums the per-window gradients and losses in index order on one thread, so the floating-point summation order is unchanged. Evidence: (a) bit-identical to the sequential version. Node job-1794097924001-tibia-train-parity3 (gcc -O2 -fopenmp) printed the same 10 loss/norm lines and wrote the same trained checkpoint, D4F63200F17FCADF, as the sequential run before it; two parallel runs are identical; run.c and our inference still agree on the trained checkpoint. (b) All 10 train_grad Cells rows green (job-1794097837825-tibia-step2e-cells). (c) Speed: the 10-step smoke with 2 windows per batch went from 1,518 ms to 590 / 609 ms (about 2.5x) on the loaded node. NEXT STEP: re-measure ms per token at the pinned T = 512 with a full thread count and size the accumulation (k micro-batches) for an affordable step.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_pinned_batch_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-PINNED-BATCH", 4u32, "2026-11-08T00:40Z", "tibia", "11-07 20:4x tibia: COST AT THE PINNED T = 512 MEASURED (node job-1794098311423-tibia-train-cost: one step, 8 windows x 512 predicted tokens = 4,096 tokens, stories260K, C with gcc -O2 -fopenmp, node at about 50 pct load from other lanes). 8 threads: 9,426 ms, i.e. 2.30 ms per token. 1 thread: 39,523 ms, i.e. 9.65 ms per token (4.2x from 8 threads). The loss is identical at both thread counts (1.4556395) and so is the norm, so the parallel batch stays deterministic. SIZING: a pinned step (128 x 512 = 65,536 tokens) costs about 151 s on 8 threads, so 20 pinned steps take about 50 min and 100 steps about 4.2 h, an overnight run. Accumulation needs no new code: summed already sums any number of windows (128 windows x a 1 MB gradient each is about 135 MB, fine). The other blocker is on the PyTorch side: curve_torch.py's per-element Python RoPE loop is about 20 s per 512-token step on europa, and would be about 43 min per pinned step. NEXT STEP: vectorize the reference's RoPE (precomputed cos/sin tensors), then pre-register a pinned-config comparison over the first 20 steps (warmup 1000 of 100000, lr ramping from 0 to 2e-5) on the node (Spiral) and europa (torch).")
inl note_of_transformer_train_pinned_batch_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-PINNED-BATCH", 5u32, "2026-11-08T01:11Z", "tibia", "11-07 21:1x tibia: PRE-REGISTERED, written before either side of the pinned comparison has run. Config: llama2.c's pinned stories260K train.py settings except dropout 0 (torch's dropout RNG cannot be matched): batch 128 windows x 512 predicted tokens (513-token windows), lr peak 1e-3, warmup 1000, cosine to 0 at 100000, betas 0.9/0.99, eps 1e-8, wd 0.01 on 2-D tensors, clip 1.0. The FIRST 20 STEPS, starting from the stories260K checkpoint, with windows from our xorshift sampler (seed 88172645463325252) over valid_tok512.bin and PyTorch fed the same windows (pinned_torch.py, vectorized RoPE). Spiral: train_pinned.spi on the node with the parallel batch (8 threads); torch on europa after pack62's build leaves it. BAND: relative loss difference at most 1e-4 at steps 1, 5, 10 and 20 (in warmup the lr is at most 1.9e-5, so the losses mostly measure 128-window gradient accumulation and the forward pass at T = 512), and data order identical at all 20 steps. DECISION: within the band means the pinned-config parity of this item is MET for the first 20 steps. Anything else means NOT MET: report the first step over 1e-4 and diagnose it; no widening.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_pinned_batch_6 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-PINNED-BATCH", 6u32, "2026-11-08T01:14Z", "tibia", "11-07 21:1x tibia: PyTorch side STARTED on europa (main's go, off by 22:15) as pinned_accum_torch.py. It is pinned_torch.py with one change made before any comparison: each window's loss / (B*T) is backpropagated on its own and the gradients accumulate. Summing all 128 windows into one loss first would keep 128 autograd graphs at T = 512 alive at once (about 7.7 GB on a 3.4 GB host). The result is the same mean-loss gradient; only the float summation order differs, and the band covers that. Wrapped in `timeout 3300`, about 25 s per step, so 20 steps in about 9 min.")
inl note_of_transformer_train_pinned_batch_7 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-TRAIN-PINNED-BATCH", 7u32, "2026-11-08T02:27Z", "tibia", "11-07 22:3x tibia: DONE. The pre-registered pinned-config comparison (note 5, 21:11, text sha256 6A77CE03C885147E) is MET. Spiral: train_pinned.spi on the node (job-1794100341928-tibia-train-pinned; C with gcc -O2 -fopenmp, 8 threads, node at 100 pct load from other lanes; 20 steps of 128 windows x 512 predicted tokens in 4,404 s, about 220 s per step, using 15,035 CPU-s). PyTorch: pinned_accum_torch.py on europa (per-window backward with accumulation, vectorized RoPE; 20 steps in about 9 min; output D02DBC52ABDF). Config: llama2.c's pinned stories260K train.py (batch 128 x 512, lr 1e-3, warmup 1000, cosine to 0 at 100000, betas 0.9/0.99, wd 0.01 on 2-D tensors, clip 1.0), except dropout 0; the stories260K start; our seeded windows over valid_tok512.bin. Data order identical at all 20 steps; NO step over 1e-4. Step 1: 1.481707 vs 1.481708 (2.83e-7); step 5: 1.460212 vs 1.460212 (1.37e-8); step 10: 1.455545 vs 1.455545 (5.50e-8); step 20: 1.45016 vs 1.45016 (2.00e-7). The gradient norms fall from 0.52 to 0.27 on both sides. Accumulation: the 128-window batch is a single summed gradient (parallel per window, summed in order); the reference accumulates per-window backward passes; they agree to about 1e-7 in the loss. BUDGET for the record: 9.65 ms per token single-threaded at T = 512, 2.30 ms per token on 8 threads (moderate load), about 220 s per pinned step under full load. The 100k-step run itself is not attempted: about 6 days of node time at this rate.")
inl note_of_idle_lane_watch_1 () = Note ("IDLE-LANE-WATCH", 1u32, "2026-11-08T03:05Z", "resilience", "Filed from user 11-07 22:5x via main: lanes sit idle with ready backlog.")
inl note_of_idle_lane_watch_2 () = Note ("IDLE-LANE-WATCH", 2u32, "2026-11-08T03:05Z", "resilience", "DESIGN + PROTOTYPE (11-07 23:0x). Prototyped the watcher: per lane, read beacon age (autonomous-<lane>.last.log) + ready-count (kit agile next --team <lane> --open). Proven on resilience (beacon 3min=active, ready=10 -> not idle). CRITICAL FINDING - the signal is not there yet: only RESILIENCE writes a beacon; all 6 other lanes are NO-BEACON, so the watcher cannot assess their idleness. And ready-count alone is a WEAK signal - every lane shows ~10 ready because the loose --team filter surfaces shared/cross-team items for all. So the deliverable is TWO-PART: (1) a per-lane BEACON CONVENTION - each lane writes autonomous-<lane>.last.log every action/heartbeat (like resilience already does); main rolls this out (it is the hard/cross-lane part). (2) the WATCHER (resilience builds): for each lane, if beacon-age > 15min AND it has GENUINELY-actionable ready items (not just loose-filter shared ones) AND no recent holding/blocked note -> emit IDLE-LANE <lane> to main via a delivery-verified channel. The alarm MUST exclude lanes legitimately event-waiting on gated items (else it false-fires on a caught-up lane like me holding on the zig-bump window). agile cadence gives only DAILY done-counts, not fine-grained last-activity, so it is not a substitute for the beacon. NEXT: main standardizes the beacon; I build the watcher against it + a kit agile verb that returns a lane`s GENUINELY-ready (own-team, undeclined, ungated) count to fix the loose-filter weakness.")
inl note_of_idle_lane_watch_3 () = Note ("IDLE-LANE-WATCH", 3u32, "2026-11-08T03:06Z", "resilience", "NEXT STEP: (depends on main standardizing per-lane beacons) build the watcher - a scheduled check iterating lanes: beacon-age >15min AND genuinely-ready-count >0 AND no recent holding-note -> IDLE-LANE <lane> alarm to main (delivery-verified). Also file a kit item for a `kit agile next --team <lane> --genuine` that excludes loose-filter cross-team + gated items (the ready-count weakness). DoD: a lane idle >15min with its OWN undeclined actionable items triggers exactly one IDLE-LANE alarm to main; a caught-up lane event-waiting on gated items does NOT false-fire; proven by a recorded fixture (one planted idle-with-work lane + one planted holding-on-gated lane). TYPEFEATURES: lane-state union = Active(beacon fresh) | IdleWithWork(beacon stale, N ready) | HoldingGated(stale but recent holding-note) | NoBeacon(unassessable). BLOCKER: the per-lane beacon convention (only resilience writes one today) - main rolls it out; until then the watcher can only assess resilience.")
inl note_of_idle_lane_watch_4 () = Note ("IDLE-LANE-WATCH", 4u32, "2026-11-08T03:17Z", "resilience", "Evidence: CLOSED as DUPLICATE of IDLE-WITH-READY-ALARM (main`s canonical P0, same deliverable + name). I created this jumping ahead of main`s filing; the prototype + design findings (beacon-convention gap, ready-count weakness, lane-state union) are consolidated onto IDLE-WITH-READY-ALARM. No separate work remains here.")
inl note_of_upstream_syntax_sync_1_seal2 () = Note ("UPSTREAM-SYNTAX-SYNC", 1u32, "2026-11-02T14:07Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, FINDINGS from the ML-library inventory (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\typed-transformer-20261102\\ml-library-nuggets.md section 4), for the fork's owner. (1) The fork master (deps/The-Spiral-Language) lacks 52 upstream host_cpp_and_cuda_backend commits (2025-06-08 to 2025-11-14). They include stack_refs/heap_refs layouts (3a9d82be, 10367ee4), the CUDA backend redesign (cc2c16f2), type if (e1cc4119), ' @ ' macro globals (dd99e8ca), the existential prepass fix (faaebec5) and the recursive type-application fix (0cb0fda0). (2) The fork core declares stack_refs/heap_refs (core.spi:113-115) but the fork compiler has no LayoutToStackRefs, so the ML library head cannot compile on fork master; our apps/compiler has it. (3) The fixes in f4e54a0e (C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP, C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT, GADT-MATCH-FORALL-CONSTRAINT, FOR-D, SPIRAL-MACRO-SPLICE-QUOTE) exist only as diff files under posts/ and are not applied to The Spiral Language 2/*.fs. (4) default-int/float and TypeLitToLit are upstream's commits, not ours.")
inl note_of_tibia_agent_1_seal2 () = Note ("TIBIA-AGENT", 1u32, "2026-11-02T11:25Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: the agent drafts were saved out of the gitignored target/predict into the kit drafts store at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\tibia\\TIBIA-AGENT\\. 39 files, 486,479 bytes, all copies hash-verified.\n- m3\\: the full M3 tree, i.e. src at 10-23 plus src\\typed_network.spi (7,794 B, sha256 4250F8110DCC…, the n49 draft: graph size state, raw/planned/frozen, width prototype eight=8/wide=25) and apps\\predict (the M3 harness main).\n- m1\\src\\search.spi: evolve_cem with the i64 elite fix (n32).\n- m2e\\: the 10-23 11:15 patched views/network/hunting_pack/pack_sim/live (sighted, wide_inputs, decide_wide, wide_features) plus apps\\predict.\nWhy the store and not src: typed_network.spi uses the pre-granular syntax (inl, if/then, forall) and needs network.wide_inputs from the M2e patch, which src/network.spi doesn't have. Its landing needs a rewrite, so the destination isn't final.\nThe target\\ originals are left in place; the m3.c build output (6.9 MB) is regenerable and was not copied. The drafts' harness scripts (m1.ps1, m3.ps1, m3-patch.ps1, judges) lived in a session scratchpad and are lost.")
inl note_of_tibia_docs_to_code_1_seal2 () = Note ("TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE", 1u32, "2026-11-07T14:35Z", "tibia", "11-07 tibia: DC1 MAPPING GAP found (main's pointer, verified in git). MODELING.md's \"graph upgrades\" table (git show 949351b^:docs/MODELING.md, line 29) had the row \"| Policy weights | Optional ternary weights {-1, 0, +1} packed 2 bits each (from TritCritNet): add/subtract instead of multiply, less memory traffic on CPU and WGSL |\". The 10-22 12:59 note mapped that table as \"held by TIBIA-AGENT\", but no backlog item mentioned ternary weights until TRANSFORMER-QUANT-TERNARY on 11-07 (full backlog search, 0 hits otherwise). So the deletion was deliberate (DC1, all four docs together), and this one claim was dropped without a reason. It is now restored as TIBIA-TERNARY-POLICY-WEIGHTS. The file is not restored.")
inl note_of_ci_single_pipeline_1_seal2 () = Note ("CI-SINGLE-PIPELINE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:39Z", "kit", "11-01 10:3x kit: the follow-up to this Done item is CI-WORKFLOW-NAMES (user 11-01: one gh-pages.yml everywhere, slim). The 10-18 audit accepted x ci.yml; the new census also found calc-parser .yaml, tictactoe dist.yml, dlm, FsClr and 12 repos without actions.lock.")
inl note_of_autonomous_stop_gate_1_seal2 () = Note ("AUTONOMOUS-STOP-GATE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:46Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit (FYI to main, owner of the hook): the smarter gate spec (PullItem|KeepWaiting|MayStop, anti-spin rule: never block twice on the same item inside 10 min) is on KIT-GROOM-ROUND n4; the hook would call kit groom gate instead of its own list logic once that verb ships.")
inl note_of_autonomous_stop_gate_2_seal2 () = Note ("AUTONOMOUS-STOP-GATE", 2u32, "2026-11-04T04:20Z", "main", "11-04 00:2x main, from the star exchange: 4 lanes report the same lost turns, so this is fleet-wide. The stop gate re-claims an item for a lane that just released it with a note: x saw MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP re-claimed 4 times and TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT 3 times; x-dumps saw it on 11-03; fiscal had MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP auto-claimed at 21:38, 20 minutes before the run ended. Each re-claim costs a turn. RULE PROPOSED: after release --as <lane> with a note, the gate does not offer that item to the same lane until the lease TTL has passed. Owner: kit.")
inl note_of_autonomous_stop_gate_3_seal2 () = Note ("AUTONOMOUS-STOP-GATE", 3u32, "2026-11-05T02:20Z", "main", "11-04 22:2x main STAR EXCHANGE ASK (x-dumps): the gate cannot tell a lane whose work is delivered and gated on a NAMED dependency (e.g. x-dumps waiting on x's trains) from an idle lane, so it keeps demanding new work and nudges lanes toward other teams' deep P0s, which x-dumps declined to avoid fumbling. Proposal: a \"blocked on <ID>\" state the lane records (kit agile set <ID> Blocked with the dependency, or a per-session blocked.txt next to teams.txt); the gate then lists only CPU-light own-team items and drops the cross-team pointer while the named dependency is open. Pair with the node-idle line agreed with kit (NODE-ALWAYS-FED n14), wired after TRAIN26 installs.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_1_seal2 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T06:16Z", "main", "main 02:2x (resilience's lab sweep, round 3): the lab folder names LIB-PROCESS-HEAL and LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT never had task ids and were folded into this item and lib/spiral/process.spi. Recorded as subsumed here, so the names resolve to this item; no new tasks.")
inl note_of_kit_standards_spi_1_seal2 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-SPI", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:43Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit RE-MEASURE (user round 5: is standards.spi paying off? the user did not ask for it). Repos using it: kit 452 standards.spi files, tibia 2, every other own repo 0 (spiral, fiscal, x, dice, alphabet, polyglot, i574n.github, fork-spiral-patch). census.spi holds 7,548 rows. Caught: the 10-24 count was 0 refusals in 1,802 job logs (it only restated contracts then). Since the ratchet (EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET) it refuses for real: tonight 2 refusals, both true positives that changed the design: agile_sprint's plain code diluting typed density (split into agile_sprint_rows) and kit_table_rows encoding rows with tab separators (tab path dropped). False positives seen: 0 tonight; the known cost is that new packages need a census row, which forces the HostFree+LibReferenced contract (intended). VERDICT: PAYS OFF inside kit as a ratchet (cheap, 2 true refusals, 0 false, both moved code toward the typefeatureset); NOT YET an ecosystem tool, since only kit uses it. Next rules toward a minimally usable ecosystem filed as KIT-STANDARDS-ECOSYSTEM.")
inl note_of_fiscal_en16931_1_3_17_1_seal2 () = Note ("FISCAL-EN16931-1-3-17", 1u32, "2026-11-01T04:50Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 02:40 fiscal: MISSED PIN found by the node corpus_checks gate: en16931_rules_parse_completely still required 979 assertions, but validation-1.3.17's EN16931-UBL-validation-preprocessed.sch has 981 (XML count: 104 rules, 981 asserts; the engine reads exactly that: \"rules 104 of 104, assertions 981 of 979\"). So the test failed everywhere since the bump. Fixed in src/corpus_checks.spi (979 -> 981, unstaged). Lesson for pin bumps: grep the tests for the old counts in the same change.")
